  <section|The construction problem and the order of its
  limits><label|ex-statement>

  The aim is to construct a relativistic field theory from finite-dimensional
  Wilson integrals. There are two limiting operations. First the lattice
  spacing tends to zero while the physical period stays fixed. Then the
  period tends to infinity. The observables and bare action coefficients must
  be chosen coherently before either operation: changing the family in
  response to a requested sequence of periods would give a weaker statement.

  <paragraph|A caution about the target of this section.> The public target
  formulated after Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-construction-headline> is
  one statement that the source verifies by machine. Read on its own, it is
  weaker than a reader might expect from the words \Pexistence of
  four-dimensional Yang\UMills theory\Q. It can be satisfied by a generalized
  free scalar field, that is, a quantum field whose vacuum correlations are
  determined by its two-point function through sums over pairings, here with
  a continuous distribution of masses; and the lattice observables that the
  target asks for can then be chosen independently of the gauge
  configuration. Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-target-free-witness> states
  this precisely and explains which features of the target allow it. The
  target alone therefore does not express the specifically Yang\UMills
  content of the construction. That content lies in how the later sections
  reach the target: they work with specific local gauge-invariant functions
  of the Wilson links, and every limiting object is obtained from the Wilson
  measure. The observation does not affect that construction. It does mean
  that a main theorem intended to express this content should also record the
  chosen lattice observables and their connections to the limiting theory.
  The source has such a detailed statement, and the group reviewing the
  source bases its assessment of these connections on a stronger target of
  this kind (Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-target-free-witness>).
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-construction-headline> proposes a stronger
  headline retaining these connections, with references to the detailed
  construction and an explicit account of the remaining assembly.

  We assume familiarity with finite-dimensional Lie groups, probability Haar
  measure on compact groups, Lebesgue integration and dominated convergence,
  and elementary Hilbert-space theory. The specific compactness, quotient and
  operator arguments used below are supplied where they enter the
  construction.

  Fix a closed subgroup <math|G\<subset\>U<around|(|r<rsub|G>|)>>, with its
  specified faithful matrix representation. Its real Lie algebra is

  <\equation*>
    \<frak-g\>=<around|{|X\<in\>M<rsub|r<rsub|G>><around|(|\<bbb-C\>|)>:exp
    <around|(|t*X|)>\<in\>G<text| for every >t\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>.
  </equation*>

  We assume that <math|\<frak-g\>> is nonabelian and simple: its only real
  subspaces invariant under every <math|<math-up|ad><space|0.17em>X>,
  <math|X\<in\>\<frak-g\>>, are <math|0> and <math|\<frak-g\>>. The group
  <math|G> is compact, being closed in <math|U<around|(|r<rsub|G>|)>>. It is
  not assumed to be connected, and the given representation is not assumed to
  be irreducible. The Wilson action of Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-lattice>
  is normalized by the dimension <math|r<rsub|G>> of this representation, so
  the same abstract group in two representations gives two instances of the
  problem. Coordinates on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>> are written
  <math|x=<around|(|\<b-x\>,t|)>>, with time last. The Minkowski form is
  <math|t<rsup|2>-<around|\||\<b-x\>|\|><rsup|2>>, and the closed forward
  cone is <math|<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>=<around|{|<around|(|\<b-p\>,E|)>:<around|\||\<b-p\>|\|>\<leqslant\>E|}>>.

  <subsection|A construction-bound main theorem><label|ex-construction-headline>

  The following theorem retains the connection between classical observables,
  lattice representatives and quantum fields. Its normalizations and local
  statements are specified below. Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-construction-assembly>
  assembles their proofs on one retained Wilson family and one limiting
  hierarchy.

  Let <math|V<rsub|d>> be the finite-dimensional space of gauge-invariant
  polynomials in radial curvature jets of weighted degree at most <math|d>, a
  curvature derivative of order <math|j> having weight <math|j+2>; see
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:local-splitting>
  and<nbsp><reference|sec:local-sections>. Fix a real polynomial basis,
  homogeneous in the number of curvature factors, and use its
  complexification as coordinates on <math|V<rsub|d>>, with the constant
  polynomial as the unit coordinate. A local section <math|L<rsub|d>> assigns
  to <math|v\<in\>V<rsub|d>> a polynomial in the entries of finitely many
  links and their conjugates, linearly in <math|v>, invariant under lattice
  gauge transformations. Its stencil is independent of spacing and period. It
  preserves the unit, conjugation and signed coordinate symmetries. For every
  radial jet <math|J> it satisfies the normalization

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:headline-jet-normalization><around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d<rsup|k>|d*h<rsup|k>>*L<rsub|d>*<around|(|v;H*<around|(|J,h|)>|)>|\|><rsub|h=0>=<around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d<rsup|k>|d*h<rsup|k>>*v*<around|(|h\<cdot\>J|)>|\|><rsub|h=0>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>d.
  </equation>

  Here <math|H*<around|(|J,h|)>> is parallel transport along lattice links
  for the polynomial radial connection at scale <math|h>, and
  <math|h\<cdot\>J> multiplies the weight-<math|j+2> component by
  <math|h<rsup|j+2>>. Thus small-field testing recovers the prescribed
  classical polynomial.

  Write <math|a<rsub|N>=D<rsub|N><rsup|-1>> for the lattice spacing,
  <math|P<rsub|p>> for the physical period, and <math|U> for a link
  configuration on the corresponding torus. For each <math|d> choose a
  reference level <math|r<rsub|d>>. At cutoff <math|<around|(|N,p|)>>,
  <math|N\<geqslant\>r<rsub|d>>, the renormalized insertion has the form

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:headline-insertion>I<rsup|<around|(|d|)>><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v;x;U|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>*L<rsub|d>*<around*|(|D<rsub|\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|-1>>*R<rsub|d,N,p><rsup|-1>*v;x;U|)>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<theta\><rsub|N>\<gtr\>0> is the small-field threshold and
  <math|D<rsub|t>> multiplies a coordinate with <math|s> curvature factors by
  <math|t<rsup|s>>. The invertible linear map <math|R<rsub|d,N,p>> is fixed
  by transporting the inserted Wilson density from level <math|N> to
  <math|r<rsub|d>> and reading its polynomial coefficient there. This is
  exact transport of the unnormalized inserted density by the same block
  integrations as the uninserted density, with the scale factors and
  remainder bounds of Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:local-splitting>,
  <reference|sec:terminal-marked-construction>
  and<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-marked-field-local>. In particular, transporting
  <math|R<rsub|d,N,p><rsup|-1>*v> gives coefficient <math|v> at the reference
  level. The maps in <eqref|eq:headline-insertion> must have this transport
  and normalization; freely chosen renormalization matrices are not an
  alternative definition. The same maps are used at every translated anchor.
  Choices at the finitely many levels <math|N\<less\>r<rsub|d>> do not affect
  the limits.

  <\remark>
    <dueto|The local normalization has content><label|rem:headline-nonconstant-sections>If
    <math|L<rsub|d><around|(|v|)>> is constant, with value <math|c>, then
    <eqref|eq:headline-jet-normalization> says that the polynomial
    <math|h\<mapsto\>v*<around|(|h\<cdot\>J|)>> has constant term <math|c>
    and all other coefficients zero: its degree is at most <math|d>. Taking
    <math|h=1> gives <math|v<around|(|J|)>=c> for every <math|J>. In
    particular <math|L<rsub|d>> is injective, and a nonconstant curvature
    polynomial cannot have a constant local representative. Since both
    <math|D<rsub|t>> for <math|t\<gtr\>0> and <math|R<rsub|d,N,p>> are
    invertible, the insertion space in <eqref|eq:headline-insertion> contains
    nonconstant functions whenever <math|V<rsub|d>> contains nonconstant
    polynomials. On sufficiently large tori the stencil does not wrap, so its
    link assignments extend to torus configurations and this remains true
    there. This rules out the specific replacement of <em|all> lattice
    insertions by constants. It does not by itself prove that the continuum
    field space contains a field independent of the unit, or that its
    correlations are non-Gaussian.
  </remark>

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|The Wilson construction><label|thm:ex-construction-headline>For
    every compact matrix group <math|G> with nonabelian simple Lie algebra as
    specified above, and every positive blocking penalty <math|b>, one can
    choose one sequence of lattice spacings <math|a<rsub|N>*\<downarrow\>*0>,
    one increasing sequence of physical periods
    <math|P<rsub|p>*\<uparrow\>*\<infty\>>, bare Wilson coefficients
    <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>>, and local observables
    <eqref|eq:headline-insertion> satisfying
    <eqref|eq:headline-jet-normalization>, all on a single Wilson
    construction. The coefficients satisfy <eqref|ex-running-input>. These
    choices precede any choice of a subsequence of periods.

    For every pair of strictly increasing index maps <math|<around|(|u,p|)>>
    there is a refinement <math|<around|(|u\<circ\>v,p\<circ\>q|)>> and one
    common system of limiting fields, correlations and phases with the
    following properties.

    <\enumerate>
      <item><em|Wilson limits and field labels.> The physical fields of
      degree at most <math|d> are quotients
      <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|d>=V<rsub|d>/\<cal-N\><rsub|d>>, where
      <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|d>> consists of labels whose insertion vanishes in
      all separated mixed correlations with arbitrary spectators. They have
      compatible injective grade inclusions, a unit, conjugation, derivatives
      and spacetime symmetry actions. Write <math|<around|[|v|]><rsub|d>> for
      the field represented by <math|v\<in\>V<rsub|d>>. At grade <math|d> the
      unit is represented by <math|a<rsub|r<rsub|d>><rsup|d>*\<b-1\>>, not in
      general by the constant polynomial <math|\<b-1\>>. The grade inclusions
      are the compatible maps constructed in
      Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-fields>; they need not send the
      class of a polynomial to the class of the same polynomial at a larger
      grade. For every finite list of graded labels
      <math|v<rsub|i>\<in\>V<rsub|d<rsub|i>>>,
      <math|1\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>n>, and every smooth compactly
      supported test <math|f> away from the coincidence diagonals,

      <\equation>
        <label|eq:headline-wilson-limit>

        <\eqsplit>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|lim<rsub|k\<to\>\<infty\>>
          lim<rsub|N\<to\>\<infty\>> a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>*<space|-0.17em><big|sum><rsub|\<b-x\>>f<around|(|\<b-x\>|)>*\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\><rsub|N,p<around|(|q<around|(|k|)>|)>>><space|-0.17em><around*|[|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>I<rsup|<around|(|d<rsub|i>|)>><rsub|N,p<around|(|q<around|(|k|)>|)>>*<around|(|v<rsub|i>;x<rsub|i>;U|)>|]>>>|<row|<cell|=<big|int>f<around|(|\<b-x\>|)>*S<rsup|<math-up|mix>><rsub|<around|[|v<rsub|1>|]><rsub|d<rsub|1>>,\<ldots\>,<around|[|v<rsub|n>|]><rsub|d<rsub|n>>><around|(|\<b-x\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<b-x\>.>>>>
        </eqsplit>
      </equation>

      Here <math|\<mu\><rsub|N,p>> is the Wilson probability measure of
      Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-lattice>, and the sum uses the physical
      lattice points in its fundamental box, with the convention of
      <eqref|ex-smearing>. The inner limit exists along the <em|full> mesh
      sequence at every suitable fixed period. One refinement works for all
      labels, arities and tests. Auxiliary field comparisons may use the
      refined mesh <math|u\<circ\>v>; the Wilson limit displayed here does
      not.

      <item><em|Phases of that same limit.> There is a compact metrizable
      space <math|X> with a full-support probability measure <math|\<nu\>>
      and smooth separated correlations <math|S<rsup|\<chi\>>>, multilinear
      in the fields, such that

      <\equation*>
        S<rsup|<math-up|mix>><rsub|\<b-A\>><around|(|\<b-x\>|)>=<big|int><rsub|X>S<rsup|\<chi\>><rsub|\<b-A\>><around|(|\<b-x\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<chi\>|)>.
      </equation*>

      These are the characters of the commutative algebra obtained by
      compressing the mixed theory's slab observables to its zero-energy
      space; <math|\<nu\>> represents its vacuum state. Thus the phase space
      and component correlations come from the mixed Wilson limit, as in
      Sections<nbsp><reference|ex-phases>
      and<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-selection>. Every component has reflection
      positivity and the same field null relations as the mixed hierarchy;
      see Sections<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-regularity>
      and<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-fields>.

      <item><em|Relativistic reconstruction.> For every
      <math|\<chi\>\<in\>X>, these correlations reconstruct a nontrivial
      local relativistic field theory on a Hilbert space with a common dense
      invariant domain, cyclic vacuum, adjoints, tempered fields, strongly
      continuous Poincaré symmetry and translation spectrum in the closed
      forward cone. The translation-invariant vectors form precisely the
      vacuum line, and nonzero field labels act nontrivially. Its vacuum
      distributions are the analytic boundary values of
      <math|S<rsup|\<chi\>>>, in the sense of
      Definition<nbsp><reference|ex-reconstructed> with <math|\<chi\>> in
      place of <math|\<chi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>. The boundary distributions also
      obey the phase-mixture identity on joint Schwartz tests. The analytic
      and field constructions are described in
      Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:joint-boundary> and
      Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:word-reconstruction>. There is a
      character <math|\<chi\><rsub|\<ast\>>\<in\>X>, with one-dimensional
      ground space as in Theorem<nbsp><reference|ex-pure-ground>, for which
      all the conclusions below hold; they use these same data.

      <item><em|Local products and the physical stress tensor.> There is one
      operator-product expansion for the mixed state and every component,
      with the coefficient and remainder bounds
      <eqref|ex-ope-coefficient>\U<eqref|ex-ope-remainder>. The physical
      field system contains a symmetric conserved stress tensor
      <math|T<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>> whose translation and rotation charges
      implement the field derivatives and rotation action. Its normalization
      is that of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-stress-normalization>;
      its separated Ward identities are those of
      Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-ward-propagation>. In the
      selected theory set

      <\equation*>
        \<Theta\>=<big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>=1><rsup|4>T<rsub|\<mu\>*\<mu\>>-<around*|\<langle\>|<big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>=1><rsup|4>T<rsub|\<mu\>*\<mu\>>|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<chi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>*\<b-1\>.
      </equation*>

      This very field, rather than a separately chosen grade-four field,
      satisfies

      <\equation*>
        lim<rsub|r*\<downarrow\>*0> r<rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|r<rsup|-1>|)>*S<rsup|\<chi\><rsub|\<ast\>>><rsub|\<Theta\>,\<Theta\>>*<around|(|-r*e<rsub|t>/2,r*e<rsub|t>/2|)>=<frac|3*dim<rsub|\<bbb-R\>>
        \<frak-g\>|\<pi\><rsup|4>>,<space|2em>e<rsub|t>=<around|(|0,0,0,1|)>.
      </equation*>

      Its vacuum one-point functional vanishes and its two-point function has
      the finite mass-shell-profile exclusion of
      Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:trace-profiles>. The comparison and
      limit use this same normalized stress tensor; see
      Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:trace-normalized-stress> and
      Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:trace-asymptotic>.
    </enumerate>

    The quotients, phases and reconstructed theories may depend on the
    requested order and refinement. The lattice family does not.
  </theorem>

  <paragraph|Scope.> This formulation selects conclusions of the
  construction; it does not restate every source result. Contact extensions,
  nested operator products, effective couplings and Maxwell scaling
  comparisons require separate precise statements and are not asserted here.
  The running slope in <eqref|ex-running-input> is
  <math|11*C<rsub|\<frak-g\>>/<around|(|24*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)>>, with the
  Casimir for the normalized trace inner product.
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:shoot-public-running> and
  <eqref|eq:public-family-running> identify this value with the bare
  coefficients of this very Wilson family.

  No uniqueness of the infinite-volume limit or independence of the spacing
  sequence, local sections or blocking scheme is claimed. The result
  constructs correlations and fields, not a probability measure on continuum
  connections, Wilson-loop observables, particle states or a mass gap. The
  operator-product bounds do not assert agreement with a perturbative series,
  and the trace test does not exclude every generalized free-field
  description. These distinctions matter in comparing the result with the
  Yang\UMills existence and mass-gap problem; see
  <cite-detail|YMG26|Sections<nbsp>1--2, 7--9>. In that comparison, the
  axiomatic field theory and its curvature-polynomial representatives address
  the first two requirements. The stress tensor, running law,
  operator-product bounds and trace asymptotic give specific short-distance
  conclusions, but not the full agreement with perturbative renormalization
  theory requested in the third requirement. The fourth requirement, a
  positive spectral gap above the vacuum, remains outside the result. The
  inverse reference maps are constructed with quantitative bounds; explicit
  formulas for all their matrix entries are not part of the assertion.

  Forgetting the normalization, the character-algebra construction, the
  reconstructions away from <math|\<chi\><rsub|\<ast\>>> and the
  stress-tensor identification gives, together with the unit, covering and
  token compatibility specified below and proved in the field construction,
  the weaker public target. Its insufficiency is discussed in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-target-free-witness>.
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-construction-assembly> proves the stronger
  same-data assertion: normalized insertions, field quotients, phase algebra,
  every-character reconstruction and physical stress all use the same
  extraction. Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-conditional-existence> records
  the logical projection to the weaker target. In particular the
  every-character assertion in item<nbsp>3 is supplied by its own vacuum
  argument, whereas the trace asymptotic in item<nbsp>4 concerns the
  distinguished selected character.

  <subsection|The weaker public target>

  The lattice family consists of strictly increasing positive integer
  sequences <math|P<rsub|p>> and <math|D<rsub|N>>, a spacing
  <math|a<rsub|N>=D<rsub|N><rsup|-1>>, real coefficients
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>>, and local gauge-invariant insertion functions
  <math|O<rsub|N,p,v><around|(|x;U|)>> indexed by tokens <math|v>. The
  periodic lattice has <math|P<rsub|p>*D<rsub|N>> sites along each axis and
  physical period <math|P<rsub|p>>. Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-lattice>
  describes its Wilson probability measure and moments. We call the family
  <em|regular> when its insertions are continuous in <math|U>, invariant
  under every lattice gauge transformation, and uniformly local in the
  following precise sense. For each token <math|v> there is a finite stencil
  <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|v>\<subset\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>, independent of
  <math|N,p,x>, such that two configurations agreeing on all links with both
  endpoints in <math|x+\<Sigma\><rsub|v>>, reduced modulo
  <math|P<rsub|p>*D<rsub|N>>, give the same insertion value. The fourth
  regularity condition is

  <\equation>
    <label|ex-running-input>\<exists\>\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0<space|1em>\<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0<space|1em>\<exists\>N<rsub|0><space|1em>\<forall\>N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0><space|1em>\<forall\>p:<space|2em><around*|\||<frac|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>|log
    <around|(|a<rsub|N><rsup|-1>|)>>-\<sigma\>|\|>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>.
  </equation>

  In particular, the choice of <math|N<rsub|0>> is uniform in the period
  index.

  Let <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)>> be the set
  of ordered <math|n>-tuples of pairwise distinct points. A separated test is
  a smooth complex function with compact support in this set, extended by
  zero outside it. For insertion tokens <math|\<b-v\>=<around|(|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|n>|)>>,
  write <math|M<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|\<b-v\>;\<b-x\>|)>> for their Wilson
  moment and put

  <\equation>
    <label|ex-smearing>W<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|\<b-v\>;f|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>*<big|sum><rsub|\<b-x\>\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|N,p><rsup|n>>f*<around|(|a<rsub|N>*\<b-x\>|)>*M<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|\<b-v\>;\<b-x\>|)>.
  </equation>

  Here the coordinates of <math|\<b-x\>> are chosen in the centered
  fundamental cell. A period is suitable for <math|f> if its support lies
  strictly inside that physical cell in every coordinate.

  The limiting data have several related components. For every integer grade
  <math|d\<geqslant\>0> there is a finite-dimensional complex space
  <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|d>>. It has a complex-linear injection into a common
  field-label space; every label is represented at some grade. Tokens map
  onto the disjoint union <math|<big|coprod><rsub|d>\<cal-F\><rsub|d>>. A
  compact metric space <math|X>, a probability measure <math|\<nu\>> of full
  support, and a selected character <math|\<chi\><rsub|\<ast\>>\<in\>X>
  describe the phases. For graded fields <math|\<b-A\>>, let
  <math|S<rsup|\<chi\>><rsub|\<b-A\>><around|(|\<b-x\>|)>> and
  <math|S<rsup|<math-up|mix>><rsub|\<b-A\>><around|(|\<b-x\>|)>> denote the
  phase and mixed separated correlations. They are smooth on configuration
  space, permutation symmetric, and continuous in <math|\<chi\>>, with

  <\equation>
    <label|ex-mixture>S<rsup|<math-up|mix>><rsub|\<b-A\>><around|(|\<b-x\>|)>=<big|int><rsub|X>S<rsup|\<chi\>><rsub|\<b-A\>><around|(|\<b-x\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<chi\>|)>.
  </equation>

  The mixed correlations are the Wilson limits. The selected correlations are
  the Euclidean correlations to be continued into the reconstructed theory.
  These two roles should be kept distinct even when the phase space has only
  one point. At the level of this target, only the selected point is required
  to carry a reconstructed relativistic theory. The other points are required
  to satisfy the displayed correlation properties and the common
  operator-product estimates. Multilinearity of all these correlations in the
  graded fields is not an additional condition of the target.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Reconstructed selected theory><label|ex-reconstructed> The
    selected theory consists of a complex Hilbert space <math|\<cal-H\>>, a
    normalized vacuum <math|\<Omega\>>, a common dense invariant domain
    <math|\<cal-D\>> containing <math|\<Omega\>>, and complex-linear fields
    <math|\<Phi\>*<around|(|A,f|)>:\<cal-D\>\<to\>\<cal-D\>>, linear
    separately in the label and test, where
    <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)>>. For fixed
    <math|A,\<psi\>>, the map <math|f\<mapsto\>\<Phi\>*<around|(|A,f|)>*\<psi\>>
    is continuous in the Schwartz topology. The domain is the span of field
    words applied to the vacuum. There is a conjugate-linear label adjoint
    <math|A\<mapsto\>A<rsup|\<ast\>>> with

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\>*<around|(|A,f|)>*\<psi\>,\<eta\>|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,\<Phi\>*<around|(|A<rsup|\<ast\>>,<wide|f|\<bar\>>|)>*\<eta\>|\<rangle\>>.
    </equation*>

    A strongly continuous unitary representation of the proper orthochronous
    Poincaré group preserves the domain and the vacuum, and transforms fields
    with the specified action on labels and the ordinary affine pullback of
    tests. Its translation spectral measure is supported in
    <math|<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>>, and every translation-invariant vector
    is a scalar multiple of <math|\<Omega\>>. Fields commute on
    <math|\<cal-D\>> when their test supports are spacelike separated. Every
    nonzero label acts nontrivially for some test, and at least one smeared
    field is nonzero.

    For fixed spatial positions the selected correlations have a holomorphic
    continuation <math|F<rsub|\<b-A\>>*<around|(|\<b-z\>;\<b-x\>|)>> on
    <math|<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>z<rsub|1>\<less\>\<cdots\>\<less\><math-up|Im><space|0.17em>z<rsub|n>>.
    At strictly ordered Euclidean times its value at
    <math|z<rsub|i>=i*t<rsub|i>> is <math|S<rsup|\<chi\><rsub|\<ast\>>><rsub|\<b-A\>><around|(|<around|(|\<b-x\><rsub|i>,t<rsub|i>|)><rsub|i>|)>>.
    For every strictly increasing real height vector <math|\<b-h\>> and all
    <math|f<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,f<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:public-selected-boundary>

      <\eqsplit>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|lim<rsub|\<epsilon\>*\<downarrow\>*0>
        <big|int><rsub|<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>>>|<cell|F<rsub|\<b-A\>>*<around*|(|<around|(|t<rsub|j>+i*\<epsilon\>*h<rsub|j>|)><rsub|j>;<around|(|\<b-x\><rsub|j>|)><rsub|j>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|j=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|j>*<around|(|\<b-x\><rsub|j>,t<rsub|j>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<b-x\>*<space|0.17em>d*\<b-t\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,\<Phi\>*<around|(|A<rsub|1>,f<rsub|1>|)>*\<cdots\>*\<Phi\>*<around|(|A<rsub|n>,f<rsub|n>|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>.>>>>
      </eqsplit>
    </equation>

    In this target clause the integral has value zero when its integrand is
    not Lebesgue integrable. Thus the clause prescribes a boundary limit
    without separately requiring absolute integrability at positive height.
    When ordinary integrals are used in the construction, their integrability
    must be supplied by the analytic estimates, as in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:tested-cr> and
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:joint-boundary>.
  </definition>

  The existence target is one regular Wilson family such that, for every
  strictly increasing period-index sequence <math|p<around|(|k|)>>, a
  strictly increasing <math|q<around|(|k|)>> and one hierarchy with the
  preceding properties can be found. For each separated test there are row
  limits <math|w<rsub|p>*<around|(|\<b-v\>;f|)>> satisfying

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|lim<rsub|N\<to\>\<infty\>>
    W<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|\<b-v\>;f|)>>|<cell|=w<rsub|p>*<around|(|\<b-v\>;f|)>>|<cell|>|<cell|<text|at
    each suitable fixed >p,<eq-number><label|ex-row-target>>>|<row|<cell|lim<rsub|k\<to\>\<infty\>>
    w<rsub|p<around|(|q<around|(|k|)>|)>>*<around|(|\<b-v\>;f|)>>|<cell|=<big|int>f<around|(|\<b-x\>|)>*S<rsup|<math-up|mix>><rsub|\<b-A\>><around|(|\<b-x\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<b-x\>,>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number><label|ex-volume-target>>>>>
  </align>

  where <math|A<rsub|i>> is the class of <math|v<rsub|i>>. Suitability holds
  eventually along the selected period sequence. The same <math|q> and
  hierarchy work for all arities, tokens and tests. The hierarchy may depend
  on the requested sequence; the Wilson family may not.

  The full endpoint also specifies a common operator-product expansion and a
  short-distance test on one centered field of grade four, described in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-assembly>. These are additional obligations
  on the same hierarchy. The scope of the target should be read literally.
  The spectral conclusions are that the energy-momentum spectrum lies in the
  closed forward cone and that the translation-invariant vectors form the
  line <math|\<bbb-C\>*\<Omega\>>; no gap above the vacuum in the spectrum is
  asserted. The short-distance test prescribes the leading behaviour
  <math|r<rsup|-8>*log<rsup|-2><around|(|r<rsup|-1>|)>> of one two-point
  function on the time axis and, as a consequence, excludes its coincidence
  near <math|r=0> with any finite combination of the mass-shell profiles of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:trace-profiles>; it does not exclude
  infinite or continuous superpositions of masses and makes no assertion
  about truncated correlations of higher order. The target requires a
  positive limit of <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>/log
  <around|(|a<rsub|N><rsup|-1>|)>> but does not prescribe its value. The
  grade-four field is existentially chosen, subject to the stated test
  (vanishing vacuum one-point functional, the exact asymptotic coefficient,
  and the profile exclusion); the target contains no stress-tensor or
  Ward-identity normalization identifying it physically, and that
  identification belongs to the detailed construction. More generally the
  target concerns a theory of the constructed gauge-invariant observables:
  the insertions <math|O<rsub|N,p,v>> are part of what is constructed, no
  lattice formula is prescribed for them beyond regularity and the joint
  convergence requirements, and the lattice measure enters the target only
  through the mixed correlations. Identifying a particular label with a
  separately prescribed physical observable requires its own construction
  argument. The detailed construction is parameterized by a positive blocking
  penalty; the target is obtained from the value <math|1> in the conditional
  assembly of Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-conditional-existence>. No
  uniqueness assertion is made.

  <\remark>
    <dueto|What the target alone ties to the Wilson
    measure><label|rem:ex-target-adequacy>The Wilson measure enters the
    target only through <eqref|ex-row-target>\U<eqref|ex-volume-target>, that
    is, through the mixed correlations. These are tied to the selected
    correlations only by the mixture identity <eqref|ex-mixture> and by the
    common operator-product estimates. The target does not require the
    correlations to be multilinear in the graded fields, nor the correlations
    at a non-selected phase to be reflection positive. Consequently the
    public clauses alone do not force the selected theory to arise from the
    Wilson measure: they admit Wilson families whose insertions do not depend
    on the gauge configuration.

    To see this, suppose hierarchy data are given that satisfy every
    requirement of the target except the two convergence statements, with
    selected correlations <math|S=S<rsup|\<chi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>>, and suppose
    that some <math|u\<in\>\<cal-F\><rsub|0>>, <math|u\<ne\>0>, enters
    <math|S> homogeneously: replacing the tag <math|<around|(|0,u|)>> by
    <math|<around|(|0,2*u|)>> in any one slot doubles <math|S>. Put
    <math|m<around|(|A|)>=1> if <math|A=<around|(|0,u|)>> and
    <math|m<around|(|A|)>=0> for every other tag, and
    <math|M<rsub|\<b-A\>>=<big|prod><rsub|i>m<around|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>, with
    <math|M<rsub|\<emptyset\>>=1>. Let the tokens be the tags themselves,
    with the identity token map; take <math|P<rsub|p>=p+1>,
    <math|D<rsub|N>=N+2>, <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>=log <around|(|N+2|)>> and
    the insertions <math|O<rsub|N,p,A><around|(|x;U|)>=m<around|(|A|)>>,
    which are constant, hence regular with empty stencils, and satisfy
    <eqref|ex-running-input> with <math|\<sigma\>=1>. Since the Wilson
    measure is a probability measure, the moments are <math|M<rsub|\<b-A\>>>,
    and Riemann sums give <math|W<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;f|)>\<to\>M<rsub|\<b-A\>>*<big|int>f>
    at every suitable <math|p>. Replace the phase data by the two-point space
    <math|<around|{|+,-|}>> with the uniform measure and selected point
    <math|+>, and set

    <\equation*>
      S<rsup|+>=S,<space|2em>S<rsup|->=2*M-S,<space|2em>S<rsup|<math-up|mix>>=M.
    </equation*>

    The mixture identity holds, the selected theory is unchanged, and the
    volume limit is <math|<big|int>f*M> along every period sequence. In the
    operator-product data add two grade-zero channels with actors <math|u>
    and <math|2*u> and coefficients <math|h<rsub|a>> and
    <math|-<tfrac|1|2>h<rsub|a>>, where <math|h<rsub|a>=<big|prod><rsub|k>m<around|(|a<rsub|k>|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i>c<rsup|0,i><rsub|a>*<space|0.17em>m<around|(|<around|(|0,E<rsub|0,i>|)>|)>>
    is the defect of the old grade-zero channels
    <math|<around|(|E<rsub|0,i>,c<rsup|0,i><rsub|a>|)>> on <math|M>. The
    coefficient bounds for the new channels follow from those of the old
    ones, because <math|<around|\||m|\|>\<leqslant\>1>. At the selected point
    the two new terms cancel by homogeneity; in the mixed state the expansion
    reproduces <math|M> exactly, because <math|M> vanishes on every actor of
    positive grade; and the remainder at <math|-> is minus that at <math|+>.
    Every requirement other than the convergence statements mentions only the
    selected point, the common operator-product data or the listed properties
    of the phase correlations, and these have just been checked. The lattice
    moment of every field of positive grade, including the grade-four field
    of the trace test, is zero in this example.

    The detailed construction does not use any of these freedoms: its
    correlations are multilinear in the fields, each of its phases carries
    its own positive reconstruction, and its insertions are fixed local
    functions of the links tied to the Wilson measure by the construction.
    The remark therefore concerns only what the displayed target certifies;
    it says nothing against the construction of the later sections.
  </remark>

  <subsection|The target is satisfied by a generalized free
  field><label|ex-target-free-witness>

  Remark<nbsp><reference|rem:ex-target-adequacy> supplies the lattice clauses
  of the target, with insertions that do not depend on the gauge
  configuration, once hierarchy data are given that satisfy the remaining
  requirements and the grade-zero homogeneity hypothesis of the remark. Such
  data need not come from a gauge theory.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|A generalized free-field solution of the
    target><label|prop:ex-target-free-witness>For every group <math|G> as in
    this section, the existence target, including the operator-product
    requirements <eqref|ex-ope-coefficient>\U<eqref|ex-ope-remainder> and the
    trace requirement <eqref|ex-trace-limit> with its profile exclusion, is
    satisfied by a regular lattice family whose insertions do not depend on
    <math|U> and by a hierarchy whose selected theory is the field system of
    Wick polynomials and derivatives of one generalized free scalar field.
    The continuum data depend on <math|G> only through
    <math|dim<rsub|\<bbb-R\>> \<frak-g\>> in the normalization of the
    grade-four field of the trace requirement.
  </proposition>

  The proposition has been verified by machine against the formal statement
  of the source, using that statement and none of the source's construction.
  The formal development and its review are documented in the accompanying
  blueprint. We give the construction here.

  <paragraph|The field.> Let <math|\<mu\>> be the measure on
  <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>> with density

  <\equation*>
    w<around|(|p|)>=<frac|<around|(|p<rsup|2>|)><rsup|2>|log<rsup|2><around|(|e+p<rsup|2>|)>>,<space|2em>p<rsup|2>=E<rsup|2>-<around|\||\<b-p\>|\|><rsup|2>,
  </equation*>

  on the closed forward cone <math|<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>> and zero outside
  it. It is invariant under the proper orthochronous Lorentz group and
  polynomially bounded. Let <math|\<phi\>> be the generalized free scalar
  field with two-point distribution <math|<big|int>e<rsup|i*<around|\<langle\>|y-x,p|\<rangle\>>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><around|(|p|)>>,
  where <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<cdummy\>,\<cdummy\>|\<rangle\>>> is the
  Euclidean pairing of <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>> and no normalizing factor is
  inserted. Relative to the invariant measure
  <math|d<rsup|3>*\<b-p\>/<around|(|2*<sqrt|s+<around|\||\<b-p\>|\|><rsup|2>>|)>>
  on the mass shell of mass squared <math|s>, its Källén\ULehmann measure is
  <math|s<rsup|2>*log<rsup|-2><around|(|e+s|)>*<space|0.17em>d*s> on
  <math|s\<geqslant\>0>: the mass spectrum is continuous. Write
  <math|\<xi\><rsub|\<alpha\>>=i<rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|t>>*\<partial\><rsup|\<alpha\>>*\<phi\>>
  for a multi-index <math|\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0><rsup|4>> with time
  component <math|\<alpha\><rsub|t>>; the factor <math|i> for each time
  derivative matches the Euclidean and relativistic conventions. The labels
  are the finite complex linear combinations of Wick monomials
  <math|:<space|-0.17em>\<xi\><rsub|\<alpha\><rsub|1>>*\<cdots\>*\<xi\><rsub|\<alpha\><rsub|k>><space|-0.17em>>:.
  A monomial has grade <math|<big|sum><rsub|j><around|(|4+<around|\||\<alpha\><rsub|j>|\|>|)>>,
  the empty monomial is the unit, and <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|d>> is the span of
  the monomials of grade at most <math|d>, which is finite-dimensional. The
  density <math|w> is strictly positive in the interior of the forward cone.
  Together with the linear independence of the polynomials in the momenta
  that distinct Wick monomials produce, this shows that every nonzero label
  acts nontrivially.

  <paragraph|The relativistic theory.> The Hilbert space is the closed span
  of the vectors obtained by applying field words to the vacuum; these
  vectors are written down explicitly as families of square-integrable
  functions of <math|k> momenta, <math|k\<geqslant\>0>. The properties of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|ex-reconstructed> are verified directly:
  linearity, the adjoint, continuity in the test, Poincaré covariance, the
  support of the spectral measure in <math|<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>>,
  uniqueness of the vacuum (in every sector with <math|k\<geqslant\>1> the
  total energy is positive almost everywhere; no mass gap is needed), and
  locality. Let

  <\equation*>
    S<around|(|\<xi\>|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|-<around|\||\<xi\>|\|>*E>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><around|(|p|)>,<space|2em>\<xi\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>\<setminus\><around|{|0|}>,
  </equation*>

  which for <math|\<xi\>=<around|(|\<b-u\>,\<tau\>|)>> with
  <math|\<tau\>\<gtr\>0> equals <math|<big|int>e<rsup|-\<tau\>*E+i*\<b-u\>\<cdot\>\<b-p\>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><around|(|p|)>>.
  At pairwise distinct insertion points, the selected correlations are sums
  over complete pairings of the individual factors
  <math|\<xi\><rsub|\<alpha\>>>, with no pair joining two factors of the same
  Wick monomial; each pair contributes the corresponding derivative of
  <math|S>, and the correlation vanishes if there is no such pairing. Their
  continuation to complex times and the boundary values
  <eqref|eq:public-selected-boundary> are obtained from the same momentum
  integrals.

  <paragraph|Operator products.> The expansion of a cluster of Wick monomials
  is given by Wick's theorem followed by Taylor expansion, about the base
  point, of the factors that are not contracted within the cluster. Each
  coefficient is a finite sum, over partial contractions and Taylor
  multi-indices, of products of derivatives of <math|S> at differences of
  cluster points with monomials in the displacements and inverse factorials.
  One family of coefficients serves every truncation degree. The Wick
  decomposition and the Taylor remainder, together with
  <math|<around|\||\<partial\><rsup|\<gamma\>>*S<around|(|\<xi\>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|\<gamma\>><around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|-8-<around|\||\<gamma\>|\|>>>,
  give the powers of <math|r> required in <eqref|ex-ope-coefficient> and
  <eqref|ex-ope-remainder>, at first with constants depending on the fields,
  the truncation degree and the geometry parameters. The separation of the
  spectators makes these estimates uniform in the allowed positions, and for
  fixed parameters the factor <math|r<rsup|-\<epsilon\>>> absorbs the
  constants.

  <paragraph|The trace requirement.> On the time axis, with
  <math|e<rsub|t>=<around|(|\<b-0\>,1|)>>,

  <\equation*>
    lim<rsub|r*\<downarrow\>*0> r<rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|r<rsup|-1>|)>*<space|0.17em>S*<around|(|r*e<rsub|t>|)>=384*\<pi\>.
  </equation*>

  Take <math|\<Theta\>=\<lambda\>*\<phi\>> with <math|\<lambda\>\<gtr\>0> and
  <math|\<lambda\><rsup|2>=dim<rsub|\<bbb-R\>>
  \<frak-g\>/<around|(|128*\<pi\><rsup|5>|)>>. Then <math|\<Theta\>> has
  grade four, its vacuum one-point functional vanishes, and
  <eqref|ex-trace-limit> holds with the required constant. The profile
  exclusion follows from this asymptotic alone, by
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:profile-exclusion>.

  <paragraph|The lattice clauses.> The correlations just described are
  multilinear in the fields, so the unit <math|u\<in\>\<cal-F\><rsub|0>>
  enters them homogeneously, and Remark<nbsp><reference|rem:ex-target-adequacy>
  applies: the tokens are the tags, the insertions are the constants
  <math|m<around|(|A|)>>, the phase space has two points, and two grade-zero
  channels are added to the operator-product data. This completes the data.
  The formal proof uses a variant of the remark in which <math|m> is the
  indicator of the zero vector of <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|0>> and one channel
  with zero field is added in each grade; it relies on the vanishing of the
  correlations when a field is zero.

  <paragraph|Why the target admits this.> Three features of the target, each
  already noted above, combine.

  <\enumerate>
    <item>The Wilson measure enters only through the mixed correlations, and
    these are tied to the selected theory only by the mixture identity and
    the common operator-product estimates. The target imposes no
    multilinearity on the phase and mixed correlation functions and no
    positivity or reconstruction at a non-selected phase.

    <item>No particular nonconstant gauge-invariant observable is prescribed
    for a field of the hierarchy. Beyond the lattice conditions, the group
    and its representation enter the requirements only through
    <math|dim<rsub|\<bbb-R\>> \<frak-g\>> in <eqref|ex-trace-limit>.

    <item>The profile exclusion constrains one two-point function. It
    excludes the specified finite sums of products of mass-shell kernels,
    while the continuous spectral weight chosen above gives exactly the
    required short-distance behaviour. The operator-product requirements are
    upper bounds, and they hold for this hierarchy of Wick polynomials. No
    clause imposes a condition on truncated correlations of higher order that
    would exclude a generalized free generating field.
  </enumerate>

  <paragraph|Relation to the review of the source.> The weakness of the
  target is not a new observation in its general form. The assessment of the
  source by its review group <cite-detail|YMG26|Sections<nbsp>7.3 and<nbsp>9>
  records that the authors of the source informed the group that the original
  public target \Pomitted some of the connections to the lattice construction
  established by its proof\Q, and it bases its assessment of these
  connections on a stronger combined target that retains the lattice
  observables, limits, phases and reconstructed fields of the construction
  itself. The same assessment notes that the trace requirement excludes the
  specified finite free-field descriptions \Pbut does not rule out every
  description in terms of composites of Gaussian fields\Q.
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-target-free-witness> and
  Remark<nbsp><reference|rem:ex-target-adequacy> make both remarks precise
  for the original target: the omitted connections can be dispensed with
  altogether, and the hierarchy of Wick polynomials and derivatives of one
  generalized free scalar field supplies the remaining continuum
  requirements. They give no information about the stronger combined target,
  which refers to the lattice family of the construction.

  <paragraph|What this does and does not say.> The proposition concerns the
  target as a specification. It shows that the target alone does not express
  the specifically Yang\UMills content of the construction: a proof of it
  need not involve Yang\UMills theory. It does not show any defect in the
  construction. The detailed theorem of the source concerns mixed
  correlations obtained as limits of Wilson expectations of a fixed family of
  local gauge-invariant insertions, a phase decomposition with a positive
  reconstruction at every phase, and a grade-four field identified with the
  centered trace of a stress tensor that satisfies translation and rotation
  Ward identities; none of this is touched by the proposition. Nor does the
  proposition bear on the question whether the theory so constructed could
  itself be a generalized free field. That question is not settled by the
  target, since the target is satisfied by one, and it is not addressed in
  this article.

  <section|Outline of the argument><label|ex-outline>

  The construction starts with finite-dimensional Wilson integrals and keeps
  one choice of lattice observables through renormalization, extraction and
  reconstruction. This section explains the route and the role of the main
  estimates. It is a guide to the proofs and their hypotheses, not a
  replacement for them.

  <subsection|The construction and its scope><label|ex-outline-status>

  The main statement is Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-construction-headline>.
  Its assembly in Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-construction-assembly>
  retains one lattice family, one refinement, one field quotient and one
  normalized stress tensor. The weaker public target in
  Section<nbsp><reference|ex-statement> is a consequence, but its abstract
  formulation alone does not identify the lattice representatives with
  curvature polynomials. That connection is supplied by the local sections
  and their reference-normalized transport. The final assembly identifies the
  actual suppliers of the construction premises, rather than inferring them
  from the existence of separate local lemmas.

  The result has no mass-gap or uniqueness-of-the-infinite-volume-limit
  assertion. Its trace law excludes a specified class of finite mass-shell
  profiles; it does not exclude every generalized free-field description. The
  scope paragraph following the headline compares these conclusions with the
  Yang\UMills existence and mass-gap problem.

  <subsection|Prerequisites><label|ex-outline-prerequisites>

  We assume finite-dimensional Lie groups, probability Haar measure on
  compact groups, and measure and integration, including Fubini\UTonelli, the
  <math|\<pi\>>\U<math|\<lambda\>> theorem and Bochner integration. We also
  use Cauchy's theorem and formula, Hahn\UBanach, elementary Hilbert-space
  theory, the Fourier transform on Schwartz space, and elementary Gaussian
  inequalities and Wick's rule. The specific uses of commutative
  <math|C<rsup|\<ast\>>>-algebras, Riesz representation, the spectral theorem
  for unitary representations, and the local analytic structure of matrix
  groups are cited where they enter. In the spectral arguments the dual of
  <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>> is identified with <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>> by
  <math|p\<mapsto\><around|(|x\<mapsto\>e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\>x>|)>>; Fourier
  normalizations are stated with the formulas that use them.

  <subsection|Context><label|ex-outline-context>

  The finite-volume measures are Wilson's lattice gauge theory
  <cite|Wilson74>. Reflection positivity of these measures and the resulting
  positive transfer matrix were established by Osterwalder and Seiler and by
  Lüscher <cite|OsterwalderSeiler|Luscher77>, and lattice gauge theories were
  analysed from the constructive point of view by Brydges, Fröhlich and
  Seiler and by Seiler <cite|BFS79|BFS80|BFS81|Seiler82>. The perturbative
  discovery of asymptotic freedom <cite|GrossWilczek73|Politzer73> motivates
  the logarithmic running law. The relation between the bare lattice coupling
  and continuum renormalization schemes was computed at one loop in
  <cite|HasenfratzHasenfratz80|DashenGross81> and at two loops in
  <cite|LuscherWeiszCoupling>. None of these results supplies the uniform
  lattice estimate proved here.

  The construction proceeds by block-spin renormalization for lattice gauge
  fields, an approach developed in Baªaban's programme. Its ingredients
  include regularity and decay of lattice Green's functions
  <cite|Balaban83Green>; propagators for the renormalization transformations,
  first for the free gauge field and then in a background field
  <cite|Balaban84Prop1|Balaban84Prop2|Balaban85Background>; averaging
  operations, spaces of regular configurations and gauge fixing
  <cite|Balaban85Averaging|Balaban85Spaces>; the variational problem for
  background fields <cite|Balaban85Variational>; ultraviolet stability in
  three dimensions <cite|Balaban85UV3>; effective actions, cluster expansions
  and coupling-constant renormalization in four dimensions
  <cite|Balaban87RG1|Balaban88RG2|Balaban88Convergent>; and the large-field
  renormalization operation <cite|Balaban89LF1|Balaban89LF2>. Dimock's
  expository series <cite|Dimock13I|Dimock13II|Dimock13III> presents the
  method in detail for a scalar model. Other constructive approaches to
  non-abelian gauge fields are Federbush's phase-cell programme
  <cite|Federbush86I|Federbush87III|Federbush88IV|Federbush90V|Federbush87VI>
  and the construction of Magnen, Rivasseau and Sénéor with an infrared
  cutoff <cite|MRS93>. Continuum limits are known for abelian lattice gauge
  theories in three and four dimensions <cite|Gross83|Driver87> and for the
  abelian Higgs model <cite|King86>, and the two-dimensional Yang\UMills
  measure has been constructed in the continuum
  <cite|Driver89|Sengupta97|Levy03>. These papers provide historical context;
  their construction theorems are not used as substitutes for the estimates
  below.

  For probabilistic background, see <cite|Chatterjee19|Chevyrev22>.
  Subsequent work constructs the Langevin dynamics for the two-dimensional
  Yang\UMills measure <cite|CCHS22>. In three dimensions, work constructs
  state spaces for distributional gauge orbits <cite|CaoChatterjee24> and a
  local Markov process on gauge orbits for the stochastic Yang\UMills\UHiggs
  flow <cite|CCHS24>. The existence and mass-gap problem is formulated in
  <cite|JaffeWitten06>.

  The passage to a relativistic theory follows the Osterwalder\USchrader
  strategy <cite|OS73|OS75>. The 1975 sequel corrects the original
  sufficiency argument and gives revised hypotheses. Glaser <cite|Glaser74>
  proves analytic continuation and a corresponding positivity property on the
  causal domain. Zinoviev <cite|Zinoviev95> proves an equivalence theorem
  with a further weak spectral condition. The Wightman framework and the
  reconstruction of a relativistic theory from Euclidean correlations are
  treated in <cite|StreaterWightman|GlimmJaffe87>. Here the continuation,
  boundary, locality and spectral arguments are carried out directly for the
  constructed correlations. The comparison with the corrected abstract
  theorem in Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-reflection> is not a premise of
  that proof.

  <subsection|The order of the limits><label|ex-outline-limits>

  The lattice spacings <math|a<rsub|N>=D<rsub|N><rsup|-1>>, physical periods
  <math|P<rsub|p>>, bare coefficients and graded insertions are chosen before
  the requested extraction order. For each pair of increasing index maps
  <math|<around|(|u,p|)>> one then chooses a refinement
  <math|<around|(|u\<circ\>v,p\<circ\>q|)>> and one continuum hierarchy. The
  Wilson limit uses the <em|full> mesh sequence at every suitable fixed
  period, followed by the selected period sequence <math|p\<circ\>q>. The
  refined mesh is used for auxiliary comparisons, not as a replacement for
  that full mesh limit. One refinement serves all arities, labels and tests.
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-full-mesh> identifies a subsequential row limit
  with an already established full row limit; it does not prove full mesh
  convergence from compactness.

  <subsection|The plan in ten steps><label|ex-outline-plan>

  <\enumerate>
    <item><em|Finite lattice and quadratic transformation.> Normalize the
    Wilson measure, establish gauge and reflection positivity, and calculate
    the free block transformation. Laurent-symbol estimates give
    exponentially decaying rows on every torus. The flat one-step estimate
    and its scalar recurrence compare different terminal cutoffs. Curvature
    factorization supplies the positive rows used by the quadratic horizon;
    its bounds retain the powers of the block side in the residue sums. See
    Sections<nbsp><reference|ex-lattice>\U<reference|sec:finite-menu-history-handoff>.

    <item><em|One nonlinear block step.> Defect geometry allocates action to
    large-field regions. On each sector, a positive precision and localized
    covariance control the Gaussian integral. Exact reassembly, a convergent
    cluster expansion and local jet extraction give the nonlinear step and
    its marked versions. These are identities and estimates for the same
    density, not separately chosen approximations. The principal
    constructions begin in Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:local-transfer>,
    <reference|sec:cluster-expansion> and<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-reassembly>.

    <item><em|Stationary analysis and the one-loop coefficient.> A uniform
    stationary chart and determinant comparison isolate the quadratic
    logarithmic term. The annulus estimate gives
    <math|\<lambda\><rsub|G>=11*C<rsub|G>/<around|(|24*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)>> in
    the color inner product fixed by the Wilson representation. It is the
    same constant as <math|2*b<rsub|0><around|(|G|)>> and the shooting slope
    <math|s<rsub|G>>; see <eqref|eq:shoot-slope> and
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:running-coefficient>.

    <item><em|One selected history and schedule.> The reset and shooting
    construction keeps the couplings within a sublinear error of a line in
    <math|log a<rsub|m><rsup|-1>>. Specialization to the finest coefficient
    gives the period-uniform running law. Numerical profiles and floors are
    chosen in the required order before the block lengths; costs evaluated
    after those choices are absorbed on the same schedule. The public family
    retains the resulting actual coefficients, levels, paths and insertion
    transport. See Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:surviving-interval-shooting>,
    <reference|sec:common-scale-selection>
    and<nbsp><reference|sec:public-run-binding>.

    <item><em|Centered estimates and ordered limits.> Reflection positivity
    turns finite-cell root bounds into estimates for separated correlations.
    A fixed intermediate scale controls the discarded higher marked terms;
    convergence of the retained data there yields the full mesh Cauchy
    estimate. Difference estimates give smoothness away from coincidences,
    and compactness gives one volume refinement for all tests. See
    Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:fullmesh-correlation-cauchy>,
    <reference|sec:fixed-rung-convergence>, <reference|ex-smooth-limits>
    and<nbsp><reference|ex-public-same-run-cauchy>. Their common-history
    premises are part of the final assembly check.

    <item><em|The Euclidean Hilbert space and phases.> Reflection positivity
    gives a contraction semigroup. Bounded slab insertions compressed to its
    ground space generate a commutative algebra. Its character space carries
    the vacuum's full-support probability measure. Evaluating whole words
    gives component correlations and the barycenter identity. Uniform slab
    bounds, derivatives and all-spectator null relations survive this
    construction. See Sections<nbsp><reference|ex-reflection>\U<reference|ex-phase-fields>.

    <item><em|Fields, stress and calibrated actions.> Finite probes and
    interacting/free comparisons identify field ranks and compatible grade
    inclusions. The resulting physical field space carries one conserved
    stress tensor with fixed Ward normalization, and one compatible rotation
    and derivative calculus. Its shell identities pass to every character
    before continuation. See Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:finite-rank-kernel-transport>,
    <reference|sec:actual-stress-normalization>,
    <reference|sec:physical-angular-actions>
    and<nbsp><reference|sec:phase-ward-inheritance>.

    <item><em|Relativistic reconstruction in every character.> Complex heat
    time gives ordered holomorphic correlations; common growth bounds give
    joint tempered boundary distributions. Lorentz covariance, locality, the
    word domain and the forward spectrum are obtained before vacuum
    uniqueness. The latter first holds almost everywhere, then for every
    character: locality and cone support give a uniform low-energy bound,
    hence a uniform decaying heat remainder; continuity in the character and
    full support remove the exceptional set.
    Theorems<nbsp><reference|thm:joint-boundary>,
    <reference|thm:field-spectrum> and<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-every-character-vacuum>
    give these steps. Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:assembly-boundary-barycenter>
    retains the mixture identity on arbitrary joint Schwartz tests.

    <item><em|The operator-product expansion.> One finite-dimensional
    prescription is matched with the local grouping of inserted fields.
    Uniform character estimates give both coefficient bounds and the
    remainder after truncation at any fixed degree. The coefficients are
    independent of the state and spectators, whereas the small-radius
    threshold may depend on the fields, geometry and truncation. See
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ope-quantitative> and the matching arguments
    in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:reference-match>.

    <item><em|The centered physical trace.> The same Ward-normalized tensor
    is centered at the selected character. Rounded lattice comparisons, the
    common chart estimates and the free contraction determine its
    short-distance amplitude. Positivity and monotonicity remove the
    rounding; the finite-profile argument then gives the stated exclusion.
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:trace-selected-endpoint> attaches this
    conclusion to the selected hierarchy without a new extraction or a new
    stress normalization.
  </enumerate>

  <subsection|Reading order and final interfaces><label|ex-outline-map><label|ex-outline-open>

  The exposition presents the analytic consequences before many of their
  construction estimates. Forward references to a later producer retain that
  producer's hypotheses; they do not identify two independently chosen
  families. The index in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:symbolic-index> records
  the principal symbols and normalization changes.

  The final assembly in Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-assembly> follows the
  order: selected finite family, full mesh and volume limits, common field
  quotients, all-character reconstruction and OPE, then the selected physical
  trace. The actual background estimates are supplied by the one-step and
  retained path construction of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:path-attachment-producer>.
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:fullmesh-selected-supplier> supplies the
  uniform all-period bounds for the calibrated insertions themselves. The
  final assembly verifies these applications on the same data; the reference
  map here is a guide to those proofs, rather than a substitute for them.

  <section|The Wilson lattice and the quadratic transformation>

  <subsection|Finite lattice gauge theory><label|sec:ex-lattice><label|ex-lattice>

  The starting point is a probability measure on finitely many group-valued
  links. Compactness makes this measure elementary to define; the difficulty
  of the continuum problem is to control its observables uniformly as the
  number of links grows. We first fix the action and its normalization.

  <subsubsection|The periodic lattice and its probability
  law><label|subsec:ex-lattice-finite>

  Fix a closed subgroup <math|G\<subset\>U<around|(|r<rsub|G>|)>> in a
  specified faithful unitary representation, with nonabelian simple real Lie
  algebra as in Section<nbsp><reference|ex-statement>. The group is compact.
  For a positive integer <math|\<ell\>>, let

  <\equation*>
    \<Lambda\><rsub|\<ell\>>=<around|(|\<bbb-Z\>/\<ell\>*\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|4>,<space|2em>E<rsub|\<ell\>>=\<Lambda\><rsub|\<ell\>>\<times\><around|{|0,1,2,3|}>.
  </equation*>

  A link configuration is a function <math|U:E<rsub|\<ell\>>\<to\>G>. The
  four coordinates are Euclidean; this finite model does not distinguish a
  time direction in its action. An oriented link in the negative direction is
  represented by the inverse of the corresponding positively oriented link.

  For <math|x\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|\<ell\>>> and distinct axes <math|i,j>,
  the positively oriented plaquette holonomy based at <math|x> is

  <\equation*>
    U<rsub|i*j><around|(|x|)>=U<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>*U<rsub|j>*<around|(|x+e<rsub|i>|)>*U<rsub|i>*<around|(|x+e<rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1>*U<rsub|j><around|(|x|)><rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  Count each pair <math|i\<less\>j> once. The action is

  <\equation*>
    S<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|x\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|\<ell\>>><big|sum><rsub|0\<leqslant\>i\<less\>j\<less\>4><around*|(|1-<frac|<math-up|Retr><space|0.17em>U<rsub|i*j><around|(|x|)>|r<rsub|G>>|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-action>
  </equation*>

  Each summand is real and nonnegative: a unitary matrix has each diagonal
  entry of modulus at most one, so <math|<math-up|Retr><space|0.17em>V\<leqslant\>r<rsub|G>>.
  The convention has no extra factor of <math|1/2> over faces. This action is
  dimensionless. The bare coefficient <math|\<beta\>> multiplies it in the
  Boltzmann weight; it is not the blocking-kernel penalty used later in the
  RG construction.

  Let <math|H<rsub|G>> be probability Haar measure on <math|G>, and let

  <\equation*>
    \<rho\><rsub|\<ell\>>=<big|otimes><rsub|<around|(|x,i|)>\<in\>E<rsub|\<ell\>>>H<rsub|G>,<space|1em>Z<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<beta\>|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|-\<beta\>*S<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|U|)>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<rho\><rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|U|)>,<space|1em>d*\<mu\><rsub|\<ell\>,\<beta\>><around|(|U|)>=Z<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<beta\>|)><rsup|-1>*e<rsup|-\<beta\>*S<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|U|)>>*d*\<rho\><rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  The partition function is finite and strictly positive. Indeed the link
  space is a finite product of compact groups, hence compact, and
  <math|S<rsub|\<ell\>>> is continuous; therefore
  <math|e<rsup|-\<beta\>*S<rsub|\<ell\>>>> is bounded and integrable. It is
  strictly positive at every configuration, so its integral against the
  probability measure <math|\<rho\><rsub|\<ell\>>> is positive. Integrating
  the displayed density gives <math|Z<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<beta\>|)>/Z<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<beta\>|)>=1>,
  so <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<ell\>,\<beta\>>> is a probability measure. This
  argument works for every real <math|\<beta\>> for which the displayed
  definition is used; the construction theorem later selects its own
  coefficient sequence.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Gauge invariance of the finite action and
    law><label|prop:ex-lattice-gauge>For any map
    <math|g:\<Lambda\><rsub|\<ell\>>\<to\>G>, transform links by

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|g\<cdot\>U|)><rsub|i><around|(|x|)>=g<around|(|x|)>*U<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>*g*<around|(|x+e<rsub|i>|)><rsup|-1>.
    </equation*>

    Then <math|S<rsub|\<ell\>>*<around|(|g\<cdot\>U|)>=S<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|U|)>>
    and <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<ell\>,\<beta\>>> is invariant under
    <math|U\<mapsto\>g\<cdot\>U>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Substitution into the four factors of <math|U<rsub|i*j><around|(|x|)>>
    cancels the group elements at the three intermediate vertices. The
    remaining factors give

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|g\<cdot\>U|)><rsub|i*j><around|(|x|)>=g<around|(|x|)>*U<rsub|i*j><around|(|x|)>*g<around|(|x|)><rsup|-1>.
    </equation*>

    Trace is invariant under conjugation, since for matrices <math|A,B> of
    matching finite size <math|<math-up|tr><around|(|A*B|)>=<math-up|tr><around|(|B*A|)>>;
    hence every plaquette summand and their finite sum are unchanged.

    For each link <math|<around|(|x,i|)>>, the transformed value is obtained
    from <math|U<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>> by left and right multiplication by
    fixed group elements. Left and right translations preserve Haar measure.
    The full transformation acts separately on the coordinates of the finite
    product <math|E<rsub|\<ell\>>>, so the product Haar measure
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|\<ell\>>> is preserved. Since the density
    <math|Z<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<beta\>|)><rsup|-1>*e<rsup|-\<beta\>*S<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|U|)>>>
    is invariant, the weighted measure is preserved as well. This proves
    invariance of the law, not gauge invariance of every observable. An
    insertion must separately satisfy the relevant invariance condition.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Reflection positivity on an even
  torus><label|subsec:ex-lattice-finite-reflection>

  Osterwalder and Seiler established physical positivity for Euclidean
  lattice gauge theories <cite|OsterwalderSeiler>. We give the finite-torus
  argument for both reflection cuts and for the specified unitary
  representation of the compact gauge group.

  We next prove the finite-volume positivity needed for the reflection
  construction. Fix an axis as the time coordinate, let <math|\<ell\>=2*h>
  with <math|h\<geqslant\>1>, and take <math|\<beta\>\<geqslant\>0>. There
  are two cuts: the site reflection <math|t\<mapsto\>-t>, whose strict
  positive sector is <math|1\<leqslant\>t\<less\>h>, and the link reflection
  <math|t\<mapsto\>-1-t>, whose positive sector is
  <math|0\<leqslant\>t\<less\>h>; both coordinates are taken modulo
  <math|2*h>. Reflections based at other time coordinates are obtained by
  translation. The reflected configuration pulls back links along the
  reflected oriented edges; a reflected negative edge is read as the inverse
  of its positive representative. Write <math|\<Theta\>*U> for this
  configuration.

  These reflections preserve the law. Fix an axis <math|a>, an integer
  <math|c> and <math|\<kappa\>\<in\><around|{|0,1|}>>, and let
  <math|<math-ss|r>> be the involution of <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|\<ell\>>>
  given by <math|<around|(|<math-ss|r>x|)><rsub|a>=2*c-\<kappa\>-x<rsub|a>>
  and <math|<around|(|<math-ss|r>x|)><rsub|i>=x<rsub|i>> for <math|i\<ne\>a>,
  coordinates modulo <math|\<ell\>>. Thus <math|\<kappa\>=0> is a site
  reflection and <math|\<kappa\>=1> a link reflection; with
  <math|\<ell\>=2*h> and <math|c=0> they are the two cuts above, and other
  <math|c> give their translates. The edge from <math|x> to
  <math|x+e<rsub|i>> is carried to the edge from <math|<math-ss|r>x> to
  <math|<math-ss|r>x+e<rsub|i>> if <math|i\<ne\>a>, and to the negatively
  oriented edge from <math|<math-ss|r>x> to <math|<math-ss|r>x-e<rsub|a>> if
  <math|i=a>. The pulled-back configuration is therefore

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-reflection-pullback><around|(|\<Theta\>*U|)><rsub|i><around|(|x|)>=U<rsub|i><around|(|<math-ss|r>x|)>*<space|1em><around|(|i\<ne\>a|)>,<space|2em><around|(|\<Theta\>*U|)><rsub|a><around|(|x|)>=U<rsub|a>*<around|(|<math-ss|r>x-e<rsub|a>|)><rsup|-1>.
  </equation>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Reflection invariance of the finite
    law><label|prop:ex-lattice-reflection-invariance>For every positive
    integer <math|\<ell\>>, axis <math|a>, integer <math|c> and
    <math|\<kappa\>\<in\><around|{|0,1|}>>, the map <math|\<Theta\>> of
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-reflection-pullback> is a measurable involution of
    <math|G<rsup|E<rsub|\<ell\>>>>. It satisfies
    <math|S<rsub|\<ell\>>*<around|(|\<Theta\>*U|)>=S<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|U|)>>,
    and both the product Haar measure <math|\<rho\><rsub|\<ell\>>> and the
    law <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<ell\>,\<beta\>>>, <math|\<beta\>\<geqslant\>0>,
    are invariant under <math|\<Theta\>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Since <math|<around|(|<math-ss|r><around|(|<math-ss|r>x-e<rsub|a>|)>|)><rsub|a>=x<rsub|a>+1>,
    applying <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-reflection-pullback> twice gives
    <math|<around|(|\<Theta\><rsup|2>*U|)><rsub|a><around|(|x|)>=U<rsub|a><around|(|x|)>>,
    and clearly <math|<around|(|\<Theta\><rsup|2>*U|)><rsub|i>=U<rsub|i>> for
    <math|i\<ne\>a>. Hence <math|\<Theta\>> is an involution. It acts on the
    coordinates of the product <math|G<rsup|E<rsub|\<ell\>>>> by the
    bijections <math|x\<mapsto\><math-ss|r>x> (for <math|i\<ne\>a>) and
    <math|x\<mapsto\><math-ss|r>x-e<rsub|a>> (for <math|i=a>) of
    <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|\<ell\>>>, composed with inversion in the
    <math|a>-coordinates. Haar measure on the compact group <math|G> is
    invariant under inversion, and a product of identical probability
    measures is invariant under a permutation of its factors. Therefore
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|\<ell\>>> is <math|\<Theta\>>-invariant.

    Now compare plaquettes. If <math|a\<nin\><around|{|i,j|}>>, then
    substitution gives <math|<around|(|\<Theta\>*U|)><rsub|i*j><around|(|x|)>=U<rsub|i*j><around|(|<math-ss|r>x|)>>.
    Let <math|i\<less\>j> with <math|i=a>, and put
    <math|z=<math-ss|r>x-e<rsub|a>>. Since
    <math|<math-ss|r><around|(|x+e<rsub|a>|)>=z> and
    <math|<math-ss|r><around|(|x+e<rsub|j>|)>=<math-ss|r>x+e<rsub|j>>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|\<Theta\>*U|)><rsub|a*j><around|(|x|)>=U<rsub|a><around|(|z|)><rsup|-1>*U<rsub|j><around|(|z|)>*U<rsub|a>*<around|(|z+e<rsub|j>|)>*U<rsub|j>*<around|(|z+e<rsub|a>|)><rsup|-1>=U<rsub|a><around|(|z|)><rsup|-1>*<space|0.17em>U<rsub|a*j><around|(|z|)><rsup|-1>*<space|0.17em>U<rsub|a><around|(|z|)>.
    </equation*>

    Similarly, if <math|j=a> and <math|z=<math-ss|r>x-e<rsub|a>>, then

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|\<Theta\>*U|)><rsub|i*a><around|(|x|)>=U<rsub|i>*<around|(|z+e<rsub|a>|)>*U<rsub|a>*<around|(|z+e<rsub|i>|)><rsup|-1>*U<rsub|i><around|(|z|)><rsup|-1>*U<rsub|a><around|(|z|)>=U<rsub|a><around|(|z|)><rsup|-1>*<space|0.17em>U<rsub|i*a><around|(|z|)><rsup|-1>*<space|0.17em>U<rsub|a><around|(|z|)>.
    </equation*>

    Trace is invariant under conjugation. For
    <math|V\<in\>U<around|(|r<rsub|G>|)>>,
    <math|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>V<rsup|-1>=<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>V<rsup|\<ast\>>=<wide|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>V|\<bar\>>>,
    so <math|<math-up|Retr><space|0.17em>V<rsup|-1>=<math-up|Retr><space|0.17em>V>.
    Hence the summand of <math|S<rsub|\<ell\>>*<around|(|\<Theta\>*U|)>>
    indexed by <math|<around|(|x,i,j|)>> equals the summand of
    <math|S<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|U|)>> indexed by
    <math|<around|(|<math-ss|r>x,i,j|)>> or by
    <math|<around|(|<math-ss|r>x-e<rsub|a>,i,j|)>>. For each fixed pair
    <math|i\<less\>j> these are bijections of
    <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|\<ell\>>>, so <math|S<rsub|\<ell\>>*<around|(|\<Theta\>*U|)>=S<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|U|)>>.

    Finally, for every bounded Borel <math|F>, invariance of
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|\<ell\>>> and of the density gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|int>F\<circ\>\<Theta\>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|\<ell\>,\<beta\>>=<frac|1|Z<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<beta\>|)>>*<big|int>F*<around|(|\<Theta\>*U|)>*e<rsup|-\<beta\>*S<rsub|\<ell\>>*<around|(|\<Theta\>*U|)>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<rho\><rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|U|)>=<big|int>F*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|\<ell\>,\<beta\>>.
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  This supplies the reflection-invariance hypothesis of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-centered-planted> for the Wilson law.
  Its seam reflection <math|R<rsub|p,a>> is the case
  <math|c=b<rsub|a>+\<ell\>*p<rsub|a>> and <math|\<kappa\>=\<delta\>>, on the
  torus of side <math|2*h*\<ell\>>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Finite-volume reflection positivity><label|prop:ex-lattice-finite-reflection>For
    either cut, let <math|F> be a bounded Borel, gauge-invariant function of
    the link configuration which depends only on links with both endpoints in
    the strict positive sector. Then

    <\equation*>
      <big|int>F<around|(|U|)><space|0.17em><wide|F*<around|(|\<Theta\>*U|)>|\<bar\>><space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|2*h,\<beta\>><around|(|U|)>\<geqslant\>0.
    </equation*>

    The inequality is real and the same statement holds at every translated
    cut and for every choice of time axis. It applies at each finite volume;
    no continuum limit is asserted.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    We first fix the transfer-kernel normalization. Let <math|X> and <math|Y>
    be the spatial link configurations on two adjacent time slices, let
    <math|u<rsub|v>> be the intervening time link at spatial vertex <math|v>,
    and let <math|e=<around|(|v,v+e<rsub|j>|)>> run over the positive spatial
    links in one slice. The temporal plaquette has holonomy
    <math|X<rsub|e>*u<rsub|v+e<rsub|j>>*Y<rsub|e><rsup|-1>*u<rsub|v><rsup|-1>>.
    Put

    <\equation*>
      H*<around|(|X,Y|)>=<big|int>exp <space|-0.17em><around*|[|-\<beta\>*<big|sum><rsub|e><around*|(|1-<frac|<math-up|Retr><around|(|X<rsub|e>*u<rsub|v+e<rsub|j>>*Y<rsub|e><rsup|-1>*u<rsub|v><rsup|-1>|)>|r<rsub|G>>|)>|]>*<big|prod><rsub|v>d*H<rsub|G><around|(|u<rsub|v>|)>.
    </equation*>

    This is the single common integration over the family of time links, not
    an independent gauge average for each spatial edge. We claim that
    <math|H> is a positive-semidefinite kernel. Define the spatial gauge
    action <math|<around|(|u\<cdot\>Y|)><rsub|e>=u<rsub|v>*Y<rsub|e>*u<rsub|v+e<rsub|j>><rsup|-1>>
    and the real inner product <math|<around|\<langle\>|X,Y|\<rangle\>>=<big|sum><rsub|e><math-up|Retr><around|(|X<rsub|e>*Y<rsub|e><rsup|\<ast\>>|)>>.
    For <math|c=\<beta\>/r<rsub|G>\<geqslant\>0>, set

    <\equation*>
      H<rsub|0>*<around|(|X,Y|)>=e<rsup|-\<beta\><around|\||E<rsub|<math-up|sp>>|\|>>*exp
      <around|(|c*<around|\<langle\>|X,Y|\<rangle\>>|)>.
    </equation*>

    For any finitely many <math|X<rsup|a>> and complex coefficients
    <math|z<rsub|a>>, <math|<big|sum><rsub|a,b><wide|z<rsub|a>|\<bar\>>z<rsub|b>*<around|\<langle\>|X<rsup|a>,X<rsup|b>|\<rangle\>><rsup|m>\<geqslant\>0>:
    view the matrix entries as real coordinates and take the squared norm of
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>z<rsub|a><around|(|X<rsup|a>|)><rsup|\<otimes\>m>>
    in the complexified real tensor space. The exponential series, with
    nonnegative coefficients, therefore makes <math|H<rsub|0>> positive
    semidefinite. It is invariant under a simultaneous spatial gauge
    transformation of both arguments. Product Haar invariance gives

    <\equation*>
      H*<around|(|X,Y|)>=<big|int>H<rsub|0>*<around|(|X,u\<cdot\>Y|)>*<space|0.17em>d*u=<big|int><space|-0.17em><space|-0.17em><big|int>H<rsub|0>*<around|(|v\<cdot\>X,u\<cdot\>Y|)>*<space|0.17em>d*u*<space|0.17em>d*v.
    </equation*>

    More explicitly, if <math|M<rsub|m><around|(|X|)>=<big|int><around|(|u\<cdot\>X|)><rsup|\<otimes\>m>*<space|0.17em>d*u>,
    then

    <\equation*>
      H*<around|(|X,Y|)>=e<rsup|-\<beta\><around|\||E<rsub|<math-up|sp>>|\|>>*<big|sum><rsub|m\<geqslant\>0><frac|c<rsup|m>|m!>*<around|\<langle\>|M<rsub|m><around|(|X|)>,M<rsub|m><around|(|Y|)>|\<rangle\>>.
    </equation*>

    Thus <math|H> is real, symmetric, and positive semidefinite. The series
    converges uniformly because the spatial-link space is compact. Products
    of such kernels are positive semidefinite by taking tensor products of
    their feature spaces; the same remains true when the two boundary slice
    variables coincide.

    For a link cut, partition the temporal links into those wholly in the
    positive half, those in its reflected half, and the two sets of crossing
    links. The spatial links are partitioned between the two halves. Every
    spatial plaquette belongs to one half, every non-crossing temporal
    plaquette belongs to one half, and the remaining temporal plaquettes
    occur at the two cut boundaries. Reflection identifies the two half
    actions. After integrating the crossing links, the unnormalized reflected
    expectation is a double integral over the two open half-configurations,
    with integrand

    <\equation*>
      \<Phi\><around|(|x|)><space|0.17em><wide|\<Phi\><around|(|y|)>|\<bar\>><space|0.17em>H*<around|(|X<rsub|1><around|(|x|)>,X<rsub|1><around|(|y|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>H*<around|(|X<rsub|2><around|(|x|)>,X<rsub|2><around|(|y|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|\<Phi\>> is <math|F> times the positive-half Boltzmann factor,
    and <math|X<rsub|1>,X<rsub|2>> are its two boundary spatial slices.
    Insert the two uniformly convergent feature expansions. Fubini turns each
    term into the squared norm of the integral of <math|\<Phi\>> against the
    corresponding tensor feature, with a nonnegative coefficient. Boundedness
    of <math|F> and compactness justify the interchange of integrals and
    sums. The integral is therefore nonnegative. This also covers <math|h=1>,
    when the two boundary slice variables within one half are the same.

    For a site cut, condition instead on the spatial links in the two fixed
    reflection planes. The remaining links occur in reflected pairs, and the
    plaquette action splits into the action in one half, its reflection, and
    the spatial plaquettes contained in the fixed planes. Since <math|F>
    reads only links with both endpoints in the strict positive sector, the
    two half integrations are conjugates. Their product is an absolute
    square, multiplied by the positive Boltzmann weight of the fixed-plane
    plaquettes. The same factorization works when the strict sector is empty.
    Product Haar invariance supplies the reflected change of variables,
    including inversion of reversed links. All integrands are bounded and
    measurable on a compact finite product, so Fubini's theorem applies.
    Finally <math|Z<rsub|2*h><around|(|\<beta\>|)>\<gtr\>0>; division by it
    preserves nonnegativity. Translation of the cut and permutation of axes
    preserve the product prior and plaquette action, proving the remaining
    cases.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Scales, observables, and the ordered
  limit><label|subsec:ex-lattice-scales>

  For mesh index <math|N> and physical-period index <math|p>, the family uses
  strictly increasing positive-integer sequences <math|D<rsub|N>> and
  <math|P<rsub|p>>. Set

  <\equation*>
    a<rsub|N>=D<rsub|N><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>\<ell\><rsub|N,p>=P<rsub|p>*D<rsub|N>=P<rsub|p>/a<rsub|N>,<space|2em>\<Lambda\><rsub|N,p>=<around|(|\<bbb-Z\>/\<ell\><rsub|N,p>*\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|4>.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|\<ell\><rsub|N,p>> is a site count per axis, while
  <math|P<rsub|p>> is the physical linear period. In the selected
  construction, every <math|P<rsub|p>> is divisible by four and every
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>> is positive. Consequently
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-finite-reflection> applies on
  every one of its finite <math|<around|(|N,p|)>> grids. These extra
  properties are of the constructed schedule and coupling; strict increase
  alone in the abstract family definition does not impose either one. A site
  is embedded in Euclidean spacetime by multiplying the symmetric
  least-absolute residue in each coordinate by <math|a<rsub|N>>. A token
  <math|v> specifies an insertion <math|O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v,z;U|)>>. We
  require the insertion to be continuous and gauge invariant, with the
  fixed-stencil locality specified in Section<nbsp><reference|ex-statement>.

  The finite <math|m>-point moment is the integral of the product of its
  <math|m> insertions under <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<ell\><rsub|N,p>,\<beta\><rsub|N,p>>>.
  Smearing uses a lattice sum weighted by <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|4*m>>.
  Continuity and compactness of the finite configuration space give
  integrability of each finite product. The public convergence interface
  first demands, at each fixed suitable physical volume, a full
  <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>> mesh limit of these smeared moments, and only then
  a volume limit along a specified strictly increasing subsequence. It is not
  an arbitrary joint or diagonal limit. The support of a test function must
  eventually fit inside the period box and its arguments must remain
  separated as specified in Section<nbsp><reference|ex-statement>.

  The next task is to control these moments as the number of links tends to
  infinity. The required estimates must concern the same bare coefficients
  and local insertions at all cutoffs and periods. The extraction argument in
  the next section isolates a local modulus of continuity and a local size
  bound; the renormalization construction must supply those estimates with
  the indicated order of quantifiers.

  <\corollary>
    <dueto|Finite smeared reflection forms on the selected
    grids><label|cor:ex-lattice-smeared-reflection>Fix finitely many
    positive-time words, of lengths <math|n<rsub|\<nu\>>\<geqslant\>0>, with
    continuous gauge-invariant insertions and compactly supported test
    functions <math|f<rsub|\<nu\>>>. Suppose every time coordinate in their
    supports lies in <math|<around|[|\<varepsilon\>,R|]>>, where
    <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, and every spatial coordinate lies in
    <math|<around|[|-R,R|]>>. Suppose the insertions in these words read only
    links whose endpoints are within <math|b> lattice steps of their
    insertion site, for one fixed <math|b\<less\>\<infty\>>. For a fixed
    selected physical period <math|P<rsub|p>\<gtr\>2*R>, define

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|\<nu\>,N,p><around|(|U|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n<rsub|\<nu\>>>*<big|sum><rsub|z<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,z<rsub|n<rsub|\<nu\>>>\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|N,p>>f<rsub|\<nu\>>*<around|(|a<rsub|N>*\<ell\><around|(|z<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,a<rsub|N>*\<ell\><around|(|z<rsub|n<rsub|\<nu\>>>|)>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|j=1><rsup|n<rsub|\<nu\>>>O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v<rsub|\<nu\>,j>,z<rsub|j>;U|)>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|\<ell\>> is the centered lift on this torus; a word of length
    zero is its constant coefficient. For all sufficiently large <math|N>,
    the matrix

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|<big|int>A<rsub|\<nu\>,N,p><around|(|U|)><wide|A<rsub|\<mu\>,N,p>*<around|(|\<Theta\>*U|)>|\<bar\>><space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|\<ell\><rsub|N,p>,\<beta\><rsub|N,p>><around|(|U|)>|)><rsub|\<nu\>,\<mu\>>
    </equation*>

    is positive semidefinite. The same mesh threshold works for every
    selected period <math|P<rsub|p>\<geqslant\>P<rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>2*R>.
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    Choose <math|N<rsub|0>> so that for <math|N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>>,

    <\equation*>
      b*a<rsub|N>\<less\><tfrac|1|2>min <around|{|\<varepsilon\>,P<rsub|\<ast\>>/2-R|}>.
    </equation*>

    Such a threshold exists because the strictly increasing integer
    denominators <math|D<rsub|N>> tend to infinity. A nonzero summand has
    every insertion time between <math|\<varepsilon\>> and <math|R>. Every
    endpoint read by its insertions therefore has physical time strictly
    between <math|0> and <math|P<rsub|p>/2>. In lattice coordinates this is
    exactly the strict positive sector of the site reflection on the even
    torus of side <math|P<rsub|p>*D<rsub|N>>. The same margin keeps all
    stencil endpoints away from the boundary of the centered coordinate box.
    Thus each <math|A<rsub|\<nu\>,N,p>> depends only on links with both
    endpoints in that sector. It is bounded and Borel: it is a finite sum of
    products of continuous functions on the compact configuration space. It
    is gauge invariant for the same reason, using the gauge invariance of
    each insertion. These properties hold also for any finite linear
    combination <math|F=<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>c<rsub|\<nu\>>*A<rsub|\<nu\>,N,p>>.
    Apply Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-finite-reflection> to
    <math|F> and expand the finite sum to obtain the stated positive form.
    The bounds used only <math|P<rsub|p>\<geqslant\>P<rsub|\<ast\>>>, which
    proves the uniformity assertion.

    Expanding an entry of the matrix gives the product of the two smearing
    weights, namely <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|4*<around|(|n<rsub|\<nu\>>+n<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>>>,
    and the corresponding finite Wilson moment under its normalized law. If
    the reflected insertion is written using its reflected label and
    reflected site, its exact covariance identity identifies this with the
    usual reflected smeared moment; there is no extra volume or
    partition-function factor. The corollary provides its finite positivity.
    Passing that identity through an ordered mesh and volume limit uses the
    convergence and reflected-label matching of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-limits-euclidean>; those are separate
    from the support-margin argument proved here.
  </proof>

  <subsection|From strip receipts to an order-two
  atlas><label|sec:ex-lattice-atlas-estimates>

  The local flat-sector estimate requires an order-two atlas for the two
  response kernels. This section proves the quantitative extraction of that
  atlas from the exponentially localized bed kernels and the supplied tower
  cross-kernel. It isolates what the construction has to provide: the strip
  receipt for the tower kernel and its first differences. The
  receipt-to-atlas argument itself is proved here.

  <subsubsection|Cover and installed kernels>

  Write <math|\<cal-C\>=\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>> with
  <math|<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>=<big|sum><rsub|a=1><rsup|4><around|\||x<rsub|a>|\|>>.
  For an odd positive integer <math|L>, let
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|L>=<around|(|\<bbb-Z\>/L*\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|4>>, let
  <math|\<pi\><rsub|L>> be reduction modulo <math|L>, and let
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|L>> denote the centered representative. There is a
  unique <math|\<rho\><rsub|L><around|(|x|)>\<in\>\<cal-C\>> such that

  <\equation*>
    x=\<ell\><rsub|L>*<around|(|\<pi\><rsub|L>*x|)>+L*\<rho\><rsub|L><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|x,y\<in\>\<cal-C\>>, put

  <\equation*>
    d<rsub|L>*<around|(|x,y|)>=L<rsup|-1>*<around|\||x-L*y|\|><rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  The centered representative satisfies <math|<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|r|)>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*L>,
  hence, for every <math|x,y\<in\>\<cal-C\>>,

  <\equation>
    d<rsub|L>*<around|(|x,y|)>\<leqslant\><around|\||y-\<rho\><rsub|L><around|(|x|)>|\|><rsub|1>+2\<leqslant\><around|\||y-\<rho\><rsub|L><around|(|x|)>|\|><rsub|1>+4.<label|eq:ex-lattice-atlas-mixed-distance>
  </equation>

  We retain the harmless margin <math|4> used in the finite-scale estimates.

  For finite type sets <math|F,G>, a cross-stencil is a family
  <math|T*<around|(|r,i,j,z|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> indexed by
  <math|r\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|L>>, <math|i\<in\>F>, <math|j\<in\>G>, and
  <math|z\<in\>\<cal-C\>>. Its width-<math|\<alpha\>> strip cost is the
  per-label bound

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|z\<in\>\<cal-C\>>e<rsup|\<alpha\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>*<around|\||T*<around|(|r,i,j,z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C.
  </equation*>

  For each coordinate direction <math|a>, define its residue difference by

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|\<nabla\><rsub|a><rsup|L>*T|)><around|(|r,i,j,z|)>=T*<around|(|r+\<pi\><rsub|L><around|(|e<rsub|a>|)>,i,j,z-\<epsilon\><rsub|a><around|(|r|)>|)>-T*<around|(|r,i,j,z|)>,<space|2em>\<epsilon\><rsub|a><around|(|r|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|e<rsub|a>,>|<cell|2*\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|r|)><rsub|a>=L-1,>>|<row|<cell|0,>|<cell|<text|otherwise.>>>>>>
  </equation*>

  Here <math|L> is odd, so the boundary condition in the displayed case is
  equivalently <math|\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|r|)><rsub|a>=<around|\<lfloor\>|L/2|\<rfloor\>>>;
  this is the centered-lift carry convention. The installed cover kernel is

  <\equation>
    <wide|T|^>*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|y,j|)>|)>=T*<around|(|\<pi\><rsub|L>*x,i,j,y-\<rho\><rsub|L>*x|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-installed-cross>
  </equation>

  Its forward difference is

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*<wide|T|^>|)><around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|y,j|)>|)>=<wide|T|^>*<around|(|<around|(|x+e<rsub|a>,i|)>,<around|(|y,j|)>|)>-<wide|T|^>*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|y,j|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The centered-lift identity gives the exact relation

  <\equation>
    \<partial\><rsub|a>*<wide|T|^>=<wide|\<nabla\><rsub|a><rsup|L>*T|^>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-install-difference>
  </equation>

  Indeed, if <math|x=\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|r|)>+L*v>, then
  <math|x+e<rsub|a>=\<ell\><rsub|L>*<around|(|r+\<pi\><rsub|L><around|(|e<rsub|a>|)>|)>+L*<around|(|v+\<epsilon\><rsub|a><around|(|r|)>|)>>;
  substituting this identity in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-installed-cross>
  produces the displayed difference formula.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Installation of strip receipts><label|lem:ex-lattice-cross-atlas>Suppose
    <math|0\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>\<leqslant\>\<alpha\>> and
    <math|C\<geqslant\>0>. If <math|T> has strip cost <math|C/L<rsup|2>> at
    width <math|\<alpha\>> and every <math|\<nabla\><rsub|a><rsup|L>*T> has
    strip cost <math|C/L<rsup|3>> at that width, then for every fixed pair of
    types <math|i,j>,

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|y>e<rsup|\<sigma\>*d<rsub|L>*<around|(|x,y|)>>*<around|\||<wide|T|^>*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|y,j|)>|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>e<rsup|4*\<sigma\>>*C*L<rsup|-2>,>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|x>e<rsup|\<sigma\>*d<rsub|L>*<around|(|x,y|)>>*<around|\||<wide|T|^>*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|y,j|)>|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>e<rsup|4*\<sigma\>>*C*L<rsup|2>,>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|y>e<rsup|\<sigma\>*d<rsub|L>*<around|(|x,y|)>>*<around|\||\<partial\><rsub|a>*<wide|T|^>*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|y,j|)>|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>e<rsup|4*\<sigma\>>*C*L<rsup|-3>.>>>>
    </align*>

    Thus <math|<wide|T|^>> has the order-two atlas bounds with cost
    <math|e<rsup|4*\<sigma\>>*C>: row <math|L<rsup|-2>>, column
    <math|L<rsup|2>>, and first-difference row <math|L<rsup|-3>>. These are
    per-label bounds; summing over a finite type set adds its cardinality
    explicitly.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Fix <math|x> and put <math|r=\<pi\><rsub|L>*x>,
    <math|v=\<rho\><rsub|L>*x>. In the row sum, the substitution <math|z=y-v>
    is a bijection of <math|\<cal-C\>>. By
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-atlas-mixed-distance>,

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|y>e<rsup|\<sigma\>*d<rsub|L>*<around|(|x,y|)>>*<around|\||<wide|T|^>*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|y,j|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|2*\<sigma\>>*<big|sum><rsub|z>e<rsup|\<sigma\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>*<around|\||T*<around|(|r,i,j,z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|2*\<sigma\>>*C*L<rsup|-2>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|4*\<sigma\>>*C*L<rsup|-2>.
    </equation*>

    The last inequality uses <math|\<sigma\>\<geqslant\>0>.

    For the column, decompose every <math|x> uniquely as
    <math|x=\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|r|)>+L*v>, where
    <math|<around|(|r,v|)>\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|L>\<times\>\<cal-C\>>. At
    fixed <math|r>, the installed coefficient is
    <math|T*<around|(|r,i,j,y-v|)>> and

    <\equation*>
      d<rsub|L>*<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|r|)>+L*v,y|)>\<leqslant\><around|\||v-y|\|><rsub|1>+2,
    </equation*>

    again because <math|<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|r|)>|\|><rsub|1>/L\<leqslant\>2>.
    Thus the sum over <math|v> is at most
    <math|e<rsup|2*\<sigma\>>*C*L<rsup|-2>> for each <math|r>. There are
    <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|L>|\|>=L<rsup|4>> residues, so the
    column sum is at most <math|e<rsup|2*\<sigma\>>*C*L<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|4*\<sigma\>>*C*L<rsup|2>>.
    Finally apply the row argument to each
    <math|\<nabla\><rsub|a><rsup|L>*T>, use
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-install-difference>, and replace the cost
    <math|C/L<rsup|2>> by <math|C/L<rsup|3>>.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Composition with translation-invariant bed kernels>

  A translation-invariant row stencil <math|I> on a finite type set <math|F>
  has coefficients <math|I*<around|(|i,b,u|)>> for <math|u\<in\>\<cal-C\>>.
  Its strip cost <math|C<rsub|I>> at width <math|\<alpha\>> means

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|u\<in\>\<cal-C\>>e<rsup|\<alpha\><around|\||u|\|><rsub|1>>*<around|\||I*<around|(|i,b,u|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|I><space|1em><text|for
    every >i,b\<in\>F.
  </equation*>

  It acts on the cover by convolution. Let <math|J> be a second such stencil,
  on a finite type set <math|G>, with strip cost <math|C<rsub|J>>. Given a
  cross kernel <math|K> from <math|\<cal-C\>\<times\>F> to
  <math|\<cal-C\>\<times\>G>, form its sandwich

  <\equation*>
    S*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|y,j|)>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|u,v\<in\>\<cal-C\>><big|sum><rsub|b\<in\>F,<space|0.17em>c\<in\>G>I*<around|(|i,b,u|)>*K*<around|(|<around|(|x+u,b|)>,<around|(|y+v,c|)>|)>*J*<around|(|c,j,-v|)>.
  </equation*>

  Assume the sums below are absolutely convergent; this follows, in each
  application here, from the weighted bounds just stated and Tonelli's
  theorem.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Sandwich atlas estimate><label|lem:ex-lattice-sandwich-atlas>Suppose
    <math|I,J> have nonnegative strip costs <math|C<rsub|I>,C<rsub|J>> at
    width <math|\<alpha\>>, and <math|K> has per-label atlas bounds of order
    two with nonnegative cost <math|Q> at width <math|\<sigma\>>. If
    <math|0\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>\<leqslant\>\<alpha\>> and
    <math|L\<geqslant\>1>, then <math|S> has per-label order-two atlas bounds
    at width <math|\<sigma\>> with cost

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||F|\|><space|0.17em><around|\||G|\|>*<space|0.17em>C<rsub|I>*C<rsub|J>*Q.
    </equation*>

    The first input difference of <math|S> is obtained by replacing <math|K>
    with its first input difference in the same formula; in particular its
    row cost is at most <math|<around|\||F|\|><space|0.17em><around|\||G|\|>*C<rsub|I>*C<rsub|J>*Q/L<rsup|3>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For <math|x,y,u,v\<in\>\<cal-C\>>, the triangle inequality gives

    <\equation*>
      d<rsub|L>*<around|(|x,y|)>\<leqslant\>d<rsub|L>*<around|(|x+u,y+v|)>+<around|\||u|\|><rsub|1>/L+<around|\||v|\|><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    Consequently the exponential weight at <math|<around|(|x,y|)>> is bounded
    by the central kernel weight multiplied by
    <math|e<rsup|\<alpha\><around|\||u|\|><rsub|1>>*e<rsup|\<alpha\><around|\||v|\|><rsub|1>>>,
    since <math|0\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>/L\<leqslant\>\<alpha\>> and
    <math|0\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>\<leqslant\>\<alpha\>>. Sum absolute values
    first in <math|y,v,c>. For each fixed <math|u,b>, the kernel row bound,
    summed over the <math|<around|\||G|\|>> possible intermediate output
    types <math|c>, is <math|<around|\||G|\|>*Q*L<rsup|-2>>. For each
    resulting intermediate output, the stencil <math|J> contributes at most
    <math|C<rsub|J>>. Summing in <math|u,b> against <math|I> costs at most
    <math|<around|\||F|\|>*C<rsub|I>>. The row bound is therefore

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||F|\|>*C<rsub|I>\<cdot\><around|\||G|\|>*Q*L<rsup|-2>\<cdot\>C<rsub|J>.
    </equation*>

    For the column, fix the external input type <math|i> and sum first in
    <math|x,u,b>. The stencil <math|I> contributes <math|C<rsub|I>>; the
    kernel column bound, summed over the <math|<around|\||F|\|>> possible
    input types, is <math|<around|\||F|\|>*Q*L<rsup|2>>; and summing the
    remaining displacement and type of <math|J> contributes
    <math|<around|\||G|\|>*C<rsub|J>>. This gives
    <math|<around|\||F|\|><around|\||G|\|>*C<rsub|I>*C<rsub|J>*Q*L<rsup|2>>.

    Subtract the formulas for <math|S*<around|(|<around|(|x+e<rsub|a>,i|)>,<around|(|y,j|)>|)>>
    and <math|S*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|y,j|)>|)>> term by term
    with the same summation variables. Since <math|I,J> do not depend on
    their torus positions, this replaces <math|K*<around|(|<around|(|x+u,b|)>,<around|(|y+v,c|)>|)>>
    by

    <\equation*>
      K*<around|(|<around|(|x+u+e<rsub|a>,b|)>,<around|(|y+v,c|)>|)>-K*<around|(|<around|(|x+u,b|)>,<around|(|y+v,c|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Repeating the row estimate with the difference-row cost
    <math|Q/L<rsup|3>> proves the last assertion. Absolute convergence
    follows from the same calculation with all exponential weights replaced
    by <math|1>; this licenses the subtraction.
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Response atlas from primitive strip
    receipts><label|thm:ex-lattice-response-atlas>Let <math|F> be the common
    finite type set of the fine inverse stencil and the coarse metric
    stencil, and let <math|T> be the tower cross-kernel. Suppose the two bed
    stencils have nonnegative strip costs <math|C<rsub|f>,C<rsub|c>> at width
    <math|\<alpha\>>, and <math|C<rsub|T>\<geqslant\>0>, while <math|T> has
    base strip cost <math|C<rsub|T>/L<rsup|2>> and each first
    residue-difference strip cost <math|C<rsub|T>/L<rsup|3>> at that width.
    For <math|0\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>\<leqslant\>\<alpha\>>, let
    <math|<wide|T|^>> be the installed cross-kernel and let

    <\equation*>
      K=I<rsub|f>*<space|0.17em><wide|T|^>*<space|0.17em>I<rsub|c>
    </equation*>

    denote its convolution sandwich with the fine inverse and coarse metric
    stencils. Then <math|K> has the order-two atlas bounds at width
    <math|\<sigma\>> with cost

    <\equation>
      Q=<around|\||F|\|><rsup|2>*e<rsup|4*\<sigma\>>*C<rsub|f>*C<rsub|c>*C<rsub|T>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-atlas-cost>
    </equation>

    In particular its per-label row, column and first-difference row bounds
    are <math|Q/L<rsup|2>>, <math|Q*L<rsup|2>> and <math|Q/L<rsup|3>>,
    respectively.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-cross-atlas> gives the order-two
    atlas for <math|<wide|T|^>> with cost
    <math|e<rsup|4*\<sigma\>>*C<rsub|T>>. Apply
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-sandwich-atlas> with
    <math|I<rsub|f>> and <math|I<rsub|c>>; the two intermediate type sums
    each contribute one factor <math|<around|\||F|\|>>. This yields exactly
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-atlas-cost> and all three asserted estimates.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Constant-column test for the constructed
    response><label|prop:ex-lattice-response-travel>In
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-response-atlas>, suppose <math|T>
    is the two-form wire tower of a flight with
    <math|4\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>t+1>, odd block side
    <math|L\<geqslant\>3> and kernel parameter <math|k\<gtr\>0>. Then the sum
    of each response row over its output cover coordinate is independent of
    the input cover coordinate. More precisely, for fixed face labels
    <math|i,j>,

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|y\<in\>\<cal-C\>>K*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|y,j|)>|)>=L<rsup|-2>*<big|sum><rsub|b\<in\>F><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|u\<in\>\<cal-C\>>I<rsub|f>*<around|(|i,b,u|)>|)><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|v\<in\>\<cal-C\>>I<rsub|c>*<around|(|b,j,v|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    For every positive coarse side <math|n>, periodizing <math|K> over the
    output fibre gives the finite carrier formed from these same fine and
    coarse bed rows and the tower installed between sides <math|L*n> and
    <math|n>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The wire calculation at zero momentum in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:kernel-transport-identities> gives the
    identity matrix at the principal alias and zero at all other aliases. In
    the residue-symbol sum of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-free-kernel-estimates>,
    the tower scaling therefore gives <math|L<rsup|-2>*I> at zero momentum
    for every residue. The strip receipt makes the coefficient rows
    absolutely summable, so evaluating their Fourier series at zero yields
    the same value after taking real parts, since this zero-momentum matrix
    is real:

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|z>T*<around|(|r,i,j,z|)>=L<rsup|-2>*\<delta\><rsub|i*j>,<space|2em>r\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|L>.
    </equation*>

    The installation formula reindexes <math|z> by
    <math|y-\<rho\><rsub|L><around|(|x|)>>, and the sum of each installed row
    is therefore the same <math|L<rsup|-2>*\<delta\><rsub|i*j>>,
    independently of <math|x>.

    Insert this identity in the sandwich formula. The two bed rows are
    translation invariant and absolutely summable; the atlas row bound makes
    the full triple sum absolutely convergent. Summing first in <math|y>
    eliminates the tower's intermediate output label and gives the displayed
    product of the two bed-row sums. In particular the result does not depend
    on <math|x>. To identify the finite carrier, periodize the final output
    <math|y> modulo <math|n>. Group the fine intermediate coordinate modulo
    <math|L*n> and the coarse one modulo <math|n>. The identity
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|L>*<around|(|x+L*n*w|)>=\<rho\><rsub|L><around|(|x|)>+n*w>
    transfers the fine period shift to the coarse argument of the tower.
    Absolute convergence permits the exchange of the fibre sums. Each
    translation-invariant bed row then gives its finite convolution matrix,
    and the centered-residue decomposition gives the installed tower matrix.
    The resulting finite sum is exactly the matrix product of these three
    factors. This is the quotient-fibre calculation of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-flat-step>, now applied to the
    two successive products. It works for every <math|n\<geqslant\>1> without
    a new period-dependent constant.
  </proof>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|What this estimate does not supply>The theorem starts with the
    weighted cross-kernel receipts for <math|T> and its first differences,
    and with the two bed-stencil receipts. The wire calculation in
    Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-free-kernel-estimates>
    and<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-free-kernel-components> supplies a
    uniform tower receipt for the flights and fixed parameters stated there.
    Applying it here also requires identifying those flights and the
    installed tower with the chosen RG history and providing both bed bounds
    for the same data, uniformly in the period. The atlas calculation alone
    does not establish those matches.<label|rem:ex-lattice-atlas-open>
  </remark>

  <subsection|One-step propagation of a neutral curvature
  representative><label|sec:ex-lattice-flat-step>

  This section gives the local mathematical content of the one-step
  flat-sector update. It concerns an order-zero quadratic precision
  difference; it does not assert convergence of the full Wilson theory. The
  inputs are exact one-step Schur data and weighted kernel estimates. All
  cost sums below are absolute weighted sums in
  <math|<around|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>; signed kernel sums are used only after
  their absolute convergence has been established.

  <subsubsection|Spaces and estimates>

  Let <math|\<cal-C\>=\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>> be the cover with Laurent distance
  <math|<math-up|sep><around|(|x,y|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a=1><rsup|4><around|\||x<rsub|a>-y<rsub|a>|\|>>.
  For a positive integer scale <math|L>, set

  <\equation*>
    \<delta\><rsub|L>*<around|(|x,y|)>=L<rsup|-1><math-up|sep><around|(|x,L*y|)>,<space|2em><math-up|bill><around|(|a,t|)>=<around|\||a|\|>*e<rsup|t>.
  </equation*>

  For finite label sets <math|I,J>, a kernel <math|K> is a real function on
  <math|<around|(|\<cal-C\>\<times\>I|)>\<times\><around|(|\<cal-C\>\<times\>J|)>>.
  All three atlas bounds below are per fixed finite label: for every cover
  point <math|x>, input label <math|i>, and output label <math|j>,

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|y\<in\>\<cal-C\>><math-up|bill><around*|(|K*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|y,j|)>|)>,\<omega\>*\<delta\><rsub|L>*<around|(|x,y|)>|)>\<leqslant\>b*L<rsup|-2>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-flat-step-row>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|x\<in\>\<cal-C\>><math-up|bill><around*|(|K*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|y,j|)>|)>,\<omega\>*\<delta\><rsub|L>*<around|(|x,y|)>|)>\<leqslant\>b*L<rsup|2>,<space|1em><around|(|y,j|)><text|
    fixed>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-flat-step-column>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|y\<in\>\<cal-C\>><math-up|bill><around*|(|K*<around|(|<around|(|x+e<rsub|a>,i|)>,<around|(|y,j|)>|)>-K*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|y,j|)>|)>,\<omega\>*\<delta\><rsub|L>*<around|(|x,y|)>|)>\<leqslant\>b*L<rsup|-3>.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-flat-step-difference>>>>>
  </align>

  In the column line, <math|i> and <math|<around|(|y,j|)>> are fixed and the
  sum is over the cover coordinate of the input. These are the order-two
  atlas costs. Summing a row bound over all <math|<around|\||J|\|>> output
  labels or a column bound over all <math|<around|\||I|\|>> input labels
  contributes the corresponding explicit cardinality factor; neither line
  above already includes that factor. Here and below an estimate stated at
  width <math|\<omega\>> also holds at every smaller nonnegative width.

  A rank-one packet <math|P> has coefficients
  <math|P*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|v,j|)>|)>>, with both
  positions in <math|\<cal-C\>> and both types in <math|I>. Define its folded
  cost by

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|P|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<tau\>,<math-up|fold>>\<assign\>sup<rsub|<around|(|x,i|)>>
    <big|sum><rsub|<around|(|v,j|)>\<in\>\<cal-C\>\<times\>I><around|\||P*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|v,j|)>|)>|\|>*e<rsup|\<tau\><math-up|sep><around|(|x,v|)>>.
  </equation*>

  The supremum is taken in <math|<around|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>; in particular it
  is zero if the input label set is empty. The packet is neutral when
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|v\<in\>\<cal-C\>>P*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|v,j|)>|)>=0>
  for every <math|x,i,j>. It is diagonally stationary when translating both
  cover positions by the same unit vector leaves each coefficient unchanged.

  Let <math|T,U> map fine cover-label spaces to coarse cover-label spaces,
  using the same finite type set <math|I>, <math|<around|\||I|\|>=m>. The
  push is

  <\equation>
    <around|(|P*\<star\><around|(|T,U|)>|)><around|(|<around|(|y,k|)>,<around|(|z,l|)>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|<around|(|x,i|)>\<in\>\<cal-C\>\<times\>I>T*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|y,k|)>|)>*<big|sum><rsub|<around|(|v,j|)>\<in\>\<cal-C\>\<times\>I>P*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|v,j|)>|)>*U*<around|(|<around|(|v,j|)>,<around|(|z,l|)>|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-flat-step-push>
  </equation>

  Its folded cost is defined by the same formula as for <math|P>, with fine
  labels replaced by coarse labels and <math|\<tau\>> replaced by
  <math|\<sigma\>>.

  <subsubsection|Centering and the kernel row bound>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Centering bound from an order-two
    atlas><label|lem:ex-lattice-flat-step-center>Suppose <math|U> has the
    per-label difference bound <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-flat-step-difference>
    with cost <math|p> and width <math|\<omega\>>. For
    <math|x,v\<in\>\<cal-C\>> and types <math|j,l\<in\>I>, set

    <\equation*>
      U<rsub|x>*<around|(|v,j;z,l|)>=U*<around|(|<around|(|v,j|)>,<around|(|z,l|)>|)>-U*<around|(|<around|(|x,j|)>,<around|(|z,l|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    If <math|\<tau\>\<gtr\>0>, <math|0\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>\<leqslant\>\<omega\>>,
    and <math|2*\<sigma\>\<leqslant\>L*\<tau\>>, then for every fixed output
    type <math|l>,

    <\equation>
      <big|sum><rsub|z\<in\>\<cal-C\>><around|\||U<rsub|x>*<around|(|v,j;z,l|)>|\|>*e<rsup|\<sigma\>*\<delta\><rsub|L>*<around|(|x,z|)>>\<leqslant\><frac|2*p|\<tau\>*L<rsup|3>>*e<rsup|\<tau\><math-up|sep><around|(|x,v|)>>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-flat-step-centered-row>
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|a=<math-up|sep><around|(|x,v|)>>. Choose a coordinate path
    <math|x=v<rsub|0>,v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|a>=v> with each increment a
    signed unit vector. The cover distance triangle inequality gives

    <\equation*>
      \<delta\><rsub|L>*<around|(|x,z|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|sep><around|(|x,v<rsub|k>|)>/L+\<delta\><rsub|L>*<around|(|v<rsub|k>,z|)>\<leqslant\>a/L+\<delta\><rsub|L>*<around|(|v<rsub|k>,z|)>.
    </equation*>

    For each path edge, the per-label difference row bound
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-flat-step-difference> applies at the initial
    endpoint for a positive edge and at the other endpoint for a negative
    edge, after reversing its sign. Both endpoints are at distance at most
    <math|a> from <math|x>. Thus its weighted row at center <math|x> is
    bounded by <math|p*L<rsup|-3>*e<rsup|\<sigma\>*a/L>>. Telescoping the
    coefficient difference and summing the <math|a> edge bounds yields

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|z><around|\||U*<around|(|<around|(|v,j|)>,<around|(|z,l|)>|)>-U*<around|(|<around|(|x,j|)>,<around|(|z,l|)>|)>|\|>*e<rsup|\<sigma\>*\<delta\><rsub|L>*<around|(|x,z|)>>\<leqslant\>p*L<rsup|-3>*a*e<rsup|\<sigma\>*a/L>.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|0\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>/L\<leqslant\>\<tau\>/2>,

    <\equation*>
      a*e<rsup|\<sigma\>*a/L>\<leqslant\>a*e<rsup|\<tau\>*a/2>\<leqslant\><around|(|2/\<tau\>|)>*e<rsup|\<tau\>*a>,
    </equation*>

    where the last inequality is <math|u\<leqslant\>e<rsup|u>> for
    <math|u=\<tau\>*a/2\<geqslant\>0>. This proves the claim, including
    <math|a=0>.
  </proof>

  <\remark>
    The path bound is for one fixed output label. Summing it over all
    <math|m> output labels gives the aggregate outgoing-row bound used below.
    No cancellation has yet been used. Neutrality is used next to replace
    <math|U> by this centered kernel inside the convolution.
  </remark>

  <subsubsection|Weighted push estimate>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Weighted convolution><label|lem:ex-lattice-flat-step-convolution>Let
    <math|P> be a neutral rank-one packet with
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|P|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<tau\>,<math-up|fold>>\<leqslant\>r>,
    where <math|r\<geqslant\>0>. Suppose <math|T> has the order-two atlas row
    and column bounds with cost <math|q\<geqslant\>0>, and <math|U> has the
    order-two atlas bounds with cost <math|p\<geqslant\>0>, both at width
    <math|\<omega\>>. Assume explicitly <math|\<tau\>\<gtr\>0>,
    <math|0\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>\<leqslant\>\<omega\>>, and
    <math|2*\<sigma\>\<leqslant\>L*\<tau\>>. Then

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|P*\<star\><around|(|T,U|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<sigma\>,<math-up|fold>>\<leqslant\><frac|m<rsup|2>*q*<around|(|2/\<tau\>|)>*p|L>*<space|0.17em>r.<label|eq:ex-lattice-flat-step-folded>
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Before using neutrality, the uncentered absolute sum is already finite.
    For a fixed output <math|<around|(|y,k|)>>, first sum over the outgoing
    cover coordinate and type: its per-label row budget is
    <math|p*L<rsup|-2>>, so summing the <math|m> output types costs
    <math|m*p*L<rsup|-2>>. The packet factor then sums to at most <math|r>,
    since its nonnegative exponential weight is at least one. Finally the
    root column has per-input- type budget <math|q*L<rsup|2>> and summing the
    <math|m> input types costs <math|m*q*L<rsup|2>>. Thus

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|x,i,v,j,z,l><around|\||T*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|y,k|)>|)>*P*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|v,j|)>|)>*U*<around|(|<around|(|v,j|)>,<around|(|z,l|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|m*q*L<rsup|2>|)>*r*<around|(|m*p*L<rsup|-2>|)>=m<rsup|2>*q*p*r.
    </equation*>

    This finite majorant justifies Fubini and all subsequent changes of
    order, including the neutrality replacement. For fixed
    <math|<around|(|x,i|)>> and output type <math|l>, neutrality gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|v,j>P*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|v,j|)>|)>*U*<around|(|<around|(|v,j|)>,<around|(|z,l|)>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|v,j>P*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|v,j|)>|)>*<around|[|U*<around|(|<around|(|v,j|)>,<around|(|z,l|)>|)>-U*<around|(|<around|(|x,j|)>,<around|(|z,l|)>|)>|]>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed, for each <math|j>, the subtracted term is independent of <math|v>
    and its coefficient sum is zero. The folded bound makes these coefficient
    sums absolutely convergent; the Atlas pointwise bounds and the
    centered-row bound make the products summable. Thus this replacement and
    the following uses of Tonelli are legitimate.

    Fix an output market <math|<around|(|y,k|)>>. For output
    <math|<around|(|z,l|)>>, the cover geometry gives

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|sep><around|(|y,z|)>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|L>*<around|(|x,y|)>+\<delta\><rsub|L>*<around|(|x,z|)>.
    </equation*>

    After taking absolute values in the push and summing in
    <math|<around|(|z,l|)>>, the root factor is weighted by
    <math|\<sigma\>*\<delta\><rsub|L>*<around|(|x,y|)>> and the outgoing
    factor by <math|\<sigma\>*\<delta\><rsub|L>*<around|(|x,z|)>>. For every
    fixed pair of input and output types of <math|U>,
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-flat-step-center> bounds the
    outgoing cover sum by <math|2*p*<around|(|\<tau\>*L<rsup|3>|)><rsup|-1>*e<rsup|\<tau\><math-up|sep><around|(|x,v|)>>>.
    Summing over its <math|m> output types gives the factor <math|m>. The
    exponential is exactly the packet weight on the leg <math|v>. Summing the
    packet over <math|<around|(|v,j|)>> therefore costs at most <math|r> for
    each fixed <math|<around|(|x,i|)>>.

    The remaining root sum is over <math|<around|(|x,i|)>>. For the fixed
    output <math|<around|(|y,k|)>>, the column bound in
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-flat-step-column> is per fixed input type <math|i>;
    summing over the <math|m> possible <math|i> costs <math|m*q*L<rsup|2>>.
    Consequently the whole nonnegative sum is bounded by

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|m*q*L<rsup|2>|)>*<around*|(|m*<frac|2*p|\<tau\>*L<rsup|3>>|)>*r=<frac|m<rsup|2>*q*<around|(|2/\<tau\>|)>*p|L>*r.
    </equation*>

    This bound is finite and proves both absolute convergence of the defining
    push and the asserted folded estimate. More formally, the argument
    applies first to finite subsets of each countable cover sum; the uniform
    right-hand side bounds their increasing limit, so monotone convergence
    gives the full sums and justifies every rearrangement.
  </proof>

  <\remark>
    The estimate is a neutral-packet estimate: the extra factor
    <math|L<rsup|-1>> comes from centering <math|U> and using its
    first-difference row cost <math|L<rsup|-3>>, while the coarse-root column
    cost contributes <math|L<rsup|2>>. Without neutrality, the centering
    identity is unavailable and the stated contraction does not follow.
  </remark>

  <subsubsection|Periodization and matching of the pushed representative>

  For each positive integer <math|n>, let
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|n>=<around|(|\<bbb-Z\>/n*\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|4>>, let
  <math|\<pi\><rsub|n>:\<cal-C\>\<to\>\<Lambda\><rsub|n>> be coordinatewise
  reduction, and let <math|\<ell\><rsub|n>:\<Lambda\><rsub|n>\<to\>\<cal-C\>>
  choose the centered representative with
  <math|\<pi\><rsub|n>*\<ell\><rsub|n>> the identity. The fibre of
  <math|\<pi\><rsub|n>> over <math|y> is exactly
  <math|<around|{|\<ell\><rsub|n><around|(|y|)>+n*r:r\<in\>\<cal-C\>|}>>: if
  <math|\<pi\><rsub|n>*z=y>, then each coordinate of
  <math|z-\<ell\><rsub|n><around|(|y|)>> is divisible by <math|n>, with
  unique quotient. Fix positive periods <math|n<rsub|f>,n<rsub|c>,n> when
  using a composition. For a kernel <math|K> and output side <math|n>, define
  its displayed finite matrix by

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|D<rsub|n>*K|)><around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|y,j|)>|)>\<assign\><big|sum><rsub|r\<in\>\<cal-C\>>K*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|n><around|(|y|)>+n*r,j|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  This display is used only when its fibre sum converges absolutely. When the
  input side is <math|n<rsub|f>> and output side is <math|n<rsub|c>>,
  <math|K> matches a matrix <math|A> if these fibre sums converge absolutely
  for every input and output index and

  <\equation>
    <around|(|D<rsub|n<rsub|c>>*K|)><around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|y,j|)>|)>=A*<around|(|<around|(|\<pi\><rsub|n<rsub|f>><around|(|x|)>,i|)>,<around|(|y,j|)>|)><space|1em><text|for
    every ><around|(|x,i|)>\<in\>\<cal-C\>\<times\>I,<around|(|y,j|)>\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|n<rsub|c>>\<times\>J.<label|eq:ex-lattice-flat-step-match>
  </equation>

  Thus matching requires the same value for every cover representative of the
  input torus point, not just its chosen lift. A kernel is city-covariant at
  ratio <math|L> if

  <\equation>
    K*<around|(|<around|(|x+L*u,i|)>,<around|(|y+u,j|)>|)>=K*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|y,j|)>|)><space|1em><around|(|u,x,y\<in\>\<cal-C\>|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-flat-step-city>
  </equation>

  For kernels <math|K> and <math|U> with an intermediate finite type set
  <math|J>, define their cover composition by

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|K*\<diamond\>*U|)><around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|z,k|)>|)>\<assign\><big|sum><rsub|v\<in\>\<cal-C\>><big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>J>K*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|v,j|)>|)>*U*<around|(|<around|(|v,j|)>,<around|(|z,k|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Suppose <math|K> matches <math|A> from side <math|n<rsub|f>> to side
  <math|n<rsub|c>>, and <math|U> matches <math|B> from side <math|n<rsub|c>>
  to side <math|n>. Assume for each fixed external input and output that the
  total double absolute sum over the intermediate and final cover coordinates
  and intermediate labels is finite. Then

  <\equation>
    <around|(|D<rsub|n>*<around|(|K*\<diamond\>*U|)>|)><around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|y,k|)>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|w\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|n<rsub|c>>><big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>J>A*<around|(|<around|(|\<pi\><rsub|n<rsub|f>><around|(|x|)>,i|)>,<around|(|w,j|)>|)>*B*<around|(|<around|(|w,j|)>,<around|(|y,k|)>|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-flat-step-composition>
  </equation>

  Indeed, replace the output fibre in <math|D<rsub|n>> by its explicit coset,
  interchange absolutely convergent sums, and first sum in the final cover
  lift:

  <\equation*>
    <\eqsplit>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|D<rsub|n>*<around|(|K*\<diamond\>*U|)>|)><around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|y,k|)>|)>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|v,j>K*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|v,j|)>|)>*<big|sum><rsub|r\<in\>\<cal-C\>>U*<around|(|<around|(|v,j|)>,<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|n><around|(|y|)>+n*r,k|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|v,j>K*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|v,j|)>|)>*B*<around|(|<around|(|\<pi\><rsub|n<rsub|c>><around|(|v|)>,j|)>,<around|(|y,k|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|w,j>A*<around|(|<around|(|\<pi\><rsub|n<rsub|f>><around|(|x|)>,i|)>,<around|(|w,j|)>|)>*B*<around|(|<around|(|w,j|)>,<around|(|y,k|)>|)>.>>>>
    </eqsplit>
  </equation*>

  The second equality is the matching identity for <math|U>. For the third,
  partition <math|\<cal-C\>> into the disjoint fibres
  <math|\<pi\><rsub|n<rsub|c>><rsup|-1><around|(|w|)>> and use the matching
  identity for <math|K> on each fibre. This proves the exact finite-matrix
  product, including the sum over intermediate types.

  The transpose also has an exact matching rule. Suppose
  <math|n<rsub|f>=L*n<rsub|c>>, <math|T> is city-covariant at ratio <math|L>
  and matches <math|M> from side <math|n<rsub|f>> to side <math|n<rsub|c>>,
  and its rows and columns are absolutely summable for each fixed pair of
  types. The atlas bounds above supply this summability. Then
  <math|<math-up|swap><space|0.17em>T>, defined by
  <math|<around|(|<math-up|swap><space|0.17em>T|)><around|(|<around|(|x,j|)>,<around|(|y,i|)>|)>=T*<around|(|<around|(|y,i|)>,<around|(|x,j|)>|)>>,
  matches <math|M<rsup|<math-ss|T>>> from side <math|n<rsub|c>> to side
  <math|n<rsub|f>>. For fixed <math|x\<in\>\<cal-C\>> and
  <math|y\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|n<rsub|f>>>, its display is

  <\equation*>
    <\eqsplit>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|D<rsub|n<rsub|f>><math-up|swap><space|0.17em>T|)><around|(|<around|(|x,j|)>,<around|(|y,i|)>|)>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|v\<in\>\<cal-C\>>T*<around|(|<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|n<rsub|f>><around|(|y|)>+n<rsub|f>*v,i|)>,<around|(|x,j|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|v\<in\>\<cal-C\>>T*<around|(|<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|n<rsub|f>><around|(|y|)>,i|)>,<around|(|x-n<rsub|c>*v,j|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|r\<in\>\<cal-C\>>T*<around|(|<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|n<rsub|f>><around|(|y|)>,i|)>,<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|n<rsub|c>>*<around|(|\<pi\><rsub|n<rsub|c>>*x|)>+n<rsub|c>*r,j|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=<around|(|D<rsub|n<rsub|c>>*T|)><around|(|<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|n<rsub|f>><around|(|y|)>,i|)>,<around|(|\<pi\><rsub|n<rsub|c>>*x,j|)>|)>=M*<around|(|<around|(|y,i|)>,<around|(|\<pi\><rsub|n<rsub|c>>*x,j|)>|)>.>>>>
    </eqsplit>
  </equation*>

  The second line uses city covariance with shift <math|u=n<rsub|c>*v>. In
  the third line use <math|x=\<ell\><rsub|n<rsub|c>>*<around|(|\<pi\><rsub|n<rsub|c>>*x|)>+n<rsub|c><space|0.17em><math-up|rent><rsub|n<rsub|c>><around|(|x|)>>
  and the bijective reindexing <math|r=<math-up|rent><rsub|n<rsub|c>><around|(|x|)>-v>
  of <math|\<cal-C\>>. The last line is the matching identity and equals the
  <math|<around|(|<around|(|\<pi\><rsub|n<rsub|c>>*x,j|)>,<around|(|y,i|)>|)>>
  entry of <math|M<rsup|<math-ss|T>>>. Thus no unproved periodization rule is
  needed.

  For a rank-one packet, define its two-point kernel by
  <math|P<rsup|\<sharp\>>*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|v,j|)>|)>=P*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|v,j|)>|)>>.
  Direct substitution in the push definition gives the cover-kernel identity

  <\equation>
    <around|(|P*\<star\><around|(|T,U|)>|)><rsup|\<sharp\>>=<around|(|<math-up|swap><space|0.17em>T|)>*\<diamond\>*<around|(|P<rsup|\<sharp\>>*\<diamond\>*U|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-flat-step-second-composition>
  </equation>

  For completeness, the absolute sum in the two compositions is finite. The
  row atlas estimate implies <math|<around|\||U*<around|(|<around|(|v,j|)>,<around|(|z,l|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>p*L<rsup|-2>>,
  and the folded bound implies <math|<big|sum><rsub|v,j><around|\||P*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|v,j|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>r>.
  Hence <math|<big|sum><rsub|v,j><around|\||P*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|v,j|)>|)>*U*<around|(|<around|(|v,j|)>,<around|(|z,l|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>p*L<rsup|-2>*r>.
  The outer sum against <math|<math-up|swap><space|0.17em>T> is finite by the
  column atlas bound, summed over its <math|m> input types. For the
  output-fibre rearrangement, fix <math|<around|(|y,k|)>> and the final type
  <math|l> and sum over all <math|z\<in\>\<cal-C\>> instead:

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|x,i,v,j,z><around|\||T*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|y,k|)>|)>*P*<around|(|<around|(|x,i|)>,<around|(|v,j|)>|)>*U*<around|(|<around|(|v,j|)>,<around|(|z,l|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|m*q*L<rsup|2>|)>*<space|0.17em>r*<space|0.17em><around|(|p*L<rsup|-2>|)>=m*q*p*r.
  </equation*>

  Here the row bound of <math|U> is uniform in <math|<around|(|v,j|)>>, the
  folded packet bound is uniform in <math|<around|(|x,i|)>>, and the column
  bound of <math|T> is summed over its <math|m> input types. Restricting
  <math|z> to any periodization fibre only decreases the sum. This bound
  justifies Fubini for both applications below, including the sum over every
  representative of the final torus point. If <math|P<rsup|\<sharp\>>>
  matches <math|A>, and <math|T,U> match <math|M,N>, respectively, apply
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-flat-step-composition> first to
  <math|P<rsup|\<sharp\>>> and <math|U>, then to
  <math|<math-up|swap><space|0.17em>T> and that composition. The transpose
  calculation above supplies the match <math|M<rsup|<math-ss|T>>> for
  <math|<math-up|swap><space|0.17em>T>. The resulting displayed coarse matrix
  is exactly <math|M<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*A*N>.

  <subsubsection|Exact propagation of the quadratic representative>

  Let <math|E<rsub|n><rsup|1>=\<bbb-R\><rsup|\<Lambda\><rsub|n>\<times\>I<rsub|1>>>
  and <math|E<rsub|n><rsup|2>=\<bbb-R\><rsup|\<Lambda\><rsub|n>\<times\>I<rsub|2>>>
  be the one-form and curvature coordinate spaces;
  <math|d<rsub|n>:E<rsub|n><rsup|1>\<to\>E<rsub|n><rsup|2>>. The precision
  matrices <math|Q<rsub|H>,Q<rsub|Y>> act on
  <math|E<rsub|n<rsub|f>><rsup|1>>, and <math|Q<rsub|H<rprime|'>>,Q<rsub|Y<rprime|'>>>
  act on <math|E<rsub|n<rsub|c>><rsup|1>>. The curvature representative
  matrix <math|A> acts on <math|E<rsub|n<rsub|f>><rsup|2>>, whose type set is
  the same <math|I> used for the packet legs. The mean matrices
  <math|M<rsub|A>,N<rsub|A>:E<rsub|n<rsub|c>><rsup|1>\<to\>E<rsub|n<rsub|f>><rsup|1>>,
  and the matched carrier matrices <math|C<rsub|Y>,C<rsub|H>:E<rsub|n<rsub|c>><rsup|2>\<to\>E<rsub|n<rsub|f>><rsup|2>>.

  Here are the exact identities used below and their derivations. The two
  one-step precision inputs share a blocking (coarse-averaging) map
  <math|B:E<rsub|n<rsub|f>><rsup|1>\<to\>E<rsub|n<rsub|c>><rsup|1>> and a
  positive blocking penalty <math|\<lambda\>>. Write
  <math|\<kappa\>=\<lambda\><rsup|-1>> for its reciprocal, which is the
  coefficient in the dual identities. For the two histories they say

  <\equation*>
    Q<rsub|H>*M<rsub|A>=B<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*Q<rsub|H<rprime|'>>,<space|1em>B*M<rsub|A>=I-\<kappa\>*Q<rsub|H<rprime|'>>,<space|2em>Q<rsub|Y>*N<rsub|A>=B<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*Q<rsub|Y<rprime|'>>,<space|1em>B*N<rsub|A>=I-\<kappa\>*Q<rsub|Y<rprime|'>>.
  </equation*>

  The floor data make all four precision matrices symmetric. Transposing the
  first identity for <math|Y> gives <math|N<rsub|A><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*Q<rsub|Y>=Q<rsub|Y<rprime|'>>*B>,
  while transposing <math|B*N<rsub|A>=I-\<kappa\>*Q<rsub|Y<rprime|'>>> gives
  <math|N<rsub|A><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*B<rsup|<math-ss|T>>=I-\<kappa\>*Q<rsub|Y<rprime|'>>>.
  Therefore

  <\equation*>
    <\eqsplit>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|N<rsub|A><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*<around|(|Q<rsub|Y>-Q<rsub|H>|)>*M<rsub|A>>|<cell|=Q<rsub|Y<rprime|'>>*B*M<rsub|A>-N<rsub|A><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*B<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*Q<rsub|H<rprime|'>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=Q<rsub|Y<rprime|'>>*<around|(|I-\<kappa\>*Q<rsub|H<rprime|'>>|)>-<around|(|I-\<kappa\>*Q<rsub|Y<rprime|'>>|)>*Q<rsub|H<rprime|'>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=Q<rsub|Y<rprime|'>>-Q<rsub|H<rprime|'>>.>>>>
    </eqsplit>
  </equation*>

  This proves the mixed Schur identity

  <\equation>
    Q<rsub|Y<rprime|'>>-Q<rsub|H<rprime|'>>=N<rsub|A><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*<around|(|Q<rsub|Y>-Q<rsub|H>|)>*M<rsub|A>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-flat-step-schur>
  </equation>

  For either history, write <math|G,G<rprime|'>> for fine/coarse metric
  matrices, <math|D,D<rprime|'>> for the diagonal matrices, <math|R> for the
  installed transfer on curvature coordinates, and <math|Z> for its mean on
  one-form coordinates. Its carrier is <math|C=G<rsup|-1>*R*G<rprime|'>>. The
  floor equalities on exact forms and the diagonal tower identity are
  respectively <math|G*d<rsub|f>=D*d<rsub|f>>,
  <math|G<rprime|'>*d<rsub|c>=D<rprime|'>*d<rsub|c>>, and
  <math|R*D<rprime|'>*d<rsub|c>=D*d<rsub|f>*Z>. The floor inverse identity is
  <math|G<rsup|-1>*G=I>. Thus, with all domains as above,

  <\equation*>
    <\eqsplit>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|C*d<rsub|c>>|<cell|=G<rsup|-1>*R*G<rprime|'>*d<rsub|c>=G<rsup|-1>*R*D<rprime|'>*d<rsub|c>=G<rsup|-1>*D*d<rsub|f>*Z=G<rsup|-1>*G*d<rsub|f>*Z=d<rsub|f>*Z.>>>>
    </eqsplit>
  </equation*>

  Applying this calculation to the <math|Y> history with <math|Z=N<rsub|A>>
  and the <math|H> history with <math|Z=M<rsub|A>> proves the inherited
  identities

  <\equation>
    C<rsub|Y>*d<rsub|c>=d<rsub|f>*N<rsub|A>,<space|2em>C<rsub|H>*d<rsub|c>=d<rsub|f>*M<rsub|A>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-flat-step-inherited>
  </equation>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|One-step flat-sector propagation><label|prop:ex-lattice-flat-step>Let
    <math|P> be a rank-one packet satisfying neutrality, diagonal
    stationarity, and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|P|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<tau\>,<math-up|fold>>\<leqslant\>r>.
    Suppose the fine two-point kernel associated with <math|P> matches a
    matrix <math|A>, which represents the curvature difference, and

    <\equation*>
      d<rsub|f><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*A*d<rsub|f>=Q<rsub|Y>-Q<rsub|H>.
    </equation*>

    Suppose <math|T,U> are matched, on the coarse torus, by their carrier
    matrices <math|M=C<rsub|Y>> and <math|N=C<rsub|H>>, respectively. They
    satisfy the order-two atlas bounds with nonnegative costs <math|q,p> and
    width <math|\<omega\>> used in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-flat-step-convolution>,
    and are city-covariant at ratio <math|L>. The fine and coarse torus
    periods satisfy <math|n<rsub|f>=L*n<rsub|c>>. Assume
    <math|\<tau\>\<gtr\>0>, <math|r\<geqslant\>0>,
    <math|0\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>\<leqslant\>\<omega\>>, and
    <math|2*\<sigma\>\<leqslant\>L*\<tau\>>. Also assume the exact Schur and
    inherited identities <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-flat-step-schur>
    and<space|0.27em><eqref|eq:ex-lattice-flat-step-inherited>. Finally
    suppose <math|U> has the travel property that each fixed pair of finite
    labels has a row sum independent of the cover point. Then the pushed
    packet <math|P<rprime|'>=P*\<star\><around|(|T,U|)>> has the following
    properties:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|P<rprime|'>> is neutral;

      <item><math|P<rprime|'>> is diagonally stationary;

      <item>its folded cost satisfies

      <\equation*>
        <around|\<\|\|\>|P<rprime|'>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<sigma\>,<math-up|fold>>\<leqslant\><frac|m<rsup|2>*q*<around|(|2/\<tau\>|)>*p|L>*<space|0.17em>r;
      </equation*>

      <item><math|P<rprime|'>> is matched by <math|M<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*A*N>,
      and

      <\equation*>
        d<rsub|c><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*<around|(|M<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*A*N|)>*d<rsub|c>=Q<rsub|Y<rprime|'>>-Q<rsub|H<rprime|'>>.
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The folded estimate is Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-flat-step-convolution>.
    For neutrality, expand the pushed constant-column sum, interchange the
    sums using the same absolute weighted bounds, and first sum in the output
    cover coordinate of <math|U>. Its value depends only on the finite labels
    and is independent of the intermediate cover point by the travel
    assumption. The remaining cover sum is then the neutral column sum of
    <math|P>, hence is zero. For diagonal stationarity, translate the outer
    summation variable and then the inner one. The two covariance identities
    for <math|T> and <math|U>, together with the simultaneous stationarity of
    <math|P>, leave each summand unchanged. Absolute convergence just
    established permits these translations in the sums.

    By the exact reindexing argument in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-flat-step-composition>
    and the transpose calculation following it, the pushed packet is matched
    by <math|M<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*A*N>; the two applications have absolutely
    convergent sums by the weighted estimates. Finally, using
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-flat-step-inherited>,

    <\equation*>
      <\eqsplit>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|d<rsub|c><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*M<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*A*N*d<rsub|c>>|<cell|=<around|(|M*d<rsub|c>|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*A*<around|(|N*d<rsub|c>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=<around|(|d<rsub|f>*N<rsub|A>|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*A*<around|(|d<rsub|f>*M<rsub|A>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=N<rsub|A><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*<around|(|d<rsub|f><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*A*d<rsub|f>|)>*M<rsub|A>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=N<rsub|A><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*<around|(|Q<rsub|Y>-Q<rsub|H>|)>*M<rsub|A>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=Q<rsub|Y<rprime|'>>-Q<rsub|H<rprime|'>>,>>>>
      </eqsplit>
    </equation*>

    where the final equality is <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-flat-step-schur>. This
    proves the propagated curvature identity.
  </proof>

  At a fixed block size <math|L>, if the displayed hypotheses hold for every
  coarse period <math|n<rsub|c>> with the same
  <math|p,q,r,\<tau\>,\<sigma\>,\<omega\>> and type set, then the folded-cost
  bound holds uniformly in <math|n<rsub|c>>. The proof made no additional
  choice or estimate depending on that period. The retained-horizon
  construction in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-rg-horizon-geometry>
  supplies the two response atlases on its fixed adjacent stages;
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-response-travel> supplies the
  matching and travel assertions for the retained wire response. The
  remaining packet, floor, width and funding hypotheses must still be matched
  to the selected history.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The quadratic output is independent of the
    completion><label|lem:ex-lattice-flat-same-output>Fix a level schedule, a
    cutoff <math|N>, and two sequences of quadratic precisions on the
    scheduled tori. At each step assume a common blocking map <math|B> and
    penalty <math|\<lambda\>\<gtr\>0>, and transports
    <math|M<rsub|A>,N<rsub|A>> satisfying

    <\equation*>
      Q<rsub|H>*M<rsub|A>=B<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*Q<rsub|H<rprime|'>>,<space|1em>B*M<rsub|A>=I-\<lambda\><rsup|-1>*Q<rsub|H<rprime|'>>,<space|2em>Q<rsub|Y>*N<rsub|A>=B<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*Q<rsub|Y<rprime|'>>,<space|1em>B*N<rsub|A>=I-\<lambda\><rsup|-1>*Q<rsub|Y<rprime|'>>.
    </equation*>

    Assume <math|Q<rsub|Y>> and <math|Q<rsub|Y<rprime|'>>> are symmetric and
    that the two terminal one-form precisions at level <math|N> agree on
    every scheduled torus. Then their one-form precisions agree at every
    level <math|i\<leqslant\>N> on every scheduled torus. If, at that level,
    their corresponding cover kernels have absolutely summable rows and both
    periodize to those finite precisions, and if the scheduled torus sides
    tend to infinity along the period index, the two cover kernels agree
    entry by entry.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    At level <math|N> equality is the hypothesis. If the two fine precisions
    agree at level <math|i+1>, the mixed Schur identity
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-flat-step-schur> writes the difference of their
    coarse precisions as a left and right matrix multiple of that fine
    difference. It is therefore zero, even if the two transports are
    different. Backward induction proves the finite-torus assertion,
    separately for each period.

    For the cover assertion, fix an input and an output cover coordinate and
    their labels. In the periodization sum the input is wrapped to the finite
    torus, and the output is summed over the congruence class of its wrapped
    coordinate. As the side tends to infinity, each fixed cover point
    eventually belongs to that class exactly when it is the chosen output
    point. The absolute row sum is a summable bound independent of the
    period, so dominated convergence recovers the single cover coefficient
    from the finite matrix entries. The same argument applies to the second
    kernel. Those finite entries are equal by the first part, hence their
    limits are equal. No equality of the two curvature metrics, their square
    roots, or their transports is implied.
  </proof>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Application to the selected RG construction>The proposition proves
    the one-step packet algebra and weighted estimate conditional on the two
    actual order-two atlas bounds, exact tower matching, quadratic floors,
    and travel condition. Those hypotheses are mathematical inputs from the
    selected construction and must be checked there. In particular,
    Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-lattice-c01-selected-application>
    verifies these hypotheses for the selected construction. The observable
    hierarchy is obtained from the centered moment and transfer estimates
    below.<label|rem:ex-lattice-flat-step-inputs>
  </remark>

  <subsection|A scalar decay estimate for cutoff
  errors><label|sec:ex-lattice-flat-rate>

  The following elementary lemma converts a one-step contraction with a
  polynomially controlled source into a power of the ratio of scales. In an
  RG application, the difficult step is to prove that the difference of two
  quadratic precisions has a positive coefficient majorant satisfying the
  hypotheses below. The lemma here does not prove that RG estimate.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Contraction with a polynomially controlled
    source><label|prop:ex-lattice-flat-rate>Let <math|M\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, and
    let

    <\equation*>
      t<rsub|0>\<less\>t<rsub|1>\<less\>\<cdots\>\<less\>t<rsub|M>
    </equation*>

    be real numbers whose successive gaps satisfy

    <\equation*>
      \<Delta\><rsub|j>\<assign\>t<rsub|j+1>-t<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>\<delta\>\<gtr\>0*<space|2em><around|(|0\<leqslant\>j\<less\>M|)>.
    </equation*>

    Fix <math|s\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>, <math|Z\<geqslant\>0>,
    <math|d\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, <math|c<rsub|0>,\<theta\>\<gtr\>0>, and
    <math|A\<geqslant\>0>. Put <math|T=t<rsub|M>>, choose

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\>=min <around|{|c<rsub|0>,\<theta\>/2|}>,<space|2em>\<lambda\>=\<theta\>-\<mu\>,<space|2em>R=max
      <around*|{|2,<frac|2*<around|(|d+1|)>|\<lambda\>>|}>,<space|2em>y<rsub|j>=R+Z+<around|\||t<rsub|j>-s|\|>,
    </equation*>

    and set <math|q=exp <around|(|-\<lambda\>*\<delta\>/2|)>> and
    <math|C=A/<around|(|1-q|)>>. Suppose <math|f<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,f<rsub|M>>
    and <math|g<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,g<rsub|M-1>> are nonnegative real numbers
    such that, for <math|0\<leqslant\>j\<less\>M>,

    <\equation>
      f<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<theta\>*\<Delta\><rsub|j>>*f<rsub|j+1>+g<rsub|j>,<space|2em>e<rsup|c<rsub|0>*<around|(|T-t<rsub|j>|)>>*g<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>A*y<rsub|j><rsup|d>,<label|eq:ex-lattice-flat-rate-recurrence>
    </equation>

    and at the terminal index

    <\equation>
      f<rsub|M>\<leqslant\>A*y<rsub|M><rsup|d>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-flat-rate-boundary>
    </equation>

    Then, for every <math|j\<in\><around|{|0,\<ldots\>,M|}>>,

    <\equation>
      f<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>C*y<rsub|j><rsup|d>*e<rsup|-\<mu\>*<around|(|T-t<rsub|j>|)>>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-flat-rate-conclusion>
    </equation>

    If positive scales are defined by <math|a<rsub|j>=e<rsup|-t<rsub|j>>>,
    the exponential factor in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-flat-rate-conclusion>
    equals <math|<around|(|a<rsub|M>/a<rsub|j>|)><rsup|\<mu\>>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The constants in the conclusion depend on
    <math|A,d,c<rsub|0>,\<theta\>,\<delta\>> but not on <math|s> or <math|Z>.
    The choices give <math|0\<less\>\<mu\>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|0>>,
    <math|\<mu\>\<leqslant\>\<theta\>/2>, and hence
    <math|\<lambda\>=\<theta\>-\<mu\>\<geqslant\>\<theta\>/2\<gtr\>0>. Thus
    <math|0\<less\>q\<less\>1> and <math|C\<geqslant\>A>. Introduce the
    tilted errors

    <\equation*>
      F<rsub|j>=e<rsup|\<mu\>*<around|(|T-t<rsub|j>|)>>*f<rsub|j>,<space|2em>G<rsub|j>=e<rsup|\<mu\>*<around|(|T-t<rsub|j>|)>>*g<rsub|j>.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|t<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>T> and
    <math|\<mu\>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|0>>, the source bound in
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-flat-rate-recurrence> implies

    <\equation>
      0\<leqslant\>G<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>A*y<rsub|j><rsup|d>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-flat-rate-tilted-source>
    </equation>

    Multiplying the recurrence by <math|e<rsup|\<mu\>*<around|(|T-t<rsub|j>|)>>>
    gives

    <\equation>
      F<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<lambda\>*\<Delta\><rsub|j>>*F<rsub|j+1>+G<rsub|j>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-flat-rate-tilted-recurrence>
    </equation>

    We first control the change in the polynomial weight. The triangle
    inequality gives

    <\equation*>
      y<rsub|j+1>=R+Z+<around|\||t<rsub|j+1>-s|\|>\<leqslant\>y<rsub|j>+\<Delta\><rsub|j>.
    </equation*>

    Because <math|y<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>R\<geqslant\>2*<around|(|d+1|)>/\<lambda\>>,
    we have <math|d/y<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>\<lambda\>/2>. Using
    <math|1+x\<leqslant\>e<rsup|x>> for <math|x\<geqslant\>0>, we obtain

    <\equation>
      <\eqsplit>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|e<rsup|-\<lambda\>*\<Delta\><rsub|j>>*y<rsub|j+1><rsup|d>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>y<rsub|j><rsup|d>*e<rsup|-\<lambda\>*\<Delta\><rsub|j>>*<around*|(|1+<frac|\<Delta\><rsub|j>|y<rsub|j>>|)><rsup|d>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>y<rsub|j><rsup|d>*e<rsup|-<around|(|\<lambda\>-d/y<rsub|j>|)>*\<Delta\><rsub|j>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>y<rsub|j><rsup|d>*e<rsup|-\<lambda\>*\<Delta\><rsub|j>/2>\<leqslant\>q*y<rsub|j><rsup|d>.>>>>
      </eqsplit>

      <label|eq:ex-lattice-flat-rate-age>
    </equation>

    For <math|d=0>, the same inequalities hold with the polynomial factor
    equal to one.

    We now argue backward from <math|M>. At the endpoint,
    <math|F<rsub|M>=f<rsub|M>\<leqslant\>A*y<rsub|M><rsup|d>\<leqslant\>C*y<rsub|M><rsup|d>>.
    Suppose <math|F<rsub|j+1>\<leqslant\>C*y<rsub|j+1><rsup|d>>. Equations
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-flat-rate-tilted-recurrence>,
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-flat-rate-tilted-source>, and
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-flat-rate-age> imply

    <\equation*>
      F<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>C*q*y<rsub|j><rsup|d>+A*y<rsub|j><rsup|d>=<around|(|C*q+A|)>*y<rsub|j><rsup|d>=C*y<rsub|j><rsup|d>,
    </equation*>

    where the last equality follows from <math|C=A/<around|(|1-q|)>>.
    Induction proves this bound for every <math|j>. Undoing the tilt gives

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|j>=e<rsup|-\<mu\>*<around|(|T-t<rsub|j>|)>>*F<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>C*y<rsub|j><rsup|d>*e<rsup|-\<mu\>*<around|(|T-t<rsub|j>|)>>,
    </equation*>

    as required. Finally, <math|e<rsup|-<around|(|T-t<rsub|j>|)>>=e<rsup|-t<rsub|M>+t<rsub|j>>=a<rsub|M>/a<rsub|j>>,
    so the exponential factor is the stated scale ratio to the power
    <math|\<mu\>>.
  </proof>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Scope of the recurrence input><label|rem:ex-lattice-flat-rate-scope>The
    proposition is a complete scalar argument once the nonnegative
    recurrence, its polynomially bounded tilted source, and its endpoint
    estimate are known. In the Yang\UMills construction, those data must be
    obtained from the scale-by-scale propagation of a neutral curvature
    representative for the difference of two quadratic RG precisions.
    Establishing that propagation requires the exact Schur-complement,
    locality, and weighted-kernel hypotheses of the RG step. This proposition
    supplies none of them. It therefore proves a power-rate consequence of
    that RG input, not the RG input itself or convergence of the public
    Wilson moments.
  </remark>

  <subsection|Weighted strip bounds for the tower
  kernel><label|sec:ex-lattice-free-kernel-estimates>

  The atlas estimate in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-atlas-estimates>
  needs a Laurent strip bound for the tower cross-kernel and for its first
  residue differences. The construction supplies these bounds from a stronger
  momentum-space estimate: each entry of the alias-dependent wire symbol is
  holomorphic and bounded by a summable polynomial weight in the alias. This
  section proves the conversion, including the scale powers and the centered
  carry in the difference. The entrywise wire estimate is then stated in its
  actual form; it is proved in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-free-kernel-components>
  (Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-wire-entrywise-bound>).

  <subsubsection|From an analytic cube to a weighted Fourier strip>

  For <math|p=<around|(|p<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,p<rsub|4>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-C\><rsup|4>>,
  let

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-Q\><rsub|\<eta\>>=<around|{|p:<around|\||<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>p<rsub|a>|\|>\<less\>\<eta\>,<space|1em><around|\||<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>p<rsub|a>|\|>\<less\>\<pi\>+\<eta\><text|
    for all >a|}>.
  </equation*>

  Suppose <math|f> is holomorphic on <math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|\<eta\>>>,
  <math|<around|\||f<around|(|p|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>A> there, and for every
  real <math|p> with <math|<around|\||p<rsub|a>|\|>\<less\>\<pi\>+\<eta\>>
  and integer vector <math|n> for which <math|<around|\||p<rsub|a>+2*\<pi\>*n<rsub|a>|\|>\<less\>\<pi\>+\<eta\>>
  in each coordinate, <math|f*<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*n|)>=f<around|(|p|)>>.
  Define <math|u> on the horizontal strip
  <math|<around|\||<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>p<rsub|a>|\|>\<less\>\<eta\>> by
  translating each real coordinate into <math|<around|[|-\<pi\>,\<pi\>|]>>
  before evaluating <math|f>. The overlap condition makes this definition
  independent of the representative on the cell boundaries. Moreover <math|u>
  is holomorphic: on each open cell it is a translate of <math|f>; on a
  shared boundary the two holomorphic expressions agree on a real open box,
  hence agree on the complex overlap by applying the one-variable identity
  theorem successively in the four coordinates. The same argument at a
  meeting of several cells gives a single analytic continuation. The bound
  <math|<around|\||u|\|>\<leqslant\>A> follows from the bound on <math|f>.

  Let

  <\equation*>
    <wide|u|^><around|(|n|)>=<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-4>*<big|int><rsub|<around|[|-\<pi\>,\<pi\>|]><rsup|4>>u<around|(|x|)>*e<rsup|-i*n\<cdot\>x>*<space|0.17em>d*x,<space|2em>n\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|h,b\<gtr\>0> with <math|h+b\<less\>\<eta\>>, shifting the
  <math|a>th contour by <math|-i*<around|(|h+b|)><math-up|sgn><around|(|n<rsub|a>|)>>
  gives

  <\equation>
    <around|\||<wide|u|^><around|(|n|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>A*e<rsup|-<around|(|h+b|)><around|\||n|\|><rsub|1>>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-wire-coefficient>
  </equation>

  To justify the shift, apply Cauchy's theorem on the rectangle with
  horizontal sides at imaginary parts <math|0> and
  <math|-<around|(|h+b|)><math-up|sgn><around|(|n<rsub|a>|)>>. The two
  vertical integrals cancel because both <math|u> and
  <math|e<rsup|-i*n<rsub|a>*p<rsub|a>>> are <math|2*\<pi\>>-periodic in the
  real direction. Repeat in each coordinate; the integrand remains
  holomorphic and bounded throughout the product of the four rectangles. On
  the shifted contour the exponential factor has modulus
  <math|e<rsup|-<around|(|h+b|)><around|\||n|\|><rsub|1>>>, proving the
  estimate.

  The geometric series is bounded explicitly. Since
  <math|e<rsup|-b>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+b|)><rsup|-1>>,

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|m\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>>e<rsup|-b<around|\||m|\|>>=<frac|1+e<rsup|-b>|1-e<rsup|-b>>\<leqslant\><frac|2*<around|(|1+b|)>|b>.
  </equation*>

  Taking products over the four coordinates and using
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-wire-coefficient> yields

  <\equation>
    <big|sum><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>e<rsup|h<around|\||n|\|><rsub|1>><around|\||<wide|u|^><around|(|n|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<around*|(|<frac|2*<around|(|1+b|)>|b>|)><rsup|4>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-wire-width>
  </equation>

  If real coefficients are needed, replace <math|<wide|u|^><around|(|n|)>> by
  its real part; the estimate is unchanged. If in addition
  <math|u*<around|(|-x|)>=<wide|u<around|(|x|)>|\<bar\>>> for real <math|x>,
  the Fourier coefficients are real, since conjugating the integral and
  changing <math|x> to <math|-x> gives <math|<wide|<wide|u|^><around|(|n|)>|\<bar\>>=<wide|u|^><around|(|n|)>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Uniform strip extraction><label|lem:ex-lattice-uniform-strip-extraction>Let
    <math|\<cal-I\>> be any index set, <math|w<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>, and let
    <math|f<rsub|i>> be a family of symbols satisfying the preceding
    hypotheses with common <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0> and
    <math|<around|\||f<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>A*w<rsub|i>>. If
    <math|0\<less\>h,b> and <math|h+b\<less\>\<eta\>>, then their real
    Fourier coefficient kernels <math|K<rsub|i><around|(|n|)>=<math-up|Re><wide|u|^><rsub|i><around|(|n|)>>
    satisfy

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>e<rsup|h<around|\||n|\|><rsub|1>><around|\||K<rsub|i><around|(|n|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<around*|(|<frac|2*<around|(|1+b|)>|b>|)><rsup|4>*w<rsub|i>*<space|1em><around|(|i\<in\>\<cal-I\>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The width and the prefactor are independent of the index.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Apply <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-wire-width> to <math|f<rsub|i>/w<rsub|i>> when
    <math|w<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0>. When <math|w<rsub|i>=0>, the bound forces
    <math|f<rsub|i>=0>, so the result is immediate. Taking real parts can
    only decrease absolute values.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The finite-residue average and its scale powers>

  For a real cross-kernel <math|K*<around|(|r,i,j,z|)>>, the strip bound of
  width <math|\<sigma\>> and cost <math|A> means

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>e<rsup|\<sigma\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>*<around|\||K*<around|(|r,i,j,z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<space|1em><text|for
    every fixed >r,i,j.
  </equation*>

  The estimate is per fixed pair of finite labels; summing over labels later
  contributes their cardinalities.

  For an odd side <math|L>, let <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|L>=<around|(|\<bbb-Z\>/L*\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|4>>
  and choose the centered lift <math|\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|r|)>>. Write
  the alias momentum as

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>=<frac|p+2*\<pi\>*\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)>|L>,<space|2em>l\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|L>.
  </equation*>

  For a word <math|u=<around|(|a<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,a<rsub|s>|)>> in the four
  coordinate directions, put

  <\equation*>
    \<chi\><rsub|u><around|(|q|)>=<big|prod><rsub|v=1><rsup|s><around|(|e<rsup|i*q<rsub|a<rsub|v>>>-1|)>,<space|2em>\<chi\><rsub|\<varnothing\>>=1.
  </equation*>

  The rescaled row symbol made from a thread <math|F*<around|(|P,p,l|)>> is

  <\equation>
    \<cal-S\><rsub|e,u><around|(|P,r,p|)>=L<rsup|-e>*<big|sum><rsub|l\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|L>>e<rsup|i*q<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>\<cdot\>\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|r|)>>*\<chi\><rsub|u><around|(|q<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>|)>*F*<around|(|P,p,l|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-spread-symbol>
  </equation>

  Here <math|e> is a fixed nonnegative integer; the tower two-form uses
  <math|e=2>. For each type pair, let <math|K<rsub|e,u>*<around|(|P,r,i,j,z|)>>
  be the real part of the normalized Fourier coefficient at <math|z> of the
  corresponding entry of <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|e,u><around|(|P,r,\<cdot\>|)>>.

  Define

  <\equation*>
    \<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)>=<big|prod><rsub|a=1><rsup|4><around|(|1+<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsub|a>|\|>|)>,<space|2em>w<rsub|j><around|(|l|)>=\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-j>.
  </equation*>

  For the centered representatives,

  <\equation>
    <big|sum><rsub|l\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|L>>\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-2>\<leqslant\>3<rsup|4>=81.<label|eq:ex-lattice-residue-sum>
  </equation>

  Indeed, each coordinate lift is a distinct integer, so the left side is at
  most <math|<big|prod><rsub|a=1><rsup|4><big|sum><rsub|m\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>><around|(|1+<around|\||m|\|>|)><rsup|-2>>.
  The one-dimensional sum is at most <math|1+2*<big|int><rsub|1><rsup|\<infty\>>x<rsup|-2>*<space|0.17em>d*x=3>.
  Also, with <math|g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)>=1+max<rsub|a><around|\||\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsub|a>|\|>>,
  we have <math|g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)>\<leqslant\>\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)>>
  and <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)>\<geqslant\>1>.

  Assume every scalar matrix entry of <math|F*<around|(|P,p,l|)>> is
  holomorphic on a fixed complex cube <math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|\<eta\>>> and
  bounded there by <math|A<rsub|0>*\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-j>>,
  uniformly in <math|P,L,l>. Assume also the reciprocal-shift law
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-wire-overlap>, on the real overlaps used for gluing.
  For <math|p\<in\>\<cal-Q\><rsub|\<eta\>>>, the alias satisfies

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>q<rsub|l><around|(|p|)><rsub|a>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>/L,<space|2em><around|\||q<rsub|l><around|(|p|)><rsub|a>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|C<rsub|\<eta\>>*<around|(|1+<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsub|a>|\|>|)>|L>,
  </equation*>

  where one may take <math|C<rsub|\<eta\>>=3*\<pi\>+2*\<eta\>> for
  <math|L\<geqslant\>1>. The elementary inequality
  <math|<around|\||e<rsup|i*z>-1|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||z|\|>*e<rsup|<around|\||<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>z|\|>>>
  therefore gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<chi\><rsub|u><around|(|q<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|\<eta\>><rsup|s>*e<rsup|s*\<eta\>>*<space|0.17em>L<rsup|-s>*g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|s>.
  </equation*>

  Moreover <math|<around|\||e<rsup|i*q<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>\<cdot\>\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|r|)>>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|2*\<eta\>>>
  because each <math|<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|r|)><rsub|a>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|L-1|)>/2>.
  Hence, whenever <math|j\<geqslant\>s+2>,

  <\equation>
    <\eqsplit>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\||\<cal-S\><rsub|e,u><around|(|P,r,p|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>e<rsup|2*\<eta\>+s*\<eta\>>*C<rsub|\<eta\>><rsup|s>*A<rsub|0>*L<rsup|-<around|(|e+s|)>>*<big|sum><rsub|l\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|L>>g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|s>*\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-j>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>81*e<rsup|2*\<eta\>+s*\<eta\>>*C<rsub|\<eta\>><rsup|s>*A<rsub|0>*L<rsup|-<around|(|e+s|)>>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-spread-supremum>>>>>
    </eqsplit>
  </equation>

  The second line follows from <math|g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|s>\<leqslant\>\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|s>>
  and <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|s-j>\<leqslant\>\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-2>>.

  Applying Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-uniform-strip-extraction> to
  the family indexed by <math|<around|(|P,r|)>> gives a common width
  <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0> and a cost <math|C<rsub|e,s>*L<rsup|-<around|(|e+s|)>>>,
  where

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|e,s>=81*e<rsup|2*\<eta\>+s*\<eta\>>*C<rsub|\<eta\>><rsup|s>*A<rsub|0><around*|(|<frac|2*<around|(|1+b|)>|b>|)><rsup|4>
  </equation*>

  for any fixed <math|b\<gtr\>0> with <math|2*b\<less\>\<eta\>>, taking
  <math|h=\<sigma\>=b> in that lemma. Thus each row of the cross-kernel
  obtained by Fourier coefficient extraction has strip cost
  <math|C<rsub|e,s>/L<rsup|e+s>>, uniformly in <math|P>, the torus residue,
  and the period.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Residue differences are the true cover
    differences><label|lem:ex-lattice-residue-difference>For a coefficient
    row <math|K> on <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|L>\<times\>\<cal-C\>>, define

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|\<nabla\><rsub|a><rsup|L>*K|)><around|(|r,i,j,z|)>=K*<around|(|r+\<pi\><rsub|L><around|(|e<rsub|a>|)>,i,j,z-c<rsub|a><around|(|r|)>|)>-K*<around|(|r,i,j,z|)>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|c<rsub|a><around|(|r|)>> is the centered-lift carry
    characterized in the proof below. For a word <math|u>, define the
    iterated operator recursively by <math|\<nabla\><rsub|\<emptyset\>><rsup|L>=<math-up|id>>
    and <math|\<nabla\><rsub|<around|(|a,u|)>><rsup|L>=\<nabla\><rsub|a><rsup|L>*\<nabla\><rsub|u><rsup|L>>.
    The real Fourier coefficient rows defined above obey

    <\equation*>
      K<rsub|e,u>=\<nabla\><rsub|u><rsup|L>*K<rsub|e,\<emptyset\>>.
    </equation*>

    Under the preceding analytic and overlap hypotheses, the order-<math|s>
    difference row has width cost <math|C<rsub|e,s>*L<rsup|-<around|(|e+s|)>>>
    whenever <math|s=<around|\||u|\|>\<leqslant\>j-2>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The centered lift has a unique carry <math|c<rsub|a><around|(|r|)>\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>
    such that

    <\equation*>
      \<ell\><rsub|L>*<around|(|r+\<pi\><rsub|L><around|(|e<rsub|a>|)>|)>+L*c<rsub|a><around|(|r|)>=\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|r|)>+e<rsub|a>.
    </equation*>

    For odd <math|L>, <math|c<rsub|a><around|(|r|)>> is <math|e<rsub|a>>
    precisely at the upper centered boundary in coordinate <math|a>, and is
    zero otherwise. In the symbol <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-spread-symbol>,
    multiplication by the new factor <math|e<rsup|i*q<rsub|l><around|(|p|)><rsub|a>>-1>
    gives

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-S\><rsub|e,<around|(|a,u|)>><around|(|P,r,p|)>=e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\>c<rsub|a><around|(|r|)>>*\<cal-S\><rsub|e,u><around|(|P,r+\<pi\><rsub|L><around|(|e<rsub|a>|)>,p|)>-\<cal-S\><rsub|e,u><around|(|P,r,p|)>.
    </equation*>

    To check the phase, use <math|L*q<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>=p+2*\<pi\>*\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)>>
    and <math|e<rsup|2*\<pi\>*i*\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)>\<cdot\>c<rsub|a><around|(|r|)>>=1>;
    the change from <math|\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|r|)>> to
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|L>*<around|(|r+\<pi\><rsub|L><around|(|e<rsub|a>|)>|)>>
    contributes exactly <math|e<rsup|i*q<rsub|l><around|(|p|)><rsub|a>>*e<rsup|-i*p\<cdot\>c<rsub|a><around|(|r|)>>>.
    This is the Fourier transform of the displacement reindexing by
    <math|c<rsub|a><around|(|r|)>> in the definition of
    <math|\<nabla\><rsub|a><rsup|L>>. Fourier coefficients are unique for
    these absolutely summable rows, so the coefficient identity follows.
    Repeating this one-step argument proves the formula for every word.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The actual wire receipt>

  The preceding estimates apply to the construction's actual dual
  cross-stencil <math|F*<around|(|P,p,l|)>>. Its suppliers give the following
  exact data. Let <math|j,t\<geqslant\>4>, <math|k\<gtr\>0>, let
  <math|\<cal-I\>> be any index set, choose a flight <math|P<rsub|i>> of
  stages <math|<around|(|j,t|)>> and a site
  <math|l<rsub|i>\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|L<rsub|i>>> for each
  <math|i\<in\>\<cal-I\>>. There are common <math|\<eta\>,A\<gtr\>0> such
  that every scalar matrix entry of <math|F*<around|(|P<rsub|i>,p,l<rsub|i>|)>>
  is holomorphic on <math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|\<eta\>>> and bounded there by
  <math|A*\<Gamma\><rsub|L<rsub|i>><around|(|l<rsub|i>|)><rsup|-j>>,
  uniformly in the indices and the period. The thread also obeys the precise
  reciprocal-shift law

  <\equation>
    F*<around|(|P,p+2*\<pi\>*x,l|)>=F*<around|(|P,p,l+<math-up|wrap><rsub|L><around|(|x|)>|)>,<label|eq:ex-lattice-wire-overlap>
  </equation>

  whenever both momenta lie in the supplier's real enlarged cube. Here
  <math|<math-up|wrap><rsub|L><around|(|x|)>> is the residue characterized by
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|L>*<around|(|l+<math-up|wrap><rsub|L><around|(|x|)>|)>\<equiv\>\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)>+x<pmod|L*\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>>.
  The formal supplier proves these statements for every odd side
  <math|L\<geqslant\>3>, every flight with the two stage indices at least
  <math|4>, and every <math|k\<gtr\>0>. Their factor-by-factor proof is
  recorded in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-free-kernel-components>;
  the present section proves the quantitative consequences of those exact
  supplier estimates.

  Here is the branch algebra in mathematical notation. Let
  <math|m<rsub|P><around|(|p|)>=<math-up|area><rsub|L><around|(|p|)><rsup|j>>
  be the patch mass factor (distinct from the cumulative smooth factor of the
  train), <math|b<rsub|v><around|(|p|)>> the vector of four bulb factors for
  a train <math|v>, and <math|\<beta\><rsub|v><around|(|p|)>> its bend
  factor. For a vector <math|g> indexed by the four coordinate directions,
  let <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|r><around|(|g|)>> be the diagonal matrix whose
  entry on an <math|r>-face is the product of the <math|g<rsub|a>> over its
  directions. Let

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-T\><rsub|2><around|(|x|)><rsub|f*f>=<big|sum><rsub|a\<in\>f>x<rsub|a>,<space|2em><math-up|yen><around|(|g|)><around|(|p|)>=g<around|(|p|)>*g*<around|(|-p|)>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|v\<in\>\<bbb-C\><rsup|4>>, let
  <math|<math-ss|W><rsub|r><around|(|v|)>> denote the map of exterior
  multiplication <math|\<alpha\>\<mapsto\>v\<wedge\>\<alpha\>> from
  <math|r>-faces to <math|<around|(|r+1|)>>-faces. Put

  <\equation*>
    \<Sigma\><rsub|S><rsup|r><around|(|p|)>=<math-ss|W><rsub|r><around*|(|<around|(|S*<around|(|e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>-1|)>|)><rsub|a=1><rsup|4>|)>,<space|1em>\<cal-P\><rsub|v><around|(|p|)>=\<beta\><rsub|v><around|(|p|)><rsup|-1>*\<cal-M\><rsub|2><around|(|b<rsub|v><around|(|p|)><rsup|-1>|)>,\<cal-V\><rsub|v><around|(|p|)>=\<beta\><rsub|v><around|(|p|)>*\<cal-M\><rsub|2><around|(|b<rsub|v><around|(|p|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Write <math|q<rsub|l>=<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)>|)>/L>,
  including <math|q<rsub|0>> at the zero residue. Write
  <math|r<rsub|L><around|(|q|)><rsub|a>=<math-up|rail><rsub|L><around|(|q<rsub|a>|)>>.
  The <math|r>-flux and latch matrices are

  <\equation*>
    \<Phi\><rsub|P,r>*<around|(|p,l|)>=<around*|[|m<rsub|P>*<around|(|-q<rsub|l>|)>*\<cal-M\><rsub|r>*<around|(|r<rsub|L>*<around|(|-q<rsub|l>|)>|)>|]><rsup|<math-ss|T>>,<space|2em>\<Lambda\><rsub|P,r><around|(|p|)>=m<rsub|P><around|(|q<rsub|0>|)><rsup|-1>*\<cal-M\><rsub|r><around|(|r<rsub|L><around|(|q<rsub|0>|)><rsup|-1>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The nonzero-residue port vector and scalar are

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|P,l,a><around|(|p|)>=<frac|b<rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>><around|(|q<rsub|l>|)><rsub|a>|<math-up|blow><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>.*m*a*s*s,L><around|(|p,l|)>>,<space|2em>R<rsub|P,l><around|(|p|)>=<math-up|blow><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>.*m*a*s*s,L><around|(|p,l|)><rsup|2>*\<beta\><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>><around|(|q<rsub|l>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The land divisor is <math|\<frak-d\><rsub|P><around|(|p|)>=<math-up|divisor><around|(|P<rsub|<math-up|land>>.*m*a*s*s|)><around|(|p|)>>,
  and the tail vector is

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|tail><rsub|P,a><around|(|p|)>=k<rsup|-1>+<big|sum><rsub|l\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|L>,<space|0.17em>l\<ne\>0><math-up|yen><space|-0.17em><around*|(|m<rsub|P>|(>\<cdot\><around|)|r<rsub|L>|(>\<cdot\><around*|)|<rsub|a>|)><around|(|q<rsub|l>|)>*D<rsub|P,l,a><around|(|p|)>.
  </equation*>

  Further set

  <\equation*>
    h<rsub|P><around|(|p|)>=\<Lambda\><rsub|P,2><around|(|p|)>*\<Sigma\><rsub|1><rsup|1><around|(|p|)>,<space|1em>f<rsub|P><around|(|p|)>=<frac|<math-up|yen><around|(|m<rsub|P>|)><around|(|q<rsub|0>|)>*<space|0.17em>\<beta\><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|land>>><around|(|p|)>|\<beta\><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>><around|(|q<rsub|0>|)>>,<space|1em>M<rsub|P><around|(|p|)>=\<cal-M\><rsub|1><around|(|b<rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|land>>><around|(|p|)>|)>,
  </equation*>

  and, with <math|x<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>=<math-up|tail><rsub|P,a><around|(|p|)>/b<rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|land>>><around|(|p|)><rsub|a>>,

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|P><around|(|p|)>=\<cal-P\><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>><around|(|q<rsub|0>|)>*<around*|(|\<cal-T\><rsub|2><around|(|x<around|(|p|)>|)>-\<frak-d\><rsub|P><around|(|p|)>*\<cal-M\><rsub|2><around|(|x<around|(|p|)>|)>|)>,
  </equation*>

  <\equation*>
    N<rsub|P><around|(|p|)>=\<frak-d\><rsub|P><around|(|p|)><rsup|2>*I-\<frak-d\><rsub|P><around|(|p|)>*\<cal-P\><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|land>>><around|(|p|)>*\<Sigma\><rsub|1><rsup|1><around|(|p|)>*M<rsub|P><around|(|p|)>*\<Sigma\><rsub|1><rsup|1>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>.
  </equation*>

  For a nonzero alias <math|l>, let <math|D<rsub|P,l>> be the vector with
  components <math|D<rsub|P,l,a>> above and set
  <math|Y<rsub|P,l><around|(|p|)>=<math-up|yen><around|(|m<rsub|P>|)><around|(|q<rsub|l>|)>*\<beta\><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|land>>><around|(|p|)>/R<rsub|P,l><around|(|p|)>>.
  The dual stencil is exactly

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|W<rsub|P,0><around|(|p|)>>|<cell|=f<rsub|P><around|(|p|)>*\<Phi\><rsub|P,2>*<around|(|p,0|)>+<around*|(|B<rsub|P><around|(|p|)>*h<rsub|P><around|(|p|)>*M<rsub|P><around|(|p|)>-\<cal-P\><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>><around|(|q<rsub|0>|)>*h<rsub|P><around|(|p|)>*\<cal-M\><rsub|1><around|(|<math-up|tail><rsub|P><around|(|p|)>|)>|)>*\<Sigma\><rsub|1><rsup|1>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-wire-zero>>>|<row|<cell|W<rsub|P,l><around|(|p|)>>|<cell|=\<cal-P\><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>><around|(|q<rsub|l>|)>*\<Sigma\><rsub|L><rsup|1><around|(|q<rsub|l>|)>*\<cal-M\><rsub|1><around|(|D<rsub|P,l><around|(|p|)>|)>*\<Phi\><rsub|P,1>*<around|(|p,l|)>*\<Sigma\><rsub|1><rsup|1>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>+Y<rsub|P,l><around|(|p|)>*\<Phi\><rsub|P,2>*<around|(|p,l|)>*N<rsub|P><around|(|p|)>,<space|1em>l\<ne\>0.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-wire-nonzero>>>>>
  </align>

  These identities follow by expanding the definitions of the two branches
  and the listed auxiliary matrices; they are not additional hypotheses.

  Write <math|<math-up|chip><around|(|z|)>=e<rsup|i*z>-1> and
  <math|<math-up|pullLine><rsub|P,a><around|(|q|)>=m<rsub|P><around|(|q|)><math-up|rail><rsub|L><around|(|q<rsub|a>|)>>,
  where <math|<math-up|rail><rsub|L><around|(|z|)>=<around|(|e<rsup|i*L*z>-1|)>/<around|(|L*<around|(|e<rsup|i*z>-1|)>|)>>
  with its removable values filled in. The centered scalar factor
  <math|<math-up|area><rsub|L><around|(|q|)>> is the product of the four
  centered one-dimensional block averages. For a scalar symbol <math|f>,
  write <math|<math-up|yen><around|(|f|)><around|(|q|)>=f<around|(|q|)>*f*<around|(|-q|)>>.

  The response-space notation in those formulas is as follows. For a train
  <math|v> and side <math|S>, set <math|<math-up|fieldD><rsub|S,v><around|(|p|)>=\<cal-M\><rsub|1>*<around|(|S<rsup|-2>*<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|v><around|(|p|)>|)>>,
  where <math|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|v>> is the vector of level
  responses. The hinge matrix is <math|<math-up|hinge><rsub|P,r><around|(|q|)>=m<rsub|P><around|(|q|)>*\<cal-M\><rsub|r><around|(|r<rsub|L><around|(|q|)>|)>>;
  the flux is <math|\<Phi\><rsub|P,r>*<around|(|p,l|)>=<math-up|hinge><rsub|P,r><around|(|-q<rsub|l>|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>>;
  and <math|<math-up|mirage><rsub|1,1><around|(|p|)>=\<Sigma\><rsub|1><rsup|1>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>>.

  Here is the exact reduction behind the branch calculations. For a train
  <math|v> of mass side <math|M>, direction <math|a>, and momentum <math|p>,
  the level response is

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|v,a><around|(|p|)>>|<cell|=<math-up|feed><rsub|v,a><around*|(|<around|(|<math-up|divisor><rsub|1>|)><rsup|-1>|)><around|(|p|)>+k<rsup|-1><math-up|noise><rsub|v,a><around|(|p|)>,>>|<row|<cell|<math-up|feed><rsub|v,a><around|(|f|)><around|(|p|)>>|<cell|=M<rsup|-2>*<big|sum><rsub|r\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|M>>D<rsub|v,a><around|(|q<rsub|r><rsup|<around|(|M|)>><around|(|p|)>|)>*D<rsub|v,a>*<around|(|-q<rsub|r><rsup|<around|(|M|)>><around|(|p|)>|)>*f<around|(|q<rsub|r><rsup|<around|(|M|)>><around|(|p|)>|)>.>>>>
  </align*>

  The feed is the normalized finite alias sum of the two-sided drive
  <math|D<rsub|v,a><around|(|q|)>*D<rsub|v,a>*<around|(|-q|)>> times its
  input, and the noise is the sum of the constant-input feeds along the
  finite nozzle history. Every train drive is reciprocal-periodic. The
  reciprocal divisor input is periodic because its chip factors are invariant
  under integer reciprocal shifts. A finite alias sum of a periodic summand
  is periodic: a reciprocal shift permutes its alias sites. More explicitly,
  for a hop of side <math|L> from a train of mass side <math|M>, the
  mixed-radix alias bijection <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|L*M>\<to\>\<Lambda\><rsub|L>\<times\>\<Lambda\><rsub|M>>
  is determined by <math|\<ell\><rsub|L*M><around|(|r|)>\<equiv\>\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)>+L*\<ell\><rsub|M><around|(|v|)><pmod|L*M>>,
  with the unique centered representatives differing by a multiple of
  <math|L*M> absorbed into a reciprocal shift. This is a bijection because
  every residue modulo <math|L*M> has a unique decomposition into a residue
  modulo <math|L> and then a residue modulo <math|M> after division by
  <math|L>. Thus <math|q<rsub|r><rsup|<around|(|L*M|)>><around|(|p|)>=q<rsub|v><rsup|<around|(|M|)>><around|(|q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|L|)>><around|(|p|)>|)>>
  modulo <math|2*\<pi\>*\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>. Reindexing this bijection in the
  normalized feed sum and using <math|<around|(|L*M|)><rsup|-2>=L<rsup|-2>*M<rsup|-2>>
  gives

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|feed><rsub|<math-up|hop>><around|(|f|)>=<math-up|slide><rsub|L><around|(|<math-up|feed><rsub|v><around|(|f|)>|)>,<space|2em>D<rsub|<math-up|hop>,a><around|(|z|)>=<math-up|pullLine><rsub|P,a><around|(|M*z|)>*D<rsub|v,a><around|(|z|)>.
  </equation*>

  The nozzle list has a top entry with constant drive, contributing <math|1>;
  each earlier entry is the lifted nozzle list of the predecessor train.
  Reindexing the same aliases gives <math|<math-up|Noise><rsub|<math-up|hop>>=1+<math-up|slide><rsub|L><space|0.17em><math-up|Noise><rsub|v>>.
  Consequently <math|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|<math-up|hop>,a>=<math-up|slide><rsub|L,a><with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|v,a>+k<rsup|-1>>,
  the response feedback used below. Induction on the finite nozzle length
  proves reciprocal periodicity of every <math|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|v,a>>.
  The level scalar

  <\equation*>
    \<rho\><rsub|v><around|(|p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a><frac|J<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|v,a><around|(|p|)>>,<space|2em>J<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>=<around|(|e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>-1|)>*<around|(|e<rsup|-i*p<rsub|a>>-1|)>,
  </equation*>

  is periodic because each <math|J<rsub|a>> and each
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|v,a>> is periodic. For nonzero real
  <math|p> in the real enlarged cube (coordinate radius
  <math|\<pi\>+\<pi\>/4>; see below) we have
  <math|<math-up|divisor><rsub|M><around|(|p|)>=4*M<rsup|2>*<big|sum><rsub|a>sin<rsup|2><around|(|p<rsub|a>/2*M|)>\<neq\>0>,
  since every coordinate satisfies <math|<around|\||p<rsub|a>|\|>\<less\>2*\<pi\>\<leqslant\>2*\<pi\>*M>.
  For such <math|p> we have the level response identity
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|v,a>=b<rsub|v,a>/<math-up|divisor><rsub|M><around|(|p|)>>
  and the recovery identity <math|\<rho\><rsub|v>=<math-up|divisor><rsub|M><around|(|p|)><rsup|2>*\<beta\><rsub|v><around|(|p|)>>,
  where <math|<math-up|divisor><rsub|M>=d<rsub|M>> and <math|b<rsub|v,a>>,
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|v>> are the bulb and bend of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-free-kernel-components>. Indeed,
  in the feed of <math|<around|(|<math-up|divisor><rsub|1>|)><rsup|-1>> the
  zero residue has <math|q<rsub|0><rsup|<around|(|M|)>><around|(|p|)>=p/M>
  and contributes <math|M<rsup|-2>*D<rsub|v,a>*<around|(|p/M|)>*D<rsub|v,a>*<around|(|-p/M|)>/d<rsub|1>*<around|(|p/M|)>=\<ell\><rsub|v,a><around|(|p|)>/d<rsub|M><around|(|p|)>>,
  because <math|M<rsup|2>*d<rsub|1>*<around|(|p/M|)>=d<rsub|M><around|(|p|)>>;
  the nonzero residues contribute exactly the high-alias term
  <math|H<rsub|v,a><around|(|p|)>>. The noise term is
  <math|k<rsup|-1>*N<rsub|v,a><around|(|p|)>>. Hence
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|v,a>=\<ell\><rsub|v,a>/d<rsub|M>+G<rsub|v,a>=b<rsub|v,a>/d<rsub|M>>.
  Summing <math|J<rsub|a>/<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|v,a>=d<rsub|M>*J<rsub|a>/b<rsub|v,a>>
  over <math|a> and using the bend identity
  <math|d<rsub|M>*\<beta\><rsub|v>=<big|sum><rsub|a>J<rsub|a>/b<rsub|v,a>>
  proved in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-train-component-bounds>
  gives <math|\<rho\><rsub|v>=d<rsub|M><rsup|2>*\<beta\><rsub|v>>.
  Substituting these identities in the definitions of pulse and vault gives

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|pulse><rsub|v><around|(|p|)>=\<rho\><rsub|v><around|(|p|)><rsup|-1>*\<cal-M\><rsub|2><around|(|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|v><around|(|p|)><rsup|-1>|)>,<space|2em><math-up|vault><rsub|v><around|(|p|)>=\<rho\><rsub|v><around|(|p|)>*\<cal-M\><rsub|2><around|(|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|v><around|(|p|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Thus pulses are reciprocal-periodic on every nonzero overlap. A nonzero
  reciprocal shift cannot have both endpoints in the small real cube while
  one endpoint is zero; the elementary overlap separation inequality proves
  this. These are the level-response and pulse identities used below, not
  periodicity of the unscaled divisor <math|<math-up|divisor><rsub|M>> (which
  is not periodic when <math|M\<gtr\>1>).

  For clarity, the branch algebra is verified here. All products below are
  matrix products in the indicated face spaces. At a nonzero alias write
  <math|q=q<rsub|l>>, <math|s=\<frak-d\><rsub|P><around|(|p|)>>,
  <math|D=\<cal-M\><rsub|1><around|(|D<rsub|P,l>|)>>,
  <math|D<rprime|'>=\<cal-M\><rsub|1><around|(|b<rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|land>>><around|(|p|)>|)>>,
  and abbreviate the two order-one signals and the order-one/order-two fluxes
  by their displayed symbols. The port definitions and the divisor recovery
  identity give

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|chainY><rsub|P><around|(|p,q|)>=Y<rsub|P,l><around|(|p|)>*<space|0.17em>\<frak-d\><rsub|P><around|(|p|)><rsup|2>,<space|2em><math-up|portD><rsub|P,l,a><around|(|p|)>=L<rsup|-2>*<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>,a><around|(|q<rsub|l>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The scalar on the left is <math|m<rsub|P><around|(|q|)>*m<rsub|P>*<around|(|-q|)>*\<rho\><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|land>>><around|(|p|)>/<around|(|L<rsup|4>*\<rho\><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>><around|(|q|)>|)>>.
  Divisor recovery gives <math|\<rho\><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|land>>>=s<rsup|2>*\<beta\><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|land>>>>
  and <math|L<rsup|4>*\<rho\><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>><around|(|q|)>=R<rsub|P,l>>,
  giving the first equality. The second follows by cancelling the blow-up
  denominator in the port vector. For the next calculation put

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|cloud><rsub|P>=\<cal-P\><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|land>>><around|(|p|)>*\<Sigma\><rsub|1><rsup|1><around|(|p|)>*\<cal-M\><rsub|1><around|(|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|land>>><around|(|p|)>|)>*\<Sigma\><rsub|1><rsup|1>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>.
  </equation*>

  Also

  <\equation*>
    N<rsub|P>=s<rsup|2>*I-s*<space|0.17em>\<cal-P\><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|land>>><around|(|p|)>*\<Sigma\><rsub|1><rsup|1><around|(|p|)>*D<rprime|'>*\<Sigma\><rsub|1><rsup|1>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>,<space|2em>N<rsub|P>=s<rsup|2>*<around|(|I-<math-up|cloud><rsub|P>|)>.
  </equation*>

  For the first identity use <math|s<math-up|fieldD><rsub|1,P<rsub|<math-up|land>>>=\<cal-M\><rsub|1><around|(|b<rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|land>>>|)>>
  in the definition of the cloud; the second follows from the mouth identity
  and the definition of the notch. Substituting these identities in the
  nonzero branch, and using <math|Y<rsub|P,l>*s<rsup|2>=<math-up|chainY><rsub|P>>,
  gives exactly

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-P\><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>><around|(|q|)>*\<Sigma\><rsub|L><rsup|1><around|(|q|)>*D*\<Phi\><rsub|P,1>*\<Sigma\><rsub|1><rsup|1>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>+<math-up|chainY><rsub|P><around|(|p,q|)>*\<Phi\><rsub|P,2>*<around|(|p,l|)>*<around|(|I-<math-up|cloud><rsub|P><around|(|p|)>|)>=W<rsub|P,l><around|(|p|)>.
  </equation*>

  This is the stake branch identity, including its order of matrix factors.

  The heel is, by definition,

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|heel><rsub|P,a><around|(|p|)>=<math-up|yen><around|(|<math-up|pullLine><rsub|P,a>|)><around|(|q<rsub|0>|)>*b<rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>,a><around|(|q<rsub|0>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The relevant one-step identity for this response is

  <\equation*>
    <with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|land>>,a><around|(|p|)>=<math-up|slide><rsub|P,a><with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>,a><around|(|p|)>+k<rsup|-1>,<space|2em><around|(|<math-up|slide><rsub|P,a><space|0.17em>g|)><around|(|p|)>=L<rsup|-2>*<big|sum><rsub|l\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|L>><math-up|yen><around|(|<math-up|pullLine><rsub|P,a>|)><around|(|q<rsub|l>|)>*g<around|(|q<rsub|l>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Use the port-to-response equality <math|<math-up|portD><rsub|P,l,a>=L<rsup|-2>*<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>,a><around|(|q<rsub|l>|)>>
  in this finite sum. The zero-residue summand is
  <math|<math-up|heel><rsub|P,a><around|(|p|)>/s>, by the zero-port identity
  <math|<math-up|blow><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>.*m*a*s*s,L><around|(|p,0|)>=s>;
  the remaining residues sum to the above-alias tail. Adding
  <math|k<rsup|-1>> gives <math|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|land>>,a>=<math-up|heel><rsub|P,a>/s+<math-up|tail><rsub|P,a>>.
  Since off the origin <math|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|land>>,a>=b<rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|land>>,a>/s>,
  multiplication by <math|s> proves the bulb network identity

  <\equation*>
    b<rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|land>>,a><around|(|p|)>=<math-up|heel><rsub|P,a><around|(|p|)>+s<math-up|tail><rsub|P,a><around|(|p|)>.
  </equation*>

  For the zero branch write <math|q=q<rsub|0>>,
  <math|s=\<frak-d\><rsub|P><around|(|p|)>>, <math|H> and <math|H<rprime|'>>
  for the wing and land pulses at <math|q> and <math|p>,
  <math|d=\<Sigma\><rsub|1><rsup|1><around|(|p|)>>,
  <math|\<delta\>=\<Sigma\><rsub|L><rsup|1><around|(|q|)>>,
  <math|v=\<Phi\><rsub|P,2>*<around|(|p,0|)>>,
  <math|u=\<Phi\><rsub|P,1>*<around|(|p,0|)>>, and
  <math|m=<math-up|mirage><rsub|1,1><around|(|p|)>>. Let
  <math|f<rsub|1>=<math-up|hinge><rsub|P,1><around|(|q|)>> and
  <math|f<rsub|2>=<math-up|hinge><rsub|P,2><around|(|q|)>>,
  <math|\<lambda\>=\<Lambda\><rsub|P,2><around|(|p|)>>,
  <math|R=\<cal-M\><rsub|1><around|(|<math-up|tail><rsub|P><around|(|p|)>|)>>,
  <math|B=B<rsub|P><around|(|p|)>>, <math|D=<math-up|fieldD><rsub|L,P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>><around|(|q|)>>,
  <math|D<rprime|'>=<math-up|fieldD><rsub|1,P<rsub|<math-up|land>>><around|(|p|)>>,
  and <math|y=f<rsub|P><around|(|p|)>>. These symbols will be used in the
  brace, one-form, and cancellation identities below. For the brace relation,
  abbreviate the land and wing bulb vectors at their respective arguments by
  <math|f> and <math|g>, the two patch masses by
  <math|c<rsub|0>=m<rsub|P><around|(|q<rsub|0>|)>> and
  <math|d<rsub|0>=m<rsub|P>*<around|(|-q<rsub|0>|)>>, and the corresponding
  rails by <math|E<rsub|a>=<math-up|rail><rsub|L><around|(|q<rsub|0,a>|)>>
  and <math|F<rsub|a>=<math-up|rail><rsub|L><around|(|-q<rsub|0,a>|)>>. Put
  <math|Z<rsub|a>=<math-up|heel><rsub|P,a><around|(|p|)>/f<rsub|a>> and
  <math|x<rsub|a>=<math-up|tail><rsub|P,a><around|(|p|)>/f<rsub|a>>. The
  definitions of heel and the port-to-response formula give, coordinate by
  coordinate,

  <\equation*>
    g<rsub|a><rsup|-1>*Z<rsub|a>=c<rsub|0>*d<rsub|0>*<space|0.17em>F<rsub|a>*f<rsub|a><rsup|-1>*E<rsub|a>.
  </equation*>

  Inserting this in the diagonal two-form pulse and multiplying by the land
  pulse, two-form flux, and two-form hinge gives
  <math|y*<space|0.17em>v*H<rprime|'>*f<rsub|2>=H*\<cal-M\><rsub|2><around|(|Z|)>>.
  The bulb network identity gives <math|Z<rsub|a>=1-s*x<rsub|a>>. On a
  two-face <math|<around|{|a,b|}>>, direct multiplication yields

  <\equation*>
    1-<around|(|1-s*x<rsub|a>|)>*<around|(|1-s*x<rsub|b>|)>=s*<around|(|x<rsub|a>+x<rsub|b>-s*x<rsub|a>*x<rsub|b>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|2><around|(|x|)>> has diagonal entry
  <math|x<rsub|a>+x<rsub|b>> and <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|2><around|(|x|)>> has
  entry <math|x<rsub|a>*x<rsub|b>>, this is the matrix identity
  <math|I-\<cal-M\><rsub|2>*<around|(|1-s*x|)>=s*<around|(|\<cal-T\><rsub|2><around|(|x|)>-s*\<cal-M\><rsub|2><around|(|x|)>|)>>.
  Multiplying by <math|H=\<cal-P\><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>><around|(|q<rsub|0>|)>>
  proves <math|s*B=H-y*v*H<rprime|'>*f<rsub|2>>, the brace relation used in
  the cancellation.

  For the one-form flux, each matrix entry of the hinge-flux product is
  diagonal in the coordinate direction. At the zero alias it equals

  <\equation*>
    <around*|(|f<rsub|1>*D*u|)><rsub|a*a>=<math-up|yen><around|(|m<rsub|P>|)><around|(|q<rsub|0>|)><math-up|rail><rsub|L><around|(|q<rsub|0,a>|)><math-up|rail><rsub|L><around|(|-q<rsub|0,a>|)><math-up|fieldD><rsub|L,P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>><around|(|q<rsub|0>|)><rsub|a>.
  </equation*>

  Because <math|<math-up|fieldD><rsub|L,P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>><around|(|q<rsub|0>|)><rsub|a>=b<rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>,a><around|(|q<rsub|0>|)>/s>,
  the right side is <math|<math-up|heel><rsub|P,a><around|(|p|)>/s>. The
  scalar toe-feed identity above says this equals
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|land>>,a><around|(|p|)>-<math-up|tail><rsub|P,a><around|(|p|)>>,
  proving <math|f<rsub|1>*D*u=D<rprime|'>-R> entry by entry. The equality
  <math|<math-up|chainY><rsub|P><around|(|p,q<rsub|0>|)>=f<rsub|P><around|(|p|)>>
  follows as follows. Divisor recovery gives
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|land>>>=s<rsup|2>*\<beta\><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|land>>>>
  and <math|\<rho\><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>>=d<rsub|w><rsup|2>*\<beta\><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>>>,
  where <math|d<rsub|w>=<math-up|divisor><around|(|P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>.*m*a*s*s|)><around|(|q<rsub|0>|)>>.
  The zero-port identity gives <math|s=L<rsup|2>*d<rsub|w>>. Since
  <math|<math-up|scaleR><rsub|1,P<rsub|<math-up|land>>>=\<rho\><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|land>>>>
  and <math|<math-up|scaleR><rsub|L,P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>>=L<rsup|4>*\<rho\><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>>>,
  their ratio is <math|\<beta\><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|land>>>/\<beta\><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>>>.
  Multiplying by <math|<math-up|yen><around|(|m<rsub|P>|)><around|(|q<rsub|0>|)>>
  gives both the chain and foot expressions.

  The bank and land-response identities are

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|mouth><rsub|P>=\<cal-M\><rsub|1><around|(|b<rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|land>>>|)>,<space|2em>s<math-up|fieldD><rsub|1,P<rsub|<math-up|land>>>=\<cal-M\><rsub|1><around|(|b<rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|land>>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|<math-up|mouth><rsub|P>=s*D<rprime|'>>. The latch inverse, hinge
  transport, and the order-one flux calculation give the identities

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f<rsub|2>*\<lambda\>=I,<space|2em>\<lambda\>*f<rsub|2>=I,>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|f<rsub|2>*\<delta\>>|<cell|=d*f<rsub|1>,>>|<row|<cell|f<rsub|1>*D*u>|<cell|=D<rprime|'>-R,>>|<row|<cell|s*B>|<cell|=H-y*<space|0.17em>v*H<rprime|'>*f<rsub|2>,<space|2em><math-up|mouth><rsub|P>=s*D<rprime|'>.>>>>
  </align*>

  The hinge-transport line follows entrywise from the alias relation
  <math|L<math-up|rail><rsub|L><around|(|q<rsub|0,a>|)><math-up|chip><around|(|q<rsub|0,a>|)>=<math-up|chip><around|(|p<rsub|a>|)>>,
  inserted into the definitions of the hinge and signal. The third line is
  the one-form identity obtained by multiplying the flux by its hinge; the
  fourth is the brace identity after the two-form flux calculation. The mouth
  identity was derived just above. Also <math|<math-up|chainY><rsub|P><around|(|p,q|)>=y>.
  These identities imply, with no commutation of noncommuting factors,

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|H*\<delta\>*D*u*<space|0.17em>m>|<cell|=H*\<lambda\>*d*f<rsub|1>*D*u*<space|0.17em>m=H*\<lambda\>*d*<around|(|D<rprime|'>-R|)>*m,>>|<row|<cell|B*\<lambda\>*d<math-up|mouth><rsub|P><space|0.17em>m-H*\<lambda\>*d*R*<space|0.17em>m>|<cell|=H*\<lambda\>*d*D<rprime|'>*m-y*v*H<rprime|'>*d*D<rprime|'>*m-H*\<lambda\>*d*R*<space|0.17em>m.>>>>
  </align*>

  For the first equality use <math|\<delta\>=\<lambda\>*d*f<rsub|1>> (which
  follows from <math|f<rsub|2>*\<delta\>=d*f<rsub|1>> and
  <math|\<lambda\>*f<rsub|2>=I>). For the second use
  <math|<math-up|mouth><rsub|P>=s*D<rprime|'>> and
  <math|s*B=H-y*v*H<rprime|'>*f<rsub|2>>, then cancel the adjacent
  <math|f<rsub|2>*\<lambda\>>. Adding <math|y*v> to the second line yields
  <math|H*\<lambda\>*d*<around|(|D<rprime|'>-R|)>*m+y*v*<around|(|I-H<rprime|'>*d*D<rprime|'>*m|)>>,
  and its first term is <math|H*\<delta\>*D*u*<space|0.17em>m>. This is
  precisely the zero-alias script formula and proves the copper branch
  identity. Each displayed identity is an equality of the finite matrices
  defined above; the component chapter supplies the scalar divisor, rail, and
  bend inputs on the common analytic cube.

  The reciprocal-shift law used in the Fourier argument also follows from the
  actual wire symbol, rather than being an independent periodicity
  assumption. For <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>> and a residue <math|l>, the
  centered alias arithmetic gives a cover vector <math|y> such that

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|l>*<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*x|)>=q<rsub|l+<math-up|wrap><rsub|L><around|(|x|)>><around|(|p|)>+2*\<pi\>*y.
  </equation*>

  Every factor of the script is invariant under these reciprocal shifts. The
  level responses are periodic by the finite-feed and nozzle induction above;
  hence their diagonal matrices and the scalar <math|\<rho\><rsub|v>> are
  periodic. The pulses are periodic on nonzero overlaps by their displayed
  formula in terms of <math|\<rho\><rsub|v>> and the level responses. The
  signals and the jewel factors are periodic because they are formed from
  <math|e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>-1> and its opposite-momentum partner. The patch
  mass is periodic by the plank identity. The scalar factor is exactly

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|chainY><rsub|P><around|(|p,q|)>=<math-up|yen><around|(|m<rsub|P>|)><around|(|q|)><space|0.17em><frac|\<rho\><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|land>>><around|(|p|)>|L<rsup|4>*\<rho\><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>><around|(|q|)>>.
  </equation*>

  It is periodic by the patch-mass and level-response identities. Define
  <math|<math-up|scriptT><rsub|P><around|(|p,q|)>> by the nonzero-branch
  display above, with <math|q> now an independent fine momentum: use
  <math|D=\<cal-M\><rsub|1>*<around|(|L<rsup|-2>*<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|P<rsub|<math-up|wing>>><around|(|q|)>|)>>
  and evaluate the reflected hinge factors at <math|-q>. The other factors in
  <math|<math-up|scriptT>> are level responses, pulses, signals, hinges, or
  fluxes already shown periodic. Substitution in its definition therefore
  yields <math|<math-up|scriptT><rsub|P><around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*x,q+2*\<pi\>*y|)>=<math-up|scriptT><rsub|P><around|(|p,q|)>>.
  For nonzero external momentum, the copper and stake branch identities just
  proved identify the wire with this script; the zero momentum endpoint
  follows directly from the definitions. Combining this script invariance
  with the residue permutation <math|l\<mapsto\>l+<math-up|wrap><rsub|L><around|(|x|)>>
  gives <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-wire-overlap>. More explicitly, when both
  external momenta lie in the real enlarged cube and <math|x\<ne\>0>, both
  are nonzero; the alias exchange above supplies the compensating fine
  reciprocal vector, and the shift identity for the script removes it. When
  <math|x=0> the claim is identity. This is the reciprocal-shift content of
  the wire matching argument. The real enlarged cube has coordinate radius
  <math|\<pi\>+<math-up|zone>>, where <math|<math-up|zone>=\<pi\>/4> is the
  padding. Since <math|\<pi\>+<math-up|zone>\<less\>2*\<pi\>>, no nonzero
  reciprocal vector can connect zero momentum to another point of this cube;
  this is the separation used above.

  The reality needed to recover the original symbol from the real coefficient
  row is a separate property from this overlap law. Entrywise complex
  conjugation does not transpose a matrix. For real momenta, the patch mass,
  train covariances and their scalar contractions are real and even. The
  exterior signal at <math|-p> is the entrywise conjugate of the signal at
  <math|p>; the same holds for its transposed partner and for the flux. The
  pulse has the same conjugation property. These statements follow directly
  from the opposite-momentum factors <math|e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>-1> and
  <math|e<rsup|-i*p<rsub|a>>-1>, the real even covariances, and the displayed
  pulse and flux formulas. Entrywise conjugation preserves sums and the order
  of matrix products. Applying it to each factor in the two script formulas
  therefore gives their values at <math|<around|(|-p,-q|)>>. For
  <math|p\<ne\>0>, the branch identities and
  <math|q<rsub|-l>*<around|(|-p|)>=-q<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>> give

  <\equation*>
    <wide|F*<around|(|P,p,l|)>|\<bar\>>=F*<around|(|P,-p,-l|)>.
  </equation*>

  At <math|p=0> this also holds: the zero-alias column is the real identity
  and the other columns vanish. The same argument gives this conjugation law
  for the one-form pilot column, using <eqref|eq:transport-A> and its
  zero-mode values. Conjugating <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-spread-symbol>, using
  <math|<wide|\<chi\><rsub|u><around|(|q|)>|\<bar\>>=\<chi\><rsub|u>*<around|(|-q|)>>
  and reindexing <math|l\<mapsto\>-l>, yields

  <\equation*>
    <wide|\<cal-S\><rsub|e,u><around|(|P,r,p|)>|\<bar\>>=\<cal-S\><rsub|e,u><around|(|P,r,-p|)>
  </equation*>

  for real <math|p> in the enlarged cube, for either column family. Its
  Fourier coefficients are consequently real by the calculation following
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-wire-width>. Thus the real coefficient row
  reconstructs the full periodic symbol, not merely its real part. This is
  the reality input used when transferring the symbol identities to real
  finite-torus transport matrices.

  The product estimates use the following weights, all uniform on a common
  complex cube and independent of <math|L>:

  <\padded-center>
    <tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-lborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-rborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-rborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|factor>|<cell|weight
    in the alias label <math|l>>>|<row|<cell|mass factor
    <math|m<rsub|P><around|(|q<rsub|l>|)>>>|<cell|<math|\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-j>>>>|<row|<cell|rail
    factors and reciprocal rails at the zero
    alias>|<cell|<math|1>>>|<row|<cell|dual multipliers, bend ratios at zero
    alias, correction and tail terms>|<cell|<math|1>>>|<row|<cell|scaled
    signal <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|L><rsup|1><around|(|q<rsub|l>|)>>>|<cell|<math|g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)>>>>|<row|<cell|port
    mask <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|1><around|(|D<rsub|P,l>|)>>>|<cell|<math|g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-2>>>>|<row|<cell|flux
    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|P,r>*<around|(|p,l|)>>>|<cell|<math|\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-j>>>>|<row|<cell|levy
    and notch at nonzero alias>|<cell|<math|1>>>>>>
  </padded-center>

  The component suppliers prove these bounds by the estimates listed in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-free-kernel-components>. For the
  zero branch every product has total weight <math|1>. In the first nonzero
  product the total weight is at most <math|g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)>*g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-2>*\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-j>\<leqslant\>\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-j>>;
  the second has weight <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-j>>. For
  matrix products, apply <math|<around|\||<around|(|A*B|)><rsub|i*j>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|r><around|\||A<rsub|i*r>|\|><around|\||B<rsub|r*j>|\|>>;
  every finite intermediate type sum contributes a fixed cardinality. Taking
  the larger of the two branch constants and the smaller of their widths
  yields the entrywise weighted analytic estimate stated above. The branch
  multiplication and the weights it must deliver are explicit here;
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-free-kernel-components> derives
  the component estimates used in that multiplication.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Wire symbol yields the tower's order-<math|e> strip
    receipt><label|thm:ex-lattice-wire-tower-strip>Fix stages
    <math|j,t\<geqslant\>4> with <math|t\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>t+1>, a
    coupling <math|k\<gtr\>0>, and an integer <math|e\<geqslant\>0>. Consider
    flights with odd coarse side <math|L\<geqslant\>3>, and suppose the
    entrywise weighted analytic bound and the reciprocal-shift overlap
    identity <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-wire-overlap> hold with common analytic
    width and cost over this flight family. Then there are
    <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0> and <math|C<rsub|e,j>\<gtr\>0>, independent of
    the flight, residue, and period, such that for every word <math|u> of
    length <math|s> with <math|s\<leqslant\>j-2>, the cross-kernel row
    <math|K<rsub|e,u>> has strip width <math|\<sigma\>> and cost
    <math|C<rsub|e,j>/L<rsup|e+s>> for every flight, residue, and fixed type
    pair. The width and cost may depend on the fixed stages and coupling, the
    input analytic width and cost, and <math|e>; no uniformity in these
    parameters is claimed. In particular <math|e=2> gives the tower component
    <math|T> the base cost <math|C<rsub|2,j>/L<rsup|2>> and each
    first-difference cost <math|C<rsub|2,j>/L<rsup|3>> required by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-cross-atlas>. For the one-form
    transport the weighted input is Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-primal-entrywise-bound>,
    with <math|e=1> and cost <math|C<rsub|1,j>*L<rsup|-<around|(|1+s|)>>>.
    Its overlap follows from <eqref|eq:transport-A>: the responses and the
    two-sided factors are reciprocal-periodic, and the alias sums are
    invariant under the induced residue permutation. A nontrivial shift on
    the common real overlap keeps both momenta away from zero, so the
    punctured formula suffices there. For the two-form transport take
    <math|e=2>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    First reduce the common analytic width to at most
    <math|<math-up|zone>=\<pi\>/4>. Then the real overlaps used below lie in
    the enlarged cube on which <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-wire-overlap> was proved.
    This reduction preserves all upper bounds and is independent of the
    flight and its side. For a fixed word <math|u> of length <math|s>, the
    finite-residue estimate <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-spread-supremum> gives a
    common analytic bound with factor <math|L<rsup|-<around|(|e+s|)>>>. To
    check the overlap, let <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>> and let
    <math|l<rprime|'>=l+<math-up|wrap><rsub|L><around|(|x|)>>. By the
    defining congruence for the wrap, there is
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>> such that

    <\equation*>
      q<rsub|l>*<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*x|)>=q<rsub|l<rprime|'>><around|(|p|)>+2*\<pi\>*n.
    </equation*>

    The chip factors and the exponential displacement factor are unchanged by
    this integral reciprocal shift, and the supplier law
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-wire-overlap> changes
    <math|F*<around|(|P,p+2*\<pi\>*x,l|)>> into
    <math|F*<around|(|P,p,l<rprime|'>|)>>. The map
    <math|l\<mapsto\>l<rprime|'>> is a permutation of the finite residue set,
    so reindexing the finite sum proves equality of the two row symbols on
    the real overlap. Holomorphic uniqueness extends the equality to the
    overlapping complex domain used for coefficient extraction.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-uniform-strip-extraction> therefore
    gives the strip cost <math|C<rsub|e,s>/L<rsup|e+s>>. There are only
    finitely many words in the four coordinate directions of length at most
    <math|j-2>. The width <math|\<sigma\>> is the same for every word; take
    the maximum of the finitely many costs <math|C<rsub|e,s>> over all words
    of length at most <math|j-2> to obtain one cost <math|C<rsub|e,j>>.
    Finally Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-residue-difference>,
    iterated along <math|u>, identifies the word's Fourier coefficient row
    with the iterated cover difference. This is exactly the stated receipt.
  </proof>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Remaining local analytic inputs>This proof discharges the passage
    from a uniform alias-weighted analytic bound to all required tower strip
    receipts, with explicit <math|L<rsup|-2-s>> scaling and the
    carry-corrected differences. Its mathematical dependence on the actual
    dual-kernel producer is restricted to the entrywise weighted analytic
    estimate of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-wire-entrywise-bound>
    and the component inequalities of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-free-kernel-components>,
    both proved there. In particular, the finite-residue geometry, scale
    powers, geometric coefficient summation, overlap gluing, and finite
    uniformization are proved here. To apply
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-response-atlas> to the selected
    response, one must additionally match the tower and its fixed data to
    that response and supply the two bed bounds required by the
    theorem.<label|rem:ex-lattice-free-kernel-inputs>
  </remark>

  <subsection|Component estimates for the dual wire
  symbol><label|sec:ex-lattice-free-kernel-components>

  This section supplies the factor bounds used in the dual-stencil
  calculation of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-free-kernel-estimates>.
  All constants below may depend on the fixed smoothing stages, the positive
  coupling parameter, and the four-dimensional cell conventions, but not on
  the odd side length or the alias. Write

  <\equation*>
    \<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)>=<big|prod><rsub|a=1><rsup|4><around|(|1+<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsub|a>|\|>|)>,<space|2em>g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)>=1+max<rsub|a><around|\||\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsub|a>|\|>.
  </equation*>

  The centered representative satisfies <math|g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)>\<leqslant\>\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)>>.
  For <math|z\<in\>\<bbb-C\>> define

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|rail><rsub|L><around|(|z|)>=<frac|1|L>*<big|sum><rsub|v=0><rsup|L-1>e<rsup|i*v*z>,<space|2em><math-up|plank><rsub|L><around|(|z|)>=e<rsup|-i*<around|\<lfloor\>|L/2|\<rfloor\>>*z><math-up|rail><rsub|L><around|(|z|)>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|p\<in\>\<bbb-C\><rsup|4>> set

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|area><rsub|L><around|(|p|)>=<big|prod><rsub|a=1><rsup|4><math-up|plank><rsub|L><around|(|p<rsub|a>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Set <math|<math-up|zone>=\<pi\>/4> and <math|R<rsub|0>=3*\<pi\>/2>. Since
  <math|<around|(|e<rsup|i*z>-1|)>/z> extends continuously and has no zero on
  the closed disk <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|0>>, compactness
  gives constants <math|0\<less\>\<ell\>\<leqslant\>h> such that

  <\equation*>
    \<ell\><around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||e<rsup|i*z>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>h<around|\||z|\|>*<space|2em><around|(|<around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|0>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Put <math|p<rsub|0>=min <around|(|1,\<ell\>/h|)>> and
  <math|c<rsub|0>=h*R<rsub|0>/p<rsub|0>>. These are the uniform rail/plank
  lower and principal-plank constants used below. In the divisor estimates
  write <math|<math-up|low>=\<ell\>> and <math|<math-up|high>=h>; for real
  four-vectors, <math|<around|\||p|\|>> denotes the Euclidean norm. At each
  stage <math|j>, a train is built by finite recursion. The top train
  <math|<math-up|top><rsub|j>> has mass side <math|1> and extra factor
  <math|1>. A hop has output stage <math|j>, coarse dilation an odd integer
  <math|L\<geqslant\>3>, and a predecessor train of stage
  <math|J\<in\><around|{|j,j+1|}>>; if the predecessor has mass side
  <math|M>, the hop has mass side <math|L*M> and extra factor
  <math|<math-up|area><rsub|L*M><around|(|p|)><rsup|J-j>> times the
  predecessor's extra factor. Thus every train is a finite history, and no
  bound on its number of hops is assumed. For a side <math|M>, set

  <\equation*>
    s<rsub|M><around|(|p|)><rsub|a>=M*<around*|(|e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>/M>-1|)>,<space|2em>d<rsub|M><around|(|p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a=1><rsup|4>s<rsub|M><around|(|p|)><rsub|a>*s<rsub|M>*<around|(|-p|)><rsub|a>.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|d<rsub|M>> is the divisor, and for real <math|p> it is the sum
  of the four squared seed magnitudes. A train <math|v> of stage <math|j> and
  mass side <math|M=v.*m*a*s*s> has cumulative filter
  <math|S<rsub|v><around|(|p|)>=<math-up|area><rsub|M><around|(|p|)><rsup|j>*v.*e*x*t*r*a<around|(|p|)>>;
  this is distinct from the patch mass factor
  <math|m<rsub|P><around|(|p|)>=<math-up|area><rsub|P.*L><around|(|p|)><rsup|P.*J>>
  used in the wire branches. A flight with indices <math|j,t\<geqslant\>4>
  consists of an odd coarse side <math|L\<geqslant\>3>, a wing train of stage
  <math|j>, and indices <math|t\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>t+1>; its land train
  is the hop of that wing with output stage <math|t>. Its patch mass is the
  separate factor <math|m<rsub|P>=<math-up|area><rsub|L><rsup|<space|0.17em>j>>.
  For a train of side <math|M> and direction <math|a>, define its drive, lid,
  and halo by

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|v,a><around|(|q|)>=S<rsub|v><around|(|q|)><math-up|rail><rsub|M><around|(|q<rsub|a>|)>,<space|1em>\<ell\><rsub|v,a><around|(|p|)>=D<rsub|v,a>*<around|(|p/M|)>*D<rsub|v,a>*<around|(|-p/M|)>,<space|1em>h<rsub|v><around|(|p|)>=S<rsub|v>*<around|(|p/M|)>*S<rsub|v>*<around|(|-p/M|)>.
  </equation*>

  For an alias side <math|L>, write <math|q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|L|)>><around|(|p|)>=<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)>|)>/L>.
  For a train of side <math|M>, set <math|<math-up|blow><rsub|M,L><around|(|p,l|)>=L<rsup|2>*d<rsub|M><around|(|q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|L|)>><around|(|p|)>|)>>.
  The lift bound <math|<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsub|a>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|L-1|)>/2>
  gives <math|q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|L|)>><around|(|\<cal-Q\><rsub|\<eta\>>|)>\<subset\>\<cal-Q\><rsub|\<eta\>>>.
  The feed with constant input one, the high-alias term, the noise drain,
  bulb, and bend are

  <\equation*>
    <\aligned>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|F<rsub|v,a><around|(|p|)>>|<cell|=M<rsup|-2>*<big|sum><rsub|l\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|M>>D<rsub|v,a><around|(|q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|M|)>><around|(|p|)>|)>*D<rsub|v,a>*<around|(|-q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|M|)>><around|(|p|)>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|H<rsub|v,a><around|(|p|)>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|l\<ne\>0><frac|D<rsub|v,a><around|(|q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|M|)>><around|(|p|)>|)>*D<rsub|v,a>*<around|(|-q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|M|)>><around|(|p|)>|)>|M<rsup|2>*d<rsub|1><around|(|q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|M|)>><around|(|p|)>|)>>,>>|<row|<cell|G<rsub|v,a><around|(|p|)>>|<cell|=H<rsub|v,a><around|(|p|)>+k<rsup|-1>*N<rsub|v,a><around|(|p|)>,>>|<row|<cell|b<rsub|v,a><around|(|p|)>>|<cell|=\<ell\><rsub|v,a><around|(|p|)>+d<rsub|M><around|(|p|)>*G<rsub|v,a><around|(|p|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<beta\><rsub|v><around|(|p|)>>|<cell|=h<rsub|v><around|(|p|)><rsup|-1>-<big|sum><rsub|a=1><rsup|4><frac|J<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>*G<rsub|v,a><around|(|p|)>|\<ell\><rsub|v,a><around|(|p|)>*b<rsub|v,a><around|(|p|)>>,<space|2em>J<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>=<around|(|e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>-1|)>*<around|(|e<rsup|-i*p<rsub|a>>-1|)>.>>>>
    </aligned>
  </equation*>

  The noise is defined from a list: the top train has empty list; a hop of
  coarse side <math|L> has as its list one top train of side one followed by
  the images of every entry of the predecessor's list under that same hop.
  For a list <math|\<cal-N\>>, its noise at direction <math|a> is the sum,
  over <math|u\<in\>\<cal-N\>>, of the feed

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-up|feed><rsub|u,a><around|(|p|)>>|<cell|=<frac|1|u.*m*a*s*s<rsup|2>>*<big|sum><rsub|l\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|u.*m*a*s*s>>D<rsub|u,a><around|(|q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|u.*m*a*s*s|)>><around|(|p|)>|)>*D<rsub|u,a>*<around|(|-q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|u.*m*a*s*s|)>><around|(|p|)>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|N<rsub|v,a><around|(|p|)>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|u\<in\>\<cal-N\><around|(|v|)>><math-up|feed><rsub|u,a><around|(|p|)>.>>>>
  </align*>

  In the high-alias term the divisor is the unit-side divisor
  <math|d<rsub|1>>, evaluated at the rescaled aliases
  <math|q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|M|)>><around|(|p|)>> and multiplied by
  <math|M<rsup|2>>; the port blow-up below uses the indicated train side
  <math|M>.

  <subsubsection|The one-dimensional alias estimate>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Rail and box decay><label|lem:ex-lattice-rail-alias-decay>There
    are constants <math|B> and <math|\<eta\><rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> such that, for
    every odd <math|L\<geqslant\>1>, every
    <math|0\<less\>\<eta\>\<leqslant\>\<eta\><rsub|0>>, every
    <math|p\<in\>\<cal-Q\><rsub|\<eta\>>>, and every centered residue
    <math|l>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||<math-up|rail><rsub|L><around|(|q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|L|)>><around|(|p|)><rsub|a>|)>|\|>,<around|\||<math-up|plank><rsub|L><around|(|q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|L|)>><around|(|p|)><rsub|a>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>B*<around|(|1+<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsub|a>|\|>|)><rsup|-1>.
    </equation*>

    At the zero residue the reciprocal rail is also uniformly bounded on a
    common smaller complex cube.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    If <math|\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsub|a>=0>, the imaginary part of
    <math|L*q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|L|)>><around|(|p|)><rsub|a>=p<rsub|a>>
    is bounded by <math|\<eta\>>, and the normalized finite geometric sum
    defining the rail has modulus at most <math|exp <around|(|\<eta\>|)>>;
    multiplication by the centered phase defining the plank costs at most
    another fixed exponential factor. If <math|\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsub|a>\<ne\>0>,
    the centered-lift estimate for aliases gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|\<pi\>/4|)>*<around|(|1+<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsub|a>|\|>|)>\<leqslant\>L<around|\||q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|L|)>><around|(|p|)><rsub|a>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|1+<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsub|a>|\|>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The lower bound uses that <math|<around|\||<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>p<rsub|a>|\|>\<less\>\<pi\>+\<eta\>>
    and the lift is a nonzero integer; the upper bound is the triangle
    inequality. On the fixed disk containing these momenta, the function
    <math|<around|(|e<rsup|i*z>-1|)>/z> is bounded above and bounded away
    from zero, with its value at zero defined by continuity. The identity

    <\equation*>
      L<space|0.17em><math-up|rail><rsub|L><around|(|z|)>*<around|(|e<rsup|i*z>-1|)>=e<rsup|i*L*z>-1
    </equation*>

    then bounds the rail by a constant divided by
    <math|L*<around|\||e<rsup|i*z>-1|\|>>, hence by the stated inverse lift
    weight. The plank differs from the rail by a centered phase whose modulus
    is bounded on the same complex cube. At the zero alias, for real
    <math|p<rsub|a>> with <math|<around|\||p<rsub|a>|\|>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|0>>,
    the same identity and the two-sided chip comparison give
    <math|<around|\||<math-up|rail><rsub|L><around|(|p<rsub|a>/L|)>|\|>\<geqslant\>\<ell\>/h>:
    apply the lower bound to <math|p<rsub|a>> and the upper bound to
    <math|p<rsub|a>/L>, with the value at zero obtained by continuity. The
    following quantitative continuation promotes this lower bound to a common
    complex cube, uniformly in the side. Suppose
    <math|C,c,w,\<eta\>,\<theta\>\<gtr\>0> and a holomorphic function has
    modulus at most <math|C*w> on a cube of width <math|\<eta\>> and modulus
    at least <math|c*w> on its real slice of width <math|\<theta\>>. At a
    complex point <math|p>, let <math|y=<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>p>. The
    one-variable Cauchy estimate along the complex line through <math|y> and
    <math|p> gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||f<around|(|p|)>-f<around|(|y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|4*C*w|\<eta\>>*<space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|p-y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>
    </equation*>

    provided <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|p-y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>/4>
    and the ball lies in the original cube. Indeed, for
    <math|d=<around|\<\|\|\>|p-y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<gtr\>0> apply
    Cauchy's derivative bound to <math|g<around|(|z|)>=f*<around|(|y+z*<around|(|p-y|)>/d|)>>
    on the disk <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<less\>\<eta\>/2>, where
    <math|<around|\||g|\|>\<leqslant\>C*w>. For
    <math|0\<leqslant\>z\<leqslant\>d> the disk of radius <math|\<eta\>/4>
    around <math|z> remains inside that domain, so
    <math|<around|\||g<rprime|'><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>4*C*w/\<eta\>>;
    integrate from <math|0> to <math|d>. The case <math|d=0> is immediate. On
    the smaller cube of width <math|r=min
    <around|(|\<eta\>/4,\<theta\>,c*\<eta\>/<around|(|8*C|)>|)>>, the
    radius-<math|\<eta\>/2> ball about <math|y> lies in the original cube,
    and <math|y> lies in the stated real slice. Consequently one has
    <math|<around|\||f<around|(|p|)>|\|>\<geqslant\><around|(|c/2|)>*w>. In
    particular the reciprocal is holomorphic there and bounded by
    <math|2/<around|(|c*w|)>>. Applying this with <math|w=1> to the
    zero-alias rail proves the asserted uniform reciprocal bound.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Principal plank estimate with unit
    coefficient><label|lem:ex-lattice-principal-plank>If <math|M> and
    <math|L> are positive integers with <math|M\<leqslant\>L>, <math|z> is
    real, and <math|L<around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|0>>, then

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||<math-up|plank><rsub|M><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<geqslant\>exp
      <around*|(|-c<rsub|0>*M/L|)>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The centered-sum formula is

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|plank><rsub|M><around|(|z|)>=<frac|1|M>*<big|sum><rsub|v=0><rsup|M-1>e<rsup|i*<around|(|v-<around|\<lfloor\>|M/2|\<rfloor\>>|)>*z>.
    </equation*>

    Each centered integer has modulus at most <math|M>. The chip upper bound
    therefore gives <math|<around|\||<math-up|plank><rsub|M><around|(|z|)>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>h*M<around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>h*R<rsub|0>*x>,
    where <math|x=M/L>. The rail-chip quotient and the two-sided chip
    comparison also give <math|<around|\||<math-up|plank><rsub|M><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<geqslant\>p<rsub|0>>
    because <math|M<around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|0>> and
    <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|0>>. Consequently
    <math|1\<leqslant\><around|\||<math-up|plank><rsub|M><around|(|z|)>|\|>+h*R<rsub|0>*x>
    by the triangle inequality. Since <math|e<rsup|c<rsub|0>*x>\<geqslant\>1+c<rsub|0>*x>
    and <math|<around|\||<math-up|plank><rsub|M><around|(|z|)>|\|>/p<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>1>,
    it follows that <math|e<rsup|c<rsub|0>*x><around|\||<math-up|plank><rsub|M><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<geqslant\><around|\||<math-up|plank><rsub|M><around|(|z|)>|\|>+h*R<rsub|0>*x\<geqslant\>1>.
    Rearranging proves the claimed estimate. The coefficient <math|c<rsub|0>>
    is fixed independently of the number of train steps; this is why its
    exponential loss can be summed by the geometric side-ratio argument.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Mass, signals, and nonzero-alias denominators>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Mass and flux weights><label|lem:ex-lattice-mass-flux-weight>For a
    flight <math|P> with patch side <math|L> and stage <math|j>, its patch
    mass factor <math|m<rsub|P><around|(|q|)>=<math-up|area><rsub|L><around|(|q|)><rsup|j>>
    obeys, on a common complex cube,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||m<rsub|P><around|(|q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|L|)>><around|(|p|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|m><rsup|j>*\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-j>.
    </equation*>

    The <math|r>-face flux has entrywise bound
    <math|C<rsub|m,r>*\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-j>> for each
    fixed <math|r> used in the wire formula. At the zero alias, the latch
    factor <math|m<rsub|P><around|(|q<rsub|0>|)><rsup|-1>> and reciprocal
    rails are bounded independently of <math|L> on a common smaller complex
    cube.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The area is the product of the four one-dimensional plank factors. Apply
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-rail-alias-decay> in each coordinate
    and raise the result to the <math|j>th power. The product of the four
    coordinate weights is exactly <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-j>>.
    A flux entry is this mass evaluated at <math|-q<rsub|l>> times the
    corresponding rail factors at <math|-q<rsub|l>>, followed by transpose;
    the rail factors have uniformly bounded modulus, so the same weight
    remains. At zero alias, the patch mass obeys

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||m<rsub|P><around|(|q<rsub|0>|)>|\|>\<geqslant\>exp
      <around|(|-4*j*<space|0.17em>c<rsub|0>|)>
    </equation*>

    on the real cube: apply the principal one-dimensional plank lower bound
    with scale ratio one in each coordinate. Its upper analytic-cube bound
    and this real lower bound give a uniform reciprocal on a smaller complex
    cube by the quantitative continuation estimate above. The zero-alias
    rails are handled by their real lower bound and the same continuation.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Scaled signal weight><label|lem:ex-lattice-scaled-signal-weight>For
    every face degree <math|r>, the scaled signal has entrywise bound
    <math|C<rsub|s>*g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)>>, independent of <math|r>. In
    particular the one-form signal costs at most
    <math|C<rsub|s>*g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The signal is exterior multiplication by the vector with entries
    <math|L*<around|(|e<rsup|i*q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|L|)>><around|(|p|)><rsub|a>>-1|)>>.
    Its matrix entry from an <math|r>-face to an <math|<around|(|r+1|)>>-face
    is zero unless the latter face is obtained by adjoining one coordinate;
    in that case it is, up to sign, the corresponding single vector entry.
    The alias upper bound and <math|<around|\||e<rsup|i*z>-1|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||z|\|>*e<rsup|<around|\||<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>z|\|>>>
    bound this entry by <math|C*<around|(|1+<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsub|a>|\|>|)>\<leqslant\>C*g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)>>.
    Thus the cost is one grade factor for every <math|r>.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Cumulative train, noise, bulb, and bend
    bounds><label|lem:ex-lattice-train-component-bounds>Fix a stage
    <math|j\<geqslant\>4> and <math|k\<gtr\>0>. Uniformly over every finite
    train history of that stage and its mass side <math|M>, there is a common
    complex cube on which for every <math|p> in the cube and every alias
    <math|l>, the cumulative filter <math|S<rsub|v><around|(|q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|M|)>><around|(|p|)>|)>>
    and directional drive <math|D<rsub|v,a><around|(|q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|M|)>><around|(|p|)>|)>>
    are bounded by <math|C*\<Gamma\><rsub|M><around|(|l|)><rsup|-j>>. At the
    zero alias the real lower bound <math|<around|\||S<rsub|v>*<around|(|p/M|)>|\|>\<geqslant\>c\<gtr\>0>
    holds uniformly on the fixed real cube. On a possibly smaller common
    complex cube, the noise drain, bulb, bend, and all reciprocals used in
    the pulse and vault are bounded. On its real slice there are uniform
    positive lower bounds for each directional lid, bulb, and bend. For each
    nonzero alias <math|l>, the directional drive therefore has the stated
    bound <math|C*\<Gamma\><rsub|M><around|(|l|)><rsup|-j>>, evaluated at the
    aliased momentum.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For a train <math|v> of stage <math|J> and side <math|M>, write
    <math|S<rsub|v><around|(|p|)>=<math-up|area><rsub|M><around|(|p|)><rsup|J>*v<rsub|<math-up|extra>><around|(|p|)>>.
    A hop with output stage <math|j> and predecessor of stage
    <math|J\<in\><around|{|j,j+1|}>> has side <math|L*M> and satisfies

    <\equation*>
      S<rsub|<math-up|hop>><around|(|p|)>=<math-up|area><rsub|L><around|(|M*p|)><rsup|J>*S<rsub|v><around|(|p|)>,<space|2em>v<rsub|<math-up|extra>,<math-up|hop>><around|(|p|)>=<math-up|area><rsub|L*M><around|(|p|)><rsup|J-j>*v<rsub|<math-up|extra>><around|(|p|)>.
    </equation*>

    The first identity follows by multiplying the four box averages and using
    their dilation composition law. For the upper bound, if <math|p> lies in
    the strip of width <math|<math-up|zone>/L<rsub|0>> and
    <math|M\<leqslant\>L<rsub|0>>, each box factor has modulus at most
    <math|e<rsup|<math-up|zone><space|0.17em>M/L<rsub|0>>>, hence the
    fourfold product at most <math|e<rsup|4<math-up|zone><space|0.17em>M/L<rsub|0>>>.
    Induction over histories proves <math|<around|\||v<rsub|<math-up|extra>><around|(|p|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|8<math-up|zone><space|0.17em>M/L<rsub|0>>>.
    At a hop with <math|J=j>, the extra is unchanged and the mass increases.
    If <math|J=j+1>, the added box factor contributes exponent
    <math|4<math-up|zone><space|0.17em>L*M/L<rsub|0>>; the predecessor's
    extra is bounded by exponent <math|<around|(|8/3|)><math-up|zone><space|0.17em>L*M/L<rsub|0>>
    since <math|L\<geqslant\>3>. Their sum is at most
    <math|8<math-up|zone><space|0.17em>L*M/L<rsub|0>>.

    For the lower bound, the principal one-dimensional estimate with
    coefficient one states that if <math|M\<leqslant\>L<rsub|0>>, <math|z> is
    real, and <math|L<rsub|0><around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|0>>, then
    <math|<around|\||<math-up|plank><rsub|M><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<geqslant\>e<rsup|-c<rsub|0>*M/L<rsub|0>>>.
    Four coordinates give the same estimate with exponent multiplied by four.
    The same induction proves <math|<around|\||v<rsub|<math-up|extra>>*<around|(|p/L<rsub|0>|)>|\|>\<geqslant\>e<rsup|-8*c<rsub|0>*M/L<rsub|0>>>.
    For <math|J=j>, the extra is unchanged and the mass increases. For
    <math|J=j+1>, the new box costs exponent
    <math|4*c<rsub|0>*L*M/L<rsub|0>>, and the old extra costs at most
    <math|<around|(|8/3|)>*c<rsub|0>*L*M/L<rsub|0>>; their sum is bounded by
    <math|8*c<rsub|0>*L*M/L<rsub|0>>. This is the geometric mass-ratio
    estimate that keeps the bound independent of the history length.

    For <math|M=1> the only alias is zero and the top train has
    <math|S<rsub|v>=D<rsub|v,a>=1>. For <math|M\<gtr\>1>, the alias estimate
    for each plank gives <math|<around|\||<math-up|area><rsub|M><around|(|q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|M|)>><around|(|p|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>B<rsup|4>*\<Gamma\><rsub|M><around|(|l|)><rsup|-1>>,
    while the upper extra estimate at <math|L<rsub|0>=M> costs at most
    <math|e<rsup|8<math-up|zone>>>. Consequently, for the fixed output stage
    <math|j>, <math|<around|\||S<rsub|v><around|(|q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|M|)>><around|(|p|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|B<rsup|4>|)><rsup|j>*e<rsup|8<math-up|zone>>*\<Gamma\><rsub|M><around|(|l|)><rsup|-j>>.
    At zero alias the lower estimate with <math|L<rsub|0>=M> gives
    <math|<around|\||S<rsub|v>*<around|(|p/M|)>|\|>\<geqslant\>exp
    <around|(|-4*j*<space|0.17em>c<rsub|0>-8*<space|0.17em>c<rsub|0>|)>>. The
    zero-root rail is at least <math|p<rsub|0>> and at most a uniform
    constant. Thus every directional drive and its two-sided lid have uniform
    upper and positive lower bounds there. At any alias, the train smooth
    upper bound is <math|C*\<Gamma\><rsub|M><around|(|l|)><rsup|-j>> and the
    rail is bounded, so the drive has that same weight. The same bound holds
    at <math|-q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|M|)>><around|(|p|)>>: for odd
    <math|M>, <math|-q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|M|)>><around|(|p|)>=q<rsub|-l><rsup|<around|(|M|)>>*<around|(|-p|)>>,
    the complex cube is invariant under <math|p\<mapsto\>-p>, and
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|M>*<around|(|-l|)>=\<Gamma\><rsub|M><around|(|l|)>>.
    Thus a two-sided drive product has weight at most
    <math|C<rsup|2>*\<Gamma\><rsub|M><around|(|l|)><rsup|-2*j>>.

    The divisor <math|d<rsub|M><around|(|p|)>> satisfies, for real <math|p>
    in the fixed disk,

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|low><rsup|2><around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>d<rsub|M><around|(|p|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|high><rsup|2><around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed each seed magnitude lies between
    <math|<math-up|low><around|\||p<rsub|a>|\|>> and
    <math|<math-up|high><around|\||p<rsub|a>|\|>>, and the divisor is the sum
    of their squares. The single-feed noise term uses the constant function
    one and equals

    <\equation*>
      M<rsup|-2>*<big|sum><rsub|l\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|M>>D<rsub|v,a><around|(|q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|M|)>><around|(|p|)>|)>*D<rsub|v,a>*<around|(|-q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|M|)>><around|(|p|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The alias drive bound gives a summand at most
    <math|C*\<Gamma\><rsub|M><around|(|l|)><rsup|-2*j>\<leqslant\>C*\<Gamma\><rsub|M><around|(|l|)><rsup|-2>>,
    whose sum is at most <math|81*C>; the retained factor <math|M<rsup|-2>>
    therefore bounds the feed by <math|81*C*M<rsup|-2>>. By definition, the
    noise list is empty for a top train and, for a hop of dilation <math|L>,
    consists of one top train followed by the hop applied to every entry of
    the previous list. Its total price <math|T> obeys
    <math|T<rsub|<math-up|top>>=0> and <math|T<rsub|<math-up|hop>>=1+L<rsup|-2>*T<rsub|<math-up|old>>>.
    As <math|L\<geqslant\>3>, induction gives
    <math|T\<leqslant\>1+2/9\<less\>2>. Summing the feed estimates at these
    actual inverse-square side prices bounds the full accumulated noise
    uniformly.

    The high-alias drain term is a finite sum of two-sided drive products
    times the reciprocal blow-up <math|<around|[|M<rsup|2>*d<rsub|1><around|(|q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|M|)>><around|(|p|)>|)>|]><rsup|-1>>.
    For nonzero <math|l>, choose a coordinate attaining
    <math|max<rsub|a><around|\||\<ell\><rsub|M><around|(|l|)><rsub|a>|\|>>.
    The centered-alias lower bound in that coordinate and the seed lower
    bound give <math|M<rsup|2>*d<rsub|1><around|(|q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|M|)>><around|(|p|)>|)>\<geqslant\>c*<space|0.17em>g<rsub|M><around|(|l|)><rsup|2>>
    on the real cube. Its complex upper bound has the same
    <math|g<rsub|M><around|(|l|)><rsup|2>> weight. The quantitative
    continuation argument above therefore bounds its reciprocal by
    <math|C*g<rsub|M><around|(|l|)><rsup|-2>> on a common smaller cube. At
    <math|M=1> the high-alias sum is empty. For <math|M\<gtr\>1>, the
    remaining drive product has weight <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|M><around|(|l|)><rsup|-2*j>>,
    so this sum is bounded by the exponent-two residue sum. Thus the full
    drain is uniformly bounded. It is nonnegative on the real slice.

    The bulb is the directional lid plus <math|d<rsub|M><around|(|p|)>> times
    the drain. The real lower bounds for the lid and nonnegativity of both
    latter factors give a positive lower bound for the bulb; the complex
    upper bound follows from the already established bounds. The halo is
    <math|S<rsub|v>*<around|(|p/M|)>*S<rsub|v>*<around|(|-p/M|)>> and has a
    positive real lower bound by the same cumulative-filter estimate. The
    exact bend identity follows as follows: the rail-chip relation gives
    <math|J<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>*h<rsub|v><around|(|p|)>=s<rsub|M><around|(|p|)><rsub|a>*s<rsub|M>*<around|(|-p|)><rsub|a>*\<ell\><rsub|v,a><around|(|p|)>>,
    where <math|h<rsub|v>=S<rsub|v>*<around|(|p/M|)>*S<rsub|v>*<around|(|-p/M|)>>
    and <math|\<ell\><rsub|v,a>> is the directional lid. Therefore
    <math|d<rsub|M><around|(|p|)>/h<rsub|v><around|(|p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a>J<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>/\<ell\><rsub|v,a><around|(|p|)>>.
    Combining this with <math|b<rsub|v,a>=\<ell\><rsub|v,a>+d<rsub|M>*G<rsub|v,a>>
    yields

    <\equation*>
      d<rsub|M><around|(|p|)>*<space|0.17em>\<beta\><rsub|v><around|(|p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a=1><rsup|4><frac|J<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>|b<rsub|v><around|(|p|)><rsub|a>>,<space|2em>J<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>=<around|(|e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>-1|)>*<around|(|e<rsup|-i*p<rsub|a>>-1|)>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed, each summand on the right is <math|J<rsub|a>/\<ell\><rsub|v,a>-d<rsub|M>*J<rsub|a>*G<rsub|v,a>/<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|v,a>*b<rsub|v,a>|)>>,
    which is exactly the corresponding term in
    <math|d<rsub|M>*\<beta\><rsub|v>>. On the real cube,
    <math|J<rsub|a>\<geqslant\>0>, <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>J<rsub|a>\<geqslant\><math-up|low><rsup|2><around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>>,
    and <math|d<rsub|M><around|(|p|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|high><rsup|2><around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>>.
    If <math|<around|\||b<rsub|v><around|(|p|)><rsub|a>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|b>>,
    the identity gives <math|\<beta\><rsub|v><around|(|p|)>\<geqslant\><math-up|low><rsup|2>/<around|(|C<rsub|b><math-up|high><rsup|2>|)>>
    for <math|p\<ne\>0>; at zero <math|\<beta\><rsub|v><around|(|0|)>=1>. The
    bend's complex-cube upper bound follows from its defining formula: first
    shrink the cube using the positive real lower bounds for the halo and
    lid, so their reciprocals are bounded. Next the bulb has a positive real
    lower bound and a complex upper bound, so shrink again to bound its
    reciprocal. With these factors, the displayed bend formula gives a
    complex upper bound. The real identity then gives a positive lower bound
    for the bend; shrink once more to bound its reciprocal for the pulse and
    vault. This proves all assertions uniformly over train histories.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Port denominator and levy weights><label|lem:ex-lattice-port-denominator-weights>For
    every nonzero alias, the port vector has entrywise weight
    <math|C<rsub|D>*g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-2>>, the reciprocal port
    normalizer has weight <math|C<rsub|R>*g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-4>>,
    and the levy scalar is uniformly bounded when <math|j\<geqslant\>4>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The bulb and reciprocal bend bounds are those of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-train-component-bounds>. The
    rescaled kernel is <math|L<rsup|2>*d<rsub|M><around|(|q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|L|)>><around|(|p|)>|)>>,
    with side <math|M>. Choose a coordinate <math|a<rsub|\<ast\>>> attaining
    <math|max<rsub|a><around|\||\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsub|a>|\|>>;
    since <math|l\<ne\>0>, its lift is nonzero. The centered alias lower
    bound gives <math|L<around|\||q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|L|)>><around|(|p|)><rsub|a<rsub|\<ast\>>>|\|>\<geqslant\>c*<around|(|1+<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsub|a<rsub|\<ast\>>>|\|>|)>=c*g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)>>
    on the real cube. The seed lower bound
    <math|<around|\||M<space|0.17em><math-up|chip><around|(|q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|L|)>><around|(|p|)><rsub|a<rsub|\<ast\>>>/M|)>|\|>\<geqslant\><math-up|low><around|\||q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|L|)>><around|(|p|)><rsub|a<rsub|\<ast\>>>|\|>>
    then implies <math|L<rsup|2>*d<rsub|M><around|(|q<rsub|l><rsup|<around|(|L|)>><around|(|p|)>|)>\<geqslant\>c<rprime|'>*g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|2>>.
    The analytic-cube upper bound for the blow-up has the same weight
    <math|g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|2>>, so the quantitative continuation
    argument gives its reciprocal at cost
    <math|C*g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-2>> on a common smaller cube. The
    port vector is the bounded bulb times this reciprocal. The reciprocal
    port normalizer is the reciprocal of blow-up squared times the bend; it
    therefore costs <math|C*g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-4>>. Finally, the
    levy contains two patch mass factors, of total weight
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-2*j>>, times the bounded land
    bend and this reciprocal port normalizer. Since both weights are at most
    one, the levy is uniformly bounded.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Uniform tails and regular zero-alias factors>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Uniform tail and regular factors><label|lem:ex-lattice-tail-notch-weights>The
    tail vector, the correction matrix, the notch matrix, and every
    zero-alias dual multiplier in the dual-stencil formula are bounded on a
    common complex cube, uniformly in <math|L> and the train. The same is
    true of the bulb and bend factors and their reciprocals wherever they
    occur in the wire formula.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The train, bulb, bend, and reciprocal bounds are
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-train-component-bounds>; the nonzero
    port bounds are Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-port-denominator-weights>.
    For each nonzero alias, a tail summand is the product of two patch
    masses, two bounded rail factors, and a port vector. It is bounded by
    <math|C*\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-2*j>*g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-2>\<leqslant\>C*\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-2>>.
    Thus the sum is uniformly bounded, since

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|l\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|L>>\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-2>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>><around|(|1+<around|\||n|\|>|)><rsup|-2>|)><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>81.
    </equation*>

    The extra constant <math|k<rsup|-1>> is finite. The divisor, scaled
    signals, and all zero-alias ratios in the correction and notch are
    bounded by the preceding train lemma. Their formulas contain only
    finitely many sums and products of these quantities, so the triangle
    inequality and the finite-dimensional matrix product bound complete the
    uniform estimate.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Uniform one-form transport bound><label|lem:ex-lattice-primal-entrywise-bound>Fix
    <math|k\<gtr\>0> and <math|4\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>t+1>.
    Over the flights of odd side <math|L\<geqslant\>3>, the one-form column
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|l>> of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:kernel-transport-identities>
    is holomorphic on one common complex cube, with each entry bounded by
    <math|C*\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-j>>. The constant and cube
    may depend on the fixed orders and penalty.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use the notation <math|s,b<rsub|a>,b<rsub|l,a>,m<rsub|l>,a,h,B<rsub|l>,g<rsub|l,a>,x<rsub|l>,\<sigma\>>
    of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:kernel-transport-identities>. In
    particular <math|h> is the sum over <math|l\<ne\>0> of
    <math|m<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>*m<rsub|-l>*<around|(|-p|)><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>D<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>>.
    In analytic expressions an adjoint on real momenta continues by
    reflection and transposition. Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-port-denominator-weights>
    and <reference|lem:ex-lattice-tail-notch-weights> bound each summand of
    <math|h> by <math|C*\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-2*j>*g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-2>>.
    Hence <math|h> is uniformly bounded by the exponent-two residue sum. The
    regular denominator

    <\equation*>
      s*a=m<rsub|0><around|(|p|)>*m<rsub|0>*<around|(|-p|)>*<big|sum><rsub|b>b<rsub|0,b>+s*h
    </equation*>

    is uniformly bounded on the common complex cube. On its real slice
    <math|s\<geqslant\>0> and <math|h\<geqslant\>0>. The zero-alias mass and
    the fine bulbs are uniformly bounded away from zero there by the
    component bounds, so the first term gives
    <math|s*a\<geqslant\>c\<gtr\>0>, also at <math|p=0>. The uniform
    continuation argument of the train component bounds therefore bounds
    <math|<around|(|s*a|)><rsup|-1>> on a smaller common cube.

    For <math|l\<ne\>0>, the leading matrix
    <math|B<rsub|l>=s*D<rsub|l>*F<rsub|1,l><rsup|\<ast\>><math-up|diag><around|(|b<rsup|-1>|)>>
    has weight <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-j>*g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-2>>
    by the port, flux and reciprocal bulb bounds. The two summands of
    <math|g<rsub|l,a>> have this same weight: each contains a reflected patch
    mass and a port, and the remaining denominators and rail factors are
    uniformly bounded after inversion where needed. The vector
    <math|x<rsub|l>> has weight <math|g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)>> and the row
    <math|\<sigma\>> has weight one by the signal, bend and bulb bounds. Thus
    the correction <math|<math-up|diag><around|(|g<rsub|l,a>|)>*x<rsub|l>*\<sigma\>>
    has weight at most <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-j>>, since
    <math|g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)>\<geqslant\>1>. For <math|l=0> the quotient
    <math|s*D<rsub|0>> is the diagonal of fine bulbs, so <math|B<rsub|0>> is
    bounded. The principal gain formula uses only the bounded tail and
    <math|h>, and the reciprocals of <math|m<rsub|0>>, <math|b<rsub|a>> and
    <math|s*a> on the smaller cube. The principal signal and <math|\<sigma\>>
    are bounded as well. These estimates prove the bound on both branches.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Closing the two wire branches>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Entrywise weighted wire bound><label|prop:ex-lattice-wire-entrywise-bound>For
    fixed stages <math|j,t\<geqslant\>4> and <math|k\<gtr\>0>, each scalar
    entry of the dual stencil is holomorphic on one common complex cube and
    satisfies

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||W<rsub|P,0><around|(|p|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|0>,<space|2em><around|\||W<rsub|P,l><around|(|p|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|1>*\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-j>*<space|1em><around|(|l\<ne\>0|)>,
    </equation*>

    uniformly over all wing train histories and odd <math|L\<geqslant\>3>.
    Hence the unified bound is <math|C*\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-j>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Use the zero and nonzero branch formulas in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-free-kernel-estimates>. In the
    zero branch, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-mass-flux-weight> and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-tail-notch-weights> show that every
    factor has weight one. In the first nonzero branch product, the scaled
    signal, port vector, and flux contribute respectively
    <math|g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)>>, <math|g<rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-2>>,
    and <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-j>>. Their product is at
    most <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-j>>. The second nonzero
    product has levy weight one and flux weight
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)><rsup|-j>>. Finite intermediate
    matrix sums multiply the constant by only the fixed dimension of the face
    spaces. The branch constants and widths can be replaced by their maximum
    and minimum, respectively. This proves the assertion.
  </proof>

  <subsection|The exact identities of the original transport
  kernels><label|sec:kernel-transport-identities>

  We now identify the kernels estimated in the preceding sections with the
  transports used in the quadratic construction. This also accounts for the
  harmonic mode. Throughout this section <math|k\<gtr\>0>, the block side
  <math|L> is odd and at least three, and the adjacent train orders
  <math|j,t> satisfy <math|4\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>t+1>.

  <subsubsection|Covariance recursion and exterior algebra>

  Fix a nonzero real coarse momentum <math|p> in the common real cube
  containing the Brillouin cube, and write
  <math|q<rsub|l>=<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)>|)>/L>
  for its centered aliases. In the following formulas the fine exterior
  symbol is rescaled: <math|\<delta\><rsub|r>=L*d<rsub|r><around|(|q<rsub|l>|)>>,
  whereas <math|d<rsub|r>=d<rsub|r><around|(|p|)>> is the coarse symbol.
  Stars denote Hermitian adjoints at these real momenta. Let
  <math|H<rsub|l>,H> be the fine and coarse two-form pulse symbols. Let
  <math|D<rsub|l>> be the diagonal matrix with entries
  <math|L<rsup|-2>*<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|f,a><around|(|q<rsub|l>|)>>,
  and <math|D> the diagonal matrix with entries
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|c,a><around|(|p|)>>, where
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|v,a>> is the propagated one-form
  covariance from Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-free-kernel-components>.
  Set

  <\equation*>
    x=d<rsub|0>,<space|1em>x<rsub|l>=\<delta\><rsub|0>,<space|1em>R=d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>*H*d<rsub|1>,<space|1em>R<rsub|l>=\<delta\><rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>*H<rsub|l>*\<delta\><rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  All diagonal entries of <math|D,D<rsub|l>> are positive. Put

  <\equation*>
    \<rho\>=x<rsup|\<ast\>>*D<rsup|-1>*x,<space|1em>\<pi\>=\<rho\><rsup|-1>*x<rsup|\<ast\>>*D<rsup|-1>,<space|1em>c=\<rho\><rsup|-1>*D<rsup|-1>*x.
  </equation*>

  The corresponding fine objects have subscript <math|l>. The pulse formula
  is <math|H=\<rho\><rsup|-1><big|wedge><rsup|2>D<rsup|-1>>; with the stated
  scaling, <math|H<rsub|l>=\<rho\><rsub|l><rsup|-1><big|wedge><rsup|2>D<rsub|l><rsup|-1>>
  as well. Consequently

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:transport-rotor>R=D<rsup|-1>-\<rho\><rsup|-1>*D<rsup|-1>*x*x<rsup|\<ast\>>*D<rsup|-1>,<space|2em>D*R=I-x*\<pi\>,<space|2em>R*D=I-c*x<rsup|\<ast\>>.
  </equation>

  For completeness, if <math|D<rsup|-1>=<math-up|diag><around|(|f<rsub|a>|)>>,
  the diagonal entry of <math|d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>><around|(|<big|wedge><rsup|2>D<rsup|-1>|)>*d<rsub|1>>
  at <math|a> is <math|f<rsub|a>*<big|sum><rsub|b\<ne\>a>f<rsub|b><around|\||x<rsub|b>|\|><rsup|2>>,
  and its off-diagonal entry at <math|<around|(|a,b|)>> is
  <math|-f<rsub|a>*f<rsub|b>*x<rsub|a><wide|x<rsub|b>|\<bar\>>>. Division by
  <math|\<rho\>=<big|sum><rsub|b>f<rsub|b><around|\||x<rsub|b>|\|><rsup|2>>
  proves the first equality; multiplication by <math|D> proves the other two.
  In particular <math|R*x=0> and <math|x<rsup|\<ast\>>*R=0>.

  Let <math|m<rsub|l>> be the patch mass filter at <math|q<rsub|l>> and
  define

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|r,l>=m<rsub|l><big|wedge><rsup|r><math-up|diag><around|(|<math-up|rail><rsub|L><around|(|q<rsub|l,a>|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The geometric-series identity <math|L<math-up|rail><rsub|L><around|(|q<rsub|l,a>|)>*<around|(|e<rsup|i*q<rsub|l,a>>-1|)>=e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>-1>
  gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:transport-filter-chain>F<rsub|r+1,l>*\<delta\><rsub|r>=d<rsub|r>*F<rsub|r,l>.
  </equation>

  The covariance recursion is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:transport-covariance-recursion><big|sum><rsub|l>F<rsub|1,l>*D<rsub|l>*F<rsub|1,l><rsup|\<ast\>>=D-k<rsup|-1>*I.
  </equation>

  Here the sum is a sum over all <math|L<rsup|4>> aliases, with no averaging
  factor. To verify the normalization and the recursion, the feed of a train
  of total side <math|M> is

  <\equation*>
    M<rsup|-2>*<big|sum><rsub|u\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|M>><around|\||<math-up|drive><rsub|v,a><around|(|q<rsub|u>|)>|\|><rsup|2>*f<around|(|q<rsub|u>|)>.
  </equation*>

  On appending a block, its drive is the old drive times the new patch filter
  evaluated at the old rescaled momentum. Decomposing a residue modulo
  <math|L*M> into its residues modulo <math|L> and <math|M> therefore turns
  this feed into the successive two feeds: the factors are
  <math|<around|(|L*M|)><rsup|-2>=L<rsup|-2>*M<rsup|-2>>, and the summands
  agree by periodicity. The noise-prefix list of the new train consists of
  its new unit prefix and the old prefixes with the block appended. Thus its
  noise feed is one plus the patch feed of the old noise. Applying these two
  facts to the free covariance and to each earlier independent noise gives
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|c,a>=k<rsup|-1>+<math-up|slide><rsub|a><with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|f,a>>.
  The diagonal entries of <eqref|eq:transport-covariance-recursion> are
  precisely this equality. All off-diagonal entries vanish.

  <subsubsection|The two punctured transport formulas>

  Define scalars

  <\equation*>
    a=<big|sum><rsub|l><around|\||m<rsub|l>|\|><rsup|2><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>D<rsub|l>,<space|2em>e<rsub|l>=<wide|m<rsub|l>|\<bar\>><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>D<rsub|l>/a,<space|2em>y<rsub|l>=<around|\||m<rsub|l>|\|><rsup|2>*\<rho\>/\<rho\><rsub|l>.
  </equation*>

  The principal mass filter is nonzero on the real cube. Since each
  <math|D<rsub|l>> is positive, its contribution shows <math|a\<gtr\>0>. In
  particular

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:transport-weight-sum><big|sum><rsub|l>e<rsub|l>*m<rsub|l>=1.
  </equation>

  Define the rescaled columns

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-A\><rsub|l>>|<cell|=D<rsub|l>*F<rsub|1,l><rsup|\<ast\>>*R+e<rsub|l>*x<rsub|l>*\<pi\>,>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number><label|eq:transport-A>>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-T\><rsub|l>>|<cell|=H<rsub|l>*\<delta\><rsub|1>*D<rsub|l>*F<rsub|1,l><rsup|\<ast\>>*d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>+y<rsub|l>*F<rsub|2,l><rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|I-C|)>,>|<cell|C>|<cell|=H*d<rsub|1>*D*d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>.<eq-number><label|eq:transport-T>>>>>
  </align>

  The complement <math|I-C> is part of the two-form transport; it cannot be
  discarded merely because it vanishes on weighted exact forms. Indeed
  <eqref|eq:transport-rotor> and <math|d<rsub|1>*x=0> imply

  <\equation*>
    R*D*d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>=d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>,<space|2em>d<rsub|1>*D*R=d<rsub|1>,<space|2em>d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|I-C|)>=0,<space|2em><around|(|I-C|)>*H*d<rsub|1>=0.
  </equation*>

  Also, the adjoint of <eqref|eq:transport-filter-chain> gives
  <math|x<rsub|l><rsup|\<ast\>>*F<rsub|1,l><rsup|\<ast\>>*d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>=0>.
  These identities prove

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:transport-punctured-three>R<rsub|l>*\<cal-A\><rsub|l>=F<rsub|1,l><rsup|\<ast\>>*R,<space|2em>\<delta\><rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<cal-T\><rsub|l>=F<rsub|1,l><rsup|\<ast\>>*d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>,<space|2em>\<cal-T\><rsub|l>*H*d<rsub|1>=H<rsub|l>*\<delta\><rsub|1>*\<cal-A\><rsub|l>.
  </equation>

  Here are the cancellations explicitly. The second term in
  <math|R<rsub|l>*\<cal-A\><rsub|l>> vanishes because
  <math|R<rsub|l>*x<rsub|l>=0>; in the first,
  <math|R<rsub|l>*D<rsub|l>=I-c<rsub|l>*x<rsub|l><rsup|\<ast\>>> and
  <math|x<rsub|l><rsup|\<ast\>>*F<rsub|1,l><rsup|\<ast\>>*R=0>. For the
  second identity the first term is <math|R<rsub|l>*D<rsub|l>*F<rsub|1,l><rsup|\<ast\>>*d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>=F<rsub|1,l><rsup|\<ast\>>*d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>>,
  while the second is <math|y<rsub|l>*F<rsub|1,l><rsup|\<ast\>>*d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|I-C|)>=0>.
  For the third identity the complement term vanishes by
  <math|<around|(|I-C|)>*H*d<rsub|1>=0>, and the remaining term is
  <math|H<rsub|l>*\<delta\><rsub|1>*D<rsub|l>*F<rsub|1,l><rsup|\<ast\>>*R>.
  The other side equals this because <math|\<delta\><rsub|1>*x<rsub|l>=0>.

  Finally <eqref|eq:transport-filter-chain> at degree zero,
  <eqref|eq:transport-weight-sum>, and <eqref|eq:transport-covariance-recursion>
  give

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|l>F<rsub|1,l>*\<cal-A\><rsub|l>>|<cell|=<around|(|D-k<rsup|-1>*I|)>*R+x*\<pi\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=I-k<rsup|-1>*R.<eq-number><label|eq:transport-alias-sum>>>>>
  </align>

  This is the identity that incorporates the new Gaussian noise.

  <subsubsection|Removing the puncture without changing the kernels>

  We identify the analytic continuations to which the component bounds apply.
  In this continuation every real-momentum adjoint is written as
  transposition after reflection <math|p\<mapsto\>-p>; for example,
  <math|<around|\||m<around|(|p|)>|\|><rsup|2>> continues as
  <math|m<around|(|p|)>*m*<around|(|-p|)>>. For an alias-indexed factor the
  reflection is <math|q<rsub|l>\<mapsto\>-q<rsub|l>>, equivalently
  <math|<around|(|p,l|)>\<mapsto\><around|(|-p,-l|)>>. Thus none of the
  complex-momentum formulas below uses literal complex conjugation of its
  variable. Let <math|s> be the coarse divisor, <math|b<rsub|a>> the coarse
  bulb and <math|b<rsub|l,a>> the fine bulb. Write <math|q<rsub|0>=p/L> and
  <math|u<rsub|a>=<math-up|tail><rsub|a>>, including the current noise.
  Splitting the covariance recursion into its principal alias and the other
  aliases gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:transport-bulb-split>b<rsub|a>=<around|\||m<rsub|0><math-up|rail><rsub|L><around|(|q<rsub|0,a>|)>|\|><rsup|2>*b<rsub|0,a>+s*u<rsub|a>.
  </equation>

  For <math|p\<ne\>0> this follows by multiplying
  <math|D<rsub|a>=<around|\||m<rsub|0><math-up|rail><rsub|L><around|(|q<rsub|0,a>|)>|\|><rsup|2>*b<rsub|0,a>/s+u<rsub|a>>
  by <math|s>. Both sides of <eqref|eq:transport-bulb-split> are continuous,
  so it holds at zero as well.

  For the one-form column put <math|B<rsub|l>=D<rsub|l>*F<rsub|1,l><rsup|\<ast\>>*D<rsup|-1>>
  and define <math|r<rsub|l,a>> by <math|B<rsub|l>*x=<math-up|diag><around|(|r<rsub|l,a>|)>*x<rsub|l>>.
  Entrywise,

  <\equation*>
    r<rsub|l,a>=<wide|m<rsub|l>|\<bar\>><around|(|D<rsub|l>|)><rsub|a*a><around|\||<math-up|rail><rsub|L><around|(|q<rsub|l,a>|)>|\|><rsup|2>/D<rsub|a*a>.
  </equation*>

  Using <eqref|eq:transport-rotor> in <eqref|eq:transport-A> gives

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-A\><rsub|l>=B<rsub|l>+<math-up|diag><around|(|e<rsub|l>-r<rsub|l,a>|)>*x<rsub|l>*\<pi\>.
  </equation*>

  Now <math|D<rsup|-1>=s<math-up|diag><around|(|b<rsub|a><rsup|-1>|)>> and
  <math|\<pi\>=s<rsup|-1>*\<sigma\>>, where
  <math|\<sigma\>=<around|(|<math-up|bend><rsub|c>|)><rsup|-1>*x<rsup|\<ast\>><math-up|diag><around|(|b<rsub|a><rsup|-1>|)>>
  is analytic. For a nonprincipal alias, <math|D<rsub|l>> has the regular
  port formula; thus <math|e<rsub|l>-r<rsub|l,a>=s*g<rsub|l,a>> with

  <\equation*>
    g<rsub|l,a>=<wide|m<rsub|l>|\<bar\>><around*|(|<frac|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>D<rsub|l>|s*a>-<frac|<around|(|D<rsub|l>|)><rsub|a*a><around|\||<math-up|rail><rsub|L><around|(|q<rsub|l,a>|)>|\|><rsup|2>|b<rsub|a>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  For the principal alias let <math|h> be the sum of the nonprincipal terms
  in <math|a>. Then <math|s*a=<around|\||m<rsub|0>|\|><rsup|2>*<big|sum><rsub|a>b<rsub|0,a>+s*h>,
  and <eqref|eq:transport-bulb-split> instead gives the regular formula

  <\equation*>
    g<rsub|0,a>=m<rsub|0><rsup|-1>*<around*|(|<frac|u<rsub|a>|b<rsub|a>>-<frac|h|s*a>|)>.
  </equation*>

  These are exactly the principal and nonprincipal gain formulas. The
  reciprocals of <math|b<rsub|a>>, <math|s*a>, and <math|m<rsub|0>> are
  uniformly bounded on the common smaller cube of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-primal-entrywise-bound>. Consequently

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-A\><rsub|l>=B<rsub|l>+<math-up|diag><around|(|g<rsub|l,a>|)>*x<rsub|l>*\<sigma\>
  </equation*>

  has the weighted bound of that lemma, including at the origin.

  For the two-form column, at a nonprincipal alias write
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|l>=<around|\||m<rsub|l>|\|><rsup|2><math-up|bend><rsub|c>/<math-up|portR><rsub|l>>.
  Then <math|y<rsub|l>=s<rsup|2>*\<lambda\><rsub|l>> and

  <\equation*>
    s<rsup|2>*<around|(|I-C|)>=s<rsup|2>*I-s*H*d<rsub|1><math-up|diag><around|(|b<rsub|a>|)>*d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>.
  </equation*>

  Substitution in <eqref|eq:transport-T> is exactly the regular nonprincipal
  wire formula. At the principal alias set <math|J=F<rsub|2,0><rsup|-1>>,
  <math|U=<math-up|diag><around|(|u<rsub|a>|)>>,
  <math|M=<math-up|diag><around|(|b<rsub|a>|)>>, and
  <math|z<rsub|a>=u<rsub|a>/b<rsub|a>>. The inverse exists on the common
  cube. The exterior chain rule and the principal covariance split give

  <\equation*>
    \<delta\><rsub|1>=J*d<rsub|1>*F<rsub|1,0>,<space|2em>F<rsub|1,0>*D<rsub|0>*F<rsub|1,0><rsup|\<ast\>>=D-U.
  </equation*>

  The cancellation needed for the apparent remaining pole is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:transport-brace-cancellation>s*\<cal-B\>=H<rsub|0>-y<rsub|0>*F<rsub|2,0><rsup|\<ast\>>*H*F<rsub|2,0>,<space|2em>\<cal-B\>=H<rsub|0>*<around*|(|<math-up|diag><rsub|<around|{|a,b|}>><around|(|z<rsub|a>+z<rsub|b>|)>-s<big|wedge><rsup|2><math-up|diag><around|(|z<rsub|a>|)>|)>.
  </equation>

  To verify it, put <math|Z<rsub|a>=<around|\||m<rsub|0><math-up|rail><rsub|L><around|(|q<rsub|0,a>|)>|\|><rsup|2>*b<rsub|0,a>/b<rsub|a>=1-s*z<rsub|a>>
  by <eqref|eq:transport-bulb-split>. The pulse formula shows
  <math|y<rsub|0>*F<rsub|2,0><rsup|\<ast\>>*H*F<rsub|2,0>=H<rsub|0><big|wedge><rsup|2><math-up|diag><around|(|Z<rsub|a>|)>>:
  on a face <math|<around|{|a,b|}>> both sides have the same product of its
  two diagonal entries and the same scalar bend ratio. Finally
  <math|1-<around|(|1-s*z<rsub|a>|)>*<around|(|1-s*z<rsub|b>|)>=s*<around|(|z<rsub|a>+z<rsub|b>-s*z<rsub|a>*z<rsub|b>|)>>
  proves <eqref|eq:transport-brace-cancellation> entrywise.

  Using <math|M=s*D>, the principal expression therefore becomes

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:transport-principal-wire>\<cal-T\><rsub|0>=y<rsub|0>*F<rsub|2,0><rsup|\<ast\>>+<around|(|\<cal-B\>*J*d<rsub|1>*M-H<rsub|0>*J*d<rsub|1>*U|)>*d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>.
  </equation>

  Indeed its second term expands to <math|H<rsub|0>*J*d<rsub|1>*<around|(|D-U|)>*d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>-y<rsub|0>*F<rsub|2,0><rsup|\<ast\>>*H*d<rsub|1>*D*d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>>,
  which is <eqref|eq:transport-T> after the two chain identities. All factors
  in <eqref|eq:transport-principal-wire> have the analytic bounds already
  proved. This verifies agreement with the principal wire as well as the
  nonprincipal one.

  At <math|p=0> the principal analytic columns have values
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|0>=I> and <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|0>=I>: the bulbs,
  bends, rails, and principal filter have value one and the exterior symbols
  vanish. For <math|l\<ne\>0> the patch mass filter vanishes at
  <math|q<rsub|l><around|(|0|)>>. Indeed some nonzero alias coordinate has a
  nontrivial <math|L>th root of unity, and the geometric-series rail vanishes
  there. The corresponding plank is a nonzero phase times that rail, and the
  patch mass is a positive power of the product of the four planks, so it
  vanishes. The nonprincipal one-form column is zero because both its leading
  term and gain contain that filter. The two-form column is zero because its
  first term contains <math|d<rsub|1><around|(|0|)><rsup|\<ast\>>=0> and its
  other term contains the mass filter. Thus
  <eqref|eq:transport-punctured-three> and <eqref|eq:transport-alias-sum>
  also hold at <math|p=0>.

  <subsubsection|Returning to physical counting measure>

  The installed one-form and two-form columns at alias <math|l> are
  <math|L<rsup|-1>*\<cal-A\><rsub|l>> and
  <math|L<rsup|-2>*\<cal-T\><rsub|l>>. The block mean <math|Q> is the alias
  row with columns <math|L*F<rsub|1,l>>. In physical counting measure the
  adjoint alias column of <math|Q> is <math|L<rsup|-3>*F<rsub|1,l><rsup|\<ast\>>>:
  the factor <math|L> is multiplied by the coarse-to-fine volume ratio
  <math|L<rsup|-4>>. Since <math|\<delta\><rsub|r>=L*d<rsub|r><around|(|q<rsub|l>|)>>,
  the second and third identities in <eqref|eq:transport-punctured-three>
  become respectively

  <\equation*>
    d<rsub|1,f><rsup|\<ast\>>*T=Q<rsup|\<ast\>>*d<rsub|1,c><rsup|\<ast\>>,<space|2em>T*H<rsub|c>*d<rsub|1,c>=H<rsub|f>*d<rsub|1,f>*A<rsub|0>.
  </equation*>

  The alias sum becomes <math|Q*A<rsub|0>=I-k<rsup|-1>*d<rsub|1,c><rsup|\<ast\>>*H<rsub|c>*d<rsub|1,c>>.
  The first identity in <eqref|eq:transport-punctured-three> gives the primal
  identity as well: the fine rotor is <math|L<rsup|-2>*R<rsub|l>> in physical
  units, so its product with <math|L<rsup|-1>*\<cal-A\><rsub|l>> is
  <math|L<rsup|-3>*F<rsub|1,l><rsup|\<ast\>>*R>. On a torus of side <math|n>
  we use <math|<wide|u|^><around|(|p|)>=n<rsup|-4>*<big|sum><rsub|x>u<around|(|x|)>*e<rsup|-i*p\<cdot\>x>>,
  with the analogous normalization on the fine torus of side <math|L*n>. In
  the periodization of a cover row the sum over each period fibre can be
  interchanged with the finite Fourier sum by absolute summability. At a grid
  momentum the exponential is constant on each fibre; the resulting cover
  Fourier series is therefore evaluated at that momentum. For a cross row the
  centered-residue decomposition groups the fine frequencies into the aliases
  <math|q<rsub|l>> of each coarse <math|p>. The reality calculation in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-free-kernel-estimates> ensures
  that the real coefficient rows recover these symbols. Fourier inversion
  then gives the real matrix identities

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|d<rsub|1,f><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*H<rsub|f>*d<rsub|1,f>*A<rsub|0>>|<cell|=Q<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*d<rsub|1,c><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*H<rsub|c>*d<rsub|1,c>,>|<cell|>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|T<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*d<rsub|1,f>>|<cell|=d<rsub|1,c>*Q,>|<cell|T*H<rsub|c>*d<rsub|1,c>>|<cell|=H<rsub|f>*d<rsub|1,f>*A<rsub|0>,>>|<row|<cell|Q*A<rsub|0>>|<cell|=I-k<rsup|-1>*d<rsub|1,c><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*H<rsub|c>*d<rsub|1,c>.>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number><label|eq:transport-installed-identities>>>>>
  </align>

  This argument includes every finite period, including the one-site period,
  and uses the Fourier coefficients of the original analytic columns. No new
  choice of transport has been made.

  <subsection|Positive Laurent rows and the actual quadratic
  beds><label|sec:positive-row-producer>

  The inverse-closed matrix algebras of Gröchenig and Leinert
  <cite-detail|GrochenigLeinert|Theorem<nbsp>6 and Remark<nbsp>1 following
  Corollary<nbsp>7> offer a functional-calculus approach to localization for
  weights satisfying the Gelfand\URaikov\UShilov condition. A fixed
  exponential weight violates that condition. Here the positivity on the real
  torus permits us to pass to a narrower complex strip and estimate its width
  before taking Laurent coefficients.

  The quadratic construction needs positive factors with exponentially
  decaying coefficients, uniformly in the finite period. Taking a separate
  matrix square root on each torus would not supply such coefficients. We
  construct one analytic symbol and then recover its Laurent row. This is
  also how the construction preserves its zero-frequency conventions.

  <subsubsection|A uniform analytic inverse and square root>

  Let <math|I> be a fixed finite index set of cardinality <math|r>. A real
  Laurent row is a collection <math|A<rsub|i*j><around|(|z|)>>,
  <math|i,j\<in\>I>, <math|z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>. Suppose, for some
  <math|\<eta\>,C\<gtr\>0>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:positive-row-strip>sup<rsub|i,j>
    <big|sum><rsub|z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>e<rsup|\<eta\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>><around|\||A<rsub|i*j><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C.
  </equation>

  Its symbol

  <\equation*>
    <wide|A|^><around|(|p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|z>A<around|(|z|)>*e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\>z>
  </equation*>

  is periodic in each real coordinate and holomorphic whenever
  <math|max<rsub|a><around|\||<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>p<rsub|a>|\|>\<less\>\<eta\>>.
  If the index set is empty all the statements below are vacuous, so assume
  <math|r\<geqslant\>1>. On every smaller strip of width <math|\<sigma\>>,
  termwise differentiation is justified by

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>*e<rsup|\<sigma\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<eta\>-\<sigma\>|)><rsup|-1>*e<rsup|\<eta\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>.
  </equation*>

  Thus each entry has derivative norm at most
  <math|C/<around|(|\<eta\>-\<sigma\>|)>>, for the maximum norm on momenta.
  This also proves local uniform convergence of the differentiated series. In
  operator norm the corresponding Lipschitz bound is at most
  <math|r*C/<around|(|\<eta\>-\<sigma\>|)>>, since a matrix with entries
  bounded by <math|a> has operator norm at most <math|r*a>.

  Assume <math|A<rsub|i*j>*<around|(|-z|)>=A<rsub|j*i><around|(|z|)>>, so
  that <math|<wide|A|^><around|(|p|)>> is Hermitian at real momenta. Suppose
  in addition that for every real momentum <math|p>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:positive-symbol-band>v*I\<leqslant\><wide|A|^><around|(|p|)>\<leqslant\>M*I,<space|2em>0\<less\>v\<leqslant\>M,
  </equation>

  as Hermitian forms. These bounds are uniform over the whole real torus. Let
  <math|\<sigma\>=\<eta\>/2>, <math|D=r*C/<around|(|\<eta\>-\<sigma\>|)>>,
  and choose

  <\equation*>
    0\<less\>\<tau\>\<leqslant\>min <around|{|\<sigma\>,v/<around|(|2*D|)>|}>.
  </equation*>

  For a complex momentum in this strip, put
  <math|q=<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>p>. The segment joining <math|q> to
  <math|p> lies in the strip of width <math|\<sigma\>>, and therefore

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|<wide|A|^><around|(|p|)>-<wide|A|^><around|(|q|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>v/2.
  </equation*>

  Diagonalizing the Hermitian matrix at <math|q> gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|M<rsup|-1>*<wide|A|^><around|(|q|)>-I|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1-v/M.
  </equation*>

  Hence throughout the smaller strip

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:positive-binomial-disk><around|\<\|\|\>|M<rsup|-1>*<wide|A|^><around|(|p|)>-I|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1-v/<around|(|2*M|)>=:\<rho\>\<less\>1.
  </equation>

  All constants depend only on the original uniform bounds and the fixed
  index set.

  Put <math|Z<around|(|p|)>=M<rsup|-1>*<wide|A|^><around|(|p|)>-I>. The
  normally convergent series

  <\equation*>
    <wide|F|^><around|(|p|)>=<sqrt|M>*<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>0><choose|1/2|n>Z<around|(|p|)><rsup|n>,<space|2em><wide|B|^><around|(|p|)>=M<rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>0><around|(|-Z<around|(|p|)>|)><rsup|n>
  </equation*>

  define holomorphic periodic symbols on this strip. Normal convergence
  follows from <math|\<rho\>\<less\>1> and absolute convergence of the scalar
  power series on every smaller closed disk. Cauchy multiplication gives
  <math|<wide|B|^>*<wide|A|^>=I> and <math|<wide|F|^><rsup|2>=<wide|A|^>>. On
  the real torus diagonalization gives eigenvalues <math|\<lambda\><rsup|-1>>
  and <math|<sqrt|\<lambda\>>>, respectively, where
  <math|v\<leqslant\>\<lambda\>\<leqslant\>M>. Consequently

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:positive-root-bands>M<rsup|-1>*I\<leqslant\><wide|B|^>\<leqslant\>v<rsup|-1>*I,<space|2em><sqrt|v>*I\<leqslant\><wide|F|^>\<leqslant\><sqrt|M>*I.
  </equation>

  In particular these are the positive inverse and positive square root, with
  no choice of eigenvectors as the momentum varies.

  We verify that they are symbols of real exponentially summable rows. Either
  symbol is bounded on a still smaller strip, say of width
  <math|\<tau\><rsub|0>\<gtr\>0>, by a constant <math|K>. Its Fourier
  coefficient is the integral over <math|<around|[|-\<pi\>,\<pi\>|]><rsup|4>>
  against <math|e<rsup|-i*p\<cdot\>z>>, divided by
  <math|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|4>>. Shift the <math|a>th contour to
  imaginary part <math|-\<tau\><rsub|1><math-up|sign><around|(|z<rsub|a>|)>>,
  where <math|0\<less\>\<tau\><rsub|1>\<less\>\<tau\><rsub|0>>. Cauchy's
  theorem applies one coordinate at a time; the vertical sides cancel by
  periodicity. The coefficient is therefore bounded by
  <math|K*e<rsup|-\<tau\><rsub|1><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>>. For
  <math|0\<less\>e\<less\>\<tau\><rsub|1>>,

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|z>e<rsup|e<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>><around|\||F<rsub|i*j><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>K<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>>e<rsup|-<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|1>-e|)><around|\||n|\|>>|)><rsup|4>\<less\>\<infty\>,
  </equation*>

  and likewise for <math|B>. The original real row satisfies
  <math|<wide|A|^>*<around|(|-p|)>=<wide|<wide|A|^><around|(|p|)>|\<bar\>>>
  on real momenta. Both power series have real coefficients, so their symbols
  have the same symmetry. Changing <math|p> to <math|-p> in the Fourier
  integral shows that every recovered coefficient is real. Absolute Fourier
  convergence identifies the recovered symbols with the displayed analytic
  functions on the real torus.

  On a finite torus, periodize these same rows. The discrete Fourier
  transform diagonalizes their convolution matrices, with symbols evaluated
  at the finite Fourier frequencies. Thus on every period <math|n>,
  simultaneously,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:positive-row-periods>B<rsub|n>*A<rsub|n>=I,<space|2em>F<rsub|n><rsup|<math-ss|T>>=F<rsub|n>,<space|2em>F<rsub|n><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*F<rsub|n>=A<rsub|n>,<space|2em><sqrt|v>*I\<leqslant\>F<rsub|n>\<leqslant\><sqrt|M>*I.
  </equation>

  The same transform transfers the inverse bounds in
  <eqref|eq:positive-root-bands>. This includes the zero frequency and the
  one-site torus. The construction has used a fixed number of form labels; no
  bound involving the growing number of lattice sites has entered the
  analytic inverse or root estimate.

  <subsubsection|The amendment of the two-form precision>

  Fix a smoothing stage <math|j\<geqslant\>4> and the positive kernel
  parameter <math|k>. For every finite train at this stage, let <math|H> and
  <math|B> be the pulse and dual-pulse rows constructed in
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-free-kernel-estimates> and
  <reference|sec:ex-lattice-free-kernel-components>. Their real symbols are
  diagonal, positive and reciprocal. Their uniform strip bounds therefore
  also give uniform positive lower bounds: if a diagonal entry of each row is
  at most <math|C> and their product is one, each is at least
  <math|C<rsup|-1>>. The preceding Fourier argument yields <math|H*B=B*H=I>
  on every period, with uniform spectral bounds.

  Let <math|d<rsub|1>,d<rsub|2>> be the exterior differences from one-forms
  to two-forms and from two-forms to three-forms; thus
  <math|d<rsub|2>*d<rsub|1>=0>. For a fixed amendment coefficient
  <math|l\<geqslant\>0>, define

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:positive-amendment>X=d<rsub|2>*B,<space|2em>A=B+l*X<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*X,<space|2em>G=A<rsup|-1>,<space|2em>R=I+l*d<rsub|2>*B*d<rsub|2><rsup|<math-ss|T>>,<space|2em>D=R<rsup|-1>.
  </equation>

  These formulas denote compositions of Laurent rows and hence also their
  matrices on every finite period. Products and sums preserve exponential
  summability, after taking a common strip width. On real frequencies
  <math|A\<geqslant\>B\<gtr\>0> and <math|R\<geqslant\>I>, because
  <math|B\<gtr\>0>. The analytic inverse construction supplies <math|G,D>
  with uniform strip and spectral bounds. The constants may depend on
  <math|j,k,l>, but are independent of the train and finite period.

  Two identities explain the choice of this amendment. First,
  <math|X*H*d<rsub|1>=d<rsub|2>*d<rsub|1>=0>, so

  <\equation*>
    A*H*d<rsub|1>=d<rsub|1>,<space|2em>G*d<rsub|1>=H*d<rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  Thus the potential precision is unchanged. Second, expansion of
  <eqref|eq:positive-amendment> gives <math|R*X=d<rsub|2>*A>, since <math|B>
  is symmetric. Multiplication by <math|D> on the left and <math|G> on the
  right therefore gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:positive-amendment-intertwine>X*G=D*d<rsub|2>.
  </equation>

  Both <math|G> and <math|D> are symmetric. At zero momentum
  <math|d<rsub|2>=0> and <math|B=I>, so <math|G<around|(|0|)>=I>; the
  harmonic convention is retained.

  Choose a uniform lower spectral bound <math|b\<gtr\>0> for <math|G>, and
  allow any direct precision <math|0\<less\>p\<leqslant\>b/2>. Then
  <math|G-p*I\<geqslant\><around|(|b/2|)>*I>. It has a uniform strip bound,
  including the subtracted constant row, so the analytic square-root
  construction gives a row <math|F> with

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|n>\<geqslant\>0,<space|2em>F<rsub|n><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*F<rsub|n>=G<rsub|n>-p*I
  </equation*>

  for every finite period, with uniform strip bounds. The full root matrix is

  <\equation*>
    J<rsub|p>=<around*|[|<tabular*|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<sqrt|p>*I>>|<row|<cell|F<rsub|n>>>>>>|]>,<space|2em>J<rsub|p><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*J<rsub|p>=G<rsub|n>.
  </equation*>

  Together with <math|H,B,G,A> this is the actual quadratic bed. Its inverse,
  symmetry, positivity and exterior-intertwining properties have all been
  verified above. In the top train the pulse <math|H> is the identity, so the
  bed's unamended diagonal row is the identity as required. This does not
  assert <math|G=I>: the amended metric is
  <math|<around|(|I+l*d<rsub|2><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*d<rsub|2>|)><rsup|-1>> in
  that train, and only its action on exact curvatures and its zero-frequency
  value agree with the identity. The admissible upper bound for <math|p> and
  all row bounds are selected before any lattice volume or train is chosen.

  <subsection|Construction of the finite precision
  tower><label|sec:ex-lattice-rg-horizon-geometry>

  This section explains the finite-dimensional algebra that turns the
  selected quadratic beds and their curvature factor into an exact Wilson
  tower. It also records how the beds and towers are produced on the actual
  schedule. All matrices act on finite real torus spaces; there is no
  limiting or spectral argument in the tower identities.

  <subsubsection|The exact finite-torus block
  identity><label|subsec:ex-lattice-rg-roll>

  Let <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|f><rsup|r>> and <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|c><rsup|r>>
  be the real coordinate spaces of r-form labels on the fine and coarse tori.
  The first coboundaries are <math|D<rsub|f>:\<cal-E\><rsub|f><rsup|1>\<to\>\<cal-E\><rsub|f><rsup|2>>
  and <math|D<rsub|c>:\<cal-E\><rsub|c><rsup|1>\<to\>\<cal-E\><rsub|c><rsup|2>>;
  the next coarse coboundary is <math|d<rsub|2>:\<cal-E\><rsub|c><rsup|2>\<to\>\<cal-E\><rsub|c><rsup|3>>,
  with <math|d<rsub|2>*D<rsub|c>=0>. Let <math|J:\<cal-E\><rsub|f><rsup|2>\<to\>\<cal-Y\><rsub|f>>
  and <math|J<rprime|'>:\<cal-E\><rsub|c><rsup|2>\<to\>\<cal-Y\><rsub|c>> be
  the full precision-factor maps for the fine and coarse beds, respectively,
  and write

  <\equation*>
    W=J<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*J,<space|2em>W<rprime|'>=J<rprime|'><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*J<rprime|'>,<space|2em>R=W<rsup|-1>,<space|2em>R<rprime|'>=<around|(|W<rprime|'>|)><rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  Assume <math|W,W<rprime|'>> are symmetric and the displayed inverses exist.
  Let <math|T> be the installed two-form transport
  <math|T:\<cal-E\><rsub|c><rsup|2>\<to\>\<cal-E\><rsub|f><rsup|2>>, and let
  <math|A<rsub|0>:\<cal-E\><rsub|c><rsup|1>\<to\>\<cal-E\><rsub|f><rsup|1>>
  be the selected one-form transport. The conditional mean
  <math|<wide|T|\<bar\>>:\<cal-E\><rsub|f><rsup|1>\<to\>\<cal-E\><rsub|c><rsup|1>>
  and these transports satisfy

  <\equation>
    T<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*D<rsub|f>=D<rsub|c>*<wide|T|\<bar\>>,<space|2em>T*W<rprime|'>*D<rsub|c>=W*D<rsub|f>*A<rsub|0>,<label|eq:ex-lattice-horizon-diagonal>
  </equation>

  where <math|W> and <math|R> act on <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|f><rsup|2>>, while
  <math|W<rprime|'>> and <math|R<rprime|'>> act on
  <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|c><rsup|2>>. Put <math|s=<sqrt|k>>, with
  <math|k\<gtr\>0>.

  Define the curvature remainder by

  <\equation>
    Z=R<rprime|'>-T<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*R*T-k<rsup|-1>*D<rsub|c>*D<rsub|c><rsup|<math-ss|T>>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-horizon-defect>
  </equation>

  Suppose this remainder has the curvature factorization

  <\equation>
    W<rprime|'>*Z*W<rprime|'>=d<rsub|2><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*P<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*P*d<rsub|2>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-horizon-bleep>
  </equation>

  Let <math|P:\<cal-E\><rsub|c><rsup|3>\<to\>\<cal-E\><rsub|c><rsup|3>> be a
  real matrix with the curvature factorization above. Define the coarse
  projection and its curvature image by

  <\equation*>
    \<Pi\>=J<rprime|'>*R<rprime|'>*J<rprime|'><rsup|<math-ss|T>>,<space|2em>X=P*d<rsub|2>*R<rprime|'>*J<rprime|'><rsup|<math-ss|T>>.
  </equation*>

  The three blocks in the tower are

  <\equation>
    M<rsub|F>=J*R*T*J<rprime|'><rsup|<math-ss|T>>,<space|2em>M<rsub|N>=s<rsup|-1>*D<rsub|c><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*J<rprime|'><rsup|<math-ss|T>>,<space|2em>M<rsub|O>=<around*|[|<tabular*|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|I-\<Pi\>>>|<row|<cell|X>>>>>|]>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-horizon-blocks>
  </equation>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Exact block completion><label|prop:ex-lattice-horizon-roll>Assume
    the inverses and factorization above exist and the two transport
    identities <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-horizon-diagonal> hold. Then the blocks
    in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-horizon-blocks> satisfy

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|M<rsub|F><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*M<rsub|F>+M<rsub|N><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*M<rsub|N>+M<rsub|O><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*M<rsub|O>>|<cell|=I,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-horizon-gram>>>|<row|<cell|s*M<rsub|N><rsup|<math-ss|T>>>|<cell|=J<rprime|'>*D<rsub|c>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-horizon-normal>>>|<row|<cell|M<rsub|F>*J<rprime|'>*D<rsub|c>>|<cell|=J*D<rsub|f>*A<rsub|0>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-horizon-primal>>>|<row|<cell|M<rsub|N>*J<rprime|'>*D<rsub|c>>|<cell|=s<rsup|-1>*D<rsub|c><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*W<rprime|'>*D<rsub|c>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-horizon-kinetic>>>|<row|<cell|M<rsub|O>*J<rprime|'>*D<rsub|c>>|<cell|=0,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-horizon-image>>>|<row|<cell|M<rsub|F><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*J*D<rsub|f>+M<rsub|N><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*<around|(|-s*<space|0.17em><wide|T|\<bar\>>|)>>|<cell|=0.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-horizon-orthogonal>>>>>
    </align>

    The three blocks have common domain <math|\<cal-Y\><rsub|c>> and
    respective ranges <math|\<cal-Y\><rsub|f>>,
    <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|c><rsup|1>>, and
    <math|\<cal-Y\><rsub|c>\<oplus\>\<cal-E\><rsub|c><rsup|3>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The inverse of a symmetric invertible real matrix is symmetric. Indeed,
    from <math|W*R=I> and <math|W<rsup|<math-ss|T>>=W> we obtain
    <math|R<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*W=I>, hence
    <math|R<rsup|<math-ss|T>>=R<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*<around|(|W*R|)>=<around|(|R<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*W|)>*R=R>;
    the same argument applies to <math|R<rprime|'>>.

    The matrix <math|\<Pi\>> is the orthogonal projection onto the range of
    <math|J<rprime|'>>. First,

    <\equation*>
      \<Pi\><rsup|<math-ss|T>>=J<rprime|'>*R<rprime|'>*J<rprime|'><rsup|<math-ss|T>>=\<Pi\>.
    </equation*>

    Second,

    <\equation*>
      \<Pi\><rsup|2>=J<rprime|'>*R<rprime|'>*<around|(|J<rprime|'><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*J<rprime|'>|)>*R<rprime|'>*J<rprime|'><rsup|<math-ss|T>>=J<rprime|'>*R<rprime|'>*W<rprime|'>*R<rprime|'>*J<rprime|'><rsup|<math-ss|T>>=\<Pi\>,<space|2em>\<Pi\>*J<rprime|'>=J<rprime|'>*R<rprime|'>*W<rprime|'>=J<rprime|'>.
    </equation*>

    For the curvature block, substitute <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-horizon-bleep>:

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|X<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*X>|<cell|=J<rprime|'>*R<rprime|'>*d<rsub|2><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*P<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*P*d<rsub|2>*R<rprime|'>*J<rprime|'><rsup|<math-ss|T>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=J<rprime|'>*R<rprime|'>*<around|(|W<rprime|'>*Z*W<rprime|'>|)>*R<rprime|'>*J<rprime|'><rsup|<math-ss|T>>=J<rprime|'>*Z*J<rprime|'><rsup|<math-ss|T>>.>>>>
    </align*>

    The two middle factors cancel because
    <math|R<rprime|'>*W<rprime|'>=W<rprime|'>*R<rprime|'>=I>. The other two
    Gram terms are

    <\equation*>
      M<rsub|F><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*M<rsub|F>=J<rprime|'>*T<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*R*T*J<rprime|'><rsup|<math-ss|T>>,<space|2em>M<rsub|N><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*M<rsub|N>=J<rprime|'>*<around|(|k<rsup|-1>*D<rsub|c>*D<rsub|c><rsup|<math-ss|T>>|)>*J<rprime|'><rsup|<math-ss|T>>.
    </equation*>

    By <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-horizon-defect>,
    <math|T<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*R*T+k<rsup|-1>*D<rsub|c>*D<rsub|c><rsup|<math-ss|T>>+Z=R<rprime|'>>.
    Consequently the sum of the three block Gram matrices is

    <\equation*>
      J<rprime|'>*R<rprime|'>*J<rprime|'><rsup|<math-ss|T>>+<around|(|I-\<Pi\>|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*<around|(|I-\<Pi\>|)>=\<Pi\>+I-\<Pi\>=I,
    </equation*>

    using <math|\<Pi\><rsup|<math-ss|T>>=\<Pi\>> and
    <math|\<Pi\><rsup|2>=\<Pi\>>. This proves
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-horizon-gram> as an identity on the full coarse
    augmented row space <math|\<cal-Y\><rsub|c>>; no restriction to the range
    of <math|J<rprime|'>> is made.

    The remaining formulas are direct consequences of the block definitions
    and <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-horizon-diagonal>. Since <math|s<rsup|2>=k>,
    <math|s*M<rsub|N><rsup|<math-ss|T>>=J<rprime|'>*D<rsub|c>>. For the fine
    transport,

    <\equation*>
      M<rsub|F>*J<rprime|'>*D<rsub|c>=J*R*T*<around|(|J<rprime|'><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*J<rprime|'>|)>*D<rsub|c>=J*R*<around|(|T*W<rprime|'>*D<rsub|c>|)>=J*R*<around|(|W*D<rsub|f>*A<rsub|0>|)>=J*D<rsub|f>*A<rsub|0>.
    </equation*>

    For the normal block,

    <\equation*>
      M<rsub|N>*J<rprime|'>*D<rsub|c>=s<rsup|-1>*D<rsub|c><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*W<rprime|'>*D<rsub|c>.
    </equation*>

    For the image block, its first component is
    <math|<around|(|I-\<Pi\>|)>*J<rprime|'>*D<rsub|c>=0>, since
    <math|\<Pi\>*J<rprime|'>=J<rprime|'>>. Its second component is

    <\equation*>
      X*J<rprime|'>*D<rsub|c>=P*d<rsub|2>*R<rprime|'>*J<rprime|'><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*J<rprime|'>*D<rsub|c>=P*d<rsub|2>*R<rprime|'>*W<rprime|'>*D<rsub|c>=P*d<rsub|2>*D<rsub|c>=0,
    </equation*>

    using <math|R<rprime|'>*W<rprime|'>=I> and <math|d<rsub|2>*D<rsub|c>=0>.
    This proves <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-horizon-image>.

    Finally, the transpose of <math|M<rsub|F>> gives

    <\equation*>
      M<rsub|F><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*J*D<rsub|f>=J<rprime|'>*T<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*R*<around|(|J<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*J|)>*D<rsub|f>=J<rprime|'>*T<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*D<rsub|f>.
    </equation*>

    By <math|T<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*D<rsub|f>=D<rsub|c>*<wide|T|\<bar\>>>, this
    is <math|J<rprime|'>*D<rsub|c>*<wide|T|\<bar\>>>. Meanwhile
    <math|M<rsub|N><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*<around|(|s*<wide|T|\<bar\>>|)>=J<rprime|'>*D<rsub|c>*<wide|T|\<bar\>>>.
    The two terms therefore cancel with the sign in
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-horizon-orthogonal>. This proves the orthogonality
    identity.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The selected schedule, its beds, and the tower
  estimates><label|subsec:ex-lattice-horizon-producer>

  The schedule input is a family of priced bed and flight receipts. At each
  integer stage <math|j\<geqslant\>4>, for every allowed direct precision
  <math|p<rsub|<math-up|direct>>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|j>> and every
  train of order <math|j>, the bottom receipt supplies a bed whose four
  precision rows are fixed as above, whose five rows have the stated weighted
  strip costs, whose floors hold on every torus side, and whose real
  precision is sworn positive. It also makes the top bed's diagonal the
  identity. For every admissible adjacent pair of orders <math|j,t> (with
  <math|t=j> or <math|t+1=j>), the flight receipt supplies donated unmodified
  primal/two-form transports with the order-<math|j-2> difference bounds,
  together with a curvature factor of the stated strip cost and positive
  lower quadratic bound. The block-length credit requires the separately
  selected lower bound on <math|L> before its quadratic floor is used. These
  receipts are obtained by the one-flight frontier and the uniform bed
  pricing; they are construction inputs here, not conclusions of the block
  algebra.

  Fix the actual level schedule <math|A> and a terminal cutoff <math|N>. Its
  train at level <math|N> is the constant top train. Descending one level,
  use the flight whose wing is the train already constructed at level
  <math|i+1> and whose land is the train at level <math|i>. Thus, for every
  <math|i\<less\>N>, the level-<math|i> train is the landing train of the
  flight whose wing is the already defined train at level <math|i+1>. This
  uses the actual adjacent block at level <math|i>; no second schedule is
  selected.

  The bed at each <math|<around|(|N,i|)>> is first constructed for the
  corresponding train. Its four precision rows are the train's pulse, dual
  pulse, amended precision and amended inverse, and its remaining root row is
  a square factor of the positive remainder
  (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:positive-row-producer>). The finite-volume
  root matrix is the vertical stack

  <\equation*>
    J<rsub|p>=<around*|[|<tabular*|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<sqrt|p<rsub|<math-up|direct>>>*I>>|<row|<cell|F>>>>>|]>,<space|2em>J<rsub|p><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*J<rsub|p>=p<rsub|<math-up|direct>>*I+F<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*F.
  </equation*>

  This identity follows by splitting the row index of the product into its
  two disjoint blocks. The lower root factor is obtained from the finite
  matrix square-factor receipt, so the equality holds at every torus side and
  includes the zero modes. The floor receipt then gives symmetry, inverse
  identities and the uniform quadratic form bounds required of a bed.

  For a step <math|i\<less\>N>, the flight's donated primal and two-form
  transports are the transports <math|A<rsub|0>> and <math|T> of its tower.
  The tangent identities are first obtained for the unamended pulses. On both
  lattices the amended metric agrees with its pulse on exact curvatures,
  <math|G*d<rsub|1>=H*d<rsub|1>> (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:positive-row-producer>).
  Thus the tangent identities give

  <\equation*>
    T<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*D<rsub|f>=D<rsub|c>*<wide|T|\<bar\>>,<space|2em>T*W<rprime|'>*D<rsub|c>=W*D<rsub|f>*A<rsub|0>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|W,W<rprime|'>> are the actual adjacent bed metrics and the
  overline is the flight's prescribed conditional mean. The flight's rebase
  receipt, followed by the positive square root of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:curvature-horizon-producer>, supplies the
  curvature factorization of the remainder <math|Z> in
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-horizon-defect>. Therefore the preceding block
  proposition applies to these actual adjacent beds, not just to an abstract
  pair of positive matrices.

  The completed tower also has explicit strip costs, uniform in <math|L>. We
  verify them by finite matrix bookkeeping. Let <math|\<alpha\>> be the
  minimum of the two bed widths (the fine bed width is also the donated
  width) and the curvature-factor width; write
  <math|\<Omega\>,\<Omega\><rprime|'>> for the two bed costs, <math|V> for
  the curvature factor cost, <math|L> for the odd block side, <math|X> for
  the maximum difference order, <math|k\<gtr\>0> for the penalty, and
  <math|n<rsub|r>=<around|\||<math-up|TypeFace><around|(|r|)>|\|>> for the
  number of <math|r>-form labels. Put

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A>|<cell|=max
    <around|{|<around|\<\|\|\>|<sqrt|p<rsub|<math-up|direct>>>|\<\|\|\>>,\<Omega\>|}>,>|<cell|B>|<cell|=max
    <around|{|<around|\<\|\|\>|<sqrt|q<rsub|<math-up|direct>>>|\<\|\|\>>,\<Omega\><rprime|'>|}>,>|<cell|>|<cell|>|<cell|>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|C<rsub|A>>|<cell|=n<rsub|2>*A*\<Omega\>,>|<cell|C<rsub|B>>|<cell|=n<rsub|2>*B*\<Omega\><rprime|'>,>|<cell|C<rsub|L>>|<cell|=n<rsub|2>*C<rsub|A>*\<Omega\>,>|<cell|C<rsub|F>>|<cell|=n<rsub|2>*C<rsub|L>*B,>>|<row|<cell|C<rsub|N>>|<cell|=k<rsup|-1/2>*n<rsub|2>*D<rsub|1>*B,>|<cell|C<rsub|<math-up|proj>>>|<cell|=n<rsub|2>*C<rsub|B>*B,>|<cell|C<rsub|<math-up|up>>>|<cell|=n<rsub|3>*V*D<rsub|2>,>|<cell|C<rsub|X>>|<cell|=n<rsub|2>*C<rsub|<math-up|up>>*\<Omega\><rprime|'>,>>|<row|<cell|C<rsub|O>>|<cell|=max
    <around|{|1+C<rsub|<math-up|proj>>,n<rsub|2>*C<rsub|X>*B|}>,>|<cell|M<rsub|0>>|<cell|=max
    <around|{|\<Omega\>,C<rsub|F>,C<rsub|N>,C<rsub|O>|}>,>|<cell|>|<cell|>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
  </align*>

  where

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|r>=<big|sum><rsub|a\<in\><math-up|TypeFace><around|(|r+1|)>><big|sum><rsub|b\<in\><math-up|TypeFace><around|(|r|)>><big|sum><rsub|z\<in\><math-up|supp><around|(|\<delta\><rsub|r>|)>>e<rsup|\<alpha\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|\<delta\><rsub|r>|)><rsub|a*b><around|(|z|)>|\<\|\|\>>,<space|2em>r=1,2,
  </equation*>

  is the finite stencil cost of the <math|r>th coboundary, where for a cover
  displacement <math|z> we set <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>=<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|4><around|\||z<rsub|j>|\|>>.
  For ordinary cover convolution, the basic weighted row bound can be checked
  entrywise. If <math|C<rsub|i*j>=<big|sum><rsub|b>A<rsub|i*b>\<ast\>B<rsub|b*j>>
  is the routed cover convolution and the weighted absolute sums of each
  entry of <math|A> and <math|B> are at most <math|a<rsub|i*b>> and
  <math|b<rsub|b*j>>, then

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|z>e<rsup|\<alpha\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>><around|\||C<rsub|i*j><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|b>a<rsub|i*b>*b<rsub|b*j>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed, write each convolution coefficient as a sum over <math|x+y=z>,
  apply the triangle inequality, use <math|<around|\||x+y|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>+<around|\||y|\|><rsub|1>>,
  and factor the two nonnegative sums over <math|x> and <math|y>. Summing the
  intermediate label <math|b> then gives the finite cardinality factor in the
  strip estimate. Each <math|n<rsub|2>> factor below is this count for a
  two-form intermediate label; <math|n<rsub|3>> counts the three-form labels
  in the curvature factor. A finite coboundary stencil contributes
  <math|D<rsub|r>>. The fine-to-coarse composition uses a mixed version of
  this estimate. Fix a fine residue <math|r> and a fine cover displacement
  <math|a>. There is a unique coarse displacement
  <math|\<kappa\>*<around|(|r,a|)>> and a new fine residue
  <math|r<rprime|'>=<math-up|wrap><around|(|<math-up|lift><space|0.17em>r+a|)>>
  such that

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|lift><space|0.17em>r+a=<math-up|lift><space|0.17em>r<rprime|'>+L*\<kappa\>*<around|(|r,a|)>.
  </equation*>

  Coordinatewise, put <math|u=<math-up|lift><space|0.17em>r>,
  <math|v=<math-up|lift><space|0.17em>r<rprime|'>>, and <math|n=L>. Both
  representatives lie in the centered interval, so
  <math|<around|\||u-v|\|>\<leqslant\>n-1>. The displayed identity yields
  <math|n<around|\||\<kappa\><rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||a<rsub|i>|\|>+n-1>.
  If <math|<around|\||\<kappa\><rsub|i>|\|>\<gtr\><around|\||a<rsub|i>|\|>>,
  integrality gives <math|<around|\||\<kappa\><rsub|i>|\|>\<geqslant\><around|\||a<rsub|i>|\|>+1>,
  hence <math|n<around|\||\<kappa\><rsub|i>|\|>\<geqslant\>n\|a<rsub|i>\|+n\<geqslant\><around|\||a<rsub|i>|\|>+n>,
  a contradiction. Thus <math|<around|\||\<kappa\>*<around|(|r,a|)>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|\||a|\|><rsub|1>>.
  In the mixed composition, each <math|a> selects exactly the single residue
  <math|r<rprime|'>> above, and its coarse shift is
  <math|\<kappa\>*<around|(|r,a|)>>. Apply the routed-convolution estimate at
  this fixed residue; summing the finite intermediate form label costs
  <math|n<rsub|2>>, with no sum over all possible residues. This is the
  <math|C<rsub|L>=n<rsub|2>*C<rsub|A>*\<Omega\>> bound, uniform in <math|L>.

  The fine factor <math|J*R> has cost <math|C<rsub|A>>. Its mixed composition
  with <math|T> has cost <math|C<rsub|L>>, and the final composition with
  <math|J<rprime|'><rsup|<math-ss|T>>> has cost <math|C<rsub|F>>; the normal
  block has cost <math|C<rsub|N>>; the projection and curvature rows have
  costs <math|C<rsub|<math-up|proj>>,C<rsub|X>>, respectively. Stacking the
  projection complement and curvature rows gives <math|C<rsub|O>>. These are
  precisely the products in the displayed formulas; the explicit factor
  <math|L<rsup|-2>> in the donated two-form strip is retained separately. The
  resulting tower has

  <\equation*>
    M<rsub|N>,M<rsub|O><text| strip-cost at most
    >M<rsub|0>,<space|2em>M<rsub|F><text| strip-cost at most
    >M<rsub|0>*L<rsup|-2>,
  </equation*>

  and its <math|r>th iterated primal and two-form differences have costs at
  most <math|M<rsub|0>*L<rsup|-<around|(|1+r|)>>> and
  <math|M<rsub|0>*L<rsup|-<around|(|2+r|)>>> for
  <math|0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>X>. These latter bounds are inherited
  unchanged from the donated tower: the block construction retains its
  <math|A<rsub|0>,T> rows, whose input receipts already include every
  iterated difference bound through order <math|X>.

  For completeness, the construction has two distinct stages. First, the
  schedule supplies a bed for every <math|<around|(|N,i|)>> and the
  recursively defined flight supplies its one-step data. The bed and hop
  receipts together with the exact block proposition above give a horizon
  with row, floor, crossing, and roll data. Second, the finite tower-price
  construction narrows widths and increases costs, while retaining the
  original <math|A<rsub|0>,T> rows; the new rows
  <math|M<rsub|F>,M<rsub|N>,M<rsub|O>> are the block completion just proved.
  The construction theorem performs this on the actual level object and
  retains the same bed family and the actual primal and two-form transports.
  In particular its retained-horizon equality is equality of the bed
  families; its tower equality preserves the <math|A<rsub|0>,T> rows used by
  the schedule.

  The resulting prices are indexed by the smoothing stage. Write <math|F> for
  the finite set of two-form labels, <math|J<rsub|i>> for the stage at level
  <math|i>, and <math|\<alpha\><rsub|j>\<gtr\>0>, <math|\<Omega\><rsub|j>>
  for the corresponding retained strip width and cost. For each level
  <math|i>, <math|\<Omega\><rsub|J<rsub|i>>\<geqslant\>0>: the covered bed
  bounds a nonnegative weighted row sum for a fixed face label, and the
  face-label set is nonempty. At a fixed step <math|i\<less\>N>, the retained
  tower and its fine bed have width <math|\<alpha\><rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>> and
  cost <math|\<Omega\><rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>>, while the coarse bed has width
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|J<rsub|i>>> and cost <math|\<Omega\><rsub|J<rsub|i>>>.
  For any <math|0\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>\<leqslant\>min
  <around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>,\<alpha\><rsub|J<rsub|i>>|)>>, the
  response of these very beds and tower therefore has the order-two atlas of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-response-atlas>, with cost

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||F|\|><rsup|2>*e<rsup|4*\<sigma\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|J<rsub|i+1>><rsup|2>*\<Omega\><rsub|J<rsub|i>>.
  </equation*>

  The first tower difference exists at the required order because the
  admissible stages are at least four. For a fixed step these prices are
  common to all terminal cutoffs <math|N\<gtr\>i>; they need not be bounded
  by one constant over all stages. Periodization and its uniformity require
  the separate quotient-fibre argument and the same retained data.

  The horizon therefore carries explicit numerical data at all <math|N,i> and
  on every finite torus side: the stage-indexed prices bound the bed strip
  widths and costs and the floor constants (by the schedule input described
  at the beginning of this subsection), and the iterated tower costs (by the
  bookkeeping above); the one-step crossing and roll identities hold by
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-horizon-roll>. The top bed is
  the unit diagonal because the top train is constant. These are exactly the
  data a horizon must carry; the prices and floors are inputs of the schedule
  and do not follow from the block identity alone. In the public construction
  the level schedule and these receipts are attached to the preselected run,
  as stated next. The public horizon contract alone does not identify its
  transport rows with the particular recursively produced ones above. For the
  flat quadratic output, the completion-independence argument of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-flat-same-output> permits the
  auxiliary priced construction to be compared on the same blocking data; it
  does not identify the two horizons or their individual tower rows.

  <subsubsection|Actual path data and the public
  binding><label|subsec:ex-lattice-horizon-path>

  Path data enter through the selected history of that run. Fix a finite
  torus, a plaquette face <math|b>, and a one-form variation <math|a>. For a
  path slot with coefficient <math|c<rsub|b*i>>, cover offset
  <math|y<rsub|b*i>>, and plaquette label <math|p<rsub|b*i>>, its physical
  face is the translate <math|b+y<rsub|b*i>> modulo the torus. Define the
  path linear response by the absolutely convergent sum
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|<math-up|path>>*<around|(|b,a|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i>c<rsub|b*i>*<space|0.17em>d<rsub|1>*a*<around|(|b+y<rsub|b*i>,p<rsub|b*i>|)>>.
  The attachment identity says that, after summing all path coefficients with
  fixed <math|<around|(|p,y|)>>, one obtains the corresponding cover entry of
  the root row. The weighted row bound implies absolute summability of the
  path coefficients: its exponential weight is at least one. It also implies
  absolute summability after multiplying by the bounded face curl of
  <math|a>. Therefore we may first group the path sum by physical face, and
  then sum over the finite set of torus faces. Let <math|W> be the selected
  bed's metric on this torus, and let <math|F<rsub|W>> be the actual roots
  block in its factorization, acting on periodic two-form fields. Periodizing
  the cover entries by the attachment identity gives, for each color
  component <math|q>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|\<ell\><rsub|<math-up|path>>*<around|(|b,a|)>|)><rsub|q>=<around*|[|F<rsub|W><around|(|<around|(|d<rsub|1>*a|)><rsub|q>|)>|]><around|(|b|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|F<rsub|W>> is the actual root matrix on the torus and
  <math|d<rsub|1>*a> is the finite-torus coboundary. The right side is a
  finite matrix-vector product. This is the entrywise path-to-root identity
  used in the physical quadratic-form computation; the absolute bound
  justifies the only infinite regrouping.

  The direct block in the full root is also needed. For two one-form
  variations <math|a,a<rprime|'>>, the identity
  <math|J<rsub|p><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*J<rsub|p>=W> and the displayed
  path-to-root equality give, with the sum over physical plaquettes,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<langle\>|d<rsub|1>*a,W*d<rsub|1>*a<rprime|'>|\<rangle\>>=p<rsub|<math-up|direct>>*<around|\<langle\>|d<rsub|1>*a,d<rsub|1>*a<rprime|'>|\<rangle\>>+<big|sum><rsub|f><around|\<langle\>|\<ell\><rsub|<math-up|path>>*<around|(|f,a|)>,\<ell\><rsub|<math-up|path>>*<around|(|f,a<rprime|'>|)>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation*>

  For colour-valued fields the inner products are taken in an orthonormal
  colour basis. This is a finite counting-measure identity; no lattice-volume
  normalization is inserted. It uses both the direct block and the attached
  root block of the same bed.

  The public-run adapter carries its original levels and retained horizon
  into the native component construction; its flat quadratic receipt uses
  that very horizon. For that same run, the attachment identity holds for
  every cutoff <math|N>, level <math|i\<leqslant\>N>, and period label
  <math|p>. Indeed, the successful history retains at
  <math|<around|(|N,p,i|)>> the path fixed by its underlying recipe. That
  recipe's path is the designated path of the retained bed family, and the
  bed construction attaches its root row to that designated path. These two
  path equalities give the attachment for the actual history; no
  independently selected family is substituted. The same run's planned path
  data supply weighted row, loop and higher-leg bounds at every such cutoff,
  level and period, with the width and price of that level. This separate
  bound supplies absolute summability; it is not concluded from attachment
  alone.

  The matrix identities prove the block calculation once its displayed inputs
  hold. The selected tower prices, path attachment and absolute bounds come
  from the separate receipts just described; they are not inferred from the
  block calculation alone.

  <subsubsection|Application on the retained
  schedule><label|subsec:ex-lattice-c01-selected-application>

  We make explicit the handoff of these local results to the selected
  construction. Use the profile and schedule choices of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:public-run-binding>, and the binding on the
  same family of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-selected-course-binding>.
  At each step <math|i>, put

  <\equation*>
    j=J<rsub|i+1>,<space|1em>t=J<rsub|i>,<space|1em>L=L<rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>,<space|2em>\<sigma\><rsub|i>=min
    <around|{|1,\<alpha\><around|(|J<rsub|i>|)>,\<alpha\>*<around|(|J<rsub|i>+1|)>|}>.
  </equation*>

  The schedule has <math|4\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>t+1> and odd
  <math|L\<geqslant\>3>. The shape-port identities of the binding identify
  the two bed pulses, their amendments, and the two transport rows with the
  same flight. The amendment coefficients were fixed by stage before the
  block lengths; the flight floor included in the selected length floor gives
  <math|m*C<rsub|1>\<leqslant\>L<rsup|2>> for its fine amendment. Thus
  <eqref|eq:curvature-paid-factor> and Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-horizon-roll>
  apply to this flight. The positive direct-precision ceiling and the bed
  prices are likewise those fixed before the schedule, so the analytic
  inverse and root constructions apply uniformly in the terminal cutoff and
  period.

  For either of two terminal cutoffs, the response uses these very fine
  inverse, transport, and coarse metric rows.
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-response-atlas> therefore gives its
  atlas at width <math|\<sigma\><rsub|i>>, with common cost at most

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-A\><rsub|i>=<around|\||F|\|><rsup|2>*e<rsup|4>*\<Omega\><around|(|J<rsub|i+1>|)><rsup|2>*\<Omega\><around|(|J<rsub|i>|)>,
  </equation*>

  and Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-response-travel> supplies
  its constant row sum and every-period carrier. City covariance follows from
  the installed residue formula: translation of the fine coordinate by
  <math|L*u> and the coarse coordinate by <math|u> changes neither the
  residue nor the relative coarse displacement; convolution with the two
  stationary bed rows preserves this identity. The entrance ticket and
  post-length payment are checked in Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-lattice-rg-funded-producer>.
  They give <math|2*\<sigma\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>L*\<sigma\><rsub|i+1>> and
  pay precisely the factor <math|2<around|\||F|\|><rsup|2>*\<cal-A\><rsub|i><rsup|2>/<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|i+1>*L|)>>
  of the one-step estimate. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-rg-terminal>
  constructs the neutral terminal packet from the same two bed metrics. Hence
  all hypotheses of the backward induction in
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-rg-construction> are supplied on
  these data, simultaneously for every finite period.

  If the flat construction uses the auxiliary completion of the retained
  levels, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-flat-same-output> transfers
  its quadratic output to the public horizon: the blocking maps, penalties
  and terminal one-form precisions agree. This transfers only that output.
  For the physical path identity above, use instead the actual binding's
  attached path and its separate level-priced bounds from
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:path-attachment-producer>. The floor, the
  direct term, the root row and counting measure are then those of that same
  bed. Thus the quadratic comparison never substitutes an auxiliary path or
  square root for the retained one. None of these finite-scale deductions
  requires a continuum field limit or reflection reconstruction.

  <subsection|The curvature factor and the retained
  horizon><label|sec:curvature-horizon-producer>

  The positive beds of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:positive-row-producer>
  must be joined by one common family of transports. The remaining issue is
  the positivity of the curvature defect. The construction first writes that
  defect as a double exterior derivative, then adds a fixed positive
  amendment before taking its square root.

  <subsubsection|An exponentially summable exterior lift>

  We give the row-level lifting argument, because a cycle on a single finite
  torus would not suffice. Write <math|q<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>=e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>-1>
  and <math|d<rsub|r><around|(|p|)>=q<around|(|p|)>\<wedge\>\<cdot\>> on
  <math|r>-forms. Let <math|A<around|(|p|)>> be the symbol of an
  exponentially summable row from <math|<around|(|r+1|)>>-forms to a fixed
  coordinate space, where <math|r+1\<gtr\>0>, and suppose
  <math|A<around|(|p|)>*d<rsub|r><around|(|p|)>=0> for every real <math|p>.

  For one coordinate <math|a>, let <math|A<rsub|0><around|(|p|)>=A*<around|(|p<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,0,\<ldots\>,p<rsub|4>|)>>.
  The difference <math|A-A<rsub|0>> is divisible by <math|q<rsub|a>> as a
  Laurent row. To see this coefficientwise, replace each monomial in that
  coordinate by

  <\equation*>
    <frac|e<rsup|i*n*p<rsub|a>>-1|e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>-1>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|h=0><rsup|n-1>e<rsup|i*h*p<rsub|a>>,>|<cell|n\<gtr\>0,>>|<row|<cell|0,>|<cell|n=0,>>|<row|<cell|-<big|sum><rsub|h=n><rsup|-1>e<rsup|i*h*p<rsub|a>>,>|<cell|n\<less\>0.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  These are finite identities, including at <math|p<rsub|a>=0> by continuity.
  If the original row has weighted norm at width <math|\<eta\>>, the quotient
  has weighted norm at width <math|\<eta\>/2> bounded by <math|2/\<eta\>>
  times the original one. Each monomial produces <math|<around|\||n|\|>>
  terms of no greater absolute displacement, and

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||n|\|>*e<rsup|<around|(|\<eta\>/2|)><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>\<leqslant\><around|(|2/\<eta\>|)>*e<rsup|\<eta\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>*<space|1em><around|(|<around|\||n|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>|)>.
  </equation*>

  This proves both convergence and the claimed bound.

  Let <math|E<rsub|a>> denote exterior multiplication by the <math|a>th basis
  vector, and <math|\<iota\><rsub|a>=E<rsub|a><rsup|<math-ss|T>>> the
  corresponding contraction. The exterior identity
  <math|d<rsub|r>*\<iota\><rsub|a>+\<iota\><rsub|a>*d<rsub|r+1>=q<rsub|a>*I>
  follows on every coordinate face by distinguishing whether that face
  contains <math|a>; the two possible orders of adjoining another coordinate
  have opposite signs. Set

  <\equation*>
    R<rsub|a>=<frac|A-A<rsub|0>|q<rsub|a>>*<space|0.17em>\<iota\><rsub|a>,<space|2em>S<rsub|a>=A<rsub|0>*<around|(|I-E<rsub|a>*\<iota\><rsub|a>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:curvature-axis-lift>R<rsub|a>*d<rsub|r+1>+S<rsub|a>=A.
  </equation>

  For <math|q<rsub|a>\<ne\>0>, multiply this identity by <math|q<rsub|a>> and
  use <math|A*d<rsub|r>=0> and <math|A<rsub|0>*d<rsub|r>=q<rsub|a>*A<rsub|0>*E<rsub|a>>;
  the latter follows from the cycle identity at <math|p<rsub|a>=0>. This
  proves it there, and continuity proves it also at <math|q<rsub|a>=0>.
  Multiplication on the right by <math|d<rsub|r>> shows that <math|S<rsub|a>>
  remains a cycle. The row <math|S<rsub|a>> has zero columns on faces
  containing <math|a>, and on the other columns it simply evaluates
  <math|p<rsub|a>> at zero.

  Apply this operation successively in the four coordinate directions.
  Previously vanished columns remain zero. Every positive-degree face
  contains some coordinate, so the final residual is zero. Summing the four
  successive <math|R<rsub|a>> gives a row <math|\<cal-L\><around|(|A|)>> with

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-L\><around|(|A|)>*d<rsub|r+1>=A.
  </equation*>

  There are only four operations. The width is reduced a fixed number of
  times and the cost is multiplied by a fixed constant; any scalar scale
  factor in the initial bound is retained. Transposition gives the
  left-handed lift. All these identities hold on every finite period by
  Fourier diagonalization. Conversely, an exponentially summable row identity
  valid on all finite periods holds for all real symbols: the finite Fourier
  frequencies are dense and the symbols are continuous.

  We shall also need a two-sided version. Suppose a two-form row <math|E>
  satisfies <math|E*d<rsub|1>=0> and <math|d<rsub|1><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*E=0>
  on every period. Let <math|W> be a left lift, so
  <math|d<rsub|2><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*W=E>, and put <math|V=W*d<rsub|1>>. Then
  <math|d<rsub|2><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*V=0>. Lift once more on the left to
  obtain <math|T> with <math|d<rsub|3><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*T=V>. The row
  <math|T*d<rsub|0>> is annihilated on the left by
  <math|d<rsub|3><rsup|<math-ss|T>>>. A further left lift would take values
  in five-forms, whose coordinate space is zero in dimension four. Hence
  <math|T*d<rsub|0>=0>. The right lift therefore gives <math|X*d<rsub|1>=T>.
  Consequently

  <\equation*>
    W<rprime|'>=W-d<rsub|3><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*X<space|1em><text|satisfies><space|1em>W<rprime|'>*d<rsub|1>=0,<space|2em>d<rsub|2><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*W<rprime|'>=E.
  </equation*>

  A final right lift <math|C> with <math|C*d<rsub|2>=W<rprime|'>> gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:curvature-double-lift>d<rsub|2><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*C*d<rsub|2>=E.
  </equation>

  If <math|E> is symmetric, replace <math|C> by
  <math|<around|(|C+C<rsup|<math-ss|T>>|)>/2>. The identity and exponential
  row bounds are preserved, and the new <math|C> is symmetric.

  <subsubsection|The unamended defect and its scale factor>

  For one flight let <math|H<rsub|c>,B<rsub|c>> and
  <math|H<rsub|f>,B<rsub|f>> be the reciprocal pulse and dual-pulse rows on
  the coarse and fine lattices. Let <math|T> be its two-form transport and
  <math|A<rsub|0>> its one-form transport. The conditional mean is denoted by
  <math|Q>. The exact unamended transport identities are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:curvature-tangent>T<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*d<rsub|1,f>=d<rsub|1,c>*Q,<space|2em>T*H<rsub|c>*d<rsub|1,c>=H<rsub|f>*d<rsub|1,f>*A<rsub|0>,<space|2em>Q*A<rsub|0>=I-k<rsup|-1>*d<rsub|1,c><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*H<rsub|c>*d<rsub|1,c>.
  </equation>

  These identities were proved for the original kernel in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:kernel-transport-identities>; the amendment
  does not alter them on exact forms, by <eqref|eq:positive-amendment-intertwine>
  and <math|G*d<rsub|1>=H*d<rsub|1>>. Define

  <\equation*>
    Z<rsub|0>=B<rsub|c>-T<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*B<rsub|f>*T-k<rsup|-1>*d<rsub|1,c>*d<rsub|1,c><rsup|<math-ss|T>>,<space|2em>E=H<rsub|c>*Z<rsub|0>*H<rsub|c>.
  </equation*>

  The matrices are symmetric. Moreover

  <\equation*>
    T<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*B<rsub|f>*T*H<rsub|c>*d<rsub|1,c>=T<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*d<rsub|1,f>*A<rsub|0>=d<rsub|1,c>*<around*|(|I-k<rsup|-1>*d<rsub|1,c><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*H<rsub|c>*d<rsub|1,c>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Substituting this in <math|Z<rsub|0>*H<rsub|c>*d<rsub|1,c>> gives zero.
  Thus <math|E*d<rsub|1,c>=0>, and symmetry gives the left cycle identity.
  The two-sided lift yields a symmetric row <math|C> such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:curvature-unamended-factor>Z<rsub|0>=<around|(|d<rsub|2,c>*B<rsub|c>|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*C*<around|(|d<rsub|2,c>*B<rsub|c>|)>.
  </equation>

  Indeed multiply <eqref|eq:curvature-double-lift> by <math|B<rsub|c>> on
  both sides and use <math|B<rsub|c>*H<rsub|c>=H<rsub|c>*B<rsub|c>=I>.

  We check uniformity in the block side <math|L>. The dual transport and the
  row <math|B<rsub|f>*T> have entrywise strip cost
  <math|O<around|(|L<rsup|-2>|)>>, including their required cover
  differences, by the wire estimates of Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-free-kernel-estimates>
  and <reference|sec:ex-lattice-free-kernel-components>. The Gram product
  <math|T<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*B<rsub|f>*T> sums over <math|L<rsup|4>> fine
  residues and a fixed number of form labels. Its entrywise cost is
  consequently <math|O<around|(|L<rsup|4>*L<rsup|-2>*L<rsup|-2>|)>=O<around|(|1|)>>.
  The other rows in <math|E> have uniform costs. All lifts use fixed exterior
  index sets on the coarse side, so the resulting cost of <math|C> is uniform
  in <math|L> too.

  There is a second lift with a stronger scale gain. Put

  <\equation*>
    Y=d<rsub|2,f>*B<rsub|f>*T*H<rsub|c>.
  </equation*>

  The second identity in <eqref|eq:curvature-tangent> gives
  <math|Y*d<rsub|1,c>=d<rsub|2,f>*d<rsub|1,f>*A<rsub|0>=0>. Its right lift is
  a row <math|S> with <math|S*d<rsub|2,c>=Y>. One fine exterior difference is
  a signed sum of at most four cover differences of <math|B<rsub|f>*T>; the
  first difference estimate supplies one additional <math|L<rsup|-1>>. Thus
  each entry of <math|Y>, and then of <math|S>, has strip cost
  <math|O<around|(|L<rsup|-3>|)>>. Multiplying the lift identity by
  <math|B<rsub|c>> gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:curvature-charge-lift>S*d<rsub|2,c>*B<rsub|c>=d<rsub|2,f>*B<rsub|f>*T.
  </equation>

  For this mixed kernel, perform the right lift separately for each fine
  residue and fine three-form label. These indices belong to the output space
  of the lift; the division and contraction act only on its coarse form
  columns. No sum over the <math|L<rsup|4>> output residues occurs in the
  lift bound. Thus its constant remains independent of <math|L>. The row
  <math|S<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*S> now has cost <math|O<around|(|L<rsup|-2>|)>>:
  its summation has <math|L<rsup|4>> residues, while both factors cost
  <math|L<rsup|-3>>. More explicitly, if each row entry at a fixed residue
  has weighted coefficient sum at most <math|c*L<rsup|-3>> at width
  <math|\<alpha\>>, then an entry of the Gram row has weighted sum at most

  <\equation*>
    L<rsup|4>*n<rsub|3>*<around|(|c*L<rsup|-3>|)><rsup|2>=n<rsub|3>*c<rsup|2>*L<rsup|-2>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|n<rsub|3>> is the fixed number of three-form labels. For each
  residue this is the convolution inequality
  <math|e<rsup|\<alpha\>*<around|\||x-y|\|><rsub|1>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<alpha\><around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>>*e<rsup|\<alpha\><around|\||y|\|><rsub|1>>>,
  followed by Tonelli; transposition reverses a displacement and does not
  change its weight. The same argument with two <math|L<rsup|-2>> factors
  gives the earlier uniform bound on <math|T<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*B<rsub|f>*T>.
  These are bounds on cover rows before periodization, so they hold
  simultaneously on every period. Retaining the residue count is essential.

  <subsubsection|Making the curvature row positive>

  Choose amendment coefficients <math|m,l\<geqslant\>0> for the fine and
  coarse beds. With <math|D<rsub|c>=<around|(|I+l*d<rsub|2,c>*B<rsub|c>*d<rsub|2,c><rsup|<math-ss|T>>|)><rsup|-1>>,
  set

  <\equation*>
    R<rsub|0>=C+l*I-m*S<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*S,<space|2em>R=D<rsub|c><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*R<rsub|0>*D<rsub|c>.
  </equation*>

  The uniform bounds just proved give constants
  <math|C<rsub|0>,C<rsub|1>\<gtr\>0>, independent of the train and <math|L>,
  with

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||<around|\<langle\>|u,C*u|\<rangle\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|0><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>,<space|2em><around|\<langle\>|u,S<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*S*u|\<rangle\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|1>*L<rsup|-2><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  Choose <math|l\<geqslant\>C<rsub|0>+2> and
  <math|L<rsup|2>\<geqslant\>m*C<rsub|1>>. Then

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<langle\>|u,R<rsub|0>*u|\<rangle\>>\<geqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  This is positivity on the entire three-form space. No projection onto a
  momentum-dependent range is needed. The row
  <math|D<rsub|c><rsup|-1>=I+l*d<rsub|2,c>*B<rsub|c>*d<rsub|2,c><rsup|<math-ss|T>>>
  has uniformly bounded operator norm, say at most <math|K>. Therefore

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<langle\>|u,R*u|\<rangle\>>\<geqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsub|c>*u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>K<rsup|-2><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  The inverse construction of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:positive-row-producer>,
  applied to <math|I+l*d<rsub|2,c>*B<rsub|c>*d<rsub|2,c><rsup|<math-ss|T>>\<geqslant\>I>
  as in <eqref|eq:positive-amendment>, supplies a common exponential row
  bound for <math|D<rsub|c>>. Here <math|l> is already fixed; the original
  row has an upper bound uniform over the trains. Together with the preceding
  bounds on <math|C> and <math|S<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*S>, this proves a uniform
  exponential row bound for every product defining <math|R>.
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:positive-row-producer> supplies its positive
  square-root row <math|P>, uniformly over all flights of these stages.

  It remains to identify this factor with the actual amended curvature
  defect. Write

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|c>=B<rsub|c>+l*<around|(|d<rsub|2,c>*B<rsub|c>|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*<around|(|d<rsub|2,c>*B<rsub|c>|)>,<space|2em>A<rsub|f>=B<rsub|f>+m*<around|(|d<rsub|2,f>*B<rsub|f>|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*<around|(|d<rsub|2,f>*B<rsub|f>|)>,<space|2em>G<rsub|c>=A<rsub|c><rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  Equations<nbsp><eqref|eq:curvature-unamended-factor> and
  <eqref|eq:curvature-charge-lift>, followed by expansion, give

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|c>-T<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*A<rsub|f>*T-k<rsup|-1>*d<rsub|1,c>*d<rsub|1,c><rsup|<math-ss|T>>=<around|(|d<rsub|2,c>*B<rsub|c>|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*R<rsub|0>*<around|(|d<rsub|2,c>*B<rsub|c>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Using <math|<around|(|d<rsub|2,c>*B<rsub|c>|)>*G<rsub|c>=D<rsub|c>*d<rsub|2,c>>
  from <eqref|eq:positive-amendment-intertwine>, multiply by <math|G<rsub|c>>
  on both sides to obtain

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:curvature-paid-factor>G<rsub|c>*<around*|(|A<rsub|c>-T<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*A<rsub|f>*T-k<rsup|-1>*d<rsub|1,c>*d<rsub|1,c><rsup|<math-ss|T>>|)>*G<rsub|c>=d<rsub|2,c><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*P<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*P*d<rsub|2,c>.
  </equation>

  This is precisely the curvature factorization in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-rg-horizon-geometry>, with the
  actual amended beds. All formulas hold on every finite period, since they
  were constructed from the same Laurent rows.

  <subsubsection|Choosing the prices before the scale sequence>

  For each adjacent pair of stages <math|j,t>, where <math|t=j> or
  <math|t=j-1>, let <math|a<rsub|j,t>\<gtr\>0> be the lower requirement on
  the coarse amendment coefficient just obtained. Select

  <\equation*>
    l<rsub|i>=max <around|(|a<rsub|i,i>,a<rsub|i+1,i>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Then every permitted transition has <math|l<rsub|t>\<geqslant\>a<rsub|j,t>>
  and <math|l<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>0>. Its required block-size floor is finite
  because the fine amendment <math|m=l<rsub|j>> is now fixed. Combine the two
  permitted flight floors at stage <math|j> by their maximum. Combine bed and
  flight strip widths and positive lower bounds by minima, and upper bounds
  by maxima. All widths and lower bounds stay positive, since each
  combination is finite.

  Choose the stage's direct precision below its positive bed ceiling. Choose
  its block length above the flight floor. These are requirements on the
  original stage profiles and pre-length floors; they are imposed before the
  common schedule of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection>.
  They hold for both within-stage steps and a change to the next stage,
  however many times either occurs.

  At a terminal cutoff <math|N>, start with the top train and descend along
  those very block lengths. At each level use its positive bed from
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:positive-row-producer>; at each step use
  <eqref|eq:curvature-paid-factor> and the exact block completion of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-rg-horizon-geometry>. The
  latter's strip calculation inherits the prescribed iterated difference
  bounds for its unchanged <math|A<rsub|0>,T> rows, narrowing their width if
  necessary, and proves the displayed strip bounds for its remaining blocks.
  This changes neither the bed rows nor the original one-form and two-form
  transports <math|A<rsub|0>,T>. It only constructs the remaining three
  blocks from their common positive curvature factor. Taking the indicated
  finite minima and maxima at each stage gives stage-indexed prices valid for
  every <math|N>, every intermediate level and every finite period. These
  prices are fixed before the level schedule, but need not have a common
  positive lower width or a common upper cost over all stages. This proves
  the bed and curvature suppliers used by the horizon construction, using the
  original pulse transport identities proved in
  <eqref|eq:transport-installed-identities>. The attachment and bounds of the
  nonlinear path remain the separate construction step.

  <subsection|Signed lattice symmetry of the quadratic
  precision><label|sec:quadratic-lattice-symmetry>

  The path attachment uses the quadratic precision of the selected bed. We
  verify its invariance under signed permutations of the coordinate axes,
  including the change of anchor caused by reversing a bond. This is a
  property of the bilinear form on one-cochains; the curvature metric need
  not satisfy a stronger symmetry away from exact curvatures.

  Let <math|s> be a signed permutation of the four axes, acting on sites and
  momenta by the same orthogonal integer matrix. A positive bond
  <math|<around|(|x,a|)>> becomes the directed bond from <math|s*x> to
  <math|s*x+s*e<rsub|a>>. If <math|s*e<rsub|a>=-e<rsub|b>>, its value in
  positive-bond coordinates is minus the value at
  <math|<around|(|s*x-e<rsub|b>,b|)>>. This defines the pullback
  <math|R<rsub|s>> on real one-cochains. For a canonical plaquette
  <math|p=<around|(|a,b|)>>, <math|a\<less\>b>, let
  <math|r<rsub|s>*<around|(|x,p|)>> be its image with its two axes reordered
  increasingly and its lower vertex shifted for each reversed axis. Its
  orientation sign is

  <\equation*>
    \<epsilon\><rsub|s><around|(|p|)>=\<epsilon\><rsub|s><around|(|a|)>*\<epsilon\><rsub|s><around|(|b|)><math-up|sgn><around|(|s<rsub|<math-up|perm>><around|(|b|)>-s<rsub|<math-up|perm>><around|(|a|)>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|s><around|(|a|)>\<in\><around|{|-1,1|}>> is
  the sign of the image axis. In the permutation subscripts, only the
  unsigned permutation is meant. Equivalently the last factor is <math|1> if
  the two image axes are in increasing order and <math|-1> otherwise. The map
  <math|r<rsub|s>> is a permutation of canonical plaquettes on every finite
  torus and <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|s><around|(|p|)><rsup|2>=1>.

  Let <math|d> denote the discrete exterior derivative. The boundary of the
  image of an oriented plaquette is the image of its boundary, with the same
  orientation sign, so

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:quadratic-curl-rotation><around|(|d*R<rsub|s>*a|)><around|(|x,p|)>=\<epsilon\><rsub|s><around|(|p|)>*<around|(|d*a|)>*<around|(|r<rsub|s>*<around|(|x,p|)>|)>.
  </equation>

  One can verify the identity directly by substituting
  <math|<around|(|d*a|)><around|(|x,a,b|)>=a*<around|(|x,a|)>+a*<around|(|x+e<rsub|a>,b|)>-a*<around|(|x+e<rsub|b>,a|)>-a*<around|(|x,b|)>>.
  Reversing an axis replaces each affected bond by its negative at the
  shifted lower endpoint; exchanging the two axes reverses the whole
  expression. These two operations generate all the signs and permutations,
  so the formula holds also across the torus seam and for even periods.

  Assume the blocking penalty is positive and the kernel level is at least
  four, as in the retained construction. Let <math|P> be the unamended
  curvature row from Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:positive-row-producer>.
  Its Fourier symbol is diagonal in the plaquette axes. For
  <math|q\<in\><around|[|-\<pi\>,\<pi\>|]><rsup|4>\<setminus\><around|{|0|}>>,
  its entry at <math|p=<around|(|a,b|)>> is

  <\equation*>
    <wide|P|^><rsub|p*p><around|(|q|)>=\<rho\><around|(|q|)><rsup|-1>*D<rsub|a><around|(|q|)><rsup|-1>*D<rsub|b><around|(|q|)><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>\<rho\><around|(|q|)>=<big|sum><rsub|c><frac|4*sin<rsup|2><around|(|q<rsub|c>/2|)>|D<rsub|c><around|(|q|)>>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|D<rsub|c>=<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|v,c>> is the positive
  level response of the selected train away from zero. We verify
  <math|D<rsub|s<rsub|<math-up|perm>><around|(|c|)>>*<around|(|s*q|)>=D<rsub|c><around|(|q|)>>
  using the feed formula in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-free-kernel-estimates>.
  The reciprocal divisor is invariant because it is the reciprocal of the sum
  of the four chip squares <math|4*sin<rsup|2><around|(|q<rsub|a>/2|)>>. In
  the two-sided drive product, the centered scalar filters are products of
  the same even factors in the four coordinates, and the two rails for
  direction <math|c> are evaluated at opposite momenta. A sign flip exchanges
  those rails and a permutation carries the distinguished coordinate to
  <math|s<rsub|<math-up|perm>><around|(|c|)>>. Thus the two-sided drive
  product is equivariant. For mass side <math|M>, the alias set consists of
  all solutions of <math|M*z=q> modulo <math|2*\<pi\>*\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>. The
  signed permutation maps this set bijectively to the alias set over
  <math|s*q>, so reindexing the feed sum preserves the response. This uses
  reciprocal periodicity of the summand, even if centered lifts at a seam
  change by a reciprocal vector. The same argument applies to the
  constant-input feeds in the noise recursion: the new noise is the invariant
  constant <math|1>, and the earlier noise is carried by the equivariant
  alias sum. Induction over the finite train proves the identity. The sine
  squares are unchanged by sign and permuted with the same axes. Consequently
  <math|\<rho\>*<around|(|s*q|)>=\<rho\><around|(|q|)>> and

  <\equation*>
    <wide|P|^><rsub|p*p><around|(|q|)>=<wide|P|^><rsub|p<rprime|'>,p<rprime|'>>*<around|(|s*q|)>,<space|2em>p<rprime|'>=s*p
  </equation*>

  with the image axes canonically ordered. At <math|q=0> the unamended symbol
  is the identity matrix, so the same identity holds there too. Off-diagonal
  entries vanish.

  The Laurent rows are absolutely summable. Uniqueness of Fourier
  coefficients and the change of variable <math|q\<mapsto\>s*q> therefore
  give

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|p*p><around|(|z|)>=P<rsub|p<rprime|'>*p<rprime|'>>*<around|(|s*z|)>,<space|2em>P<rsub|p*q>=0*<space|1em><around|(|p\<ne\>q|)>.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|P<rsub|n>> denote the periodized row on a torus of side <math|n>.
  The lower vertex of an image plaquette has the form <math|s*x+t<rsub|p>>,
  where <math|t<rsub|p>> depends only on its axes. For two plaquettes with
  the same axes, this shift cancels from their separation. Reindexing the
  absolutely convergent periodization sum by <math|z\<mapsto\>s*z> thus
  proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:quadratic-face-symmetry>P<rsub|n>*<around|(|<around|(|x,p|)>,<around|(|y,q|)>|)>*\<epsilon\><rsub|s><around|(|p|)>*\<epsilon\><rsub|s><around|(|q|)>=P<rsub|n>*<around|(|r<rsub|s>*<around|(|x,p|)>,r<rsub|s>*<around|(|y,q|)>|)>.
  </equation>

  For unequal axes both sides are zero; for equal axes the two signs multiply
  to one. This cancellation is why the anchor shifts cannot be ignored before
  passing to the bilinear form.

  Using <eqref|eq:quadratic-curl-rotation> and
  <eqref|eq:quadratic-face-symmetry>, expand the finite double sum:

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<langle\>|d*R<rsub|s>*a,P<rsub|n>*d*R<rsub|s>*b|\<rangle\>>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|u,v>\<epsilon\><rsub|s><around|(|u|)>*\<epsilon\><rsub|s><around|(|v|)>*<around|(|d*a|)>*<around|(|r<rsub|s>*u|)>*P<rsub|n>*<around|(|u,v|)>*<around|(|d*b|)>*<around|(|r<rsub|s>*v|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|u,v><around|(|d*a|)>*<around|(|r<rsub|s>*u|)>*P<rsub|n>*<around|(|r<rsub|s>*u,r<rsub|s>*v|)>*<around|(|d*b|)>*<around|(|r<rsub|s>*v|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=<around|\<langle\>|d*a,P<rsub|n>*d*b|\<rangle\>>.>>>>
  </align*>

  The last equality reindexes by the permutation <math|r<rsub|s>>.

  The positive amendment <eqref|eq:positive-amendment> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:positive-row-producer> vanishes on exact
  curvatures. In the notation there, <math|P> is the pulse row <math|H> and
  the amended metric is <math|M=G>; the identity
  <math|G*d<rsub|1>=H*d<rsub|1>> proved after <eqref|eq:positive-amendment>
  holds on every period. Hence <math|d<rsup|\<ast\>>*M<rsub|n>*d=d<rsup|\<ast\>>*P<rsub|n>*d>,
  and the quadratic bilinear form of <math|M> satisfies

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<langle\>|R<rsub|s>*a,d<rsup|\<ast\>>*M<rsub|n>*d*R<rsub|s>*b|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|a,d<rsup|\<ast\>>*M<rsub|n>*d*b|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation*>

  For a Lie-algebra-valued field, expand in an orthonormal colour basis. The
  signed lattice pullback acts separately in every colour, and the gauge
  quadratic form is the sum of the real bilinear forms just considered.
  Summing their equalities proves the required invariance for every pair of
  such fields and every period. The positive square root used to represent
  the amended metric is not needed in this symmetry argument.

  <subsection|Finite-scale propagation of the neutral curvature
  representative><label|sec:ex-lattice-rg-construction>

  The organization by successive localized effective actions follows
  Baªaban's small-field renormalization group
  <cite-detail|Balaban87RG1|ŸŸ0--2>. The scale selection and the estimates
  for the marked observables used here are developed below.

  This section records the finite-scale construction that supplies the scalar
  recurrence used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-flat-rate>. It
  starts from a terminal difference of two quadratic precisions, propagates a
  representative of that difference modulo linear-curvature null directions,
  and controls its cost at every earlier level. The one-step operation is the
  local Schur and matching argument of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-flat-step>;
  the present section supplies its actual finite-history inputs and closes
  the resulting recurrence. No limiting argument is used here.

  <subsubsection|Data on a finite interval of levels>

  For each <math|x> in an index set <math|X>, fix one schedule, a measuring
  level <math|k<rsub|x>>, and finite indices
  <math|i<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>N<rsub|x>\<leqslant\>n<rsub|x>>.
  In formulas for one fixed <math|x>, suppress its subscript and write

  <\equation*>
    J<rsub|i>=J<around|(|i|)>,<space|2em>L<rsub|i>=<around|\||<text|block at
    step >i|\|>,<space|2em>t<rsub|i>=log a<rsub|i><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>\<Omega\><rsub|i>=\<Omega\><around|(|J<rsub|i>|)>,<space|2em>\<sigma\><rsub|i>=w<around|(|J<rsub|i>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|a<rsub|i>> is the positive length at level <math|i>. The
  schedule identities are

  <\equation*>
    t<rsub|i+1>-t<rsub|i>=log L<rsub|i>,<space|2em>L<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>3.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|\<cal-F\>> be the finite set of face labels and put
  <math|m=<around|\||\<cal-F\>|\|>>. For a horizon metric <math|H> at a torus
  side <math|c>, let <math|D<rsub|c>> be the discrete exterior derivative
  from one-cochains to two-cochains and write its quadratic precision as

  <\equation*>
    <with|math-font|cal*|Q>*<around|(|H,c|)>=D<rsub|c><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*H<rsub|c>*D<rsub|c>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|H<rsub|c>> is the finite matrix obtained from the metric by
  periodization. The packets below represent differences of these forms by
  periodizing a cover curvature matrix between the two copies of
  <math|D<rsub|c>>. The scale allotment for adjacent levels and its one-step
  null correction are

  <\equation>
    \<cal-A\><rsub|i>=m<rsup|2>*e<rsup|4>*\<Omega\><rsub|i+1><rsup|2>*\<Omega\><rsub|i>,<space|2em>\<nu\><rsub|i><around|(|u|)>=<frac|2*m<rsup|2>*\<cal-A\><rsub|i><rsup|2>|u*L<rsub|i>>*<space|1em><around|(|u\<gtr\>0|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-rg-coin>
  </equation>

  These quantities are nonnegative. Indeed, the metric row bounds imply
  <math|\<Omega\><rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>. The expression
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>> is the allotment entering the two order-two
  response-kernel estimates. The factor <math|m<rsup|2>> in
  <math|\<nu\><rsub|i>> counts the two finite face labels; the factor
  <math|L<rsub|i><rsup|-1>> and the factor <math|2/u> are the centered
  null-packet correction.

  Assume the following finite data.

  <\enumerate>
    <item>For every member <math|x> of an index set <math|X>, there is a
    horizon of metrics <math|H<rsub|x>*<around|(|N,i|)>> defining quadratic
    precisions, with metric row bounds at widths
    <math|\<alpha\><around|(|J<rsub|i>|)>> and costs
    <math|\<Omega\><rsub|i>>. These bounds include the diagonal, crossing,
    and torus-floor estimates used in the one-step lemma. For every
    <math|i<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>i\<less\>N<rsub|x>>, the
    two-form transport row of the horizon tower at
    <math|<around|(|N<rsub|x>,i|)>> equals the corresponding row of the
    order-two Wilson flight from level <math|i+1> to level <math|i>, as a
    cross-lattice kernel before periodization.

    <item>There is a positive width curve <math|w>, with
    <math|w<around|(|k|)>\<leqslant\>\<alpha\><around|(|k|)>>, such that for
    each <math|x> and every <math|i<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>i\<less\>N<rsub|x>>,

    <\equation>
      \<sigma\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>min <around|{|1,\<alpha\><around|(|J<rsub|i>|)>,\<alpha\><around|(|J<rsub|i+1>|)>|}>,<space|2em>2*\<sigma\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|i>*\<sigma\><rsub|i+1>,<space|2em>\<nu\><rsub|i><around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|i+1>|)>\<leqslant\>exp
      <around*|(|-\<theta\>*<around|(|t<rsub|i+1>-t<rsub|i>|)>|)><label|eq:ex-lattice-rg-funding>
    </equation>

    for one fixed <math|\<theta\>\<gtr\>0>.

    <item>The terminal allowance <math|T<rsub|x,N>=2*m*\<Omega\><rsub|N>> has
    a uniform polynomial age bound

    <\equation>
      T<rsub|x,N>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|0>*<space|0.17em>\<frak-a\><rsub|x><around|(|N|)><rsup|d>,<space|2em>\<frak-a\><rsub|x><around|(|i|)>=2+Z<rsub|x>+<around|\||t<rsub|i>-t<rsub|k<rsub|x>>|\|>,<label|eq:ex-lattice-rg-topcap>
    </equation>

    with <math|Z<rsub|x>\<geqslant\>0>, integer <math|d\<geqslant\>0>, and
    <math|A<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0> independent of <math|x,N,n>.
  </enumerate>

  The three items record the plan and funding conditions used below; the
  terminal polynomial bound is part of the funding data. The first inequality
  in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-rg-funding> keeps all response kernels inside both
  adjacent analytic widths. The second is the block-folding requirement. The
  third pays the exact one-step null-packet cost.

  <subsubsection|Production of the funded width and top
  allowance><label|subsec:ex-lattice-rg-funded-producer>

  For a map price <math|X>, put

  <\equation*>
    \<omega\><rsub|j>=min <around|{|1,\<alpha\><rsub|X><around|(|j|)>,\<alpha\><rsub|X>*<around|(|j+1|)>|}>,<space|2em>A<rsub|X>*<around|(|j,u|)>=m<rsup|2>*e<rsup|4>*\<Omega\><rsub|X><around|(|j|)><rsup|2>*\<Omega\><rsub|X><around|(|u|)>,
  </equation*>

  <\equation*>
    b<rsub|X>*<around|(|j,u|)>=m*A<rsub|X>*<around|(|j,u|)>*<around*|(|m*<frac|2|\<omega\><rsub|j>>*A<rsub|X>*<around|(|j,u|)>|)>=<frac|2*m<rsup|2>*A<rsub|X>*<around|(|j,u|)><rsup|2>|\<omega\><rsub|j>>.
  </equation*>

  <\equation*>
    E<rsub|X><around|(|j|)>=<around*|\<lceil\>|<frac|2|\<omega\><rsub|j>>+b<rsub|X>*<around|(|j,j|)><rsup|2>+b<rsub|X>*<around|(|j,j-1|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>.
  </equation*>

  We call <math|E<rsub|X><around|(|j|)>> the entrance ticket at stage
  <math|j>; its last two summands are the two adjacent row prices. The
  schedule satisfies <math|J<rsub|i+1>=J<rsub|i>> or <math|J<rsub|i>+1>.
  Suppose the selected floors give <math|E<rsub|X><around|(|J<rsub|i+1>|)>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|i>>
  and the delay receipt gives <math|\<Omega\><rsub|X><around|(|J<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>>. Then the world width is
  <math|w<around|(|j|)>=\<omega\><rsub|j>> and <math|\<theta\>=1/2>.

  The width is positive, at most one, and no larger than
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|X>>. If the stage rises, its bridge width is exactly
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|J<rsub|i>>>; if the stage stays fixed, the bridge is
  <math|min <around|{|1,\<alpha\><rsub|X><around|(|J<rsub|i>|)>|}>\<geqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|J<rsub|i>>>.
  The entrance summand <math|2/\<omega\><rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>> implies
  <math|L<rsub|i>*\<omega\><rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>\<geqslant\>2>, while
  <math|w<around|(|J<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>1>, proving
  <math|2*w<around|(|J<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|i>*w<around|(|J<rsub|i+1>|)>>.

  Since <math|J<rsub|i>> is either <math|J<rsub|i+1>> or its predecessor,
  <math|b<rsub|X>*<around|(|J<rsub|i+1>,J<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|i>>.
  The quote, by the definitions of allotment and neutral coin, is exactly

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|quote><rsub|i><around|(|w<around|(|J<rsub|i+1>|)>|)>=<frac|2*m<rsup|2>*A<rsub|X>*<around|(|J<rsub|i+1>,J<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>|w<around|(|J<rsub|i+1>|)>*L<rsub|i>>=<frac|b<rsub|X>*<around|(|J<rsub|i+1>,J<rsub|i>|)>|L<rsub|i>>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|i><rsup|-1/2>=e<rsup|-<around|(|t<rsub|i+1>-t<rsub|i>|)>/2>.
  </equation*>

  This proves the paid condition and fixes its exponent from the actual
  entrance-price formula.

  For the top allowance let <math|s<rsub|G>\<gtr\>0> be the gauge slope
  constant <eqref|eq:shoot-slope> and choose <math|Z<rsub|x>=3+log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|x>>>. The selected spacing hypothesis
  includes <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|x>>\<gtr\>1>; therefore
  <math|Z<rsub|x>=3+log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|x>>\<geqslant\>0>. The
  schedule's delayed-scale inequality and <math|log r\<leqslant\>r-1> imply
  <math|log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|x>>+s<rsub|G>*<around|\||t<rsub|i>-t<rsub|k<rsub|x>>|\|>>.
  Consequently

  <\equation*>
    \<Omega\><rsub|X><around|(|J<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+s<rsub|G>|)>*<around|(|2+Z<rsub|x>+<around|\||t<rsub|i>-t<rsub|k<rsub|x>>|\|>|)>.
  </equation*>

  As <math|T<rsub|x,N>=2*m*\<Omega\><rsub|X><around|(|J<rsub|N>|)>>, the top
  cap holds with degree one and constant <math|2*m*<around|(|1+s<rsub|G>|)>>.
  Thus the full funding conclusion follows from the selected integer entrance
  floor and the post-length delay, rather than being an independent
  large-scale assumption.

  <subsubsection|Production of the horizon rows and exact tower
  identity><label|subsec:ex-lattice-rg-horizon-producer>

  The exact input consumed below is a positive horizon metric with the stated
  diagonal, crossing, floor and row bounds, together with the identity of its
  two-form transport row and the corresponding row of the order-two flight
  from <math|i+1> to <math|i> as cross-lattice kernels. Their periodizations
  then agree on every required finite torus.
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-rg-horizon-geometry> constructs
  the Gram completion and verifies its root, block and harmonic identities,
  then explains their application to the selected schedule. That construction
  uses the priced bed and flight receipts and the retained row and path
  bounds stated there; the block identities alone do not produce those
  inputs. On the selected run, the construction binds the resulting flat
  estimate to that run's own schedule and horizon, using its retained length
  floors and post-length stage payment. On the public route the world theorem
  uses auxiliary shadow levels and an auxiliary retained horizon;
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-flat-same-output> transfers its
  quadratic output to the selected run. This auxiliary factorization does not
  assert equality of all horizon data or higher-order kernels. Those payments
  and the horizon estimates remain inputs to this application. We use the
  earlier construction rather than repeating it here.

  <subsubsection|The selected run's nonlinear one-step
  supplier><label|subsec:ex-lattice-volant-producer>

  At the selected adjacent levels, let <math|L> be the block side,
  <math|\<beta\>> the lower scale, <math|B<rsub|0>,B<rsub|1>> the lower and
  upper budget radii, <math|b,e<rsub|1>> the lower and upper support
  exponents, <math|\<mu\>> the shape strength, and <math|Q> the menu target.
  For a finite set <math|V> of markers with positive weights
  <math|r<rsub|v>>, write

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|price><rsub|r><around|(|P|)>=<big|sum><rsub|I><around|\||P<rsub|I>|\|>*<big|prod><rsub|v\<in\>I>r<rsub|v>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|I> ranges over a downward-closed family of subsets of <math|V>
  containing the empty set and all singletons, the allowed modes of <math|P>.
  The target has a nonnegative integer halo size <math|Q.<math-up|halo>>, a
  real geometric exponent <math|Q.<math-up|relative>>, and a decay exponent
  <math|\<rho\>=Q.<math-up|rate>\<in\><around|(|-1/2,0|)>>. With the selected
  positive gauge slope <math|s<rsub|G>>, put

  <\equation*>
    \<beta\><rsub|F>=\<beta\>+s<rsub|G>*log
    L,<space|2em><math-up|preCost><around|(|h|)>=<around|(|2*<around|(|h+6|)>+1|)><rsup|4>,<space|2em><math-up|siteFactor><around|(|B|)>=e*<around|{|2*<around|(|16*B+9|)>|}><rsup|4>,
  </equation*>

  and, at the selected coupling <math|\<beta\><rsub|F>\<gtr\>1>, define

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|ribbon><around|(|Q,L,\<beta\>|)>=\<beta\><rsub|F><rsup|\<rho\>>*exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|(|Q.<math-up|relative><space|0.17em><math-up|preCost><around|(|Q.<math-up|halo>+5|)>|)>,<space|2em>\<Delta\><rsub|G,L><around|(|\<beta\>|)>=exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<around|(|log log
    \<beta\><rsub|F>|)><rsup|2>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The shipment tariff is

  <\equation>
    N<space|0.17em><math-up|siteFactor><around|(|B<rsub|0>|)>*exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<around|(|Q.<math-up|relative>+1|)><math-up|preCost><around|(|Q.<math-up|halo>+5|)>|)>*a\<leqslant\><math-up|ribbon><around|(|Q,L,\<beta\>|)>,<label|eq:ex-lattice-volant-tariff>
  </equation>

  where <math|N> is the occupancy count and <math|a> is the available
  shipment amount. The second-row allotment is

  <\equation>
    \<frak-s\><around|(|\<mu\>,L,\<beta\>|)>=2*exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|(|-<frac|\<mu\>|16>*\<Delta\><rsub|G,L><around|(|\<beta\>|)><rsup|2>|)>,<label|eq:ex-lattice-volant-straw>
  </equation>

  where <math|\<Delta\><rsub|G,L>> is the fine-scale monitor just defined.

  The selected response produces ungrouped terms
  <math|A<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>> and <math|D<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>>, with
  support indices <math|s<around|(|i|)>> and <math|p<around|(|i|)>>,
  respectively. Let <math|a<rsub|i>,d<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0> be summable
  majorants for their weighted prices, and let <math|S<rsub|i>,T<rsub|i>> be
  their support sets. The precise paid inputs are

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-up|price><rsub|r><around|(|A<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>a<rsub|i>,>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i:z\<in\>S<rsub|i>>a<rsub|i>*e<rsup|<around|(|b+1/2|)><math-up|coverNumber><around|(|B<rsub|0>,S<rsub|i>|)>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>1,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-volant-first-ledger>>>|<row|<cell|<math-up|price><rsub|r><around|(|D<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>d<rsub|i>,>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i:z\<in\>T<rsub|i>>d<rsub|i>*e<rsup|<around|(|b+1|)><math-up|coverNumber><around|(|B<rsub|0>,T<rsub|i>|)>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<frak-s\><around|(|\<mu\>,L,\<beta\>|)>.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-volant-second-ledger>>>>>
  </align>

  Every <math|A<rsub|i>> has zero quiet coefficient and is supported in the
  assigned support <math|S<rsub|i>=s<around|(|i|)>>. Every <math|D<rsub|i>>
  is supported in the full box support <math|T<rsub|i>=p<around|(|i|)>.*1>
  and in the prescribed threshold shell. Thus the cover weights in the
  ledgers are the supports used by the resulting families themselves. The
  selected response receipts also give, for every box <math|p> and
  configuration <math|U>,

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i:p<around|(|i|)>=p><around|[|D<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>|]><rsub|\<varnothing\>>>|<cell|=<math-up|profile><rsub|p><around|(|U|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-volant-quiet>>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i:p<around|(|i|)>=p><around|[|D<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>|]><rsub|j>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|l><math-up|owl><around|(|q<rsub|l>|)><rsub|j><around|(|p,U|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-volant-field>>>>>
  </align>

  and the ungrouped expenditure identity

  <\equation>
    \<cal-D\><around|(|\<cal-E\>|)><around|(|U|)>=e<rsup|E<around|(|U|)>><math-up|pass><space|-0.17em><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i>A<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>|)>\<ast\><math-up|field><space|-0.17em><around*|(|<around|(|<big|sum><rsub|i:p<around|(|i|)>=p>D<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>|)><rsub|p>,<math-up|flags><around|(|U|)>|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-volant-play>
  </equation>

  Here <math|<math-up|pass>> is the truncated exponential
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|<around|\||V|\|>>P<rsup|j>/j!> in the finite
  marker algebra. The field coefficient identity is for each box <math|p> and
  singleton mode <math|j>; higher coefficients are the full products in the
  field polynomial.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Paid menu assembly and shipment
    conversion><label|thm:ex-lattice-volant-supplier>Assume the selected
    history provides the ungrouped paid terms and exact identities above, at
    lower exponent <math|b> and upper exponent
    <math|e<rsub|1>\<geqslant\>100>. Assume
    <math|Q.<math-up|relative>\<geqslant\>4>,
    <math|4*\<varepsilon\><rsub|Q>\<less\>e<rsub|1>>, foam at scale <math|L>,
    <math|B<rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|0>>, and parent-site occupancy at
    most <math|N>. Assume positive marker weights and the birth estimate
    displayed in the proof. The offer is a site-rooted ledger at exponent
    <math|e<rsub|1>-4> and allowance <math|a>, with the fine sites of its
    named origins in the crests, and the shipment tariff
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-volant-tariff> holds. Then, with
    <math|c=e<rsup|<around|(|Q.<math-up|relative>+1|)><math-up|preCost><around|(|Q.<math-up|halo>+5|)>>*a>,
    the birth-region weights <math|f<rsub|i>> defined in the proof satisfy,
    at every site <math|z>,

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i:z\<in\><math-up|birth><rsub|i>>f<rsub|i>*e<rsup|Q.<math-up|relativecoverNumber><around|(|B<rsub|0>,<math-up|birth><rsub|i>|)>>\<leqslant\>N<math-up|siteFactor><around|(|B<rsub|0>|)>*c\<leqslant\><math-up|ribbon><around|(|Q,L,\<beta\>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Moreover, the grouped families

    <\equation*>
      F<rsub|s><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i:s<around|(|i|)>=s>A<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>,<space|2em>K<rsub|p><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i:p<around|(|i|)>=p>D<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>
    </equation*>

    have the same rooted budgets as the ungrouped families, retain the
    support and shell conditions, and satisfy the per-box quiet and singleton
    identities and the push identity in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-volant-quiet>\U
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-volant-play>. The selected door, tuning, and
    slot-separation conditions are retained. The producers of the paid terms
    are treated separately in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-rg-multisource>;
    this theorem proves their tariff conversion and assembly.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    For menu <math|i>, write <math|n<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|birth>>> for the
    cover number of its birth region at radius <math|B<rsub|0>> and
    <math|n<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|crest>>> for the cover number of its crest
    at radius <math|B<rsub|1>>. The selected birth estimate is

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|Q.<math-up|relative>+1|)>*n<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|birth>>\<leqslant\><around|(|Q.<math-up|relative>+1|)><math-up|preCost><around|(|Q.<math-up|halo>+5|)>+<frac|5|4>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|Q>*n<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|crest>>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|Q>\<gtr\>0>. Let <math|w<rsub|i>> be the
    menu wage, equal to its intrinsic profit times its total menu fee, and
    set <math|f<rsub|i>=w<rsub|i>*exp <around|(|2*\<varepsilon\><rsub|Q>*n<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|crest>>|)>>.
    By the assumed inequality <math|4*\<varepsilon\><rsub|Q>\<less\>e<rsub|1>>,

    <\equation*>
      <frac|13|4>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|Q>\<less\><frac|13|16>*e<rsub|1>\<leqslant\>e<rsub|1>-4,
    </equation*>

    where the last inequality follows from <math|e<rsub|1>\<geqslant\>100>.
    Adding the wage weight to the birth estimate gives

    <\equation*>
      e<rsup|<around|(|Q.<math-up|relative>+1|)>*n<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|birth>>>*f<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<around|(|Q.<math-up|relative>+1|)><math-up|preCost><around|(|Q.<math-up|halo>+5|)>>*w<rsub|i>*e<rsup|<around|(|e<rsub|1>-4|)>*n<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|crest>>>.
    </equation*>

    For every named marker <math|v>, the offer gives
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|i:v\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|i>>w<rsub|i>*e<rsup|<around|(|e<rsub|1>-4|)>*n<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|crest>>>\<leqslant\>a>,
    where <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|i>> is the set of available named origins for
    menu <math|i>. Indeed, the fine site of <math|v> belongs to the crest of
    every such menu, so this nonnegative sum is bounded by the offer's
    site-rooted ledger at that site. Choose, for each positive wage, a named
    origin in the menu mode whose parent image lies in that menu's birth
    region. The birth estimate and the displayed
    <math|13*\<varepsilon\><rsub|Q>/4> margin transfer the offer to a rooted
    budget on birth regions, with exponent <math|Q.<math-up|relative>+1> and
    budget <math|c=exp <around|(|<around|(|Q.<math-up|relative>+1|)><math-up|preCost><around|(|Q.<math-up|halo>+5|)>|)>*a>.
    Apply the rooted projection estimate: if the origin family has occupancy
    at most <math|N> at each parent site and every positive-wage mode has one
    such origin in its birth support, then lowering the rooted exponent by
    one gives

    <\equation>
      <big|sum><rsub|i:z\<in\><math-up|birth><rsub|i>>f<rsub|i>*e<rsup|Q.<math-up|relative><space|0.17em><math-up|coverNumber><around|(|B<rsub|0>,<math-up|birth><rsub|i>|)>>\<leqslant\>N<math-up|siteFactor><around|(|B<rsub|0>|)>*c\<leqslant\><math-up|ribbon><around|(|Q,L,\<beta\>|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-volant-root-lift>
    </equation>

    The final inequality is exactly the purchased tariff
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-volant-tariff>; the tariff is an input from the
    schedule. Zero-wage terms contribute nothing.

    Now group terms by their support and box indices. The first ledger
    already uses exactly the supports <math|S<rsub|i>=s<around|(|i|)>>, and
    the second uses exactly the box supports
    <math|T<rsub|i>=p<around|(|i|)>.*1>. By the triangle inequality,
    <math|<math-up|price><rsub|r><around|(|F<rsub|s><around|(|U|)>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i:s<around|(|i|)>=s>a<rsub|i>>
    and <math|<math-up|price><rsub|r><around|(|K<rsub|p><around|(|U|)>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i:p<around|(|i|)>=p>d<rsub|i>>.
    Summing these bounds over fibers at a fixed site gives exactly the two
    budgets in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-volant-first-ledger>\U
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-volant-second-ledger>. Tonelli's theorem applies
    because the majorants are nonnegative and summable. Absolute convergence
    allows each finite marker coefficient to be grouped termwise, proving the
    per-box quiet and singleton identities.

    The pass is a finite coefficient operation and commutes with the
    absolutely convergent grouping of <math|A<rsub|i>>. The field operation
    is a finite sum of finite products on the marker algebra and likewise
    commutes with grouping the <math|D<rsub|i>> by box. Substituting both
    regroupings into the ungrouped expenditure identity gives the stated push
    identity, including the exponential scalar and the pass of
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|s>F<rsub|s>>. All support conditions pass to the
    unions within each fiber. This proves the assembly without assuming the
    grouped conclusion as an input.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The terminal representative>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Terminal neutral representative><label|lem:ex-lattice-rg-terminal>Let
    <math|H<rsub|x>> satisfy the row and floor bounds at level <math|N>, and
    suppose <math|0\<less\>\<sigma\><rsub|N>\<leqslant\>\<alpha\><around|(|J<rsub|N>|)>>.
    There is a summable, translation-covariant packet <math|B<rsub|N>> which
    is neutral, has folded cost at most <math|T<rsub|x,N>> at width
    <math|\<sigma\><rsub|N>>, and represents

    <\equation*>
      <with|math-font|cal*|Q>*<around|(|H<rsub|x>*<around|(|n,N|)>,c|)>-<with|math-font|cal*|Q>*<around|(|H<rsub|x>*<around|(|N,N|)>,c|)>
    </equation*>

    as a linear-curvature quadratic form on every torus side <math|c>
    occurring at level <math|N>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Set <math|Y=H<rsub|x>*<around|(|n,N|)>> and
    <math|Z=H<rsub|x>*<around|(|N,N|)>>, and define the packet by subtracting
    their saddle kernels entrywise. The row bounds give the two floor
    inequalities with the common parameter <math|\<Omega\><rsub|N>> and width
    <math|\<sigma\><rsub|N>>. For each metric row <math|R>, its saddle kernel
    is the plain translation-invariant kernel evaluated on a constant output
    position. A strip bound of width <math|\<sigma\><rsub|N>> and cost
    <math|\<Omega\><rsub|N>> makes this saddle packet's folded norm at most
    <math|m*\<Omega\><rsub|N>>: sum first over the cover position and use the
    strip bound for each of the <math|m> output labels. The triangle
    inequality therefore bounds the difference packet by
    <math|m*<around|(|\<Omega\><rsub|N>+\<Omega\><rsub|N>|)>=T<rsub|x,N>>.
    Translation covariance follows from translation invariance of each plain
    kernel.

    For neutrality, fix the root position and output face label and sum the
    packet over the cover. Absolute convergence follows from the folded
    bound. Each saddle sum is the zero-frequency row sum of its metric. The
    floor condition pins both metric zero modes to the same identity matrix;
    hence their row sums agree and the difference sum is zero. Finally, on
    each torus side <math|c>, periodizing the packet gives the difference of
    the two metric matrices. Each floor identifies its metric with the
    quadratic form obtained from its full map, so subtracting the identities
    represents the precision difference by the exterior derivative of the
    difference of those finite curvature matrices. This proves the claim
    without inverting the curl precision; the identity holds on the tested
    curvature form including its harmonic zero mode.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|One backward step and the actual kernel inputs>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|One-step predecessor><label|lem:ex-lattice-rg-one-step>Fix
    <math|i\<less\>N> and assume a packet <math|B<rsub|i+1>> represents the
    difference <math|<with|math-font|cal*|Q>*<around|(|H<rsub|x>*<around|(|n,i+1|)>,c|)>-<with|math-font|cal*|Q>*<around|(|H<rsub|x>*<around|(|N,i+1|)>,c|)>>
    on every required torus side. Suppose the two response kernels for the
    histories <math|n> and <math|N> obey order-two atlas bounds with common
    exponential width

    <\equation*>
      \<omega\><rsub|i>=min <around|{|1,\<alpha\><around|(|J<rsub|i>|)>,\<alpha\><around|(|J<rsub|i+1>|)>|}>
    </equation*>

    and cost <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>>, satisfy the city and matching
    identities with the corresponding tower carriers, and the response kernel
    used for the coarser history is a travelling kernel. Denote the
    finer-history response by <math|T<rsub|i>> and the coarser-history
    response by <math|U<rsub|i>>. If <math|B<rsub|i+1>> is neutral and
    translation covariant and has folded cost <math|p\<geqslant\>0> at width
    <math|u=\<sigma\><rsub|i+1>>, where <math|\<sigma\><rsub|i>\<gtr\>0>,
    <math|u\<gtr\>0>, <math|\<sigma\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|i>>,
    and <math|2*\<sigma\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|i>*u>, then the composed
    packet

    <\equation*>
      B<rsub|i>=B<rsub|i+1>*\<star\><around|(|T<rsub|i>,U<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation*>

    is neutral and translation covariant, represents the quadratic-precision
    difference at level <math|i> on every required torus side, and has folded
    cost at width <math|\<sigma\><rsub|i>> at most
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|i><around|(|u|)>*p>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The two kernels are the responses obtained by composing the inverse
    precision at the fine level <math|i+1>, the installed Wilson tower from
    <math|i+1> to <math|i>, and the metric at the coarse level <math|i>, once
    for cutoff <math|n> and once for cutoff <math|N>. The deep order-two
    tower estimate and the two adjacent precision row estimates give their
    order-two atlas bounds. The priced atlas initially contains
    <math|e<rsup|4*\<omega\><rsub|i>>*\<Omega\><rsub|i+1>> in its high-side
    entry; since <math|0\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1>,
    enlarging this to <math|e<rsup|4>*\<Omega\><rsub|i+1>> gives exactly the
    allotment <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>> in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-rg-coin>. The
    crossing identities for the actual towers give the required matches with
    their periodized carrier maps. The response composition has the city law
    because each factor has its own translation law and these laws add under
    convolution. The widths are <math|\<omega\><rsub|i>> for both responses.
    The atlas bounds control both weighted row and column sums, with
    nonnegative cost <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>>. Since
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>, each weight is at least one; the
    ordinary row and column sums, and their restrictions to periodization
    fibres, are therefore absolutely summable. This also justifies the
    transpose matching rule when it is used in the mixed Schur step. The
    finite folded bound on <math|B<rsub|i+1>> likewise makes each of its
    displayed periodization fibres absolutely summable. In the two response
    compositions, the column bound for <math|T<rsub|i>> (summed over input
    labels), the uniform folded bound for <math|B<rsub|i+1>>, and the row
    bound for <math|U<rsub|i>> give an absolute sum at most
    <math|<around|(|m*\<cal-A\><rsub|i>*L<rsub|i><rsup|2>|)>*p*<around|(|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>*L<rsub|i><rsup|-2>|)>=m*\<cal-A\><rsub|i><rsup|2>*p>,
    with the coarse input position and both external face labels fixed, even
    after summing over all output cover points. Restricting that sum to a
    periodization fibre proves the corresponding absolute convergence for the
    composed packet; the finite face-label sums introduce no further
    convergence requirement.

    The travel, city, and matching conditions are hypotheses of this lemma.
    These are precisely the finite matching, city, and travel hypotheses in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-flat-step>; applying its proved
    Schur and weighted-packet estimate gives the asserted identities and cost
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|i><around|(|u|)>*p>. The width, positivity, and folding
    assumptions in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-rg-funding> are exactly that lemma's
    remaining hypotheses.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Backward induction and the scalar rate>

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Uniform finite-interval flat-sector
    bound><label|thm:ex-lattice-rg-construction>Suppose that, for every
    <math|x\<in\>X>, the terminal and one-step hypotheses above hold on its
    interval <math|<around|[|i<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>,N<rsub|x>|]>> and for
    its cutoff <math|n<rsub|x>\<geqslant\>N<rsub|x>>. There exist a
    nonnegative constant <math|A<rsub|1>>, an integer <math|d<rsub|1>>, and
    an exponent <math|c<rsub|1>\<gtr\>0>, independent of <math|x>, and for
    each <math|x> packets <math|B<rsub|i>> for all
    <math|i\<in\><around|[|i<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>,N<rsub|x>|]>>, such that
    each <math|B<rsub|i>> is neutral and translation covariant, represents
    <math|<with|math-font|cal*|Q>*<around|(|H<rsub|x>*<around|(|n,i|)>,c|)>-<with|math-font|cal*|Q>*<around|(|H<rsub|x>*<around|(|N,i|)>,c|)>>
    on all required torus sides, and has folded cost

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<sigma\><rsub|i>,<math-up|fold>>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|1>*<space|0.17em>\<frak-a\><rsub|x><around|(|i|)><rsup|d<rsub|1>><around*|(|<frac|a<rsub|N>|a<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|c<rsub|1>>.
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Define the nonnegative allowance recursively by

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|N>=T<rsub|x,N>,<space|2em>C<rsub|i>=C<rsub|i+1>*exp
      <around*|(|-\<theta\>*<around|(|t<rsub|i+1>-t<rsub|i>|)>|)>*<space|1em><around|(|i\<less\>N|)>.
    </equation*>

    At <math|i=N>, the terminal lemma constructs <math|B<rsub|N>> with cost
    at most <math|C<rsub|N>>. If <math|B<rsub|i+1>> has cost at most
    <math|C<rsub|i+1>>, the one-step lemma and the funding inequality give

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<sigma\><rsub|i>,<math-up|fold>>\<leqslant\>\<nu\><rsub|i><around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|i+1>|)>*C<rsub|i+1>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|i+1>*exp
      <around*|(|-\<theta\>*<around|(|t<rsub|i+1>-t<rsub|i>|)>|)>=C<rsub|i>.
    </equation*>

    Neutrality, covariance, and the tested curvature identity are inherited
    from the one-step lemma. Backward induction therefore constructs all
    packets and proves their costs are bounded by <math|C<rsub|i>>.

    For each fixed <math|x>, the schedule times have gaps
    <math|\<Delta\><rsub|i>=t<rsub|i+1>-t<rsub|i>=log
    L<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>log 3>. The scalar estimate of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-flat-rate>, applied on this
    finite interval with terminal value <math|C<rsub|N>>, zero interior
    source, source decay exponent <math|c<rsub|0>=\<theta\>>, and the age
    <math|R+Z<rsub|x>+<around|\||t<rsub|i>-t<rsub|k<rsub|x>>|\|>>, converts
    the terminal polynomial bound <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-rg-topcap> and the
    exact recurrence for <math|C<rsub|i>> into

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|1>*<around*|(|2+Z<rsub|x>+<around|\||t<rsub|i>-t<rsub|k<rsub|x>>|\|>|)><rsup|d<rsub|1>>*e<rsup|-c<rsub|1>*<around|(|t<rsub|N>-t<rsub|i>|)>>.
    </equation*>

    The scalar estimate gives <math|c<rsub|1>=\<theta\>/2> and
    <math|d<rsub|1>=d>. The fixed <math|R> in that estimate is absorbed into
    the age because <math|2+Z<rsub|x>+<around|\||t<rsub|i>-t<rsub|k<rsub|x>>|\|>\<geqslant\>2>.
    The constants are independent of <math|x> and of the interval length.
    Since <math|e<rsup|-<around|(|t<rsub|N>-t<rsub|i>|)>>=a<rsub|N>/a<rsub|i>>,
    this is the claimed bound.
  </proof>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Role in the selected construction><label|rem:ex-lattice-rg-public-route>This
    finite-scale producer is the one used by the flat supply in the selected
    construction. The public route first selects the Wilson run with treasury
    and period funding and retains its original sheet, quote, and post-length
    payments. Its native component choice includes the flat entrance
    supplier. The flat entrance applies its world theorem to auxiliary shadow
    levels and an auxiliary retained horizon.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-flat-same-output> transfers the
    resulting quadratic output to the selected run; it does not identify all
    horizon data. The flat entrance supplier obtains an admissible spacing
    schedule and invokes the finite-history supplier whose meshing step uses
    the world construction proved above. This chapter discharges that
    row-level producer conditional on the stated funding, response-kernel,
    tower-matching, and cost hypotheses. Their origin in the complete public
    construction remains tracked in the accompanying audit; the present
    theorem alone is not the final continuum or moment limit.
  </remark>

  <subsection|Menu fees and the two response
  budgets><label|sec:ex-lattice-rg-multisource>

  This section expands the two quantitative suppliers consumed by the
  selected one-step construction. Work at one selected adjacent pair of
  levels and retain its schedule, menu family, tuned representation, and
  activity model. All sums below use the selected countable menu or field
  index sets; no new selection of menus or rates is made.

  <subsubsection|The paid fragrance response><label|subsec:ex-lattice-rg-usable>

  Let <math|i> be a fragrance label. Its order is <math|n<rsub|i>> and its
  spatial labels are <math|u<rsub|i,1>,\<ldots\>,u<rsub|i,n<rsub|i>>>. Write
  <math|c<rsub|i>> for the corresponding Ursell coefficient and
  <math|t<rsub|i,\<ell\>>\<geqslant\>0> for the selected tuned component fee,
  with <math|\<ell\>> running over the distinguished base label and the
  <math|n<rsub|i>> spatial labels. The fee majorant is

  <\equation>
    \<ell\><rsub|i>=<frac|<around|\||c<rsub|i>|\|>|<around|(|n<rsub|i>+1|)>!>*<big|prod><rsub|\<ell\>\<in\><around|{|0,1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|i>|}>>t<rsub|i,\<ell\>>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-multi-lace>
  </equation>

  A switching observable is paid at cost <math|c> when its coefficient series
  is controlled by a nonnegative majorant with total sum at most <math|c>,
  uniformly for all allowed interpolation parameters and configurations. The
  trained fragrance response is <math|s<rsub|i>*<around|(|U,t,F|)>=Y<rsub|i><around|(|<math-up|color><around|(|U,t,F|)>|)>>;
  the paid observable is

  <\equation*>
    \<tau\><rsub|i>*<around|(|U,t,F|)>=s<rsub|i>*<around|(|U,t,F|)>-s<rsub|i>*<around|(|U,0,\<varnothing\>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|F> is a finite set of selected menu indices. The product
  formula for <math|s<rsub|i>> writes it as its Ursell coefficient divided by
  <math|<around|(|n<rsub|i>+1|)>>! times the product of the component votes.
  The product has <math|n<rsub|i>+1> factors: one distinguished base position
  and the <math|n<rsub|i>> spatial positions. The switching payment for a
  product is as follows. For a finite marker set <math|I>, a real-valued
  score family <math|g<rsub|t><around|(|A|)>> represents

  <\equation*>
    f<rsub|t><around|(|F|)>=<big|sum><rsub|A\<subseteq\>F>t<rsup|<around|\||A|\|>>*g<rsub|t><around|(|A|)>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  A payment certificate supplies a nonnegative majorant
  <math|b<around|(|A|)>>, independent of <math|t> and the configuration, with
  <math|<around|\||g<rsub|t><around|(|A|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>b<around|(|A|)>>,
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|A>b<around|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>c>, and convergence of
  each score as <math|t*\<downarrow\>*0>. If <math|g<rsub|t>> and
  <math|h<rsub|t>> represent two paid observables, the score for their
  product is

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|g<rsub|t>*\<star\><rsub|t>*h<rsub|t>|)><around|(|C|)>=<big|sum><rsub|A\<cup\>B=C>t<rsup|<around|\||A\<cap\>B|\|>>*g<rsub|t><around|(|A|)>*h<rsub|t><around|(|B|)>.
  </equation*>

  Expanding the product and regrouping by <math|C=A\<cup\>B> gives the
  original weight, since <math|<around|\||A|\|>+<around|\||B|\|>=<around|\||A\<cup\>B|\|>+<around|\||A\<cap\>B|\|>>.
  The coefficient bound at <math|t=1> is the same convolution of the two
  nonnegative majorants; its total sum is the product of their sums. There
  are finitely many scores, so their limits as <math|t*\<downarrow\>*0> are
  preserved by this product. Iterating over the <math|n<rsub|i>+1> component
  votes and multiplying by the scalar coefficient gives the fee
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>> in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-multi-lace>. The zero-menu
  term is paid at the same cost; the triangle inequality for the two score
  majorants therefore pays <math|\<tau\><rsub|i>> at cost
  <math|2*\<ell\><rsub|i>>.

  Let the selected thin mixed activities have nonempty supports
  <math|X<rsub|i>>, nonnegative weights <math|v<rsub|i>>, and cover radius
  <math|B>. We use the following conditional forest input: <math|e\<gtr\>0>,
  <math|P<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0>, <math|a+e\<geqslant\>0>, and, at every site
  <math|z>,

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|i:z\<in\>X<rsub|i>>v<rsub|i>*e<rsup|<around|(|a+2*e|)>*C<rsub|B><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|0>,<space|2em><frac|4*<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*P<rsub|0>|e>\<leqslant\>\<rho\>\<less\>1.
  </equation*>

  Thus the vertex input <eqref|eq:cluster-vertex-input> holds with output
  exponent <math|a+e>, reserve <math|e>, budget <math|P<rsub|0>>, and ratio
  <math|\<rho\>>, all at the same radius <math|B>. In the selected producer
  <math|\<rho\>=\<beta\><rsub|F><rsup|Q.<math-up|rate>/2>>, with
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|F>\<gtr\>1> and <math|Q.<math-up|rate>\<less\>0>. The
  shipment conversion in Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-volant-supplier>
  supplies the menu plot used in this producer, but the remaining vertex and
  smallness inputs above are separate obligations. The source-specific
  expression for <math|P<rsub|0>> and its distinct numerical forest parameter
  are recorded in the correspondence record. Apply the zero-depth tail
  <eqref|eq:cluster-local-tail> to these supports and weights. Here <math|a>
  is the selected earned exponent and <math|e> the selected allowance; in the
  one-step data, <math|a+e=<math-up|earn>+w=r<rsub|4>>. Fix a site <math|z>
  and mark each underlying component whose full support contains <math|z>.
  The zero-depth cluster-tail estimate sums all connected clusters containing
  at least one marked component. Its geometric series is
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|m\<geqslant\>0>\<rho\><rsup|m>=<around|(|1-\<rho\>|)><rsup|-1>>,
  so the total cluster charge at <math|z> is at most
  <math|P<rsub|0>/<around|(|1-\<rho\>|)>>. For each such cluster, the
  connected-coefficient cover inequality bounds its Ursell coefficient times
  the support currency by the fee majorant of its union support. The
  resulting fragrance fee ledger is exactly

  <\equation>
    <big|sum><rsub|i:z\<in\>R<rsub|i>>\<ell\><rsub|i>*exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|(|r<rsub|4><math-up|coverNumber><around|(|B,R<rsub|i>|)>|)>\<leqslant\><frac|P<rsub|0>|1-\<rho\>>,<label|eq:ex-lattice-multi-lucent>
  </equation>

  where <math|R<rsub|i>> is the full fragrance support and <math|B> is the
  selected lower radius. Here <math|P<rsub|0>> is the pledged first-row
  allotment, and <math|\<rho\>> is the upward sail loss. This is the
  zero-depth specialization of the geometric cluster-tail estimate. No extra
  exponent margin is used. The coefficient sequence is summable with an
  explicit finite-volume factor. The selected conditions give
  <math|r<rsub|4>\<geqslant\>0>. For nonnegative fees on nonempty supports,
  sum the rooted bound over all sites in the finite lower torus. Each fee
  contributes at at least one site, with its exponential factor at least one.
  There are <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|c>|\|>> sites, so

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|i>\<ell\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|c>|\|><frac|P<rsub|0>|1-\<rho\>>.
  </equation*>

  This counts each fee once, at a site of its nonempty support. The
  finite-volume factor is needed for summability; it is not absorbed into the
  rooted budget. The rooted bound prices supports, while this summability
  justifies the countable expansion.

  The subtraction cost is <math|2*\<ell\><rsub|i>>. Multiplying the rooted
  fee sequence by two scales its rooted sum and budget by two. The selected
  stage identities identify the resulting exponent and budget as

  <\equation*>
    r<rsub|4>=e<rsub|<math-up|earn>>+w,<space|2em>d<rsub|4>=P<rsub|0>*<around|(|1-\<rho\>|)><rsup|-1>,
  </equation*>

  so the response fee ledger has exponent <math|r<rsub|4>> and budget
  <math|2*d<rsub|4>>. Its zero coefficient at the empty marker mode follows
  from the ways/color identity: when the marker set is empty, every way has
  the same value as at interpolation parameter zero, so the trained response
  equals its subtracted zero-menu value. Nonemptiness of each menu mode is
  used separately in the nilpotence calculation below. The exact locality
  statement is a noninterference implication. For each menu label <math|i>,
  marker site <math|u> in its crest image, and activity label <math|j>, if
  <math|u> is outside the support of the <math|j>th forced activity term,
  erasing switch <math|i> leaves that activity term unchanged. Consequently a
  term can depend on switch <math|i> only when its support contains the
  entire selected crest image. The implication passes to the absolutely
  convergent sum, with no cancellation argument.

  For the combined observable, the selected cash identity first holds as an
  identity of switched scalar values, for every
  <math|t\<in\><around|[|0,1|]>>, finite marker set <math|F>, and
  configuration <math|U>:

  <\equation>
    e<rsup|E<around|(|U|)>>*e<rsup|<big|sum><rsub|i>\<tau\><rsub|i>*<around|(|U,t,F|)>><math-up|gas><rsub|\<cal-A\>><around*|(|K*<around|(|t,F|)>|)><around|(|U|)>=<math-up|push><around|(|<math-up|buy><around|(|t,F|)>|)><around|(|U|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-multi-closed>
  </equation>

  Here <math|K*<around|(|t,F|)>> is the final response code, and
  <math|\<cal-A\>> is the same activity family before and after the push. To
  verify the identity, let <math|L<rsub|0>,L<rsub|1>> be the logarithm sums
  of the entrance and branched responses and let <math|G<rsub|0>,G<rsub|1>>
  be their gas polynomials. The switching calculation gives
  <math|G<rsub|1>=G<rsub|0>*e<rsup|L<rsub|0>>> and
  <math|<math-up|gas><rsub|\<cal-A\>><around|(|K|)>=G<rsub|1>*e<rsup|L<rsub|1>>>.
  The base purchase identity is <math|<math-up|push><around|(|<math-up|buy>|)>=C<around|(|U|)>*M<around|(|U|)>*G<rsub|0><around|(|U|)>>,
  where <math|C> is the selected overhead and <math|M> the homestead
  multiplier. The cash assembly gives <math|M=e<rsup|<big|sum><rsub|i>s<rsub|i>*<around|(|U,t,F|)>>>
  and the zero-menu multiplier is <math|exp
  <around|(|<big|sum><rsub|i>s<rsub|i>*<around|(|U,0,\<varnothing\>|)>|)>=<math-ss|P><rsub|<math-up|vac>><around|(|U|)>>.
  Finally, the selected exponent identity is

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|E<around|(|U|)>>*e<rsup|L<rsub|0>+L<rsub|1>>=C<around|(|U|)><math-ss|P><rsub|<math-up|vac>><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  Multiplying the switching identities and substituting this exponent
  identity reduces the left side of <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-multi-closed> to
  <math|C<around|(|U|)>*M<around|(|U|)>*G<rsub|0><around|(|U|)>>, which is
  the base purchase. The paid fees are summable, so the logarithm series
  converges absolutely and each switched scalar product is well-defined. For
  <math|t\<gtr\>0>, the triangular evaluation map
  <math|g\<mapsto\><around|(|f<around|(|F|)>=<big|sum><rsub|A\<subseteq\>F>t<rsup|<around|\||A|\|>>*g<around|(|A|)>|)>>
  is injective: induction on <math|<around|\||F|\|>> recovers
  <math|g<around|(|F|)>> after the proper-subset terms are known. Thus the
  switched scalar identity determines equal score families for every
  <math|t\<gtr\>0>; letting <math|t*\<downarrow\>*0> under the uniform
  summable score bounds gives equal limiting scores. To obtain the nilpotent
  polynomial identity, for a family of scores <math|g<rsub|t><around|(|A|)>>
  and square-zero elements <math|z<rsub|i>>, take
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|A><around|(|lim<rsub|t*\<downarrow\>*0>
  g<rsub|t><around|(|A|)>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>A>z<rsub|i>>. Addition is
  preserved by linearity. Multiplication is preserved because the score
  convolution has weight <math|t<rsup|<around|\||A\<cap\>B|\|>>>, whose limit
  is zero whenever the same marker occurs in both factors; the corresponding
  product then vanishes by <math|z<rsub|i><rsup|2>=0>. If the score family
  has payment <math|c>, the <math|n>th power has payment at most
  <math|c<rsup|n>> by the convolution estimate. Dividing by <math|n>! gives
  the summable majorant <math|c<rsup|n>/n>!, so the score limit and the
  exponential series may be interchanged coefficient by coefficient. Applying
  the product rule term by term gives the exponential identity. For the
  countable logarithm sum, the uniform summable fee majorants permit
  dominated convergence coefficient by coefficient and then summation over
  markers. This establishes the coefficientwise closed-response identity used
  in the first paid response certificate; scalar equality alone would not
  suffice.

  The full response certificate also includes a paid gas response and a
  locality statement. These are distinct obligations: the rooted fragrance
  ledger above does not imply the gas budget. The first is obtained in
  Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-lattice-rg-crowd>: the selected
  hard and soft budgets are combined into the rate price of
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-multi-capital>\U<eqref|eq:ex-lattice-multi-rate>, and
  the hard/soft fusion lemma of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:soft-log-fusion>,
  applied through the finite-product harvesting estimate, gives the gas
  payment. The arrival certificates that return the rate price are inputs
  there. The locality statement follows from the explicit crest support of
  each menu contribution.

  <subsubsection|Pricing the second response
  row><label|subsec:ex-lattice-rg-crowd>

  At level <math|m>, let <math|P> be the lower budget point, <math|\<Xi\>>
  the selected parameter profile, <math|T<rsub|0>> the shelved activity,
  <math|T<rsub|1>> the branched activity, and <math|\<alpha\>> the selected
  kinetic parameter. Write <math|S,\<sigma\>,C> for the clip, width and path
  count in <math|\<Xi\>>, and <math|H=<math-up|credit><rsub|G><around|(|\<Xi\>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|E><around|(|\<beta\>|)>>.
  The time profile and budget point satisfy the selected matching relation.
  The ordinary field request is

  <\equation>
    f=<math-up|softNeed><rsub|G><around|(|P,S,\<sigma\>,C,\<alpha\>,H|)><label|eq:ex-lattice-multi-softneed>
  </equation>

  By definition this is <math|f=<math-up|softReset><rsub|G><around|(|P,H|)>+<math-up|hitNeed><around|(|P,\<alpha\>,H|)><math-up|lowHard><around|(|P,S,\<sigma\>,C|)>>.
  Write <math|R=<math-up|raiser><around|(|P|)>> for the raised point. It
  changes only the support exponent, so its radius agrees with the base
  radius: <math|B<rsub|+>=B=P.*B>. Retain the same selected profile
  <math|\<Xi\>> for its clip, width, path count, and credit. The raised final
  demand is the base-point price which pays a vapor receipt at <math|R>:

  <\equation>
    f<rsub|+>=F<around|(|H|)>+N<around|(|P,\<alpha\>,H|)>*Q*<around|(|P,S,\<sigma\>,C|)>,<label|eq:ex-lattice-multi-raised-demand>
  </equation>

  Let <math|N<around|(|P,\<alpha\>,H|)>=<math-up|hitNeed><around|(|P,\<alpha\>,H|)>>
  and define the selected reset and soft-column prices by

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|F<around|(|H|)>>|<cell|=\<cal-P\>*<around|(|1,4*b+2,0,1/2,<math-up|resetLoss><around|(|G,P|)>,H,e+2,81|)>,>>|<row|<cell|Q*<around|(|P,S,\<sigma\>,C|)>>|<cell|=\<cal-P\>*<around|(|1,b,0,\<sigma\>/8,1,d<rsub|<math-up|sig>>*L<rsub|<math-up|low>>*<around|(|S,C|)>,e+2,C<rsub|<math-up|low>>|)>,>>>>
  </align*>

  where <math|b=B=P.*B>, <math|e=P.<math-up|supportExponent>>,
  <math|d<rsub|<math-up|sig>>> is the number of signed directions, and
  <math|C<rsub|<math-up|low>>> is the fixed low-column count. For nonnegative
  integers <math|n,W,k> and <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, the footprint price
  used here is the explicit function

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-P\>*<around|(|n,W,k,\<epsilon\>,C,\<gamma\>,a,v|)>=<frac|e<rsup|a*v>*\<gamma\>*C*<space|0.17em>n<space|0.17em><math-up|ballCost><around|(|W|)>*<space|0.17em>\<epsilon\><rsup|-<around|(|4+k|)>>*<around|(|4+k|)>!e<rsup|\<epsilon\>>|1-e<rsup|-\<epsilon\>>>.
  </equation*>

  The selected width satisfies <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0>, so both displayed
  applications have positive <math|\<epsilon\>> and a positive denominator.
  Thus <math|f<rsub|+>=F<around|(|H|)>+N<around|(|P,\<alpha\>,H|)>*Q*<around|(|P,S,\<sigma\>,C|)>>
  is the reset plus hit-weighted soft-column cost. These are the two terms in
  the enlarged- support vapor estimate at the raised point <math|R>; in
  general <math|f<rsub|+>\<ne\>f>. The estimate is used with the profile's
  clip, width and path count, and its credit
  <math|H=<math-up|credit><rsub|G><around|(|\<Xi\>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|E><around|(|\<beta\>|)>>.
  The matching relation supplies equality of power, order and support
  exponent between <math|P> and <math|\<Xi\>>. The estimate requires the
  selected local table, bounds, width and decent-row conditions, the room
  condition, and a coarse torus side length greater than <math|20>. In both
  footprint prices the exponent argument is <math|e+2>, the base budget's
  support exponent plus two; raising the budget changes the target receipt,
  not the argument at which the numerical price is evaluated. There are two
  distinct applications of the arrival certificate in the selected proof. The
  precursor is the purchased entrance response, of cost
  <math|z<rsub|0>=2*P<rsub|Q>*exp <around|(|-E<rsub|0>|)>>, where
  <math|P<rsub|Q>> is the selected pledge and <math|E<rsub|0>> its marking
  exponent. The gain inequality gives <math|2*P<rsub|Q>\<leqslant\>1>, so
  <math|0\<leqslant\>z<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>exp <around|(|-E<rsub|0>|)>>. For
  the ordinary path, the column-tail receipt gives
  <math|0\<leqslant\>p\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rsub|F><rsup|-1/8>>; the first
  arrival clause prices the precursor and the finite-product harvesting
  estimate transfers it to the branched activity at <math|<math-up|stems>>.
  For the raised path, the first-row column receipt is instead
  <math|0\<leqslant\>p<rsub|3>\<leqslant\>exp
  <around|(|\<chi\><rsub|0>|)>*\<beta\><rsub|F><rsup|-1/8>>, with
  <math|\<chi\><rsub|0>> denoting the selected cover ticket. This is the soft
  allowance paired with its raised precursor. The final vapor estimate is a
  separate local estimate and has a different budget, stated below.

  For the final row, the second rate clause of the corresponding ordinary or
  raised certificate is used with the exact same budget, clip, width, path
  count, and schedule identity. Its hypotheses are
  <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<alpha\><rsub|max>>, equality of
  the current parameters with the selected profile, and nonnegativity of the
  relevant soft demand. We first display the ordinary demand <math|f>; the
  raised demand <math|f<rsub|+>> is treated below. The ordinary clause
  returns a rate price <math|\<kappa\>> for the pair of goals

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|straw>=2<space|0.17em><math-up|lauge><around|(|L,\<mu\>/4,\<beta\>,1/4|)>,<space|2em><math-up|stems>=2<space|0.17em><math-up|lauge><around|(|L,\<mu\>/4,\<beta\>,1/2|)>.
  </equation*>

  By the definition of the exponential taper,

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|stems>=2<space|0.17em><math-up|lauge><around|(|L,\<mu\>/8,\<beta\>,1|)>,<space|2em><math-up|straw>=2<space|0.17em><math-up|lauge><around|(|L,\<mu\>/8,\<beta\>,1/2|)>.
  </equation*>

  This exact factor of two is kept in the payment: no volume factor is
  removed from the row budget.

  The rate certificate has hard goal <math|<math-up|stems>>, soft goal
  <math|f>, and target toll <math|<math-up|straw>>. Its two numerical
  conditions are

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*<around*|(|e<rsup|\<kappa\>><math-up|stems>+2*f|)>\<leqslant\>1,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-multi-capital>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<math-up|Quote><around|(|\<kappa\>,e<rsup|\<kappa\>><math-up|stems>+2*f|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|straw>.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-multi-rate>>>>>
  </align>

  Here <math|B> is the integer budget radius. The rate also requires
  <math|\<kappa\>\<geqslant\>0>, <math|0\<leqslant\><math-up|stems>>, and
  <math|0\<leqslant\>f\<leqslant\>1>. The first inequality pays the
  fourth-degree local inflation and the soft reserve; the second is the
  selected financial price. The ordinary arrival certificate and the distinct
  raised-response certificate supply these fields for their respective
  requests. To pass a paid precursor to the widened activity family, use the
  selected injection <math|u> from old activity labels to the new drawer
  labels. Put the old code at label <math|u<around|(|i|)>> and set it to zero
  at every label outside the image. The widening identity says the forced
  field at <math|u<around|(|i|)>> is exactly the old forced field at
  <math|i>, and all forced fields outside the image vanish. Thus the score
  majorant is extended by zero with the same total price; the physical
  supports at retained labels are unchanged, so the rooted hit receipt is
  transported without any site map. The payment's support-exponent allowance
  is the one supplied by the widening step.

  Set <math|b=<math-up|stems>> and <math|w=f>. The rate inflation is
  <math|\<Gamma\>=e<rsup|\<kappa\>>*b+2*w>. By definition,
  <math|<math-up|Quote><around|(|\<kappa\>,\<Gamma\>|)>=e<rsup|-\<kappa\>>*<around|(|e<rsup|\<Gamma\>>-1|)>>.
  The capital and quote inequalities above therefore give exactly the
  hypotheses and site output of the hard/soft fusion lemma in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:soft-log-fusion>:

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*\<Gamma\>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>e<rsup|-\<kappa\>>*<around|(|e<rsup|\<Gamma\>>-1|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|straw>.
  </equation*>

  That section proves the gas identity <eqref|eq:fusion-gas-identity> by a
  weight-preserving bijection of compatible families. It also proves the
  priced site estimate <eqref|eq:fusion-site-output> from
  <math|h<rsub|B>*\<Gamma\>\<leqslant\>1>. In the selected construction, the
  widening identity transports the paid hard response, and the finite-product
  harvesting estimate applies these results to the hard precursor at
  <math|<math-up|stems>> and the soft logarithmic response at <math|f>. The
  rate return inequality is exactly <math|e<rsup|-\<kappa\>>*<around|(|e<rsup|\<Gamma\>>-1|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|straw>>,
  so <eqref|eq:fusion-site-output> gives the purchased site budget. All
  overlap cases are accounted for by the compatible-family bijection,
  including eligibility and exact flag coverage.

  For the ordinary path, the second arrival clause prices the request
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-multi-softneed> directly at the straw row, using the
  selected profile equality, exact schedule identity, and within-step bound
  on <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>>. For the raised path, the
  enlarged-support local vapor estimate pays <math|f<rsub|+>> from
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-multi-raised-demand>; the cover-weighted allowance
  <math|p<rsub|3>> belongs to its earlier precursor receipt, not to this
  final vapor bound. The final vapor receipt is attached to the raised
  budget, while its second rate clause prices at the base budget point with
  the selected profile's clip, width, paths and credit. In particular, with
  raised rate <math|\<kappa\><rsub|+>> and the common radius
  <math|B<rsub|+>=B> it requires

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|2*B<rsub|+>+1|)><rsup|4>*<around|(|e<rsup|\<kappa\><rsub|+>><math-up|stems>+2*f<rsub|+>|)>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|+>>*<around|(|e<rsup|e<rsup|\<kappa\><rsub|+>><math-up|stems>+2*f<rsub|+>>-1|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|straw>.
  </equation*>

  The proof first widens the support and then harvests the finite product. In
  both cases the payment is transported to the result activity by the
  finalization identity. The ordinary paid-response certificate uses the
  first path; a separate raised branch of the construction uses the second
  path. The raised path supplies the flagged ledger at exponent <math|b+1>
  and the straw budget; the ordinary path supplies the gas payment in the
  usable certificate. Neither path follows from the other by changing only
  the exponent.

  <subsubsection|Constructing the per-box response from the paid
  rows><label|subsec:ex-lattice-rg-canopy>

  For the per-box construction, let <math|z<rsub|i>> be the normalized
  polynomial supported on the nonempty marker mode <math|S<rsub|i>>, with
  coefficient <math|a<rsub|i>/\<phi\><rsub|i>>, where <math|a<rsub|i>> is the
  menu charge and <math|\<phi\><rsub|i>=<math-up|cost><rsub|r><around|(|S<rsub|i>|)><around|\||a<rsub|i>|\|>>.
  Let <math|s*t*a*m*p<rsub|i>> be the polynomial on that mode with
  coefficient <math|a<rsub|i>>. Then

  <\equation>
    <around|[|z<rsub|i>|]><rsub|\<varnothing\>>=0,<space|2em>z<rsub|i><rsup|2>=0,<space|2em><math-up|price><rsub|r><around|(|z<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>\<phi\><rsub|i>*z<rsub|i>=s*t*a*m*p<rsub|i>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-menu-singleton>
  </equation>

  If <math|\<phi\><rsub|i>=0>, positivity of the cost on a nonempty mode
  forces <math|a<rsub|i>=0>, and these statements hold with both polynomials
  zero. If <math|\<phi\><rsub|i>\<gtr\>0>, they follow directly from the
  definition of the weighted price and the fact that the mode is not disjoint
  from itself.

  For each activity label <math|j>, configuration <math|U>, and
  <math|t\<in\><around|[|0,1|]>>, write <math|g<rsub|j,t>*<around|(|A,U|)>>
  for the switching score of the selected code. The paid certificate gives a
  nonnegative summable majorant <math|b<rsub|j><around|(|A|)>> with
  <math|<around|\||g<rsub|j,t>*<around|(|A,U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|j><around|(|A|)>>,
  and each score has a limit as <math|t*\<downarrow\>*0>. We regard these
  real score limits as complex scalars when they act on the marker algebra.
  Define the activity polynomial

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|j><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|A><around*|(|lim<rsub|t*\<downarrow\>*0>
    g<rsub|j,t>*<around|(|A,U|)>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>A>z<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  Its weighted price is bounded by <math|<big|sum><rsub|A>b<rsub|j><around|(|A|)>>:
  submultiplicativity of weighted price gives

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|price><rsub|r><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>A>z<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  The triangle inequality then gives the bound first for finite partial sums
  and then for their absolutely convergent limit. A product of two normalized
  menu factors either vanishes by a repeated marker or has support of size at
  least two. Therefore the coefficient of a singleton marker in
  <math|F<rsub|j>> comes only from singleton score sets
  <math|A=<around|{|i|}>>.

  The exact local jet receipt, proved in Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-lattice-menu-jet-proof>,
  is

  <\equation>
    lim<rsub|t*\<downarrow\>*0> g<rsub|j,t>*<around|(|<around|{|i|}>,U|)>=\<phi\><rsub|i>*D<rsub|i,j><around|(|U|)>,<label|eq:ex-lattice-menu-jet-input>
  </equation>

  where <math|D<rsub|i,j>> is the derivative of the selected one-menu final
  response at activity label <math|j> and configuration <math|U>. Using it in
  the coefficient formula above and then using
  <math|\<phi\><rsub|i>*z<rsub|i>=s*t*a*m*p<rsub|i>> gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|[|F<rsub|j><around|(|U|)>|]><rsub|<around|{|v|}>>=<big|sum><rsub|i><around|[|D<rsub|i,j><around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>s*t*a*m*p<rsub|i>|]><rsub|<around|{|v|}>>,
  </equation*>

  which is the quoted singleton coefficient. The series converges absolutely:
  the door-volume estimate bounds the hinge coefficients by the menu's
  weighted load, and the menu offer has a summable fee-load ledger.

  Group activity polynomials by the box label
  <math|p=<math-up|barcode><around|(|j|)>>:

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|p><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j:<space|0.17em><math-up|barcode><around|(|j|)>=p>F<rsub|j><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  Each fiber is countable. The paid coefficient majorants and their rooted
  hit ledger imply absolute convergence on each fiber and preserve its price
  when the terms are summed. The quiet coefficient of <math|K<rsub|p>> is the
  activity profile on <math|p>: at the empty marker set the interpolated code
  is its vacuum code, so its score reconstructs that value. Thus the profile
  term is retained, while the singleton calculation above concerns only
  nonquiet coefficients. Higher coefficients of <math|K<rsub|p>> contain all
  compatible products and are not sums of singleton responses.

  Write <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|t>*<around|(|A,U|)>> for the combined score of
  the full fragrance response; its evaluation satisfies
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|A\<subseteq\>F>t<rsup|<around|\||A|\|>>*\<cal-L\><rsub|t>*<around|(|A,U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i>\<tau\><rsub|i>*<around|(|U,t,F|)>>.
  For any such score family <math|q<rsub|t><around|(|A|)>>, define its
  limiting marker polynomial by

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|ScoreLimit><around|(|q|)>=<big|sum><rsub|A><around*|(|lim<rsub|t*\<downarrow\>*0>
    q<rsub|t><around|(|A|)>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>A>z<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  For a support mode <math|s>, define <math|<math-ss|Field><around|(|<around|(|K<rsub|p>|)>,s|)>>
  by the finite sum

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|Field><around|(|<around|(|K<rsub|p>|)>,s|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<cal-C\>:<space|0.17em><big|sqcup><rsub|p\<in\>\<cal-C\>>Q<rsub|p>=s><big|prod><rsub|p\<in\>\<cal-C\>>K<rsub|p>,
  </equation*>

  where a box is a pair <math|p=<around|(|P<rsub|p>,Q<rsub|p>|)>> consisting
  of its full support <math|P<rsub|p>> and assigned flag
  <math|Q<rsub|p>\<subseteq\>P<rsub|p>>. In every family in the sum the full
  supports <math|P<rsub|p>> are pairwise disjoint and the flags have union
  <math|s>. For the constructed family <math|K<rsub|p>>, a nonzero term has
  nonempty assigned flag: each contributing activity has nonempty inventory,
  and boxes with no contributing activities have zero coefficient. The
  general field pattern itself does not impose nonemptiness. This is the
  support pattern condition: disjoint flags alone would permit two
  interacting polymers to overlap and would not reproduce the gas. Grouping
  by barcode preserves the condition. Indeed, two nonzero compatible activity
  terms cannot have the same barcode, because that barcode has a nonempty
  full support. Thus each barcode occurs at most once in a compatible family;
  its full support is unchanged by grouping, and its assigned flag remains a
  subset of that support. Expanding the products after this bijective
  grouping gives exactly the original full-support-disjoint families and
  their flag union. Absolute summability justifies the countable regrouping.
  Because the full logarithm score has zero quiet coefficient, its marker
  polynomial is nilpotent on the finite marker algebra. Its truncated
  exponential is therefore a finite polynomial. The full pushed expression is

  <\equation>
    <math-up|push><around|(|<math-up|Expense>|)><around|(|U|)>=e<rsup|E<around|(|U|)>>*<space|0.17em>exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<math-ss|ScoreLimit><around|(|\<cal-L\><around|(|U|)>|)>|)>\<ast\><math-ss|Field><around*|(|<around|(|K<rsub|p><around|(|U|)>|)><rsub|p>,<math-up|flags><around|(|U|)>|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-canopy-push>
  </equation>

  For a finite ticket truncation, expand the purchase as its finite ticket
  sum and push each ticket term using linearity of the kernel integral; the
  lawful input makes each term integrable. The score-to-marker map preserves
  products because repeated switches vanish. Its exponential is the finite
  nilpotent exponential, and it preserves the countable fragrance sum by the
  summable majorants. Expanding the field pattern then gives
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-canopy-push>; the absolute price bounds pass to the
  countable limit. The quiet profile, singleton coefficient, support, shell,
  measurability, and gauge conditions are exactly the receipts needed for the
  per-box response. The hinge identity <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-menu-jet-input>
  and the selected geometric price receipts are upstream inputs; the next
  section proves the hinge identity itself.

  <subsubsection|What these estimates produce>

  The two row estimates have separate roles. The fragrance estimate gives a
  summable family of paid logarithmic terms, with rooted budget
  <math|2*P<rsub|0>/<around|(|1-\<rho\>|)>> at exponent <math|r<rsub|4>>. The
  field estimate gives a paid activity response at the straw budget
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-volant-straw>. Together with the selected crest
  locality and the coefficientwise cash identity
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-multi-closed>, they supply the paid-response inputs to
  the per-box assembly in Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-volant-supplier>.
  All numerical dependence on <math|L,\<beta\>,\<mu\>>, the budget radius,
  and the menu profile remains visible in the displayed prices.

  <subsection|The single-menu derivative supplies the field
  jet><label|sec:ex-lattice-menu-jets><label|subsec:ex-lattice-menu-jet-proof>

  Fix one selected step and its finite menu family indexed by
  <math|i\<in\>I>. Let <math|M<rsub|i>> be the leaf attached to menu
  <math|i>, let <math|r<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0> be its tuning weight, and let
  <math|\<phi\><rsub|i>> be its charged fee. The selected menu profile has
  <math|r<rsub|i>=\<phi\><rsub|i>>. For each activity label <math|j> and
  configuration <math|U>, let <math|K*<around|(|t,F|)><rsub|j><around|(|U|)>>
  be the final response code with switch parameter <math|t> and selected menu
  set <math|F\<subseteq\>I>. The activity payment supplies a real-valued
  score family <math|g<rsub|j,U,t><around|(|A|)>> satisfying

  <\equation>
    K*<around|(|t,F|)><rsub|j><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|A\<subseteq\>F>t<rsup|<around|\||A|\|>>*g<rsub|j,U,t><around|(|A|)>,<space|2em><around|\||g<rsub|j,U,t><around|(|A|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|j,U><around|(|A|)>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|A\<subseteq\>I>b<rsub|j,U><around|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>,<label|eq:ex-lattice-menu-score>
  </equation>

  with each score converging as <math|t*\<downarrow\>*0>. The exact selected
  interpolation identities are

  <\equation>
    K*<around|(|t,\<varnothing\>|)><rsub|j><around|(|U|)>=P<rsub|i><around|(|0|)><rsub|j><around|(|U|)>,<space|2em>K*<around|(|t,<around|{|i|}>|)><rsub|j><around|(|U|)>=P<rsub|i>*<around|(|r<rsub|i>*t|)><rsub|j><around|(|U|)>,<label|eq:ex-lattice-menu-singleton-path>
  </equation>

  where <math|P<rsub|i><around|(|\<theta\>|)>> is the final response produced
  from the single leaf <math|M<rsub|i>> at amplitude <math|\<theta\>>. These
  are not approximations: with no active switch the selected color is the
  vacuum color, and with just switch <math|i> its color is the single-leaf
  color at amplitude <math|r<rsub|i>*t>.

  The path <math|P<rsub|i>> is differentiable at zero. Before the last
  branch, its color has the form <math|c+\<theta\>*a> for fixed <math|c,a>,
  so every activity entry is affine in <math|\<theta\>>. Each selected
  fragrance entry is a finite product of such entries, raised to fixed
  integer powers and divided by a fixed factorial. If
  <math|f<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,f<rsub|N>> are differentiable at zero, then

  <\equation*>
    <around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|k=1><rsup|N>f<rsub|k>|)><rprime|'><around|(|0|)>=<big|sum><rsub|k=1><rsup|N>f<rsub|k><rprime|'><around|(|0|)>*<big|prod><rsub|\<ell\>\<ne\>k>f<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|0|)>,
  </equation*>

  by repeated use of the product rule. The final response at a fixed activity
  coordinate is itself a product over a finite set of labels; branches
  outside the selected address range are identically zero, and branches
  inside it are these finite products. Thus every coordinate
  <math|P<rsub|i><around|(|\<theta\>|)><rsub|j><around|(|U|)>> is
  differentiable at zero. Denote its derivative by

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|i,j><around|(|U|)>=<around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d|d*\<theta\>>*P<rsub|i><around|(|\<theta\>|)><rsub|j><around|(|U|)>|\|><rsub|\<theta\>=0>.
  </equation*>

  This is exactly the hinge of the selected gauge door, since that door is
  defined by differentiating the same one-leaf final response.

  For completeness, the score extraction from
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-menu-score> is triangular. For <math|t\<gtr\>0> and
  any <math|F\<subseteq\>I>,

  <\equation*>
    g<rsub|j,U,t><around|(|F|)>=t<rsup|-<around|\||F|\|>>*<around*|(|K*<around|(|t,F|)><rsub|j><around|(|U|)>-<big|sum><rsub|A\<subsetneq\>F>t<rsup|<around|\||A|\|>>*g<rsub|j,U,t><around|(|A|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Induction on <math|<around|\||F|\|>> therefore shows that all scores are
  uniquely determined by the switched values. The payment model also gives
  convergence to the zero-parameter score; the uniform summable majorant in
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-menu-score> permits passage to that limit.

  Take <math|F=<around|{|i|}>>. From <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-menu-score>,

  <\equation*>
    K*<around|(|t,<around|{|i|}>|)><rsub|j><around|(|U|)>=g<rsub|j,U,t><around|(|\<varnothing\>|)>+t*<space|0.17em>g<rsub|j,U,t><around|(|<around|{|i|}>|)>.
  </equation*>

  At <math|F=\<varnothing\>> the same representation gives
  <math|K*<around|(|t,\<varnothing\>|)><rsub|j><around|(|U|)>=g<rsub|j,U,t><around|(|\<varnothing\>|)>>.
  Subtract these equations and use <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-menu-singleton-path>:

  <\equation*>
    t*<space|0.17em>g<rsub|j,U,t><around|(|<around|{|i|}>|)>=P<rsub|i>*<around|(|r<rsub|i>*t|)><rsub|j><around|(|U|)>-P<rsub|i><around|(|0|)><rsub|j><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  Dividing by <math|t> and letting <math|t*\<downarrow\>*0>,
  differentiability at zero implies

  <\equation>
    lim<rsub|t*\<downarrow\>*0> g<rsub|j,U,t><around|(|<around|{|i|}>|)>=r<rsub|i>*D<rsub|i,j><around|(|U|)>=\<phi\><rsub|i>*D<rsub|i,j><around|(|U|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-menu-jet-proof>
  </equation>

  This is the exact menu-to-field jet identity required in
  Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-lattice-rg-canopy>. It supplies the
  fee-weighted hinge for each menu separately; the subsequent finite
  marker-algebra calculation and absolutely convergent barcode sum produce
  the per-box singleton coefficient.

  <subsection|Scalar prices for a nonlinear
  step><label|sec:ex-lattice-price-receipts>

  This section proves the scalar scale estimates for the shipment, probe,
  purchase, lodging, and credit fields of the finite-menu tariff. The
  allowance and gain estimates come from a separate arrival receipt on the
  same selected run; they are not consequences of these scalar inequalities.
  All limits below are as the scale parameter <math|x> tends to infinity,
  with the block length fixed.

  Fix a block side <math|L>, an integer occupancy bound <math|N>, a positive
  strength <math|\<mu\>>, a positive relative weight <math|\<lambda\>>, an
  exponent <math|\<rho\>\<in\><around|(|-1,0|)>>, and a fixed cost
  <math|C\<gtr\>0>. Write

  <\equation*>
    b<around|(|x|)>=x+c<rsub|L>,<space|2em>R<around|(|x|)>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>,<space|2em>M<around|(|x|)>=exp
    <around*|(|<around|(|log log b<around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|2>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|c<rsub|L>> is fixed once <math|L> is fixed. Set

  <\equation*>
    f<around|(|x|)>=b<around|(|x|)><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>y<around|(|x|)>=b<around|(|x|)><rsup|-2>,
  </equation*>

  <\equation*>
    k<around|(|x|)>=<around*|(|N*\<frak-s\><around|(|R<around|(|b<around|(|x|)>|)>|)>+4|)>*y<around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>z<around|(|x|)>=2*exp
    <around*|(|-\<mu\>*M<around|(|x|)><rsup|2>/16|)>+N*\<frak-s\><around|(|R<around|(|x|)>|)>*y<around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here

  <\equation*>
    \<frak-s\><around|(|r|)>=e*<space|0.17em><around*|(|2*<around|(|16*r+9|)>|)><rsup|4>,<space|2em>\<cal-R\><around|(|x|)>=b<around|(|x|)><rsup|\<rho\>>*e<rsup|\<lambda\>*C>.
  </equation*>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Rate calculus and decaying costs><label|lem:ex-lattice-price-rate-calculus>For
    a function <math|u>, say that it has rate <math|a> if, for every
    <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>, eventually <math|<around|\||u<around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>b<around|(|x|)><rsup|a+\<delta\>>>.
    Products add rates, sums of finitely many terms keep the larger rate, and
    multiplication by a fixed constant does not change a rate. Moreover, if
    <math|u> has rate <math|a\<less\>0>, then <math|u<around|(|x|)>\<to\>0>.

    The functions <math|R<around|(|x|)>> and every fixed polynomial in
    <math|R<around|(|x|)>> have rate zero. The same is true of
    <math|\<frak-s\><around|(|R<around|(|x|)>|)>> and
    <math|\<frak-s\><around|(|R<around|(|b<around|(|x|)>|)>|)>>. If
    <math|\<mu\>\<gtr\>0>, then the first summand in <math|z<around|(|x|)>>
    has rate less than every real number; in particular it has rate
    <math|-2>. The fixed shift yields

    <\equation>
      R<around|(|b<around|(|x|)>|)>\<leqslant\>2*R<around|(|x|)><space|1em><text|eventually>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-pressure-radius>
    </equation>

    Consequently <math|k> and <math|z> have rate <math|-2>, as does

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|2*R<around|(|x|)>+1|)><rsup|4>*z<around|(|x|)>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For products, apply the two rate bounds with exponent slack
    <math|\<delta\>/2> and use

    <\equation*>
      b<around|(|x|)><rsup|a+\<delta\>/2>*b<around|(|x|)><rsup|c+\<delta\>/2>=b<around|(|x|)><rsup|a+c+\<delta\>>.
    </equation*>

    For sums, use the same slack and the fact that
    <math|b<around|(|x|)><rsup|\<delta\>/2>> eventually exceeds the fixed
    number of summands. Fixed constants are absorbed by any positive slack.
    Since <math|b<around|(|x|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>, a negative rate implies
    convergence to zero by choosing an intermediate exponent still below
    zero.

    Since <math|b<around|(|x|)>=x+c<rsub|L>>, the ratio <math|<around|(|log
    b<around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|2>/<around|(|log x|)><rsup|2>> tends to one.
    Thus, eventually, <math|<around|(|log
    b<around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|(|3/2|)><around|(|log
    x|)><rsup|2>> and <math|<around|(|log x|)><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>2>.
    Therefore

    <\equation*>
      R<around|(|b<around|(|x|)>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|log
      b<around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|2>+1\<leqslant\><around|(|3/2|)><around|(|log
      x|)><rsup|2>+1\<leqslant\>2<around|(|log
      x|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2*R<around|(|x|)>,
    </equation*>

    as claimed. Also, the definition <math|R<around|(|x|)>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>> and the fixed-shift relation show that
    <math|R<around|(|x|)>> is bounded by a constant times <math|<around|(|log
    b<around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|2>> for large <math|x>. Applying this same
    comparison with argument <math|b<around|(|x|)>> controls
    <math|R<around|(|b<around|(|x|)>|)>>. Every fixed power of <math|log
    b<around|(|x|)>> is smaller than <math|b<around|(|x|)><rsup|\<delta\>>>
    for every <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>; hence <math|R> and its fixed
    polynomials have rate zero. The site factor is a fixed quartic polynomial
    in its argument, so it also has rate zero, including after the comparison
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-pressure-radius>.

    Finally, <math|M<around|(|x|)><rsup|2>=exp <around|(|2<around|(|log log
    b<around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|2>|)>>. For every <math|A\<gtr\>0>,

    <\equation*>
      <frac|\<mu\>|16>*M<around|(|x|)><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>A*log
      b<around|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    for all sufficiently large <math|x>, since with <math|t=log
    b<around|(|x|)>> the logarithm of the ratio
    <math|M<around|(|x|)><rsup|2>/t> is <math|2<around|(|log t|)><rsup|2>-log
    t\<to\>\<infty\>>. Taking <math|A> as large as desired proves the claimed
    decay of the Gaussian summand. The remaining rate statements follow by
    the product and sum rules just proved.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Eventually purchased tariff><label|prop:ex-lattice-eventual-tariff>Assume
    <math|\<rho\>\<in\><around|(|-1,0|)>>, <math|\<lambda\>\<gtr\>0>, and
    <math|\<mu\>\<gtr\>0>. Then, for all sufficiently large <math|x>, the
    following inequalities hold simultaneously:

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|0>|<cell|\<leqslant\>f<around|(|x|)>,>|<cell|0>|<cell|\<less\>y<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>1,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-price-basic>>>|<row|<cell|N*\<frak-s\><around|(|R<around|(|x|)>|)>*e<rsup|<around|(|\<lambda\>+1|)>*C>*f<around|(|x|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<cal-R\><around|(|x|)>,>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-price-shipping>>>|<row|<cell|k<around|(|x|)>+e<rsup|k<around|(|x|)>>*k<around|(|x|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>f<around|(|x|)>,>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-price-buy>>>|<row|<cell|<around|(|2*R<around|(|x|)>+1|)><rsup|4>*z<around|(|x|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>,>|<cell|e<rsup|z<around|(|x|)>>-1>|<cell|\<leqslant\>1.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-price-lodging>>>>>
    </align>

    The lodging and credit cost is <math|z<around|(|x|)>>, whose Gaussian
    term is

    <\equation*>
      2*exp <around|(|-\<mu\>*M<around|(|x|)><rsup|2>/16|)>.
    </equation*>

    Here the fine monitor equals <math|M<around|(|x|)>>. Thus these are
    exactly the nonnegativity, probe, shipment, purchase, lodging, and credit
    conditions required by the local tariff.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    For <math|x> large, <math|b<around|(|x|)>\<gtr\>1>, so
    <math|f=b<rsup|-1>\<gtr\>0> and <math|0\<less\>y=b<rsup|-2>\<leqslant\>1>.
    The polynomial factor has rate zero, while <math|f> has rate <math|-1>.
    Thus

    <\equation*>
      N*\<frak-s\><around|(|R<around|(|x|)>|)>*e<rsup|<around|(|\<lambda\>+1|)>*C>*f<around|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    has rate <math|-1>. Choose <math|q<rsub|0>> strictly between <math|-1>
    and <math|\<rho\>>. Eventually the left side is at most
    <math|b<around|(|x|)><rsup|q<rsub|0>>>, which is at most
    <math|b<around|(|x|)><rsup|\<rho\>>> for large <math|x>. Multiplying the
    fixed factor <math|e<rsup|\<lambda\>*C>> on the right gives

    <\equation*>
      N*\<frak-s\><around|(|R<around|(|x|)>|)>*e<rsup|<around|(|\<lambda\>+1|)>*C>*f<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>b<around|(|x|)><rsup|\<rho\>>*e<rsup|\<lambda\>*C>,
    </equation*>

    as claimed.

    By the rate-calculus lemma, <math|k> has rate <math|-2>. In particular,
    eventually <math|0\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>b<rsup|-3/2>\<leqslant\>1>. On
    a further tail, <math|b<rsup|1/2>\<geqslant\>1+e>, and therefore

    <\equation*>
      k+e*k\<leqslant\><around|(|1+e|)>*b<rsup|-3/2>\<leqslant\>b<rsup|-1>.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|e<rsup|k>\<leqslant\>e> for <math|k\<leqslant\>1>, this
    proves <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-price-buy>. The product
    <math|<around|(|2*R+1|)><rsup|4>*z> has rate <math|-2>, so it is
    eventually at most <math|1/2>. Also <math|z\<to\>0> and
    <math|z\<geqslant\>0>; hence eventually <math|z\<leqslant\>log 2>, which
    gives <math|e<rsup|z>-1\<leqslant\>1>. This proves all the claims.
  </proof>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Placement in the selected construction><label|rem:ex-lattice-price-receipt-placement>On
    the selected run, the Gaussian pressure estimate supplies this tariff.
    The arrival receipt supplies local activity, allowance, and gain bounds;
    the reset receipt supplies separate rate payments for soft response. The
    delay is enlarged to satisfy all finitely many transitions at each stage.
    The finite-menu estimate uses arrival and reset as distinct inputs:
    neither is a consequence of the tariff. Its shipment budget is the tariff
    inequality proved above, combined with the predecessor's paid activity
    bounds.
  </remark>

  <subsection|The paid menu response on the selected
  history><label|sec:finite-menu-history-handoff>

  The centered-field argument requires the menu response and the ordinary
  transition to belong to one history. We now identify their parameters and
  prices. The analytic inputs are the funded path and Gaussian data used in
  the preceding one-step estimates; this step makes no new choice of a
  background transition or of a door transporting insertions.

  Fix a finite history, adjacent scales <math|m+1,m>, and one point of its
  interpolating path. Denote the fine representation by <math|R<rsub|f>>, the
  result by <math|R<rsub|c>>, and the corresponding local coordinate change
  and insertion transport by <math|\<cal-D\>>. The construction of the
  unmarked result fixes all three. Use the same tuned representation, forced
  array and derivative of the one-leaf response when constructing the menu
  terms of Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-rg-multisource> and
  <reference|sec:ex-lattice-menu-jets>. Their quiet identity is therefore the
  profile of <math|R<rsub|c>>, and their singleton identity uses the hinge of
  precisely <math|\<cal-D\>>.

  Write <math|b<rsub|f>,b<rsub|c>\<geqslant\>100> for the support exponents,
  <math|N> for the finite occupancy bound, and <math|u\<geqslant\>0> for the
  menu shipment allowance. A menu offer is measured at exponent
  <math|b<rsub|f>-4> and allowance <math|u>. Its marker weights are positive,
  its modes nonempty, and its named sites lie in the corresponding fine
  crests. The occupancy bound is on their images under the parent map. It
  also bounds occupancy at each fine site, because markers with the same fine
  site have the same parent. The birth-region conversion subsequently places
  a chosen parent image in the corresponding coarse birth support. The
  shipment inequality of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-price-receipts>,
  together with the birth-region conversion in
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-volant-supplier>, gives the rooted
  majorants required for the paid component construction. This is uniform
  over every finite menu satisfying the offer bound.

  Let <math|a> be the earned exponent and <math|e\<gtr\>0> the additional
  reserve, so <math|a\<geqslant\>b<rsub|c>+3>. Let
  <math|P<rsub|0>,P<rsub|1>\<geqslant\>0> be the ordinary and flagged
  component budgets, <math|0\<leqslant\>\<rho\>\<leqslant\>1/2> the cluster
  ratio, and <math|E<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0> the marked exponential cost. The
  field-price calculation gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:menu-total-pledge>4*e<rsup|E<rsub|0>>*P<rsub|1>+4*<frac|P<rsub|0>|1-\<rho\>>\<leqslant\>V<rsub|Q>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>.
  </equation>

  Here the first inequality follows from the same component majorants used in
  the cluster sums. To make its numerical content explicit, the total pledge
  is <math|V<rsub|Q>=10*<around|(|P<rsub|0>+E<rprime|'>|)>>, with
  <math|P<rsub|1>=E<rprime|'>*e<rsup|-E<rsub|0>>>,
  <math|E<rprime|'>\<geqslant\>0>. Since <math|<around|(|1-\<rho\>|)><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>2>,
  its left side is at most <math|4*E<rprime|'>+8*P<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>10*<around|(|E<rprime|'>+P<rsub|0>|)>>.
  The upper bound <math|V<rsub|Q>\<leqslant\>1/2> is purchased by increasing
  the entry coupling: both <math|P<rsub|0>> and <math|E<rprime|'>> have the
  fixed negative upper exponent of the component estimates. This is the same
  arrival threshold used to pay their degree prices, so all inequalities hold
  together.

  The logarithmic response has the rooted bound
  <math|2*P<rsub|0>/<around|(|1-\<rho\>|)>> at exponent <math|a+e>, by
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-multi-lucent>. In particular it is at most <math|1>,
  indeed at most <math|1/4>, by <eqref|eq:menu-total-pledge>. Replacing its
  exponent by <math|b<rsub|c>+1/2> only decreases each nonnegative summand,
  since <math|b<rsub|c>+1/2\<leqslant\>a+e> and all cover numbers are
  nonnegative. The flagged response has the separate rooted bound

  <\equation*>
    t=2*exp <around|{|-\<mu\>*\<Delta\><rsub|G,L><around|(|\<beta\>|)><rsup|2>/16|}>
  </equation*>

  at exponent <math|b<rsub|c>+1>. This is the second, larger-reserve response
  from Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-rg-multisource>, paid by the
  two successive absorptions of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:nonlinear-payment-selection>.
  It is not deduced from the logarithmic bound.

  The paid marker substitution and grouping now yield ordinary and flagged
  families with exactly the ledgers <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-volant-first-ledger>
  and <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-volant-second-ledger>. Their supports, threshold
  shells and quiet and singleton identities are those of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-volant-supplier>. The full push
  identity retains the exponential of the ordinary polynomial and the
  complete flagged gas. In particular no linearity of that full expression in
  the marker families is used. The derivative construction fixes the tuning
  law, and the retained regular and forced slots provide the input labels
  used by the offer. Thus the response is available for the same door
  <math|\<cal-D\>> and the same pair <math|R<rsub|f>,R<rsub|c>>.

  This response can supply the finite-list integration input
  <eqref|eq:transfer-nonlinear-input> when the prepared elementary list from
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-transfer> is an offer for this same
  transition and door, with its fine sites in the corresponding crests and
  its parent-site occupancy and price within the bounds above. The ordinary
  and flagged output exponents and prices then agree with the specialization
  in that section, and the quiet, singleton, and full push identities are the
  required ones. The preparation of the list, its transition-slice
  integrability, and the single-insertion identities are separate hypotheses;
  this application does not replace their construction.

  <subsubsection|Identifying the centered transfer allowances>

  Let <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>> be the selected running coupling and
  <math|L> the block side at this step. Their exact relation is

  <\equation*>
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>=\<beta\><rsub|F>*<around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>,L|)>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>+s<rsub|G>*log
    L.
  </equation*>

  The fine and coarse radii are correspondingly
  <math|B<rsub|f>=B<around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>|)>> and
  <math|B<rsub|c>=B<around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|)>>. Take the offer
  allowance and the single-insertion price to be

  <\equation*>
    u=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>\<delta\>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1><rsup|-2>.
  </equation*>

  These are the tariff's station and probe prices; they are evaluated at the
  same fine coupling. The tariff proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-price-receipts> says precisely
  that <math|u\<geqslant\>0>, <math|0\<less\>\<delta\>\<leqslant\>1>, and

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|T+e<rsup|T>*T>|<cell|\<leqslant\>u,>|<cell|T>|<cell|=N*\<frak-s\><around|(|B<rsub|f>|)>*\<delta\>+4*\<delta\>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|(|2*B<rsub|c>+1|)><rsup|4>*<around|(|t+N*\<frak-s\><around|(|B<rsub|c>|)>*\<delta\>|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>,>|<cell|e<rsup|t+N*\<frak-s\><around|(|B<rsub|c>|)>*\<delta\>>-1>|<cell|\<leqslant\>1.>>>>
  </align*>

  The exponent margins are <math|b<rsub|f>-4> for the input offer,
  <math|b<rsub|c>+1/2> for the ordinary output and <math|b<rsub|c>+1> for its
  flagged output. These are exactly the margins in the local served-transfer
  proof of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-transfer>. The finite wing
  and its singleton coefficients are transported by <math|\<cal-D\>>, whose
  soundness is the exact one-leaf transition already retained above.

  Finally, the point of the interpolating path represents the fine history
  entry at <math|<around|(|N,m+1|)>>, and its result represents the coarse
  entry at <math|<around|(|N,m|)>>. Substituting these two equalities in the
  formulas just proved changes no map or coefficient. It identifies the door
  with the history's chosen adjacent-scale door. Hence the paid menu response
  and the three displayed payment inequalities are the ones required in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-construction>. This identification
  is conditional on the selected path carrying the arrival and
  raised-response receipts, slot and representation data, and the tariff
  inequalities used above. Producing those receipts uniformly on the public
  run remains a separate obligation; the existence of a funded path alone
  does not supply them.

  <section|Centered bounds and the continuum limit>

  <subsection|Centered cell budgets imply finite-cutoff
  control><label|ex-centered-estimates>

  This section proves the quantitative step used before taking any continuum
  limit. The estimates are conditional on a centered cell bound along one
  fixed finite-volume trajectory. They do not produce that bound from the
  renormalisation-group flow; the latter is a separate and substantially
  stronger input recorded in the blueprint. The point of the argument here is
  to show in detail how a bound on one observable centered in one cell
  controls arbitrary separated tuples, and how the same bound controls
  changes of lattice positions.

  Write <math|a<rsub|N>> for the lattice spacing at mesh index <math|N>, and
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|N,p>> for the number of sites in one coordinate
  direction of the period-<math|p> torus. Thus its physical linear period is
  <math|P<rsub|p>=a<rsub|N>*\<ell\><rsub|N,p>>, independent of <math|N> when
  the schedule is embedded. A configuration of <math|q> marked points is a
  point of <math|E<rsub|q>=<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|q>>. Let
  <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|q>> be the set of configurations with no
  coincident points, and let <math|<math-up|Gen><rsub|q>> consist of
  configurations for which each coordinate projection is injective on the
  marked points. For period <math|p>, let <math|<math-up|Cyc><rsub|p>> be the
  set where every pairwise coordinate difference avoids
  <math|P<rsub|p>*\<bbb-Z\>>, and let <math|<math-up|NoHit><rsub|p>> be the
  set where each pair is nonperiodic in at least one coordinate. Thus
  <math|<math-up|Gen><rsub|q>\<subset\><math-up|Conf><rsub|q>>, while
  <math|<math-up|NoHit><rsub|p>> permits coordinatewise ties. We use the sup
  norm on each spatial coordinate when discussing separations. A tuple is
  <em|cyclically separated at scale <math|r>> if it lies in
  <math|<math-up|Cyc><rsub|p>> and, for each pair and coordinate, its
  coordinate difference has distance at least <math|r> from every multiple of
  <math|P<rsub|p>>. It is <em|separated in some coordinate at scale <math|r>>
  if for each pair at least one coordinate has this property. No condition is
  imposed on tuples with coincident marked points when we assert a
  compact-set bound only away from collisions.

  For a local observable <math|O> (that is, one of the tokens in the fixed
  observable register of the finite-volume family) and a lattice site
  <math|x>, let <math|O<rsub|N><around|(|x|)>> be its normalized
  finite-volume readout. For a finite list
  <math|\<b-up-O\>=<around|(|O<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,O<rsub|q-1>|)>> and sites
  <math|\<b-up-x\>=<around|(|x<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|q-1>|)>>, write

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|N,p,\<b-up-O\>><around|(|\<b-up-x\>|)>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|N,p><space|-0.17em><around*|[|<big|prod><rsub|i=0><rsup|q-1>O<rsub|i><rsub|N><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>|]>.
  </equation*>

  The same notation is used for the lifted lattice function on
  <math|E<rsub|q>>. The list is allowed to contain any fixed local
  observables, including reflected or conjugated observables. A centered cell
  of physical width comparable to <math|a<rsub|m>> is the centered link
  tiling at depth <math|m\<leqslant\>N>, with its first chamber centered at
  the chosen site. Call a complex observable chamber-admissible if it is
  gauge invariant, measurable, uniformly bounded on the finite-volume
  configuration space, and depends only on link variables whose two endpoints
  lie in that chamber. Admissibility does not mean that the observable is
  pointwise nonnegative; reflection positivity is a property of the
  expectation and the reflected product.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Centered budget><label|ex-centered-budget> For an observable
    <math|O>, a nonnegative number <math|A>, and indices
    <math|m\<leqslant\>N>, a centered budget
    <math|<math-ss|CB><around|(|O;A,m,N,p|)>> means: (i) the depth of
    <math|O> is represented at volume <math|N>; (ii) at every torus site
    <math|x>, the readout <math|O<rsub|N><around|(|x|)>> is
    chamber-admissible for the home chamber of the centered tiling of depth
    <math|m> based at <math|x>; and (iii) its centered root test is at most
    <math|A>. The root test is the <math|M>th root of the absolute normalized
    expectation of the product of the observable copied, with the prescribed
    reflected/conjugated orientation, to all <math|M> cells of that tiling.
    The reflected copies and the root test use the finite-volume
    reflection-positive transfer structure.
  </definition>

  For a centered tiling <math|\<cal-T\>> with <math|M> cells, write

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-R\><rsub|\<cal-T\>><around|(|F|)>\<assign\><around*|\||\<bbb-E\><rsub|N,p><around*|[|<big|prod><rsub|C\<in\><math-up|Cell><around|(|\<cal-T\>|)>>F<rsup|<around|(|C|)>>|]>|\|><rsup|1/M>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|F<rsup|<around|(|C|)>>> is the prescribed translated copy of
  <math|F>, reflected in each axis whose cell coordinate is odd and
  conjugated exactly when an odd number of axes are reflected. This notation
  is used only when the observable is admissible in the home chamber and the
  normalized expectation is defined.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Finite-cell dissemination from positive
    seams><label|prop:ex-centered-planted>Fix one finite torus, one
    normalized probability law <math|\<mu\>> on its configurations, and one
    tiling <math|\<cal-T\>>. Write its side as <math|2*h*\<ell\>>, where
    <math|h,\<ell\>\<geqslant\>1> are integers, and index its cells by
    <math|\<cal-C\>=<around|(|\<bbb-Z\>/<around|(|2*h|)>*\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|4>>,
    so <math|M=<around|(|2*h|)><rsup|4>\<gtr\>0>. All seams have the same
    kind: put <math|\<delta\>=1> for link seams and <math|\<delta\>=0> for
    site seams. For a fixed base site <math|b>, cell <math|v> starts at
    <math|b+\<ell\>*v>. Its strict chamber consists of the sites whose
    relative coordinates <math|r<rsub|i>> satisfy
    <math|0\<leqslant\>r<rsub|i>\<less\>\<ell\>> for link seams, and
    <math|0\<less\>r<rsub|i>\<less\>\<ell\>> for site seams. Observables are
    complex, bounded, measurable and gauge invariant, and depend only on
    links with both endpoints in the indicated strict chamber.

    For <math|v\<in\>\<cal-C\>>, define the affine signed map
    <math|\<phi\><rsub|v>> by

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ex-centered-copy-geometry><around|(|\<phi\><rsub|v><around|(|x|)>|)><rsub|i>=b<rsub|i>+\<ell\>*v<rsub|i>+<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x<rsub|i>-b<rsub|i>,>|<cell|v<rsub|i><text|
      even>,>>|<row|<cell|\<ell\>-\<delta\>-<around|(|x<rsub|i>-b<rsub|i>|)>,>|<cell|v<rsub|i><text|
      odd>,>>>>><space|2em><pmod|2*h*\<ell\>>.
    </equation>

    Parity is well defined modulo <math|2*h>. Let
    <math|P<rsub|\<phi\><rsub|v>>> be the pullback of configurations along
    this map, including reversal of oriented links. The copy
    <math|F<rsup|<around|(|v|)>>> is <math|F\<circ\>P<rsub|\<phi\><rsub|v>>>,
    conjugated exactly when an odd number of the coordinates <math|v<rsub|i>>
    are odd. Use these same copies in <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|\<cal-T\>>>.

    For every <math|p\<in\>\<cal-C\>> and coordinate <math|a>, assume the law
    is invariant under the configuration pullback <math|R<rsub|p,a>> of the
    site reflection

    <\equation*>
      x<rsub|a>\<longmapsto\>2*<around|(|b<rsub|a>+\<ell\>*p<rsub|a>|)>-\<delta\>-x<rsub|a>,<space|2em>x<rsub|i>\<longmapsto\>x<rsub|i>*<space|1em><around|(|i\<ne\>a|)>.
    </equation*>

    Assume also seam positivity on its positive strict half torus: for every
    bounded measurable gauge-invariant <math|H> depending only on links with
    both endpoints in that half,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ex-centered-seam-positive>\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\>><around|[|H<space|0.17em><wide|H\<circ\>R<rsub|p,a>|\<bar\>>|]>\<in\><around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>.
    </equation>

    The positive half has relative <math|a>-coordinate in
    <math|<around|[|0,h*\<ell\>|)>> for link seams, and in
    <math|<around|(|0,h*\<ell\>|)>> for site seams. Then for every family of
    home-chamber observables <math|<around|(|F<rsub|v>|)><rsub|v\<in\>\<cal-C\>>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ex-centered-seam-full><around*|\||\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\>>*<big|prod><rsub|v\<in\>\<cal-C\>>F<rsub|v><rsup|<around|(|v|)>>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|v\<in\>\<cal-C\>>\<cal-R\><rsub|\<cal-T\>><around|(|F<rsub|v>|)>.
    </equation>

    For every finite set <math|I>, injection <math|c:I\<to\>\<cal-C\>> and
    such observables <math|F<rsub|i>>, it follows that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ex-centered-seam-planted><around*|\||\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\>>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I>F<rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|c<around|(|i|)>|)>>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I>\<cal-R\><rsub|\<cal-T\>><around|(|F<rsub|i>|)>.
    </equation>

    These statements include zero root tests and the empty planted family.
    There is no power-of-two restriction on <math|2*h>.
  </proposition>

  This is the chessboard principle; compare <cite-detail|Biskup09|Ÿ5.3,
  Theorem<nbsp>5.8>. That statement uses real block functions and site
  reflections. We give the complex strict-chamber argument, including both
  seam kinds and vanishing roots, explicitly.

  <\proof>
    All products below are bounded measurable functions, hence integrable for
    the same probability law. The maps in
    <eqref|eq:ex-centered-copy-geometry> carry the strict home chamber into
    the indicated strict chamber: the reversed relative coordinate is
    <math|\<ell\>-1-r> in the link case and <math|\<ell\>-r> in the site
    case. Both preserve the stated strict ranges. Their pullbacks therefore
    preserve locality in the transported chamber; signed link permutations
    preserve measurability and transport gauge transformations. Conjugation
    preserves all these properties.

    Fix a seam <math|<around|(|p,a|)>>. On cell indices define the involution
    <math|m> by

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|m*v|)><rsub|a>=2*p<rsub|a>-1-v<rsub|a>,<space|2em><around|(|m*v|)><rsub|i>=v<rsub|i>*<around|(|i\<ne\>a|)>,
    </equation*>

    and put <math|S=<around|{|v:<around|(|v<rsub|a>-p<rsub|a>|)><bmod>2*h\<less\>h|}>>,
    using residues in <math|<around|{|0,\<ldots\>,2*h-1|}>>. If that residue
    is <math|t>, its mate has residue <math|2*h-1-t>, so <math|m>
    interchanges <math|S> and its complement. It reverses the parity of
    coordinate <math|a> and preserves the other parities. Substitution in
    <eqref|eq:ex-centered-copy-geometry>, in each of the two parity cases,
    gives <math|\<phi\><rsub|m*v>=r<rsub|p,a>\<circ\>\<phi\><rsub|v>>, where
    <math|r<rsub|p,a>> is the site reflection defining <math|R<rsub|p,a>>.
    Indeed the changed coordinate is <math|2*<around|(|b<rsub|a>+\<ell\>*p<rsub|a>|)>-\<delta\>-<around|(|\<phi\><rsub|v><around|(|x|)>|)><rsub|a>>;
    all other coordinates are unchanged. Composition of configuration
    pullbacks and the parity change now give the exact identity

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ex-centered-copy-opposite>F<rsup|<around|(|m*v|)>>=<wide|F<rsup|<around|(|v|)>>\<circ\>R<rsub|p,a>|\<bar\>>.
    </equation>

    There is exactly one extra conjugation, even when other coordinates were
    already reflected.

    Each chamber with index in <math|S> lies in the positive half torus: its
    relative coordinate there is <math|\<ell\>*t+r<rsub|a>> with
    <math|0\<leqslant\>t\<less\>h>. This is in
    <math|<around|[|0,h*\<ell\>|)>> for link seams and in
    <math|<around|(|0,h*\<ell\>|)>> for site seams. Thus finite products of
    copies from <math|S> are admissible for
    <eqref|eq:ex-centered-seam-positive>; in particular, no link crossing a
    seam is introduced.

    For positive-half observables set <math|B*<around|(|P,Q|)>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\>><around|[|P<space|0.17em><wide|Q\<circ\>R<rsub|p,a>|\<bar\>>|]>>.
    Invariance of <math|\<mu\>>, the involution <math|R<rsub|p,a><rsup|2>=1>,
    and conjugation of integrals show <math|B*<around|(|P,Q|)>=<wide|B*<around|(|Q,P|)>|\<bar\>>>.
    The form is linear in its first variable and conjugate-linear in its
    second, and its diagonal is nonnegative by assumption. Here is the
    Cauchy\USchwarz argument including a zero diagonal. Write
    <math|u=B*<around|(|P,P|)>>, <math|v=B*<around|(|Q,Q|)>> and
    <math|z=B*<around|(|P,Q|)>>. Positivity of <math|P-t*Q> gives

    <\equation*>
      0\<leqslant\>u-<wide|t|\<bar\>>*z-t*<wide|z|\<bar\>>+<around|\||t|\|><rsup|2>*v*<space|2em><around|(|t\<in\>\<bbb-C\>|)>.
    </equation*>

    If <math|v\<gtr\>0>, take <math|t=z/v> to get
    <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>u*v>. If <math|v=0> and
    <math|z\<ne\>0>, taking <math|t=R*z> with a sufficiently large positive
    real <math|R> makes the right side negative. Thus <math|z=0>, and the
    same bound holds also in this case.

    For two assignments <math|A,D> of home observables to cells, define

    <\equation*>
      <around|[|A,D|]><around|(|v|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<around|(|v|)>,>|<cell|v\<in\>S,>>|<row|<cell|D*<around|(|m*v|)>,>|<cell|v\<nin\>S,>>>>><space|1em>P<rsub|A>=<big|prod><rsub|v\<in\>S>A<around|(|v|)><rsup|<around|(|v|)>>,<space|1em>V<around|(|A|)>=<around*|\||\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\>>*<big|prod><rsub|v>A<around|(|v|)><rsup|<around|(|v|)>>|\|>.
    </equation*>

    Reindexing the complementary half by <math|m> and using
    <eqref|eq:ex-centered-copy-opposite> proves the pointwise identity

    <\equation*>
      <big|prod><rsub|v><around|[|A,D|]><around|(|v|)><rsup|<around|(|v|)>>=P<rsub|A><space|0.17em><wide|P<rsub|D>\<circ\>R<rsub|p,a>|\<bar\>>.
    </equation*>

    Consequently Cauchy\USchwarz proves, rather than assumes, the fold bound

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ex-centered-fold>V<around|(|<around|[|A,D|]>|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>V<around|(|<around|[|A,A|]>|)>*V<around|(|<around|[|D,D|]>|)>.
    </equation>

    The operation <math|A\<mapsto\><around|[|A,A|]>> retains the positive
    half and reflects its labels into the negative half, with the copy
    orientations fixed.

    Choose any nonempty finite palette of home observables. There are only
    finitely many assignments from this palette to <math|\<cal-C\>>, so
    <math|V> has a maximum <math|K\<geqslant\>0>. Every fold of a maximizing
    assignment still maximizes <math|V>. To prove this, for a maximizing
    <math|A> put <math|D=A\<circ\>m>. Then <math|<around|[|A,D|]>=A>, since
    <math|m<rsup|2>=1>. Both folds in <eqref|eq:ex-centered-fold> have value
    at most <math|K>, and

    <\equation*>
      K<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>V<around|(|<around|[|A,A|]>|)>*V<around|(|<around|[|D,D|]>|)>\<leqslant\>K<rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    If <math|K=0> every assignment maximizes. If <math|K\<gtr\>0>, either
    factor being strictly less than <math|K> would make their product less
    than <math|K<rsup|2>>. Thus in both cases each fold is a maximizer, for
    every translated seam.

    We next show in finitely many folds that some maximizing assignment is
    constant. The following argument applies to any property preserved by all
    these folds. Suppose an assignment is already independent of the
    coordinates in a set <math|E>, and choose an axis <math|a\<nin\>E>. A
    strip of <math|k> equal slices starting at <math|p<rsub|a>> means that
    whenever <math|x,y> have the same coordinates off <math|a> and their
    <math|a>-residues relative to <math|p<rsub|a>> lie in
    <math|<around|{|0,\<ldots\>,k-1|}>>, their labels agree. A strip of
    length one always has this property.

    For <math|1\<leqslant\>k\<less\>h>, fold at the seam starting at
    <math|p<rsub|a>>. In the new strip starting at <math|p<rsub|a>-1> of
    length <math|k+1>, the first slice is reflected to <math|p<rsub|a>>,
    whereas the remaining slices <math|p<rsub|a>,\<ldots\>,p<rsub|a>+k-1> are
    kept. All these slices therefore acquire the same labels for each fixed
    choice of the other coordinates. More explicitly, a new-strip residue
    <math|t=0> is sent by the fold to old residue zero; a residue
    <math|1\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>k> is kept and has old residue
    <math|t-1\<less\>k\<leqslant\>h>. This proves the extension and checks
    that the kept slices really are in the positive half. Repeating
    <math|h-1> times yields a strip of <math|h> equal slices. Fold once more
    at its starting seam. Every cell is then either in that half or mapped
    into it by <math|m>, and the map changes no other coordinate. The
    resulting assignment is independent of <math|a> on the entire torus.

    No earlier independence is lost: for <math|b\<in\>E>, two cells differing
    only in coordinate <math|b> lie on the same side of an <math|a>-seam. If
    kept, their old labels agree; if reflected, their images still differ
    only in <math|b>, so their old labels also agree. Thus all folds just
    used preserve independence of every coordinate in <math|E>. This also
    covers <math|h=1>, when only the final half-to-whole fold is needed.
    Performing this construction for the four coordinates makes the
    assignment independent of all of them. Changing coordinates one at a time
    then shows it is constant. Since all folds preserve maximality, the
    original maximum <math|K> is attained by a constant assignment from the
    same palette. This finite argument uses no divisibility beyond the even
    cell period.

    Finally fix <math|<around|(|F<rsub|v>|)>> and
    <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, and abbreviate
    <math|\<rho\><around|(|F|)>=\<cal-R\><rsub|\<cal-T\>><around|(|F|)>> and
    <math|r<rsub|v>=\<rho\><around|(|F<rsub|v>|)>+\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>.
    Apply the maximum conclusion to the finite palette
    <math|X<rsub|v>=F<rsub|v>/r<rsub|v>>. Positive real scaling commutes with
    every copy, including its conjugation. A constant assignment
    <math|X<rsub|v>> has value

    <\equation*>
      V<around|(|<text|constant >X<rsub|v>|)>=<frac|\<rho\><around|(|F<rsub|v>|)><rsup|M>|r<rsub|v><rsup|M>>\<leqslant\>1.
    </equation*>

    Hence every assignment from this palette has value at most one, in
    particular the assignment putting <math|X<rsub|v>> at <math|v>. Pulling
    its positive real constants out of the product and integral gives

    <\equation*>
      V<around|(|<around|(|F<rsub|v>|)>|)>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|v><around|(|\<rho\><around|(|F<rsub|v>|)>+\<varepsilon\>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The right side is a finite product of continuous functions of
    <math|\<varepsilon\>>. Letting <math|\<varepsilon\>*\<downarrow\>*0>
    proves <eqref|eq:ex-centered-seam-full>, without dividing by a possibly
    zero root. This scaling argument uses only the definition of the root
    test, not its triangle inequality.

    The constant function <math|1> is admissible, every copy of it is
    <math|1>, and normalization gives <math|\<rho\><around|(|1|)>=<around|\||\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\>>*1|\|><rsup|1/M>=1>.
    For an injective <math|c>, fill cell <math|c<around|(|i|)>> with
    <math|F<rsub|i>> and every unoccupied cell with <math|1>. The full-cell
    inequality is exactly <eqref|eq:ex-centered-seam-planted>; injectivity
    makes the assignment unambiguous. For <math|I=\<varnothing\>> both sides
    are <math|1>.
  </proof>

  In particular, Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-centered-planted>
  supplies the full-cell premise of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-centered-root-seminorm>
  below for this same law, tiling and copy convention. Its seam positivity is
  an upstream assumption, not a consequence of the root-seminorm argument.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Seminorm from the planted inequality><label|lem:ex-centered-root-seminorm>Fix
    one normalized finite-volume Wilson law, one centered tiling
    <math|\<cal-T\>>, and its home chamber, with
    <math|M=<around|\||<math-up|Cell><around|(|\<cal-T\>|)>|\|>\<geqslant\>1>.
    Let <math|\<cal-A\>> be the complex chamber-admissible class defined
    above, with dependence only on links whose two endpoints lie in the same
    strict home chamber. Keep the prescribed copy maps and their conjugation
    choices fixed. Assume the full-cell case of the planted inequality used
    in Theorem<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-producers>: for every family
    <math|<around|(|H<rsub|C>|)><rsub|C\<in\><math-up|Cell><around|(|\<cal-T\>|)>>>
    in <math|\<cal-A\>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ex-centered-root-planted><around*|\||\<bbb-E\><rsub|N,p>*<big|prod><rsub|C\<in\><math-up|Cell><around|(|\<cal-T\>|)>>H<rsub|C><rsup|<around|(|C|)>>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|C\<in\><math-up|Cell><around|(|\<cal-T\>|)>>\<cal-R\><rsub|\<cal-T\>><around|(|H<rsub|C>|)>.
    </equation>

    Then <math|\<cal-A\>> is a complex vector space closed under pointwise
    conjugation, and <math|\<rho\>=\<cal-R\><rsub|\<cal-T\>>> is a
    finite-valued complex seminorm on it, invariant under conjugation.
    Explicitly, for <math|F,G\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and <math|z\<in\>\<bbb-C\>>,

    <\equation*>
      \<rho\><around|(|0|)>=0,<space|2em>\<rho\><around|(|F|)>\<geqslant\>0,<space|2em>\<rho\>*<around|(|z*F|)>=<around|\||z|\|>*\<rho\><around|(|F|)>,<space|2em>\<rho\>*<around|(|F+G|)>\<leqslant\>\<rho\><around|(|F|)>+\<rho\><around|(|G|)>,<space|2em>\<rho\><around|(|<wide|F|\<bar\>>|)>=\<rho\><around|(|F|)>.
    </equation*>

    Consequently, for every finite index set <math|I>, complex coefficients
    <math|z<rsub|i>> and <math|F<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ex-centered-root-finite-sum><big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>z<rsub|i>*F<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\>,<space|2em>\<rho\><space|-0.17em><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>z<rsub|i>*F<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I><around|\||z<rsub|i>|\|>*\<rho\><around|(|F<rsub|i>|)>.
    </equation>

    In particular, if <math|\<rho\><around|(|F<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|i>>
    with <math|A<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>, the right side may be replaced by
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\||z<rsub|i>|\|>*A<rsub|i>>, including
    zero budgets and the empty sum. No positive-definiteness assertion is
    made for <math|\<rho\>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The zero function is admissible. Gauge invariance and dependence only on
    the specified links are preserved by addition, scalar multiplication and
    conjugation: configurations agreeing on those links give equal values
    before and after any of these operations. Measurability is preserved by
    the same operations on complex-valued functions. If
    <math|<around|\||F|\|>\<leqslant\>B> and
    <math|<around|\||G|\|>\<leqslant\>D> with finite nonnegative bounds, then
    <math|<around|\||F+G|\|>\<leqslant\>B+D>,
    <math|<around|\||z*F|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||z|\|>*B> and
    <math|<around|\||<wide|F|\<bar\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>B>. This proves all the
    stated closure properties without enlarging the chamber or allowing links
    across its boundary.

    For each cell <math|C>, write <math|P<rsub|C>> for its fixed pullback on
    configurations and <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|C>\<in\><around|{|0,1|}>> for
    its conjugation choice. Thus <math|F<rsup|<around|(|C|)>>=F\<circ\>P<rsub|C>>
    if <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|C>=0>, and <math|F<rsup|<around|(|C|)>>=<wide|F\<circ\>P<rsub|C>|\<bar\>>>
    if <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|C>=1>. These geometric pullbacks are
    measurable; copied functions and all their finite products are therefore
    bounded and measurable. They are integrable for the fixed probability
    law. All expectations below are thus ordinary finite integrals. The
    definition gives finiteness and nonnegativity of <math|\<rho\>>. Since
    <math|M\<geqslant\>1>, the product of the copies of zero is zero, so
    <math|\<rho\><around|(|0|)>=0>.

    Put <math|w<rsub|C>=z> when <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|C>=0> and
    <math|w<rsub|C>=<wide|z|\<bar\>>> otherwise. Before taking any absolute
    value, one has <math|<around|(|z*F|)><rsup|<around|(|C|)>>=w<rsub|C>*F<rsup|<around|(|C|)>>>.
    Linearity of the expectation and <math|<around|\||<big|prod><rsub|C>w<rsub|C>|\|>=<around|\||z|\|><rsup|M>>
    give

    <\equation*>
      \<rho\>*<around|(|z*F|)><rsup|M>=<around*|\||<around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|C>w<rsub|C>|)>*\<bbb-E\><rsub|N,p>*<big|prod><rsub|C>F<rsup|<around|(|C|)>>|\|>=<around|\||z|\|><rsup|M>*\<rho\><around|(|F|)><rsup|M>.
    </equation*>

    Taking the unique nonnegative <math|M>th root proves absolute
    homogeneity, also when <math|z=0>. No equality between the numbers of
    conjugated and unconjugated cells is needed.

    Each copy is additive, including when it conjugates. For every subset
    <math|S\<subseteq\><math-up|Cell><around|(|\<cal-T\>|)>> set
    <math|H<rsub|S,C>=F> if <math|C\<in\>S> and <math|H<rsub|S,C>=G>
    otherwise. Finite distributivity gives the pointwise identity

    <\equation*>
      <big|prod><rsub|C><around|(|F+G|)><rsup|<around|(|C|)>>=<big|sum><rsub|S\<subseteq\><math-up|Cell><around|(|\<cal-T\>|)>><big|prod><rsub|C>H<rsub|S,C><rsup|<around|(|C|)>>.
    </equation*>

    Integrating this finite sum, applying the complex triangle inequality to
    its expectations, and then applying <eqref|eq:ex-centered-root-planted>
    separately to each assignment yields

    <\equation*>
      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<rho\>*<around|(|F+G|)><rsup|M>>|<cell|\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|S\<subseteq\><math-up|Cell><around|(|\<cal-T\>|)>><around*|\||\<bbb-E\><rsub|N,p>*<big|prod><rsub|C>H<rsub|S,C><rsup|<around|(|C|)>>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|S\<subseteq\><math-up|Cell><around|(|\<cal-T\>|)>><big|prod><rsub|C\<in\>S>\<rho\><around|(|F|)>*<big|prod><rsub|C\<nin\>S>\<rho\><around|(|G|)>=<big|prod><rsub|C><around|(|\<rho\><around|(|F|)>+\<rho\><around|(|G|)>|)>=<around|(|\<rho\><around|(|F|)>+\<rho\><around|(|G|)>|)><rsup|M>.>>>>
      </aligned>
    </equation*>

    The last equality is again finite distributivity. All factors are
    nonnegative, so the increasing nonnegative <math|M>th-root function
    proves the triangle inequality. This argument divides by neither root
    budget; it includes <math|\<rho\><around|(|F|)>=0> or
    <math|\<rho\><around|(|G|)>=0> and every positive finite <math|M>.

    Both choices of <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|C>> give
    <math|<around|(|<wide|F|\<bar\>>|)><rsup|<around|(|C|)>>=<wide|F<rsup|<around|(|C|)>>|\<bar\>>>.
    Conjugation commutes with finite products and with integration against
    the real probability measure. Consequently

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-E\><rsub|N,p>*<big|prod><rsub|C><around|(|<wide|F|\<bar\>>|)><rsup|<around|(|C|)>>=<wide|\<bbb-E\><rsub|N,p>*<big|prod><rsub|C>F<rsup|<around|(|C|)>>|\<bar\>>.
    </equation*>

    Absolute values and nonnegative roots give conjugation invariance.
    Finally induction on <math|<around|\||I|\|>>, using closure, the triangle
    inequality and absolute homogeneity, proves
    <eqref|eq:ex-centered-root-finite-sum>. For <math|I=\<varnothing\>> both
    sides are zero; replacing each nonnegative root budget by its upper bound
    proves the final assertion.
  </proof>

  For example, if <math|U,U<rprime|'>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and
  <math|<wide|U|\<bar\>>=U>, the real projection
  <math|V=<around|(|U<rprime|'>+<wide|U<rprime|'>|\<bar\>>|)>/2> belongs to
  <math|\<cal-A\>> and satisfies

  <\equation*>
    \<rho\>*<around|(|V-U|)>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>\<rho\>*<around|(|U<rprime|'>-U|)>+<tfrac|1|2>\<rho\><around|(|<wide|U<rprime|'>-U|\<bar\>>|)>=\<rho\>*<around|(|U<rprime|'>-U|)>.
  </equation*>

  This uses one law and one chamber for both errors. The lemma proves only a
  consequence of the planted inequality. Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-centered-planted>
  supplies that inequality from its explicit seam hypotheses on the same
  tiling; neither result constructs a centered budget along a selected
  trajectory.

  The quantitative assumption has two distinct orders of quantifiers. For
  every fixed observable <math|O> and period index <math|p>, there is a tail
  of depths <math|m> such that for each such <math|m> one can choose
  <math|A=A*<around|(|O,p,m|)>\<geqslant\>0> and a mesh tail on which
  <math|<math-ss|CB><around|(|O;A,m,N,p|)>> holds. In addition, for each
  <math|O> there is a tail of depths <math|m> such that one can choose
  <math|A=A*<around|(|O,m|)>\<geqslant\>0> for which there is an outer
  period-index tail and, for each period in it, an inner mesh-index tail on
  which the budget holds. The constants may depend on the displayed fixed
  data. This is the precise centered-budget hypothesis used in the next
  result.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Finite families share one budget><label|ex-centered-finite>
    Suppose each observable in a finite list has the fixed-period
    centered-budget property (respectively, the expanding-period property).
    Then, after increasing the depth tail, there is one nonnegative budget
    for the whole list and one common eventuality on which all the individual
    centered budgets hold. The same conclusion applies to any finite family
    of reflected or conjugated observables that belong to the register and
    have the corresponding centered-budget property.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For the empty list take budget zero. Otherwise, for each member
    <math|O<rsub|i>>, choose its depth threshold <math|m<rsub|i>>, and let
    <math|m<rsub|\<ast\>>=max<rsub|i> m<rsub|i>>. Fix any
    <math|m\<geqslant\>m<rsub|\<ast\>>>. At this particular depth, the
    hypotheses give a possibly depth-dependent budget
    <math|A<rsub|i><around|(|m|)>> and an eventuality for <math|O<rsub|i>>.
    Take <math|A<around|(|m|)>=max<rsub|i> A<rsub|i><around|(|m|)>> and
    intersect the finitely many mesh tails (or the finitely many ordered
    <math|<around|(|N,p|)>> tails). The result is a common eventuality and a
    single budget for the entire list at this same depth <math|m>. Since this
    works for every <math|m\<geqslant\>m<rsub|\<ast\>>>, it proves the
    asserted eventual-in-depth statement. No monotonicity of the centered
    budgets as depth changes is used or claimed. There are finitely many
    orientations of each fixed observable: take all compositions of the four
    coordinate reflections, with or without conjugation. Whenever these are
    registered observables with the required property, include the entire
    finite family in the same list. This argument does not claim a bound
    uniform over all observables or over an uncountable test class.
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Centered budgets give local moduli and compact
    bounds><label|ex-centered-producers> Assume the finite-volume Wilson
    family has the following properties, as well as the centered-budget
    property above. Its fixed schedule satisfies <math|a<rsub|N>\<to\>0>,
    <math|P<rsub|p>\<to\>\<infty\>> along the period schedule, and the nested
    lattice scales and centered tilings have the stated integer alignment;
    the finite-volume side is divisible into the <math|4*s> equal chambers
    used by the centered transfer word. The observable register is closed
    under the coordinate reflections and conjugation used below. Each
    registered observable has a fixed finite stencil, its readout is
    continuous and covariant under translations and coordinate reflections,
    and conjugation is implemented by the reflected token. Every coordinate
    cut has a positive seam and the associated continuous transfer
    representation: a compact metric slice space <math|X>, a finite Borel
    measure <math|\<mu\>>, a continuous positive-semidefinite heat kernel
    <math|T>, and continuous kernels <math|B<rsub|i>> for local slab
    observables. If a cyclic word consists of bricks <math|B<rsub|i>> of
    common width <math|w> and nonnegative integer free gaps <math|H<rsub|i>>,
    with <math|q*w+<big|sum><rsub|i>H<rsub|i>=L>, the unnormalized
    finite-volume expectation is the cyclic kernel integral

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|Cl><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|K<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,K<rsub|r>|)>\<assign\><big|int><rsub|X<rsup|r>><big|prod><rsub|j=1><rsup|r>K<rsub|j>*<around|(|x<rsub|j>,x<rsub|j+1>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsup|r>*<around|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|r>|)>,<space|2em>x<rsub|r+1>=x<rsub|1>,
    </equation*>

    for the collapsed cyclic list of <math|q> brick kernels and the
    individual unit heat steps, which has
    <math|r=q+<big|sum><rsub|i>H<rsub|i>> kernel factors. The physical tour
    length is <math|L=q*w+<big|sum><rsub|i>H<rsub|i>>. Division by the
    <math|L>-step partition trace <math|Z=<math-up|Cl><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|T,\<ldots\>,T|)>\<gtr\>0>
    gives the normalized Wilson expectation. The finite-volume representation
    identifies a change of one marked position by <math|l> steps with
    replacing two free gaps <math|H<rsub|i>,H<rsub|j>> by
    <math|H<rsub|i>-l,H<rsub|j>+l>. Finally, assume each cut tiling and this
    same normalized law satisfy the geometric, reflection-invariance and
    seam-positivity hypotheses of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-centered-planted>,
    with its copy convention. That proposition then gives, for every such
    tiling <math|\<cal-T\>>, finite index set <math|I>, injection
    <math|c:I\<to\><math-up|Cell><around|(|\<cal-T\>|)>>, and
    home-chamber-admissible functions <math|F<rsub|i>>, the planted
    inequality

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||\<bbb-E\><rsub|N,p><space|-0.17em><around*|[|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I>F<rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|c<around|(|i|)>|)>>|]>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I>\<cal-R\><rsub|\<cal-T\>><around|(|F<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation*>

    holds. These are the precise locality, transfer, symmetry, reality, seam,
    and cell-packing properties used below. Then the following four
    conclusions hold.

    <\enumerate>
      <item>For each fixed period <math|p>, each compact set
      <math|K\<subset\><math-up|Cyc><rsub|p>>, each finite list
      <math|\<b-up-O\>>, and every <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, there exist
      <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0> and a mesh threshold <math|N<rsub|0>> such
      that, for <math|N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>>, any two lattice tuples
      <math|\<b-up-x\>,\<b-up-y\>> whose physical embeddings lie in <math|K>
      and satisfy <math|<around|\||\<b-up-x\>-\<b-up-y\>|\|>\<less\>\<delta\>>
      obey

      <\equation*>
        <around|\||F<rsub|N,p,\<b-up-O\>><around|(|\<b-up-x\>|)>-F<rsub|N,p,\<b-up-O\>><around|(|\<b-up-y\>|)>|\|>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>.
      </equation*>

      <item>For each compact <math|K\<subset\><math-up|Gen><rsub|q>>, each
      finite list <math|\<b-up-O\>>, and every <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>,
      there are <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0> and an outer period-index threshold
      such that for every period index in that tail the same mesh modulus
      holds eventually in the inner mesh index. The mesh threshold may depend
      on the period, while <math|\<delta\>> does not.

      <item>For each compact <math|K\<subset\><math-up|Conf><rsub|q>> and
      each finite list <math|\<b-up-O\>>, there are <math|D\<less\>\<infty\>>
      and an outer period-index threshold such that for every period index in
      that tail the same finite-volume functions have absolute value at most
      <math|D> eventually in the inner mesh index on lattice tuples embedded
      in <math|K>.

      <item>For each fixed period <math|p>, each compact
      <math|K\<subset\><math-up|NoHit><rsub|p>>, and each finite list
      <math|\<b-up-O\>>, there is <math|D<rsub|p>\<less\>\<infty\>> such that
      <math|<around|\||F<rsub|N,p,\<b-up-O\>><around|(|\<b-up-x\>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>D<rsub|p>>
      for all lattice tuples embedded in <math|K>, eventually in the mesh
      limit. This hypothesis permits coordinatewise ties and applies even
      when the points are not separated in every coordinate.
    </enumerate>

    The first modulus may depend on the fixed period; the second modulus is
    uniform in the outer period tail. The third bound has one constant in
    that tail, with a mesh threshold that may depend on the period. The
    fourth bound is only for a fixed period. No assertion extends a bound
    through point collisions.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    If the compact set <math|K> in any assertion is empty, that assertion is
    immediate. We therefore take <math|K> nonempty when using compact minima
    below. For the modulus assertions, <math|q=0> gives a constant empty
    product and <math|q=1> gives a position-independent correlation by
    translation covariance. We now derive the directional estimate for
    <math|q\<geqslant\>2>. Fix a compact set <math|K> in the fixed-period
    cyclic patch. Since that patch is open, there is a compact neighborhood
    <math|K<rprime|'>> of <math|K> still contained in it. Compactness and
    continuity of the finitely many periodic coordinate-distance functions
    give a number <math|\<rho\>\<gtr\>0> such that every tuple in
    <math|K<rprime|'>> has, for every pair of marks and every axis, distance
    at least <math|4*\<rho\>> from the period lattice. Choose a
    centered-budget depth <math|m> with <math|a<rsub|m>\<less\>\<rho\>>, and
    choose one common budget <math|A> for the finite list consisting of the
    requested observables and their axis-reflected/conjugated versions. For
    sufficiently fine mesh <math|N>, the stencil fit conditions hold and the
    mesh is small enough for the following integer choices.

    Move one marked site by <math|l\<geqslant\>0> lattice steps in an axis
    <math|\<alpha\>>, where <math|l*a<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>\<rho\>>. For a
    negative displacement, reverse the endpoints and apply the same estimate
    along the reversed segment; the enlarged compact neighborhood keeps both
    paths admissible. Let <math|R=\<rho\>/a<rsub|N>> be the corresponding
    radius in lattice units. The coordinate gaps in the cyclic order exceed
    the common brick width plus <math|2*R>: the compact separation gives at
    least <math|4*\<rho\>/a<rsub|N>> lattice steps, whereas the brick has
    width at most <math|2*a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|N>\<less\>2*R>. Subtracting that
    width leaves free gaps greater than <math|2*R>. The move changes two free
    gaps from <math|<around|(|H<rsub|0>,H<rsub|q-1>|)>> to
    <math|<around|(|H<rsub|0>-l,H<rsub|q-1>+l|)>>; all other gaps remain
    fixed, and total tour length is unchanged.

    The centered root bound supplies the column estimate needed for this
    tour. Cut a marked observable at the transfer plane through its centered
    cell. Locality identifies it with a continuous one-dimensional slab
    observable, represented by a brick of width
    <math|w=2*a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|N>> in lattice units. The reflected cut gives
    a second slab function, obtained from the reflected token; both are
    admissible in the relevant home chamber and have centered root test at
    most <math|A>. The centered tiling has <math|4*s> cells along that axis.
    Put the <math|2*s> alternating original and reflected slab functions in
    the distinct even-indexed cells; their spacing is two cells, and the
    planted inequality applies to this injection. The planted inequality
    bounds the absolute expectation of their product by <math|A<rsup|2*s>>.
    Translation and reflection identify that product with the <math|s>-fold
    reflected column word of the brick. Thus the normalized column moment to
    power <math|\<theta\>=1/<around|(|2*s|)>> is at most <math|A>.

    We now justify the transfer estimate for the continuous slice kernels;
    they are not finite matrices. Fix a compact slice space <math|X>, finite
    measure <math|\<mu\>>, continuous kernels
    <math|T,B<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,B<rsub|q-1>>, and the positive integer word
    length <math|L=2*s*w>. Choose finite Borel partitions
    <math|<around|{|C<rsub|u><rsup|<around|(|j|)>>|}><rsub|u>> of <math|X>
    whose maximum cell diameter tends to zero, and choose a tag
    <math|\<xi\><rsub|u><rsup|<around|(|j|)>>\<in\>C<rsub|u><rsup|<around|(|j|)>>>
    in each nonempty cell. Form the sampled matrix

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|K<rsub|j>|)><rsub|u*v>=\<mu\><around|(|C<rsub|u><rsup|<around|(|j|)>>|)><rsup|1/2>*K*<around|(|\<xi\><rsub|u><rsup|<around|(|j|)>>,\<xi\><rsub|v><rsup|<around|(|j|)>>|)>*\<mu\><around|(|C<rsub|v><rsup|<around|(|j|)>>|)><rsup|1/2>.
    </equation*>

    If <math|T> is a positive-semidefinite kernel, <math|T<rsub|j>> is a
    positive-semidefinite matrix: it is the Gram matrix of the tagged kernel,
    conjugated by the diagonal matrix of square-root cell masses. For any
    fixed cyclic list <math|K<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,K<rsub|r>>, multiplication
    and taking the matrix trace gives the exact finite sum

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|Tr><around|(|<around|(|K<rsub|1>|)><rsub|j>*\<cdots\><around|(|K<rsub|r>|)><rsub|j>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|u<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,u<rsub|r>><big|prod><rsub|t=1><rsup|r>\<mu\><around|(|C<rsub|u<rsub|t>><rsup|<around|(|j|)>>|)>*K<rsub|t>*<around|(|\<xi\><rsub|u<rsub|t>><rsup|<around|(|j|)>>,\<xi\><rsub|u<rsub|t+1>><rsup|<around|(|j|)>>|)>,<space|2em>u<rsub|r+1>=u<rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    The integrand <math|<big|prod><rsub|t>K<rsub|t>*<around|(|x<rsub|t>,x<rsub|t+1>|)>>
    is continuous on compact <math|X<rsup|r>>. Since the cell diameters tend
    to zero, this tagged sum converges to its integral, the cyclic kernel
    integral <math|<math-up|Cl><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|K<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,K<rsub|r>|)>>.
    The same quadrature sequence works for the partition word, each shifted
    tour word, and each fixed replicated column word
    <math|<around|(|<around|(|B<rsub|i>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*B<rsub|i>|)><rsup|s>>,
    since these are finitely many continuous cyclic integrands. In
    particular, if <math|Z=<math-up|Cl><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|T,\<ldots\>,T|)>\<gtr\>0>
    uses <math|L> copies, then <math|Z<rsub|j>=<math-up|Tr><around|(|T<rsub|j><rsup|L>|)>\<to\>Z>
    and <math|Z<rsub|j>\<gtr\>0> eventually. Define the adjoint kernel by
    <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|x,y|)>=<wide|B<rsub|i>*<around|(|y,x|)>|\<bar\>>>.
    The column quantities <math|f<rsub|i,j>> converge to

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|i>=<around*|\||<math-up|Cl><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|B<rsub|i><rsup|\<ast\>>,B<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,B<rsub|i><rsup|\<ast\>>,B<rsub|i>|)>|\|><rsup|1/<around|(|2*s|)>>,
    </equation*>

    and the centered root bound just proved says
    <math|f<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>A*Z<rsup|1/<around|(|2*s|)>>>.

    For completeness, the finite-matrix estimate applied to these sampled
    matrices is an immediate Schatten\UHölder bound. For a finite matrix
    <math|A>, write <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A|\<\|\|\>><rsub|r>=<around|(|<math-up|Tr><around|\||A|\|><rsup|r>|)><rsup|1/r>>
    for <math|1\<leqslant\>r\<less\>\<infty\>> and use the operator norm for
    <math|r=\<infty\>>. The two-factor Schatten Hölder inequality is
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A*B|\<\|\|\>><rsub|r>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|A|\<\|\|\>><rsub|u><around|\<\|\|\>|B|\<\|\|\>><rsub|v>>
    when <math|1/r=1/u+1/v\<leqslant\>1>. It follows from Tropp's generalized
    unitarily invariant norm Hölder inequality
    <cite-detail|Tropp|Theorem<nbsp>6.32, p.<nbsp>52>: take the unitarily
    invariant norm to be the Schatten <math|r> norm and its conjugate powers
    to be <math|u/r> and <math|v/r>. The endpoint cases follow by continuity
    of finite-dimensional Schatten norms as an exponent tends to infinity;
    when both tend to infinity this is the operator norm inequality.
    Iterating this two-factor statement with partial sums of reciprocal
    exponents, and finishing with <math|<around|\||<math-up|Tr><space|0.17em>A|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|A|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
    gives <math|<around|\||<math-up|Tr><around|(|A<rsub|1>*\<cdots\>*A<rsub|k>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|j><around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|r<rsub|j>>>
    whenever <math|<big|sum><rsub|j>r<rsub|j><rsup|-1>=1>. Put
    <math|Z<rsub|j>=<math-up|Tr><around|(|T<rsub|j><rsup|L>|)>> and
    <math|\<theta\>=1/<around|(|2*s|)>>. For each insertion
    <math|B<rsub|i,j>> use exponent <math|2*s>, so its Schatten norm is

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|i,j>=<around*|(|<math-up|Tr><around*|(|<around|(|B<rsub|i,j><rsup|\<ast\>>*B<rsub|i,j>|)><rsup|s>|)>|)><rsup|1/<around|(|2*s|)>>.
    </equation*>

    For a heat factor <math|T<rsub|j><rsup|h<rsub|i>>> use exponent
    <math|L/<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>h<rsub|i>>; if
    <math|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>h<rsub|i>=0>, use <math|\<infty\>>. The
    spectral theorem gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsub|j><rsup|h<rsub|i>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L/<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>h<rsub|i>>=Z<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>h<rsub|i>/L>>,
    and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsub|j><rsup|h<rsub|i>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>1>
    on the imaginary boundary after the zero-eigenvalue convention. The
    reciprocal exponents sum to one because
    <math|q/<around|(|2*s|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i><math-up|Re><space|0.17em>h<rsub|i>/L=1>.
    Hence

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<math-up|Tr><big|prod><rsub|i>B<rsub|i,j>*T<rsub|j><rsup|h<rsub|i>>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i>f<rsub|i,j>|)>*Z<rsub|j><rsup|1-q*\<theta\>>.
    </equation*>

    Now take <math|h<rsub|0>=H<rsub|0>-z>, <math|h<rsub|q-1>=H<rsub|q-1>+z>,
    and <math|h<rsub|i>=H<rsub|i>> otherwise. Their sum is constant; each
    real part is nonnegative for <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>2*R>
    because every free gap exceeds <math|2*R>. The matrix powers are entire
    in <math|z> by spectral calculus, with the zero eigenspace assigned the
    constant-zero value. Thus this trace is an entire function
    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|j><around|(|z|)>> satisfying

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<Phi\><rsub|j><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i>f<rsub|i,j>|)>*Z<rsub|j><rsup|1-q*\<theta\>>.
    </equation*>

    For any real <math|t\<in\><around|[|0,l|]>>, the radius-<math|R> circle
    around <math|t> is inside <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>2*R>, so
    Cauchy's estimate gives <math|<around|\||\<Phi\><rsub|j><rprime|'><around|(|t|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|<big|prod><rsub|i>f<rsub|i,j>|)>*Z<rsub|j><rsup|1-q*\<theta\>>/R>.
    Integrate from <math|0> to <math|l>. Taking <math|j\<to\>\<infty\>> in
    the resulting endpoint inequality is justified by the cyclic quadrature
    convergence above. It gives the continuous-kernel estimate

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||F<rsub|N,p,\<b-up-O\>>*<around|(|\<b-up-x\>+l*a<rsub|N>*e<rsub|k,\<alpha\>>|)>-F<rsub|N,p,\<b-up-O\>><around|(|\<b-up-x\>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|l|R>*<space|0.17em><frac|<around|(|<big|prod><rsub|i>f<rsub|i>|)>*Z<rsup|1-q*\<theta\>>|Z>\<leqslant\><frac|A<rsup|q>|\<rho\>>*<space|0.17em>l*a<rsub|N>.
    </equation*>

    This is the required one-coordinate bound. It derives the continuous
    transfer estimate from finite sampled matrices and explicitly passes the
    traces, denominator, and all column norms to the limit; no
    finite-dimensional representation of the Wilson measure is assumed.

    To compare arbitrary lattice tuples whose embeddings lie in <math|K>,
    change their <math|4*q> scalar coordinates one at a time. Intermediate
    tuples can lie outside <math|K>; by first choosing <math|K<rprime|'>> as
    above and then taking <math|\<delta\>> smaller than its neighborhood
    margin, every intermediate tuple remains in <math|K<rprime|'>>. The same
    <math|A,\<rho\>> work at all stages. The sum of the <math|4*q> coordinate
    displacements is at most <math|4*q> times the sup distance, so

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||F<rsub|N,p,\<b-up-O\>><around|(|\<b-up-x\>|)>-F<rsub|N,p,\<b-up-O\>><around|(|\<b-up-y\>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>4*q*<space|0.17em><frac|A<rsup|q>|\<rho\>>*<space|0.17em><around|\||\<b-up-x\>-\<b-up-y\>|\|>.
    </equation*>

    Choosing <math|\<delta\>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>*\<rho\>/<around|(|4*q*A<rsup|q>|)>>,
    with any sufficiently small neighborhood margin if <math|A=0>, proves the
    fixed-period modulus.

    For the expanding-period modulus, let <math|K> be compact in the generic
    set, where each coordinate projection separates distinct points. Choose a
    compact neighborhood still inside that open set. Since its coordinate
    differences are bounded and the physical period tends to infinity, for
    every sufficiently large period all pairs are uniformly separated from
    every periodic image; the same positive <math|\<rho\>> works for all
    those periods. Choose the centered-budget depth <math|m> with
    <math|a<rsub|m>\<less\>\<rho\>>. The expanding clause gives a common
    budget <math|A> eventually in period, then eventually in mesh for each
    such period. The preceding coordinate telescope uses the same
    <math|\<rho\>,A,\<delta\>> for all these periods, with the inner mesh
    threshold allowed to depend on the period. This is exactly the expanding
    modulus. Genericity, rather than absence of full point collisions alone,
    is needed here because all four coordinate gaps are used by the
    directional telescope.

    We next prove both compact-bound assertions. The cases <math|q=0,1> are
    immediate: the empty correlation is <math|1>, while for one observable
    the planted inequality with one occupied chamber bounds its expectation
    by its centered root test, at most <math|A>; translation covariance makes
    this uniform in its position. Hence assume <math|q\<geqslant\>2>. Let
    <math|K> be compact with no full collisions. Define for each pair
    <math|i\<ne\>j>

    <\equation*>
      d<rsub|i*j><around|(|x|)>=max<rsub|\<alpha\>=1,\<ldots\>,4><around|\||x<rsub|i><rsup|\<alpha\>>-x<rsub|j><rsup|\<alpha\>>|\|>.
    </equation*>

    Its minimum over the finite set of pairs and compact <math|K> is a
    positive number. For sufficiently large periods, every coordinate
    difference has absolute value less than half the physical period; hence
    its distance to the period lattice is its absolute value. It follows that
    there is <math|\<rho\><rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> such that every pair in every
    tuple of <math|K> is separated in at least one torus coordinate by
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|0>>, uniformly on a period tail. The same conclusion
    for a fixed period holds under the weaker NoHit hypothesis: use instead
    the continuous positive function

    <\equation*>
      d<rsub|i*j,p><around|(|x|)>=max<rsub|\<alpha\>=1,\<ldots\>,4><math-up|dist><around|(|x<rsub|i><rsup|\<alpha\>>-x<rsub|j><rsup|\<alpha\>>,P<rsub|p>*\<bbb-Z\>|)>.
    </equation*>

    NoHit says precisely that <math|d<rsub|i*j,p><around|(|x|)>\<gtr\>0> for
    every pair; compactness and finiteness of the pair set give
    <math|min<rsub|x\<in\>K,i\<ne\>j> d<rsub|i*j,p><around|(|x|)>\<gtr\>0>.
    This maximum over coordinates is essential: taking the minimum over
    coordinates would incorrectly rule out coordinatewise ties.

    Here is the packing argument, with the two scales distinguished. Choose a
    coarse depth <math|m> with <math|a<rsub|m>\<less\>\<rho\><rsub|0>>. Then
    choose a finer depth <math|k\<geqslant\>m> in the relevant
    centered-budget tail so that the budget at depth <math|k> is available
    and

    <\equation*>
      6*q*a<rsub|k>\<less\>a<rsub|m>.
    </equation*>

    For expanding bounds use the expanding centered-budget clause; for a
    fixed-period bound use the fixed-<math|p> clause. After fixing <math|k>,
    take the mesh index large enough that every fixed stencil fits inside a
    depth-<math|k> chamber. The finite torus placement lemma supplies a
    translate of the coarse tiling <math|X> of depth <math|m> such that all
    <math|q> marked sites lie in its margin of three fine-cell spans. The
    proof is a boundary count in each coordinate: one point forbids at most
    <math|6*a<rsub|k>> of the coarse translation interval, so all <math|q>
    forbid at most <math|6*q*a<rsub|k>\<less\>a<rsub|m>>; hence an allowed
    translate exists. Let <math|D<rsub|i>> be the coarse cell of <math|X>
    containing <math|x<rsub|i>>. These cells are distinct, since two sites in
    the same cell differ by less than <math|a<rsub|m>\<less\>\<rho\><rsub|0>>
    in every coordinate, contradicting the some-coordinate separation.

    The centered budgets are at the fine depth <math|k>, whereas the tuple
    will be placed in a coarse tiling <math|X> of depth <math|m>. First
    choose the home site <math|o> of <math|X> on the fine center grid and in
    chamber zero of <math|X>. Match the entire coarse translated-copy orbit
    of the observable at <math|o>, one copy for each cell of <math|X>,
    injectively to cells of the fine centered tiling at <math|o>. The aligned
    scale divisibility supplies the matching; the required reflected and
    conjugated orientations are in the finite family for which the common
    budget was chosen. The fine planted inequality bounds the absolute
    expectation of the whole coarse copy product by
    <math|A<rsup|<around|\||<math-up|Cell><around|(|X|)>|\|>>>. By the
    definition of the root test, the root test of the observable at <math|o>
    relative to the coarse tiling <math|X> is at most <math|A>. This is the
    full replicated-orbit argument; bounding just the original tuple's
    <math|q> entries would not prove a coarse root estimate.

    For each marked point <math|x<rsub|i>>, let <math|D<rsub|i>> be its
    assigned coarse cell. The tiling's pointed-site map chooses
    <math|y<rsub|i>> in the home chamber (the chamber based at cell zero),
    and reflection of the margin shows that <math|y<rsub|i>> has a margin of
    three fine-cell spans from the home-chamber boundary. The coarse cell
    orientation determines a reflected or conjugated token at
    <math|y<rsub|i>>; signed-coordinate covariance identifies its copy in
    <math|D<rsub|i>> with the original observable at <math|x<rsub|i>>. The
    finite list of all such oriented tokens was included in the common
    budget. We next move the coarse home point from <math|o> to any such
    <math|y<rsub|i>>. In each of four coordinates, bracket <math|y<rsub|i>>
    by two fine-center-grid points separated by at most one fine span
    <math|d>. The sixteen corner points lie in the margin of two spans. The
    fixed-stencil fit gives chamber interiority at every intermediate point
    on these four coordinate segments. For a fixed axis, let <math|u<rsub|j>>
    be the root test at successive integer translates of the full coarse
    copied product, and let <math|M<rsub|X>> be the number of cells.
    Translate the entire copied product by one lattice step. In each chamber
    the translated copy becomes one of the two neighboring translates, with
    the sign determined by that chamber's orientation. Reflection positivity
    applied to this translated product bounds its moment by the planted
    product of the root tests of those neighboring translates. Pairing cells
    by the involution that reverses the orientation along this axis gives
    exactly <math|M<rsub|X>/2> copies of each neighbor. Thus

    <\equation*>
      u<rsub|j><rsup|M<rsub|X>>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|j-1><rsup|M<rsub|X>/2>*u<rsub|j+1><rsup|M<rsub|X>/2>,
    </equation*>

    and raising both sides to the power <math|2/M<rsub|X>> gives
    <math|u<rsub|j><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|j-1>*u<rsub|j+1>>. If an
    interior value exceeds both endpoint bounds, choose a maximum
    <math|u<rsub|j>> on the finite interval; it is positive. Both neighbors
    are at most <math|u<rsub|j>>, so the inequality forces both to equal
    <math|u<rsub|j>>. Repeating this at the adjacent interior maxima
    propagates that value to an endpoint, contradicting the endpoint bound.
    This proves the finite-interval maximum principle, including possible
    zeros elsewhere. Repeatedly applying this maximum principle along the
    four segments bounds the root at <math|y<rsub|i>> by the maximum of the
    sixteen corner roots, all at most <math|A>. This proves the coarse root
    estimate at each needed marked home point, with chamber interiority
    there; covariance transfers that bound to the coarse insertion associated
    with <math|x<rsub|i>>.

    Now apply the planted inequality in the coarse tiling <math|X> to the
    injection <math|i\<mapsto\>D<rsub|i>>. It bounds the tuple expectation by
    the product of the <math|q> coarse root tests, hence by <math|A<rsup|q>>.
    All choices were uniform over tuples in <math|K>, so this is the
    expanding compact bound; for fixed <math|p> it gives the fixed-period
    compact NoHit bound. The former uses compactness away from full
    collisions and the period tail; the latter uses only NoHit at that fixed
    period and permits coordinate ties. Neither gives control at a
    torus-coincidence configuration, nor smoothness through a collision
    diagonal.
  </proof>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|What remains upstream><label|ex-centered-open> The theorem assumes
    the centered budget. The exact upstream estimate needed is: for each
    grade <math|d>, norm bound <math|R\<gtr\>0>, and exponent slack
    <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>, there is <math|A\<geqslant\>0> such that, for
    every depth <math|m> beyond the reference depth for grade <math|d>,
    eventually in volume index <math|N>, uniformly for every period index
    <math|p> and every label <math|v> of grade <math|d> with
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>R>, the centered root test
    is at most <math|A*a<rsub|m><rsup|-d-\<eta\>>>, with representation and
    chamber-interiority. The same volume tail works for all <math|p>;
    restricting either to a fixed period or to the cofinal period schedule
    gives the two budget regimes used above. No proof of this Wilson estimate
    is contained here. In particular, the finite-volume cell-packing and
    transfer arguments above cannot supply its decay in <math|a<rsub|m>>, nor
    can they supply the required uniformity in labels of bounded norm. This
    is the next analytic input to establish from the renormalisation-group
    trajectory.
  </remark>

  <subsection|The centered estimate on the selected Wilson
  history><label|ex-centered-construction>

  Fix the family selected for a compact simple gauge group and positive
  penalty by the construction theorem. The compactness argument in
  Section<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-producers> needs a uniform root bound
  for its centered local observables. We derive it from the paid
  single-source history selected with that family. The essential
  finite-volume input is a marked-copy estimate: placing one copy in each
  cell turns the RG bound for a finite census into a bound on the normalized
  root moment. The scale schedule and the factorial-column estimate then make
  its cost at most the desired power of the mesh.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Uniform centered power bound from the single-source
    receipts><label|ex-centered-main-bound> Assume a compact simple gauge
    group and positive penalty, and a single selected history with the served
    height, branch, and occupancy fields and one common budget for all labels
    in each bounded grade ball. Assume the occupancy bounds satisfy
    <math|1\<leqslant\>r<rsub|i>>; the selected schedule, its width receipt
    and fixed finite local stencil at each grade; the factorial-columns
    condition; the treasury payment <math|t<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>\<leqslant\>q<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>>;
    and the subpower column input of Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|ex-column-subpower>.
    The schedule and quantitative inequalities used below are part of these
    hypotheses. For each grade <math|d>, each <math|R\<gtr\>0>, and each
    <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, there is a finite <math|A\<geqslant\>0>
    such that for every <math|m\<geqslant\>m<rsub|<math-up|ref>><around|(|d|)>>,
    for all sufficiently large volumes <math|N>, uniformly in every period
    <math|p>, every label <math|v> of grade <math|d> with
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>R>, and every site
    <math|x>, the centered cell at depth <math|m> and site <math|x> contains
    the observable in its positive chamber and

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-R\><rsub|m,N,p><around|(|O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v,x|)>|)>\<leqslant\>A*a<rsub|m><rsup|-d-\<varepsilon\>>.
    </equation*>

    The following quantitative contracts are assumed uniformly on that one
    history. For each fixed <math|d,R>, let <math|g<rsub|d,R>> be the
    coefficient-ball bound and write <math|c<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1> for the
    columns, <math|\<nu\><rsub|i>> for the factorial order, and
    <math|F<rsub|i>=<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>i>log c<rsub|j+1>>. Assume
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|i>!\<leqslant\>c<rsub|i>>,
    <math|F<rsub|i>=o<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|i>|)>>, and
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+c<rsub|G>*\<sigma\><rsub|i>>
    with <math|c<rsub|G>\<gtr\>0>. The width is

    <\equation*>
      W<rsub|i>=<frac|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i><rsup|-n<rsub|d>/2>|g<rsub|d,R>*c<rsub|i>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<less\>i>c<rsub|j+1>>,<space|1em>P*<around|(|d,R,i|)>=a<rsub|i><rsup|d>*W<rsub|i>,<space|1em>n<rsub|d>=2*<around|(|deg
      F<rsub|d>+4|)>,
    </equation*>

    and its calibrated coefficients obey <math|q<rsub|i><around|(|v|)>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|d,R>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<less\>i>c<rsub|j+1>>
    throughout the ball. The selected opening
    <math|k<around|(|m|)>\<geqslant\>m> tends to infinity and, for each
    <math|z\<gtr\>0>, eventually obeys

    <\equation*>
      w=<frac|a<rsub|m>|a<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>>\<geqslant\>1,<space|1em>q<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>\<geqslant\>1,<space|1em>w<rsup|4>*q<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>\<leqslant\>z*\<sigma\><rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>,<space|1em>\<sigma\><rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>-\<sigma\><rsub|m>=log
      w,<space|1em>t<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>\<leqslant\>q<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>.
    </equation*>

    For every finite centered census and sufficiently large terminal volume,
    uniformly in period, site and labels in the ball, assume the same-history
    served representation, positive weights, vault, occupancy, migration and
    separation inputs of the finite-census estimate below. The local
    representative is lawful with nonnegative coupling and evaluation, the
    represented polynomial is integrable, and the selected threshold admits
    <math|\<rho\>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|-1/2>\<leqslant\>1>; at this
    radius the treasury bounds the extensive local bill. The width payment
    and the fixed stencil are uniform in the same variables.

    The volume threshold may depend on <math|m>, but not on <math|p>,
    <math|v>, or <math|x>.
  </theorem>

  The argument below proves this conditional estimate through the actual
  single-source supplier used by the public construction. The upstream
  production of the uniform fields is tracked separately and is not folded
  into this theorem's hypotheses.

  <\proof>
    <subsubsection|The finite-volume marked-copy estimate>

    Fix a grade <math|d>, a label-norm radius <math|R\<gtr\>0>, a target
    depth <math|m>, and an opened depth <math|k\<geqslant\>m>. The
    finite-dimensional grade-<math|d> label space has compact closed
    <math|R>-ball, and its coefficient-size function is continuous. Thus

    <\equation*>
      g<rsub|d,R>=max <space|-0.17em><around*|(|1,sup<rsub|<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>R><math-up|coeffSize><rsub|d><around|(|v|)>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>.
    </equation*>

    Use this coefficient-size radius in the local payment and width
    estimates. Write <math|a<rsub|i>> for the lattice spacing,
    <math|\<sigma\><rsub|i>=log <around|(|a<rsub|i><rsup|-1>|)>>, and

    <\equation*>
      P*<around|(|d,R,k|)>=a<rsub|k><rsup|d>*<space|0.17em>W*<around|(|d,g<rsub|d,R>,k|)>,<space|2em>E*<around|(|d,R;m,k|)>=<around*|(|<frac|a<rsub|m>|a<rsub|k>>|)><rsup|4>*t<rsub|k>+6-log
      P*<around|(|d,R,k|)>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|W*<around|(|d,R,k|)>> is the width allocated to the
    grade-<math|d> coefficient ball at depth <math|k>, and <math|t<rsub|k>>
    is the vacuum treasury per site at that depth. All these quantities are
    positive where they occur in logarithms. Let <math|M<rsub|m>> be the
    number of cells in the centered tiling of depth <math|m>. For an
    observable copy <math|U<rsub|j>> of the same grade-<math|d> label, write
    <math|c<rsub|k><around|(|U<rsub|j>|)>> for its column-weighted local
    production cost: the scale-<math|k> column count times the paid
    coefficient size of that label in the selected history. The paid-ticket
    inequality is <math|P*<around|(|d,R,k|)>*c<rsub|k><around|(|U<rsub|j>|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|fly><rsub|k>>.
    In particular this cost includes the column factor required by the local
    response estimate, even though the weaker unweighted size suffices for
    the separate propagation induction.

    The selected coordinate and width bounds give this payment explicitly.
    Write <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1> for the column count, put
    <math|D<rsub|k>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<less\>k>\<Gamma\><rsub|i+1>>, and let
    <math|q<rsub|k><around|(|v|)>> be the coefficient-size norm of the
    calibrated entry at level <math|k> for a label <math|v> in the ball. At
    the grade reference level the calibrated coordinate is <math|v>; at each
    subsequent step its size grows by at most the next column count. Since
    all column counts are at least one, induction gives

    <\equation*>
      q<rsub|k><around|(|v|)>\<leqslant\>D<rsub|k>*g<rsub|d,R>.
    </equation*>

    Let <math|F<rsub|d>> be the grade-<math|d> frame, let
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>\<gtr\>0> be the hatted coupling, and put
    <math|h<rsub|k>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|-1/2>> and
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|d>=deg F<rsub|d>+4>. The selected width is

    <\equation*>
      W*<around|(|d,g<rsub|d,R>,k|)>=<frac|h<rsub|k><rsup|2*\<mu\><rsub|d>>|g<rsub|d,R>*\<Gamma\><rsub|k>*D<rsub|k>>,<space|2em><math-up|fly><rsub|k>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|-\<mu\><rsub|d>>=h<rsub|k><rsup|2*\<mu\><rsub|d>>.
    </equation*>

    The grade dilation has norm <math|a<rsub|k><rsup|-d>>, so the
    column-weighted cost is <math|c<rsub|k><around|(|U<rsub|j>|)>=\<Gamma\><rsub|k>*a<rsub|k><rsup|-d>*q<rsub|k><around|(|v|)>>.
    All denominators are positive, and therefore

    <\equation*>
      P*<around|(|d,R,k|)>*c<rsub|k><around|(|U<rsub|j>|)>=W*<around|(|d,g<rsub|d,R>,k|)>*\<Gamma\><rsub|k>*q<rsub|k><around|(|v|)>\<leqslant\>h<rsub|k><rsup|2*\<mu\><rsub|d>>=<math-up|fly><rsub|k>.
    </equation*>

    This uses the calibrated adjacent coordinate bound and the displayed
    width choice on the same finite-volume history. It does not by itself
    establish the later subpower estimate for the width.

    We now give the finite-census estimate used in the proof. Let <math|V> be
    the cells of the centered depth-<math|m> tiling, so
    <math|<around|\||V|\|>=M<rsub|m>>. For each <math|j\<in\>V>, let
    <math|U<rsub|j>> be the transported observable placed at that cell. Write
    <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|V>> for the finite squarefree marker algebra: its
    basis elements are the modes <math|I> in a downward-closed family
    <math|\<cal-C\>> of subsets of <math|V> containing the empty set and
    every singleton; multiplication is disjoint union when the union is an
    allowed mode, and zero otherwise, and

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|price><rsub|r><around|(|F|)>=<big|sum><rsub|I\<in\>\<cal-C\>><around|\||F<rsub|I>|\|>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>I>r<rsub|j>,<space|2em><math-up|toll><rsub|r><around|(|I|)>=<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>I>r<rsub|j>
    </equation*>

    for positive weights <math|r<rsub|j>>. The mode-zero coefficient is the
    quiet coefficient. The polynomial pass is the finite exponential series
    through degree <math|<around|\||V|\|>>; it satisfies
    <math|<math-up|price><rsub|r><around|(|<math-up|pass><space|0.17em>F|)>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around|(|<math-up|price><rsub|r><around|(|F|)>|)>>, by
    submultiplicativity and the ordinary exponential-series bound.

    The single-source receipt at each intermediate scale represents the
    propagated density in the form

    <\equation*>
      X<around|(|U|)>=e<rsup|E<around|(|U|)>><math-up|pass><space|-0.17em><around*|(|<math-up|Mach><around|(|F<around|(|U|)>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|S>H<rsub|S><around|(|U|)>|)><math-up|field><space|-0.17em><around*|(|<wide|K|\<bar\>><around|(|U|)>+<math-up|Mach><around|(|G<around|(|U|)>|)>+K<around|(|U|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|<math-up|Mach><around|(|z|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>V><math-up|send><rsub|j><around|(|z<rsub|j>|)>>
    places a scalar source in the marker for copy <math|j>; each local unit
    <math|H<rsub|S>> and forced-box source <math|K<rsub|S>> has marker degree
    at least two whenever it is nonzero. The field is the sum over admissible
    families of pairwise disjoint boxes whose FLAG sets have union equal to
    the global flag set, with each term the product of its box factors. The
    vacuum terms are <math|<wide|K|\<bar\>>> and the vacuum part of the box
    factors. This is the representation supplied by the source's served-slab
    receipt.

    For positive <math|r<rsub|j>>, the support budgets give four
    contributions to the price. The scalar observables contribute at most
    <math|<around|\||V|\|>*x> by the first part of the vault bound. The
    box-marked observables contribute at most <math|<around|\||V|\|>*y> by
    its second part. The local unit family and the forced-box family each
    contribute at most <math|<around|\||V|\|>*f> by their towed support
    budgets. Here is the origin-counting step in full. For an active unit
    <math|H<rsub|S><around|(|U|)>\<neq\>0>, choose a nonzero marker
    coefficient. The degree condition gives a marker <math|j> in that
    coefficient; the support-placement condition for that unit says its site
    <math|o<rsub|j>> lies in <math|S>. Thus every active support contains a
    named origin. For a forced-box term the same argument gives
    <math|o<rsub|j>> in its box. If <math|a<rsub|S>\<geqslant\>0> is the
    envelope supplied by the towed bound, its weighted coefficient price
    <math|q<rsub|S><around|(|U|)>> satisfies
    <math|q<rsub|S><around|(|U|)>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|S>> and
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|S:o<rsub|j>\<in\>S>a<rsub|S>*exp
    <around|(|e<space|0.17em><math-up|cover><around|(|S|)>|)>\<leqslant\>f>
    at every origin. Only active supports need be charged; a zero polynomial
    has price zero even if its envelope is positive. Because
    <math|e\<geqslant\>0>, the exponential is at least one. Summing the
    envelope bounds over origins therefore gives
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|S<space|0.17em><math-up|active>>q<rsub|S><around|(|U|)>\<leqslant\><around|\||V|\|>*f>.
    For the unmarked vacuum activities, each active box has a nonempty
    support and its nonnegative envelope has rooted sum at most
    <math|\<kappa\>> at each site. Assign one site in the support to each
    active box and sum the rooted bounds over all <math|s> lattice sites. The
    total vacuum price is therefore at most <math|s*\<kappa\>>. Thus the
    total price inside the finite pass is at most
    <math|<around|\||V|\|>*<around|(|x+f|)>>, and the total price of the box
    factors is at most <math|s*\<kappa\>+<around|\||V|\|>*<around|(|y+f|)>>.

    For the field, sum first over admissible box families. Dropping the
    disjointness restriction only adds nonnegative terms, so its price is at
    most the product over boxes of one plus the price of that box factor. The
    inequality <math|1+t\<leqslant\>e<rsup|t>> bounds this by the exponential
    of the sum of box prices. The same <math|1+t\<leqslant\>e<rsup|t>>
    argument for the finite polynomial pass, together with
    submultiplicativity of price, now yields the pointwise estimate

    <\equation>
      <label|ex-centered-steer><math-up|price><rsub|r><around|(|X<around|(|U|)>|)>\<leqslant\>exp
      <space|-0.17em><around*|(|E<around|(|U|)>+<around|\||V|\|>*<around|(|x+y+2*f|)>+s*\<kappa\>|)>.
    </equation>

    The lawfulness estimate bounds the scalar action by
    <math|<math-up|scalar>+s<space|0.17em><math-up|exCost>>, and the
    pointwise vacuum price contributes <math|s*\<kappa\>>. Assume the
    polynomial-valued function <math|X> is integrable against the prior; its
    mode coefficients are then integrable. Because the prior is a probability
    measure and <math|<around|\||X<rsub|I><around|(|U|)>|\|><math-up|toll><rsub|r><around|(|I|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|price><rsub|r><around|(|X<around|(|U|)>|)>>,
    integrating <eqref|ex-centered-steer> gives

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<big|int>X<rsub|I>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\>|\|><math-up|toll><rsub|r><around|(|I|)>\<leqslant\>exp
      <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<math-up|scalar>+s*<around|(|<math-up|exCost>+\<kappa\>|)>+<around|\||V|\|>*<around|(|x+y+2*f|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Assume the action parameters satisfy <math|0\<less\>\<rho\>\<leqslant\>\<rho\><rsub|0>\<leqslant\>1>,
    where <math|\<rho\><rsub|0>> is the flag-vanishing threshold, and
    <math|\<beta\>\<geqslant\>0>, the Wilson action is lawful, and its
    representation is pointwise nonnegative. The product prior is the
    normalized product Haar probability <math|\<mu\>> on edge variables. The
    integral <math|Z<rsub|A>=<big|int><math-up|eval><around|(|A|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\>>
    is positive and has the explicit lower bound

    <\equation*>
      Z<rsub|A>\<geqslant\>exp <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<math-up|scalar>-s*<around*|[|<math-up|exCost>+\<beta\><space|0.17em><math-up|baseBudget><around|(|\<rho\>|)>+4*d<rsub|G>*log
      <around|(|16/\<rho\>|)>|]>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|d<rsub|G>=dim<rsub|\<bbb-R\>> G.<math-up|Mat>> is the
    dimension of the ambient real matrix space used by the gauge
    representation. For completeness, the Haar small-ball estimate used here
    follows from a packing argument. Put <math|D=d<rsub|G>>, take
    <math|0\<less\>r\<leqslant\>1>, and use the Frobenius norm. Every group
    matrix is unitary and has the same positive norm <math|\<gamma\>>, the
    color scale. The chord is normalized by this scale:
    <math|<math-up|chord><around|(|g|)>=<around|\<\|\|\>|g-I|\<\|\|\>><rsub|F>/\<gamma\>>.
    Choose a maximal finite family with pairwise distances strictly greater
    than <math|r*\<gamma\>>. Its open balls of radius <math|r*\<gamma\>/2>
    are disjoint and lie in the ambient ball of radius <math|2*\<gamma\>>.
    Comparing Euclidean volumes gives <math|M*<around|(|r/4|)><rsup|D>\<leqslant\>1>.
    Maximality says the closed balls of radius <math|r*\<gamma\>> cover the
    group. Unitary left multiplication preserves the ambient matrix norm, so
    each such ball is a left translate of the identity ball; Haar invariance
    gives them equal measure. Thus <math|1\<leqslant\>M*<space|0.17em>\<mu\>*<around|(|B*<around|(|e,r*\<gamma\>|)>|)>>
    and hence <math|\<mu\>*<around|(|B*<around|(|e,r*\<gamma\>|)>|)>\<geqslant\><around|(|r/4|)><rsup|D>>.
    Taking <math|r=\<rho\>/4>, each edge ball has probability at least
    <math|<around|(|\<rho\>/16|)><rsup|d<rsub|G>>>. Restrict each of the
    <math|4*s> forward edge variables to this ball. A unitary edge and its
    inverse have the same Frobenius distance from the identity, so the same
    bound holds for reverse traversals. A four-edge plaquette path differs
    from the identity by at most the sum of its four edge deviations, hence
    its chord is at most <math|\<rho\>> and the flag term vanishes. The loop
    action is bounded by the stated loop cost, and nonnegativity of
    <math|\<beta\>> bounds the remaining local action by <math|\<beta\>>
    times the base budget. Independence under the normalized product prior
    gives event probability at least <math|<around|(|\<rho\>/16|)><rsup|4*s*d<rsub|G>>>.
    On that event the exponential density is at least the scalar exponential
    times the exponential of minus the displayed action and vacuum costs.
    Integrating this nonnegative density over the event proves the bound.
    Dividing the numerator estimate by this denominator gives the integrated
    mode estimate

    <\equation>
      <label|ex-centered-sail><frac|<around*|\||<big|int>X<rsub|I>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\>|\|>|Z<rsub|A>><math-up|toll><rsub|r><around|(|I|)>\<leqslant\>exp
      <space|-0.17em><around*|(|s*<around*|[|2<space|0.17em><math-up|exCost>+\<kappa\>+\<beta\><space|0.17em><math-up|baseBudget>+4*d<rsub|G>*log
      <around|(|16/\<rho\>|)>|]>+<around|\||V|\|>*<around|(|x+y+2*f|)>|)>,
    </equation>

    where <math|Z<rsub|A>\<gtr\>0> is the integral of the local
    representative, <math|\<rho\>> is the small-ball radius below the action
    threshold, and <math|d<rsub|G>> is the real dimension of the ambient
    gauge-matrix space. The base-budget term is evaluated at this same radius
    and the fixed lawful path parameter. For a selected history with
    <math|k\<leqslant\>N>, the transition-mass identity identifies
    <math|Z<rsub|A>> with the terminal Wilson partition function
    <math|Z<rsub|N,p>>. This is a finite-volume estimate; no infinite-volume
    measure is involved.

    Now propagate the terminal birth polynomial <math|b<rsub|N>> backwards
    through the finite-volume transitions:
    <math|b<rsub|i>=T<rsub|i>*b<rsub|i+1>> for <math|i=N-1,\<ldots\>,k>.
    Start from the actual terminal birth, which is integrable because it is a
    finite polynomial in continuous observables on compact configuration
    space. At each step the transition density is jointly measurable,
    nonnegative and uniformly bounded in both configurations. Thus its
    product with a prior-integrable function is integrable against the
    product of the two priors, and Fubini's theorem makes the pushed function
    integrable. The kernel mass identity then preserves its integral against
    the corresponding normalized prior. The mode extraction commutes with
    this transition because it is a continuous linear coefficient map on the
    finite-dimensional polynomial space: applying it before or after
    integration gives the same scalar integral. Induction therefore preserves
    integrability, each coefficient identity, and the same partition
    normalization at every scale. The transition equation and terminal birth
    identity consequently give

    <\equation*>
      <frac|<big|int><around|(|b<rsub|k>|)><rsub|I>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|k>|Z<rsub|N,p>>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|N,p>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>I>U<rsub|j>.
    </equation*>

    The intermediate height, locality, and terminal Fourier statements are
    the exact hypotheses used to identify the propagated coefficient with the
    served-slab census; the conversion from these fields to the displayed
    squarefree price is set out below. The weighted local-payment inequality
    is <math|r<rsub|j>*c<rsub|k><around|(|U<rsub|j>|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|fly><rsub|k>>.
    Separation controls the cover cost of each multi-marker support, while
    the occupancy bound controls the number of named origins per site. These
    are the geometric and payment hypotheses needed for the four
    support-price bounds above.

    At the opened depth, the weight march returns its initial value, so
    <math|r<rsub|j>=P*<around|(|d,R,k|)>> for all <math|j>. The local scalar
    bounds are <math|x=3<space|0.17em><math-up|fly><rsub|k>> and
    <math|y=<math-up|fly><rsub|k>>; the action estimate has <math|f=1> and
    <math|<math-up|fly><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>1>. Thus
    <math|x+y+2*f=4<space|0.17em><math-up|fly><rsub|k>+2\<leqslant\>6>. Now
    specialize the census to the full power set of <math|V> and choose
    <math|\<rho\>=<wide|h|^><rsub|k>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|-1/2>>.
    The selected delayed-coupling bound and vacuum threshold give
    <math|0\<less\><wide|h|^><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>min
    <around|{|1,\<theta\><rsub|k>|}>>, as in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-public-service>. At precisely this
    radius, the treasury receipt on the same selected history bounds the
    extensive bracket in <eqref|ex-centered-sail> by
    <math|M<rsub|m>*<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4>*t<rsub|k>>.
    Taking <math|I=V> therefore gives

    <\equation>
      <label|ex-centered-marked-copy><around*|\||\<bbb-E\><rsub|N,p>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>V>U<rsub|j>|\|>*P*<around|(|d,R,k|)><rsup|M<rsub|m>>\<leqslant\>exp
      <space|-0.17em><around*|(|M<rsub|m>*<around*|[|<around*|(|<frac|a<rsub|m>|a<rsub|k>>|)><rsup|4>*t<rsub|k>+6|]>|)>.
    </equation>

    This is the marked-copy inequality with all terms traced: the
    <math|6*M<rsub|m>> is the sum of the <math|3<space|0.17em><math-up|fly><rsub|k>+<math-up|fly><rsub|k>+2>
    scalar, forced, and action charges; the treasury is the cost of all
    <math|M<rsub|m>*<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4>> scale-<math|k>
    cells.

    By definition, the centered root test is <math|X<rsup|1/M<rsub|m>>>,
    where <math|X=<around|\||\<bbb-E\><rsub|N,p>*<big|prod><rsub|j>U<rsub|j>|\|>>.
    If <math|X=0> the desired estimate is immediate. Otherwise divide
    <eqref|ex-centered-marked-copy> by <math|P<rsup|M<rsub|m>>\<gtr\>0> to
    get

    <\equation*>
      X\<leqslant\><around*|(|<frac|exp <around|(|<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4>*t<rsub|k>+6|)>|P*<around|(|d,R,k|)>>|)><rsup|M<rsub|m>>.
    </equation*>

    The map <math|x\<mapsto\>x<rsup|1/M<rsub|m>>> is increasing on
    <math|<around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>> and <math|M<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>1>, so
    taking its value on the two sides yields

    <\equation>
      <label|ex-centered-opened-root>\<cal-R\><rsub|m,N,p><around|(|O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v,x|)>|)>\<leqslant\><frac|exp
      <around|(|<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4>*t<rsub|k>+6|)>|P*<around|(|d,R,k|)>>=exp
      <around|(|E*<around|(|d,R;m,k|)>|)>,
    </equation>

    for <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>R>, once the centered
    tiling has interiority at the chosen site. Translation covariance
    transfers the estimate from the origin to any site; the finitely many
    signed-coordinate copies have the same coefficient and payment bounds. In
    the source proof, the marked-copy inequality is obtained by applying its
    finite-census RG estimate to the full census, then applying the root-test
    inequality. No continuum or limiting operation is used in this step.

    <subsubsection|The width bill and the choice of opened depth>

    Set <math|n<rsub|d>=2<space|0.17em><math-up|winter><around|(|d|)>> and,
    at scale <math|i>, write <math|c<rsub|i>> for the column count,
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|i>> for the factorial order,
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>> for the hatted coupling, and

    <\equation*>
      W<rsub|i>=<frac|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i><rsup|-n<rsub|d>/2>|g<rsub|d,R>*<space|0.17em>c<rsub|i>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<less\>i>c<rsub|j+1>>,<space|2em>L<rsub|i>=<frac|W<rsub|i>|\<nu\><rsub|i>!>.
    </equation*>

    These are the actual width, envelope, and paid last-column quantities in
    the factorial-column estimate. The accumulated column-log estimate
    <math|F<rsub|i>=o<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|i>|)>> is proved by the
    scale-increment telescoping in Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|ex-column-subpower>.
    The hatted coupling has the exact schedule form
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+c<rsub|G>*\<sigma\><rsub|i>>
    with <math|c<rsub|G>\<gtr\>0>. Thus for all sufficiently large <math|i>
    there is a fixed <math|C\<gtr\>0> such that
    <math|1\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>C*\<sigma\><rsub|i>>,
    and

    <\equation*>
      0\<leqslant\><frac|log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>|\<sigma\><rsub|i>>\<leqslant\><frac|log
      <around|(|C*\<sigma\><rsub|i>|)>|\<sigma\><rsub|i>>\<longrightarrow\>0.
    </equation*>

    This proves the logarithmic coupling subpower directly from the affine
    reference schedule; the actual finite-volume coefficient's running limit
    is not used in this width step. The paid factorial condition
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|i>!\<leqslant\>c<rsub|i>> and the width identity give,
    for every <math|z\<gtr\>0>, eventually

    <\equation*>
      5-log L<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><frac|z|2>*\<sigma\><rsub|i>.
    </equation*>

    The exact logarithmic expression is

    <\equation*>
      5-log L<rsub|i>=5+log g<rsub|d,R>+<frac|n<rsub|d>|2>*log
      <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>+log c<rsub|i>+<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>i>log
      c<rsub|j+1>+log <around|(|\<nu\><rsub|i>!|)>.
    </equation*>

    For <math|i\<geqslant\>1>, the sum <math|F<rsub|i>=<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>i>log
    c<rsub|j+1>> contains <math|log c<rsub|i>>; since
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|i>!\<leqslant\>c<rsub|i>>, it also dominates <math|log
    <around|(|\<nu\><rsub|i>!|)>>. Thus the last three terms are bounded by
    <math|3*F<rsub|i>>. The fixed prefactor and coupling-log terms are
    <math|o<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|i>|)>> by the estimates just given. This
    is exactly the I04 inequality with slack <math|z/2>, and makes the
    finite-prefix handling explicit. Since
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|i>!\<geqslant\>1>, one has
    <math|L<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>W<rsub|i>>, and therefore <math|5-log
    W<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>z*\<sigma\><rsub|i>/2>. For all sufficiently large
    <math|i>, also <math|1\<leqslant\>z*\<sigma\><rsub|i>/2>. Hence

    <\equation*>
      6-log W<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>z*\<sigma\><rsub|i>.
    </equation*>

    The physical palette width is <math|P*<around|(|d,R,i|)>=a<rsub|i><rsup|d>*W<rsub|i>>,
    so <math|-d*log a<rsub|i>=d*\<sigma\><rsub|i>> yields the exact bill

    <\equation>
      <label|ex-centered-width-bill>6-log
      P*<around|(|d,R,i|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|d+z|)>*\<sigma\><rsub|i>.
    </equation>

    This also explains the constants: the last-column estimate is on
    <math|5-log L<rsub|i>>; the extra <math|1> is absorbed into
    <math|z*\<sigma\><rsub|i>/2>, and the physical grade factor contributes
    exactly <math|d*\<sigma\><rsub|i>>. No additional arbitrary width slack
    is hidden in <eqref|ex-centered-width-bill>.

    Let <math|k<around|(|m|)>> be the depth opened by the selected schedule
    for target depth <math|m>. The schedule gives
    <math|k<around|(|m|)>\<geqslant\>m> and
    <math|k<around|(|m|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>. Given <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>,
    choose

    <\equation*>
      0\<less\>z\<leqslant\>min <around*|{|<frac|\<eta\>|4>,<frac|\<eta\>|4*<around|(|d+\<eta\>|)>>|}>.
    </equation*>

    The schedule's pay bound and the vacuum-treasury estimate give,
    eventually in <math|m>,

    <\equation*>
      w<rsup|4>*q<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>\<leqslant\>z*\<sigma\><rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>,<space|2em>t<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>\<leqslant\>q<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>,<space|2em>w=<frac|a<rsub|m>|a<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>>\<geqslant\>1,
    </equation*>

    where <math|q<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>1> is the paid fiat at depth <math|k>.
    Also <math|\<sigma\><rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>-\<sigma\><rsub|m>=log
    w\<leqslant\>w<rsup|4>*q<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>>. Combining these facts
    with <eqref|ex-centered-width-bill> yields

    <\equation*>
      E*<around|(|d,R;m,k<around|(|m|)>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|d+2*z|)>*\<sigma\><rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>\<leqslant\><around|(|d+\<eta\>|)>*<around|(|1-z|)>*\<sigma\><rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>\<leqslant\><around|(|d+\<eta\>|)>*\<sigma\><rsub|m>.
    </equation*>

    The middle inequality follows from the two restrictions on <math|z>.
    Hence, for any <math|0\<less\>\<eta\>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>, the
    opened-depth estimate is at most <math|a<rsub|m><rsup|-d-\<eta\>>> for
    every sufficiently large <math|m>.

    For the finitely many smaller depths, choose an opened
    <math|k<around|(|m|)>\<geqslant\>m> and use the finite estimate
    <eqref|ex-centered-opened-root>. The maximum of <math|1> and the finitely
    many numbers

    <\equation*>
      exp <around|(|E*<around|(|d,R;m,k<around|(|m|)>|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>a<rsub|m><rsup|d+\<varepsilon\>>
    </equation*>

    is a single finite constant <math|A>. It is independent of the period,
    volume, site, and label within the specified coefficient ball. For late
    depths, <math|a<rsub|m><rsup|-d-\<eta\>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|m><rsup|-d-\<varepsilon\>>>
    because <math|a<rsub|m>\<less\>1> and
    <math|\<eta\>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>. This proves the power bound at all
    depths at or beyond the grade reference.

    The common interiority tail follows from the fixed finite stencil. Fix
    <math|m> and <math|d>, and let <math|S<rsub|d>> be that stencil of
    integer offsets. Since <math|a<rsub|N>\<to\>0>, for all sufficiently
    large <math|N>,

    <\equation*>
      2*a<rsub|N>*<around*|(|1+max <around*|(|<around|{|0|}>\<cup\><around|{|<around|\||z<rsub|\<alpha\>>|\|>:z\<in\>S<rsub|d>,\<alpha\>=1,\<ldots\>,4|}>|)>|)>\<less\>a<rsub|m>.
    </equation*>

    The schedule has odd block sizes, so the depth-<math|m> cell has an odd
    integer number of depth-<math|N> mesh steps along each axis, and its
    centered home site is at least the integer part of half that count from
    each boundary. The displayed strict inequality therefore places every
    link used by the stencil, including both endpoints, strictly inside the
    home chamber. The choice of <math|N> depends only on <math|m,d> and the
    fixed stencil, not on <math|p>, the site, or the label. The selected
    observable is gauge invariant and continuous, hence measurable and
    bounded on each compact finite-volume configuration space; together with
    this link locality it is chamber-admissible. Intersecting this tail with
    the root-estimate tail gives the asserted common eventual volume
    threshold.

    <subsubsection|The centered supplier on the public construction route>

    Write <math|S<rsub|B>=e*<around|{|2*<around|(|16*B+9|)>|}><rsup|4>> for
    the packing factor of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-transfer>.

    The public construction uses a single-source per-label supplier, followed
    by a downward induction across the scale transitions. We spell out the
    quantitative propagation and cost checks; the local transfer argument
    itself is supplied in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-transfer>. The
    public route does not call the separate multisource-lane construction.
    The finite-menu nonlinear response remains an explicit selected-history
    input to this conditional proof. At the adjacent step it must provide
    ordinary and flagged polynomial ledgers for finite nonquiet offers
    supported in their crests, under the history's regularity, arrival,
    response-rate, slot, and shipment-tariff conditions. The ordinary ledger
    is paid at exponent equal to the lower profile exponent plus <math|1/2>
    and site budget <math|1>; the flagged ledger is paid at exponent plus
    <math|1> and site budget <math|2*exp <around|(|-\<mu\>*\<Delta\><rsub|G,L><around|(|\<beta\>|)><rsup|2>/16|)>>.
    Its push identity fixes the quiet coefficient and the specified singleton
    coefficients for each box only; no arbitrary nonquiet coefficients are
    assumed. The offer threshold in this application is the upper support
    exponent minus <math|4>, with allowance fixed by that step's tariff. The
    conditional response record also retains propagation, tuning, slot
    separation, and the finite-wing price data used below.
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:selected-retention-public> supplies this
    record uniformly on the public run under its common-purchase hypotheses.
    The adjacent-scale finite-wing input is produced from the selected
    history: its nested realization, tariff, climbing-door data, and
    support-exponent bounds are passed through the one-step response
    construction. At step <math|i> the two support exponents are at least
    <math|100>, the price shrinkage <math|d<rsub|i>=\<delta\><rsub|i>> lies
    in <math|<around|(|0,1|]>>, and the one-step total price <math|t<rsub|i>>
    and local-response allowance <math|y<rsub|i>> obey the exact inequalities
    displayed below. These are the finite-wing response and one-step payment
    data required by the local-transfer step. The tariff and compatibility
    data are carried on the same chosen history; their producer is identified
    in the evidence file. At a step <math|i>, the resulting one-step
    conditions are <math|\<nu\><rsub|i>,\<nu\><rsub|i+1>\<geqslant\>100>,
    <math|0\<less\>d<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1>, and, with
    <math|T<rsub|i>=r<rsub|i>*<space|0.17em>S<rsub|B<rsub|i+1>>*d<rsub|i>+4*d<rsub|i>>,
    <math|T<rsub|i>+e<rsup|T<rsub|i>>*T<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|i>> and
    <math|<around|(|2*B<rsub|i>+1|)><rsup|4>*<around|(|y<rsub|i>+r<rsub|i>*<space|0.17em>S<rsub|B<rsub|i>>*d<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>>,
    <math|e<rsup|y<rsub|i>+r<rsub|i>*<space|0.17em>S<rsub|B<rsub|i>>*d<rsub|i>>-1\<leqslant\>1>.
    These are the one-step payment inequalities, with <math|t<rsub|i>> the
    total response price and <math|y<rsub|i>> the local-response allowance.
    The climbing witness and tariff are used by the one-step response lemma
    to produce the finite wing; their nonlinear estimate is a separately
    tracked input, not inferred from scale geometry.

    For occupancy and separation, write <math|B<rsub|i>> for the integer
    separation scale, <math|o<rsub|i><around|(|j|)>> for the designated site,
    <math|r<rsub|i>> for the occupancy bound, and
    <math|W<rsub|i><around|(|j|)>=\<phi\><rsub|i-1>*\<cdots\>*\<phi\><rsub|k>*w<around|(|j|)>>
    for the marched weight. The scale fields give
    <math|0\<less\>\<phi\><rsub|i>,\<delta\><rsub|i>>,
    <math|1\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|i>*\<phi\><rsub|i>*e<rsup|B<rsub|i>/128>>,
    <math|B<rsub|i+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|2>>,
    and <math|8\<leqslant\>B<rsub|i>>, where <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>> is the
    integer ratio count. The migration identity is
    <math|o<rsub|i+1>=<math-up|embed><rsub|i>\<circ\>o<rsub|i>>, and
    <math|r<rsub|i>> is nondecreasing. Occupancy means that each site has at
    most <math|r<rsub|i>> names mapped to it. Because the embedding is
    injective, the fiber of <math|o<rsub|i+1>> over a site in its image is
    the corresponding fiber of <math|o<rsub|i>>, and the other fibers are
    empty. Thus the fine occupancy is at most the old occupancy bound
    <math|r<rsub|i>>, which is at most <math|r<rsub|i+1>>. Iterating this
    comparison propagates occupancy from the opened scale through all finer
    scales. For a mode with at least two names, distinct origins have coarse
    separation at least <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|2>>. The scale comparison gives
    fine separation at least <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>> times that coarse
    separation. The split inequality <math|B<rsub|i+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|2>>
    therefore propagates the required separation forward.

    At a given scale, take any mode <math|I> of cardinality at least two and
    any support <math|X> containing its origin image. The following tour
    argument proves the required geometric estimate. Choose a minimizing
    cover of <math|X> by <math|D=<math-up|cover><around|(|B<rsub|i>,X|)>>
    balls of radius <math|B<rsub|i>>. In the source connected-halo cover, the
    centers form a connected graph: two centers are adjacent when their
    radius-<math|B<rsub|i>> halos meet or contain sites at max-distance at
    most one. Thus adjacent centers are at max-distance at most
    <math|2*B<rsub|i>+1>. Take a spanning tree. Join each tree edge by a unit
    max-distance path, advancing coordinates simultaneously so its length is
    at most <math|2*B<rsub|i>+1>, and traverse each such path out and back.
    This gives a walk visiting every center with length at most
    <math|2*<around|(|2*B<rsub|i>+1|)>*<around|(|D-1|)>\<leqslant\>D*<around|(|4*B<rsub|i>+2|)>>.
    Enumerate its unit steps as <math|R<around|(|0|)>,\<ldots\>,R<around|(|N|)>>;
    successive sites, including intermediate sites that need not be cover
    centers, are at distance at most one. For each origin
    <math|o<rsub|i><around|(|j|)>>, choose a covering center and a time
    <math|t<rsub|j>> at which the walk visits it; that center is within
    distance <math|B<rsub|i>> of the origin. If <math|j\<ne\>j<rprime|'>> are
    in the mode, their origins are separated by at least
    <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|2>>. The triangle inequality and the unit-speed walk
    give, after ordering <math|t<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|j<rprime|'>>>,
    <math|t<rsub|j>+<around|(|B<rsub|i><rsup|2>-2*B<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|j<rprime|'>>>.
    Therefore the buckets of width <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|2>-2*B<rsub|i>>
    containing these times are distinct. Since each time is at most <math|N>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|<around|\||I|\|>-1|)>*<around|(|B<rsub|i><rsup|2>-2*B<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>N.
    </equation*>

    For <math|B<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>8>, <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>4*<around|(|<around|\||I|\|>-1|)>*<around|(|B<rsub|i><rsup|2>-2*B<rsub|i>|)>/<around|\||I|\|>>
    when <math|<around|\||I|\|>\<geqslant\>2>. Combining with the tour length
    and <math|4*B<rsub|i>+2\<leqslant\>16*B<rsub|i>> yields
    <math|B<rsub|i><around|\||I|\|>\<leqslant\>64*D>. The support <math|X>
    can be any support containing the origin image, so in particular

    <\equation*>
      B<rsub|i><around|\||I|\|>\<leqslant\>64<space|0.17em><math-up|cover><around|(|B<rsub|i>,X|)>.
    </equation*>

    Set <math|a<rsub|j>=W<rsub|i><around|(|j|)>> and
    <math|v<rsub|j>=\<delta\><rsub|i>*W<rsub|i+1><around|(|j|)>>. Since
    <math|W<rsub|i+1>=\<phi\><rsub|i>*W<rsub|i>>, the march and the
    inequality <math|1\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|i>*\<phi\><rsub|i>*e<rsup|B<rsub|i>/128>>
    give <math|a<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>v<rsub|j>*e<rsup|B<rsub|i>/128>>, with
    both weights positive. Multiplying over <math|I> and applying the
    preceding geometric estimate yields

    <\equation*>
      <big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>I>a<rsub|j>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>I>v<rsub|j>*<space|0.17em>exp
      <around|{|<math-up|cover><around|(|B<rsub|i>,X|)>/2|}>.
    </equation*>

    This is the support-price comparison with cover exponent <math|1/2> and
    multiplier one. Its definition only asks about modes of cardinality at
    least two, so no singleton or empty-mode case is needed. Together with
    propagated occupancy, site migration, and positive marched weights, this
    gives the site/separation package data used downstream. For each label
    <math|v>, let <math|A<rsub|i>> be the scale-dependent grade dilation,
    <math|\<tau\><rsub|i><around|(|v|)>> its coefficient in the same-history
    representation, <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|i><around|(|v|)>> the associated
    coefficient family charge, and <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|i>> the paid column
    count. Define the paid bond <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i><around|(|v|)>=<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><space|0.17em><math-up|coeffSize><around|(|\<tau\><rsub|i><around|(|v|)>|)>>.
    The representation supplies the coefficient cone estimate

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-C\><rsub|i><around|(|v|)>\<leqslant\>\<Gamma\><rsub|i><math-up|coeffSize><around|(|<math-up|core><around|(|\<tau\><rsub|i><around|(|v|)>|)>|)>\<leqslant\>\<Gamma\><rsub|i><math-up|coeffSize><around|(|\<tau\><rsub|i><around|(|v|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The dilation step gives <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i+1><around|(|v|)>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|d>*\<Gamma\><rsub|i+1>*\<Lambda\><rsub|i><around|(|v|)>>.
    The paid scale data give, at every <math|i>,
    <math|0\<less\>\<phi\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1>,
    <math|\<phi\><rsub|i>*\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|d>*\<Gamma\><rsub|i+1>*\<zeta\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|i+1>>,
    <math|\<zeta\><rsub|i>*\<Gamma\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1>,
    <math|\<psi\><rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>, and
    <math|2<space|0.17em><math-up|orbitCost><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|i>+2*\<Gamma\><rsub|i+1>|)>*\<zeta\><rsub|i+1>\<leqslant\>1>.
    The opened-scale payment is <math|w<rsub|k>*\<Lambda\><rsub|k><around|(|v|)>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|k>>.
    The marched weights satisfy <math|w<rsub|i+1>=\<phi\><rsub|i>*w<rsub|i>>.
    Multiplying <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i+1><around|(|v|)>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|d>*\<Gamma\><rsub|i+1>*\<Lambda\><rsub|i><around|(|v|)>>
    by <math|w<rsub|i+1>> and using the displayed price inequality gives
    <math|w<rsub|i+1>*\<Lambda\><rsub|i+1><around|(|v|)>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|i+1>>
    from <math|w<rsub|i>*\<Lambda\><rsub|i><around|(|v|)>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|i>>;
    induction proves the bound at all scales. The one-step estimate charges
    the observable by <math|b=w<rsub|i+1>>, and
    <math|b\<leqslant\>w<rsub|i>>, so <math|b<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>*\<cal-C\><rsub|i><around|(|v|)>\<leqslant\>b*\<Lambda\><rsub|i><around|(|v|)>*\<Gamma\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>w<rsub|i>*\<Lambda\><rsub|i><around|(|v|)>*\<Gamma\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|i>*\<Gamma\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1>.
    For the finite-wing increment, put <math|b=w<rsub|i+1>> and
    <math|r=w<rsub|i>>. The march has <math|b\<leqslant\>r>; the adjacent
    paid bound and the column budget give exactly

    <\equation*>
      2*b<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|i+1>|\<\|\|\>><space|0.17em><math-up|orbitCost><space|0.17em><math-up|coeffSize><around|(|\<tau\><rsub|i+1><around|(|v|)>|)>*<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|i>+2*\<Gamma\><rsub|i+1>|)>\<leqslant\>1.
    </equation*>

    These are the two numeric inequalities passed to the scale-step
    construction. The same-history compatibility bound is a finite set of
    tales, one for each coefficient in the frame; each tale relates the fine
    exponential entry and forced profile to the coarser forced profile, with
    the gauge-door carrying the same local coordinate change. Together with
    the gauge-door transport law, it yields a finite family of insertions and
    the two bounds <math|2*w<rsub|i+1>*s\<leqslant\>1> and
    <math|w<rsub|i+1>*t\<leqslant\>1>. The charge estimate above is the first
    term in that tale budget; the displayed orbit-cost inequality pays the
    remaining coefficient increment and column contribution.

    Here is the per-scale profile construction. Reorient the coefficient by
    the selected motion and gauge move, and denote the resulting audited
    coefficient by <math|Q<rsub|i><around|(|v|)>>. Let
    <math|F<rsub|i><around|(|v|)>> be its exponential-entry field and let
    <math|g<rsub|i><around|(|v|)>> be the sequence of marked activities
    obtained by applying the grade dilation <math|A<rsub|i>> to its forced
    activities. For clarity, the coefficient coin is

    <\equation*>
      \<chi\><rsub|i>*<around|(|v;U|)>=<around|(|R<rsub|i>.<math-up|eval><around|(|U|)>|)>*F<rsub|i>*<around|(|v;U|)>+e<rsup|E<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>>*<big|sum><rsub|j>N<rsub|i,j><around|(|g<rsub|i><around|(|v|)>;U|)>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|E<rsub|i>> is the scalar action exponent. The audition
    identities identify this coin with the oriented density and identify the
    marked profile with the motion-transported forced profile. The remnant
    estimate gives a summable dominating function for
    <math|Q<rsub|i><around|(|v|)>.<math-up|forced>>, hence for
    <math|g<rsub|i><around|(|v|)>>. Reorientation preserves the normalized
    prior because it is a composition of a lattice motion and a gauge change;
    therefore the transformed ordinary field remains integrable. At the
    terminal level the same identity is the birth coefficient equation
    <math|R<rsub|N>.<math-up|eval><around|(|U|)>*b<rsub|N>*<around|(|v;U|)>=\<chi\><rsub|N>*<around|(|v;U|)>>.
    This proves the profile and coefficient identities used at every scale,
    rather than only a formal compatibility of names.

    At terminal scale, the calibrated response construction uses the actual
    attainment, the place bound

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|S*<around|(|<math-up|axis><rsub|j>,0,U|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>t,
    </equation*>

    cover number at most one, and bank inequality
    <math|e<rsup|\<nu\><rsub|N>+2>*\<frak-c\><rsub|N><around|(|t|)>*\<zeta\><rsub|N>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|N>>
    to produce the response with <math|w<rsub|N>*a<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|N>>
    and the prescribed forced hook. Here <math|\<frak-c\><rsub|N><around|(|t|)>>
    is the full coefficient cost at section budget <math|t>. The terminal
    response's hook agrees with the profile identity above, so the exact
    terminal birth coefficient is recovered.

    Define the pitch <math|\<pi\><rsub|i>> by
    <math|\<pi\><rsub|i>=\<nu\><rsub|i>> for <math|i\<less\>N> and
    <math|\<pi\><rsub|N>=\<nu\><rsub|N>-3>. Then
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|i+1>-4\<leqslant\>\<pi\><rsub|i+1>> at every
    transition, including the terminal step. Now descend from scale <math|N>.
    The terminal birth identity gives each singleton coefficient as its
    manifest and gives every allowed mode as the corresponding product of
    terminal insertions. To check terminal servedness quantitatively, put
    <math|c<rsub|N>=<math-up|forcedAllowance><rsub|N>> and
    <math|C<rsub|N>=c<rsub|N>+r<rsub|N>*S<rsub|B<rsub|N>>*u<rsub|N>>. The
    terminal receipt gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|<around|(|2*B<rsub|N>+1|)><rsup|4>|)>*C<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>3,<space|2em>e<rsup|C<rsub|N>>-1\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>\<nu\><rsub|N>-3\<geqslant\>0.
    </equation*>

    The terminal response at label <math|v> has price
    <math|a<rsub|N><around|(|v|)>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|N>|\<\|\|\>>*e<rsup|\<nu\><rsub|N>+2>*\<frak-c\><rsub|N><around|(|t<rsub|N>|)><math-up|coeffSize><around|(|<math-up|core><around|(|\<tau\><rsub|N><around|(|v|)>|)>|)>>.
    Since core deletion does not increase coefficient size, the paid bond
    gives <math|w<rsub|N><around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|N>|\<\|\|\>><math-up|coeffSize><around|(|<math-up|core><around|(|\<tau\><rsub|N><around|(|v|)>|)>|)>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|N>>.
    Hence <math|w<rsub|N>*a<rsub|N><around|(|v|)>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<nu\><rsub|N>+2>*\<frak-c\><rsub|N><around|(|t<rsub|N>|)>*\<zeta\><rsub|N>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|N>>,
    by the bank budget. Its toll estimate converts this into the required
    weighted section bound; the forced response is dominated. More
    explicitly, the terminal bank price requires
    <math|t<rsub|N>\<geqslant\>1> and

    <\equation*>
      e<rsup|\<nu\><rsub|N>+2>*<space|0.17em>\<frak-c\><rsub|N><around|(|t<rsub|N>|)>*<space|0.17em>\<zeta\><rsub|N>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|N>,
    </equation*>

    and gives the response price estimate stated above. Combining this toll
    control with the terminal birth expansion, Fourier hook, and the two
    displayed smallness conditions gives the served representation with
    exponent <math|\<nu\><rsub|N>-3>, exactly the terminal pitch.

    For the induction step, assume integrability, the singleton manifest
    identities, and the served representation at pitch
    <math|\<pi\><rsub|i+1>> at scale <math|i+1>. The bounded, measurable
    transition density preserves integrability as above; applying a mode
    coefficient before or after integration agrees because coefficient
    extraction is a continuous linear map on the finite-dimensional
    polynomial space. Thus each pushed coefficient at scale <math|i> is the
    transition applied to its scale-<math|i+1> coefficient. The separate
    single-insertion transition identity identifies each singleton
    coefficient with its scale-<math|i> manifest; higher modes remain
    coefficients of the propagated census polynomial. The one-step geometry
    supplies positive old and new weights, occupancy at both the old and
    embedded site families with multiplicity at most <math|r<rsub|i>>, the
    parent-site identity, and the support-price comparison. The transported
    representation is dominated, and its finite-wing response satisfies
    <math|2*w<rsub|i+1>*s\<leqslant\>1> and
    <math|w<rsub|i+1>*t\<leqslant\>1>. The one-step payment data are

    <\equation*>
      0\<less\>d<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1,<space|1em>\<nu\><rsub|i>,\<nu\><rsub|i+1>\<geqslant\>100,<space|1em>T<rsub|i>+e<rsup|T<rsub|i>>*T<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|i>,<space|1em><around|(|2*B<rsub|i>+1|)><rsup|4>*<around|(|y<rsub|i>+r<rsub|i>*<space|0.17em>S<rsub|B<rsub|i>>*d<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>,<space|1em>e<rsup|y<rsub|i>+r<rsub|i>*<space|0.17em>S<rsub|B<rsub|i>>*d<rsub|i>>-1\<leqslant\>1,
    </equation*>

    where <math|T<rsub|i>=r<rsub|i>*S<rsub|B<rsub|i+1>>*d<rsub|i>+4*d<rsub|i>>
    is the prepared insertion price and <math|t<rsub|i>> its response budget.
    The local-transfer theorem of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-transfer>
    applies with these values; it yields servedness at scale <math|i>. Finite
    downward induction proves the served-slab statement for every
    <math|m\<in\><around|[|k,N|]>>. The remaining section-compatibility and
    response-construction receipts used to obtain these displayed one-step
    inputs are named explicitly in the evidence file; they are part of the
    conditional input, not consequences of the transfer lemma.

    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-c02-selected-record> and
    Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:ex-c02-uniform-census> attach these inputs
    uniformly on the retained selected history, under their stated
    common-purchase hypotheses. The inputs are: the levels, doors, weights
    and budgets of the selected run at every terminal volume; the
    adjacent-scale compatibility, place and bank bounds of the same history;
    and, at the terminal scale, the birth polynomial, the transition
    identities and the Fourier hook. Together they form the funded
    selected-history record; the same record is the hypothesis of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-selected-run-service>. The common width
    and the uniform served-census assertion are proved in that corollary. The
    present argument uses those fields and the separate scale and opening
    hypotheses stated in the theorem.

    The dependency chain is therefore

    <\equation*>
      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<text|funded selected
        run>>|<cell|\<longrightarrow\><text|paid same-history
        receipts>\<longrightarrow\><eqref|ex-centered-marked-copy>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<longrightarrow\><eqref|ex-centered-opened-root>\<longrightarrow\><text|uniform
        centered power bound>\<longrightarrow\><text|continuum
        extraction>.>>>>
      </aligned>
    </equation*>

    The compact-bound and local-modulus arguments of
    Section<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-producers> use that final centered
    estimate. The separate multisource-lane route, whose offered lanes
    already package terminal births and raw adjacent-scale bounds, is not
    called along this public dependency chain. In particular, the normalized
    overlay-density price calculation in the optional multisource draft is
    not an input here.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Producing the selected finite-volume transfer
  data><label|ex-centered-freight-producer>

  The centered estimate uses a finite-volume representation at every period
  and volume. On the public construction route these data are produced on the
  same selected history: the run chooses one terminal coefficient family,
  then reconstructs compatible coefficient families at all earlier scales.
  This section isolates that producer and explains the two algebraic steps
  that turn the reconstruction into transfer data. The tariff and one-step
  interpolation estimates remain the quantitative inputs of the selected
  history; the proof below does not replace them by an unpriced compactness
  argument.

  <subsubsection|One-step response and its finite-dimensional assembly>

  Fix a volume, period, and adjacent scales <math|m,m+1>. Let <math|d> be the
  chosen grade, let <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>\<geqslant\>1> be the block side,
  let <math|F> be its finite-dimensional complex coefficient space, and let
  <math|s\<geqslant\>1> be the number of paid columns at scale <math|m+1>.
  Write <math|\<mu\><rsub|F><around|(|u|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a><around|\||u<rsub|a>|\|>>
  for its coefficient <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>> norm, where
  <math|<around|\||\<cdummy\>|\|>> is the ordinary complex modulus, and let
  <math|<math-up|core><around|(|u|)>> erase the vacuum coordinate. Every
  frame coordinate has degree between <math|0> and <math|d>. The scale
  dilation <math|<math-up|vowel><rsub|m>> multiplies coordinate <math|a> by
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|d-deg <around|(|a|)>>>, so

  <\equation*>
    \<mu\><rsub|F><around|(|u|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsub|F><around|(|<math-up|vowel><rsub|m><space|0.17em>u|)>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|d>*\<mu\><rsub|F><around|(|u|)>.
  </equation*>

  A coefficient family at scale <math|m+1> consists of a density family
  <math|f<rsub|v>>, a nonnegative charge, and an index isomorphism
  <math|\<iota\>:F\<to\>F>. The selected coefficient family certificate
  requires <math|\<iota\>> to commute with each motion on <math|F>. The
  family is complex-linear in <math|v> and its motion covariance is
  semilinear according to the independent Boolean conjugation flag in
  <math|<math-up|Motion>=<math-up|Signed>\<times\><math-up|Bool>>; it is not
  determined by coordinate orientation. For each <math|v>, the selected
  coefficient family certificate also supplies the format and remainder
  bounds; the associated finite-volume integrability lemma gives
  <math|f<rsub|v>\<in\>L<rsup|1>> of the normalized prior. We do not use
  continuity of the densities. The selected transition defines the Wilson
  push

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|<math-ss|P><rsub|m>f|)><around|(|U|)>=\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|d>*<big|int>f<around|(|V|)>*<space|0.17em>K<rsub|m>*<around|(|U,d*V|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|K<rsub|m>*<around|(|U,d*V|)>> is the one-step Wilson transition
  density against normalized Haar measure and <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>> is the
  block side. Thus <math|<math-ss|P><rsub|m>> includes the grade-scaling
  factor <math|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|d>>; it is not a probability-preserving
  Markov operator. The selected transition density is jointly measurable,
  nonnegative and uniformly bounded in both configurations, and its integral
  in the coarse configuration equals one for every fine configuration. These
  properties give slice integrability: for every output <math|U>, the
  transition-weighted section of a prior-integrable input is integrable, and
  the resulting pushed function is measurable. This is the conditional-kernel
  conclusion attached to the lawful transition, not a consequence of finite
  total mass alone. The transition commutes with the selected signed motions.
  These properties make the push well-defined and linear, and for each signed
  motion <math|g>,

  <\equation*>
    g\<cdot\><around|(|<math-ss|P><rsub|m>f<rsub|v>|)>=<math-ss|P><rsub|m>f<rsub|g\<cdot\>v>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed, both identities follow first for finite linear combinations from
  linearity of the integral; the motion identity follows by the change of
  variables preserving the Wilson transition density. These identities are
  exact, not limiting statements.

  The selected ticket's one-column estimates have three exact forms. For each
  nonzero frame axis <math|e<rsub|a>>, it supplies a coarse coefficient whose
  density is the pushed fine density, whose orbit-cost-weighted lead error is
  at most <math|\<eta\><space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><around|(|e<rsub|a>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
  whose activity residual price is at most
  <math|C<space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|vowel><rsub|m><around|(|<math-up|core><around|(|e<rsub|a>|)>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
  and whose finite gate-crossing certificate costs <math|t>. For each regular
  supported term of mass <math|u>, both coupon prices are <math|\<eta\>*u>
  and the gate-crossing cost is <math|2*u>. For each forced term of mass
  <math|u> on a marked wing site, both coupon prices are
  <math|\<eta\>*u/\<kappa\>> and the gate-crossing cost is <math|2*u>, where
  <math|0\<less\>\<kappa\>=<sqrt|<text|forcedAllowance><rsub|m+1>>\<leqslant\>1>
  uses the fine level. The output remainder is measured with the separate
  coarse-level forced allowance. Each such coupon includes a format bound,
  the exact density or activity identity, an orbit-cost lead bound, a finite
  response expansion, and its paid gate-crossing estimate through the
  selected door. These are the exact hypotheses used below; we do not infer
  their numerical bounds from compactness.

  On the public route these coupons are produced together for the selected
  adjacent history. A held spring supplies an interval
  <math|<around|[|x,y|]>>, a parameter <math|z> in that interval, and a local
  realization <math|K<around|(|z|)>>. The two selected fields are the fine
  representation <math|K<around|(|z|)>> and the coarse result of that same
  spring at <math|z>. The gate is the composed local comparison between those
  fields; the same spring and parameter give the climb witness. No strict
  inequality between <math|x> and <math|y> is needed. For a grade <math|d>
  with <math|d+3> no larger than the coarse clock, the ticket's wing contains
  the principal-field tags indexed by supports containing the origin and the
  marked keeper tags. It is finite because the finite-volume lattice has
  finitely many supports and keeper tags.

  Write <math|E<rsub|m+1>> for the support exponent at the fine level,
  <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|m+1>> for its profile title, and <math|b<rsub|m>>
  for the block side. Denote the coarse and fine clock values by
  <math|J<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|c>>=J<around|(|m|)>> and
  <math|J<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|f>>=J*<around|(|m+1|)>>. The raw coupon
  prices are <math|\<eta\><rsub|m>=<math-up|rate><around|(|b<rsub|m>|)>> for
  the lead and <math|C<rsub|m>=E<around|(|J<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|f>>|)>> for
  the residual, where <math|E> is the selected schedule. The exact total
  gate-crossing allowance is

  <\equation*>
    t<rsub|m>=2*e<rsup|E<rsub|m+1>>*\<vartheta\><rsub|m+1>.
  </equation*>

  The selected clock interpolation chooses one heading for the whole wing.
  Its three coupon families are exactly the axis, regular-activity, and
  forced-activity cases displayed above. In the raw ticket, the axis lead
  price is <math|\<eta\><rsub|m>*\<mu\><rsub|F><around|(|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>e<rsub|a>|)>>,
  the residual price is <math|C<rsub|m>*\<mu\><rsub|F><around|(|<math-up|vowel><rsub|m><around|(|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>e<rsub|a>|)>|)>>,
  and the crossing bill is <math|t<rsub|m>>. A regular support of mass
  <math|u> has equal lead and residual price <math|\<eta\><rsub|m>*u> and
  bill <math|2*u>; a marked forced support of mass <math|u> has equal price
  <math|\<eta\><rsub|m>*u/\<kappa\>> and bill <math|2*u>. These are the three
  fields of the finite coupon certificate. The origin unit is supplied by its
  exact unit coupon.

  The coupon-production step is conditional on its field estimates. Assume
  the selected spring supplies the two adjacent fields at the same parameter
  and a sound local section; assume the heading forecast and cap satisfy the
  two-sided map bound, the principal-field estimate has the displayed axis
  prices, each supported regular field has the displayed regular prices, and
  each slot-fitted forced field at a marked keeper site has the displayed
  forced prices. Then one heading and one ticket for the whole finite wing
  are obtained by applying those three estimates to their respective terms
  and collecting the resulting certificates. The heading forecast comes from
  the calibrated flight matrix. The selected schedule-price bound pays both
  the cap's triangular-price estimate and the graded remainder price. The
  axis, regular, and forced estimates are separate hypotheses; no one of them
  follows from another. This is the precise conditional finite coupon package
  used here. It leaves the derivation of those analytic field estimates and
  the selected schedule-price data to their upstream producers.

  More precisely, we use the following finite interpolation statement: when
  the two fields at the selected spring parameter, slot table, motion/rate
  data, schedule-price bound, and keeper are those of the selected history,
  and <math|d+3\<leqslant\>J<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|c>>>, it returns one
  heading and a ticket for every wing containing the principal-field tags
  just described. The ticket has axis residual price <math|C<rsub|m>>, lead
  price <math|\<eta\><rsub|m>>, and crossing allowance <math|t<rsub|m>>, with
  the regular and forced prices just listed. The heading and all prices are
  common to that whole wing. Substituting the finite wing formed by those
  tags together with the keeper's marked tags gives the ticket used by the
  public run. This statement and its three analytic coupon estimates are
  inputs here; this conditional assembly does not prove their field-level
  derivation. Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:heading-history-handoff> and
  <reference|sec:three-port-history> supply this interface on the same funded
  interpolation. In their notation <math|L=b<rsub|m>=\<ell\><rsub|m>>,
  <math|b<rsub|f>=E<rsub|m+1>> and <math|\<tau\><rsub|f>=\<vartheta\><rsub|m+1>>,
  so the crossing allowance is exactly <math|2*e<rsup|b<rsub|f>>*\<tau\><rsub|f>>.
  Their coefficient allowance is <math|C=C<rsub|m>>; the schedule pays both
  its triangular and graded remainder requests. The purchase inequalities
  below belong to the common scale purchase, rather than being conclusions of
  the local interpolation theorem. Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-reprise>
  attaches this ticket to the retained return and supplies the countable
  merger needed for its application to a whole coefficient family.

  The ticket prices <math|C<rsub|m>,\<eta\><rsub|m>> are then checked against
  the one-step reserves. Let <math|T<rsub|m>=B:F\<to\>F> be the selected
  heading's coefficient isomorphism. Its dilated lead map is
  <math|B\<circ\><math-up|vowel><rsub|m>>; the two-sided bounds below concern
  <math|B>, not this composition. The purchase inequalities are

  <\equation*>
    \<mu\><rsub|F>*<around|(|T<rsub|m>*v|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|m>*\<mu\><rsub|F><around|(|v|)>,<space|2em>\<mu\><rsub|F>*<around|(|T<rsub|m><rsup|-1>*v|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|m>*\<mu\><rsub|F><around|(|v|)>.
  </equation*>

  The same selected clock purchase pays the ticket through

  <\equation*>
    <\aligned>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|1>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C<rsub|m>,>|<cell|0>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<eta\><rsub|m>,>>|<row|<cell|\<eta\><rsub|m>*<around|(|1+s|)><math-up|orbitCost><around|(|F|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\><math-up|barunit><around|(|b<rsub|m>|)>,>|<cell|>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<around|(|C<rsub|m>+\<eta\><rsub|m>*s|)><math-up|orbitCost><around|(|F|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>W<rsub|m+1>.>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
    </aligned>
  </equation*>

  where <math|s> is the number of paid columns and <math|W<rsub|m+1>> is the
  available fine-level column reserve. The one-column theorem combines the
  raw coupon with these purchases: after all <math|s> columns its residual
  and lead allowances are <math|c<rsub|m>=C<rsub|m>+\<eta\><rsub|m>*s> and
  <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m>=\<eta\><rsub|m>*<around|(|1+s|)>>, and its
  crossing bill is <math|t<rsub|m>+2*s>. These inequalities are the actual
  interpolation and funding checks consumed by the one-column construction;
  the proof does not choose a new adjacent pair or reset the history.

  The reason the ticket pays these three kinds of term is the local
  decomposition of the marked field. For a fine coefficient <math|P>, first
  apply the local rack map: it preserves the coefficient coordinate and
  exponential entry, while its keeper supplies the forced-profile and
  remainder bounds. The density is then

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|<text|exponential entry>|)><space|0.17em>\<cdot\><around|(|<text|local
    regular part>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|j><around|(|<text|forced activity at
    >j|)><space|0.17em>\<cdot\><around|(|<text|dressed field at >j|)>.
  </equation*>

  The regular summands use the regular coupons. A forced summand with zero
  coefficient vanishes. For a nonzero forced coefficient, its support is
  marked and belongs to the selected finite wing; its absolute mass uses the
  forced coupon after division by <math|\<kappa\>>. The origin term uses the
  principal axis coupon. The keeper's remainder bound and the
  exponential-entry envelope, which are hypotheses of the local rack map
  above, dominate both countable activity series absolutely; Tonelli's
  theorem applied to the resulting absolute majorant proves integrability and
  permits summing the densities term by term. The coupon-summation identity
  adds the costs and errors, so the three displayed prices give the
  one-column response and preserve the exact pushed density.

  The local selected-history ticket is a finite set of marked insertions with
  a linear cap. It is paid with nonnegative allowances <math|C,\<eta\>,t> and
  yields an endpoint response on every coefficient of an incoming coefficient
  family. By the one-column theorem above, the response allowances after
  accounting for all <math|s> columns are

  <\equation*>
    c=C+\<eta\>*s,<space|2em>\<epsilon\>=\<eta\>*<around|(|1+s|)>,<space|2em>t+2*s.
  </equation*>

  For each frame coordinate <math|e<rsub|a>>, the one-column estimate gives a
  marked polynomial <math|Z<rsub|a>> for <math|j<rsub|a>=\<iota\><rsup|-1><around|(|e<rsub|a>|)>>.
  Applying a selected motion <math|g> gives <math|Z<rsub|g,a>> with density
  <math|g\<cdot\><around|(|<math-ss|P><rsub|m>f<rsub|j<rsub|a>>|)>=<math-ss|P><rsub|m>f<rsub|\<iota\><rsup|-1>*<around|(|g\<cdot\>e<rsub|a>|)>>>,
  lead error at most <math|\<epsilon\>*<space|0.17em>\<mu\><rsub|F><around|(|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>e<rsub|a>|)>>,
  residual activity at most <math|c*D<rsub|a>>, and charged cost at most
  <math|t+2*s>, where

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|a>=\<mu\><rsub|F><space|-0.17em><around*|(|<math-up|vowel><rsub|m><around|(|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>e<rsub|a>|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|\<cal-G\>=<math-up|Motion>=<math-up|Signed>\<times\><math-up|Bool>>
  be the finite motion set. Write <math|g=<around|(|\<pi\>,\<epsilon\>|)>>,
  where <math|\<pi\>> is a signed coordinate permutation and
  <math|\<epsilon\>\<in\><around|{|0,1|}>> is the independent conjugation
  flag. Let <math|\<rho\><rsub|g>> be its (possibly semilinear) action on
  <math|F> and let <math|\<sigma\><rsub|g>> be its scalar action, identity
  for <math|\<epsilon\>=0> and complex conjugation for <math|\<epsilon\>=1>.
  Then the actual averaging chips are

  <\equation*>
    \<chi\>*<around|(|v;g,a|)>=<frac|1|<around|\||\<cal-G\>|\|>>*<space|0.17em>\<sigma\><rsub|g><space|-0.17em><around*|(|<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|g><rsup|-1>*\<iota\>*v|)><rsub|a>|)>,<space|2em><around|(|g,a|)>\<in\>\<cal-G\>\<times\><around|{|1,\<ldots\>,q|}>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|q=dim<rsub|\<bbb-C\>> F>. Applying the inverse motion, taking
  its <math|a>th coordinate, and then applying the matching scalar action
  makes <math|v\<mapsto\>\<chi\>*<around|(|v;g,a|)>> complex-linear,
  including when <math|\<epsilon\>=1>. Averaging the reindexed coordinate
  expansions gives

  <\equation*>
    \<iota\><around|(|v|)>=<big|sum><rsub|g,a>\<chi\>*<around|(|v;g,a|)>*<space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|g>*e<rsub|a>,<space|2em>v=<big|sum><rsub|g,a>\<chi\>*<around|(|v;g,a|)>*<space|0.17em>\<iota\><rsup|-1>*<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|g>*e<rsub|a>|)>.
  </equation*>

  For any selected <math|h>, reindexing <math|g=h*q<rprime|'>> gives

  <\equation*>
    \<chi\>*<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|h>*v;h*g,a|)>=\<sigma\><rsub|h>*<around|(|\<chi\>*<around|(|v;g,a|)>|)>,
  </equation*>

  so the assembled response has the required semilinear motion covariance.
  Thus conjugation comes from the independent flag, not from whether the
  signed coordinate permutation reverses orientation. The action preserves
  the grade and commutes with the core, but it need not permute the
  coordinate axes. Define the finite frame orbit price by

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|orbitCost><around|(|F|)>\<assign\>1+<big|sum><rsub|g\<in\>\<cal-G\>><big|sum><rsub|a=1><rsup|q>\<mu\><rsub|F>*<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|g>*e<rsub|a>|)>.
  </equation*>

  For any <math|x=<big|sum><rsub|a>x<rsub|a>*e<rsub|a>>, axis expansion and
  the triangle inequality give

  <\equation*>
    \<mu\><rsub|F>*<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|g>*x|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|a><around|\||x<rsub|a>|\|>*\<mu\><rsub|F>*<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|g>*e<rsub|a>|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|orbitCost><around|(|F|)>*\<mu\><rsub|F><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  Apply this bound to <math|\<rho\><rsub|g><rsup|-1>> and to
  <math|x=<math-up|core><around|(|\<iota\>*v|)>> or
  <math|x=<math-up|vowel><rsub|m><around|(|<math-up|core><around|(|\<iota\>*v|)>|)>>.
  The scalar actions preserve modulus, and both core and vowel commute with
  the motion. Averaging over <math|g> in the displayed chip formula gives the
  two estimates

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|<around|(|g,a|)>><around|\||\<chi\>*<around|(|v;g,a|)>|\|>*<space|0.17em>\<mu\><rsub|F><around|(|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>e<rsub|a>|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|orbitCost><around|(|F|)>*\<mu\><rsub|F><around|(|<math-up|core><around|(|\<iota\>*v|)>|)>,
  </equation*>

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|<around|(|g,a|)>><around|\||\<chi\>*<around|(|v;g,a|)>|\|>*D<rsub|a>\<leqslant\><math-up|orbitCost><around|(|F|)>*\<mu\><rsub|F><space|-0.17em><around*|(|<math-up|vowel><rsub|m><around|(|<math-up|core><around|(|\<iota\>*v|)>|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  They are the finite tally inequalities obtained by summing the absolute
  chip weights against the axis and dilated-axis costs. Therefore the
  weighted sums of lead errors and residual activities are at most
  <math|\<epsilon\><math-up|orbitCost><around|(|F|)>*\<mu\><rsub|F><around|(|<math-up|core><around|(|\<iota\>*v|)>|)>>
  and <math|c<math-up|orbitCost><around|(|F|)>*\<mu\><rsub|F><around|(|<math-up|vowel><rsub|m><around|(|<math-up|core><around|(|\<iota\>*v|)>|)>|)>>,
  respectively; the corresponding finite tally of gate-crossing bills is the
  purchased wing allowance. These are the exact finite sums paid by the
  selected column and orbit-cost budgets. The estimates hold after each
  signed motion because the marked ticket is closed under those motions and
  the Wilson transition is motion covariant. Here is the finite assembly
  step. Let <math|\<iota\>:F\<to\>F> be the coefficient family's
  coefficient-index isomorphism, and let <math|\<cal-J\>> be the finite tally
  of pairs <math|<around|(|g,a|)>> consisting of a selected signed motion
  <math|g> and a coordinate index <math|a>. Put <math|e<rsub|a>> for the
  coordinate vector. Let <math|j<rsub|a>=\<iota\><rsup|-1><around|(|e<rsub|a>|)>>
  and <math|v<rsub|g,a>=g\<cdot\>j<rsub|a>=\<iota\><rsup|-1>*<around|(|g\<cdot\>e<rsub|a>|)>>;
  the equality uses equivariance of the index map. The frame interpolation
  coefficients <math|\<chi\>*<around|(|v;g,a|)>> are the coefficients of the
  finite frame expansion of <math|\<iota\><around|(|v|)>>, so

  <\equation*>
    \<iota\><around|(|v|)>=<big|sum><rsub|<around|(|g,a|)>\<in\>\<cal-J\>>\<chi\>*<around|(|v;g,a|)>*<around|(|g\<cdot\>e<rsub|a>|)>,<space|2em>v=<big|sum><rsub|<around|(|g,a|)>\<in\>\<cal-J\>>\<chi\>*<around|(|v;g,a|)>*v<rsub|g,a>.
  </equation*>

  For each coordinate <math|a>, first take the one-column response at
  <math|j<rsub|a>>, then apply the selected signed motion <math|g> to it. The
  resulting moved response <math|Z<rsub|g,a>> has fine density
  <math|g\<cdot\>f<rsub|j<rsub|a>>> and, by covariance of the transition,
  coarse density <math|g\<cdot\><around|(|<math-ss|P><rsub|m>f<rsub|j<rsub|a>>|)>=<math-ss|P><rsub|m>f<rsub|v<rsub|g,a>>>.
  Its scalar lead is one term in the reconstructed coordinate, and its forced
  profile is the corresponding moved profile. Assemble them by the finite sum

  <\equation*>
    Z<around|(|v|)>=<big|sum><rsub|<around|(|g,a|)>\<in\>\<cal-J\>>\<chi\>*<around|(|v;g,a|)>*Z<rsub|g,a>.
  </equation*>

  The assembled scalar lead and forced profile are the same finite weighted
  sums of their one-column counterparts. By the exact one-column density
  identity, linearity of the push, and the second frame identity,

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-up|density><around|(|Z<around|(|v|)>|)>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|<around|(|g,a|)>>\<chi\>*<around|(|v;g,a|)>*g\<cdot\><around|(|<math-ss|P><rsub|m>f<rsub|j<rsub|a>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=<math-ss|P><rsub|m><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|<around|(|g,a|)>>\<chi\>*<around|(|v;g,a|)>*f<rsub|v<rsub|g,a>>|)>=<math-ss|P><rsub|m>f<rsub|v>.>>>>
  </align*>

  All coefficient and residual bounds are summed with
  <math|<around|\||\<chi\>*<around|(|v;g,a|)>|\|>>. The frame interpolation
  inequalities are proved by the finite frame identities: the lead error is
  bounded by <math|\<epsilon\><space|0.17em><math-up|orbitCost><around|(|F|)><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><around|(|\<iota\>*v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
  and the residual by <math|c<space|0.17em><math-up|orbitCost><around|(|F|)><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|vowel><rsub|m><around|(|<math-up|core><around|(|\<iota\>*v|)>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>.
  The first is the triangle inequality followed by the frame orbit-cost
  bound; the second follows by summing the coordinate bounds and the vowel
  size inequality. The paid ticket bounds the total gate-crossing costs by
  the corresponding finite-wing budget. Thus no infinite series or exchange
  of an infinite sum with the transition integral occurs. The one-step
  direction estimates give the cone bounds; here is the numerical argument.
  Put <math|L=b<rsub|m>>, let <math|J<rsub|f>> and <math|J<rsub|c>> be the
  fine and coarse clocks with <math|J<rsub|f>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|c>+1>, and
  set <math|C=W.*K<around|(|J<rsub|f>|)>>,
  <math|D<rsub|f>=W.*K*<around|(|J<rsub|f>+1|)>>,
  <math|D<rsub|c>=W.*K*<around|(|J<rsub|c>+1|)>>. Let <math|s<rsub|f>> be the
  fine column count, <math|s<rsub|c>> the coarse column count, and
  <math|e=<math-up|barunit><around|(|L|)>>. Write <math|u> and <math|v> for
  the coarse and fine frame coordinates, <math|r> and <math|t> for their
  charges, and <math|y=<math-up|vowel><rsub|L><around|(|v|)>>. The direction
  certificate provides a coefficient isomorphism <math|B> such that

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|B*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,<around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsup|-1>*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,<space|2em>B<around|(|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>x|)>=<math-up|core><around|(|B*x|)>,
  </equation*>

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|u-B*y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>e*<around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>+t|)>,<space|2em>r\<leqslant\>C\<\|\|\><math-up|core><space|0.17em>y\<\|\|\><rsub|1>+e*t,<space|2em>t\<leqslant\>s<rsub|f><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  The selected price growth gives <math|1\<leqslant\>C\<leqslant\>D<rsub|f>>
  and, by the slow-step condition, <math|C\<leqslant\>D<rsub|c>>. The bank
  certificate says

  <\equation*>
    100000*D<rsub|f><rsup|6>*<around|(|1+s<rsub|f>|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><math-up|puffs><around|(|L|)>,<space|2em>e=<math-up|puffs><around|(|L|)><rsup|-3>.
  </equation*>

  In particular <math|<math-up|puffs><around|(|L|)>\<geqslant\>1> and
  <math|2*C*<around|(|1+s<rsub|f>|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|puffs><around|(|L|)>>,
  so <math|2*C*e*<around|(|1+s<rsub|f>|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|puffs><around|(|L|)><rsup|-2>\<leqslant\>1>
  and <math|e\<geqslant\>0>. The fine room certificate gives
  <math|64*D<rsub|f><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|f>>, and the coarse room
  certificate gives <math|64*D<rsub|c><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|c>>. Hence
  <math|2*C\<leqslant\>s<rsub|f>> and <math|4*C<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|c>>.
  Since <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>=<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|vowel><rsub|L><around|(|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<geqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
  the incoming charge bound implies

  <\equation*>
    2*C*e*<around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>+t|)>\<leqslant\>2*C*e*<around|(|1+s<rsub|f>|)><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  Consequently <math|2*C*<around|\<\|\|\>|u-B*y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>.
  Put <math|\<delta\>=<around|\<\|\|\>|u-B*y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>. Then
  <math|C*\<delta\>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>/2>, and
  the inverse norm bound gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>=<around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsup|-1>*<around|(|B*y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C*<around|\<\|\|\>|B*y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C\<\|\|\>u*<around|\<\|\|\>|<rsub|1>+C*\<delta\>\<leqslant\>C|\<\|\|\>>*u\<\|\|\><rsub|1>+<tfrac|1|2><around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,
  </equation*>

  so <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*C<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>.
  In the other direction,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|B*y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>+\<delta\>\<leqslant\>C\<\|\|\>y\<\|\|\><rsub|1>+<frac|1|2*C><around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*C<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,
  </equation*>

  since <math|C\<geqslant\>1>. Apply the same absorption argument to the core
  vectors; the core projection contracts the <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>> norm and
  commutes with <math|B>. It yields

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*C<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  It follows, using <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>L<rsup|d><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
  that

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|f><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|f>*L<rsup|d><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  Also <math|e*t\<leqslant\>C<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
  by the displayed bank estimate, so

  <\equation*>
    r\<leqslant\>2*C<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>4*C<rsup|2><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|c><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  These are exactly the charge, fine-to-coarse, and coarse-to-fine bounds
  used below. In particular, the two norm inequalities are full coefficient
  norms; the core norm occurs only in the charge estimate.

  The response defines a linear map <math|Q<rsub|m>:F\<to\>F> by its coarse
  reference coordinate. The first cone inequality gives
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<iota\><around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|f>*<around|\<\|\|\>|Q<rsub|m>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>.
  If <math|Q<rsub|m>*v=0>, then <math|\<iota\><around|(|v|)>=0>; since
  <math|\<iota\>> is an isomorphism, <math|v=0>. Thus <math|Q<rsub|m>> is
  injective and, since <math|F> is finite dimensional, bijective. The
  one-step output stores this isomorphism as the new coefficient family
  index. The density family is not precomposed by its inverse. Across several
  scales, the full-norm cone inequality is iterated from the reference to the
  terminal frame; the exact recurrence and terminal isomorphism give the
  single global transfer normalization used below.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Finite backward reconstruction><label|ex-freight-backward> Let
    <math|t\<leqslant\>N>. Suppose a selected finite-volume history has (i) a
    terminal coefficient family which agrees with the calibrated top
    coefficient frame and satisfies the terminal reflection condition; (ii) a
    motion-covariant Wilson push between each adjacent pair of scales whose
    transition density has the boundedness, measurability and mass properties
    stated above; (iii) at every <math|m\<in\><around|[|t,N|)>> a selected
    door, a sound local section, and a finite paid ticket whose one-step
    response satisfies the column estimates and cap conditions described
    above; and (iv) the room and bank bounds needed to pay the cumulative
    responses. Then there is a sequence of coefficient families
    <math|V<rsub|m>>, <math|t\<leqslant\>m\<leqslant\>N>, such that the
    terminal coefficient family is the selected one, every adjacent density
    satisfies

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|density><around|(|V<rsub|m><around|(|v|)>|)>=<math-ss|P><rsub|m><math-up|density><around|(|V<rsub|m+1><around|(|v|)>|)>,
    </equation*>

    and each scale has the selected reality, motion, and coefficient-cone
    bounds. The pushed coefficient maps define a linear transfer system,
    normalized by the inverse of its reference coefficient map.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use downward induction. At <math|m=N>, take the supplied terminal
    coefficient family. Suppose <math|V<rsub|m+1>> has been constructed and
    has the required integrability, reality, and motion properties. Apply the
    one-step response assembly above to its paid ticket. The density identity
    follows from the exact finite linear assembly. The local cap gives the
    response and residual bounds at scale <math|m>; the paid room and bank
    inequalities keep the accumulated expenditure within its assigned budget.
    The terminal reflection condition propagates because the Wilson push is
    invariant under reflection and the ticket is real on the selected real
    subspace. The coefficient-cone estimate propagates by the one-step paid
    inequality. The one-step construction packages its own invertible index
    map with its density family as a coefficient family; we do not alter the
    family by precomposing with the inverse. This completes one induction
    step. There are only <math|N-t> steps, so finite downward induction
    constructs every <math|V<rsub|m>> and all the claimed identities.
    Finally, if the reference readout of a coefficient <math|v> is zero, the
    full-norm cone inequality propagates zero from scale <math|t> to scale
    <math|N>. The terminal coefficient family index is an isomorphism, so
    <math|v=0>. The reference map is injective and hence bijective on
    <math|F>; its inverse gives the one global normalization in the
    conclusion.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The transfer map and its normalization>

  For the unnormalized music just constructed, write
  <math|x<rsub|m><around|(|v|)>> for the coefficient index of its coefficient
  family at scale <math|m>. The full-norm cone estimates at each adjacent
  pair have the form

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|m+1><around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|m+1><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|m><around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|m><around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|m+1>*\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|d><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|m+1><around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|s<rsub|m+1>> is the paid column count and
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>> the block side. Let
  <math|Q<rsub|t><around|(|v|)>=x<rsub|t><around|(|v|)>> be the reference
  readout. If <math|Q<rsub|t><around|(|v|)>=0>, the first inequality
  propagates zero successively through every scale up to <math|N>. At the
  terminal scale <math|x<rsub|N>> is an isomorphism, so <math|v=0>. Thus
  <math|Q<rsub|t>> is injective, and finite dimensionality makes it
  bijective. This is the sole global normalization. The transfer data record
  the unnormalized music entries and set their terminal coordinate map to the
  reference readout <math|Q<rsub|t>>. When viewed on external labels
  <math|w>, every scale uses the same reparametrization
  <math|V<rsub|m>*<around|(|Q<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*w|)>>; no scale gets an
  independently fitted inverse. The selected public supplier applies this
  construction for every grade and every pair <math|<around|(|N,p|)>> beyond
  the grade reference. Its first finitary step is the selected endpoint: the
  terminal frame is obtained from the calibrated section and the keeper
  chosen by the nested history. The endpoint is on top because the selected
  Wilson trajectory represents the terminal field and the paid terminal
  receipt gives the exact coefficient identities. Its reflection condition
  follows from the real section and the terminal keeper. The selected climb
  provides the door and the finite ticket at each adjacent pair. The ticket's
  allowance is the scale's tariff, and the paid room and bank bounds control
  the total cost. Applying Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-freight-backward> and
  then the reference normalization gives the transfer data required by the
  centered-volume calculation.

  The resulting ordinary mathematical object is a music of compatible
  coefficient families with a selected terminal frame, the adjacent
  finite-wing estimates, and a normalized transfer map. In the center
  argument, the separate terminal birth polynomial has coefficients given by
  finite products of the named observables. It is continuous on compact
  configuration space and hence integrable. The coefficient family and its
  transition identities supply the individual insertion profiles used in the
  served representation. The full birth polynomial is propagated separately;
  its higher-mode coefficients belong to the census construction, not to a
  single coefficient family. The backward integral identity then relates each
  propagated mode to the corresponding terminal moment. All later bounds
  concern normalized finite-volume expectations.

  <subsubsection|Scope of the producer statement>

  The preceding induction proves the finite reconstruction from its exact
  selected-history ticket and terminal inputs. In the public run, the
  schedule is chosen from the purchased trajectory so that the grade
  reference lies before every requested terminal level. The selected climb
  and terminal receipt are retained on this same trajectory; they are not
  assembled by selecting a new history for each period. The consumer then
  uses the resulting transfer system to form the terminal birth and the root
  estimate.

  The quantitative production of the ticket's nonlinear coefficient estimates
  is a distinct finite-volume step and is not proved in this chapter. It
  consists of the field-interpolation estimates together with the mode-slot
  and shipment estimates of the funded selected-history record; this chapter
  only states the numerical inequalities that the one-step theorem consumes.
  Likewise, uniformity over every coefficient in a bounded grade ball is part
  of the selected-history estimates; the attachment and uniform served-census
  construction are now Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-c02-selected-record>
  and Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:ex-c02-uniform-census>. Their
  common-purchase hypotheses remain explicit. What is proved here is the
  finite backward assembly.

  <subsection|Producing the occupied, migrating, separated
  sites><label|ex-centered-site-geometry>

  The mode-moment receipt needs a site assignment at its opened scale: it
  must have bounded occupancy, agree with its descendants under every
  selected coarse-graining map, and keep distinct names in every nontrivial
  mode far apart. On the public root-test route, one finite copy of the
  opened lattice supplies all three properties. This section proves that
  geometric production from the selected tab-opening schedule.

  Let <math|m\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>N> be levels in one selected history
  and fix a period <math|p>. Put <math|V=\<Lambda\><rsub|m>> for the finite
  site set at level <math|m>. Write <math|a<rsub|j>> for the physical lattice
  spacing at level <math|j>, <math|L<rsub|j>=a<rsub|j>/a<rsub|j+1>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
  for the exact adjacent ratio, and <math|B<rsub|j>> for the schedule's
  covering radius. Write <math|n<rsub|j>> for the integer side of the
  periodic lattice <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|j>>, so an aligned embedding with
  ratio <math|q<rsub|j>=a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|j>> has
  <math|n<rsub|j>=q<rsub|j>*n<rsub|m>>. The selected tab schedule gives

  <\equation*>
    L<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>3,<space|2em>B<rsub|j+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|j><rsup|2>,<space|2em>B<rsub|k><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>
  </equation*>

  when the root level <math|m> is opened at <math|k> with parameter one. At
  every level <math|j\<geqslant\>m>, define

  <\equation*>
    o<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>=<math-up|embed><rsub|m\<to\>j><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>x\<in\>V,
  </equation*>

  using the canonical aligned lattice embedding, and put
  <math|o<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>=0> below <math|m>. Let <math|B<rsub|j>> be
  the schedule's separation radius at level <math|j>; for the selected Wilson
  history this is also the radius of the local configuration there.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Opened-copy geometry><label|lem:ex-centered-opened-copy> Assume
    the selected family identifies the represented field radius with
    <math|B<rsub|j>>, and the schedule bounds just stated hold. Then the
    opened-copy assignment has the following properties for
    <math|k\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>N>:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|o<rsub|j>:V\<to\>\<Lambda\><rsub|j>> is injective, and
      hence has occupancy at most one.

      <item>For each <math|k\<leqslant\>j\<less\>N>,
      <math|o<rsub|j+1><around|(|x|)>> is the canonical embedding of
      <math|o<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>> into level <math|j+1>; in particular its
      parent is <math|o<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>>.

      <item>For distinct <math|x,y\<in\>V>,
      <math|<math-up|sep><around|(|o<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>,o<rsub|j><around|(|y|)>|)>\<geqslant\>B<rsub|j><rsup|2>>.

      <item>At the terminal level, the root copy map sends each
      <math|x\<in\>V> to <math|o<rsub|N><around|(|x|)>>.
    </enumerate>

    Consequently, if an occupancy schedule <math|r<rsub|j>> obeys
    <math|1\<leqslant\>r<rsub|j>>, the assignment is migrating, has occupancy
    at most <math|r<rsub|k>> at scale <math|k>, and satisfies the
    separated-origin condition for every census mode whose distinct names are
    drawn from <math|V>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The opening condition gives <math|B<rsub|k><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>>.
    We propagate this to every later <math|j>. If
    <math|B<rsub|j><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|j>>, then

    <\equation*>
      B<rsub|j+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|j><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2*<frac|a<rsub|m>|a<rsub|j>>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|j>*<frac|a<rsub|m>|a<rsub|j>>=<frac|a<rsub|m>|a<rsub|j+1>>,
    </equation*>

    because <math|L<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>3>. Thus
    <math|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>B<rsub|j><rsup|2>> for all
    <math|j\<geqslant\>k>. The level-<math|m> to level-<math|j> ratio
    <math|q<rsub|j>=a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|j>> is an integer, by multiplication of
    the adjacent integer ratios.

    For sites on the periodic lattice <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|m>>, define
    separation as the maximum over coordinates of the shortest periodic
    distance, namely

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|sep><around|(|x,y|)>=max<rsub|\<alpha\>>
      min<rsub|r\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>><around|\||<wide|x|~><rsub|\<alpha\>>-<wide|y|~><rsub|\<alpha\>>+r*n<rsub|m>|\|>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|<wide|x|~>,<wide|y|~>> are any integer lifts of the periodic
    sites; the minimum does not depend on the chosen lifts. Choose in each
    coordinate a centered integer representative of the difference. Under the
    aligned ratio embedding of factor <math|q<rsub|j>>, each such
    representative is multiplied by <math|q<rsub|j>> and remains centered
    modulo the new periodic side <math|n<rsub|j>=q<rsub|j>*n<rsub|m>>,
    including the half-side tie case. Hence

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|sep><around|(|o<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>,o<rsub|j><around|(|y|)>|)>=q<rsub|j><math-up|sep><around|(|x,y|)>.
    </equation*>

    Distinct <math|x,y> in the level-<math|m> lattice have integer separation
    at least one. Therefore

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|sep><around|(|o<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>,o<rsub|j><around|(|y|)>|)>=q<rsub|j><math-up|sep><around|(|x,y|)>\<geqslant\>q<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>B<rsub|j><rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    In particular they are distinct; this proves injectivity and occupancy at
    most one. The exact ratio-composition identity for canonical embeddings
    gives <math|o<rsub|j+1>=<math-up|embed><rsub|j\<to\>j+1>\<circ\>o<rsub|j>>.
    Applying the parent map, which is a left inverse of that embedding, gives
    the stated parent identity. The forward embedding identity is the
    migration condition used in the service theorem. By the definition of the
    opened root cell, its terminal copy map is precisely the canonical
    embedding <math|o<rsub|N>>. Finally, <math|1\<leqslant\>r<rsub|k>>
    upgrades occupancy one to the requested bound, while the separation
    estimate is exactly the required bound for every pair of distinct names
    in any nontrivial mode.
  </proof>

  For the selected root test, take its finite root set as <math|V>.
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-centered-opened-copy> gives the migration,
  occupancy, and separated-origin inputs used by the finite-volume moment
  receipt. It gives these inputs for the same selected period, stop level,
  and schedule; it does not choose new sites independently at each scale. The
  estimate does not supply the weights or the response budget, which remain
  separate selected-history inputs.

  <subsection|Propagating the selected terminal birth to a moment
  bound><label|ex-selected-birth-receipt>

  This section records the finite-volume terminal receipt used on the public
  selected-history route. It starts from the calibrated family, its
  same-history multiscale data, and the birth polynomial made from a finite
  list of observables. The result is a mode-by-mode normalized moment
  estimate. It is a conditional receipt: it uses the selected-history service
  and migration data stated below, rather than deriving those upstream
  construction inputs.

  Let <math|\<cal-V\>> be a finite marker set and let <math|<math-ss|B>> be a
  downward-closed family of allowed subsets containing the empty set and all
  singletons. Its elements are the census modes. For each
  <math|a\<in\>\<cal-V\>>, choose a grade label <math|s<rsub|a>>, a terminal
  site <math|o<rsub|N><around|(|a|)>>, and a motion
  <math|<around|(|\<pi\><rsub|a>,\<epsilon\><rsub|a>|)>> consisting of a
  signed coordinate transformation and an independent conjugation flag. Let
  <math|O<rsub|a>> be the corresponding local cylinder observable, and define
  its terminal translate by

  <\equation*>
    T<rsub|a><around|(|U|)>=\<sigma\><rsub|\<epsilon\><rsub|a>><space|-0.17em><around*|(|O<rsub|a><around*|(|<math-up|pull><rsub|\<pi\><rsub|a>,o<rsub|N><around|(|a|)>><space|0.17em>U|)>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<sigma\><rsub|0>> is the identity and <math|\<sigma\><rsub|1>>
  is complex conjugation. The pullback includes both the signed
  transformation and the translation to <math|o<rsub|N><around|(|a|)>>.
  Assume the selected family makes each <math|T<rsub|a>> continuous on the
  compact terminal configuration space. Write <math|R<rsub|N>> for the
  selected terminal representative and set

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-B\><rsub|N><around|(|U|)>=R<rsub|N><around|(|U|)><space|0.17em><math-up|Pass><space|-0.17em><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|a\<in\>\<cal-V\>>e<rsub|<around|{|a|}>>*T<rsub|a><around|(|U|)>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where the marker algebra has one basis element for each allowed mode and
  square-free multiplication: <math|e<rsub|S>*e<rsub|S<rprime|'>>=e<rsub|S\<cup\>S<rprime|'>>>
  if the sets are disjoint and their union is allowed, and <math|0>
  otherwise. The polynomial pass is the finite exponential in this nilpotent
  algebra. For every allowed mode <math|I\<in\><math-ss|B>>, its coefficient
  satisfies

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|\<cal-B\><rsub|N><around|(|U|)>|)><rsub|I>=R<rsub|N><around|(|U|)>*<big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>I>T<rsub|a><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  To verify the identity, expand the <math|r>th power in the finite
  exponential. A term contributing to an allowed mode <math|I> must use
  precisely the <math|<around|\||I|\|>> distinct singleton markers in
  <math|I>: repeated markers vanish, and no term of another degree can
  produce <math|I>. Every ordering of those singletons contributes the same
  product. Downward closure ensures that each intermediate union in such an
  ordering is allowed, so the <math|<around|\||I|\|>>! orderings cancel the
  factorial in the exponential coefficient. The empty mode comes from the
  zeroth power and has coefficient <math|R<rsub|N>>. The centered-copy
  application uses the full census <math|<math-ss|B>=2<rsup|\<cal-V\>>>; the
  general mode estimate below does not require that choice. For this selected
  terminal representative, <math|R<rsub|N>> is the bare Wilson density. Write

  <\equation*>
    Z<rsub|N,p>\<assign\><big|int>R<rsub|N><around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|N><around|(|U|)>\<gtr\>0,<space|2em>\<omega\><rsub|N,p><around|(|F|)>\<assign\>Z<rsub|N,p><rsup|-1>*<big|int>R<rsub|N><around|(|U|)>*F<around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|N><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Selected-history birth moment bound><label|thm:ex-selected-birth-moment>
    Fix a selected family, volume <math|N>, period <math|p>, grade <math|d>,
    and reference scale <math|k> with <math|C.<math-up|ref><around|(|d|)>\<leqslant\>k\<less\>N>.
    Assume all labels <math|s<rsub|a>> have grade <math|d>. Suppose the
    family satisfies its calibration conditions and the same-history
    finite-volume receipt supplies the terminal birth, its backward Wilson
    transitions, the scale-<math|k> service resources, and the positive
    vacuum treasury. Assume also the geometric inputs at <math|k>: positive
    weights <math|w<rsub|a>>, site assignments
    <math|o<rsub|k><around|(|a|)>>, an occupancy bound, the migration
    identity for the site assignments, and the separated-origin condition for
    every nontrivial mode. Suppose further that

    <\equation*>
      w<rsub|a>*<space|0.17em>\<frak-m\><rsub|a>\<leqslant\>\<frak-f\><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>1*<space|1em><around|(|a\<in\>\<cal-V\>|)>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|\<frak-m\><rsub|a>=\<Gamma\><rsub|k><space|0.17em><math-up|paid><rsub|a>>
    is the column-weighted coefficient size of label <math|s<rsub|a>> at
    scale <math|k>, <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|k>\<geqslant\>1> is the selected
    column count, and <math|\<frak-f\><rsub|k>> is the scale's small price.
    The factor <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|k>> is needed by the local coefficient
    and activity response; the unweighted payment alone is insufficient for
    that response estimate. Require the service receipt to give measurable
    mode densities <math|H<rsub|I>>, a common scalar <math|\<sigma\>>, and
    real costs <math|A<rsub|k>,D<rsub|k>> such that its representative is
    nonnegative, its support exponent is nonnegative, and its reserve is
    <math|f=1>, with

    <\equation*>
      w<rsub|I><around|\||H<rsub|I><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>exp
      <around|(|\<sigma\>+A<rsub|k>|)>,<space|1em>Z<rsub|N,p>\<geqslant\>exp
      <around|(|\<sigma\>-D<rsub|k>|)>,<space|1em>A<rsub|k>+D<rsub|k>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|k>|\|>*\<cal-T\><rsub|k>+<around|\||\<cal-V\>|\|>*<around|(|x+y+2*f|)>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|w<rsub|I>=<big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>I>w<rsub|a>>,
    <math|x=3*\<frak-f\><rsub|k>>, and <math|y=\<frak-f\><rsub|k>>. The
    same-history receipt identifies <math|H<rsub|I>> with the scale-<math|k>
    coefficient of the backward birth. Then for every census mode <math|I>,

    <\equation>
      <around*|\||\<omega\><rsub|N,p><space|-0.17em><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>I>T<rsub|a>|)>|\|>*<big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>I>w<rsub|a>\<leqslant\>exp
      <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|k>|\|>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-T\><rsub|k>+6<around|\||\<cal-V\>|\|>|)>,<label|ex-selected-birth-bound>
    </equation>

    where <math|\<omega\><rsub|N,p>> is the normalized finite-volume Wilson
    expectation, <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|k>> is the scale-<math|k> lattice, and
    <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|k>> is the finite-volume vacuum treasury cost. The
    conclusion is conditional on the listed service, geometry, and
    paid-coefficient hypotheses.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    First, <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|N>> is integrable. The selected terminal
    representative is continuous, each <math|T<rsub|a>> is continuous by
    assumption, and the finite marker polynomial is a finite sum of products
    of these functions. Hence every coefficient of <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|N>>
    is continuous. The terminal configuration space is compact, so each
    coefficient is integrable against the normalized product-Haar prior.

    For <math|k\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>N>, let <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|i>> be
    the coefficient polynomial obtained by the selected backward transition.
    It satisfies

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-B\><rsub|i>=<math-up|Push><rsub|i><around|(|\<cal-B\><rsub|i+1>|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-B\><rsub|N><text|
      as above>,
    </equation*>

    where the fine-to-coarse kernel is normalized in its output variable:

    <\equation*>
      <big|int>k<rsub|i>*<around|(|U,V|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|i><around|(|V|)>=1<space|2em><text|for
      each fine configuration >U.
    </equation*>

    The density push is therefore

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|<math-up|Push><rsub|i><space|0.17em>F|)><around|(|V|)>=<big|int>k<rsub|i>*<around|(|U,V|)>*F<around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|i+1><around|(|U|)>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|U> is fine and <math|V> is coarse. For an integrable
    coefficient <math|F>, slice integrability and Fubini give the precise
    conservation identity

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><around|(|<math-up|Push><rsub|i><space|0.17em>F|)><around|(|V|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|i><around|(|V|)>=<big|int>F<around|(|U|)><around*|(|<big|int>k<rsub|i>*<around|(|U,V|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|i><around|(|V|)>|)>*d*\<mu\><rsub|i+1><around|(|U|)>=<big|int>F<around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|i+1><around|(|U|)>.
    </equation*>

    For this terminal birth, <math|R<rsub|N>> is the bare Wilson density, so
    <math|Z<rsub|N,p>=<big|int>R<rsub|N>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|N>>. The
    selected transition density is jointly measurable, nonnegative and
    uniformly bounded on the product of the finite-volume configuration
    spaces. Multiplying an integrable coefficient by that density gives an
    integrable function on the product of the priors; the same bound also
    gives integrability on every fixed coarse slice. Fubini therefore applies
    at each step. Since coefficient extraction is a continuous linear map on
    the finite-dimensional marker algebra, it commutes with this finite
    integral. Inductively, for each mode <math|I>,

    <\equation>
      <big|int><around|(|\<cal-B\><rsub|k><around|(|U|)>|)><rsub|I>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|k><around|(|U|)>=<big|int><around|(|\<cal-B\><rsub|N><around|(|U|)>|)><rsub|I>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|N><around|(|U|)>=Z<rsub|N,p>*<space|0.17em>\<omega\><rsub|N,p><space|-0.17em><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>I>T<rsub|a>|)>,<label|ex-selected-birth-integral>
    </equation>

    where <math|Z<rsub|N,p>\<gtr\>0> is the finite-volume Wilson partition
    function. The second equality uses the terminal birth identity and the
    definition of normalized expectation. This establishes the exact
    finite-volume transfer of the moment; no continuum limit or interchange
    of an infinite sum is involved.

    At scale <math|k>, the same-history receipt yields a served marked
    representation of <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|k>>. Its finite response estimate
    gives at marker <math|a> a coefficient bound by three times the
    column-weighted coefficient size <math|\<frak-m\><rsub|a>> and a total
    activity bound by <math|\<frak-m\><rsub|a>>. Multiplying by
    <math|w<rsub|a>> and using <math|w<rsub|a>*\<frak-m\><rsub|a>\<leqslant\>\<frak-f\><rsub|k>>
    gives the coefficient and activity bills <math|x=3*\<frak-f\><rsub|k>>
    and <math|y=\<frak-f\><rsub|k>>. The factor three is the local
    coefficient bound from the response estimate; the activity bill is its
    unmultiplied support charge. The separated-origin condition and occupancy
    estimate are what make this finite response estimate valid for a whole
    mode, and migration transports those facts through the backward scales.
    Since <math|\<frak-f\><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>1>, these satisfy
    <math|x+y+2\<leqslant\>6>.

    Here are the precise conditional price assumptions used at the
    scale-<math|k> service step. The marked representation is lawful, its
    representative is pointwise nonnegative, its support exponent is
    nonnegative, and its served reserve is <math|f=1>. For each mode
    <math|I>, the local density estimate is

    <\equation*>
      w<rsub|I><around|\||H<rsub|I><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>exp
      <around|(|\<sigma\>+A<rsub|k>|)>,<space|2em>w<rsub|I>=<big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>I>w<rsub|a>,
    </equation*>

    with <math|x=3*\<frak-f\><rsub|k>> and <math|y=\<frak-f\><rsub|k>>. The
    vacuum denominator bound is

    <\equation*>
      Z<rsub|N,p>\<geqslant\>exp <around|(|\<sigma\>-D<rsub|k>|)>,
    </equation*>

    and the receipts give

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|k>+D<rsub|k>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|k>|\|>*\<cal-T\><rsub|k>+<around|\||\<cal-V\>|\|>*<around|(|x+y+2*f|)>,<space|2em>f=1.
    </equation*>

    The reserve contributes the term <math|2*f>; the coefficient <math|+2> is
    not paid merely by the marker fee. Integrating the pointwise inequality
    yields

    <\equation*>
      w<rsub|I><around*|\||<big|int>H<rsub|I>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|k>|\|>\<leqslant\>exp
      <around|(|\<sigma\>+A<rsub|k>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Divide by the positive denominator lower bound. The scalar
    <math|\<sigma\>> cancels:

    <\equation*>
      w<rsub|I>*<frac|<around|\||<big|int>H<rsub|I>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|k>|\|>|Z<rsub|N,p>>\<leqslant\>exp
      <around|(|A<rsub|k>+D<rsub|k>|)>\<leqslant\>exp
      <around*|(|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|k>|\|>*\<cal-T\><rsub|k>+6<around|\||\<cal-V\>|\|>|)>,
    </equation*>

    using <math|x+y+2*f=4*\<frak-f\><rsub|k>+2\<leqslant\>6>. Equation
    <eqref|ex-selected-birth-integral> identifies this normalized coefficient
    integral with the desired Wilson moment and proves the result.
  </proof>

  The terminal product, continuity, and exact backward integration have been
  proved above. For a general history the service certificate, its local
  coefficient envelope, the origin-counting response and the finite-volume
  vacuum lower bound are explicit inputs. For the retained public family,
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-c02-selected-record> and
  Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:ex-c02-uniform-census> attach them to this
  receipt under the common-purchase hypotheses.

  <subsubsection|The local denominator on the retained history>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Quiet coefficient and partition
    function><label|lem:ex-selected-birth-denominator>Fix the selected
    history at <math|<around|(|N,p|)>>. For each <math|i\<leqslant\>N> let
    <math|A<rsub|i>> be its unmarked representative, and put
    <math|A<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|ev>><around|(|U|)>=<math-up|eval><around|(|A<rsub|i>|)><around|(|U|)>>.
    Assume the selected Wilson identities
    <math|A<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|ev>>=R<rsub|N>> and
    <math|A<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|ev>>=<math-up|Push><rsub|i><space|0.17em>A<rsub|i+1><rsup|<math-up|ev>>>
    for <math|i\<less\>N>, where the push is the normalized transition in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-selected-birth-receipt>. Its kernel is that
    of step <math|i> at the coupling <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,i+1>>, the same
    coupling used by the transfer and by the birth chain. Then

    <\equation*>
      <big|int>A<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|ev>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|i>=Z<rsub|N,p><space|1em><text|for
      all >i\<leqslant\>N,<space|2em><around|(|\<cal-B\><rsub|i>|)><rsub|\<varnothing\>>=A<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|ev>><space|1em><text|for
      >k\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>N,
    </equation*>

    the range on which the chain <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|i>> is defined.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    At <math|N>, the constant coefficient of the finite exponential is one,
    so <math|<around|(|\<cal-B\><rsub|N>|)><rsub|\<varnothing\>>=R<rsub|N>=A<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|ev>>>.
    If the assertion holds at <math|i+1>, extracting the quiet coefficient
    commutes with the normalized push by the same finite-dimensional
    integration argument used in <eqref|ex-selected-birth-integral>. Thus

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|\<cal-B\><rsub|i>|)><rsub|\<varnothing\>>=<math-up|Push><rsub|i><around|(|\<cal-B\><rsub|i+1>|)><rsub|\<varnothing\>>=<math-up|Push><rsub|i><space|0.17em>A<rsub|i+1><rsup|<math-up|ev>>=A<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|ev>>.
    </equation*>

    For the integral identity, each <math|A<rsub|i+1><rsup|<math-up|ev>>> is
    integrable. At <math|i+1=N> it is continuous on a compact space. Below
    <math|N> it is the push of an integrable function by the bounded
    normalized kernel, by the argument used for
    <eqref|ex-selected-birth-integral>. Integrating and using the normalized
    transition mass at every step gives the claimed equality with the
    positive terminal partition function.
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|Common width and the vacuum cutoff.> Fix an admitted grade
  <math|d> and radius <math|R\<gtr\>0>. Let <math|g<rsub|d,R>\<geqslant\>1>
  bound the coefficient norm on the closed grade ball, which is compact in
  the finite-dimensional grade space. Using the calibrated reference readout
  and the adjacent coefficient cone of Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-freight-producer>,
  the coefficient at level <math|k> satisfies
  <math|q<rsub|k><around|(|v|)>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|d,R>*D<rsub|k>> throughout
  the ball, with <math|D<rsub|k>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<less\>k>\<Gamma\><rsub|i+1>>.
  Indeed the reference coefficient is <math|v> and each forward bound
  multiplies by at most the next column count. Extending the product back to
  zero only increases it. Here <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1> are the
  columns of the fixed schedule. Writing <math|a<rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|mesh>>>
  for the mesh spacing, take the single positive weight

  <\equation*>
    \<omega\><rsub|k>=<frac|<around|(|a<rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|mesh>>|)><rsup|d>*\<zeta\><rsub|d,k>|\<Gamma\><rsub|k>*g<rsub|d,R>*D<rsub|k>>.
  </equation*>

  Multiplication by the local column-weighted price
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|k><around|(|a<rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|mesh>>|)><rsup|-d>*q<rsub|k><around|(|v|)>>
  gives at most <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,k>> for every label in the ball. The
  weight is independent of the terminal volume, period, site and particular
  label. Moreover <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,k>\<leqslant\>1>. This follows from
  the coin condition <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,k>*\<Gamma\><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>1>
  of the purchased schedule, item<nbsp>(R5) of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-marked-portfolio-calibration>,
  together with <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|k>\<geqslant\>1>. So the small price
  <math|\<frak-f\><rsub|k>=\<zeta\><rsub|d,k>> satisfies the hypothesis
  <math|\<frak-f\><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>1> of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-selected-birth-moment>.

  On a served census at <math|k\<less\>N>, use the local prices
  <math|x=3*\<zeta\><rsub|d,k>>, <math|y=\<zeta\><rsub|d,k>> and reserve one
  in <eqref|ex-centered-steer>. In the small-ball bound of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-construction> choose the radius
  <math|s<rsub|k>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|-1/2>>. The paid scale has
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>\<gtr\>1> and the selected threshold is
  <math|s<rsub|k>> times an exponential of a square, so
  <math|0\<less\>s<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>1> and <math|s<rsub|k>> is at most the
  threshold. Write <math|E> for the local excess cost, <math|\<kappa\>> for
  the unmarked activity allowance, and <math|\<beta\>> for the local
  coupling. The numerator pays per site <math|E+\<kappa\>> and the
  denominator pays <math|E+\<beta\><space|0.17em><math-up|baseBudget><around|(|s<rsub|k>|)>+4*d<rsub|G>*log
  <around|(|16/s<rsub|k>|)>>, with the same scalar in both. To compare their
  sum with the selected treasury, use the selected representation bounds: the
  local excess cost is at most the regular allowance plus the loop price at
  its coupling, the activity allowance is bounded by the scheduled forced
  allowance, and <math|0\<leqslant\>\<beta\>\<leqslant\>2*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>.
  The loop price is an affine function of the coupling with nonnegative
  slope, and the base budget is nonnegative. Replacing the coupling in both
  costs by <math|2*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>> therefore gives at most the
  scheduled treasury at radius <math|s<rsub|k>>. The denominator in this
  calculation is <math|<big|int><math-up|eval><around|(|A|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|k>>,
  where <math|A> is the representative of the served representation at scale
  <math|k>. The served census input is taken over the history's own
  representative, that is <math|A=A<rsub|k>>; this is part of the conditional
  served-census input below. Hence, by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-selected-birth-denominator>,
  the denominator is <math|<big|int>A<rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|ev>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|k>=Z<rsub|N,p>>.
  Thus, whenever the shared retention and transfer give the served census and
  its local response on the actual geometry, the bound
  <eqref|ex-selected-birth-bound> holds with this same width throughout the
  grade ball. This argument uses the conditional served response only at the
  opened scale and makes no new choice of history.

  <subsection|Large entrance scales for the selected
  service><label|ex-centered-geyser-scale-estimates>

  This section proves the finite-volume scale estimates used by the selected
  multiscale service. It isolates the elementary large-parameter estimates
  and then explains how their separate eventual thresholds are made into one
  monotone delay sequence. All scale, profile, and gauge data below are the
  actual fixed data of the selected run.

  Write

  <\equation*>
    B<around|(|x|)>=<around*|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>,<space|2em>h<around|(|x|)>=e<rsup|-<frac|1|2>*log
    x>,<space|2em>f<rsub|d><around|(|x|)>=x<rsup|-<around*|(|deg
    F<rsub|d>+4|)>>,<space|2em>s<around|(|x|)>=<tfrac|1|4>h<around|(|x|)>,
  </equation*>

  and, for <math|x\<gtr\>1> and an integer radius <math|B>,

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|stain><around|(|x|)>=exp <space|-0.17em><around*|[|-exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<tfrac|1|5><around|(|log log
    x|)><rsup|2>|)>|]>,<space|2em><math-up|siteFactor><around|(|B|)>=e*<around|{|2*<around|(|16*B+9|)>|}><rsup|4>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|F<rsub|d>> is the finite frame at grade <math|d>. Fix a
  nonnegative integer occupancy schedule <math|Q> and the positive gauge
  slope constant <math|c<rsub|G>=s<rsub|G>> of <eqref|eq:shoot-slope>. For a
  fixed stage <math|j>, write <math|L<rsub|j>> for its block side,
  <math|b<rsub|j>=c<rsub|G>*log L<rsub|j>>,
  <math|y<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>=x+b<rsub|j>>, and
  <math|p<rsub|j>=Q<around|(|j|)>> for its occupancy allowance. At stage
  <math|j> fix the column count <math|C<rsub|j>> and support exponent
  <math|E<rsub|j>>. For each grade fix its finite local-section constant
  <math|A<rsub|d>>. These quantities remain fixed while
  <math|x\<to\>\<infty\>>. The stage index is not a finite-volume period
  index.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Uniform eventual scale estimates for one
    transition><label|lem:ex-geyser-radial>Fix stages <math|i,j> satisfying
    <math|<math-up|Step><around|(|j,i|)>>, meaning
    <math|i\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>i+1>, and a finite grade range
    <math|0\<leqslant\>d\<leqslant\>i>. Let
    <math|C<rsub|i>,C<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1> be the stage column counts and
    <math|E<rsub|i>,E<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>0> the support exponents. For each
    grade let <math|A<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>1> be its uniform local-section
    constant, <math|a<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>1> its motion orbit cost, and
    <math|m<rsub|d>=deg F<rsub|d>> its frame degree. Let
    <math|L<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1> be the integer block side and assume the
    finite title cost <math|T<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>0>. Put

    <\equation*>
      r<rsub|j>=2*e<rsup|E<rsub|j>>*<space|0.17em>T<rsub|j>.
    </equation*>

    Then there is <math|x<rsub|0>\<gtr\>1> so that every
    <math|x\<geqslant\>x<rsub|0>> satisfies

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|8\<leqslant\>B<around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>y<rsub|j><around|(|x|)><rsup|3>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|B<around|(|x|)>/128>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-geyser-first>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|2*B<around|(|x|)>+1|)><rsup|4>*<around*|(|<math-up|stain><around|(|x|)>+p<rsub|j><space|0.17em><math-up|siteFactor><around|(|B<around|(|x|)>|)>*s<around|(|x|)>|)>\<leqslant\>3,>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|e<rsup|<math-up|stain><around|(|x|)>+p<rsub|j><math-up|siteFactor><around|(|B<around|(|x|)>|)>*s<around|(|x|)>>-1\<leqslant\>1,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-geyser-second>>>>>
    </align>

    and, for every <math|d\<leqslant\>i>,

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|f<rsub|d><around|(|x|)>*C<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-geyser-creek1>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|2*a<rsub|d>*<around*|(|r<rsub|j>+2*C<rsub|j>|)>*f<rsub|d><around|(|y<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>|)>\<leqslant\>1,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-geyser-creek2>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|e<rsup|E<rsub|i>+2>*A<rsub|d>*x<rsup|m<rsub|d>>*f<rsub|d><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>s<around|(|x|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-geyser-creek3>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|y<rsub|j><around|(|x|)><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>y<rsub|j><around|(|x|)><rsup|-1>*L<rsub|j><rsup|d>*C<rsub|j>*f<rsub|d><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|d><around|(|y<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-geyser-creek4>>>>>
    </align>

    as well as <math|\<nu\><rsub|d>\<leqslant\>B<around|(|x|)>>, where
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|d>> is the minimum stencil side at grade <math|d>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    We check each bound from its large-<math|x> mechanism. Since
    <math|B<around|(|x|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>, eventually
    <math|B<around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>8> and
    <math|B<around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>\<nu\><rsub|d>> for all the finitely
    many grades. The first polynomial estimate follows from the same
    elementary comparison: for any fixed <math|b> and integer <math|q>,
    eventually <math|x+b\<leqslant\>2*x>, <math|C*2<rsup|q>\<leqslant\>x>,
    and <math|<around|(|q+1|)>*log x\<leqslant\><around|(|log
    x|)><rsup|2>/128>. Thus

    <\equation*>
      C*<around|(|x+b|)><rsup|q>\<leqslant\>C*2<rsup|q>*x<rsup|q>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|q+1>=e<rsup|<around|(|q+1|)>*log
      x>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|B<around|(|x|)>/128>.
    </equation*>

    Apply this with <math|C=1,q=3,b=b<rsub|j>> to get
    <eqref|eq:ex-geyser-first>.

    For <eqref|eq:ex-geyser-second>, set <math|H=h<around|(|x|)>>. We verify
    the reserves directly. The hat is <math|H=x<rsup|-1/2>>, so
    <math|H\<leqslant\>1/4> eventually. Define

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|smallK><around|(|x|)>\<assign\>exp
      <space|-0.17em><around*|(|-<tfrac|1|2>exp
      <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<tfrac|1|5><around|(|log log
      x|)><rsup|2>|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Writing <math|y=log log x>, for <math|y\<geqslant\>10> we have
    <math|y<rsup|2>/5-y\<geqslant\>10\<gtr\>log 6>, hence <math|exp
    <around|(|y<rsup|2>/5|)>\<geqslant\>6*e<rsup|y>=6*log x>. Comparing
    exponents yields <math|<math-up|smallK><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|6>>.

    For the contact bound, put <math|t=<frac|1|2>*log x>, so
    <math|H=e<rsup|-t>>. For <math|t\<geqslant\>1>,
    <math|B<around|(|x|)>=<around|\<lceil\>|4*t<rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>\<leqslant\>5*t<rsup|2>>,
    and therefore

    <\equation*>
      2*B<around|(|x|)>+1\<leqslant\>11*t<rsup|2>,<space|2em><math-up|siteFactor><around|(|B<around|(|x|)>|)>=e*<around*|(|2*<around|(|16*B<around|(|x|)>+9|)>|)><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>e*<around|(|178*t<rsup|2>|)><rsup|4>.
    </equation*>

    Thus the contact expression is at most
    <math|C<rsub|p>*t<rsup|16>*e<rsup|-t>> for a constant <math|C<rsub|p>>
    depending only on the fixed occupancy allowance <math|p<rsub|j>>. For any
    fixed integer <math|q\<geqslant\>0>, the exponential series gives
    <math|e<rsup|t>\<geqslant\>t<rsup|q+1>/<around|(|q+1|)>>!, and so
    <math|C*t<rsup|q>*e<rsup|-t>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|q+1|)>!/t\<to\>0>.
    Taking <math|q=16> proves the contact reserve. Consequently, for large
    <math|x>,

    <\equation*>
      0\<less\>H\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|4>,<space|2em><math-up|smallK><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|6>,<space|2em><around|(|1+p<rsub|j><math-up|siteFactor><around|(|B<around|(|x|)>|)>|)>*<around|(|2*B<around|(|x|)>+1|)><rsup|4>*H\<leqslant\>1.
    </equation*>

    The stain is <math|<math-up|smallK><around|(|x|)><rsup|2>>. Squaring the
    small-<math|K> bound gives <math|<math-up|stain><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|12>\<leqslant\>H>
    because <math|0\<less\>H\<leqslant\>1>. Also
    <math|s<around|(|x|)>=H/4\<leqslant\>H>. Therefore

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|stain><around|(|x|)>+p<rsub|j><math-up|siteFactor><around|(|B<around|(|x|)>|)>*s<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+p<rsub|j><math-up|siteFactor><around|(|B<around|(|x|)>|)>|)>*H,
    </equation*>

    which proves the product bound by the third reserve estimate. Since
    <math|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>\<geqslant\>2> once
    <math|B\<geqslant\>8>, the exponent in that display is at most
    <math|1/2>. It follows from <math|1/2\<less\>log 2> that its exponential
    minus one is at most <math|e<rsup|1/2>-1\<less\>1>.

    For the remaining five estimates, fix <math|d\<leqslant\>i> and put
    <math|m=deg F<rsub|d>+4>, so <math|m\<geqslant\>1>, and
    <math|D=L<rsub|j><rsup|d>*C<rsub|j>>. All constants are fixed, while
    <math|x\<to\>\<infty\>> and <math|y=x+b<rsub|j>\<to\>\<infty\>>.
    Eventually

    <\equation*>
      x\<geqslant\>max <around|{|1,C<rsub|i>,4*e<rsup|E<rsub|i>+2>*A<rsub|d>|}>,<space|1em>y\<geqslant\>max
      <around|{|1,2*a<rsub|d>*<around|(|r<rsub|j>+2*C<rsub|j>|)>,D*2<rsup|m>|}>,<space|1em>y\<leqslant\>2*x.
    </equation*>

    Now <math|f<rsub|d><around|(|x|)>=x<rsup|-m>>, so
    <math|C<rsub|i>*x<rsup|-m>\<leqslant\>1> and

    <\equation*>
      2*a<rsub|d>*<around|(|r<rsub|j>+2*C<rsub|j>|)>*y<rsup|-m>\<leqslant\>1.
    </equation*>

    For the local-section term, put <math|m=m<rsub|d>+4=deg F<rsub|d>+4>;
    then

    <\equation*>
      e<rsup|E<rsub|i>+2>*A<rsub|d>*x<rsup|deg
      F<rsub|d>>*f<rsub|d><around|(|x|)>=<frac|e<rsup|E<rsub|i>+2>*A<rsub|d>|x<rsup|4>>\<leqslant\><frac|1|4*<sqrt|x>>=s<around|(|x|)>,
    </equation*>

    using <math|4*e<rsup|E<rsub|i>+2>*A<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>x\<leqslant\>x<rsup|7/2>>
    for <math|x\<geqslant\>1> (the weaker power inequality suffices). Also
    <math|y<rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>1>.

    Finally, <math|D*2<rsup|m>\<leqslant\>y> and <math|y\<leqslant\>2*x>
    imply

    <\equation*>
      D*y<rsup|m>\<leqslant\>y*x<rsup|m>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed <math|y<rsup|m>\<leqslant\><around|(|2*x|)><rsup|m>> and
    <math|D*2<rsup|m>\<leqslant\>y>. Dividing by the positive quantity
    <math|y<rsup|m+1>*x<rsup|m>> yields

    <\equation*>
      y<rsup|-1>*D*x<rsup|-m>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|-m>,
    </equation*>

    which is the last inequality. There are finitely many
    <math|d\<leqslant\>i>, so the finitely many thresholds just chosen have a
    common maximum. This proves the lemma.
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|One monotone delay for all selected
    transitions><label|thm:ex-selected-geyser-steam>Fix the selected
    schedule, profile, gauge, stage block sides, and an initial delay
    sequence <math|z<rsub|j>>. There is a coordinatewise larger delay
    sequence <math|v<rsub|j>>, nondecreasing in <math|j>, such that for every
    later delay sequence <math|u<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>v<rsub|j>>, every
    <math|j,i,x> with <math|<math-up|Step><around|(|j,i|)>> and
    <math|u<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>x> satisfies
    <eqref|eq:ex-geyser-first>\U<eqref|eq:ex-geyser-creek4> for the actual
    profile, as well as the frame-fit inequality
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|d>\<leqslant\>B<around|(|x|)>> for every
    <math|d\<leqslant\>i>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    For a fixed pair <math|<around|(|j,i|)>> with
    <math|<math-up|Step><around|(|j,i|)>>, apply
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-geyser-radial> to its finite range
    <math|d\<leqslant\>i>. It provides a threshold <math|t<rsub|j,i>> above
    which all its conclusions hold. A fixed <math|j> has only two possible
    stages, <math|i=j> and (when <math|j\<gtr\>0>) <math|i=j-1>, because
    <math|<math-up|Step><around|(|j,i|)>> means
    <math|i\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>i+1>. Set <math|t<rsub|j>> to the maximum
    of the available <math|t<rsub|j,i>>, and enlarge it to include
    <math|z<rsub|j>>. Define

    <\equation*>
      v<rsub|j>=max<rsub|0\<leqslant\>\<ell\>\<leqslant\>j> t<rsub|\<ell\>>.
    </equation*>

    Then <math|v<rsub|j>> is nondecreasing and
    <math|z<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>v<rsub|j>>. Whenever
    <math|v<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>x>, we have <math|t<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>x>, so
    the required estimates hold for either possible stage. If
    <math|u\<geqslant\>v> coordinatewise and <math|u<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>x>,
    then <math|v<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>x>, giving the same conclusions. This is
    the finite-prefix maximum construction used to collect the eventual
    estimates; no uniformity over unbounded profile ranges is presumed at a
    fixed scale.
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|Use in the service construction.> The theorem gives the exact
  quantitative service inequalities used by the selected finite producer: the
  cubic exponential bound, damping and support reserve, five interpolation
  bounds, and finite stencil fit. In Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-selected-run-service>
  these are combined, as its hypothesis (ii), with the selected history,
  opening, menu, and finite-transfer inputs. This section proves the
  large-entrance estimates and their common monotone threshold; it does not
  prove the separate selected nonlinear menu response or the paid purchase
  that supplies the fixed profile data.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|From scale estimates to service
    inequalities><label|prop:ex-geyser-service-fields>Let <math|x<rsub|m>> be
    the selected hatted scale, <math|L<rsub|m>> its block side, and
    <math|B<rsub|m>=B<around|(|x<rsub|m>|)>>. Let <math|E<rsub|m>> be its
    support exponent and <math|T<rsub|m>> its finite title cost. Suppose the
    selected schedule and delay <math|u> satisfy

    <\equation*>
      x<rsub|m+1>=x<rsub|m>+b<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>,<space|2em>x<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>1,<space|2em>B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|m><rsup|2>,<space|2em>L<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>2,<space|2em>u<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|m>,
    </equation*>

    with <math|u<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>v<rsub|j>> as in
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-selected-geyser-steam>. Assume
    <math|<math-up|threshold><rsub|m>\<gtr\>0> and
    <math|<math-up|threshold><rsub|m><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|m>>. Let
    <math|d\<leqslant\>J<around|(|k|)>>, <math|k\<leqslant\>m>, and set

    <\equation*>
      \<zeta\><rsub|m>=f<rsub|d><around|(|x<rsub|m>|)>,<space|1em>\<phi\><rsub|m>=x<rsub|m+1><rsup|-1>,<space|1em>\<delta\><rsub|m>=x<rsub|m+1><rsup|-2>,<space|1em>\<psi\><rsub|m>=2*e<rsup|E<rsub|m+1>>*T<rsub|m+1>,<space|1em>\<eta\><rsub|m>=s<around|(|x<rsub|m>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Write <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|m>> for the level-<math|m> column count,
    <math|a<rsub|d>> for the finite motion orbit cost, and let
    <math|A<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>1> be the same grade-specific local-section
    constant used in the scale estimates. Then for every
    <math|m\<geqslant\>k> the scale estimates imply

    <\gather*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<Gamma\><rsub|m>*\<zeta\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>2*a<rsub|d>*<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|m>+2*\<Gamma\><rsub|m+1>|)>*\<zeta\><rsub|m+1>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>\<psi\><rsub|m>\<geqslant\>0,>>|<row|<cell|0\<less\>\<phi\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1,<space|1em>\<delta\><rsub|m>\<gtr\>0,<space|1em>\<phi\><rsub|m>*L<rsub|m><rsup|d>*\<Gamma\><rsub|m+1>*\<zeta\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|m+1>,>>|<row|<cell|e<rsup|E<rsub|m>+2>*A<rsub|d><space|0.17em><math-up|threshold><rsub|m><rsup|-deg
      F<rsub|d>><space|0.17em>\<zeta\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>\<eta\><rsub|m>,>>|<row|<cell|1\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|m>*\<phi\><rsub|m>*e<rsup|B<rsub|m>/128>,<space|1em>B<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>8,<space|1em>B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|m>*B<rsub|m><rsup|2>.>>>>
    </gather*>

    If the local-section bounds are taken from the uniform estimate
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|places><rsub|d><around|(|i,0,U|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|d>>,
    its place bound is uniform in <math|m>; its footprint cover is at most
    one once <math|\<nu\><rsub|d>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|m>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Apply Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-selected-geyser-steam> at
    <math|j=J*<around|(|m+1|)>>, <math|i=J<around|(|m|)>>, and
    <math|x=x<rsub|m>>. The selected clock identities give
    <math|<math-up|Step><around|(|j,i|)>> and identify the next scale with
    <math|y<rsub|j><around|(|x<rsub|m>|)>=x<rsub|m+1>>. The delay condition
    places <math|x<rsub|m>> beyond the collected threshold. Since
    <math|d\<leqslant\>J<around|(|k|)>\<leqslant\>J<around|(|m|)>>, its scale
    inequalities apply to grade <math|d>.

    Write <math|F<rsub|d>> for the grade frame and <math|m<rsub|d>=deg
    F<rsub|d>>. The first scale inequality is

    <\equation*>
      x<rsub|m><rsup|-<around|(|m<rsub|d>+4|)>><space|0.17em><math-up|columns><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1,
    </equation*>

    the first coefficient-budget inequality. Its second is

    <\equation*>
      2<space|0.17em><math-up|orbitCost><around|(|F<rsub|d>|)>*<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|m>+2<space|0.17em><math-up|columns><rsub|m+1>|)>*x<rsub|m+1><rsup|-<around|(|m<rsub|d>+4|)>>\<leqslant\>1,
    </equation*>

    the second coefficient-budget inequality. The title cost is nonnegative,
    so <math|\<psi\><rsub|m>\<geqslant\>0>.

    The shifted reciprocal estimate <math|x<rsub|m+1><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>1>
    gives <math|\<phi\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1>. The final scale estimate reads

    <\equation*>
      x<rsub|m+1><rsup|-1>*L<rsub|m><rsup|d><math-up|columns><rsub|m+1><space|0.17em>x<rsub|m><rsup|-<around|(|m<rsub|d>+4|)>>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|m+1><rsup|-<around|(|m<rsub|d>+4|)>>;
    </equation*>

    this is the adjacent price inequality with
    <math|\<phi\><rsub|m>=x<rsub|m+1><rsup|-1>> and the adjacent marker
    prices <math|\<zeta\><rsub|m>,\<zeta\><rsub|m+1>>.

    The local-section bound satisfies <math|A<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>1>. Its
    weighted norm cost is

    <\equation*>
      e<rsup|E<rsub|m>+2>*<space|0.17em>A<rsub|d><space|0.17em><math-up|threshold><rsub|m><rsup|-m<rsub|d>><space|0.17em>x<rsub|m><rsup|-<around|(|m<rsub|d>+4|)>>.
    </equation*>

    The threshold comparison and <math|<math-up|threshold><rsub|m><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|m>>
    bound this by

    <\equation*>
      e<rsup|E<rsub|m>+2>*<space|0.17em>A<rsub|d>*<space|0.17em>x<rsub|m><rsup|m<rsub|d>>*x<rsub|m><rsup|-<around|(|m<rsub|d>+4|)>>\<leqslant\>s<around|(|x<rsub|m>|)>=\<eta\><rsub|m>,
    </equation*>

    using the third scale inequality. This verifies the weighted norm-cost
    bound. The separate local-section estimate gives the stated uniform place
    bound; the footprint estimate follows from the fitted stencil radius and
    the one-site cover lemma.

    Positivity of <math|\<phi\><rsub|m>> and <math|\<delta\><rsub|m>> is
    immediate because <math|x<rsub|m+1>\<gtr\>0>. The exponential scale
    inequality follows from the second scale estimate:

    <\equation*>
      \<delta\><rsub|m>*\<phi\><rsub|m>*e<rsup|B<rsub|m>/128>=x<rsub|m+1><rsup|-3>*e<rsup|B<rsub|m>/128>\<geqslant\>1.
    </equation*>

    The contact inequality supplies support <math|B<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>8>.
    Finally,

    <\equation*>
      B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|m><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|m>*B<rsub|m><rsup|2>,
    </equation*>

    so the scale split holds. These inequalities supply the scale conditions
    used by the service certificate.
  </proof>

  <subsection|The service receipt on the selected public
  run><label|ex-centered-public-service>

  The finite-volume moment estimate uses one service certificate at each
  period, reference scale, and terminal volume. The public route builds these
  certificates from a single funded run and its same-history multiscale
  record. This section follows that finite producer and spells out how its
  fields are used; it does not assume that a new history can be selected for
  each volume.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Same-history service certificates><label|thm:ex-selected-run-service>
    Fix the selected run for a simple gauge group and a campaign album on its
    trail, with its one family and gauge doors. For each grade <math|d>, let
    <math|k<rsub|d>> denote its selected reference scale. Assume the funded
    record on this trail supplies (i) a single transition history with
    retention clauses at every level and a terminal endowment; (ii) the scale
    room and bank budgets, the initial positivity, exponential scale,
    radius-splitting, and support-size inequalities of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-geyser-service-fields>, and a
    nondecreasing occupancy schedule <math|\<rho\><rsub|i>>; (iii) for each
    grade <math|d>, period <math|p>, and indices
    <math|k<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>N>, a height bound
    <math|X=X*<around|(|d,k,N,p|)>\<geqslant\>1>, the coefficient and
    activity-price inequalities and adjacent weight-growth inequalities at
    levels at least <math|k>, and the terminal bank, footprint-cover, and
    local-section estimates stated below; (iv) for each grade <math|d>,
    period <math|p> and terminal volume <math|N\<geqslant\>k<rsub|d>>, a
    calibrated finite music whose coefficient families are linear (the
    coordinate, regular and forced parts of each coefficient family depend
    complex-linearly on the coefficient, and the support assigned to each
    address does not depend on the coefficient; this is the assigned support
    address, not the set where its regular or forced part is nonzero),
    adjacent door estimates, and its terminal forced-profile identity,
    realized by a response on the field footprint whose reserved mass is at
    most <math|e<rsup|E<rsub|N>+2><math-up|fullCost><rsub|d><around|(|P<rsub|N>,X|)><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>
    for terminal coefficient <math|v>; and (v) gradewise thresholds, positive
    scale prices with adjacent growth, and finite local tone tickets on those
    same doors. Then for every period <math|p>, reference level
    <math|k\<geqslant\>1>, and terminal volume <math|N>, the certificate has
    retention and terminal endowment on the restricted history; room, bank,
    all four stated scale inequalities, and monotone occupancy at levels
    <math|i\<geqslant\>k>; and, for every grade with
    <math|k<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>N>, the calibrated finite
    transfer and the height, coefficient, activity, adjacent-weight,
    terminal-bank, footprint-cover (at most one), and uniform local-section
    fields specified in the hypotheses. More explicitly, for each such grade
    and every <math|i\<geqslant\>k>, writing <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|i>> for the
    columns, <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>> for the block side,
    <math|\<zeta\><rsub|i>=\<zeta\><rsub|d><around|(|i|)>> and
    <math|a<rsub|d>> for the orbit cost, the stock witness satisfies

    <\equation*>
      \<zeta\><rsub|i>*\<Gamma\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1,<space|1em>\<psi\><rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0,<space|1em>2*a<rsub|d>*<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|i>+2*\<Gamma\><rsub|i+1>|)>*\<zeta\><rsub|i+1>\<leqslant\>1,<space|1em>\<phi\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1,<space|1em>\<phi\><rsub|i>*\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|d>*\<Gamma\><rsub|i+1>*\<zeta\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|i+1>.
    </equation*>

    At <math|N>, let <math|P<rsub|N>> be the terminal budget data,
    <math|E<rsub|N>> its support exponent, <math|\<theta\><rsub|N>> its
    positive threshold, and put <math|<math-up|fullCost><rsub|d><around|(|P<rsub|N>,X|)>=X*\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|-deg
    F<rsub|d>>>. The witness also satisfies

    <\equation*>
      X\<geqslant\>1,<space|1em>e<rsup|E<rsub|N>+2><math-up|fullCost><rsub|d><around|(|P<rsub|N>,X|)>*\<zeta\><rsub|N>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|N>,<space|1em>C<rsub|B<rsub|N>><around|(|<math-up|footprint><rsub|d>|)>\<leqslant\>1,<space|1em><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|places><rsub|d><around|(|<math-up|axis><rsub|j>,0,U|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>X
    </equation*>

    for every frame index <math|j> and terminal configuration <math|U>. The
    calibrated transfer uses the same doors and terminal profile identity as
    in the hypotheses. The lower restriction <math|k\<geqslant\>1> is
    retained from the public service constructor. All fields refer to the
    same selected history. The height witness can depend on all four
    displayed indices; its local-section bound is uniform over the finite
    frame indices and configurations at that volume.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    The selected run fixes one trail <math|T>, one family <math|C>, transfer
    stories, and gauge doors at every adjacent scale; the purchase record
    supplies the multiscale ledgers. The public route applies the global
    Geyser service producer to exactly this tuple; its per-period Pond is a
    derived, weaker interface. Its output contains a retained history, the
    tab schedule, a terminal keeper, marked portfolios, purchases, and weak
    specifications. The history's cost field contains the numerical room and
    bank estimates, the scale inequalities, monotone occupancy, gradewise
    height data, and terminal music records. All are attached to the same
    selected run.

    Fix <math|p,k,N>. The service certificate is assembled from those records
    as follows.

    First, restrict the retained history to levels <math|i\<geqslant\>k>. At
    each adjacent scale in this range, the selected spring and purchased
    tariff give a local comparison. The endpoint support exponents are at
    least <math|100>, so the finite interpolation constructor returns both
    its local gate ticket and the duty estimate. These are exactly the
    clauses needed for the retained-history field; the terminal endowment is
    the terminal clause of the same history.

    Next, use the room and bank estimates from the same clock. Restrict those
    initial positivity, exponential scale, radius-splitting, and support-size
    inequalities to indices <math|i\<geqslant\>k>; their bounds are
    unchanged. The occupancy schedule remains monotone under this
    restriction.

    Finally, when <math|k\<leqslant\>N>, for every grade <math|d> with
    reference level at most <math|k>, the height record at
    <math|<around|(|d,k,N,p|)>> supplies a constant <math|X> and the data
    needed downstream: the coefficient and activity-price bounds and adjacent
    weight-growth inequalities at every level at least <math|k>, the terminal
    bank bound, the terminal footprint-cover estimate, and the uniform bound
    on the local-section insertions. The abstract certificate allows <math|X>
    to depend on all four indices. In the selected construction, the uniform
    insertion estimate gives a stronger witness <math|X<rsub|d>> depending
    only on the grade: it bounds every frame-axis insertion at every finite
    volume, period and configuration, while the fitted stencil gives the
    required footprint cover. The terminal music record supplies the other
    part: its finite music begins with the calibrated factory entries, has
    this linearity at the grade reference, pays each adjacent transition
    through the same doors, and satisfies the terminal forced-profile
    identity. Joining these witnesses gives, for each such grade, the
    complete terminal service record. When <math|k\<gtr\>N>, the stock clause
    has no obligation. The same construction applies at every allowed
    <math|<around|(|p,k,N|)>> and introduces no new history; the other
    service fields retain their stated index dependence.

    The same-history per-label construction applies this service record to a
    finite list of observables. The affordability induction in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-construction> propagates each
    label's initial payment through the adjacent coefficient and scale-price
    bounds. It uses the prescribed transported weights and one music on the
    selected history; it does not choose a new history at each scale.

    For the moment estimate, take a finite set <math|V> of labels of one
    fixed grade <math|d>, with positive marker weights <math|w<rsub|i>>. The
    per-label construction supplies compatible marked activities; finite
    backward propagation of their terminal product then gives a served marked
    representation at the reference scale. Let <math|X<around|(|U|)>> be its
    represented marked polynomial, <math|A> the local Wilson representative,
    <math|\<sigma\>=<math-up|scalar><around|(|A|)>> its common scalar, and
    <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|k>> the vacuum cost per site. Write
    <math|<math-up|toll><rsub|w><around|(|I|)>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I>w<rsub|i>>
    and <math|<math-up|price><rsub|w><around|(|Y|)>=<big|sum><rsub|I><around|\||Y<rsub|I>|\|><math-up|toll><rsub|w><around|(|I|)>>
    for its mode toll and polynomial price. Suppose the terminal data satisfy
    the hypotheses of the selected root-test estimate: <math|k\<less\>N>, the
    local section is sound, the representation is selected, weights are
    positive, the column-weighted prices obey
    <math|w<rsub|i>*\<Gamma\><rsub|k><space|0.17em><math-up|paid><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|d><around|(|k|)>>,
    and the label sites have the stated migration, occupancy, and separation
    bounds. The strict inequality <math|k\<less\>N> identifies the propagated
    support exponent at level <math|k> with the current support exponent; the
    service certificate itself is also defined when <math|k=N>. Here
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|k>\<geqslant\>1> is the column count and each
    <math|<math-up|paid><rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>, so the same inequality also
    implies the unweighted initial payment
    <math|w<rsub|i><space|0.17em><math-up|paid><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|d><around|(|k|)>>
    used in the preceding induction. If <math|a=\<zeta\><rsub|d><around|(|k|)>=\<frak-f\><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>1>
    is the selected label-price allowance, the column-weighted bound gives
    the vault budgets <math|x=3*a> and <math|y=a> by the finite response
    estimate. The local representative is lawful, has nonnegative coupling
    and pointwise nonnegative evaluation, and its support exponent is
    nonnegative. At this selected level put
    <math|s=<wide|h|^><rsub|k>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|-1/2>>. The
    selected delayed-coupling bound gives
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>\<gtr\>1>, hence
    <math|0\<less\>s\<leqslant\>1>; the selected vacuum threshold is <math|s>
    times the exponential of a square, so
    <math|s\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsub|k>>. This choice uses the same selected
    history and is the radius in its treasury receipt. Its served
    representation has reserve <math|f=1> and integrable mode coefficients,
    as furnished by the finite transition receipt. The pointwise price
    estimate is

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|price><rsub|w><around|(|X<around|(|U|)>|)>\<leqslant\>exp
      <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<math-up|scalar><around|(|A|)>+<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|k>|\|>*<around*|(|<math-up|exCost><around|(|A|)>+\<kappa\>|)>+<around|\||V|\|>*<around|(|x+y+2|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed this is the pointwise price bound <eqref|ex-centered-steer> of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-construction>, with the site-count
    parameter in that formula equal to <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|k>|\|>>,
    <math|x=3*a>, <math|y=a> and reserve <math|f=1>, combined there with the
    lawfulness bound <math|E<around|(|U|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|scalar><around|(|A|)>+<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|k>|\|><math-up|exCost><around|(|A|)>>;
    the two extra units are charged by that reserve. The denominator lower
    bound is

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><math-up|eval><around|(|A|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\>\<geqslant\>exp
      <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<math-up|scalar><around|(|A|)>-<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|k>|\|>*<around*|(|<math-up|exCost><around|(|A|)>+\<beta\><space|0.17em><math-up|baseBudget>+4*d<rsub|G>*log
      <around|(|16/s|)>|)>|)>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|\<beta\>\<geqslant\>0> and <math|d<rsub|G>> is the real
    dimension of the ambient matrix representation space. The same vacuum
    treasury receipt bounds the per-site bill

    <\equation*>
      2<math-up|exCost><around|(|A|)>+\<kappa\>+\<beta\><space|0.17em><math-up|baseBudget>+4*d<rsub|G>*log
      <around|(|16/s|)>\<leqslant\>\<cal-T\><rsub|k>.
    </equation*>

    The selected Wilson transition identities preserve the integral of the
    unmarked density, so the denominator here equals the terminal Wilson
    partition function, denoted <math|Z<rsub|N,p>>. The common scalar
    cancels. Integrating a fixed mode coefficient, using its positive toll,
    and dividing by this denominator gives the finite local bound

    <\equation*>
      <\eqsplit>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<frac|<around*|\||<big|int>X<rsub|I>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\>|\|>|<big|int><math-up|eval><around|(|A|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\>><math-up|toll><rsub|w><around|(|I|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>exp
        <space|-0.17em><around*|{|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|k>|\|>*<around*|(|2<math-up|exCost><around|(|A|)>+\<kappa\>+\<beta\><space|0.17em><math-up|baseBudget>|\<nobracket\>>|\<nobracket\>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around*|\<nobracket\>|<around*|\<nobracket\>|<space|47mm>+4*d<rsub|G>*log
        <around|(|16/s|)>|)>+<around|\||V|\|>*<around|(|4*a+2|)>|}>.>>>>
      </eqsplit>
    </equation*>

    This follows directly from the displayed numerator and denominator:
    <math|<around|\||X<rsub|I>|\|><math-up|toll><rsub|w><around|(|I|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|price><rsub|w><around|(|X|)>>
    pointwise, so <math|<around*|\||<big|int>X<rsub|I>|\|><math-up|toll><rsub|w><around|(|I|)>\<leqslant\><big|int><math-up|price><rsub|w><around|(|X|)>>;
    the prior is normalized, and <math|x+y+2=4*a+2\<leqslant\>6>. The finite
    marked-coefficient estimate is therefore at most <math|exp
    <around|(|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|k>|\|>*\<cal-T\><rsub|k>+6<around|\||V|\|>|)>>.
    In the public root test, let <math|M> be the number of cells at root
    scale <math|m>, with one replicated label per cell and common weight
    <math|w\<gtr\>0>, and put <math|q=<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4>>,
    the four-dimensional volume ratio. The centered spacer estimate applies
    to this moment bound. The aligned side-count identity is
    <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|k>|\|>=M*q>. Thus the finite-volume
    exponent is <math|M*<around|(|q*\<cal-T\><rsub|k>+6|)>>. Taking the
    normalized <math|M>th root and dividing by <math|w> gives the centered
    root-test estimate

    <\equation*>
      exp <around|(|q*\<cal-T\><rsub|k>+6|)>/w.
    </equation*>

    This is the marked-copy spacer estimate<nbsp><eqref|ex-centered-opened-root>
    of Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-construction>. The treasury
    contribution survives with factor <math|q> in the root exponent; it is
    not canceled by the finite-volume denominator. Finite backward Wilson
    integration preserves each mode integral, as proved in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-selected-birth-receipt>.

    The service record, the same-history propagation and the finite local
    price all come from the one funded record assumed in (i)\U(v); the
    displayed computations show how its clauses enter the bound, and no
    unpriced per-volume assumption is added.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The funded record on the retained public
  family><label|sec:ex-c02-selected-attachment>

  The hypotheses of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-selected-run-service> are
  an interface for a general history. For the public family they are supplied
  by the following assembly. We use the direct family <math|C<rsub|R>>,
  reference map <math|t<rsub|R>>, transfers <math|S<rsub|R>> and doors
  <math|D<rsub|R>> of Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-stored-service-budget>.
  In particular, no identification with the level-zero default door of a
  separately chosen campaign is needed.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The selected family supplies the service
    record><label|prop:ex-c02-selected-record>Retain the public run of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-centered-service>, with its
    common funded-history and observable purchases, including the local
    analytic estimates and common delays of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:selected-retention-public>. On this one
    family <math|C<rsub|R>> and its stored doors, all five hypotheses of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-selected-run-service> hold. Its reference
    level is <math|k<rsub|d>=t<rsub|R><around|(|d|)>>, with
    <math|1\<leqslant\>t<rsub|R><around|(|d|)>> and
    <math|d+3\<leqslant\>J<around|(|t<rsub|R><around|(|d|)>|)>>. The transfer
    in its fourth hypothesis is the stored sequence of coefficient maps from
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-music>, normalized once at
    <math|t<rsub|R><around|(|d|)>>. Its terminal profile is the singleton
    hook of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-terminal-voice>.

    These conclusions hold simultaneously for all periods and terminal
    volumes in the theorem's domains. The numerical data and the height
    constant <math|X<rsub|d>> are fixed independently of those two indices.
    The stock assertion is required only when
    <math|t<rsub|R><around|(|d|)>\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>N>; when <math|k=N>
    it retains the terminal data and has no adjacent-step obligation.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The retained shooting parameter identifies every adjacent pair with one
    purchased local return. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-value-input>
    identifies its fine input, paths, clipping and coupling with those used
    in the local estimates. These are equalities of the stored data, not a
    new choice of transition. Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:selected-retention-public>
    therefore supplies retention on each <math|D<rsub|R>*<around|(|N,p,i|)>>,
    <math|i\<less\>N>, and the separate numerical endowment at <math|N>. It
    supplies the response to every admitted finite offer, including the quiet
    and singleton identities, the common coefficientwise majorants and the
    flagged price. Restricting to <math|k\<leqslant\>i\<less\>N> leaves the
    representatives and door unchanged. This is hypothesis (i), including the
    shipment and mode-slot data needed in its use for finite censuses.

    For (ii), put <math|C<rsub|i>=c<rsub|J<around|(|i|)>>=\<Gamma\><rsub|i>>
    and <math|L<rsub|i>=\<ell\><rsub|J*<around|(|i+1|)>>>.
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-stored-service-budget> gives

    <\equation*>
      64*W*<around|(|J<around|(|i|)>+1|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>\<Gamma\><rsub|i>,<space|2em>100000*W*<around|(|J*<around|(|i+1|)>+1|)><rsup|6>*<around|(|1+\<Gamma\><rsub|i+1>|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|i><rsup|1/8>.
    </equation*>

    The level-<math|i> length is purchased at the entrance stage
    <math|J*<around|(|i+1|)>>, so the fine column in the second formula has
    exactly the required index. The same formula holds at level zero. The
    common delays include those of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-selected-geyser-steam>;
    applying Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-geyser-service-fields> gives
    positivity, <math|B<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>8>, the cubic exponential reserve
    and the radius split. The occupancy <math|r<rsub|i>=N<rsub|J*<around|(|i+1|)>>>
    is nondecreasing and at least one, because the prescribed stage occupancy
    and the clock have these properties. Restriction to <math|i\<geqslant\>k>
    changes none of these bounds.

    For (iii) use the dictionary

    <\equation*>
      x<rsub|i>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>,<space|1em>\<zeta\><rsub|d,i>=x<rsub|i><rsup|-<around|(|deg
      F<rsub|d>+4|)>>,<space|1em>\<phi\><rsub|i>=x<rsub|i+1><rsup|-1>,<space|1em>\<delta\><rsub|i>=x<rsub|i+1><rsup|-2>,<space|1em>u<rsub|i>=<tfrac|1|4>x<rsub|i><rsup|-1/2>,<space|1em>\<psi\><rsub|i>=2*e<rsup|E<rsub|i+1>>*\<vartheta\><rsub|i+1>.
    </equation*>

    All entries are fixed by the same schedule; <math|E<rsub|i+1>> and
    <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|i+1>> are its fine support exponent and fine
    section bound. The coefficient, activity and adjacent-price inequalities
    are precisely those of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-geyser-service-fields>.
    It applies because <math|d\<leqslant\>J<around|(|t<rsub|R><around|(|d|)>|)>\<leqslant\>J<around|(|k|)>\<leqslant\>J<around|(|i|)>>.
    Choose <math|X<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>1> to be the uniform grade-<math|d>
    section bound used in that proposition. At <math|i=N> its bank estimate
    is

    <\equation*>
      e<rsup|E<rsub|N>+2>*X<rsub|d>*\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|-deg
      F<rsub|d>>*\<zeta\><rsub|d,N>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|N>.
    </equation*>

    The same fitted stencil gives footprint cover at most one. Thus this one
    <math|X<rsub|d>> supplies every height witness
    <math|X*<around|(|d,k,N,p|)>>; no maximum over periods or volumes is
    being taken.

    For (iv), Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-terminal-voice>
    supplies the actual top coefficient family, its linear fields, unit,
    index isomorphism and singleton hook.
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-reprise> constructs the lower
    coefficient family and its two-cost comparison on each retained door. The
    run retains the finite backward construction of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-music> and its witness
    identifying the coefficient maps with its stored transfer, for every
    admissible <math|<around|(|d,N,p|)>>. This is the transfer-construction
    clause of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-original-history-attachment>:
    the history and doors are fixed first, then the transfers and their
    witnesses are stored together. We use that retained witness, rather than
    producing an arbitrary second transfer sequence. Let <math|x<rsub|t>> be
    its readout at <math|t=t<rsub|R><around|(|d|)>>. Precompose every play
    with the same linear inverse <math|x<rsub|t><rsup|-1>>. Then the readout
    at <math|t> is the identity and all adjacent density and profile
    identities persist by substitution. This describes the normalization used
    when the retained transfer <math|S<rsub|R>> was defined in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-freight-backward>; it is not a replacement of
    <math|S<rsub|R>> by another valid transfer. It preserves linearity and
    the coefficient-independent support dictionary, the cone bounds and the
    physical unit. One does not normalize again at an intervening level or
    after choosing a label. The top hook is obtained from the top coefficient
    family under this same substitution. Its response also has the
    reserved-mass bound required for terminal birth. Indeed, if <math|v> is
    the terminal coordinate, the response is the singleton supplement
    <math|f<rsub|v>> on the footprint <math|Y> of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:terminal-marked-construction>. It is
    local, gauge invariant, measurable and zero when <math|Y> is healthy,
    with

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||f<rsub|v>|\|>\<leqslant\>X<rsub|d>*\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|-deg
      F<rsub|d>><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    Reserved mass charges a support by <math|exp
    <around|(|<around|(|E<rsub|N>+2|)>*C<rsub|B<rsub|N>><around|(|Y|)>|)>>
    times this majorant (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-splitting>).
    Since <math|C<rsub|B<rsub|N>><around|(|Y|)>\<leqslant\>1>, its cost is at
    most <math|e<rsup|E<rsub|N>+2><math-up|fullCost><rsub|d><around|(|P<rsub|N>,X<rsub|d>|)><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>.
    The exact hook identity is the profile calculation for this same
    supplement. The adjacent profile-difference identity alone would supply
    neither the terminal hook nor this response witness.

    Finally, (v) uses the positive prices above and the finite local Coupons
    of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-zero-coordinate-coupon>. Its
    regular and forced insertion hypothesis is supplied by
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:three-port-history> on the retained
    return, with the full ordered-derivative majorants of its remainder
    convention. Its soundness is part of the retention theorem and its merger
    is Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-coupon-merger>. The grade-zero
    floor supplies <math|<around|(|1+2*\<Gamma\><rsub|i>|)>*L<rsub|i><rsup|-2/5>\<leqslant\><math-up|barunit><around|(|L<rsub|i>|)>>;
    the insertion estimates themselves are bought at grade <math|d>. All
    these purchases precede selection of the history and of the finite wing.
    This verifies (v), with no additional per-period or per-volume
    hypothesis. The stronger positive height and height-jump fields, when
    required, are the positive <math|x<rsub|i>> powers and the common delay
    inequality <eqref|eq:ex-marked-height-jump> of the same purchase.
  </proof>

  <\corollary>
    <dueto|One width for the selected centered
    censuses><label|cor:ex-c02-uniform-census>Under the hypotheses of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-c02-selected-record>, fix a grade
    <math|d>, a label-norm radius <math|R\<gtr\>0>, and a root level
    <math|m>. Choose its opened level <math|k\<geqslant\>max
    <around|{|m,t<rsub|R><around|(|d|)>|}>> as in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-site-geometry>. For every
    <math|N\<gtr\>k> and every period, the aligned root copies have the
    served birth representation over the history's own unmarked
    representative at level <math|k>. One positive width, independent of
    <math|N>, period, site and label in the ball, pays all their labels. For
    every allowed census mode <math|I> its normalized moment satisfies
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-selected-birth-moment> with this width,
    local prices <math|x=3*\<zeta\><rsub|d,k>>, <math|y=\<zeta\><rsub|d,k>>
    and reserve one. After the common stencil-interiority tail, this includes
    the centered root test at every site.
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-centered-opened-copy> gives the aligned
    migrating sites with occupancy one and separation at least
    <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|2>> at every <math|i\<geqslant\>k>. Since
    <math|r<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1>, these satisfy the purchased occupancy
    bounds, uniformly in the period. The support-price comparison in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-construction> applies to every
    nontrivial census mode. No restriction on the total number of root cells
    is introduced: the hypotheses are rooted occupancy and separation bounds.

    Let <math|g<rsub|d,R>\<geqslant\>1> bound the coordinate norm on the
    finite-dimensional grade ball, and put
    <math|D<rsub|k>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<less\>k>\<Gamma\><rsub|i+1>>. The
    normalized transfer and its forward cone bound give
    <math|q<rsub|k><around|(|v|)>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|d,R>*D<rsub|k>>. Thus the
    common width

    <\equation*>
      w<rsub|k>=<frac|a<rsub|k><rsup|d>*\<zeta\><rsub|d,k>|\<Gamma\><rsub|k>*g<rsub|d,R>*D<rsub|k>>
    </equation*>

    satisfies <math|w<rsub|k>*\<Gamma\><rsub|k>*a<rsub|k><rsup|-d>*q<rsub|k><around|(|v|)>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|d,k>\<leqslant\>1>.
    The last inequality follows from <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|k>*\<zeta\><rsub|d,k>\<leqslant\>1>
    and <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|k>\<geqslant\>1>. March the weight by
    <math|w<rsub|i+1>=\<phi\><rsub|i>*w<rsub|i>>. The adjacent cone and price
    inequality then propagate the weaker payment
    <math|w<rsub|i>*a<rsub|i><rsup|-d>*q<rsub|i><around|(|v|)>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|d,i>>
    at every later level, exactly as in Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-construction>.

    We now instantiate the finite downward construction in that section. At
    <math|N> the marked polynomial is the finite exponential of the selected
    terminal insertions multiplied by the bare Wilson density. Its mode
    coefficients and its singleton profiles are those in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-selected-birth-receipt>. The top response
    from Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-c02-selected-record> can be
    moved to the label site and multiplied by its physical grade factor
    <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>>. Its reserved cost is at most

    <\equation*>
      a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>*e<rsup|E<rsub|N>+2><math-up|fullCost><rsub|d><around|(|P<rsub|N>,X<rsub|d>|)><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>\<tau\><rsub|N><around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    Multiplying by <math|w<rsub|N>>, using core contraction and the
    propagated payment <math|w<rsub|N>*a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>*q<rsub|N><around|(|v|)>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|d,N>>,
    bounds this cost by <math|e<rsup|E<rsub|N>+2><math-up|fullCost><rsub|d><around|(|P<rsub|N>,X<rsub|d>|)>*\<zeta\><rsub|d,N>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|N>>.
    The response's hook is the moved physical forced profile, by linearity
    and motion covariance of the same singleton construction. The separate
    terminal endowment then starts servedness with pitch <math|E<rsub|N>-3>.
    At each step the same record supplies the two-cost comparison for every
    label and the finite-menu response on the stored door. The marched
    payments give the two one-step smallness bounds, and opened-copy geometry
    supplies the support-price comparison.
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-transfer> therefore advances
    servedness to the coarse representative of that very transition. The
    pitch is <math|E<rsub|i>> for <math|i\<less\>N>; the input inequality
    <math|E<rsub|i+1>-4\<leqslant\>\<pi\><rsub|i+1>> holds also at the first
    step from the terminal pitch. Finite induction gives a served
    representation of the backward birth over <math|A<rsub|k>=R<rsub|N,p,k>>,
    with its exact singleton identities. Bounded measurable transition
    kernels preserve integrability at every step.

    The local response estimate in Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-construction>
    now applies with the column-weighted payment at <math|k>. It gives
    <math|x=3*\<zeta\><rsub|d,k>>, <math|y=\<zeta\><rsub|d,k>> and reserve
    one. Its numerator uses the scalar of <math|A<rsub|k>>. The denominator
    is the integral of the same <math|A<rsub|k>>, which equals
    <math|Z<rsub|N,p>> by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-selected-birth-denominator>.
    The treasury comparison at the radius
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|-1/2>> in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-selected-birth-receipt> bounds the sum of
    numerator and denominator costs, so the scalar cancels and the asserted
    normalized mode bound follows.

    The width and every numerical constant used above depend only on
    <math|d,R>, the opened level <math|k> and the fixed schedule. The
    interiority argument in Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-construction>
    uses only the fixed finite stencil and <math|a<rsub|N>\<to\>0>;
    intersecting its tail with <math|N\<gtr\>k> gives one volume threshold
    independent of the period, site and label.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Convergence of correlations along the full mesh
  sequence><label|sec:fullmesh-correlation-cauchy>

  We now pass from convergence of the local data at each fixed level to
  convergence of the finite-volume correlations themselves. The two limits
  are different: a terminal correlation contains interactions involving
  several marked insertions, whereas the fixed-level convergence theorem
  controls the single-insertion coefficients and their forced profiles. The
  missing estimate makes the effect of the former interactions small at a
  sufficiently fine intermediate level, uniformly in the terminal cutoff.

  Fix a period <math|p>, a finite set <math|V> of marked insertions, their
  grades <math|d<rsub|i>>, coefficient vectors <math|z<rsub|i>>, and one of
  the modes <math|S> of their finite marker algebra. Throughout the argument
  the history, the local fields, and the transfer maps belong to the one
  selected family. At cutoff <math|N>, let <math|R<rsub|N,m>> be its
  representation at level <math|m\<leqslant\>N>. Its product-Haar prior is
  denoted by <math|\<mu\><rsub|m,p>>, and the positive terminal Wilson
  partition function by <math|Z<rsub|N,p>>. The transferred polynomial
  density <math|X<rsub|N,m>> satisfies the exact identities

  <\equation*>
    X<rsub|N,m>=\<cal-T\><rsub|N,m>*X<rsub|N,m+1>,<space|2em><big|int>X<rsub|N,m>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|m,p>=<big|int>X<rsub|N,m+1>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|m+1,p>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|N,m>> is the actual gauge-covariant transfer at
  the coupling of <math|R<rsub|N,m+1>>; it is applied coefficient by
  coefficient. The integral of the coefficient of mode <math|S>, divided by
  <math|Z<rsub|N,p>>, is the correlation under consideration. In particular
  all normalizations in this section use the same partition function.

  <subsubsection|The fixed-level data and their uniform bounds>

  At level <math|m> write <math|\<Phi\><rsub|N,m,i><around|(|U|)>> for the
  single-insertion term in the exponent and
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|N,m,i>*<around|(|Q,U|)>> for its forced profile, where
  <math|Q> records a support together with its exact set of flagged sites.
  Both include the field's scaling factor, its translated origin, and its
  prescribed motion. These are the actual transferred fields of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-history-limits>.

  For every fixed <math|m> above the entry levels of the finitely many
  grades, the fixed-level comparison of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:fixed-rung-convergence>,
  which retains its adjacent-scale stability input, supplies uniform Cauchy
  convergence in <math|U> of each <math|\<Phi\><rsub|N,m,i>> and each
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|N,m,i>*<around|(|Q,\<cdot\>|)>>, and eventual uniform
  boundedness. This assertion uses both parts of that comparison. The
  finite-dimensional principal coefficient vectors are Cauchy. The captured
  remainder vectors are Cauchy in the local norm which controls their
  evaluation uniformly in <math|U>. Evaluation, translation, and the fixed
  motion preserve these bounds. The local masks are the same at this fixed
  level for every sufficiently large <math|N>, so multiplying by a mask
  preserves uniform Cauchy convergence even if the mask is discontinuous. The
  selected paths agree with the stationary paths of the common horizon; their
  larger-width convergence supplies the path part of the vacuum comparison.
  The fixed-level activity comparison supplies its remaining part.

  Here and below finite initial segments cause no difficulty. A Cauchy
  sequence of coefficient vectors is bounded, including its finite initial
  segment. Since the coefficient price is a continuous seminorm times the
  fixed scaling factor, there is a number <math|A<rsub|i>> such that the
  actual paid coefficient of insertion <math|i> at a fixed root <math|r> is
  at most <math|A<rsub|i>> for every cutoff. Choose

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:fullmesh-root-weights>w<rsub|i>=<frac|\<zeta\><rsub|d<rsub|i>><around|(|r|)>|<around|\||A<rsub|i>|\|>+1>\<gtr\>0,
  </equation>

  where <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d><around|(|r|)>\<gtr\>0> is the available
  single-insertion allowance. Then <math|w<rsub|i>> times that paid
  coefficient is at most <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d<rsub|i>><around|(|r|)>>. The
  root <math|r> is chosen after the finite list of grades, so these
  allowances are positive there.

  For later use, at any fixed level <math|m> and for any fixed positive
  weights <math|v<rsub|i>>, there exist <math|x<rsub|m>,y<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>0>,
  independent of all sufficiently large <math|N>, such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:fullmesh-vault>v<rsub|i><around|\||\<Phi\><rsub|N,m,i><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|m>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|Q>v<rsub|i>*<around|\||\<omega\><rsub|N,m,i>*<around|(|Q,U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>y<rsub|m>*<space|1em><around|(|i\<in\>V|)>.
  </equation>

  Indeed, take eventual bounds <math|a<rsub|i>> and <math|b<rsub|i,Q>> for
  these functions and put <math|x<rsub|m>=<big|sum><rsub|i>v<rsub|i><around|\||a<rsub|i>|\|>>
  and <math|y<rsub|m>=<big|sum><rsub|i,Q>v<rsub|i><around|\||b<rsub|i,Q>|\|>>.
  There are only finitely many supports and flag inventories on the fixed
  torus. Taking the maximum of the finitely many entry thresholds proves both
  inequalities simultaneously. Measurability follows from the continuous
  local functions, measurable masks and absolutely summable forced series in
  their construction.

  <subsubsection|Separating the higher marker terms>

  Write <math|<math-ss|M><around|(|\<Phi\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i>\<Phi\><rsub|i>*e<rsub|<around|{|i|}>>>
  for the single-marker polynomial. With the level and cutoff suppressed, the
  transferred density has the exact form

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:fullmesh-restore>

    <\eqsplit>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-R\>*<around|(|H,K|)><around|(|U|)>=>|<cell|e<rsup|R.<math-up|exponent><around|(|U|)>><math-up|Pass><space|-0.17em><around*|(|<math-ss|M><around|(|\<Phi\><around|(|U|)>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|P>H<rsub|P><around|(|U|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<times\>\<cal-F\>*<space|-0.17em><around*|(|<wide|R|\<bar\>><rsub|Q><around|(|U|)>+<math-ss|M><around|(|\<omega\>*<around|(|Q,U|)>|)>+K<rsub|Q><around|(|U|)>;<math-up|flags><around|(|U|)>|)>.>>>>
    </eqsplit>
  </equation>

  The finite nilpotent exponential <math|<math-up|Pass>> was defined in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-construction>. In this formula
  <math|<wide|R|\<bar\>><rsub|Q>> is the vacuum forced profile. For an
  inventory <math|F>, <math|\<cal-F\>*<around|(|A;F|)>> is the sum of
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|Q\<in\>\<cal-Q\>>A<rsub|Q>> over families
  <math|\<cal-Q\>> with disjoint supports whose flag inventories have union
  <math|F>. This is a finite sum on the fixed torus. Every nonzero mode in
  <math|H<rsub|P>> or <math|K<rsub|Q>> contains at least two markers. Its
  marker origins lie in its support. The functions <math|H<rsub|P>> vanish
  off the healthy set of <math|P>; the functions <math|K<rsub|Q>> vanish
  unless the flag inventory is exactly that specified by <math|Q>. Both are
  local, gauge-invariant and measurable.

  For positive marker weights <math|v<rsub|i>>, use the weighted coefficient
  price of Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-construction>. The higher
  terms have nonnegative majorants <math|a<rsub|P>> and <math|b<rsub|Q>>
  bounding this price uniformly in the field. Their rooted support budgets,
  with weight <math|e<rsup|e<rsub|m><math-up|cov><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|P|)>>>,
  are at most one, for both <math|H> and <math|K>. Here <math|e<rsub|m>> is
  the level's support exponent, <math|B<rsub|m>> its support scale and
  <math|<math-up|cov><rsub|B<rsub|m>>> the cover number already used in those
  budgets.

  We explain why these statements concern the actual density. The centered
  terminal construction gives <eqref|eq:fullmesh-restore> at level <math|N>
  with exponent <math|e<rsub|N>-3>. Its price uses

  <\equation*>
    c<rsub|N>=\<kappa\><rsub|N>+r<rsub|N><math-up|siteFactor><around|(|B<rsub|N>|)>*u<rsub|N>,<space|2em><around|(|2*B<rsub|N>+1|)><rsup|4>*c<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>3,<space|2em>e<rsup|c<rsub|N>>-1\<leqslant\>1,
  </equation*>

  where <math|r<rsub|N>> is the allowed number of origins per site,
  <math|\<kappa\><rsub|N>> the vacuum forced allowance and <math|u<rsub|N>>
  the terminal insertion cost. These are the terminal estimates in the
  centered birth construction. At a step <math|m+1\<to\>m>, the purchased
  finite-menu estimate accepts support exponent <math|e<rsub|m+1>-4> and
  produces the full exponent <math|e<rsub|m>>. The incoming exponent is
  sufficient, including at <math|m+1=N>, because
  <math|e<rsub|N>-4\<leqslant\>e<rsub|N>-3>. The one-insertion transfer
  identities identify its single-marker coefficients with the actual
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|N,m,i>> and <math|\<omega\><rsub|N,m,i>>; they are not
  replaced by estimates. The local transfer ledger and the occupancy budget
  then give precisely the higher-term majorants above. Integrability
  propagates through the same transfer. Backward induction proves the
  assertion at every <math|m\<less\>N>.

  The hypotheses for this induction are the funded finite-menu service of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:finite-menu-history-handoff> and the
  centered service of Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-public-service>.
  Their single-insertion component is obtained as follows. At the root use
  <eqref|eq:fullmesh-root-weights>, and propagate weights by
  <math|v<rsub|i>*<around|(|m+1|)>=\<varphi\><rsub|m>*v<rsub|i><around|(|m|)>>.
  The centered scale estimates give

  <\equation*>
    0\<less\>\<varphi\><rsub|m>,<space|1em>0\<less\>\<delta\><rsub|m>,<space|1em>1\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|m>*\<varphi\><rsub|m>*e<rsup|B<rsub|m>/128>,<space|1em>B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|m>*B<rsub|m><rsup|2>,<space|1em>B<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>8,<space|1em>e<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>4.
  </equation*>

  The actual single-insertion transfer estimate and its coefficient price
  recursion propagate the root inequality to every later level. The terminal
  local section has cover number at most one and its purchased coefficient
  bound. The coefficient generators in that estimate therefore give the
  terminal insertion, the exact single-insertion recursion, and its local
  finite transfer ledgers. Summing their forced coefficients by support and
  exact flag inventory returns the original <math|\<omega\><rsub|N,m,i>>:
  this is the defining profile identity, not a change of the fields. The
  remaining applicability conditions are the funded common service and the
  convergence comparisons just described; their simultaneous production is
  required in the common-family assembly.

  <subsubsection|Removing the higher terms at a fine intermediate level>

  Suppose distinct origins at level <math|j> are separated by at least
  <math|B<rsub|j><rsup|2>> in the lattice metric. A support of a nonzero
  higher term contains at least two such origins. The cover estimate of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-construction> gives

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>64<math-up|cov><rsub|B<rsub|j>><around|(|P|)>.
  </equation*>

  Put <math|S<rsub|j>=e<rsup|B<rsub|j>/64>>. For
  <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>S<rsub|j>>, replace <math|H,K> by
  <math|z*H,z*K>. The support and flag conditions are unchanged. The price
  majorant of a nonzero term is multiplied by <math|<around|\||z|\|>>, and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:fullmesh-inflation><around|\||z|\|>*e<rsup|<around|(|e<rsub|j>-2|)><math-up|cov><rsub|B<rsub|j>><around|(|P|)>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<math-up|cov><rsub|B<rsub|j>><around|(|P|)>>*e<rsup|<around|(|e<rsub|j>-2|)><math-up|cov><rsub|B<rsub|j>><around|(|P|)>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|e<rsub|j><math-up|cov><rsub|B<rsub|j>><around|(|P|)>>.
  </equation>

  Zero terms retain the zero majorant. Thus the inflated density has the same
  cost one at exponent <math|e<rsub|j>-2>. Its single-insertion terms are
  unchanged. Transport it from <math|j> down to a fixed root
  <math|r\<less\>j>. The first transfer accepts it since
  <math|e<rsub|j>-4\<leqslant\>e<rsub|j>-2>; subsequent steps again have the
  full exponent. The preceding induction therefore applies, with the same
  root single-insertion terms and weights.

  For clarity, separation also supplies the weight reservation needed in this
  transport. If a mode contains <math|q\<geqslant\>2> distinct origins, the
  stronger cover estimate is <math|B<rsub|m>*q\<leqslant\>64<math-up|cov><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|P|)>>.
  Changing each marker weight by at most <math|e<rsup|B<rsub|m>/128>>
  multiplies its toll by at most <math|e<rsup|<math-up|cov><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|P|)>/2>>.
  This pays the half-unit reservation in the finite-menu transfer. The
  identities between parent origins preserve occupancy, and
  <math|B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|m>*B<rsub|m><rsup|2>> preserves
  separation under the exact lattice embeddings. These observations verify
  the geometric conditions at every step of the transport.

  Let <math|X<rsub|N,j>=\<cal-R\>*<around|(|H,K|)>> be the actual density at
  <math|j\<less\>N>, and set

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|N><around|(|z|)>=<frac|1|Z<rsub|N,p>>*<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-R\>*<around|(|z*H,z*K|)><around|(|U|)>|)><rsub|S>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|j,p><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  This is holomorphic in <math|z>. Indeed, the marker algebra is nilpotent
  and the inventory sum finite, so the expression is a polynomial in <math|z>
  with measurable coefficients. The price bounds give integrable coefficient
  majorants on every smaller disk. At the root, let
  <math|x<rsub|r>,y<rsub|r>> be the eventual bounds <eqref|eq:fullmesh-vault>
  with weights <math|w<rsub|i>>. The normalized density estimate proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-construction> gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:fullmesh-root-bound><around|\||F<rsub|N><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|r>\<assign\><frac|exp
    <around|{|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|r,p>|\|>*\<tau\><rsub|r>+<around|\||V|\|>*<around|(|x<rsub|r>+y<rsub|r>+2|)>|}>|<math-up|toll><rsub|w><around|(|S|)>>*<space|2em><around|(|<around|\||z|\|>\<less\>S<rsub|j>|)>.
  </equation>

  To apply it, transfer the inflated density to <math|r>. Its integral and
  its vacuum normalization remain exactly those above. The support budgets at
  <math|r> are one, and its single-insertion data have the root bounds
  <math|x<rsub|r>,y<rsub|r>>. This proves <eqref|eq:fullmesh-root-bound> with
  a constant independent of <math|j> and of all sufficiently large <math|N>.
  The bounds at <math|j> needed for integrability may depend on <math|j>;
  they do not enter <math|C<rsub|r>>.

  If <math|S<rsub|j>\<gtr\>2>, Cauchy's estimate bounds
  <math|<around|\||F<rsub|N><rprime|'><around|(|z|)>|\|>> by
  <math|2*C<rsub|r>/S<rsub|j>> for <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>1>: the
  closed disk of radius <math|S<rsub|j>/2> about such a point lies in
  <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<less\>S<rsub|j>>. Integrating the derivative on
  <math|<around|[|0,1|]>> therefore gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:fullmesh-omission><around*|\||<frac|1|Z<rsub|N,p>>*<big|int><around|(|X<rsub|N,j>|)><rsub|S>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|j,p>-<frac|1|Z<rsub|N,p>>*<big|int><around|(|\<cal-R\>*<around|(|0,0|)>|)><rsub|S>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|j,p>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*C<rsub|r>*e<rsup|-B<rsub|j>/64>.
  </equation>

  The first term is the original terminal correlation. Only the terms with at
  least two markers were erased in the second term.

  <subsubsection|Cauchy convergence of the erased expression>

  Fix <math|j>. Remove the scalar normalization from the representation and
  put

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|N,j><around|(|U|)>=e<rsup|-R<rsub|N,j>.<math-up|scalar>>*\<cal-R\>*<around|(|0,0|)><around|(|U|)>,<space|2em>\<ell\><rsub|N,j>=e<rsup|-R<rsub|N,j>.<math-up|scalar>>*Z<rsub|N,p>.
  </equation*>

  The second term in <eqref|eq:fullmesh-omission> is exactly
  <math|<big|int><around|(|D<rsub|N,j>|)><rsub|S>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|j,p>/\<ell\><rsub|N,j>>;
  the exponential scalar cancels. The fixed-level vacuum estimate gives a
  number <math|a<rsub|j,p>\<gtr\>0> with <math|\<ell\><rsub|N,j>\<geqslant\>a<rsub|j,p>>
  eventually, and the fixed-level convergence theorem gives Cauchy
  convergence of <math|\<ell\><rsub|N,j>>.

  The functions <math|<around|(|D<rsub|N,j>|)><rsub|S>> are uniformly Cauchy
  and eventually uniformly bounded. To see this directly from
  <eqref|eq:fullmesh-restore>, combine the fixed-level vacuum, activity,
  single-insertion and forced-profile comparisons. There are finitely many
  coordinates on the fixed torus. Finite products, sums and the nilpotent
  exponential are continuous functions of those coordinates, uniformly
  continuous on the compact closures of their bounded ranges. Fixed masks can
  be kept in every term, as noted earlier. These operations consequently
  preserve uniform Cauchy convergence. As the prior is a probability measure,

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||<big|int><around|(|D<rsub|N,j>|)><rsub|S>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|j,p>-<big|int><around|(|D<rsub|M,j>|)><rsub|S>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|j,p>|\|>\<leqslant\>sup<rsub|U><around|\||<around|(|D<rsub|N,j><around|(|U|)>-D<rsub|M,j><around|(|U|)>|)><rsub|S>|\|>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  The numerator is Cauchy and the denominator has a positive limit, so their
  ratio is Cauchy as well.

  Now <math|B<rsub|j>\<to\>\<infty\>>. Given <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>,
  first choose a fixed <math|j\<gtr\>r> with <math|S<rsub|j>\<gtr\>2> and
  <math|2*C<rsub|r>*e<rsup|-B<rsub|j>/64>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>/4>. At this
  fixed <math|j> the erased expressions differ by less than
  <math|\<varepsilon\>/4> for sufficiently large cutoffs <math|N,M\<gtr\>j>.
  Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:fullmesh-omission> and the triangle inequality
  bound the difference of the original correlations by
  <math|3*\<varepsilon\>/4\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>. This proves Cauchy
  convergence along the full cutoff sequence, without choosing an additional
  subsequence.

  <subsubsection|From nested-grid points to all distinct configurations>

  It remains to supply the separated origins used above and to pass to
  general points. Call a physical point ancestral if it lies on the mesh at
  some level. Integer block lengths imply that it lies on every finer mesh,
  and its addresses obey the exact embedding identity. Ancestral
  configurations are dense: round each coordinate to the nearest mesh point
  and use <math|a<rsub|m>\<to\>0>.

  For a fixed ancestral configuration of distinct points on the period
  <math|p> torus, let <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0> be its minimum pairwise physical
  separation, measured in the periodic maximum-coordinate
  (<math|\<ell\><rsup|\<infty\>>>) distance, as in
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-fullmesh-fixedbounds-producer>. For zero or
  one insertion take any <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0> instead. Its lattice
  separation at level <math|m>, in the same periodic
  <math|\<ell\><rsup|\<infty\>>> distance between sites, is then at least
  <math|\<eta\>/a<rsub|m>>. (With the Euclidean distance one would only get
  <math|\<eta\>/<around|(|2*a<rsub|m>|)>> in four dimensions, and the
  condition <math|a<rsub|m>*B<rsub|m><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>> below would
  become <math|2*a<rsub|m>*B<rsub|m><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>>.) The chosen
  schedule satisfies <math|a<rsub|m>*B<rsub|m><rsup|2>\<to\>0>. Here is the
  quantitative reason for this limit. Its support-scale estimate eventually
  gives <math|B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|m><rsup|2>>, whereas
  the block lengths satisfy <math|L<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>3> and
  <math|a<rsub|m+1>=a<rsub|m>/L<rsub|m>>. Thus
  <math|a<rsub|m+1>*B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|(|2/3|)>*a<rsub|m>*B<rsub|m><rsup|2>>
  on a tail; iteration proves the limit. Choose a common root after all entry
  levels and after <math|a<rsub|m>*B<rsub|m><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>>
  holds on that tail. The origins are then separated by
  <math|B<rsub|m><rsup|2>> at every later level. They are distinct at the
  root, so occupancy there is one, within the purchased occupancy schedule.
  This verifies all geometric conditions for the preceding Cauchy argument.

  Finally apply the all-period finite-difference bounds of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-fullmesh-fixedbounds>. They give local
  boundedness and an eventual common modulus on
  <math|<math-up|NoHit><rsub|p>>, the open set of configurations distinct
  modulo the physical period. The rounded lattice readouts need not
  themselves be continuous; their mesh errors tend to zero. The explicit
  local bound is <math|C*<around|(|<around|\||x-y|\|>+2*d*a<rsub|N>|)>> for a
  fixed finite coordinate dimension <math|d>, so one first chooses
  <math|<around|\||x-y|\|>> small and then the mesh tail. Cauchy convergence
  on the dense ancestral set in this domain extends to locally uniform
  convergence there: cover a compact set by finitely many neighborhoods with
  a common equicontinuity modulus, choose an ancestral point in each, and use
  the triangle inequality between each point and its chosen center. The
  corresponding higher difference bounds give the smooth limiting
  correlations by the argument of Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-smooth-limits>.
  Restriction to the ordered Euclidean configuration domain gives the stated
  position-space ultraviolet limit.

  In applying this result to the selected construction, the cutoff parameter
  is <math|N> itself and every branch is the history chosen by the same
  shooting construction. The alignment identities identify its paths with the
  common horizon; the selected transfer maps and their based local sections
  identify all of the <math|\<Phi\>> and <math|\<omega\>> above. The common
  centered service supplies the root allowances and their positive tails, the
  transfer ledgers, and the scale estimates. These identifications are part
  of the common-family assembly: a service on a different history would not
  establish the result here.

  <subsection|Ordered limits and Euclidean properties><label|sec:ex-limits>

  The lattice spacing is sent to zero at fixed volume before the volume is
  sent to infinity. A subsequence of pairs
  <math|<around|(|N<rsub|k>,p<rsub|k>|)>> tending to infinity in both
  coordinates need not give either iterated limit. We record the compactness
  argument that produces one common pair of subsequences for all finite
  correlation signatures, and then explain which Euclidean properties pass to
  the resulting table. The construction is conditional on uniform
  finite-cutoff estimates stated below; their derivation from the Wilson
  trajectory is a separate input.

  Fix one Wilson family <math|\<cal-C\>>. Write <math|a<rsub|N>> for its
  mesh, <math|P<rsub|p>> for its physical linear period, and
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|N,p>> for the number of sites in that period. For an
  <math|n>-tuple of field labels <math|v>, let <math|U<rsub|N,p,v>> denote
  the finite-lattice correlation, namely the finite expectation of the chosen
  insertions. We assume it is multilinear in each label. The factor
  <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>> appears when these values are smeared by a test
  function. Let <math|<math-up|Cyc><rsub|p>> be the open set of
  configurations for which no two distinct insertions have coordinate
  differences equal to an integer multiple of the physical period
  <math|P<rsub|p>> in any coordinate. Write <math|<math-up|NoHit><rsub|p>>
  for configurations in which each distinct pair has at least one coordinate
  difference that is not an integer multiple of <math|P<rsub|p>>. This
  excludes torus-coincident pairs but permits coordinatewise ties. Let
  <math|<math-up|Gen>> be the set for which every coordinate projection
  separates distinct insertions. Let <math|<math-up|Conf>> be the set of
  configurations with no coincident points. Thus
  <math|<math-up|Gen>\<subset\><math-up|Conf>>; <math|<math-up|Gen>> also
  excludes noncoincident coordinate-projection ties, while
  <math|<math-up|Conf>> permits them. The table <math|S> below is the
  limiting Euclidean correlation table.

  Here are the two uniform estimates used for extraction. In each statement
  \Peventually\Q has its ordinary meaning along the indicated strictly
  increasing sequence.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Uniform bounds and moduli><label|def:ex-limits-estimates>Fix an
    ordered pair <math|f=<around|(|f<rsub|<math-up|mesh>>,f<rsub|<math-up|per>>|)>>
    of strictly increasing sequences.

    <\enumerate>
      <item>A local mesh modulus means that for every fixed period <math|p>,
      arity <math|n>, label tuple <math|v>, compact
      <math|K\<subset\><math-up|Cyc><rsub|p>>, and
      <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, there are <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0> and
      <math|j<rsub|0>> such that, for <math|j\<geqslant\>j<rsub|0>> and
      lattice configurations <math|x,y\<in\>K> at mesh
      <math|a<rsub|f<rsub|<math-up|mesh>><around|(|j|)>>>,

      <\equation*>
        <around|\||U<rsub|f<rsub|<math-up|mesh>><around|(|j|)>,p,v><around|(|x|)>-U<rsub|f<rsub|<math-up|mesh>><around|(|j|)>,p,v><around|(|y|)>|\|>\<less\>\<varepsilon\><space|1em><text|whenever
        ><around|\||x-y|\|>\<less\>\<delta\>.
      </equation*>

      The modulus may depend on <math|p,n,v,K,\<varepsilon\>>.

      <item>An expanding-volume modulus means that for every <math|n,v>,
      compact <math|K\<subset\><math-up|Gen>>, and
      <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, there are <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0> and
      thresholds <math|k<rsub|0>> and <math|j<rsub|0><around|(|k|)>> such
      that for <math|k\<geqslant\>k<rsub|0>>,
      <math|j\<geqslant\>j<rsub|0><around|(|k|)>>, and lattice configurations
      <math|x,y\<in\>K> at mesh <math|a<rsub|f<rsub|<math-up|mesh>><around|(|j|)>>>,

      <\equation*>
        <around|\||U<rsub|f<rsub|<math-up|mesh>><around|(|j|)>,f<rsub|<math-up|per>><around|(|k|)>,v><around|(|x|)>-U<rsub|f<rsub|<math-up|mesh>><around|(|j|)>,f<rsub|<math-up|per>><around|(|k|)>,v><around|(|y|)>|\|>\<less\>\<varepsilon\><space|1em><text|whenever
        ><around|\||x-y|\|>\<less\>\<delta\>.
      </equation*>

      The mesh threshold may depend on the fixed volume index <math|k>.

      <item>A compact norm bound means both: for every fixed <math|p,n,v> and
      compact <math|K\<subset\><math-up|NoHit><rsub|p>>, the lattice values
      <math|<around|\||U<rsub|f<rsub|<math-up|mesh>><around|(|j|)>,p,v><around|(|x|)>|\|>>
      are bounded by one constant for all <math|x\<in\>K> and all
      sufficiently large <math|j>; and for every <math|n,v> and compact
      <math|K\<subset\><math-up|Conf>> they are bounded by one constant for
      all sufficiently large pairs in the ordered sense
      <math|\<forall\>k\<geqslant\>k<rsub|0><space|0.27em>\<forall\>j\<geqslant\>j<rsub|0><around|(|k|)>>.
    </enumerate>

    In the ordered-volume clause, the outer threshold is the period threshold
    and the inner mesh threshold may depend on that period. In particular
    these assumptions do not assert a single diagonal limit or a modulus
    uniform over all periods.
  </definition>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Common ordered extraction><label|prop:ex-limits-extraction>Fix a
    countable algebraic basis for the local field-label spaces. Assume the
    local mesh modulus, expanding-volume modulus, and compact norm bound of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:ex-limits-estimates> for every finite
    tuple from this basis, for the same family <math|\<cal-C\>> and initial
    order <math|f>. Then there are strictly increasing subsequences
    <math|t,u> of the mesh and volume indices. Put
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|k>=f<rsub|<math-up|per>><around|(|u<around|(|k|)>|)>>.
    There are intermediate continuous functions <math|J<rsub|p,v>> on each
    <math|<math-up|Cyc><rsub|p>> and a table <math|S> continuous on
    <math|<math-up|Gen>>, with the following properties:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>for each fixed <math|p,n,v>, the address-cell interpolants along
      the mesh subsequence <math|f<rsub|<math-up|mesh>>\<circ\>t> converge to
      <math|J<rsub|p,v>> uniformly on every compact subset of
      <math|<math-up|Cyc><rsub|p>>;

      <item>for each <math|n,v>, <math|J<rsub|\<rho\><rsub|k>,v>> converges
      to <math|S> uniformly on every compact subset of <math|<math-up|Gen>>
      as <math|k\<to\>\<infty\>>;

      <item>for each separated test function suitable at fixed <math|p>, the
      finite-lattice smeared correlations converge first along the mesh
      subsequence to the integral against <math|J<rsub|p,v>>, and then along
      the volume subsequence to the integral against <math|S> (extended by
      zero off <math|<math-up|Gen>>, which does not change this integral).
    </enumerate>

    All signature limits use the same <math|t,u>. The mesh subsequence is
    chosen independently of the requested test support. The volume limit is
    only asserted along <math|f<rsub|<math-up|per>>\<circ\>u>, and the mesh
    limit remains an ordinary limit along the full mesh subsequence at each
    eligible fixed period.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    We give the compactness argument, including the common-subsequence step.
    For each mesh index <math|j> and point <math|x>, let
    <math|Q<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>> be the cell representative selected by the
    lattice address map, so that <math|<around|\||Q<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>-x|\|>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|n>*a<rsub|f<rsub|<math-up|mesh>><around|(|j|)>>>
    for a constant <math|c<rsub|n>> depending only on the arity. Define the
    position-space function <math|<wide|U|~><rsub|j><around|(|x|)>=U<rsub|f<rsub|<math-up|mesh>><around|(|j|)>,p,v><around|(|Q<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>|)>>.
    This function is defined even when <math|x> is not a lattice point. Let
    <math|K> be a compact subset of an open patch and choose a compact
    neighborhood <math|K<rprime|'>> still inside that patch. For
    <math|x,y\<in\>K> with <math|<around|\||x-y|\|>\<less\>\<delta\>/2>, the
    representatives <math|Q<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>,Q<rsub|j><around|(|y|)>>
    belong to <math|K<rprime|'>> for all sufficiently large <math|j>, and

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||Q<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>-Q<rsub|j><around|(|y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||x-y|\|>+2*c<rsub|n>*a<rsub|f<rsub|<math-up|mesh>><around|(|j|)>>\<less\>\<delta\>.
    </equation*>

    The lattice modulus on <math|K<rprime|'>> therefore makes
    <math|<wide|U|~><rsub|j>> asymptotically equicontinuous on <math|K>; the
    rounded functions need not themselves be continuous. The compact norm
    bound on <math|K<rprime|'>> makes their values bounded there for all
    sufficiently large <math|j>.

    The basis-signature set and periods are countable. Choose a countable
    dense subset <math|D> of each patch. The values
    <math|<wide|U|~><rsub|j><around|(|d|)>>, over all dense points and
    signatures for the fixed period, lie eventually in bounded closed disks
    in <math|\<bbb-C\>>. Countable diagonal extraction
    <cite-detail|RudinPMA|Theorem<nbsp>7.23> makes all these scalar sequences
    converge along one subsequence. We check that this gives compact-uniform
    convergence, not just convergence on <math|D>. Let <math|K> be compact in
    the patch and <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>. Choose a compact
    neighborhood <math|K<rprime|'>> inside the patch. Use the common modulus
    on <math|K<rprime|'>> to choose a radius <math|\<delta\>> for error
    <math|\<varepsilon\>/3>. Finitely many balls of radius <math|\<delta\>/3>
    centered at points of <math|D\<cap\>K<rprime|'>> cover <math|K>. At these
    finitely many centers the selected subsequence is Cauchy. For any
    <math|x\<in\>K>, choose one such center <math|d>; for all large indices
    <math|i,i<rprime|'>>, the two modulus errors
    <math|<around|\||<wide|U|~><rsub|i><around|(|x|)>-<wide|U|~><rsub|i><around|(|d|)>|\|>>
    and <math|<around|\||<wide|U|~><rsub|i<rprime|'>><around|(|x|)>-<wide|U|~><rsub|i<rprime|'>><around|(|d|)>|\|>>
    are each less than <math|\<varepsilon\>/3>, while the center values
    differ by less than <math|\<varepsilon\>/3>. The triangle inequality
    proves the subsequence is uniformly Cauchy on <math|K>. Completeness of
    <math|\<bbb-C\>> gives a uniform limit on <math|K>; limits agree on
    overlaps, and the same modulus estimate proves the resulting function is
    continuous. Exhausting the patch by compact sets gives one continuous
    limit there and uniform convergence on every compact subset. Diagonalize
    over the countable collection of signatures and a compact exhaustion of
    each patch to obtain one mesh subsequence <math|t> and all the
    intermediate functions <math|J<rsub|p,v>>.

    Now consider the sequence of mesh-limit functions
    <math|J<rsub|f<rsub|<math-up|per>><around|(|k|)>,v>> on
    <math|<math-up|Gen>>. For a compact <math|K\<subset\><math-up|Gen>>
    choose a compact neighborhood <math|K<rprime|'>\<subset\><math-up|Gen>>
    with <math|K> in its interior. For all sufficiently large <math|k>,
    <math|K<rprime|'>> lies inside the fundamental box; since
    <math|K<rprime|'>\<subset\><math-up|Gen>>, this gives
    <math|K<rprime|'>\<subset\><math-up|Cyc><rsub|f<rsub|<math-up|per>><around|(|k|)>>>.
    If <math|x,y\<in\>K> and <math|<around|\||x-y|\|>\<less\>\<delta\>/2>,
    their representatives at mesh <math|a<rsub|f<rsub|<math-up|mesh>><around|(|j|)>>>
    lie in <math|K<rprime|'>> and have mutual distance less than
    <math|\<delta\>> for all sufficiently large <math|j>. Apply the
    expanding-volume modulus at the fixed outer index <math|k>, then pass
    <math|j\<to\>\<infty\>> using the already established mesh convergence on
    <math|K<rprime|'>>. This proves asymptotic equicontinuity of the
    functions <math|J<rsub|f<rsub|<math-up|per>><around|(|k|)>,v>> on
    <math|K>. Similarly, the ordered compact norm bound and mesh convergence
    give a uniform bound for these functions on <math|K> for all sufficiently
    large <math|k>.

    Choose countable dense subsets in each generic configuration space and
    repeat the closed-disk diagonal argument, now with <math|k> as the
    sequence index. The finite-cover argument proves compact-uniform
    convergence on every compact subset of <math|<math-up|Gen>> and gives a
    continuous limit <math|S>. Diagonalizing over the countable signature
    index makes the same volume subsequence <math|u> work for all signatures.
    Use arity together with finite tuples from a countable algebraic basis as
    the countable signature index. Every arbitrary finite tuple of labels is
    a finite linear combination of such basis tuples, and finite lattice
    moments are multilinear, so finite linearity extends these limits to
    arbitrary labels without any further subsequence. This is a diagonal
    argument over the countable basis index, not over the uncountably many
    arbitrary test functions.

    It remains to pass from pointwise limits to smeared moments. Let
    <math|\<varphi\>> be a separated test, meaning its support <math|K> is
    compact and contained in <math|<math-up|Conf>>, and fix an eligible
    period <math|p>. Assume <math|p> is eligible for this test, so <math|K>
    lies strictly inside the comparison cell. Choose a compact neighborhood
    <math|K<rsub|1>> of <math|K> whose closure stays inside the comparison
    cell. For all fine meshes, every cell representative of a point in
    <math|K> lies in <math|K<rsub|1>>. The cell boundaries in this bounded
    region lie in finitely many affine hyperplanes and have Lebesgue measure
    zero. The complement of the cyclic patch is a countable union of affine
    hyperplanes and therefore has Lebesgue measure zero. At almost every
    point in <math|K>, the cell representatives tend to that point and
    eventually belong to the cyclic patch. Extend <math|J<rsub|p,v>> by zero
    off that patch. The mesh convergence gives almost-everywhere convergence
    of the cell density to <math|\<varphi\>*J<rsub|p,v>>. Since <math|K> is
    compactly contained in the fundamental cell and in <math|<math-up|Conf>>,
    it lies in the fixed-period no-hit set. Choose a compact neighborhood
    <math|K<rsub|2>> of <math|K> contained in that open set. For all
    sufficiently fine meshes the rounded samples of points in <math|K> lie in
    <math|K<rsub|2>>. The fixed-period compact norm bound on <math|K<rsub|2>>
    bounds the finite-lattice moments for all sufficiently fine meshes. If
    <math|\<varphi\><around|(|Q<rsub|N><around|(|x|)>|)>\<ne\>0>, the cell
    representative <math|Q<rsub|N><around|(|x|)>> belongs to <math|K> and
    hence to <math|K<rsub|2>>. Therefore the absolute value of the piecewise
    constant cell density is at most <math|D<around|\<\|\|\>|\<varphi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>
    on the whole fundamental box and is zero outside the union of cells
    meeting the support. The indicator of the bounded fundamental box times
    this constant is integrable. The exact cell identity writes the smeared
    sum as the integral of this density: each cell has volume
    <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>>. The strict cell margin keeps all samples
    inside the no-wrap region. At points outside the closed support <math|K>,
    the cell density is eventually zero because
    <math|Q<rsub|N><around|(|x|)>\<to\>x>; outside the fixed box it is
    identically zero. Dominated convergence proves the fixed-period smeared
    limit.

    For the volume limit, choose compact neighborhoods
    <math|K<rsub|0>\<subset\>K<rsub|1>\<subset\><math-up|Conf>> of the
    support <math|K>. For sufficiently fine meshes, every cell representative
    of a point in <math|K<rsub|0>> lies in <math|K<rsub|1>>. The ordered
    compact norm bound on <math|K<rsub|1>> gives one constant for these
    lattice values once the period index is sufficiently large and then the
    mesh threshold is met. For all sufficiently large period indices,
    <math|K<rsub|1>> lies inside the fundamental cell. At generic points
    <math|x\<in\>K>, the no-wrap equivalence identifies the cyclic patch with
    <math|<math-up|Gen>>, and the mesh evaluation lemma passes the lattice
    bound to <math|J<rsub|f<rsub|<math-up|per>><around|(|k|)>,v><around|(|x|)>>.
    The set <math|K\<setminus\><math-up|Gen>> is contained in finitely many
    coordinate hyperplanes and has Lebesgue measure zero. Therefore these
    intermediate functions are bounded by the same constant almost everywhere
    on <math|K>. Extend <math|S> by zero off <math|<math-up|Gen>>. The
    volume-point convergence holds at each
    <math|x\<in\>K\<cap\><math-up|Gen>>, hence almost everywhere on <math|K>.
    Dominated convergence gives the second integral limit for every separated
    test, including supports that meet noncoincident coordinate-projection
    ties. Here <math|<math-up|Gen>> supplies a full-measure set for the
    integral passage, while the larger set <math|<math-up|Conf>> contains the
    entire test support.
  </proof>

  The two compactness stages use distinct moduli: one at each fixed period
  and one in the ordered volume limit. The argument preserves the order of
  limits: no estimate uniform in an unbounded volume threshold is substituted
  for the permitted threshold <math|j<rsub|0><around|(|k|)>>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Passage of Euclidean identities and
    positivity><label|prop:ex-limits-euclidean>Let <math|S> be a table
    obtained with the ordered smeared limits of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-limits-extraction>. Assume in
    addition that:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>finite-lattice correlations satisfy exact permutation and lattice
      translation identities, signed coordinate symmetries, conjugation, and
      normalization at admissible grid points; labels transform accordingly;

      <item>the ordered point limits exist on the generic domain and <math|S>
      is continuous there; assume also that for each signature it extends to
      a smooth function on <math|<math-up|Conf>> agreeing with <math|S> on
      <math|<math-up|Gen>>;

      <item>for each finite list of positive-time smeared observables, the
      exact finite-lattice pairing identity expresses each reflected product
      as a finite linear combination of separated smeared correlations
      covered by the preceding extraction, with coefficients independent of
      the lattice indices; the same combination defines the limiting smeared
      pairing. The finite-lattice smeared reflection form is nonnegative for
      all sufficiently late indices in the ordered sense.
    </enumerate>

    Then the corresponding permutation, translation, signed
    coordinate-permutation, and conjugation identities hold for <math|S> on
    the noncoincident domain, and the smeared reflection form of <math|S> is
    nonnegative for every finite list of admissible positive-time test
    observables. If, in addition, <math|S> satisfies the separate cap bound
    required by the Euclidean hierarchy interface, these conclusions together
    with normalization give its Euclidean positivity and symmetry clauses.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    For permutation and conjugation, fix the signature and a generic
    configuration and approximate it by admissible lattice configurations. At
    each sufficiently fine mesh the finite-lattice change of variables gives
    the exact identity; the point limits along the common ordered filter and
    uniqueness of limits in <math|\<bbb-C\>> give the identity for <math|S>.
    A signed coordinate permutation preserves the lattice, so the same
    argument applies after transforming the grid points and the insertion
    labels. For translation by an arbitrary vector <math|z\<in\>E<rsub|4>>,
    choose lattice translations <math|z<rsub|j>> converging to <math|z> as
    the mesh tends to zero. The finite trajectory's anchoring identity gives
    equality between the two grid evaluations separated by <math|z<rsub|j>>.
    The grid points converge to the desired translated configurations, and
    continuity of the limiting correlation gives the translation identity.
    The generic set is dense in the noncoincident domain; if an identity has
    first been proved only there, continuity of both sides extends it
    throughout that domain.

    For positivity, fix <math|m> positive-time smeared observables
    <math|A<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,A<rsub|m>> and coefficients
    <math|c<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,c<rsub|m>\<in\>\<bbb-C\>>. The pairing
    assumption and the ordered convergence of each separated entry show that
    every entry in the finite reflection matrix converges to its limiting
    smeared pairing. At each sufficiently late admissible lattice pair,
    reflection positivity gives

    <\equation*>
      Q<rsub|N,p>\<assign\><big|sum><rsub|i,j=1><rsup|m><wide|c<rsub|i>|\<bar\>>c<rsub|j>*<space|0.17em><around|\<langle\>|\<theta\>*A<rsub|i>*<space|0.17em>A<rsub|j>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>\<geqslant\>0.
    </equation*>

    There are only <math|m<rsup|2>> terms. Since every matrix entry has the
    same ordered limit, finite-sum continuity gives

    <\equation*>
      Q<rsub|N,p>\<longrightarrow\><big|sum><rsub|i,j><wide|c<rsub|i>|\<bar\>>c<rsub|j>*<around|\<langle\>|\<theta\>*A<rsub|i>*A<rsub|j>|\<rangle\>><rsub|S>.
    </equation*>

    This convergence holds along the same ordered filter. The nonnegative
    real half-line is closed, so the limit is nonnegative. Since the list and
    coefficients were arbitrary, the limiting smeared reflection matrix is
    positive semidefinite. Pointwise reflection positivity for point
    insertions then follows from the separate bump-approximation argument in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-rp-bumps>; that step uses continuity away from
    coincident points and is not a consequence of generic point convergence
    alone.

    These limit arguments establish only the clauses just named. The
    extraction gives continuity on <math|<math-up|Gen>>; passing identities
    to <math|<math-up|Conf>> requires the separate smoothness hypothesis
    there. Smoothness on <math|<math-up|Conf>> includes noncoincident
    coordinate-projection ties but excludes actual coincident points. Field
    quotients and Wightman reconstruction do not follow from a subsequence
    argument; they are separate hypotheses or results in the hierarchy and
    reconstruction units.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|A unit insertion in the original limiting
    table><label|prop:ex-limits-unit>Fix the same family <math|\<cal-C\>>,
    its raw label spaces and reference depths <math|r<rsub|d>> before taking
    limits. In grade <math|d>, let <math|e<rsub|0,d>> be the scalar
    coordinate, let <math|D<rsub|N,d>> be the threshold scaling of
    coefficients, and write the insertion at site <math|o> and lattice
    configuration <math|A> as

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ex-limits-unit-insertion>\<cal-O\><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v;o;A|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-P\><rsub|d>*<around*|(|D<rsub|N,d>*X<rsub|N,p,d><rsup|-1>*v;o;A|)>.
    </equation>

    Here <math|X<rsub|N,p,d>> is the invertible terminal coefficient map, and
    <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|d>> is the continuous local section, linear in its
    coefficient argument. For the positive threshold <math|\<tau\><rsub|N>>,
    the threshold scaling multiplies a coefficient of factor degree <math|q>
    by <math|\<tau\><rsub|N><rsup|-q>>; the scalar coordinate has factor
    degree zero. Assume the following grade-zero calibration laws on this
    same family. The local section satisfies
    <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|0>*<around|(|e<rsub|0,0>;o;A|)>=1> for every site
    and configuration. For <math|N\<geqslant\>r<rsub|0>> and every period
    <math|p>, the coefficient readouts <math|L<rsub|m><rsup|N,p>> satisfy

    <\equation*>
      L<rsub|r<rsub|0>><rsup|N,p><around|(|v|)>=X<rsub|N,p,0>*v,<space|2em>L<rsub|m><rsup|N,p>*<around|(|a<rsub|N><rsup|0>*e<rsub|0,0>|)>=a<rsub|m><rsup|0>*e<rsub|0,0>*<space|1em><around|(|r<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>m\<leqslant\>N|)>.
    </equation*>

    Finite correlations are expectations of products of these insertions
    under the normalized finite-volume Wilson probability measure.

    Let <math|S> be their original, unselected limiting table. Assume the
    same ordered point-limit hypotheses as in
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-limits-euclidean>, for all signatures
    including arity zero, with one common order and table. More explicitly,
    the fixed-period mesh limits apply to the slotwise address samples in
    each cyclic patch, and the subsequent period limits give <math|S> on
    <math|<math-up|Gen>>, as in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-limits-extraction>.
    Assume the correlations of <math|S> extend continuously to
    <math|<math-up|Conf>>; the smoothness hypothesis there is more than
    sufficient. Then the fixed raw grade-zero label
    <math|\<b-1\>=e<rsub|0,0>> satisfies

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ex-limits-unit-law>S*<around|(|\<b-1\>,v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|n>;x<rsub|0>,x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>=S*<around|(|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|n>;x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>
    </equation>

    for every <math|n\<geqslant\>0>, every finite raw label tuple and every
    pairwise distinct configuration. Moreover
    <math|S<around|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=1>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    All raw label spaces and their scalar coordinates were fixed with the
    family. In particular <math|\<b-1\>=e<rsub|0,0>> has no dependence on
    <math|N> or <math|p>. At a general grade the physical unit coordinate at
    level <math|m> is <math|a<rsub|m><rsup|d>*e<rsub|0,d>>; in grade zero it
    is exactly <math|e<rsub|0,0>> at every level. Taking <math|m=r<rsub|0>>
    in the calibration law and using the terminal readout gives

    <\equation*>
      X<rsub|N,p,0>*e<rsub|0,0>=L<rsub|r<rsub|0>><rsup|N,p><around|(|e<rsub|0,0>|)>=e<rsub|0,0>,<space|2em>X<rsub|N,p,0><rsup|-1>*e<rsub|0,0>=e<rsub|0,0>*<space|1em><around|(|N\<geqslant\>r<rsub|0>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Threshold scaling also fixes this coordinate: its only nonzero component
    has factor degree zero, so its multiplier is
    <math|\<tau\><rsub|N><rsup|0>=1>. The physical prefactor in
    <eqref|eq:ex-limits-unit-insertion> is <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|0>=1>. The
    section's unit law therefore yields

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-O\><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|\<b-1\>;o;A|)>=1<space|1em><text|for
      every >N\<geqslant\>r<rsub|0>,<text| every >p,o,A.
    </equation*>

    Thus no period-dependent or configuration-dependent threshold is needed
    for this identity; the terminal inverse cannot change the unit.

    For any lattice sites <math|<around|(|o<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,o<rsub|n>|)>>,
    multiplication of the insertions and the identity just proved give the
    pointwise equality of integrands

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-O\><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|\<b-1\>;o<rsub|0>;A|)>*<big|prod><rsub|j=1><rsup|n>\<cal-O\><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v<rsub|j>;o<rsub|j>;A|)>=<big|prod><rsub|j=1><rsup|n>\<cal-O\><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v<rsub|j>;o<rsub|j>;A|)>.
    </equation*>

    The local sections are continuous on the compact finite-lattice
    configuration space, so these finite products are integrable. Taking the
    same finite-volume expectation on both sides proves the exact
    unit-deletion identity for the finite correlations. For <math|n=0> the
    product on the right is the constant one. Independently, the arity-zero
    finite correlation is the expectation of the empty product, hence is one
    by probability normalization at every cutoff and period. These are
    identities of point correlations, with no smearing factor
    <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>>.

    We pass the deletion identity to the limits without changing the family
    or the order. Fix first <math|x=<around|(|x<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>\<in\><math-up|Gen>>.
    Its deleted tuple <math|x<rprime|'>=<around|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>>
    is also generic: each coordinate projection remains injective after
    deleting a point. For every sufficiently large fixed period, both tuples
    lie in their cyclic patches and inside the comparison cell. Indeed their
    finitely many coordinate differences are nonzero, and the growing period
    eventually exceeds all their absolute values and the size of a box
    containing the tuples.

    Use the slotwise address map to choose the lattice sites for <math|x>,
    and for <math|x<rprime|'>> delete its zeroth site. Rounding each point
    coordinate separately, and then wrapping it into the periodic lattice,
    commutes exactly with this deletion. The lifted samples converge to
    <math|x> and <math|x<rprime|'>> as the mesh tends to zero. Openness of
    the two cyclic patches places both samples in compact neighborhoods
    inside those patches on a common mesh tail. The assumed local uniform
    mesh convergence there, together with continuity of the intermediate
    limits, identifies their limits with <math|J<rsub|p,<around|(|\<b-1\>,v|)>><around|(|x|)>>
    and <math|J<rsub|p,v><around|(|x<rprime|'>|)>>. Intersect this tail with
    <math|N\<geqslant\>r<rsub|0>> (and the finitely many reference-depth
    tails of the requested labels, if needed). The exact finite identity then
    gives

    <\equation*>
      J<rsub|p,<around|(|\<b-1\>,v|)>><around|(|x|)>=J<rsub|p,v><around|(|x<rprime|'>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Now send the period index to infinity along the same chosen sequence.
    Both signatures converge to their respective values in the same table
    <math|S>, so uniqueness of limits proves <eqref|eq:ex-limits-unit-law> on
    <math|<math-up|Gen>>. All mesh thresholds may depend on the fixed period;
    intersecting the two tails preserves the ordered quantifiers and requires
    neither a diagonal limit nor a mesh threshold uniform over periods. At
    arity zero the configuration space has one point; the two successive
    limits of its constant finite correlation are still one. This proves
    <math|S<around|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=1> and also treats the empty deleted
    tuple when <math|n=0>.

    Finally, <math|<math-up|Gen>> is dense in <math|<math-up|Conf>>: its
    complement there is the finite union of coordinate-equality hyperplanes.
    A distinct configuration has a neighborhood inside <math|<math-up|Conf>>,
    and arbitrarily small perturbations within that neighborhood avoid all
    these hyperplanes. The two sides of <eqref|eq:ex-limits-unit-law> are
    continuous on <math|<math-up|Conf>>; for the right side use continuity of
    deletion and the fact that deleting a point preserves distinctness.
    Equality on the dense subset therefore extends to every pairwise distinct
    configuration.
  </proof>

  This proposition derives a raw unit law from the retained finite
  calibration and the same-family limit hypotheses. It does not construct the
  calibrated family, prove its convergence or smoothness, or construct
  inclusions between field quotients. No selected-phase unit law is asserted
  here.

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Scope of the extraction><label|rem:ex-limits-scope>The local
    estimates are not a proof that the Wilson family has a convergent
    subsequence by themselves. One must first establish the exact norm and
    oscillation bounds for that family, uniformly over the compact sets and
    with the specified fixed-period or ordered-volume quantifiers. Also, the
    extracted first surface is only continuous on generic patches. The later
    proof of smoothness on the full noncoincident set (including
    noncoincident coordinate-projection ties), followed by the full Euclidean
    hierarchy, has its own proof obligations.
  </remark>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Full mesh convergence versus extraction><label|rem:ex-limits-fullmesh>Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-limits-extraction>
    is a compactness statement: its first stage selects a mesh subsequence
    <math|t>, and its second a volume subsequence <math|u>. The construction
    used for the main theorem still extracts its hierarchy along a refined
    order whose mesh component is a subsequence
    (Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-assembly>). Its public statement is
    nevertheless not limited to that mesh subsequence, because at each fixed
    period it also proves convergence along the whole mesh sequence, by a
    different argument: the finite-difference bounds of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-fullmesh-fixedbounds> hold at all
    sufficiently fine meshes, uniformly in the period, and
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:fullmesh-correlation-cauchy> proves that
    the lattice correlations of one selected history are Cauchy at every
    ancestral configuration. Theorem<nbsp><reference|ex-smooth-grid-limit>
    turns these two inputs into locally uniform convergence of all difference
    quotients along the full mesh sequence. Its limit is a smooth function on
    <math|<math-up|NoHit><rsub|p>>, a larger set than
    <math|<math-up|Cyc><rsub|p>>. Nothing in that argument passes to a
    subsequence. Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-full-mesh> then identifies, at
    every suitable period, the full-mesh limit with the row limit of the
    extracted hierarchy, so the volume limit of the extracted rows is also
    the volume limit of the full-mesh rows.

    An alternative route is also available for the correlation functions
    themselves; it is not the route of Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-assembly>.
    With these inputs one may take <math|t> to be the identity and
    <math|J<rsub|p,v>> to be the full-mesh limit, for every eligible period
    <math|p>, not only the periods on the volume sequence. The second stage
    uses only the functions <math|J<rsub|p,v>>, the expanding-volume modulus
    and the ordered compact norm bound. It applies unchanged and still
    selects a volume subsequence <math|u>; no full volume limit is asserted.
    The resulting statement is the order of limits in the public theorem: a
    full mesh limit at every suitable period, followed by a volume limit
    along a subsequence of the given period sequence. This alternative
    concerns only the correlations treated in
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-limits-extraction>: the other data of
    the extracted hierarchy (fields, phases and the properties stated along
    its own order) are not thereby transferred to the full-mesh order, and
    for them the construction uses the identification above. The two stages
    must not be conflated. A diagonal subsequence
    <math|<around|(|N<rsub|k>,p<rsub|k>|)>> yields neither iterated limit. A
    mesh subsequence chosen separately for each period would not give the
    full-mesh assertion.
  </remark>

  <subsection|Smooth limits from lattice differences><label|ex-smooth-limits>

  The extraction argument initially gives continuity only on generic
  configuration patches. A stronger family of difference bounds removes
  coordinate ties and identifies every derivative of the limit. This argument
  also explains the additional input needed for convergence along the whole
  mesh sequence: pointwise Cauchy convergence on a dense set, without another
  subsequence selection.

  We state the analytic result on an arbitrary open set
  <math|U\<subset\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>. In the fixed-period application,
  <math|d=4*n>, <math|U> is the set of configurations with no coincident
  points modulo the period, and the functions are periodic lifts of lattice
  correlations. Thus <math|U> includes configurations with coordinate ties
  and can cross a fundamental-domain seam. Write
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|N>=a<rsub|N>*\<bbb-Z\><rsup|d>>, where
  <math|a<rsub|N>\<gtr\>0> and <math|a<rsub|N>\<to\>0>. For functions
  <math|F<rsub|N>:\<Gamma\><rsub|N>\<to\>\<bbb-C\>>, define

  <\equation*>
    \<Delta\><rsub|N,j>*F<rsub|N><around|(|x|)>=<frac|F<rsub|N>*<around|(|x+a<rsub|N>*e<rsub|j>|)>-F<rsub|N><around|(|x|)>|a<rsub|N>>,<space|2em>F<rsub|N,\<alpha\>>=\<Delta\><rsub|N,\<alpha\><rsub|1>>*\<cdots\>*\<Delta\><rsub|N,\<alpha\><rsub|k>>*F<rsub|N>
  </equation*>

  for a finite list <math|\<alpha\>> of coordinates, including the empty
  list. The order convention is that prefixing a coordinate applies one
  further difference to the previous function. Choose cell representatives
  <math|Q<rsub|N>:\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>\<to\>\<Gamma\><rsub|N>> by rounding each
  coordinate, so <math|<around|\||Q<rsub|N>*x-x|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>d*a<rsub|N>>
  and <math|Q<rsub|N>*x=x> on <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|N>>.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Full-sequence smooth convergence><label|ex-smooth-grid-limit>
    Assume that for every compact <math|K\<subset\>U> and every finite list
    <math|\<alpha\>>, there are <math|C<rsub|K,\<alpha\>>\<less\>\<infty\>>
    and <math|N<rsub|K,\<alpha\>>> such that

    <\equation>
      <label|ex-grid-derivative-budget><around|\||F<rsub|N,\<alpha\>><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|K,\<alpha\>>*<space|1em><around|(|N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|K,\<alpha\>>,x\<in\>\<Gamma\><rsub|N>\<cap\>K|)>.
    </equation>

    Suppose also that there is a dense subset <math|D\<subset\>U> for which
    <math|F<rsub|N>*<around|(|Q<rsub|N>*x|)>> is Cauchy for every
    <math|x\<in\>D>. Then there is a smooth function
    <math|f:U\<to\>\<bbb-C\>> such that, for every <math|\<alpha\>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|ex-grid-all-limits>F<rsub|N,\<alpha\>>*<around|(|Q<rsub|N>*x|)>\<longrightarrow\>\<partial\><rsup|\<alpha\>>*f<around|(|x|)><space|1em><text|uniformly
      on every compact subset of >U.
    </equation>

    No further subsequence is taken. In particular, whenever
    <math|N<rsub|k>\<to\>\<infty\>> and <math|x<rsub|k>\<in\>\<Gamma\><rsub|N<rsub|k>>>
    tends to <math|x\<in\>U>, <math|F<rsub|N<rsub|k>,\<alpha\>><around|(|x<rsub|k>|)>\<to\>\<partial\><rsup|\<alpha\>>*f<around|(|x|)>>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    We give the details in the <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>> norm; equivalence of
    norms in fixed dimension gives the same local convergence in the
    Euclidean norm. All estimates below are made in a compact neighborhood
    strictly inside <math|U>, and finitely many thresholds in
    <eqref|ex-grid-derivative-budget> can be replaced by their maximum.

    <em|Grid Lipschitz and Taylor estimates.> Fix <math|\<alpha\>>, and
    abbreviate <math|G<rsub|N>=F<rsub|N,\<alpha\>>>. On a slightly enlarged
    compact set, bound all its first differences by <math|B<rsub|1>> and all
    its second differences by <math|B<rsub|2>>. Join sufficiently nearby grid
    points <math|x,y> by moving one coordinate at a time, always
    monotonically toward <math|y>. The path has total length
    <math|R=<around|\||y-x|\|><rsub|1>> and stays in the coordinate box
    between <math|x,y>. Each edge changes <math|G<rsub|N>> by at most
    <math|a<rsub|N>*B<rsub|1>>, including a negative edge by using the
    forward difference at its lower endpoint. Telescoping gives

    <\equation>
      <label|ex-grid-lipschitz><around|\||G<rsub|N><around|(|y|)>-G<rsub|N><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|1>*<around|\||y-x|\|><rsub|1>.
    </equation>

    Apply the same argument to each <math|\<Delta\><rsub|N,j>*G<rsub|N>>. At
    every base point <math|z> of an edge of the path,
    <math|<around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|N,j>*G<rsub|N><around|(|z|)>-\<Delta\><rsub|N,j>*G<rsub|N><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|2>*R>.
    Subtract the linear function whose coordinate coefficients are
    <math|\<Delta\><rsub|N,j>*G<rsub|N><around|(|x|)>> from the edge
    increments, and telescope again:

    <\equation>
      <label|ex-grid-taylor><around*|\||G<rsub|N><around|(|y|)>-G<rsub|N><around|(|x|)>-<big|sum><rsub|j><around|(|y<rsub|j>-x<rsub|j>|)>*\<Delta\><rsub|N,j>*G<rsub|N><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|2>*<around|\||y-x|\|><rsub|1><rsup|2>.
    </equation>

    The slightly enlarged compact set ensures that all shifted points used by
    these differences remain inside the region of the bounds. This is
    possible because only finitely many shifts occur and
    <math|a<rsub|N>\<to\>0>.

    <em|Extension from dense pointwise convergence.>
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|ex-grid-lipschitz> and the rounding bound imply on
    any compact patch, for sufficiently nearby <math|x,y>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||G<rsub|N>*<around|(|Q<rsub|N>*x|)>-G<rsub|N>*<around|(|Q<rsub|N>*y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|1>*<around|(|<around|\||x-y|\|><rsub|1>+2*d*a<rsub|N>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Thus the rounded functions are asymptotically equicontinuous; they need
    not themselves be continuous. For <math|\<alpha\>=\<varnothing\>>, this
    and the Cauchy property on <math|D> imply uniform Cauchy convergence on
    each compact set. To see it explicitly, cover that compact set by
    finitely many sufficiently small balls with centers in <math|D>, lying in
    a common compact neighborhood in <math|U>. At the centers, choose one
    common Cauchy threshold. Choose the mesh still finer so that the two
    oscillation errors, from a point to its center for each of the two mesh
    functions, are small. The triangle inequality then bounds their
    difference uniformly on the whole compact set. Completeness of
    <math|\<bbb-C\>> gives a uniform limit. The displayed modulus makes the
    limit continuous. Limits on overlapping compact neighborhoods agree by
    pointwise uniqueness, giving a single continuous <math|f> on <math|U>.

    This reasoning also proves a useful moving-point assertion: if the
    rounded <math|G<rsub|N>> converge locally uniformly to a continuous
    <math|g>, and grid points <math|y<rsub|N>\<to\>y\<in\>U>, then
    <math|G<rsub|N><around|(|y<rsub|N>|)>\<to\>g<around|(|y|)>>. Indeed,
    eventually the points lie in one compact neighborhood; use
    <math|Q<rsub|N>*y<rsub|N>=y<rsub|N>>, uniform convergence there, and
    continuity of <math|g>. The same statement holds after any indices
    <math|N<rsub|k>\<to\>\<infty\>>.

    <em|Cauchy convergence of the next differences.> Suppose now the rounded
    <math|G<rsub|N>=F<rsub|N,\<alpha\>>> converge locally uniformly to
    <math|g>. Fix <math|x\<in\>U> and a coordinate <math|j>. Take a small
    <math|h\<gtr\>0> such that a compact neighborhood of the segment
    <math|<around|[|x,x+h*e<rsub|j>|]>> lies in <math|U>. Put
    <math|h<rsub|N>=a<rsub|N>*<around|\<lceil\>|h/a<rsub|N>|\<rceil\>>>,
    <math|x<rsub|N>=Q<rsub|N>*x>, and <math|y<rsub|N>=x<rsub|N>+h<rsub|N>*e<rsub|j>>.
    Then <math|h\<leqslant\>h<rsub|N>\<less\>h+a<rsub|N>>, so the
    moving-point assertion implies convergence of

    <\equation*>
      V<rsub|N>=<frac|G<rsub|N><around|(|y<rsub|N>|)>-G<rsub|N><around|(|x<rsub|N>|)>|h<rsub|N>>\<longrightarrow\><frac|g*<around|(|x+h*e<rsub|j>|)>-g<around|(|x|)>|h>.
    </equation*>

    The Taylor estimate gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|N,j>*G<rsub|N><around|(|x<rsub|N>|)>-V<rsub|N>|\|>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|2>*h<rsub|N>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|B<rsub|2>> can be fixed on one compact neighborhood for every
    sufficiently small <math|h>. Given <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, first
    choose <math|h> so that <math|4*B<rsub|2>*h\<less\>\<epsilon\>/2>, then
    choose <math|N> large so that <math|h<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>2*h> and
    <math|V<rsub|N>> is Cauchy with error less than <math|\<epsilon\>/2>. The
    triangle inequality proves that <math|\<Delta\><rsub|N,j>*G<rsub|N>*<around|(|Q<rsub|N>*x|)>>
    is Cauchy. Bounds for its first differences give asymptotic
    equicontinuity by the previous argument. It therefore converges locally
    uniformly to a continuous function <math|g<rsub|j>>.

    <em|Identification of derivatives and induction.> Apply
    <eqref|ex-grid-taylor> with <math|Q<rsub|N>*x,Q<rsub|N>*y>, for fixed
    nearby <math|x,y>, and pass to the limit using the just-established
    convergence of all first differences. This gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||g<around|(|y|)>-g<around|(|x|)>-<big|sum><rsub|j><around|(|y<rsub|j>-x<rsub|j>|)>*g<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|2>*<around|\||y-x|\|><rsub|1><rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    The bound proves Fréchet differentiability, with
    <math|D*g<around|(|x|)><around|[|u|]>=<big|sum><rsub|j>u<rsub|j>*g<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>>.
    Since the <math|g<rsub|j>> are continuous, <math|g> is <math|C<rsup|1>>.
    Repeat this argument for every finite list <math|\<alpha\>>. Induction
    gives locally uniform limits for all iterated differences and identifies
    them with the corresponding derivatives of <math|f>. Their continuity
    proves <math|f\<in\>C<rsup|k>> for every finite <math|k>, hence
    smoothness. The case <math|d=0> is simply convergence of one scalar
    sequence; there are no derivative steps. This completes the proof.
  </proof>

  <\corollary>
    <dueto|Other fixed difference stencils><label|ex-other-stencils> The same
    limits hold for any fixed finite composition of forward, backward or
    centered coordinate differences, provided every difference is divided by
    its full physical interval length.
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    For fixed integers <math|b,c\<geqslant\>0> with <math|b+c\<gtr\>0>,
    telescope:

    <\equation*>
      <frac|G<rsub|N>*<around|(|x+c*a<rsub|N>*e<rsub|j>|)>-G<rsub|N>*<around|(|x-b*a<rsub|N>*e<rsub|j>|)>|<around|(|b+c|)>*a<rsub|N>>=<frac|1|b+c>*<big|sum><rsub|r=0><rsup|b+c-1>\<Delta\><rsub|N,j>*G<rsub|N>*<around|(|x+<around|(|r-b|)>*a<rsub|N>*e<rsub|j>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Each of the finitely many sample points approaches the same point, so the
    moving-point conclusion in Theorem<nbsp><reference|ex-smooth-grid-limit>
    gives the same derivative for each summand. Translation averages commute
    with coordinate differences. A finite composition is therefore a finite
    average of shifted iterated unit forward differences, and has exactly the
    stated limiting derivative. Uniform convergence on compacts follows by
    using one slightly enlarged compact neighborhood for all the finitely
    many shifts. No assertion is made when the stencil sizes vary with
    <math|N>.
  </proof>

  The theorem is applied separately for each fixed period, label tuple and
  finite derivative request. Its derivative bounds are supplied by the
  full-precision lattice estimates, and its dense Cauchy input by the
  renormalization-group comparison of different cutoffs. Neither input
  follows from a subsequential limit alone. Once they are supplied, the
  fixed-period smooth kernel and all its locally uniform difference limits
  follow without further mesh extraction. The fixed-period smeared limits
  then follow by the cell-integral dominated-convergence argument of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-limits-extraction>, applied to each
  fixed stencil.

  <subsection|Buying the full-mesh finite-difference
  bounds><label|sec:ex-fullmesh-fixedbounds>

  We prove the finite-volume estimate needed before any continuum extraction.
  The important order is to pay for the bounded projected coefficient first,
  and only then use the exact Wilson difference identity. The result is
  uniform over every period at all sufficiently fine meshes. The proof has
  two parts: a finite dimensional absorption estimate for one coefficient
  row, and a packing argument which plants finitely many such rows into one
  large periodic box.

  For a period <math|p>, mesh <math|N>, field list
  <math|v=<around|(|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|n>|)>>, and finite list
  <math|l> of slot-and-coordinate directions, write
  <math|K<rsub|N,p;v,l><around|(|x|)>> for the finite Wilson expectation of
  the product of the corresponding iterated periodic difference insertions at
  the sites <math|x=<around|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>> of the
  period-<math|p> torus at mesh <math|N>. Sites have integer coordinates; the
  physical position of a site <math|z> is <math|a<rsub|N>*z>. The
  normalization is <math|\<Delta\><rsub|N,\<alpha\>>*F<around|(|z|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|-1>*<around*|(|F*<around|(|z+e<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>-F<around|(|z|)>|)>>
  for a site <math|z> and a unit lattice step <math|e<rsub|\<alpha\>>>. Thus
  the theorem below concerns the unscaled derivative kernel; the exact
  one-step identity used in its proof is <math|F*<around|(|z+e<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>-F<around|(|z|)>=a<rsub|N>*\<Delta\><rsub|N,\<alpha\>>*F<around|(|z|)>>.

  Throughout this section the index set <math|I> is the set of mesh-period
  pairs <math|i=<around|(|N,p|)>> with the preorder
  <math|<around|(|N,p|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|N<rprime|'>,p<rprime|'>|)>> if
  and only if <math|N\<leqslant\>N<rprime|'>>. A statement holds
  <em|eventually in <math|I>> if there is <math|N<rsub|0>> such that it holds
  for all <math|N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>> and every period <math|p>. A limit in
  <math|I> is understood in the same sense: <math|y<rsub|i>\<to\>y> means
  that for every <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0> there is <math|N<rsub|0>> with
  <math|<around|\||y<rsub|<around|(|N,p|)>>-y|\|>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>> for
  all <math|N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>> and all <math|p>. With the product order
  instead, \Peventually\Q would exclude small periods and the uniformity
  asserted below would not follow.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Absorption of the projected coefficient><label|lem:ex-fullmesh-cap>
    Let <math|I> be the mesh-period index set just described, and let
    <math|X<rsub|i>> be a finite-dimensional normed coefficient space.
    Suppose that for each <math|i> there are a projection
    <math|P<rsub|i>:X<rsub|i>\<to\>X<rsub|i>>, a linear map
    <math|L<rsub|i>:X<rsub|i>\<to\>Y<rsub|i>>, and a constant
    <math|Z\<gtr\>0> such that

    <\equation>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|i>*z|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>Z*<around|\<\|\|\>|L<rsub|i>*<around|(|P<rsub|i>*z|)>|\<\|\|\>>.<label|eq:ex-cap-rank>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x<rsub|i>\<in\>X<rsub|i>>, put
    <math|X<rsub|i><rsup|0>=P<rsub|i>*x<rsub|i>>, and set
    <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>=<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|i><rsup|0>|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|-1>>.
    Assume the following estimates hold eventually in <math|I>:

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|L<rsub|i>*x<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>c,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-cap-tested>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|L<rsub|i>*<around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|i>*<around|(|x<rsub|i>-X<rsub|i><rsup|0>|)>|)>|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\><frac|1|2*Z>.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-cap-remainder>>>>>
    </align>

    Then eventually

    <\equation>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|i><rsup|0>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*Z*c+1.<label|eq:ex-cap-conclusion>
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Write <math|R<rsub|i>=x<rsub|i>-X<rsub|i><rsup|0>>. By linearity and
    <eqref|eq:ex-cap-tested>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|i><rsup|0>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>Z*<around|\<\|\|\>|L<rsub|i>*X<rsub|i><rsup|0>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>Z*c+Z*<around|\<\|\|\>|L<rsub|i>*R<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>=<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|i><rsup|0>|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|-1>>,
    <eqref|eq:ex-cap-remainder> implies <math|Z*<around|\<\|\|\>|L<rsub|i>*R<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|i><rsup|0>|\<\|\|\>>|)>/2>.
    Therefore

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|i><rsup|0>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>Z*c+<tfrac|1|2><around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|i><rsup|0>|\<\|\|\>>|)>,
    </equation*>

    and rearranging gives <eqref|eq:ex-cap-conclusion>. All estimates were
    eventual, so their two mesh thresholds can be replaced by their maximum.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|One coefficient row is bounded at every
    period><label|prop:ex-fullmesh-cap-source>Let
    <math|C\<subseteq\><big|prod><rsub|i>X<rsub|i>> be one class of
    coefficient row families satisfying the following normalized-cost
    conditions. Suppose that the full family <math|x=<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>>
    belongs to <math|C>, that <math|C> is closed under subtraction and
    pointwise multiplication by scalar families whose absolute values are at
    most one, and that every uniformly norm-bounded row family belongs to
    <math|C>. Suppose also that the detector estimates

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|<around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|i>*z|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>Z*<around|\<\|\|\>|L<rsub|i>*<around|(|P<rsub|i>*z|)>|\<\|\|\>>>
      for all <math|z\<in\>X<rsub|i>> with a scale-independent
      <math|Z\<gtr\>0>;

      <item>for the primary paid trial there are assay values
      <math|b<rsub|i>\<in\>Y<rsub|i>> with
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>v> and
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|L<rsub|i>*x<rsub|i>-b<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<to\>0>;

      <item>for every row family <math|z\<in\>C> satisfying
      <math|P<rsub|i>*z<rsub|i>=0> for every <math|i>, one has
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|L<rsub|i>*z<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<to\>0>.
    </enumerate>

    Then, eventually in <math|I>, that is, for all sufficiently fine meshes
    and uniformly over all periods,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|i>*x<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*Z*<around|(|v+1|)>+1.
    </equation*>

    The uniformity means that the limits in items<nbsp>2 and<nbsp>3 are taken
    in <math|I>: they hold beyond one mesh threshold for every period.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    From item<nbsp>2, eventually in <math|I>,
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|L<rsub|i>*x<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>v+1=:c>.
    Put <math|X<rsub|i><rsup|0>=P<rsub|i>*x<rsub|i>> and

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsub|i>=<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|i><rsup|0>|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>z<rsub|i>=\<lambda\><rsub|i>*<around|(|x<rsub|i>-X<rsub|i><rsup|0>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The scalar family <math|\<lambda\>=<around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|i>|)><rsub|i>>
    satisfies <math|<around|\||\<lambda\><rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>1>, so
    <math|\<lambda\>*x\<in\>C>. The family <math|\<lambda\>*X<rsup|0>> is
    uniformly bounded by one, so it belongs to <math|C> as well. Therefore
    <math|z=\<lambda\>*<around|(|x-X<rsup|0>|)>\<in\>C> by subtraction, and
    <math|P<rsub|i>*z<rsub|i>=0> because <math|P<rsub|i>*<around|(|x<rsub|i>-P<rsub|i>*x<rsub|i>|)>=0>.
    By item<nbsp>3, for all sufficiently fine meshes
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|L<rsub|i>*z<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|2*Z|)><rsup|-1>>.
    Since <math|L<rsub|i>*z<rsub|i>=\<lambda\><rsub|i>*L<rsub|i>*<around|(|x<rsub|i>-X<rsub|i><rsup|0>|)>>,
    estimate <eqref|eq:ex-cap-rank> gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|i><rsup|0>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>Z*<around|\<\|\|\>|L<rsub|i>*X<rsub|i><rsup|0>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>Z*c+Z*<around|\<\|\|\>|L<rsub|i>*<around|(|x<rsub|i>-X<rsub|i><rsup|0>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>Z*c+<frac|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|i><rsup|0>|\<\|\|\>>|2>.
    </equation*>

    Rearranging proves the claim. The common mesh threshold is the maximum of
    the mesh thresholds in items<nbsp>2 and<nbsp>3.
  </proof>

  The cap alone bounds a projected row. The difference trial is paid by a
  separate closure argument which uses that bound; we give its steps. The
  primary paid trial, the exact equation with its vanishing owed cost, the
  bounded-coefficient receipt and the sweep for rows with zero projection are
  first kept explicit in Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-fullmesh-fixedbounds-producer>
  below. Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:fullmesh-selected-supplier>
  verifies them for the retained public family. Let <math|x> be the
  coefficient row for the requested insertion and let <math|F> be its primary
  paid trial. The exact equation gives an owed row
  <math|e=<math-up|upper><around|(|x|)>-<math-up|hops><around|(|F|)>>, with a
  root-test cost <math|u> tending to zero. Applying the cap to
  <math|<around|(|x,F,e|)>> bounds <math|P*x> uniformly. Thus <math|y=P*x> is
  a uniformly bounded row family and its primary insertion is paid by the
  bounded-coefficient receipt. The row <math|x-y> lies in the normalized cost
  class by the class closure, and <math|P*<around|(|x-y|)>=0>. The sweep
  theorem therefore makes <math|<math-up|upper><around|(|x-y|)>> owed with a
  cost tending to zero. We have the identity

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|hops><around|(|F|)>=<math-up|upper><around|(|y|)>+<math-up|upper><around|(|x-y|)>-e.
  </equation*>

  It follows from <math|<math-up|upper><around|(|x|)>=<math-up|upper><around|(|y|)>+<math-up|upper><around|(|x-y|)>>
  and the defining equation for <math|e>. The paid budget is closed under
  finite addition and subtraction: once each vanishing cost is below the unit
  allowance at a sufficiently fine mesh, the primary paid budget together
  with the two unit owed budgets yields a finite paid budget for the
  requested difference trial. This is exactly the projection-and-remainder
  argument; it does not require the unsplit equation residual to be bounded
  by a quantity that depends on <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|P*x|\<\|\|\>>>.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Full-mesh bounds for all finite
    differences><label|thm:ex-fullmesh-fixedbounds-producer>Fix a family for
    which the preceding coefficient-row estimate and the paid-remainder
    decomposition just described apply to every finite list of fields and
    difference directions, including each resulting difference trial
    transformed by a signed-coordinate action, with or without
    coefficientwise conjugation. Assume that each bounded primary coefficient
    row family and each of its finitely many corresponding orientations has a
    paid root-test budget in the following sense: for each such oriented row
    family there is a depth <math|k<rsub|0>> such that, for every centred
    scale <math|k\<geqslant\>k<rsub|0>>, there are a budget
    <math|b\<less\>\<infty\>> and a mesh threshold valid for every period
    beyond which the centred root test at scale <math|k> is at most <math|b>.
    The bounds and thresholds may depend on the oriented family and <math|k>.
    Assume also that the exact periodic one-step difference identity holds,
    and that translated insertions satisfy the product root-test inequality
    in distinct interior cells. Then for each finite field list <math|v>,
    difference list <math|l>, and <math|r\<gtr\>0>, there are
    <math|B\<less\>\<infty\>> and a mesh threshold <math|N<rsub|0>> such that
    for every <math|N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>>, every period <math|p>, and every
    periodic position tuple <math|x> such that any two distinct slot indices
    <math|u\<ne\>u<rprime|'>> have sites <math|x<rsub|u>,x<rsub|u<rprime|'>>>
    with

    <\equation*>
      a<rsub|N><space|0.17em><math-up|sep><rsub|N,p><around|(|x<rsub|u>,x<rsub|u<rprime|'>>|)>\<geqslant\>r
    </equation*>

    (even if the corresponding insertions agree),

    <\equation>
      <around|\||K<rsub|N,p;v,l><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>B.<label|eq:ex-fullmesh-bound>
    </equation>

    The threshold is independent of <math|p>; the bound is independent of
    both <math|N> and <math|p>. Here <math|<math-up|sep><rsub|N,p><around|(|y,y<rprime|'>|)>=max<rsub|\<alpha\>><around|\||y<rsub|\<alpha\>>-y<rprime|'><rsub|\<alpha\>>|\|><rsub|<math-up|per>>>
    is the periodic maximum-coordinate distance of two sites of the
    period-<math|p> torus at mesh <math|N>, where
    <math|<around|\||k|\|><rsub|<math-up|per>>> denotes the distance from the
    integer <math|k> to the nearest multiple of the side length
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|N,p>>. Thus the separation is measured in the periodic
    <math|\<ell\><rsup|\<infty\>>> metric, not the Euclidean one; the
    injectivity argument below uses exactly this metric.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    First fund one row. For a field label <math|w>, the constant coefficient
    row <math|x<rsub|i>=w> has bounded normalized cost. The assumed primary
    receipt and the preceding cap-and-remainder argument give a paid budget
    for each finite difference list applied to that insertion, eventually at
    every mesh and every period. For a general finite list
    <math|v=<around|(|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|n>|)>> and positional
    derivative list, make one trial for each slot by applying to
    <math|v<rsub|j>> the subsequence of directions assigned to that slot. The
    exact one-step identity is the periodic discrete difference formula

    <\equation*>
      F<rsub|j>*<around|(|z+e<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>-F<rsub|j><around|(|z|)>=a<rsub|N>*<space|0.17em>\<Delta\><rsub|N,\<alpha\>>*F<rsub|j><around|(|z|)>,
    </equation*>

    with the normalization of the difference operator. Iterating this
    identity and the exact product measure definition gives the desired
    multi-difference kernel, including wrapped coordinates. The list is
    finite, so the paid records have finitely many depth thresholds
    <math|k<rsub|0>>.

    We now explain the two-scale tiling argument. First choose a tiling scale
    <math|\<ell\>> with <math|a<rsub|\<ell\>>\<less\>r>. Then use
    <math|a<rsub|k>\<to\>0> to choose one recentering scale
    <math|k\<geqslant\>\<ell\>> which lies beyond all the finitely many depth
    thresholds <math|k<rsub|0>> of the paid records and satisfies

    <\equation*>
      6*n*a<rsub|k>\<less\>a<rsub|\<ell\>>.
    </equation*>

    At this scale <math|k> all the root-test budgets hold beyond a common
    mesh threshold, uniformly over periods (take the maximum of the finitely
    many mesh thresholds). At a fine mesh <math|N> beyond both scales, let
    <math|t> be the integer span of scale <math|k> and let <math|T> be a
    periodic tiling whose side is the integer span of scale <math|\<ell\>>.
    The aligned-scale identities give <math|a<rsub|N>*t=a<rsub|k>> and
    <math|a<rsub|N>*T.<math-up|side>=a<rsub|\<ell\>>>. Thus
    <math|n*<around|(|2*<around|(|3*t|)>|)>\<less\>T.<math-up|side>>. The
    tiling placement lemma, applied with margin <math|3*t>, gives one
    translated tiling and an assignment of the <math|n> insertion sites to
    cells, each site lying at least <math|3*t> from the cell boundary. The
    boundary count is the one in the proof of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-producers>: in each coordinate one
    site forbids at most <math|6*t> of the <math|T.<math-up|side>> possible
    translations, so the <math|n> sites forbid fewer than
    <math|T.<math-up|side>> of them. This is uniform in the period: alignment
    gives the same physical side <math|a<rsub|\<ell\>>> for every period
    index, once <math|N> is sufficiently fine.

    The cell assignment is injective for a geometric reason. If two sites
    were assigned to the same cell, then in each coordinate both lie in one
    window of <math|T.<math-up|side>> consecutive residues, so their periodic
    coordinate distance is at most <math|T.<math-up|side>>; hence
    <math|<math-up|sep><rsub|N,p>\<leqslant\>T.<math-up|side>>. Multiplying
    by <math|a<rsub|N>> would give <math|a<rsub|N><math-up|sep><rsub|N,p>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|N>*T.<math-up|side>=a<rsub|\<ell\>>\<less\>r>,
    contradicting the assumption. (With Euclidean separation one would
    instead need <math|2*a<rsub|\<ell\>>\<less\>r> in four dimensions; the
    statement above avoids this by using the <math|\<ell\><rsup|\<infty\>>>
    metric throughout.) The three-span margin is exactly what the centering
    lemma needs to transport each local insertion from the centered tile to
    its assigned cell.

    For each occupied cell, recenter its insertion to the distinguished
    center cell. The budgets are root tests relative to the fine centred
    tiling at scale <math|k>, whereas the planted inequality below is applied
    in the coarse tiling of side <math|a<rsub|\<ell\>>>. Invariance of the
    Wilson law under translations and signed coordinate symmetries does not
    by itself compare root tests of different tilings. The comparison is the
    replicated-orbit and maximum-principle argument in the proof of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-producers> (the packing argument,
    with its coarse depth <math|m> and fine depth <math|k> taken to be
    <math|\<ell\>> and <math|k> here): the fine planted inequality bounds the
    root test of each insertion relative to the coarse tiling at a home point
    on the fine centre grid by the fine budget, the one-dimensional maximum
    principle moves the home point to the assigned site, and
    signed-coordinate covariance identifies the oriented copy in each
    occupied cell with the original insertion. The hypothesis supplies the
    root-test budgets for the finitely many signed-coordinate and conjugated
    orientations of each trial. Include their paid records in the finite list
    above, as in Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-finite>, before taking the
    maximum of the depth thresholds and the maximum of the mesh thresholds at
    the chosen scale. This transports both the interior support condition and
    the same root-test budget to the occupied cell. No statistical
    independence is asserted: all factors are evaluated under the same Wilson
    law. Instead, the planted-cell inequality applies to the translated
    copies in distinct cells and gives

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||\<bbb-E\>*<big|prod><rsub|j=1><rsup|n>F<rsub|j><around|(|x<rsub|j>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|j=1><rsup|n>b<rsub|j>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|b<rsub|j>> is the paid root-test bound for the <math|j>th
    trial. Their finite product is a constant independent of mesh and period.
    This proves <eqref|eq:ex-fullmesh-bound>.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The uniform supplier on the retained family>

  We now verify the hypotheses above on <math|C<rsub|R>>. In this subsection
  every occurrence of \Peventually\Q means eventually in <math|I>, including
  periods smaller than any prescribed comparison level. This is stronger than
  an ordered extraction statement.

  Call a row <math|x=<around|(|x<rsub|N,p>|)>> of grade <math|d>
  <em|uniformly subpower> if

  <\equation*>
    \<forall\>\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0\<exists\>N<rsub|\<epsilon\>>\<forall\>N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|\<epsilon\>>
    \<forall\>p,<space|2em><around|\||X<rsub|N,p><rsup|-1>*x<rsub|N,p>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<epsilon\>*s<rsub|N>>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|X> is the actual terminal calibration of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-equation-kernel-relay>. Denote this
  class by <math|<with|math-font|cal*|C><rsub|d>>. It is closed under
  addition, subtraction and scalar multiplication by families of modulus at
  most one: split the target exponent between the finitely many prices and
  absorb their fixed sum using <math|s<rsub|N>\<to\>\<infty\>>. Every
  norm-bounded row belongs to it. Indeed the reverse-column estimate of
  Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|ex-column-subpower> bounds its inverse
  coordinates by a constant times <math|J<rsub|N>>, and the displayed proof
  <math|log J<rsub|N>=o<around|(|s<rsub|N>|)>> involves only the schedule
  index <math|N>, not <math|p>. The same observation applies to the prices
  used below. The threshold powers, column allowances, profile costs and
  reserve inverses are sequences of <math|N> alone. Their subpower proofs use
  the clock and finite stage purchases; in particular the reserve inverse is
  bounded by <math|4*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>> and the derivative-shift
  price by <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>>. Their finite products therefore
  have one subpower majorant independent of <math|p>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Uniform equation sweep and local
    encoding><label|lem:fullmesh-uniform-sweep>Let <math|\<Pi\><rsub|N,p>> be
    the uniformly bounded finite projection with kernel <math|K<rsub|N,p>>
    constructed in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-equation-kernel-relay>,
    using Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:finite-rank-kernel-transport>. For
    <math|z\<in\><with|math-font|cal*|C><rsub|d>> with
    <math|\<Pi\><rsub|N,p>*z<rsub|N,p>=0>, the physical insertion
    <math|O<around|(|z|)>> has centered root price tending to zero in
    <math|I>, at every sufficiently fine fixed cell depth, in all its
    finitely many signed and conjugated orientations. The same holds in
    translated cells with a fixed relative interior margin.

    Every continuous protected trial whose two prices are uniformly subpower
    has, on the same family, a comparison row
    <math|x\<in\><with|math-font|cal*|C><rsub|e>> and a discrepancy
    <math|O<around|(|x|)>-F> with such a vanishing root price. In particular
    this applies to every finite difference word of a bounded primary row,
    with <math|e> increased by the word length.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The columns in <eqref|eq:equation-column-root> are constructed beyond one
    <math|N>-threshold for every <math|p>, with the same price
    <math|E<rsub|N>>. All factors in that price are the <math|N>-only factors
    just described. Consequently its subpower bound holds in <math|I>. For a
    row in the kernel, put <math|v=P<rsub|N,p><rsup|-1>*X<rsub|N,p><rsup|-1>*z>
    in the notation of that section; <math|<around|\||P<rsub|N,p><rsup|-1>|\|>\<leqslant\>2>.
    Thus <math|v> has a uniform subpower price and the finite sum of oriented
    column errors has price

    <\equation*>
      c<rsub|d,m,k>*E<rsub|N><around|\||v<rsub|N,p>|\|><rsub|1>*e<rsup|-s<rsub|N>/8>.
    </equation*>

    For each fixed opened pair this is at most <math|e<rsup|-s<rsub|N>/16>>
    eventually in <math|I>. The common-tiling argument there transfers it to
    any prescribed coarser cell and relative margin, using a finer fixed
    opened depth. Its finite sum and fixed geometric factors can, if needed,
    be absorbed by reducing the rate to <math|e<rsup|-s<rsub|N>/32>>. The
    alignment identities and interiority thresholds depend on
    <math|a<rsub|N>> and the fixed stencils, not on <math|p>.

    It remains essential to dispose of the comparison insertion itself,
    without using an ordered spectator estimate. Since
    <math|z\<in\>K<rsub|N,p>>, the comparison is the campaign insertion of an
    element of the exact Maxwell equation ideal: the factors
    <math|S<rsub|\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|-1>>*S<rsub|\<theta\><rsub|N>>>
    cancel exactly. Choose a finite basis of that ideal and one radius
    containing all its microscopic equation stencils. In a centered root test
    at a fixed physical cell size, the other reflected copies depend only on
    links outside this radius once <math|N> is large. The smallest admitted
    physical period is positive, so the finite nonwrapping floors are also
    reached by one <math|N>-threshold for every <math|p>. Apply the exact
    microscopic integration identity to one copy and the product of all other
    copies. This product is bounded and measurable at each finite cutoff. Its
    integral is exactly zero, even if the coefficients of the equation vector
    are large. The centered root of the comparison is therefore zero.
    Linearity covers every vector in the ideal, including cutoff-dependent
    vectors; the stencil threshold is independent of those coefficients. The
    root triangle inequality now gives the claimed sweep. No conversion to an
    ordered spectator limit occurs.

    For the second assertion use the terminal construction in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-insertion-derivative-relay>. Its
    price <math|E<rsub|N,p>> is a finite sum of products of the two protected
    prices and the same schedule factors. It has a uniform subpower majorant.
    The actual descent gives <math|<around|\||X<rsub|N,p><rsup|-1>*x<rsub|N,p>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*E<rsub|N,p>>
    and root discrepancy <math|c<rsub|e,m,k>*E<rsub|N,p>*e<rsup|-s<rsub|N>/8>>.
    The preceding common-tiling argument is therefore uniform in <math|p>
    here as well. For a word of length <math|q>, repeat
    <eqref|eq:relay-difference-weight> <math|q> times. At engineering weight
    <math|d+q> the powers <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|-q>> cancel; the stencil is a
    finite union of at most <math|2<rsup|q>> fixed translates. The derivative
    price is at most <math|<around|(|J<rsub|N>+1|)><rsup|q>> times the
    initial price and the global price at most <math|2<rsup|q>> times it.
    These are uniformly subpower. This proves the assertion for all finite
    words and their orientations without assuming a bound for the
    differentiated correlations.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|A uniformly bounded primary and a finite
    detector><label|lem:fullmesh-uniform-detector>Fix a grade <math|e> and a
    finite direction word <math|l>. There are finite linear detectors
    <math|L<rsub|i>> on its reference coefficient space and <math|Z\<gtr\>0>,
    chosen before <math|N,p>, such that eventually in <math|I>

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<Pi\><rsub|i>*v|\|>\<leqslant\>Z*<around|\||L<rsub|i>*\<Pi\><rsub|i>*v|\|>*<space|2em><around|(|v\<in\>V<rsub|e>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The detectors are averages of correlations with finitely many bounded
    primary spectator rows in small neighborhoods of the purchased generic
    detector configurations. They have the following two properties:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|F> has a uniform paid primary root budget, the assay of
      <math|D<rsub|l>*F> is bounded eventually in <math|I>.

      <item>A continuous trial with a vanishing uniform root price has assay
      tending to zero in <math|I>.
    </enumerate>

    Every bounded primary row has the required uniform root budget, at every
    sufficiently fine fixed centered depth, as do its orientations.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The last assertion follows directly from
    Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:ex-c02-uniform-census>. At a fixed grade
    and radius its width <math|w<rsub|k>> is independent of <math|N,p> and
    the label in that ball; its displayed normalized moment bound gives a
    finite root budget. For a requested cell depth choose a finer opened
    depth and transfer that budget by the replicated-orbit and
    maximum-principle argument of Theorem<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-producers>.
    All thresholds are stencil interiority thresholds or <math|N\<gtr\>k>,
    uniformly in <math|p>. Finitely many orientations remain in a bounded
    ball because their coefficient maps are fixed linear or conjugate-linear
    maps.

    Write <math|r=dim <around|(|V<rsub|e>/\<cal-I\><rsub|e>|)>> for the free
    quotient dimension, with the campaign identification understood. The
    exact calibrated isomorphism taking <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|e>> to
    <math|K<rsub|i>> gives <math|<math-up|rank><space|0.17em>\<Pi\><rsub|i>=r>
    at each finite cutoff; this does not use a limiting null-space assertion.
    If <math|r=0> the projection vanishes and there is nothing to prove.
    Otherwise choose the <math|r> independent free columns and finitely many
    separating rows used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-rank-purchases>.
    Their target matrix <math|G> is injective. In supremum norms choose
    <math|K> with <math|<around|\||c|\|>\<leqslant\>K*<around|\||G*c|\|>>. If
    every entry of <math|Q-G> is less than
    <math|\<delta\>=<around|(|2*K*r|)><rsup|-1>>, then
    <math|<around|\||c|\|>\<leqslant\>K\|Q*c\|+<tfrac|1|2><around|\||c|\|>>,
    hence <math|<around|\||c|\|>\<leqslant\>2*K*<around|\||Q*c|\|>>.

    The purchased comparison in that section holds before extraction: its
    corrected root error is <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|k>>, and undoing the
    finitely many connected subtractions costs the free-subset moment
    polynomial <math|P<rsub|k>> of <eqref|eq:rank-purchased-point-comparison>.
    At each fixed comparison level its rescaled lattice configurations are
    fixed before <math|N,p>; let <math|G<rsub|k>> be their free measurement
    matrix. These configurations converge to the prescribed generic
    configurations as <math|k> increases. Continuity of the finitely many
    free moments on a common separated neighborhood gives
    <math|G<rsub|k>\<to\>G> and bounds <math|P<rsub|k>>. Choose one
    sufficiently deep comparison level <math|k> so that, entrywise,
    <math|<around|\||G<rsub|k>-G|\|>\<less\>\<delta\>/8> and
    <math|P<rsub|k>*\<epsilon\><rsub|k>\<less\>\<delta\>/8>. Before either
    cutoff limit is taken, the finite-cell inequality applied to the paid
    corrected coefficient therefore puts the actual point measurement matrix
    within <math|\<delta\>/4> of <math|G>, beyond one mesh threshold for
    every period. This is the finite-cutoff estimate used here; its later
    passage to the ordered limits is not needed. Its lifts <math|u<rsub|j,i>>
    have one norm bound <math|B<rsub|k>> for all <math|N,p>, by
    <eqref|eq:rank-fixed-scale-bounded>. Their detector positions have a
    fixed interior margin in the comparison cell. Choose small closed
    neighborhoods of those positions inside the smallest admitted torus and
    the generic set. All coordinate gaps there are at least
    <math|4*\<rho\>\<gtr\>0> modulo every period: for the smallest period
    this follows by a compact minimum; larger periods cannot create a wrap
    inside this common interior box. Shrink the neighborhoods if necessary.

    The primary budgets just established give, on these neighborhoods, the
    uniform lattice modulus proved in the directional part of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-producers>:

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||H<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>-H<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>4*n*A<rsup|n>*\<rho\><rsup|-1>*<around|\||x-y|\|>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|n> is the arity of the detector row, all its labels lie in the
    fixed ball containing the lifts, and the centered depth is chosen with
    <math|a<rsub|m>\<less\>\<rho\>>. The proof uses the bounded primary
    bricks and the Schatten\UCauchy transfer estimate; it does not use a
    derivative-field bound. All gaps and primary budgets here are uniform
    over every period, so the displayed proof gives one mesh threshold for
    this neighborhood. The exact aligned detector sites represent the same
    physical centers once <math|N> exceeds their fixed depth.

    We specify the averaging operation, also to control derivatives. Choose
    <math|b\<gtr\>0> small enough and put
    <math|h<rsub|N>=<around|\<lceil\>|b/a<rsub|N>|\<rceil\>>>. Average a
    detector correlation over shifts <math|0,\<ldots\>,h<rsub|N>-1> in the
    distinguished slot, once for each direction in <math|l>. The total
    physical displacement is at most <math|2<around|\||l|\|>*b> eventually.
    Choose <math|b> so that this lies inside the neighborhoods and changes
    each primary detector entry by less than <math|\<delta\>/4>. For the
    empty word the averaging is the identity. These averages define
    <math|L<rsub|i>>. The sweep lemma applies to the bounded rows
    <math|<around|(|I-\<Pi\><rsub|i>|)>*u<rsub|j,i>>: both the rows and their
    projected parts are bounded uniformly, and thus belong to
    <math|<with|math-font|cal*|C><rsub|e>>. Planting with the other bounded
    detector rows bounds each averaged swept correlation by a constant times
    its vanishing root price, uniformly on these neighborhoods. Consequently
    the matrix <math|Q<rsub|i>=<around|(|L<rsub|i>*\<Pi\><rsub|i>*u<rsub|j,i>|)><rsub|j>>
    is within <math|\<delta\>> of <math|G> eventually in <math|I>.

    It follows that the <math|r> vectors <math|\<Pi\><rsub|i>*u<rsub|j,i>>
    are independent and therefore span <math|<math-up|ran><space|0.17em>\<Pi\><rsub|i>>.
    Write <math|\<Pi\><rsub|i>*v=<big|sum><rsub|j>c<rsub|j>*\<Pi\><rsub|i>*u<rsub|j,i>>.
    If <math|A> bounds <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Pi\><rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>>, then

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<Pi\><rsub|i>*v|\|>\<leqslant\>A*r*B<rsub|k><around|\||c|\|>\<leqslant\>2*A*r*B<rsub|k>*K*<around|\||L<rsub|i>*\<Pi\><rsub|i>*v|\|>.
    </equation*>

    Take <math|Z\<geqslant\>1> at least this last constant. This proves the
    detector bound on every vector, not merely on the purchased columns.

    For its assay properties, use the same separated neighborhoods and plant
    the distinguished trial with the bounded primary spectators in distinct
    cells. If its root price is <math|u<rsub|i>>, the result is bounded by
    <math|C*u<rsub|i>>, with <math|C> independent of <math|N,p> and the point
    in the neighborhood. Averages have total mass one, so this also proves
    assertion (2). For (1), move differences through the expectation by
    linearity. One box average telescopes a difference exactly:

    <\equation*>
      <frac|1|h<rsub|N>>*<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|h<rsub|N>-1><frac|H*<around|(|x+<around|(|j+1|)>*e<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>-H*<around|(|x+j*e<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>|a<rsub|N>>=<frac|H*<around|(|x+h<rsub|N>*e<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>-H<around|(|x|)>|h<rsub|N>*a<rsub|N>>.
    </equation*>

    The difference and averaging operators commute. Applying this identity
    once for each entry of <math|l> bounds the averaged derivative by
    <math|<around|(|2/b|)><rsup|<around|\||l|\|>>> times the undifferentiated
    correlation bound, since <math|h<rsub|N>*a<rsub|N>\<geqslant\>b>. That
    bound follows from the paid root budget of <math|F> and the bounded
    spectator budgets. All translated endpoints remain in the chosen
    neighborhoods. This proves (1) without estimating <math|D<rsub|l>*F>
    pointwise in advance.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|All-period payment for the actual difference
    trials><label|prop:fullmesh-selected-supplier>The retained public family
    supplies every coefficient, remainder, orientation and root-budget
    hypothesis of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-fullmesh-fixedbounds-producer>.
    Consequently that theorem's conclusion holds for its actual normalized
    insertions, with one mesh threshold valid for every period.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|F> be a bounded primary row, initially a fixed public label,
    and <math|l> a finite direction word. At grade
    <math|e=d+<around|\||l|\|>>, the encoding lemma gives
    <math|x\<in\><with|math-font|cal*|C><rsub|e>> and
    <math|E=O<around|(|x|)>-D<rsub|l>*F> with uniform vanishing root price.
    Apply the detector lemma at this grade and word. Its assay of
    <math|D<rsub|l>*F> is bounded and that of <math|E> tends to zero. Thus
    <math|<around|\||L<rsub|i>*x<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>c> eventually for
    some <math|c> independent of <math|p>.

    Put <math|y<rsub|i>=\<Pi\><rsub|i>*x<rsub|i>> and
    <math|z<rsub|i>=<around|(|1+<around|\||y<rsub|i>|\|>|)><rsup|-1>*<around|(|x<rsub|i>-y<rsub|i>|)>>.
    The first term in this subtraction belongs to
    <math|<with|math-font|cal*|C><rsub|e>> by its scalar closure, and the
    second is norm-bounded by one. Thus <math|z\<in\><with|math-font|cal*|C><rsub|e>>,
    and <math|\<Pi\><rsub|i>*z<rsub|i>=0>. The sweep and assay lemmas give
    <math|<around|\||L<rsub|i>*z<rsub|i>|\|>\<to\>0> in <math|I>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-fullmesh-cap> now gives
    <math|<around|\||y<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*Z*c+1> eventually. Changing
    <math|y<rsub|i>> to zero at earlier mesh cutoffs makes it a globally
    bounded row without affecting any eventual assertion.

    This bounded row has a uniform paid primary budget by the detector lemma.
    The difference <math|x-y> belongs to <math|<with|math-font|cal*|C><rsub|e>>
    and has exact projected kernel zero on the tail, so its insertion has a
    vanishing uniform root price. The exact identity

    <\equation*>
      D<rsub|l>*F=O<around|(|y|)>+O<around|(|x-y|)>-E
    </equation*>

    therefore gives a finite root budget for <math|D<rsub|l>*F>, at every
    sufficiently fine fixed cell depth. Each of its two vanishing prices is
    eventually at most one. The root triangle inequality gives the sum of the
    primary budget and two, with one common mesh threshold valid for all
    <math|p>. Apply the same argument to the finitely many orientations, or
    retain them in the finite lists throughout; take maxima only after fixing
    the cell depth. No limit of a moving projection is used.

    Finally the periodic difference formula is the definition of these trials
    and is exact also at wrapped sites. The planted-cell product inequality
    is the Wilson reflection-positive inequality of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-estimates>; its chamber
    interiority follows from the fixed finite stencils. These verify the
    remaining hypotheses of the full-mesh theorem. Its two-scale packing
    argument now applies to each finite list of actual insertions and
    difference directions. The use of full-mesh estimates has been confined
    throughout to uniformly subpower rows and uniform primary budgets; no
    assertion for arbitrary ordered subpower rows has been strengthened by
    changing its quantifiers.
  </proof>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|What this estimate supplies>The statement is the full-mesh
    separated-kernel estimate: each finite difference order and each positive
    separation have one eventual mesh threshold valid at all periods. The
    bounds at fixed period also control compact families of separated
    positions and their coordinate ties. The estimate does not itself give
    convergence of smeared correlations; that is the separate same-history
    convergence estimate. With both inputs, the positional theorem follows by
    the interpolation, compactness, and dominated-convergence argument in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-limits>.
  </remark>

  <subsection|From one-field purchases to the public comparison
  grid><label|ex-public-grill-producer>

  This section isolates the quantitative steps on the selected finite-volume
  route to the comparison grid. It proves the finite-frame price estimate,
  the radial decay once the trajectory bounds hold, and the realification and
  four-term assembly steps. The local approximation and separated-pair
  receipts are substantive upstream inputs to this finite assembly. Their
  construction from the paid selected history is given in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-phase-grid-approximants>.

  Write <math|a<rsub|j>> for the physical spacing at depth <math|j>,
  <math|c<rsub|j>> for the number of columns there, and
  <math|\<sigma\><rsub|j>> for its running scale. For a coefficient <math|L>
  of grade <math|d>, let <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|L|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>> be its
  coefficient norm in the finite frame. The price appearing in the
  construction is

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|j><around|(|L|)>=2*exp <around|(|\<ell\><rsub|j>|)><around|\<\|\|\>|L|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>> is the accumulated column logarithm. The
  rooted price of a block of side at depth <math|m>, compared with a fine
  observable at depth <math|j>, is

  <\equation*>
    S<rsub|m,j>=exp <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<around*|(|<frac|a<rsub|m>|a<rsub|j>>|)><rsup|4>*\<cal-T\><rsub|j>+5|)>,
  </equation*>

  with <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|j>> the vacuum treasury. This is the
  four-dimensional marked-copy estimate<nbsp><eqref|ex-centered-sail>. For
  depths <math|m\<leqslant\>j> the gain is the calibrated factor

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|gain><around|(|j,m|)>=e<rsup|<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|j>-\<sigma\><rsub|m>|)>/8>
  </equation*>

  of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-marked-portfolio-calibration>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Finite-frame recovery from approximate coordinate
    atoms><label|lem:ex-public-frame-recovery>Let <math|V> be a
    finite-dimensional complex coefficient space with coordinate norm
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>> and basis
    <math|<around|(|e<rsub|s>|)><rsub|s\<in\>I>>. Suppose a family
    <math|<around|(|y<rsub|s>|)><rsub|s\<in\>I>> satisfies
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|e<rsub|s>-y<rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>1/4>
    for every <math|s>. Define <math|A*v=<big|sum><rsub|s\<in\>I>v<rsub|s>*y<rsub|s>>.
    Then <math|A> is invertible and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsup|-1>*z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>
    for all <math|z\<in\>V>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For <math|v=<big|sum><rsub|s>v<rsub|s>*e<rsub|s>>, the triangle
    inequality gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|v-A*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>=<around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|s>v<rsub|s>*<around|(|e<rsub|s>-y<rsub|s>|)>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|s><around|\||v<rsub|s>|\|>*<around|\<\|\|\>|e<rsub|s>-y<rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|4><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    Consequently <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<geqslant\>3<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>/4>.
    Thus <math|A> is injective and, by finite dimensionality, surjective. The
    displayed inequality gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsup|-1>*z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>4<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>/3\<leqslant\>2<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Linear assembly preserves the local
    price><label|lem:ex-public-origin-sum>Fix a finite coefficient frame and
    a compact local region <math|W>. Suppose the complex basis approximants
    <math|Z<rsub|s>> satisfy

    <\equation*>
      sup<rsub|x><around|\||Z<rsub|s><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|0>,<space|2em>Z<rsub|s><text|
      is supported in >W,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsub|s>-f<rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|root>,m>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|0>*<space|1em><around|(|s\<in\>I|)>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|f<rsub|s>> are the finite-volume insertions corresponding to
    the frame columns. Let <math|\<cal-C\>> denote the centered
    chamber-interior test class; assume <math|Z<rsub|s>,f<rsub|s>,Z<rsub|s>-f<rsub|s>\<in\>\<cal-C\>>
    and, for finite combinations,

    <\equation*>
      \<rho\><space|-0.17em><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|s>t<rsub|s>*H<rsub|s>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|s><around|\||t<rsub|s>|\|>*\<rho\><around|(|H<rsub|s>|)>*<space|2em><around|(|H<rsub|s>\<in\>\<cal-C\>|)>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|\<rho\>> is the rooted test. If
    <math|L=<big|sum><rsub|s>t<rsub|s>*f<rsub|s>> with
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|s><around|\||t<rsub|s>|\|>\<leqslant\>K>, then

    <\equation*>
      Z=<big|sum><rsub|s>t<rsub|s>*Z<rsub|s>
    </equation*>

    is supported in <math|W>, belongs to <math|\<cal-C\>>, satisfies
    <math|sup<rsub|x><around|\||Z<around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|0>*K>,
    and

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|Z-L|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|root>,m>\<leqslant\>K*\<epsilon\><rsub|0>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Support is preserved under finite linear combinations. For every
    <math|x>, <math|<around|\||Z<around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|s><around|\||t<rsub|s>|\|><around|\||Z<rsub|s><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|0>*K>.
    Also <math|Z-L=<big|sum><rsub|s>t<rsub|s>*<around|(|Z<rsub|s>-f<rsub|s>|)>>,
    so subadditivity and homogeneity give the root bound. The same closure
    assumptions give <math|Z-L\<in\>\<cal-C\>>. No independence assumption is
    used: all rooted tests are evaluated in the same finite-volume Wilson
    law.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|A finite-depth real approximation
    receipt><label|prop:ex-public-origin>Fix grade <math|d>, a star-real
    coefficient <math|L>, a station depth <math|J\<leqslant\>N>, an opened
    intermediate depth <math|m>, and a fine depth <math|k\<less\>N> with
    <math|J\<leqslant\>m> and <math|C.*r*e*f<around|(|d|)>\<leqslant\>m\<leqslant\>k>.
    The centered block below is the block of the centered tiling at the
    station depth <math|J> (of physical side <math|a<rsub|J>>); it carries
    the support and the rooted test. The depth <math|m> enters only through
    the opening condition in item<nbsp>4, the scale inequality and the error
    <math|S<rsub|J,m>>. Assume the following selected-history data at these
    depths. The rooted quantity below is the centered root test of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-budget>, for the same
    finite-volume law and centered chamber. It is absolutely homogeneous:
    multiplying the observable by <math|t\<in\>\<bbb-C\>> multiplies each of
    the <math|M> copies, original or conjugated, by <math|t> or
    <math|<wide|t|\<bar\>>>, hence the product by a number of modulus
    <math|<around|\||t|\|><rsup|M>>, and the <math|M>th root by
    <math|<around|\||t|\|>>. We assume, in addition, its triangle inequality
    and its invariance under complex conjugation on the chamber-interior test
    class; these two properties are inputs here and are not proved in this
    section.

    <\enumerate>
      <item>each coordinate coefficient has a complex local approximation in
      the window of radius <math|R<rsub|d>+4>, with supremum bound
      <math|A<rsub|0>> and rooted error at most <math|S<rsub|J,m>/w>;

      <item>the finite-volume insertion columns span the requested insertion
      <math|L>, with coefficient sum at most <math|K<rsub|k><around|(|L|)>>,
      and the resulting complex sum <math|Z> lies in the centered chamber
      interior; that interior is preserved by the real-part projection used
      below;

      <item>transporting each local approximation from the fine depth
      <math|k> to the cutoff lattice preserves its support inside the
      centered station block, its supremum bound <math|A<rsub|0>>, and its
      rooted error bound;

      <item>the scales fit: the local window embeds in the block, the station
      block of depth <math|J> is open at depth <math|m>, and

      <\equation*>
        w<space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><rsub|m,d>|\<\|\|\>><frac|4*<around|(|1+c<rsub|k>|)>|<math-up|gain><around|(|k,m|)>>\<leqslant\><frac|\<zeta\><rsub|d,m>|2>,<space|2em>w\<gtr\>0,<space|1em>\<zeta\><rsub|d,m>\<gtr\>0.
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>

    Then there is a real field <math|B<rsub|N>> supported in the centered
    station block, uniformly bounded by <math|A<rsub|0>*K<rsub|k><around|(|L|)>>,
    whose rooted distance from the insertion <math|L> is at most

    <\equation*>
      K<rsub|k><around|(|L|)><space|0.17em><frac|S<rsub|J,m>|w>.
    </equation*>

    The same bound holds at every period once the station and scale
    inequalities hold there.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Use the representation and coefficient bound in item<nbsp>2. Transport
    the complex coordinate approximants from depth <math|k> to the cutoff
    lattice, inside the common station block; by item<nbsp>3 they keep the
    supremum bound <math|A<rsub|0>> and their rooted errors. Form their
    complex linear combination <math|Z>. The finite-sum lemma gives
    <math|<around|\||Z|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|0>*K<rsub|k><around|(|L|)>> and
    rooted error at most <math|K<rsub|k><around|(|L|)>*S<rsub|J,m>/w>. The
    exact selected-family reality identity says that the target insertion
    <math|U> is real when <math|L> is star-real. Put
    <math|B=<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>Z>. Then
    <math|<around|\||B|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||Z|\|>>, and
    <math|<math-up|paint><around|(|B|)>> has the same local support. The
    selected realification lemma, namely chamber-interior compatibility for
    averaging a field with its conjugate, gives its chamber interior. Since
    <math|U=<wide|U|\<bar\>>>,

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|paint><around|(|B|)>-U=<tfrac|1|2><around*|(|<around|(|Z-U|)>+<wide|<around|(|Z-U|)>|\<bar\>>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Conjugation preserves the rooted test, and its triangle inequality and
    homogeneity therefore give the same rooted error bound for <math|B>. This
    is the realification argument of the selected route, not an assumption
    that the complex coordinate approximants are themselves real. The support
    condition and scale inequality ensure that all summands are legal tests
    in one block. Uniformity in period follows from the common station and
    aligned physical block in the hypotheses.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Replacing a finite local word><label|lem:ex-public-replace-word>Let
    <math|\<cal-I\>> be a finite index set, <math|\<cal-T\>> a finite tiling,
    and <math|\<tau\><rsub|a>> its copy maps, indexed by the cells
    <math|a\<in\><math-up|Cell><around|(|\<cal-T\>|)>>. Assume each copy map
    is additive. Let <math|\<mu\>> be a probability law and <math|\<rho\>>
    the same nonnegative rooted test on all functions considered. Let
    <math|a:\<cal-I\>\<to\><math-up|Cell><around|(|\<cal-T\>|)>> be
    injective. Suppose <math|X<rsub|i>,Y<rsub|i>> lie in a linear local test
    class that is closed under cell copies, and suppose all finite products
    below are integrable.

    <\equation*>
      \<rho\><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>M,<space|2em>\<rho\><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>M,<space|2em>\<rho\>*<around|(|Y<rsub|i>-X<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>*<space|1em><around|(|i\<in\>\<cal-I\>|)>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|M,\<delta\>\<geqslant\>0>. Assume the planted-product
    estimate

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<big|int><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-I\>>\<tau\><rsub|a<around|(|i|)>><around|(|Z<rsub|i>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-I\>>\<rho\><around|(|Z<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation*>

    for every family in this local test class. If
    <math|<around|\||\<cal-I\>|\|>=n\<geqslant\>1>, then

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<big|int><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-I\>>\<tau\><rsub|a<around|(|i|)>><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\>-<big|int><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-I\>>\<tau\><rsub|a<around|(|i|)>><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\>|\|>\<leqslant\>n*\<delta\>*M<rsup|n-1>.
    </equation*>

    For <math|n=0> the two empty products agree and the difference is zero.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Order the finite index set as <math|i<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,i<rsub|n>>, and
    define <math|Z<rsup|<around|(|r|)>>> to use <math|Y<rsub|i<rsub|s>>> for
    <math|s\<leqslant\>r> and <math|X<rsub|i<rsub|s>>> for <math|s\<gtr\>r>.
    Then the difference of the two products is the sum, for
    <math|r=1,\<ldots\>,n>, of the product with
    <math|\<tau\><rsub|a<around|(|i<rsub|r>|)>>*<around|(|Y<rsub|i<rsub|r>>-X<rsub|i<rsub|r>>|)>>
    in position <math|r> and the hybrid factors in all other positions. The
    copy operation is additive, including its reflected cases, so this is an
    exact telescoping identity. Each hybrid family remains in the local test
    class. The planted-product estimate bounds the absolute value of the
    <math|r>th integral by <math|\<delta\>*M<rsup|n-1>>: the changed factor
    costs at most <math|\<delta\>> and each of the other <math|n-1> factors
    costs at most <math|M>. Summing these <math|n> bounds proves the claim.
  </proof>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|The selected pair receipt><label|rem:ex-public-layout-receipt>This
    is the quantitative replacement step used to turn centered single-word
    receipts into whole-word product receipts. Its local inputs are: for each
    copy and each mesh/period in the fused late set, the exact insertion
    <math|X<rsub|i>> and real prepared insertion <math|Y<rsub|i>> lie in the
    centered chamber-interior test class, both rooted costs are at most a
    common <math|M>, their difference costs at most
    <math|<math-up|costHint><around|(|k|)>>, and the prepared field has a
    finite supremum bound. The finite-dimensional spin frame is absorbed by
    summing its centered and purchase budgets. For an injective list of
    slots, the measure's planted inequality is applied to each telescoping
    term above. Thus the word-product discrepancy is bounded by
    <math|n<space|0.17em><math-up|costHint><around|(|k|)>*M<rsup|n-1>>,
    uniformly over the finite grid positions; concatenating two finite lists
    gives the four pair receipts used in the reflection-difference assembly.
    The selected proof still needs the origin receipts that feed the centered
    single-word estimates, but the many-factor replacement estimate itself is
    proved here.
  </remark>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The radial choice makes the coefficient price
    affordable><label|lem:ex-public-radial-choice>Fix <math|d,m,J> and
    <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>. Suppose the scale schedule satisfies

    <\equation*>
      \<sigma\><rsub|j>\<longrightarrow\>+\<infty\>,<space|2em>\<ell\><rsub|j>=o<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|j>|)>,<space|2em>log
      <around|(|1+c<rsub|j>|)>=o<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|j>|)>,<space|2em><math-up|gain><around|(|j,m|)><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|m>*e<rsup|-\<gamma\>*<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|j>-\<sigma\><rsub|m>|)>>
    </equation*>

    for some <math|\<gamma\>\<gtr\>0>. Assume
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><rsub|m,d>|\<\|\|\>>\<gtr\>0>,
    <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,m>\<gtr\>0>, and
    <math|<math-up|gain><around|(|j,m|)>\<gtr\>0>. Let <math|w<rsub|j>> be
    the positive allowance

    <\equation*>
      w<rsub|j>=<frac|\<zeta\><rsub|d,m><math-up|gain><around|(|j,m|)>|8<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><rsub|m,d>|\<\|\|\>>*<around|(|1+c<rsub|j>|)>>.
    </equation*>

    If <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|L|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>> and
    <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,m><rsup|-1>> are fixed, then for all sufficiently
    large <math|j>,

    <\equation*>
      K<rsub|j><around|(|L|)><space|0.17em><frac|S<rsub|J,m>|w<rsub|j>>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For fixed <math|d,m,J,L>, all factors except <math|exp
    <around|(|\<ell\><rsub|j>|)>>, <math|1+c<rsub|j>>, and
    <math|<math-up|gain><around|(|j,m|)><rsup|-1>> are constant in <math|j>;
    the remaining treasury ratio in <math|S<rsub|J,m>> is fixed at the chosen
    <math|m,J>. By the first two hypotheses, for any <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>
    both <math|exp <around|(|\<ell\><rsub|j>|)>> and <math|1+c<rsub|j>> are
    eventually at most <math|exp <around|(|\<eta\>*\<sigma\><rsub|j>|)>>.
    Choose <math|\<eta\>\<less\>\<gamma\>/3>. The gain factor is bounded by a
    constant times <math|e<rsup|-\<gamma\>*\<sigma\><rsub|j>>>, after
    absorbing <math|e<rsup|\<gamma\>*\<sigma\><rsub|m>>> into that constant.
    The product is thus bounded by a constant times
    <math|e<rsup|-<around|(|\<gamma\>-2*\<eta\>|)>*\<sigma\><rsub|j>>>, which
    tends to zero. This gives the claimed threshold.
  </proof>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|The schedule hypotheses><label|rem:ex-public-radial-schedule>For
    the calibrated gain the last hypothesis of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-public-radial-choice> holds with equality,
    <math|C<rsub|m>=1> and <math|\<gamma\>=1/8>. The first two are
    consequences of a logarithmic-cost bound on the schedule, of the kind
    used in Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|ex-column-subpower>. Suppose that
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>=<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>j>log c<rsub|i+1>>, that
    every column count satisfies <math|c<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1>, that
    <math|\<sigma\><rsub|i+1>-\<sigma\><rsub|i>=log L<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> with
    <math|L<rsub|i>> the block side, and that there are numbers
    <math|h<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>> with <math|log
    c<rsub|i+1>\<leqslant\>h<rsub|i><rsup|-2>*log L<rsub|i>>. Given
    <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, choose <math|k> with
    <math|h<rsub|i><rsup|-2>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>/2> for
    <math|i\<geqslant\>k>; telescoping gives
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|k>+<tfrac|\<epsilon\>|2><around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|j>-\<sigma\><rsub|k>|)>>
    for <math|j\<geqslant\>k>, and <math|\<ell\><rsub|k>-<tfrac|\<epsilon\>|2>\<sigma\><rsub|k>\<leqslant\><tfrac|\<epsilon\>|2>\<sigma\><rsub|j>>
    eventually because <math|\<sigma\><rsub|j>\<to\>\<infty\>>. Thus
    <math|0\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>*\<sigma\><rsub|j>>
    eventually, that is, <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>=o<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|j>|)>>.
    Since <math|c<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1>, <math|log
    <around|(|1+c<rsub|j>|)>\<leqslant\>log 2+log c<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>log
    2+\<ell\><rsub|j>>, so also <math|log
    <around|(|1+c<rsub|j>|)>=o<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|j>|)>>. That the
    selected schedule satisfies the logarithmic-cost bound with its column
    counts included, and diverges, is an input of this section.
  </remark>

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Conditional assembly of the public comparison
    grid><label|thm:ex-public-grill>Fix a finite list of star-real tokens
    <math|v<rsub|i>>, that is, tokens whose label is fixed by complex
    conjugation, <math|v<rsub|i><rsup|\<ast\>>=v<rsub|i>>, and a generic grid
    of pairwise separated positions <math|x<rsub|i>>. Write <math|H<rsub|i>>
    and <math|R<rsub|i>> for the exact unreflected and reflected whole-word
    insertions at common volume, period, and translation; reflection acts on
    both labels and positions. For each approximation index <math|k>, suppose
    there are real measurable approximants <math|F<rsub|k,N,p,t,i>> and
    <math|G<rsub|k,N,p,t,i>> to the unreflected and reflected insertions.
    Assume:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|A<around|(|k|)>\<geqslant\>0>,
      <math|\<delta\><around|(|k|)>\<geqslant\>0>, and
      <math|\<delta\><around|(|k|)>\<to\>0>. For each fixed <math|k,t,i>,
      eventually in the fused mesh/period filter <math|f.<math-up|fuse>>,
      <math|F<rsub|k,N,p,t,i>> and <math|G<rsub|k,N,p,t,i>> are measurable
      and bounded in absolute value by the common reserve
      <math|A<around|(|k|)>>.

      <item>For each <math|k> and each <math|i\<less\>j>, there is a
      translation threshold such that for every <math|t> beyond it,
      eventually in <math|f.<math-up|late>> in <math|<around|(|N,p|)>>, the
      four approximating products and their target products are integrable
      and their integrals differ by at most <math|\<delta\><around|(|k|)>>:

      <\align*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int>F<rsub|i>*F<rsub|j>>|<cell|\<leftrightarrow\><big|int><math-up|Re><around|(|H<rsub|i>*H<rsub|j>|)>,>|<cell|<big|int>F<rsub|i>*G<rsub|j>>|<cell|\<leftrightarrow\><big|int><math-up|Re><around|(|H<rsub|i>*R<rsub|j>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|<big|int>G<rsub|i>*F<rsub|j>>|<cell|\<leftrightarrow\><big|int><math-up|Re><around|(|R<rsub|i>*H<rsub|j>|)>,>|<cell|<big|int>G<rsub|i>*G<rsub|j>>|<cell|\<leftrightarrow\><big|int><math-up|Re><around|(|R<rsub|i>*R<rsub|j>|)>.>>>>
      </align*>

      The arrows mean that the absolute difference of the two real numbers is
      at most <math|\<delta\><around|(|k|)>>.
    </enumerate>

    Then the real plots <math|P<rsub|k,N,p,t,i>=F<rsub|k,N,p,t,i>-G<rsub|k,N,p,t,i>>,
    with reserve <math|2*A<around|(|k|)>> and error schedule
    <math|4*\<delta\><around|(|k|)>>, define a covering satisfying the exact
    Works quantifiers: controls hold eventually in <math|f.<math-up|fuse>>
    for each fixed <math|k,t,i>; and for every <math|k> and
    <math|i\<less\>j>, its pair product differs from the bill

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><math-up|Re><around*|[|<around|(|H<rsub|i>-R<rsub|i>|)>*<around|(|H<rsub|j>-R<rsub|j>|)>|]>
    </equation*>

    by at most <math|4*\<delta\><around|(|k|)>> eventually in
    <math|f.<math-up|late>> for every sufficiently large translation
    <math|t>. Thus <math|k> is the approximation index whose error tends to
    zero; it is not the extraction index. This is a conditional finite
    assembly statement, not a proof of the four local pair receipts.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Put <math|P<rsub|i>=F<rsub|i>-G<rsub|i>>. The bound
    <math|<around|\||P<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||F<rsub|i>|\|>+<around|\||G<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*A<around|(|k|)>>
    proves the reserve assertion. The identity

    <\equation*>
      P<rsub|i>*P<rsub|j>=F<rsub|i>*F<rsub|j>-F<rsub|i>*G<rsub|j>-G<rsub|i>*F<rsub|j>+G<rsub|i>*G<rsub|j>
    </equation*>

    is an exact identity of real functions. The target bill has the same
    four-term expansion after taking real parts of the exact complex products
    <math|H<rsub|i>*H<rsub|j>,H<rsub|i>*R<rsub|j>,R<rsub|i>*H<rsub|j>,R<rsub|i>*R<rsub|j>>.
    By the four assumed receipts and the triangle inequality, the difference
    of the two integrals is at most <math|4*\<delta\><around|(|k|)>>. The
    common late set exists because there are finitely many controls and four
    receipts per pair. The translation threshold is chosen first, followed by
    the mesh/period late set. This is precisely the order of quantifiers in
    Works.
  </proof>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Selected-route boundary>The public endpoint reaches this
    construction through the selected run's history records, then uses the
    finite-dimensional insertion recovery, a finite real sum, a radial choice
    of the intermediate depth, and the four-term reflection expansion. The
    arguments above prove the coefficient recovery, finite-sum estimates,
    radial decay under the displayed scale bounds, and the final four-term
    assembly. The selected-history receipts supplied by
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-phase-grid-approximants> are: the
    real local coordinate approximations, their rooted one-field errors, the
    back-transport support and price estimate, and the separated pair
    integral estimates. These are the substantive content of the history
    lemmas producing the local approximants and their served pair bounds;
    this section does not treat their names alone as proofs. The public
    theorem also separately requires same-history full-mesh convergence for
    positioned correlations. This chapter supplies the real grid estimate on
    its own hypotheses and does not identify it with that convergence
    theorem.
  </remark>

  <subsection|The selected one-scale linear
  interpolation><label|ex-selected-linear-interpolation>

  We next expand the finite construction that turns the one-column coupons
  into one coherent coarser coefficient family. It is essential that the
  coupons be combined before the coefficient is chosen: choosing separately
  for each label would not give a single linear coefficient family, and the
  next scale's cone estimate would not apply. Let <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|m>>
  denote the actual fine-to-coarse density map,
  <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|m>*f=L<rsup|d><math-up|push><rsub|m><space|0.17em>f>
  on grade <math|d>, where <math|L> is the block scale and
  <math|<math-up|push><rsub|m>> integrates against the selected transition
  kernel. It is integration against the selected transition kernel, so its
  linearity on the displayed integrable columns follows from the
  probability-kernel identity.

  Let <math|F> be the finite coefficient frame, <math|V> a coefficient family
  at the fine scale, and write <math|I> for its invertible coefficient
  readout. Assume the admissible identities hold:

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(H1)>the played coefficient has <math|x>-coordinate
    <math|I<around|(|v|)>>;

    <item*|(H2)>its density is a complex-linear function of <math|v>, and the
    density obeys the stated motion covariance;

    <item*|(H3)>its charge is bounded by <math|s<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>I<around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
    where <math|s> is the fine column count, and is subadditive in <math|v>;

    <item*|(H4)>the readout intertwines the motions:
    <math|I*<around|(|g*v|)>=g*I<around|(|v|)>> for every motion
    <math|g\<in\>\<cal-M\>> defined below, including the conjugation flag;

    <item*|(H5)>the coefficient, regular and forced fields of
    <math|V<around|(|v|)>> are complex-linear in <math|v>, and the supports
    of the regular atoms of <math|V<around|(|v|)>> do not depend on <math|v>;

    <item*|(H6)>the readout maps the physical unit at the cutoff to the unit
    at the coarse scale: <math|I<around|(|u<rsub|N>|)>=u<rsub|m+1>>, where
    <math|u<rsub|m>=a<rsub|m><rsup|d>*e<rsub|0>>.
  </enumerate>

  Hypotheses (H4)\U(H6) are used in the proof of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-selected-interpolation> below: (H4) to
  identify the recombined coefficient with <math|V<around|(|v|)>> and for
  covariance of the new readout, (H5) to recover the exponential entry and
  forced profile of <math|V<around|(|v|)>> from those of the basis columns,
  and (H6) for the unit statement. For the profile conclusions below assume
  the coefficient law is sound under local reorientation, the forced profiles
  transform covariantly, and the local evaluation is nonzero on every
  configuration. Density covariance alone does not imply covariance of the
  exponential entry. Let <math|L> be the block scale and <math|T> the
  selected heading. Set

  <\equation*>
    D<around|(|z|)>=<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|vowel><rsub|L><around|(|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>z|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,<space|1em>c=C+\<eta\>*s,<space|1em>\<epsilon\>=\<eta\>*<around|(|1+s|)>,<space|1em>R<rsub|F>=<math-up|orbitCost><around|(|F|)>.
  </equation*>

  The frame has basis <math|<around|(|e<rsub|j>|)><rsub|j\<in\>J>> and finite
  motion set <math|\<cal-M\>=<math-up|Signed>\<times\><around|{|0,1|}>>; the
  second coordinate is the independent conjugation flag. Write
  <math|\<sigma\><rsub|g><around|(|c|)>=c> or <math|<wide|c|\<bar\>>>
  according to that flag, and <math|g*z> for the corresponding coefficient
  action. The exact repainting coefficient is

  <\equation*>
    \<chi\><rsub|g,j><around|(|z|)>=<around|\||\<cal-M\>|\|><rsup|-1>*\<sigma\><rsub|g><around|(|<around|(|g<rsup|-1>*z|)><rsub|j>|)>,<space|2em>R<rsub|F>=1+<big|sum><rsub|g\<in\>\<cal-M\>><big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>J><around|\<\|\|\>|g<around|(|e<rsub|j>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  It is complex-linear: applying <math|g<rsup|-1>> to <math|c*z> contributes
  <math|\<sigma\><rsub|g<rsup|-1>><around|(|c|)>>, and the outer
  <math|\<sigma\><rsub|g>> changes it back to <math|c>. For fixed <math|g>,
  coordinate expansion gives

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|j>\<chi\><rsub|g,j><around|(|z|)>*g<around|(|e<rsub|j>|)>=<around|\||\<cal-M\>|\|><rsup|-1>*z;
  </equation*>

  summing over <math|g> proves exact reconstruction. The weighted bounds used
  below are stronger than the unweighted reconstruction bound. For
  <math|h<around|(|z|)>=<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>
  or <math|h<around|(|z|)>=<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|vowel><rsub|L><around|(|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>z|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
  coordinate expansion gives

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|g,j><around|\||\<chi\><rsub|g,j><around|(|z|)>|\|>*h<around|(|e<rsub|j>|)>=<around|\||\<cal-M\>|\|><rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|g>h*<around|(|g<rsup|-1>*z|)>.
  </equation*>

  For each fixed motion, the weighted coordinate sum is exactly
  <math|h*<around|(|g<rsup|-1>*z|)>>, by expanding in the frame basis; the
  core and grade weights commute with motion. The finite patch-cost estimate
  gives <math|h*<around|(|g<rsup|-1>*z|)>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|F>*h<around|(|z|)>>,
  and therefore yields

  <\equation>
    <big|sum><rsub|g,j><around|\||\<chi\><rsub|g,j><around|(|z|)>|\|>*h<around|(|e<rsub|j>|)>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|F>*h<around|(|z|)>.<label|eq:ex-motion-weighted>
  </equation>

  This weighted identity also explains why one must include the motion cost:
  a motion need not preserve the frame's <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>> norm. The
  same coefficient formula with <math|h<around|(|z|)>=<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>
  gives the unweighted budget used for finite-wing additions:

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|g,j><around|\||\<chi\><rsub|g,j><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|F><around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,
  </equation*>

  by the same finite patch-cost estimate. Assume each grade weight in
  <math|<math-up|vowel><rsub|L>> lies in <math|<around|[|1,L<rsup|d>|]>>;
  this is the grading convention used below. The selected purchase
  inequalities are

  <\equation>
    C\<geqslant\>1,<space|1em>\<eta\>\<geqslant\>0,<space|1em>\<eta\>*<around|(|1+s|)>*R<rsub|F>\<leqslant\><math-up|barunit><around|(|L|)>,<space|1em><around|(|C+\<eta\>*s|)>*R<rsub|F>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>.<label|eq:ex-interpolation-buy>
  </equation>

  For each basis vector <math|e<rsub|j>>, set
  <math|v<rsub|j>=I<rsup|-1>*e<rsub|j>>. The fine played column
  <math|V<around|(|v<rsub|j>|)>> is formatted and integrable, and its
  remainder is bounded by its charge. The selected one-column purchase record
  has three clauses: the principal insertion when <math|j\<ne\>0>, every
  regular remainder atom with its support and supremum bounds, and every
  forced atom with its support-mass bound. The forced clause applies to every
  forced slot of the funded wing at which the insertion function is nonzero;
  the keeper condition places every such slot in the funded wing. The unit
  coordinate uses the exact vacuum coupon. The density has an absolutely
  convergent principal-plus-regular-plus-forced expansion: the regular and
  forced majorants are dominated by the coefficient envelope and remainder
  bound. For each component, the corresponding purchase clause gives a
  coupon. The coupon merger sums their densities and profiles termwise;
  Tonelli applies to the nonnegative majorants, and each scalar cost sum is
  bounded by the given purchase budget. We use the merger in the following
  form, which this chapter does not prove: for a countable family of
  formatted coefficients <math|P<rsub|i>> with the same vacuum, each with
  remainder bound <math|t<rsub|i>> at a common positive allowance, with
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>t<rsub|i>\<less\>\<infty\>> and
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|i>.*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<less\>\<infty\>>,
  the merged coefficient (whose lead is the absolutely convergent sum
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>P<rsub|i>.*x>, whose regular atoms are those of the
  <math|P<rsub|i>>, kept separately, and whose forced coefficient at each
  address is <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>P<rsub|i>.<math-up|forced>>) is
  formatted, has remainder bound <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>t<rsub|i>>, and its
  forced profile is the sum of the forced profiles of the <math|P<rsub|i>>.
  The summable remainder budgets control the regular and forced series; they
  do not by themselves control the leads. For the density identity the same
  lawful representation and the required dominated, measurable density
  expansion are also retained. The merger, including the coefficient
  remainder bound and the Tale witnesses, is proved in
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-coefficient-merger>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-coupon-merger>, under the summability and
  dominated density hypotheses just stated.

  In the column application, let <math|u<rsub|i>> be the predicted lead and
  let <math|x<rsub|i>> be the actual lead chosen by the component coupon, in
  each of the finitely many required motion orientations. The separate
  lead-price receipt must give <math|<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<less\>\<infty\>>
  and summable nonnegative errors <math|c<rsub|i>> with
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|i>-u<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|i>>.
  Then the triangle inequality gives <math|<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
  The predicted family has one principal lead
  <math|T<around|(|L|)>*e<rsub|j>> and zero leads for the regular and forced
  components, so its lead sum is finite; the summable error and remainder
  prices are separate requirements on the purchased coupons. Subject to these
  receipts and the merger of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-coupon-merger>,
  this gives the basis coupon with predicted lead
  <math|T<around|(|L|)>*e<rsub|j>> and the lead-error budget displayed below.
  Before simplification its three budgets are

  <\equation*>
    \<eta\>*<around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>e<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>+V.<math-up|charge><around|(|v<rsub|j>|)>|)>,<space|1em>C*D<around|(|e<rsub|j>|)>+\<eta\>*V.<math-up|charge><around|(|v<rsub|j>|)>,<space|1em>t+2*V.<math-up|charge><around|(|v<rsub|j>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Each paid column receipt controls its lead after every motion: the
  transported coefficient <math|X<rsub|g,j>> satisfies
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|g,j>-T<around|(|L|)>*<around|(|g*e<rsub|j>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>e<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>
  for all <math|g,j>. This is the all-motion clause in the one-column
  payment; no norm-isometry of a motion is used. The admissible charge
  estimate gives <math|V.<math-up|charge><around|(|v<rsub|j>|)>\<leqslant\>s<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>e<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>;
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>e<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>1>
  and <math|D<around|(|e<rsub|j>|)>\<geqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>e<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>.
  Hence the budgets are at most <math|\<epsilon\><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>e<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
  <math|c*D<around|(|e<rsub|j>|)>>, and <math|t+2*s>. The marked
  coefficient/profile payment is proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:projection-payment-producer>. The forced
  component is paid there after the forced allowance is at most one. The
  selected primary and regular-card comparisons are supplied by
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:primary-calibration-producer> and
  <reference|sec:regular-principal-producer>, assembled on the retained
  transition in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:three-port-history>.
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-reprise> attaches these component
  estimates to the stored doors under the common selected-history purchase
  hypotheses. Every component here is evaluated under one background law and
  one transition kernel.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The paid one-step cone used in the
    interpolation><label|lem:ex-selected-cone-step>Let <math|K\<geqslant\>1>,
    <math|s\<geqslant\>1>, <math|L\<geqslant\>1>, and put
    <math|q=L<rsup|1/8>>, <math|e=q<rsup|-3>>. Suppose

    <\equation*>
      100000*D<rsup|6>*<around|(|1+s|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>q,<space|1em>K\<leqslant\>D,<space|1em>64*D<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|->,<space|1em>64*D<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|+>.
    </equation*>

    Let <math|B> be an invertible linear map with both <math|B> and
    <math|B<rsup|-1>> of <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>> norm at most <math|K>,
    commuting with deletion of the unit coordinate. For vectors <math|u,v>
    set <math|y=<math-up|vowel><rsub|L><around|(|v|)>> and assume

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|u-B*y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>e*<around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>+t|)>,<space|2em>r\<leqslant\>K\<\|\|\><math-up|core><space|0.17em>y\<\|\|\><rsub|1>+e*t,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>s<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    Then

    <\equation*>
      r\<leqslant\>c<rsub|-><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|+><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|+>*L<rsup|d><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The bank inequality, <math|K\<leqslant\>D>, and <math|K,D\<geqslant\>1>
    imply

    <\equation*>
      2*K*e*<around|(|1+s|)>=<frac|2*K*<around|(|1+s|)>|q<rsup|3>>\<leqslant\>1.
    </equation*>

    Indeed the assumed lower bound on <math|q> is at least
    <math|2*K*<around|(|1+s|)>>, and <math|q\<geqslant\>1>. The error bound
    and <math|t\<leqslant\>s<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>
    therefore give

    <\equation*>
      2*K*<around|\<\|\|\>|u-B*y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,
    </equation*>

    where the second inequality is the coordinatewise fact that all
    grade-weights in <math|<math-up|vowel><rsub|L>> are at least one. The
    elementary perturbation estimate for <math|B> now gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*K<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*K<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    Apply the same estimate after deleting the unit coordinate. The map
    <math|B> commutes with that deletion and deletion is contractive, so the
    same error bound holds for <math|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>u-B<around|(|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>y|)>>.
    Thus

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*K<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    Also

    <\equation*>
      e*t\<leqslant\>e*<around|(|1+s|)><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,
    </equation*>

    since <math|2*K*e*<around|(|1+s|)>\<leqslant\>1> and
    <math|2*K\<geqslant\>1>. Hence

    <\equation*>
      r\<leqslant\>2*K<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>4*K<rsup|2><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|-><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    Finally, the grade weights also satisfy
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>L<rsup|d><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>.
    Consequently

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*K<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|+><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,<space|1em><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*K<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*K*L<rsup|d><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|+>*L<rsup|d><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    The two room assumptions imply the needed
    <math|4*K<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|->> and
    <math|2*K\<leqslant\>c<rsub|+>>.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Common linear interpolation of the purchased
    columns><label|prop:ex-selected-interpolation>Assume
    <math|s\<geqslant\>1>, <math|L\<geqslant\>1>, and <math|t\<geqslant\>0>.
    Write the heading as <math|T<around|(|L|)>=B\<circ\><math-up|vowel><rsub|L>>,
    where <math|B> commutes with core deletion and
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|B|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,<around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>D<rsub|0>\<assign\>K<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>>.
    Assume the bank and room bounds

    <\equation*>
      100000*D<rsub|0><rsup|6>*<around|(|1+s|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>L<rsup|1/8>,<space|2em>64*D<rsub|0><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|->,<space|1em>64*D<rsub|0><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|+>,
    </equation*>

    with <math|<math-up|barunit><around|(|L|)>=L<rsup|-3/8>> and the purchase
    inequalities <eqref|eq:ex-interpolation-buy>. These are exactly the
    scalar hypotheses of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-selected-cone-step>,
    with <math|K=D=D<rsub|0>>. Assume that the fine coefficient family
    <math|V> satisfies the admissible identities (H1)\U(H6) and the profile
    hypotheses stated before <eqref|eq:ex-interpolation-buy>. The basis
    coupons have their stated exact density/profile identities, summable
    remainder and lead-error prices as above, and a common gauge door. Assume
    the merger of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-coupon-merger> applies
    to these component coupons. Their wings are the motion images of the
    common funded wing. There is a coarser coefficient family <math|Q> with:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>every <math|Q<around|(|v|)>> is formatted and has remainder bound
      <math|a<around|(|v|)>=c*R<rsub|F>*D*<around|(|I*v|)>> at the coarse
      forced allowance;

      <item>its readout <math|I<rsub|Q><around|(|v|)>=Q<around|(|v|)>.*x> is
      a linear isomorphism;

      <item>at the centered anchor, the exact transition identity
      <math|Q<around|(|v|)>.<math-up|density>=\<cal-T\><rsub|m><around|(|V<around|(|v|)>.<math-up|density>|)>>
      holds;

      <item>its lead discrepancy satisfies

      <\equation*>
        <around|\<\|\|\>|Q<around|(|v|)>.*x-T<around|(|L|)>*I<around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>*R<rsub|F><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>I<around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>;
      </equation*>

      <item>the finite-wing witness for the exponential entry and forced
      profile of <math|V<around|(|v|)>> has budgets

      <\equation*>
        R<rsub|F><around|\<\|\|\>|I<around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>*<around|(|t+2*s|)><space|1em><text|and><space|1em>a<around|(|v|)>,
      </equation*>

      respectively.
    </enumerate>

    The coefficient, regular, and forced fields are complex-linear in
    <math|v>. The density and readout obey motion covariance, while the
    forced profile and exponential entry obey the corresponding covariance
    after reindexing their address dictionaries; the conjugation flag is
    independent of orientation.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    For each basis coordinate put <math|v<rsub|j>=I<rsup|-1>*e<rsub|j>>. Its
    played coefficient has <math|x>-coordinate <math|e<rsub|j>> by the
    admissible identity. Let <math|Z<rsub|j>> be the coefficient from its
    elementary coupon. For each motion <math|g>, the transition slice has a
    move taking the coarse origin to the required fine anchor. Reorient
    <math|Z<rsub|j>> by that move and, on the conjugating branch, apply
    complex conjugation to all coefficient fields. The centered transport
    identities give a coefficient <math|Z<rsub|g,j>> with

    <\equation*>
      Z<rsub|g,j>.*x=g*<around|(|Z<rsub|j>.*x|)>,<space|2em>Z<rsub|g,j>.<math-up|density>=g<space|-0.17em><around*|(|\<cal-T\><rsub|m><around|(|V<around|(|v<rsub|j>|)>.<math-up|density>|)>|)>,
    </equation*>

    with the same remainder and two Tale budgets. The remainder assertion
    follows from the turned tab estimate and the coupon's future bound; the
    finite wing is carried to its image under the move. Soundness of the
    transition door gives the common transport, and the side condition
    <math|2\<leqslant\>n<rsub|m>> makes every move admissible on the selected
    schedule. This explains the coordinate, format/remainder, density, and
    wing transport used below.

    Index these transported coefficients by the finite set

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-I\>=<around|{|<around|(|g,j|)>:g<text| a signed motion>,j<text| a
      frame coordinate>|}>.
    </equation*>

    For a vector <math|v>, assign weight

    <\equation*>
      w<rsub|v>*<around|(|g,j|)>=\<chi\><rsub|g,j>*<around|(|I*v|)>.
    </equation*>

    The finite orbit coefficients are the averaging/rebasing coefficients;
    their identity is the finite reconstruction formula

    <\equation>
      I*v=<big|sum><rsub|<around|(|g,j|)>\<in\>\<cal-I\>>w<rsub|v>*<around|(|g,j|)>*<space|0.17em>g<around|(|e<rsub|j>|)>.<label|eq:ex-interpolation-chips>
    </equation>

    Define <math|Q<around|(|v|)>> by summing the transported coefficients
    with these weights, using the disjoint-address merge that retains their
    support and label data. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:ex-interpolation-chips>
    makes the operation complex-linear in <math|v>. Since the index set is
    finite, there is no limiting argument in this assembly.

    The <math|x>-coordinate is the weighted sum of the transported column
    coordinates. Its leading part is reconstructed by the finite motion
    average, since <math|T<around|(|L|)>> intertwines the motion action. For
    the error use the paid all-motion estimate and
    <eqref|eq:ex-motion-weighted> with <math|h<around|(|z|)>=<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>:

    <\equation*>
      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|Q<around|(|v|)>.*x-T<around|(|L|)>*I*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>|<cell|\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|g,j><around|\||\<chi\><rsub|g,j>*<around|(|I*v|)>|\|>*<space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|g,j>-T<around|(|L|)>*<around|(|g*e<rsub|j>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>*<big|sum><rsub|g,j><around|\||\<chi\><rsub|g,j>*<around|(|I*v|)>|\|><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>e<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>*R<rsub|F>*<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>I*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>.>>>>
      </aligned>
    </equation*>

    Each transported remainder has price <math|c*D<around|(|e<rsub|j>|)>>.
    Summing its absolute coefficient-weighted price and using
    <eqref|eq:ex-motion-weighted> with <math|h<around|(|z|)>=<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|vowel><rsub|L><around|(|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>z|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>
    gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|g,j><around|\||\<chi\><rsub|g,j>*<around|(|I*v|)>|\|>*c*D<around|(|e<rsub|j>|)>\<leqslant\>c*R<rsub|F>*D*<around|(|I*v|)>.
    </equation*>

    The finite coefficient merge preserves formatting and adds these
    remainder budgets, proving item 1.

    For the density, linearity of the transition integral is justified by the
    integrability of each played column. Motion covariance of the admissible
    density identifies the transported coupon density with the corresponding
    motion of the transitioned basis density, so that
    <math|Z<rsub|g,j>.<math-up|density>=\<cal-T\><rsub|m>*<around|(|V*<around|(|g*v<rsub|j>|)>.<math-up|density>|)>>.
    By (H4), <math|g*v<rsub|j>=g*I<rsup|-1>*e<rsub|j>=I<rsup|-1>*<around|(|g*e<rsub|j>|)>>.
    Sum with the weights in <eqref|eq:ex-motion-weighted> and use the
    linearity of the density (H2) and of <math|I<rsup|-1>>: the result is
    <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|m>> applied to the density of
    <math|V*<around*|(|I<rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|g,j>\<chi\><rsub|g,j>*<around|(|I*v|)>*<space|0.17em>g*e<rsub|j>|)>=V<around|(|v|)>>,
    by <eqref|eq:ex-interpolation-chips>. The same computation with the
    regular and forced fields, which are complex-linear in <math|v> by (H5),
    recovers the forced profile of <math|V<around|(|v|)>> from those of the
    basis columns; the exponential entry then follows from the dressing
    identity below. Without (H4) the weighted sum would be <math|V> evaluated
    at <math|<big|sum><rsub|g,j>\<chi\><rsub|g,j>*<around|(|I*v|)>*<space|0.17em>g*I<rsup|-1>*e<rsub|j>>,
    which need not equal <math|v>. The regular and forced address arrays are
    finite coefficient sums, hence complex-linear in <math|v>; their address
    dictionaries are reindexed by motion and need not themselves be
    invariant. The admissible density is motion-covariant by hypothesis. The
    forced profile covariance is an explicit assumption. For the exponential
    entry, use the coefficient identity <math|<math-up|density><around|(|U|)>=<math-up|eval><around|(|U|)><space|0.17em><math-up|exponentialEntry><around|(|U|)>+<math-up|forcedDress><around|(|U|)>>.
    The profile covariance and lawful dressing identity give covariance of
    the last term; subtract it from the density covariance identity and
    divide by the nonzero local evaluation. This proves the needed
    exponential-entry covariance. Covariance of the new readout uses (H4)
    once more: the weights satisfy <math|\<chi\><rsub|g,j>*<around|(|I*<around|(|h*v|)>|)>=\<chi\><rsub|g,j>*<around|(|h*I*v|)>>
    for every motion <math|h>. Thus the readout and the two profile outputs
    obey the finite motion reindexing identities, without asserting
    invariance of raw address arrays. Every summand is supported on the same
    common stencil, so the output support is independent of <math|v>. The
    unit identity follows from (H6): <math|I<around|(|u<rsub|N>|)>=u<rsub|m+1>>,
    the core of the unit coordinate vanishes, so the lead error is zero, and
    the heading preserves the unit coordinate; the unit at each scale is
    <math|u<rsub|m>=a<rsub|m><rsup|d>*e<rsub|0>>.

    For each <math|v>, put <math|u=Q<around|(|v|)>.*x> and
    <math|y=<math-up|vowel><rsub|L><around|(|I*v|)>>. The heading map gives
    the two-sided map bound with <math|K=K<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>>;
    <eqref|eq:ex-interpolation-buy> bounds the leading error by
    <math|<math-up|barunit><around|(|L|)><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><around|(|I*v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>
    and the new charge <math|a<around|(|v|)>=c*R<rsub|F>*D*<around|(|I*v|)>>
    by <math|K<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>.
    Heard gives <math|V.<math-up|charge><around|(|v|)>\<leqslant\>s<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><around|(|I*v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>.
    Apply Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-selected-cone-step> with the room and
    bank bounds. It yields the new charge cone and the forward/backward
    coordinate estimates. In particular, if <math|Q<around|(|v|)>.*x=0>, its
    estimate <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|I*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|+>*<around|\<\|\|\>|Q<around|(|v|)>.*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>
    gives <math|I*v=0>, and therefore <math|v=0>. Thus the linear coordinate
    map of the new coefficient family is injective and, in finite dimension,
    is an isomorphism.

    Finally, transport each finite-wing witness to its motion address,
    multiply it by <math|w<rsub|v>*<around|(|g,j|)>>, and take their finite
    union. The finite repainting identity, motion covariance, (H4) and the
    linearity (H5) show, as for the density above, that the exponential
    entries and forced profiles sum to those of <math|V<around|(|v|)>>. The
    first budget is bounded by

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|<around|(|g,j|)>><around|\||w<rsub|v>*<around|(|g,j|)>|\|>*<around|(|t+2*s|)>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|F>*<around|\<\|\|\>|I*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>*<around|(|t+2*s|)>,
    </equation*>

    while the second is the already proved remainder-price sum
    <math|a<around|(|v|)>>. The finite-wing addition rule therefore gives the
    required Tale witness. This proves item 5 and completes the common-pencil
    construction.
  </proof>

  The proposition is the finite assembly used by the selected one-scale
  transition. Its three purchase clauses do not have the same status in this
  monograph. The forced clause is supplied by the large-projection estimate
  after the forced allowance is at most one and the keeper contains every
  nonzero slot. The other two are supplied on the retained interpolation by
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:regular-principal-producer> and
  <reference|sec:primary-calibration-producer>, with their common-history
  attachment in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-reprise>:
  <em|(regular clause)> the regular-card estimate for the small-support,
  vanishing-jet principal term; and <em|(main clause)> for each nonunit basis
  coordinate, the selected primary-card and budget-card comparison. Neither
  follows from the finite pencil. Once the exact component receipts are
  supplied, their absolutely convergent density decomposition is merged using
  the merger of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-coupon-merger>, the
  summable lead and remainder receipts, and the dominated-series argument
  above, and the finite-wing merger sums the two budgets using the same
  motion coefficients. No compactness or diagonal extraction is involved.

  <subsection|Same-run Cauchy input for the full
  mesh><label|ex-public-same-run-cauchy>

  We record the actual Cauchy route used to upgrade the positioned limit to
  the whole mesh. This is a same-run statement. Fix a selected run and its
  campaign, Winter witness <math|H>, and Pillars witness <math|W>, together
  with the chosen growth function <math|f<rsub|i>> and the selected history
  tables <math|C<rsub|i>>. For a fixed period <math|p>, word length <math|n>,
  list of local labels <math|v>, and physical tuple <math|x> which is both
  non-hit and an ancestor point, write

  <\equation*>
    o<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>=<math-up|wrap><rsub|a<rsub|j>><around*|(|<math-up|address><rsub|a<rsub|j>><around|(|x|)>|)>,<space|2em>U<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>=C<rsub|i>.<math-up|numeric><around*|(|f<rsub|i>,p,v,o<rsub|f<rsub|i>><around|(|x|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  We show that <math|<around|(|U<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>|)><rsub|i>> is Cauchy,
  conditional on the growth, price, transfer and geometric receipts of one
  fixed run listed at the end of this section; the same conclusion is proved
  in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:fullmesh-correlation-cauchy>. For the
  full-mesh conclusion at the end of this section we use the specialisation
  <math|f<rsub|i>=i> for every <math|i> and <math|C<rsub|i>=C>, one fixed
  selected history table for all cutoffs; the grade-dependent reference depth
  that enters the receipts is a separate function <math|\<psi\>> of the
  grade, not the cutoff map. With this specialisation
  <math|U<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>> is the finite-volume correlation of the
  fixed family at the cutoff <math|i> itself. For a general growth function
  or <math|i>-dependent tables, Cauchyness of
  <math|<around|(|U<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>|)><rsub|i>> would not by itself
  give convergence of the cutoff sequence of one family. This section proves
  the Cauchy implication and the analytic interpolation step. The
  selected-history limits and fixed-level comparisons supplying the inputs
  below are constructed in Subsections<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-history-limits>
  and<nbsp><reference|sec:fixed-rung-convergence>.

  The Cauchy step uses one quantitative insertion comparison. We state its
  exact logical form so the upstream estimate is visible. Fix a finite label
  set <math|A>, a reference rung <math|k>, a later rung <math|j>, and a
  terminal mesh index <math|i>. Let <math|U<rsub|i>> be the full-volume pilot
  and <math|V<rsub|j,i>> its fixed-rung interpolant. Suppose
  <math|F<rsub|0>\<less\>\<infty\>> is independent of <math|j>,
  <math|G<rsub|j>\<to\>\<infty\>>, and, for every sufficiently large fixed
  <math|j>, the same-history receipts give

  <\equation>
    <around|\||U<rsub|i>-V<rsub|j,i>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|F<rsub|0>|G<rsub|j>/2><space|1em><text|for
    all sufficiently large >i,<label|eq:ex-fixed-rung-comparison>
  </equation>

  and <math|<around|(|V<rsub|j,i>|)><rsub|i>> is Cauchy at each such fixed
  <math|j>. A sufficient exact condition for this fixed-rung Cauchyness is
  that its finite mode integrand is a polynomial in finitely many measurable
  coefficient fields that converge pointwise, these mode integrands have a
  common integrable dominator, and their positive partition normalizers
  converge to a positive limit. Dominated convergence then gives convergence
  of the numerator integrals, and division by the normalizers gives
  convergence of <math|V<rsub|j,i>>. Under these hypotheses
  <math|<around|(|U<rsub|i>|)><rsub|i>> is Cauchy: given
  <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, first choose <math|j> so that
  <math|F<rsub|0>/<around|(|G<rsub|j>/2|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>/4>, then
  take the maximum of the threshold in <eqref|eq:ex-fixed-rung-comparison>
  and the fixed-<math|j> Cauchy threshold for error <math|\<varepsilon\>/4>.
  For later <math|i,i<rprime|'>>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||U<rsub|i>-U<rsub|i<rprime|'>>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||U<rsub|i>-V<rsub|j,i>|\|>+<around|\||V<rsub|j,i>-V<rsub|j,i<rprime|'>>|\|>+<around|\||V<rsub|j,i<rprime|'>>-U<rsub|i<rprime|'>>|\|>\<less\>3*\<varepsilon\>/4\<less\>\<varepsilon\>.
  </equation*>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Holomorphic insertion estimate><label|lem:ex-holomorphic-insertion>Let
    <math|S\<gtr\>2> and let <math|\<Phi\>> be holomorphic on
    <math|B*<around|(|0,S|)>>. If <math|<around|\||\<Phi\><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>F<rsub|0>>
    for all <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<less\>S>, then

    <\equation>
      <around|\||\<Phi\><around|(|1|)>-\<Phi\><around|(|0|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>F<rsub|0>/<around|(|S/2|)>.<label|eq:ex-holomorphic-insertion>
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Put <math|r=S/2>. For every <math|w> with
    <math|<around|\||w|\|>\<leqslant\>1>, the closed disk
    <math|<wide|B|\<bar\>>*<around|(|w,r|)>> lies in
    <math|B*<around|(|0,S|)>> since <math|1+S/2\<less\>S>. The Cauchy
    integral formula on its boundary gives
    <math|<around|\||\<Phi\><rprime|'><around|(|w|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>F<rsub|0>/r>.
    Integrating the derivative on the real segment from <math|0> to <math|1>
    proves <eqref|eq:ex-holomorphic-insertion>.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Uniform confluence supplies the fixed-rung
    vault><label|lem:ex-uniform-confluence-vault>Let <math|I> be a finite
    set, let <math|\<cal-B\>> be a finite set, and let <math|\<cal-X\>> be a
    nonempty set. Suppose <math|F<rsub|n,i>:\<cal-X\>\<to\>\<bbb-C\>> and
    <math|G<rsub|n,i,b>:\<cal-X\>\<to\>\<bbb-C\>> have uniform eventual
    bounds: for each <math|i\<in\>I> there is <math|c<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>
    such that eventually <math|sup<rsub|x\<in\>\<cal-X\>><around|\||F<rsub|n,i><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|i>>,
    and for each <math|i,b> there is <math|d<rsub|i,b>\<geqslant\>0> such
    that eventually <math|sup<rsub|x\<in\>\<cal-X\>><around|\||G<rsub|n,i,b><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>d<rsub|i,b>>.
    For any weights <math|w<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0>, there are finite
    <math|x<rsub|0>,y<rsub|0>> such that, eventually in <math|n>,

    <\equation*>
      w<rsub|i><around|\||F<rsub|n,i><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|0>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|b\<in\>\<cal-B\>>w<rsub|i><around|\||G<rsub|n,i,b><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>y<rsub|0>*<space|1em><around|(|i\<in\>I,x\<in\>\<cal-X\>|)>,
    </equation*>

    where one may take

    <\equation*>
      x<rsub|0>=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>w<rsub|i>*c<rsub|i>,<space|2em>y<rsub|0>=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I><big|sum><rsub|b\<in\>\<cal-B\>>w<rsub|i>*d<rsub|i,b>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    There are only finitely many bounds. Intersect their eventual tails to
    get one index <math|n<rsub|0>> after which all hold at once. For each
    fixed <math|i>, positivity of the weights and nonnegativity of every
    summand give <math|w<rsub|i><around|\||F<rsub|n,i><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>w<rsub|i>*c<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|0>>.
    Likewise <math|<big|sum><rsub|b>w<rsub|i><around|\||G<rsub|n,i,b><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|b>w<rsub|i>*d<rsub|i,b>\<leqslant\>y<rsub|0>>.
    No compactness or extraction is needed.
  </proof>

  This is the two-part vault bound used for the scalar and box-marked fields.
  In the application, the eventual uniform bound over configurations for each
  fixed rung and each field is the eventual uniform boundedness supplied by
  the fixed-level comparison of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:fixed-rung-convergence>,
  as in<nbsp><eqref|eq:fullmesh-vault>; it is an input here. Since the label
  set and the box set of a fixed finite torus are finite, their thresholds
  can be intersected once.

  The fixed-rung vaults in this argument are produced by the same history,
  not by an additional choice of subsequence. At a fixed rung <math|j>, the
  retained history interpolation gives uniform confluence of each scalar
  insertion and each box-marked insertion. Applying
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-uniform-confluence-vault> to the finite label
  set, with weights <math|w<rsub|i>>, gives the constants <math|t,v> in the
  later-rung vault. Applying it at the opened rung <math|k> gives <math|x,y>
  in the opened-rung vault. The constants may depend on <math|k,j>, the
  finite labels, and the weights, but the opened-rung constants used in the
  Cauchy comparison are held fixed as <math|j> varies. The positive weights
  are obtained from the paid allowances <math|B<rsub|i>> by
  <math|w<rsub|i>=\<zeta\><rsub|i>/<around|(|<around|\||B<rsub|i>|\|>+1|)>>;
  indeed <math|w<rsub|i><around|\||B<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|i>>
  and <math|w<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> when <math|\<zeta\><rsub|i>\<gtr\>0>.

  In the public same-run application, these are actual receipts of the
  selected history. Its branch-stability estimate gives uniform confluence at
  every fixed rung for the scalar and box-marked fields, and its credit
  estimate gives the finite constants <math|B<rsub|i>>. The preceding finite
  sum therefore supplies the two vaults required at the opened rung and at
  each later rung. The history's scale profile supplies the positive weights
  and their march relation. Its selected position map supplies the remaining
  geometry: ancestor-point separation gives injectivity at the chosen rung,
  hence occupancy one, and continues to give the required Drake inequalities
  at later rungs. These properties are intersected with the eventual pond,
  positive-reserve, and delay tails before fixing the rung; thus the
  estimates are simultaneous for the finite label family and hold along the
  original volume sequence.

  To apply Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-holomorphic-insertion>, let
  <math|P<rsub|z><around|(|U|)>> be the selected census-mode coefficient of
  the restored field at rung <math|j>, with its principal and forced field
  families multiplied by <math|z>, and put

  <\equation*>
    \<Phi\><around|(|z|)>=Z<rsub|N,p><rsup|-1>*<big|int>P<rsub|z><around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|j><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  The finite restored polynomial depends polynomially on <math|z>. The
  quantitative majorant establishing integrability of its coefficients is
  given below.

  Here is the selected-field verification of its uniform bound, conditional
  on the served-slab and uniform price receipts at rung <math|j>. Write
  <math|B<rsub|j>> for its Drake separation scale and <math|E<rsub|j>> for
  the common support exponent. Assume <math|B<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>8>,
  <math|E<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>2>, and <math|S<rsub|j>=e<rsup|B<rsub|j>/64>\<gtr\>2>.
  The last inequality is a separate late-scale condition; it does not follow
  from <math|B<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>8> alone. For a nonzero polynomial
  component on a support <math|X>, each nonzero mode has at least two
  markers, and the support-placement condition puts every named origin of
  those markers in <math|X>. Drake separation says distinct origins of that
  mode are at max-distance at least <math|B<rsub|j><rsup|2>>. The
  connected-halo tour estimate in Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-construction>
  therefore gives

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|j><space|0.17em><around|\||I|\|>\<leqslant\>64<math-up|cover><around|(|B<rsub|j>,X|)>,<space|2em><math-up|cover><around|(|B<rsub|j>,X|)>\<geqslant\>B<rsub|j>/64,
  </equation*>

  for its marker set <math|I>. Thus <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<less\>e<rsup|B<rsub|j>/64>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<math-up|cover><around|(|B<rsub|j>,X|)>>>.
  Scaling the component by <math|z> multiplies its envelope by
  <math|<around|\||z|\|>>. Its original weighted support bill contains the
  factor <math|e<rsup|E<rsub|j><math-up|cover><around|(|B<rsub|j>,X|)>>>;
  since <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|2<math-up|cover><around|(|B<rsub|j>,X|)>>>,
  the same bill pays the scaled envelope at exponent <math|E<rsub|j>-2>. This
  argument applies to every active support in both the principal and forced
  families; zero components cost nothing. The correction families are scaled,
  while the exponential-entry and marked activity carriers are fixed. The
  support, gauge, and minimum-degree conditions remain true under scalar
  multiplication; the envelopes in the support ledger scale by
  <math|<around|\||z|\|>>. Hence the same served decomposition with those
  scaled correction families witnesses servedness of the restored field at
  exponent <math|E<rsub|j>-2>. This does not assert that the restore
  operation itself is linear. For integrability, the vault bounds the
  finitely many scalar and marked fields and the served ledger bounds the
  finitely many correction coefficients. The selected lawfulness upper bound
  for the action and the vacuum-activity envelope give the pointwise
  exponential majorant <eqref|ex-centered-steer>; all coefficient fields are
  measurable. Expanding the finite restored polynomial therefore gives a
  measurable, integrable majorant for each coefficient of <math|P<rsub|z>>,
  uniform for <math|z> in the disk. The prior is a probability measure. Thus
  the integral defining <math|\<Phi\>> is holomorphic on
  <math|B*<around|(|0,S<rsub|j>|)>>.

  To pass to the opened rung, the served witness at the lowered exponent is
  propagated by the downward servedness induction: at each intervening scale
  the retained matchup and wake data give the transition hypotheses, and the
  exact push identity fixes each marked coefficient. The induction's
  integrability step follows from the transition's finite probability mass.
  This is the served-transfer result of Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-construction>,
  with its displayed one-step response and payment hypotheses; Fubini
  supplies the mode-integral identity below, but by itself does not imply
  servedness. The backward transition from rung <math|j> to <math|k> is
  finite, and at each transition, if <math|K<rsub|m>*<around|(|U,V|)>>
  denotes its kernel from finer configuration <math|U> to coarser
  configuration <math|V>, its normalization is
  <math|<big|int>K<rsub|m>*<around|(|U,V|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|m><around|(|V|)>=1>
  for every <math|U>. For each integrable coefficient <math|q>, the push
  identity is

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|m><around|(|V|)>=<big|int>K<rsub|m>*<around|(|U,V|)>*q<rsub|m+1><around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|m+1><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  Moreover, nonnegativity and normalization give

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<big|int><space|-0.17em><big|int>K<rsub|m>*<around|(|U,V|)><around|\||q<rsub|m+1><around|(|U|)>|\|>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|m+1><around|(|U|)>*d*\<mu\><rsub|m><around|(|V|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|2em>=<big|int><around|\||q<rsub|m+1><around|(|U|)>|\|>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|m+1><around|(|U|)>\<less\>\<infty\>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Fubini therefore proves

  <\equation*>
    <big|int>q<rsub|m><around|(|V|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|m><around|(|V|)>=<big|int>q<rsub|m+1><around|(|U|)><around*|(|<big|int>K<rsub|m>*<around|(|U,V|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|m><around|(|V|)>|)>*d*\<mu\><rsub|m+1><around|(|U|)>=<big|int>q<rsub|m+1><around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|m+1><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  This proves the mode-integral preservation at each transition. The
  opened-rung vaults and finite-volume mode
  estimate<nbsp><eqref|ex-centered-sail> then give exactly

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||<big|int>P<rsub|z>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|j>|\|><space|0.17em><math-up|toll><rsub|w><around|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>Z<rsub|N,p>*exp
    <around|(|<around|\||<math-up|Site><rsub|k>|\|>*\<cal-T\><rsub|k>+<around|\||A|\|>*<around|(|x+y+2|)>|)>,
  </equation*>

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<Phi\><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>F<rsub|0>\<assign\><frac|exp
    <around|(|<around|\||<math-up|Site><rsub|k>|\|>*\<cal-T\><rsub|k>+<around|\||A|\|>*<around|(|x+y+2|)>|)>|<math-up|toll><rsub|w><around|(|A|)>>.
  </equation*>

  All weights, the opened rung, and its vault budgets are fixed as <math|j>
  varies, so <math|F<rsub|0>> is independent of <math|j>. This checks the
  bound hypothesis of the holomorphic estimate from the explicit scaling,
  backward transfer, and integrated-mode receipts.

  In the public pilot comparison, the finite transition recurrence identifies
  <math|U<rsub|i>=\<Phi\><around|(|1|)>> and
  <math|V<rsub|j,i>=\<Phi\><around|(|0|)>>, with the same terminal law and
  normalization. The terminal birth identity supplies the value at
  <math|z=1>; the later-rung restoration with its correction families set to
  zero supplies the value at <math|z=0>. The opened rung <math|k> is used to
  price this value after transfer. The transition preserves each mode
  integral, so this identification introduces no change of partition
  function. Thus <eqref|eq:ex-holomorphic-insertion> is precisely
  <eqref|eq:ex-fixed-rung-comparison>, not an additional compactness input.

  There is an exact finite identity connecting this pilot observable to the
  ordinary finite-volume Wilson observable. For the census mode containing
  all <math|n> labels, all copy switches are the identity. The product of the
  local insertions is therefore exactly the finite-volume numeric observable,
  at the same address tuple <math|o<rsub|f<rsub|i>><around|(|x|)>>. Thus
  Cauchyness of the pilot sequence is Cauchyness of
  <math|U<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>> itself; this identity introduces no error
  term and no subsequence.

  To pass from ancestor points to the full positional function, use that
  ancestor points are dense. The full-mesh local Lipschitz receipt on a
  compact subset of the non-hit set, together with <math|a<rsub|i>\<to\>0>,
  gives asymptotic uniform equicontinuity there: if the continuum points are
  within <math|\<delta\>/2>, their lattice addresses are within
  <math|\<delta\>> once <math|2*c<rsub|n>*a<rsub|i>\<less\>\<delta\>/2>. For
  compact <math|K\<subset\>U=<math-up|NoHit>>, choose a compact neighborhood
  <math|K\<Subset\>K*\<prime\>\<Subset\>U> and a common asymptotic modulus on
  <math|K*\<prime\>>. A finite <math|\<delta\>/3>-net for <math|K> can be
  chosen with centers in the dense ancestor set intersected with <math|U>.
  Pointwise Cauchyness at these finitely many centers gives one threshold.
  For any two later indices and any <math|x\<in\>K>, compare their values at
  <math|x> to the value at a net center: the two modulus errors are each
  below <math|\<varepsilon\>/3>, and the center values differ by less than
  <math|\<varepsilon\>/3>. Hence the functions are uniformly Cauchy on
  <math|K>; completeness gives a uniform limit, and the same modulus makes it
  continuous. The generic finite-difference bounds in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-fullmesh-fixedbounds> provide the
  required bounds and local Lipschitz receipts from the same run. The
  application is conditional on the following same-run receipts: uniform
  branch confluence and a paid credit for each fixed finite label set; the
  pond, migration, occupancy, and Drake data needed to construct the
  transition fields; a served witness at each chosen later rung with its
  displayed support-price bounds; the finite downward service-transfer
  hypotheses; and a fixed-rung Cauchy sequence. It also uses the full-mesh
  local Lipschitz bound on each compact subset. Given these receipts,
  <eqref|eq:ex-fixed-rung-comparison> is the quantitative insertion estimate
  just proved, and the pointwise Cauchy and compact-uniform limits required
  by the full-mesh positioned construction follow for every period, length,
  and label word. Together with the same-run finite-difference bounds in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-fullmesh-fixedbounds>, and with the
  specialisation <math|f<rsub|i>=i>, <math|C<rsub|i>=C> fixed above, these
  receipts yield the smooth positioned correlation along the full mesh,
  without further extraction. The same conclusion, for the cutoff sequence
  itself, is stated in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:fullmesh-correlation-cauchy>.
  The per-label transition fields used in the served-slab step are among
  these inputs. The marked coefficients and one-step comparisons are
  constructed in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-centered-service>,
  using the component merger of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-coupon-merger>.
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-construction> gives the centered
  estimates from these transition data.

  <section|Reflection positivity and the phase decomposition>

  <subsection|Reflection positivity and the Euclidean Hilbert
  space><label|ex-reflection>

  The reflected inner product follows the Osterwalder\USchrader construction
  <cite-detail|OS73|Ÿ3>. For the present separated correlation tables, the
  large-separation estimates used in proving contractivity come from the slab
  bounds.

  Reflection positivity is the mechanism that turns Euclidean correlations
  into an inner product. A second ingredient is needed to make positive-time
  translation contractive: a bound on correlations as the two halves move
  apart. We give the complete argument, keeping this bound explicit.

  A word <math|w=<around|(|<around|(|x<rsub|1>,v<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,<around|(|x<rsub|n>,v<rsub|n>|)>|)>>
  records finitely many distinct insertion points and their labels. Write
  <math|S<around|(|w|)>> for its correlation and <math|\<varnothing\>> for
  the empty word. Fix a coordinate axis and call its coordinate <math|t>. Let
  <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|+>> consist of words whose points have strictly
  positive <math|t>, including the empty word. Reflection <math|\<theta\>>
  reverses the order of a word, reflects the points across <math|t=0>, and
  applies the reflected conjugate to each label. Assume
  <math|\<theta\><rsup|2>=1>. For <math|v,w\<in\>\<cal-W\><rsub|+>>, the
  concatenation <math|<around|(|\<theta\>*v|)>*w> has no coincident points:
  its two parts lie in opposite open half-spaces.

  The inputs used in this section are normalization
  <math|S<around|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=1>, translation invariance, the
  reflection relation <math|S*<around|(|\<theta\>*w|)>=<wide|S<around|(|w|)>|\<bar\>>>,
  permutation symmetry on distinct points, and the following two conditions:

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i,j=1><rsup|m><wide|c<rsub|i>|\<bar\>>c<rsub|j>*S*<around|(|<around|(|\<theta\>*w<rsub|i>|)>*w<rsub|j>|)>\<geqslant\>0*<space|1em><around|(|w<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-W\><rsub|+>,c<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-C\>|)>,<eq-number><label|ex-rp>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|x\<longmapsto\>S*<around|(|<around|(|\<theta\>*v|)>*<around|(|w+x|)>|)><text|
    is continuous and bounded on ><around|{|x:t<around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>0|}><space|1em><around|(|v,w\<in\>\<cal-W\><rsub|+>|)>.<eq-number><label|ex-cap>>>>>
  </align>

  The positivity in <eqref|ex-rp> means that the displayed complex number is
  real and nonnegative.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|From smeared to point reflection positivity><label|ex-rp-bumps>
    Suppose the correlations are continuous at every configuration of
    distinct points. Assume reflection positivity for finite sums of smooth
    compactly supported tests on positive-time configurations, with arbitrary
    fixed label tuples for the separate summands. Then <eqref|ex-rp> holds.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Fix positive words <math|w<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,w<rsub|m>> and coefficients
    <math|c<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,c<rsub|m>>. For each nonempty word choose a
    small configuration neighborhood in which its points stay distinct and
    strictly in the positive half-space. Place a nonnegative smooth bump of
    integral one in that neighborhood, centered at the word's positions, with
    support radius <math|\<epsilon\>>. Such bumps are obtained by translation
    and rescaling of <math|exp <around|(|-1/<around|(|1-<around|\||x|\|><rsup|2>|)>|)>>
    on the unit ball, zero outside, followed by division by its positive
    integral. The empty word has a zero-dimensional configuration space; use
    the constant test one there and counting measure of total mass one.

    For each pair <math|i,j>, the concatenation of a reflected configuration
    near <math|w<rsub|i>> with a configuration near <math|w<rsub|j>> has
    distinct points. Within either half this follows from the choice of
    neighborhood; across halves it follows from the strict time separation.
    Thus its correlation kernel is continuous near the pair of centers, even
    if some spatial coordinates agree. Write
    <math|K<rsub|i*j><rsup|\<epsilon\>>> for its integral against the two
    bumps. Nonnegativity and unit integrals give

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||K<rsub|i*j><rsup|\<epsilon\>>-S*<around|(|<around|(|\<theta\>*w<rsub|i>|)>*w<rsub|j>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>sup
      <around*|\||K<rsub|i*j>*<around|(|x,y|)>-K<rsub|i*j>*<around|(|x<rsub|i>,y<rsub|j>|)>|\|>\<longrightarrow\>0,
    </equation*>

    where the supremum is over the shrinking product support. The kernels are
    bounded on these compact supports, so the integrals are well-defined.
    Smeared reflection positivity says <math|<big|sum><rsub|i,j><wide|c<rsub|i>|\<bar\>>c<rsub|j>*K<rsub|i*j><rsup|\<epsilon\>>\<geqslant\>0>.
    There are finitely many terms, and the nonnegative real half-line is
    closed in <math|\<bbb-C\>>. Passing to the limit proves <eqref|ex-rp>.
  </proof>

  This argument needs continuity at all distinct-point configurations.
  Continuity only where every coordinate is pairwise different would not
  suffice: a word and its reflection share spatial coordinates, including in
  the diagonal entries of the reflection matrix.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|A sufficient separated-correlation bound><label|ex-cap-proof>
    Suppose the correlations are smooth at distinct points and, for each
    fixed finite list of labels, there are <math|C\<geqslant\>0> and
    <math|A\<geqslant\>0> such that

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||S<rsub|\<b-v\>><around|(|\<b-x\>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*\<rho\><rsup|-A><space|1em><text|whenever
      >0\<less\>\<rho\>\<leqslant\>1<text| and
      ><around|\||x<rsub|i>-x<rsub|j>|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>\<geqslant\>\<rho\>*<around|(|i\<ne\>j|)>.
    </equation*>

    Then <eqref|ex-cap> holds.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Fix <math|v,w>. The distances between points within either word are
    unchanged by translation. For a reflected point from <math|v> and a point
    of <math|w+x>, their time separation is the sum of their original
    positive times plus <math|t<around|(|x|)>>. It is bounded below by a
    positive number independent of <math|x> when
    <math|t<around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>0>. There are only finitely many pairs.
    Choose <math|\<rho\>\<leqslant\>1> smaller than all their positive lower
    bounds; if there are no pairs, take <math|\<rho\>=1>. The assumed
    estimate gives the required uniform bound. The configuration depends
    continuously on <math|x> and always remains in the distinct-point set, so
    smoothness gives continuity.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Hilbert space and vacuum><label|ex-gns> There is a Hilbert space
    <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|E>> with vectors <math|<around|[|w|]>>,
    <math|w\<in\>\<cal-W\><rsub|+>>, whose linear span is dense and such that

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|<around|[|v|]>,<around|[|w|]>|\<rangle\>>=S*<around|(|<around|(|\<theta\>*v|)>*w|)>.
    </equation*>

    The vector <math|\<Omega\><rsub|E>=<around|[|\<varnothing\>|]>> has norm
    one.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    On the free complex vector space of finitely supported coefficients on
    <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|+>>, define

    <\equation*>
      B*<around|(|c,d|)>=<big|sum><rsub|v,w><wide|c<rsub|v>|\<bar\>>d<rsub|w>*S*<around|(|<around|(|\<theta\>*v|)>*w|)>.
    </equation*>

    Reflection, <math|\<theta\><rsup|2>=1>, and permutation symmetry show
    <math|B*<around|(|d,c|)>=<wide|B*<around|(|c,d|)>|\<bar\>>>; positivity
    follows from <eqref|ex-rp>. For completeness, Cauchy\USchwarz follows by
    applying positivity to <math|c+\<lambda\>*d>: if
    <math|B*<around|(|d,d|)>\<gtr\>0>, minimize the resulting quadratic
    expression in <math|\<lambda\>>; if <math|B*<around|(|d,d|)>=0>, varying
    the magnitude and phase of <math|\<lambda\>> forces
    <math|B*<around|(|c,d|)>=0>. Thus <math|<around|\||B*<around|(|c,d|)>|\|><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>B*<around|(|c,c|)>*B*<around|(|d,d|)>>.
    Consequently the null vectors <math|\<cal-N\>=<around|{|c:B*<around|(|c,c|)>=0|}>>
    are orthogonal to every vector and form a subspace. The quotient by
    <math|\<cal-N\>> is an inner-product space. Complete it and map each word
    to the class of its basis vector. This gives the stated inner product and
    density. Finally <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Omega\><rsub|E>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=S<around|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=1>.
  </proof>

  Let <math|\<tau\><rsub|s>*w> translate every point of <math|w> by <math|s>
  in the positive time direction, where <math|s\<geqslant\>0>. Define for a
  finite formal sum <math|c> the vector <math|T<rsub|s>*c=<big|sum><rsub|w>c<rsub|w>*<around|[|\<tau\><rsub|s>*w|]>>.
  At this point <math|T<rsub|s>> is defined on formal sums; descent to the
  null quotient will follow from its norm bound. Translation invariance gives

  <\equation>
    <label|ex-time-symmetry><around|\<langle\>|T<rsub|s>*c,T<rsub|t>*d|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|T<rsub|0>*c,T<rsub|s+t>*d|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation>

  Indeed, reflecting the translation by <math|s> shifts the first word by
  <math|-s>; translating the whole concatenation by <math|s> gives the right
  side. Sesquilinearity extends the identity from words to finite sums.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The contraction estimate><label|ex-contraction> For every finite
    sum <math|c> and every <math|s\<geqslant\>0>,
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsub|s>*c|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsub|0>*c|\<\|\|\>>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For a single word, <eqref|ex-time-symmetry> gives
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|[|\<tau\><rsub|s>*w|]>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=S*<around|(|<around|(|\<theta\>*w|)>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|2*s>*w|)>|)>>.
    Condition<nbsp><eqref|ex-cap> bounds this expression uniformly in
    <math|s>. The triangle inequality therefore bounds
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsub|s>*c|\<\|\|\>>> uniformly for every finite
    sum <math|c>. Put <math|M=sup<rsub|s\<geqslant\>0><around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsub|s>*c|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>\<infty\>>
    and <math|a=<around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsub|0>*c|\<\|\|\>>>. By
    <eqref|ex-time-symmetry> and Cauchy\USchwarz,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsub|s>*c|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<around|\||<around|\<langle\>|T<rsub|0>*c,T<rsub|2*s>*c|\<rangle\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>a*M.
    </equation*>

    Taking the supremum gives <math|M<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a*M>. Since
    <math|M\<geqslant\>0>, either <math|M=0>, or division by <math|M> gives
    <math|M\<leqslant\>a>. Both cases prove the result.
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Euclidean translations><label|ex-euclidean-semigroup> Positive
    time translations extend uniquely to a strongly continuous semigroup
    <math|E<rsub|s>>, <math|s\<geqslant\>0>, of self-adjoint contractions on
    <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|E>>. Spatial translations extend to a strongly
    continuous unitary representation <math|U<rsub|y>>,
    <math|y\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|3>>, commuting with <math|E<rsub|s>>. Both
    leave <math|\<Omega\><rsub|E>> fixed.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    The contraction estimate sends null vectors to null vectors. It therefore
    defines a contraction on the quotient, and then uniquely on its
    completion. On word vectors, <math|E<rsub|0>=1> and
    <math|E<rsub|s>*E<rsub|t>=E<rsub|s+t>>; continuity of the bounded
    operators extends these identities from their dense span to the whole
    Hilbert space. Equation<nbsp><eqref|ex-time-symmetry> with one time equal
    to zero says that <math|E<rsub|s>> is symmetric. A bounded symmetric
    operator defined everywhere is self-adjoint: its adjoint has the same
    matrix elements against all vectors, hence is the same operator.

    For a spatial shift <math|y>, reflection commutes with the shift.
    Translation invariance gives <math|<around|\<langle\>|<around|[|v+y|]>,<around|[|w+y|]>|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|<around|[|v|]>,<around|[|w|]>|\<rangle\>>>.
    Thus the shift descends to an isometry on the quotient and its
    completion. Its inverse is the shift by <math|-y>, so it is unitary. The
    group law and commutation with <math|E<rsub|s>> follow on words from
    commutation of ordinary translations, and extend by density. The empty
    word is unchanged by any translation, proving the vacuum assertions.

    It remains to prove strong continuity. For a word <math|w>, the square of
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|E<rsub|s>*w-E<rsub|t>*w|\<\|\|\>>>, with word
    brackets understood, is the sum of the two diagonal inner products minus
    twice the real part of the cross term. By <eqref|ex-time-symmetry> these
    are values of the continuous function
    <math|u\<mapsto\>S*<around|(|<around|(|\<theta\>*w|)>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|u>*w|)>|)>>
    at <math|2*s,2*t,s+t>. Their combination tends to zero as
    <math|s\<to\>t>. The identical norm-square argument and <eqref|ex-cap>
    give continuity of spatial translations on word vectors. Finite linear
    combinations remain continuous. For an arbitrary <math|\<psi\>>, choose a
    finite word sum <math|v> with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<psi\>-v|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>/3>.
    For either family <math|V<rsub|z>>, its norm bound gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|V<rsub|z>*\<psi\>-V<rsub|z<rsub|0>>*\<psi\>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2\<\|\|\>\<psi\>-v\<\|\|\>+<around|\<\|\|\>|V<rsub|z>*v-V<rsub|z<rsub|0>>*v|\<\|\|\>>.
    </equation*>

    The last term is smaller than <math|\<varepsilon\>/3> near
    <math|z<rsub|0>>, proving strong continuity.
  </proof>

  This theorem establishes Euclidean Hilbert-space dynamics from the
  displayed correlation hypotheses. Relativistic reconstruction also requires
  field operators, rotations, locality, boundary distributions and a selected
  phase with a unique invariant vacuum. These do not follow merely by naming
  the Hilbert space. The reconstruction in this monograph is given directly
  by the complex heat and joint boundary constructions, the word-field and
  word-motion propositions, and the covariance, locality and spectral
  arguments below; Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-every-character-vacuum>
  supplies vacuum uniqueness. The corrected theorem of
  Osterwalder\USchrader<nbsp><cite-detail|OS75|ŸIV.1> is an alternative
  literature route. We do not invoke it here: its linear growth and other
  hypotheses have not been matched to this construction.

  <subsection|The algebra of time-invariant observables><label|ex-phases>

  The Hilbert space constructed from an unselected correlation table need not
  have a one-dimensional vacuum space. We first identify the whole subspace
  left fixed by time evolution. Compressed slab observables on this subspace
  will then distinguish its phases. Throughout this section the hypotheses of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|ex-euclidean-semigroup> remain in force. We write
  <math|\<cal-H\>=\<cal-H\><rsub|E>> and <math|\<Omega\>=\<Omega\><rsub|E>>.
  In this section <math|e<rsub|0>> denotes the chosen time axis and
  <math|x<rsub|0>> its coordinate; this local notation does not change the
  earlier coordinate ordering.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The large-time projection><label|ex-ground-projection> The
    subspace

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-H\><rsub|0>=<around|{|v\<in\>\<cal-H\>:E<rsub|t>*v=v<text| for
      every >t\<geqslant\>0|}>
    </equation*>

    is closed. If <math|P<rsub|0>> denotes its orthogonal projection, then
    <math|E<rsub|t>*v\<to\>P<rsub|0>*v> in norm as <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>>,
    for every <math|v\<in\>\<cal-H\>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Put <math|A=E<rsub|1>>. This is a positive contraction since
    <math|E<rsub|1>=E<rsub|1/2><rsup|2>>. If <math|A*v=v>, contractivity
    gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|E<rsub|s>*v|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>>
    for <math|0\<less\>s\<less\>1>, using
    <math|E<rsub|1>=E<rsub|1-s>*E<rsub|s>>. Since <math|E<rsub|s>> is
    positive, <math|I-E<rsub|s><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>0> and equality of norms
    implies <math|E<rsub|s><rsup|2>*v=v>, hence <math|E<rsub|s>*v=v> by the
    positive square root. The semigroup law then shows <math|ker
    <around|(|I-A|)>=\<cal-H\><rsub|0>>, which is closed. By the bounded
    Borel functional calculus <cite-detail|ReedSimonI|Theorem<nbsp>VII.2>,
    the powers <math|A<rsup|n>> converge strongly to the spectral projection
    at <math|1>, by dominated convergence on <math|<around|[|0,1|]>>. This
    projection is <math|P<rsub|0>>. For <math|t=n+s> with integer <math|n>
    and <math|0\<leqslant\>s\<less\>1>, contractivity on
    <math|A<rsup|n>*v-P<rsub|0>*v> gives the same convergence for
    <math|E<rsub|t>>.
  </proof>

  We next explain the analytic estimate needed to insert an entire word as a
  bounded operator. A word <math|w> lies in a slab of width <math|d\<gtr\>0>
  if all its points are distinct and have time coordinates in
  <math|<around|(|0,d|)>>. For a positive word <math|v>, put

  <\equation*>
    I<rsub|w>*v=w*<space|0.17em>\<tau\><rsub|d>*v,<space|2em>w<rsup|\<sharp\>>=\<tau\><rsub|d>*\<theta\>*w.
  </equation*>

  Both are positive words, and <math|w<rsup|\<sharp\>>> lies in the same
  slab. Let <math|I<rsub|w>> also denote the linear map on finite formal word
  sums. Translation invariance and reflection give

  <\equation>
    <label|ex-slab-formal-adjoint><around|\<langle\>|<around|[|I<rsub|w>*c|]>,<around|[|b|]>|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|<around|[|c|]>,<around|[|I<rsub|w<rsup|\<sharp\>>>*b|]>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation>

  Indeed, translating the configuration <math|\<theta\>*<around|(|w*\<tau\><rsub|d>*v|)>*<space|0.17em>u=<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|-d>*\<theta\>*v|)>*<around|(|\<theta\>*w|)>*u>
  by <math|d> gives <math|<around|(|\<theta\>*v|)>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|d>*\<theta\>*w|)>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|d>*u|)>>,
  the configuration in the right-hand pairing. This proves the identity on
  words and then on finite sums.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|From repeated insertion to a bounded
    operator><label|ex-train-bound>Fix a slab word <math|w> and
    <math|D\<gtr\>0>. Suppose that for every positive word <math|v> there is
    <math|B<rsub|v>\<geqslant\>0> such that

    <\equation>
      <label|ex-train-input><around*|\||<around|[|<around|(|I<rsub|w<rsup|\<sharp\>>>*I<rsub|w>|)><rsup|n>*v|]>|\|><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|v>*D<rsup|4*n>*<space|1em><around|(|n=0,1,2,\<ldots\>|)>.
    </equation>

    There is a unique bounded operator <math|A<rsub|d><around|(|w|)>> on
    <math|\<cal-H\>> with <math|A<rsub|d><around|(|w|)><around|[|v|]>=<around|[|w*\<tau\><rsub|d>*v|]>>;
    it satisfies <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|d><around|(|w|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>D>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Write <math|T=I<rsub|w<rsup|\<sharp\>>>*I<rsub|w>> on formal sums and
    <math|X*c=<around|[|c|]>>. The triangle inequality and
    <eqref|ex-train-input> imply, for <math|c=<big|sum><rsub|v>c<rsub|v>*v>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|X*T<rsup|n>*c|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|c>*D<rsup|2*n>,<space|2em>C<rsub|c>=<big|sum><rsub|v><around|\||c<rsub|v>|\|><sqrt|B<rsub|v>>\<less\>\<infty\>.
    </equation*>

    Equation<nbsp><eqref|ex-slab-formal-adjoint> shows that <math|T> is
    symmetric for the possibly degenerate form induced by <math|X>.
    Repeatedly moving <math|T> across the inner product therefore gives
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|X*T<rsup|n>*c|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<around|\<langle\>|X*c,X*T<rsup|2*n>*c|\<rangle\>>>.
    Put <math|b<rsub|n>=D<rsup|-2*n>*<around|\<\|\|\>|X*T<rsup|n>*c|\<\|\|\>>>
    and <math|M=sup<rsub|n\<geqslant\>0> b<rsub|n>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
    Cauchy\USchwarz gives <math|b<rsub|n><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|X*c|\<\|\|\>>*b<rsub|2*n>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|X*c|\<\|\|\>>*M>.
    Taking the supremum yields <math|M<rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|X*c|\<\|\|\>>*M>,
    hence <math|M\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|X*c|\<\|\|\>>>. Finally,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|X*I<rsub|w>*c|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<around|\||<around|\<langle\>|X*c,X*T*c|\<rangle\>>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|X*c|\<\|\|\>>*<around|\<\|\|\>|X*T*c|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>D<rsup|2>*<around|\<\|\|\>|X*c|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    Thus insertion respects the null space and induces a bounded operator on
    the quotient. Its unique extension to the completion has the asserted
    norm and action. Uniqueness follows from density of the word span.
  </proof>

  The estimate in <eqref|ex-train-input> concerns arbitrarily long repeated
  words; the constants <math|B<rsub|v>> may depend on the exterior word.
  Bounds for each fixed correlation order alone do not supply this estimate.
  Its production from the lattice construction remains an input at this
  stage. Assume henceforth that it holds, with some <math|D>, for every slab
  word. The resulting operators obey

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsub|d><around|(|\<varnothing\>|)>>|<cell|=E<rsub|d>,<eq-number><label|ex-slab-laws>>>|<row|<cell|A<rsub|d+e>*<around|(|w*\<tau\><rsub|d>*v|)>>|<cell|=A<rsub|d><around|(|w|)>*A<rsub|e><around|(|v|)>,>>|<row|<cell|A<rsub|d><around|(|w|)><rsup|\<ast\>>>|<cell|=A<rsub|d><around|(|w<rsup|\<sharp\>>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|A<rsub|d+r><around|(|w|)>>|<cell|=A<rsub|d><around|(|w|)>*E<rsub|r>,<space|2em>A<rsub|r+d>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|r>*w|)>=E<rsub|r>*A<rsub|d><around|(|w|)>.>>>>
  </align>

  Here each word lies in its indicated slab and <math|r\<geqslant\>0>. The
  first, second and last two identities follow by applying both sides to a
  positive word and concatenating and translating its entries. The adjoint
  identity follows from <eqref|ex-slab-formal-adjoint>. All extend from the
  dense word span by boundedness. Spatial translation also gives
  <math|U<rsub|y>*A<rsub|d><around|(|w|)>=A<rsub|d>*<around|(|w+y|)>*U<rsub|y>>,
  by the same calculation on words.

  <subsubsection|Spatial invariance of the ground space>

  Assume now full orthogonal covariance of the correlation table: there is an
  action on quotient field labels such that, for every
  <math|K\<in\>O<around|(|4|)>> and every distinct-point word <math|w>, the
  jointly rotated word <math|K*w> satisfies
  <math|S*<around|(|K*w|)>=S<around|(|w|)>>. Rotation includes this label
  action in what follows. To evaluate a rotated word, project each raw label
  to its field class, rotate that class, and choose a raw representative.
  Correlations are independent of that choice. No group action on the chosen
  raw representatives is assumed. This is stronger than covariance under
  signed coordinate permutations.

  <\lemma>
    <label|ex-ground-spatial>Every <math|v\<in\>\<cal-H\><rsub|0>> is fixed
    by all spatial translations.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For two distinct-point words <math|p,q>, set
    <math|F<rsub|p,q><around|(|x|)>=S*<around|(|p*<around|(|q+x|)>|)>>
    whenever the two clusters are disjoint. Translate <math|\<theta\>*p> and
    <math|q> separately far enough in positive time to obtain positive words
    <math|v,w>. If the translation amounts are <math|r,s>, then, whenever
    <math|x<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>r+s>, translation invariance gives

    <\equation>
      <label|ex-separated-pair>F<rsub|p,q><around|(|x|)>=<around|\<langle\>|<around|[|v|]>,U<rsub|x<rsub|\<perp\>>>*E<rsub|x<rsub|0>-r-s><around|[|w|]>|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em>x<rsub|\<perp\>>=x-x<rsub|0>*e<rsub|0>.
    </equation>

    The time separation also ensures that the two clusters are disjoint. For
    any fixed <math|h\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> and any path <math|x<around|(|t|)>>
    with <math|x<rsub|0><around|(|t|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>, it follows that

    <\equation>
      <label|ex-normal-shift>F<rsub|p,q>*<around|(|x<around|(|t|)>+h*e<rsub|0>|)>-F<rsub|p,q><around|(|x<around|(|t|)>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0.
    </equation>

    Indeed, the difference of the two heat-evolved vectors in
    <eqref|ex-separated-pair> tends to zero by
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-ground-projection>; the spatial unitary
    has norm one, even when <math|x<rsub|\<perp\>><around|(|t|)>> varies
    without bound.

    If <math|b<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0>, choose an orthogonal map <math|K> taking
    <math|b> to <math|<around|\||b|\|>*e<rsub|0>>. Orthogonality implies
    <math|<around|(|K*e<rsub|0>|)><rsub|0>=b<rsub|0>/<around|\||b|\|>\<gtr\>0>.
    Rotate both clusters and apply <eqref|ex-normal-shift> along the path
    <math|t*K*e<rsub|0>>, whose time coordinate tends to infinity. Covariance
    gives

    <\equation*>
      F<rsub|p,q>*<around|(|t*e<rsub|0>+b|)>-F<rsub|p,q>*<around|(|t*e<rsub|0>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0.
    </equation*>

    For a spatial <math|y>, take <math|b=y+e<rsub|0>> and subtract
    <eqref|ex-normal-shift> along <math|x<around|(|t|)>=t*e<rsub|0>+y>, with
    <math|h=1>. We obtain <math|F<rsub|p,q>*<around|(|t*e<rsub|0>+y|)>-F<rsub|p,q>*<around|(|t*e<rsub|0>|)>\<to\>0>.
    Apply this with <math|p=\<theta\>*v,q=w> for positive words. The two
    correlations are <math|<around|\<langle\>|<around|[|v|]>,U<rsub|y>*E<rsub|t><around|[|w|]>|\<rangle\>>>
    and <math|<around|\<langle\>|<around|[|v|]>,E<rsub|t><around|[|w|]>|\<rangle\>>>.
    Their limits show <math|<around|\<langle\>|<around|[|v|]>,<around|(|U<rsub|y>-I|)>*P<rsub|0><around|[|w|]>|\<rangle\>>=0>.
    Density of word vectors first gives <math|<around|(|U<rsub|y>-I|)>*P<rsub|0><around|[|w|]>=0>
    and then <math|<around|(|U<rsub|y>-I|)>*P<rsub|0>=0>. Since
    <math|P<rsub|0>> is the identity on <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|0>>, the
    conclusion follows.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Compression and separation of clusters>

  For a slab word define <math|M<around|(|w|)>=P<rsub|0>*A<rsub|d><around|(|w|)>\|<rsub|\<cal-H\><rsub|0>>>.
  The width is immaterial: increasing it appends an <math|E<rsub|r>>, which
  is the identity on <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|0>>; two widths can be compared
  with their sum. Likewise a positive time shift inserts <math|E<rsub|r>> on
  the left, and <math|P<rsub|0>*E<rsub|r>=P<rsub|0>>. Spatial shifts give the
  same operator by Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-ground-spatial> and spatial
  covariance. For a negative time shift, reverse the comparison. Every finite
  distinct-point word can be translated into a slab. Thus
  <math|M<around|(|w|)>> is well-defined for every such word, independent of
  both the placement and width, and

  <\equation>
    <label|ex-compression-values>M*<around|(|w+b|)>=M<around|(|w|)>,<space|2em><around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,M<around|(|w|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>=S<around|(|w|)>.
  </equation>

  The latter follows from <math|A<rsub|d><around|(|w|)>*\<Omega\>=<around|[|w|]>>
  after a slab placement, <math|P<rsub|0>*\<Omega\>=\<Omega\>>, and
  translation invariance.

  For a positive word also put <math|X<around|(|w|)>=A<rsub|d><around|(|w|)>\|<rsub|\<cal-H\><rsub|0>>>,
  as a map into <math|\<cal-H\>>. Width independence follows as above, and

  <\equation*>
    X<around|(|w|)>*\<Omega\>=<around|[|w|]>,<space|2em>M<around|(|w|)>*\<Omega\>=P<rsub|0><around|[|w|]>,<space|2em>M*<around|(|<around|(|\<theta\>*v|)>*w|)>=X<around|(|v|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*X<around|(|w|)>.
  </equation*>

  For the last identity, place the reflected word into the slab of width
  <math|d>, append the word <math|w> in a subsequent slab of width <math|e>,
  and use the concatenation and adjoint identities in <eqref|ex-slab-laws>.
  Compression then gives exactly the displayed product. In particular
  <math|M*<around|(|\<theta\>*v|)>=M<around|(|v|)><rsup|\<ast\>>>. This also
  shows that the generators are closed under adjoints, after translation into
  slabs.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Separated-cluster limit><label|ex-cluster-train> Let
    <math|w<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,w<rsub|m>> be words with distinct points.
    Suppose <math|b<rsub|1><around|(|n|)>,\<ldots\>,b<rsub|m><around|(|n|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>
    satisfy <math|<around|(|b<rsub|j+1><around|(|n|)>-b<rsub|j><around|(|n|)>|)><rsub|0>\<to\>\<infty\>>
    for <math|j\<less\>m>. Then

    <\equation*>
      S*<around|(|<around|(|w<rsub|1>+b<rsub|1><around|(|n|)>|)>*\<cdots\>*<around|(|w<rsub|m>+b<rsub|m><around|(|n|)>|)>|)>\<longrightarrow\><around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,M<around|(|w<rsub|1>|)>*\<cdots\>*M<around|(|w<rsub|m>|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>.
    </equation*>

    The concatenated words have distinct points for all sufficiently large
    <math|n>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Fixed translations of individual words, compensated in their
    <math|b<rsub|j>>, preserve the assertion and the gap condition. Make all
    words positive. We prove a stronger vector limit by induction from the
    last cluster: after translating the concatenation so that its first
    cluster has its original position, its vector tends to
    <math|X<around|(|w<rsub|1>|)>*M<around|(|w<rsub|2>|)>*\<cdots\>*M<around|(|w<rsub|m>|)>*\<Omega\>>.
    For one cluster this is <math|X<around|(|w<rsub|1>|)>*\<Omega\>=<around|[|w<rsub|1>|]>>.

    Choose a slab width <math|d> for <math|w<rsub|1>>. Write the displacement
    of the second cluster relative to the first as
    <math|g<rsub|n>*e<rsub|0>+y<rsub|n>>, with <math|y<rsub|n>> spatial.
    Eventually <math|g<rsub|n>\<gtr\>d>. If <math|z<rsub|n>> is the positive
    word vector of the remaining clusters relative to the second, the whole
    vector is <math|A<rsub|d><around|(|w<rsub|1>|)>*U<rsub|y<rsub|n>>*E<rsub|g<rsub|n>-d>*z<rsub|n>>.
    By induction <math|z<rsub|n>\<to\>z=X<around|(|w<rsub|2>|)>*M<around|(|w<rsub|3>|)>*\<cdots\>*M<around|(|w<rsub|m>|)>*\<Omega\>>.
    The estimates

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|U<rsub|y<rsub|n>>*E<rsub|g<rsub|n>-d>*z<rsub|n>-P<rsub|0>*z|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|z<rsub|n>-z|\<\|\|\>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|E<rsub|g<rsub|n>-d>*z-P<rsub|0>*z|\<\|\|\>>\<longrightarrow\>0
    </equation*>

    use contraction, unitarity and <math|U<rsub|y<rsub|n>>*P<rsub|0>*z=P<rsub|0>*z>.
    Boundedness of <math|A<rsub|d><around|(|w<rsub|1>|)>> proves the vector
    limit. The same induction shows positivity and distinctness eventually,
    since the successive time gaps tend to infinity. Pairing the vector limit
    with <math|\<Omega\>>, and using translation invariance and
    <math|P<rsub|0>*\<Omega\>=\<Omega\>>, proves the scalar assertion. The
    empty list has correlation one and needs no induction.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Commutativity><label|ex-ground-commutative> The norm-closed unital
    star algebra <math|\<cal-A\>\<subset\>B<around|(|\<cal-H\><rsub|0>|)>>
    generated by the <math|M<around|(|w|)>> is commutative, and
    <math|\<Omega\>> is cyclic and separating for <math|\<cal-A\>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Write <math|F*<around|(|w<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,w<rsub|m>|)>=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,<big|prod><rsub|j>M<around|(|w<rsub|j>|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>>,
    in the indicated order. If <math|b<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> and
    <math|<around|(|K*b|)><rsub|0>\<gtr\>0>, place the clusters at
    <math|j*n*b>, rotate the configuration by <math|K>, and use
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-cluster-train> before and after rotation.
    Covariance gives <math|F*<around|(|w<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,w<rsub|m>|)>=F*<around|(|K*w<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,K*w<rsub|m>|)>>.

    To exchange the middle two of four words, use one spatial axis
    <math|e<rsub|1>> and displace the four clusters by

    <\equation*>
      0,<space|1em>n*<around|(|e<rsub|0>+2*e<rsub|1>|)>,<space|1em>n*<around|(|2*e<rsub|0>+e<rsub|1>|)>,<space|1em>3*n*<around|(|e<rsub|0>+e<rsub|1>|)>.
    </equation*>

    They are ordered by time as written. Interchange
    <math|e<rsub|0>,e<rsub|1>> by an orthogonal map <math|K>. The resulting
    time order is first, third, second, fourth. Permutation symmetry of
    distinct-point correlations and the cluster limit give
    <math|F*<around|(|x,y,w,z|)>=F*<around|(|K*x,K*w,K*y,K*z|)>>. Use the
    preceding rotation identity with <math|b=e<rsub|0>+e<rsub|1>>, fixed by
    <math|K>, to conclude <math|F*<around|(|x,y,w,z|)>=F*<around|(|x,w,y,z|)>>.

    The vectors <math|M<around|(|v|)>*\<Omega\>=P<rsub|0><around|[|v|]>>, for
    positive words <math|v>, have dense linear span in
    <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|0>>: approximate any vector in
    <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|0>> by word sums in <math|\<cal-H\>> and apply
    <math|P<rsub|0>>. For two generators, their commutator has zero matrix
    elements between these dense vectors, since

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|M<around|(|u|)>*\<Omega\>,<around|[|M<around|(|w|)>,M<around|(|z|)>|]>*M<around|(|v|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>=F*<around|(|\<theta\>*u,w,z,v|)>-F*<around|(|\<theta\>*u,z,w,v|)>=0.
    </equation*>

    The commutator is bounded, hence is zero. The generators and their
    adjoints therefore commute; polynomial approximation and continuity of
    multiplication show that their norm closure is commutative. The same
    density proves cyclicity. If <math|A*\<Omega\>=0>, then
    <math|A*B*\<Omega\>=B*A*\<Omega\>=0> for every <math|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.
    Cyclicity and boundedness give <math|A=0>, which is separation.
  </proof>

  This proves commutativity from the displayed slab and covariance inputs.
  The next construction must establish norm separability of <math|\<cal-A\>>,
  represent the vacuum state on its compact character space, and show that
  each resulting whole-word correlation table retains the required
  regularity, null spaces and field identities. Those conclusions are not
  part of Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-ground-commutative>.

  <subsection|Uniform bounds for arbitrarily many slab
  copies><label|sec:actual-slab-copy-bounds>

  The centered estimates give more than bounds for a fixed number of fields.
  They also control arbitrarily long strings of reflected copies with one
  constant per insertion. This distinction is needed to construct bounded
  operators on the reflection-positive Hilbert space. We prove that
  implication on the same selected family and the same order of limits.

  Write <math|a<rsub|k>> for its decreasing mesh scales, with
  <math|a<rsub|0>=1> and <math|a<rsub|k>\<to\>0>. We use the scale inequality

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:slab-mesh-gentle>a<rsub|k+1>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|\<ast\>>*a<rsub|k><rsup|2>*<space|2em><around|(|c<rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0|)>,
  </equation>

  supplied by the selected mesh. A word is a finite ordered list of distinct
  points with raw field labels; its length is denoted by <math|n>. Reflection
  reverses the word, reflects the points in the time-zero plane, and applies
  the corresponding reflected, conjugated labels. Fix a grade bound <math|D>
  and a bound <math|R> for the norms of the raw labels. All constants below
  may depend on <math|D,R> and the selected family. They will not depend on
  <math|n>, the slab width, its separation buffer, or the number of copies.

  The two inputs from the selected construction are the centered service of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-centered-service>, with its
  uniformity over bounded label sets, and the smooth point limits of that
  very family. We retain its normalization, permutation and translation
  symmetries, and reflection positivity, together with the positive-word
  Hilbert space and contractive heat semigroup constructed earlier. We use
  the expanding order: first the fine mesh limit at a suitable fixed period,
  then the cofinal period limit. The centered estimates and point limits must
  use this same order. No bound at every fixed period is needed for the
  argument below.

  <subsubsection|One price for the entire reflected family>

  For every <math|D,R> there are <math|F\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, <math|J\<gtr\>0>,
  and <math|P=D+1> such that, for <math|k\<geqslant\>F>, every label of grade
  at most <math|D> and norm at most <math|R> has centered root price at most

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:slab-one-price>J*a<rsub|k><rsup|-P>.
  </equation>

  Here the price includes every signed-coordinate transform and adjoint of
  the label, as well as the chamber interiority and stencil fit needed to
  apply the planted inequality.

  To see the uniformity, first bound all those transformed labels by a single
  <math|Q\<gtr\>0>. There are only finitely many grades and signed-coordinate
  transformations. On each finite-dimensional grade space each transformation
  has finite operator norm, and coefficientwise conjugation preserves the raw
  norm. The adjoint of a label is the reflected conjugate
  <math|R<rsub|\<rho\>>*<wide|v|\<bar\>>>. By the commutation of conjugation
  with signed-coordinate transformations in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-reflection-transport>,
  <math|R<rsub|\<rho\>><wide|R<rsub|s>*v|\<bar\>>=R<rsub|\<rho\>*s>*<wide|v|\<bar\>>>.
  This label belongs to the same finite family and is covered by the same
  bound. We do not assume that the signed-coordinate action is isometric.
  Apply the centered service in each grade <math|d\<leqslant\>D>, with norm
  bound <math|Q> and exponent slack <math|1>. In the expanding formulation an
  arbitrarily small additive error is allowed; taking that error to be
  <math|1> gives <math|A<rsub|d>*a<rsub|k><rsup|-<around|(|d+1|)>>+1>. Since
  <math|a<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>1>, this is at most
  <math|<around|(|A<rsub|d>+1|)>*a<rsub|k><rsup|-<around|(|d+1|)>>>. Take the
  maximum of the finitely many grade thresholds and constants and use
  <math|d+1\<leqslant\>P>. Fixed stencils fit eventually at every such
  reference scale. For a fixed word only finitely many labels and
  orientations occur, so their eventual mesh and period requirements have a
  common tail. The price <math|J> itself was selected before that word.

  <subsubsection|Two scales for the main word and the exterior>

  We first record how to combine different centered prices. At finite cutoff
  let <math|T> be a link-cell tiling, and let <math|Y> be an aligned
  subdivision of it. Suppose main insertions lie in distinct <math|T>-cells,
  and no exterior insertion lies in those cells. The exterior insertions may
  share a <math|T>-cell, but lie in distinct <math|Y>-cells. Suppose all
  required margins and stencil fits hold. If the centered price of each main
  insertion is <math|A>, and that of each exterior insertion is
  <math|X\<geqslant\>0>, then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:slab-mixed-cells><around*|\||\<bbb-E\><around*|[|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I>f<rsub|i>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>E>g<rsub|j>|]>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsup|<around|\||I|\|>>*X<rsup|<around|\||E|\|>>.
  </equation>

  These are moments of the original observables; no absolute values have been
  inserted inside the expectation.

  Here is the extra point when several exterior fields share a coarse cell.
  Move that cell to the home cell by its signed reflection, transforming all
  its labels accordingly, and let <math|G> be the product of its <math|h>
  exterior factors. Its coarse root is obtained by copying the entire product
  to all <math|M<rsub|T>> coarse cells. The resulting <math|M<rsub|T>*h>
  factors lie in distinct fine cells: equality of their fine cells first
  forces equality of their coarse cells, by alignment, and then equality of
  the original factors, by their fine-scale separation. Reflections preserve
  the fine margins and separation. The planted inequality in <math|Y>,
  together with the one-field root bounds, therefore gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<bbb-E\><around|[|T<text|-full-copy><around|(|G|)>|]>|\|>\<leqslant\>X<rsup|M<rsub|T>*h>.
  </equation*>

  Taking the <math|M<rsub|T>>th root bounds the coarse root of <math|G> by
  <math|X<rsup|h>>. The empty product is covered by the same argument. The
  chamber-interior property is preserved under finite products. For the main
  insertions the one-field coarse root bound is the bracketing and
  copied-orbit argument of Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-estimates>.
  Apply the coarse planted inequality to these main insertions and the
  grouped exterior products. Their coarse cells are distinct. Multiplying
  their prices proves <eqref|eq:slab-mixed-cells>.

  We also need to place all those cells without paying for the number of main
  replicas. Suppose the main points have only <math|N<rsub|0>> residue
  patterns modulo <math|a<rsub|m>*\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>, and choose levels
  <math|m\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>l\<leqslant\>b> satisfying

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:slab-two-room>N<rsub|0>*<around|(|10*a<rsub|k>|)>\<less\>a<rsub|m>,<space|2em><around|\||E|\|>*<around|(|6*a<rsub|b>|)>\<less\>a<rsub|l>.
  </equation>

  Assume main\Umain and main\Uexterior sup distances exceed <math|a<rsub|m>>,
  and exterior\Uexterior distances exceed <math|a<rsub|l>>. In each
  coordinate, the <math|N<rsub|0>> patterns forbid at most
  <math|N<rsub|0>*<around|(|10*a<rsub|k>|)>> of the possible coarse offsets
  when a margin of <math|5*a<rsub|k>> is required. Thus one can first place
  all main patterns in such margins. At the fine lattice level the identical
  argument is a count of forbidden residues; scale divisibility gives the
  same strict inequalities.

  Choose the exterior offset within one aligned cell of side
  <math|a<rsub|l>>. The second inequality in <eqref|eq:slab-two-room> lets us
  give every exterior point a margin of <math|3*a<rsub|b>>. This changes the
  main offset by at most <math|a<rsub|l>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|k>>. Allow also
  independent perturbations of the main points by at most <math|a<rsub|k>> in
  every coordinate. Their original margin <math|5*a<rsub|k>> then leaves a
  margin of <math|3*a<rsub|k>>. This is why the first placement uses
  <math|5>, rather than <math|3>, fine spans. The same estimate remains valid
  after rounding to the fine mesh: if two coordinates differ by at most
  <math|K*a<rsub|N>> with <math|K> a nonnegative integer, their ceilings in
  units <math|a<rsub|N>> differ by at most <math|K> lattice steps. Congruent
  unperturbed main patterns have identical coarse residues. Thus the number
  of forbidden patterns stays <math|N<rsub|0>>, even though every occurrence
  may be perturbed independently.

  Apply <eqref|eq:slab-mixed-cells> at that cutoff. To pass to continuum
  points, first take a nearby generic configuration where the prescribed
  point-limit theorem applies and where all the strict separation
  inequalities remain valid. At sufficiently large period there is no
  wrapping collision; at sufficiently fine mesh the rounded configuration has
  the required separations and margins. The centered bounds hold on the same
  mesh and period tails. Pass first to the mesh limit and then to the period
  limit. Finally approach the original configuration through generic
  configurations. Their density and smoothness of the limiting correlations
  give <eqref|eq:slab-mixed-cells> there too. This argument uses independent
  perturbations of all slots, and so does not assume point convergence on a
  projection tie or on the lower-dimensional set of exactly congruent
  configurations.

  <subsubsection|Paying for the main word before choosing its exterior>

  Let <math|w\<ne\>\<varnothing\>> lie in a time slab <math|<around|(|0,d|)>>
  and have buffer <math|r>, where <math|0\<less\>r\<leqslant\>1>: distinct
  points have sup distance at least <math|r>, and every time coordinate lies
  in <math|<around|[|r,d-r|]>>. Choose <math|m\<geqslant\>F> minimal with
  <math|a<rsub|m>\<less\>r/32>, and put

  <\equation*>
    s=a<rsub|m>*<around|\<lfloor\>|d/a<rsub|m>|\<rfloor\>>.
  </equation*>

  Then <math|s\<leqslant\>d\<less\>s+a<rsub|m>>. The word remains in the slab
  <math|<around|(|0,s|)>> with buffer <math|r/2>, and <math|s\<gtr\>0>
  because the nonempty word has both boundary margins. Crucially, <math|s>
  has been chosen before any exterior word.

  For later use, if <math|j\<geqslant\>F> is the first level with
  <math|a<rsub|j>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>, where
  <math|0\<less\>\<varepsilon\>\<leqslant\>1>, then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:slab-first-level>t*\<varepsilon\><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|j>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>,<space|2em>t=min
    <around|(|a<rsub|F>,c<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation>

  For <math|j=F> the lower bound follows from
  <math|\<varepsilon\>\<leqslant\>1>; otherwise
  <math|a<rsub|j-1>\<geqslant\>\<varepsilon\>> and
  <eqref|eq:slab-mesh-gentle> proves it. Set <math|b<rsub|0>=t/32<rsup|2>>
  and <math|u=min <around|(|b<rsub|0>/21,1|)>>. For
  <math|n=<around|\||w|\|>\<geqslant\>1>, choose <math|k\<geqslant\>F>
  minimal with <math|a<rsub|k>\<less\>\<delta\>=u*<around|(|r/n|)><rsup|2>>.
  Then

  <\equation*>
    20*n*a<rsub|k>\<less\>20*n*\<delta\>=20*u*r<rsup|2>/n\<leqslant\>20*u*r<rsup|2>\<less\>b<rsub|0>*r<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|m>.
  </equation*>

  In particular <math|k\<geqslant\>m>. Applying <eqref|eq:slab-first-level>
  again gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:slab-fine-lower>a<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>c*<around|(|r/n|)><rsup|4>,<space|2em>c=t*u<rsup|2>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation>

  All main fields and their reflected versions can therefore be paid at this
  one level with price <math|A=J*a<rsub|k><rsup|-P>>, and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:slab-radius-produced>A<rsup|n>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|0><rsup|n>*<around|(|n/r|)><rsup|4*P*n>,<space|2em>C<rsub|0>=J*c<rsup|-P>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation>

  To apply this to repeated insertion, write
  <math|w<rsup|\<sharp\>>=\<tau\><rsub|s>*\<theta\>*w> and
  <math|I<rsub|w>*v=w*<space|0.17em>\<tau\><rsub|s>*v>. The word
  <math|<around|(|I<rsub|w<rsup|\<sharp\>>>*I<rsub|w>|)><rsup|q>*v> consists
  of <math|2*q> alternating copies of the main word followed by one
  translated exterior <math|v>. Its reflection pairing contains <math|4*q*n>
  main fields and <math|2<around|\||v|\|>> exterior fields. Every main field
  is congruent modulo <math|a<rsub|m>*\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>> to one of the
  <math|2*n> points of <math|w> and <math|\<theta\>*w>, since <math|s> is a
  multiple of <math|a<rsub|m>>. Translation and reflection preserve
  separation. Consecutive slabs retain their buffer <math|r/2>, so the main
  points are mutually separated and separated from the exterior by at least
  <math|r/2\<gtr\>a<rsub|m>>. These statements also follow directly by
  induction on <math|q>: attaching one reflected slab and one original slab
  preserves the boundary margins, adds <math|2*n> points, and translates the
  remaining word by <math|2*s>. The final reflected pairing doubles the main
  count and creates exactly two exterior copies, independently of <math|q>.

  Fix now an arbitrary positive exterior word <math|v>. Its finitely many
  points have some positive mutual and boundary buffer <math|t<rsub|v>> and
  some finite grade and norm bounds. Choose <math|l\<geqslant\>k> with
  <math|a<rsub|l>\<less\>t<rsub|v>>, then <math|b\<geqslant\>l> beyond its
  one-field threshold and so fine that <math|2<around|\||v|\|>*<around|(|6*a<rsub|b>|)>\<less\>a<rsub|l>>.
  Let <math|X> be its one-field price at level <math|b>. Neither <math|l,b>
  nor <math|X> depends on <math|q>. The mixed estimate, with
  <math|N<rsub|0>=2*n>, yields

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:slab-replica-produced><mid|\|><around|[|<around|(|I<rsub|w<rsup|\<sharp\>>>*I<rsub|w>|)><rsup|q>*v|]><mid|\|><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>X<rsup|2<around|\||v|\|>>*A<rsup|4*q*n>\<leqslant\>X<rsup|2<around|\||v|\|>>*<around*|(|C<rsub|0><rsup|n>*<around|(|n/r|)><rsup|4*P*n>|)><rsup|4*q>*<space|2em><around|(|q\<geqslant\>0|)>.
  </equation>

  For the empty exterior its cost is <math|1> and the same proof applies. The
  exterior constant may depend on <math|v,w,r>; its independence of <math|q>
  is the essential conclusion.

  <subsubsection|The actual bounded slab operators>

  The repeated-insertion argument of Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-train-bound>
  applies to <eqref|eq:slab-replica-produced>, with
  <math|B<rsub|v>=X<rsup|2<around|\||v|\|>>> and with the norm constant of
  that lemma equal to <math|C<rsub|0><rsup|n>*<around|(|n/r|)><rsup|4*P*n>>.
  (That constant is called <math|D> there; it is unrelated to the grade bound
  <math|D> of this section.) It gives the operator at width <math|s> with
  norm at most <math|C<rsub|0><rsup|n>*<around|(|n/r|)><rsup|4*P*n>>. To
  recover the original width <math|d>, first apply the heat contraction
  <math|E<rsub|d-s>> and then the width-<math|s> insertion. On a positive
  word this produces <math|w*<space|0.17em>\<tau\><rsub|s>*\<tau\><rsub|d-s>*v=w*<space|0.17em>\<tau\><rsub|d>*v>,
  exactly the required insertion. The norm cannot increase. Thus there is a
  common family of slab operators satisfying

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:slab-allcopy-produced><around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|d><around|(|w|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|0><rsup|<around|\||w|\|>>*<around|(|<around|\||w|\|>/r|)><rsup|K<around|\||w|\|>>,<space|2em>K=4*<around|(|D+1|)>,
  </equation>

  for every nonempty word with the stated grade, norm and buffer bounds. For
  the empty word take <math|A<rsub|d><around|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=E<rsub|d>>
  with norm at most <math|1>; the right side is then interpreted as <math|1>.

  The choices used to find a bounded operator may depend on an affordable
  buffer or label bound for a given word. They nevertheless produce a single
  operator: two such choices agree on all planted positive words, whose
  linear span is dense, and are bounded. Hence they agree everywhere. The
  common operator inherits every bound obtained with any admissible
  <math|D,R,r>. This proves the uniform all-copy estimate required by the
  phase construction, on the same hierarchy as the centered service.

  <subsection|Regularity in operator norm><label|ex-slab-regularity>

  To pass from scalar correlation identities to the character construction,
  we need regularity in operator norm. Weak continuity of matrix elements
  alone would not provide it. The additional ingredient is a local uniform
  bound on the operators representing the differentiated insertions.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Operator remainder estimate><label|ex-operator-remainder> Let
    <math|U> be an open subset of a finite-dimensional real normed space
    <math|E>, let <math|H> be a complex Hilbert space, and let
    <math|\<cal-D\>> be a dense linear subspace. Suppose
    <math|A:U\<to\>B<around|(|H|)>> and <math|B:U\<to\>\<cal-L\><rsub|\<bbb-R\>>*<around|(|E,B<around|(|H|)>|)>>
    satisfy

    <\equation*>
      D*<around|\<langle\>|u,A|(>\<cdot\><around|)|v|\<rangle\>><around|(|z|)><around|[|h|]>=<around|\<langle\>|u,B<around|(|z|)><around|[|h|]>*v|\<rangle\>><space|1em><around|(|u,v\<in\>\<cal-D\>,z\<in\>U,h\<in\>E|)>.
    </equation*>

    If the segment from <math|x> to <math|y> lies in <math|U> and
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|B<around|(|z|)>-B<rsub|0>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C>
    along the segment, with <math|C\<geqslant\>0>, then

    <\equation>
      <label|ex-operator-remainder-bound><around|\<\|\|\>|A<around|(|y|)>-A<around|(|x|)>-B<rsub|0>*<around|[|y-x|]>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*<around|\<\|\|\>|y-x|\<\|\|\>>.
    </equation>

    Consequently, local boundedness of <math|B> implies local Lipschitz
    continuity of <math|A>; continuity of <math|B> implies <math|D*A=B> in
    operator norm.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Fix <math|u,v\<in\>\<cal-D\>>. Along the segment, subtract the linear
    function <math|z\<mapsto\><around|\<langle\>|u,B<rsub|0><around|[|z|]>*v|\<rangle\>>>.
    The derivative of the resulting complex-valued function has norm at most
    <math|C<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>>. The
    scalar mean-value inequality yields

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||<around|\<langle\>|u,<around|(|A<around|(|y|)>-A<around|(|x|)>-B<rsub|0>*<around|[|y-x|]>|)>*v|\<rangle\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>*<around|\<\|\|\>|y-x|\<\|\|\>>.
    </equation*>

    This inequality also follows directly by composing with the segment and
    then with every real-linear functional of norm one on <math|\<bbb-C\>>,
    using the real mean-value theorem, and taking the supremum. Density
    extends the inequality to all <math|u,v\<in\>H>. For any bounded operator
    <math|T>, the supremum of <math|<around|\||<around|\<langle\>|u,T*v|\<rangle\>>|\|>>
    over unit vectors <math|u,v> is <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|T|\<\|\|\>>>:
    Cauchy\USchwarz gives one inequality and, when <math|T*v\<ne\>0>, the
    choice <math|u=T*v/<around|\<\|\|\>|T*v|\<\|\|\>>> gives the other. This
    proves <eqref|ex-operator-remainder-bound>.

    On a sufficiently small ball where <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|B|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C>,
    use <math|B<rsub|0>=0> to obtain the Lipschitz bound. If <math|B> is
    continuous at <math|x>, then for any <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0> choose
    such a ball with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|B<around|(|z|)>-B<around|(|x|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>>.
    Taking <math|B<rsub|0>=B<around|(|x|)>> proves that the remainder is at
    most <math|\<epsilon\>*<around|\<\|\|\>|y-x|\<\|\|\>>>, which is
    precisely Fréchet differentiability with derivative
    <math|B<around|(|x|)>>.
  </proof>

  We apply the lemma to the slab operators of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-phases>. For fixed width <math|d\<gtr\>0>,
  arity <math|n>, and labels <math|\<b-v\>=<around|(|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|n>|)>>,
  write <math|A<rsub|\<b-v\>><around|(|x|)>=A<rsub|d>*<around|(|<around|(|x<rsub|1>,v<rsub|1>|)>*\<cdots\><around|(|x<rsub|n>,v<rsub|n>|)>|)>>
  on

  <\equation*>
    U<rsub|d,n>=<around|{|x:x<rsub|i>\<ne\>x<rsub|j>*<around|(|i\<ne\>j|)>,<space|1em>0\<less\><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsub|0>\<less\>d|}>.
  </equation*>

  This is open, since finitely many strict inequalities describe the time
  conditions and finitely many diagonals have been excluded.

  The two quantitative and differential inputs are as follows. First, for
  each grade bound <math|D> and label norm bound <math|R>, there are
  <math|C\<gtr\>0> and <math|K\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that every slab word of
  length <math|n>, with these label bounds, satisfies

  <\equation>
    <label|ex-all-copy-bound><around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|d><around|(|w|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsup|n>*<around|(|n/r|)><rsup|K*n>
  </equation>

  whenever <math|0\<less\>r\<leqslant\>1>, its points have mutual sup-norm
  distances at least <math|r>, and each time coordinate lies in
  <math|<around|[|r,d-r|]>>. The constants are independent of <math|n,d> and
  the positions. For the empty word the right side is interpreted as one, in
  agreement with <math|A<rsub|d><around|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=E<rsub|d>>.
  Second, for each raw label <math|v> of grade <math|d<rprime|'>> and each
  coordinate <math|j>, choose a raw label <math|\<delta\><rsub|j>*v> of grade
  <math|d<rprime|'>+1> representing its field derivative. Assume that the
  smooth scalar correlations obey the exact slot identity

  <\equation>
    <label|ex-raw-slot-identity>\<partial\><rsub|<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsub|j>>*S<rsub|\<b-v\>><around|(|x|)>=S<rsub|\<b-v\>*<around|[|i\<leftarrow\>\<delta\><rsub|j>*v<rsub|i>|]>><around|(|x|)>
  </equation>

  at distinct points, also with any finite list of exterior insertions. The
  choice of raw representative need not itself be linear. For a phase using
  the original field quotient, the identity includes the assertion that the
  chosen representatives have the indicated values in that phase.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Smooth slab operators><label|ex-smooth-slabs> Under
    <eqref|ex-all-copy-bound> and <eqref|ex-raw-slot-identity>,
    <math|A<rsub|\<b-v\>>:U<rsub|d,n>\<to\>B<around|(|\<cal-H\>|)>> is smooth
    in operator norm, and

    <\equation*>
      \<partial\><rsub|<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsub|j>>*A<rsub|\<b-v\>><around|(|x|)>=A<rsub|\<b-v\>*<around|[|i\<leftarrow\>\<delta\><rsub|j>*v<rsub|i>|]>><around|(|x|)>.
    </equation*>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Fix a point <math|x\<in\>U<rsub|d,n>>. Choose
    <math|0\<less\>r\<leqslant\>1> below its finitely many positive time
    margins and pairwise distances. In a neighborhood of <math|x>, the same
    margins and distances are at least <math|r/2>. For every fixed label
    tuple, <eqref|ex-all-copy-bound> therefore gives a local operator bound.
    The same holds for every one-slot differentiated tuple, since there are
    finitely many labels and their grades and norms have finite maxima.

    Define the real-linear derivative candidate

    <\equation*>
      B<rsub|\<b-v\>><around|(|x|)><around|[|h|]>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|3>h<rsub|i,j>*A<rsub|\<b-v\>*<around|[|i\<leftarrow\>\<delta\><rsub|j>*v<rsub|i>|]>><around|(|x|)>.
    </equation*>

    The finite collection of local bounds gives a local bound on
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsub|\<b-v\>><around|(|x|)>|\<\|\|\>>>. For
    positive words <math|p,q>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|<around|[|p|]>,A<rsub|\<b-v\>><around|(|x|)><around|[|q|]>|\<rangle\>>=S*<around|(|<around|(|\<theta\>*p|)>*<space|0.17em>w*<around|(|x,\<b-v\>|)>*<space|0.17em>\<tau\><rsub|d>*q|)>.
    </equation*>

    The exterior points lie respectively below zero and above <math|d>, so
    all configurations remain distinct near <math|x>. Differentiating by
    <eqref|ex-raw-slot-identity> gives exactly the matrix elements of
    <math|B<rsub|\<b-v\>>>. Sesquilinearity extends this statement to finite
    word sums, a dense linear subspace. The remainder lemma now proves norm
    continuity of <math|A<rsub|\<b-v\>>>, for every tuple.

    In particular each differentiated tuple has norm-continuous operators.
    Thus the finite sum defining <math|B<rsub|\<b-v\>>> is norm-continuous. A
    second application of the remainder lemma proves
    <math|D*A<rsub|\<b-v\>>=B<rsub|\<b-v\>>>. Inductively, if all the tuple
    maps are <math|C<rsup|k>>, then each <math|B<rsub|\<b-v\>>> is
    <math|C<rsup|k>> as a finite sum of fixed coordinate functionals times
    such maps. The derivative formula makes all tuple maps
    <math|C<rsup|k+1>>. Starting from continuity proves smoothness of every
    finite order, with the stated coordinate derivatives.
  </proof>

  <\corollary>
    <dueto|Norm separability of the compression
    algebra><label|ex-norm-separable>If the scalar correlations are
    multilinear in the finite-dimensional raw label spaces, the hypotheses of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-smooth-slabs> imply that <math|\<cal-A\>>
    is separable in operator norm.
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    Fix a finite basis in every raw label space. At fixed positions, the slab
    operator is multilinear in the labels: pairing the purported linear
    identity with any two word vectors reduces it to scalar multilinearity;
    density then proves equality of bounded operators. Every compressed word
    operator is therefore a finite linear combination of ones with basis
    labels.

    Place a word in positive time and choose a positive rational width
    greater than all its time coordinates. Approximate its distinct positions
    by rational positions inside this slab. Operator-norm continuity from
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-smooth-slabs>, followed by the bounded
    compression <math|A\<mapsto\>P<rsub|0>*A\|<rsub|\<cal-H\><rsub|0>>>,
    proves norm convergence of the compressed operators. There are countably
    many rational widths, rational configurations, arities and finite
    basis-label tuples. Their compressed operators thus generate a norm-dense
    subalgebra. Star polynomials in this countable set with coefficients in
    <math|\<bbb-Q\>+i*\<bbb-Q\>> form a countable set dense in the whole
    norm-closed algebra: approximate the finitely many generators and
    coefficients in any given polynomial, and then use the definition of the
    closure. This is the required norm separability.
  </proof>

  The first analytic input, the estimate uniform in the length of the word,
  is stronger than the separated-correlation bound used to construct time
  translations. The second input is an exact differential identity. The
  argument above supplies operator smoothness and norm separability from
  these two inputs; it leaves their derivation from the lattice fields to the
  construction chapters.

  <subsection|Characters and selection of a vacuum><label|ex-phase-selection>

  The preceding section constructs a commutative algebra on the whole ground
  space. Its characters provide a useful way of separating the vacuum state
  into components. The argument below also explains why a one-dimensional
  ground space is first obtained almost everywhere, rather than assumed for
  the unselected table. After relativistic reconstruction, a uniform
  low-energy estimate extends vacuum uniqueness to every character.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The representing probability><label|ex-character-measure> Suppose
    the algebra <math|\<cal-A\>> of Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-ground-commutative>
    is separable in operator norm. Its space <math|\<Xi\>> of unital complex
    star characters, with the topology of pointwise convergence on
    <math|\<cal-A\>>, is compact and metrizable. There is a probability
    measure <math|\<mu\>> on <math|\<Xi\>>, positive on every nonempty open
    set, such that

    <\equation>
      <label|ex-spectral-state><big|int><rsub|\<Xi\>>\<chi\><around|(|A|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><around|(|\<chi\>|)>=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,A*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>*<space|1em><around|(|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|)>.
    </equation>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The algebra is a nonzero unital commutative
    <math|C<rsup|\<ast\>>>-algebra: it is a norm-closed star subalgebra of
    <math|B<around|(|\<cal-H\><rsub|0>|)>>, and
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Omega\>|\<\|\|\>>=1>. The commutative Gelfand
    theorem identifies it isometrically, as a star algebra, with
    <math|C<around|(|\<Xi\>|)>>, by <math|A\<mapsto\><wide|A|^>>, where
    <math|<wide|A|^><around|(|\<chi\>|)>=\<chi\><around|(|A|)>>
    <cite-detail|Williams|Theorem 2.13>. Its character space is compact since
    the algebra is unital.

    Choose a countable norm-dense sequence <math|<around|(|A<rsub|j>|)>> in
    <math|\<cal-A\>>. The continuous map <math|\<chi\>\<mapsto\><around|(|\<chi\><around|(|A<rsub|j>|)>|)><rsub|j>>
    into <math|\<bbb-C\><rsup|\<bbb-N\>>> is injective: equality on the dense
    sequence implies equality everywhere by continuity of the characters. A
    continuous injection of a compact space into a Hausdorff space is a
    homeomorphism onto its image (images of closed sets are compact and hence
    closed). The countable product is metrizable, for example by
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|j>2<rsup|-j-1>*min
    <around|(|1,<around|\||z<rsub|j>-z<rprime|'><rsub|j>|\|>|)>>, so
    <math|\<Xi\>> is metrizable.

    Transfer the functional <math|A\<mapsto\><around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,A*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>>
    to <math|C<around|(|\<Xi\>|)>>. It is positive: a nonnegative continuous
    function is <math|<wide|g|\<bar\>>*g> for its continuous nonnegative
    square root <math|g>, and its inverse Gelfand transform has the form
    <math|B<rsup|\<ast\>>*B>. The functional takes the value
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|B*\<Omega\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>0> there.
    The Riesz representation theorem supplies a positive Borel measure
    representing it <cite-detail|Sawyer|Chapter 6, Theorem 32>. Compactness
    gives <math|C<rsub|c><around|(|\<Xi\>|)>=C<around|(|\<Xi\>|)>>, so that
    theorem applies directly. At the constant function one it gives
    <math|\<mu\><around|(|\<Xi\>|)>=<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Omega\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=1>.

    To prove full support, suppose a nonempty open set <math|O> has zero
    measure. Choose <math|x\<in\>O> and <math|r\<gtr\>0> with
    <math|B*<around|(|x,r|)>\<subset\>O>. The function
    <math|f<around|(|y|)>=max <around|(|0,r-<math-up|dist><around|(|x,y|)>|)>>
    is continuous, nonzero at <math|x>, and zero outside <math|O>. Let
    <math|A> be its inverse Gelfand transform. Then <eqref|ex-spectral-state>
    applied to <math|A<rsup|\<ast\>>*A> gives
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A*\<Omega\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<big|int><around|\||f|\|><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\>=0>.
    Separation of <math|\<Omega\>> forces <math|A=0>, contradicting
    <math|f<around|(|x|)>=r>. Thus every such open set has positive measure.
  </proof>

  For <math|\<chi\>\<in\>\<Xi\>>, define the whole-word values

  <\equation>
    <label|ex-phase-word>S<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|w|)>=\<chi\><around|(|M<around|(|w|)>|)><space|1em><text|for
    distinct-point words >w.
  </equation>

  It is essential to apply the character to the operator for the entire word.
  The compressed operator <math|M*<around|(|w<rsub|1>*w<rsub|2>|)>> need not
  equal <math|M<around|(|w<rsub|1>|)>*M<around|(|w<rsub|2>|)>> at finite
  separation. Equations<nbsp><eqref|ex-spectral-state> and
  <eqref|ex-compression-values> give the barycenter identity

  <\equation>
    <label|ex-phase-barycenter>S<around|(|w|)>=<big|int><rsub|\<Xi\>>S<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|w|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><around|(|\<chi\>|)>.
  </equation>

  For a fixed word the integrand is continuous, and hence integrable.

  Some properties of <math|S<rsub|\<chi\>>> already follow at this level.
  Normalization and translation invariance follow from
  <math|M<around|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=I> and <eqref|ex-compression-values>. It
  is also reflection positive. For positive words <math|v<rsub|i>> and
  coefficients <math|c<rsub|i>>, the Gram identity in the preceding section
  gives

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|i,j><wide|c<rsub|i>|\<bar\>>c<rsub|j>*M*<around|(|<around|(|\<theta\>*v<rsub|i>|)>*v<rsub|j>|)>=<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i>c<rsub|i>*X<around|(|v<rsub|i>|)>|)><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|j>c<rsub|j>*X<around|(|v<rsub|j>|)>|)>\<geqslant\>0<space|1em><text|in
    >B<around|(|\<cal-H\><rsub|0>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The operator on the left lies in <math|\<cal-A\>>. Positivity is the same
  in a unital <math|C<rsup|\<ast\>>>-subalgebra as in the ambient operator
  algebra (spectral permanence and continuous functional calculus give its
  nonnegative square root in the subalgebra; see
  <cite-detail|Williams|Theorems 2.17 and 2.19>). Characters take positive
  elements to nonnegative numbers. Applying <math|\<chi\>> proves the desired
  Gram positivity. Regularity and the remaining covariance and field
  identities still need their own operator estimates; the barycenter formula
  alone does not establish them.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|A common countable collection of words><label|ex-phase-horizon>
    Suppose the raw label spaces are finite-dimensional complex vector spaces
    indexed by the nonnegative integers. Suppose that each
    <math|S<rsub|\<chi\>>> is multilinear in its labels and continuous on the
    distinct-point configuration space, and has the Hilbert realization of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-gns>. There is one countable collection of
    positive words whose vectors span a dense subspace in every
    <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|\<chi\>>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Choose a finite basis in every raw label space. Take all positive words
    with rational coordinates and with basis-vector labels, for every arity
    and every finite list of label grades; include the empty word. There are
    countably many choices. Every word is a finite linear combination of
    words with basis labels, by expanding each label in its basis. This
    identity holds for the Hilbert-space vectors: pair the difference with
    any word vector and use multilinearity of the correlation in the
    positive-word labels. All such inner products vanish, so density forces
    the difference to be zero. For a fixed basis-labelled positive word,
    approximate its positions by rational positions, remaining within the
    open set of positive distinct-point configurations. Its vectors converge
    in every <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|\<chi\>>>: the squared norm of their
    difference is the sum of two diagonal Gram values minus twice the real
    part of the cross Gram value, and continuity makes this tend to zero. The
    reflected and positive halves remain strictly separated throughout this
    limit. The full word span is dense by construction; thus the selected
    countable span is dense as well. The positions and bases were chosen
    without reference to <math|\<chi\>>, so the collection is common to every
    phase.
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|A one-dimensional ground space in almost every
    phase><label|ex-pure-ground>Assume every <math|S<rsub|\<chi\>>> has the
    Euclidean Hilbert-space dynamics of Theorem<nbsp><reference|ex-euclidean-semigroup>,
    with unit vacuum <math|\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>>. Assume there is a common
    countable collection of positive words whose vectors span densely in
    every phase Hilbert space. Then, for <math|\<mu\>>-almost every
    <math|\<chi\>>,

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-H\><rsub|\<chi\>,0>=\<bbb-C\>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>.
    </equation*>

    In particular there exists a phase with this property.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Fix a positive word <math|v>. For integers <math|n\<geqslant\>0>, set

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|n><around|(|\<chi\>|)>=<around|\<\|\|\>|E<rsub|\<chi\>,n><around|[|v|]><rsub|\<chi\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|<around|(|\<theta\>*\<tau\><rsub|n>*v|)>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|n>*v|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Each is a continuous function of <math|\<chi\>>, by
    <eqref|ex-phase-word>. Contractivity gives
    <math|0\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|0>>; the latter is
    integrable on the compact probability space. The large-time projection
    theorem gives <math|f<rsub|n><around|(|\<chi\>|)>\<to\><around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|\<chi\>,0><around|[|v|]><rsub|\<chi\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>.
    Dominated convergence and the barycenter identity imply

    <\equation>
      <label|ex-phase-ground-average><big|int><rsub|\<Xi\>><around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|\<chi\>,0><around|[|v|]><rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|\<\|\|\>|<rsup|2><space|0.17em>d*\<mu\>=lim<rsub|n\<to\>\<infty\>>|\<\|\|\>>*E<rsub|n><around|[|v|]>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|0><around|[|v|]>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>.
    </equation>

    All limit functions here are measurable as pointwise limits of the
    continuous <math|f<rsub|n>>.

    Apply <eqref|ex-spectral-state> to <math|M<around|(|v|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*M<around|(|v|)>>.
    Since characters preserve products and adjoints and
    <math|M<around|(|v|)>*\<Omega\>=P<rsub|0><around|[|v|]>>,

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsub|\<Xi\>><around|\||S<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|v|)>|\|><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\>=<around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|0><around|[|v|]>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    Moreover <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>,P<rsub|\<chi\>,0><around|[|v|]><rsub|\<chi\>>|\<rangle\>>=S<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|v|)>>,
    because the projection fixes the vacuum. Orthogonal decomposition along
    the unit vector <math|\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>> gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|\<chi\>,0><around|[|v|]><rsub|\<chi\>>-S<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|v|)>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|\<chi\>,0><around|[|v|]><rsub|\<chi\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>-<around|\||S<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|v|)>|\|><rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    The nonnegative right side is integrable and, by the two preceding
    identities, has integral zero. It is therefore zero almost everywhere.

    Apply this conclusion to the common countable collection and intersect
    its full-measure sets. For every <math|\<chi\>> in the intersection, the
    projection of its dense word span lies in
    <math|\<bbb-C\>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>>. This one-dimensional subspace
    is closed. Continuity of the projection therefore implies
    <math|<math-up|ran><space|0.17em>P<rsub|\<chi\>,0>\<subset\>\<bbb-C\>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>>.
    The reverse inclusion holds because the vacuum is fixed. Finally a set of
    measure one in a probability space is nonempty.
  </proof>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Only the ground space is used in the
    selection><label|rem:ex-selection-ground-only>The character
    <math|\<chi\><rsub|\<ast\>>> is chosen from the full-measure set of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|ex-pure-ground>. No condition on the
    short-distance behaviour of its correlations enters the choice. The trace
    asymptotic of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:trace-asymptotic> is proved
    afterwards at the selected character. The comparison bounds in
    Step<nbsp>2 of Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:trace-rounded-comparison>
    hold uniformly over all characters, and the identification in its
    Step<nbsp>3 uses the null relations at the selected character. One could
    add a second condition to the selection: for every
    <math|e\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>, the product <math|r<rsup|8>> times the two-point
    function of the shifted trace <math|\<Theta\>+e> tends to zero along a
    fixed subsequence of radii. This would change nothing. That two-point
    function equals <math|T<around|(|r|)>+e<rsup|2>>, because the one-point
    function of <math|\<Theta\>> vanishes. The conclusion
    <eqref|ex-trace-limit> gives <math|r<rsup|8>*T<around|(|r|)>\<to\>0>, so
    the condition holds at <math|\<chi\><rsub|\<ast\>>> for every <math|e>
    and along every sequence <math|r*\<downarrow\>*0>.
  </remark>

  This selection argument requires the phase Hilbert spaces and their
  contraction semigroups before it can be applied. It does not construct them
  from a probability decomposition alone. Retention of the raw-label null
  spaces, field derivatives, stress tensor and relativistic reconstruction
  remain additional obligations for the chosen phase.

  <subsubsection|Vacuum uniqueness at every character>

  Almost-everywhere vacuum uniqueness does not by itself imply uniqueness at
  every character: a positive spectral value could approach zero at an
  exceptional character. The following quantitative argument excludes this
  possibility. It uses the relativistic reconstruction of each character
  <em|without> its vacuum-uniqueness clause. That construction uses the
  common phase caps and slab estimates of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-regularity>, the continuation and
  boundary arguments, and the word construction. Its forward spectrum follows
  from the first assertion of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:field-spectrum>,
  which does not assume a unique vacuum. Thus the use of that assertion below
  is not circular.

  For a character <math|\<chi\>>, write <math|U<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|t,x|)>>
  for the reconstructed translations, with <math|t\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> and
  <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|3>>, and let <math|P<rsub|\<chi\>>> be their
  momentum measure. Our convention is <math|U<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|t,x|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|i*<around|(|E*t+p\<cdot\>x|)>>*<space|0.17em>d*P<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|E,p|)>>.
  Write <math|H<rsub|\<chi\>>> for the nonnegative energy generator and
  <math|Q<rsub|\<chi\>>=P<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|<around|{|E=0|}>|)>>. Its
  range is the translation-invariant space, since the forward cone intersects
  <math|<around|{|E=0|}>> only at the origin. The vacuum belongs to this
  range.

  A compactly smeared word sum is a finite linear combination
  <math|A<rsub|\<chi\>>> of products of fields with smooth compactly
  supported spacetime tests, acting on the common invariant domain. The
  labels, coefficients and tests are fixed independently of <math|\<chi\>>.
  Its formal adjoint <math|A<rsub|\<chi\>><rsup|\<ast\>>> is obtained by
  reversing each word and conjugating labels, tests and coefficients. Both
  <math|A<rsub|\<chi\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>> and
  <math|A<rsub|\<chi\>><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>> are well
  defined. All norms and scalar products below are vacuum word correlations,
  so their dependence on <math|\<chi\>> is controlled by the common boundary
  construction.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|A uniform low-energy estimate><label|lem:phase-uniform-low-energy>For
    each compactly smeared word sum there is
    <math|D<rsub|A>\<less\>\<infty\>>, independent of the character, such
    that its positive spectral measure

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|B|)>=<around|\<langle\>|A<rsub|\<chi\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>,P<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|B|)>*A<rsub|\<chi\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>|\<rangle\>>
    </equation*>

    satisfies

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:phase-low-energy>\<mu\><rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|{|0\<less\>E\<leqslant\>u|}>\<leqslant\>D<rsub|A>*u<rsup|2>*<space|2em><around|(|u\<gtr\>0|)>.
    </equation>

    Consequently, for every <math|s\<gtr\>0>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:phase-heat-remainder>0\<leqslant\><around|\<langle\>|A<rsub|\<chi\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>,<around|(|e<rsup|-s*H<rsub|\<chi\>>>-Q<rsub|\<chi\>>|)>*A<rsub|\<chi\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>|\<rangle\>>\<leqslant\>2*D<rsub|A>*s<rsup|-2>.
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|\<nu\><rsub|\<chi\>>> be the analogous spectral measure for
    <math|A<rsub|\<chi\>><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>>. The
    boundary continuity in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:assembly-boundary-barycenter>
    makes both total masses continuous in <math|\<chi\>>. Compactness of the
    character space gives one <math|M\<less\>\<infty\>> bounding their sum.
    Both measures are supported in the forward cone.

    Choose <math|T,S\<less\>\<infty\>> so that every test support lies in
    <math|<around|{|<around|\||t|\|>\<leqslant\>T,<around|\||x|\|>\<leqslant\>S|}>>,
    and take <math|R\<gtr\>1+2*T+2*S>. For
    <math|<around|\||t|\|>\<leqslant\>1> and <math|<around|\||x|\|>\<gtr\>R>,
    every point in a test support translated by <math|<around|(|t,x|)>> is
    spacelike separated from every point in any untranslated test support:
    the spatial distance exceeds <math|<around|\||x|\|>-2*S\<gtr\>1+2*T>,
    whereas the time difference is at most <math|1+2*T>. Locality allows all
    factors in the translated word sum to commute past all factors of the
    untranslated adjoint word sum on the invariant domain. Vacuum translation
    invariance therefore gives

    <\equation*>
      \<delta\><rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|t,x|)>\<assign\><big|int>e<rsup|i*<around|(|E*t+p\<cdot\>x|)>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|E,p|)>-<big|int>e<rsup|-i*<around|(|E*t+p\<cdot\>x|)>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|E,p|)>=0.
    </equation*>

    Indeed these two terms are respectively
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>,A<rsub|\<chi\>><rsup|\<ast\>>*A<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|t,x|)>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>|\<rangle\>>>
    and <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>,A<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|t,x|)>*A<rsub|\<chi\>><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>|\<rangle\>>>.
    The same argument covers finite sums and the empty word.

    Put

    <\equation*>
      h<around|(|E|)>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1>sin
      <around|(|t*E|)>*<space|0.17em>d*t=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|1-cos
      E|)>/E,>|<cell|E\<gtr\>0,>>|<row|<cell|0,>|<cell|E=0,>>>>><space|2em>g<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|x|)>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1><frac|\<delta\><rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|t,x|)>-\<delta\><rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|-t,x|)>|2*i>*<space|0.17em>d*t.
    </equation*>

    For <math|E\<geqslant\>0>, <math|0\<leqslant\>h<around|(|E|)>\<leqslant\>1>;
    for <math|0\<leqslant\>E\<leqslant\>1>, <math|sin
    <around|(|t*E|)>\<geqslant\>2*t*E/\<pi\>> and hence
    <math|h<around|(|E|)>\<geqslant\>E/\<pi\>>. Fubini is justified by the
    finite masses. It expresses <math|g<rsub|\<chi\>>> as the Fourier
    transform, with positive exponent, of the positive measure

    <\equation*>
      \<rho\><rsub|\<chi\>>=<around|(|p|)><rsub|\<ast\>>*<around|(|h<around|(|E|)>*\<mu\><rsub|\<chi\>>|)>+<around|(|-p|)><rsub|\<ast\>>*<around|(|h<around|(|E|)>*\<nu\><rsub|\<chi\>>|)><space|1em><text|on
      >\<bbb-R\><rsup|3>.
    </equation*>

    The plus sign in the second term is important: time reversal changes the
    sign of its sine as well as the sign preceding
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|\<chi\>>>. We have <math|<around|\||g<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M>
    and <math|g<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|x|)>=0> for
    <math|<around|\||x|\|>\<gtr\>R>.

    For <math|0\<less\>u\<leqslant\>1>, choose a smooth
    <math|B<rsub|u>:\<bbb-R\><rsup|3>\<to\><around|[|0,1|]>> equal to one on
    the ball of radius <math|u> and supported in the ball of radius
    <math|2*u>. Define <math|<wide|B|^><rsub|u><around|(|x|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|-i*p\<cdot\>x>*B<rsub|u><around|(|p|)>*<space|0.17em>d*p>.
    Then <math|<around|\||<wide|B|^><rsub|u><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><math-up|vol><around|(|B<rsub|2*u>|)>>.
    Fourier inversion and Fubini give

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|3>*<big|int>B<rsub|u>*<space|0.17em>d*\<rho\><rsub|\<chi\>>=<big|int>g<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|x|)>*<wide|B|^><rsub|u><around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x\<leqslant\>M<math-up|vol><around|(|B<rsub|R>|)><math-up|vol><around|(|B<rsub|2>|)>*u<rsup|3>.
    </equation*>

    For Fubini one may use the rapid decay of <math|<wide|B|^><rsub|u>> and
    the finite mass of <math|\<rho\><rsub|\<chi\>>>. The left side is real
    and nonnegative; the inequality follows by taking the absolute value of
    the right side. On <math|u/2\<less\>E\<leqslant\>u>, cone support gives
    <math|<around|\||p|\|>\<leqslant\>u>, so <math|B<rsub|u><around|(|p|)>=1>
    and <math|h<around|(|E|)>\<geqslant\>u/<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)>>. Positivity
    of both terms in <math|\<rho\><rsub|\<chi\>>> therefore yields

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|{|u/2\<less\>E\<leqslant\>u|}>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|A>*u<rsup|2>,<space|2em>C<rsub|A>=<frac|M<math-up|vol><around|(|B<rsub|R>|)><math-up|vol><around|(|B<rsub|2>|)>|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|2>>.
    </equation*>

    The disjoint dyadic rings with upper endpoints <math|2<rsup|-j>*u> cover
    <math|<around|{|0\<less\>E\<leqslant\>u|}>>. Summing their bounds gives
    at most <math|<around|(|4/3|)>*C<rsub|A>*u<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2*C<rsub|A>*u<rsup|2>>.
    For <math|u\<gtr\>1> use <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)>\<leqslant\>M\<leqslant\>M*u<rsup|2>>.
    Thus <eqref|eq:phase-low-energy> holds with <math|D<rsub|A>=max
    <around|(|M,2*C<rsub|A>|)>>.

    Finally the spectral theorem identifies the middle expression of
    <eqref|eq:phase-heat-remainder> with <math|<big|int><rsub|E\<gtr\>0>e<rsup|-s*E>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|\<chi\>>>.
    Since <math|e<rsup|-s*E>=<big|int><rsub|E><rsup|\<infty\>>s*e<rsup|-s*t>*<space|0.17em>d*t>,
    Tonelli and <eqref|eq:phase-low-energy> bound this by

    <\equation*>
      s*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>>e<rsup|-s*t>*\<mu\><rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|{|0\<less\>E\<leqslant\>t|}>*<space|0.17em>d*t\<leqslant\>D<rsub|A>*s*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>>t<rsup|2>*e<rsup|-s*t>*<space|0.17em>d*t=2*D<rsub|A>*s<rsup|-2>.
    </equation*>

    All constants depend on the fixed word sum, not on the character.
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|The vacuum line in every reconstructed
    character><label|thm:ex-every-character-vacuum>For every character
    <math|\<chi\>>, the translation-invariant vectors in its reconstructed
    Hilbert space are exactly <math|\<bbb-C\>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    For almost every character, Theorem<nbsp><reference|ex-pure-ground> and
    the heat-vector isometry imply this assertion by the last part of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:field-spectrum>. Fix a compactly smeared word
    sum and <math|s\<gtr\>0>. The scalar function

    <\equation*>
      \<chi\>\<longmapsto\><around|\<langle\>|A<rsub|\<chi\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>,e<rsup|-s*H<rsub|\<chi\>>>*A<rsub|\<chi\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>|\<rangle\>>-<around|\||<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>,A<rsub|\<chi\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>|\<rangle\>>|\|><rsup|2>
    </equation*>

    is continuous by Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:assembly-boundary-barycenter>:
    the heat pairing is the same vacuum word distribution at a fixed
    nonnegative imaginary gap <math|s> between its two halves. Indeed the
    mixed Gram identity <eqref|eq:point-gram-continuation>, integrated
    against the tests, gives this identity at strictly positive internal
    heights. Send those heights to zero using the independent-height vector
    limits of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:heat-vectors>. The insertion of
    <math|e<rsup|-s*H<rsub|\<chi\>>>> is a bounded contraction and commutes
    with the translation intertwiner, so the vector pairings converge. The
    common weakly ordered boundary limit gives the stated fixed-gap
    distribution. On the full-measure set just obtained,
    <math|Q<rsub|\<chi\>>> is the vacuum projection, so
    <eqref|eq:phase-heat-remainder> places this function in the closed
    interval <math|<around|[|0,2*D<rsub|A>*s<rsup|-2>|]>>. The bound holds
    everywhere. Otherwise continuity would give a nonempty open set on which
    it fails, and full support of <math|\<mu\>> would give this set positive
    measure, a contradiction.

    Fix now any character and let <math|s\<to\>\<infty\>>. Spectral dominated
    convergence gives <math|e<rsup|-s*H<rsub|\<chi\>>>\<to\>Q<rsub|\<chi\>>>
    strongly. The preceding bound implies

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|Q<rsub|\<chi\>>*A<rsub|\<chi\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<around|\||<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>,A<rsub|\<chi\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>|\<rangle\>>|\|><rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|Q<rsub|\<chi\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>=\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>>,
    orthogonal decomposition along the unit vector
    <math|\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>> shows

    <\equation*>
      Q<rsub|\<chi\>>*A<rsub|\<chi\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>,A<rsub|\<chi\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>|\<rangle\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<chi\>>.
    </equation*>

    Such compactly smeared word vectors span densely. To verify the compact
    support restriction, choose a smooth cutoff <math|\<zeta\>=1> near zero
    and replace each Schwartz test <math|f> by
    <math|\<zeta\>*<around|(|<space|0.17em>\<cdummy\>/n|)>*f>. These converge
    to <math|f> in every Schwartz seminorm, by Leibniz' rule and rapid decay
    on the escaping support of the cutoff derivatives. Joint continuity of
    word correlations implies convergence of the resulting word vectors: the
    squared norm difference is a finite sum of convergent Gram values.
    Cyclicity then gives density. Since <math|Q<rsub|\<chi\>>> is bounded and
    the vacuum line is closed, its range on the whole Hilbert space lies in
    that line. The reverse inclusion follows from vacuum invariance.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Regularity and bounded insertions in the component
  theories><label|ex-phase-regularity>

  Several hypotheses in the selection theorem can now be supplied from the
  unselected operator estimates. Assume in this section that
  <eqref|ex-all-copy-bound> and <eqref|ex-raw-slot-identity> hold for the
  unselected theory, and that its correlations are multilinear and
  permutation symmetric. By Corollary<nbsp><reference|ex-norm-separable>, the
  representing probability of Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-character-measure>
  is then available.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Smoothness and caps in every character><label|ex-phase-caps>The
    whole-word functions <math|S<rsub|\<chi\>>> are multilinear in the raw
    labels, permutation symmetric, and smooth on distinct-point
    configurations. They obey the slot derivative identity
    <eqref|ex-raw-slot-identity>. For every grade bound <math|D> and label
    norm bound <math|R>, the constants <math|C,K> in
    <eqref|ex-all-copy-bound> also give

    <\equation>
      <label|ex-phase-zeroth-cap><around|\||S<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|w|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsup|n>*<around|(|n/r|)><rsup|K*n>
    </equation>

    for all <math|\<chi\>>, all words with these label bounds, and
    <math|0\<less\>r\<leqslant\>1> with pairwise sup-norm separations at
    least <math|r>. For every fixed label tuple and derivative order
    <math|q>, there are <math|C<rsub|q>\<geqslant\>0> and
    <math|A<rsub|q>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, independent of <math|\<chi\>> and the
    positions, such that

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|q>*S<rsub|\<chi\>,\<b-v\>><around|(|x|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|q>*r<rsup|-A<rsub|q>>
    </equation*>

    on the same separated configurations.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Multilinearity of slab operators was proved in the norm-separability
    argument. Their permutation symmetry follows by the same dense
    matrix-element test, using scalar permutation symmetry. Compression and
    the linear character preserve both identities. For local smoothness, fix
    a distinct configuration, translate it into a slab, and keep the
    translation and width fixed in a small neighborhood. Proposition
    <reference|ex-smooth-slabs> gives smoothness of the operator-valued map
    and its slot derivative formula. Compression is bounded linear, and the
    character is bounded linear on <math|\<cal-A\>>; their composition gives
    the same statements for <math|S<rsub|\<chi\>>>. Equivalently one may
    extend the character to the ambient operator space by Hahn\UBanach before
    composing; the extension agrees on every compressed word and its
    derivatives.

    To obtain <eqref|ex-phase-zeroth-cap>, translate a given word into a slab
    with one unit of padding at each time boundary. Translation leaves all
    pairwise separations unchanged, and the boundary padding is at least
    <math|r>. Contractivity of characters and of orthogonal compression gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||S<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|w|)>|\|>=<around|\||\<chi\><around|(|M<around|(|w|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|M<around|(|w|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|d><around|(|w|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsup|n>*<around|(|n/r|)><rsup|K*n>.
    </equation*>

    For the empty word use normalization. For nonzero arity, iterate the slot
    derivative formula <math|q> times. There are finitely many resulting
    label tuples, one for each sequence of <math|q> coordinate directions.
    Their grades and raw-label norms have finite maxima, so apply
    <eqref|ex-phase-zeroth-cap> with these maxima. Expand the real
    <math|q>-linear derivative in the standard coordinate basis of
    <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4*n>>. On unit arguments every coordinate
    coefficient has absolute value at most one, so its operator norm is
    bounded by the sum of the absolute values of its at most
    <math|<around|(|4*n|)><rsup|q>> entries. This yields the claimed
    constants and the integer exponent <math|A<rsub|q>=K*n> for the enlarged
    label bounds. At arity zero the function is constant one, with all
    positive-order derivatives zero.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Original null relations hold in every
    character><label|ex-phase-original-null>Fix a grade <math|d>. Let
    <math|N<rsub|d>> be the space of raw labels <math|v> of grade <math|d>
    whose unselected separated correlations vanish whenever <math|v> is
    inserted, with arbitrary finitely many spectator labels of arbitrary
    grades. Define <math|N<rsub|\<chi\>,d>> by the same condition for
    <math|S<rsub|\<chi\>>>. Then

    <\equation*>
      N<rsub|d>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|\<chi\>,d><space|2em><text|for every
      >\<chi\>\<in\>\<Xi\>.
    </equation*>

    Consequently every phase correlation factors, in each slot, through the
    unselected field quotient at that grade.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|w> be any distinct-point word containing a label
    <math|v\<in\>N<rsub|d>>. Translate the whole word into a slab of width
    <math|a\<gtr\>0>, and denote its bounded insertion operator on the
    unselected Euclidean Hilbert space by <math|A<rsub|a><around|(|w|)>>. For
    positive-time word vectors <math|<around|[|u|]>> and
    <math|<around|[|z|]>>, the matrix-element identity expresses

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|<around|[|u|]>,A<rsub|a><around|(|w|)><around|[|z|]>|\<rangle\>>
    </equation*>

    as an unselected correlation with the translated word in the middle, a
    reflected copy of <math|u> below the slab and a shifted copy of <math|z>
    above it. All points are distinct: each exterior word is internally
    distinct, and the three groups have disjoint time ranges. This is an
    allowed all-spectator test of the label <math|v>, so the matrix element
    is zero. By linearity it is zero on the spans of positive-time word
    vectors. Those spans are dense, and <math|A<rsub|a><around|(|w|)>> is
    bounded; hence all its matrix elements vanish and
    <math|A<rsub|a><around|(|w|)>=0>.

    Its compression to the ground subspace is therefore zero. Placement
    independence identifies this compression with <math|M<around|(|w|)>>, and
    evaluating any character gives <math|S<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|w|)>=\<chi\><around|(|M<around|(|w|)>|)>=0>.
    Permutation symmetry allows the null label in any slot. This proves the
    inclusion for every character, without discarding a set of measure zero.
    Finally, if two raw labels represent the same unselected class, their
    difference is in <math|N<rsub|d>>; multilinearity shows that replacing
    one by the other leaves every phase correlation unchanged.
  </proof>

  <\corollary>
    <dueto|Phase Hilbert spaces and selection><label|ex-phase-dynamics> Every
    character table has the Hilbert space and strongly continuous contraction
    semigroup of Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-reflection>. The common
    countable family in Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-horizon> exists.
    Consequently Theorem<nbsp><reference|ex-pure-ground> applies, giving a
    phase whose time-invariant subspace is exactly its vacuum line.
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    Normalization, translation invariance and time reflection positivity were
    proved after <eqref|ex-phase-word>. The adjoint identity
    <math|M*<around|(|\<theta\>*w|)>=M<around|(|w|)><rsup|\<ast\>>> gives
    <math|S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|\<theta\>*w|)>=<wide|S<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|w|)>|\<bar\>>>.
    For a word in arbitrary position, this operator identity follows by
    translating it into a slab, using its opposite word there and the slab
    adjoint identity, and then using placement independence of <math|M>.
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-caps> supplies permutation symmetry,
    continuity and the separated zeroth-order bound.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-cap-proof> gives the boundedness under
    separating translations required in <eqref|ex-cap>. All hypotheses of the
    Hilbert-space and contraction construction are therefore met for each
    <math|S<rsub|\<chi\>>>. Multilinearity, finite-dimensional raw-label
    spaces and continuity give the common countable dense span by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-horizon>. The selection theorem now
    applies.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Smeared reflection positivity><label|ex-phase-smeared-rp> For any
    correlation table with continuous distinct-point correlations and point
    reflection positivity, the reflection form is nonnegative on smooth
    compactly supported positive-time configuration tests.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For one fixed label tuple, the map <math|x\<mapsto\><around|[|w<around|(|x|)>|]>>
    into the GNS space is norm-continuous on positive-time distinct-point
    configurations. Indeed its squared norm difference is a finite
    combination of continuous Gram entries, as in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-horizon>. On the compact support of a test
    <math|\<varphi\>>, it is bounded and its image is a separable compact
    set. Hence the compactly supported continuous vector function
    <math|\<varphi\><around|(|x|)><around|[|w<around|(|x|)>|]>> is Bochner
    integrable. Denote its integral by <math|\<Psi\><rsub|\<varphi\>>>. For
    two such tests, continuity of the inner product and Fubini give

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|\<Psi\><rsub|\<varphi\>>,\<Psi\><rsub|\<psi\>>|\<rangle\>>=<big|iint><wide|\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>|\<bar\>>\<psi\><around|(|y|)>*S*<around|(|<around|(|\<theta\>*w<around|(|x|)>|)>*w<around|(|y|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x*<space|0.17em>d*y.
    </equation*>

    The scalar integrand is absolutely integrable: on the product of the two
    compact supports the two reflected halves have a positive time gap, each
    half has internally distinct points, and the continuous kernel is
    bounded. Different arities and labels cause no change to this argument;
    each pair has its own compact product support. The empty word uses the
    one-point zero-dimensional configuration space. A finite combination of
    tests has reflection form <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<big|sum><rsub|i>c<rsub|i>*\<Psi\><rsub|\<varphi\><rsub|i>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>0>.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Slab bounds survive character selection><label|ex-phase-slab-bounds>Let
    <math|A<rsub|\<chi\>,d><around|(|w|)>> denote insertion in the Hilbert
    space of a character table. Such an operator exists for every slab word,
    and

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|\<chi\>,d><around|(|w|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|d><around|(|w|)>|\<\|\|\>>.
    </equation*>

    In particular the uniform bound <eqref|ex-all-copy-bound> holds in every
    phase with the same constants. Its slab operators are smooth in operator
    norm and obey the same slot derivative identities.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|a=A<rsub|d><around|(|w|)>> in the unselected Hilbert space and
    put <math|c=<around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>>>. For a positive word <math|v>,
    let <math|v<rsub|n>=<around|(|I<rsub|w<rsup|\<sharp\>>>*I<rsub|w>|)><rsup|n>*v>.
    The maps <math|X<around|(|v|)>:\<cal-H\><rsub|0>\<to\>\<cal-H\>> from
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-phases> satisfy

    <\equation*>
      X*<around|(|I<rsub|w>*v|)>=a*X<around|(|v|)>,<space|2em>X<around|(|v<rsub|n>|)>=<around|(|a<rsup|\<ast\>>*a|)><rsup|n>*X<around|(|v|)>.
    </equation*>

    The first equality is the slab concatenation law restricted to
    <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|0>>; the second follows by induction and the adjoint
    law. Therefore <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|X<around|(|v<rsub|n>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>c<rsup|2*n><around|\<\|\|\>|X<around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>>>.
    For any <math|D\<gtr\>c>, the character Gram identity gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<around|[|v<rsub|n>|]><rsub|\<chi\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=\<chi\>*<around*|(|M*<around|(|<around|(|\<theta\>*v<rsub|n>|)>*v<rsub|n>|)>|)>=\<chi\>*<around*|(|X<around|(|v<rsub|n>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*X<around|(|v<rsub|n>|)>|)>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|X<around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>*D<rsup|4*n>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|X<around|(|v<rsub|n>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*X<around|(|v<rsub|n>|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
    by its equality with the compressed whole-word operator, and the
    character bound applies in that algebra.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-train-bound> produces a phase insertion operator
    with norm at most <math|D>. These operators are all identical: their
    action on every positive word is prescribed, and the word span is dense.
    Taking the infimum over <math|D\<gtr\>c> proves the bound <math|c>, also
    when <math|c=0>. The unselected uniform estimate therefore transfers. The
    character slot identities were proved in
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-caps>; applying
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-smooth-slabs> inside the phase Hilbert
    space proves its operator smoothness and derivative formulas.
  </proof>

  These results construct the time-axis Euclidean component theories from the
  unselected slab and differential estimates. Full orthogonal covariance of
  each character, and ordinary conjugation separately from reflected
  conjugation, still require the reflection-transport argument. Equality of
  null spaces requires the detector inputs in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-fields>.

  <subsection|Ordinary reflections and covariance of individual
  phases><label|ex-reflection-transport>

  Reflected conjugation is built into the Hilbert inner product. Ordinary
  reflection, which acts complex-linearly on labels, is a different
  operation. Proving that it fixes each compressed observable is the extra
  step needed to obtain orthogonal covariance in each character separately.
  We denote ordinary time reflection by <math|\<rho\>>; thus <math|\<theta\>>
  also conjugates labels and reverses the word, whereas <math|\<rho\>> does
  neither. Permutation symmetry makes the reversal immaterial to <math|M>.

  We use the following relation between the two label operations, which is
  how the reflected conjugate is defined. Write
  <math|v\<mapsto\><wide|v|\<bar\>>> for the conjugation of raw labels and
  <math|R<rsub|\<rho\>>> for the complex-linear label action of the time
  reflection, one of the signed coordinate transformations. Then the
  reflected conjugate of <math|v> is

  <\equation*>
    J*v=R<rsub|\<rho\>>*<wide|v|\<bar\>>,<space|2em><text|and><space|2em>R<rsub|\<rho\>>*<wide|v|\<bar\>>=<wide|R<rsub|\<rho\>>*v|\<bar\>>.
  </equation*>

  Here the second identity is the case <math|s=\<rho\>> of the commutation

  <\equation*>
    R<rsub|s>*<wide|v|\<bar\>>=<wide|R<rsub|s>*v|\<bar\>><space|2em><text|for
    every signed coordinate transformation >s,
  </equation*>

  of conjugation with the signed coordinate actions on raw labels. Two facts
  used below follow from the definition of <math|J> alone: if
  <math|<wide|v|\<bar\>>=v> then <math|J*v=R<rsub|\<rho\>>*v>; and, for every
  word, <math|\<theta\>*w> is the reversal of
  <math|\<rho\>*<wide|w|\<bar\>>>, where <math|<wide|w|\<bar\>>> conjugates
  each label, because <math|\<theta\>> reflects, reverses and applies
  <math|J> letter by letter. The commutation is not needed for these two
  facts. It is what makes the two possible orders of the operations agree,
  <math|R<rsub|\<rho\>>*<wide|v|\<bar\>>=<wide|R<rsub|\<rho\>>*v|\<bar\>>>,
  and it gives <math|J<rsup|2>*v=R<rsub|\<rho\>><wide|R<rsub|\<rho\>>*<wide|v|\<bar\>>|\<bar\>>=R<rsub|\<rho\>>*R<rsub|\<rho\>>*v=v>,
  hence <math|\<theta\><rsup|2>=1>, as assumed in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-reflection>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Positivity from bounded real approximations><label|ex-replicated-positivity>Suppose
    real numbers <math|b<rsub|t>*<around|(|i,j|)>>, for integers
    <math|i\<less\>j>, satisfy <math|b<rsub|t>*<around|(|i,j|)>\<to\>a> as
    <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>>, with the same limit <math|a> for every pair.
    Suppose there are <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|k>\<geqslant\>0> tending to zero
    and finite <math|R<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>0> with the following property. For
    every <math|k> and finite <math|m>, for all sufficiently large <math|t>
    there exist simultaneous real random variables
    <math|X<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,X<rsub|m>>, possibly on cutoff-dependent
    probability spaces, such that eventually in the ordered cutoff limit

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||X<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|k>,<space|2em>\<bbb-E\>*<around|(|X<rsub|i>*X<rsub|j>|)>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|<math-up|cut>,t>*<around|(|i,j|)>+\<epsilon\><rsub|k>*<space|1em><around|(|i\<less\>j|)>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|b<rsub|<math-up|cut>,t>*<around|(|i,j|)>\<to\>b<rsub|t>*<around|(|i,j|)>>
    in that cutoff limit. Then <math|a\<geqslant\>0>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The variables are bounded and measurable, so their products are
    integrable. Nonnegativity of a square gives

    <\equation*>
      0\<leqslant\>\<bbb-E\><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>X<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>m*R<rsub|k><rsup|2>+2*<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>j><around*|(|b<rsub|<math-up|cut>,t>*<around|(|i,j|)>+\<epsilon\><rsub|k>|)>.
    </equation*>

    For fixed <math|k,m,t>, all eventual conditions can be imposed together:
    only finitely many variables and pairs occur. Remove the cutoff in the
    specified order, then send <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>>, obtaining
    <math|0\<leqslant\>m*R<rsub|k><rsup|2>+m*<around|(|m-1|)>*<around|(|a+\<epsilon\><rsub|k>|)>>.
    For <math|m\<geqslant\>2>, divide by <math|m*<around|(|m-1|)>> and send
    <math|m\<to\>\<infty\>>. This yields <math|a+\<epsilon\><rsub|k>\<geqslant\>0>.
    Now send <math|k\<to\>\<infty\>>. The order of these limits matters:
    <math|R<rsub|k>> may diverge as <math|k> grows.
  </proof>

  The lattice construction applies the lemma to a real-labelled word <math|w>
  at an internally generic grid configuration, minus its ordinary reflection.
  Translate several copies along one vector with strictly positive
  coordinates, spacing the <math|i>-th copy by <math|i*t> times that vector.
  For fixed distinct copies and large <math|t>, their combined configurations
  are generic in every coordinate. The covariance to be approximated is the
  four-term expression

  <\equation*>
    S*<around|(|w<rsub|i>*w<rsub|j>|)>-S*<around|(|w<rsub|i>*<around|(|\<rho\>*w|)><rsub|j>|)>-S*<around|(|<around|(|\<rho\>*w|)><rsub|i>*w<rsub|j>|)>+S*<around|(|<around|(|\<rho\>*w|)><rsub|i>*<around|(|\<rho\>*w|)><rsub|j>|)>,
  </equation*>

  or its real part. By Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-cluster-train>, its
  large-separation limit is

  <\equation>
    <label|ex-reflection-square>a=<math-up|Re><around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,<around|(|M<around|(|w|)>-M*<around|(|\<rho\>*w|)>|)><rsup|2>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation>

  In the lemma, <math|b<rsub|t>*<around|(|i,j|)>> is this four-term
  expression for the copies <math|i\<less\>j> at spacing <math|t>, and
  <math|b<rsub|<math-up|cut>,t>*<around|(|i,j|)>> is the same four-term
  expression for the lattice correlations at finite cutoff. Two inputs are
  required here, and they are distinct. The first is the existence of the
  bounded real variables in Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-replicated-positivity>,
  with errors independent of the number and offsets of copies. The second is
  the convergence <math|b<rsub|<math-up|cut>,t>*<around|(|i,j|)>\<to\>b<rsub|t>*<around|(|i,j|)>>
  in the ordered cutoff limit. The second is not supplied by the
  approximations; it follows from the ordered point limits of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-limits-extraction> for the same
  constructed lattice family, extraction order and hierarchy table. In this
  application the construction and hierarchy hypotheses hold, each token of
  the chosen real basis is fixed by conjugation, and the original grid
  configuration is internally generic, as required in the proposition below.
  Signed reflection preserves that genericity. For each fixed pair
  <math|i\<less\>j>, the positive-coordinate displacement makes each of the
  four combined configurations generic for all sufficiently large <math|t>.
  Fix such a <math|t> and apply the point limits to the four correlations,
  taking the mesh limit at each selected period before the period limit. The
  resulting convergence is for the real four-term covariance. The separate
  bounded approximation input provides the simultaneous real approximants and
  their uniform error bounds. Wilson positivity then supplies the nonnegative
  square.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Ordinary reflection fixes compressed
    words><label|ex-ordinary-reflection>Assume the bounded real approximation
    property just described holds on a dense set of internally generic grid
    configurations for each tuple of real basis labels, and that at those
    configurations the four-term cutoff covariances converge in the ordered
    cutoff limit as just described. Assume the norm-regularity hypotheses of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-slab-regularity> and that each raw label
    space has a basis fixed by conjugation. Then
    <math|M*<around|(|\<rho\>*w|)>=M<around|(|w|)>> for every distinct-point
    word.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    First take a real basis-labelled word at an admitted configuration. Its
    labels are fixed by conjugation, so <math|\<theta\>*w> is the reversal of
    <math|\<rho\>*w> by the relation <math|J*v=R<rsub|\<rho\>>*<wide|v|\<bar\>>>
    above. Permutation symmetry and the adjoint identity therefore give
    <math|M*<around|(|\<rho\>*w|)>=M*<around|(|\<theta\>*w|)>=M<around|(|w|)><rsup|\<ast\>>>.
    Thus <math|D=M<around|(|w|)>-M*<around|(|\<rho\>*w|)>> satisfies
    <math|D<rsup|\<ast\>>=-D>. The previous lemma and
    <eqref|ex-reflection-square> imply

    <\equation*>
      0\<leqslant\><math-up|Re><around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,D<rsup|2>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>=-<around|\<\|\|\>|D*\<Omega\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    Hence <math|D*\<Omega\>=0>, and separation of <math|\<Omega\>> for
    <math|\<cal-A\>> gives <math|D=0>. Norm continuity of compressed words
    and their reflected words extends the identity from the dense admitted
    configurations to all distinct configurations for these basis labels.
    Both <math|M> and ordinary reflection are complex-multilinear in the
    labels. Expanding an arbitrary tuple in the real basis therefore extends
    the equality to every tuple of complex labels.
  </proof>

  We make explicit one other algebraic point used below. Define a null raw
  label to be one whose insertion gives zero correlation with every finite
  list of exterior insertions at distinct points. A slab operator containing
  a null label is zero: pair it with two arbitrary word vectors, use the
  null-label property on the resulting whole correlation, and use density.
  Consequently <math|M<around|(|w|)>> depends only on the quotient classes of
  its labels. Assume that the orthogonal action on those classes is an actual
  group action and agrees, for signed coordinate transformations, with the
  raw signed-label action after projection. These are the precise
  compatibilities used when composing rotations below.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Orthogonal covariance in each character><label|ex-individual-rotations>Under
    these compatibilities and Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-ordinary-reflection>,
    <math|M*<around|(|K*w|)>=M<around|(|w|)>> for every
    <math|K\<in\>O<around|(|4|)>> and distinct-point word <math|w>. Thus
    <math|S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|K*w|)>=S<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|w|)>> for
    every character.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Write <math|F*<around|(|p,w|)>=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,M<around|(|p|)>*M<around|(|w|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>>.
    We first show simultaneous invariance

    <\equation>
      <label|ex-pair-rotations>F*<around|(|K*p,K*w|)>=F*<around|(|p,w|)>*<space|2em><around|(|K\<in\>O<around|(|4|)>|)>.
    </equation>

    The cluster argument in Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-ground-commutative>
    already proves this whenever there exists a vector <math|b> with
    <math|b<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> and <math|<around|(|K*b|)><rsub|0>\<gtr\>0>. Put
    <math|a=<around|(|K*e<rsub|0>|)><rsub|0>>. If <math|a\<gtr\>-1>, choose
    <math|b=e<rsub|0>+K<rsup|-1>*e<rsub|0>>; orthogonality gives
    <math|b<rsub|0>=<around|(|K*b|)><rsub|0>=1+a\<gtr\>0>. If <math|a=-1>,
    then <math|<around|(|\<rho\>*K*e<rsub|0>|)><rsub|0>=1>, so the argument
    applies to <math|\<rho\>*K>. Individual invariance under <math|\<rho\>>
    removes the extra reflection from each factor and again gives
    <eqref|ex-pair-rotations>.

    For any <math|J\<in\>O<around|(|4|)>>, rotate a pair backwards by
    <math|J<rsup|-1>>, apply ordinary reflection to its second factor alone,
    and rotate forwards by <math|J>. Equation<nbsp><eqref|ex-pair-rotations>
    gives

    <\equation*>
      F*<around|(|p,w|)>=F*<around|(|J<rsup|-1>*p,J<rsup|-1>*w|)>=F*<around|(|J<rsup|-1>*p,\<rho\>*J<rsup|-1>*w|)>=F*<around|(|p,J*\<rho\>*J<rsup|-1>*w|)>.
    </equation*>

    There is no ambiguity from raw representatives, by quotient independence.
    Pair values separate compressed operators: if
    <math|F*<around|(|p,w|)>=F*<around|(|p,z|)>> for every <math|p>, replace
    <math|p> by <math|\<theta\>*v> to obtain

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|M<around|(|v|)>*\<Omega\>,<around|(|M<around|(|w|)>-M<around|(|z|)>|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>=0.
    </equation*>

    The linear span of <math|M<around|(|v|)>*\<Omega\>> is dense in
    <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|0>>, so <math|<around|(|M<around|(|w|)>-M<around|(|z|)>|)>*\<Omega\>=0>;
    separation gives <math|M<around|(|w|)>=M<around|(|z|)>>. Thus <math|M> is
    individually invariant under every conjugate <math|J*\<rho\>*J<rsup|-1>>.

    These conjugates are all hyperplane reflections: map <math|e<rsub|0>> to
    any chosen unit normal by an orthogonal map. For completeness, hyperplane
    reflections generate <math|O<around|(|d|)>>. Given an orthogonal map
    <math|K>, if <math|K*e<rsub|1>\<ne\>e<rsub|1>>, reflection in the
    hyperplane normal to <math|K*e<rsub|1>-e<rsub|1>> sends
    <math|K*e<rsub|1>> to <math|e<rsub|1>>; this follows immediately from the
    reflection formula and <math|<around|\||K*e<rsub|1>|\|>=<around|\||e<rsub|1>|\|>=1>.
    Composing with this reflection gives a map fixing <math|e<rsub|1>>. It
    preserves <math|e<rsub|1><rsup|\<perp\>>>, and induction on dimension
    writes its restriction as a product of reflections there, extended to fix
    <math|e<rsub|1>>. If it already fixes <math|e<rsub|1>>, start with that
    restriction. Dimension zero is trivial. Invariance under products follows
    from the group action on quotient labels. This proves individual
    invariance under every orthogonal map, and evaluation by a character
    gives the asserted phase covariance.
  </proof>

  Ordinary conjugation now follows as well. Since <math|\<theta\>*w> is a
  permutation of <math|\<rho\>*<wide|w|\<bar\>>>, by the same relation,

  <\equation*>
    S<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|<wide|w|\<bar\>>|)>=S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|\<rho\>*<wide|w|\<bar\>>|)>=S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|\<theta\>*w|)>=<wide|S<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|w|)>|\<bar\>>.
  </equation*>

  Signed coordinate covariance follows from the compatibility of the quotient
  action with the raw signed action. Transporting time-axis reflection
  positivity by signed permutations gives reflection positivity about every
  coordinate axis: choose a signed permutation taking that axis to the time
  axis, rotate each positive word and its labels, and use covariance and the
  conjugation compatibility in the Gram matrix.
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-smeared-rp> then supplies the corresponding
  smeared positivity. The bounded real approximation on arbitrarily fine
  generic grids is constructed in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-phase-grid-approximants>
  and applied in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-phase-detector-moments>;
  its uniformity over finitely many replicated copies is indispensable.

  <subsection|Keeping the same field space in each
  phase><label|ex-phase-fields>

  A field label may disappear when one passes to a component of a mixture.
  For the selected phase to represent the intended fields, one must rule out
  additional null labels. The following argument explains the role of the
  finite detectors and the uniform moment estimates used for this purpose.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Moment bounds at every character><label|ex-character-moments> Let
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>, let <math|t\<geqslant\>0>, and suppose

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,<around|(|A<rsup|\<ast\>>*A|)><rsup|n>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>\<leqslant\>t<rsup|2*n>*<space|2em><around|(|n=0,1,2,\<ldots\>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Then <math|<around|\||\<chi\><around|(|A|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>t> for every
    <math|\<chi\>\<in\>\<Xi\>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The representing measure gives <math|<big|int><around|\||\<chi\><around|(|A|)>|\|><rsup|2*n>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\>\<leqslant\>t<rsup|2*n>>.
    If <math|<around|\||\<chi\><rsub|0><around|(|A|)>|\|>\<gtr\>t>, choose
    <math|p> strictly between these numbers. The nonempty open set
    <math|O=<around|{|\<chi\>:<around|\||\<chi\><around|(|A|)>|\|>\<gtr\>p|}>>
    has positive measure <math|c> by full support. Hence
    <math|c*p<rsup|2*n>\<leqslant\><big|int><around|\||\<chi\><around|(|A|)>|\|><rsup|2*n>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\>\<leqslant\>t<rsup|2*n>>.
    Since <math|p\<gtr\>0>, division gives
    <math|c\<leqslant\><around|(|t/p|)><rsup|2*n>\<to\>0>, a contradiction.
    This also covers <math|t=0>, when the right side already vanishes for
    positive <math|n>.
  </proof>

  Here is the elementary two-point calculation to which these moment
  estimates are applied. At a fixed scale <math|k>, let <math|a,b> denote two
  insertions at distinct points and let <math|j<rsub|a>,j<rsub|b>,\<kappa\>\<in\>\<bbb-C\>>
  be the prescribed comparison one-point values and connected two-point
  value. Form the elements

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|D<rsub|a>>|<cell|=M<around|(|a|)>-j<rsub|a>*I,<space|2em>D<rsub|b>=M<around|(|b|)>-j<rsub|b>*I,>>|<row|<cell|D<rsub|a*b>>|<cell|=M*<around|(|a*b|)>-j<rsub|a>*M<around|(|b|)>-j<rsub|b>*M<around|(|a|)>+<around|(|j<rsub|a>*j<rsub|b>-\<kappa\>|)>*I.>>>>
  </align*>

  Assume the preceding lemma's moment bounds hold for each of these three
  elements with the same number <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|k>\<geqslant\>0>. It
  follows for every character that

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\||S<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|a|)>-j<rsub|a>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|k>,<eq-number><label|ex-detector-one>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|a*b|)>-<around|(|\<kappa\>+j<rsub|a>*j<rsub|b>|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|k>*<around|(|1+<around|\||j<rsub|a>|\|>+<around|\||j<rsub|b>|\|>|)>.<eq-number><label|ex-detector-two>>>>>
  </align>

  Indeed, evaluating <math|D<rsub|a*b>> and rearranging gives

  <\equation*>
    S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|a*b|)>-<around|(|\<kappa\>+j<rsub|a>*j<rsub|b>|)>=\<chi\><around|(|D<rsub|a*b>|)>+j<rsub|a>*<around|(|S<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|b|)>-j<rsub|b>|)>+j<rsub|b>*<around|(|S<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|a|)>-j<rsub|a>|)>,
  </equation*>

  so the triangle inequality proves <eqref|ex-detector-two>. If
  <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|k>\<to\>0> and the prescribed one-point values
  converge, these errors tend to zero in every phase, with a bound
  independent of the character. This conclusion requires moments of the full
  corrected operators <math|D<rsub|a*b>>, not only pointwise estimates for
  the unselected two-point correlations.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Finite detectors prevent an additional null
    space><label|ex-no-new-null>Let <math|E> be a complex vector space of raw
    labels, and let <math|N\<subset\>N<rsub|\<chi\>>> be subspaces. Write
    <math|\<pi\>:E\<to\>E/N=V> and <math|\<pi\><rsub|\<chi\>>:E\<to\>E/N<rsub|\<chi\>>=V<rsub|\<chi\>>>.
    Let <math|W> be a finite-dimensional complex normed space. Suppose there
    are linear maps <math|J<rsub|k>:W\<to\>E>, linear tests
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|k,i>:V<rsub|\<chi\>>\<to\>\<bbb-C\>>,
    <math|1\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>r>, and an injective linear map
    <math|F:W\<to\>\<bbb-C\><rsup|r>> such that:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<pi\>*J<rsub|k>> is surjective for all sufficiently large
      <math|k>;

      <item>for every vector of a fixed basis of <math|W>,

      <\equation*>
        F<rsub|k>*w\<assign\><around*|(|\<ell\><rsub|k,i>*<around|(|\<pi\><rsub|\<chi\>>*J<rsub|k>*w|)>|)><rsub|i=1><rsup|r>\<longrightarrow\>F*w.
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>

    Then <math|N<rsub|\<chi\>>=N>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Convergence on a finite basis implies convergence in operator norm. To
    see this, let <math|e<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,e<rsub|s>> be that basis with
    continuous coordinate functionals <math|e<rsub|j><rsup|\<ast\>>>. Then

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|F<rsub|k>-F|)>*w|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|s><around|\||e<rsub|j><rsup|\<ast\>><around|(|w|)>|\|>*<space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|F<rsub|k>-F|)>*e<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>>*<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|s><around|\<\|\|\>|e<rsub|j><rsup|\<ast\>>|\<\|\|\>>*<space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|F<rsub|k>-F|)>*e<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>.
    </equation*>

    The finite sum tends to zero. If <math|W\<ne\><around|{|0|}>>,
    compactness of its unit sphere and injectivity of <math|F> give
    <math|c=min<rsub|<around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>>=1><around|\<\|\|\>|F*w|\<\|\|\>>\<gtr\>0>.
    Choose <math|k> late enough that <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|k>-F|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>c/2>
    and <math|\<pi\>*J<rsub|k>> is onto. Then
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|k>*w|\<\|\|\>>\<geqslant\><around|(|c/2|)><around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>>>,
    so <math|F<rsub|k>> is injective. If <math|W=<around|{|0|}>>,
    surjectivity already gives <math|V=0>, and the result is immediate.

    Now take <math|e\<in\>N<rsub|\<chi\>>>. By surjectivity choose
    <math|w\<in\>W> with <math|\<pi\>*J<rsub|k>*w=\<pi\>*e>. Thus
    <math|J<rsub|k>*w-e\<in\>N\<subset\>N<rsub|\<chi\>>>, and since
    <math|e\<in\>N<rsub|\<chi\>>>, also <math|J<rsub|k>*w\<in\>N<rsub|\<chi\>>>.
    All tests vanish on <math|\<pi\><rsub|\<chi\>>*J<rsub|k>*w=0>; hence
    <math|F<rsub|k>*w=0>. Injectivity forces <math|w=0>, so
    <math|\<pi\>*e=\<pi\>*J<rsub|k>*0=0>, or <math|e\<in\>N>. This proves
    <math|N<rsub|\<chi\>>\<subset\>N>; the reverse inclusion was assumed.
  </proof>

  In the application at label degree <math|d>, <math|W> is the comparison
  field space, <math|J<rsub|k>> consists of its corrected labels at the
  chosen scales, and the tests <math|\<ell\><rsub|k,i>> are one- or two-point
  correlations whose exterior labels are the corrected labels at comparison
  index <math|k>; they depend on <math|k>, as the proposition allows. The
  test points are <math|x<rsub|k,i>=\<sigma\><rsub|k>*y<rsub|k,i>>, where
  <math|\<sigma\><rsub|k>\<to\>0> is the comparison scale and the rescaled
  configurations <math|y<rsub|k,i>> converge to configurations
  <math|y<rsub|i>> of distinct points; the physical configurations
  <math|x<rsub|k,i>> do not converge to a configuration of distinct points.
  Estimates<nbsp><eqref|ex-detector-one>\U<eqref|ex-detector-two> compare the
  phase correlations at <math|x<rsub|k,i>> with the free values at
  <math|y<rsub|k,i>>, with errors <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|k>\<to\>0> uniform
  in the character. Continuity of the free comparison table at the distinct
  configurations <math|y<rsub|i>> then gives convergence of <math|F<rsub|k>>
  to <math|F> on a basis, where <math|F> is given by the free tests at
  <math|y<rsub|i>>. The comparison limit <math|k\<to\>\<infty\>> is taken
  after the mesh and period limits that define the table. The comparison
  tests separate <math|W>, so their joint map <math|F> is injective. The
  first hypothesis of the proposition is the eventual spanning statement for
  the corrected labels in the unselected quotient. The same-label
  construction of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-rank-purchases>,
  followed by the exact-kernel argument in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:finite-rank-kernel-transport>, supplies this
  spanning statement. Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-phase-detector-moments>
  supplies the corrected-operator moment bounds on those trials, and
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-all-grade-free-detector> supplies
  the separating free tests. These applications retain the same corrected
  lifts and all separated correlations when passing to the smooth table.
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-original-null> supplies
  <math|N\<subseteq\>N<rsub|\<chi\>>>. The proposition then proves equality
  in every grade for every character. Thus the finite-dimensional argument
  uses the actual comparison and moment constructions; finite dimensionality
  alone does not supply them.

  <subsubsection|Field inclusions from simultaneous tested
  representatives><label|subsec:phase-field-inclusions>

  In this subsection we reuse notation: <math|A<rsub|d>> denotes the raw
  space, <math|E<rsub|d>> its quotient, <math|E> their union,
  <math|J<rsub|d>> a response map, and <math|W> a target space; these differ
  from the letters in Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-no-new-null>.

  Equality of the null spaces at each grade does not construct a map between
  those grades. We now give that construction from a separate realization
  input, on one fixed table. No change of phase or of the extraction defining
  the table is made in this argument.

  Let <math|A<rsub|d>> be a finite-dimensional complex raw label space for
  each <math|d\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>. A raw label includes its grade. Fix a table
  <math|S> whose correlations

  <\equation*>
    S*<around|(|x;<around|(|d<rsub|1>,a<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,<around|(|d<rsub|m>,a<rsub|m>|)>|)>,<space|2em>x\<in\><math-up|Conf><rsub|m><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)>,<space|1em>a<rsub|j>\<in\>A<rsub|d<rsub|j>>,
  </equation*>

  are complex multilinear in the labels, smooth in <math|x>, and symmetric
  under simultaneous permutation of labels and positions. Here
  <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|m><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)>> is the open set
  of pairwise distinct positions. Spectator tuples may have arbitrary finite
  length and arbitrary grades; no upper grade or arity bound is imposed on
  them.

  An exterior <math|\<xi\>=<around|(|x;\<b-b\>|)>> contains a tuple
  <math|\<b-b\>> of <math|n> raw spectators and a configuration of <math|n+1>
  distinct positions, <math|x\<in\><math-up|Conf><rsub|n+1><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)>>.
  Allow <math|n=0>. Let <math|\<cal-X\>> be the set of all such exteriors and
  define the linear response map

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-R\><rsub|d>:A<rsub|d>\<longrightarrow\>\<bbb-C\><rsup|\<cal-X\>>,<space|2em><around|(|\<cal-R\><rsub|d>*a|)><around|(|x;\<b-b\>|)>=S*<around|(|x;<around|(|d,a|)>,\<b-b\>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Set

  <\equation*>
    N<rsub|d>=ker \<cal-R\><rsub|d>,<space|2em>E<rsub|d>=A<rsub|d>/N<rsub|d>,<space|2em>\<pi\><rsub|d>:A<rsub|d>\<longrightarrow\>E<rsub|d>.
  </equation*>

  Thus nullity means vanishing against every exterior, not just a finite
  detector family. The spaces <math|E<rsub|d>> are finite-dimensional. The
  response map descends to an injective linear map
  <math|J<rsub|d>:E<rsub|d>\<to\>\<bbb-C\><rsup|\<cal-X\>>>: if
  <math|J<rsub|d>*\<pi\><rsub|d>*a=0>, then <math|a\<in\>N<rsub|d>>, so
  <math|\<pi\><rsub|d>*a=0>.

  Choose a basis <math|<around|(|a<rsub|d,j>|)><rsub|j\<in\>B<rsub|d>>> of
  each <math|A<rsub|d>>. The incoming <em|simultaneous tested realization> is
  the following statement. For every <math|d\<leqslant\>e> and every
  <math|j\<in\>B<rsub|d>>, there is one <math|b<rsub|d*e,j>\<in\>A<rsub|e>>
  such that, for every spectator tuple <math|\<b-b\>> of length <math|n> and
  every <math|\<varphi\>\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n+1><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)>|)>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:phase-inclusion-tested-input><big|int>\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>*S*<around|(|x;<around|(|e,b<rsub|d*e,j>|)>,\<b-b\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x=<big|int>\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>*S*<around|(|x;<around|(|d,a<rsub|d,j>|)>,\<b-b\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x.
  </equation>

  The order of these quantifiers matters: <math|b<rsub|d*e,j>> is chosen
  before the spectators and the test. A representative depending on either
  would not supply a linear map of label spaces. This input asks only for raw
  representatives of insertion functionals; it assumes neither quotient
  inclusions nor their injectivity or coherence.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Conditional construction of the field
    inclusions><label|prop:phase-field-inclusions>For the fixed smooth
    symmetric table above, the simultaneous tested realization supplies
    unique linear maps <math|\<iota\><rsub|d*e>:E<rsub|d>\<to\>E<rsub|e>> for
    <math|d\<leqslant\>e> preserving every exterior response. They are
    injective and satisfy

    <\equation*>
      \<iota\><rsub|d*d>=1,<space|2em>\<iota\><rsub|e*f>*\<iota\><rsub|d*e>=\<iota\><rsub|d*f>*<space|1em><around|(|d\<leqslant\>e\<leqslant\>f|)>.
    </equation*>

    Correlations descend to the quotient labels in every slot. For any fixed
    configuration of distinct points, any slot, and arbitrary quotient
    spectators, replacing a label <math|v\<in\>E<rsub|d>> in that slot by
    <math|\<iota\><rsub|d*e>*v\<in\>E<rsub|e>> leaves the correlation
    unchanged.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    First define a raw linear map by basis expansion:

    <\equation*>
      K<rsub|d*e><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>B<rsub|d>>c<rsub|j>*a<rsub|d,j>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>B<rsub|d>>c<rsub|j>*b<rsub|d*e,j>.
    </equation*>

    For each fixed spectator tuple and test, multilinearity and the finite
    sum in this definition extend <eqref|eq:phase-inclusion-tested-input> to
    every <math|a\<in\>A<rsub|d>>, with <math|b<rsub|d*e,j>> replaced by
    <math|K<rsub|d*e>*a>. The same map works for all tests and all spectators
    because its basis images were fixed first.

    We spell out why this tested equality is pointwise. For a fixed <math|a>
    and spectator tuple, let

    <\equation*>
      h<around|(|x|)>=S*<around|(|x;<around|(|e,K<rsub|d*e>*a|)>,\<b-b\>|)>-S*<around|(|x;<around|(|d,a|)>,\<b-b\>|)>.
    </equation*>

    It is continuous on the open configuration space and has zero integral
    against every smooth compactly supported test there. If
    <math|h<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>\<ne\>0>, choose <math|c\<in\>\<bbb-C\>>
    with <math|<around|\||c|\|>=1> and <math|c*h<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>=<around|\||h<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>|\|>>.
    There is a ball about <math|x<rsub|0>>, with closure in the configuration
    space, on which <math|<math-up|Re><around|(|c*h|)>\<gtr\><around|\||h<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>|\|>/2>.
    Choose a nonnegative smooth function <math|\<psi\>>, supported in that
    ball, with positive integral. Such a function is obtained by translating
    and rescaling <math|exp <around|(|-1/<around|(|1-<around|\||x|\|><rsup|2>|)>|)>>
    on <math|<around|\||x|\|>\<less\>1>, extended by zero outside. Then

    <\equation*>
      0=<math-up|Re><around*|(|c*<big|int>\<psi\>*h|)>\<gtr\><frac|<around|\||h<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>|\|>|2>*<big|int>\<psi\>\<gtr\>0,
    </equation*>

    a contradiction. Thus <math|h=0> on the configuration space. In
    particular,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:phase-inclusion-raw-response>\<cal-R\><rsub|e>*K<rsub|d*e>=\<cal-R\><rsub|d>.
    </equation>

    No equality at coincident points is used or asserted.

    If <math|a\<in\>N<rsub|d>>, this identity gives
    <math|K<rsub|d*e>*a\<in\>N<rsub|e>>. Consequently

    <\equation*>
      \<iota\><rsub|d*e>*<around|(|\<pi\><rsub|d>*a|)>=\<pi\><rsub|e>*K<rsub|d*e>*a
    </equation*>

    is well defined and linear. If different simultaneous representatives, or
    a different basis, give a raw map <math|K<rprime|'><rsub|d*e>> with the
    same tested property, then <eqref|eq:phase-inclusion-raw-response> holds
    for both maps. Their difference takes values in <math|N<rsub|e>>, so they
    induce the same quotient map. For every <math|v\<in\>E<rsub|d>> we have

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:phase-inclusion-faithful-response>J<rsub|e>*\<iota\><rsub|d*e>*v=J<rsub|d>*v.
    </equation>

    Since <math|J<rsub|d>> is injective, this also proves injectivity of
    <math|\<iota\><rsub|d*e>>. Since <math|J<rsub|e>> is injective, it proves
    uniqueness of a response-preserving map <math|E<rsub|d>\<to\>E<rsub|e>>.

    For completeness, null labels vanish in every slot. Permute the chosen
    slot and its position to the first position; symmetry expresses the
    correlation as a head insertion against another raw exterior. If the
    chosen label belongs to its <math|N<rsub|d>>, that head correlation is
    zero by definition. Thus changing any raw representative by a null label
    changes no correlation. Changing the finitely many representatives one at
    a time proves well-defined multilinear correlations on quotient tuples.
    Smoothness and permutation symmetry pass to these correlations by taking
    fixed raw representatives. The pointwise identity already proved at the
    head, followed by the same permutation, gives explicitly

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:phase-inclusion-slot>S*<around|(|x;v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,\<iota\><rsub|d*e>*v<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|m>|)>=S*<around|(|x;v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|m>|)>,<space|2em>v<rsub|i>\<in\>E<rsub|d>,
    </equation>

    where the other labels have any fixed grades and the changed slot is
    assigned grade <math|e> on the left. Repeated application gives the
    identity when any finite collection of slots is raised, even to different
    grades. Integration against a compactly supported test preserves the
    identity.

    Finally <math|J<rsub|d>*\<iota\><rsub|d*d>=J<rsub|d>> implies
    <math|\<iota\><rsub|d*d>=1>. If <math|d\<leqslant\>e\<leqslant\>f>, then

    <\equation*>
      J<rsub|f>*\<iota\><rsub|e*f>*\<iota\><rsub|d*e>=J<rsub|e>*\<iota\><rsub|d*e>=J<rsub|d>=J<rsub|f>*\<iota\><rsub|d*f>.
    </equation*>

    Injectivity of <math|J<rsub|f>> proves composition. These identities are
    conclusions on the quotients; the chosen raw maps need not compose or
    equal the raw identity when <math|d=e>.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The common union and its universal
    property><label|prop:phase-field-union>Under the hypotheses of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:phase-field-inclusions>, the spaces
    <math|E<rsub|d>> have an algebraic direct limit <math|E>, with injective
    maps <math|j<rsub|d>:E<rsub|d>\<to\>E> and
    <math|j<rsub|e>*\<iota\><rsub|d*e>=j<rsub|d>>. Every element of <math|E>
    comes from one grade, and every finite collection comes from one common
    grade. The table correlations are well defined on finite tuples from
    <math|E>. Fix any unital ring endomorphism
    <math|\<sigma\>:\<bbb-C\>\<to\>\<bbb-C\>>; surjectivity is not assumed.
    If <math|W> is a complex vector space and additive maps
    <math|F<rsub|d>:E<rsub|d>\<to\>W> satisfy

    <\equation*>
      F<rsub|d>*<around|(|c*v|)>=\<sigma\><around|(|c|)>*F<rsub|d><around|(|v|)>,<space|2em>F<rsub|e>*\<iota\><rsub|d*e>=F<rsub|d>*<space|1em><around|(|d\<leqslant\>e|)>,
    </equation*>

    there is a unique additive <math|\<sigma\>>-semilinear map
    <math|F:E\<to\>W> with <math|F*j<rsub|d>=F<rsub|d>> for every <math|d>.
    The complex-linear universal property is the case
    <math|\<sigma\>=<math-up|id>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Realize the union in the single response space:

    <\equation*>
      E=<big|cup><rsub|d\<geqslant\>0>J<rsub|d><around|(|E<rsub|d>|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-C\><rsup|\<cal-X\>>.
    </equation*>

    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:phase-inclusion-faithful-response> makes these
    ranges increasing. If <math|u=J<rsub|d>*v> and <math|w=J<rsub|e>*z>, put
    <math|D=max <around|(|d,e|)>>. For any scalars <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-C\>>,

    <\equation*>
      a*u+b*w=J<rsub|D>*<around*|(|a*\<iota\><rsub|d*D>*v+b*\<iota\><rsub|e*D>*z|)>\<in\>E.
    </equation*>

    The zero vector belongs to every range, so <math|E> is a vector subspace.
    Take <math|j<rsub|d>> to be <math|J<rsub|d>> with codomain restricted to
    <math|E>. These maps are injective and satisfy the stated compatibility.
    If <math|u<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,u<rsub|m>\<in\>E>, choose
    <math|u<rsub|i>=j<rsub|d<rsub|i>>*v<rsub|i>> and let <math|D=max<rsub|i>
    d<rsub|i>>. Then <math|u<rsub|i>=j<rsub|D>*\<iota\><rsub|d<rsub|i>*D>*v<rsub|i>>
    for every <math|i>. For the empty collection choose <math|D=0>.
    Surjectivity of <math|\<pi\><rsub|D>> also gives simultaneous raw
    representatives in <math|A<rsub|D>> for any such finite collection. No
    bound uniform over all elements of <math|E> is implied.

    To evaluate a correlation of <math|u<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,u<rsub|m>>, choose
    these representatives at a common grade and use the quotient correlation
    constructed above. Suppose a second choice uses grade <math|D<rprime|'>>.
    At <math|L=max <around|(|D,D<rprime|'>|)>>, the two representatives of
    each <math|u<rsub|i>> have the same image under the injective map
    <math|j<rsub|L>> and hence are equal in <math|E<rsub|L>>. Raising all
    slots to <math|L> leaves the correlation unchanged by
    <eqref|eq:phase-inclusion-slot>. Thus the value is independent of both
    the common grade and all representatives. The same argument treats
    initially different grades slot by slot. Multilinearity, symmetry and
    smoothness follow by evaluating each finite tuple at one fixed common
    grade; the zero-point correlation is the original vacuum value and
    requires no label representative.

    Define <math|F*<around|(|j<rsub|d>*v|)>=F<rsub|d>*v>. If
    <math|j<rsub|d>*v=j<rsub|e>*z>, then at <math|D=max <around|(|d,e|)>>,

    <\equation*>
      j<rsub|D>*\<iota\><rsub|d*D>*v=j<rsub|D>*\<iota\><rsub|e*D>*z,<space|2em><text|hence><space|2em>\<iota\><rsub|d*D>*v=\<iota\><rsub|e*D>*z.
    </equation*>

    Compatibility of the <math|F<rsub|d>> proves
    <math|F<rsub|d>*v=F<rsub|e>*z>, so <math|F> is well defined. For
    <math|u=j<rsub|d>*v> and <math|w=j<rsub|e>*z>, put <math|D=max
    <around|(|d,e|)>> as before. Then

    <\equation*>
      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|F*<around|(|u+w|)>>|<cell|=F<rsub|D>*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|d*D>*v+\<iota\><rsub|e*D>*z|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=F<rsub|D>*\<iota\><rsub|d*D>*v+F<rsub|D>*\<iota\><rsub|e*D>*z=F<around|(|u|)>+F<around|(|w|)>.>>>>
      </aligned>
    </equation*>

    Also <math|F<around|(|0|)>=F<rsub|d><around|(|0|)>=0>, and for each
    <math|c\<in\>\<bbb-C\>>,

    <\equation*>
      F*<around|(|c*u|)>=F*<around|(|j<rsub|d>*<around|(|c*v|)>|)>=F<rsub|d>*<around|(|c*v|)>=\<sigma\><around|(|c|)>*F<rsub|d><around|(|v|)>=\<sigma\><around|(|c|)>*F<around|(|u|)>.
    </equation*>

    Only the image <math|\<sigma\><around|(|c|)>> is used; no preimage under
    <math|\<sigma\>> is chosen. Its restriction to each grade is
    <math|F<rsub|d>>. Any other such map agrees on every
    <math|j<rsub|d><around|(|E<rsub|d>|)>>, whose union is <math|E>, and
    therefore equals <math|F>. This is the algebraic direct-limit property of
    complex vector spaces; no topology or multiplication on the union is
    being asserted.
  </proof>

  These conclusions apply to a selected phase only with the realization
  receipt for that same selected table. More generally, suppose a second
  smooth symmetric table <math|T> on the same raw spaces has
  <math|N<rsub|d><rsup|S>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|d><rsup|T>> at every grade, and
  the same raw maps <math|K<rsub|d*e>> satisfy
  <eqref|eq:phase-inclusion-tested-input> for both tables. The natural
  surjections <math|q<rsub|d>:E<rsub|d><rsup|S>\<to\>E<rsub|d><rsup|T>>,
  <math|q<rsub|d>*\<pi\><rsub|d><rsup|S>*a=\<pi\><rsub|d><rsup|T>*a>, then
  obey

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|e>*\<iota\><rsub|d*e><rsup|S>*\<pi\><rsub|d><rsup|S>*a=\<pi\><rsub|e><rsup|T>*K<rsub|d*e>*a=\<iota\><rsub|d*e><rsup|T>*q<rsub|d>*\<pi\><rsub|d><rsup|S>*a.
  </equation*>

  Surjectivity of <math|\<pi\><rsub|d><rsup|S>> proves compatibility on all
  quotient labels. The universal property gives the induced map between the
  two unions. Correlations of <math|T> on the <math|S>-quotients are well
  defined by null-space containment, and their inclusion compatibility
  follows from the same pointwise tested identity for <math|T>. If the null
  spaces are equal, each <math|q<rsub|d>> is an isomorphism, and the induced
  union map is an isomorphism: its inverse is obtained from the compatible
  inverses <math|q<rsub|d><rsup|-1>>. The numerical correlations of the two
  tables need not be equal. Without the tested realization for <math|T> on
  these same raw maps, grade-wise null-space equality alone supplies none of
  these cross-grade correlation identities. Derivative maps and their slot
  identities, and compatibility with calibrated actions, require their own
  receipts. The reflected involution is constructed below only under its
  explicit additional raw symmetry and inclusion-compatibility hypotheses.

  <subsubsection|Attaching one table and passing its inclusions to every
  character><label|subsec:phase-inclusion-inheritance>

  <subparagraph|The same-table realization input.>
  <with|the-label|5.8.2|<label|par:phase-inclusion-attachment>> There is a
  conditional supplier for <eqref|eq:phase-inclusion-tested-input> in the
  preceding constructions. Fix one finite-volume family, its raw reference
  spaces <math|A<rsub|d>>, its terminal calibrations, and one retained
  extraction. Let <math|S> be the smooth symmetric table representing the
  ordered smeared limits of that same family. All the hypotheses of the
  insertion comparison in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-insertion-derivative-relay>,
  the projection and all-test kernel construction in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:finite-rank-kernel-transport>, and the
  normalized test argument in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-filtration>
  are retained on these data. In particular, this retains their local service
  and equation inputs, comparison and rank purchases, inverse-calibration
  bounds, and convergence of the smeared correlations to this <math|S>. The
  ordered limit permits the mesh threshold to depend on the selected period;
  no mesh threshold uniform in the period or diagonal limit is substituted.

  Here is the precise application. Fix <math|d\<leqslant\>e> and one basis
  label <math|a<rsub|d,j>>. The insertion comparison gives one row
  <math|u<rsub|c>\<in\>A<rsub|e>>, chosen before any spectator tuple or
  compact separated test <math|\<theta\>>, whose finite-cutoff tested values
  tend to the tested values <math|M<rsub|\<theta\>>> of <math|a<rsub|d,j>> on
  <math|S>. Write <math|P<rsub|e,c>> and <math|P<rsub|e>> for the finite and
  limiting projections in the kernel construction, and
  <math|f<rsub|\<theta\>,c>,f<rsub|\<theta\>>> for the corresponding tested
  linear functionals on <math|A<rsub|e>>. Their convergence and exact kernel
  identity are the hypotheses of <eqref|eq:ope-test-kernel>. The subpower
  local equation input applies also to

  <\equation*>
    <frac|u<rsub|c>-P<rsub|e,c>*u<rsub|c>|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|e,c>*u<rsub|c>|\<\|\|\>>>\<in\>ker
    P<rsub|e,c>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed this is exactly the normalized residual treated following
  <eqref|eq:ope-normalized-tests>: its first term has subpower bare norm by
  the relay bound, and its second is bounded in reference coordinates, hence
  has subpower bare norm by <eqref|eq:ope-bounded-bare>. Here the bare or
  inverse-calibrated norm is the norm in the actual inverse terminal
  coordinates, <math|<around|\||X<rsub|e,c><rsup|-1>*v|\|><rsub|1>>, with
  <math|X<rsub|e,c>> the same terminal calibration used by the relay. Thus
  both limits in <eqref|eq:ope-normalized-tests> hold, on the same filter and
  with the same row <math|u<rsub|c>>. The compactness argument there supplies
  one <math|b<rsub|d*e,j>\<in\><math-up|ran><space|0.17em>P<rsub|e>> with
  <math|f<rsub|\<theta\>><around|(|b<rsub|d*e,j>|)>=M<rsub|\<theta\>>> for
  every <math|\<theta\>>. This is <eqref|eq:phase-inclusion-tested-input>.
  Its one compact refinement is made for this row, not separately for each
  test. The functionals <math|f<rsub|\<theta\>>> and values
  <math|M<rsub|\<theta\>>> are fixed limits along the original ordered
  filter. Each resulting representative therefore satisfies an identity
  between refinement-independent quantities. Different basis labels may use
  different compact refinements; no joint extraction of those refinements is
  needed to assemble their representatives. Applying it to the basis labels
  supplies the raw maps <math|K<rsub|d*e>> above. This attachment uses only
  the inclusion comparison; it asserts neither a derivative identity nor the
  production of the retained analytic hypotheses.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The same inclusions in every whole-word
    character><label|prop:phase-inclusion-every-character>Let <math|S>,
    <math|A<rsub|d>> and the raw maps <math|K<rsub|d*e>> satisfy the
    hypotheses of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:phase-field-inclusions>.
    Assume in addition the unselected Hilbert space, bounded slab insertions,
    translation and permutation identities, and placement-independent
    compressed operators <math|M<around|(|w|)>> of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-phases> for this same <math|S>. Let
    <math|S<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|w|)>=\<chi\><around|(|M<around|(|w|)>|)>>,
    <math|\<chi\>\<in\>\<Xi\>>, be its whole-word character tables as in
    <eqref|ex-phase-word>. Assume these tables are continuous on each
    distinct-point configuration space, as supplied, with its own hypotheses,
    by Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-caps>. Then, for every character,
    every <math|d\<leqslant\>e>, every <math|a\<in\>A<rsub|d>>, every finite
    raw spectator tuple <math|\<b-b\>> of arbitrary grades, and every
    <math|x\<in\><math-up|Conf><rsub|n+1><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:phase-inclusion-character>S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|x;<around|(|e,K<rsub|d*e>*a|)>,\<b-b\>|)>=S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|x;<around|(|d,a|)>,\<b-b\>|)>.
    </equation>

    The identity holds in any slot and after integration against every smooth
    compactly supported test in configuration space. The maps
    <math|K<rsub|d*e>> are those fixed for <math|S>, independent of the
    character and of the exterior. They induce coherent injective inclusions
    between the phase field quotients and a common algebraic union in each
    phase. The natural maps from the original quotients and union to these
    phase quotients and union commute with all inclusions. If, for a given
    character, <math|N<rsub|d>=N<rsub|\<chi\>,d>> for every <math|d>, these
    comparison maps are isomorphisms.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:phase-field-inclusions> has already
    converted the tested input on <math|S> to the pointwise all-exterior
    identity <eqref|eq:phase-inclusion-raw-response>. Fix
    <math|d,e,a,x,\<b-b\>>, and let <math|w> and <math|w<rprime|'>> be the
    words at these same positions, with distinguished labels
    <math|<around|(|d,a|)>> and <math|<around|(|e,K<rsub|d*e>*a|)>>
    respectively. Translate both into the same open slab of width
    <math|h\<gtr\>0>. This is possible because their common finite set of
    times is bounded: choose the translation so that its smallest time is
    positive and then choose <math|h> larger than its largest time. Denote
    the two bounded insertion operators by <math|B> and <math|B<rprime|'>>.

    Use the matrix-element identity from the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-original-null>. For any positive-time
    words <math|u,z>, the two quantities

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|<around|[|u|]>,B<around|[|z|]>|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em><around|\<langle\>|<around|[|u|]>,B<rprime|'><around|[|z|]>|\<rangle\>>
    </equation*>

    are unselected correlations obtained by placing a reflected copy of
    <math|u> below the slab and a shifted copy of <math|z> above it. The
    exterior words and all their labels are the same in both expressions.
    Their points are internally distinct, and the three time ranges are
    disjoint; hence these are legitimate separated exteriors. Translation and
    permutation invariance of <math|S> put the distinguished label first and
    undo the common translation. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:phase-inclusion-raw-response>,
    with the original spectators and these additional exterior labels, makes
    the two matrix elements equal. This step uses all finite spectators, not
    merely a finite family of two-point tests.

    Sesquilinearity gives equality for finite linear combinations of
    positive-time word vectors on both sides. Those vectors span a dense
    subspace of the unselected Hilbert space. Since <math|B-B<rprime|'>> is
    bounded, continuity gives <math|<around|\<langle\>|v,<around|(|B-B<rprime|'>|)>*z|\<rangle\>>=0>
    for all Hilbert vectors <math|v,z>. Taking
    <math|v=<around|(|B-B<rprime|'>|)>*z> proves
    <math|<around|(|B-B<rprime|'>|)>*z=0> for every <math|z>, so
    <math|B=B<rprime|'>>. Compression to the ground subspace preserves this
    equality. Placement independence identifies the compressed operators with
    <math|M<around|(|w|)>> and <math|M<around|(|w<rprime|'>|)>>. Applying any
    character proves <eqref|eq:phase-inclusion-character>. There is no
    exceptional set of characters. Equivalently, one may apply a continuous
    linear extension of the character after compression; only its values on
    these whole-word operators are used. No multiplicativity for separated
    subwords, positivity of such an extension, or differentiability of a slab
    insertion is needed for this step.

    Multilinearity and permutation symmetry of the slab operators, obtained
    by the same dense matrix-element test from those properties of <math|S>,
    pass to every <math|S<rsub|\<chi\>>>. It moves the distinguished slot to
    any prescribed slot. Continuity on configuration space makes each side
    integrable against a compactly supported smooth test there; integrating
    the pointwise identity proves the tested identity. Empty spectator tuples
    are included in this argument; the comparison then concerns one
    insertion. The empty word has no slot to raise and keeps its original
    normalization.

    For clarity, this also gives the identity formulated using the original
    quotient labels, without imposing linearity on a choice of raw
    representatives. If <math|v=\<pi\><rsub|d>*a> and
    <math|r<rsub|d><around|(|v|)>> is any representative of <math|v>, then
    <math|r<rsub|d><around|(|v|)>-a\<in\>N<rsub|d>>. Similarly,

    <\equation*>
      r<rsub|e>*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|d*e>*v|)>-K<rsub|d*e>*a\<in\>N<rsub|e>,<space|2em>\<iota\><rsub|d*e>*v=\<pi\><rsub|e>*K<rsub|d*e>*a.
    </equation*>

    Here we use only the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-original-null>:
    bounded slab insertion, its matrix-element identity, density and
    placement independence imply original-null containment. All these
    hypotheses are already assumed here; the stronger derivative and all-copy
    bounds standing in its section are not used in that proof. It follows
    that both differences vanish in every character correlation against
    arbitrary spectators. The same is true for any changes of spectator
    representatives. Thus the identity on the fixed original quotient plan
    and the raw identity <eqref|eq:phase-inclusion-character> agree. The
    linear map is induced by <math|K<rsub|d*e>> on the quotient, not by the
    representative section <math|r<rsub|d>>.

    Finally apply the construction of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:phase-field-inclusions>
    to the phase response maps: <eqref|eq:phase-inclusion-character> gives
    <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|\<chi\>,e>*K<rsub|d*e>=\<cal-R\><rsub|\<chi\>,d>>
    directly. The descent, injectivity, uniqueness and composition proof
    there uses this identity, linearity and symmetry only. Its earlier
    smoothness assumption was used to obtain the pointwise identity from the
    tested one; that step is already proved here.
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:phase-field-union> therefore constructs
    the phase union with continuous correlations; its smoothness clauses are
    replaced by continuity in this application. Smoothness follows only when
    the additional hypotheses of Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-caps>
    are supplied. By Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-original-null>,
    <math|N<rsub|d>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|\<chi\>,d>>, so the two-table
    comparison preceding this subsection applies to these same raw maps.
    Explicitly, with <math|q<rsub|\<chi\>,d>*\<pi\><rsub|d>*a=\<pi\><rsub|\<chi\>,d>*a>,

    <\equation*>
      q<rsub|\<chi\>,e>*\<iota\><rsub|d*e>*\<pi\><rsub|d>*a=\<pi\><rsub|\<chi\>,e>*K<rsub|d*e>*a=\<iota\><rsup|\<chi\>><rsub|d*e>*q<rsub|\<chi\>,d>*\<pi\><rsub|d>*a.
    </equation*>

    Surjectivity of <math|\<pi\><rsub|d>> gives the commuting identity on
    <math|E<rsub|d>> and hence on its algebraic union. If the null spaces are
    equal in every grade, the <math|q<rsub|\<chi\>,d>> and their compatible
    union map are isomorphisms as proved there. This last conclusion compares
    field spaces and their inclusions; it does not assert equality of
    numerical correlations of <math|S> and <math|S<rsub|\<chi\>>>.
  </proof>

  This supplies the same-map selected-table attachment conditional on the
  stated unselected realization and whole-word construction. It does not
  supply the local service, calibrated comparison, rank or ordered-limit
  inputs in the <hlink|same-table realization
  paragraph|#par:phase-inclusion-attachment>. Equality of the null spaces,
  when needed for isomorphisms, retains the separate hypotheses of the
  earlier phase-null argument. Derivative and Ward identities remain separate
  statements. The next subsection treats only the reflected conjugation,
  under its stated additional symmetry and orientation inputs.

  <subsubsection|A compatible reflected adjoint on the common
  union><label|subsec:phase-union-adjoint>

  We retain the raw spaces, null quotients and inclusions already
  constructed. The following are additional inputs, not consequences of
  equality of null spaces. On each <math|A<rsub|d>> assume a conjugate-linear
  involution <math|a\<mapsto\><wide|a|\<bar\>>> and a complex-linear action
  <math|R<rsub|d,s>> of the signed coordinate transformations, commuting with
  conjugation. Assume that the same table <math|S> obeys, at every
  distinct-point configuration,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:phase-raw-real-covariance>

    <\eqsplit>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|S*<around|(|x;<wide|\<b-a\>|\<bar\>>|)>>|<cell|=<wide|S*<around|(|x;\<b-a\>|)>|\<bar\>>,>>|<row|<cell|S*<around|(|s*x;R<rsub|s>*\<b-a\>|)>>|<cell|=S*<around|(|x;\<b-a\>|)>.>>>>
    </eqsplit>
  </equation>

  Here each operation acts at the grade of its label, and <math|s*x>
  transforms all positions. These are the reality and signed covariance
  receipts for this table. Fix a coordinate reflection <math|\<rho\>>, so
  <math|\<rho\><rsup|2>=1>. The raw reflected conjugation is
  <math|a<rsup|\<dagger\><rsub|\<rho\>>>=R<rsub|d,\<rho\>>*<wide|a|\<bar\>>>.
  This is the fixed-grade operation of Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-reflection-transport>,
  not ordinary reflection alone. Its conjugate linearity and square-one
  identity follow from the commuting real structure and signed action, as
  proved there. We now check descent and compatibility with this particular
  field union.

  First these operations preserve each null space. If
  <math|a\<in\>N<rsub|d>>, then for arbitrary raw spectators <math|\<b-b\>>
  and separated <math|x>,

  <\equation*>
    S*<around|(|x;<wide|a|\<bar\>>,\<b-b\>|)>=<wide|S*<around|(|x;a,<wide|\<b-b\>|\<bar\>>|)>|\<bar\>>=0.
  </equation*>

  For a signed transformation <math|s>, covariance likewise gives

  <\equation*>
    S*<around|(|x;R<rsub|d,s>*a,\<b-b\>|)>=S*<around|(|s<rsup|-1>*x;a,R<rsub|s<rsup|-1>>*\<b-b\>|)>=0.
  </equation*>

  The transformed positions remain distinct, and the transformed spectators
  are admissible raw labels of the same grades. Thus conjugation and
  <math|R<rsub|d,s>> preserve <math|N<rsub|d>>. Applying the inverse
  operations shows that they carry it onto itself. Consequently the quotient
  maps

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|c><rsub|d><around|(|\<pi\><rsub|d>*a|)>=\<pi\><rsub|d>*<wide|a|\<bar\>>,<space|2em><math-ss|m><rsub|d,s><around|(|\<pi\><rsub|d>*a|)>=\<pi\><rsub|d>*R<rsub|d,s>*a
  </equation*>

  are well defined: replacing <math|a> by <math|a+n> with
  <math|n\<in\>N<rsub|d>> changes either image by a null class. Additivity
  and conjugate homogeneity of <math|<math-ss|c><rsub|d>>, and complex
  linearity of <math|<math-ss|m><rsub|d,s>>, follow by applying these
  formulas to sums and scalar multiples of raw representatives. Surjectivity
  of <math|\<pi\><rsub|d>> passes the raw group law, commutation with
  conjugation and square-one identities to the quotient. In particular

  <\equation*>
    \<delta\><rsub|d,\<rho\>>=<math-ss|m><rsub|d,\<rho\>><math-ss|c><rsub|d>:E<rsub|d>\<longrightarrow\>E<rsub|d>
  </equation*>

  is conjugate-linear and satisfies <math|\<delta\><rsub|d,\<rho\>><rsup|2>=1>.

  To pass to the union retain the inclusion-only orientation identities

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:phase-inclusion-orientation><math-ss|c><rsub|e>\<iota\><rsub|d*e>=\<iota\><rsub|d*e><math-ss|c><rsub|d>,<space|2em><math-ss|m><rsub|e,\<rho\>>\<iota\><rsub|d*e>=\<iota\><rsub|d*e><math-ss|m><rsub|d,\<rho\>><space|1em><around|(|d\<leqslant\>e|)>.
  </equation>

  It suffices to supply these for the chosen reflection; the stronger signed
  orientation receipt supplies the second identity for every signed <math|s>.
  For the raw maps used above, these assumptions have the precise equivalent
  form

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:phase-raw-inclusion-orientation>

    <\eqsplit>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<wide|K<rsub|d*e>*a|\<bar\>>-K<rsub|d*e>*<wide|a|\<bar\>>>|<cell|\<in\>N<rsub|e>,>>|<row|<cell|R<rsub|e,\<rho\>>*K<rsub|d*e>*a-K<rsub|d*e>*R<rsub|d,\<rho\>>*a>|<cell|\<in\>N<rsub|e>*<space|2em><around|(|a\<in\>A<rsub|d>|)>.>>>>
    </eqsplit>
  </equation>

  Indeed, substitute <math|\<iota\><rsub|d*e>*\<pi\><rsub|d>*a=\<pi\><rsub|e>*K<rsub|d*e>*a>
  in each side of <eqref|eq:phase-inclusion-orientation>. Their difference is
  the projection of the corresponding expression in
  <eqref|eq:phase-raw-inclusion-orientation>; its projection vanishes exactly
  when it lies in <math|N<rsub|e>>. Every quotient label is a projection, so
  this proves both directions. Exact commutation of arbitrarily chosen raw
  representatives is not required. These orientation receipts are retained
  explicitly here; neither grade-wise null equality nor the choice of basis
  images for <math|K<rsub|d*e>> is being used as their proof.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Reflected adjoint of the field
    union><label|prop:phase-union-adjoint>Under the hypotheses above, the
    same algebraic union <math|E> has a unique conjugate-linear map
    <math|\<cal-J\><rsub|\<rho\>>:E\<to\>E> satisfying

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:phase-union-adjoint>\<cal-J\><rsub|\<rho\>>*j<rsub|d>*\<pi\><rsub|d>*a=j<rsub|d>*\<pi\><rsub|d>*a<rsup|\<dagger\><rsub|\<rho\>>>*<space|2em><around|(|d\<geqslant\>0,a\<in\>A<rsub|d>|)>.
    </equation>

    It is an involution and hence a bijection. In particular it preserves
    every level subspace <math|j<rsub|d><around|(|E<rsub|d>|)>>, with
    restriction equal to the already specified reflected adjoint of that
    grade. No topology on <math|E> is required.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The two identities in <eqref|eq:phase-inclusion-orientation> imply

    <\equation*>
      \<delta\><rsub|e,\<rho\>>*\<iota\><rsub|d*e>=<math-ss|m><rsub|e,\<rho\>><math-ss|c><rsub|e>\<iota\><rsub|d*e>=<math-ss|m><rsub|e,\<rho\>>\<iota\><rsub|d*e><math-ss|c><rsub|d>=\<iota\><rsub|d*e>*\<delta\><rsub|d,\<rho\>>.
    </equation*>

    Thus <math|F<rsub|d>=j<rsub|d>*\<delta\><rsub|d,\<rho\>>:E<rsub|d>\<to\>E>
    is a compatible family:

    <\equation*>
      F<rsub|e>*\<iota\><rsub|d*e>=j<rsub|e>*\<delta\><rsub|e,\<rho\>>*\<iota\><rsub|d*e>=j<rsub|e>*\<iota\><rsub|d*e>*\<delta\><rsub|d,\<rho\>>=F<rsub|d>.
    </equation*>

    Each <math|F<rsub|d>> is additive and semilinear for
    <math|\<sigma\><around|(|c|)>=<wide|c|\<bar\>>>.
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:phase-field-union> supplies the unique
    such map <math|\<cal-J\><rsub|\<rho\>>>, and its restriction formula
    gives <eqref|eq:phase-union-adjoint>. More concretely, if the same
    element is represented as <math|j<rsub|d>*v=j<rsub|e>*z>, then at
    <math|D=max <around|(|d,e|)>> its representatives are equal. Applying
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|D,\<rho\>>> and the compatibility just proved gives
    <math|j<rsub|d>*\<delta\><rsub|d,\<rho\>>*v=j<rsub|e>*\<delta\><rsub|e,\<rho\>>*z>,
    which is the representative independence needed for this formula. For any
    <math|u\<in\>E> choose <math|u=j<rsub|d>*v>. Then

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-J\><rsub|\<rho\>><rsup|2>*u=\<cal-J\><rsub|\<rho\>>*j<rsub|d>*\<delta\><rsub|d,\<rho\>>*v=j<rsub|d>*\<delta\><rsub|d,\<rho\>><rsup|2>*v=j<rsub|d>*v=u.
    </equation*>

    Hence <math|\<cal-J\><rsub|\<rho\>>> is its own inverse. The same
    computation within a fixed grade shows that its image of
    <math|j<rsub|d><around|(|E<rsub|d>|)>> is all of that level subspace. Any
    competing conjugate-linear map with the raw restriction formula agrees on
    every <math|j<rsub|d><around|(|E<rsub|d>|)>>, since <math|\<pi\><rsub|d>>
    is onto, and therefore agrees on their union. This proves the stated
    uniqueness.
  </proof>

  For a second table <math|T>, comparison of adjoints requires the same raw
  conjugation and signed actions, its own reality and covariance receipts
  <eqref|eq:phase-raw-real-covariance>, and compatible inclusion maps for
  both tables as in <eqref|eq:phase-inclusion-orientation>. Assume also
  <math|N<rsub|d><rsup|S>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|d><rsup|T>> and the
  same-<math|K<rsub|d*e>> correlation identities required by the earlier
  two-table comparison. The resulting maps
  <math|q<rsub|d>:E<rsub|d><rsup|S>\<to\>E<rsub|d><rsup|T>> then satisfy, on
  every raw class,

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|d>*\<delta\><rsup|S><rsub|d,\<rho\>>*\<pi\><rsub|d><rsup|S>*a=\<pi\><rsub|d><rsup|T>*a<rsup|\<dagger\><rsub|\<rho\>>>=\<delta\><rsup|T><rsub|d,\<rho\>>*q<rsub|d>*\<pi\><rsub|d><rsup|S>*a.
  </equation*>

  They therefore intertwine the grade adjoints. If
  <math|q:E<rsup|S>\<to\>E<rsup|T>> is the induced union map, the defining
  identities for <math|q> and <math|\<cal-J\><rsub|\<rho\>>> give
  <math|q*\<cal-J\><rsup|S><rsub|\<rho\>>*j<rsub|d><rsup|S>=\<cal-J\><rsup|T><rsub|\<rho\>>*q*j<rsub|d><rsup|S>>
  at every grade, hence <math|q*\<cal-J\><rsup|S><rsub|\<rho\>>=\<cal-J\><rsup|T><rsub|\<rho\>>*q>
  on the union. If the null spaces are equal, <math|q> is the earlier union
  isomorphism and this identity also intertwines its inverse. This applies to
  a character table only when the stated common raw actions and its
  covariance and orientation receipts are supplied. Original-null containment
  alone does not assert them. Nor does this algebraic construction prove
  compatibility with derivatives, a calibrated reference identification,
  continuous rotation or Lorentz actions, or any Ward identity.

  <subsubsection|Constructing the field derivatives from finite
  differences><label|subsec:phase-field-derivatives>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Derivative representatives on the same
    table><label|prop:phase-field-derivatives>Retain the finite-volume
    family, calibrations, ordered extraction and smooth mixed table of
    paragraph<nbsp><reference|par:phase-inclusion-attachment>, with the
    insertion and difference comparisons of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-insertion-derivative-relay>. There
    are linear maps

    <\equation*>
      \<partial\><rsub|\<alpha\>>:E<rsub|d>\<longrightarrow\>E<rsub|d+1>,<space|2em>\<alpha\>=1,\<ldots\>,4,
    </equation*>

    compatible with the grade inclusions, such that insertion of
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|\<alpha\>>*A> in any slot differentiates the
    correlation in that slot's <math|\<alpha\>> coordinate. They commute with
    one another, kill the unit, and commute with conjugation. Under a signed
    orthogonal coordinate symmetry <math|g> they obey
    <math|\<rho\><around|(|g|)>*\<partial\><rsub|\<alpha\>>=<big|sum><rsub|\<beta\>>g<rsub|\<beta\>*\<alpha\>>*\<partial\><rsub|\<beta\>>*\<rho\><around|(|g|)>>.
    These identities hold in the common field union and in every whole-word
    character correlation. In particular they supply
    <eqref|ex-raw-slot-identity> with any chosen raw representatives.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Fix a raw label <math|v> at grade <math|d> and a coordinate
    <math|\<alpha\>>. Apply the finite-difference comparison to

    <\equation*>
      Z<rsub|c><around|(|o|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|-1>*<around|{|O<rsub|c><around|(|v;o+e<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>-O<rsub|c><around|(|v;o|)>|}>,<space|2em>c=<around|(|N,p|)>.
    </equation*>

    It supplies one comparison label <math|u<rsub|c>\<in\>A<rsub|d+1>>,
    independent of spectators and tests, with subpower inverse terminal
    coordinates and vanishing tested discrepancy. We identify its tested
    limit explicitly. For a smooth compactly supported separated joint test
    <math|f>, the lattice sum for <math|Z<rsub|c>> can be reindexed in its
    distinguished slot. On its compact support, the mesh is eventually
    smaller than the separation margin; at every suitable fixed period there
    is also a positive margin to the boundary of the chosen coordinate box.
    The reindexing is therefore legitimate (or equivalently is reindexing of
    the periodic sum), and gives

    <\equation*>
      a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>*<big|sum><rsub|\<b-x\>>f<around|(|\<b-x\>|)>*\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|Z<rsub|c><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<ne\>i>O<rsub|c><around|(|v<rsub|j>;x<rsub|j>|)>|]>=a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>*<big|sum><rsub|\<b-x\>><frac|f*<around|(|\<b-x\>-a<rsub|N>*e<rsub|i,\<alpha\>>|)>-f<around|(|\<b-x\>|)>|a<rsub|N>>*\<bbb-E\><around|[|<big|prod><rsub|j>O<rsub|c><around|(|v<rsub|j>;x<rsub|j>|)>|]>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|v<rsub|i>=v>. The test on the right converges in every smooth
    norm, on one fixed compact separated set, to
    <math|-\<partial\><rsub|i,\<alpha\>>*f>. The zeroth-order compact-uniform
    bounds of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-fullmesh-fixedbounds-producer>
    bound the absolute lattice pairing on that set by a constant times the
    supremum norm of its test: the mesh weight times the number of sites
    there is bounded, and the correlations are uniformly bounded there on the
    relevant late tail. Consequently replacing that varying test by its limit
    changes the pairing by a quantity tending to zero. First take the mesh
    limit at each suitable period and then the retained volume limit. The
    resulting functional is

    <\equation*>
      M<rsub|\<theta\>>=-<big|int><around|(|\<partial\><rsub|i,\<alpha\>>*f|)><around|(|x|)>*S<rsub|\<b-v\>><around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x=<big|int>f<around|(|x|)>*\<partial\><rsub|i,\<alpha\>>*S<rsub|\<b-v\>><around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x.
    </equation*>

    The last equality is ordinary integration by parts on the open separated
    configuration domain; the support is compactly contained in it. This
    argument does not interchange mesh and volume limits or require a mesh
    threshold uniform in the period.

    Use the same finite and limiting grade-<math|<around|(|d+1|)>> projectors
    as in paragraph<nbsp><reference|par:phase-inclusion-attachment>. Their
    exact all-test kernel identity and the local equation estimate apply to
    <math|<around|(|u<rsub|c>-P<rsub|c>*u<rsub|c>|)>/<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|c>*u<rsub|c>|\<\|\|\>>|)>>:
    the first summand has subpower inverse terminal norm by the difference
    relay, and the second has bounded reference norm and hence subpower
    inverse terminal norm. Thus the two limits in
    <eqref|eq:ope-normalized-tests> hold with the <math|M<rsub|\<theta\>>>
    just computed. That compactness argument supplies a single
    <math|w\<in\><math-up|ran><space|0.17em>P<rsub|d+1>> with
    <math|f<rsub|\<theta\>><around|(|w|)>=M<rsub|\<theta\>>> for <em|all>
    spectator tuples and separated tests. It does not select a different
    label for each test. Smoothness of both correlation kernels turns this
    distributional identity into their pointwise equality. Define
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|\<alpha\>><around|[|v|]><rsub|d>=<around|[|w|]><rsub|d+1>>.

    Any two such representatives have zero correlations against all
    spectators, and hence differ by the defining null space. In particular,
    any derivative plan on this same table satisfying the all-spectator slot
    identity agrees with these maps on the quotient; previously retained
    derivative fields are thereby identified, not independently reselected.
    If <math|v> is null, all its correlations vanish identically, so their
    derivatives vanish and <math|w> is null. This proves well-definedness on
    the quotient. Linearity follows by differentiating the linear correlation
    identities and invoking the same null-space criterion. For two
    representatives of the same field at different grades, the inclusion slot
    identity <eqref|eq:phase-inclusion-slot>, differentiated in that slot,
    shows that the resulting derivatives agree after inclusion. Thus the maps
    define operators on the common union.

    Apply the slot identity twice. Equality of mixed partial derivatives of
    the smooth table, followed again by the all-spectator null criterion,
    gives <math|\<partial\><rsub|\<alpha\>>*\<partial\><rsub|\<beta\>>=\<partial\><rsub|\<beta\>>*\<partial\><rsub|\<alpha\>>>
    in <math|E<rsub|d+2>>. The unit slot is constant in its position, so its
    derivative is zero. Differentiating the conjugation identity at real
    positions proves commutation with conjugation. Differentiating the
    covariance identity and using the ordinary chain rule for
    <math|x\<mapsto\>g*x> proves the displayed signed symmetry rule. These
    conclusions concern quotient fields, so no linear or equivariant choice
    of raw representatives is necessary.

    Finally fix a slab and exterior word vectors. Their matrix element is a
    mixed correlation with the inserted word in the slab and the exterior
    words strictly outside it. The slot identity just proved holds for that
    correlation with all exterior labels. On a compact neighborhood of the
    insertion positions, the all-copy slab bounds also bound the operators
    with the differentiated labels. The operator remainder argument of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-operator-remainder>, iterated with these labels,
    therefore yields the slot identity in operator norm. Compressing to the
    ground space and applying the bounded character preserves that identity.
    This proves it for every character, with the original derivative fields;
    there is no new choice of derivatives in individual phases.
  </proof>

  <section|Relativistic reconstruction>

  <subsection|Complex heat time and real-time
  translations><label|sec:complex-heat>

  The complex heat extension used in the boundary argument can be constructed
  from the real Euclidean semigroup alone. This also fixes the sign of the
  real-time translation group. Let <math|<around|(|E<rsub|t>|)><rsub|t\<geqslant\>0>>
  be a strongly continuous semigroup of self-adjoint contractions on a
  complex Hilbert space, with <math|E<rsub|0>=I>. Each <math|E<rsub|t>> is
  positive because <math|E<rsub|t>=E<rsub|t/2><rsup|2>>.

  We use continuous functional calculus for a bounded self-adjoint operator:
  continuous scalar functions on its spectrum act as a unital isometric star
  homomorphism, with the usual composition rule. This is the self-adjoint
  instance of the continuous calculus in <cite-detail|Williams|Theorem 2.19>.
  We use the continuous calculus for positive roots and the bounded Borel
  calculus for the imaginary powers.

  <\proposition>
    <label|prop:complex-heat>There is a holomorphic operator-valued function
    <math|E<around|(|z|)>> on <math|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>z\<gtr\>0> such
    that

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|E<around|(|z|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1,<space|1em>E*<around|(|z+w|)>=E<around|(|z|)>*E<around|(|w|)>,<space|1em>E<around|(|z|)><rsup|\<ast\>>=E<around|(|<wide|z|\<bar\>>|)>,<space|1em>E<around|(|t|)>=E<rsub|t>*<space|1em><around|(|t\<gtr\>0|)>.
    </equation*>

    Each <math|E<rsub|t>>, <math|t\<gtr\>0>, is injective and has dense
    range. There is a strongly continuous unitary group
    <math|V<around|(|t|)>>, <math|t\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>, characterized by

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:complex-heat-edge>V<around|(|t|)>*E<around|(|z|)>=E<around|(|z|)>*V<around|(|t|)>=E*<around|(|z-i*t|)>*<space|2em><around|(|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>z\<gtr\>0|)>.
    </equation>

    Every bounded operator commuting with all <math|E<rsub|t>> commutes with
    <math|E<around|(|z|)>> and <math|V<around|(|t|)>>. If
    <math|E<rsub|t>*\<Omega\>=\<Omega\>> for all <math|t>, then
    <math|E<around|(|z|)>*\<Omega\>=V<around|(|t|)>*\<Omega\>=\<Omega\>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    If <math|E<rsub|t>*x=0> for some <math|t\<gtr\>0>, self-adjointness and
    the semigroup law give <math|E<rsub|t/2>*x=0>. Iterate and use strong
    continuity at zero to get <math|x=0>. Since <math|E<rsub|t>> is
    self-adjoint, its range is dense.

    Write <math|A=E<rsub|1>>. By the bounded Borel functional calculus
    <cite-detail|ReedSimonI|Theorem<nbsp>VII.2> the spectral measure of
    <math|A> is supported on <math|<around|[|0,1|]>> and has zero projection
    at <math|0> by injectivity. Define <math|E<around|(|z|)>=A<rsup|z>> and
    <math|V<around|(|t|)>=A<rsup|-i*t>> using the real logarithm on
    <math|<around|(|0,1|]>>. The values of the multipliers at zero are
    immaterial. The product, adjoint, norm and edge identities follow from
    the scalar multipliers. Dominated convergence for the vector spectral
    measures makes <math|V<around|(|t|)>> strongly continuous. On each
    compact subset of <math|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>z\<gtr\>0>, the bound
    on <math|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>><around|\||log \<lambda\>|\|><rsup|2>>
    for any <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0> gives the uniform Taylor remainder
    needed for norm holomorphy.

    For positive integers <math|n>, <math|E<rsub|1/n>> is the positive
    <math|n>th root of <math|A> by the semigroup law and the continuous
    calculus. Thus <math|E*<around|(|p/n|)>=E<rsub|p/n>> for positive
    rational <math|p/n>. Norm continuity of <math|E<around|(|z|)>> and strong
    continuity of the semigroup identify them on all positive real times. A
    bounded operator commuting with the semigroup commutes with <math|A> and
    hence its Borel calculus. If <math|A*\<Omega\>=\<Omega\>>, the scalar
    multipliers at <math|1> give both vacuum identities. Finally, the edge
    identity on the dense range of <math|A> determines
    <math|V<around|(|t|)>>.
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|Coordinate convention in Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:complex-heat>\U<reference|sec:locality>.>
  Section<nbsp><reference|ex-statement> writes points of
  <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>> as <math|x=<around|(|\<b-x\>,t|)>>, with time
  last. In Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:complex-heat>\U<reference|sec:locality>
  we write instead <math|a=<around|(|a<rsup|0>,\<b-up-a\>|)>> and
  <math|X<rsub|i>=<around|(|t<rsub|i>,x<rsub|i>|)>> with time first, so that
  index <math|0> is the time coordinate, and matrices such as
  <math|\<eta\>=<math-up|diag><around|(|1,-1,-1,-1|)>> are written in this
  order. The two descriptions are related by the linear change of coordinates
  <math|C*<around|(|t,\<b-up-x\>|)>=<around|(|\<b-up-x\>,t|)>> from
  time-first to time-last coordinates, with <math|<around|\||det C|\|>=1> and
  <math|C<rsup|-1>=C<rsup|<math-ss|T>>>. This is a change of realization, not
  a symmetry of either one. The Minkowski matrices satisfy
  <math|\<eta\><rsub|<math-up|last>>=C*\<eta\><rsub|<math-up|first>>*C<rsup|<math-ss|T>>>;
  a Lorentz matrix <math|\<Lambda\>> in time-first coordinates corresponds to
  <math|C*\<Lambda\>*C<rsup|-1>>; the time-last translation by <math|a>
  corresponds to the time-first translation by <math|C<rsup|-1>*a>; and the
  forward cone <math|<around|{|p<rsup|0>\<geqslant\><around|\||\<b-up-p\>|\|>|}>>
  corresponds to <math|<around|{|<around|(|\<b-up-p\>,E|)>:<around|\||\<b-up-p\>|\|>\<leqslant\>E|}>>.
  Accordingly, the statements of these sections transfer to the time-last
  convention of Section<nbsp><reference|ex-statement> under the following
  substitutions: test functions are pulled back,
  <math|f\<mapsto\>f\<circ\>C>; the Poincaré action is conjugated by
  <math|C>; and supports, cones and spectral measures are pushed forward by
  <math|C>. The pullback is a topological linear isomorphism of
  <math|\<cal-S\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)>> commuting with complex
  conjugation. It preserves spacelike separation of supports for the
  corresponding forms, and integrals acquire no Jacobian factor.

  Suppose now that spatial translations <math|U<around|(|x|)>> form a
  strongly continuous unitary representation commuting with the heat
  semigroup and fixing <math|\<Omega\><rsub|E>>. The proposition defines a
  strongly continuous unitary representation of all real translations,

  <\equation*>
    T<rsub|E>*<around|(|a<rsup|0>,\<b-up-a\>|)>=V<around|(|a<rsup|0>|)>*U<around|(|\<b-up-a\>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The group law follows from commutation. Joint strong continuity follows by
  adding and subtracting <math|V<around|(|a<rsup|0>|)>*U<around|(|\<b-up-b\>|)>*\<xi\>>
  and using the unitary norms. Moreover <math|E*<around|(|\<varepsilon\>-i*t|)>*\<xi\>=V<around|(|t|)>*E<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>*\<xi\>\<to\>V<around|(|t|)>*\<xi\>>
  as <math|\<varepsilon\>*\<downarrow\>*0>, which justifies the boundary
  notation <math|E*<around|(|-i*t|)>> used in the heat-vector chapter. No
  operator-norm continuity at the boundary is required.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lem:time-fixed-heat>The vectors fixed by every
    <math|V<around|(|t|)>> are exactly the vectors fixed by every
    <math|E<rsub|s>>. Consequently, if the selected heat ground space is
    <math|\<bbb-C\>*\<Omega\><rsub|E>>, the same is true of the subspace
    fixed by all real translations.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    A heat-fixed vector satisfies <math|A*\<xi\>=\<xi\>>, so functional
    calculus and the definition of <math|V> give
    <math|V<around|(|t|)>*\<xi\>=\<xi\>>. Conversely suppose
    <math|V<around|(|t|)>*\<xi\>=\<xi\>>. The holomorphic vector function
    <math|g<around|(|z|)>=E<around|(|z|)>*\<xi\>> satisfies
    <math|g*<around|(|z-i*t|)>=E<around|(|z|)>*V<around|(|t|)>*\<xi\>=g<around|(|z|)>>.
    Its derivative in the imaginary direction is zero; complex
    differentiability therefore makes its full derivative zero. It is
    constant on the connected right half-plane. Since
    <math|E<rsub|s>*\<xi\>\<to\>\<xi\>> as <math|s*\<downarrow\>*0>, this
    constant is <math|\<xi\>>. A vector invariant under all translations is,
    in particular, invariant under <math|V<around|(|t|)>>, and hence belongs
    to the heat ground space. The vacuum itself is translation-invariant,
    which proves the last assertion.
  </proof>

  Combining this lemma with the isometric translation intertwiner in
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:word-motion> proves uniqueness of the
  invariant vacuum in the reconstructed field Hilbert space: an invariant
  <math|\<xi\>> maps to <math|c*\<Omega\><rsub|E>=\<cal-V\>*<around|(|c*\<Omega\>|)>>,
  and injectivity of the intertwiner <math|\<cal-V\>> gives
  <math|\<xi\>=c*\<Omega\>>.

  <subsection|Analytic time continuation and joint boundary
  values><label|sec:analytic-boundary>

  We next construct the tempered boundary distribution used in the field
  reconstruction argument. The crucial bound is a power of the smallest
  imaginary-time gap. No bound uniform in the number of insertions is needed
  in this section.

  <subsubsection|Centered insertions and continuation>

  Work in the selected Euclidean Hilbert space with heat contractions
  <math|E<rsub|t>>, spatial translations <math|U<around|(|x|)>>, and vacuum
  <math|\<Omega\><rsub|E>>. In this section we use the complex heat extension
  <math|E<around|(|z|)>> on <math|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>z\<gtr\>0>: it is
  holomorphic in operator norm, satisfies the semigroup law and
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|E<around|(|z|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1>, agrees with
  <math|E<rsub|t>> for positive real <math|z>, and commutes with
  <math|U<around|(|x|)>>. These properties were established in
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:complex-heat>.

  For a finite-level label <math|v>, define <math|B<rsub|p><around|(|v|)>>,
  <math|p\<gtr\>0>, to be the slab operator of width <math|2*p> with its
  single insertion at spatial position zero and time <math|p>. All exterior
  matrix elements are precisely the corresponding Euclidean correlations.
  Multilinearity of those correlations, tested against the dense span of
  Euclidean words on both sides, proves linearity in <math|v>. The same
  argument, using compatibility of the finite-level inclusions in every
  correlation, proves that these maps agree under inclusion. They therefore
  define one linear map <math|B<rsub|p>:E\<to\>\<cal-B\><around|(|\<cal-H\><rsub|E>|)>>
  on the union of the field spaces. This use of all exterior words is what
  makes the union map well-defined.

  The single-insertion case of the all-copy slab bound gives, for each fixed
  <math|v\<in\>E>, constants <math|C<rsub|v>\<gtr\>0> and an integer
  <math|a<rsub|v>\<geqslant\>0> such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:bar-power><around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsub|p><around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|v>*p<rsup|-a<rsub|v>>,<space|2em>0\<less\>p\<less\>1.
  </equation>

  Indeed, choose a finite level representing <math|v> and a bound for the
  norm of its chosen raw representative. The centered slab has both boundary
  clearances <math|p>, so the estimate with one insertion applies with buffer
  <math|p>. The padding identities for slab operators give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:bar-padding>B<rsub|p+u><around|(|v|)>=E<rsub|u>*B<rsub|p><around|(|v|)>*E<rsub|u>*<space|2em><around|(|p\<gtr\>0,u\<geqslant\>0|)>.
  </equation>

  Both statements retain the constants of the all-copy estimate; no uniform
  bound over all labels in the union is asserted.

  For times <math|z<rsub|i>=t<rsub|i>+i*y<rsub|i>> with
  <math|y<rsub|1>\<less\>\<cdots\>\<less\>y<rsub|n>>, choose
  <math|0\<less\>p\<less\>1> with <math|4*p\<less\>y<rsub|j>-y<rsub|i>>
  whenever <math|i\<less\>j>. Write

  <\equation*>
    R<rsub|p>*<around|(|z,x|)>=E*<around|(|-i*z-2*p|)>*U<around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  Define

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:continued-matrix>F<rsub|n>*<around|(|z,x;v|)>=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|E>,B<rsub|p><around|(|v<rsub|1>|)>*R<rsub|p>*<around|(|z<rsub|2>-z<rsub|1>,x<rsub|2>-x<rsub|1>|)>*B<rsub|p><around|(|v<rsub|2>|)>*\<cdots\>*R<rsub|p>*<around|(|z<rsub|n>-z<rsub|n-1>,x<rsub|n>-x<rsub|n-1>|)>*B<rsub|p><around|(|v<rsub|n>|)>*\<Omega\><rsub|E>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation>

  For <math|n=1> the intervening factors are absent; for <math|n=0> the value
  is one. All arguments of <math|E> have positive real part.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lem:continued-matrix>The value <eqref|eq:continued-matrix> is
    independent of the admissible width <math|p>. It is multilinear in the
    labels, jointly continuous in <math|<around|(|z,x|)>>, and holomorphic in
    the complex time variables on the strictly ordered tube. At purely
    imaginary times it equals the Euclidean correlation of the insertions at
    <math|<around|(|x<rsub|i>,y<rsub|i>|)>>. For each fixed label tuple there
    are <math|C\<geqslant\>0> and <math|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that, for
    <math|0\<less\>k\<leqslant\>1>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:gap-power>y<rsub|j>-y<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>k*<around|(|i\<less\>j|)><space|1em>\<Longrightarrow\><space|1em><around|\||F<rsub|n>*<around|(|t+i*y,x;v|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*k<rsup|-m>
    </equation>

    uniformly in all real times <math|t> and spatial positions <math|x>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Compare widths <math|p> and <math|q=p+u>. For <math|u\<gtr\>0>, the
    semigroup law and spatial commutation imply

    <\equation*>
      R<rsub|p>*<around|(|z,x|)>=E<rsub|u>*R<rsub|q>*<around|(|z,x|)>*E<rsub|u>.
    </equation*>

    Substituting <eqref|eq:bar-padding> for every <math|B<rsub|q>> in the
    product at width <math|q> merges the two adjacent <math|E<rsub|u>>
    factors around each <math|R<rsub|q>> into <math|R<rsub|p>>. The exterior
    <math|E<rsub|u>> factors disappear against the invariant vacuum. This
    proves equality; <math|u=0> is immediate, and any two widths can be
    ordered. The admissibility inequalities are strict, so the same fixed
    width works on a neighborhood of every point of the tube. There the
    product is holomorphic in all time variables. It is jointly continuous in
    the spatial variables as a vector matrix element: strong continuity of
    <math|U<around|(|x|)>>, local norm continuity of the other factors, and
    their local uniform bounds prove this successively from the rightmost
    vector. No operator-norm continuity of spatial translations is required.
    Multilinearity follows from that of each <math|B<rsub|p>>.

    At imaginary times each intervening factor is the ordinary positive heat
    translation through the unused gap. Starting with the rightmost
    insertion, the slab insertion rule and the time and space translation
    rules show that the suffix train after a preceding reference
    <math|<around|(|X,Y|)>> is the Euclidean word vector with suffix
    positions <math|<around|(|x<rsub|i>-X,y<rsub|i>-Y-p|)>>. For the first
    suffix point its preceding heat duration is
    <math|y<rsub|i>-Y-2*p\<gtr\>0>: the centered slab places that insertion
    at height <math|p>, and translation through this duration places it at
    <math|y<rsub|i>-Y-p>. The remaining suffix points follow the same
    translation, proving the induction invariant. Pairing the resulting train
    with the initial centered insertion gives the complete word shifted by
    <math|<around|(|-x<rsub|1>,-y<rsub|1>+p|)>>. Pairing with the vacuum and
    using translation invariance removes this last shift and gives the
    asserted Euclidean correlation.

    All intervening factors have norm at most one. Thus the absolute value is
    at most <math|<big|prod><rsub|i><around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsub|p><around|(|v<rsub|i>|)>|\<\|\|\>>>,
    since <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Omega\><rsub|E>|\<\|\|\>>=1>. When the
    gaps are at least <math|k>, choose <math|p=k/8>.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:bar-power> gives

    <\equation*>
      m=<big|sum><rsub|i>a<rsub|v<rsub|i>>,<space|2em>C=<big|prod><rsub|i><around|(|C<rsub|v<rsub|i>>*8<rsup|a<rsub|v<rsub|i>>>|)>,
    </equation*>

    and proves <eqref|eq:gap-power>. The empty product and sum also cover
    <math|n=0>.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Differentiating tested functions>

  Fix the arity and labels, and write <math|X=<around|(|t<rsub|1>,x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,t<rsub|n>,x<rsub|n>|)>>
  for real positions. For strictly ordered heights <math|y> and a joint
  Schwartz test <math|f>, put

  <\equation*>
    \<psi\><rsub|y><around|(|f|)>=<big|int>F<rsub|n>*<around|(|t+i*y,x;v|)>*f<around|(|X|)>*<space|0.17em>d*X.
  </equation*>

  For a real height vector <math|a>, let <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>> denote
  differentiation of the real time coordinates in direction <math|a>, and put
  <math|D<rsub|a>=-i*\<partial\><rsub|a>> on tests.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lem:tested-cr>The integral defining <math|\<psi\><rsub|y>> is
    absolutely convergent, depends continuously on <math|y> in the open
    ordered cone, and satisfies

    <\equation*>
      <frac|d|d*s>*\<psi\><rsub|y+s*a><around|(|f|)>=\<psi\><rsub|y+s*a>*<around|(|D<rsub|a>*f|)>
    </equation*>

    whenever the heights remain strictly ordered.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Near any strictly ordered <math|y>, all gaps have a common positive lower
    bound. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:gap-power> therefore supplies a bound
    uniform in <math|X> and in the nearby heights. Multiplication by the
    integrable function <math|<around|\||f|\|>> proves integrability and, by
    dominated convergence, height continuity. For the derivative, restrict
    the holomorphic function to the complex line which changes all complex
    time coordinates by <math|\<zeta\>*a>. A fixed disc about zero stays in
    the tube for every real configuration <math|X> and every height in a
    sufficiently small neighborhood. The same gap bound holds throughout that
    disc. Cauchy's formula therefore bounds the first complex derivative by a
    constant independent of <math|X>. The Cauchy\URiemann equations give the
    pointwise height derivative <math|i*\<partial\><rsub|a>*F>. This uniform
    bound times <math|<around|\||f|\|>> justifies differentiation under the
    integral.

    To move the derivative onto the test, use smooth compactly supported
    cutoffs <math|\<chi\><rsub|R><around|(|X|)>=\<chi\>*<around|(|X/R|)>>
    equal to one on the radius-<math|R> ball and supported on the
    radius-<math|2*R> ball. Integration by parts for the real time direction
    gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|int>i*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*F|)>*f*\<chi\><rsub|R>=-<big|int>i*F*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*f|)>*\<chi\><rsub|R>-<big|int>i*F*f*\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\><rsub|R>.
    </equation*>

    The last integral has magnitude at most a fixed constant times
    <math|R<rsup|-1><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>>, hence
    tends to zero. The other two integrals converge by the uniform bounds for
    <math|F> and <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>*F> and the integrability of
    <math|f,\<partial\><rsub|a>*f>. This proves the identity, including its
    sign.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|An exact Taylor formula at the edge>

  Let <math|r=<around|(|0,1,\<ldots\>,n-1|)>> and <math|D=D<rsub|r>>. If
  <math|y> is only weakly ordered, then <math|y+s*r> has all distinct-index
  gaps at least <math|s> for <math|s\<gtr\>0>; for arities zero and one the
  conditions on pairs are empty. Choose <math|m,C> from <eqref|eq:gap-power>
  for the fixed labels. Define

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<wide|\<psi\>|~><rsub|y><around|(|f|)>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|m><frac|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|j>|j!>*\<psi\><rsub|y+r>*<around|(|D<rsup|j>*f|)>-<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1><frac|<around|(|-s|)><rsup|m>|m!>*\<psi\><rsub|y+s*r>*<around|(|D<rsup|m+1>*f|)>*<space|0.17em>d*s.<eq-number><label|eq:edge-taylor>>>>>
  </align>

  This formula uses values strictly inside the tube even when some heights of
  <math|y> coincide.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Joint tempered boundary><label|thm:joint-boundary>
    Formula<nbsp><eqref|eq:edge-taylor> defines a continuous linear
    functional of <math|f> on the joint Schwartz space for every weakly
    ordered <math|y>; for each fixed <math|f> the scalar
    <math|<wide|\<psi\>|~><rsub|y><around|(|f|)>> is continuous in the weakly
    ordered <math|y> (that is, <math|y\<mapsto\><wide|\<psi\>|~><rsub|y>> is
    weak-<math|\<ast\>> continuous). It agrees with
    <math|\<psi\><rsub|y><around|(|f|)>> when <math|y> is strictly ordered.
    In particular

    <\equation*>
      W<rsub|n><around|(|v|)><around|(|f|)>\<assign\><wide|\<psi\>|~><rsub|0><around|(|f|)>
    </equation*>

    is a tempered distribution and

    <\equation*>
      \<psi\><rsub|y><around|(|f|)>\<longrightarrow\>W<rsub|n><around|(|v|)><around|(|f|)><space|1em><text|as
      >y\<to\>0<text| through all strictly ordered heights>.
    </equation*>

    The limit is multilinear in the labels. No restriction on the ratios of
    height gaps is imposed.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    First take strictly ordered <math|y> and set

    <\equation*>
      P<around|(|s|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|m><frac|<around|(|-s|)><rsup|j>|j!>*\<psi\><rsub|y+s*r>*<around|(|D<rsup|j>*f|)>.
    </equation*>

    By Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:tested-cr>, differentiating the finite sum
    cancels successive terms, leaving

    <\equation*>
      P<rprime|'><around|(|s|)>=<frac|<around|(|-s|)><rsup|m>|m!>*\<psi\><rsub|y+s*r>*<around|(|D<rsup|m+1>*f|)>.
    </equation*>

    Integrating from zero to one and using
    <math|P<around|(|0|)>=\<psi\><rsub|y><around|(|f|)>> proves the asserted
    identity with <eqref|eq:edge-taylor>.

    For weakly ordered <math|y> and <math|0\<less\>s\<leqslant\>1>, the gap
    estimate gives

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<frac|<around|(|-s|)><rsup|m>|m!>*\<psi\><rsub|y+s*r>*<around|(|D<rsup|m+1>*f|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|C|m!>*<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|m+1>*f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>.
    </equation*>

    Thus the remainder is absolutely integrable uniformly in <math|y>. For
    each <math|s\<gtr\>0> its integrand is continuous in <math|y> on the
    closed ordered cone, by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:tested-cr>. Dominated
    convergence proves continuity of the remainder, and the finite sum in
    <eqref|eq:edge-taylor> is continuous for the same reason. The formula
    also gives the uniform estimate

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||<wide|\<psi\>|~><rsub|y><around|(|f|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|m><frac|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|j>*f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>|j!>+<frac|C|m!>*<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|m+1>*f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>.
    </equation*>

    These are continuous seminorms of <math|f> in the Schwartz topology.
    Explicitly, in real dimension <math|d=4*n>, each <math|L<rsup|1>> norm is
    bounded by <math|<big|int><around|(|1+<around|\||X|\|>|)><rsup|-d-1>*d*X>
    times the corresponding weighted supremum norm; <math|D<rsup|j>> is a
    finite sum of ordinary derivatives. This proves continuity as a test
    functional.

    The cone of strictly ordered heights accumulates at every weakly ordered
    height, by the path <math|y+s*r>. Hence its limits determine the
    functional uniquely. Linearity in <math|f> and multilinearity in every
    label follow by passing those identities for the interior integrals to
    the common boundary limit. This also shows that the answer is independent
    of the chosen power bound <math|m,C>, although those bounds may depend on
    the label tuple. Finally, continuity of <eqref|eq:edge-taylor> at zero
    and its agreement with the interior integrals give the full asserted
    limit.
  </proof>

  The theorem constructs boundary values for joint tests on all real
  positions, not just factorized tests and not just pointwise limits. To
  obtain the heat vectors needed for positivity, one must also identify their
  Gram matrix with these same continued functions at mixed positive and
  negative heights. That identification is the next step.

  <subsection|The first spectral support restriction><label|sec:fourier-support>

  The joint boundary theorem also controls the Fourier support of the
  continued distributions. We use the radian convention

  <\equation*>
    <wide|f|^><around|(|X|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|-i*X\<cdot\>p>*f<around|(|p|)>*<space|0.17em>d*p,<space|2em><wide|W|^><around|(|f|)>=W<around|(|<wide|f|^>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Thus the inverse test transform has factor
  <math|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-4*n>>. The dot product here is the
  Euclidean pairing of the absolute coordinates, including each real time
  coordinate; no relative-coordinate reduction has been made. For a height
  tuple <math|y>, write <math|d<around|(|y|)>=<around|(|y<rsub|1>*e<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,y<rsub|n>*e<rsub|0>|)>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Exponential testing identity><label|lem:exponential-testing> For
    strictly ordered <math|y> and every smooth compactly supported frequency
    test <math|f>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:frequency-height><wide|W|^><rsub|n><around|(|v|)><around|(|f|)>=\<psi\><rsub|y><around*|(|<wide|<space|0.17em>e<rsup|d<around|(|y|)>\<cdot\>p>*f<around|(|p|)><space|0.17em>|^>|)>.
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Set <math|f<rsub|s><around|(|p|)>=e<rsup|s*d<around|(|y|)>\<cdot\>p>*f<around|(|p|)>>
    and <math|g<rsub|s>=<wide|f|^><rsub|s>>. All <math|f<rsub|s>> have the
    same compact support. Differentiating their explicit formula, including
    all spatial derivatives, shows that <math|s\<mapsto\>f<rsub|s>> is
    differentiable in the Schwartz topology with derivative
    <math|<around|(|d<around|(|y|)>\<cdot\>p|)>*f<rsub|s>>. To see the
    topology directly, every weighted derivative is supported in that fixed
    compact set; Taylor's formula in <math|s>, uniformly on it for each
    derivative, proves convergence in each Schwartz seminorm. Continuity of
    the Fourier transform then gives

    <\equation*>
      g<rsub|s><rprime|'>=<wide|<around|(|d<around|(|y|)>\<cdot\>p|)>*f<rsub|s>|^>=-D<rsub|y>*g<rsub|s>.
    </equation*>

    The sign follows by differentiating the kernel
    <math|e<rsup|-i*X\<cdot\>p>> and using
    <math|D<rsub|y>=-i*\<partial\><rsub|y>>.

    For <math|s\<gtr\>0>, differentiate <math|\<psi\><rsub|s*y><around|(|g<rsub|s>|)>>.
    The varying-test step is legitimate with the uniform seminorm bound
    proved in Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:joint-boundary>: write a difference
    quotient as the sum of the change in the test at the new height and the
    change in height with the old test fixed. The first part converges to
    <math|\<psi\><rsub|s*y><around|(|g<rsub|s><rprime|'>|)>>, because the
    test quotient converges in Schwartz space and the family of functionals
    has a common continuous seminorm bound.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:tested-cr> handles the second part. Their sum
    is <math|\<psi\><rsub|s*y>*<around|(|-D<rsub|y>*g<rsub|s>|)>+\<psi\><rsub|s*y>*<around|(|D<rsub|y>*g<rsub|s>|)>=0>.
    The function is therefore constant on <math|<around|(|0,\<infty\>|)>>. As
    <math|s*\<downarrow\>*0>, <math|g<rsub|s>\<to\><wide|f|^>> in Schwartz
    space, and the same uniform bound plus the joint boundary limit gives
    <math|\<psi\><rsub|s*y><around|(|g<rsub|s>|)>\<to\>W<rsub|n><around|(|v|)><around|(|<wide|f|^>|)>>.
    Comparing this with its value at <math|s=1> proves the formula.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <label|prop:half-support>For every label tuple, the distribution
    <math|<wide|W|^><rsub|n><around|(|v|)>> is supported in

    <\equation*>
      H<rsub|n>=<around*|{|p:<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>y<rsub|i>*p<rsub|i><rsup|0>\<geqslant\>0<text|
      for every >y<rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<cdots\>\<leqslant\>y<rsub|n>|}>.
    </equation*>

    Equivalently,

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>p<rsub|i><rsup|0>=0,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|i=j><rsup|n>p<rsub|i><rsup|0>\<geqslant\>0*<space|1em><around|(|2\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>n|)>.
    </equation*>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Fix a weakly ordered <math|y> and a smooth compactly supported <math|f>
    whose support lies in the open set <math|d<around|(|y|)>\<cdot\>p\<less\>-1>.
    Put <math|y<around|(|t|)>=t*y+r>, where
    <math|r=<around|(|0,1,\<ldots\>,n-1|)>> and <math|t\<geqslant\>0>. These
    heights are strictly ordered, with all gaps at least one. The functions

    <\equation*>
      h<rsub|t><around|(|p|)>=e<rsup|d<around|(|y<around|(|t|)>|)>\<cdot\>p>*f<around|(|p|)>
    </equation*>

    tend to zero in Schwartz space. Indeed, on the fixed compact support
    every derivative is a finite sum of a fixed smooth coefficient times a
    power of <math|t> times <math|e<rsup|t*d<around|(|y|)>\<cdot\>p>>, and
    the latter is bounded by <math|e<rsup|-t>>. All polynomially weighted
    derivative suprema therefore tend to zero.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:frequency-height> and the gap bound at <math|k=1>
    imply

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||<wide|W|^><rsub|n><around|(|v|)><around|(|f|)>|\|>=<around|\||\<psi\><rsub|y<around|(|t|)>><around|(|<wide|h|^><rsub|t>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<around|\<\|\|\>|<wide|h|^><rsub|t>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>\<longrightarrow\>0.
    </equation*>

    Thus the distribution vanishes on that open set. If a point has
    <math|d<around|(|y|)>\<cdot\>p\<less\>0>, rescale <math|y> by a positive
    constant so that its value is less than <math|-1>. A neighborhood of the
    point then has the same strict inequality. This proves support in
    <math|H<rsub|n>>.

    To identify <math|H<rsub|n>>, the constant height tuples <math|1> and
    <math|-1> give zero total time momentum. The weakly increasing step tuple
    equal to zero before <math|j> and one from <math|j> onward gives
    nonnegativity of each suffix sum. Conversely, for any weakly ordered
    <math|y>,

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i>y<rsub|i>*p<rsub|i><rsup|0>=y<rsub|1>*<big|sum><rsub|i>p<rsub|i><rsup|0>+<big|sum><rsub|j=2><rsup|n><around|(|y<rsub|j>-y<rsub|j-1>|)>*<big|sum><rsub|i=j><rsup|n>p<rsub|i><rsup|0>\<geqslant\>0.
    </equation*>

    This proves the equivalence, with the empty and single-slot conventions
    included.
  </proof>

  <\corollary>
    <dueto|Conditional Lorentz improvement><label|cor:cone-support> If the
    boundary distributions are covariant under the label representation
    <math|\<rho\>> of the proper orthochronous Lorentz group of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:lorentz-covariance> (which preserves every
    finite-dimensional field level and is continuous there; only the
    covariance identity is used below), then the support of every
    <math|<wide|W|^><rsub|n><around|(|v|)>> lies in

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|p:<big|sum><rsub|i>p<rsub|i>=0,<space|1em><big|sum><rsub|i=j><rsup|n>p<rsub|i>\<in\><wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>*<around|(|2\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>n|)>|}>,<space|2em><wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>=<around|{|p:p<rsup|0>\<geqslant\><around|\||\<b-up-p\>|\|>|}>.
    </equation*>
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    Fourier transformation of the covariance identity changes momenta by the
    dual Lorentz action: this follows by the linear change of variables in
    the test transform, whose Jacobian has absolute value one. The
    transformed labels are again an allowed tuple, so
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:half-support> applies after every such
    dual Lorentz transformation. The inverse-transpose action ranges over the
    same group: for the Minkowski metric matrix <math|\<eta\>>,
    <math|\<Lambda\><rsup|-T>=\<eta\>*\<Lambda\>*\<eta\>>, which preserves
    the future cone, and this operation is an involution. In particular every
    transformed suffix momentum has nonnegative time coordinate.

    A real vector <math|q> has this property exactly when
    <math|q\<in\><wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>>. One implication follows from
    preservation of the future cone. For the other, if
    <math|q<rsup|0>\<less\>0> the identity transformation suffices. If
    <math|0\<leqslant\>q<rsup|0>\<less\><around|\||\<b-up-q\>|\|>>, choose a
    boost in the direction of <math|\<b-up-q\>> with speed <math|\<beta\>>
    strictly between <math|q<rsup|0>/<around|\||\<b-up-q\>|\|>> and one; the
    transformed time coordinate <math|<around|(|q<rsup|0>-\<beta\><around|\||\<b-up-q\>|\|>|)>/<sqrt|1-\<beta\><rsup|2>>>
    is negative, a contradiction. This proves the suffix assertion. The total
    momentum has zero time coordinate in every frame. The identity frame
    makes its time coordinate zero, and the same boost argument forces its
    spatial part to vanish.
  </proof>

  The Lorentz covariance hypothesis in this last corollary is still to be
  proved from the infinitesimal Euclidean rotation identities. The conclusion
  here is support of the distributions in momentum variables; constructing
  the projection-valued momentum measure of the Hilbert-space representation
  is a separate step.

  <subsection|Compatibility of the Lorentz action across field
  levels><label|sec:label-descent>

  The continuation argument uses one Lorentz action on the union of the field
  spaces. Constructing an action separately at every finite level would not
  be enough: the changes of basis used to compare with the free theory need
  not commute with the inclusions. The compatibility is instead proved from
  the Euclidean action and the infinitesimal boost relation.

  Write <math|E<rsub|d>> for the interacting finite-dimensional complex field
  space and <math|\<iota\><rsub|d*e>:E<rsub|d>\<to\>E<rsub|e>>,
  <math|d\<leqslant\>e>, for its injective inclusions. Let
  <math|E<rsub|d><rsup|0>> be the corresponding reference spaces. The
  following input isolates the finite-level comparison produced by the
  calibration argument.

  <\assumption>
    <dueto|Calibrated finite-level actions><label|input:calibrated-actions>
    For each <math|d> there is a complex-linear isomorphism
    <math|Q<rsub|d>:E<rsub|d><rsup|0>\<to\>E<rsub|d>>. The reference spaces
    carry continuous representations <math|R<rsub|d><rsup|0>> of
    <math|O<around|(|4|)>> and <math|T<rsub|d><rsup|0>> of the proper
    orthochronous Lorentz group. For each spatial coordinate <math|a>, their
    generators satisfy

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d|d*t>*T<rsub|d><rsup|0><around|(|B<rsub|a><around|(|t|)>|)>|\|><rsub|t=0>=-i*L<rsub|d,a><rsup|0>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|L<rsub|d,a><rsup|0>> is the generator of the Euclidean
    time--<math|a> plane rotation, with the conventions of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:lorentz-covariance>. On spatial rotations
    the two reference actions agree. Define
    <math|R<rsub|d><around|(|g|)>=Q<rsub|d>*R<rsub|d><rsup|0><around|(|g|)>*Q<rsub|d><rsup|-1>>
    and let <math|L<rsub|d,a>> be its indicated Euclidean generator. These
    actions satisfy

    <\equation*>
      R<rsub|e><around|(|g|)>*\<iota\><rsub|d*e>=\<iota\><rsub|d*e>*R<rsub|d><around|(|g|)>,<space|2em>L<rsub|d,a>*Q<rsub|d>=Q<rsub|d>*L<rsub|d,a><rsup|0>.
    </equation*>

    There are antilinear involutions <math|J<rsub|d>,J<rsub|d><rsup|0>>,
    compatible with inclusions, such that
    <math|J<rsub|d>*Q<rsub|d>=Q<rsub|d>*J<rsub|d><rsup|0>> and
    <math|J<rsub|d><rsup|0>> commutes with <math|T<rsub|d><rsup|0>>. The
    inclusions form a directed system and the field union is its algebraic
    direct limit.
  </assumption>

  The comparison maps <math|Q<rsub|d>> are the same maps that define
  <math|R<rsub|d>>; a fresh choice at the Lorentz stage would not justify the
  displayed generator identity. Production of these maps and the reference
  actions is separate from the compatibility proof below.

  <\proposition>
    <label|prop:lorentz-label-descent>Under
    Input<nbsp><reference|input:calibrated-actions>, the finite-level
    representations

    <\equation*>
      T<rsub|d><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>=Q<rsub|d>*T<rsub|d><rsup|0><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>*Q<rsub|d><rsup|-1>
    </equation*>

    commute with all inclusions. They therefore induce one representation
    <math|T> on the field union, continuous on each finite level. Its spatial
    action is the Euclidean one, its boost generator on <math|E<rsub|d>> is
    <math|-i*L<rsub|d,a>>, and it commutes with the induced involution
    <math|J>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Conjugation immediately gives the representation laws and continuity on
    each level. Differentiation at zero, using the fixed linear map
    <math|Q<rsub|d>>, gives the boost generator
    <math|K<rsub|d,a>=-i*L<rsub|d,a>>. Differentiating the Euclidean
    inclusion law along its plane rotation gives

    <\equation*>
      L<rsub|e,a>*\<iota\><rsub|d*e>=\<iota\><rsub|d*e>*L<rsub|d,a>,<space|2em>K<rsub|e,a>*\<iota\><rsub|d*e>=\<iota\><rsub|d*e>*K<rsub|d,a>.
    </equation*>

    For <math|v\<in\>E<rsub|d>>, the two curves

    <\equation*>
      f<around|(|t|)>=T<rsub|e><around|(|B<rsub|a><around|(|t|)>|)>*\<iota\><rsub|d*e>*v,<space|2em>g<around|(|t|)>=\<iota\><rsub|d*e>*T<rsub|d><around|(|B<rsub|a><around|(|t|)>|)>*v
    </equation*>

    have the same initial value and solve <math|h<rprime|'>=K<rsub|e,a>*h>.
    The derivative at arbitrary <math|t> follows from the group law by
    translating the parameter in the derivative at zero; for <math|g> use the
    generator intertwining just proved. Uniqueness of this
    constant-coefficient equation can be seen directly: the derivative of
    <math|e<rsup|-t*K<rsub|e,a>>*<around|(|f<around|(|t|)>-g<around|(|t|)>|)>>
    is zero, and its value at zero is zero. Hence the inclusion commutes with
    every coordinate boost.

    It commutes with each spatial rotation because the transported spatial
    action is <math|R<rsub|d>> and the Euclidean inclusion law already holds.
    For fixed <math|d,e>, the transformations commuting with
    <math|\<iota\><rsub|d*e>> form a subgroup. Products follow by two
    substitutions. For inverses, if <math|T<rsub|e><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>*\<iota\><rsub|d*e>=\<iota\><rsub|d*e>*T<rsub|d><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>>,
    multiply on the left and right by the respective inverse transformations.
    Spatial rotations and coordinate boosts generate the full proper
    orthochronous group, by the explicit reduction of
    <math|\<Lambda\>*e<rsub|0>> in the proof of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:lorentz-covariance>. Thus every
    transformation commutes with the inclusions. Notice that this argument
    has not asserted any compatibility of the maps <math|Q<rsub|d>>
    themselves.

    Define the action on a union element represented by
    <math|v\<in\>E<rsub|d>> to be represented by
    <math|T<rsub|d><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>*v>. If another representative lies
    in <math|E<rsub|e>>, pass to a common higher level; the inclusion
    compatibility proves the same resulting class. This proves
    well-definedness. Linearity and the group laws hold after putting
    finitely many representatives in a common level, and the inverse action
    is that of <math|\<Lambda\><rsup|-1>>. The asserted spatial and
    derivative identities follow in the same way. Finally

    <\equation*>
      J<rsub|d>*T<rsub|d><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>*Q<rsub|d>*v=Q<rsub|d>*J<rsub|d><rsup|0>*T<rsub|d><rsup|0><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>*v=Q<rsub|d>*T<rsub|d><rsup|0><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>*J<rsub|d><rsup|0>*v=T<rsub|d><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>*J<rsub|d>*Q<rsub|d>*v.
    </equation*>

    Surjectivity of <math|Q<rsub|d>> and compatibility of the involutions
    prove commutation with <math|J> on the union.
  </proof>

  <subsection|From Euclidean rotations to Lorentz
  covariance><label|sec:lorentz-covariance>

  This section proves covariance of the boundary distributions from the
  Euclidean rotation identities, retaining the same field labels. The
  finite-dimensional Lorentz action on those labels must first be available.
  We state its required compatibility explicitly rather than identify it with
  an arbitrary representation having the same dimension.

  On each finite level let <math|R> be the Euclidean rotation representation,
  and write <math|L<rsub|a>> for its infinitesimal generator in the plane
  consisting of time and spatial coordinate <math|a>. Our convention for the
  coordinate generator is

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-R\><rsub|a>=<big|sum><rsub|i><around*|(|x<rsub|i><rsup|a>*\<partial\><rsub|y<rsub|i>>-y<rsub|i>*\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|i><rsup|a>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Assume <math|R> acts compatibly under the inclusions of field levels, and
  the smooth Euclidean correlation <math|S*<around|(|v;X|)>> obeys
  simultaneous rotation covariance. Differentiating it gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:euclid-infinitesimal>\<cal-R\><rsub|a>*S*<around|(|v;X|)>=-<big|sum><rsub|i>S*<around|(|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,L<rsub|a>*v<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|n>;X|)>.
  </equation>

  Indeed, covariance says <math|S*<around|(|v;R<rsub|t>*X|)>=S*<around|(|R<rsub|-t>*v;X|)>>;
  the ordinary chain rule on positions and the finite multilinear expansion
  on labels give the displayed formula. This is applied only at distinct
  Euclidean positions.

  Suppose also that the proper orthochronous Lorentz group has a
  representation <math|\<rho\>> on the field union preserving every finite
  level, continuous there, compatible with the inclusions, and agreeing with
  <math|R> on spatial rotations. For the coordinate boost
  <math|B<rsub|a><around|(|s|)>>, with coordinate infinitesimal generator
  <math|x<rsup|a>*\<partial\><rsub|t>+t*\<partial\><rsub|x<rsup|a>>>, require

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:label-boost-input><around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d|d*s>*\<rho\><around|(|B<rsub|a><around|(|s|)>|)>*v|\|><rsub|s=0>=-i*L<rsub|a>*v.
  </equation>

  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:lorentz-label-descent> supplies this
  action from the calibrated finite-level actions. Their construction remains
  an explicit earlier input. Set <math|K<rsub|a>=-i*L<rsub|a>>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The tested boost identity><label|prop:boost-ward> For strictly
    ordered <math|y> and any joint Schwartz test <math|f>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:boost-interior><big|sum><rsub|i>\<psi\><rsub|y>*<around|(|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,K<rsub|a>*v<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|n>;f|)>=\<psi\><rsub|y>*<around*|(|v;<big|sum><rsub|i><around|[|x<rsub|i><rsup|a>*\<partial\><rsub|t<rsub|i>>+<around|(|t<rsub|i>+i*y<rsub|i>|)>*\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|i><rsup|a>>|]>*f|)>.
    </equation>

    Consequently the boundary distributions satisfy

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:boost-boundary><big|sum><rsub|i>W<rsub|n>*<around|(|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,K<rsub|a>*v<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|n>|)><around|(|f|)>=W<rsub|n><around|(|v|)>*<around|(|\<cal-B\><rsub|a>*f|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-B\><rsub|a>=<big|sum><rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i><rsup|a>*\<partial\><rsub|t<rsub|i>>+t<rsub|i>*\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|i><rsup|a>>|)>.
    </equation>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    First suppose <math|f> has compact support <math|C>. For a complex
    parameter <math|c> put <math|z<rsub|i><around|(|c|)>=i*y<rsub|i>+c*t<rsub|i>>
    and

    <\equation*>
      F<rsub|c>*<around|(|v;X|)>=F<rsub|n>*<around|(|z<around|(|c|)>,x;v|)>,<space|2em>T<rsub|c>*f=<big|sum><rsub|i><around|[|x<rsub|i><rsup|a>*\<partial\><rsub|t<rsub|i>>+c*z<rsub|i><around|(|c|)>*\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|i><rsup|a>>|]>*f.
    </equation*>

    Let <math|D> consist of parameters for which <math|z<around|(|c|)>> has
    strictly ordered imaginary parts for every <math|X\<in\>C>. This is an
    open convex set containing the real axis: its constraints are affine
    strict inequalities in <math|<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>c>, and
    compactness of <math|C> makes their margins uniform near each admissible
    parameter. Consider the integral

    <\equation*>
      H<around|(|c|)>=<big|int><rsub|C><around*|[|F<rsub|c>*<around|(|v;X|)>*T<rsub|c>*f<around|(|X|)>-c*<big|sum><rsub|i>F<rsub|c>*<around|(|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,K<rsub|a>*v<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|n>;X|)>*f<around|(|X|)>|]>*d*X.
    </equation*>

    It is holomorphic on <math|D>. In fact the integrand is jointly
    continuous on <math|D\<times\>C> and holomorphic in <math|c> for every
    <math|X>; on each compact subdisc, Cauchy's formula gives uniformly
    bounded derivatives, so differentiation under this finite-volume integral
    is justified.

    At <math|c=i*\<sigma\>>, put <math|Y<rsub|i>=y<rsub|i>+\<sigma\>*t<rsub|i>>.
    All these heights are strictly ordered on a neighborhood of <math|C>, and
    <math|F<rsub|i*\<sigma\>>*<around|(|v;X|)>=S*<around|(|v;<around|(|x,Y|)>|)>>.
    The real vector field

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|\<sigma\>>=<big|sum><rsub|i><around*|[|x<rsub|i><rsup|a>*\<partial\><rsub|t<rsub|i>>-\<sigma\>*<around|(|y<rsub|i>+\<sigma\>*t<rsub|i>|)>*\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|i><rsup|a>>|]>
    </equation*>

    is divergence free. Under the map <math|<around|(|t,x|)>\<mapsto\><around|(|Y,x|)>>
    it becomes <math|\<sigma\>*\<cal-R\><rsub|a>>. Thus
    <eqref|eq:euclid-infinitesimal> gives

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|\<sigma\>>*F<rsub|i*\<sigma\>>*<around|(|v;X|)>=-\<sigma\>*<big|sum><rsub|i>S*<around|(|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,L<rsub|a>*v<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|n>;<around|(|x,Y|)>|)>=-i*\<sigma\>*<big|sum><rsub|i>F<rsub|i*\<sigma\>>*<around|(|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,K<rsub|a>*v<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|n>;X|)>.
    </equation*>

    Also <math|T<rsub|i*\<sigma\>>*f=A<rsub|\<sigma\>>*f>. The integrand of
    <math|H*<around|(|i*\<sigma\>|)>> is therefore
    <math|A<rsub|\<sigma\>>*<around|(|F<rsub|i*\<sigma\>>*<around|(|v;X|)>*f<around|(|X|)>|)>>,
    whose integral is zero. The product is smooth near <math|C> and zero off
    <math|C>, so its extension by zero is a smooth compactly supported
    function; no regularity of the continued function at positions outside
    that neighborhood is being assumed. The identity theorem on the connected
    set <math|D> now gives <math|H<around|(|1|)>=0>, which is
    <eqref|eq:boost-interior> for compactly supported tests.

    To pass to Schwartz tests, choose a smooth cutoff
    <math|\<chi\>*<around|(|X/R|)>> equal to one near zero. Then
    <math|\<chi\>*<around|(|X/R|)>*f\<to\>f> in every Schwartz seminorm: on
    the outside region use one extra polynomial decay weight, and for each
    positive-order derivative of the cutoff use its factor <math|R<rsup|-1>>
    or a higher inverse power and the same tail estimate. At fixed strictly
    ordered <math|y>, every <math|\<psi\><rsub|y>> is continuous on Schwartz
    space, and all coordinate multiplications and derivatives in
    <eqref|eq:boost-interior> are continuous there. The compact-test identity
    consequently extends to <math|f>.

    Finally take the full ordered-height limit <math|y\<to\>0>. The test on
    the right is <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|a>*f+<big|sum><rsub|i>i*y<rsub|i>*\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|i><rsup|a>>*f>.
    Each fixed-test pairing <math|\<psi\><rsub|y>*<around|(|v;\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|i><rsup|a>>*f|)>>
    has a finite boundary limit, so multiplication by <math|y<rsub|i>> makes
    the correction tend to zero. All other terms have their specified joint
    boundary limits, proving <eqref|eq:boost-boundary>.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Affine pullback of tests><label|lem:schwartz-motion> The action
    <math|P<rsub|g>*f<around|(|X|)>=f*<around|(|g<rsup|-1>*X|)>> of the
    Poincaré group, acting on all positions at once, is continuous in the
    Schwartz topology. More generally, for a fixed Schwartz test <math|f> on
    the joint position space, the map <math|A\<mapsto\>f\<circ\>A<rsup|-1>>
    is continuous from the group of invertible affine maps of that space (an
    open subset of a finite-dimensional real vector space) into the Schwartz
    space; hence so is <math|g\<mapsto\>f\<circ\>A<rsub|g><rsup|-1>> for
    every continuous map <math|g\<mapsto\>A<rsub|g>> into that group. For
    coordinate boosts,

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d|d*s>*P<rsub|B<rsub|a><around|(|s|)>>*f|\|><rsub|s=0>=-\<cal-B\><rsub|a>*f
    </equation*>

    in that topology.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    We prove continuity in the general form; the Poincaré action is the case
    <math|A<rsub|g>=g> acting diagonally on all positions. On a compact
    neighborhood of a fixed invertible affine map, its linear part and
    inverse have uniformly bounded norms and its translation is bounded.
    Hence <math|1+<around|\||X|\|>> and <math|1+<around|\||g<rsup|-1>*X|\|>>
    are comparable uniformly there. The chain rule expresses every derivative
    of <math|f\<circ\>g<rsup|-1>> as a finite sum of derivatives of <math|f>
    at <math|g<rsup|-1>*X> times products of matrix entries. Each weighted
    supremum seminorm is therefore bounded by finitely many Schwartz
    seminorms of <math|f>, uniformly on that neighborhood.

    For continuity, split the supremum into a fixed ball and its complement.
    On the ball, uniform continuity of each derivative and continuous
    variation of the matrix coefficients give convergence. On the complement,
    the comparison of weights and one extra decay seminorm make the tail
    uniformly small. This proves continuity in every seminorm. For the boost
    derivative, the pointwise chain rule at zero gives
    <math|-\<cal-B\><rsub|a>*f>. To justify the same derivative in Schwartz
    space, use Taylor's formula along the smooth curve of invertible matrices
    <math|B<rsub|a>*<around|(|-s|)>>. Its second derivative acts on <math|f>
    as a finite sum of first and second derivatives composed with
    <math|B<rsub|a>*<around|(|-s|)>>, multiplied by coefficients polynomial
    in <math|X> of degree at most two. The preceding weight comparison bounds
    every seminorm of this second derivative uniformly for <math|s> in a
    small compact interval. The integral Taylor remainder is therefore
    <math|O<around|(|s<rsup|2>|)>> in each seminorm, proving the stated
    derivative.
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|thm:lorentz-covariance>Under the Euclidean covariance and
    compatible label-action hypotheses above, the boundary distributions
    satisfy

    <\equation*>
      W<rsub|n>*<around|(|\<rho\><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>*v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,\<rho\><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>*v<rsub|n>|)>*<around|(|P<rsub|\<Lambda\>>*f|)>=W<rsub|n><around|(|v|)><around|(|f|)>
    </equation*>

    for every proper orthochronous Lorentz transformation <math|\<Lambda\>>.
    They are also invariant under common spacetime translations.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    For a fixed coordinate boost set

    <\equation*>
      A<around|(|s|)>=W<rsub|n>*<around|(|\<rho\><around|(|B<rsub|a><around|(|s|)>|)>*v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,\<rho\><around|(|B<rsub|a><around|(|s|)>|)>*v<rsub|n>|)>*<around|(|P<rsub|B<rsub|a><around|(|s|)>>*f|)>.
    </equation*>

    Put the finitely many labels in one common finite level. Expanding them
    in a finite basis expresses <math|A<around|(|s|)>> as a finite sum of
    smooth scalar coefficients times fixed-label distributions applied to the
    moving test. The label coefficients are smooth along the boost: its group
    law and the derivative at zero give the linear differential equation
    <math|\<rho\><around|(|B<rsub|a><around|(|s|)>|)><rprime|'>=\<rho\><around|(|B<rsub|a><around|(|s|)>|)>*K<rsub|a>>
    on each finite level. Thus Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:schwartz-motion> and
    <eqref|eq:label-boost-input> justify the ordinary product rule at zero.
    Its label terms are the left side of <eqref|eq:boost-boundary>, and its
    test term is the negative of the right side. Hence
    <math|A<rprime|'><around|(|0|)>=0>. The representation laws for labels
    and pullbacks give the same calculation at any <math|s>, using the labels
    and test already moved by <math|B<rsub|a><around|(|s|)>>. Therefore
    <math|A<rprime|'><around|(|s|)>=0> everywhere and
    <math|A<around|(|s|)>=A<around|(|0|)>>.

    Spatial rotations and common spatial translations are inherited by the
    strict-time continuation from their Euclidean identities. To check this
    without an assumption of holomorphy in spatial coordinates, fix all real
    positions, compare the two scalar continued expressions, and replace each
    time by <math|i*y<rsub|i>+c*t<rsub|i>>. Both sides are holomorphic on an
    open strip about the real axis and agree for <math|c=i*\<sigma\>> near
    zero by Euclidean covariance. The identity theorem gives equality at
    <math|c=1>. For a common real-time translation <math|b>, compare times
    <math|i*y<rsub|i>+c*<around|(|t<rsub|i>+b|)>> on one side with
    <math|i*y<rsub|i>+c*t<rsub|i>> on the other. Their gaps agree, and at
    <math|c=i*\<sigma\>> they differ by the common Euclidean time translation
    <math|\<sigma\>*b>. The same proof applies. Changing variables in the
    joint test integrals, with unit absolute Jacobian, and taking boundary
    limits gives spatial-rotation covariance and translation invariance of
    <math|W>.

    Finally these spatial rotations and coordinate boosts generate the proper
    orthochronous Lorentz group. Here is a direct verification. Given
    <math|\<Lambda\>>, its image <math|q=\<Lambda\>*e<rsub|0>> is future
    timelike with Minkowski norm squared one. A boost in coordinate <math|a>
    with rapidity <math|-<math-up|artanh><around|(|q<rsup|a>/q<rsup|0>|)>>
    makes that spatial component zero; <math|<around|\||q<rsup|a>|\|>\<less\>q<rsup|0>>
    ensures the parameter exists. It leaves the other spatial components
    unchanged. Three such boosts produce a vector with zero spatial part and
    positive time component, necessarily <math|e<rsub|0>>. Their product
    <math|B> therefore makes <math|B*\<Lambda\>> fix <math|e<rsub|0>>.
    Preservation of the Minkowski form then shows that <math|B*\<Lambda\>>
    preserves its spatial orthogonal complement and is orthogonal on it;
    determinant one makes its spatial part a rotation. Thus <math|\<Lambda\>>
    is a product of coordinate boosts and a spatial rotation. Covariance is
    closed under products and inverses by the representation laws, proving
    the assertion for every <math|\<Lambda\>>.
  </proof>

  Together with Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:cone-support>, this gives the
  full forward suffix-cone support of the boundary distributions, conditional
  only on the specified compatible Lorentz action and the previously stated
  Euclidean construction inputs. It does not yet prove spacelike
  commutativity.

  <subsection|Continuation to the full spacetime tube><label|sec:full-tube>

  Hall and Wightman <cite-detail|HallWightman57|parts I--III> use tube
  analyticity and Lorentz invariance to continue scalar vacuum correlations.
  Our starting point is the continuation in the time variables of the
  Euclidean tables. The Fourier\ULaplace construction below supplies the full
  tube for the present field multiplets and controls its tested boundary
  limits.

  The ordered complex-time functions constructed earlier are holomorphic in
  the time variables. Locality needs a larger domain: the spatial variables
  must also become complex. We construct this continuation from the spectral
  support of the boundary distributions. A smooth cutoff near that support
  makes the Fourier\ULaplace expression an actual Schwartz test. Keeping the
  cutoff also permits limits in which some imaginary gaps lie on the boundary
  of the forward cone.

  Fix <math|n> and set <math|D=4*n>. All coordinates in this section are
  absolute coordinates in <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|D>>, rather than relative
  coordinates. Write

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|K<rsub|n>>|<cell|=<around*|{|p:<big|sum><rsub|i>p<rsub|i>=0,<space|1em><big|sum><rsub|i=j><rsup|n>p<rsub|i>\<in\><wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>*<around|(|2\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>n|)>|}>,>>|<row|<cell|C<rsub|n>>|<cell|=<around|{|y:y<rsub|i+1>-y<rsub|i>\<in\><wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>*<around|(|1\<leqslant\>i\<less\>n|)>|}>,>>|<row|<cell|C<rsub|n><rsup|\<circ\>>>|<cell|=<around|{|y:y<rsub|i+1>-y<rsub|i>\<in\>V<rsub|+>*<around|(|1\<leqslant\>i\<less\>n|)>|}>,<space|2em>V<rsub|+>=<around|{|q:q<rsup|0>\<gtr\><around|\||\<b-up-q\>|\|>|}>.>>>>
  </align*>

  The superscript on <math|C<rsub|n><rsup|\<circ\>>> denotes the strict-gap
  set, which is open in the full space. For <math|n=0> or <math|1> the gap
  conditions are vacuous. The tube is <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|n>=\<bbb-R\><rsup|D>+i*C<rsub|n><rsup|\<circ\>>>.
  By Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:cone-support> and
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:lorentz-covariance>, the distribution
  <math|<wide|W|^><rsub|n><around|(|v|)>> has support in <math|K<rsub|n>>,
  under the hypotheses stated there.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Dual positivity and a strict margin><label|lem:tube-margin> If
    <math|p\<in\>K<rsub|n>> and <math|y\<in\>C<rsub|n>>, then
    <math|p\<cdot\>y\<geqslant\>0>. For each
    <math|y\<in\>C<rsub|n><rsup|\<circ\>>> there are <math|c\<gtr\>0> and a
    neighborhood <math|N> of <math|y> such that

    <\equation*>
      p\<cdot\>u\<geqslant\>c<around|\||p|\|><space|2em><around|(|p\<in\>K<rsub|n>,u\<in\>N|)>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Put <math|q<rsub|j>=<big|sum><rsub|i=j><rsup|n>p<rsub|i>>. The zero total
    momentum and telescoping give

    <\equation*>
      p\<cdot\>y=<big|sum><rsub|j=2><rsup|n>q<rsub|j>\<cdot\><around|(|y<rsub|j>-y<rsub|j-1>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The Euclidean pairing of two forward-cone vectors is nonnegative:
    <math|a\<cdot\>b=a<rsup|0>*b<rsup|0>+\<b-up-a\>\<cdot\>\<b-up-b\>\<geqslant\>a<rsup|0>*b<rsup|0>-<around|\||\<b-up-a\>|\|><around|\||\<b-up-b\>|\|>\<geqslant\>0>.
    This proves the first assertion. Choose <math|r\<gtr\>0> with
    <math|B*<around|(|y,r|)>\<subseteq\>C<rsub|n><rsup|\<circ\>>>, and put
    <math|c=r/2>. For <math|u\<in\>B*<around|(|y,r/2|)>> and <math|p\<ne\>0>,
    the point <math|u-c*p/<around|\||p|\|>> belongs to
    <math|B*<around|(|y,r|)>> and hence to <math|C<rsub|n>>. Applying the
    first assertion to this point gives <math|p\<cdot\>u-c<around|\||p|\|>\<geqslant\>0>.
    The case <math|p=0> is immediate. This argument also covers the vacuous
    gap cases, where <math|K<rsub|n>=<around|{|0|}>>.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|A smooth cutoff and closed imaginary shifts>

  Let <math|K<rsub|n><rsup|<around|(|a|)>>=<around|{|p:<math-up|dist><around|(|p,K<rsub|n>|)>\<less\>a|}>>.
  Choose a nonnegative smooth bump <math|\<rho\>>, supported in
  <math|B*<around|(|0,1/2|)>>, with integral one, and define

  <\equation*>
    \<chi\>=\<rho\>\<ast\>\<b-1\><rsub|K<rsub|n><rsup|<around|(|2|)>>>.
  </equation*>

  Then <math|0\<leqslant\>\<chi\>\<leqslant\>1>, <math|\<chi\>=1> on
  <math|K<rsub|n><rsup|<around|(|1|)>>>, and
  <math|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<chi\>\<subseteq\>K<rsub|n><rsup|<around|(|3|)>>>,
  with support understood as closed support. Indeed, for
  <math|p\<in\>K<rsub|n><rsup|<around|(|1|)>>> every point <math|p-q> with
  <math|q\<in\><math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<rho\>> has distance less than
  <math|3/2> from <math|K<rsub|n>>; the convolution therefore integrates the
  constant one. Nonzero values require distance less than <math|5/2> from
  <math|K<rsub|n>>, and their closure still has distance at most
  <math|5/2\<less\>3>. Every derivative is globally bounded, since

  <\equation*>
    \<partial\><rsup|\<alpha\>>*\<chi\>=<around|(|\<partial\><rsup|\<alpha\>>*\<rho\>|)>\<ast\>\<b-1\><rsub|K<rsub|n><rsup|<around|(|2|)>>>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|\<partial\><rsup|\<alpha\>>*\<chi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|\<partial\><rsup|\<alpha\>>*\<rho\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  Differentiating the compactly supported convolution proves smoothness:
  difference quotients of the smooth factor converge in <math|L<rsup|1>>, so
  their convolutions with the bounded indicator converge uniformly. In
  dimension zero simply take <math|\<chi\>=1>.

  For <math|y\<in\>C<rsub|n>> set <math|a<rsub|y><around|(|p|)>=e<rsup|-y\<cdot\>p>*\<chi\><around|(|p|)>>.
  This is a smooth multiplier of Schwartz space. More precisely, for every
  <math|R> and multi-index <math|\<alpha\>> there is a finite constant
  <math|A<rsub|R,\<alpha\>>> such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:closed-damper-bound>sup<rsub|p><around|\||\<partial\><rsup|\<alpha\>>*a<rsub|y><around|(|p|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|R,\<alpha\>>*<space|2em><around|(|y\<in\>C<rsub|n>,<around|\||y|\|>\<leqslant\>R|)>.
  </equation>

  On the closed support of <math|\<chi\>> choose <math|q\<in\>K<rsub|n>> with
  <math|<around|\||p-q|\|>\<less\>3>. Dual positivity gives
  <math|-y\<cdot\>p\<leqslant\>3<around|\||y|\|>>. Each derivative of the
  exponential adds a product of components of <math|y>, while derivatives of
  <math|\<chi\>> are bounded. Leibniz's rule proves
  <eqref|eq:closed-damper-bound>; outside that support all derivatives of
  <math|a<rsub|y>> vanish.

  For each <math|f\<in\><with|math-font|cal*|S>>, the map
  <math|y\<mapsto\>a<rsub|y>*f> is continuous from <math|C<rsub|n>> to
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|S>>. On a compact frequency ball this follows
  uniformly for every derivative from the explicit smooth formula. Outside a
  large ball, the uniform derivative bounds and Leibniz's rule bound a
  seminorm of weight <math|k> by a constant times
  <math|<around|(|1+<around|\||p|\|>|)><rsup|-1>> times finitely many
  seminorms of <math|f> of weight <math|k+1>. The tail is therefore uniformly
  small. Combining the compact and tail estimates proves convergence in every
  Schwartz seminorm, including at closed-cone boundary shifts.

  For a fixed label tuple abbreviate <math|w=<wide|W|^><rsub|n><around|(|v|)>>
  and define a tempered distribution <math|W<rsub|y>> by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:partial-damping>W<rsub|y><around|(|f|)>=w*<around|(|a<rsub|y>*<wide|f|\<check\>>|)>,<space|2em><wide|f|\<check\>><around|(|p|)>=<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-D>*<big|int>e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\>x>*f<around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x.
  </equation>

  The preceding continuity shows that <math|W<rsub|y><around|(|f|)>> is
  continuous in <math|y\<in\>C<rsub|n>>. Moreover
  <math|W<rsub|0>=W<rsub|n><around|(|v|)>>. To see this, multiplication by
  <math|\<chi\>> leaves <math|w> unchanged because <math|\<chi\>=1> on a
  neighborhood of its support. The support assertion applies to Schwartz
  tests as well as compact tests: multiply a test vanishing near the support
  by compact cutoffs, apply distributional support, and pass to the Schwartz
  limit. Fourier inversion now gives <math|w<around|(|<wide|f|\<check\>>|)>=W<rsub|n><around|(|v|)><around|(|f|)>>.

  <subsubsection|The holomorphic Fourier\ULaplace expression>

  For <math|z=x+i*y\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|n>> define

  <\equation*>
    L<rsub|z><around|(|p|)>=e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\>z>*\<chi\><around|(|p|)>,<space|2em>H<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;z|)>=<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-D>*w<around|(|L<rsub|z>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|p\<cdot\>z> is the complex bilinear extension of the Euclidean
  pairing, with no conjugation. The test <math|L<rsub|z>> is in
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|S>>. In fact, the margin lemma at <math|y> and
  the distance-three support property give, on
  <math|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<chi\>>,

  <\equation*>
    -y\<cdot\>p\<leqslant\>3*<around|(|<around|\||y|\|>+c|)>-c<around|\||p|\|>.
  </equation*>

  To obtain this, choose <math|q\<in\>K<rsub|n>> with
  <math|<around|\||p-q|\|>\<less\>3>, use
  <math|y\<cdot\>q\<geqslant\>c<around|\||q|\|>>, and
  <math|<around|\||q|\|>\<geqslant\><around|\||p|\|>-3>. Frequency
  derivatives of <math|L<rsub|z>> only introduce bounded derivatives of
  <math|\<chi\>> and powers of the fixed vector <math|z>. They consequently
  have exponential decay. The same estimates hold in a sufficiently small
  neighborhood of <math|z>, with a common positive decay rate.

  <\proposition>
    <label|prop:full-tube-holomorphic>The map <math|z\<mapsto\>L<rsub|z>> is
    complex differentiable into Schwartz space, with derivative
    <math|h\<mapsto\>i*<around|(|p\<cdot\>h|)>*L<rsub|z><around|(|p|)>>.
    Consequently <math|H<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;z|)>> is holomorphic jointly in
    all <math|D> complex coordinates. For each fixed
    <math|y\<in\>C<rsub|n><rsup|\<circ\>>> it grows at most polynomially in
    <math|x>, and for every <math|f\<in\><with|math-font|cal*|S>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:tube-integral>W<rsub|y><around|(|f|)>=<big|int>H<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;x+i*y|)>*f<around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x,
    </equation>

    with an absolutely convergent integral.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The difference from the proposed derivative is

    <\equation*>
      L<rsub|z+h>-L<rsub|z>-i*<around|(|p\<cdot\>h|)>*L<rsub|z>=L<rsub|z>*<around*|(|e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\>h>-1-i*p\<cdot\>h|)>.
    </equation*>

    For <math|<around|\||h|\|>\<leqslant\>1>, every fixed-order frequency
    derivative of the factor in parentheses has absolute value at most
    <math|C<rsub|\<alpha\>><around|\||h|\|><rsup|2>*<around|(|1+<around|\||p|\|>|)><rsup|2>*e<rsup|<around|\||h|\|><around|\||p|\|>>>.
    At derivative order zero use <math|<around|\||e<rsup|u>-1-u|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||u|\|><rsup|2>*e<rsup|<around|\||u|\|>>>;
    at order one use <math|<around|\||e<rsup|u>-1|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||u|\|>*e<rsup|<around|\||u|\|>>>;
    at higher orders differentiate the exponential, which contributes at
    least two factors of <math|h>. These inequalities follow directly from
    its power series. Choose <math|<around|\||h|\|>\<less\>c/2>. Leibniz's
    rule and the exponential decay of <math|L<rsub|z>> show that the
    remainder is <math|O<around|(|<around|\||h|\|><rsup|2>|)>> in each
    Schwartz seminorm. The derivative map is continuous from the
    finite-dimensional parameter space into <math|<with|math-font|cal*|S>>,
    since multiplication by each frequency coordinate is continuous.
    Composition with the continuous linear functional <math|w> proves complex
    differentiability of <math|H<rsub|n>>; in finite complex dimension this
    gives holomorphy.

    For fixed <math|y>, put <math|g<around|(|p|)>=a<rsub|y><around|(|p|)>=L<rsub|i*y><around|(|p|)>>.
    Modulation becomes translation under Fourier transformation:

    <\equation*>
      <wide|L<rsub|x+i*y>|^><around|(|u|)>=<wide|g|^>*<around|(|u-x|)>.
    </equation*>

    For <math|s<rsub|k,j><around|(|h|)>=sup<rsub|u><around|\||u|\|><rsup|k>*<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|j>*h<around|(|u|)>|\<\|\|\>>>,
    the triangle inequality gives the explicit translation bound

    <\equation*>
      s<rsub|k,j><around|(|h|(><space|0.17em>\<cdot\>-x))\<leqslant\>2<rsup|k>*<around*|(|s<rsub|k,j><around|(|h|)>+<around|\||x|\|><rsup|k>*s<rsub|0,j><around|(|h|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    A continuous linear functional on Schwartz space is bounded by finitely
    many such seminorms. Fourier inversion and the displayed bound therefore
    bound <math|<around|\||w<around|(|L<rsub|x+i*y>|)>|\|>> by a polynomial
    in <math|<around|\||x|\|>>. Translation is continuous in Schwartz space:
    its derivatives converge uniformly on compact sets, and the same bound
    with one extra decay weight controls the tails. Thus the integrand in
    <eqref|eq:tube-integral> is continuous and absolutely integrable for
    every Schwartz <math|f>.

    First let <math|f> be compactly supported. Fourier inversion, pointwise
    in <math|p>, gives

    <\equation*>
      a<rsub|y><around|(|p|)>*<wide|f|\<check\>><around|(|p|)>=<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-D>*<big|int>f<around|(|x|)>*L<rsub|x+i*y><around|(|p|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x.
    </equation*>

    This integral can be passed through <math|w> in the Schwartz topology.
    For an explicit justification take Riemann sums in a box containing the
    support of <math|f>. The integrand is continuous into Schwartz space and
    has compact parameter support. For each seminorm the error is bounded by
    the box volume times its uniform modulus of continuity in that seminorm.
    The sums therefore converge in every seminorm to the displayed function;
    continuity of <math|w> gives <eqref|eq:tube-integral>. For general
    <math|f>, use <math|f<rsub|R>=\<kappa\>*<around|(|x/R|)>*f> with
    <math|0\<leqslant\>\<kappa\>\<leqslant\>1> and <math|\<kappa\>=1> near
    zero. Then <math|f<rsub|R>\<to\>f> in Schwartz space, while polynomial
    growth gives an integrable majorant for
    <math|<around|\||H<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;x+i*y|)>*f<rsub|R><around|(|x|)>|\|>>
    independent of <math|R>. Continuity on the left and dominated convergence
    on the right finish the proof.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Identification with the ordered-time
    function><label|prop:tube-identification>For strictly increasing scalar
    heights <math|y<rsub|1>\<less\>\<cdots\>\<less\>y<rsub|n>>,

    <\equation*>
      H<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;X+i*d<around|(|y|)>|)>=F<rsub|n>*<around|(|t+i*y,x;v|)>,<space|2em>X=<around|(|<around|(|t<rsub|1>,x<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,<around|(|t<rsub|n>,x<rsub|n>|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    For any <math|Y\<in\>C<rsub|n>>, the integrals of
    <math|H<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;X+i*Y<rprime|'>|)>*f<around|(|X|)>> converge
    to <math|W<rsub|Y><around|(|f|)>> as <math|Y<rprime|'>\<to\>Y> through
    <math|C<rsub|n><rsup|\<circ\>>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    At fixed strict scalar heights, <math|\<psi\><rsub|y>> is a tempered
    distribution by the uniform real-position bound on <math|F<rsub|n>>. Let
    <math|h> be a compactly supported smooth frequency test. Since
    <math|\<chi\>=1> near <math|K<rsub|n>> and <math|w> is supported there,
    the exponential testing identity gives

    <\equation*>
      w*<around|(|a<rsub|d<around|(|y|)>>*h|)>=w*<around|(|e<rsup|-d<around|(|y|)>\<cdot\>p>*h|)>=\<psi\><rsub|y><around|(|<wide|h|^>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The middle expression is used only for compactly supported <math|h>,
    since multiplication by <math|e<rsup|-d<around|(|y|)>\<cdot\>p>> does not
    preserve <math|<with|math-font|cal*|S>>. The two outer expressions,
    <math|h\<mapsto\>w*<around|(|a<rsub|d<around|(|y|)>>*h|)>> and
    <math|h\<mapsto\>\<psi\><rsub|y><around|(|<wide|h|^>|)>>, are continuous
    linear functionals on Schwartz space. Compact-test density therefore
    extends the equality of the outer expressions to every
    <math|h\<in\><with|math-font|cal*|S>>. Apply inverse Fourier
    transformation: <math|W<rsub|d<around|(|y|)>>=\<psi\><rsub|y>>.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:tube-integral> now identifies the integrals of
    the two proposed continuous functions against every Schwartz test. They
    agree pointwise: a nonzero difference would have, after a constant phase
    change, strictly positive real part on a ball and would be detected by a
    nonnegative smooth bump there. The final boundary statement follows from
    <eqref|eq:tube-integral> and continuity of
    <math|W<rsub|Y<rprime|'>><around|(|f|)>> on <math|C<rsub|n>>.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Lorentz covariance in the tube><label|prop:tube-covariance> For
    every proper orthochronous Lorentz matrix <math|\<Lambda\>>,

    <\equation*>
      H<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;z|)>=H<rsub|n>*<around|(|\<rho\><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>*v;\<Lambda\>*z|)>*<space|2em><around|(|z\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|n>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The action is simultaneous on all slots.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The matrix <math|\<Lambda\>> preserves strict forward gaps, so
    <math|\<Lambda\>*z> is in the tube. Its dual <math|A=\<Lambda\><rsup|-T>>
    preserves <math|K<rsub|n>>, because it is again proper orthochronous and
    preserves total momentum and suffix cones. The Fourier form of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:lorentz-covariance>, obtained by the change
    of variables of determinant one, is

    <\equation*>
      <wide|W|^><rsub|n>*<around|(|\<rho\><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>*v|)>*<around|(|h\<circ\>A<rsup|-1>|)>=<wide|W|^><rsub|n><around|(|v|)><around|(|h|)>.
    </equation*>

    The two Schwartz functions <math|L<rsub|z>> and
    <math|L<rsub|\<Lambda\>*z>\<circ\>A> agree near <math|K<rsub|n>>. Indeed
    their exponential factors agree because
    <math|<around|(|A*p|)>\<cdot\><around|(|\<Lambda\>*z|)>=p\<cdot\>z>. At
    each <math|p\<in\>K<rsub|n>>, both cutoffs are one on the neighborhood
    <math|B*<around|(|p,1|)>\<cap\>A<rsup|-1>*B*<around|(|A*p,1|)>>. Their
    difference therefore vanishes near the support of <math|w>. Apply the
    Fourier covariance identity to <math|h=L<rsub|\<Lambda\>*z>\<circ\>A>,
    discard this vanishing difference, and multiply by
    <math|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-D>>. This is exactly the claimed
    equality.
  </proof>

  <subsection|The heat-vector bridge><label|sec:heat-vectors>

  We now supply the independent-height Gram limit in
  Input<nbsp><reference|input:word-boundary> from the joint boundary theorem.
  Throughout this section the complex heat and slab properties used in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:analytic-boundary> are retained. The
  involution <math|J> is the one induced on the field union by Euclidean time
  reflection and conjugation. This identification is essential in the Gram
  formula below.

  For <math|0\<less\>y<rsub|1>\<less\>\<cdots\>\<less\>y<rsub|n>>, real
  positions <math|X<rsub|i>=<around|(|t<rsub|i>,x<rsub|i>|)>>, and an
  admissible width <math|p> satisfying <math|4*p\<less\>y<rsub|1>> and
  <math|4*p\<less\>y<rsub|j>-y<rsub|i>> for <math|i\<less\>j>, define

  <\multline>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|Q<rsub|p,y>*<around|(|X;v|)>=E*<around|(|-i*<around|(|t<rsub|1>+i*y<rsub|1>|)>-p|)>*U<around|(|x<rsub|1>|)>*B<rsub|p><around|(|v<rsub|1>|)>>>|<row|<cell|\<cdot\>R<rsub|p>*<around|(|t<rsub|2>-t<rsub|1>+i*<around|(|y<rsub|2>-y<rsub|1>|)>,x<rsub|2>-x<rsub|1>|)>*B<rsub|p><around|(|v<rsub|2>|)>*\<cdots\>*R<rsub|p>*<around|(|t<rsub|n>-t<rsub|n-1>+i*<around|(|y<rsub|n>-y<rsub|n-1>|)>,x<rsub|n>-x<rsub|n-1>|)>*B<rsub|p><around|(|v<rsub|n>|)>*\<Omega\><rsub|E>.<eq-number><label|eq:point-heat-vector>>>>>
  </multline>

  The empty tuple gives <math|\<Omega\><rsub|E>>. The subtraction in the
  first complex heat time is <math|p>, whereas that between two insertions is
  <math|2*p>. Each complex heat factor is a contraction, so

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:point-heat-bound><around|\<\|\|\>|Q<rsub|p,y>*<around|(|X;v|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|i><around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsub|p><around|(|v<rsub|i>|)>|\<\|\|\>>.
  </equation>

  For fixed <math|p,y,v> this vector is continuous in <math|X>, by the same
  successive strong-continuity argument used for <eqref|eq:continued-matrix>.
  Consequently <math|f<around|(|X|)>*Q<rsub|p,y>*<around|(|X;v|)>> is Bochner
  integrable for every joint Schwartz test <math|f>. To justify strong
  measurability without a global separability assumption on the Hilbert
  space, its continuous image of the separable position space is separable.
  The bound <eqref|eq:point-heat-bound> then gives the integrable majorant
  <math|<around|\||f<around|(|X|)>|\|>*<big|prod><rsub|i><around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsub|p><around|(|v<rsub|i>|)>|\<\|\|\>>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Mixed Gram identity><label|lem:mixed-heat-gram> For positive
    strictly ordered height tuples <math|y,z>, separately admissible widths
    <math|p,q>, positions <math|X,Y> and labels <math|v,u>, put

    <\equation*>
      <wide|y|^>=<around|(|-y<rsub|n>,\<ldots\>,-y<rsub|1>,z<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,z<rsub|m>|)>,<space|1em><wide|X|^>=<around|(|X<rsub|n>,\<ldots\>,X<rsub|1>,Y<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,Y<rsub|m>|)>,<space|1em><wide|v|^>=<around|(|J*v<rsub|n>,\<ldots\>,J*v<rsub|1>,u<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,u<rsub|m>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Then

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:point-gram-continuation><around|\<langle\>|Q<rsub|p,y>*<around|(|X;v|)>,Q<rsub|q,z>*<around|(|Y;u|)>|\<rangle\>>=F<rsub|n+m>*<around|(|<wide|t|^>+i*<wide|y|^>,<wide|x|^>;<wide|v|^>|)>.
    </equation>

    Here <math|<wide|t|^>,<wide|x|^>> are the real time and spatial parts of
    <math|<wide|X|^>>. The real positions in the first tuple are reversed in
    order; their real time coordinates are not negated.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    When all real time coordinates are zero, the vector in
    <eqref|eq:point-heat-vector> is the Euclidean word vector with insertions
    <math|<around|(|x<rsub|i>,y<rsub|i>|)>> and labels <math|v<rsub|i>>. This
    follows directly by induction from the right: a centered insertion adds
    its point at height <math|p>, the preceding positive heat interval
    supplies the missing distance, and the spatial translation changes the
    common reference point. More explicitly, with a preceding reference
    <math|<around|(|X<rsub|0>,Y<rsub|0>|)>> and preceding half-width
    <math|q<rsub|0>>, the partial vector has points
    <math|<around|(|x<rsub|i>-X<rsub|0>,y<rsub|i>-Y<rsub|0>-q<rsub|0>|)>>.
    The recursion uses first heat time <math|y<rsub|1>-Y<rsub|0>-q<rsub|0>-p>;
    after insertion it produces the same formula with the first point
    included. Taking <math|q<rsub|0>=0> and
    <math|<around|(|X<rsub|0>,Y<rsub|0>|)>=0> gives the stated word. Pairing
    the two Euclidean words reflects the first tuple in time and applies
    <math|J> to its labels. Permutation symmetry allows its reversed order,
    and the imaginary-time identity of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:continued-matrix>
    proves <eqref|eq:point-gram-continuation> in this case. This argument
    does not require equal widths <math|p,q>.

    For arbitrary fixed real positions, introduce one complex parameter
    <math|c>. In the first vector replace its times by
    <math|i*y<rsub|i>+c*t<rsub|i>>, and in the second by
    <math|i*z<rsub|j>+c*s<rsub|j>>. Call the resulting holomorphic vector
    functions <math|A<around|(|c|)>> and <math|B<around|(|c|)>>. Since all
    clearance inequalities are strict and there are only finitely many of
    them, there is a <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0> such that these vectors are
    well-defined on <math|<around|\||<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>c|\|>\<less\>\<delta\>>.
    The same strip works for <math|A<around|(|<wide|c|\<bar\>>|)>>, after
    decreasing <math|\<delta\>> if necessary. The scalar function

    <\equation*>
      c\<longmapsto\><around|\<langle\>|A<around|(|<wide|c|\<bar\>>|)>,B<around|(|c|)>|\<rangle\>>
    </equation*>

    is holomorphic: expand the difference quotient and use antilinearity in
    the first argument, which cancels the conjugation of its increment. Its
    proposed right side is <math|F> at times
    <math|i*<wide|y|^>+c*<wide|t|^>>, with the fixed combined spatial tuple.
    This too is holomorphic on a sufficiently small horizontal strip; its
    heights are the concatenation of the reversed negatives of
    <math|y<rsub|i>-<around|(|<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>c|)>*t<rsub|i>> and
    the tuple <math|z<rsub|j>+<around|(|<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>c|)>*s<rsub|j>>,
    and remain strictly ordered there.

    At <math|c=i*\<tau\>> near zero, both vectors have purely imaginary
    times, with these modified positive height tuples. The already proved
    Euclidean identity therefore equates the two scalar functions. The
    identity theorem on the connected strip extends equality to <math|c=1>.
    This is the desired identity with arbitrary real positions. Empty tuples
    cause no difficulty: their vector is the fixed vacuum and the same
    argument applies.
  </proof>

  For a word <math|b=<around|(|<around|(|v<rsub|1>,f<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,<around|(|v<rsub|n>,f<rsub|n>|)>|)>>,
  choose

  <\equation*>
    y<around|(|t|)>=<around|(|t,2*t,\<ldots\>,n*t|)>,<space|2em>p<around|(|t|)>=<tfrac|1|8>min
    <around|(|t,1|)>,<space|1em>t\<gtr\>0,
  </equation*>

  and define

  <\equation*>
    h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>=<big|int><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i>f<rsub|i><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>|)>*Q<rsub|p<around|(|t|)>,y<around|(|t|)>>*<around|(|X;v|)>*<space|0.17em>d*X.
  </equation*>

  The clearance conditions hold for every <math|t\<gtr\>0>, and the
  fixed-height bound above proves integrability. No uniform pointwise bound
  as <math|t*\<downarrow\>*0> is asserted or needed.

  <\proposition>
    <label|prop:heat-cups>These vectors satisfy
    <eqref|eq:independent-heat-gram> for the boundary functional of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:joint-boundary>, and
    <math|h<rsub|t><around|(|1|)>=\<Omega\><rsub|E>>. Moreover, let
    <math|T<rsub|E><around|(|a|)>=E*<around|(|-i*a<rsup|0>|)>*U<around|(|\<b-up-a\>|)>>
    be the joint real-time translation representation on
    <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|E>>, and for <math|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>> let
    <math|\<tau\><rsub|a>> act on words letterwise by replacing each test
    <math|f> by <math|f*<around|(|<space|0.17em>\<cdummy\>-a|)>>. Then for
    every <math|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>, every word <math|b> and every
    <math|t\<gtr\>0>,

    <\equation*>
      h<rsub|t>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|a>*b|)>=T<rsub|E><around|(|a|)>*h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>.
    </equation*>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    For words <math|b,c> of lengths <math|n,m>, use
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:mixed-heat-gram> inside the scalar product of
    their Bochner integrals. The integrand is absolutely integrable on the
    product space: its absolute value is bounded by the product of the two
    integrable scalar test magnitudes times the two fixed-height bounds
    <eqref|eq:point-heat-bound>. Fubini and the coordinate permutation
    <math|<around|(|X,Y|)>\<mapsto\><around|(|X<rsub|n>,\<ldots\>,X<rsub|1>,Y<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,Y<rsub|m>|)>>
    therefore give

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|h<rsub|s><around|(|b|)>,h<rsub|t><around|(|c|)>|\<rangle\>>=\<psi\><rsub|<around|(|-n*s,\<ldots\>,-s,t,\<ldots\>,m*t|)>><around|(|f<rsub|b<rsup|\<ast\>>*c>|)>,
    </equation*>

    with labels <math|<around|(|J*v<rsub|n>,\<ldots\>,J*v<rsub|1>,u<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,u<rsub|m>|)>>
    and joint product test <math|f<rsub|b<rsup|\<ast\>>*c>>, whose first
    factors are the reversed conjugates of the tests in <math|b>. The
    displayed heights are strictly ordered for every <math|s,t\<gtr\>0> and
    approach zero as the two parameters independently approach zero.
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:joint-boundary> gives the limit
    <math|w*<around|(|b<rsup|\<ast\>>*c|)>=K*<around|(|b,c|)>>. The
    zero-dimensional integral for the empty word is the vacuum itself.

    For translations write <math|T<rsub|E><around|(|a|)>=E*<around|(|-i*a<rsup|0>|)>*U<around|(|\<b-up-a\>|)>>,
    where the imaginary-axis values of <math|E> denote its strongly
    continuous unitary boundary values. These boundary values and their law
    with the interior extension are part of the complex heat extension used
    here. Translating all real positions by <math|a> leaves all differences
    in <eqref|eq:point-heat-vector> unchanged; its first factor changes by
    the commuting factor <math|T<rsub|E><around|(|a|)>>. Hence

    <\equation*>
      Q<rsub|p,y>*<around|(|X+a;v|)>=T<rsub|E><around|(|a|)>*Q<rsub|p,y>*<around|(|X;v|)>.
    </equation*>

    For the empty word the same assertion follows from translation invariance
    of the vacuum. Substitute <math|X\<mapsto\>X+a> in the integral with
    translated tests <math|f<rsub|i>*<around|(|<space|0.17em>\<cdummy\>-a|)>>
    and move the bounded operator <math|T<rsub|E><around|(|a|)>> outside the
    Bochner integral. This proves the translation identity for every
    <math|t\<gtr\>0>.
  </proof>

  Thus the heat-vector limit required for positivity and for the isometric
  embedding of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:word-motion> follows from the
  slab bounds and complex heat extension. Lorentz covariance, spacelike
  locality, and retention of nonzero labels at the boundary are distinct
  remaining steps; they have not been inferred from the Gram identity.

  <subsection|From boundary values to fields on a common
  domain><label|sec:word-reconstruction>

  The construction by quotienting the positive form on field words goes back
  to Wightman <cite-detail|Wightman56|Ÿ6>. We use joint tests and the common
  graded field labels. The joint boundary theorem supplies the positive form,
  and the arguments below identify the field operators and motions on its
  common domain.

  The Euclidean Hilbert space and the relativistic field Hilbert space have
  related, but different, definitions. The bridge between them is a limit of
  vectors with small positive imaginary times. This section proves the bridge
  and the ensuing operator construction. The preceding two sections construct
  the analytic boundary values and verify their heat-vector bridge from the
  stated slab hypotheses. Positivity of the boundary values will now follow
  from that bridge.

  Write <math|E> for the algebraic union of the finite-dimensional field
  spaces and <math|<with|math-font|cal*|S>=<with|math-font|cal*|S>*<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>;\<bbb-C\>|)>>
  for the Schwartz space. A letter is a pair
  <math|a=<around|(|v,f|)>\<in\>E\<times\><with|math-font|cal*|S>>, and a
  word is a finite ordered list of letters. Let <math|J:E\<to\>E> be an
  antilinear involution, put <math|a<rsup|\<ast\>>=<around|(|J*v,<wide|f|\<bar\>>|)>>,
  and define the star of a word by reversing its order and starring each
  letter. The empty word is denoted by <math|1>. Concatenation is written
  without an intervening symbol.

  Suppose <math|W<rsub|n>*<around|(|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|n>|)>> is a
  tempered distribution on <math|<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>>,
  multilinear in the labels. Its value on a product test function defines
  <math|w*<around|(|a<rsub|1>*\<cdots\>*a<rsub|n>|)>>. Set

  <\equation*>
    K*<around|(|b,c|)>=w*<around|(|b<rsup|\<ast\>>*c|)>.
  </equation*>

  The joint distribution, rather than merely this restricted family of tests,
  will be needed in constructing the boundary values. For the operator
  argument below the following properties specify exactly what is used.

  <\assumption>
    <dueto|Boundary data><label|input:word-boundary> The functional <math|w>
    is multilinear separately in every label and every test function, and
    <math|w<around|(|1|)>=1>. For fixed words <math|b,c> and labels
    <math|v,u>, the function

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|f,g|)>\<longmapsto\>K*<around|(|<around|(|v,f|)>*b,<around|(|u,g|)>*c|)>
    </equation*>

    is jointly continuous in the Schwartz topology. There are vectors
    <math|h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>>, <math|t\<gtr\>0>, in the selected
    Euclidean Hilbert space <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|E>>, with unit vacuum
    <math|\<Omega\><rsub|E>>, such that

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|lim<rsub|s,t*\<downarrow\>*0><around|\<langle\>|h<rsub|s><around|(|b|)>,h<rsub|t><around|(|c|)>|\<rangle\>>>|<cell|=K*<around|(|b,c|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:independent-heat-gram>>>|<row|<cell|lim<rsub|t*\<downarrow\>*0>
      h<rsub|t><around|(|1|)>>|<cell|=\<Omega\><rsub|E>.>>>>
    </align>

    The two positive parameters in the first limit vary independently.
  </assumption>

  Here and below the inner product is antilinear in its first argument. In
  the application <math|h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>> is the integral of a product
  of bounded slab insertions and intervening complex-time translations, at
  strictly increasing heights <math|<around|(|t,2*t,\<ldots\>,n*t|)>>. Thus
  the integrability of this vector-valued integral and the joint limit
  <eqref|eq:independent-heat-gram> require proof. A scalar boundary limit
  along one fixed ray does not by itself give this input.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Removing the imaginary heights><label|lem:cold-vectors> Under
    Input<nbsp><reference|input:word-boundary>, each
    <math|h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>> has a norm limit
    <math|q<around|(|b|)>\<in\>\<cal-H\><rsub|E>>, and

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|q<around|(|b|)>,q<around|(|c|)>|\<rangle\>>=K*<around|(|b,c|)>,<space|2em>q<around|(|1|)>=\<Omega\><rsub|E>.
    </equation*>

    In particular <math|K> is a positive semidefinite kernel.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Fix <math|b>. Expanding a squared norm gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|h<rsub|s><around|(|b|)>-h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<around|\<langle\>|h<rsub|s><around|(|b|)>,h<rsub|s><around|(|b|)>|\<rangle\>>+<around|\<langle\>|h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>,h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>|\<rangle\>>-<around|\<langle\>|h<rsub|s><around|(|b|)>,h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>|\<rangle\>>-<around|\<langle\>|h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>,h<rsub|s><around|(|b|)>|\<rangle\>>.
    </equation*>

    All four terms tend to <math|K*<around|(|b,b|)>>, including the diagonal
    terms, by the joint limit. The expression therefore tends to zero as
    <math|s,t*\<downarrow\>*0>. Completeness gives the norm limit. Continuity
    of the inner product gives the asserted Gram identities and the specified
    empty-word limit gives the vacuum. For any finitely many coefficients
    <math|c<rsub|i>> and words <math|b<rsub|i>>,

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i,j><wide|c<rsub|i>|\<bar\>>K*<around|(|b<rsub|i>,b<rsub|j>|)>*c<rsub|j>=<mid|\|><big|sum><rsub|i>c<rsub|i>*q<around|(|b<rsub|i>|)><mid|\|><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>0.
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  Let <math|\<cal-P\>> be the vector space of formal finite complex linear
  combinations of words. Extend <math|K> sesquilinearly to <math|\<cal-P\>>
  and let <math|\<cal-N\>=<around|{|p:K*<around|(|p,p|)>=0|}>>.
  Cauchy\USchwarz for a positive semidefinite form follows by applying
  positivity to <math|p+z*q> and minimizing over <math|z> (the case
  <math|K*<around|(|q,q|)>=0> follows by allowing <math|<around|\||z|\|>> to
  grow). Consequently <math|\<cal-N\>> is the radical of the form. The
  quotient <math|\<cal-D\>=\<cal-P\>/\<cal-N\>> is an inner-product space;
  let <math|\<cal-H\>> be its completion and
  <math|<around|[|b|]>\<in\>\<cal-D\>> the class of a word.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The common invariant domain><label|prop:word-fields> There is a
    bilinear map

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|v,f|)>\<longmapsto\>\<Phi\>*<around|(|v,f|)>:\<cal-D\>\<to\>\<cal-D\>,<space|2em>\<Phi\>*<around|(|v,f|)><around|[|b|]>=<around|[|<around|(|v,f|)>*b|]>.
    </equation*>

    The vacuum <math|\<Omega\>=<around|[|1|]>> has norm one, the domain
    <math|\<cal-D\>> is dense and spanned by its word vectors, and

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\>*<around|(|v,f|)>*\<xi\>,\<eta\>|\<rangle\>>>|<cell|=<around|\<langle\>|\<xi\>,\<Phi\>*<around|(|J*v,<wide|f|\<bar\>>|)>*\<eta\>|\<rangle\>>,<eq-number><label|eq:word-adjoint>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,\<Phi\><around|(|a<rsub|1>|)>*\<cdots\>*\<Phi\><around|(|a<rsub|n>|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>>|<cell|=w*<around|(|a<rsub|1>*\<cdots\>*a<rsub|n>|)>.>>>>
    </align>

    For each <math|v> and <math|\<xi\>\<in\>\<cal-D\>>, the map
    <math|f\<mapsto\>\<Phi\>*<around|(|v,f|)>*\<xi\>> is continuous from
    <math|<with|math-font|cal*|S>> to <math|\<cal-H\>>. As densely defined
    operators on <math|\<cal-H\>>, <math|\<Phi\>*<around|(|J*v,<wide|f|\<bar\>>|)>\<subseteq\>\<Phi\>*<around|(|v,f|)><rsup|\<ast\>>>;
    in particular every field operator is closable.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|L<rsub|a>> denote left concatenation on formal sums. Word
    reversal gives the exact identity

    <\equation*>
      K*<around|(|L<rsub|a>*p,q|)>=K*<around|(|p,L<rsub|a<rsup|\<ast\>>>*q|)>.
    </equation*>

    If <math|p\<in\>\<cal-N\>>, the right side vanishes for every <math|q>.
    Taking <math|q=L<rsub|a>*p> proves <math|K*<around|(|L<rsub|a>*p,L<rsub|a>*p|)>=0>.
    Thus <math|L<rsub|a>> preserves <math|\<cal-N\>> and descends to
    <math|\<cal-D\>>. This is the necessary null-space argument; positivity
    without the concatenation identity would not suffice. The same identity
    proves <eqref|eq:word-adjoint>. Multilinearity of <math|w> shows that
    every matrix element against a word of the defect in a proposed linearity
    identity is zero. Since words span <math|\<cal-D\>>, each such defect is
    zero. This proves bilinearity of <math|\<Phi\>> even though no
    multilinearity relations were imposed on the formal word space initially.

    The vacuum norm, cyclicity and moment identity follow directly from the
    construction. To prove continuity first take <math|\<xi\>=<around|[|b|]>>
    and write <math|\<eta\><rsub|f>=<around|[|<around|(|v,f|)>*b|]>>. At any
    <math|f<rsub|0>>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<eta\><rsub|f>-\<eta\><rsub|f<rsub|0>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=K*<around|(|<around|(|v,f|)>*b,<around|(|v,f|)>*b|)>+K*<around|(|<around|(|v,f<rsub|0>|)>*b,<around|(|v,f<rsub|0>|)>*b|)>-2<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>K*<around|(|<around|(|v,f<rsub|0>|)>*b,<around|(|v,f|)>*b|)>,
    </equation*>

    which tends to zero by the assumed joint continuity. Finite linear
    combinations give the result for every <math|\<xi\>\<in\>\<cal-D\>>.
    Finally <eqref|eq:word-adjoint>, with <math|\<cal-D\>> dense, is
    precisely the criterion for the asserted adjoint inclusion. The adjoint
    domain contains the dense space <math|\<cal-D\>>; hence the original
    operator is closable. Equivalently, if <math|\<xi\><rsub|n>\<to\>0> and
    <math|\<Phi\>*<around|(|v,f|)>*\<xi\><rsub|n>\<to\>\<eta\>>, pairing with
    each vector of <math|\<cal-D\>> gives <math|\<eta\>=0>, which directly
    proves closability.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Locality and retention of labels><label|prop:word-locality>
    Suppose, whenever <math|f,g> have mutually spacelike supports, that

    <\equation*>
      w*<around|(|b*<around|(|v,f|)><around|(|u,g|)>*c|)>=w*<around|(|b*<around|(|u,g|)><around|(|v,f|)>*c|)>
    </equation*>

    for all exterior words <math|b,c>. Then
    <math|<around|[|\<Phi\>*<around|(|v,f|)>,\<Phi\>*<around|(|u,g|)>|]>=0>
    on <math|\<cal-D\>>. Suppose also that a label <math|v> whose insertion
    has zero expectation against all exterior words and all tests must be
    zero. Then for every <math|v\<ne\>0> there is an <math|f> with
    <math|\<Phi\>*<around|(|v,f|)>\<ne\>0>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Pair the commutator applied to <math|<around|[|c|]>> with
    <math|<around|[|b|]>>. Its value is the difference of the two assumed
    expectations, with left exterior word <math|b<rsup|\<ast\>>>, and so is
    zero. Density and linearity prove the operator identity on
    <math|\<cal-D\>>. If all <math|\<Phi\>*<around|(|v,f|)>> vanish, then for
    any <math|b,c,f>,

    <\equation*>
      w*<around|(|b*<around|(|v,f|)>*c|)>=<around|\<langle\>|<around|[|b<rsup|\<ast\>>|]>,\<Phi\>*<around|(|v,f|)><around|[|c|]>|\<rangle\>>=0,
    </equation*>

    because <math|<around|(|b<rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>=b>. The
    detection assumption now gives <math|v=0>.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The group action and its Euclidean embedding>

  Let <math|G> be the Poincaré group with proper orthochronous Lorentz part.
  Suppose its action on labels and pullback action on test functions induce
  an action <math|b\<mapsto\>g*b> on words, preserving the empty word. Assume

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:word-motion-input>K*<around|(|g*b,g*c|)>=K*<around|(|b,c|)>,<space|2em>g\<longmapsto\>K*<around|(|b,g*c|)><text|is
    continuous>
  </equation>

  for all words. These are properties of the continued distributions and the
  label action on each finite level. For the boundary distributions of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:joint-boundary> they are verified in
  Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:word-motion-inputs>, under the hypotheses
  stated there.

  <\proposition>
    <label|prop:word-motion>There is a strongly continuous unitary
    representation <math|U> of <math|G> on <math|\<cal-H\>> with
    <math|U<around|(|g|)><around|[|b|]>=<around|[|g*b|]>>. It fixes
    <math|\<Omega\>>, preserves <math|\<cal-D\>>, and satisfies field
    covariance there. There is also a linear isometry
    <math|\<cal-V\>:\<cal-H\>\<to\>\<cal-H\><rsub|E>> with
    <math|\<cal-V\><around|[|b|]>=q<around|(|b|)>> and
    <math|\<cal-V\>*\<Omega\>=\<Omega\><rsub|E>>. Let <math|T<rsub|E>> be a
    unitary representation of the translation group on
    <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|E>>, and write <math|\<tau\><rsub|a>*b> for the
    action of the translation by <math|a> on a word <math|b>. Suppose that
    for every translation <math|a> and every word <math|b> there is
    <math|\<varepsilon\>=\<varepsilon\>*<around|(|a,b|)>\<gtr\>0> such that
    the heat vectors obey

    <\equation*>
      h<rsub|t>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|a>*b|)>=T<rsub|E><around|(|a|)>*h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>*<space|2em><around|(|0\<less\>t\<less\>\<varepsilon\>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Then <math|\<cal-V\>*U<around|(|a|)>=T<rsub|E><around|(|a|)>*\<cal-V\>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Extend the action on words linearly. Gram invariance makes it an isometry
    of the quotient; the inverse action makes its extension to the completion
    unitary. Multiplication and identity laws hold on words, hence on the
    dense span, hence on the completion. For a word <math|b>, Gram invariance
    and <eqref|eq:word-motion-input> give

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<around|[|g*b|]>-<around|[|g<rsub|0>*b|]>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=2*K*<around|(|b,b|)>-2<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>K*<around|(|g<rsub|0>*b,g*b|)>\<longrightarrow\>0.
    </equation*>

    Thus orbit maps are continuous first on finite word sums and then on
    every vector: approximate that vector by a finite sum, and use the
    isometry at both <math|g> and <math|g<rsub|0>> to bound the approximation
    errors uniformly. The vacuum and domain statements follow on words. For a
    letter <math|a> and a word <math|b> the equality
    <math|g*<around|(|a*b|)>=<around|(|g*a|)>*<around|(|g*b|)>> gives
    covariance on their span.

    The map <math|<big|sum>c<rsub|i><around|[|b<rsub|i>|]>\<mapsto\><big|sum>c<rsub|i>*q<around|(|b<rsub|i>|)>>
    preserves squared norms by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:cold-vectors>, so is
    well-defined and extends isometrically. Its vacuum identity is already
    known. Taking the norm limit in the stated translation identity for heat
    vectors proves intertwining on every word; boundedness then extends it to
    <math|\<cal-H\>>.
  </proof>

  These arguments construct all fields on one common invariant domain. They
  still require the continued distributions, their locality and
  label-detection properties, the continuous group action, and the
  independent-height limit. The forward spectral condition and uniqueness of
  the invariant vacuum require additional facts about the Euclidean
  translation representation and are not consequences of the formal word
  construction alone.

  <subsection|Joint tests and survival of field
  labels><label|sec:boundary-detection>

  Two details complete the common-domain reconstruction, apart from locality
  and the momentum projection measure. First, the joint tempered
  distributions supply the test-function continuity used in the word
  construction. Second, a label detectable by Euclidean correlations remains
  detectable after continuation to real time. The latter uses the full-tube
  construction and its identification with the ordered-time function; no
  pointwise boundary values of the fields are required.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lem:tensor-tests>The finite linear span of functions
    <math|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>,
    <math|f<rsub|i>\<in\><with|math-font|cal*|S><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)>>,
    is dense in <math|<with|math-font|cal*|S><around|(|<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>|)>>.
    The product map from the <math|n> test spaces into the joint test space
    is continuous multilinear.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Every joint derivative of a product separates into the corresponding
    single-slot derivatives. Also <math|1+<around|\||X|\|>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|i><around|(|1+<around|\||X<rsub|i>|\|>|)>>
    up to a fixed constant depending on <math|n>. Thus each weighted
    derivative supremum of the product is bounded by a constant times a
    product of single-slot Schwartz seminorms. This proves continuous
    multilinearity.

    For density, the canonical map <math|<with|math-font|cal*|S><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|a>|)><wide|\<otimes\>|^>*<with|math-font|cal*|S><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|b>|)>\<to\><with|math-font|cal*|S><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|a+b>|)>>
    is an isomorphism <cite-detail|Treves67|Theorem<nbsp>51.6,
    Eq.<nbsp>(51.3)>. The algebraic tensor product is dense in the completed
    product. Iterate with <math|a=4*<around|(|n-1|)>> and <math|b=4>, using
    continuity of the product map to approximate each
    <math|<around|(|n-1|)>>-slot factor. The case <math|n=1> is immediate.
    For <math|n=0> the empty product spans the scalar space.
  </proof>

  <\corollary>
    <label|cor:word-test-inputs>For the jointly tempered distributions
    constructed in Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:joint-boundary>, the word
    functional is separately linear in every label and test. Its mixed Gram
    expressions

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|f,g|)>\<longmapsto\>K*<around|(|<around|(|v,f|)>*b,<around|(|u,g|)>*c|)>
    </equation*>

    are jointly continuous. Thus these parts of
    Input<nbsp><reference|input:word-boundary> follow from the construction.
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    Multilinearity follows by composing the label-multilinear distribution
    with the multilinear product test. In the Gram expression the first
    variable occurs as <math|<wide|f|\<bar\>>> in a fixed slot and the second
    as <math|g> in another fixed slot; all other factors and labels are
    fixed. Conjugation is continuous on Schwartz space.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:tensor-tests> and continuity of the fixed-label
    distribution therefore give the asserted joint continuity. The empty-word
    value is one because the zero-insertion continuation is constantly one.
  </proof>

  The remaining hypothesis <eqref|eq:word-motion-input> of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:word-motion> concerns the Poincaré group.
  Let <math|g=<around|(|a,\<Lambda\>|)>> act on spacetime by
  <math|g*X=\<Lambda\>*X+a>, with <math|\<Lambda\>> proper orthochronous, and
  on tests by <math|P<rsub|g>*f=f\<circ\>g<rsup|-1>>, as in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:schwartz-motion>. Let <math|\<rho\>> be the label
  action of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:lorentz-label-descent>, on which
  translations act trivially. Define

  <\equation*>
    g*<around|(|v,f|)>=<around|(|\<rho\><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>*v,P<rsub|g>*f|)>
  </equation*>

  on letters, extend it letter by letter to words, and let it fix the empty
  word. The word construction uses the label involution <math|J> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-reflection>, the reflected conjugate. The
  following corollary assumes that this is the involution with which
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:lorentz-label-descent> proves commutation,
  that is, the involution of Input<nbsp><reference|input:calibrated-actions>.

  <\corollary>
    <dueto|Poincaré invariance of the word
    kernel><label|cor:word-motion-inputs>Assume the hypotheses of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:lorentz-covariance> for the action
    <math|\<rho\>> just described, and assume the identification of
    involutions just stated. Then <math|b\<mapsto\>g*b> is an action of the
    Poincaré group on words, <math|<around|(|g*b|)><rsup|\<ast\>>=g<around|(|b<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>>,
    and

    <\equation*>
      K*<around|(|g*b,g*c|)>=K*<around|(|b,c|)>,<space|2em>g\<longmapsto\>K*<around|(|b,g*c|)><text|is
      continuous>,
    </equation*>

    for all words <math|b,c>. Thus <eqref|eq:word-motion-input> holds. The
    translation hypothesis of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:word-motion>
    is supplied, for every <math|t\<gtr\>0>, by
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:heat-cups>.
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    For <math|g,h> in the Poincaré group,
    <math|P<rsub|g>*<around|(|P<rsub|h>*f|)><around|(|X|)>=f*<around|(|h<rsup|-1>*g<rsup|-1>*X|)>=P<rsub|g*h>*f<around|(|X|)>>,
    the Lorentz part of <math|g*h> is the product of the Lorentz parts, and
    <math|\<rho\>> is a representation. Hence the letterwise rule is a group
    action on words, and <math|P<rsub|e>> and <math|\<rho\><around|(|I|)>>
    are identities.

    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:lorentz-label-descent> gives
    <math|J*\<rho\><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>=\<rho\><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>*J>.
    Since <math|P<rsub|g>> commutes with complex conjugation of tests,

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|g*<around|(|v,f|)>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>=<around|(|J*\<rho\><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>*v,<wide|P<rsub|g>*f|\<bar\>>|)>=<around|(|\<rho\><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>*J*v,P<rsub|g>*<wide|f|\<bar\>>|)>=g<around*|(|<around|(|v,f|)><rsup|\<ast\>>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The star of a word reverses the order of its letters and stars each
    letter. Reversal commutes with a letterwise action, so
    <math|<around|(|g*b|)><rsup|\<ast\>>=g<around|(|b<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>> and
    <math|<around|(|g*b|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|g*c|)>=g*<around|(|b<rsup|\<ast\>>*c|)>>.

    Now let <math|d=<around|(|v<rsub|1>,f<rsub|1>|)>*\<cdots\><around|(|v<rsub|n>,f<rsub|n>|)>>
    be any word and <math|F=<big|prod><rsub|i>f<rsub|i><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>
    its product test. The product of the moved tests is
    <math|<big|prod><rsub|i><around|(|P<rsub|g>*f<rsub|i>|)><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>=F*<around|(|g<rsup|-1>*X<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,g<rsup|-1>*X<rsub|n>|)>>,
    the simultaneous pullback of <math|F> by <math|g>. Write
    <math|g=<around|(|a,I|)><around|(|0,\<Lambda\>|)>>. The translation
    invariance and Lorentz covariance in Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:lorentz-covariance>
    give

    <\equation*>
      w*<around|(|g*d|)>=W<rsub|n>*<around|(|\<rho\><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>*v|)>*<around*|(|P<rsub|<around|(|a,I|)>>*P<rsub|<around|(|0,\<Lambda\>|)>>*F|)>=W<rsub|n>*<around|(|\<rho\><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>*v|)>*<around*|(|P<rsub|<around|(|0,\<Lambda\>|)>>*F|)>=W<rsub|n><around|(|v|)><around|(|F|)>=w<around|(|d|)>.
    </equation*>

    Applying this to <math|d=b<rsup|\<ast\>>*c> gives
    <math|K*<around|(|g*b,g*c|)>=w*<around|(|g*<around|(|b<rsup|\<ast\>>*c|)>|)>=w*<around|(|b<rsup|\<ast\>>*c|)>=K*<around|(|b,c|)>>.

    For continuity fix words <math|b> of length <math|n> and
    <math|c=<around|(|u<rsub|1>,\<varphi\><rsub|1>|)>*\<cdots\><around|(|u<rsub|m>,\<varphi\><rsub|m>|)>>.
    All labels lie in one finite level <math|E<rsub|d>>. Choose a basis
    <math|e<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,e<rsub|N>> of <math|E<rsub|d>> and write
    <math|\<rho\><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>*u<rsub|j>=<big|sum><rsub|k>r<rsub|k*j><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>*e<rsub|k>>.
    The coefficients <math|r<rsub|k*j>> are continuous, because
    <math|\<rho\>> is continuous on each finite level. Let <math|F<rsub|g>>
    be the joint test of <math|b<rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|g*c|)>>. It equals
    <math|F<rsub|e>\<circ\><wide|g|~><rsup|-1>>, where <math|<wide|g|~>> is
    the invertible affine map of the joint position space which acts by the
    identity on the first <math|n> positions and by <math|g> on each of the
    last <math|m> positions. Multilinearity of <math|W<rsub|n+m>> in the
    labels gives

    <\equation*>
      K*<around|(|b,g*c|)>=<big|sum><rsub|k<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,k<rsub|m>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j=1><rsup|m>r<rsub|k<rsub|j>*j><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>|)>*W<rsub|n+m>*<around|(|J*v<rsub|n>,\<ldots\>,J*v<rsub|1>,e<rsub|k<rsub|1>>,\<ldots\>,e<rsub|k<rsub|m>>|)><around|(|F<rsub|g>|)>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|n>> are the labels of <math|b>.
    Each fixed-label distribution is continuous on the joint Schwartz space.
    The map <math|g\<mapsto\><wide|g|~>> is continuous into the group of
    invertible affine maps of the joint position space, so the general form
    of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:schwartz-motion> shows that
    <math|g\<mapsto\>F<rsub|g>> is continuous into the joint Schwartz space.
    The finite sum of products of continuous functions is continuous in
    <math|g>.

    The last statement is Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:heat-cups>: for
    <math|g=<around|(|a,I|)>> the word action translates every test, and the
    heat vectors satisfy <math|h<rsub|t>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|a>*b|)>=T<rsub|E><around|(|a|)>*h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>>
    for every <math|t\<gtr\>0>.
  </proof>

  The identification of involutions is a same-data requirement, not a
  convention. Let <math|\<theta\>> be the label action of the time reflection
  and <math|\<kappa\>> the real structure, so that the reflected conjugate is
  <math|J=\<theta\>*\<kappa\>>. The time reflection conjugates the rotation
  in the time\U<math|a> plane to its inverse, so
  <math|\<theta\>*L<rsub|a>*\<theta\>=-L<rsub|a>>. If the Euclidean
  generators are real, <math|\<kappa\>*L<rsub|a>*\<kappa\>=L<rsub|a>>,
  antilinearity of <math|J> gives

  <\equation*>
    J*<around|(|-i*L<rsub|a>|)>*J<rsup|-1>=i*<space|0.17em>\<theta\>*L<rsub|a>*\<theta\>=-i*L<rsub|a>,
  </equation*>

  so <math|J> commutes with the boost generators
  <math|K<rsub|a>=-i*L<rsub|a>>, while <math|\<kappa\>> alone would
  anticommute with them. Both <math|\<theta\>> and <math|\<kappa\>> commute
  with spatial rotations.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|A zero boundary has zero strict
    continuation><label|lem:zero-boundary>Fix an ordered label tuple
    <math|v>. If <math|W<rsub|n><around|(|v|)>=0> as a joint tempered
    distribution, then <math|F<rsub|n>*<around|(|t+i*y,x;v|)>=0> for every
    strictly ordered height tuple <math|y> and every real position tuple
    <math|<around|(|t,x|)>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The full-tube function is defined by <math|H<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;z|)>=<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-4*n>*<wide|W|^><rsub|n><around|(|v|)><around|(|L<rsub|z>|)>>.
    It is therefore identically zero if <math|W<rsub|n><around|(|v|)>=0>.
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:tube-identification> identifies its
    restriction at <math|z=X+i*d<around|(|y|)>> with the ordered-time
    function for every strictly ordered <math|y>. This proves the assertion.
  </proof>

  Let <math|T> denote the selected Euclidean hierarchy. At each finite raw
  label level, its null space consists of labels whose insertion vanishes in
  every distinct-point correlation with arbitrary exterior labels. Assume the
  selected and original null spaces agree at every level, as established by
  the detector argument when its moment and rank hypotheses are supplied.
  Assume also the finite-level quotient embeddings into the union are
  injective and every union label has a finite-level representative.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Boundary detection><label|prop:boundary-detection> If a union
    label <math|v> satisfies

    <\equation*>
      w*<around|(|b*<around|(|v,f|)>*c|)>=0
    </equation*>

    for every pair of exterior words <math|b,c> and every single-slot
    Schwartz test <math|f>, then <math|v=0>. Consequently every nonzero label
    defines a nonzero smeared field operator in
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:word-fields>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Take any ordered tuple of labels containing <math|v>, at any slot. The
    assumption says that its joint distribution vanishes on every product
    test: group the factors before the distinguished slot into <math|b>, and
    those after it into <math|c>. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:tensor-tests> and
    tempered continuity therefore make that entire joint distribution zero.
    By Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:zero-boundary>, its strict-time continuation
    is zero. At real times zero, the pure-imaginary identity gives zero for
    the corresponding selected Euclidean correlation whenever the Euclidean
    times are strictly increasing.

    For arbitrary positions with pairwise distinct time coordinates, sort
    them by time. The label tuple is permuted with the positions and still
    contains <math|v>, so the preceding zero-distribution argument applies to
    the sorted tuple. Euclidean permutation symmetry transfers its zero value
    back to the original ordering. Such position tuples are dense in the full
    configuration space of distinct points: perturb the finitely many time
    coordinates by distinct arbitrarily small amounts, avoiding the finitely
    many equality hyperplanes. Openness of the distinct-point configuration
    space keeps all points distinct during a sufficiently small perturbation.
    Continuity of the Euclidean correlations extends the zero value to every
    configuration, including those with time ties.

    Represent <math|v> by a finite-level raw label. The preceding conclusion,
    with arbitrary raw exterior labels projected into the union, is exactly
    the all-exterior null criterion for that label in <math|T>. Equality of
    selected and original null spaces makes its original quotient class zero.
    Its union image is <math|v>, so <math|v=0>. This proves the detection
    premise of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:word-locality>; that
    proposition then supplies the nonzero smeared operator for every nonzero
    label.
  </proof>

  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:boundary-detection> does not by itself
  produce a nonzero field: if every field space were zero, all of its
  hypotheses would hold and every smeared field would vanish. A nonzero label
  comes from the unit insertion of the unselected limiting hierarchy, whose
  field quotients <math|E<rsub|d>=<around|(|<text|raw labels of grade
  >d|)>/N<rsub|d>> form the union <math|E> (the null spaces <math|N<rsub|d>>
  are those of Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-original-null>). The following
  property of that hierarchy is used.

  <\assumption>
    <dueto|Unit insertion in the unselected
    limit><label|input:unit-insertion>There is a grade <math|d<rsub|0>> and a
    raw label <math|\<b-1\>> of grade <math|d<rsub|0>> with the following two
    properties, for the unselected limiting correlations <math|S>.

    <\enumerate>
      <item>(Unit law.) For every <math|n\<geqslant\>0>, all raw labels
      <math|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|n>> of arbitrary grades and every
      configuration <math|<around|(|x<rsub|0>,x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>>
      of pairwise distinct points,

      <\equation*>
        S*<around*|(|\<b-1\>,v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|n>;<space|0.17em>x<rsub|0>,x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>=S*<around*|(|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|n>;<space|0.17em>x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>.
      </equation*>

      <item>(Normalization.) The correlation of the empty word is <math|1>.
    </enumerate>
  </assumption>

  In the defining condition of <math|N<rsub|d>>, \Parbitrary finitely many
  spectator labels\Q includes the case of no spectators; then the word is the
  single insertion at one point, a configuration of pairwise distinct points.

  <\corollary>
    <dueto|A nonzero smeared field><label|cor:nonzero-field>Assume the
    hypotheses of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:boundary-detection> and
    Assumption<nbsp><reference|input:unit-insertion>. Let <math|u\<in\>E> be
    the image of the class of <math|\<b-1\>> in <math|E<rsub|d<rsub|0>>>.
    Then <math|u\<neq\>0>, and there is <math|f\<in\><with|math-font|cal*|S><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)>>
    with <math|\<Phi\>*<around|(|u,f|)>\<neq\>0> in
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:word-fields>. In particular at least one
    smeared field operator is nonzero.
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    Suppose the class of <math|\<b-1\>> in <math|E<rsub|d<rsub|0>>> were
    zero, that is, <math|\<b-1\>\<in\>N<rsub|d<rsub|0>>>. Then every
    unselected correlation of a distinct-point word containing <math|\<b-1\>>
    vanishes. Take the word with no spectators, the single insertion of
    <math|\<b-1\>> at a point <math|x<rsub|0>>. By the unit law with
    <math|n=0> and by normalization,

    <\equation*>
      0=S*<around|(|\<b-1\>;x<rsub|0>|)>=S<around|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=1,
    </equation*>

    a contradiction. Hence the class of <math|\<b-1\>> in
    <math|E<rsub|d<rsub|0>>> is nonzero. The embedding
    <math|E<rsub|d<rsub|0>>\<to\>E> is injective by the standing assumption
    before Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:boundary-detection>, so
    <math|u\<ne\>0>.

    By Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:boundary-detection>, applied in
    contrapositive form, there are exterior words <math|b,c> and a test
    <math|f> with <math|w*<around|(|b*<around|(|u,f|)>*c|)>\<neq\>0>. By the
    defining formula of the fields in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:word-fields>
    and <math|<around|(|b<rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>=b>,

    <\equation*>
      w*<around|(|b*<around|(|u,f|)>*c|)>=<around|\<langle\>|<around|[|b<rsup|\<ast\>>|]>,\<Phi\>*<around|(|u,f|)><around|[|c|]>|\<rangle\>>.
    </equation*>

    Hence <math|\<Phi\>*<around|(|u,f|)><around|[|c|]>\<neq\>0>, and so
    <math|\<Phi\>*<around|(|u,f|)>\<neq\>0>.
  </proof>

  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-limits-unit> supplies
  Assumption<nbsp><reference|input:unit-insertion> in grade zero for the
  original, unselected limiting hierarchy, under the same-family calibration
  and ordered-limit hypotheses stated there. It proves both the unit
  insertion law and normalization. The present corollary uses no unit of a
  separately selected hierarchy; the grade-zero unit already suffices.

  Boundary detection uses equality of the original and character null spaces.
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-no-new-null> gives that equality using the
  same-label rank construction of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-rank-purchases>,
  the kernel transport of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:finite-rank-kernel-transport>,
  the moments of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-phase-detector-moments>,
  and the free separating tests of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-all-grade-free-detector>.

  <subsection|Positive energy and the joint momentum
  measure><label|sec:spectral-measure>

  The Fourier support of the vacuum distributions and the spectrum of the
  translation operators are different assertions. We now prove the latter.
  The essential analytic fact is elementary: a positive finite measure whose
  Fourier transform extends boundedly to the upper half-plane cannot charge
  negative frequencies. Positivity is what makes a Gaussian probe detect such
  a charge without cancellation.

  We use the spectral theorem for strongly continuous unitary representations
  of locally compact abelian groups, in the form of the SNAG theorem
  <cite-detail|BHV|Theorem D.3.1, p.397>. Applied to a strongly continuous
  unitary representation <math|T> of <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>, it supplies a
  unique regular projection-valued measure <math|P> on
  <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>> with

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:snag-convention>T<around|(|a|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\>a>*<space|0.17em>d*P<around|(|p|)>,<space|2em>P<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)>=I.
  </equation>

  Here we identify the character indexed by <math|p> with
  <math|a\<mapsto\>e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\>a>>; the pairing is Euclidean, as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:fourier-support>. The hypotheses hold for
  the translation representation constructed in
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:word-motion>, and for its Euclidean
  counterpart in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:complex-heat>. In particular,
  continuity is strong operator continuity, not operator-norm continuity. For
  each vector <math|v>, <math|\<mu\><rsub|v><around|(|B|)>=<around|\<langle\>|v,P<around|(|B|)>*v|\<rangle\>>>
  is a positive regular finite measure, of mass
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>, with characteristic function
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|v,T<around|(|a|)>*v|\<rangle\>>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|A bounded upper-half-plane extension><label|lem:paley-positive>
    Let <math|\<mu\>> be a positive finite Borel measure on
    <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>, and let <math|\<ell\>:\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>\<to\>\<bbb-R\>>
    be continuous. Suppose a function <math|F> is continuous on
    <math|<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>z\<geqslant\>0>, holomorphic on
    <math|<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>z\<gtr\>0>, bounded there by <math|C>,
    and satisfies

    <\equation*>
      F<around|(|t|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|i*t*\<ell\><around|(|p|)>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><around|(|p|)>*<space|2em><around|(|t\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Then <math|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<mu\>\<subseteq\><around|{|p:\<ell\><around|(|p|)>\<geqslant\>0|}>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Fix <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, <math|b\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> and
    <math|s\<geqslant\>0>. Multiply <math|F<around|(|z|)>> by <math|exp
    <around|(|-i*b*z-\<varepsilon\>*z<rsup|2>|)>> and integrate around the
    rectangle with corners <math|-R,R,R+i*s,-R+i*s>. Cauchy's theorem
    applies: the function is continuous on the rectangle and holomorphic in
    its interior. One can also apply the theorem above height
    <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0> and let <math|\<delta\>*\<downarrow\>*0>;
    continuity on each compact rectangle justifies that limit. On either
    vertical edge the magnitude is at most <math|C*exp
    <around|(|-\<varepsilon\>*R<rsup|2>+<around|\||b|\|>*s+\<varepsilon\>*s<rsup|2>|)>>.
    The vertical integrals tend to zero. The horizontal integrals converge
    absolutely, since at height <math|u> the common majorant is <math|C*exp
    <around|(|-\<varepsilon\>*t<rsup|2>+b*u+\<varepsilon\>*u<rsup|2>|)>>.
    Consequently the integrals along heights zero and <math|s> agree, and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:gaussian-shift-bound><around*|\||<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\>>F<around|(|t|)>*e<rsup|-i*b*t-\<varepsilon\>*t<rsup|2>>*<space|0.17em>d*t|\|>\<leqslant\>C*<sqrt|<frac|\<pi\>|\<varepsilon\>>>*<space|0.17em>e<rsup|b*s+\<varepsilon\>*s<rsup|2>>.
    </equation>

    Fubini applies to the boundary integral because <math|\<mu\>> is finite
    and <math|e<rsup|-\<varepsilon\>*t<rsup|2>>> is integrable. The Gaussian
    transform gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\>>e<rsup|i*t*<around|(|\<ell\><around|(|p|)>-b|)>-\<varepsilon\>*t<rsup|2>>*<space|0.17em>d*t=<sqrt|<frac|\<pi\>|\<varepsilon\>>>*<space|0.17em>e<rsup|-<around|(|\<ell\><around|(|p|)>-b|)><rsup|2>/<around|(|4*\<varepsilon\>|)>>.
    </equation*>

    For completeness, if <math|G<around|(|a|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|i*a*t-\<varepsilon\>*t<rsup|2>>*<space|0.17em>d*t>,
    differentiation under the integral and integration by parts give
    <math|G<rprime|'><around|(|a|)>=-a*G<around|(|a|)>/<around|(|2*\<varepsilon\>|)>>.
    The value <math|G<around|(|0|)>=<sqrt|\<pi\>/\<varepsilon\>>> follows by
    squaring the Gaussian integral and using polar coordinates. Solving this
    differential equation proves the formula with its stated normalization.

    Suppose <math|p<rsub|0>\<in\><math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<mu\>> and
    <math|b=\<ell\><around|(|p<rsub|0>|)>\<less\>0>. In
    <eqref|eq:gaussian-shift-bound> choose
    <math|s=-b/<around|(|2*\<varepsilon\>|)>> and cancel the positive factor
    <math|<sqrt|\<pi\>/\<varepsilon\>>>. Positivity of the resulting integral
    yields

    <\equation*>
      <big|int>e<rsup|-<around|(|\<ell\><around|(|p|)>-b|)><rsup|2>/<around|(|4*\<varepsilon\>|)>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><around|(|p|)>\<leqslant\>C*e<rsup|-b<rsup|2>/<around|(|4*\<varepsilon\>|)>>.
    </equation*>

    The open neighborhood <math|A=<around|{|p:<around|(|\<ell\><around|(|p|)>-b|)><rsup|2>\<less\>b<rsup|2>/4|}>>
    of <math|p<rsub|0>> has mass <math|m\<gtr\>0>. Its contribution to the
    left side is at least <math|m*e<rsup|-b<rsup|2>/<around|(|16*\<varepsilon\>|)>>>.
    Hence <math|m\<leqslant\>C*e<rsup|-3*b<rsup|2>/<around|(|16*\<varepsilon\>|)>>>
    for every <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>. Letting
    <math|\<varepsilon\>*\<downarrow\>*0> is a contradiction.
  </proof>

  Apply the lemma first on the Euclidean Hilbert space. Write
  <math|T<rsub|E>*<around|(|t,\<b-up-x\>|)>=V<around|(|t|)>*U<rsub|E><around|(|\<b-up-x\>|)>>
  for its joint translations, where <math|V> is the unitary time group of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:complex-heat>. For
  <math|v\<in\>\<cal-H\><rsub|E>> define

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|v>*<around|(|t+i*s|)>=<around|\<langle\>|v,V<around|(|t|)>*E<rsub|s>*v|\<rangle\>>*<space|2em><around|(|s\<geqslant\>0|)>.
  </equation*>

  This function is continuous on the closed upper half-plane by strong
  continuity and the uniform operator bounds. On its interior it equals
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|v,E*<around|(|-i*z|)>*v|\<rangle\>>>, so is
  holomorphic and bounded by <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>. Its
  boundary is the characteristic function of the time coordinate of the joint
  measure <math|\<mu\><rsup|E><rsub|v>>. The lemma gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:euclidean-positive-energy><math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<mu\><rsup|E><rsub|v>\<subseteq\><around|{|p:p<rsup|0>\<geqslant\>0|}>.
  </equation>

  Spatial covariance is not used in this step.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Forward spectrum and the unique vacuum><label|thm:field-spectrum>
    Suppose the word construction has a strongly continuous Poincaré
    representation <math|U> with proper orthochronous Lorentz part, and an
    isometric translation intertwiner <math|\<cal-V\>:\<cal-H\>\<to\>\<cal-H\><rsub|E>>
    as in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:word-motion>. Then its joint
    momentum measure satisfies

    <\equation*>
      P*<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>\<setminus\><wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>|)>=0,<space|2em><wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>=<around|{|p:p<rsup|0>\<geqslant\><around|\||\<b-up-p\>|\|>|}>.
    </equation*>

    If the heat-fixed subspace of <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|E>> is
    <math|\<bbb-C\>*\<Omega\><rsub|E>>, then the vectors fixed by all
    translations on <math|\<cal-H\>> are exactly <math|\<bbb-C\>*\<Omega\>>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    The intertwining identity and isometry give

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|v,U<around|(|a|)>*v|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|\<cal-V\>*v,T<rsub|E><around|(|a|)>*\<cal-V\>*v|\<rangle\>>.
    </equation*>

    Thus <math|\<mu\><rsub|v>> and <math|\<mu\><rsup|E><rsub|\<cal-V\>*v>>
    have equal characteristic functions and are equal measures. Here is the
    measure-uniqueness step explicitly. For any Schwartz function <math|f>,
    Fourier inversion and Fubini express its integral against a finite
    measure in terms of that characteristic function; Fubini follows from
    integrability of <math|<wide|f|^>>. The two measures therefore agree on
    all smooth compactly supported tests. Continuous compactly supported
    tests are uniformly approximated, in a fixed compact neighborhood of
    their supports, by smooth mollifications. They too have equal integrals.
    Two finite positive Borel measures on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>> with equal
    integrals on <math|C<rsub|c><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)>> are equal.
    Indeed, for compact <math|K> the functions
    <math|\<phi\><rsub|k><around|(|p|)>=max
    <around|(|0,1-k<math-up|dist><around|(|p,K|)>|)>> lie in
    <math|C<rsub|c><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)>> and decrease pointwise to
    the indicator of <math|K>, so dominated convergence gives equal masses on
    every compact set; compact sets form a <math|\<pi\>>-system generating
    the Borel <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra, and the total masses agree by
    monotone convergence along balls, so Dynkin's
    <math|\<pi\>>\U<math|\<lambda\>> theorem gives equality of the measures.
    In particular every <math|\<mu\><rsub|v>> obeys
    <eqref|eq:euclidean-positive-energy>.

    For a Lorentz matrix <math|\<Lambda\>>, covariance of translations
    implies

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsub|U<around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>*v>=<around|(|\<Lambda\><rsup|-T>|)><rsub|\<ast\>>*\<mu\><rsub|v>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed the characteristic function of the left side at <math|a> equals
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|v,U*<around|(|\<Lambda\><rsup|-1>*a|)>*v|\<rangle\>>>,
    which is that of the right side. The preceding uniqueness argument
    applies again. A homeomorphism carries the support of a measure to the
    support of its pushforward: every neighborhood of the image has a
    preimage neighborhood of positive measure, and the converse follows by
    its inverse. It follows that, for any
    <math|p\<in\><math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<mu\><rsub|v>>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|\<Lambda\><rsup|-T>*p|)><rsup|0>\<geqslant\>0<space|1em><text|for
      every proper orthochronous >\<Lambda\>.
    </equation*>

    The dual matrices <math|\<Lambda\><rsup|-T>=\<eta\>*\<Lambda\>*\<eta\>>,
    with <math|\<eta\>=<math-up|diag><around|(|1,-1,-1,-1|)>>, range over the
    same group. The identity gives <math|p<rsup|0>\<geqslant\>0>. If
    <math|<around|\||\<b-up-p\>|\|>\<gtr\>p<rsup|0>>, take a boost in the
    direction <math|\<b-up-p\>/<around|\||\<b-up-p\>|\|>> with positive
    rapidity <math|r> such that <math|tanh
    r\<gtr\>p<rsup|0>/<around|\||\<b-up-p\>|\|>>. Its time coordinate is
    <math|p<rsup|0>*cosh r-<around|\||\<b-up-p\>|\|>*sinh r\<less\>0>, a
    contradiction. Thus every <math|\<mu\><rsub|v>> is supported in
    <math|<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>>. The complement of the support of a
    finite Borel measure on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>> has measure zero: it is
    a union of a countable collection of zero-measure basis neighborhoods.
    Hence <math|\<mu\><rsub|v><around|(|<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+><rsup|c>|)>=0>
    for every <math|v>. Since <math|P<around|(|B|)>> is an orthogonal
    projection, <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|P<around|(|B|)>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=\<mu\><rsub|v><around|(|B|)>>,
    which proves the asserted operator identity.

    Finally let <math|v> be translation invariant. Its image
    <math|\<cal-V\>*v> is fixed by all Euclidean real-time translations. The
    fixed-vector equivalence proved in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:complex-heat>
    makes it heat fixed. Therefore <math|\<cal-V\>*v=c*\<Omega\><rsub|E>=\<cal-V\>*<around|(|c*\<Omega\>|)>>;
    injectivity of the isometry gives <math|v=c*\<Omega\>>. The converse
    follows from vacuum invariance. This proves uniqueness under the stated
    heat hypothesis, and makes no spectral-gap assertion.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Spacelike exchange and locality><label|sec:locality>

  We prove that exchanging two adjacent spacelike-separated insertions does
  not change the boundary distribution. The argument keeps all the other
  insertions present. It first makes the two tube functions analytic near a
  partial boundary with one tied imaginary-time gap. Euclidean symmetry
  identifies their germs when the real times vanish. Analytic continuation
  along a path then identifies them at arbitrary spacelike configurations.
  Finally their partial boundary distributions agree, and the remaining
  imaginary heights can be removed.

  Fix adjacent slots <math|i,j=i+1> and let <math|\<sigma\>> exchange them.
  It acts on tuples of labels, real points and complex points by the same
  permutation. Put

  <\equation*>
    O<rsub|i*j>=<around|{|X:X<rsub|j>-X<rsub|i><text| is
    spacelike>|}>=<around|{|X:<around|\||X<rsub|j><rsup|0>-X<rsub|i><rsup|0>|\|>\<less\><around|\||\<b-up-X\><rsub|j>-\<b-up-X\><rsub|i>|\|>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Choose scalar heights <math|y> with <math|y<rsub|i>=y<rsub|j>> and with
  every other ordered pair strictly increasing. Then
  <math|d<around|(|y|)>\<in\>C<rsub|n>> and
  <math|\<sigma\>*d<around|(|y|)>=d<around|(|y|)>>. The point
  <math|X+i*d<around|(|y|)>> lies in the closures of both
  <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|n>> and <math|\<sigma\><rsup|-1>*\<cal-T\><rsub|n>>:
  strictly separate the tied heights in the appropriate order by arbitrarily
  small amounts. The functions to be compared are

  <\equation*>
    z\<longmapsto\>H<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;z|)>,<space|2em>z\<longmapsto\>H<rsub|n>*<around|(|v\<circ\>\<sigma\>;\<sigma\>*z|)>.
  </equation*>

  <subsubsection|Small imaginary boosts enter both tubes>

  For a spatial unit vector <math|e>, write, in time-first coordinates,

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|e>*<around|(|t,\<b-up-x\>|)>=<around|(|e\<cdot\>\<b-up-x\>,t*e|)>,<space|2em>P<rsub|e>*<around|(|t,\<b-up-x\>|)>=<around|(|t,<around|(|e\<cdot\>\<b-up-x\>|)>*e|)>.
  </equation*>

  The boost and its entire complex extension are
  <math|B<rsub|e><around|(|z|)>=I+sinh z*<space|0.17em>C<rsub|e>+<around|(|cosh
  z-1|)>*P<rsub|e>>. For a spacelike vector <math|a>, the choice
  <math|e=\<b-up-a\>/<around|\||\<b-up-a\>|\|>> gives
  <math|C<rsub|e>*a=<around|(|<around|\||\<b-up-a\>|\|>,a<rsup|0>*e|)>\<in\>V<rsub|+>>.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lem:imaginary-boost-path>For <math|X\<in\>O<rsub|i*j>> and
    <math|q=X+i*d<around|(|y|)>> there are <math|e> and
    <math|\<tau\>\<gtr\>0> such that <math|B<rsub|e>*<around|(|i*\<tau\>|)>*q\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|n>>
    and <math|\<sigma\>*B<rsub|e>*<around|(|-i*\<tau\>|)>*q\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|n>>.
    Moreover, for all <math|z> sufficiently close to <math|q> in
    <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|n>>, <math|B<rsub|e>*<around|(|i*b|)>*z\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|n>>
    for every <math|0\<leqslant\>b\<leqslant\>\<tau\>>. For all <math|z>
    sufficiently close to <math|q> in <math|\<sigma\><rsup|-1>*\<cal-T\><rsub|n>>,
    <math|\<sigma\>*B<rsub|e>*<around|(|-i*b|)>*z\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|n>>
    throughout the same interval.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Take <math|e> from the spacelike difference <math|X<rsub|j>-X<rsub|i>> as
    above. For any adjacent difference <math|z<rsub|k+1>-z<rsub|k>>, write
    its real and imaginary parts as <math|x> and <math|Y>. The imaginary part
    after the positive imaginary boost is

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:boost-imaginary-gap>Y+sin
      b*<space|0.17em>C<rsub|e>*x+<around|(|cos b-1|)>*P<rsub|e>*Y.
    </equation>

    At <math|q>, every gap other than <math|<around|(|i,j|)>> has
    <math|Y\<in\>V<rsub|+>>. By openness and continuity those finitely many
    transformed gaps remain in <math|V<rsub|+>> for <math|<around|(|b,z|)>>
    in a product neighborhood of <math|<around|(|0,q|)>>. At the
    distinguished gap <math|Y=0> and <math|C<rsub|e>*x\<in\>V<rsub|+>>. For
    small <math|b> rewrite <eqref|eq:boost-imaginary-gap> as

    <\equation*>
      Y+sin b*<around*|(|C<rsub|e>*x-<frac|sin b|1+cos b>*P<rsub|e>*Y|)>.
    </equation*>

    The bracket is continuous near <math|<around|(|0,q|)>> and lies in
    <math|V<rsub|+>> there, after shrinking the neighborhood. For
    <math|0\<less\>b\<less\>\<pi\>>, the sine is positive. If
    <math|Y\<in\><wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>>, the sum is strictly in
    <math|V<rsub|+>>: the spatial triangle inequality shows that a closed
    forward-cone vector plus a positive multiple of a strict one is strict.

    For the negative boost followed by the exchange, the original tied gap is
    reversed and the sine changes sign. Its inward vector is again
    <math|C<rsub|e>*<around|(|X<rsub|j>-X<rsub|i>|)>>. All other imaginary
    gaps at <math|q> remain strictly forward, since the tied heights agree
    and are the only tie. The same neighborhood argument applies. Take a
    common product neighborhood for both orientations and all gaps, and
    choose <math|\<tau\>\<gtr\>0> inside its positive parameter interval. At
    <math|q> the closed-gap condition gives the two strict endpoints. At
    nearby interior points it gives strictness for <math|b\<gtr\>0>, while
    <math|b=0> follows from membership in the relevant original tube. This
    proves the asserted uniform path property, including its endpoint at
    zero.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Holomorphic germs across the tied gap>

  The labels in any finite tuple lie in one common finite-dimensional level.
  On that level the continuous representation
  <math|r\<mapsto\>\<rho\><around|(|B<rsub|e><around|(|r|)>|)>> has an entire
  extension. Here is the elementary argument, which uses operator-norm
  continuity only in this finite-dimensional space. For a continuous
  one-parameter group <math|R<around|(|r|)>> in its matrix algebra, put
  <math|A<around|(|t|)>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t>R<around|(|s|)>*<space|0.17em>d*s>.
  Then <math|A<around|(|t|)>/t\<to\>I> as <math|t\<to\>0>, so
  <math|A<around|(|a|)>> is invertible for a sufficiently small nonzero
  <math|a>. The group law gives

  <\equation*>
    R<around|(|t|)>*A<around|(|a|)>=A*<around|(|t+a|)>-A<around|(|t|)>.
  </equation*>

  The right side is continuously differentiable. Thus <math|R> is
  differentiable; differentiating the group law gives
  <math|R<rprime|'><around|(|t|)>=R<around|(|t|)>*K>,
  <math|K=R<rprime|'><around|(|0|)>>. The derivative of
  <math|R<around|(|t|)>*e<rsup|-t*K>> is zero and its value at zero is
  <math|I>. Therefore <math|R<around|(|t|)>=e<rsup|t*K>>, and
  <math|R<around|(|z|)>=e<rsup|z*K>> is the required entire extension. This
  construction is compatible with the finite-level inclusions, since the real
  representations are compatible and the identity theorem extends their
  equality to complex parameters.

  For a point <math|q> and parameters supplied by
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:imaginary-boost-path>, define near <math|q>

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsub|q><around|(|z|)>>|<cell|=H<rsub|n>*<around|(|R*<around|(|i*\<tau\>|)>*v;B<rsub|e>*<around|(|i*\<tau\>|)>*z|)>,>>|<row|<cell|B<rsub|q><around|(|z|)>>|<cell|=H<rsub|n>*<around|(|R*<around|(|-i*\<tau\>|)>*<around|(|v\<circ\>\<sigma\>|)>;\<sigma\>*B<rsub|e>*<around|(|-i*\<tau\>|)>*z|)>.>>>>
  </align*>

  They are holomorphic in a neighborhood of <math|q>, because their arguments
  at <math|q> lie strictly in the tube. They extend the respective original
  tube functions. To prove this for <math|A<rsub|q>>, fix a nearby
  <math|z\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|n>> for which the entire path stays in the
  tube. The function of one complex parameter

  <\equation*>
    \<zeta\>\<longmapsto\>H<rsub|n>*<around|(|R<around|(|\<zeta\>|)>*v;B<rsub|e><around|(|\<zeta\>|)>*z|)>
  </equation*>

  is holomorphic on an open neighborhood of the segment
  <math|i*<around|[|0,\<tau\>|]>>. Moving labels cause no difficulty:
  expansion in a basis of the common finite level makes the expression a
  finite sum of holomorphic coefficients times the fixed-label holomorphic
  tube functions. For real <math|\<zeta\>> near zero the expression equals
  <math|H<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;z|)>> by tube covariance. The identity theorem
  on the connected component containing the segment makes it constant along
  that segment. Its value at <math|i*\<tau\>> proves the assertion. The same
  proof with the negative segment and the exchange gives the assertion for
  <math|B<rsub|q>>.

  Such a germ is uniquely determined by its original tube function. Indeed,
  two candidate germs are holomorphic on a common ball about <math|q> and
  agree on the intersection of that ball with the relevant open tube. This
  intersection contains a nonempty open set because <math|q> is in the tube's
  closure. The identity theorem on the ball makes the germs equal.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Equality on the Euclidean slice><label|lem:euclidean-germs> If
    every real time coordinate of <math|X> is zero, then the germs
    <math|A<rsub|q>> and <math|B<rsub|q>> coincide at
    <math|q=X+i*d<around|(|y|)>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|M> be the real-linear Wick map that sends Euclidean time to
    imaginary time and leaves spatial coordinates real. Put
    <math|p=<around|(|<around|(|y<rsub|k>,\<b-up-X\><rsub|k>|)>|)><rsub|k=1><rsup|n>>,
    so <math|M*p=q>. This is a distinct-point Euclidean configuration. All
    pairs other than <math|<around|(|i,j|)>> have different time coordinates,
    and the distinguished pair has different spatial coordinates because its
    real separation is spacelike. The Euclidean correlation <math|S<rsub|v>>
    is consequently smooth near <math|p>.

    On the open side where the Euclidean times are strictly ordered,
    <math|A<rsub|q>\<circ\>M=S<rsub|v>> near <math|p>, by the tube
    restriction and pure-imaginary identification. On the open side where
    their exchanged tuple is strictly ordered,
    <math|B<rsub|q>\<circ\>M=S<rsub|v>>, using Euclidean permutation symmetry
    as well. The point <math|p> is in the closure of each side. Differentiate
    the respective identities on their open sides. For each order, continuity
    of the derivatives up to <math|p> shows that the two real derivatives at
    <math|p> both equal the corresponding derivative of <math|S<rsub|v>>.

    The image of <math|M> spans the full complex coordinate space over
    <math|\<bbb-C\>>: each spatial basis vector is already in the image, and
    each time basis vector is <math|-i> times an image vector. By the chain
    rule the real derivatives just compared are the restrictions of the
    complex multilinear derivatives of <math|A<rsub|q>> and <math|B<rsub|q>>
    to this image. Complex multilinearity and spanning make all their full
    complex derivatives equal. Their convergent local power series therefore
    agree. This proves equality of germs, rather than merely equality of
    values on the Euclidean slice.
  </proof>

  The equality propagates to every <math|X\<in\>O<rsub|i*j>>. Keep its
  spatial coordinates fixed and multiply all its real time coordinates by
  <math|s\<in\><around|[|0,1|]>>. The distinguished separation remains
  spacelike throughout. Along <math|q<around|(|s|)>=<around|(|<around|(|s*X<rsub|k><rsup|0>,\<b-up-X\><rsub|k>|)>|)><rsub|k>+i*d<around|(|y|)>>
  both germs exist, and both underlying tubes have <math|q<around|(|s|)>> in
  their closures. Germ equality is locally constant in <math|s>. To verify
  this, choose a common ball where the two germs at
  <math|q<around|(|s<rsub|0>|)>> are holomorphic and extend their original
  tube functions. For <math|s> near <math|s<rsub|0>>, uniqueness of germs
  identifies the germs at <math|q<around|(|s|)>> with these same functions.
  The identity theorem on the ball says that equality near either point is
  equivalent to equality near the other. Thus the set of parameters with
  equal germs and its complement are both open in <math|<around|[|0,1|]>>.
  Connectedness and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:euclidean-germs> at <math|s=0>
  give equality at <math|s=1>.

  <subsubsection|From germs to boundary distributions>

  Let <math|W<rsub|d<around|(|y|)>,v>> denote the partial boundary
  distribution <eqref|eq:partial-damping>. Near a given
  <math|X\<in\>O<rsub|i*j>> it is represented by the germ <math|A<rsub|q>> at
  the partial height:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:germ-boundary-integral>W<rsub|d<around|(|y|)>,v><around|(|f|)>=<big|int>A<rsub|q>*<around|(|Z+i*d<around|(|y|)>|)>*f<around|(|Z|)>*<space|0.17em>d*Z
  </equation>

  for compactly supported smooth <math|f> in a sufficiently small ball about
  <math|X>. Indeed, choose a smaller product neighborhood of
  <math|<around|(|X,d<around|(|y|)>|)>> on which the germ is bounded and
  agrees with the tube function at interior heights. For
  <math|Y\<to\>d<around|(|y|)>> through <math|C<rsub|n><rsup|\<circ\>>>, its
  integrals against <math|f> converge to the displayed integral by dominated
  convergence, using a constant times <math|<around|\||f|\|>> as majorant.
  The tube boundary theorem identifies the same limit with
  <math|W<rsub|d<around|(|y|)>,v><around|(|f|)>>. Applying this argument to
  the exchanged tube, and changing variables by the coordinate permutation of
  absolute Jacobian one, gives the analogous formula for
  <math|W<rsub|\<sigma\>*d<around|(|y|)>,v\<circ\>\<sigma\>>*<around|(|f\<circ\>\<sigma\>|)>>
  with germ <math|B<rsub|q>>. Since these germs agree, the two distributions
  agree locally on <math|O<rsub|i*j>>.

  They agree on every Schwartz test whose closed support is contained in
  <math|O<rsub|i*j>>. For compact support, cover it by finitely many of the
  local balls and apply a smooth partition of unity. For an arbitrary
  Schwartz test <math|f> with closed support contained in <math|O<rsub|i*j>>,
  put <math|f<rsub|R>=\<chi\>*<around|(|X/R|)>*f> with <math|\<chi\>> as in
  the proof of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:boost-ward>:
  <math|\<chi\>\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>>> and <math|\<chi\>=1> near the
  origin. Each <math|f<rsub|R>> is smooth with compact support contained in
  <math|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>f\<subset\>O<rsub|i*j>>, so the
  compact-support case applies to it, and <math|f<rsub|R>\<to\>f> in the
  Schwartz topology as <math|R\<to\>\<infty\>>, by the estimate given in that
  proof. Both sides of the equality are continuous on Schwartz space, so the
  equality passes to the limit. (The cutoffs
  <math|\<chi\>*<around|(|\<cdummy\>/R|)>> themselves do not converge in
  Schwartz space; only the products <math|f<rsub|R>> do.)

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Adjacent spacelike exchange><label|thm:spacelike-exchange> For
    every joint Schwartz test <math|f> supported in <math|O<rsub|i*j>>,

    <\equation*>
      W<rsub|n><around|(|v|)><around|(|f|)>=W<rsub|n>*<around|(|v\<circ\>\<sigma\>|)>*<around|(|f\<circ\>\<sigma\>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Consequently the reconstructed field operators commute on their common
    domain whenever their test functions have mutually spacelike supports.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    There is a sequence of tied profiles of the kind used above tending to
    zero. For example, with slots numbered <math|1,\<ldots\>,n>, take
    <math|y<rsub|k><rsup|<around|(|m|)>>=a<rsub|k>/<around|(|m+1|)>> where
    <math|a<rsub|k>=k> except <math|a<rsub|j>=i>. All other ordered pairs
    have positive gaps, and the exchanged profile is unchanged. The preceding
    partial-distribution equality holds for each <math|m>. Continuity on the
    closed shift set, and <math|W<rsub|0>=W<rsub|n><around|(|v|)>>, give the
    asserted equality by letting <math|m\<to\>\<infty\>>.

    For the operator conclusion take any two exterior words and place the two
    test functions <math|f<rsub|i>,f<rsub|j>> between them. The closed
    support of their joint product test is contained in the inverse image of
    <math|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>f<rsub|i>\<times\><math-up|supp><space|0.17em>f<rsub|j>>
    under the two coordinate projections: wherever either factor vanishes on
    a neighborhood, so does the product. Mutually spacelike supports
    therefore put that joint support in <math|O<rsub|i*j>>. The
    distributional identity exchanges exactly these two letters and retains
    all spectators. Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:word-locality> now
    proves that the commutator vanishes on <math|\<cal-D\>>.
  </proof>

  <section|Local fields and the operator product expansion>

  <subsection|One field prescription for every truncation
  degree><label|sec:ope-prescription>

  For comparison with remainder estimates involving spectator fields, see
  <cite-detail|HollandsKopper|Ÿ1>, which treats the OPE at fixed perturbative
  order in massive Euclidean <math|\<phi\><rsup|4>> theory. Here the
  coefficients and estimates are constructed on the limiting Wilson hierarchy
  at each fixed truncation degree.

  The coefficients must agree at different truncation degrees. Choosing a new
  basis independently at each finite degree would not ensure this. We
  construct a single compatible system of fields and finite correlation
  tests, one degree at a time. The tests are fixed before choosing the
  cluster geometry or its spectators.

  Let <math|E<rsub|n>> be the finite-dimensional complex space of physical
  fields through degree <math|n>. Denote its injective inclusion maps by
  <math|\<iota\><rsub|m*n>:E<rsub|m>\<to\>E<rsub|n>>, <math|m\<leqslant\>n>,
  with <math|\<iota\><rsub|n*n>=I> and <math|\<iota\><rsub|n*k>*\<iota\><rsub|m*n>=\<iota\><rsub|m*k>>.
  These are the physical filtration maps, so inserting a field and its image
  in a higher degree gives the same separated correlations.

  An exterior test <math|\<lambda\>> consists of finitely many fixed
  spectator labels and a separated configuration including a distinguished
  first position. Inserting <math|v\<in\>E<rsub|n>> at that first position
  and evaluating the mixed Euclidean correlation defines a linear functional
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|n><around|(|v|)>>. The physical field quotient has
  been defined by vanishing of all such correlations. Thus these functionals
  separate <math|E<rsub|n>>:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-detector-separation><around|[|\<lambda\><rsub|n><around|(|v|)>=0<text|
    for every exterior test >\<lambda\>|]>\<Longrightarrow\>v=0.
  </equation>

  Their compatibility with filtration is <math|\<lambda\><rsub|n>*\<iota\><rsub|m*n>=\<lambda\><rsub|m>>.
  For a finite linear combination <math|b=<big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>\<in\>F>b<rsub|\<lambda\>>*\<lambda\>>
  use the notation <math|b<rsub|n>=<big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>\<in\>F>b<rsub|\<lambda\>>*\<lambda\><rsub|n>>.
  The same finite combination then defines a test on every level.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lem:ope-detector-span>Every linear functional on <math|E<rsub|n>>
    is <math|b<rsub|n>> for some finite combination <math|b> of exterior
    tests.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|V> be the span of the exterior functionals inside
    <math|E<rsub|n><rsup|\<ast\>>>. If <math|V> were proper, a basis of
    <math|V> would consist of fewer than <math|dim E<rsub|n>> functionals.
    The linear map from <math|E<rsub|n>> to their coordinate values would
    then have a nonzero kernel, by rank\Unullity. A nonzero vector in that
    kernel would be annihilated by all of <math|V>, hence by every exterior
    test, contrary to <eqref|eq:ope-detector-separation>. Therefore
    <math|V=E<rsub|n><rsup|\<ast\>>>. Membership in a span means a finite
    linear combination, as required.
  </proof>

  Put <math|E<rsub|-1>=0> and <math|d<rsub|n>=dim
  <around|(|E<rsub|n>/\<iota\><rsub|n-1,n>*E<rsub|n-1>|)>>, where the
  denominator is zero when <math|n=0>. A channel is a pair
  <math|\<alpha\>=<around|(|n,j|)>> with <math|1\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>d<rsub|n>>;
  its degree is <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>=n>. We seek fields
  <math|u<rsub|\<alpha\>>\<in\>E<rsub|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>>> and finite
  exterior tests <math|b<rsub|\<alpha\>>> such that, for every <math|n>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-biorthogonal>b<rsub|\<alpha\>,n>*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|<around|\||\<beta\>|\|>,n>*u<rsub|\<beta\>>|)>=\<delta\><rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>>*<space|1em><around|(|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>,<around|\||\<beta\>|\|>\<leqslant\>n|)>,
  </equation>

  and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-rebuild>v=<big|sum><rsub|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>n>b<rsub|\<alpha\>,n><around|(|v|)>*\<iota\><rsub|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>,n>*u<rsub|\<alpha\>>*<space|2em><around|(|v\<in\>E<rsub|n>|)>.
  </equation>

  The sums contain finitely many channels. No assertion of a convergent
  infinite expansion is involved.

  <\proposition>
    <label|prop:ope-prescription>There is one choice of all the fields
    <math|u<rsub|\<alpha\>>> and tests <math|b<rsub|\<alpha\>>> satisfying
    <eqref|eq:ope-biorthogonal> and <eqref|eq:ope-rebuild> simultaneously for
    every <math|n>. Moreover <math|b<rsub|\<alpha\>,m>=0> whenever
    <math|m\<less\><around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    At degree zero choose a basis of <math|E<rsub|0>> and represent each
    coordinate functional by a finite exterior test, using
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ope-detector-span>. The two identities are then
    the usual basis identities.

    Suppose the fields and tests through degree <math|n> have been chosen.
    Write <math|R=\<iota\><rsub|n,n+1>>, let
    <math|q:E<rsub|n+1>\<to\>E<rsub|n+1>/R<around|(|E<rsub|n>|)>> be the
    quotient map, and set

    <\equation*>
      B*v=<big|sum><rsub|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>n>b<rsub|\<alpha\>,n+1><around|(|v|)>*\<iota\><rsub|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>,n>*u<rsub|\<alpha\>>.
    </equation*>

    Test compatibility and <eqref|eq:ope-rebuild> give
    <math|B*R=I<rsub|E<rsub|n>>>. Choose a linear right inverse <math|S> of
    <math|q>; it exists by lifting a finite basis of the quotient. Correct
    this arbitrary lift by putting

    <\equation*>
      F=S-R*B*S.
    </equation*>

    Then <math|q*F=I> and <math|B*F=0>. The old tests vanish on the new image
    <math|F>. Indeed <eqref|eq:ope-biorthogonal> gives
    <math|b<rsub|\<alpha\>,n>*B=b<rsub|\<alpha\>,n+1>> for every old channel
    <math|\<alpha\>>, so <math|b<rsub|\<alpha\>,n+1>*F=b<rsub|\<alpha\>,n>*B*F=0>.

    Every <math|v\<in\>E<rsub|n+1>> has the exact decomposition

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ope-quotient-split>v=F*q*v+R*B*v.
    </equation>

    To prove it, <math|q*<around|(|v-F*q*v|)>=0> implies <math|v-F*q*v=R*a>
    for some <math|a\<in\>E<rsub|n>>. Applying <math|B> and using
    <math|B*F=0>, <math|B*R=I> gives <math|a=B*v>. Now choose a basis
    <math|<around|(|e<rsub|j>|)>> of the quotient. The new fields are
    <math|F*e<rsub|j>>. For each new channel choose a finite exterior test
    representing <math|e<rsub|j><rsup|\<ast\>>*q> on <math|E<rsub|n+1>>.
    Leave every old field and every old test unchanged. The new tests
    annihilate <math|R<around|(|E<rsub|n>|)>>, are dual to the new fields
    because <math|q*F=I>, and the old tests annihilate the new fields as just
    proved. Old fields and tests retain their mutual duality by
    compatibility. This proves all four old/new cases of
    <eqref|eq:ope-biorthogonal>. Expanding the two terms in
    <eqref|eq:ope-quotient-split> proves <eqref|eq:ope-rebuild> at degree
    <math|n+1>.

    Iterate. A channel and its finite test, once chosen, never change. Their
    value in the resulting infinite list is the one fixed at their birth
    degree. Any identity at degree <math|n> involves only the finitely many
    channels already fixed by stage <math|n>, so it remains valid in the
    final list. Finally take <math|m\<less\><around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>> and
    expand <math|v\<in\>E<rsub|m>> using <eqref|eq:ope-rebuild>. Lift that
    identity to degree <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>> and apply
    <math|b<rsub|\<alpha\>>>. Biorthogonality makes every term zero, because
    all its channels have degree at most <math|m>. Compatibility then gives
    <math|b<rsub|\<alpha\>,m><around|(|v|)>=0>.
  </proof>

  The subtraction <math|R*B*S> is the reason this construction works at all
  degrees. It places every new quotient lift in the exact kernels of all old
  tests, while retaining the old tests as actual finite correlation
  evaluations.

  <subsubsection|Testing a product at the cluster center>

  For each exterior test <math|\<lambda\>>, translate its distinguished
  position to zero. Write its other positions as <math|z<rsub|\<lambda\>,j>>
  and its spectator labels as <math|w<rsub|\<lambda\>,j>>. These positions
  are pairwise distinct and none is zero. For a cluster of fields
  <math|v<rsub|i>> at <math|x<rsub|i>> and center <math|o>, define

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-R\><rsub|\<lambda\>>*<around|(|\<b-x\>,\<b-v\>;o|)>=S<rsub|<math-up|mix>>*<around*|(|<around|(|v<rsub|i>,x<rsub|i>-o|)><rsub|i>,<around|(|w<rsub|\<lambda\>,j>,z<rsub|\<lambda\>,j>|)><rsub|j>|)>
  </equation*>

  when the combined configuration is separated, and assign value zero
  otherwise. This convention gives a function everywhere; every asymptotic
  use below is in its separated region. Define

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-raw-coefficient>c<rsub|\<alpha\>><rsup|<math-up|raw>>*<around|(|\<b-x\>,\<b-v\>;o|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>\<in\><math-up|supp><space|0.17em>b<rsub|\<alpha\>>>b<rsub|\<alpha\>,\<lambda\>>*\<cal-R\><rsub|\<lambda\>>*<around|(|\<b-x\>,\<b-v\>;o|)>,
  </equation>

  and the finite field-valued expression

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-raw-shadow>H<rsub|n>*<around|(|\<b-x\>,\<b-v\>;o|)>=<big|sum><rsub|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>n>c<rsub|\<alpha\>><rsup|<math-up|raw>>*<around|(|\<b-x\>,\<b-v\>;o|)>*\<iota\><rsub|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>,n>*u<rsub|\<alpha\>>.
  </equation>

  Each coefficient is fixed independently of the truncation degree, the
  phase, or any later choice of exterior spectators. It is multilinear in the
  cluster labels on its separated region. The separation condition depends
  only on positions, so the zero extension is multilinear as well.
  Permutation symmetry of the correlations gives simultaneous permutation
  symmetry in cluster positions and labels. Simultaneous translation of the
  cluster and center leaves their differences unchanged.

  For a single field <math|v\<in\>E<rsub|m>> placed at <math|o>, translation
  invariance and the definition of the exterior test give

  <\equation*>
    c<rsub|\<alpha\>><rsup|<math-up|raw>>*<around|(|<around|(|o|)>,<around|(|v|)>;o|)>=b<rsub|\<alpha\>,m><around|(|v|)>.
  </equation*>

  All the detector configurations in this evaluation are separated. Hence,
  for <math|m\<leqslant\>n>, <eqref|eq:ope-rebuild> implies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-raw-single>H<rsub|n>*<around|(|<around|(|o|)>,<around|(|v|)>;o|)>=\<iota\><rsub|m*n>*v.
  </equation>

  The same testing operation is compatible with replacing any input field by
  its filtration image, since separated correlations have that compatibility.

  For every fixed finite list of coefficients there is a common positive
  detector clearance. To see this, take the minimum of the finitely many
  nonzero lengths <math|<around|\||z<rsub|\<lambda\>,j>|\|>> occurring in
  their finite test supports; if there are no spectator positions, there is
  no such restriction. If <math|<around|\||x<rsub|i>-o|\|>> is less than half
  that minimum, no cluster point can meet a detector position. Distinctness
  among cluster points and among detector points supplies the remaining
  separation conditions. The same argument works after any orthogonal
  rotation of the relative cluster, because rotations preserve
  <math|<around|\||x<rsub|i>-o|\|>>. Smoothness of the mixed correlations on
  separated configurations makes each such finite coefficient collection
  smooth on this common region. This is a finite-degree statement; it gives
  no uniform clearance for infinitely many degrees.

  The raw expressions above do not yet have the required rotational and
  reality properties, because the finite tests were chosen by linear algebra.
  Averaging the field-valued expression over <math|O<around|(|4|)>> and
  symmetrizing conjugation supplies those properties. The subsequent
  homogeneous projection must be made using these averaged tests themselves,
  so that its lower-degree components agree exactly. These operations and
  their quantitative error estimates are the next steps of the OPE
  construction.

  <subsection|Covariant homogeneous OPE coefficients><label|sec:ope-homogeneous>

  The finite tests of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-prescription> are
  chosen for exact compatibility, without imposing rotational symmetry. We
  first average their field-valued output, retaining both the input and
  output rotations. We then construct homogeneous complements using these
  averaged tests. This order ensures that the final coefficients have
  compatible finite truncations as well as the required symmetries.

  Write <math|R<rsub|n><around|(|K|)>> for the continuous
  <math|O<around|(|4|)>> action on <math|E<rsub|n>> and <math|J<rsub|n>> for
  complex conjugation of fields. Assume, as supplied by the field structure,
  that these actions commute with the inclusions, that <math|J<rsub|n>> is an
  antilinear involution, and that <math|J<rsub|n>*R<rsub|n><around|(|K|)>=R<rsub|n><around|(|K|)>*J<rsub|n>>.
  Assume moreover that the mixed correlations are Euclidean covariant and
  real on separated configurations: for every finite label list
  <math|\<b-v\>>, every separated position list <math|\<b-x\>>, every
  <math|K\<in\>O<around|(|4|)>> and every <math|s\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-mixed-covariance>S<rsub|<math-up|mix>>*<around|(|R<around|(|K|)>*\<b-v\>;K*\<b-x\>|)>=S<rsub|<math-up|mix>>*<around|(|\<b-v\>;\<b-x\>|)>,<space|2em>S<rsub|<math-up|mix>>*<around|(|\<b-v\>;\<b-x\>+s|)>=S<rsub|<math-up|mix>>*<around|(|\<b-v\>;\<b-x\>|)>,<space|2em>S<rsub|<math-up|mix>>*<around|(|J*\<b-v\>;\<b-x\>|)>=<wide|S<rsub|<math-up|mix>>*<around|(|\<b-v\>;\<b-x\>|)>|\<bar\>>,
  </equation>

  where <math|R<around|(|K|)>> and <math|J> act on each label with its own
  degree. These laws are not consequences of the preceding assumptions on the
  actions: they are properties of the correlations and are inputs to this
  section; they are to be supplied, for the constructed correlations on the
  same hierarchy, by the producer of the Euclidean field structure.
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-estimates> uses all three. Haar measure
  <math|d*K> on <math|O<around|(|4|)>> is normalized to have mass one.

  <subsubsection|Averaging on a common separated region>

  For a fixed degree <math|n>, let <math|\<cal-U\><rsub|n>> be the set of
  cluster configurations <math|<around|(|\<b-x\>,o|)>> for which every
  exterior test occurring through degree <math|n> remains separated from the
  relative cluster after every orthogonal rotation. This is an open set.
  Indeed, for a fixed finite test, separation is an open condition in the
  configuration and the rotation. If it holds for every rotation at a
  configuration, compactness of <math|O<around|(|4|)>> supplies finitely many
  rotation neighborhoods and a common configuration neighborhood on which it
  still holds. Intersect over the finitely many tests. The set is invariant
  under simultaneous translations, rotations and permutations of the cluster.
  It contains every sufficiently small separated cluster around its center,
  by the clearance argument of the preceding section.

  On <math|\<cal-U\><rsub|n>> define

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-average>A<rsub|n>*<around|(|\<b-x\>,\<b-v\>;o|)>=<big|int><rsub|O<around|(|4|)>>R<rsub|n><around|(|K|)>*H<rsub|n>*<around|(|K<rsup|-1>*\<b-x\>,R<around|(|K<rsup|-1>|)>*\<b-v\>;K<rsup|-1>*o|)>*<space|0.17em>d*K,
  </equation>

  where the action on each input label uses its own degree. Set
  <math|A<rsub|n>=0> outside <math|\<cal-U\><rsub|n>>. The integrand is
  continuous in <math|K>, takes values in a finite-dimensional space, and is
  therefore integrable on the compact group. Multilinearity passes through
  the integral; the zero extension preserves it because the domain condition
  is independent of labels.

  The map <math|A<rsub|n>> is smooth in positions on
  <math|\<cal-U\><rsub|n>>. Here one must not assume differentiability of the
  representation in <math|K> to justify position derivatives. Expand the
  input labels and output in finite bases. Each matrix entry of
  <math|R<around|(|K|)>> is continuous and bounded on the compact group. The
  remaining factors are derivatives in positions of separated correlations
  evaluated at rotated coordinates. On a sufficiently small closed
  configuration neighborhood times <math|O<around|(|4|)>>, all finitely many
  separations have a positive minimum. The correlation derivatives of each
  fixed order are therefore continuous and bounded on this compact set. The
  chain rule contributes only bounded entries of the rotation matrix. Thus
  each position derivative has a uniform integrable bound. Differentiation
  under the integral, repeated for every finite order, proves smoothness. The
  same finite-basis expansion justifies smooth variation of labels when
  needed.

  Left invariance of Haar measure gives rotation covariance. For a fixed
  <math|L\<in\>O<around|(|4|)>> substitute <math|K=L*H> in
  <math|A<rsub|n>*<around|(|L*\<b-x\>,R<around|(|L|)>*\<b-v\>;L*o|)>>. Since
  <math|<around|(|L*H|)><rsup|-1>*L=H<rsup|-1>>, the integrand becomes
  <math|R<rsub|n><around|(|L|)>> times the integrand in
  <eqref|eq:ope-average>. Hence

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|n>*<around|(|L*\<b-x\>,R<around|(|L|)>*\<b-v\>;L*o|)>=R<rsub|n><around|(|L|)>*A<rsub|n>*<around|(|\<b-x\>,\<b-v\>;o|)>.
  </equation*>

  This also holds outside the domain by its invariance. Translation and
  permutation identities follow directly from the corresponding identities
  for <math|H<rsub|n>> inside the integral. For a single field
  <math|v\<in\>E<rsub|q>> at its center, with <math|q\<leqslant\>n>,
  <eqref|eq:ope-raw-single> shows that the integrand is the constant
  <math|R<rsub|n><around|(|K|)>*\<iota\><rsub|q*n>*R<rsub|q><around|(|K<rsup|-1>|)>*v=\<iota\><rsub|q*n>*v>.
  Therefore <math|A<rsub|n>*<around|(|<around|(|o|)>,<around|(|v|)>;o|)>=\<iota\><rsub|q*n>*v>.

  Now define the real-symmetric readout

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-real-average>C<rsub|n>*<around|(|\<b-x\>,\<b-v\>;o|)>=<tfrac|1|2><around*|(|A<rsub|n>*<around|(|\<b-x\>,\<b-v\>;o|)>+J<rsub|n>*A<rsub|n>*<around|(|\<b-x\>,J*\<b-v\>;o|)>|)>.
  </equation>

  It is still complex multilinear: an input scalar is conjugated by the input
  <math|J> and conjugated back by the output <math|J<rsub|n>>. It is smooth
  on <math|\<cal-U\><rsub|n>>, has the same translation, permutation and
  rotation identities, and satisfies

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|n>*<around|(|\<b-x\>,J*\<b-v\>;o|)>=J<rsub|n>*C<rsub|n>*<around|(|\<b-x\>,\<b-v\>;o|)>,<space|2em>C<rsub|n>*<around|(|<around|(|o|)>,<around|(|v|)>;o|)>=\<iota\><rsub|q*n>*v*<space|1em><around|(|q\<leqslant\>n|)>.
  </equation*>

  The first identity uses <math|J<rsup|2>=I> and exchanges the two summands.
  The second follows from the corresponding identity for <math|A<rsub|n>> and
  compatibility of <math|J> with inclusions.

  <subsubsection|Compatible projections onto new degrees>

  For every pair <math|m,q\<geqslant\>0>, define a linear map

  <\equation*>
    L<rsub|m*q>:E<rsub|q>\<to\>E<rsub|m>,<space|2em>L<rsub|m*q>*v=C<rsub|m>*<around|(|<around|(|0|)>,<around|(|v|)>;0|)>.
  </equation*>

  The centered single-field configuration always belongs to
  <math|\<cal-U\><rsub|m>>. The preceding identities give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-single-compat>L<rsub|m*q>=\<iota\><rsub|q*m>*<around|(|q\<leqslant\>m|)>,<space|2em>L<rsub|m*q>*\<iota\><rsub|r*q>=L<rsub|m*r>*<around|(|r\<leqslant\>q|)>.
  </equation>

  For the second equality, every exterior correlation used in the raw tests
  is unchanged under the physical inclusion. This equality passes through
  rotation averaging, since the inclusions commute with rotations, and
  through conjugation averaging for the same reason. It holds even when
  <math|m\<less\>r>; no extension of a low-degree inverse is being assumed.
  The maps <math|L<rsub|m*q>> intertwine rotations and conjugation as well.

  Define recursively

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|m>=I<rsub|E<rsub|m>>-<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>m>\<iota\><rsub|i*m>*K<rsub|i>*L<rsub|i*m>,<space|2em>P<rsub|m*q>=K<rsub|m>*L<rsub|m*q>,<space|2em>V<rsub|m>=<big|cap><rsub|i\<less\>m>ker
    P<rsub|i*m>.
  </equation*>

  The empty sum makes <math|K<rsub|0>=I>. These are finite sums of fixed
  linear maps. From <eqref|eq:ope-single-compat>, for <math|q\<leqslant\>m>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-projection-recursion>P<rsub|m*q>*v=\<iota\><rsub|q*m>*v-<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>m>\<iota\><rsub|i*m>*P<rsub|i*q>*v,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|i\<leqslant\>m>\<iota\><rsub|i*m>*P<rsub|i*q>*v=\<iota\><rsub|q*m>*v.
  </equation>

  Also <math|P<rsub|m*q>*\<iota\><rsub|r*q>=P<rsub|m*r>> whenever
  <math|r\<leqslant\>q>. If <math|q\<less\>m>, apply the second identity at
  degree <math|m-1> and then include in <math|E<rsub|m>>. Its sum is the one
  subtracted in the first identity, so

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-higher-kills>P<rsub|m*q>=0*<space|1em><around|(|q\<less\>m|)>.
  </equation>

  For <math|v\<in\>V<rsub|m>>, the recursion with <math|q=m> gives
  <math|P<rsub|m*m>*v=v>.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lem:ope-homogeneous-projections>The range of <math|K<rsub|m>> is
    contained in <math|V<rsub|m>>. If <math|v\<in\>V<rsub|j>>, then
    <math|P<rsub|m*j>*v=\<delta\><rsub|m*j>*v>, interpreting the right side
    as zero when <math|m\<neq\>j>. Consequently

    <\equation*>
      E<rsub|m>=<big|oplus><rsub|j\<leqslant\>m>\<iota\><rsub|j*m>*V<rsub|j>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    We prove the range assertion by induction on <math|m>. Fix
    <math|i\<less\>m> and apply <math|P<rsub|i*m>> to the defining formula
    for <math|K<rsub|m>*x>. Compatibility gives

    <\equation*>
      P<rsub|i*m>*K<rsub|m>*x=P<rsub|i*m>*x-<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>m>P<rsub|i*j>*<around|(|K<rsub|j>*L<rsub|j*m>*x|)>.
    </equation*>

    By the induction hypothesis <math|K<rsub|j>*L<rsub|j*m>*x\<in\>V<rsub|j>>.
    If <math|i\<less\>j>, its image under <math|P<rsub|i*j>> is zero by the
    definition of <math|V<rsub|j>>; if <math|i\<gtr\>j>, it is zero by
    <eqref|eq:ope-higher-kills>. At <math|i=j>, the identity on
    <math|V<rsub|i>> proved above makes the term
    <math|K<rsub|i>*L<rsub|i*m>*x=P<rsub|i*m>*x>. The sum therefore cancels
    the first term. This proves <math|K<rsub|m>*x\<in\>V<rsub|m>>. The
    asserted action on <math|V<rsub|j>> follows from the same three cases.

    Take <math|q=m> in <eqref|eq:ope-projection-recursion>. Each
    <math|P<rsub|j*m>*x=K<rsub|j>*L<rsub|j*m>*x> belongs to <math|V<rsub|j>>,
    so this identity proves spanning. If <math|<big|sum><rsub|j\<leqslant\>m>\<iota\><rsub|j*m>*v<rsub|j>=0>
    with <math|v<rsub|j>\<in\>V<rsub|j>>, apply <math|P<rsub|k*m>> and use
    inclusion compatibility. All terms vanish except <math|v<rsub|k>>. Thus
    every <math|v<rsub|k>=0>, proving directness.
  </proof>

  Induction in the formula for <math|K<rsub|m>> shows that it commutes with
  rotations and conjugation: the inclusions and <math|L<rsub|i*m>> intertwine
  them, and the previous <math|K<rsub|i>> do so by induction. It follows that
  <math|V<rsub|m>> is invariant under both operations, since all its defining
  tests intertwine them. No claim that the original arbitrary quotient lifts
  were covariant was needed.

  Define the homogeneous output at degree <math|m> by

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|m>*<around|(|\<b-x\>,\<b-v\>;o|)>=K<rsub|m>*C<rsub|m>*<around|(|\<b-x\>,\<b-v\>;o|)>\<in\>V<rsub|m>.
  </equation*>

  It is multilinear, has the same symmetries as <math|C<rsub|m>>, and is
  smooth on <math|\<cal-U\><rsub|m>>. At a single centered field
  <math|v\<in\>E<rsub|q>> it equals <math|P<rsub|m*q>*v>. In particular the
  finite packet

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-P\><rsub|D>*<around|(|\<b-x\>,\<b-v\>;o|)>=<big|sum><rsub|m\<leqslant\>D>\<iota\><rsub|m*D>*F<rsub|m>*<around|(|\<b-x\>,\<b-v\>;o|)>
  </equation*>

  satisfies the exact compatibility

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-packet-projection>P<rsub|j*D>*\<cal-P\><rsub|D>=F<rsub|j>*<space|2em><around|(|j\<leqslant\>D|)>,
  </equation>

  by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ope-homogeneous-projections>. On one centered
  field of degree <math|q\<leqslant\>D>, the packet equals
  <math|\<iota\><rsub|q*D>*v> by <eqref|eq:ope-projection-recursion>.

  <subsubsection|Real channel bases and scalar coefficients>

  The conjugation <math|J<rsub|m>> restricts to an antilinear involution of
  <math|V<rsub|m>>. Its fixed real vector space has a real basis
  <math|<around|(|e<rsub|m,\<alpha\>>|)>>; this is also a complex basis of
  <math|V<rsub|m>>. Indeed every <math|v\<in\>V<rsub|m>> decomposes as

  <\equation*>
    v=<frac|v+J<rsub|m>*v|2>+i*<frac|v-J<rsub|m>*v|2*i>,
  </equation*>

  and both displayed components are fixed by <math|J<rsub|m>>. This proves
  complex spanning. If <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>z<rsub|\<alpha\>>*e<rsub|m,\<alpha\>>=0>,
  applying <math|J<rsub|m>> and then adding and subtracting the two equations
  shows separately that the real and imaginary coefficient sums vanish. Real
  linear independence forces every <math|z<rsub|\<alpha\>>=0>.

  Choose these bases once for every <math|m> and write

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-final-coefficients>F<rsub|m>*<around|(|\<b-x\>,\<b-v\>;o|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>c<rsub|\<b-v\>><rsup|m,\<alpha\>>*<around|(|\<b-x\>;o|)>*e<rsub|m,\<alpha\>>.
  </equation>

  The coordinate functionals are continuous because the spaces are finite
  dimensional. Thus the coefficients inherit smoothness, multilinearity,
  translation and permutation symmetry; reality gives conjugation of their
  values under conjugation of all input labels. Rotation covariance holds for
  the field-valued output, or equivalently through the finite matrix of
  <math|R<rsub|m>> on this basis. The individual basis vectors need not be
  rotation invariant.

  Let <math|\<ell\><rsub|\<chi\>,m,o,\<b-y\>,\<b-w\>>> be the linear
  functional obtained by inserting an <math|E<rsub|m>> field at <math|o>
  alongside separated spectators, in any phase <math|\<chi\>> or in the mixed
  state. Physical inclusion compatibility gives
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|\<chi\>,D>*\<iota\><rsub|m*D>=\<ell\><rsub|\<chi\>,m>>.
  Therefore evaluation of the packet is exactly

  <\equation*>
    \<ell\><rsub|\<chi\>,D><around|(|\<cal-P\><rsub|D>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|m\<leqslant\>D><big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>c<rsub|\<b-v\>><rsup|m,\<alpha\>>*<around|(|\<b-x\>;o|)>*S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|e<rsub|m,\<alpha\>>,\<b-w\>;o,\<b-y\>|)>.
  </equation*>

  This identifies the final scalar coefficients with one fixed covariant
  field prescription, independent of phase, spectators and truncation. It
  does not yet estimate the difference from the original cluster correlation.
  That quantitative difference is the next proof obligation.

  <subsection|Centered bounds, phase prices, and matched
  discrepancies><label|sec:ope-phase-prices>

  The raw OPE estimates must hold in every character phase, including phases
  of zero measure for the original mixture. An estimate of the mixed-state
  expectation alone does not give this. We prove the required transfer by
  bounding all even moments of a compressed whole-word operator. The cutoff
  argument keeps a matched discrepancy as a single observable throughout; it
  never replaces its small price by separate prices for its two terms.

  We use the finite-volume planted inequality and the change-of-cell root
  estimate established in Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-estimates>.
  In the latter, a centered observable whose root norm is at most <math|B>
  can be moved, with the corresponding reflection or conjugation, into a
  coarser cell at a margin of three of its original cell spans, still with
  root norm at most <math|B>. The proof uses the entire reflected copy orbit
  and coordinatewise log convexity. These statements apply to any observable
  satisfying the centered interior and root conditions, including a
  difference of two observables.

  <subsubsection|A mixed two-scale chessboard estimate>

  Let <math|W<rsub|0>,W> be aligned link tilings of a finite torus, with the
  side of <math|W<rsub|0>> dividing that of <math|W>. Write
  <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|W>> for the root norm. Suppose fine-group observables
  <math|F<rsub|j>> have centered prices <math|A<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>0> at a
  span dividing the side of <math|W<rsub|0>>, and their positions
  <math|u<rsub|j>> lie in the required three-span margins of
  <math|W<rsub|0>>. Suppose further that

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|dist><rsub|\<infty\>><around|(|u<rsub|j>,u<rsub|j<rprime|'>>|)>\<gtr\><math-up|side><around|(|W<rsub|0>|)>*<space|1em><around|(|j\<ne\>j<rprime|'>|)>.
  </equation*>

  For one fixed cell <math|C> of <math|W>, collect the fine-group observables
  lying in <math|C>. Their product is a copy in <math|C> of an interior
  observable <math|Z<rsub|C>>, and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-exterior-root>\<cal-R\><rsub|W><around|(|Z<rsub|C>|)>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|u<rsub|j>\<in\>C>A<rsub|j>.
  </equation>

  Indeed pull each factor back to the home cell of <math|W>, with the
  reflection and conjugation determined by <math|C>. Now copy this product
  into every cell of <math|W>. The copied positions still lie in the
  corresponding fine-tiling margins, since the tilings are aligned and their
  sides divide. They occupy distinct cells of <math|W<rsub|0>>. Points copied
  into different cells of <math|W> cannot share a fine cell; within one
  coarse cell, reflection preserves distances, and the displayed separation
  prevents sharing a fine cell. The planted inequality on <math|W<rsub|0>>
  therefore bounds the full copied-product expectation by

  <\equation*>
    <around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|u<rsub|j>\<in\>C>A<rsub|j>|)><rsup|#<math-up|Cell><around|(|W|)>>.
  </equation*>

  Taking the defining root proves <eqref|eq:ope-exterior-root>. Interiority
  of the home product follows by multiplying interior factors.

  Let a second group <math|G<rsub|i>> have centered prices
  <math|B<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0> at spans dividing the side of <math|W>, with
  positions <math|v<rsub|i>> in the corresponding margins of <math|W>. Assume
  their mutual distances, and their distances to all <math|u<rsub|j>>, exceed
  <math|<math-up|side><around|(|W|)>>. The <math|G<rsub|i>> then occupy
  distinct coarse cells, and none of those cells contains an
  <math|F<rsub|j>>. Apply the planted inequality on <math|W> to the
  individual <math|G<rsub|i>> and the products <math|Z<rsub|C>>. This gives

  <\equation>
    <around*|\<nobracket\>|<around*|\<nobracket\>|<label|eq:ope-mixed-plant><around*|\||\<bbb-E\><around*|[|<big|prod><rsub|i>G<rsub|i>|(>\<cdot\>+v<rsub|i>|)><big|prod><rsub|j>F<rsub|j>*<around|(|\<cdummy\>+u<rsub|j>|)>|]>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|i>B<rsub|i>*<big|prod><rsub|j>A<rsub|j>.
  </equation>

  Grouping by cells loses no factors: the disjoint groups of indices
  <math|<around|{|j:u<rsub|j>\<in\>C|}>> partition the fine-group list. Empty
  groups contribute one.

  Here are sufficient geometric conditions for placing both groups. Use
  depths <math|b\<leqslant\>f\<leqslant\>l\<leqslant\>n> and
  <math|f\<leqslant\>q>. The coarse tiling is at depth <math|b>, the fine
  tiling at depth <math|l>, the centered prices for the coarse group at depth
  <math|q>, and those for the fine group at depth <math|n>. If the group
  sizes are <math|I<rsub|0>,J<rsub|0>> and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-two-scale-room>10*I<rsub|0>*a<rsub|f>\<less\>a<rsub|b>,<space|2em>6*J<rsub|0>*a<rsub|n>\<less\>a<rsub|l>,
  </equation>

  there is a common origin for both tilings placing all coarse-group points
  at margin <math|3*a<rsub|q>> and all fine-group points at margin
  <math|3*a<rsub|n>>. To prove this, first choose the coarse origin
  coordinate by coordinate. For each point, origins giving distance less than
  <math|5*a<rsub|f>> from a cell boundary occupy an interval of length at
  most <math|10*a<rsub|f>> in the circle of possible origins modulo
  <math|a<rsub|b>>. The first inequality leaves an allowed origin. Next
  choose the fine origin modulo <math|a<rsub|l>> avoiding the intervals of
  total length at most <math|6*J<rsub|0>*a<rsub|n>> forbidden by the
  fine-group points. It can be reached from the chosen coarse origin by a
  shift of coordinate size at most <math|a<rsub|l>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|f>>.
  This shift decreases the coarse margins by at most <math|2*a<rsub|f>> even
  with the endpoint conventions for link cells, leaving
  <math|3*a<rsub|f>\<geqslant\>3*a<rsub|q>>. On the finite lattice, if a cell
  side is <math|B> and the desired integer margin is <math|r>, a point
  forbids the stripe residues <math|0,\<ldots\>,r-1> and
  <math|B-r,\<ldots\>,B-1>: at most <math|2*r> residues of the origin modulo
  <math|B>. Summing over the points leaves a permitted origin whenever their
  total count is less than <math|B>. Multiplying
  <eqref|eq:ope-two-scale-room> by <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|-1>> gives precisely
  these strict inequalities, first with margin <math|5*a<rsub|f>/a<rsub|N>>,
  then with margin <math|3*a<rsub|n>/a<rsub|N>>. To attain the permitted fine
  origin, choose a common lattice shift with
  <math|0\<leqslant\>s<rsub|\<mu\>>\<less\>a<rsub|l>/a<rsub|N>> in each
  coordinate. The coarse <math|5*a<rsub|f>/a<rsub|N>> margin loses at most
  <math|2*a<rsub|f>/a<rsub|N>>, leaving the required three spans. Nested mesh
  divisibility makes all sides and margins integers.

  A Euclidean separation greater than <math|2*a<rsub|b>> implies a
  maximum-coordinate separation greater than <math|a<rsub|b>> in four
  dimensions; likewise for <math|a<rsub|l>>. For fixed physical grid points
  these inequalities persist on the periodic lattice for all sufficiently
  large periods: their finitely many coordinate differences then do not wrap.
  They persist at all finer cutoffs by the exact mesh embedding. Thus
  Euclidean separation at <math|2*a<rsub|b>> within and across the coarse
  group, and at <math|2*a<rsub|l>> within the fine group, suffices for
  <eqref|eq:ope-mixed-plant> on the ordered cutoff tail.

  <subsubsection|Even replicas and the norm in the ground algebra>

  Let <math|Z> be a finite linear combination of products of local fields, at
  fixed coordinate-generic grid configurations. Write <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
  for the same linear combination of their compressed whole-word operators.
  The cutoff version <math|Z<rsub|N,p>> may use labels converging in their
  fixed finite-dimensional label spaces along a nontrivial refinement of the
  ordered cutoff limit.

  Suppose each cutoff <math|Z<rsub|N,p>> factors as the product of two groups
  as in <eqref|eq:ope-mixed-plant>, with the same centered price bounds and
  geometry. The statement includes a group consisting of a single matched
  discrepancy. Set <math|C=<big|prod><rsub|i>B<rsub|i>*<big|prod><rsub|j>A<rsub|j>>.
  For each <math|m\<geqslant\>1>, translate <math|2*m> copies of
  <math|Z<rsub|N,p>> by the even coarse-cell displacements

  <\equation*>
    d<rsub|j><around|(|t|)>=2*t*j*<space|0.17em>a<rsub|b>*<around|(|1,1,1,1|)>,<space|2em>t\<in\>\<bbb-N\>,<space|1em>0\<leqslant\>j\<less\>2*m,
  </equation*>

  conjugating the copies with even <math|j>. These translations preserve all
  tilings' margins, because they are multiples of the coarse cell side, and
  this side is a multiple of the fine side. For fixed <math|m>, copies at
  distinct indices become separated in every coordinate as
  <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>>. For each sufficiently large fixed <math|t>, take
  the period large enough to avoid wrapping, and then the cutoff fine enough
  to use all the centered prices. Applying <eqref|eq:ope-mixed-plant> to the
  replicated lists gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-replica-price><around*|\||\<bbb-E\><rsub|N,p>*<big|prod><rsub|j=0><rsup|2*m-1><choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<wide|Z<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|\<cdummy\>+d<rsub|j><around|(|t|)>|)>|\<bar\>>,>|<cell|j<text|
    even>,>>|<row|<cell|Z<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|\<cdummy\>+d<rsub|j><around|(|t|)>|)>,>|<cell|j<text|
    odd>>>>>>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsup|2*m>.
  </equation>

  Only the two original arities occur in <eqref|eq:ope-two-scale-room>. Once
  an origin works for one copy, it works for all its even-cell translates.
  The period and the translation threshold may depend on <math|m>; the bound
  <math|C> does not.

  Expand the finite polynomial in <eqref|eq:ope-replica-price> and first pass
  to the ordered cutoff limit at fixed <math|t>. For sufficiently large
  <math|t> each expanded word is coordinate-generic, so the point-limit
  theorem applies. Converging labels cause no extra difficulty: in a fixed
  basis a multilinear correlation is a finite sum of basis correlations times
  products of label coordinates. The basis correlations converge and the
  coordinates converge, proving convergence of each term. This also works on
  any finer nontrivial cutoff filter. It does not assume that the moving
  labels have separate limits in the two cutoff indices.

  Next let <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>>. The conjugated copies are translated, not
  reflected, so the adjoint law <math|M*<around|(|\<theta\>*w|)>=M<around|(|w|)><rsup|\<ast\>>>
  of Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-phases>, which involves reflection, does
  not apply to them directly. We use instead the unreflected conjugation
  identity <eqref|eq:episode-conjugate>, <math|M<around|(|<wide|w|\<bar\>>|)>=M<around|(|w|)><rsup|\<ast\>>>
  for ordinary conjugation of all labels at unchanged points. As recorded
  there, it is a substantive input obtained from the ordinary-reflection
  identity <math|M*<around|(|\<rho\>*w|)>=M<around|(|w|)>> of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-ordinary-reflection>, under that
  proposition's hypotheses, on this same hierarchy; it is an input of the
  present section as well. With it, the separated-cluster limit of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-cluster-train> identifies the limit as

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,<around|(|A<rsup|\<ast\>>*A|)><rsup|m>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation*>

  For a linear combination this follows by expanding the ordered product; its
  alternating factors are exactly <math|A<rsup|\<ast\>>,A,\<ldots\>,A<rsup|\<ast\>>,A>.
  Thus

  <\equation*>
    <big|int><rsub|\<Xi\>><around|\||\<chi\><around|(|A|)>|\|><rsup|2*m>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><around|(|\<chi\>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsup|2*m>*<space|1em><around|(|m\<geqslant\>1|)>.
  </equation*>

  Full support of <math|\<mu\>> now gives
  <math|<around|\||\<chi\><around|(|A|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C> for every
  character. If one value exceeded <math|C>, continuity would give an open
  set of positive measure on which it exceeded some
  <math|C<rprime|'>\<gtr\>C>. Then <math|\<mu\><around|(|O|)><around|(|C<rprime|'>|)><rsup|2*m>\<leqslant\>C<rsup|2*m>>
  for every <math|m>, a contradiction. The same argument covers <math|C=0>.
  The isometric Gelfand transform consequently gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-ground-price><around|\<\|\|\>|A|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C.
  </equation>

  Both every character evaluation and the mixed-state functional have norm
  one, so the same bound holds for their evaluations of <math|A>.

  <subsubsection|Removing coordinate ties>

  To extend <eqref|eq:ope-ground-price> to arbitrary separated
  configurations, first approximate all their points by the generic grids of
  <eqref|eq:ope-grid-round>. Strict Euclidean separations persist. For each
  fixed character, its correlation is continuous on separated configurations
  by Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-caps>, so the generic bound passes
  to the limit. This continuity is needed for every character, not only for
  almost every character, because the conclusion is used for every character
  and along arbitrary sequences of phases; full support and the barycentre
  formula alone do not supply it. Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-caps>
  is conditional on the all-copy bound and the slot identity
  <eqref|ex-raw-slot-identity>; those remain inputs here. Taking the supremum
  over characters then proves the ground-algebra norm bound at the original
  configuration. This uses scalar continuity in each phase, not norm
  continuity of the ground-algebra-valued correlation.

  For a matched discrepancy whose cluster is already fixed and generic, only
  the spectator positions are approximated. They must remain
  coordinate-generic relative to both that cluster and the origin. Here is
  the required extension of the grid construction. Let <math|W> be the finite
  set consisting of the cluster points and the origin, <math|I<rsub|0>> the
  number of spectators (the coarse group of <eqref|eq:ope-two-scale-room>),
  and <math|K=<around|(|I<rsub|0>+1|)>*<around|(|<around|\||W|\|>+1|)>>. In
  each coordinate choose for spectator <math|j> a residue from the block

  <\equation*>
    <around|{|j*<around|(|<around|\||W|\|>+1|)>,\<ldots\>,j*<around|(|<around|\||W|\|>+1|)>+<around|\||W|\|>|}>
  </equation*>

  which avoids the residues <math|<around|\<lfloor\>|w<rsub|\<mu\>>/h|\<rfloor\>><bmod>K>
  for <math|w\<in\>W>. There are <math|<around|\||W|\|>+1> candidates and at
  most <math|<around|\||W|\|>> forbidden residues. The blocks are disjoint,
  so the chosen residues distinguish all spectators. Adding the chosen
  residue to the integer <math|K*<around|\<lfloor\>|y<rsub|j,\<mu\>>/<around|(|h*K|)>|\<rfloor\>>>
  and multiplying by the mesh <math|h> gives a grid point within <math|h*K>
  in each coordinate, and it cannot equal any <math|w<rsub|\<mu\>>>. Thus
  these spectator grids converge and retain all required coordinate
  inequalities. If the new grid is finer than the fixed cluster grid,
  reexpress the cluster points on it by the integer mesh ratio; their
  physical positions and cutoff observables are unchanged. Apply the
  preceding replica argument on that common grid, then take the spectator
  approximation limit in each phase. This proves the complete-discrepancy
  bound at arbitrary separated spectator configurations.

  <subsubsection|Engineering rates from centered prices>

  We now use the centered estimate in its full quantifier order: for a fixed
  degree <math|d>, bounded set of labels and every
  <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, one constant controls the centered root price
  at all depths after the reference depth, with an eventual cutoff tail
  uniform in period and those labels. In particular a fixed field of degree
  <math|d<rsub|i>> has a price

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|i><around|(|k|)>=<around|(|1+C<rsub|i>|)>*a<rsub|k><rsup|-d<rsub|i>-\<epsilon\>>+1
  </equation*>

  for a suitable <math|C<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>. If
  <math|s<rsub|k<rsub|n>>/t<rsub|n>\<to\>u\<geqslant\>0>, this positive price
  has logarithmic rate <math|<around|(|d<rsub|i>+\<epsilon\>|)>*u>. For a
  finite group the product therefore has rate
  <math|<around|(|<big|sum><rsub|i>d<rsub|i>+\<epsilon\>#I|)>*u>.

  More generally, suppose two groups have mutual separations at upper scales
  <math|0\<leqslant\>a\<leqslant\>b>, and cross separation at scale <math|a>.
  This means that for every positive slack the corresponding distances
  eventually exceed <math|e<rsup|-<around|(|a+\<delta\>|)>*t<rsub|n>>> or
  <math|e<rsup|-<around|(|b+\<delta\>|)>*t<rsub|n>>>. Choose mesh depths of
  logarithmic rates

  <\equation*>
    a+\<delta\>/3,<space|1em>a+\<delta\>/2,<space|1em>a+\<delta\>,<space|1em>b+3*\<delta\>/4,<space|1em>b+\<delta\>
  </equation*>

  for the coarse placement, buffer, coarse-group price, fine placement, and
  fine-group price respectively. The inequalities of the rates imply all
  depth orderings and <eqref|eq:ope-two-scale-room> eventually. The
  separation assumptions imply the required strict gaps at the placement
  meshes. Let <math|w<rsub|1>> and <math|w<rsub|2>> be the total field
  degrees of the coarse group (separated at scale <math|a>, of size
  <math|I<rsub|0>>) and of the fine group (separated at scale <math|b>, of
  size <math|J<rsub|0>>). The bounds just proved yield ground-algebra upper
  rate

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|w<rsub|1>+\<epsilon\>*I<rsub|0>|)>*<around|(|a+\<delta\>|)>+<around|(|w<rsub|2>+\<epsilon\>*J<rsub|0>|)>*<around|(|b+\<delta\>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|\<epsilon\>,\<delta\>*\<downarrow\>*0> by allocating half of any
  desired rate slack to these choices and half to the eventual estimate. The
  result is the upper rate <math|w<rsub|1>*a+w<rsub|2>*b>, uniformly along
  every sequence of phases, because the ground-algebra norm controls them
  all.

  For the OPE, take the fixed-clearance spectators as the first group
  (<math|a=0>) and the scale-<math|x> cluster as the second (<math|b=x>).
  Their cross clearance is fixed for all sufficiently large indices. This
  proves the rough rate <math|x*w<around|(|p|)>> used in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-grid>. Taking every group at scale zero
  proves rate zero for the fixed single-field spectator insertion in
  Input<nbsp><reference|input:ope-raw-estimates>.

  Finally suppose a cutoff matched discrepancy is centered and priced by
  <math|A<rsub|n>> at a depth of logarithmic rate <math|b\<gtr\>0>, and its
  coefficient label converges along a refinement of the same ordered cutoff
  limit. Assume <math|A<rsub|n>> has upper rate <math|\<gamma\>>. Choose, for
  any small <math|0\<less\>\<delta\>\<less\>b>, placement, buffer,
  fine-placement and spectator-price depths of rates
  <math|\<delta\>/4,\<delta\>/2,3*\<delta\>/4,\<delta\>>. The centered
  discrepancy price at rate <math|b> fits this geometry.
  Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:ope-ground-price> and the spectator approximation
  give, with the spectators as the coarse group (total degree
  <math|w<rsub|1>>, number <math|I<rsub|0>>) and the discrepancy as the
  single-member fine group, upper rate at most
  <math|\<gamma\>+<around|(|w<rsub|1>+\<epsilon\>*I<rsub|0>|)>*\<delta\>> for
  the entire discrepancy, in every phase and at arbitrary fixed-clearance
  spectator positions. Sending <math|\<delta\>*\<downarrow\>*0> proves rate
  <math|\<gamma\>>. Thus the finite-cutoff match supplies exactly the
  complete-discrepancy part of Input<nbsp><reference|input:ope-match>, once
  its price and convergent label are constructed. Neither this transfer nor
  the even-moment argument creates that finite-cutoff match.

  <subsection|Triangular transport and the physical
  filtration><label|sec:ope-filtration>

  The distance estimate in <eqref|eq:ope-match-distance> concerns the
  physical filtration. A bare coefficient of low engineering degree need not
  stay in the corresponding coordinate plane under renormalization. We first
  estimate its transported plane, then identify limits of that plane by their
  correlations. The two parts use different information: triangular estimates
  for the first, and the comparison of local observables for the second.

  <subsubsection|Expansion of the low coordinates>

  Fix a degree <math|d>, its finite coefficient space <math|F>, and an
  integer <math|0\<leqslant\>e\<less\>d>. Each coordinate has an engineering
  weight in <math|<around|{|0,\<ldots\>,d|}>>. Write
  <math|\<mu\><around|(|v|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>><around|\||v<rsub|\<alpha\>>|\|>>
  and let <math|P> retain the coordinates of weight at most <math|e>. For a
  block ratio <math|L\<geqslant\>1>, let

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|D<rsub|L>*v|)><rsub|\<alpha\>>=L<rsup|d-deg
    \<alpha\>>*v<rsub|\<alpha\>>.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|P> commutes with <math|D<rsub|L>> and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-dilation-split>\<mu\>*<around|(|P*D<rsub|L>*v|)>\<geqslant\>L<rsup|d-e>*\<mu\>*<around|(|P*v|)>,<space|2em>\<mu\>*<around|(|<around|(|1-P|)>*D<rsub|L>*v|)>\<leqslant\>L<rsup|d-e-1>*\<mu\><around|(|v|)>.
  </equation>

  Both inequalities follow by summing the coordinate inequalities.

  Here is the exact one-step input needed from the calibrated construction.
  At consecutive levels <math|i+1,i>, the readouts of the same bare vector
  are <math|a,b>. There is an invertible linear map <math|B> preserving
  <math|ker P>, with

  <\equation*>
    \<mu\>*<around|(|B*v|)>,\<mu\>*<around|(|B<rsup|-1>*v|)>\<leqslant\>K*\<mu\><around|(|v|)>,<space|2em>\<mu\>*<around|(|b-B*D<rsub|L>*a|)>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>*\<mu\><around|(|a|)>.
  </equation*>

  The last estimate follows in the construction from the stronger bound with
  the nonconstant-coordinate part of <math|a> on the right. Its numerical
  conditions are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-triangle-numbers>K\<geqslant\>1,<space|1em>s<rsub|0>,s\<geqslant\>64*K<rsup|2>,<space|1em>s\<leqslant\>L,<space|1em>\<epsilon\>\<geqslant\>0,<space|1em>2*K*\<epsilon\>*<around|(|1+s|)>\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation>

  Here <math|s<rsub|0>=\<zeta\><rsub|i>> and <math|s=\<zeta\><rsub|i+1>> are
  the column allowances. These triangular estimates concern the actual
  readouts, including their nonlinear corrections. On the selected family
  their hypotheses follow from the paid heading and the stored backward
  transfer, as follows.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The triangular step on the selected
    family><label|lem:ope-selected-triangle>Use the reference-normalized
    transfer of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-c02-selected-record> at
    grade <math|d> and an adjacent step above its reference level. Its
    consecutive readouts satisfy the preceding one-step hypotheses and
    <eqref|eq:ope-triangle-numbers> for every <math|e\<less\>d>, with the
    same readouts and heading matrix for all these <math|e>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The matrix <math|B> in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:heading-history-handoff>
    is the heading of this very transition. Its off-diagonal entry
    <math|B<rsub|b*i>> can be nonzero only when
    <math|g<rsub|i>\<less\>g<rsub|b>>; its diagonal entries are nonzero.
    Consequently a vector supported on grades greater than <math|e> is sent
    to one supported on grades greater than <math|e>. This proves
    preservation of <math|ker P>. Equation<nbsp><reference|eq:heading-both-prices>
    bounds both <math|B> and its inverse by a stage constant
    <math|K\<geqslant\>1>, which the purchased heading price <math|D>
    dominates. The heading is <math|B<math-up|vowel><rsub|L>=B*D<rsub|L>>,
    because its diagonal grade factor is <math|L<rsup|d-g<rsub|i>>> in
    coordinate <math|i>.

    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-reprise> constructs the lower
    coefficient family from the upper one using this heading and the common
    interpolation of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-selected-interpolation>.
    Its lead error is at most <math|\<epsilon\><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
    where <math|\<epsilon\>=\<eta\>*<around|(|1+s|)>*R<rsub|F>\<geqslant\>0>
    in <eqref|eq:ex-interpolation-buy>. Thus it is at most
    <math|\<epsilon\>*\<mu\><around|(|a|)>>. Reference normalization
    precomposes both readouts with the same inverse and leaves this
    inequality valid for every bare vector; it does not replace <math|B> or
    either adjacent coefficient family.

    Put <math|s<rsub|0>=C<rsub|i>> and <math|s=C<rsub|i+1>> for the two
    column allowances. The room and bank estimates of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-stored-service-budget>, as used in
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-reprise>, give

    <\equation*>
      64*D<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|0>,s,<space|2em>100000*D<rsup|6>*<around|(|1+s|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>L<rsup|1/8>.
    </equation*>

    In particular <math|K\<geqslant\>1>, <math|s<rsub|0>,s\<geqslant\>64*K<rsup|2>>,
    and <math|s\<leqslant\>L>. The interpolation purchase gives
    <math|\<epsilon\>\<leqslant\>L<rsup|-3/8>>. Put
    <math|q=L<rsup|1/8>\<geqslant\>1>. Its bank bound implies
    <math|q\<geqslant\>2*K*<around|(|1+s|)>>, and hence

    <\equation*>
      2*K*\<epsilon\>*<around|(|1+s|)>\<leqslant\>2*K*<around|(|1+s|)>*q<rsup|-3>\<leqslant\>q<rsup|-2>\<leqslant\>1.
    </equation*>

    Thus the factor <math|1+s> in the actual lead error and the further
    factor <math|1+s> in the cone condition have both been paid. These are
    all the numerical conditions in <eqref|eq:ope-triangle-numbers>. The
    construction fixes the heading, coefficient families and prices before
    <math|e> is selected, so the same bounds apply to each of the finitely
    many low-coordinate planes.
  </proof>

  Since <math|B> maps the finite-dimensional space <math|ker P> injectively
  into itself, it maps it onto itself. Its inverse therefore also preserves
  <math|ker P>. Applying this observation to <math|B*v-P*B*v> gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-low-inverse>\<mu\>*<around|(|P*v|)>=\<mu\>*<around|(|P*B<rsup|-1>*P*B*v|)>\<leqslant\>K*\<mu\>*<around|(|P*B*v|)>.
  </equation>

  Suppose now that <math|\<mu\><around|(|a|)>\<leqslant\>s*\<mu\>*<around|(|P*a|)>>,
  and set <math|Y=\<mu\>*<around|(|P*D<rsub|L>*a|)>>. By
  <eqref|eq:ope-dilation-split> and <math|s\<leqslant\>L>,

  <\equation*>
    \<mu\>*<around|(|D<rsub|L>*a|)>\<leqslant\>Y+L<rsup|d-e-1>*s*\<mu\>*<around|(|P*a|)>\<leqslant\>2*Y,<space|2em>\<mu\><around|(|a|)>\<leqslant\>s*Y.
  </equation*>

  The error <math|E=\<mu\>*<around|(|b-B*D<rsub|L>*a|)>> consequently
  satisfies <math|2*K*E\<leqslant\>Y>. On the other hand,
  <eqref|eq:ope-low-inverse> gives <math|Y\<leqslant\>K*<around|(|\<mu\>*<around|(|P*b|)>+E|)>>,
  so <math|Y\<leqslant\>2*K*\<mu\>*<around|(|P*b|)>>. It follows that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-cone-step>\<mu\><around|(|b|)>\<leqslant\>3*K*Y\<leqslant\>6*K<rsup|2>*\<mu\>*<around|(|P*b|)>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|0>*\<mu\>*<around|(|P*b|)>,<space|2em>L<rsup|d-e>*\<mu\>*<around|(|P*a|)>\<leqslant\>s*\<mu\>*<around|(|P*b|)>.
  </equation>

  If instead <math|P*a=0>, <eqref|eq:ope-dilation-split>,
  <math|\<epsilon\>\<leqslant\>1>, and <math|\<mu\><around|(|a|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\>*<around|(|D<rsub|L>*a|)>>
  give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-transverse-step>\<mu\><around|(|b|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|K+1|)>*\<mu\>*<around|(|D<rsub|L>*a|)>\<leqslant\>s*L<rsup|d-e-1>*\<mu\><around|(|a|)>.
  </equation>

  The lost power of <math|L> distinguishes the transverse estimate from the
  expansion of the low coordinates.

  <subsubsection|Subtracting the transported low plane>

  Fix a finest level <math|N>, a period, and a reference level
  <math|U\<leqslant\>N>. Let <math|T<rsub|k>:F\<to\>F> be the linear readout
  at level <math|k>, for <math|U\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>N>. Thus
  <math|T<rsub|N>=1> and <math|T<rsub|U>=K<rsub|N>>, where <math|K<rsub|N>>
  is the invertible map to reference coefficients. For each adjacent pair
  assume the preceding one-step estimates, with
  <math|a<rsub|k>=L<rsub|k>*a<rsub|k+1>> for the meshes. Define

  <\equation*>
    J<rsub|k>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<less\>k>\<zeta\><rsub|i+1>,<space|2em>H<rsub|k>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<less\>k><around|(|1+\<zeta\><rsub|i>|)>*\<zeta\><rsub|i+1>.
  </equation*>

  All these factors are at least one. If <math|P*v=v>, backward induction in
  <eqref|eq:ope-cone-step> gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-plane-expansion>\<mu\>*<around|(|T<rsub|k>*v|)>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|k>*\<mu\>*<around|(|P*T<rsub|k>*v|)>,<space|2em>J<rsub|k>*<around|(|a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|N>|)><rsup|d-e>*\<mu\><around|(|v|)>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|N>*\<mu\>*<around|(|P*T<rsub|k>*v|)>.
  </equation>

  For the second inequality, multiply the one-step lower expansion by
  <math|J<rsub|i>>, use <math|J<rsub|i+1>=\<zeta\><rsub|i+1>*J<rsub|i>>, and
  telescope the mesh ratios. The terminal equality <math|T<rsub|N>*v=v>
  starts both inductions.

  In particular <math|P*T<rsub|k>> restricted to
  <math|<math-up|ran><space|0.17em>P> is injective. Its domain and codomain
  have equal finite dimension, so it is onto. For any <math|y\<in\>F> choose
  <math|v\<in\><math-up|ran><space|0.17em>P> with
  <math|P*T<rsub|k>*v=P*T<rsub|k>*y>. The first bound in
  <eqref|eq:ope-plane-expansion> shows

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-plane-cancel>P*T<rsub|k>*<around|(|y-v|)>=0,<space|2em>\<mu\>*<around|(|T<rsub|k>*<around|(|y-v|)>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+\<zeta\><rsub|k>|)>*\<mu\>*<around|(|T<rsub|k>*y|)>.
  </equation>

  This cancellation must be repeated after each step: the fixed coordinate
  complement is not asserted to be invariant under the exact readouts. If
  <math|P*T<rsub|i+1>*y=0>, first apply <eqref|eq:ope-transverse-step>, then
  <eqref|eq:ope-plane-cancel> at level <math|i>. The cost is
  <math|<around|(|1+\<zeta\><rsub|i>|)>*\<zeta\><rsub|i+1>*L<rsub|i><rsup|d-e-1>>.
  Induction therefore gives, whenever <math|U\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>N>
  and <math|P*T<rsub|j>*y=0>, a <math|v\<in\><math-up|ran><space|0.17em>P>
  such that

  <\equation*>
    P*T<rsub|k>*<around|(|y-v|)>=0,<space|2em>H<rsub|k>*\<mu\>*<around|(|T<rsub|k>*<around|(|y-v|)>|)>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|j>*<around|(|a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|j>|)><rsup|d-e-1>*\<mu\>*<around|(|T<rsub|j>*y|)>.
  </equation*>

  At an induction step add the newly subtracted low vector to the old one;
  their sum remains in <math|<math-up|ran><space|0.17em>P>. The recurrence
  for <math|H<rsub|k>> and the identity <math|a<rsub|i>=L<rsub|i>*a<rsub|i+1>>
  give the displayed product.

  Cancel first at level <math|k>, then transport to <math|U>, and discard the
  factor <math|H<rsub|U>\<geqslant\>1>. For every reference vector <math|x>
  this yields a vector <math|y> in the transported plane
  <math|K<rsub|N><math-up|ran><space|0.17em>P> with

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-transport-distance><around|\<\|\|\>|x-y|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>F<rsub|k,d,e>*<space|0.17em>q<rsub|k><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>F<rsub|k,d,e>=H<rsub|k>*<around|(|1+\<zeta\><rsub|k>|)>*<around|(|a<rsub|U>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|d-e-1>,
  </equation>

  where <math|q<rsub|k><around|(|x|)>=\<mu\>*<around|(|T<rsub|k>*K<rsub|N><rsup|-1>*x|)>>,
  and the reference norm is chosen so that
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around|(|z|)>>. Indeed
  apply the preceding result to <math|K<rsub|N><rsup|-1>*x> and use
  <math|T<rsub|U>=K<rsub|N>>. For comparison, the calibrated bound
  <math|q<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|i+1>*L<rsub|i><rsup|d>*q<rsub|i+1><around|(|x|)>>
  gives by forward induction

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-whole-distance><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|k>*<around|(|a<rsub|U>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|d>*q<rsub|k><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation>

  At <math|U> use <math|q<rsub|U><around|(|x|)>=\<mu\><around|(|x|)>> and
  <math|J<rsub|U>\<geqslant\>1>; the recurrence for <math|J> and the mesh
  identity give each subsequent step.

  <subsubsection|Subpower calibration and smeared
  discrepancies><label|ex-column-subpower>

  The cumulative column factor used above is subpower for a precise reason.
  Put <math|s<rsub|i>=log <around|(|1/a<rsub|i>|)>\<geqslant\>0> and
  <math|C<rsub|N>=<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>N>log \<zeta\><rsub|i+1>=log
  J<rsub|N>>. The schedule provides numbers <math|h<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>
  and the bound

  <\equation*>
    0\<leqslant\>log \<zeta\><rsub|i+1>\<leqslant\>h<rsub|i><rsup|-2>*log
    L<rsub|i>,<space|2em>s<rsub|i+1>-s<rsub|i>=log L<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation*>

  The first inequality follows also when the scheduled logarithmic cost
  contains other nonnegative charges, since it then dominates <math|log
  \<zeta\><rsub|i+1>>. Given <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, choose <math|k> so
  that <math|h<rsub|i><rsup|-2>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>/2> for
  <math|i\<geqslant\>k>. Telescoping gives

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|k>+<tfrac|\<epsilon\>|2><around|(|s<rsub|N>-s<rsub|k>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|k>+<tfrac|\<epsilon\>|2>s<rsub|N>.
  </equation*>

  Eventually <math|C<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>*s<rsub|N>/2>, because
  <math|s<rsub|N>\<to\>\<infty\>>. Thus <math|C<rsub|N>=o<around|(|s<rsub|N>|)>>.
  In particular <math|J<rsub|N>> has logarithmic rate zero; so does
  <math|\<zeta\><rsub|N>>, since <math|1\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|N>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|N>>
  for <math|N\<geqslant\>1>.

  The calibrated reverse estimate is

  <\equation*>
    \<mu\>*<around|(|T<rsub|i+1>*K<rsub|N><rsup|-1>*x|)>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|i+1>*\<mu\>*<around|(|T<rsub|i>*K<rsub|N><rsup|-1>*x|)>.
  </equation*>

  Starting at <math|T<rsub|U>*K<rsub|N><rsup|-1>*x=x> and enlarging the
  product to the full prefix gives <math|\<mu\>*<around|(|T<rsub|i>*K<rsub|N><rsup|-1>*x|)>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|i>*\<mu\><around|(|x|)>>.
  At <math|i=N>, therefore,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|K<rsub|N><rsup|-1>*x|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|N>*\<mu\><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|N>*<space|0.17em>dim
    <around|(|F|)><space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>>,
  </equation*>

  using the coordinate supremum norm for the last inequality. For every fixed
  radius <math|R> and <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, both <math|J<rsub|N>> and
  <math|dim <around|(|F|)>*R> are eventually bounded by
  <math|e<rsup|\<epsilon\>*s<rsub|N>/2>>. Consequently

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-bounded-bare>sup<rsub|p>
    sup<rsub|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>R><around|\<\|\|\>|K<rsub|N,p><rsup|-1>*x|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<epsilon\>*s<rsub|N>><space|1em><text|eventually
    in >N.
  </equation>

  The same estimate applies to any bounded family along the ordered cutoff
  filter. The calibration inequalities are uniform in the period; no new
  period extraction is needed.

  We will also use the following passage from separated lattice bounds to
  smeared tests. Let a trial <math|Z<rsub|N,p>> replace one distinguished
  field in an <math|n>-point correlation, and suppose that, for each fixed
  spectator list and every <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>, its correlation has
  absolute value at most <math|A<rsub|\<delta\>><around|\||r<rsub|N,p>|\|>>
  whenever all physical pairwise separations are at least <math|\<delta\>>.
  Assume <math|r<rsub|N,p>\<to\>0> along the ordered cutoff filter. For every
  smooth test <math|\<varphi\>> compactly supported in the separated
  configuration space, the corresponding lattice sum, with the factor
  <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>>, tends to zero.

  Indeed the test support <math|K> is compact and has positive distance from
  every pairwise diagonal. Choose a smaller clearance
  <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>. For sufficiently large periods, its lattice
  representatives have that clearance also in the periodic metric: a bounded
  set does not meet a periodic translate of itself near a diagonal. Express
  the weighted lattice sum exactly as the integral of its piecewise constant
  function on the mesh cells in the fundamental region. A nonzero cell value
  has its corner in <math|K>. The cell diameter tends to zero, so every such
  cell lies in a fixed compact enlargement <math|B> of <math|K>. The absolute
  value of the step function is at most

  <\equation*>
    \<b-1\><rsub|B><space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|\<varphi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*A<rsub|\<delta\>><around|\||r<rsub|N,p>|\|>.
  </equation*>

  Its integral is bounded by <math|<around|\||B|\|><around|\<\|\|\>|\<varphi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*A<rsub|\<delta\>><around|\||r<rsub|N,p>|\|>>,
  which tends to zero. This is a norm estimate on the same ordered filter,
  not an interchange of mesh and volume limits. Coordinate ties do not cause
  a problem: the test support is separated in the Euclidean sense, and one
  separating coordinate for each pair suffices to control its periodic
  separation.

  <subsubsection|Identifying the limiting plane by tests>

  We spell out the finite-dimensional limit argument, since convergence of
  the moving planes themselves is neither needed nor asserted. Let <math|V>
  be a finite-dimensional complex normed space, <math|p<rsub|c>> projections
  converging in operator norm to a projection <math|p>, and
  <math|f<rsub|\<theta\>,c>> linear functionals converging in norm to
  <math|f<rsub|\<theta\>>>, one for each test <math|\<theta\>>. Assume

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-test-kernel>ker p=<big|cap><rsub|\<theta\>>ker
    f<rsub|\<theta\>>.
  </equation>

  The index <math|c> tends along any nontrivial filter. Suppose vectors
  <math|u<rsub|c>> satisfy, for every <math|\<theta\>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-normalized-tests>f<rsub|\<theta\>,c><around|(|u<rsub|c>|)>\<longrightarrow\>M<rsub|\<theta\>>,<space|2em><frac|f<rsub|\<theta\>,c>*<around|(|u<rsub|c>-p<rsub|c>*u<rsub|c>|)>|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|p<rsub|c>*u<rsub|c>|\<\|\|\>>>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation>

  Then there is <math|u\<in\><math-up|ran><space|0.17em>p> with
  <math|f<rsub|\<theta\>><around|(|u|)>=M<rsub|\<theta\>>> for all
  <math|\<theta\>>.

  To prove this, put <math|b<rsub|c>=<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|p<rsub|c>*u<rsub|c>|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|-1>>
  and <math|z<rsub|c>=b<rsub|c>*p<rsub|c>*u<rsub|c>>. The pairs
  <math|<around|(|b<rsub|c>,z<rsub|c>|)>> lie in the compact set
  <math|<around|[|0,1|]>\<times\><wide|B|\<bar\>><rsub|1>>. Along a finer
  nontrivial filter they converge to <math|<around|(|b,z|)>>, with
  <math|b+<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>=1>. Since
  <math|p<rsub|c>*z<rsub|c>=z<rsub|c>>, convergence of the projectors gives
  <math|p*z=z>. Equations <eqref|eq:ope-normalized-tests> imply
  <math|f<rsub|\<theta\>><around|(|z|)>=b*M<rsub|\<theta\>>>. If <math|b=0>,
  <eqref|eq:ope-test-kernel> forces <math|p*z=0>, hence <math|z=0>,
  contradicting <math|b+<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>=1>. Thus
  <math|b\<gtr\>0> and <math|u=b<rsup|-1>*z> proves the claim. There is only
  one compact extraction; the limits for every test pass to its refinement.
  No extraction indexed by the tests is used.

  Here is how the required hypotheses arise for the transported plane. A
  local trial at degree <math|e> is a family of observables
  <math|Z<rsub|N,p>>, indexed by cutoff and period along the ordered cutoff
  filter, with the following properties on the late tail of that filter. (i)
  Locality: there is one fixed finite stencil <math|X> containing the origin
  such that each <math|Z<rsub|N,p>> is a measurable gauge-invariant function
  of the link variables in the translate of <math|X> at its lattice origin,
  and the observable at any other origin is the literal translate of the one
  at the origin. (ii) Eventual continuity: <math|Z<rsub|N,p>> is continuous
  for all sufficiently late cutoffs. (iii) Two prices: there are nonnegative
  prices <math|c<rsub|N,p>> and <math|b<rsub|N,p>> with subpower upper bounds
  along that filter: for every <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0> both are eventually
  at most <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|-\<epsilon\>>>. On a common late tail,
  <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|e><around|\||Z<rsub|N,p>|\|>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|N,p>>
  everywhere, and <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|e>*Z<rsub|N,p>> satisfies the
  prescribed left-derivative bounds with constant <math|c<rsub|N,p>> on the
  healthy configurations, those at which no site of the stencil is incident
  to a plaquette whose chord distance from the identity exceeds the health
  threshold of the cutoff; no derivative bound is required on unhealthy
  configurations. In addition, at every healthy configuration <math|U>, the
  scaled trial <math|u=a<rsub|N><rsup|e>*Z<rsub|N,p>> has a
  <math|C<rsup|q<rsub|N>>> germ at zero in the full real left-increment
  variables <math|A>, namely <math|A\<mapsto\>u*<around|(|exp
  <around|(|A|)>*U|)>>, where <math|q<rsub|N>> is the purchased order. This
  condition includes configurations at equality in the health threshold. For
  every ordered list of fewer than <math|q<rsub|N>> directions and every
  further direction, the derivative at zero of the iterated left derivative
  along that increment exists and equals the recursively defined next left
  derivative. These regularity and recursive derivative conditions are
  separate from the price bounds; they are not required at unhealthy
  configurations. These bounds are uniform in the lattice origin. Here the
  derivative order, spatial weight power and direction threshold are the
  purchased cutoff-dependent parameters: the bound holds simultaneously for
  every ordered left derivative of length at most that cutoff's purchased
  order, with directions satisfying its weighted threshold. Bounds only for
  each fixed derivative order would not suffice. The local comparison
  construction supplies degree-<math|e> labels <math|u<rsub|c>> with subpower
  bare norm, whose difference from that trial has a centered price tending to
  zero. It also supplies the projections <math|p<rsub|c>\<to\>p> of
  <eqref|eq:ope-test-kernel>. Labels in <math|ker p<rsub|c>> with subpower
  bare norm have vanishing tested correlations. Bounded reference labels have
  subpower bare norm by <eqref|eq:ope-bounded-bare>. The local comparison is
  proved below from the protected local construction. The projections come
  from the equation-kernel and rank construction on this same table, as
  specified in the next paragraph. All statements use the same ordered cutoff
  filter.

  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:finite-rank-kernel-transport> supplies
  projections of this kind: its idempotents <math|A<rsub|c>> converge to
  <math|T> with <math|ker T=<big|cap><rsub|\<theta\>>ker f<rsub|\<theta\>>>,
  by <eqref|eq:finite-rank-exact-kernel>, so one may take
  <math|p<rsub|c>=A<rsub|c>> and <math|p=T> in <eqref|eq:ope-test-kernel>.
  Its vanishing statement <eqref|eq:finite-rank-silent> is displayed for
  bounded families. The argument there applies verbatim to families of
  subpower bare (inverse-calibrated) norm, since its local equation input is
  stated for such families and boundedness is used only to enter that input.
  The use here requires that subpower form, for the same projections, tests
  and filter. That section in turn rests on its own inputs: its local
  equation input, the purchased comparison
  <eqref|eq:finite-rank-purchased-matrix> and the equation count
  <eqref|eq:finite-rank-equation-count>. The local trial comparison is the
  following application of the protected insertion construction.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Comparison of a protected local
    trial><label|prop:ope-local-trial-comparison>On the same funded
    calibrated family used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-insertion-derivative-relay>,
    let <math|Z<rsub|N,p>> be a degree-<math|e> local trial satisfying
    (i)\U(iii) above. There are grade-<math|e> reference labels
    <math|u<rsub|N,p>> with subpower inverse terminal coordinates such that,
    for every fixed opened pair of depths eligible at grade <math|e>, the
    centered root price of <math|Z<rsub|N,p>-O<rsub|e><around|(|u<rsub|N,p>|)>>
    tends to zero along the ordered cutoff filter. The discrepancy tends to
    zero uniformly on each fixed separated configuration region after
    multiplying by any fixed finite spectator list. It also tends to zero
    after smearing with any compactly supported smooth separated test. The
    same labels serve every such test and all refinements of the original
    order.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Set <math|f<rsub|N,p>=a<rsub|N><rsup|e>*Z<rsub|N,p>> at the lattice
    origin. Conditions (i)\U(iii) are precisely the two protected prices and
    the local regularity required in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-insertion-derivative-relay>.
    The regularity is available through the actual purchased order, not
    merely any previously fixed finite order. Enlarge the fixed trial stencil
    by the grade-<math|e> section stencil; this set is still finite and
    independent of cutoff and period. On a late tail the purchased order is
    large enough to take the grade-<math|e> readout.

    Apply the terminal construction <eqref|eq:relay-protected-split> to this
    <math|f>. It subtracts its own leading section so that the regular
    remainder has zero readout; no zero-readout condition is imposed on the
    original trial. Its exact density is <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|e>*R<rsub|N>*Z<rsub|N,p>>,
    with the actual unmarked top representative <math|R<rsub|N>>. The total
    terminal price is <math|E<rsub|N,p>> of <eqref|eq:relay-protected-total>.
    That formula is a finite sum and product of the trial's two prices, the
    purchased column and threshold prices, the support weight, and the paid
    reserve inverse. Every one of these factors is subpower on this family,
    so <math|E<rsub|N,p>> is subpower on the same ordered filter. The actual
    top keeper supplies the off-health attachment. The trial itself is
    eventually continuous, even though individual masked pieces need not be;
    this is the continuity required in the descent.

    The descent in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-insertion-derivative-relay>
    now produces one reference label <math|u<rsub|N,p>>, with

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||X<rsub|N,p><rsup|-1>*u<rsub|N,p>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*E<rsub|N,p>,<space|2em>\<cal-R\><rsub|m,N,p>*<around|{|Z<rsub|N,p>-O<rsub|e><around|(|u<rsub|N,p>|)>|}>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|e,m,k>*E<rsub|N,p>*e<rsup|-s<rsub|N>/8>
    </equation*>

    for every fixed eligible opened pair <math|<around|(|m,k|)>> on its
    eventual tail. The label is defined by the descent before an opened pair
    or a spectator test is chosen. At excluded initial cutoffs take it to be
    zero. To verify decay, use subpower with exponent <math|1/16> and
    <math|s<rsub|N>\<to\>\<infty\>>: the last bound is eventually at most
    <math|C<rsub|e,m,k>*e<rsup|-s<rsub|N>/16>\<to\>0>. Constants and mesh
    thresholds may depend on the fixed opened pair and period as prescribed
    by the ordered filter. No period-uniform mesh threshold is being
    introduced.

    Both terms of the discrepancy have one fixed translated stencil. The
    common-tiling argument and <eqref|eq:equation-spectator-bound> therefore
    bound a correlation with any fixed separated spectator tuple by the same
    vanishing root price times a constant depending on that tuple and its
    separation. The step-integral estimate proved above gives vanishing of
    its smeared correlations. Choosing a refinement preserves the eventual
    inequalities, the subpower bounds and these limits; it does not require a
    new comparison label.
  </proof>

  For clarity, these inputs imply the second limit in
  <eqref|eq:ope-normalized-tests> without assuming <math|u<rsub|c>> bounded.
  Multiplication by <math|b<rsub|c>=<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|p<rsub|c>*u<rsub|c>|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>1>
  preserves subpower bare norm. The vectors
  <math|b<rsub|c>*p<rsub|c>*u<rsub|c>> are bounded, so also have subpower
  bare norm. Their difference <math|b<rsub|c>*<around|(|u<rsub|c>-p<rsub|c>*u<rsub|c>|)>>
  consequently has subpower bare norm and belongs to <math|ker p<rsub|c>>,
  because <math|p<rsub|c><rsup|2>=p<rsub|c>>. The vanishing-test assertion
  gives exactly the normalized residual limit. The first limit follows by
  comparing the trial with <math|u<rsub|c>>: the centered estimate gives a
  separated correlation bound by a constant times the price, and the
  step-integral estimate just proved sends this bound to zero in every
  smeared correlation. Linearity here is legitimate at finite cutoff, since
  the trials are bounded measurable functions and the remaining fields are
  continuous on the compact configuration space.

  It remains to check the degree of a bounded transported low vector
  <math|v<rsub|c>\<in\>K<rsub|N><math-up|ran><space|0.17em>P>. Write
  <math|x<rsub|c>=K<rsub|N><rsup|-1>*v<rsub|c>>. Taking <math|k=U> in
  <eqref|eq:ope-plane-expansion> gives

  <\equation*>
    a<rsub|N><rsup|-<around|(|d-e|)>><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|c>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|U><rsup|-<around|(|d-e|)>>*J<rsub|N>*\<mu\><around|(|v<rsub|c>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|ex-column-subpower> gives zero logarithmic
  rate for <math|J<rsub|N>>. Thus the bare coefficients of
  <math|w<rsub|c>=a<rsub|N><rsup|-<around|(|d-e|)>>*v<rsub|c>> have subpower
  norm. We check all the local-trial conditions for this moving row. Put
  <math|\<xi\><rsub|c>=X<rsub|d,N,p><rsup|-1>*w<rsub|c>>, so the preceding
  bound makes <math|\<xi\><rsub|c>> subpower. Its unnormalized local function
  is <math|L<rsub|d>*<around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|d><around|(|\<vartheta\><rsub|N><rsup|-1>|)>*\<xi\><rsub|c>|)>>
  on the fixed section stencil. The paid column estimate gives derivative
  price <math|C<rsub|N><around|\||\<xi\><rsub|c>|\|><rsub|1>> through the
  actual order <math|q<rsub|N>> and at its actual spatial power. The global
  price is <math|A<rsub|d>*<around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|d><around|(|\<vartheta\><rsub|N><rsup|-1>|)>*\<xi\><rsub|c>|\|><rsub|1>>,
  where <math|A<rsub|d>> is the fixed cylinder bound of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-insertion-derivative-relay>. Both are
  subpower: <math|C<rsub|N>> and every fixed threshold power are subpower,
  and there are finitely many factor degrees at grade <math|d>. This uses the
  paid column estimate at <math|q<rsub|N>>, not a sequence of bounds with
  uncontrolled constants at increasing orders. The section is a polynomial in
  finitely many links. Thus it is continuous, literally translates with its
  anchor, and in all real left-increment variables it is smooth on a
  neighborhood of every configuration. In particular the required
  <math|C<rsup|q<rsub|N>>> germ and the recursive ordered-left-derivative
  identities hold at healthy configurations, including their boundary. Gauge
  invariance is preserved by the scalar coefficient row. Finally linearity
  gives

  <\equation*>
    a<rsub|N><rsup|d>*\<cal-O\><rsub|d><around|(|w<rsub|c>|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|e>*\<cal-O\><rsub|d><around|(|v<rsub|c>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Thus the literal observable <math|\<cal-O\><rsub|d><around|(|v<rsub|c>|)>>,
  not a replacement with different support, is a degree-<math|e> trial. Apply
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ope-local-trial-comparison>. If
  <math|v<rsub|c>\<to\>v> along a refinement, its tested correlations
  converge to those of the reference label <math|v>: convergence of linear
  tests on a fixed basis is convergence in operator norm. Apply the preceding
  comparison and compact argument to obtain a degree-<math|e> label having
  all the same smeared correlations. The smooth correlation kernels
  consequently agree: their difference integrates to zero against every
  compactly supported smooth test on the separated configuration domain,
  hence vanishes there. By the physical null-space definition, the class of
  <math|v> belongs to <math|E<rsub|e>>.

  Only boundedness and low-plane membership on the refined filter are needed.
  To apply comparison inputs stated on the original filter, replace
  <math|v<rsub|c>> by zero whenever either
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|c>|\<\|\|\>>\<gtr\><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>+1>
  or low-plane membership fails. This modified family is bounded and in the
  plane on the original filter, and equals <math|v<rsub|c>> eventually on the
  refinement. It has the same limit and the same limiting correlations there.

  <subsubsection|The distance estimate after extraction>

  Suppose reference labels <math|v<rsub|c>\<to\>v> on a refinement of the
  ordered cutoff filter and <math|q<rsub|k><around|(|v<rsub|c>|)>\<leqslant\>V>
  eventually, with <math|k> fixed. Choose
  <math|y<rsub|c>\<in\>K<rsub|N><math-up|ran><space|0.17em>P> by
  <eqref|eq:ope-transport-distance>. They are eventually bounded, because
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|y<rsub|c>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|c>|\<\|\|\>>+F<rsub|k,d,e>*V>.
  Extract a further nontrivial filter on which <math|y<rsub|c>\<to\>y>. The
  preceding argument puts <math|<around|[|y|]>> in <math|E<rsub|e>>, and
  continuity of the norm gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-physical-distance><around|[|y|]>\<in\>E<rsub|e>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|v-y|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>F<rsub|k,d,e>*V.
  </equation>

  The whole-vector bound <eqref|eq:ope-whole-distance> passes to the limit
  directly. Both estimates hold for the same limit <math|v>; only the
  auxiliary approximant <math|y> requires a further extraction.

  Finally these finite-scale factors have the advertised rates. By
  Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|ex-column-subpower>, <math|log
  J<rsub|k>=o<around|(|s<rsub|k>|)>> and <math|log
  \<zeta\><rsub|k>=o<around|(|s<rsub|k>|)>>, where <math|s<rsub|k>=log
  <around|(|1/a<rsub|k>|)>>. The column allowances also satisfy
  <math|\<zeta\><rsub|k>\<geqslant\>2>. In fact

  <\equation*>
    H<rsub|k>*\<zeta\><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|k><rsup|3>*\<zeta\><rsub|0>.
  </equation*>

  This holds at <math|k=0>. For the induction step use
  <math|1+\<zeta\><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|k>*\<zeta\><rsub|k+1>>
  and the recurrences for <math|H,J>:

  <\equation*>
    H<rsub|k+1>*\<zeta\><rsub|k+1>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|k>*\<zeta\><rsub|k>*\<zeta\><rsub|k+1><rsup|3>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|k><rsup|3>*\<zeta\><rsub|0>*\<zeta\><rsub|k+1><rsup|3>=J<rsub|k+1><rsup|3>*\<zeta\><rsub|0>.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|H<rsub|k>> also has zero logarithmic rate. If
  <math|k<rsub|n>\<to\>\<infty\>>, <math|t<rsub|n>\<to\>+\<infty\>>, and
  <math|s<rsub|k<rsub|n>>/t<rsub|n>\<to\>a>, then
  <math|F<rsub|k<rsub|n>,d,e>> has rate <math|<around|(|d-e-1|)>*a>, while
  the factor in <eqref|eq:ope-whole-distance> has rate <math|d*a>. Taking
  <math|d=q> and <math|e=m-1> proves precisely the factors in
  <eqref|eq:ope-match-norm>\U<eqref|eq:ope-match-distance>, once a cutoff
  match with quota <math|V<rsub|n>> and convergent reference labels has been
  produced. This argument preserves the exact reference labels and physical
  filtration; it does not require convergence of the individual
  renormalization matrices.

  <subsection|The weighted support estimate for
  grouping><label|sec:ope-grouping>

  The loss in <eqref|eq:ope-group-weight> pays for two effects. Moving
  several marks to a common representative enlarges the supports carrying
  them. Expanding the resulting product then creates connected unions of
  these enlarged supports. We prove the two counting estimates that control
  these operations. They apply to distinct activity labels; no claim about a
  logarithmic expansion with repeated labels is needed.

  <subsubsection|Connected unions with distinct labels>

  Let <math|X<rsub|i>> be finite supports in a four-dimensional periodic
  lattice, indexed by an arbitrary set, and let
  <math|c<rsub|M><around|(|X|)>> be their connected covering number at a
  positive integer block size <math|M>: the least number of lattice balls of
  radius <math|M> whose union covers <math|X> and is connected by unit steps.
  This is the covering number in the support prices of the local
  representations. We use its elementary properties

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||X|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|2*M+1|)><rsup|4>*c<rsub|M><around|(|X|)>,<space|2em>c<rsub|M>*<around|(|X\<cup\>Y|)>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|M><around|(|X|)>+c<rsub|M><around|(|Y|)><space|1em><text|if
    >X\<cap\>Y\<ne\>\<varnothing\>.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|f<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>, <math|a\<geqslant\>0>,
  <math|\<gamma\>\<geqslant\>0>, and suppose that, for every site <math|x>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-hit-hypothesis><big|sum><rsub|i:x\<in\>X<rsub|i>>f<rsub|i>*e<rsup|<around|(|a+v|)>*c<rsub|M><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\>,<space|2em><around|(|2*M+1|)><rsup|4>*\<gamma\>\<leqslant\>v.
  </equation>

  All sums of nonnegative terms have their supremum-of-finite-sums meaning. A
  nonempty finite label set <math|A> is connected when its intersection
  graph, with an edge for overlapping supports, is connected. Then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-forest-price><big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<text|
    finite, connected>>>|<row|<cell|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>A>X<rsub|i>>>>>>>e<rsup|a*c<rsub|M><around|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>A>X<rsub|i>|)>>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>A>f<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<gamma\>>-1.
  </equation>

  We prove this by a finite recursion. For a finite set of allowed labels
  <math|D> and a support <math|Y>, let <math|\<cal-P\>*<around|(|D,Y|)>>
  consist of all subsets of <math|D> whose every connected component has a
  support meeting <math|Y>; the empty subset is included. Set

  <\equation*>
    F*<around|(|D,Y|)>=<big|sum><rsub|A\<in\>\<cal-P\>*<around|(|D,Y|)>><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>A>p<rsub|i>,<space|2em>p<rsub|i>=f<rsub|i>*e<rsup|a*c<rsub|M><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>.
  </equation*>

  The following inequality retains each label at most once:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-forest-recursion>F*<around|(|D,Y|)>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|i\<in\>D>>|<row|<cell|X<rsub|i>\<cap\>Y\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>>>>>><around*|(|1+p<rsub|i>*F*<around|(|D\<setminus\><around|{|i|}>,X<rsub|i>|)>|)>.
  </equation>

  To see it, choose for each connected component of <math|A> one label whose
  support meets <math|Y>. Different components choose different labels.
  Deleting the chosen label leaves a set every one of whose connected
  components meets its support: follow a path to the deleted vertex in the
  old component. Record that remaining set at the chosen label, and record
  the empty option at every unchosen label. The union of the recorded sets
  with their chosen labels recovers <math|A>, so the encoding is injective.
  Its weight is exactly <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>A>p<rsub|i>>. Expanding
  the product on the right of <eqref|eq:ope-forest-recursion> permits
  additional choices, possibly sharing labels between different options; all
  their weights are nonnegative. This proves the inequality without
  introducing factorial or ordering losses.

  Strong induction on <math|<around|\||D|\|>> gives

  <\equation*>
    F*<around|(|D,Y|)>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<around|\||Y|\|>*\<gamma\>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|v*c<rsub|M><around|(|Y|)>>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed insert the induction bound in <eqref|eq:ope-forest-recursion> and
  use <math|1+r\<leqslant\>e<rsup|r>>:

  <\equation*>
    F*<around|(|D,Y|)>\<leqslant\>exp <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|i\<in\>D>>|<row|<cell|X<rsub|i>\<cap\>Y\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>>>>>>f<rsub|i>*e<rsup|<around|(|a+v|)>*c<rsub|M><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>|)>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<around|\||Y|\|>*\<gamma\>>.
  </equation*>

  The last inequality sums <eqref|eq:ope-hit-hypothesis> over sites of
  <math|Y>; a support meeting <math|Y> is counted at least once. The
  cardinality bound and <eqref|eq:ope-hit-hypothesis> prove the second
  inequality, which closes the induction. Now take <math|Y=<around|{|x|}>>
  and remove the empty subset, whose weight is one. For a connected <math|A>,
  order its vertices so that each new support meets the preceding union; a
  spanning tree supplies such an order. Repeated covering subadditivity gives
  <math|c<rsub|M><around|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>A>X<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>A>c<rsub|M><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>.
  Thus each term in <eqref|eq:ope-forest-price> is bounded by its
  corresponding product of <math|p<rsub|i>>. This proves the desired estimate
  for every finite family of connected sets: take <math|D> to contain all
  their labels. Taking the supremum over finite families proves the full
  assertion and its absolute summability.

  <subsubsection|Paying for the new representative sites>

  Let a finite set of old marks <math|I> be partitioned into groups <math|V>,
  with a chosen old mark in each group and a positive integer bound <math|h>
  on the group size. Write <math|r<rsub|i>> for the old site,
  <math|c<rsub|v>> for its group's representative site, and
  <math|\<pi\>:I\<to\>V> for the partition. Assume that at most <math|h> old
  marks belong to a group and at most <math|\<ell\>> old sites occur at any
  one lattice site. The same occupancy bound holds for the representatives,
  since their selected old marks are distinct. Suppose

  <\equation*>
    c<rsub|M><around|(|<around|{|r<rsub|i>,c<rsub|\<pi\><around|(|i|)>>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>k.
  </equation*>

  An activity indexed by <math|\<alpha\>> has a mark set
  <math|A<rsub|\<alpha\>>\<subseteq\>I> and support <math|X<rsub|\<alpha\>>>
  containing every <math|r<rsub|i>> for <math|i\<in\>A<rsub|\<alpha\>>>.
  Enlarge it to

  <\equation*>
    <wide|X|~><rsub|\<alpha\>>=X<rsub|\<alpha\>>\<cup\><around|{|c<rsub|\<pi\><around|(|i|)>>:i\<in\>A<rsub|\<alpha\>>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Adding the pairs one at a time gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-enlarged-cover>c<rsub|M><around|(|<wide|X|~><rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|M><around|(|X<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>+k<around|\||A<rsub|\<alpha\>>|\|>,
  </equation>

  because each pair meets the original support at <math|r<rsub|i>>. For
  positive mark weights <math|w<rsub|i>> and
  <math|0\<less\>\<eta\>\<leqslant\>1>, put

  <\equation*>
    <wide|w|~><rsub|i>=<around|(|\<eta\>/h|)>*w<rsub|i>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*k>,<space|2em>\<sigma\>\<geqslant\>0.
  </equation*>

  For every nonnegative coefficient majorant <math|q<rsub|\<alpha\>>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-enlargement-price><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>A<rsub|\<alpha\>>><wide|w|~><rsub|i>|)>*q<rsub|\<alpha\>>*e<rsup|\<sigma\>*c<rsub|M><around|(|<wide|X|~><rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<eta\>/h|)><rsup|<around|\||A<rsub|\<alpha\>>|\|>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>A<rsub|\<alpha\>>>w<rsub|i>|)>*q<rsub|\<alpha\>>*e<rsup|\<sigma\>*c<rsub|M><around|(|X<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>>.
  </equation>

  This follows immediately from <eqref|eq:ope-enlarged-cover>; the
  exponential price paid by each mark cancels its added connecting cover.

  We need a local summation version of this observation. Suppose
  <math|b,g\<geqslant\>0> and nonnegative quantities <math|F<rsub|\<alpha\>>>
  have the bound <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>:y\<in\>X<rsub|\<alpha\>>>F<rsub|\<alpha\>>\<leqslant\>g>
  at every site. Suppose new weighted quantities
  <math|W<rsub|\<alpha\>>\<geqslant\>0> satisfy
  <math|W<rsub|\<alpha\>>\<leqslant\>b*F<rsub|\<alpha\>>> for all
  <math|\<alpha\>>, and the stronger <math|W<rsub|\<alpha\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|b/h|)>*F<rsub|\<alpha\>>>
  whenever <math|A<rsub|\<alpha\>>\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>. Then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-enlarged-hit><big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>:y\<in\><wide|X|~><rsub|\<alpha\>>>W<rsub|\<alpha\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+\<ell\>|)>*b*g.
  </equation>

  The old-support contribution is at most <math|b*g>. If <math|y> is a new
  site of <math|<wide|X|~><rsub|\<alpha\>>>, some group <math|v> has
  <math|c<rsub|v>=y> and some <math|i\<in\>A<rsub|\<alpha\>>\<cap\>\<pi\><rsup|-1><around|(|v|)>>
  has <math|r<rsub|i>\<in\>X<rsub|\<alpha\>>>. Bound this contribution by
  summing over all such <math|v> and all its old marks. For fixed <math|i>
  the sum is at most <math|b*g/h>; there are at most <math|h> marks in that
  group, and at most <math|\<ell\>> groups at <math|y>. This proves
  <eqref|eq:ope-enlarged-hit>. Activities with no marks have no added
  representative sites and are accounted for entirely in the old-support
  contribution. This is why the factor <math|1/h> is needed only for
  activities carrying marks.

  <subsubsection|The resulting local majorant>

  Take nonnegative budget constants <math|T,A,B>. There are three kinds of
  entries in the regrouping expansion. The first are singleton coefficients
  <math|u<rsub|i>>, with <math|w<rsub|i><around|\||u<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>T>.
  Their local sum, including the support weight, is at most
  <math|\<ell\>*T*e<rsup|\<sigma\>>>, because
  <math|c<rsub|M><around|(|<around|{|r<rsub|i>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>1> and the
  occupancy is at most <math|\<ell\>>. The second are the nonempty marked
  coefficients of the ordinary activity, whose local weighted sum is at most
  <math|A>. Together these have bound

  <\equation*>
    g<rsub|0>=\<ell\>*T*e<rsup|\<sigma\>>+A.
  </equation*>

  Both carry a nonempty mark set. Equation <eqref|eq:ope-enlargement-price>
  bounds their new weight by <math|\<eta\>/h> times the old weight. Applying
  <eqref|eq:ope-enlarged-hit> with <math|b=\<eta\>> gives
  <math|<around|(|1+\<ell\>|)>*\<eta\>*g<rsub|0>>. Each entry is split into
  its healthy and unhealthy support cases by indicator functions. Each piece
  is bounded by the same coefficient majorant, so their combined bound is
  twice this.

  The third kind comprises the activity on flagged supports, including its
  zero-mark part. Let its original local weighted sum be at most <math|B>.
  For these entries <eqref|eq:ope-enlargement-price> gives a factor at most
  one in general, and at most <math|1/h> for a nonempty mark set. Thus
  <eqref|eq:ope-enlarged-hit> with <math|b=1> gives
  <math|<around|(|1+\<ell\>|)>*B>. The complete local majorant is therefore

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-group-majorant>\<Gamma\>=2*<around|(|1+\<ell\>|)>*\<eta\>*<around|(|\<ell\>*T*e<rsup|\<sigma\>>+A|)>+<around|(|1+\<ell\>|)>*B.
  </equation>

  This bound is independent of the total number of cells and groups.

  Apply <eqref|eq:ope-forest-price> with <math|a=\<nu\>> and support exponent
  <math|\<sigma\>=\<nu\>+v>, provided <math|<around|(|2*M+1|)><rsup|4>*\<Gamma\>\<leqslant\>v>.
  Since the forest estimate assumes <math|a\<geqslant\>0>, this application
  requires <math|\<nu\>\<geqslant\>0>; the purchased support exponents of the
  schedule satisfy it. Every connected set of distinct entries then has local
  summed price at most <math|e<rsup|\<Gamma\>>-1> with exponent
  <math|\<nu\>>. For a component whose mark set is a union of complete
  groups, let <math|W<rsub|v>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>\<pi\><rsup|-1><around|(|v|)>><wide|w|~><rsub|i>>.
  The product of its old mark weights is exactly the product of these
  <math|W<rsub|v>> over its new group marks. Distinct constituent entries
  have disjoint marks when their squarefree marker product is nonzero; there
  is no multiplicity correction in this equality. Each constituent-label set
  must yield at most one output entry across the ordinary and flagged kinds;
  its support and prescribed flag set are fixed by its constituents. This
  uniqueness is proved in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-grouping-algebra>.
  Grouping the connected terms by their resulting support and group mark set,
  the triangle inequality preserves the local bound
  <math|e<rsup|\<Gamma\>>-1>. Restrictions to the healthy or prescribed-flag
  events only reduce the coefficient majorants. Thus both the ordinary and
  flagged connected arrays have this same bound.

  For <eqref|eq:ope-group-weight>, the old weights are
  <math|\<delta\>*W<rsub|i>>, <math|\<eta\>=\<delta\>>,
  <math|\<sigma\>=\<nu\>+1/2>, and the maximum group size is <math|h>. The
  new weight of an old mark is precisely <math|\<delta\><rsup|2>*W<rsub|i>*e<rsup|-<around|(|\<nu\>+1/2|)>*k>/h>.
  Their product over a group is the numerator in <eqref|eq:ope-group-weight>.
  The purchased smallness conditions are <math|<around|(|2*M+1|)><rsup|4>*\<Gamma\>\<leqslant\>1/2>
  and <math|e<rsup|\<Gamma\>>-1\<leqslant\>1>. They provide the claimed unit
  bound for both connected arrays before the subsequent factor <math|1/8> is
  reserved for their local presentation. This proves the quantitative support
  and coefficient estimates used by regrouping. The exact polynomial
  decomposition, its gauge/locality properties and the local projection
  estimate are separate steps; these counting bounds do not by themselves
  establish those identities.

  <subsection|The exact regrouping identity><label|sec:ope-grouping-algebra>

  The estimates of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-grouping> concern an
  exact reorganization of the marked density. We now prove the identity. In
  particular the coefficient of the empty mark set, which determines the
  vacuum activity, is preserved.

  <subsubsection|Squarefree marks and complete groups>

  For a finite mark set <math|I>, use the commutative algebra with basis
  <math|e<rsub|A>>, <math|A\<subseteq\>I>, and multiplication

  <\equation*>
    e<rsub|A>*e<rsub|B>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|e<rsub|A\<cup\>B>,>|<cell|A\<cap\>B=\<varnothing\>,>>|<row|<cell|0,>|<cell|<text|otherwise>.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  Its unit is <math|e<rsub|\<varnothing\>>>. The ideal spanned by the
  nonempty <math|A> is nilpotent, so its exponential is a finite sum. In
  particular, for finitely many nonempty <math|A<rsub|r>>,

  <\equation*>
    exp <around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|r>z<rsub|r>*e<rsub|A<rsub|r>>|)>=<big|prod><rsub|r><around|(|1+z<rsub|r>*e<rsub|A<rsub|r>>|)>,
  </equation*>

  since each individual summand squares to zero and all commute. Repeated use
  of a mark thus vanishes automatically.

  Let <math|\<pi\>:I\<to\>V> be the partition from the previous section, with
  nonempty groups <math|I<rsub|v>=\<pi\><rsup|-1><around|(|v|)>>. Define a
  linear map <math|\<cal-R\>> to the analogous algebra on <math|V> by

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-R\>*e<rsub|A>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|e<rsub|B>,>|<cell|A=<big|cup><rsub|v\<in\>B>I<rsub|v><text|
    for some >B\<subseteq\>V,>>|<row|<cell|0,>|<cell|<text|otherwise>.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  Equivalently, the coefficient at <math|B> after applying <math|\<cal-R\>>
  is the old coefficient at <math|\<pi\><rsup|-1><around|(|B|)>>. This map is
  not asserted to preserve arbitrary products. It does preserve a product of
  monomials whose mark sets are individually unions of complete groups. If
  these sets are disjoint, the assertion follows from their unions; if they
  overlap, both products vanish. We will apply multiplicativity only after
  the connected-component decomposition has made every component complete in
  this sense.

  <subsubsection|Ordinary and flagged entries>

  Fix a lattice configuration and let <math|F> be its set of flags. Take
  finite families of ordinary and flagged entries. An ordinary entry <math|r>
  has a nonempty old mark set <math|A<rsub|r>>, a support <math|X<rsub|r>>
  and a coefficient <math|z<rsub|r>>. A flagged entry <math|r> has, in
  addition, a nonempty prescribed flag set
  <math|Z<rsub|r>\<subseteq\>X<rsub|r>>, and its coefficient vanishes unless
  <math|X<rsub|r>\<cap\>F=Z<rsub|r>>. Its mark set may be empty. The flagged
  part of the density is the sum over selections of flagged entries with
  pairwise disjoint supports and prescribed flag sets whose union is
  <math|F>. Multiply this sum by the exponential of the ordinary entries. The
  finite exponential formula shows that the product is a sum over sets
  <math|S> of distinct ordinary and flagged entries, with weight

  <\equation*>
    <big|prod><rsub|r\<in\>S>z<rsub|r>*e<rsub|A<rsub|r>>,
  </equation*>

  subject to the flagged-support and flag-inventory conditions. Terms with
  overlapping old mark sets vanish.

  Enlarge each support as in <eqref|eq:ope-enlarged-cover>, writing
  <math|<wide|X|~><rsub|r>> for the result. Split each ordinary entry into
  two entries with the same marks, multiplied respectively by
  <math|\<b-1\><rsub|<wide|X|~><rsub|r>\<cap\>F=\<varnothing\>>> and its
  complement. Call the second kind <em|dirty>; all flagged entries are dirty
  too. The sum of the two pieces is the original entry, so this split leaves
  the density unchanged. At most one piece is nonzero at any fixed
  configuration. Every enlarged support contains the representative
  <math|c<rsub|v>> of each group mentioned by its old marks. A flagged entry
  with no marks is not enlarged.

  We describe a canonical partition of a selected set <math|S>. First join
  entries which mention a common group. Call a component of this first graph
  hot if it contains a dirty entry. Next join hot components whenever their
  enlarged supports intersect. The resulting components are the components
  used below. Equivalently each entry always carries its group names as graph
  coordinates, and carries its enlarged support as additional graph
  coordinates precisely when its first component is hot. Connected components
  are then taken in this single intersection graph. Distinct components have
  disjoint group-name sets, and distinct hot components have disjoint
  enlarged supports. Every component is connected by ordinary support
  overlaps: a common group supplies its representative site as a common
  point.

  This partition is unchanged by restricting to one of its components. Indeed
  a path using group names never leaves that component, so its hot status can
  be determined within it. Conversely, a family of internally connected
  components with disjoint group-name sets and pairwise disjoint hot supports
  is exactly the component partition of its union. No group-name path or
  hot-support edge can cross between members of the family. These two
  observations prove the uniqueness needed for the reorganization.

  <subsubsection|Reconstructing the selected density>

  Consider a nonzero old term which survives <math|\<cal-R\>>. Its total mark
  set is a union of complete groups. Every one of its components also has
  this property. For if one mark in a group occurs in that component, every
  other selected mark of the same group is joined to it through their common
  group name. Since the total selection contains the whole group, the
  component contains the whole group.

  A cold component contains only healthy ordinary entries, so its coefficient
  is supported on configurations with no flags in its combined enlarged
  support. It has a nonempty group mark set. A hot component necessarily
  contains a flagged entry. To prove this last assertion, choose a dirty
  ordinary entry if no flagged entry has yet been found. Its enlarged support
  meets <math|F>. The global flag-inventory condition supplies a selected
  flagged entry whose prescribed flag set contains that point. Both entries
  are hot and their supports intersect, so they are in the same component, a
  contradiction.

  For each possible cold component, make a new ordinary entry: its support is
  the union of its enlarged supports, its coefficient is the product of its
  constituent coefficients, and its new marks are its group names. For each
  possible component containing a flagged entry, make a new flagged entry
  with that same combined support, flag set <math|Z> equal to the union of
  its old prescribed flag sets, and coefficient

  <\equation*>
    \<b-1\><rsub|<wide|X|~>\<cap\>F=Z>*<big|prod><rsub|r\<in\>S>z<rsub|r>.
  </equation*>

  Only internally connected components with disjoint old mark sets, complete
  group marks and pairwise disjoint old flagged supports are included. The
  new flag set is nonempty.

  For the components of a nonzero old selection, the displayed indicator is
  one. Any flag in the enlarged support of a hot component belongs to a
  selected flagged entry somewhere in the old term; intersection of the two
  hot supports puts that entry in this same component. The converse inclusion
  follows from the old flag conditions. Distinct hot supports are disjoint,
  and their prescribed flag sets still partition <math|F>. Distinct
  components have disjoint new mark sets. The old term therefore gives an
  allowed new term with exactly the same coefficient after <math|\<cal-R\>>.

  Conversely, take a nonzero term in the new ordinary-exponential and
  flagged-selection expansion. Its component entries have disjoint new mark
  sets and pairwise disjoint hot supports. Their inventories of old labels
  are disjoint. A shared old label carrying a mark would force a shared group
  name; a shared label without marks must be a flagged entry and would force
  intersecting hot supports. Thus no old label is counted twice. Within each
  new component the old flagged supports are disjoint; between components
  they lie inside disjoint hot supports. Their old flag sets have union
  <math|F>. The old mark sets are disjoint, since they are disjoint within
  components and complete disjoint groups between components. Hence their
  union gives an allowed old selection. The uniqueness of the component
  partition proves that this construction inverts the preceding one.

  The products of coefficients agree in both directions. On complete group
  components, <math|\<cal-R\>> preserves their monomial product by the first
  subsection. Summing the resulting weight-preserving bijection proves the
  exact regrouping identity. All sums here are finite: the torus, mark sets,
  supports and their admissible patterns are finite. No convergence or
  rearrangement assumption enters this algebraic step. The connected-array
  estimates of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-grouping> apply to these
  very component entries. The ordinary and flagged kinds cannot represent the
  same constituent set, since one is cold and the other contains a flagged
  entry; thus summing their bounds does not hide an additional multiplicity.

  <subsubsection|Vacuum, locality, and covariance>

  A new entry with no group marks must be a single old zero-mark flagged
  entry. In fact no ordinary entry has empty marks. All entries of such a
  component are therefore flagged entries with no marks, whose supports were
  not enlarged. Their old supports are pairwise disjoint, so no support edge
  connects two different entries; and there are no group-name edges.
  Connectedness forces a singleton. Its new support, flag set and coefficient
  coincide with the old ones; the indicator is already implied by the old
  flag condition. Thus the zero-mark coefficient of every flagged support is
  unchanged. The ordinary array has no zero-mark coefficient. The same vacuum
  activity is retained by the regrouping.

  The construction also preserves the required local dependence. Each
  coefficient is a finite product of original coefficients, with indicators
  determined by the flags in the combined support. It therefore depends only
  on the original field and flag data in that support. Products, finite sums
  and these measurable indicators preserve measurability. If the original
  coefficients and flags are gauge invariant, so are the new coefficients.
  Healthy ordinary components vanish when their support meets the flags;
  flagged components vanish unless the intersection with the flags is their
  specified flag set. These are the support conditions used in the local
  representations. They do not assert smoothness across a flag boundary.

  Finally we state covariance. Let <math|a> be a permutation of the old marks
  and <math|b> a permutation of the group names, and let a translation or
  signed coordinate change, with the accompanying conjugation, act on
  positions and flags. Assume: (a) <math|a> and <math|b> are compatible with
  the partition, <math|\<pi\><around|(|a<around|(|i|)>|)>=b<around|(|\<pi\><around|(|i|)>|)>>
  for every old mark <math|i>, and they carry the chosen representative of
  each group to the chosen representative of its image; (b) for a fixed
  copied family <math|v> of group names with image <math|u>, one has
  <math|b<around|(|i|)>\<in\>u> if and only if <math|i\<in\>v>; (c) for marks
  in groups of <math|v>, the positions and flag data of
  <math|a<around|(|i|)>> are the transformed positions and flag data of
  <math|i>; and (d) the original coefficient identities hold for every mark
  set contained in the copied family. Compatibility (a) is an assumption on
  the relabelling, not a consequence of (d): a permutation of old marks that
  does not map groups to groups does not intertwine the regrouping. In the
  application the same partition is used in each copy, as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-cutoff>, and (a)\U(c) hold by
  construction. Under (a)\U(d) the transformation relabels entries
  bijectively, preserves group-name incidence, health and flag inventory, and
  sends support intersections to support intersections. Hence they send the
  canonical components to the corresponding components after transformation.
  Reindexing the finite sums proves covariance of the new arrays for every
  coefficient whose group names lie in the copied family <math|v>; no
  statement is made for other coefficients. This keeps the replication
  identities through repeated regrouping. To obtain the local
  normalized-field representation used in <eqref|eq:ope-group-weight>, one
  must in addition extract its singleton coefficients and apply the purchased
  local analytic projection bounds; those estimates are not consequences of
  this finite algebraic identity.

  <subsection|Recovering the local fields after
  regrouping><label|sec:ope-singletons>

  The preceding regrouping gives a polynomial density with controlled
  coefficients. To continue the scale induction, its linear coefficients must
  again be presented as local fields, with the remaining terms assigned to
  interactions involving at least two marks. We give this passage explicitly.
  It uses no Taylor approximation: the separation by the number of marks is
  exact.

  Fix a finite periodic lattice, a finite mark set <math|V>, and positive
  weights <math|w<rsub|v>>. Write <math|e<rsub|A>> for the squarefree
  monomial belonging to an allowed mark set <math|A>, as in the preceding
  section. Ordinary polynomial activities <math|F<rsub|X>> are indexed by
  supports <math|X>; flagged activities <math|K<rsub|X,Z>> are indexed by
  pairs with <math|\<varnothing\>\<ne\>Z\<subseteq\>X>. Their coefficients
  are measurable, gauge invariant, and depend only on the field and flag data
  in <math|X>. A nonzero coefficient with mark set <math|A> requires
  <math|<around|{|o<rsub|v>:v\<in\>A|}>\<subseteq\>X>. Ordinary activities
  have zero constant coefficient and vanish unless <math|X> is healthy.
  Flagged activities vanish unless the flags in <math|X> are exactly
  <math|Z>.

  Put <math|\<nu\>=\<nu\><rsub|R>>, <math|M=M<rsub|R>> for the support
  parameters of the current representation. Suppose the ordinary and flagged
  arrays have the weighted coefficient bounds from
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-grouping>, with local hit bounds
  <math|a> and <math|b>, respectively. In concrete terms there are
  nonnegative coefficient majorants <math|t<rsub|X,A>> and
  <math|s<rsub|<around|(|X,Z|)>,A>>, valid for every field configuration,
  such that for every site <math|x>

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|X\<ni\>x>e<rsup|\<nu\>*c<rsub|M><around|(|X|)>>*<big|sum><rsub|A>t<rsub|X,A>*<big|prod><rsub|v\<in\>A>w<rsub|v>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>a,<eq-number><label|eq:ope-single-hit>>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|<around|(|X,Z|)>:x\<in\>X>e<rsup|\<nu\>*c<rsub|M><around|(|X|)>>*<big|sum><rsub|A>s<rsub|<around|(|X,Z|)>,A>*<big|prod><rsub|v\<in\>A>w<rsub|v>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>b.>>>>
  </align>

  The underlying lattice is finite, so the support indices in these
  expressions are finite. The activity dictionary used to represent them may
  nevertheless be countable.

  We use the following precise property of that dictionary. Every nonempty
  support has an unused ordinary slot with that support and zero background
  coefficient. Every admissible flagged pair has a flagged slot with that
  pair, eligible whenever the specified flag condition holds. A real
  measurable gauge-invariant function <math|h> in such a slot is an allowed
  elementary insertion when it has the required local dependence and vanishes
  off the slot's eligibility set. If <math|<around|\||h|\|>\<leqslant\>t>,
  its cost is

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|\<nu\>*c<rsub|M><around|(|X|)>>*t.
  </equation*>

  This is a property of the available representation, not a consequence of
  the support count. Its production in the evolving activity dictionary
  remains an input to this local argument.

  <subsubsection|The price of a singleton>

  Fix a mark <math|v>. In the first inequality of <eqref|eq:ope-single-hit>,
  take <math|x=o<rsub|v>> and retain only the coefficient of
  <math|e<rsub|<around|{|v|}>>>. Coefficients on supports not containing
  <math|o<rsub|v>> vanish. Dividing by <math|w<rsub|v>\<gtr\>0> gives
  majorants <math|t<rsub|X>\<geqslant\>0> with

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||<around|[|e<rsub|<around|{|v|}>>|]>*F<rsub|X><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|X>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|X>e<rsup|\<nu\>*c<rsub|M><around|(|X|)>>*t<rsub|X>\<leqslant\>a/w<rsub|v>.
  </equation*>

  The same argument for the flagged array gives majorants <math|s<rsub|X,Z>>
  with weighted sum at most <math|b/w<rsub|v>>. There is no factor
  <math|<around|\||V|\|>> in these bounds: only one coordinate of the price
  at <math|o<rsub|v>> was retained.

  For an ordinary singleton function <math|f> supported in <math|X>, use the
  unused ordinary slot and write <math|f=<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>f+<math-up|iIm><space|0.17em>f>.
  Both real functions have bound <math|t<rsub|X>>, retain local dependence,
  measurability and gauge invariance, and vanish outside the healthy set.
  They are therefore two elementary insertions, with coefficients <math|1>
  and <math|<math-up|i>>, of total cost at most
  <math|2*e<rsup|\<nu\>*c<rsub|M><around|(|X|)>>*t<rsub|X>>. They contribute
  <math|f> to the ordinary field and zero to its flagged profile. The same
  construction in the flagged slot contributes zero to the ordinary field and
  <math|f> at the specified flagged pair, at cost at most
  <math|2*e<rsup|\<nu\>*c<rsub|M><around|(|X|)>>*s<rsub|X,Z>>. Zero functions
  require no slot.

  Consequently, for arbitrary constants <math|u<rsub|v>\<in\>\<bbb-C\>>, the
  pair

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-single-pair>g<rsub|v><around|(|U|)>=u<rsub|v>+<big|sum><rsub|X><around|[|e<rsub|<around|{|v|}>>|]>*F<rsub|X><around|(|U|)>,<space|2em>q<rsub|v>*<around|(|X,Z;U|)>=<around|[|e<rsub|<around|{|v|}>>|]>*K<rsub|X,Z><around|(|U|)>
  </equation>

  has a finite elementary representation anchored at <math|o<rsub|v>>, with
  constant part <math|u<rsub|v>> and cost at most

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-single-price>2*a/w<rsub|v>+2*b/w<rsub|v>.
  </equation>

  Indeed concatenate the finite lists just constructed. Adding lists adds
  their costs, and multiplication by a scalar multiplies cost by its modulus.
  The constant <math|u<rsub|v>> is recorded separately and costs nothing in
  this representation; its later affordability is a separate condition.

  Each flagged elementary insertion is carried by its chosen index in the
  countable activity dictionary. Adding the finitely many such indexed
  functions gives a family <math|k<rsub|v,j><around|(|U|)>> with a summable
  uniform majorant. Summing it over all indices with flagged pair
  <math|<around|(|X,Z|)>> gives exactly <math|q<rsub|v>*<around|(|X,Z;U|)>>.
  Thus a finite local representation also provides the dominated indexed
  family required by the transfer formula. The estimates concern the local
  representation and do not require a canonically chosen index for each
  singleton.

  <subsubsection|Exact separation of the higher terms>

  Let <math|k<rsub|j><rsup|0><around|(|U|)>> be the real background flagged
  activities and let <math|E<around|(|U|)>> be the ordinary background
  exponent. For a fixed configuration <math|U>, denote its flag set by
  <math|\<cal-Z\><around|(|U|)>>. For an array <math|L> indexed by flagged
  pairs set

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-G\><rsub|U><around|(|L|)>=<big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-P\><text|
    a set of flagged pairs>>>|<row|<cell|<text|supports pairwise
    disjoint>,<space|0.27em><big|cup><rsub|<around|(|X,Z|)>\<in\>\<cal-P\>>Z=\<cal-Z\><around|(|U|)>>>>>>><big|prod><rsub|<around|(|X,Z|)>\<in\>\<cal-P\>>L<rsub|X,Z>.
  </equation*>

  The current density is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-single-density>D<around|(|U|)>=e<rsup|E<around|(|U|)>>*exp
    <around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|v>u<rsub|v>*e<rsub|<around|{|v|}>>+<big|sum><rsub|X>F<rsub|X><around|(|U|)>|)>*\<cal-G\><rsub|U><around|(|K<around|(|U|)>|)>.
  </equation>

  Assume the constant coefficient of <math|K<rsub|X,Z>> is the sum of
  <math|k<rsub|j><rsup|0>> over dictionary indices with pair
  <math|<around|(|X,Z|)>>; assume also the background activities are
  absolutely summable. These are the vacuum consistency and validity
  conditions retained in the preceding regrouping.

  Let <math|F<rsub|X><rsup|\<geqslant\>2>> and
  <math|K<rsub|X,Z><rsup|\<geqslant\>2>> denote the arrays obtained by
  discarding coefficients with fewer than two marks. Then, coefficient by
  coefficient,

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|v>u<rsub|v>*e<rsub|<around|{|v|}>>+<big|sum><rsub|X>F<rsub|X>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|v>g<rsub|v>*e<rsub|<around|{|v|}>>+<big|sum><rsub|X>F<rsub|X><rsup|\<geqslant\>2>,>>|<row|<cell|K<rsub|X,Z>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|j:<space|0.17em><around|(|X<rsub|j>,Z<rsub|j>|)>=<around|(|X,Z|)>>k<rsub|j><rsup|0>+<big|sum><rsub|v>q<rsub|v>*<around|(|X,Z|)>*e<rsub|<around|{|v|}>>+K<rsub|X,Z><rsup|\<geqslant\>2>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Substitution in <eqref|eq:ope-single-density> proves the desired local
  representation of the same density, with fields <math|g<rsub|v>>, flagged
  profiles <math|q<rsub|v>>, and interactions of order at least two.
  Discarding coefficients cannot increase a sum of nonnegative coefficient
  majorants. Hence the two higher arrays retain hit bounds <math|a> and
  <math|b>. Their support, eligibility and invariance properties are retained
  as well, since each remaining coefficient is unchanged.

  For completeness, this representation has the expected linear density. Put
  <math|r<around|(|U|)>=e<rsup|E<around|(|U|)>>*\<cal-G\><rsub|U><around|(|k<rsup|0>|)>>,
  the unmarked density. Expanding a product to first order gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-single-coin><around|[|e<rsub|<around|{|v|}>>|]>*D<around|(|U|)>=r<around|(|U|)>*g<rsub|v><around|(|U|)>+e<rsup|E<around|(|U|)>>*<big|sum><rsub|S<text|
    admissible>><big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>S>k<rsub|v,j><around|(|U|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S\<setminus\><around|{|j|}>>k<rsub|i><rsup|0><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation>

  Here admissibility means that the indexed supports are disjoint and their
  flags cover <math|\<cal-Z\><around|(|U|)>>. The exponential contributes its
  singleton coefficient <math|g<rsub|v>>, while the flagged product
  contributes exactly one differentiated factor. Replacing sums over flagged
  pairs by sums over dictionary indices is legitimate: if
  <math|B<rsub|0>=<big|sum><rsub|j><around|\||k<rsub|j><rsup|0><around|(|U|)>|\|>>
  and <math|B<rsub|v>=<big|sum><rsub|j><around|\||k<rsub|v,j><around|(|U|)>|\|>>,
  the absolute sum of all differentiated products, even without
  admissibility, is bounded by <math|B<rsub|v>*<big|prod><rsub|j><around|(|1+<around|\||k<rsub|j><rsup|0><around|(|U|)>|\|>|)>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|v>*e<rsup|B<rsub|0>>>.
  The corresponding vacuum sum is bounded by <math|e<rsup|B<rsub|0>>>. Finite
  expansions followed by absolute convergence establish the identity.

  Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:ope-single-coin> also explains why the ordinary
  singleton is determined by its density and flagged profile whenever
  <math|r<around|(|U|)>\<ne\>0>: subtract the flagged term and divide by
  <math|r<around|(|U|)>>. This is the uniqueness used when a transfer step
  first identifies the singleton density rather than the ordinary field
  itself.

  <subsubsection|Returning to the scale induction>

  Apply this construction to the regrouped arrays of the preceding section.
  If both hit bounds are at most <math|Z>, each grouped field has cost at
  most <math|4*Z/w<rsub|v>>, and the higher interactions have hit bound
  <math|Z>. The regrouping creates no separately recorded linear constant, so
  here <math|u<rsub|v>=0>. In particular, when <math|Z=1>, reducing the mark
  weight from <math|w<rsub|v>> to <math|w<rprime|'><rsub|v>=w<rsub|v>/8>
  leaves

  <\equation*>
    <text|singleton cost>\<leqslant\><frac|4|w<rsub|v>>=<frac|1|2*w<rprime|'><rsub|v>>.
  </equation*>

  The coefficient prices of the higher interactions only decrease under this
  reduction. This proves the representation and the singleton budget needed
  after a grouping operation, conditional on the stated dictionary and
  background-validity properties.

  Assume the input arrays and constants have the covariance inherited from
  the preceding regrouping. Translations and coordinate symmetries then
  preserve the ordinary singletons by reindexing the finite support sum in
  <eqref|eq:ope-single-pair>; conjugating symmetries also conjugate its
  scalar coefficients. The flagged profiles transform in the same way. No
  equivariance of the auxiliary choice of dictionary indices is needed,
  because only their summed profiles occur in the representation and in
  <eqref|eq:ope-single-coin>. This argument applies to any finite number of
  slots in each copied cell. The remaining analytic step is to replace these
  local fields by degree-bounded coefficient fields while controlling the
  error under transfer to the next scale. The singleton extraction alone does
  not provide that projection estimate.

  <subsection|Transporting a local representation through one
  scale><label|sec:local-transfer>

  We now explain the common transport argument used by the centered estimate
  and the local OPE construction. Its analytic input is a bound for
  integrating finitely many elementary insertions against one fixed
  background transition. The argument below turns that bound into a
  transition for arbitrary local fields represented by absolutely summable
  elementary insertions. It also restores the prescribed singleton profiles
  after integration. The same background transition is used throughout.

  <subsubsection|From an anchored estimate to a site estimate>

  The first estimate accounts for summing over all named sources. Write

  <\equation*>
    S<rsub|M>=e*<around|{|2*<around|(|16*M+9|)>|}><rsup|4>.
  </equation*>

  Suppose at most <math|N> marks are anchored at any one site. Let
  <math|X<rsub|i>> be supports, <math|A<rsub|i>> their nonempty sets of
  marks, and <math|f<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>. Every <math|i> with
  <math|f<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> is assumed to have some <math|v\<in\>A<rsub|i>>
  with <math|o<rsub|v>\<in\>X<rsub|i>>. If

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|i:v\<in\>A<rsub|i>>e<rsup|<around|(|a+1|)>*c<rsub|M><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>*f<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>c<space|1em><text|for
    every >v,
  </equation*>

  where <math|c\<geqslant\>0>, then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:transfer-root-hit><big|sum><rsub|i:x\<in\>X<rsub|i>>e<rsup|a*c<rsub|M><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>*f<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>N*S<rsub|M>*c<space|1em><text|for
    every >x.
  </equation>

  Indeed, the defining connected-cover bound gives, for <math|x,y\<in\>X>,

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|dist><around|(|x,y|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|4*M+2|)>*c<rsub|M><around|(|X|)>+2*M.
  </equation*>

  Hence a term on the left of <eqref|eq:transfer-root-hit> is at most

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|v\<in\>A<rsub|i>>e<rsup|1-<math-up|dist><around|(|x,o<rsub|v>|)>/<around|(|4*M+2|)>>*e<rsup|<around|(|a+1|)>*c<rsub|M><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>*f<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  Retain the anchored <math|v> to verify this inequality; all other terms are
  nonnegative. Sum first over <math|i>, use the anchored bound, and then the
  occupancy bound. It remains to check

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|y>e<rsup|-<math-up|dist><around|(|x,y|)>/<around|(|4*M+2|)>>\<leqslant\><around|{|2*<around|(|16*M+9|)>|}><rsup|4>.
  </equation*>

  Lift <math|y-x> to its minimal integer representative. These lifts are
  distinct, and their <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>> norm is at most four times their
  periodic maximum distance. With <math|b=1/<around|(|4*<around|(|4*M+2|)>|)>>,
  the sum is therefore bounded by

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>e<rsup|-b<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>=<around*|(|1+<frac|2|e<rsup|b>-1>|)><rsup|4>\<leqslant\><around|{|2*<around|(|1+b|)>/b|}><rsup|4>=<around|{|2*<around|(|16*M+9|)>|}><rsup|4>.
  </equation*>

  This proves the estimate, uniformly in the period. The argument also proves
  summability for countable activity families by bounding every finite
  subsum.

  <subsubsection|Preparing a finite family of insertions>

  Consider a fine representation <math|R> and a coarse representation
  <math|E>. Let <math|\<cal-T\>> be their block-integration operator. For a
  fine elementary insertion <math|M>, write <math|\<cal-Q\><around|(|M|)>>
  for its quoted coarse flagged profile. We assume this map is real linear
  under absolutely summable combinations in a fixed slot, with a uniform
  bound by the elementary insertion cost. These are properties of the fixed
  transition; their analytic construction is not being asserted here.

  For each mark <math|v>, suppose we have an anchored expansion, using only
  finitely many distinct slots but possibly countably many functions in each
  slot,

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|g<rsub|v>-u<rsub|v>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|j>z<rsub|v*j><space|0.17em><math-up|ord><around|(|M<rsub|v*j>|)>,>|<cell|q<rsub|v>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|j>z<rsub|v*j><space|0.17em><math-up|flag><around|(|M<rsub|v*j>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|q<rprime|'><rsub|v>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|j>z<rsub|v*j>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-Q\><around|(|M<rsub|v*j>|)>+\<cal-H\><rsub|E><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|v>|)>.>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
  </align*>

  Here <math|<big|sum><rsub|j><around|\||z<rsub|v*j>|\|><math-up|cost><around|(|M<rsub|v*j>|)>\<leqslant\>y<rsub|v>>,
  and <math|\<psi\><rsub|v>*<around|(|X,U|)>> is a coarse response anchored
  at the parent of <math|o<rsub|v>>. It has local dependence, is measurable
  and gauge invariant, and vanishes when <math|X> is healthy. Its cost
  hypothesis is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:transfer-response-budget><big|sum><rsub|X>e<rsup|<around|(|p+1|)>*c<rsub|M<rsub|E>><around|(|X|)>>*w<rsub|v>*sup<rsub|U><around|\||\<psi\><rsub|v>*<around|(|X,U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>W.
  </equation>

  An available majorant can replace the displayed supremum. The operator
  <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|E>> will be described below. The constant
  <math|u<rsub|v>> is outside the priced sum.

  Compress the countable sum separately in each slot. Its real and imaginary
  parts converge uniformly, since their absolute values are bounded by the
  summable insertion weights. The sums are real measurable local
  gauge-invariant functions in that same slot. Their bounds are the
  respective sums of absolute coefficient weights. Thus two real insertions,
  multiplied by <math|1> and <math|<math-up|i>>, replace the whole complex
  series. Both the old profiles and their quoted profiles are preserved, by
  the assumed continuity and linearity. The resulting finite list for mark
  <math|v> has weighted cost at most <math|2*w<rsub|v>*y<rsub|v>>. If
  <math|2*w<rsub|v>*y<rsub|v>\<leqslant\>d<rsub|0>> and the fine-site
  occupancy is at most <math|N<rsub|f>>, <eqref|eq:transfer-root-hit> bounds
  its site price at exponent <math|a> by <math|N<rsub|f>*S<rsub|M<rsub|R>>*d<rsub|0>>,
  provided <math|a+1\<leqslant\>\<nu\><rsub|R>>.

  Suppose the higher ordinary and flagged arrays in the fine local
  representation each have site price at most <math|q> at exponent <math|a>.
  The real/imaginary construction of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-singletons>,
  now applied to every coefficient of order at least two, represents them by
  finite elementary insertions at total site price at most <math|4*q>. The
  unused-slot property is used here. Combining the two lists gives a finite
  list with price

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:transfer-inventory-cost>T=N<rsub|f>*S<rsub|M<rsub|R>>*d<rsub|0>+4*q.
  </equation>

  Its ordinary sum is <math|<big|sum><rsub|v><around|(|g<rsub|v>-u<rsub|v>|)>*e<rsub|<around|{|v|}>>+H>,
  and its flagged sum is <math|<big|sum><rsub|v>q<rsub|v>*e<rsub|<around|{|v|}>>+K>,
  where <math|H,K> are the higher arrays. All marks of each insertion lie in
  its support.

  The elementary integration input takes a family of linear slot insertions.
  To turn the exponential insertions in the last display into that form,
  adjoin, at each ordinary slot, the product of every compatible set of at
  least two members of the list. Compatibility means that their mark sets are
  disjoint and their union is an allowed monomial. Keep all original members.
  The added product uses the same slot and support, and its price is the
  product of the constituent prices. No product is added to a flagged slot.

  This operation has price at most

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:transfer-expanded-cost>T+e<rsup|T>*T.
  </equation>

  To verify it, let <math|r<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>0> be the product of the
  marker toll, the modulus of the complex coefficient, and the elementary
  supremum-norm weight, excluding the support exponential
  <math|e<rsup|a*c<rsub|M><around|(|X|)>>>. For one ordinary slot put
  <math|s=<big|sum><rsub|j>r<rsub|j>>. Because every member has at least one
  mark in that support and <math|a\<geqslant\>0>, the site bound at any point
  of the support implies <math|s\<leqslant\>T>. The sum of the product prices
  of all nonempty subsets is at most

  <\equation*>
    <big|prod><rsub|j><around|(|1+r<rsub|j>|)>-1\<leqslant\>e<rsup|s>-1\<leqslant\>s*e<rsup|s>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|T>*s.
  </equation*>

  The added compatible subsets of size at least two form a subfamily.
  Multiplying by the support exponential and summing over distinct slots
  meeting a given site gives at most <math|e<rsup|T>*T>. Adding the original
  price proves <eqref|eq:transfer-expanded-cost>.

  The density identity is exact. For each ordinary slot, the sum of all the
  compatible products is its squarefree exponential minus one: expand
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|j><around|(|1+m<rsub|j>|)>=exp
  <around|(|<big|sum><rsub|j>m<rsub|j>|)>>, where each nonempty monomial
  <math|m<rsub|j>> has square zero. Terms with incompatible marks vanish in
  the algebra. Thus replacing the exponential at that slot by one plus the
  expanded list changes no coefficient. Flagged slots have not changed.
  Denote the resulting elementary density by <math|\<cal-E\>>. The fine
  density <math|X> satisfies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:transfer-elementary-identity>X=exp
    <around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|v>u<rsub|v>*e<rsub|<around|{|v|}>>|)>*\<cal-E\>.
  </equation>

  Every added insertion has at least two marks, so the sum of quoted
  singleton profiles is still <math|q<rprime|'><rsub|v>-\<cal-H\><rsub|E><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|v>|)>>.

  <subsubsection|Absorbing the coarse response>

  We state explicitly the nonlinear integration input used at this point. For
  every prepared finite list of price at most <math|M<rsub|\<ast\>>>, the
  operator <math|\<cal-T\>> produces ordinary and flagged arrays
  <math|<wide|F|^>,<wide|K|^>>, with the support and eligibility properties
  of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-singletons>, and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:transfer-nonlinear-input>\<cal-T\>*\<cal-E\>=e<rsup|E<rsub|E>>*exp
    <around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|X><wide|F|^><rsub|X>|)>*\<cal-G\><around|(|<wide|K|^>|)>.
  </equation>

  The ordinary array has no constant term and site price at most <math|k> at
  exponent <math|t+\<eta\>>; the flagged array has site price at most
  <math|u> at exponent <math|p>. Its vacuum coefficient is the coarse
  background profile, and its singleton coefficient is precisely the sum of
  the quoted singleton insertions. This estimate is required uniformly in the
  finite list and period, subject to the specified parent-site occupancy. Its
  production is a separate analytic obligation.

  Assume <math|T+e<rsup|T>*T\<leqslant\>M<rsub|\<ast\>>>. Require also that
  every prepared elementary density is integrable under the fine prior and in
  each transition slice. The lawful background representation and its
  transition-kernel property supply these integrability conditions in the
  application. Integration is componentwise on the finite-dimensional
  polynomial algebra, so its continuous linear maps commute with an
  integrable transition. In particular the constant polynomial exponential in
  <eqref|eq:transfer-elementary-identity> can be pulled out. We obtain the
  same representation as <eqref|eq:transfer-nonlinear-input>, with exponent
  <math|P=<big|sum><rsub|v>u<rsub|v>*e<rsub|<around|{|v|}>>+<big|sum><rsub|X><wide|F|^><rsub|X>>.

  Set <math|J<around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|v,X>\<psi\><rsub|v>*<around|(|X,U|)>*e<rsub|<around|{|v|}>>>.
  We next move <math|J> from the ordinary exponent into the flagged array,
  without changing the density. In a term of

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-G\><around|(|<wide|K|^>|)>*exp <around|(|J|)>,
  </equation*>

  regard each selected flagged pair and each selected response insertion
  <math|<around|(|v,X|)>> as a vertex, and join vertices whose supports
  overlap. Every nonzero response insertion meets a flag, because it vanishes
  on healthy supports. That flag is covered by a selected flagged pair in the
  gas term. Hence every connected component contains a flagged vertex. Within
  a component the flagged supports are disjoint; give the component the union
  support and the union of their flag sets. Define <math|<wide|K|\<bar\>>> as
  the sum of these component products with that output pair, retaining its
  required flag indicator. Every flag in a response support is covered by an
  old flagged vertex meeting that support, hence by a vertex in the same
  component. Thus the component flag set is exactly the set of flags in its
  union support.

  Connected-component decomposition is a bijection on nonzero terms. In the
  forward direction, different components have disjoint supports and their
  flag sets cover all flags. In the reverse direction, the constituent
  vertices of components with disjoint output supports cannot coincide or
  acquire a new overlap edge. Their union recovers the original selection.
  The products of their coefficients agree. Thus

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:transfer-closure>\<cal-G\><around|(|<wide|K|\<bar\>>|)>=\<cal-G\><around|(|<wide|K|^>|)>*exp
    <around|(|J|)>,<space|2em>e<rsup|P>*\<cal-G\><around|(|<wide|K|^>|)>=e<rsup|P-J>*\<cal-G\><around|(|<wide|K|\<bar\>>|)>.
  </equation>

  All these expansions are finite in support and mark indices.

  With no response vertex a connected component is a single old flagged
  vertex, since old flagged supports are disjoint. This proves preservation
  of the vacuum. With exactly one response vertex <math|<around|(|v,X|)>>,
  every flagged vertex in its component meets <math|X>: there are no edges
  between distinct flagged vertices, so any path to the response must be a
  direct edge. Let <math|\<Pi\><rsub|E>*<around|(|X;Y,Z;U|)>> be the sum of
  products of the background flagged activities over these nonempty,
  pairwise-disjoint collections meeting <math|X>, with union support <math|Y>
  after adjoining <math|X> and union flag set <math|Z>, including the output
  flag indicator. Then

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-H\><rsub|E><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|v>|)><around|(|Y,Z;U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|X>\<psi\><rsub|v>*<around|(|X,U|)>*\<Pi\><rsub|E>*<around|(|X;Y,Z;U|)>.
  </equation*>

  This defines the operator in the transition expansion above. Two or more
  response vertices have degree at least two and cannot contribute a
  singleton. Therefore

  <\equation*>
    <around|[|e<rsub|<around|{|v|}>>|]>*<wide|K|\<bar\>>=<around|[|e<rsub|<around|{|v|}>>|]>*<wide|K|^>+\<cal-H\><rsub|E><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|v>|)>=q<rprime|'><rsub|v>.
  </equation*>

  Replacing the background profiles in <math|\<Pi\><rsub|E>> by their
  countable indexed sums is justified by absolute convergence, bounded by the
  product of <math|1+<around|\||k<rsub|j><rsup|0>|\|>>, as in
  <eqref|eq:ope-single-coin>.

  The response insertions have site price at most
  <math|N<rsub|c>*S<rsub|M<rsub|E>>*W> at exponent <math|p>, by
  <eqref|eq:transfer-response-budget> and <eqref|eq:transfer-root-hit>, where
  <math|N<rsub|c>> is the parent-site occupancy. Put

  <\equation*>
    A=u+N<rsub|c>*S<rsub|M<rsub|E>>*W.
  </equation*>

  If

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:transfer-closure-budget>t+\<eta\>\<geqslant\>0,<space|2em><around|(|2*M<rsub|E>+1|)><rsup|4>*A\<leqslant\>p-<around|(|t+\<eta\>|)>,
  </equation>

  the connected-support estimate <eqref|eq:ope-forest-price> bounds the site
  price of <math|<wide|K|\<bar\>>> at exponent <math|t+\<eta\>> by
  <math|e<rsup|A>-1>. Its coefficients are products of old coefficient
  majorants, and the map from a constituent set to its output pair is unique;
  thus the counting lemma applies without a multiplicity factor. The ordinary
  higher coefficients retain price <math|k>, since <math|J> is linear. Local
  dependence, gauge invariance, measurability and the flag condition follow
  from finite products and support-local flag indicators.

  Finally suppose the singleton coefficient of the actual integrated density
  has already been identified as the linear density of a prescribed pair
  <math|<around|(|g<rprime|'><rsub|v>,k<rprime|'><rsub|v>|)>>, with
  <math|k<rprime|'><rsub|v>> dominated and profile
  <math|q<rprime|'><rsub|v>>. Assume the coarse unmarked density is nonzero.
  The uniqueness following <eqref|eq:ope-single-coin> identifies the ordinary
  singleton of <math|P-J> with <math|g<rprime|'><rsub|v>>. Removing all
  coefficients of degree zero and one now yields a local representation with
  these prescribed fields, with ordinary price <math|k> and flagged price
  <math|e<rsup|A>-1> at exponent <math|t+\<eta\>>.

  <subsubsection|Changing the mark weights>

  The final operation pays for changing weights. Suppose
  <math|w<rsub|v>,w<rprime|'><rsub|v>\<gtr\>0> and for every allowed monomial
  <math|B> with <math|<around|\||B|\|>\<geqslant\>2> supported in <math|X>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:transfer-reweight><big|prod><rsub|v\<in\>B>w<rprime|'><rsub|v>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|\<ast\>>*<big|prod><rsub|v\<in\>B>w<rsub|v>*<space|0.17em>e<rsup|\<eta\>*c<rsub|M<rsub|E>><around|(|X|)>>,<space|2em>a<rsub|\<ast\>>\<geqslant\>0.
  </equation>

  Multiply this inequality by each coefficient majorant and sum. It changes
  an exponent-<math|t+\<eta\>> price <math|C> into an exponent-<math|t> price
  at most <math|a<rsub|\<ast\>>*C>. Hence the transported representation has
  both higher-array prices bounded by <math|g> whenever

  <\equation*>
    a<rsub|\<ast\>>*k\<leqslant\>g,<space|2em>a<rsub|\<ast\>>*<around|(|e<rsup|A>-1|)>\<leqslant\>g.
  </equation*>

  Here is the specialization used in the scale induction. Start with weights
  <math|I<rsub|v>\<gtr\>0>, higher-array price <math|1>, and exponent at
  least <math|\<nu\><rsub|R>-4>. Choose <math|0\<less\>d\<leqslant\>1> and
  first reduce the weights to <math|w<rsub|v>=d*I<rsub|v>>. A monomial with
  at least two marks acquires the factor <math|d<rsup|<around|\||B|\|>>\<leqslant\>d>,
  so the higher-array price becomes <math|q=d>. Suppose the singleton
  transition costs obey <math|2*I<rsub|v>*y<rsub|v>\<leqslant\>1> and
  <math|I<rsub|v>*r<rsub|v>\<leqslant\>1>, with response cost
  <math|r<rsub|v>> measured at the full coarse exponent plus two. After
  multiplying by <math|w<rsub|v>>, the fine singleton budget is
  <math|d<rsub|0>=d> and the response budget is <math|W=d> at
  <math|p=\<nu\><rsub|E>+1>.

  Assume <math|\<nu\><rsub|R>,\<nu\><rsub|E>\<geqslant\>100>, and write
  <math|N> for a bound on both occupancies. The two numerical conditions are

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|T>|<cell|=N*S<rsub|M<rsub|R>>*d+4*d,>|<cell|T+e<rsup|T>*T>|<cell|\<leqslant\>M<rsub|\<ast\>>,>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number><label|eq:transfer-duty>>>|<row|<cell|A>|<cell|=u+N*S<rsub|M<rsub|E>>*d,>|<cell|<around|(|2*M<rsub|E>+1|)><rsup|4>*A>|<cell|\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>,>|<cell|e<rsup|A>-1>|<cell|\<leqslant\>1.>>>>
  </align>

  Use the nonlinear input with fine exponent <math|\<nu\><rsub|R>-4>,
  ordinary output exponent <math|\<nu\><rsub|E>+1/2> and price <math|1>, and
  flagged output exponent <math|\<nu\><rsub|E>+1> and price <math|u>. The
  fine anchored estimate requires only <math|\<nu\><rsub|R>-3\<leqslant\>\<nu\><rsub|R>>,
  and all exponents used for support counting are nonnegative.
  Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:transfer-duty> therefore verifies
  <eqref|eq:transfer-closure-budget> with <math|t=\<nu\><rsub|E>> and
  <math|\<eta\>=1/2>. Any new weights satisfying <eqref|eq:transfer-reweight>
  with <math|a<rsub|\<ast\>>=1> give a coarse local representation at
  exponent <math|\<nu\><rsub|E>> and price <math|1>. This proves the
  one-scale propagation from the stated elementary transition estimate and
  singleton transition expansions. It does not replace the proof of that
  analytic estimate or the construction of those expansions.

  <subsection|Local jet extraction and exact masked
  splitting><label|sec:local-splitting>

  A transferred local field is not yet a coefficient field. Its low-order jet
  has to be extracted, and subtracting that jet changes the support of its
  healthy-set indicator. The difference of indicators is retained as an
  explicit flagged response. We give the extraction and this exact splitting
  before applying it to the one-step integral.

  Fix a finite lattice, an anchor <math|o>, a degree <math|d>, and a
  coefficient frame <math|V<rsub|d>> for the gauge-invariant curvature-jet
  polynomials of weighted degree at most <math|d>. A curvature derivative of
  order <math|j> has weight <math|j+2>. Write
  <math|\<mu\><around|(|v|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>><around|\||v<rsub|\<alpha\>>|\|>>,
  <math|e<rsub|0>> for the unit coordinate,
  <math|v<rsup|\<circ\>>=v-v<rsub|0>*e<rsub|0>>, and <math|D<rsub|t>> for the
  diagonal map multiplying a coordinate with <math|r> curvature factors by
  <math|t<rsup|r>>. The budget consists of a threshold
  <math|0\<less\>t\<leqslant\>1>, derivative order <math|q\<geqslant\>d>,
  spatial power <math|p\<geqslant\>d>, positive support exponent <math|u>,
  and integer covering radius <math|B\<geqslant\>1>.

  A regular field on a support <math|X> is a measurable, gauge-invariant
  function depending only on its links, whose exponential-coordinate germ is
  <math|C<rsup|q>> at every configuration healthy on <math|X>. Its ordered
  left derivatives exist through order <math|q>, including at the threshold
  boundary. Write <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|o,X>\<leqslant\>c>
  when <math|c\<geqslant\>0> and all these derivatives of orders at most
  <math|q> have modulus at most <math|c>, for direction lists whose every
  entry satisfies

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||A<around|(|e|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>t*<around|(|1+<math-up|dist><around|(|e<rsub|->,o|)>|)><rsup|p>.
  </equation*>

  In particular the zeroth-order estimate bounds <math|f> on the healthy set.
  No derivatives of a healthy-set indicator occur in this definition.

  <subsubsection|Reading the unmasked jet>

  Let <math|H<rsub|o>*<around|(|J,h|)>> be the local test holonomy for a
  curvature jet <math|J>, and write <math|h\<cdot\>J> for weighted scaling,
  with component <math|h<rsup|j+2>*J<rsub|j>>. Its defining local identities
  are

  <\equation*>
    H<rsub|o>*<around|(|J,0|)>=1,<space|2em>H<rsub|o>*<around|(|J,h|)>=H<rsub|o>*<around|(|h\<cdot\>J,1|)>,<space|2em>H<rsub|o>*<around|(|g*J,h|)>=g*<space|0.17em>H<rsub|o>*<around|(|J,h|)>*<space|0.17em>g<rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  Here only the components of weights at most <math|d> are retained on the
  right. The finite-dimensional map <math|Z\<mapsto\>H<rsub|o>*<around|(|Z,1|)>>
  is analytic near <math|Z=0> in the genuine group exponential chart. These
  properties of the chosen test holonomy, and the existence of the local
  polynomial section below, are the geometric inputs used in this section.

  Here a support <math|X> is nonwrapping at <math|o> precisely when
  <math|10<math-up|dist><around|(|x,o|)>\<less\>n> for every <math|x\<in\>X>,
  where <math|n> is the lattice side. For such a support, define
  <math|\<Pi\><rsub|o,X>*f> by requiring that for every <math|J>, the
  functions

  <\equation*>
    h\<longmapsto\>f*<around|(|H<rsub|o>*<around|(|J,h|)>|)>,<space|2em>h\<longmapsto\><around|(|\<Pi\><rsub|o,X>*f|)>*<around|(|h\<cdot\>J|)>
  </equation*>

  have the same derivatives at zero through order <math|d>. For a wrapping
  support the convention is <math|\<Pi\><rsub|o,X>*f=f<around|(|1|)>*<space|0.17em>1>.

  We first prove existence in the nonwrapping case. In the finite space of
  truncated jets put <math|F<around|(|Z|)>=f*<around|(|H<rsub|o>*<around|(|Z,1|)>|)>>.
  The identity configuration is healthy, and the local logarithm of
  <math|H<rsub|o>*<around|(|Z,1|)>> is analytic and vanishes at zero. Thus
  <math|F> is <math|C<rsup|d>> near zero. Its Taylor polynomial

  <\equation*>
    T<around|(|Z|)>=<big|sum><rsub|r=0><rsup|d><frac|1|r!>*D<rsup|r>*F<around|(|0|)><around|[|Z,\<ldots\>,Z|]>
  </equation*>

  is a polynomial in the real coordinates of the curvature jets. Delete its
  monomials of weighted degree greater than <math|d>, obtaining <math|P>. The
  ordinary Taylor polynomial has the same derivatives as <math|F> through
  order <math|d>. Composing with the polynomial curve <math|h\<cdot\>J>
  preserves that equality: in the chain rule for a derivative of order
  <math|k\<leqslant\>d>, only derivatives of <math|F> of orders at most
  <math|k> enter. A deleted monomial becomes a multiple of <math|h<rsup|r>>
  with <math|r\<gtr\>d>, so contributes nothing through order <math|d>. Hence
  <math|P> has exactly the required test jets.

  Gauge invariance of <math|f> and covariance of <math|H> give the same test
  jets for <math|P*<around|(|g*J|)>> and <math|P<around|(|J|)>>. Their
  difference has weighted degree at most <math|d>. For each fixed <math|J>,
  all its coefficients as a polynomial in <math|h> vanish, since its
  derivatives at zero vanish. Taking <math|h=1> proves
  <math|P*<around|(|g*J|)>=P<around|(|J|)>>. Thus <math|P> belongs to the
  gauge-invariant label space. The same argument applied to two candidates
  proves uniqueness. Subtraction and scalar multiplication of the test
  identities now show that <math|\<Pi\><rsub|o,X>> is linear on regular
  fields with a fixed support. The wrapping convention has these properties
  as well.

  For later use set

  <\equation*>
    v<around|(|f|)>=D<rsub|t><math-up|coord><around|(|\<Pi\><rsub|o,X>*f|)>.
  </equation*>

  The uniform estimate for this map follows from a precise local bound on
  test holonomies. Namely, for every fixed <math|J,d> and
  <math|r\<leqslant\>d>, suppose the logarithmic test curve
  <math|Z<around|(|h|)>=log H<rsub|o>*<around|(|t*J,h|)>> satisfies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:local-test-log-input><around|\||Z<rsup|<around|(|r|)>><around|(|0|)><around|(|e|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|r>*<around|(|J,d|)>*<space|0.17em>t*<around|(|1+<math-up|dist><around|(|e<rsub|->,o|)>|)><rsup|max
    <around|(|r-1,0|)>>.
  </equation>

  The constants are independent of the period, anchor, support and threshold.
  We prove this estimate for the actual radial test below; first we explain
  precisely how it is used.

  Here is why it gives a constant <math|R<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>1> such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:local-read-price>\<mu\><around|(|v<around|(|f|)>|)>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|d>*c.
  </equation>

  First bound a mixed exponential-coordinate derivative of order <math|k> on
  directions <math|w<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,w<rsub|k>> bounded by fixed multiples
  <math|a<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,a<rsub|k>> of the allowed direction weight.
  Restrict the symmetric <math|k>th Fréchet derivative at zero to their span,
  using the map <math|L*x=<big|sum><rsub|i>x<rsub|i>*w<rsub|i>> from
  <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|k>>. Its diagonal at <math|x> is bounded by
  <math|<around|(|1+<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\||x<rsub|i>|\|>*a<rsub|i>|)><rsup|k>*c>:
  normalize <math|L*x> by this positive factor and use the repeated
  same-direction left derivative along <math|exp <around|(|s*L*x|)>>. Such a
  repeated derivative equals the derivative of <math|f<around|(|exp
  <around|(|s*L*x|)>|)>>; the one-parameter group identity proves this by
  induction. Only the Fréchet derivative in this finite-dimensional chart is
  being treated as symmetric.

  Diagonal evaluations separate symmetric tensors on
  <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|k>>. A finite number of them therefore bound any fixed
  mixed evaluation. For completeness, if separating linear functionals
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>> on a finite-dimensional space obey
  <math|<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|i>*A|\|>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|i>*c>, but no
  uniform norm bound follows, choose <math|A<rsub|n>,c<rsub|n>> with
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|n>|\<\|\|\>>\<gtr\><around|(|n+1|)>*c<rsub|n>>.
  A subsequence of <math|A<rsub|n>/<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|n>|\<\|\|\>>>
  converges on the unit sphere. Every <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>> vanishes on its
  limit, a contradiction. Apply this argument to the symmetric tensors just
  described. The resulting mixed bound depends on <math|k> and the
  <math|a<rsub|i>>, not on the lattice.

  The finite-order chain rule for <math|f<around|(|exp Z<around|(|h|)>|)>>,
  together with <eqref|eq:local-test-log-input> and <math|p\<geqslant\>d>,
  now bounds each fixed jet probe by <math|C<rsub|J,k>*c>. These probes, for
  <math|k\<leqslant\>d>, separate <math|V<rsub|d>>: a weighted polynomial all
  of whose test derivatives vanish is zero, by the uniqueness argument above.
  Apply the same finite-dimensional argument to <math|V<rsub|d>> to obtain
  <eqref|eq:local-read-price>. The weights are exactly <math|D<rsub|t>>,
  since evaluating a polynomial at <math|t*J> multiplies a monomial with
  <math|r> curvature factors by <math|t<rsup|r>>. For a wrapping support only
  <math|f<around|(|1|)>> remains, and <math|<around|\||f<around|(|1|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>c>.

  <subsubsection|Uniform bounds for the logarithmic test links>

  We establish <eqref|eq:local-test-log-input> by the same rescaling used to
  construct the test. A truncated radial connection is a finite sum of
  homogeneous connection tensors <math|J<rsub|j>> of degree <math|j+1>. On a
  positive link starting at a covering-space point <math|x> in direction
  <math|e<rsub|i>>, its coefficient at test scale <math|h> and amplitude
  <math|t> is

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|d-2>t*h<rsup|j+2>*J<rsub|j><around|(|e<rsub|i>|)><around|[|x+s*e<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,x+s*e<rsub|i>|]>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>s\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  An empty sum is allowed. The link is the endpoint of the
  right-multiplication equation <math|U<rprime|'>=U*A>,
  <math|U<around|(|0|)>=1>, with this Lie-algebra-valued coefficient. The
  local logarithm is taken only near <math|h=0>.

  We need an elementary parameter regularity fact for this equation. On
  continuous matrix paths with the supremum norm define
  <math|T<rsub|A>*V<around|(|s|)>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|s>V<around|(|r|)>*A<around|(|r|)>*<space|0.17em>d*r>.
  Its norm is at most <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>. If
  <math|A> depends linearly on a finite-dimensional parameter <math|z>, then
  <math|T<rsub|A>> is a continuous linear function of <math|z>. For <math|z>
  near zero, <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsub|A>|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>1>, and the
  integral equation has the unique solution

  <\equation*>
    U=<around|(|I-T<rsub|A>|)><rsup|-1>*1=<big|sum><rsub|k=0><rsup|\<infty\>>T<rsub|A><rsup|k>*1.
  </equation*>

  The convergent operator power series makes this an analytic function of
  <math|z>. The given link solution satisfies the same integral equation, so
  agrees with it. Endpoint evaluation and the analytic local group logarithm
  therefore give an analytic logarithmic endpoint map <math|E<around|(|z|)>>
  near zero, with <math|E<around|(|0|)>=0>. This argument applies to the
  finite-dimensional space of all polynomial-in-<math|s> connection profiles
  of the indicated degrees, not only to profiles coming from a particular
  link.

  Put <math|R=1+<math-up|dist><around|(|e<rsub|->,o|)>>, <math|r=R<rsup|-1>>
  and <math|y=r<space|0.17em><math-up|lift><around|(|e<rsub|->-o|)>>. The
  centered lift has each coordinate at most <math|R> in modulus, so
  <math|<around|\||y|\|><rsub|2>\<leqslant\>2>, while
  <math|0\<less\>r\<leqslant\>1>. Consider the polynomial profile

  <\equation*>
    P*<around|(|y,r,a,h|)><around|(|s|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|d-2>a*h<rsup|j+2>*J<rsub|j><around|(|e<rsub|i>|)><around|[|y+r*s*e<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,y+r*s*e<rsub|i>|]>.
  </equation*>

  For all parameters <math|<around|(|y,r,a|)>> the profile is zero at
  <math|h=0>. Consequently

  <\equation*>
    Q<rsub|k>*<around|(|y,r,a|)>=<around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d<rsup|k>|d*h<rsup|k>>*E*<around|(|P*<around|(|y,r,a,h|)>|)>|\|><rsub|h=0>
  </equation*>

  is smooth in <math|<around|(|y,r,a|)>>: near each point it is obtained by
  composing a polynomial map with the analytic germ <math|E>, then
  differentiating a fixed finite number of times. Also
  <math|Q<rsub|k>*<around|(|y,r,0|)>=0>. On the compact set
  <math|<around|\||y|\|><rsub|2>\<leqslant\>2>,
  <math|0\<leqslant\>r,a\<leqslant\>1>, its derivative with respect to
  <math|a> is bounded. Integrating that derivative from zero to <math|a>
  gives <math|<around|\||Q<rsub|k>*<around|(|y,r,a|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|J,d,k,i>*a>.
  This compactness is in the fixed profile-parameter space and has no
  dimension dependence on the lattice.

  Homogeneity of <math|J<rsub|j>> shows that the actual link at scale
  <math|h*r> and amplitude <math|t> has profile
  <math|P*<around|(|y,r,t*r,h|)>>: both coefficients are
  <math|t*h<rsup|j+2>*r<rsup|j+2>*J<rsub|j><around|(|e<rsub|i>|)><around|[|x+s*e<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,x+s*e<rsub|i>|]>>.
  Uniqueness of the link equation identifies their endpoints. Differentiating
  this identity at zero gives

  <\equation*>
    r<rsup|k><around|\||Z<rsup|<around|(|k|)>><around|(|0|)><around|(|e|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|J,d,k,i>*t*r.
  </equation*>

  For <math|k\<geqslant\>1> this is precisely
  <math|<around|\||Z<rsup|<around|(|k|)>><around|(|0|)><around|(|e|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|J,d,k,i>*t*R<rsup|k-1>>.
  For <math|k=0> the logarithm is zero. Taking the maximum over the four
  directions proves <eqref|eq:local-test-log-input>. The argument uses only
  derivatives at zero; it asserts no common analytic radius for links
  arbitrarily far from the anchor.

  <subsubsection|The support correction>

  Choose a linear local section of the labels, supported on a fixed stencil
  <math|Y> containing <math|o>. Its placed field
  <math|P<rsub|t><around|(|v|)>> represents <math|D<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*v>, has
  the prescribed jet, and satisfies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:local-section-price><around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|t><around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|o,Y>\<leqslant\>w*\<mu\><around|(|v|)>,<space|2em>w\<geqslant\>0.
  </equation>

  The stencil is assumed to fit without wrapping. The section sends
  <math|e<rsub|0>> to the constant one. These are hypotheses about the chosen
  section; they do not follow from the abstract existence of <math|\<Pi\>>.
  Let <math|\<chi\><rsub|X>> be the indicator that <math|X> is healthy, and
  put

  <\equation*>
    p<rsub|t><around|(|v|)>=v<rsub|0>+\<chi\><rsub|Y>*P<rsub|t><around|(|v<rsup|\<circ\>>|)>,<space|2em>Z=X\<cup\>Y.
  </equation*>

  Then <math|<around|\||p<rsub|t><around|(|v|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+w|)>*\<mu\><around|(|v|)>>
  everywhere, and <math|p<rsub|t><around|(|v|)>=P<rsub|t><around|(|v|)>> when
  <math|Y> is healthy. Define

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|g>|<cell|=f-P<rsub|t><around|(|v<around|(|f|)>|)>,<eq-number>>>|<row|<cell|r>|<cell|=\<chi\><rsub|X>*f-p<rsub|t><around|(|v<around|(|f|)>|)>-\<chi\><rsub|Z>*g.<eq-number><label|eq:local-rebate>>>>>
  </align>

  These definitions give the exact, pointwise identity

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:local-atom-identity>\<chi\><rsub|X>*f=p<rsub|t><around|(|v<around|(|f|)>|)>+\<chi\><rsub|Z>*g+r.
  </equation>

  The field <math|g> is regular on <math|Z>. It has zero identity value and,
  when <math|Z> is nonwrapping, zero test jets through order <math|d>. Indeed
  <math|Y> is nonwrapping, so <math|Z> is nonwrapping exactly when <math|X>
  is; subtract the two equal jets in that case. In the wrapping case
  <math|v<around|(|f|)>=f<around|(|1|)>*e<rsub|0>>, and the same subtraction
  gives the zero identity value.

  The response <math|r> is measurable, gauge invariant, and determined by the
  links and final flags in <math|Z>. It vanishes whenever <math|Z> is
  healthy, by <eqref|eq:local-atom-identity> and
  <math|p<rsub|t><around|(|v|)>=P<rsub|t><around|(|v|)>> there. Put
  <math|A<rsub|d>=1+<around|(|1+w|)>*R<rsub|d>>. Then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:local-atom-cost><around|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|o,Z>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|d>*c,<space|2em><around|\||r<around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|d>*c.
  </equation>

  The first estimate is the triangle inequality using
  <eqref|eq:local-read-price>\U<eqref|eq:local-section-price>. For the
  second, <math|r=0> on the healthy set of <math|Z>. On its complement
  <math|\<chi\><rsub|Z>=0>, so <math|<around|\||r|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<chi\><rsub|X>*f|\|>+<around|\||p<rsub|t><around|(|v<around|(|f|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>c+<around|(|1+w|)>*R<rsub|d>*c>.
  This proves the global bound without differentiating any mask.

  <subsubsection|Putting the response into the existing gas>

  Let <math|K<rsub|j>> be the original flagged activities, with supports
  <math|P<rsub|j>> and nonempty flag inventories
  <math|I<rsub|j>\<subseteq\>P<rsub|j>>. An admissible finite family has
  disjoint supports and its inventories cover exactly the final flags. Let
  <math|\<cal-G\><around|(|U|)>> be its gas sum. For each response
  <math|r<rsub|i>> supported on <math|Z<rsub|i>> and each nonempty pairwise
  disjoint family <math|q> of old activities meeting <math|Z<rsub|i>>, assume
  a distinct available address with support and inventory

  <\equation*>
    Z<rsub|i>\<cup\><big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>q>P<rsub|j>,<space|2em><big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>q>I<rsub|j>.
  </equation*>

  The address's additional eligibility predicate must hold whenever its
  support has exactly this flag inventory. No zero value of its old activity
  is required. Insert at this address the coefficient

  <\equation*>
    r<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>q>K<rsub|j><around|(|U|)>
  </equation*>

  when eligible, and zero otherwise. Denote the resulting source array by
  <math|\<cal-K\><around|(|r|)>>. This is a linear operation in <math|r>,
  with addresses fixed before their values are evaluated.

  Its exact marked-gas sum is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:local-gas-recovery>\<cal-G\><rsup|<around|[|1|]>><around|(|\<cal-K\><around|(|r|)>|)><around|(|U|)>=<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i>r<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>|)>*\<cal-G\><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation>

  To prove this, take a nonzero term on the right, consisting of <math|i> and
  an admissible old family <math|b>. Because <math|r<rsub|i>> vanishes on
  healthy <math|Z<rsub|i>>, some final flag lies in <math|Z<rsub|i>>. Exact
  inventory coverage implies that the subfamily
  <math|q=<around|{|j\<in\>b:P<rsub|j>\<cap\>Z<rsub|i>\<ne\>\<varnothing\>|}>>
  is nonempty. Replace precisely this subfamily by the designated new
  address. Every remaining support is disjoint from the new support; the
  inventories still cover the same flags. This produces an admissible pointed
  family on the left, with identical value. Conversely, expand the
  distinguished new address into its encoded family <math|q>. The other
  supports are disjoint from its total support, so <math|q> is exactly the
  subfamily meeting <math|Z<rsub|i>>. This recovers <math|i,b> uniquely. Thus
  there is a value-preserving bijection of nonzero terms. Absolute
  summability, established next, permits reindexing.

  Let <math|C<rsub|B><around|(|X|)>> be the covering number used in the local
  norm and set

  <\equation*>
    m*<around|(|X,a|)>=e<rsup|u*C<rsub|B><around|(|X|)>>*a,<space|2em>m<rsub|+>*<around|(|X,a|)>=e<rsup|<around|(|u+2|)>*C<rsub|B><around|(|X|)>>*a.
  </equation*>

  Suppose <math|<around|\||K<rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|j>>,
  <math|b<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>0>, and, for a site price
  <math|\<varsigma\>\<geqslant\>0> (not to be confused with the coefficient
  vectors <math|v> above), <math|<big|sum><rsub|j:x\<in\>P<rsub|j>>m*<around|(|P<rsub|j>,b<rsub|j>|)>\<leqslant\>\<varsigma\>>
  at every site <math|x>. For one response with
  <math|<around|\||r<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|i>>, the inserted array
  has total mass at most

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:local-merge-cost>m*<around|(|Z<rsub|i>,a<rsub|i>|)>*<around*|(|e<rsup|<around|\||Z<rsub|i>|\|>*\<varsigma\>>-1|)>.
  </equation>

  Indeed the total mass of old activities meeting <math|Z<rsub|i>> is at most
  <math|<around|\||Z<rsub|i>|\|>*\<varsigma\>>, by summing their site bounds
  over <math|Z<rsub|i>>. The mass of the union is at most the product of the
  masses, since each old support meets <math|Z<rsub|i>> and their covering
  numbers are subadditive along such unions. Summing products over nonempty
  finite subsets is bounded by <math|e<rsup|<big|sum>b>-1>; dropping the
  disjointness restriction only increases this nonnegative sum. This proves
  <eqref|eq:local-merge-cost>. If the sum of the right sides of
  <eqref|eq:local-merge-cost> over <math|i> is finite, summing these bounds
  proves absolute domination of the inserted array and hence of its pointed
  gas series. The reserved-mass hypothesis below will establish this
  finiteness. The old gas series is absolutely convergent under the same site
  bound on the finite lattice. This justifies <eqref|eq:local-gas-recovery>.

  <subsubsection|The two-unit reserve in the support exponent>

  Let <math|T> bound <math|C<rsub|B><around|(|Y|)>> and put
  <math|h<rsub|B>=<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>>. The elementary covering
  estimates used here are

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|B>*<around|(|X\<cup\>Y|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|B><around|(|X|)>+T*<space|1em><around|(|o\<in\>X\<cap\>Y|)>,<space|2em><around|\||X|\|>\<leqslant\>h<rsub|B>*C<rsub|B><around|(|X|)>.
  </equation*>

  Set <math|F<rsub|d>=e<rsup|<around|(|u+2|)>*T>*A<rsub|d>>. Then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:local-join-price>m<rsub|+>*<around|(|X\<cup\>Y,A<rsub|d>*c|)>\<leqslant\>F<rsub|d>*m<rsub|+>*<around|(|X,c|)>.
  </equation>

  This follows immediately by inserting the first covering inequality in the
  exponential. If <math|h<rsub|B>*\<varsigma\>\<leqslant\>1>, then for
  <math|a\<geqslant\>0>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:local-meeting-reserve>m*<around|(|X,a|)>*<around|(|e<rsup|<around|\||X|\|>*\<varsigma\>>-1|)>\<leqslant\>h<rsub|B>*\<varsigma\>*<space|0.17em>m<rsub|+>*<around|(|X,a|)>.
  </equation>

  For <math|D=C<rsub|B><around|(|X|)>>, put
  <math|q<rsub|0>=h<rsub|B>*\<varsigma\>>. We have
  <math|<around|\||X|\|>*\<varsigma\>\<leqslant\>D*q<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>D>.
  Using <math|e<rsup|x>-1\<leqslant\>x*e<rsup|x>> for <math|x\<geqslant\>0>
  and <math|D\<leqslant\>e<rsup|D>> gives
  <math|e<rsup|<around|\||X|\|>*\<varsigma\>>-1\<leqslant\>q<rsub|0>*D*e<rsup|D>\<leqslant\>q<rsub|0>*e<rsup|2*D>>,
  as required.

  Consider now a raw expansion with seed <math|v<rsub|0>>, regular fields
  <math|f<rsub|i>> on anchored supports <math|X<rsub|i>>, and an existing
  forced source <math|k>. Suppose <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|o,X<rsub|i>>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|i>>
  and

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|i>m<rsub|+>*<around|(|X<rsub|i>,c<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>a,<space|2em><math-up|mass><around|(|k|)>\<leqslant\>\<kappa\>*b,<space|2em>\<kappa\>=<sqrt|\<varsigma\>>\<gtr\>0,<space|1em>h<rsub|B>*\<kappa\>\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  All constants <math|a,b> are nonnegative, and <math|\<varsigma\>> is the
  site price of the original flagged activities <math|K<rsub|j>> of the
  preceding subsection. Form <math|v<rsub|i>=v<around|(|f<rsub|i>|)>>,
  <math|g<rsub|i>,r<rsub|i>> as above, and define the coefficient field by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:local-built-field>v=v<rsub|0>+<big|sum><rsub|i>v<rsub|i>,<space|2em><text|regular
    terms >g<rsub|i><text| on >X<rsub|i>\<cup\>Y,<space|2em><text|forced
    array >k+\<cal-K\><around|(|r|)>.
  </equation>

  The coordinate sum converges absolutely by <eqref|eq:local-read-price> and
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>c<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>a>. Its change is bounded by
  <math|R<rsub|d>*a>. The regular remainder mass is at most
  <math|F<rsub|d>*a>. Furthermore <math|h<rsub|B>*\<varsigma\>=h<rsub|B>*\<kappa\><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>\<kappa\>\<leqslant\>1>,
  so <eqref|eq:local-meeting-reserve> and <eqref|eq:local-join-price> bound
  the added forced mass by <math|\<kappa\>*F<rsub|d>*a>. Measuring forced
  mass in units of <math|\<kappa\>>, so that the forced array has normalized
  mass at most <math|b+F<rsub|d>*a>, the normalized total remainder bound is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:local-built-cost>2*F<rsub|d>*a+b.
  </equation>

  The original density is preserved exactly: sum
  <eqref|eq:local-atom-identity>, use linearity of <math|p<rsub|t>>, and then
  <eqref|eq:local-gas-recovery> for the response terms. Multiplication by the
  common vacuum exponential preserves this identity. Every sum is absolutely
  dominated by the bounds just proved. In particular this construction uses
  the original vacuum activities and the original threshold predicates
  throughout.

  Finally, collect the responses with the same support:
  <math|\<psi\><around|(|Z|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i:X<rsub|i>\<cup\>Y=Z>r<rsub|i>>.
  Their majorants obey

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|Z>m<rsub|+>*<around*|(|Z,<big|sum><rsub|i:X<rsub|i>\<cup\>Y=Z>A<rsub|d>*c<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>F<rsub|d>*a.
  </equation*>

  Consequently <math|\<psi\>> is an anchored, gauge-invariant response
  vanishing on healthy supports, with the stated reserved mass. Its insertion
  profile is exactly the profile of <math|\<cal-K\><around|(|r|)>>: grouping
  the absolutely convergent terms by their encoded support and inventory
  proves the equality. These are the response and profile identities needed
  by the marked transport of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-transfer>.
  The raw expansion and its bounds still have to be produced by the one-step
  integral; this section proves the projection, mask correction, and cost
  conversion once that expansion is given.

  <subsection|Constructing the local polynomial
  sections><label|sec:local-sections>

  We now construct the fixed section and its price used in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-splitting>. The construction uses a
  finite collection of based, transported plaquettes. The finite collection
  and all constants are chosen before the lattice period and threshold.

  <subsubsection|A finite sampler for radial jets>

  Let <math|\<cal-J\><rsub|d>> be the finite-dimensional real space of radial
  connection jets <math|A=<around|(|A<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,A<rsub|d-2>|)>>. Here
  <math|A<rsub|j>> is a homogeneous one-form of polynomial degree <math|j+1>
  satisfying the radial identity <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>x<rsub|i>*A<rsub|j,i><around|(|x|)>=0>.
  It is this radial description of curvature jets that underlies the label
  space. If <math|d\<less\>2>, the space is zero. For a lattice path from the
  origin to <math|x> and an oriented plaquette at <math|x>, let
  <math|s<around|(|U|)>> be the Lie-algebra curvature of that plaquette,
  transported back to the origin. Its value is zero at the identity. Define
  the sampler <math|S<rsub|s><around|(|A|)>> by evaluating <math|s> on the
  test holonomy of the truncated radial connection <math|A> at scale one.

  The endpoint argument in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-splitting>
  shows that <math|S<rsub|s>> is analytic near zero. Differentiating the link
  integral equation at the zero connection gives the line integral of
  <math|A>. At the identity, the derivative of a product is the sum of its
  factor derivatives, and inversion changes their signs. The derivative of
  transported plaquette curvature is consequently

  <\equation*>
    D*S<rsub|s><around|(|0|)>*A=<big|oint><rsub|\<partial\>*\<square\><rsub|x,i*j>><big|sum><rsub|l=0><rsup|d-2>A<rsub|l>.
  </equation*>

  Transport along the based path contributes nothing to this derivative: it
  conjugates a curvature whose value at zero is zero. The Lie projection of
  the linearized plaquette is its Lie-algebra value.

  These linear samplers jointly separate <math|\<cal-J\><rsub|d>>. To see
  this, for a homogeneous <math|A<rsub|l>> write

  <\equation*>
    N<rsub|l><around|(|x,h|)>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1><space|-0.17em><big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1>\<partial\><rsub|i>*A<rsub|l,j>*<around|(|x+h*s*e<rsub|j>+h*r*e<rsub|i>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*r*<space|0.17em>d*s-<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1><space|-0.17em><big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1>\<partial\><rsub|j>*A<rsub|l,i>*<around|(|x+h*s*e<rsub|i>+h*r*e<rsub|j>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*r*<space|0.17em>d*s.
  </equation*>

  The fundamental theorem of calculus identifies the unit-square circulation
  with <math|N<rsub|l><around|(|x,1|)>>. Also
  <math|N<rsub|l><around|(|h*x,h|)>=h<rsup|l>*N<rsub|l><around|(|x,1|)>> and
  <math|N<rsub|l><around|(|x,0|)>=\<partial\><rsub|i>*A<rsub|l,j><around|(|x|)>-\<partial\><rsub|j>*A<rsub|l,i><around|(|x|)>>.
  Suppose all sampled circulations vanish. If <math|k> is the largest
  remaining degree, multiply the identity at an integer point <math|z> by
  <math|h<rsup|k>> and use homogeneity to get

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|l=0><rsup|k>h<rsup|k-l>*N<rsub|l><around|(|h*z,h|)>=0.
  </equation*>

  For any real <math|x>, choose integer <math|z<rsub|h>> with
  <math|h*z<rsub|h>\<to\>x> as <math|h*\<downarrow\>*0>. Continuity gives
  <math|N<rsub|k><around|(|x,0|)>=0>. Thus <math|d*A<rsub|k>=0>. The radial
  identity and Euler's identity give, componentwise,

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|i>x<rsub|i>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|i>*A<rsub|k,j>-\<partial\><rsub|j>*A<rsub|k,i>|)>=<around|(|k+2|)>*A<rsub|k,j>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed the first term is <math|<around|(|k+1|)>*A<rsub|k,j>>, and
  differentiation of <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>x<rsub|i>*A<rsub|k,i>=0> makes
  the second term <math|-A<rsub|k,j>>. Hence <math|A<rsub|k>=0>. Descending
  induction proves <math|A=0>.

  A finite subcollection already separates. Start with the whole space as the
  common kernel. If that kernel is nonzero, one of the samplers is nonzero on
  a vector in it; adding that sampler strictly reduces the kernel dimension.
  After finitely many steps the kernel is zero. Fix the resulting slots
  <math|s<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,s<rsub|m>> and the analytic map
  <math|S=<around|(|S<rsub|s<rsub|1>>,\<ldots\>,S<rsub|s<rsub|m>>|)>> into
  the panel of <math|m> Lie-algebra values. Its derivative
  <math|D=D*S<around|(|0|)>> is injective.

  <subsubsection|Equivariant recovery and polynomial substitution>

  The panel has its invariant Euclidean inner product. Constant gauge
  transformations act orthogonally there, preserve
  <math|<math-up|ran><space|0.17em>D>, and intertwine <math|D> with their
  action on <math|\<cal-J\><rsub|d>>. Let <math|\<pi\>> be orthogonal
  projection onto <math|<math-up|ran><space|0.17em>D> and put
  <math|C=D<rsup|-1>*\<pi\>>. The range and its orthogonal complement are
  invariant, so <math|C> is equivariant and <math|C*D=I>. The analytic
  inverse function theorem applied to <math|C*S> at zero gives an analytic
  local inverse <math|g>. Set <math|F=g*C>. Then

  <\equation*>
    F<around|(|0|)>=0,<space|2em>F<around|(|S<around|(|A|)>|)>=A
  </equation*>

  near zero. For each fixed gauge transformation, equivariance of <math|C*S>
  and uniqueness of its local inverse imply equivariance of <math|F> as
  germs. This assertion needs no common invariant neighborhood for all
  transformations simultaneously.

  Let <math|T<rsub|d>*F> be the Taylor polynomial of this one recovery map
  through ordinary degree <math|d>. It is equivariant: differentiate the germ
  identity <math|F*<around|(|R*x|)>=R<rprime|'>*F<around|(|x|)>> at zero, and
  use linearity of <math|R,R<rprime|'>> in each Taylor coefficient. It has
  zero constant term. For each real homogeneous basis label
  <math|p<rsub|\<alpha\>>> define a polynomial in panel coordinates by

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|x|)>=p<rsub|\<alpha\>>*<around|(|T<rsub|d>*F<around|(|x|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  It is gauge invariant and real valued on real panels, and
  <math|P<rsub|0>=1>. If <math|p<rsub|\<alpha\>>> has
  <math|r<rsub|\<alpha\>>> curvature factors, every monomial of
  <math|P<rsub|\<alpha\>>> has ordinary panel degree at least
  <math|r<rsub|\<alpha\>>>: each substituted coordinate has zero constant
  term, so each of the <math|r<rsub|\<alpha\>>> factors contributes at least
  one panel coordinate. No deletion of higher-degree terms after substitution
  is necessary.

  These polynomials read the correct jets. Along the radial curve
  <math|A<around|(|h|)>=h\<cdot\>J>, put <math|W<around|(|h|)>=S<around|(|A<around|(|h|)>|)>>.
  It is smooth and <math|W<around|(|0|)>=0>. The Taylor polynomial
  <math|T<rsub|d>*F> and <math|F> have identical derivatives through order
  <math|d> at zero, so their compositions with <math|W> do too. Since
  <math|F<around|(|W<around|(|h|)>|)>=A<around|(|h|)>> near zero, composition
  with the polynomial <math|p<rsub|\<alpha\>>> gives

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|\<alpha\>>*<around*|(|s<rsub|1>*<around|(|H*<around|(|J,h|)>|)>,\<ldots\>,s<rsub|m>*<around|(|H*<around|(|J,h|)>|)>|)><space|1em><text|and><space|1em>p<rsub|\<alpha\>>*<around|(|h\<cdot\>J|)>
  </equation*>

  the same derivatives through order <math|d>. Linearly extending the
  <math|P<rsub|\<alpha\>>> therefore gives a local section <math|T<rsub|0>>
  of every label in <math|V<rsub|d>> at the level of test jets.

  <subsubsection|Lattice symmetry and a common stencil>

  Let <math|\<cal-H\>> be the finite group of signed coordinate permutations.
  For its action <math|\<rho\>> on labels, define

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:local-section-average>T<around|(|v|)><around|(|U|)>=<frac|1|<around|\||\<cal-H\>|\|>>*<big|sum><rsub|s\<in\>\<cal-H\>>T<rsub|0>*<around|(|\<rho\><around|(|s<rsup|-1>|)>*v|)><around|(|<math-up|pull><rsub|s><space|0.17em>U|)>.
  </equation>

  The test holonomy transforms by the inverse tensor action. This can be
  checked directly in the link equation. Positive coordinate changes
  transport its coefficient. For a reversed link, if <math|u<rprime|'>=u*A>,
  then <math|u<around|(|1|)><rsup|-1>*u*<around|(|1-h|)>> solves the reversed
  equation with coefficient <math|-A*<around|(|1-h|)>> and starts at the
  identity. Uniqueness identifies its endpoint with the inverse link.
  Constant conjugation likewise conjugates the solution of the equation.
  These identities prove the stated tensor and gauge transformation laws of
  the sampler.

  It follows that each summand of <eqref|eq:local-section-average> has label
  <math|v>. Their average does also. Reindexing the finite group proves the
  signed-permutation covariance <math|T<around|(|v|)><around|(|<math-up|pull><rsub|s><space|0.17em>U|)>=T*<around|(|\<rho\><around|(|s|)>*v|)><around|(|U|)>>.
  Its unit is one, and it commutes with ordinary conjugation because the
  basis labels and the initial panel polynomials are real. Each transported
  plaquette transforms into another transported plaquette, possibly with a
  minus sign from reversing its orientation. Take the finite union of the
  slots in all these terms and enclose their paths and plaquettes in a
  centered box <math|Y>.

  The average remains polynomial in this common finite list of slots.
  Replacing a slot by a signed slot changes no monomial degree. Moreover
  <math|\<rho\><around|(|s|)>> preserves the number of curvature factors in a
  homogeneous label. Thus the polynomial representing the column
  <math|T<around|(|e<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>> still has no terms of panel degree
  below <math|r<rsub|\<alpha\>>>. Each slot is itself a polynomial in the
  positive-link matrix entries and their conjugates: use matrix
  multiplication for the path and plaquette, conjugate transpose for inverse
  links, and the real-linear Lie projection of
  <math|<around|(|M-M<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>/2> for curvature. A real-linear matrix
  functional is a linear combination of real and imaginary entry parts, each
  a linear combination of entries and their conjugates. Therefore <math|T> is
  a genuine finite polynomial cylinder on <math|Y>.

  <subsubsection|Threshold prices at every period>

  Fix derivative order <math|q> and spatial power <math|p>. For any period,
  anchor <math|o> and allowed direction <math|A>, a link from the fixed
  stencil satisfies <math|<around|\||A<around|(|e|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|Y>*t>,
  since its torus distance from <math|o> is at most the size of its chosen
  covering-space position. An ordered left derivative of a positive-link
  matrix for the list <math|<around|(|A<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,A<rsub|k>|)>> is
  exactly <math|<math-up|col><around|(|A<rsub|k><around|(|e|)>|)>*\<cdots\><math-up|col><around|(|A<rsub|1><around|(|e|)>|)>*U<rsub|e>>,
  where <math|<math-up|col>> is the Lie-algebra matrix representation. The
  inverse link is its conjugate transpose. Consequently all derivatives of
  order <math|k\<leqslant\>q> of any fixed path matrix are bounded by
  <math|C*t<rsup|k>>, uniformly in the period and <math|t>. The product rule
  has finitely many terms, and compactness of the matrix group bounds the
  undifferentiated factors. Smoothness holds globally in exponential
  coordinates.

  The same derivative bound holds for each transported plaquette slot. At
  order zero a stronger estimate comes from health: on a configuration
  healthy on the projected stencil, the plaquette curvature has norm at most
  <math|t>, and parallel transport preserves that norm. At every positive
  order <math|k>, the previous bound is at most <math|C*t> because
  <math|0\<less\>t\<leqslant\>1>. Thus each slot and each of its tested
  derivatives through order <math|q> has norm at most <math|C*t> on that
  healthy set. This also holds when the stencil wraps: the path identities
  and the bound on torus distance still apply.

  Apply the ordered product rule to a monomial with <math|r> slot factors.
  Each term assigns the derivatives to those factors in their original
  orders. Every factor, including those receiving no derivative, contributes
  a bound <math|C<rsub|i>*t>. The monomial is therefore bounded by
  <math|C*t<rsup|r>> through order <math|q>. Finite summation and
  <math|t\<leqslant\>1> give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:local-section-column-price><around|\<\|\|\>|T<around|(|e<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|o,Y>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|d,q,p>*t<rsup|r<rsub|\<alpha\>>>.
  </equation>

  This proves both regularity and the exact factor-count threshold power of
  the constructed section. Multiplying its column coefficients by
  <math|t<rsup|-r<rsub|\<alpha\>>>> and using the triangle inequality yields
  <eqref|eq:local-section-price> with <math|w=C<rsub|d,q,p>>.

  All choices are independent of the period and threshold. They can also be
  priced before choosing a scale: for an integer <math|D>, choose one bound
  for each triple <math|d,q,p\<leqslant\>D> and set

  <\equation*>
    w<rsub|D>=1+<big|sum><rsub|d,q,p\<leqslant\>D>max
    <around|(|0,C<rsub|d,q,p>|)>.
  </equation*>

  It bounds every requested price in this finite range. The stencil fits
  whenever the side exceeds <math|max<rsub|x\<in\>Y,i>
  20*<around|(|<around|\||x<rsub|i>|\|>+1|)>>. These are fixed local
  requirements for the later calendar to satisfy. The construction here
  supplies the section and its uniform price; it does not choose that
  calendar.

  <subsection|Polynomial moments and decay of irrelevant
  insertions><label|sec:polynomial-moment-attenuation>

  The subtraction in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-splitting> removes
  the low-order holonomy tests of an insertion. We now explain why this
  improves its coarse-to-fine estimate. The argument has two parts: a
  qualitative implication from holonomy tests to polynomial moments, and a
  quantitative decomposition of the actual interpolating field. Both use the
  same centered lattice lift as the local subtraction.

  <subsubsection|From holonomy tests to polynomial moments>

  Let <math|X> be a nonwrapping support about <math|o> on a torus of side at
  least two. Let <math|f> be local on <math|X>, gauge invariant, and
  <math|C<rsup|d>> in logarithmic coordinates near the identity. Suppose that
  every holonomy test through degree <math|d> vanishes:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:moment-test-null><around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d<rsup|j>|d*t<rsup|j>>*f*<around|(|H<rsub|o>*<around|(|J,t|)>|)>|\|><rsub|t=0>=0,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>d,<space|1em><text|every
    >J.
  </equation>

  Tests with a larger available cutoff are allowed; only degrees through
  <math|d> enter the argument. For <math|a\<geqslant\>1> let
  <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|a>> be the space of link fields screened to the links
  with both endpoints in <math|X>, whose value on a link in direction
  <math|i> is a polynomial of degree at most <math|a-1> in
  <math|x=<math-up|lift><around|(|e<rsub|->-o|)>>. We claim that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:polynomial-moment-null>D<rsup|m>*<around|(|f\<circ\>exp|)><around|(|0|)><around|[|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|m>|]>=0<space|1em><text|if
    >v<rsub|i>\|<rsub|X>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsub|a<rsub|i>>,<space|1em>a<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1,<space|1em><big|sum><rsub|i>a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>d.
  </equation>

  The restriction means screening the link field, not restricting its support
  before applying the coarse-to-fine map.

  Here are the details of the change of coordinates behind this claim.
  Introduce a site gauge parameter <math|q> and the finite list <math|J> of
  radial connection coefficients of weights <math|2,3,\<ldots\>>. For each
  link set

  <\equation*>
    \<Psi\>*<around|(|q,J|)><rsub|e>=log <around*|(|e<rsup|q<around|(|e<rsub|->|)>>*H<rsub|o>*<around|(|J,1|)><rsub|e>*e<rsup|-q<around|(|e<rsub|+>|)>>|)>
  </equation*>

  on the links tested by <math|X>, and set it to zero elsewhere. This is an
  analytic germ with <math|\<Psi\><around|(|0|)>=0>. The endpoint map is
  analytic by the integral-equation argument in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-splitting>. Locality and gauge
  invariance give, as germs,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:moment-gauge-germ><around|(|f\<circ\>exp|)>\<circ\>\<Psi\>*<around|(|q,J|)>=f*<around|(|H<rsub|o>*<around|(|J,1|)>|)>.
  </equation>

  The identity uses only tested links and a finite common chart.

  Give a direction <math|<around|(|q,J|)>> filtration degree <math|a> when
  <math|q> is polynomial of degree at most <math|a> on <math|X> and the
  components of <math|J> of weight greater than <math|a> vanish. Denote this
  space by <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|a>>. The following two properties hold:

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|D*\<Psi\><around|(|0|)>*\<cal-K\><rsub|a>>|<cell|=\<cal-M\><rsub|a>,>|<cell|>|<cell|1\<leqslant\>a\<leqslant\>d,<eq-number><label|eq:moment-surjection>>>|<row|<cell|D<rsup|r>*\<Psi\><around|(|0|)><around|[|u<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,u<rsub|r>|]>>|<cell|\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsub|<big|sum><rsub|i>a<rsub|i>>,>|<cell|>|<cell|u<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-K\><rsub|a<rsub|i>>,r\<geqslant\>1.<eq-number><label|eq:moment-filtration>>>>>
  </align>

  We prove them, since the nonlinear coordinate change matters.

  For the first property the linearized link is

  <\equation*>
    D*\<Psi\><around|(|0|)><around|(|q,J|)><rsub|<around|(|x,i|)>>=q<around|(|x|)>-q*<around|(|x+e<rsub|i>|)>+<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1>A<rsub|J>*<around|(|x+s*e<rsub|i>|)><around|(|e<rsub|i>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*s.
  </equation*>

  Nonwrapping and side at least two identify the centered lift at the
  endpoint with <math|x+e<rsub|i>>. Every constant one-form is the gradient
  of a linear site polynomial, so it occurs with <math|J=0>. For a
  homogeneous polynomial one-form <math|Q> of degree <math|k\<geqslant\>1>,
  put

  <\equation*>
    r<around|(|x|)>=<frac|1|k+1>*<big|sum><rsub|i>x<rsub|i>*Q<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>K<rsub|i>=Q<rsub|i>-\<partial\><rsub|i>*r.
  </equation*>

  Euler's identity gives <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>x<rsub|i>*K<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>=0>.
  Thus <math|K> is precisely an admissible radial connection coefficient of
  weight <math|k+1>. Taking <math|q=-r> and that coefficient gives the link
  average of <math|Q>. The difference between this average and
  <math|Q<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>> has degree at most <math|k-1>: expand
  <math|Q<rsub|i>*<around|(|x+s*e<rsub|i>|)>> as a polynomial in <math|s>,
  and observe that its constant term is <math|Q<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>>.
  Induction on the degree supplies the difference by lower-weight parameters.
  Splitting a general polynomial into homogeneous parts proves
  <eqref|eq:moment-surjection>.

  For <eqref|eq:moment-filtration>, write the link germ in variables

  <\equation*>
    q<around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>q*<around|(|x+e<rsub|i>|)>-q<around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>A<rsub|J>*<around|(|x+s*e<rsub|i>|)>.
  </equation*>

  For a direction of degree <math|a>, the first expression has degree at most
  <math|a> in <math|x>, and each of the other two has degree at most
  <math|a-1>. When both of the latter expressions vanish, the link germ is
  identically zero, since <math|e<rsup|q<around|(|x|)>>*1*e<rsup|-q<around|(|x|)>>=1>.
  Expand any multilinear derivative according to these three components in
  each argument. The term using only the first components vanishes. Every
  remaining term contains a component with a one-degree loss; its degree is
  at most <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>a<rsub|i>-1>. Screening completes
  <eqref|eq:moment-filtration>.

  Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:moment-test-null> implies that all mixed
  derivatives of the right side of <eqref|eq:moment-gauge-germ> with total
  filtration weight at most <math|d> vanish. To verify this precisely, take
  its Taylor polynomial through ordinary degree <math|d> in the finite
  coordinates <math|J>. Substitute the weighted scaling
  <math|J<rsub|a>\<mapsto\>t<rsup|a>*J<rsub|a>>. The coefficient of every
  <math|t<rsup|j>>, <math|j\<leqslant\>d>, is zero for all <math|J>, by
  <eqref|eq:moment-test-null>. Each such coefficient is a polynomial, hence
  all its monomial coefficients vanish. Polarization gives the asserted mixed
  derivatives. Gauge-coordinate derivatives vanish because the right side is
  independent of <math|q>.

  Apply the chain rule to the left side of <eqref|eq:moment-gauge-germ>.
  Induct on the number <math|m> of arguments in
  <eqref|eq:polynomial-moment-null>, lifting each <math|v<rsub|i>> to
  <math|u<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-K\><rsub|a<rsub|i>>> by
  <eqref|eq:moment-surjection>. The partition into singletons contributes
  exactly the desired derivative of <math|f\<circ\>exp>. Every other
  partition has fewer than <math|m> blocks; by <eqref|eq:moment-filtration>,
  its arguments have the block weights, whose sum is still
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>a<rsub|i>>. The induction hypothesis kills all these
  terms. The whole derivative of the composite is zero, so the singleton term
  is zero too. The case <math|m=0> is <math|f<around|(|1|)>=0>. Finally,
  locality makes <math|f\<circ\>exp> equal to its composition with the linear
  screening map. Differentiating that identity allows unscreened directions
  whose restrictions have the stated polynomial form. This proves
  <eqref|eq:polynomial-moment-null>.

  <subsubsection|A discrete Taylor decomposition on the cover>

  Write <math|<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|4><around|\||x<rsub|i>|\|>>
  for <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>> and
  <math|\<Delta\><rsub|i>*v<around|(|x|)>=v*<around|(|x+e<rsub|i>|)>-v<around|(|x|)>>.
  For a word <math|w> in the four axes let <math|\<Delta\><rsub|w>> be the
  corresponding iterated difference. Suppose <math|d,p\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>,
  <math|L\<geqslant\>1>, <math|p\<geqslant\>d>, <math|M\<geqslant\>0>,
  <math|\<theta\>\<gtr\>0>, and a colour-valued cover field <math|v>
  satisfies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:moment-drift><around|\<\|\|\>|\<Delta\><rsub|w>*v<around|(|x|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>M*\<theta\>*L<rsup|-1-<around|\||w|\|>>*<around|(|1+<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>/L|)><rsup|p>,<space|2em><around|\||w|\|>\<leqslant\>d.
  </equation>

  There are polynomial pieces <math|P<rsub|j>>,
  <math|0\<leqslant\>j\<less\>d>, and a remainder <math|R<rsub|d>> such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:moment-newton>v<around|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>d>P<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>+R<rsub|d><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em><choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|deg
    P<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>j,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|d,p>*M*\<theta\>*L<rsup|-j-1>*<around|(|1+<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>|)><rsup|p>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|R<rsub|d><around|(|x|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|d,p>*M*\<theta\>*L<rsup|-d-1>*<around|(|1+<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>|)><rsup|p>.>>>>>
  </equation>

  The constant precedes the choice of <math|L> and of the period.

  For completeness use the total-degree Newton polynomials

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>=j><binom|x|\<alpha\>>\<Delta\><rsup|\<alpha\>>*v<around|(|0|)>,<space|2em><binom|x|\<alpha\>>=<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|4><frac|x<rsub|i>*<around|(|x<rsub|i>-1|)>*\<cdots\>*<around|(|x<rsub|i>-\<alpha\><rsub|i>+1|)>|\<alpha\><rsub|i>!>.
  </equation*>

  These formulas are polynomial identities for real <math|x>, so allow
  negative lattice offsets. The identity <math|<binom|t+1|a>-<binom|t|a>=<binom|t|a-1>>
  implies

  <\equation*>
    \<Delta\><rsub|i>*P<rsub|j+1><around|(|v|)>=P<rsub|j>*<around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|i>*v|)>,<space|2em>P<rsub|j><around|(|v|)><around|(|0|)>=0*<around|(|j\<gtr\>0|)>.
  </equation*>

  They are also the polynomials obtained by the recursion

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|j+1><around|(|v|)><around|(|x|)>=<around|(|j+1|)><rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|i>x<rsub|i>*P<rsub|j>*<around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|i>*v|)>*<around|(|x-e<rsub|i>|)>.
  </equation*>

  To check this, expand the falling factorials and use
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>\<alpha\><rsub|i>=j+1>. Their coefficients give

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|j>*M*\<theta\>*L<rsup|-j-1>*<around|(|1+<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>|)><rsup|j>,<space|2em>c<rsub|j>=<around|[|4*<around|(|j+1|)>|]><rsup|j>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed each falling-factorial factor is bounded by
  <math|<around|(|1+<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>+j|)><rsup|j>>, while summing the
  reciprocal factorials at fixed degree contributes
  <math|4<rsup|j>/j!\<leqslant\>4<rsup|j>>.

  If <math|<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>L>, all differences of order
  <math|d> in the ball of radius <math|L> are at most
  <math|2<rsup|p>*M*\<theta\>*L<rsup|-d-1>>. The remainder
  <math|R<rsub|d>=v-<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>d>P<rsub|j>> is zero at zero for
  <math|d\<gtr\>0>, and <math|\<Delta\><rsub|i>*R<rsub|d><around|(|v|)>=R<rsub|d-1>*<around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|i>*v|)>>.
  Induction on <math|d>, followed by summation along a shortest signed
  coordinate path from zero to <math|x>, gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|R<rsub|d><around|(|x|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|p>*M*\<theta\>*L<rsup|-d-1>*<around|(|1+<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>|)><rsup|d>.
  </equation*>

  For a negative step the forward difference is evaluated at the new
  endpoint, which still lies in the same ball. Thus this summation does not
  assume positive offsets. Since <math|p\<geqslant\>d>, it is bounded by the
  remainder in <eqref|eq:moment-newton>. If
  <math|<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>\<gtr\>L>, then

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|1+<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>/L|)><rsup|p>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|p>*L<rsup|-p>*<around|(|1+<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>|)><rsup|p>
  </equation*>

  and, for <math|j\<less\>d>, <math|L<rsup|d-j>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>|)><rsup|d-j>>.
  Apply these inequalities to <eqref|eq:moment-drift> with the empty word and
  to the piece bounds, then use <math|R<rsub|d>=v-<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>d>P<rsub|j>>.
  This gives <eqref|eq:moment-newton> with
  <math|C<rsub|d,p>=2<rsup|p>+<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>d>c<rsub|j>>. When
  <math|d=0> only the remainder is present, and the original bound suffices.

  Evaluate these pieces at the actual centered lift on the torus. The lift
  satisfies <math|<around|\||<math-up|lift><around|(|y-o|)>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>4<math-up|dist><around|(|y,o|)>>.
  Multiplying the constant by <math|4<rsup|p>> therefore gives a global
  decomposition into <math|d> polynomial pieces and a last remainder. The
  screened <math|j>th piece belongs to <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|j+1>>. Nothing is
  required of the polynomial behavior of the last remainder, including across
  the lift boundary.

  <subsubsection|Applying the decomposition to the actual kernel>

  The coarse-to-fine linear field is the installed <math|A<rsub|0>> kernel,
  with the physical normalization of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:kernel-transport-identities>.
  We assume that its iterated spatial differences obey the strip estimates

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|\<Delta\><rsub|w>*A<rsub|0>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<alpha\>>\<leqslant\>A*L<rsup|-1-<around|\||w|\|>>,<space|2em><around|\||w|\|>\<leqslant\>d,
  </equation*>

  for some strip rate <math|\<alpha\>\<gtr\>0> and some constant <math|A>,
  both fixed before the block factor <math|L> and independent of <math|L> and
  of the lattice size. These are the <math|A<rsub|0>> difference bounds in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:curvature-horizon-producer>. That section
  supplies them only for words of length at most <math|j-2>, where
  <math|j\<geqslant\>4> is the smoothing order of the stage (the
  order-<math|<around|(|j-2|)>> difference bounds of the block completion;
  compare Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-wire-tower-strip>, stated
  for words of length <math|s\<leqslant\>j-2>). The application below
  therefore requires

  <\equation*>
    d\<leqslant\>j-2,
  </equation*>

  or else a separate estimate of the differences of orders
  <math|j-1,\<ldots\>,d>; no such estimate is provided here. No assertion
  about the other tower blocks is needed. If a coarse input obeys
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u<around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<theta\>*<around|(|1+<math-up|dist><around|(|y,o<rsub|c>|)>|)><rsup|p>>,
  where <math|o<rsub|c>> is the parent of the chosen fine anchor <math|o>,
  use the installed row at <math|o+<math-up|wrap><around|(|x|)>>. Its
  summation addresses are the parent of this site plus
  <math|<math-up|wrap><around|(|z|)>>, where <math|z> is the cover
  displacement in that residue row. The parent map can cross a block
  boundary; its centered residue changes at the same time. The coordinate
  division with remainder gives

  <\equation*>
    1+<math-up|dist><around*|(|<math-up|parent><around|(|o+<math-up|wrap><around|(|x|)>|)>+<math-up|wrap><around|(|z|)>,o<rsub|c>|)>\<leqslant\>3*<around|(|1+<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>/L|)>*<around|(|1+<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed in each coordinate the parent displacement is the integer quotient
  of <math|x> plus a residue correction of absolute value at most one;
  periodic distance can only decrease this bound. Taking the maximum of the
  coordinate bounds and then adding the cover shift gives the displayed
  factor three. Taking absolute values in the convolution now costs
  <math|3<rsup|p>*<around|(|1+<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>/L|)><rsup|p>> times
  the strip row sum with the additional weight
  <math|<around|(|1+<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>|)><rsup|p>>. The inequality
  <math|<around|(|1+t|)><rsup|p>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|p,\<alpha\>>*e<rsup|\<alpha\>*t>>
  absorbs that weight into the available strip estimate. Sum also over the
  fixed form types. Differences can be taken on the row before summation by
  absolute convergence; the installed odd-block carry identifies them with
  the cover forward differences. We obtain <eqref|eq:moment-drift>, with
  <math|M> depending only on <math|A,\<alpha\>,p> and the fixed component
  count. The preceding decomposition therefore applies to this actual field,
  uniformly in the lattice size and block factor.

  <subsubsection|Quantitative cancellation and displacement>

  Let <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|P>> denote the
  direction norm

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|P>=sup<rsub|e>
    <frac|<around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|e>|\<\|\|\>>|\<theta\>*<around|(|1+<math-up|dist><around|(|o,e<rsub|->|)>|)><rsup|p>>.
  </equation*>

  Say that a direction admits a graded decomposition at scale
  <math|0\<leqslant\>\<xi\>\<leqslant\>1> and cost <math|C\<geqslant\>1> if

  <\equation*>
    v=<big|sum><rsub|a=1><rsup|d+1>v<rsub|a>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|a>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|P>\<leqslant\>C*\<xi\><rsup|a>,<space|2em>v<rsub|a>\|<rsub|X>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsub|a>*<space|1em><around|(|a\<leqslant\>d|)>.
  </equation*>

  The preceding construction gives this property with
  <math|\<xi\>=L<rsup|-1>>. Suppose the regular derivative bound of
  <math|F=f\<circ\>exp> is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:moment-regular-bound><around|\||D<rsup|m>*F<around|(|b|)><around|[|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|m>|]>|\|>\<leqslant\>Q*\<tau\>*<big|prod><rsub|i><around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|P>,<space|2em>m\<leqslant\>n,
  </equation>

  on the logarithmic region used below, with <math|n\<geqslant\>d+1>. Here
  <math|Q> is the fixed conversion constant from the local regularity norm,
  and <math|\<tau\>> is its price. The chart and health conditions in that
  norm are retained in <eqref|eq:moment-regular-bound>.

  At zero, expand each argument into its <math|d+1> pieces. All terms of
  total weight at most <math|d> vanish by <eqref|eq:polynomial-moment-null>.
  Every remaining term has a factor at most
  <math|C<rsup|m>*\<xi\><rsup|d+1>>. There are
  <math|<around|(|d+1|)><rsup|m>> terms. For <math|m\<leqslant\>n> this
  proves

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||D<rsup|m>*F<around|(|0|)><around|[|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|m>|]>|\|>\<leqslant\>Q*\<tau\>*<around|(|<around|(|d+1|)>*C|)><rsup|n>*\<xi\><rsup|d+1>=:S.
  </equation*>

  Now let the displacement <math|b> have the same decomposition, and suppose
  that the segment <math|t*b>, <math|0\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>1>, remains in
  the healthy logarithmic region, with the tested links and direction widths
  bounded by one as required by the regular norm. At order <math|n>,
  <eqref|eq:moment-regular-bound> bounds the derivative on this segment by
  <math|S>, since each argument has norm at most
  <math|<around|(|d+1|)>*C*\<xi\>> and <math|n\<geqslant\>d+1>. If the
  order-<math|m+1> derivative is bounded there by <math|<around|(|n-m|)>*S>,
  the fundamental theorem of calculus applied to
  <math|t\<mapsto\>D<rsup|m>*F*<around|(|t*b|)><around|[|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|m>|]>>
  bounds its change from zero by <math|<around|(|n-m|)>*S>. Its value at zero
  is at most <math|S>. Descending induction gives the uniform bound
  <math|<around|(|n+1|)>*S> at every order through <math|n>. This proves the
  attenuation after displacement; it does not require differentiating the
  health indicator.

  <subsection|The elementary one-step projection
  estimate><label|sec:leaf-transfer>

  We connect the local splitting to the elementary insertion estimate used in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-local-projection>. The analytic input is
  now the localized expansion of a real inserted field under one fixed
  nonlinear block integral. We state the part of that expansion used here and
  prove the remaining conversion and its constants.

  Let <math|\<cal-T\>> be the fine-to-coarse density transfer with block
  ratio <math|L>. Let <math|M> be one real elementary insertion. It has a
  local <em|ingredient> <math|\<phi\><rsub|M>>, a real function of the fine
  field with anchored fine support <math|X> and majorant <math|c>, that is
  <math|<around|\||\<phi\><rsub|M>|\|>\<leqslant\>c>, and mass
  <math|m=m<rsub|<math-up|fine>>*<around|(|X,c|)>\<geqslant\>0>. Its
  <em|density> <math|f=<math-up|dens><around|(|M|)>> is the ingredient
  inserted in the fine background gas: the fine background weight times the
  sum, over admissible families of the remaining fine activities, of
  <math|\<phi\><rsub|M>> times the product of those activities. The support
  and the majorant refer to the ingredient; the transfer <math|\<cal-T\>>
  acts on the density. This is the reading of the consumer in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-local-projection>, which writes
  <math|<math-up|dens><around|(|Q<rsub|M>|)>=L<rsup|d>*\<cal-T\><math-up|dens><around|(|M|)>>.
  Use the same fixed coarse background, threshold and activity alphabet as
  the unmarked transition. A localized expansion has real regular terms
  <math|h<rsub|i>> on anchored coarse supports <math|X<rsub|i>> and a real
  flagged source array <math|k> such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:leaf-raw-identity>\<cal-T\>*f=\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|coarse>>*<big|sum><rsub|i>\<chi\><rsub|X<rsub|i>>*h<rsub|i>+e<rsup|E<rsub|<math-up|coarse>>>*\<cal-G\><rsup|<around|[|1|]>><around|(|k|)>.
  </equation>

  All regular and marked series must be absolutely dominated, and the
  original insertion must be integrable under the fine prior and every
  transition slice. These conditions are part of the analytic expansion. They
  justify the displayed identity as an identity of actual integrals. In
  addition, require the marked identity: the raw forced profile of <math|k>
  is the transferred singleton profile of the insertion in its fixed ordinary
  or flagged input slot, with the same background and activity alphabet. This
  profile identity is a separate part of the expansion hypothesis.

  Split the localized terms into the distinguished principal term, when
  present, and the remaining terms. The required bounds have the following
  form, in the coarse derivative norm and reserved support mass:

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i<math-up|nonprincipal>>m<rsub|+>*<around|(|X<rsub|i>,<around|\<\|\|\>|h<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>M,<eq-number><label|eq:leaf-tail-input>>>|<row|<cell|m<rsub|+>*<around|(|X<rsub|\<ast\>>,<around|\<\|\|\>|h<rsub|\<ast\>>|\<\|\|\>>|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>V*<space|0.17em>m/g,<eq-number><label|eq:leaf-principal-input>>>|<row|<cell|<math-up|mass><around|(|k|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>D*s.<eq-number><label|eq:leaf-flag-input>>>>>
  </align>

  Here <math|g\<gtr\>0> is the normalization gain, <math|V\<geqslant\>0> a
  fixed-order derivative price, <math|D\<geqslant\>0> the insertion debt, and
  <math|s\<geqslant\>0> the flagged-response price. For a flagged elementary
  insertion there is no principal term. For a regular elementary insertion
  there is just one, at the fixed principal location. The indices and
  supports are selected before forming complex linear combinations.

  <subsubsection|The principal-term reserve>

  The principal estimate <eqref|eq:leaf-principal-input> follows from three
  more primitive bounds used by the local integral expansion. Write
  <math|D<rsub|X>=C<rsub|B><around|(|X<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>>, let
  <math|\<omega\>\<geqslant\>0> be its localized majorant, and suppose

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|h<rsub|\<ast\>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>D*\<omega\>*<space|0.17em>\<Phi\><around|(|X<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>,<space|2em>\<omega\>*e<rsup|<around|(|a+e|)>*D<rsub|X>>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>D\<leqslant\>m/g,
  </equation*>

  where <math|e\<geqslant\>0> and <math|a\<geqslant\>u+3>. Its derivative
  price has the form <math|\<Phi\><around|(|X<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>\<leqslant\>V*e<rsup|D<rsub|X>/2>>.
  The latter is the polynomial-to-exponential bound on the support size: for
  a fixed integer <math|r\<geqslant\>0> one can take
  <math|C<rsub|r>=sup<rsub|z\<geqslant\>0>
  z<rsup|r>*e<rsup|-z/2>\<less\>\<infty\>> and bound <math|z<rsup|r>> by
  <math|C<rsub|r>*e<rsup|z/2>>. The additional fixed factor in <math|V> is
  the spatial weight of the derivative directions at the given coupling.
  Consequently

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|<around|(|u+2|)>*D<rsub|X>><around|\<\|\|\>|h<rsub|\<ast\>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>D*V*\<omega\>*e<rsup|<around|(|u+5/2|)>*D<rsub|X>>\<leqslant\>D*V*\<omega\>*e<rsup|<around|(|a+e|)>*D<rsub|X>>\<leqslant\>V*m/g.
  </equation*>

  If <math|D<rsub|X>\<geqslant\>B>, the same calculation leaves a factor
  <math|e<rsup|-B/2>>, because <math|a+e-<around|(|u+5/2|)>\<geqslant\>1/2>.
  This is the stronger principal estimate used when its support is large. The
  local derivative estimate and the weighted bound on <math|\<omega\>> remain
  substantive inputs from the nonlinear expansion.

  <subsubsection|Complex fields and the raw expansion>

  To treat a complex insertion, take two real elementary insertions whose
  ingredients have the same support, type and majorant, let
  <math|f<rsub|1>,f<rsub|2>> be their densities, and combine them as
  <math|f=f<rsub|1>+i*f<rsub|2>>. Their masses are the same <math|m>; their
  principal support and index agree. For <math|z\<in\>\<bbb-C\>>, define

  <\equation*>
    H<rsub|i>=z*<around|(|h<rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|1|)>>+i*h<rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|2|)>>|)>,<space|2em>K=z*<around|(|k<rsup|<around|(|1|)>>+i*k<rsup|<around|(|2|)>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The sum of the two real majorants times <math|<around|\||z|\|>> dominates
  each complex term. Thus the nonprincipal reserved mass is at most
  <math|2<around|\||z|\|>*M>, the principal reserved mass is at most
  <math|2<around|\||z|\|>*V*m/g>, and <math|<math-up|mass><around|(|K|)>\<leqslant\>2<around|\||z|\|>*D*s>.
  The raw seed is zero. Pad the common countable list by zero terms on the
  singleton anchor support if a natural-number indexing is needed; neither
  the represented density nor its cost changes.

  Linearity of masks, marked-gas insertion and <math|\<cal-T\>>, with
  absolute convergence of the series, gives the exact raw density
  <math|z*\<cal-T\>*<around|(|f<rsub|1>+i*f<rsub|2>|)>>. This is the reason
  the same supports and principal indices were retained. Applying the
  splitting of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-splitting> to these raw
  terms preserves the identity. That splitting has two hypotheses on the
  coarse data, which we assume here and list among the inputs at the end of
  this section:

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(A)><em|Addresses.> For each response <math|r<rsub|i>> produced by
    the splitting, supported on <math|Z<rsub|i>>, and each nonempty pairwise
    disjoint family <math|q> of original coarse flagged activities meeting
    <math|Z<rsub|i>>, there is a distinct available address with support
    <math|Z<rsub|i>\<cup\><big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>q>P<rsub|j>>, flag inventory
    <math|<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>q>I<rsub|j>>, and additional eligibility
    predicate satisfied whenever its support has exactly this inventory
    (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-splitting>, \PPutting the response
    into the existing gas\Q).

    <item*|(B)><em|Background bound.> The original coarse flagged activities
    <math|K<rsub|j>> have majorants <math|<around|\||K<rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|j>>,
    <math|b<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>0>, with site price
    <math|\<varsigma\>=\<kappa\><rsup|2>>:
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|j:x\<in\>P<rsub|j>>m*<around|(|P<rsub|j>,b<rsub|j>|)>\<leqslant\>\<kappa\><rsup|2>>
    at every coarse site <math|x>, where <math|\<kappa\>\<gtr\>0> is the
    constant of <eqref|eq:leaf-small-inputs>.
  </enumerate>

  In the elementary application take <math|z=L<rsup|d>> and
  <math|f<rsub|2>=0> with the same majorant as <math|f<rsub|1>>. The bound
  with the factor two remains valid and avoids making the chosen dictionary
  depend on whether one component happens to vanish.

  <subsubsection|The projection allowance>

  Let <math|F<rsub|d>> be the support-join and jet-extraction factor from
  <eqref|eq:local-join-price>, let <math|C<rsub|<math-up|orb>>\<geqslant\>1>
  be the finite coefficient-action price, and let
  <math|C<rsub|<math-up|turn>>\<geqslant\>1> be the price for transporting
  the local remainder norm under the required lattice motions. Put

  <\equation*>
    H<rsub|d>=1+2*F<rsub|d>*<around|(|1+C<rsub|<math-up|orb>>+C<rsub|<math-up|turn>>|)>,<space|2em>\<eta\><rsub|0>=<math-up|rate><around|(|L|)>/H<rsub|d>.
  </equation*>

  Assume the already chosen coupling satisfies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:leaf-small-inputs>2*L<rsup|d>*M\<leqslant\>\<eta\><rsub|0>*m,<space|2em>2*\<kappa\><rsup|-1>*L<rsup|d>*D*s\<leqslant\>\<eta\><rsub|0>*m,<space|2em><around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*\<kappa\>\<leqslant\>1,
  </equation>

  where <math|\<kappa\>\<gtr\>0> is the square root of the coarse forced
  allowance, that is of the site price <math|\<varsigma\>> in hypothesis (B).
  The first two inequalities are required uniformly over insertions. They are
  paid by the same coupling delay as the chosen nonlinear transition;
  choosing a different history here is not allowed.

  Set

  <\equation*>
    E=2*L<rsup|d>*V/g,<space|2em>e<rsub|0>=<around|(|\<eta\><rsub|0>+<around|\||E|\|>|)>*m.
  </equation*>

  Then <math|e<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>\<eta\><rsub|0>*m\<geqslant\>0>. The
  principal bill is at most <math|e<rsub|0>>, and the nonprincipal bill is at
  most <math|\<eta\><rsub|0>*m>. The total raw regular reserved mass is
  therefore at most <math|\<eta\><rsub|0>*m+e<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>2*e<rsub|0>>.
  Write <math|p<rsub|0>=F<rsub|d>*<around|(|\<eta\><rsub|0>*m+e<rsub|0>|)>\<leqslant\>2*F<rsub|d>*e<rsub|0>>
  and <math|b<rsub|0>=2*\<kappa\><rsup|-1>*L<rsup|d>*D*s\<leqslant\>e<rsub|0>>.
  The local splitting gives a coefficient change at most <math|p<rsub|0>>, a
  regular remainder of mass at most <math|p<rsub|0>>, a normalized forced
  remainder of mass at most <math|b<rsub|0>+p<rsub|0>>, and a response of
  reserved mass at most <math|p<rsub|0>>. To bound the coefficient change by
  <math|p<rsub|0>>, use <math|R<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>F<rsub|d>>, which follows
  from <math|w\<geqslant\>0> and the definition of <math|F<rsub|d>>.

  It follows directly that

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|<math-up|orb>>*\<mu\><around|(|q|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|orb>>*p<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|d>*e<rsub|0>,<space|2em>C<rsub|<math-up|turn>>*p<rsub|0>+<around|(|b<rsub|0>+p<rsub|0>|)>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|d>*e<rsub|0>.
  </equation*>

  The response cost is also at most <math|H<rsub|d>*e<rsub|0>>. Therefore any
  <math|Q<rsub|e>> satisfying

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:leaf-projection-allowance>Q<rsub|e>\<geqslant\><around|(|\<eta\><rsub|0>+<around|\||2*L<rsup|d>*V/g|\|>|)>*H<rsub|d>
  </equation>

  gives a coefficient field with coefficient and remainder bounds
  <math|Q<rsub|e>*m> and exact density <math|L<rsup|d>*\<cal-T\>*f=L<rsup|d>*\<cal-T\><math-up|dens><around|(|M|)>>.
  This is the numerical projection bound in <eqref|eq:ope-leaf-projection>.

  The marked transition is retained as well. The original real insertion
  occupies its fixed ordinary or flagged slot with cost <math|m>; the
  real/imaginary convention just used gives the available bound <math|2*m>.
  Its raw output forced profile is the transferred singleton profile.
  Splitting adds exactly the hook of the response <math|\<psi\>> constructed
  in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-splitting>, of cost at most
  <math|Q<rsub|e>*m>. Thus the transition identity has fine cost <math|2*m>,
  coarse response cost <math|Q<rsub|e>*m>, and multiplier <math|L<rsup|d>>,
  precisely as required in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-local-projection>.
  This last assertion uses the marked profile hypothesis stated alongside
  <eqref|eq:leaf-raw-identity>; the scalar density identity alone would not
  suffice.

  For the selected transition, Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:leaf-expansion>
  constructs the localized expansion and gives the singleton, tail and flag
  estimates. Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:projection-payment-producer>
  checks the address and background conditions and bounds the small terms in
  <eqref|eq:leaf-small-inputs>; Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-history-service>
  uses them on the selected history. The finite projection, support
  correction, principal reserve conversion, and passage to the elementary
  allowance have now been proved from those inputs.

  <section|Block integration and polymer expansions>

  <subsection|Connected clusters with repeated
  labels><label|sec:cluster-expansion>

  The abstract polymer expansion and its convergence criterion are developed
  in Kotecký and Preiss <cite-detail|KoteckyPreiss|the theorem on
  pp.<nbsp>492--493>. The argument here also keeps a geometric bound at each
  occurrence number in the weighted expansion, for the later differentiated
  estimates.

  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-grouping> counted connected sets of
  distinct activity labels. A logarithmic expansion is different: its
  vertices are numbered occurrences, and the same activity label may occur
  several times. We now prove the bound for this expansion, including its
  factorials and the identity with the real logarithm of the gas. No
  positivity of the gas is assumed in obtaining the identity.

  Let <math|\<Lambda\>> be a finite periodic lattice. Each label <math|i> has
  a nonempty support <math|X<rsub|i>>, and write
  <math|c<around|(|X|)>=C<rsub|B><around|(|X|)>> and
  <math|h<rsub|B>=<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>>. We use
  <math|<around|\||X|\|>\<leqslant\>h<rsub|B>*c<around|(|X|)>> and covering
  subadditivity for intersecting supports. The incompatibility relation
  <math|R> is symmetric and reflexive, and

  <\equation*>
    R*<around|(|i,j|)><space|1em>\<Longrightarrow\><space|1em>X<rsub|i>\<cap\>X<rsub|j>\<ne\>\<varnothing\>.
  </equation*>

  The converse is not required. Let <math|w<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>,
  <math|a\<geqslant\>0>, <math|\<gamma\>\<gtr\>0>, <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>,
  and suppose

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:cluster-vertex-input>sup<rsub|x\<in\>\<Lambda\>>
    <big|sum><rsub|i:x\<in\>X<rsub|i>>w<rsub|i>*e<rsup|<around|(|a+\<delta\>|)>*c<around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\>,<space|2em>q<rsub|0>=<frac|4*h<rsub|B>*\<gamma\>|\<delta\>>\<leqslant\>q\<less\>1.
  </equation>

  In particular <math|q\<gtr\>0>.

  For an ordered tuple <math|z=<around|(|i<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,i<rsub|m>|)>>,
  let <math|T<around|(|z|)>> be the graph on
  <math|<around|{|0,\<ldots\>,m|}>> with an edge <math|<around|{|r,s|}>> when
  <math|r\<ne\>s> and <math|R*<around|(|i<rsub|r>,i<rsub|s>|)>>. Define its
  connected coefficient and majorant by

  <\equation*>
    u<around|(|z|)>=<big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|G\<subseteq\>T<around|(|z|)>>>|<row|<cell|G<math-up|connected>>>>>>><around|(|-1|)><rsup|<around|\||E<around|(|G|)>|\|>>,<space|2em>W<around|(|z|)>=<frac|<around|\||u<around|(|z|)>|\|>|<around|(|m+1|)>!>*<big|prod><rsub|r=0><rsup|m>w<rsub|i<rsub|r>>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|m=0> the graph with no edges is connected and
  <math|u<around|(|z|)>=1>. Repeated labels are allowed in every tuple sum
  below.

  <subsubsection|Cancellation before taking absolute values>

  For any graph <math|T> on <math|<around|{|0,\<ldots\>,m|}>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:cluster-tree-graph><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|G\<subseteq\>T>>|<row|<cell|G<math-up|connected>>>>>>><around|(|-1|)><rsup|<around|\||E<around|(|G|)>|\|>>|\|>\<leqslant\>#<around|{|<text|spanning
    trees of >T|}>.
  </equation>

  This is the Penrose tree-graph bound <cite-detail|FernandezProcacci|Proposition<nbsp>5,
  Eq.<nbsp>(4.3)>. Apply the identity to the finite simple graph on the
  occurrence indices. The alternating sum has absolute value equal to the
  number of Penrose trees, a subset of its spanning trees. For a disconnected
  graph both counts vanish. Repeated activity labels are permitted because
  different occurrences are different vertices, joined when the labels are
  incompatible.

  <subsubsection|The weighted forest estimate>

  A parent map <math|p:<around|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>\<to\><around|{|0,\<ldots\>,m|}>>
  is called rooted if iterating it always reaches the external root zero.
  Place a fixed support <math|Y> at zero and a support
  <math|X<rsub|i<rsub|v>>> at vertex <math|v\<ne\>0>. Only assignments in
  which each child support meets its parent support are retained. We claim

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:cluster-forest-bound><frac|1|m!>*<big|sum><rsub|p<math-up|rooted>><big|sum><rsub|i<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,i<rsub|m>>\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|<text|all
    parent edges overlap>|}>>*<big|prod><rsub|v=1><rsup|m>w<rsub|i<rsub|v>>*e<rsup|a*c<around|(|X<rsub|i<rsub|v>>|)>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<delta\>*c<around|(|Y|)>>*q<rsup|m>.
  </equation>

  The labels in this sum need not be distinct.

  Put <math|t=h<rsub|B>*\<gamma\>/\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>,
  <math|F<around|(|Y|)>=max <around|(|\<delta\>*c<around|(|Y|)>,1|)>>, and
  introduce the nonnegative kernel

  <\equation*>
    K*<around|(|Y,i|)>=<frac|\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|X<rsub|i>\<cap\>Y\<ne\>\<varnothing\>|}>>*w<rsub|i>*e<rsup|<around|(|a+\<delta\>|)>*c<around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>|t*F<around|(|Y|)>>.
  </equation*>

  The site bound in <eqref|eq:cluster-vertex-input> implies

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|i>\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|X<rsub|i>\<cap\>Y\<ne\>\<varnothing\>|}>>*w<rsub|i>*e<rsup|<around|(|a+\<delta\>|)>*c<around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>\<leqslant\><around|\||Y|\|>*\<gamma\>\<leqslant\>h<rsub|B>*\<gamma\>*c<around|(|Y|)>\<leqslant\>t*F<around|(|Y|)>.
  </equation*>

  Thus every row of <math|K> sums to at most one. For a fixed rooted parent
  map, the sum over all label assignments of the product of its edge kernels
  is at most one: sum the label at a leaf first, apply its row bound, and
  repeat until no vertex remains. For infinite alphabets one first restricts
  to a finite alphabet; the row bounds still hold, and nonnegative
  convergence removes the restriction.

  Let <math|d<rsub|v>> be the number of children of vertex <math|v>,
  including the external root. The exponential series gives

  <\equation*>
    F<around|(|Z|)><rsup|k>\<leqslant\>k!e<rsup|\<delta\>*c<around|(|Z|)>>*<space|1em><around|(|k\<geqslant\>0|)>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed if <math|\<delta\>*c<around|(|Z|)>\<geqslant\>1> this is one term in
  the exponential series; otherwise use <math|F<around|(|Z|)>=1> and
  <math|k!e<rsup|\<delta\>*c<around|(|Z|)>>\<geqslant\>1>. Replacing each
  overlap weight with its kernel expression and collecting the factors at
  parents gives

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|v=1><rsup|m>\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|X<rsub|i<rsub|v>><text|
    meets parent>|}>>*w<rsub|i<rsub|v>>*e<rsup|a*c<around|(|X<rsub|i<rsub|v>>|)>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|2em>\<leqslant\>t<rsup|m>*e<rsup|\<delta\>*c<around|(|Y|)>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|v=0><rsup|m>d<rsub|v>!|)>*<big|prod><rsub|v=1><rsup|m>K*<around|(|X<rsub|i<rsub|p<around|(|v|)>>>,i<rsub|v>|)>,>>>>
  </align*>

  where <math|X<rsub|i<rsub|0>>=Y>. To check the reserve cancellation, before
  the inequality there is a factor <math|e<rsup|-\<delta\>*c<around|(|X<rsub|i<rsub|v>>|)>>>
  at every nonroot vertex. Applying the bound on <math|F<rsup|d<rsub|v>>>
  supplies exactly one inverse factor there, leaving only
  <math|e<rsup|\<delta\>*c<around|(|Y|)>>> at the external root.

  It remains to sum the child factorials. Summing over all parent maps,
  including those which are not rooted, can only increase the answer. For
  fixed child counts <math|d<rsub|0>+\<cdots\>+d<rsub|m>=m> there are
  <math|m!/<big|prod><rsub|v>d<rsub|v>!> maps. Therefore

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|p:<around|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>\<to\><around|{|0,\<ldots\>,m|}>><big|prod><rsub|v=0><rsup|m>d<rsub|v>!=m!<binom|2*m|m>\<leqslant\>m!4<rsup|m>.
  </equation*>

  The binomial coefficient counts weak compositions of <math|m> into
  <math|m+1> parts. The inequality follows by comparing it with the sum of
  all binomial coefficients of order <math|2*m>. After division by <math|m>!,
  the kernel bound and this count prove <eqref|eq:cluster-forest-bound>,
  since <math|<around|(|4*t|)><rsup|m>=q<rsub|0><rsup|m>\<leqslant\>q<rsup|m>>.
  This also proves the case <math|m=0>, whose left side is one.

  A distinguished root may carry a different nonnegative weight
  <math|v<rsub|i>>. If <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>v<rsub|i>*e<rsup|<around|(|a+\<delta\>|)>*c<around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>\<leqslant\>b>,
  multiply <eqref|eq:cluster-forest-bound> with <math|Y=X<rsub|i<rsub|0>>> by
  <math|v<rsub|i<rsub|0>>*e<rsup|a*c<around|(|X<rsub|i<rsub|0>>|)>>> and sum
  <math|i<rsub|0>>. The result is the rooted bound <math|b*q<rsup|m>>, with
  denominator <math|m>! for the other vertices.

  <subsubsection|A root anywhere in the tuple>

  Let <math|Q> be any property of an activity label and assume

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|i:Q<around|(|i|)>>w<rsub|i>*e<rsup|<around|(|a+\<delta\>|)>*c<around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>\<leqslant\>b.
  </equation*>

  Apply <eqref|eq:cluster-tree-graph> to each occurrence graph and the rooted
  forest bound with <math|v<rsub|i>=\<b-1\><rsub|Q<around|(|i|)>>*w<rsub|i>>
  at a specified vertex. This gives

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|z><frac|<around|\||u<around|(|z|)>|\|>|m!>*<space|0.17em>\<b-1\><rsub|Q<around|(|i<rsub|r>|)>>*<big|prod><rsub|s=0><rsup|m>w<rsub|i<rsub|s>>*e<rsup|a*c<around|(|X<rsub|i<rsub|s>>|)>>\<leqslant\>b*q<rsup|m>
  </equation*>

  for every root position <math|r>. Relabeling occurrence positions preserves
  both the connected coefficient and the tuple sum, so the same bound holds
  for all <math|r>. If a tuple contains at least one label satisfying
  <math|Q>, its cluster majorant, with denominator <math|<around|(|m+1|)>>!,
  is bounded by <math|1/<around|(|m+1|)>> times the sum of the displayed
  rooted integrands over all <math|r>. Summing yields

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:cluster-marked-size><big|sum><rsub|z:<space|0.17em>\<exists\>r*<space|0.17em>Q<around|(|i<rsub|r>|)>><frac|<around|\||u<around|(|z|)>|\|>|<around|(|m+1|)>!>*<big|prod><rsub|r=0><rsup|m>w<rsub|i<rsub|r>>*e<rsup|a*c<around|(|X<rsub|i<rsub|r>>|)>>\<leqslant\>b*q<rsup|m>.
  </equation>

  This is the normalization that pays for choosing a root; no additional
  factor <math|m+1> remains.

  If <math|u<around|(|z|)>\<ne\>0>, its occurrence graph contains a connected
  spanning subgraph, hence a spanning tree. Incompatibility edges imply
  support overlap. Adding supports along this tree gives

  <\equation*>
    c<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|r=0><rsup|m>X<rsub|i<rsub|r>>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|r=0><rsup|m>c<around|(|X<rsub|i<rsub|r>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  As <math|a\<geqslant\>0>, the exponential weight of the actual union is
  therefore bounded by the product weight in <eqref|eq:cluster-marked-size>.
  Take <math|Q<around|(|i|)>> to mean <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|i>> and
  <math|b=\<gamma\>>. The union contains <math|x> exactly when some label has
  this property. Finally fix an integer <math|m<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0> and sum
  over <math|m\<geqslant\>m<rsub|0>> to obtain

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:cluster-local-tail><big|sum><rsub|m\<geqslant\>m<rsub|0>><big|sum><rsub|i<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,i<rsub|m>>\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|r>X<rsub|i<rsub|r>>|}>>*W*<around|(|i<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,i<rsub|m>|)>*e<rsup|a*c<around|(|<big|cup><rsub|r>X<rsub|i<rsub|r>>|)>>\<leqslant\><frac|\<gamma\>*q<rsup|m<rsub|0>>|1-q>.
  </equation>

  Thus the connected estimate <eqref|eq:leaf-cluster-tail> follows from a
  vertex site bound and the explicit smallness inequality in
  <eqref|eq:cluster-vertex-input>: use the exponent <math|a+e> there in place
  of <math|a>, the vertex price written <math|t> in
  <eqref|eq:leaf-cluster-tail> in place of <math|\<gamma\>>, the tail
  parameter <math|\<gamma\>> of that section in place of <math|q>, and its
  order threshold <math|z> in place of <math|m<rsub|0>>. (The auxiliary
  quantity <math|t=h<rsub|B>*\<gamma\>/\<delta\>> of the forest estimate
  above is unrelated.) The reserve <math|\<delta\>> pays for tree branching
  and is distinct from the reserve <math|e> used later for spatial derivative
  weights.

  <subsubsection|The exact logarithm identity>

  Now let <math|v<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> be possibly signed activities and
  apply the preceding estimates to <math|w<rsub|i>=<around|\||v<rsub|i>|\|>>.
  Define the gas and connected sum

  <\equation*>
    Z=<big|sum><rsub|A<math-up|finite,compatible>><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>A>v<rsub|i>,<space|2em>\<cal-L\>=<big|sum><rsub|m\<geqslant\>0><frac|1|<around|(|m+1|)>!>*<big|sum><rsub|i<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,i<rsub|m>>u*<around|(|i<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,i<rsub|m>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|r=0><rsup|m>v<rsub|i<rsub|r>>.
  </equation*>

  A finite set is compatible if it contains no incompatible distinct labels.
  The empty set contributes one. We will prove

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:cluster-exact-log>Z=e<rsup|\<cal-L\>>\<gtr\>0,<space|2em>log
    Z=\<cal-L\>.
  </equation>

  First both series converge absolutely. Nonempty supports and
  <eqref|eq:cluster-vertex-input> imply <math|<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\||v<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*\<gamma\>>,
  so the gas is dominated by <math|<big|prod><rsub|i><around|(|1+<around|\||v<rsub|i>|\|>|)>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*\<gamma\>>>.
  For the logarithm, use <eqref|eq:cluster-marked-size> with <math|Q> always
  true and <math|b=<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*\<gamma\>>, and drop the support
  weights. Its absolute sum at order <math|m> is at most
  <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*\<gamma\>*q<rsup|m>>; hence the total is at
  most <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*\<gamma\>/<around|(|1-q|)>>.

  Begin with a finite alphabet <math|D>. Let <math|P<around|(|t|)>> be its
  gas polynomial, with each activity replaced by <math|t*v<rsub|i>>, and let

  <\equation*>
    L<around|(|t|)>=<big|sum><rsub|m\<geqslant\>0>\<ell\><rsub|m>*t<rsup|m+1>,<space|2em>\<ell\><rsub|m>=<frac|1|<around|(|m+1|)>!>*<big|sum><rsub|i<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,i<rsub|m>\<in\>D>u*<around|(|i<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,i<rsub|m>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|r>v<rsub|i<rsub|r>>.
  </equation*>

  The bound <math|<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|m>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*\<gamma\>*q<rsup|m>>
  shows that <math|L> is differentiable for
  <math|<around|\||t|\|>\<less\>q<rsup|-1>> by termwise differentiation.
  Indeed on <math|<around|\||t|\|>\<leqslant\>u\<less\>q<rsup|-1>> the
  derivative terms are bounded by the summable series
  <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*\<gamma\>*<around|(|m+1|)>*<around|(|q*u|)><rsup|m>>.
  This interval contains <math|<around|[|0,1|]>>.

  We verify the coefficient identity <math|P<rprime|'>=L<rprime|'>*P>, rather
  than assuming a formal logarithm can be evaluated. On a tuple of <math|n>
  occurrences, the sum of <math|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|<around|\||E<around|(|G|)>|\|>>>
  over all subgraphs of its incompatibility graph is
  <math|<around|(|1-1|)><rsup|<around|\||E<around|(|T|)>|\|>>>. It is one if
  the graph has no edge and zero otherwise. Because <math|R> is reflexive, a
  tuple with repeated labels has an edge between equal occurrences and
  contributes zero. Thus summing this all-graph weight over tuples,
  multiplying by their activities, and dividing by <math|n>! gives exactly
  the coefficient <math|p<rsub|n>> of <math|P<around|(|t|)>>: each compatible
  <math|n>-element set has <math|n>! enumerations.

  For <math|n=m+1>, distinguish occurrence zero in an arbitrary graph. Its
  connected component consists of zero and some <math|k> of the other
  <math|m> vertices. Choose those vertices in <math|<binom|m|k>> ways. A
  graph is then uniquely specified by a connected graph on this component and
  an arbitrary graph on its complement, with no cross edges. Edge signs and
  label products factor. Summing the choices and dividing by <math|m>! gives
  the exact recurrence

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|m+1|)>*p<rsub|m+1>=<big|sum><rsub|k=0><rsup|m><around|(|k+1|)>*\<ell\><rsub|k>*p<rsub|m-k>.
  </equation*>

  Absolute convergence of <math|L<rprime|'>> and the finiteness of <math|P>
  allow its coefficients to be multiplied and summed, proving
  <math|P<rprime|'>=L<rprime|'>*P> on the interval. Hence

  <\equation*>
    <frac|d|d*t>*<around|{|P<around|(|t|)>*e<rsup|-L<around|(|t|)>>|}>=0.
  </equation*>

  At zero, <math|P<around|(|0|)>=1> and <math|L<around|(|0|)>=0>. The mean
  value theorem implies <math|P<around|(|t|)>*e<rsup|-L<around|(|t|)>>=1>
  throughout the interval, and evaluation at <math|t=1> proves
  <eqref|eq:cluster-exact-log> for the finite alphabet.

  Finally let finite alphabets increase to all labels. Every gas term and
  every occurrence tuple is eventually retained. The absolute majorants
  already proved are independent of this restriction. Dominated convergence
  therefore gives <math|P<rsub|D><around|(|1|)>\<to\>Z> and
  <math|L<rsub|D><around|(|1|)>\<to\>\<cal-L\>>. Continuity of the
  exponential yields <math|Z=e<rsup|\<cal-L\>>>, and its strict positivity
  gives the claimed real logarithm. For an arbitrary index set the same
  argument uses the directed family of all finite subsets; alternatively
  absolute summability leaves only countably many nonzero terms. This
  completes the proof, with no assumption of positivity or a choice of a
  complex logarithm branch.

  Applied to the uniformly dominated tilted activities of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:leaf-expansion>, this result constructs
  their positive normalization and logarithmic cluster series once the vertex
  site bound <eqref|eq:cluster-vertex-input> is established. The joint bounds
  proved there then justify differentiating it. Constructing the actual
  block-integral activities and proving their vertex bounds remain separate
  analytic tasks.

  <subsection|Prices for packets and marked connected
  components><label|sec:mixed-activity-prices>

  The vertex bound used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:cluster-expansion>
  combines two populations: mandatory defect packets and optional local
  activities. We prove the counting and reserve estimates for this
  combination, then show how a single insertion is added to its actual
  alphabet. The analytic bound on the weight of one packet remains a separate
  input.

  <subsubsection|Counting physical defect packets>

  Fix a block ratio <math|L\<geqslant\>3> and nonnegative integers
  <math|\<ell\>,B>. A seed is a fine plaquette, a coarse oriented bond, or a
  coarse site. These are distinct types even when they have the same
  location. Each seed has a coarse anchor and a finite set of points within
  max-distance one of its anchor. For a plaquette these points are the coarse
  parents of its touched fine sites; for a bond they are its two endpoints;
  for a site it is the site itself. For the plaquette, the parent-distance
  estimate is <math|L<space|0.17em><math-up|dist><around|(|<math-up|parent><around|(|x|)>,<math-up|parent><around|(|y|)>|)>\<leqslant\>L+2>.
  Since the distance is integral and <math|L\<geqslant\>3>, it is at most
  one. The halo of radius <math|\<ell\>*B> around these points is its
  support, contained in the ball of radius <math|\<ell\>*B+1> about the
  anchor. A packet is a nonempty finite set of seeds whose halo intersection
  graph is connected. Its support is the union of these halos, and its size
  <math|n> is the number of typed seeds.

  The number of seeds with anchors in a set <math|Y> is at most
  <math|T<rsub|L><around|\||Y|\|>>, where

  <\equation*>
    T<rsub|L>=L<rsup|4>#<around|{|<text|plaquette axes>|}>+4+1.
  </equation*>

  The fine site is determined by its coarse parent and one of
  <math|L<rsup|4>> residues, which proves the plaquette count; the two other
  counts are immediate. Consequently at most

  <\equation*>
    H<rsub|B>=<around|(|2*<around|(|\<ell\>*B+1|)>+1|)><rsup|4>*T<rsub|L>
  </equation*>

  seed halos can contain a given site. If two halos overlap, their anchors
  have distance at most <math|2*<around|(|\<ell\>*B+1|)>>, so each seed has
  at most

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|B>=<around|(|4*<around|(|\<ell\>*B+1|)>+1|)><rsup|4>*T<rsub|L>\<geqslant\>1
  </equation*>

  possible neighboring seeds. The root count <math|H<rsub|B>>, the adjacency
  count <math|D<rsub|B>> and the per-seed cover cost <math|c<rsub|\<ell\>>>
  defined below are the quantities used under the same names in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:packet-estimates>. These bounds remain valid
  when balls wrap around the periodic lattice, since wrapping only decreases
  the number of distinct sites.

  There are at most <math|H<rsub|B>*D<rsub|B><rsup|2*n>> packets of size
  <math|n> containing a fixed site in their support. To prove this, choose a
  seed whose halo contains that site; there are at most <math|H<rsub|B>>
  choices. A connected <math|n>-seed packet has a spanning tree. Traversing
  every tree edge out and back gives a walk of length
  <math|2*<around|(|n-1|)>>, starting at the chosen seed, whose visited set
  is exactly the packet. There are at most
  <math|D<rsub|B><rsup|2*<around|(|n-1|)>>> such walks. Different visited
  sets cannot come from the same walk, so this counts packets without an
  injectivity assumption on their placements. Enlarging the bound to
  <math|H<rsub|B>*D<rsub|B><rsup|2*n>> also covers <math|n=1> uniformly.
  There is no packet with <math|n=0>.

  A single halo has <math|B>-cover number at most
  <math|c<rsub|\<ell\>>=<around|(|2*\<ell\>+3|)><rsup|4>>. For
  <math|B\<geqslant\>1> it lies in the ball of radius
  <math|<around|(|\<ell\>+1|)>*B>, tiled by <math|B>-balls with centers on
  the <math|B>-spaced grid; there are at most
  <math|<around|(|2*\<ell\>+3|)><rsup|4>> centers, forming a connected grid.
  For <math|B=0> use at most <math|3<rsup|4>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|\<ell\>>>
  singleton centers. Covering subadditivity along a spanning tree therefore
  bounds a packet's cover number by <math|n*c<rsub|\<ell\>>>.

  Suppose its nonnegative weight obeys the analytic estimate

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:mixed-packet-analytic>p<rsub|A>\<leqslant\>C*exp
    <around|(|<around|(|s-g|)>*n<around|(|A|)>|)>,<space|2em>C\<geqslant\>0,
  </equation>

  where <math|s> is the expense per seed and <math|g> the gain per seed. For
  a support exponent <math|h\<geqslant\>0>, put

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|p>=D<rsub|B><rsup|2>*exp <around|(|s-g+h*c<rsub|\<ell\>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  If <math|q<rsub|p>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|p>\<less\>1>, multiplication by the
  support weight, the packet count, and a geometric sum give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:mixed-packet-hit><big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A:<space|0.17em>x\<in\>X<rsub|A>>>|<row|<cell|n<around|(|A|)>\<geqslant\>n<rsub|0>>>>>>>p<rsub|A>*e<rsup|h*c<around|(|X<rsub|A>|)>>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|B>*C*<frac|r<rsub|p><rsup|max
    <around|(|1,n<rsub|0>|)>>|1-r<rsub|p>>.
  </equation>

  Indeed the summand at fixed size <math|n> is at most
  <math|H<rsub|B>*C*q<rsub|p><rsup|n>>. The lower limit is <math|max
  <around|(|1,n<rsub|0>|)>> because packets are nonempty. This explains the
  single-seed extraction in the packet price. If a packet has several
  analytic variants, the hypothesis <eqref|eq:mixed-packet-analytic> must
  bound their <em|sum> at that packet. It cannot be applied separately to
  every variant for free. A variant with smaller support has no larger
  support price and is handled by the same fiberwise summation.

  <subsubsection|Reserving the cost of a whole component>

  Let <math|f<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0> be weights on a combined alphabet, with
  supports <math|X<rsub|i>>, extra nonnegative costs <math|d<rsub|i>>, and
  nonnegative marks <math|e<rsub|i>>. Define

  <\equation*>
    <wide|f|~><rsub|i>=f<rsub|i>*exp <around|(|2*\<varepsilon\>*d<rsub|i>+e<rsub|i>|)>,<space|2em>D<around|(|A|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>A><around|(|c<around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>+d<rsub|i>|)>,<space|2em>E<around|(|A|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>A>e<rsub|i>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>. Assume the vertex condition
  <eqref|eq:cluster-vertex-input> holds for <math|<wide|f|~>> with support
  exponent <math|a+2*\<varepsilon\>>, site price <math|\<gamma\>>, tree
  reserve <math|\<delta\>> and tail ratio <math|q>. We will use the same tree
  bound for distinct connected sets.

  For each connected finite set <math|A> containing a root label <math|i>,
  choose a spanning tree rooted at <math|i>. If <math|<around|\||A|\|>-1=m>,
  the <math|m>! enumerations of its other labels give <math|m>! different
  numbered trees. Their weights in <eqref|eq:cluster-forest-bound> each
  contain <math|1/m>!. Thus the sum over distinct sets and their chosen trees
  is bounded by the full numbered tree sum. Choose a root whose support
  contains <math|x>, or bound by the sum over all such roots. The rooted
  bound established in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:cluster-expansion>,
  followed by the geometric sum in <math|m>, proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:mixed-distinct-tail><big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<math-up|connected>,x\<in\>\<cup\><rsub|i\<in\>A>X<rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||A|\|>-1\<geqslant\>m>>>>>><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>A><wide|f|~><rsub|i>*e<rsup|<around|(|a+2*\<varepsilon\>|)>*c<around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>\<leqslant\><frac|\<gamma\>*q<rsup|m>|1-q>.
  </equation>

  No logarithmic coefficient is present in this distinct-set estimate.

  Let <math|k,m\<geqslant\>0> be integers and restrict the sets further by
  <math|D<around|(|A|)>\<geqslant\>T> and
  <math|E<around|(|A|)>\<geqslant\>U>, where <math|T,U> are real. The
  elementary polynomial estimate is

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|1+D|)><rsup|k>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,k>*e<rsup|\<varepsilon\>*D>,<space|2em>P<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,k>=\<varepsilon\><rsup|-k>*k!e<rsup|\<varepsilon\>>*<space|1em><around|(|D\<geqslant\>0|)>.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|D\<geqslant\>T> and <math|E\<geqslant\>U>,

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|\<varepsilon\>*D>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<varepsilon\>*T-U>*e<rsup|2*\<varepsilon\>*D+E>.
  </equation*>

  For connected <math|A> we also have <math|c(\<cup\><rsub|i>X<rsub|i>)\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i>c<around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>.
  Multiplying these inequalities, and then using
  <eqref|eq:mixed-distinct-tail>, proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:mixed-reserved-tail><big|sum><rsub|A><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>A>f<rsub|i>|)>*<around|(|1+D<around|(|A|)>|)><rsup|k>*e<rsup|a*c(\<cup\><rsub|i\<in\>A>X<rsub|i>)>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,k>*e<rsup|-\<varepsilon\>*T-U>*<frac|\<gamma\>*q<rsup|m>|1-q>.
  </equation>

  The sum on the left has all the preceding connectedness, site, cardinality,
  cost and mark restrictions. The two reserves have separate uses: one pays
  the polynomial, and the other leaves the explicit tail
  <math|e<rsup|-\<varepsilon\>*T>>.

  <subsubsection|Combining the two vertex populations>

  For an optional label take its original weight <math|w<rsub|i>>, extra cost
  <math|d<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0> and no mark. Assume its site estimate with
  the full head exponent <math|h=a+2*\<varepsilon\>+\<delta\>> is

  <\equation*>
    sup<rsub|x> <big|sum><rsub|i:x\<in\>X<rsub|i>>w<rsub|i>*e<rsup|2*\<varepsilon\>*d<rsub|i>>*e<rsup|h*c<around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>\<leqslant\>\<Gamma\><rsub|o>.
  </equation*>

  For a packet of size <math|n>, set extra cost <math|b*n> and mark
  <math|\<mu\>*n>, where <math|b,\<mu\>\<geqslant\>0>. Its modified weight is
  <math|p<rsub|A>*e<rsup|<around|(|2*\<varepsilon\>*b+\<mu\>|)>*n>>. Thus
  <eqref|eq:mixed-packet-hit> applies with seed expense replaced by
  <math|s+2*\<varepsilon\>*b+\<mu\>>, leaving the analytic packet bound
  <eqref|eq:mixed-packet-analytic> unchanged. If the resulting packet site
  price is <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|p>>, the combined alphabet has site price at
  most <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|p>+\<Gamma\><rsub|o>>. Choose
  <math|\<gamma\>\<gtr\>0> with

  <\equation*>
    \<Gamma\><rsub|p>+\<Gamma\><rsub|o>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\>,<space|2em>4*h<rsub|B>*\<gamma\>/\<delta\>\<leqslant\>q\<less\>1.
  </equation*>

  This is exactly the vertex condition needed for
  <eqref|eq:mixed-reserved-tail>.

  If the allowed component contains at least <math|r> seeds in total, then
  <math|E<around|(|A|)>\<geqslant\>\<mu\>*r>. Hence the right-hand side
  becomes

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:mixed-ceiling>P<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,k>*e<rsup|-\<varepsilon\>*T-\<mu\>*r>*<frac|\<gamma\>*q<rsup|m>|1-q>.
  </equation>

  This bound controls simultaneously excess label count <math|m>, polynomial
  order <math|k>, minimum seed count <math|r>, and minimum total cost
  <math|T>. In particular, <math|r=1> may be used only for components known
  to contain a mandatory packet. The support of an output may be smaller than
  the component support. Summing all output majorants over each component
  fiber first, a bound by <math|C<rsub|0>> times its component price gives
  <math|C<rsub|0>> times <eqref|eq:mixed-ceiling>, by support monotonicity
  and nonnegative summation. This is the required rule for summing analytic
  variants without losing their multiplicities.

  <subsubsection|Adding a normalized elementary insertion>

  The insertion changes one optional label already present in the alphabet.
  Its fine support has cover number <math|c<rsub|f>>, support exponent
  <math|\<nu\><rsub|f>>, and original scalar majorant <math|w\<geqslant\>0>.
  Let <math|g\<gtr\>0> denote the normalization gain and set

  <\equation*>
    j=g*e<rsup|-<around|(|\<nu\><rsub|f>-\<varepsilon\>|)>*c<rsub|f>>,<space|2em>D=<frac|w*e<rsup|\<nu\><rsub|f>*c<rsub|f>>*e<rsup|-\<varepsilon\>*c<rsub|f>>|g>.
  </equation*>

  Then <math|D*j=w> exactly. Thus <math|D> is the insertion debt and <math|j>
  the normalized vertex weight, not two independent prices.

  Let <math|X<rsub|\<ast\>>> be this label's actual coarse footprint, and
  suppose its projection geometry gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:mixed-marked-footprint>h*c<around|(|X<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>\<leqslant\>h*P+\<varepsilon\>*c<rsub|f>,<space|2em>\<nu\><rsub|f>\<geqslant\>4*\<varepsilon\>,
  </equation>

  where <math|h=a+2*\<varepsilon\>+\<delta\>> is the head exponent and
  <math|P> the fixed halo constant. Its extra cost is <math|c<rsub|f>>.
  Therefore

  <\equation*>
    j*e<rsup|2*\<varepsilon\>*c<rsub|f>>*e<rsup|h*c<around|(|X<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>>\<leqslant\>g*exp
    <around|{|h*P+<around|(|4*\<varepsilon\>-\<nu\><rsub|f>|)>*c<rsub|f>|}>\<leqslant\>g*e<rsup|h*P>=:R<rsub|\<ast\>>.
  </equation*>

  At a fixed site there is just this one modified label. Replacing its old
  nonnegative weight by <math|j> thus increases the optional site bound by at
  most <math|R<rsub|\<ast\>>>. This follows even if <math|j> is smaller than
  the old weight, by bounding the new array by the old array plus the single
  new entry. Set the new optional price and total vertex price to

  <\equation*>
    \<Gamma\><rsub|o><rprime|'>=\<Gamma\><rsub|o>+R<rsub|\<ast\>>,<space|2em>\<gamma\><rprime|'>=\<gamma\>+R<rsub|\<ast\>>.
  </equation*>

  The packet estimates are unchanged. Once
  <math|4*h<rsub|B>*\<gamma\><rprime|'>/\<delta\>\<leqslant\>q<rprime|'>\<less\>1>,
  the preceding mixed-component bound applies to the modified alphabet with
  these prices. Retaining only components containing the marked label
  decreases each nonnegative sum. This produces the marked-molecule estimate
  used for the order-zero tail in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:leaf-expansion>.

  For the actual projected footprint the sufficient geometric condition is
  <math|h*<around|(|10*P/L|)>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>>, together with the
  comparison of fine and coarse cover radii: the coarse cover radius
  <math|B<rsub|c>> is positive and the fine cover radius satisfies
  <math|B<rsub|f>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|c>>. Under these two conditions the
  cover estimate for the enlarged inspected footprint <math|X<rsub|\<ast\>>>,
  the projection of the fine support enlarged by the fixed halo, is
  <math|c<around|(|X<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>\<leqslant\>P+<around|(|10*P/L|)>*c<rsub|f>>,
  which gives <eqref|eq:mixed-marked-footprint>. The fixed block ratio is
  chosen large enough for this condition before the coupling is increased. If
  <math|g> and <math|\<gamma\>> have negative upper exponent
  <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>\<less\>0> in the fine coupling, then
  <math|R<rsub|\<ast\>>=g*e<rsup|h*P>> and <math|\<gamma\><rprime|'>> have
  the same upper exponent, with <math|L> and the halo constants fixed. The
  factor <math|h<rsub|B>> has upper exponent zero. The degree price therefore
  has upper exponent <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>, and is eventually bounded by the
  strictly larger power <math|<math-ss|h><around|(|x|)><rsup|q<rsub|\<ast\>>/2>>
  of the coupling scale <math|<math-ss|h><around|(|x|)>\<to\>\<infty\>> used
  to define upper exponents in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:packet-estimates>
  (written <math|h<around|(|x|)>> there; here <math|h> is the head exponent).
  This power tends to zero. This proves affordability of the augmented tree
  price under precisely these rate and geometric inputs.

  The combinatorial part of the mixed and marked estimates is now proved. The
  remaining analytic input is the packet weight bound
  <eqref|eq:mixed-packet-analytic>, including all its variants, together with
  the optional activity site bound and the quoted projection and rate
  estimates on the actual selected block step.

  <subsection|Simultaneous Gaussian warnings and integrated
  activities><label|sec:gaussian-warnings>

  The expansion contains factors which are small only after integration. A
  warning factor, for example, need not be small on a configuration with a
  large fluctuation. What is small is the probability that all warnings in a
  given term are present. Their fluctuations are correlated, and several
  warnings may be witnessed by the same Gaussian coordinate. We first account
  for both effects explicitly.

  <subsubsection|A bound for correlated coordinates>

  Let <math|I> be a finite coordinate set, and let <math|Z> be a centred real
  Gaussian vector with covariance matrix <math|A>. Suppose

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:warning-covariance>A\<geqslant\>0,<space|2em>v<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*A*v\<leqslant\>M*<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>v<rsub|i><rsup|2>*<space|1em><around|(|v\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|I>|)>,<space|2em>M\<geqslant\>0.
  </equation>

  The covariance may be singular. For <math|J\<subseteq\>I>,
  <math|t\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> and <math|s\<geqslant\>0>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:warning-rectangle>\<bbb-P\>*<around|{|<around|\||Z<rsub|i>|\|>\<geqslant\>t<text|
    for every >i\<in\>J|}>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|<around|\||J|\|>>*exp
    <around|{|<around|(|-s*t+M*s<rsup|2>/2|)><around|\||J|\|>|}>.
  </equation>

  Indeed, for each choice of signs <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|i>\<in\><around|{|-1,1|}>>
  on <math|J>, put <math|v<rsub|i>=\<epsilon\><rsub|i>> on <math|J> and
  <math|v<rsub|i>=0> elsewhere. On the corresponding orthant event,
  <math|v\<cdot\>Z\<geqslant\>t<around|\||J|\|>>. The Gaussian exponential
  identity gives

  <\equation*>
    \<bbb-E\>*e<rsup|s*v\<cdot\>Z>=e<rsup|s<rsup|2>*v<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*A*v/2>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|M*s<rsup|2><around|\||J|\|>/2>.
  </equation*>

  For completeness, write <math|Z=A<rsup|1/2>*G> with <math|G> a vector of
  independent standard real Gaussians. In each coordinate, completing the
  square in <math|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-1/2>*<big|int>e<rsup|h*u-u<rsup|2>/2>*<space|0.17em>d*u>
  gives <math|e<rsup|h<rsup|2>/2>>; multiplication proves the identity,
  including zero eigenvalues of <math|A>. Markov's inequality applied to
  <math|e<rsup|s*v\<cdot\>Z>> bounds one orthant by
  <math|e<rsup|<around|(|-s*t+M*s<rsup|2>/2|)><around|\||J|\|>>>, and the
  union of the <math|2<rsup|<around|\||J|\|>>> orthants contains the event in
  <eqref|eq:warning-rectangle>. This also proves the formula when <math|J> is
  empty.

  Let <math|\<Lambda\>> be the coarse periodic lattice, with its lattice
  distance. Write

  <\equation*>
    b<rsub|D>=<around|(|2*D+1|)><rsup|4>.
  </equation*>

  Every ball of radius <math|D> has at most <math|b<rsub|D>> sites, including
  on small periods where some representatives coincide. Assign each Gaussian
  coordinate a site <math|g<around|(|i|)>\<in\>\<Lambda\>>, with at most
  <math|p> coordinates at any site. Warning addresses form a finite set
  <math|K>, with site map <math|o:K\<to\>\<Lambda\>> and at most <math|m>
  addresses at any site. For a set <math|X\<subseteq\>K>, define

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-C\>*<around|(|X;t,D|)>=<around|{|z:\<forall\>a\<in\>X\<exists\>i\<in\>I,d*<around|(|o<around|(|a|)>,g<around|(|i|)>|)>\<leqslant\>D,<space|1em><around|\||z<rsub|i>|\|>\<geqslant\>t|}>.
  </equation*>

  These are measurable sets, being finite intersections of finite unions of
  closed coordinate inequalities.

  <\proposition>
    <label|prop:warning-crowding>If <math|H\<geqslant\>0> and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:warning-threshold>M/2+H*b<rsub|D>*m\<leqslant\>t,
    </equation>

    then

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:warning-joint>\<bbb-P\>*<around|{|Z\<in\>\<cal-C\>*<around|(|X;t,D|)>|}>\<leqslant\><around|(|2*b<rsub|D>*p*<space|0.17em>e<rsup|-H>|)><rsup|<around|\||X|\|>>.
    </equation>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    For each warning in <math|X>, choose a witnessing coordinate within
    distance <math|D>. There are at most <math|<around|(|b<rsub|D>*p|)><rsup|<around|\||X|\|>>>
    such assignments. For one assignment, let <math|J> be the set of distinct
    coordinates chosen. Then <math|<around|\||J|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||X|\|>>.
    Conversely, any fixed chosen coordinate can witness at most
    <math|b<rsub|D>*m> addresses, since all such addresses lie within
    distance <math|D> of its site. Thus <math|<around|\||X|\|>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|D>*m<around|\||J|\|>>.
    Applying <eqref|eq:warning-rectangle> with <math|s=1>, the event
    belonging to this assignment has probability at most

    <\equation*>
      2<rsup|<around|\||J|\|>>*e<rsup|<around|(|-t+M/2|)><around|\||J|\|>>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|<around|\||X|\|>>*e<rsup|-H*b<rsub|D>*m<around|\||J|\|>>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|<around|\||X|\|>>*e<rsup|-H<around|\||X|\|>>.
    </equation*>

    The union bound over assignments proves the assertion. If a warning has
    no possible coordinate, its event is empty; the empty set of warnings has
    probability one. Thus no nonemptiness exception is needed.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The scales used for warnings>

  Fix the block length <math|L>. Let <math|h=h<around|(|x|)>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|f>>
  be the fine reference coupling, so that <math|h=x+c<rsub|L>> for a constant
  <math|c<rsub|L>> depending on the fixed group and block length. For
  sufficiently large <math|x>, put

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:warning-scales>B=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>,<space|2em>P=exp <around|{|<around|(|log log
    h|)><rsup|2>|}>,<space|2em>d=1+B+<around|(|log
    h|)><rsup|2>,<space|2em>\<tau\>=P/<sqrt|h>.
  </equation>

  The actual fine coupling satisfies <math|h/2\<leqslant\>\<beta\>\<leqslant\>2*h>.
  The fluctuation is written <math|\<mu\>+\<beta\><rsup|-1/2><math-up|erect><around|(|Z|)>>;
  <math|\<mu\>> is its mean and <math|<math-up|erect>> identifies colour
  coordinates with bond variables. For a fixed <math|\<xi\>\<gtr\>0>, the
  coordinate warning threshold is

  <\equation*>
    t=\<xi\>*\<tau\>*<sqrt|\<beta\>>\<geqslant\>\<xi\>*P/2.
  </equation*>

  The weaker constant <math|1/2> is convenient and follows from
  <math|<sqrt|\<beta\>>\<geqslant\><sqrt|h>/2>.

  For the actual warning alphabet, its density per coarse site is bounded by
  the fixed integer

  <\equation*>
    m=L<rsup|4>#<around|{|<text|plaquette
    axes>|}>+2#<around|{|<text|axes>|}>+1+L<rsup|4>+L<rsup|4>#<around|{|<text|axes>|}>.
  </equation*>

  The five first types are fine plaquette masks, coarse bond masks, coarse
  bond likelihoods, coarse site likelihoods, and fine site contractions; the
  last type is a fine bond cutoff. The coordinate density <math|p> is the
  fixed bound for the tubular colour coordinates. Both bounds count distinct
  addresses, including different types at the same site.

  Suppose that the physical covariance satisfies
  <eqref|eq:warning-covariance> with a constant <math|M> independent of the
  period and of <math|x>. Set <math|D=2*B>, <math|\<varepsilon\>=\<xi\>/4>,
  and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:warning-price>E<around|(|x|)>=2*b<rsub|2*B>*p*exp
    <around|{|-\<varepsilon\>*<sqrt|P>|}>.
  </equation>

  Then, for all sufficiently large <math|x>, uniformly in the period and in
  <math|X>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:warning-actual-price>\<bbb-P\>*<around|{|Z\<in\>\<cal-C\>*<around|(|X;t,2*B|)>|}>\<leqslant\>E<around|(|x|)><rsup|<around|\||X|\|>>.
  </equation>

  Here is the scale comparison needed to justify this assertion. First
  <math|d\<leqslant\>7<around|(|log h|)><rsup|2>> eventually, since
  <math|h=x+c<rsub|L>>, <math|log x\<leqslant\>2*log h> eventually, and
  <math|B\<less\><around|(|log x|)><rsup|2>+1>. For every fixed
  <math|a\<gtr\>0>, integer <math|r\<geqslant\>0> and constant
  <math|C\<geqslant\>0>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:warning-monitor-pays>C*d<rsup|r>\<leqslant\>P<rsup|a><space|1em><text|eventually>.
  </equation>

  If <math|C=0> there is nothing to prove. For <math|C\<gtr\>0>, taking
  logarithms, it suffices to compare a constant plus <math|2*r*log log h>
  with <math|a<around|(|log log h|)><rsup|2>>; the latter dominates. Since
  <math|b<rsub|2*B>*m> is bounded by a fixed multiple of <math|d<rsup|4>>,
  <eqref|eq:warning-monitor-pays> implies
  <math|b<rsub|2*B>*m\<leqslant\><sqrt|P>> and
  <math|M/2\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>*P> eventually. Consequently

  <\equation*>
    M/2+\<varepsilon\>*<sqrt|P>*<space|0.17em>b<rsub|2*B>*m\<leqslant\>2*\<varepsilon\>*P=\<xi\>*P/2\<leqslant\>t.
  </equation*>

  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:warning-crowding>, with
  <math|H=\<varepsilon\>*<sqrt|P>>, gives <eqref|eq:warning-actual-price>.

  In fact <math|E> decays faster than every negative power of <math|h>. To
  see this, write <math|y=log log h>. Then <math|log h=e<rsup|y>> and
  <math|<sqrt|P>=e<rsup|y<rsup|2>/2>>. For each <math|N\<gtr\>0>,

  <\equation*>
    \<varepsilon\>*e<rsup|y<rsup|2>/2>-N*e<rsup|y>-log
    <around|(|2*p*b<rsub|2*B>|)>\<longrightarrow\>+\<infty\>,
  </equation*>

  because <math|log b<rsub|2*B>=O<around|(|y|)>> and
  <math|e<rsup|y<rsup|2>/2>/e<rsup|y>\<to\>\<infty\>>. Thus
  <math|E<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>h<rsup|-N>> eventually. The thresholds may
  depend on <math|L,\<xi\>,M,p,m,N>; they do not depend on the lattice
  period.

  <subsubsection|Why a nonzero warning forces an excursion>

  We spell out the deterministic step used to connect the preceding
  probability estimate with the actual factors. Let <math|q> be the number of
  colour coordinates on one bond. On the event that all coordinates within
  distance <math|B> of a site obey <math|<around|\||Z<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<xi\>*\<tau\>*<sqrt|\<beta\>>>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|\<beta\><rsup|-1/2><math-up|erect><around|(|Z|)><rsub|b>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>q*\<xi\>*\<tau\>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|b>+\<beta\><rsup|-1/2><math-up|erect><around|(|Z|)><rsub|b>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|C+q*\<xi\>|)>*\<tau\>
  </equation*>

  for the bonds in question, provided <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|b>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*\<tau\>>
  there. The first inequality follows by the triangle inequality in an
  orthonormal colour basis; it does not require a sharp Euclidean bound.
  Choose <math|q*\<xi\>\<leqslant\>1> and an outer cutoff plateau
  <math|a\<geqslant\>C+1>. The bond cutoff is then identically one throughout
  this smaller event.

  For the remaining masks we use the local fine and predictor estimates on
  the same radius-<math|B> event. At each coarse site at distance greater
  than <math|R<rsub|p>> from the seed set, assume a local error bound
  <math|\<delta\>*\<tau\>> for each fine or predictor reading, with guide
  bounds <math|A*\<tau\>/L<rsup|2>> and <math|A*\<tau\>>, respectively. The
  local error has three parts: the mean error, the change under the
  controlled fluctuation, and the nonlinear remainder. Each is bounded by
  <math|\<delta\>*\<tau\>/3>. If the positive quadratic constants of the fine
  action and predictor penalty are <math|c<rsub|f>,c<rsub|p>>, the resulting
  bounds are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:warning-plateau><text|fine reading>\<leqslant\><frac|2|<sqrt|c<rsub|f>>>*<around|(|A/L<rsup|2>+\<delta\>|)>*\<tau\>,<space|2em><text|predictor
    reading>\<leqslant\><frac|2|<sqrt|c<rsub|p>>>*<around|(|A+\<delta\>|)>*\<tau\>.
  </equation>

  Choose <math|\<delta\>=<sqrt|c<rsub|f>>/32>, then <math|L> large enough
  that <math|A/L<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>>, and finally
  <math|W\<gtr\><around|(|2/<sqrt|c<rsub|p>>|)>*<around|(|A+\<delta\>|)>>.
  The first coefficient in <eqref|eq:warning-plateau> is at most
  <math|1/8\<less\>1/4>, and the second is strictly less than <math|W>. The
  corresponding fine and predictor masks therefore equal one; the local
  likelihood factors also equal one by the same local plateau estimates.
  Taking <math|\<xi\>> smaller if necessary bounds the linear fluctuation
  error by <math|\<delta\>*\<tau\>/3>; the two remaining errors are
  eventually below <math|\<delta\>*\<tau\>/3> by their strictly smaller scale
  estimates.

  A warning factor is either a bounded cutoff times one of these factors
  minus one, or a bond cutoff minus one. Factors inside their compulsory seed
  region are defined to be zero. We now make the passage from these local
  statements to the warning precise; this requires the contraction type,
  which the preceding paragraphs do not treat, and the change of radius from
  <math|B> to <math|2*B>.

  For a coarse site <math|j> write <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|r><around|(|j|)>> for
  the event that all coordinates within distance <math|r> of <math|j> obey
  <math|<around|\||Z<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<xi\>*\<tau\>*<sqrt|\<beta\>>>.
  Let <math|t<rsub|f>> be the fine chord threshold of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-energy> (written <math|t> there; it is
  unrelated to the coordinate threshold <math|t> of this section). Say that
  the <em|plateau conclusion> holds at <math|j> if: the site likelihood at
  <math|j> equals one; every fine plaquette whose anchor has parent <math|j>
  has chord less than <math|t<rsub|f>/4>; and for every axis <math|e> the
  bond likelihood of the coarse bond <math|<around|(|j,e|)>> equals one and
  its predictor chord is less than <math|W*t<rsub|f>>. The local estimates
  described above give the plateau conclusion at every coarse site <math|j>
  at distance greater than <math|R<rsub|p>> from the seed set on the event
  <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|B><around|(|j|)>>; this is the form in which they
  enter, and it remains an analytic input. We assume that the compulsory seed
  region has radius <math|R<rsub|c>\<geqslant\>R<rsub|p>+B>.

  The <em|fine site contraction> at a fine site <math|y>, for the given
  finite list <math|l> of forced activities, is the factor equal to <math|0>
  if <math|y> is not covered by the supports in <math|l> and <math|y> is a
  fine flag of the current fine field at the flag threshold of the
  representation, and equal to <math|1> otherwise. Here <math|y> is a fine
  flag at threshold <math|\<theta\>> if some fine plaquette having <math|y>
  as a corner has chord greater than <math|\<theta\>>. The representation's
  flag threshold is the fine threshold <math|t<rsub|f>>; this identification
  is part of the data of the representation.

  The assigned coarse site of a warning is the parent of its fine address
  (fine plaquette masks, through the anchor, and fine site contractions), the
  base point of its coarse bond (bond masks, bond likelihoods and bond
  cutoffs), or its coarse site (site likelihoods).

  <\lemma>
    <label|lem:warning-vanishes>Let a warning have assigned coarse site
    <math|i> outside the compulsory seed region, and suppose
    <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|2*B><around|(|i|)>> holds and <math|\<mu\>> obeys
    the bound <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|b>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*\<tau\>>
    used above on the bonds within distance <math|2*B> of <math|i>. Then the
    warning factor is zero.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|j> be a coarse site with <math|<math-up|dist><around|(|i,j|)>\<leqslant\>B>.
    The ball of radius <math|B> about <math|j> lies in the ball of radius
    <math|2*B> about <math|i>, so <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|B><around|(|j|)>>
    holds. Since <math|<math-up|dist><around|(|i,<text|seeds>|)>\<gtr\>R<rsub|c>\<geqslant\>R<rsub|p>+B>,
    we have <math|<math-up|dist><around|(|j,<text|seeds>|)>\<gtr\>R<rsub|p>>.
    Hence the plateau conclusion holds at every such <math|j>; in particular
    at <math|j=i>.

    <em|Bond cutoff.> On <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|B><around|(|i|)>> the first
    paragraph of this subsection shows that the bond cutoff equals one on the
    bonds based at <math|i>, so the warning, a bond cutoff minus one,
    vanishes.

    <em|Fine and predictor masks, bond and site likelihoods.> The fine
    readings at plaquettes with parent <math|i> and the predictor readings on
    bonds based at <math|i> are below the mask plateaus by
    <eqref|eq:warning-plateau> and the choice of <math|\<delta\>,L,W>, and
    the likelihoods at <math|i> equal one, by the plateau conclusion at
    <math|i>. Each such factor equals one, and the warning, a bounded cutoff
    times the factor minus one, vanishes.

    <em|Fine site contraction.> Let the warning be the contraction at a fine
    site <math|y> with parent <math|i>, and suppose its factor is <math|0>.
    Then <math|y> is a fine flag at threshold <math|t<rsub|f>>, so some fine
    plaquette <math|v> having <math|y> as a corner has chord greater than
    <math|t<rsub|f>>. The anchor of <math|v> differs from <math|y> by at most
    one unit in each coordinate, and the parent map changes each coordinate
    by at most one when its argument changes by one, so the parent <math|j>
    of the anchor of <math|v> satisfies <math|<math-up|dist><around|(|i,j|)>\<leqslant\>1\<leqslant\>B>.
    The plateau conclusion at <math|j> gives
    chord<math|<around|(|v|)>\<less\>t<rsub|f>/4\<less\>t<rsub|f>>, a
    contradiction. Hence the contraction equals one and the warning vanishes.
  </proof>

  Taking the contrapositive: a nonzero warning outside the compulsory region
  implies that <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|2*B><around|(|i|)>> fails, that is, a
  coordinate excursion within distance <math|2*B> of its assigned site
  <math|i>; inside the compulsory region the warning is zero by definition.
  This is the implication used below, with the radius <math|2*B> of
  <eqref|eq:warning-actual-price>. The local estimates producing
  <eqref|eq:warning-plateau> and the plateau conclusion, the bound on
  <math|\<mu\>>, and the uniform physical covariance bound remain explicit
  analytic inputs here. The probability and integration arguments do not
  assume or replace their production.

  <subsubsection|Integration before multiplication of prices>

  Fix a tree sector. Its integration variables are a Haar variable <math|Y>
  and the single Gaussian vector <math|Z> above. Their reference measure
  <math|\<nu\>*<around|(|d*Y|)>*\<gamma\><rsub|A>*<around|(|d*Z|)>> is a
  product of probability measures. All factors in a term are evaluated at the
  same <math|<around|(|Y,Z|)>>.

  A term is indexed by a finite set <math|S> of distinct vertices of four
  types: connected packets, forced activities, exponential logarithmic
  corrections, and warnings. Assume these factors are measurable and satisfy
  the following uniform pointwise bounds:

  <\equation*>
    <tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-lborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-rborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-rborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|<text|vertex
    >i>|<cell|<around|\||f<rsub|i>*<around|(|Y,Z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|<text|packet
    with >n<rsub|i><text| seeds>>|<cell|C<rsub|0>*e<rsup|<around|(|s-g|)>*n<rsub|i>>>>|<row|<cell|<text|forced
    activity >i>|<cell|v<rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|<text|logarithmic correction
    >i>|<cell|w<rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|<text|warning >i>|<cell|T>>>>>
  </equation*>

  Here all displayed prices are nonnegative. The price <math|w<rsub|i>>
  bounds the actual factor <math|e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|i>>-1> as well as the
  logarithmic quantity when that additional estimate is used elsewhere. A
  disconnected packet is assigned factor and price zero. Write
  <math|X<around|(|S|)>> for the distinct warning addresses in <math|S>.
  Suppose each individual nonzero warning forces its coordinate excursion, as
  established under the local inputs above. Thus for every finite <math|S>, a
  nonzero product forces <math|Z\<in\>\<cal-C\>*<around|(|X<around|(|S|)>;t,2*B|)>>.
  Then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:warning-sector-mass><big|int><around*|\||<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S>f<rsub|i>*<around|(|Y,Z|)>|\|>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|A>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S><wide|c|~><rsub|i>,<space|2em><wide|c|~><rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|E<around|(|x|)>*T,>|<cell|i<text|
    a warning>,>>|<row|<cell|c<rsub|i>,>|<cell|<text|otherwise>.>>>>>
  </equation>

  Indeed the integrand is bounded by <math|\<b-1\><rsub|\<cal-C\>*<around|(|X<around|(|S|)>;t,2*B|)>><around|(|Z|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S>c<rsub|i>>.
  It is integrable, being measurable and uniformly bounded on a probability
  space. Fubini's theorem and <eqref|eq:warning-actual-price> give a bound
  <math|E<around|(|x|)><rsup|<around|\||X<around|(|S|)>|\|>>*<big|prod>c<rsub|i>>,
  which is exactly the right-hand side of <eqref|eq:warning-sector-mass>. In
  particular, no product of marginal warning probabilities has been used.

  The same calculation covers one marked insertion. Let its distinguished
  vertex be <math|i<rsub|\<ast\>>> and let its measurable factor
  <math|F<rsub|\<ast\>>> satisfy <math|<around|\||F<rsub|\<ast\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>w<rsub|\<ast\>>>.
  Multiply it by a cutoff taking values in <math|<around|[|0,1|]>>. Define
  the marked term to be zero unless <math|i<rsub|\<ast\>>\<in\>S>, and
  otherwise replace <math|f<rsub|i<rsub|\<ast\>>>> by this cutoff times
  <math|F<rsub|\<ast\>>>. Applying <eqref|eq:warning-sector-mass> to
  <math|S\<setminus\><around|{|i<rsub|\<ast\>>|}>> gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:warning-marked-mass><big|int><around|\||<text|marked
    term>|\|>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|A>\<leqslant\>\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|i<rsub|\<ast\>>\<in\>S|}>>*w<rsub|\<ast\>>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S\<setminus\><around|{|i<rsub|\<ast\>>|}>><wide|c|~><rsub|i>.
  </equation>

  If the removed vertex was a warning, its probability price is removed as
  well. The remaining warnings alone are used in the event bound. This is the
  marked vertex price required for the connected sums of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mixed-activity-prices>.

  For the actual factors one may take <math|T=1+#<around|{|<text|frame
  tags>|}>+#<around|{|<text|point tags>|}>>. Indeed the unmodified mask or
  likelihood lies in <math|<around|[|0,T|]>>, the enforcing cutoff lies in
  <math|<around|[|0,1|]>>, and <math|T\<geqslant\>1>, so its cutoff times the
  difference from one has absolute value at most <math|T>. A bond cutoff
  minus one is bounded by one. Thus the small warning price in
  <eqref|eq:warning-sector-mass> is supplied by the proved Gaussian estimate.
  The packet, forced, and logarithmic pointwise bounds in the table are
  separate analytic obligations; <eqref|eq:warning-sector-mass> is the
  precise passage from those bounds to integrated vertex products.

  <subsection|Gauge comparison and the geometry of large
  defects><label|sec:seed-geometry>

  We now prove the geometric estimates used in the seed energy bound. The
  essential analytic point is a first-power estimate for the actual coarse
  predictor. It must hold on both branches of its definition and for every
  output with positive weight. Small-field uniqueness alone would not give
  the estimate needed here.

  Write <math|r<around|(|g|)>=D<rsup|-1>*<around|\<\|\|\>|g-1|\<\|\|\>>> for
  the normalized matrix chord. Thus <math|r\<leqslant\>2>,
  <math|r<around|(|g<rsup|-1>|)>=r<around|(|g|)>>, <math|r> is conjugation
  invariant, and <math|r*<around|(|g*h|)>\<leqslant\>r<around|(|g|)>+r<around|(|h|)>>.
  For a finite lattice box <math|X>, let

  <\equation*>
    E<rsub|X><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|x,a|)>>>|<row|<cell|x,x+e<rsub|a>\<in\>X>>>>>>r<around|(|U<rsub|x,a>|)>,<space|2em>F<rsub|X><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|x,p|)>>>|<row|<cell|<text|all
    corners in >X>>>>>>r<around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The sums include positive bonds and one orientation for each plaquette. All
  boxes in the first part of the argument lie in the covering lattice
  <math|\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>; periodic multiplicities will be restored at the
  end.

  <subsubsection|An axial gauge with a first-power bound>

  For the box of radius <math|R>, with side <math|q=2*R+1>, there is a
  coordinate path gauge <math|H> such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:geometry-axial>E<rsub|X>*<around|(|H\<cdot\>U|)>\<leqslant\>16*q*F<rsub|X><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation>

  Here the gauge at <math|x> is parallel transport from a suitable base point
  to <math|x>, moving along the coordinates in a prescribed order. We prove
  more generally the bound <math|4*k*q*F<rsub|X>> on a <math|k>-dimensional
  coordinate slab. It is zero on a one-point slab. Suppose the assertion
  holds in <math|k> dimensions and add a coordinate <math|j>. Choose a slice
  perpendicular to <math|j> with least plaquette sum; its sum is at most
  <math|F<rsub|X>/q>, since the plaquettes in different slices are distinct.
  Choose the base point there by induction, and extend the paths by moving
  last along <math|j>. All <math|j>-bonds in the resulting gauge are the
  identity.

  For a transverse bond, two neighbouring slice values differ in chord by at
  most the chord of the plaquette between them. Indeed, with the two vertical
  bonds equal to <math|1>, that plaquette is <math|a*b<rsup|-1>>, and
  <math|<around|\||r<around|(|a|)>-r<around|(|b|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>r*<around|(|a*b<rsup|-1>|)>>.
  Summing over the transverse directions costs at most four times the
  plaquette sum between the slices. Telescoping in <math|j> shows that the
  bond sums of any two slices differ by at most <math|4*F<rsub|X>>. The total
  bond sum is consequently at most

  <\equation*>
    q*<around*|(|4*k*q*<frac|F<rsub|X>|q>+4*F<rsub|X>|)>=4*<around|(|k+1|)>*q*F<rsub|X>.
  </equation*>

  This proves <eqref|eq:geometry-axial>, with the required coordinate path
  form of the gauge. In particular the fallback rule which permits axial
  gauges with bound <math|K<rsub|<math-up|ax>>*q*F<rsub|X>> has an eligible
  choice whenever <math|K<rsub|<math-up|ax>>\<geqslant\>16>.

  We also need a path counting estimate. If <math|N=<around|\||X|\|>>, then
  for any fixed coordinate order,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:geometry-path-count><big|sum><rsub|x,y\<in\>X>r<around|(|U<rsub|x\<to\>y>|)>\<leqslant\>4*N*q*E<rsub|X><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation>

  For each coordinate segment use the sum of the bond chords on its
  coordinate line. During that segment the other coordinates are taken from
  either <math|x> or <math|y>, according to whether they have already been
  changed. Exchanging these coordinate slots between the two endpoints is a
  bijection of <math|X\<times\>X>. Thus the mixed point is uniformly
  distributed when the endpoints are uniformly distributed. Averaging the sum
  over its coordinate line gives <math|q*E<rsub|X,a>/N>, where
  <math|E<rsub|X,a>> is the bond sum in direction <math|a>. Bounding each of
  the at most four segments by <math|q*E<rsub|X>/N> and multiplying by
  <math|N<rsup|2>> proves <eqref|eq:geometry-path-count>. Reversed segments
  have the same bound because the chord is invariant under inversion.

  <subsubsection|Synchronizing minimizing frames>

  Let <math|I> and <math|A> be nonempty finite sets, and suppose
  <math|W<rsub|i*j*\<alpha\>>\<in\>G>, with <math|W<rsub|i*i*\<alpha\>>=1>
  and <math|W<rsub|j*i*<wide|\<alpha\>|\<bar\>>>=W<rsub|i*j*\<alpha\>><rsup|-1>>
  for a permutation <math|\<alpha\>\<mapsto\><wide|\<alpha\>|\<bar\>>> of
  <math|A>. A minimizing frame <math|h:I\<to\>G> minimizes

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-E\><around|(|h|)>=<math-up|av><rsub|i,j,\<alpha\>><tfrac|1|2>r*<around|(|h<rsub|i>*W<rsub|i*j*\<alpha\>>*h<rsub|j><rsup|-1>|)><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  Such a minimum exists by compactness. Put

  <\equation*>
    b<rsub|i>=<math-up|av><rsub|j,\<alpha\>><space|0.17em>r<around|(|W<rsub|i*j*\<alpha\>>|)>,<space|2em>b=<math-up|av><rsub|i><space|0.17em>b<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  There is a constant <math|C<rsub|G>>, independent of both finite sets and
  of <math|W>, such that some <math|c\<in\>G> satisfies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:geometry-cluster><math-up|av><rsub|i><space|0.17em>r*<around|(|h<rsub|i>*c<rsup|-1>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|G>*b.
  </equation>

  Here are the details which improve the square-root estimate to this
  first-power bound. Comparison with the constant frame gives
  <math|\<cal-E\><around|(|h|)>\<leqslant\>b>, since
  <math|r<rsup|2>/2\<leqslant\>r>. The triangle inequality and
  <math|<around|(|u+v|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2*u<rsup|2>+2*v<rsup|2>>
  therefore give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:geometry-pair-square><math-up|av><rsub|i,j><around|\<\|\|\>|h<rsub|i>-h<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>8*D<rsup|2>*b.
  </equation>

  Write <math|m=<math-up|av><rsub|j><space|0.17em>h<rsub|j>> for the matrix
  centroid and <math|m<rsub|i>=<math-up|av><rsub|j,\<alpha\>><space|0.17em>h<rsub|j>*W<rsub|i*j*\<alpha\>><rsup|-1>>
  for its local replacement. Then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:geometry-feedback><around|\<\|\|\>|m<rsub|i>-m|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>D*b<rsub|i>,<space|2em>h<rsub|i><text|
    is a nearest group element to >m<rsub|i>.
  </equation>

  The norm bound follows by averaging matrix differences. For the second
  assertion, change only <math|h<rsub|i>> in the minimizing energy. The two
  off-diagonal parts have equal costs by the reversal identity. The diagonal
  term in the actual energy remains zero. Freezing its second endpoint would
  add the nonnegative term <math|r*<around|(|k*h<rsub|i><rsup|-1>|)><rsup|2>/2>,
  which is zero at <math|k=h<rsub|i>>; hence <math|h<rsub|i>> also minimizes
  the full frozen-neighbour cost. Expanding the squared Frobenius norms shows
  that this is exactly nearest-point minimization for <math|m<rsub|i>>.

  Let <math|\<pi\>> and <math|\<rho\>> be the nearest-point projection and
  its radius from Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:branch-nearest-projection>. Its
  derivative at <math|1> is the orthogonal projection <math|\<Pi\>>, of
  operator norm at most <math|1>, and <math|D*\<pi\>> is continuous, so there
  is <math|\<delta\>\<in\><around|(|0,\<rho\>|]>> with
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D*\<pi\>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2> on the ball
  <math|B*<around|(|1,\<delta\>|)>>. By part<nbsp>(b) of that lemma, every
  matrix <math|A> in this ball has exactly one nearest group element,
  <math|\<pi\><around|(|A|)>>; integrating <math|D*\<pi\>> along segments in
  the convex ball gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<pi\><around|(|A|)>-\<pi\><around|(|B|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*<around|\<\|\|\>|A-B|\<\|\|\>>>.
  Thus two matrices in the <math|\<delta\>>-ball have nearest points at
  distance at most twice their distance. Set

  <\equation*>
    \<sigma\>=<frac|<around|(|\<delta\>/2|)><rsup|2>|8*D<rsup|2>>,<space|2em>a=<frac|\<delta\>|2*D>.
  </equation*>

  If <math|b\<less\>\<sigma\>>, choose <math|c> nearest to <math|m> and
  rotate the frame by <math|c<rsup|-1>>. In the rotated coordinates <math|1>
  is nearest to its centroid. Choose <math|k> whose mean squared distance to
  the frame is at most the double average in <eqref|eq:geometry-pair-square>.
  Jensen's inequality and the nearest-point property give

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|m-1|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|m-h<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><math-up|av><rsub|j><around|\<\|\|\>|h<rsub|j>-h<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<less\><around|(|\<delta\>/2|)><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|b<rsub|i>\<less\>a>, <eqref|eq:geometry-feedback> puts
  <math|m<rsub|i>> in the <math|\<delta\>>-ball and gives
  <math|D*r<around|(|h<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>2*D*b<rsub|i>>. For
  <math|b<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>a> use <math|r<around|(|h<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>2\<leqslant\>2*b<rsub|i>/a>.
  Averaging proves the desired inequality in this case. If
  <math|b\<geqslant\>\<sigma\>>, simply use the bound
  <math|2\<leqslant\>2*b/\<sigma\>>. Thus <eqref|eq:geometry-cluster> holds
  with <math|C<rsub|G>=max <around|{|2,2/a,2/\<sigma\>|}>>. Left and right
  rotations give the same chord, by conjugation invariance.

  Apply this to the coordinate path transports on a patch <math|X> of side
  <math|q>. The path orders supply <math|A>, with reversed orders supplying
  the involution above. If <math|f> is the actual minimizing frame and
  <math|H> is any comparison gauge, then <math|h<rsub|i>=f<rsub|i>*H<rsub|i><rsup|-1>>
  minimizes the path cost for <math|H\<cdot\>U>. This follows by substituting
  into every path product; normalizing a competitor at the patch centre is
  harmless because a common left rotation preserves the cost. Equations
  <eqref|eq:geometry-path-count> and <eqref|eq:geometry-cluster> yield

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>X>r*<around|(|c<rsup|-1>*f<rsub|i>*H<rsub|i><rsup|-1>|)>\<leqslant\>4*C<rsub|G>*q*E<rsub|X>*<around|(|H\<cdot\>U|)>.
  </equation*>

  For an active fallback frame, eligibility instead gives
  <math|E<rsub|X>*<around|(|f\<cdot\>U|)>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|<math-up|ax>>*q*F<rsub|X><around|(|U|)>>.
  The pure-gauge discrepancy <math|d<rsub|i>=f<rsub|i>*H<rsub|i><rsup|-1>>
  satisfies

  <\equation*>
    E<rsub|X>*<around|(|d\<cdot\>1|)>\<leqslant\>E<rsub|X>*<around|(|f\<cdot\>U|)>+E<rsub|X>*<around|(|H\<cdot\>U|)>.
  </equation*>

  This is the bondwise triangle inequality, with conjugation removing
  <math|H\<cdot\>U>. Its path transport from <math|i> to <math|j> is
  <math|d<rsub|i>*d<rsub|j><rsup|-1>>. Average <eqref|eq:geometry-path-count>
  over <math|j> and select one <math|j> whose sum is no larger than the
  average. Taking <math|c=d<rsub|j>> proves the same conclusion with the
  added axial term. Both branches therefore satisfy, for <math|A<rsub|G>=max
  <around|{|4*C<rsub|G>,4|}>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:geometry-frame><big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>X>r*<around|(|c<rsup|-1>*f<rsub|i>*H<rsub|i><rsup|-1>|)>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|G>*q*<around|{|E<rsub|X>*<around|(|H\<cdot\>U|)>+K<rsub|<math-up|ax>>*q*F<rsub|X><around|(|U|)>|}>.
  </equation>

  Only positivity of the selected frame weight is required; the fallback
  assertion would be false for an ineligible tag.

  <subsubsection|The actual coarse predictor>

  Fix the smoothing order <math|J\<geqslant\>4> and the kernel constants. The
  odd block side <math|L> may vary. The patch has radius
  <math|<around|(|J+3|)>*L>, and the macro box has radius
  <math|<around|(|J+7|)>*L>. Fix a coarse site <math|x>, lift the field
  around <math|L*x> to this macro box, and write <math|P<rsub|x>> for its
  plaquette chord sum. All translated patches at coarse offsets in the
  radius-two box lie inside the macro box. Their connecting straight paths of
  length <math|L> lie there as well.

  By <eqref|eq:geometry-axial> choose a macro gauge <math|H> with
  <math|E<rsub|<math-up|macro>>*<around|(|H\<cdot\>U|)>\<leqslant\>D<rsub|0>*L*P<rsub|x>>,
  where <math|D<rsub|0>> depends only on <math|J>. Applying
  <eqref|eq:geometry-frame> on each translated patch gives constants
  <math|c<rsub|y>\<in\>G> with

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:geometry-macro-frame><big|sum><rsub|i>r*<around|(|c<rsub|y><rsup|-1>*f<rsub|x+y><around|(|i|)>*H<rsub|i+L*y><rsup|-1>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|0>*L<rsup|2>*P<rsub|x>.
  </equation>

  Indeed each patch side is at most a constant times <math|L>, and its bond
  and plaquette sums are bounded by the macro sums. The constants
  <math|C<rsub|0>,D<rsub|0>> are fixed before choosing <math|L>.

  The patch weights <math|w<rsub|i>> are the distribution of the sum of
  <math|J> independent uniform points in the small box of side <math|L>. They
  obey

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:geometry-light>w<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>L<rsup|-4>.
  </equation>

  Condition on all but the first point. For a prescribed sum there is at most
  one possible first point, among <math|L<rsup|4>> equiprobable choices. This
  proves <eqref|eq:geometry-light>, including coincidences among different
  tuples of points.

  For a coarse bond from <math|x+y> to <math|x+y+e<rsub|a>>, let
  <math|Y<rsub|i>> be its connector in the two selected frames. Gauging the
  middle straight path by <math|H> expresses
  <math|c<rsub|y><rsup|-1>*Y<rsub|i>*c<rsub|y+e<rsub|a>>> as the product of
  the two frame errors and that path transport. At each of its <math|L>
  steps, translation of <math|i> is injective; hence the sum of the path
  chords is at most <math|L*E<rsub|<math-up|macro>>*<around|(|H\<cdot\>U|)>>.
  Equations <eqref|eq:geometry-macro-frame> and <eqref|eq:geometry-light>
  give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:geometry-connector><big|sum><rsub|i>w<rsub|i>*r*<around|(|c<rsub|y><rsup|-1>*Y<rsub|i>*c<rsub|y+e<rsub|a>>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|2*C<rsub|0>+D<rsub|0>|)>*L<rsup|-2>*P<rsub|x>.
  </equation>

  The predictor <math|Q> either minimizes the weighted squared chord to the
  connectors, or selects a connector when that minimum is at least the fixed
  positive cutoff <math|\<zeta\>>. For every group element <math|b> its
  output satisfies

  <\equation*>
    r*<around|(|Q*b<rsup|-1>|)>\<leqslant\>2*max
    <around|{|1,\<zeta\><rsup|-1>|}>*<big|sum><rsub|i>w<rsub|i>*r*<around|(|Y<rsub|i>*b<rsup|-1>|)>.
  </equation*>

  On the minimizing branch, <math|Q> is nearest to the weighted matrix
  centroid; comparison with <math|b> and two triangle inequalities prove the
  factor <math|2>. On the other branch, <math|\<zeta\>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i>w<rsub|i>*r*<around|(|Y<rsub|i>*b<rsup|-1>|)><rsup|2>/2\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i>w<rsub|i>*r*<around|(|Y<rsub|i>*b<rsup|-1>|)>>,
  while <math|r*<around|(|Q*b<rsup|-1>|)>\<leqslant\>2>. This proves the
  claim for every output tag. Using <math|b=c<rsub|y>*c<rsub|y+e<rsub|a>><rsup|-1>>
  in <eqref|eq:geometry-connector> bounds each gauged coarse bond by
  <math|C<rsub|1>*L<rsup|-2>*P<rsub|x>>. The four corner gauges cancel around
  a plaquette, so

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:geometry-predictor-curvature>r<around|(|Q<rsub|p>|)>\<leqslant\>C*L<rsup|-2>*P<rsub|x>,<space|2em>r<around|(|V<rsub|p>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|e\<in\>\<partial\>*p>r*<around|(|V<rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)>+C*L<rsup|-2>*P<rsub|x>,<space|1em>C=4*C<rsub|1>.
  </equation>

  The second inequality follows by replacing the four factors of the
  plaquette one at a time, conjugating the intervening products. No smallness
  assumption on <math|U> was used. The hypothesis is that the frames at the
  radius-two coarse offsets have positive weight.

  <subsubsection|Periodic multiplicity and the large-defect witness>

  On a periodic lattice, let <math|m*<around|(|x,p|)>> count the lifts of the
  fine plaquette <math|p> wholly contained in the macro box centred at
  <math|L*x>. Then, with <math|r<rsub|p>=r<around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:geometry-port>P<rsub|x>=<big|sum><rsub|p>m*<around|(|x,p|)>*r<rsub|p>.
  </equation>

  This equality counts lifts, not merely distinct periodic plaquettes. If the
  fine period is greater than <math|2*<around|(|J+7|)>*L>, different anchors
  in one macro box cannot wrap to the same point, and
  <math|m*<around|(|x,p|)>\<leqslant\>1>. Furthermore, if
  <math|m*<around|(|x,p|)>\<gtr\>0>, the coarse parent of <math|p> lies at
  periodic sup distance at most <math|R=J+8> from <math|x>: the fine anchor
  is within <math|<around|(|J+7|)>*L> of <math|L*x>, its distance to its
  block centre is at most <math|L/2>, and distances between embedded coarse
  sites are <math|L> times their coarse distances. Consequently, under the
  same nonwrapping condition,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:geometry-port-column><big|sum><rsub|x>m*<around|(|x,p|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|2*R+1|)><rsup|4>.
  </equation>

  The periodic ball bound holds even when the ball wraps, since reducing an
  integer box modulo the period is surjective onto that ball.

  A seed collection consists of fine plaquettes <math|\<cal-F\>>, coarse
  predictor bonds <math|\<cal-I\>> and coarse flags <math|\<cal-G\>>. Its
  size is <math|n=<around|\||\<cal-F\>|\|>+<around|\||\<cal-I\>|\|>+<around|\||\<cal-G\>|\|>>.
  Associate to it the coarse parents of the fine plaquette corners, the
  endpoints of the predictor bonds and the flag sites. Their union has size
  at most <math|4*n>. A radius-<math|b> neighbourhood of this union therefore
  contains at most

  <\equation*>
    v<around|(|b|)>*n,<space|2em>v<around|(|b|)>=4*<around|(|2*b+1|)><rsup|4>*N<rsub|p>,
  </equation*>

  coarse plaquette addresses, where <math|N<rsub|p>> is the number of
  plaquette orientations. For <math|B\<geqslant\>1>,
  <math|v*<around|(|2*B|)>\<leqslant\>v<around|(|2|)>*B<rsup|4>>. This proves
  the outer-region bound used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-energy>.

  Let <math|t> be the fine threshold, <math|T> the very large threshold, and
  <math|W> the predictor cutoff parameter. Retain the seeds in
  <math|\<cal-F\>> with <math|r<rsub|p>\<gtr\>T> and those in
  <math|\<cal-I\>> with <math|r*<around|(|V<rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)>\<gtr\>T>,
  and discard the flags. Call this collection the very large seeds, of size
  <math|d<rsub|A>>.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Local sector conditions><label|def:geometry-sector-conditions> Let
    <math|\<cal-N\>> be the set of coarse sites at distance at most
    <math|5*B> from the associated coarse sites of the seed collection. A
    fine plaquette <em|meets> <math|\<cal-N\>> if the coarse parent of at
    least one of its four corners lies in <math|\<cal-N\>>; a coarse bond
    meets <math|\<cal-N\>> if at least one of its two endpoints lies in
    <math|\<cal-N\>>. Write <math|r<rsub|p>> for the chord of the fine
    plaquette <math|p> and <math|z<rsub|e>=r*<around|(|V<rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)>>
    for the predictor chord of the coarse bond <math|e>. The <em|local sector
    conditions> are:

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(S1)>for every fine plaquette <math|p> meeting <math|\<cal-N\>>:
      <math|r<rsub|p>\<geqslant\>t/4> if <math|p\<in\>\<cal-F\>>, and
      <math|r<rsub|p>\<leqslant\>t/2> if <math|p\<nin\>\<cal-F\>>;

      <item*|(S2)>for every coarse bond <math|e> meeting <math|\<cal-N\>>:
      <math|z<rsub|e>\<geqslant\>W*t> if <math|e\<in\>\<cal-I\>>, and
      <math|z<rsub|e>\<leqslant\>2*W*t> if <math|e\<nin\>\<cal-I\>>;

      <item*|(S3)>for every <math|i\<in\>\<cal-N\>>: <math|i\<in\>\<cal-G\>>
      if and only if <math|i> is a coarse flag of <math|V> at the coarse
      threshold <math|s>;

      <item*|(S4)>for every <math|i\<in\>\<cal-N\>>: the selected frame
      weight (the retained site likelihood) of the patch at <math|i> is
      strictly positive.
    </enumerate>
  </definition>

  These are the conditions defining the packet sector; they are part of the
  hypotheses of every estimate below and in
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-energy>
  and<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-hit-comparison> that refers to them. Every
  fine plaquette whose anchor has its parent in <math|\<cal-N\>> meets
  <math|\<cal-N\>>, and every coarse bond whose base point is in
  <math|\<cal-N\>> meets <math|\<cal-N\>>, so the conditions imply the weaker
  anchor-based statements; the arguments of this section only use those. The
  incidence form matters in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-energy>,
  where the data are transformed by signed coordinate maps: such a map sends
  the corners (endpoints) of an address to the corners (endpoints) of the
  image address and commutes with the parent map, so it preserves the class
  of addresses meeting <math|\<cal-N\>> once <math|\<cal-N\>> is replaced by
  its image, whereas it changes anchors.

  Suppose

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:geometry-witness-conditions>R\<leqslant\><around|\<lfloor\>|4*B/5|\<rfloor\>>,<space|1em>t/2\<leqslant\>T,<space|1em>2*W*t\<leqslant\>T,<space|1em><around*|(|4+C*L<rsup|-2>*<around|(|2*<around|(|J+7|)>*L+1|)><rsup|4>*N<rsub|p>|)>*T\<leqslant\>\<delta\>.
  </equation>

  Then every coarse plaquette of chord greater than <math|\<delta\>> whose
  anchor lies in the radius-<math|2*B> seed neighbourhood is within distance
  <math|R> of a very large seed. To prove this, suppose no such seed exists.
  Every fine plaquette counted by <math|P<rsub|x>> has parent within <math|R>
  of <math|x>. If listed, its chord is at most <math|T>, or it would be a
  witness; if unlisted, its chord is at most <math|t/2\<leqslant\>T>. The
  same reasoning applies to the four predictor discrepancies, using
  <math|2*W*t\<leqslant\>T>. All these addresses and the required radius-two
  frames lie in the radius-<math|5*B> neighbourhood, since <math|x> starts
  within <math|2*B> and the added radii are at most <math|3*B>. Thus
  <eqref|eq:geometry-predictor-curvature> applies and gives
  <math|r<around|(|V<rsub|p>|)>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>>, a contradiction.

  It follows that these large coarse plaquettes belong to the hit region,
  defined using the radius-<math|<around|\<lfloor\>|4*B/5|\<rfloor\>>>
  neighbourhood of the very large seeds, and their number is at most
  <math|v<around|(|R|)>*d<rsub|A>>. The whole hit region has at most
  <math|v<around|(|2|)>*B<rsup|4>*d<rsub|A>> plaquettes. A coarse flag is, by
  definition, a corner of a coarse plaquette with chord above the coarse
  threshold <math|s>; each such plaquette has at most four corners. Hence

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<cal-G\>|\|>\<leqslant\>4#<around|{|p<text| in the outer
    region>:r<around|(|V<rsub|p>|)>\<gtr\>s|}>.
  </equation*>

  Splitting these plaquettes at <math|\<delta\>> gives precisely the flag
  count and the <math|K<rsub|0>*d<rsub|A>> large-plaquette count used in the
  energy allocation.

  All constants in these geometric counts can be chosen before <math|L> and
  <math|W>: take, for example, <math|K<rsub|0>=v<around|(|R|)>+v<around|(|2|)>+R+<around|(|2*R+1|)><rsup|4>>.
  For each fixed <math|L,W>, the conditions
  <eqref|eq:geometry-witness-conditions> hold eventually along the coupling
  parameter. Indeed <math|B\<to\>\<infty\>>, <math|T=h<rsup|-49/100>\<to\>0>,
  and <math|t/T=P<around|(|h|)>*h<rsup|-1/100>\<to\>0>, by the threshold
  estimates already proved. The allowed coarse periods are at least
  <math|100*<around|(|1+B|)>>, which also ensures the nonwrapping condition
  for the macro box. Thus the geometric constants are independent of both the
  volume and the subsequent choice of block side. The guide bounds and the
  hit and unhit energy comparison estimates are separate analytic inputs;
  this argument establishes the geometric part of their allocation.

  <subsection|Kernel sums and the bounded guide
  fields><label|sec:seed-guides>

  The energy argument uses kernels after parallel transport, truncation and
  periodization. We derive their row and column bounds from the cover
  estimates, and then bound the clipped guide fields. The inputs are the
  tower strip bounds and the exponentially summable path expansions at the
  two adjacent stages. The passage to the physical kernels does not require a
  small-field assumption.

  <subsubsection|Counting types before periodization>

  Let <math|K<rsub|a*b><around|(|z|)>> be a scalar cover kernel, with finite
  type sets <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\>>, satisfying, for every fixed pair of
  types,

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>><around|\||K<rsub|a*b><around|(|z|)>|\|>*e<rsup|\<eta\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>\<leqslant\>C,<space|2em>\<eta\>\<geqslant\>0.
  </equation*>

  where <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>> is the <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>> norm of
  the cover displacement (the same norm is used in the strip hypotheses
  below; neighbourhoods are still measured in the periodic supremum
  distance). A physical entry from <math|<around|(|x,a|)>> to
  <math|<around|(|y,b|)>> is a sum of selected terms
  <math|K<rsub|a*b><around|(|z|)><math-up|Ad><around|(|U<rsub|\<gamma\><rsub|x,z>>|)>>
  with <math|y=x+<math-up|wrap><around|(|z|)>>. Adjoint transport is an
  isometry of the real colour space, including along winding paths, so the
  operator norm of each term is at most <math|<around|\||K<rsub|a*b><around|(|z|)>|\|>>.
  Absolute convergence therefore permits the following rearrangements:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:guide-periodic-rows>sup<rsub|x,a>
    <big|sum><rsub|y,b><around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|<around|(|x,a|)>,<around|(|y,b|)>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<cal-B\>|\|>*C,<space|2em>sup<rsub|y,b>
    <big|sum><rsub|x,a><around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|<around|(|x,a|)>,<around|(|y,b|)>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<cal-A\>|\|>*C.
  </equation>

  For the first, fix <math|z,b>: exactly one <math|y> can contribute. For the
  second, fix <math|z,a>: exactly one <math|x=y-<math-up|wrap><around|(|z|)>>
  can contribute. Dropping the truncation only increases the absolute sum.
  Thus different lifts of one periodic displacement are all paid.

  The wide kernel <math|\<Phi\>> has fine doubled plaquette rows and coarse
  doubled plaquette columns. In coarse coordinates a fine type includes its
  residue in the block. There are <math|L<rsup|4>> residues. Its fixed-type
  strip cost is <math|C/L<rsup|2>>, so <eqref|eq:guide-periodic-rows> gives,
  after absorbing only fixed orientation counts,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:guide-allowance><tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-lborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-rborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|3|3|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|3|3|cell-rborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<text|row
    sum>>|<cell|<text|column sum>>>|<row|<cell|\<Phi\>>|<cell|a/L<rsup|2>>|<cell|a*L<rsup|2>>>|<row|<cell|\<Phi\><rprime|'>>|<cell|a>|<cell|a>>|<row|<cell|N>|<cell|a>|<cell|a>>>>>
  </equation>

  with <math|a> fixed before <math|L>. Here <math|\<Phi\><rprime|'>> has
  predictor-bond rows and coarse doubled-plaquette columns; <math|N> has
  auxiliary coarse rows and the same columns. Their fixed-type strip costs
  are bounded independently of <math|L>. The tower estimates provide
  precisely these three strip inputs; no residue factor is present in the
  narrow two kernels. In particular the column bound for <math|\<Phi\>>
  cannot be replaced by its row bound. The bounds
  <eqref|eq:guide-periodic-rows> hold for arbitrary isometric transports.
  When these kernels are identified with those of the energy comparison in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-energy>, all transports come from one
  coarse field <math|V>: the narrow kernels <math|\<Phi\><rprime|'>> and
  <math|N> are transported in <math|V>, and the wide kernel <math|\<Phi\>> in
  the fine replica <math|V<rsup|\<uparrow\>>> of the same <math|V>, whose
  links are identities or links of <math|V> (as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-global-row-prices>), along the path
  from the residue <math|r> to <math|L*z> described below.

  All three kernels are cut at path length at most
  <math|R<rsub|0>=<around|\<lfloor\>|B/100000|\<rfloor\>>> in fine units. The
  endpoint of the wide path beginning at a fine site of residue <math|r> and
  ending at coarse displacement <math|z> is the embedded coarse point at
  parent plus <math|z>: this is the path from <math|r> to <math|L*z> on the
  cover, reduced modulo the fine period. The parent changes by at most one
  coarse step on every fine bond. For the narrow paths the ordinary endpoint
  bound applies. Thus every nonzero entry has anchor separation at most
  <math|B/50> in coarse units. This is an incidence assertion for these
  kernels, and does not bound all auxiliary reads of a larger operation.

  <subsubsection|Absolute path expansions and their transposes>

  At one stage write the path expansion of a plaquette field in the form

  <\equation*>
    \<ell\><rsub|p><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i\<geqslant\>0>c<rsub|a,i><space|0.17em><math-up|Ad><around|(|U<rsub|\<gamma\><rsub|p,i>>|)>*F<around|(|U<rsub|p+s<rsub|a,i>>|)>,<space|2em>p=<around|(|x,a|)>.
  </equation*>

  The label <math|i> includes the target plaquette orientation and the
  transport path. Its length <math|\<ell\><rsub|a,i>> is the number of bonds
  of the transport path <math|\<gamma\><rsub|p,i>> plus four, the length of
  the boundary of the target plaquette; in particular it bounds the
  <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>> distance from <math|x> to the target anchor. The
  truncation below uses this length, and the clipped field depends on this
  convention. The stage input is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:guide-path-input><big|sum><rsub|i><around|\||c<rsub|a,i>|\|>*e<rsup|\<sigma\>*\<ell\><rsub|a,i>>\<leqslant\>P,<space|2em>\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0,<space|1em>P\<gtr\>0,
  </equation>

  uniformly in <math|a>, with clipping relaxation radius
  <math|r<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> satisfying <math|\<sigma\>*r<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>1>.
  Both stages have such bounds, with constants fixed before the step side
  <math|L>. We retain the full label set when different labels land at the
  same periodic plaquette.

  Keep terms with <math|\<kappa\>*\<ell\><rsub|a,i>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|0>>,
  where the stage factor is <math|\<kappa\>=1> on the fine stage and
  <math|\<kappa\>=L> on the coarse stage. (It is unrelated to the guide
  constant <math|d> introduced at the end of this section.) Let
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|p><rsup|<math-up|cut>>> be their sum and let

  <\equation*>
    k<rsub|p*u>=<big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|i:\<kappa\>*\<ell\><rsub|a,i>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|0>>>|<row|<cell|p+s<rsub|a,i>=u>>>>>><around|\||c<rsub|a,i>|\|>.
  </equation*>

  Absolute convergence follows from <eqref|eq:guide-path-input>. The
  curvature bound <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|F<around|(|U<rsub|u>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>r<around|(|U<rsub|u>|)>>
  gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:guide-path-sums><around|\<\|\|\>|\<ell\><rsub|p><rsup|<math-up|cut>><around|(|U|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|u>k<rsub|p*u>*r<around|(|U<rsub|u>|)>,<space|1em><big|sum><rsub|u>k<rsub|p*u>\<leqslant\>P,<space|1em><big|sum><rsub|p>k<rsub|p*u>\<leqslant\>N<rsub|p>*P.
  </equation>

  For the column bound, fix an input orientation <math|a> and a label
  <math|i>. Translation by its offset is a permutation of the periodic sites,
  so at most one anchor can land at the specified <math|u>. Sum first over
  labels and then over the <math|N<rsub|p>> possible input orientations. This
  argument also explains why merging repeated targets before taking absolute
  values would not justify the bound.

  A retained site satisfies <math|r<around|(|U<rsub|u>|)>\<leqslant\>t*exp
  <around|{|d*<around|(|p,u|)>/r<rsub|0>|}>> for every target <math|u>. Since
  <math|d*<around|(|p,u|)>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|a,i>> and
  <math|1/r<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>>, <eqref|eq:guide-path-input>
  implies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:guide-kept><around|\<\|\|\>|\<ell\><rsub|p><rsup|<math-up|cut>><around|(|U|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>P*t.
  </equation>

  The direct component is <math|<sqrt|a<rsub|0>>*F<around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>>,
  where <math|a<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> is the stage quadratic coefficient. Its
  clipped version multiplies it by <math|min
  <around|{|1,t/r<around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>|}>>, with multiplier <math|1> at
  zero. Its norm is at most <math|<sqrt|a<rsub|0>>*t>. The second component
  is <math|\<ell\><rsub|p><rsup|<math-up|cut>>> at retained sites and zero at
  discarded sites. Thus the two-component clipped field <math|v> satisfies
  the pointwise bound

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:guide-clipped><around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|p,\<varepsilon\>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|0>*t,<space|2em>A<rsub|0>=<around|(|N<rsub|p>+1|)>*P+<sqrt|a<rsub|0>>.
  </equation>

  This same <math|A<rsub|0>> bounds both row and column sums in
  <eqref|eq:guide-path-sums>.

  The selected and discarded rows have the locality needed for the energy
  proof. For a cut path, the parent separation is at most its length plus
  one, hence at most <math|R<rsub|0>+1>. A witness to discarding at lattice
  distance <math|k> obeys <math|t*e<rsup|k/r<rsub|0>>\<less\>r<around|(|U<rsub|u>|)>\<leqslant\>2>.
  For <math|t=P<around|(|h|)>/<sqrt|h>\<geqslant\>h<rsup|-1/2>> this implies
  <math|k\<less\>r<rsub|0>*log <around|(|2*<sqrt|h>|)>>. With
  <math|h=x+c<rsub|L>>, <math|c<rsub|L>> fixed, this is at most
  <math|r<rsub|0>*log x> eventually. Since
  <math|B=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>, both
  <math|R<rsub|0>+1> and <math|k+1> are at most <math|B/50> eventually. The
  parent map increases distances by at most one when compared with fine
  lattice distance; therefore these bounds give the asserted coarse anchor
  locality too. All tails are uniform in the period.

  Finally let <math|s=t<around|(|x|)>> be the coarse threshold and
  <math|t=t*<around|(|x+c<rsub|L>|)>> the fine threshold. The shift
  <math|c<rsub|L>> is nonnegative: its group factor is a positive multiple of
  a sum of squares of adjoint matrix entries, and <math|log L\<geqslant\>0>.
  For large <math|x>, <math|s\<leqslant\>2*t>: the numerator
  <math|P<around|(|x|)>=exp <around|{|<around|(|log log x|)><rsup|2>|}>> is
  increasing once <math|log x\<geqslant\>1>, and
  <math|<sqrt|x+c<rsub|L>>\<leqslant\>2*<sqrt|x>> for
  <math|x\<geqslant\>c<rsub|L>>. Choose <math|A> larger than the two stage
  constants <math|A<rsub|0>>. Then <eqref|eq:guide-clipped>, applied on the
  coarse stage, and <eqref|eq:guide-allowance> imply

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:guide-maximum><around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>d*t,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|\<Phi\>*v|)><rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>d*L<rsup|-2>*t,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|\<Phi\><rprime|'>*v|)><rsub|b>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>d*t,<space|2em>d=2*A*<around|(|1+a|)>.
  </equation>

  Indeed a matrix with operator-norm row sum <math|r> sends a field bounded
  by <math|u> to a field bounded by <math|r*u>. These are exactly the guide
  bounds used for the fine and predictor energy rows in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-energy>, with constants fixed before
  <math|L,W>. The derivation uses the stated tower and path-expansion inputs;
  the construction of the path expansions remains a separate input to be
  proved below.

  <subsection|Paying for the affected comparison
  terms><label|sec:seed-hit-comparison>

  The hit region consists of coarse plaquettes near the very large seeds. Its
  size is controlled by Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-geometry>. We now
  bound the comparison terms on this region, including the negative coarse
  energy of large plaquettes. The decisive distinction is between a small
  contribution at every tested row and a summable excess. The former may have
  constants depending on <math|L,W>; the latter must retain constants chosen
  before <math|L>.

  Keep the notation <math|s,t> for coarse and fine thresholds, with
  <math|s\<leqslant\>2*t>. Let <math|a,d> be the kernel and guide constants
  in <eqref|eq:guide-allowance> and <eqref|eq:guide-maximum>. Let <math|D> be
  the predictor-curvature constant in <eqref|eq:geometry-predictor-curvature>,
  and let <math|N> bound the macro column count and the hit-region size.
  These constants are fixed before <math|L,W>. Write <math|g=<sqrt|p>>, where
  <math|p\<gtr\>0> is the predictor penalty, and

  <\equation*>
    u=<sqrt|a<rsub|f>>+d,<space|2em>v<rsub|0>=<sqrt|a<rsub|c>>+d.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|a<rsub|f>,a<rsub|c>> are the direct quadratic coefficients. All
  kernels and fields below are real; a star denotes the physical
  Hilbert-space adjoint, after transport and truncation.

  <subsubsection|A small part and a summable excess>

  For a matrix <math|F<rsub|i*j>> with operator-norm row bound <math|r> and
  column bound <math|c>, suppose <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|z<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>b+e<rsub|i>>
  on the inputs of the tested columns <math|j\<in\>S>, with
  <math|b,e<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>. Then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:hit-transpose><big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>S><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|F<rsup|\<ast\>>*z|)><rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\||S|\|>*c*b+r*<big|sum><rsub|i>e<rsub|i>.
  </equation>

  Indeed <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|F<rsup|\<ast\>>*z|)><rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i><around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|i*j>|\<\|\|\>><around|\<\|\|\>|z<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>>
  because an operator and its adjoint have the same norm. The constant part
  is bounded using the column sum. In the excess part exchange the two finite
  sums and enlarge <math|S> to all columns; the row sum then gives
  <math|r*<big|sum><rsub|i>e<rsub|i>>. Inputs outside the nonzero stencil
  need no estimate.

  For the unclipped two-component plaquette field <math|X>, define the excess

  <\equation*>
    E<rsup|\<sharp\>><rsub|p>=<sqrt|a<rsub|0>>*E<rsub|p>+<big|sum><rsub|q>k<rsub|p*q>*E<rsub|q>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|k> is the nonnegative path matrix of
  <eqref|eq:guide-path-sums>. If <math|r<around|(|U<rsub|q>|)>\<leqslant\>b+E<rsub|q>>
  on the local stencil, those bounds give, for either component
  <math|\<varepsilon\>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:hit-plotted><around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|p,\<varepsilon\>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|<sqrt|a<rsub|0>>+d|)>*b+E<rsup|\<sharp\>><rsub|p>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|p,\<varepsilon\>>E<rsup|\<sharp\>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|<sqrt|a<rsub|0>>+d|)>*<big|sum><rsub|p>E<rsub|p>.
  </equation>

  For the direct component use the curvature bound. For the path component
  use the row bound for the constant part. For the total excess use the
  column bound. The factor <math|2> is the actual number of components, even
  though a sharper componentwise estimate is possible.

  Freeze the following excess arrays:

  <\equation*>
    E<rsub|p>=\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|p\<in\>\<cal-F\>,r<around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>\<gtr\>t|}>>*r<around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>,<space|2em>F<rsub|e>=\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|e\<in\>\<cal-I\>|}>>*r*<around|(|V<rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-B\>=L<rsup|-2>*<big|sum><rsub|p>E<rsub|p>+<big|sum><rsub|e>F<rsub|e>.
  </equation*>

  The threshold in <math|E> is <math|t>, and <math|F> includes every listed
  predictor bond. The larger threshold <math|T> is used only to count the hit
  region. The sector conditions (S1) and (S2) of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:geometry-sector-conditions> give, for every
  address meeting the radius-<math|5*B> neighbourhood,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:hit-input-split>r<around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>\<leqslant\>t+E<rsub|p>,<space|2em>r*<around|(|V<rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)>\<leqslant\>2*W*t+F<rsub|e>.
  </equation>

  For a listed fine plaquette outside the excess set its chord is at most
  <math|t>; an unlisted one is at most <math|t/2>. Listed predictor bonds are
  included entirely in <math|F>, while unlisted ones are at most
  <math|2*W*t>.

  Let <math|M<rsub|L>=<around|(|2*<around|(|J+7|)>*L+1|)><rsup|4>*N<rsub|p>>
  bound the number of lifted plaquettes in a macro box. Put

  <\equation*>
    r<rsub|\<ast\>>=8*W+D*L<rsup|-2>*M<rsub|L>,<space|2em>H<rsub|p>=<big|sum><rsub|e\<in\>\<partial\>*p>F<rsub|e>+D*L<rsup|-2>*<big|sum><rsub|q>m*<around|(|x<rsub|p>,q|)>*E<rsub|q>,<space|2em>H<rsub|\<ast\>>=4*N<rsub|p>+D*N<rsub|p>*N.
  </equation*>

  Equations <eqref|eq:geometry-predictor-curvature> and
  <eqref|eq:hit-input-split> imply

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:hit-coarse-split>r<around|(|V<rsub|p>|)>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|\<ast\>>*t+H<rsub|p><space|1em><text|in
    the radius-<math|3*B> neighbourhood>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|p>H<rsub|p>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|\<ast\>>*\<cal-B\>.
  </equation>

  For the local assertion, the four boundary bonds contribute <math|8*W*t>
  and their excesses; the macro row contributes
  <math|D*L<rsup|-2>*M<rsub|L>*t> and its excess. All input anchors remain
  within radius <math|5*B> once <math|B\<geqslant\>N+1>, and the required
  nearby frames have positive weight. For the total assertion, each of the
  four boundary-bond maps, for a fixed plaquette orientation, is injective on
  the periodic lattice; thus its sum is at most
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|e>F<rsub|e>>. The macro column sum is at most <math|N>
  for each fine plaquette, and summing over coarse plaquette orientations
  contributes <math|N<rsub|p>>. Consequently

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|p>H<rsub|p>\<leqslant\>4*N<rsub|p>*<big|sum><rsub|e>F<rsub|e>+D*N<rsub|p>*N*L<rsup|-2>*<big|sum><rsub|q>E<rsub|q>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|\<ast\>>*\<cal-B\>.
  </equation*>

  In contrast to <math|r<rsub|\<ast\>>>, the coefficient
  <math|H<rsub|\<ast\>>> is independent of <math|L,W>.

  <subsubsection|The two comparison rows>

  Let <math|S> be the hit plaquettes, <math|m=<around|\||S|\|>>, and
  <math|<wide|S|~>=S\<times\><around|{|0,1|}>>, so
  <math|<around|\||<wide|S|~>|\|>=2*m>. Put
  <math|Z<rsub|e>=g*F*<around|(|V<rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)>> for the
  predictor curvature field. The three norm totals entering the linear
  comparison satisfy

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\><wide|S|~>><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>*X<rsub|f>|)><rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>2*m*<space|0.17em>a*L<rsup|2>*u*t+2*a*u*\<cal-B\>,<eq-number><label|eq:hit-fine>>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\><wide|S|~>><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|<around|(|\<Phi\><rprime|'>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*Z|)><rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>2*m*<space|0.17em>a*g*<around|(|2*W*t|)>+a*g*\<cal-B\>,<eq-number><label|eq:hit-noise>>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\><wide|S|~>><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|X<rsub|c>|)><rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>2*m*<space|0.17em>v<rsub|0>*r<rsub|\<ast\>>*t+2*v<rsub|0>*H<rsub|\<ast\>>*\<cal-B\>.<eq-number><label|eq:hit-coarse>>>>>
  </align>

  For <eqref|eq:hit-fine>, use <eqref|eq:hit-plotted> with <math|b=t> and
  then <eqref|eq:hit-transpose> with row <math|a/L<rsup|2>> and column
  <math|a*L<rsup|2>>. The total excess is at most <math|2*u*<big|sum>E>,
  giving <math|2*a*u*L<rsup|-2>*<big|sum>E\<leqslant\>2*a*u*\<cal-B\>>. The
  two successive stencils extend the tested radius-<math|2*B> region first to
  radius <math|3*B> and then to radius <math|5*B>, so the assumed local
  envelope is sufficient. For <eqref|eq:hit-noise>, use
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsub|e>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>g*<around|(|2*W*t+F<rsub|e>|)>>
  and the row and column bounds <math|a> for <math|\<Phi\><rprime|'>>. For
  <eqref|eq:hit-coarse>, apply <eqref|eq:hit-plotted> to
  <eqref|eq:hit-coarse-split> and sum its excess. Again the stencil lies
  within the region where that inequality is valid.

  The linear comparison row is

  <\equation*>
    L<rsub|p>=<big|sum><rsub|\<varepsilon\>=0><rsup|1><around|\<langle\>|v<rsub|p,\<varepsilon\>>,<around|(|\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>*X<rsub|f>+<around|(|\<Phi\><rprime|'>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*Z-X<rsub|c>|)><rsub|p,\<varepsilon\>>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation*>

  By <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>d*t> and
  Cauchy\USchwarz, the sum over <math|S> is bounded below by <math|-d*t>
  times the sum of the three right sides above. The quadratic defect row is

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|p>=<frac|1|2>*<big|sum><rsub|\<varepsilon\>=0><rsup|1><around*|\<langle\>|v<rsub|j>,<around*|(|<around|{|1-\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Phi\>-<around|(|\<Phi\><rprime|'>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Phi\><rprime|'>-N<rsup|\<ast\>>*N|}>*v|)><rsub|j>|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em>j=<around|(|p,\<varepsilon\>|)>.
  </equation*>

  There is no assertion here that the bracket is zero at a curved field. From
  <eqref|eq:guide-maximum> and the column bounds,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Phi\>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>a*d*t,<space|1em><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|\<Phi\><rprime|'>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Phi\><rprime|'>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>a*d*t,<space|1em><around|\<\|\|\>|N<rsup|\<ast\>>*N*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>a<rsup|2>*d*t.
  </equation*>

  In the first inequality the factor <math|L<rsup|2>> in the column cancels
  the <math|L<rsup|-2>> in the guide bound. Thus

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:hit-defect>D<rsub|p>\<geqslant\>-d<rsub|0>*t<rsup|2>,<space|2em>d<rsub|0>=d*<around|(|d+a*d+a*d+a<rsup|2>*d|)>.
  </equation>

  The factor <math|1/2> cancels the two component slots.

  Let <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|2>> be the coarse plaquettes in the outer region
  with chord greater than <math|\<delta\>>. By
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-geometry>,
  <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|2>\<subset\>S>. Therefore

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|p\<in\>\<cal-C\><rsub|2>>r<around|(|V<rsub|p>|)>\<leqslant\>m*r<rsub|\<ast\>>*t+H<rsub|\<ast\>>*\<cal-B\>.
  </equation*>

  Writing <math|\<lambda\><rsub|c>> for the coarse linear coefficient and
  using <math|s\<leqslant\>2*t>, combine this with
  <eqref|eq:hit-fine>\U<eqref|eq:hit-defect> to obtain

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:hit-final><big|sum><rsub|p\<in\>S><around|(|L<rsub|p>+D<rsub|p>|)>\<geqslant\>\<lambda\><rsub|c>*s*<big|sum><rsub|p\<in\>\<cal-C\><rsub|2>>r<around|(|V<rsub|p>|)>-C<rsub|1>*t*\<cal-B\>-Z<rsub|0>*t<rsup|2>*m,
  </equation>

  where the explicit nonnegative constants are

  <\equation*>
    <\eqsplit>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|C<rsub|1>>|<cell|=d*<around|(|2*a*u+a*g+2*v<rsub|0>*H<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>+2*\<lambda\><rsub|c>*H<rsub|\<ast\>>,>>|<row|<cell|Z<rsub|0>>|<cell|=2*d*<around|{|a*L<rsup|2>*u+2*a*g*W+v<rsub|0>*r<rsub|\<ast\>>|}>+d<rsub|0>+2*\<lambda\><rsub|c>*r<rsub|\<ast\>>.>>>>
    </eqsplit>
  </equation*>

  This is just addition of the bounds; in particular <math|C<rsub|1>> is
  chosen before <math|L,W>, while <math|Z<rsub|0>> may depend on them. The
  geometric estimate <math|m\<leqslant\>N*B<rsup|4>*d<rsub|A>> now changes
  the last term into <math|-Z*t<rsup|2>*B<rsup|4>*d<rsub|A>>, with
  <math|Z=N*Z<rsub|0>+1>. Since <math|\<cal-B\>=L<rsup|-2>*<big|sum><rsub|\<cal-S\>>r<around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|\<cal-I\>>r*<around|(|V<rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)>>,
  <eqref|eq:hit-final> is the hit-region input used in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-energy>. Its proof retains the two
  distinct thresholds and all transposed-kernel multiplicities. The
  unhit-region comparison still requires the local flat-field approximation.

  <subsection|A common small gauge away from very large
  seeds><label|sec:seed-tree-patch>

  The remaining energy comparison needs a gauge in which both the fine and
  coarse fields are small near an unhit plaquette. The fine field is given on
  its actual block-tree slice: its tree bonds equal <math|1>. We use that
  tree, including paths crossing a periodic seam.

  <subsubsection|Comparing one fine bond with the coarse replica>

  In each block the rooted forest gives a path from a fine site to its block
  centre, of length at most <math|4*L>. On the tree slice its transport is
  <math|1>, since every step is a tree bond or its inverse. For a fine bond
  <math|e=<around|(|x,a|)>>, concatenate the reverse path from <math|x> to
  its root, the bond itself, and the path from <math|x+e<rsub|a>> to its
  root. This gives a walk <math|w> between the two roots with

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||w|\|>\<leqslant\>8*L+1,<space|2em>U<rsub|w>=U<rsub|e>.
  </equation*>

  Lift this entire walk to the covering lattice starting at zero, with origin
  the first root. Its length fits in the macro radius
  <math|<around|(|J+7|)>*L> because <math|J\<geqslant\>4>. Its endpoint
  <math|z> lies in that macro box. For fine period at least
  <math|100*<around|(|J+7|)>*L>, wrapping is injective on the macro box.
  Hence <math|z=0> if the two roots coincide, and <math|z=L*e<rsub|a>> if the
  bond crosses to the next block. The latter is true even at a periodic seam:
  the periodic endpoints agree with the two stated cover endpoints, and
  injectivity identifies their lifts.

  Use the macro gauge <math|H> of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-geometry>
  at the first coarse root <math|i>, and write <math|P<rsub|i>> for its macro
  plaquette sum. If its bond sum is at most <math|D<rsub|0>*L*P<rsub|i>>, the
  chord of the transport of <math|w> is at most its length times that bond
  sum. This deliberately allows repeated steps and gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:tree-fine-link>r*<around|(|H<rsub|0>*U<rsub|e>*H<rsub|z><rsup|-1>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|8*L+1|)>*D<rsub|0>*L*P<rsub|i>.
  </equation>

  The normalized local frames take value <math|1> at their own centres. Thus
  the centre term of <eqref|eq:geometry-macro-frame> gives

  <\equation*>
    r*<around|(|H<rsub|L*y>*c<rsub|y>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|0>*L<rsup|2>*P<rsub|i>*<space|1em><around|(|y=0,e<rsub|a>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Together with the gauged predictor bond bound this yields

  <\equation*>
    r*<around|(|H<rsub|0>*Q<rsub|i,a>*H<rsub|L*e<rsub|a>><rsup|-1>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|2*C<rsub|0>*L<rsup|2>+C<rsub|1>*L<rsup|-2>|)>*P<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed insert <math|c<rsub|0>*c<rsub|0><rsup|-1>> and
  <math|c<rsub|e<rsub|a>>*c<rsub|e<rsub|a>><rsup|-1>> and apply the triangle
  inequality to the two centre errors and the predictor term.

  The coarse replica <math|<wide|V|\<bar\>>> is <math|1> on internal fine
  bonds and <math|V<rsub|i,a>> on crossing bonds. If <math|e> is internal,
  <eqref|eq:tree-fine-link> has <math|z=0> and conjugation removes
  <math|H<rsub|0>>. If it crosses, compare the last two displayed bounds in
  the same macro gauge and then compare <math|Q<rsub|i,a>> with
  <math|V<rsub|i,a>>. Thus, with

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|L>=<around|(|8*L+1|)>*D<rsub|0>*L+2*C<rsub|0>*L<rsup|2>+C<rsub|1>*L<rsup|-2>,
  </equation*>

  we obtain the shell comparison

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:tree-shell>r*<around|(|U<rsub|e>*<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)>\<leqslant\>D<rsub|L>*P<rsub|i>+<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|0,>|<cell|e<text|
    internal to a block>,>>|<row|<cell|r*<around|(|V<rsub|i,a>*Q<rsub|i,a><rsup|-1>|)>,>|<cell|e<text|
    crossing from >i.>>>>>
  </equation>

  The constant is allowed to depend on the fixed block side. The only frame
  hypothesis is the same positive-weight condition in the macro comparison.
  No replacement of the prescribed forest was made.

  <subsubsection|Constructing the gauge in an unhit neighbourhood>

  Fix an outer plaquette with anchor <math|o> which is not in the hit region.
  Let <math|T> be the very large threshold. Throughout distance
  <math|<around|\<lfloor\>|4*B/5|\<rfloor\>>> from <math|o>, all fine
  plaquette chords and all predictor discrepancies are at most <math|T>. For
  listed defects this follows from being unhit: any value above <math|T>
  would place a very large seed in that neighbourhood. For unlisted defects
  it follows from the sector bounds <math|t/2\<leqslant\>T> and
  <math|2*W*t\<leqslant\>T>.

  Write <math|M=<around|(|J+7|)>*L> and <math|K<rsub|L>=<around|(|2*M+1|)><rsup|4>*N<rsub|p>>.
  For <math|B> sufficiently large after fixing <math|L,W>, every macro box
  whose coarse centre lies within <math|r=<around|\<lfloor\>|B/2|\<rfloor\>>>
  of <math|o> is contained, in coarse parent distance, in the above
  neighbourhood. Its macro sum is at most <math|K<rsub|L>*T>, with lifted
  multiplicities included. The necessary frames are active there as well. By
  <eqref|eq:geometry-predictor-curvature>, every coarse plaquette with anchor
  within <math|r> has chord at most

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|L>*T,<space|2em>F<rsub|L>=4+C*L<rsup|-2>*K<rsub|L>.
  </equation*>

  The allowed coarse period is at least <math|100*<around|(|1+B|)>>, so the
  cover box of radius <math|r> is nonwrapping. Apply the axial gauge
  construction <eqref|eq:geometry-axial> on this box. Its plaquette sum is at
  most <math|<around|(|2*r+1|)><rsup|4>*N<rsub|p>*F<rsub|L>*T>, so the total
  gauged bond sum is at most <math|16*<around|(|2*r+1|)><rsup|5>*N<rsub|p>*F<rsub|L>*T>.
  Descend the cover gauge by the centred lift
  <math|y\<mapsto\><math-up|lift><around|(|y-o|)>>. For a bond whose anchor
  is within <math|<around|\<lfloor\>|2*B/5|\<rfloor\>>> of <math|o>, both
  endpoints lie in the box; the descended gauge therefore agrees with the
  cover gauge at both endpoints. Each such coarse bond has chord at most

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:tree-coarse-small>H<rsub|L>*B<rsup|5>*T,<space|2em>H<rsub|L>=16*<space|0.17em>3<rsup|5>*N<rsub|p>*F<rsub|L>.
  </equation>

  This follows by bounding one nonnegative summand by the total and using
  <math|2*r+1\<leqslant\>3*B>.

  Call the resulting coarse gauge <math|z> and use
  <math|z\<circ\><math-up|parent>> on the fine lattice. Under this
  block-constant gauge, the replica of <math|V> becomes the replica of
  <math|z\<cdot\>V>: odd <math|L> ensures the prescribed parent changes
  across a positive fine bond either not at all or by the corresponding
  positive coarse step. Thus its bond chord is either zero or bounded by
  <eqref|eq:tree-coarse-small>. On the other hand, the chord of
  <math|U<rsub|e>*<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|e><rsup|-1>> is unchanged by the
  simultaneous gauge, since that product is conjugated at the initial
  endpoint. Equation <eqref|eq:tree-shell> consequently gives

  <\equation*>
    r*<around|(|<around|(|z\<circ\><math-up|parent>|)>\<cdot\>U|)><rsub|e>\<leqslant\><around|(|D<rsub|L>*K<rsub|L>+1|)>*T+H<rsub|L>*B<rsup|5>*T.
  </equation*>

  With <math|C<rsub|L>=H<rsub|L>+D<rsub|L>*K<rsub|L>+1> and
  <math|B\<geqslant\>1>, both fine and coarse fields therefore satisfy the
  common bound

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:tree-common-gauge>r*<around|(|<around|(|z\<circ\><math-up|parent>|)>\<cdot\>U|)><rsub|e>\<leqslant\>h<around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>r*<around|(|z\<cdot\>V|)><rsub|b>\<leqslant\>h<around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>h<around|(|x|)>=C<rsub|L>*B<rsup|10>*T,
  </equation>

  on the radius-<math|<around|\<lfloor\>|2*B/5|\<rfloor\>>> neighbourhood of
  <math|o>. The fine bound uses parent distance for the anchor. All choices,
  including the gauge, refer to the same original fields, tree and tags. The
  threshold for <math|x> may depend on <math|L,W> and the fixed construction
  parameters, but not on the torus volume or on the chosen unhit plaquette.
  This proves the common small-gauge input needed for the local comparison.

  <subsection|The minimizing branches and the predictor
  derivative><label|sec:predictor-branch>

  We establish the branch properties used in the local comparison. The
  cutoffs for uniqueness can be chosen using only the group, before choosing
  a patch. Analyticity near the identity is then a local finite-dimensional
  statement, and its derivative includes the root normalization explicitly.

  <subsubsection|The nearest-point projection onto the group>

  The gauge group <math|G> is a closed subgroup of <math|U<around|(|N|)>>. We
  regard <math|\<cal-M\>=M<rsub|N><around|(|\<bbb-C\>|)>> as a real vector
  space with the Frobenius inner product <math|<around|\<langle\>|A,B|\<rangle\>>=<math-up|Retr><around|(|A<rsup|\<ast\>>*B|)>>
  and norm <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>>>. Left and right
  multiplication by a unitary matrix are isometries of <math|\<cal-M\>>. Let
  <math|\<frak-g\>=<around|{|X\<in\>\<cal-M\>:exp
  <around|(|s*X|)>\<in\>G<text| for all >s\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>> be the Lie
  algebra of <math|G>, a real subspace of the skew-Hermitian matrices, let
  <math|\<frak-g\><rsup|\<perp\>>> be its orthogonal complement in
  <math|\<cal-M\>>, and let <math|\<Pi\>> be the orthogonal projection of
  <math|\<cal-M\>> onto <math|\<frak-g\>>. A point <math|u\<in\>G> is
  <em|nearest> to <math|Z\<in\>\<cal-M\>> if
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|Z-u|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|Z-v|\<\|\|\>>>
  for all <math|v\<in\>G>. Since <math|G> is compact, every <math|Z> has at
  least one nearest point. The group may be disconnected, and it may be
  finite, in which case <math|\<frak-g\>=0>.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lem:branch-nearest-projection>There are <math|\<rho\>\<gtr\>0> and
    a real-analytic map <math|\<pi\>:B*<around|(|1,2*\<rho\>|)>\<to\>G>,
    defined on the open Frobenius ball of radius <math|2*\<rho\>> about
    <math|1> in <math|\<cal-M\>>, with the following properties.

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(a)><math|\<pi\><around|(|u|)>=u> for
      <math|u\<in\>G\<cap\>B*<around|(|1,2*\<rho\>|)>>, and
      <math|D*\<pi\><around|(|1|)>=\<Pi\>>.

      <item*|(b)>For every <math|Z\<in\>B*<around|(|1,\<rho\>|)>>,
      <math|\<pi\><around|(|Z|)>> is the only point of <math|G> nearest to
      <math|Z>.

      <item*|(c)>If <math|Z\<in\>\<cal-M\>> and
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|Z-v|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>\<rho\>> for some
      <math|v\<in\>G>, then <math|Z> has exactly one nearest point in
      <math|G>, namely <math|v*\<pi\>*<around|(|v<rsup|-1>*Z|)>>.

      <item*|(d)>The first and second derivatives of <math|\<pi\>> are
      bounded on the closed ball <math|<wide|B|\<bar\>>*<around|(|1,\<rho\>|)>>.
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Since <math|U<around|(|N|)>> is closed in
    <math|G*L*<around|(|N,\<bbb-C\>|)>>, <math|G> is a matrix Lie group in
    the sense of <cite-detail|hall2000|Definition<nbsp>2.2>. Its Lie algebra
    uses the one-parameter subgroup criterion of
    <cite-detail|hall2000|Definition<nbsp>3.13>. By
    <cite-detail|hall2000|Theorem<nbsp>3.23>, the exponential maps a
    neighborhood of zero in <math|\<frak-g\>> homeomorphically onto a
    neighborhood of <math|1> in <math|G>. The ambient logarithm is its
    inverse by <cite-detail|hall2000|Theorem<nbsp>3.6>. Thus, on a
    sufficiently small ambient ball of radius
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>\<less\>log 2>, <math|exp X\<in\>G> if and
    only if <math|X\<in\>\<frak-g\>>. Indeed, if <math|exp X\<in\>G>, it is
    the exponential of a small algebra element, which the ambient logarithm
    identifies with <math|X>. The ambient maps are analytic by their power
    series. Every <math|u\<in\>G> with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u-1|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>\<theta\>>,
    for sufficiently small <math|\<theta\>\<gtr\>0>, therefore has a unique
    logarithm in this ball, tending to zero as <math|u\<to\>1>.

    <em|The normal chart.> Define <math|\<Phi\>:\<frak-g\>\<times\>\<frak-g\><rsup|\<perp\>>\<to\>\<cal-M\>>
    by <math|\<Phi\>*<around|(|X,N|)>=exp <around|(|X|)>*<around|(|1+N|)>>.
    It is real-analytic, <math|\<Phi\>*<around|(|0,0|)>=1>, and
    <math|D*\<Phi\>*<around|(|0,0|)><around|(|X,N|)>=X+N>. Since
    <math|\<cal-M\>=\<frak-g\>\<oplus\>\<frak-g\><rsup|\<perp\>>>, this
    derivative is a linear isomorphism. Complexify the finite-dimensional
    real spaces. The convergent power series of <math|\<Phi\>> gives a
    holomorphic extension whose derivative at zero is the complexification of
    <math|<around|(|X,N|)>\<mapsto\>X+N>, hence an isomorphism. After
    translating the base point and composing with the inverse of the
    derivative, the complexified map has the form
    <math|z\<mapsto\>z+h<around|(|z|)>> with <math|h<around|(|0|)>=0> and
    <math|D*h<around|(|0|)>=0>. On a sufficiently small closed complex ball
    of radius <math|r<rsub|0>>, we have <math|sup
    <around|\<\|\|\>|D*h|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1/2>. For
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>r<rsub|0>/2>, the iteration
    <math|z<rsub|n+1>=w-h<around|(|z<rsub|n>|)>>, starting at zero, stays in
    the ball and converges uniformly there as a function of <math|w> by the
    contraction estimate. Its limit is holomorphic in <math|w> and is the
    unique preimage in the ball. This gives the local holomorphic inverse.
    Conjugation invariance of <math|\<Phi\>> and uniqueness of that inverse
    imply that it maps real points to real points. Its restriction is
    therefore real-analytic. Thus there is
    <math|r\<in\><around|(|0,\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>|)>> such that, with
    <math|O<rsub|r>=<around|{|<around|(|X,N|)>:<around|\<\|\|\>|X|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>r,<around|\<\|\|\>|N|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>r|}>>,
    the set <math|\<Phi\><around|(|O<rsub|r>|)>> is an open neighbourhood of
    <math|1> and <math|\<Phi\>:O<rsub|r>\<to\>\<Phi\><around|(|O<rsub|r>|)>>
    is a real-analytic diffeomorphism. Choose <math|\<rho\>\<gtr\>0> so small
    that <math|<wide|B|\<bar\>>*<around|(|1,3*\<rho\>|)>\<subset\>\<Phi\><around|(|O<rsub|r>|)>>,
    <math|2*\<rho\>\<less\>\<theta\>>, <math|\<rho\>\<leqslant\>r>, and every
    <math|u\<in\>G> with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u-1|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>2*\<rho\>>
    is <math|exp X> with <math|X\<in\>\<frak-g\>>,
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|X|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>r>. For
    <math|Z\<in\>B*<around|(|1,2*\<rho\>|)>> write
    <math|\<Phi\><rsup|-1><around|(|Z|)>=<around|(|X<around|(|Z|)>,N<around|(|Z|)>|)>>
    and put <math|\<pi\><around|(|Z|)>=exp X<around|(|Z|)>>. Then
    <math|\<pi\>> is real-analytic and takes values in <math|G>, because
    <math|X<around|(|Z|)>\<in\>\<frak-g\>>.

    <em|Property (a).> If <math|u\<in\>G\<cap\>B*<around|(|1,2*\<rho\>|)>>,
    then <math|u=exp X> with <math|X\<in\>\<frak-g\>>,
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|X|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>r>, so
    <math|u=\<Phi\>*<around|(|X,0|)>> with
    <math|<around|(|X,0|)>\<in\>O<rsub|r>>. Injectivity of <math|\<Phi\>> on
    <math|O<rsub|r>> gives <math|X<around|(|u|)>=X> and
    <math|\<pi\><around|(|u|)>=u>. Differentiating
    <math|\<pi\>*<around|(|\<Phi\>*<around|(|X,N|)>|)>=exp X> at
    <math|<around|(|0,0|)>> gives <math|D*\<pi\><around|(|1|)>*<around|(|X+N|)>=X>,
    that is <math|D*\<pi\><around|(|1|)>=\<Pi\>>.

    <em|Property (b).> Let <math|Z\<in\>B*<around|(|1,\<rho\>|)>> and let
    <math|u> be a nearest point. Since <math|1\<in\>G>,
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|Z-u|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|Z-1|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>\<rho\>>
    and therefore <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u-1|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>2*\<rho\>>, so
    <math|u=exp X> with <math|X\<in\>\<frak-g\>>,
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|X|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>r>. For
    <math|Y\<in\>\<frak-g\>>, <math|s\<mapsto\><around|\<\|\|\>|Z-u*exp
    <around|(|s*Y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>> is differentiable and minimal at
    <math|s=0>, because <math|u*exp <around|(|s*Y|)>\<in\>G>. Its derivative
    there is <math|-2*<around|\<langle\>|Z-u,u*Y|\<rangle\>>=-2*<around|\<langle\>|u<rsup|\<ast\>>*Z-1,Y|\<rangle\>>>,
    using that <math|u> is unitary. Hence
    <math|N\<assign\>u<rsup|\<ast\>>*Z-1\<in\>\<frak-g\><rsup|\<perp\>>>, and
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|N|\<\|\|\>>=<around|\<\|\|\>|Z-u|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>\<rho\>\<leqslant\>r>.
    Thus <math|Z=\<Phi\>*<around|(|X,N|)>> with
    <math|<around|(|X,N|)>\<in\>O<rsub|r>>, so
    <math|<around|(|X,N|)>=\<Phi\><rsup|-1><around|(|Z|)>> and
    <math|u=\<pi\><around|(|Z|)>>. As a nearest point exists,
    <math|\<pi\><around|(|Z|)>> is the unique one.

    <em|Property (c).> Left multiplication by <math|v<rsup|-1>\<in\>G> is an
    isometry of <math|\<cal-M\>> mapping <math|G> onto <math|G>. Hence
    <math|u> is nearest to <math|Z> if and only if <math|v<rsup|-1>*u> is
    nearest to <math|v<rsup|-1>*Z>, and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsup|-1>*Z-1|\<\|\|\>>=<around|\<\|\|\>|Z-v|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>\<rho\>>.
    By (b), <math|v<rsup|-1>*u=\<pi\>*<around|(|v<rsup|-1>*Z|)>>.

    <em|Property (d).> The derivatives of <math|\<pi\>> are continuous on the
    compact set <math|<wide|B|\<bar\>>*<around|(|1,\<rho\>|)>\<subset\>B*<around|(|1,2*\<rho\>|)>>.
  </proof>

  In the rest of this chapter and in Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-geometry>
  and<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-energy>, <math|\<pi\>> denotes this map. Its
  construction uses only <math|G>; no other part of the data enters.

  <subsubsection|Uniform uniqueness of minimizing frames>

  Use the finite path family <math|W<rsub|i*j*\<alpha\>>> and minimizing
  energy from Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-geometry>. Let
  <math|\<pi\>> and <math|\<rho\>> be as in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:branch-nearest-projection>. By continuity of
  <math|D*\<pi\>> at <math|1> and <math|D*\<pi\><around|(|1|)>=\<Pi\>>, there
  is a convex ball about <math|1>, contained in
  <math|B*<around|(|1,\<rho\>|)>>, on which
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D*\<pi\>-\<Pi\>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|4>>;
  for all <math|A,B> in that ball,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:branch-projection><around|\<\|\|\>|\<pi\><around|(|A|)>-\<pi\><around|(|B|)>-\<Pi\>*<around|(|A-B|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|4><around|\<\|\|\>|A-B|\<\|\|\>>.
  </equation>

  This follows by integrating <math|D*\<pi\>-\<Pi\>> along the segment.
  Choose <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0> for such a ball, and choose
  <math|0\<less\>\<varepsilon\>\<leqslant\>min <around|{|\<delta\>/4,1/4|}>>.
  Suppose

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|W<rsub|i*j*\<alpha\>>-1|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><space|1em><text|for
    every >i,j,\<alpha\>.
  </equation*>

  We claim that any two minimizing frames differ by a common left
  multiplication, independently of the number of points or paths.

  Comparison with the constant frame and the squared triangle inequality give

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|av><rsub|i,j><around|\<\|\|\>|h<rsub|i>-h<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>4*\<varepsilon\><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  For clarity, the mean squared transported discrepancy
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|h<rsub|i>*W<rsub|i*j*\<alpha\>>-h<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>
  is at most <math|\<varepsilon\><rsup|2>> by minimality. Each untransported
  discrepancy squared is at most twice this term plus
  <math|2*\<varepsilon\><rsup|2>>; averaging gives the claim. Rotate each
  minimizing frame separately so that <math|1> is nearest to its centroid
  <math|m>. Choosing a point with mean squared distance no larger than the
  double average and applying Jensen gives
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|m-1|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*\<varepsilon\>>. Its
  feedback matrix <math|m<rsub|i>=<math-up|av><rsub|j,\<alpha\>><space|0.17em>h<rsub|j>*W<rsub|i*j*\<alpha\>><rsup|-1>>
  satisfies <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|m<rsub|i>-m|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>>,
  so <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|m<rsub|i>-1|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>3*\<varepsilon\>\<less\>\<delta\>>.
  By <eqref|eq:geometry-feedback>, <math|h<rsub|i>> is a nearest group
  element to <math|m<rsub|i>>, and <math|m<rsub|i>> lies in the
  <math|\<delta\>>-ball, which is contained in
  <math|B*<around|(|1,\<rho\>|)>>. Hence Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:branch-nearest-projection>(b)
  identifies <math|h<rsub|i>=\<pi\><around|(|m<rsub|i>|)>>.

  Let <math|h,k> be the two rotated minimizers and set
  <math|v=max<rsub|i><around|\<\|\|\>|h<rsub|i>-k<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>>. Their
  feedback differences <math|D<rsub|i>> satisfy

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>v,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsub|i>-<around|(|m<rsub|h>-m<rsub|k>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>v*\<varepsilon\>.
  </equation*>

  The first follows by averaging and unitary invariance. For the second,
  subtract the centroid difference to obtain the average of
  <math|<around|(|h<rsub|j>-k<rsub|j>|)>*<around|(|W<rsub|i*j*\<alpha\>><rsup|-1>-1|)>>
  and use the matrix product bound. Since <math|1> is nearest to each
  centroid, <math|\<Pi\>*<around|(|m<rsub|h>-1|)>=\<Pi\>*<around|(|m<rsub|k>-1|)>=0>:
  differentiate squared distance along <math|exp <around|(|t*X|)>> for each
  tangent vector <math|X>. Thus <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Pi\>*D<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>v*\<varepsilon\>>.
  At an index attaining the maximum, <eqref|eq:branch-projection> yields

  <\equation*>
    v\<leqslant\>v*\<varepsilon\>+<tfrac|1|4>v\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>v,
  </equation*>

  so <math|v=0>. Undoing the rotations proves uniqueness up to a common left
  multiplier. If both frames are normalized at their centre, that multiplier
  is <math|1>.

  The actual frame branch tests that, for every rooted axial gauge centred at
  the patch centre (every coordinate order of the axial tree), every
  coordinate path transport has chord less than a fixed cutoff. Take this
  cutoff at most <math|\<varepsilon\>/D>. Choose one of those axial gauges;
  the resulting <math|W> satisfies the preceding matrix bound. Changing
  variables by that gauge in the minimizing energy proves uniqueness of the
  normalized frame on the entire tested branch. These cutoffs do not depend
  on the patch size or smoothing order. At the identity all tested chords
  vanish, so this branch is an open neighbourhood of the identity field.

  For the predictor branch, let <math|w<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>,
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>w<rsub|i>=1>, and let
  <math|m=<big|sum><rsub|i>w<rsub|i>*Y<rsub|i>>. Expanding the squares shows
  that minimizing <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>w<rsub|i>*r*<around|(|Y<rsub|i>*u<rsup|-1>|)><rsup|2>/2>
  is equivalent to choosing a nearest group element to <math|m>. If the
  weighted cost at some <math|u> is less than <math|\<zeta\>>, Jensen gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|m-u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i>w<rsub|i>*<around|\<\|\|\>|Y<rsub|i>-u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<less\>2*D<rsup|2>*\<zeta\>.
  </equation*>

  Choose <math|\<zeta\>> so that <math|<sqrt|2*D<rsup|2>*\<zeta\>>\<less\>\<rho\>>.
  Then <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|m-u|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>\<rho\>> with
  <math|u\<in\>G>, and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:branch-nearest-projection>(c),
  the uniform nearest-point tube obtained by left translation, shows that
  <math|m> has exactly one nearest group element. Then the predictor minimum
  is unique on its tested branch, uniformly over all finite weight
  distributions. Finally the fallback frame rule has an eligible choice by
  <eqref|eq:geometry-axial> with axial factor <math|16>. Together these facts
  construct the required branch constants for any fixed positive predictor
  penalty.

  <subsubsection|Analyticity of the normalized frame>

  Fix a patch and a finite torus for the moment. Write <math|a> for the fine
  logarithmic bond field and <math|h<rsub|i><around|(|a|)>> for the actual
  frame matrices. At <math|a=0> they are all <math|1>. The chosen minimizer
  is continuous on the uniqueness branch. To verify this without assuming
  regularity of a choice function, consider a convergent sequence of input
  fields. Compactness of the finite product of groups gives a convergent
  subsequence of any sequence of minimizers. Continuity of the energy and the
  centre constraint makes every limit a minimizer at the limiting field.
  Uniqueness makes that limit the selected one. A sequence staying outside a
  fixed neighbourhood of that value would contradict this argument, which
  proves continuity.

  Let <math|o> be the patch centre and let
  <math|W<rsub|i*j*\<alpha\>><around|(|a|)>> be its actual coordinate path
  transports. Define an ambient matrix-valued system

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-F\><rsub|i>*<around|(|a,H|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|H<rsub|o>-1,>|<cell|i=o,>>|<row|<cell|H<rsub|i>-\<pi\>*<around*|(|<math-up|av><rsub|j,\<alpha\>><space|0.17em>H<rsub|j>*W<rsub|i*j*\<alpha\>><around|(|a|)><rsup|-1>|)>,>|<cell|i\<ne\>o.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  It is analytic near <math|<around|(|0,\<b-1\>|)>>. Continuity of the actual
  frame and its feedback, together with self-consistency, shows that
  <math|\<cal-F\>*<around|(|a,h<around|(|a|)>|)>=0> near zero. The derivative
  in the ambient frame variable is

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|\<cal-D\>*v|)><rsub|o>=v<rsub|o>,<space|2em><around|(|\<cal-D\>*v|)><rsub|i>=v<rsub|i>-\<Pi\>*<wide|v|\<bar\>>*<space|1em><around|(|i\<ne\>o|)>,<space|2em><wide|v|\<bar\>>=<math-up|av><rsub|j><space|0.17em>v<rsub|j>.
  </equation*>

  It is invertible. Indeed if <math|\<cal-D\>*v=0>, put
  <math|A=\<Pi\>*<wide|v|\<bar\>>>. Then <math|v<rsub|o>=0> and
  <math|v<rsub|i>=A> for <math|i\<ne\>o>. If the patch has <math|n> points,
  <math|<wide|v|\<bar\>>=<around|(|n-1|)>*A/n>, so applying <math|\<Pi\>>
  gives <math|A=<around|(|n-1|)>*A/n> and <math|A=0>. The derivative is an
  injective endomorphism of a finite -dimensional space and is therefore
  bijective. The analytic implicit function theorem gives a unique analytic
  local solution. Here this theorem follows from the same inverse theorem:
  for an equation <math|F*<around|(|x,y|)>=0> with invertible
  <math|D<rsub|y>*F>, apply it to <math|<around|(|x,y|)>\<mapsto\><around|(|x,F*<around|(|x,y|)>|)>>
  and restrict the inverse to <math|<around|(|x,0|)>>. Continuity places the
  actual frame in that local solution neighbourhood, so
  <math|h<around|(|a|)>> is analytic. This argument works in ambient
  matrices; it does not require choosing coordinates for a complement at
  every frame.

  <subsubsection|The root correction in the derivative>

  For a Lie-algebra-valued bond perturbation <math|a>, write
  <math|<big|int><rsub|\<gamma\>>a> for its signed sum along a path. Define

  <\equation*>
    \<tau\><rsub|y>*<around|(|i;a|)>=-<math-up|av><rsub|j,\<alpha\>><big|int><rsub|i\<to\>j,\<alpha\>>a,
  </equation*>

  where the paths are in the patch centred at the coarse site <math|y>.
  Throughout, a coarse site <math|y> is embedded in the fine lattice as
  <math|L*y>, and the patch at <math|y> consists of the fine points
  <math|L*y+i>, with <math|i> ranging over the patch offsets in fine units
  and periodic reduction understood; thus
  <math|<big|int><rsub|i\<to\>j,\<alpha\>>a> is the sum of <math|a> along the
  path from <math|L*y+i> to <math|L*y+j> of order <math|\<alpha\>>.
  Differentiate the feedback equation at zero. If
  <math|<wide|h|\<dot\>><rsub|i>> is the matrix derivative and
  <math|<wide|<wide|h|\<dot\>>|\<bar\>>> its uniform average, differentiation
  of the inverse transport gives

  <\equation*>
    <wide|h|\<dot\>><rsub|i>=\<Pi\>*<wide|<wide|h|\<dot\>>|\<bar\>>+<math-up|col><around|(|\<tau\><rsub|y>*<around|(|i;a|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The feedback identity holds also at the centre: the cost is invariant under
  a common left multiplication, so the centre-normalized minimizer is an
  unconstrained minimizer and <eqref|eq:geometry-feedback> applies at
  <math|o> as well (see Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-geometry>). Since
  the normalized frame has <math|h<rsub|o><around|(|a|)>=1>, its centre
  derivative is zero. Subtraction therefore yields

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:branch-frame-derivative><wide|h|\<dot\>><rsub|i>=<math-up|col><around|{|\<tau\><rsub|y>*<around|(|i;a|)>-\<tau\><rsub|y>*<around|(|o;a|)>|}>.
  </equation>

  In particular the frame derivative uses uniform averages over patch points
  and path orders, before the separate connector weights enter.

  For a coarse bond <math|e=<around|(|y,b|)>>, the connector at patch point
  <math|i> consists of its frame at <math|y>, the length-<math|L> fine path,
  and the inverse frame at <math|y+e<rsub|b>>. Its colour derivative is
  consequently

  <\equation*>
    \<tau\><rsub|y>*<around|(|i;a|)>-\<tau\><rsub|y>*<around|(|o;a|)>+<big|int><rsub|L*y+i\<to\>L*<around|(|y+e<rsub|b>|)>+i>a-\<tau\><rsub|y+e<rsub|b>>*<around|(|i;a|)>+\<tau\><rsub|y+e<rsub|b>>*<around|(|o;a|)>.
  </equation*>

  At zero all connectors equal <math|1>. Nearby the predictor is on its
  minimizing branch and equals <math|\<pi\>> of their weighted matrix
  centroid. This follows from the weighted nearest-point identity above; the
  branch remains open since its minimum cost is at most the continuous cost
  of comparison with <math|1>, which vanishes at zero. Thus the predictor is
  analytic near zero. The derivative of <math|\<pi\>> is <math|\<Pi\>>, which
  fixes the above colour derivatives, so the predictor derivative is their
  weighted sum.

  Define the bond averaging map and the site correction by

  <\equation*>
    <\eqsplit>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|\<cal-A\>*a|)><rsub|y,b>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|i>w<rsub|i>*<big|int><rsub|L*y+i\<to\>L*<around|(|y+e<rsub|b>|)>+i>a,>>|<row|<cell|<around|(|\<cal-R\>*a|)><rsub|y>>|<cell|=\<tau\><rsub|y>*<around|(|o;a|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i>w<rsub|i>*\<tau\><rsub|y>*<around|(|i;a|)>.>>>>
    </eqsplit>
  </equation*>

  The weighted connector derivative is exactly

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:branch-probe>\<cal-Q\>*a=\<cal-A\>*a+d<rsub|c><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>*\<cal-R\>*a.
  </equation>

  The sign is determined by the subtraction of the frame at the final
  endpoint: the remaining root terms are <math|<around|(|\<cal-R\>*a|)><rsub|y+e<rsub|b>>-<around|(|\<cal-R\>*a|)><rsub|y>>.
  For a fine gradient <math|a=d<rsub|f><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>*v>, path sums
  telescope, giving

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|\<cal-R\>*d<rsub|f><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>*v|)><rsub|y>=v*<around|(|L*y|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i>w<rsub|i>*v*<around|(|L*y+i|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-Q\>*d<rsub|f><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>*v=d<rsub|c><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>*<around|{|v*<around|(|L*y|)>|}><rsub|y>.
  </equation*>

  This checks the root normalization directly. Equations
  <eqref|eq:branch-frame-derivative> and <eqref|eq:branch-probe> prove the
  analytic predictor law and derivative used in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-unhit-comparison>. Its transplantation
  argument then supplies a uniform fixed-patch Taylor radius for all periods.

  The covariance needed there follows from the same definitions. A gauge
  change turns a frame into <math|f<rprime|'><rsub|i>=g<rsub|o>*f<rsub|i>*g<rsub|i><rsup|-1>>.
  On a minimizing branch it preserves the energy and centre normalization, so
  uniqueness identifies this with the selected frame; on a fallback branch
  the identity follows by telescoping its rooted paths. Connector endpoint
  factors then reduce to <math|g<rsub|L*y>*Y<rsub|i>*g<rsub|L*<around|(|y+e<rsub|b>|)>><rsup|-1>>.
  The weighted cost is invariant under this change of variables. Uniqueness
  identifies the minimizing predictor, and direct substitution handles a
  selected connector on the other branch. The original tags are retained in a
  gauge change. These identities give the block-constant covariance used in
  the unhit comparison.

  <subsection|The comparison error on the unhit
  region><label|sec:seed-unhit-comparison>

  We compare the actual cut kernels with the full linear kernels at the
  identity. The linear cancellation uses the derivative of the actual rooted
  predictor. Its gradient correction disappears only after the coarse curl is
  applied. Throughout this section <math|L> and the patch are fixed;
  constants may depend on them but are uniform in the period, the truncation
  length, and the selected tags.

  <subsubsection|The full linear identities>

  Let <math|B<rsub|f>,B<rsub|c>> be the two stacked curvature maps: their
  first components are <math|<sqrt|a<rsub|f>>> and <math|<sqrt|a<rsub|c>>>
  times the identity, and their second components are the full path-root
  kernels. Path coefficients are aligned with these root kernels by summing
  all labels with the same displacement and target orientation. Absolute
  convergence in <eqref|eq:guide-path-input> permits periodization and
  grouping. Consequently the linearizations of the two plaquette fields are

  <\equation*>
    X<rsub|f><rsup|0>=B<rsub|f>*d<rsub|f>*a,<space|2em>X<rsub|c><rsup|0>=B<rsub|c>*d<rsub|c>*b,
  </equation*>

  where <math|a,b> are fine and coarse logarithmic bond fields and
  <math|d<rsub|f>,d<rsub|c>> are the discrete exterior derivatives from bonds
  to plaquettes. Let <math|F<rsub|0>,I<rsub|0>,N<rsub|0>> be the full real
  kernels corresponding to <math|\<Phi\>,\<Phi\><rprime|'>,N> at the
  identity. The flat stack identities needed here are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:unhit-flat-stack>

    <\eqsplit>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|F<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*F<rsub|0>+I<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*I<rsub|0>+N<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*N<rsub|0>>|<cell|=1,>>|<row|<cell|<sqrt|p>*<space|0.17em>I<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>>|<cell|=B<rsub|c>*d<rsub|c>,>>|<row|<cell|F<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*B<rsub|f>*d<rsub|f>>|<cell|=<sqrt|p>*<space|0.17em>I<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<cal-A\>.>>>>
    </eqsplit>
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<cal-A\>> is the unrooted linear averaging map. These are
  identities of full finite matrices in physical counting coordinates, for
  the same step and the same two stages. We state their exact role because
  none of them is asserted for the transported cut kernels. Their production,
  together with the aligned path expansion, is the flat-stack input to this
  comparison.

  The derivative <math|\<cal-Q\>> of the actual predictor has the form
  <math|\<cal-Q\>=\<cal-A\>+d<rsub|c><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>*\<cal-R\>>, where
  <math|d<rsub|c><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>> sends site fields to bond gradients.
  Thus, since <math|d<rsub|c>*d<rsub|c><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>=0>,

  <\equation*>
    <sqrt|p>*<space|0.17em>I<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<cal-Q\>=B<rsub|c>*d<rsub|c>*\<cal-A\>.
  </equation*>

  The discrete identity <math|d<rsub|c>*d<rsub|c><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>=0>
  follows by cancellation of the four endpoint differences around each
  plaquette. With <math|Z<rsup|0>=<sqrt|p>*<space|0.17em><around|(|b-\<cal-Q\>*a|)>>,
  the last two identities in <eqref|eq:unhit-flat-stack> therefore give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:unhit-linear-zero>F<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*X<rsub|f><rsup|0>+I<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*Z<rsup|0>-X<rsub|c><rsup|0>=0.
  </equation>

  The first identity in <eqref|eq:unhit-flat-stack> also gives
  <math|<around|(|1-F<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*F<rsub|0>-I<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*I<rsub|0>-N<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*N<rsub|0>|)>*w=0>
  for every doubled coarse field <math|w>. No closedness assumption on
  <math|w> or removal of harmonic modes is used.

  <subsubsection|Uniform Taylor bounds for local reads>

  For a real logarithmic field <math|a>, put <math|U<rsub|e>=exp
  <around|(|a<rsub|e>|)>> and use its supremum norm. On a fixed small group
  chart,

  <\equation*>
    r<around|(|exp v|)>\<leqslant\>C<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|log
    g|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*r<around|(|g|)>,<space|2em>exp <around|(|log
    g|)>=g.
  </equation*>

  These follow by integrating the first derivative of the analytic
  exponential and local logarithm on smaller closed balls. Likewise, Taylor's
  formula for the product of the four exponentials around a plaquette gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:unhit-face-taylor><around|\<\|\|\>|F<around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>-<around|(|d*a|)><rsub|p>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>><rsup|2>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|d*a|)><rsub|p>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>.
  </equation>

  Indeed differentiating the product at zero gives the signed sum of the four
  logarithms; the derivative of the curvature coordinate is the identity on
  the Lie algebra. Bound the second derivative on the fixed four-slot ball
  and integrate the remainder. Repeated periodic bonds simply repeat
  coordinates in that four-slot map and do not change the constant.

  For the actual predictor we use its local analytic law at the identity, for
  each allowed tag, with derivative <math|\<cal-Q\>> above. The law is part
  of the branch construction: it includes uniqueness of the normalized
  minimizing frame on the branch where that frame is used. It implies the
  uniform estimate

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:unhit-predictor-taylor><around|\<\|\|\>|Z-Z<rsup|0>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C*h<rsup|2>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsup|0>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C*h,<space|1em>h=max
    <around|{|<around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>,<around|\<\|\|\>|b|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|}>,
  </equation>

  with a radius independent of the torus. Here is why finite-dimensional
  Taylor estimates give that uniformity. A predictor bond reads only two
  frame patches, their coordinate paths, and their length-<math|L>
  connectors. This is a fixed finite set of cover bond anchors. Choose one
  reference torus large enough that these anchors have their actual cover
  lifts and its two coarse endpoints are distinct. Transplant the local fine
  logarithms to it by reading the original periodic field at each
  corresponding cover anchor. Transplant the coarse logarithm at the tested
  bond as a constant field, and copy the actual two frame tags and connector
  tag. The transplanted sup norm does not increase. All path products, frame
  scores, selected frames and connectors agree. On a minimizing branch,
  equality of scores gives equality of the selected normalized frame by its
  uniqueness; on the other branch the chosen axial paths agree directly.

  There are only finitely many tags and tested directions on this reference
  torus. Take the minimum of their positive Taylor radii and the maximum of
  their Taylor bounds. Equality of the transplanted reads along every ray
  <math|v\<mapsto\>v*<around|(|a,b|)>> gives equality of their derivatives at
  zero as well, so these bounds prove <eqref|eq:unhit-predictor-taylor> on
  every torus, including short ones. This proves the uniformization; the
  stated analytic branch law and identification of its derivative are
  required inputs.

  <subsubsection|Truncation and transport errors>

  For cutoff <math|R> in fine units define

  <\equation*>
    \<omega\>*<around|(|R,h|)>=<around|(|1+R|)>*h+e<rsup|-\<tau\>*R>,<space|2em>\<tau\>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation*>

  The kernel strip width here is strictly positive, <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>.
  The value of <math|\<tau\>> can be decreased a finite number of times
  below. If all logarithms have norm at most <math|h> in the chart, every
  link has chord at most <math|C*h>, and a path of length <math|k> has chord
  at most <math|C*k*h> by the product triangle inequality. On the colour
  space,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:unhit-adjoint><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|Ad><around|(|g|)>-1|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*D*r<around|(|g|)>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|D> is the fixed colour normalization. To see this, write
  <math|g*X*g<rsup|\<ast\>>-X=<around|(|g-1|)>*X*g<rsup|\<ast\>>+X*<around|(|g<rsup|\<ast\>>-1|)>>,
  use the Frobenius product bound and unitary invariance, then divide by the
  colour normalization.

  Consider a transported kernel term with cover displacement <math|z> and
  physical path length <math|k\<leqslant\>D<rsub|L>*<around|(|1+<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>|)>>,
  with <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>> the <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>> norm as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-guides>. This bound holds for the
  actual paths: a narrow path has fine length
  <math|L<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>, while the wide path from residue <math|r>
  to <math|L*z> has length at most <math|L\|z\|<rsub|1>+4*L>. If
  <math|k\<leqslant\>R>, <eqref|eq:unhit-adjoint> bounds the difference from
  the scalar flat term by <math|C*R*h<around|\||K<rsub|a*b><around|(|z|)>|\|>>.
  If <math|k\<gtr\>R>, the flat term is omitted, and

  <\equation*>
    1\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<eta\>>*e<rsup|\<eta\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>*e<rsup|-\<eta\>*R/D<rsub|L>>.
  </equation*>

  Paying both cases with the weighted strip sum and using the same row and
  column counting as <eqref|eq:guide-periodic-rows> proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:unhit-kernel-near><around|\<\|\|\>|F|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|row>>,<around|\<\|\|\>|F|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|col>>,<around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|0>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|row>>,<around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|0>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|col>>\<leqslant\>C,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|F-F<rsub|0>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|row>>,<around|\<\|\|\>|F-F<rsub|0>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|col>>\<leqslant\>C*\<omega\>*<around|(|R,h|)>.
  </equation>

  This holds for each of the three kernels, after increasing <math|C> and
  choosing <math|\<tau\>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>/D<rsub|L>>. The type counts,
  including residues, are absorbed after <math|L> is fixed. Every cover term
  is estimated before periodization, so winding paths cause no exception.

  The same argument applies to the two plaquette fields. For a retained path
  term, first use <eqref|eq:unhit-face-taylor>, then <eqref|eq:unhit-adjoint>
  on its transport. Its error is at most <math|C*<around|(|1+R|)>*h<rsup|2>>
  times its coefficient. For an omitted term use its full linear value,
  bounded by <math|C*h>, and <math|1\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<sigma\>*\<ell\>>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*R/\<kappa\>>>
  when <math|\<kappa\>*\<ell\>\<gtr\>R>, where
  <math|\<kappa\>\<in\><around|{|1,L|}>> is the stage factor of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-guides>. The weighted absolute path sum
  therefore gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:unhit-field-near><around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsup|0>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C*h,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|X-X<rsup|0>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C*h*\<omega\>*<around|(|R,h|)>
  </equation>

  for both stages, with <math|\<tau\>\<leqslant\>min
  <around|{|\<sigma\><rsub|f>,\<sigma\><rsub|c>/L|}>>. Absolute convergence
  justifies subtracting and summing the path series, even if the set of
  labels below the length cutoff is infinite. Equation
  <eqref|eq:unhit-predictor-taylor> has the same form since
  <math|h\<leqslant\>\<omega\>*<around|(|R,h|)>>.

  If <math|F,F<rsub|0>> satisfy <eqref|eq:unhit-kernel-near> and
  <math|z,z<rsup|0>> satisfy <eqref|eq:unhit-field-near>, then

  <\equation*>
    F<rsup|\<ast\>>*z-F<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*z<rsup|0>=F<rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|z-z<rsup|0>|)>+<around|(|F-F<rsub|0>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*z<rsup|0>
  </equation*>

  has sup norm at most <math|2*C<rsup|2>*h*\<omega\>*<around|(|R,h|)>>. For
  an arbitrary bounded field <math|w>, the same decomposition gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|F<rsup|\<ast\>>*F-F<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*F<rsub|0>|)>*w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>2*C<rsup|2><around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*\<omega\>*<around|(|R,h|)>.
  </equation*>

  Insert these bounds in the two full linear cancellations. After increasing
  <math|C>, the actual defect vectors satisfy

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:unhit-global><around|\<\|\|\>|\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>*X<rsub|f>+<around|(|\<Phi\><rprime|'>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*Z-X<rsub|c>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C*h*\<omega\>*<around|(|R,h|)>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|1-\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Phi\>-<around|(|\<Phi\><rprime|'>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Phi\><rprime|'>-N<rsup|\<ast\>>*N|)>*w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C<around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*\<omega\>*<around|(|R,h|)>.
  </equation>

  The argument keeps <math|w> arbitrary; it requires no regularity of the
  clipping indicators that will later define <math|w>.

  <subsubsection|Restriction to a neighbourhood and summation>

  A cut kernel entry has endpoints within distance <math|R> in coarse anchor
  units and reads only the transport path of that length. The cut path field
  reads through radius <math|R+4>. A predictor bond reads through a fixed
  patch radius <math|R<rsub|<math-up|pred>>>. Thus the first defect at anchor
  <math|o> depends only on fine and coarse bonds through radius
  <math|3*R+4+R<rsub|<math-up|pred>>>, using parent distance for fine bonds.
  For example, the outer transpose moves the input anchor by at most
  <math|R>, its kernel path adds at most <math|R>, and the input field adds
  <math|R+4> or the predictor radius. The second defect, as an operator
  applied to a fixed <math|w>, reads its kernels through radius <math|2*R>.
  Equality of these reads gives equality term by term in each cut series and
  finite matrix sum.

  Suppose a coarse gauge <math|z> makes the fine and coarse bond chords at
  most <math|h> on this neighbourhood. Take the local logarithms there and
  set them to zero outside. Their global sup norms are at most <math|A*h> and
  their exponentials agree with the gauged fields on all required reads.
  Apply <eqref|eq:unhit-global> to these extensions. Replacing <math|h> by
  <math|A*h> changes only the constant, since for <math|A\<geqslant\>1>,
  <math|\<omega\>*<around|(|R,A*h|)>\<leqslant\>A*\<omega\>*<around|(|R,h|)>>.

  To remove the gauge, every transported plaquette term transforms by
  <math|<math-up|Ad><around|(|z<rsub|o>|)>> at its anchor; the endpoint gauge
  factors cancel. The same endpoint calculation for kernels gives

  <\equation*>
    F<rprime|'><rsub|i*j>=<math-up|Ad><around|(|z<rsub|i>|)>*F<rsub|i*j><math-up|Ad><around|(|z<rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1>,
  </equation*>

  with the parent anchor on fine rows. Taking physical adjoints preserves
  this identity, because adjoint transport is orthogonal. The actual
  predictor is covariant with the same tags under the block-constant fine
  gauge. Hence both defect vectors transform by
  <math|<math-up|Ad><around|(|z<rsub|o>|)>>, provided the free field <math|w>
  is transformed at its anchors. Its norm is unchanged. Thus the local
  version of <eqref|eq:unhit-global> holds for the original fields and for
  any bounded <math|w>. In particular we may use the original clipped field
  <math|v> without changing its indicators in the extension argument.

  Take <math|R=<around|\<lfloor\>|B/100000|\<rfloor\>>> and
  <math|h=C<rsub|L>*B<rsup|10>*T> from <eqref|eq:tree-common-gauge>. For
  large <math|B>, <math|3*R+4+R<rsub|<math-up|pred>>\<leqslant\><around|\<lfloor\>|2*B/5|\<rfloor\>>>,
  so this estimate applies to every unhit outer plaquette. As
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>d*t>, its two
  comparison rows are bounded below by minus

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:unhit-row-loss>\<cal-L\>=2*d*t*<space|0.17em>C*h*\<omega\>*<around|(|R,h|)>+d*t*<space|0.17em>C*d*t*<space|0.17em>\<omega\>*<around|(|R,h|)>.
  </equation>

  There are two components in the linear row, while the factor <math|1/2> in
  the quadratic row cancels its two components.

  For completeness, the required delay is stronger than <math|h\<to\>0>.
  Write <math|H=x+c<rsub|L>>, so <math|T=H<rsup|-49/100>> and
  <math|t=P<around|(|H|)>*H<rsup|-1/2>\<geqslant\>H<rsup|-1/2>>. Any fixed
  power of <math|B=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>
  is smaller than any positive power of <math|H> eventually. Consequently,
  for every fixed integer <math|n>,

  <\equation*>
    B<rsup|n>*h\<longrightarrow\>0,<space|2em>B<rsup|n>*<frac|h<rsup|2>|t>\<longrightarrow\>0,
  </equation*>

  the latter because <math|T<rsup|2>/t\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-48/100>>. Also
  <math|e<rsup|-\<tau\>*R>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1>> eventually: <math|R> grows
  quadratically in <math|log x>, whereas <math|log H> grows linearly. It
  follows that

  <\equation*>
    B<rsup|n>*\<omega\>*<around|(|R,h|)>\<longrightarrow\>0,<space|2em>B<rsup|n>*<frac|h*\<omega\>*<around|(|R,h|)>|t>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  Expanding <math|\<omega\>> proves the second assertion from the
  <math|h<rsup|2>/t> bound and <math|e<rsup|-\<tau\>*R>/t\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1/2>>.
  The outer region has at most <math|N*B<rsup|4>*n> plaquettes for <math|n>
  seeds. Given <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, choose the coupling threshold so
  that <math|N*B<rsup|4>*\<cal-L\>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>*t<rsup|2>>. Summing
  <eqref|eq:unhit-row-loss> gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:unhit-final><big|sum><rsub|p<text| outer,
    unhit>><around|(|L<rsub|p>+D<rsub|p>|)>\<geqslant\>-\<epsilon\>*t<rsup|2>*n.
  </equation>

  This is the unhit-region estimate used in the energy allocation. Its
  remaining construction inputs are the full flat stack and aligned path
  expansions, and the analytic law and derivative of the actual predictor.
  The estimates, localization, common-gauge use and delay from those inputs
  have all been given above.

  <subsection|The energy gain assigned to defect
  seeds><label|sec:seed-energy>

  The exponential gain in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:packet-estimates>
  comes from a lower bound on the energy assigned to a finite set of defects.
  Fine plaquette defects, predictor defects and coarse flags require
  different arguments. In particular, some coarse terms can be negative. The
  proof retains a reserve from the very large fine and predictor defects to
  pay for these terms. We give the local algebra and the final allocation,
  keeping the geometric and local comparison inputs explicit.

  <subsubsection|A quartic bound for the curvature defect>

  Use the real Frobenius inner product in the defining unitary matrix
  representation of the group. Write <math|\<frak-g\>> for its tangent space
  at the identity and <math|\<Pi\>> for orthogonal projection onto
  <math|\<frak-g\>>. Let <math|D\<gtr\>0> be the colour normalization, so
  that

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|U-1|\<\|\|\>>=D*<space|0.17em>r<around|(|U|)>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|col><around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>>=D<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|r<around|(|U|)>> is the group chord and <math|<math-up|col>> is
  the isometric identification up to the factor <math|D>. The curvature
  coordinate <math|F<around|(|U|)>> is obtained by projecting
  <math|<around|(|U-U<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>/2> onto <math|\<frak-g\>>. Since the
  Hermitian part of <math|U-1> is orthogonal, for the real Frobenius inner
  product, to every skew-Hermitian matrix,

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|col><around|(|F<around|(|U|)>|)>=\<Pi\>*<around|(|U-1|)>.
  </equation*>

  To check the orthogonality, if <math|H<rsup|\<ast\>>=H> and
  <math|X<rsup|\<ast\>>=-X>, cyclicity of the trace shows that
  <math|<math-up|Retr><around|(|H*X<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>> is its own negative.
  Pythagoras therefore gives the exact identity

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:seed-normal-defect>D<rsup|2>*<around|{|r<around|(|U|)><rsup|2>-<around|\<\|\|\>|F<around|(|U|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>|}>=<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|1-\<Pi\>|)>*<around|(|U-1|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>.
  </equation>

  In particular <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|F<around|(|U|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>r<around|(|U|)>>
  for every group element.

  The nearest-point projection <math|\<pi\>> of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:branch-nearest-projection> is not part of the
  group data; it is constructed from <math|G> there. It is real-analytic on
  <math|B*<around|(|1,2*\<rho\>|)>>, equals the identity on
  <math|G\<cap\>B*<around|(|1,2*\<rho\>|)>>, and has derivative <math|\<Pi\>>
  at <math|1>. For <math|U\<in\>G> with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|U-1|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<rho\>>
  we therefore have <math|<around|(|1-\<Pi\>|)>*<around|(|U-1|)>=\<pi\><around|(|U|)>-\<pi\><around|(|1|)>-D*\<pi\><around|(|1|)>*<around|(|U-1|)>>,
  and Taylor's formula with integral remainder along the segment from
  <math|1> to <math|U>, which lies in the convex closed ball
  <math|<wide|B|\<bar\>>*<around|(|1,\<rho\>|)>>, gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|1-\<Pi\>|)>*<around|(|U-1|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*<around|\<\|\|\>|U-1|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>,<space|2em>C=<tfrac|1|2>sup<rsub|<wide|B|\<bar\>>*<around|(|1,\<rho\>|)>><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|2>*\<pi\>|\<\|\|\>>,
  </equation*>

  which is finite by part<nbsp>(d) of the lemma. (If <math|C=0> replace it by
  <math|1>.) Combining it with <eqref|eq:seed-normal-defect> gives constants
  <math|K,\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>, depending only on the group data, such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:seed-curvature-bound>r<rsup|2>-K*r<rsup|4>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|F<around|(|U|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>r<rsup|2>,<space|2em><tfrac|3|4>r<rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|F<around|(|U|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2><space|1em><text|when
    >r=r<around|(|U|)>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>.
  </equation>

  One can take <math|K=C<rsup|2>*D<rsup|2>> and choose <math|\<delta\>> with
  <math|D*\<delta\>\<leqslant\>\<rho\>> (so that
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|U-1|\<\|\|\>>=D*r<around|(|U|)>\<leqslant\>\<rho\>>
  and the Taylor bound applies) and <math|K*\<delta\><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>1/4>.
  Thus the nonnegative defect <math|\<frak-f\><around|(|U|)>=<around|(|r<around|(|U|)><rsup|2>-<around|\<\|\|\>|F<around|(|U|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>|)>/2>
  is at most <math|K*r<around|(|U|)><rsup|4>/2> in that neighbourhood.

  This proves the group estimate needed for the coarse energy row. Let
  <math|a<rsub|c>\<gtr\>0> be its quadratic coefficient and
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|c>\<gtr\>0> its linear coefficient, with
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|c>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|c>/8>. At coarse threshold
  <math|s\<geqslant\>0>, the clipping function and the radial attenuation are

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|s><around|(|r|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|a<rsub|c>*r<rsup|2>/2,>|<cell|r\<leqslant\>s,>>|<row|<cell|\<lambda\><rsub|c>*s*r,>|<cell|r\<gtr\>s,>>>>><space|2em>\<alpha\><rsub|s><around|(|r|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|1,>|<cell|r=0,>>|<row|<cell|min
    <around|{|1,s/r|}>,>|<cell|r\<gtr\>0.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  Cancellation of the other coarse quadratic components gives the row

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:seed-coarse-row>C<rsub|s><around|(|U|)>=a<rsub|c>*<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|s>-\<alpha\><rsub|s><rsup|2>/2|)>\<\|\|\>F<around|(|U|)>\<\|\|\><rsup|2>-q<rsub|s><around|(|r<around|(|U|)>|)>.
  </equation>

  For <math|0\<leqslant\>s\<leqslant\>\<delta\>> this has three useful
  bounds:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:seed-three-ranges><tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-halign|l>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-lborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-halign|l>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-rborder|0ln>|<table|<row|<cell|r\<leqslant\>s:>|<cell|C<rsub|s><around|(|U|)>\<geqslant\>-B<rsub|0>*s<rsup|4>,<space|1em>B<rsub|0>=a<rsub|c>*K/2,>>|<row|<cell|s\<less\>r\<leqslant\>\<delta\>:>|<cell|C<rsub|s><around|(|U|)>\<geqslant\><around|(|a<rsub|c>/4|)>*s*r,>>|<row|<cell|r\<gtr\>\<delta\>:>|<cell|C<rsub|s><around|(|U|)>\<geqslant\>-\<lambda\><rsub|c>*s*r.>>>>>
  </equation>

  For the first, <math|\<alpha\><rsub|s>=1> and use the quartic defect. In
  the other two ranges <math|\<alpha\><rsub|s>=s/r\<in\><around|[|0,1|]>>, so
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|s>-\<alpha\><rsub|s><rsup|2>/2\<geqslant\>s/<around|(|2*r|)>>.
  In the middle range this gives <math|C<rsub|s>\<geqslant\><around|(|3*a<rsub|c>/8-\<lambda\><rsub|c>|)>*s*r\<geqslant\>a<rsub|c>*s*r/4>;
  in the last range drop the nonnegative curvature term. These arguments
  include <math|s=0>.

  <subsubsection|The fine row and the discarded quadratic component>

  Let the fine threshold be <math|t\<gtr\>0>. Write
  <math|a<rsub|f>,\<lambda\><rsub|f>\<gtr\>0> for the fine quadratic and
  linear coefficients, and let <math|r<rsub|i>> be the fine plaquette chords.
  The direct part of its energy row is

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|i>=q<rsub|t><around|(|r<rsub|i>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>\<\|\|\>v<rsub|i>\<\|\|\><rsup|2>-<around|\<langle\>|v<rsub|i>,y<rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|y<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><sqrt|a<rsub|f>>*<space|0.17em>r<rsub|i>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|q<rsub|t>> uses coefficients
  <math|a<rsub|f>,\<lambda\><rsub|f>>. The second part is

  <\equation*>
    H<rsub|i>=\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|i<text|
    retained>|}>><tfrac|1|2>\<\|\|\>u<rsub|i>*<around|\<\|\|\>|<rsup|2>+<tfrac|1|2>|\<\|\|\>>*v<rprime|'><rsub|i>\<\|\|\><rsup|2>-<around|\<langle\>|v<rprime|'><rsub|i>,u<rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation*>

  Their sum is the fine row. The guide bounds are
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>,<around|\<\|\|\>|v<rprime|'><rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>*t>,
  with <math|\<epsilon\>=C/L<rsup|2>>. The guide constant
  <math|C\<geqslant\>0>, the coefficients
  <math|a<rsub|f>,\<lambda\><rsub|f>\<gtr\>0>, and the fine clipping radius
  <math|R\<gtr\>0> are fixed before <math|L> is chosen.

  Let <math|\<cal-F\>> be the listed fine defects and
  <math|\<cal-S\>=<around|{|i\<in\>\<cal-F\>:r<rsub|i>\<gtr\>t|}>> their
  large part. The local sector conditions
  (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:geometry-sector-conditions>, part (S1))
  ensure <math|r<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>t/4> on <math|\<cal-F\>> and
  <math|r<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>t/2> on unlisted plaquettes in the relevant
  neighbourhood. For <math|r<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>t>, Cauchy\USchwarz gives

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|i>\<geqslant\><tfrac|1|2><around|(|<sqrt|a<rsub|f>>*<space|0.17em>r<rsub|i>-<around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>0.
  </equation*>

  If additionally <math|i\<in\>\<cal-F\>\<setminus\>\<cal-S\>> and
  <math|\<epsilon\>\<leqslant\><sqrt|a<rsub|f>>/8>, the last square is at
  least <math|a<rsub|f>*t<rsup|2>/128>. For <math|i\<in\>\<cal-S\>>, the
  linear part of the clipping and <math|\<epsilon\>*<sqrt|a<rsub|f>>\<leqslant\>\<lambda\><rsub|f>/4>
  give <math|D<rsub|i>\<geqslant\><around|(|3*\<lambda\><rsub|f>/4|)>*t*r<rsub|i>>.
  Summing over the fine rows in the <math|2*B> neighbourhood of the seeds
  therefore gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:seed-direct-income><big|sum><rsub|i>D<rsub|i>\<geqslant\><frac|3*\<lambda\><rsub|f>|4>*t*<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-S\>>r<rsub|i>+<frac|a<rsub|f>|128>*t<rsup|2>*<around|\||\<cal-F\>\<setminus\>\<cal-S\>|\|>.
  </equation>

  The second component is nonnegative on retained rows, since there it is
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|i>-v<rprime|'><rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>/2>.
  It is not necessarily nonnegative on discarded rows. We count these rows on
  the original fine lattice. A discarded row at <math|i> has a witness
  <math|j> with

  <\equation*>
    t*exp <around|{|d*<around|(|i,j|)>/R|}>\<less\>r<rsub|j>.
  </equation*>

  The finite-range locality conditions ensure that each such witness belongs
  to <math|\<cal-S\>>. For <math|y\<geqslant\>1>, the number of fine
  plaquette addresses within distance <math|R*log y> of a point is at most

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|R>*y,<space|2em>C<rsub|R>=<around|(|8*R+1|)><rsup|4>#<around|{|<text|plaquette
    axes>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed <math|1+<around|(|log y|)>/4\<leqslant\>y<rsup|1/4>> implies
  <math|2*R*log y+1\<leqslant\><around|(|8*R+1|)>*y<rsup|1/4>>, and the
  lattice ball has at most <math|<around|(|2*<around|\<lfloor\>|R*log
  y|\<rfloor\>>+1|)><rsup|4>> sites. A union bound over witnesses therefore
  gives, for the discarded set <math|\<cal-D\>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:seed-discarded-count>t<around|\||\<cal-D\>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|R>*<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>\<cal-S\>>r<rsub|j>.
  </equation>

  There is no factor <math|L<rsup|4>> in this count.

  For the shortened path field <math|u<rsub|i>>, use the nonnegative
  finite-range coefficient matrix <math|k<rsub|i*j>>, whose row and column
  sums are at most <math|C> and which satisfies
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|j>k<rsub|i*j>*r<rsub|j>>.
  Locality and the sector conditions imply <math|r<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>t> for
  a contributing <math|j> outside <math|\<cal-S\>>. Thus

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*t+<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>\<cal-S\>>k<rsub|i*j>*r<rsub|j>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-D\>><around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|C<rsub|R>+1|)>*<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>\<cal-S\>>r<rsub|j>.
  </equation*>

  The second inequality uses <eqref|eq:seed-discarded-count> for the constant
  part and the column bound for the large part. Since
  <math|H<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>-\<epsilon\>*t<around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>>
  on discarded rows, choosing <math|L> so large that
  <math|\<epsilon\>*C*<around|(|C<rsub|R>+1|)>\<leqslant\>\<lambda\><rsub|f>/4>
  and combining with <eqref|eq:seed-direct-income> proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:seed-fine-income>F<rsub|A>\<assign\><big|sum><rsub|i><around|(|D<rsub|i>+H<rsub|i>|)>\<geqslant\><frac|\<lambda\><rsub|f>|2>*t*<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-S\>>r<rsub|i>+d<rsub|f>*t<rsup|2>*<around|\||\<cal-F\>\<setminus\>\<cal-S\>|\|>,<space|2em>d<rsub|f>=a<rsub|f>/128\<gtr\>0.
  </equation>

  The geometric row and column estimates and the guide bound used here are
  explicit inputs; the discarded-row argument shows precisely how they enter
  the energy estimate.

  For predictor defects the argument is simpler. Let <math|p\<gtr\>0> be the
  predictor penalty and <math|z<rsub|i>> its bond chord. A predictor energy
  row is at least <math|<around|(|<sqrt|p>*<space|0.17em>z<rsub|i>-<around|\<\|\|\>|w<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|2>/2>,
  with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|w<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*t>. On each
  listed predictor defect <math|i\<in\>\<cal-I\>>, the sector condition (S2)
  of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:geometry-sector-conditions> is
  <math|z<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>W*t>. Choose <math|W> after <math|L> so that
  <math|C\<leqslant\><sqrt|p>*<space|0.17em>W/2>. The unlisted rows are
  nonnegative, and hence

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:seed-predictor-income>I<rsub|A>\<geqslant\><frac|p|8>*<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-I\>>z<rsub|i><rsup|2>.
  </equation>

  <subsubsection|The reserve for very large defects>

  Let <math|T\<geqslant\>t> be a larger threshold and put

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-F\><rsub|T>=<around|{|i\<in\>\<cal-F\>:r<rsub|i>\<gtr\>T|}>,<space|1em>\<cal-I\><rsub|T>=<around|{|i\<in\>\<cal-I\>:z<rsub|i>\<gtr\>T|}>,<space|1em>d<rsub|A>=<around|\||\<cal-F\><rsub|T>|\|>+<around|\||\<cal-I\><rsub|T>|\|>.
  </equation*>

  The use of <math|T> here does not change the seed set. Let
  <math|m\<geqslant\>0> satisfy

  <\equation*>
    m\<leqslant\>\<lambda\><rsub|f>/8,<space|2em>m\<leqslant\>d<rsub|f>,<space|2em>m\<leqslant\>p/32.
  </equation*>

  If <math|c<rsub|F>\<leqslant\>\<lambda\><rsub|f>/4>, then
  <eqref|eq:seed-fine-income> implies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:seed-fine-reserve>F<rsub|A>\<geqslant\>m*t<rsup|2>\|\<cal-F\>*<around|\||+m*t*T|\|>*\<cal-F\><rsub|T>\|+c<rsub|F>*t*<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-S\>>r<rsub|i>.
  </equation>

  To check this, both <math|t<around|\||\<cal-S\>|\|>> and
  <math|T<around|\||\<cal-F\><rsub|T>|\|>> are at most
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<cal-S\>>r<rsub|i>>. Their two charges and the
  <math|c<rsub|F>> charge use at most <math|<around|(|2*m+c<rsub|F>|)>*t*<big|sum><rsub|\<cal-S\>>r<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|f>/2|)>*t*<big|sum><rsub|\<cal-S\>>r<rsub|i>>.
  The remaining fine seeds are paid by <math|d<rsub|f>*t<rsup|2>>.

  Similarly suppose <math|W\<geqslant\>1> and
  <math|C<rsub|1>\<leqslant\>p*W/16>. Then <eqref|eq:seed-predictor-income>
  implies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:seed-predictor-reserve>I<rsub|A>\<geqslant\>m*t<rsup|2>\|\<cal-I\>*<around|\||+m*t*T|\|>*\<cal-I\><rsub|T>\|+C<rsub|1>*t*<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-I\>>z<rsub|i>.
  </equation>

  Indeed <math|z<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>W*t\<geqslant\>t> on every listed bond,
  so <math|t<rsup|2><around|\||\<cal-I\>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|sum>z<rsub|i><rsup|2>>
  and <math|t*T<around|\||\<cal-I\><rsub|T>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|sum>z<rsub|i><rsup|2>>.
  Also <math|C<rsub|1>*t*z<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><around|(|p/16|)>*z<rsub|i><rsup|2>>.
  The two first charges use at most <math|2*m*<big|sum>z<rsub|i><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|(|p/16|)>*<big|sum>z<rsub|i><rsup|2>>,
  leaving the required linear charge within the total <math|p/8>.

  <subsubsection|Coarse flags and the affected region>

  Let <math|\<cal-O\>> be the coarse plaquette rows in the <math|2*B>
  neighbourhood of all seeds, and let <math|\<cal-H\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-O\>>
  be the rows affected by a very large defect. Split the coarse plaquettes
  into

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-C\><rsub|0>=<around|{|r<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>s|}>,<space|1em>\<cal-C\><rsub|1>=<around|{|s\<less\>r<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>|}>,<space|1em>\<cal-C\><rsub|2>=<around|{|r<rsub|i>\<gtr\>\<delta\>|}>,<space|2em>i\<in\>\<cal-O\>.
  </equation*>

  The geometry supplies a constant <math|K<rsub|0>>, fixed before <math|L,W>,
  such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:seed-geometric-counts>\<cal-C\><rsub|2>\<subseteq\>\<cal-H\>,<space|1em><around|\||\<cal-C\><rsub|2>|\|>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|0>*d<rsub|A>,<space|1em>n<rsub|g>\<leqslant\>4*<around|(|<around|\||\<cal-C\><rsub|1>|\|>+K<rsub|0>*d<rsub|A>|)>,<space|1em><around|\||\<cal-O\>|\|>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|0>*B<rsup|4>*n,
  </equation>

  where <math|n<rsub|g>> is the number of coarse flag seeds and
  <math|n=<around|\||\<cal-F\>|\|>+<around|\||\<cal-I\>|\|>+n<rsub|g>> is the
  total number of seeds. These are the precise geometric counting inputs used
  below. For <math|t\<leqslant\>2*s> and <math|m\<leqslant\>a<rsub|c>/64>,
  the middle bound in <eqref|eq:seed-three-ranges> gives at least
  <math|4*m*t<rsup|2>> on each row of <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|1>>. Summing all
  three ranges yields

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:seed-coarse-reserve>C<rsub|A>\<geqslant\>4*m*t<rsup|2>*<around|\||\<cal-C\><rsub|1>*<around|\||-\<lambda\><rsub|c>*s*<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-C\><rsub|2>>r<rsub|i>-B<rsub|0>*s<rsup|4>|\|>*\<cal-O\>|\|>.
  </equation>

  The remaining two comparison rows, denoted together by <math|L<rsub|A>>,
  are split over <math|\<cal-H\>> and its complement. Their local estimates
  have the form

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|L<rsub|\<cal-H\>>>|<cell|\<geqslant\>\<lambda\><rsub|c>*s*<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-C\><rsub|2>>r<rsub|i>-<frac|C<rsub|1>|L<rsup|2>>*t*<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-S\>>r<rsub|i>-C<rsub|1>*t*<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-I\>>z<rsub|i>-Z*t<rsup|2>*B<rsup|4>*d<rsub|A>,<eq-number><label|eq:seed-hit-input>>>|<row|<cell|L<rsub|\<cal-O\>\<setminus\>\<cal-H\>>>|<cell|\<geqslant\>-\<eta\>*t<rsup|2>*n.<eq-number><label|eq:seed-unhit-input>>>>>
  </align>

  The constant <math|C<rsub|1>> is fixed before <math|L,W>, whereas <math|Z>
  may depend on both. The second estimate is available for every fixed
  <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0> by taking <math|x> sufficiently large after the
  other parameters. The row estimates producing these inequalities remain
  inputs here. The positive normalization row, denoted <math|N<rsub|A>>,
  satisfies <math|N<rsub|A>\<geqslant\>0>. The total assigned energy is
  exactly <math|F<rsub|A>+I<rsub|A>+C<rsub|A>+N<rsub|A>+L<rsub|\<cal-H\>>+L<rsub|\<cal-O\>\<setminus\>\<cal-H\>>>.

  Choose <math|L> large enough for the fine estimate and
  <math|C<rsub|1>/L<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>\<lambda\><rsub|f>/4>, and use
  <eqref|eq:seed-fine-reserve> with <math|c<rsub|F>=C<rsub|1>/L<rsup|2>>;
  this is the coefficient of the fine linear expense in
  <eqref|eq:seed-hit-input>. Choose <math|W> large enough for the predictor
  estimate, <math|W\<geqslant\>1> and <math|C<rsub|1>\<leqslant\>p*W/16>.
  Choose

  <\equation*>
    0\<less\>m\<leqslant\>min <around|{|\<lambda\><rsub|f>/8,d<rsub|f>,p/32,a<rsub|c>/64|}>,<space|2em>\<eta\>=m/4.
  </equation*>

  Suppose finally that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:seed-final-smallness><around|(|4*m*K<rsub|0>+Z*B<rsup|4>|)>*t<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>m*t*T,<space|2em>B<rsub|0>*s<rsup|4>*K<rsub|0>*B<rsup|4>\<leqslant\><around|(|m/4|)>*t<rsup|2>.
  </equation>

  Adding <eqref|eq:seed-fine-reserve>\U<eqref|eq:seed-unhit-input> cancels
  the three explicit linear expenses: the term in <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|2>>
  against its counterpart in <eqref|eq:seed-coarse-reserve>, and the fine and
  predictor terms against the <math|c<rsub|F>> and <math|C<rsub|1>> charges
  of <eqref|eq:seed-fine-reserve> and <eqref|eq:seed-predictor-reserve>. The
  first inequality in <eqref|eq:seed-final-smallness> leaves
  <math|4*m*K<rsub|0>*t<rsup|2>*d<rsub|A>> from the very-large-defect
  reserve, and the second pays the small coarse rows. Thus the assigned
  energy is at least

  <\equation*>
    m*t<rsup|2>*<around|(|<around|\||\<cal-F\>|\|>+<around|\||\<cal-I\>|\|>|)>+4*m*t<rsup|2>*<around|(|<around|\||\<cal-C\><rsub|1>|\|>+K<rsub|0>*d<rsub|A>|)>-<tfrac|m|4>t<rsup|2>*n-<tfrac|m|4>t<rsup|2>*n\<geqslant\><tfrac|m|2>t<rsup|2>*n.
  </equation*>

  The last step is the flag count in <eqref|eq:seed-geometric-counts>. This
  is the required positive gain per seed, with <math|\<mu\>=m/2>.

  <subsubsection|The order of choices and orientation average>

  For the actual thresholds put

  <\equation*>
    t=<frac|P<around|(|h|)>|<sqrt|h>>,<space|2em>s=<frac|P<around|(|x|)>|<sqrt|x>>,<space|2em>T=h<rsup|-49/100>,<space|2em>h=x+c<rsub|L>.
  </equation*>

  As <math|x\<to\>\<infty\>> at fixed <math|L>,

  <\equation*>
    s\<to\>0,<space|1em>t/s\<to\>1,<space|1em>B<rsup|r>*<space|0.17em>t/T\<to\>0,<space|1em>B<rsup|r>*s\<to\>0<space|1em><text|for
    every fixed >r\<geqslant\>0.
  </equation*>

  For the ratio <math|t/s>, subtract the logarithms: <math|log
  t=<around|(|log log h|)><rsup|2>-<frac|1|2>*log h> and the corresponding
  formula at <math|x>. Their difference tends to zero under a fixed shift, by
  the mean value theorem, since the derivative of <math|<around|(|log log
  x|)><rsup|2>-<frac|1|2>*log x> tends to zero. The other statements follow
  from <math|B> and <math|P> growing slower than every positive power, using
  <math|-1/2+49/100=-1/100> for <math|t/T>. It follows that
  <math|0\<less\>s\<leqslant\>\<delta\>>, <math|t\<leqslant\>2*s>,
  <math|t\<leqslant\>T>, and both inequalities
  <eqref|eq:seed-final-smallness> hold eventually. For the second, divide by
  <math|t<rsup|2>> and use <math|s/t\<to\>1> and
  <math|B<rsup|4>*s<rsup|2>\<to\>0>. All choices have therefore been made in
  the order

  <\equation*>
    <text|fixed group and row data>;L<text| large>;W<text|
    large>;\<mu\>\<gtr\>0;x<text| large>,
  </equation*>

  with no dependence on the lattice period in the last threshold.

  The packet construction uses an average of the assigned energies over
  signed coordinate transformations. Here the block side is odd, so the
  parent map commutes with the signed coordinate action. We use the patch's
  signed covariance laws for the predictor and the local likelihoods. Turn
  the tree, seed set and tag array together with the fields. Tree edges
  remain equal to the group identity; the predictor and likelihood transform
  covariantly; distances, chords and the number of seeds are preserved. The
  local sector conditions of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:geometry-sector-conditions>,
  including the strict positivity (S4) of the retained site likelihoods,
  therefore hold in every transformed channel. Here the incidence convention
  of that definition is used: a signed coordinate map sends the corners of a
  fine plaquette (the endpoints of a coarse bond) to the corners (endpoints)
  of its image and commutes with the parent map, so an address meets the
  transformed seed neighbourhood if and only if its preimage meets the
  original one. An anchor-based formulation would not be invariant, because a
  reflection changes the anchor of a positively oriented plaquette or bond.
  Applying the same energy bound there and averaging with normalized weights
  yields <math|Q<rsub|A><rsup|<math-up|core>>\<geqslant\>\<mu\>*t<rsup|2>*n>.
  This supplies the core input to <eqref|eq:packet-assigned-energy> once the
  geometric, guide and comparison inputs specified in this section have been
  established. The average is of energies on transformed copies of the same
  data; there is no independent choice of trees or tags between channels.

  <subsection|The local annular remainder><label|sec:packet-annulus>

  This section proves the annular estimate used in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:packet-estimates>. The comparison is made
  before Gaussian integration, on the actual fine configuration, coarse
  configuration and predictor tags. In particular, removing a remote cutoff
  does not permit us to replace the predictor by a newly chosen one. The
  argument needs only the local conditions stated below.

  Fix the block length <math|L>, the predictor patch, the positive direct
  coefficients <math|a<rsub|f>,a<rsub|c>>, and the predictor penalty
  <math|p\<gtr\>0>. Retain the exponent convention of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:packet-estimates>, with large parameter
  <math|h=h<around|(|x|)>>. Put <math|t=P<around|(|h|)>/<sqrt|h>>; this is
  the fluctuation scale written <math|\<tau\>> in <eqref|eq:warning-scales>
  and in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:packet-estimates> (not the coordinate
  warning threshold called <math|t> there). Let <math|s> be the coarse
  threshold, and let <math|B> and <math|R> be the shell and truncation radii.
  We use <math|s\<leqslant\>2*t>, <math|R=<around|\<lfloor\>|B/100000|\<rfloor\>>>,
  and the eventual geometric inequality

  <\equation*>
    10000*<around|(|\<rho\>+R<rsub|<math-up|macro>>+L+10|)>\<leqslant\>B,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<rho\>> is the predictor reach. In addition, the coarse period
  <math|c> is assumed to satisfy

  <\equation*>
    100*<around|(|1+B|)>\<leqslant\>c.
  </equation*>

  This is not an eventual inequality: <math|c> is arbitrary, and the
  condition is a standing hypothesis on the period, part of the funded-step
  condition under which the packet estimates are applied. Both <math|B> and
  <math|R> have upper exponent zero, whereas <math|e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*R>> has
  every negative upper exponent for fixed <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0>. These
  properties follow from <math|B\<asymp\><around|(|log x|)><rsup|2>>. All
  thresholds in this section are chosen after the fixed local data; all
  estimates are uniform over all periods <math|c> with
  <math|100*<around|(|1+B|)>\<leqslant\>c>.

  The incoming linear data are the path and kernel bounds of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-guides>, the flat identities of
  <eqref|eq:unhit-flat-stack>, and the additional orthogonality

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:annulus-null-row>N<rsub|0>*B<rsub|c>*d<rsub|c>=0.
  </equation>

  The last identity is the null-row part of the same flat decomposition. Its
  construction, like that of the flat identities, belongs to the flat-stack
  input. We will identify exactly where it is used.

  <subsubsection|A common local chart>

  Call a coarse patch clean when no coarse flag occurs there. On a clean
  patch every plaquette chord is at most <math|s>. The axial gauge argument
  in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-tree-patch>, applied on a box of
  radius <math|d+1> with <math|d\<leqslant\>32*B>, gives a gauge in which the
  bond chords on the smaller box are bounded by

  <\equation*>
    u<rsub|0>=16*<around|{|2*<around|(|32*B+1|)>+1|}><rsup|5>*N<rsub|<math-up|plaq>>*<around|(|2*t|)>.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|u<rsub|0>> has upper exponent <math|-1/2>. For large <math|x>
  these bonds are in the logarithm chart. Take their logarithms and set all
  other bond logarithms to zero. This gives a full array <math|b> with
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|b|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C*u<rsub|0>>
  whose exponential agrees with the gauged coarse configuration on the
  required patch. No assertion about the coarse configuration outside that
  patch is needed.

  Write <math|V<rsup|\<uparrow\>>> for the block replica of <math|V>.
  Suppose, on a patch about <math|o>, the fine configuration and a colour
  array <math|u> satisfy

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:annulus-graft>U<rsub|e>=exp
    <around|(|u<rsub|e>|)>*V<rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|e>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|e>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>w<around|(|x|)>,
  </equation>

  where <math|w\<geqslant\>0> has upper exponent <math|-1/2>. Under the
  block-constant lift of the coarse gauge, <math|u<rsub|e>> is conjugated at
  its initial point; its norm is unchanged. Since a replica bond is either
  the identity or one coarse bond, the chord triangle inequality gives

  <\equation*>
    r<around|(|U<rsub|e>|)>\<leqslant\>C*w+C*u<rsub|0>.
  </equation*>

  Consequently both fields have small bonds in this same gauge, with a bound
  <math|H<around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>w<around|(|x|)>> of upper exponent
  <math|-1/2>. The coarse logarithm extension also has norm at most <math|H>
  after enlarging <math|H>.

  For any prescribed <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>, all predictor reads on a
  sufficiently small such patch lie in the minimizing branch and have chord
  at most <math|\<delta\>>. Here is the uniformity argument. At the identity
  configuration the normalized minimizing frames and the best connector are
  the identity. Their continuity was proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:predictor-branch>. The branch inequalities
  are strict there. The finitely many frame tags, connector tags and axes
  therefore have a common neighbourhood on which these inequalities hold and
  the predictor chord is less than <math|\<delta\>>. In the product topology
  on cover configurations, this neighbourhood contains a cylinder restricting
  finitely many bonds. Enclose these bonds in one fixed patch and choose one
  positive chord radius for all of them. Every torus configuration whose
  lifted patch satisfies these restrictions belongs to the same
  neighbourhood, independently of its period. For large <math|x> the patch
  fits within <math|B/2> and its bonds satisfy the restrictions. In
  particular, the normalized tag likelihoods are exactly one there: in the
  minimizing branch the frame is the selected minimizer, and each frame tag
  has probability the reciprocal of the total number of frame tags. The
  four-link triangle inequality also makes every touched fine plaquette chord
  at most <math|\<delta\>>. These statements hold for the original tags,
  including tags retained after remote masks were erased.

  <subsubsection|Affine approximation at the actual coarse field>

  Let <math|\<cal-C\>*<around|(|U,V|)>> be the row field consisting of the
  two fine curvature rows and the predictor row:

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-C\>*<around|(|U,V|)>=<around*|(|X<rsub|f><around|(|U|)>,<sqrt|p>*<space|0.17em>F*<around|(|V*Q<around|(|U|)><rsup|-1>|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|X<rsub|f>> includes the truncated path row and the direct
  curvature row, and <math|Q> is the actual predictor. Each row norm is the
  physical colour norm at one address; an array norm is its supremum over
  addresses. No whole-volume Euclidean norm is used in these estimates. Its
  full linearization is

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-C\><rsup|0>*<around|(|a,b|)>=<around*|(|B<rsub|f>*d<rsub|f>*a,<sqrt|p>*<space|0.17em><around|(|b-\<cal-Q\>*a|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The chart estimates of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-unhit-comparison>
  give, for <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>>,<around|\<\|\|\>|b|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>H>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:annulus-origin-error><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-C\><rsup|0>*<around|(|a,b|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C*H,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-C\>*<around|(|e<rsup|a>,e<rsup|b>|)>-\<cal-C\><rsup|0>*<around|(|a,b|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C*H*<around|{|<around|(|1+R|)>*H+e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*R>|}>.
  </equation>

  The predictor term has a quadratic error; the path row has the displayed
  additional truncation error. The right side has upper exponent <math|-1>.

  Define <math|A<rsub|\<infty\>>*a=\<cal-C\><rsup|0>*<around|(|a,0|)>>, and
  let <math|A<rsub|R>> be its truncated version. Absolute exponential
  summability of the path coefficients and the four-bond curl estimate give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:annulus-linear-rows><around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|\<infty\>>*a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|R>*a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C<around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|A<rsub|\<infty\>>-A<rsub|R>|)>*a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C<around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*R>.
  </equation>

  Indeed, each face curl has norm at most
  <math|4<around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>. For a discarded path
  of length <math|\<ell\>\<gtr\>R>, insert
  <math|e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*R>*e<rsup|\<sigma\>*\<ell\>>\<geqslant\>1> into its
  absolute coefficient bound and sum. The predictor part is local and is not
  truncated.

  Let <math|A<rsub|R><rsup|V>> be the actual transported row: input colours
  are parallel transported along the prescribed coarse routes before the same
  cut linear row is applied. If the coarse bonds along these routes have
  chord at most <math|h<rsub|0>>, and the routes have length at most
  <math|d>, then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:annulus-transport-error><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|A<rsub|R><rsup|V>-A<rsub|R>|)>*v|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*C*D<rsub|<math-up|col>>*<space|0.17em>d*h<rsub|0><space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>.
  </equation>

  The route product has chord at most <math|d*h<rsub|0>> by the triangle
  inequality. The bound <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|Ad><around|(|g|)>-1|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*D<rsub|<math-up|col>>*r<around|(|g|)>>
  then bounds each changed input, and <eqref|eq:annulus-linear-rows> bounds
  the resulting row. Only inputs within distance <math|R+\<rho\>+4> occur;
  <math|4*<around|(|R+\<rho\>+4|)>\<leqslant\>d> ensures the routes remain in
  the controlled patch. One may set all other input differences to zero to
  apply the supremum-norm bound.

  We claim that, under <eqref|eq:annulus-graft> on a clean patch of radius
  <math|B/2>, there are nonnegative functions <math|M,E>, of upper exponents
  <math|-1/2> and <math|-1> respectively, such that at its central row

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:annulus-affine-error><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-C\>*<around|(|U,V|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>M,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-C\>*<around|(|U,V|)>-\<cal-C\>*<around|(|V<rsup|\<uparrow\>>,V|)>-A<rsub|R><rsup|V>*u|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>E.
  </equation>

  To prove this, work in the common gauge above and restrict to the smaller
  radius <math|d=<around|\<lfloor\>|B/4|\<rfloor\>>>. Extend <math|u> by zero
  outside this patch, obtaining <math|v> with
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>H>. Let
  <math|a> be the linear replica of the coarse logarithm extension <math|b>;
  then <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>H> and
  <math|e<rsup|a>=<around|(|e<rsup|b>|)><rsup|\<uparrow\>>>.

  The local product logarithm satisfies

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|w<rsub|e>>=e<rsup|v<rsub|e>>*e<rsup|a<rsub|e>>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|w-<around|(|v+a|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>J*H<rsup|2>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>J<rprime|'>*H.
  </equation*>

  For completeness, <math|<around|(|v,a|)>\<mapsto\>log
  <around|(|e<rsup|v>*e<rsup|a>|)>> is analytic near zero, vanishes at zero
  and has derivative <math|<around|(|v,a|)>\<mapsto\>v+a>. Integrate its
  second derivative on a smaller closed ball to obtain the quadratic
  remainder. The first-derivative bound gives the last inequality, bond by
  bond, with constants independent of the number of bonds. Locality
  identifies the actual central rows with those at
  <math|<around|(|e<rsup|w>,e<rsup|b>|)>> and
  <math|<around|(|e<rsup|a>,e<rsup|b>|)>>.

  Put <math|z=\<cal-C\><rsup|0>*<around|(|w,b|)>> and
  <math|z<rsub|0>=\<cal-C\><rsup|0>*<around|(|a,b|)>>. Linearity gives the
  exact decomposition

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-C\>*<around|(|U,V|)>-\<cal-C\>*<around|(|V<rsup|\<uparrow\>>,V|)>-A<rsub|R><rsup|V>*u=>|<cell|\<cal-C\>*<around|(|U,V|)>-z+A<rsub|\<infty\>>*<around|(|w-v-a|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|+z<rsub|0>-\<cal-C\>*<around|(|V<rsup|\<uparrow\>>,V|)>+<around|(|A<rsub|\<infty\>>-A<rsub|R>|)>*v+<around|(|A<rsub|R>-A<rsub|R><rsup|V>|)>*v.>>>>
  </align*>

  The equality uses locality to replace <math|u> by <math|v> in the
  transported row. Equations <eqref|eq:annulus-origin-error>\U
  <eqref|eq:annulus-transport-error> bound these five terms by

  <\equation*>
    2*E<rsub|0>+C*J*H<rsup|2>+C*H*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*R>+C<rprime|'>*B*H<rsup|2>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|E<rsub|0>> has upper exponent <math|-1>. This proves the error
  bound in <eqref|eq:annulus-affine-error>; the bound on the row itself
  follows from its linear bound plus <math|E<rsub|0>>. Gauge covariance of
  all three actual rows and invariance of the colour norm return the estimate
  to the original gauge.

  <subsubsection|Subtracting the selected squares>

  Let <math|g> be the coarse guide row and assume the already established
  guide bound <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>A*t>. Define the
  actual and affine residuals

  <\equation*>
    v=\<cal-C\>*<around|(|U,V|)>-g,<space|2em>v<rsub|0>=\<cal-C\>*<around|(|V<rsup|\<uparrow\>>,V|)>-g+A<rsub|R><rsup|V>*u.
  </equation*>

  They satisfy <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>m=M+A*t> and
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v-v<rsub|0>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>E>. The elementary
  identity for a difference of squares implies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:annulus-square-error><around*|\||<frac|<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>|2>-<frac|<around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|0>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>|2>|\|>\<leqslant\>Z\<assign\><frac|E*<around|(|2*m+E|)>|2>.
  </equation>

  Indeed <math|<around|\||<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>-<around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|0>|\<\|\|\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>E>
  and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|0>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>m+E>. Hence
  <math|Z> has upper exponent <math|-3/2>.

  Write <math|<math-up|flaw><around|(|q|)>=<around|(|r<around|(|q|)><rsup|2>-<around|\<\|\|\>|F<around|(|q|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>|)>/2>.
  The curvature argument in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-energy> gives

  <\equation*>
    0\<leqslant\><math-up|flaw><around|(|q|)>\<leqslant\>K*r<around|(|q|)><rsup|4>/2,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|F<around|(|q|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>3*r<around|(|q|)><rsup|2>/4
  </equation*>

  for small <math|r<around|(|q|)>>. The branch and plaquette bounds above put
  the required arguments in this neighbourhood. Since the appropriate direct
  component of <math|\<cal-C\>> has norm at most <math|M>,

  <\equation*>
    r<around|(|q<rsub|f>|)>\<leqslant\>2*M/<sqrt|a<rsub|f>>,<space|2em>r<around|(|q<rsub|I>|)>\<leqslant\>2*M/<sqrt|p>.
  </equation*>

  Thus their flaw contributions are bounded by

  <\equation*>
    X=a<rsub|f>*<around|(|K/2|)>*<around|(|2*M/<sqrt|a<rsub|f>>|)><rsup|4>,<space|2em>Y=p*<around|(|K/2|)>*<around|(|2*M/<sqrt|p>|)><rsup|4>,
  </equation*>

  both of upper exponent <math|-2>.

  On a kept fine row below its threshold the fine energy is exactly

  <\equation*>
    a<rsub|f><math-up|flaw><around|(|q<rsub|f>|)>+<frac|1|2>*<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|1><around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|f,j>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  Expand the squares to verify this: their cross terms are the guide
  pairings, and their direct curvature square together with
  <math|a<rsub|f><math-up|flaw>> restores
  <math|a<rsub|f>*r<around|(|q<rsub|f>|)><rsup|2>/2>. The predictor energy
  has, without a kept condition, the exact form

  <\equation*>
    p<math-up|flaw><around|(|q<rsub|I>|)>+<around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|I>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>/2.
  </equation*>

  It follows from <eqref|eq:annulus-square-error> that subtracting the two
  affine fine squares costs at most <math|X+2*Z> in absolute value, and
  subtracting the predictor square costs at most <math|Y+Z>. One common
  nonnegative bound is <math|X+Y+2*Z>, of upper exponent <math|-3/2>. At an
  unselected fine or predictor address no square is subtracted. Its
  corresponding displayed energy is nonnegative. This gives the required
  one-sided bound there, without asserting an absolute bound on an unselected
  row.

  <subsubsection|The remaining coarse rows>

  Three further terms occur in the assigned energy. First, on a coarse
  plaquette with <math|r\<leqslant\>s>, the coarse counterterm is

  <\equation*>
    <frac|a<rsub|c>|2>*<around|{|<around|\<\|\|\>|F<around|(|q|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>-r<around|(|q|)><rsup|2>|}>.
  </equation*>

  It is nonpositive and its absolute value is at most
  <math|a<rsub|c>*K*s<rsup|4>/2>, of upper exponent <math|-2>.

  Second, consider a guide kernel <math|K<around|(|V|)>> at one central row.
  On the clean patch its clipped coarse field agrees with the ordinary
  truncated curvature field at every address touched by this row: the
  curvature is below threshold, and the exponential kept test is automatic
  outside the smaller radius where it needs checking. Kernel locality and the
  local agreement <math|V=e<rsup|b>> then give the exact central-row equality

  <\equation*>
    <around|[|K<around|(|V|)><math-up|vision><around|(|V|)>|]><rsub|i>=<around|[|K<around|(|e<rsup|b>|)>*X<rsub|c><around|(|e<rsup|b>|)>|]><rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  This equality is taken before any supremum norm is used. Add and subtract
  <math|K<around|(|e<rsup|b>|)>*X<rsub|c><rsup|0><around|(|b|)>> to obtain

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||<around|[|K<around|(|V|)><math-up|vision><around|(|V|)>-K<rsub|0>*X<rsub|c><rsup|0><around|(|b|)>|]><rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*<around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|c><around|(|e<rsup|b>|)>-X<rsub|c><rsup|0><around|(|b|)>*<around|\<\|\|\>|<rsub|\<infty\>>+C*\<omega\>*<around|(|R,H|)>|\<\|\|\>>*X<rsub|c><rsup|0><around|(|b|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<omega\>*<around|(|R,H|)>=<around|(|1+R|)>*H+e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*R>>.
  The row bounds in <eqref|eq:unhit-kernel-near> justify this inequality. The
  globally small extension <math|b> permits both supremum estimates on the
  right; no global bound on the original <math|V> is required. The right side
  has upper exponent <math|-1>. For the null row <math|K=N>, its full linear
  comparator vanishes by <eqref|eq:annulus-null-row>. The null energy, half
  the square of this row, consequently has upper exponent <math|-2>. This is
  the only extra flat identity used here.

  Finally, the linear and quadratic mismatch rows are precisely the gaps
  estimated in <eqref|eq:unhit-global>. The common local gauge and locality
  argument used there apply to the present grafted configuration, with
  smallness bound <math|H> of upper exponent <math|-1/2>. Pairing the linear
  gap with a guide of size <math|A*t> gives at most
  <math|A*t*<space|0.17em>C*H*\<omega\>*<around|(|R,H|)>> for each of the two
  fine-row channels. Pairing the quadratic gap with that guide gives at most
  <math|A*t*<space|0.17em>C*A*t*<space|0.17em>\<omega\>*<around|(|R,H|)>/2>
  per channel. Hence their combined absolute cost is at most

  <\equation*>
    2*A*t*<space|0.17em>C*H*\<omega\>*<around|(|R,H|)>+<around|(|A*t|)><rsup|2>*C*\<omega\>*<around|(|R,H|)>,
  </equation*>

  of upper exponent <math|-3/2>. Adding all row costs gives one nonnegative
  function <math|r<around|(|x|)>> of upper exponent <math|-3/2>.

  <subsubsection|The protected annulus of an actual packet>

  We verify the local hypotheses on the packet itself. Its fine configuration
  is formed in a fixed tree slice. On active free bonds it is <math|exp
  <around|(|u<rsub|e>|)>*V<rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|e>>; on frozen free bonds
  it is the fixed slice value <math|Y<rsub|e>>; on tree bonds it is the
  identity. Let <math|K\<gtr\>0> be a fixed shell constant and define

  <\equation*>
    \<ell\><rsub|K><around|(|q|)>=\<chi\><around*|(|<around|(|r<around|(|q|)>/<around|(|K*t|)>|)><rsup|2>|)>*log
    q,
  </equation*>

  where the smooth cutoff equals one for <math|r<around|(|q|)>\<leqslant\>K*t>
  and vanishes for <math|r<around|(|q|)>\<geqslant\>2*K*t>. The local
  logarithm estimate gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<ell\><rsub|K><around|(|q|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*\<gamma\>*K*t>
  everywhere. This assertion also holds outside the chart because the cutoff
  is zero there; choose <math|x> so that the closed ball
  <math|r<around|(|q|)>\<leqslant\>2*K*t> lies in the logarithm chart. The
  resulting map is smooth wherever its group argument varies smoothly, by the
  same two local cases.

  Define the actual colour signal <math|<wide|u|~>> to be <math|u> on active
  bonds, <math|\<ell\><rsub|K><around|(|Y<rsub|e><around|(|V<rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|e>|)><rsup|-1>|)>>
  on frozen free bonds, and zero on tree bonds. On a weak interior patch the
  fine plaquettes have chord at most <math|t/2>, the predictor mismatch at
  most <math|2*W*t>, and the selected frames have positive likelihood. The
  tree comparison <eqref|eq:tree-shell> then gives

  <\equation*>
    r<around|(|U<rsub|e><around|(|V<rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|e>|)><rsup|-1>|)>\<leqslant\>K*t,<space|2em>K=C*<space|0.17em>N<rsub|<math-up|macro>>/2+2*W,
  </equation*>

  where <math|N<rsub|<math-up|macro>>> counts the fine plaquettes in the
  fixed macro patch. The frozen cutoff is therefore one and its logarithm
  exponentiates to the original mismatch. On tree bonds both <math|U<rsub|e>>
  and <math|V<rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|e>> are the identity. Thus on this patch

  <\equation*>
    U<rsub|e>=exp <around|(|<wide|u|~><rsub|e>|)>*V<rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|e>.
  </equation*>

  If the active variables there obey <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|e>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>w<around|(|x|)>>
  and <math|2*\<gamma\>*K*t\<leqslant\>w<around|(|x|)>>, the signal obeys the
  same bound on all bonds. This proves <eqref|eq:annulus-graft> for the
  actual signal.

  Let <math|A> be the packet's seed set, and consider an energy address whose
  coarse anchor belongs to its <math|200*B> neighbourhood but not its
  <math|2*B> neighbourhood. Retain the weak fine, predictor and coarse flag
  conditions through <math|300*B>, and the bounds on active variables through
  <math|304*B>. For a bond within <math|B> of this anchor, its <math|B/4>
  interior patch lies within the <math|300*B> neighbourhood and outside the
  seeds. This follows from the triangle inequality: the outer distance is at
  most <math|201*B+B/4\<less\>300*B>, whereas entering the seeds would put
  the anchor within <math|B+B/4\<less\>2*B> of them. The retained conditions
  therefore give the weak interior just used. The active-variable condition
  is available since <math|201*B\<less\>304*B>. The same argument supplies a
  clean coarse patch at the anchor.

  Signed changes of orientation preserve these conclusions, allowing one
  additional bond of radius for reversed bonds. Norms are unchanged by the
  associated conjugations. Finally, the fine kept test holds on this annulus:
  a plaquette whose test is nontrivial has distance at most <math|B/25> by
  the exponential threshold bound; the weak interior makes its chord at most
  <math|t/2\<leqslant\>t*e<rsup|<math-up|dist>/r<rsub|0>>>. At all other
  plaquettes the right side is at least the universal chord bound <math|2>.
  Thus the actual fine energy equals the square expression used above,
  including at threshold equality.

  At every selected address the extracted affine square is the square of the
  actual signal row with the same tags, orientations and coarse field. At
  every unselected address it is zero. We have therefore proved, on the
  protected annulus,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:annulus-final><text|actual assigned energy at the
    address>\<geqslant\>-r<around|(|x|)>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|r\<geqslant\>0> has upper exponent <math|-3/2>. Summing over its
  at most <math|C*B<rsup|4>> addresses per seed and averaging over the signed
  channels gives exactly the annular input of
  <eqref|eq:packet-assigned-energy>. This proves the value estimate;
  derivative bounds for the density, covariance and chart factors remain
  separate inputs to the packet jet estimate.

  <subsection|The Gaussian quadratic form in a sector><label|sec:sector-gram>

  The quadratic reference must remain nondegenerate after the large-field
  bonds have been frozen. A lower bound for a flat Hessian alone would not
  establish this. We prove the lower bound for the transported-flat selected
  rows <math|A<rsup|V><rsub|s,a>> defined below (not for the curved
  derivative rows, which are treated in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-global-row-prices>),
  using a separate local gauge around each active bond. All norms in the
  quadratic inequalities below are physical counting norms: squared colour
  norms are summed over bonds without dividing by the volume.

  Fix the tree, seed set, coarse field and original predictor tags of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:packet-annulus>. Let <math|\<cal-H\>> be the
  active free bonds and let <math|P<rsub|\<cal-H\>>> denote restriction to
  them, extended by zero on all other bonds. The seed store's coarse-flag
  component equals the actual coarse flags; it may have other seed
  components. An active bond has both coarse endpoints outside the
  <math|10*B> seed neighbourhood. A row is selected when its anchor is
  outside the <math|6*B> seed neighbourhood. These are fixed selections for
  this quadratic form.

  Let <math|q> be the number of signed channels and write
  <math|\<lambda\>=q<rsup|-1/2>>. For a channel <math|s> and a fine or
  predictor row <math|a>, set

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|s,a>*u=<with|font-series|bold|1><rsub|<around|{|<around|(|s,a|)><text|selected>|}>>*A<rsup|V><rsub|s,a>*P<rsub|\<cal-H\>>*u.
  </equation*>

  The map <math|A<rsup|V><rsub|s,a>> is the flat linear cut row transported
  using <math|V>, including the signed displacement of input colours. It is
  not, in general, the derivative at the replica of <math|V> of the nonlinear
  curvature or predictor. In orthonormal colour coordinates, define <math|M>
  by

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|M*u|)><rsub|s,a>=\<lambda\>*D<rsub|s,a>*u.
  </equation*>

  Here and below a colour array and its scalar coordinate vector are
  identified. Define the completed precision matrix

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:sector-precision>A=M<rsup|\<ast\>>*M+P<rsub|\<cal-H\><rsup|c>>.
  </equation>

  Every column of <math|M> indexed by an inactive bond is zero. Consequently
  <math|A> acts as the identity on the inactive coordinates and has no cross
  terms between them and the active coordinates. The completion supplies no
  energy on the active subspace.

  If <math|b> is the vector of selected affine rows at zero fluctuation,
  including the same factor <math|\<lambda\>>, linearity of the transported
  rows gives

  <\equation*>
    Q<rsub|<math-up|quad>><around|(|u|)>=<tfrac|1|2><around|\<\|\|\>|b+M*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  This is exactly the orientation average of the extracted squares:
  <math|\<lambda\><rsup|2>=1/q> supplies its normalization. In particular,

  <\equation*>
    Q<rsub|<math-up|quad>>*<around|(|v+u|)>-Q<rsub|<math-up|quad>><around|(|v|)>=<around|\<langle\>|u,M<rsup|\<ast\>>*b+M<rsup|\<ast\>>*M*v|\<rangle\>>+<tfrac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|u,M<rsup|\<ast\>>*M*u|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation*>

  The same fine field, tags and orientation choices are used in every term.
  We have not averaged independently chosen Gaussian matrices.

  <subsubsection|A local linear inequality from the tree comparison>

  Let <math|w> be a fine colour array which vanishes on the fixed tree. The
  tree comparison of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-tree-patch>, with
  coarse configuration equal to the identity, implies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:sector-local-linear><around|\<\|\|\>|w<rsub|e>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>K*<big|sum><rsub|z\<in\>\<cal-B\>><big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|d<rsub|f>*w|)><rsub|<wide|z|\<bar\>>+L*o,\<alpha\>>|\<\|\|\>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|\<cal-Q\>*w|)><rsub|o,a>|\<\|\|\>>,<space|2em>o=<math-up|parent><around|(|e|)>,
  </equation>

  where <math|a> is the axis of <math|e>, <math|\<cal-B\>> is the fixed macro
  box, and <math|\<cal-Q\>> is the derivative of the actual predictor. The
  box sum may count periodic images more than once; this only enlarges its
  nonnegative right side.

  Here is the passage to the linear inequality. Apply the nonlinear
  comparison to <math|U<rsub|e><around|(|\<varepsilon\>|)>=exp
  <around|(|\<varepsilon\>*w<rsub|e>|)>>. It remains in the same tree slice
  since <math|w> vanishes on the tree. For sufficiently small
  <math|\<varepsilon\>> all required frame likelihoods are positive: at zero
  every frame is in the minimizing branch, and the finitely many conditions
  are open. Divide the comparison by <math|<around|\||\<varepsilon\>|\|>> and
  let <math|\<varepsilon\>\<to\>0> through nonzero real values. If
  <math|g<around|(|0|)>=1> and its matrix derivative is the colour matrix of
  <math|v>, then

  <\equation*>
    lim<rsub|\<varepsilon\>\<to\>0> <frac|r<around|(|g<around|(|\<varepsilon\>|)>|)>|<around|\||\<varepsilon\>|\|>>=<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>.
  </equation*>

  This follows directly from differentiability and the physical normalization
  of the chord. The derivatives of the fine links, plaquettes and predictor
  are respectively <math|w>, <math|d<rsub|f>*w> and <math|\<cal-Q\>*w>, the
  last by Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:predictor-branch>. Passing through
  the finite box sum proves <eqref|eq:sector-local-linear>. The limiting
  neighbourhood can depend on the finite torus; the resulting constant
  <math|K>, inherited from the nonlinear tree inequality, does not.

  <subsubsection|A lower bound for the transported selected rows>

  For an anchor <math|o>, define

  <\equation*>
    <\eqsplit>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|J<rsub|o><around|(|u|)><rsup|2>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|<math-up|dist><around|(|o,<math-up|parent><around|(|e|)>|)>\<leqslant\>3*B><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|P<rsub|\<cal-H\>>*u|)><rsub|e>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>,>>|<row|<cell|R<rsub|o><around|(|u|)><rsup|2>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|<math-up|dist><around|(|o,<math-up|anchor><around|(|s,a|)>|)>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|<math-up|macro>>+2><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsub|s,a>*u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>.>>>>
    </eqsplit>
  </equation*>

  The square roots are nonnegative. An active bond at <math|o> is more than
  <math|10*B> from the seeds. Since the macro radius is much smaller than
  <math|B>, every row with anchor within <math|R<rsub|<math-up|macro>>+2> of
  <math|o> is selected in the identity signed channel. Also the coarse patch
  of radius <math|2*B+1> is clean, because the coarse flags belong to the
  seeds. The local axial construction gives a gauge <math|g> with

  <\equation*>
    r<around|(|<around|(|g\<cdot\>V|)><rsub|e>|)>\<leqslant\>h<rsub|0><around|(|x|)><space|1em><text|on
    radius >2*B,
  </equation*>

  where <math|h<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0> has upper exponent <math|-1/2>.

  Apply <eqref|eq:sector-local-linear> to
  <math|w=<math-up|Ad><around|(|g|)>*P<rsub|\<cal-H\>>*u>. This array still
  vanishes on the tree. For every required row, the transport estimate
  <eqref|eq:annulus-transport-error> and gauge covariance give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:sector-flat-row-control><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|A<rsub|R>*w|)><rsub|a>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|o><around|(|u|)>+\<varepsilon\><around|(|x|)>*J<rsub|o><around|(|u|)>,<space|2em>\<varepsilon\><around|(|x|)>=C*<space|0.17em>2*D<rsub|<math-up|col>>*<around|(|B+1|)>*h<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation>

  Indeed the transported row in the chosen gauge has the same norm as its
  selected original row and is at most <math|R<rsub|o><around|(|u|)>>. Every
  input used in the comparison lies within <math|3*B> of <math|o>, so its
  norm is at most <math|J<rsub|o><around|(|u|)>>. All comparison routes lie
  inside the radius <math|2*B> patch. The identity signed channel suffices
  for this lower bound; the sum defining <math|R<rsub|o>> may include all the
  other channels.

  The direct fine component of <math|A<rsub|R>*w> is
  <math|<sqrt|a<rsub|f>>*<space|0.17em>d<rsub|f>*w>, and its predictor
  component is <math|-<sqrt|p>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-Q\>*w>. Writing
  <math|N<rsub|<math-up|macro>>=<around|\||\<cal-B\>|\|>*N<rsub|<math-up|plaq>>>,
  insert <eqref|eq:sector-flat-row-control> into
  <eqref|eq:sector-local-linear>. We obtain

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:sector-bond-control><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|P<rsub|\<cal-H\>>*u|)><rsub|e>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>Z*R<rsub|o><around|(|u|)>+E<around|(|x|)>*J<rsub|o><around|(|u|)>,<space|1em>Z=K*N<rsub|<math-up|macro>>/<sqrt|a<rsub|f>>+1/<sqrt|p>,<space|1em>E=Z*\<varepsilon\>.
  </equation>

  This also holds on inactive bonds because its left side is zero. <math|Z>
  is fixed after <math|L>; <math|E> has upper exponent <math|-1/2>.

  There are <math|L<rsup|4>*N<rsub|<math-up|axis>>> fine positive bonds per
  coarse site. Let

  <\equation*>
    S<rsub|L><around|(|d|)>=<around|(|2*d+1|)><rsup|4>*L<rsup|4>*N<rsub|<math-up|axis>>.
  </equation*>

  For every nonnegative family <math|f<rsub|a>> anchored on coarse sites,
  changing the order of the two finite sums shows

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|e><big|sum><rsub|<math-up|dist><around|(|<math-up|parent><around|(|e|)>,<math-up|anchor><around|(|a|)>|)>\<leqslant\>d>f<rsub|a>\<leqslant\>S<rsub|L><around|(|d|)>*<big|sum><rsub|a>f<rsub|a>.
  </equation*>

  For fixed <math|a>, at most <math|<around|(|2*d+1|)><rsup|4>> coarse sites
  can occur, each with at most <math|L<rsup|4>*N<rsub|<math-up|axis>>> bonds.
  This remains valid when the neighbourhood wraps around the torus: wrapping
  reduces the number of distinct sites.

  Square <eqref|eq:sector-bond-control>, using
  <math|<around|(|r+s|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2*r<rsup|2>+2*s<rsup|2>>, and sum
  over bonds. The counting bound gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|\<cal-H\>>*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2*Z<rsup|2>*S<rsub|L>*<around|(|R<rsub|<math-up|macro>>+2|)>*<big|sum><rsub|s,a><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsub|s,a>*u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>+2*E<around|(|x|)><rsup|2>*S<rsub|L>*<around|(|3*B|)>*<around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|\<cal-H\>>*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  The last coefficient has upper exponent <math|-1> and is eventually at most
  <math|1/2>. Move it to the left to get

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|\<cal-H\>>*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|\<ast\>>*<big|sum><rsub|s,a><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsub|s,a>*u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>,<space|2em>a<rsub|\<ast\>>=1+4*Z<rsup|2>*S<rsub|L>*<around|(|R<rsub|<math-up|macro>>+2|)>.
  </equation*>

  The added one ensures a strictly positive constant in every case. Since
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|M*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>=\<lambda\><rsup|2>*<big|sum><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsub|s,a>*u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>,
  the orthogonal active/inactive decomposition proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:sector-gram-floor><around|\<langle\>|u,A*u|\<rangle\>>\<geqslant\>\<kappa\><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>,<space|2em>\<kappa\>=min
    <around|{|\<lambda\><rsup|2>/a<rsub|\<ast\>>,1|}>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation>

  This includes an empty active set, in which case <math|A=1>. No globally
  small gauge for <math|V> has been constructed or assumed.

  <subsubsection|Upper bounds with spatial weights>

  We also need a bound independent of the period on the matrix entries and
  their weighted sums. The cut row <math|A<rsub|R>> vanishes on an impulse at
  a bond farther than <math|R+\<rho\>+4> from its anchor. Together with
  <eqref|eq:annulus-linear-rows>, this implies

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|\<sigma\>*d><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|A<rsub|R>*\<delta\><rsub|e>*v|)><rsub|a>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*C*e<rsup|\<sigma\>*<around|(|\<rho\>+6|)>><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>,<space|2em>d=<math-up|dist><around|(|<math-up|anchor><around|(|a|)>,<math-up|parent><around|(|e|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  To see this, if <math|d\<leqslant\>\<rho\>+5> use the uniform cut-row
  bound. Otherwise take <math|r=d-<around|(|\<rho\>+5|)>>, so that
  <math|A<rsub|r>*\<delta\><rsub|e>*v=0>. If <math|R\<less\>r> locality gives
  zero as well. For <math|R\<geqslant\>r>, compare both <math|A<rsub|R>> and
  <math|A<rsub|r>> with <math|A<rsub|\<infty\>>>; the two tails are at most
  <math|C<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*r>> each. This proves
  the stated bound.

  Signed displacement and parallel transport send a single impulse to a
  single impulse with unchanged colour norm. The coarse anchor may move by
  one under reversal of a bond. Thus the preceding constant becomes
  <math|2*C*e<rsup|\<sigma\>*<around|(|\<rho\>+7|)>>> for the transported
  oriented row. Selection and restriction to active bonds only set entries to
  zero. In scalar coordinates,

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|\<sigma\><math-up|dist><around|(|i,j|)>><around|\||M<rsub|i*j>|\|>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|\<ast\>>=\<lambda\>*<space|0.17em>2*C*e<rsup|\<sigma\>*<around|(|\<rho\>+7|)>>.
  </equation*>

  The anchors of input coordinates have multiplicity at most
  <math|m=L<rsup|4>*N<rsub|<math-up|axis>>*q<rsub|<math-up|col>>>, where
  <math|q<rsub|<math-up|col>>> is the colour dimension. Output-coordinate
  anchors have multiplicity at most

  <\equation*>
    n=q*<space|0.17em><around|(|2*L<rsup|4>*N<rsub|<math-up|plaq>>+N<rsub|<math-up|axis>>|)>*q<rsub|<math-up|col>>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0> put <math|P<rsub|0><around|(|\<eta\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|k\<geqslant\>0><around|(|2*k+1|)><rsup|4>*e<rsup|-\<eta\>*k>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
  Grouping coordinates by their distance from an anchor gives, for
  <math|0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>/2>,

  <\equation*>
    sup<rsub|i> <big|sum><rsub|j>e<rsup|r<math-up|dist><around|(|i,j|)>><around|\||M<rsub|i*j>|\|>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|\<ast\>>*m*P<rsub|0>*<around|(|\<sigma\>/2|)>,<space|2em>sup<rsub|j>
    <big|sum><rsub|i>e<rsup|r<math-up|dist><around|(|i,j|)>><around|\||M<rsub|i*j>|\|>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|\<ast\>>*n*P<rsub|0>*<around|(|\<sigma\>/2|)>.
  </equation*>

  Choose one constant <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>> larger than these two bounds and
  one. The triangle inequality for anchor distances proves the product rule
  for such weighted row and column sums. Therefore

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:sector-gram-upper>sup<rsub|i>
    <big|sum><rsub|j>e<rsup|r<math-up|dist><around|(|i,j|)>><around|\||A<rsub|i*j>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|2>+1.
  </equation>

  The extra one is the inactive diagonal. Symmetry gives the same column
  bound. The usual finite Cauchy\USchwarz argument gives
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2\<to\>2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|2>+1>:
  bound <math|<around|\||<around|(|A*u|)><rsub|i>|\|><rsup|2>> by the
  absolute row sum times <math|<big|sum><rsub|j><around|\||A<rsub|i*j>|\|><around|\||u<rsub|j>|\|><rsup|2>>
  and then sum over <math|i>. Also <math|M<rsub|i*j>=0> beyond distance
  <math|B/4> by its stencil, so <math|A<rsub|i*j>=0> beyond <math|B/2>.

  Combining <eqref|eq:sector-gram-floor> and <eqref|eq:sector-gram-upper>
  proves a strictly positive, uniform spectral interval for the
  transported-flat completed precision matrix. In particular its inverse
  exists and satisfies <math|0\<less\>A<rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>\<kappa\><rsup|-1>*1>.
  For <math|\<beta\>\<gtr\>0>, the centered finite-dimensional Gaussian with
  covariance <math|\<beta\><rsup|-1>*A<rsup|-1>> is consequently well
  defined, including when there are no active coordinates. Restricting to the
  active subspace gives the physical Gaussian; the inactive identity
  coordinates are an algebraic completion. Spatial locality of the inverse,
  comparison of local Gaussian normalizations, and control of the affine mean
  require the additional estimates developed at their respective steps.

  <subsection|Local inverses and Gaussian
  normalization><label|sec:local-covariance>

  For comparison, Demko, Moss and Smith <cite-detail|DemkoMossSmith|Theorem<nbsp>2.4>
  prove exponential inverse decay for band matrices from spectral bounds. A
  fixed bandwidth in a linear ordering is unavailable here. We use the
  distance between anchors and exponential row and column sums instead.

  The Gaussian covariance used in the expansion is assembled from local
  principal inverses. It is not the full inverse of the precision matrix. We
  prove its positivity, estimate the resulting error, and express the
  normalization through local terms and convergent correction series. This
  follows the real interpolation from the identity used in the construction.

  Let <math|I> be a finite coordinate set, with anchors <math|a<rsub|i>> on a
  coarse four-dimensional torus. Assume at most <math|m> coordinates have any
  given anchor. For a real matrix <math|T> define

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|T|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<eta\>>=max <around*|{|sup<rsub|i>
    <big|sum><rsub|j>e<rsup|\<eta\><math-up|dist><around|(|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|j>|)>><around|\||T<rsub|i*j>|\|>,sup<rsub|j>
    <big|sum><rsub|i>e<rsup|\<eta\><math-up|dist><around|(|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|j>|)>><around|\||T<rsub|i*j>|\|>|}>.
  </equation*>

  On an empty coordinate set both suprema are zero. The triangle inequality
  for distance and an interchange of finite sums give
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|S*T|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<eta\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|S|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<eta\>><around|\<\|\|\>|T|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<eta\>>>
  for <math|\<eta\>\<geqslant\>0>. Transposition preserves this norm, and
  decreasing <math|\<eta\>> cannot increase it. It dominates the Euclidean
  operator norm by the row-and-column Cauchy\USchwarz estimate used above.

  Let <math|A=A<rsup|\<ast\>>> and suppose

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:covariance-data>A\<geqslant\>r*1,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|A|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<eta\>>\<leqslant\>Q,<space|2em>0\<less\>r\<leqslant\>1,<space|1em>Q\<geqslant\>1,<space|1em>\<eta\>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation>

  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:sector-gram> supplies these constants for
  the actual completed sector precision, after fixing <math|L> and then
  taking the large parameter sufficiently large. Every principal submatrix
  has the same lower bound and weighted estimate: extend a test vector by
  zero for the first assertion, and restrict the nonnegative row and column
  sums for the second.

  <subsubsection|Exponential decay of the inverse>

  The qualitative exponential decay of the inverse is a special case of
  Jaffard's inverse-decay theorem <cite-detail|Jaffard|Proposition<nbsp>2,
  p.<nbsp>464>. His hypothesis requires exponential distance sums to be
  uniformly finite. For the finite coordinate set here, put
  <math|d<rprime|'>*<around|(|i,j|)>=<math-up|dist><around|(|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|j>|)>+\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|i\<ne\>j|}>>>.
  This is a metric, and bounded anchor multiplicity gives

  <\equation*>
    sup<rsub|i> <big|sum><rsub|j>e<rsup|-\<varepsilon\>*d<rprime|'>*<around|(|i,j|)>>\<leqslant\>m*<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>0><around|(|2*n+1|)><rsup|4>*e<rsup|-\<varepsilon\>*n>\<less\>\<infty\>*<space|2em><around|(|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0|)>.
  </equation*>

  Moreover <eqref|eq:covariance-data> gives
  <math|<around|\||A<rsub|i*j>|\|>\<leqslant\>Q*e<rsup|\<eta\>>*e<rsup|-\<eta\>*d<rprime|'>*<around|(|i,j|)>>>
  and makes <math|A> invertible. We prove the explicit estimate below because
  we need constants uniform in the period and in all principal windows; the
  empty case is immediate. Set

  <\equation*>
    \<theta\>=min <around|{|1,r/<around|(|2*Q|)>|}>,<space|2em>\<gamma\>=\<theta\>*\<eta\>,<space|2em>c<rsub|0>=min
    <around|{|1,r/2|}>.
  </equation*>

  For every <math|u\<geqslant\>0>, the matrix <math|A+u*1> is invertible and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:covariance-entry>e<rsup|\<gamma\><math-up|dist><around|(|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|j>|)>><around|\||<around|(|A+u*1|)><rsup|-1><rsub|i*j>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|{|c<rsub|0>*<around|(|1+u|)>|}><rsup|-1>.
  </equation>

  We give the weighted argument, which is uniform in the number of
  coordinates. Fix <math|j> and put <math|s<rsub|i>=e<rsup|\<gamma\><math-up|dist><around|(|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|j>|)>>>.
  For <math|0\<leqslant\>\<theta\>\<leqslant\>1> and
  <math|<around|\||v|\|>\<leqslant\>\<theta\>*D> with <math|D\<geqslant\>0>,
  convexity of the exponential gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||e<rsup|v>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>\<theta\>*e<rsup|D>.
  </equation*>

  For positive <math|v>, use <math|e<rsup|\<theta\>*D>\<leqslant\>1+\<theta\>*<around|(|e<rsup|D>-1|)>>;
  for negative <math|v>, use <math|1-e<rsup|v>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-v>-1>. The
  reverse triangle inequality for distances now implies

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||s<rsub|i>/s<rsub|k>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>\<theta\>*e<rsup|\<eta\><math-up|dist><around|(|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|k>|)>>.
  </equation*>

  Conjugate <math|A+u*1> by the positive diagonal matrix with entries
  <math|s<rsub|i>>. Its difference <math|F> from <math|A+u*1> has entries
  <math|<around|(|A+u*1|)><rsub|i*k>*<around|(|s<rsub|i>/s<rsub|k>-1|)>>. The
  diagonal contribution is zero, so both absolute row and column sums of
  <math|F> are at most <math|\<theta\>*Q\<leqslant\>r/2>. Thus
  <math|<around|\||<around|\<langle\>|w,F*w|\<rangle\>>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|r/2|)><around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>>.

  Let <math|y=<around|(|A+u*1|)><rsup|-1>*e<rsub|j>> and
  <math|w<rsub|i>=s<rsub|i>*y<rsub|i>>. The conjugated equation is
  <math|<around|(|A+u*1+F|)>*w=e<rsub|j>>, since <math|s<rsub|j>=1>. It
  follows that

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|r/2+u|)><around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>w<rsub|j>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>.
  </equation*>

  If <math|w\<ne\>0>, division gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>\<leqslant\><around|(|r/2+u|)><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\><around|{|c<rsub|0>*<around|(|1+u|)>|}><rsup|-1>>;
  the case <math|w=0> is immediate. Each <math|<around|\||w<rsub|i>|\|>> is
  bounded by this norm, proving <eqref|eq:covariance-entry>. Positive
  definiteness gave invertibility before this computation. The same proof
  applies to every principal submatrix.

  Put <math|k=\<gamma\>/3> and

  <\equation*>
    S=c<rsub|0><rsup|-1>*m*P<rsub|0>*<around|(|\<gamma\>-2*k|)>,<space|2em>P<rsub|0><around|(|t|)>=<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>0><around|(|2*n+1|)><rsup|4>*e<rsup|-t*n>.
  </equation*>

  Summing <eqref|eq:covariance-entry> at <math|u=0> with weight
  <math|e<rsup|2*k<math-up|dist>>> gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2*k>\<leqslant\>S>.
  The summation uses the anchor multiplicity <math|m> and the same
  distance-shell count as in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:sector-gram>.

  <subsubsection|Principal windows and their error>

  For each coordinate <math|j>, choose a window <math|W<rsub|j>> containing
  every coordinate within distance <math|B> of <math|a<rsub|j>> and no
  coordinate farther than <math|B+2>. Define <math|P> by its columns:

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|i*j>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|A<rsub|W<rsub|j>><rsup|-1>|)><rsub|i*j>,>|<cell|i\<in\>W<rsub|j>,>>|<row|<cell|0,>|<cell|i\<nin\>W<rsub|j>.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  Indices inside a window mean the corresponding principal coordinates. All
  these inverses exist by <eqref|eq:covariance-data>. Applying
  <eqref|eq:covariance-entry> separately to each column yields the same
  pointwise exponential bound for <math|P>, hence
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|P|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2*k>\<leqslant\>S>. This also
  controls column sums even though the windows vary with the column.

  Let <math|E=A*P-1>. If <math|i\<in\>W<rsub|j>>, the principal inverse
  equation gives <math|E<rsub|i*j>=0>: the column <math|P<rsub|\<cdot\>j>> is
  zero outside <math|W<rsub|j>>, so the full product at <math|i> is exactly
  the principal product. In particular <math|E<rsub|i*j>=0> when the anchor
  distance is at most <math|B>. The product bound gives
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|E|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2*k>\<leqslant\>Q*S+1>. On its
  remaining entries the weight can be reduced from <math|2*k> to <math|k> at
  cost <math|e<rsup|-k*B>>. Therefore

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:window-error><around|\<\|\|\>|E|\<\|\|\>><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|B>\<assign\>e<rsup|-k*B>*<around|(|Q*S+1|)>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|P-A<rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>S*\<epsilon\><rsub|B>.
  </equation>

  The second inequality uses the exact identity
  <math|P-A<rsup|-1>=A<rsup|-1>*E>. Notice the order of the factors.

  The physical covariance is the symmetric matrix

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:local-covariance>C=<tfrac|1|2><around|(|P+P<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>.
  </equation>

  Symmetry of <math|A<rsup|-1>> and <eqref|eq:window-error> give

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|C|\<\|\|\>><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>S,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|C-A<rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|B>\<assign\>S*\<epsilon\><rsub|B>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|A*C-1|\<\|\|\>><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>Q*\<delta\><rsub|B>.
  </equation*>

  The upper bound <math|A\<leqslant\>Q*1> implies
  <math|A<rsup|-1>\<geqslant\>Q<rsup|-1>*1>. It can also be checked directly:
  for <math|w=A<rsup|-1>*v> and <math|x=Q<rsup|-1>*v>, positivity of
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|w-x,A*<around|(|w-x|)>|\<rangle\>>> and
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|x,A*x|\<rangle\>>\<leqslant\>Q<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>
  give <math|<around|\<langle\>|v,A<rsup|-1>*v|\<rangle\>>\<geqslant\>Q<rsup|-1><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>.
  Consequently, once <math|\<delta\><rsub|B>\<leqslant\>1/<around|(|2*Q|)>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:local-covariance-floor>C\<geqslant\><around|(|2*Q|)><rsup|-1>*1.
  </equation>

  The local activity bond windows are colour independent: a coordinate
  belongs to <math|W<rsub|j>> when some coarse endpoint of its bond is within
  <math|B> of some endpoint of the bond of <math|j>. Each endpoint is within
  one of its bond's parent anchor. Choosing the two anchors proves the inner
  bound; the triangle inequality proves the outer bound <math|B+2>. Thus
  complete colour blocks occur together, and <math|C> has range at most
  <math|B+2>.

  A different panel is used for the global source-row estimates in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-global-row-prices>. There membership
  requires <em|every> pair of coarse endpoints to be within an integer
  threshold <math|R=<around|\<lfloor\>|<math-up|radiusB><around|(|x|)>/20|\<rfloor\>>>.
  For <math|R\<geqslant\>2>, it contains the anchor ball of radius <math|R-2>
  and is contained in the anchor ball of radius <math|R>: use the same
  endpoint-to-anchor bound for the first inclusion, and take the anchors
  themselves for the second. It too is colour independent. The conservative
  inner radius <math|<around|\<lfloor\>|<math-up|radiusB><around|(|x|)>/60|\<rfloor\>>>
  used in the global estimates is valid on the stated sufficiently late tail.
  The two window prescriptions need not coincide. In either case, the
  closeness of the symmetrized window inverse to <math|A<rsup|-1>> is a
  conclusion of the preceding estimates, not an identification of the two
  matrices.

  For the sector precision, inactive rows and columns of <math|A> are those
  of the identity. This property passes to every principal inverse, hence to
  <math|P> and <math|C>. Also <math|M<rsup|\<ast\>>*b> vanishes on inactive
  coordinates, so the chosen local mean <math|-P<rsup|\<ast\>>*M<rsup|\<ast\>>*b>
  vanishes there. These observations justify restricting the local Gaussian
  to the active coordinates without changing its physical entries.

  <subsubsection|Ordered inverse series>

  If a matrix <math|E> satisfies <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|E|\<\|\|\>><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>\<less\>1>,
  then

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|1+E|)><rsup|-1>=<big|sum><rsub|n=0><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|-E|)><rsup|n>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed the partial geometric identity has remainder
  <math|<around|(|-E|)><rsup|N>>, whose norm tends to zero; the series
  converges absolutely in the finite matrix space. For <math|E=A*P-1> this
  gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:local-inverse-series>A<rsup|-1>=<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>0>P*<around|(|-E|)><rsup|n>.
  </equation>

  For <math|D=A*C-1> the corresponding identity is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:covariance-inverse-series>C<rsup|-1>=<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>0><around|(|-D|)><rsup|n>*A,<space|2em>C<rsup|-1>-A=<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>0><around|(|-D|)><rsup|n+1>*A.
  </equation>

  Multiply these formulas by <math|A> on the left and by <math|C> on the
  right, respectively, to verify them. No commutation of <math|P>, <math|C>
  and <math|A> is asserted. Since <math|B\<asymp\><around|(|log x|)><rsup|2>>
  and <math|k\<gtr\>0> is fixed after <math|L>, <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|B>>
  and <math|\<delta\><rsub|B>> have every negative upper exponent. Both
  convergence conditions can thus be met on one sufficiently late tail,
  uniformly over sectors and periods.

  <subsubsection|Logarithms as local terms and correction series>

  For <math|0\<leqslant\>s\<leqslant\>1> put
  <math|A<rsub|s>=1+s*<around|(|A-1|)>>. The hypotheses
  <eqref|eq:covariance-data> hold for <math|A<rsub|s>> with the same
  <math|r,Q,\<eta\>>, since <math|r\<leqslant\>1\<leqslant\>Q>. Let
  <math|P<rsub|s>> be its window inverse and
  <math|E<rsub|s>=A<rsub|s>*P<rsub|s>-1>. All bounds above are uniform in
  <math|s>. Define the local diagonal terms and their correction terms by

  <\equation*>
    \<ell\><rsub|i>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1><around|[|<around|(|A-1|)>*P<rsub|s>|]><rsub|i*i>*<space|0.17em>d*s,<space|2em>\<ell\><rsub|i,n>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1><around|[|<around|(|A-1|)>*P<rsub|s>*<around|(|-E<rsub|s>|)><rsup|n>|]><rsub|i*i>*<space|0.17em>d*s.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>=\<ell\><rsub|i,0>>. We have the exact identity

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:local-logdet>log det A=<big|sum><rsub|i>\<ell\><rsub|i>+<big|sum><rsub|i><big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>1>\<ell\><rsub|i,n>.
  </equation>

  To justify it, the determinant's linear term at the identity is the trace,
  as follows by expanding the finite determinant by permutations. Factoring

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|s+t>=<around|(|1+t*<around|(|A-1|)>*A<rsub|s><rsup|-1>|)>*A<rsub|s>
  </equation*>

  therefore gives <math|<frac|d|d*s>*log det
  A<rsub|s>=<math-up|tr><around|(|<around|(|A-1|)>*A<rsub|s><rsup|-1>|)>>.
  All <math|A<rsub|s>> are positive definite. Their inverses and principal
  inverses vary continuously, by the adjugate formula and the nonvanishing
  determinant. Integrate the derivative from zero to one and substitute
  <eqref|eq:local-inverse-series>. Its diagonal terms are bounded, uniformly
  in <math|s>, by <math|<around|(|Q+1|)>*S*\<epsilon\><rsub|B><rsup|n>>. This
  summable geometric bound justifies termwise integration for each <math|i>.
  The coordinate sum is finite. In particular,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<ell\><rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|Q+1|)>*S,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>1><around|\||\<ell\><rsub|i,n>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|<around|(|Q+1|)>*S*\<epsilon\><rsub|B>|1-\<epsilon\><rsub|B>>.
  </equation*>

  These are per-coordinate bounds. Summing them over the entire torus
  introduces its coordinate count; it does not yield a per-seed estimate
  without the further support assignment.

  There is a second determinant correction for the covariance <math|C>. With
  <math|D=A*C-1> and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D|\<\|\|\>><rsub|k>\<less\>1>,
  the path <math|1+s*D> is invertible for <math|s\<in\><around|[|0,1|]>> by
  its Neumann series. Its real determinant starts at one and never vanishes,
  so it remains positive. The same determinant derivative and geometric
  domination give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:covariance-logdet>log det A+log det
    C=<big|sum><rsub|i><big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>0><big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1><around|[|D*<around|(|-s*D|)><rsup|n>|]><rsub|i*i>*<space|0.17em>d*s.
  </equation>

  Here <math|1+D=A*C> and both <math|A,C> have positive determinant, so
  multiplicativity of the determinant justifies the sum of logarithms.
  Although <math|D> need not be symmetric, no spectral logarithm of <math|D>
  is used. The integral of its <math|n>th term is
  <math|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|n><around|(|D<rsup|n+1>|)><rsub|i*i>/<around|(|n+1|)>>,
  with absolute value at most <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D|\<\|\|\>><rsub|k><rsup|n+1>/<around|(|n+1|)>>.

  <subsubsection|Local comparison of normalization terms>

  For a physical bond <math|v>, group its colour coordinates and set
  <math|J<rsub|v><around|(|A|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i:<space|0.17em>i<text| lies
  over >v>\<ell\><rsub|i>>. The term <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>> reads only the
  principal window <math|W<rsub|i>>:

  <\equation*>
    <around|[|<around|(|A-1|)>*P<rsub|s>|]><rsub|i*i>=<around|[|<around|(|A<rsub|W<rsub|i>>-1|)>*<around|(|1+s*<around|(|A<rsub|W<rsub|i>>-1|)>|)><rsup|-1>|]><rsub|i*i>.
  </equation*>

  This follows because column <math|i> of <math|P<rsub|s>> vanishes outside
  <math|W<rsub|i>>. Suppose <math|A> and <math|Z> both satisfy
  <eqref|eq:covariance-data>, use the same windows, and differ entrywise by
  at most <math|e> on <math|W<rsub|i>\<times\>W<rsub|i>>. The window has at
  most <math|\<Delta\>=m*<around|(|2*B+5|)><rsup|4>> coordinates. Thus the
  unweighted row and column norms of <math|A<rsub|W<rsub|i>>-Z<rsub|W<rsub|i>>>
  are at most <math|\<Delta\>*e>. The same bound holds for the difference of
  their interpolants. The inverse identity, with its order retained, is

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|Z<rsub|s>|)><rsub|W<rsub|i>><rsup|-1>-<around|(|A<rsub|s>|)><rsub|W<rsub|i>><rsup|-1>=<around|(|Z<rsub|s>|)><rsub|W<rsub|i>><rsup|-1>*<around|(|<around|(|A<rsub|s>|)><rsub|W<rsub|i>>-<around|(|Z<rsub|s>|)><rsub|W<rsub|i>>|)><around|(|A<rsub|s>|)><rsub|W<rsub|i>><rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  Each inverse has row-and-column norm at most <math|S>. Splitting the
  difference of the two local products into a difference of first factors and
  a difference of inverses bounds its norm by

  <\equation*>
    <around|{|S+<around|(|Q+1|)>*S<rsup|2>|}>*\<Delta\>*e.
  </equation*>

  Integration and the colour sum give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:local-log-comparison><around|\||J<rsub|v><around|(|A|)>-J<rsub|v><around|(|Z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>q<rsub|<math-up|col>>*<around|{|S+<around|(|Q+1|)>*S<rsup|2>|}>*\<Delta\>*e,
  </equation>

  provided the entrywise hypothesis holds on the window for each colour over
  <math|v>.

  Finally <math|J<rsub|v>> is invariant under bondwise orthogonal changes of
  colour frame. The windows contain whole colour blocks, so such a change
  restricts to an orthogonal matrix on every window and commutes with the
  projection onto the colours over <math|v>. Principal interpolants and their
  inverses are conjugated by this matrix. The sum of diagonal entries over
  <math|v> is the trace after that projection; cyclicity of the finite trace
  proves invariance before integration. This is why one compares the whole
  colour trace, rather than assigning independent gauge-invariant meanings to
  its individual diagonal terms.

  <subsection|The Gaussian mean away from seeds><label|sec:local-mean>

  The local covariance is accompanied by a local mean. Its defining formula
  alone does not make the affine energy small: a uniform bound on the inverse
  would only preserve the size of the original source. The additional gain
  comes from the stationary linear response. We first prove its cancellation,
  and then compare it with the actual mean on one common clean neighbourhood.

  <subsubsection|The stationary response cancels every linear row>

  Fix the step, forest and full flat kernels of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-unhit-comparison>. Write
  <math|\<cal-U\><rsub|F>*b> for the fine stationary field in tree
  coordinates with coarse input <math|b>. It is the tree-straightened
  installed linear response. Its relative field is

  <\equation*>
    v<rsub|F><around|(|b|)>=\<cal-U\><rsub|F>*b-<wide|b|\<bar\>>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|<wide|b|\<bar\>>> is the linear coarse replica. On every forest
  bond this difference is zero. We use the actual diagonalization identity
  for this response: if <math|K<rsub|f>,K<rsub|c>> are the fine and coarse
  quadratic forms and <math|\<cal-Q\>> is the rooted linear predictor,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:mean-diagonal>K<rsub|c>=\<cal-U\><rsub|F><rsup|\<ast\>>*K<rsub|f>*\<cal-U\><rsub|F>+p*<around|(|1-\<cal-Q\>*\<cal-U\><rsub|F>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|1-\<cal-Q\>*\<cal-U\><rsub|F>|)>.
  </equation>

  These are the full physical forms, including the prescribed root
  correction. This is an incoming flat construction identity; it does not
  follow merely from positivity of the forms.

  Let <math|b<rsub|s>> and <math|a<rsub|s>> be the pullbacks, by one signed
  lattice symmetry, of <math|b> and <math|\<cal-U\><rsub|F>*b>. Set

  <\equation*>
    w=B<rsub|c>*d<rsub|c>*b<rsub|s>,<space|2em>u=B<rsub|f>*d<rsub|f>*a<rsub|s>,<space|2em>z=<sqrt|p>*<space|0.17em><around|(|b<rsub|s>-\<cal-Q\>*a<rsub|s>|)>,<space|2em>U=F<rsub|0>*w,<space|1em>V=I<rsub|0>*w.
  </equation*>

  All norms in this paragraph are counting Euclidean norms, summed over their
  physical sites and colours. The two curvature squares are the corresponding
  quadratic forms. Indeed expand each colour component in the curvature
  factorization <math|K=d<rsup|\<ast\>>*B<rsup|\<ast\>>*B*d>; the predictor
  square is exactly the last term in <eqref|eq:mean-diagonal>. Thus that
  identity gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:mean-norm-equality><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>+<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>=<around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>.
  </equation>

  The same equality holds after the signed pullback: the two full quadratic
  forms are invariant, and the nonlinear predictor energy is invariant with
  the transported tags. Apply that last invariance to <math|exp
  <around|(|t*b|)>> and <math|exp <around|(|t*\<cal-U\><rsub|F>*b|)>>, divide
  by <math|t<rsup|2>> and let <math|t\<to\>0>. The derivative proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:predictor-branch> gives the stated predictor
  square. This also explains why the rooted response need not itself commute
  with the chosen symmetry.

  The full stack identities give

  <\equation*>
    N<rsub|0>*w=0,<space|2em>F<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*U+I<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*V=w,<space|2em>F<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*u+I<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*z=w.
  </equation*>

  The first is the extra identity <math|N<rsub|0>*B<rsub|c>*d<rsub|c>=0>; the
  second follows from the stack isometry and the first, and the third is
  <eqref|eq:unhit-linear-zero>. Taking inner products with <math|w> gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|U|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>+<around|\<\|\|\>|V|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>=<around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>,<space|2em><around|\<langle\>|U,u|\<rangle\>>+<around|\<langle\>|V,z|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  Together with <eqref|eq:mean-norm-equality>, these imply
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|U-u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>+<around|\<\|\|\>|V-z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>=0>.
  Every summand is nonnegative, so <math|U=u> and <math|V=z> at every
  address. In the notation of affine energy rows, this says

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:mean-stationary-zero>C<rsub|0>*<around|(|<wide|b<rsub|s>|\<bar\>>,b<rsub|s>|)>-G<rsub|0><around|(|b<rsub|s>|)>+\<cal-A\><rsub|\<infty\>><around|(|v<rsub|F><around|(|b|)>|)><rsub|s>=0.
  </equation>

  Here <math|C<rsub|0>> is the linear fine-curvature/predictor pair,
  <math|G<rsub|0>> the linear guide, and <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\<infty\>>> the
  full fluctuation row; the subscript <math|s> includes the signed action on
  bonds. Linearity of <math|C<rsub|0>> in its fine input and commutation of
  the replica with the signed action give the displayed equation. In
  particular the cancellation holds for each row, not just after summing the
  energy.

  We will also need a volume-independent bound

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:mean-response-cap><around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|F><around|(|b|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|resp>><around|\<\|\|\>|b|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>.
  </equation>

  Here is the quantitative tree estimate behind this bound. Put
  <math|r<rsub|0>=<around|(|J+3|)>*L> and let the installed cross kernel have
  weighted row price <math|C/L> and width <math|\<eta\>\<geqslant\>0>. At
  fine sites within <math|t*L> of a fixed origin, moving the exponential
  weight costs at most <math|e<rsup|\<eta\>*t>>. Consequently its action on a
  scalar coarse field bounded by that weight is at most

  <\equation*>
    A/L,<space|2em>A=e<rsup|\<eta\>*<around|(|12*<around|(|J+3|)>+5|)>>*e<rsup|\<eta\>>*<space|0.17em>4*max
    <around|(|C,1|)>.
  </equation*>

  A tree hike has length at most <math|4*L>. Each coordinate route in the
  root patch has length at most <math|8*r<rsub|0>>; the root correction is
  the difference of two averages of such path integrals. It is therefore
  bounded by <math|16*r<rsub|0>*A/L>. Combining the hike and root correction
  gives <math|C<rsub|1>=<around|(|4+16*<around|(|J+3|)>|)>*A> for the
  straightening potential. Taking its bond gradient costs <math|2*C<rsub|1>>,
  so the extracted response costs at most <math|A+2*C<rsub|1>>. Subtracting
  the replica costs another <math|e<rsup|\<eta\>>> in the weighted estimate.
  For a field of sup norm one all its colour coordinates satisfy the weighted
  premise, since the weight is at least one. Summing the absolute colour
  coordinates bounds the physical colour norm. Thus one may take

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|<math-up|resp>>=max <around|{|1,q*max
    <around|(|0,A+2*C<rsub|1>+e<rsup|\<eta\>>|)>|}>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|q> is the colour dimension. Homogeneity proves
  <eqref|eq:mean-response-cap> for arbitrary <math|b>. All tree and path
  lengths were bounded before periodization, so the estimate is uniform in
  the period and in the forest.

  <subsubsection|A common logarithmic comparison field>

  Retain the upper-exponent convention of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:packet-estimates>. Away from the seed set
  the coarse field <math|V> is clean: its plaquette chords are bounded by the
  small threshold. If the ball of radius <math|d+1> about <math|o> is clean,
  <math|d\<leqslant\>32*B>, the axial construction gives a gauge with bond
  chords, on the radius-<math|d> ball, at most

  <\equation*>
    16*<around|{|2*<around|(|32*B+1|)>+1|}><rsup|5>*N<rsub|p>*<space|0.17em>2*\<tau\><rsub|c>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<tau\><rsub|c>> is the coarse small threshold and has upper
  exponent <math|-1/2>. Apply the local logarithm there and extend the
  logarithmic field by zero elsewhere. We obtain a single global field
  <math|b> and a coarse gauge <math|g> such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:mean-common-log>g\<cdot\>V=exp b<text|on the radius-<math|d>
    ball>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|b|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>w<around|(|x|)>,
  </equation>

  where <math|w\<geqslant\>0> has upper exponent <math|-1/2>. The period and
  chart-radius conditions hold at one threshold depending only on the fixed
  step parameters. The zero extension is used for the full linear kernels; no
  smallness of <math|V> outside the ball is asserted.

  Consider a row whose anchor is at distance greater than <math|20*B> from
  all seeds, and suppose <eqref|eq:mean-common-log> holds on its
  radius-<math|2*B> ball. In the original gauge use the candidate

  <\equation*>
    v=<math-up|Ad><around|(|g<rsup|-1>\<circ\><math-up|parent>|)>*v<rsub|F><around|(|b|)>.
  </equation*>

  It vanishes on tree bonds and has norm at most
  <math|W=C<rsub|<math-up|resp>>*w>. Within the stencil of this remote row
  all non-tree bonds are active. Thus the sector's affine signal agrees there
  with <math|v>, including its tree zeros. Gauge covariance transfers its
  actual affine row to the same <math|b> and <math|v<rsub|F><around|(|b|)>>.

  Let <math|C<around|(|V|)>> be the fine-curvature/predictor pair at the
  coarse replica, <math|G<around|(|V|)>> the guide, and
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|R><rsup|V>> the transported cut fluctuation row. Their
  residual is <math|C<around|(|V|)>-G<around|(|V|)>+\<cal-A\><rsub|R><rsup|V>*v<rsub|F><around|(|b|)>>
  in this common gauge. Subtract <eqref|eq:mean-stationary-zero>. The four
  errors are

  <\equation*>
    <\eqsplit>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|C<around|(|V|)>-C<rsub|0>*<around|(|<wide|b|\<bar\>>,b|)>,<space|2em>-<around|(|G<around|(|V|)>-G<rsub|0><around|(|b|)>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|\<cal-A\><rsub|R><rsup|V>-\<cal-A\><rsub|R>|)>*v<rsub|F><around|(|b|)>,<space|2em><around|(|\<cal-A\><rsub|R>-\<cal-A\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>*v<rsub|F><around|(|b|)>.>>>>
    </eqsplit>
  </equation*>

  The first two have upper exponent <math|-1>, by the local Taylor and guide
  comparisons of Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-unhit-comparison>
  and<nbsp><reference|sec:packet-annulus>: their bounds are fixed multiples
  of <math|w*<around|{|<around|(|1+R|)>*w+e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*R>|}>>. The clean
  condition makes the guide's clipping inactive on precisely the read
  stencil; it is removed before the comparison with the globally extended
  logarithm. For the third error, all coarse transport bonds have chord at
  most <math|C*w> on the common hull. The adjoint estimate and the absolute
  path price give a bound <math|C*<around|(|B+1|)>*w*W>. The fourth is at
  most <math|C*W*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*R>>. The cutoff
  <math|R=<around|\<lfloor\>|B/100000|\<rfloor\>>> has an exponentially small
  tail of every prescribed negative upper exponent. We have therefore proved,
  for one <math|a<around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>0> of upper exponent <math|-1>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:mean-candidate-residual><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-R\><rsub|j><around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>a<around|(|x|)>
  </equation>

  on every row whose comparison hull is included in the common clean ball.
  Here <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|j>> is the actual unscaled affine row, with its
  original orientation and tags. One field <math|b> is used for all rows in
  the ball, so these estimates can be inserted into a matrix identity without
  changing the candidate from row to row.

  <subsubsection|Comparing the candidate with the chosen local mean>

  Use counting coordinates and the matrices of
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:sector-gram>
  and<nbsp><reference|sec:local-covariance>. Write <math|M> for the selected
  row matrix, <math|A=M<rsup|\<ast\>>*M+\<Pi\><rsub|<math-up|inactive>>>,
  <math|\<ell\>=M<rsup|\<ast\>>*r<around|(|0|)>> for the linear source, and

  <\equation*>
    r<around|(|v|)>=M*v+r<around|(|0|)>,<space|2em>P=<text|the padded
    principal inverse>,<space|2em>m=-P<rsup|\<ast\>>*\<ell\>,<space|1em>E=A*P-1.
  </equation*>

  The row <math|r<rsub|j><around|(|v|)>> is
  <math|s*\<cal-R\><rsub|j><around|(|v|)>> when selected, and zero otherwise;
  <math|s\<gtr\>0> is the fixed signed-channel normalization. The chosen
  physical mean <math|\<mu\>> is obtained from <math|m> by reassembling its
  colour coordinates. We have the exact identity

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:mean-error-identity>m-v=E<rsup|\<ast\>>*v-P<rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|A*v+\<ell\>|)>,<space|2em>A*v+\<ell\>=M<rsup|\<ast\>>*r<around|(|v|)>+\<Pi\><rsub|<math-up|inactive>>*v.
  </equation>

  For the first equality use <math|E<rsup|\<ast\>>=P<rsup|\<ast\>>*A-1> and
  cancel <math|P<rsup|\<ast\>>*A*v>. The second follows directly from the
  definition of <math|A> and <math|\<ell\>>.

  Let <math|S> bound both unweighted row and column sums of <math|P>, let
  <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|B>> bound those of <math|E>, and let <math|Y>
  bound those of <math|M>. These constants are supplied by the weighted
  estimates already proved; <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|B>> has every negative
  upper exponent. If the normal residual <math|A*v+\<ell\>> is bounded by
  <math|b<rsub|0>> on the principal window attached to coordinate <math|i>,
  <eqref|eq:mean-error-identity> gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:mean-point><around|\||m<rsub|i>-v<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>S*b<rsub|0>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|B><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>.
  </equation>

  Indeed the <math|i>th row of <math|P<rsup|\<ast\>>> is supported on that
  very window, and the column bounds give both estimates. This uses
  <math|P<rsup|\<ast\>>> in the mean; replacing it by the symmetrized
  covariance would change the identity.

  Fix a target row at anchor <math|o> farther than <math|30*B> from every
  seed. Choose <eqref|eq:mean-common-log> on the radius-<math|8*B> ball. For
  each coordinate within <math|B> of <math|o>, its principal window lies
  within <math|3*B> of <math|o>, since the window radius is at most
  <math|B+2>. At such window coordinates the inactive term vanishes: a
  non-tree bond is active there, and a tree bond has candidate value zero. A
  nonzero entry of <math|M<rsup|\<ast\>>> reads a row within <math|B/4>, so
  all its affine residuals obey <eqref|eq:mean-candidate-residual>, using
  subballs of the same radius-<math|8*B> comparison. Hence
  <math|b<rsub|0>=Y*s*a>, and

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||m<rsub|i>-v<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>U<around|(|x|)>\<assign\>S*Y*s*<space|0.17em>a<around|(|x|)>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|B>*W<around|(|x|)><space|1em><text|when
    >d*<around|(|o,i|)>\<leqslant\>B.
  </equation*>

  The function <math|U> has upper exponent <math|-1>.

  The target row is selected and reads only coordinates within <math|B/4>.
  Its difference on <math|\<mu\>> and <math|v> is linear, so the row bound on
  <math|M> and the passage from colour coordinates to the colour norm imply

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-R\><rsub|o><around|(|\<mu\>|)>-\<cal-R\><rsub|o><around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>q*s<rsup|-1>*Y*U.
  </equation*>

  Combining this with <eqref|eq:mean-candidate-residual> proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:mean-remote-final><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-R\><rsub|o><around|(|\<mu\>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>a<around|(|x|)>+q*s<rsup|-1>*Y*U<around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>d*<around|(|o,<text|seeds>|)>\<gtr\>30*B.
  </equation>

  The right-hand side has upper exponent <math|-1>, uniformly in the period,
  forest, tags and all sectors satisfying the fixed construction conditions.
  This is the required extra order of smallness of the actual affine mean.
  The argument uses the flat stationary response and its diagonal identity;
  it makes no assertion about derivatives of the mean or a nonlinear global
  stationary solution.

  <subsection|Producing the warning plateaus><label|sec:warning-safety>

  We prove the deterministic input to the warning estimate of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-warnings>. Fix the patch and the
  positive fine-action and predictor-penalty constants
  <math|c<rsub|f>,c<rsub|p>>. We retain the funded path bounds, the
  clean-sector precision bounds and the stationary response of
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:sector-gram> and <reference|sec:local-mean>.
  Let <math|t=<math-up|fiery><around|(|x|)>>,
  <math|B=<math-up|radius><around|(|x|)>> and <math|q=dim \<frak-g\>>. We use
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>> for a fixed small error allowance, to distinguish
  it from the field scale <math|t>.

  The guide estimate supplies a constant <math|A\<gtr\>0>, chosen before
  <math|L>, such that the fine guide has norm at most <math|A*t/L<rsup|2>>
  and the predictor guide at most <math|A*t>. Choose

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:safety-goals>\<delta\><rsub|0>=<frac|<sqrt|c<rsub|f>>|32>,<space|2em><frac|A|L<rsup|2>>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|0>,<space|2em>W\<gtr\><frac|2|<sqrt|c<rsub|p>>>*<around|(|A+\<delta\><rsub|0>|)>.
  </equation>

  For example, any integer lower bound strictly larger than
  <math|A/\<delta\><rsub|0>> suffices for <math|L>, since
  <math|L\<geqslant\>1>. We will choose the remaining constants after
  <math|L> and <math|W>, and finally one lower bound for <math|x>. None of
  these choices depends on the lattice period or on the seed allocation.

  <subsubsection|A uniform bound for the affine mean>

  First we need <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|e>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*t>
  with a fixed post-<math|L> constant <math|C>. A bound with upper exponent
  <math|-1/2> alone would not imply this stronger comparison with the
  particular scale <math|t>.

  On a clean ball of fixed radius <math|n>, the axial gauge construction of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-geometry> bounds every coarse bond
  chord by <math|K<rsub|n>*t>, where one may take

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|n>=32*<around|(|2*<around|(|n+1|)>+1|)><rsup|5>#<around|{|<text|plaquette
    axes>|}>.
  </equation*>

  The number <math|n> is fixed before <math|x>. For a fine plaquette in the
  coarse replica, use <math|n=4>: each of its four links has chord at most
  <math|K<rsub|4>*t> in that gauge, so its curvature norm is at most
  <math|4*K<rsub|4>*t>. Every path term retained by the fine cutoff leaves
  the required radius-five margin at its endpoint inside the clean
  <math|B>-ball: its endpoint separation plus five is at most <math|B>. Apply
  the same radius-four argument separately at that endpoint. Unitary
  transport preserves its norm; summing the absolute path coefficients bounds
  the entire fine base stroke by

  <\equation*>
    4*K<rsub|4>*<around|(|<sqrt|c<rsub|f>>+P<rsub|f>|)>*t,
  </equation*>

  where <math|P<rsub|f>> is the funded absolute fine-path price. This is a
  sum of individual fixed-radius estimates; no gauge on the whole growing
  <math|B>-ball is required for this bound.

  For a predictor base stroke, take <math|n> equal to the fixed predictor
  read radius. The logarithm chart extends the locally gauged coarse field to
  a global logarithmic field of norm at most <math|K<rprime|'><rsub|n>*t>,
  while preserving the actual links on that stencil. Its linear replica has
  no larger norm. The fixed-patch Taylor estimate from
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:predictor-branch> bounds the predictor by
  <math|J*<around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>|)>>
  for the pair of logarithmic fields <math|w>. Eventually
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>K<rprime|'><rsub|n>*t\<leqslant\>1>,
  so this is at most <math|2*J*K<rprime|'><rsub|n>*t>. Locality and gauge
  covariance identify it with the original predictor base stroke for every
  original tag choice. We have therefore proved a common bound
  <math|A<rsub|1>*t> for both base-stroke types on clean balls.

  Let <math|s\<gtr\>0> be the fixed row normalization, <math|N<rsub|1>> the
  row operator bound, and <math|\<gamma\>,K> the shell-access constants. The
  saved frozen logarithmic field is bounded by <math|2*\<gamma\>*K*t>. A
  selected affine row at zero is the base stroke minus its guide plus the row
  applied to this saved field. Unselected rows vanish. Hence all normalized
  affine rows have norm at most <math|b<rsub|1>*t>, where

  <\equation*>
    b<rsub|1>=s*<around|(|A<rsub|1>+A+2*N<rsub|1>*\<gamma\>*K|)>.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|Y> bound the absolute column sums of the row matrix, and let
  <math|P<rsub|0>> bound both row and column sums of the unsymmetrized local
  inverse <math|P>. The affine linear coefficient <math|\<ell\>> and the mean
  <math|m=-P<rsup|T>*\<ell\>> then satisfy coordinatewise

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<ell\><rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>Y*b<rsub|1>*t,<space|2em><around|\||m<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|0>*Y*b<rsub|1>*t,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|e>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>q*P<rsub|0>*Y*b<rsub|1>*t.
  </equation*>

  Each inequality is the elementary bound
  <math|<around|\||<big|sum><rsub|j>M<rsub|i*j>*v<rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|<big|sum><rsub|j><around|\||M<rsub|i*j>|\|>|)>*sup<rsub|j><around|\||v<rsub|j>|\|>>;
  the last sums the <math|q> colour coordinates. Choose once and for all

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:safety-mean-cap>C=1+max <around|{|0,b<rsub|1>,Y*b<rsub|1>,q*P<rsub|0>*Y*b<rsub|1>|}>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation>

  The same algebra gives the residual identity
  <math|A*m+\<ell\>=-A*<around|(|P-A<rsup|-1>|)><rsup|T>*\<ell\>>. Its
  entries are bounded by a fixed multiple of
  <math|<math-up|distPay><around|(|B|)>*t>, and hence have every prescribed
  negative upper exponent by the local inverse tail estimate. The mean and
  its residual thus come from the same affine system.

  <subsubsection|The genuine local minimizing branches>

  We also need exact likelihood values, before restoring other masks. Fix any
  <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>. There exist a radius <math|r\<gtr\>0> and a
  finite read distance <math|d>, depending on the patch and
  <math|\<varepsilon\>>, such that if all fine bond chords within distance
  <math|d> of a coarse site are at most <math|r>, then its site likelihood
  equals one, every outgoing bond likelihood equals one, and the predictor
  bonds have chord at most <math|\<varepsilon\>>.

  Here is the uniformity argument. On the covering lattice, the identity
  configuration lies in the open unique-minimizer branches for both the frame
  and the connector. Their values there are the identity. The continuity and
  uniqueness proved in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:predictor-branch> imply
  a neighbourhood on which these branches remain selected and each predictor
  chord is below <math|\<varepsilon\>>. Intersect these neighbourhoods over
  the finitely many axes and frame-tag pairs. A product-topology
  neighbourhood contains one obtained by restricting finitely many covering
  bonds to chord balls of a common positive radius <math|r>. Enclose their
  anchors in a ball of finite read distance <math|d>.

  Pulling a periodic configuration to the cover preserves all these finite
  reads, even when some periodic bonds coincide. Thus the same <math|r,d>
  work for every period and every tag array. On a minimizing branch the
  chosen tag is uniform: its mass is the reciprocal of the number of tags.
  The physical likelihood multiplies by that number, so its value is exactly
  one. This proves both likelihood identities, not merely their convergence
  to one.

  Suppose now that the coarse field is clean on the <math|B>-ball about
  <math|o> and that, on its half-ball, the actual fine field is <math|U=exp
  <around|(|v|)><math-up|replica><space|0.17em>V> with
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|e>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>w<around|(|x|)>>,
  where <math|w> has upper exponent <math|-1/2>. The local axial gauge and
  logarithm extension put both coarse and fine chords below a common bound
  <math|W<rsub|1><around|(|x|)>> of upper exponent <math|-1/2>. Indeed the
  coarse logarithm has that bound, the exponential is locally Lipschitz, and

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|chord><around|(|exp <around|(|v<rsub|e>|)><math-up|replica><space|0.17em>V<rsub|e>|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|chord><around|(|exp
    <around|(|v<rsub|e>|)>|)>+<math-up|chord><around|(|<math-up|replica><space|0.17em>V<rsub|e>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Choose <math|x> so that <math|B\<geqslant\>2*d+12> and
  <math|W<rsub|1><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>min
  <around|{|r,\<varepsilon\>/4|}>>, applying the preceding branch result with
  target <math|\<varepsilon\>/2>. The predictor/coarse relative chord is at
  most <math|\<varepsilon\>/2+W<rsub|1><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>>.
  Every fine plaquette chord is at most <math|4*W<rsub|1><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>>.
  Gauge covariance transfers these statements and the likelihood identities
  to the original fields.

  <subsubsection|The three errors on a small fluctuation event>

  Let <math|o> have distance greater than <math|30*B> from the seeds. The
  exact flag condition implies that the coarse field is clean on its needed
  hull. For <math|\<beta\>\<gtr\>0>, assume

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:safety-watch><around|\||z<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<xi\>*t*<sqrt|\<beta\>><space|1em><text|for
    every coordinate whose anchor lies within >B<text| of >o.
  </equation>

  With <math|v=\<mu\>+\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>*z>, interpreted in the colour
  basis, <eqref|eq:safety-mean-cap> gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|e>-\<mu\><rsub|e>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>q*\<xi\>*t,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|e>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|C+q*\<xi\>|)>*t.
  </equation*>

  Away from the seeds the actual fine field agrees with this increment of the
  coarse replica. Thus the small-chart branch result just proved applies to
  it, with <math|w=<around|(|C+q*\<xi\>|)>*t>.

  For a fine or predictor row based at <math|o>, write
  <math|B<rsub|p>*<around|(|U,V|)>> for its actual stroke minus its guide,
  and <math|R<rsub|p><around|(|v|)>> for the transported affine row with the
  same coarse field, tags and saved variables. There is a nonnegative error
  <math|H<around|(|x|)>> of upper exponent <math|-1> such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:safety-nonlinear-error><around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsub|p>*<around|(|U,V|)>-R<rsub|p><around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>H<around|(|x|)>.
  </equation>

  To detail the estimate, use the preceding common local gauge and extend its
  two logarithmic fields by zero outside the read hull. Let <math|h> bound
  their norms; it has upper exponent <math|-1/2>. The local product logarithm
  differs from the sum by at most <math|J*h<rsup|2>>. Taylor expansion of
  each genuine fine/predictor stroke at zero leaves a remainder
  <math|E<rsub|0>> of upper exponent <math|-1>. Subtract the corresponding
  Taylor formula at the replica. The five resulting errors are bounded by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:safety-five-errors>2*E<rsub|0>+N<rsub|1>*J*h<rsup|2>+N<rsub|1>*h*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*R>+2*N<rsub|1>*c<rsub|G>*B*A<rsub|2>*h<rsup|2>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|R=<around|\<lfloor\>|B/100000|\<rfloor\>>> is the retained path
  cutoff, and <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0> is the funded path decay exponent.
  They are, respectively, the two Taylor remainders, the product-log error
  acted on by the linear row, the cutoff tail of that row, and the change
  from flat to coarse-field transport. For the last term, each transport path
  has length at most <math|B>, each coarse chord is at most
  <math|A<rsub|2>*h>, and the adjoint perturbation bound is
  <math|2*c<rsub|G>> times the path chord; multiply by the input norm
  <math|h> and the row price <math|N<rsub|1>>. The retained read radius is at
  most <math|B/4>, so truncating the extended fields does not alter the
  actual row. Since <math|B> has upper exponent zero and
  <math|e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*R>> has every negative exponent, every term in
  <eqref|eq:safety-five-errors> has upper exponent <math|-1>. This proves
  <eqref|eq:safety-nonlinear-error> with the original tags.

  The affine row matrix has absolute row price <math|Y> after normalization
  by <math|s>. Therefore <eqref|eq:safety-watch> implies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:safety-linear-error><around|\<\|\|\>|R<rsub|p><around|(|v|)>-R<rsub|p><around|(|\<mu\>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>q*s<rsup|-1>*Y*\<xi\>*t.
  </equation>

  The local mean comparison in <eqref|eq:mean-remote-final> gives a
  nonnegative <math|E<around|(|x|)>> of upper exponent <math|-1> with
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|R<rsub|p><around|(|\<mu\>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>E<around|(|x|)>>.
  Choose

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:safety-xi>\<xi\>=min <around*|{|<around|(|q+1|)><rsup|-1>,<frac|\<delta\><rsub|0>|3*q*s<rsup|-1>\|Y\|+1>|}>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation>

  Then <math|q*\<xi\>\<leqslant\>1> and <math|q*s<rsup|-1>*Y*\<xi\>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|0>/3>.
  An upper-exponent-<math|-1> function is eventually at most
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>*t/3>: multiplying it by
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|f><rsup|1/2>> gives a quantity tending to
  zero, while <math|t=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|f><rsup|-1/2><math-up|fineMonitor>\<geqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|f><rsup|-1/2>>.
  Hence <math|H,E\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|0>*t/3> eventually. The triangle
  inequality proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:safety-bill><around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsub|p>*<around|(|U,V|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|0>*t.
  </equation>

  Choose the earlier small-chart target <math|\<varepsilon\>> below the fixed
  curvature-comparison radius. In that neighbourhood,
  <math|<math-up|chord><around|(|g|)>\<leqslant\>2<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|curvature><around|(|g|)>|\<\|\|\>>>.
  Adding the guide bounds to <eqref|eq:safety-bill>, and retaining the
  square-root penalty normalizations, gives

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|toll><around|(|U,p|)>\<leqslant\><frac|2|<sqrt|c<rsub|f>>>*<around|(|A/L<rsup|2>+\<delta\><rsub|0>|)>*t\<less\>t/4,<space|2em><math-up|wire><around|(|Q<around|(|U|)>,V,<around|(|o,a|)>|)>\<leqslant\><frac|2|<sqrt|c<rsub|p>>>*<around|(|A+\<delta\><rsub|0>|)>*t\<less\>W*t.
  </equation*>

  The strict inequalities follow from <eqref|eq:safety-goals>; the first
  coefficient is at most <math|1/8>. Both physical likelihoods are already
  exactly one by the local minimizing-branch argument.

  <subsubsection|The cutoff margin and the final order of choices>

  Set <math|a=max <around|{|C+1,\<gamma\>*K|}>>. The same event has
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|e>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|C+q*\<xi\>|)>*t\<leqslant\>a*t>,
  so the broad bond cutoff is one. For derivatives, use the event with
  <math|\<xi\>/2>. It gives the strict margin <math|C+q*\<xi\>/2\<less\>a>,
  while the fine and predictor inequalities above are strict as well.
  Continuity makes these masks constant on a neighbourhood of the base point.
  This is exactly the plateau required in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:activity-jet-assembly>; no derivative is
  taken through a chosen gauge or a varying seed assignment.

  The order is now explicit: choose the guide bound <math|A> and error
  allowance before <math|L>; choose <math|L>, then <math|W>; obtain the fixed
  shell and precision constants, then <math|C,a,\<xi\>>; finally enlarge the
  <math|x> threshold for the finite-read branches, the two order-<math|-1>
  errors, the chart radius and the inverse-series bounds. This supplies the
  previously displayed safety input on every funded sector whose adjacent
  quadratic data and paths have the compatibility specified in
  Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-local-warnings>.
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-tube-compatibility> supplies that
  compatibility for the stored tubes. Funding, the stationary response and
  the guide bounds retain their separate construction hypotheses.

  <subsection|Global row and source bounds on the actual
  sector><label|sec:actual-global-row-prices>

  The palette comparison needs estimates on the whole selected row matrix,
  including the source created by frozen links. A small covariance error on a
  posed clean panel does not supply these estimates. We establish the
  required global bounds using the actual sector precision and the selected
  background rows.

  Fix the gauge group, a one-step patch with block length <math|L>, its
  kernel, and its funded tower and path prices. Assume the patch satisfies
  the predictor branch conditions. All constants below are chosen from these
  fixed data. Put

  <\equation*>
    B=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>,<space|2em>t=<math-up|fiery><around|(|x,L|)>,<space|2em>R=<around|\<lfloor\>|B/100000|\<rfloor\>>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|t> is the fine small-field threshold of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-guides>, while
  <math|<math-up|temp><around|(|x|)>> is the coarse threshold; eventually
  <math|<math-up|temp><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>2*t>. The coarse period
  satisfies <math|c\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|1+B|)>>. Use arbitrary stores,
  forest, predictor tags and coarse field <math|V> with the stores' flags
  equal to the actual coarse flags at threshold
  <math|<math-up|temp><around|(|x|)>>. No assertion about their fine costs or
  other noise entries is required. The large-<math|x> threshold is uniform
  over all these choices and all funded towers having the fixed prices.

  Let <math|I> be the active link-color coordinates and <math|J> the
  row-color coordinates, with unselected rows set to zero. An input
  coordinate is anchored at the coarse parent of its fine link. For a row in
  signed channel <math|s>, apply <math|s> to the row's coarse root: the
  parent of the fine plaquette base for a curvature row, or the initial
  coarse site for a predictor row. This is the output anchor used in the
  weighted bounds. The selector here retains a row exactly when its
  prescribed stencil meets an active bond; that stencil contains all
  fine-direction inputs of the row. The anchor-distance selector of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:sector-gram> uses a different rule; no
  equality of selected sets is assumed.

  We make the selection rule and its consequence for flags explicit. For
  <math|k\<geqslant\>0>, the <math|k>-neighbourhood of the seeds is the set
  of coarse sites within distance <math|k> of the seed place of the stores;
  the coarse flags belong to the seed place. As in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:sector-gram>, the active bonds are the free
  bonds whose coarse endpoints lie outside the <math|10*B>-neighbourhood of
  the seeds; below we use only that the coarse parent of an active bond lies
  outside it. Let <math|r<rsub|<math-up|pred>>> be the fixed reach of the
  predictor and put

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|span><around|(|x|)>=R+5+r<rsub|<math-up|pred>>.
  </equation*>

  The <em|stencil> of a row in signed channel <math|s> consists of the fine
  bonds whose coarse parent is within distance
  <math|<math-up|span><around|(|x|)>> of its output anchor <math|o>. Here
  <math|R> is the cutoff on the length of the retained paths, and the stencil
  contains every fine-direction input of the row. Since
  <math|R=<around|\<lfloor\>|B/100000|\<rfloor\>>>, one has
  <math|<math-up|span><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around|\<lfloor\>|B/50|\<rfloor\>>>
  as soon as <math|B\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|5+r<rsub|<math-up|pred>>|)>>;
  this and <math|B\<geqslant\>7> hold eventually.

  <em|Clearance of selected rows.> Suppose a row with output anchor <math|o>
  is selected, so that its stencil contains an active bond <math|a> with
  coarse parent <math|l>. Then <math|<math-up|dist><around|(|o,l|)>\<leqslant\><around|\<lfloor\>|B/50|\<rfloor\>>>.
  If a coarse site <math|m> and <math|k\<geqslant\>0> satisfy
  <math|k+<math-up|dist><around|(|m,l|)>\<leqslant\>10*B>, then <math|m> is
  outside the <math|k>-neighbourhood of the seeds: otherwise some seed point
  <math|y> has <math|<math-up|dist><around|(|y,m|)>\<leqslant\>k>, hence
  <math|<math-up|dist><around|(|y,l|)>\<leqslant\>10*B>, contradicting that
  <math|a> is active. Applying this with <math|m=o> and
  <math|k=9*B+<around|\<lfloor\>|9*B/10|\<rfloor\>>> (note
  <math|<around|\<lfloor\>|9*B/10|\<rfloor\>>+<around|\<lfloor\>|B/50|\<rfloor\>>\<leqslant\>B>)
  shows that <math|o> is outside the <math|<around|(|9*B+<around|\<lfloor\>|9*B/10|\<rfloor\>>|)>>-neighbourhood
  of the seeds. For a bond <math|e> of the stencil, its coarse parent
  <math|m> satisfies <math|<math-up|dist><around|(|m,l|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|dist><around|(|m,o|)>+<math-up|dist><around|(|o,l|)>\<leqslant\>2*<around|\<lfloor\>|B/50|\<rfloor\>>>,
  and the coarse parent <math|n> of its other endpoint is within one more.
  With <math|k=9*B+<around|\<lfloor\>|8*B/10|\<rfloor\>>> and
  <math|<around|\<lfloor\>|8*B/10|\<rfloor\>>+1+2*<around|\<lfloor\>|B/50|\<rfloor\>>\<leqslant\>B>
  (valid for <math|B\<geqslant\>7>), both <math|m> and <math|n> lie outside
  the <math|<around|(|9*B+<around|\<lfloor\>|8*B/10|\<rfloor\>>|)>>-neighbourhood
  of the seeds. Since the flags lie in the seed place, every coarse ball of
  radius at most <math|9*B> about the output anchor or about an endpoint of
  the stencil is free of coarse flags. All the neighbourhoods used below for
  a selected row (the branch neighbourhood of a predictor read, the clean
  <math|B>-ball of the base-row estimate, and the chart boxes of the
  plaquettes reached by retained paths) have radius at most <math|B> for
  large <math|x>, so they are clean. This is the meaning of the phrase \Prow
  selection clears the required neighbourhood of flags\Q below. Unselected
  rows are set to zero and need no clearance. Write

  <\equation*>
    M:\<bbb-R\><rsup|I>\<longrightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|J>,<space|2em>Q=M<rsup|\<ast\>>*M
  </equation*>

  for the actual selected derivative matrix and its Gram matrix, including
  the fixed row normalization <math|\<lambda\>\<gtr\>0>. Thus <math|M> is the
  matrix obtained by applying the selected row derivative to each active
  coordinate impulse, not a new linearization at the identity. These
  derivatives are taken at the actual replica; the predictor derivative
  applies curvature to the predictor at the displaced field multiplied by the
  inverse predictor at zero displacement. They need not equal the transported
  flat rows used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:sector-gram>; their
  coercivity requires the separate comparison below. For matrices between
  either pair of anchor sets, use the weighted maximum of absolute row and
  column sums with exponential weight <math|w\<geqslant\>0>, denoted
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|w>>; this is the norm
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<eta\>>> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-covariance> with <math|\<eta\>=w>.
  (The letter <math|Q> below is the Gram matrix, not the amplitude constant
  of <eqref|eq:covariance-data>; when that section is applied to <math|Q>,
  its amplitude constant is supplied by <eqref|eq:global-row-error>.) For
  each <math|j\<in\>I>, let <math|W<rsub|j>> be its actual panel. Let
  <math|N> be the matrix whose <math|j>th column is the inverse of
  <math|Q<rsub|W<rsub|j>>>, extended by zero, and let
  <math|C=<around|(|N+N<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>/2>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Uniform global prices><label|prop:actual-global-row-prices>There
    are <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>, <math|K\<geqslant\>1>, <math|u,v\<gtr\>0>,
    and <math|L<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0>, depending only on the fixed data, such that
    eventually in <math|x>, uniformly over the choices just specified,

    <\gather>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|Q\<geqslant\>\<eta\>*I,<space|2em>C\<geqslant\>\<eta\>*I,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|Q|\<\|\|\>><rsub|0>,<around|\<\|\|\>|C|\<\|\|\>><rsub|0>,<around|\<\|\|\>|N|\<\|\|\>><rsub|0>\<leqslant\>K,<eq-number><label|eq:global-row-kernels>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|N-Q<rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|0>\<leqslant\>u*e<rsup|-v*<around|\<lfloor\>|B/60|\<rfloor\>>>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|M|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<sigma\>/2>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|0>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|Q|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<sigma\>/2>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|0><rsup|2>,<eq-number><label|eq:global-row-error>>>>>
    </gather>

    where <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0> is the fixed path width. In particular
    <math|Q> and <math|C> are positive definite. Given any fixed
    <math|r\<geqslant\>0>, there is also <math|Z\<gtr\>0> such that, for
    <math|0\<less\>d\<leqslant\>r*t>, the actual source vector
    <math|s<around|(|Y|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|J>> created by an arbitrary
    frozen field <math|Y> obeys

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:global-row-source><around|\||s<around|(|Y|)><rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>Z*t*<space|2em><around|(|i\<in\>J|)>.
    </equation>

    The same tail in <math|x> may be used for all these inequalities. The
    source constant and tail may additionally depend on <math|r>.
  </proposition>

  <subsubsection|The actual matrix and its principal windows>

  The actual derivative has a uniform exponentially decaying impulse bound.
  For a plaquette derivative, the product rule gives a fixed sum over its
  four input bonds; path transports preserve colour norms and the funded
  absolute path coefficients pay the remaining distance. The predictor
  derivative reads a fixed stencil and has a uniform bound on its actual
  branch by the finite-read argument of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mean-action-jets>.
  That finite-read argument uses the fixed patch and predictor branch
  conditions; it does not use the precision bounds assumed for other parts of
  that section. Row selection clears the required neighbourhood of flags, by
  the clearance argument above, so this branch estimate applies at <math|V>.
  Reversing a bond changes its parent anchor by at most one, which costs only
  a fixed factor in the exponential weight. Restrictions to selected rows or
  active coordinates delete nonnegative terms from the absolute sums. This is
  the actual-derivative counterpart of the impulse estimate used in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:sector-gram>.

  More explicitly, the resulting actual entry estimate has the form

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|\<sigma\><math-up|dist><around|(|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|j>|)>><around|\||M<rsub|i*j>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|0>.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|m<rsub|I>,m<rsub|J>> bound the numbers of input and output
  coordinates per coarse anchor. They are finite constants fixed after
  <math|L>, including all color, orientation and block-residue factors. For
  <math|\<cal-W\><around|(|v|)>=<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>0><around|(|2*n+1|)><rsup|4>*e<rsup|-v*n>>,
  grouping by anchor distance gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|M|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<sigma\>/2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|0>*max
    <around|{|m<rsub|I>,m<rsub|J>|}>*\<cal-W\>*<around|(|\<sigma\>/2|)>=:L<rsub|0>.
  </equation*>

  Increasing <math|L<rsub|0>> if needed makes it positive. This is a global
  row and column bound; its proof does not introduce the torus cardinality.
  The same product estimate gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|Q|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<sigma\>/2>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|0><rsup|2>>:
  the weighted norm is submultiplicative, because the triangle inequality
  <math|<math-up|dist><around|(|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|k>|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|dist><around|(|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|j>|)>+<math-up|dist><around|(|a<rsub|j>,a<rsub|k>|)>>
  through the intermediate anchor splits the weight, and the adjoint has the
  same weighted norm since row and column sums are interchanged. This is the
  last bound in <eqref|eq:global-row-error>. We prove a uniform lower bound
  for these actual derivatives. Let <math|a> be an active-coordinate vector
  and extend it by zero to a fine-bond field <math|u>, including zeros on
  tree and frozen bonds. At each active bond choose a coarse gauge on a fixed
  macro patch around its parent; the active-bond clearance and the flag
  identity make this patch clean. The tree comparison's fixed macrobox fits
  on the fine torus: eventually <math|B> dominates its fixed radius, while
  the fine period <math|f=L*c> satisfies <math|f\<geqslant\>c\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|1+B|)>>
  and <math|c\<gtr\>1>. The axial estimate makes every replica link read by
  the comparison arbitrarily close to the identity, uniformly in the period
  and in the subsequently chosen sector. Use one such gauge for the whole
  comparison at that bond, including every row in its macro patch.

  Fix <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>. On a sufficiently small identity
  neighbourhood, the derivatives of a plaquette and of the selected predictor
  differ from their flat derivatives by at most <math|\<varepsilon\>> times
  the sum of the norms of the input directions on their respective finite
  stencils. For a plaquette this follows by differentiating its finite link
  product and using continuity of its derivative. For the predictor, use the
  finite-read analytic branch of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mean-action-jets>;
  its derivative at the identity is the flat predictor row. Continuity of
  that derivative on the fixed reference patch gives the required
  <math|\<varepsilon\>> bound for each axis and local tag choice. Their
  finite intersection is one neighbourhood, independent of the period. The
  read pullback has supremum norm at most one even when periodic reads
  coincide, and the supremum of the input directions is bounded by their sum
  on the stencil. Gauge covariance and invariance of the colour norm return
  the estimates to the original actual derivatives. The tree inequality
  <eqref|eq:sector-local-linear>, applied in the chosen gauge, therefore
  bounds each active <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|e>|\<\|\|\>>> by a fixed
  linear combination of the norms of the actual direct plaquette and
  predictor derivatives, plus <math|\<varepsilon\>> times sums of
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>>> over fixed-radius stencils. The gauge
  may depend on <math|e>; it is fixed while differentiating the rows.

  Square this inequality and sum over active bonds. Reordering the finite
  neighbourhood sums, as in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:sector-gram>,
  gives fixed constants <math|K<rsub|f>,K<rsub|p>,H<rsub|f>,H<rsub|p>\<geqslant\>0>,
  independent of the period, such that

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2*K<rsub|f>*<big|sum><rsub|p><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsub|p>*u*<around|\<\|\|\>|<rsup|2>+2*K<rsub|p>*<big|sum><rsub|b>|\<\|\|\>>*D<rsub|b>*u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>+2*\<varepsilon\><rsup|2>*<around|(|K<rsub|f>*H<rsub|f>+K<rsub|p>*H<rsub|p>|)><around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|D<rsub|p>,D<rsub|b>> are the actual direct plaquette and
  predictor derivatives in the identity signed channel. These constants
  include the macrobox and stencil multiplicities: Cauchy\USchwarz bounds the
  square of each local input sum by the stencil cardinality times its sum of
  squares, and each input bond occurs in only a fixed number of these sums
  per coarse anchor. The number of fine bonds over an anchor is
  <math|L<rsup|4>> times the number of axes. The same finite counting applies
  to the plaquette and predictor terms, including on a wrapped torus. Choose
  <math|\<varepsilon\>> before the large-<math|x> threshold so that the final
  coefficient is at most <math|1/2>. The row-selection rule retains a row
  whenever its read stencil meets an active bond. Conversely, if the row is
  omitted, the zero-extended direction vanishes on its entire stencil, so its
  derivative is zero. For a path row the stencil contains the reads of every
  retained path; for a predictor row it contains the finite branch reads.
  Thus inserting row selection does not change the derivative on the
  zero-extended active space. The normalized Gram energy obeys

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<langle\>|a,Q*a|\<rangle\>>\<geqslant\>\<lambda\><rsup|2>*<around*|(|c<rsub|f>*<big|sum><rsub|p><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsub|p>*u*<around|\<\|\|\>|<rsup|2>+c<rsub|p>*<big|sum><rsub|b>|\<\|\|\>>*D<rsub|b>*u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>|)>,
  </equation*>

  with the positive direct and predictor coefficients
  <math|c<rsub|f>,c<rsub|p>>. The remaining signed channels and path rows
  contribute nonnegative squares. Absorbing the error gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>4*<around|(|K<rsub|f>+K<rsub|p>+1|)><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|p><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsub|p>*u*<around|\<\|\|\>|<rsup|2>+<big|sum><rsub|b>|\<\|\|\>>*D<rsub|b>*u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Thus one may take <math|\<eta\><rsub|0>=\<lambda\><rsup|2>*min
  <around|(|c<rsub|f>,c<rsub|p>|)>/<around|{|4*<around|(|K<rsub|f>+K<rsub|p>+1|)>|}>\<gtr\>0>,
  uniformly in all the stated sector choices. The empty active space is
  included. This argument uses the same tree inequality as
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:sector-gram>, but does not identify its
  transported-flat matrix with <math|M>.

  Set <math|K<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>max <around|{|1,L<rsub|0><rsup|2>|}>>. Apply
  the inverse estimates of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-covariance>
  to this actual <math|Q> and its principal submatrices. They give
  <math|w\<gtr\>0> and <math|S\<gtr\>0>, independent of the period, for which

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|Q<rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|w>\<leqslant\>S,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|N|\<\|\|\>><rsub|w>\<leqslant\>S.
  </equation*>

  For the latter assertion the windows vary by column, but every principal
  inverse has the same entrywise exponential bound; summing that bound over
  either anchor set proves both sums. This is why a bound on each column's
  Euclidean norm alone would be insufficient. We may decrease <math|w> to
  ensure <math|w\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>/2>.

  Each actual panel contains every coordinate within anchor distance
  <math|\<rho\>=<around|\<lfloor\>|B/60|\<rfloor\>>> of its center, once
  <math|B\<geqslant\>120>. Indeed the actual panel requires every pair of
  coarse bond endpoints to be at distance at most
  <math|<around|\<lfloor\>|B/20|\<rfloor\>>>. Each endpoint is at distance at
  most one from its anchor, and <math|2+<around|\<lfloor\>|B/60|\<rfloor\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<lfloor\>|B/20|\<rfloor\>>>
  for <math|B\<geqslant\>120>. The triangle inequality proves the asserted
  containment. Equivalently one may use any family of panels with this
  ball-containment property. The principal-inverse equation implies

  <\equation*>
    E\<assign\>Q*N-I,<space|2em>E<rsub|i*j>=0<space|1em><text|if
    ><math-up|dist><around|(|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|j>|)>\<leqslant\>\<rho\>.
  </equation*>

  The product norm gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|E|\<\|\|\>><rsub|w>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|0>*S+1>.
  Remove its exponential weight only on the remaining entries to obtain

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|E|\<\|\|\>><rsub|0>\<leqslant\><around|(|K<rsub|0>*S+1|)>*e<rsup|-w*\<rho\>>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|N-Q<rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|0>\<leqslant\>S*<around|(|K<rsub|0>*S+1|)>*e<rsup|-w*\<rho\>>.
  </equation*>

  The second inequality follows from the ordered identity
  <math|N-Q<rsup|-1>=Q<rsup|-1>*E>.

  Since <math|Q<rsup|-1>> is symmetric, averaging <math|N> with its transpose
  does not increase this error. Also <math|Q\<leqslant\>K<rsub|0>*I>, so
  <math|Q<rsup|-1>\<geqslant\>K<rsub|0><rsup|-1>*I>. Because
  <math|\<rho\>\<to\>\<infty\>>, enlarge the <math|x> threshold until
  <math|S*<around|(|K<rsub|0>*S+1|)>*e<rsup|-w*\<rho\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|2*K<rsub|0>|)><rsup|-1>>.
  Then <math|C\<geqslant\><around|(|2*K<rsub|0>|)><rsup|-1>*I>. Take

  <\equation*>
    \<eta\>=min <around|{|\<eta\><rsub|0>,<around|(|2*K<rsub|0>|)><rsup|-1>|}>,<space|1em>K\<geqslant\>max
    <around|{|1,K<rsub|0>,S|}>,<space|1em>u=S*<around|(|K<rsub|0>*S+1|)>+1,<space|1em>v=w.
  </equation*>

  These choices prove <eqref|eq:global-row-kernels> and
  <eqref|eq:global-row-error>. The argument works on an empty active set as
  well, with all matrix inequalities interpreted on the zero space. No local
  mean or source bound was used to prove these matrix estimates.

  <subsubsection|Selected background rows>

  The first subsection of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:warning-safety>
  proves the needed bound on the affine base strokes by fixed-radius endpoint
  gauges, absolute path sums, the global guide bound and the local predictor
  estimate for the original tags. We check its application to the actual
  selected rows here. This uses only that subsection's base-stroke argument,
  not its subsequent mean calculation.

  A selected row has no coarse flags in its required surrounding ball, by the
  clearance argument following the definition of the stencil. For large
  <math|B>, this contains every fixed chart box needed by a predictor read or
  by a plaquette reached by a retained path. The actual flag identity in the
  proposition therefore gives the local small plaquette condition used by the
  axial gauge. Denote the fixed box constant by <math|c<rsub|\<ast\>>>. Its
  coarse gauge and the parent-lifted replica gauge obey

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:global-selected-gauge>r<around|(|V<rsup|g><rsub|e>|)>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|\<ast\>><math-up|temp><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>r<around|(|<around|(|V<rsup|\<uparrow\>>|)><rsup|g\<circ\><math-up|parent>><rsub|e>|)>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|\<ast\>><math-up|temp><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation>

  A replica link is either the identity or a coarse link. In a signed
  channel, pull back the gauge; reversal moves an anchor by at most one and
  preserves chord under inversion. The extra layer in each fixed box pays
  this displacement. The period condition makes the boxes nonwrapping.

  The cited argument may consequently be applied separately at each path
  target, since only color norms are compared. Four-link curvature costs at
  most <math|4*c<rsub|\<ast\>><math-up|temp><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>8*c<rsub|\<ast\>>*t>;
  its absolute path sum has a fixed price. The predictor uses its fixed local
  logarithm chart, with size <math|O<around|(|t|)>>, and the same original
  tags. The subtracted fine and predictor guides are precisely those of
  <eqref|eq:guide-maximum>; their global bounds do not require flags to
  vanish at an unselected site. Thus the cited proof gives, for the unscaled
  affine base row,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:global-selected-base><around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsub|i><around|(|V|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|b>*t<space|1em><text|on
    every selected row >i,
  </equation>

  with <math|C<rsub|b>\<gtr\>0> fixed before the stores, period and field.
  Selection uses no assumption about the stores' fine costs or their other
  noise entries. This identifies the actual base rows to which the canonical
  argument applies without equating them to their flat approximations.

  <subsubsection|Directions that include frozen links>

  The source contains the derivative evaluated on the frozen displacement,
  which need not be supported on the active links. We require a separate
  supremum estimate on all such directions. Let <math|a> be any fine-link
  Lie-algebra field with <math|sup<rsub|e><around|\<\|\|\>|a<rsub|e>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>A>,
  <math|A\<geqslant\>0>. At a plaquette, the directional derivative is a sum
  of four transported link directions, followed by the fixed curvature
  projection. Hence its norm is bounded by <math|C<rsub|f>*A>, uniformly in
  the base configuration. One can equivalently sum the norms over the fixed
  neighbouring fine bonds; their number is bounded in terms of <math|L>.

  For a transported path row, the differentiated curvature inputs are summed
  with its absolutely summable path coefficients and isometric adjoint
  transports. Thus its bound is the coefficient row sum times
  <math|C<rsub|f>*A>. The direct row is bounded by its fixed square-root
  coefficient times <math|C<rsub|f>*A>. These are the two components of the
  selected linear curvature row. For the predictor derivative, use the local
  branch estimate at <eqref|eq:global-selected-gauge>. It is bounded by a
  constant times the sum of <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|a<rsub|e>|\<\|\|\>>> on
  the fixed predictor stencil, hence by <math|C<rsub|p>*A>. Conjugating
  directions by the local gauge or applying a signed channel preserves their
  norms. The same constants therefore apply to the original direction,
  including its values on frozen links. There is a <math|C<rsub|a>\<gtr\>0>
  such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:global-selected-bump><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-D\><rsub|i><around|(|V|)>*a|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|a>*A<space|1em><text|on
    every selected row >i,
  </equation>

  where <math|\<cal-D\><rsub|i><around|(|V|)>> is the derivative used in the
  actual row construction. Its path transports are held as prescribed in that
  construction; the estimate does not differentiate a newly chosen gauge.

  Finally the frozen input <math|a<around|(|Y|)>> is the patched displacement
  on frozen links and zero on the other links. Its clipping gives
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|a<around|(|Y|)><rsub|e>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*d\<leqslant\>2*r*t>
  for every <math|Y> and every link. The exact source is the selected
  restriction of <math|\<lambda\>*<around|{|b<around|(|V|)>+\<cal-D\><around|(|V|)>*a<around|(|Y|)>|}>>
  in scalar color coordinates. Projection onto a color coordinate has norm at
  most one. Equations <eqref|eq:global-selected-base> and
  <eqref|eq:global-selected-bump> therefore give

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||s<around|(|Y|)><rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<lambda\>*<around|(|C<rsub|b>+2*r*C<rsub|a>|)>*t.
  </equation*>

  Taking <math|Z=\<lambda\>*<around|(|C<rsub|b>+2*r*C<rsub|a>|)>+1> proves
  <eqref|eq:global-row-source>. Intersect the finitely many eventual tails
  used for the matrix, base, branch and source bounds. All their constants
  were fixed before the period, stores, field, tags and frozen input, proving
  the asserted uniformity. No differentiability of the frozen field as a
  function of <math|V> was used or concluded.

  <subsection|The all-pilot palette and the local zero-socket
  test><label|sec:actual-palette-warning>

  This section isolates the warning input used by the opacity construction.
  There are two different estimates here. The palette estimate is a global
  row-and-column bound at spatial rate zero for the actual screened Gaussian
  matrix. The socket conclusion is deterministic: on a prescribed local
  small-noise event, every factor in the local mask product equals one. A
  comparison for one posed reader does not imply the first estimate, and a
  Gaussian probability estimate does not by itself imply the second.

  For <math|x\<gtr\>1> write <math|<math-up|radius><around|(|x|)>=<math-up|radiusB><around|(|x|)>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
  x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>; both names denote this radius below.

  Write <math|\<cal-I\>> for the active link-colour coordinates of a fixed
  forest and <math|\<cal-R\>> for the retained response-row coordinates. A
  move matrix <math|M=<around|(|M<rsub|r*i>|)><rsub|r\<in\>\<cal-R\>,i\<in\>\<cal-I\>>>
  maps active-coordinate vectors to response-row vectors;
  <math|a<around|(|r|)>> and <math|a<around|(|i|)>> denote their anchor
  sites. In the selected application, row anchors include the signed channel
  action specified in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-global-row-prices>.
  For a rectangular matrix define its exponential row-and-column price at
  rate <math|\<sigma\>\<geqslant\>0> by

  <\equation*>
    \<frak-p\><rsub|\<sigma\>><around|(|M|)>=max
    <around*|{|sup<rsub|r\<in\>\<cal-R\>>
    <big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-I\>>e<rsup|\<sigma\>*d*<around|(|a<around|(|r|)>,a<around|(|i|)>|)>><around|\||M<rsub|r*i>|\|>,sup<rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-I\>>
    <big|sum><rsub|r\<in\>\<cal-R\>>e<rsup|\<sigma\>*d*<around|(|a<around|(|r|)>,a<around|(|i|)>|)>><around|\||M<rsub|r*i>|\|>|}>.
  </equation*>

  The bounds below are uniform in the torus size, but their constants may
  depend on the fixed patch, tube, and interaction range. Let <math|Q> be the
  Gram matrix of the actual row family, let <math|W> be a family of principal
  panels, each containing its own coordinate, and put

  <\equation*>
    C=<math-up|clouded><around|(|W,Q|)>,<space|2em>N=<math-up|per><around|(|W,Q|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|s\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|\<cal-R\>>> is the source row,
  <math|b=M<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*s\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|\<cal-I\>>> its
  transported row and <math|m=-N<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*b> the selected mean. The
  corresponding direct and auxiliary rows are

  <\equation*>
    r=M*m+s,<space|2em>z<rsup|<math-up|aux>>=-<around|(|<math-up|coupling><around|(|\<beta\>|)>|)><rsup|-1>*Q*<around*|(|Q<rsup|-1>-N<rsup|<math-ss|T>>|)>*b.
  </equation*>

  These are the finite-dimensional identities behind the estimates; the
  transpose is the ordinary real transpose. The windowed covariance <math|C>
  and its inverse comparison are taken on the same active coordinate set;
  each column of <math|N> uses the inverse on its own panel, extended by
  zero.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Weighted response implies a uniform palette
    bound><label|prop:actual-palette-response>Suppose that, for all
    sufficiently large <math|x>, uniformly in the period, forest, tube,
    choices and configuration under consideration, the following prices hold:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|Q> and <math|C> are positive definite and
      <math|\<frak-p\><rsub|0><around|(|Q|)>,\<frak-p\><rsub|0><around|(|C|)>,\<frak-p\><rsub|0><around|(|N|)>\<leqslant\>K>;

      <item><math|\<frak-p\><rsub|0><around|(|M|)>\<leqslant\>L>;

      <item><math|<around|\||s<rsub|r>|\|>\<leqslant\>Z*<space|0.17em>\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>>
      at every source coordinate <math|r\<in\>\<cal-R\>>, where
      <math|K,L,Z\<gtr\>0>, <math|\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>0>, and
      <math|\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>\<to\>0>; the local width satisfies
      <math|0\<leqslant\>d\<leqslant\>r*\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>> for a fixed
      <math|r\<geqslant\>0>;

      <item>the window defect obeys

      <\equation*>
        \<frak-p\><rsub|0>*<around*|(|N-Q<rsup|-1>|)>\<leqslant\>u*exp
        <around|{|-v<space|0.17em><math-up|radius><around|(|x|)>/60|}>
      </equation*>

      for fixed <math|u,v\<gtr\>0>;

      <item><math|0\<less\>\<beta\>\<leqslant\>4*x> and
      <math|x\<geqslant\>4>. Since <math|g=<math-up|coupling><around|(|\<beta\>|)>=\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>>,
      we have <math|g<rsup|-1>=<sqrt|\<beta\>>\<leqslant\>2*<sqrt|x>\<leqslant\>x>.
      The local door product is a product of broad factors and obeys this
      implication: if it is nonzero, then
      <math|<around|\||m<rsub|i>+g*\<zeta\><rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>1>
      whenever <math|<around|\||m<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>1> and
      <math|2*d\<leqslant\>1>, where <math|g=<math-up|coupling><around|(|\<beta\>|)>>
      and <math|\<zeta\>> is the noise vector.
    </enumerate>

    Then there is a fixed <math|\<ell\>\<geqslant\>1> such that, eventually
    in <math|x>, the following four bounds hold for every configuration row
    in this family:

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||m<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em><around|\||r<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em><around|\||z<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|aux>>|\|>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|-4>,<space|2em><text|door
      product>\<ne\>0\<Longrightarrow\><around|\||\<zeta\><rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    Moreover the screened palette matrix <math|C> satisfies

    <\equation*>
      \<frak-p\><rsub|0><around|(|C|)>\<leqslant\>\<ell\>.
    </equation*>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    For any rectangular matrix <math|A> with
    <math|\<frak-p\><rsub|0><around|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>c> and any vector
    <math|u> on its column set with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|0>>,
    the triangle inequality gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||<around|(|A*u|)><rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|j><around|\||A<rsub|i*j>|\|><space|0.17em><around|\||u<rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>c*b<rsub|0>.
    </equation*>

    The move-map price first gives <math|<around|\||b<rsub|i>|\|>=<around|\||<around|(|M<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*s|)><rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>L*Z*\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>>
    by its column bound. Apply the row-and-column estimate to
    <math|N<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*b>. The column bound for <math|N> is the row
    bound for its transpose, so <math|<around|\||m<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>K*L*Z*\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>>.
    Applying the same calculation to <math|M*m+s> gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||r<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|L<rsup|2>*K*Z+Z|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>.
    </equation*>

    For the auxiliary row, factor the displayed identity exactly as written:

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||z<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|aux>>|\|>\<leqslant\>x*<space|0.17em>K*u*L*Z*\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>*e<rsup|-v<math-up|radius><around|(|x|)>/60>.
    </equation*>

    Because <math|<math-up|radius><around|(|x|)>> grows quadratically in
    <math|log x>, the exponential factor is eventually at most
    <math|x<rsup|-5>>. The right side is then at most
    <math|K*u*L*Z*\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>*x<rsup|-4>>. Since <math|K,u,L,Z>
    are fixed and <math|\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>\<to\>0>, its coefficient is
    eventually at most one; hence <math|<around|\||z<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|aux>>|\|>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|-4>>.
    The first two row bounds also tend to zero, hence are eventually at most
    one. The width condition and <math|\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>\<to\>0>
    ensure <math|2*d\<leqslant\>1> eventually. If the door product is
    nonzero, its broad-span implication gives
    <math|<around|\||m<rsub|i>+g*\<zeta\><rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>1>. Thus
    <math|<around|\||g*\<zeta\><rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>2>, and the
    coupling-price hypothesis gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<zeta\><rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*g<rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>2*x\<leqslant\>x<rsup|2>
    </equation*>

    for <math|x\<geqslant\>2>. Finally, the rate-zero price of <math|C> is
    already at most <math|K>; take <math|\<ell\>=max <around|(|1,K|)>>. This
    proves all conclusions with one threshold, independent of the period and
    of the subsequently chosen configuration.
  </proof>

  The four conclusions, together with <math|\<frak-p\><rsub|0><around|(|C|)>\<leqslant\>\<ell\>>,
  are the complete response receipt needed here: they give a positive palette
  price at rate zero, a mean bound, a direct-row bound, an auxiliary-row
  bound of order <math|x<rsup|-4>>, and the implication from a nonzero door
  product to the noise bound <math|x<rsup|2>>.
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-global-row-prices> supplies the
  distinct global matrix, source-row, and move-row estimates used in this
  calculation.

  In the selected application, Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-global-row-prices>
  supplies the matrix price and gap for <math|Q>, its panelized covariance
  <math|C>, the windowed matrix <math|N>, and the exponentially small price
  for <math|N-Q<rsup|-1>>. It also supplies the source-row bound
  <math|Z<math-up|fiery><around|(|x|)>> and the move-row price <math|L> when
  the local width is strictly positive. Its panel error is stated with
  <math|<around|\<lfloor\>|B/60|\<rfloor\>>>; since
  <math|<around|\<lfloor\>|B/60|\<rfloor\>>\<geqslant\>B/60-1>, enlarging
  <math|u> by the fixed factor <math|e<rsup|v>> gives the form used in the
  proposition. The calculation above is included because it shows exactly how
  these different receipts combine, with no factor depending on the number of
  sites. The local covariance comparison of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-reader-local-gaussian-prices> is a
  distinct input; its entrywise estimate cannot replace these global
  row-and-column bounds.

  <subsubsection|Why the palette estimate is all-matched>

  For a pilot <math|p>, let <math|\<Pi\><rsub|p>> be its endpoint palette,
  namely the normalized Gaussian matrix on the forest coordinates after
  retaining the panel addresses. A palette has a <math|\<sigma\>>-price
  <math|C> when its two weighted sums above are at most <math|C>. The
  endpoint convention identifies <math|\<Pi\><rsub|p>> exactly with the
  clouded panel Gram matrix <math|C>; it is not an approximate identification
  and involves no limiting field.

  For each matched pilot, specialize the uniform response estimate to that
  pilot's store, forest, configuration and tag. The endpoint palette then
  satisfies

  <\equation*>
    \<frak-p\><rsub|0><around|(|\<Pi\><rsub|p>|)>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><space|2em><text|for
    every matched >p.
  </equation*>

  Because the matrix prices were obtained uniformly before selecting the
  pilot, the same constant works for the entire matched family. This
  universal quantifier comes from all-configuration screened-kernel bounds,
  not from testing a dense set of readers or passing from one posed pilot by
  continuity.

  <subsubsection|The local zero-socket implication>

  Fix a matched pilot <math|p> and a mask address <math|i>, and put
  <math|q<rsub|i>=<math-up|toes><around|(|i|)>> and
  <math|B=<math-up|radius><around|(|x|)>>. Write <math|z<rsub|p>> for its
  local fluctuation vector and <math|g=<math-up|coupling><around|(|\<beta\>|)>\<gtr\>0>.
  For a site <math|o>, define <math|<math-ss|Toned><around|(|R,o,z,h|)>> to
  mean <math|<around|\||z<rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>h> for each active
  coordinate <math|j> whose anchor is at distance at most <math|R> from
  <math|o>. The local small-noise condition is

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|Toned><around|(|2*B,q<rsub|i>,z<rsub|p>,\<varepsilon\>*<space|0.17em>t/g|)>,<space|2em>t=<math-up|fiery><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  By definition, the socket is zero when the support foot meets the store's
  radius-<math|300*B> dome. It remains to treat the branch in which the foot
  and that dome are disjoint. The foot contains its toe <math|q<rsub|i>>.
  Since the radius-<math|50*B> dome is contained in the radius-<math|300*B>
  dome, disjointness immediately gives <math|q<rsub|i>\<nin\>A.*d*o*m*e*<around|(|50*B|)>>.

  The beacon receipt is evaluated on the actual selected configuration,
  store, tag and mean-adjusted field. At every site <math|y> outside the
  radius-<math|40*B> store dome, it gives active tape coordinates of norm
  below the chart radius, fine-face toll at most <math|t/4>, coarse-bond wire
  at most <math|W*t>, and both normalized likelihoods equal to one, provided
  <math|<math-ss|Toned><around|(|B,y,z<rsub|p>,\<varepsilon\>*t/g|)>> holds.
  The toe is outside the radius-<math|50*B> dome. Every site within distance
  <math|B> of the toe is therefore outside the radius-<math|40*B> dome.
  Restricting the radius-<math|2*B> toned bound gives the required
  radius-<math|B> bound at these sites. The face, bond-wire and frame parts
  of the beacon receipt consequently give the local outpost condition.

  The port estimate turns this local condition into the door bound. Let
  <math|N<rsub|<math-up|port>>> be the number of plaquettes in the fixed
  macrobox for the patch (the macrobox site count times the number of
  plaquette orientations). The port inequality sums at most
  <math|N<rsub|<math-up|port>>> face tolls, and the braid inequality bounds
  the connector by <math|W*t>. Thus the chord of the relative link at any
  active row is at most <math|<around|(|C<rsub|0>*N<rsub|<math-up|port>>+W|)>*t>,
  where <math|C<rsub|0>> is the fixed port constant. On the chart ball, the
  logarithm has norm at most <math|K<rsub|0>> times this chord. The beacon
  places the actual relative link in the injectivity ball, so its logarithm
  is the actual tape coordinate. Therefore the outpost condition and the
  distant-door estimate give

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|\<ell\><rsub|p,j>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|\<ast\>>*t,<space|2em>C<rsub|\<ast\>>=K<rsub|0>*<around|(|C<rsub|0>*N<rsub|<math-up|port>>+W|)>,
  </equation*>

  for each active door row. An inactive door factor equals one by its
  definition. The selected width has <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>*t\<leqslant\><math-up|arc>>,
  so the broad factor equals one for active rows too. The integer
  <math|N<rsub|<math-up|port>>> is fixed by the patch and does not grow with
  the torus.

  The mask has five coordinate types: fine-face share, coarse-bond wire
  share, site likelihood, bond likelihood, and door. The first two equal one
  because their nonnegative tolls are below their full-share thresholds; the
  likelihoods equal one by the beacon receipt; and the door equals one by the
  preceding argument. Thus the local ask is one, so the socket (ask minus
  one) is zero. Every estimate is on the same matched pilot; there is no
  perturbation or replacement of its store or tag.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Exact assembly of the palette condition><label|prop:actual-palette-condition>Assume
    <math|\<varepsilon\>\<geqslant\>0> and <math|\<beta\>\<gtr\>0>, and the
    uniform rate-zero response prices of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:actual-palette-response>;
    these give a common price bound <math|\<ell\>\<geqslant\>1>. Let
    <math|\<cal-P\>> be the set of matched pilots. Assume the selected field
    and forest satisfy the same-history response conditions of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-global-row-prices>, and that the
    following uniform local receipts hold for every <math|p\<in\>\<cal-P\>>
    and mask address: the disjoint-foot branch geometry, beacon estimates,
    port estimate, and distant-door estimate just stated. These receipts are
    on the same field, forest, tag and store, with thresholds uniform over
    the matched family. Then for

    <\equation*>
      q=\<varepsilon\><space|0.17em><math-up|fiery><around|(|x|)>/<math-up|coupling><around|(|\<beta\>|)>
    </equation*>

    the endpoint palette has a uniform Pours price at rate zero, with a
    positive price constant, for every matched pilot. Its socket vanishes
    whenever the specified <math|<math-ss|Toned><around|(|2*B,q<rsub|i>,z<rsub|p>,\<varepsilon\>*t/g|)>>
    condition holds. These are the two components of the all-matched palette
    condition used by the opacity step.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The rate-zero matrix estimate is uniform over the matched family, so its
    constant is the common Pours price <math|\<ell\>>. For each matched pilot
    and each mask address, the local argument above gives zero socket under
    its toned hypothesis. Since neither conclusion changes the pilot, the
    quantified statements hold for the full matched family.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The selected all-pilot package>

  The geometric and local receipts are selected together. For a fixed patch,
  a permitted chart ratio, a positive mask width <math|W>, and the reserve
  and wedge hypotheses of the sector construction, there is a lower scale
  <math|L<rsub|0>> and a beacon parameter <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>. For
  each fixed chosen width <math|\<lambda\><rsub|<math-up|w>>\<geqslant\>L<rsub|0>>,
  the chart and size conditions, the annular response at width
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|<math-up|w>>>, the local beacon, and the distant-door
  estimate all hold eventually in <math|x>. Their cutoff is uniform over
  legal stores, forests, tags, configurations and mask addresses, hence over
  matched pilots. The port/log-chart and distant-door route gives the fixed
  constant <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>> above; choose
  <math|L<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>C<rsub|\<ast\>>>, so
  <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>*t\<leqslant\>\<lambda\><rsub|<math-up|w>>*t=<math-up|arc>>.
  The annular estimate is a separate component of the selected package.

  Here is the mean-switch argument that makes the beacon a statement about
  the actual sewn field. The centered affine background (CAB) calibration is
  an auxiliary calculation; its matrices must not be identified with the
  actual-reader matrices <math|Q,C,N> above. Fix one selected store, forest,
  tag, field <math|V>, and a site <math|o> outside the radius-<math|30*B>
  store dome, where <math|B=<math-up|radiusB><around|(|x|)>>. Write
  <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>=2*\<varepsilon\>>, where <math|\<varepsilon\>>
  is the final all-matched beacon fee and <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>> is
  the CAB plateau threshold. The based chart construction supplies a gauge
  and a chart vector <math|b> with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|b|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><math-up|clearance><around|(|x|)>/2>,
  agreeing with that gauge on the radius-<math|20*B> room. Write <math|v> for
  the corresponding local affine direction. For the auxiliary finite
  calibration, write <math|H> for its sheet, <math|P<rsub|<math-up|CAB>>> for
  its within-sheet map, <math|E=H*P<rsub|<math-up|CAB>>-I> for its error, and
  <math|s<rsub|<math-up|CAB>>> for its source. The exact finite-dimensional
  subtraction identity is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:actual-palette-cab-subtraction>\<mu\><rsub|<math-up|CAB>>-v=-P<rsub|<math-up|CAB>><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*<around|(|H*v+s<rsub|<math-up|CAB>>|)>+E<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*v.
  </equation>

  It follows by expanding <math|E=H*P<rsub|<math-up|CAB>>-I> and the
  definitions of the centered calibration mean. To state the paid rates
  without source shorthand, put

  <\equation*>
    \<rho\><rsub|r,k><around|(|x|)>=<math-up|radiusB><around|(|x|)><rsup|k><space|0.17em><math-up|fiery><around|(|x|)><rsup|r>.
  </equation*>

  The bought central residual and error-sheet estimates are, respectively,
  the rates <math|\<rho\><rsub|2,25>> and <math|\<rho\><rsub|2,26>>; the
  selected scale schedule includes <math|\<rho\><rsub|2,27><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|-7/8>>
  eventually. In the first term of (<reference|eq:actual-palette-cab-subtraction>),
  the central residual bound <math|\<rho\><rsub|2,25>> is multiplied by the
  fixed within-sheet row price; one additional factor of the radius is
  absorbed into the next rate, giving <math|\<rho\><rsub|2,26>>. The second
  term is the error-sheet tail, also at that rate. The reserve's allowance
  growth then gives, coordinatewise,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:actual-palette-cab-velocity><around|\||<around|(|\<mu\><rsub|<math-up|CAB>>-v|)><rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<rho\><rsub|2,27><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|-7/8>.
  </equation>

  These are the two matrix terms in (<reference|eq:actual-palette-cab-subtraction>);
  no equality between the auxiliary calibration law and the actual palette
  law is used.

  The chart comparison of the actual portrait with this same affine direction
  has a second, separately paid error. The finite chart estimate and the
  identity between the local face read and its short-row read give a
  per-color error at most <math|x<rsup|-7/8>>; summing over the fixed color
  coordinates and combining with (<reference|eq:actual-palette-cab-velocity>)
  gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:actual-palette-mean-switch><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rprime|'><rsub|j>-\<mu\><rsub|<math-up|CAB>,j>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*dim
    <around|(|G<rsub|<math-up|color>>|)>*x<rsup|-7/8>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|-3/4>
  </equation>

  for all sufficiently large <math|x>. Here <math|D<rprime|'>> is the
  portrait of the original selected field, in the local chart, and the last
  inequality is just a fixed-dimension power comparison. If the local
  fluctuation is <math|z> and <math|g=<math-up|coupling><around|(|\<beta\>|)>\<gtr\>0>,
  the switched active coordinate is exactly

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|D<rprime|'>+g*z-\<mu\><rsub|<math-up|CAB>>|)><rsub|j>.
  </equation*>

  For any point in the radius-<math|B> neighborhood of <math|o>,
  (<reference|eq:actual-palette-mean-switch>) and the Toned bound
  <math|<around|\||z<rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>*t/<around|(|2*g|)>>
  imply

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||<around|(|D<rprime|'>+g*z-\<mu\><rsub|<math-up|CAB>>|)><rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|-3/4>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>*t/2\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>*t
  </equation*>

  eventually; the final step uses the bought threshold
  <math|x<rsup|-3/4>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>*t/2>. Thus the shifted
  affine field is Awake with threshold <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>*t> on
  that neighborhood.

  The charged plateau estimate is applied to this auxiliary affine field.
  Write <math|W<rsub|<math-up|wire>>=W>, <math|a<rsub|\<ast\>>=<sqrt|<math-up|direct>>>,
  and <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>>=<sqrt|<math-up|penalty>>>. The reserve and wedge
  purchases give <math|a<rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0>,
  <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0>, and the uniform row price satisfies
  <math|W<rsub|<math-up|row>>\<gtr\>0>; these are the positive quadratic and
  penalty floors in the selected calibration.

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:actual-palette-cab-wedge><frac|C|L<rsup|2>>\<leqslant\><frac|a<rsub|\<ast\>>|64>,<space|2em>C+<frac|a<rsub|\<ast\>>|64>\<leqslant\><frac|W<rsub|<math-up|wire>>*b<rsub|\<ast\>>|4>.
  </equation>

  Let <math|W<rsub|<math-up|row>>> be the bought uniform row price for the
  selected CAB system and let <math|\<omega\><rsub|<math-up|CAB>>\<gtr\>0> be
  its fixed quadratic-response weight. Set
  <math|\<delta\>=a<rsub|\<ast\>>/64> and choose the plateau threshold

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:actual-palette-cab-target-fee>\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>=min
    <around*|{|1,<frac|\<delta\>*<sqrt|\<omega\><rsub|<math-up|CAB>>>|4*<around|(|1+<sqrt|dim
    <around|(|G<rsub|<math-up|color>>|)>>|)>*W<rsub|<math-up|row>>>|}>.
  </equation>

  The local target estimate gives target size at most <math|\<delta\>*t>
  under this shift. Here is its payment. The action of the CAB mean is
  bounded by the rate <math|\<rho\><rsub|2,29>>, which is eventually at most
  <math|W<rsub|<math-up|row>>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>*t>; the sparse shifted
  action has the same bound. Thus the full shifted action is at most
  <math|2*W<rsub|<math-up|row>>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>*t>. In the target
  expansion, the linear term is at most <math|\<delta\>*t/2> by the choice in
  (<reference|eq:actual-palette-cab-target-fee>), and the nonlinear term is
  at most <math|\<rho\><rsub|2,40>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>*t/2> by the paid rate
  comparison. Their sum is at most <math|\<delta\>*t>. Decreasing a permitted
  shift threshold preserves these inequalities, so the minimum with one in
  (<reference|eq:actual-palette-cab-target-fee>) is harmless.

  The first inequality in (<reference|eq:actual-palette-cab-wedge>) bounds
  the unshifted face response by <math|a<rsub|\<ast\>>*t/64>. The paid target
  response changes it by at most <math|a<rsub|\<ast\>>*t/64>, so the
  resulting row has norm at most <math|a<rsub|\<ast\>>*t/32>. The face read
  is <math|a<rsub|\<ast\>>> times the curvature norm of the face holonomy, so
  that curvature norm is at most <math|t/32>. On the purchased chart the face
  holonomy has chord at most the radius <math|\<delta\><rsub|G>> of the local
  curvature estimate <math|<tfrac|3|4><math-up|chord><around|(|U|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|\<kappa\><rsub|G><around|(|U|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>
  recalled in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-reader-guarded-curvature>;
  hence its chord is at most twice its curvature norm. The face toll is the
  chord of the face holonomy, so it is at most <math|t/16\<less\>t/4>. For a
  bond row the unshifted term is at most <math|C*t> and the response term at
  most <math|a<rsub|\<ast\>>*t/64>. Its normalized read is
  <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>>> times its curvature norm, so the second inequality
  in (<reference|eq:actual-palette-cab-wedge>) gives curvature at most
  <math|W<rsub|<math-up|wire>>*t/4>. Chord is at most twice curvature on this
  chart; hence wire is below <math|W<rsub|<math-up|wire>>*t>. The likelihood
  plateau estimate sets the site and bond factors equal to one.

  For the broad chart, the reserve bounds each CAB-mean color coordinate by
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|1,12>>. The shift coordinates are at most
  <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>*t>. Because
  <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>\<leqslant\>1> and
  <math|<math-up|radiusB><around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>1>,
  <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>*t\<leqslant\>\<rho\><rsub|1,13>> and
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|1,12>\<leqslant\>\<rho\><rsub|1,13>>. The Euclidean
  color norm of the shifted row is at most <math|2*<sqrt|dim
  <around|(|G<rsub|<math-up|color>>|)>>*\<rho\><rsub|1,13>>, which the
  purchased chart budget bounds by the chart radius. Thus the single
  threshold <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>> ensures the target, face, wire and
  chart conclusions for every allowed calibration and every local shift. The
  coordinate-to-color step is the elementary inequality
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><sqrt|dim
  <around|(|G<rsub|<math-up|color>>|)>>*max<rsub|j><around|\||a<rsub|j>|\|>>.

  Finally the CAB construction is sewn back without changing the physical
  field. Let <math|\<cal-B\>> be the auxiliary configuration map built from
  the original field <math|V> and its frozen-link data, and let
  <math|a=D<rprime|'>+g*z> be the desired active read. Define
  <math|\<chi\><around|(|a|)>> to be <math|a-\<mu\><rsub|<math-up|CAB>>> on
  active coordinates and zero on frozen coordinates. The sewing identity is,
  link by link,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:actual-palette-cab-shelter>\<cal-B\>*<around|(|\<mu\><rsub|<math-up|CAB>>+\<chi\><around|(|a|)>|)>=V<rsub|<math-up|sewn>><around|(|a|)>,
  </equation>

  where <math|V<rsub|<math-up|sewn>><around|(|a|)>> uses the prescribed tape
  <math|a> on active links and the original stored values on frozen links. On
  a trunk both sides are the identity. On an active link the auxiliary mean
  cancels and leaves exactly the prescribed tape <math|a>. On a frozen link
  the correction is zero and the stored link is unchanged. Thus the plateau
  bounds apply to the actual sewn configuration. This proves the local beacon
  receipt used in the socket argument; its uniformity in all stores, forests,
  tags and periods is the uniformity of the chart, reserve and wedge
  purchases, before the pilot is chosen.

  Now fix one such selected field, with <math|0\<less\>\<beta\>\<leqslant\>4*x>,
  local width <math|0\<less\>d\<leqslant\>u*t>, chosen broad-response width
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|<math-up|w>>\<geqslant\>L<rsub|0>>, and arc
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|<math-up|w>>*t>. Suppose its budget certificate gives
  both the chart compatibility needed for the beacon and the auxiliary sector
  comparison, and suppose the all-matched response estimate above holds. For
  each matched pilot the actual response receipt gives the endpoint Pours
  bound with the common constant <math|\<ell\>>. The deterministic argument
  above gives zero socket under <math|<math-ss|Toned><around|(|2*B,q<rsub|i>,z<rsub|p>,\<varepsilon\>*t/<math-up|coupling><around|(|\<beta\>|)>|)>>.
  The fee <math|\<varepsilon\>*t/<math-up|coupling><around|(|\<beta\>|)>> is
  nonnegative. Thus one and the same selected field satisfies both palette
  clauses for all matched pilots. This is the exact two-clause output used in
  the later opacity estimate; it is not obtained by selecting a different
  field for each pilot.

  In Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:actual-palette-condition> these inputs
  enter as follows. An enlarged patch supplies the local chart, while the
  separate port/log-chart estimate and the distant-door estimate supply the
  far-door bound. The annular response supplies its own fixed-scale response
  receipt; it does not supply the port constant <math|C<rsub|0>>. Combining
  these with the all-configuration rate-zero palette estimate gives the two
  hypotheses of the proposition on one selected sector. The
  correlated-warning bound of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-warnings>
  prices the probability of violating many such local conditions, while
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:warning-safety> proves the deterministic
  likelihood and row plateaus on the small-fluctuation event. The annular
  argument in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:packet-annulus> controls the
  energy outside the packet core. None of these three statements alone is the
  all-matched Pours estimate; that part remains tied to the global
  kernel-price supplier.

  <subsection|The actual Haar density in exponential
  coordinates><label|sec:haar-chart>

  The chart factor in the packet is the density of probability Haar measure.
  We construct it from the given local exponential and logarithm, keeping its
  scalar normalization. Write <math|\<frak-g\>> for the physical colour
  space, with its counting Lebesgue measure, and <math|exp> for the group
  exponential. The local logarithm is inverse to <math|exp> near zero. After
  shrinking the radius, <math|exp> is a homeomorphism from a ball <math|D>
  about zero onto an open neighbourhood <math|U> of the identity. To check
  openness, if <math|b> is in a sufficiently small open set <math|O>,
  continuity of the local logarithm gives a neighbourhood of <math|exp b>
  whose logarithms lie in <math|O>; the local right-inverse identity places
  that neighbourhood in <math|exp O>.

  For a group element <math|u>, where the expression stays in the chart set

  <\equation*>
    T<rsub|u><around|(|b|)>=log <around|(|u*exp
    b|)>,<space|2em>H<around|(|b|)>=D*T<rsub|<around|(|exp
    b|)><rsup|-1>><around|(|b|)>,<space|2em>J<around|(|b|)>=det
    H<around|(|b|)>.
  </equation*>

  In the left trivialization, <math|H<around|(|b|)>=<around|(|d*L<rsub|exp
  <around|(|-b|)>>|)><rsub|exp b>*D*exp <around|(|b|)>>. The exponential
  differential formula <cite-detail|DuistermaatKolk|Theorem<nbsp>1.5.3,
  Eq.<nbsp>(1.5.1)> gives

  <\equation*>
    H<around|(|b|)>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1>e<rsup|-t<math-up|ad><space|0.17em>b>*<space|0.17em>d*t.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|J> is analytic with <math|J<around|(|0|)>=1>. Shrink <math|D> so
  that <math|1/2\<less\>J\<less\>2> there. Transporting Lebesgue density at
  the identity by left translations defines a smooth left-invariant density
  on all of <math|G>. It has finite nonzero total mass by compactness, so its
  normalization is probability Haar measure by uniqueness. Its pullback to
  <math|D> has density <math|J<around|(|b|)>> by the left trivialization
  above. In particular, with <math|m<rsub|G>> denoting probability Haar
  measure and <math|c<rsub|<math-up|chart>>=m<rsub|G><around|(|U|)>/<big|int><rsub|D>J<around|(|b|)>*<space|0.17em>d*b>,
  we have

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:haar-chart-measure>exp<rsub|\<ast\>><around*|(|1<rsub|D>*c<rsub|<math-up|chart>>*J<around|(|b|)>*<space|0.17em>d*b|)>=m<rsub|G>\|<rsub|U>.
  </equation>

  The normalization uses the entire compact group, including all components
  if <math|G> is disconnected.

  <subsubsection|The protected chart correction>

  Fix <math|a\<gtr\>0> and use the prescribed small fluctuation scale
  <math|\<tau\>=P/<sqrt|h>\<gtr\>0> from <eqref|eq:warning-scales>, where
  <math|h> is the fine reference coupling. (In <eqref|eq:warning-scales> the
  letter <math|t> denotes the larger coordinate warning threshold
  <math|\<xi\>*\<tau\>*<sqrt|\<beta\>>>; it does not occur in this section.)
  The scale <math|\<tau\>> has upper exponent <math|-1/2>, and
  <math|\<tau\><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\><sqrt|h>> because <math|P\<geqslant\>1>;
  thus its reciprocal has upper exponent <math|1/2>. Let
  <math|\<chi\>:<around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>\<to\><around|[|0,1|]>> be the smooth
  cutoff, equal to one on <math|<around|[|0,1|]>> and zero on
  <math|<around|[|4,\<infty\>|)>>. For a fluctuation bond <math|u<rsub|i>>
  set

  <\equation*>
    \<gamma\><rsub|a><around|(|u<rsub|i>|)>=\<chi\><space|-0.17em><around*|(|<frac|<around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>|a<rsup|2>*\<tau\><rsup|2>>|)>,<space|2em>j<rsub|i><around|(|u|)>=\<gamma\><rsub|2*a><around|(|u<rsub|i>|)>*log
    J<around|(|u<rsub|i>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Outside the chart this last expression can use any specified measurable
  extension of <math|J> and the total real logarithm convention; the cutoff
  makes its value zero there once <math|4*a*\<tau\>> is smaller than the
  chart radius. Where <math|\<gamma\><rsub|a><around|(|u<rsub|i>|)>\<ne\>0>,
  we have <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*a*\<tau\>>,
  and hence <math|\<gamma\><rsub|2*a><around|(|u<rsub|i>|)>=1>. Positivity of
  <math|J> then proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:haar-chart-exact>e<rsup|j<rsub|i><around|(|u|)>>=J<around|(|u<rsub|i>|)><space|1em><text|where
    >\<gamma\><rsub|a><around|(|u<rsub|i>|)>\<ne\>0.
  </equation>

  Thus the doubled cutoff preserves the true Haar factor on the support of
  the narrower protected integral.

  Since <math|log J> is analytic near zero and vanishes there, the
  fundamental theorem of calculus on a smaller closed ball gives
  <math|<around|\||log J<around|(|v|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>>.
  If <math|\<gamma\><rsub|2*a><around|(|u<rsub|i>|)>> is nonzero,
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>4*a*\<tau\>>; if it
  is zero, <math|j<rsub|i>=0>. Therefore, globally in <math|u>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:haar-chart-value><around|\||j<rsub|i><around|(|u|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>4*a*C*\<tau\>.
  </equation>

  In particular the bound <math|<around|\||j<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>1> used
  in the packet estimate holds at one sufficiently large coupling threshold,
  uniformly in the period and in the number of chart bonds.

  We record the differentiated version with its exact incoming premise.
  Suppose a variation <math|u<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>> is <math|C<rsup|k>> near
  <math|y<rsub|0>>, and its increment <math|u<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>-u<rsub|i><around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>>
  has derivatives through order <math|k> bounded by <math|v<around|(|x|)>>,
  where <math|v> has upper exponent <math|-1/2>. This is the compensated
  variation supplied by the Gaussian change of variables; its production is
  separate from the chart calculation here. If
  <math|\<gamma\><rsub|b><around|(|u<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>|)>> is zero on a
  neighbourhood of <math|y<rsub|0>>, all its jets vanish. Otherwise
  continuity gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|i><around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*b*\<tau\>>.
  Thus every derivative, including order zero, of <math|u<rsub|i>> at
  <math|y<rsub|0>> is bounded by <math|v+2*b*\<tau\>>. For
  <math|g=u<rsub|i>/<around|(|b*\<tau\>|)>> these bounds are at most

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|b>=<around|(|b*\<tau\>|)><rsup|-1>*<around|(|v+2*b*\<tau\>|)>,
  </equation*>

  which has upper exponent zero. The derivatives of
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>> are bounded by a fixed
  polynomial in <math|K<rsub|b>>; this follows directly by the product rule
  for the inner product. Derivatives of <math|\<chi\>> are bounded on
  <math|<around|[|0,4|]>>, and its value is at most one. The chain rule,
  indexed by partitions of the derivative arguments, therefore gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:haar-cutoff-jets><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r>*\<gamma\><rsub|b><around|(|u<rsub|i><around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|b><around|(|x|)><rsup|r>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>k,
  </equation>

  for an <math|L<rsub|b>\<geqslant\>1> of upper exponent zero. The zero-order
  inequality uses the range of <math|\<chi\>>; a uniform amplitude from the
  positive-order bounds can be absorbed into <math|L<rsub|b>>.

  Apply this to the broader cutoff <math|b=2*a>. If its germ vanishes, so do
  all jets of <math|j<rsub|i>>. Otherwise
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|i><around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>4*a*\<tau\>>,
  and all jets of <math|u<rsub|i>> are bounded by <math|V=v+4*a*\<tau\>>,
  which tends to zero. All derivatives of <math|log J> through order <math|k>
  are bounded on a fixed smaller chart ball. For positive derivative order,
  each partition term in the chain rule contains at least one derivative of
  <math|u<rsub|i>>; since <math|V\<leqslant\>1>, its product is at most
  <math|V>. At order zero use <math|<around|\||log
  J<around|(|u<rsub|i><around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*V>. Hence

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r>*<around|(|log
    J\<circ\>u<rsub|i>|)><around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|k>*V*<space|1em><around|(|0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>k|)>.
  </equation*>

  Leibniz's formula with <eqref|eq:haar-cutoff-jets> yields

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:haar-chart-jets><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r>*j<rsub|i><around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|k>*V*<around|(|1+L<rsub|2*a>|)><rsup|k>*<space|1em><around|(|0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>k|)>.
  </equation>

  The common right-hand side has upper exponent <math|-1/2>. The argument
  covers points on the mask boundary and arbitrary Gaussian inputs: smallness
  is inferred from the nonvanishing germ of the protected factor, not assumed
  for the Gaussian field globally.

  <subsection|Local normalization and its
  derivatives><label|sec:normalization-jets>

  The local scalar correction is a difference between the actual sector and
  its flat reference. The reference uses the same forest, tags, cutoff radius
  and windows; its coarse field is the identity and its seed sets are empty.
  Write <math|A<rsup|\<circ\>>> for its completed precision matrix. With the
  local colour trace <math|J<rsub|e><around|(|A|)>> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-covariance>, the correction at a
  physical bond is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:normalization-correction>c<rsub|e>=<frac|1|2>*<around|{|J<rsub|e><around|(|A<rsup|\<circ\>>|)>-J<rsub|e><around|(|A|)>|}>.
  </equation>

  We first establish its scalar bounds and then differentiate the actual
  window formula. A bound on the full determinant would not provide these
  local estimates.

  <subsubsection|Uniform and remote scalar bounds>

  Let <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<eta\>>,<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsup|\<circ\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<eta\>>\<leqslant\>Q>
  and let <math|S> be the uniform row and column bound for every principal
  inverse along <math|A<rsub|s>=1+s*<around|(|A-1|)>> and the corresponding
  flat interpolation, <math|0\<leqslant\>s\<leqslant\>1>. The local formula
  is

  <\equation*>
    J<rsub|e><around|(|A|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>=1><rsup|q><big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1><around|[|<around|(|A-1|)>*P<rsub|s>|]><rsub|<around|(|e,\<alpha\>|)>,<around|(|e,\<alpha\>|)>>*<space|0.17em>d*s.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|<around|\||J<rsub|e><around|(|A|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>q*<around|(|Q+1|)>*S>,
  and the same holds for <math|A<rsup|\<circ\>>>. By
  <eqref|eq:normalization-correction>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:normalization-uniform><around|\||c<rsub|e>|\|>\<leqslant\>q*<around|(|Q+1|)>*S.
  </equation>

  Both precisions meet the hypotheses of the Gram estimate: the flat
  reference has no flags, and gauge transformations preserve the flags of the
  actual sector. Hence the same post-<math|L> constants can be used for the
  actual, flat and gauge-transformed sectors.

  If the anchor of <math|e> is farther than <math|30*B> from the seeds,
  choose a single gauge making the coarse bond chords at most
  <math|C*w<around|(|x|)>> on the radius-<math|6*B> ball, as in
  <eqref|eq:mean-common-log>. Here <math|w> has upper exponent <math|-1/2>.
  For any coordinate in the window of <math|<around|(|e,\<alpha\>|)>>, its
  anchor lies within <math|B+2> of that of <math|e>. On these coordinates the
  inactive diagonal of the gauged sector agrees with the flat reference:
  exactly the tree bonds are inactive. If an entry of the selected row matrix
  meets such a coordinate, either its row anchor lies within <math|B/4> or
  both entries are zero. In the former case that row is selected in both
  sectors. The gauge transport comparison, applied to the same unit
  coordinate input, gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||M<rprime|'><rsub|p*i>-M<rsup|\<circ\>><rsub|p*i>|\|>\<leqslant\>d<around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>d<around|(|x|)>=C<rsub|1>*<around|(|B+1|)>*w<around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  The same estimate holds for the other tested coordinate. All transport
  paths lie in the common <math|6*B> hull. Expanding the two products in the
  Gram entry gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||M<rprime|'><rsub|p*i>*M<rprime|'><rsub|p*j>-M<rsup|\<circ\>><rsub|p*i>*M<rsup|\<circ\>><rsub|p*j>|\|>\<leqslant\>d<around|(|x|)>\|M<rprime|'><rsub|p*j>\|+d<around|(|x|)><around|\||M<rsup|\<circ\>><rsub|p*i>|\|>.
  </equation*>

  Sum over <math|p> and use the column bound <math|Y> for both row matrices:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:normalization-plate-near><around|\||A<rprime|'><rsub|i*j>-A<rsup|\<circ\>><rsub|i*j>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*Y*d<around|(|x|)>=:\<delta\><around|(|x|)><space|1em><text|on
    each tested principal window>.
  </equation>

  No count of all rows enters this bound.

  The window contains at most <math|\<Delta\><rsub|B>=m*<around|(|2*B+5|)><rsup|4>>
  coordinates. The local resolvent comparison of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-covariance> now yields

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||J<rsub|e><around|(|A<rprime|'>|)>-J<rsub|e><around|(|A<rsup|\<circ\>>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>q*<around|{|S+<around|(|Q+1|)>*S<rsup|2>|}>*\<Delta\><rsub|B>*\<delta\><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  The complete colour trace is gauge invariant; the endpoint windows are
  invariant under colour rotations. Therefore
  <math|J<rsub|e><around|(|A<rprime|'>|)>=J<rsub|e><around|(|A|)>>, and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:normalization-remote><around|\||c<rsub|e>|\|>\<leqslant\>q*<around|{|S+<around|(|Q+1|)>*S<rsup|2>|}>*\<Delta\><rsub|B>*\<delta\><around|(|x|)>=:a<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>d*<around|(|e,<text|seeds>|)>\<gtr\>30*B.
  </equation>

  We dropped a harmless factor <math|1/2>. Since <math|B> has upper exponent
  zero, <math|a<rsub|0>> has upper exponent <math|-1/2>.

  <subsubsection|Matrix derivative bounds with spatial weights>

  For a <math|C<rsup|k>> matrix-valued map <math|A<around|(|y|)>> near
  <math|y<rsub|0>>, a convenient quantitative statement, for
  <math|f,v,\<gamma\>\<geqslant\>0>, is the existence of a nonnegative matrix
  <math|H> such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:matrix-jet-envelope><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r>*A<rsub|i*j><around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|i*j>*v<rsup|r>*<space|1em><around|(|0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>k|)>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|H|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<gamma\>>\<leqslant\>f.
  </equation>

  The norm on the right is the maximum of the weighted row and column sums.
  Thus it controls a common entrywise majorant of all jets, not just the norm
  of each derivative separately. If <math|A> and <math|B> have bounds
  <math|<around|(|f,v|)>> and <math|<around|(|g,z|)>> with
  <math|g,z\<geqslant\>0>, Leibniz's rule gives the envelope <math|H*K> for
  their product with bounds <math|<around|(|f*g,v+z|)>>: the binomial sum is
  <math|<around|(|v+z|)><rsup|r>>, and the triangle inequality for distance
  proves <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|H*K|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<gamma\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|H|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<gamma\>><around|\<\|\|\>|K|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<gamma\>>>.
  Addition, transpose and restriction to a principal set preserve the
  corresponding bounds. These operations introduce no volume factor.

  Suppose <math|A<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>> is invertible and its inverse has
  weighted price <math|g>. Set <math|F=A<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)><rsup|-1>> and
  <math|V<around|(|y|)>=1-F*A<around|(|y|)>>. Then
  <math|V<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>=0>, and, wherever <math|A<around|(|y|)>> is
  invertible,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:inverse-finite-jet>A<around|(|y|)><rsup|-1>=<big|sum><rsub|r=0><rsup|k>V<around|(|y|)><rsup|r>*F+V<around|(|y|)><rsup|k+1>*A<around|(|y|)><rsup|-1>.
  </equation>

  Multiplying the finite geometric identity on the right by
  <math|A<around|(|y|)><rsup|-1>> proves this without commuting any factors.
  Invertibility holds on a neighbourhood by continuity of the determinant.
  The inverse is <math|C<rsup|k>> there by the adjugate formula. Every
  derivative of order at most <math|k> of <math|V<rsup|k+1>> vanishes at
  <math|y<rsub|0>>: in each Leibniz term at least one of the <math|k+1>
  factors is undifferentiated and equals zero. The remainder in
  <eqref|eq:inverse-finite-jet> consequently has zero jets through order
  <math|k>.

  The envelope for <math|V> has bounds <math|<around|(|1+f*g,v|)>>. Applying
  the product rules to the finite sum proves an inverse envelope with bounds

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:inverse-jet-price>T<rsub|k>*<around|(|f,g|)>=<big|sum><rsub|r=0><rsup|k><around|(|1+f*g|)><rsup|r>*g,<space|2em>k*v.
  </equation>

  Only the inverse at the base point was priced. A uniform inverse bound on
  an entire neighbourhood was not assumed.

  Apply this argument separately in every prescribed principal window. Each
  restriction of <math|A<around|(|y|)>> retains the envelope
  <math|<around|(|f,v|)>>, and each base inverse has weighted price <math|S>.
  Its <math|<around|(|i,j|)>> entry has majorant
  <math|T<rsub|k>*<around|(|f,S|)>*e<rsup|-\<gamma\>*d*<around|(|i,j|)>>>.
  Padding it by zero outside the window gives a common envelope for
  <math|P<around|(|y|)>>, because window membership is fixed. Both its row
  and column sums are bounded by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:pad-jet-price>F<rsub|B>=\<Delta\><rsub|B>*T<rsub|k>*<around|(|f,S|)>,<space|2em><text|with
    derivative scale >k*v.
  </equation>

  For the row bound, even though different columns use different windows, any
  nonzero entry has <math|d*<around|(|i,j|)>\<leqslant\>B+2>, and there are
  at most <math|\<Delta\><rsub|B>> such <math|j>. The same count proves the
  column bound.

  The error <math|A<around|(|y|)>*P<around|(|y|)>-1> vanishes, as a germ,
  whenever <math|d*<around|(|i,j|)>\<leqslant\>B>. Indeed each such
  coordinate belongs to the window, and its principal inverse solves the
  corresponding row equation for all nearby <math|y>. Erasing these zero jets
  and decreasing the spatial weight from <math|\<gamma\>> to
  <math|\<gamma\>/2> gives an envelope with price

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:inverse-error-jets>e<rsup|-\<gamma\>*B/2>*<around|(|f*F<rsub|B>+1|)>,<space|2em><text|derivative
    scale >v+k*v.
  </equation>

  This is the differentiated version of the boundary error. Multiplying by
  the full inverse envelope also prices <math|P-A<rsup|-1>>; taking the
  symmetric part and then multiplying by <math|A> prices the covariance
  defect <math|A*<around|(|P+P<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>/2-1>. For fixed <math|k> all
  these prices are a polynomial in <math|B> times
  <math|e<rsup|-\<gamma\>*B/2>>, hence have every prescribed negative upper
  exponent. The derivative scales are fixed multiples of <math|v>.

  <subsubsection|The actual sector matrix supplies the premise>

  Freeze the seeds, tree, tags, windows and active sets while varying the
  coarse field <math|U<around|(|y|)>>. Suppose every link matrix has jets

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r>*U<rsub|e><around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|G>*v<rsup|r>,<space|2em>R<rsub|G>=<sqrt|dim
    <around|(|<text|matrix representation>|)>>,<space|1em>0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>k.
  </equation*>

  The same bound holds for inverses by taking the adjoint, for signed
  pullbacks, and for replicas. Constant gauge transformations preserve it by
  unitary invariance of the Frobenius norm.

  A product along a walk of length <math|n> has amplitude <math|R<rsub|G>>
  and derivative scale <math|n*R<rsub|G>*v>. To verify the absence of an
  exponential amplitude, split its first factor as
  <math|U<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>+<around|(|U<around|(|y|)>-U<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|)>>.
  Multiplication by <math|U<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>> is an isometry; in every
  positive-order term from the difference, its bound
  <math|R<rsub|G>*v<rsup|r>> is at most <math|<around|(|R<rsub|G>*v|)><rsup|r>>.
  The binomial formula therefore adds <math|R<rsub|G>*v> to the derivative
  scale while preserving the amplitude of the remaining product. Induction
  proves the claim, including inverse letters and repeated physical links.

  For the selected sector row matrix, a single coordinate input is
  transported from its physical bond to the row anchor. The coordinate path
  has length at most <math|4*B>. A reversed signed bond adds its replica
  adjoint, whose derivative scale is <math|2*v>. The two sides of the
  transported adjoint add twice the path scale. Thus the transported input
  has fixed amplitude and derivative scale

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:normalization-kernel-growth>z=8*B*R<rsub|G>*v+2*v.
  </equation>

  The remaining cut flat row is a fixed linear map. The weighted impulse
  estimate from Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:sector-gram>, before
  periodization, bounds its operator norm by
  <math|C*e<rsup|-\<eta\>*d*<around|(|p,i|)>>>. The extra one-step shift from
  a reversed bond is absorbed into this fixed <math|C>. Hence

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r>*M<rsub|p*i><around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*e<rsup|-\<eta\>*d*<around|(|p,i|)>>*z<rsup|r>.
  </equation*>

  Inactive columns, unselected rows and entries outside the cutoff are
  identically zero throughout the variation; their indicators are not
  differentiated.

  For <math|0\<less\>\<gamma\>\<less\>\<eta\>>, sum the displayed envelope
  using the bounded number of row and column coordinates per coarse site. The
  shell sum <math|<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>0><around|(|2*n+1|)><rsup|4>*e<rsup|-<around|(|\<eta\>-\<gamma\>|)>*n>>
  is finite. It follows that <math|M> has an envelope
  <math|<around|(|C<rsub|\<gamma\>>,z|)>>, with <math|C<rsub|\<gamma\>>>
  independent of the volume and of <math|B>. The identity
  <math|A=M<rsup|\<ast\>>*M+\<Pi\><rsub|<math-up|inactive>>> therefore
  supplies <eqref|eq:matrix-jet-envelope> with

  <\equation*>
    f=C<rsub|\<gamma\>><rsup|2>+1,<space|2em>v<rsub|A>=2*z.
  </equation*>

  The inactive projection is constant. If the incoming link scale <math|v>
  has upper exponent <math|-1/2>, so do <math|z> and <math|v<rsub|A>>.

  <subsubsection|Differentiating the local logarithmic trace>

  Choose <math|\<gamma\>> also below the retained inverse width. The glide
  <math|A<rsub|s><around|(|y|)>=1+s*<around|(|A<around|(|y|)>-1|)>> has
  envelope <math|<around|(|f+2,v<rsub|A>|)>> uniformly for
  <math|0\<leqslant\>s\<leqslant\>1>. Its principal inverses at
  <math|y<rsub|0>> satisfy the same uniform price <math|S> as before.
  Multiplication by <math|A<around|(|y|)>-1> and <eqref|eq:pad-jet-price>
  consequently give, for every local diagonal entry, the amplitude

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|f+1|)>*\<Delta\><rsub|B>*T<rsub|k>*<around|(|f+2,S|)>
  </equation*>

  and derivative scale <math|<around|(|k+1|)>*v<rsub|A>>, uniformly in
  <math|s>.

  To justify differentiation under the integral, note that the principal
  determinants are nonzero at every <math|<around|(|y<rsub|0>,s|)>> on the
  compact interval. There are finitely many windows. Joint continuity gives
  one neighbourhood of <math|y<rsub|0>> on which all these determinants
  remain nonzero for all <math|s\<in\><around|[|0,1|]>>. The adjugate formula
  makes the integrand jointly <math|C<rsup|k>> there. On a smaller closed
  parameter ball times the interval, all derivatives are bounded by
  compactness. Difference quotients and the fundamental theorem of calculus,
  followed by dominated convergence, now permit differentiation under the
  integral, successively through order <math|k>. For this argument the
  variation space is finite dimensional, as it is for each finite-volume
  field variation. The local bounds at <math|y<rsub|0>> are unaffected by the
  auxiliary compactness bound. Summing the <math|q> colour entries proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:normalization-log-jets><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r>*J<rsub|e><around|(|A<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>q*<around|(|f+1|)>*\<Delta\><rsub|B>*T<rsub|k>*<around|(|f+2,S|)>*<around|{|<around|(|k+1|)>*v<rsub|A>|}><rsup|r>,<space|1em>0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>k.
  </equation>

  The flat-reference term in <eqref|eq:normalization-correction> is constant
  in <math|y>. Combining its scalar bound with
  <eqref|eq:normalization-log-jets>, and enlarging the harmless factor
  <math|1/2>, gives an envelope for <math|c<rsub|e><around|(|y|)>> with
  amplitude <math|b<around|(|x|)>> of upper exponent zero and derivative
  scale <math|z<rsub|1><around|(|x|)>> of upper exponent <math|-1/2>. It
  follows that all its jets are bounded by
  <math|b<around|(|x|)>*<around|(|1+z<rsub|1><around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|k>>,
  again of upper exponent zero. At a remote bond we can do better: its value
  is at most <math|a<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>> by
  <eqref|eq:normalization-remote>, while for positive orders, once
  <math|0\<leqslant\>z<rsub|1>\<leqslant\>1>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r>*c<rsub|e><around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>b<around|(|x|)>*z<rsub|1><around|(|x|)><rsup|r>\<leqslant\>b<around|(|x|)>*z<rsub|1><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  Thus the common bound <math|a<rsub|0>+b*z<rsub|1>>, of upper exponent
  <math|-1/2>, controls all jets through order <math|k> at remote bonds. This
  obtains small normalization jets without differentiating a chosen local
  gauge. The needed link variation and its coherent Gaussian compensation
  remain distinct inputs to the eventual packet variation.

  <subsection|Varying the coarse field while holding the fine field
  fixed><label|sec:compensated-variation>

  Differentiating a packet directly in its coarse field would also change the
  fine configuration represented by its fluctuation variable. The resulting
  derivatives would meet the fine masks and branch choices. We instead make
  the actual compensated change of variables. It is a global bijection on the
  fluctuation space near the chosen coarse configuration, and agrees with the
  required logarithmic adjustment where the protected integrand can be
  nonzero.

  <subsubsection|A globally defined adjustment of one logarithm>

  We first specify the logarithm on nearby ambient matrices. As in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:predictor-branch>, regard
  <math|\<cal-M\>=M<rsub|N><around|(|\<bbb-C\>|)>> as a real inner-product
  space with <math|<around|\<langle\>|A,B|\<rangle\>>=<math-up|Retr><around|(|A<rsup|\<ast\>>*B|)>>,
  and split it as the colour space <math|\<frak-g\>> and its orthogonal
  complement <math|\<frak-g\><rsup|\<perp\>>>. The map
  <math|<around|(|u,n|)>\<mapsto\>exp <around|(|u|)>*exp <around|(|n|)>> on
  <math|\<frak-g\>\<times\>\<frak-g\><rsup|\<perp\>>> has derivative
  <math|<around|(|u,n|)>\<mapsto\>u+n> at <math|<around|(|0,0|)>>, which is
  invertible. By the inverse function theorem there is a neighbourhood
  <math|O> of <math|<around|(|0,0|)>> in <math|\<frak-g\>\<times\>\<frak-g\><rsup|\<perp\>>>
  such that this map is a diffeomorphism of <math|O> onto an open
  neighbourhood <math|V> of the identity <math|1> in <math|\<cal-M\>>. Its
  inverse <math|V\<to\>O> is smooth; let <math|<math-up|Log><rsub|\<ast\>>:V\<to\>\<frak-g\>>
  be the colour component of this inverse. In particular,
  <math|<math-up|Log><rsub|\<ast\>>> is colour-valued even when its matrix
  argument is not a group element.

  For a group element <math|g> near the identity,
  <math|<math-up|Log><rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|g|)>> is the local group
  logarithm. This uses one fact from the theory of matrix Lie groups, already
  used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:predictor-branch>: by Cartan's
  closed-subgroup theorem in the form of <cite-detail|Hall2015|Corollary<nbsp>3.44>,
  there is <math|\<theta\>\<gtr\>0> such that every <math|g\<in\>G> with
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|g-1|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>\<theta\>> equals <math|exp X>
  for some <math|X\<in\>\<frak-g\>>, with <math|X\<to\>0> as <math|g\<to\>1>.
  For such <math|g> close enough to <math|1>, the pair
  <math|<around|(|X,0|)>> lies in <math|O> and is mapped to <math|g>, so
  <math|g\<in\>V> and, the map being injective on <math|O>,
  <math|<math-up|Log><rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|g|)>=X>. Without this theorem
  one would know only that <math|g> is a matrix near <math|1>, and its local
  preimage could have a nonzero <math|\<frak-g\><rsup|\<perp\>>> component.

  Choose fixed <math|d<rsub|\<ast\>>,A\<gtr\>0> so small that

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|Log><rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|exp
    u|)>=u*<space|1em><around|(|u\<in\>\<frak-g\>,<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*d<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>
  </equation*>

  and this extension is smooth at <math|exp <around|(|u|)>*M> whenever
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*d<rsub|\<ast\>>> and
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|M-1|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>A>. Also require

  <\equation*>
    exp <math-up|Log><rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|exp <around|(|u|)>*g|)>=exp
    <around|(|u|)>*g*<space|2em><around|(|u\<in\>\<frak-g\>,<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>d<rsub|\<ast\>>,g\<in\>G,<around|\<\|\|\>|g-1|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>A|)>.
  </equation*>

  This identity is asserted only for group elements <math|g>: for a matrix
  <math|X> outside <math|G> the left-hand side lies in <math|G> and the
  right-hand side does not. Such constants exist by continuity of
  multiplication and the exponential at <math|<around|(|0,1|)>>, after
  choosing a smaller product neighbourhood inside the given logarithm domain;
  for the last identity note that <math|exp <around|(|u|)>*g\<in\>G> is close
  to <math|1>, so the previous paragraph applies to it. The constants are
  chosen before the coupling threshold and before the particular cutoff. The
  fixed chart radius <math|d<rsub|\<ast\>>> is not the growing scale
  <math|d=1+B+<around|(|log h|)><rsup|2>> of <eqref|eq:warning-scales>.

  Using the same smooth cutoff as before, define

  <\equation*>
    \<psi\>*<around|(|M,u|)>=\<chi\>*<around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>/d<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|2>|)>*<around|{|<math-up|Log><rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|exp
    <around|(|u|)>*M|)>-u|}>,<space|2em>F<rsub|M><around|(|u|)>=u+\<psi\>*<around|(|M,u|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here group elements are represented by their unitary matrices in the
  logarithm expression. The correction is zero for
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>>\<geqslant\>2*d<rsub|\<ast\>>>. On the
  closed ball it is smooth in <math|<around|(|M,u|)>>; outside the ball it
  vanishes locally, so these formulas define a smooth map everywhere for
  <math|M> sufficiently near <math|1>. At <math|M=1> the correction and all
  its <math|u> derivatives vanish.

  Compactness of <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*d<rsub|\<ast\>>>
  now supplies one neighbourhood of <math|M=1> on which, for every <math|u>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:compensator-small><around|\<\|\|\>|\<psi\>*<around|(|M,u|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>d<rsub|\<ast\>>/2,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsub|u>*\<psi\>*<around|(|M,u|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>1/2.
  </equation>

  Indeed these inequalities hold at <math|<around|(|1,u|)>>, and their
  continuous left-hand sides are uniformly small on that compact set; off it
  they are zero. Integration of the derivative on line segments shows that
  <math|\<psi\>*<around|(|M,\<cdot\>|)>> is <math|1/2>-Lipschitz. Thus
  <math|F<rsub|M>> is injective, since <math|F<rsub|M><around|(|u|)>=F<rsub|M><around|(|v|)>>
  implies <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u-v|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|u-v|\<\|\|\>>/2>.
  For every <math|z>, iteration of <math|u\<mapsto\>z-\<psi\>*<around|(|M,u|)>>
  is a contraction on the complete colour space. Its iterates are Cauchy
  because successive distances form a geometric series; their limit is its
  unique fixed point and solves <math|F<rsub|M><around|(|u|)>=z>. Hence
  <math|F<rsub|M>> is surjective as well.

  Its derivative is <math|1+D<rsub|u>*\<psi\>>, which is invertible by the
  convergent geometric series. The determinant

  <\equation*>
    p*<around|(|M,u|)>=det <around|(|1+D<rsub|u>*\<psi\>*<around|(|M,u|)>|)>
  </equation*>

  is positive for all <math|u> after a further uniform restriction on
  <math|M>: it equals one at <math|M=1>, is continuous on the compact
  <math|u> ball, and is one outside it. Thus <math|F<rsub|M>> is a smooth
  bijection with positive Jacobian. Its local smooth inverses agree by
  injectivity, giving a global smooth inverse.

  For an actual group element <math|g> near <math|1> and
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>d<rsub|\<ast\>>>, the cutoff
  equals one. Consequently

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:compensator-exact>exp <around|(|F<rsub|g><around|(|u|)>|)>=exp
    <around|(|u|)>*g.
  </equation>

  The map is global, but this exact group identity is used only within that
  smaller ball.

  <subsubsection|The coherent coarse and fluctuation variables>

  Fix a sector with coarse field <math|V<rsub|0>>, and keep its seeds,
  forest, tags, frozen fine bonds and active sets fixed. In the affine source
  replace the clipped coarse guide by its unmasked path-field formula; let
  <math|<wide|\<ell\>|~><around|(|V|)>> be the resulting linear source and
  put

  <\equation*>
    \<nu\><around|(|V|)>=-P<around|(|V|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*<wide|\<ell\>|~><around|(|V|)>
  </equation*>

  in physical colour coordinates. This specifies the extension used in the
  change of variables. At a valid sector, every selected row has a clean
  neighbourhood. The clipping is then inactive at every read of that row, as
  proved in the guide comparison. Its unmasked source equals the original
  source, and hence <math|\<nu\><around|(|V<rsub|0>|)>=\<mu\>>. The same
  statement holds at any other valid sector. Away from valid sectors the
  unmasked expression is retained as its definition.

  Let <math|g<rsub|\<beta\>>=\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>\<gtr\>0> and identify a
  counting Gaussian coordinate <math|z> with its colour vector on each fine
  bond. The extended fluctuation field is

  <\equation*>
    u<rsub|0>=\<nu\><around|(|V<rsub|0>|)>+g<rsub|\<beta\>>*z.
  </equation*>

  For a nearby coarse field <math|V>, define separately at each fine bond

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:compensator-course>M<rsub|i><around|(|V|)>=<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|0,i>*<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|i><rsup|-1>,<space|1em>u<rsub|i><around|(|V|)>=F<rsub|M<rsub|i><around|(|V|)>><around|(|u<rsub|0,i>|)>,<space|1em>z<rsub|i><around|(|V|)>=g<rsub|\<beta\>><rsup|-1>*<around|{|u<rsub|i><around|(|V|)>-\<nu\><rsub|i><around|(|V|)>|}>.
  </equation>

  The replica is the prescribed one. It follows identically that
  <math|\<nu\><around|(|V|)>+g<rsub|\<beta\>>*z<around|(|V|)>=u<around|(|V|)>>,
  and at <math|V=V<rsub|0>> the map is the identity on <math|z>.

  The fine field on an active bond is <math|exp
  <around|(|u<rsub|i>|)>*<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|i>>; on an inactive bond it is
  the fixed original fine field. Suppose <math|8*a*\<tau\>\<leqslant\>d<rsub|\<ast\>>/2>
  and the varied active fluctuation satisfies
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|i><around|(|V|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>8*a*\<tau\>>.
  By <eqref|eq:compensator-small>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|0,i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|i><around|(|V|)>|\<\|\|\>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|\<psi\>*<around|(|M<rsub|i><around|(|V|)>,u<rsub|0,i>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>d<rsub|\<ast\>>.
  </equation*>

  Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:compensator-exact> therefore gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:compensator-held-fine>exp <around|(|u<rsub|i><around|(|V|)>|)>*<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|i>=exp
    <around|(|u<rsub|0,i>|)>*<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|0,i>.
  </equation>

  Inactive bonds agree by definition. On the protected support all fine reads
  are consequently fixed, including their masks, likelihoods and selected
  branch tags. The larger <math|8*a*\<tau\>> allowance is deliberate: at a
  point where the broader cutoff germ is not identically zero, continuity
  first gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|i><around|(|V<rsub|0>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>4*a*\<tau\>>,
  then gives the strict <math|8*a*\<tau\>> bound on a neighbourhood. A finite
  set of protected bonds has one common such neighbourhood. No condition is
  imposed on the unprotected Gaussian coordinates.

  <subsubsection|Uniform derivative bounds for the compensation>

  Fix the derivative order <math|k>. On <math|<around|{|1|}>\<times\><around|{|u:<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*d<rsub|\<ast\>>|}>>,
  all derivatives in <math|M> of <math|\<psi\>> and <math|p> through order
  <math|k> have fixed bounds, by smoothness and compactness. Outside this set
  their <math|u>-sections are respectively zero and one as functions of
  <math|M>. Hence these bounds hold for every <math|u\<in\>\<frak-g\>>, with
  constants independent of the Gaussian coordinate. Moreover
  <math|\<psi\>*<around|(|1,u|)>=0> and <math|p*<around|(|1,u|)>=1>.

  Suppose the coarse variation <math|V<around|(|y|)>> has the link derivative
  bounds of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:normalization-jets>, with
  <math|V<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>=V<rsub|0>> and scale <math|v<around|(|x|)>>
  of upper exponent <math|-1/2>. Inversion, replication and multiplication by
  the fixed unitary replica show that <math|M<rsub|i><around|(|V<around|(|y|)>|)>>
  has the same bound. In a chain-rule term of positive order
  <math|r\<leqslant\>k>, the product of derivatives of <math|M<rsub|i>> has
  total scale <math|v<rsup|r>>; its amplitude is bounded by a constant
  depending only on <math|k> and the representation. There are finitely many
  partitions of <math|r>. Thus, for every <math|u<rsub|0,i>>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r><rsub|y>*\<psi\>*<around|(|M<rsub|i><around|(|V<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|)>,u<rsub|0,i>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|k>*v<rsup|r>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r><rsub|y>*p*<around|(|M<rsub|i><around|(|V<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|)>,u<rsub|0,i>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rprime|'><rsub|k>*v<rsup|r>*<space|1em><around|(|1\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>k|)>.
  </equation*>

  Their zero-order values are zero and one. Since <math|v\<to\>0>, enlarge
  constants and obtain

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:compensator-jets><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r><rsub|y>*<around|(|u<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>-u<rsub|0,i>|)>\|<rsub|y=y<rsub|0>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>s<around|(|x|)>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r><rsub|y>*p*<around|(|M<rsub|i><around|(|V<around|(|y|)>|)>,u<rsub|0,i>|)>\|<rsub|y=y<rsub|0>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>D<around|(|x|)><rsup|r>*<space|1em><around|(|0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>k|)>,
  </equation>

  where <math|s\<geqslant\>0> has upper exponent <math|-1/2>, and
  <math|D\<geqslant\>0> has upper exponent zero. For the first bound use
  <math|C<rsub|k>*v> for positive orders and zero at order zero; for the
  second take <math|D=max <around|(|1,C<rprime|'><rsub|k>|)>*v>, increasing
  the fixed constant if necessary. For <math|r\<geqslant\>1>, <math|max
  <around|(|1,C<rprime|'><rsub|k>|)><rsup|r>\<geqslant\>C<rprime|'><rsub|k>>.
  At order zero the second bound is equality, with the convention
  <math|D<rsup|0>=1>. These are precisely the compensated increment bounds
  used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:haar-chart>. They are uniform over
  all Gaussian samples; they do not require a bound on
  <math|z<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>> or on the derivatives of the mean extension.

  The incoming link-variation premise itself holds for the prescribed scaled
  exponential variations. For example, let <math|V<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>=exp
  <around|(|\<epsilon\>*L<rsub|i>*y|)>*V<rsub|0,i>>, where the linear maps
  <math|L<rsub|i>> have uniformly bounded norm and <math|y> stays in a fixed
  bounded set. The derivatives of the matrix exponential are uniformly
  bounded on the resulting fixed colour ball. The linear chain rule
  contributes <math|\<epsilon\><rsup|r>> at order <math|r>. Right
  multiplication by <math|V<rsub|0,i>> is an isometry. Enlarging one fixed
  constant therefore gives the link bound with
  <math|v=C<rsub|k>*\<epsilon\>>. In particular the required scale holds when
  <math|\<epsilon\>> has upper exponent <math|-1/2>, as for the prescribed
  inverse-square-root coupling normalization. Finite collections of marked
  directions are handled by taking the maximum of their norms.

  <subsubsection|The exact finite-dimensional Jacobian>

  For a fixed nearby <math|V>, the map in <eqref|eq:compensator-course> acts
  separately on the colour coordinate of every fine bond. On a finite set
  <math|P> of bonds it is conjugate, by the fixed coordinate regrouping, to
  the product of maps

  <\equation*>
    z<rsub|i>\<longmapsto\>g<rsub|\<beta\>><rsup|-1>*<around|{|F<rsub|M<rsub|i><around|(|V|)>>*<around|(|\<nu\><rsub|i><around|(|V<rsub|0>|)>+g<rsub|\<beta\>>*z<rsub|i>|)>-\<nu\><rsub|i><around|(|V|)>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Each is a bijection. Its derivative is exactly
  <math|D*F<rsub|M<rsub|i><around|(|V|)>><around|(|u<rsub|0,i>|)>>: the two
  scalar factors <math|g<rsub|\<beta\>><rsup|-1>> and <math|g<rsub|\<beta\>>>
  cancel. The derivative of the product is block diagonal, so its determinant
  is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:compensator-jacobian><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>P>p*<around|(|M<rsub|i><around|(|V|)>,u<rsub|0,i>|)>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation>

  A fixed regrouping of counting coordinates conjugates the derivative and
  does not change its determinant. This remains true if the regrouped spaces
  are temporarily given different finite-dimensional norms. For the empty set
  the map is the identity of the zero-dimensional space and the product is
  one.

  The ordinary change-of-variables formula therefore gives, for every
  nonnegative measurable test <math|f> on these coordinates (and hence for
  every integrable test),

  <\equation*>
    <big|int>f<around|(|z<rprime|'>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*z<rprime|'>=<big|int>f<around|(|z<around|(|V|)>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>P>p*<around|(|M<rsub|i><around|(|V|)>,u<rsub|0,i>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*z.
  </equation*>

  Together with <eqref|eq:compensator-held-fine> and
  <eqref|eq:compensator-jets>, this supplies the coherent protected variation
  and its exact Jacobian. Differentiating a Gaussian density under this
  substitution still requires its own covariance and mean score bounds; those
  do not follow from this Jacobian calculation.

  <subsection|Derivatives of the mean and of the local
  action><label|sec:mean-action-jets>

  We now prove the differentiated estimates needed for the logarithm of a
  packet. The pointwise comparison with a stationary response is not
  differentiated: its gauge was chosen from the base configuration. Instead
  we use that one fixed gauge to express nearby configurations in logarithmic
  coordinates, estimate the smooth unmasked expressions there, and finally
  restore the original gauge. This distinction keeps the argument valid at
  the boundary of a cutoff.

  Fix a derivative order <math|j>, a patch, the branch constants, the forest,
  tags and sector indicators. Assume the uniform path bounds, clean-sector
  conditions and positive precision bounds of
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:sector-gram>\U<reference|sec:normalization-jets>.
  All these choices precede the large-<math|x> limit. A coarse variation
  <math|V<around|(|y|)>> is measured by its matrix derivatives on each bond:

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r>*\<rho\><around|(|V<rsub|e>|)><around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|G>*v<around|(|x|)><rsup|r>*<space|1em><around|(|0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>j|)>,<space|2em>v\<geqslant\>0,
  </equation*>

  where <math|R<rsub|G>> is the fixed representation bound and <math|v> has
  upper exponent <math|-1/2>. We use the derivative notation
  <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|j>> of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:packet-estimates>;
  the same definition applies to colour-valued and operator-valued functions.

  <subsubsection|Uniform bounds for the actual predictor branch>

  The analytic branch of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:predictor-branch>
  gives bounds of every fixed finite order on a neighbourhood of the
  identity. We need that neighbourhood to be independent of the torus size.
  Here is the finite-read argument that ensures this.

  For a fixed patch and direction, take the union of the sites visited by all
  frame paths, their translates to the neighbouring coarse root, and all the
  straight connectors of length <math|L>. This is a finite subset of the
  covering lattice. Choose a reference torus large enough that lifting after
  wrapping fixes every site in this union, and with distinct neighbouring
  coarse roots. There are only finitely many tag choices and output bonds on
  this reference torus. For each, analyticity gives a positive logarithmic
  radius and a bound on derivatives through order <math|k>. Take the minimum
  of the radii and one plus the sum of the bounds.

  For a bond on any other torus, pull its logarithmic coordinates to the
  reference torus by translation and wrapping. This linear map has norm at
  most one in the supremum norm, even when some input coordinates coincide.
  Assign the two reference frame tags the actual tags at the two endpoints,
  and assign the actual predictor tag to the reference bond. Every read path
  has the same transport. Thus the two frame score functions coincide;
  uniqueness on the minimizing branch, and the explicit axial formula on its
  complement, identify the frames. The connector and predictor then coincide
  as well. Composition with the norm-one linear map transfers the derivative
  bound without a dimension factor.

  Consequently, for each <math|k> there are <math|r,C\<gtr\>0>, independent
  of the period, such that the actual predictor on logarithmic fields of norm
  at most <math|r> belongs to <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|k>*<around|(|C,1|)>> on
  each output bond. Shrink <math|r> once more to use the logarithm and
  exponential charts. The chain rule shows that a near-identity group
  variation with derivative scale <math|v> has predictor derivative scale
  <math|C<rsub|k>*v>. This statement includes the original tags and the
  branch tests.

  In particular, on a fixed ball in pairs <math|<around|(|b,u|)>> of coarse
  and fine logarithmic fields, the four fields

  <\equation*>
    V=exp b,<space|2em>U=exp u<space|0.17em><math-up|replica><space|0.17em>V,<space|2em>Q<around|(|U|)>,<space|2em>Q<around|(|<math-up|replica><space|0.17em>V|)>
  </equation*>

  have uniform derivatives of every prescribed finite order. Indeed both
  coordinate projections have norm one; differentiation of the exponential on
  a fixed compact chart, replication, and the unitary product rule give
  uniform bounds for <math|V,U>. Their chords are bounded by a fixed multiple
  of <math|max <around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|b|\<\|\|\>>,<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>>|)>>,
  so the predictor estimate just proved applies after shrinking the ball.

  Let <math|f*<around|(|b,u|)>> be one unmasked affine residual at zero
  fluctuation, namely the fine or predictor base stroke minus its transported
  coarse guide, with coarse field <math|exp b>. It does not depend on
  <math|u>, but retaining the pair space allows the same chart for all local
  actions. The preceding bounds and the absolute path prices imply

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:action-fixed-ball>f\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|k>*<around|(|A<around|(|x|)>,1;<around|(|b,u|)>|)><space|1em><text|for
    ><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|b,u|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>r,<space|2em>f*<around|(|0,0|)>=0,
  </equation>

  where <math|A> has upper exponent zero. To see the rate assertion, each cut
  path has length at most a fixed multiple of <math|R+1> and bounded absolute
  coefficient sum. Transport adds a derivative scale
  <math|C*<around|(|R+1|)>>; a guide sums over at most
  <math|C*<around|(|R+1|)><rsup|4>> inputs with bounded coefficients. At
  fixed order, the product rule therefore costs only a polynomial in
  <math|R+1>. The predictor bounds are fixed constants. Here
  <math|R=<around|\<lfloor\>|B/100000|\<rfloor\>>> has upper exponent zero.
  At the identity all curvature strokes vanish, the predictor equals the
  identity, and the guides are linear in zero coarse strokes, which proves
  the last equality in <eqref|eq:action-fixed-ball>.

  <subsubsection|Recovering small derivatives by flat interpolation>

  We give the interpolation step explicitly because its constants are
  independent of the dimension of the logarithmic field space. Let
  <math|f:E\<to\>F> be <math|C<rsup|k>> near the closed ball of radius
  <math|B<rsub|0>> in a finite-dimensional normed space, with
  <math|k\<geqslant\>3>. Suppose

  <\equation*>
    f\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|k>*<around|(|A,1;z|)>*<space|1em><around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|0>|)>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|f<around|(|z|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>u*<space|1em><around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>3*t|)>,<space|1em>0\<less\>t\<leqslant\>1,<space|1em>3*t\<leqslant\>B<rsub|0>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|A,u\<geqslant\>0>. Put

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|W>|<cell|=1000*<around|(|u/t<rsup|2>+A*B<rsub|0>|)>,>|<cell|Y>|<cell|=1000*u/t+3*W*B<rsub|0>,>>|<row|<cell|H>|<cell|=u+Y*B<rsub|0>+Y*t+W*t<rsup|2>+A*t<rsup|3>.>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
  </align*>

  Then, throughout the larger ball,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:action-interpolation>f\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|k>*<around|(|H,t<rsup|-1>|)>.
  </equation>

  First, if <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D*f<around|(|z|)>-D*f<around|(|a|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>e*d>
  and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|f<around|(|z|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>u> on
  <math|<wide|B|\<bar\>>*<around|(|a,d|)>>, integration along a segment of
  length <math|d> in any unit direction gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|D*f<around|(|a|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*u/d+e*d.
  </equation*>

  Write <math|H<rsub|0>=<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|2>*f<around|(|0|)>|\<\|\|\>>>.
  The third derivative bound gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|2>*f<around|(|z|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|0>+A<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>>.
  Apply the preceding inequality on balls of radius <math|t/4> centred in
  <math|<wide|B|\<bar\>>*<around|(|0,2*t|)>>. These balls lie in
  <math|<wide|B|\<bar\>>*<around|(|0,3*t|)>>, and hence

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|D*f<around|(|z|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|1>\<assign\>8*u/t+<around|(|H<rsub|0>+3*A*t|)>*t/4*<space|1em><around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*t|)>.
  </equation*>

  Apply the same inequality to <math|D*f> on the radius-<math|2*t> ball,
  using the third derivative bound to control the variation of
  <math|D<rsup|2>*f>. It yields <math|H<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|1>/t+2*A*t>,
  and therefore

  <\equation*>
    H<rsub|0>\<leqslant\><tfrac|32|3>u/t<rsup|2>+<tfrac|11|3>A*t\<leqslant\>50*<around|(|u/t<rsup|2>+A*t|)>.
  </equation*>

  Integration out to radius <math|B<rsub|0>> now gives
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|2>*f|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>W>,
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D*f|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>Y>, and
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>u+Y*B<rsub|0>>. For orders
  <math|r\<geqslant\>3>, the original bound gives
  <math|t<rsup|r>*<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r>*f|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>A*t<rsup|3>>.
  Each of the bounds for orders zero, one, two and at least three is a
  summand of <math|H>, proving <eqref|eq:action-interpolation>.

  Apply this with <math|k=j+3> and <math|t=h<rsup|-1/2>>. This <math|t> is
  the flat probing radius, not the larger fluctuation threshold
  <math|\<tau\>=P<around|(|h|)>*h<rsup|-1/2>>. In a clean ball, choose one
  gauge from the base field <math|V<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>> and keep it fixed.
  The small-log construction and the smooth local logarithm give a
  logarithmic variation <math|b<around|(|y|)>>, zero outside the required
  ball, with

  <\equation*>
    b\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around|(|F<around|(|x|)>,1;y<rsub|0>|)>,<space|2em>F<text|
    of upper exponent >-1/2.
  </equation*>

  For positive derivatives this follows by composing the bounded derivatives
  of the logarithm with the bond derivative scale <math|v>; for the value it
  is the small-log bound at the base field. Finitely many strict chart
  inequalities give a common neighbourhood on which exponentiating <math|b>
  agrees with the gauge-transformed field on the whole read stencil. No
  derivative of the chosen gauge is taken.

  Set <math|B<rsub|0>=F+3*t>, which eventually lies in the fixed ball of
  <eqref|eq:action-fixed-ball>. Since <math|f<around|(|0|)>=0>, integration
  of its first derivative gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>3*A*t>
  on the radius-<math|3*t> ball. Thus take <math|u=3*A*t> above. The upper
  exponents of <math|W,Y,H> are, respectively, <math|1/2,0,-1/2>: each term
  follows from the displayed formulas, <math|B<rsub|0>,t> of upper exponent
  <math|-1/2>, and <math|t<rsup|-1>> of upper exponent <math|1/2>.

  The chain rule for <math|f*<around|(|b<around|(|y|)>,0|)>> costs at most a
  fixed factorial times <math|H> and a fixed-order polynomial in
  <math|1+2*F*<around|(|1+t<rsup|-1>|)>>. To verify this directly, in a term
  indexed by a partition into <math|m> blocks, the outer derivative
  contributes <math|H*t<rsup|-m>> and each inner derivative contributes at
  most <math|2*F>; there are at most <math|j>! partitions. The displayed
  polynomial has upper exponent zero. Thus every derivative through order
  <math|j> of the residual has upper exponent <math|-1/2>. Locality
  identifies this expression with the actual unmasked residual in the fixed
  gauge; gauge covariance restores the original field by a fixed isometry.

  <subsubsection|The source and the mean>

  The selected affine source also contains frozen cut logarithms. They obey
  the same upper exponent <math|-1/2> derivative bound. For completeness,
  write such a logarithm as

  <\equation*>
    \<chi\>*<around*|(|<math-up|chord><around|(|U|)><rsup|2>/Z<rsup|2>|)>*<space|0.17em>log
    U,<space|2em>Z=K*\<tau\>,
  </equation*>

  with fixed <math|K\<gtr\>0>. If the base chord is strictly larger than
  <math|2*Z>, the function is locally zero. Otherwise normalize the matrix
  displacement by <math|R<rsub|G>*Z>. Its value is at most two and its
  positive derivatives have size bounded by constants times <math|v/Z> after
  enlarging the scale. Since <math|Z<rsup|-1>> has upper exponent <math|1/2>,
  the derivatives of the cutoff have upper exponent zero. On the fixed
  logarithm chart, the value of <math|log U> is at most <math|C*Z>; its
  positive derivatives are at most <math|C<rsub|j>*v> after
  <math|v\<leqslant\>1>. The product rule proves the assertion, including the
  closed boundary <math|<math-up|chord><around|(|U|)>=2*Z>.

  On a selected row, the zero-fluctuation affine source is the fixed row
  scale times the sum of the unmasked residual and the transported frozen
  logarithm. The latter transport reads only a radius-<math|B> neighbourhood
  and has bounded absolute row price. Its derivative scale is bounded by
  <math|C*<around|(|B+1|)>*v+1>, of upper exponent zero. Combining the
  preceding estimates gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:action-source-jets>r<around|(|0|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around|(|F<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>,1|)><space|1em><text|coordinatewise>,<space|2em>F<rsub|0><text|
    of upper exponent >-1/2.
  </equation>

  On unselected rows the function is identically zero. The selection is held
  fixed throughout this differentiation.

  Use the same counting-coordinate matrices <math|M,A,P> as before, and put
  <math|\<ell\>=M<rsup|\<ast\>>*r<around|(|0|)>> and
  <math|\<nu\>=-P<rsup|\<ast\>>*\<ell\>>. Weighted row and column derivative
  bounds for <math|M>, together with <eqref|eq:action-source-jets>, show that
  each coordinate of <math|\<ell\>> has derivatives of upper exponent
  <math|-1/2>. The padded inverse derivative bounds have upper exponent zero,
  so the same is true of <math|\<nu\>>. Reassembling a colour vector costs at
  most its fixed colour dimension. On the clean selected sector <math|\<nu\>>
  equals the actual mean <math|\<mu\>>, by the local clipping identity
  already proved.

  There is a stronger estimate for the normal residual. Since <math|A> is
  symmetric, on a neighbourhood where it is invertible,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:action-score-small>A*\<nu\>+\<ell\>=-A*<around|(|P-A<rsup|-1>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<ell\>.
  </equation>

  This follows by substituting <math|\<nu\>=-P<rsup|\<ast\>>*\<ell\>> and
  using <math|<around|(|A<rsup|-1>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>=A<rsup|-1>>. Every
  prescribed finite derivative of <math|P-A<rsup|-1>> has every prescribed
  negative upper exponent, by Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:normalization-jets>.
  The weighted product rule therefore proves the same assertion for
  <eqref|eq:action-score-small>. For <math|0\<less\>\<beta\>\<leqslant\>2*h>,
  multiplication by <math|<sqrt|\<beta\>>> loses at most upper exponent
  <math|1/2>, so the normalized linear Gaussian score
  <math|-<sqrt|\<beta\>>*<around|(|A*\<nu\>+\<ell\>|)>> still has any
  prescribed upper exponent. This proves the score bound without replacing
  the local mean by the exact global inverse mean.

  <subsubsection|Local action derivatives with a held fine field>

  We also require a cruder bound that holds for every fixed fine
  configuration <math|U>. Keep <math|U> and its actual predictor
  <math|Q<around|(|U|)>> fixed while varying the coarse field. Since
  <math|v\<leqslant\>1> eventually, all coarse matrix derivatives may be
  bounded with scale one.

  The cut curvature strokes and predictor-noise stroke have a common constant
  amplitude <math|C<rsub|0>> and derivative scale
  <math|D<rsub|0>=C*<around|(|R+1|)>>. For curvature strokes this follows by
  differentiating their cut path sums, whose absolute coefficients are
  bounded; the unextended plaquette alternative is a product of four unitary
  bonds. For the noise stroke use the product
  <math|V*Q<around|(|U|)><rsup|-1>> and the bounded curvature map. A
  transported guide adds a finite input sum and transport: its amplitude is
  at most <math|C*<around|(|R+1|)><rsup|4>*C<rsub|0>> and its derivative
  scale at most <math|D<rsub|0>+C*<around|(|R+1|)>>. A second application of
  the transported operator has the corresponding squared polynomial price.

  All unmasked local action terms now have upper exponent zero in every
  derivative through order <math|j>. Indeed they are finite sums of inner
  products of the above strokes and guides, their squared norms, and the
  plaquette discrepancy

  <\equation*>
    <tfrac|1|2><around*|(|<math-up|chord><around|(|g|)><rsup|2>-<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|curv><around|(|g|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>|)>.
  </equation*>

  An inner product multiplies amplitudes and adds derivative scales; this is
  precisely the bilinear product rule. The discrepancy has bounded
  derivatives because the chord squared is a fixed multiple of
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<rho\><around|(|g|)>-1|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>, and the
  curvature is a fixed linear projection of the matrix displacement. At fixed
  <math|j>, converting a polynomial derivative scale to scale one preserves
  upper exponent zero.

  The fine energy requires one separate observation: its clipped part need
  not be smooth in the fixed fine input. It is constant in the variable being
  differentiated. The chord is at most two and <math|\<tau\>\<leqslant\>1>,
  so the direct and linear pieces of that clipped energy are bounded by twice
  their fixed coefficients. If <math|C<rsub|<math-up|path>>> is the fixed
  absolute path price, the complete fine constant is

  <\equation*>
    F=<tfrac|1|2><around|(|C<rsub|<math-up|path>>*R<rsub|G><rsup|2>|)><rsup|2>+2*c<rsub|<math-up|direct>>+2*c<rsub|<math-up|linear>>.
  </equation*>

  The remaining fine terms are, for the two curvature components,
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|G|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>/2-<around|\<langle\>|G,X<rsub|f>|\<rangle\>>>,
  where <math|G> is the varying guide and <math|X<rsub|f>> is fixed and
  bounded. The preceding product estimates apply. Thus no smoothness of the
  arbitrary held fine configuration is assumed.

  Finally consider an extracted affine square. If a varying fluctuation
  <math|w> has derivatives bounded by <math|p<around|(|x|)>> of upper
  exponent zero on the hot bonds in its read ball, then

  <\equation*>
    r<around|(|w|)>=r<around|(|0|)>+M*w
  </equation*>

  has coordinatewise derivatives of upper exponent zero. Rows of <math|M>
  vanish off the hot bonds and outside radius <math|B/4>, so only these local
  bounds on <math|w> enter. Squaring, dividing by twice the square of the
  fixed row normalization, and summing over the at most two parent rows at an
  energy address preserves that exponent. This applies both to the
  compensated fluctuation on protected bonds and to the mean <math|\<nu\>>.

  <subsubsection|The logarithmic packet estimate>

  Let <math|n> be the number of seeds. If the product of protected masks is
  not locally zero, their closed support bounds and the compensated variation
  of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:compensated-variation> give derivatives
  of the fluctuation of upper exponent <math|-1/2> on the protected
  <math|304*B> region. The mean has the same bound by the preceding argument.
  Thus the local action and both extracted-square costs just estimated are
  available at every address of the <math|200*B> region. There are at most
  <math|n*b<rsub|200*B+1>*s<rsub|L>> such addresses in each signed channel.
  Taking the normalized signed average preserves this bound.

  Let <math|C<rsub|a>,C<rsub|q>,C<rsub|c>,C<rsub|J>\<geqslant\>0> bound
  respectively the derivatives of the unmasked action, one extracted-square
  cost, the Gaussian normalization correction and the chart correction, all
  at scale one through order <math|j>. Their upper exponents are zero, zero,
  zero and <math|-1/2>, respectively. With <math|\<rho\><rsub|L>> the
  fine-bond density, put

  <\equation*>
    S<around|(|x|)>=2*h*<space|0.17em>b<rsub|200*B+1>*s<rsub|L>*<around|(|C<rsub|a>+2*C<rsub|q>|)>+b<rsub|200*B+2>*\<rho\><rsub|L>*C<rsub|c>+b<rsub|304*B+2>*\<rho\><rsub|L>*C<rsub|J>.
  </equation*>

  It has upper exponent one. The formula <eqref|eq:packet-log-density>, the
  local bounds, and the bond census show that its nonconstant part belongs to
  <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around|(|n*S,1;y<rsub|0>|)>>. The term
  <math|-N<rsub|A>*log m<rsub|\<beta\>>> is constant in this variation.
  Subtracting the value at <math|y<rsub|0>> costs at most a factor two, so

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:action-packet-log-jets>\<Phi\><rsub|A>-\<Phi\><rsub|A><around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around|(|2*n*S<around|(|x|)>,1;y<rsub|0>|)>.
  </equation>

  This proves the first bound in <eqref|eq:packet-local-jet-inputs>, with the
  original local covariance, chart and compensated fluctuation. If the
  protected mask product is locally zero, the packet derivatives vanish and
  this bound on its logarithm is not needed.

  <subsection|Small derivatives of the remote
  action><label|sec:remote-action-jets>

  The packet estimate permits a polynomial expense in its logarithm. Outside
  the packets we need a stronger conclusion: each local logarithmic activity,
  together with its derivatives, must be small. We obtain the extra power
  from the local quadratic comparison and the flat interpolation argument
  already proved. Throughout this section the derivative order, block length,
  patch, forest and tags are fixed. The path bounds, flat identities and
  positive precision estimates retain the hypotheses of the preceding
  sections.

  <subsubsection|A cubic remainder in a flat chart>

  For a local energy address <math|p>, let
  <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|p>*<around|(|U,V|)>> denote its unmasked energy, with
  the original predictor tags. Let <math|I<around|(|p|)>> be its set of
  extracted quadratic rows; <math|<around|\||I<around|(|p|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>2>.
  Write <math|f<rsub|i><around|(|V|)>> for the zero-fluctuation residual and
  <math|M<rsub|i><around|(|V|)>*u> for the transported cut linear row. Define

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|R<rsub|p>*<around|(|U,V,u|)>>|<cell|=\<cal-E\><rsub|p>*<around|(|U,V|)>-<frac|1|2>*<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I<around|(|p|)>><around|\||f<rsub|i><around|(|V|)>+M<rsub|i><around|(|V|)>*u|\|><rsup|2>,<eq-number><label|eq:remote-square-remainder>>>|<row|<cell|F<rsub|p>*<around|(|b,u|)>>|<cell|=R<rsub|p>*<around|(|exp
    <around|(|u|)><math-up|replica><around|(|exp b|)>,exp
    b,u|)>.<eq-number><label|eq:remote-flat-remainder>>>>>
  </align>

  The four address types are the fine curvature, predictor penalty, coarse
  curvature with its two mismatch terms, and the null-row square. For the
  last two, the parent set is empty. These are the same unmasked expressions
  as in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:packet-annulus>.

  For every fixed <math|k>, there are a radius <math|r\<gtr\>0> independent
  of the period and a function <math|A> of upper exponent zero such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:remote-fixed-ball>F<rsub|p>\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|k>*<around|(|A<around|(|x|)>,1;<around|(|b,u|)>|)>,<space|2em>max
    <around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|b|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>,<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>\<leqslant\>r.
  </equation>

  Here and below the same bound holds at every address. To verify it, use the
  four dressed fields and their predictor bounds in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mean-action-jets>. Each cut path has length
  <math|O<around|(|R+1|)>>, its absolute coefficients have bounded sum, and
  its transport derivatives cost a polynomial in <math|R+1>. The local energy
  is a sum of squared curvature rows and inner products of the coarse stroke
  with linear guide rows. Its derivatives therefore cost only another
  polynomial in <math|R+1>. Each affine row in
  <eqref|eq:remote-square-remainder> is the sum of the residual already
  estimated there and a cut linear map applied to <math|u>. On the unit ball,
  the coordinate map <math|u\<mapsto\>u<rsub|e>> has value and first
  derivative at most one and all higher derivatives zero. The product rule
  bounds its transported image by a polynomial in <math|B+1>. Squaring and
  summing at most two such rows proves <eqref|eq:remote-fixed-ball>. This is
  a bound on the unmasked formula; it makes no small-field assertion on that
  whole fixed ball.

  Put <math|t=q*h<rsup|-1/2>> with <math|q\<gtr\>0> fixed and sufficiently
  small. Every coarse field <math|exp b> with
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|b|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>3*t> is clean
  on all its plaquettes. Indeed the exponential chart gives
  <math|<math-up|chord><around|(|exp b<rsub|e>|)>\<leqslant\>C<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsub|e>|\<\|\|\>>>,
  and the four-link triangle inequality gives plaquette chord at most
  <math|12*C*q*h<rsup|-1/2>>. Choose <math|q\<leqslant\><around|(|12*C|)><rsup|-1>>
  and use <math|h<rsup|-1/2>> no larger than the coarse flag threshold. This
  check applies on every period.

  On the radius-<math|3*t> ball, the fields in
  <eqref|eq:remote-flat-remainder> have the exact local graft <math|U=exp
  u<math-up|replica><space|0.17em>V>. The annular comparison then gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:remote-probe-value><around|\||F<rsub|p>*<around|(|b,u|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>E<around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>E<text|
    of upper exponent >-3/2.
  </equation>

  More explicitly, the fine and predictor rows have affine error of upper
  exponent <math|-1> and amplitude of upper exponent <math|-1/2>; the
  difference of their squares consequently has upper exponent <math|-3/2>.
  Their extracted rows agree with <math|f<rsub|i>+M<rsub|i>*u>, since
  cleanliness removes the clipping on the complete read stencil. The coarse
  curvature remainder and null-row square have upper exponent <math|-2>. Each
  mismatch term is an inner product of a coarse stroke of upper exponent
  <math|-1/2> with an error of upper exponent <math|-1>. Their sum has upper
  exponent <math|-3/2>. Taking the sum of these four nonnegative bounds gives
  one <math|E> for all address types. These are precisely the estimates
  proved in the annular argument; no inequality is differentiated in
  obtaining <eqref|eq:remote-probe-value>.

  Let <math|Q\<geqslant\>0> have upper exponent <math|-1/2>, and put
  <math|B<rsub|0>=Q+3*t>. Apply <eqref|eq:action-interpolation> to
  <math|F<rsub|p>>, with <math|k=j+3>, value bound <math|E> and fixed-ball
  derivative bound <math|A>. Its constants satisfy

  <\equation*>
    W=1000*<around|(|E/t<rsup|2>+A*B<rsub|0>|)>,<space|2em>Y=1000*E/t+3*W*B<rsub|0>,<space|2em>H=E+Y*B<rsub|0>+Y*t+W*t<rsup|2>+A*t<rsup|3>.
  </equation*>

  Their upper exponents are <math|-1/2,-1,-3/2>, respectively. For example
  <math|E/t<rsup|2>> and <math|A*B<rsub|0>> have upper exponent <math|-1/2>,
  and each of <math|Y*B<rsub|0>,Y*t,W*t<rsup|2>,A*t<rsup|3>> has upper
  exponent <math|-3/2>. Thus

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:remote-probe-jets>F<rsub|p>\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around|(|H,t<rsup|-1>;z|)>*<space|1em><around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>Q|)>,<space|2em>H<text|
    of upper exponent >-3/2.
  </equation>

  The reciprocal <math|t<rsup|-1>> has upper exponent <math|1/2> and is paid
  by the small derivatives of the chart coordinates in the next step.

  <subsubsection|Returning to a protected remote address>

  Suppose the anchor of <math|p> is farther than <math|200*B> from the seeds.
  Let <math|V<around|(|y|)>> be a coarse variation of derivative scale with
  upper exponent <math|-1/2>, and let <math|u<around|(|y|)>> be its
  coherently compensated fine increment. Hold the underlying fine
  configuration <math|U<rsub|\<ast\>>> fixed, as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:compensated-variation>. Let
  <math|\<psi\><rsub|p><around|(|y|)>> be the product of the doubled
  protective cutoffs on the local read region. If <math|\<psi\><rsub|p>>
  vanishes on a neighbourhood of <math|y<rsub|0>>, the protected remainder
  has zero jets there. Otherwise the closed support argument bounds the
  increment on that read region by <math|q<rsub|0>+4*a*\<tau\>>, with
  <math|q<rsub|0>> of upper exponent <math|-1/2>.

  After a signed pullback, the relative increment is <math|<math-up|Ad>> of
  this increment by the appropriate replica link. The product rule and the
  unitary bounds give amplitude

  <\equation*>
    R<rsub|G><rsup|2>*<around|(|q<rsub|0>+4*a*\<tau\>|)>*<around|{|1+<around|(|v+1+v|)>|}><rsup|j>
  </equation*>

  for its jets through order <math|j>; this still has upper exponent
  <math|-1/2>. On tree bonds the increment is identically zero. All other
  bonds in the radius-<math|B> read region are active because the address is
  remote from the seeds. The compensated identity therefore gives, on a
  neighbourhood of <math|y<rsub|0>>,

  <\equation*>
    U<rsub|\<ast\>>=exp u<around|(|y|)><math-up|replica><space|0.17em>V<around|(|y|)><space|1em><text|on
    the radius-><around|\<lfloor\>|7*B/8|\<rfloor\>><text| patch>,
  </equation*>

  with the signed pullback understood on both sides.

  Choose one coarse gauge from <math|V<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>> on this patch.
  The coarse logarithmic coordinates <math|b<around|(|y|)>> have jets of
  upper exponent <math|-1/2> by the fixed-gauge construction in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mean-action-jets>. Conjugate
  <math|u<around|(|y|)>> by this fixed gauge and set it to zero outside the
  patch. This preserves its derivative bound. Denote the resulting pair of
  arrays by <math|z<around|(|y|)>>. For one nonnegative <math|Q> of upper
  exponent <math|-1/2>, we have <math|z\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around|(|Q,1;y<rsub|0>|)>>.

  All terms in <eqref|eq:remote-square-remainder> are determined by this
  patch. To check the radius, the cut guide and transported linear rows read
  within <math|B/4> of their address; the local energy and its predicted
  coarse field read within <math|B/2>, including the fixed predictor reach.
  Both fit inside <math|7*B/8> for the previously chosen geometric threshold.
  The row transports, predictor tags and quadratic extraction decisions are
  unchanged there. Gauge covariance and the local equality of the fields
  consequently identify the actual remainder with
  <math|F<rsub|p><around|(|z<around|(|y|)>|)>> throughout a neighbourhood.

  For a derivative of order <math|k\<leqslant\>j>, the chain rule has at most
  <math|k>! partition terms. A term with <math|m> blocks is bounded by
  <math|H*t<rsup|-m>*Q<rsup|m>>. Hence every derivative of
  <math|F<rsub|p><around|(|z<around|(|y|)>|)>> is bounded by

  <\equation*>
    H*max <around|(|1,j!|)>*<around|(|1+Q*t<rsup|-1>|)><rsup|j>.
  </equation*>

  The second factor has upper exponent zero. We have proved that the remote
  unmasked remainder has jets of upper exponent <math|-3/2> whenever its
  protector is not locally zero. The protector has amplitude one and
  derivative scale bounded by a function of upper exponent zero times its
  support cover. Multiplying it back in preserves the amplitude exponent
  <math|-3/2>, allowing this support factor in the derivative scale.

  <subsubsection|The remote mean square and other short terms>

  The local mean residual <math|m<rsub|p>> has remote value bound of upper
  exponent <math|-1>, proved in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-mean>.
  Its jets have the same exponent. Here is why the scalar estimate suffices
  in this application. On a flat probe ball all sectors are clean and the
  actual mean agrees with its unmasked expression. The derivative estimate on
  the larger <em|shrinking> ball requires an additional check, since the
  intermediate probes need not satisfy the sector flag conditions. Let
  <math|B<rsub|0>=Q+3*t> as above and write <math|A<around|(|z|)>> for the
  empty-seed precision at logarithmic probe <math|z>. The same-history
  comparison at the identity supplies <math|A<around|(|0|)>\<geqslant\>r<rsub|0>*I>
  for a fixed <math|r<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0>. On
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|0>>, the
  transported-row estimates give an entry derivative bound
  <math|C*v<rsub|1>>, where <math|C> is fixed and <math|v<rsub|1>> has upper
  exponent zero. Integrating this derivative along the segment from <math|0>
  to <math|z> gives <math|<around|\||A<around|(|z|)><rsub|i*k>-A<around|(|0|)><rsub|i*k>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*v<rsub|1>*B<rsub|0>>.
  The matrices have range at most <math|B> and coordinate density at most
  <math|\<rho\>> per site. Both the row and column sums of this difference
  are therefore at most <math|b<rsub|B>*\<rho\>*C*v<rsub|1>*B<rsub|0>>, which
  tends to zero. The elementary row-column bound on the Euclidean operator
  norm follows by Cauchy\USchwarz: <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|H*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|(|sup<rsub|i>
  <big|sum><rsub|k><around|\||H<rsub|i*k>|\|>|)><around|(|sup<rsub|k>
  <big|sum><rsub|i><around|\||H<rsub|i*k>|\|>|)><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>>.
  Consequently <math|A<around|(|z|)>\<geqslant\><around|(|r<rsub|0>/2|)>*I>
  throughout the shrinking ball for large <math|x>. Every principal
  restriction has the same floor. The exponentially weighted entry estimates
  remain uniform there; the inverse and padded-inverse jet bounds proved
  earlier thus apply with fixed reduced floor and width. They cost only a
  polynomial in <math|B> at fixed derivative order.

  The empty-seed residual is the zero-fluctuation row residual minus the
  transported row applied to its padded-inverse response, that is
  <math|f-M*P*M<rsup|\<ast\>>*f> after the fixed row normalization. All four
  factors now have jet bounds of upper exponent zero on this shrinking ball.
  The product and row-sum estimates give the required upper exponent zero
  bound for the residual there. On the smaller radius-<math|3*t> ball every
  empty-seed probe is clean, so the same-history stationary comparison
  applies separately to each probe and gives the remote value exponent
  <math|-1>. No flag condition was assumed on the entire larger ball. Apply
  the same interpolation above with this probe value exponent <math|-1>. Then
  <math|W,Y,H> have upper exponents <math|0,-1/2,-1>. The fixed-gauge
  composition again costs only upper exponent zero. Finally the mean residual
  depends only on the radius-<math|24*B> patch and the unchanged windows; a
  point farther than <math|30*B> from the seeds has the same local formula as
  the empty-seed comparison. This gives the claimed exponent <math|-1> for
  every fixed jet order, with no derivative of a gauge choice or stationary
  minimizer.

  At most two such residuals enter a local mean-square term, so its jets have
  upper exponent <math|-2>. The remote logarithmic action is
  <math|-\<theta\>*\<beta\>> times the sum of this mean-square term and the
  protected remainder, where <math|0\<leqslant\>\<theta\>\<leqslant\>1> is
  the fixed channel weight and <math|0\<less\>\<beta\>\<leqslant\>2*h>. Its
  amplitude therefore has upper exponent <math|-1/2>. This uses the same
  <math|\<beta\>> as in the original action.

  The other two short logarithmic terms are the remote normalization
  correction and the protected chart logarithm. Their jets have upper
  exponent <math|-1/2> by Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:normalization-jets>
  and <reference|sec:haar-chart>. Each term is set to zero on its prescribed
  packet neighbourhood; this indicator depends only on the frozen sector.
  Consequently the three short families have one common nonnegative amplitude
  <math|b<around|(|x|)>> of upper exponent <math|-1/2>, and derivative scale

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:remote-short-price>v<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>*<around|{|1+<math-up|cov><rsub|B><around|(|S<rsub|p>|)>|}>,<space|2em>v<rsub|0><text|
    of upper exponent zero>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|S<rsub|p>> is the full enlarged read support of the term. For
  fixed enlargement <math|\<ell\>>, these supports lie in radius
  <math|<around|(|\<ell\>+1|)>*B> balls about their anchors and have
  uniformly bounded cover number. The number of anchors meeting a given site
  is at most <math|b<rsub|<around|(|\<ell\>+1|)>*B>> times the fixed density
  of the three families. Their exponentially cover-weighted site sum is
  therefore bounded by this polynomial count times <math|b<around|(|x|)>> and
  a fixed exponential in the cover bound. It still has upper exponent
  <math|-1/2>, uniformly in the period. This supplies the short-activity part
  of the logarithmic price estimate.

  <subsection|Derivative prices for the Gaussian correction
  chains><label|sec:itinerary-prices>

  The corrections in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-covariance> are
  sums over ordered itineraries of physical bonds. Their operator bounds
  alone do not price each activity in the connected expansion. We now keep
  the itinerary labels and their full read supports while differentiating and
  summing them.

  <subsubsection|Products along a fixed itinerary>

  Write <math|q> for the number of colour coordinates. An itinerary of length
  <math|N> consists of physical bonds <math|e<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,e<rsub|N>>;
  colour indices are summed within its matrix product. Suppose the entry jets
  of its <math|r>th matrix have amplitude <math|a<rsub|r>\<geqslant\>0> and
  common derivative scale <math|d\<geqslant\>0>. Repeated matrix
  multiplication and the product rule give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:itinerary-entry-price>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<space|-0.17em><around*|(|q<rsup|N>*<big|prod><rsub|r=0><rsup|N-1>a<rsub|r>,N*d|)>
  </equation>

  for any entry of the colour-block product. This follows by induction on
  <math|N>: multiplying the next entry adds <math|d> to the derivative scale,
  multiplies the amplitude by <math|a<rsub|r>>, and introduces at most
  <math|q> choices for the next colour. The estimate deliberately overcounts
  when <math|N=1>. Taking a colour trace adds a factor <math|q>. Integrating
  such a trace over <math|s\<in\><around|[|0,1|]>> preserves this bound when
  the entry estimates are uniform in <math|s>.

  For the present matrices, this last differentiation under the integral is
  valid by the compact-glide argument of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:normalization-jets>:
  all principal determinants are nonzero on one common parameter
  neighbourhood for <math|s\<in\><around|[|0,1|]>>, and the finite matrix
  products are jointly smooth there. A closed itinerary contributes its
  diagonal trace; a nonclosed itinerary contributes zero to that trace.

  All the matrices in these words have range at most <math|3*B>. If some
  consecutive pair of bonds has all endpoint distances greater than
  <math|3*B>, the corresponding matrix block vanishes identically as the
  coarse field varies. Thus the whole word is a zero germ. Call an itinerary
  admissible when every consecutive pair has endpoints at distance at most
  <math|3*B>. This condition is fixed by its geometry, and is retained in
  every price below.

  Let <math|a> be the common weighted jet amplitude of the actual precision
  <math|A>, let <math|h<rsub|0>> price the padded inverse of the glide, and
  let <math|t<rsub|0>> price its error <math|A<rsub|s>*P<rsub|s>-1>. These
  quantities were constructed in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:normalization-jets>,
  under other names. There <math|A> has the envelope <math|<around|(|f,v|)>>
  of <eqref|eq:matrix-jet-envelope>, and the glide
  <math|A<rsub|s>=1+s*<around|(|A-1|)>> has the envelope
  <math|<around|(|f+2,v<rsub|A>|)>> uniformly in
  <math|s\<in\><around|[|0,1|]>>; we take <math|a=f+2>, which covers both.
  The padded principal inverse <math|P<rsub|s>> of the glide has row and
  column price <eqref|eq:pad-jet-price> with <math|f> replaced by <math|f+2>,
  so <math|h<rsub|0>=\<Delta\><rsub|B>*T<rsub|k>*<around|(|f+2,S|)>>. Its
  error <math|E<rsub|s>=A<rsub|s>*P<rsub|s>-1> has the price
  <eqref|eq:inverse-error-jets> with the same replacement, so
  <math|t<rsub|0>=e<rsup|-\<gamma\>*B/2>*<around|{|<around|(|f+2|)>*h<rsub|0>+1|}>>.
  The derivative scales are the fixed multiples of <math|v<rsub|A>> recorded
  there. The amplitudes <math|a,h<rsub|0>> have upper exponent zero;
  <math|t<rsub|0>> has every negative upper exponent. The first logarithmic
  correction has word

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|A-1|)>*P<rsub|s>*<around|(|-E<rsub|s>|)><rsup|n+1>,<space|2em>E<rsub|s>=A<rsub|s>*P<rsub|s>-1.
  </equation*>

  It has <math|n+3> matrix factors. If <math|d<rsub|A>> bounds their
  derivative scales, <eqref|eq:itinerary-entry-price> and the colour trace
  give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:itinerary-A-price>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around*|(|C<rsub|A>*Q<rsub|A><rsup|n>,<around|(|n+3|)>*d<rsub|A>|)>,<space|2em>C<rsub|A>=q<rsup|4>*<around|(|a+1|)>*h<rsub|0>*t<rsub|0>,<space|1em>Q<rsub|A>=q*t<rsub|0>.
  </equation>

  The same argument applies to the flat-reference correction. Its coarse
  field is constant and satisfies the same entry bounds.

  For the second correction put <math|D=A*C-1>, where <math|C> is the
  symmetric padded covariance. Its entry jets have amplitude <math|w<rsub|0>>
  with every negative upper exponent, obtained by multiplying the
  padded-inverse error by the full inverse envelope and then by <math|A>. If
  <math|d<rsub|D>> is their derivative scale, the word
  <math|D*<around|(|-s*D|)><rsup|n>> has <math|n+1> factors. Since
  <math|0\<leqslant\>s\<leqslant\>1>, its integrated trace has bound

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:itinerary-S-price>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around*|(|C<rsub|S>*Q<rsub|S><rsup|n>,<around|(|n+1|)>*d<rsub|D>|)>,<space|2em>C<rsub|S>=q<rsup|2>*w<rsub|0>,<space|1em>Q<rsub|S>=q*w<rsub|0>.
  </equation>

  These formulas price the actual trace words, including their endpoint
  identifications. Discarding a factor <math|1/2> or another coefficient of
  absolute value at most one only enlarges the bounds.

  <subsubsection|The two protected Gaussian endpoints>

  The inverse correction is a quadratic form with word
  <math|<around|(|-D|)><rsup|n+1>*A>, of length <math|n+2>, and a Gaussian
  coordinate at each endpoint. Those coordinates are not bounded on the whole
  sample space. Their protective cutoffs supply the needed local bound.

  Let <math|u<around|(|y|)>=\<nu\><around|(|y|)>+g<rsub|\<beta\>>*z<around|(|y|)>>,
  <math|g<rsub|\<beta\>>=\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>>, be the compensated increment
  on an active physical bond. If its doubled cutoff is not a zero germ, the
  increment jet bound has amplitude <math|s<rsub|0>+4*a<rsub|0>*\<tau\>>,
  where <math|s<rsub|0>> has upper exponent <math|-1/2> and
  <math|a<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> is the fixed cutoff parameter. The mean jets have
  amplitude <math|m<rsub|0>> of upper exponent <math|-1/2>. Hence

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:itinerary-endpoint-price>z<rsub|e>\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around|(|p,1|)>,<space|2em>p=<sqrt|\<beta\>>*<space|0.17em><around|(|s<rsub|0>+4*a<rsub|0>*\<tau\>+m<rsub|0>|)>,
  </equation>

  which has upper exponent zero since <math|\<beta\>\<leqslant\>2*h>. If
  either cutoff is a zero germ, the protected quadratic activity is a zero
  germ instead. If an endpoint is inactive, the inactive block of the
  completed precision and covariance is the identity; the corresponding
  defect block is zero. These block identities persist on the neighbourhood
  where the fixed principal windows remain invertible. The quadratic word
  therefore vanishes as a germ unless both endpoints are active. This
  justifies using <eqref|eq:itinerary-endpoint-price> only where it applies.

  Suppose the cutoff jets have amplitude one and scale <math|d<rsub|0>>. The
  word has entry amplitude <math|q<rsup|n+2>*a*w<rsub|0><rsup|n+1>>. The two
  endpoint colour sums add <math|q<rsup|2>*p<rsup|2>>, and the product rule
  adds two endpoint scales and two cutoff scales. Enlarging the resulting
  scale gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:itinerary-quadratic-price>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around*|(|C<rsub|Q>*Q<rsub|Q><rsup|n>,<around|(|n+2|)>*d<rsub|Q>|)>,<space|2em>C<rsub|Q>=p<rsup|2>*q<rsup|4>*a*w<rsub|0>,<space|1em>Q<rsub|Q>=q*w<rsub|0>,<space|1em>d<rsub|Q>=d<rsub|D>+d<rsub|0>+1,
  </equation>

  after enlarging <math|d<rsub|D>> to include the scale of <math|A>. All
  derivative scales here have upper exponent zero. The unprotected linear
  score is handled separately: its coefficient has every negative upper
  exponent by the mean identity <eqref|eq:action-score-small>,
  <math|A*\<nu\>+\<ell\>=-A*<around|(|P-A<rsup|-1>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<ell\>>,
  and the price of <math|P-A<rsup|-1>> that follows
  <eqref|eq:inverse-error-jets>, while its one protected endpoint has the
  bound just proved. Summing its <math|q> colours and multiplying its cutoff
  produces one local bond activity with every negative upper exponent, even
  after its fixed-radius support is counted.

  <subsubsection|Paying the derivative order before the chain length>

  All three itinerary families have the form

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around|(|C*Q<rsup|n>,<around|(|n+u|)>*d|)>,<space|2em>u\<in\><around|{|3,1,2|}>,
  </equation*>

  on admissible itineraries and zero germs otherwise. To give every jet a
  common amplitude and unit derivative scale, multiply the amplitude by
  <math|<around|(|1+<around|(|n+u|)>*d|)><rsup|j>>. The elementary inequality
  <math|n+1\<leqslant\>2<rsup|n>> implies

  <\equation*>
    1+<around|(|n+u|)>*d\<leqslant\>2<rsup|n>*<around|{|1+<around|(|1+u|)>*d|}>.
  </equation*>

  It is therefore enough to replace

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:itinerary-inflated-price>C\<longmapsto\>C<rprime|'>=C*<around|{|1+<around|(|1+u|)>*d|}><rsup|j>,<space|2em>Q\<longmapsto\>Q<rprime|'>=2<rsup|j>*Q.
  </equation>

  No factor depending on <math|n> remains outside the geometric power. For
  fixed <math|j>, <math|C<rprime|'>> retains every negative upper exponent of
  <math|C>, and <math|Q<rprime|'>> retains every negative upper exponent of
  <math|Q>.

  <subsubsection|The rooted support sum>

  Fix a halo multiplier <math|m\<geqslant\>4>, also large enough to contain
  the previously established mean, window and compensated-variation read
  radii, a support exponent <math|a<rsub|1>\<geqslant\>0>, and a length
  reserve <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>. The inequality <math|m\<geqslant\>4>
  alone is used in the count below; the additional read-radius requirement is
  needed when applying it to the actual activities. The support of an
  itinerary is the union of the radius-<math|m*B> halos of the endpoint sets
  of all its bonds. Put

  <\equation*>
    \<rho\><rsub|L>=L<rsup|4>#<around|{|<math-up|axes>|}>,<space|1em>K=b<rsub|3*B+2>*\<rho\><rsub|L>,<space|1em>s<rsub|m>=b<rsub|m+1>.
  </equation*>

  There are at most <math|K> choices for a next bond in an admissible step:
  its anchor is within <math|3*B+2> of the preceding anchor. Thus there are
  at most <math|K<rsup|n+u>> admissible itineraries of length <math|n+u> with
  prescribed initial bond.

  Each bond halo has <math|B>-cover number at most <math|s<rsub|m>>.
  Consecutive halos intersect because <math|3*B+2\<leqslant\>m*B>.
  Subadditivity along the ordered chain gives support cover at most
  <math|<around|(|n+u+1|)>*s<rsub|m>>. If this support contains a fixed site
  <math|x<rsub|0>>, the initial anchor is within
  <math|<around|(|n+u+1|)>*<around|(|m*B+1|)>> of <math|x<rsub|0>>: follow
  the chain to a halo containing <math|x<rsub|0>> and sum the step distances.
  Since <math|n+u+1\<leqslant\><around|(|u+1|)>*2<rsup|n>>, the number of
  possible initial bonds is at most

  <\equation*>
    b<rsub|<around|(|u+1|)>*<around|(|m*B+1|)>>*16<rsup|n>*\<rho\><rsub|L>.
  </equation*>

  The factor <math|16<rsup|n>> is the fourth power of <math|2<rsup|n>>; it
  also bounds the additive one in the lattice ball formula. Wrapping on a
  torus can only reduce these counts.

  Multiplying the price <math|C<rprime|'><around|(|Q<rprime|'>|)><rsup|n>>,
  the count, the support weight and the extra length weight
  <math|e<rsup|2*\<varepsilon\>*n>> gives the bound

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:itinerary-site-series><big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>:x<rsub|0>\<in\>S<rsub|\<lambda\>>>p<rsub|\<lambda\>>*e<rsup|a<rsub|1><math-up|cov><rsub|B><around|(|S<rsub|\<lambda\>>|)>>*e<rsup|2*\<varepsilon\>*n<around|(|\<lambda\>|)>>\<leqslant\>H*C<rprime|'>*<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>0><around*|[|Q<rprime|'>*16*K*e<rsup|a<rsub|1>*s<rsub|m>+2*\<varepsilon\>>|]><rsup|n>,
  </equation>

  where

  <\equation*>
    H=b<rsub|<around|(|u+1|)>*<around|(|m*B+1|)>>*\<rho\><rsub|L>*K<rsup|u>*e<rsup|a<rsub|1>*<around|(|u+1|)>*s<rsub|m>>.
  </equation*>

  All sums have nonnegative terms, so grouping first by length and then by
  initial bond is justified without a convergence assumption. At fixed
  <math|m,L,j>, both <math|K> and <math|H> have upper exponent zero. The
  geometric ratio in <eqref|eq:itinerary-site-series> is eventually at most
  <math|1/2>, and its sum is at most <math|2*H*C<rprime|'>>. Consequently
  every prescribed negative upper exponent is available for the rooted price
  of these correction chains. This pays the actual full read supports and
  every itinerary label, including repeated physical bonds; it does not
  assume disjoint paths or independent Gaussian coordinates.

  <subsection|Prices for the inherited activities><label|sec:inherited-activity-prices>

  The remaining logarithmic activities come from the incoming representation:
  its regular activities, cubic and quartic path terms, quadratic loop terms,
  and the true long-path tails on the fine and coarse levels. We convert
  their existing coefficient bounds into prices on the full coarse read
  supports. This section assumes those incoming coefficient bounds on the
  same representation and paths as the selected step. It proves the price
  conversion; maintenance of the incoming bounds along the renormalisation
  run is a separate construction requirement.

  <subsubsection|Coarsening a connected cover>

  Let <math|\<pi\>> be the parent map with block length <math|L>, and let
  <math|c<rsub|M><around|(|X|)>> be the connected covering number at radius
  <math|M>. Thus <math|X> is contained in the union of <math|M>-balls around
  <math|c<rsub|M><around|(|X|)>> centres, and that union is connected by unit
  lattice steps. For <math|B\<geqslant\>1>, <math|M\<leqslant\>2*B> and
  integer <math|\<ell\>\<geqslant\>0>, put

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|\<ell\>>=b<rsub|\<ell\>+6>,<space|2em>v<rsub|\<ell\>>=10*P<rsub|\<ell\>>/L,<space|2em>Y=<math-up|halo><rsub|\<ell\>*B><around|(|\<pi\>*X|)>.
  </equation*>

  Then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:inherited-cover-conversion>c<rsub|B><around|(|Y|)>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|\<ell\>>+v<rsub|\<ell\>>*c<rsub|M><around|(|X|)>.
  </equation>

  Here is a proof retaining the factor <math|L<rsup|-1>>. Choose a minimal
  cover with centre set <math|D>. The graph joining centres at distance at
  most <math|2*M+1> is connected. Traverse a spanning tree out and back,
  replacing each edge by steps of max-distance at most one (diagonal steps
  are allowed). This gives a walk visiting all centres, of length at most
  <math|<around|(|4*M+2|)><around|\||D|\|>>. Sample it every <math|L*B> steps
  and apply <math|\<pi\>>. If its length is <math|N>, the resulting centre
  set <math|D<rprime|'>> satisfies <math|L*B<around|\||D<rprime|'>|\|>\<leqslant\>N+L*B>.
  The parent-distance inequality

  <\equation*>
    L*<space|0.17em>d*<around|(|\<pi\>*x,\<pi\>*y|)>\<leqslant\>d*<around|(|x,y|)>+L
  </equation*>

  shows that every projected walk point is within <math|2*B> of a sampled
  centre and that consecutive sampled centres are within <math|2*B>. Every
  point of <math|X> is within <math|M\<leqslant\>2*B> of an original centre;
  its parent is therefore within <math|3*B> of that centre's parent. Thus
  <math|Y> is covered by the <math|<around|(|\<ell\>+5|)>*B>-balls about
  <math|D<rprime|'>>, with connected union. Tile their slightly enlarged
  <math|<around|(|\<ell\>+6|)>*B>-balls by <math|B>-balls with centres on a
  <math|B>-spaced four-dimensional grid. There are at most
  <math|P<rsub|\<ell\>>> tiles per centre. These enlarged halos overlap along
  the sampled walk, so the resulting cover remains connected. Finally
  <math|<around|(|4*M+2|)><around|\||D|\|>\<leqslant\>10*B<around|\||D|\|>>
  proves <eqref|eq:inherited-cover-conversion>. For an empty set the
  inequality is immediate. Wrapping any of these walks or grids only
  decreases the number of distinct centres.

  If <math|o\<in\>X> and <math|y\<in\>Y>, the same spanning walk gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:inherited-anchor-distance>d*<around|(|y,\<pi\>*o|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<ell\>+20|)>*B*<around|{|1+c<rsub|M><around|(|X|)>|}>.
  </equation>

  Indeed two points of <math|X> are at distance at most
  <math|<around|(|4*M+2|)>*c<rsub|M><around|(|X|)>+2*M>; apply the
  parent-distance inequality and then add the <math|\<ell\>*B> halo. The
  displayed larger constant bounds the result for <math|M\<leqslant\>2*B>,
  <math|B\<geqslant\>1>. Inspecting the fine plaquettes touching a support
  adds at most four fine lattice steps. Their parents lie within five coarse
  steps, so replacing <math|\<ell\>> by <math|\<ell\>+5> pays all these
  reads.

  <subsubsection|From an anchor bound to a site bound>

  We give a general counting estimate. Suppose nonnegative weights
  <math|b<rsub|i>>, integer costs <math|d<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>, fine anchors
  <math|o<rsub|i>>, and coarse supports <math|Y<rsub|i>> satisfy

  <\equation*>
    sup<rsub|o> <big|sum><rsub|i:o<rsub|i>=o>b<rsub|i>*e<rsup|\<sigma\>*d<rsub|i>>\<leqslant\>\<Gamma\>,<space|2em>c<rsub|B><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>v+v<rprime|'>*d<rsub|i>,<space|2em>y\<in\>Y<rsub|i>\<Longrightarrow\>d*<around|(|y,\<pi\>*o<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>W*<around|(|1+d<rsub|i>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|k,W> be nonnegative integers and
  <math|0\<leqslant\>g<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>C*b<rsub|i>*<around|(|1+d<rsub|i>|)><rsup|k>>
  with <math|C\<geqslant\>0>, and assume

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:inherited-reserve>a\<geqslant\>0,<space|1em>\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0,<space|1em>a*v<rprime|'>+2*\<varepsilon\>\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>.
  </equation>

  For a fixed coarse site <math|y>, the fine anchors of labels with
  <math|d<rsub|i>=n> and <math|y\<in\>Y<rsub|i>> have parents in a
  radius-<math|W*<around|(|1+n|)>> ball. There are at most
  <math|L<rsup|4>*b<rsub|W>*<around|(|1+n|)><rsup|4>> such anchors, because a
  parent has exactly <math|L<rsup|4>> residues. The sum of
  <math|g<rsub|i>*e<rsup|a*c<rsub|B><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>>> over these
  labels is at most

  <\equation*>
    L<rsup|4>*b<rsub|W>*\<Gamma\>*C*e<rsup|a*v>*<around|(|1+n|)><rsup|4+k>*e<rsup|-2*\<varepsilon\>*n>.
  </equation*>

  Use the exponential series at <math|\<varepsilon\>*<around|(|1+n|)>> to
  obtain

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|1+n|)><rsup|4+k>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsup|-<around|(|4+k|)>>*<around|(|4+k|)>!e<rsup|\<varepsilon\>>*e<rsup|\<varepsilon\>*n>.
  </equation*>

  Summing the geometric series proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:inherited-payment>sup<rsub|y>
    <big|sum><rsub|i:y\<in\>Y<rsub|i>>g<rsub|i>*e<rsup|a*c<rsub|B><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|a*v>*\<Gamma\>*C*L<rsup|4>*b<rsub|W>*<frac|\<varepsilon\><rsup|-<around|(|4+k|)>>*<around|(|4+k|)>!e<rsup|\<varepsilon\>>|1-e<rsup|-\<varepsilon\>>>.
  </equation>

  Restricting to <math|d<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>n<rsub|0>> adds the factor
  <math|e<rsup|-\<varepsilon\>*n<rsub|0>>>. The proof works with arbitrary
  label multiplicities, since the input already sums all labels at an anchor.
  Nonnegative finite partial sums justify the estimate also for a countable
  alphabet.

  We need an additional factor <math|e<rsup|2*\<varepsilon\>*d<rsub|i>>> in
  the output price. Apply the preceding argument to weights
  <math|b<rsub|i>*e<rsup|2*\<varepsilon\>*d<rsub|i>>> and exponent
  <math|\<sigma\>-2*\<varepsilon\>>. The sufficient condition becomes
  <math|a*v<rprime|'>+4*\<varepsilon\>\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>>. For inherited
  supports use <eqref|eq:inherited-cover-conversion> with
  <math|d<rsub|i>=c<rsub|M><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>> and
  <math|W=<around|(|\<ell\>+20|)>*B>. If <math|4*\<varepsilon\>\<less\>\<sigma\>>,
  choose <math|L> large enough that <math|a*10*P<rsub|\<ell\>>/L+4*\<varepsilon\>\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>>,
  before increasing <math|x>. The incoming cover radius
  <math|M=B<around|(|h|)>> is eventually at most <math|2*B<around|(|x|)>>:
  <math|h=x+c<rsub|L>> with <math|c<rsub|L>> fixed, and the
  logarithmic-square radius has <math|B*<around|(|x+c<rsub|L>|)>/B<around|(|x|)>\<to\>1>.
  All factors in <eqref|eq:inherited-payment> other than <math|\<Gamma\>*C>
  then have upper exponent zero.

  <subsubsection|Path lengths, including winding paths>

  For a bundle of <math|r> paths based at one fine anchor, let
  <math|d<rsub|i>> be the sum of their true lengths, including their final
  plaquette walks. Dividing each walk into pieces of <math|B> steps shows
  that its visited sites have connected <math|B>-cover number at most
  <math|1+<text|length>/B>. All paths share the anchor, so their union with
  the anchor has cover at most <math|1+r+d<rsub|i>/B>. Applying
  <eqref|eq:inherited-cover-conversion> at <math|M=B> gives

  <\equation*>
    c<rsub|B><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|\<ell\>>+v<rsub|\<ell\>>*<around|(|1+r|)>+<around|(|v<rsub|\<ell\>>/B|)>*d<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  The support lies within <math|<around|(|\<ell\>*B+1|)>*<around|(|1+d<rsub|i>|)>>
  of the parent anchor. Thus the preceding site estimate applies with
  <math|v<rprime|'>=v<rsub|\<ell\>>/B>. For any fixed <math|L>, this
  coefficient tends to zero, so the required strict exponential reserve is
  eventually available. No path length is replaced by its endpoint distance;
  in particular, winding and repeated edges retain their costs.

  Suppose the path coefficients satisfy <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>b<rsub|i>*e<rsup|\<sigma\>*d<rsub|i>>\<leqslant\>\<Gamma\>>
  at each translated anchor. An order-<math|r> atom evaluated with its
  original small-field mask has absolute value at most
  <math|b<rsub|i>*\<tau\><rsup|r>>: every one of its <math|r> curvature
  arguments has norm at most <math|\<tau\>>, while the unhealthy branch is
  zero. An external multiplier bounded by <math|D<around|(|x|)>> therefore
  has scalar price <math|D<around|(|x|)>*\<tau\><rsup|r>*b<rsub|i>>. The site
  conversion preserves the sum of the upper exponents of <math|D>,
  <math|\<tau\><rsup|r>>, and <math|\<Gamma\>>, allowing any strictly larger
  power for the final bound. Applied to the actual families,

  <\equation*>
    <tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-lborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-rborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-rborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|-1|3|3|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|3|3|cell-rborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|<text|family>>|<cell|<text|multiplier
    and coefficient budget>>|<cell|<text|upper
    exponent>>>|<row|<cell|<text|cubic>>|<cell|2*h*<space|0.17em>\<tau\><rsup|3>\<cdot\>C>|<cell|-1/2>>|<row|<cell|<text|quartic>>|<cell|2*h*<space|0.17em>\<tau\><rsup|4>\<cdot\>C>|<cell|-1>>|<row|<cell|<text|quadratic
    loop>>|<cell|\<tau\><rsup|2>\<cdot\>2*P<around|(|h|)>>|<cell|-1>>>>>
  </equation*>

  Here <math|C> is the incoming fixed path bound, <math|P<around|(|h|)>> is
  the monitor, and the channel weight has absolute value at most one. The
  multiplier is the original <math|\<beta\>\<leqslant\>2*h>, wherever it
  occurs. The fixed number of signed orientations and plaquette axes is
  summed, not discarded.

  For the tail of a squared path row, retain the ordered pair of path labels
  <math|<around|(|i,k|)>>. With coefficients <math|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|k>> and
  lengths <math|l<rsub|i>,l<rsub|k>>, its price is

  <\equation*>
    b<rsub|i*k>=2*<around|\||a<rsub|i>*a<rsub|k>|\|>*<space|0.17em>1<rsub|<around|{|l<rsub|i>*d\<gtr\>R<math-up|or><space|0.17em>l<rsub|k>*d\<gtr\>R|}>>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|d=1> for the fine row and <math|d=L> for the coarse row. This
  bounds the actual half inner product because each unmasked transported
  curvature has norm at most two; the kept-row indicator can only decrease
  its magnitude. If <math|<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\||a<rsub|i>|\|>*e<rsup|\<sigma\>*l<rsub|i>>\<leqslant\>C>,
  then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:inherited-tail-budget><big|sum><rsub|i,k>b<rsub|i*k>*e<rsup|<around|(|\<sigma\>/2|)>*<around|(|l<rsub|i>+l<rsub|k>|)>>\<leqslant\>2*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*R/<around|(|2*d|)>>*C<rsup|2>.
  </equation>

  Indeed every retained pair has <math|l<rsub|i>+l<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>R/d>;
  extract <math|e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*R/<around|(|2*d|)>>> and use the product of
  the two weighted row sums. The genuine read support includes both full
  paths and the kept-row test halo. After projection that extra halo is paid
  by replacing <math|\<ell\>> by <math|\<ell\>+2>. The site bound with its
  length reserve is valid if <math|8*\<varepsilon\>\<less\>\<sigma\>>, since
  its available input exponent is now <math|\<sigma\>/2>. As
  <math|R=<around|\<lfloor\>|B/100000|\<rfloor\>>>, the right side of
  <eqref|eq:inherited-tail-budget> has every negative upper exponent, even
  after multiplication by <math|2*h>, fixed powers of the true length and the
  site-conversion cost. For the coarse-level tail the parent map is the
  identity; its population factor is one, and the exponential still has fixed
  <math|d=L>. The argument thus covers both tails without confusing their
  spatial scales.

  <subsubsection|Inherited jets in the compensated variation>

  The incoming regular activity budget has upper exponent <math|-11/10>; the
  incoming forced activity budget has every negative upper exponent. The
  former bounds the original masked occurrence on its healthy domain,
  extended by zero, and the latter bounds the forced occurrence everywhere.
  Apply the anchor-to-site estimate to their original supports and masks.
  This preserves those exponents and all label multiplicities.

  During the compensated variation the underlying fine field
  <math|U<rsub|\<ast\>>> is held fixed. Hence the regular, cubic, quartic,
  loop, fine-tail and forced values are constant in the coarse parameter.
  Their protective factors vary. On a coarse support <math|Y>, each
  protective bond cutoff has amplitude one and derivative scale of upper
  exponent zero. Covering <math|Y> by its <math|B>-cover, its full bond read
  set contains at most <math|b<rsub|B>*b<rsub|1>*\<rho\><rsub|L>*c<rsub|B><around|(|Y|)>>
  bonds. The product rule therefore gives the protector derivative scale
  <math|v<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>*c<rsub|B><around|(|Y|)>>, with
  <math|v<rsub|0>> of upper exponent zero. Multiplying a constant value by
  this protector preserves its scalar price.

  The coarse tail is the exception: its original coarse field varies. For a
  path of length <math|l>, the unitary product argument gives its transported
  curvature jets amplitude <math|<around|\||a<rsub|i>|\|>*R<rsub|G><rsup|2>>
  and derivative scale <math|2*R<rsub|G>*v*l>. A product of two paths thus
  has scale <math|2*R<rsub|G>*v*<around|(|l<rsub|i>+l<rsub|k>|)>>, and its
  half inner product has amplitude at most
  <math|<around|\||a<rsub|i>*a<rsub|k>|\|>*R<rsub|G><rsup|4>>. Multiplying by
  the original <math|\<beta\>> and channel coefficient is paid by
  <math|2*h*R<rsub|G><rsup|4>*b<rsub|i*k>>. The same tail estimate prices
  these labels with every negative upper exponent, retaining the total length
  in the derivative scale. The function differentiated here is the unmasked
  extension of the coarse tail: the smooth path-pair expression with the
  fixed long-pair selector and with the coarse kept-row test omitted. The
  actual coarse tail carries that kept-row test, which is a threshold test in
  the coarse field and is not differentiated. At a base field under the
  funded-room condition whose full footprint of the term is healthy, the kept
  test holds on that footprint, so the unmasked extension equals the actual
  coarse tail in value there. The healthy-footprint hypothesis is the one
  supplied by the healthy family in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:activity-jet-assembly>.
  No derivative of the kept test is asserted, and the identification is made
  only at such base fields.

  Choose <math|-1/2\<less\>t\<less\>0>. By the strict exponent slack, the six
  inherited logarithmic families together have a rooted price at most
  <math|h<rsup|t>> for sufficiently large <math|x>, with the support and
  <math|2*\<varepsilon\>> extra-cost reserves just proved. For the coarse
  tail add its differentiated price to the scalar one; both are nonnegative
  and negligible, so this addition is paid by choosing an intermediate
  exponent between <math|-1/2> and <math|t> first. The forced family has the
  same bound. Their common derivative scale can be taken as

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:inherited-jet-price>v<rsub|1><around|(|x|)>*<around|{|c<rsub|B><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>+d<rsub|i>|}>,<space|2em>v<rsub|1><text|
    of upper exponent zero>,
  </equation>

  where <math|d<rsub|i>> is the old support cover or the true path length, as
  appropriate. The bounds are uniform over the held fine configuration and
  preserve its original labels and parameters.

  <subsection|From a seed energy gain to packet
  estimates><label|sec:packet-estimates>

  We now supply the passage from local energy and chart estimates to the
  packet price used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mixed-activity-prices>.
  There are three distinct costs. Extending the energy from a seed core to
  its full support introduces a small annular error. The density and
  normalization factors introduce a polynomial logarithmic expense.
  Differentiating the packet introduces a further polynomial factor. All
  three are paid from the same energy gain per seed.

  Keep the scales <math|h,P,d,\<tau\>,B> of <eqref|eq:warning-scales>, and
  fix the block length, chart, shell width and all other local parameters
  before sending <math|x> to infinity. All constants below are independent of
  the lattice period. For clarity, say that <math|r<around|(|x|)>> has upper
  exponent <math|a> if, for every <math|b\<gtr\>a>,
  <math|<around|\||r<around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>h<around|(|x|)><rsup|b>>
  eventually. This permits subpower losses. Products add upper exponents, and
  a fixed finite sum preserves a common upper exponent: in each case choose a
  strictly smaller exponent than the one finally required and absorb fixed
  constants using <math|h\<to\>\<infty\>>. In particular <math|B>, <math|d>
  and every fixed real power of <math|P> have upper exponent zero, while
  <math|\<tau\>> has upper exponent <math|-1/2>.

  <subsubsection|Extending a core energy bound>

  Consider a packet with <math|n> seeds. In each signed channel let
  <math|D<rsub|2>\<subseteq\>D<rsub|200>> be the sets of local energy
  addresses within <math|2*B> and <math|200*B> of its seeds. Their sizes
  satisfy

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||D<rsub|200>|\|>\<leqslant\>n*<space|0.17em>b<rsub|200*B+1>*<space|0.17em>s<rsub|L>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|s<rsub|L>> bounds the number of local energy addresses per
  coarse site. Let <math|e<rsub|p>> be the assigned energy at address
  <math|p> in that channel. Suppose the core energy is
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|p\<in\>D<rsub|2>>e<rsub|p>> and the annular terms
  satisfy <math|e<rsub|p>\<geqslant\>-r<around|(|x|)>> on
  <math|D<rsub|200>\<setminus\>D<rsub|2>>, with
  <math|r<around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>0>. Then

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|p\<in\>D<rsub|200>>e<rsub|p>\<geqslant\><big|sum><rsub|p\<in\>D<rsub|2>>e<rsub|p>-r<around|(|x|)>*b<rsub|200*B+1>*s<rsub|L>*n.
  </equation*>

  Average this inequality over signed channels with their normalized weights.
  If the averaged core energy is at least <math|\<mu\>*\<tau\><rsup|2>*n>,
  the averaged extended energy <math|Q<rsub|A>> obeys

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:packet-assigned-energy>Q<rsub|A>\<geqslant\><around|(|\<mu\>*\<tau\><rsup|2>-E<around|(|x|)>|)>*n,<space|2em>E<around|(|x|)>=r<around|(|x|)>*b<rsub|200*B+1>*s<rsub|L>.
  </equation>

  No energy gain from one channel is multiplied by a factor from another: the
  average is taken on the common configuration and common packet.

  The local inputs in this application are a positive seed charge
  <math|\<mu\>>, the displayed core bound, and an annular remainder with
  upper exponent <math|-3/2>. The latter is proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:packet-annulus> by comparing the fine and
  predictor energies with their extracted affine squares. On an address where
  no quadratic term was extracted, the fine and predictor energies are
  nonnegative; only the one-sided lower bound is needed there. On selected
  addresses, the local Taylor estimates bound the difference from the
  extracted quadratic energy in absolute value. The protected <math|304*B>
  fluctuation region and the <math|300*B> mask conditions allow those local
  estimates to be used throughout the <math|200*B\<setminus\>2*B> annulus.
  Multiplying <math|r> by the fixed-density ball count preserves its upper
  exponent, so <math|E> also has upper exponent <math|-3/2>.

  <subsubsection|The packet density and its normalization>

  Let <math|A> denote the packet. Its logarithmic density has the form

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:packet-log-density>\<Phi\><rsub|A>=-\<beta\>*<around|(|Q<rsub|A>+Q<rsub|A><rsup|<math-up|mean>>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>V<rsub|200><around|(|A|)>>c<rsub|i>+<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>V<rsub|304><around|(|A|)>>j<rsub|i>-N<rsub|A>*log
    m<rsub|\<beta\>>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|Q<rsub|A><rsup|<math-up|mean>>\<geqslant\>0> is the extracted
  mean-square contribution; <math|c<rsub|i>> are the local Gaussian
  normalization corrections; <math|j<rsub|i>> are the protected chart
  corrections; and <math|N<rsub|A>> counts the free chart bonds within
  distance <math|10*B> of the packet. The bond sets and this count satisfy,
  with constants fixed at <math|L>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||V<rsub|200><around|(|A|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|200>*d<rsup|4>*n,<space|2em><around|\||V<rsub|304><around|(|A|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|304>*d<rsup|4>*n,<space|2em>N<rsub|A>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|10>*d<rsup|4>*n.
  </equation*>

  Cover each seed neighbourhood by a lattice ball. There are
  <math|L<rsup|4>#<around|{|<text|axes>|}>> fine bonds per coarse site, so
  summing these bounds over seeds gives the claimed counts. Overlaps reduce
  the count.

  The one-bond Gaussian normalization is

  <\equation*>
    m<rsub|\<beta\>>=c<rsub|<math-up|chart>>*<around|(|<sqrt|2*\<pi\>>*<space|0.17em>\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>|)><rsup|q>,<space|2em>c<rsub|<math-up|chart>>\<gtr\>0,
  </equation*>

  where <math|q> is the colour dimension. Thus

  <\equation*>
    -log m<rsub|\<beta\>>=-log c<rsub|<math-up|chart>>-<frac|q|2>*log
    <around|(|2*\<pi\>|)>+<frac|q|2>*log \<beta\>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|norm>>*d
  </equation*>

  for <math|0\<less\>\<beta\>\<leqslant\>2*h> and sufficiently large
  <math|x>. The bound holds even when <math|\<beta\>> is very small, since
  only an upper bound on <math|log \<beta\>> is used. Suppose also
  <math|<around|\||c<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|cov>>>,
  <math|<around|\||j<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>1>, and that the
  likelihood-ticket product is bounded by <math|exp
  <around|(|C<rsub|<math-up|tag>>*d<rsup|4>*n|)>>. These are uniform local
  analytic bounds. The other packet masks, enforcement factors and
  contractions take values in <math|<around|[|0,1|]>>. Dropping the
  nonpositive mean-square term in <eqref|eq:packet-log-density>, and using
  <math|d\<geqslant\>1>, gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:packet-normalization>\<Phi\><rsub|A>+C<rsub|<math-up|tag>>*d<rsup|4>*n\<leqslant\>-\<beta\>*Q<rsub|A>+K*d<rsup|5>*n
  </equation>

  for a fixed <math|K\<geqslant\>0>.

  Here the ticket bound follows directly from the normalized tag
  probabilities. Let <math|N<rsub|f>> and <math|N<rsub|p>> be the numbers of
  frame and predictor tags in the fixed patch. A site likelihood is
  <math|N<rsub|f>> times its tag probability, and a bond likelihood is
  <math|N<rsub|p>> times its tag probability. The probabilities are
  nonnegative and at most one: in the minimizing frame branch the frame is
  the single selected minimizer and the tag probabilities are uniform on all
  frame tags; in the fallback branch they are uniform on the set of eligible
  tags and zero outside it; and the predictor weights are nonnegative with
  sum one. Thus each likelihood is at most
  <math|N<rsub|f>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|N<rsub|f>>> or
  <math|N<rsub|p>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|N<rsub|p>>>, respectively. The
  radius-<math|r> seed neighbourhood contains at most <math|n*b<rsub|r+1>>
  sites, and the bonds incident to it number at most
  <math|n*b<rsub|r+2>#<around|{|<math-up|axes>|}>>. Multiplying one factor at
  each such physical site and bond gives

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|tickets><rsub|r>\<leqslant\>exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|[|n*<around|{|b<rsub|r+1>*N<rsub|f>+b<rsub|r+2>#<around|{|<math-up|axes>|}>*N<rsub|p>|}>|]>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|r=300*B> this is the asserted <math|exp
  <around|(|C<rsub|<math-up|tag>>*d<rsup|4>*n|)>> bound. A tag used by
  several local tests is still charged only once in this product. There is no
  independent tag resampling at each use.

  A packet is set to zero unless its seed support is contained in the sector
  seed support and its own flag set agrees with the actual coarse flags
  throughout its <math|300*B> neighbourhood. On a healthy packet
  neighbourhood, where there are no actual coarse flags, this flag test is
  equivalent to the packet's flag component being empty. The latter is the
  eligibility rule of the locally smooth stock extension
  <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|A><rsup|<math-up|st>>> used in the local jet argument.
  The extension differs from <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|A>> in two respects: its
  eligibility test is the fixed one just described, and its action exponent
  is built from the unmasked rows rather than the clipped rows of
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|A>>. The funded room and manifest-sector conditions and
  a healthy neighbourhood through radius at least <math|500*B> are used for
  both identifications below, and the second of them is also what gives the
  value bound of the extension itself. Indeed, every packet flag lies in its
  own support and hence in its <math|300*B> neighbourhood, so the flag
  agreement forces the packet flag set to be empty when that neighbourhood is
  healthy; the converse follows because both flag sets then vanish there. For
  the remaining factors, the <math|500*B> health region contains the
  <math|200*B> energy addresses together with their clean read
  neighbourhoods, as well as the <math|300*B> and <math|304*B> mask regions.
  The clean-hull agreement of the clipped and unmasked rows and the
  manifest-sector mean identity therefore identify the two exponents; the
  held fine field, likelihoods, masks and normalization factors are the same
  in both products. Neither rule requires the sector store's flag component
  to be globally empty. When the packet value is nonzero, every retained mask
  is nonzero. The mask support conditions then give the local hypotheses of
  <eqref|eq:packet-assigned-energy>, including the control of the hot
  fluctuation bonds. Therefore

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<cal-P\><rsub|A>|\|>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around|{|\<Phi\><rsub|A>+C<rsub|<math-up|tag>>*d<rsup|4>*n|}>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around|{|<around|(|<around|(|K*d<rsup|5>+\<beta\>*E|)>-\<beta\>*\<mu\>*\<tau\><rsup|2>|)>*n|}>.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|\<beta\>\<leqslant\>2*h> and <math|E> has upper exponent
  <math|-3/2>, the product <math|\<beta\>*E> tends to zero and is eventually
  at most <math|1\<leqslant\>d<rsup|5>>. We have proved

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:packet-value-price><around|\||\<cal-P\><rsub|A>|\|>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around|{|<around|(|C*d<rsup|5>-\<beta\>*\<mu\>*\<tau\><rsup|2>|)>*n|}>,<space|2em>C=K+1.
  </equation>

  The estimate also holds for packets which vanish, since its right-hand side
  is positive. This argument retains the Gaussian normalization
  <math|m<rsub|\<beta\>>>; it does not replace the original density by an
  unnormalized free density.

  <subsubsection|Derivatives at the edge of a mask>

  For a <math|C<rsup|j>> map <math|F> defined near a point <math|y> in a
  finite-dimensional real normed space, write

  <\equation*>
    F\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around|(|U,v;y|)><space|1em><text|if><space|1em><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|k>*F<around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>U*v<rsup|k>*<space|1em><around|(|0\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>j|)>,<space|2em>U,v\<geqslant\>0.
  </equation*>

  The zero-order bound is included. The product rule implies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:packet-derivative-product>F\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around|(|U,v;y|)>,<space|1em>G\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around|(|V,w;y|)>\<Longrightarrow\>F*G\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around|(|U*V,v+w;y|)>.
  </equation>

  Indeed the norm of the <math|k>th derivative is bounded by
  <math|U*V*<big|sum><rsub|r=0><rsup|k><binom|k|r>v<rsup|r>*w<rsup|k-r>=U*V*<around|(|v+w|)><rsup|k>>.
  Repeated application gives a product bound with multiplied amplitudes and
  added derivative scales.

  For the packet's stock-extension variation, the underlying fine
  configuration in its mask and likelihood factors is held fixed; the coarse
  field and its compensating fluctuation vary coherently. This matters at the
  boundary of a cutoff. If any of the retained mask products is identically
  zero on a neighbourhood of <math|y>, the extension and all its derivatives
  through order <math|j> vanish at <math|y>. In the complementary case, every
  relevant individual mask has points of nonzero value arbitrarily close to
  <math|y>. Continuity of its argument therefore places that argument in the
  <em|closed> support of the mask at <math|y>. For example, a cutoff which
  vanishes when <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>>\<gtr\>2*a*\<tau\>> implies
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u<around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*a*\<tau\>> in
  this case. The same argument gives the closed fine and predictor threshold
  inequalities. It does not claim that the mask value at <math|y> is nonzero.
  The scalar estimate <eqref|eq:packet-normalization> and the core estimate
  must hold on these closed conditions; this is the closed support version of
  the local input used below.

  For the local jet estimate, use the locally smooth stock extension
  <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|A><rsup|<math-up|st>>> described above; its
  seed-eligibility indicator is fixed as the coarse field varies. The
  estimate is made at a base field <math|y> at which the funded-room and
  manifest-sector conditions hold and the packet's <math|500*B> neighbourhood
  is healthy. These hypotheses are used twice. They identify the unmasked
  rows in the exponent of <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|A><rsup|<math-up|st>>> with
  the clipped rows of <math|\<Phi\><rsub|A>>, which is how the energy bound
  <eqref|eq:packet-assigned-energy> gives the value bound of the extension's
  exponent (the last line of <eqref|eq:packet-local-jet-inputs> below); and
  they give <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|A><rsup|<math-up|st>><around|(|y|)>=\<cal-P\><rsub|A><around|(|y|)>>
  in value. No derivative of the physical packet's flag test is taken.
  Separate the extension as <math|e<rsup|\<phi\><around|(|v|)>>*H<around|(|v|)>>,
  apart from its fixed indicator. The local differentiated estimates give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:packet-local-jet-inputs>

    <\eqsplit>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<phi\>-\<phi\><around|(|y|)>>|<cell|\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around|(|b<around|(|x|)>*n,1;y|)>,>>|<row|<cell|H>|<cell|\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around|(|e<rsup|\<ell\>*n>,c<around|(|x|)>*n;y|)>,>>|<row|<cell|e<rsup|\<phi\><around|(|y|)>+\<ell\>*n>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<around|(|C*d<rsup|5>-g|)>*n>,<space|2em>g=\<beta\>*\<mu\>*\<tau\><rsup|2>,>>>>
    </eqsplit>
  </equation>

  where <math|b,c\<geqslant\>0>, <math|b> has upper exponent <math|1> and
  <math|c> upper exponent zero. Here <math|\<ell\>> is the ticket expense per
  seed (not the support radius parameter of the next subsection), and in the
  next paragraph <math|v> denotes the coarse displacement scale, not the
  derivative-scale slot of <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|j>>. The first bound is
  <eqref|eq:action-packet-log-jets>. To check the bound for <math|H>, first
  consider a coarse noise cutoff. Its argument is the squared chord of
  <math|V<rsub|e>*Q<around|(|U|)><rsub|e><rsup|-1>> divided by
  <math|<around|(|W*\<tau\>|)><rsup|2>>, with <math|W\<gtr\>0> fixed and
  <math|U> held fixed. If the chord is strictly larger than
  <math|2*W*\<tau\>>, the inner cutoff is locally zero. Otherwise the
  normalized matrix displacement has bounded value and derivatives of upper
  exponent zero: its positive derivatives are bounded using <math|v/\<tau\>>,
  where <math|v> has upper exponent <math|-1/2> and <math|\<tau\><rsup|-1>>
  has upper exponent <math|1/2>. Composition with the fixed smooth cutoff on
  <math|<around|[|0,4|]>> gives derivative scale of upper exponent zero. Its
  value is in <math|<around|[|0,1|]>>. The complementary cutoff
  <math|1-\<chi\>> has the same property: its value is still at most one, and
  its positive derivatives differ only by a sign. At a boundary point use the
  closed chord inequality, as above. A fixed enlargement of the derivative
  scale absorbs the finite-order composition constants while keeping
  amplitude one.

  The protected bond cutoffs have the corresponding bound by
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:haar-chart> and the compensated increment
  estimate. Fine masks, contractions and likelihoods evaluated at the held
  fine configuration are constants. The first two have absolute value at most
  one; the product of likelihoods has the stated bound
  <math|e<rsup|\<ell\>*n>>. Apply <eqref|eq:packet-derivative-product> to all
  these factors. There are at most <math|n*b<rsub|300*B+2>#<around|{|<math-up|axes>|}>>
  coarse noise cutoffs and <math|n*b<rsub|304*B+2>*\<rho\><rsub|L>> protected
  bond cutoffs, where <math|\<rho\><rsub|L>> is the fine-bond density. Their
  derivative scales add, giving <math|c<around|(|x|)>*n> with upper exponent
  zero, as asserted. The last inequality in
  <eqref|eq:packet-local-jet-inputs> is the closed support version just
  explained, applied to the clipped exponent, which equals the extension's
  exponent at <math|y> under the healthy-neighbourhood, funded-room and
  manifest-sector hypotheses. Thus the differentiated local action,
  covariance and chart bounds proved in the preceding sections supply the
  required estimates.

  We give the remaining derivative calculation. The repeated chain rule for
  <math|e<rsup|\<phi\>>> has one term for each partition <math|\<pi\>> of the
  <math|k> derivative arguments:

  <\equation*>
    D<rsup|k><around|(|e<rsup|\<phi\>>|)><around|(|y|)><around|[|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|k>|]>=e<rsup|\<phi\><around|(|y|)>>*<big|sum><rsub|\<pi\>><big|prod><rsub|C\<in\>\<pi\>>D<rsup|<around|\||C|\|>>*\<phi\><around|(|y|)><around|[|v<rsub|i>:i\<in\>C|]>.
  </equation*>

  This identity follows by induction: differentiation either adds the new
  argument as a singleton block or inserts it into one existing block. Each
  partition appears exactly once. There are at most <math|k>! partitions:
  send each partition to the permutation whose cycles list each block in
  increasing order. This map is injective. For unit arguments,
  <eqref|eq:packet-local-jet-inputs> therefore gives, for
  <math|1\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>j>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|k><around|(|e<rsup|\<phi\>>|)><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<phi\><around|(|y|)>>*k!<around|(|1+b*n|)><rsup|k>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<phi\><around|(|y|)>>*<around|{|J<rsub|j>*<around|(|1+b*n|)>|}><rsup|k>,<space|2em>J<rsub|j>=max
    <around|{|1,j!|}>.
  </equation*>

  The zero-order bound is equality. Using
  <eqref|eq:packet-derivative-product> and <math|n\<geqslant\>1>, the stock
  extension <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|A><rsup|<math-up|st>>> belongs to

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around*|(|e<rsup|<around|(|C*d<rsup|5>-g|)>*n>,a<around|(|x|)>*n;y|)>,<space|2em>a<around|(|x|)>=J<rsub|j>*<around|(|1+b<around|(|x|)>|)>+c<around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  The function <math|a> has upper exponent <math|1>, so
  <math|a<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>h<rsup|2>> eventually. As
  <math|d\<geqslant\>1> and <math|d\<geqslant\>log h> eventually, for
  <math|n\<geqslant\>1>,

  <\equation*>
    a<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|2*d<rsup|5>*n>,<space|2em>n\<leqslant\>e<rsup|d<rsup|5>*n>,<space|2em><around|(|a<around|(|x|)>*n|)><rsup|j>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|3*j*d<rsup|5>*n>.
  </equation*>

  Enlarge <math|a> to at least one if necessary. Every derivative of order at
  most <math|j> is now bounded by the same number:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:packet-jet-price><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|k>*\<cal-P\><rsub|A><rsup|<math-up|st>><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around|{|<around|(|<around|(|C+3*j|)>*d<rsup|5>-g|)>*n|}>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>j.
  </equation>

  At <math|k=0> this is also a bound for <math|<around|\||\<cal-P\><rsub|A><around|(|y|)>|\|>>,
  since the two agree in value at <math|y>. Thus finite differentiation
  changes the fixed coefficient of the logarithmic expense; it does not
  consume a fixed fraction of the energy gain. This argument applies, with
  the same constants, at every point of the variation at which the
  funded-room, manifest-sector and <math|500*B> healthy-neighbourhood
  hypotheses hold, the estimate being re-based at that point. It does not
  apply at other points of the variation: health is a closed condition, and
  manifestness is not preserved when only the coarse field is varied with the
  seeds held fixed.

  <subsubsection|Absorbing the expense and the packet count>

  Here is the quantitative comparison used to turn these estimates into small
  connected-support prices. Suppose <math|\<mu\>\<gtr\>0> and
  <math|\<beta\>\<geqslant\>u*h> for some fixed <math|u\<gtr\>0>. Define

  <\equation*>
    M=\<mu\>*u*P<rsup|2>,<space|2em>q<rsub|\<ast\>>=e<rsup|-M/2>.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|h*\<tau\><rsup|2>=P<rsup|2>>, the gain satisfies
  <math|g=\<beta\>*\<mu\>*\<tau\><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>M>. For any fixed
  <math|D\<geqslant\>0>, integer <math|r\<geqslant\>0> and <math|z\<less\>2>,
  the monitor comparison <eqref|eq:warning-monitor-pays> gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:packet-expense-absorption>D*d<rsup|r>*P<rsup|z>\<leqslant\>M/16<space|1em><text|eventually>.
  </equation>

  Indeed apply that comparison with exponent <math|2-z> and coefficient
  <math|16*D/<around|(|\<mu\>*u|)>>, and multiply by
  <math|\<mu\>*u*P<rsup|z>/16>.

  Fix a packet support radius parameter <math|\<ell\>> and a support exponent
  <math|\<alpha\>\<geqslant\>0>. Let <math|H<rsub|B>> and <math|D<rsub|B>> be
  the root and adjacency counts from Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mixed-activity-prices>,
  and let <math|c<rsub|\<ell\>>> be its cover cost per seed. All are
  polynomial in <math|B> except the fixed <math|c<rsub|\<ell\>>>.
  Consequently, for sufficiently large <math|x>,

  <\equation*>
    H<rsub|B>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|M/16>,<space|2em>D<rsub|B><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|M/16>,<space|2em>\<alpha\>*c<rsub|\<ell\>>\<leqslant\>M/16,<space|2em>2\<leqslant\>M/16.
  </equation*>

  For example, bound both counts by fixed powers of <math|d>, use
  <eqref|eq:packet-expense-absorption>, and then
  <math|t\<leqslant\>e<rsup|t>>. Consider a nonnegative packet price
  <math|C<rsub|0>*e<rsup|<around|(|s-g|)>*n>> satisfying

  <\equation*>
    s\<leqslant\>D*d<rsup|r>*P<rsup|z>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>C<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|D*d<rsup|r>*P<rsup|z>>.
  </equation*>

  Then <math|C<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|M/16>> and the counting ratio obeys

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|B><rsup|2>*e<rsup|s-g+\<alpha\>*c<rsub|\<ell\>>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|M/16+M/16-M+M/16>\<leqslant\>q<rsub|\<ast\>>.
  </equation*>

  In particular <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>1/2>. The packet counting
  estimate already proved therefore bounds the site sum over packets with at
  least <math|n<rsub|0>> seeds by

  <\equation*>
    H<rsub|B>*C<rsub|0>*<frac|q<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|max
    <around|(|1,n<rsub|0>|)>>|1-q<rsub|\<ast\>>>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around|{|-M*max <around|(|1,n<rsub|0>|)>/4|}>.
  </equation*>

  To verify the last inequality, put <math|r<rsub|0>=max
  <around|(|1,n<rsub|0>|)>>. Since <math|<around|(|1-q<rsub|\<ast\>>|)><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>2\<leqslant\>e<rsup|M/16>>,
  its logarithm is bounded above by <math|3*M/16-M*r<rsub|0>/2\<leqslant\>-M*r<rsub|0>/4>.
  This applies to <eqref|eq:packet-value-price> and
  <eqref|eq:packet-jet-price> with <math|z=0> and <math|r=5>. Hence once the
  stated local energy, chart and derivative inputs have been established,
  their full packet and support sums are small uniformly in the period. The
  scalar comparison selects a threshold after all fixed constants; it does
  not by itself construct the schedule that reaches that threshold at each
  renormalization step.

  <subsection|Differentiating the projected Gaussian
  integrals><label|sec:gaussian-differentiation>

  The Gaussian used in a local activity varies with the coarse field.
  Differentiating its expectation therefore involves both the activity and
  the Gaussian density. We first reduce to the coordinates read by that
  activity, and then use the compensated change of variables to put all
  nearby integrals on one reference probability space.

  <subsubsection|Projection and the relative density>

  Let <math|C> be the positive covariance of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-covariance>, in counting colour
  coordinates. For a finite coordinate set <math|I>, let <math|C<rsub|I>>
  denote its principal submatrix. Projection of the centered Gaussian with
  covariance <math|C> is the centered Gaussian with covariance
  <math|C<rsub|I>>. Indeed its characteristic function at
  <math|\<xi\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|I>> is <math|exp
  <around|(|-<around|\<langle\>|\<xi\>,C<rsub|I>*\<xi\>|\<rangle\>>/2|)>>, by
  extending <math|\<xi\>> by zero. A function depending only on these
  coordinates can therefore be integrated against this marginal; the other
  coordinates need not be independent. The marginal density is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:gaussian-local-density>p<rsub|C><around|(|z|)>=<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-N/2>*exp
    <around|{|-<tfrac|1|2>log det C-<tfrac|1|2>z<rsup|T>*C<rsup|-1>*z|}>,<space|2em>N=<around|\||I|\|>,
  </equation>

  where here and below <math|C> denotes the projected covariance when there
  is no ambiguity. Positivity of a principal submatrix follows by extending a
  nonzero vector by zero. Formula <eqref|eq:gaussian-local-density> follows
  by diagonalizing <math|C> and changing variables in the product of <math|N>
  one-dimensional standard Gaussian densities. It is also valid in dimension
  zero, with determinant and density equal to one.

  Fix a base coarse parameter <math|y<rsub|0>> and write
  <math|C<rsub|0>=C<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>>. For fixed frozen Haar variables,
  the compensated transformation induces a diffeomorphism <math|T<rsub|y>> of
  the <math|I> coordinates with <math|T<rsub|y<rsub|0>>*z=z> and positive
  Jacobian <math|J<rsub|y><around|(|z|)>>. The exact expectation identity is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:gaussian-relative-identity><big|int>F<rsub|y><around|(|w|)>*p<rsub|C<around|(|y|)>><around|(|w|)>*<space|0.17em>d*w=<big|int>J<rsub|y><around|(|z|)><frac|p<rsub|C<around|(|y|)>>*<around|(|T<rsub|y>*z|)>|p<rsub|C<rsub|0>><around|(|z|)>>*F<rsub|y>*<around|(|T<rsub|y>*z|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|C<rsub|0>><around|(|z|)>.
  </equation>

  The positive reference density cancels, leaving the ordinary
  change-of-variables formula on the right. The set <math|I> includes all
  colours of every bond in the activity's enlarged read support. The
  transformation acts bond by bond, and both it and its Jacobian on <math|I>
  depend only on the old coordinates in <math|I>. Locality therefore
  identifies the transformed marginal integrand with the original activity
  evaluated on the transformed full field. This explains why the determinant
  in this formula is <math|det C<rsub|I>>, rather than the determinant of the
  covariance on the full torus.

  <subsubsection|Polynomial rank costs at the base point>

  There is a useful finite-order improvement over estimating a full
  determinant by its permutation expansion. If
  <math|A<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>=0> is an <math|N\<times\>N> matrix,
  multilinearity in the rows gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:gaussian-small-minors>det <around|(|1+A<around|(|y|)>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|J\<subseteq\><around|{|1,\<ldots\>,N|}>>det
    A<rsub|J><around|(|y|)>.
  </equation>

  To verify the formula, in each row choose either the row of <math|A> or the
  corresponding identity row. The identity rows force their columns, leaving
  precisely the principal minor on the chosen set. Every term of <math|det
  A<rsub|J>> is a product of <math|<around|\||J|\|>> functions vanishing at
  <math|y<rsub|0>>. Its derivatives of order less than
  <math|<around|\||J|\|>> vanish, because at least one factor receives no
  derivative. Thus derivatives through order <math|j> in
  <eqref|eq:gaussian-small-minors> use only
  <math|<around|\||J|\|>\<leqslant\>j>.

  Suppose every entry belongs to <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around|(|v,1;y<rsub|0>|)>>,
  with <math|v\<geqslant\>0>. A minor of size <math|r\<leqslant\>j> has at
  most <math|r>! permutation terms. The product rule bounds each term by
  amplitude <math|<around|(|1+v|)><rsup|j>> and derivative scale <math|j>.
  There are at most <math|<around|(|j+1|)>*<around|(|1+N|)><rsup|j>> subsets
  of size at most <math|j>, since <math|<binom|N|r>\<leqslant\>N<rsup|r>>.
  Consequently

  <\equation*>
    det <around|(|1+A|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around*|(|<around|(|j+1|)>*<around|(|1+N|)><rsup|j>*j!<around|(|1+v|)><rsup|j>,j;y<rsub|0>|)>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|j=0> this just states the value one.

  The relative density itself needs one more bookkeeping step, because the
  quadratic form must be priced without an operator norm that has not been
  estimated. We use the row-sum norm

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|K|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>=max<rsub|i>
    <big|sum><rsub|k><around|\||K<rsub|i*k>|\|>
  </equation*>

  of an <math|N\<times\>N> matrix, which is the operator norm on
  <math|<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|N>,|\<\|\|\>>\<cdot\><around|\<\|\|\>|<rsub|\<infty\>>|)>>
  and is submultiplicative. It is bounded by every exponentially weighted row
  price of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-covariance>, since the
  weights are at least one. For vectors <math|x,x<rprime|'>> and a matrix
  <math|K>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:gaussian-l1-linf><around|\||x<rsup|T>*K*x<rprime|'>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i><around|\||x<rsub|i>|\|>*<big|sum><rsub|k><around|\||K<rsub|i*k>|\|><around|\||x<rprime|'><rsub|k>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1><around|\<\|\|\>|K|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rprime|'>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>,<space|2em><around|\||<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>K|\|>\<leqslant\>N<around|\<\|\|\>|K|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>.
  </equation>

  For a multilinear derivative <math|D<rsup|b>*K<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>> with
  matrix values, we write <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|b>*K<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>c>
  if

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|b>*K<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)><around|[|h<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,h<rsub|b>|]>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>c<around|\<\|\|\>|h<rsub|1>|\<\|\|\>>*\<cdots\><around|\<\|\|\>|h<rsub|b>|\<\|\|\>>
  </equation*>

  for all directions <math|h<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,h<rsub|b>>; likewise for
  vector values and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lem:gaussian-base-density>Let <math|N=<around|\||I|\|>\<geqslant\>1>,
    <math|j\<geqslant\>0>, and let <math|C<around|(|y|)>> be a
    <math|C<rsup|j>> family of symmetric positive definite <math|N\<times\>N>
    matrices near <math|y<rsub|0>>, with <math|C<rsub|0>=C<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>>.
    Let <math|w<around|(|y|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|N>> be <math|C<rsup|j>>
    near <math|y<rsub|0>>, with <math|w<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>=z>. Suppose
    that <math|P,Q,v,W\<geqslant\>0> satisfy

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:gaussian-base-inputs><around|\<\|\|\>|C<rsub|0><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>P,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|b>*C<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>Q*v<rsup|b>,<space|2em><around|\||D<rsup|r>*w<rsub|i><around|(|y<rsub|0>|)><around|[|h<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,h<rsub|r>|]>|\|>\<leqslant\>W<around|\<\|\|\>|h<rsub|1>|\<\|\|\>>*\<cdots\><around|\<\|\|\>|h<rsub|r>|\<\|\|\>>
    </equation>

    for <math|1\<leqslant\>b\<leqslant\>j>,
    <math|0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>j> and every <math|i\<in\>I>. Put
    <math|\<kappa\>=max <around|(|1,P|)>*max <around|(|1,Q|)>>. Then the
    relative density

    <\equation*>
      R<around|(|y|)>=<frac|p<rsub|C<around|(|y|)>><around|(|w<around|(|y|)>|)>|p<rsub|C<rsub|0>><around|(|z|)>>
    </equation*>

    satisfies

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:gaussian-base-density>R\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around*|(|1,<space|0.17em>K<rsub|j>*<space|0.17em>N*<around|(|1+W|)><rsup|2>*\<kappa\><rsup|3>*max
      <around|(|1,v|)>;<space|0.17em>y<rsub|0>|)>,
    </equation>

    where <math|K<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1> depends only on <math|j>. For
    <math|N=0> the ratio is identically one.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    By <eqref|eq:gaussian-local-density>, <math|R=e<rsup|L>> with

    <\equation*>
      L<around|(|y|)>=-<tfrac|1|2><around|{|log det C<around|(|y|)>-log det
      C<rsub|0>|}>-<tfrac|1|2><around|{|w<around|(|y|)><rsup|T>*\<Gamma\><around|(|y|)>*w<around|(|y|)>-z<rsup|T>*\<Gamma\><around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>*z|}>,<space|2em>\<Gamma\>=C<rsup|-1>,
    </equation*>

    and <math|L<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>=0>.

    <em|The case <math|j=0>.> Here <eqref|eq:gaussian-base-density> asks only
    for <math|<around|\||R<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>1>, whatever
    the scale, and indeed <math|R<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>=e<rsup|L<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>>=1>.
    So the lemma holds with <math|K<rsub|0>=1>, and no derivative of <math|C>
    or <math|w> enters. From now on <math|j\<geqslant\>1>. Throughout,
    <math|c<rsub|r>,c<rprime|'><rsub|r>,c<rprime|''><rsub|r>> denote
    constants depending only on <math|r>, and
    <math|1\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>j>.

    <em|Inverse.> Differentiating <math|\<Gamma\>*C=1> gives
    <math|D<rsub|h>*\<Gamma\>=-\<Gamma\>*<around|(|D<rsub|h>*C|)>*\<Gamma\>>.
    Induction on <math|r> shows that

    <\equation*>
      D<rsup|r>*\<Gamma\><around|(|y<rsub|0>|)><around|[|h<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,h<rsub|r>|]>
    </equation*>

    is a sum of at most <math|c<rsub|r>> terms of the form

    <\equation*>
      \<pm\>\<Gamma\><rsub|0>*<around|(|D<rsup|b<rsub|1>>*C|)>*\<Gamma\><rsub|0>*<around|(|D<rsup|b<rsub|2>>*C|)>*\<cdots\>*<around|(|D<rsup|b<rsub|m>>*C|)>*\<Gamma\><rsub|0>,<space|2em>b<rsub|1>+\<cdots\>+b<rsub|m>=r,b<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1,
    </equation*>

    in which the directions <math|h<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,h<rsub|r>> are
    distributed among the derivatives of <math|C>, all evaluated at
    <math|y<rsub|0>>. (The induction step applies <math|D<rsub|h>> either to
    one factor <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|0>>, producing
    <math|-\<Gamma\><rsub|0>*<around|(|D<rsub|h>*C|)>*\<Gamma\><rsub|0>>, or
    to one factor <math|D<rsup|b>*C>, raising <math|b> by one; this bounds
    <math|c<rsub|r+1>> by <math|<around|(|2*r+1|)>*c<rsub|r>>.) By
    submultiplicativity and <eqref|eq:gaussian-base-inputs>, each term has
    row-sum norm at most <math|P<rsup|m+1>*Q<rsup|m>*v<rsup|r>\<leqslant\>\<kappa\><rsup|r+1>*v<rsup|r>>,
    since <math|m\<leqslant\>r>. Hence <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r>*\<Gamma\><around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|r>*\<kappa\><rsup|r+1>*v<rsup|r>>,
    and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Gamma\><around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>\<kappa\>>.

    <em|Determinant.> Near <math|y<rsub|0>>, <math|D<rsub|h>*log det
    C=<math-up|tr><around|(|\<Gamma\>*<space|0.17em>D<rsub|h>*C|)>>.
    Differentiating this <math|r-1> more times by Leibniz's rule gives at
    most <math|2<rsup|r-1>> terms <math|<math-up|tr><around*|(|D<rsup|a>*\<Gamma\>\<cdot\>D<rsup|b+1>*C|)>>
    with <math|a+b=r-1>. By <eqref|eq:gaussian-l1-linf> and the inverse
    bound, each is at most <math|N\<cdot\>c<rsub|a>*\<kappa\><rsup|a+1>*v<rsup|a>\<cdot\>Q*v<rsup|b+1>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|a>*N*\<kappa\><rsup|r+1>*v<rsup|r>>.
    Thus

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||D<rsup|r>*log det C<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>c<rprime|'><rsub|r>*N*\<kappa\><rsup|r+1>*v<rsup|r>.
    </equation*>

    <em|Quadratic form.> Leibniz's rule for the trilinear expression
    <math|w<rsup|T>*\<Gamma\>*w> writes <math|D<rsup|r>*<around|(|w<rsup|T>*\<Gamma\>*w|)><around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>>
    as a sum of at most <math|3<rsup|r>> terms
    <math|<around|(|D<rsup|a>*w|)><rsup|T>*<around|(|D<rsup|b>*\<Gamma\>|)>*<around|(|D<rsup|c>*w|)>>
    with <math|a+b+c=r>, all at <math|y<rsub|0>>. By
    <eqref|eq:gaussian-base-inputs>, <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|a>*w<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>W>
    and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|a>*w<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>N*W>,
    including <math|a=0>, where <math|D<rsup|0>*w<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>=z>.
    Now <eqref|eq:gaussian-l1-linf> and the inverse bound give, for each
    term, the bound <math|N*W\<cdot\>c<rsub|b>*\<kappa\><rsup|b+1>*max
    <around|(|1,v|)><rsup|b>\<cdot\>W>. Hence

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||D<rsup|r>*<around|(|w<rsup|T>*\<Gamma\>*w|)><around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>c<rprime|''><rsub|r>*N*W<rsup|2>*\<kappa\><rsup|r+1>*max
      <around|(|1,v|)><rsup|r>.
    </equation*>

    The Euclidean norm of <math|z> and the operator norm of <math|\<Gamma\>>
    on <math|\<ell\><rsup|2>> never enter.

    <em|The exponent.> Combining the two bounds, with
    <math|Q\<leqslant\>\<kappa\>> and <math|W<rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+W|)><rsup|2>>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||D<rsup|r>*L<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>K<rprime|'><rsub|r>*N*<around|(|1+W|)><rsup|2>*\<kappa\><rsup|r+1>*max
      <around|(|1,v|)><rsup|r>,<space|2em>K<rprime|'><rsub|r>=<tfrac|1|2><around|(|c<rprime|'><rsub|r>+c<rprime|''><rsub|r>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Let <math|K=max<rsub|1\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>j> max
    <around|(|1,K<rprime|'><rsub|r>|)>> and
    <math|S=K*N*<around|(|1+W|)><rsup|2>*\<kappa\><rsup|3>*max
    <around|(|1,v|)>>. Every factor in <math|S> is at least one, because
    <math|N\<geqslant\>1>. Therefore, for <math|1\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>j>,
    using <math|\<kappa\><rsup|r+1>\<leqslant\>\<kappa\><rsup|3*r>> and
    <math|N*<around|(|1+W|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|{|N*<around|(|1+W|)><rsup|2>|}><rsup|r>>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||D<rsup|r>*L<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>S<rsup|r>.
    </equation*>

    This absorbs the constants <math|K<rprime|'><rsub|r>> into the scale.

    <em|Exponentiation.> By the exponential partition formula used in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:packet-estimates>,
    <math|D<rsup|r>*e<rsup|L><around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>> is the sum over the set
    partitions of the <math|r> directions of
    <math|e<rsup|L<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>>> times the product, over the
    blocks, of the derivatives of <math|L> of the block sizes. Since
    <math|L<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>=0>, each such product is at most
    <math|S<rsup|r>>, and there are at most <math|r>! partitions (the Bell
    number is at most <math|r>!). Hence <math|<around|\||D<rsup|r>*R<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>r!S<rsup|r>\<leqslant\><around|(|j!S|)><rsup|r>>
    for <math|1\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>j>, while
    <math|R<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>=1>. This is
    <eqref|eq:gaussian-base-density> with <math|K<rsub|j>=max
    <around|(|1,j!|)>*K>.
  </proof>

  In the application <math|w=T<rsub|y>*z>, the coordinate bound <math|W> is
  that of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-moment-prices>, of the form
  <math|W\<leqslant\><sqrt|\<beta\>>*<around|{|u<around|(|x|)>+2*m<around|(|x|)>|}>+F<rsub|I><around|(|z|)>>,
  and the quantities <math|P,Q,v> are the weighted row prices of the
  principal covariance, its inverse and their derivatives from
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:local-covariance>
  and<nbsp><reference|sec:normalization-jets>. Since
  <math|1\<leqslant\>F<rsub|I><around|(|z|)>> and
  <math|N\<leqslant\>F<rsub|I><around|(|z|)>>, and
  <math|1+a+F\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1+a|)>*F> for <math|a\<geqslant\>0>,
  <math|F\<geqslant\>1>, the scale in <eqref|eq:gaussian-base-density> is at
  most <math|D<rprime|'><around|(|x|)>*F<rsub|I><around|(|z|)><rsup|3>> with
  <math|D<rprime|'>=4*K<rsub|j>*<around|{|1+<sqrt|\<beta\>>*<around|(|u+2*m|)>|}><rsup|2>*\<kappa\><rsup|3>*max
  <around|(|1,v|)>>, which has upper exponent zero when <math|\<kappa\>> and
  <math|v> do. This is the form <eqref|eq:moment-ratio-jets> used there,
  before the Jacobian factor is multiplied in. This is a finite-rank
  derivative estimate at the base point. It does not yet justify taking
  derivatives under an integral over unbounded <math|z>; that is the purpose
  of the next subsection.

  <subsubsection|An integrable bound on one open neighbourhood>

  We prove the additional assertion needed for that interchange. Let <math|y>
  range over a finite-dimensional real parameter space and let
  <math|C<around|(|y|)>> be a <math|C<rsup|j>> family of symmetric matrices
  near <math|y<rsub|0>>, with <math|C<rsub|0>> positive definite. Suppose

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|0><rsup|-1>\<geqslant\>\<lambda\>*1,<space|2em>\<lambda\>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|z> be an arbitrary sample and let <math|w<rsub|z><around|(|y|)>>
  be a family of <math|C<rsup|j>> maps such that, on one neighbourhood of
  <math|y<rsub|0>>, uniformly in <math|z> and in any auxiliary frozen
  variables,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:gaussian-bounded-displacement>w<rsub|z><around|(|y|)>-z\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around|(|B,1;y|)>,<space|2em>B\<geqslant\>0.
  </equation>

  Then there is a nonnegative function <math|M>, integrable for
  <math|\<gamma\><rsub|C<rsub|0>>>, which bounds every derivative through
  order <math|j> of <math|p<rsub|C<around|(|y|)>><around|(|w<rsub|z><around|(|y|)>|)>/p<rsub|C<rsub|0>><around|(|z|)>>
  on a possibly smaller common neighbourhood.

  By continuity and finite dimension, shrink the neighbourhood so that
  <math|C<around|(|y|)>> is positive definite and
  <math|C<around|(|y|)><rsup|-1>\<geqslant\><around|(|\<lambda\>/2|)>*1>. For
  an explicit verification of the last inequality, make every entry of
  <math|C<around|(|y|)><rsup|-1>-C<rsub|0><rsup|-1>> at most
  <math|\<lambda\>/<around|(|2*<around|(|1+N|)>|)>> in absolute value. Its
  row and column sums are at most <math|\<lambda\>/2>, so its quadratic form
  is bounded in absolute value by <math|<around|(|\<lambda\>/2|)><around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>.
  On a smaller closed neighbourhood, all required derivatives of
  <math|C<rsup|-1>> and <math|log det C> have finite bounds. These bounds may
  depend on <math|N> and the fixed period.

  The derivatives of <math|w<rsub|z>> have amplitude at most
  <math|B+<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>>. Thus the logarithm of the numerator
  has derivatives bounded by <math|K*<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|2>>
  for a fixed <math|K>. Moreover

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2\<\|\|\>w<rsub|z><around|(|y|)>\<\|\|\><rsup|2>+2*B<rsup|2>,
  </equation*>

  and hence

  <\equation*>
    -<tfrac|1|2>log det C<around|(|y|)>-<tfrac|1|2>w<rsub|z><around|(|y|)><rsup|T>*C<around|(|y|)><rsup|-1>*w<rsub|z><around|(|y|)>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|0>+\<lambda\>*B<rsup|2>/4-\<lambda\><around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>/8.
  </equation*>

  The repeated exponential chain rule now bounds every derivative through
  order <math|j> of the relative density by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:gaussian-open-envelope>M<around|(|z|)>=K<rsub|1>*<frac|e<rsup|-\<lambda\><around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>/8>*<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|2*j>|p<rsub|C<rsub|0>><around|(|z|)>>.
  </equation>

  This is integrable against <math|\<gamma\><rsub|C<rsub|0>>>: multiplication
  by its density cancels the denominator, and a polynomial times a strictly
  decaying Gaussian is integrable in finite dimension. Explicitly,
  <math|<around|(|1+t|)><rsup|2*j>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|2*j*t>> for
  <math|t\<geqslant\>0>, and completing the square absorbs <math|2*j*t> into
  half the negative quadratic exponent. The remaining integral factors into
  <math|N> finite one-dimensional Gaussian integrals. The same proof works
  when <math|z> is a measurable function of a larger probability space with
  law <math|\<gamma\><rsub|C<rsub|0>>>.

  <subsubsection|Why the activity satisfies the neighbourhood hypotheses>

  For packet factors we use the locally smooth stock extension of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:packet-estimates>, with its fixed
  seed-eligibility indicator. Under the stated healthy-footprint and sector
  conditions, its value at the base field equals the physical packet. We do
  not differentiate the physical packet's varying flag test.

  For the compensated map of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:compensated-variation>,
  write <math|u<rsub|0>=\<nu\><rsub|0>+\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>*z> and
  <math|F<rsub|M><around|(|u|)>=u+\<psi\>*<around|(|M,u|)>>. Its displacement
  in Gaussian coordinates is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:gaussian-compensated-displacement>T<rsub|y>*z-z=<sqrt|\<beta\>>*<space|0.17em><around|{|\<psi\>*<around|(|M<around|(|y|)>,u<rsub|0>|)>+\<nu\><rsub|0>-\<nu\><around|(|y|)>|}>.
  </equation>

  Here the fixed coupling satisfies <math|\<beta\>\<gtr\>0>. The perturbation
  <math|\<psi\>> and all its parameter derivatives are uniformly bounded for
  all <math|u<rsub|0>>: outside the fixed radius-<math|2*d<rsub|\<ast\>>>
  ball of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:compensated-variation> it is zero,
  and inside that ball compactness applies. The unmasked mean and its
  derivatives are bounded on a common neighbourhood uniformly in the frozen
  Haar variables. Indeed the frozen cut logarithms are bounded smooth
  functions of the coarse variation; the finitely many source sums, row
  matrices and principal inverses have uniform derivative bounds there. All
  principal determinants stay nonzero after one common shrink. Formula
  <eqref|eq:gaussian-compensated-displacement> proves
  <eqref|eq:gaussian-bounded-displacement>. The same compact argument and the
  finite product rule bound the Jacobian derivatives uniformly.

  The remaining activity integrand also has bounded derivatives on one
  neighbourhood, uniformly in the Gaussian sample. The required localization
  is worth spelling out. Compare two fluctuation arrays <math|u,<wide|u|~>>
  such that at each bond either they agree or both have norm greater than
  <math|4*a*\<tau\>>. Every cutoff of width at most <math|2*a*\<tau\>> is
  then equal on the two arrays, and if that cutoff is nonzero the arrays
  agree at that bond. A masked chart logarithm therefore agrees as well. A
  packet's nonzero protected product forces agreement throughout its
  <math|304*B> region, which contains the read stencils of all its
  <math|200*B> action terms. Thus its logarithmic exponent and its complete
  value agree.

  For a linear Gaussian ratio term, the score vanishes on inactive bonds and
  its hot endpoint carries the doubled cutoff. For a quadratic ratio term,
  every nonzero matrix itinerary has two hot endpoints. To check this,
  <math|A*C-1> is zero if either coordinate is inactive, since both <math|A>
  and <math|C> equal the identity on that coordinate. In a product of copies
  of <math|-<around|(|A*C-1|)>> followed by <math|A>, the first nonzero
  factor forces the initial endpoint hot, and the last factor cannot join a
  hot coordinate to an inactive one. Both endpoint cutoffs therefore force
  equality of the coordinates actually used by the quadratic term. This
  argument holds nearby as long as the principal inverses exist; it does not
  require the nearby point to satisfy the original sector inequalities.

  It follows that every fixed activity factor is unchanged when large initial
  fluctuation coordinates are replaced by bounded ones, provided this
  comparison is made through the compensated map and the fine field is held
  fixed. Apply radial truncation at radius <math|3*d<rsub|\<ast\>>> to the
  initial fluctuation coordinates <math|u<rsub|0>=\<nu\><rsub|0>+\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>*z>
  of each bond. These do not depend on the parameter <math|y>, so the lack of
  smoothness of radial truncation in its argument plays no role. The
  truncation leaves the closed radius-<math|3*d<rsub|\<ast\>>> ball
  unchanged, and in particular the radius-<math|2*d<rsub|\<ast\>>> ball,
  which is all that is used. Outside that ball <math|F<rsub|M>> is the
  identity; both the old and truncated coordinates then remain beyond the
  cutoff threshold, since <math|8*a*\<tau\>\<leqslant\>d<rsub|\<ast\>>/2>.
  Inside it, the coordinates agree. The resulting bounded input family has
  uniform parameter derivatives by the compact chart construction. The frozen
  fine factors remain merely bounded measurable functions: their regular
  activities, forced activities, likelihoods and contractions use the
  supplied finite-volume bounds for the chosen labels. No differentiation of
  these frozen factors is required.

  For each fixed finite product of activity factors, finite sums, products
  and smooth compositions on bounded sets now give a common bound on all
  derivatives through order <math|j>. The local action, normalization and
  chart functions used here are the unmasked smooth extensions already
  constructed. Hard selection indicators are fixed. Together with
  <eqref|eq:gaussian-open-envelope> and the Jacobian bound, this gives an
  integrable envelope for the entire right-hand side of
  <eqref|eq:gaussian-relative-identity>, also after integrating the frozen
  variables against their Haar probability law.

  <subsubsection|Interchange and quantitative use>

  Here is the precise final differentiation argument. Suppose
  <math|G*<around|(|y,\<omega\>|)>> is measurable in <math|\<omega\>>,
  <math|C<rsup|j>> in <math|y> on an open neighbourhood, and for each
  <math|r\<leqslant\>j> its <math|r>th derivative is bounded there by an
  integrable function <math|M<rsub|r><around|(|\<omega\>|)>>. Then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:gaussian-differentiate-integral>D<rsup|r>*<big|int>G*<around|(|y,\<omega\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><around|(|\<omega\>|)>=<big|int>D<rsub|y><rsup|r>*G*<around|(|y,\<omega\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><around|(|\<omega\>|)>,<space|2em>r\<leqslant\>j.
  </equation>

  Derivative measurability follows in a fixed finite basis by taking limits
  of difference quotients of measurable functions. Dominated convergence
  first gives continuity of each derivative integral. For the induction step,
  the fundamental theorem of calculus on a short parameter segment writes its
  difference quotient as the integral of the next derivative along that
  segment. The bound <math|M<rsub|r+1>> permits dominated convergence as the
  segment shrinks. Starting at order zero proves the derivative identity and
  its continuity successively through order <math|j>.

  Apply this to the common reference expression above. It proves smoothness
  of each projected activity integral. If a sharper estimate at the base
  point gives <math|G*<around|(|\<cdummy\>,\<omega\>|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around|(|q<around|(|\<omega\>|)>,D;y<rsub|0>|)>>
  and <math|<big|int>q*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\>\<leqslant\>Q>, then
  <eqref|eq:gaussian-differentiate-integral> and the triangle inequality give

  <\equation*>
    <big|int>G*<around|(|\<cdummy\>,\<omega\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><around|(|\<omega\>|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around|(|Q,D;y<rsub|0>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The neighbourhood envelope establishes the validity of differentiation; the
  sharper moment estimate supplies its period-independent price. Those moment
  estimates must be paid separately. In particular the period-dependent
  constants used to justify <eqref|eq:gaussian-differentiate-integral> are
  never used as uniform renormalization bounds.

  <subsection|Uniform moment prices after changing Gaussian
  coordinates><label|sec:gaussian-moment-prices>

  For real centered Gaussian coordinates we use the Wick\UIsserlis pairing
  rule <cite-detail|IsserlisProof|Theorem<nbsp>1>. For singular covariance,
  add independent centered Gaussian noise of covariance <math|\<epsilon\>*I>
  and let <math|\<epsilon\>*\<downarrow\>*0> in the finitely many polynomial
  moments. Repeated coordinates are allowed, and complex linear combinations
  follow by multilinearity.

  We now estimate the polynomial in the Gaussian sample introduced by
  differentiation of the projected density. The covariance may couple every
  coordinate to every other coordinate. Only its uniform diagonal bound is
  needed for this step.

  <subsubsection|A polynomial moment bound without independence>

  Let <math|Z> be a centered Gaussian vector with covariance
  <math|C\<geqslant\>0> and <math|C<rsub|i*i>\<leqslant\>b>. Each coordinate
  has exponential moment

  <\equation*>
    \<bbb-E\>*e<rsup|t*Z<rsub|i>>=e<rsup|C<rsub|i*i>*t<rsup|2>/2>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|b*t<rsup|2>/2>.
  </equation*>

  This follows directly by completing the square in its one-dimensional
  Gaussian law, including the zero-variance case. For an integer
  <math|r\<geqslant\>0>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|1+<around|\||z|\|>|)><rsup|r>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|r<around|\||z|\|>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|r*z>+e<rsup|-r*z>.
  </equation*>

  Hence

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:moment-coordinate>\<bbb-E\>*<around|(|1+<around|\||Z<rsub|i>|\|>|)><rsup|r>\<leqslant\>2*e<rsup|b*r<rsup|2>/2>.
  </equation>

  Let <math|I> be a set of physical bonds, let <math|I<rsub|<math-up|col>>>
  contain all their colour coordinates, and put <math|n=<around|\||I|\|>>,
  <math|N=<around|\||I<rsub|<math-up|col>>|\|>>. Define

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|I><around|(|z|)>=1+n+N+<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I<rsub|<math-up|col>>><around|\||z<rsub|i>|\|>,<space|2em>Y<rsub|I>=1+n+2*N.
  </equation*>

  Then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:moment-face>\<bbb-E\>*F<rsub|I><around|(|Z|)><rsup|r>\<leqslant\>Y<rsub|I><rsup|r>*<around|{|1+2*N*e<rsup|b*r<rsup|2>/2>|}>.
  </equation>

  For nonempty <math|I<rsub|<math-up|col>>> choose a coordinate at which
  <math|<around|\||z<rsub|i>|\|>> is maximal. The definition gives
  <math|F<rsub|I><around|(|z|)>\<leqslant\>Y<rsub|I>*<around|(|1+<around|\||z<rsub|i>|\|>|)>>,
  and therefore

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|I><around|(|z|)><rsup|r>\<leqslant\>Y<rsub|I><rsup|r>*<around*|(|1+<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I<rsub|<math-up|col>>><around|(|1+<around|\||z<rsub|i>|\|>|)><rsup|r>|)>.
  </equation*>

  For an empty coordinate set the same inequality is immediate. Take
  expectations and use <eqref|eq:moment-coordinate>. In particular all these
  moments are integrable. This argument uses one-coordinate marginals and a
  finite sum, without factoring a joint moment.

  For the local covariance, the weighted matrix bounds of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-covariance> supply a fixed <math|b>,
  independent of the period. Suppose an activity has nonnegative support
  price <math|s>, equal to the sum of its covering numbers and nonnegative
  length charges. A cover by radius-<math|B> balls gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||<math-up|supp>|\|>\<leqslant\>s*<space|0.17em>b<rsub|B>,<space|2em><around|\||I|\|>\<leqslant\>s*<space|0.17em>b<rsub|B>*\<rho\><rsub|L>,<space|2em>N=q<around|\||I|\|>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|q> is the colour dimension and <math|\<rho\><rsub|L>> the fixed
  bond density. The first inequality follows by summing the covering balls
  over all factors; overlaps can only decrease the union. The second uses
  that every bond of <math|I> has its anchor in that union. Since
  <math|b<rsub|B>> has upper exponent zero, <eqref|eq:moment-face> implies,
  for every fixed <math|r>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:moment-support>\<bbb-E\>*F<rsub|I><around|(|Z|)><rsup|r>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|r><around|(|x|)>*<around|(|1+s|)><rsup|m<rsub|r>>,
  </equation>

  for an integer <math|m<rsub|r>> and a nonnegative <math|A<rsub|r>> of upper
  exponent zero. Indeed insert the displayed bounds for <math|n,N> into
  <eqref|eq:moment-face> and expand the resulting fixed polynomial.

  <subsubsection|The derivative of the density and Jacobian>

  At the base parameter the compensated transformation is the identity. The
  coordinate derivative bound following from
  <eqref|eq:gaussian-compensated-displacement> is

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r>*<around|(|T<rsub|y>*z|)><rsub|i>\|<rsub|y=y<rsub|0>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><sqrt|\<beta\>>*<around|{|u<around|(|x|)>+2*m<around|(|x|)>|}>+F<rsub|I><around|(|z|)>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>j,
  </equation*>

  where <math|u,m> bound respectively the compensation increment and mean
  derivatives and have upper exponent <math|-1/2>. For the value, use
  <math|<around|\||z<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>F<rsub|I><around|(|z|)>>; for
  positive orders differentiate the compensated displacement. As
  <math|\<beta\>\<leqslant\>2*h>, the first term has upper exponent zero.

  The principal covariance and its inverse inherit the weighted derivative
  bounds proved earlier. The finite-order relative determinant argument of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-differentiation> therefore gives a
  derivative scale polynomial in <math|N>, <math|F<rsub|I><around|(|z|)>> and
  functions of upper exponent zero. The Jacobian is a product of <math|n>
  one-bond factors. Each has value one at the base parameter and derivative
  scale of upper exponent zero, by Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:compensated-variation>.
  Its product has amplitude one and the sum of these scales. Since
  <math|1,n,N,<around|\||z<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>F<rsub|I><around|(|z|)>>,
  every term of the fixed polynomial is bounded by one common power of
  <math|F<rsub|I><around|(|z|)>>. We obtain an integer <math|k> and a
  nonnegative <math|D<around|(|x|)>> of upper exponent zero such that the
  complete density ratio times Jacobian, denoted
  <math|R<rsub|y><around|(|z|)>>, satisfies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:moment-ratio-jets>R<rsub|\<cdot\>><around|(|z|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around|(|1,D<around|(|x|)>*F<rsub|I><around|(|z|)><rsup|k>;y<rsub|0>|)>.
  </equation>

  The exponent <math|k> depends on the derivative order and fixed
  construction data, not on the support size or period.

  <subsubsection|Retaining the warning gain under integration>

  Consider a finite product of local activity factors indexed by
  <math|\<cal-P\>>. Write <math|t<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0> for their pointwise
  prices and <math|s> for their total support price. Suppose their proved
  local derivative estimates, under the coherent variation, give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:moment-activity-premise>\<Phi\><rsub|\<cdot\>><around|(|z|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around*|(|\<b-1\><rsub|\<cal-E\>><around|(|z|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-P\>>t<rsub|i>,<space|0.17em>2*r<around|(|x|)>*s;y<rsub|0>|)>,<space|2em>r\<geqslant\>1,
  </equation>

  where <math|r> has upper exponent zero and <math|\<cal-E\>> is the joint
  warning event for the <math|m> warning factors. The displayed estimate
  implies that all derivatives through order <math|j> vanish outside
  <math|\<cal-E\>>. In this application the stronger local-vanishing property
  holds: an unsatisfied warning factor is zero on a neighbourhood of
  <math|y<rsub|0>>, as in the closed-support argument for packet masks. For
  the other factors, their derivative scales add: each nonzero supported
  factor has covering number at least one, so its unit scale and length scale
  together cost at most twice its assigned support price. This proves the
  factor <math|2*s> in <eqref|eq:moment-activity-premise> from the individual
  bounds. The individual estimates for all species remain necessary inputs to
  this product step.

  Multiply <eqref|eq:moment-activity-premise> by
  <eqref|eq:moment-ratio-jets>. Since <math|F<rsub|I>\<geqslant\>1> and
  <math|1+s\<geqslant\>1>,

  <\equation*>
    1+D*F<rsub|I><rsup|k>+2*r*s\<leqslant\><around|(|1+D+2*r|)>*<around|(|1+s|)>*F<rsub|I><rsup|k>.
  </equation*>

  Converting to derivative scale one therefore bounds all derivatives through
  order <math|j> of the transformed integrand by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:moment-integrand-price>\<b-1\><rsub|\<cal-E\>><around|(|z|)><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-P\>>t<rsub|i>|)>*<around|(|1+D+2*r|)><rsup|j>*<around|(|1+s|)><rsup|j>*F<rsub|I><around|(|z|)><rsup|k*j>.
  </equation>

  Suppose the simultaneous-warning estimate of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-warnings> gives
  <math|\<bbb-P\><around|(|\<cal-E\>|)>\<leqslant\>\<rho\><rsup|2*m>> with
  <math|\<rho\>\<geqslant\>0>. By Cauchy\USchwarz and
  <eqref|eq:moment-support> at order <math|2*k*j>,

  <\equation*>
    \<bbb-E\>*<around*|[|\<b-1\><rsub|\<cal-E\>>*F<rsub|I><rsup|k*j>|]>\<leqslant\>\<bbb-P\><around|(|\<cal-E\>|)><rsup|1/2>*<around|(|\<bbb-E\>*F<rsub|I><rsup|2*k*j>|)><rsup|1/2>\<leqslant\>E<around|(|x|)>*<around|(|1+s|)><rsup|a>*\<rho\><rsup|m>,
  </equation*>

  for a nonnegative upper-exponent-zero function <math|E> and an integer
  <math|a>. For example replace the square root of the moment bound by
  <math|<around|(|1+A<rsub|2*k*j>|)>*<around|(|1+s|)><rsup|m<rsub|2*k*j>>>.
  This also proves integrability of <eqref|eq:moment-integrand-price>.
  Integrating any frozen Haar variables adds no factor, since their total
  mass is one and the estimate is uniform in them.

  Consequently the integrated derivatives obey

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:moment-final-price><around*|\||D<rsup|r>*<big|int>R<rsub|y>*\<Phi\><rsub|y>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><mid|\|><rsub|y=y<rsub|0>>|\|>\<leqslant\>E<around|(|x|)>*<around|(|1+D+2*r<around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|j>*<around|(|1+s|)><rsup|a+j>*\<rho\><rsup|m>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-P\>>t<rsub|i>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>j.
  </equation>

  The derivative interchange is justified by the separate common
  neighbourhood bound of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-differentiation>.
  Absorb one factor <math|\<rho\>> into the price of each warning factor. The
  right-hand side then has exactly the product-price form used by the
  connected support summation, with one fixed polynomial in <math|1+s>. The
  support-price estimate of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mixed-activity-prices>
  allows such a fixed polynomial through its exponential reserve. Thus
  Gaussian differentiation preserves the available warning gain while adding
  only upper-exponent-zero and polynomial support costs. No independence of
  warning events or Gaussian coordinates has been used.

  <subsection|The common differentiated activity
  bound><label|sec:activity-jet-assembly>

  We assemble the estimates on the actual four-part alphabet: connected seed
  packets, forced activities, exponentiated logarithmic activities, and
  warnings. All factors use the same sector, fine representation, tags,
  Gaussian law and compensated variation. Besides the hypotheses of the
  preceding local estimates, retain the deterministic safety and plateau
  inequalities of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-warnings>, whose
  safety conditions are produced in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:warning-safety>.
  Their construction is an incoming requirement at this stage. The argument
  below explains both their derivative use and the common price they yield.

  <subsubsection|Exponentiating the logarithmic activities>

  If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around|(|w,d;y<rsub|0>|)>> with
  <math|0\<leqslant\>w\<leqslant\>1>, then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:activity-exp-jets>e<rsup|f>-1\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around|(|C<rsub|j>*w,d;y<rsub|0>|)>
  </equation>

  for a constant depending only on <math|j>. For the value, the mean value
  theorem gives <math|<around|\||e<rsup|f<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>e*w>.
  For a positive derivative order <math|k\<leqslant\>j>, use the partition
  formula for the exponential. A partition with <math|m\<geqslant\>1> blocks
  contributes at most <math|e*w<rsup|m>*d<rsup|k>\<leqslant\>e*w*d<rsup|k>>.
  There are at most <math|k>! partitions, so <math|C<rsub|j>=e*max
  <around|(|1,j!|)>> suffices. This retains the small amplitude and does not
  require that the parameter variation stay within the small-amplitude bound
  on a whole neighbourhood.

  Fix <math|-1/2\<less\>t\<less\>0>. The inherited logarithmic prices of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:inherited-activity-prices>, the three short
  prices of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:remote-action-jets>, and the
  correction chain prices of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:itinerary-prices>
  have a common rooted bound <math|J<around|(|x|)>> of upper exponent
  <math|t>, after an arbitrarily small strict exponent loss. Their supports
  are nonempty. Since the support exponent and extra-cost weight are
  nonnegative, each individual price is at most <math|J<around|(|x|)>>:
  choose one site in its support and retain that summand in the rooted bound.
  Eventually <math|J<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>1>. Thus
  <eqref|eq:activity-exp-jets> applies to every logarithmic label with the
  same <math|C<rsub|j>> and the same threshold, even when the alphabet is
  countably infinite. Multiplying all its prices by <math|C<rsub|j>>
  preserves their upper exponent.

  The forced family needs no exponentiation. By enlarging a nonnegative
  function <math|r<around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>1> of upper exponent zero, both
  families have jets with derivative scale

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:activity-common-scale>r<around|(|x|)>*<around|{|1+c<rsub|B><around|(|S<rsub|i>|)>+d<rsub|i>|}>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|S<rsub|i>> is the full assigned support and <math|d<rsub|i>> its
  old-cover or path-length charge; for a correction itinerary it is the
  correction order. The short terms have <math|d<rsub|i>=0>. All earlier
  scale bounds are dominated by this expression.

  <subsubsection|Warnings vanish as germs at a reduced threshold>

  Let <math|\<xi\>\<gtr\>0> be the safety margin and use the smaller
  threshold <math|\<xi\>/2> in the Gaussian event. Suppose every coordinate
  within distance <math|2*B> of a warning anchor satisfies

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||z<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<xi\>/2|)>*\<tau\>*<sqrt|\<beta\>>.
  </equation*>

  The deterministic safety estimate at margin <math|\<xi\>> implies that the
  warning factor is zero on a neighbourhood of the base parameter. We check
  the different dependencies, since a value equal to zero by itself would not
  imply vanishing derivatives.

  A warning in its mandatory seed neighbourhood is identically zero by its
  frozen indicator. Outside that neighbourhood, the safety estimate makes
  every relevant fine plaquette reading strictly smaller than
  <math|\<tau\>/4>, and every predictor chord strictly smaller than
  <math|W*\<tau\>>. The fine mask, site likelihood, bond likelihood and
  contraction test are evaluated at the held fine configuration
  <math|U<rsub|\<ast\>>>. Their values are consequently constant under the
  variation and equal to one. The contraction statement uses the fine
  plaquette estimates at all sites in its read set, which fits in the same
  <math|2*B> event window. The predictor chord depends continuously on the
  varied coarse field, with its fine predictor held fixed. Its strict
  inequality persists on a neighbourhood, so its cutoff also remains one
  there.

  For a fine-bond cutoff, the reduced threshold bounds the base increment by
  <math|<around|(|C+q*\<xi\>/2|)>*\<tau\>>. The safety constants satisfy
  <math|C+q*\<xi\>/2\<less\>a>, where <math|a*\<tau\>> is its plateau radius.
  The compensated increment is continuous, so this strict inequality also
  persists locally. Its cutoff-minus-one is thus a zero germ. Intersecting
  the finitely many neighbourhoods needed by a given factor proves the
  assertion. In particular, every nonzero jet of a warning is supported on
  the reduced-margin excursion event; no assertion about differentiation of
  an indicator of that event is needed.

  The warning amplitude is bounded by the fixed constant

  <\equation*>
    T=1+#<around|{|<text|frame tags>|}>+#<around|{|<text|predictor tags>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed the held likelihoods are bounded by their tag counts, all masks lie
  in <math|<around|[|0,1|]>>, and the warning is a protected difference from
  one. Its derivative scale has the form <eqref|eq:activity-common-scale>
  with <math|d<rsub|i>=0>. For a varying noise cutoff, normalize its chord by
  <math|W*\<tau\>>; the positive derivatives cost the coarse scale divided by
  <math|\<tau\>>, of upper exponent zero. For a bond cutoff use the
  corresponding normalized compensated increment. A cutoff outside its closed
  support is a zero germ; on the closed support the smooth scalar cutoff has
  bounded derivatives. Multiplying over its protective read set adds at most
  a function of upper exponent zero times its support cover. This proves the
  asserted scale in all cases, including boundary points.

  For every <math|t\<less\>0>, the Gaussian estimate at margin
  <math|\<xi\>/2> gives, after increasing the threshold in <math|x>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:activity-warning-square>\<bbb-P\><around|(|<text|all <math|m>
    reduced-margin warnings>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|h<rsup|2*t>|)><rsup|m>.
  </equation>

  This follows from the faster-than-every-power bound already proved, applied
  at an exponent strictly below <math|2*t>. Set <math|\<rho\>=h<rsup|t>>. The
  Gaussian moment argument consumes the square root of
  <eqref|eq:activity-warning-square>, leaving the price <math|T*\<rho\>> per
  distinct warning. Its site sum has upper exponent <math|t>: warning
  addresses have fixed density, each support lies in a
  fixed-multiple-<math|B> ball, its cover is bounded by a fixed constant, and
  the number of such supports through a site is polynomial in <math|B>.

  <subsubsection|Packets and the product estimate>

  The jets of the packet's stock extension
  <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|A><rsup|<math-up|st>>> at a base field satisfying the
  hypotheses of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:packet-estimates> have price

  <\equation*>
    p<rsub|A>=exp <around|{|<around|(|<around|(|C+3*j|)>*d<rsup|5>-\<beta\>*\<mu\>*\<tau\><rsup|2>|)>*n<around|(|A|)>|}>
  </equation*>

  and derivative scale one by <eqref|eq:packet-jet-price>. The same energy
  gain pays their root counts, support exponents and any prescribed fixed
  seed marks by the absorption argument in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:packet-estimates>. Thus their available
  weighted site price is smaller than every fixed negative power of <math|h>.
  A disconnected packet has factor and price zero. All thresholds are chosen
  after the fixed derivative order, halo, support and length reserves.

  Let <math|\<cal-P\>> be a finite family of distinct labels of the four
  types whose assigned supports are healthy for the base coarse field: no
  coarse flag at the threshold <math|<math-up|temp><around|(|x|)>> lies in
  any <math|S<rsub|i>>. In particular, each packet's full enlarged support
  must have this property to apply its packet jet estimate. This is a
  separate hypothesis on the tested family; the sector's manifest flag
  identity alone does not imply it. For packet jets we use the locally smooth
  stock extension of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:packet-estimates>; the
  healthy <math|500*B> neighbourhood, together with the funded-room and
  manifest-sector conditions, identifies its value with the physical packet
  at the base field. Likewise, for the coarse tail of the inherited
  logarithmic activities we differentiate the unmasked extension of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:inherited-activity-prices>, with the fixed
  long-pair selector and without the kept-row test; on a healthy footprint
  and under the funded-room condition it equals the actual coarse tail in
  value at the base field. No derivative of the physical flag test or of the
  kept-row test is asserted. Put

  <\equation*>
    s=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-P\>><around|{|c<rsub|B><around|(|S<rsub|i>|)>+d<rsub|i>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Every nonzero factor has nonempty support, hence
  <math|c<rsub|B><around|(|S<rsub|i>|)>\<geqslant\>1>. The derivative scales
  in <eqref|eq:activity-common-scale> therefore sum to at most
  <math|2*r<around|(|x|)>*s>, also covering the unit packet scales. Let
  <math|a<rsub|i>> denote the packet, forced or exponentiated-log price, and
  put <math|a<rsub|i>=T> for a warning. The product rule and the zero-germ
  property give exactly

  <\equation*>
    <big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-P\>>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<space|-0.17em><around*|(|\<b-1\><rsub|\<cal-C\>><around|(|z|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-P\>>a<rsub|i>,2*r<around|(|x|)>*s;y<rsub|0>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<cal-C\>> is the joint reduced-margin warning event. This
  verifies <eqref|eq:moment-activity-premise> for all species on the same
  variation. Applying the Gaussian differentiation and moment estimates
  yields

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:activity-integrated-jet><around*|\||D<rsup|k>*<big|int><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-P\>>f<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>*<around|(|1+s|)><rsup|m<rsub|j>>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-P\>><wide|a|~><rsub|i>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>j,
  </equation>

  with <math|A<rsub|j>> of upper exponent zero and
  <math|<wide|a|~><rsub|i>=a<rsub|i>> except that a warning has price
  <math|T*\<rho\>>. The integral includes the Gaussian dependence on the
  varied field and the frozen Haar variables, as in that argument.

  The combined optional site sum has upper exponent <math|t>, and the packet
  site sum is negligible. For a desired output exponent
  <math|t<rsub|<math-up|out>>\<in\><around|(|-1/2,0|)>>, choose
  <math|t\<in\><around|(|-1/2,t<rsub|<math-up|out>>|)>>. Fixed
  upper-exponent-zero factors and the fixed polynomial in <math|1+s> are then
  paid by the strict exponent slack and the support reserve of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mixed-activity-prices>. Its tree degree
  condition holds eventually because the geometric neighbour count has upper
  exponent zero whereas the vertex price has negative upper exponent. This
  supplies the differentiated product input for the connected-support
  estimates. It preserves the incoming representation and its selected
  history; it does not construct or reset their parameters.

  <subsection|Recovering the block integral from the
  activities><label|sec:gaussian-reassembly>

  The preceding estimates concern a dictionary of local factors. We now
  verify that its absolutely convergent sum is the original block integral.
  This verification fixes the scalar normalization and the incidence
  conditions before any logarithm of a gas is taken. Throughout, fix the
  coarse field, forest, allocation of seeds, and tag array. The funded sector
  hypotheses, the chart-access conclusion and the locality statements used in
  the preceding sections are retained. In particular, all precisions are
  positive, the covariance has its specified finite range, and the same
  assigned supports include every variable read by their factors.

  <subsubsection|The Gaussian density and the scalar factor>

  Write <math|A> for the completed sector precision, <math|A<rsub|0>> for its
  flat reference, and <math|C> for the localized covariance. The reference
  <math|A<rsub|0>> is formed at the identity coarse field with no seeds and
  the same forest: all free bonds are active and only tree coordinates
  receive identity completion. The precision <math|A> comes from the selected
  affine row square with an identity completion on inactive coordinates;
  <math|C> is a positive finite-range approximation to its inverse. We retain
  the correction <math|C<rsup|-1>-A> below, rather than identifying the two.
  In particular, this affine precision is not asserted to be the Hessian of
  the full nonlinear effective action. All three act on the full real
  coordinate space, of dimension <math|D>. Let <math|q> be the colour
  dimension, <math|N> the number of free forest bonds, and <math|n> the
  number of active free bonds. The other <math|N-n> free bonds are integrated
  in their original Haar coordinates. Put

  <\equation*>
    g=\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>,<space|2em>b=<sqrt|2*\<pi\>>,<space|2em>m=c<rsub|<math-up|H>>*<around|(|b*g|)><rsup|q>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|c<rsub|<math-up|H>>\<gtr\>0> is the constant in the local
  probability-Haar density of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:haar-chart>. The
  mean <math|\<mu\>> is the physical field associated with
  <math|-P<rsup|\<ast\>>*\<ell\>>, where <math|P> is the padded,
  column-dependent principal inverse and <math|\<ell\>> the linear row source
  of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-mean>. This uses
  <math|P<rsup|\<ast\>>>, rather than <math|C> or <math|A<rsup|-1>>. Its
  inactive coordinates vanish: the precision has an inactive identity block,
  every principal inverse preserves that block, and the inactive source
  coordinates are zero. With <math|u=\<mu\>+g*z>, the reference Gaussian has
  density

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reassembly-gaussian-density>\<vartheta\><rsub|C><around|(|z|)>=b<rsup|-D>*exp
    <around*|(|-<tfrac|1|2>log det C-<tfrac|1|2>z<rsup|T>*C<rsup|-1>*z|)>.
  </equation>

  Inactive coordinates are completed by independent standard Gaussians. Their
  energy is <math|<with|math-font|cal*|G><around|(|z|)>=<frac|1|2>*<big|sum><rsub|i<math-up|inactive>>z<rsub|i><rsup|2>>.
  There are <math|D-n*q> such coordinates, including the tree coordinates.
  Consequently the change of variables in the active Haar coordinates, with
  the dummy Gaussian integrals inserted, gives the multiplier

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reassembly-jacobian>b<rsup|-<around|(|D-n*q|)>>*g<rsup|n*q>*c<rsub|<math-up|H>><rsup|n>*exp
    <around*|(|-<with|math-font|cal*|G><around|(|z|)>+<big|sum><rsub|e<math-up|active>>j<rsub|e><around|(|u|)>|)>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|j<rsub|e>> is the localized logarithmic Haar correction; its
  auxiliary cutoff agrees with the actual correction wherever the smaller
  chart mask is nonzero. The constant in <eqref|eq:reassembly-jacobian> is
  exactly

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reassembly-count>b<rsup|-D>*m<rsup|N>*exp
    <around|(|-<around|(|N-n|)>*log m|)>.
  </equation>

  Indeed the right side is <math|b<rsup|-D>*m<rsup|n>=b<rsup|-<around|(|D-n*q|)>>*g<rsup|n*q>*c<rsub|<math-up|H>><rsup|n>>.
  This uses <math|n+<around|(|N-n|)>=N>, not the number of active coordinates
  as a substitute for the number of free bonds.

  The dummy insertion is exact even though the active integrand depends on
  the remaining Haar coordinates. Split the latter product measure into
  active and inactive free bonds and freeze the inactive variables. The
  active affine substitution has Jacobian <math|g<rsup|n*q>>. In the
  orthogonal coordinate decomposition into active and dummy real variables,
  the dummy density integrates to one. Fubini therefore proves the identity
  for each fixed inactive configuration and then for its Haar integral. Chart
  access guarantees that the nonzero original integrand is covered by this
  substitution; the smaller mask can be inserted there with value one. No
  assertion that the entire Haar group lies in one chart is used.

  Let <math|<with|math-font|cal*|L>> denote the original positive prefactor:
  it is the noise normalization times <math|exp
  <around|(|v<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|f>|\|>|)>> times the coarse-field
  scalar extracted from the inherited exponent. The scalar retained outside
  all activities is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reassembly-overhead><with|math-font|cal*|O>=<with|math-font|cal*|L>*<space|0.17em>m<rsup|N><around|(|det
    A<rsub|0>|)><rsup|-1/2>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation>

  The flat reference uses the very same forest. Its determinant is not
  replaced by a componentwise determinant, nor is
  <eqref|eq:reassembly-overhead> charged to every component.

  <subsubsection|Restoring the masks and the exponent>

  We spell out the cancellations that identify the density. Let
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|E><around|(|u|)>> be the whole
  quadratic-plus-remainder block energy, and let
  <math|r<rsub|j><around|(|u|)>> be the inherited logarithms. Let
  <math|d<rsub|p><around|(|u|)>> be the individual nonlinear remainders, so
  the signed channel average satisfies

  <\equation*>
    <with|math-font|cal*|E><around|(|u|)>=\<langle\><with|math-display|false|<big|sum><rsub|p>d<rsub|p><around|(|u|)>\<rangle\>+Q<around|(|u|)>,>
  </equation*>

  where <math|Q<around|(|u|)>=<frac|1|2>*<around|\<\|\|\>|M*u+b<rsub|<math-up|row>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>
  is the physical quadratic expression. The selected row matrix <math|M> has
  zero inactive columns; <math|A=M<rsup|T>*M+I<rsub|<math-up|inactive>>> and
  <math|\<ell\>=M<rsup|T>*b<rsub|<math-up|row>>>. The inactive coordinates of
  <math|\<mu\>> and <math|\<ell\>> are zero. This is the rowwise square
  expansion used in Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:mean-action-jets> and
  <reference|sec:remote-action-jets>: each row is assigned either to its seed
  neighbourhood or to the remote remainder, with no omitted row. Write
  <math|c<rsub|e>=<frac|1|2>*<around|(|J<rsub|e><around|(|A<rsub|0>|)>-J<rsub|e><around|(|A|)>|)>>
  for the local normalization correction.

  The compulsory packet exponent, summed over the packet layout, is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reassembly-packet-log>s<around|(|u|)>=-\<beta\>*<around*|(|D<rsub|<math-up|near>><around|(|u|)>+Q<rsub|<math-up|seed>><around|(|\<mu\>|)>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|e<math-up|near>>c<rsub|e>+<big|sum><rsub|e<math-up|near>>j<rsub|e><around|(|u|)>-<around|(|N-n|)>*log
    m.
  </equation>

  Here <math|D<rsub|<math-up|near>><around|(|u|)>> is the signed average of
  <math|d<rsub|p><around|(|u|)>> over the posted rows at radius <math|200*B>,
  and <math|Q<rsub|<math-up|seed>><around|(|\<mu\>|)>> is the signed average
  of the extracted row squares at <math|\<mu\>> over those same posted rows.
  They are not complementary row sets. The correction sum
  <math|<big|sum>c<rsub|e>> uses radius <math|200*B>, whereas the Haar
  correction sum <math|<big|sum>j<rsub|e>> uses radius <math|304*B>; they are
  different sets. The packet layout uses disjoint halos of radius at least
  <math|500*B>, so all reads through <math|304*B>, including the extra two
  lattice steps, belong to a unique packet. Thus summing the packet exponents
  gives exactly <eqref|eq:reassembly-packet-log>. Likewise the packet
  products reproduce the compulsory likelihoods, flag tests and chart masks
  once each. A test whose location belongs to a packet has the same seed
  membership in that packet as in the full allocation: a seed changing the
  test would meet that packet's halo, and hence would lie in the same
  component.

  Let <math|f<rsub|i><around|(|z|)>> be the optional logarithms in the
  complete dictionary. On the nonzero smaller chart mask their sum is

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i>f<rsub|i><around|(|z|)>=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|j>r<rsub|j><around|(|u|)>+<big|sum><rsub|p>h<rsub|p><around|(|u|)>+<big|sum><rsub|e<math-up|remote>>c<rsub|e>+<big|sum><rsub|e<math-up|remote>>j<rsub|e><around|(|u|)>+<with|math-font|cal*|R><around|(|z|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:reassembly-logs>>>|<row|<cell|<with|math-font|cal*|R><around|(|z|)>=>|<cell|<tfrac|1|2><around|(|log
    det C+log det A<rsub|0>|)>-<big|sum><rsub|e>c<rsub|e>+z<rsup|T>*<wide|h|^>+<tfrac|1|2>z<rsup|T>*<around|(|C<rsup|-1>-A|)>*z.>>>>
  </align>

  The remote index sets here are the exact complements of the two near sets
  above. The determinant and inverse series giving
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|R>> are those of
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:local-covariance> and
  <reference|sec:itinerary-prices>. Their three determinant contributions are

  <\equation*>
    -<tfrac|1|2><around|(|log det A-<big|sum><rsub|e>J<rsub|e><around|(|A|)>|)>,<space|1em><tfrac|1|2><around|(|log
    det A<rsub|0>-<big|sum><rsub|e>J<rsub|e><around|(|A<rsub|0>|)>|)>,<space|1em><tfrac|1|2><around|(|log
    det A+log det C|)>.
  </equation*>

  Their sum is the determinant part of <math|<with|math-font|cal*|R>>. The
  linear itineraries sum to <math|z<rsup|T>*<wide|h|^>> and the quadratic
  itineraries sum to <math|<frac|1|2>*z<rsup|T>*<around|(|C<rsup|-1>-A|)>*z>.
  Their endpoint protectors equal one on the smaller full mask. Absolute
  summability was proved before this recombination, so these are identities
  of convergent series.

  The remote action terms obey

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reassembly-remote-cancel><big|sum><rsub|p>h<rsub|p><around|(|u|)>-\<beta\>*<around*|(|D<rsub|<math-up|near>><around|(|u|)>+Q<rsub|<math-up|seed>><around|(|\<mu\>|)>|)>=-\<beta\><around*|(|\<langle\><with|math-display|false|<around*|\<nobracket\>|<big|sum><rsub|p>d<rsub|p><around|(|u|)>\<rangle\>+Q<around|(|\<mu\>|)>|)>.>|\<nobracket\>>
  </equation>

  For a near row this follows from its assigned debit and mean-square term.
  For a remote row it is precisely the unmasked local-square identity: its
  protecting cutoff equals one on the smaller full mask. Summing first over
  rows and then over the finite signed channels proves
  <eqref|eq:reassembly-remote-cancel>, including rows with zero factors.

  Finally, if <math|\<ell\>> is the linear coefficient of <math|Q>, then
  <math|<wide|h|^>=-g<rsup|-1>*<around|(|A*\<mu\>+\<ell\>|)>> and
  <math|\<beta\>*g<rsup|2>=1> give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reassembly-square-cancel>-\<beta\>*Q<around|(|u|)>-<with|math-font|cal*|G><around|(|z|)>=-\<beta\>*Q<around|(|\<mu\>|)>+z<rsup|T>*<wide|h|^>+<tfrac|1|2>z<rsup|T>*<around|(|C<rsup|-1>-A|)>*z-<tfrac|1|2>z<rsup|T>*C<rsup|-1>*z.
  </equation>

  To verify this, expand <math|Q*<around|(|\<mu\>+g*z|)>>, use that the
  inactive mean and linear coefficient vanish, and add the inactive identity
  quadratic form supplied by <math|<with|math-font|cal*|G>>. This is an
  algebraic identity, valid also where the masks vanish.

  Combining <eqref|eq:reassembly-packet-log>\U<eqref|eq:reassembly-square-cancel>
  cancels both local sums <math|<big|sum>c<rsub|e>> and both full
  determinants. It gives

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|-<tfrac|1|2>log det
    A<rsub|0>+s<around|(|u|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i>f<rsub|i><around|(|z|)>-<tfrac|1|2>log
    det C-<tfrac|1|2>z<rsup|T>*C<rsup|-1>*z>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|2em>=<big|sum><rsub|j>r<rsub|j><around|(|u|)>-\<beta\>*<with|math-font|cal*|E><around|(|u|)>-<with|math-font|cal*|G><around|(|z|)>+<big|sum><rsub|e<math-up|active>>j<rsub|e><around|(|u|)>-<around|(|N-n|)>*log
    m.<eq-number><label|eq:reassembly-exponents>>>>>
  </align>

  Multiplication by <math|b<rsup|-D>*m<rsup|N>*<with|math-font|cal*|L>> and
  <eqref|eq:reassembly-count> now recover the exact original density and its
  Haar Jacobian.

  There is also a finite product identity for the masks. Write
  <math|a<rsub|p>> for a compulsory test and <math|w<rsub|p>> for its
  optional warning. At a compulsory position <math|w<rsub|p>=0> and its
  packet supplies <math|a<rsub|p>>; at every other position the optional
  factor is <math|1+w<rsub|p>=a<rsub|p>> on the smaller full mask. The
  auxiliary mask warnings restore that full mask from the packet masks. If it
  vanishes, the entire product on both sides is zero. If it is nonzero, every
  doubled mask is one and the preceding identities apply literally. The
  complete product of physical tag likelihoods equals the number of tag
  choices times the original normalized likelihood. Thus a single factor
  <math|<around|\||\<cal-T\>|\|><rsup|-1>>, where <math|\<cal-T\>> is the
  tag-array space, remains outside the product. This is the uniform tag
  average, not a new likelihood for each use of a tag.

  <subsubsection|Absolute expansion and return to probability Haar>

  Fix a finite old forced-label set <math|K> and a packet layout
  <math|\<cal-P\>>. For finite sets <math|I> of logarithmic labels and
  <math|J> of warning labels, the expanded integrand is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reassembly-molecule>\<Phi\><rsub|K,I,J>=<big|prod><rsub|P\<in\>\<cal-P\>>P<around|(|u|)>*<big|prod><rsub|k\<in\>K>K<rsub|k><around|(|u|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I><around|(|e<rsup|f<rsub|i><around|(|z|)>>-1|)>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>J>w<rsub|j><around|(|u|)>.
  </equation>

  The four alphabets form a disjoint union; coincident physical locations do
  not identify their labels. On the finite lattice the nonempty supports and
  rooted price bounds imply <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>p<rsub|i>\<less\>\<infty\>>
  for the whole alphabet. Separately, the all-argument logarithm estimates of
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:remote-action-jets>\U<reference|sec:inherited-activity-prices>
  give envelopes <math|<around|\||f<rsub|i><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|i>>
  with <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>a<rsub|i>\<less\>\<infty\>>. Their endpoint
  cutoffs are retained in this assertion. Thus
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\||f<rsub|i><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<less\>\<infty\>>,
  and

  <\equation*>
    exp <around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i>f<rsub|i><around|(|z|)>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|I<math-up|finite>><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I><around|(|e<rsup|f<rsub|i><around|(|z|)>>-1|)>.
  </equation*>

  For a proof, exhaust the countable alphabet by finite subsets and expand
  each finite product. Absolute convergence follows from
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\||e<rsup|f<rsub|i>>-1|\|>\<less\>\<infty\>>:
  outside finitely many indices, <math|<around|\||e<rsup|f<rsub|i>>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>2<around|\||f<rsub|i>|\|>>.
  The warning alphabet is finite and its expansion is the ordinary identity
  for <math|<big|prod><rsub|j><around|(|1+w<rsub|j>|)>>.

  The simultaneous product estimate <eqref|eq:warning-sector-mass> gives,
  with the original probability Haar variables <math|y> retained,

  <\equation*>
    <big|int><around|\||\<Phi\><rsub|K,I,J>*<around|(|y,z|)>|\|>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|F><around|(|y|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|C><around|(|z|)>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|v\<in\>\<cal-P\>\<sqcup\>K\<sqcup\>I\<sqcup\>J>p<rsub|v>.
  </equation*>

  Summing the right side over all finite label sets gives at most
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|v><around|(|1+p<rsub|v>|)>\<leqslant\>exp
  <around|(|<big|sum><rsub|v>p<rsub|v>|)>>. Distinct triples
  <math|<around|(|K,I,J|)>> give distinct label sets. Tonelli applied to the
  absolute values therefore justifies every exchange between these sums and
  integrals. The same argument covers the subsequent finite sum over seed
  allocations and tag arrays. There is no division by the value of a vacuum
  or flagged term.

  An allocation contributes only when its flags match the coarse field, the
  old forced labels are admissible, and their inventories have the required
  incidence with the seeds. These conditions are necessary for a nonzero
  original integrand: the profile enforces its exact flags, and a nonzero old
  gas term enforces its admissibility and incidence. Set the entire integral
  to zero when any condition fails. On a valid allocation the density
  calculation above applies; on an invalid one both sides vanish. Hence the
  absolutely convergent sum, multiplied by
  <math|<around|\||\<cal-T\>|\|><rsup|-1>*<with|math-font|cal*|O>>, equals
  the original contribution for the fixed tags and seed allocation. Summing
  the old forced sets, then the tags and seed allocations, recovers the
  probability-Haar block push with scalar factor
  <eqref|eq:reassembly-overhead>.

  <subsubsection|Factorization into connected activities>

  It remains to show that the integral of a molecule factors over its
  connected components. Connectivity is defined by intersection of the full
  assigned supports. For fixed tags, different components read disjoint Haar
  coordinates. Their Gaussian coordinates may be larger than their physical
  read sets; the assigned supports include the full covariance-range halo
  around every Gaussian coordinate read. If two components have disjoint
  assigned supports, any two Gaussian coordinates read by them are farther
  apart than the range of <math|C>. Their cross covariance is zero. The
  Gaussian characteristic function consequently splits into the product of
  its component characteristic functions, proving independence of these
  coordinate projections. It follows that the Gaussian integral of the
  product factors. The remaining product-Haar integral factors by its
  disjoint read sets. All integrands are measurable and integrable by the
  preceding absolute bounds.

  Lastly average over the tags. A component integral depends only on the site
  tags and on the bond tags anchored in its assigned support. These
  coordinate sets are disjoint for different components. The uniform measure
  on tag arrays is a product measure, so averaging first the bond tags and
  then the site tags proves the same factorization for the averaged
  integrals. Averages are taken after each component has been identified,
  using the common original tag space.

  For each connected label set <math|M>, define its activity
  <math|a<rsub|M><around|(|V|)>> by this averaged integral with the exact
  local incidence tests: its inventory must equal the flags of <math|V>
  inside its assigned support, and all its forced-label and packet tests must
  hold. The integral uses the globally flagged sector with the same local
  data. Locality shows that its value agrees with the restriction of the full
  allocation: on this support the seeds, tags, forced labels and coarse field
  coincide. The flags outside the support cannot change its value.

  Taking connected components and taking their union are inverse on
  admissible label families. Indeed every label has nonempty support, so
  distinct components cannot share a label. Their supports are disjoint by
  definition. Exact global flag coverage is equivalent to exact local
  inventory equality in each component together with coverage by all
  inventories. This proves the weight-preserving factorization, including the
  empty family. Reindexing the absolutely convergent sum gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reassembly-block-gas><around|(|\<cal-B\>*R|)><around|(|V|)>=<with|math-font|cal*|O><around|(|V|)>*<big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-A\><math-up|finite,
    compatible>>>|<row|<cell|<big|cup><rsub|M\<in\>\<cal-A\>>I<rsub|M>=<math-up|Flags><around|(|V|)>>>>>>><big|prod><rsub|M\<in\>\<cal-A\>>a<rsub|M><around|(|V|)>.
  </equation>

  Thus the local prices bound an exact representation of the original block
  push. The later separation of flag-free logarithms and the iteration of the
  resulting bounds still use their stated construction inputs; neither
  follows merely from <eqref|eq:reassembly-block-gas>.

  <subsection|Averaging the forests and separating the regular
  gas><label|sec:forest-average-log>

  For maximal-tree gauge fixing on a lattice, see Creutz <cite|Creutz77>. The
  comparisons below track changes between the particular rooted forests used
  in the block construction and the associated chart factors.

  The exact block gas of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-reassembly>
  was constructed with a fixed forest. To restore the lattice symmetries we
  average over forests before taking the logarithm of the flag-free gas. We
  first show that this operation leaves the scalar normalization unchanged,
  and then choose an average compatible with local factorization.

  <subsubsection|The flat determinant is independent of the forest>

  Every block forest has one outgoing edge from each nonroot vertex, directed
  towards the root. Distinct nonroot vertices give distinct unoriented
  positive-bond representatives of these edges. If two outgoing edges
  represented the same bond with opposite orientations, the forest height
  would strictly decrease in both directions, which is impossible. Thus the
  tree bonds are in bijection with the nonroot vertices. In particular all
  forests have the same number of tree bonds and the same number <math|N> of
  free bonds.

  Let <math|F,H> be two forests and choose a bijection <math|t> between their
  tree bonds. We construct an integer change of basis on all bond
  coordinates, including the dummy tree coordinates. For a bond cochain
  <math|u>, let <math|D<rsub|F>*u> set its <math|F>-tree coordinates to zero.
  Let <math|\<alpha\><rsub|F>*u<around|(|x|)>> be the signed sum of <math|u>
  along the forest path from <math|x> to its block root, and put

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|F>*u=u+\<nabla\>*\<alpha\><rsub|F>*u.
  </equation*>

  Telescoping along a path proves the following four facts:
  <math|B<rsub|F>*u> is zero on the <math|F>-tree bonds; <math|B<rsub|F>*u=u>
  if <math|u> was already zero there; <math|\<alpha\><rsub|F>*u> is zero at
  each root; and <math|B<rsub|F>*\<nabla\>*v=0> when <math|v> is zero at all
  roots. These assertions hold over any abelian group, since they involve
  only signed sums.

  Define <math|T<rsub|F*H>*u> on the <math|H>-tree bonds by transporting the
  old <math|F>-tree coordinates through <math|t>, and on the other bonds by
  <math|B<rsub|H>*D<rsub|F>*u>. Then

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|H>*T<rsub|F*H>*u=B<rsub|H>*D<rsub|F>*u.
  </equation*>

  The reverse construction uses <math|t<rsup|-1>>. On tree coordinates it
  plainly recovers <math|u>. On the complementary coordinates,

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|F>*D<rsub|H>*T<rsub|F*H>*u=B<rsub|F>*<around|(|D<rsub|F>*u+\<nabla\>*\<alpha\><rsub|H>*D<rsub|F>*u|)>=B<rsub|F>*D<rsub|F>*u=D<rsub|F>*u,
  </equation*>

  because <math|\<alpha\><rsub|H>*D<rsub|F>*u> vanishes at the roots. Thus
  <math|T<rsub|H*F>*T<rsub|F*H>=1>; reversing the argument gives the other
  inverse identity. Its matrix and inverse have integer entries. Their
  integer determinants multiply to one, so <math|<around|\||det
  T<rsub|F*H>|\|>=1>. Apply this same construction to every colour
  coordinate.

  Let <math|M<rsub|F>,M<rsub|H>> be the flat selected row matrices, and let
  <math|D<rsub|F><rsup|0>,D<rsub|H><rsup|0>> be the diagonal projections onto
  their tree coordinates. There is one forest-independent flat row map
  <math|M>, with <math|M<rsub|F>=M*D<rsub|F>> and
  <math|M<rsub|H>=M*D<rsub|H>>. Flat rows annihilate gradients of root-zero
  functions. For a fine row this is the telescoping identity around its
  plaquette, preserved by each linear path coefficient. For a predictor row
  the coarse sampling operator sends a gradient to the coarse gradient of its
  root values; these values are zero. Signed coordinate changes preserve
  gradients, so the statement holds simultaneously in all signed channels.
  Hence

  <\equation*>
    M<rsub|H>*T<rsub|F*H>=M<rsub|F>,<space|2em>T<rsub|F*H><rsup|T>*D<rsub|H><rsup|0>*T<rsub|F*H>=D<rsub|F><rsup|0>.
  </equation*>

  The second identity follows because the tree coordinates are simply
  permuted. For the completed flat precisions we obtain

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|F><rsup|0>=M<rsub|F><rsup|T>*M<rsub|F>+D<rsub|F><rsup|0>=T<rsub|F*H><rsup|T>*A<rsub|H><rsup|0>*T<rsub|F*H>,<space|2em>det
    A<rsub|F><rsup|0>=det A<rsub|H><rsup|0>.
  </equation*>

  Together with the common free-bond count, this proves that the scalar
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|O>> in <eqref|eq:reassembly-overhead> is forest
  independent.

  <subsubsection|An independent choice at each coarse site>

  There are finitely many allowed forests on a finite torus: each vertex
  chooses one of finitely many oriented neighbouring edges and one of
  finitely many heights. The set is nonempty by the block-tree construction.
  At each coarse site <math|x>, choose independently a uniform forest
  <math|F<rsub|x>> from this finite set. On the block with root <math|x>, use
  only the restriction of <math|F<rsub|x>>. The assembled object is a valid
  forest, since each original forest edge remains in its parent block and its
  height decreases along that edge. Repeated global forest choices producing
  the same assembled forest cause no difficulty; the average is defined on
  the product space of choices.

  For a connected component <math|M>, its integrated activity depends on
  these choices only at coarse sites in its full assigned support
  <math|X<rsub|M>>. This follows from the local reads of the rows, inverse
  windows, tags and chart factors: agreement of the tree edges in that
  support gives equality of each such read and of its Gaussian marginal. To
  compare the Haar factors, realize both local charts as pullbacks of the
  same full product Haar measure; agreement of the principal Gaussian
  marginals then gives equality of the integrals. In particular changing a
  forest outside <math|X<rsub|M>> does not alter the component integral.
  Compatible components have disjoint <math|X<rsub|M>>. Independence of the
  site choices therefore gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:forest-product-average>\<bbb-E\><rsub|F>*<big|prod><rsub|M\<in\>\<cal-A\>>a<rsub|M><rsup|F><around|(|V|)>=<big|prod><rsub|M\<in\>\<cal-A\>>\<bbb-E\><rsub|F>*a<rsub|M><rsup|F><around|(|V|)>.
  </equation>

  This is the factorization of a product measure over disjoint coordinate
  sets; it does not hold for an arbitrary average over one global forest.

  Set <math|<wide|a|\<bar\>><rsub|M><around|(|V|)>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|F>*a<rsub|M><rsup|F><around|(|V|)>>.
  The bounds on <math|a<rsub|M><rsup|F>> are uniform in <math|F>, so the same
  prices bound <math|<wide|a|\<bar\>><rsub|M>>. The finite average commutes
  with the absolutely convergent gas sum, and the forest-independent
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|O>> factors out. Thus
  <eqref|eq:reassembly-block-gas> holds with <math|<wide|a|\<bar\>><rsub|M>>
  in place of <math|a<rsub|M><rsup|F>>. A signed lattice transformation
  permutes the site choices and transforms every chosen forest by a
  bijection. Its product uniform measure is unchanged. The covariance of the
  individual sector formulas consequently gives the same covariance for
  <math|<wide|a|\<bar\>><rsub|M>>, with the original transformed activity
  addresses.

  <subsubsection|The cost of excluding regular activities>

  Separate the connected labels into regular labels <math|i> with empty
  inventory and forced labels <math|j> with nonempty inventory. Write their
  values as <math|w<rsub|i><around|(|V|)>> and
  <math|k<rsub|j><around|(|V|)>>, and their supports as
  <math|X<rsub|i>,Y<rsub|j>>. A nonzero regular value has no flag in its
  support. Fix a compatible finite forced family <math|A> whose inventories
  cover all flags, and put <math|Y<rsub|A>=<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>A>Y<rsub|j>>.
  The remaining sum is the regular gas restricted to labels whose supports
  avoid <math|Y<rsub|A>>:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:forest-forced-first><around|(|\<cal-B\>*R|)><around|(|V|)>=<with|math-font|cal*|O><around|(|V|)>*<big|sum><rsub|A<math-up|admissible>><big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>A>k<rsub|j><around|(|V|)>*<space|0.17em>Z<rsub|Y<rsub|A>><around|(|V|)>.
  </equation>

  The reindexing is exact: a compatible family splits uniquely into its
  regular and forced parts, and cross compatibility means precisely
  <math|X<rsub|i>\<cap\>Y<rsub|A>=\<varnothing\>> for every retained regular
  label. Absolute convergence justifies summing in this order.

  Assume the regular prices satisfy the connected-cluster bound of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:cluster-expansion>, with nonnegative support
  weight, rooted allowance <math|\<gamma\>> and tail ratio
  <math|q<rsub|0>\<less\>1>. Let <math|c<rsub|\<Gamma\>>> be the
  corresponding logarithmic term, indexed by a finite ordered tuple of
  occurrences, with its Ursell coefficient divided by the tuple factorial.
  Labels may repeat in this tuple. Then

  <\equation*>
    Z=exp <around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|\<Gamma\>>c<rsub|\<Gamma\>>|)>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation*>

  For a finite set <math|Y> define

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|Y>=<big|sum><rsub|\<Gamma\>:<space|0.17em>X<rsub|\<Gamma\>>\<cap\>Y\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>c<rsub|\<Gamma\>>,<space|2em>X<rsub|\<Gamma\>>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<Gamma\>>X<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  Deleting labels that meet <math|Y> replaces <math|c<rsub|\<Gamma\>>> by
  zero precisely when its union support meets <math|Y>. Otherwise it leaves
  the term unchanged. The same absolute majorant applies to the deleted gas.
  Subtracting the two absolutely convergent logarithmic series yields

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:forest-deletion-ratio>Z<rsub|Y>=Z*e<rsup|-P<rsub|Y>>.
  </equation>

  The rooted cluster estimate, summed over <math|x\<in\>Y>, gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:forest-deletion-price><big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<Gamma\>:<space|0.17em>X<rsub|\<Gamma\>>\<cap\>Y\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||\<Gamma\>|\|>-1\<geqslant\>m>>>>>><around|\||c<rsub|\<Gamma\>>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||Y|\|><frac|\<gamma\>*q<rsub|0><rsup|m>|1-q<rsub|0>>.
  </equation>

  Indeed each term on the left is counted by at least one site of
  <math|Y\<cap\>X<rsub|\<Gamma\>>>; overcounting is harmless in this
  nonnegative sum. In particular, with <math|C<rsub|Y>=<around|\||Y|\|>*\<gamma\>/<around|(|1-q<rsub|0>|)>>,

  <\equation*>
    Z*e<rsup|-C<rsub|Y>>\<leqslant\>Z<rsub|Y>\<leqslant\>Z*e<rsup|C<rsub|Y>>.
  </equation*>

  The correction <math|P<rsub|Y>> retains its actual sign; only its absolute
  estimate uses <math|C<rsub|Y>>. Combining <eqref|eq:forest-forced-first>
  and <eqref|eq:forest-deletion-ratio> gives the exact separation

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:forest-regular-separated><around|(|\<cal-B\>*R|)><around|(|V|)>=<with|math-font|cal*|O><around|(|V|)>*Z<around|(|V|)>*<big|sum><rsub|A<math-up|admissible>><big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>A>k<rsub|j><around|(|V|)>*e<rsup|-P<rsub|Y<rsub|A>><around|(|V|)>>.
  </equation>

  For each fixed <math|A>, the ratio is finite and positive. The original
  absolute family sum implies absolute summability of this outer series: its
  term is exactly the signed forced product times the restricted regular gas,
  whose absolute value is bounded by the corresponding sum of absolute
  original terms. No volume-independent bound on the extensive scalar
  <math|Z> is asserted.

  <subsubsection|Local regular functions and their derivatives>

  For each regular component, the integrated construction gives a measurable
  local extension <math|f<rsub|i>>, smooth through the purchased finite order
  at configurations healthy on <math|X<rsub|i>>, with

  <\equation*>
    w<rsub|i><around|(|V|)>=\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|X<rsub|i>\<cap\><math-up|Flags><around|(|V|)>=\<varnothing\>|}>>*f<rsub|i><around|(|V|)>.
  </equation*>

  The forest average preserves locality, gauge invariance, measurability and
  each uniform derivative bound, by linearity and the triangle inequality for
  the finite average. For a cluster tuple
  <math|\<Gamma\>=<around|(|i<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,i<rsub|n>|)>>, define

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|\<Gamma\>><around|(|V|)>=<frac|u*<around|(|i<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,i<rsub|n>|)>|n!>*<big|prod><rsub|r=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i<rsub|r>><around|(|V|)>.
  </equation*>

  Then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:forest-cluster-mask>c<rsub|\<Gamma\>><around|(|V|)>=\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|X<rsub|\<Gamma\>>\<cap\><math-up|Flags><around|(|V|)>=\<varnothing\>|}>>*F<rsub|\<Gamma\>><around|(|V|)>.
  </equation>

  If the union is healthy, every constituent mask is one. If it is not, a
  flagged site belongs to some constituent support, so that factor vanishes.
  This proves the equality even for repeated labels.

  Suppose the local extension of the <math|r>th occurrence has derivatives
  through order <math|j> bounded by <math|p<rsub|i<rsub|r>>> at derivative
  scale one at configurations healthy on its support. Restrict throughout the
  following derivative estimates to configurations healthy on
  <math|X<rsub|\<Gamma\>>>; every constituent hypothesis then holds.
  Leibniz's formula gives product amplitude
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|r>p<rsub|i<rsub|r>>> at derivative scale <math|n>.
  Thus its derivatives through order <math|j> are bounded by

  <\equation*>
    <frac|<around|\||u*<around|(|i<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,i<rsub|n>|)>|\|>|n!>*<around|(|1+n|)><rsup|j>*<big|prod><rsub|r>p<rsub|i<rsub|r>>\<leqslant\><frac|<around|\||u*<around|(|i<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,i<rsub|n>|)>|\|>|n!>*<big|prod><rsub|r><around|(|2<rsup|j>*p<rsub|i<rsub|r>>|)>,
  </equation*>

  since <math|1+n\<leqslant\>2<rsup|n>> for <math|n\<geqslant\>1>. Buying the
  regular cluster bound with the inflated prices <math|2<rsup|j>*p<rsub|i>>
  therefore pays these derivatives on the same union support. A spatial
  derivative weight is also local: from any anchor in
  <math|X<rsub|\<Gamma\>>>, the connected-cover diameter bound places every
  read within a fixed multiple of <math|<around|(|B+1|)>*<around|(|1+<math-up|cover><rsub|B><space|0.17em>X<rsub|\<Gamma\>>|)>>.
  Its polynomial cost is paid by the reserved exponential support weight as
  in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mixed-activity-prices>.

  Equations<nbsp><eqref|eq:forest-regular-separated> and
  <eqref|eq:forest-cluster-mask> supply the regular logarithm and the exact
  exclusion correction of the nonlinear step. The exclusion exponential must
  still be regrouped with the forced labels using its occurrence
  multiplicities; it cannot be dropped or replaced by an independent
  probability. Its quantitative allowance is the rooted price
  <eqref|eq:forest-deletion-price>.

  <subsection|Regrouping the exclusion correction><label|sec:forced-exclusion>

  The regular gas in <eqref|eq:forest-regular-separated> has been extracted,
  but the forced family still carries its exclusion factor. We now put this
  factor into local forced activities. The factorials and signs of the
  repeated logarithmic terms are retained throughout.

  Fix a coarse configuration <math|V>. Continue to write <math|k<rsub|j>> for
  a forced activity with support <math|Y<rsub|j>> and nonempty inventory
  <math|I<rsub|j>\<subseteq\>Y<rsub|j>>, and <math|c<rsub|\<Gamma\>>> for a
  regular logarithmic cluster term with nonempty support
  <math|X<rsub|\<Gamma\>>>. We have summable majorants

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||k<rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>p<rsub|j>,<space|2em><around|\||c<rsub|\<Gamma\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>q<rsub|\<Gamma\>>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|j>p<rsub|j>+<big|sum><rsub|\<Gamma\>>q<rsub|\<Gamma\>>\<less\>\<infty\>.
  </equation*>

  These follow on a finite lattice from the previously purchased rooted
  bounds. Here <math|q<rsub|\<Gamma\>>> is specifically the absolute Ursell
  coefficient divided by the tuple factorial, multiplied by the purchased
  regular constituent prices. Thus the rooted cluster estimates apply to this
  majorant itself, not merely to <math|<around|\||c<rsub|\<Gamma\>>|\|>>. All
  component addresses, support sets and eligibility tests are fixed before
  their values at <math|V> are evaluated.

  For a finite admissible forced family <math|A>, the exclusion correction
  has the absolutely convergent expansion

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:exclusion-multiplicities>e<rsup|-P<rsub|Y<rsub|A>>>=<big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|n:<text|finite
    support in the cluster alphabet>>>|<row|<cell|n<rsub|\<Gamma\>>\<gtr\>0\<Rightarrow\>X<rsub|\<Gamma\>>\<cap\>Y<rsub|A>\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>>>>>><big|prod><rsub|\<Gamma\>:n<rsub|\<Gamma\>>\<gtr\>0><frac|<around|(|-c<rsub|\<Gamma\>>|)><rsup|n<rsub|\<Gamma\>>>|n<rsub|\<Gamma\>>!>,<space|2em>n<rsub|\<Gamma\>>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>.
  </equation>

  For a finite cluster alphabet this is the product of the ordinary
  exponential series. For the whole alphabet, the sum of the absolute
  products is at most <math|exp <around|(|<big|sum><rsub|\<Gamma\>>q<rsub|\<Gamma\>>|)>>;
  increasing finite subsets and dominated convergence prove the identity.
  Moreover the sum after multiplication by
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>A>k<rsub|j>> and summation over <math|A> is
  absolutely dominated by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:exclusion-absolute><big|prod><rsub|j><around|(|1+p<rsub|j>|)>*exp
    <around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|\<Gamma\>>q<rsub|\<Gamma\>>|)>.
  </equation>

  The restriction that every retained cluster meet <math|Y<rsub|A>> is
  essential: the correction deletes exactly those regular clusters.

  A finite decorated family consists of a finite set of forced labels and a
  finite set of cluster labels, with a positive integer multiplicity attached
  to each cluster label. Give a forced label its support <math|Y<rsub|j>> and
  multiplicity one, and a cluster label its support <math|X<rsub|\<Gamma\>>>.
  Its graph has one vertex per distinct label, with edges for intersecting
  supports. A new forced label <math|D> is a connected decorated family such
  that the old forced supports are pairwise disjoint and every cluster
  support meets the union of the old forced supports in <math|D>. Write
  <math|Z<rsub|D>> for its union support and
  <math|I<rsub|D>=<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>D<math-up|forced>>I<rsub|j>> for its
  inventory. In particular <math|D> contains a forced label and
  <math|I<rsub|D>\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>.

  Define its value by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:exclusion-new-value><wide|k|~><rsub|D><around|(|V|)>=\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|<math-up|Flags><around|(|V|)>\<cap\>Z<rsub|D>=I<rsub|D>|}>>*\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|<text|the
    old local eligibility tests hold>|}>>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>D<math-up|forced>>k<rsub|j><around|(|V|)>*<big|prod><rsub|\<Gamma\>\<in\>D<math-up|cluster>><frac|<around|(|-c<rsub|\<Gamma\>><around|(|V|)>|)><rsup|n<rsub|\<Gamma\>>>|n<rsub|\<Gamma\>>!>.
  </equation>

  The cluster label itself already includes its original tuple factorial in
  <math|c<rsub|\<Gamma\>>>. The additional <math|n<rsub|\<Gamma\>>>! belongs
  to the exponential in <eqref|eq:exclusion-multiplicities>; these are two
  different factorials.

  Consider a nonzero term in the expansion for <math|<around|(|A,n|)>>. Split
  its support graph into connected components. Every retained cluster meets a
  forced support in <math|A>, and that forced label is in its component. Thus
  each component is a new forced label as just defined. Its inventory equals
  the flags inside its union support: a flag there belongs to some old
  inventory because <math|A> covers all flags, and the corresponding old
  forced support meets this component, so belongs to it. The reverse
  inclusion follows from the original inventory condition. Old eligibility
  tests hold for each nonzero factor. Hence the product of
  <eqref|eq:exclusion-new-value> over the components equals the original
  term, with every sign and factorial.

  Conversely, flatten a finite compatible family of new labels whose
  inventories cover all flags and whose product is nonzero. Its old forced
  labels are pairwise compatible, both within and between the new labels.
  They satisfy all eligibility and exact flag-coverage conditions. A cluster
  label cannot occur in two new components: its nonempty support would
  intersect both of their supposedly disjoint union supports. Its
  multiplicity is therefore recovered unambiguously. Each retained cluster
  meets a forced support in its own component, as required in
  <eqref|eq:exclusion-multiplicities>. The connected components of the
  flattened graph are exactly the original new labels. Flattening and taking
  components are inverse.

  This weight-preserving bijection and <eqref|eq:exclusion-absolute> give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:exclusion-output-gas><around|(|\<cal-B\>*R|)><around|(|V|)>=<with|math-font|cal*|O><around|(|V|)>*exp
    <around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|\<Gamma\>>c<rsub|\<Gamma\>><around|(|V|)>|)>*<big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-D\><math-up|finite,
    compatible>>>|<row|<cell|<big|cup><rsub|D\<in\>\<cal-D\>>I<rsub|D>=<math-up|Flags><around|(|V|)>>>>>>><big|prod><rsub|D\<in\>\<cal-D\>><wide|k|~><rsub|D><around|(|V|)>.
  </equation>

  If there are no flags, no nonzero forced component is possible and the
  empty family contributes one. If there are flags, every nonzero component
  has a nonempty inventory. This is the required separation into a regular
  exponential and a forced gas.

  The new values are measurable and gauge invariant: each factor and
  eligibility predicate is, and the products are finite. They use only the
  final coarse field and its flags on <math|Z<rsub|D>>, by locality of the
  constituents and of the inventory test. Under a signed lattice
  transformation the old labels and the cluster occurrence tuples are
  permuted, with their multiplicities unchanged. Thus the value, support and
  inventory transform together. A common countable enumeration may then be
  chosen for the new labels, assigning zero to any unused addresses. Such an
  enumeration only reindexes the absolutely convergent gas and changes none
  of its values or support conditions.

  For later bounds the same construction has the explicit nonnegative
  majorant

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:exclusion-output-price><wide|p|~><rsub|D>=<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>D<math-up|forced>>p<rsub|j>*<big|prod><rsub|\<Gamma\>\<in\>D<math-up|cluster>><frac|q<rsub|\<Gamma\>><rsup|n<rsub|\<Gamma\>>>|n<rsub|\<Gamma\>>!>.
  </equation>

  Taking absolute values in <eqref|eq:exclusion-new-value> proves the bound
  by <eqref|eq:exclusion-output-price>. We next prove the uniform rooted
  estimate for these majorants.

  <subsubsection|Keeping one compulsory gain through the second expansion>

  Let <math|a\<geqslant\>0> be the desired support exponent and
  <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0> a reserve. Suppose the regular constituent
  prices obey the cluster criterion at support exponent
  <math|a+\<varepsilon\>>, rooted allowance <math|u\<gtr\>0> and tail ratio
  <math|1/2>. Suppose also that the old forced prices obey

  <\equation*>
    sup<rsub|x> <big|sum><rsub|j:x\<in\>Y<rsub|j>>p<rsub|j>*e<rsup|<around|(|a+\<varepsilon\>|)>*c<rsub|B><around|(|Y<rsub|j>|)>>\<leqslant\>u*e<rsup|-M>,<space|2em>M\<geqslant\>0.
  </equation*>

  The regular cluster bound then gives

  <\equation*>
    sup<rsub|x> <big|sum><rsub|\<Gamma\>:x\<in\>X<rsub|\<Gamma\>>>q<rsub|\<Gamma\>>*e<rsup|<around|(|a+\<varepsilon\>|)>*c<rsub|B><around|(|X<rsub|\<Gamma\>>|)>>\<leqslant\>2*u.
  </equation*>

  Set <math|\<eta\>=20*u> and assume

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:exclusion-second-smallness>\<eta\>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>,<space|2em><frac|4*b<rsub|B>*\<eta\>|\<varepsilon\>>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>,<space|2em>b<rsub|B>=<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>.
  </equation>

  Here is the distinct-set form of the connected-tree bound used below. If
  the site price of nonnegative weights <math|w<rsub|i>> at exponent
  <math|a+\<varepsilon\>> is at most <math|\<gamma\>>, the sum at exponent
  <math|a> over distinct connected sets of <math|m+1> labels meeting a given
  site is at most <math|\<gamma\>*<around|(|4*b<rsub|B>*\<gamma\>/\<varepsilon\>|)><rsup|m>>.
  Indeed, choose a root whose support contains the site. Enumerate the other
  <math|m> labels in each of their <math|m>! orders and choose a spanning
  tree oriented toward that root. This gives at least <math|m>! terms in the
  rooted forest sum, with root weights restricted to the given site, and the
  factorial in <eqref|eq:cluster-forest-bound> cancels them. The covering
  number of the union is at most the sum of the individual covering numbers,
  so the same bound applies with the union weight. The second condition in
  <eqref|eq:exclusion-second-smallness> is this degree bound with
  <math|\<gamma\>=\<eta\>>.

  We prove that, for every site <math|x> and integer <math|m\<geqslant\>0>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:exclusion-rooted-final><big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|D:<space|0.17em>x\<in\>Z<rsub|D>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||D|\|><rsub|<math-up|copies>>\<geqslant\>m>>>>>><wide|p|~><rsub|D>*e<rsup|a*c<rsub|B><around|(|Z<rsub|D>|)>>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|-m>*e<rsup|-M>*<space|0.17em>2*\<eta\>.
  </equation>

  For an upper bound forget the old compatibility tests, allow forced labels
  to repeat as well, and retain only connectedness and the presence of at
  least one forced label. On the resulting combined alphabet put
  <math|w<rsub|j>=p<rsub|j>>, <math|w<rsub|\<Gamma\>>=q<rsub|\<Gamma\>>>, and
  give each forced occurrence a mark <math|M>, each regular occurrence a mark
  zero. Inflate a price to

  <\equation*>
    v<rsub|i>=2*w<rsub|i>*e<rsup|e<rsub|i>>,<space|2em>e<rsub|j>=M,<space|1em>e<rsub|\<Gamma\>>=0.
  </equation*>

  The rooted bound for <math|2*v<rsub|i>> at exponent <math|a+\<varepsilon\>>
  is at most <math|4*e<rsup|M>*u*e<rsup|-M>+4*<around|(|2*u|)>=12*u\<leqslant\>\<eta\>>.
  Thus these prices meet the distinct connected-set criterion with allowance
  <math|\<eta\>> and tail ratio <math|1/2>, by
  <eqref|eq:exclusion-second-smallness>.

  To include multiplicities, first charge the desired support weight to each
  occurrence by setting <math|v<rsub|i><rprime|'>=v<rsub|i>*e<rsup|a*c<rsub|B><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>>,
  with <math|X<rsub|i>> denoting either kind of support. Since
  <math|X<rsub|i>\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>, choose a site in <math|X<rsub|i>> and
  use the single term of the rooted bound for <math|2*v<rsub|i>> to obtain
  <math|v<rsub|i><rprime|'>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>\<leqslant\>1/2>. For a fixed
  distinct set of labels <math|S>, summing all positive multiplicities gives

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|<around|(|n<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1|)><rsub|i\<in\>S>><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S><frac|<around|(|v<rsub|i><rprime|'>|)><rsup|n<rsub|i>>|n<rsub|i>!>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S><big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>1>v<rsub|i><rprime|'>*<around|(|1/2|)><rsup|n-1>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S>2*v<rsub|i><rprime|'>.
  </equation*>

  Here we used <math|n!\<geqslant\>1> and
  <math|v<rsub|i><rprime|'>\<leqslant\>1/2>. Connectedness depends only on
  the distinct supports, so the distinct connected-set bound now shows that
  the sum over all rooted connected multiplicity families is at most
  <math|\<eta\>/<around|(|1-1/2|)>=2*\<eta\>>. Cover subadditivity on a
  connected set gives <math|c<rsub|B><around|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>S>X<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>S>c<rsub|B><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i>n<rsub|i>*c<rsub|B><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>,
  justifying the charge per occurrence.

  Finally the inflated product differs from the original one by
  <math|2<rsup|<big|sum><rsub|i>n<rsub|i>>*exp
  <around|(|<big|sum><rsub|i>n<rsub|i>*e<rsub|i>|)>>. For a retained family
  these two factors are at least <math|2<rsup|m>> and <math|e<rsup|M>>,
  respectively. Extracting them proves <eqref|eq:exclusion-rooted-final>.
  Restricting back to the actual new labels only decreases the nonnegative
  sum. This argument preserves a compulsory gain even after arbitrary
  repeated regular corrections have been attached.

  In the selected scale estimates, <math|u<around|(|x|)>> has a fixed
  negative upper exponent and <math|B<around|(|x|)>> has upper exponent zero.
  Both inequalities in <eqref|eq:exclusion-second-smallness>, and the first
  regular degree bound with its own fixed positive reserve, therefore hold
  eventually. The compulsory mark is

  <\equation*>
    M<around|(|x|)>=<frac|\<mu\>|4><math-up|fineMonitor><around|(|x|)><rsup|2>,<space|2em>\<mu\>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation*>

  Thus <eqref|eq:exclusion-rooted-final> is precisely
  <math|40*u<around|(|x|)>*2<rsup|-m>*e<rsup|-M<around|(|x|)>>>. It pays
  every fixed outgoing gain <math|exp <around|(|-A<rsub|0>*P<around|(|x|)><rsup|\<delta\>>|)>>
  with <math|A<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0> and <math|0\<leqslant\>\<delta\>\<less\>2>,
  where <math|P<around|(|x|)>=exp <around|(|<around|(|log log
  x|)><rsup|2>|)>> is the unshifted monitor. Indeed
  <math|P<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|fineMonitor><around|(|x|)>>
  eventually, the ratio <math|<math-up|fineMonitor><around|(|x|)><rsup|\<delta\>>/<math-up|fineMonitor><around|(|x|)><rsup|2>>
  tends to zero, and <math|40*u<around|(|x|)>*2<rsup|-m>\<leqslant\>1>
  eventually. This proves the outgoing forced allowance from the incoming
  regular/forced prices, with the same scale and history.

  <subsection|Absorbing localized logarithms into the forced
  gas><label|sec:soft-log-fusion>

  The two exponential shifts in <eqref|eq:leaf-tilted-identity> arise from an
  exact regrouping of the forced activities with localized logarithms. We
  prove this regrouping, including its flag constraints and the derivative
  price needed for a tilted family.

  All activities and logarithms in this section are real valued. Fix a coarse
  configuration <math|U> and let <math|F<around|(|U|)>> be its set of flagged
  sites. A hard activity <math|K<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>> has a nonempty
  support <math|X<rsub|i>>, a nonempty inventory
  <math|I<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>X<rsub|i>>, and a fixed eligibility condition.
  The hard factors and their eligibility predicates are measurable, gauge
  invariant and local on <math|X<rsub|i>>, with the same final-flag
  convention as the logarithms below. Nonzero hard values satisfy their
  eligibility condition. The hard gas sums over finite families <math|A> of
  pairwise disjoint supports with <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>A>I<rsub|i>=F<around|(|U|)>>:

  <\equation*>
    G<around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|A<math-up|admissible>><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>A>K<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  The empty family is allowed precisely when
  <math|F<around|(|U|)>=\<varnothing\>>. Each logarithmic term
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|j><around|(|U|)>> has nonempty support <math|Y<rsub|j>>,
  is measurable and gauge invariant, and is local with the same threshold
  convention as the hard activities. Assume

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:fusion-vanishing>Y<rsub|j>\<cap\>F<around|(|U|)>=\<varnothing\>\<Longrightarrow\>\<ell\><rsub|j><around|(|U|)>=0.
  </equation>

  Suppose there are summable uniform majorants for both <math|K<rsub|i>> and
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>>. These hypotheses will also imply absolute
  convergence of the new gas; it need not be assumed separately.

  Use a disjoint union of the hard and logarithmic labels, assigning the
  factors

  <\equation*>
    r<rsub|i>=K<rsub|i>,<space|2em>r<rsub|j>=e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|j>>-1
  </equation*>

  and the supports <math|X<rsub|i>>, <math|Y<rsub|j>>, respectively. A new
  activity label <math|C> is a finite connected set of these labels which
  contains at least one hard label and whose hard supports are pairwise
  disjoint. Write <math|Z<rsub|C>> for the union of all its supports and
  <math|J<rsub|C>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>C<math-up|hard>>I<rsub|i>> for its
  inventory. The new activity is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:fusion-new-activity><wide|K|^><rsub|C><around|(|U|)>=\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|Z<rsub|C>\<cap\>F<around|(|U|)>=J<rsub|C>|}>>*\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|<text|all
    hard labels in >C<text| are eligible at
    >U|}>>*<big|prod><rsub|v\<in\>C>r<rsub|v><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation>

  Its support and inventory are fixed by <math|C> before <math|U> is chosen.
  Its inventory is nonempty. The extra eligibility test is exactly the
  conjunction of the original tests, not a relaxed test. The new gas
  <math|<wide|G|^>> uses pairwise disjoint <math|Z<rsub|C>> and exact
  coverage of <math|F<around|(|U|)>> by the <math|J<rsub|C>>.

  <subsubsection|The exact compatible-family bijection>

  The claimed identity is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:fusion-gas-identity><wide|G|^><around|(|U|)>=G<around|(|U|)>*exp
    <around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|j>\<ell\><rsub|j><around|(|U|)>|)>.
  </equation>

  First expand the right side as a sum over a finite admissible hard family
  <math|A> and a finite set <math|B> of logarithmic labels:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:fusion-expanded><big|sum><rsub|A<math-up|admissible>><big|sum><rsub|B<math-up|finite>><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>A>K<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>B><around|(|e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|j><around|(|U|)>>-1|)>.
  </equation>

  To justify this, absolute summability of
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|j><around|(|U|)>> implies absolute summability of
  <math|e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|j><around|(|U|)>>-1>. Outside finitely many
  indices <math|<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|j><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>1>,
  and <math|<around|\||e<rsup|x>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>2<around|\||x|\|>> there.
  Finite products <math|<big|prod><rsub|j><around|[|1+<around|(|e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|j>>-1|)>|]>>
  equal the corresponding exponentials; their expansions converge absolutely,
  dominated by <math|<big|prod><rsub|j><around|(|1+<around|\||e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|j>>-1|\|>|)>>.
  Passing to the limit proves the logarithmic part of
  <eqref|eq:fusion-expanded>. The hard sum is absolutely dominated by
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|i><around|(|1+<around|\||K<rsub|i>|\|>|)>>, so the
  double sum is also absolutely convergent.

  Consider a nonzero term of <eqref|eq:fusion-expanded> and split
  <math|A\<sqcup\>B> into connected components of its support intersection
  graph. Every component contains a hard label. In fact, if it contains a
  logarithmic label <math|j>, its nonzero factor implies
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|j><around|(|U|)>\<ne\>0>. By
  <eqref|eq:fusion-vanishing>, some flagged site lies in <math|Y<rsub|j>>.
  Exact coverage of all flags by the old hard inventories places that site in
  some <math|I<rsub|i>>, <math|i\<in\>A>. Since
  <math|I<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>X<rsub|i>>, the labels <math|i,j> have
  overlapping supports and lie in the same component. Each component is
  therefore an allowed new label.

  Distinct components have disjoint supports. For a component <math|C>, any
  flag in <math|Z<rsub|C>> belongs to an old inventory <math|I<rsub|i>>; its
  hard support then meets <math|Z<rsub|C>> and so <math|i> lies in <math|C>.
  Conversely every <math|I<rsub|i>> in <math|C> consists of flags and lies in
  <math|Z<rsub|C>>. Thus <math|Z<rsub|C>\<cap\>F<around|(|U|)>=J<rsub|C>>.
  Every old hard factor in the nonzero term is eligible. The product of the
  new activities over the components consequently equals the original term,
  and their inventories cover <math|F<around|(|U|)>>.

  Conversely, take an admissible new family with nonzero product and flatten
  its labels. Within each component the old hard supports are disjoint by
  definition; between components they are disjoint because the whole
  component supports are disjoint. The old hard inventories cover
  <math|F<around|(|U|)>>, and the local eligibility indicators in
  <eqref|eq:fusion-new-activity> equal one. Flattening therefore gives an
  admissible hard family and a finite set of logarithmic labels. No label is
  duplicated between components, because its support is nonempty and would
  otherwise meet both disjoint component supports. The connected components
  of the flattened support graph are exactly the original new labels. This
  proves that flattening and taking components are inverse on nonzero terms.

  The identity follows by this weight-preserving bijection and absolute
  convergence of <eqref|eq:fusion-expanded>. In particular the bijection
  transports its absolute sum to that of the new gas. It also includes the
  empty-family case: when there are no flags, every logarithm vanishes and no
  nonzero new component can occur.

  The new activities have the required locality and symmetry. Products
  inherit gauge invariance; flags and all old eligibility predicates are
  gauge invariant, so the two indicators are also invariant. Measurability
  follows from measurable predicates and finite products. For locality on
  <math|Z<rsub|C>>, agreement of the configurations and their flag data there
  gives agreement on every constituent support and on every eligibility test.
  Finally the first indicator enforces exact local inventory equality. These
  arguments apply to the original addresses and predicates throughout.

  <subsubsection|Preserving the derivative allowance>

  Now let the hard factors be <math|K<rsub|i>*<around|(|\<theta\>,U|)>>, with
  the logarithmic terms independent of <math|\<theta\>>. All addresses and
  eligibility tests remain fixed. Let <math|D\<geqslant\>0>, and suppose the
  hard factors have the joint bound of <eqref|eq:leaf-joint-bound> with
  majorants <math|p<rsub|i>> on <math|<around|\||\<theta\>|\|>*D\<leqslant\>1>,
  and are independent of <math|\<theta\>> whenever their support does not
  contain a fixed anchor <math|o>. Suppose also that
  <math|<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|j><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>v<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>1>,
  and, for an exponent <math|u\<geqslant\>0>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:fusion-site-inputs>sup<rsub|x>
    <big|sum><rsub|i:x\<in\>X<rsub|i>>p<rsub|i>*e<rsup|<around|(|u+1|)>*c<around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>\<leqslant\>b,<space|2em>sup<rsub|x>
    <big|sum><rsub|j:x\<in\>Y<rsub|j>>v<rsub|j>*e<rsup|<around|(|u+1|)>*c<around|(|Y<rsub|j>|)>>\<leqslant\>w.
  </equation>

  Choose <math|\<kappa\>\<geqslant\>0> and define inflated constituent prices

  <\equation*>
    a<rsub|i>=e<rsup|\<kappa\>>*p<rsub|i><space|1em><around|(|i<math-up|hard>|)>,<space|2em>a<rsub|j>=2*v<rsub|j><space|1em><around|(|j<math-up|logarithmic>|)>,<space|2em>\<Gamma\>=e<rsup|\<kappa\>>*b+2*w.
  </equation*>

  For a logarithmic factor the constant joint bound is <math|2*v<rsub|j>>.
  For a hard factor the joint bound is <math|e<rsup|-\<kappa\>>*a<rsub|i>>.
  By the product rule for joint bounds, the product in a new label <math|C>
  has joint bound

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|-\<kappa\>#<around|{|i\<in\>C:i<math-up|hard>|}>>*<big|prod><rsub|v\<in\>C>a<rsub|v>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<kappa\>>*<big|prod><rsub|v\<in\>C>a<rsub|v>=:p<rsub|C>.
  </equation*>

  The two indicators in <eqref|eq:fusion-new-activity> are independent of
  <math|\<theta\>>, so the same bound holds for <math|<wide|K|^><rsub|C>>. It
  is independent of <math|\<theta\>> outside the anchor support as well: if
  <math|o\<nin\>Z<rsub|C>>, each hard constituent is independent of
  <math|\<theta\>>.

  The constituent site price at exponent <math|u+1> is at most
  <math|\<Gamma\>>. If <math|h<rsub|B>*\<Gamma\>\<leqslant\>1>, the distinct
  connected-set estimate <eqref|eq:ope-forest-price>, with reserve one, gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:fusion-site-output>sup<rsub|x>
    <big|sum><rsub|C:x\<in\>Z<rsub|C>>p<rsub|C>*e<rsup|u*c<around|(|Z<rsub|C>|)>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<kappa\>>*<around|(|e<rsup|\<Gamma\>>-1|)>.
  </equation>

  Restricting to labels with nonempty hard part and separated hard supports
  only decreases the nonnegative sum. Thus an allowance <math|A> is
  sufficient whenever

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|-\<kappa\>>*<around|(|e<rsup|\<Gamma\>>-1|)>\<leqslant\>A.
  </equation*>

  All the properties in <eqref|eq:leaf-season-bounds> are preserved, with the
  same anchor and insertion debt <math|D> and with new site price <math|A>.
  Applying Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:leaf-expansion> then gives marked
  forced cost <math|D*A> on the fused alphabet. The original derivative
  parameter is never rescaled when a logarithmic contribution is absorbed.

  For uniform absolute convergence in this argument, the finite lattice and
  nonempty supports imply summability of <math|p<rsub|i>> and
  <math|v<rsub|j>> from <eqref|eq:fusion-site-inputs>. The same estimate and
  <eqref|eq:fusion-site-output> give summability of <math|p<rsub|C>>. Thus
  the scalar bijection, the parameter derivatives, and the site bounds refer
  to the same series. Applying this construction successively to the two
  localized logarithmic corrections proves the two gas shift identities used
  in <eqref|eq:leaf-tilted-identity>, provided their actual corrections
  satisfy <eqref|eq:fusion-vanishing>, the fixed-address conditions and the
  purchased bounds above. Producing those corrections from the nonlinear
  block integral, and paying these inequalities on the selected history,
  remain the analytic inputs.

  <subsection|The marked block integral on the original activity
  addresses><label|sec:marked-block-integral>

  We now obtain the exact tilted identity used in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:leaf-expansion>. The insertion is
  transported through the same integral and the same connected-component
  decomposition as the background. There is no division by a forced activity,
  which may vanish, and the distinguished activity address remains fixed.

  <subsubsection|The fine inserted density>

  Write the fine density as

  <\equation*>
    \<rho\><around|(|U|)>=e<rsup|E<around|(|U|)>>*<big|sum><rsub|A<math-up|admissible>><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>A>K<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  Choose a bounded real measurable gauge-invariant function <math|h>, with
  <math|<around|\||h|\|>\<leqslant\>w>, supported on the eligibility
  condition of one fixed activity address. Its dependence on the
  configuration is confined to that address's full inspected support. There
  are two possibilities. For a regular address <math|b>, its background
  regular activity is identically zero and <math|h> vanishes outside the
  healthy set for its support. For a forced address <math|b>, <math|h>
  vanishes whenever that forced address fails its original eligibility test.
  Define

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:marked-fine-density>f<rsub|h><around|(|U|)>=e<rsup|E<around|(|U|)>>*<big|sum><rsub|A<math-up|admissible>>B<rsub|h>*<around|(|A,U|)>,<space|2em>B<rsub|h>*<around|(|A,U|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|h<around|(|U|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>A>K<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>,>|<cell|b<text|
    regular>,>>|<row|<cell|\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|b\<in\>A|}>>*h<around|(|U|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>A\<setminus\><around|{|b|}>>K<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>,>|<cell|b<text|
    forced>.>>>>>
  </equation>

  The admissibility tests, including exact flag coverage, are those of the
  background. In the forced case the address is replaced even when its
  original value <math|K<rsub|b><around|(|U|)>> is zero.

  Here and below use the same funded sector data and all-species integrated
  product bounds as in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-reassembly>.
  On the fixed finite lattice, choose summable uniform prices
  <math|p<rsub|i>\<geqslant\><around|\||K<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>|\|>>. In the
  regular case the absolute family sum in <eqref|eq:marked-fine-density> is
  bounded by <math|w*<big|prod><rsub|i><around|(|1+p<rsub|i>|)>>. In the
  forced case replace <math|p<rsub|b>> by <math|w> and use the same product
  bound. Thus the series converges uniformly and absolutely. The fine
  exponent has a bounded measurable envelope, so <math|f<rsub|h>> is bounded
  and measurable. Gauge invariance follows termwise from the invariance of
  <math|h>, the activities and the flag tests. In particular the fine Haar
  integral and every fixed transition slice integral exist. This argument
  does not require a sign for <math|h>.

  <subsubsection|One marked factor in the Gaussian expansion>

  Denote the elementary alphabet of the unmarked expansion by
  <math|\<cal-I\>> and the letter assigned to <math|b> by <math|\<star\>>.
  Its support is the same enlarged inspected support as the original address.
  For one sector, one tag assignment and one old Haar configuration, let
  <math|v<rsub|i><around|(|z|)>> be the elementary factors of the Gaussian
  expansion. The marked factor is

  <\equation*>
    H<around|(|z|)>=h<around|(|U<around|(|z|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>\<zeta\><rsub|\<star\>><around|(|z|)>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|\<star\>>\<leqslant\>1,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<zeta\><rsub|\<star\>>> is the larger local fluctuation cutoff
  on the marked support. For a finite set of letters <math|P> put

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:marked-letter-product>\<Psi\><rsub|h>*<around|(|P,z|)>=\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|\<star\>\<in\>P|}>>*H<around|(|z|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>P\<setminus\><around|{|\<star\>|}>>v<rsub|i><around|(|z|)>.
  </equation>

  The cutoff makes the marked factor part of the same local integral
  construction. On the nonzero support of the genuine common chart cutoff it
  equals one: the stronger common small-fluctuation bound places each marked
  coordinate in its plateau. If the common cutoff is zero, both sides of the
  chart identity are zero. Hence inserting <math|\<zeta\><rsub|\<star\>>>
  changes none of the represented fine integral.

  The regular and forced addresses give two slightly different expansion
  identities. At a blank regular address the old exponential-minus-one factor
  <math|v<rsub|\<star\>>> is zero. Every old term containing that letter
  therefore vanishes. Insert <math|\<star\>> into each remaining finite
  letter set and replace its zero factor by <math|H>. Erasing <math|\<star\>>
  is the inverse map, so the resulting sum is <math|H> times the original
  sum. In the forced case, a family not containing the address contributes
  zero. For a family containing it, erase just that forced factor and put
  <math|H> in its place. The optional logarithmic and warning expansions are
  unchanged. These two identities give precisely the two cases of
  <eqref|eq:marked-fine-density>; there is no extra multiplicity.

  To justify integration of these expansions, let
  <math|r<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0> be the summable elementary integrated prices
  already established for the unmarked product construction. Its bound
  applies to every finite set of letters, in particular to
  <math|P\<setminus\><around|{|\<star\>|}>>. Since
  <math|<around|\||H|\|>\<leqslant\>w>, it gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:marked-integrated-price><big|int><around|\||\<Psi\><rsub|h>*<around|(|P,z|)>|\|>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|z|)>\<leqslant\>\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|\<star\>\<in\>P|}>>*w*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>P\<setminus\><around|{|\<star\>|}>>r<rsub|i>,
  </equation>

  where <math|\<nu\>> includes the old probability Haar variables and the
  Gaussian variables. The right side is summable over <math|P>: erasure maps
  the sets containing <math|\<star\>> injectively to all finite sets, whose
  product-price sum is at most <math|exp <around|(|<big|sum><rsub|i>r<rsub|i>|)>>.
  The separate uniform envelope prices give measurability and an integrable
  envelope exactly as in the unmarked construction. Thus Fubini and absolute
  summation justify every exchange here.

  For clarity, the chart and Gaussian normalization in this argument are
  unchanged. First split the free Haar coordinates into the active chart
  coordinates and their complement. On a nonzero sector the chart-support
  lemma puts every active bond in the original exponential chart. The product
  change of variables contributes <math|c<rsub|H><rsup|N<rsub|<math-up|active>>>*<big|prod>J<around|(|u<rsub|i>|)>>;
  the affine rescaling contributes <math|g<rsup|q*N<rsub|<math-up|active>>>>;
  the unused Gaussian coordinates integrate to one with their normalized
  density. The quadratic completion and logarithmic cancellation from
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-reassembly> then apply with the
  extra factor <math|h<around|(|U|)>> untouched. Its local cutoff has just
  been restored to one on this support. Summing the sector partition and
  averaging the original tags consequently recovers
  <math|\<cal-T\>*f<rsub|h>>, with the same scalar overhead
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|O>> as for <math|\<cal-T\>*\<rho\>>.

  <subsubsection|The distinguished connected component>

  Split a finite elementary letter set into its connected components, using
  their full assigned supports. If it contains <math|\<star\>>, exactly one
  component <math|C> contains that letter. Formula
  <eqref|eq:marked-letter-product> factors into the marked product on
  <math|C> and the ordinary products on the other components. The Gaussian
  cross covariances between their read sets vanish; the Haar and tag read
  sets are disjoint. Thus the product integral factors. This is the same
  finite-range independence argument used in the unmarked case, now with one
  bounded local factor substituted.

  Let <math|a<rsub|C><rsup|F><around|(|V|)>> be an ordinary integrated
  component value and <math|b<rsub|C><rsup|F><around|(|V|)>> the marked
  value, set to zero unless <math|\<star\>\<in\>C>. The component and
  flattening maps are inverse on the permitted families. The sector and
  eligibility masks are identical on both sides: if a component fails its
  local test, its corresponding ordinary or marked value is zero; on a
  nonzero family the union of its inventories is exactly the set of flags.
  Absolute convergence from <eqref|eq:marked-integrated-price> therefore
  proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:marked-forest-integral>\<cal-T\>*f<rsub|h><around|(|V|)>=<with|math-font|cal*|O><big|sum><rsub|\<cal-A\><math-up|admissible>><big|sum><rsub|C\<in\>\<cal-A\>>b<rsub|C><rsup|F><around|(|V|)>*<big|prod><rsub|D\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<setminus\><around|{|C|}>>a<rsub|D><rsup|F><around|(|V|)>.
  </equation>

  The marked value has the same locality in the forest as the ordinary one.
  Indeed <math|h> reads only its inspected support, and its added cutoff
  reads only the corresponding buffered coordinates. Forests agreeing there
  give equal values after pulling their free Haar variables back to the
  common full Haar space. Their principal Gaussian covariances agree, so
  integration gives equality too. We may therefore average independent forest
  choices at coarse sites as in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:forest-average-log>.
  For a compatible family the marked component and all the ordinary
  components use disjoint sets of site choices, so the average of their
  product is the product of their averages. Set

  <\equation*>
    a<rsub|C>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|F>*a<rsub|C><rsup|F>,<space|2em>b<rsub|C>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|F>*b<rsub|C><rsup|F>.
  </equation*>

  Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:marked-forest-integral> holds with these averaged
  values. The same absolute majorants apply to this finite average.

  Two distinct compatible components cannot both have nonzero marked value,
  since both would contain <math|\<star\>>. Hence, for every real
  <math|\<theta\>> and every compatible family,

  <\equation*>
    <big|prod><rsub|C\<in\>\<cal-A\>><around|(|a<rsub|C>+\<theta\>*b<rsub|C>|)>=<big|prod><rsub|C\<in\>\<cal-A\>>a<rsub|C>+\<theta\>*<big|sum><rsub|C\<in\>\<cal-A\>>b<rsub|C>*<big|prod><rsub|D\<ne\>C>a<rsub|D>.
  </equation*>

  The terms of degree at least two vanish individually. Together with the
  unmarked identity this proves the exact integral formula

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:marked-affine-integral>\<cal-T\>*\<rho\>+\<theta\>*\<cal-T\>*f<rsub|h>=<with|math-font|cal*|O><big|sum><rsub|\<cal-A\><math-up|admissible>><big|prod><rsub|C\<in\>\<cal-A\>><around|(|a<rsub|C>+\<theta\>*b<rsub|C>|)>.
  </equation>

  The right side is affine despite its product notation. The masks, support
  assignments, inventories and distinguished fine address have never changed.

  <subsubsection|Passing the mark through the two regroupings>

  For <math|\<theta\>> in the interval allowed by the purchased marked
  prices, apply the regular cluster expansion and forced exclusion regrouping
  to the values <math|a<rsub|C>+\<theta\>*b<rsub|C>>. Denote their regular
  exponential by <math|a<around|(|\<theta\>|)>> and their forced gas by
  <math|G<rsub|\<theta\>>>. The exact identities of
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:forest-average-log>
  and<nbsp><reference|sec:forced-exclusion> give

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-T\>*\<rho\>+\<theta\>*\<cal-T\>*f<rsub|h>=<with|math-font|cal*|O>a<around|(|\<theta\>|)>*G<rsub|\<theta\>>.
  </equation*>

  The absolute product and cluster majorants apply uniformly on this
  interval. Indeed if <math|<around|\||a<rsub|C>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|C>>,
  <math|<around|\||b<rsub|C>|\|>\<leqslant\>D*B<rsub|C>> and
  <math|<around|\||\<theta\>|\|>*D\<leqslant\>1>, the affine factor has the
  joint value/derivative bound <math|A<rsub|C>+2*B<rsub|C>> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:leaf-expansion>. Products, cluster
  coefficients and exponential multiplicities retain that joint bound by the
  product rule proved there. This uses the marked-price purchases; the
  unmarked prices alone do not supply the derivative bound.

  Finally let <math|R<rsub|0>,S<rsub|0>> be the two fixed local logarithmic
  corrections absorbed into the forced gas in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:soft-log-fusion>. That regrouping uses the
  same fixed background and gives <math|b<around|(|\<theta\>|)>=G<rsub|\<theta\>>*e<rsup|R<rsub|0>+S<rsub|0>>>.

  We identify the coarse exponent on the same configuration, background, path
  and clipping threshold as the integral above. The regular families have the
  summability, symmetry and local reset hypotheses of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-reset-reorganization>, including
  its side-size and clipping conditions. Let <math|F> be the exponent of the
  intermediate representation with regular terms frozen, and let <math|P> be
  the path contribution subtracted with that quadratic coefficient included
  in it. We shall prove

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|E<rsub|<math-up|coarse>>+S<rsub|0>>|<cell|=F-P,<eq-number><label|eq:marked-output-exponent-input>>>|<row|<cell|e<rsup|F+R<rsub|0>-P>>|<cell|=<with|math-font|cal*|O>a<around|(|0|)>.<eq-number><label|eq:marked-regular-accounting-input>>>>>
  </align>

  Here are the two calculations. All sums below are over the same finite
  coarse lattice and, for the local entries, over absolutely summable
  families. Write the frozen exponent as <math|F=\<tau\>*N-K<rsub|\<beta\>>-A>,
  where <math|N> is the number of coarse sites, <math|K<rsub|\<beta\>>> is
  the retained path exponent before the coupling reset, and <math|A> is the
  frozen regular sum. In the second regrouping let <math|v> be the vacuum
  subtraction, <math|d> the sum of the discarded regular terms, <math|a> the
  sum of the untagged terms, <math|H> the quadratic proxy sum, and
  <math|L<rsub|0>> the sum of the path loans. Let <math|\<Delta\>> be the
  extracted quadratic coefficient. The splitting of the regular entries and
  the path ledger take the form

  <\equation*>
    A=v+\<Delta\>*H-d+a,<space|2em>K<rsub|\<beta\>+\<gamma\>+\<Delta\>>-K<rsub|\<beta\>>+J=P+\<Delta\>*H+L<rsub|0>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|J> is the sum of the lower and upper path corrections added to
  the regular terms, and <math|P> is <math|\<gamma\>> times the masked path
  receipt. These are precisely <eqref|eq:reset-first-split> and
  <eqref|eq:reset-path-identity>: in that section's notation the present
  <math|<around|(|v,H,d,a,J,L<rsub|0>,\<gamma\>|)>> are
  <math|<around|(|c<rsub|0>,h,D,R<rsub|0>,F,J,\<alpha\>|)>>. The kinetic
  difference is <math|<around|(|\<gamma\>+\<Delta\>|)>*\<cal-K\>>, since only
  its coupling changes; the loop term is unchanged. Thus these identities
  hold on every field, including unhealthy ones. The reset uses vacuum
  <math|\<tau\>-v/N>, regular sum <math|a+J> and logarithmic sum
  <math|S<rsub|0>=d+L<rsub|0>>. Collecting the terms gives

  <\equation*>
    E<rsub|<math-up|coarse>>+S<rsub|0>=\<tau\>*N-K<rsub|\<beta\>>-<around|(|v+a+\<Delta\>*H-d|)>-P=F-P.
  </equation*>

  The final regrouping preserves this exponent because it preserves the sum
  of the regular entries, leaving the vacuum and path terms unchanged.

  For completeness, the first transfer can be checked directly on its
  entries. Let <math|A<rsub|\<ast\>>> be the original regular sum and
  <math|A<rsub|<math-up|rem>>> the sum after removing its selected finite set
  <math|I<rsub|0>> of entries. Distribute each selected masked entry
  <math|f<rsub|i>> over sites with its allocation weights
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|o*i>>, for which <math|<big|sum><rsub|o>\<lambda\><rsub|o*i>=1>,
  and put <math|e<rsub|o>=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I<rsub|0>>\<lambda\><rsub|o*i>*f<rsub|i>>.
  Hence <math|A<rsub|\<ast\>>=A<rsub|<math-up|rem>>+<big|sum><rsub|o>e<rsub|o>>.
  Let <math|c<rsub|\<ast\>>> be the common flat value of the allocated local
  reference (independent of <math|o> by translation), let <math|g<rsub|o>> be
  its local replacement, and write <math|\<chi\><rsub|o>> for that
  replacement's healthy mask. The discrepancy used by the transfer is

  <\equation*>
    t<rsub|o>=e<rsub|o>-c<rsub|\<ast\>>-\<chi\><rsub|o>*g<rsub|o>.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|A<rsub|\<ast\>>=A<rsub|<math-up|rem>>+N*c<rsub|\<ast\>>+<big|sum><rsub|o>\<chi\><rsub|o>*g<rsub|o>+<big|sum><rsub|o>t<rsub|o>>.
  This uses the actual masked entries and is valid on unhealthy fields too.
  The local replacement makes <math|t<rsub|o>> vanish on its larger healthy
  support, as required for absorbing <math|-t<rsub|o>> into the gas.

  Write <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<cdummy\>|\<rangle\>>> for the uniform
  average over signed translations of the coarse lattice. The original and
  remaining regular sums are invariant under this average: their label
  permutations preserve values and masks, and their series converge
  absolutely. The new regular sum, vacuum and logarithmic correction are

  <\equation*>
    A=A<rsub|<math-up|rem>>+<around*|\<langle\>|<big|sum><rsub|o>\<chi\><rsub|o>*g<rsub|o>|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em>\<tau\>=\<tau\><rsub|\<ast\>>-c<rsub|\<ast\>>,<space|2em>R<rsub|0>=-<around*|\<langle\>|<big|sum><rsub|o>t<rsub|o>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation*>

  The averaging factor is the reciprocal of the number of signed
  translations, so the constant <math|N*c<rsub|\<ast\>>> is unchanged.
  Substitution gives <math|F+R<rsub|0>=N*\<tau\><rsub|\<ast\>>-K<rsub|\<ast\>>-A<rsub|\<ast\>>>.
  Enumerating the two disjoint label families changes no sum, so this also
  proves the identity for the actual enumerated arrays.

  We next identify <math|A<rsub|\<ast\>>>. Write
  <math|c<rsub|\<Gamma\>><around|(|U|)>> for the absolutely summable
  flag-free cluster logarithms of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:forest-average-log>.
  Their sum is <math|log a<around|(|0|)>> and <math|a<around|(|0|)>=exp
  <around|(|<big|sum><rsub|\<Gamma\>>c<rsub|\<Gamma\>>|)>\<gtr\>0>. The first
  part of the regular alphabet has values <math|-c<rsub|\<Gamma\>>>,
  transported to fixed addresses with the same full supports. Its assigned
  healthy mask changes no value, because <math|c<rsub|\<Gamma\>>> already
  vanishes off that healthy set. The transport is a bijection, so this part
  sums to <math|-log a<around|(|0|)>> without a multiplicity.

  The other part consists of the low-path receipts with coefficient
  <math|-\<gamma\>>, the cubic and quartic atoms with coefficient
  <math|-\<beta\>>, and the loop atoms with coefficient <math|-1>. Each
  retains its original mask and signed-channel weight. The low channels sum
  to <math|-P> and the other three families sum to <math|-B<rsub|\<ast\>>>,
  where

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|\<ast\>>=\<beta\>*<around|(|\<cal-B\><rsub|3>+\<cal-B\><rsub|4>|)>+\<cal-B\><rsub|<math-up|loop>>,<space|2em>K<rsub|\<ast\>>=K<rsub|<math-up|base>>+B<rsub|\<ast\>>.
  </equation*>

  Each <math|\<cal-B\>> is the signed average of its translated masked path
  atoms. Partitioning the path alphabet proves these equalities; the path
  bounds give absolute summability. Translation and signed averaging
  therefore commute with the sums. The cluster sum is invariant under the
  same symmetries by their bijections of cluster addresses. The further
  uniform signed-translation average of the whole regular alphabet leaves its
  sum unchanged. This proves <math|A<rsub|\<ast\>>=-log
  a<around|(|0|)>-P-B<rsub|\<ast\>>>.

  Finally let <math|T<rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0> be the field-independent factor
  in <eqref|eq:reassembly-overhead>, evaluated at the identity with the same
  forest and incoming vacuum. The extracted field-dependent part of the
  inherited exponent is <math|exp <around|(|-K<rsub|<math-up|base>>|)>>,
  whence <math|<with|math-font|cal*|O>=T<rsub|\<ast\>>*exp
  <around|(|-K<rsub|<math-up|base>>|)>>. The vacuum is defined by
  <math|\<tau\><rsub|\<ast\>>=N<rsup|-1>*log T<rsub|\<ast\>>>;
  <math|N\<gtr\>0> and <math|T<rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0> justify this
  definition. Writing <math|v<rsub|\<ast\>>=N*\<tau\><rsub|\<ast\>>> now
  gives

  <\equation*>
    F+R<rsub|0>-P=v<rsub|\<ast\>>-K<rsub|<math-up|base>>-B<rsub|\<ast\>>-A<rsub|\<ast\>>-P=v<rsub|\<ast\>>-K<rsub|<math-up|base>>+log
    a<around|(|0|)>.
  </equation*>

  Exponentiating proves <eqref|eq:marked-regular-accounting-input>. Every
  reindexing above preserves the original threshold and path; in particular
  the receipt <math|P> used in the reset is the one just summed in the
  regular alphabet.

  Indeed, adding <math|R<rsub|0>> to <eqref|eq:marked-output-exponent-input>
  and exponentiating, then using <eqref|eq:marked-regular-accounting-input>,
  gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:marked-coarse-normalization>e<rsup|E<rsub|<math-up|coarse>>+R<rsub|0>+S<rsub|0>>=<with|math-font|cal*|O>a<around|(|0|)>.
  </equation>

  With this normalization, substitution proves

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-T\>*\<rho\>+\<theta\>*\<cal-T\>*f<rsub|h>=e<rsup|E<rsub|<math-up|coarse>>>*<frac|a<around|(|\<theta\>|)>|a<around|(|0|)>>*b<around|(|\<theta\>|)>,
  </equation*>

  which is <eqref|eq:leaf-tilted-identity> for the actual insertion
  <eqref|eq:marked-fine-density>. Differentiation is justified by the joint
  summable bounds, not by a formal manipulation of the series. This
  establishes the exact marked integral underlying that argument. The
  remaining quantitative task is to purchase its marked local derivative
  prices and principal-term estimate on the selected history; the density
  identity alone does not provide those estimates.

  <subsection|Prices and local derivatives of the marked
  activities><label|sec:marked-activity-jets>

  The marked integral permits a bounded measurable insertion. Its coarse
  local extension nevertheless has the derivatives needed for the projection
  argument. The reason is the compensated transport: it holds the fine field
  read by the insertion fixed. Only its smooth cutoff and the surrounding
  integral are differentiated.

  <subsubsection|Paying for the distinguished letter>

  Let <math|X> be the fine insertion support,
  <math|c=C<rsub|B<rsub|f>><around|(|X|)>> its fine cover number, <math|s>
  the fine support exponent, and <math|w> its uniform bound. Its fine mass is
  <math|m=e<rsup|s*c>*w>. Fix the support reserve
  <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, with <math|s\<gtr\>4*\<varepsilon\>>, and
  let <math|g\<gtr\>0> be the normalization gain. Introduce

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:marked-debt-price>D=<frac|m*e<rsup|-\<varepsilon\>*c>|g>,<space|2em>J=g*e<rsup|-<around|(|s-\<varepsilon\>|)>*c>,<space|2em>D*J=w.
  </equation>

  The last equality follows by cancellation of the two exponentials. It also
  covers <math|w=0>. We give the marked letter price <math|J> in the mixed
  component estimate and keep the factor <math|D> outside it.

  Let <math|X<rprime|'>> be its full enlarged coarse support. The footprint
  estimate of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:inherited-activity-prices> has
  the form

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|B><around|(|X<rprime|'>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|0>+<frac|10*C<rsub|0>|L>*c,
  </equation*>

  where <math|C<rsub|0>> depends only on the fixed enlargement depth. Choose
  the block ratio so that <math|10*u*C<rsub|0>/L\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>>,
  where <math|u> is the mixed estimate's support exponent. The marked
  contribution to the elementary price, including the two length reserves,
  then obeys

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|J*e<rsup|2*\<varepsilon\>*c>*e<rsup|u*C<rsub|B><around|(|X<rprime|'>|)>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>g*e<rsup|u*C<rsub|0>>*e<rsup|-<around|(|s-4*\<varepsilon\>|)>*c>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>g*e<rsup|u*C<rsub|0>>.>>>>
  </align*>

  At any root there is at most this one new letter. Thus replacing the old
  price at that address by <math|J> increases both the optional root bound
  and the total root bound by at most <math|g*e<rsup|u*C<rsub|0>>>. All other
  prices and all supports remain unchanged. Applying the mixed
  connected-component estimate of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mixed-activity-prices>
  to this enlarged price family bounds the marked components, including any
  excess-cardinality, support-size and forced-count restrictions required
  there.

  This enlargement is purchased at the same scale as the background. If its
  original root price and <math|g> have negative upper exponent
  <math|q\<less\>0>, their sum has that exponent too. The geometric degree
  factor has upper exponent zero. It is therefore eventually at most
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|f><rsup|q/2>>; this quantity tends to zero and may be
  used as the new tree ratio. This follows directly from the strict exponent
  comparison in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:leaf-expansion>. It makes no
  new choice of a history or a fine-to-coarse transition.

  <subsubsection|Holding a merely measurable insertion fixed>

  Fix a component <math|P> containing the marked letter, a healthy coarse
  configuration on its full support, and a smooth coarse probe based there.
  Use the compensated transport from Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:compensated-variation>.
  For fixed old Haar variables and Gaussian point <math|z>, let
  <math|U<rsub|0><around|(|z|)>> be the physical fine field at the base
  point. On the nonzero support of the transported marked cutoff, the
  transport identity gives agreement with <math|U<rsub|0><around|(|z|)>> on
  the inspected support of <math|h>. Since <math|h> is local, the marked
  factor becomes

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:marked-held-factor>h<around|(|U<rsub|0><around|(|z|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>\<zeta\><rsub|\<star\>>*<around|(|y,z|)>.
  </equation>

  If the cutoff is zero, both expressions are zero. The identity holds as a
  germ in the coarse probe parameter <math|y>. The truncation in the
  compensated transport preserves this germ, because its threshold is larger
  than the cutoff support. This is exactly the cutoff comparison used for the
  unmarked transported factors.

  The function <math|h<around|(|U<rsub|0><around|(|z|)>|)>> is constant in
  <math|y> and has absolute value at most <math|w>. The cutoff derivative
  estimate therefore gives amplitude <math|w> and derivative scale at most
  <math|J<rsub|0>*\<sigma\><rsub|\<star\>>>, where
  <math|\<sigma\><rsub|\<star\>>> is the marked letter's support scale and
  <math|J<rsub|0>> has upper exponent zero. No derivative of <math|h> has
  been taken. This observation also gives the locally uniform integrable
  derivative envelopes: multiply the existing smooth cutoff envelopes by the
  bounded fixed function <math|h<around|(|U<rsub|0><around|(|z|)>|)>>.

  Write <math|a<rsub|i>> for the unmarked elementary jet amplitudes and
  <math|r> for their common scale factor. Set
  <math|\<sigma\><rsub|i>=1+c<rsub|B><around|(|S<rsub|i>|)>+d<rsub|i>>, where
  <math|d<rsub|i>> is its length cost, and
  <math|W<rsub|P>=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>P><around|(|c<rsub|B><around|(|S<rsub|i>|)>+d<rsub|i>|)>>.
  Since <math|c<rsub|B><around|(|S<rsub|i>|)>\<geqslant\>1> for a nonzero
  factor, <math|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>P>\<sigma\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>2*W<rsub|P>>,
  as in <eqref|eq:activity-common-scale> and the product argument preceding
  <eqref|eq:activity-integrated-jet>. Leibniz's rule applied to
  <eqref|eq:marked-held-factor> and the unmarked factors gives amplitude

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|P>=w*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>P\<setminus\><around|{|\<star\>|}>>a<rsub|i>
  </equation*>

  and derivative scale at most <math|2*<around|(|r+J<rsub|0>|)>*W<rsub|P>>.
  The same joint warning event can be retained. Outside that event, one
  warning factor has the zero germ supplied by the strict small-fluctuation
  plateau. The marked letter is a regular or forced insertion letter, never a
  warning letter; erasing it leaves the warning set unchanged. Thus the zero
  germ still occurs among the remaining factors.

  Now include the Jacobian and projected Gaussian density of
  <eqref|eq:moment-ratio-jets>. Their joint derivative scale is bounded by
  <math|D<rsub|0>*\<cal-F\><rsub|I><around|(|z|)><rsup|k>>, with amplitude
  one. Here <math|D<rsub|0>> is the upper-exponent-zero density scale in that
  estimate, and <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|I>\<geqslant\>1> is the Gaussian
  coordinate polynomial on the actual local read set <math|I>. For
  derivatives through order <math|j>, the full integrand has the bound

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:marked-pointwise-jets>\<b-1\><rsub|\<cal-W\><rsub|P>><around|(|z|)>*A<rsub|P>*<around|[|1+D<rsub|0>+2*<around|(|r+J<rsub|0>|)>|]><rsup|j>*<around|(|1+W<rsub|P>|)><rsup|j>*\<cal-F\><rsub|I><around|(|z|)><rsup|k*j>.
  </equation>

  To verify the scale conversion, use

  <\equation*>
    1+D<rsub|0>*\<cal-F\><rsub|I><rsup|k>+2*<around|(|r+J<rsub|0>|)>*W<rsub|P>\<leqslant\><around|[|1+D<rsub|0>+2*<around|(|r+J<rsub|0>|)>|]>*<around|(|1+W<rsub|P>|)>*\<cal-F\><rsub|I><rsup|k>
  </equation*>

  and raise both nonnegative sides to the integer power <math|j>. The
  estimates concern the unmasked extension on a neighborhood of the healthy
  base point; they do not differentiate the flag mask.

  <subsubsection|Integrating the derivative bound>

  The Gaussian moment and warning estimate of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-moment-prices> gives, for some
  fixed integer <math|K> and scalar <math|F> of upper exponent zero,

  <\equation*>
    <big|int>\<b-1\><rsub|\<cal-W\><rsub|P>>*\<cal-F\><rsub|I><rsup|k*j>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+<around|\||F|\|>|)>*<around|(|1+W<rsub|P>|)><rsup|K>*\<rho\><rsup|N<rsub|P>>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|N<rsub|P>> is the number of warning letters and <math|\<rho\>>
  is the half-warning factor of that estimate. Explicitly, Cauchy\USchwarz
  uses the moment of degree <math|2*k*j> and the square root of the joint
  warning probability. This explains both the doubled moment degree and the
  preserved half-warning gain. The old Haar measure has total mass one, so
  introduces no further volume factor.

  Integrating <eqref|eq:marked-pointwise-jets> therefore bounds the local
  derivative amplitude by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:marked-integral-jets>B<rsub|\<ast\>>*A<rsub|P>*\<rho\><rsup|N<rsub|P>>*<around|(|1+W<rsub|P>|)><rsup|K+j>,<space|2em>B<rsub|\<ast\>>=<around|(|1+<around|\||F|\|>|)>*<around|[|1+D<rsub|0>+2*<around|(|r+J<rsub|0>|)>|]><rsup|j>.
  </equation>

  The finite-order differentiation-under-the-integral argument of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-differentiation> applies: the
  relative Gaussian density supplies an integrable local envelope, the
  transported insertion is bounded and constant in the probe, and all
  remaining factors have the already proved smooth envelopes. Thus this is a
  bound for derivatives of the actual local integral. The constants
  <math|j,k,K> are fixed before the coupling limit. Products, sums and fixed
  powers show that <math|B<rsub|\<ast\>>> has upper exponent zero.

  Let <math|v<rsub|P>> be the mixed component price with the marked
  elementary price replaced by <math|J>, with the warning gain and the size
  weight <math|<around|(|1+W<rsub|P>|)><rsup|K+j>> included. The exact
  identity <math|D*J=w> from <eqref|eq:marked-debt-price> rewrites
  <eqref|eq:marked-integral-jets> as <math|D*B<rsub|\<ast\>>*v<rsub|P>>. This
  proves the marked analogue of the unmarked local jet estimate from the same
  sector data, rather than postulating a new regularity property of <math|h>.

  To make a globally measurable gauge-invariant extension, first average the
  local projected integral over the original finite tag choices, then average
  its gauge translates against product probability Haar measure. Locality is
  preserved because only the gauge coordinates incident to its read support
  matter. Haar invariance makes the result gauge invariant. At a healthy base
  point, the local covariance and chart bounds persist on a probe
  neighborhood. Change of variables in the local integral shows that it is
  already gauge invariant there. Hence the gauge average agrees with that
  local integral as a germ, and the derivative bound is unchanged.
  Independent forest averaging also preserves the bound. We have constructed
  a measurable local extension <math|g<rsub|P>> satisfying

  <\equation*>
    b<rsub|P><around|(|V|)>=\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|X<rsub|P>\<cap\><math-up|Flags><around|(|V|)>=\<varnothing\>|}>>*g<rsub|P><around|(|V|)>
  </equation*>

  for regular components, with derivatives through the purchased order
  bounded by <math|D*B<rsub|\<ast\>>*v<rsub|P>> at healthy configurations.
  This is finite-order smoothness on the stated domain.

  <subsubsection|The logarithmic derivative and its cluster prices>

  Let <math|f<rsub|P>> be the unmarked local extension. For a regular cluster
  tuple <math|\<Gamma\>=<around|(|P<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,P<rsub|n>|)>> its
  unmasked marked logarithmic term is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:marked-local-log-term>h<rsub|\<Gamma\>>=<frac|u*<around|(|P<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,P<rsub|n>|)>|n!>*<big|sum><rsub|r=1><rsup|n>g<rsub|P<rsub|r>>*<big|prod><rsub|s\<ne\>r>f<rsub|P<rsub|s>>.
  </equation>

  This is the derivative at zero of the corresponding product of affine
  tilted factors. All terms have the mask of the union support. Indeed on a
  healthy union every constituent mask is one; if the union contains a flag,
  each summand has a zero factor, either its marked factor or one of its
  ordinary factors.

  Suppose <math|f<rsub|P>> has local amplitude <math|A<rsub|P><rsup|0>> and
  <math|g<rsub|P>> has amplitude <math|D*A<rsub|P><rsup|1>>, both at
  derivative scale one. Put <math|A<rsub|P>=A<rsub|P><rsup|0>+2*A<rsub|P><rsup|1>>
  and inflate this price to <math|<wide|A|^><rsub|P>=2<rsup|j+2>*A<rsub|P>>.
  On a healthy union each summand in <eqref|eq:marked-local-log-term> has
  amplitude at most <math|D*<big|prod><rsub|r>A<rsub|P<rsub|r>>> and
  derivative scale <math|n>. Its derivatives through order <math|j> are
  consequently bounded by

  <\equation*>
    D*<frac|<around|\||u*<around|(|P<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,P<rsub|n>|)>|\|>|n!>*n*<around|(|1+n|)><rsup|j>*<big|prod><rsub|r>A<rsub|P<rsub|r>>\<leqslant\>D*<frac|<around|\||u*<around|(|P<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,P<rsub|n>|)>|\|>|n!>*<big|prod><rsub|r><wide|A|^><rsub|P<rsub|r>>.
  </equation*>

  For the last inequality use <math|1+n\<leqslant\>2<rsup|n>> and
  <math|n\<leqslant\>2<rsup|2*n>>. Thus the canonical cluster majorant at the
  inflated prices pays all these derivatives as well as the scalar tilt
  derivative. The rooted cluster estimate and its tail then give
  <eqref|eq:leaf-cluster-tail>. The marked mixed-component estimate proved
  above supplies its order-zero counterpart, with the excess-cardinality
  restriction for nonprincipal terms. The polynomial support-weight
  conversion and strict exponent reserve in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:leaf-expansion> therefore apply to these
  actual local terms.

  For forced components use their absolute marked integral price from
  <eqref|eq:marked-integrated-price> and the same mixed estimate. The affine
  joint bounds propagate through the exclusion regrouping by the product
  rule, keeping the exponential multiplicity factorials.
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:forced-exclusion>'s rooted argument applies
  to these inflated prices and gives the summable joint forced-price family
  required in <eqref|eq:leaf-season-bounds>. Every nonzero marked component
  contains the projected insertion anchor, so the derivative vanishes at all
  other supports. This proves the structural and local analytic inputs to the
  marked differentiation. Their simultaneous scale purchases are made as
  follows.

  <subsubsection|One coupling threshold for the marked estimates>

  Fix the derivative and spatial orders, the target support exponent
  <math|a\<geqslant\>0>, and a reserve <math|e\<gtr\>0> with <math|a+2*e> no
  larger than the available mixed support exponent. Let
  <math|\<cal-O\><rsub|n>> be the unmarked rooted component price with size
  weight <math|n>, and <math|C<rsub|n>> the corresponding marked mixed price
  after adjoining <math|J>. Both have upper exponent <math|q\<less\>0>. Write
  <math|C<rsub|n><rsup|<around|(|1|)>>> for the marked price with excess
  cardinality at least one; its extra tree factor gives upper exponent
  <math|3*q/2>. Put

  <\equation*>
    t=<around|(|1+2<rsup|j+2>*B<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>*<around|(|\<cal-O\><rsub|n>+2*C<rsub|n>|)>,<space|2em>v=\<cal-O\><rsub|n>+2*C<rsub|0>,<space|2em>u=10*<around|(|t+v|)>,<space|2em>\<gamma\>=\<beta\><rsub|f><rsup|q/2>.
  </equation*>

  These quantities are positive in the selected construction. The inflated
  regular prices above have rooted allowance at most <math|t>. The forced
  prices have allowance at most <math|v*e<rsup|-M>>, where <math|M> is the
  compulsory forced mark. These assertions follow by adding the unmarked and
  marked mixed bounds, then multiplying the regular bound by
  <math|2<rsup|j+2>*B<rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>1+2<rsup|j+2>*B<rsub|\<ast\>>>.

  The functions <math|t,v,u> have upper exponent <math|q>. Since the
  geometric degree factor <math|4*<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>/e> has upper
  exponent zero, eventually

  <\equation*>
    <frac|4*<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*t|e>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\>,<space|2em><frac|4*<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*u|e>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>,<space|2em>\<gamma\>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>,<space|2em>u\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>.
  </equation*>

  At the same scale the shifted fine radius is at most twice the coarse
  radius, by the radius estimate of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-price-receipts>.
  There are finitely many eventual inequalities at fixed orders; choose their
  common threshold before choosing the finite torus or insertion. The rooted
  bounds are uniform in both, so this threshold works for every permitted
  insertion on every such torus.

  For the second expansion the two rooted contributions, after inflating
  copies and extracting the compulsory mark, are bounded by

  <\equation*>
    4*e<rsup|M>*<around|(|v*e<rsup|-M>|)>+4*t*<around|(|1-\<gamma\>|)><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>4*v+8*t\<leqslant\>10*<around|(|t+v|)>=u.
  </equation*>

  The connected multiplicity argument in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:forced-exclusion>
  thus gives forced joint price at most <math|2*u*e<rsup|-M>>. Its
  differentiated price is <math|D> times this quantity, as required in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:leaf-expansion>.

  Finally, let <math|A<rsub|e,r>> be the polynomial support-weight price in
  that section. It has upper exponent zero at fixed orders. The nonprincipal
  regular tail divided by the fine mass reserve is bounded by

  <\equation*>
    <frac|A<rsub|e,r>|g>*<around*|(|B<rsub|\<ast\>>*C<rsub|n><rsup|<around|(|1|)>>+t*<frac|\<gamma\>|1-\<gamma\>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|g=\<beta\><rsub|f><rsup|q>>, so <math|g<rsup|-1>> has upper
  exponent <math|-q>. Both numerator terms have upper exponent <math|3*q/2>,
  since <math|<around|(|1-\<gamma\>|)><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>2>. The quotient
  therefore has upper exponent <math|q/2> and is eventually at most
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|f><rsup|q/4>>. This purchases the tail and joint forced
  prices used by the actual marked expansion. The final principal-term and
  projection allowances must still be compared with the selected outgoing
  budget, using the same already chosen scale.

  <section|Transfer and stability of local insertions>

  <subsection|Differentiating the nonlinear insertion
  expansion><label|sec:leaf-expansion>

  The elementary insertion is obtained by differentiating a real tilt of the
  same nonlinear block integral used for the background. This matters for two
  reasons. The background normalization must cancel exactly, and the
  derivative of the gas must retain the original activity addresses. We first
  prove the summation argument which makes this differentiation legitimate,
  then account for the nonprincipal terms.

  <subsubsection|A joint bound for values and derivatives>

  Fix a number <math|D\<geqslant\>0> and an open interval <math|I> containing
  zero. For a differentiable real function <math|f> and a nonnegative number
  <math|p>, say that <math|f> has joint bound <math|p> at <math|\<theta\>> if
  there are <math|a,b\<geqslant\>0> with

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:leaf-joint-bound>a+b\<leqslant\>p,<space|2em><around|\||f<around|(|\<theta\>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>a,<space|2em><around|\||f<rprime|'><around|(|\<theta\>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>D*b.
  </equation>

  The witnesses <math|a,b> may depend on <math|\<theta\>>. This is stronger
  than bounding the value by <math|p> and the derivative by <math|D*p>
  separately. Its advantage is that products have no extra factor equal to
  their degree. Indeed, if <math|f<rsub|1>,f<rsub|2>> have witnesses
  <math|<around|(|a<rsub|1>,b<rsub|1>|)>,<around|(|a<rsub|2>,b<rsub|2>|)>>,
  take

  <\equation*>
    a=a<rsub|1>*a<rsub|2>,<space|2em>b=a<rsub|1>*b<rsub|2>+b<rsub|1>*a<rsub|2>.
  </equation*>

  The product rule gives the required value and derivative bounds, and
  <math|a+b\<leqslant\><around|(|a<rsub|1>+b<rsub|1>|)>*<around|(|a<rsub|2>+b<rsub|2>|)>\<leqslant\>p<rsub|1>*p<rsub|2>>.
  Induction gives joint bound <math|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>q>p<rsub|j>> for
  any finite product. A constant multiplier <math|c> multiplies the bound by
  <math|<around|\||c|\|>>. These statements include <math|D=0>. For the
  affine tilt <math|f+\<theta\>*g>, if <math|<around|\||f|\|>\<leqslant\>a>,
  <math|<around|\||g|\|>\<leqslant\>D*b> and
  <math|<around|\||\<theta\>|\|>*D\<leqslant\>1>, the witnesses
  <math|<around|(|a+b,b|)>> give joint bound <math|a+2*b>.

  Here is the precise infinite-sum consequence. Suppose <math|f<rsub|i>> is
  differentiable on <math|I> and has joint bound <math|p<rsub|i>> at every
  point of <math|I>, where <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>p<rsub|i>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
  Both <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>f<rsub|i><around|(|\<theta\>|)>> and
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>f<rsub|i><rprime|'><around|(|\<theta\>|)>> converge
  absolutely. For a closed segment joining <math|\<theta\>> and
  <math|\<theta\>+h> inside <math|I>, the mean value theorem gives

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||<frac|f<rsub|i>*<around|(|\<theta\>+h|)>-f<rsub|i><around|(|\<theta\>|)>|h>|\|>\<leqslant\>D*p<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  Dominated convergence on the counting measure therefore yields

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:leaf-differentiate-sum><frac|d|d*\<theta\>>*<big|sum><rsub|i>f<rsub|i><around|(|\<theta\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i>f<rsub|i><rprime|'><around|(|\<theta\>|)>.
  </equation>

  This proof needs no continuity of the individual derivatives. It also shows
  that the choice of an enumeration of the countable index set has no effect
  on the result.

  <subsubsection|The gas derivative and its support>

  Work on a fixed finite coarse lattice <math|\<Lambda\>>. Let
  <math|A<rsub|j>\<ne\>\<varnothing\>> be the support of forced activity
  <math|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>. The ordinary activities, the support and inventory
  of each forced activity, and the admissibility test are held fixed. Only
  the values <math|K<rsub|j>> change, along a real family
  <math|F<rsub|j>*<around|(|\<theta\>,U|)>>, with
  <math|F<rsub|j>*<around|(|0,U|)>=K<rsub|j><around|(|U|)>>. Assume that, for
  <math|<around|\||\<theta\>|\|>*D\<leqslant\>1>, these are measurable, gauge
  invariant forced activities with the same locality and flag conditions as
  <math|K<rsub|j>>. Also assume

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:leaf-season-bounds>

    <\eqsplit>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|F<rsub|j>*<around|(|\<theta\>,\<cdot\>|)>=F<rsub|j>*<around|(|0,\<cdummy\>|)><space|1em><text|if
      >o\<nin\>A<rsub|j>,>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|F<rsub|j>*<around|(|\<cdummy\>,U|)><text|
      has joint bound >p<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>0,>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|sup<rsub|x\<in\>\<Lambda\>>
      <big|sum><rsub|j:x\<in\>A<rsub|j>>p<rsub|j>*e<rsup|u*C<rsub|B><around|(|A<rsub|j>|)>>\<leqslant\>s,<space|2em>u\<geqslant\>0.>>>>
    </eqsplit>
  </equation>

  The middle line is required on an open neighborhood of zero contained in
  <math|<around|{|<around|\||\<theta\>|\|>*D\<leqslant\>1|}>>, for every
  <math|U>. Such a neighborhood exists also when <math|D=0>. Since each
  support is nonempty,

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|j>p<rsub|j>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*s\<less\>\<infty\>.
  </equation*>

  For a finite family <math|q> put <math|p<rsub|q>=<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>q>p<rsub|j>>.
  Expanding finite products and taking increasing finite subsets of
  <math|\<bbb-N\>> proves

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|q\<subset\>\<bbb-N\><space|0.27em><math-up|finite>>>>>>>p<rsub|q>=<big|prod><rsub|j><around|(|1+p<rsub|j>|)>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|j>p<rsub|j>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>.
  </equation*>

  For fixed <math|U>, admissibility does not depend on <math|\<theta\>>. Thus
  each admissible product <math|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>q>F<rsub|j>*<around|(|\<theta\>,U|)>>
  has joint bound <math|p<rsub|q>>, and an inadmissible term is zero.
  Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:leaf-differentiate-sum> now proves that the gas
  derivative at zero is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:leaf-gas-derivative>\<cal-G\><rsup|<around|[|1|]>><around|(|k|)><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|q<math-up|admissible>><big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>q>k<rsub|j><around|(|U|)>*<big|prod><rsub|\<ell\>\<in\>q\<setminus\><around|{|j|}>>K<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|U|)>,<space|2em>k<rsub|j><around|(|U|)>=<around*|\<nobracket\>|\<partial\><rsub|\<theta\>>*F<rsub|j>*<around|(|\<theta\>,U|)>|\|><rsub|0>.
  </equation>

  There is also absolute convergence before the marked index is summed. For
  joint-bound witnesses <math|a<rsub|j>,b<rsub|j>> at zero,

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>q><around|\||F<rsub|j><rprime|'>*<around|(|0,U|)>|\|>*<big|prod><rsub|\<ell\>\<ne\>j><around|\||K<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>D*<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>q>b<rsub|j>*<big|prod><rsub|\<ell\>\<ne\>j>a<rsub|\<ell\>>\<leqslant\>D*<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>q><around|(|a<rsub|j>+b<rsub|j>|)>\<leqslant\>D*p<rsub|q>.
  </equation*>

  The middle inequality keeps just the terms with exactly one <math|b> in the
  product expansion. Summing over <math|q> proves absolute convergence of the
  full pointed series. This is the marked gas on the original addresses.

  The marked family retains all required structural properties. If
  <math|o\<nin\>A<rsub|j>>, its derivative is zero by the first line of
  <eqref|eq:leaf-season-bounds>. Wherever the flag condition requires
  <math|F<rsub|j>*<around|(|\<theta\>,U|)>=0>, it also requires
  <math|k<rsub|j><around|(|U|)>=0>. Gauge invariance and locality follow by
  differentiating the corresponding equalities in <math|\<theta\>>.
  Measurability follows by choosing any nonzero sequence
  <math|\<theta\><rsub|n>\<to\>0> in the permitted interval and writing
  <math|k<rsub|j>> as the pointwise limit of measurable difference quotients.
  Finally,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||k<rsub|j><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>D*p<rsub|j>*<space|0.17em>\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|o\<in\>A<rsub|j>|}>>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|j>D*p<rsub|j>*\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|o\<in\>A<rsub|j>|}>>*e<rsup|u*C<rsub|B><around|(|A<rsub|j>|)>>\<leqslant\>D*s.
  </equation*>

  This proves the flagged cost <eqref|eq:leaf-flag-input>, with exactly the
  insertion debt <math|D> and the site bound <math|s> in
  <eqref|eq:leaf-season-bounds>.

  <subsubsection|The logarithmic derivative and the exact raw identity>

  The localized regular terms arise from the logarithm of the positive
  normalization. Write this normalization as

  <\equation*>
    a*<around|(|\<theta\>,U|)>=exp <around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i>\<ell\><rsub|i>*<around|(|\<theta\>,U|)>|)>,<space|2em>a<rsub|0><around|(|U|)>=a*<around|(|0,U|)>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation*>

  Suppose that each logarithmic term has a summable joint majorant, uniformly
  for <math|\<theta\>> near zero, and that its derivative is the localized
  insertion

  <\equation*>
    \<ell\><rsub|i><rprime|'>*<around|(|0,U|)>=\<chi\><rsub|X<rsub|i>><around|(|U|)>*h<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  The product bound above applies, in particular, to each connected cluster
  term <math|c*<big|prod><rsub|j>f<rsub|j><around|(|\<theta\>|)>/r>!: its
  majorant is <math|<around|\||c|\|>*<big|prod><rsub|j>p<rsub|j>/r>!. Thus an
  absolutely summable connected-cluster majorant justifies differentiating
  the logarithmic expansion. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:leaf-differentiate-sum>
  and the chain rule give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:leaf-log-derivative>a<rprime|'>*<around|(|0,U|)>=a<rsub|0><around|(|U|)>*z<around|(|U|)>,<space|2em>z<around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i>\<chi\><rsub|X<rsub|i>><around|(|U|)>*h<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation>

  The exact tilted block identity, after the two activity regroupings, has
  the form

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:leaf-tilted-identity>\<cal-T\>*\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|fine>><around|(|U|)>+\<theta\>*\<cal-T\>*f<around|(|U|)>=e<rsup|E<around|(|U|)>>*<frac|a*<around|(|\<theta\>,U|)>|a<rsub|0><around|(|U|)>>*<space|0.17em>b*<around|(|\<theta\>,U|)>,<space|2em>b*<around|(|0,U|)>=\<cal-G\><around|(|K|)><around|(|U|)>,
  </equation>

  where <math|b> is the gas of <math|F<around|(|\<theta\>|)>>. All quantities
  use the same background and history. This identity is required for the
  actual block integral, for <math|<around|\||\<theta\>|\|>*D\<leqslant\>1>.
  Differentiating it, using <eqref|eq:leaf-gas-derivative> and
  <eqref|eq:leaf-log-derivative>, yields

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-T\>*f<around|(|U|)>=e<rsup|E<around|(|U|)>>*\<cal-G\><around|(|K|)><around|(|U|)>*z<around|(|U|)>+e<rsup|E<around|(|U|)>>*\<cal-G\><rsup|<around|[|1|]>><around|(|k|)><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  As <math|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|coarse>>=e<rsup|E>*\<cal-G\><around|(|K|)>>,
  this is exactly <eqref|eq:leaf-raw-identity>. In particular, the derivative
  of the normalization is multiplied by the full coarse density. Omitting
  <math|a<rsub|0><rsup|-1>> before differentiation would give an incorrect
  factor.

  The normalization cancellation in <eqref|eq:leaf-tilted-identity> is
  algebraic, but uses exact identities. To spell it out, let
  <math|G<rsub|\<theta\>>> be the gas before the two regroupings, whose
  logarithmic shifts are <math|R> and <math|S>. Their identities read

  <\equation*>
    b<around|(|\<theta\>|)>=G<rsub|\<theta\>>*e<rsup|R+S>,<space|2em>e<rsup|E+R+S>=<with|math-font|cal*|O>a<rsub|0>,<space|2em><with|math-font|cal*|O>a<around|(|\<theta\>|)>*G<rsub|\<theta\>>=\<cal-T\>*\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|fine>>+\<theta\>*\<cal-T\>*f.
  </equation*>

  Substitution proves <eqref|eq:leaf-tilted-identity>. The two exact gas
  regroupings and their numerical majorants are inputs here, as is the exact
  block expansion in the last identity. The middle identity is
  <eqref|eq:marked-coarse-normalization>: proved there by the output-exponent
  and regular-accounting identities <eqref|eq:marked-output-exponent-input>
  and <eqref|eq:marked-regular-accounting-input> on the same data. Their
  application must retain the same support, inventory and flag data. The
  scalar differentiation argument does not by itself establish the additional
  transferred singleton-profile identity required in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:leaf-transfer>.

  <subsubsection|Separating the principal term from the tail>

  Here is the quantitative bookkeeping for the nonprincipal regular terms. It
  identifies precisely which connected estimates are needed. Let <math|j> be
  the derivative order, <math|p> the spatial weight exponent, <math|B> the
  cover radius, and <math|a\<geqslant\>0>, <math|e\<gtr\>0> the support
  exponent and its reserve. Put

  <\equation*>
    r=p*j,<space|2em>\<Phi\><around|(|C|)>=<around|[|\<sigma\>*<around|(|1+C|)>|]><rsup|r>,<space|2em>P<rsub|e,r>=e<rsup|-r>*r!exp
    <around|(|e|)>,<space|2em>A<rsub|e,r>=\<sigma\><rsup|r>*P<rsub|e,r>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<sigma\>=4*<around|(|2*B+1|)>\<gtr\>0> and <math|e<rsup|-r>>
  denotes the inverse power of the reserve <math|e>. The exponential series
  gives <math|<around|[|e*<around|(|1+C|)>|]><rsup|r>/r!\<leqslant\>exp
  <around|(|e*<around|(|1+C|)>|)>> for <math|C\<geqslant\>0>. Consequently

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:leaf-polynomial-reserve>\<Phi\><around|(|C|)>*e<rsup|a*C>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|e,r>*e<rsup|<around|(|a+e|)>*C>.
  </equation>

  The localized logarithmic terms have a nonnegative integer order. An
  order-zero term corresponds to a single thin molecule; the higher orders
  are connected clusters. Terms which do not contain the insertion vanish.
  The remaining terms have supports containing the chosen anchor <math|o>.
  Write <math|C<rsub|i>> for their cover numbers and
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0> for their cluster majorants. Suppose
  the connected estimate gives, for every <math|z\<geqslant\>0> and every
  site <math|x>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:leaf-cluster-tail><big|sum><rsub|i:<space|0.17em>x\<in\>X<rsub|i>,<space|0.17em><math-up|ord><around|(|i|)>\<geqslant\>z>\<omega\><rsub|i>*e<rsup|<around|(|a+e|)>*C<rsub|i>>\<leqslant\>t*\<gamma\><rsup|z>*<around|(|1-\<gamma\>|)><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>\<gamma\>\<less\>1.
  </equation>

  For positive-order terms the derivative estimate is
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|h<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>D*\<omega\><rsub|i>*\<Phi\><around|(|C<rsub|i>|)>>.
  Keeping only terms containing the insertion, using <math|o\<in\>X<rsub|i>>,
  and applying <eqref|eq:leaf-polynomial-reserve> and
  <eqref|eq:leaf-cluster-tail> at <math|z=1> gives the total bound

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:leaf-positive-tail><big|sum><rsub|<math-up|ord><around|(|i|)>\<geqslant\>1>e<rsup|a*C<rsub|i>><around|\<\|\|\>|h<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>D*A<rsub|e,r>*<space|0.17em>t*\<gamma\>*<around|(|1-\<gamma\>|)><rsup|-1>.
  </equation>

  Order zero must be treated separately, because its single-insertion
  molecule is the principal term. If an order-zero molecule containing the
  insertion is not principal, it has at least two elements. Indeed, a
  singleton containing the marked element consists exactly of that element.
  Thus its excess cardinality is at least one. Let <math|v<rsub|i>> be its
  nonnegative marked-molecule majorant, with the restriction that excess
  cardinality is at least one. The mixed marked estimate needed here is

  <\equation*>
    sup<rsub|x> <big|sum><rsub|i:<space|0.17em>x\<in\>X<rsub|i>>v<rsub|i>*e<rsup|<around|(|a+e|)>*C<rsub|i>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  Assume the order-zero derivative bound <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|h<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>D*B<rsub|\<ast\>>*v<rsub|i>*\<Phi\><around|(|C<rsub|i>|)>>
  for a nonnegative derivative price <math|B<rsub|\<ast\>>>. The
  molecule-to-order-zero-cluster map is injective, so no multiplicity is lost
  in the sum. Applying the reserve inequality and the site estimate at
  <math|o> proves

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-up|ord><around|(|i|)>=0>>|<row|<cell|i<math-up|nonprincipal>>>>>>>e<rsup|a*C<rsub|i>><around|\<\|\|\>|h<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>D*B<rsub|\<ast\>>*A<rsub|e,r>*C<rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  Together with <eqref|eq:leaf-positive-tail>, this gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:leaf-tail-produced>M\<leqslant\>D*A<rsub|e,r>*<around*|(|B<rsub|\<ast\>>*C<rsub|1>+<frac|t*\<gamma\>|1-\<gamma\>>|)>.
  </equation>

  To obtain the reserved mass of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:leaf-transfer>,
  take <math|a> at least its reserved support exponent. The same calculation
  with a larger <math|a> continues to apply if the displayed estimates have
  that reserve.

  In the actual insertion normalization, <math|D=m*exp
  <around|(|-\<varepsilon\>*C<rsub|<math-up|fine>>|)>/g>, where
  <math|g\<gtr\>0> is the gain and <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0> is the fine
  support reserve. Therefore, defining

  <\equation*>
    T<rsub|<math-up|tail>>=<frac|A<rsub|e,r>|g>*<around*|(|B<rsub|\<ast\>>*C<rsub|1>+<frac|t*\<gamma\>|1-\<gamma\>>|)>,
  </equation*>

  we have <math|M\<leqslant\>m*e<rsup|-\<varepsilon\>*C<rsub|<math-up|fine>>>*T<rsub|<math-up|tail>>>.
  This accounts for both the support decay and the normalization gain; they
  are not independent assumptions on <math|M>.

  <subsubsection|The strict power reserve>

  For completeness, the tail comparison used in choosing the coupling can be
  verified directly from the price exponents. Write
  <math|H<around|(|x|)>=\<beta\><rsub|<math-up|fine>><around|(|x|)>>, with
  <math|H<around|(|x|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>. A function has upper exponent
  <math|q> if for every <math|v\<gtr\>q>, eventually
  <math|<around|\||f<around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>H<around|(|x|)><rsup|v>>.
  Products add upper exponents and sums with the same upper exponent retain
  it: use half the available exponent margin in each bound and absorb the
  fixed factor two into the remaining power of <math|H>. Fixed powers of a
  function with upper exponent zero still have upper exponent zero.

  Let <math|q\<less\>0> be the connected-expansion exponent. The prices used
  above have the following exponents, with <math|L>, derivative orders and
  <math|e> fixed before <math|x> is taken large:

  <\equation*>
    <tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-lborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-rborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|3|3|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|4|4|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|5|5|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|6|6|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|7|7|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|7|7|cell-rborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|<text|price>>|<cell|A<rsub|e,r>>|<cell|B<rsub|\<ast\>>>|<cell|C<rsub|1>>|<cell|t>|<cell|\<gamma\>>|<cell|g<rsup|-1>>>|<row|<cell|<text|upper
    exponent>>|<cell|0>|<cell|0>|<cell|3*q/2>|<cell|q>|<cell|q/2>|<cell|-q.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<gamma\>=H<rsup|q/2>> and <math|g=H<rsup|q>>; hence eventually
  <math|0\<leqslant\>\<gamma\>\<leqslant\>1/2> and
  <math|<around|(|1-\<gamma\>|)><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>2>. Both terms defining
  <math|T<rsub|<math-up|tail>>> consequently have upper exponent <math|q/2>.
  Since <math|q/2\<less\>q/4>, the definition gives, eventually,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:leaf-tail-power>T<rsub|<math-up|tail>>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|q/4>,<space|2em>M\<leqslant\>m*e<rsup|-\<varepsilon\>*C<rsub|<math-up|fine>>>*H<rsup|q/4>.
  </equation>

  This is a strict exponent comparison. An upper exponent zero by itself
  would not justify paying a price from a fixed power of <math|log H>.

  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:cluster-expansion>
  and<nbsp><reference|sec:mixed-activity-prices> prove the connected and
  mixed site estimates from their vertex prices;
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:soft-log-fusion> proves the two gas
  regrouping identities under their exact flag and reserve hypotheses. The
  remaining analytic work is to construct the actual tilted block expansion,
  establish its vertex prices and localized derivative estimates, and
  purchase all these bounds and their stated upper exponents on the selected
  history. With those inputs, the differentiation, flagged cost, nonprincipal
  tail accounting and strict-power comparison above supply the corresponding
  inputs to the elementary projection estimate.

  <subsection|Paying for the elementary projection on the selected
  scales><label|sec:projection-payment-producer>

  We now verify the principal reserve and the numerical projection allowance
  used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:leaf-transfer>. All quantities refer
  to the marked expansion constructed in Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-block-integral>
  and <reference|sec:marked-activity-jets>. In particular the regular
  logarithmic terms and the differentiated forced array are those of the same
  background transition. We retain the incoming sector and path estimates of
  that construction.

  <subsubsection|The singleton identity before evaluating the gas>

  For each real elementary insertion <math|M>, denote by
  <math|F<rsup|M><rsub|j>*<around|(|\<theta\>,U|)>> the actual tilted forced
  coefficient after the two regroupings of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-block-integral>. The index <math|j>
  includes its fixed support and flag inventory. Define one common transition
  map on elementary insertions by

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-D\><around|(|M|)><rsub|j><around|(|U|)>=<around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d|d*\<theta\>>*F<rsup|M><rsub|j>*<around|(|\<theta\>,U|)>|\|><rsub|\<theta\>=0>.
  </equation*>

  The coefficientwise differentiability and domination were proved in
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-activity-jets> and
  <reference|sec:leaf-expansion>. The raw forced coefficient <math|k<rsub|j>>
  in that expansion is this very derivative. Hence

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:projection-singleton-entrywise>k<rsub|j><around|(|U|)>=\<cal-D\><around|(|M|)><rsub|j><around|(|U|)>
  </equation>

  for every original output address and every configuration. This fixes the
  transition map from the constructed coefficient array itself; it is not
  selected from its gas evaluation.

  Let <math|\<cal-P\>> denote the profile map, which sums coefficients with a
  common support and inventory. Its absolute convergence follows from the
  dominated forced price. For real insertions <math|M<rsub|1>,M<rsub|2>> of
  the same type and majorant, and <math|v=L<rsup|d>>, the raw complex forced
  array is <math|v*<around|(|\<cal-D\><around|(|M<rsub|1>|)>+i*\<cal-D\><around|(|M<rsub|2>|)>|)>>.
  The local splitting of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-splitting>
  produces a coefficient field <math|Q> and a response <math|\<psi\>> with

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:projection-profile-retained>\<cal-P\><around|(|Q<rsub|<math-up|forced>>|)>-<math-up|hook><around|(|\<psi\>|)>=v*<around|{|\<cal-P\><around|(|\<cal-D\><around|(|M<rsub|1>|)>|)>+i*\<cal-P\><around|(|\<cal-D\><around|(|M<rsub|2>|)>|)>|}>.
  </equation>

  Indeed splitting adds exactly <math|<math-up|hook><around|(|\<psi\>|)>> to
  the raw profile, and the profile map is linear by absolute convergence.
  Subtracting that hook proves the identity. Its sign agrees with the
  compensating response in the ordinary part of the density.

  The input profile is zero for an ordinary insertion. For a flagged
  insertion it is its inserted value at its original support and inventory,
  and zero at other addresses. Thus the input ordinary part and input profile
  in this transition are precisely those of <math|M<rsub|1>+i*M<rsub|2>>.
  Their fine cost is at most <math|2*m>. Taking <math|M<rsub|2>=0> proves the
  claimed singleton relation for a real insertion, with the same available
  cost <math|2*m>.

  These identities persist under affordable linear combinations. For a
  countable family with coefficients <math|z<rsub|i>> and
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\||z<rsub|i>|\|>*m<rsub|i>\<less\>\<infty\>>,
  the forced-price estimate bounds the sum of absolute output coefficients by
  a fixed multiple of this sum. Tonelli's theorem applies to the nonnegative
  majorants on the product of insertion and output indices. One may therefore
  interchange those sums in the profile and obtain the linear combination of
  <eqref|eq:projection-profile-retained>. The permutation covariance of the
  actual arrays also survives: differentiate their coefficientwise
  relabelling identity and then apply the same absolutely convergent profile
  map. This verifies the marked-profile requirement of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:leaf-transfer> on the selected transition.

  <subsubsection|The principal cluster already has a paid majorant>

  For an ordinary insertion there is exactly one principal term: the
  order-zero cluster whose molecule consists of the insertion letter alone.
  Its support is the selected enlarged insertion support
  <math|X<rsub|\<ast\>>>, and its majorant is denoted by
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|\<ast\>>>. The distinction between this term and the
  order-zero tail is the excess-cardinality condition in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:leaf-expansion>. For a flagged insertion
  this cluster cannot be regular, so there is no principal term.

  Write <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>=C<rsub|B><around|(|X<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>>, and use
  the positive prices <math|t,v,u> and <math|\<gamma\>> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-activity-jets>. The rooted
  logarithmic estimate at order zero, evaluated at the principal anchor,
  gives

  <\equation*>
    \<omega\><rsub|\<ast\>>*e<rsup|<around|(|a+e|)>*C<rsub|\<ast\>>>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|i:<space|0.17em>o\<in\>X<rsub|i>>>>>>>\<omega\><rsub|i>*e<rsup|<around|(|a+e|)>*C<rsub|B><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>\<leqslant\><frac|t|1-\<gamma\>>\<leqslant\>2*t\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  The first inequality retains one nonnegative summand. The last uses
  <math|u=10*<around|(|t+v|)>\<leqslant\>1/2>. In the combined expansion one
  may instead use the common bound <math|4*v+8*t\<leqslant\>u>; either bound
  leaves the same unit reserve. No separate smallness assumption on this
  individual cluster is needed.

  The local derivative estimate already proved there has amplitude
  <math|D*\<omega\><rsub|\<ast\>>*\<Phi\><around|(|X<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>>, where

  <\equation*>
    D=<frac|m*e<rsup|-\<varepsilon\>*C<rsub|B<rsub|f>><around|(|X|)>>|g>\<leqslant\><frac|m|g>,<space|2em>\<Phi\><around|(|X<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>\<leqslant\>V*e<rsup|C<rsub|\<ast\>>/2>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|m> is the fine reserved mass and <math|g\<gtr\>0> the
  normalization gain. At fixed derivative order <math|p> and spatial power
  <math|r>, one can take

  <\equation*>
    V=<around*|[|4*<around|(|2*B+1|)>|]><rsup|r*p>*C<rsub|r*p>,<space|2em>C<rsub|r*p>=sup<rsub|z\<geqslant\>0><around|(|1+z|)><rsup|r*p>*e<rsup|-z/2>\<less\>\<infty\>.
  </equation*>

  Finiteness follows from polynomial growth versus exponential decay. If
  <math|a\<geqslant\>b+3> and <math|e\<geqslant\>0>, where <math|b> is the
  outgoing support exponent, then

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|<around|(|b+2|)>*C<rsub|\<ast\>>><around|\<\|\|\>|h<rsub|\<ast\>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>D*V*\<omega\><rsub|\<ast\>>*e<rsup|<around|(|b+5/2|)>*C<rsub|\<ast\>>>\<leqslant\>D*V*\<omega\><rsub|\<ast\>>*e<rsup|<around|(|a+e|)>*C<rsub|\<ast\>>>\<leqslant\>V*m/g.
  </equation*>

  This proves the principal estimate in <eqref|eq:leaf-principal-input> for
  the actual localized term. If <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>\<geqslant\>B>, the same
  proof retains <math|e<rsup|-B/2>>, because
  <math|a+e-<around|(|b+5/2|)>\<geqslant\>1/2>.

  For a complex insertion the real and imaginary parts have the same support,
  type and mass majorant. Their principal terms are at the same index. The
  triangle inequality therefore gives total principal mass at most
  <math|2<around|\||z|\|>*V*m/g> after multiplication by <math|z>. For a
  flagged input both principal contributions are zero. This verifies the
  principal contribution used in the complex raw expansion, including the
  convention of pairing a real insertion with an identically zero imaginary
  part.

  <subsubsection|Combining the three bills>

  Fix a degree <math|d> and block ratio <math|L>. Let <math|H<rsub|d>> be the
  local splitting factor of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:leaf-transfer>,
  and put

  <\equation*>
    c<rsub|d>=<math-up|rate><around|(|L|)>/H<rsub|d>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation*>

  We first pay the two small bills in <eqref|eq:leaf-small-inputs>. Put
  <math|H=\<beta\><rsub|f><around|(|x|)>> and

  <\equation*>
    P<around|(|x|)>=e<rsup|<around|(|log log
    x|)><rsup|2>>,<space|2em>P<rsub|f>=P<around|(|H|)>,<space|2em>\<kappa\>=e<rsup|-P<around|(|x|)><rsup|1/5>/2>,<space|2em>s=2*e<rsup|-\<mu\>*P<rsub|f><rsup|2>/16>,<space|1em>\<mu\>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation*>

  These are the actual square root of the outgoing forced allowance and the
  second forced-gas price. Eventually <math|e\<less\>x\<leqslant\>H>, so
  <math|1\<leqslant\>P<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|f>>. If
  <math|P<rsub|f>\<geqslant\>64/\<mu\>>, then

  <\equation*>
    <tfrac|1|2>P<around|(|x|)><rsup|1/5>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>P<rsub|f>\<leqslant\>\<mu\>*P<rsub|f><rsup|2>/32,<space|2em>\<kappa\><rsup|-1>*s\<leqslant\>2*e<rsup|-\<mu\>*P<rsub|f><rsup|2>/32>.
  </equation*>

  The last bound has every negative upper exponent in <math|H>. Indeed, with
  <math|y=log log H>, the logarithm of its ratio to <math|H<rsup|-a>> is at
  most <math|log 2-<around|(|\<mu\>/32|)>*e<rsup|2*y<rsup|2>>+a*e<rsup|y>>,
  which tends to <math|-\<infty\>> for every fixed <math|a\<gtr\>0>. Since
  <math|g<rsup|-1>=H<rsup|-q>> with <math|q\<less\>0>,

  <\equation*>
    2*L<rsup|d>*g<rsup|-1>*\<kappa\><rsup|-1>*s\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  The tail bound <eqref|eq:leaf-tail-power> likewise gives
  <math|2*L<rsup|d>*H<rsup|q/4>\<to\>0>. The debt and tail both contain the
  factor <math|m*e<rsup|-\<varepsilon\>*C<rsub|B<rsub|f>><around|(|X|)>>\<leqslant\>m>.
  Choose one coupling threshold after which the two preceding scalar factors
  are at most <math|c<rsub|d>>. Multiplying by this nonnegative mass factor
  proves both small-bill inequalities uniformly over all insertions,
  including <math|m=0>; no division by <math|m> is used.

  The remaining local splitting condition is paid at the same time. Since
  <math|B=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>, the
  logarithm of <math|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*\<kappa\>> is bounded above,
  for large <math|x>, by

  <\equation*>
    C+8*log log x-<tfrac|1|2>e<rsup|<around|(|log log x|)><rsup|2>/5>,
  </equation*>

  which tends to <math|-\<infty\>>. Hence
  <math|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*\<kappa\>\<leqslant\>1> eventually. This
  establishes all three inequalities in <eqref|eq:leaf-small-inputs>,
  including the hit-width bound. The thresholds depend on the fixed stage and
  degree, but not on the finite volume or insertion.

  Define

  <\equation*>
    E<rsub|d><around|(|x|)>=2*L<rsup|d>*V<around|(|x|)>/g<around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>Q<rsub|d><around|(|x|)>=<around*|(|c<rsub|d>+<around|\||E<rsub|d><around|(|x|)>|\|>|)>*H<rsub|d>.
  </equation*>

  The principal bill is at most <math|<around|\||E<rsub|d><around|(|x|)>|\|>*m>.
  Hence both it and the tail bill are bounded by
  <math|e<rsub|0>=<around|(|c<rsub|d>+<around|\||E<rsub|d><around|(|x|)>|\|>|)>*m>.
  The local splitting proof gives coefficient, remainder and response bills
  at most <math|H<rsub|d>*e<rsub|0>=Q<rsub|d><around|(|x|)>*m>. Its density
  is exactly <math|L<rsup|d>*\<cal-T\>*f>, and the fine singleton expansion
  costs at most <math|2*m>. This uses the separate marked-profile identity of
  the expansion as well as its scalar density identity.

  The quantity <math|Q<rsub|d>> may grow as a power of the fine coupling.
  That is harmless for this last projection: its permitted allowance is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:projection-large-allowance>Q<rsub|d><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<sqrt|\<beta\><rsub|f><around|(|x|)>>>.
  </equation>

  We verify this inequality without changing the selected block or insertion.
  The price <math|V<around|(|x|)>> has upper exponent zero, since it is a
  fixed power of <math|1+B<around|(|x|)>> times a constant. The inverse gain
  <math|g<around|(|x|)><rsup|-1>=\<beta\><rsub|f><around|(|x|)><rsup|-q>> has
  upper exponent <math|-q>. Thus <math|Q<rsub|d>> has some finite upper
  exponent, with <math|L,d> and the stage profile fixed. For any function
  <math|F> of finite upper exponent <math|v>, choose <math|w\<gtr\>max
  <around|(|v,0|)>>. Eventually <math|<around|\||F<around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rsub|f><around|(|x|)><rsup|w>>.
  Writing <math|y=<sqrt|\<beta\><rsub|f><around|(|x|)>>\<to\>\<infty\>>, we
  have

  <\equation*>
    \<beta\><rsub|f><around|(|x|)><rsup|w>*e<rsup|-<sqrt|\<beta\><rsub|f><around|(|x|)>>>=y<rsup|2*w>*e<rsup|-y>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  For example, <math|2*w*log y-y\<to\>-\<infty\>>, since <math|log
  y/y\<to\>0>. Consequently <math|F<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<sqrt|\<beta\><rsub|f><around|(|x|)>>>>
  eventually. Applying this to <math|Q<rsub|d>> proves
  <eqref|eq:projection-large-allowance>. The threshold is uniform in the
  finite volume and the insertion because <math|Q<rsub|d>> depends only on
  the displayed stage data.

  <subsubsection|The simultaneous multiple-insertion requirements>

  There are a few additional numerical inequalities on this same transition.
  We verify them explicitly, since their order of selection matters. Fix an
  occupancy bound <math|N\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, a support exponent
  <math|b\<geqslant\>0>, and a positive suppression constant. Write

  <\equation*>
    H=\<beta\><rsub|f><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>\<eta\>=H<rsup|-2>,<space|2em>\<alpha\>=b+<tfrac|1|2>,<space|2em>s<around|(|x|)>=2*<around|(|1+N|)>*\<eta\>*<around|(|N*e<rsup|\<alpha\>>+1|)>+<around|(|1+N|)>*\<tau\><around|(|x|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<tau\>> is the forced price from the second exclusion
  estimate. Its Gaussian suppression gives any prescribed negative upper
  exponent, in particular <math|-2>; this is the price estimate used in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-price-receipts>. Thus <math|s>
  has upper exponent <math|-2>. Since <math|B<around|(|x|)>> has upper
  exponent zero, so does every fixed polynomial in <math|B<around|(|x|)>>. It
  follows that eventually

  <\gather>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*s<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>,<space|2em>e<rsup|s<around|(|x|)>>-1\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>\<tau\><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>1,>>|<row|<cell|\<alpha\>*<around*|(|2\<cdot\>1024*B+1|)><rsup|4>\<leqslant\><sqrt|H>.<eq-number><label|eq:projection-occupancy-payments>>>>>
  </gather>

  Indeed the first and third quantities tend to zero. For the second first
  arrange <math|s<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>log 2>. For the fourth compare its
  upper exponent zero with the strictly larger exponent <math|1/2>. The
  expression for <math|s> is the sum of the two ordinary copied contributions
  and the forced contribution; in particular the factor <math|1+N> is
  retained in each of the three terms.

  The geometric floor required for the local transport is selected earlier.
  If <math|R<rsub|j,i>> is its fixed support-enlargement charge for a
  transition from stage <math|j> to stage <math|i>, choose the integer length
  floor

  <\equation*>
    n<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>max<rsub|i\<leqslant\>j><around*|\<lceil\>|e<rsup|R<rsub|j,i>>|\<rceil\>>.
  </equation*>

  Every block length <math|L<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>n<rsub|j>> then satisfies
  <math|e<rsup|R<rsub|j,i>>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|j>> for each permitted
  transition. Only finitely many indices occur in this maximum. This
  requirement is independent of the coupling and is therefore included before
  selecting the lengths in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection>.

  After the lengths are fixed, at stage <math|j> there are finitely many
  permitted predecessor stages <math|i> and degrees <math|d\<leqslant\>i>.
  Choose the maximum of their thresholds for
  <eqref|eq:projection-large-allowance>, <eqref|eq:projection-occupancy-payments>,
  and the tail and forced payments. Raise the common entry threshold to this
  maximum. Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection> produces
  one realization paying all these requirements. Every later compatible
  realization paying enlarged thresholds retains them.

  Thus the elementary projection allowance can be taken to be
  <math|e<rsup|<sqrt|\<beta\><rsub|f>>>> simultaneously for every permitted
  degree at each stage, on the same nonlinear transition as the ordinary and
  multiple-insertion estimates. With the marked-profile identity, this
  supplies the elementary input in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-local-projection>;
  its finite linear assembly then gives the terminal price there. The
  underlying sector and path estimates are still those of the construction,
  rather than consequences of this numerical selection.

  <subsection|Comparing the principal insertion with its Gaussian
  reference><label|sec:principal-gaussian-comparison>

  The principal cluster is not itself a small remainder. Its leading part is
  a local Gaussian reference observable. We compare these two objects on one
  probability space, keeping the insertion fixed while differentiating the
  surrounding densities. This is necessary even when the insertion is only
  bounded and measurable.

  <subsubsection|The common reference integral>

  Fix a forest, its tags, a regular insertion of amplitude
  <math|\<tau\>\<geqslant\>0>, and a healthy base configuration. Let
  <math|y\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|j>>, <math|j\<leqslant\>n>, parameterize an
  ordered local variation about that configuration, with base point <math|0>.
  The read coordinates form a finite set of size <math|N<rsub|0>>, including
  every colour of each read bond. Write <math|C<rsub|6><around|(|y|)>,C<rsub|9><around|(|y|)>>
  for the actual-sector and Gaussian-reference covariances on these
  coordinates, and <math|w<rsub|6>*<around|(|y,z|)>,w<rsub|9>*<around|(|y,z|)>>
  for the two compensated coordinate maps. At the base point

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|0>=C<rsub|6><around|(|0|)>,<space|2em>w<rsub|6>*<around|(|0,z|)>=z.
  </equation*>

  Both covariances are positive in a neighborhood of zero. Let
  <math|J*<around|(|y,z|)>> be the common compensated Jacobian and put

  <\equation*>
    R<rsub|a>*<around|(|y,z|)>=J*<around|(|y,z|)><frac|p<rsub|C<rsub|a><around|(|y|)>>*<around|(|w<rsub|a>*<around|(|y,z|)>|)>|p<rsub|C<rsub|0>><around|(|z|)>>,<space|2em>a=6,9,
  </equation*>

  where <math|p<rsub|C>> is the Gaussian density in
  <eqref|eq:gaussian-local-density>. The reference probability measure is
  always <math|\<gamma\><rsub|C<rsub|0>>>.

  Let <math|h<around|(|z|)>> be the original masked insertion evaluated at
  the fixed base configuration and its original Gaussian coordinates. It is
  measurable and <math|<around|\||h<around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<tau\>>.
  The exact two integrands are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:principal-two-integrands>R<rsub|6>*<around|(|y,z|)>*\<chi\><rsub|6>*<around|(|y,z|)>*h<around|(|z|)>,<space|2em>R<rsub|9>*<around|(|y,z|)>*\<chi\><rsub|9>*<around|(|y,z|)>*h<around|(|z|)>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<chi\><rsub|6>> is the sector cutoff and <math|\<chi\><rsub|9>>
  the reference cutoff, both smooth with values in <math|<around|[|0,1|]>>.
  The first identity follows from the compensated change of variables of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-differentiation>: on the support of
  its cutoff the new configuration agrees with the fixed one on the
  insertion's read support. Locality therefore identifies their insertion
  values. For the second identity, the reference cutoff implies that the
  first cutoff is one and that the fixed configuration is healthy on the
  insertion support. The mask can then be removed, and the same local
  agreement identifies the unmasked reference insertion with
  <math|h<around|(|z|)>>. Where a cutoff vanishes both sides of its identity
  are zero. Thus no derivative of <math|h> is taken in either formula, and no
  insertion-dependent change of Gaussian measure has been made.

  We state the quantitative finite-dimensional data used below explicitly.
  They are uniform over all the forests, tags and local variations under
  consideration. There are <math|N,Q,S\<geqslant\>1>,
  <math|\<delta\>\<geqslant\>0>, and a fixed <math|M\<gtr\>0> such that:

  <\enumerate>
    <item><math|N<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>N> and <math|C<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>M*I>;

    <item>the entries of <math|C<rsub|6>,C<rsub|9>> have jets through order
    <math|n> bounded by <math|Q> at zero, their inverses at zero have entries
    bounded by <math|Q>, and the jets of <math|C<rsub|9>-C<rsub|6>> are
    bounded by <math|\<delta\>>;

    <item>for <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>S>,
    the entries of <math|w<rsub|6>,w<rsub|9>> and the scalar <math|J> have
    jets bounded by <math|Q>, and those of <math|w<rsub|9>-w<rsub|6>> are
    bounded by <math|\<delta\>>;

    <item>on a common neighborhood of zero both integrands in
    <eqref|eq:principal-two-integrands> vanish for
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<gtr\>S>. The cutoff
    jets on this ball are bounded by a fixed polynomial in <math|N,Q,S>. For
    every <math|z> with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\>v>,
    both cutoffs are identically one on some neighborhood of zero.
  </enumerate>

  Here a jet bound includes the value and the operator norms of derivatives
  of all orders at most <math|n>. The last neighborhood may depend on
  <math|z>; the common compact support neighborhood does not. The
  covariances, coordinate maps and Jacobian are smooth on the compact
  coordinate ball and a neighborhood of the base parameter. These hypotheses
  are the specific comparison data; they are not consequences of an abstract
  equality between the two gas profiles.

  <subsubsection|A quantitative density difference>

  There is a polynomial <math|P<rsub|n>> with positive coefficients,
  depending only on <math|n>, such that, after increasing <math|P<rsub|n>> if
  necessary and provided

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:principal-delta-small>\<delta\>*P<rsub|n>*<around|(|N,Q,S|)>\<leqslant\>1,
  </equation>

  the following bounds hold for <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>S>:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:principal-density-jets><around|\<\|\|\>|R<rsub|6>|(>*\<cdummy\>,z)\<\|\|\><rsub|J<rsup|n><rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|n>*<around|(|N,Q,S|)>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|R<rsub|9>|(>*\<cdummy\>,z)-R<rsub|6>*<around|(|\<cdummy\>,z|)>\<\|\|\><rsub|J<rsup|n><rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>*P<rsub|n>*<around|(|N,Q,S|)>.
  </equation>

  We prove the polynomial form of these estimates rather than leave the
  dependence on the number of read coordinates implicit.

  For matrices use the maximum entry norm. Multiplication costs at most
  <math|N> per contracted index. Differentiating <math|C*C<rsup|-1>=I> gives,
  for a nonempty labelled derivative set <math|I>,

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|I><around|(|C<rsup|-1>|)>=-C<rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|\<varnothing\>\<ne\>A\<subseteq\>I><around|(|D<rsub|A>*C|)>*D<rsub|I\<setminus\>A><around|(|C<rsup|-1>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Induction in <math|<around|\||I|\|>\<leqslant\>n> bounds all inverse jets
  by a polynomial in <math|N,Q>. The exact identity

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|9><rsup|-1>-C<rsub|6><rsup|-1>=C<rsub|9><rsup|-1>*<around|(|C<rsub|6>-C<rsub|9>|)>*C<rsub|6><rsup|-1>
  </equation*>

  and the Leibniz rule give a factor <math|\<delta\>> in every derivative of
  the inverse difference. The remaining factors have the polynomial bounds
  just obtained.

  For the logarithmic determinant, its positive-order derivatives follow by
  repeatedly differentiating <math|D*log det
  C=<math-up|tr><around|(|C<rsup|-1>*D*C|)>>. Telescoping each product shows
  that the difference of these derivatives for <math|C<rsub|9>> and
  <math|C<rsub|6>> is <math|\<delta\>> times a polynomial. For the zeroth
  order join <math|C<rsub|6><around|(|0|)>> to <math|C<rsub|9><around|(|0|)>>
  by the straight positive segment. Since the endpoint inverse operator norms
  are at most <math|N*Q>, both endpoints are at least
  <math|<around|(|N*Q|)><rsup|-1>*I>; the whole segment has the same lower
  bound. Hence

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||log det C<rsub|9><around|(|0|)>-log det
    C<rsub|6><around|(|0|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1>N<around|\<\|\|\>|C<rsub|s><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>*<around|\<\|\|\>|C<rsub|9><around|(|0|)>-C<rsub|6><around|(|0|)>|\<\|\|\>>*<space|0.17em>d*s\<leqslant\>N<rsup|3>*Q*\<delta\>.
  </equation*>

  The two quadratic forms in the Gaussian exponent differ by

  <\equation*>
    <\eqsplit>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|w<rsub|9><rsup|T>*C<rsub|9><rsup|-1>*w<rsub|9>-w<rsub|6><rsup|T>*C<rsub|6><rsup|-1>*w<rsub|6>=>|<cell|<around|(|w<rsub|9>-w<rsub|6>|)><rsup|T>*C<rsub|9><rsup|-1>*w<rsub|9>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|+w<rsub|6><rsup|T>*C<rsub|9><rsup|-1>*<around|(|w<rsub|9>-w<rsub|6>|)>+w<rsub|6><rsup|T>*<around|(|C<rsub|9><rsup|-1>-C<rsub|6><rsup|-1>|)>*w<rsub|6>.>>>>
    </eqsplit>
  </equation*>

  Every derivative therefore has the same <math|\<delta\>> factor.

  Let <math|\<ell\><rsub|a>=log <around|[|p<rsub|C<rsub|a><around|(|y|)>>*<around|(|w<rsub|a>*<around|(|y,z|)>|)>/p<rsub|C<rsub|0>><around|(|z|)>|]>>.
  The preceding calculations bound all jets of
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|9>-\<ell\><rsub|6>> by <math|\<delta\>> times a
  polynomial. All positive-order jets of <math|\<ell\><rsub|6>> have
  polynomial bounds, and <math|\<ell\><rsub|6>*<around|(|0,z|)>=0>. The
  labelled-partition formula

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|I>*e<rsup|\<ell\>>=e<rsup|\<ell\>>*<big|sum><rsub|\<pi\>\<in\><math-up|Part><around|(|I|)>><big|prod><rsub|A\<in\>\<pi\>>D<rsub|A>*\<ell\>
  </equation*>

  thus gives polynomial jets of <math|e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|6>>>. For
  <math|e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|9>-\<ell\><rsub|6>>-1>, the value is bounded by
  <math|e*<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|9>-\<ell\><rsub|6>|\|>> under
  <eqref|eq:principal-delta-small>. Each positive-order partition has at
  least one derivative factor, so all its terms retain a factor
  <math|\<delta\>>. The other factors can be bounded by one times an enlarged
  polynomial, using <eqref|eq:principal-delta-small>. Finally

  <\equation*>
    R<rsub|9>-R<rsub|6>=J*e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|6>>*<around|(|e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|9>-\<ell\><rsub|6>>-1|)>
  </equation*>

  and the Leibniz rule prove <eqref|eq:principal-density-jets>. All
  operations involve at most <math|n> derivatives, so the number and degree
  of polynomial factors are fixed, independently of <math|N>.

  <subsubsection|The good-coordinate region and the Gaussian tail>

  On <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\>v>, the cutoff
  jets equal those of the constant one. The difference of the two integrands
  consequently has jet norm at most <math|\<tau\>*\<delta\>*P<rsub|n>*<around|(|N,Q,S|)>>.
  On the rest of the compact ball, the product rule and
  <eqref|eq:principal-density-jets> bound its jets by
  <math|\<tau\>*P<rsub|n>*<around|(|N,Q,S|)>>, after enlarging the polynomial
  once more. Outside that ball the integrands vanish on a parameter
  neighborhood, so their jets vanish too.

  The reference Gaussian obeys the dimension-explicit tail estimate

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:principal-outage>\<gamma\><rsub|C<rsub|0>>*<around|{|<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<geqslant\>v|}>\<leqslant\>2*N*e<rsup|-v+M/2>.
  </equation>

  For a single coordinate, Markov's inequality applied to
  <math|e<rsup|z<rsub|i>>> and <math|e<rsup|-z<rsub|i>>> gives
  <math|\<bbb-P\>*<around|(|<around|\||z<rsub|i>|\|>\<geqslant\>v|)>\<leqslant\>2*e<rsup|-v+<around|(|C<rsub|0>|)><rsub|i*i>/2>>.
  Sum over the at most <math|N> coordinates and use
  <math|<around|(|C<rsub|0>|)><rsub|i*i>\<leqslant\>M>. No independence of
  these coordinates is required.

  Differentiation under the integral is justified even though <math|h> need
  not be continuous. On a fixed compact coordinate ball the smooth
  density-cutoff factors and all their derivatives through order <math|n> are
  bounded on a smaller parameter neighborhood. Multiplication by the fixed
  measurable <math|h> supplies an integrable constant bound. Difference
  quotients are dominated using the fundamental theorem of calculus along a
  short parameter segment. Apply dominated convergence, successively through
  order <math|n>, as in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-differentiation>.
  Integration of the two pointwise bounds and <eqref|eq:principal-outage>
  gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:principal-comparison-final><around*|\||<big|int><around|(|R<rsub|6>*\<chi\><rsub|6>-R<rsub|9>*\<chi\><rsub|9>|)>*h*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|C<rsub|0>>|\|><rsub|J<rsup|n><rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>\<tau\>*P<rsub|n>*<around|(|N,Q,S|)>*<around*|(|\<delta\>+2*N*e<rsup|-v+M/2>|)>.
  </equation>

  Finite forest and tag averages preserve this estimate, because their
  weights are nonnegative and sum to one. The insertion and the reference
  transition remain paired before taking these averages.

  <subsubsection|How the comparison is paid>

  Write <math|\<beta\><rsub|f><around|(|x|)>> for the fine inverse coupling,
  and use the upper-exponent convention of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:nonlinear-payment-selection>. Suppose
  <math|N,Q,S> have upper exponent zero, <math|\<delta\>> has upper exponent
  <math|-1/2>, and <math|e<rsup|-v>> has every negative upper exponent. Then
  the multiplier of <math|\<tau\>> in <eqref|eq:principal-comparison-final>
  has upper exponent <math|-1/2>. Indeed a fixed polynomial in exponent-zero
  quantities still has upper exponent zero. Its product with <math|\<delta\>>
  has exponent <math|-1/2>, whereas its product with <math|e<rsup|-v>> can be
  assigned any negative exponent. A finite sum preserves the larger exponent.
  The smallness requirement <eqref|eq:principal-delta-small> therefore holds
  beyond a finite post-length threshold.

  Here are the exact scales that produce the comparison data in the
  construction. The local support diameter is at most
  <math|D<rsub|x>=B<rsub|x>*<around|(|4*B<rsub|x>+2|)>+2*B<rsub|x>>, and its
  read dimension is at most

  <\equation*>
    N<around|(|x|)>=<around|(|2*D<rsub|x>+1|)><rsup|4>*L<rsup|4>*<space|0.17em>4*dim
    \<frak-g\>.
  </equation*>

  This has upper exponent zero for fixed <math|L>. The local direction bound
  is <math|2*\<theta\><rsub|f><around|(|x|)>*<around|(|1+D<rsub|x>|)><rsup|p>>,
  of upper exponent <math|-1/2>. Let <math|J<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>> bound the
  unscaled displacement of the two means, and <math|V<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>>
  the covariance difference, including their jets. The construction requires
  the bounds

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:principal-primitive-comparison>J<rsub|0>:-1,<space|2em>V<rsub|0>:-1/2,
  </equation>

  where the numbers denote upper exponents. Here <math|\<beta\>> is the
  actual fine inverse coupling of the incoming representation, within its
  prescribed window about <math|\<beta\><rsub|f><around|(|x|)>>. Gaussian
  coordinates divide the mean displacement by <math|g=\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>>,
  so a common jet difference bound is

  <\equation*>
    \<delta\><around|(|x|)>=<around|\||V<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>*<around|\||+g<rsup|-1>|\|>*J<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>|\|>.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|g<rsup|-1>> has upper exponent <math|1/2>, this has exponent
  <math|-1/2>. Absolute mean and coordinate bounds, covariance inverse
  bounds, and the fixed-order Jacobian bounds are exponent zero after this
  rescaling. The support radius in Gaussian coordinates is bounded by
  <math|g<rsup|-1>*<around|(|5*a*\<theta\><rsub|f>+<around|\||c|\|>*\<theta\><rsub|f>|)>>,
  also exponent zero. The product cutoffs have polynomial fixed-order jet
  bounds: in a derivative of order at most <math|n>, at most <math|n> factors
  are differentiated; the others have magnitude at most one. There are at
  most <math|N<rsup|n>> choices of these factors. The one-factor derivatives
  are bounded by the fixed smooth cutoff and its rescaled aperture.

  Choose the good-coordinate radius

  <\equation*>
    v<around|(|x|)>=<frac|\<varepsilon\>|128*<around|(|dim
    \<frak-g\>+1|)>>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-M\><rsub|f><around|(|x|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|f>> is the fine monitor. The monitor estimate
  gives every negative upper exponent for <math|e<rsup|-v>>. The
  construction's aperture comparison is <math|\<theta\><rsub|f>/g\<geqslant\>\<cal-M\><rsub|f>/2>.
  Eventually <math|\<delta\>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>*\<cal-M\><rsub|f>/<around|[|128*<around|(|dim
  \<frak-g\>+1|)>|]>>; hence

  <\equation*>
    g*dim \<frak-g\>*<space|0.17em><around|(|v+\<delta\>|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>*\<theta\><rsub|f>/8.
  </equation*>

  Each reference coordinate then lies strictly inside its cutoff plateau when
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\>v>. Continuity
  gives the required parameter neighborhood; the cutoff implication in the
  common integral gives the other plateau as well. This proves the tail and
  numerical purchases directly, once the primitive mean and covariance
  comparison <eqref|eq:principal-primitive-comparison> and their absolute jet
  bounds have been supplied. Those primitive estimates concern the actual
  sector and reference covariances; the value-only remote mean bound does not
  by itself prove them.

  <subsection|The mean and covariance jets in the principal
  comparison><label|sec:principal-comparison-jets>

  We prove the extra smallness used in <eqref|eq:principal-primitive-comparison>.
  The two means need not agree away from the flat configuration. At the flat
  configuration their values vanish and their first derivatives differ only
  by the local inverse error. This gives one more small factor for their
  difference than for the covariance difference.

  <subsubsection|Uniform derivatives of the two local kernels>

  We first record why the kernel bounds are uniform in the period. All row
  estimates in this subsection use the maximum of weighted row and column
  sums from Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-covariance>. For a fixed
  derivative order <math|n>, suppose a family of precisions
  <math|A<around|(|b|)>> has uniformly positive lower bound <math|r\<gtr\>0>,
  uniform exponential row bound, and derivatives through order <math|n> with
  weighted row bounds at most <math|C<around|(|x|)>>, where <math|C> has
  upper exponent zero. The logarithmic fields <math|b> range over a ball of
  radius <math|v<around|(|x|)>>, with upper exponent <math|-1/2>. These
  hypotheses hold for the unmasked local kernels under the fixed path and
  positive precision bounds used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mean-action-jets>.

  The inverse derivative formula, in multilinear notation, is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:comparison-inverse-derivative>D<rsub|I>*A<rsup|-1>=<big|sum><rsub|<around|(|I<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,I<rsub|k>|)>><around|(|-1|)><rsup|k>*A<rsup|-1>*<around|(|D<rsub|I<rsub|1>>*A|)>*A<rsup|-1>*\<cdots\>*<around|(|D<rsub|I<rsub|k>>*A|)>*A<rsup|-1>.
  </equation>

  The sum is over ordered partitions of the labelled set <math|I> into
  nonempty blocks. Differentiate <math|A*A<rsup|-1>=I> and induct on
  <math|<around|\||I|\|>> to prove this formula: the first differentiated
  block is the nonempty subset falling on <math|A>, and the induction
  partitions the remaining labels. Submultiplicativity and the inverse decay
  estimate of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-covariance> bound each
  term by a fixed power of <math|C<around|(|x|)>> and of the inverse row
  bound. The number of terms depends only on <math|n>. Thus all inverse jets
  have upper exponent zero.

  The same argument applies to every principal window, with a small reduction
  of the exponential weight when the windows vary. Indeed, at some fixed
  weight <math|\<gamma\>\<gtr\>0> the ordered-partition formula bounds each
  inverse derivative on every window by <math|K<rsub|n>>. An assembled column
  is one such inverse column extended by zero; thus each of its entries is at
  most <math|K<rsub|n>*e<rsup|-\<gamma\><math-up|dist><around|(|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|j>|)>>>.
  At weight <math|\<gamma\>/2>, both row and column sums are bounded by

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|n>*m*<big|sum><rsub|q\<geqslant\>0><around|(|2*q+1|)><rsup|4>*e<rsup|-\<gamma\>*q/2>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|m> bounds the number of coordinates at an anchor. This bound
  does not require two columns to use the same window. All windows and
  coordinate projections remain fixed during differentiation. Symmetrizing
  preserves the estimate.

  On the reference side the coupling is the matrix of the unmasked curvature
  and predictor derivatives, restricted by the fixed keep test to the active
  coordinates. Denote it by <math|D<around|(|b|)>>; its precision is exactly
  <math|D<around|(|b|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*D<around|(|b|)>>. Away from zero this
  coupling differs from the completed-sector coupling: it uses the actual
  derivatives of curved plaquettes and of the predictor, whereas the
  completed sector transports their flat derivatives. We bound the reference
  derivatives separately. A derivative of order <math|j> of a plaquette
  derivative is a derivative of order <math|j+1> of its finite product of
  link matrices; the product rule bounds it by the same finite path-length
  moments, now through order <math|j+1>. The predictor contribution is
  treated with its fixed-ball derivative bound through that order. Purchase
  these bounds through <math|n+1> when requesting <math|n> coupling
  derivatives. All coefficients are bounded by the absolute path sums already
  used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mean-action-jets>. Summing their
  exponentially weighted rows and columns, and then applying the fixed keep
  and active projections, gives an exponent-zero bound for each reference
  coupling derivative. The product rule gives the precision derivative bound,
  with a finite sum over subsets of the derivative labels. The flat identity
  proved below identifies <math|D<around|(|0|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*D<around|(|0|)>>
  with the active principal block of the completed sector precision; hence
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:sector-gram> gives its lower bound <math|r>.
  Its perturbation on the logarithmic ball is bounded by
  <math|C<around|(|x|)>*v<around|(|x|)>>, so it remains at least <math|r/2>
  eventually. Thus the inverse argument applies to the reference matrix as
  well as the completed one.

  The reference panels have a different geometric size from the sector
  windows. For <math|B\<geqslant\>120> they contain every active coordinate
  whose parent anchor is within <math|<around|\<lfloor\>|B/60|\<rfloor\>>> of
  the column anchor. Each endpoint of a bond lies within one coarse step of
  its parent anchor. The triangle inequality therefore bounds the separation
  of any two such endpoints by <math|<around|\<lfloor\>|B/60|\<rfloor\>>+2>;
  the reference middle-panel criterion follows from
  <math|20*<around|\<lfloor\>|B/60|\<rfloor\>>+80\<leqslant\>B>. These are
  the two panel inclusions required here. The inverse-error proof uses only
  this inner radius, not an equality of the two window systems. Accordingly
  the reference error costs <math|e<rsup|-c*<around|\<lfloor\>|B/60|\<rfloor\>>>>
  in place of <math|e<rsup|-c*B>>. Both have every negative upper exponent.

  For clarity, these derivatives are taken with the forest, tags, read
  windows and sector indicators fixed. The unmasked matrix entries are
  products of path derivatives and parallel transports. Their row costs are
  finite sums of the absolute path prices multiplied by fixed powers of path
  length. The fixed-ball predictor bounds in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mean-action-jets> make those costs exponent
  zero. On the shrinking ball, the change from the flat precision is at most
  <math|C<around|(|x|)>*v<around|(|x|)>> in operator norm, by the fundamental
  theorem of calculus. Eventually this is less than <math|r/2>, so the same
  inverse estimates apply throughout the ball. This proves the required
  neighborhood of positivity without changing any sector indicator.

  Both affine sources are products of these kernel rows, their coupling
  matrices and the unmasked residual vector. Their derivatives therefore have
  exponent-zero bounds as well. The residual and its derivatives have the
  fixed-ball bounds <eqref|eq:action-fixed-ball>. A row acting on a bounded
  vector uses its absolute row sum, not the total number of lattice
  coordinates. The preceding product estimates are consequently uniform over
  all finite periods and all allowed forests and tags.

  <subsubsection|The exact comparison at the flat configuration>

  Let <math|A> denote the full flat sector precision, padded by the identity
  on inactive coordinates, and let <math|U> denote its active coordinate set.
  At a flat field all parallel transports are identities. Differentiating a
  plaquette gives its signed exterior difference, and differentiating the
  predictor noise gives the flat coarse response with the same sign in both
  constructions. The normalization of the signed average is the same
  <math|<around|\||\<cal-S\>|\|><rsup|-1/2>> in both matrices. Evaluating on
  each colour coordinate vector therefore identifies the restricted sector
  coupling with the reference coupling. Taking its Gram matrix gives the
  exact flat identity

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:comparison-flat-precision>A<rsub|U*U>=A<rsub|<math-up|ref>>.
  </equation>

  The padding contributes zero on <math|U>. Rows discarded by the reference
  keep test already vanish in this coupling, so their removal does not change
  the product.

  Since inactive rows and columns of <math|A> are identity rows and columns,
  <math|A> is the direct sum of <math|A<rsub|U*U>> and an identity matrix.
  Hence

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|A<rsup|-1>|)><rsub|U*U>=A<rsub|U*U><rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  This conclusion uses the actual direct-sum property; it would not follow
  merely by compressing an arbitrary inverse. Let <math|P<rsub|6>> be the
  sector window inverse and <math|P<rsub|9>> the reference window inverse at
  the flat point. The window-error calculation of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-covariance>, applied to their
  respective windows, gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:comparison-flat-window-error><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|P<rsub|6>|)><rsub|U*U>-P<rsub|9>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|0>\<leqslant\>t<around|(|x|)>,
  </equation>

  where <math|t> has every negative upper exponent. Indeed each error is
  bounded by its fixed inverse-decay constant times
  <math|e<rsup|-c*<around|\<lfloor\>|B<rsub|x>/60|\<rfloor\>>>>, and the
  cutoff radius <math|B<rsub|x>> dominates every fixed multiple of <math|log
  \<beta\><rsub|f><around|(|x|)>>. Taking the sum of the two errors preserves
  this property. Symmetrizing <eqref|eq:comparison-flat-window-error> gives
  the same bound for the flat covariance difference.

  Write the actual and reference means in logarithmic coordinates as

  <\equation*>
    m<rsub|6><around|(|b|)>=-P<rsub|6><around|(|b|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*s<rsub|6><around|(|b|)>,<space|2em>m<rsub|9><around|(|b|)>=-P<rsub|9><around|(|b|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*s<rsub|9><around|(|b|)>.
  </equation*>

  On the actual side the formula is restricted to <math|U> after the
  matrix-vector product. Each source is its coupling transpose applied to the
  residual. The residuals vanish at <math|b=0>: all curvature strokes are
  zero, the predictor is the identity, and all coarse guides are linear in
  zero strokes. Thus both sources and both means vanish there.

  The source derivatives agree after restriction:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:comparison-source-first><around|(|D*s<rsub|6><around|(|0|)>*h|)>\|<rsub|U>=D*s<rsub|9><around|(|0|)>*h.
  </equation>

  To see this, write <math|s=M<rsup|\<ast\>>*a>. At zero,
  <math|a<around|(|0|)>=0>, so its derivative is
  <math|M<around|(|0|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*D*a<around|(|0|)>*h> and the derivative
  of <math|M> contributes zero. The two residual formulas are the same
  unmasked curvature and noise strokes, after restricting the kept rows. On
  omitted rows the coupling is zero, as in
  <eqref|eq:comparison-flat-precision>. This proves
  <eqref|eq:comparison-source-first>. The actual source derivative also
  vanishes outside <math|U>, because the corresponding coupling columns are
  zero.

  Differentiating the mean products now gives the exact identity

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:comparison-mean-first>D*<around|(|m<rsub|9>-m<rsub|6>|)><around|(|0|)>*h=<around*|(|<around|(|P<rsub|6><around|(|0|)>|)><rsub|U*U>-P<rsub|9><around|(|0|)>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*D*s<rsub|9><around|(|0|)>*h.
  </equation>

  Derivatives of <math|P<rsub|a>> multiply the zero source and disappear. By
  the row bound and the exponent-zero source derivative bound,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|D*<around|(|m<rsub|9>-m<rsub|6>|)><around|(|0|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>t<around|(|x|)>*C<around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  This proves a first-derivative comparison, not merely an equality of
  quadratic forms.

  <subsubsection|Transferring the estimates along a local variation>

  We give the elementary Taylor argument used for this transfer. Let
  <math|f:E\<to\>F> be <math|C<rsup|n>>, <math|n\<geqslant\>2>, on a
  neighborhood of the closed ball of radius <math|v\<leqslant\>1>, and
  suppose

  <\equation*>
    f<around|(|0|)>=0,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|D*f<around|(|0|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*t,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|k>*f<around|(|b|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*<space|1em><around|(|2\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>n,<around|\<\|\|\>|b|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>v|)>.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|u> be a <math|C<rsup|n>> chart about the parameter base point
  with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u<around|(|0|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>v> and
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|k>*u<around|(|0|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>v<rsup|k>>
  for <math|1\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>n>. Taylor's formula along the segment
  from <math|0> to <math|b> gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|f<around|(|b|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*t*<space|0.17em>v+C*v<rsup|2>/2,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|D*f<around|(|b|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|t+v|)>.
  </equation*>

  For a derivative of <math|f\<circ\>u> of order <math|k\<geqslant\>1>, a
  partition with <math|r> blocks contributes
  <math|D<rsup|r>*f<around|(|u<around|(|0|)>|)>> applied to the derivatives
  of <math|u> of those block sizes. Their product costs <math|v<rsup|k>>.
  When <math|r=1> this is at most <math|C*<around|(|t+v|)>*v>; when
  <math|r\<geqslant\>2> it is at most <math|C*v<rsup|2>>, since
  <math|k\<geqslant\>2> and <math|v\<leqslant\>1>. There are at most
  <math|n!2<rsup|n>> partitions for any <math|k\<leqslant\>n>. Together with
  the value estimate this proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:comparison-quadratic-jet-gain><around|\<\|\|\>|f\<circ\>u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|J<rsup|n><rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>n!2<rsup|n>*<around|{|C*<around|(|t+v|)>*v+C*v<rsup|2>|}>.
  </equation>

  Similarly, if <math|g<around|(|0|)>> has norm at most <math|t> and all its
  positive-order derivatives on the ball are bounded by <math|2*C>, the
  mean-value theorem and the same partition formula give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:comparison-linear-jet-gain><around|\<\|\|\>|g\<circ\>u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|J<rsup|n><rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>n!2<rsup|n>*<around|(|t+2*C*v|)>.
  </equation>

  Apply <eqref|eq:comparison-quadratic-jet-gain> to
  <math|f=m<rsub|9>-m<rsub|6>>, using <eqref|eq:comparison-mean-first>. Apply
  <eqref|eq:comparison-linear-jet-gain> to each entry of the covariance
  difference, using <eqref|eq:comparison-flat-window-error>. The common
  logarithmic chart from Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:haar-chart> and
  <reference|sec:mean-action-jets> supplies the required <math|u>. For a
  support anchored at <math|o>, its radius is at most <math|D<rsub|x>>. The
  value bound needs the base field as well as the direction bounds. Here is a
  direct local estimate. On the nonwrapping read box of radius
  <math|R=45*<around|(|1+B|)>>, choose the gauge given by parallel transport
  from its anchor along coordinate-ordered paths. A gauged bond is the
  holonomy of the two such paths and that bond. Interchanging adjacent
  coordinate steps reduces this loop to the empty path using at most
  <math|C*<around|(|1+R|)><rsup|2>> plaquettes in the box, with <math|C>
  depending only on dimension. Indeed each path has at most
  <math|4*<around|(|2*R+2|)>> steps; moving each step past the earlier steps
  uses at most the square of this number of swaps. Each swap inserts a
  conjugate of one elementary plaquette. The representation norm is invariant
  under conjugation and satisfies <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|U*V-1|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|U-1|\<\|\|\>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|V-1|\<\|\|\>>>.
  Health therefore bounds the gauged bond chord by
  <math|C*<around|(|1+R|)><rsup|2>*\<theta\><rsub|c>>. The logarithm is
  Lipschitz on its fixed smaller chart, so it obeys the same bound after
  enlarging <math|C>. The period and chart conditions used below ensure that
  this box lifts and that all these logarithms are defined. The
  positive-order derivatives instead follow from the direction bound. A
  common size and derivative scale is therefore

  <\equation*>
    v<around|(|x|)>=C<rsub|n>*<around*|{|<around|(|1+R|)><rsup|2>*\<theta\><rsub|c><around|(|x|)>+2*\<theta\><rsub|f><around|(|x|)>*<around|(|1+<math-up|reach><around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|p>*<around|(|1+D<rsub|x>|)><rsup|p>|}>.
  </equation*>

  For the derivative part apply the weighted direction bound at <math|o> to
  every read site and using the uniform derivatives of the logarithm on the
  fixed chart. An ordered product of <math|k> local variations contributes
  the <math|k>th power of that scale. It has upper exponent <math|-1/2> and
  is eventually at most one.

  <subsubsection|Returning from the local logarithm to the physical field>

  The preceding comparison is made with blank local sector data. We must
  identify its readings with the physical ones; global agreement of the
  fields or of their sector indicators is not available. Let <math|Y> be the
  allocated coarse support, disjoint from the sector's seed set. For each
  coordinate read by the insertion, with parent anchor <math|p>, its
  allocation includes

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|halo><rsub|R+4><around|{|p|}>\<subset\>Y,<space|2em>R=45*<around|(|1+B|)>.
  </equation*>

  The field is healthy on <math|Y>, and the period and chart bounds ensure
  that this read region has the local gauge chart of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:haar-chart>. Fix that gauge <math|g> once,
  at the base configuration. For every nearby configuration <math|W> define

  <\equation*>
    b<around|(|W|)><rsub|e>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|log
    <around|(|<around|(|g\<cdot\>W|)><rsub|e>|)>,>|<cell|<math-up|dist><around|(|p,e<rsub|->|)>\<leqslant\>R,>>|<row|<cell|0,>|<cell|<math-up|dist><around|(|p,e<rsub|->|)>\<gtr\>R.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  The finite set of logarithms stays in its common chart on a neighborhood of
  the base point. Thus <math|b> is smooth there, and <math|exp
  b<around|(|W|)>=g\<cdot\>W> on the entire read halo. No assertion is made
  about plaquettes crossing the boundary of its zero extension. The
  derivative estimates used above hold for precisely this extension: the
  entries outside the halo have zero derivatives.

  Here is why this local agreement suffices. A seed in the read halo would
  belong to <math|Y>, contrary to seed separation. The local indicator tests
  therefore agree with the blank tests there. The forest, signed choices,
  widths and fixed path data are the same. Every entry used by a sector
  window, and every source entry used in its local mean, reads at most
  <math|17*B> from the column anchor. This fits inside <math|R>. In the
  reference construction the middle and end panels and their field queries
  are contained in the same allocated halo, with the extra four sites
  allowing for the bond endpoints. For the reference construction this
  containment can be checked numerically. The panel radius is at most
  <math|<around|\<lfloor\>|B/20|\<rfloor\>>>. If <math|E> denotes the extent
  of a single path query, the coupling read radius is <math|E+10*B+4>, and
  the covariance read radius is this quantity plus
  <math|2*<around|\<lfloor\>|B/20|\<rfloor\>>>. The mean adds the residual
  read radius, the coupling range, and one more panel radius; write the
  resulting radius as <math|S>. The fixed range inequalities give
  <math|S+E\<leqslant\>36*B>. Even an allowed displacement of the query
  anchor by <math|7*<around|(|1+B|)>> therefore costs at most
  <math|7*<around|(|1+B|)>+36*B\<leqslant\>45*<around|(|1+B|)>=R>. These
  inequalities cover the whole local product, including source entries in a
  mean row. Consequently their restricted coupling and precision entries, and
  their source entries, agree after the local gauge change. Equal principal
  matrices have equal inverses; their restricted matrix products also agree.
  This proves equality of the mean readings and window covariance blocks. It
  uses the local tests only, so sector indicators outside the halo can be
  arbitrary.

  More explicitly, write <math|O<rsub|j>> for the colour adjoint of the fixed
  gauge at the parent of coordinate <math|j>. For a mean read at <math|k>,
  and a covariance block between <math|k> and <math|l>, the preceding
  identification gives

  <\equation*>
    O<rsub|k>m<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|phys>><around|(|W|)><rsub|k>=m<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|loc>><around|(|b<around|(|W|)>|)><rsub|k>,<space|2em>C<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|phys>><around|(|W|)><rsub|k*l>=O<rsub|k><rsup|\<ast\>>C<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|loc>><around|(|b<around|(|W|)>|)><rsub|k*l>*O<rsub|l>,<space|2em>a=6,9.
  </equation*>

  For the sector covariance its locality initially identifies the column at
  <math|k>; symmetry gives the displayed row identity. The reference identity
  reads that row directly. Thus the same chart at <math|k> compares any
  requested block, including <math|l> outside the insertion's support. The
  mean identity on the reference side holds for every fixed fine field and
  auxiliary parameter: its local affine source is the same one just
  identified. All these equalities hold on a neighborhood, so their
  derivatives through the required order agree as well.

  A colour adjoint is an isometry, so returning from the local mean does not
  change its norm. Each entry of <math|O<rsub|j>> has magnitude at most one,
  so the covariance entry bound acquires at most <math|<around|(|dim
  \<frak-g\>|)><rsup|2>>. The gauge is fixed while differentiating.
  Consequently the actual unscaled mean difference has upper exponent
  <math|-1>, whereas the covariance difference has upper exponent
  <math|-1/2>, exactly as in <eqref|eq:principal-primitive-comparison>.

  The argument uses the same local histories, flags and reference windows on
  both sides. It does not identify the two precisions on nonflat fields. The
  hypotheses inherited from the earlier kernel and chart sections are the
  common path bounds, their local smooth formulas, and the positive precision
  estimates; these remain part of the selected construction throughout.

  <subsection|Attenuation under the principal Gaussian
  reference><label|sec:principal-reference-attenuation>

  We apply the polynomial cancellation just proved to the reference integral
  in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:principal-gaussian-comparison>. The
  important feature is the order of constants: the coefficient of
  <math|L<rsup|-d-1>> is chosen before <math|L>. Bounds for the nonlinear
  chart and the Gaussian can be chosen after <math|L>, because their
  additional costs tend to zero as the entrance coupling increases.
  Throughout, <math|n\<geqslant\>max <around|{|2,d+1|}>>, the insertion has
  the fine regularity through order <math|n> used below, and its polynomial
  moments through degree <math|d> vanish. The coarse weight exponent does not
  exceed the fine one. The stage smoothing order <math|j> also satisfies
  <math|d\<leqslant\>j-2>, so that the installed kernel supplies every
  spatial difference used in the polynomial decomposition. In particular
  degree-four attenuation uses <math|j\<geqslant\>6>.

  <subsubsection|The first derivative of the reference mean>

  Let <math|u> be a coarse logarithmic field and
  <math|v<rsub|F><around|(|u|)>> the stationary relative field in tree
  coordinates from Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-mean>. At the
  identity the full affine row satisfies

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|\<infty\>>*v<rsub|F><around|(|u|)>+r<rprime|'><rsub|0><around|(|u|)>=0.
  </equation*>

  This is <eqref|eq:mean-stationary-zero>, with the same signed normalization
  on the two terms. The reference construction uses the cut row
  <math|D<rsub|R>> and its cut residual derivative
  <math|r<rprime|'><rsub|R>>. The exponential path tail gives, uniformly in
  the period,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reference-tangent-tail><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsub|R>*v<rsub|F><around|(|u|)>+r<rprime|'><rsub|R><around|(|u|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|1+R|)><rsup|a>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*R><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>.
  </equation>

  To see this, subtract the full identity. The row difference acts on
  <math|v<rsub|F><around|(|u|)>>, whose sup norm is at most
  <math|C<rsub|<math-up|resp>><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>.
  The guide difference acts on <math|u>. Each is a sum of omitted path rows;
  their weighted absolute tails give the displayed bound, including the
  finite path-length power. All inactive coordinates are omitted on both
  sides, and tree coordinates of <math|v<rsub|F>> are zero.

  Let <math|A<rsub|R>=D<rsub|R><rsup|\<ast\>>*D<rsub|R>>, let
  <math|P<rsub|R>> be the reference principal-window inverse, and put
  <math|E<rsub|R>=A<rsub|R>*P<rsub|R>-I>. The reference mean in active
  coordinates is <math|m<around|(|u|)>=-P<rsub|R><around|(|u|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*D<rsub|R><around|(|u|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*r<rsub|R><around|(|u|)>>.
  Since <math|r<rsub|R><around|(|0|)>=0>, its derivative at zero is

  <\equation*>
    D*m<around|(|0|)>*u=-P<rsub|R><around|(|0|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*D<rsub|R><around|(|0|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*r<rprime|'><rsub|R><around|(|u|)>.
  </equation*>

  The elementary normal-equation identity therefore gives

  <\equation*>
    D*m<around|(|0|)>*u-v<rsub|F><around|(|u|)>=E<rsub|R><around|(|0|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*v<rsub|F><around|(|u|)>-P<rsub|R><around|(|0|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*D<rsub|R><around|(|0|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|D<rsub|R><around|(|0|)>*v<rsub|F><around|(|u|)>+r<rprime|'><rsub|R><around|(|u|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The window and row bounds of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:principal-comparison-jets>,
  together with <eqref|eq:reference-tangent-tail>, imply

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reference-mean-tangent><around|\<\|\|\>|D*m<around|(|0|)>-v<rsub|F>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>\<to\>\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C*<around|{|e<rsup|-c*<around|\<lfloor\>|B/60|\<rfloor\>>>+<around|(|1+R|)><rsup|a>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*R>|}>.
  </equation>

  Here and below the restriction to active counting coordinates and the fixed
  colour reassembly contribute only fixed factors. Because
  <math|R=<around|\<lfloor\>|B/100000|\<rfloor\>>> and <math|B> grows
  quadratically in the logarithm of the coupling, the right side is
  eventually at most the coarse small-field threshold
  <math|\<theta\><rsub|c>>. This proves the required first-order
  approximation for the selected windowed mean, without replacing it by the
  exact inverse mean.

  <subsubsection|The nonlinear local display>

  The tree-straightening potential is a linear site field
  <math|\<phi\><around|(|u|)>>. Its discrete gradient corrects the tree
  response to the actual installed <math|A<rsub|0>> response, denoted
  <math|\<cal-A\>*u>:

  <\equation*>
    \<phi\><around|(|u|)><around|(|e<rsub|->|)>-\<phi\><around|(|u|)><around|(|e<rsub|+>|)>+v<rsub|F><around|(|u|)><rsub|e>+<wide|u|\<bar\>><rsub|e>=<around|(|\<cal-A\>*u|)><rsub|e>.
  </equation*>

  This follows by summing the tree potential along a bond: the interior terms
  telescope, leaving its endpoint difference. The root correction is included
  in the same potential, as in the response bound of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-mean>.

  For noise <math|w> in fine link logarithmic units define the local
  logarithmic display

  <\equation*>
    \<Phi\>*<around|(|u,w|)><rsub|e>=log <around*|(|e<rsup|\<phi\><around|(|u|)><around|(|e<rsub|->|)>>*e<rsup|<wide|m|~><around|(|u|)><rsub|e>+w<rsub|e>>*e<rsup|<wide|u|\<bar\>><rsub|e>>*e<rsup|-\<phi\><around|(|u|)><around|(|e<rsub|+>|)>>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|<wide|m|~>> is the colour-reassembled mean, zero on tree links.
  The logarithm is taken on one common fixed ball. Its existence and uniform
  derivatives follow from the fixed-dimensional group chart, the mean row
  bounds, the tree-potential bound and the replica bound. Shrink the input
  ball so that the four entries of the logarithm's argument lie in that
  chart. The derivative of <math|log <around|(|e<rsup|a>*e<rsup|b>*e<rsup|c>*e<rsup|-d>|)>>
  at zero is <math|a+b+c-d>. Consequently, with
  <math|T*<around|(|u,w|)>=\<cal-A\>*u+w>, there are <math|Q\<geqslant\>1>
  and <math|r\<gtr\>0>, uniform over all periods, forests and tags, such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reference-display-data>\<Phi\><around|(|0|)>=0,<space|1em><around|\<\|\|\>|D*\<Phi\><around|(|0|)>-T|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>Q*\<theta\><rsub|c>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|j>*\<Phi\><around|(|z|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>Q*<space|1em><around|(|j\<leqslant\>n,<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>r|)>.
  </equation>

  The constants <math|Q,r> may depend on the already chosen block factor. The
  tangent error is <eqref|eq:reference-mean-tangent>; the other bounds follow
  by the finite product and composition rules and the separate reference
  inverse estimates already proved.

  Let <math|a<around|(|y|)>=<around|(|u<around|(|y|)>,w|)>>, where
  <math|u<around|(|y|)>> is the fixed-gauge local logarithm along the ordered
  coarse variation. Write <math|s> for its common size and derivative scale,
  so that

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|a<around|(|0|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>s,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|j>*a<around|(|0|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>s<rsup|j>*<space|1em><around|(|1\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>n|)>,<space|2em>\<theta\><rsub|c>\<leqslant\>s\<leqslant\>min
    <around|{|1,r|}>.
  </equation*>

  The noise is controlled separately from the health condition. In counting
  Gaussian coordinates <math|z>, it is <math|w=g<space|0.17em><math-up|tape><around|(|z|)>>,
  up to the fixed colour isometries. We use a compactly supported
  <math|C<rsup|n>> cutoff <math|\<Gamma\>> with
  <math|<around|\||\<Gamma\>|\|>\<leqslant\>1> and

  <\equation*>
    z\<in\><math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<Gamma\><space|1em>\<Longrightarrow\><space|1em>g<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|tape><around|(|z|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>*\<theta\><rsub|f>\<leqslant\>s.
  </equation*>

  The last inequality is an actual cutoff purchase. For fixed block factor
  the thresholds are <math|\<theta\><rsub|c>=P<around|(|x|)>/<sqrt|x>> and
  <math|\<theta\><rsub|f>=P*<around|(|x+c<rsub|L>|)>/<sqrt|x+c<rsub|L>>>,
  where <math|P<around|(|x|)>=exp <around|(|<around|(|log log
  x|)><rsup|2>|)>> and <math|c<rsub|L>> is fixed. Their ratio tends to one:
  the square-root ratio does, and the mean-value theorem gives

  <\equation*>
    <mid|\|><around|(|log log <around|(|x+c<rsub|L>|)>|)><rsup|2>-<around|(|log
    log x|)><rsup|2><mid|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||c<rsub|L>|\|>*sup<rsub|\<xi\><text|between
    >x<text| and >x+c<rsub|L>> <frac|2<around|\||log log
    \<xi\>|\|>|\<xi\>*log \<xi\>>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|\<theta\><rsub|c>/\<theta\><rsub|f>\<geqslant\>1/2> eventually.
  Choose the cutoff constant <math|\<varepsilon\>\<leqslant\>1/2>; then
  <math|\<varepsilon\>*\<theta\><rsub|f>\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsub|c>\<leqslant\>s>.
  Increasing the entrance coupling achieves this after <math|L> is fixed. The
  chart bound gives <math|s=C*\<theta\><rsub|c>> times a fixed polynomial in
  its read radius, with the local-gauge constant <math|C> purchased before
  <math|L>. The nonlinear display constants <math|Q,r> are chosen after
  <math|L>. Thus the asserted bound for <math|a> holds on the cutoff support,
  including its boundary. Only the coarse component varies. Integrating the
  first two derivative bounds in <eqref|eq:reference-display-data> gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|\<Phi\><around|(|z|)>-T*z|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>Q*<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|c>+<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>|)><around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|D*\<Phi\><around|(|z|)>-T|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>Q*<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|c>+<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Apply the partition chain rule to <math|<around|(|\<Phi\>-T|)>\<circ\>a>. A
  term with one block costs at most <math|2*Q*s\<cdot\>s<rsup|j>\<leqslant\>2*Q*s<rsup|2>>.
  A term with at least two blocks costs at most
  <math|Q*s<rsup|j>\<leqslant\>Q*s<rsup|2>>; linear maps have zero
  derivatives of those orders. The value is bounded by <math|2*Q*s<rsup|2>>
  too. Thus for a constant <math|c<rsub|n>> depending only on the derivative
  order,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reference-display-remainder><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|j>*<around|(|\<Phi\>\<circ\>a|)><around|(|0|)>-\<cal-A\>*D<rsup|j>*u<around|(|0|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|n>*Q*s<rsup|2>+<around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>>,<space|2em>j\<leqslant\>n.
  </equation>

  The noise term is needed only at <math|j=0> but is harmless at all orders.

  Each <math|\<cal-A\>*D<rsup|j>*u<around|(|0|)>> has the graded
  decomposition of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:polynomial-moment-attenuation>,
  with cost <math|M> chosen before <math|L>. Put the error in
  <eqref|eq:reference-display-remainder> entirely into its last piece. Since
  the fine direction width is at least <math|\<theta\><rsub|f>>, all the
  display derivatives have a graded decomposition at scale <math|L<rsup|-1>>
  and cost

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reference-graded-cost>M+<frac|c<rsub|n>*Q*s<rsup|2>+<around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>>|\<theta\><rsub|f>>*L<rsup|d+1>.
  </equation>

  This operation requires no polynomial property of the error.

  <subsubsection|The common healthy segment>

  For a short fine support, meaning that its cover number <math|q> obeys
  <math|q<rsup|3>\<less\>L>, the whole inspected support lies in the read
  halo of one coarse chart. Seed separation, identical local flags, and the
  mean-locality argument of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:principal-comparison-jets>
  identify the displayed configuration with the physical Gaussian-reference
  configuration on that support, after two gauge transformations. One gauge
  is the fixed coarse chart gauge; the second is the exponential of
  <math|\<phi\><around|(|u|)>>. Gauge invariance of the insertion removes
  both. The equality holds as a germ in the coarse variation, so derivatives
  of the insertion agree. Neither the frozen fine field nor the Gaussian
  coordinates are resampled in this identity.

  The cutoff restricts to configurations healthy on <math|X>. We also need
  health on the radial segment of the display. Here is the small elementary
  estimate that supplies it. If a plaquette has four logarithmic bonds of
  norm at most <math|h\<leqslant\>1>, let <math|K<around|(|t|)>> be the
  matrix chord <math|K<around|(|t|)>=<around|(|<math-up|mat><around|(|<math-up|plaquette><around|(|t|)>|)>-I|)>/c<rsub|G>>,
  where <math|c<rsub|G>> is the fixed colour normalization and the norm is
  the ambient matrix norm of the health test. The group product and this
  chord map have bounded second derivatives on this fixed ball, so

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|K<around|(|t|)>-t*K<around|(|1|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|G>*t*<around|(|1-t|)>*h<rsup|2>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  Indeed subtract the affine interpolation of <math|K> at its endpoints and
  integrate its second derivative against the Dirichlet Green kernel on
  <math|<around|[|0,1|]>>; the kernel integral is
  <math|t*<around|(|1-t|)>/2>. All four bonds in the touched-plaquette halo
  are included. The chord at zero is zero. Hence, if the endpoint is healthy
  and <math|C<rsub|G>*h<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsub|f>>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|K<around|(|t|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>t*\<theta\><rsub|f>+t*<around|(|1-t|)>*\<theta\><rsub|f>\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsub|f>.
  </equation*>

  Take <math|h=Q*s>. The two required smallness conditions
  <math|Q*s\<leqslant\>1> and <math|C<rsub|G>*<around|(|Q*s|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsub|f>>
  hold eventually: <math|s> is <math|\<theta\><rsub|c>> times a fixed
  polynomial in the read radius, and <math|\<theta\><rsub|c><rsup|2>/\<theta\><rsub|f>>
  has negative upper exponent. The direction width bound follows likewise
  from <math|\<theta\><rsub|f>*<around|(|1+6*<around|(|1+B|)>*L|)><rsup|p>\<leqslant\>1>.
  These purchases occur after <math|L>. We can therefore apply the displaced
  cancellation bound to every partition in the derivatives of
  <math|f\<circ\>exp\<circ\>\<Phi\>\<circ\>a>. It gives the fine-insertion
  price

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reference-short-pointwise>C<rsub|n,d>*\<tau\>*L<rsup|-d-1>*<around*|(|M+<frac|c<rsub|n>*Q*s<rsup|2>+<around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>>|\<theta\><rsub|f>>*L<rsup|d+1>|)><rsup|n>.
  </equation>

  The finite number of partitions is included in <math|C<rsub|n,d>>.

  <subsubsection|Separate local gauges for a winding support>

  For a general support <math|X>, one common logarithm need not exist. We
  instead base the insertion chart at the actual received configuration
  <math|W<rsub|0>>. Keep the frozen fine field, the Gaussian coordinates, and
  every localization tag fixed. For each tested fine bond <math|i>, choose
  the coarse tree gauge <math|g<rsub|i>> on the read halo centered at its
  coarse parent. The local mean and covariance agreement proved earlier
  identifies this local calculation with the same received configuration. The
  gauges may differ with <math|i>.

  Let <math|b<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>> be the coarse logarithm in this gauge
  along the coarse variation, and let <math|w<rsub|i>> be the adjoint
  transform of the same noise. Use the unstraightened tree display

  <\equation*>
    S<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>=e<rsup|<wide|m|~><around|(|b<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>|)><rsub|i>+<around|(|w<rsub|i>|)><rsub|i>>*e<rsup|<wide|b<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>|\<bar\>><rsub|i>>.
  </equation*>

  Its linear response is the tree response
  <math|\<cal-W\>*b=v<rsub|F><around|(|b|)>+<wide|b|\<bar\>>>, whose weighted
  bound is <math|M>. Take <math|log <around|(|S<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>*S<rsub|i><around|(|0|)><rsup|-1>|)>>
  and return it by the fixed adjoint <math|g<rsub|i><rsup|-1>> at the initial
  endpoint. The variable straightening potential
  <math|\<phi\><around|(|b<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>|)>> is absent from this
  relative display. Denote the result by <math|Z<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>>; set
  it to zero on untested bonds. The relative logarithm is defined near
  <math|y=0> because both arguments lie in the fixed chart. It satisfies

  <\equation*>
    Z<around|(|0|)>=0,<space|2em>W<around|(|y|)><rsub|i>=exp
    <around|(|Z<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>|)><around|(|W<rsub|0>|)><rsub|i><space|1em><text|on
    every tested bond>.
  </equation*>

  This is an identity of germs: the relative exponential cancels the base
  display in that bond's own gauge. Finitely many tested bonds admit one
  common neighborhood. Local dependence of <math|f> then gives
  <math|f<around|(|W<around|(|y|)>|)>=f*<around|(|exp
  <around|(|Z<around|(|y|)>|)>*W<rsub|0>|)>> there. No compatibility between
  different <math|g<rsub|i>> is needed, since <math|Z> is an ordinary fine
  bond field in the original coordinates. In particular this argument does
  not require a logarithm of <math|W<rsub|0>> around a noncontractible loop.

  Here is the bound on this field. Write <math|\<omega\><around|(|i|)>=\<theta\><rsub|f>*<around|(|1+<math-up|dist><around|(|i<rsub|->,o|)>|)><rsup|p<rsub|f>>>
  for the fine direction width. Let <math|C\<geqslant\>1> be the fixed local
  gauge response constant, <math|M\<geqslant\>1> the weighted tree-response
  constant just specified, and put

  <\equation*>
    H<rsub|X>=<around|(|2+<math-up|breadth><around|(|X|)>|)><rsup|n*p<rsub|c>>,<space|2em>A<rsub|X>=2*M*C+<frac|<around|(|c<rsub|n>*Q+M|)>*s<rsup|2>*H<rsub|X>|\<theta\><rsub|f>>.
  </equation*>

  The first derivative of <math|b<rsub|i>> obeys the coarse weighted
  direction bound with cost <math|C>; the linear response estimate and
  <math|\<theta\><rsub|c>\<leqslant\>2*\<theta\><rsub|f>>,
  <math|p<rsub|c>\<leqslant\>p<rsub|f>>, therefore give
  <math|2*M*C*\<omega\><around|(|i|)>>. For derivatives of order
  <math|j\<geqslant\>2>, the local chart bound gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|j>*b<rsub|i><around|(|0|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>s<rsup|j>*<around|(|1+<math-up|dist><around|(|<math-up|parent><around|(|i<rsub|->|)>,<math-up|parent><around|(|o|)>|)>|)><rsup|j*p<rsub|c>>\<leqslant\>s<rsup|2>*H<rsub|X>.
  </equation*>

  The last inequality uses <math|s\<leqslant\>1>, <math|j\<leqslant\>n>, and
  the diameter bound for a tested bond relative to <math|o\<in\>X>. The
  linear response on this bounded field costs at most
  <math|M*s<rsup|2>*H<rsub|X>>.

  The relative display on two copies of its inputs vanishes at zero; its
  derivative there is the linear response of their difference, up to
  <math|Q*\<theta\><rsub|c>>. Its first derivative away from zero differs by
  at most a constant times <math|Q*s>, and derivatives of higher order are
  bounded by <math|Q>. The partition chain rule, exactly as for
  <eqref|eq:reference-display-remainder>, bounds the remaining terms at order
  <math|j\<geqslant\>1> by <math|c<rsub|n>*Q*s<rsup|2>*H<rsub|X>>. Adjoint
  return preserves the norm. Since <math|\<omega\><around|(|i|)>\<geqslant\>\<theta\><rsub|f>>,
  these estimates prove

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|j>*Z<rsub|i><around|(|0|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|X>*\<omega\><around|(|i|)>,<space|2em>1\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>n.
  </equation*>

  The cutoff and the local health estimate make <math|W<rsub|0>> healthy.
  Apply the ordinary fine regularity bound to <math|f*<around|(|exp
  <around|(|Z|)>*W<rsub|0>|)>> at <math|Z=0>. For each partition its argument
  fields have the displayed fine widths, so that the bound is at most
  <math|C<rsub|n>*\<tau\>*A<rsub|X><rsup|n>> after summing partitions. The
  zeroth derivative is bounded by <math|\<tau\>>. This proves a
  general-support bound whose leading constant
  <math|C<rsub|n>*<around|(|2*M*C|)><rsup|n>> is chosen before <math|L>.
  Every dependence on the later chart constant <math|Q> is accompanied by
  <math|s<rsup|2>/\<theta\><rsub|f>>, which can be made small after <math|L>
  is fixed. There is no sum of separate insertion derivatives over bonds: the
  entire field <math|Z> is inserted into the same regularity estimate.

  <subsubsection|Integrating the density derivatives>

  Use the same finite Gaussian coordinates for all derivatives, with its
  positive covariance and the frozen fine field fixed. The moment and density
  differentiation arguments of Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-differentiation>
  and <reference|sec:gaussian-moment-prices> apply to this reference as well:
  its precision floor, inverse jets and local affine source were proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:principal-comparison-jets>. The cutoff is
  held fixed in these coordinates. Only on its support is the unmasked
  regular insertion evaluated; the health conclusion in the preceding
  paragraph applies there.

  We detail the dependence of the bound on the support. Let <math|q> be its
  fine cover number and let <math|H=<around|(|r<rsub|0>+48|)>*<around|(|1+B|)>>,
  where <math|r<rsub|0>> is the fixed allocation radius. The number of read
  coordinates and all polynomial weights on this set are bounded by fixed
  powers of <math|H*<around|(|1+q|)>>, with constants allowed to depend on
  <math|L>. Differentiating a Gaussian density inserts sums of products of
  inverse-covariance entries and at most quadratic coordinate factors. At
  order at most <math|n> the result is a polynomial in the Gaussian
  coordinates of degree at most <math|2*n>. The derivative bounds and the
  product rule therefore bound the price in
  <eqref|eq:reference-short-pointwise>, including the density factors, by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reference-short-envelope>C<rsub|0>*\<tau\>*L<rsup|-d-1>*<around|{|1+\<rho\><rsup|2*n>*<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|4*n>|}>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|C<rsub|0>> depends on the pre-<math|L> graded-field cost and the
  regularity conversion constants, while

  <\equation*>
    0\<leqslant\>\<rho\>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|L>*<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|f>+g/\<theta\><rsub|f>|)>*<around|{|H*<around|(|1+q|)>|}><rsup|a<rsub|n>>.
  </equation*>

  To check this factor, insert <math|w=g*z> into
  <eqref|eq:reference-graded-cost>. The nonlinear remainder
  <math|s<rsup|2>/\<theta\><rsub|f>> is bounded by <math|\<theta\><rsub|f>>
  times a polynomial in <math|B> because the two thresholds have bounded
  ratio. The noise contributes <math|<around|(|g/\<theta\><rsub|f>|)><around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>>.
  Every positive-order density or covariance variation has at least one
  coarse direction factor, which is at most <math|\<theta\><rsub|c>> times
  such a polynomial; enlarge <math|K<rsub|L>> and <math|a<rsub|n>> to include
  it. Separate the fixed term <math|M> from these errors, and use
  <math|<around|(|a+b|)><rsup|2*n>\<leqslant\><around|(|2*a|)><rsup|2*n>*<around|(|1+b<rsup|2*n>|)>>
  for <math|a\<geqslant\>1>, <math|b\<geqslant\>0>. This gives
  <eqref|eq:reference-short-envelope>. For a general support, use the
  separate-gauge bound just proved, with its leading constant
  <math|C<rsub|n>*<around|(|2*M*C|)><rsup|n>> and error
  <math|<around|(|c<rsub|n>*Q+M|)>*s<rsup|2>*H<rsub|X>/\<theta\><rsub|f>>.
  Combining it with the same density factors gives <math|C<rsub|1>*\<tau\>>
  in place of <math|C<rsub|0>*\<tau\>*L<rsup|-d-1>>, with possibly larger
  fixed exponents. Its pre-<math|L> constants are the ordinary local
  regularity and linear-response constants.

  The Gaussian moment of <math|<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|4*n>>
  is bounded by <math|K<rprime|'><rsub|L>*<around|{|H*<around|(|1+q|)>|}><rsup|b<rsub|n>>>.
  This follows by expanding the norm bound into coordinate moments and using
  the covariance upper bound, exactly as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-moment-prices>. Choose
  <math|h<rsub|n>=2*n*a<rsub|n>+b<rsub|n>>. For each fixed <math|L>,
  increasing the entrance coupling makes

  <\equation*>
    <around|[|K<rsub|L>*<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|f>+g/\<theta\><rsub|f>|)>|]><rsup|2*n>*K<rprime|'><rsub|L>*H<rsup|h<rsub|n>>\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  Indeed <math|\<theta\><rsub|f>> times any fixed power of <math|B> tends to
  zero, and, uniformly over the incoming beta window,
  <math|g/\<theta\><rsub|f>\<leqslant\>2/<math-up|monitor><around|(|\<beta\><rsub|f>|)>>.
  The monitor grows faster than every fixed power of <math|B>. This argument
  does not assign a negative power exponent to <math|g/\<theta\><rsub|f>>.
  The resulting integrated derivative bounds are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reference-integrated-attenuation><choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|C*\<tau\>*L<rsup|-d-1>*<around|(|1+q|)><rsup|h<rsub|n>>,>|<cell|q<rsup|3>\<less\>L,>>|<row|<cell|C<rprime|'>*\<tau\>*<around|(|1+q|)><rsup|h<rprime|'><rsub|n>>,>|<cell|q<rsup|3>\<geqslant\>L.>>>>>
  </equation>

  All differentiation under the integral is justified by the same polynomial
  Gaussian majorants on a common parameter neighborhood.

  <subsubsection|Absorbing the enlarged support>

  Let <math|A\<gtr\>0> be the fine support exponent, <math|e\<geqslant\>0>
  the requested coarse exponent including its reserve, and <math|q<rprime|'>>
  the coarse cover number of the allocated output support. The geometric
  cover estimate gives

  <\equation*>
    L*q<rprime|'>\<leqslant\><around|(|L+10*q|)>*t<rsub|r<rsub|0>>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|t<rsub|r<rsub|0>>> depends only on the fixed allocation radius.
  Choose <math|L> so that <math|20*e*t<rsub|r<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>L*A>. Then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reference-cover-release>e*q<rprime|'>\<leqslant\>e*t<rsub|r<rsub|0>>+<tfrac|1|2>A*q.
  </equation>

  For every fixed integer <math|h>, the function
  <math|<around|(|1+q|)><rsup|h>*e<rsup|-A*q/2>> is bounded on
  <math|q\<geqslant\>0>: it tends to zero at infinity and is continuous on
  compact intervals. Thus the short-support case of
  <eqref|eq:reference-integrated-attenuation> and
  <eqref|eq:reference-cover-release> yield

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|e*q<rprime|'>><space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|<text|reference
    integral>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|J<rsup|n>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|2>*L<rsup|-d-1>*\<tau\>*e<rsup|A*q>.
  </equation*>

  For the long-support case, <math|q<rsup|3>\<geqslant\>L> implies
  <math|1\<leqslant\><around|(|<around|(|1+q|)><rsup|3>/L|)><rsup|d+1>>.
  Insert this inequality into its bound, then absorb the larger fixed
  polynomial in the same half of the support exponential. We obtain the
  identical form with another constant <math|C<rsub|3>>, also chosen before
  <math|L>. This is the required gain for all supports, including wrapping
  ones in the long-support case.

  Finally, the allotted regular price has the form
  <math|L<rsup|-d-2/5>/<around|(|2*h|)>>, where <math|h\<gtr\>0> depends only
  on the fixed profile. Since <math|L<rsup|-d-1>/L<rsup|-d-2/5>=L<rsup|-3/5>>,
  choose <math|L> large enough that both <math|C<rsub|2>*L<rsup|-d-1>> and
  <math|C<rsub|3>*L<rsup|-d-1>> are at most half that price. All nonlinear
  chart, moment and Gaussian-comparison thresholds are chosen afterwards.
  This preserves the order of constants needed for the one-step regular
  estimate.

  <subsection|Nonlinear singleton inheritance><label|sec:nonlinear-singleton-inheritance>

  A surviving singleton contributes an exponential of an old regular
  activity. Its first order part must be retained as a Gaussian reference
  observable. The error after this subtraction is smaller for two distinct
  reasons: the exponential remainder is quadratic, and the actual Gaussian
  law is close to the reference law. We prove both estimates on the same
  coordinates and then sum them over the inherited occurrences.

  <subsubsection|The nonlinear comparison on fixed coordinates>

  Use the notation and quantitative hypotheses of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:principal-gaussian-comparison>. In
  particular, <math|R<rsub|6>,R<rsub|9>> are density ratios with the same
  compensated Jacobian, <math|\<gamma\><rsub|C<rsub|0>>> is the fixed
  reference probability measure, and <math|w<around|(|z|)>> is the original
  masked activity. Thus <math|w> is measurable and
  <math|<around|\||w<around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<tau\>>. Assume

  <\equation*>
    0\<leqslant\>\<tau\>\<leqslant\>K\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  Write <math|c*<around|(|y,z|)>> and <math|v*<around|(|y,z|)>> for the
  actual and reference cutoffs, respectively. They take values in
  <math|<around|[|0,1|]>> and have the common compact support and local
  plateau properties specified there. The exact integrand for the singleton
  plus its reference observable is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:singleton-nonlinear-integrand>T*<around|(|y,z|)>=R<rsub|6>*<around|(|y,z|)>*<around|{|e<rsup|-w<around|(|z|)>*c*<around|(|y,z|)>>-1|}>+R<rsub|9>*<around|(|y,z|)>*v*<around|(|y,z|)>*w<around|(|z|)>.
  </equation>

  The plus sign in the second term cancels the negative linear term of the
  first. The reference guard ensures that, wherever it is nonzero, the actual
  cutoff is one and the frozen field is healthy on the insertion support.
  Consequently the same <math|w> occurs in both terms, by the compensated
  locality identity. No derivative of <math|w> is needed.

  Let <math|P<rsub|0>,P<rsub|1>,P<rsub|2>> denote fixed positive polynomials
  in the local coordinate count and jet bounds, enlarged below when
  necessary. The density comparison already proved gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|R<rsub|6>|(>*\<cdummy\>,z)\<\|\|\><rsub|J<rsup|n><rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|0>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|R<rsub|9>|(>*\<cdummy\>,z)-R<rsub|6>*<around|(|\<cdummy\>,z|)>\<\|\|\><rsub|J<rsup|n><rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>*P<rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  Here and below the jet norm includes derivatives of every order from zero
  through <math|n>. The polynomial constants depend on <math|n>, not on the
  number of occurrences or the torus volume. If <math|Q<rsub|c>\<geqslant\>1>
  bounds the cutoff jets, impose also <math|K*Q<rsub|c>\<leqslant\>1>. For a
  nonempty derivative set <math|I>, the derivative of <math|e<rsup|-w*c>> is

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|-w*c>*<big|sum><rsub|\<pi\>\<in\>\<cal-P\><around|(|I|)>><big|prod><rsub|A\<in\>\<pi\>><around|(|-w*D<rsub|A>*c|)>.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|<around|\||w|\|>*Q<rsub|c>\<leqslant\>1>, every product has
  size at most <math|\<tau\>*Q<rsub|c>>, up to the fixed number of
  partitions. For the zero-order term, <math|<around|\||e<rsup|-w*c>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>e*\<tau\>>.
  Product differentiation in <eqref|eq:singleton-nonlinear-integrand>
  therefore gives the global bound <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|T|(>\<cdot\>,z)\<\|\|\><rsub|J<rsup|n><rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>\<tau\>*P<rsub|2>>
  on the compact read ball.

  On the smaller ball where both cutoffs have germ one at <math|y=0>, the
  exact identity is

  <\equation*>
    T=R<rsub|6>*<around|(|e<rsup|-w>-1+w|)>+<around|(|R<rsub|9>-R<rsub|6>|)>*w.
  </equation*>

  For real <math|<around|\||w|\|>\<leqslant\>1>, the exponential series gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||e<rsup|-w>-1+w|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||w|\|><rsup|2>*<big|sum><rsub|k\<geqslant\>2><frac|1|k!>\<leqslant\><around|\||w|\|><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>\<tau\>*K.
  </equation*>

  The two factors involving <math|w> are constant in the differentiation
  parameter. Hence on this ball

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:singleton-good-price><around|\<\|\|\>|T|(>\<cdot\>,z)\<\|\|\><rsub|J<rsup|n><rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>\<tau\>*<around|(|K*P<rsub|0>+\<delta\>*P<rsub|1>|)>.
  </equation>

  Let its radius be <math|v<rsub|0>> in the coordinate maximum norm and
  suppose <math|C<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>M*I>. The coordinate Gaussian tail bound
  used in the principal comparison gives

  <\equation*>
    \<gamma\><rsub|C<rsub|0>>*<around|{|<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<geqslant\>v<rsub|0>|}>\<leqslant\>2*N*exp
    <around|(|-v<rsub|0>+M/2|)>.
  </equation*>

  The same compact-coordinate domination justifies differentiating
  <eqref|eq:singleton-nonlinear-integrand> under the integral: on a common
  parameter neighborhood the smooth factors and their finitely many
  derivatives are bounded on the fixed compact ball, and <math|w> is bounded.
  Splitting that integral into the good ball and its complement proves

  <\equation>
    <around*|\<nobracket\>|<label|eq:singleton-comparison-price><around*|\||<big|int>T|(>\<cdot\>,z)<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|C<rsub|0>><around|(|z|)>|\|><rsub|J<rsup|n><rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>\<tau\>*<around|{|K*P<rsub|0>+\<delta\>*P<rsub|1>+2*N*e<rsup|-v<rsub|0>+M/2>*P<rsub|2>|}>.
  </equation>

  This estimate includes the entire near-weight on the bad set; it does not
  use cancellation outside the common plateau.

  In the renormalization step, <math|K> is the incoming regular budget, of
  upper exponent <math|-11/10>. The local covariance and mean comparison of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:principal-comparison-jets> gives
  <math|\<delta\>> of upper exponent <math|-1/2>: its two terms are the
  covariance discrepancy and the unscaled mean discrepancy multiplied by the
  inverse noise scale. The local coordinate counts, cutoff bounds, and
  polynomials above have upper exponent zero. The plateau radius is a fixed
  positive multiple of the fine monitor and its Gaussian tail has every
  negative upper exponent. Thus the braces in
  <eqref|eq:singleton-comparison-price> have upper exponent <math|-1/2>. In
  particular, all the smallness conditions in the density comparison and
  <math|K*Q<rsub|c>\<leqslant\>1> hold after increasing the entrance
  coupling, with the block size already fixed. Denote a common nonnegative
  bound for the braces by <math|b<around|(|x|)>>.

  <subsubsection|The retained reference term>

  These principal and remainder prices feed the regular local return of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:regular-principal-producer>. We spell out
  the summation cost of the reference term, since a per-occurrence gain alone
  does not give the required coarse budget. Let <math|X<rsub|i>> be the fine
  support of occurrence <math|i>, <math|a<rsub|i>> its fine anchor,
  <math|Y<rsub|i>> its allocated coarse support, and
  <math|<wide|a|\<bar\>><rsub|i>> the parent of <math|a<rsub|i>>. Put
  <math|q<rsub|i>=<math-up|cov><rsub|f><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>> and
  <math|q<rsub|i><rprime|'>=<math-up|cov><rsub|c><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>> for
  their cover numbers. Suppose the incoming amplitudes satisfy

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:singleton-incoming-budget>sup<rsub|a>
    <big|sum><rsub|i:a<rsub|i>=a>e<rsup|A*q<rsub|i>>*\<tau\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>E<rsub|<math-up|in>>,<space|2em>A\<gtr\>0.
  </equation>

  All sums use the given occurrence enumeration, including repeated supports.
  Fix a purchased derivative order <math|n\<geqslant\>6>, spatial weight
  order <math|p>, and allocation radius <math|r<rsub|0>>. The bounds through
  any smaller requested order follow by restriction. In particular, the
  degree-four attenuation used below requires the insertion regularity and
  moment cancellation of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:principal-reference-attenuation>
  through that degree. Here that cancellation follows from the defining
  incoming regular-family conditions; it is not an extra moment assumption.
  We give the argument on a nonwrapping support <math|X> anchored at
  <math|o>, on a fine torus of side at least four. Write <math|f> for one
  inherited entry. It is local and gauge invariant, satisfies
  <math|f<around|(|1|)>=0>, is <math|C<rsup|n>> in logarithmic coordinates,
  and its Hessian at the identity vanishes on pairs of affine link fields.
  These are the regular-family conditions preserved by the reset.

  Put <math|F=f\<circ\>exp>. Its first derivative at zero vanishes: constant
  gauge transformations make its restriction to each link an invariant linear
  functional on the simple Lie algebra, and hence zero on the Lie algebra,
  which is spanned by commutators. Use the germ
  <math|\<Psi\>*<around|(|q,J|)>> and the filtered spaces
  <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|a>,\<cal-M\><rsub|a>> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:polynomial-moment-attenuation>, with
  <math|d=4>. By locality and gauge invariance, <math|F\<circ\>\<Psi\>> is
  independent of <math|q>. Consequently a mixed derivative vanishes if any
  argument has weight one: such an argument has no radial coefficient, since
  those coefficients start at weight two.

  If every argument has weight at least two and their total weight is at most
  four, there are at most two arguments. With zero arguments the value is
  <math|f<around|(|1|)>=0>. With one argument the derivative is zero by
  <math|D*F<around|(|0|)>=0>. With two arguments both weights equal two, and
  the second-order chain rule gives

  <\equation*>
    D<rsup|2>*<around|(|F\<circ\>\<Psi\>|)><around|(|0|)><around|[|u,v|]>=D<rsup|2>*F<around|(|0|)><around|[|D*\<Psi\><around|(|0|)>*u,D*\<Psi\><around|(|0|)>*v|]>,
  </equation*>

  because the term containing <math|D*F<around|(|0|)>> vanishes. The two
  image directions lie in <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|2>> by
  <eqref|eq:moment-filtration>; they agree on <math|X> with affine link
  fields. Locality and the incoming affine-Hessian condition kill this term
  too. Thus every derivative of <math|F\<circ\>\<Psi\>> of total weight at
  most four vanishes.

  To transfer this assertion back to <math|F>, lift each polynomial direction
  of weight <math|a<rsub|i>> through the surjection
  <eqref|eq:moment-surjection> and induct on the number of arguments. In the
  partition form of the chain rule, the singleton partition is the desired
  derivative of <math|F>. Every other partition has fewer blocks; its block
  directions lie in the spaces with weights equal to the sums of the
  corresponding <math|a<rsub|i>>, by <eqref|eq:moment-filtration>. The
  induction hypothesis kills those terms. The composite derivative was just
  shown to vanish, so the singleton term vanishes. Finally locality allows
  the directions to be screened to <math|X>, by differentiating
  <math|F=F\<circ\><math-up|screen><rsub|X>>. This proves precisely
  <eqref|eq:polynomial-moment-null> through degree four for the inherited
  entry. Wrapping supports use the long-support estimate below and do not
  require this cancellation. The fine side-size condition follows in the
  application from the coarse-side bound and the positive block ratio. Let
  <math|t\<geqslant\>0> be the full requested coarse support exponent,
  including any reserve needed later.

  The reference attenuation proof in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:principal-reference-attenuation>
  applies to each forest and tag. In its notation the pointwise derivative
  bound is

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|0>*\<tau\><rsub|i>*d<rsub|L><around|(|q<rsub|i>|)>*<around|{|1+\<rho\><rsub|i><rsup|2*n>*<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|4*n>|}>,<space|2em>d<rsub|L><around|(|q|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|L<rsup|-5>,>|<cell|q<rsup|3>\<less\>L,>>|<row|<cell|1,>|<cell|q<rsup|3>\<geqslant\>L.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  Here <math|B<rsub|0>> is fixed before <math|L>. The same local Gaussian
  moment estimate bounds the integral of the polynomial factor. More
  precisely, write <math|H=<around|(|r<rsub|0>+48|)>*<around|(|1+B|)>>, where
  <math|B> is the scale radius. There are fixed integers <math|a,v> such that

  <\equation*>
    \<rho\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|L>*<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|f>+g/\<theta\><rsub|f>|)>*<around|[|H*<around|(|1+q<rsub|i>|)>|]><rsup|a>,<space|1em><big|int><around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|4*n>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|L>*<around|[|H*<around|(|1+q<rsub|i>|)>|]><rsup|v>.
  </equation*>

  The fine and coarse thresholds here have the bounded ratio required by that
  proof. With <math|h=2*n*a+v>, impose the explicit post-<math|L> condition

  <\equation*>
    <around|[|K<rsub|L>*<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|f>+g/\<theta\><rsub|f>|)>|]><rsup|2*n>*M<rsub|L>*H<rsup|h>\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  It is available by the threshold and monitor argument proved there; no
  negative power rate is assigned to <math|g/\<theta\><rsub|f>>. Integration
  yields <math|B<rsub|0>*\<tau\><rsub|i>*d<rsub|L><around|(|q<rsub|i>|)>*<around|{|1+<around|(|1+q<rsub|i>|)><rsup|h>|}>>.
  Since

  <\equation*>
    d<rsub|L><around|(|q|)>\<leqslant\>L<rsup|-5>*<around|(|1+q|)><rsup|15>,
  </equation*>

  including when <math|q<rsup|3>\<geqslant\>L>, the reference price is at
  most <math|2*B<rsub|0>*\<tau\><rsub|i>*L<rsup|-5>*<around|(|1+q<rsub|i>|)><rsup|15+h>>.

  The support compression estimate gives, provided
  <math|20*t*<space|0.17em>t<rsub|r<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>L*A>,

  <\equation*>
    t*q<rsub|i><rprime|'>\<leqslant\>t*<space|0.17em>t<rsub|r<rsub|0>>+<tfrac|1|2>A*q<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  Choose <math|R> so that <math|<around|(|1+q|)><rsup|15+h>\<leqslant\>R*e<rsup|A*q/2>>
  for <math|q\<geqslant\>0>. Such an <math|R> exists by polynomial versus
  exponential growth and depends only on the displayed pre-<math|L> data.
  Thus for <math|C<rprime|'>=1+2*B<rsub|0>*R*e<rsup|t*t<rsub|r<rsub|0>>>>,
  the weighted reference price is bounded by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:singleton-reference-occurrence>e<rsup|t*q<rsub|i><rprime|'>>*s<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>C<rprime|'>*L<rsup|-5>*e<rsup|A*q<rsub|i>>*\<tau\><rsub|i>.
  </equation>

  A finite average over forests and tags whose sum of absolute weights is at
  most one preserves this estimate. This is the actual fixed average used in
  the singleton construction; no new Gaussian marginal or smaller-period
  replacement is introduced.

  Each coarse site has exactly <math|L<rsup|4>> fine sites above it in four
  dimensions. For a coarse site <math|<wide|a|\<bar\>>>, summing
  <eqref|eq:singleton-reference-occurrence> first over each fine anchor above
  <math|<wide|a|\<bar\>>> and then over those <math|L<rsup|4>> anchors proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:singleton-reference-anchored>sup<rsub|<wide|a|\<bar\>>>
    <big|sum><rsub|i:<wide|a|\<bar\>><rsub|i>=<wide|a|\<bar\>>>e<rsup|t*q<rsub|i><rprime|'>>*s<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><around|(|C<rprime|'>/L|)>*E<rsub|<math-up|in>>.
  </equation>

  The factor <math|L<rsup|4>> is essential. It is paid by the fifth power in
  the per-occurrence attenuation.

  <subsubsection|Inheritance of the small remainder>

  The corresponding nonlinear remainder is the actual singleton plus the
  positive reference observable. Equation
  <eqref|eq:singleton-comparison-price> bounds its local ordered jets by
  <math|\<tau\><rsub|i>*b<around|(|x|)>> for each fixed forest and tag. The
  common finite average preserves this bound.

  For completeness, the coordinate-to-field step uses the same local
  variation as the actual singleton, not a separately chosen test field.
  Restrict each direction to <math|Y<rsub|i>>, which leaves every derivative
  of a <math|Y<rsub|i>>-local function unchanged. For
  <math|q<rsub|i><rprime|'>\<leqslant\>B> the weighted admissibility bounds
  imply the local direction bound used in the Gaussian comparison. The
  support cover restriction and the coarse threshold bound by twice the fine
  scale threshold give the required direction norm bound directly. Indeed the
  anchor belongs to <math|Y<rsub|i>> and the cover restriction bounds each
  support-point distance from it by the local radius <math|D<rsub|x>>; thus
  the truncated direction satisfies

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|U|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsub|c>*<around|(|1+D<rsub|x>|)><rsup|p>\<leqslant\>2*\<theta\><rsub|f>*<around|(|1+D<rsub|x>|)><rsup|p>.
  </equation*>

  Apply the comparison to the ordered parameter map formed from these actual
  restricted directions. Its mixed derivative is the prescribed ordered field
  derivative: evaluate the corresponding multilinear differential on the
  coordinate unit vectors, each of norm one. This conversion incurs no
  inverse rescaling factor. Consequently the estimate controls the prescribed
  spatially weighted derivatives through order <math|n> on healthy
  configurations whenever <math|q<rsub|i><rprime|'>\<leqslant\>B>.
  Regularity, gauge invariance, and locality on every support follow from the
  actual singleton regularity and the reference construction. The latter is
  invariant under the orthogonal colour-coordinate gauge change; locality
  follows from agreement on <math|Y<rsub|i>>. Its measurable extension
  outside the healthy domain has the same local germ there.

  For the remainder use the weaker consequence
  <math|t*q<rsub|i><rprime|'>\<leqslant\>t*t<rsub|r<rsub|0>>+A*q<rsub|i>> of
  support compression. Combining it with <eqref|eq:singleton-incoming-budget>
  and again counting <math|L<rsup|4>> fine anchors above a coarse anchor
  gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:singleton-remainder-anchored>sup<rsub|<wide|a|\<bar\>>>
    <big|sum><rsub|i:<wide|a|\<bar\>><rsub|i>=<wide|a|\<bar\>>>e<rsup|t*q<rsub|i><rprime|'>>*\<tau\><rsub|i>*b<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>L<rsup|4>*e<rsup|t*t<rsub|r<rsub|0>>>*b<around|(|x|)>*E<rsub|<math-up|in>>.
  </equation>

  For fixed <math|L,r<rsub|0>,t>, the coefficient on the right retains upper
  exponent <math|-1/2>. Equations <eqref|eq:singleton-reference-anchored> and
  <eqref|eq:singleton-remainder-anchored> are the two required inheritance
  bounds. The first applies to every reference support; the derivative
  assertion for the remainder retains the restriction
  <math|q<rsub|i><rprime|'>\<leqslant\>B>. The pre-existing full singleton
  regularity and the common funded path are inputs to this step. The argument
  does not replace their construction or the estimates for clusters
  containing more than one old occurrence.

  <subsection|Extra decay from the degree of a
  cluster><label|sec:high-degree-cluster-prices>

  After extracting the singleton reference, many terms can be paid without a
  further subtraction. The relevant degree counts elementary occurrences
  inside every connected component, together with an extra grade for certain
  species. Counting only the number of components in a cluster would miss
  this gain. We follow the two successive connected expansions and retain
  their separate convergence conditions.

  <subsubsection|Grading the elementary activities>

  Write <math|\<beta\><rsub|+><around|(|x|)>=\<beta\><rsub|F>*<around|(|G,x,L|)>>
  for the shifted fine coupling and set

  <\equation*>
    D<around|(|x|)>=\<beta\><rsub|+><around|(|x|)><rsup|3/8>.
  </equation*>

  Eventually <math|D\<geqslant\>1>. Give the six inherited logarithmic
  species the following additional grades:

  <\equation*>
    <tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-lborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-rborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-halign|r>|<cwith|1|-1|3|3|cell-halign|r>|<cwith|1|-1|4|4|cell-halign|r>|<cwith|1|-1|5|5|cell-halign|r>|<cwith|1|-1|6|6|cell-halign|r>|<cwith|1|-1|7|7|cell-halign|r>|<cwith|1|-1|7|7|cell-rborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|<text|species>>|<cell|<text|regular>>|<cell|<text|cubic>>|<cell|<text|quartic>>|<cell|<text|quadratic
    loop>>|<cell|<text|fine tail>>|<cell|<text|coarse
    tail>>>|<row|<cell|h>|<cell|1>|<cell|0>|<cell|1>|<cell|1>|<cell|2>|<cell|2.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  These are extra grades; a further factor <math|D> per occurrence will be
  inserted at the connected-component stage.

  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:inherited-activity-prices> supplies the
  required full-support site bounds. Fix <math|-1/2\<less\>t\<less\>-3/8>,
  and choose <math|s> strictly between <math|-1/2> and <math|t>. The ungraded
  regular, quartic and loop prices can each be bounded by
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|+><rsup|-15/16>> eventually. Multiplication by <math|D>
  changes this exponent to <math|-9/16\<less\>s>. The cubic price has upper
  exponent <math|-1/2> and is bounded by <math|\<beta\><rsub|+><rsup|s>>.
  Both true path tails can be bounded by <math|\<beta\><rsub|+><rsup|-2>>;
  multiplication by <math|D<rsup|2>> changes this to <math|-5/4\<less\>s>.
  The six prices therefore sum to at most
  <math|6*\<beta\><rsub|+><rsup|s>\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rsub|+><rsup|t>>
  eventually. These inequalities preserve the original supports and all
  length charges. The forced inherited family retains every negative upper
  exponent and receives no extra grade.

  The same argument applies to the local derivatives. Hold the original
  insertion fixed in the compensated coordinates as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:inherited-activity-prices>. Its transported
  cutoff has jet scale bounded by an upper-exponent-zero function times the
  full cover plus length charge. The coarse tail has an additional varying
  coarse-field term; its own jet price has upper exponent <math|-2>, and so
  the factor <math|D<rsup|2>> again leaves exponent <math|-5/4>. Adding this
  term to its old price preserves the common bound
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|+><rsup|t>> by first taking the intermediate exponent
  <math|s>. The cutoff product rule only changes the upper-exponent-zero jet
  scale.

  For the other optional letters, assign grade zero to the quadratic local
  term and grade two to the remaining small correction family. Their ungraded
  prices are, respectively, of upper exponent <math|-1/2> and at most
  <math|-2>. The density and exponential jet estimates of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:activity-jet-assembly> therefore preserve a
  common upper exponent <math|t> after the grades are inserted. More
  explicitly, if <math|a<rsub|i>> is a nonnegative logarithmic amplitude and
  its jet scale is <math|b<rsub|i>>, the full-support site bound implies
  <math|a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1> eventually: choose a root in its nonempty
  support, bound this single nonnegative summand by the site sum, and drop
  its weights, all at least one. The zero-order exponential difference is
  bounded by <math|e*a<rsub|i>>. For a positive-order derivative, Faà di
  Bruno's formula is a finite sum indexed by partitions; a partition with
  <math|q> blocks contributes at most <math|e*a<rsub|i><rsup|q>*b<rsub|i><rsup|r>>
  at order <math|r>. Since <math|a<rsub|i><rsup|q>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|i>>,
  this bounds every jet through order <math|n> by
  <math|C<rsub|n>*a<rsub|i>*max <around|(|1,b<rsub|i>|)><rsup|n>>. The scale
  <math|b<rsub|i>> retains its full cover and length charges; only its scalar
  prefactor has upper exponent zero. The polynomial charge factor is paid by
  the reserve in the next paragraph. Give warning letters grade two and
  forced letters grade zero. The joint warning probability can be purchased
  with any fixed negative upper exponent per warning. For example, choose
  upper exponent <math|-5>; its square root, multiplied by
  <math|D<rsup|2>=\<beta\><rsub|+><rsup|3/4>>, has upper exponent
  <math|-7/4\<less\>t>. Cauchy\USchwarz then retains one such purchased price
  for each warning, without requiring independence of their events. Thus the
  grading is compatible with the actual Gaussian derivative majorants, not
  only their zeroth-order values.

  The passage from graded letter prices to differentiated component integrals
  uses the common random field. For a component <math|P>, take <math|I> to be
  the full set of fine bonds read by its enlarged union support, and let
  <math|J> be its joint warning event. For the Gaussian coordinates
  <math|z<rsub|b,c>> indexed by bond and colour, define

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-F\><rsub|I><around|(|z|)>=1+<around|\||I|\|>+<around|\||I\<times\>\<cal-C\>|\|>+<big|sum><rsub|<around|(|b,c|)>\<in\>I\<times\>\<cal-C\>><around|\||z<rsub|b,c>|\|>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<cal-C\>> is the finite colour index set. The product-rule
  bound has the form

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|P>*\<b-1\><rsub|J>*\<cal-F\><rsub|I><rsup|k*j>,<space|2em>A<rsub|P>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>P>\<alpha\><rsub|i>*<space|0.17em>V<around|(|x|)>*<around|(|1+S<around|(|P|)>|)><rsup|j>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i>> are the nonnegative elementary packet and
  letter prices, <math|V\<geqslant\>0> has upper exponent zero, and
  <math|S<around|(|P|)>> is the full footprint charge. The read-count bound
  is <math|<around|\||I|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+S<around|(|P|)>|)>*b<rsub|B>*d<rsub|L>>,
  with the fixed block-density factor <math|d<rsub|L>>. The Gaussian moment
  estimate of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-moment-prices>, at the
  doubled order <math|2*k*j>, gives <math|<big|int>\<cal-F\><rsub|I><rsup|2*k*j>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\>\<leqslant\><around|[|E<around|(|x|)>*<around|(|1+S<around|(|P|)>|)><rsup|K>|]><rsup|2>>
  for some fixed <math|K> and upper-exponent-zero <math|E\<geqslant\>0>. The
  joint warning bound is <math|\<mu\><around|(|J|)>\<leqslant\>\<rho\><rsup|2*b<around|(|P|)>>>,
  where <math|b<around|(|P|)>> is the number of warning labels and
  <math|\<rho\>\<geqslant\>0> is the retained warning price. Cauchy\USchwarz
  therefore yields

  <\equation*>
    <big|int>A<rsub|P>*\<b-1\><rsub|J>*\<cal-F\><rsub|I><rsup|k*j>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|P>*E<around|(|x|)>*<around|(|1+S<around|(|P|)>|)><rsup|K>*\<rho\><rsup|b<around|(|P|)>>.
  </equation*>

  The remaining Haar integral has mass one. This is precisely the integrated
  component majorant, with its same warning power and a larger fixed
  footprint exponent. It uses no independence between overlapping reads or
  warning events. The local jet construction and its common integrable
  majorant justify differentiation as proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:activity-jet-assembly>.

  <subsubsection|Recovering the footprint weight in the first expansion>

  We state the needed positive-weight calculation explicitly. For an
  elementary label <math|i>, let <math|X<rsub|i>> be its support,
  <math|u<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0> its price, and <math|e<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>
  its additional length charge. For a finite connected set <math|P> put

  <\equation*>
    C<around|(|P|)>=c<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>P>X<rsub|i>|)>,<space|2em>S<around|(|P|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>P><around|(|c<around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>+e<rsub|i>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|c> is the connected cover number at the current scale. The cover
  subadditivity along a spanning tree gives
  <math|C<around|(|P|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i>c<around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>.
  Fix <math|a\<geqslant\>0>, <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0> and integer
  <math|k\<geqslant\>0>. If the elementary site estimate is available with
  exponent <math|a+2*\<epsilon\>> on cover and <math|2*\<epsilon\>> on the
  length charge, the first tree estimate yields its rooted connected sum. It
  also pays the stronger summand

  <\equation*>
    \<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|z\<in\><big|cup>X<rsub|i>|}>>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>P>u<rsub|i>*<space|0.17em>e<rsup|a*C<around|(|P|)>>*<around|(|1+S<around|(|P|)>|)><rsup|k>*e<rsup|\<epsilon\>*S<around|(|P|)>>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|1+S|)><rsup|k>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<epsilon\>>*k!\<epsilon\><rsup|-k>*e<rsup|\<epsilon\>*S>,<space|2em>S\<geqslant\>0,
  </equation*>

  by the exponential series at <math|\<epsilon\>*<around|(|1+S|)>>. Together
  with cover subadditivity this bounds the displayed summand by
  <math|e<rsup|\<epsilon\>>*k!\<epsilon\><rsup|-k>> times the summand already
  paid by the elementary site estimate. The same <math|\<epsilon\>> therefore
  pays both the retained full-footprint exponential and the polynomial
  moment; we have used two copies of its reserve, not discarded either cost.

  Apply this calculation to the mixed elementary alphabet of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mixed-activity-prices>. Its mandatory packet
  weights already include their seed payment, while its optional letters have
  the grades above. For a component <math|P>, let <math|h<around|(|P|)>> be
  the sum of its extra grades, <math|<around|\||P|\|>> its number of
  elementary occurrences, <math|m<around|(|P|)>> its number of mandatory
  fine-cell, coarse-bond and flagged-site seeds, and <math|S<around|(|P|)>>
  its full footprint charge. The seed count includes all three types; cluster
  multiplicities are retained in the next expansion. The strengthened
  component multiplier is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:degree-component-multiplier>D<rsup|h<around|(|P|)>+<around|\||P|\|>>*exp
    <around|{|M*m<around|(|P|)>+\<epsilon\>*S<around|(|P|)>|}>,
  </equation>

  where <math|M\<geqslant\>0> is the seed mark. This factor is the product of
  the elementary factors <math|D<rsup|1+h<rsub|i>>*e<rsup|M*m<rsub|i>>> and
  the retained footprint factor. Multiplying every elementary price by
  <math|D> multiplies its site budget by <math|D>. Consequently the first
  expansion is valid if

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:degree-first-smallness><frac|4*b<rsub|B>*D*r<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>|\<delta\><rsub|0>>\<leqslant\><frac|1|2>,
  </equation>

  where <math|r<rsub|0>> and <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> are the
  original elementary root price and its reserve, and
  <math|b<rsub|B>=<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>>. The selected root price
  <math|r<rsub|0>> has upper exponent <math|t\<less\>-3/8>, as fixed above;
  hence <math|D*r<rsub|0>> has negative upper exponent and this inequality
  holds eventually. Its component root price is
  <math|D*\<omega\><around|(|x|)>>, where <math|\<omega\>> is the original
  component price including the footprint polynomial just proved. All these
  statements require the graded elementary site bound on the same incoming
  representation.

  <subsubsection|The second expansion and its degree gain>

  Let <math|j> be the field derivative order, and let
  <math|B<rsub|1><around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>0> be the upper-exponent-zero
  constant from the component derivative estimates. A convenient
  second-expansion root price is

  <\equation*>
    \<sigma\><around|(|x|)>=<around|(|1+2<rsup|j>*B<rsub|1><around|(|x|)>|)>*\<omega\><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  Choose a target support exponent <math|a\<geqslant\>0> and a reserve
  <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0> with <math|a+2*\<eta\>> no larger than the available
  component exponent. In addition to <eqref|eq:degree-first-smallness>,
  impose

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:degree-second-smallness><frac|4*b<rsub|B>*D*\<sigma\><around|(|x|)>|\<eta\>>\<leqslant\><frac|1|2>.
  </equation>

  The cluster estimate of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:cluster-expansion>
  now applies to the strengthened component weights, with root price
  <math|D*\<sigma\>> and tail ratio <math|1/2>.

  For a cluster <math|\<Gamma\>=<around|(|P<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,P<rsub|q>|)>>,
  counted with its actual occurrence multiplicities, define

  <\equation*>
    d<around|(|\<Gamma\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|r=1><rsup|q><around|{|h<around|(|P<rsub|r>|)>+<around|\||P<rsub|r>|\|>|}>,<space|1em>m<around|(|\<Gamma\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|r>m<around|(|P<rsub|r>|)>,<space|1em>s<around|(|\<Gamma\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|r>S<around|(|P<rsub|r>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Its strengthened weight is its old positive cluster majorant times

  <\equation*>
    D<rsup|d<around|(|\<Gamma\>|)>>*e<rsup|M*m<around|(|\<Gamma\>|)>+\<epsilon\>*s<around|(|\<Gamma\>|)>>.
  </equation*>

  This identity follows by multiplying <eqref|eq:degree-component-multiplier>
  over the cluster occurrences; repeated labels are counted at each
  occurrence. Suppose

  <\equation*>
    D<rsup|3>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|M>,<space|2em>D<rsup|3>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<epsilon\>*T>,<space|2em>T\<geqslant\>0.
  </equation*>

  For any cluster satisfying at least one of

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:degree-large-class>d<around|(|\<Gamma\>|)>\<geqslant\>3,<space|2em>m<around|(|\<Gamma\>|)>\<geqslant\>1,<space|2em>s<around|(|\<Gamma\>|)>\<geqslant\>T,
  </equation>

  the strengthened multiplier is at least <math|D<rsup|3>>. Every other
  factor is at least one. Its unstrengthened price is therefore bounded by
  <math|D<rsup|-3>> times the strengthened price.

  Spatially weighted derivatives of order at most <math|j>, with spatial
  power <math|p>, cost <math|<around|[|\<ell\><around|(|x|)>*<around|(|1+C<around|(|\<Gamma\>|)>|)>|]><rsup|p*j>>,
  where <math|\<ell\><around|(|x|)>=4*<around|(|2*B<around|(|x|)>+1|)>>. The
  same exponential-series inequality pays this polynomial from the reserve
  <math|\<eta\>>, at cost

  <\equation*>
    H<rsub|\<eta\>><around|(|x|)>=\<ell\><around|(|x|)><rsup|p*j>*e<rsup|\<eta\>>*<around|(|p*j|)>!\<eta\><rsup|-p*j>.
  </equation*>

  Using the cluster root bound at exponent <math|a+\<eta\>>, then removing
  this reserve, proves the site estimate at exponent <math|a>

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:degree-large-budget>sup<rsub|z>
    <big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<Gamma\>:z\<in\><math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<Gamma\>>>|<row|<cell|\<Gamma\><text|
    satisfies ><eqref|eq:degree-large-class>>>>>>><around|\<\|\|\>|\<Phi\><rsub|\<Gamma\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,p>*e<rsup|a*C<around|(|\<Gamma\>|)>>\<leqslant\>D<rsup|-3>*H<rsub|\<eta\>><around|(|x|)><frac|D*\<sigma\><around|(|x|)>|1-1/2>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<Phi\><rsub|\<Gamma\>>> denotes the cluster contribution and
  the norm is its local spatially weighted jet bound. Restricting a
  nonnegative sum to the displayed class is legitimate without analyticity of
  a field-dependent classification: the class depends only on labels,
  supports and charges. Uniform forest averaging preserves the bound.

  <subsubsection|Paying an arbitrary fraction of the forecast>

  We check the exponents and the order of choices. Suppose the unstrengthened
  site bound is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:degree-old-budget>H<rsub|i><around|(|x|)><frac|\<sigma\><around|(|x|)>|1-1/2>\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rsub|+><around|(|x|)><rsup|\<varphi\>>,<space|2em>\<varphi\>\<less\>-3/8,
  </equation>

  for some <math|i\<gtr\>0> with <math|a+2*i\<leqslant\>E>, where <math|E> is
  the available component exponent. This receipt follows from the
  unstrengthened component construction when its upper exponent <math|q>
  satisfies <math|q\<less\>\<varphi\>>: <math|B<rsub|1>> and <math|H<rsub|i>>
  have upper exponent zero, <math|\<omega\>> has upper exponent <math|q>, and
  therefore the entire left side has upper exponent <math|q>. Taking the
  reserve <math|i=<around|(|E-a|)>/2> also makes its ordinary degree price
  without the factor <math|D> at most <math|1/2> eventually, since
  <math|q\<less\>0>. The same graded local construction must supply the
  actual component majorants; the scalar exponent calculation alone does not
  construct them. Put <math|\<eta\>=<around|(|E-a|)>/2>. Then
  <math|0\<less\>i\<leqslant\>\<eta\>> and, writing <math|r=p*j>, we have

  <\equation*>
    \<eta\><rsup|-r>*r!\<leqslant\>i<rsup|-r>*r!,<space|2em>H<rsub|\<eta\>><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<eta\>>*H<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  Also <math|H<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>\<eta\><rsup|-r>*r!\<gtr\>0>,
  since <math|\<ell\>\<geqslant\>1> and <math|e<rsup|i>\<geqslant\>1>. Thus
  <eqref|eq:degree-old-budget> bounds <math|\<sigma\>> by a fixed constant
  times <math|\<beta\><rsub|+><rsup|\<varphi\>>>. Since <math|b<rsub|B>> has
  upper exponent zero and <math|3/8+\<varphi\>\<less\>0>, it implies
  <eqref|eq:degree-second-smallness> at this fixed reserve for all large
  <math|x>. The first condition follows in exactly the same way from the
  strictly smaller than <math|-3/8> exponent of <math|r<rsub|0>>.

  The seed mark and the large-footprint threshold obey <math|e<rsup|-M>> and
  <math|e<rsup|-\<epsilon\>*T>> estimates with every negative upper exponent,
  so the two inequalities <math|D<rsup|3>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|M>,e<rsup|\<epsilon\>*T>>
  hold eventually as well. Substituting <eqref|eq:degree-old-budget> into
  <eqref|eq:degree-large-budget> gives

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|\<eta\>>*D<rsup|-2>*\<beta\><rsub|+><rsup|\<varphi\>>=e<rsup|\<eta\>>*\<beta\><rsub|+><rsup|\<varphi\>-3/4>.
  </equation*>

  The incoming regular forecast is <math|\<delta\><rsub|E><around|(|x|)>=x<rsup|-11/10>>.
  Since

  <\equation*>
    \<varphi\>-<frac|3|4>+<frac|11|10>=\<varphi\>+<frac|7|20>\<less\>0,
  </equation*>

  the last price is at most <math|c*\<delta\><rsub|E><around|(|x|)>> for any
  prescribed <math|c\<gtr\>0> after increasing the entrance coupling. The
  comparison uses <math|\<beta\><rsub|+><around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>x> and the
  negative exponent of <math|\<delta\><rsub|E>>.

  For each stage only finitely many transitions and fixed derivative orders
  occur. First take the maximum of their block-size requirements; then take
  the maximum of their coupling thresholds and the previous entrance value.
  This yields one increasing collection of entrance requirements. Enlarging
  them preserves all the inequalities above. It does not require a uniform
  threshold over infinitely many derivative orders. The estimates for the
  remaining degree-one and degree-two classes, and the identities which
  attach their subtractions to the renormalized path, are separate steps.

  <subsection|Comparing the local factors at neighboring
  couplings><label|sec:local-parameter-comparison>

  We now prove the compensated comparison used in the fixed-volume continuity
  argument. The important simplification is to preserve the physical fine
  field while changing the Gaussian coordinate. Thus the incoming measurable
  activity is evaluated at the same point in the two representations. Only
  its value difference, and not a derivative of that activity, is needed.

  Fix a derivative order <math|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and a fine torus. For a
  scalar function of the coarse variation <math|y>, write

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>=max<rsub|0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>j><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r>*f<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|\<\|\|\>>.
  </equation*>

  All derivatives below are taken on a common admissible local chart, with
  its fixed normalized directions. In particular, the assertions include
  existence and continuity of these derivatives near <math|y<rsub|0>>. We use
  the product estimate

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:local-parameter-product-jet><around|\<\|\|\>|f*h|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>3<rsup|j><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>><around|\<\|\|\>|h|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>.
  </equation>

  The Leibniz formula has <math|2<rsup|r>> terms at order <math|r>, so the
  slightly larger factor <math|3<rsup|j>> is valid also for <math|j=0>.

  The base representation supplies summable elementary prices
  <math|u<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>, with <math|B=<big|sum><rsub|i>u<rsub|i>\<less\>\<infty\>>,
  for its compensated factors <math|w<rsub|i>>. All logarithms here are real.
  Assume explicitly a common base value bound
  <math|<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|i><around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M>
  for their real exponents; in the construction this follows from the
  summable incoming logarithmic prices and the held-field estimate. A bound
  on <math|e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|i>>-1> alone would not control a large
  negative <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>>. We also use the following local estimates
  already required by the compensated construction: uniform jets of its
  enforcement cutoffs; a bound <math|A*s> for the packet action jets on every
  packet of size <math|s> whose cutoff is not identically zero near
  <math|y<rsub|0>>; and, for its density compensation <math|K<rsub|I>> and
  quadratic exponent <math|q<rsub|I>>, for every subset <math|I> of read
  bonds,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:local-parameter-density-input><around|\<\|\|\>|K|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|0>*F<rsub|0><around|(|z|)><rsup|N>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|q|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|0>*F<rsub|0><around|(|z|)><rsup|2>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|N\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, <math|C<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0>, and
  <math|F<rsub|0><around|(|z|)>=1+d+<big|sum><rsub|r=1><rsup|d><around|\||z<rsub|r>|\|>\<geqslant\>1>
  uses the full finite Gaussian coordinate space. The constants
  <math|N,C<rsub|0>> are common to every such <math|I>; we suppress that
  index in the formulas. These estimates must hold for the same base
  representation, forests and compensation maps. Their uniform purchase in
  the scale construction is a separate requirement; the comparison below uses
  their actual values at the fixed stage.

  Let <math|R<rprime|'>> be a neighboring representation with the same
  dictionaries, paths and masks as <math|R>, and let <math|t\<gtr\>0> bound
  its input differences as in the fixed-volume continuity argument. Put
  <math|\<lambda\>=g<around|(|\<beta\>|)>/g<around|(|\<beta\><rprime|'>|)>>.
  The identity <math|g<around|(|\<beta\><rprime|'>|)>*\<lambda\>=g<around|(|\<beta\>|)>>
  implies that the fine field at <math|<around|(|R<rprime|'>,\<lambda\>*z|)>>
  is precisely the fine field at <math|<around|(|R,z|)>>. It also gives
  <math|\<beta\>*\<lambda\><rsup|2>=\<beta\><rprime|'>> and makes the
  compensated noise course for <math|R<rprime|'>> equal to <math|\<lambda\>>
  times the course for <math|R>.

  <subsubsection|Elementary exponential comparisons>

  Two elementary facts explain the logarithmic and packet estimates. First,
  suppose <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|e<rsup|f>-1|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>u>,
  <math|u\<leqslant\>B>, and <math|<around|\||f<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M>.
  Then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:local-parameter-log-recovery><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,M,B>*u.
  </equation>

  Indeed <math|f=log <around|(|1+<around|(|e<rsup|f>-1|)>|)>>. The
  derivatives of <math|v\<mapsto\>log <around|(|1+v|)>> are bounded on
  <math|<around|[|e<rsup|-M>-1,e<rsup|M>-1|]>>. In each positive-order
  chain-rule term there is at least one derivative of <math|e<rsup|f>-1>. A
  product of <math|r> such derivatives is bounded by
  <math|u<rsup|r>\<leqslant\>u*max <around|(|1,B|)><rsup|r-1>>. At order
  zero, the mean value theorem between <math|0> and
  <math|e<rsup|f<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>>-1> gives the same linear bound. The
  chain rule is used on the open interval <math|v\<gtr\>-1>, so no assertion
  of a fixed neighborhood contained in that compact interval is needed.

  Second, if <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|e<rsup|f>-1|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>B>
  and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|h|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>v\<leqslant\>D>,
  then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:local-parameter-exp-difference><around|\<\|\|\>|e<rsup|f+h>-e<rsup|f>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,B,D>*v.
  </equation>

  Use <math|e<rsup|f+h>-e<rsup|f>=e<rsup|f>*<around|(|e<rsup|h>-1|)>>. The
  zero-order estimate for the second factor is
  <math|<around|\||e<rsup|h<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|D><around|\||h<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>|\|>>.
  For positive orders, the partition formula for derivatives of
  <math|e<rsup|h>> gives finitely many products of derivatives of <math|h>,
  each containing at least one factor. Thus
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|e<rsup|h>-1|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,D>*v>,
  by <math|v<rsup|r>\<leqslant\>v*max <around|(|1,D|)><rsup|r-1>>. Apply
  <eqref|eq:local-parameter-product-jet> and
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|e<rsup|f>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>B+1>.

  <subsubsection|The four elementary kinds of factor>

  We compare every neighboring factor after the noise rescaling. For a packet
  <math|s>, write its base compensated factor as <math|w<rsub|s>>. The exact
  ratio identity is

  <\equation*>
    w<rsub|s><rprime|'>-w<rsub|s>=w<rsub|s>*<around|(|e<rsup|H<rsub|s>>-1|)>,<space|2em>H<rsub|s>=-<around|(|\<beta\><rprime|'>-\<beta\>|)>*E<rsub|s>-n<rsub|s>*<around|{|log
    m<around|(|\<beta\><rprime|'>|)>-log m<around|(|\<beta\>|)>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|E<rsub|s>> is its compensated action including the fixed
  far-field term, and <math|n<rsub|s>> is the number of chart normalizers in
  the packet. The restrictions deciding whether the packet occurs are the
  same in both representations. If its enforcement cutoff vanishes on a
  neighborhood of <math|y<rsub|0>>, both germs vanish. Otherwise the packet
  action estimate applies. Let <math|s<rsub|\<ast\>>> be the maximum packet
  size and let <math|m<rsub|\<ast\>>> be the total number of fine bonds. Both
  are finite on this torus. Then, with <math|Y=1+A*s<rsub|\<ast\>>+m<rsub|\<ast\>>>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|H<rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>t*Y,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|w<rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>B.
  </equation*>

  Choose <math|t\<leqslant\>Y<rsup|-1>>. The exponential comparison and
  product rule give <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|w<rsub|s><rprime|'>-w<rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|p>>*t>,
  uniformly over the finite packet catalogue and all its admissible auxiliary
  choices.

  For a forced incoming factor, the held fine field is the same and the
  enforcement cutoff is the same. Its value difference is at most
  <math|b<rsub|i>>, and its cutoff has a common jet bound
  <math|C<rsub|<math-up|e>>>. Hence its factor difference has jet bound
  <math|C<rsub|<math-up|e>>*b<rsub|i>>. This differentiates only the cutoff,
  not the incoming forced activity.

  For logarithmic factors write <math|w<rsub|i>=e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|i>>-1>.
  The bounded base logarithm and the summable base factor prices give
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<ell\><rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>C*u<rsub|i>>
  by <eqref|eq:local-parameter-log-recovery>. The actual logarithmic
  catalogue has two cases. An inherited regular activity has logarithm minus
  its held value times the common enforcement cutoff; its logarithm
  difference therefore has jet bound <math|C<rsub|<math-up|e>>*a<rsub|i>>.
  Every other catalogue entry is either unchanged by the rescaling, or is
  multiplied by <math|\<lambda\><rsup|2>>. This follows term by term from the
  quadratic noise terms and <math|\<beta\>*\<lambda\><rsup|2>=\<beta\><rprime|'>>;
  the path and loop terms without that quadratic scaling remain unchanged.
  Thus

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|\<ell\><rsub|i><rprime|'>-\<ell\><rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>C*<around|{|<wide|a|~><rsub|i>+t*u<rsub|i>|}>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|<wide|a|~><rsub|i>> places the regular difference amplitudes at
  their inherited logarithmic addresses and is zero elsewhere. The address
  embedding is injective, so <math|<big|sum><rsub|i><wide|a|~><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>t>.
  Since <math|t\<leqslant\>1> and <math|u<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>B>, the right
  side is at most <math|C*<around|(|1+B|)>>. Applying
  <eqref|eq:local-parameter-exp-difference> gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|w<rsub|i><rprime|'>-w<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|l>>*<around|(|<wide|a|~><rsub|i>+t*u<rsub|i>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Finally, warning factors are exactly unchanged: their charge sets, forced
  supports and physical field agree. Their difference price is zero.

  There are finitely many packet labels, say <math|n<rsub|\<ast\>>>.
  Combining the four cases gives nonnegative difference prices
  <math|v<rsub|i>> satisfying

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:local-parameter-elementary-differences><around|\<\|\|\>|w<rsub|i><rprime|'>-w<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>v<rsub|i>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|i>v<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>t*K<rsub|\<ast\>>,<space|1em>K<rsub|\<ast\>>=n<rsub|\<ast\>>*C<rsub|<math-up|p>>+C<rsub|<math-up|e>>+C<rsub|<math-up|l>>*<around|(|1+B|)>.
  </equation>

  The constants are uniform over the auxiliary choices at this fixed torus.
  This is stronger than continuity of each individual factor: the whole
  catalogue has a summable difference bound.

  <subsubsection|Products over components>

  Set <math|a<rsub|i>=u<rsub|i>+v<rsub|i>/t>. Then
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>B+K<rsub|\<ast\>>>,
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|w<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|i>>,
  and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|w<rsub|i><rprime|'>-w<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>t*a<rsub|i>>.
  For every finite set of labels <math|P>, put

  <\equation*>
    p<rsub|P>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>P>3*<space|0.17em>3<rsup|j>*a<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|0\<less\>t\<leqslant\>1>, induction on <math|<around|\||P|\|>>
  yields

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:local-parameter-component-products><around*|\||<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>P>w<rsub|i><rprime|'>|\|><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>p<rsub|P>,<space|2em><around*|\||<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>P>w<rsub|i><rprime|'>-<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>P>w<rsub|i>|\|><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>t*p<rsub|P>.
  </equation>

  For the induction step use <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|w<rsub|i><rprime|'>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>2*a<rsub|i>>
  and

  <\equation*>
    w<rsub|i><rprime|'>*G<rprime|'>-w<rsub|i>*G=<around|(|w<rsub|i><rprime|'>-w<rsub|i>|)>*G<rprime|'>+w<rsub|i>*<around|(|G<rprime|'>-G|)>.
  </equation*>

  Each product costs at most <math|3<rsup|j>>; the chosen factor
  <math|3*<space|0.17em>3<rsup|j>*a<rsub|i>> pays both terms and the bound
  for the new product. The empty product has value one and difference zero.

  Summing nonnegative products over finite subsets gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:local-parameter-product-sum><big|sum><rsub|P>p<rsub|P>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around|{|3*<space|0.17em>3<rsup|j>*<around|(|B+K<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>|}>.
  </equation>

  For a finite catalogue this is <math|<big|prod><rsub|i><around|(|1+3*<space|0.17em>3<rsup|j>*a<rsub|i>|)>>
  and <math|1+x\<leqslant\>e<rsup|x>>. Exhaustion and monotone convergence
  prove the countable case. Restricting to admissible molecules only
  decreases the sum. All supports and selection conditions agree under the
  common address identification.

  We must identify these products with the actual component integrands.
  Assume both representations satisfy the incoming room and activity bounds
  for this same sector. The protected compensation map holds the physical
  fine field fixed on a neighborhood of <math|y<rsub|0>>. On that
  neighborhood, the definition of each component therefore equals the product
  of the elementary compensated factors with its fixed charge set. The
  neighboring compensation has the same protected patch: its noise course is
  multiplied by <math|\<lambda\>>, and its coupling cancels that factor in
  the physical field. Applying the same identity to the neighboring
  representation proves equality of its component germ with the primed
  product. Intersect the two neighborhoods. Health of the union of the
  assigned supports implies health of every factor support, because each is
  contained in that union. Thus all the bounds above apply to these actual
  germs, and their derivatives are the derivatives of the displayed finite
  products.

  <subsubsection|The density compensation and the Gaussian ratio>

  The exact compensation identity has the anchored form

  <\equation*>
    K<rprime|'>*<around|(|y,\<lambda\>*z|)>=K*<around|(|y,z|)>*exp
    <around|{|<around|(|1-\<lambda\><rsup|2>|)>*<around|(|q*<around|(|y,z|)>-q*<around|(|y<rsub|0>,z|)>|)>|}>.
  </equation*>

  The subtraction at <math|y<rsub|0>> is essential. Put
  <math|h<around|(|y|)>=<around|(|1-\<lambda\><rsup|2>|)>*<around|(|q*<around|(|y,z|)>-q*<around|(|y<rsub|0>,z|)>|)>>.
  Then <math|h<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>=0>, and every positive-order derivative
  through <math|j> is bounded by <math|t*C*F<rsub|0><around|(|z|)><rsup|2>>.
  In the partition formula for a derivative of <math|e<rsup|h>> at
  <math|y<rsub|0>>, a term with <math|r\<geqslant\>1> blocks is bounded by
  <math|t<rsup|r>*C<rsup|r>*F<rsub|0><around|(|z|)><rsup|2*r>>. Since
  <math|t\<leqslant\>1> and <math|F<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>1>, it is at most
  <math|t*C<rsup|r>*F<rsub|0><rsup|2*j>>. The zero-order difference
  <math|e<rsup|h<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>>-1> is zero. Consequently, also when
  <math|j=0>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|e<rsup|h>-1|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>t*C<rsub|j>*F<rsub|0><around|(|z|)><rsup|2*j>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|K<rprime|'>-K|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>t*C<rsub|1>*F<rsub|0><around|(|z|)><rsup|N+2*j>.
  </equation*>

  No exponential of an uncontrolled positive polynomial in <math|z> is
  introduced here.

  Write <math|g<rsub|P>,g<rsub|P><rprime|'>> for the component products in
  <eqref|eq:local-parameter-component-products>. With the fixed Gaussian
  density ratio <math|J<rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|z|)>>, which is independent
  of <math|y>, use the identity

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|J<rsub|\<lambda\>>*K<rprime|'>*g<rsub|P><rprime|'>-K*g<rsub|P>=>|<cell|J<rsub|\<lambda\>>*<around|(|K<rprime|'>-K|)>*g<rsub|P><rprime|'>+<around|(|J<rsub|\<lambda\>>-1|)>*K*g<rsub|P><rprime|'>+K*<around|(|g<rsub|P><rprime|'>-g<rsub|P>|)>.>>>>
  </align*>

  The preceding estimates and the product rule give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:local-parameter-compensated-bound><around|\<\|\|\>|J<rsub|\<lambda\>>*K<rprime|'>*g<rsub|P><rprime|'>-K*g<rsub|P>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>t*C<rsub|2>*p<rsub|P>*F<rsub|0><around|(|z|)><rsup|N+2*j>*<around|{|J<rsub|\<lambda\>>+<around|\||J<rsub|\<lambda\>>-1|\|>/t+1|}>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|C<rsub|2>> is fixed and <math|<big|sum><rsub|P>C<rsub|2>*p<rsub|P>\<less\>\<infty\>>
  by <eqref|eq:local-parameter-product-sum>. This is the required local
  compensated comparison. The Gaussian estimates then integrate it with a
  common integrable dominator and preserve all jets through order <math|j>.

  <subsubsection|Packet values outside the healthy jet domain>

  For the forced output one also needs value estimates for packets outside
  the healthy domain used above. An upper bound on the packet energy itself
  is neither available nor needed. The actual packet has the form

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|\<beta\>>=m<rsub|0>*exp <around|{|-\<beta\>*u+v<rsub|\<beta\>>|}>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>m<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|0>,<space|1em>u\<geqslant\>-d<rsub|0>,<space|1em>v<rsub|\<beta\>>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|0>,
  </equation*>

  with <math|d<rsub|0>,M<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0> fixed on this torus. The
  packet energy lower bound supplies <math|d<rsub|0>>; bounded cutoff and
  charge factors supply <math|M<rsub|0>>. The unchanged condensation and
  chart terms, and the finite number of normalizers, give
  <math|<around|\||v<rsub|\<beta\><rprime|'>>-v<rsub|\<beta\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>n<rsub|0>*t>
  for a finite <math|n<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0>. Assume
  <math|<around|\||\<beta\><rprime|'>-\<beta\>|\|>\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>min
  <around|(|1,\<beta\>/2|)>> and put

  <\equation*>
    c=\<beta\>/2\<gtr\>0,<space|2em>D=A<rsub|0>+n<rsub|0>+<around|(|\<beta\>+1|)>*d<rsub|0>.
  </equation*>

  If <math|-d<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>u\<leqslant\>0>, both exponential arguments
  are at most <math|D>, and their difference is at most
  <math|t*<around|(|d<rsub|0>+n<rsub|0>|)>>. The mean value theorem gives a
  bound <math|t*m<rsub|0>*e<rsup|D>*<around|(|d<rsub|0>+n<rsub|0>|)>>. If
  <math|u\<gtr\>0>, both arguments are at most <math|D-c*u> and their
  difference is at most <math|t*<around|(|u+n<rsub|0>|)>>. Thus

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||P<rsub|\<beta\><rprime|'>>-P<rsub|\<beta\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>t*m<rsub|0>*e<rsup|D>*<around|(|u+n<rsub|0>|)>*e<rsup|-c*u>\<leqslant\>t*m<rsub|0>*e<rsup|D>*<around|(|c<rsup|-1>+n<rsub|0>|)>,
  </equation*>

  using <math|c*u\<leqslant\>e<rsup|c*u>> and
  <math|e<rsup|-c*u>\<leqslant\>1>. Combining both cases yields

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:local-parameter-forced-packet><around|\||P<rsub|\<beta\><rprime|'>>-P<rsub|\<beta\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>t*M<rsub|0>*e<rsup|D>*<around|(|d<rsub|0>+2*n<rsub|0>+c<rsup|-1>|)>.
  </equation>

  Zero packet masks remain zero under the common data and require no
  estimate. This proves the packet value comparison uniformly over the
  unconstrained positive-energy tail. The corresponding forced product and
  Gaussian integration arguments use value bounds; they do not assert
  derivative estimates on that tail.

  For completeness, the other unrestricted value factors have the same
  summable comparison. Let <math|r<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0> bound the base
  logarithms and put <math|R<rsub|0>=<big|sum><rsub|i>r<rsub|i>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
  Their differences are bounded by <math|d<rsub|i>=t*r<rsub|i>+<wide|a|~><rsub|i>>,
  so <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>d<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>t*<around|(|R<rsub|0>+1|)>>.
  Both the base and new logarithms are at most <math|2*R<rsub|0>+1> when
  <math|t\<leqslant\>1>. The mean value theorem gives the exponential-factor
  difference price <math|e<rsup|2*R<rsub|0>+1>*d<rsub|i>>. Forced incoming
  factors have difference price <math|b<rsub|i>>, since their common cutoff
  lies in <math|<around|[|0,1|]>>; warnings cancel. Together with
  <eqref|eq:local-parameter-forced-packet>, the finite packet catalogue
  therefore has an overall elementary difference budget <math|t*C<rsub|3>>.

  The corresponding base value prices are summable as well. Packets and
  warnings have finite catalogues. The logarithmic catalogue has its summable
  base price, while the forced incoming prices are bounded by their rooted
  weighted prices. Every forced support is nonempty: it contains the parent
  of an inspected incoming inventory site. Summing the rooted bound over the
  finitely many torus sites therefore bounds the unrooted sum of forced
  prices. The weights removed in this argument are at least one.

  Apply the product argument above with <math|j=0> to these value prices.
  There are summable <math|p<rsub|P><rsup|<math-up|v>>\<geqslant\>0> such
  that <math|<around|\||G<rsub|P><rprime|'>|\|>\<leqslant\>p<rsub|P><rsup|<math-up|v>>>
  and <math|<around|\||G<rsub|P><rprime|'>-G<rsub|P>|\|>\<leqslant\>t*p<rsub|P><rsup|<math-up|v>>>
  for the rescaled full component values. Before integration use

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||J<rsub|\<lambda\>>*G<rsub|P><rprime|'>-G<rsub|P>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||J<rsub|\<lambda\>>-1|\|>*p<rsub|P><rsup|<math-up|v>>+t*p<rsub|P><rsup|<math-up|v>>.
  </equation*>

  The Gaussian ratio estimate at moment order zero bounds the integral by
  <math|t*C<rsub|4>*p<rsub|P><rsup|<math-up|v>>>. Haar variables keep their
  normalized measure. The common support and charge data also identify all
  eligibility conditions. A normalized finite tag average preserves this
  bound; if a component is ineligible, both terms are zero. This proves the
  forced value comparison needed for the exponential expansion of the output
  array.

  <subsection|Continuity of the nonlinear output on a fixed
  torus><label|sec:finite-volume-parameter-continuity>

  The nonlinear step must vary continuously with its incoming coupling and
  activity array. This assertion concerns the actual array in a fixed support
  dictionary. Equality of the evaluated gas would be insufficient. The
  constants in this section may depend on the fixed torus; uniform small
  prices for the scale construction are established separately.

  <subsubsection|Which data are held fixed>

  Consider two incoming representations <math|R,R<rprime|'>> on the same fine
  torus. They have the same regular and forced addresses, thresholds,
  clipping convention and path data. Write
  <math|\<beta\>,\<beta\><rprime|'>\<gtr\>0> for their couplings. Their
  regular and forced activity differences have nonnegative amplitudes
  <math|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>> with

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|i>a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>t,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|i>b<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>t.
  </equation*>

  The regular differences here are differences of the actual masked
  occurrences. Their supports and healthy masks agree exactly. Let
  <math|g<around|(|\<beta\>|)>=\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>> and put
  <math|\<lambda\>=g<around|(|\<beta\>|)>/g<around|(|\<beta\><rprime|'>|)>\<gtr\>0>.
  The remaining comparison conditions are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:parameter-close-input><around|\||\<lambda\><rsup|2>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>t,<space|1em><around|\||\<beta\><rprime|'>-\<beta\>|\|>\<leqslant\>t,<space|1em><around|\||log
    m<around|(|\<beta\><rprime|'>|)>-log m<around|(|\<beta\>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>t,<space|2em>0\<less\>t\<leqslant\>min
    <around|(|1/4,\<beta\>/2|)>,
  </equation>

  where <math|m<around|(|\<beta\>|)>\<gtr\>0> is the fixed local Haar-chart
  normalizing factor. All forests, tags, component labels and output address
  identifications are retained. In particular, their compatibility and
  eligibility conditions agree under the identity on elementary labels.

  For a continuous incoming curve these conditions hold locally for
  arbitrarily small <math|t>. The activity conditions follow from its array
  continuity; multiplication by the fixed healthy mask preserves the value
  difference and makes both occurrences zero off that mask. The scalar
  conditions follow from continuity of the positive coupling, its inverse
  square root, and the logarithm of <math|m>. This observation uses the same
  path throughout the curve.

  <subsubsection|A Gaussian rescaling estimate>

  We explain why changing the coupling does not require differentiating the
  incoming measurable activities. On one fixed Gaussian space of dimension
  <math|d>, with positive precision matrix <math|Q>, put

  <\equation*>
    p<around|(|z|)>=c<rsub|Q>*e<rsup|-z<rsup|T>*Q*z/2>,<space|2em>J<rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|z|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|d>*<frac|p*<around|(|\<lambda\>*z|)>|p<around|(|z|)>>.
  </equation*>

  Thus, if <math|C> denotes the Gaussian covariance, <math|Q=C<rsup|-1>>; the
  two matrices are kept distinct throughout this comparison. Then for every
  integrable function <math|F>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:parameter-gaussian-rescale><big|int>F<around|(|z|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|Q><around|(|z|)>=<big|int>J<rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|z|)>*F*<around|(|\<lambda\>*z|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|Q><around|(|z|)>.
  </equation>

  This is the ordinary change of variables <math|w=\<lambda\>*z>. The Haar
  factor, when present, is left unchanged. Since
  <math|g<around|(|\<beta\><rprime|'>|)>*\<lambda\>=g<around|(|\<beta\>|)>>,
  the rescaled incoming field has the same physical noise; the corresponding
  compensation map keeps the original insertion values fixed.

  For <math|<around|\||\<lambda\><rsup|2>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>1/4>,
  we have <math|\<lambda\>\<geqslant\>1/2> and <math|<around|\||log
  \<lambda\>|\|>\<leqslant\>t>. Consequently

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||log J<rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>t*<around|{|d+<tfrac|1|2><around|\<\|\|\>|Q|\<\|\|\>><space|0.17em><around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|F<rsub|0><around|(|z|)>=1+d+<big|sum><rsub|r=1><rsup|d><around|\||z<rsub|r>|\|>>
  and choose a finite constant <math|C<rsub|Q>> such that the braces are at
  most <math|C<rsub|Q>*F<rsub|0><around|(|z|)><rsup|2>>. The inequality
  <math|<around|\||u-1|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+u|)><around|\||log u|\|>>
  for <math|u\<gtr\>0> follows by integrating <math|e<rsup|s>> between
  <math|0> and <math|log u>. Thus

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||J<rsub|\<lambda\>>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>t*C<rsub|Q>*<around|(|1+J<rsub|\<lambda\>>|)>*F<rsub|0><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  All polynomial moments of <math|F<rsub|0>> are finite, by the finite
  Gaussian moment argument. If <math|M<rsub|k>=<big|int>F<rsub|0><rsup|k>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|Q>>,
  then <eqref|eq:parameter-gaussian-rescale> and
  <math|F<rsub|0>*<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|-1>*z|)>\<leqslant\>2*F<rsub|0><around|(|z|)>>
  give

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int>J<rsub|\<lambda\>>*F<rsub|0><rsup|k>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|Q>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>2<rsup|k>*M<rsub|k>,<big|int><around|\||J<rsub|\<lambda\>>-1|\|>*F<rsub|0><rsup|k>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|Q>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>t*C<rsub|Q>*<around|(|1+2<rsup|k+2>|)>*M<rsub|k+2>.<eq-number><label|eq:parameter-gaussian-moment-difference>>>>>
  </align>

  The same estimates hold for the full finite collection of Gaussian read
  coordinates and the product with normalized Haar measure. No smaller
  marginal is substituted when the parameter changes.

  Here is the precise pointwise input needed to integrate local jet
  differences. Fix integers <math|j,k\<geqslant\>0>. For every component
  <math|P>, suppose its compensated integrands
  <math|f<rsub|P>,f<rsub|P><rprime|'>> have, on each admissible local
  variation, the bound

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:parameter-integrand-difference><around|\<\|\|\>|J<rsub|\<lambda\>>*f<rsub|P><rprime|'>|(>*\<cdummy\>,\<lambda\>*z)-f<rsub|P>*<around|(|\<cdummy\>,z|)>\<\|\|\><rsub|J<rsup|j>>\<leqslant\>t*c<rsub|P>*F<rsub|0><around|(|z|)><rsup|k>*<around|{|J<rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|z|)>+t<rsup|-1>\|J<rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|z|)>-1\|+1|}>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|P>c<rsub|P>\<leqslant\>C.
  </equation>

  Here <math|c<rsub|P>\<geqslant\>0>. The constants and <math|k> are common
  to the neighboring representation and to the forests and tags on this fixed
  torus. This is the pointwise compensated comparison estimate, a stronger
  input than individual boundedness of the two integrands. Its role is
  explicit: <eqref|eq:parameter-gaussian-moment-difference> gives uniform
  integral bounds. For a pointwise dominator, use

  <\equation*>
    J<rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|z|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|5/4|)><rsup|d/2>*exp
    <around|(|z<rsup|T>*Q*z/8|)>.
  </equation*>

  Together with <math|t<rsup|-1>*<around|\||J<rsub|\<lambda\>>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|Q>*<around|(|1+J<rsub|\<lambda\>>|)>*F<rsub|0><rsup|2>>,
  this bounds the integrand after division by <math|t> by a fixed polynomial
  times <math|1+exp <around|(|z<rsup|T>*Q*z/8|)>>. Multiplying by the
  Gaussian density leaves a decaying exponential <math|exp
  <around|(|-3*z<rsup|T>*Q*z/8|)>>, so this dominator is integrable.
  Integration and the derivative identities of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-differentiation> give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:parameter-component-difference><around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|P><rprime|'>-F<rsub|P>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|J<rsup|j>>\<leqslant\>t*C<rsub|1>*c<rsub|P>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|P>C<rsub|1>*c<rsub|P>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|1>*C.
  </equation>

  For example one may take <math|C<rsub|1>=<around|(|1+2<rsup|k>|)>*M<rsub|k>+C<rsub|Q>*<around|(|1+2<rsup|k+2>|)>*M<rsub|k+2>>.
  The same bound survives normalized forest and tag averaging and the common
  local extension, since their germs agree on the healthy domain. The
  construction of <eqref|eq:parameter-integrand-difference> from the
  individual logarithmic, packet and compensation factors remains a separate
  local estimate; it is not inferred merely from continuity of the scalar
  coupling.

  <subsubsection|A summable difference through the cluster logarithm>

  We prove the summation step rather than interchange an infinite series with
  a limit without a common bound. Write <math|u<rsub|P>\<geqslant\>0> for
  component jet prices. Fix a target support exponent <math|a\<geqslant\>0>;
  the cluster comparison itself also holds for arbitrary real <math|a>.
  Suppose the ordinary cluster criterion holds for
  <math|2<rsup|j>*u<rsub|P>>, with reserve <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>, root
  price <math|\<gamma\>\<gtr\>0> and ratio <math|1/2>. Suppose component
  differences have prices <math|t*d<rsub|P>>, where
  <math|d<rsub|P>\<geqslant\>0> and <math|<big|sum><rsub|P>d<rsub|P>\<leqslant\>C>.
  On the fixed torus let

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|0>=<big|sum><rsub|Y\<subseteq\>\<Lambda\>>e<rsup|<around|(|a+\<delta\>|)>*c<around|(|Y|)>>,<space|2em>\<kappa\>=<frac|\<gamma\>|2*K<rsub|0>*2<rsup|j>*<around|(|1+C|)>>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation*>

  The finite sum includes every possible support. For every root, the
  additional vertex budget from <math|2<rsup|j>*\<kappa\>*d<rsub|P>> is at
  most <math|K<rsub|0>*2<rsup|j>*\<kappa\>*C\<leqslant\>\<gamma\>/2>. The
  augmented prices <math|2<rsup|j>*<around|(|u<rsub|P>+\<kappa\>*d<rsub|P>|)>>
  therefore satisfy the cluster criterion with root price
  <math|3*\<gamma\>/2> and ratio <math|3/4>: the degree quantity is linear in
  the root price. This chooses one neighborhood size <math|\<kappa\>> after
  the torus and old criterion are fixed.

  For <math|0\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>\<kappa\>>, set
  <math|\<theta\>=t/\<kappa\>\<leqslant\>1>. The finite product identity
  gives, in the joint jet bounds,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:parameter-product-difference><around*|\||<big|prod><rsub|r=1><rsup|q>F<rsub|P<rsub|r>><rprime|'>-<big|prod><rsub|r=1><rsup|q>F<rsub|P<rsub|r>>|\|><rsub|J<rsup|j>>\<leqslant\>\<theta\>*<around|(|1+q|)><rsup|j>*<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|r=1><rsup|q><around|(|u<rsub|P<rsub|r>>+\<kappa\>*d<rsub|P<rsub|r>>|)>-<big|prod><rsub|r=1><rsup|q>u<rsub|P<rsub|r>>|}>.
  </equation>

  To verify it, expand each <math|F<rsub|P><rprime|'>=F<rsub|P>+<around|(|F<rsub|P><rprime|'>-F<rsub|P>|)>>.
  Every nonempty subset of difference factors contains at least one factor
  <math|\<theta\>>; its higher powers are bounded by <math|\<theta\>>. At
  derivative order <math|r> there are <math|q<rsup|r>> assignments to the
  factors, bounded by <math|<around|(|1+q|)><rsup|j>>. Summing the nonempty
  subsets gives exactly the difference of the positive products on the right.
  Repeated component labels are still distinct positions in this calculation.

  Since <math|1+q\<leqslant\>2<rsup|q>> for <math|q\<geqslant\>1>, the
  derivative factor is paid by <math|2<rsup|j>> per component. Multiply by
  the absolute Ursell coefficient and its factorial normalization, then use
  the augmented cluster criterion. Summing rooted bounds over all
  <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>> sites bounds the unrooted sum, because
  every nonzero cluster has nonempty support. Thus cluster differences have
  nonnegative prices <math|t*e<rsub|\<Gamma\>>> with

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|\<Gamma\>>e<rsub|\<Gamma\>>\<leqslant\>\<kappa\><rsup|-1><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|><frac|3*\<gamma\>/2|1-3/4>.
  </equation*>

  This estimate uses the original cluster criterion with a paid perturbation
  margin; no new uniform small-price theorem for the neighboring
  representation has been assumed.

  <subsubsection|Forced products and the output array>

  The same product calculation applies to the forced exponential expansion.
  If its component values have summable bounds <math|h<rsub|i>> and
  differences bounded by <math|t*d<rsub|i>>, with
  <math|0\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>1>, then a finite multiplicity vector
  <math|\<nu\>> satisfies

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||<big|prod><rsub|i><frac|<around|(|F<rsub|i><rprime|'>|)><rsup|\<nu\><rsub|i>>|\<nu\><rsub|i>!>-<big|prod><rsub|i><frac|F<rsub|i><rsup|\<nu\><rsub|i>>|\<nu\><rsub|i>!>|\|>\<leqslant\>t*<big|prod><rsub|i><frac|<around|(|h<rsub|i>+d<rsub|i>|)><rsup|\<nu\><rsub|i>>|\<nu\><rsub|i>!>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed apply the same difference-of-products argument to repeated factors
  and divide by their positive factorials. Summing over all finite
  multiplicity vectors yields

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>><big|prod><rsub|i><frac|<around|(|h<rsub|i>+d<rsub|i>|)><rsup|\<nu\><rsub|i>>|\<nu\><rsub|i>!>=exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i><around|(|h<rsub|i>+d<rsub|i>|)>|)>,
  </equation*>

  by finite products of the exponential series followed by monotone
  convergence. Compatibility or eligibility conditions only remove terms;
  they agree under the fixed address identification. This proves a summable
  forced difference bound.

  The remaining fixed path terms are affine in the coupling. If
  <math|S<rsub|i><around|(|\<beta\>|)>> is such a term, then
  <math|S<rsub|i><around|(|\<beta\>|)>-S<rsub|i><around|(|\<beta\><rprime|'>|)>=<around|(|\<beta\>-\<beta\><rprime|'>|)>*<around|{|S<rsub|i><around|(|1|)>-S<rsub|i><around|(|0|)>|}>>.
  Their two summable derivative prices therefore bound the difference by
  <math|t> times a summable array. A finite symmetry average preserves
  summability after its explicit fixed derivative factor. Any support weight
  <math|e<rsup|A*c<around|(|Y|)>>> on this fixed torus is at most
  <math|e<rsup|A<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>>>, and moving an anchor costs at
  most a fixed power of <math|1+<math-up|diam><space|0.17em>\<Lambda\>>.
  Hence all these operations convert the preceding sums into the prescribed
  regular array budget in the jet norm and forced array budget in the value
  supremum norm, bounded by <math|K*t> for a finite <math|K> depending on the
  base representation and torus.

  This also supplies the common majorant needed along a subsequence. If input
  parameters <math|x<rsub|n>> tend to <math|x>, select an increasing
  subsequence <math|n<rsub|k>> for which the output differences from <math|x>
  have summable nonnegative prices <math|d<rsub|k,i>> with
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>d<rsub|k,i>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|-k>>. If
  <math|h<rsub|i>> majorizes the output at <math|x>, put

  <\equation*>
    H<rsub|i>=h<rsub|i>+<big|sum><rsub|k>d<rsub|k,i>.
  </equation*>

  Tonelli's theorem gives <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>H<rsub|i>\<less\>\<infty\>>,
  and the triangle inequality shows that <math|H<rsub|i>> bounds both the
  output at <math|x> and all outputs on this subsequence, component by
  component in the required jet or value norm. The regular and forced arrays
  are treated separately in this construction. It proves the required
  continuity and subsequential domination in the common dictionary,
  conditional on the local compensated difference estimate
  <eqref|eq:parameter-integrand-difference> and its forced counterpart.

  <subsection|Supplying the common comparison
  estimates><label|sec:parameter-comparison-suppliers>

  The preceding comparison arguments use one collection of local bounds for
  the base representation. We show how the local estimates already proved
  provide that collection, and how its parameters are chosen before a running
  representation is inserted. Constants in a fixed-volume continuity estimate
  may depend on that volume. The radius of the allowed coarse variations and
  the large-coupling threshold used for the construction have a stronger
  uniformity.

  Fix the branch data, a positive target support reserve, the derivative
  order <math|j>, the cutoff convention, and positive width and packet-energy
  parameters. The scale factor is taken above the common floor in the local
  activity and Gaussian estimates. Fix a coarse variation scale
  <math|v<around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>0> of upper exponent <math|-1/2>. All
  estimates in this section refer to the same fine representation, sector,
  tags and held-field compensation.

  <subsubsection|Elementary factors and conditional packet actions>

  The construction in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:activity-jet-assembly>
  provides prices for the elementary compensated factors together with their
  support-dependent derivative scales. At the fixed derivative order, choose
  a positive spare support and extra-cost reserve <math|\<delta\>>. If
  <math|s> denotes the support-cover plus length charge, then
  <math|<around|(|1+s|)><rsup|j>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,\<delta\>>*e<rsup|\<delta\>*s>>
  for <math|s\<geqslant\>0>: the continuous function
  <math|<around|(|1+s|)><rsup|j>*e<rsup|-\<delta\>*s>> is bounded on
  <math|<around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>>. Apply the weighted price estimate with
  this reserve before removing the weights. This pays the factor
  <math|r<around|(|x|)><rsup|j>*<around|(|1+s|)><rsup|j>> from the derivative
  scale and gives scale-one jet prices at every order through <math|j>. The
  warning catalogue is finite on the fixed torus and its finite jet prices
  can also be summed. To pass from a rooted weighted sum to its unweighted
  sum on a fixed torus, sum the rooted inequality over all its sites. Every
  nonzero factor has nonempty assigned support and is counted at least once;
  all removed support and extra-cost weights are at least one. The same
  argument applies to the base logarithmic prices. It gives both the finite
  total elementary price and the finite common bound on the absolute values
  of the real logarithms used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-parameter-comparison>.
  The prices need not be small after this volume-dependent summation.

  There is a separate condition on the packet action: its differentiated
  bound is needed whenever the enforcement cutoff is not a zero germ. Write
  that cutoff as a finite product of its bond cutoffs. If any bond cutoff
  were zero on a neighborhood of the base point, the whole product would be
  zero there. Thus a nonzero packet germ makes every relevant bond cutoff a
  nonzero germ. The compensated cutoff estimates then bound the corresponding
  increments and their derivatives by

  <\equation*>
    s<around|(|x|)>+2*a*\<tau\><around|(|x|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|s> has upper exponent <math|-1/2> and <math|a\<gtr\>0> is the
  fixed compensation radius parameter. The mean coordinates have the same
  upper exponent, by Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mean-action-jets>.
  Enlarge these bounds to one nonnegative function of upper exponent zero.

  The last subsection of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mean-action-jets>
  applies to an arbitrary held fine field. Each local packet-action summand
  is a held fine action, a varying coarse action, or an extracted affine
  square. Its derivatives are bounded by the corresponding local constants
  there. Let <math|s> count all seeds of the packet: its fine plaquettes,
  coarse bonds and flagged coarse sites. The number of such energy addresses
  is at most

  <\equation*>
    s*<space|0.17em>b*<around|(|200*B+1|)>*<space|0.17em>n<rsub|L>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|b<around|(|r|)>> is the finite lattice-ball count at radius
  <math|r> and <math|n<rsub|L>> is the fixed number of fine energy addresses
  per coarse block. Summing the local jet bounds, including the far-field
  term, gives <math|A<around|(|x|)>*s> with one finite
  <math|A<around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>0>. The same bound holds on every
  admissible chart and for every held fine field. In particular this supplies
  the conditional packet-action estimate, without requiring smoothness of the
  fine input or an upper bound on its uncut energy.

  <subsubsection|Every read subset has the same polynomial bound>

  Let <math|I> be any subset of fine bonds, including the empty subset. The
  projected covariance contains all colours of those bonds. Write
  <math|C<rsub|I><around|(|y|)>> for this covariance and
  <math|w<rsub|I>*<around|(|y,z|)>> for the compensated Gaussian coordinate.
  Its inverse exists by the common positive covariance estimate. The
  quadratic exponent is

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|I>*<around|(|y,z|)>=<tfrac|1|2>w<rsub|I>*<around|(|y,z|)><rsup|T>*C<rsub|I><around|(|y|)><rsup|-1>*w<rsub|I>*<around|(|y,z|)>.
  </equation*>

  The inverse-matrix estimates and weighted matrix product estimates of
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:normalization-jets> and
  <reference|sec:gaussian-differentiation> apply to every principal read
  subset with the same row constants. This is where the projected covariance
  bounds, rather than merely positivity of the full covariance, are used.

  For clarity, the finite-dimensional multiplication giving the quadratic
  bound can be seen directly. Suppose the coordinate jets of <math|w<rsub|I>>
  are at most <math|W>, and the jets of the inverse matrix have row sums at
  most <math|B<rsub|r>> at order <math|r\<leqslant\>j>. A derivative of
  <math|w<rsub|I><rsup|T>*C<rsub|I><rsup|-1>*w<rsub|I>> distributes its
  ordered directions among three factors, giving at most <math|3<rsup|j>>
  terms. Sum the middle matrix row first. Each term is at most
  <math|<around|\||I|\|>*dim \<frak-g\>> times
  <math|W<rsup|2>*max<rsub|r\<leqslant\>j> B<rsub|r>>. The factor <math|1/2>
  can be discarded in an upper bound. On the full torus the
  compensated-coordinate estimates give

  <\equation*>
    W\<leqslant\><around*|[|1+g<around|(|\<beta\>|)><rsup|-1>*<around|{|s<around|(|x|)>+2*A<rsub|<math-up|mean>><around|(|x|)>|}>|]>*F<rsub|0><around|(|z|)>=V<rsub|0>*F<rsub|0><around|(|z|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|F<rsub|0>> uses every fine bond and colour and
  <math|V<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>1> is finite. Since <math|<around|\||I|\|>*dim
  \<frak-g\>> is bounded by the full Gaussian dimension, we obtain

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:comparison-quadratic-supplier><around|\<\|\|\>|q<rsub|I>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|\<ast\>>*F<rsub|0><around|(|z|)><rsup|2><space|1em><text|for
    every >I.
  </equation>

  All constants on the right are independent of the chosen subset. For
  <math|I=\<varnothing\>>, the quadratic form is zero.

  The relative density compensation <math|K<rsub|I>> is the Jacobian from
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:compensated-variation> times the Gaussian
  density ratio from Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-differentiation>.
  At the base point its value is one. The finite-order determinant and
  inverse estimates there give, for some integer <math|k> and nonnegative
  finite <math|U<around|(|x|)>>, a derivative scale bounded by
  <math|U<around|(|x|)>*F<rsub|I><around|(|z|)><rsup|k>>, where

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|I><around|(|z|)>=1+<around|\||I|\|>*dim
    \<frak-g\>+<big|sum><rsub|<around|(|e,a|)>\<in\>I\<times\>\<frak-g\>><around|\||z<rsub|e,a>|\|>.
  </equation*>

  In this expression the colour index means a fixed orthonormal basis. The
  bound uses only the prescribed derivative order, the local row prices and
  the compensation Jacobian bounds. Its polynomial degree is fixed before the
  subset is selected. Since <math|1\<leqslant\>F<rsub|I>\<leqslant\>F<rsub|0>>,

  <\equation*>
    max<rsub|0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>j><around|{|U<around|(|x|)>*F<rsub|I><around|(|z|)><rsup|k>|}><rsup|r>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+U<around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|j>*F<rsub|0><around|(|z|)><rsup|k*j>.
  </equation*>

  Together with <eqref|eq:comparison-quadratic-supplier>, this supplies
  <eqref|eq:local-parameter-density-input> simultaneously for all <math|I>,
  with <math|N=k*j> and <math|C<rsub|0>=<around|(|1+U<around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|j>+C<rsub|\<ast\>>>.
  The quadratic constant <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>> is allowed to depend on the
  torus and base coupling; no uniform volume bound is asserted for it.

  The same compensated map provides the held-field and protected-patch
  identities. The open-neighborhood envelope in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-differentiation> justifies
  differentiation of its integrals. Thus the algebraic pointwise comparison
  uses the actual integrands to which that differentiation theorem applies.

  <subsubsection|Unrestricted packet values>

  The lower packet-energy bound in the seed estimates applies on the support
  of the packet mask. Adding the nonnegative far-field energy preserves this
  lower bound. At a fixed stage it gives <math|u\<geqslant\>-E<around|(|x|)>*s\<geqslant\>-E<around|(|x|)>*s<rsub|\<ast\>>>
  in the notation of <eqref|eq:local-parameter-forced-packet>. The product of
  profile, charge and enforcement factors lies in <math|<around|[|0,1|]>>.
  Its remaining ticket factor is bounded by <math|exp
  <around|(|<around|\||c<rsub|<math-up|ticket>>|\|>*s<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>>, so
  this number can be taken as <math|M<rsub|0>>.

  Each condensation value has a finite base bound, and each local Haar-chart
  correction has a finite absolute bound. There are at most as many terms as
  fine bonds. The remaining normalization term is <math|-n<rsub|s>*log
  m<around|(|\<beta\>|)>>, again with <math|n<rsub|s>> no larger than that
  finite bond count. Their sum gives a finite <math|A<rsub|0>> bounding
  <math|v<rsub|\<beta\>>> from above. Only this last normalization term
  changes between the compared representations; hence
  <math|<around|\||v<rsub|\<beta\><rprime|'>>-v<rsub|\<beta\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>n<rsub|\<ast\>>*t>.
  These are exactly the hypotheses of the damping estimate already proved.
  Positive unbounded energies are controlled by
  <math|\<beta\><rprime|'>\<geqslant\>\<beta\>/2> and require no further
  cutoff.

  <subsubsection|Choosing the common variation scale and thresholds>

  We spell out the quantifier order needed for a running history. The common
  scale-factor floor is chosen from the elementary-activity and Gaussian
  local estimates before the derivative order, cutoff convention and positive
  width parameters are supplied. For a scale factor above that floor and
  fixed branch data, the compensation chart has a fixed positive radius. Its
  protected-field requirement is eventually satisfied because
  <math|\<tau\><around|(|x|)>\<to\>0>.

  When a protected comparison chart is obtained from the stationary
  construction, restrict it to the coarse horizon and retain the fine zone of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:uniform-stationary-chart>. If its stationary
  representative is a critical point of the same tested action in that zone,
  uniqueness there identifies it with the graph <math|\<rho\>> used in the
  stationary estimates, for every tag. This identification uses the same
  block, path and clipping data; shrinking a chart alone does not identify
  stationary points of different actions. The comparison may then use a still
  smaller common variation neighborhood inside the protected chart. The
  fixed-volume continuity constants may depend on that restriction.

  The elementary activity estimate is applied at the prescribed scale
  <math|v<around|(|x|)>> and the compensated-increment scale
  <math|s<around|(|x|)>>, both of upper exponent <math|-1/2>. The
  packet-action, Jacobian, density and quadratic estimates just established
  have eventual validity at those same scales. Take the intersection of their
  finitely many eventual conditions. This fixes one threshold in <math|x>
  before the finite tori and the incoming representation are supplied. The
  associated constants and summable prices are then instantiated at that
  actual representation. In particular, a later fixed-volume comparison
  neighborhood may depend on its total prices and covariance, as the
  continuity proof permits.

  To combine this with the common integrated derivative estimate, choose a
  fixed <math|Z\<gtr\>0> large enough for the normalized exponential coarse
  variations at derivative order <math|j>. Set

  <\equation*>
    t<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>=2*\<tau\><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>v<around|(|x|)>=<around|(|j+1|)>*Z*t<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  This function is eventually positive and has upper exponent <math|-1/2>.
  The elementary inequality <math|j*Z*t<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>v> pays the
  ordered variation directions. Apply both the local comparison construction
  and the integrated Gaussian estimate at this same <math|v>. The latter
  supplies the ordinary component price and derivative-scale data. The
  cluster budget construction, with the strictly positive target support
  reserve, then supplies the ordinary cluster criterion with its strict spare
  margin. All are evaluated at the same incoming data; no alternate
  representation is substituted between these steps.

  Finally, a schedule has only finitely many transitions at each fixed stage.
  Take the maximum of their scale-factor floors at that stage. After those
  factors are fixed, take a common upper threshold for the finitely many
  transition conditions, enlarge the stage entry coupling to that threshold,
  and preserve any earlier lower bound. Increasing entry couplings further
  preserves these conclusions. Consequently the comparison data and cluster
  margin are available uniformly for each already supplied transition
  satisfying the underlying sector, shell-access, protected-chart and
  incoming activity hypotheses. This selection does not construct those
  incoming data.

  For an application to a running curve one must also retain its renewed
  price bounds, the geometric bounds on the downward map, the admissible
  initial action, and the inequality <math|<math-up|preExponent>/B\<leqslant\><math-up|width>/2>.
  The curve has the same clipping and path data and the same regular and
  forced dictionaries. Its displayed output must equal the constructed
  nonlinear output under the chosen dictionary identifications. With these
  hypotheses, the fixed-volume theorem applies to that actual output, giving
  regular jet and forced value control and its subsequential summable
  majorants. The construction of an entire history satisfying all these
  hypotheses remains a separate step.

  <subsection|The remaining small-field budget
  inequalities><label|sec:small-field-budget-selection>

  The nonlinear step uses several estimates at different small powers of the
  coupling. We verify their numerical compatibility before choosing the stage
  thresholds. Throughout this section the block factor and stage data are
  fixed. Write <math|z=\<beta\><rsub|F><around|(|x|)>> for the shifted fine
  coupling, <math|B=B<around|(|x|)>> for the block radius, and
  <math|\<tau\>=\<tau\><around|(|x|)>> for the fluctuation threshold. Thus
  <math|z\<to\>\<infty\>>, <math|B> has upper exponent zero, and
  <math|\<tau\>> has upper exponent <math|-1/2>. We also use the stronger
  growth facts <math|B/log z\<to\>\<infty\>> and
  <math|P<rsub|F><around|(|x|)><rsup|2>/log z\<to\>\<infty\>> for the actual
  radius and fine monitor. They follow from their prescribed logarithmic
  growth laws; upper exponent zero alone would not suffice for the
  exponential estimates. All constants may depend on the fixed derivative
  order, spatial weights, path prices and positive support-width reserves.

  <subsubsection|The low-degree action and its support tail>

  The explicit low-degree correction has four contributions: the quadratic
  reset term, cubic and quartic bare-action terms, and a quadratic loop term.
  Their rooted jet prices, with the fixed support weights already included,
  have the form

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:small-field-four-costs>u\<leqslant\>C<rsub|2><around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>*\<tau\><rsup|2>+C<rsub|3><around|\||\<beta\>|\|>*\<tau\><rsup|3>+C<rsub|4><around|\||\<beta\>|\|>*\<tau\><rsup|4>+C<rsub|<math-up|loop>>*\<Lambda\>*\<tau\><rsup|2>.
  </equation>

  The constants include the finite coordinate and orientation costs, and the
  anchored support prices for the corresponding degrees. This is the direct
  sum of the quadratic and homogeneous action bounds: a degree-<math|r> term
  tested in directions of size <math|\<tau\>> contributes
  <math|\<tau\><rsup|r>>, while its coefficient contributes the displayed
  <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>>, <math|<around|\||\<beta\>|\|>>, or
  <math|\<Lambda\>>. The stage fixes the derivative order, spatial power and
  support exponent, so these constants are unchanged when its actual
  threshold varies.

  Assume <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>A>,
  <math|<around|\||\<beta\>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*z>, and
  <math|0\<leqslant\>\<Lambda\>\<leqslant\>\<Lambda\><rsub|0><around|(|x|)>>,
  where <math|A> is fixed and <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|0>> has upper exponent
  zero. The four terms in <eqref|eq:small-field-four-costs> have upper
  exponents respectively <math|-1,-1/2,-1,-1>. Hence one common nonnegative
  majorant for <math|u> has upper exponent <math|-1/2>. Since
  <math|-1/2\<less\>-7/16>, eventually

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:small-field-action-price>u\<leqslant\>z<rsup|-7/16>.
  </equation>

  If <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0> is the fixed support width, then
  <math|e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*B/4>> has every negative upper exponent.
  Multiplying the same majorant by this tail gives, after increasing the
  threshold,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:small-field-action-tail>e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*B/4>*u\<leqslant\>z<rsup|-23/20>.
  </equation>

  Also <math|<around|(|a+3|)>/B\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>/4> eventually for any
  fixed target support exponent <math|a>. The thresholds here are uniform
  over the stated ranges of <math|\<alpha\>,\<beta\>,\<Lambda\>> and over the
  actual finite torus.

  The reset bound itself follows from the existing coupling estimate. If the
  running reset satisfies <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>+c<rsub|G>*log
  L|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|0>>, then <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|0>+<around|\||c<rsub|G>*log
  L|\|>>. Enlarging the right side by one makes a fixed positive bound
  available for this chosen block factor. It does not require selecting a
  different reset at each application.

  <subsubsection|The strengthened cluster remainder>

  Let <math|D=z<rsup|3/8>> be the extra-degree weight, so
  <math|D\<geqslant\>1> eventually. Write
  <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|\<eta\>><around|(|x|)>=C<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>*e<rsup|\<eta\>>*\<eta\><rsup|-p*j>>
  for the derivative price at positive reserve <math|\<eta\>>, where
  <math|j,p\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> are the fixed derivative order and spatial power.
  The factor <math|C<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>> includes <math|<around|(|p*j|)>>!
  and the lattice scale factor; it is common to all reserves but may vary
  with the block radius. It has upper exponent zero. We suppress <math|x> in
  these prices below. Suppose the ordinary component/cluster budget at
  reserve <math|i\<gtr\>0> has already given

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-P\><rsub|i>*<space|0.17em>S*<around|(|1-1/2|)><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>z<rsup|q>,<space|2em>q\<leqslant\>-7/16.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|S=<around|(|1+2<rsup|j>*B<rsub|1>|)>*\<omega\>\<geqslant\>0> is
  the unstrengthened root price for the second expansion, in the notation of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:high-degree-cluster-prices>; it includes the
  component derivative factor. The reserve <math|i> lies below the fixed
  available reserve <math|y\<gtr\>0>, and let <math|a\<geqslant\>0> be the
  target support exponent. Retain the graded elementary bound and the two
  smallness conditions from Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:high-degree-cluster-prices>,
  namely

  <\equation*>
    <frac|4*<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*D*r<rsub|0>|\<delta\><rsub|0>>\<leqslant\><frac|1|2>,<space|2em><frac|4*<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*D*S|y>\<leqslant\><frac|1|2>,
  </equation*>

  with the available support exponent covering the reserve <math|a+2*y>. Here
  <math|r<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0> and <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> are the
  first expansion's root price and reserve. Also retain its seed-mark and
  footprint purchases, for <math|M\<geqslant\>0>, <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>
  and <math|T\<geqslant\>0>, <math|D<rsup|3>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|M>> and
  <math|D<rsup|3>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<epsilon\>*T>>, in the notation of that
  section. These conditions concern the same incoming representation and
  graded component prices. The extra-degree argument of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:high-degree-cluster-prices> gives a residual
  bound

  <\equation*>
    D<rsup|-3>*\<cal-P\><rsub|y>*<space|0.17em>D*S*<around|(|1-1/2|)><rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|i\<leqslant\>y>, the inverse powers satisfy
  <math|y<rsup|-p*j>\<leqslant\>i<rsup|-p*j>>, while
  <math|e<rsup|i>\<geqslant\>1>. Consequently
  <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|y>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|y>*\<cal-P\><rsub|i>>. The
  displayed remainder is at most

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|y>*D<rsup|-2>*z<rsup|q>=e<rsup|y>*z<rsup|q-3/4>.
  </equation*>

  Now <math|q-3/4\<leqslant\>-19/16\<less\>-23/20>. The strict gap absorbs
  the fixed factor <math|e<rsup|y>> and proves the common residual budget
  <math|z<rsup|-23/20>>. This calculation consumes the unstrengthened price
  <math|S> built from the original component price and its derivative factor;
  it does not assume an independent price for the strengthened expansion.

  <subsubsection|Polynomial localization costs and the incoming allowance>

  The auxiliary periods in the five-period construction have side lengths
  bounded by a fixed polynomial in <math|B>. Their volumes, local ball counts
  and ordered-derivative transport costs therefore all have upper exponent
  zero. Multiplication by any of them preserves the upper exponent of a
  price.

  In particular, localizing the ordinary error of size <math|z<rsup|-7/16>>
  produces a total auxiliary error of upper exponent <math|-7/16>. A further
  fixed support conversion, including the constant exponential of the
  support-cover bound, still has that exponent. Since
  <math|-7/16\<less\>-1/8>, its final price is eventually at most
  <math|z<rsup|-1/8>>. Similarly, localizing a price <math|z<rsup|-23/20>>,
  including the factor <math|j!3<rsup|j>> from
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:healthy-boundary-jets>, gives a price of
  upper exponent <math|-23/20>.

  Let <math|G<around|(|x|)>> be the sum of the latter localized price and the
  finitely many unlocalized <math|z<rsup|-23/20>> remainders, each with its
  fixed coefficient. Then <math|G> also has upper exponent <math|-23/20>. If
  <math|c\<gtr\>0> is the fixed regular allowance constant, the strict
  inequality <math|-23/20\<less\>-11/10> gives

  <\equation*>
    G<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><tfrac|c|2>z<rsup|-11/10>\<leqslant\><tfrac|c|2>x<rsup|-11/10>
  </equation*>

  eventually; the second inequality uses <math|z\<geqslant\>x\<gtr\>0>. Thus
  these remainders use at most half of the available regular allowance. All
  polynomial localization factors are paid before this final comparison.

  <subsubsection|Absorbing the forced correction at its prescribed price>

  A second exponential absorption must retain its prescribed positive strong
  forced price <math|v>. Let <math|a,b\<geqslant\>0> be its hard and soft
  input prices, with

  <\equation*>
    a\<leqslant\>v/8,<space|2em>b\<leqslant\>z<rsup|-1/8>.
  </equation*>

  Put <math|p=z<rsup|1/16>>. The constructed strong price has the form
  <math|v=C*exp <around|[|-r*<around|(|\<tau\>/g<around|(|\<beta\>|)>|)><rsup|2>|]>>
  with fixed <math|C,r\<gtr\>0>, where <math|g<around|(|\<beta\>|)>=\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>>.
  It therefore has the monitor estimate <math|v\<leqslant\>C*exp
  <around|(|-c*P<rsub|F><around|(|x|)><rsup|2>|)>> uniformly for the allowed
  coupling band, with fixed <math|C,c\<gtr\>0>. Thus <math|v*p\<leqslant\>1>
  eventually. Also, uniformly for <math|b> in the stated range,

  <\equation*>
    b\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>p*b\<leqslant\>1/8,<space|2em><around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*<around|{|<around|(|8*p|)><rsup|-1>+2*b|}>\<leqslant\>1
  </equation*>

  eventually: the two powers in the last braces are strictly negative and the
  prefactor has upper exponent zero.

  Choose

  <\equation*>
    \<kappa\>=-log <around|(|v*p|)>\<geqslant\>0,<space|2em>I=e<rsup|\<kappa\>>*a+2*b.
  </equation*>

  Then <math|I\<leqslant\><around|(|8*p|)><rsup|-1>+2*b>, so
  <math|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*I\<leqslant\>1> and in particular
  <math|0\<leqslant\>I\<leqslant\>1>. Using <math|e<rsup|I>-1\<leqslant\>2*I>
  gives

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|-\<kappa\>>*<around|(|e<rsup|I>-1|)>\<leqslant\>2*v*p*<around|{|<around|(|8*p|)><rsup|-1>+2*b|}>=v*<around|(|1/4+4*p*b|)>\<leqslant\>3*v/4\<leqslant\>v.
  </equation*>

  These are the exact smallness and output inequalities required by the
  forced-gas absorption, at the original allowance <math|v>. Its uniform
  monitor estimate follows from the coupling-band comparison for the gap.
  Indeed <math|\<tau\>=z<rsup|-1/2>*P<rsub|F><around|(|x|)>> and
  <math|\<beta\>\<geqslant\>z/4> give <math|\<beta\>*\<tau\><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>P<rsub|F><around|(|x|)><rsup|2>/4>.
  The fixed positive gap and normalization constants are absorbed into
  <math|c>. All constants and the threshold are chosen before the particular
  coupling in the band.

  Each assertion above is eventually valid for a fixed transition. Take the
  finite maximum of its required thresholds at each stage, then enlarge the
  entry coupling accordingly. Auxiliary periods are also chosen above their
  fixed geometric lower bounds, and the volume growth exponent is increased
  to at least one. These choices are preserved by any later increase of the
  delays. They supply the numerical budgets; the actual local action
  identities, stationary comparison and funded incoming representations are
  still required where each budget is used.

  <section|Stationary backgrounds and local convergence>

  <subsection|Local action prices for the stationary
  construction><label|sec:stationary-action-prices>

  We verify the localized derivative input of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:uniform-stationary-chart> for the actual
  unmasked block action. The action is a sum over sites. Its positive-order
  coordinate derivatives have uniform local bounds, although its scalar value
  need not have a volume-independent bound.

  Fix the block side <math|L>, the patch, the direct clip, and the kernel
  parameters, including its positive frame and predictor cutoffs and positive
  penalty coefficient. Fix also an order <math|n\<geqslant\>3> and an
  exponential path width <math|\<mu\>\<gtr\>0>. The incoming path data have
  row, cubic and quartic budgets at most <math|C<rsub|0>> at width
  <math|\<mu\>>; the loop budget is at most <math|\<Lambda\>>. These budgets
  are the sums of the absolute coefficients with their specified exponential
  length weights. All constants below may depend on this fixed data and on
  the group, but not on the auxiliary torus, its forest, or the tag choices.

  <subsubsection|From a finite read set to a localized derivative>

  A function based at a site <math|v> has read radius <math|R> if changing
  coordinates anchored farther than <math|R> from <math|v> leaves the
  function unchanged. A derivative containing any one such coordinate is
  zero: restrict to a line varying that coordinate, differentiate the
  constant function, and commute derivatives. If derivatives through order
  <math|n> have operator norm at most <math|A>, then, for
  <math|q\<leqslant\>n>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:stationary-read-price><around|\||\<partial\><rsub|i<rsub|1>>*\<cdots\>*\<partial\><rsub|i<rsub|q>>*u|\|>\<leqslant\>A*e<rsup|a*n*R>*exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|(|-a*<big|sum><rsub|h=1><rsup|q><math-up|dist><around|(|v,a<rsub|i<rsub|h>>|)>|)>.
  </equation>

  If every tested coordinate lies in the read set, the sum of their distances
  is at most <math|n*R>; otherwise the left side vanishes. This also holds
  for vector-valued expressions with norms in place of absolute values.

  For a path atom of total length <math|t>, the fine forest chart and a
  signed motion enlarge its read radius to at most <math|2*t+1+L>. The path
  itself travels at most <math|2*t> in fine units; reversing an oriented bond
  can move its initial point by one, and the coarse replica coordinate is
  anchored at most <math|L> from the fine bond. This estimate remains valid
  for winding paths: periodic distance is bounded by lifted path length.

  Choose

  <\equation*>
    a=<frac|\<mu\>|4*<around|(|n+1|)>*<around|(|1+L|)>>.
  </equation*>

  The elementary inequality

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:stationary-support-payment>e<rsup|\<mu\>*t/2>*e<rsup|a*n*<around|(|2*t+1+L|)>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<mu\>*t>*e<rsup|\<mu\>>
  </equation>

  follows by comparing exponents: the second exponent is at most
  <math|\<mu\>*t/2+\<mu\>>. The holomorphic path bounds at width
  <math|\<mu\>/2> give a derivative price <math|A<rsub|n>*e<rsup|\<mu\>*t/2>>
  for a slot, and <math|A<rsub|n,k><math-up|magnitude><around|(|q|)>*e<rsup|\<mu\>*t/2>>
  for a degree-<math|k> atom <math|q>. To recall why the constants are
  uniform, each matrix transport is a finite product; on the fixed complex
  neighborhood its norm is bounded exponentially in path length. The Cauchy
  estimate on a fixed smaller neighborhood bounds derivatives by that
  exponential times a constant depending only on <math|n> and the
  neighborhood. Composing with the fine exponential chart uses its uniform
  link derivatives and the finite partition formula. The fixed color
  projections and multilinear atom contractions contribute only the constants
  <math|A<rsub|n>,A<rsub|n,k>>. Combining this estimate with
  <eqref|eq:stationary-read-price> and <eqref|eq:stationary-support-payment>
  pays the localized derivative by the original width-<math|\<mu\>>
  coefficient budget.

  For an infinite row or atom family, the paid coefficient sequence is
  summable. It bounds the values and derivatives through order <math|n> on
  the common coordinate ball. Uniform convergence and the fundamental theorem
  of calculus along coordinate segments justify termwise differentiation,
  inductively in the derivative order. The underlying holomorphic series
  converges normally on a smaller complex neighborhood, so it is smooth to
  all orders there. Consequently a row based at <math|v> has localized
  derivative cost <math|C<rsub|0>*A<rsub|n>>, and a cubic or quartic family
  has cost <math|C<rsub|0>*A<rsub|n,3>> or <math|C<rsub|0>*A<rsub|n,4>>. A
  loop family has cost <math|\<Lambda\>*A<rsub|n,2>>. The latter dependence
  is linear in <math|\<Lambda\>>.

  <subsubsection|Summing sites without a volume factor>

  Suppose a site term <math|u<rsub|v>> has derivatives bounded by

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<partial\><rsub|i<rsub|1>>*\<cdots\>*\<partial\><rsub|i<rsub|q>>*u<rsub|v>|\|>\<leqslant\>B*exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|(|-a*<big|sum><rsub|h><math-up|dist><around|(|v,a<rsub|i<rsub|h>>|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Fix a tested coordinate <math|i<rsub|t>> and set
  <math|s=<big|sum><rsub|h><math-up|dist><around|(|v,a<rsub|i<rsub|h>>|)>>.
  Then <math|<math-up|dist><around|(|v,a<rsub|i<rsub|t>>|)>\<leqslant\>s> and

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|h><math-up|dist><around|(|a<rsub|i<rsub|t>>,a<rsub|i<rsub|h>>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|q+1|)>*s\<leqslant\><around|(|n+1|)>*s.
  </equation*>

  Splitting the exponent in two gives

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|-a*s>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-<around|(|a/2|)><math-up|dist><around|(|v,a<rsub|i<rsub|t>>|)>>*exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|(|-<frac|a|2*<around|(|n+1|)>>*<big|sum><rsub|h><math-up|dist><around|(|a<rsub|i<rsub|t>>,a<rsub|i<rsub|h>>|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Summing <math|v> costs only the distance-shell sum at width <math|a/2>. If
  at most <math|h> terms have the same base site, multiply this cost by
  <math|h>. We have obtained the bound <eqref|eq:stationary-scatter-input>,
  with width <math|a/<around|[|2*<around|(|n+1|)>|]>>, for every positive
  derivative of the site sum. The argument needs a tested coordinate as root,
  so it makes no assertion at order zero. Averaging over signed motions
  preserves the cost: each motion permutes sites and the averaging weights
  sum to one.

  The classical site density has three types of terms. The direct plaquette
  term has a fixed read radius and derivative bounds from its four unitary
  factors and the smooth group penalty. The squared-row term is
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<ell\><around|(|U|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>/2>. Its row
  value and derivatives are bounded by the row budget just proved; the finite
  product rule bounds its derivatives by a constant depending on
  <math|C<rsub|0>>. Its localized bounds are preserved by that product rule,
  since the exponential factors for the split derivative slots multiply to
  the factor for all slots. Finally the cubic and quartic terms have the atom
  bounds above. Adding these terms and summing sites proves the required
  localized derivative estimate for the fine classical action. The classical
  price depends on <math|C<rsub|0>> and the direct clip, and does not depend
  on the loop budget <math|\<Lambda\>>.

  <subsubsection|The prediction penalty on the same ball>

  The minimizing frame and predictor of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:predictor-branch>
  are smooth near the identity. The finite-read transplantation argument of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mean-action-jets> makes their fixed-order
  neighborhood and derivative bounds uniform over periods. We must also
  verify that all tags select that same branch there.

  On a coordinate ball of radius <math|r>, each fine bond chord is at most
  <math|C<rsub|1>*r>, by integrating its first derivative from the identity.
  Every path used by a frame is of bounded length for the fixed patch, so its
  transport chord is at most <math|C<rsub|2>*r>. Shrink <math|r> until the
  strict frame test holds. The uniqueness of the normalized minimizing frame
  identifies all frame tags there. Its coordinates and first derivatives are
  uniformly bounded on a smaller ball. A connector contains the frame at each
  of its two endpoints and a straight fine path. Its chord is therefore at
  most <math|C<rsub|3>*r>, with a common constant <math|C<rsub|3>\<gtr\>0>.

  Let <math|Y<rsub|i>> be these connectors and <math|w<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>
  their weights, <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>w<rsub|i>=1>. Comparing the
  prediction cost with the identity gives

  <\equation*>
    min<rsub|u> <big|sum><rsub|i>w<rsub|i><math-up|price><around|(|Y<rsub|i>*u<rsup|-1>|)>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2><around|(|C<rsub|3>*r|)><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  Choose <math|r\<less\><sqrt|\<zeta\>>/C<rsub|3>>, where
  <math|\<zeta\>\<gtr\>0> is the predictor cutoff. This inequality is then
  strictly below the cutoff. Also

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|i>w<rsub|i><math-up|mat><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|G>*C<rsub|3>*r,
  </equation*>

  where <math|c<rsub|G>> converts chord to matrix norm. Shrink <math|r>
  further so this lies in the fixed nearest-point tube. The selected
  predictor is the smooth nearest-point projection of this weighted matrix
  average. Hence every predictor tag agrees with this same smooth expression
  throughout the ball. Product and composition rules bound its derivatives
  uniformly, using the same actual endpoint frames in both occurrences.

  For each coarse bond, the smoothing penalty is the group price of the
  coarse field times the inverse predicted field. Its value and its
  derivatives through order <math|n> have a common bound on this ball. Its
  read set is the finite union of the patch paths at the two endpoints and
  the coarse bond coordinate. Apply <eqref|eq:stationary-read-price> with
  that fixed radius, then the site-summation argument. The embedded coarse
  sites are distinct; there are four coarse bond directions at each such
  site. Thus this sum has the same localized derivative bound, with
  multiplicity four, multiplied by the fixed kernel penalty coefficient.

  The full tested action is the sum of this smoothing penalty and the fine
  classical action. Taking the smaller common radius and adding their costs
  proves <eqref|eq:stationary-scatter-input> for the actual action, with all
  tags equal there. At the identity the curvature slots, plaquette cost and
  prediction discrepancy vanish; their first derivatives in the full action
  vanish as well, since the direct penalty is stationary and every other term
  has degree at least two in these vanishing slots. Thus
  <math|f<around|(|0|)>=0> and <math|D<rsub|y>*f<around|(|0|)>=0>. Together
  with the tree-gauge Hessian gap, this supplies all inputs to the uniform
  stationary construction. The argument fixes the block and path budgets
  first; it does not assert a new estimate for the evolution of those budgets
  along a history.

  <subsection|A uniform stationary chart and its auxiliary-volume
  derivatives><label|sec:uniform-stationary-chart>

  We construct the stationary branch on the real coordinate neighborhood used
  by the nonlinear step. The neighborhood must be uniform in the auxiliary
  torus; the subsequent derivative bounds may pay a fixed polynomial in its
  volume. These are different requirements.

  <subsubsection|Local derivative data and the Hessian gap>

  Let <math|I> and <math|J> be the coarse and free fine real-coordinate sets,
  and write <math|w=<around|(|x,y|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|I>\<times\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|J>>.
  Both coordinate spaces initially carry the supremum norm. Give every
  coordinate an anchor <math|a<rsub|i>> on the fine torus, with at most
  <math|K> coordinates at each site. Put

  <\equation*>
    S<rsub|a>=K*<big|sum><rsub|r\<geqslant\>0><around|(|2*r+1|)><rsup|4>*e<rsup|-a*r>*<space|2em><around|(|a\<gtr\>0|)>.
  </equation*>

  The distance-shell bound implies <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>e<rsup|-a<math-up|dist><around|(|v,a<rsub|i>|)>>\<leqslant\>S<rsub|a>>,
  uniformly in the torus and in <math|v>.

  Fix an order <math|N\<geqslant\>3>. The local action estimates provide a
  smooth real function <math|f*<around|(|x,y|)>> on
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\>r<rsub|0>> and
  constants <math|a,C,m,r<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> such that
  <math|f<around|(|0|)>=0>, <math|D<rsub|y>*f<around|(|0|)>=0>, and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:stationary-scatter-input><around|\||\<partial\><rsub|i<rsub|1>>*\<cdots\>*\<partial\><rsub|i<rsub|q>>*f<around|(|w|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|(|-a*<big|sum><rsub|h=1><rsup|q><math-up|dist><around|(|a<rsub|i<rsub|t>>,a<rsub|i<rsub|h>>|)>|)><space|1em><around|(|1\<leqslant\>q\<leqslant\>N,1\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>q|)>.
  </equation>

  The vertical Hessian <math|H<around|(|0|)>=D<rsub|y><rsup|2>*f<around|(|0|)>>
  satisfies <math|H<around|(|0|)>\<geqslant\>m*I> in the Euclidean
  quadratic-form sense. The constants and coordinate multiplicity are
  independent of the auxiliary torus after the block data have been fixed.
  The Hessian gap is the tree-gauge energy comparison in
  Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:path-stationary-chart>; the derivative
  estimate is the additional quantitative local-action input. Its application
  here uses the larger one-step data of that comparison: the coarse side
  exceeds one and the fine side is at least
  <math|100*R<rsub|<math-up|macro>>>, where
  <math|R<rsub|<math-up|macro>>=<around|(|j<rsub|<math-up|ph>>+7|)>*\<ell\>>,
  the blocking side <math|\<ell\>\<geqslant\>3> is odd, the phase
  <math|j<rsub|<math-up|ph>>\<geqslant\>4>, and the kernel's
  branch-compatibility hypothesis holds.

  For two points <math|v,w> in the original coordinate ball, differentiate a
  Hessian entry along their joining segment. Applying
  <eqref|eq:stationary-scatter-input> with the first Hessian coordinate as
  root, and summing the varied coordinate, gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||H<around|(|v|)><rsub|i*j>-H<around|(|w|)><rsub|i*j>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*S<rsub|a>*<around|\<\|\|\>|v-w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*e<rsup|-a<math-up|dist><around|(|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|j>|)>>.
  </equation*>

  Both row and column sums of the difference are therefore at most
  <math|C*S<rsub|a><rsup|2>*<around|\<\|\|\>|v-w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>.
  Its Euclidean operator norm and its supremum operator norm have this same
  bound. Choose

  <\equation*>
    r<rsub|H>=min <around*|{|r<rsub|0>/2,<frac|m|2*<around|(|1+C*S<rsub|a><rsup|2>|)>>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Then <math|H<around|(|w|)>\<geqslant\><around|(|m/2|)>*I> on
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|H>>.
  The second-derivative case of <eqref|eq:stationary-scatter-input> also
  gives uniformly bounded exponentially weighted row and column sums of
  <math|H<around|(|w|)>>, after reducing <math|a> to <math|a/2>. The inverse
  argument of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-covariance>, applied with
  lower bound <math|min <around|(|1,m/2|)>>, supplies an exponential bound on
  <math|H<around|(|w|)><rsup|-1>>. Summing that bound gives a fixed constant
  <math|J<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>1> with

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:stationary-uniform-inverse><around|\<\|\|\>|H<around|(|w|)><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>\<to\>\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|0>.
  </equation>

  The Euclidean bound <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|H<around|(|w|)><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2\<to\>2>\<leqslant\>2/m>
  follows directly from the positive gap. No conversion between the two
  vector norms is used to obtain either uniform bound.

  <subsubsection|Contraction on a common coordinate ball>

  Let <math|F*<around|(|x,y|)>=D<rsub|y>*f*<around|(|x,y|)>>, viewed as its
  array of real coordinates. Choose

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|r<rsub|V>>|<cell|=min
    <around|{|r<rsub|H>,<around|[|4*J<rsub|0>*<around|(|1+C*S<rsub|a><rsup|2>|)>|]><rsup|-1>|}>,>>|<row|<cell|r<rsub|X>>|<cell|=min
    <around|{|r<rsub|V>,r<rsub|V>/<around|[|4*J<rsub|0>*<around|(|1+C*S<rsub|a>|)>|]>|}>.>>>>
  </align*>

  For <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\>r<rsub|X>>,
  define on the closed ball <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|V>>

  <\equation*>
    T<rsub|x><around|(|y|)>=y-H<around|(|0|)><rsup|-1>*F*<around|(|x,y|)>.
  </equation*>

  The Hessian difference estimate gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsub|y>*T<rsub|x><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>\<to\>\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|0>*C*S<rsub|a><rsup|2>*r<rsub|V>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|4>.
  </equation*>

  The second-derivative bound, summed with its vertical coordinate as root,
  gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|F*<around|(|x,0|)>-F*<around|(|0,0|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C*S<rsub|a><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>.
  </equation*>

  Consequently <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsub|x><around|(|0|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|V>/4>
  and

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsub|x><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|4>\<\|\|\>y\<\|\|\><rsub|\<infty\>>+r<rsub|V>/4\<leqslant\>r<rsub|V>/2.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|T<rsub|x>> maps the complete closed ball into itself and
  contracts by <math|1/4>. Its iterates from zero form a Cauchy sequence:
  consecutive differences are bounded by a geometric sequence of ratio
  <math|1/4>. Their limit <math|\<rho\><around|(|x|)>> is a fixed point by
  continuity. If two fixed points existed, their distance would be at most
  one quarter of itself, so they would coincide. We have proved

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|V>/2,<space|2em>F*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>=0,<space|2em>\<rho\><around|(|0|)>=0,
  </equation*>

  with uniqueness among all stationary points in the closed
  <math|r<rsub|V>>-ball.

  At each <math|<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>> the vertical derivative
  of <math|F> is the invertible Hessian <math|H*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>>.
  The finite-dimensional smooth implicit-function theorem therefore supplies
  a local smooth stationary graph. It remains inside the open
  <math|r<rsub|V>>-ball after restricting its neighborhood, and uniqueness
  identifies it with the graph just constructed. These local identifications
  prove smoothness of <math|\<rho\>> throughout
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\>r<rsub|X>>. If
  several local tags give the same function on the original coordinate ball,
  the constructed graph is stationary for every tag and is the same graph for
  all of them. No extension outside the common ball is asserted.

  Differentiating <math|F*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>=0> once gives

  <\equation*>
    D*\<rho\><around|(|x|)>=-H*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|-1>*D<rsub|x>*F*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The rooted row sum bounds <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsub|x>*F|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>\<to\>\<infty\>>>
  by <math|C*S<rsub|a>>. Hence <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D*\<rho\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>\<to\>\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|0>*C*S<rsub|a>>.
  Integration along a coarse line segment proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:stationary-uniform-lipschitz><around|\<\|\|\>|\<rho\><around|(|x|)>-\<rho\><around|(|x<rprime|'>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|\<rho\>>*<around|\<\|\|\>|x-x<rprime|'>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>,<space|2em>C<rsub|\<rho\>>=1+J<rsub|0>*C*S<rsub|a>.
  </equation>

  In particular the constant used to spread fine-field slack is independent
  of the auxiliary volume.

  <subsubsection|Fixed-order derivatives with polynomial volume cost>

  Let <math|W\<geqslant\>1> bound <math|<around|\||I|\|>+<around|\||J|\|>>.
  We now allow polynomial costs in <math|W>. A scalar <math|q>-linear form
  with coordinate entries bounded by <math|C> has supremum operator norm at
  most <math|C*W<rsup|q>>: expand its arguments in coordinate basis vectors
  and bound the resulting at most <math|W<rsup|q>> terms. For a vector-valued
  form with supremum output norm the same bound holds coordinate by
  coordinate. Passing that output to its Euclidean norm costs at most
  <math|<sqrt|W>\<leqslant\>W>.

  These elementary conversions turn <eqref|eq:stationary-scatter-input> into
  polynomial bounds for the ordinary derivatives of <math|f> through order
  <math|N>. Its value has the same kind of bound on the fixed ball, because
  <math|f<around|(|0|)>=0> and the first derivative can be integrated
  radially. They also give polynomial bounds for every derivative of
  <math|\<rho\>> through order <math|N-1>. For completeness, differentiate
  <math|F\<circ\>\<Gamma\>=0>, where <math|\<Gamma\><around|(|x|)>=<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>>.
  At order <math|k\<geqslant\>2>, the partition formula reads

  <\equation*>
    0=H<around|(|\<Gamma\><around|(|x|)>|)>*D<rsup|k>*\<rho\><around|(|x|)>+<big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<pi\><text|
    a partition of ><around|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>\<geqslant\>2>>>>>>D<rsup|<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>>*F<around|(|\<Gamma\><around|(|x|)>|)><around|[|D<rsup|<around|\||B|\|>>*\<Gamma\><around|(|x|)>:B\<in\>\<pi\>|]>.
  </equation*>

  Every block in the sum has size less than <math|k>; the single-block term
  is exactly the isolated Hessian term, since the coarse component of
  <math|D<rsup|k>*\<Gamma\>> vanishes. The first derivative was already
  bounded in <eqref|eq:stationary-uniform-lipschitz>. Inductively, if the
  lower derivatives have polynomial bounds <math|P<rsub|h><around|(|W|)>>,
  the sum is bounded by

  <\equation*>
    C*<big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<pi\>\<vdash\><around|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>\<geqslant\>2>>>>>>W<rsup|<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>>*<big|prod><rsub|B\<in\>\<pi\>><around|(|1+P<rsub|<around|\||B|\|>><around|(|W|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Multiplying by <math|J<rsub|0>> bounds <math|D<rsup|k>*\<rho\>>. This is a
  fixed polynomial in <math|W> at every fixed <math|k>, and uses derivatives
  of <math|f> only through order <math|k+1>. The Euclidean output version
  follows by the preceding factor <math|W>. Thus the coordinate conversion
  does not affect the uniform radius or the uniform first-derivative bound.

  Put <math|M<around|(|x|)>=H<around|(|\<Gamma\><around|(|x|)>|)>>. The chain
  rule gives polynomial bounds for its entry derivatives through order
  <math|N-2>. A matrix with entries bounded by <math|B> has Euclidean
  operator norm at most <math|W*B>, so these are polynomial operator bounds
  as well. The inverse identity gives, for each nonempty set of derivative
  slots <math|A>,

  <\equation*>
    \<partial\><rsub|A>*M<rsup|-1>=-M<rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|\<varnothing\>\<ne\>B\<subseteq\>A><around|(|\<partial\><rsub|B>*M|)>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|A\<setminus\>B>*M<rsup|-1>|)>.
  </equation*>

  All inverse derivatives on the right have lower order. Together with
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|M<rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2\<to\>2>\<leqslant\>2/m>,
  this proves polynomial bounds by induction. Finally

  <\equation*>
    D*log det M<around|[|h|]>=<math-up|tr><around|(|M<rsup|-1>*D*M<around|[|h|]>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Repeated product differentiation and <math|<around|\||<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>A|\|>\<leqslant\>W<around|\<\|\|\>|A|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2\<to\>2>>
  give polynomial bounds for every positive derivative of <math|log det M>
  through order <math|N-2>. Its centered value is bounded by integrating the
  first derivative. Its uncentered value is bounded too: the eigenvalues lie
  between <math|m/2> and a fixed upper row-sum bound, so the sum of their
  logarithms has absolute value at most a fixed constant times <math|W>. In
  particular all determinants used here are positive.

  <subsubsection|The loop exponent and the Gaussian comparison data>

  The remaining exponent is

  <\equation*>
    \<psi\>*<around|(|x,y|)>=\<ell\><around|(|\<cal-D\><rsub|x><around|(|y|)>|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>J<rsub|<math-up|bond>>>log
    J<rsub|H><around|(|y<rsub|i>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|J<rsub|<math-up|bond>>> denotes the free bonds and
  <math|J<rsub|H>> the single-bond Haar Jacobian. The notation separates the
  bond set from the earlier real-coordinate set <math|J>. On the fixed
  combined coordinate unit ball, the actual loop coefficient budget bounds
  each coordinate derivative through the requested order by a fixed constant
  times <math|\<Lambda\>>, summed over at most <math|W> sites. Its
  exponentially summable coefficient series converges normally there, which
  justifies differentiation before summation. Conversion to operator
  derivatives costs at most a further power of <math|W>. On the half-radius
  Haar chart, Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:haar-chart> bounds the
  derivatives of <math|log J<rsub|H>>. Summing over at most <math|W> free
  bonds and composing with the fixed Lie-coordinate maps has polynomial cost
  in <math|W>. Thus the <math|\<psi\>> bounds have the form

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|0><around|(|W|)>+P<rsub|1><around|(|W|)>*\<Lambda\>
  </equation*>

  for fixed nonnegative polynomials <math|P<rsub|0>,P<rsub|1>>.

  Apply the preceding construction at order <math|N=n+3>. It supplies bounds
  through order <math|n+2> for <math|\<rho\>>, through order <math|n+3> for
  <math|f>, and through order <math|n+1> for the relative log determinant.
  The loop and Haar estimates are purchased through order <math|n+4>. Take
  coarse radius <math|u> and fluctuation parameter radius <math|v> with

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|1+C<rsub|\<rho\>>|)>*u+v*D\<less\>min
    <around|(|r<rsub|H>,1|)>,<space|2em>u\<leqslant\>r<rsub|X>,<space|1em>c<rsub|<math-up|Lie>>*<around|(|C<rsub|\<rho\>>*u+v*D|)>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|H><rsup|<math-up|Haar>>/2.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|c<rsub|<math-up|Lie>>> is the fixed norm of the Lie-coordinate
  map and <math|r<rsub|H><rsup|<math-up|Haar>>\<gtr\>0> is the Haar-chart
  radius. On every segment <math|<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>+t*b*z|)>>,
  with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\>u>,
  <math|<around|\||b|\|>\<less\>v>, <math|0\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>1> and
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>\<leqslant\>D>, these
  inequalities verify the needed action, coordinate and Haar domains. The
  stationary Hessian is positive with inverse covariance bound <math|2/m>,
  and stationarity removes the linear vertical Taylor term. These are exactly
  the data required in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:truncated-stationary-log>.
  For its final estimate through order <math|j> with the even second-order
  gain, choose <math|n\<geqslant\>j+2>; the two additional parameter
  derivatives must be included when fixing the stationary-chart order.

  For the selected auxiliary volumes, <math|W> is bounded by a polynomial in
  the auxiliary size parameter. All the prices obtained here are therefore
  polynomial in that parameter, except for the single linear loop factor.
  With <math|\<Lambda\>\<leqslant\>2*P<rsub|F>> their common bound can be
  written <math|p<rsub|0>+p<rsub|1>*P<rsub|F>>, with polynomially bounded
  <math|p<rsub|0>,p<rsub|1>>. This verifies the precise price dependence used
  in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:stationary-exterior-receipt>. The
  remaining quantitative inputs are the local-action estimate
  <eqref|eq:stationary-scatter-input>, the initial Hessian gap, and the loop
  coefficient and Haar-chart budgets. The construction never changes the
  selected finite-volume action or its tags.

  <subsection|The logarithm of a truncated stationary Gaussian
  integral><label|sec:truncated-stationary-log>

  The local small-field comparison uses a normalized integral near the actual
  stationary point. Its first correction has order <math|\<beta\><rsup|-1>>.
  Two details matter: the cutoff is retained when the coupling parameter
  vanishes, and the stationary Hessian varies with the coarse field. We prove
  the corresponding parameter estimate with both features.

  <subsubsection|The normalized integral>

  Let <math|E> be the finite-dimensional space of coarse logarithmic
  coordinates and let <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>> be the fine integration space,
  with its Euclidean norm. Let <math|\<varphi\>*<around|(|x,y|)>> be the real
  action, <math|\<psi\>*<around|(|x,y|)>> the additional real exponent, and
  <math|\<rho\><around|(|x|)>> the stationary point. Assume

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|y>*\<varphi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>=0,<space|2em>D<rsub|y><rsup|2>*\<varphi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>=M<around|(|x|)>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation*>

  On the parameter domains under consideration assume smoothness of
  <math|\<rho\>>, <math|\<varphi\>>, <math|\<psi\>> and <math|<tfrac|1|2>log
  det M>, with derivatives through the orders used below. More precisely, a
  derivative estimate through order <math|k> for the integral uses orders
  <math|k+2> for <math|\<varphi\>> and <math|\<rho\>>, and order <math|k> for
  <math|\<psi\>> and the relative log determinant, on every segment
  <math|<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>+t*b*z|)>> with
  <math|0\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>1> and <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>D>.
  Let their common bounds be <math|p>. These are bounds on the actual moving
  stationary branch and its Hessian, not on a separately chosen quadratic
  approximation.

  Define the smooth divided difference

  <\equation*>
    V*<around|(|b,x,z|)>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1><around|(|1-t|)>*D<rsub|y><rsup|2>*\<varphi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>+t*b*z|)>*<around|[|z,z|]>,d*t.
  </equation*>

  Taylor's formula and stationarity give

  <\equation*>
    V*<around|(|b,x,z|)>=<frac|\<varphi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>+b*z|)>-\<varphi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>|b<rsup|2>>*<space|1em><around|(|b\<ne\>0|)>,<space|2em>V*<around|(|0,x,z|)>=<tfrac|1|2>z<rsup|T>*M<around|(|x|)>*z.
  </equation*>

  The integral formula supplies its derivatives also at <math|b=0>. Put
  <math|s<around|(|x|)>=<tfrac|1|2>log det M<around|(|x|)>> and

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A*<around|(|b,x,z|)>=>|<cell|s<around|(|x|)>-s<around|(|0|)>+V*<around|(|0,0,z|)>-V*<around|(|b,x,z|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|+\<psi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>-\<psi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>+b*z|)>.>>>>
  </align*>

  With <math|\<gamma\><rsub|0>> the Gaussian of covariance
  <math|M<around|(|0|)><rsup|-1>>, set

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:stationary-normalized-integral>R*<around|(|b,x|)>=<big|int><rsub|<around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>D>e<rsup|A*<around|(|b,x,z|)>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|0><around|(|z|)>,<space|2em>L*<around|(|b,x|)>=log
    R*<around|(|b,x|)>.
  </equation>

  The normalization is fixed at the base point in the measure; the varying
  determinant is explicitly present in <math|A>.

  At <math|b=0> the density identity gives exactly

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:stationary-zero-mass>R*<around|(|0,x|)>=\<gamma\><rsub|x>*<around|{|<around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>D|}>,<space|2em>\<gamma\><rsub|x>=N<around|(|0,M<around|(|x|)><rsup|-1>|)>.
  </equation>

  Indeed the factor <math|e<rsup|s<around|(|x|)>-s<around|(|0|)>>> converts
  the determinant prefactor, and <math|V*<around|(|0,0,z|)>-V*<around|(|0,x,z|)>>
  converts the quadratic exponent. The two <math|\<psi\>> terms cancel. The
  result is the truncated Gaussian mass, which need not equal one.

  There is also an exact parity identity

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:stationary-even>R*<around|(|-b,x|)>=R*<around|(|b,x|)>.
  </equation>

  Changing <math|z> to <math|-z> preserves the ball and the centered
  Gaussian. The segment <math|\<rho\><around|(|x|)>+t*<around|(|-b|)>*<around|(|-z|)>>
  equals the original segment, and the two appearances of <math|z> in the
  quadratic evaluation cancel their signs. Thus
  <math|A*<around|(|-b,x,-z|)>=A*<around|(|b,x,z|)>>, proving the identity
  before taking any expansion.

  <subsubsection|Uniform derivatives on the truncated ball>

  The chain rule in the integral defining <math|V> gives a polynomial bound
  <math|P<rsub|k>*<around|(|p,D|)>> for all derivatives of <math|A> through
  order <math|k>, uniformly for <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>D>. To see
  the polynomial nature, the map <math|<around|(|b,x|)>\<mapsto\><around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>+t*b*z|)>>
  has derivatives bounded by fixed polynomials in <math|1+p+D>; each
  chain-rule term has at most <math|k> factors. Evaluating the two additional
  slots at <math|z> costs at most <math|D<rsup|2>>. Integration against
  <math|1-t> adds no large constant. The <math|\<psi\>> and log-determinant
  terms have the same type of bound.

  Purchase <math|k\<geqslant\>1> (replace a requested order zero by order one
  here). Suppose <math|<around|\||b|\|>\<less\>v\<leqslant\>1>,
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>u\<leqslant\>1>, and
  <math|P<rsub|k>*<around|(|p,D|)>*max <around|(|u,v|)>\<leqslant\>1>,
  enlarging <math|P<rsub|k>> to include its first order bound. Since
  <math|A*<around|(|0,0,z|)>=0>, integration along the parameter segment from
  zero gives <math|<around|\||A*<around|(|b,x,z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>1>.
  Derivatives of <math|e<rsup|A>> through order <math|k> are consequently
  bounded by another fixed polynomial in <math|P<rsub|k>>, times <math|e>.
  The cutoff ball is fixed in these parameters, and the reference Gaussian
  has mass one. This bound is integrable and uniform on the open parameter
  domain, so differentiation under the integral is justified at every order
  through <math|k>.

  If <math|\<gamma\><rsub|0>*<around|{|<around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>D|}>\<geqslant\>1/2>,
  then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:stationary-positive-range>e<rsup|-1>/2\<leqslant\>R*<around|(|b,x|)>\<leqslant\>e.
  </equation>

  It follows by integrating <math|e<rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|A>\<leqslant\>e>
  on the ball. Derivatives of <math|log> are bounded on this fixed positive
  interval, so the chain rule gives a common bound for all derivatives of
  <math|L> through order <math|k>. If <math|p,D> have upper exponent zero,
  this bound also has upper exponent zero. The smallness condition on
  <math|u,v> holds eventually whenever both have a fixed negative upper
  exponent.

  <subsubsection|The cutoff error when the dimension grows>

  The auxiliary tori have dimension at most <math|Q<around|(|x|)>>, where
  <math|Q> is bounded by a fixed polynomial in <math|1+<around|(|log
  \<beta\><rsub|F><around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|2>>. Suppose
  <math|M<around|(|y|)><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>c*I> uniformly on the
  coarse-field ball, with <math|c\<gtr\>0> fixed. Let
  <math|P<rsub|F><around|(|x|)>> be the fine monitor and take
  <math|D=d<rsub|0>*P<rsub|F><around|(|x|)>>, where <math|d<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0>
  is fixed. For the actual fine monitor, <math|P<rsub|F><around|(|x|)>/log
  z\<to\>\<infty\>>, and every fixed polynomial in <math|log z> is eventually
  bounded by <math|P<rsub|F><around|(|x|)><rsup|\<epsilon\>>> for every
  <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, where <math|z=\<beta\><rsub|F><around|(|x|)>>.
  These stronger growth properties are used in the following tail estimate.

  Here is a dimension-explicit tail estimate. If a centered Gaussian has
  covariance at most <math|c*I> in dimension at most <math|P\<geqslant\>1>,
  then for <math|r,s\<geqslant\>0>,

  <\equation*>
    \<bbb-P\>*<around|{|<around|\||Z|\|>\<gtr\>P*r|}>\<leqslant\>2*P*exp
    <around|(|-s*r+c*s<rsup|2>/2|)>.
  </equation*>

  If every coordinate has absolute value less than <math|r>, then
  <math|<around|\||Z|\|><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>P*r<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>P<rsup|2>*r<rsup|2>>.
  A union bound and the one-coordinate exponential-moment estimate therefore
  prove the assertion. For <math|P=1+Q<around|(|x|)>>, the monitor growth
  gives <math|P\<leqslant\><sqrt|P<rsub|F><around|(|x|)>>> eventually. Choose
  <math|r=d<rsub|0>*<sqrt|P<rsub|F><around|(|x|)>>> and <math|s=r/c>. Then
  <math|P*r\<leqslant\>D> and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:stationary-tail>1-\<gamma\><rsub|y>*<around|{|<around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>D|}>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1+Q<around|(|x|)>|)>*exp
    <around|{|-d<rsub|0><rsup|2>*P<rsub|F><around|(|x|)>/<around|(|2*c|)>|}>.
  </equation>

  The right side has every negative upper exponent. In particular it is at
  most <math|1/2> eventually, proving the mass condition above.

  If <math|0\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>\<leqslant\>1/2> and
  <math|1-\<epsilon\>\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>1>, integration of <math|1/t>
  on <math|<around|[|r,1|]>> gives <math|<around|\||log
  r|\|>\<leqslant\>2*\<epsilon\>>. Applying this to
  <eqref|eq:stationary-zero-mass> shows that <math|L*<around|(|0,y|)>> has a
  value smaller than every fixed negative power of <math|\<beta\><rsub|F>>,
  uniformly on the allowed coarse ball. Derivatives require an additional
  argument; a small value by itself is insufficient.

  <subsubsection|Turning a small value into small jets>

  We give the finite-order interpolation used for that purpose. Fix
  <math|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, <math|a\<gtr\>0>, and real <math|d,m>. There is an
  exponent <math|B> such that, for all sufficiently large <math|z>, the
  following implication holds in any finite-dimensional normed spaces. If on
  <math|<wide|B|\<bar\>>*<around|(|y,z<rsup|-a>|)>> a function <math|F> has
  derivatives through order <math|j+1> bounded by <math|z<rsup|d>>, and its
  value is bounded by <math|z<rsup|B>>, then its jets through order <math|j>
  at <math|y> are bounded by <math|z<rsup|m>>.

  The elementary one-step estimate is

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r+1>*F<around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*v/s+C*s
  </equation*>

  whenever <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r>*F|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>v> and
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r+2>*F|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C> on the
  radius-<math|s> ball. Subtract the tangent map of <math|D<rsup|r>*F> at
  <math|y>, integrate its derivative along a segment of length <math|s>, and
  use the second-derivative bound to control the tangent-map error. This
  gives the estimate in every unit direction, hence in operator norm, without
  a dimension factor.

  Induct on <math|j>. The case <math|j=0> takes <math|B=m>. In the induction
  step choose

  <\equation*>
    c=a+max <around|(|0,d-m|)>+1,<space|2em>b=m-1-c,<space|2em>s=z<rsup|-c>.
  </equation*>

  Eventually <math|2*s\<leqslant\>z<rsup|-a>>. Apply the induction hypothesis
  with target exponent <math|b> on every radius-<math|s> ball whose center
  lies within <math|s> of <math|y>; those balls lie in the original domain. A
  sufficiently negative common value exponent <math|B> then bounds jets
  through order <math|j-1> by <math|z<rsup|b>> on that inner ball. The
  one-step estimate bounds the order-<math|j> derivative at <math|y> by

  <\equation*>
    2*z<rsup|b+c>+z<rsup|d-c>.
  </equation*>

  Both exponents are strictly less than <math|m>, and <math|b\<less\>m> also
  bounds all lower derivatives. Increasing the lower threshold on <math|z>
  proves the assertion. This is a finite induction, with all exponents chosen
  before the function or dimension is supplied.

  Apply it to <math|F<around|(|y|)>=L*<around|(|0,y|)>> on a microscopic ball
  of radius <math|z<rsup|-1>> inside the coarse-field domain. The derivative
  bound of upper exponent zero is at most <math|z> eventually. The tail
  estimate provides any value exponent <math|B> the induction requests.
  Hence, for every fixed real <math|e>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:stationary-zero-jets>\<\|\|\>L(0,\<cdot\><around|)||\<\|\|\>><rsub|J<rsup|j>,y>\<leqslant\>z<rsup|e>
  </equation>

  eventually, with the stated microscopic margin.

  <subsubsection|The even second-order gain>

  Purchase two additional parameter derivatives in the earlier bounds. For
  every <math|r\<leqslant\>j>, the function
  <math|b\<mapsto\>D<rsub|y><rsup|r>*L*<around|(|b,y|)>> is even by
  <eqref|eq:stationary-even>. Its first derivative at zero vanishes. If its
  second derivative is bounded by <math|C<around|(|x|)>> for
  <math|<around|\||b|\|>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>>, Taylor's formula gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsub|y><rsup|r>*L*<around|(|\<epsilon\>,y|)>-D<rsub|y><rsup|r>*L*<around|(|0,y|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<around|(|x|)>*\<epsilon\><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  The derivative constant <math|C> has upper exponent zero. Combining with
  <eqref|eq:stationary-zero-jets>, assuming <math|\<beta\>\<gtr\>0> and
  <math|\<epsilon\>=\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>\<less\>v>, we obtain

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:stationary-log-price>\<\|\|\>L(\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>,\<cdot\><around|)||\<\|\|\>><rsub|J<rsup|j>,y>\<leqslant\>C<around|(|x|)>*\<beta\><rsup|-1>+z<rsup|e>.
  </equation>

  This is the required first-order inverse-coupling price, uniform for the
  prescribed auxiliary volumes and coupling band, with the closed microscopic
  ball <math|<wide|B|\<bar\>>*<around|(|y,z<rsup|-1>|)>> contained in the
  coarse-field parameter domain, provided the actual stationary branch,
  segment derivative and Hessian bounds stated above hold. Identifying this
  truncated integral with the full nonlinear push additionally requires its
  separate exterior estimate; that estimate is not part of the parity
  argument.

  <subsection|The retained stationary region and its
  exterior><label|sec:stationary-exterior-receipt>

  The Gaussian comparison must describe a region of the actual fine
  configuration space. In particular, the fine-field cutoff must be one
  throughout that region. We establish this using the funded expansion to
  find a configuration with slack, then propagating the slack by a uniform
  Lipschitz estimate.

  <subsubsection|Coordinates and the normalized integral>

  Fix an auxiliary finite torus, a tree gauge, and the actual coarse field
  <math|V<around|(|x|)>> in logarithmic coordinates <math|x\<in\>X>. Let
  <math|Y> be the Euclidean space of free fine logarithmic coordinates,
  <math|d=dim Y>, and <math|N> the number of free fine bonds. Its coordinate
  map is

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-D\><rsub|x><around|(|y|)><rsub|i>=exp
    <around|(|y<rsub|i>|)>*<space|0.17em>V<around|(|x|)><rsup|<math-up|rep>><rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|y<rsub|i>> includes the fixed orthonormal identification with
  the Lie algebra, and tree bonds are restored by the gauge fixing. The space
  <math|X> uses the supremum norm on real color coordinates. The space
  <math|Y> uses its Euclidean norm for Gaussian integration; write
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>> for the supremum norm
  after forgetting that Euclidean norm. Thus
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>>.
  The combined chart norm is <math|max <around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>,<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>>.
  Write <math|\<rho\><around|(|x|)>> for the actual stationary branch,
  <math|b=\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>>, and

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-S\><rsub|x>=\<cal-D\><rsub|x>*<around*|(|\<rho\><around|(|x|)>+b*<wide|B|\<bar\>><rsub|Y>*<around|(|0,D|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  This is a compact measurable subset of the free configuration space. All
  Haar measures in this section are probability measures.

  The local Haar density of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:haar-chart> is
  <math|c<rsub|H>*J<around|(|y<rsub|i>|)>> on each free bond. Set

  <\equation*>
    \<psi\>*<around|(|x,y|)>=\<ell\><around|(|\<cal-D\><rsub|x><around|(|y|)>|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|N>log
    J<around|(|y<rsub|i>|)>,<space|2em>M<around|(|x|)>=D<rsub|y><rsup|2>*\<phi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<phi\>> is the fine classical action plus the smoothing
  penalty, and <math|\<ell\>> is the fine loop correction. Assume that the
  stationary chart estimates hold on the region in question, with
  <math|M<around|(|x|)>\<gtr\>0>. Introduce

  <\equation*>
    \<Phi\><around|(|x|)>=\<beta\>*\<phi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>+\<psi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>log
    det M<around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>\<Omega\>=\<pi\><rsub|\<beta\>>*<around|(|<sqrt|2*\<pi\>>*<space|0.17em>b|)><rsup|d>*c<rsub|H><rsup|N>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<pi\><rsub|\<beta\>>\<gtr\>0> is the smoothing-kernel
  prefactor. The tagged integrand of the pure push is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:exterior-tag-integrand>U<rsub|x><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|k>\<pi\><rsub|\<beta\>>*\<lambda\><rsub|k><around|(|U|)>*e<rsup|-\<beta\>*<around|[|a<around|(|U|)>+n<rsub|k>*<around|(|U,V<around|(|x|)>|)>|]>-\<ell\><around|(|U|)>>*<with|font-series|bold|1><rsub|<around|{|<text|fine
    field healthy>|}>>.
  </equation>

  The nonnegative tag weights satisfy <math|<big|sum><rsub|k>\<lambda\><rsub|k><around|(|U|)>=1>;
  a dead tag has zero weight. Tree-gauge integration identifies the pure push
  with <math|Z<around|(|x|)>=<big|int>U<rsub|x>*<space|0.17em>d*U>. The
  incoming density and kernel have integrable envelopes, so all restricted
  integrals below exist.

  Suppose every point of <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|x>> is fine healthy, the whole
  logarithmic ball is inside the Haar chart, and all live tag actions there
  equal <math|\<phi\>>. Then the product chart formula and the affine change
  <math|y=\<rho\><around|(|x|)>+b*z> give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:exterior-exact-interior><big|int><rsub|\<cal-S\><rsub|x>>U<rsub|x>*<space|0.17em>d*U=\<Omega\>*e<rsup|-\<Phi\><around|(|x|)>>*R*<around|(|b,x|)>,
  </equation>

  with exactly the truncated integral <math|R> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:truncated-stationary-log>. Indeed the
  Jacobian is <math|b<rsup|d>*c<rsub|H><rsup|N>*<big|prod><rsub|i>J<around|(|y<rsub|i>|)>>,
  the tag sum is one, and <math|\<beta\>*b<rsup|2>=1>. The quadratic Gaussian
  factor contributes <math|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|d/2><around|(|det
  M<around|(|0|)>|)><rsup|-1/2>>; its determinant and the relative
  determinant in the exponent defining <math|R> combine to
  <math|<around|(|det M<around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|-1/2>>. The remaining exponent
  is precisely <math|-\<Phi\><around|(|x|)>>. No tag-count factor occurs.
  Injectivity of the Haar chart ensures that the inverse image of
  <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|x>> within that chart is the chosen closed ball, so
  this change of variables also handles its boundary.

  <subsubsection|The energy cost outside the retained region>

  Let <math|\<gamma\>\<gtr\>0> be the stationary chart gap and put
  <math|\<Delta\>=\<gamma\>*D<rsup|2>/4>. On the common safe chart assume the
  fine Hessian satisfies <math|D<rsub|y><rsup|2>*\<phi\>\<geqslant\><around|(|\<gamma\>/2|)>*I>.
  Stationarity and Taylor's integral formula then imply

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:exterior-convex-gap>\<phi\>*<around|(|x,y|)>-\<phi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>\<geqslant\><frac|\<gamma\>|4>*<around|\<\|\|\>|y-\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>.
  </equation>

  Here the safe chart is the inverse image of the closed combined coordinate
  supremum ball, while the displayed quadratic lower bound uses the Euclidean
  norm on <math|Y>. Both endpoints lie in that chart, and its convexity
  contains their entire joining segment. Consequently any point of that chart
  outside <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|x>> pays at least <math|\<Delta\>> after
  multiplication by <math|\<beta\>>.

  We also need to recognize chart points without presupposing that the fine
  field is in the chart. The tree-gauge geometric estimate supplies a
  constant <math|C<rsub|0>> such that an alive tag, fine plaquette distances
  at most <math|\<delta\>>, and prediction-wire distances at most
  <math|\<delta\>> imply

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|dist><around|(|U<rsub|i>,V<around|(|x|)><rsup|<math-up|rep>><rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|C<rsub|0>*s+1|)>*\<delta\>
  </equation*>

  for every free bond. Here <math|s> bounds the number of plaquettes in the
  fixed local comparison box. The estimate follows by comparing that bond
  with its tree-gauge replica: the local filling contributes at most
  <math|s*\<delta\>>, its geometric comparison costs <math|C<rsub|0>>, and
  the prediction wire contributes the final <math|\<delta\>>. Choose
  logarithm-chart constants <math|r<rsub|0>,C<rsub|log>\<gtr\>0>, so that
  distances at most <math|r<rsub|0>> have logarithms bounded by
  <math|C<rsub|log>> times the distance. For a prescribed safe coordinate
  radius <math|r<rsub|s>\<gtr\>0>, choose

  <\equation*>
    \<delta\>\<leqslant\>min <around*|{|<frac|r<rsub|0>|C<rsub|0>*s+1>,<frac|r<rsub|s>|C<rsub|log>*<around|(|C<rsub|0>*s+1|)>>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Taking the logarithm of each relative free bond now yields
  <math|U=\<cal-D\><rsub|x><around|(|y|)>> with
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|s>>.
  The coarse coordinates are already inside the safe radius. This argument
  uses the geometric comparison for alive tags only.

  Write <math|\<tau\>> for the fine health threshold, and suppose
  <math|\<tau\>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>>. Let <math|W\<geqslant\>1> bound all
  relevant site and coordinate counts. The path budgets on a healthy field
  give

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<ell\><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>W*\<Lambda\>*\<tau\><rsup|2>,<space|2em>a<around|(|U|)>\<geqslant\>-W*C*<around|(|\<tau\><rsup|3>+\<tau\><rsup|4>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The second bound uses the nonnegative quadratic part of the action and the
  absolute cubic and quartic budgets. Define

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|<math-up|loop>>=\<beta\>*C*<around|(|\<tau\><rsup|3>+\<tau\><rsup|4>|)>+\<Lambda\>*\<tau\><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|m<rsub|W>> bound <math|<around|\||\<phi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>|\|>/<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>>>
  on the coarse chart, and let <math|p+t<rsub|W>> bound
  <math|<around|\||\<Phi\><around|(|x|)>-\<beta\>*\<phi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>|\|>>.
  The latter consists of the loop/Haar bound <math|p> and a log-determinant
  bound <math|t<rsub|W>>. If a prediction wire exceeds <math|\<delta\>>, its
  nonnegative smoothing penalty is at least
  <math|\<kappa\>*\<delta\><rsup|2>/2>, where <math|\<kappa\>\<gtr\>0> is the
  kernel penalty constant. Assume

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:exterior-wire-reserve>\<beta\>*m<rsub|W>\<\|\|\>x\<\|\|\>+\<Delta\>\<leqslant\>\<beta\>*\<kappa\>*\<delta\><rsup|2>/2.
  </equation>

  For a healthy point outside <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|x>>, there are now two
  cases. If all prediction wires are small, the preceding chart-recognition
  argument and <eqref|eq:exterior-convex-gap> apply. Otherwise the large-wire
  penalty and <eqref|eq:exterior-wire-reserve> apply. In both cases the
  exponent in each live tag satisfies

  <\equation*>
    -\<beta\>*<around|[|a<around|(|U|)>+n<rsub|k>*<around|(|U,V<around|(|x|)>|)>|]>-\<ell\><around|(|U|)>\<leqslant\>-\<Phi\><around|(|x|)>-\<Delta\>+W*B<rsub|<math-up|loop>>+p+t<rsub|W>.
  </equation*>

  Unhealthy fields and dead tags contribute zero. Summing the tag weights
  therefore proves a pointwise exterior bound.

  To express it in stationary units, put <math|m<rsub|H>=c<rsub|H>*<around|(|<sqrt|2*\<pi\>>*<space|0.17em>b|)><rsup|dim
  \<frak-g\>>>, so <math|\<Omega\>=\<pi\><rsub|\<beta\>>*m<rsub|H><rsup|N>>,
  and define

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:exterior-exponent>Y<rsub|\<ast\>>=-N*log
    m<rsub|H>-\<Delta\>+W*B<rsub|<math-up|loop>>+p+t<rsub|W>.
  </equation>

  Then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:exterior-pointwise>U<rsub|x><around|(|U|)>\<leqslant\>\<Omega\>*e<rsup|-\<Phi\><around|(|x|)>>*e<rsup|Y<rsub|\<ast\>>>*<space|1em><around|(|U\<nin\>\<cal-S\><rsub|x>|)>.
  </equation>

  Since the ambient Haar measure has mass one, the same bound holds for the
  exterior integral.

  <subsubsection|Finding one configuration with slack>

  Let <math|Z<rsub|<math-up|sl>><around|(|x|)>> denote the portion of the
  pure push allocated to the empty coarse and fine defect sector. The sector
  allocation has values between zero and one. Its nonzero support requires
  all fine plaquette distances to be at most <math|\<tau\>/2>. The exact
  funded expansion of this sector is

  <\equation*>
    Z<rsub|<math-up|sl>><around|(|x|)>=\<cal-O\><around|(|x|)>*<big|sum><rsub|I>e<rsub|I><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>e<rsub|\<varnothing\>><around|(|x|)>=1,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<cal-O\><around|(|x|)>\<gtr\>0> is the same sector overhead as
  in the block integral, and the expansion gives
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|I><around|\||e<rsub|I><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<varepsilon\>>>.
  These are the actual funded sector identities, with its fixed tree and
  allocation. They imply

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:exterior-slack-lower>Z<rsub|<math-up|sl>><around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>\<cal-O\><around|(|x|)>*<around|(|2-e<rsup|\<varepsilon\>>|)>.
  </equation>

  To see the factor two, separate the empty term: the absolute sum of the
  remaining terms is at most <math|e<rsup|\<varepsilon\>>-1>, so their sum is
  at least <math|1-e<rsup|\<varepsilon\>>>. The expansion's absolute
  convergence justifies this separation. On the auxiliary volume, its rooted
  budget gives <math|\<varepsilon\>=<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|c>|\|>*<space|0.17em>o<around|(|x|)>>,
  with a negative upper exponent for <math|o>. Since
  <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|c>|\|>> has upper exponent zero,
  <math|e<rsup|\<varepsilon\>>\<leqslant\>3/2> eventually.

  We compare <math|\<cal-O\>> to the stationary normalization before using
  <eqref|eq:exterior-slack-lower>. On a healthy coarse field its exact
  formula is

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-O\>=\<Omega\>*exp <around|{|-\<beta\>*a<rsub|<math-up|coarse>,2><around|(|x|)>-<tfrac|1|2>log
    det A<rsub|0>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|a<rsub|<math-up|coarse>,2>> is the coarse quadratic action and
  <math|A<rsub|0>> is the empty-sector covariance precision matrix. The
  funded matrix bound gives <math|log det
  A<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>W*a<rsub|\<ast\>>>. Classical matching through degree
  four, with fifth-derivative cost <math|K<rsub|5>>, and the coarse cubic and
  quartic budgets give

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<phi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>-a<rsub|<math-up|coarse>><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>W*K<rsub|5><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsup|5>,<space|1em><around|\||a<rsub|<math-up|coarse>><around|(|x|)>-a<rsub|<math-up|coarse>,2><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>W*C<rprime|'>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|c><rsup|3>+\<tau\><rsub|c><rsup|4>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Combining these inequalities with the bound <math|p+t<rsub|W>> yields

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:exterior-overhead-compare>\<cal-O\>\<geqslant\>\<Omega\>*e<rsup|-\<Phi\>-e<rsub|\<ast\>>>,<space|2em>e<rsub|\<ast\>>=\<beta\>*W*<around|[|K<rsub|5>\<\|\|\>x\<\|\|\><rsup|5>+C<rprime|'>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|c><rsup|3>+\<tau\><rsub|c><rsup|4>|)>|]>+p+t<rsub|W>+W*a<rsub|\<ast\>>/2.
  </equation>

  If

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:exterior-two-reserves>Y<rsub|\<ast\>>+e<rsub|\<ast\>>+2\<leqslant\>-\<Delta\>/2,<space|2em>Y<rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>-\<Delta\>/2,
  </equation>

  then the exterior upper bound is strictly less than
  <math|<tfrac|1|2>\<cal-O\>\<leqslant\>Z<rsub|<math-up|sl>>>, since
  <math|e<rsup|-2>\<less\>1/2>. If <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|x>> contained no
  point with all fine plaquettes at most <math|\<tau\>/2>, the allocated
  integrand would vanish throughout <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|x>>. Outside it,
  that integrand is bounded by <math|U<rsub|x>>. Its integral would
  consequently be bounded by the exterior upper bound, a contradiction. Thus
  there exists

  <\equation*>
    U<rsub|0>=\<cal-D\><rsub|x>*<around|(|\<rho\><around|(|x|)>+b*z<rsub|0>|)>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|z<rsub|0>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>D,<space|2em><math-up|toll><rsub|i><around|(|U<rsub|0>|)>\<leqslant\>\<tau\>/2<space|1em><text|for
    all >i.
  </equation*>

  This existence argument does not differentiate the fine cutoff.

  <subsubsection|Spreading the slack and choosing the parameters>

  The fine plaquette tolls are uniformly Lipschitz, with a constant
  <math|L<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> independent of the auxiliary volume, on the
  combined coordinate supremum unit ball, with Lipschitz distance measured in
  that same norm. Indeed each plaquette contains four links; the uniform
  first-derivative estimate for a link, the product rule, and unitarity bound
  the derivative of its matrix holonomy. The reverse triangle inequality for
  the matrix norm then bounds the difference of tolls. Integrating that
  derivative along the joining segment proves the asserted Lipschitz
  estimate.

  Let <math|C<rsub|\<rho\>>> be the supremum-coordinate Lipschitz bound for
  the stationary branch, with <math|\<rho\><around|(|0|)>=0>. For
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|y-x|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>r> and
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>D>, the supremum distance
  between the combined coordinates of <math|\<cal-D\><rsub|y>*<around|(|\<rho\><around|(|y|)>+b*z|)>>
  and <math|U<rsub|0>> is at most

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|1+C<rsub|\<rho\>>|)>*r+2*b*D.
  </equation*>

  We used <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|z-z<rsub|0>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|z-z<rsub|0>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>\<leqslant\>2*D>.
  Thus every such field is fine healthy provided

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:exterior-slack-propagation>L<rsub|0>*<around|[|<around|(|1+C<rsub|\<rho\>>|)>*r+2*b*D|]>\<leqslant\>\<tau\>/2.
  </equation>

  The two configurations lie in the coordinate unit ball under the radius
  conditions below. The same conditions place the entire region inside the
  Haar chart and inside the region where all tag actions coincide.

  Here are the simultaneous purchases used in the construction. The action
  constants <math|C,C<rprime|'>> are fixed and nonnegative, while
  <math|\<kappa\>,\<gamma\>> are fixed and positive. The constant
  <math|C<rsub|\<rho\>>> is fixed at this stage independently of the
  auxiliary volume. The loop budget obeys
  <math|0\<leqslant\>\<Lambda\>\<leqslant\>2*P<rsub|F>>. Write
  <math|z=\<beta\><rsub|F>>, <math|P<rsub|F>> for the fine monitor,
  <math|u=z<rsup|-7/16>> and <math|r=z<rsup|-1>>, and take

  <\equation*>
    \<beta\>\<in\><around|[|z/2,2*z|]>,<space|2em>\<tau\>=z<rsup|-1/2>*P<rsub|F>,<space|2em>D=d<rsub|0>*P<rsub|F>,<space|2em>d<rsub|0>=<around|(|16*L<rsub|0>|)><rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  Every fixed polynomial in the auxiliary size parameter is
  <math|o<around|(|P<rsub|F><rsup|\<epsilon\>>|)>> for every
  <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>; <math|P<rsub|F>> itself has upper exponent
  zero. These are the monitor's two distinct growth properties. Since
  <math|b\<leqslant\>2*z<rsup|-1/2>>, <math|2*L<rsub|0>*b*D\<leqslant\>\<tau\>/4>,
  and <math|L<rsub|0>*<around|(|1+C<rsub|\<rho\>>|)>*r\<leqslant\>\<tau\>/4>
  eventually. This proves <eqref|eq:exterior-slack-propagation>. Moreover
  <math|<around|(|1+C<rsub|\<rho\>>|)>*u+u*D> tends to zero. It is eventually
  smaller than the fixed safe radii, the action-coincidence radius and one,
  while its fine-coordinate component times the Lie-coordinate norm cost is
  at most half the Haar-chart radius. Also <math|b\<less\>u> and
  <math|\<tau\>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>> eventually.

  The coefficients <math|m<rsub|W>,t<rsub|W>,W,a<rsub|\<ast\>>,K<rsub|5>> are
  bounded by fixed polynomials in the auxiliary size parameter. The
  stationary jet price has the more precise form

  <\equation*>
    p=p<rsub|0>+p<rsub|1>*P<rsub|F>,
  </equation*>

  with both <math|p<rsub|0>,p<rsub|1>> bounded by such polynomials: only the
  loop price contributes the linear monitor factor. Also <math|-N*log
  m<rsub|H>> is bounded above by a polynomial in that parameter, including
  its logarithmic coupling cost. The two action remainders
  <math|W*B<rsub|<math-up|loop>>> and <math|\<beta\>*W*<around|[|K<rsub|5>*u<rsup|5>+C<rprime|'>*\<tau\><rsub|c><rsup|3>+C<rprime|'>*\<tau\><rsub|c><rsup|4>|]>>
  tend to zero, for <math|\<tau\><rsub|c>\<leqslant\>2*\<tau\>>. It follows
  that every positive term in <math|Y<rsub|\<ast\>>+\<Delta\>+e<rsub|\<ast\>>+2>
  is <math|o<around|(|P<rsub|F><rsup|2>|)>>. Since
  <math|\<Delta\>=\<gamma\>*d<rsub|0><rsup|2>*P<rsub|F><rsup|2>/4>, both
  inequalities in <eqref|eq:exterior-two-reserves> hold eventually. Finally
  <math|m<rsub|W>*u\<to\>0> and <math|\<Delta\>/\<beta\>\<to\>0>, proving
  <eqref|eq:exterior-wire-reserve> uniformly for
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>u>. All these thresholds can be
  enlarged together at a fixed stage.

  We can now use <eqref|eq:exterior-exact-interior>: slack propagation has
  verified its cutoff condition on the entire retained region. Split the
  positive full integral into that region and its complement, and use
  <eqref|eq:exterior-pointwise> and <eqref|eq:exterior-two-reserves>. The
  result is

  <\equation*>
    0\<leqslant\>Z<around|(|y|)>-\<Omega\>*e<rsup|-\<Phi\><around|(|y|)>>*R*<around|(|b,y|)>\<leqslant\>\<Omega\>*e<rsup|-\<Phi\><around|(|y|)>>*exp
    <around|(|-\<gamma\>*D<rsup|2>/8|)>
  </equation*>

  for every <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>u> with
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|y-x|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>r>, where the center
  <math|x> is coarse healthy. This is the exterior estimate required in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:pure-action-small-field>, on its full
  microscopic comparison ball. Its hypotheses retain the actual funded sector
  expansion, the stationary chart and Hessian estimates, and the classical
  matching for the same coefficient data.

  <subsection|The small-field remainder of the pure nonlinear
  push><label|sec:pure-action-small-field>

  We combine the truncated stationary integral with the full positive push
  and the classical matching conditions. The full logarithm and the
  stationary logarithm are kept distinct until their difference has been
  estimated.

  <subsubsection|From an exterior bound to a logarithmic error>

  Let <math|Z<around|(|x|)>> be the actual pure nonlinear push in coarse
  logarithmic coordinates, and let <math|\<Omega\>\<gtr\>0> be its
  parameter-independent normalizing factor. The stationary construction
  supplies a real normalizing exponent <math|\<Phi\><around|(|x|)>> and the
  positive truncated integral <math|R*<around|(|\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>,x|)>> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:truncated-stationary-log>. Suppose its
  exterior estimate is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:pure-exterior-receipt>0\<leqslant\>Z<around|(|x|)>-\<Omega\>*e<rsup|-\<Phi\><around|(|x|)>>*R*<around|(|\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>,x|)>\<leqslant\>\<Omega\>*e<rsup|-\<Phi\><around|(|x|)>>*\<eta\>.
  </equation>

  The integral over the retained stationary region is exactly the middle
  term, with the same moving Hessian and Haar-chart factor. The complementary
  integral is nonnegative. Its upper bound in
  <eqref|eq:pure-exterior-receipt> is a substantive exterior estimate, not a
  consequence of positivity.

  Assume <math|R\<geqslant\>a<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0>. Then <math|Z\<gtr\>0> and,
  for

  <\equation*>
    E<around|(|x|)>=log Z<around|(|x|)>-log
    \<Omega\>+\<Phi\><around|(|x|)>-log R*<around|(|\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>,x|)>,
  </equation*>

  we have

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:pure-log-value>0\<leqslant\>E<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>/a<rsub|0>.
  </equation>

  Indeed the ratio <math|Z/<around|(|\<Omega\>*e<rsup|-\<Phi\>>*R|)>> lies
  between <math|1> and <math|1+\<eta\>/R>, and <math|0\<leqslant\>log
  <around|(|1+s|)>\<leqslant\>s> for <math|s\<geqslant\>0>. In the
  construction <math|a<rsub|0>=e<rsup|-1>/2> and <math|\<eta\>=exp
  <around|(|-c*P<rsub|F><rsup|2>|)>> with a fixed <math|c\<gtr\>0>.

  <subsubsection|Polynomial derivative bounds for the full logarithm>

  The coarse field enters the positive integral through the smoothing kernel.
  At a fixed fine configuration and fixed tag its contribution has the form

  <\equation*>
    U*<around|(|x,\<omega\>|)>=a<around|(|\<omega\>|)>*e<rsup|p*<around|(|x,\<omega\>|)>>,<space|2em>a<around|(|\<omega\>|)>\<geqslant\>0.
  </equation*>

  The fine density and tag likelihood are held in <math|a>. The exponent
  <math|p> is minus <math|\<beta\>> times the finite sum of kernel penalties
  between the varied coarse bonds and the fixed predicted bonds. On a fixed
  logarithmic ball, smoothness of the group exponential and the penalty gives
  a common derivative bound

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|p|(>\<cdot\>,\<omega\>)\<\|\|\><rsub|J<rsup|k>,x>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|k><around|\||\<beta\>|\|>*<space|0.17em>n<rsub|b>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|n<rsub|b>> bounds the number of coarse bonds. It is uniform in
  the held fine field because the group representation is unitary and that
  field enters only through fixed group multiplication.

  Subtract <math|p*<around|(|x,\<omega\>|)>> before differentiating the
  exponential. The finite partition formula gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|U|(>\<cdot\>,\<omega\>)\<\|\|\><rsub|J<rsup|k>,x>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|k>*U*<around|(|x,\<omega\>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|K<rsub|k>> is a fixed polynomial in
  <math|1+<around|\||\<beta\>|\|>*n<rsub|b>>. Finite tag summation preserves
  this relative bound. Differentiation under the Haar integral is justified
  by the bounded fine density and kernel on the compact configuration space,
  together with the uniform derivative bound on a neighborhood. Consequently

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:pure-relative-derivatives><around|\<\|\|\>|Z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|J<rsup|k>,x>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|k>*Z<around|(|x|)>.
  </equation>

  At a point where <math|Z<around|(|x|)>\<gtr\>0>, normalize by that one
  value: <math|f<around|(|y|)>=Z<around|(|y|)>/Z<around|(|x|)>>. Then
  <math|f<around|(|x|)>=1> and its jets are bounded by <math|K<rsub|k>>. The
  logarithm chain rule at one bounds the jets of <math|log
  Z<around|(|y|)>-log Z<around|(|x|)>> by a fixed polynomial in
  <math|K<rsub|k>>. This argument never divides an absolute derivative bound
  by an uncontrolled small partition function; the factor
  <math|Z<around|(|x|)>> has already canceled in
  <eqref|eq:pure-relative-derivatives>.

  The other terms defining <math|E> have polynomial derivative bounds:
  <math|\<Phi\>> consists of the stationary action multiplied by
  <math|\<beta\>>, the stationary extra exponent and the relative log
  determinant; the truncated logarithm has the bounds proved in the preceding
  section. For auxiliary volumes polynomial in the block radius and
  <math|\<beta\>> in its allowed band, these give a bound <math|z<rsup|d>>
  through any fixed positive order, for some fixed <math|d>, where
  <math|z=\<beta\><rsub|F><around|(|x<rsub|<math-up|scale>>|)>>. The
  order-zero bound for <math|E> follows directly from
  <eqref|eq:pure-log-value>. The exponentially small value bound
  <eqref|eq:pure-log-value> holds on a microscopic ball of radius
  <math|z<rsup|-1>> about each tested coarse point. Applying the
  interpolation lemma of the preceding section yields, for every prescribed
  <math|e>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:pure-log-jets><around|\<\|\|\>|E|\<\|\|\>><rsub|J<rsup|j>>\<leqslant\>z<rsup|e>.
  </equation>

  This uses the exterior estimate throughout that ball. An estimate only at
  its center would not suffice.

  <subsubsection|The exact decomposition and the two cancellations>

  Write <math|a<rsub|<math-up|fine>><around|(|x|)>> for the fine action
  evaluated on the actual stationary branch,
  <math|a<rsub|<math-up|coarse>><around|(|x|)>> for the coarse classical
  action, and <math|d<rsub|<math-up|coarse>><around|(|x|)>> for the
  prescribed coarse quantum and reset contribution. Define

  <\equation*>
    C<around|(|x|)>=a<rsub|<math-up|coarse>><around|(|x|)>-a<rsub|<math-up|fine>><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>W<around|(|x|)>=d<rsub|<math-up|coarse>><around|(|x|)>-\<psi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>-<tfrac|1|2>log
    det M<around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  Thus the normalizing exponent is <math|\<Phi\>=\<beta\>*a<rsub|<math-up|fine>>+\<psi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>log
  det M<around|(|x|)>>. The actual corrected logarithm

  <\equation*>
    G<around|(|x|)>=log Z<around|(|x|)>+\<beta\>*a<rsub|<math-up|coarse>><around|(|x|)>+d<rsub|<math-up|coarse>><around|(|x|)>
  </equation*>

  satisfies the exact identity

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:pure-action-decomposition>G=log
    \<Omega\>+L+E+\<beta\>*C+W,<space|2em>L<around|(|x|)>=log
    R*<around|(|\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>,x|)>.
  </equation>

  Every function in this identity uses the same stationary branch,
  coefficient data and finite torus.

  Classical matching through degree four says
  <math|D<rsup|r>*C<around|(|0|)>=0> for <math|0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>4>.
  Suppose its derivatives of orders <math|1> through <math|n\<geqslant\>5> on
  the ball of radius <math|h\<leqslant\>1> are bounded by <math|K<rsub|C>>.
  Taylor's formula, applied also to each derivative of order less than five,
  gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:pure-classical-fifth>C\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|n>*<around|(|K<rsub|C>*h<rsup|5>,h<rsup|-1>|)>.
  </equation>

  For orders at least five the original derivative bound suffices, because
  <math|h<rsup|r>\<leqslant\>h<rsup|5>>; for lower orders integrate the fifth
  derivative along the radial segment, using the zero jets at its starting
  point.

  There is a distinct cancellation for the linear part of <math|W>. The
  actual corrected logarithm <math|G> is invariant under the prescribed
  lattice translations and signed coordinate symmetries. For each coordinate
  basis vector supported on the bond <math|<around|(|b,\<mu\>|)>>, central
  inversion followed by translation by <math|2*b+e<rsub|\<mu\>>> fixes that
  unoriented bond and reverses its orientation. It therefore sends the
  single-coordinate logarithmic vector <math|e<rsub|i>> to <math|-e<rsub|i>>.
  Differentiating invariance at zero gives
  <math|D*G<around|(|0|)>*e<rsub|i>=-D*G<around|(|0|)>*e<rsub|i>>, hence
  <math|D*G<around|(|0|)>=0>. Since <math|D*C<around|(|0|)>=0>,
  <eqref|eq:pure-action-decomposition> implies

  <\equation*>
    D*W<around|(|0|)>=-D*L<around|(|0|)>-D*E<around|(|0|)>.
  </equation*>

  In particular, if the relevant jets of <math|L,E> are bounded by
  <math|a,v>, then <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D*W<around|(|0|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>a+v>.
  This cancellation uses the symmetry of the full logarithm, rather than
  asserting that its separate stationary terms are individually even in every
  coarse coordinate.

  The finite-volume quadratic matching estimate supplies a further bound
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|2>*W<around|(|0|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>q>.
  Its operator norm includes the conversion from the tested momentum waves to
  the actual coordinate space. If the number of coordinates is at most
  <math|M>, that conversion has a fixed polynomial cost in <math|M>. Thus the
  exponentially small finite-volume bound has the form

  <\equation*>
    q\<leqslant\>P<around|(|M|)>*\<Theta\>*\<Lambda\>*e<rsup|-\<nu\>*\<ell\>>,<space|2em>\<Theta\>,\<nu\>\<gtr\>0,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<ell\>> is the auxiliary torus side and <math|P> is a fixed
  nonnegative polynomial including the coordinate norm conversion. This
  quadratic matching is a separate input from classical matching through
  degree four.

  If derivatives of <math|W> of orders three through <math|n> are bounded by
  <math|K<rsub|W>> on the same ball, Taylor's formula now gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:pure-quantum-third>W-W<around|(|0|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|n>*<around*|(|K<rsub|W>*h<rsup|3>+3*<around|{|<around|(|a+v|)>*h+q*h<rsup|2>|}>,h<rsup|-1>|)>.
  </equation>

  For orders zero, one and two, integrate the third derivative and retain the
  displayed first and second jets. For higher orders,
  <math|h<rsup|r>\<leqslant\>h<rsup|3>> pays the original bound. The factor
  three is a common upper bound for all these finite Taylor coefficients.

  <subsubsection|The final small-field price>

  Take <math|h=z<rsup|-7/16>> and assume <math|\<beta\>\<in\><around|[|z/2,2*z|]>>.
  The stationary estimate gives <math|a=C<around|(|x<rsub|<math-up|scale>>|)>*\<beta\><rsup|-1>+z<rsup|-2>>
  with <math|C> of upper exponent zero; <eqref|eq:pure-log-jets> gives
  <math|v=z<rsup|-2>>. On the radial segment to the tested point, the
  centered stationary logarithm has price <math|a*h> at scale
  <math|h<rsup|-1>>, by integrating its first derivative; the higher
  derivative bounds give the same price since <math|h\<leqslant\>1>. The
  centered error has price <math|2*v>. Using
  <eqref|eq:pure-action-decomposition>\U<eqref|eq:pure-quantum-third>
  therefore gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:pure-remainder-budget>G-G<around|(|0|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around*|(|a*h+2*v+\<beta\>*K<rsub|C>*h<rsup|5>+K<rsub|W>*h<rsup|3>+3*<around|{|<around|(|a+v|)>*h+q*h<rsup|2>|}>,h<rsup|-1>|)>*<space|2em><around|(|1\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>n|)>.
  </equation>

  All constants refer to the same original data.

  The coefficients <math|K<rsub|C>,K<rsub|W>>, the coordinate bound <math|M>,
  and the loop price <math|\<Lambda\>> have upper exponent zero;
  <math|\<Theta\>,\<nu\>> are fixed positive constants. The auxiliary side is
  at least the prescribed logarithmic-square scale, so its exponential
  finite-volume factor pays every fixed inverse power; in particular <math|q>
  may be bounded by a function of upper exponent <math|-1>. The terms in the
  displayed amplitude have upper exponents at most

  <\equation*>
    -23/16,<space|1em>-2,<space|1em>-19/16,<space|1em>-21/16,<space|1em>-23/16,<space|1em>-15/8.
  </equation*>

  Their maximum is <math|-19/16\<less\>-23/20>. After one common increase of
  the stage threshold, the amplitude is at most <math|z<rsup|-23/20>>. The
  estimate holds at every healthy coarse point with the two microscopic
  margins needed for the comparison ball and the radial segment. It supplies
  the centered chart estimate used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:healthy-boundary-jets>.
  Its remaining hypotheses are the actual stationary-branch bounds, classical
  and quadratic matching, and the exterior estimate
  <eqref|eq:pure-exterior-receipt>; none is replaced by a comparison with a
  different finite-volume model.

  <subsection|Extending small-field derivative estimates to the
  boundary><label|sec:healthy-boundary-jets>

  A small-field configuration may have a plaquette exactly at the health
  threshold. Identities first proved where every plaquette is strictly below
  that threshold must therefore be extended to this boundary. We follow the
  actual radial argument. It uses the smallness of the logarithmic field and
  a second-derivative estimate; it does not assume that the healthy set is
  convex.

  <subsubsection|A radial retreat lemma>

  Let <math|X:<around|[|0,1|]>\<to\>E> be a twice continuously differentiable
  curve in a finite-dimensional normed space, with

  <\equation*>
    sup<rsub|0\<leqslant\>s\<leqslant\>1><around|\<\|\|\>|X<rprime|''><around|(|s|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>K\<less\>t,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|X<around|(|1|)>-X<around|(|0|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>t.
  </equation*>

  Then <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|X<around|(|s|)>-X<around|(|0|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>t>
  for all <math|s\<less\>1> sufficiently close to one. If the endpoint
  inequality is strict, this is continuity. In the remaining case put
  <math|D=X<around|(|1|)>-X<around|(|0|)>>, so
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D|\<\|\|\>>=t>, and
  <math|g<around|(|s|)>=X<around|(|s|)>-s*D>. The fundamental theorem of
  calculus gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|X<rprime|'><around|(|1|)>-D|\<\|\|\>>=<around*|\||<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1><around|{|X<rprime|'><around|(|1|)>-X<rprime|'><around|(|r|)>|}>*<space|0.17em>d*r|\|>\<leqslant\>K\<less\>t.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|g<rprime|'><around|(|1|)>=X<rprime|'><around|(|1|)>-D>, the
  one-sided difference quotient satisfies
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|g<around|(|s|)>-g<around|(|1|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<less\><around|(|1-s|)>*t>
  near one. For <math|0\<leqslant\>s\<less\>1> in that neighborhood,

  <\equation*>
    X<around|(|s|)>-X<around|(|0|)>=s*D+g<around|(|s|)>-g<around|(|1|)>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|X<around|(|s|)>-X<around|(|0|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>s*t+<around|(|1-s|)>*t=t.
  </equation*>

  The inequality is strict even when the original endpoint lies exactly on
  the boundary.

  Let <math|W> be the actual logarithmic coordinate of a coarse field, and
  write <math|U<rsub|s>=<math-up|actual><around|(|s*W|)>> for the field
  obtained by exponentiating its bond coordinates. For one plaquette let
  <math|X<around|(|s|)>=\<rho\><around|(|<math-up|Hol><rsub|\<square\>><space|0.17em>U<rsub|s>|)>>,
  where <math|\<rho\>> is the fixed unitary representation. Then
  <math|X<around|(|0|)>=1> and

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|chord><around|(|<math-up|Hol><rsub|\<square\>><space|0.17em>U<rsub|s>|)>=<around|\<\|\|\>|X<around|(|s|)>-1|\<\|\|\>>/r<rsub|G>,
  </equation*>

  with the fixed representation normalization <math|r<rsub|G>\<gtr\>0>. On a
  fixed small logarithmic ball the exponential has bounded first and second
  derivatives. If the coordinate norm is at most <math|w\<leqslant\>1>, its
  bond derivative scale is at most <math|C*w>. The product rule over the four
  plaquette bonds gives

  <\equation*>
    sup<rsub|s\<in\><around|[|0,1|]>><around|\<\|\|\>|X<rprime|''><around|(|s|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|G>*<around|(|4*r<rsub|G>*C*w|)><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  Thus, if <math|<around|(|4*r<rsub|G>*C*w|)><rsup|2>\<less\>\<tau\>> and all
  endpoint plaquette chords are at most <math|\<tau\>>, the retreat lemma
  with <math|t=r<rsub|G>*\<tau\>> makes every plaquette strictly healthy for
  <math|s\<less\>1> close enough to one. There are finitely many plaquettes,
  so one neighborhood works for all of them.

  In the construction the logarithmic radius is
  <math|h=\<beta\><rsub|F><rsup|-7/16>>, while the health threshold has scale
  <math|\<tau\>=\<beta\><rsub|F><rsup|-1/2>> times its prescribed slowly
  varying factor. The coordinate norm conversion has a fixed constant. Since
  <math|h<rsup|2>> has exponent <math|-7/8\<less\>-1/2>, the displayed strict
  curvature inequality holds eventually, uniformly over every field in that
  logarithmic ball. This is the needed strict radial approximation.

  <subsubsection|Transferring chart jets to ordered group derivatives>

  We record the normalization used with this approximation. Let <math|j> be
  fixed. Compactness on a small Lie-algebra chart supplies constants
  <math|J,d\<gtr\>0> for derivatives through order <math|j> of the local
  logarithm. Shrink the chart so that the logarithm inverts the exponential
  there. Let <math|U=<math-up|actual><around|(|W|)>>, with
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|W|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>h>, and suppose

  <\equation*>
    f\<circ\><math-up|actual>\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>*<around|(|T,h<rsup|-1>;W|)>.
  </equation*>

  Consider an admissible group-valued variation <math|V<around|(|y|)>>
  through <math|U>, whose link derivative scale is <math|C>. The local
  logarithm and the inverse linear coordinate map produce a chart variation
  <math|a<around|(|y|)>> with <math|a<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>=W> and
  <math|V=<math-up|actual>\<circ\>a> near <math|y<rsub|0>>. The logarithm
  chain rule bounds its positive-order derivatives by

  <\equation*>
    j!J*<around|{|<around|(|1+r<rsub|G>|)>*C|}><rsup|r>*<space|2em><around|(|1\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>j|)>.
  </equation*>

  Assume

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|1+r<rsub|G>|)>*C\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>j!J*<around|(|1+r<rsub|G>|)>*C\<leqslant\>h.
  </equation*>

  Then every derivative of <math|a> through order <math|j>, including its
  value, is at most <math|h>. In the chain rule for
  <math|f\<circ\><math-up|actual>\<circ\>a>, an outer derivative of order
  <math|m> costs <math|T*h<rsup|-m>> and its <math|m> inner factors cost at
  most <math|h<rsup|m>>. They cancel. Bounding the finite partition count
  conservatively gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:healthy-chart-ticket><around|\<\|\|\>|f\<circ\>V|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j,y<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>j!3<rsup|j>*T.
  </equation>

  The same estimate applies to ordered left derivatives along any list of at
  most <math|j> directions of norm at most <math|\<tau\>>. Indeed use the
  corresponding ordered products of exponentials as the variation; the
  compact exponential bounds give a link scale at most
  <math|C<rsub|j>*j*\<tau\>>. Choose <math|C> to dominate that value. In the
  present construction <math|C> has upper exponent <math|-1/2>, strictly
  below the radius exponent <math|-7/16>, so both normalization inequalities
  hold eventually. The construction also proves the required local
  differentiability of <math|f> in the group chart.

  <subsubsection|Subtracting a summable activity array>

  Let <math|u<rsub|i>> be regular activities with nonempty assigned supports
  and nonnegative jet prices <math|k<rsub|i>>. Suppose their rooted
  support-weighted price is at most <math|b> per torus site and their
  derivatives in directions bounded by <math|\<tau\>> are at most
  <math|k<rsub|i>>. Then

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|i>k<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*b.
  </equation*>

  This follows by summing over the roots and removing weights at least one.
  On the open set <math|\<cal-S\>> of strictly healthy configurations, every
  <math|u<rsub|i>> is differentiable to order <math|j>. Their series can be
  differentiated term by term there. To check the needed domination for any
  fixed list of directions, scale every direction by a common positive number
  so that its norm is at most <math|\<tau\>>. Multilinearity of the ordered
  derivative then multiplies the summable majorant <math|k<rsub|i>> by only
  one fixed power of that number. The common majorant is uniform on
  <math|\<cal-S\>>. The local differentiation theorem for normally convergent
  derivative series therefore applies, order by order. In particular, for
  <math|U\<in\>\<cal-S\>> and normalized directions,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:healthy-array-sum><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|i>u<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*b,<space|2em><around*|\||D<rsub|\<ell\>>*<big|sum><rsub|i>u<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*b*<space|1em><around|(|<around|\||\<ell\>|\|>\<leqslant\>j|)>.
  </equation>

  Suppose the local action identity on <math|\<cal-S\>> is

  <\equation*>
    f<around|(|U|)>=z-H<around|(|U|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i>u<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|z> is constant. For any function <math|q> write
  <math|<wide|q|~><around|(|U|)>=q<around|(|U|)>-q<around|(|1|)>>. The
  identity field belongs to <math|\<cal-S\>>, because <math|\<tau\>\<gtr\>0>.
  Subtracting its value yields

  <\equation*>
    <wide|H|~><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i>u<rsub|i><around|(|1|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i>u<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>-<wide|f|~><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  Assume now that the chart bound with amplitude <math|T> and scale
  <math|h<rsup|-1>> holds for <math|<wide|f|~>\<circ\><math-up|actual>> at
  every <math|W<rprime|'>> such that <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|W<rprime|'>|\<\|\|\>>+2*d\<less\>h>
  and <math|<math-up|actual><around|(|W<rprime|'>|)>\<in\>\<cal-S\>>. This is
  a bound on the centered function itself. Apply
  <eqref|eq:healthy-chart-ticket> to <math|<wide|f|~>>. Combining it with
  <eqref|eq:healthy-array-sum> gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:healthy-interior-remainder><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsub|\<ell\>>*<wide|H|~><around|(|U|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>j!3<rsup|j>*T+2<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*b.
  </equation>

  The extra factor two covers the centered value at derivative order zero;
  for positive orders the identity-field constant differentiates to zero.

  Now let <math|U=<math-up|actual><around|(|W|)>> have no flagged plaquettes,
  allowing equality at the threshold, and assume
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|W|\<\|\|\>>+2*d\<less\>h> with <math|d\<gtr\>0>. For
  <math|0\<leqslant\>s\<leqslant\>1>, <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|s*W|\<\|\|\>>+2*d\<less\>h>
  as well. The radial retreat places <math|U<rsub|s>> in <math|\<cal-S\>> for
  <math|s\<less\>1> sufficiently close to one, so the same interior estimate
  applies there. Assume <math|H> is regular on the full healthy domain, as
  supplied by the local action construction. Its ordered derivatives through
  order <math|j> are continuous at <math|U> in the local group chart. Since
  <math|U<rsub|s>\<to\>U>, passing to the limit in
  <eqref|eq:healthy-interior-remainder> gives the same bound at <math|U>.
  Only <math|H> needs this boundary regularity: no boundary smoothness is
  inferred for the original masked series or for <math|f> from its interior
  identity.

  <subsection|The principal regular insertion after one
  step><label|sec:regular-principal-producer>

  We combine the reference attenuation with the comparison to the actual
  principal cluster. This supplies the remaining ordinary principal term in
  the zero-coefficient transition of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:leaf-transfer>.
  The forced and nonprincipal terms retain the prices proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:projection-payment-producer>.

  Let <math|f> be a real regular fine insertion on <math|X>, of price
  <math|\<tau\>\<geqslant\>0>, and let <math|Y> be its selected coarse
  enlargement. Write <math|q=C<rsub|B<rsub|f>><around|(|X|)>>,
  <math|q<rprime|'>=C<rsub|B<rsub|c>><around|(|Y|)>>, and
  <math|m<rsub|f>=e<rsup|a<rsub|f>*q>*\<tau\>> for its fine mass. Its
  polynomial moments through degree <math|d>, in the sense of
  <eqref|eq:polynomial-moment-null>, are assumed zero whenever <math|X> is
  nonwrapping. For the selected transition, assume <math|X> has an anchor,
  <math|B<rsub|f>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|c>>, and the coarse period side
  <math|c> satisfies <math|100*<around|(|1+B<rsub|c>|)>\<leqslant\>c>. These
  are the radius and room conditions used in the short-support geometry. The
  order and weight requirements are

  <\equation*>
    max <around|{|2,d+1|}>\<leqslant\>n<rsub|c>,<space|2em>d\<leqslant\>p<rsub|f>,<space|2em>d+2\<leqslant\>J<rsub|f>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|J<rsub|f>> is the fine kernel's available derivative level.
  Together with the local regularity required in the preceding sections,
  these permit the strip derivatives and the extra insertion derivative used
  in the Taylor argument. Let <math|e=a<rsub|c>+2> be the exponent charged by
  the coarse reserved mass, and put

  <\equation*>
    \<frak-m\><rsub|c><around|(|h|)>=e<rsup|e*q<rprime|'>>*h,<space|2em>\<gamma\><rsub|L>=<frac|L<rsup|-2/5>|h<rsub|d>>,<space|2em>w<rsub|L>=<frac|\<gamma\><rsub|L>|2*L<rsup|d>>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|h<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>1> is the fixed projection and frame cost.
  We prove a coarse derivative bound of reserved mass at most
  <math|w<rsub|L>*m<rsub|f>> for the actual principal real insertion.

  The constants in the short and long reference estimates, say
  <math|A<rsub|d>> and <math|A<rprime|'><rsub|d>>, are chosen before
  <math|L>. Choose <math|L> so that the support compression inequality holds
  and

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|d>*L<rsup|-d-1>\<leqslant\>w<rsub|L>/2,<space|2em>A<rprime|'><rsub|d>*L<rsup|-d-1>\<leqslant\>w<rsub|L>/2.
  </equation*>

  This is possible since each reduces to <math|4*h<rsub|d>*A<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>L<rsup|3/5>>
  or the analogous inequality for <math|A<rprime|'><rsub|d>>. The
  strip-to-polynomial bound is chosen before these two constants; it is
  uniform in all admissible periods. Only after these choices do we increase
  the entrance coupling to meet the reference smallness conditions.

  Denote the actual principal insertion by <math|F> and the reference
  Gaussian integral by <math|H>. For <math|q<rprime|'>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|c>>,
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:principal-gaussian-comparison> gives,
  uniformly over the forests and replica choices,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||D<rsup|j>*<around|(|F-H|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<tau\>*z<rsub|\<beta\>>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>n<rsub|c>,
  </equation*>

  for the admissible coarse ordered directions. Here
  <math|z<rsub|\<beta\>>\<to\>0> after <math|L> is fixed. Averaging over the
  forests and replica choices preserves this bound: the norm of a finite
  average of differences is at most their common bound. The enlargement
  estimate gives

  <\equation*>
    \<frak-m\><rsub|c>*<around|(|\<tau\>*z<rsub|\<beta\>>|)>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|e*t<rsub|r>>*z<rsub|\<beta\>>*m<rsub|f>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|t<rsub|r>> is the fixed allocation cost. Increase the entrance
  coupling so that <math|e<rsup|e*t<rsub|r>>*z<rsub|\<beta\>>\<leqslant\>w<rsub|L>/2>.

  If <math|q<rsup|3>\<less\>L>, the stated radius, room and anchor conditions
  make <math|X> nonwrapping by the short-support geometry, so its assumed
  moments vanish. The short reference estimate then gives

  <\equation*>
    \<frak-m\><rsub|c><around|(|<text|price of
    >H|)>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|d>*L<rsup|-d-1>*m<rsub|f>.
  </equation*>

  If <math|q<rsup|3>\<geqslant\>L>, use the separate-gauge reference bound.
  Multiplying it by <math|<around|[|<around|(|1+q|)><rsup|3>/L|]><rsup|d+1>\<geqslant\>1>
  inserts <math|L<rsup|-d-1>>; the extra support power is absorbed by the
  unused exponential fine mass, as proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:principal-reference-attenuation>. Thus its
  reserved mass is at most <math|A<rprime|'><rsub|d>*L<rsup|-d-1>*m<rsub|f>>.
  In either case the reference price and comparison price add to at most
  <math|w<rsub|L>*m<rsub|f>>.

  It remains to consider <math|q<rprime|'>\<gtr\>B<rsub|c>>. Here the
  principal reserve from Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:projection-payment-producer>
  suffices directly. The large-support derivative bound has a fixed
  polynomial cost in <math|q<rprime|'>> multiplied by an exponential with at
  least one spare unit of support exponent. For a fixed integer <math|k>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|1+q<rprime|'>|)><rsup|k>*e<rsup|-q<rprime|'>>\<leqslant\><around*|[|sup<rsub|t\<geqslant\>0><around|(|1+t|)><rsup|k>*e<rsup|-t/2>|]>*e<rsup|-B<rsub|c>/2>.
  </equation*>

  The supremum is finite: differentiation of <math|k*log
  <around|(|1+t|)>-t/2> puts its maximum at <math|t=max
  <around|{|0,2*k-1|}>>. The remaining multiplier also contains the
  covariance and chart price divided by the Gaussian scale: denote it by
  <math|E<rsub|L><around|(|\<beta\>|)>>. It has finite upper power exponent
  in the incoming coupling, as proved in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:projection-payment-producer>;
  it is not a fixed constant. The principal reserved mass is at most
  <math|E<rsub|L><around|(|\<beta\>|)>*e<rsup|-B<rsub|c>/2>*m<rsub|f>>. For
  some fixed <math|a,C>, eventually <math|E<rsub|L><around|(|\<beta\>|)>\<leqslant\>C*\<beta\><rsup|a>>.
  Since <math|B<rsub|c>> grows quadratically in <math|log \<beta\><rsub|c>>,
  and <math|\<beta\><rsub|f>=\<beta\><rsub|c>+> a fixed shift after <math|L>
  is chosen, <math|log <around|(|C*\<beta\><rsup|a>*e<rsup|-B<rsub|c>/2>|)>\<to\>-\<infty\>>.
  Increase the coupling until <math|E<rsub|L><around|(|\<beta\>|)>*e<rsup|-B<rsub|c>/2>\<leqslant\>w<rsub|L>>.
  This estimate applies without a logarithmic chart on the whole support.

  All cases give the asserted principal bound. For a complex insertion, apply
  the result to its real and imaginary parts. Each has the same admissible
  fine price, and combining their two coarse bounds costs
  <math|2*w<rsub|L>*m<rsub|f>>. Physical normalization multiplies by
  <math|L<rsup|d>>, giving

  <\equation*>
    L<rsup|d>*<around|(|2*w<rsub|L>*m<rsub|f>|)>=\<gamma\><rsub|L>*m<rsub|f>.
  </equation*>

  This is the principal share used by the ordinary projection estimate. It is
  produced for the same insertion, support, forest average and transition as
  the other terms; no replacement of the transition operator is involved. The
  case of a primary coefficient of positive grade uses moments only through
  one less than that grade and is handled separately below.

  <subsection|Calibration of the primary coefficient
  transition><label|sec:primary-calibration-producer>

  The primary transition must do more than bound a regular insertion. Its
  coefficient must approach the prescribed linear transport on the finite
  coefficient frame. We prove this by the same three comparisons used in the
  construction: the actual principal term to its Gaussian reference, the
  Gaussian reference to the linear predicted field, and that field to the
  finite polynomial probe calculation.

  Fix a frame of total degree <math|d> and a nonconstant coordinate <math|i>.
  Write <math|g<rsub|i>\<in\><around|{|1,\<ldots\>,d|}>> for its spatial
  grade and <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>> for its order of vanishing in the field
  amplitude. These are different gradings. The primary insertion
  <math|h<rsub|i>> is the local section at fine threshold
  <math|\<theta\><rsub|f>>, normalized by
  <math|\<theta\><rsub|f><rsup|-\<ell\><rsub|i>>> as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-sections>. Its regularity price is
  bounded by the fixed fine-frame constant <math|T<rsub|f>>. All constants in
  this section may depend on the chosen frame and the block factor <math|L>.
  Periods and the incoming coupling remain uniform. Both adjacent kernel
  levels are at least four, and the fine level is at least <math|d>.

  <subsubsection|Removing the Gaussian fluctuation at the flat field>

  Let <math|h> be one real component of <math|h<rsub|i>>. Denote by
  <math|H<around|(|V|)>> its reference Gaussian integral and by

  <\equation*>
    h<rsub|0><around|(|V|)>=h<around*|(|exp
    <around*|(|\<cal-A\><space|0.17em><math-up|crop><rsub|R><around|(|log
    V|)>|)>|)>
  </equation*>

  the linear prediction, with the installed response <math|\<cal-A\>> and
  <math|R=45*<around|(|1+B<rsub|c>|)>>. This formula is used as a germ at
  <math|V=1>; its crop is in the identity gauge. Let <math|k> be the
  derivative order of a frame probe. Purchase regularity through order
  <math|n\<geqslant\>k+1>, as well as <math|n\<geqslant\>2>. The additional
  derivative will control the change of the insertion's base point.

  Use the fixed Gaussian measure at <math|V=1> and let
  <math|R<rsub|V><around|(|z|)>> be the normalized density ratio. Put
  <math|A<rsub|V><around|(|z|)>=h*<around|(|W*<around|(|V,z|)>|)>>, where
  <math|W> is the actual received reference configuration. With the cutoff
  <math|\<Gamma\>> of the preceding section, the exact difference of
  integrands is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:primary-integrand-difference>\<Gamma\>*R<rsub|V>*A<rsub|V>-h<rsub|0><around|(|V|)>=\<Gamma\>*R<rsub|V>*<around|(|A<rsub|V>-h<rsub|0><around|(|V|)>|)>+\<Gamma\>*<around|(|R<rsub|V>-1|)>*h<rsub|0><around|(|V|)>+<around|(|\<Gamma\>-1|)>*h<rsub|0><around|(|V|)>.
  </equation>

  Thus no derivative of the varying density is discarded.

  Write <math|P<around|(|z|)>\<geqslant\>max
  <around|{|1,<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|tape><around|(|z|)>|\<\|\|\>>|}>> for
  the fixed Gaussian coordinate norm augmented by one. On the cutoff support,
  the display comparison of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:principal-reference-attenuation>
  gives, for every jet through order <math|n>, an error of fine direction
  price

  <\equation*>
    p<around|(|z|)>=C<rsub|n>*<frac|Q*s<rsup|2>+g<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|tape><around|(|z|)>|\<\|\|\>>|\<theta\><rsub|f>>.
  </equation*>

  The linear predictor's argument jets have bounded fine direction price
  <math|W\<geqslant\>1>. The nonlinear jets have price at most
  <math|W+p<around|(|z|)>>. For a partition with <math|r> arguments, subtract
  the two chain-rule terms by first changing their base point and then their
  arguments one at a time. Changing the base point costs at most
  <math|C*\<tau\>*p<around|(|z|)>*<around|(|W+p<around|(|z|)>|)><rsup|r>>:
  integrate the <math|<around|(|r+1|)>>st derivative of the insertion along
  the healthy radial segment proved earlier. Changing the arguments costs at
  most <math|C*r*\<tau\>*p<around|(|z|)>*<around|(|W+p<around|(|z|)>|)><rsup|r-1>>.
  All these terms use <math|r+1\<leqslant\>n>. Summing the finitely many
  partitions proves

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||D<rsup|j>*<around|(|A<rsub|V><around|(|z|)>-h<rsub|0><around|(|V|)>|)>|\|><rsub|V=1>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|n>*\<tau\>*p<around|(|z|)>*<around|(|W+p<around|(|z|)>|)><rsup|n>,<space|2em>j\<leqslant\>k.
  </equation*>

  The same argument without subtraction gives
  <math|<around|\||D<rsup|j>*h<rsub|0><around|(|1|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|n>*\<tau\>>.

  The density-ratio calculation in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-differentiation>
  gives a small factor <math|\<theta\><rsub|f>> times a polynomial in the
  read radius and <math|P<around|(|z|)><rsup|2>> in every positive-order jet
  of <math|R<rsub|V>-1> at <math|1>. Its value there is zero since
  <math|R<rsub|1>=1>. More explicitly, differentiate <math|<tfrac|1|2>log det
  C<around|(|V|)>-<tfrac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|z,C<around|(|V|)>*z|\<rangle\>>>
  with the reference precision <math|C>. Each positive-order term contains at
  least one admissible coarse direction, and the inverse and precision jets
  bound it by <math|K<rsub|L>*\<theta\><rsub|f>*<around|(|1+B<rsub|c>|)><rsup|a>*P<around|(|z|)><rsup|2>>.
  The partition formula for its exponential multiplies such factors,
  retaining at least one.

  The cutoff error is controlled even outside its support. It is one on the
  plateau <math|g<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|tape><around|(|z|)><rsub|a>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>*\<theta\><rsub|f>/8>
  for every coordinate <math|a>. If a coordinate fails this test, then
  <math|<around|(|g/\<theta\><rsub|f>|)>*P<around|(|z|)><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>\<varepsilon\>/8>,
  while <math|<around|\||\<Gamma\>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>2>. Consequently

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<Gamma\><around|(|z|)>-1|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|16|\<varepsilon\>>*<frac|g|\<theta\><rsub|f>>*P<around|(|z|)><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  This bound does not require evaluating <math|A<rsub|V>> off the cutoff
  support: there the first two terms of <eqref|eq:primary-integrand-difference>
  vanish identically.

  Collect the displayed small factors into
  <math|J<rsub|\<beta\>>\<geqslant\>0> so that

  <\equation*>
    J<rsub|\<beta\>>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|L>*<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|f>+g/\<theta\><rsub|f>|)>*<around|(|1+B<rsub|c>|)><rsup|a>,<space|2em>p<around|(|z|)>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|\<beta\>>*P<around|(|z|)><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  The bounded threshold ratio supplies <math|s<rsup|2>/\<theta\><rsub|f>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|L>*\<theta\><rsub|f>*<around|(|1+B<rsub|c>|)><rsup|a>>.
  Increase the coupling until <math|J<rsub|\<beta\>>\<leqslant\>1>. Leibniz'
  rule in <eqref|eq:primary-integrand-difference> and the partition bounds
  then give

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||D<rsup|j>*<around|(|\<Gamma\>*R<rsub|V>*A<rsub|V>-h<rsub|0><around|(|V|)>|)>|\|><rsub|V=1>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|n>*\<tau\>*J<rsub|\<beta\>>*P<around|(|z|)><rsup|4*<around|(|n+1|)>>,<space|2em>j\<leqslant\>k.
  </equation*>

  All factors from higher-order products are absorbed by the displayed power,
  since <math|P\<geqslant\>1> and <math|J<rsub|\<beta\>>\<leqslant\>1>. The
  fixed Gaussian moments bound its integral by
  <math|K<rprime|'><rsub|L>*<around|(|1+B<rsub|c>|)><rsup|b>>. Compact cutoff
  support and the common local smoothness justify differentiation under the
  integral; the polynomial bound also dominates the subtracted constant term.
  The monitor estimate from the preceding section implies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:primary-flat-gaussian>max<rsub|j\<leqslant\>k><around|\||D<rsup|j>*H<around|(|1|)>-D<rsup|j>*h<rsub|0><around|(|1|)>|\|>\<longrightarrow\>0*<space|1em><around|(|\<beta\>\<to\>\<infty\>|)>.
  </equation>

  The bound is uniform in every forest, replica choice and allowed period.
  Averaging therefore preserves it. Combining it with the actual-to-reference
  comparison gives the same convergence for the actual principal insertion.

  <subsubsection|Normalized stencil functions and their jets>

  We give the finite-dimensional estimate needed to sample <math|h<rsub|0>>
  on the frame probes. Let <math|H<rsub|i>> be the analytic unnormalized
  stencil function of coordinate <math|i>, on the finite-dimensional space of
  its link logs. It satisfies <math|D<rsup|j>*H<rsub|i><around|(|0|)>=0> for
  <math|j\<less\>\<ell\><rsub|i>>. For <math|0\<less\>t\<leqslant\>1> set

  <\equation*>
    H<rsub|i,t><around|(|v|)>=t<rsup|-\<ell\><rsub|i>>*H<rsub|i>*<around|(|t*v|)>.
  </equation*>

  On any fixed ball <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|t*v|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|0>>,
  Taylor's formula and compactness give, for every prescribed order <math|j>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:primary-normalized-jets><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|j>*H<rsub|i,t><around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|\<ell\><rsub|i>>.
  </equation>

  Indeed let <math|a=max <around|{|\<ell\><rsub|i>-j,0|}>>. The vanishing
  derivatives imply <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|j>*H<rsub|i>*<around|(|t*v|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*<around|\<\|\|\>|t*v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|a>>
  by integrating <math|a> times from zero. For <math|a=0>, use the maximum of
  the continuous derivative on the ball. The scaling factor is
  <math|t<rsup|j-\<ell\><rsub|i>+a>\<leqslant\>1>, with nonnegative exponent;
  and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|a>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|\<ell\><rsub|i>>>.
  Taking a maximum over the finitely many required orders proves the bound
  with one <math|C>.

  Suppose two <math|C<rsup|k>> curves <math|v,q> have jets at zero bounded by
  <math|U+e,U>, respectively, and their jet differences through order
  <math|k> are at most <math|e\<geqslant\>0>. Suppose also their values,
  multiplied by <math|t>, lie in the fixed ball. Along the segment joining
  their values, use <eqref|eq:primary-normalized-jets> through order
  <math|k+1>. For a partition with <math|r\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>k> blocks,
  the difference of the outer <math|r>th derivatives costs at most
  <math|C*<around|(|1+U+e|)><rsup|\<ell\><rsub|i>>*e>. Replacing its <math|r>
  arguments one at a time costs at most <math|C*r*<around|(|1+U+e|)><rsup|\<ell\><rsub|i>+r>*e>.
  Thus

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:primary-stencil-stability>max<rsub|j\<leqslant\>k><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|j>*<around|(|H<rsub|i,t>\<circ\>v|)><around|(|0|)>-D<rsup|j>*<around|(|H<rsub|i,t>\<circ\>q|)><around|(|0|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|k,i,U>*<around|(|1+e|)><rsup|\<ell\><rsub|i>+k>*e.
  </equation>

  This estimate is uniform as <math|t*\<downarrow\>*0>. The same partition
  calculation without subtraction bounds the jets of
  <math|H<rsub|i,t>\<circ\>q> uniformly. The constant is a finite sum over
  partitions, not a limit at the singular normalization <math|t=0>.

  <subsubsection|The cropped torus probe and the full polynomial forecast>

  A probe consists of a fixed polynomial potential and a derivative order
  <math|k\<leqslant\>d>. Its test-link curve is the holonomy curve defined in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-sections>. At derivative order
  <math|j\<leqslant\>k>, its link-log jet on the covering lattice is a
  polynomial field of degree at most <math|d*j>. This follows by expanding
  the exponential, product and logarithm to order <math|j>: every term is a
  product of at most <math|j> potential factors, each of degree at most
  <math|d>. Integration along a unit bond and finite differences do not
  increase that bound. The Taylor coefficients are fixed by the probe.

  Normalize the coarse probe by its threshold <math|y=\<theta\><rsub|c>> and
  the fine link field by <math|t=\<theta\><rsub|f>>, with
  <math|y/t\<leqslant\>2> and <math|y\<leqslant\>1>. Apply the installed
  response to the cropped torus link-log curve and restrict to the fixed fine
  stencil; call the resulting normalized curve <math|v<rsub|R>>. Let <math|q>
  be the full covering-lattice prediction obtained by applying the same
  response to the polynomial link-log jets. Only its <math|k>-jet is used; it
  is equivalently obtained by the finite polynomial jet algebra of the probe.
  Both curves vanish at zero. The exact correspondence between periodic and
  covering convolution holds because the retained kernel is periodized from
  its absolutely summable covering kernel.

  On covering sites of distance at most <math|R>, the crop and the full probe
  have identical jets. The room inequality <math|2*R\<less\>c> ensures that
  these sites have their canonical lifts, so there is no seam error there.
  Outside this region, the probe jets have polynomial growth and the cropped
  jets have norm at most <math|K*<around|(|1+4*R|)><rsup|d*k>>. The response
  strip bound gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:primary-probe-tail>max<rsub|j\<leqslant\>k><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|j>*v<rsub|R><around|(|0|)>-D<rsup|j>*q<around|(|0|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*<around|{|1+K*<around|(|1+4*R|)><rsup|d*k>|}>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*R/2>=:e<rsub|R>.
  </equation>

  To verify the tail estimate, take half the exponential weight to bound the
  polynomial factor uniformly, and sum the remaining weighted kernel. The
  finite stencil offsets and four directions supply only a fixed multiplier.
  The torus sum is the same sum regrouped by residue class; absolute
  convergence permits this regrouping. This is the cropped-versus-full
  comparison, including all derivative orders, for the actual retained
  response.

  The full predicted jets have a bound <math|U> independent of the period and
  <math|R>. Indeed convolution of a degree-<math|d*j> polynomial costs its
  fixed weighted kernel moment. In a nonzero chain-rule term with
  <math|r\<geqslant\>1> blocks, the threshold factor is
  <math|y<rsup|r>/t\<leqslant\>y/t\<leqslant\>2>. The finitely many probe
  partitions therefore have a common bound. Apply
  <eqref|eq:primary-stencil-stability> with <math|t=\<theta\><rsub|f>> and
  <math|R<rsub|0>=1>: both input values are zero and hence lie in this fixed
  positive-radius ball. Since <math|e<rsub|R>\<to\>0>, the resulting
  normalized stencil jet difference tends to zero. Multiplication by the
  fixed physical factor <math|L<rsup|d>> preserves that convergence.

  To identify the prescribed coefficient transport explicitly, let
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|t>> be diagonal multiplication by
  <math|t<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|i>>>, let <math|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|L>>
  be diagonal multiplication by <math|L<rsup|g<rsub|i>>>, and let
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|V><rsub|L>> be diagonal multiplication by
  <math|L<rsup|d-g<rsub|i>>>. If <math|<with|math-font|cal*|R>> is
  coefficient recovery of the full polynomial forecast, the construction's
  heading is the matrix

  <\equation*>
    <with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|\<theta\><rsub|c>>*<space|0.17em><with|math-font|cal*|R>*<space|0.17em><with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|L>*<space|0.17em><with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|\<theta\><rsub|f><rsup|-1>>,<space|2em>x<rsub|i>=<with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|\<theta\><rsub|c>>*<with|math-font|cal*|RD><rsub|L><with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|\<theta\><rsub|f><rsup|-1>>*<with|math-font|cal*|V><rsub|L>*e<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  On column <math|i>, the last three diagonal factors contribute
  <math|L<rsup|g<rsub|i>>*\<theta\><rsub|f><rsup|-\<ell\><rsub|i>>*L<rsup|d-g<rsub|i>>=L<rsup|d>*\<theta\><rsub|f><rsup|-\<ell\><rsub|i>>>.
  The remaining coarse scaling substitutes <math|\<theta\><rsub|c>> times the
  probe potential into its polynomial jet. Consequently, for every frame
  probe <math|p>,

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|probe><rsub|p><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>=L<rsup|d>*D<rsup|k<rsub|p>>*<around|(|H<rsub|i,\<theta\><rsub|f>>\<circ\>q<rsub|p>|)><around|(|0|)>.
  </equation*>

  This identity is an equality of the finite jet calculation: evaluate the
  polynomial transport on the probe, substitute the response's polynomial
  moments, and use the local section's exact coefficient recovery. The
  normalization on each side is <math|L<rsup|d>*\<theta\><rsub|f><rsup|-\<ell\><rsub|i>>>,
  with the same coarse threshold in the probe. Thus
  <eqref|eq:primary-probe-tail> identifies the linear prediction's probes
  with those of <math|x<rsub|i>>, to an error tending to zero. It does not
  replace the retained response by another continuum kernel.

  <subsubsection|From probes to the paid primary coefficient>

  Let <math|F<rsub|i>> be the actual complex principal insertion, already
  multiplied by <math|L<rsup|d>>, and let <math|b<rsub|i>> be its extracted
  coefficient vector. The nonwrapping coarse stencil allows coefficient
  recovery by the fixed finite probe family. Ordered derivative convergence
  in <eqref|eq:primary-flat-gaussian> implies probe convergence: each probe
  derivative is a finite combination of ordered derivatives along its fixed
  test-link curve. The coefficients are uniformly bounded by its finite local
  price. The last subsection and the triangle inequality therefore give

  <\equation*>
    max<rsub|p><around|\||<math-up|probe><rsub|p><around|(|b<rsub|i>-x<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  A finite separating family of linear probes controls the coefficient norm:
  on its unit sphere their maximum has a positive minimum by compactness.
  Equivalently use the finite recovery matrix from
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-sections>. Denote its bound by
  <math|T<rsub|<math-up|test>>>. Then <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsub|i>-x<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>T<rsub|<math-up|test>>*\<delta\><rsub|\<beta\>>>,
  where <math|\<delta\><rsub|\<beta\>>\<to\>0> is the maximum displayed probe
  error.

  The remaining regular rows have weighted sum tending to zero after <math|L>
  is fixed, by the nonprincipal estimate of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:leaf-expansion>. The extraction map on them
  costs the fixed read constant <math|T<rsub|<math-up|read>>>. If their
  summed majorant is <math|u<rsub|\<beta\>>>, the total extracted coefficient
  differs from <math|x<rsub|i>> by at most

  <\equation*>
    p<rsub|\<beta\>>=T<rsub|<math-up|test>>*\<delta\><rsub|\<beta\>>+T<rsub|<math-up|read>>*u<rsub|\<beta\>>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  The equality splitting the sum into its principal row and the other rows is
  justified by their absolute weighted summability.

  Move this coefficient error into the local-section remainder. The local
  splitting identity of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-splitting> makes
  this an exact equality of densities and of the ordinary and flagged
  profiles. Its additional cost is at most
  <math|C<rsub|<math-up|sec>>*p<rsub|\<beta\>>>, with
  <math|C<rsub|<math-up|sec>>> the fixed section and symmetry cost. Choose
  the probe error to spend half the desired allowance <math|L<rsup|-2/5>> and
  the nonprincipal error to spend the other half. The probe family and frame
  are finite, so one entrance threshold meets all these demands. This proves
  the small primary coefficient error for the prescribed transport
  <math|x<rsub|i>>, on the actual selected transition.

  For completeness, its principal remainder has the required graded bound as
  well. The local section for coordinate <math|i> has zero moments through
  degree <math|g<rsub|i>-1>. Apply the short reference estimate with that
  degree to its fixed stencil; it gives <math|C<rsub|i>*L<rsup|-g<rsub|i>>>
  times the fine mass. After <math|L> is fixed, choose the comparison error
  so that its reserved mass is at most <math|L<rsup|-g<rsub|i>>> times that
  mass. Apply this to the real and imaginary parts and multiply by
  <math|L<rsup|d>>. The resulting principal price is at most

  <\equation*>
    2*<around|(|C<rsub|i>+1|)>*L<rsup|d-g<rsub|i>>*m<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  Here the constant <math|C<rsub|i>> is purchased before the block factor
  using the weighted response derivatives through order <math|g<rsub|i>-1>.
  The projection calculation already proved bounds the remaining cost by
  fixed multiples of this price and <math|m<rsub|i>>. Since
  <math|L<rsup|d-g<rsub|i>>\<geqslant\>1> and the normalized local-section
  mass <math|m<rsub|i>> is bounded by the fixed frame credibility constant,
  choose the primary ledger constant <math|E<rsub|J>> larger than their
  finite sum over the frame. This gives the graded remainder budget
  <math|E<rsub|J>*L<rsup|d-g<rsub|i>>>. It is the ordinary primary budget
  used in the calibrated coefficient-field recursion, together with the small
  coefficient error just proved.

  <subsection|Polynomial transport and the invertible coefficient
  map><label|sec:heading-history-handoff>

  The primary comparison of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:primary-calibration-producer>
  uses a particular coefficient map. We prove the two-sided bound needed to
  iterate it, and then combine the primary, regular and forced insertions on
  one transition. The coefficient norm throughout is the sum of the absolute
  values of the coordinates.

  <subsubsection|Exact polynomial transport on the cover>

  Fix the positive blocking penalty <math|k\<gtr\>0>. Let <math|L> be the
  block side, <math|u=L<rsup|-1>>, and let <math|j,t\<geqslant\>4> be the
  adjacent fine and coarse kernel levels. Write
  <math|A<rsub|x><rsup|a*b><around|(|z|)>> for the real one-form transport
  row at the fine site <math|x>, with output direction <math|a>, input
  direction <math|b>, and coarse displacement <math|z>. The actual input site
  in this row is <math|<around|\<lfloor\>|x/L|\<rfloor\>>+z>; the floor here
  means the centered block quotient. The strip estimate gives constants
  <math|\<sigma\>,C\<gtr\>0>, selected before <math|L>, such that

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|z><around|\||A<rsub|0><rsup|a*b><around|(|z|)>|\|>*e<rsup|\<sigma\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>\<leqslant\>C*u.
  </equation*>

  The analogous rows at other residues are exponentially summable too. We
  first show that, for every vector-valued polynomial <math|p> of degree less
  than <math|j>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:heading-polynomial-row><around|(|A*p|)><around|(|x|)><rsub|a>=<big|sum><rsub|b><big|sum><rsub|z>A<rsub|0><rsup|a*b><around|(|z|)>*p<rsub|b>*<around|(|u*x+z|)>.
  </equation>

  This is an exact identity, including for <math|x> outside the central
  block.

  Here are the low-frequency details. Denote the analytic alias column of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:kernel-transport-identities> by
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|l><around|(|q|)>>, in its rescaled one-form
  convention. Fourier reconstruction and <math|x=L*<around|\<lfloor\>|x/L|\<rfloor\>>+<math-up|rem><around|(|x|)>>
  give

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|i*q\<cdot\><around|\<lfloor\>|x/L|\<rfloor\>>>*<wide|A|^><rsub|x><rsup|a*b><around|(|q|)>=u*<big|sum><rsub|l>e<rsup|i*<around|(|q+2*\<pi\>*l|)>\<cdot\>x/L>*\<cal-A\><rsub|l><around|(|q|)><rsub|a*b>.
  </equation*>

  For a nonzero alias <math|l>, both terms in the regular one-form formula
  have the factor

  <\equation*>
    z<rsub|l><around|(|q|)><rsup|j>,<space|2em>z<rsub|l><around|(|q|)>=<math-up|area><rsub|L><around|(|-<around|(|q+2*\<pi\>*l|)>/L|)>,<space|2em>z<rsub|l><around|(|0|)>=0.
  </equation*>

  Indeed the transposed filter contains the <math|j>th power of this area
  factor, and the nonprincipal gain contains the same factor. Removing it
  leaves the analytic port, rail and gain factors from the component
  estimates. Their denominators are nonzero at the origin. Thus
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|l>=z<rsub|l><rsup|j>*F<rsub|l>> with <math|F<rsub|l>>
  analytic there. Every derivative of order less than <math|j> of this term,
  even after multiplication by the displayed exponential, vanishes at zero:
  in the product of <math|j> vanishing factors some factor remains
  undifferentiated. The principal column has
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|0><around|(|0|)>=I>, obtained by the regular principal
  formula at zero. In particular

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:heading-row-mass><big|sum><rsub|z>A<rsub|0><rsup|a*b><around|(|z|)>=u*\<delta\><rsub|a*b>.
  </equation>

  Restrict <math|q> to <math|s*\<xi\>> for a real covector <math|\<xi\>>, and
  differentiate <math|r\<less\>j> times in <math|s>. Exponential summability
  justifies differentiation of the Fourier series: its differentiated terms
  are bounded by a polynomial in <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>> times the
  summable exponential. All nonprincipal aliases disappear. Comparing the
  resulting principal expression at <math|x> and at zero, after inserting
  <math|e<rsup|i*s*\<xi\>*<around|(|u*x|)>>> at zero, gives

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|z>A<rsub|x><rsup|a*b><around|(|z|)>*\<xi\>*<around|(|<around|\<lfloor\>|x/L|\<rfloor\>>+z|)><rsup|r>=<big|sum><rsub|z>A<rsub|0><rsup|a*b><around|(|z|)>*\<xi\>*<around|(|u*x+z|)><rsup|r>.
  </equation*>

  Polarization of the symmetric <math|r>-linear forms gives the same identity
  for products of <math|r> possibly different covectors. Such products span
  the homogeneous polynomials. Summing the homogeneous parts and the finitely
  many colour coordinates proves <eqref|eq:heading-polynomial-row>.

  Let <math|p> now have homogeneous degree <math|r\<less\>j>. Expanding
  <math|p*<around|(|u*x+z|)>> multilinearly, a term containing <math|s>
  copies of <math|x> has a factor <math|u<rsup|s>>. Its remaining <math|r-s>
  factors are bounded by a constant times
  <math|<around|(|1+<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>|)><rsup|r-s>>. Consequently its
  coefficient tensor has norm at most

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|r><around|\<\|\|\>|p|\<\|\|\>>*u<rsup|s+1>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>s\<leqslant\>r.
  </equation*>

  The constants are independent of <math|L>: use
  <math|sup<rsub|v\<geqslant\>0><around|(|1+v|)><rsup|r-s>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*v>\<less\>\<infty\>>
  and the row bound. Passing from diagonal polynomial values to symmetric
  tensor norms costs only a fixed finite-dimensional constant. This follows
  by compactness of the tensor unit sphere and injectivity of the diagonal
  map, or directly by polarization. The degree-<math|r> term is exactly
  <math|u<rsup|r+1>*p<around|(|x|)>> by <eqref|eq:heading-row-mass>. Adding
  homogeneous parts proves that, if <math|deg p\<less\>a\<leqslant\>j>, then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:heading-polynomial-leading>deg
    <around|(|A*p|)>\<less\>a,<space|2em>deg
    <around|(|A*p-u<rsup|a>*p|)>\<less\>a-1,
  </equation>

  and the degree-<math|s> output tensor is bounded by
  <math|C<rsub|a>*u<rsup|s+1>> times the sum of the input tensor norms.
  Lower-degree input terms do not affect the asserted highest-degree
  identity.

  <subsubsection|The two gradings of the primary matrix>

  Let <math|e<rsub|i>> be the canonical coordinate basis of the
  degree-<math|d> frame, with <math|e<rsub|0>> the unit, and write
  <math|<around|(|g<rsub|i>,\<ell\><rsub|i>|)>> for spatial grade and
  field-amplitude grade. Both lie between zero and <math|d>. Let <math|R> be
  the unscaled polynomial coefficient transport used in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:primary-calibration-producer>. We claim

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|R<rsub|b*i>>|<cell|=u<rsup|g<rsub|i>>*\<delta\><rsub|b*i>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|g<rsub|b>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|i>|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:heading-triangular-receipt>>>|<row|<cell|R<rsub|b*i>>|<cell|=0>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|b>\<less\>\<ell\><rsub|i>|)>,<space|2em><around|\||R<rsub|b*i>|\|>\<leqslant\>D*u<rsup|g<rsub|i>>.<eq-number><label|eq:heading-receipt-price>>>>>
  </align>

  Here <math|D\<geqslant\>1> is chosen before <math|L>, for the fixed frame
  and kernel levels, and we assume <math|d\<leqslant\>j>.

  To verify the first identity, differentiate the local holonomy display in
  directions of engineering weights <math|w<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,w<rsub|n>>. A
  block of a chain-rule partition has weight <math|a=<big|sum><rsub|r<text|
  in block>>w<rsub|r>>. Its unsmoothed potential is polynomial of degree less
  than <math|a>. By <eqref|eq:heading-polynomial-leading>, the smoothed
  potential is <math|u<rsup|a>> times that potential plus a polynomial of
  degree less than <math|a-1>. Expand the outer multilinear derivative of the
  <math|i>th section in these sums. If the total weight is at most
  <math|g<rsub|i>>, every term using one lower-degree correction has weight
  strictly below <math|g<rsub|i>> and vanishes by the primary section's
  lower-grade cancellation: its multilinear derivative is zero on polynomial
  tuples of total engineering weight less than <math|g<rsub|i>>. Indeed its
  local-section tests read the homogeneous label <math|e<rsub|i>>, so all
  smaller spatial grades vanish (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-sections>);
  the filtered gauge-coordinate argument of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:polynomial-moment-attenuation> then gives
  exactly this lower-weight assertion. No vanishing of its
  grade-<math|g<rsub|i>> test is claimed. The remaining term has factor
  <math|u<rsup|<big|sum><rsub|r>w<rsub|r>>>. The unsmoothed section reads
  exactly <math|e<rsub|i>>, by the defining local-section identity.
  Extracting its homogeneous part of output bidegree
  <math|<around|(|g<rsub|b>,\<ell\><rsub|b>|)>> proves
  <eqref|eq:heading-triangular-receipt>. This also covers order zero.

  For the second identity, the section has ordinary derivatives zero through
  order <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>-1> at the flat field. In a derivative of total
  order less than <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>> of its composition, the outer
  derivative has no more arguments than that total order. It therefore
  vanishes. This proves the amplitude-grade restriction, regardless of
  spatial grade.

  For the uniform bound, use the homogeneous decomposition following
  <eqref|eq:heading-polynomial-leading> in every inner block of the chain
  rule. A term of polynomial degree <math|s> in that block has weight
  <math|s+1> and coefficient norm at most <math|C*u<rsup|s+1>>. Expand the
  outer multilinear derivative. Terms whose weights sum to less than
  <math|g<rsub|i>> vanish; every other term has a factor at most
  <math|u<rsup|g<rsub|i>>>, since <math|0\<less\>u\<leqslant\>1>. The fixed
  stencil, bounded derivative order and finite partition set give a constant
  for each polynomial test. A finite family of these tests controls the
  coefficient norm, as proved in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-splitting>.
  Taking the maximum over the finitely many columns and tests proves the last
  inequality in <eqref|eq:heading-receipt-price>.

  Let <math|f,c\<in\><around|(|0,1|]>> be the fine and coarse small-field
  thresholds, with <math|c/f\<leqslant\>\<pi\>> and
  <math|f/c\<leqslant\>\<pi\>>, where <math|\<pi\>\<geqslant\>1>. The actual
  coefficient map <math|B> has entries

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|b*i>=c<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|b>>*f<rsup|-\<ell\><rsub|i>>*L<rsup|g<rsub|i>>*R<rsub|b*i>.
  </equation*>

  If <math|\<ell\><rsub|b>\<geqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|i>>, then
  <math|c<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|b>>/f<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|i>>\<leqslant\><around|(|c/f|)><rsup|\<ell\><rsub|i>>\<leqslant\>\<pi\><rsup|d>>;
  otherwise the entry is zero. Set <math|a=\<pi\><rsup|d>*D>. We have

  <\equation*>
    a\<geqslant\>1,<space|2em><around|\||B<rsub|b*i>|\|>\<leqslant\>a,<space|2em><around|\||B<rsub|i*i>|\|>=<around|(|c/f|)><rsup|\<ell\><rsub|i>>\<geqslant\>a<rsup|-1>,
  </equation*>

  and an off-diagonal nonzero entry requires
  <math|g<rsub|i>\<less\>g<rsub|b>>. The unit is fixed. The actual polynomial
  transport commutes with signed coordinate motions and conjugation: change
  variables in its absolutely convergent real kernel sum, use the covariance
  of the local section, and then take its finite jet. The three diagonal
  scalings in <math|B> preserve those actions because the bidegrees do.

  <subsubsection|A bound for the inverse without a perturbative assumption>

  Let <math|n> be the number of coordinates and put
  <math|K=a+n*a<rsup|2>\<geqslant\>1>. For <math|y=B*v>, induction on the
  grade proves

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||v<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>K<rsup|r><around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>*<space|1em><around|(|g<rsub|i>\<less\>r|)>.
  </equation*>

  The claim is empty for <math|r=0>. If <math|g<rsub|i>\<less\>r+1>, the
  other nonzero entries in row <math|i> come from grades less than
  <math|g<rsub|i>>, hence less than <math|r>. Isolating the diagonal entry
  and applying the induction hypothesis gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||v<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>a<around*|(|<around|\||y<rsub|i>*<around|\||+<big|sum><rsub|b\<ne\>i>|\|>*B<rsub|i*b>*v<rsub|b>|\|>|)>\<leqslant\>a*<around|\<\|\|\>|y*<around|\<\|\|\>|<rsub|1>+n*a<rsup|2>*K<rsup|r><around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>K<rsup|r+1>|\<\|\|\>>*y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  Summing with <math|r=d+1>, and bounding the forward map row by row, yields

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:heading-both-prices><around|\<\|\|\>|B*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|B><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|B>*<around|\<\|\|\>|B*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,<space|2em>C<rsub|B>=n*<around|(|a+n*a<rsup|2>|)><rsup|d+1>.
  </equation>

  Thus <math|B> is injective and, in this finite-dimensional space,
  bijective. Substituting <math|v=B<rsup|-1>*w> gives the same bound for its
  inverse. The unit column and grade-zero row show also that <math|B>
  commutes with deletion of the unit coordinate, denoted by
  <math|<math-up|core>>. In particular this argument requires no smallness of
  the off-diagonal entries.

  <subsection|Three elementary insertions on one history
  transition><label|sec:three-port-history>

  Fix one funded interpolating transition, its point on the interpolation
  interval, and its fine and coarse representations
  <math|R<rsub|f>,R<rsub|c>>. Retain the actual local slots and their support
  and inventory identifications. The marked derivative fixes one map
  <math|\<cal-D\>> from real elementary insertions to coarse forced arrays,
  as in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:projection-payment-producer>. The
  equalities identifying <math|R<rsub|f>,R<rsub|c>> with adjacent entries of
  the chosen history are part of these data. None of the selections below
  changes that transition or its input and output representations.

  Write <math|\<cal-T\>> for its density transfer, <math|v=L<rsup|d>> for the
  physical factor, <math|\<eta\>=L<rsup|-2/5>> for its small error allowance,
  and <math|\<kappa\><rsub|f>,\<kappa\><rsub|c>> for the square roots of the
  fine and coarse forced allowances. They satisfy
  <math|0\<less\>\<kappa\><rsub|f>,\<kappa\><rsub|c>\<leqslant\>1>. For a
  support <math|X> and majorant <math|h\<geqslant\>0>, its fine mass is

  <\equation*>
    m<rsub|f>*<around|(|X,h|)>=e<rsup|b<rsub|f>*C<rsub|B<rsub|f>><around|(|X|)>>*h.
  </equation*>

  The frame's finite motion price is <math|C<rsub|<math-up|orb>>\<geqslant\>1>.
  Let <math|C> be the selected coefficient allowance, at least the price
  <math|C<rsub|B>> in <eqref|eq:heading-both-prices> and the graded remainder
  price in the primary estimate. All these choices precede the final coupling
  threshold.

  <subsubsection|Keeping the three exact identities>

  For an inserted density <math|F>, the local splitting constructs a
  coefficient field <math|Q> and a response <math|\<psi\>> such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:history-three-identities>

    <\eqsplit>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-up|dens><around|(|Q|)>>|<cell|=v*\<cal-T\>*F,>>|<row|<cell|\<alpha\>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|r>z<rsub|r><math-up|ord><around|(|M<rsub|r>|)>,<space|2em>\<omega\>=<big|sum><rsub|r>z<rsub|r><math-up|flag><around|(|M<rsub|r>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-P\><rsub|c><around|(|Q<rsub|<math-up|forced>>|)>-<math-up|hook><around|(|\<psi\>|)>>|<cell|=v*<big|sum><rsub|r>z<rsub|r>*\<cal-P\><rsub|c>*<around|(|\<cal-D\>*M<rsub|r>|)>.>>>>
    </eqsplit>
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<alpha\>> is the input exponential entry, <math|\<omega\>> its
  input forced profile, <math|M<rsub|r>> are real elementary insertions, and
  <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|c>> groups output coefficients with the same support
  and inventory. The ordinary and flagged entries are separate functions; the
  density combines them with the background density and gas. The first
  identity follows from the actual integral expansion and the exact
  subtraction identity of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-splitting>.
  The last is the retained singleton identity
  <eqref|eq:projection-profile-retained>. The middle identities simply
  specify the input decomposition; they are not inferred from the first.

  For a complex elementary insertion use its real and imaginary parts with
  the same majorant, and coefficients <math|1,i>. Their input cost is at most
  twice their common mass. The response <math|\<psi\>> is supported on sets
  containing the origin, is local, gauge invariant and measurable, and
  vanishes on healthy configurations of its support. If its reserved mass
  bound is <math|a<rsub|0>>, the local splitting supplies remainder budgets
  <math|a<rsub|1>,a<rsub|2>\<geqslant\>0> with

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|rem><rsub|\<kappa\><rsub|c>><around|(|Q|)>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|1>+a<rsub|2>,<space|2em>C<rsub|<math-up|turn>>*a<rsub|1>+a<rsub|2>\<leqslant\>r,<space|2em>a<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|1>\<leqslant\>r,
  </equation*>

  where <math|C<rsub|<math-up|turn>>\<geqslant\>1>. More precisely the first
  two budgets retain the separate ordinary and forced estimates, so the same
  bound <math|r> holds after the allowed finite motions. These assertions are
  the summation and support-join bounds already proved in the local
  splitting; the response is the sum of its rebates. Absolute majorants
  justify both its grouping by support and the profile identity in
  <eqref|eq:history-three-identities>.

  <subsubsection|The three choices and their prices>

  For the primary insertion with coordinate <math|e<rsub|i>>,
  <math|i\<ne\>0>, take its local section on the canonical stencil. Its
  exponential entry is its masked section, its input forced profile is zero,
  and its density is that entry times the fine background density. The
  small-error and graded remainder estimates of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:primary-calibration-producer>, followed by
  the same local splitting, give

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|<math-up|orb>>*<around|\<\|\|\>|Q.*x-B*L<rsup|d-g>*e<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<eta\><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>e<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,<space|2em>r=C<around|\<\|\|\>|L<rsup|d-g><math-up|core><space|0.17em>e<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  The diagonal notation <math|L<rsup|d-g>> acts coordinatewise. Let
  <math|\<tau\><rsub|f>> be the common fine local-section derivative bound.
  Its stencil is contained in one <math|B<rsub|f>>-covering set once the
  selected geometric floor is met. Thus its input mass is at most
  <math|e<rsup|b<rsub|f>>*\<tau\><rsub|f>>. Both real components therefore
  fit the common input allowance

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:history-common-waybill>t<rsub|f>=2*e<rsup|b<rsub|f>>*\<tau\><rsub|f>.
  </equation>

  This allowance is independent of the coordinate.

  For a regular insertion <math|f> on <math|X\<ni\>0>, assume the local
  regularity, gauge invariance and vanishing holonomy tests through degree
  <math|d>, with the prescribed derivative and spatial-weight bound <math|h>.
  Its input exponential entry is <math|\<chi\><rsub|X>*f>, its forced profile
  is zero, and its density is <math|\<rho\><rsub|f>*\<chi\><rsub|X>*f>. Put
  <math|m=m<rsub|f>*<around|(|X,h|)>>. Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:regular-principal-producer>
  pays its principal term; the nonprincipal and forced payments are those of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:projection-payment-producer>. The split
  field therefore satisfies

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|<math-up|orb>>*<around|\<\|\|\>|Q.*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>*m,<space|2em>r=\<eta\>*m,<space|2em><text|input
    allowance >2*m.
  </equation*>

  All three equalities in <eqref|eq:history-three-identities> use this same
  masked insertion.

  For an allowed forced slot <math|s>, let <math|f\<ne\>0> be its local,
  gauge-invariant measurable insertion, with the slot's support and
  eligibility conditions, <math|<around|\||f|\|>\<leqslant\>h>, and origin in
  its support. Put <math|m=m<rsub|f>*<around|(|X<rsub|s>,h|)>>. Its
  exponential entry is zero. Its input profile equals <math|f> at the
  original support\Uinventory address and zero at every other address. It has
  no ordinary principal cluster: the marked slot is flagged, whereas that
  cluster requires an ordinary insertion. The nonprincipal and forced prices
  thus give the zero-leading-coefficient bound without a principal bill. The
  fine forced norm charges this insertion by <math|m/\<kappa\><rsub|f>>.
  Since <math|0\<less\>\<kappa\><rsub|f>\<leqslant\>1>, enlarging the already
  paid bound <math|\<eta\>*m> gives the required form

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:history-forced-price>C<rsub|<math-up|orb>>*<around|\<\|\|\>|Q.*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>*m/\<kappa\><rsub|f>,<space|2em>r=\<eta\>*m/\<kappa\><rsub|f>,<space|2em><text|input
    allowance >2*m.
  </equation>

  The input allowance remains <math|2*m>; it is not multiplied by
  <math|\<kappa\><rsub|f><rsup|-1>>. The output remainder uses
  <math|\<kappa\><rsub|c>> through the local splitting. The two square roots
  serve different roles. For <math|f=0> take zero fields and zero response,
  so no support claim for a nonzero insertion is needed.

  <subsubsection|One finite set of insertion types for every grade>

  On the fixed finite fine torus, form a set containing the ordinary slot
  selected for every support containing the origin. Adjoin the forced slots
  selected by the retained slot realization for all support\Uinventory tags.
  Both indexing sets are finite, so their union is finite even if different
  indices select the same slot. This set contains all three input types
  above. It depends on the representation and its slot realization, not on
  the coefficient vector later to be transported.

  The map <math|\<cal-D\>> was fixed before choosing the degree <math|d>. For
  each <math|d> with <math|d+3> at most the coarse kernel level, apply the
  three estimates and <eqref|eq:heading-both-prices> with its own frame. The
  resulting heading is chosen for that frame and may depend on <math|d>; the
  transition and its marked derivative map remain common to all the grades.
  Its common allowance is <eqref|eq:history-common-waybill>, using the
  stage's local-section price, which bounds all those grades. The exact
  history identifications replace the interpolating input and output by
  <math|R<rsub|N,m+1>> and <math|R<rsub|N,m>> in every equality. This is
  substitution in fixed maps; it selects no new transition. The transition
  remains sound because its unmarked density identity, singleton derivative
  and motion identities were established for this interpolating point.

  Finally the common scale payments give, with <math|s<rsub|f>> the fine
  column allowance and <math|K<rsub|f>> the next coefficient budget,

  <\equation*>
    \<eta\>*<around|(|1+s<rsub|f>|)>*C<rsub|<math-up|orb>>\<leqslant\>L<rsup|-3/8>,<space|2em><around|(|C+\<eta\>*s<rsub|f>|)>*C<rsub|<math-up|orb>>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|f>.
  </equation*>

  These are exactly the two purchase inequalities in
  <eqref|eq:ex-interpolation-buy> of Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-selected-linear-interpolation>,
  with <math|R<rsub|F>=C<rsub|<math-up|orb>>>, <math|s=s<rsub|f>> and
  <math|<math-up|barunit><around|(|L|)>=L<rsup|-3/8>>; the remaining two
  clauses there hold because <math|C\<geqslant\>C<rsub|B>\<geqslant\>1> and
  <math|\<eta\>=L<rsup|-2/5>\<geqslant\>0>.
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-selected-interpolation> then combines
  all columns into one linear coefficient family once all of its hypotheses
  hold. We list them and say which are supplied here.

  <em|Supplied by this section>, on the retained interpolating transition and
  for each grade <math|d> as above:

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(a)>the heading. By the definition of the grade weight,
    <math|<math-up|vowel><rsub|L>> multiplies the coordinate <math|e<rsub|i>>
    by <math|L<rsup|d-g<rsub|i>>>, so the primary comparison above has the
    form <math|T<around|(|L|)>=B\<circ\><math-up|vowel><rsub|L>>. The map
    <math|B> commutes with core deletion, and by
    <eqref|eq:heading-both-prices> both <math|B> and <math|B<rsup|-1>> have
    norm at most <math|C<rsub|B>>. Since <math|C\<geqslant\>C<rsub|B>>,
    <math|C<rsub|<math-up|orb>>\<geqslant\>1> and
    <math|\<eta\>*s<rsub|f>\<geqslant\>0>, the second purchase inequality
    gives <math|C<rsub|B>\<leqslant\>C\<leqslant\><around|(|C+\<eta\>*s<rsub|f>|)>*C<rsub|<math-up|orb>>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|f>>,
    which is the norm bound required there with <math|D<rsub|0>=K<rsub|f>>;

    <item*|(b)>the purchase inequalities <eqref|eq:ex-interpolation-buy>, as
    just shown, and <math|t=t<rsub|f>\<geqslant\>0> from
    <eqref|eq:history-common-waybill>;

    <item*|(c)>the common gauge door: the single transition and its marked
    derivative map <math|\<cal-D\>>, fixed before <math|d>, together with its
    soundness;

    <item*|(d)>the component coupons. For every primary, regular and forced
    insertion, the three exact identities <eqref|eq:history-three-identities>
    hold on that one door, and the lead-error, remainder and input prices are
    those of the three choices above;

    <item*|(e)>the common funded wing. It is the finite set of insertion
    types constructed at the start of this subsection. It contains every
    forced slot of the retained slot realization and is chosen before the
    coefficient vector, so the same wing serves every column. Its motion
    images are the wings of the reoriented coupons formed in the proof of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-selected-interpolation>.
  </enumerate>

  <em|Not supplied by this section>; these remain inputs to that finite
  assembly:

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(i)>all six admissible identities (H1)\U(H6) of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-selected-linear-interpolation> for the
    incoming fine coefficient family. These are the <math|x>-coordinate
    identity; linearity and motion covariance of the density; the charge cone
    and its subadditivity; the intertwining of motion and conjugation by the
    readout; complex-linearity of the coefficient, regular and forced fields
    with coefficient-independent supports; and preservation of the unit;

    <item*|(ii)>the three profile hypotheses stated before
    <eqref|eq:ex-interpolation-buy>: the coefficient law is sound under local
    reorientation, the forced profiles transform covariantly, and the local
    evaluation is nonzero on every configuration;

    <item*|(iii)>the passage from component to basis Coupons, using
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-coupon-merger> with the summable
    lead-price and dominated-density receipts stated before the proposition;

    <item*|(iv)>the bank and room bounds of the proposition. On the selected
    run, they are to be taken from the stage purchases of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-stored-service-budget>.
  </enumerate>

  The first two items concern the incoming coefficient family, not the
  transition, and nothing in this section constructs that coefficient family.
  For the public application, Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-terminal-voice>
  constructs the terminal admissible coefficient family and its profile
  identities; Propositions<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-reprise>
  and<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-music> propagate them through the stored
  doors under the common purchases.

  At a fixed stage there are only finitely many permitted earlier stages and
  grades. Taking the maximum of their coupling thresholds supplies all the
  estimates of this section simultaneously, and any subsequent increase of
  those thresholds preserves them.

  This proves the three elementary estimates, the inverse-map bound, and
  their common-history use relative to the retained funded interpolation, its
  slot realization and the common scale payments. The nonlinear interpolation
  data are constructed in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:path-attachment-producer>
  under its stated stationary-plan hypotheses; the common funded-history
  construction retains those data on the selected doors. The elementary card
  calculation here does not itself produce that path.

  <subsection|Stability in the common support
  dictionary><label|sec:local-source-stability>

  The fixed-scale contraction argument needs convergence of a one-step map on
  each fixed input. Its output is a countable list of local terms. Pointwise
  convergence of the terms and boundedness of their total price would not be
  enough: price could escape to later addresses. The needed extra property is
  uniform smallness of the tails. We prove the summation and
  local-subtraction argument with this property explicit. The supports,
  thresholds and address encodings are fixed throughout.

  <subsubsection|Uniform tails and products>

  For nonnegative numbers <math|a<rsub|N,i>>, indexed by a countable set
  <math|I>, say that their tails are uniformly small if

  <\equation*>
    sup<rsub|N> <big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>a<rsub|N,i>\<less\>\<infty\>,<space|2em>\<forall\>\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0\<exists\>I<rsub|0>\<subset\>I<text|
    finite>:<space|1em>sup<rsub|N> <big|sum><rsub|i\<nin\>I<rsub|0>>a<rsub|N,i>\<less\>\<epsilon\>.
  </equation*>

  The definition is unchanged if the tail sums are replaced by sums over
  arbitrary finite subsets of <math|I\<setminus\>I<rsub|0>>. Indeed all
  summands are nonnegative. Domination, addition and multiplication by a
  fixed nonnegative constant preserve this property. Extension by zero
  through a fixed injection preserves it as well.

  It also passes to finite-subset products. If
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>a<rsub|N,i>\<leqslant\>C>, set
  <math|A<rsub|N,S>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S>a<rsub|N,i>> for finite subsets
  <math|S\<subset\>I>, including the empty subset. For every finite
  <math|J\<subset\>I>,

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|S\<subset\>J>A<rsub|N,S>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>J><around|(|1+a<rsub|N,i>|)>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>J>a<rsub|N,i>|)>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|C>.
  </equation*>

  This bounds the entire sum by monotone passage through finite <math|J>. If
  <math|S> is not contained in <math|I<rsub|0>>, choose one of its elements
  outside <math|I<rsub|0>> and overcount by all such choices. Removing the
  chosen element gives

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|S\<nsubset\>I<rsub|0>>A<rsub|N,S>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i\<nin\>I<rsub|0>>a<rsub|N,i>*<big|sum><rsub|T\<subset\>I\<setminus\><around|{|i|}>>A<rsub|N,T>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|C>*<big|sum><rsub|i\<nin\>I<rsub|0>>a<rsub|N,i>.
  </equation*>

  Thus the finite collection of subsets of <math|I<rsub|0>> captures all but
  an arbitrarily small price. Compatibility restrictions only remove
  nonnegative terms and preserve the conclusion. This is the tail estimate
  for the finite-subset products in the gas.

  There is a corresponding assertion for connected clusters, where indices
  may repeat. Suppose the elementary prices <math|a<rsub|N,i>> have uniformly
  small tails and obey the rooted cluster criterion with one fixed margin
  <math|\<rho\>\<less\>1>. The rooted tree bound controls the total price of
  clusters of length at least <math|k+1> by

  <\equation*>
    V*\<gamma\>*<frac|\<rho\><rsup|k>|1-\<rho\>>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|V> is the number of sites. Every cluster has a nonempty
  support, so summing the rooted bounds over sites bounds the unrooted sum.
  Choose <math|k> making this bound small. At each of the finitely many
  remaining lengths <math|r\<leqslant\>k>, the absolute connected-graph
  coefficient divided by <math|r>! has a finite maximum: there are finitely
  many graphs on <math|r> labeled vertices. The remaining price is bounded by
  a constant times a product of <math|r> elementary prices. The total price
  of ordered tuples with some entry outside <math|I<rsub|0>> is at most

  <\equation*>
    r*C<rsup|r-1>*<big|sum><rsub|i\<nin\>I<rsub|0>>a<rsub|N,i>,<space|2em>C=sup<rsub|N>
    <big|sum><rsub|i>a<rsub|N,i>.
  </equation*>

  Choose <math|I<rsub|0>> to make these finitely many bounds small. The
  finite set of words of length at most <math|k> in <math|I<rsub|0>> then
  captures all but an arbitrarily small cluster price. This proves uniform
  tails for the connected expansion.

  For marked clusters the margin is obtained as follows. Suppose the unmarked
  criterion has margin <math|1/2> and rooted allowance
  <math|\<gamma\>\<gtr\>0>, and the marked elementary prices
  <math|v<rsub|N,i>> have uniformly small tails and total bound <math|B>. Let
  <math|M> bound the support weight in the rooted criterion on this finite
  torus. Take

  <\equation*>
    \<delta\>=<frac|\<gamma\>|2*<around|(|1+M*B|)>>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation*>

  The additional rooted price of <math|\<delta\>*v> is at most
  <math|\<delta\>*M*B\<leqslant\>\<gamma\>/2>. Thus <math|a+\<delta\>*v>
  satisfies the criterion with allowance <math|3*\<gamma\>/2>. The degree
  price is linear in this rooted allowance: its original bound is
  <math|<math-up|degreePrice><around|(|\<gamma\>|)>\<leqslant\>1/2>, and
  hence <math|<math-up|degreePrice><around|(|3*\<gamma\>/2|)>=<around|(|3/2|)><math-up|degreePrice><around|(|\<gamma\>|)>\<leqslant\>3/4>.
  For each word, expansion of the product gives

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|r>v<rsub|N,i<rsub|j>>*<big|prod><rsub|h\<ne\>j>a<rsub|N,i<rsub|h>>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsup|-1>*<big|prod><rsub|j=1><rsup|r><around|(|a<rsub|N,i<rsub|j>>+\<delta\>*v<rsub|N,i<rsub|j>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The right side includes all terms with exactly one marked factor and has
  nonnegative remaining terms. At derivative order <math|j>, the product rule
  costs at most <math|<around|(|r+1|)><rsup|j>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|j*r>>;
  absorb <math|2<rsup|j>> into each elementary price before this comparison.
  The paid derivative criterion supplies the enlarged unmarked prices. Use
  the total bound for the enlarged marked prices when choosing <math|B> and
  then <math|\<delta\>> above; thus the choice may depend on the fixed order
  <math|j>. An unmarked price may be replaced by the smaller of its
  uniform-tail majorant and its rooted-criterion majorant, preserving both
  bounds. The cluster argument just proved then gives uniform tails for the
  differentiated logarithm, not only a bound on its total price.

  Let <math|u<rsub|N,i>,v<rsub|N,i>> take values in a Banach space, and
  suppose their norms have uniformly small tails. If
  <math|u<rsub|N,i>-v<rsub|N,i>\<to\>0> for every fixed <math|i>, then

  <\equation>
    <big|sum><rsub|i>u<rsub|N,i>-<big|sum><rsub|i>v<rsub|N,i>\<longrightarrow\>0.<label|eq:source-tight-sum>
  </equation>

  To prove it, choose a common finite <math|I<rsub|0>> making each norm tail
  less than <math|\<epsilon\>/4>. The difference of the two sums over
  <math|I<rsub|0>> tends to zero, and is eventually less than
  <math|\<epsilon\>/2>. The triangle inequality proves the assertion.
  Absolute convergence justifies the subtraction. The same proof applies to
  supremum norms of bounded functions and to norms of their fixed-order
  derivatives.

  One useful form of uniform convergence does not require a compact
  configuration space. If for every sequence of configurations
  <math|U<rsub|N>> one has <math|f<rsub|N><around|(|U<rsub|N>|)>-g<rsub|N><around|(|U<rsub|N>|)>\<to\>0>,
  then

  <\equation*>
    sup<rsub|U><around|\||f<rsub|N><around|(|U|)>-g<rsub|N><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  Otherwise some subsequence would have supremum at least <math|\<epsilon\>>;
  choose on it points with difference greater than <math|\<epsilon\>/2>, and
  complete them to a sequence arbitrarily. This contradicts the hypothesis.
  The same selection argument applies to derivative evaluations along
  normalized local charts. It is this uniform form, rather than pointwise
  convergence at each fixed configuration, that is needed for moving field
  insertions.

  <subsubsection|From entrywise convergence to a small remainder price>

  At the fixed scale let <math|X<rsub|i>> be the regular supports and
  <math|Y<rsub|i>> the forced supports. Write <math|M<around|(|X|)>*t> for
  the support price of an amplitude <math|t\<geqslant\>0>. Since the torus is
  finite, it has finitely many supports, and

  <\equation*>
    M<rsub|\<ast\>>\<assign\>max<rsub|X> M<around|(|X|)>\<less\>\<infty\>.
  </equation*>

  The price is linear in the amplitude. Suppose two regular lists
  <math|f<rsub|N,i>,g<rsub|N,i>> have derivative majorants with uniformly
  small tails, and for every fixed <math|i> the difference has arbitrarily
  small derivative majorant for sufficiently large <math|N>. For any
  <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, choose <math|I<rsub|0>> so that the sum of the
  two tail majorants is below <math|\<epsilon\>/<around|(|2*M<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>>.
  On <math|I<rsub|0>> use the common eventual majorant

  <\equation*>
    <frac|\<epsilon\>|2*M<rsub|\<ast\>>*<around|(|<around|\||I<rsub|0>|\|>+1|)>>.
  </equation*>

  Outside <math|I<rsub|0>> use the sum of the original two majorants. The
  resulting majorants bound every difference, and their total support price
  is at most <math|\<epsilon\>>. No supremum over infinitely many convergence
  thresholds has been taken.

  For forced lists use the identical argument with uniform value bounds. If
  their contribution to the remainder norm is divided by
  <math|\<kappa\>\<gtr\>0>, make their total unnormalized support price at
  most <math|\<kappa\>*\<epsilon\>/2>, and the regular price at most
  <math|\<epsilon\>/2>. Their sum has remainder seminorm at most
  <math|\<epsilon\>>. This proves smallness in the full remainder space of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:fixed-rung-convergence>. In particular it
  does not identify arrays merely because they have the same gas evaluation.

  <subsubsection|Local subtraction preserves this convergence>

  Let <math|f<rsub|N,i>> be a regular local term on <math|X<rsub|i>>. Its
  subtraction uses the fixed linear jet projection <math|\<pi\><rsub|i>>, the
  fixed local section <math|\<sigma\><rsub|i>>, and the enlarged support
  <math|<wide|X|^><rsub|i>>:

  <\equation*>
    p<rsub|N,i>=\<pi\><rsub|i>*f<rsub|N,i>,<space|2em>r<rsub|N,i>=f<rsub|N,i>-\<sigma\><rsub|i>*p<rsub|N,i>.
  </equation*>

  The mask identity leaves the forced rebate

  <\equation*>
    b<rsub|N,i>=\<chi\><rsub|X<rsub|i>>*f<rsub|N,i>-\<sigma\><rsub|i>*p<rsub|N,i>-\<chi\><rsub|<wide|X|^><rsub|i>>*r<rsub|N,i>.
  </equation*>

  At this fixed scale the already established projection and local section
  estimates bound each of these three linear maps by a fixed constant times
  its input derivative majorant. Subtracting two inputs gives exactly the
  same maps of their difference:

  <\equation*>
    \<Delta\>*p<rsub|i>=\<pi\><rsub|i>*\<Delta\>*f<rsub|i>,<space|1em>\<Delta\>*r<rsub|i>=\<Delta\>*f<rsub|i>-\<sigma\><rsub|i>*\<pi\><rsub|i>*\<Delta\>*f<rsub|i>,<space|1em>\<Delta\>*b<rsub|i>=\<chi\><rsub|X<rsub|i>>*\<Delta\>*f<rsub|i>-\<sigma\><rsub|i>*\<pi\><rsub|i>*\<Delta\>*f<rsub|i>-\<chi\><rsub|<wide|X|^><rsub|i>>*\<Delta\>*r<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  Thus the projected coefficients converge by <eqref|eq:source-tight-sum>,
  and the residual and rebate lists retain the required small-tail bounds and
  entrywise convergence. Masks may be discontinuous at the threshold. They
  are identical on the two sides and have modulus at most one, so they do not
  create a continuity assumption at that threshold.

  The forced correction also contains products with the background
  activities. On a fixed support\Uinventory profile it is a sum of terms

  <\equation*>
    b<rsub|N,i><around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>1<rsub|\<cal-E\>*<around|(|i,S,U|)>>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>S>K<rsub|N,j><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<cal-E\>> is the fixed compatibility and flag condition. For
  two backgrounds its indicator is the same: the address dictionary,
  supports, inventory and threshold are the same. For a fixed finite
  <math|S>, the identity

  <\equation*>
    <big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>S>u<rsub|j>-<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>S>v<rsub|j>=<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>S><around|(|u<rsub|j>-v<rsub|j>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|k\<less\>j>u<rsub|k>*<big|prod><rsub|k\<gtr\>j>v<rsub|k>
  </equation*>

  in any chosen ordering shows convergence of the product when each activity
  converges uniformly and is uniformly bounded. The product-tail estimate
  above and <eqref|eq:source-tight-sum> justify summing over <math|S>.
  Multiplying by the convergent rebate, summing the finitely many profiles,
  and using the fixed extension of each profile to its address therefore
  preserves convergence. The same argument applies to the raw forced output
  before the rebate is added.

  Consequently local subtraction, barcode grouping, addition of the
  background hook, and placement in the common dictionary preserve
  convergence of coefficients and smallness of the full remainder difference.
  These are exact operations on the common representation. Changing its
  forced dictionary midway would invalidate the product and mask comparisons.

  <subsubsection|Summing the fixed input columns>

  For one fixed input remainder, its regular and forced prices are summable.
  The selected one-step expansion has one primary column and one column for
  each regular and forced input entry. The primary column has a single fixed
  bound; the other columns have bounds proportional to these summable input
  prices, with the forced price divided by its fine-scale <math|\<kappa\>>.
  Thus there is a summable sequence <math|w<rsub|i>> bounding, uniformly in
  terminal cutoff, both the coefficient norm and remainder price of output
  column <math|i>.

  Suppose corresponding individual output columns have the same regular
  supports, convergent coefficient differences, and remainder differences
  tending to zero in the preceding sense. Choose a finite set of columns
  outside which <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>2*w<rsub|i>> is less than
  <math|\<epsilon\>/2>. On that finite set take a common convergence
  threshold and sum the small coefficient and remainder prices. Outside it
  use <math|2*w<rsub|i>>. This proves convergence after all columns have been
  merged. The unit column is constant and requires no limiting argument. For
  the nonunit primary column the fixed input function is its local-section
  basis element. For regular columns it is the fixed masked regular input;
  for forced columns it is the fixed profile of the input array. Hence each
  column comparison uses the same inserted function on the two histories,
  although its background changes.

  Signed motions and complex conjugation are bounded linear or real-linear
  maps on these data, with the fixed motion prices already proved. The finite
  motion average therefore preserves the convergence. Applying the fixed
  coefficient pencil and the common profile capture gives convergence of the
  actual one-step coefficient map and its recorded remainder on every fixed
  coefficient vector. The coefficient space is finite dimensional; comparison
  on its finite basis, followed by the triangle inequality, gives
  operator-norm convergence. This is the local stability input used by the
  fixed-scale graph argument.

  To apply this result to the selected histories one still needs the
  individual marked-column comparisons, with uniformly small tails, for their
  ordinary and forced expansions. Their hypotheses are more specific than a
  uniform total price bound: convergence of the adjacent path and Gaussian
  kernels, convergence of the background activities and coupling, and the
  common marked input are all required. The next analytic comparison must
  supply these on the same selected histories. The preceding proof isolates
  the exact summation, subtraction and normalization steps after that
  comparison; it does not replace it by an unproved assertion of continuity
  of the infinite expansion.

  <subsection|Comparing the marked local integrals><label|sec:marked-kernel-convergence>

  We now supply the local analytic comparison used in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-source-stability>. Fix an adjacent
  pair of scales and one period. Compare two sequences of paid transitions on
  the same support dictionary. Assume their fine and coarse canonical path
  coefficients, retained Gaussian rows and couplings have common limits. The
  path sums have the strict-width bounds proved above. For the two
  backgrounds assume that each ordinary and forced activity difference tends
  uniformly to zero and that their activity majorants have uniformly small
  tails. In a marked comparison the inserted function and its address are the
  same on both sides and its paid size is bounded. An application must supply
  these assumptions for the actual pair of admissible background histories
  and their retained path data. The insertion's support and eligibility
  conditions must hold for each background. When a regular address is used as
  a separate marked slot, its background entry is zero in each history.

  The claim is convergence of the difference of each local marked integral
  and of every paid derivative on its healthy support. It includes uniformity
  over moving coarse configurations and normalized local charts. The
  principal issue is that the Gaussian measures in the two integrals vary. We
  first put them on a common physical variable; this also keeps possibly
  discontinuous inserted functions at exactly the same argument.

  <subsubsection|One common physical integration variable>

  Fix a forest, its finite coordinate choice and the support pattern of a
  local term. Its integral is over a compact gauge slice <math|Y> with
  probability Haar measure and a real Gaussian coordinate
  <math|z\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|D>>. The coordinate change in the local
  integral has the form

  <\equation*>
    w=\<mu\><rsub|N><around|(|Y|)>+g<rsub|N>*z,<space|2em>z=r<rsub|N>*<around|(|w-\<mu\><rsub|N><around|(|Y|)>|)>,<space|1em>r<rsub|N>=g<rsub|N><rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  Coordinate permutations belonging to the fixed support pattern are included
  in this notation; they have unit absolute Jacobian. The paid coupling
  window gives constants <math|0\<less\>u\<leqslant\>v\<less\>\<infty\>> such
  that <math|u\<leqslant\>r<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>v>, and the local mean
  estimate gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|N><around|(|Y|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>B>
  independently of <math|N,Y>. If <math|\<rho\><rsub|N>> is the centered
  Gaussian density, the transformed density is

  <\equation*>
    d<rsub|N>*<around|(|Y,w|)>=r<rsub|N><rsup|D>*\<rho\><rsub|N>*<around|(|r<rsub|N>*<around|(|w-\<mu\><rsub|N><around|(|Y|)>|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The actual covariance bounds give fixed <math|C,\<tau\>\<gtr\>0> with
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|N><around|(|z|)>\<leqslant\>C*e<rsup|-\<tau\><around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>>.
  Since

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2\<\|\|\>w-\<mu\><rsub|N><around|(|Y|)>\<\|\|\><rsup|2>+2*B<rsup|2>,
  </equation*>

  we have

  <\equation>
    d<rsub|N>*<around|(|Y,w|)>\<leqslant\>v<rsup|D>*C*e<rsup|\<tau\>*u<rsup|2>*B<rsup|2>>*e<rsup|-\<tau\>*u<rsup|2><around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>/2>.<label|eq:marked-common-gaussian-envelope>
  </equation>

  Also

  <\equation*>
    1+<around|\<\|\|\>|r<rsub|N>*<around|(|w-\<mu\><rsub|N><around|(|Y|)>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+v+v*B|)>*<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Thus a local integrand with bound <math|C<rsub|k>*<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|k>>
  becomes bounded, after multiplication by <math|d<rsub|N>>, by a constant
  times

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|-\<tau\>*u<rsup|2><around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>/2>*<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|k>.
  </equation*>

  This is integrable on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|D>>. The constants may depend on
  the fixed scale, period, support and derivative order, but not on the
  terminal cutoff, the slice point, or the moving coarse configuration.

  The local differentiation theorem of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-differentiation>
  writes each derivative as an integral of the differentiated compensated
  integrand against the Gaussian measure frozen at the base configuration.
  The bounds in Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:packet-estimates>
  and<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-activity-jets> give the polynomial bound
  just used. Therefore the change of variable is applied to that already
  justified derivative integral; it does not differentiate a moving measure
  without compensation.

  <subsubsection|Why the transformed integrand differences vanish>

  First hold the finite support pattern and coordinate choice fixed. At a
  fixed physical point <math|<around|(|Y,w|)>> the fine configuration read by
  an inserted activity is independent of the Gaussian covariance and of the
  coupling: the identity <math|\<mu\><rsub|N>+g<rsub|N>*r<rsub|N>*<around|(|w-\<mu\><rsub|N>|)>=w>
  cancels their dependence exactly. Hence a common bounded marked function is
  evaluated at the same fine configuration on both sides. No continuity of
  that function is needed. An unmarked background activity has the same
  property; its two values differ by the assumed uniform activity difference.

  The other factors in the compensated integrand are smooth finite
  expressions in the local path and retained-kernel data. Their continuity
  can be checked directly. A fixed length cutoff leaves only finitely many
  canonical paths: their displacement vectors lie in a finite lattice box.
  Each path holonomy is a finite product of link matrices; all its
  fixed-order derivatives are finite sums of such products. Convergence of
  its coefficient therefore gives convergence of these derivatives along any
  sequence of bounded local chart jets. Infinite canonical path sums have the
  same conclusion by the strict-width summability proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:fixed-rung-convergence>.

  The local Gram matrices are finite sums and products of those rows. The
  common positive lower bound passes to their limits. Their inverses converge
  by

  <\equation*>
    A<rsup|-1>-B<rsup|-1>=A<rsup|-1>*<around|(|B-A|)>*B<rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  Repeated differentiation of <math|A*A<rsup|-1>=I> proves convergence of all
  required inverse jets by induction. Determinants are polynomials in the
  entries, and the determinant stays strictly positive. Their logarithms and
  inverse square roots, and their fixed-order derivatives, consequently
  converge. This covers the selected covariance, means, Gaussian densities
  and density ratios, including the projected covariance in the compensation.
  The denominator density at a fixed <math|w> has a strictly positive limit;
  its reciprocal therefore causes no loss of pointwise convergence. Uniform
  domination is supplied separately by <eqref|eq:marked-common-gaussian-envelope>
  and the compensated derivative bounds.

  Before differentiating these factors, retain the full protected support of
  each activity and of the marked insertion. The base coarse configuration is
  healthy on the enlarged union of these supports, and the normalized chart
  is based there. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:compensator-held-fine> holds on
  every active protected bond wherever the broader cutoff is nonzero near the
  base point; inactive bonds stay fixed by definition. Indeed, a nonzero
  cutoff germ gives the base fluctuation bound <math|4*a*t>; continuity gives
  the strict <math|8*a*t> bound on a neighborhood. The compensator identity
  then applies on all the finitely many protected bonds on one common
  neighborhood. Where a cutoff has zero germ, the complete factor has zero
  germ. Consequently an activity or mark <math|h<rsub|N>> occurs, as a germ
  in the chart parameter <math|y>, only as

  <\equation*>
    h<rsub|N>*<around|(|U<rsub|N>*<around|(|Y,w|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>\<zeta\><rsub|N>*<around|(|y;Y,w|)>,
  </equation*>

  with <math|U<rsub|N>*<around|(|Y,w|)>> independent of <math|y> and
  <math|\<zeta\><rsub|N>> smooth. This is exactly the held-factor
  identity<nbsp><eqref|eq:marked-held-factor>, applied to each relevant read
  support. All derivatives fall on <math|\<zeta\><rsub|N>> and the remaining
  smooth factors. Uniform convergence of activity values is therefore
  sufficient; convergence of their uncompensated derivatives is not being
  inferred.

  For completeness, the elementary factors in a local product fall into four
  mathematical cases. A cell-action factor is the exponential of a finite
  local action and fixed cutoff functions; its action jets converge by the
  preceding path argument. A forced-activity factor is its value at the
  common fine configuration times a smooth local cutoff. An ordinary
  logarithmic factor is the exponential minus one of an ordinary activity
  value together with the local quadratic compensation; the first value is
  compared uniformly and the second by the matrix argument. A warning factor
  is a fixed local mask or charge times a smooth cutoff, possibly minus its
  constant term. The flag and compatibility decisions have the same values on
  both sides, because their geometric support data, threshold and physical
  configuration are identical. Product and chain rules now prove that the
  differences of the fixed-order jets of every finite product tend to zero.
  With one marked factor, replace the corresponding ordinary factor by the
  common inserted function times its cutoff; the same proof applies.

  Moving configurations do not require convergence along the whole sequence.
  For any subsequence, take a further subsequence on which their finitely
  many link matrices and the bounded chart jets converge. An order-<math|r>
  derivative evaluation can be pulled back to <math|r> real parameters, so
  these finitely many jets lie in one fixed finite-dimensional bounded set.
  Compactness of the gauge group and of the closure of that set gives the
  subsequence. The support and coordinate choices range over finite sets, so
  take them constant on a further subsequence. The calculations above make
  the difference of the two transformed integrands tend to zero there. A
  common bounded mark need not itself converge: it multiplies a smooth-factor
  difference tending to zero, and its uniform bound suffices. If a difference
  failed to tend to zero in the original sequence, a subsequence would stay a
  positive distance from zero and could not have such a further subsequence.
  Thus the differences tend to zero without any preliminary convergence
  assumption on the moving configurations. Shared discontinuous indicators
  may also vary: for their common value <math|\<b-1\><rsub|E<rsub|N>>> use
  <math|<around|\||\<b-1\><rsub|E<rsub|N>>*<around|(|A<rsub|N>-B<rsub|N>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||A<rsub|N>-B<rsub|N>|\|>>.
  Only the finite structural support and coordinate choices are frozen; no
  simultaneous stabilization of indicators for every <math|<around|(|Y,w|)>>
  is needed.

  <subsubsection|Integration and local derivative norms>

  Let <math|F<rsub|N>*<around|(|Y,z|)>> and <math|G<rsub|N>*<around|(|Y,z|)>>
  be the two compensated derivative integrands. For fixed <math|Y,w>, their
  density-weighted transforms have difference tending to zero. Each is
  dominated by an integrable function of <math|w> independent of <math|Y,N>.
  Dominated convergence first in <math|w> proves convergence of the
  difference of their inner integrals for almost every <math|Y>. The norms of
  these differences are bounded by the integral of the sum of the two
  majorants. A second application of dominated convergence against the
  probability Haar measure in <math|Y> gives convergence of the complete
  local integrals. This argument also justifies the two uses of Fubini, since
  absolute integrability was established before passing to the limit.

  Apply this to every moving base configuration and normalized chart of each
  order up to the paid order. The selection argument of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-source-stability> turns it into
  uniform convergence of those derivative evaluations. On the fixed finite
  torus, the conversion from normalized chart derivatives to the local
  derivative price has a finite constant: all site separations are bounded,
  and only finitely many derivative orders occur. Therefore each individual
  ordinary output entry has difference tending to zero in its required
  derivative price. For the forced entry use order zero and arbitrary moving
  configurations; its uniform value difference tends to zero.

  Finite averaging over forests and coordinate choices preserves both
  statements. The ordinary marked logarithm is a sum of finite products with
  one marked factor, and the forced marked polynomial is formed by
  finite-subset products, addition of the ordinary logarithm, and
  differentiation in its one source parameter. Each operation preserves the
  entrywise convergence just proved. The marked cluster and product estimates
  in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-source-stability> give their
  uniform tails. Local subtraction and the background hook then give the
  convergence of each fixed input column in the actual common dictionary.
  Summation and finite motion averaging give the adjacent-scale stability
  required in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:fixed-rung-convergence>.

  This comparison uses both backgrounds' common-limit hypotheses; it does not
  prove convergence of the background activity trajectory. It proves the
  needed continuous dependence of the selected ordinary and marked local
  transformations, with their masks, Gaussian measures, derivatives and
  countable output lists retained.

  <subsection|Convergence at a fixed scale and
  normalization><label|sec:fixed-rung-convergence>

  The full-mesh argument in Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-public-same-run-cauchy>
  compares the final observable with one at a fixed intermediate scale. We
  now prove the convergence mechanism at that scale. The period and the
  intermediate scale are fixed throughout; the terminal scale tends to
  infinity. In particular the configuration space in each convergence
  statement below is one fixed finite torus.

  <subsubsection|Contraction of the remainder graph>

  At scale <math|m>, keep every regular support and forced address in one
  fixed dictionary. Let <math|E<rsub|m>> be its vector space of admissible
  remainder arrays, with the seminorm obtained by minimizing their total
  regular mass plus <math|\<kappa\><rsub|m><rsup|-1>> times their forced
  mass. The regular mass uses the prescribed derivative bounds. This seminorm
  retains the actual arrays; quotienting by equality of their gas evaluations
  is not part of its definition. The elementary sum and scaling estimates
  give its triangle inequality and absolute homogeneity. Evaluation of the
  masked ordinary sum has norm at most one, and evaluation of any forced
  support\Uinventory profile has norm at most <math|\<kappa\><rsub|m>>. For
  example,

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|a>\<chi\><rsub|X<rsub|a>>*r<rsub|a><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|a><around|\<\|\|\>|r<rsub|a>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|X<rsub|a>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|r|\<\|\|\>><rsub|E<rsub|m>>,<space|2em><around|\||\<cal-P\><around|(|r<rsub|<math-up|forced>>|)><around|(|b,U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<kappa\><rsub|m><around|\<\|\|\>|r|\<\|\|\>><rsub|E<rsub|m>>.
  </equation*>

  To justify the last inequalities when the infimum defines the norm, first
  use any admissible majorant within <math|\<varepsilon\>> of the infimum and
  let <math|\<varepsilon\>*\<downarrow\>*0>. The same estimates applied to a
  difference give uniform Lipschitz bounds on these evaluation maps. The
  absolutely summable majorants justify subtracting the series.

  Represent a linear family of remainders by a map
  <math|G:F<rsub|d>\<to\>E<rsub|m>> with

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|G*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|E<rsub|m>>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|m><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,<space|2em>s<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  Two such maps are within <math|a\<geqslant\>0> if

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|G-H|)>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|E<rsub|m>>\<leqslant\>a*s<rsub|m><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1><space|1em><text|for
    every >v.
  </equation*>

  This relation is symmetric, has the additive triangle property, and any two
  allowed graphs are within <math|2>.

  One step produces from <math|G> a coarse graph <math|\<Phi\>*G> and an
  invertible coefficient map <math|I<rsub|G>>. Before reparametrizing by the
  coarse coefficient, write its remainder map as
  <math|J<rsub|G>=<around|(|\<Phi\>*G|)>*I<rsub|G>>. The paid regular and
  forced estimates of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:three-port-history>,
  applied to differences of insertions on the same supports, give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:fixed-rung-difference>

    <\eqsplit>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|I<rsub|G>-I<rsub|H>|)>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<eta\>*a*s<rsub|m+1>*C<rsub|<math-up|orb>><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|J<rsub|G>-J<rsub|H>|)>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|E<rsub|m>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<eta\>*a*s<rsub|m+1>*C<rsub|<math-up|orb>><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>>>>
    </eqsplit>
  </equation>

  when <math|G,H> are within <math|a>. Here the same raw supports are used in
  the two expansions. Subtraction cancels their primary insertion. The
  ordinary integral and the marked singleton derivative are linear in the
  remaining insertion; local subtraction uses the same linear section. Thus
  the difference has the price of the difference of the inputs. If
  representatives are chosen through derivative majorants, the bound is
  obtained with those majorants and then their infimum. Finite motion
  averaging costs <math|C<rsub|<math-up|orb>>>, exactly as in the weighted
  sum in Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-selected-linear-interpolation>. This
  explains why one must retain the common slots and supports.

  The same one-step estimate gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|I<rsub|G><rsup|-1>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|m+1><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>.
  Put <math|\<delta\>=\<eta\>*s<rsub|m+1>*C<rsub|<math-up|orb>>> and let
  <math|b=L<rsup|-3/8>>. The schedule's two relevant payments are

  <\equation*>
    \<delta\>\<leqslant\>b,<space|2em>4*s<rsub|m+1>*b\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  The second follows from its bank inequality: if
  <math|q=L<rsup|1/8>\<geqslant\>1>, that inequality gives
  <math|4*s<rsub|m+1>\<leqslant\>q>, while <math|b=q<rsup|-3>>. For a coarse
  vector <math|x>, set <math|y=I<rsub|G><rsup|-1>*x>. Then

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|\<Phi\>*G-\<Phi\>*H|)>*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|E<rsub|m>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|J<rsub|G>-J<rsub|H>|)>*y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|E<rsub|m>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Phi\>*H*<around|(|I<rsub|H>*y-x|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|E<rsub|m>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\><around|(|1+s<rsub|m>|)>*\<delta\>*a<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\><around|(|1+s<rsub|m>|)>*\<delta\>*a*s<rsub|m+1><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>a*s<rsub|m><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Thus the coarse graphs are within <math|a/2>. This contraction acts on the
  complete regular and forced remainder, after reparametrizing by its actual
  coefficient map.

  <subsubsection|Forgetting the terminal graph>

  Write <math|G<rsub|m,N>> for the selected graph at scale <math|m> in the
  history with terminal index <math|N>. For each fixed <math|m>, let
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|m,N>> be its one-step map. Retain the following precise
  local stability input: for every fixed admissible graph <math|H>, the
  graphs <math|\<Phi\><rsub|m,N>*H> are Cauchy in the preceding \Pwithin\Q
  relation, and their coefficient maps <math|I<rsub|m,N,H>> are Cauchy in
  operator norm. Their dependence on <math|H> has the uniform bounds just
  proved. This is a statement about the selected adjacent-scale kernels and
  their marked derivatives; the contraction alone does not imply it.

  First, if <math|H<rsub|N>> is Cauchy, then
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|m,N>*H<rsub|N>> is Cauchy. For a prescribed small error,
  fix a sufficiently late <math|N<rsub|0>>. Compare
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|m,N>*H<rsub|N>> with
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|m,N>*H<rsub|N<rsub|0>>> using contraction, compare the
  latter at two terminal indices using fixed-input stability, and compare
  back to <math|\<Phi\><rsub|m,M>*H<rsub|M>>. The two outside errors are half
  the corresponding input errors. The identical three-term comparison with
  <eqref|eq:fixed-rung-difference> proves that <math|I<rsub|m,N,H<rsub|N>>>
  is Cauchy.

  For a fixed integer <math|h\<geqslant\>0>, replace <math|G<rsub|m+h,N>> by
  one fixed admissible graph and propagate it downward through <math|h>
  steps. The resulting graph <math|V<rsub|m,N><rsup|<around|(|h|)>>> is
  Cauchy by the preceding paragraph and finite induction. The exact history
  recursion and the bound <math|2> on any initial graph difference imply

  <\equation*>
    G<rsub|m,N><text| is within >2<rsup|1-h><text| of
    >V<rsub|m,N><rsup|<around|(|h|)>>
  </equation*>

  for all sufficiently large <math|N>. Given <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>,
  choose <math|h> so that <math|2<rsup|1-h>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>/4>, and
  then use the Cauchy property of <math|V<rsup|<around|(|h|)>>>. The triangle
  inequality proves that <math|G<rsub|m,N>> is Cauchy. Only a finite number
  of levels is used for each error tolerance; no infinite product of inverse
  maps occurs.

  Now fix the label <math|y> at its reference scale <math|r>. Its coefficient
  at that scale is exactly <math|y>, because the terminal coefficient is
  normalized by the inverse of its readout at <math|r>. For each fixed
  <math|m\<geqslant\>r>, let <math|x<rsub|m,N>> be the resulting coefficient.
  The history relation is

  <\equation*>
    x<rsub|m,N>=I<rsub|m,N,G<rsub|m+1,N>>*x<rsub|m+1,N>.
  </equation*>

  Suppose <math|x<rsub|N>=S<rsub|N>*y<rsub|N>> is Cauchy, <math|S<rsub|N>> is
  Cauchy in operator norm, and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|S<rsub|N><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>b>.
  Then <math|y<rsub|N>> is bounded and

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|y<rsub|N>-y<rsub|M>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>b<around*|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|N>-x<rsub|M>*<around|\<\|\|\>|<rsub|1>+<around|\<\|\|\>|S<rsub|M>-S<rsub|N>|\<\|\|\>><space|0.17em>|\<\|\|\>>*y<rsub|M>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  Starting with the constant reference coefficient, finite induction
  therefore proves that <math|x<rsub|m,N>> is Cauchy at every fixed <math|m>.
  Moreover

  <\equation*>
    G<rsub|m,N>*x<rsub|m,N>-G<rsub|m,M>*x<rsub|m,M>=G<rsub|m,N>*<around|(|x<rsub|m,N>-x<rsub|m,M>|)>+<around|(|G<rsub|m,N>-G<rsub|m,M>|)>*x<rsub|m,M>
  </equation*>

  is Cauchy in <math|E<rsub|m>>. This proves convergence of the coefficient
  and Cauchyness of the full remainder data in <math|E<rsub|m>> at the fixed
  scale, conditional only on the specified adjacent-scale stability input and
  its paid history realization. Their coefficient norms are bounded for all
  terminal indices; finitely many indices before the eventual recursion can
  be included by enlarging the bound.

  <subsubsection|Uniform convergence of the inserted functions>

  The primary exponential-entry map is linear in <math|x<rsub|m,N>> and
  uniformly bounded on the fixed finite torus. Indeed each of its finitely
  many local-section columns is continuous on the compact gauge configuration
  space; multiplication by its fixed mask preserves its supremum bound.
  Expanding in the coefficient basis therefore bounds its difference by
  <math|C*<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|m,N>-x<rsub|m,M>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>. The
  ordinary remainder and each forced profile are uniformly Cauchy by their
  evaluation bounds in the first subsection. Adding them proves that the
  exponential entries and forced profiles of the inserted fields are
  uniformly Cauchy over all configurations. Their bounded scalar-valued
  function spaces are complete, so they have uniform limits; no completeness
  of <math|E<rsub|m>> is needed. This proof permits discontinuous masks:
  their supports and thresholds are fixed at this scale. Translation, signed
  coordinate motion and conjugation preserve the conclusion. The fixed
  physical scaling factor does too.

  For a finite set of inserted labels all these statements hold on one common
  tail. Their coefficient prices have finite bounds <math|M<rsub|i>>. Given
  positive allotted prices <math|\<zeta\><rsub|i>>, take
  <math|w<rsub|i>=\<zeta\><rsub|i>/<around|(|<around|\||M<rsub|i>|\|>+1|)>\<gtr\>0>.
  Then <math|w<rsub|i>> times the actual coefficient price is at most
  <math|\<zeta\><rsub|i>>, uniformly in terminal index. This is the positive
  weight choice used in the fixed-rung vault of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-public-same-run-cauchy>.

  <subsubsection|Convergence of the background density>

  We spell out the background convergence needed for the same normalized
  moments. At the fixed scale retain common path stencils and activity
  addresses. Suppose the path coefficients converge in the weighted sums of
  their differences: for one <math|v\<gtr\>0>,

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|a><around|\||c<rsub|N,a>-c<rsub|a>|\|>*e<rsup|v<math-up|length><around|(|a|)>>\<longrightarrow\>0
  </equation*>

  for each one-form row, and the analogous atom-budget sums tend to zero for
  the quadratic loop, cubic and quartic arrays. All paths have the common
  bounds of the construction. Suppose also that the couplings
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|N>> are Cauchy, and the background activity arrays are
  Cauchy in their common norm of rooted ordinary derivative prices and forced
  value prices. These are the precise background inputs used here.

  The path convergence gives uniform convergence of the action on the fixed
  configuration space. To see this directly, each transported curvature in a
  row has norm at most <math|2>, so a row difference is at most twice its
  coefficient-difference budget. A degree-<math|q> atom difference, with the
  same stencil, is at most <math|2<rsup|q>> times its coefficient budget. The
  masks are identical for the two terms and hence do not add an error. The
  common path bounds bound all row values uniformly; consequently
  <math|<around|\||<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>-<around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>>|)>*<around|\<\|\|\>|u-w|\<\|\|\>>>
  treats the quadratic row action. There are finitely many sites, plaquette
  directions and signed motions. Summing their estimates, and using
  boundedness and convergence of <math|\<beta\><rsub|N>>, proves the
  assertion for the full symmetrized action.

  For the activity arrays, sum a rooted budget over the finitely many sites.
  Every ordinary term has its designated site, and every forced term has a
  nonempty marked support, so each term is counted at least once. If <math|V>
  is the number of sites and <math|b,k> are the fixed two activity prices,
  the resulting uniform evaluation bounds are
  <math|V*b<around|\<\|\|\>|A|\<\|\|\>>> for the ordinary sum and
  <math|V*k<around|\<\|\|\>|A|\<\|\|\>>> for a forced profile. Applied to
  differences, these give uniform convergence of the ordinary activity sum
  and every support\Uinventory profile. The fixed torus has finitely many
  such profiles. Its gas is a finite sum over compatible subsets of these
  profiles, each multiplied by the fixed flag indicator. It therefore
  converges uniformly as well. This finite profile description does not
  require the original activity list to be finite; its regrouping was
  justified by the absolute budgets.

  Write the full density as <math|\<rho\><rsub|N>> and its scalar term as
  <math|s<rsub|N>>. Its scalar-stripped density

  <\equation*>
    <wide|\<rho\>|~><rsub|N>=e<rsup|-s<rsub|N>>*\<rho\><rsub|N>
  </equation*>

  is the product of the gas and the exponential of minus the path action and
  ordinary activity sum. All exponent arguments are uniformly bounded on a
  tail. Exponentiation is uniformly continuous on that bounded interval, so
  the stripped density is uniformly Cauchy. Its integral
  <math|Z<rsub|N><rsup|0>> against the probability Haar prior is Cauchy,
  since

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||Z<rsub|N><rsup|0>-Z<rsub|M><rsup|0>|\|>\<leqslant\>sup<rsub|U><around|\||<wide|\<rho\>|~><rsub|N><around|(|U|)>-<wide|\<rho\>|~><rsub|M><around|(|U|)>|\|>.
  </equation*>

  The weighted path convergence just used follows from coefficientwise
  convergence at a strictly larger exponential width. Here is the useful
  precise implication. If <math|v\<less\>w>, the row coefficients at width
  <math|w> have a common budget <math|B>, and each coefficient converges,
  then

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||c<rsub|N,a>-c<rsub|a>|\|>*e<rsup|v<math-up|length><around|(|a|)>>\<leqslant\>2*B*e<rsup|-<around|(|w-v|)><math-up|length><around|(|a|)>>.
  </equation*>

  Here the canonical row index enumerates a plaquette direction and a vector
  <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>; its chosen coordinate path has length
  <math|<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>+4>. Consequently, for every
  <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>,

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|a>e<rsup|-\<delta\><math-up|length><around|(|a|)>>=<around|\||\<cal-A\>|\|>*e<rsup|-4*\<delta\>><around*|(|<frac|1+e<rsup|-\<delta\>>|1-e<rsup|-\<delta\>>>|)><rsup|4>\<less\>\<infty\>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<cal-A\>> is the finite set of plaquette directions. This is a
  sum over the chosen coordinate paths, not over all walks. Dominated
  convergence for series gives convergence of the whole row difference
  budget.

  For an atom of degree <math|q=2,3,4>, the canonical index enumerates
  <math|q> plaquette directions and <math|q-1> displacement vectors. The
  total length is <math|\<ell\>=<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|q-1><around|\||x<rsub|j>|\|><rsub|1>+4*q>.
  Its coefficients have the form <math|e<rsup|-w*\<ell\>>*C<rsub|N>*<around|(|a,\<nu\>|)>>,
  where <math|\<nu\>> runs through the finite color multi-indices. The
  coefficient arrays <math|C<rsub|N>> converge coordinatewise and have a
  common Hilbert norm bound <math|B>; finite partial sums give the same bound
  for their limit <math|C>. The atom difference budget at width
  <math|v\<less\>w> is exactly

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|a,\<nu\>>e<rsup|-<around|(|w-v|)>*\<ell\><around|(|a|)>>*<around|\||C<rsub|N>*<around|(|a,\<nu\>|)>-C*<around|(|a,\<nu\>|)>|\|>.
  </equation*>

  Each summand is at most <math|2*B*e<rsup|-<around|(|w-v|)>*\<ell\><around|(|a|)>>>.
  Summing this majorant gives a finite multiple of

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|-4*q*<around|(|w-v|)>><around*|(|<frac|1+e<rsup|-<around|(|w-v|)>>|1-e<rsup|-<around|(|w-v|)>>>|)><rsup|4*<around|(|q-1|)>>.
  </equation*>

  A second application of dominated convergence proves the required
  atom-budget convergence. Choose <math|v> strictly between the paid width
  and the minimum of the larger row and three atom widths. All four
  conclusions then hold at this one width, on a common tail. It is essential
  that these are the same canonical stencils; convergence of an unrelated
  finite-volume matrix is insufficient.

  <subsubsection|A positive limit of the partition functions>

  Positivity of each <math|Z<rsub|N><rsup|0>> alone would not suffice. We
  need one strictly positive lower bound independent of <math|N>. Let
  <math|0\<less\>\<theta\>\<leqslant\>1> be the fixed scale's small-field
  threshold, let <math|H=2*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>> be the common upper
  bound on its coupling, and let <math|F,T> be the ordinary and loop
  allowances there. With <math|A> the path price and <math|\<cal-C\>> its
  quadratic clipping data, set

  <\equation*>
    K=V*<around|{|F+<math-up|loopsPrice><around|(|\<theta\>,H,A,T|)>+H<math-up|baseBudget><around|(|\<cal-C\>,A,\<theta\>|)>|}>.
  </equation*>

  These quantities are finite and fixed while <math|N> varies. The
  small-link-ball proof in Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-construction>
  gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:fixed-rung-partition-positive>Z<rsub|N><rsup|0>\<geqslant\>a<rsub|m>\<assign\>exp
    <around|{|-K-<around|\||E<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|bond>>|\|>*d<rsub|G>*log
    <around|(|16/\<theta\>|)>|}>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation>

  Here <math|E<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|bond>>> is the finite set of positive
  bonds, not the remainder space, and <math|d<rsub|G>> is the ambient real
  matrix dimension. The bound follows by taking every link in the ball of
  chord radius <math|\<theta\>/4>: all flags vanish, the action is bounded by
  <math|K>, and the product Haar probability is at least
  <math|<around|(|\<theta\>/16|)><rsup|<around|\||E<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|bond>>|\|>*d<rsub|G>>>.
  Nonnegativity of the full selected density permits restriction of its
  integral to this event. The scalar factor cancels in
  <math|Z<rsub|N><rsup|0>>. Replacing <math|\<beta\><rsub|N>> by <math|H>
  only increases the nonnegative action bound. Thus the Cauchy limit
  <math|Z<rsup|0>> satisfies <math|Z<rsup|0>\<geqslant\>a<rsub|m>\<gtr\>0>.

  Finally let <math|P<rsub|N,i><around|(|U|)>> and
  <math|F<rsub|N,i,b><around|(|U|)>> be the inserted exponential entries and
  forced profiles already shown uniformly Cauchy. Fix a finite census
  algebra, whose nonconstant generators are nilpotent. The scalar-stripped
  restored polynomial is

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|-<text|path action><rsub|N>-<text|ordinary
    sum><rsub|N>>*exp<rsub|<math-up|nil>><space|-0.17em><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i>t<rsub|i>*P<rsub|N,i>|)>*\<cal-G\>*<space|-0.17em><around*|(|<text|background
    profiles><rsub|N>+<big|sum><rsub|i>t<rsub|i>*F<rsub|N,i>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The nilpotent exponential and the grouped gas are finite polynomials in
  their entries. Hence every mode is uniformly Cauchy and bounded. Their
  measurability follows from that of the local sections, masks and
  coefficient arrays and from the absolutely convergent groupings. Their Haar
  integrals are Cauchy. Dividing by <math|Z<rsub|N><rsup|0>\<to\>Z<rsup|0>\<gtr\>0>
  proves convergence of each normalized fixed-scale mode. The
  scalar-stripping identity identifies this ratio with the actual restored
  history moment, since its numerator and partition function contain the
  identical factor <math|e<rsup|-s<rsub|N>>>. This proves the fixed-rung
  convergence required by the full-mesh comparison, under the stated selected
  branch and background convergence inputs. Their production must still be
  established for the same funded history.

  <section|The one-loop coefficient and stationary paths>

  <subsection|A uniform analytic price for the bare plaquette
  energy><label|sec:ex-wilson-vacuum-price>

  The path construction uses uniform analytic bounds for the unperturbed
  Wilson energy, before it compares that energy with a general path. This
  section records the finite-volume estimate and its proof. All constants are
  independent of the torus side.

  Write <math|\<cal-A\>> for the finite set of positive oriented plaquette
  types, <math|N<rsub|\<cal-A\>>=<around|\||\<cal-A\>|\|>>, and let
  <math|d<rsub|G>> be the dimension of the fixed matrix representation. For a
  link field <math|U> on a torus of side <math|m>, put

  <\equation*>
    W<rsub|m><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|x\<in\><around|(|\<bbb-Z\>/m*\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|4>><big|sum><rsub|a\<in\>\<cal-A\>><around*|(|1-<frac|<math-up|Retr><space|0.17em>U<around|(|\<partial\><around|(|x,a|)>|)>|d<rsub|G>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|U<around|(|\<partial\><around|(|x,a|)>|)>> is the ordered
  four-edge holonomy. The estimate below is a coefficientwise price uniform
  in <math|m>; the action itself is extensive. We use Lie-algebra coordinates
  <math|z> at the identity and write <math|<wide|W|~><rsub|m><around|(|z|)>=W<rsub|m><around|(|exp
  z|)>>; every derivative below means the derivative of this coordinate
  function at <math|z=0>. The norm on link-coordinate arrays and on their
  local Fourier-mode restrictions is the supremum norm.

  For <math|t\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, set <math|n=t+1>. A coefficient picture of
  order <math|t> assigns a function of <math|t> independent complex momenta
  <math|q<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,q<rsub|t>> to every list of <math|n> colors and
  oriented-link directions; the zeroth momentum is fixed by
  <math|q<rsub|0>=-<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|t>q<rsub|j>>. The width-one strip
  is the set where every coordinate of each independent momentum has
  imaginary part of absolute value at most one. Let
  <math|<math-up|CTax><around|(|R,0|)>=1> and
  <math|<math-up|CTax><around|(|R,k+1|)>=<math-up|CTax><around|(|R,k|)>/<around|(|R/2<rsup|k+1>|)>>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Uniform bare-energy coefficient
    price><label|prop:ex-wilson-stream>There are constants
    <math|R<rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0> and <math|A<rsub|\<ast\>>\<less\>\<infty\>>,
    depending only on the compact gauge representation and plaquette
    convention, and for each <math|t> there is a single picture
    <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|t>> that works for every torus side <math|m>. Its
    components are holomorphic on the width-one momentum strip and satisfy

    <\equation*>
      sup<rsub|q<text| in the strip>><around|\||\<cal-W\><rsub|t><around|(|q|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|t>\<assign\>N<rsub|\<cal-A\>>*A<rsub|\<ast\>>*exp
      <around*|(|16*<around|(|t+1|)><rsup|2>|)><frac|<math-up|CTax><around|(|R<rsub|\<ast\>>,t+1|)>|<around|(|t+1|)>!>.
    </equation*>

    Moreover, let <math|p<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,p<rsub|t>> be dual-torus momenta,
    <math|c<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,c<rsub|t>> colors, and
    <math|b<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,b<rsub|t>> oriented-link directions. Let
    <math|e<rsub|p,b,c>> denote the corresponding Fourier-mode variation of
    the link field, and put <math|q=<around|(|p<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,p<rsub|t>|)>>.
    Then

    <\equation*>
      <frac|1|n!>*D<rsup|n>*<wide|W|~><rsub|m><around|(|0|)><around*|[|e<rsub|p<rsub|0>,b<rsub|0>,c<rsub|0>>,\<ldots\>,e<rsub|p<rsub|t>,b<rsub|t>,c<rsub|t>>|]>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\||<around|(|\<bbb-Z\>/m*\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|4>|\|>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-W\><rsub|t>*<around|(|\<b-up-c\>,\<b-up-b\>;q|)>,>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|t>p<rsub|j>=0,>>|<row|<cell|0,>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|t>p<rsub|j>\<ne\>0.>>>>>
    </equation*>

    Thus the volume factor in the zero-total-momentum coefficient is exactly
    the torus cardinality, and every nonzero-total-momentum coefficient
    vanishes.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    First construct a single plaquette extension. Let
    <math|\<frak-g\><rsub|\<bbb-C\>>> be the complexification of the
    represented Lie algebra and let <math|\<cal-E\>:\<frak-g\><rsub|\<bbb-C\>>\<to\><math-up|Mat><rsub|d<rsub|G>><around|(|\<bbb-C\>|)>>
    be the linear representation map. For a complex link-coordinate array
    <math|z>, the two oriented link matrices are <math|exp
    <around|(|\<cal-E\>*z<rsub|e>|)>> and <math|exp
    <around|(|-\<cal-E\>*z<rsub|e>|)>>. These depend holomorphically on
    <math|z>, since the matrix exponential is entire. Multiplication along
    the four edges, trace, and the average of the forward and reverse loop
    traces are holomorphic too. On real coordinates that average is the real
    trace of the holonomy, because for the unitary holonomy <math|H> one has
    <math|<math-up|tr><around|(|H<rsup|-1>|)>=<wide|<math-up|tr><around|(|H|)>|\<bar\>>>.
    Thus this extension restricts to the real plaquette energy summand.

    Continuity of the exponential at zero gives
    <math|R<rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0> such that complex link coordinates in the
    radius-<math|R<rsub|\<ast\>>> ball give oriented matrices within
    <math|<math-up|rad><around|(|1|)>> of their physical unitary matrices,
    distance being measured in the Frobenius norm
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|F>>. This implies the
    product estimate as follows. If <math|M> is within
    <math|r=<math-up|rad><around|(|1|)>> of a unitary <math|V>, then for
    every matrix <math|X>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|M*X|\<\|\|\>><rsub|F>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|V*X|\<\|\|\>><rsub|F>+<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|M-V|)>*X|\<\|\|\>><rsub|F>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+r|)><around|\<\|\|\>|X|\<\|\|\>><rsub|F>,
    </equation*>

    since left multiplication by <math|V> is an isometry and the operator
    norm of <math|M-V> is at most its Frobenius norm. Iterating this
    inequality through four factors, starting with <math|X=I>, gives
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|M<rsub|1>*M<rsub|2>*M<rsub|3>*M<rsub|4>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|F>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+r|)><rsup|4><around|\<\|\|\>|I|\<\|\|\>><rsub|F>>.
    The reverse loop has the same bound. The continuous pulse

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|M,N|)>\<longmapsto\>1-<frac|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>M+<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>N|2*d<rsub|G>>
    </equation*>

    is bounded on the product of the two closed Frobenius balls of radius
    <math|T=<around|(|1+r|)><rsup|4><around|\<\|\|\>|I|\<\|\|\>><rsub|F>>
    about zero, since both four-edge products lie there. Compactness in
    finite dimension gives a common bound <math|A<rsub|\<ast\>>>. Together
    these facts give a uniform holomorphic extension bounded by
    <math|A<rsub|\<ast\>>> on a fixed ball, at every plaquette anchor and
    every torus size.

    Each plaquette is supported in the radius-four coordinate box about its
    anchor: every link in its four-edge boundary has an anchor at
    max-distance at most four. Hence its real local action is unchanged when
    the cover configuration is changed outside that box.

    To define the common local symbol, place each radius-four box in the
    auxiliary torus <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|9>=<around|(|\<bbb-Z\>/9*\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|4>>,
    with <math|9=2*d+1> and <math|d=4>. The box embeds in this torus without
    identifications. For each plaquette type, evaluate its local Taylor form
    there on the restricted Fourier modes
    <math|y\<mapsto\><with|font-series|bold|1><rsub|<around|(|b,c|)>>*exp
    <around|(|i*<around|\<langle\>|p,<math-up|lift><space|0.17em>y|\<rangle\>>|)>>,
    and sum these local symbols over the plaquette types; this defines
    <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|t>> at the corresponding independent momenta. For a
    period of any side <math|m>, pull a period-<math|m> field <math|z> to the
    auxiliary torus by

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|P<rsub|m>*z|)><around|(|y,b,c|)>=z*<around|(|<math-up|wrap><rsub|m><around|(|<math-up|lift><space|0.17em>y|)>,b,c|)>.
    </equation*>

    Then the local plaquette function satisfies
    <math|<math-up|local><rsub|m><around|(|z|)>=<math-up|local><rsub|9><around|(|P<rsub|m>*z|)>>.
    The pullback of a period-<math|m> Fourier mode is exactly the restricted
    Fourier mode used above. This identity holds even when <math|m\<less\>9>
    and distinct auxiliary sites wrap to the same period-<math|m> link:
    evaluating <math|<math-up|local><rsub|9>> on <math|P<rsub|m>*z> retains
    all repeated-link contributions. Thus the local symbol is the same for
    every period, including the small periods. The picture variables are
    momenta, rather than link coordinates. To obtain the strip bound,
    evaluate the order-<math|n> Taylor multilinear form of the local
    plaquette extension on the Fourier-mode vectors
    <math|e<rsub|p<rsub|j>,b<rsub|j>,c<rsub|j>>>. The Cauchy derivative
    estimate on successively shrinking balls of radii
    <math|R<rsub|\<ast\>>/2,R<rsub|\<ast\>>/2<rsup|2>,\<ldots\>,R<rsub|\<ast\>>/2<rsup|n>>
    bounds its order-<math|n> derivative by
    <math|A<rsub|\<ast\>><math-up|CTax><around|(|R<rsub|\<ast\>>,n|)>>. The
    Taylor coefficient is normalized by <math|1/n>!. For a momentum in the
    width-one strip, a Fourier mode restricted to the radius-four stencil has
    supremum norm at most <math|exp <around|(|16*n|)>>: the dependent
    momentum has imaginary coordinates bounded by <math|t>, and a stencil
    displacement has four coordinates, each of absolute value at most four.
    There are <math|n> input modes in the multilinear form, so their product
    costs at most <math|exp <around|(|16*n<rsup|2>|)>>. This proves the
    claimed bound for each plaquette type. Summing over the finite set
    <math|\<cal-A\>> multiplies it by <math|N<rsub|\<cal-A\>>> and yields a
    single picture <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|t>>, independent of the torus side.

    It remains to check the finite-volume coefficient identity. Expand the
    translated plaquette sum and apply the multilinear coefficient to an
    ordered momentum/color/direction tuple. Translating a local plaquette
    coefficient by an anchor multiplies its Fourier mode by the character at
    total momentum <math|<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|t>p<rsub|j>>. The sum over
    all anchors is zero unless that total momentum is zero; in that case each
    anchor contributes the same local coefficient, so the sum is exactly
    <math|<around|\||<around|(|\<bbb-Z\>/m*\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|4>|\|>> times
    the local symbol <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|t>> evaluated at the output phase.
    This proves the displayed identity, including its volume normalization.
  </proof>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Use in the selected path estimate><label|rem:ex-wilson-stream-use>The
    one-step path estimate uses the pictures <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|2>> and
    <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|3>> of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-wilson-stream>,
    that is, the Taylor coefficients of degrees three and four. Only the
    finitely many plaquette types enter, each with support in a box of radius
    four, so the finite sum over <math|\<cal-A\>> in the proof is all that is
    needed; no summation over infinitely many interaction terms occurs. The
    two pictures are uniform over torus sides; no limit in the volume is used
    here.
  </remark>

  <subsection|The weighted quadratic row estimate><label|sec:ex-row-quadratic-price>

  The part of the one-step action quadratic in the row field has a uniform
  coefficient price. Its cost is the square of the exponentially weighted row
  budget. The proof is an exact two-index expansion followed by a summable
  Cauchy estimate; no finite-volume truncation of the row family is needed.

  Let <math|<math-up|CTax>> be the iterated Cauchy derivative factor defined
  in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-wilson-vacuum-price>. Let
  <math|\<cal-A\>> be the finite set of plaquette types. Write
  <math|d<rsub|<math-up|col>>> for the number of color coordinates and
  <math|d<rsub|<math-up|mat>>> for the dimension of the matrix
  representation; these constants have different roles below. For each
  <math|a\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, let
  <math|s<rsub|a,i>*<around|(|U;x|)>> be a color vector obtained by
  evaluating one path slot at the translated anchor <math|x>, and let
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|a,i>> be that slot's length. Let
  <math|c<rsub|a,i>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> be its row coefficient, and assume
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|a,i>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>. The color vector takes values in
  the finite-dimensional real Euclidean color space on physical fields; its
  complex extension applies the complex-linear extension of the real color
  projection to half the difference between the forward slot word and its
  reversed word. On real fields this is the original skew-color slot. The
  quadratic extension is bilinear, <math|<big|sum><rsub|c>s<rsub|c>*s<rsub|c>>,
  not a Hermitian norm. Assume the row budget

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-row-budget><big|sum><rsub|i><around|\||c<rsub|a,i>|\|>*e<rsup|\<sigma\>*\<ell\><rsub|a,i>>\<leqslant\>C*<space|1em><around|(|a\<in\>\<cal-A\>|)>,<space|2em>\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation>

  We use the following elementary path bound. For each
  <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0> there are a common complex coordinate radius
  <math|R<rsub|\<sigma\>/2>\<gtr\>0> and a constant <math|S<rsub|G>> such
  that, on the radius-<math|R<rsub|\<sigma\>/2>> ball about the identity,
  every color component of a slot of length <math|\<ell\>> has a holomorphic
  extension bounded by <math|S<rsub|G>*e<rsup|<around|(|\<sigma\>/2|)>*\<ell\>>>.
  More explicitly, if <math|H<rsub|a><around|(|z|)>> is the matrix product
  along the slot walk and <math|H<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|rev>><around|(|z|)>>
  the product along the reversed slot walk, the extension is

  <\equation*>
    s<rsub|a><rsup|\<bbb-C\>><around|(|z|)>=\<Pi\><rsub|G,\<bbb-C\>><space|-0.17em><around*|(|<frac|H<rsub|a><around|(|z|)>-H<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|rev>><around|(|z|)>|2>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<Pi\><rsub|G,\<bbb-C\>>> is the complex-linear extension of
  the real skew-color projection. On real links, the reversed full slot walk
  is the inverse walk; its string matrix is the adjoint of the forward string
  matrix. Their half-difference is skew-adjoint, and the physical color
  projection of this skew matrix is exactly the transported-curvature slot.
  Thus the complex extension agrees with the original real slot on physical
  fields. The two words each contain at most <math|2*\<ell\>> edge factors.
  Choose the radius so that each complexified edge matrix and its inverse has
  operator norm at most <math|e<rsup|\<sigma\>/4>>; this is possible by
  continuity near the compact unitary group. Each word then has norm at most
  <math|e<rsup|<around|(|\<sigma\>/2|)>*\<ell\>>>. Apply the bounded
  complex-linear projection; its norm and the fixed factor from the
  difference are absorbed in <math|S<rsub|G>>. This proves the bound
  uniformly in location and length.

  Define the quadratic row action on a torus of side <math|m> by

  <\equation*>
    Q<rsup|<around|(|2|)>><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>=<frac|1|2>*<big|sum><rsub|x\<in\><around|(|\<bbb-Z\>/m*\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|4>><big|sum><rsub|a\<in\>\<cal-A\>><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|i>c<rsub|a,i>*s<rsub|a,i>*<around|(|U;x|)>|\|><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  The sum over <math|i> converges absolutely on the real compact
  configuration space by <eqref|eq:ex-row-budget> and the path bound. On the
  common complex coordinate ball, the two-index expansion below also
  converges uniformly: its absolute majorant is the product of the two row
  sums with exponent <math|\<sigma\>/2>, which is bounded by the assumed row
  budget. Thus the complexified action is holomorphic there before any
  derivatives or momentum coefficients are taken.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Uniform price of the quadratic row
    action><label|prop:ex-row-quadratic-price>For each
    <math|t\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, put <math|n=t+1> and

    <\equation*>
      \<eta\><rsub|t>=min <space|-0.17em><around*|(|1,<frac|\<sigma\>|8*n<rsup|2>>|)>.
    </equation*>

    There is one coefficient picture <math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|t>> valid at every
    torus side, holomorphic for <math|t> independent complex momenta of width
    <math|\<eta\><rsub|t>>, whose components are bounded by

    <\equation*>
      <frac|<around|\||\<cal-A\>|\|>*d<rsub|<math-up|col>>|2>*C<rsup|2>*S<rsub|G><rsup|2>*<frac|<math-up|CTax><around|(|R<rsub|\<sigma\>/2>,n|)>|n!>.
    </equation*>

    For each ordered list of <math|n> Fourier-mode variations, its
    coefficient is zero unless their total momentum vanishes; in the zero
    case it is the torus cardinality times the value of
    <math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|t>> at the independent momenta.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    For each <math|a,i,j>, expand the squared norm into the two-slot term

    <\equation*>
      \<Gamma\><rsub|a,i,j>*<around|(|U;x|)>=<frac|1|2>*c<rsub|a,i>*c<rsub|a,j>*<big|sum><rsub|c=1><rsup|d<rsub|<math-up|col>>>s<rsub|a,i,c>*<around|(|U;x|)>*s<rsub|a,j,c>*<around|(|U;x|)>.
    </equation*>

    It has support length at most <math|\<ell\><rsub|a,i>+\<ell\><rsub|a,j>>
    and total absolute coefficient <math|d<rsub|<math-up|col>>*<around|\||c<rsub|a,i>*c<rsub|a,j>|\|>/2>.
    Therefore its total weighted activity satisfies, by Tonelli and
    <eqref|eq:ex-row-budget>,

    <\equation*>
      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|a,i,j><frac|d<rsub|<math-up|col>>|2>*<around|\||c<rsub|a,i>*c<rsub|a,j>|\|>*e<rsup|\<sigma\>*<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|a,i>+\<ell\><rsub|a,j>|)>>>|<cell|=<frac|d<rsub|<math-up|col>>|2>*<big|sum><rsub|a><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\||c<rsub|a,i>|\|>*e<rsup|\<sigma\>*\<ell\><rsub|a,i>>|)><rsup|2>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\><frac|<around|\||\<cal-A\>|\|>*d<rsub|<math-up|col>>|2>*C<rsup|2>.>>>>
      </aligned>
    </equation*>

    We now estimate the coefficient of each two-slot term. On the common
    radius-<math|R<rsub|\<sigma\>/2>> ball, the product of its two
    holomorphic slot extensions is bounded by
    <math|S<rsub|G><rsup|2>*e<rsup|<around|(|\<sigma\>/2|)>*<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|a,i>+\<ell\><rsub|a,j>|)>>>.
    The Cauchy derivative estimate on the nested radii
    <math|R<rsub|\<sigma\>/2>/2,\<ldots\>,R<rsub|\<sigma\>/2>/2<rsup|n>>
    contributes <math|<math-up|CTax><around|(|R<rsub|\<sigma\>/2>,n|)>/n>! to
    the normalized order-<math|n> coefficient. Restricting a
    width-<math|\<eta\><rsub|t>> Fourier mode to a support of length
    <math|\<ell\>> costs at most <math|e<rsup|4*n*\<eta\><rsub|t>*\<ell\>>>;
    for <math|n> inputs the total additional factor is at most
    <math|e<rsup|4*n<rsup|2>*\<eta\><rsub|t>*\<ell\>>>. Since
    <math|4*n<rsup|2>*\<eta\><rsub|t>\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>/2>, this factor is
    at most <math|e<rsup|<around|(|\<sigma\>/2|)>*\<ell\>>>. Consequently the
    slot-extension and Fourier-mode factors together are bounded by
    <math|S<rsub|G><rsup|2>*e<rsup|\<sigma\>*<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|a,i>+\<ell\><rsub|a,j>|)>>>.

    The weighted activity sum just computed is thus a summable majorant for
    the series of coefficient pictures, uniformly on the smaller momentum
    strip. The Weierstrass <math|M>-test gives uniform convergence on compact
    subsets; its sum is holomorphic, and Cauchy's integral formula permits
    termwise extraction of each Taylor coefficient. On the real field,
    absolute convergence also permits expanding the row square and
    interchanging the sums, so this summed picture represents exactly
    <math|Q<rsup|<around|(|2|)>><rsub|m>>.

    Finally, translating each local two-slot term over all anchors multiplies
    its Fourier coefficient by the character of the total momentum. The
    character sum on the finite torus is zero at nonzero total momentum and
    equals the torus cardinality at zero total momentum. For each pair atom,
    its finite support lies in a max-distance box of radius
    <math|d=\<ell\><rsub|a,i>+\<ell\><rsub|a,j>>. Compute its local symbol on
    the auxiliary torus <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|2*d+1>>; this box has no
    internal identifications there. Pull any period-<math|m> field back to
    this auxiliary torus by <math|z<around|(|y|)>=z<rsub|m><around|(|<math-up|wrap><rsub|m><around|(|<math-up|lift><space|0.17em>y|)>|)>>.
    The identity holds even when <math|m\<less\>2*d+1>, so repeated links are
    retained. Summing the local symbols over atoms gives one picture
    independent of <math|m>, with the asserted volume factor and price.
  </proof>

  <\corollary>
    <dueto|The averaged path-kernel coefficient
    price><label|cor:ex-row-controlled-action>Suppose the unaveraged path
    kernel has the exact decomposition

    <\equation*>
      Q<rsub|p,R,m>=\<lambda\>*W<rsub|m>+Q<rsup|<around|(|2|)>><rsub|m>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>\<lambda\>\<leqslant\>Z,
    </equation*>

    with <math|Q<rsup|<around|(|2|)>><rsub|m>> as above. Then the unaveraged
    order-<math|n> picture has width <math|\<eta\><rsub|t>> and price at most

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|r*a*w>=Z*A<rsub|t>+<frac|<around|\||\<cal-A\>|\|>*d<rsub|<math-up|col>>|2>*C<rsup|2>*S<rsub|G><rsup|2>*<frac|<math-up|CTax><around|(|R<rsub|\<sigma\>/2>,n|)>|n!>,<space|2em>n=t+1.
    </equation*>

    Let <math|\<cal-S\>> be the finite signed-coordinate group and define the
    average <math|Q<rsup|\<sharp\>><around|(|U|)>=<around|\||\<cal-S\>|\|><rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|s\<in\>\<cal-S\>>Q<rsub|p,R,m>*<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|s>*U|)>>,
    where <math|\<rho\><rsub|s>> is the corresponding signed coordinate
    action on link fluctuations. For this averaged kernel, the common picture
    has width <math|\<eta\><rsub|t>> and price at most

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|a*v*g>=<math-up|ReflectBill><around|(|t,\<eta\><rsub|t>|)>*A<rsub|r*a*w>,<space|2em><math-up|ReflectBill><around|(|t,\<eta\>|)>=exp
      <around*|(|<around|(|t+1|)><rsup|2>*\<eta\>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Both coefficient identities hold at every torus side, with the exact
    zero-total-momentum volume factor.
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    The unaveraged assertion follows by adding the bare-action picture and
    the quadratic picture, after restricting the former to width
    <math|\<eta\><rsub|t>\<leqslant\>1>. For completeness, the signed action
    on a picture entry is

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|<math-up|rot><rsub|s><space|0.17em>L|)><around|(|x,<around|(|j<rsub|r>|)><rsub|r=1><rsup|n>,p|)>=<around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|r=1><rsup|n><math-up|reflectFee><rsub|s><around|(|<math-up|plotted><around|(|p,r|)>,k<rsub|r>|)>|)>*L*<around*|(|x,<around|(|k<rsub|r>|)><rsub|r=1><rsup|n>,<math-up|rotatePhase><rsub|s><around|(|p|)>|)>,
    </equation*>

    Here <math|n=t+1> and <math|j<rsub|r>\<in\><around|{|0,1,2,3|}>> is the
    coordinate axis attached to the <math|r>th slot and
    <math|k<rsub|r>=s<rsub|<math-up|perm>><rsup|-1><around|(|j<rsub|r>|)>> is
    the corresponding input axis, used both in the fee and in the original
    picture's axis argument. Thus the original picture is evaluated on the
    tuple <math|<around|(|k<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,k<rsub|n>|)>>. Each factor
    <math|<math-up|reflectFee><rsub|s>> is either <math|1> or minus the
    Fourier character <math|exp <around|(|i*<around|\<langle\>|p,-e<rsub|s<around|(|k<rsub|r>|)>>|\<rangle\>>|)>>
    of one unit direction. On the width-<math|\<eta\><rsub|t>> strip, each
    independent momentum has imaginary coordinates bounded by
    <math|\<eta\><rsub|t>>, and the plotted momentum (including the dependent
    <math|p<rsub|0>=-<big|sum><rsub|r=1><rsup|t>p<rsub|r>>) has imaginary
    coordinates bounded by <math|n*\<eta\><rsub|t>>. Thus each such factor
    has price at most <math|e<rsup|n*\<eta\><rsub|t>>>. The product of the
    <math|n> factors therefore costs at most
    <math|e<rsup|n<rsup|2>*\<eta\><rsub|t>>>. The map
    <math|p\<mapsto\><math-up|rotatePhase><rsub|s><around|(|p|)>> applies the
    signed coordinate action <math|s<rsup|-1>> independently to each
    independent momentum; the dependent momentum transforms by the same
    action because it is minus their sum. This preserves the coordinate strip
    and incurs no additional exponential price. This is precisely the picture
    transformation formula: the character factors account for the translated
    base point in the signed coordinate action, and the remaining coefficient
    is the original picture evaluated on the reindexed directions and
    transformed momenta. Hence each rotated picture has price at most
    <math|<math-up|ReflectBill><around|(|t,\<eta\><rsub|t>|)>*A<rsub|r*a*w>>.
    The normalized finite signed average preserves the same bound by the
    triangle inequality. Linearity of the coefficient identity shows that the
    averaged picture represents the averaged kernel and retains the same
    zero-momentum volume factor. This is the exact orientation-average step
    needed for the input kernel.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Soft response of the cubic and quartic curvature
  terms><label|sec:ex-soft-curvature-receipts>

  This section supplies the order-two and order-three response estimates used
  by the selected one-loop route. The argument has two parts. First, the
  absolute path budgets give common analytic pictures for the cubic and
  quartic terms, uniformly over the volume sequence. Second, a finite
  gauge-variation identity removes the unwanted low-momentum coefficients.
  The latter step is needed: analyticity and an absolute activity bound alone
  do not imply the extra powers of momentum.

  <subsubsection|Summable local atoms and their coefficient pictures>

  An atom of degree <math|r> consists of <math|r> transported curvature slots
  and a finite coefficient array <math|c<rsub|\<alpha\>>>, where
  <math|\<alpha\>> records one color coordinate for each slot. Write
  <math|d<around|(|a|)>> for the sum of the slot lengths and

  <\equation*>
    b<rsub|\<sigma\>><around|(|a|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>><around|\||c<rsub|\<alpha\>>|\|>*e<rsup|\<sigma\>*d<around|(|a|)>>.
  </equation*>

  The corresponding local function is the polynomial in those <math|r> slot
  values. For a sequence <math|a<rsub|i>>, its absolute activity budget is

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|i>b<rsub|\<sigma\>><around|(|a<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>A.
  </equation*>

  Gauge invariance means that simultaneous adjoint rotation of all slot
  arguments leaves each polynomial unchanged. These are precisely the two
  properties of the cubic and quartic streams used below.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Uniform coefficient picture for an atom
    stream><label|prop:ex-atom-stream-picture>Fix <math|r,t\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    and <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0>. There are constants
    <math|\<eta\><rsub|r,t>\<gtr\>0> and <math|C<rsub|r,t>\<less\>\<infty\>>,
    depending only on <math|G,r,t,\<sigma\>>, such that the following holds.
    If <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>b<rsub|\<sigma\>><around|(|a<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>A>,
    then the translation sum of the local atom functions has an exact
    order-<math|<around|(|t+1|)>> Fourier coefficient picture
    <math|P<rsub|r,t>>. Its components are holomorphic on the independent
    momentum strip of width <math|\<eta\><rsub|r,t>>, have uniform bound

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|r,t>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<eta\><rsub|r,t>>\<leqslant\>A*C<rsub|r,t>,
    </equation*>

    and represent the finite-volume Taylor coefficient on every torus. At
    nonzero total momentum the coefficient vanishes; at zero total momentum
    it is the torus cardinality times the picture value. The bound is
    independent of the torus side and of the number of atoms.

    If the atoms are gauge invariant, the same conclusion holds for their
    orientation-averaged action, with the price multiplied by

    <\equation*>
      exp <around|(|<around|(|t+1|)><rsup|2>*\<eta\><rsub|r,t>|)>.
    </equation*>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The one-slot estimate in the proof of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-row-quadratic-price> gives, on one
    common complex ball about the physical identity, a bound

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||s<rsub|\<nu\>,\<alpha\><rsub|\<nu\>>><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>S<rsub|G>*e<rsup|<around|(|\<sigma\>/2|)>*\<ell\><rsub|\<nu\>>>.
    </equation*>

    It was obtained from the complexified skew-color projection of the
    forward slot string minus its reverse; thus it applies to the real color
    coordinates and their complex-linear extension. Multiplying the <math|r>
    slot bounds and summing the coefficient magnitudes gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||a<rsub|i><rsup|\<bbb-C\>><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>S<rsub|G><rsup|r>*b<rsub|\<sigma\>/2><around|(|a<rsub|i>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The complex extension is entire in the finitely many link variables in
    the atom's stencil. Cauchy's derivative estimate on nested balls of radii
    <math|R,R/2,\<ldots\>,R/2<rsup|t+1>> bounds the normalized
    order-<math|<around|(|t+1|)>> derivative by

    <\equation*>
      b<rsub|\<sigma\>/2><around|(|a<rsub|i>|)>*S<rsub|G><rsup|r>*<frac|<math-up|circleTax><around|(|R,t+1|)>|<around|(|t+1|)>!>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|<math-up|circleTax><around|(|R,0|)>=1> and
    <math|<math-up|circleTax><around|(|R,k+1|)>=<math-up|circleTax><around|(|R,k|)>/<around|(|R/2<rsup|k+1>|)>>;
    the factorial is the Taylor coefficient normalization.

    The local coefficient picture inserts one Fourier character for each of
    the <math|t+1> differentiated slots. A slot support lies in the
    four-dimensional box of radius at most <math|d<around|(|a<rsub|i>|)>>. On
    a momentum strip of width <math|\<eta\>>, the imaginary part of each
    plotted momentum is bounded coordinatewise by
    <math|<around|(|t+1|)>*\<eta\>>. Hence the product of the <math|t+1>
    characters is bounded by

    <\equation*>
      exp <around*|(|4*<around|(|t+1|)><rsup|2>*\<eta\>*d<around|(|a<rsub|i>|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Choose <math|\<eta\>=\<sigma\>/<around|(|8*<around|(|t+1|)><rsup|2>|)>>,
    so <math|4*<around|(|t+1|)><rsup|2>*\<eta\>=\<sigma\>/2>. The resulting
    term price is bounded by

    <\equation*>
      b<rsub|\<sigma\>/2><around|(|a<rsub|i>|)>*S<rsub|G><rsup|r>*<frac|<math-up|circleTax><around|(|R,t+1|)>|<around|(|t+1|)>!>*e<rsup|<around|(|\<sigma\>/2|)>*d<around|(|a<rsub|i>|)>>=C<rsub|r,t>*b<rsub|\<sigma\>><around|(|a<rsub|i>|)>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|C<rsub|r,t>=S<rsub|G><rsup|r><math-up|circleTax><around|(|R,t+1|)>/<around|(|t+1|)>>!.
    Thus the coefficient pictures form a normally convergent series on every
    smaller strip. The same majorant on the common complex link ball permits
    termwise differentiation of the atom series before extracting any
    momentum coefficient. This proves analyticity and the claimed uniform
    bound.

    For the finite-volume identity, translate the local atom over all
    anchors. The sum of its characters is the finite-torus character sum: it
    is zero unless the total momentum is zero, and equals the number of sites
    when the total momentum is zero. The local picture is defined on a cover
    box large enough to contain the stencil, then pulled back by the wrapping
    map. This keeps the identity exact even on small tori where different
    cover points wrap to the same link. Absolute summability just proved
    permits exchanging the atom sum, derivative, and finite anchor sum.

    For an orientation <math|s>, the picture transforms by multiplying the
    <math|t+1> reflected-character fees and evaluating the original picture
    at the inverse-permuted direction labels and transformed independent
    momenta. Each fee has strip price at most
    <math|e<rsup|<around|(|t+1|)>*\<eta\>>>, because its plotted momentum has
    coordinatewise imaginary parts bounded by
    <math|<around|(|t+1|)>*\<eta\>>. The product therefore costs at most
    <math|e<rsup|<around|(|t+1|)><rsup|2>*\<eta\>>>. The normalized finite
    orientation average preserves this bound by the triangle inequality, and
    its coefficient identity is the average of the exact finite-volume
    identities. This proves the final assertion.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The finite gauge-variation identity>

  A coefficient picture of order <math|t> records the normalized
  <math|<around|(|t+1|)>>st Taylor coefficient. For an external color word
  <math|v>, color run <math|a>, and independent momentum tuple <math|p>, its
  one-leg contraction at leg <math|j> is

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|\<cal-C\><rsub|j>*P|)><rsub|v,a><around|(|p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>q*<around|(|p<rsub|j>,\<mu\>|)>*P<rsub|v,<math-up|update><around|(|a,j,\<mu\>|)>><around|(|p|)>,<space|2em>q*<around|(|p<rsub|j>,\<mu\>|)>=e<rsup|i*p<rsub|j,\<mu\>>>-1,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<cal-A\>> is the finite set of spatial axes. The color word
  <math|v> is held fixed; the sum is over the spatial difference axis. Here
  <math|j> ranges over all <math|t+1> legs and
  <math|p<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,p<rsub|t>> denote the external momenta of the
  tuple: <math|p<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,p<rsub|t>> are the independent momenta and
  <math|p<rsub|0>=-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|t>p<rsub|i>>.

  A function of the independent momenta has ray order at least <math|s> if,
  for every real tuple <math|p>, its value at <math|\<lambda\>*p> is
  <math|O<around|(|<around|\||\<lambda\>|\|><rsup|s>|)>> as the real
  parameter <math|\<lambda\>> tends to zero.

  Gauge invariance of an action <math|F> on link fluctuations is used in the
  following form. For a site field <math|\<theta\>> and a link fluctuation
  <math|u>, both Lie-algebra valued, let <math|g*<around|(|\<theta\>,u|)>> be
  the gauge transform of <math|exp u> by <math|exp \<theta\>> in logarithmic
  link coordinates: on the link from <math|x> in direction <math|\<mu\>>,

  <\equation*>
    g*<around|(|\<theta\>,u|)><rsub|x,\<mu\>>=log <around*|(|exp
    <around|(|\<theta\><rsub|x>|)>*exp <around|(|u<rsub|x,\<mu\>>|)>*exp
    <around|(|\<theta\><rsub|x+e<rsub|\<mu\>>>|)><rsup|-1>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Then <math|F*<around|(|g*<around|(|\<theta\>,u|)>|)>=F<around|(|u|)>> for
  all <math|<around|(|\<theta\>,u|)>> near zero. The map <math|g> is given on
  every link of every torus by one and the same analytic function
  <math|\<omega\>> of the three arguments
  <math|<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|x>,u<rsub|x,\<mu\>>,\<theta\><rsub|x+e<rsub|\<mu\>>>|)>>.
  Its first derivative at zero is <math|D<rsub|u>*g*<around|(|0,0|)>=I> and
  <math|D<rsub|\<theta\>>*g*<around|(|0,0|)>*\<theta\>=-d<rsub|0>*\<theta\>>,
  where <math|<around|(|d<rsub|0>*\<theta\>|)><rsub|x,\<mu\>>=\<theta\><rsub|x+e<rsub|\<mu\>>>-\<theta\><rsub|x>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Ward partition identity><label|lem:ex-ward-partition>Let
    <math|m<rsub|k>\<to\>\<infty\>>, and let <math|F<rsub|k>> be real
    analytic, gauge invariant actions with
    <math|D*F<rsub|k><around|(|0|)>=0>. Suppose an order-<math|t> picture
    <math|P> is holomorphic and uniformly bounded on a momentum strip and
    records the <math|<around|(|t+1|)>>st coefficient of <math|F<rsub|k>>
    exactly on every torus of the sequence. Suppose further that for each
    <math|b=2,\<ldots\>,t> there is one order-<math|<around|(|b-1|)>> picture
    <math|P<rsub|b-1>>, holomorphic and bounded on a momentum strip, which
    records the <math|b>th coefficient of <math|F<rsub|k>> for every <math|k>
    and each of whose entries has ray order at least <math|s>. Then each
    contraction <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|j>*P> has ray order at least <math|s>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Fix the leg <math|j>, the color word <math|v> and the run <math|a>. On
    the torus of side <math|m<rsub|k>> take mesh momenta
    <math|p<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,p<rsub|t>> with zero sum, and differentiate the
    identity <math|F<rsub|k>*<around|(|g*<around|(|\<theta\>,u|)>|)>=F<rsub|k><around|(|u|)>>
    once in each of <math|t+1> directions: in slot <math|j> the direction is
    the site wave of momentum <math|p<rsub|j>> and color <math|v<rsub|j>>,
    and in every other slot <math|i> it is the link wave of momentum
    <math|p<rsub|i>>, axis <math|a<rsub|i>> and color <math|v<rsub|i>>. The
    right side gives zero, because its slot <math|j> has no link component.
    The left side is, by the chain rule, the finite sum over the set
    partitions <math|\<pi\>> of the <math|t+1> slots, the blocks being
    ordered by their least slot, of <math|D<rsup|b>*F<rsub|k><around|(|0|)>>
    applied to the block derivatives of <math|g>; here <math|b> is the number
    of blocks of <math|\<pi\>>.

    The all-singleton partition contributes the first derivative of <math|g>
    in each slot. In slot <math|j> this is <math|-d<rsub|0>> of the site
    wave, whose link components are <math|-q*<around|(|p<rsub|j>,\<mu\>|)>>
    times the link waves of momentum <math|p<rsub|j>> and axis <math|\<mu\>>;
    in the other slots it is the link wave itself. By the exact coefficient
    record this term equals <math|-<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|m<rsub|k>>|\|>*<around|(|t+1|)>!<space|0.17em><around|(|\<cal-C\><rsub|j>*P|)><rsub|v,a>>
    at the independent momenta. The one-block term vanishes since
    <math|D*F<rsub|k><around|(|0|)>=0>.

    Now let <math|\<pi\>> have <math|b> blocks
    <math|\<pi\><rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,\<pi\><rsub|b>> with
    <math|2\<leqslant\>b\<leqslant\>t>. Since <math|g> is given by the one
    local function <math|\<omega\>> on every link, the derivative of <math|g>
    of order <math|<around|\||\<pi\><rsub|r>|\|>> applied to waves of momenta
    <math|<around|(|p<rsub|i>|)><rsub|i\<in\>\<pi\><rsub|r>>> is the link
    wave of momentum <math|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>\<pi\><rsub|r>>p<rsub|i>>
    whose amplitude, at axis <math|\<mu\>> and color <math|c>, is the
    corresponding component of <math|D<rsup|<around|\||\<pi\><rsub|r>|\|>>*\<omega\><around|(|0|)>>
    evaluated on the triples <math|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|i>,\<upsilon\><rsub|i>,e<rsup|i*p<rsub|i,\<mu\>>>*\<alpha\><rsub|i>|)>>
    formed from the site and link amplitudes of the inserted waves. Call this
    amplitude <math|c<rsub|\<pi\>,r>*<around|(|\<mu\>,c;p|)>>. It is a
    polynomial in the phases <math|e<rsup|i*p<rsub|i,\<mu\>>>>, so it is a
    continuous function of real momenta, and it does not depend on the
    period. The lower picture <math|P<rsub|b-1>> records the <math|b>th
    coefficient of every <math|F<rsub|k>>, and the block momenta sum to zero.
    Hence the contribution of <math|\<pi\>> is
    <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|m<rsub|k>>|\|>*<space|0.17em>g<rsub|\<pi\>>*<around|(|p<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,p<rsub|t>|)>>,
    where

    <\equation*>
      g<rsub|\<pi\>><around|(|p|)>=b!<big|sum><rsub|\<b-mu\>,\<b-up-c\>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|r=1><rsup|b>c<rsub|\<pi\>,r>*<around|(|\<mu\><rsub|r>,c<rsub|r>;p|)>|)><around|(|P<rsub|b-1>|)><rsub|\<b-up-c\>,\<b-mu\>><around*|(|<with|math-display|false|<around*|\<nobracket\>|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>\<pi\><rsub|2>>p<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>\<pi\><rsub|b>>p<rsub|i>|)>>|\<nobracket\>>
    </equation*>

    is one function for all <math|k>; in these sums <math|p<rsub|0>> stands
    for <math|-<big|sum><rsub|i\<geqslant\>1>p<rsub|i>>. It is defined and
    continuous at every real tuple. Its arguments in <math|P<rsub|b-1>> are
    linear in <math|p>, so along a real ray through zero the picture factor
    is <math|O<around|(|<around|\||\<lambda\>|\|><rsup|s>|)>>, while the
    continuous multipliers stay bounded. Thus <math|g<rsub|\<pi\>>> has ray
    order at least <math|s>.

    Dividing the differentiated identity by the positive factor
    <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|m<rsub|k>>|\|>>, which is common to the
    top and to every lower term, we obtain on the period mesh

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|t+1|)>!<space|0.17em><around|(|\<cal-C\><rsub|j>*P|)><rsub|v,a><around|(|p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<pi\>>g<rsub|\<pi\>><around|(|p|)>,
    </equation*>

    the sum being over the partitions with <math|2\<leqslant\>b\<leqslant\>t>
    blocks. Every real momentum tuple is a limit of mesh tuples along
    <math|m<rsub|k>\<to\>\<infty\>>: round the <math|t> independent momenta
    to the period mesh and let the remaining momentum <math|p<rsub|0>> be
    their negative sum. The mesh momenta are determined modulo
    <math|2*\<pi\>>, and both sides are evaluated at this real
    representative; periodicity of the pictures makes the choice immaterial.
    Both sides are continuous at real tuples, the left one because <math|P>
    is holomorphic on a strip. The identity therefore holds at every real
    tuple. The finite sum on the right has ray order <math|s>, proving the
    claim.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Recovering powers from the contracted
    kernel><label|lem:ex-ward-rays>Assume the picture entries are analytic at
    zero. For every fixed color word and run with at least one independent
    momentum variable, contractions of ray order at least two imply that each
    entry has ray order at least one. If the contractions have ray order at
    least three and there are at least two independent momentum blocks
    (equivalently, at least three external legs), then each entry has ray
    order at least two.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The first-order expansion <math|q*<around|(|\<lambda\>*p<rsub|j>,\<mu\>|)>=i*\<lambda\>*p<rsub|j,\<mu\>>+O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|2>|)>>
    shows, after dividing a contraction of order two by <math|\<lambda\>>,
    that

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>p<rsub|j,\<mu\>>*P<rsub|v,<math-up|update><around|(|a,j,\<mu\>|)>><around|(|0|)>=0.
    </equation*>

    Choose <math|p> supported at the unique axis which updates
    <math|a<rsub|j>> back to itself. Then <math|P<rsub|v,a><around|(|0|)>=0>.
    Analyticity gives ray order one.

    For a contraction of order three, the next coefficient, using that
    constant terms vanish, is

    <\equation>
      <big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>p<rsub|j,\<mu\>>*D*P<rsub|v,<math-up|update><around|(|a,j,\<mu\>|)>><around|(|0|)><around|[|p|]>=0.<label|eq:soft-curvature-receipts-display-WR>
    </equation>

    Fix leg <math|j> and a vector <math|w> whose <math|j>th block is zero.
    Let <math|q> be the coordinate unit vector on leg <math|j> at the axis
    that updates <math|a<rsub|j>> back to itself. Evaluate
    <eqref|eq:soft-curvature-receipts-display-WR> at <math|q+w> and subtract
    its value at <math|q>. Only the selected axis remains, giving
    <math|D*P<rsub|v,a><around|(|0|)><around|[|w|]>=0>. The equation at
    <math|q> also gives <math|D*P<rsub|v,a><around|(|0|)><around|[|q|]>=0>.
    Thus the derivative vanishes on all vectors transverse to leg <math|j>.
    With two distinct legs, split any vector into a part supported on the
    first and a part with that leg zero; apply the transverse conclusion
    using the other leg for the first part and the first leg for the second.
    The full differential vanishes. Together with the vanishing constant
    term, this gives ray order two.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Cubic and quartic terms>

  Let <math|A<rsup|<around|(|3|)>><rsub|i>> and
  <math|A<rsup|<around|(|4|)>><rsub|i>> be gauge-invariant atom streams of
  degrees three and four, each with activity budget at most <math|A> at width
  <math|\<sigma\>>. Write <math|T<rsub|r>> for their finite
  signed-orientation-averaged translation sums.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Two soft pictures for the curvature
    sum><label|prop:ex-extra-curves>If <math|m<rsub|k>\<to\>\<infty\>>, there
    are <math|\<eta\><rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0> and
    <math|K<rsub|\<ast\>>\<less\>\<infty\>>, depending only on
    <math|G,\<sigma\>>, such that

    <\equation*>
      F<rsub|k>=T<rsub|3><around|(|m<rsub|k>|)>+T<rsub|4><around|(|m<rsub|k>|)>
    </equation*>

    has exact recorded pictures <math|P<rsub|2>> of order <math|2> and
    <math|P<rsub|3>> of order <math|3> with uniform price at most
    <math|A*K<rsub|\<ast\>>>. The first picture has vanishing order <math|2>;
    the second has vanishing order <math|1>. The functions <math|F<rsub|k>>
    are analytic, gauge invariant, and vanish to order at least three at
    zero.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Apply Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-atom-stream-picture> to each of
    the four choices

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|r,t|)>=<around|(|3,2|)>,<around|(|3,3|)>,<around|(|4,2|)>,<around|(|4,3|)>.
    </equation*>

    For each fixed <math|t>, add the degree-three and degree-four pictures.
    Their recording identities add exactly, and their prices add. Choose the
    minimum of their four positive widths and the sum of their four finite
    price constants; this yields one <math|\<eta\><rsub|\<ast\>>> and one
    <math|K<rsub|\<ast\>>>, uniform over the stream and all periods.

    The individual atom series vanish to at least their degree at zero,
    because each transported curvature slot vanishes there. Absolute
    convergence of the differentiated series preserves these vanishing
    orders, so <math|F<rsub|k>> has order at least three. Gauge invariance is
    preserved by the finite orientation average. The zero picture records its
    second Taylor coefficient, and the zero first derivative is automatic.
    Thus the order-two picture supplies the lower-order coefficient needed
    for the order-three Ward argument.

    Apply Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-ward-partition> with <math|t=2> to the
    order-two picture. The zero lower coefficient and the order-three
    vanishing of <math|F<rsub|k>> give contractions of order three.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-ward-rays> then upgrades the order-two
    picture to vanishing order two. For the order-three picture, apply
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-ward-partition> with <math|t=3>; the zero
    second coefficient and the order-two picture just obtained supply the
    lower-order data at block lengths two and three. The contractions vanish
    to order two, and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-ward-rays> gives vanishing
    order one for the order-three picture. These are the two claimed Ray
    orders.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The curvature stream and the Wilson subtraction>

  We fix the objects. On the torus of side <math|m> a link fluctuation
  <math|u> assigns a Lie-algebra element to every positively oriented link,
  and <math|U=exp u> is the corresponding configuration. Let
  <math|\<varrho\>> be the defining unitary representation, of dimension
  <math|d<rsub|G>>, and normalize the inner product on the Lie algebra by
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<varrho\><rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|c|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=d<rsub|G><around|\||c|\|><rsup|2>>,
  with the Hilbert\USchmidt norm on the left. The plaquette energy is
  <math|\<varpi\><around|(|g|)>=1-<math-up|Retr><space|0.17em>\<varrho\><around|(|g|)>/d<rsub|G>=<around|\<\|\|\>|\<varrho\><around|(|g|)>-1|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>/<around|(|2*d<rsub|G>|)>>,
  and the Wilson action is <math|W<rsub|m><around|(|u|)>=<big|sum><rsub|f>\<varpi\><around|(|U<rsub|f>|)>>,
  summed over the plaquettes <math|f> of the torus with holonomies
  <math|U<rsub|f>>. The curvature of a group element is
  <math|\<kappa\><around|(|g|)>=\<Pi\><rsub|G><around*|(|<tfrac|1|2><around|(|\<varrho\><around|(|g|)>-\<varrho\><around|(|g|)><rsup|-1>|)>|)>>,
  where <math|\<Pi\><rsub|G>> is the projection onto the Lie algebra, so that
  <math|\<kappa\><around|(|exp c|)>=c+O<around|(|<around|\||c|\|><rsup|2>|)>>.
  A slot consists of a lattice walk from the origin and a plaquette type; its
  value <math|s*<around|(|U;x|)>> at the anchor <math|x> is the curvature of
  the plaquette of that type at the endpoint of the translated walk,
  transported back to <math|x> by the adjoint action of the holonomy along
  the walk.

  A path family <math|R> consists of rows, a loop stream, and cubic and
  quartic atom streams <math|R<rsup|<around|(|3|)>>,R<rsup|<around|(|4|)>>>.
  For each plaquette type <math|b> the row is a sequence of real coefficients
  <math|c<rsub|b,i>> and slots <math|s<rsub|b,i>>. Its row field at the
  plaquette <math|f=<around|(|x,b|)>> is the sum
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|R>*<around|(|U;f|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i>c<rsub|b,i>*s<rsub|b,i>*<around|(|U;x|)>>;
  it converges absolutely when <math|R> satisfies the path bound stated
  below, which is assumed whenever the row field is used. A clip <math|p>
  supplies the direct coefficient <math|p.<math-up|direct>\<gtr\>0>. The
  unaveraged quadratic path action is

  <\equation*>
    Q<rsub|p,R,m><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|f><around*|(|p.<math-up|direct><space|0.17em>\<varpi\><around|(|U<rsub|f>|)>+<tfrac|1|2><around|\||\<ell\><rsub|R>*<around|(|U;f|)>|\|><rsup|2>|)>=p.<math-up|direct><space|0.17em>W<rsub|m>+Q<rsup|<around|(|2|)>><rsub|m>,
  </equation*>

  in the notation of Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:ex-row-controlled-action>.
  Let <math|\<cal-S\>> be the finite signed-coordinate group acting on
  configurations by <math|U\<mapsto\>\<rho\><rsub|s>*U>. For a period
  sequence <math|m<rsub|k>> put

  <\equation*>
    J<rsub|k><around|(|u|)>=<frac|1|<around|\||\<cal-S\>|\|>>*<big|sum><rsub|s\<in\>\<cal-S\>>Q<rsub|p,R,m<rsub|k>>*<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|s>*exp
    u|)>,<space|2em>C<rsub|k>=T<rsub|3><around|(|m<rsub|k>|)>+T<rsub|4><around|(|m<rsub|k>|)>,<space|2em>W<rsub|k>=W<rsub|m<rsub|k>>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|T<rsub|3>,T<rsub|4>> are the signed-orientation-averaged
  translation sums of the atom streams <math|R<rsup|<around|(|3|)>>,R<rsup|<around|(|4|)>>>,
  as in the preceding subsection. The selected action is

  <\equation*>
    I<rsub|k>=<math-up|intended><around|(|p,R;m<rsub|k>|)>=J<rsub|k>+C<rsub|k>.
  </equation*>

  The loop stream of <math|R> is not part of <math|I<rsub|k>>. The path bound
  <math|R.<math-up|Bounds><around|(|p,\<tau\>,A,\<Lambda\>|)>>, recalled in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:path-attachment-producer>, provides the row
  budgets <math|<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\||c<rsub|b,i>|\|>*e<rsup|\<tau\>*\<ell\><rsub|b,i>>\<leqslant\>A>,
  where <math|\<ell\><rsub|b,i>> is the length of the slot
  <math|s<rsub|b,i>>, the cubic and quartic activity budgets at most <math|A>
  at the same width <math|\<tau\>>, and the invariance of every atom.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Curvature stream pictures><label|prop:ex-veneer>Fix
    <math|\<tau\>\<gtr\>0>. There are constants
    <math|\<eta\><rsub|c>\<gtr\>0>, <math|K<rsub|c>\<less\>\<infty\>>,
    depending only on <math|G> and <math|\<tau\>>, such that the following
    holds. If <math|m<rsub|k>\<to\>\<infty\>> and the cubic and quartic
    streams are invariant with activity budgets at most <math|A> at width
    <math|\<tau\>>, then for each <math|j=1,2,3> the sequence
    <math|<around|(|C<rsub|k>|)><rsub|k>> has an exactly recorded picture of
    order <math|j>, holomorphic on the strip of width <math|\<eta\><rsub|c>>,
    of ray order at least <math|4-j>, with price at most <math|A*K<rsub|c>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-extra-curves>, applied at width
    <math|\<tau\>>, supplies the order-two and order-three pictures. For
    order two its vanishing order is two, as required. For order three its
    vanishing order is one. The order-one picture is identically zero:
    <math|C<rsub|k>> vanishes to third order, so its second Taylor
    coefficient is zero. The zero picture records it exactly, its price is
    zero, and it has every ray order. Taking the common minimum width and the
    maximum of the two prices proves the assertion.
  </proof>

  We next describe the metric hypotheses. A bed <math|B> consists of five
  real coefficient arrays on <math|\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>, each indexed by pairs
  of plaquette types: the metric <math|H>, the diagonal metric
  <math|<wide|H|~>>, designated inverses of these two, and the root <math|r>.
  For such an array <math|E> write <math|E<rsub|m>> for its periodization to
  the torus of side <math|m>, a matrix acting on two-form fields, whose
  entries are the sums of <math|E> over residue classes modulo <math|m>.
  Since the arrays of a bed are arbitrary, these sums, and the value
  <math|<wide|H|^><around|(|0|)>=<big|sum><rsub|x>H<around|(|x|)>> occurring
  in the floor condition, are defined by the following convention: the sum of
  a countable family of real numbers is its unconditional sum if the family
  is summable, which for real families is the same as absolutely summable,
  and is <math|0> otherwise. The floor and sworn conditions below are
  conditions on the matrices so defined. In this section the symbol of
  <math|H> is used only under a strip bound on <math|H>, where all these
  series converge absolutely. If <math|E> is entrywise absolutely summable,
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|x><around|\||E<rsub|b*b<rprime|'>><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<less\>\<infty\>>
  for every pair of types, write <math|<wide|E|^><around|(|p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|x>e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\>x>*E<around|(|x|)>>
  for its symbol; then <math|E<rsub|m>> multiplies a Fourier wave of mesh
  momentum <math|k> by <math|<wide|E|^><around|(|k|)>>. The strip bound
  <math|E.<math-up|strip><around|(|\<sigma\>,M|)>> means
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|x>e<rsup|\<sigma\><around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>><around|\||E<rsub|b*b<rprime|'>><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M>
  for every pair of types. For <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0> it implies absolute
  summability, and that every entry of <math|<wide|E|^>> is holomorphic and
  bounded in modulus by <math|M> on the strip of width <math|\<sigma\>>.

  <\itemize>
    <item><math|B> is <em|attached> to <math|R> if, for all plaquette types
    <math|b,b<rprime|'>> and every <math|y\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>, the
    coefficients <math|c<rsub|b,i>> of the slots of row <math|b> with
    plaquette type <math|b<rprime|'>> and walk endpoint <math|y> sum to
    <math|r<rsub|b*b<rprime|'>><around|(|y|)>>.

    <item><math|<math-up|Floor><around|(|B,p,m,\<varepsilon\>,M|)>> is the
    conjunction of the following conditions at the side <math|m>: the
    designated inverses are inverse to <math|H<rsub|m>> and
    <math|<wide|H|~><rsub|m>>; <math|H<rsub|m>=p.<math-up|direct><space|0.17em>I+r<rsub|m><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*r<rsub|m>>;
    <math|<wide|H|^><around|(|0|)>=I>; <math|H<rsub|m>*d<rsub|1>=<wide|H|~><rsub|m>*d<rsub|1>>,
    with <math|d<rsub|1>> the lattice curl from one-forms to two-forms;
    <math|H<rsub|m>> and <math|<wide|H|~><rsub|m>> are symmetric; and both
    satisfy <math|\<varepsilon\><around|\||v|\|><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|\<langle\>|v,H<rsub|m>*v|\<rangle\>>\<leqslant\>M<around|\||v|\|><rsup|2>>,
    respectively the same with <math|<wide|H|~><rsub|m>>, for every real
    two-form <math|v>.

    <item>The <em|sworn> condition at the side <math|m> says that the
    quadratic form

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-K\><rsub|m>*<around|(|x,y|)>=<big|sum><rsub|f><around|\<langle\>|<around|(|d<rsub|1>*x|)><rsub|f>,<around|(|H<rsub|m>*d<rsub|1>*y|)><rsub|f>|\<rangle\>>
    </equation*>

    on Lie-algebra valued one-forms is invariant under the signed-coordinate
    group: <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|m>*<around|(|x<rsup|s>,y<rsup|s>|)>=\<cal-K\><rsub|m>*<around|(|x,y|)>>
    for <math|s\<in\>\<cal-S\>>, where <math|x\<mapsto\>x<rsup|s>> is the
    linear action for which <math|\<rho\><rsub|s>*exp x=exp x<rsup|s>>.
  </itemize>

  Here <math|H<rsub|m>> acts on each color component, in an orthonormal basis
  of the Lie algebra, and <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<cdummy\>,\<cdummy\>|\<rangle\>>>
  is the color inner product. Of the floor conditions, this section uses only
  the root identity, the normalization <math|<wide|H|^><around|(|0|)>=I> and
  the symmetry of <math|H<rsub|m>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Quadratic coefficients of the Wilson and path
    actions><label|lem:ex-soft-quadratic-hessian>Let <math|m> be a torus
    side.

    <\enumerate>
      <item>On every torus, <math|W<rsub|m><around|(|0|)>=0>,
      <math|D*W<rsub|m><around|(|0|)>=0> and

      <\equation*>
        D<rsup|2>*W<rsub|m><around|(|0|)><around|[|x,y|]>=<big|sum><rsub|f><around|\<langle\>|<around|(|d<rsub|1>*x|)><rsub|f>,<around|(|d<rsub|1>*y|)><rsub|f>|\<rangle\>>.
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|R> satisfy the path bound with parameters
      <math|<around|(|p,\<tau\>,A,\<Lambda\>|)>> and let <math|B> be attached
      to <math|R>. Suppose that <math|<math-up|Floor><around|(|B,p,m,\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>
      and the sworn condition hold at the side <math|m>. Then the signed
      average <math|J> of <math|Q<rsub|p,R,m>> satisfies

      <\equation*>
        J<around|(|0|)>=0,<space|2em>D*J<around|(|0|)>=0,<space|2em>D<rsup|2>*J<around|(|0|)><around|[|x,y|]>=\<cal-K\><rsub|m>*<around|(|x,y|)>.
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Both functions are real analytic near zero, by the holomorphic extensions
    constructed for Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-wilson-stream> and
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-row-quadratic-price>. We use the
    following elementary fact. Let <math|f> be twice continuously
    differentiable near zero in a finite-dimensional space, and let
    <math|\<cal-Q\>> be a symmetric bilinear form such that
    <math|f*<around|(|\<lambda\>*a|)>/\<lambda\><rsup|2>\<to\>\<cal-Q\>*<around|(|a,a|)>/2>
    as <math|\<lambda\>\<to\>0>, for every <math|a>. Then
    <math|f<around|(|0|)>=0>, <math|D*f<around|(|0|)>=0> and
    <math|D<rsup|2>*f<around|(|0|)>=\<cal-Q\>>. Indeed the second-order
    Taylor formula along the line through <math|a> forces
    <math|f<around|(|0|)>=0>, then <math|D*f<around|(|0|)>*a=0>, then
    <math|D<rsup|2>*f<around|(|0|)><around|[|a,a|]>=\<cal-Q\>*<around|(|a,a|)>>,
    and polarization of the two symmetric forms gives the last claim.

    Fix a one-form <math|a> and put <math|U<around|(|\<lambda\>|)>=exp
    <around|(|\<lambda\>*a|)>>. The holonomy
    <math|V<rsub|f><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>> around a plaquette <math|f> is a
    product of four factors <math|exp <around|(|\<pm\>\<lambda\>*a<rsub|e>|)>>,
    so <math|V<rsub|f><around|(|0|)>=1> and
    <math|<frac|d|d*\<lambda\>>*\<varrho\><around|(|V<rsub|f><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>|)>\|<rsub|\<lambda\>=0>=\<varrho\><rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|<around|(|d<rsub|1>*a|)><rsub|f>|)>>.
    By the normalization of the color inner product,

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsup|-2>*\<varpi\><around|(|V<rsub|f><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>|)>=<frac|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<lambda\><rsup|-1>*<around|(|\<varrho\><around|(|V<rsub|f><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>|)>-1|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>|2*d<rsub|G>>\<longrightarrow\><tfrac|1|2><around|\||<around|(|d<rsub|1>*a|)><rsub|f>|\|><rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    Summing over the finitely many plaquettes and applying the elementary
    fact with <math|\<cal-Q\>*<around|(|x,y|)>=<big|sum><rsub|f><around|\<langle\>|<around|(|d<rsub|1>*x|)><rsub|f>,<around|(|d<rsub|1>*y|)><rsub|f>|\<rangle\>>>
    proves the first part.

    For the second part consider one slot <math|s> at an anchor <math|x>, and
    let <math|f<rprime|'>> be the torus plaquette it reads. Its value at
    <math|U<around|(|\<lambda\>|)>> is <math|\<kappa\>*<around|(|T<around|(|\<lambda\>|)>*V<rsub|f<rprime|'>><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>*T<around|(|\<lambda\>|)><rsup|-1>|)>>,
    where <math|T<around|(|\<lambda\>|)>> is the holonomy along the walk and
    <math|T<around|(|0|)>=1>. The derivative at zero of the conjugated
    holonomy is that of <math|V<rsub|f<rprime|'>>>, hence

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsup|-1>*s*<around|(|U<around|(|\<lambda\>|)>;x|)>\<longrightarrow\><around|(|d<rsub|1>*a|)><rsub|f<rprime|'>>.
    </equation*>

    Moreover, the adjoint action is isometric and <math|V<rsub|f<rprime|'>>>
    ranges over the finitely many plaquettes of the torus. Hence there is a
    constant <math|b<rsub|a>>, for instance
    <math|1+<big|sum><rsub|f><around|\||<around|(|d<rsub|1>*a|)><rsub|f>|\|>>,
    such that <math|<around|\||\<lambda\><rsup|-1>*s*<around|(|U<around|(|\<lambda\>|)>;x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|a>>
    for all sufficiently small <math|\<lambda\>\<neq\>0>, simultaneously for
    all slots and anchors. The row budget gives
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\||c<rsub|b,i>|\|>\<leqslant\>A>.
    Dominated convergence therefore yields, for every plaquette
    <math|f=<around|(|x,b|)>>,

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsup|-1>*\<ell\><rsub|R>*<around|(|U<around|(|\<lambda\>|)>;f|)>\<longrightarrow\>\<ell\><rsub|<math-up|path>>*<around|(|f,a|)>\<assign\><big|sum><rsub|i>c<rsub|b,i>*<space|0.17em><around|(|d<rsub|1>*a|)><rsub|f<rsub|b,i><around|(|x|)>>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|f<rsub|b,i><around|(|x|)>> is the plaquette read by the slot
    <math|s<rsub|b,i>> at the anchor <math|x>. Consequently

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsup|-2>*Q<rsub|p,R,m><around|(|U<around|(|\<lambda\>|)>|)>\<longrightarrow\><tfrac|1|2><big|sum><rsub|f><around*|(|p.<math-up|direct><space|0.17em>\|<around|(|d<rsub|1>*a|)><rsub|f>\|<rsup|2>+<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|<math-up|path>>*<around|(|f,a|)>|\|><rsup|2>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The right side equals <math|<tfrac|1|2>\<cal-K\><rsub|m>*<around|(|a,a|)>>.
    This is the path-to-root computation of
    Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-lattice-horizon-path>: grouping
    the absolutely summable row by the plaquette read and using attachment
    gives <math|\<ell\><rsub|<math-up|path>>*<around|(|f,a|)>=<around|[|r<rsub|m>*<around|(|d<rsub|1>*a|)>|]><around|(|f|)>>
    in each color component, and the root identity
    <math|H<rsub|m>=p.<math-up|direct><space|0.17em>I+r<rsub|m><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*r<rsub|m>>
    of the floor then gives

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-K\><rsub|m>*<around|(|a,a<rprime|'>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|f><around*|(|p.<math-up|direct><space|0.17em><around|\<langle\>|<around|(|d<rsub|1>*a|)><rsub|f>,<around|(|d<rsub|1>*a<rprime|'>|)><rsub|f>|\<rangle\>>+<around|\<langle\>|\<ell\><rsub|<math-up|path>>*<around|(|f,a|)>,\<ell\><rsub|<math-up|path>>*<around|(|f,a<rprime|'>|)>|\<rangle\>>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Finally <math|\<rho\><rsub|s>*exp <around|(|\<lambda\>*a|)>=exp
    <around|(|\<lambda\>*a<rsup|s>|)>>, so the same limit applies to each
    term of the signed average, with <math|a<rsup|s>> in place of <math|a>:

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsup|-2>*J*<around|(|\<lambda\>*a|)>\<longrightarrow\><frac|1|<around|\||\<cal-S\>|\|>>*<big|sum><rsub|s\<in\>\<cal-S\>><tfrac|1|2>\<cal-K\><rsub|m>*<around|(|a<rsup|s>,a<rsup|s>|)>=<tfrac|1|2>\<cal-K\><rsub|m>*<around|(|a,a|)>,
    </equation*>

    the last equality being the sworn condition. The form
    <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|m>> is symmetric because <math|H<rsub|m>> is. The
    elementary fact proves the second part.
  </proof>

  The Hessian formulas of the lemma become coefficient pictures as follows.
  Let <math|d<rsub|1><around|(|p|)>> be the symbol of the lattice curl, a
  matrix from link directions to plaquette types whose entries are <math|0>
  or <math|\<pm\>q*<around|(|p,\<mu\>|)>>. Let <math|E> be one of the arrays
  above, assumed entrywise absolutely summable, or the unit array <math|I>,
  which is the identity matrix at the origin and zero elsewhere; its symbol
  is the identity and it has strip bound <math|<around|(|\<sigma\>,1|)>> for
  every <math|\<sigma\>>. Define the order-one picture

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|S><around|[|E|]><rsub|\<b-up-c\>,\<b-up-a\>><around|(|p|)>=<tfrac|1|2><space|0.17em>\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|c<rsub|0>=c<rsub|1>|}>>*<around*|[|d<rsub|1>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*<wide|E|^><around|(|p|)>*<space|0.17em>d<rsub|1><around|(|p|)>|]><rsub|a<rsub|0>*a<rsub|1>>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|p> is the one independent momentum, <math|\<b-up-c\>> the pair
  of colors and <math|\<b-up-a\>> the pair of link directions. The transpose
  is algebraic; for real <math|p>, <math|d<rsub|1>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>=d<rsub|1><around|(|p|)><rsup|\<ast\>>>.
  The factor <math|<tfrac|1|2>> is the Taylor normalization of the second
  coefficient. Inserting two Fourier waves of mesh momenta
  <math|k<rsub|0>,k<rsub|1>> into the complex-bilinear extensions of the
  forms of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-soft-quadratic-hessian>, with colors
  <math|c<rsub|0>,c<rsub|1>> and link directions <math|a<rsub|0>,a<rsub|1>>,
  gives zero unless <math|k<rsub|0>+k<rsub|1>=0> and
  <math|c<rsub|0>=c<rsub|1>>, and otherwise the number of sites times the
  displayed bracket at <math|p=k<rsub|1>>, with <math|<wide|E|^>=I> for the
  Wilson action and <math|<wide|E|^>=<wide|H|^>> for <math|J>; the normalized
  second coefficient is half of this. Thus <math|<math-ss|S><around|[|I|]>>,
  the unit leader, records the second Taylor coefficient of <math|W<rsub|m>>
  exactly on every torus. Under the hypotheses of the lemma, and assuming in
  addition that the metric <math|H> is entrywise absolutely summable, the
  metric-slot picture <math|<math-ss|S><around|[|H|]>> records that of
  <math|J> exactly on the torus of side <math|m>. In the applications below
  this additional condition follows from <math|H.<math-up|strip><around|(|\<sigma\>,C|)>>
  with <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0>. If <math|E.<math-up|strip><around|(|\<sigma\>,D|)>>,
  then each entry of <math|<math-ss|S><around|[|E|]>> is bounded on the strip
  of width <math|\<sigma\>> by

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|bedCharge><around|(|\<sigma\>,D|)>=36\<cdot\><around*|(|4*<around|(|e<rsup|\<sigma\>>+1|)>|)><rsup|2>*D,
  </equation*>

  the product of the two counts of plaquette types and the elementary strip
  bounds for the two curl factors; it is independent of the torus side. Each
  curl factor is <math|O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|>|)>> along real rays, so
  <math|<math-ss|S><around|[|E|]>> has ray order at least two.

  Now impose the selected metric hypotheses: <math|B> is attached to
  <math|R>; its metric satisfies <math|H.<math-up|strip><around|(|\<sigma\>,C|)>>;
  the floor holds at every torus side; and the sworn condition holds at each
  selected side <math|m<rsub|k>>. The following are the only uses of these
  assumptions. The metric-slot picture <math|<math-ss|S><around|[|H|]>> has
  price <math|b<rsub|0>=<math-up|bedCharge><around|(|\<sigma\>,C|)>> and
  records the quadratic coefficient of <math|J<rsub|k>> for every <math|k>.
  The unit leader has price <math|b<rsub|1>=<math-up|bedCharge><around|(|\<sigma\>,1|)>>
  and records the Wilson coefficient. The floor gives the zero-momentum
  normalization <math|<wide|H|^><around|(|0|)>=I>, and analyticity then gives
  <math|<wide|H|^><around|(|p|)>-I=O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|>|)>>. The
  difference

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|S><around|[|H|]>-<math-ss|S><around|[|I|]>=<tfrac|1|2><space|0.17em>\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|c<rsub|0>=c<rsub|1>|}>>*<around*|[|d<rsub|1>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*<around|(|<wide|H|^><around|(|p|)>-I|)>*<space|0.17em>d<rsub|1><around|(|p|)>|]>
  </equation*>

  therefore has ray order three. This is the order-one picture for
  <math|J<rsub|k>-W<rsub|k>>, with price <math|b<rsub|0>+b<rsub|1>>.

  The higher pictures of <math|J<rsub|k>> are supplied by
  Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:ex-row-controlled-action>, whose averaged
  kernel <math|Q<rsup|\<sharp\>>> is <math|J<rsub|k>> in logarithmic
  coordinates. Apply it with its row budget equal to <math|A>, its width
  equal to the path width <math|\<tau\>>, and the bound <math|Z> for the
  direct coefficient. For <math|j=2,3> it gives one picture of order
  <math|j>, recording the <math|<around|(|j+1|)>>st coefficient of
  <math|J<rsub|k>> on every torus, holomorphic on the strip of width
  <math|\<eta\><rsub|j>=min <around|(|1,\<tau\>/<around|(|8*<around|(|j+1|)><rsup|2>|)>|)>>,
  with price

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|j>=e<rsup|<around|(|j+1|)><rsup|2>*\<eta\><rsub|j>>*<around*|(|Z*X<rsub|j>+<frac|<around|\||\<cal-A\><rsub|<math-up|pl>>|\|>*d<rsub|<math-up|col>>|2>*A<rsup|2>*S<rsub|G><rsup|2>*<frac|<math-up|circleTax><around|(|R<rsub|\<tau\>/2>,j+1|)>|<around|(|j+1|)>!>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|<around|\||\<cal-A\><rsub|<math-up|pl>>|\|>> is the number of
  plaquette types, <math|d<rsub|<math-up|col>>> the number of color
  coordinates, and <math|X<rsub|j>> the price of the Wilson picture of order
  <math|j> in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-wilson-stream>. That
  proposition supplies the Wilson pictures of orders two and three
  themselves, with prices <math|X<rsub|2>,X<rsub|3>> and width one.
  Subtracting pictures preserves exact recording and adds their prices.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Soft Wilson pictures><label|lem:ex-soft-wilson-pictures>Let
    <math|m<rsub|k>\<to\>\<infty\>>. For <math|j=1,2,3> the Wilson actions
    <math|W<rsub|m<rsub|k>>> have an exactly recorded picture of order
    <math|j>, holomorphic and bounded on the strip of width one, of ray order
    at least <math|3-j>. The sum of the three prices is at most
    <math|P<rsub|W>=<math-up|bedCharge><around|(|1,1|)>+X<rsub|2>+X<rsub|3>>,
    which does not depend on the sequence.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For <math|j=1> take the unit leader <math|<math-ss|S><around|[|I|]>>. It
    records the second coefficient by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-soft-quadratic-hessian>,
    has price <math|<math-up|bedCharge><around|(|1,1|)>> at width one, and
    has ray order two. For <math|j=2> take the picture of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-wilson-stream>. The Wilson action is
    real analytic and gauge invariant, and <math|D*W<rsub|m><around|(|0|)>=0>
    by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-soft-quadratic-hessian>. Apply
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-ward-partition> with <math|t=2>; its single
    lower picture is the unit leader, of ray order two. The contractions
    therefore have ray order two, and the first part of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-ward-rays> gives ray order one. For
    <math|j=3> take the order-three picture of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-wilson-stream>; ray order zero only
    requires boundedness near zero.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The selected Wilson-subtracted response><label|prop:ex-greyson>Fix
    <math|\<sigma\>,\<tau\>\<gtr\>0>, <math|C,Z,A\<geqslant\>0> and
    <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>. There are <math|s\<gtr\>0> and
    <math|M\<less\>\<infty\>> such that the following holds for every
    divergent period sequence <math|m<rsub|k>>, bed <math|B>, clip <math|p>,
    path family <math|R> and <math|\<Lambda\>>. Assume that <math|R> has path
    bounds <math|<around|(|p,\<tau\>,A,\<Lambda\>|)>>, that <math|B> is
    attached to <math|R> with metric strip bound
    <math|<around|(|\<sigma\>,C|)>>, that
    <math|<math-up|Floor><around|(|B,p,n,\<varepsilon\>,C|)>> holds at every
    torus side <math|n>, that the sworn condition holds at each
    <math|m<rsub|k>>, and that

    <\equation*>
      p.<math-up|direct>\<leqslant\>Z.
    </equation*>

    Then <math|<around|(|I<rsub|k>-W<rsub|k>|)><rsub|k>> has exactly recorded
    pictures of orders <math|j=1,2,3>, each holomorphic on the strip of width
    <math|s> with price at most <math|M>, and of ray order at least
    <math|4-j>. One valid price is

    <\equation*>
      M=<math-up|bedCharge><around|(|\<sigma\>,C|)>+<math-up|bedCharge><around|(|\<sigma\>,1|)>+P<rsub|2>+P<rsub|3>+X<rsub|2>+X<rsub|3>+A*K<rsub|c>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|P<rsub|2>,P<rsub|3>> are the two row-action prices displayed
    above, <math|X<rsub|2>,X<rsub|3>> the Wilson prices of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-wilson-stream>, and <math|K<rsub|c>>
    is from Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-veneer> at the width
    <math|\<tau\>>. One valid width is the minimum of <math|\<sigma\>>,
    <math|1>, the two row widths <math|\<eta\><rsub|2>,\<eta\><rsub|3>>, and
    <math|\<eta\><rsub|c>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Set <math|F<rsub|k>=J<rsub|k>-W<rsub|k>>. Let
    <math|S<rsub|1>=<math-ss|S><around|[|H|]>-<math-ss|S><around|[|I|]>> be
    the metric-slot picture minus the unit leader picture. It records the
    second Taylor coefficient of <math|F<rsub|k>> for every <math|k>, has
    price <math|b<rsub|0>+b<rsub|1>>, and has vanishing order three by the
    floor normalization <math|<wide|H|^><around|(|0|)>=I>. In the notation of
    the Ward lemma it is the lower picture of order one for <math|F<rsub|k>>.
    Let <math|S<rsub|2>> be the order-two picture of <math|J<rsub|k>> from
    Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:ex-row-controlled-action> minus the
    order-two Wilson picture, and let <math|S<rsub|3>> be the corresponding
    order-three picture minus the order-three Wilson picture. They exactly
    record the third and fourth Taylor coefficients of <math|F<rsub|k>> and
    have prices at most <math|P<rsub|2>+X<rsub|2>> and
    <math|P<rsub|3>+X<rsub|3>>.

    The action <math|F<rsub|k>> is real analytic and has zero first
    derivative by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-soft-quadratic-hessian>. It is
    gauge invariant: a gauge transformation conjugates every plaquette
    holonomy and rotates every slot value, hence every row field
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|R>*<around|(|U;f|)>>, by the adjoint action of the
    gauge element at the anchor, which preserves <math|\<varpi\>> and the
    color norm; and the signed-coordinate action carries gauge
    transformations to gauge transformations. For <math|t=2>, apply
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-ward-partition> to <math|S<rsub|2>>, with
    the single lower picture <math|S<rsub|1>>; the resulting contraction has
    vanishing order three, and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-ward-rays> gives
    vanishing order two for <math|S<rsub|2>>. For <math|t=3>, apply the
    partition lemma to <math|S<rsub|3>>, using <math|S<rsub|1>> and the now
    improved <math|S<rsub|2>> at its two lower block counts. The contraction
    has vanishing order two, so the same recovery lemma gives vanishing order
    one for <math|S<rsub|3>>. This supplies the exact three records and
    prices required for <math|F<rsub|k>>.

    The cubic and quartic terms <math|C<rsub|k>> have the order-one,
    order-two, and order-three pictures of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-veneer>. Add the order-<math|j>
    picture of <math|C<rsub|k>> to the order-<math|j> picture of
    <math|F<rsub|k>> for each <math|j=1,2,3>. The sum records
    <math|F<rsub|k>+C<rsub|k>=I<rsub|k>-W<rsub|k>> exactly. Its price is
    bounded by the displayed <math|M>, and its vanishing order is at least
    <math|4-j>, since the order of a sum is at least the minimum order of its
    summands. Taking the common minimum strip width gives <math|s>.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|From coefficient pictures to local response arrays>

  We state the finite conversion in its concrete two-sided form. For
  <math|j=1,2,3>, a coefficient picture <math|P<rsub|j>> records exactly the
  normalized <math|<around|(|j+1|)>>st derivative of <math|U<rsub|m>> on
  every finite torus. It is uniformly priced at width <math|s> if each entry
  is holomorphic and bounded by the same constant on the independent-momentum
  strip of width <math|s>. It has ray order <math|r> if, on every real ray
  through zero, each entry is <math|O<around|(|<around|\||\<lambda\>|\|><rsup|r>|)>>.
  For an analytic entry this means every Taylor homogeneous polynomial below
  degree <math|r> vanishes: evaluating that polynomial on each real direction
  gives zero, hence the polynomial is identically zero. Thus ray order
  <math|r> supplies exactly the Taylor-vanishing hypothesis used in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-soft-factorization>. The required input at
  level <math|j> is ray order <math|3-j>.

  For <math|n=0,1,2>, an external color address is an
  <math|<around|(|n+2|)>>-tuple of one-link color-coordinate labels, each a
  pair consisting of a spatial axis and a Lie-color basis index. The
  multilinear test fields <math|w<rsub|r>> carry the corresponding link and
  site dependence. The stitch address set <math|<math-ss|St><rsub|n>> is the
  set of ordered <math|<around|(|2-n|)>>-tuples from
  <math|<around|{|0,\<ldots\>,n|}>\<times\>\<cal-A\>>. The fixed rule
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|n>> routes each key <math|<around|(|r,\<mu\>|)>> to its
  assigned response leg and axis: for <math|n=0> the two keys are routed to
  legs zero and one; for <math|n=1> its single key
  <math|<around|(|r,\<mu\>|)>> is routed to leg <math|r+1>; and for
  <math|n=2> there are no keys. Let <math|<math-ss|Shift><rsub|n>> be the
  integer lattice on the <math|<around|(|n+1|)>> independent momentum-axis
  coordinates. A response array is a map

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|n>:<math-ss|Color><rsub|n+2>\<times\><math-ss|St><rsub|n>\<times\><math-ss|Shift><rsub|n>\<longrightarrow\>\<bbb-C\>.
  </equation*>

  Its width at <math|\<eta\>> is the supremum, over the finite color and
  stitch addresses, of

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|z\<in\><math-ss|Shift><rsub|n>>e<rsup|\<eta\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>*<around|\||F<rsub|n>*<around|(|b,i,z|)>|\|>.
  </equation*>

  The stitched Fourier picture is

  <\equation>
    <wide|Q|~><rsub|n>*<around|(|b,p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><math-ss|St><rsub|n>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|r=0><rsup|n+1><big|prod><rsub|e\<in\>\<ell\><rsub|n><around|(|i|)><rsub|r>><around|(|e<rsup|i<space|0.17em><math-up|plot><rsub|r><around|(|p|)>\<cdot\>e>-1|)>|)>*<big|sum><rsub|z>F<rsub|n>*<around|(|b,i,z|)>*e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\>z>.<label|eq:soft-curvature-receipts-display-SR1>
  </equation>

  Here <math|<math-up|plot><rsub|r><around|(|p|)>> are the <math|n+2>
  external momenta: the first is minus the sum of the <math|n+1> independent
  momenta, and the remaining ones are those independent momenta in order. For
  <math|n=0> the two listed differences are distributed one per leg; for
  <math|n=1> there is one difference, and for <math|n=2> there are none.

  To state the response identity, split a link fluctuation into two species
  <math|x<rsub|+>,x<rsub|->> and let <math|<math-ss|D><rsub|m><around|(|x|)>>
  be the logarithmic link coordinate of the product of the two exponentials:
  on every link <math|e>,

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|D><rsub|m><around|(|x<rsub|+>,x<rsub|->|)><rsub|e>=log
    <around*|(|exp <around|(|x<rsub|+,e>|)>*exp <around|(|x<rsub|-,e>|)>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|log> is the local inverse of <math|exp> at the identity and
  <math|x<rsub|\<pm\>>> are near zero. Let <math|v<rsub|n>> put species
  <math|-> in the first <math|n> slots and species <math|+> in the final two.
  Let <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|n,m><around|(|U<rsub|m>|)>> be the
  <math|<around|(|n+2|)>>-linear derivative of
  <math|U<rsub|m>\<circ\><math-ss|D><rsub|m>> at zero, restricted to those
  species in that order; it is a tensor whose components are indexed by
  external color addresses <math|b>. For a list of axes
  <math|\<ell\>=<around|(|e<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,e<rsub|r>|)>>, let
  <math|\<Delta\><rsub|\<ell\>>=<around|(|T<rsub|e<rsub|1>>-I|)>*\<cdots\>*<around|(|T<rsub|e<rsub|r>>-I|)>>
  be the corresponding product of translated one-link differences. For a test
  tuple <math|w=<around|(|w<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,w<rsub|n+1>|)>>, define

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|Local><rsub|m,\<ell\><rsub|n><around|(|i|)>>(F<rsub|n>(b,i,\<cdot\><around|)||)><around|[|w|]>=<big|sum><rsub|z>F<rsub|n>*<around|(|b,i,z|)>*<big|sum><rsub|x\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|m>><big|prod><rsub|r=0><rsup|n+1><around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|\<ell\><rsub|n><around|(|i|)><rsub|r>>*w<rsub|r>|)><around|(|x+<math-up|wrap><rsub|m><around|(|<math-up|lane><rsub|r><around|(|z|)>|)>,b<rsub|r>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|<math-up|lane><rsub|0><around|(|z|)>=0>, while
  <math|<math-up|lane><rsub|r><around|(|z|)>> for <math|r\<geqslant\>1> is
  the shift in the <math|<around|(|r-1|)>>st independent momentum coordinate.
  The width bound makes the Laurent sum absolutely convergent. The response
  identity required below is componentwise and multilinear:

  <\equation>
    <around|(|\<cal-R\><rsub|n,m><around|(|U<rsub|m>|)>|)><rsub|b><around|[|w|]>=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><math-ss|St><rsub|n>><math-up|Local><rsub|m,\<ell\><rsub|n><around|(|i|)>>(F<rsub|n>(b,i,\<cdot\><around|)||)><around|[|w|]>*<space|2em><around|(|b\<in\><math-ss|Color><rsub|n+2>|)>.<label|eq:soft-curvature-receipts-display-SR2>
  </equation>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Finite difference division><label|lem:ex-soft-factorization>Let
    <math|E> be a finite set of phase coordinates. Suppose <math|f> is
    <math|2*\<pi\>>-periodic in each coordinate and holomorphic, bounded by
    <math|A> on a strip of width <math|s\<gtr\>0>, and its Taylor expansion
    at zero has no terms of degree below <math|q>. Then on a narrower strip
    it has a finite representation

    <\equation*>
      f<around|(|p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|<around|(|a<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,a<rsub|q>|)>\<in\>E<rsup|q>><big|prod><rsub|r=1><rsup|q><around|(|e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a<rsub|r>>>-1|)>*<space|0.17em>g<rsub|a<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,a<rsub|q>><around|(|p|)>,
    </equation*>

    where the residual functions are holomorphic on width at least
    <math|s/2<rsup|q>> and uniformly bounded there by
    <math|A*C*<around|(|E,q,s|)>>. For <math|q=0> take the one-term
    representation <math|g=f>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For <math|J\<subseteq\>E>, write <math|p<rsup|<around|[|J|]>>> for
    <math|p> with the coordinates in <math|J> set to zero. For each <math|a>,
    periodicity implies that <math|f<around|(|p|)>-f<around|(|p<rsup|<around|[|<around|{|a|}>|]>>|)>>
    vanishes whenever <math|p<rsub|a>\<in\>2*\<pi\>*\<bbb-Z\>>. Consequently
    it is divisible, as a holomorphic periodic function, by
    <math|d<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>=e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>-1>. The quotient is
    removable at every zero. On a fundamental real period cell, away from
    fixed neighborhoods of these zeros the denominator is bounded below;
    inside those neighborhoods the quotient is bounded by Cauchy's estimate
    on a narrower strip. Thus

    <\equation*>
      f<around|(|p|)>-f<around|(|p<rsup|<around|[|<around|{|a|}>|]>>|)>=d<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>*g<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>
    </equation*>

    with a uniform bound for <math|g<rsub|a>>. In the telescoping identity
    over <math|J>, replace <math|f> at each step by the function with the
    previously selected coordinates staged to zero. Each corresponding
    quotient lowers the vanishing order by one. Induction on <math|q> applied
    to these residuals therefore yields the displayed finite sum. At most
    <math|<around|\||E|\|><rsup|q>> terms occur; at each division, Cauchy's
    estimate on the current strip of width <math|r> bounds the quotient on
    width <math|r/2>; after <math|q> divisions the residual strip has width
    <math|s/2<rsup|q>>. Each step multiplies the bound by a constant
    depending only on <math|E,q,s>. The fully staged term is zero by the
    order-<math|q> hypothesis. This proves the result.
  </proof>

  For the local response array, the number of factors is <math|q=2-n>, not
  <math|n>. If <math|n=1,2>, apply the factorization to every picture entry
  with <math|q=2-n> differences and place them on the prescribed stitch legs.
  For <math|n=0>, two factors are required, one on each of the two response
  legs. The division first gives the two factors in the single independent
  momentum; the second is moved to the other leg by the exact identity

  <\equation*>
    q*<around|(|p,\<mu\>|)>=-<math-up|mode><around|(|p,<math-up|step><space|0.17em>\<mu\>|)>*q*<around|(|-p,\<mu\>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The phase is absorbed into the Laurent coefficient, with its strip cost
  included in the constant. Thus this construction yields precisely the three
  stitch patterns: two factors for <math|n=0>, one for <math|n=1>, and none
  for <math|n=2>.

  To control the Laurent width, let <math|g> be one residual, bounded by
  <math|B> on a strip of width <math|\<eta\>>. Shifting the contour in each
  periodic coordinate gives <math|<around|\||<wide|g|^><around|(|\<nu\>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>B*e<rsup|-\<eta\><around|\||\<nu\>|\|><rsub|1>>>.
  At width <math|\<eta\>/2>,

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>e<rsup|<around|(|\<eta\>/2|)><around|\||\<nu\>|\|><rsub|1>><around|\||<wide|g|^><around|(|\<nu\>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>B<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>>e<rsup|-<around|(|\<eta\>/2|)><around|\||k|\|>>|)><rsup|<around|\||E|\|>>=B*<around*|(|1+<frac|2|e<rsup|\<eta\>/2>-1>|)><rsup|<around|\||E|\|>>.
  </equation*>

  Thus each residual defines a Laurent entry with a quantitative width bound;
  summing the at most <math|<around|\||E|\|><rsup|q>> residuals multiplies it
  by that finite factor. This is the coefficient-norm estimate used in the
  array construction.

  We now verify the response identity and its price using the actual
  mixed-field composition. Let <math|D<around|(|x|)>> be the local map which
  forms the product of the two split fields and then takes its logarithmic
  link coordinate. For a Boolean word <math|v> on the external slots, the
  response of <math|U<rsub|m>> is the corresponding mixed derivative of
  <math|U<rsub|m>\<circ\>D> at zero. At level <math|n>, the word has <math|n>
  entries of the first species followed by two entries of the second species;
  its length is <math|n+2>.

  For a set partition <math|\<pi\>> of these <math|n+2> slots into <math|b>
  nonempty blocks, order the blocks canonically by their least slot. The
  chain rule gives the derivative of <math|U<rsub|m>> of order <math|b>
  evaluated at the sums of external momenta in the blocks, multiplied by the
  derivatives of <math|D> on each block. The single-block term is zero
  because <math|D*U<rsub|m><around|(|0|)>=0>. For
  <math|2\<leqslant\>b\<leqslant\>n+2>, the action coefficient has picture
  order <math|b-1>. Its available ray order is <math|3-<around|(|b-1|)>=4-b>,
  which is at least <math|2-n>. Thus every term in the mixed response picture
  has ray order <math|2-n> and is analytic on a common narrower strip.

  More explicitly, for a partition <math|\<pi\>> and a basis phase assignment
  <math|w>, the corresponding mixed coefficient is

  <\equation*>
    b!<big|sum><rsub|a<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,a<rsub|b>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|r=1><rsup|b>c<rsub|\<pi\>,r>*<around|(|a<rsub|r>;w<rsub|r>|)>|)>*P<rsub|b-1>*<around*|(|a<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,a<rsub|b>;<with|math-display|false|<around*|\<nobracket\>|<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>\<pi\><rsub|1>>p<rsub|j>,\<ldots\>,<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>\<pi\><rsub|b>>p<rsub|j>|)>,>|\<nobracket\>>
  </equation*>

  where <math|c<rsub|\<pi\>,r>*<around|(|a<rsub|r>;w<rsub|r>|)>> is the
  coordinate coefficient of the derivative of <math|D> on block <math|r>. The
  factorial is the normalization between the symmetric derivative picture and
  the raw <math|b>th action derivative; it does not enumerate permutations of
  the blocks. If the input pictures are bounded by <math|A>, its absolute
  price is bounded by

  <\equation*>
    A*b!<big|sum><rsub|a<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,a<rsub|b>><big|prod><rsub|r=1><rsup|b><around|\||c<rsub|\<pi\>,r>*<around|(|a<rsub|r>;w<rsub|r>|)>|\|>.
  </equation*>

  Summing the absolute coefficient multiplier (the displayed sum without the
  input price <math|A>) over partitions, color basis labels, and phase basis
  labels defines the response constant <math|P<rsub|n>>. These coefficients
  are uniformly finite: the map <math|<math-ss|D><rsub|m>> is the same
  pointwise product-and-log map on each link. On a fixed identity
  neighborhood in the finite-dimensional gauge group, this map is analytic
  and bounded. Cauchy's estimate on a smaller ball bounds every derivative of
  order at most four by a constant depending only on the group and the order.
  The number of partitions and color/phase labels is finite, so
  <math|P<rsub|n>\<less\>\<infty\>> independently of <math|m>. The common
  analytic strip for the mixed picture can be chosen as

  <\equation*>
    s<rsub|n>=<frac|s|<around|(|n+3|)><rsup|2>>.
  </equation*>

  Every independent external momentum has imaginary coordinates at most
  <math|s<rsub|n>>, and the missing momentum, their negative sum, has
  coordinates at most <math|<around|(|n+1|)>*s<rsub|n>>. A block sum of at
  most <math|n+2> external momenta therefore has imaginary coordinates at
  most <math|<around|(|n+2|)>*<around|(|n+1|)>*s<rsub|n>\<less\>s>. Thus the
  input pictures can be evaluated at every block sum. The mixed picture is
  bounded by <math|A*P<rsub|n>> on width <math|s<rsub|n>> when the input
  picture prices are at most <math|A>.

  For each entry of the mixed picture <math|<wide|Q|~><rsub|n>>, finite
  difference division now produces the prescribed <math|2-n> stitch factors
  and residual functions on width <math|d<rsub|n>=s<rsub|n>/2<rsup|2-n>>.
  Shifting each contour within the smaller strip of width <math|d<rsub|n>/2>
  gives Laurent coefficients bounded by <math|B*e<rsup|-<around|(|d<rsub|n>/2|)><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>>
  when the residual supremum is <math|B>. At array width <math|d<rsub|n>/4>,
  their weighted sum is bounded by

  <\equation*>
    B*<around*|(|1+<frac|2|e<rsup|d<rsub|n>/4>-1>|)><rsup|D<rsub|n>>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|D<rsub|n>> is the finite number of independent Laurent
  coordinates. The number of partitions and addresses is finite, so all
  residuals assemble into a response array of width at most
  <math|A*C<rsub|n>> for a constant <math|C<rsub|n>> depending only on the
  finite labels and input width.

  To check exactness, evaluate the factorization on a finite torus. Each
  Laurent term is absolutely summable by its weighted width. Its spatial
  translation sum is a finite character sum; orthogonality makes it zero
  unless total momentum is zero, in which case it equals the number of sites.
  The coefficient picture recording identity then identifies the sum with the
  mixed derivative of <math|U<rsub|m>\<circ\>D>. This is the exact
  finite-volume response identity, not only equality of Taylor polynomials.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Three-level response arrays><label|prop:ex-three-level-conversion>Let
    <math|U<rsub|m>> be real analytic, with <math|U<rsub|m><around|(|0|)>=0>
    and <math|D*U<rsub|m><around|(|0|)>=0>. Suppose it has exact coefficient
    pictures <math|P<rsub|j>> at orders <math|j=1,2,3>, whose entries are
    <math|2*\<pi\>>-periodic in each phase coordinate, bounded on one common
    strip of width <math|s\<gtr\>0>, and have ray orders at least <math|3-j>.
    Then there are a common positive strip width <math|\<eta\><rsub|\<ast\>>>
    and a finite constant <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>> such that the response
    arrays at levels <math|n=0,1,2> satisfy
    <eqref|eq:soft-curvature-receipts-display-SR1>, the exact finite-period
    response identity, and width at most <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>> times the sum
    of the three input picture prices, uniformly in <math|m>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    For each <math|n>, first form the mixed picture <math|<wide|Q|~><rsub|n>>
    by the ordered-partition chain rule. Every term is the input picture
    <math|P<rsub|<around|\||b|\|>-1>> at the block-summed momenta, multiplied
    by the block coefficients. The one-block term is zero. Since
    <math|P<rsub|<around|\||b|\|>-1>> has ray order
    <math|4-<around|\||b|\|>\<geqslant\>2-n>, the mixed picture has ray order
    <math|2-n>. Apply the finite difference lemma to
    <math|<wide|Q|~><rsub|n>> with <math|q=2-n> and assign the factors to the
    prescribed legs; for the two-leg split use the phase identity above. The
    finite character sum proves the response identity. The Laurent estimate
    bounds the width. For each <math|n=0,1,2>, let <math|C<rsub|n>> be the
    finite price multiplier from the ordered-partition and division
    construction, including the Laurent coefficient sum. For the common input
    strip <math|s>, the mixed-picture strip at level <math|n> is
    <math|s<rsub|n>=s/<around|(|n+3|)><rsup|2>>, and the residual strip is
    <math|d<rsub|n>=s<rsub|n>/2<rsup|2-n>>. Choose

    <\equation*>
      \<eta\><rsub|\<ast\>>=min<rsub|0\<leqslant\>n\<leqslant\>2><around|(|d<rsub|n>/4|)>,<space|2em>C<rsub|\<ast\>>=C<rsub|0>+C<rsub|1>+C<rsub|2>.
    </equation*>

    Then all arrays have width at most <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>> times the sum
    of the three input picture prices. These finite constants and the
    positive width are independent of the period and action.
  </proof>

  The division and response construction above outputs an array of exactly
  this type. The factorization proves <eqref|eq:soft-curvature-receipts-display-SR1>
  entrywise. The finite-volume character identity proves
  <eqref|eq:soft-curvature-receipts-display-SR2>; Laurent absolute
  convergence justifies the sum over shifts. Thus the exact response is
  supplied at each finite period, not merely in the limiting Taylor series.

  Apply the conversion to <math|I<rsub|k>=<around|(|I<rsub|k>-W<rsub|k>|)>+W<rsub|k>>
  along a divergent period sequence. Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-greyson>
  supplies pictures with ray orders <math|3,2,1> at orders <math|1,2,3>. By
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-soft-wilson-pictures> the Wilson pictures have
  ray orders <math|2,1,0>, so the sums have the required orders <math|2,1,0>.
  Their exact records and prices add. The value and first derivative of
  <math|I<rsub|k>> vanish at zero by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-soft-quadratic-hessian>
  and the third-order vanishing of the curvature part <math|C<rsub|k>>. We
  obtain three uniformly priced response arrays. Let <math|M<rsub|g>> denote
  the displayed price in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-greyson>, and
  let <math|P<rsub|W>> be the price of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-soft-wilson-pictures>.
  Each of the three input pictures has price at most
  <math|M<rsub|g>+P<rsub|W>> on the common strip of width <math|min
  <around|(|1,s|)>>, so the common array width is bounded by
  <math|K=3*C<rsub|\<ast\>>*<around|(|M<rsub|g>+P<rsub|W>|)>>, where
  <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>> is the multiplier of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-three-level-conversion> for that strip.

  Define the mockup width property by the common Laurent bound above at each
  level <math|n\<leqslant\>2>, and define its response property to be
  <eqref|eq:soft-curvature-receipts-display-SR2> at every such level. Then
  the theorem below supplies both properties, conventionally called the width
  and exact-response requirements for the three mockups.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Selected soft-response arrays><label|thm:ex-soft-response-mockups>Fix
    <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0>, <math|M,Z,A\<geqslant\>0>,
    <math|\<tau\>\<gtr\>0>, and <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>. There
    are <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0> and <math|K\<less\>\<infty\>> such that the
    following holds for every divergent period sequence <math|m<rsub|k>>,
    clip <math|p>, path family <math|R>, bed <math|B>, and
    <math|\<Lambda\>\<geqslant\>0> satisfying

    <\equation*>
      p.<math-up|direct>\<leqslant\>Z,<space|1em>R.<math-up|Bounds><around|(|p,\<tau\>,A,\<Lambda\>|)>,<space|1em>B<text|
      attached to >R,<space|1em>B.*<math-up|metric.strip><around|(|\<sigma\>,M|)>,
    </equation*>

    with <math|<math-up|Floor><around|(|B,p,n,\<varepsilon\>,M|)>> for every
    torus side <math|n>, and the sworn condition at each selected side
    <math|m<rsub|k>>. Put <math|U<rsub|m<rsub|k>>=<math-up|intended><around|(|p,R;m<rsub|k>|)>>.
    Then there is one family of arrays <math|F<rsub|0>,F<rsub|1>,F<rsub|2>>,
    not depending on <math|k>, such that each <math|F<rsub|n>> has width at
    most <math|K> at <math|\<eta\>>, and such that the exact response
    identity <eqref|eq:soft-curvature-receipts-display-SR2> holds for
    <math|U<rsub|m<rsub|k>>> at every level <math|n\<leqslant\>2> and for
    every <math|k>. Set <math|F<rsub|n>=0> for <math|n\<gtr\>2>. By
    construction the stitched pictures <eqref|eq:soft-curvature-receipts-display-SR1>
    of these arrays are the mixed response pictures of the sequence. One may
    take <math|K=3*C<rsub|\<ast\>>*<around|(|M<rsub|g>+P<rsub|W>|)>>, where
    <math|M<rsub|g>> is the price displayed in
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-greyson> (with <math|C=M>),
    <math|P<rsub|W>> is the Wilson price of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-soft-wilson-pictures>, and
    <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>> is the response-array multiplier above. The
    constants <math|\<eta\>,K> depend only on the listed scalar bounds and
    the fixed gauge and finite coordinate sets. The identity
    <eqref|eq:soft-curvature-receipts-display-SR2> is asserted only on the
    tori of the selected sequence, where the sworn condition is assumed.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-greyson> supplies pictures of
    <math|<around|(|U<rsub|m<rsub|k>>-W<rsub|k>|)><rsub|k>> of orders
    <math|j=1,2,3> and ray orders <math|4-j>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-soft-wilson-pictures> supplies Wilson
    pictures of ray orders <math|3-j>. Their sums are pictures of
    <math|<around|(|U<rsub|m<rsub|k>>|)><rsub|k>> with the required ray
    orders <math|3-j>, on the common strip of width <math|min
    <around|(|1,s|)>>. Prices and exact recording identities add. Each
    <math|U<rsub|m<rsub|k>>> is real analytic, and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-soft-quadratic-hessian> together with the
    third-order vanishing of the curvature part gives
    <math|U<rsub|m<rsub|k>><around|(|0|)>=0> and
    <math|D*U<rsub|m<rsub|k>><around|(|0|)>=0>. Apply
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-three-level-conversion> to the
    sequence <math|<around|(|U<rsub|m<rsub|k>>|)><rsub|k>>. Its finite
    constants depend only on the listed bounds and the finite color, phase,
    and stitch sets, so the common width and price are uniform in the path,
    the bed, the period sequence, and the selected action.
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|Scope.> This section proves the uniform atom, Ward, and
  Wilson-subtracted coefficient estimates, together with the finite division
  and the exact stitched response identity. The dependence of downstream
  applications on the displayed metric, floor, and sworn hypotheses remains
  as stated.

  <subsection|From Ward identities to invariant curvature
  coefficients><label|sec:multileg-curvature-lift>

  For the algebraic Poincaré lemma on continuum local jet forms, see
  <cite-detail|BarnichBrandtHenneaux|Theorem<nbsp>4.2>. Here the contraction
  is constructed on periodic momentum symbols, with quantitative strip and
  coefficient bounds used in the lattice comparison.

  The leading Taylor terms of a gauge-invariant action satisfy a Ward
  identity in every field argument. We now turn these identities into an
  exponentially summable expansion in curvature variables. The momenta of the
  arguments are constrained to sum to zero, so one cannot simply apply
  independent one-variable divisions to all of them. The following
  construction resolves that dependence before choosing the invariant, real
  coefficients of the path expansion.

  <subsubsection|A single exterior division with auxiliary constraints>

  Put <math|q<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>=e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>-1>. On alternating
  coordinate tensors in four dimensions let <math|E<rsub|a>> denote exterior
  multiplication by the <math|a>th basis vector and let
  <math|\<iota\><rsub|a>=E<rsub|a><rsup|<math-ss|T>>>. Write

  <\equation*>
    \<delta\><around|(|p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a=1><rsup|4>q<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>*\<iota\><rsub|a>.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|\<delta\>> lowers exterior degree by one and
  <math|\<delta\><rsup|2>=0>. Tensor factors other than the one on which this
  operator acts are coefficient spaces. Operators on distinct factors
  commute. All transposes here are algebraic transposes, without complex
  conjugation. This convention is appropriate for the multilinear Taylor
  coefficients of a scalar action.

  A periodic coefficient on <math|d> complex momentum coordinates has strip
  bound <math|<around|(|\<sigma\>,A|)>> if it is holomorphic on
  <math|<around|\||<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>p<rsub|j>|\|>\<less\>\<sigma\>>
  and has absolute value at most <math|A> there. Tensor bounds mean this
  entry by entry. Say that it has order <math|s> at zero if its restriction
  to each real ray <math|p=t*u> is <math|O<around|(|t<rsup|s>|)>> as
  <math|t\<to\>0>. For an analytic function this is equivalent to the
  vanishing of all homogeneous Taylor polynomials of degree less than
  <math|s>: restrict the Taylor expansion to each ray and use that a
  polynomial vanishing on every real vector is zero.

  The division from Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:curvature-horizon-producer>
  has the following strip version. For each coordinate <math|j>, there is a
  periodic holomorphic <math|D<rsub|j>*f> such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:multileg-division><around|(|e<rsup|i*p<rsub|j>>-1|)>*D<rsub|j>*f<around|(|p|)>=f<around|(|p|)>-f<around|(|p\|<rsub|p<rsub|j>=0>|)>,<space|2em><around|\||D<rsub|j>*f|\|><rsub|\<sigma\>/4>\<leqslant\>A*<space|0.17em>d<rsub|\<sigma\>>,
  </equation>

  where one may take

  <\equation*>
    d<rsub|\<sigma\>>=<frac|4|\<sigma\>><around*|(|<frac|2*<around|(|1+\<sigma\>/4|)>|\<sigma\>/4>|)><rsup|d>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed, Fourier expansion gives coefficients
  <math|<wide|f|^><around|(|z|)>> with

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|z>e<rsup|<around|(|\<sigma\>/2|)><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>><around|\||<wide|f|^><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<around*|(|<frac|2*<around|(|1+\<sigma\>/4|)>|\<sigma\>/4>|)><rsup|d>.
  </equation*>

  Replace each monomial in coordinate <math|j> by the finite geometric sum
  displayed in that earlier section. It has at most
  <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>> terms, all with displacement norm at most
  <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>. On the smaller strip its absolute value is
  at most <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>*e<rsup|<around|(|\<sigma\>/4|)><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>>,
  which is bounded by <math|<around|(|4/\<sigma\>|)>*e<rsup|<around|(|\<sigma\>/2|)><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>>.
  This proves normal convergence, the bound, and the identity, including the
  zeros of the divisor. If <math|f> has order <math|s>, this division has
  order at least <math|max <around|(|s-1,0|)>>. To verify this, the right
  side of <eqref|eq:multileg-division> has order <math|s>, while the lowest
  nonzero homogeneous term of the divisor is <math|i*p<rsub|j>>. A nonzero
  lowest homogeneous term of the quotient therefore gains exactly one degree
  upon multiplication.

  Suppose a tensor <math|f> of exterior degree <math|r> is a cycle,
  <math|\<delta\>*f=0>. Set <math|f<rsub|0>=f\|<rsub|p<rsub|a>=0>> and

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|a>=E<rsub|a>*D<rsub|a>*f,<space|2em>R<rsub|a>=<around|(|I-E<rsub|a>*\<iota\><rsub|a>|)>*f<rsub|0>.
  </equation*>

  The Cartan identity <math|\<delta\>*E<rsub|a>+E<rsub|a>*\<delta\>=q<rsub|a>*I>
  gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:multileg-axis>f=\<delta\>*B<rsub|a>+R<rsub|a>.
  </equation>

  For example, where <math|q<rsub|a>\<ne\>0> the cycle identities for
  <math|f> and <math|f<rsub|0>> imply <math|\<delta\>*D<rsub|a>*f=-\<iota\><rsub|a>*f<rsub|0>>;
  substitution gives the displayed formula. Continuity extends it to
  <math|q<rsub|a>=0>. The residual is still a cycle, has zero entries on
  faces containing <math|a>, and evaluates all other entries at
  <math|p<rsub|a>=0>. Successive residual operations in the four coordinates
  therefore leave zero when <math|r\<gtr\>0>. When <math|r=0>, they leave the
  value at <math|p=0>, so the same conclusion holds if that value vanishes.
  Adding the four lifts proves the following fact.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lem:multileg-single>Let <math|r,s\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>. Let <math|f>
    have strip bound <math|<around|(|\<sigma\>,A|)>>, order <math|s>, and
    exterior degree <math|r>. Assume <math|\<delta\>*f=0> and either
    <math|r\<gtr\>0> or <math|f\|<rsub|p=0>=0>. Then <math|f=\<delta\>*B>,
    where <math|B> has degree <math|r+1>, strip bound
    <math|<around|(|\<sigma\>/4,4*A*d<rsub|\<sigma\>>|)>>, and order at least
    <math|max <around|(|s-1,0|)>>. Here other momentum coordinates may remain
    as parameters, and <math|p=0> in the degree-zero hypothesis means only
    that the four coordinates of the divided momentum vanish.

    If, in addition, for each exterior component the vector of coefficients
    in the other tensor factors belongs to a fixed linear subspace of that
    other-factor coefficient space, depending only on the other momenta, the
    same membership holds separately for every exterior component of <math|B>
    at every real momentum.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Only the last assertion remains. Away from <math|q<rsub|a>=0>, the
    quotient in <eqref|eq:multileg-division> is a scalar multiple of the
    difference of two vectors in that subspace. On <math|q<rsub|a>=0>, vary
    just <math|p<rsub|a>> and take the limit. The subspace is fixed during
    this limit and is closed, since the coefficient space is finite
    dimensional. Exterior multiplication, subtraction, and coordinate
    restriction preserve membership as well. Thus each of the four lifts and
    their sum preserve the constraint. Each residual retains the original
    strip bound and order; summing four lifts accounts for the factor four,
    with only one loss of Taylor order.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Restoring the dependent momentum constraint>

  There are <math|t> independent momenta <math|p<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,p<rsub|t>\<in\>\<bbb-C\><rsup|4>>,
  and we set <math|p<rsub|0>=-<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|t>p<rsub|j>>. Write
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|j>=\<delta\><around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>> acting on tensor
  factor <math|j>. A Ward identity on that factor is
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|j>*f=0>. We first allow the zeroth factor to have
  degree <math|r+1> and all independent factors to have degree one.

  One can solve the zeroth-factor cycle while preserving all Ward identities
  except, provisionally, the first. Exchange <math|p<rsub|1>> and
  <math|p<rsub|0>> by the linear involution

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|p<rsub|1>,p<rsub|2>,\<ldots\>,p<rsub|t>|)>\<longmapsto\><around|(|-p<rsub|1>-\<cdots\>-p<rsub|t>,p<rsub|2>,\<ldots\>,p<rsub|t>|)>.
  </equation*>

  It preserves the integer periodicity lattice and sends a sufficiently
  narrow strip into the original strip; width
  <math|\<sigma\>/<around|(|t+1|)>> is sufficient for <math|t\<geqslant\>1>.
  Apply Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:multileg-single> in the new first variable
  and exchange back. The Ward subspaces for factors <math|2,\<ldots\>,t> do
  not depend on this variable, so they are retained. The cost is a fixed
  multiple of the old cost, the width remains strictly positive, and Taylor
  order drops by at most one. Denote this operation simply as a zeroth-factor
  lift. Likewise, if a coefficient has no first tensor factor and vanishes
  when <math|p<rsub|1>=0>, the degree-zero case of the lemma adds a
  degree-one first factor whose contraction is the original coefficient,
  while retaining the remaining Ward identities.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Dimension shift><label|lem:multileg-shift> Fix
    <math|t,r\<geqslant\>0> with <math|r+1+2*t\<gtr\>4> and
    <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0>. There are <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0> and
    <math|K\<less\>\<infty\>>, depending only on these data, such that the
    following holds for every <math|A\<geqslant\>0>. If <math|u> has strip
    bound <math|<around|(|\<sigma\>,A|)>>, degrees
    <math|<around|(|r+1,1,\<ldots\>,1|)>>, and <math|\<delta\><rsub|j>*u=0>
    for <math|j=0,\<ldots\>,t>, there is <math|v> of degrees
    <math|<around|(|r+2,1,\<ldots\>,1|)>> with strip bound
    <math|<around|(|\<eta\>,K*A|)>> satisfying

    <\equation*>
      \<delta\><rsub|0>*v=u,<space|2em>\<delta\><rsub|j>*v=0*<space|1em><around|(|1\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>t|)>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Induct on <math|t>, allowing <math|r> to vary. At <math|t=0> the degree
    <math|r+1> exceeds four, so <math|u=0> and one takes <math|v=0>. For
    <math|t\<geqslant\>1>, first lift the zeroth cycle, temporarily
    forgetting the first Ward identity. This gives <math|X> with
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>*X=u> and all remaining Ward identities. Contract
    its first factor:

    <\equation*>
      V=\<delta\><rsub|1>*X,<space|2em>\<delta\><rsub|0>*V=\<delta\><rsub|1>*u=0.
    </equation*>

    A second zeroth-factor lift gives <math|Y> of zeroth degree <math|r+3>
    with <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>*Y=V> and the remaining Ward identities.
    Restrict <math|Y> to <math|p<rsub|1>=0>, obtaining <math|Z>. Since
    <math|q<around|(|0|)>=0>, <math|V\|<rsub|p<rsub|1>=0>=0>, so <math|Z> is
    a zeroth-factor cycle in <math|t-1> independent variables. Its zeroth
    degree is <math|r+3>, and

    <\equation*>
      r+3+2*<around|(|t-1|)>=r+1+2*t\<gtr\>4.
    </equation*>

    The induction hypothesis gives <math|W> of degree <math|r+4> with
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>*W=Z>, retaining every remaining Ward identity.
    Extend <math|W> constantly in <math|p<rsub|1>>, writing the extension as
    <math|W<rprime|'>>, and set

    <\equation*>
      L=Y-\<delta\><rsub|0>*W<rprime|'>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>> uses the full momentum
    <math|-p<rsub|1>-\<cdots\>-p<rsub|t>>. Thus <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>*L=V>
    by <math|\<delta\><rsub|0><rsup|2>=0>, while
    <math|L\|<rsub|p<rsub|1>=0>=0> by the induction identity. The degree-zero
    first-factor lift now gives <math|F> with <math|\<delta\><rsub|1>*F=L>
    and all remaining Ward identities. Finally put
    <math|v=X-\<delta\><rsub|0>*F>. Commutation on distinct factors gives

    <\equation*>
      \<delta\><rsub|0>*v=u,<space|2em>\<delta\><rsub|1>*v=V-\<delta\><rsub|0>*L=0.
    </equation*>

    The other identities hold term by term. All operations are finite sums,
    fixed linear changes of variables, restrictions, exterior contractions,
    and the lifts already bounded above. At each stage choose a smaller
    positive common width. Their number is determined by <math|t,r>, and
    every bound remains linear in <math|A>. This gives <math|\<eta\>> and
    <math|K> before the particular input tensor is chosen.
  </proof>

  Take now a tensor <math|f> with <math|t+1\<geqslant\>3> degree-one factors
  and all Ward identities. The dimension-shift lemma with <math|r=0> lifts
  factor zero while preserving the independent identities. Lift each
  independent factor in turn by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:multileg-single>.
  At each step the identities in the remaining factors are auxiliary
  subspaces independent of the divided momentum; the already lifted indices
  are just finite coefficient indices. We obtain <math|g> with all factors of
  degree two such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:multileg-all-curls>f*<around|(|p<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,p<rsub|t>|)>=\<delta\><rsub|0>*\<delta\><rsub|1>*\<cdots\>*\<delta\><rsub|t>*g*<around|(|p<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,p<rsub|t>|)>.
  </equation>

  Its strip width and cost multiplier depend only on the original width and
  the number of factors. In particular the cubic and quartic applications
  have constants independent of the torus.

  <subsubsection|The quadratic case and retained moments>

  For two factors the dimension inequality fails. Suppose instead that
  <math|f<around|(|p|)>> has both Ward identities at momenta
  <math|<around|(|-p,p|)>> and order <math|s+2> at zero. The preceding
  construction can be shortened in a way that retains precisely <math|s>
  orders after both lifts. Lift factor zero to obtain <math|X> of degrees
  <math|<around|(|2,1|)>> and order <math|s+1>, with
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>*X=f>. The contraction <math|V=\<delta\><rsub|1>*X>
  has order <math|s+2>, since the contraction symbol vanishes to first order.
  It is a zeroth-factor cycle. Lift it to obtain <math|Y> of zeroth degree
  three and order <math|s+1> with <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>*Y=V>.

  There are no other momenta now. Hence <math|Y\|<rsub|p=0>=0>, and the
  degree-zero first-factor lift gives <math|D> of degrees
  <math|<around|(|3,1|)>>, order <math|s>, and <math|\<delta\><rsub|1>*D=Y>.
  Set <math|Z=X-\<delta\><rsub|0>*D>. Then

  <\equation*>
    \<delta\><rsub|0>*Z=f,<space|2em>\<delta\><rsub|1>*Z=0,<space|2em>Z<text|
    has order >s+1.
  </equation*>

  One last first-factor lift gives <math|g> of degrees
  <math|<around|(|2,2|)>> and order <math|s>, with
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>*\<delta\><rsub|1>*g=f>. All bounds remain linear in
  the initial strip cost, with a positive width chosen before <math|f> and
  <math|s>. For the loop remainder we use <math|s=2>: fourth-order vanishing
  of the potential coefficient yields second-order vanishing of its curvature
  coefficient.

  <subsubsection|Real coefficients and invariance at each placement>

  Fix a real orthonormal basis of the finite-dimensional Lie algebra. The
  tensors now also have one colour index for each field factor. Assume the
  original tensor satisfies

  <\equation*>
    <wide|f*<around|(|-p|)>|\<bar\>>=f<around|(|p|)><space|1em><text|for real
    independent momenta >p,
  </equation*>

  and that its real and imaginary colour tensors are invariant under the
  simultaneous adjoint action of the gauge group. The exterior lifts need not
  respect these properties, so we restore them explicitly.

  For each entry of <math|g>, take the real part of its Fourier coefficients.
  The resulting real coefficient array has symbol

  <\equation*>
    g<rsub|\<bbb-R\>><around|(|p|)>=<tfrac|1|2><around*|(|g<around|(|p|)>+<wide|g*<around|(|-p|)>|\<bar\>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|<wide|q*<around|(|-p|)>|\<bar\>>=q<around|(|p|)>> for real
  <math|p>, contraction of <math|g<rsub|\<bbb-R\>>> is still <math|f>. If
  <math|g> has order <math|s>, so does <math|g<rsub|\<bbb-R\>>>. Fourier
  coefficient estimates on a smaller strip give an exponentially weighted
  summation bound. More precisely, from strip width <math|w> and entry bound
  <math|B> one obtains width <math|w/2> and bound

  <\equation*>
    B<around*|(|<frac|2*<around|(|1+w/4|)>|w/4>|)><rsup|4*t>.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|\<cal-I\>> be the subspace of invariant real colour tensors, and
  choose a fixed linear projection <math|P> onto <math|\<cal-I\>>. Apply
  <math|P> separately at each tuple of face directions and each tuple of
  lattice displacements. This gives real coefficients
  <math|R<rsub|\<b-up-a\>,\<b-up-c\>><around|(|z|)>> that are invariant at
  every such placement. Exterior contractions do not act on colour indices,
  so they commute with <math|P>. Since <math|P> fixes the real and imaginary
  parts of <math|f>, the reconstructed potential coefficient remains
  <math|f>. The weighted bound is multiplied by at most

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|i,j><around|\||P<rsub|i*j>|\|>\<less\>\<infty\>.
  </equation*>

  This is a fixed finite-dimensional projection; no assertion that its norm
  is one is needed. It also preserves each retained order of vanishing. Thus,
  for some <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0> and <math|C\<less\>\<infty\>> fixed by
  width, arity, and gauge group, every face and colour entry obeys

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:multileg-real-budget><big|sum><rsub|z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4*t>>e<rsup|\<eta\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>><around|\||R<rsub|\<b-up-a\>,\<b-up-c\>><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*A.
  </equation>

  For two factors with retained order two, these coefficients have both zero
  total mass and zero first moments, entry by entry:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:multileg-moments><big|sum><rsub|z>R<around|(|z|)>=0,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|z>z<rsub|a>*R<around|(|z|)>=0*<space|1em><around|(|a=1,\<ldots\>,4|)>.
  </equation>

  Indeed the symbol at zero is the first sum. Exponential summability implies
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|z><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1><around|\||R<around|(|z|)>|\|>\<less\>\<infty\>>,
  so its derivative along the <math|a>th real momentum coordinate is
  <math|i*<big|sum><rsub|z>z<rsub|a>*R<around|(|z|)>>. The symbol's value and
  first derivatives vanish by its retained second order. This proves both
  identities with absolute convergence.

  <subsubsection|The invariant path expansion>

  For each tuple <math|<around|(|\<b-up-a\>,z|)>> of oriented coordinate
  plaquettes and displacements, put the zeroth plaquette at the origin and
  plaquette <math|j> at <math|z<rsub|j>\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>> for
  <math|1\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>t>. Use the coefficient colour tensor
  <math|R<rsub|\<b-up-a\>,\<cdot\>><around|(|z|)>> for this atom. It is
  invariant by the preceding construction. In the path convention of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:path-attachment-producer>, the atom's length
  is exactly

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>+4*<around|(|t+1|)>.
  </equation*>

  The four edges of every plaquette, including the anchored one, contribute
  to this length. Summing the weighted absolute coefficients over the finite
  face and colour index sets and over all displacements gives total atom
  budget at most

  <\equation*>
    C*A<space|0.17em>#<around|{|<text|face
    tuples>|}><space|0.17em>#<around|{|<text|colour
    tuples>|}>*<space|0.17em>e<rsup|4*\<eta\>*<around|(|t+1|)>>.
  </equation*>

  An enumeration of this countable family changes neither its sum nor its
  budget. In the two-factor case, filtering the enumeration by a fixed pair
  of face directions and a fixed colour tuple turns
  <eqref|eq:multileg-moments> into the required zero total loop coefficient
  and zero first moment of the relative displacement of the two legs. The
  latter displacement is <math|z<rsub|1>-0>.

  The potential coefficient reconstructed from these atoms is exactly
  <eqref|eq:multileg-all-curls>, with the two-factor version proved
  separately above. Absolute convergence permits periodization and evaluation
  on every finite torus. This result constructs the invariant coefficients
  from the specified analytic Ward data. The subsequent stationary action
  calculation must still supply those data, their vanishing orders, and the
  scalar normalization of the recorded Taylor terms.

  <subsection|Weighted derivatives of the centered block
  frame><label|sec:ex-weighted-frame-penalty>

  The penalty estimate in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-classical-stationary-vertices>
  requires uniformity as the block side increases. Here the averaging in the
  frame construction is essential. We prove the weighted derivative bound at
  the identity, retaining the same minimizing frame and predictor as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:predictor-branch>.

  <subsubsection|A calculus on finite probability spaces>

  For a probability measure <math|\<nu\>> on a finite set, define

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<nu\>,0>=max
    <around|\||f|\|>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<nu\>,1>=<around*|(|<big|sum>\<nu\><around|\||f|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|1/2>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<nu\>,2>=<big|sum>\<nu\><around|\||f|\|>.
  </equation*>

  An input direction has charge zero, one or two. Fix, for each charge
  <math|b\<in\><around|{|0,1,2|}>>, a set <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|b>> of input
  directions, called the admissible directions of charge <math|b>; the
  calculus below is valid for any such choice. A family of smooth maps
  <math|f<rsub|i>>, indexed by the points <math|i> of the probability space,
  has derivative bound <math|C> through order <math|n> if, for every
  <math|1\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>n> and all directions
  <math|u<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|b<rsub|1>>,\<ldots\>,u<rsub|k>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|b<rsub|k>>>
  with <math|<big|sum>b<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>2>, the function
  <math|i\<mapsto\>D<rsup|k>*f<rsub|i><around|(|0|)><around|[|u<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,u<rsub|k>|]>>
  has norm at most <math|C> in the output norm of charge
  <math|<big|sum>b<rsub|j>>. All derivatives are taken at the origin. Zeroth
  derivatives are specified separately. In the application of this section,
  let <math|L> be the blocking side and put <math|\<mu\>=L<rsup|-1>>. A
  direction consists of fine-link fields on a padded patch together with
  finitely many coarse and gauge coordinates. It is admissible of charge
  <math|b>, with constant <math|C>, if each fine-link field has norm at most
  <math|\<mu\>*C> in the norm <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<nu\>,b>>
  of normalized counting measure on the patch and each coarse and gauge
  coordinate has norm at most <math|C>. These are the bounds verified at the
  end of this section. Products add charges. The only nontrivial product rule
  for these norms is <math|L<rsup|2>\<cdot\>L<rsup|2>\<subset\>L<rsup|1>>,
  which is Cauchy\USchwarz. Averaging over one variable contracts each norm
  by the triangle inequality or Jensen's inequality. If
  <math|\<nu\><rprime|'>\<leqslant\>H*\<nu\>> with <math|H\<geqslant\>1>,
  changing the measure costs at most <math|H> in all three norms.

  These observations give quantitative product and composition rules. For a
  fixed smooth finite-dimensional map <math|F> at <math|z>, set

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-C\><rsub|n,F,z><around|(|C|)>=<big|sum><rsub|k=1><rsup|n><big|sum><rsub|\<pi\>\<in\>\<cal-P\><rsub|k>><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>>*F<around|(|z|)>|\<\|\|\>>*C<rsup|<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|k>> is the set of partitions of
  <math|<around|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>. If <math|f<around|(|0|)>=z> and <math|f>
  has derivative bound <math|C> through order <math|n>, the partition chain
  rule and the preceding product inequalities bound <math|F\<circ\>f> by
  <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|n,F,z><around|(|C|)>>. Each differentiated direction
  appears once, so the sum of charges is unchanged. If
  <math|D*F<around|(|z|)>=0>, the same chain rule bounds the derivative of
  order <math|n+1> using derivatives of <math|f> only through order <math|n>:
  the excluded single-block partition is the only one that would contain
  <math|D<rsup|n+1>*f>.

  <subsubsection|Long paths with small link derivatives>

  Suppose each link matrix equals <math|1> at the origin and has derivative
  bound <math|\<mu\>*C> through a fixed target order <math|N>, where
  <math|0\<less\>\<mu\>\<leqslant\>1>. A path contains at most
  <math|K/\<mu\>> links, with <math|K> fixed. Its ordered product has a bound
  depending on <math|K,C,N> only. The following induction proves this without
  an exponential loss in the number of links.

  For <math|0\<leqslant\>n\<less\>N>, assume all partial products have
  derivatives through order <math|n> bounded by <math|Q<rsub|n>>,
  independently of path length. Write a new factor as <math|1+v> and a
  partial product as <math|1+u>, where <math|u<around|(|0|)>=v<around|(|0|)>=0>.
  In the derivative of order <math|n+1> of <math|u*v>, both factors have
  positive derivative order. Thus derivatives of <math|u> only through order
  <math|n> occur, while every term has a factor <math|\<mu\>> from a
  derivative of <math|v>. The finite Leibniz sum is bounded by
  <math|\<mu\>*R<rsub|n>*<around|(|Q<rsub|n>,C|)>>, for a polynomial
  <math|R<rsub|n>> depending only on <math|n> and the matrix multiplication
  norm. The new factor alone contributes at most <math|\<mu\>*C>. Starting
  with the empty product and adding at most <math|K/\<mu\>> factors bounds
  the derivative of order <math|n+1> by <math|K*<around|(|C+R<rsub|n>*<around|(|Q<rsub|n>,C|)>|)>>.
  Taking its sum with <math|Q<rsub|n>> defines a common bound
  <math|Q<rsub|n+1>>. The induction begins at order zero, where every partial
  product has value <math|1>.

  Apply this on the enlarged block <math|\<cal-B\>> of radius
  <math|<around|(|J+3|)>*L>. For pairs of endpoints, a coordinate path has
  four straight pieces, each of length at most <math|2*<around|(|J+3|)>*L>.
  At a fixed position along one piece, the link read is a translate of the
  coordinatewise splice of the two endpoints. Swapping the corresponding
  endpoint coordinates is a bijection of <math|\<cal-B\>\<times\>\<cal-B\>>
  and preserves its uniform measure. A translate restricted to reads inside
  <math|\<cal-B\>> is injective; reads outside the path contribute the
  constant identity. Splitting positive and negative directions costs at most
  two; inverse link matrices are adjoints and have the same derivative norms.
  Consequently the preceding link estimate applies on the pair space, with a
  constant independent of <math|L>. Multiplying the four pieces and averaging
  the finitely many coordinate orders gives uniform derivative bounds for
  <math|E<rsub|x*y>>, defined as the average of the adjoint (equivalently,
  inverse) of the transport along <math|x\<to\>y>, with
  <math|E<rsub|x*y><around|(|0|)>=1>.

  <subsubsection|Eliminating the center of the frame>

  Let <math|F<rsub|x>> be the matrix of the minimizing frame after
  recentering by the nearest group element to its weighted mean. Let
  <math|\<nu\>> be the uniform measure on <math|\<cal-B\>>, and
  <math|\<omega\>> the actual smoothing probability used for this
  recentering. The latter is the distribution of the sum of <math|J>
  independent points, each uniform on the centred box
  <math|<around|{|z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>:<around|\||z|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|L-1|)>/2|}>>
  of side <math|L>; the block side <math|L> is odd
  (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection>), so this box is a
  complete residue system modulo <math|L>. The reduction of the sum modulo
  <math|L> is therefore uniform: fix all summands except the first, and let
  the first run over its complete residue system. Every residue class thus
  has <math|\<omega\>>-probability <math|L<rsup|-4>>, and in particular
  <math|\<omega\><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>L<rsup|-4>>. Moreover
  <math|\<omega\>> is supported in the box of radius
  <math|J*<around|(|L-1|)>/2\<less\><around|(|J+3|)>*L>, hence in
  <math|\<cal-B\>>, where <math|\<nu\>=<around|\||\<cal-B\>|\|><rsup|-1>>.
  Hence

  <\equation*>
    \<omega\><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>L<rsup|-4>,<space|2em>\<omega\><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|J>*\<nu\><around|(|x|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|H<rsub|J>> is a fixed upper bound for
  <math|<around|\||\<cal-B\>|\|>/L<rsup|4>>. This proves the measure
  comparison needed below with no assumption that <math|\<omega\>> itself is
  uniform on <math|\<cal-B\>>.

  Let <math|\<cal-F\>> denote the matrix-valued nearest-group map near
  <math|1>, let <math|\<Pi\>=D*\<cal-F\><around|(|1|)>> be the orthogonal
  projection onto the Lie algebra, and put
  <math|R<around|(|z|)>=\<cal-F\><around|(|z|)>-\<Pi\>*z>. Then
  <math|D*R<around|(|1|)>=0>. Define

  <\equation*>
    J<rsub|0>=<big|sum><rsub|y>\<nu\><around|(|y|)>*F<rsub|y>,<space|2em>B<rsub|x>=<big|sum><rsub|y>\<nu\><around|(|y|)>*F<rsub|y>*<around|(|E<rsub|x*y>-1|)>,<space|2em>T<rsub|x>=J<rsub|0>+B<rsub|x>.
  </equation*>

  The frame equation and its normalization are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-centered-frame-equations>F<rsub|x>=\<cal-F\><around|(|T<rsub|x>|)>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|x>\<omega\><around|(|x|)>*\<Pi\>*F<rsub|x>=\<Pi\>*1.
  </equation>

  Indeed, minimization at each frame coordinate says that it is the nearest
  group element to the average of the transported other frame coordinates. At
  the identity this average is <math|1>, so the smooth nearest map applies in
  a neighborhood. Common left multiplication by the recentering group element
  preserves the minimizing equation. The nearest-point normal equation for
  that recentering element gives the second equality in
  <eqref|eq:ex-centered-frame-equations>. Existence and smoothness near the
  identity are those of the selected minimizer already constructed; only its
  uniform derivatives are at issue here.

  Put <math|S<rsub|x>=\<Pi\>*B<rsub|x>+R<around|(|T<rsub|x>|)>>. Since
  <math|\<Pi\><rsup|2>=\<Pi\>>, the two equations imply the exact identity

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-centered-frame-elimination>F<rsub|x>=\<Pi\>*1-<big|sum><rsub|y>\<omega\><around|(|y|)>*\<Pi\>*S<rsub|y>+S<rsub|x>.
  </equation>

  To check it, first write <math|F<rsub|x>=\<Pi\>*J<rsub|0>+S<rsub|x>>. Apply
  <math|\<Pi\>>, average with <math|\<omega\>>, and solve for the constant
  <math|\<Pi\>*J<rsub|0>> using the normalization. This is the step that
  removes the derivative of the unknown center.

  We now induct on derivative order. Suppose derivatives of <math|F> through
  order <math|n> have uniform bound <math|A<rsub|n>>. The transport <math|E>
  has derivatives through order <math|n+1> bounded by a fixed
  <math|D<rsub|n+1>>. In differentiating <math|F<rsub|y>*<around|(|E<rsub|x*y>-1|)>>
  to order <math|n+1>, the term with <math|D<rsup|n+1>*F<rsub|y>> vanishes
  because <math|E<rsub|x*y><around|(|0|)>-1=0>. Every remaining term is
  bounded by the product rules, and averaging in <math|y> gives a bound for
  <math|B<rsub|x>> through order <math|n+1>. It follows that <math|T<rsub|x>>
  is bounded through order <math|n>. Since <math|D*R<around|(|1|)>=0>, the
  composition rule above bounds <math|R<around|(|T<rsub|x>|)>> through order
  <math|n+1> using only those derivatives. Thus <math|S<rsub|x>> has a bound
  <math|A<rprime|'><rsub|n>>, depending only on
  <math|A<rsub|n>,D<rsub|n+1>,G,n>. Equation
  <eqref|eq:ex-centered-frame-elimination> now bounds <math|F<rsub|x>>
  through order <math|n+1> by <math|<around|(|1+H<rsub|J>|)>*A<rprime|'><rsub|n>>.
  The induction starts at order zero, with <math|F<rsub|x><around|(|0|)>=1>.
  Every constant depends on <math|G,J,n> and the incoming link price, but not
  on <math|L>. In particular no inverse of a rooted-frame counting-norm
  Hessian has been inserted into this argument.

  <subsubsection|The predictor and penalty>

  The connector for a coarse direction consists of the centered frame at one
  endpoint, a straight path of length <math|L>, and the inverse centered
  frame at the translated endpoint. Use a padded box of radius
  <math|<around|(|J+5|)>*L> for the link fields. Translation from either
  endpoint patch into this box is injective, and the ratio of the two
  normalized counting measures is bounded in terms of <math|J> only. The
  long-path argument and the frame induction therefore give uniform
  derivative bounds for the connector. Changing its average to the smoothing
  probability costs <math|H<rsub|J>>. Composing the resulting matrix average,
  whose value is <math|1>, with <math|\<cal-F\>> bounds the actual predictor
  through order <math|n>. The strict local predictor test identifies this
  expression with every selected tag near the identity, as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:stationary-action-prices>.

  Next apply the fixed group exponential, multiplication, adjoint and
  nearest-group maps. The fine-link directions have norm at most
  <math|\<mu\>*C> on the padded box, while the finitely many coarse and gauge
  coordinates have norm at most <math|C>. Composition with an exponential
  preserves a factor <math|\<mu\>> in every positive fine-link derivative:
  each chain-rule term contains <math|\<mu\><rsup|k>>, <math|k\<geqslant\>1>,
  and <math|\<mu\><rsup|k>\<leqslant\>\<mu\>>. Thus these are precisely the
  link bounds used above. The penalty in one direction is the fixed smooth
  function

  <\equation*>
    Z\<longmapsto\><frac|k|2*c<rsub|G><rsup|2>>*<around|\<\|\|\>|Z-1|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>
  </equation*>

  of the coarse group element times the inverse predictor; <math|k> is the
  penalty coefficient and <math|c<rsub|G>> the fixed color normalization. The
  finite composition rule now gives its derivative bound <math|B<rsub|n>>.
  All constants were selected before <math|L>. Summing the four directions
  and complexifying gives <eqref|eq:ex-vertex-penalty-price>.

  <subsubsection|Verification for the Fourier legs>

  We finally check the weighted stencil hypotheses just used. Write
  <math|k<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>=<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*l|)>/L>, and let
  <math|P<around|(|p|)>> be the normalized block Fourier matrix, whose
  entries have magnitude <math|L<rsup|-2>> at real <math|p>. The weighted
  norm of a fine block vector <math|u> is

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p><rsup|2>=<big|sum><rsub|l>h<rsub|l><around|(|p|)><rsup|2><around|\||<around|(|P<around|(|p|)><rsup|-1>*u|)><rsub|l>|\|><rsup|2>,<space|2em>h<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>=<around*|(|<around|\||q<around|(|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>k<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>|)>|\|><rsup|2>+\<mu\><rsup|2>|)><rsup|1/2>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|q<rsub|a><around|(|k|)>=e<rsup|i*k<rsub|a>>-1>; color components
  are included in the sum. On the strip of width one, <math|P<around|(|p|)>>
  and its inverse have Euclidean operator norms at most <math|e<rsup|4>>:
  factor them into the unitary real Fourier matrix and diagonal imaginary
  phases. Since <math|h<rsub|l>\<geqslant\>\<mu\>>, a unit weighted vector
  satisfies <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<ell\><rsup|2>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|4>*\<mu\><rsup|-1>>.
  Dividing by <math|L<rsup|2>> gives its normalized block <math|L<rsup|2>>
  bound <math|e<rsup|4>*\<mu\>>. Also

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||q<rsub|a><around|(|k<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>5*h<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>.
  </equation*>

  For this last inequality, split the symbol into its real-momentum value and
  its change in the imaginary direction. Put
  <math|x=<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>k<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>> and
  <math|w=k<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>-x=i<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>p/L>. On the
  strip, <math|<around|\||w<rsub|a>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<mu\>\<leqslant\>1> for
  every coordinate <math|a>, since <math|\<mu\>=L<rsup|-1>>. Because <math|x>
  is real,

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|a><around|(|k<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>|)>-q<rsub|a><around|(|x|)>=e<rsup|i*x<rsub|a>>*<around*|(|e<rsup|i*w<rsub|a>>-1|)>,<space|2em><around|\||e<rsup|z>-1|\|>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>1><frac|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|n>|n!>\<leqslant\><around|(|e-1|)><around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>2<around|\||z|\|>*<space|1em><around|(|<around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>1|)>.
  </equation*>

  Hence each coordinate changes by at most <math|2*\<mu\>>, and the Euclidean
  norm on <math|\<bbb-C\><rsup|4>> by at most
  <math|4*\<mu\>\<leqslant\>4*h<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>>. The real-momentum
  value is at most <math|h<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>>, which gives the factor
  <math|5>. Fourier multiplication therefore bounds one fine difference in
  unnormalized <math|\<ell\><rsup|2>> by <math|5*e<rsup|4>>, and in
  normalized <math|L<rsup|2>> by <math|5*e<rsup|4>*\<mu\><rsup|2>>. These are
  exactly the one-internal-leg bounds used in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-classical-stationary-vertices>. The
  auxiliary scalar part of the internal norm is ordinary Euclidean, so its
  coordinates are bounded by one. Smooth bridge coordinate legs satisfy the
  required supremum bounds by <eqref|eq:ex-vertex-cruise>, with a fixed
  color-dimension factor when components are combined into a vector.

  To extend a block Fourier vector to the padded stencil, write
  <math|z=<wide|z|\<bar\>>+L*r<around|(|z|)>>, where <math|<wide|z|\<bar\>>>
  is the chosen residue representative. Throughout the padded box,
  <math|<around|\||r<around|(|z|)>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>4+4*<around|(|J+5|)>>.
  The extension is <math|e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\>r<around|(|z|)>>*u<around|(|<wide|z|\<bar\>>|)>>,
  whose phase is bounded by <math|exp <around|(|4+4*<around|(|J+5|)>|)>>. The
  map <math|z\<mapsto\><around|(|r<around|(|z|)>,<wide|z|\<bar\>>|)>> is
  injective, and <math|r<around|(|z|)>> ranges over a fixed finite set
  depending only on <math|J>. Comparing normalized counting measures
  therefore costs another constant depending only on <math|J>. The fine field
  retains its factor <math|\<mu\>> in the appropriate supremum or normalized
  <math|L<rsup|2>> norm. Coarse stencil shifts are at most one step and cost
  at most <math|e>; taking real or imaginary parts only decreases the
  coordinate norms. This verifies every fine, coarse and gauge stencil bound
  used to obtain <math|B<rsub|n>> and closes the weighted penalty and
  internal Fourier inputs of the preceding section. The quantitative smooth
  bridge bound <eqref|eq:ex-vertex-cruise> is the remaining independent
  input.

  <subsection|Uniform inverse bounds for the stationary
  matrices><label|sec:ex-weighted-stationary-inverses>

  We use the block Fourier norm of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-weighted-frame-penalty>.
  Its weight at alias <math|l> is <math|h<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>=<around|(|<around|\||q<around|(|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>k<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>|)>|\|><rsup|2>+\<mu\><rsup|2>|)><rsup|1/2>>.
  The internal space is the direct sum of the fine one-form space in this
  norm and the coarse scalar space in its Euclidean norm. We denote it by
  <math|W<rsub|p>>. The dual always pairs opposite momenta. The weights are
  not holomorphic functions of <math|p>, so a complex inverse estimate
  requires more than a direct application of Cauchy's inequality to a
  weighted matrix.

  <subsubsection|Continuing a real lower bound to a complex strip>

  Use the maximum norm on the four momentum coordinates throughout. Let
  <math|X<rsub|p>,Y<rsub|p>> be two weighted norms, represented by invertible
  matrices <math|S<rsub|X><around|(|p|)>,S<rsub|Y><around|(|p|)>>:
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|X<rsub|p>>=<around|\||S<rsub|X><around|(|p|)><rsup|-1>*u|\|><rsub|2>>.
  Suppose that, at each real momentum <math|x>, these matrices admit
  holomorphic replacements on the ball <math|<around|\||z-x|\|>\<less\>\<sigma\>>.
  The replacements agree with the actual matrices on the vertical slice
  <math|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>z=x> and compare to the actual weighted
  norms in both directions by a fixed factor <math|H>, and first restrict
  <math|0\<less\>\<sigma\>\<leqslant\>1>. Let <math|K> be periodic and
  holomorphic, with weighted operator bound <math|A> on the strip of width
  <math|\<sigma\>>, and suppose its weighted real matrix has least singular
  value at least <math|a\<gtr\>0>. Then there are <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0> and
  <math|F\<less\>\<infty\>>, depending on <math|\<sigma\>,H,A,a> only, such
  that <math|K<rsup|-1>:X<rsub|p>\<to\>Y<rsub|p>> has norm at most <math|F>
  for <math|p> in the strip of width <math|\<eta\>>.

  Here is a quantitative proof. At a real anchor <math|x>, transform
  <math|K<around|(|z|)>> using the holomorphic replacement matrices. The
  resulting matrix <math|Q<rsub|x><around|(|z|)>> is holomorphic and has
  Euclidean operator norm at most <math|H<rsup|2>*A> on the ball of radius
  <math|\<sigma\>>. Cauchy's estimate on complex lines, tested against unit
  vectors on both sides, gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|Q<rsub|x><around|(|p|)>-Q<rsub|x><around|(|x|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>k*<around|\||p-x|\|>,<space|2em>k=<frac|H<rsup|2>*A|\<sigma\>/2>,<space|1em><around|\||p-x|\|>\<less\>\<sigma\>/2.
  </equation*>

  Choose <math|\<eta\>=min <around|{|\<sigma\>/2,a/<around|(|2*<around|(|1+k|)>|)>|}>>.
  For <math|x=<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>p>, the actual and replacement norms
  coincide, and

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||Q<rsub|x><around|(|p|)>*v|\|>\<geqslant\><around|(|a-k*<around|\||p-x|\|>|)><around|\||v|\|>\<geqslant\><around|(|a/2|)><around|\||v|\|>.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|Q<rsub|x><around|(|p|)>> is invertible and its inverse has norm
  at most <math|2/a>. The same is true of the weighted inverse of
  <math|K<around|(|p|)>>. Holomorphy of the inverse follows from the adjugate
  formula; periodicity follows from uniqueness of the inverse. No dimension
  enters this argument.

  For the block Fourier weights the required replacement is explicit: freeze
  <math|h<rsub|l>> at the real anchor <math|x>, while retaining the
  holomorphic Fourier matrix <math|P<around|(|z|)>>. The inequality

  <\equation*>
    h<rsub|l><around|(|z|)>\<leqslant\>6*h<rsub|l><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>h<rsub|l><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>6*h<rsub|l><around|(|z|)>*<space|1em><around|(|<around|\||z-x|\|>\<leqslant\>1|)>
  </equation*>

  follows from <math|h<rsub|l>\<geqslant\>\<mu\>>, the triangle inequality
  and the Lipschitz bound <math|<around|\||q<around|(|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>k<rsub|l><around|(|z|)>|)>-q<around|(|k<rsub|l><around|(|x|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>4*\<mu\>*<around|\||z-x|\|>>.
  The Fourier matrices cancel in the comparison of the two norms; the
  remaining matrix is diagonal with these ratios. Hence <math|H=6> works,
  uniformly in <math|L>. Direct sums with the ordinary scalar norm and dual
  norms have the same property.

  <subsubsection|The scalar and gauge-completed fine matrices>

  Put <math|\<omega\><around|(|k|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a>q<rsub|a>*<around|(|-k|)>*q<rsub|a><around|(|k|)>>
  and define

  <\equation*>
    s<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|k|)>=\<omega\><around|(|k|)>+\<mu\><rsup|2>,<space|1em>d<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|k|)>=<frac|\<mu\><rsup|2>|s<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|k|)>>,<space|1em>c<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|k|)>=\<mu\><rsup|2>*d<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|k|)><rsup|9>,<space|1em>\<gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|k|)>=\<omega\><around|(|k|)>+c<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|k|)>.
  </equation*>

  At real <math|k>, <math|\<omega\><around|(|k|)>=<around|\||q<around|(|k|)>|\|><rsup|2>>.
  The sign convention in this display uses
  <math|q<rsub|a>*<around|(|-k|)>*q<rsub|a><around|(|k|)>=<around|\||q<rsub|a><around|(|k|)>|\|><rsup|2>>
  on the real slice. For <math|x\<geqslant\>0>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-scalar-weight-floor>x+\<mu\><rsup|2><around*|(|<frac|\<mu\><rsup|2>|x+\<mu\><rsup|2>>|)><rsup|9>\<geqslant\>2<rsup|-10>*<around|(|x+\<mu\><rsup|2>|)>.
  </equation>

  If <math|x\<geqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|2>>, its first term alone suffices. If
  <math|x\<less\>\<mu\><rsup|2>>, the ratio is greater than <math|1/2>, so
  the second term is at least <math|2<rsup|-9>*\<mu\><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>2<rsup|-10>*<around|(|x+\<mu\><rsup|2>|)>>.
  On the alias strip <math|<around|\||<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>k|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\>\<mu\>/128>,
  comparison with the real symbol gives <math|<around|\||s<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|k|)>|\|>\<geqslant\><tfrac|1|2><around|(|<around|\||q<around|(|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>k|)>|\|><rsup|2>+\<mu\><rsup|2>|)>>.
  Indeed the change of <math|q> has norm at most <math|4*\<mu\>/128> and the
  change of its quadratic product is bounded by twice this times the real
  norm plus its square; <math|2*a*b\<leqslant\>a<rsup|2>+b<rsup|2>> absorbs
  this into less than half the real denominator. In particular all displayed
  rational symbols are holomorphic there,
  <math|<around|\||d<rsub|\<mu\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>2>, and
  <math|<around|\||c<rsub|\<mu\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|9>*\<mu\><rsup|2>>.

  Fix <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, <math|\<Theta\>\<geqslant\>0> and a
  coarse width <math|0\<less\>\<sigma\>\<leqslant\>1>. Let
  <math|H<around|(|k|)>> be the symbol of the fine two-form metric,
  holomorphic and bounded by <math|\<Theta\>> on the fine strip
  <math|<around|\||<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>k|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\>\<mu\>*\<sigma\>>,
  which contains the aliases <math|k<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>> of the coarse
  strip of width <math|\<sigma\>>. Write <math|K<rsub|f>=d<rsub|1>*<around|(|-k|)><rsup|T>*H<around|(|k|)>*d<rsub|1><around|(|k|)>>
  and <math|d<rsub|0><around|(|k|)>*v=q<around|(|k|)>\<wedge\>v>, and suppose
  that the real kinetic form satisfies the all-period lower bound

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<langle\>|v,K<rsub|f><around|(|k|)>*v|\<rangle\>>\<geqslant\>\<varepsilon\>*<around|\||d<rsub|1><around|(|k|)>*v|\|><rsup|2>
  </equation*>

  at every momentum <math|k> of every finite-period Fourier grid, with the
  same <math|\<varepsilon\>> for all periods. In the application this is a
  consequence of the floor condition of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-soft-curvature-receipts>,
  which gives <math|<around|\<langle\>|w,H<rsub|m>*w|\<rangle\>>\<geqslant\>\<varepsilon\><around|\||w|\|><rsup|2>>
  for every real two-form <math|w> at every period <math|m>. The positivity
  of <math|\<varepsilon\>> is used twice: the weighted floor of the
  gauge-completed matrix <math|D<rsub|\<mu\>>> and the constant
  <math|a<rsub|0>> of the quotient bound <eqref|eq:ex-quotient-floor> below
  are positive only because <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>. The scalar matrix
  <math|s<rsub|\<mu\>>*I> and the scalar matrix
  <math|\<gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>>*I> have weighted real floors <math|1> and
  <math|2<rsup|-10>>. The gauge-completed one-form matrix

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|k|)>=K<rsub|f><around|(|k|)>+d<rsub|0><around|(|k|)>*d<rsub|0>*<around|(|-k|)><rsup|T>+c<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|k|)>*I
  </equation*>

  has weighted floor <math|2<rsup|-10>*min <around|{|\<varepsilon\>,1|}>>.
  This follows from

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||d<rsub|1>*v|\|><rsup|2>+<around|\||d<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*v|\|><rsup|2>=<around|\||q|\|><rsup|2><around|\||v|\|><rsup|2>
  </equation*>

  and <eqref|eq:ex-scalar-weight-floor>. The exterior identity is obtained by
  expanding the wedge and contraction in the coordinate basis. The all-period
  kinetic bound applies at every real momentum by density of finite-torus
  frequencies and continuity.

  For these scalar and fine symbols restrict further to
  <math|\<sigma\>\<leqslant\>1/128>. Their complex weighted upper bounds are
  also uniform. Each exterior matrix costs at most a fixed color-direction
  constant times <math|<around|\||q|\|>>,
  <math|<around|\||q<around|(|k<rsub|l>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>5*h<rsub|l>>, and
  <math|<around|\||c<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|k<rsub|l>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|9>*h<rsub|l><rsup|2>>.
  Thus division by <math|h<rsub|l>> at both ends leaves a fixed bound
  depending only on <math|G,\<Theta\>>. The block Fourier conjugations cancel
  between opposite momenta, leaving a unitary alias permutation and these
  diagonal weights. The preceding continuation argument gives inverse bounds
  for all three matrices on one coarse strip, before <math|L> and before any
  higher-order interaction budget are chosen.

  <subsubsection|A real lower bound modulo gauge directions>

  Let <math|Q<around|(|p|)>> be the linear block mean on one-forms and
  <math|J<around|(|p|)>> its scalar counterpart, so
  <math|Q*d<rsub|0>=d<rsub|0,c>*J>. Set

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-R\>*a=<around|(|-d<rsub|0>*a,-J*a|)>,<space|2em>N<around|(|u,r|)>=d<rsub|0,c>*r-Q*u,<space|2em>\<cal-G\>*<around|(|u,r|)>=<around|\<langle\>|u,K<rsub|f>*u|\<rangle\>>+\<kappa\><around|\||N<around|(|u,r|)>|\|><rsup|2>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<kappa\>\<gtr\>0> and all operators are expressed in the same
  block Fourier coordinates. The curl and <math|N> annihilate
  <math|\<cal-R\>*a>, so <math|\<cal-G\>> is unchanged when a gauge vector is
  subtracted. Recall that <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0> and
  <math|\<kappa\>\<gtr\>0>. We claim that, for some <math|a<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0>
  depending only on <math|G,J,\<kappa\>,\<varepsilon\>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-quotient-floor><text|for every >v<text| there is >a<text|
    such that >a<rsub|0>*<around|\<\|\|\>|v-\<cal-R\>*a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|W<rsub|p>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>\<cal-G\><around|(|v|)>.
  </equation>

  Choose the real coarse momentum in <math|<around|[|-\<pi\>,\<pi\>|]><rsup|4>>.
  For every nonzero alias, <math|<around|\||q<around|(|k<rsub|l>|)>|\|>\<geqslant\>2*\<mu\>>.
  Subtract its longitudinal component by solving
  <math|d<rsub|0><around|(|k<rsub|l>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|u<rsub|l>+d<rsub|0><around|(|k<rsub|l>|)>*a<rsub|l>|)>=0>;
  the scalar denominator is <math|<around|\||q<around|(|k<rsub|l>|)>|\|><rsup|2>\<gtr\>0>.
  The principal alias is left available to eliminate the coarse scalar
  coordinate. In normalized Fourier coordinates the scalar block mean on that
  alias is <math|\<mu\><rsup|2><math-up|rollMean><around|(|k<rsub|0>|)>>,
  which is nonzero. No uniform bound on the eliminating gauge parameter is
  needed. Here <math|<math-up|rollMean>> is the product of the four
  normalized half-block geometric sums, raised to power <math|J>. For
  <math|<around|\||k<rsub|a>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<pi\>/L>, each sum has modulus
  at least <math|1/16>; this follows from the geometric-sum formula and
  <math|sin t\<geqslant\>2*t/\<pi\>> for <math|0\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>\<pi\>/2>,
  with the value at zero understood by continuity. Thus
  <math|<around|\||<math-up|rollMean>|\|>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|J>=<around|(|1/16|)><rsup|4*J>>.
  We obtain a representative <math|<around|(|P<around|(|p|)>*b,0|)>> with all
  nonprincipal <math|b<rsub|l>> transverse.

  Put <math|A<rsub|h>=<big|sum><rsub|l\<ne\>0>h<rsub|l><rsup|2><around|\||b<rsub|l>|\|><rsup|2>>
  and <math|B<rsub|0>=<around|\||Q*P<around|(|p|)>*b|\|><rsup|2>>. The
  kinetic bound and transversality give

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-G\><around|(|v|)>\<geqslant\><around|(|\<varepsilon\>/2|)>*A<rsub|h>+\<kappa\>*B<rsub|0>.
  </equation*>

  The principal part of <math|Q*P<around|(|p|)>> has smallest singular value
  at least <math|<around|(|c<rsub|J>/16|)>*\<mu\>>: the straight connector
  contributes a geometric sum of size at least <math|L/16>, and <math|P>
  contributes <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>>. The full block mean has weighted norm at
  most a constant <math|C<rsub|J>>. To verify this upper bound directly, each
  fixed-position read has coefficients bounded by
  <math|C<rsub|J>*\<mu\><rsup|4>> on <math|O<rsub|J><around|(|L<rsup|4>|)>>
  sites, hence Euclidean row norm at most
  <math|C<rprime|'><rsub|J>*\<mu\><rsup|2>>; sum the <math|L> positions and
  use <math|h<rsub|l>\<geqslant\>\<mu\>>. The same argument gives the scalar
  averaging norm <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|J|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rprime|'><rsub|J>*\<mu\><rsup|2>>.
  Separating the principal and nonprincipal aliases and squaring the triangle
  inequality now gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|<around|(|c<rsub|J>/16|)>*\<mu\><around|\||b<rsub|0>|\|>|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|0>+2*C<rsub|J><rsup|2>*A<rsub|h>.
  </equation*>

  Finally <math|h<rsub|0><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|(|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|2>+1|)>*\<mu\><rsup|2>>.
  It follows that <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|P*b,0|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|W<rsub|p>><rsup|2>=A<rsub|h>+h<rsub|0><rsup|2><around|\||b<rsub|0>|\|><rsup|2>>
  is bounded by a fixed multiple of <math|<around|(|\<varepsilon\>/2|)>*A<rsub|h>+\<kappa\>*B<rsub|0>>,
  proving <eqref|eq:ex-quotient-floor>. This proof includes <math|p=0>.

  <subsubsection|Adding the gauge-fixing square>

  Let <math|F> be the actual linear gauge-fixing map, periodic and
  holomorphic on the chosen strip, and satisfying
  <math|F*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|T>=F<around|(|p|)><rsup|\<ast\>>> on real
  momenta. Its required identities and estimate are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-gauge-fixing-receipt>F*\<cal-R\>=\<Gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>>,<space|2em><around|\||F*v|\|>\<leqslant\>D<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|W<rsub|p>>,
  </equation>

  where the bound holds throughout that complex strip, and
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>>> is the block scalar operator with symbol
  <math|\<gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>>>. These are exact same-frame identities; they
  remain inputs to this subsection until the gauge-fixing construction is
  identified with these symbols. The bound on <math|J>, the definition of
  <math|\<cal-R\>> and <eqref|eq:ex-scalar-weight-floor> give

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-R\>*a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|W<rsub|p>>\<leqslant\>C*<space|0.17em><around|\||\<Gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>>*a|\|>=C*<around|\||F*\<cal-R\>*a|\|>,
  </equation*>

  with <math|C> independent of <math|L>. For the fine component this is the
  pointwise inequality <math|h<rsub|l><around|\||q<rsub|l>|\|>\<leqslant\>h<rsub|l><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|10>*\<gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|k<rsub|l>|)>>;
  for the coarse component use <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>h<rsub|l><rsup|2>>
  and the scalar mean bound above. Given <math|v>, take <math|a> from
  <eqref|eq:ex-quotient-floor> and put <math|b=<around|\<\|\|\>|v-\<cal-R\>*a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|W<rsub|p>>>,
  <math|c=<around|\||F*v|\|>>. Then

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|W<rsub|p>>\<leqslant\>C*c+<around|(|C*D+1|)>*b,<space|2em>a<rsub|0>*b<rsup|2>+c<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>\<cal-G\><around|(|v|)>+<around|\||F*v|\|><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  Squaring the first inequality proves a uniform positive lower bound for the
  full quadratic form <math|\<cal-G\>+F<rsup|\<ast\>>*F>; for example its
  inverse floor can be taken as <math|max
  <around|{|2*C<rsup|2>,2*<around|(|C*D+1|)><rsup|2>/a<rsub|0>|}>>. On the
  complex strip its weighted upper bound is the sum of the kinetic bound,
  <math|\<kappa\>> times the square of the bound for <math|N>, and
  <math|D<rsup|2>>. The holomorphic opposite-momentum transpose replaces
  <math|F<rsup|\<ast\>>> off the real slice. The continuation lemma therefore
  gives a common inverse width and cost for this actual stationary
  fluctuation matrix.

  Once the common matrix is identified on the Fourier grids of an unbounded
  family of tori, the identification extends to the entire real slice by
  density, and then to the strip by analytic uniqueness. Consequently any two
  stationary matrix presentations representing that same family inherit this
  one inverse estimate. This last statement does not identify arbitrary
  independently chosen matrices: the all-period representation and the exact
  identities <eqref|eq:ex-gauge-fixing-receipt> must refer to the same
  construction.

  <subsection|The selected bridge and gauge-fixing
  map><label|sec:ex-selected-stationary-linear-data>

  The preceding estimates must be supplied on the actual retained horizon. We
  verify two identifications: the fine-difference bounds of its linear
  stationary bridge, and the exact gauge identity of its fluctuation matrix.

  <subsubsection|The unchanged one-form transport>

  Let <math|A<rsub|0>> be the one-form transport of the retained tower at a
  fixed adjacent pair of scales. The construction in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:curvature-horizon-producer> retains this row
  unchanged; only the curvature completion rows are replaced. Apply
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-wire-tower-strip> with <math|e=1>.
  The schedule has <math|j\<geqslant\>4>, so every difference word <math|u>
  of length at most two is covered. Its actual Laurent row satisfies

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|z>e<rsup|w<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>><around|\||<around|(|\<nabla\><rsup|u>*A<rsub|0>|)><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>D*L<rsup|-1-<around|\||u|\|>>.
  </equation*>

  The width <math|w\<gtr\>0> and the cost <math|D> are those of the stage of
  this adjacent pair. They are common to every terminal cutoff, every level
  at that stage and every finite period, but, as stated at the end of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:curvature-horizon-producer>, no common
  positive width or common cost over all stages is asserted. The hypotheses
  of the cited theorem for the retained rows, and the resulting difference
  bounds, are part of the horizon supplier of that section; here they are an
  input. The price enlargement used in the retained construction can only
  increase <math|D> and narrow <math|w>.

  Define <math|\<Phi\><around|(|p|)>> by Fourier evaluation of
  <math|A<rsub|0>>, with the color identity on its color factor. Fourier
  evaluation commutes with a cover difference, including its centered carry:
  this is exactly the residue-difference identity in the proof of the cited
  theorem. Thus, for a coordinate leg,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<nabla\><rsup|u>*\<Phi\><around|(|p|)>*e<rsub|a><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>D*L<rsup|-1-<around|\||u|\|>>*<space|2em><around|(|p\<in\><math-up|strip><around|(|w|)>,<around|\||u|\|>\<leqslant\>2|)>.
  </equation*>

  Absolute exponential summability gives holomorphy and periodicity on the
  strip. Restriction to a smaller width preserves every bound. This proves
  <eqref|eq:ex-vertex-cruise> for the selected bridge and supplies the
  identical hypothesis in the inherited-loop estimate.

  We must also identify this row with the derivative of the stationary
  representative used below. Write <math|W=A<rsub|0>> in finite-volume
  coordinates. In the original tree gauge chart the positive internal Hessian
  gives a unique local stationary branch. Its fine tangent is not <math|W>
  itself: straightening up the tree adds an infinitesimal gauge
  transformation, so the expected tangent is

  <\equation*>
    E=W+d<rsub|0>*K,
  </equation*>

  where <math|K> is the linear tree-straightening map: at a fine site,
  <math|K> integrates <math|W> along the tree path to the block root and
  subtracts the root value <math|<math-up|fixRoot><space|0.17em>W>. Existence
  and uniqueness of the branch do not by themselves show that its derivative
  is this particular matrix. The identification is the following input, which
  is the installed-tangent identity proved in
  Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:path-stationary-chart>.

  <\quote-env>
    <em|Stationary tangent input.> On each finite torus of the selected
    family, for the same forest, incoming path, fine and coarse beds and the
    same installed matrix <math|W> of the retained tower, the stationary
    branch <math|\<rho\>> of the tree chart satisfies

    <\equation*>
      D*\<rho\><around|(|0|)>*x=<around|(|E*x-\<iota\>*x|)><mid|\|><rsub|<math-up|free>>
    </equation*>

    for every coarse direction <math|x>. Here the tree chart records the fine
    field on the free (non-tree) links relative to the linear copy
    <math|\<iota\>*x> of the coarse field, which equals the coarse value on
    fine links joining two different blocks and zero on links inside a block.
  </quote-env>

  <no-indent>This is the precise form of the statement that the fine tangent
  of the branch is <math|E>. The cited section derives it by differentiating
  the vertical stationarity equation: by the finite-volume Hessian identity
  the bilinear form in that equation is the fine bed kinetic form plus the
  penalty pairing of the predictor residual, the candidate <math|E> solves
  the same linear equation by the two matching identities
  <math|K<rsub|f>*W=Q<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*K<rsub|c>> and
  <math|Q*W=I-\<kappa\><rsup|-1>*K<rsub|c>> of the retained tower for these
  two beds, and positivity of the vertical Hessian gives uniqueness. Its
  premises (the Hessian identity, the Hessian gap and the two matching
  identities for the same beds and the same <math|W>) are therefore premises
  of everything that follows. Gauge this branch back by the group exponential
  of <math|K*x>. The derivative of the resulting fine field is
  <math|E-d<rsub|0>*K=W>. At a block root the straightening identity reads
  <math|K<rsub|<math-up|root>>=-<math-up|fixRoot><space|0.17em>W>. The
  derivative of the rooted frame is <math|-<math-up|fixRoot><space|0.17em>W>
  as well; multiplication by the inverse root gauge factor cancels it. Thus
  recentering the gauged-back branch gives the joint derivative
  <math|<around|(|W,0|)>>. Gauge covariance preserves its stationarity. We
  use uniqueness only in the original tree chart: the centered merit on the
  full joint space has gauge null directions. Finite Fourier inversion now
  identifies this actual derivative on every torus grid with <math|\<Phi\>>.
  Hence the difference bounds above concern the selected stationary
  representative.

  <subsubsection|The exact gauge identity>

  Work first on one finite torus. Let <math|d> be the infinitesimal fine
  gauge action at the identity, in the sign convention of the joint action,
  and let <math|\<cal-R\>> be its expression in centered block coordinates.
  Its two components are <math|<around|(|-d<rsub|0>*a,-J*a|)>> as in the
  preceding section. Let <math|X> be the negative right logarithmic
  derivative of the frame in these coordinates, and let <math|C<rsub|\<mu\>>>
  be the positive regulator with scalar symbol <math|c<rsub|\<mu\>>>. The
  linear gauge-fixing map is

  <\equation*>
    F=d<rsup|\<ast\>><math-up|pr><rsub|<math-up|fine>>+C<rsub|\<mu\>>*X.
  </equation*>

  For a gauge curve through the identity, frame covariance gives its frame
  ratio as the inverse gauge transformation. Applying the negative right
  logarithm therefore returns exactly the gauge parameter near zero.
  Differentiating this identity gives <math|X*\<cal-R\>=I>, after expressing
  the gauge tangent in the same coordinates. Also the fine component of the
  gauge tangent is <math|d>. Consequently

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-actual-gauge-identity>F*\<cal-R\>=d<rsup|\<ast\>>*d+C<rsub|\<mu\>>=\<Gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>>.
  </equation>

  There is a coordinate issue in this calculation. The rooted gauge
  coordinates and the centered coordinates are related by a triangular change
  whose derivative is

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|u,r|)>\<longmapsto\><around|(|u,r+K<rsub|0>*u|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|K<rsub|0>> is the derivative of the recentering frame. The
  frame covariance identity gives <math|K<rsub|0>*d<rsub|0>*a=a<rsub|<math-up|root>>-J*a>.
  It follows by substitution that this triangular derivative sends the
  centered gauge tangent to the rooted gauge tangent. The fine projection is
  unchanged. The chain rule sends <math|X> to its product with that same
  triangular derivative, so both sides of <eqref|eq:ex-actual-gauge-identity>
  remain identical. This verifies the identity in the centered chart used for
  the estimates, including its sign; it would be incorrect to combine the
  rooted <math|X> with a centered <math|\<cal-R\>> without this change.

  Fourier transformation yields the identity on every torus grid. The symbols
  are common to the unbounded family of periods, by the local stencil
  construction. They therefore agree at every real momentum by density and at
  every complex momentum in their common strip by analytic uniqueness. For
  real momentum, the Fourier symbol of each real finite-volume matrix
  satisfies <math|F*<around|(|-p|)>=<wide|F<around|(|p|)>|\<bar\>>>. This
  applies to the frame linearization, the fine difference, and the real
  regulator, hence to <math|F> itself. Density of the common finite Fourier
  grids gives this identity at every real momentum, and therefore
  <math|F*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|T>=F<around|(|p|)><rsup|\<ast\>>> there. Thus
  the bilinear holomorphic continuation used below agrees with the positive
  Hermitian square on the real slice. The same argument identifies the
  constant stationary matrix with <math|\<cal-G\>+F*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|T>*F<around|(|p|)>>:
  the fine action contributes its attached kinetic form, the block penalty
  contributes <math|\<kappa\>*N*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|T>*N<around|(|p|)>>, and
  the gauge-fixing term contributes the displayed square. Each is the Hessian
  of the corresponding term of the same action at its identity.

  <subsubsection|The uniform bound for the gauge-fixing map>

  For a unit vector in <math|W<rsub|p>>, the fine derivative term
  <math|d<rsup|\<ast\>>*u> is bounded in unnormalized Euclidean norm by a
  constant depending only on <math|G>, by the Fourier multiplier estimate in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-weighted-frame-penalty>. The frame
  derivative <math|X*v> has normalized block <math|L<rsup|2>> norm at most a
  constant, by the centered-frame induction, restriction to one residue
  block, and the finite coordinate operations defining its logarithm.
  Restriction from the padded patch to this block costs a bounded ratio of
  volumes. Its unnormalized Euclidean norm is therefore at most
  <math|C*L<rsup|2>>. On the fixed small coarse strip the regulator has
  Euclidean operator norm at most <math|C<rprime|'>*\<mu\><rsup|2>>: its
  alias symbol is <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>*<around|(|\<mu\><rsup|2>/<around|(|\<omega\>+\<mu\><rsup|2>|)>|)><rsup|9>>,
  with the denominator estimate of the preceding section, and the two Fourier
  conjugations have uniformly bounded norms. Hence

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||C<rsub|\<mu\>>*X*v|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rprime|'>*C*\<mu\><rsup|2>*L<rsup|2>=C<rprime|'>*C.
  </equation*>

  Adding the fine derivative term gives the required bound
  <math|<around|\||F*v|\|>\<leqslant\>D>, with <math|D> fixed before
  <math|L>. The same stencil construction is holomorphic and periodic in the
  external momentum, since it uses only linear Fourier legs in finite real
  derivatives at the identity and the holomorphic regulator symbol. This
  proves both parts of <eqref|eq:ex-gauge-fixing-receipt> for the selected
  data.

  The inverse estimates of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-weighted-stationary-inverses>
  now apply to the actual common stationary matrix. At the origin the two
  stationary matrix presentations used for the action and its comparison have
  this same matrix. Their inverse bounds consequently use the same width and
  cost; no bound for their positive-order derivatives is asserted by this
  constant-matrix identification.

  <subsection|The kinetic response on the selected
  bridge><label|sec:ex-selected-kinetic-response>

  We supply the fine kinetic matrix bound used in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-stationary-determinant-prices>, and
  identify its three derivative kernels on the selected finite periods. Let
  <math|F<rsub|n>>, <math|0\<leqslant\>n\<leqslant\>2>, be the response
  arrays of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-soft-response-mockups>, with width
  at most <math|C\<geqslant\>0> at the Laurent weight
  <math|t<rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0> (the constants <math|K> and <math|\<eta\>>
  of that theorem). Retain all the theorem's hypotheses on the incoming path
  and metric; in particular the response identity
  <eqref|eq:soft-curvature-receipts-display-SR2> is available on the tori of
  the selected period sequence. Let <math|\<Phi\>> be the actual bridge of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-selected-stationary-linear-data>, with
  fine differences through order one bounded by <math|D*\<mu\><rsup|1+j>>.
  Fix <math|0\<less\>\<sigma\>\<leqslant\>1> in its strip and require
  <math|\<mu\>*\<sigma\>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|\<ast\>>>. This last condition is
  imposed by increasing <math|L> after <math|t<rsub|\<ast\>>> and
  <math|\<sigma\>> have been chosen.

  <subsubsection|Contracting the response arrays>

  For external momenta <math|p<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,p<rsub|n>> and coarse
  coordinate legs <math|a<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,a<rsub|n>>, put
  <math|v<rsub|j>=\<Phi\><around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>*e<rsub|a<rsub|j>>>. For an
  internal input momentum <math|q> the output momentum is
  <math|q<rprime|'>=q+<big|sum><rsub|j>p<rsub|j>>. Define a matrix
  <math|K<rsub|n>*<around|(|p<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,p<rsub|n>;q|)>> by its
  bilinear pairing with arbitrary internal vectors <math|u,w>:

  <\equation*>
    u<rsup|T>*K<rsub|n>*<around|(|p<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,p<rsub|n>;q|)>*w=\<cal-B\><rsub|n>*<around|(|p<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,p<rsub|n>,-q<rprime|'>,q|)>*<around|[|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|n>,u,w|]>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|n>> is the block-periodized multilinear form
  obtained by inserting the array <math|F<rsub|n>> in
  <eqref|eq:soft-curvature-receipts-display-SR2> and summing the base site
  over one fine residue block. Translation of a residue across a block
  boundary carries the corresponding Bloch phase. This defines the pairing on
  coordinate vectors, hence defines the matrix uniquely. We only use momenta
  for which <math|p<rsub|j>,q,q<rprime|'>> belong to the
  width-<math|\<sigma\>> strip.

  Every stitch uses words of length at most one, and its lengths obey

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-kinetic-stitch-count><around|(|n+2|)>+<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|n+2><around|\||\<ell\><rsub|j>|\|>=4.
  </equation>

  This is the precise derivative count: two differences for the value, one
  for the first background derivative, and none for the second. Set
  <math|R=D+5*e<rsup|4>>. In normalized counting measure on a residue block,
  the external leg with word <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>> has supremum norm at most
  <math|\<mu\><rsup|1+<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|j>|\|>>*R>. A unit internal leg
  in the weighted Fourier norm has normalized <math|L<rsup|2>> norm at most
  the same expression, by the multiplier estimate of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-weighted-frame-penalty>. There are
  exactly two such internal legs. Hölder's inequality therefore bounds the
  normalized sum of their product and all external factors by

  <\equation*>
    <big|prod><rsub|j=1><rsup|n+2>\<mu\><rsup|1+<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|j>|\|>>*R.
  </equation*>

  Restoring the unnormalized residue sum multiplies by <math|L<rsup|4>>.
  Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:ex-kinetic-stitch-count> gives the cancellation

  <\equation*>
    L<rsup|4>*<big|prod><rsub|j=1><rsup|n+2>\<mu\><rsup|1+<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|j>|\|>>*R=R<rsup|n+2>.
  </equation*>

  Thus neither a count of fine sites nor a conversion of a full matrix to
  entrywise bounds introduces an extra power of <math|L>.

  We also check complex translation factors. For a shift <math|z> of a
  residue leg, its Bloch extension differs from the permuted residue vector
  by a phase with modulus at most <math|exp
  <around|(|\<sigma\>*<around|(|8+\<mu\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>|)>|)>>. To
  see the bound, write the translated site as its residue plus <math|L> times
  its block quotient. The difference between the two residues has coordinate
  sum at most <math|8*L>; the remaining displacement contributes
  <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>. Multiplying the phases of the <math|n+2>
  legs costs at most

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|8*\<sigma\>*<around|(|n+2|)>>*e<rsup|\<mu\>*\<sigma\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>,
  </equation*>

  where now <math|z> is the whole array shift. The last factor is paid by its
  Laurent width. Summing shifts, color addresses and stitch addresses
  therefore gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-selected-kinetic-bound><around|\<\|\|\>|K<rsub|n>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|n>,<space|2em>A<rsub|n>=N<rsub|n>*e<rsup|8*\<sigma\>*<around|(|n+2|)>>*R<rsup|n+2>*C,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>n\<leqslant\>2,
  </equation>

  where <math|N<rsub|n>> is the product of the numbers of color and stitch
  addresses at level <math|n>. These numbers depend only on the fixed group
  and <math|n>. The norm is the operator norm between the weighted fine space
  and its bilinear dual at the two indicated momenta. Taking
  <math|A<rsub|K>=A<rsub|0>+A<rsub|1>+A<rsub|2>> supplies the common bound
  required in the determinant comparison.

  Absolute Laurent convergence, uniformly on every strictly smaller strip,
  also proves holomorphy of these matrices. Moving a momentum by a reciprocal
  lattice vector changes its residue phases and its block quotient phases by
  inverse factors, so the matrices are periodic. This verifies the common
  analytic extension directly from the arrays and the bridge.

  <subsubsection|Identification with the actual Hessian derivatives>

  Let <math|I<rsub|m>> be the incoming action on a fine torus, as a function
  of logarithmic link coordinates, and let
  <math|\<cal-D\>=<math-ss|D><rsub|m>> be the split-field map of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-soft-curvature-receipts>: on every link
  <math|e>,

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-D\>*<around|(|x<rsub|+>,x<rsub|->|)><rsub|e>=log <around*|(|exp
    <around|(|x<rsub|+,e>|)>*exp <around|(|x<rsub|-,e>|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Near zero, <math|I<rsub|m>*<around|(|\<cal-D\>*<around|(|x<rsub|+>,x<rsub|->|)>|)>>
  is exactly the action at the background configuration <math|exp
  <around|(|x<rsub|->|)>> after the left increment <math|exp
  <around|(|x<rsub|+>|)>>. Its Hessian in <math|x<rsub|+>> is therefore the
  fine kinetic Hessian in the left coordinates used for the stationary
  determinant. Differentiating it <math|n> times in the background
  <math|x<rsub|->=W*x>, and testing with two arbitrary internal vectors,
  gives precisely the mixed response with <math|n> minus-species legs
  followed by two plus-species legs. Analyticity permits this reordering of
  derivatives. This is the species order in
  <eqref|eq:soft-curvature-receipts-display-SR2>.

  On a coarse Fourier wave, the actual installed matrix <math|W> produces the
  block wave with residue amplitude <math|\<Phi\><around|(|p|)>*e<rsub|a>>,
  by the finite Fourier identity of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-selected-stationary-linear-data>.
  Insert these waves and the two internal block waves into
  <eqref|eq:soft-curvature-receipts-display-SR2>. The sum over coarse base
  sites is zero unless the total coarse momentum vanishes; otherwise it is
  the coarse site cardinality. In the latter case the remaining residue and
  shift sum is exactly the defining pairing of <math|K<rsub|n>> above.
  Fourier waves span the full finite-volume space, and testing against every
  outgoing wave determines a matrix. Thus
  <math|K<rsub|0>,K<rsub|1>,K<rsub|2>> are the actual value, first derivative
  and second derivative kernels of the same kinetic Hessian on every selected
  torus, including nonzero momentum transfer. They are not only limiting
  coefficient pictures.

  Finally the local bridge legs used by the frame and penalty estimates obey
  their required common pointwise bound: at a residue the color Euclidean
  norm is at most <math|<sqrt|d<rsub|1>>*<space|0.17em>D*\<mu\>>, because
  there are <math|d<rsub|1>> one-form color coordinates. Enlarging the local
  price to <math|<sqrt|d<rsub|1>>*D+e<rsup|4>> also covers the fixed
  coordinate legs. This price is independent of <math|L>. Consequently the
  local estimates, the kinetic triple and all finite-volume identifications
  in the determinant comparison refer to one and the same selected bridge and
  path.

  <subsection|Uniform prices for the determinant
  comparison><label|sec:ex-stationary-determinant-prices>

  The absolute one-loop term counts all fine variables in a block and
  therefore has a bound proportional to <math|L<rsup|4>>. Its comparison with
  the gauge-completed fine determinant has a better bound. We prove that
  bound before extracting the quadratic coefficient. Throughout this section
  <math|L> is odd, <math|\<mu\>=L<rsup|-1>>, and all constants are chosen
  from the fixed group, patch size, bridge price and penalty coefficient,
  before <math|L>. The fine kinetic response is an additional input: on a
  positive common strip of width <math|\<sigma\>>, assume its value and first
  two background kernels satisfy <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|K<rsub|0>|\<\|\|\>>,<around|\<\|\|\>|K<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>,<around|\<\|\|\>|K<rsub|i*j>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|K>>
  in the weighted fine one-form norms, at every admissible set of
  intermediate momenta. Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-selected-kinetic-response>
  constructs that response and its bound for the selected actions.

  <subsubsection|Differentiated Fourier matrices>

  We need the value and the first two background derivatives of a matrix. An
  external leg of momentum <math|p> changes an input momentum <math|q> to
  <math|q+p>. Write these three kernels as <math|M<rsub|0><around|(|q|)>>,
  <math|M<rsub|i>*<around|(|p;q|)>> and <math|M<rsub|i*j>*<around|(|p,r;q|)>>.
  All momenta that occur as intermediate inputs or outputs must lie in the
  specified strip. A bound on these three kernels always uses the weighted
  norms at their respective input and output momenta. It does not mean an
  entrywise bound. The holomorphic rear reverses the endpoints while
  retaining the external momenta:

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|M<rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|0><around|(|q|)>=M<rsub|0>*<around|(|-q|)><rsup|T>,<space|1em><around|(|M<rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|i><around|(|p;q|)>=M<rsub|i>*<around|(|p;-q-p|)><rsup|T>,<space|1em><around|(|M<rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|i*j><around|(|p,r;q|)>=M<rsub|i*j>*<around|(|p,r;-q-p-r|)><rsup|T>.
  </equation*>

  This convention is used in all products below.

  Products obey the ordinary Leibniz rule with these intermediate momenta.
  Thus common bounds <math|A,B> for two triples give the bound <math|4*A*B>
  for their product: there is one value term, two first-derivative terms, and
  four second-derivative terms. If <math|M<rsub|0><rsup|-1>> has bound
  <math|F>, its derivative kernels follow by differentiating
  <math|M<rsup|-1>*M=1>:

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|M<rsup|-1>|)><rsub|i>>|<cell|=-M<rsup|-1>*M<rsub|i>*M<rsup|-1>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|(|M<rsup|-1>|)><rsub|i*j>>|<cell|=M<rsup|-1>*M<rsub|i>*M<rsup|-1>*M<rsub|j>*M<rsup|-1>+M<rsup|-1>*M<rsub|j>*M<rsup|-1>*M<rsub|i>*M<rsup|-1>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|1em>-M<rsup|-1>*M<rsub|i*j>*M<rsup|-1>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Intermediate momenta in these formulas are determined by composition. In
  particular a common inverse-jet bound is
  <math|F+F<rsup|2>*A+2*F<rsup|3>*A<rsup|2>+F<rsup|2>*A>. These formulas use
  only the inverse at zero background; they impose no uniform neighborhood of
  invertibility in the full background space.

  Here are the local inputs to this calculus. Let <math|d<around|(|U|)>> be
  the fine covariant difference at background <math|U>. On each edge its
  difference from <math|d<around|(|1|)>> is the adjoint action of the edge
  variable minus the identity, followed by the fixed endpoint shift. The
  identity

  <\equation*>
    d<around|(|U|)>=d<around|(|1|)>+<around|(|d<around|(|U|)>-d<around|(|1|)>|)>
  </equation*>

  is therefore a fixed shift operator multiplied by local smooth functions of
  one fine link. Positive derivatives through order two of these functions
  have a factor <math|\<mu\>>: every differentiated fine link has size at
  most <math|\<mu\>*C>, and products of more such factors are smaller since
  <math|\<mu\>\<leqslant\>1>. Passing from Euclidean norms to a single
  weighted fine norm costs at most <math|e<rsup|4>*\<mu\><rsup|-1>>, by
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-weighted-frame-penalty>. The factor
  <math|\<mu\>> pays this cost. The constant term <math|d<around|(|1|)>> has
  a uniform weighted bound directly from its multiplier <math|q>: on the real
  slice <math|<around|\||q<around|(|k<rsub|l>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>h<rsub|l>>,
  and throughout the complex strip of width at most one
  <math|<around|\||q<around|(|k<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>5*h<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>>,
  by the multiplier estimate of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-weighted-frame-penalty>.
  The weight <math|h<rsub|l>> is defined with the real part of the momentum,
  so the inequality without the factor five does not extend to complex
  momenta. Consequently the first two background derivatives of
  <math|d<around|(|U|)>> are uniformly bounded in both placements needed for
  <math|d<rsup|\<ast\>>*d> and <math|d*d<rsup|\<ast\>>>.

  The derivative of the centered frame, denoted by <math|X<around|(|U|)>>,
  has bound <math|C*L<rsup|2>> from the joint weighted space to the ordinary
  fine scalar space, through two background derivatives. Indeed these are at
  most third derivatives of the frame, with one internal direction of charge
  one and the remaining external directions of charge zero. The frame
  calculus bounds the normalized <math|L<rsup|2>> output; removing that
  normalization multiplies by <math|L<rsup|2>>. The logarithm and the finite
  chart changes preserve this estimate. The block constraint derivative
  <math|Q<around|(|U|)>> and its coarse penalty form <math|H<around|(|U|)>>
  have uniformly bounded kernels. The actual penalty Hessian
  <math|B<around|(|U|)>> has the same property: its first two background
  derivatives require derivatives through order four, with two internal
  directions of charge one. Their total charge is two, exactly the
  <math|L<rsup|1>> case of the weighted penalty estimate.

  These estimates apply on one common smaller strip. The external directions
  are the actual stationary bridge of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-selected-stationary-linear-data>.
  The same finite stencils define the kernels on every torus. Thus the
  preceding bounds apply to their common holomorphic Fourier matrices, rather
  than to separately chosen extensions of finite-grid matrices.

  <subsubsection|Why the regulator has a uniform trace bound>

  For a map between weighted Hilbert spaces, let
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>> denote the nuclear
  norm after transforming to orthonormal coordinates. We use

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|A*B*C|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|A|\<\|\|\>><around|\<\|\|\>|B|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1><around|\<\|\|\>|C|\<\|\|\>>,<space|2em><around|\||<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>B|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|B|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  The second inequality is applied to endomorphisms with the same input and
  output momentum. Both statements follow from a singular value
  decomposition; the first also follows by decomposing <math|B> as a sum of
  rank-one operators. They introduce no dimension factor.

  Set

  <\equation*>
    P<around|(|U|)>=\<mu\><rsup|2>*<around|(|d<around|(|U|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*d<around|(|U|)>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*I|)><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>C<around|(|U|)>=\<mu\><rsup|2>*P<around|(|U|)><rsup|9>.
  </equation*>

  Here and below the star means transpose with opposite Fourier momentum in
  the holomorphic formulas. On the real slice it is the usual adjoint. The
  scalar inverse estimate of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-weighted-stationary-inverses>,
  the preceding product and inverse rules, and the two weight conversions
  show that all three kernels of <math|P> have a common Euclidean bound
  independent of <math|L>. The factor <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>> cancels the two
  costs <math|O<around|(|\<mu\><rsup|-1>|)>>. It follows that the three
  kernels of <math|C> have operator bound <math|C<rsub|0>*\<mu\><rsup|2>>.

  We need the stronger nuclear bound, for which the ninth power is useful. At
  zero background, <math|P<rsub|0>> has alias eigenvalues
  <math|d<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|k<rsub|l>|)>=\<mu\><rsup|2>/<around|(|\<omega\><around|(|k<rsub|l>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|2>|)>>.
  On the strip of width <math|1/128>, after choosing a real fundamental cube,
  the alias distance <math|n=<math-up|dist><rsub|\<infty\>><around|(|l,0|)>>
  satisfies

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||d<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|k<rsub|l>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|2|<around|(|1+n|)><rsup|2>>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|n=0> this is the scalar denominator bound. For <math|n\<gtr\>0>,
  the real alias difference has size at least <math|2*\<mu\>*n>; the same
  denominator comparison gives the displayed estimate. The number of residues
  with distance <math|n> is at most <math|<around|(|2*n+1|)><rsup|4>>. Hence

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|l><around|\||d<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|k<rsub|l>|)>|\|><rsup|3>\<leqslant\>8*<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>0><frac|<around|(|2*n+1|)><rsup|4>|<around|(|n+1|)><rsup|6>>\<leqslant\>128*<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>0><around|(|n+1|)><rsup|-2>\<less\>\<infty\>.
  </equation*>

  The fixed color multiplicity and the bounded Fourier conjugations only
  multiply this constant. Thus <math|<around|(|P<rsup|3>|)><rsub|0>> has a
  uniform nuclear bound, although <math|P<rsub|0>> itself need not have one.

  If a triple <math|V> has operator bound <math|a> and its value
  <math|V<rsub|0>> has nuclear bound <math|b>, the triple for
  <math|V<rsup|3>> has nuclear bound <math|20*a<rsup|2>*b>. To see this,
  distribute at most two derivatives among three factors. Every term has an
  undifferentiated factor, which can be estimated in nuclear norm; all other
  factors use operator norm. The value, first derivative and second
  derivative have at most one, three and nine terms, respectively. The larger
  constant 20 also accommodates the grouped product estimates above. Apply
  this to <math|V=P<rsup|3>>. We obtain

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-regulator-nuclear><around|\<\|\|\>|C<rsub|0>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,<around|\<\|\|\>|C<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,<around|\<\|\|\>|C<rsub|i*j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|1>*\<mu\><rsup|2>.
  </equation>

  This argument needs only the value-level spectral calculation for
  <math|P<rsup|3>>; derivatives of its eigenvectors are not used.

  <subsubsection|Cancellation of the fine kinetic form>

  Let <math|b> project the joint space onto its fine one-form part, and let
  <math|e> project it onto its coarse scalar part. Let <math|s<rsub|a>> be
  the fixed map selecting the one-form component in direction <math|a>.
  Denote the fine kinetic Hessian triple by <math|K>. Define

  <\equation*>
    X=d<rsup|\<ast\>>*b,<space|2em>T=C*X<rsub|<math-up|fr>>,<space|2em>F=X+T,
  </equation*>

  where <math|X<rsub|<math-up|fr>>> is the centered frame derivative denoted
  by <math|X<around|(|U|)>> above. The change of notation separates it from
  <math|d<rsup|\<ast\>>*b>. The three matrix triples to be compared are

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|M<rsub|<math-up|act>>>|<cell|=b<rsup|\<ast\>>*K*b+B+F<rsup|\<ast\>>*F,>>|<row|<cell|M<rsub|<math-up|core>>>|<cell|=b<rsup|\<ast\>>*K*b+Q<rsup|\<ast\>>*H*Q+F<rsup|\<ast\>>*F,>>|<row|<cell|M<rsub|<math-up|rank>>>|<cell|=b<rsup|\<ast\>>*<around*|(|K+d*d<rsup|\<ast\>>+<big|sum><rsub|a=1><rsup|4>s<rsub|a><rsup|\<ast\>>*C*s<rsub|a>|)>*b+e<rsup|\<ast\>>*e.>>>>
  </align*>

  The first uses the full penalty Hessian; the second retains its constraint
  pullback. Their distinction will matter in the next comparison of
  low-momentum coefficients.

  The weighted norm bounds above, including the bound for <math|K>, give a
  common operator bound <math|A> for all three triples and for
  <math|d<rsup|\<ast\>>*d+C>. Their zero-background inverses have one common
  bound <math|F<rsub|0>> on a smaller strip of width <math|\<eta\>>, by the
  previous two sections. Indeed the zero-background penalty Hessian equals
  its constraint pullback, and the gauge-completed fine matrix is precisely
  the auxiliary matrix treated there. Take the minimum of the finitely many
  widths and the maximum of their costs.

  The difference of the second and third triples is exactly

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-core-rank-difference>M<rsub|<math-up|core>>-M<rsub|<math-up|rank>>=Q<rsup|\<ast\>>*H*Q+T<rsup|\<ast\>>*F+X<rsup|\<ast\>>*T-b<rsup|\<ast\>>*<big|sum><rsub|a>s<rsub|a><rsup|\<ast\>>*C*s<rsub|a>*b-e<rsup|\<ast\>>*e.
  </equation>

  This is an identity at all three derivative levels. In particular the
  entire <math|K> triple cancels, not just its value at the origin. It
  follows by expanding <math|F=X+T> and using
  <math|X<rsup|\<ast\>>*X=b<rsup|\<ast\>>*d*d<rsup|\<ast\>>*b>.

  Every term on the right has a nuclear bound independent of <math|L>. For
  <math|Q<rsup|\<ast\>>*H*Q>, take the nuclear bound on the fixed-dimensional
  coarse space before multiplying by <math|Q<rsup|\<ast\>>> and <math|Q>; the
  estimate <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|H<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>dim
  <around|(|<text|coarse one-forms>|)><around|\<\|\|\>|H<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>>
  works for each derivative. The same argument applies to
  <math|e<rsup|\<ast\>>*e>. For <math|T=C*X<rsub|<math-up|fr>>>,
  <eqref|eq:ex-regulator-nuclear> cancels the <math|L<rsup|2>> frame cost
  because <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>*L<rsup|2>=1>. Multiplication by <math|F> or
  <math|X> preserves its nuclear bound. Finally, converting the regulator
  term to weighted fine norms costs at most <math|e<rsup|8>*\<mu\><rsup|-2>>,
  paid by its factor <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>>; the four component maps
  <math|s<rsub|a>> have fixed bounds. Leibniz's rule proves the same
  estimates through order two. Thus there is <math|k>, independent of
  <math|L> and of the fine kinetic price, with

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-core-rank-nuclear><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|M<rsub|<math-up|core>>-M<rsub|<math-up|rank>>|)><rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>k,<space|2em>j=0,1,2.
  </equation>

  The inverse bound <math|F<rsub|0>> and absolute bound <math|A> may still
  depend on the stated kinetic hypotheses. That dependence has not been
  removed by this cancellation.

  <subsubsection|The second logarithmic derivative>

  For any of the invertible triples define its two-leg trace density by

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-T\><rsub|M>*<around|(|i,j;p,q|)>=<math-up|tr><around*|[|M<rsub|0><around|(|q|)><rsup|-1>*M<rsub|i*j>*<around|(|-p,p;q|)>-M<rsub|0><around|(|q|)><rsup|-1>*M<rsub|i>*<around|(|-p;q+p|)>*M<rsub|0>*<around|(|q+p|)><rsup|-1>*M<rsub|j>*<around|(|p;q|)>|]>.
  </equation*>

  This is the second derivative of <math|log det M>, obtained by
  differentiating <math|D*log det M=<math-up|tr><around|(|M<rsup|-1>*D*M|)>>.
  It requires no choice of a global logarithm on the strip. The formula
  itself is holomorphic wherever the constant inverses exist. Restrict both
  independent momenta to width <math|\<eta\>/2>, so every intermediate
  momentum lies in width <math|\<eta\>>.

  If the fiber dimension is <math|d>, the operator bounds give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-absolute-trace-density><around|\||\<cal-T\><rsub|M>|\|>\<leqslant\>d*<around|(|F<rsub|0>*A+F<rsub|0><rsup|2>*A<rsup|2>|)>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|d\<leqslant\>L<rsup|4>*<around|(|d<rsub|0>+d<rsub|1>|)>>, with
  <math|d<rsub|0>,d<rsub|1>> the fixed scalar and one-form color dimensions.
  This explains the absolute <math|L<rsup|4>> cost.

  For <math|M=M<rsub|<math-up|core>>> and <math|N=M<rsub|<math-up|rank>>> put

  <\equation*>
    D=F<rsub|0>*k*F<rsub|0>,<space|2em>E=D*A+F<rsub|0>*k.
  </equation*>

  The resolvent identity gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|M<rsub|0><rsup|-1>-N<rsub|0><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>D>.
  The elementary telescoping identity <math|U*V-U<rprime|'>*V<rprime|'>=<around|(|U-U<rprime|'>|)>*V+U<rprime|'>*<around|(|V-V<rprime|'>|)>>
  then bounds the difference of <math|M<rsub|0><rsup|-1>*M<rsub|i*j>> and
  <math|N<rsub|0><rsup|-1>*N<rsub|i*j>> by <math|E> in nuclear norm. For the
  four-factor term, first compare the initial two factors, at cost <math|E>;
  compare the next inverse, at additional cost <math|F<rsub|0>*A*D>; and
  finally compare the last derivative. Keeping the remaining factors in
  operator norm gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|M<rsub|0><rsup|-1>*M<rsub|i>*M<rsub|0><rsup|-1>*M<rsub|j>-N<rsub|0><rsup|-1>*N<rsub|i>*N<rsub|0><rsup|-1>*N<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|(|E*F<rsub|0>+F<rsub|0>*A*D|)>*A+F<rsub|0>*A*F<rsub|0>*k.
  </equation*>

  All these products respect the displayed intermediate momenta. Taking
  traces proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-relative-trace-density><around|\||\<cal-T\><rsub|M<rsub|<math-up|core>>>-\<cal-T\><rsub|M<rsub|<math-up|rank>>>|\|>\<leqslant\>E+<around|(|E*F<rsub|0>+F<rsub|0>*A*D|)>*A+F<rsub|0>*A*F<rsub|0>*k.
  </equation>

  There is no dimension factor in this estimate.

  The one-loop densities are <math|<frac|1|2>*\<cal-T\><rsub|M<rsub|<math-up|act>>>-\<cal-T\><rsub|d<rsup|\<ast\>>*d+C>>,
  <math|<frac|1|2>*\<cal-T\><rsub|M<rsub|<math-up|core>>>-\<cal-T\><rsub|d<rsup|\<ast\>>*d+C>>
  and <math|<frac|1|2>*\<cal-T\><rsub|M<rsub|<math-up|rank>>>-\<cal-T\><rsub|d<rsup|\<ast\>>*d+C>>.
  Their individual bounds may all be enlarged to twice the right-hand side of
  <eqref|eq:ex-absolute-trace-density>. In the difference of the latter two
  densities the scalar determinant cancels exactly, leaving half the
  difference in <eqref|eq:ex-relative-trace-density>. This proves the
  required uniform comparison price. Identification with the thermal
  stationary coefficient, and the disappearance of the remaining
  action-versus-core quadratic difference, are separate steps below.

  <subsection|The thermal determinant and its contact
  term><label|sec:ex-thermal-contact-cancellation>

  We now relate the determinant matrices to the stationary integral and show
  that replacing the full penalty Hessian by its constraint pullback does not
  alter the leading quadratic coefficient. We retain the actual stationary
  branch, predictor and tree gauge from the preceding sections. The group has
  nonabelian simple real Lie algebra. The finite-volume stationary Hessian
  gap and the predictor branch hypotheses remain in force. We also retain the
  exact matching of the incoming and outgoing quadratic forms with the
  selected bridge; in particular its constraint map obeys
  <math|Q*W=I-\<kappa\><rsup|-1>*K<rsub|c>> for the selected outgoing kinetic
  matrix <math|K<rsub|c>>. Stationarity and the gap alone do not identify an
  independently chosen outgoing matrix. All identities below concern germs at
  the trivial field.

  <subsubsection|The Haar factor in tree coordinates>

  Let <math|y> denote the free tree-gauge coordinates of a stationary point,
  and let <math|j<around|(|y|)>\<gtr\>0> be the product, over these
  coordinates, of the Haar density ratio <math|J> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:haar-chart>; it is analytic and positive
  near zero, with <math|j<around|(|0|)>=1>, and by
  <eqref|eq:haar-chart-measure> the probability Haar measure in exponential
  coordinates has density a positive constant times <math|J>. Define the
  left-increment coordinate map, componentwise, by

  <\equation*>
    s<rsub|y><around|(|z|)>=log <around*|(|exp <around|(|z|)>*exp
    <around|(|y|)><rsup|-1>|)>,<space|2em>s<rsub|y><around|(|y|)>=0.
  </equation*>

  It is a local diffeomorphism. Right invariance of probability Haar measure
  and the ordinary change-of-variables formula give

  <\equation*>
    j<around|(|z|)>=<around|\||det D*s<rsub|y><around|(|z|)>|\|>*j<around|(|s<rsub|y><around|(|z|)>|)>
  </equation*>

  on the common coordinate neighborhood. For completeness, apply both
  change-of-variables formulas to any compact subset of that neighborhood.
  The two continuous positive densities have equal integrals over every such
  set. If they differed at a point, continuity would give a closed ball of
  positive Lebesgue measure on which one density is strictly larger,
  contradicting that equality. Evaluating at <math|z=y> yields

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-haar-left-jacobian>j<around|(|y|)>=<around|\||det
    D*s<rsub|y><around|(|y|)>|\|>.
  </equation>

  Thus the Haar density cannot be discarded when the stationary point moves
  away from zero.

  Let <math|H> be the Hessian of the joint action in left coordinates at a
  stationary configuration, let <math|R> be its fine gauge-tangent matrix,
  and let <math|P> insert the free tree increments. Gauge invariance and
  stationarity give <math|H*R=0>; <math|H> is symmetric. The square matrix
  <math|S=<around|[|R*P|]>> has <math|det <around|(|S|)><rsup|2>=1> in the
  physical counting bases. To verify this last assertion, order the fine
  vertices by tree height, assigning to a nonroot vertex its upward tree edge
  and to a root its coarse scalar coordinate. On the tree edges the gauge
  variation is the signed difference between the vertex and its parent,
  because those edge variables equal the identity. At a root it is minus the
  root gauge parameter. The resulting gauge block is triangular, with
  diagonal entries <math|\<pm\>1>. The free-increment block is the identity,
  and the other upper block is zero. Hence the squared determinant is one,
  regardless of the free edge variables.

  Let <math|L> be the gauge-fixing derivative and <math|\<Gamma\>=L*R> the
  positive scalar gauge matrix. Set <math|C=P<rsup|T>*H*P> and <math|B=L*P>.
  Direct multiplication gives

  <\equation*>
    S<rsup|T>*<around|(|H+L<rsup|T>*L|)>*S=<matrix|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<Gamma\>>|<cell|B>>|<row|<cell|0>|<cell|I>>>>><rsup|<space|-0.17em>T><matrix|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|I>|<cell|0>>|<row|<cell|0>|<cell|C>>>>><matrix|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<Gamma\>>|<cell|B>>|<row|<cell|0>|<cell|I>>>>>.
  </equation*>

  Consequently

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-gauge-det-factorization>det <around|(|H+L<rsup|T>*L|)>=det
    <around|(|\<Gamma\>|)><rsup|2>*det C.
  </equation>

  The tree-chart Hessian <math|H<rsub|<math-up|tree>>> is
  <math|D*s<rsub|y><around|(|y|)><rsup|T>*C*D*s<rsub|y><around|(|y|)>>: the
  additional second-derivative term in the chain rule vanishes by
  stationarity. Combining this with <eqref|eq:ex-haar-left-jacobian> proves

  <\equation*>
    j<around|(|y|)><rsup|2>*det M<rsub|<math-up|act>>=det
    H<rsub|<math-up|tree>>*det \<Gamma\><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  The tree gap, positive Haar density, and positive scalar determinant show
  that every determinant inside the logarithms is positive near the origin.
  Thus

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-thermal-determinant><frac|1|2>*log det
    H<rsub|<math-up|tree>>-log j<around|(|y|)>=<frac|1|2>*log det
    M<rsub|<math-up|act>>-log det \<Gamma\>.
  </equation>

  Adding the inherited loop gives the thermal term of the stationary Gaussian
  integral with exactly its original normalization.

  <subsubsection|Replacing the stationary graph by its linear tangent>

  Write <math|\<zeta\><around|(|x|)>> for the actual centered stationary
  representative. Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-selected-stationary-linear-data>
  gives <math|\<zeta\><around|(|0|)>=0> and, under the stationary tangent
  input stated there, <math|D*\<zeta\><around|(|0|)>*x=<around|(|W*x,0|)>>.
  Each scalar logarithmic determinant under consideration is invariant under
  simultaneous constant color rotation in the coarse, fine and scalar
  coordinates. Indeed these rotations act orthogonally; they conjugate the
  Hessian and the scalar gauge matrix. The centered-frame change is
  equivariant because group multiplication, the nearest-group map and the
  logarithm are equivariant under conjugation.

  An invariant linear functional on one copy of the Lie algebra is zero. Its
  derivative along <math|<math-up|Ad><around|(|exp t*a|)>*b> annihilates
  every bracket <math|<around|[|a,b|]>>. The derived algebra is a nonzero
  ideal and therefore is the whole algebra by nonabelian simplicity. Applying
  this argument to each coordinate proves that the first derivative of each
  invariant scalar function vanishes at zero. The second-derivative chain
  rule therefore gives

  <\equation*>
    D<rsup|2>*<around*|(|f*<around|(|x,\<zeta\><around|(|x|)>|)>|)><rsub|x=0><around|[|u,v|]>=D<rsup|2>*f<around|(|0|)><around|[|<around|(|u,W*u,0|)>,<around|(|v,W*v,0|)>|]>.
  </equation*>

  This is why the linear external legs used in the Fourier matrix calculus
  compute the thermal Hessian of the actual nonlinear branch. There is no
  assertion that the nonlinear branches themselves coincide.

  Rooted and centered internal increments are related by a triangular
  coordinate change with determinant one. On shell the full Hessians
  transform by congruence; the constraint-pullback Hessian does so even off
  shell. Hence this change preserves their determinants in the calculations
  below. Gauge changes preserve the same determinants by orthogonal
  congruence. These observations justify working in rooted coordinates for
  the contact calculation and centered coordinates for the uniform estimates.

  <subsubsection|The finite-volume contact identity>

  Let <math|w<rsub|x><around|(|v|)>> be the logarithmic change of the block
  predictor when an internal left increment <math|v> is applied at background
  <math|x>; <math|w<rsub|x><around|(|0|)>=0>. Let <math|b<around|(|x|)>> be
  the gradient, with respect to this predictor increment, of the coarse
  penalty. Write <math|A<around|(|x|)>=M<rsub|<math-up|core>><around|(|x|)>>
  in rooted coordinates and define

  <\equation*>
    S<rsub|x><around|(|a|)><rsub|i*j>=<around|\<langle\>|a,D<rsub|v><rsup|2>*w<rsub|x><around|(|0|)><around|[|e<rsub|i>,e<rsub|j>|]>|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em>Z<around|(|x|)>=S<rsub|x><around|(|b<around|(|x|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The Hessian chain rule for the penalty says exactly

  <\equation*>
    M<rsub|<math-up|act>><around|(|x|)>=A<around|(|x|)>+Z<around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  It separates the pullback of the penalty Hessian from the gradient of the
  penalty paired with the second derivative of its constraint. At zero
  <math|b<around|(|0|)>=0>, and hence <math|Z<around|(|0|)>=0>.

  Define a coarse vector <math|Y<around|(|x|)>> by

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<langle\>|a,Y<around|(|x|)>|\<rangle\>>=<math-up|tr><around*|(|A<around|(|x|)><rsup|-1>*S<rsub|x><around|(|a|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  It is analytic for this fixed finite volume. Gauge covariance follows
  directly from this formula: the internal matrices transform by the same
  congruence and the predictor output by the coarse adjoint action, so
  cyclicity of trace gives the coarse adjoint action on <math|Y>. At zero
  each component of <math|Y<around|(|0|)>> is fixed by constant color
  rotations and is therefore zero. This also follows by differentiating the
  adjoint action: an invariant vector is central, and a nonabelian simple Lie
  algebra has zero center.

  Let <math|K<rsub|c>> be the coarse kinetic matrix and put
  <math|J=-K<rsub|c>>. The exact linear bridge identity is

  <\equation*>
    Q*W=I-\<kappa\><rsup|-1>*K<rsub|c>.
  </equation*>

  The quadratic penalty at the identity is
  <math|<frac|\<kappa\>|2>*<around|\||x-z|\|><rsup|2>>. Its gradient in
  <math|z> has derivative <math|\<kappa\>*<around|(|z-x|)>>. Applying the
  bridge identity therefore gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-penalty-gradient-derivative>D*b<around|(|0|)>=J.
  </equation>

  Along the stationary graph this remains the same derivative, because its
  tangent is <math|W>. In particular <math|D*Z<around|(|0|)>*u=S<rsub|0>*<around|(|J*u|)>>.

  The identity <math|det <around|(|A+Z|)>=det A*det
  <around|(|1+A<rsup|-1>*Z|)>> and <math|Z<around|(|0|)>=0> give, through
  second order,

  <\equation*>
    log det <around|(|A+Z|)>-log det A=<math-up|tr><around|(|A<rsup|-1>*Z|)>-<tfrac|1|2><math-up|tr><around|(|A<rsup|-1>*Z*A<rsup|-1>*Z|)>+O<around|(|<around|\||x|\|><rsup|3>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|<math-up|tr><around|(|A<rsup|-1>*Z|)>=<around|\<langle\>|b,Y|\<rangle\>>>
  and <math|b<around|(|0|)>=Y<around|(|0|)>=0>, differentiation gives the
  exact identity

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|D<rsup|2>*<around|(|log det
    M<rsub|<math-up|act>>-log det M<rsub|<math-up|core>>|)><around|(|0|)><around|[|u,v|]>>|<cell|=<around|\<langle\>|J*u,D*Y<around|(|0|)>*v|\<rangle\>>+<around|\<langle\>|J*v,D*Y<around|(|0|)>*u|\<rangle\>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|1em>-<math-up|tr><around*|(|A<around|(|0|)><rsup|-1>*S<rsub|0>*<around|(|J*u|)>*A<around|(|0|)><rsup|-1>*S<rsub|0>*<around|(|J*v|)>|)>.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-contact-hessian>>>>>
  </align>

  The first two terms include the derivative of the inverse matrix; it has
  not been dropped. The vanishing of <math|Y<around|(|0|)>> is what removes
  the term involving the second derivative of <math|b>.

  There is also a Ward identity

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-contact-ward>D*Y<around|(|0|)>*d<rsub|c>*a=0
  </equation>

  for every coarse scalar field <math|a>. To prove it on the actual branch,
  gauge the trivial stationary configuration by <math|exp <around|(|t*a|)>>
  at coarse sites, then restore tree gauge. Gauge invariance preserves
  stationarity; uniqueness in the tree chart identifies the restored
  configuration with the selected stationary branch on this curve. The coarse
  field has tangent <math|-d<rsub|c>*a>. Covariance of <math|Y> and
  <math|Y<around|(|0|)>=0> show that <math|Y> vanishes along the curve.
  Differentiation gives <eqref|eq:ex-contact-ward>. Replacing the branch by
  its linear tangent does not alter <math|D*Y<around|(|0|)>>, by the
  first-derivative chain rule. This proves the identity including the zero
  modes of the coarse difference.

  <subsubsection|Passing the identity to the common momentum picture>

  Fix <math|L> for this paragraph. The rooted frame and its change to
  centered coordinates are analytic finite stencils. The inverse of
  <math|A<rsub|0>> is holomorphic on a fixed strip. These facts construct a
  common holomorphic matrix kernel for <math|A<around|(|x|)><rsup|-1>*S<rsub|x><around|(|a|)>>
  and for its square, with two kinds of external legs: a coarse background
  leg and a separate coarse test leg <math|a>. Indeed express
  <math|S<rsub|x><around|(|a|)>> using the second derivative of the local
  predictor, conjugate the centered inverse by the triangular change, and
  multiply. Every factor has the same finite-volume Fourier representation;
  products and inverse derivatives preserve it. Only a finite bound for this
  fixed <math|L> is required here.

  Take normalized traces per coarse site. Finite Fourier inversion identifies
  them with averages of the common kernels over the internal momentum grid.
  Holomorphy on a smaller strip bounds the Fourier coefficients
  exponentially, so these averages converge to the torus integral, uniformly
  for real external momentum. The same convergence holds when an external
  momentum varies along grid points approaching a prescribed real momentum:
  subtract the limiting integral at that grid point and use its continuity.
  Thus both the finite-volume contact identity and its Ward identity pass to
  all real momenta along any divergent family of coarse periods.

  Denote the resulting mixed background/test trace kernel by
  <math|M<rsub|i*j><around|(|p|)>>, and the second test/test derivative
  kernel of the trace of the square by <math|B<rsub|i*j><around|(|p|)>>.
  Explicitly, at zero background its finite-volume bilinear value on test
  fields <math|a,b> is

  <\equation*>
    2<math-up|tr><around*|(|A<around|(|0|)><rsup|-1>*S<rsub|0><around|(|a|)>*A<around|(|0|)><rsup|-1>*S<rsub|0><around|(|b|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|B> includes both ordered cross terms. They are analytic near
  zero. The Ward identity says

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|i><around|(|d<rsub|c>*<around|(|-p|)>*a|)><rsub|i>*M<rsub|i*j><around|(|p|)>=0.
  </equation*>

  Fix a component <math|i=<around|(|\<nu\>,\<alpha\>|)>> and take <math|a> in
  color <math|\<alpha\>> and <math|p=t*e<rsub|\<nu\>>>. Only that directional
  difference survives, giving <math|q<rsub|\<nu\>>*<around|(|-t*e<rsub|\<nu\>>|)>*M<rsub|i*j>*<around|(|t*e<rsub|\<nu\>>|)>=0>.
  The first factor is nonzero for all sufficiently small nonzero real
  <math|t>. Continuity gives <math|M<rsub|i*j><around|(|0|)>=0>. Analyticity
  then implies <math|M<rsub|i*j><around|(|p|)>=O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|>|)>>
  along every ray, not just that coordinate ray.

  For a fixed external component <math|a>, put
  <math|x<rsub|j><around|(|p|)>=<around|(|-K<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>*e<rsub|a>|)><rsub|j>>.
  Since <math|K<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>=d<rsub|1>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|T>*H<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>*d<rsub|1><around|(|p|)>>
  with <math|H<rsub|c>> analytic, <math|x<rsub|j><around|(|p|)>=O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>|)>>.
  The infinite-volume version of <eqref|eq:ex-contact-hessian>, including the
  factor one half in the thermal determinant, has diagonal density

  <\equation*>
    <frac|1|2>*<around*|[|W<around|(|p|)>+W*<around|(|-p|)>-<frac|1|2>*V<around|(|p|)>|]>,<space|2em>W<around|(|p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j>x<rsub|j><around|(|p|)>*M<rsub|a*j><around|(|p|)>,<space|1em>V<around|(|p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i,j>x<rsub|i>*<around|(|-p|)>*x<rsub|j><around|(|p|)>*B<rsub|i*j><around|(|p|)>.
  </equation*>

  The factor <math|1/2> in front of <math|V> arises because the second
  derivative of the trace of the square contains the two equal cross terms.
  Now <math|W> has ray order at least three and <math|V> has ray order at
  least four. Their sum therefore has zero quadratic Taylor coefficient.
  Passing from a Hessian density to the normalized quadratic picture
  introduces one more factor <math|1/2> and preserves this vanishing.

  We have proved that the actual and the core one-loop determinant terms,

  <\equation*>
    <tfrac|1|2>log det M<rsub|<math-up|act>>-log det
    \<Gamma\><space|2em><text|and><space|2em><tfrac|1|2>log det
    M<rsub|<math-up|core>>-log det \<Gamma\>,
  </equation*>

  have the same leading quadratic coefficient. By
  <eqref|eq:ex-thermal-determinant>, the thermal term of the stationary
  integral is the sum of the first of these and the inherited loop evaluated
  on the stationary branch. The contact calculation says nothing about that
  inherited summand. It is treated in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:inherited-loop-transport>
  (Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:inherited-loop-picture>): for the same
  incoming path, the same beds and the same stationary bridge, and under the
  hypotheses stated there, the common coarse picture of the inherited loop
  has order four at zero, so its quadratic Taylor coefficient vanishes. With
  that input, the thermal term and the core determinant term have the same
  leading quadratic coefficient. This does not say that the inherited
  finite-volume Hessian vanishes; only its leading low-momentum coefficient
  does.

  Combining the exact cancellation for the determinant terms with the uniform
  core-versus-rank bound from Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-stationary-determinant-prices>
  bounds the remaining coefficient difference between the actual and the rank
  determinant terms by Cauchy's inequality on the common strip. The
  qualitative rooted-frame constants used in the vanishing argument do not
  enter that uniform comparison bound.

  <subsection|Classical vertices after stationary
  elimination><label|sec:ex-classical-stationary-vertices>

  We now construct the cubic and quartic coefficient pictures of the
  stationary action. Two estimates must be kept distinct. The fine action is
  controlled by its vanishing order in momentum; the internal contraction in
  the quartic term is controlled in the weighted fluctuation norm. A
  coordinatewise estimate for that contraction would introduce an unwanted
  factor equal to the number of fine variables in a block.

  <subsubsection|The weighted vertex hypotheses>

  Write <math|\<mu\>=L<rsup|-1>>, where <math|L> is the block side. All
  momenta in this section are coarse momenta. Let
  <math|\<Phi\><around|(|p|)>> be the linear fine-field response to a coarse
  field. In block coordinates the full tangent vector is

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-L\><rsub|p>*h=<around|(|h,\<Phi\><around|(|p|)>*h,0|)>.
  </equation*>

  The last component is the gauge coordinate. An internal vector is written
  <math|\<iota\>*v=<around|(|0,v|)>>, with the internal space including the
  fine field and the auxiliary scalar variables. Let
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>> be its weighted
  fluctuation norm at coarse momentum <math|p>. This is the norm defined in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-weighted-frame-penalty>: writing a fine
  block vector <math|u> in the block Fourier basis with matrix
  <math|P<around|(|p|)>> and alias momenta
  <math|k<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>=<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*l|)>/L>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p><rsup|2>=<big|sum><rsub|l>h<rsub|l><around|(|p|)><rsup|2><around|\||<around|(|P<around|(|p|)><rsup|-1>*u|)><rsub|l>|\|><rsup|2>,<space|2em>h<rsub|l><around|(|p|)><rsup|2>=<around|\||q<around|(|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>k<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>|)>|\|><rsup|2>+\<mu\><rsup|2>,
  </equation*>

  and the auxiliary scalar component carries its Euclidean norm. The
  <math|L>-independent estimates below depend on this particular weight. The
  corresponding bilinear dual norm is

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|s|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p,\<ast\>>=sup<rsub|<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|-p>\<leqslant\>1><around|\||v<rsup|T>*s|\|>.
  </equation*>

  The sign in this definition is dictated by momentum conservation.

  Fix <math|0\<less\>\<eta\>\<leqslant\>1> and <math|D\<geqslant\>0>. The
  quantitative bridge hypothesis is holomorphy and periodicity together with

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-vertex-cruise>sup<rsub|z><around|\||\<nabla\><rsup|j>*\<Phi\><around|(|p|)>*e<rsub|a><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>D*\<mu\><rsup|1+j>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>2,<space|1em>p\<in\><math-up|strip><around|(|\<eta\>|)>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|e<rsub|a>> is a unit color-direction coordinate and
  <math|\<nabla\><rsup|j>> is any ordered string of <math|j> fine
  differences, with the coarse Fourier phase included at a block crossing.
  Choose <math|D<rsub|\<ast\>>=1+D+5*e<rsup|4>>. The same bound with <math|D>
  replaced by <math|D<rsub|\<ast\>>> applies to the fine component of
  <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|p>*e<rsub|a>>. For one internal vector of norm at most
  one, the weighted discrete Fourier estimate supplies the analogous bounds
  with one unit of integrability charge, through difference order one. More
  explicitly, if <math|H<rsub|b>> denotes the normalized block norm
  <math|L<rsup|2/b>> for <math|b\<gtr\>0> and <math|L<rsup|\<infty\>>> for
  <math|b=0>, the required estimates are

  <\equation*>
    H<rsub|0>*<around|(|\<nabla\><rsup|j>*\<cal-L\><rsub|p>*e<rsub|a>|)>\<leqslant\>D<rsub|\<ast\>>*\<mu\><rsup|1+j>,<space|2em>H<rsub|1>*<around|(|\<nabla\><rsup|j>*\<iota\>*v|)>\<leqslant\>D<rsub|\<ast\>>*\<mu\><rsup|1+j>*<space|1em><around|(|j\<leqslant\>1|)>.
  </equation*>

  Only the fine-field component occurs in these two inequalities. The
  weighted Fourier estimate and the block penalty estimate below are explicit
  inputs here; an ordinary bound for chart derivatives at fixed <math|L>
  would not imply either uniform estimate.

  An <math|n>-linear vertex, a function of <math|n> independent momenta in
  the strip of width <math|\<eta\>>, has price <math|C<rsub|n>> if its
  absolute value is at most <math|C<rsub|n>> on smooth coordinate legs
  <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|p<rsub|i>>*e<rsub|a<rsub|i>>>, and also with the first
  leg replaced by <math|\<iota\>*v>, <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p<rsub|1>>\<leqslant\>1>
  for the first momentum <math|p<rsub|1>>, and all other legs smooth. No
  relation between the <math|n> momenta is imposed in this definition. These
  are <em|unnormalized> derivatives. Their coefficient pictures therefore
  carry a factor <math|1/n>!.

  For the block penalty we use the following precise receipt. For
  <math|n=3,4>, its real <math|n>th derivative in each of the four coarse
  directions is bounded by <math|B<rsub|n>>, on the stencil fields induced by
  these legs, either all smooth or with one internal leg. The constants
  <math|B<rsub|n>> depend on the fixed group, patch enlargement, penalty
  coefficient and <math|D>, but not on <math|L> or the incoming interaction
  budget. The stencil field bounds are to hold for both real and imaginary
  parts. Complex multilinearity then gives price

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-vertex-penalty-price>N<rsub|n>=2<rsup|n>*<space|0.17em>4*B<rsub|n>.
  </equation>

  Indeed, expand each complex leg into its real and imaginary parts. There
  are <math|2<rsup|n>> terms, each with a coefficient of modulus one; sum the
  four directional penalties. This deduction does not replace the required
  real weighted stencil estimate.

  <subsubsection|Why the residual vertices gain a scale factor>

  The coefficient pictures proved in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-soft-curvature-receipts>
  split the fine classical action into its Wilson part and its residual part.
  At degree three the Wilson picture vanishes to order one and the residual
  picture to order two; at degree four their respective orders are zero and
  one. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-soft-factorization> writes each as a
  finite sum of products of one-link difference factors and analytic
  coefficient arrays. The factors may be assigned to legs other than the
  first. Thus the leg that will later be tested in the internal norm carries
  no differences. For the four cases, the number of legs <math|n> and the
  number of differences <math|q> satisfy

  <\equation*>
    <tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-lborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-rborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|3|3|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|3|3|cell-rborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|-1|4|4|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|4|4|cell-rborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|n>|<cell|q>|<cell|n+q-4>>|<row|<cell|<text|Wilson
    cubic>>|<cell|3>|<cell|1>|<cell|0>>|<row|<cell|<text|Wilson
    quartic>>|<cell|4>|<cell|0>|<cell|0>>|<row|<cell|<text|residual
    cubic>>|<cell|3>|<cell|2>|<cell|1>>|<row|<cell|<text|residual
    quartic>>|<cell|4>|<cell|1>|<cell|1.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  To see the significance of the last column, consider one term with
  <math|j<rsub|i>> differences on leg <math|i>.
  Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:ex-vertex-cruise> contributes
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|i>\<mu\><rsup|1+j<rsub|i>>=\<mu\><rsup|n+q>>.
  Summation over one fine block contributes <math|L<rsup|4>=\<mu\><rsup|-4>>.
  The result is <math|\<mu\><rsup|n+q-4>>. This count remains valid with one
  internal leg: use its normalized <math|L<rsup|2>> bound and the supremum
  bounds on the other legs, then Hölder on the normalized block measure. The
  displaced arguments and analytic coefficient sums are bounded by the
  absolute exponentially weighted coefficient sum furnished by the difference
  factorization. Fourier phases are bounded on the chosen strip; these
  factors and the finite color and difference labels are included in the
  constant. The first leg has no differences, so its weaker derivative
  allowance is sufficient. This proves both parts of the vertex price.

  The order of choices and the family to which the conclusion applies are as
  follows. Fix the gauge group, the kernel with its penalty coefficient, the
  phase <math|J> of the patch, a bed strip width <math|w\<gtr\>0> and bound
  <math|Q\<geqslant\>0>, a floor constant <math|\<varepsilon\>>, a bound
  <math|Z\<geqslant\>0> for the direct coefficient, the bridge constant
  <math|D>, and the coarse width <math|0\<less\>\<eta\>\<leqslant\>1>. All of
  this is fixed before the incoming path width <math|\<tau\>\<gtr\>0> and
  budget <math|A\<geqslant\>0>. The Wilson arrays and the penalty prices then
  give constants <math|a,b\<geqslant\>0> independent of <math|\<tau\>,A,L>.
  For each <math|\<tau\>,A> the residual arrays give a fine width
  <math|\<sigma\><rprime|'>\<gtr\>0> and constants
  <math|\<ell\>,r\<geqslant\>0>; these depend on the data just listed and on
  <math|\<tau\>,A>, but not on the path, the bed, the loop budget
  <math|\<Lambda\>>, the block side or the tori. The conclusion is asserted
  for every sequence of fine torus sides <math|n<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>,
  every clip <math|p> with direct coefficient at most <math|Z>, every path
  <math|R> with the bounds <math|<around|(|\<tau\>,A,\<Lambda\>|)>> in
  <math|p>, and every bed <math|B> such that <math|R> is attached to
  <math|B>, the metric row of <math|B> has strip bound
  <math|<around|(|w,Q|)>>, the floor <math|<around|(|\<varepsilon\>,Q|)>>
  holds for <math|B,p> at every torus side, and the sworn condition holds at
  each <math|n<rsub|i>>; and for every patch of phase <math|J> satisfying the
  branch compatibility of the kernel, every family of block steps
  <math|S<rsub|i>> with that patch from coarse tori of sides <math|m<rsub|i>>
  to the fine tori of sides <math|n<rsub|i>=L*m<rsub|i>>, every choice of
  block representatives <math|t<rsub|i>>, and every bridge <math|\<Phi\>>
  satisfying <eqref|eq:ex-vertex-cruise>. The hypotheses on <math|R,B,p> are
  exactly those of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-greyson>, which
  supplies the residual arrays; the Wilson arrays need none of them.

  Provided <math|\<mu\>*\<eta\>\<leqslant\>\<sigma\><rprime|'>>, addition of
  the three contributions gives vertices <math|T<rsub|3>,T<rsub|4>>,
  holomorphic on the strip of width <math|\<eta\>> in each momentum and
  periodic, with prices

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-classical-vertex-prices>C<rsub|3>=a+\<mu\>*\<ell\>,<space|2em>C<rsub|4>=b+\<mu\>*r.
  </equation>

  These vertices represent the actual block action <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>>
  of the step <math|S<rsub|i>> with representatives <math|t<rsub|i>>, clip
  <math|p> and path <math|R> (the fine action of <math|R> composed with the
  block chart, plus the block penalty) on every torus in the family just
  described: for every <math|i>, the <math|n>th derivative of
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>> at the origin on block Fourier waves of coarse
  momenta <math|p<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,p<rsub|n>> is zero unless
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|j>p<rsub|j>=0>, and is otherwise the number of coarse
  sites times <math|T<rsub|n>> at these momenta. To verify this, insert
  Fourier waves into each local translation sum. Summing the coarse anchor
  gives zero unless the total momentum is zero, and otherwise gives the
  coarse volume. The remaining fine-block sum is exactly the array just
  estimated. The germ identity between the block action and the sum of the
  fine action and block penalty identifies these derivatives for the actual
  tags. No change of stationary branch has been made.

  <subsubsection|The stationary contraction formula>

  Let <math|\<cal-A\>*<around|(|x,y|)>> be this real analytic block action on
  one torus, with coarse variable <math|x> and internal variable <math|y>,
  and let <math|y<around|(|x|)>> be real analytic near <math|0> with
  <math|D<rsub|y>*\<cal-A\>*<around|(|x,y<around|(|x|)>|)>=0> and
  <math|y<around|(|0|)>=0>. Put

  <\equation*>
    H=D<rsub|y><rsup|2>*\<cal-A\>*<around|(|0,0|)>,<space|2em>E<around|(|x|)>=\<cal-A\>*<around|(|x,y<around|(|x|)>|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-L\>*h=<around|(|h,D*y<around|(|0|)>*h|)>.
  </equation*>

  The internal variable contains the gauge directions, and <math|\<cal-A\>>
  is gauge invariant. Hence the symmetric matrix <math|H> is <em|not>
  invertible: it annihilates the infinitesimal gauge directions. The matrix
  inverted in the construction is its gauge completion. The facts used are
  the following, in the notation of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-selected-stationary-linear-data>:
  <math|\<cal-R\>> is the matrix of infinitesimal gauge directions, <math|F>
  the linear gauge-fixing map with regulator <math|\<mu\>=L<rsup|-1>>, and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-stationary-completion>H<rsup|<math-ss|T>>=H,<space|2em>H*\<cal-R\>=0,<space|2em>F*\<cal-R\>=\<Gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>><text|invertible>,<space|2em>M=H+F<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*F<text|invertible>.
  </equation>

  The second property follows from gauge invariance of <math|\<cal-A\>> and
  stationarity at <math|<around|(|0,0|)>>: differentiating
  <math|\<cal-A\>*<around|(|0,\<varphi\><rsub|t><around|(|y|)>|)>=\<cal-A\>*<around|(|0,y|)>>
  along a one-parameter gauge flow <math|\<varphi\><rsub|t>> once in <math|t>
  and once in <math|y> at <math|t=0>, <math|y=0> leaves
  <math|D<rsub|y><rsup|2>*\<cal-A\>*<around|(|0,0|)><around|[|<wide|\<varphi\>|\<dot\>><rsub|0><around|(|0|)>,<space|0.17em>\<cdot\><space|0.17em>|]>=0>,
  because the other term contains <math|D<rsub|y>*\<cal-A\>*<around|(|0,0|)>=0>.
  The third is <eqref|eq:ex-actual-gauge-identity>, where
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>>=d<rsup|\<ast\>>*d+C<rsub|\<mu\>>> is positive
  definite for <math|\<mu\>\<gtr\>0>. The last follows from the determinant
  factorization <eqref|eq:ex-gauge-det-factorization> (written there as
  <math|det <around|(|H+L<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*L|)>=det
  <around|(|\<Gamma\>|)><rsup|2>*det C>), the positive stationary Hessian gap
  and <math|\<mu\>\<gtr\>0>. All four are properties of the selected step on
  its finite torus; the argument of this subsection uses nothing else about
  <math|F> and <math|\<cal-R\>>.

  Differentiating stationarity once gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-stationary-orthogonality>D<rsup|2>*\<cal-A\><around|(|0|)><around|[|\<cal-L\>*h,\<iota\>*v|]>=0.
  </equation>

  Define the internal covector

  <\equation*>
    s*<around|(|h,k|)><around|[|v|]>=D<rsup|3>*\<cal-A\><around|(|0|)><around|[|\<iota\>*v,\<cal-L\>*h,\<cal-L\>*k|]>.
  </equation*>

  A second differentiation of stationarity gives, for
  <math|u<rsub|h*k>=D<rsup|2>*y<around|(|0|)><around|[|h,k|]>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-stationary-second>H*u<rsub|h*k>=-s*<around|(|h,k|)>.
  </equation>

  Because <math|H> is degenerate this equation does not determine
  <math|u<rsub|h*k>>, which depends on the gauge of the chosen branch. It
  does determine the contractions we need, by the identity

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-completion-projection>H*M<rsup|-1>*H=H.
  </equation>

  To prove <eqref|eq:ex-completion-projection>, put <math|N=M<rsup|-1>*H>, so
  that <math|M*N=H>. Transposing <math|H*\<cal-R\>=0> gives
  <math|\<cal-R\><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*H=0>, hence
  <math|\<cal-R\><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*M=<around|(|F*\<cal-R\>|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*F>
  and

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|F*\<cal-R\>|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*F*N=\<cal-R\><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*M*N=\<cal-R\><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*H=0.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|F*\<cal-R\>> is invertible, <math|F*N=0>, and therefore
  <math|H*N=<around|(|M-F<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*F|)>*N=M*N=H>. Consequently, by
  <eqref|eq:ex-stationary-second> and the symmetry of <math|H>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-stationary-pairing>s*<around|(|h,k|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*M<rsup|-1>*s*<around|(|l,m|)>=u<rsub|h*k><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*H*M<rsup|-1>*H*u<rsub|l*m>=u<rsub|h*k><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*H*u<rsub|l*m>.
  </equation>

  The resulting third and fourth derivatives are

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|D<rsup|3>*E<around|(|0|)><around|[|h<rsub|1>,h<rsub|2>,h<rsub|3>|]>>|<cell|=D<rsup|3>*\<cal-A\><around|(|0|)><around|[|\<cal-L\>*h<rsub|1>,\<cal-L\>*h<rsub|2>,\<cal-L\>*h<rsub|3>|]>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-stationary-third>>>|<row|<cell|D<rsup|4>*E<around|(|0|)><around|[|h<rsub|1>,h<rsub|2>,h<rsub|3>,h<rsub|4>|]>>|<cell|=D<rsup|4>*\<cal-A\><around|(|0|)><around|[|\<cal-L\>*h<rsub|1>,\<cal-L\>*h<rsub|2>,\<cal-L\>*h<rsub|3>,\<cal-L\>*h<rsub|4>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|1em>-<big|sum><rsub|<around|{|<around|{|i,j|}>,<around|{|k,l|}>|}>>s*<around|(|h<rsub|i>,h<rsub|j>|)><rsup|T>*M<rsup|-1>*s*<around|(|h<rsub|k>,h<rsub|l>|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-stationary-fourth>>>>>
  </align>

  where the sum runs over the three unordered pairings of four indices.

  For completeness, these identities can be checked without suppressing any
  chain-rule terms. Along a ray, write <math|y*<around|(|t*h|)>=t*D*y<around|(|0|)>*h+t<rsup|2>*u/2+t<rsup|3>*v/6+O<around|(|t<rsup|4>|)>>.
  The terms involving <math|D<rsub|y>*\<cal-A\><around|(|0|)>> vanish by
  stationarity. The mixed Hessian terms involving <math|\<cal-L\>*h> vanish
  by <eqref|eq:ex-stationary-orthogonality>. The cubic coefficient is
  therefore <math|D<rsup|3>*\<cal-A\><around|(|0|)>*<around|[|\<cal-L\>*h|]><rsup|3>/6>.
  The fourth-order coefficient is

  <\equation*>
    <frac|D<rsup|4>*\<cal-A\><around|(|0|)>*<around|[|\<cal-L\>*h|]><rsup|4>|24>+<frac|s*<around|(|h,h|)><around|[|u|]>|4>+<frac|u<rsup|T>*H*u|8>=<frac|D<rsup|4>*\<cal-A\><around|(|0|)>*<around|[|\<cal-L\>*h|]><rsup|4>|24>-<frac|s*<around|(|h,h|)><rsup|T>*M<rsup|-1>*s*<around|(|h,h|)>|8>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|u=u<rsub|h*h>>, so that <math|s*<around|(|h,h|)><around|[|u|]>=-u<rsup|T>*H*u>
  by <eqref|eq:ex-stationary-second>, and
  <math|u<rsup|T>*H*u=s*<around|(|h,h|)><rsup|T>*M<rsup|-1>*s*<around|(|h,h|)>>
  by <eqref|eq:ex-stationary-pairing>. Polarization of these symmetric
  multilinear forms proves <eqref|eq:ex-stationary-third>\U<eqref|eq:ex-stationary-fourth>,
  including the coefficient and sign of all three pairings.

  <subsubsection|Common analytic pictures and their prices>

  Assume that the inverse <math|M<rsup|-1>> of the same completed matrix
  <eqref|eq:ex-stationary-completion>, on every torus of the family, has one
  common periodic holomorphic Fourier symbol <math|V<around|(|p|)>>, with the
  weighted operator estimate

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-exchange-inverse><around|\<\|\|\>|V<around|(|p|)>*s|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\>F<around|\<\|\|\>|s|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p,\<ast\>>
  </equation>

  on the strip of width <math|\<eta\>>, where <math|F\<geqslant\>0> is fixed
  before the incoming width and budget. This is an input about the actual
  inverse, not an arbitrary comparison matrix. It is not proved in the
  present section: the inverse bound for the quadratic form
  <math|\<cal-G\>+F*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*F<around|(|p|)>> is the
  subject of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-weighted-stationary-inverses>,
  and the identification of that form with the Fourier symbol of <math|M> is
  the subject of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-selected-stationary-linear-data>.

  With smooth legs at momenta <math|p,q>, let

  <\equation*>
    s<rsub|a*b>*<around|(|p,q|)><around|[|v|]>=T<rsub|3>*<around|(|-<around|(|p+q|)>,p,q|)><around|[|\<iota\>*v,\<cal-L\><rsub|p>*e<rsub|a>,\<cal-L\><rsub|q>*e<rsub|b>|]>.
  </equation*>

  By the one-internal-leg estimate, <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|s<rsub|a*b>*<around|(|p,q|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p+q,\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|3>>.
  Consequently every contraction in <eqref|eq:ex-stationary-fourth> has
  modulus at most <math|C<rsub|3>*F*C<rsub|3>>. This is duality in the
  weighted norm; no sum of coordinatewise bounds and no dimension factor is
  required. The pairing uses opposite momenta on its two internal legs.

  For <math|n> external legs, take the first momentum to be minus the sum of
  the other <math|n-1>. On the independent strip of width
  <math|\<eta\>/<around|(|2*n|)>> all external momenta and all paired
  internal momenta lie in the original strip. Products, contractions and sums
  of the holomorphic arrays are therefore holomorphic there. Periodicity
  follows from that of the vertices, bridge and inverse. Equations
  <eqref|eq:ex-stationary-third>\U<eqref|eq:ex-stationary-fourth>, divided by
  the factorials, define common coefficient pictures
  <math|P<rsub|3>,P<rsub|4>> with

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-classical-picture-prices><around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|3>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<eta\>/6>\<leqslant\><frac|C<rsub|3>|6>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|4>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<eta\>/8>\<leqslant\><frac|C<rsub|4>+3*C<rsub|3>*F*C<rsub|3>|24>.
  </equation>

  They record the stationary derivatives exactly on every torus: the vertex
  identities already have the coarse-volume normalization, and Fourier
  inversion of the internal Hessian makes its contraction precisely <math|V>
  at the paired momentum. Thus the contraction formula preserves the same
  momentum-conservation rule and the same volume factor.

  We collect the statement in the form in which it is used by the one-step
  construction.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Cubic and quartic pictures of the stationary
    value><label|prop:ex-classical-stationary-pictures>Let the constants and
    the family of tori, clips, paths, beds, block steps <math|S<rsub|i>>,
    representatives <math|t<rsub|i>> and bridge <math|\<Phi\>> be as
    described before <eqref|eq:ex-classical-vertex-prices>, with
    <math|\<mu\>*\<eta\>\<leqslant\>\<sigma\><rprime|'>>, and let
    <math|T<rsub|3>,T<rsub|4>> be the vertices constructed there. For every
    <math|i> suppose given, on the <math|i>th coarse torus,

    <\enumerate>
      <item>a forest and an analytic map <math|\<rho\><rsub|i>> into its tree
      chart with <math|\<rho\><rsub|i><around|(|0|)>=0> which is stationary,
      near the origin, for the tested action of every choice of block
      representatives, these tested actions being real analytic near the
      origin, and a constant <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>, independent of
      <math|i>, which is a lower bound for the Hessian of every tested action
      in the tree-chart variables at the origin;

      <item>a matrix <math|W<rsub|i>> such that the derivative of
      <math|t\<mapsto\>\<rho\><rsub|i>*<around|(|t*x|)>> at <math|t=0> is the
      linear prediction of the tree chart from <math|W<rsub|i>> for every
      coarse direction <math|x>, and such that <math|\<Phi\>> is the common
      Fourier representation of <math|W<rsub|i>>;

      <item>a function <math|V<around|(|p|)>>, the same for all <math|i>,
      holomorphic and periodic on the strip of width <math|\<eta\>>, which is
      the Fourier symbol of the inverse of the completed matrix
      <eqref|eq:ex-stationary-completion> of the step <math|S<rsub|i>> with
      representatives <math|t<rsub|i>> and regulator
      <math|\<mu\>=L<rsup|-1>>, and satisfies <eqref|eq:ex-exchange-inverse>
      with a constant <math|F\<geqslant\>0>.
    </enumerate>

    Let <math|E<rsub|i>> be the tested action of the representatives
    <math|t<rsub|i>> evaluated along <math|\<rho\><rsub|i>>, a real analytic
    function of the coarse field near zero. Then the pictures
    <math|P<rsub|3>,P<rsub|4>> defined by
    <eqref|eq:ex-stationary-third>\U<eqref|eq:ex-stationary-fourth> from
    <math|T<rsub|3>,T<rsub|4>,\<Phi\>,V> satisfy
    <eqref|eq:ex-classical-picture-prices>, and for every <math|i> they
    record exactly the normalized Taylor coefficients
    <math|D<rsup|3>*E<rsub|i><around|(|0|)>/3>! and
    <math|D<rsup|4>*E<rsub|i><around|(|0|)>/4>!: on unit Fourier waves these
    coefficients vanish unless the momenta sum to zero, and otherwise equal
    the number of coarse sites times <math|P<rsub|3>>, respectively
    <math|P<rsub|4>>, at the last two, respectively three, momenta.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Fix <math|i>. By Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-selected-stationary-linear-data>,
    gauging the branch <math|\<rho\><rsub|i>> back and recentering gives a
    stationary representative <math|y<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>> of the centered
    block action <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>>, in all internal directions, with
    <math|y<rsub|i><around|(|0|)>=0> and <math|D*y<rsub|i><around|(|0|)>*x=<around|(|W<rsub|i>*x,0|)>>;
    hypotheses<nbsp>(1) and<nbsp>(2) are what that construction uses. The
    fine action and the block penalty are gauge invariant, so
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>*<around|(|x,y<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>|)>=E<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>>
    near zero. The gap in<nbsp>(1) and <math|\<mu\>\<gtr\>0> give
    <eqref|eq:ex-stationary-completion>. The contraction formulas
    <eqref|eq:ex-stationary-third>\U<eqref|eq:ex-stationary-fourth> therefore
    express <math|D<rsup|3>*E<rsub|i><around|(|0|)>> and
    <math|D<rsup|4>*E<rsub|i><around|(|0|)>> through the derivatives of
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>> on the legs <math|\<cal-L\>*h=<around|(|h,W<rsub|i>*h,0|)>>
    and through <math|M<rsup|-1>>. Insert unit coarse Fourier waves.
    By<nbsp>(2) the leg of a wave of momentum <math|p> is the block Fourier
    wave with fiber <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|p>*e<rsub|a>>; the vertex identity
    stated after <eqref|eq:ex-classical-vertex-prices> evaluates the
    derivatives of <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>> as the number of coarse sites
    times <math|T<rsub|3>> or <math|T<rsub|4>> at conserving momenta; and
    by<nbsp>(3) the contraction with <math|M<rsup|-1>> is the contraction
    with <math|V> at the paired momentum, which preserves momentum
    conservation and introduces no further volume factor. Dividing by the
    factorials gives the recording identities. The bounds were proved before
    the statement: they use the two parts of the vertex prices,
    <eqref|eq:ex-exchange-inverse> and weighted duality.
  </proof>

  The hypotheses of this proposition are extracted from the standing data of
  the one-step construction. There, hypothesis<nbsp>(3) is not assumed
  directly: the identification of the inverse symbol is derived from the
  frame and kinetic response data of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-selected-kinetic-response>,
  and only the bound <eqref|eq:ex-exchange-inverse> is a separate estimate.
  The proof above uses nothing beyond (1)\U(3), the path bounds, the branch
  compatibility and the vertex identity. The hypotheses are inputs with the
  following producers. The bridge bound <eqref|eq:ex-vertex-cruise> for the
  retained tower, and the identification of <math|\<Phi\>> with the tangent
  of the recentered stationary representative, are proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-selected-stationary-linear-data>. The
  weighted Fourier and penalty estimates are proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-weighted-frame-penalty>; the inverse
  estimate in<nbsp>(3) is the subject of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-weighted-stationary-inverses>.
  The stationary branch, its gap and its installed tangent in<nbsp>(1)
  and<nbsp>(2) are the chart receipts of Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:path-stationary-chart>;
  they are hypotheses of the one-step construction and are not produced here.
  Note that<nbsp>(1) requires one gap <math|\<delta\>> for all <math|i>, and
  that the path <math|R> is the incoming path, attached to the fine bed. A
  construction of the branch at each fixed period does not by itself give a
  gap that is uniform in the period; that uniformity is part of the input.

  Finally choose <math|L> sufficiently large that
  <math|\<mu\>*\<ell\>,\<mu\>*r\<leqslant\>1>. Both pictures are then bounded
  on their common strip by

  <\equation*>
    M<rsub|\<ast\>>=1+<frac|a+1|6>+<frac|<around|(|b+1|)>+3*<around|(|a+1|)>*F*<around|(|a+1|)>|24>.
  </equation*>

  The width and <math|M<rsub|\<ast\>>> are chosen before <math|\<tau\>,A>;
  only the lower threshold for <math|L> depends on the incoming path. This
  order of choices is what allows the stationary output construction to be
  iterated. The weighted bridge, weighted penalty and common inverse
  estimates remain the precise supplier interfaces of this section.

  <subsection|Priced local families give the three face
  estimates><label|sec:ex-annulus-fleet-faces>

  We derive the upper-face bound, the full lower-face bound, and the
  lower-face-minus-halo bound used in the annulus estimate. The proof is the
  local price calculation in the selected construction. We then verify the
  scale and data choices for the two populations used by the annulus: all
  centered configurations and the regulator-thin shell. We derive the
  selected row's contact identities and the scale-weighted face prices,
  including the separate shell restriction required by the unregulated
  reference inverses.

  <subsubsection|Derivative prices>

  For analytic matrix paths <math|M<rsub|i><around|(|t|)>> near <math|t=0>,
  with values in a fixed finite-dimensional matrix space of dimension
  <math|d>, write

  <\equation>
    <math-ss|P><rsub|r><around|(|M;C|)><space|1em>\<Longleftrightarrow\><space|1em>M<rsub|i><text|
    is analytic near >0<text| and >r<rsub|i><rsup|j><space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|M<rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|j|)>><around|(|0|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|op>>\<leqslant\>C*<space|1em><around|(|j=0,1,2|)>,<label|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F1>
  </equation>

  uniformly in the index <math|i>. Here <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|op>>>
  is the Euclidean operator norm; it is submultiplicative, and
  <math|<around|\||<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>M|\|>\<leqslant\>d<around|\<\|\|\>|M|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|op>>>,
  where <math|d> is the matrix dimension. Constants may be enlarged by a
  fixed factor without changing the notation.

  The elementary rules used below are as follows. If <math|M,N> have prices
  <math|<around|(|r,C|)>> and <math|<around|(|r,D|)>>, then <math|M+N> has
  price <math|<around|(|r,C+D|)>> and <math|M*N> has price
  <math|<around|(|r,4*C*D|)>>. For the product, the zeroth and first
  derivatives are the ordinary product rule; the second derivative has four
  terms, each bounded by submultiplicativity and the corresponding two price
  bounds. If <math|a\<gtr\>0> and <math|C,D\<geqslant\>0>,
  <math|M<around|(|0|)>> is invertible and
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|M<around|(|0|)><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|op>>\<leqslant\>D/a>,
  while <math|M> has price <math|<around|(|r,a*C|)>>, differentiating
  <math|M<rsup|-1>*M=I> once and twice gives

  <\equation>
    <math-ss|P><rsub|r><around|(|M<rsup|-1>;\<cal-I\><around|(|C,D|)>/a|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-I\><around|(|C,D|)>=D+2*C*D<rsup|2>+2*C<rsup|2>*D<rsup|3>.<label|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F2>
  </equation>

  Indeed, <math|<around|(|M<rsup|-1>|)><rprime|'>=-M<rsup|-1>*M<rprime|'>*M<rsup|-1>>
  and <math|<around|(|M<rsup|-1>|)><rprime|''>=2*M<rsup|-1>*M<rprime|'>*M<rsup|-1>*M<rprime|'>*M<rsup|-1>-M<rsup|-1>*M<rprime|''>*M<rsup|-1>>;
  submultiplicativity bounds the terms by the three summands in
  <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F2>. Finally,

  <\equation>
    <math-ss|P><rsub|r><around|(|M;C*r<rsub|i><rsup|-2>|)>\<Longrightarrow\><around|\||<around|(|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>M<rsub|i>|)><rprime|''><around|(|0|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>d*C*r<rsub|i><rsup|-4>,<label|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F3>
  </equation>

  by the <math|j=2> case of <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F1>
  and the trace inequality. These are the finite-dimensional price rules
  underlying the estimates below.

  <subsubsection|Upper, lower, and halo faces>

  For each configuration <math|i>, let <math|x<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>
  be its background momentum, <math|e<rsub|i>> its boost,
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> its regulator and <math|T<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0>
  its scale. Norms of momenta are Euclidean. The local geometry used here is

  <\equation>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|e<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>T<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|\<ast\>>,<space|2em>0\<less\>\<mu\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>T<rsub|i>.<label|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F4>
  </equation>

  The proposition below uses only <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F4>
  and the prices (F6). The derivation of those prices from the selected data,
  given later in this section, uses in addition

  <\equation>
    x<rsub|i>\<in\><around|[|-\<pi\>,\<pi\>|]><rsup|4>,<space|2em>\<mu\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>T<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>+\<mu\><rsub|i>.<label|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F4-prime>
  </equation>

  The last inequality is needed for the lower bound of the denominators:
  <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F4> alone allows
  <math|x<rsub|i>=0>, <math|T<rsub|i>=1> and <math|\<mu\><rsub|i>>
  arbitrarily small, in which case no bound
  <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>+\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|2>|)>>
  holds. Both scale choices made below satisfy
  <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F4-prime>.

  The matrices entering the faces are slots of a <em|film>: a triple of
  analytic matrix functions of momentum consisting of a tab
  <math|a<around|(|p|)>>, a one-leg slot <math|b<rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|k,p|)>>
  for a mark <math|\<xi\>> inserted with momentum <math|k> on the incoming
  momentum <math|p>, and a two-leg slot <math|c<rsub|\<xi\>*\<zeta\>>*<around|(|k,k<rprime|'>,p|)>>.
  Sums, products and inverses of films are formed with the insertion rules
  recalled in the trajectory-price paragraph below. Each configuration
  carries a mark <math|\<xi\><rsub|i>>, a local film and a regulator film;
  their sum is the total film. With <math|a,b,c> the slots of the total film,
  the objects in the next paragraph are

  <\equation*>
    S<rsub|i><around|(|t|)>=a*<around|(|x<rsub|i>+t*e<rsub|i>|)>,<space|2em>B<rsub|i><rsup|+><around|(|t|)>=b<rsub|\<xi\><rsub|i>>*<around|(|t*e<rsub|i>,x<rsub|i>|)>,<space|2em>B<rsub|i><rsup|-><around|(|t|)>=b<rsub|\<xi\><rsub|i>>*<around|(|-t*e<rsub|i>,x<rsub|i>+t*e<rsub|i>|)>,
  </equation*>

  and <math|J<rsub|i><around|(|t|)>+J<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|ref>><around|(|t|)>=c<rsub|\<xi\><rsub|i>*\<xi\><rsub|i>>*<around|(|-t*e<rsub|i>,t*e<rsub|i>,x<rsub|i>|)>>,
  where <math|J<rsub|i>> is the two-leg slot of the local film and
  <math|J<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|ref>>> that of the regulator film. The second
  leg <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|->> is evaluated at the shifted momentum
  <math|x<rsub|i>+t*e<rsub|i>>. Thus <math|S<rsub|i>> denotes the total
  denominator (local plus regulator film), and <math|J<rsub|i>> and
  <math|J<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|ref>>> are the two two-leg arch insertions.
  Write <math|K<rsub|0,i>=S<rsub|i><around|(|0|)><rsup|-1>> for the inverse
  at the unshifted background and <math|K<rsub|1,i><around|(|t|)>=S<rsub|i><around|(|t|)><rsup|-1>>
  for the inverse at the displaced background <math|x<rsub|i>+t*e<rsub|i>>.
  Write <math|D<rsub|0,i>=<around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>*I|)><rsup|-1>>
  and <math|D<rsub|1,i><around|(|t|)>=<around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|i>+t*e<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>*I|)><rsup|-1>>
  for the corresponding unregulated free inverses, defined when
  <math|x<rsub|i>\<ne\>0>, and <math|<wide|B|~><rsub|i><rsup|\<pm\>>> for the
  legs obtained from <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|\<pm\>>> by replacing the slot
  <math|b<rsub|\<xi\><rsub|i>>> with its linear part
  <math|v<rsub|\<xi\><rsub|i>>>, which is determined below. The unshifted
  inverse is constant in <math|t>; the shifted inverse varies. With
  <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|->,B<rsub|i><rsup|+>> the displaced insertion legs,
  define the scalar upper and lower faces by

  <\equation>
    U<rsub|i><around|(|t|)>=<math-up|tr><around*|[|K<rsub|0,i>*<around|(|J<rsub|i><around|(|t|)>+J<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|ref>><around|(|t|)>|)>|]>,<space|2em>L<rsub|i><around|(|t|)>=<math-up|tr><around*|[|K<rsub|0,i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|-><around|(|t|)>*K<rsub|1,i><around|(|t|)>*B<rsub|i><rsup|+><around|(|t|)>|]>.<label|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F5>
  </equation>

  The halo is <math|H<rsub|i><around|(|t|)>=<math-up|tr><around|[|D<rsub|0,i>*<wide|B|~><rsub|i><rsup|-><around|(|t|)>*D<rsub|1,i><around|(|t|)>*<wide|B|~><rsub|i><rsup|+><around|(|t|)>|]>>,
  with the four factors the corresponding free values. Thus it replaces both
  inverses and both legs. In this subsection and in (F6) the word
  \Preference\Q and the superscript <with|font-family|rm|ref> refer to the
  regulator film of the configuration; the halo factors are called free.

  The selected row transfer yields the following prices, with one constant
  <math|C<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0> independent of <math|i>:

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<math-ss|P><rsub|T<rsub|i>><around|(|K<rsub|0,i>;C<rsub|0>*T<rsub|i><rsup|-2>|)>,<space|1em><math-ss|P><rsub|T<rsub|i>><around|(|K<rsub|1,i>;C<rsub|0>*T<rsub|i><rsup|-2>|)>,<eq-number><label|F6a>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<math-ss|P><rsub|T<rsub|i>><around|(|J<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|ref>>;C<rsub|0>*\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|2>*T<rsub|i><rsup|-2>|)>,<space|2em><math-ss|P><rsub|1><around|(|J<rsub|i>;C<rsub|0>|)>,<eq-number><label|F6b>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<math-ss|P><rsub|T<rsub|i>><around|(|B<rsub|i><rsup|\<pm\>>;C<rsub|0>*T<rsub|i>|)>,<eq-number><label|F6c>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<math-ss|P><rsub|T<rsub|i>><around|(|K<rsub|0,i>-D<rsub|0,i>;C<rsub|0>*<space|0.17em>\<delta\><rsub|i>*T<rsub|i><rsup|-2>|)>,<space|1em><math-ss|P><rsub|T<rsub|i>><around|(|D<rsub|0,i>;C<rsub|0>*T<rsub|i><rsup|-2>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<math-ss|P><rsub|T<rsub|i>><around|(|D<rsub|1,i>;C<rsub|0>*T<rsub|i><rsup|-2>|)>,<space|1em><math-ss|P><rsub|T<rsub|i>><around|(|K<rsub|1,i>-D<rsub|1,i>;C<rsub|0>*<space|0.17em>\<delta\><rsub|i>*T<rsub|i><rsup|-2>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<math-ss|P><rsub|T<rsub|i>><around|(|B<rsub|i><rsup|\<pm\>>-<wide|B|~><rsub|i><rsup|\<pm\>>;C<rsub|0>*<space|0.17em>\<delta\><rsub|i>*T<rsub|i>|)>,<eq-number><label|F6d>>>>>
  </align>

  where <math|C<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0> and
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|i>=T<rsub|i>+\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|2>/T<rsub|i><rsup|2>>.
  The tilded factors are the free halo factors just described. Also
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|K<rsub|0,i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|op>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|0>*T<rsub|i><rsup|-2>>.
  The ordinary face prices (F6a)\U(F6c) apply to the all-centered population.
  The extra differences (F6d) are required only on the shell
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>T<rsub|i>=<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>>;
  at the center <math|x<rsub|i>=0> the unregulated halo inverse need not
  exist. The derivation from this same selected row and the reference field
  is given below; in particular, the free inverses in (F6d) are used only on
  the shell.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Face estimates><label|prop:ex-fleet-face-bounds>Under
    <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F4>\U(F6c), there are
    constants <math|A,B,C\<geqslant\>0>, independent of <math|i>, such that

    <\equation>
      <around|\||U<rsub|i><rprime|''><around|(|0|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|A|T<rsub|i><rsup|2>>+B*<frac|\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|2>|T<rsub|i><rsup|6>>,<space|2em><around|\||L<rsub|i><rprime|''><around|(|0|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|C|T<rsub|i><rsup|4>>,<label|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F7>
    </equation>

    and, whenever the additional shell prices (F6d) hold,

    <\equation>
      <around|\||L<rsub|i><rprime|''><around|(|0|)>-H<rsub|i><rprime|''><around|(|0|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*<frac|\<delta\><rsub|i>|T<rsub|i><rsup|4>>.<label|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F8>
    </equation>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    For the reference part of the upper face, (F6a)\U(F6b) and the product
    rule give price <math|C*\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|2>/T<rsub|i><rsup|4>> at
    scale <math|T<rsub|i>>. By <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F3>,
    its second derivative is at most <math|C*\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|2>/T<rsub|i><rsup|6>>.
    For the interacting part, <math|S<rsub|i><around|(|0|)><rsup|-1>> is
    constant in <math|t> and has norm at most
    <math|C<rsub|0>/T<rsub|i><rsup|2>>; combine this with the unit-scale
    price of <math|J<rsub|i>> and apply the trace bound at unit scale. This
    gives <math|A/T<rsub|i><rsup|2>>. Adding proves the first inequality in
    <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F7>.

    The lower-face package supplies price <math|C/T<rsub|i><rsup|2>> at scale
    <math|T<rsub|i>> for the four-factor product in
    <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F5>: each inverse has
    price proportional to <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|-2>> and each displaced leg
    has price proportional to <math|T<rsub|i>>. Applying
    <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F3> yields
    <math|C/T<rsub|i><rsup|4>>, proving the second inequality.

    For the difference estimate, assume in addition (F6d), as is valid on the
    shell. Since <math|T<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|\<ast\>>> and
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>T<rsub|i>>, we have
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|\<ast\>>+1>. The sum rule for
    prices and (F6c)\U(F6d) therefore give each tilded leg the ordinary price
    <math|<math-ss|P><rsub|T<rsub|i>><around|(|<wide|B|~><rsub|i><rsup|\<pm\>>;C<rsub|1>*T<rsub|i>|)>>,
    with <math|C<rsub|1>> depending only on <math|C<rsub|0>> and
    <math|r<rsub|\<ast\>>>. Use the exact ordered telescoping identity

    <\equation>
      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsub|1>*A<rsub|2>*A<rsub|3>*A<rsub|4>-B<rsub|1>*B<rsub|2>*B<rsub|3>*B<rsub|4>>|<cell|=<around|(|A<rsub|1>-B<rsub|1>|)>*A<rsub|2>*A<rsub|3>*A<rsub|4>+B<rsub|1>*<around|(|A<rsub|2>-B<rsub|2>|)>*A<rsub|3>*A<rsub|4>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|1em>+B<rsub|1>*B<rsub|2>*<around|(|A<rsub|3>-B<rsub|3>|)>*A<rsub|4>+B<rsub|1>*B<rsub|2>*B<rsub|3>*<around|(|A<rsub|4>-B<rsub|4>|)>.>>>>
      </aligned>

      <label|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F9>
    </equation>

    Both inverse differences, at the unshifted and shifted legs, have price
    <math|C*\<delta\><rsub|i>/T<rsub|i><rsup|2>> by (F6d); the leg
    differences have price <math|C*\<delta\><rsub|i>*T<rsub|i>> by (F6d). The
    remaining inverse factors cost <math|C/T<rsub|i><rsup|2>> and the
    remaining legs cost <math|C*T<rsub|i>>. In each of the four summands the
    product rule therefore gives price at most
    <math|C*\<delta\><rsub|i>/T<rsub|i><rsup|2>> at scale <math|T<rsub|i>>.
    Apply <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F3> to the trace of
    their sum. Its second derivative is bounded by
    <math|C*\<delta\><rsub|i>/T<rsub|i><rsup|4>>, as claimed.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The two populations of centered records and the two scale
  choices>

  We now state the input family precisely. Let <math|G> be simple. A
  <em|response> is a triple of analytic coefficient pictures with two, three
  and four legs, <math|R<rsub|0>,R<rsub|1>,R<rsub|2>>; it determines a film
  whose tab, one-leg and two-leg slots are its zero-, one- and two-mark
  layers, with the marked momenta first, then the outgoing and the incoming
  momentum:

  <\equation*>
    a<around|(|k|)>=R<rsub|0>*<around|(|-k,k|)>,<space|2em>b<rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|p,k|)>=R<rsub|1>*<around|(|\<xi\>;p,-k-p,k|)>,<space|2em>c<rsub|\<xi\>*\<zeta\>>*<around|(|p,q,k|)>=R<rsub|2>*<around|(|\<xi\>,\<zeta\>;p,q,-k-p-q,k|)>,
  </equation*>

  the direction and color indices of the last two legs being the matrix
  indices. A response has fees <math|<around|(|\<eta\>,C|)>> if all
  components of its three pictures are holomorphic and periodic on the strip
  of width <math|\<eta\>> and bounded by <math|C> there. Fix

  <\equation*>
    \<eta\>\<gtr\>0,<space|2em>\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0,<space|2em>C,D,A\<geqslant\>0,
  </equation*>

  and one <em|vacuum response> <math|V> with fees
  <math|<around|(|\<eta\>,C|)>> which is <em|grey>: at every torus side its
  zero-, one- and two-mark layers record exactly, with the volume factor at
  conserving momenta and with no error, the corresponding marked derivatives
  of the Wilson action <math|\<cal-W\>> on that torus. These are the
  derivatives at the origin of <math|<around|(|y,z|)>\<mapsto\>\<cal-W\>*<around|(|e<rsup|y>*e<rsup|z>|)>>,
  the product of the two exponentiated link fields being taken link by link,
  of order two in <math|y> and of order zero, one or two in the marked
  variable <math|z>. The same convention defines the layers recorded by any
  response.

  A <em|local record> consists of a strip width <math|\<sigma\>>, a regulator
  <math|\<mu\>>, a color <math|c>, a bed <math|B>, a clip <math|p> and a
  response <math|l>, the <em|local correction>, such that

  <\enumerate>
    <item><math|l> has fees <math|<around|(|\<eta\>,A|)>>, and its zero-,
    one- and two-mark layers vanish at zero momentum to orders three, two and
    one;

    <item><math|0\<less\>\<mu\>\<leqslant\>1> and
    <math|0\<less\>\<sigma\>\<leqslant\>\<mu\>/128>;

    <item>the three layers of the combined response <math|l+V> are
    holomorphic and periodic on the strip of width <math|\<sigma\>>, and the
    metric row of <math|B> has strip bound <math|<around|(|\<sigma\>,D|)>>;

    <item>the denominator matching <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F13>
    below holds on the strip of width <math|\<sigma\>> for the vector film of
    <math|l+V>, the bed <math|B> and the regulator <math|\<mu\>>;

    <item>the floor of <math|B> in the clip <math|p> holds with constants
    <math|<around|(|\<epsilon\>,D|)>> at every torus side.
  </enumerate>

  The numbers <math|\<eta\>,\<epsilon\>,C,D,A> and the response <math|V> are
  common to all records. The width <math|\<sigma\>> belongs to the individual
  record and is forced to tend to zero with <math|\<mu\>>; no common strip
  for the regulated denominators is assumed. A <em|centered record> (an Hstar
  datum) is a local record together with a momentum
  <math|x\<in\><around|[|-\<pi\>,\<pi\>|]><rsup|4>>. The constants obtained
  below depend only on <math|G>, <math|V>, <math|\<eta\>>,
  <math|\<epsilon\>>, <math|C>, <math|D>, <math|A>: they are chosen before
  the record, so they are uniform over all centered records, in particular in
  <math|\<mu\>>, <math|\<sigma\>>, the color, the bed and the clip.

  Each centered record is used in two <em|channels>, both with the mark
  <math|\<xi\>=<around|(|1,c|)>> (direction <math|1>, color <math|c>) and the
  unit axial boost <math|e<rsub|0>>. Let <math|<math-ss|B>> be the fixed
  gauge-fixing film. It is the film of the covariant difference: for a link
  configuration <math|U> and a Lie-algebra valued function <math|h> on sites,

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|D<rsub|U>*h|)><around|(|x,a|)>=h<around|(|x|)>-<math-up|Ad><rsub|U*<around|(|x,a|)>><space|0.17em>h*<around|(|x+e<rsub|a>|)>,
  </equation*>

  and the tab and the one- and two-leg slots of <math|<math-ss|B>> are the
  Fourier symbols of <math|D<rsub|1>> and of the first and second derivatives
  of <math|D<rsub|exp z>> in the background <math|z> at <math|z=0>, with the
  routing of momenta displayed above. In particular its tab is
  <math|-<math-ss|T><around|(|P<rsub|0>|)>>, the color-diagonal extension of
  minus the zero-form symbol <math|P<rsub|0>> below, and its marked slots do
  not vanish. Let <math|<math-ss|B><rsup|<math-up|r>>> be its rear film
  (transposed, with reversed momenta). The sign of <math|<math-ss|B>> cancels
  in the two products <math|<math-ss|BB><rsup|<math-up|r>>> and
  <math|<math-ss|B><rsup|<math-up|r>><math-ss|B>>, which are the only places
  where <math|<math-ss|B>> enters. That these slots record <math|D<rsub|exp
  z>> on every torus, and the corresponding statement for products, rears and
  inverses, is proved in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:annulus-fixed-film-tickets>.

  <\padded-center>
    <tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-halign|l>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-lborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-rborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-halign|l>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-rborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|-1|3|3|cell-halign|l>|<cwith|1|-1|3|3|cell-rborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|channel>|<cell|local
    film>|<cell|regulator film>>|<row|<cell|vector>|<cell|film of <math|l+V>,
    plus <math|<math-ss|BB><rsup|<math-up|r>>>>|<cell|<math|R<rsub|<math-up|vec>,\<mu\>>>>>|<row|<cell|ghost>|<cell|<math|Q<rsub|<math-up|gh>>=<math-ss|B><rsup|<math-up|r>><math-ss|B>>>|<cell|<math|R<rsub|<math-up|gh>,\<mu\>>>>>>>>
  </padded-center>

  The regulator films are constructed in the trajectory-price paragraph. The
  response whose second derivative the annulus estimate concerns is one half
  of the vector-channel total film's two-leg figure minus the ghost-channel
  one; with <math|U,L> the faces <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F5>
  of each channel,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-fleet-response>\<kappa\><around|(|x|)>=<frac|1|2>*<around*|(|<frac|1|2>*<around*|(|U<rsub|<math-up|vec>><rprime|''><around|(|0|)>-L<rsub|<math-up|vec>><rprime|''><around|(|0|)>|)>-<around*|(|U<rsub|<math-up|gh>><rprime|''><around|(|0|)>-L<rsub|<math-up|gh>><rprime|''><around|(|0|)>|)>|)>.
  </equation>

  For each selected row, the path bounds give caps for the tab, the one-leg
  insertions, and the two-leg insertion, together with the two soft contacts.
  The regulated reference path supplies its own bounds. Combining the paths
  gives the total stationary denominator and its scale-<math|T<rsup|2>> lower
  bound. The derivation of (F6) below shows explicitly how these data yield
  the required prices. In particular, no scale-weighted estimate is inferred
  from analyticity alone: the rates come from the path budget, its one-leg
  weight <math|T<rsup|-1>>, and its two-leg weight <math|T<rsup|-2>>; the
  extra shell estimates use subtraction identities.

  For a centered-box track, each coordinate has modulus at most
  <math|\<pi\>>, so <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>\<leqslant\><sqrt|4*\<pi\><rsup|2>>=2*\<pi\>>;
  the regulator satisfies <math|0\<less\>\<mu\>\<leqslant\>1>. The
  all-centered scale is

  <\equation>
    T=<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>+\<mu\>,<space|2em>0\<less\>T\<leqslant\>1+2*\<pi\>=:r<rsub|\<ast\>>.<label|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F10>
  </equation>

  Thus <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F4> and
  <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F4-prime> hold and
  <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F7>'s upper estimate gives
  the first two-wave bound after combining it with the lower-face estimate.
  On the shell <math|\<mu\>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>>,
  reset the scale to <math|T=<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>>. Then
  <math|0\<less\>\<mu\>\<leqslant\>T\<leqslant\>r<rsub|\<ast\>>> and
  <math|T\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>+\<mu\>>, so
  <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F4> and
  <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F4-prime> hold again, and
  the same package gives both the upper estimate and
  <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F8>. The shell population is
  the subfamily of the same centered records with
  <math|\<mu\>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>>; only the
  scale is changed, not the response, bed, color or regulator. Use the halo
  identity, the second identity in <eqref|eq:ex-two-wave-identities> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-annulus-two-wave>, to replace the halo by
  the continuum pole. Since <math|T\<leqslant\>r<rsub|\<ast\>>>,

  <\equation*>
    T<rsup|-2>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|\<ast\>>*T<rsup|-3>,<space|2em>T<rsup|-2>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|2>*T<rsup|-4>,<space|2em><frac|\<mu\><rsup|2>|T<rsup|6>>\<leqslant\><frac|1|T<rsup|4>>,<space|2em><frac|\<delta\>|T<rsup|4>>=<frac|1|T<rsup|3>>+<frac|\<mu\><rsup|2>|T<rsup|6>>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed, the response is given by <eqref|eq:ex-fleet-response>. The upper
  estimate and lower-face estimate in <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F7>,
  applied in both channels with <math|\<mu\>\<leqslant\>T>, therefore give
  <math|<around|\||\<kappa\><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>a*T<rsup|-4>>. For
  the shell discrepancy, the exact algebra is

  <\equation>
    \<kappa\><around|(|x|)>-\<Pi\><rsub|G><around|(|x|)>=<frac|1|2>*<around*|(|<frac|1|2>*U<rsub|<math-up|vec>><rprime|''>-U<rsub|<math-up|gh>><rprime|''>+<around|(|L<rsub|<math-up|gh>><rprime|''>-H<rsub|<math-up|gh>><rprime|''>|)>-<frac|1|2>*<around|(|L<rsub|<math-up|vec>><rprime|''>-H<rsub|<math-up|vec>><rprime|''>|)>|)>,<label|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F12>
  </equation>

  all second derivatives being taken at <math|t=0>, where the halo
  combination <math|<frac|1|2>*<around|(|H<rsub|<math-up|gh>><rprime|''>-<frac|1|2>*H<rsub|<math-up|vec>><rprime|''>|)>=\<Pi\><rsub|G>>
  is the halo identity just cited. The two upper terms are bounded by
  <math|C/T<rsup|2>+C*\<mu\><rsup|2>/T<rsup|6>>, and the lower remainders by
  <math|C*\<delta\>/T<rsup|4>>; the displayed scale inequalities give the two
  powers in <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F11>. Enlarge
  constants once to cover the bounded part of the centered box outside the
  unit ball. We obtain constants <math|a,b> independent of <math|x> and
  <math|\<mu\>> such that

  <\equation>
    <around|\||\<kappa\><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>a*<around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>+\<mu\>|)><rsup|-4>,<space|2em><around|\||\<kappa\><around|(|x|)>-\<Pi\><rsub|G><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>b*<around|\<\|\|\>|x*<around|\<\|\|\>|<rsub|2><rsup|-3>+b*\<mu\><rsup|2>|\<\|\|\>>*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|-6>*<space|1em><around|(|\<mu\>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>\<leqslant\>1|)>.<label|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F11>
  </equation>

  The constants <math|a,b> depend only on <math|G>, <math|V>, <math|\<eta\>>,
  <math|\<epsilon\>>, <math|C>, <math|D>, <math|A>. In particular the same
  <math|a,b> serve every local record, every color <math|c> and every
  regulator <math|0\<less\>\<mu\>\<leqslant\>1>, and they are not affected by
  the record's own strip width <math|\<sigma\>>. These are the uniform local
  estimates consumed in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-annulus-two-wave>.

  <subsubsection|The local row bridge to the interacting family>

  Here are the exact row identities behind the local contact conditions. For
  a selected bed <math|B<rsub|i>>, write <math|M<rsub|i><around|(|p|)>=B<rsub|i>.<math-up|metric><around|(|p|)>>
  and let <math|M<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|p|)>> be the constant leader metric.
  Let <math|D<around|(|p|)>> be the first edge symbol, with components
  <math|q<rsub|\<nu\>><around|(|p|)>>, and let <math|A<rsub|0><around|(|p|)>>
  be the zero-form symbol. The color-diagonal projection of the direction
  matrix is denoted by <math|<math-ss|T>>. Put

  <\equation*>
    G<rsub|i><around|(|p|)>=<text|the two-point row of the interacting
    film>,<space|2em>J<rsub|i,b>*<around|(|p,k|)>=<text|its marked two-leg
    row>.
  </equation*>

  For a local record, the three layers of <math|l> and of <math|V> have the
  common fee strip of width <math|\<eta\>>; the record satisfies the
  denominator matching identity on its own strip of width <math|\<sigma\>>,

  <\equation>
    (<text|vector local film>+<text|vector regulator
    film>)<rsub|<math-up|tab>><around|(|p|)>=D<rsup|<math-up|bed>><around|(|p|)>,<label|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F13>
  </equation>

  where the bed denominator <math|D<rsup|<math-up|bed>>> is displayed in the
  derivation below; the floor at every torus side (in particular the
  zero-momentum normalization <math|M<rsub|i><around|(|0|)>=I=M<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|0|)>>
  of the metric row, which is one clause of the floor at any one side); the
  soft order-three condition on the local correction; and the vacuum matching
  condition (greyness of <math|V>). These properties all concern the same bed
  and the same combined response <math|l+V>. The regulator satisfies
  <math|0\<less\>\<mu\>\<leqslant\>1>, and the tab of the vector regulator
  film is the scalar multiple <math|s<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>*I> on the
  strip of width <math|\<mu\>/128>, where, with <math|\<omega\>> as below,

  <\equation*>
    s<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>=\<mu\><rsup|2><around*|(|<frac|\<mu\><rsup|2>|\<omega\><around|(|p|)>+\<mu\><rsup|2>>|)><rsup|9>.
  </equation*>

  They are properties of the local record, rather than consequences of
  analyticity alone.

  Let <math|\<omega\><around|(|p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>q<rsub|\<nu\>>*<around|(|-p|)>*q<rsub|\<nu\>><around|(|p|)>>.
  The exact row and free identities use the color-diagonal extension
  <math|<math-ss|T><around|(|A|)><rsub|<around|(|i,c|)>,<around|(|j,d|)>>=\<b-1\><rsub|c=d>*A<rsub|i*j>>
  of a direction matrix; this extension preserves matrix sums and products.
  They are

  <\equation>
    G<rsub|i><around|(|p|)>-<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>*I=<math-ss|T><space|-0.17em><around*|[|P<rsub|1>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|T>*<around|(|M<rsub|i><around|(|p|)>-M<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|p|)>|)>*P<rsub|1><around|(|p|)>|]>+<around|(|\<omega\><around|(|p|)>-<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>|)>*I,<label|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F14>
  </equation>

  <\equation>
    J<rsub|i,b>*<around|(|p,k|)>-v<rsub|b>*<around|(|p,k|)>=J<rsub|i,b><rsup|<math-up|loc>>*<around|(|p,k|)>+<around*|(|J<rsub|i,b><rsup|<math-up|vac>>*<around|(|p,k|)>-v<rsub|b>*<around|(|p,k|)>|)>,<label|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F15>
  </equation>

  and

  <\equation>
    <math-ss|T><space|-0.17em><around*|[|P<rsub|1>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|T>*M<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|p|)>*P<rsub|1><around|(|p|)>+P<rsub|0><around|(|p|)>*P<rsub|0>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|T>|]>=\<omega\><around|(|p|)>*I.<label|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F16>
  </equation>

  Here <math|v<rsub|b>> is the linear vector insertion determined by the
  first radial coefficient of the vacuum marked row.

  <\render-proof|Derivation of <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F14>--<eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F16>>
    Write <math|P<rsub|0><around|(|p|)>> and <math|P<rsub|1><around|(|p|)>>
    for the zero-form and first-edge symbols, so <math|A<rsub|0>=P<rsub|0>>
    and <math|D=P<rsub|1>>. The selected denominator matching equates the tab
    of the interacting film plus its regulator film to the prescribed bed
    denominator. The two sides expand as

    <\equation*>
      <\eqsplit>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|<math-up|interacting>+<math-up|regulator>|)><rsub|<math-up|tab>><around|(|p|)>>|<cell|=G<rsub|i><around|(|p|)>+s<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>*I,>>|<row|<cell|<around|(|<math-up|bed
        denominator>|)><around|(|p|)>>|<cell|=<math-ss|T><space|-0.17em><around*|[|P<rsub|1>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|T>*M<rsub|i><around|(|p|)>*P<rsub|1><around|(|p|)>+P<rsub|0><around|(|p|)>*P<rsub|0>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|T>|]>+s<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>*I.>>>>
      </eqsplit>
    </equation*>

    The regulator tab is scalar on the slot space, so equality cancels that
    term entrywise and gives, on real momenta,

    <\equation*>
      G<rsub|i><around|(|p|)>=<math-ss|T><space|-0.17em><around*|[|P<rsub|1>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|T>*M<rsub|i><around|(|p|)>*P<rsub|1><around|(|p|)>+P<rsub|0><around|(|p|)>*P<rsub|0>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|T>|]>.
    </equation*>

    The first summand is the metric cross product; the second is the
    zero-form product. The cancellation is exact, so it introduces no
    regulator remainder. For the leader bed, the degree-zero exterior
    identity gives

    <\equation*>
      <math-ss|T><space|-0.17em><around*|[|P<rsub|1>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|T>*M<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|p|)>*P<rsub|1><around|(|p|)>+P<rsub|0><around|(|p|)>*P<rsub|0>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|T>|]>=\<omega\><around|(|p|)>*I.
    </equation*>

    Indeed, expand the first product by direction. The longitudinal rank-one
    part of the zero-form product cancels the complementary part of the
    first-edge product, leaving <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>q<rsub|\<nu\>>*<around|(|-p|)>*q<rsub|\<nu\>><around|(|p|)>>
    times the identity. The exact degree-zero exterior identity is
    <math|P<rsub|0><around|(|p|)>*P<rsub|0>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|T>+P<rsub|1>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|T>*M<rsub|\<ast\>>*P<rsub|1><around|(|p|)>=\<omega\><around|(|p|)>*I>,
    since <math|\<omega\><around|(|p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>q<rsub|\<nu\>>*<around|(|-p|)>*q<rsub|\<nu\>><around|(|p|)>>
    by definition. Since <math|M<rsub|\<ast\>>> is the constant identity
    metric, this proves <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F16>.
    Subtracting the leader identity from the row-to-film identity proves
    <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F14>, with the
    lattice-dispersion term <math|\<omega\><around|(|p|)>-<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>>
    retained explicitly.

    For the marked row, the vector local film is the sum of the film of the
    local correction <math|l>, the film of the vacuum response <math|V>, and
    the fixed gauge-fixing film <math|<math-ss|BB><rsup|<math-up|r>>>.
    Accordingly <math|J<rsup|<math-up|loc>><rsub|i,b>> in
    <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F15> is the one-mark slot
    of <math|l>, and <math|J<rsup|<math-up|vac>><rsub|i,b>> is the sum of the
    one-mark slots of <math|V> and of <math|<math-ss|BB><rsup|<math-up|r>>>.
    Linearity of the marked coefficient gives
    <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F15>. The soft-3
    hypothesis assigns radial order two to the one-mark layer; composing that
    layer with the linear two-momentum map gives the required second-order
    vanishing. The slot of <math|V> is identified by greyness with the Wilson
    three-leg coefficient. Greyness gives this equality at the grid momenta
    of every torus side; both sides are continuous on the real momenta and
    the union of the grids is dense, so the equality holds at all real
    momenta. An identity on one fixed grid would not suffice for the radial
    expansion. Let the mark be <math|<around|(|a,b|)>>, with direction
    <math|a> and color <math|b>, let the two other legs have directions
    <math|i,j> and colors <math|x,y> and momenta <math|-<around|(|k+p|)>> and
    <math|k>, the mark carrying momentum <math|p>, and write

    <\equation*>
      \<tau\><around|(|u|)><rsub|a>=i*u<rsub|a>,<space|2em>c<rsub|b*x*y>=<around|\<langle\>|e<rsub|x>,<around|[|e<rsub|b>,e<rsub|y>|]>|\<rangle\>>
    </equation*>

    for the structure coefficient in the orthonormal color basis; it is
    totally antisymmetric. After summing over all positively oriented
    plaquette types, the contribution to the linear part of the three-leg
    coefficient in which a chosen leg is differentiated in the curl and the
    two others in the commutator is <math|<around|(|\<tau\><around|(|u|)><rsub|i<rprime|'>>*\<delta\><rsub|a<rprime|'>*j<rprime|'>>-\<tau\><around|(|u|)><rsub|j<rprime|'>>*\<delta\><rsub|a<rprime|'>*i<rprime|'>>|)>>
    times the structure coefficient with the color of the chosen leg in the
    middle, where <math|u,a<rprime|'>> are the momentum and direction of the
    chosen leg and <math|i<rprime|'>,j<rprime|'>> the directions of the
    others. Choosing in turn the mark, the leg <math|<around|(|i,x|)>> and
    the leg <math|<around|(|j,y|)>>, and reordering the color indices by
    antisymmetry, gives the three contributions

    <\equation*>
      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around|[|-\<tau\><around|(|p|)><rsub|i>*\<delta\><rsub|a*j>+\<tau\><around|(|p|)><rsub|j>*\<delta\><rsub|a*i>|]>*<space|0.17em>c<rsub|b*x*y>,>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around|[|-<around|(|\<tau\><around|(|k|)><rsub|a>+\<tau\><around|(|p|)><rsub|a>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|i*j>+<around|(|\<tau\><around|(|k|)><rsub|j>+\<tau\><around|(|p|)><rsub|j>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|i*a>|]>*<space|0.17em>c<rsub|b*x*y>,>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around|[|-\<tau\><around|(|k|)><rsub|a>*\<delta\><rsub|i*j>+\<tau\><around|(|k|)><rsub|i>*\<delta\><rsub|j*a>|]>*<space|0.17em>c<rsub|b*x*y>.>>>>
      </aligned>
    </equation*>

    Their sum is the first radial coefficient of the Wilson slot,

    <\equation*>
      <around|[|-<around|(|2*\<tau\><around|(|k|)><rsub|a>+\<tau\><around|(|p|)><rsub|a>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|i*j>+<around|(|\<tau\><around|(|k|)><rsub|j>+2*\<tau\><around|(|p|)><rsub|j>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|i*a>+<around|(|\<tau\><around|(|k|)><rsub|i>-\<tau\><around|(|p|)><rsub|i>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|j*a>|]>*c<rsub|b*x*y>.
    </equation*>

    The marked slot of the gauge-fixing film
    <math|<math-ss|BB><rsup|<math-up|r>>> has first radial coefficient

    <\equation*>
      <around|[|-\<tau\><around|(|k|)><rsub|j>*\<delta\><rsub|i*a>-\<tau\>*<around|(|k+p|)><rsub|i>*\<delta\><rsub|j*a>|]>*c<rsub|b*x*y>.
    </equation*>

    Adding these expressions and simplifying gives

    <\equation*>
      v<rsub|b,i*j>*<around|(|p,k|)>=<around|[|-<around|(|2*\<tau\><around|(|k|)><rsub|a>+\<tau\><around|(|p|)><rsub|a>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|i*j>+2*\<tau\><around|(|p|)><rsub|j>*\<delta\><rsub|i*a>-2*\<tau\><around|(|p|)><rsub|i>*\<delta\><rsub|j*a>|]>*c<rsub|b*x*y>.
    </equation*>

    This is homogeneous of degree one. Thus the vacuum marked row minus
    <math|v<rsub|b>> has zero value and zero first radial coefficient;
    analyticity implies that it has radial order at least two. With the soft
    local term, <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F15> has the
    required quadratic contact. The common strip bounds then give uniform
    compact derivative bounds by Cauchy's formula.
  </render-proof>

  <subparagraph|The ghost channel.> The ghost local film
  <math|Q<rsub|<math-up|gh>>=<math-ss|B><rsup|<math-up|r>><math-ss|B>> is a
  fixed film, independent of the record. Its tab is
  <math|\<omega\><around|(|p|)>*I> on the color space, so the analogue of
  <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F14> is
  <math|Q<rsub|<math-up|gh>,<math-up|tab>><around|(|p|)>-<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>*I=<around|(|\<omega\><around|(|p|)>-<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>|)>*I>,
  of radial order three. Its one-mark slot at the mark
  <math|<around|(|a,b|)>> has entries analytic in the two momenta and the
  linear part

  <\equation*>
    w<rsub|b>*<around|(|p,k|)><rsub|x*y>=-<around|(|2*\<tau\><around|(|k|)><rsub|a>+\<tau\><around|(|p|)><rsub|a>|)>*<space|0.17em>c<rsub|b*x*y>,
  </equation*>

  obtained from the explicit slot of the product by expanding the two lattice
  symbols to first order; the remainder has radial order two. These are the
  two contacts of the ghost channel, with <math|w<rsub|b>> in the place of
  <math|v<rsub|b>> as the linear leg of its halo. Its compact derivative caps
  are those of a fixed analytic periodic film. The tab of the ghost total
  film is <math|<around|(|\<omega\><around|(|p|)>+s<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>|)>*I>
  on the strip of width <math|\<mu\>/128>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Row data give the priced insertion
    family><label|lem:ex-row-to-floe>For the family of all local records with
    the common fee bounds, denominator matching, floor at every torus side,
    soft order-three local correction and grey vacuum response just
    described, the vector local film has uniform compact derivative prices
    through order three. Its two-point row has a cubic radial remainder after
    subtraction of <math|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>*I>, and its marked row has
    a quadratic radial remainder after subtraction of <math|v<rsub|b>>.
    Consequently it supplies the compact caps and the two contact orders used
    in the ordinary (non-halo) part of (F6b)\U(F6c).
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For each row layer and each fixed pair of matrix entries, the common
    strip fee and Cauchy's estimate bound derivatives of every fixed order on
    a compact real ball. The tab, spot and arch are fixed finite linear
    combinations of these entries after fixed linear maps of momentum; the
    chain rule therefore gives uniform compact derivative caps through the
    orders used here. A finite sum of these caps bounds the matrix operator
    norm, since the matrix dimension is fixed.

    For the two-point contact, <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F14>
    and the zero-momentum normalization supplied by the floor give
    <math|M<rsub|i><around|(|0|)>-M<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|0|)>=0>.
    Analyticity makes this metric difference
    <math|O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|>|)>>. Each edge symbol vanishes
    linearly, <math|q<rsub|\<nu\>><around|(|p|)>=i*p<rsub|\<nu\>>+O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>|)>>,
    so the metric term in <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F14>
    is <math|O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|3>|)>>. Expanding each product
    <math|q<rsub|\<nu\>>*<around|(|-p|)>*q<rsub|\<nu\>><around|(|p|)>> shows
    <math|\<omega\><around|(|p|)>-<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>=O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|3>|)>>
    as well.

    For the marked row, the local soft condition contributes
    <math|O<around|(|<around|(|<around|\||p|\|>+<around|\||k|\|>|)><rsup|2>|)>>
    and the vacuum-minus-<math|v<rsub|b>> term has the same order by the
    preceding first-coefficient calculation. Thus
    <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F15> has quadratic
    contact. To convert these radial orders into derivative prices, note that
    if an analytic scalar entry <math|g> has radial order at least <math|m>,
    then each homogeneous Taylor polynomial of degree <math|j\<less\>m>
    vanishes on every real direction and hence vanishes identically. Taylor's
    formula with integral remainder applied to <math|D<rsup|n>*g> on the
    segment from <math|0> to the base point <math|a> gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|n>*g<around|(|a|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>K<around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>><rsup|m-n>/<around|(|m-n|)>!,<space|2em>n\<less\>m,
    </equation*>

    when the <math|m>th derivative is bounded by <math|K> on that segment.
    For <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>T>, this gives
    <math|T<rsup|n>*<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|n>*g<around|(|a|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>K*T<rsup|m>>.
    At orders above the contact order use the compact derivative cap and the
    uniform upper bound <math|T\<leqslant\>r<rsub|\<ast\>>>. This proves the
    local compact and contact prices. The trajectory-price argument below
    derives the total-field prices in (F6a) and the shell-separation prices
    in (F6d) from these contacts and the reference path budgets.
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|From a centered record to the row package.> Every centered
  record contains one bed, clip, regulator, and local correction. Its
  denominator matching is the identity <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F13>,
  its fees supply the uniform analytic strip, and its floor supplies the
  zero-momentum normalization. Its local correction is soft of order three.
  Applying the row argument to the single combined response <math|l+V> gives
  the vector local film used by the annulus. The free halo comparison is
  <math|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>*I> with the linear legs <math|v<rsub|b>>
  (vector) and <math|w<rsub|b>> (ghost). Since the vacuum response <math|V>
  is grey and has the required fees, the finite-volume Wilson matching above
  identifies its marked vertex and hence its linear term. These statements
  establish the row contacts from the data of a centered record. The scalar
  regulator term and its positive floor are then included in the total
  denominator; the next price calculation uses the free comparison only on
  the shell, where its inverse exists.

  Recall <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>. The denominator lower bound is also tied
  to this same bed. At a real momentum <math|p>, write

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|bed>><around|(|p|)>=<math-ss|T><space|-0.17em><around*|[|P<rsub|1>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|T>*M<rsub|i><around|(|p|)>*P<rsub|1><around|(|p|)>+P<rsub|0><around|(|p|)>*P<rsub|0>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|T>|]>+s<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>*I
  </equation*>

  for the denominator matrix in <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F13>,
  and put

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|peb><around|(|p,\<mu\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>><around|\||q<rsub|\<nu\>><around|(|p|)>|\|><rsup|2>+\<mu\><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  Assume <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F4> and
  <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F4-prime> and let <math|p=x>
  be the background. On the centered box

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||q<rsub|\<nu\>><around|(|x|)>|\|>=2<around|\||sin
    <around|(|x<rsub|\<nu\>>/2|)>|\|>\<geqslant\><frac|2|\<pi\>><around|\||x<rsub|\<nu\>>|\|>\<geqslant\><frac|<around|\||x<rsub|\<nu\>>|\|>|4>,<space|2em><text|so><space|2em><big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>><around|\||q<rsub|\<nu\>><around|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|2>\<geqslant\><frac|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>|16>.
  </equation*>

  With <math|T\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>>+\<mu\>> this gives
  <math|T<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2\<\|\|\>x\<\|\|\><rsup|2>+2*\<mu\><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>32*<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>><around|\||q<rsub|\<nu\>><around|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|2>+2*\<mu\><rsup|2>>,
  hence

  <\equation*>
    T<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>64<space|0.17em><math-up|peb><around|(|x,\<mu\>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The bed-floor estimate for this matrix, which uses the floor at every torus
  side, the metric strip bound and <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, gives at every
  real momentum <math|p>

  <\equation*>
    <frac|min <around|(|\<epsilon\>,1|)>|2<rsup|10>><space|0.17em><math-up|peb><around|(|p,\<mu\>|)><around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><math-up|Re><around|\<langle\>|z,D<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|bed>><around|(|p|)>*z|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation*>

  Using the denominator matching <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F13>
  at the real momentum <math|x> to identify this matrix with the vector total
  denominator, we obtain

  <\equation*>
    c*T<rsup|2><around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><math-up|Re><around|\<langle\>|z,S<rsub|i><around|(|0|)>*z|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em>c=<frac|min
    <around|(|\<epsilon\>,1|)>|2<rsup|16>>.
  </equation*>

  For the ghost channel the total tab at real momentum is the scalar
  <math|\<omega\><around|(|p|)>+s<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>>, and
  <math|\<omega\><around|(|p|)>+s<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>\<geqslant\>2<rsup|-10><space|0.17em><math-up|peb><around|(|p,\<mu\>|)>>:
  if <math|\<omega\><around|(|p|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|2>> then
  <math|s<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>\<geqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|2>/2<rsup|9>>,
  and otherwise <math|\<omega\><around|(|p|)>\<geqslant\><frac|1|2><math-up|peb><around|(|p,\<mu\>|)>>.
  The same cold lower bound therefore gives the ghost denominator bound with
  <math|c=2<rsup|-16>>. For either square matrix <math|S>, the coercive
  estimate implies <math|c*T<rsup|2><around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><math-up|Re><around|\<langle\>|z,S*z|\<rangle\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>*<space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|S*z|\<\|\|\>>>.
  Hence <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|S*z|\<\|\|\>>\<geqslant\>c*T<rsup|2><around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>>;
  in finite dimension this makes <math|S> invertible with
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|S<rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|op>>\<leqslant\><around|(|c*T<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>.

  <subparagraph|Trajectory prices.> We derive (F6) from the row contacts and
  the regulated reference field. At scale <math|T<rsub|i>>, a path estimate
  with tab budget <math|L<rsub|i>> consists of three component bounds. The
  tab derivatives satisfy <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|j><around|\<\|\|\>|M<rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|j|)>><around|(|0|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|op>>\<leqslant\>C*L<rsub|i>>
  for <math|j=0,1,2>; the one-leg slot has budget <math|L<rsub|i>/T<rsub|i>>,
  and the two-leg arch has budget <math|L<rsub|i>/T<rsub|i><rsup|2>>. These
  are separate components of the path estimate; the spot and arch bounds do
  not follow from the tab bound alone. For the interacting row, the compact
  derivative caps come from the common strip and selected fees. Along
  <math|x<rsub|i>+t*e<rsub|i>>, each derivative is a momentum derivative
  contracted with <math|e<rsub|i>>, whose norm is at most one. If an analytic
  entry has radial order <math|m>, all Taylor tensors of degree below
  <math|m> vanish: their diagonal polynomial vanishes in every real
  direction, hence the polynomial itself is zero. Taylor's integral remainder
  applied to its <math|j>th derivative therefore bounds it by
  <math|C<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|m-j>> for
  <math|j\<less\>m>; for <math|j=m> use the compact derivative cap. Since
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>T<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|\<ast\>>>,
  this gives the path budget <math|C*T<rsub|i><rsup|m>> for
  <math|j\<leqslant\>m>. Thus the trajectory transfer with tab budget
  <math|L<rsub|i>> gives

  <\equation>
    <math-ss|P><rsub|T<rsub|i>>(<text|tab>;C*L<rsub|i>),<space|2em><math-ss|P><rsub|T<rsub|i>>(<text|spot>;C*L<rsub|i>/T<rsub|i>),<space|2em><math-ss|P><rsub|T<rsub|i>>(<text|arch>;C*L<rsub|i>/T<rsub|i><rsup|2>).<label|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F17>
  </equation>

  Thus the interacting transfer uses <math|L<rsub|i>=T<rsub|i><rsup|2>>,
  while the regulated reference transfer uses
  <math|L<rsub|i>=\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|2>>.

  For the interacting row, <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F14>
  says the tab is <math|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>*I+O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|3>|)>>;
  therefore its path has tab budget <math|C*T<rsub|i><rsup|2>>. For a marked
  leg, <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F15> says it is the
  linear vector term plus a quadratic remainder. Along the path, the linear
  term has size <math|O<around|(|T<rsub|i>|)>>, first derivative
  <math|O<around|(|1|)>>, and zero second derivative. The quadratic remainder
  has <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|j>>-weighted <math|j>th derivative
  <math|O<around|(|T<rsub|i><rsup|2>|)>> for <math|j=0,1,2>; since
  <math|T<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|\<ast\>>>, this is at most
  <math|O<around|(|T<rsub|i>|)>>. Thus the leg has budget <math|C*T<rsub|i>>.
  The uniform arch cap gives budget <math|C> at unit scale. The regulated
  reference field has the following three trajectory budgets, which we derive
  from its actual construction below:

  <\equation*>
    C*\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|2>,<space|2em>C*<frac|\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|2>|T<rsub|i>>,<space|2em>C*<frac|\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|2>|T<rsub|i><rsup|2>>.
  </equation*>

  These weights follow from its construction. The regulator films of both
  channels are built from the ghost film <math|Q<rsub|<math-up|gh>>=<math-ss|B><rsup|<math-up|r>><math-ss|B>>,
  not from the vector film:

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|\<mu\>>=Q<rsub|<math-up|gh>>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*I,<space|2em>H<rsub|\<mu\>>=\<mu\><rsup|2>*C<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>R<rsub|<math-up|gh>,\<mu\>>=\<mu\><rsup|2>*H<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|9>,<space|2em>R<rsub|<math-up|vec>,\<mu\>>=<big|sum><rsub|a=0><rsup|3><math-ss|D><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|r>>R<rsub|<math-up|gh>,\<mu\>><math-ss|D><rsub|a>,
  </equation*>

  with products and inverses of films, including their marked slots, and with
  <math|<math-ss|D><rsub|a>> the fixed constant matrix selecting the
  direction <math|a>, regarded as a film without marked slots. Begin with the
  massive ghost film <math|C<rsub|\<mu\><rsub|i>>>. The ghost film has tab
  budget <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|2>>: its tab <math|\<omega\><around|(|p|)>*I>
  has a quadratic zero, while its compact derivatives are uniformly bounded.
  Its marked leg has the linear first term <math|w<rsub|b>> and a quadratic
  remainder, hence budget <math|T<rsub|i>>; its two-leg arch has a uniform
  unit-scale cap. The added constant mass has tab budget
  <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|2>> since <math|\<mu\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>T<rsub|i>>,
  and has zero spot and arch. The massive film therefore has the
  three-component path budgets <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|2>,T<rsub|i>,1>. Its
  scalar tab is the free dispersion plus <math|\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|2>>; the
  cold geometric inequality bounds it below by <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|2>/64>.
  Thus its inverse has norm at most <math|64*T<rsub|i><rsup|-2>> and its
  inverse path has tab budget <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|-2>>. If
  <math|C=A<rsup|-1>>, <math|B<rsub|i>> is the original one-leg slot, and
  <math|D<rsub|i*j>> is the original two-leg slot, the inverse film's
  insertion formulas, with <math|<wide|B|^><rsub|i>> and
  <math|<wide|D|^><rsub|i*j>> denoting the inverse film's slots, are

  <\equation*>
    <wide|B|^><rsub|i>*<around|(|p,q|)>=-C*<around|(|q+p|)>*B<rsub|i>*<around|(|p,q|)>*C<around|(|q|)>,
  </equation*>

  <\equation*>
    <wide|D|^><rsub|i*j>*<around|(|p,q,r|)>=-<around*|[|<wide|B|^><rsub|j>*<around|(|q,r+p|)>*B<rsub|i>*<around|(|p,r|)>+<wide|B|^><rsub|i>*<around|(|p,r+q|)>*B<rsub|j>*<around|(|q,r|)>+C*<around|(|r+p+q|)>*D<rsub|i*j>*<around|(|p,q,r|)>|]>*C<around|(|r|)>.
  </equation*>

  The one-leg term has size <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|-2>*<space|0.17em>T<rsub|i>*<space|0.17em>T<rsub|i><rsup|-2>=T<rsub|i><rsup|-3>>.
  Each of the three two-leg summands has size <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|-4>>: the
  first two use <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|-3>*<space|0.17em>T<rsub|i>*<space|0.17em>T<rsub|i><rsup|-2>>,
  and the last uses <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|-2>*<space|0.17em>1*<space|0.17em>T<rsub|i><rsup|-2>>.
  Thus the inverse film has tab, one-leg, and two-leg budgets
  <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|-2>>, <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|-3>>, and
  <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|-4>>. The reference construction multiplies this
  inverse film by <math|\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|2>>, giving budgets
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|2>/T<rsub|i><rsup|2>>,
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|2>/T<rsub|i><rsup|3>>, and
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|2>/T<rsub|i><rsup|4>>. The same prices hold for
  derivatives of orders <math|j=0,1,2> along every front or rear path: apply
  the product rule to each displayed slot formula and use the corresponding
  Week estimate for each differentiated factor. The shifted momenta have path
  velocities among <math|e<rsub|i>,-e<rsub|i>> and their sums, whose norms
  are bounded by <math|2>; this changes only fixed constants. Each derivative
  of total order <math|j> costs <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|-j>>, which is canceled
  by the <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|j>> factor in the path price. Since
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|2>/T<rsub|i><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>1>, the
  inverse-reference film has the Week estimate with tab budget <math|1>, and
  consequently spot and arch budgets <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|-1>> and
  <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|-2>>.

  The reference insertion is formed from nine copies of this
  inverse-reference film and then weighted by <math|\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|2>>.
  For two films with tab/spot/arch budgets
  <math|<around|(|L,L/T,L/T<rsup|2>|)>> and
  <math|<around|(|M,M/T,M/T<rsup|2>|)>>, the product has tab budget
  <math|L*M>. Its spot formula has two summands, each bounded by
  <math|L*M/T>; its arch formula has four summands, each bounded by
  <math|L*M/T<rsup|2>>. Iterating through the nine factors changes only fixed
  numerical constants: the tab, spot, and arch rates remain <math|1>,
  <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|-1>>, and <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|-2>>. The final weight
  gives <math|\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|2>>, <math|\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|2>/T<rsub|i>>,
  and <math|\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|2>/T<rsub|i><rsup|2>>. This is the ghost
  regulator film <math|R<rsub|<math-up|gh>,\<mu\><rsub|i>>>. Each directional
  sandwich in the vector regulator film <math|R<rsub|<math-up|vec>,\<mu\><rsub|i>>>
  has constant unit-budget factors on both sides, so it preserves these
  prices. Summing the four directions changes only the common constant. This
  proves the displayed regulator budgets, in both channels, from the
  construction.

  Because <math|\<mu\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>T<rsub|i>>, adding the local and
  reference tab budgets gives <math|C*<around|(|T<rsub|i><rsup|2>+\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|2>|)>\<leqslant\>2*C*T<rsub|i><rsup|2>>.
  The total denominator has the same <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|2>> tab budget on
  both the unshifted and boosted path. The bed floor proved above gives its
  inverse norm at <math|t=0> at most <math|C/T<rsub|i><rsup|2>>.
  Differentiating the inverse with <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F2>
  gives both inverse prices in (F6a). The reference arch budget is exactly
  <math|C*\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|2>/T<rsub|i><rsup|2>>, and the interacting arch
  has unit-scale budget <math|C>, proving (F6b). The two total marked legs
  have budget at most <math|C*<around|(|T<rsub|i>+\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|2>/T<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>2*C*T<rsub|i>>,
  which proves (F6c). All constants here are uniform over the family of all
  centered records because the fees, compact caps, regulator bound, floor
  constant and centered-box radius are uniform.

  On the shell, set <math|T<rsub|i>=<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>\<gtr\>0>.
  The unregulated reference denominator along the path is

  <\equation*>
    R<rsub|i><around|(|t|)>=<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|i>+t*e<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>*I,<space|2em>R<rsub|i><around|(|0|)>=T<rsub|i><rsup|2>*I.
  </equation*>

  Its path budget is <math|C*T<rsub|i><rsup|2>> and its inverse has price
  <math|C/T<rsub|i><rsup|2>> by <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F2>.
  Let <math|S<rsub|i><around|(|t|)>> be the total regulated denominator. Its
  difference from <math|R<rsub|i>> splits into the local tab minus the free
  quadratic tab, which has budget <math|C*T<rsub|i><rsup|3>> by
  <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F14>, and the regulator tab,
  which has budget <math|C*\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|2>>. Thus

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|P><rsub|T<rsub|i>><around|(|S<rsub|i>-R<rsub|i>;C*<around|(|T<rsub|i><rsup|3>+\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|2>|)>|)>=<math-ss|P><rsub|T<rsub|i>><around|(|S<rsub|i>-R<rsub|i>;C*\<delta\><rsub|i>*T<rsub|i><rsup|2>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Both inverse paths have price <math|C/T<rsub|i><rsup|2>>; the identity

  <\equation*>
    S<rsub|i><rsup|-1>-R<rsub|i><rsup|-1>=-S<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*<around|(|S<rsub|i>-R<rsub|i>|)>*R<rsub|i><rsup|-1>
  </equation*>

  and the product rule therefore give the two inverse differences in (F6d)
  with price <math|C*\<delta\><rsub|i>/T<rsub|i><rsup|2>>.

  For the marked legs, subtract the unregulated free leg, which is the linear
  vector coefficient <math|v<rsub|b>> (respectively <math|w<rsub|b>> in the
  ghost channel). By the explicit coefficient calculation above the local
  marked row has this linear part. Their difference has radial order two.
  Hence the local marked row minus the halo leg has price
  <math|C*T<rsub|i><rsup|2>> along the shell path. The regulated reference
  leg has the one-leg budget <math|C*\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|2>/T<rsub|i>> from
  its path spot component. Since

  <\equation*>
    T<rsub|i><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|i>*T<rsub|i>,<space|2em><frac|\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|2>|T<rsub|i>>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|i>*T<rsub|i>,
  </equation*>

  their sum has price <math|C*\<delta\><rsub|i>*T<rsub|i>>, proving the leg
  part of (F6d). This derivation requires <math|T<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> and is
  used only on the shell. At <math|x<rsub|i>=0> the unregulated reference
  path is singular, so no halo inverse is asserted there.

  <subsubsection|Uniform path bounds from the selected records>

  For completeness, we describe how the path hypotheses used above are
  obtained uniformly for the selected family. Each selected local action has
  the same fee bound and positive strip width, and the external vacuum has
  its own common fee bound on that strip. Their sum therefore has a uniform
  fee bound. Cauchy's integral formula on a smaller common strip bounds every
  fixed momentum derivative of each matrix entry uniformly; for the tab use
  derivatives through order three, and for the spot and arch through order
  two. The momentum maps entering the three components are fixed linear maps,
  so the chain rule multiplies these estimates only by their fixed operator
  norms. The matrix dimension is fixed, hence the entrywise bounds imply
  operator-norm bounds. The additional fixed gauge-fixing film
  <math|<math-ss|BB><rsup|<math-up|r>>> is not a polynomial in the momenta:
  its three slots are finite sums of products of lattice exponentials. They
  are entire and periodic, so their derivatives of every fixed order are
  bounded on the centered box. Adding these bounds gives the compact path
  caps used in <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F17>.

  The selected action's soft hypothesis is componentwise: its unmarked layer
  vanishes to radial order three, its one-mark layer to order two, and its
  two-mark layer to order one. The local marked insertion uses the one-mark
  layer composed with a linear two-momentum map, so it has the second-order
  vanishing required for the local contact. The grey vacuum's marked row,
  together with that of the gauge-fixing film, has the linear term
  <math|v<rsub|b>>: the three plaquette placements give the displayed Wilson
  coefficient, and the gauge-fixing film supplies the complementary part.
  Their difference has order two. Thus the selected combined row has exactly
  the tab and marked-leg contacts used in
  <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F14>\U<eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F15>,
  with uniform compact caps.

  Finally, the reference field is constructed from the same regulator
  <math|\<mu\>>. Its trajectory tab budget is <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>>, and its
  two other components carry the factors <math|T<rsup|-1>> and
  <math|T<rsup|-2>>. The local field has tab budget <math|T<rsup|2>>. Since
  <math|\<mu\>\<leqslant\>T>, their sum has tab budget at most
  <math|2*T<rsup|2>>. The floor of the record and the cold geometric bound
  give the total tab a coercive lower bound <math|c*T<rsup|2>>; the
  elementary inverse calculation preceding
  <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F17> then yields the inverse
  estimates for both the unshifted and boosted paths. On the shell, the free
  comparison is positive because <math|T=<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>\<gtr\>0>,
  and the same uniform strip/cap constants apply after resetting the scale.
  This verifies that the family-level path bounds used in the face proof are
  common to all selected rows and all regulators in the stated range.

  <subsection|The annular pole and the two-wave
  estimate><label|sec:ex-annulus-two-wave>

  Lüscher and Weisz <cite-detail|LuscherWeiszCoupling|Ÿ1> compute
  perturbative coupling matching for the Wilson
  <math|<math-up|SU><around|(|N|)>> action. The calculation below identifies
  the logarithmic coefficient for the present real Lie-algebra inner product
  and estimates the regulated finite-volume response used in the scale
  iteration.

  The selected one-step construction needs two facts about one finite-volume
  response. Its logarithmic coefficient is an integral of the response over
  four-dimensional momentum space, and that response is close to the
  continuum quadratic pole on an annulus. This section gives the exact polar
  calculation and expands the finite-volume two-channel estimate that
  supplies the two radial bounds. The weighted-face estimates that feed it
  are proved in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-annulus-fleet-faces>; they
  are stated here at their precise input interface.

  <subsubsection|From the finite response to a momentum integral>

  Let <math|E<rsub|4>=<around|(|-\<pi\>,\<pi\>|]><rsup|4>> be the fundamental
  momentum cube in Euclidean coordinates, with normalized measure
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|wt>>*<space|0.17em>d*x> and
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|wt>>=<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-4>>. It has finite
  volume and contains the unit ball. Two different objects occur, and the
  coefficient functional is defined on the second.

  A <em|response kernel> <math|F> has entries
  <math|F<rsub|\<xi\>*\<zeta\>>*<around|(|p,x|)>>, indexed by two marks
  (direction and color) and depending on an external momentum <math|p> and an
  internal momentum <math|x>. We assume that every entry is jointly
  holomorphic, periodic and bounded on a common strip of positive width in
  <math|<around|(|p,x|)>>. Its associated <em|picture> is one half of the
  normalized integral over the internal momentum,

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-Y\><rsub|\<xi\>*\<zeta\>><around|(|p|)>=<frac|1|2>*<space|0.17em>c<rsub|<math-up|wt>>*<big|int><rsub|E<rsub|4>>F<rsub|\<xi\>*\<zeta\>>*<around|(|p,x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x.
  </equation*>

  For a two-leg picture <math|\<cal-Y\>> and a color <math|a>, the
  coefficient functional is twice the real part of the quadratic Taylor
  coefficient along the axis <math|e<rsub|0>> of the component with both
  marks equal to <math|<around|(|1,a|)>>:

  <\equation*>
    \<alpha\><rsub|a><around|(|\<cal-Y\>|)>=2<math-up|Re><space|0.17em><around|[|t<rsup|2>|]>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-Y\><rsub|<around|(|1,a|)><around|(|1,a|)>>*<around|(|t*e<rsub|0>|)>=<math-up|Re><frac|d<rsup|2>|d*t<rsup|2>>*\<cal-Y\><rsub|<around|(|1,a|)><around|(|1,a|)>>*<around|(|t*e<rsub|0>|)><mid|\|><rsub|t=0>.
  </equation*>

  For the kernel put

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-K\><rsub|a><around|(|x|)>=<frac|1|2>*<frac|d<rsup|2>|d*t<rsup|2>>*F<rsub|<around|(|1,a|)><around|(|1,a|)>>*<around|(|t*e<rsub|0>,x|)><mid|\|><rsub|t=0>,<space|2em>g<rsub|a><around|(|x|)>=<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>\<cal-K\><rsub|a><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  Differentiation under the integral gives, for the picture <math|\<cal-Y\>>
  associated with <math|F>,

  <\equation>
    \<alpha\><rsub|a><around|(|\<cal-Y\>|)>=c<rsub|<math-up|wt>>*<big|int><rsub|E<rsub|4>>g<rsub|a><around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x.<label|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT1>
  </equation>

  The common analytic bound and Cauchy's estimate bound the second
  <math|t>-derivative of the integrand uniformly in <math|x>, which justifies
  the differentiation and shows that <math|g<rsub|a>> is bounded and
  continuous on the closure of <math|E<rsub|4>>. Equation
  <eqref|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT1> is an identity for the
  analytically assembled response picture. The comparison of that picture
  with the finite sampled pole test is a separate recording estimate with an
  error term; it is not an equality and is not asserted by
  <eqref|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT1>.

  For <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>\<setminus\><around|{|0|}>> let
  <math|r=<around|\||x|\|>> and define the gauge pole

  <\equation>
    \<Pi\><rsub|G><around|(|x|)>=4*C<rsub|G>*<around*|(|<frac|x<rsub|1><rsup|2>|r<rsup|6>>-4*<frac|x<rsub|1><rsup|2>*x<rsub|0><rsup|2>|r<rsup|8>>-<frac|1|r<rsup|4>>|)>,<label|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT2>
  </equation>

  where the gauge group is simple and <math|C<rsub|G>> is its adjoint Casimir
  in the normalization of the colour inner product on the Lie algebra. For a
  group of unitary <math|r<rsub|G>\<times\>r<rsub|G>> matrices this inner
  product is

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<langle\>|u,v|\<rangle\>>=-<frac|1|r<rsub|G>><math-up|Retr><around|(|u*v|)>,
  </equation*>

  the colour basis <math|<around|(|e<rsub|b>|)>> used below is orthonormal
  for it, and

  <\equation*>
    -<math-up|tr><around|(|<math-up|ad><space|0.17em>u<space|0.17em><math-up|ad><space|0.17em>v|)>=C<rsub|G>*<around|\<langle\>|u,v|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em>C<rsub|G>=-<frac|1|dim
    \<frak-g\>>*<big|sum><rsub|b><math-up|tr><around*|(|<around|(|<math-up|ad><space|0.17em>e<rsub|b>|)><rsup|2>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Simplicity is what makes the left side a multiple of the inner product. The
  normalization matters for the constant <math|\<lambda\><rsub|G>> below:
  with the trace form <math|-<math-up|tr><around|(|u*v|)>> in place of
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|u,v|\<rangle\>>> the constant <math|C<rsub|G>>
  would be divided by <math|r<rsub|G>>. For <math|S*U<around|(|N|)>> in its
  defining representation, <math|C<rsub|G>=2*N<rsup|2>>. The spherical area
  of <math|S<rsup|3>> is <math|2*\<pi\><rsup|2>>. Rotational invariance gives

  <\equation>
    <big|int><rsub|S<rsup|3>>\<theta\><rsub|1><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d*\<theta\>=<frac|2*\<pi\><rsup|2>|4>,<space|2em><big|int><rsub|S<rsup|3>>\<theta\><rsub|1><rsup|2>*\<theta\><rsub|0><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d*\<theta\>=<frac|2*\<pi\><rsup|2>|24>.<label|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT3>
  </equation>

  For the second identity, set <math|A=<big|int>\<theta\><rsub|0><rsup|4>>
  and <math|B=<big|int>\<theta\><rsub|0><rsup|2>*\<theta\><rsub|1><rsup|2>>.
  Coordinate permutations make these independent of the distinct indices.
  From <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>\<theta\><rsub|i><rsup|2>=1>,
  <math|4*A+12*B=2*\<pi\><rsup|2>>. Rotating the <math|0>-axis to
  <math|<around|(|e<rsub|0>+e<rsub|1>|)>/<sqrt|2>> gives
  <math|A=<around|(|2*A+6*B|)>/4>, hence <math|A=3*B> and
  <math|B=2*\<pi\><rsup|2>/24>. The first identity follows by integrating
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>\<theta\><rsub|i><rsup|2>=1>.

  Consequently the angular mean of the parenthesis in
  <eqref|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT2> is

  <\equation*>
    r<rsup|-4>*<around*|(|<frac|1|4>-<frac|4|24>-1|)>=-<frac|11|12>*r<rsup|-4>.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|0\<less\>\<mu\>\<leqslant\>1>. Since the Euclidean polar Jacobian
  is <math|r<rsup|3>*d*r*<space|0.17em>d*\<theta\>>,

  <\equation*>
    <big|int><rsub|\<mu\>\<less\><around|\||x|\|>\<leqslant\>1><around|\||x|\|><rsup|-4>*<space|0.17em>d*x=2*\<pi\><rsup|2>*<big|int><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|1><frac|d*r|r>=2*\<pi\><rsup|2>*log
    \<mu\><rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  Using <math|c<rsub|<math-up|wt>>=<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-4>>, we obtain
  the exact pole law

  <\equation>
    c<rsub|<math-up|wt>>*<big|int><rsub|\<mu\>\<less\><around|\||x|\|>\<leqslant\>1>\<Pi\><rsub|G><around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x=-<frac|11*C<rsub|G>|24*\<pi\><rsup|2>>*log
    \<mu\><rsup|-1>=-\<lambda\><rsub|G>*log
    \<mu\><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>\<lambda\><rsub|G>=<frac|11*C<rsub|G>|24*\<pi\><rsup|2>>.<label|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT4>
  </equation>

  This fixes the sign and normalization of the logarithm; in particular the
  pole is not defined only up to an angular constant.

  <subsubsection|The finite-volume two-channel calculation>

  We now state the two scale receipts used by the finite-volume construction.
  Let <math|x\<in\><around|[|-\<pi\>,\<pi\>|]><rsup|4>> be a centered
  momentum, <math|r=<around|\||x|\|>>, and
  <math|0\<less\>\<mu\>\<leqslant\>1>. The response kernel is the one of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-annulus-fleet-faces>: for a local record
  with color <math|b> and regulator <math|\<mu\>>, it is one half of the
  two-leg figure of the vector-channel total film minus that of the
  ghost-channel total film. Write <math|\<cal-K\><around|(|x|)>\<in\>\<bbb-C\>>
  for its coefficient <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|b><around|(|x|)>> defined before
  <eqref|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT1>, and
  <math|g<around|(|x|)>=<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>\<cal-K\><around|(|x|)>>.
  With the faces <math|U,L,H> of <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F5>
  in that section, formed in each of the two channels with the same
  background <math|x>, the same mark <math|<around|(|1,b|)>>, the same boost
  <math|e<rsub|0>> and the same regulator <math|\<mu\>>, the six complex
  second-derivative traces are

  <\equation*>
    <tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-halign|l>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-lborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-halign|l>|<cwith|1|-1|3|3|cell-halign|l>|<cwith|1|-1|3|3|cell-rborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-valign|top>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-vmode|exact>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-height|13pt>|<table|<row|<cell|D=U<rsub|<math-up|vec>><rprime|''><around|(|0|)>,>|<cell|d=L<rsub|<math-up|vec>><rprime|''><around|(|0|)>,>|<cell|d<rsub|0>=H<rsub|<math-up|vec>><rprime|''><around|(|0|)>,>>|<row|<cell|Y=U<rsub|<math-up|gh>><rprime|''><around|(|0|)>,>|<cell|c=L<rsub|<math-up|gh>><rprime|''><around|(|0|)>,>|<cell|c<rsub|0>=H<rsub|<math-up|gh>><rprime|''><around|(|0|)>.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  The halo traces <math|c<rsub|0>,d<rsub|0>> and the pole
  <math|\<Pi\><rsub|G>> are defined only for <math|r\<gtr\>0>. The exact
  identities are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-two-wave-identities>\<cal-K\>=<frac|1|2>*<around*|(|<frac|1|2>*<around|(|D-d|)>-<around|(|Y-c|)>|)>,<space|2em>\<Pi\><rsub|G>=<frac|1|2>*<around*|(|c<rsub|0>-<frac|1|2>*d<rsub|0>|)>,
  </equation>

  <\equation>
    \<cal-K\>-\<Pi\><rsub|G>=<frac|1|2>*<around*|(|<frac|1|2>*D-Y|)>+<frac|1|2>*<around*|(|<around|(|c-c<rsub|0>|)>-<frac|1|2>*<around|(|d-d<rsub|0>|)>|)>.<label|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT5b>
  </equation>

  The first identity holds at every centered <math|x>, including <math|x=0>:
  the two-leg figure of a film is the trace of its upper face minus that of
  its lower face, and the response kernel is one half of the vector figure
  minus the ghost figure. The second is the free halo identity, with marked
  direction and color fixed; it and <eqref|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT5b>
  hold for <math|r\<gtr\>0> and are used only on <math|\<mu\>\<leqslant\>r>.
  The third is their algebraic difference; the real-valued pole
  <math|\<Pi\><rsub|G>> is embedded in <math|\<bbb-C\>> in these formulas.
  None of the three involves a torus side.

  For clarity, the second identity follows directly from the two free
  propagator numerators, that is, from the linear legs <math|w<rsub|b>>
  (ghost) and <math|v<rsub|b>> (vector) of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-annulus-fleet-faces> and the free
  inverses <math|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|-2>*I>. In the selected unit color
  direction <math|e<rsub|b>>, use the normalization
  <math|<math-up|tr><around|(|<math-up|ad><around|(|e<rsub|b>|)><rsup|2>|)>=-C<rsub|G>>.
  Put <math|\<rho\>=<around|\||x|\|><rsup|2>>,
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|col>>=-C<rsub|G>>, and <math|L=-4*x<rsub|1><rsup|2>>.
  Along the marked displacement <math|t*e<rsub|0>>, the common denominator is
  <math|\<Delta\><around|(|t|)>=\<rho\>+2*x<rsub|0>*t+t<rsup|2>>. The ghost
  numerator is the scalar orbital factor <math|L> times the color trace,
  giving <math|L*c<rsub|<math-up|col>>/\<rho\>>. For the vector numerator,
  write <math|p=-2*i*x<rsub|1>> and let <math|A<around|(|t|)>> have entries
  <math|A<rsub|01><around|(|t|)>=-2*i*t>,
  <math|A<rsub|10><around|(|t|)>=2*i*t>, and all other entries zero. Then
  <math|p<rsup|2>=L> and direct multiplication gives

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|tr><around*|[|<around|(|p*I+A*<around|(|-t|)>|)>*<around|(|p*I+A<around|(|t|)>|)>|]>=4*p<rsup|2>-8*t<rsup|2>=4*L-8*t<rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  Multiplying by the same color trace and scalar denominator factor gives the
  vector numerator <math|\<rho\><rsup|-1>*<around|(|4*L-8*t<rsup|2>|)>*c<rsub|<math-up|col>>>.
  Differentiating the quotients twice at <math|t=0> gives

  <\equation*>
    <around*|(|<frac|L*c<rsub|<math-up|col>>/\<rho\>|\<Delta\>>|)><rprime|''><rsub|t=0>=-<frac|2*L*c<rsub|<math-up|col>>|\<rho\><rsup|3>>+<frac|8*L*c<rsub|<math-up|col>>*x<rsub|0><rsup|2>|\<rho\><rsup|4>>,<space|2em><around*|(|<frac|\<rho\><rsup|-1>*<around|(|4*L-8*t<rsup|2>|)>*c<rsub|<math-up|col>>|\<Delta\>>|)><rprime|''><rsub|t=0>=-<frac|16*c<rsub|<math-up|col>>|\<rho\><rsup|2>>-<frac|8*L*c<rsub|<math-up|col>>|\<rho\><rsup|3>>+<frac|32*L*c<rsub|<math-up|col>>*x<rsub|0><rsup|2>|\<rho\><rsup|4>>.
  </equation*>

  Thus the orbital trace minus one half of the vector trace is

  <\equation*>
    <frac|8*c<rsub|<math-up|col>>|\<rho\><rsup|2>>+<frac|2*L*c<rsub|<math-up|col>>|\<rho\><rsup|3>>-<frac|8*L*c<rsub|<math-up|col>>*x<rsub|0><rsup|2>|\<rho\><rsup|4>>=8*C<rsub|G>*<around*|(|<frac|x<rsub|1><rsup|2>|r<rsup|6>>-4*<frac|x<rsub|1><rsup|2>*x<rsub|0><rsup|2>|r<rsup|8>>-<frac|1|r<rsup|4>>|)>=2*\<Pi\><rsub|G><around|(|x|)>,
  </equation*>

  which proves the halo identity in <eqref|eq:ex-two-wave-identities>.
  <eqref|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT5b> is then obtained by
  subtracting the just-computed free identity from the first identity and
  collecting terms.

  The first family of estimates is the all-centered-record receipt, with
  <math|T<rsub|1>=r+\<mu\>> and <math|R<rsub|0>=1+2*\<pi\>>:

  <\equation>
    0\<leqslant\>r,<space|1em>T<rsub|1>=r+\<mu\>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|0>,<space|2em><around|\||\<cal-K\>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|1>*T<rsub|1><rsup|-4>.<label|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT6>
  </equation>

  The second is the shell receipt, available only on
  <math|\<mu\>\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>1> and using <math|T<rsub|2>=r>:

  <\equation>
    <around|\||D|\|>\<leqslant\>u*r<rsup|-2>+v*\<mu\><rsup|2>*r<rsup|-6>,<space|2em><around|\||Y|\|>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|<math-up|ref>>*r<rsup|-2>+v<rsub|<math-up|ref>>*\<mu\><rsup|2>*r<rsup|-6>,<label|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT7>
  </equation>

  <\equation>
    <around|\||c-c<rsub|0>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|0>*<around|(|r+\<mu\><rsup|2>*r<rsup|-2>|)>*r<rsup|-4>,<space|2em><around|\||d-d<rsub|0>|\|>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|0>*<around|(|r+\<mu\><rsup|2>*r<rsup|-2>|)>*r<rsup|-4>.<label|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT8>
  </equation>

  All constants <math|A<rsub|1>,u,v,u<rsub|<math-up|ref>>,v<rsub|<math-up|ref>>,A<rsub|0>,B<rsub|0>>
  are nonnegative and are chosen before the local record: they do not depend
  on its regulator, color, bed, clip or strip width. The subscript
  <with|font-family|rm|ref> marks the ghost channel. These two receipts are
  kept separate: the all-centered estimate uses scale <math|r+\<mu\>>, while
  the shell comparison uses scale <math|r>. They are the conclusions
  <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F7> and
  <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F8> of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-fleet-face-bounds>, applied in the two
  channels to the family of all centered records with the prices derived
  there (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-row-to-floe> and the trajectory-price
  paragraph): <eqref|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT6> follows from
  <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F7> at scale <math|r+\<mu\>>
  and the first identity in <eqref|eq:ex-two-wave-identities>,
  <eqref|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT7> is the upper bound in
  <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F7> at scale <math|r>, and
  <eqref|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT8> is
  <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F8> at scale <math|r>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Two wave bounds from the finite
    response><label|prop:ex-two-wave>Suppose
    <eqref|eq:ex-two-wave-identities>\U<eqref|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT8>
    hold uniformly. Then

    <\equation>
      <around|\||g<around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|A<rsub|1>|<around|(|r+\<mu\>|)><rsup|4>>,<space|2em><around|\||g<around|(|x|)>-\<Pi\><rsub|G><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|A<rsub|2>|r<rsup|3>>+<frac|A<rsub|2>*\<mu\><rsup|2>|r<rsup|6>>*<space|1em><around|(|\<mu\>\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>1|)>,<label|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT9>
    </equation>

    where

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|2>=max <around|{|u+u<rsub|<math-up|ref>>+A<rsub|0>+B<rsub|0>,v+v<rsub|<math-up|ref>>+A<rsub|0>+B<rsub|0>|}>.
    </equation*>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The first estimate follows from <eqref|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT6>
    and <math|<around|\||<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>z|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||z|\|>>.
    On the annulus, use <eqref|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT5b>
    and <eqref|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT7>\U<eqref|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT8>:

    <\equation>
      <around|\||\<cal-K\>-\<Pi\><rsub|G>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|u+u<rsub|<math-up|ref>>|)>*r<rsup|-2>+<around|(|v+v<rsub|<math-up|ref>>|)>*\<mu\><rsup|2>*r<rsup|-6>+<around|(|A<rsub|0>+B<rsub|0>|)>*<around|(|r<rsup|-3>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*r<rsup|-6>|)>.<label|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT10>
    </equation>

    Here <math|<tfrac|1|2>> factors are bounded by one, and
    <math|<around|(|r+\<mu\><rsup|2>*r<rsup|-2>|)>*r<rsup|-4>=r<rsup|-3>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*r<rsup|-6>>.
    Since <math|r\<leqslant\>1>, <math|r<rsup|-2>\<leqslant\>r<rsup|-3>>. The
    displayed <math|A<rsub|2>> bounds <eqref|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT10>
    by <math|A<rsub|2>*<around|(|r<rsup|-3>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*r<rsup|-6>|)>>.
    Taking real parts gives <math|<around|\||g-\<Pi\><rsub|G>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<cal-K\>-\<Pi\><rsub|G>|\|>>,
    proving <eqref|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT9>.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The two applications of the finite-volume receipts>

  The common family of local records is used twice, with different scale
  parameters. Each record supplies a centered momentum <math|x>, a positive
  regulator <math|\<mu\>\<leqslant\>1>, and the same complex response
  coefficient <math|\<cal-K\><around|(|x|)>>. The centered-box condition
  <math|<around|\||x<rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<pi\>> gives
  <math|<around|\||x|\|>\<leqslant\>2*\<pi\>>.

  For the all-centered estimate, use scale
  <math|T<rsub|1>=<around|\||x|\|>+\<mu\>>. The whole-frame bound from the
  two channels is

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||D-d|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|<math-up|int>>*T<rsub|1><rsup|-4>,<space|2em><around|\||Y-c|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|<math-up|ref>>*T<rsub|1><rsup|-4>.
  </equation*>

  The exact first identity in <eqref|eq:ex-two-wave-identities> then gives
  <math|<around|\||\<cal-K\>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|A<rsub|<math-up|int>>/4+A<rsub|<math-up|ref>>/2|)>*T<rsub|1><rsup|-4>>.
  This is the first radial estimate; it applies also at <math|x=0>.

  For the annular comparison, restrict the same records to
  <math|\<mu\>\<leqslant\><around|\||x|\|>> and use the second scale
  <math|T<rsub|2>=<around|\||x|\|>>. The upper face of the vector channel is
  split into a principal term and a tadpole term; their charged bounds are
  <math|u*T<rsub|2><rsup|-2>> and <math|v*\<mu\><rsup|2>*T<rsub|2><rsup|-6>>.
  The same split for the ghost channel has bounds
  <math|u<rsub|<math-up|ref>>*T<rsub|2><rsup|-2>> and
  <math|v<rsub|<math-up|ref>>*\<mu\><rsup|2>*T<rsub|2><rsup|-6>>. The
  lower-channel remainders after subtracting their respective halos are
  bounded by <math|A<rsub|0>*<around|(|T<rsub|2>+\<mu\><rsup|2>/T<rsub|2><rsup|2>|)>*T<rsub|2><rsup|-4>>
  and <math|B<rsub|0>*<around|(|T<rsub|2>+\<mu\><rsup|2>/T<rsub|2><rsup|2>|)>*T<rsub|2><rsup|-4>>.
  The free quotient calculation gives <math|\<Pi\><rsub|G>=<frac|1|2>*<around|(|c<rsub|0>-<frac|1|2>*d<rsub|0>|)>>,
  so these four estimates feed exactly into
  <eqref|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT5b> and yield
  <eqref|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT7>\U<eqref|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT10>.

  These scale receipts are thus two separate applications of the
  finite-volume receipts to the common family of local records: the first
  applies the frame estimate to every centered record, while the second
  restricts to the subfamily <math|\<mu\>\<leqslant\><around|\||x|\|>> before
  applying the shell estimate. They use the same response coefficient and
  common constants. The annulus proof therefore does not interchange a limit
  with a volume-dependent integral. The comparison of this analytic
  coefficient with the finite sampled pole test is a separate recording
  estimate, with an error equal to the site count times a quantity tending to
  zero; exact equality with the finite sampled coefficient is not asserted,
  and nothing of the kind is inferred from
  <eqref|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT1>.

  <subparagraph|Scope.> This section proves the normalized pole integral, the
  halo identity and the algebraic conversion from the two scale receipts to
  the radial bounds. The receipts themselves are proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-annulus-fleet-faces> for the family of
  all centered records. The recording estimate is proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-annulus-recording>. The order of choices
  there differs from the one for the radial constants: the fee strip of the
  response and the constants <math|A<rsub|1>,A<rsub|2>> are fixed before the
  blocking side <math|L>, whereas the strip width <math|z<rsub|L>> and the
  bound <math|K<rsub|L>> of the regulated recording kernel are obtained after
  <math|L> is fixed, with <math|\<mu\>=L<rsup|-1>>, and may depend on
  <math|L>. For fixed <math|L> the recording error is the site count times
  <math|\<frak-r\><around|(|z<rsub|L>,K<rsub|L>|)>*e<rsup|-z<rsub|L>*\<ell\>/4>>,
  in the notation of that section, which tends to zero along every sequence
  of sides <math|\<ell\>\<to\>\<infty\>>.

  <subsection|Finite-volume recording and uniform
  drift><label|sec:ex-annulus-recording>

  The continuum pole calculation controls a coefficient of the analytically
  assembled response. The finite-volume argument needs two additional steps:
  an exact determinant identity at each torus size, and a quantitative bound
  on the difference between a torus mean and its zero Fourier mode. This
  section proves that bound, explains the determinant calculation and
  positive-matrix domain, and gives the exact scale order in the final
  annulus output. The local estimates used to feed this construction are
  those of Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-annulus-fleet-faces>
  and<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-annulus-two-wave>.

  Write <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|\<sigma\>><around|(|A|)>> for the class of
  periodic functions that are holomorphic when each imaginary momentum
  coordinate has modulus less than <math|\<sigma\>> and have supremum norm at
  most <math|A> there. The coefficient of a translation-invariant two-leg
  picture is its mixed second derivative; its finite-volume realization at a
  conserving pair is <math|\<ell\><rsup|4>> times that coefficient. Write
  <math|<math-up|Err><rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<cal-Y\>,\<cal-P\>;\<delta\>|)>>
  when every mixed two-leg coefficient of the finite test <math|\<cal-P\>>
  differs from this finite-volume realization for <math|\<cal-Y\>> by at most
  <math|\<ell\><rsup|4>*\<delta\>>.

  <subsubsection|A torus mean differs from the zero mode by an exponential
  tail>

  Let <math|\<ell\>\<geqslant\>1> be an integer and let <math|f> be a
  periodic function of four real momenta, holomorphic and bounded by <math|A>
  on the complex strip of width <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0>. Write its
  absolutely convergent Fourier series as

  <\equation*>
    f<around|(|\<theta\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>c<rsub|n>*e<rsup|i*n\<cdot\>\<theta\>>,<space|2em><around|\||n|\|><rsub|1>=<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|3><around|\||n<rsub|j>|\|>.
  </equation*>

  The normalized mean over the side-<math|\<ell\>> grid is

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<langle\>|f|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<ell\>>=\<ell\><rsup|-4>*<big|sum><rsub|q\<in\><around|(|\<bbb-Z\>/\<ell\>*\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|4>>f*<around|(|2*\<pi\>*q/\<ell\>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Character orthogonality in each coordinate says

  <\equation*>
    \<ell\><rsup|-4>*<big|sum><rsub|q>e<rsup|2*\<pi\>*i*n\<cdot\>q/\<ell\>>=\<b-1\><rsub|n\<in\>\<ell\>*\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>.
  </equation*>

  Consequently <math|<around|\<langle\>|f|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<ell\>>-c<rsub|0>>
  is the sum of precisely the nonzero Fourier coefficients whose indices lie
  in <math|\<ell\>*\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>. Every such index satisfies
  <math|<around|\||n|\|><rsub|1>\<geqslant\>\<ell\>>. Cauchy's integral
  formula, applied after shifting each coordinate contour by
  <math|\<pm\>i*<around|(|\<sigma\>/2|)>>, and the geometric-series bound in
  four coordinates give

  <\equation>
    <big|sum><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>e<rsup|<around|(|\<sigma\>/4|)><around|\||n|\|><rsub|1>><around|\||c<rsub|n>|\|>\<leqslant\>A*<around*|(|2*<frac|1+\<sigma\>/4|\<sigma\>/4>|)><rsup|4>.<label|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY1>
  </equation>

  For completeness, the one-coordinate sum used here is bounded by

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>>e<rsup|-h<around|\||k|\|>>=1+2*<big|sum><rsub|k\<geqslant\>1>e<rsup|-h*k>\<leqslant\>2*<frac|1+h|h>,<space|2em>h=\<sigma\>/4;
  </equation*>

  the last inequality follows from <math|e<rsup|h>\<geqslant\>1+h>. The
  contour shift gives the factor <math|e<rsup|-<around|(|\<sigma\>/2|)><around|\||n|\|><rsub|1>>>
  for an individual coefficient, leaving the weight
  <math|e<rsup|<around|(|\<sigma\>/4|)><around|\||n|\|><rsub|1>>> in
  <eqref|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY1>. Therefore

  <\equation>
    <around|\||<around|\<langle\>|f|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<ell\>>-c<rsub|0>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<frak-r\><around|(|\<sigma\>,A|)>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*\<ell\>/4>,<space|2em>\<frak-r\><around|(|\<sigma\>,A|)>=A*<around*|(|2*<frac|1+\<sigma\>/4|\<sigma\>/4>|)><rsup|4>.<label|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY2>
  </equation>

  This estimate is for the actual full grid at each <math|\<ell\>>; no
  subsequence selection is involved.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Recorded second-order coefficients><label|lem:ex-pole-recording>Let
    <math|F<rsub|i*j>*<around|(|p,q|)>> be two-momentum response entries in
    <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|\<sigma\>><around|(|A|)>> jointly in <math|p,q>. Let
    <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|\<ell\>>> be a real finite-volume test and assume
    its exact local coefficient identity: for every pair of colored sites
    <math|q,q<rprime|'>>, the mixed second coefficient of
    <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|\<ell\>>> is zero when <math|q+q<rprime|'>\<ne\>0>,
    and otherwise is

    <\equation>
      \<ell\><rsup|4><space|0.17em>\<langle\>F<rsub|i*j>(<math-up|mon><around|(|q<rprime|'>|)>,<space|0.17em>\<cdot\><space|0.17em><around|)||\<rangle\>><rsub|\<ell\>>.<label|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY3>
    </equation>

    Define the internal zero mode and limiting picture by

    <\equation*>
      <wide|F|^><rsub|i*j,0><around|(|p|)>=<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-T\><rsup|4>>F<rsub|i*j>*<around|(|p,q|)>*<space|0.17em>d*q,<space|2em>\<cal-Y\><rsub|i*j><around|(|p|)>=<tfrac|1|2><space|0.17em><wide|F|^><rsub|i*j,0><around|(|p|)>,
    </equation*>

    where Haar measure on <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|4>> is normalized to one. We
    use the translation-invariant two-leg convention in which the mixed
    second derivative is the polarized coefficient (there is no factorial in
    the definition of that coefficient). Thus the finite-volume realization
    at a conserving pair is <math|\<ell\><rsup|4>> times this coefficient,
    namely <math|\<ell\><rsup|4>*<space|0.17em>2*\<cal-Y\><rsub|i*j>>. Then
    <math|\<cal-Y\>> is interpreted as the translation-invariant two-leg
    picture: its mixed coefficient at external momenta <math|p,p<rprime|'>>
    is zero unless <math|p+p<rprime|'>=0>, and in the conserving case is
    <math|2*\<cal-Y\><rsub|i*j><around|(|p<rprime|'>|)>=<wide|F|^><rsub|i*j,0><around|(|p<rprime|'>|)>>.
    <math|\<cal-Y\>\<in\>\<cal-H\><rsub|\<sigma\>/2><around|(|A|)>>, and for
    every side <math|\<ell\>>,

    <\equation>
      <math-up|Err><rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<cal-Y\>,\<cal-P\><rsub|\<ell\>>;\<frak-r\><around|(|\<sigma\>,A|)>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*\<ell\>/4>|)>.<label|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY4>
    </equation>

    In the normalized convention <math|C<rsub|2>=D<rsup|2>/2>! used for
    coefficient pictures elsewhere (see <eqref|eq:output-record>),
    <eqref|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY4> says that for unit Fourier
    waves <math|v,w> of momenta <math|p,p<rprime|'>> and marks <math|i,j>

    <\equation>
      <around*|\||C<rsub|2><around|(|\<cal-P\><rsub|\<ell\>>|)><around|[|v,w|]>-\<b-1\><rsub|p+p<rprime|'>=0>*<space|0.17em>\<ell\><rsup|4>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-Y\><rsub|i*j><around|(|p<rprime|'>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2><space|0.17em>\<ell\><rsup|4>*<space|0.17em>\<frak-r\><around|(|\<sigma\>,A|)>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*\<ell\>/4>;<label|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY4-prime>
    </equation>

    in particular <math|\<cal-Y\>> records <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|\<ell\>>> in
    the sense of <eqref|eq:output-record> with error
    <math|\<ell\><rsup|4>*\<frak-r\><around|(|\<sigma\>,A|)>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*\<ell\>/4>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The normalized Haar integral defining <math|<wide|F|^><rsub|i*j,0>> is
    holomorphic in <math|p> on the width-<math|\<sigma\>> strip: on each
    compact substrip its integrand and derivatives are uniformly bounded, so
    differentiation under the integral is valid. Its norm is at most
    <math|A>, hence the half-scaled picture is bounded by <math|A> on the
    width-<math|\<sigma\>/2> strip. For each fixed real <math|p>, apply
    <eqref|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY2> to the one-variable
    function <math|q\<mapsto\>F<rsub|i*j>*<around|(|p,q|)>>; its Fourier zero
    mode is exactly <math|<wide|F|^><rsub|i*j,0><around|(|p|)>>. This gives

    <\equation*>
      \|\<langle\>F<rsub|i*j>*<around|(|p,\<cdot\><around|)||\<rangle\>><rsub|\<ell\>>-<wide|F|^><rsub|i*j,0><around|(|p|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<frak-r\><around|(|\<sigma\>,A|)>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*\<ell\>/4>
    </equation*>

    on the real external momentum torus. If <math|q+q<rprime|'>\<ne\>0>, both
    mixed coefficients in the definition of
    <math|<math-up|Err><rsub|\<ell\>>> vanish by momentum conservation. If
    <math|q+q<rprime|'>=0>, the second Taylor coefficient of
    <math|\<cal-Y\>=<frac|1|2>*<wide|F|^><rsub|i*j,0>> has the factor
    <math|2!=2>, so it equals <math|<wide|F|^><rsub|i*j,0>>; subtracting
    <eqref|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY3> and multiplying the mean
    estimate by the number <math|\<ell\><rsup|4>> of sites gives
    <eqref|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY4>. Dividing by <math|2>!
    gives <eqref|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY4-prime>.
  </proof>

  For a finite side <math|\<ell\>>, set

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-Y\><rsub|\<ell\>,i*j><around|(|p|)>=<tfrac|1|2><space|0.17em>\<ell\><rsup|-4>*<big|sum><rsub|q\<in\><around|(|\<bbb-Z\>/\<ell\>*\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|4>>F<rsub|i*j>*<around|(|p,2*\<pi\>*q/\<ell\>|)>
  </equation*>

  at each external grid momentum. By <eqref|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY3>,
  its second finite-volume coefficient is
  <math|2*\<ell\><rsup|4>*\<cal-Y\><rsub|\<ell\>,i*j><around|(|p|)>>. The
  limiting picture also has the exchange and reality symmetries

  <\equation>
    \<cal-Y\><rsub|i*j><around|(|p|)>=\<cal-Y\><rsub|j*i>*<around|(|-p|)>,<space|2em><wide|\<cal-Y\><rsub|i*j><around|(|p|)>|\<bar\>>=\<cal-Y\><rsub|i*j>*<around|(|-p|)>.<label|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY4a>
  </equation>

  At each finite side, the logarithmic determinant is a real scalar function
  of real field coordinates. Equality of its mixed derivatives gives exchange
  of the two external legs, while reality of its Hessian gives conjugation
  under Fourier inversion. Thus the finite-volume normalized pictures have
  both relations at every allowed grid momentum. Estimate
  <eqref|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY2> says
  <math|\<cal-Y\><rsub|\<ell\>,i*j><around|(|p|)>\<to\>\<cal-Y\><rsub|i*j><around|(|p|)>>
  uniformly over the external grid points as <math|\<ell\>\<to\>\<infty\>>.
  Thus their difference from <math|\<cal-Y\>> tends uniformly to zero on
  these grids as the side tends to infinity. The union of the full side grids
  is dense in the momentum torus, and <math|\<cal-Y\>> is continuous by its
  analytic strip bound. Passing along cofinal sides and then using density
  proves <eqref|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY4a> everywhere. This
  supplies the Hessian symmetry and reality of the half-scaled zero-mode
  picture; it does not follow from factorial normalization alone.

  The factor <math|1/2> in <math|\<cal-Y\>> is essential: the finite-volume
  mixed second derivative of <math|<frac|1|2>*<wide|F|^><rsub|i*j,0>>
  contributes <math|2!<space|0.17em><frac|1|2>*<wide|F|^><rsub|i*j,0>=<wide|F|^><rsub|i*j,0>>.
  This is why <eqref|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY3> compares the
  normalized grid mean to the continuum coefficient and why the error is the
  per-site Fourier error multiplied by the site count exactly once.

  <subsubsection|Determinant identity and positive-matrix domain>

  In the application, the pole test is

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-P\><rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|x|)>=<tfrac|1|2>log det
    P<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|x|)>-log det V<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|x|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|P<rsub|\<ell\>>> is the positive rank matrix and
  <math|V<rsub|\<ell\>>> the positive reference matrix. The finite ticket
  identifies the zero-, one-, and two-leg Taylor layers of
  <math|P<rsub|\<ell\>>> with those of the interacting response, and those of
  <math|V<rsub|\<ell\>>> with the reference response. The floor and
  positive-regulator hypotheses make both matrices positive definite at
  <math|x=0>; by continuity their real logarithmic determinants are smooth
  nearby. Ordinary matrix differentiation gives

  <\equation>
    <\aligned>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|D*log det
      P<around|[|H|]>>|<cell|=<math-up|tr><around|(|P<rsup|-1>*D*P<around|[|H|]>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|D<rsup|2>*log
      det P*<around|[|H,K|]>>|<cell|=<math-up|tr><space|-0.17em><around*|(|P<rsup|-1>*D<rsup|2>*P*<around|[|H,K|]>-P<rsup|-1>*D*P<around|[|K|]>*P<rsup|-1>*D*P<around|[|H|]>|)>.>>>>
    </aligned>

    <label|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY8>
  </equation>

  and the same formulas for <math|V>. Write <math|A<around|(|r|)>> for either
  matrix family, <math|B<rsub|i>*<around|(|p,r|)>> for its first variation
  (from background momentum <math|r> to <math|r+p>), and
  <math|C<rsub|i*j>*<around|(|p,q,r|)>> for its mixed second variation (from
  <math|r> to <math|r+p+q>). The shifted fiber block of the mixed derivative
  of the logarithmic determinant at the ordered pair of legs is the trace of

  <\equation>
    A*<around|(|r+p+q|)><rsup|-1>*C<rsub|i*j>*<around|(|p,q,r|)>-A*<around|(|r+p+q|)><rsup|-1>*B<rsub|i>*<around|(|p,r+q|)>*A*<around|(|r+q|)><rsup|-1>*B<rsub|j>*<around|(|q,r|)>.<label|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY8a>
  </equation>

  This follows by differentiating <math|D*log det
  A<around|[|H|]>=<math-up|tr><around|(|A<rsup|-1>*D*A<around|[|H|]>|)>> and
  using

  <\equation*>
    D<around|(|A<rsup|-1>|)><around|[|K|]>=-A<rsup|-1>*D*A<around|[|K|]>*A<rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  There is one ordered product for the specified ordered pair; exchanging the
  legs gives the corresponding other entry, not an extra copy in
  <eqref|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY8a>. Sum this expression over
  internal momentum and apply the weights in the finite ticket. Formula
  <eqref|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY8a> is the shifted fiber block
  of the Hessian. Translation invariance makes the full trace entry zero
  unless the external momenta conserve; in the conserving case summing the
  fiber blocks gives the normalized torus mean multiplied by
  <math|\<ell\><rsup|4>>. This finite-volume determinant calculation precedes
  the Fourier tail limit.

  The Fourier estimate is entrywise. Any closed-cone passage for finite
  reflection matrices and the smooth-word approximation to point
  configurations belong to the separate reflection argument.

  <subsubsection|Order of choices in the all-period output>

  Here is the exact downstream implication, with its inputs exposed. Fix
  <math|\<sigma\>,\<epsilon\>,\<tau\>\<gtr\>0> and <math|C,Z,A\<geqslant\>0>.
  Assume a baseline analytic family <math|H> has strip fee
  <math|<around|(|k,E|)>> with <math|k\<gtr\>0>, <math|E\<geqslant\>0>, and
  matches the free Wilson response at every volume. Assume the response
  service chooses <math|v\<gtr\>0,M\<geqslant\>0>, depending only on these
  external data, with the following property: for every sequence of positive
  integer sides <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>\<to\>\<infty\>>, every bed <math|B>,
  clip <math|p>, path <math|R>, and budget <math|\<Lambda\>> satisfying the
  path bound <math|<around|(|\<tau\>,A,\<Lambda\>|)>> in <math|p>, attachment
  of <math|B> to <math|R>, metric strip bound
  <math|<around|(|\<sigma\>,C|)>>, direct-term bound <math|Z>, the floors
  <math|<around|(|\<epsilon\>,C|)>> of <math|B,p> at every torus side (not
  only at the sides <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>>), and the sworn condition at every
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>>, it provides a family <math|T> with fee
  <math|<around|(|v,M|)>>, whose zero-, one-, and two-leg layers vanish to
  orders three, two, and one respectively, and exact analytic cancellation of
  the target response against the Wilson term at every
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>>. Require also the exact finite-layer realization:
  for every <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>>, the interacting and reference matrices
  are real analytic on the real momentum torus, Hermitian positive definite
  at the origin with a positive lower eigenvalue bound uniform in <math|j>,
  and their Taylor layers of orders zero, one and two are the layers
  specified by the response ticket. The resulting shifted-fiber kernel
  <math|F<rsub|c,\<ell\><rsub|j>>*<around|(|p,q|)>> is the restriction of one
  common periodic kernel <math|F<rsub|c>*<around|(|p,q|)>>, holomorphic and
  bounded by some <math|K> whenever every imaginary momentum coordinate has
  modulus less than some <math|z\<gtr\>0>, uniformly in <math|j> and color
  <math|c>. This is the common-strip and common-layer content needed for the
  full-grid limit; positivity at the origin alone does not imply it. Assume
  also the uniform two-wave output: for every combined family below, every
  color <math|c>, and every regulator <math|0\<less\>y\<leqslant\>1>, the
  same constants <math|a,b\<geqslant\>0> give

  <\equation>
    <around|\||\<kappa\><rsub|y,c><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>a*<around|(|<around|\||x|\|>+y|)><rsup|-4>,<space|2em><around|\||\<kappa\><rsub|y,c><around|(|x|)>-\<Pi\><rsub|G,c><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>b*<around|\||x*<around|\||<rsup|-3>+b*y<rsup|2>|\|>*x|\|><rsup|-6>*<space|1em><around|(|y\<leqslant\><around|\||x|\|>\<leqslant\>1|)><label|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY9>
  </equation>

  on the centered momentum box. This states the uniform local estimate needed
  from the next producer; it is not deduced from the finite-volume recording
  argument.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|All-period recording and drift><label|thm:ex-all-period-recording>Under
    these hypotheses, for every admissible path datum, every odd integer
    <math|L\<geqslant\>1>, and every side sequence
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>\<to\>\<infty\>> there are a picture
    <math|\<cal-Y\><rsub|L>>, constants <math|z<rsub|L>\<gtr\>0>,
    <math|K<rsub|L>\<geqslant\>0>, and numbers
    <math|e<rsub|L,j>\<geqslant\>0> such that

    <\equation>
      \<cal-Y\><rsub|L>\<in\>\<cal-H\><rsub|z<rsub|L>/2><around|(|K<rsub|L>|)>,<space|2em>e<rsub|L,j>=\<frak-r\><around|(|z<rsub|L>,K<rsub|L>|)>*e<rsup|-z<rsub|L>*\<ell\><rsub|j>/4>\<longrightarrow\>0,<space|2em><math-up|Err><rsub|\<ell\><rsub|j>><around|(|\<cal-Y\><rsub|L>,\<cal-P\><rsub|\<ell\><rsub|j>>;e<rsub|L,j>|)>,<label|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY10>
    </equation>

    and, for every color <math|c>,

    <\equation>
      <around*|\||\<alpha\><rsub|c><around|(|\<cal-Y\><rsub|L>|)>+\<lambda\><rsub|G>*log
      L|\|>\<leqslant\>D<rsub|0>,<space|1em>D<rsub|0>=c<rsub|<math-up|wt>>*<around*|[|a*<around*|(|<frac|\<pi\><rsup|2>|2>+<around|\||E<rsub|4>|\|>|)>+3*\<pi\><rsup|2>*b|]>.<label|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY11>
    </equation>

    The constant <math|D<rsub|0>> is independent of <math|L> and of the side
    sequence.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Choose the baseline before the response family. Put <math|w=min
    <around|(|k,v|)>\<gtr\>0>; restrict both fee bounds to <math|w> and add
    the families, obtaining fee <math|<around|(|w,M+E|)>>. Now fix <math|L>
    and set

    <\equation>
      y=L<rsup|-1>,<space|2em>s=min <around|{|\<sigma\>,w,y/128|}>\<gtr\>0.<label|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY5>
    </equation>

    Then <math|0\<less\>y\<leqslant\>1>, and <math|s\<leqslant\>y/128> is the
    phase condition in the local determinant-floor estimate. At every
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>>, the response ticket matches the target minus the
    Wilson term, while the baseline ticket matches the Wilson term. Adding
    their zero-, one-, and two-leg Taylor identities gives the target ticket.
    The finite determinant formula <eqref|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY8>
    turns this into the exact local coefficient identity
    <eqref|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY3>, and the floor and
    positive regulator bounds ensure both determinants are nonzero at zero.
    The floors at every torus side and the metric assumptions identify the
    regulated interacting table with the tested local determinant table; the
    reference table is identified by its explicit regulator formula. These
    are the two tab identities required by the local positivity/floor
    argument at scale <math|s>. The exact finite-layer and common-strip
    receipt in the hypotheses supplies <math|z<rsub|L>\<gtr\>0>,
    <math|K<rsub|L>\<geqslant\>0> and the common analytic two-momentum kernel
    in <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|z<rsub|L>><around|(|K<rsub|L>|)>>. Applying
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-pole-recording> yields

    <\equation>
      e<rsub|L,j>=\<frak-r\><around|(|z<rsub|L>,K<rsub|L>|)>*e<rsup|-z<rsub|L>*\<ell\><rsub|j>/4>,<space|2em><math-up|Err><rsub|\<ell\><rsub|j>><around|(|\<cal-Y\><rsub|L>,\<cal-P\><rsub|\<ell\><rsub|j>>;e<rsub|L,j>|)>.<label|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY12>
    </equation>

    The positive <math|z<rsub|L>> and <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>\<to\>\<infty\>>
    imply <math|e<rsub|L,j>\<to\>0> for this fixed <math|L>. No uniformity of
    <math|z<rsub|L>,K<rsub|L>> in <math|L> is required or asserted.

    The same response satisfies <eqref|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY9>,
    uniformly in color <math|c>, at regulator <math|y>. The
    coefficient-integral identity <eqref|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT1>
    of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-annulus-two-wave> identifies
    <math|\<alpha\><rsub|c><around|(|\<cal-Y\><rsub|L>|)>> with the
    normalized integral over <math|E<rsub|4>> of
    <math|g=<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>\<kappa\><rsub|y,c>>, which is bounded
    and continuous, and <eqref|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT4>
    gives the pole integral <math|c<rsub|<math-up|wt>>*<big|int><rsub|y\<less\><around|\||x|\|>\<leqslant\>1>\<Pi\><rsub|G>=-\<lambda\><rsub|G>*log
    y<rsup|-1>>. These are the hypotheses of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:path-radial-log>, with <math|A<rsub|1>=a>,
    <math|A<rsub|2>=b> and <math|\<mu\>=y>; its conclusion is
    <eqref|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY11>, with the same
    <math|D<rsub|0>> for every color. Explicitly, split <math|E<rsub|4>> into
    the ball <math|<around|\||x|\|>\<leqslant\>y>, the annulus
    <math|y\<less\><around|\||x|\|>\<leqslant\>1> and the rest. On the ball
    the first bound in <eqref|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY9> gives
    <math|<around|\||g|\|>\<leqslant\>a*y<rsup|-4>> and the volume is
    <math|\<pi\><rsup|2>*y<rsup|4>/2>. Outside the unit ball
    <math|<around|\||g|\|>\<leqslant\>a>. On the annulus
    <math|<big|int><around|\||x|\|><rsup|-3>*d*x=2*\<pi\><rsup|2>*<around|(|1-y|)>>
    and <math|y<rsup|2>*<big|int><around|\||x|\|><rsup|-6>*d*x=\<pi\><rsup|2>*<around|(|1-y<rsup|2>|)>>,
    so the second bound in <eqref|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY9>
    contributes at most <math|3*\<pi\><rsup|2>*b>. Finally <math|log
    y<rsup|-1>=log L>, while <math|a,b> were fixed uniformly before choosing
    <math|L>. Taking <math|L<rsub|min>=1> supplies the all-odd-side clause
    needed by the outer construction; oddness is not otherwise used in this
    estimate.
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|Scope.> This proves the finite Fourier tail, the exact
  conversion of local coefficient identities into a site-count-scaled
  recording error, and the downstream assembly from the stated baseline,
  response-service, local-positivity/floor, and uniform two-wave outputs. Of
  these four hypotheses, the finite-layer realization with its common strip
  is obtained in Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-annulus-same-data>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-annulus-common-strip>. The fixed-film identities
  are proved in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:annulus-fixed-film-tickets>, and
  the Wilson and correction response services in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:annulus-priced-responses> and its applications.
  The uniform two-wave output <eqref|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY9>
  is conclusion <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F11> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-annulus-fleet-faces>. Their combined
  statement is Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-annulus-estimate>, with
  the path, metric and floor hypotheses displayed there. No finite-volume
  coefficient is identified with the continuum integral without first passing
  through <eqref|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY12>.

  <subsection|The finite-volume and annulus estimates use one
  response><label|sec:ex-annulus-same-data>

  The finite-volume recording and the annulus bound must be attached to the
  same analytic response. This is a short compatibility step, but it prevents
  an invalid substitution of a different family after taking the Fourier
  limit. We state the join explicitly.

  Fix a simple gauge group, a clip <math|p> and path <math|R>, a bed
  <math|B>, and a sequence of torus sides <math|n<rsub|i>> tending to
  infinity. Responses, their fees, their three marked layers and greyness are
  as defined in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-annulus-fleet-faces>. We
  say that a response has the <em|tickets> of an analytic function
  <math|\<cal-U\>> of the link field on a torus if its zero-, one- and
  two-mark layers record exactly, on that torus, the marked derivatives of
  <math|\<cal-U\>>: the derivatives at the origin of
  <math|<around|(|y,z|)>\<mapsto\>\<cal-U\>*<around|(|e<rsup|y>*e<rsup|z>|)>>
  of order two in <math|y> and of order zero, one and two in <math|z>. Let
  <math|W> be one fee-bounded analytic Wilson response which is grey: it has
  the tickets of the Wilson action at <em|every> torus side, not only at the
  sides <math|n<rsub|i>>. This is the form in which the face estimates use
  it. Let <math|Q> be one fee-bounded correction response, with soft orders
  three, two, and one in its zero-, one-, and two-mark layers, which at every
  chosen side <math|n<rsub|i>> has the tickets of the finite action of
  <math|p,R> minus the Wilson action. Assume, for this same bed and path: the
  path bounds of <math|R> in <math|p>; the attachment of <math|B> to
  <math|R>; a metric strip bound for <math|B>; the floor of <math|B,p> with a
  constant <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0> at every torus side; and the sworn
  condition at every chosen side <math|n<rsub|i>>. Finally, the local-record
  family of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-annulus-fleet-faces> is formed
  with <math|V=W> as its vacuum response. Assume <math|W> and <math|Q> have a
  common positive response strip width <math|\<eta\>> and finite fee fixed
  before the odd side <math|L> is chosen. This is the width used for the
  uniform radial constants; it is distinct from the possibly smaller strip
  <math|z<rsub|L>> of the regulated recording kernel. For the logarithmic
  estimate, fix <math|0\<less\>\<mu\>\<leqslant\>1> before applying the
  radial bound, with <math|\<mu\>=L<rsup|-1>> for the requested odd side
  <math|L>. The Wilson fee, correction fee, floor parameter, and metric bound
  are common to the family; the bed, clip, and side sequence are the same in
  the recording and face estimates. The strip width and bound for the
  recorded kernel may depend on this fixed <math|L>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Same-response compatibility><label|lem:ex-annulus-same-data>Under
    these assumptions set <math|F=Q+W>. Then the following conclusions
    concern this one <math|F> and the same selected data:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>For every chosen torus side, the zero-, one-, and two-mark
      tickets of <math|F> are exactly the corresponding finite-action
      tickets. These are the three marked response layers used to identify
      the action variations in the finite test. If the regulated rank and
      reference matrices have their positive floors, the finite pole test is
      their logarithmic-determinant difference, and its conserving mixed
      coefficient is the site count times the normalized mesh mean of the
      shifted-fiber kernel of <math|F>.

      <item>The analytic kernel obtained from those shifted-fiber
      coefficients is independent of the torus side. Define the analytic
      picture by

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-Y\><rsub|i*j><around|(|p|)>=<frac|1|2>*<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-T\><rsup|4>>F<rsub|i*j>*<around|(|p,q|)>*<space|0.17em>d*q,
      </equation*>

      with normalized Haar measure. At a conserving pair the exact
      finite-volume mixed derivative of the pole test is
      <math|2*n<rsub|i><rsup|4>*\<cal-Y\><rsub|n<rsub|i>,i*j><around|(|p|)>>,
      where

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-Y\><rsub|n,i*j><around|(|p|)>=<frac|1|2*n<rsup|4>>*<big|sum><rsub|q\<in\><around|(|2*\<pi\>/n|)>*<around|(|\<bbb-Z\>/n*\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|4>>F<rsub|i*j>*<around|(|p,q|)>
      </equation*>

      is formed with the mesh mean; it is not
      <math|2*n<rsub|i><rsup|4>*\<cal-Y\><rsub|i*j><around|(|p|)>>, the
      quantity with which <math|<math-up|Err>> compares it. The difference
      between the mesh picture and <math|\<cal-Y\>> is controlled as follows.
      If the common kernel has bound <math|K> on a strip of width
      <math|z\<gtr\>0>, the picture <math|\<cal-Y\>> satisfies the recorded
      estimate

      <\equation*>
        <math-up|Err><rsub|n<rsub|i>><around|(|\<cal-Y\>,\<cal-P\><rsub|n<rsub|i>>;\<frak-r\><around|(|z,K|)>*e<rsup|-z*n<rsub|i>/4>|)>
      </equation*>

      for every chosen side <math|n<rsub|i>>.

      <item>For each color <math|c>, the radial bounds obtained from the same
      local-frame family and the same Wilson reference give

      <\equation*>
        <around|\||\<alpha\><rsub|c><around|(|\<cal-Y\>|)>+\<lambda\><rsub|G>*log
        \<mu\><rsup|-1>|\|>\<leqslant\>D<rsub|0>.
      </equation*>

      Here <math|D<rsub|0>> is fixed before the odd side parameter is chosen,
      while the strip constants <math|z,K> and hence the recording error may
      depend on <math|L>.
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The fee bounds on <math|Q> and <math|W> restrict to their common smaller
    strip and add. The exact response identities add as well: at each chosen
    side the zero-, one-, and two-mark ticket of <math|Q> is the
    finite-action ticket minus the Wilson ticket, while the corresponding
    ticket of <math|W> is the Wilson ticket. Hence these three tickets of
    <math|F> are exactly the finite-action tickets. The order-zero remainder
    construction fixes the normalization; no extra factor of the number of
    sites is inserted here.

    For each side, the positive floors make the two determinant matrices
    invertible at zero. Differentiating their logarithmic determinants gives
    one direct second variation and the single ordered product of first
    variations for each specified ordered pair. The ticket identity therefore
    identifies that finite mixed coefficient with the conserving mesh sum of
    the common shifted-fiber kernel. Nonconserving entries vanish by momentum
    conservation. The fiber trace contributes a site count exactly once. That
    the kernel is one function of the momenta, independent of the side, is
    immediate, since the same <math|F> is used. That it has a strip bound is
    not: the kernel contains the inverses of the two regulated denominators.
    The strip width <math|z> and the bound <math|K> are supplied by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-annulus-common-strip> below. The full-mesh
    Fourier estimate of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-pole-recording> now
    applies to these exact finite identities, giving the displayed error at
    every side, without a subsequence extraction.

    For the third conclusion, apply the all-centered and shell face estimates
    to the same selected local-frame family. The interacting and regulated
    reference paths are the ones formed from <math|F> and the fixed Wilson
    response; the shell comparison uses the same bed and only momenta with
    positive unregulated scale. The resulting two radial estimates are
    uniform in the color and were fixed before choosing the odd side.
    Integrating over the inner ball, the annulus, and the part outside the
    unit ball gives the logarithmic estimate by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:path-radial-log>. The analytic coefficient in
    that integral is <math|\<alpha\><rsub|c><around|(|\<cal-Y\>|)>> for the
    picture defined above: both it and the finite recorded coefficients are
    obtained from the same shifted-fiber kernel, with the factor <math|1/2>
    canceled by the two-leg mixed derivative. This proves that the finite
    recording and the radial drift belong to one response.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The common strip of the regulated kernel>

  Fix <math|L>, put <math|\<mu\>=L<rsup|-1>\<in\><around|(|0,1|]>>, and let
  <math|F=Q+W> be as above, with all three layers holomorphic and periodic on
  a strip of width <math|w\<gtr\>0>. Let the metric strip bound of <math|B>
  hold at width <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0> and put

  <\equation*>
    s=min <around|{|\<sigma\>,w,\<mu\>/128|}>.
  </equation*>

  In the notation of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-annulus-fleet-faces>,
  the vector total film is the film of <math|F> plus
  <math|<math-ss|BB><rsup|<math-up|r>>> plus
  <math|R<rsub|<math-up|vec>,\<mu\>>>, and the ghost total film is
  <math|Q<rsub|<math-up|gh>>+R<rsub|<math-up|gh>,\<mu\>>>. For a film with
  tab <math|a> and slots <math|b,c>, the two-leg figure is

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|fig><rsub|\<xi\>*\<zeta\>><around|(|p,q|)>=<math-up|tr><around*|[|a<around|(|q|)><rsup|-1>*c<rsub|\<xi\>*\<zeta\>>*<around|(|-p,p,q|)>-a<around|(|q|)><rsup|-1>*b<rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|-p,q+p|)>*<space|0.17em>a*<around|(|q+p|)><rsup|-1>*b<rsub|\<zeta\>>*<around|(|p,q|)>|]>,
  </equation*>

  and the response kernel is one half of the figure of the vector total film
  minus the figure of the ghost total film. This is the kernel
  <math|F<rsub|\<xi\>*\<zeta\>>*<around|(|p,q|)>> of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-pole-recording>: by the determinant formula
  <eqref|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY8a>, the conserving mixed
  coefficient of the pole test on the torus of side <math|n<rsub|i>> is the
  site count times the grid mean over <math|q> of this kernel, once the
  tickets of the two total films are known. The tickets of the vector total
  film are those of <math|F>, proved in the first conclusion of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-annulus-same-data>, together with the tickets
  of the fixed films <math|<math-ss|B>>, <math|<math-ss|D><rsub|a>> and of
  their products, rears and inverses. The required fixed-film identities are
  proved below in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:annulus-fixed-film-tickets>.

  The one fact about the selected path that enters is the value of the
  zero-mark layer. By Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-soft-quadratic-hessian>,
  under the path bounds, attachment, the floor at the side <math|n> and the
  sworn condition at the side <math|n>, the Hessian at zero of the quadratic
  part of the finite action of <math|p,R> on the torus of side <math|n> is
  the kinetic form of the bed, <math|<big|sum><rsub|f><around|\<langle\>|<around|(|d<rsub|1>*x|)><rsub|f>,<around|(|M<rsub|n>*d<rsub|1>*y|)><rsub|f>|\<rangle\>>>
  with <math|M<rsub|n>> the metric of <math|B> on that torus. The cubic and
  quartic atom terms of the action vanish to third order at zero and do not
  contribute to the Hessian. In Fourier variables: at every grid momentum
  <math|k> of the torus of side <math|n<rsub|i>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-annulus-zero-layer>a<rsub|F><around|(|k|)>=<math-ss|T><around*|[|P<rsub|1>*<around|(|-k|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*M<around|(|k|)>*P<rsub|1><around|(|k|)>|]>,
  </equation>

  where <math|a<rsub|F>> is the tab of the film of <math|F>,
  <math|M<around|(|k|)>> the metric row of <math|B> and <math|<math-ss|T>>,
  <math|P<rsub|1>> are as in <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F14>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Common strip><label|lem:ex-annulus-common-strip>Under the standing
    assumptions of this section, with <math|L> fixed, there are
    <math|z\<gtr\>0> and <math|K\<geqslant\>0> such that every entry of the
    response kernel is holomorphic and periodic in <math|<around|(|p,q|)>> on
    the strip of width <math|z> and bounded by <math|K> there. The numbers
    <math|z,K> do not depend on the torus side; they may depend on <math|L>,
    on the bed and on the path.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    <em|Step 1: the denominator matching <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F13>
    on the strip of width <math|s>.> Write
    <math|<wide|P|^><rsub|j>=<math-ss|T><around|(|P<rsub|j>|)>> for the
    color-diagonal extensions of the exterior symbols, which act on colored
    forms. The tab of the vector total film is
    <math|a<rsub|F><around|(|k|)>+<wide|P|^><rsub|0><around|(|k|)>*<wide|P|^><rsub|0>*<around|(|-k|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>+s<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|k|)>*I>.
    Indeed tabs multiply under products of films and invert under inversion,
    so the tab of <math|R<rsub|<math-up|gh>,\<mu\>>> is
    <math|s<rsub|\<mu\>>*I> on colors, and the directional sandwiches sum to
    <math|s<rsub|\<mu\>>*I>. This function is holomorphic and periodic on the
    strip of width <math|s>: <math|a<rsub|F>> by the fees, and
    <math|s<rsub|\<mu\>>> because <math|\<omega\><around|(|k|)>+\<mu\><rsup|2>>
    does not vanish on the strip of width <math|\<mu\>/128>. Indeed
    <math|\<omega\><around|(|k|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>><around|(|2-2*cos
    k<rsub|\<nu\>>|)>>, and for <math|k<rsub|\<nu\>>=u+i*\<theta\>> with
    <math|<around|\||\<theta\>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<mu\>/128\<leqslant\>1> one
    has <math|<math-up|Re><around|(|2-2*cos k<rsub|\<nu\>>|)>=2-2*cos u*cosh
    \<theta\>\<geqslant\>-2*<around|(|cosh
    \<theta\>-1|)>\<geqslant\>-2*\<theta\><rsup|2>>, so
    <math|<math-up|Re><around|(|\<omega\><around|(|k|)>+\<mu\><rsup|2>|)>\<geqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|2>-8*<around|(|\<mu\>/128|)><rsup|2>\<gtr\>0>.
    The bed denominator <math|D<rsup|<math-up|bed>><around|(|k|)>> displayed
    in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-annulus-fleet-faces> is holomorphic
    and periodic on the same strip, by the metric strip bound. By
    <eqref|eq:ex-annulus-zero-layer> the two functions agree at every grid
    momentum of every side <math|n<rsub|i>>. Since
    <math|n<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>, these grids are dense in the real
    momentum torus, and both functions are continuous; they agree at all real
    momenta. A holomorphic function on the strip which vanishes at all real
    points vanishes identically, so they agree on the strip. This is
    <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F13>.

    <em|Step 2: invertibility at real momenta.> Let <math|k> be real and
    <math|x> a vector. The floor at every torus side gives, after passing
    from the grids to all real momenta by density and continuity,
    <math|<math-up|Re><around|\<langle\>|v,M<around|(|k|)>*v|\<rangle\>>\<geqslant\>\<epsilon\><around|\||v|\|><rsup|2>>
    for the metric row. With the exterior identity
    <math|<around|\||<wide|P|^><rsub|1><around|(|k|)>*x|\|><rsup|2>+<around|\||<wide|P|^><rsub|0><around|(|k|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*x|\|><rsup|2>=\<omega\><around|(|k|)><around|\||x|\|><rsup|2>>
    for a colored one-form <math|x>, and <math|s<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|k|)>\<geqslant\>0>,

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|Re><around|\<langle\>|x,D<rsup|<math-up|bed>><around|(|k|)>*x|\<rangle\>>\<geqslant\>\<epsilon\>\|<wide|P|^><rsub|1><around|(|k|)>*x\|<rsup|2>+<around|\||<wide|P|^><rsub|0><around|(|k|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*x|\|><rsup|2>+s<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|k|)><around|\||x|\|><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>min
      <around|(|\<epsilon\>,1|)>*<around*|(|\<omega\><around|(|k|)>+s<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|k|)>|)><around|\||x|\|><rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    As shown in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-annulus-fleet-faces>,
    <math|\<omega\>+s<rsub|\<mu\>>\<geqslant\>2<rsup|-10>*<around|(|\<omega\>+\<mu\><rsup|2>|)>\<geqslant\>2<rsup|-10>*\<mu\><rsup|2>\<gtr\>0>.
    Hence the tab of the vector total film is invertible at every real
    momentum. The tab of the ghost total film is the scalar
    <math|\<omega\>+s<rsub|\<mu\>>> on colors and is invertible there as
    well.

    <em|Step 3: a complex neighborhood.> Let <math|a> be either tab. The set
    of points of the strip of width <math|s> at which <math|det a\<ne\>0> is
    open and contains the compact real cell
    <math|<around|[|-\<pi\>,\<pi\>|]><rsup|4>>. It therefore contains all
    <math|k> with <math|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>k\<in\><around|[|-\<pi\>,\<pi\>|]><rsup|4>>
    and <math|<around|\||<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>k<rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>r>
    for some <math|0\<less\>r\<leqslant\>s>, and by periodicity the whole
    closed strip of width <math|r>. Take <math|r> valid for both tabs.

    <em|Step 4: the kernel.> On the strip of width <math|r> the inverse tabs
    are holomorphic and periodic. The figure is a polynomial in the entries
    of <math|a<rsup|-1>>, <math|b> and <math|c> evaluated at <math|q>,
    <math|q+p>, <math|<around|(|-p,q+p|)>>, <math|<around|(|p,q|)>> and
    <math|<around|(|-p,p,q|)>>. If <math|<around|(|p,q|)>> lies in the strip
    of width <math|r/2>, all these arguments and their partial sums lie in
    the strip of width <math|r>, where the three layers of both total films
    are holomorphic and periodic. Hence every entry of the response kernel is
    holomorphic and periodic on the joint strip of width <math|r/2>. Put
    <math|z=r/4>. The closed strip of width <math|z> is, modulo periods, the
    compact set <math|<around|[|-\<pi\>,\<pi\>|]><rsup|8>\<times\><around|[|-z,z|]><rsup|8>>,
    on which the continuous entries are bounded. Let <math|K> be the sum of
    these bounds.
  </proof>

  Nothing in this proof is uniform in <math|L>: the lower bound
  <math|2<rsup|-10>*\<mu\><rsup|2>> degenerates as <math|\<mu\>\<to\>0>, and
  the radius <math|r> comes from a compactness argument. This is why the
  recording strip is chosen after <math|L>, while the radial constants, which
  never use this strip, are chosen before it.

  <subsubsection|The annulus estimate>

  We can now state the output of Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-annulus-fleet-faces>\U<reference|sec:ex-annulus-same-data>
  in the form consumed by the one-step construction of
  Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:path-stationary-chart>, where it is
  denoted <math|<math-up|Ann><around|(|G,\<sigma\>,\<epsilon\>,C,Z,\<tau\>,A|)>>.
  Its remaining response inputs are supplied by the following finite
  coefficient calculation.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Coefficient pictures give priced marked
    responses><label|lem:annulus-priced-responses>Suppose analytic actions
    <math|U<rsub|n>> have <math|D*U<rsub|n><around|(|0|)>=0> and exact
    normalized Taylor coefficient pictures of orders <math|j=1,2,3>, with one
    strip width <math|s\<gtr\>0> and component bound <math|A\<geqslant\>0>.
    There is one marked response with fees
    <math|<around|(|s/25,A*C<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>>, recording the required two-,
    three- and four-slot mixed derivatives at every side for which the input
    records hold. Here <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>> is finite and depends only on
    the fixed gauge-coordinate sets. If the input pictures have ray orders
    <math|q,q-1,q-2>, with nonnegative orders understood as zero when
    necessary, the response layers have these same respective ray orders.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Retain the intermediate mixed pictures in the construction preceding
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-three-level-conversion>; no division
    into spatial response arrays is needed here. At layer <math|r=0,1,2>
    there are <math|r+2> slots, two for the variation and <math|r> for the
    background. For every partition <math|\<pi\>> of these labelled slots
    into <math|b> nonempty blocks, the chain rule for
    <math|D*<around|(|X,Y|)>=log <around|(|exp X*exp Y|)>> contributes

    <\equation*>
      b!<big|sum><rsub|a<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,a<rsub|b>>P<rsub|b-1>*<around*|(|a<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,a<rsub|b>;<with|math-display|false|<around*|\<nobracket\>|<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>\<pi\><rsub|1>>p<rsub|j>,\<ldots\>,<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>\<pi\><rsub|b>>p<rsub|j>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|h=1><rsup|b>c<rsub|\<pi\>,h><around|(|a<rsub|h>|)>.>|\<nobracket\>>
    </equation*>

    The coefficients <math|c<rsub|\<pi\>,h>> are the components of the
    derivatives of <math|D> on that block, in the finite link and color
    bases. The <math|b=1> term is zero by <math|D*U<rsub|n><around|(|0|)>=0>.
    The factor <math|b>! changes the normalized Taylor picture into the raw
    derivative; it is not an additional enumeration of partitions. The
    pointwise analytic map <math|D> is the same at every link and side, so
    these finitely many derivatives through order four have finite bounds
    independent of <math|n>. Let <math|C<rsub|r>> be the sum of the absolute
    multipliers <math|b!<big|prod><rsub|h><around|\||c<rsub|\<pi\>,h><around|(|a<rsub|h>|)>|\|>>
    over partitions and coordinate assignments, enlarged to cover each output
    component, and put <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>=1+C<rsub|0>+C<rsub|1>+C<rsub|2>>.

    The external momenta have one conserving relation. On width
    <math|s/<around|(|r+3|)><rsup|2>>, each momentum sum in a block lies in
    the original width <math|s>: there are at most <math|r+2> slots and the
    eliminated momentum has at most <math|r+1> independent summands. Thus the
    displayed finite sum is holomorphic and periodic on that strip with bound
    <math|A*C<rsub|r>>. The common width <math|s/25> works for all three
    layers. Linear substitution of block momenta preserves ray vanishing. The
    input of index <math|b-1> vanishes to order at least
    <math|q-<around|(|b-2|)>\<geqslant\>q-r>, since <math|b\<leqslant\>r+2>;
    hence the output has the asserted ray order. Multiplication by the local
    coefficients and finite summation preserve it.

    At a mesh momentum the exact input picture contributes zero unless its
    block momenta sum to zero; this is the same total momentum condition as
    for the external slots. In the conserving case its contribution is the
    site count times the displayed coefficient. The ordinary finite chain
    rule therefore identifies the sum with the exact mixed derivative of
    <math|U<rsub|n>\<circ\>D>. This proves all three marked records, with
    precisely one site-count factor. Every operation used the same pictures
    for every side, so the resulting response is side independent.
  </proof>

  Apply the lemma to the Wilson pictures of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-wilson-stream>, taking the sum of their
  three finite bounds and a common positive strip. The Wilson action has zero
  first derivative at the identity. We obtain a response <math|W> with fees
  <math|<around|(|k,E|)>>, <math|k\<gtr\>0>, <math|E\<geqslant\>0>, whose
  records hold at every torus side. This is exactly the grey Wilson response
  used above.

  Next apply it to <math|U<rsub|n<rsub|i>>=I<rsub|n<rsub|i>>-\<cal-W\><rsub|n<rsub|i>>>.
  Under <eqref|eq:ex-annulus-hypotheses>,
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-greyson> supplies its exact pictures,
  one positive strip and finite bound depending only on the displayed scalar
  parameters, and ray orders <math|3,2,1>. The first derivative vanishes by
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-soft-quadratic-hessian> and the Wilson
  identity. The lemma gives a correction response <math|Q> with fees
  <math|<around|(|v,M|)>> and soft orders <math|3,2,1>, recording
  <math|I<rsub|n<rsub|i>>-\<cal-W\><rsub|n<rsub|i>>> on every selected side.
  Its constants are fixed before the bed, path, side sequence and odd block
  side are chosen. Thus both responses required in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-annulus-same-data> have now been constructed.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Exact fixed-film records><label|lem:annulus-fixed-film-tickets>The
    gauge-fixing film, the constant directional films, and their sums,
    products, rears and regulated inverses record their finite-torus matrix
    families and the first two background derivatives at every side.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For a wave <math|h<around|(|x|)>=e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\>x>*v>, the covariant
    difference is

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|D<rsub|exp z>*h|)><around|(|x,a|)>=e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\>x>*<around|{|v-e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>*e<rsup|<math-up|ad><space|0.17em>z*<around|(|x,a|)>>*v|}>.
    </equation*>

    At <math|z=0> its tab is <math|<around|(|1-e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>|)>*I>,
    namely the negative zero-form symbol in the conventions of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-annulus-fleet-faces>. For a marked
    background <math|z*<around|(|x,a|)>=t*<space|0.17em>\<b-1\><rsub|a=b>*e<rsup|i*k\<cdot\>x>*c>,
    the first derivative is the wave of momentum <math|p+k> with coefficient
    <math|-\<b-1\><rsub|a=b>*e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>><math-up|ad><space|0.17em>c>.
    For a second mark <math|s*<space|0.17em>\<b-1\><rsub|a=d>*e<rsup|i*l\<cdot\>x>*f>,
    the mixed second derivative has momentum <math|p+k+l> and coefficient

    <\equation*>
      -<tfrac|1|2>\<b-1\><rsub|a=b>*\<b-1\><rsub|a=d>*e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>*<around|(|<math-up|ad><space|0.17em>c<math-up|ad><space|0.17em>f+<math-up|ad><space|0.17em>f<math-up|ad><space|0.17em>c|)>.
    </equation*>

    These follow directly from the first three terms of the exponential
    series. Complex waves are obtained by complexifying these real
    multilinear identities. They are precisely the tab and marked slots of
    the covariant-difference film as defined there. The direction selectors
    are constant matrices, so their marked slots are zero.

    For clarity write a film as <math|<around|(|A,B<rsub|i>,C<rsub|i*j>|)>>,
    where <math|B<rsub|i>*<around|(|k,p|)>> sends incoming momentum <math|p>
    to <math|p+k> and <math|C<rsub|i*j>*<around|(|k,l,p|)>> sends it to
    <math|p+k+l>. For a second film <math|<around|(|E,H<rsub|i>,J<rsub|i*j>|)>>,
    the product slots are

    <\equation*>
      A*E<around|(|p|)>=A<around|(|p|)>*E<around|(|p|)>,<space|2em><around|(|F*H|)><rsub|i><around|(|k,p|)>=B<rsub|i>*<around|(|k,p|)>*E<around|(|p|)>+A*<around|(|p+k|)>*H<rsub|i>*<around|(|k,p|)>,
    </equation*>

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|F*H|)><rsub|i*j><around|(|k,l,p|)>=>|<cell|C<rsub|i*j>*<around|(|k,l,p|)>*E<around|(|p|)>+B<rsub|i>*<around|(|k,p+l|)>*H<rsub|j>*<around|(|l,p|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|+B<rsub|j>*<around|(|l,p+k|)>*H<rsub|i>*<around|(|k,p|)>+A*<around|(|p+k+l|)>*J<rsub|i*j>*<around|(|k,l,p|)>.>>>>
    </align*>

    They are the ordinary Leibniz rule, with the outgoing momentum of the
    right factor used as the incoming momentum of the left factor. Thus
    finite-torus recording is preserved by products. Sums are immediate.
    Transposing the real finite matrix reverses the wave momenta and
    interchanges its incoming and outgoing indices; applying this to each of
    the three displayed derivatives proves the rear rule.

    If the zero-background matrix is invertible, its inverse is analytic near
    zero, by the finite-dimensional Neumann series. Put
    <math|K<around|(|p|)>=A<around|(|p|)><rsup|-1>>. Differentiating the
    identity <math|F<rsup|-1>*F=I> gives inverse slots

    <\equation*>
      <wide|B|^><rsub|i>*<around|(|k,p|)>=-K*<around|(|p+k|)>*B<rsub|i>*<around|(|k,p|)>*K<around|(|p|)>
    </equation*>

    and

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<wide|C|^><rsub|i*j>*<around|(|k,l,p|)>=>|<cell|K*<around|(|p+k+l|)>*B<rsub|i>*<around|(|k,p+l|)>*K*<around|(|p+l|)>*B<rsub|j>*<around|(|l,p|)>*K<around|(|p|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|+K*<around|(|p+k+l|)>*B<rsub|j>*<around|(|l,p+k|)>*K*<around|(|p+k|)>*B<rsub|i>*<around|(|k,p|)>*K<around|(|p|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|-K*<around|(|p+k+l|)>*C<rsub|i*j>*<around|(|k,l,p|)>*K<around|(|p|)>.>>>>
    </align*>

    These identities prove the inverse record including both orders of the
    cross term. The massive ghost matrix <math|D<rsub|1><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*D<rsub|1>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*I>
    is positive definite for <math|\<mu\>\<gtr\>0>. Hence its inverse, its
    powers, scalar multiples and directional sandwiches have these exact
    records. These are precisely the ghost and vector regulator films
    displayed in the trajectory-price paragraph. The positive regulated
    floors similarly justify every further inverse used for the determinant
    coefficients. All identities are finite-dimensional and hold at each
    torus size; no limit or uniform inverse bound is needed for their
    exactness.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The annulus estimate><label|prop:ex-annulus-estimate>Let <math|G>
    be simple, let <math|\<sigma\>,\<epsilon\>,\<tau\>\<gtr\>0> and
    <math|C,Z,A\<geqslant\>0>, and use the Wilson and correction responses
    and the fixed-film identities just constructed. There is
    <math|D<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0> such that the following holds for every odd
    integer <math|L\<geqslant\>1>, every sequence of torus sides
    <math|n<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>, every bed <math|B>, clip <math|p>, path
    <math|R> and number <math|\<Lambda\>> such that

    <\gather>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|R.<math-up|Bounds><around|(|p,\<tau\>,A,\<Lambda\>|)>,<space|2em><math-up|Attached><around|(|B,R|)>,<space|2em>B.*<math-up|metric.strip><around|(|\<sigma\>,C|)>,<space|2em>p.<math-up|direct>\<leqslant\>Z,>>|<row|<cell|<math-up|Floor><around|(|B,p,n,\<epsilon\>,C|)><text|for
      every torus side >n,<space|2em><math-up|Match><around|(|B,n<rsub|i>,G|)><text|for
      every >i.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-annulus-hypotheses>>>>>
    </gather>

    There are a two-leg picture <math|\<cal-Y\>>, numbers <math|z\<gtr\>0>,
    <math|K\<geqslant\>0> and a sequence <math|e<rsub|i>\<to\>0> such that

    <\enumerate>
      <item>every component of <math|\<cal-Y\>> is holomorphic and periodic
      on the strip of width <math|z/2> and bounded by <math|K> there;

      <item>for every <math|i>, <math|\<cal-Y\>> records the pole test
      <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|n<rsub|i>>> of <math|p,R> with regulator
      <math|L<rsup|-1>> on the torus of side <math|n<rsub|i>>, in the
      normalized sense <eqref|eq:output-record> with <math|n=2>, with error
      <math|n<rsub|i><rsup|4>*e<rsub|i>>; here
      <math|e<rsub|i>=\<frak-r\><around|(|z,K|)>*e<rsup|-z*n<rsub|i>/4>>;

      <item><math|<around|\||\<alpha\><rsub|c><around|(|\<cal-Y\>|)>+\<lambda\><rsub|G>*log
      L|\|>\<leqslant\>D<rsub|0>> for every color <math|c>.
    </enumerate>

    The constant <math|D<rsub|0>> depends only on
    <math|G,\<sigma\>,\<epsilon\>,C,Z,\<tau\>,A>; the picture
    <math|\<cal-Y\>> and the numbers <math|z,K> may depend on all the data,
    including <math|L>. No limit in <math|i> is part of the assertion.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Take <math|W,k,E> and <math|v,M> from the two applications of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:annulus-priced-responses>; put <math|w=min
    <around|(|k,v|)>>, so that <math|W> has fees <math|<around|(|w,E|)>>.
    Apply the conclusion <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F11>
    of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-annulus-fleet-faces> with vacuum
    response <math|V=W>, fee width <math|\<eta\>=w>, floor constant
    <math|\<epsilon\>>, bed bound <math|D=C> and correction fee <math|A=M>.
    It gives <math|a,b\<geqslant\>0>, and we put
    <math|D<rsub|0>=c<rsub|<math-up|wt>>*<around|[|a*<around|(|\<pi\><rsup|2>/2+<around|\||E<rsub|4>|\|>|)>+3*\<pi\><rsup|2>*b|]>>.
    All of this precedes <math|L> and the path data.

    Now let <math|L> and the data <eqref|eq:ex-annulus-hypotheses> be given,
    let <math|Q> be the correction response just constructed, and put
    <math|F=Q+W>, <math|\<mu\>=L<rsup|-1>> and <math|s=min
    <around|{|\<sigma\>,w,\<mu\>/128|}>>. The first conclusion of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-annulus-same-data> gives the tickets of
    <math|F>, and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-annulus-common-strip> gives
    <math|z,K>. Let <math|\<cal-Y\>> be the picture associated with the
    response kernel. The second conclusion of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-annulus-same-data>, that is
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-pole-recording> in its normalized form
    <eqref|eq:annulus-recording-yield-display-RY4-prime>, gives the first two
    assertions; <math|e<rsub|i>\<to\>0> because <math|z\<gtr\>0> and
    <math|n<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>.

    For the third, observe that <math|<around|(|s,\<mu\>,c,B,p,Q|)>> is a
    local record in the sense of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-annulus-fleet-faces>,
    for every color <math|c>: <math|Q> has fees <math|<around|(|w,M|)>> and
    the required soft orders; <math|0\<less\>\<mu\>\<leqslant\>1> and
    <math|0\<less\>s\<leqslant\>\<mu\>/128>; the layers of <math|F=Q+W> are
    holomorphic on the strip of width <math|s\<leqslant\>w> and the metric
    bound holds at width <math|s\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>>; the denominator
    matching on the strip of width <math|s> is Step<nbsp>1 of the preceding
    proof; and the floors hold at every side. This is the same response
    <math|F> and the same strip <math|s> as in the recording. Hence
    <eqref|eq:annulus-fleet-face-estimates-display-F11> holds for its
    response coefficient with the constants <math|a,b> chosen above. The
    coefficient identity <eqref|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT1>
    applies to <math|\<cal-Y\>> because the kernel has the strip bound of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-annulus-common-strip>, and the radial
    integration in the proof of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-all-period-recording>
    gives the third assertion with <math|log \<mu\><rsup|-1>=log L>.
  </proof>

  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-annulus-estimate> is
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-all-period-recording> with its hypotheses on
  the finite-layer realization, the common strip and the two-wave bounds
  discharged. Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:annulus-priced-responses> and
  <reference|lem:annulus-fixed-film-tickets> supply the remaining response
  and finite-film records; Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-soft-quadratic-hessian>
  supplies the quadratic coefficient under the stated path hypotheses.

  <subparagraph|Selected construction of the common data.> In the selected
  construction, the baseline <math|W> is the fee-bounded Wilson response. The
  correction <math|Q> is the response produced from the attached path and
  bed. Its finite tickets are the difference between the intended action and
  the Wilson action. Combining them before evaluating any side gives
  <math|F=Q+W>. The fee, ticket, floor, and metric conditions used above are
  all on this combined response and the original selected bed.

  The local-frame bounds are produced from that same Wilson reference: the
  finite-volume vector and ghost responses, together with their one-leg and
  two-leg extensions, are obtained from one family of local frames. The
  all-centered estimate is applied at scale <math|<around|\||x|\|>+\<mu\>>;
  the shell estimate is applied to the restriction
  <math|\<mu\>\<leqslant\><around|\||x|\|>>, where the unregulated reference
  inverse exists. The two applications give the upper face and the lower
  face-minus-halo price, respectively. These are precisely the hypotheses
  used in the preceding face calculation; no change of bed, reference, or
  finite-volume sequence is made between the face estimates and the ticket
  identity.

  In summary, the proof of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-annulus-estimate>
  composes the Wilson baseline <math|W>, the path correction <math|Q>, the
  addition <math|F=Q+W> of their tickets, the finite determinant recording of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-pole-recording>, and the uniform radial
  evaluation of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-all-period-recording>.

  <subsection|Symmetries of the selected stationary
  germs><label|sec:stationary-germ-symmetry>

  The Taylor construction of the output action uses the symmetries of three
  finite-volume functions: the classical stationary value, the completed
  determinant correction, and the inherited loop evaluated on the stationary
  fine field. We prove these symmetries for the selected stationary branch.
  In particular the determinant argument includes the moving frame and its
  regularization.

  Fix a block step, its spanning forest, a positive regulator <math|\<mu\>>,
  and the analytic stationary branch <math|U<around|(|V|)>> in the original
  rooted tree chart. The price gap is strictly positive. The standing
  hypotheses, which are inputs to this section, are precisely the following:
  the patch satisfies the branch compatibility of the kernel; the incoming
  path satisfies its path bounds in the incoming clip; the branch vanishes at
  zero, is analytic, and is stationary near the origin for the tested action
  of <em|every> choice of block representatives, not only of one; the Hessian
  of every tested action in the tree-chart variables at the origin has one
  positive lower bound; <math|\<mu\>\<gtr\>0>; and a local Haar chart with
  its density is fixed. Write <math|t> for a choice of block representatives.
  For a coarse field <math|V> and a joint fine field and frame <math|U>, let
  <math|S<rsub|t>*<around|(|V,U|)>> be the classical action with the blocking
  penalty. Let <math|H<rsub|t>*<around|(|V,U|)>> be its Hessian in the joint
  left coordinates, and let <math|F<rsub|t><around|(|U|)>> be the completed
  gauge-fixing derivative. In the notation of the determinant construction,
  put

  <\equation*>
    M<rsub|t>*<around|(|V,U|)>=H<rsub|t>*<around|(|V,U|)>+F<rsub|t><around|(|U|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*F<rsub|t><around|(|U|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-B\><rsub|t>*<around|(|V,U|)>=<tfrac|1|2>log
    det M<rsub|t>*<around|(|V,U|)>-log det \<Gamma\><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<Gamma\><around|(|U|)>=d<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>*d<rsub|U>+C<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|U|)>>
  is the same scalar gauge-orbit operator, with
  <math|C<rsub|\<mu\>>=\<mu\><rsup|2>*P<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|9>>. All matrices
  in this section are real. The positive price gap and positive regulator
  make the relevant determinants positive near the stationary origin, so
  these expressions define analytic real germs there.

  <subsubsection|Transport of the completed determinant>

  The two determinant identities below are stated at base points
  <math|<around|(|V,U|)>> satisfying the following side conditions, for
  representatives <math|t>.

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(a)><em|Frame regularity.> The block frames of the incremented
    joint field <math|exp <around|(|X|)>*U> are real-analytic functions of
    the increment <math|X> at <math|X=0>.

    <item*|(b)><em|Analyticity.> The action
    <math|X\<mapsto\>S<rsub|t>*<around|(|V,exp <around|(|X|)>*U|)>> is
    real-analytic at <math|X=0>.
  </enumerate>

  For the signed identity we also use

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(c)><em|Stationarity.> The first derivative of
    <math|X\<mapsto\>S<rsub|t>*<around|(|V,exp <around|(|X|)>*U|)>> vanishes
    at <math|X=0>.
  </enumerate>

  The precise requirements are stated with each identity;
  <math|\<mu\>\<gtr\>0> throughout. All three conditions hold at every point
  of the selected stationary branch near the trivial joint field. Condition
  (a) holds on a neighbourhood of the trivial field, because the frame
  logarithm and the nearest-group map are analytic near the identity.
  Condition (b) holds there because the action is a finite composition of
  analytic maps. Condition (c) is the stationarity of the branch, assumed
  above for every choice of representatives. The transformations considered
  below fix the trivial field and map the stationary equation to itself, as
  shown in the next subsection. These identities are used only along the
  branch. We do not claim the signed identity without condition (c).

  A fine gauge transformation <math|k> and coarse gauge transformation
  <math|u> act on the joint field and frame. Their tangent maps in joint,
  fine-link and scalar coordinates are orthogonal: they are products of
  adjoint colour rotations and coordinate permutations. Denote the joint
  tangent map by <math|T> and the scalar map by <math|A>. Equivariance of the
  block ribbon and of its frame logarithm gives

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|t><around|(|U<rprime|'>|)>*T=A*F<rsub|t><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  We verify why this identity includes both parts of the gauge fixing. For
  its frame part, the logarithm near the identity obeys <math|log
  <around|(|k*h*k<rsup|-1>|)>=<math-up|Ad><rsub|k><space|0.17em>log h>;
  differentiating the block-frame identity gives the intertwining of its
  derivative. For its link part, the covariant difference intertwines the
  scalar and link adjoint maps, and transposition gives the corresponding
  identity for the adjoint difference. The scalar resolvent and hence its
  ninth power intertwine because multiplication and inversion preserve an
  intertwining relation. These are the two summands of <math|F<rsub|t>>.

  The classical action is gauge invariant. In left increment coordinates the
  transformed action composed with <math|T> equals the original action near
  the increment origin. Twice differentiating this identity yields

  <\equation*>
    T<rsup|\<ast\>>*H<rsub|t>*<around|(|V<rprime|'>,U<rprime|'>|)>*T=H<rsub|t>*<around|(|V,U|)>.
  </equation*>

  There is no derivative of <math|T> in this calculation: the base fields are
  fixed while differentiating the increment variable. Combining this
  congruence with the gauge-fixing identity and <math|A<rsup|\<ast\>>*A=I>
  gives

  <\equation*>
    T<rsup|\<ast\>>*M<rsub|t>*<around|(|V<rprime|'>,U<rprime|'>|)>*T=M<rsub|t>*<around|(|V,U|)>.
  </equation*>

  Similarly <math|\<Gamma\><around|(|U<rprime|'>|)>> is orthogonally
  conjugate to <math|\<Gamma\><around|(|U|)>>. Taking determinants and using
  <math|<around|(|det T|)><rsup|2>=<around|(|det A|)><rsup|2>=1> proves, when
  <math|<around|(|V,U|)>> and <math|<around|(|V<rprime|'>,U<rprime|'>|)>>
  satisfy (a) and <math|<around|(|V<rprime|'>,U<rprime|'>|)>> satisfies (b),

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:stationary-germ-gauge-det>\<cal-B\><rsub|t>*<around|(|V<rprime|'>,U<rprime|'>|)>=\<cal-B\><rsub|t>*<around|(|V,U|)>.
  </equation>

  The same transformation preserves <math|S<rsub|t>> and the inherited loop.

  Signed lattice transformations require a slightly different tangent map.
  For a transformed link <math|e>, let <math|e<rprime|'>> be the original
  link it reads. If its orientation is preserved, the tangent reads
  <math|X<rsub|e<rprime|'>>>; if reversed, it reads
  <math|-<math-up|Ad><rsub|U<rsub|e<rprime|'>><rsup|-1>><space|0.17em>X<rsub|e<rprime|'>>>.
  This map is orthogonal and satisfies the exact increment identity

  <\equation*>
    s<rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|exp <around|(|X|)>*U|)>=exp
    <around|(|T<rsub|U>*X|)>*<space|0.17em>s<rsup|\<ast\>>*U.
  </equation*>

  The scalar tangent is the coordinate permutation; the joint tangent also
  includes the corresponding frame permutation. The block representative
  changes from <math|t> to the transported choice <math|r>. Equivariance of
  the ribbon, frame logarithm and covariant difference therefore gives, with
  these tangent maps,

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|t>*<around|(|s<rsup|\<ast\>>*U|)>*T<rsub|U>=A*F<rsub|r><around|(|U|)>,<space|2em>T<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>*H<rsub|t>*<around|(|s<rsup|\<ast\>>*V,s<rsup|\<ast\>>*U|)>*T<rsub|U>=H<rsub|r>*<around|(|V,U|)>.
  </equation*>

  For the penalty term this uses invariance of the group price under
  inversion and conjugation: reversing both links in its relative product
  leaves its price unchanged. The same resolvent argument handles
  <math|C<rsub|\<mu\>>>. Taking determinants as above gives, when
  <math|<around|(|V,U|)>> satisfies (a) for <math|r> and
  <math|<around|(|s<rsup|\<ast\>>*V,s<rsup|\<ast\>>*U|)>> satisfies (a)\U(c)
  for <math|t>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:stationary-germ-signed-det>\<cal-B\><rsub|t>*<around|(|s<rsup|\<ast\>>*V,s<rsup|\<ast\>>*U|)>=\<cal-B\><rsub|r>*<around|(|V,U|)>.
  </equation>

  This is the completed moving determinant. It does not assert full signed
  covariance of the separate auxiliary fine-lattice pole symbol.

  <subsubsection|Returning a moved solution to the original chart>

  A transformed stationary solution need not lie in the original tree chart.
  Gauge it back using the tree gauge map. This map is analytic near the
  trivial joint field, sends that field to itself, and leaves the coarse
  input fixed when the coarse gauge transformation is the identity. The
  transformed stationarity equation is still zero: under the invertible
  tangent map its first derivative is the original zero first derivative. The
  local uniqueness in the original chart, proved by the stationary
  implicit-function construction, now identifies the regauged solution with
  the selected solution at the transformed coarse input. We use uniqueness
  only in that chart.

  The classical value, the determinant correction by
  <eqref|eq:stationary-germ-gauge-det>, and the inherited loop are unchanged
  by this regauging. Consequently their values along any analytic stationary
  probe through the trivial field equal their values on the selected branch
  at that probe's coarse input. The argument also applies to
  parameter-dependent gauge transformations: freeze the parameter when
  comparing the action and matrices, and then use analyticity and the local
  branch uniqueness on a common neighborhood.

  We must also remove the changed representative <math|r> in
  <eqref|eq:stationary-germ-signed-det>. The tested classical actions for
  <math|t> and <math|r> agree as germs in both the coarse and tree-chart
  variables, by the local branch construction of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:stationary-action-prices>. Therefore their
  stationary values and their tree-chart Hessians agree. The moving Haar
  identity of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-thermal-contact-cancellation>
  expresses each thermal value as that tree Hessian determinant with the same
  Haar factor, or equivalently as the same inherited loop plus
  <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|t>> or <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|r>>. Equality of the tree
  Hessians and cancellation of the common inherited loop give equality of
  <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|t>> and <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|r>> along the selected
  branch. Thus both tested functions are independent of the representative as
  germs. This uses equality on a neighborhood of the moving stationary point,
  which follows from equality of the joint germs; equality at the stationary
  value alone would not identify their Hessians.

  <subsubsection|The identities used by the Taylor coefficients>

  Apply the preceding argument first to a signed lattice transformation, then
  to constant colour conjugation, and finally to a small variable coarse
  gauge transformation. For the last operation the transformed coarse
  logarithm is its actual nonlinear gauge action; transform the joint frame
  accordingly and gauge the resulting stationary solution back to the
  original chart. Each of the three analytic stationary functions is
  unchanged as a germ under all these operations. The inherited loop has the
  same invariances directly from its gauge invariant atoms and finite signed
  average.

  In summary, for every choice <math|t> of block representatives each of the
  three functions of the coarse link field, the classical stationary value
  <math|E<rsub|t>>, the completed determinant correction
  <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|t>> along the branch, and the inherited loop along the
  branch, is real analytic at zero and is unchanged, as a germ at zero, by
  every signed lattice transformation, by every constant colour conjugation,
  and by the nonlinear action of small coarse gauge transformations.
  Moreover, for two choices <math|t,r> of representatives the germs of
  <math|E<rsub|t>> and <math|E<rsub|r>> coincide, and so do those of
  <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|t>> and <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|r>> along the branch. All
  statements are for one forest. The comparison of the stationary data of two
  different forests is a separate statement, which is not proved in this
  section.

  Differentiating these germ identities supplies the signed and colour
  symmetries and the nonlinear gauge identities of the finite-volume Taylor
  coefficients in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:stationary-output-construction>.
  When the lower positive-degree coefficients vanish, the differentiated
  nonlinear gauge identity reduces to its leading linear Ward identity, as
  proved there. All three functions here use the same stationary branch,
  regulator and finite torus; no symmetry claim is imported from a separately
  chosen stationary solution.

  <subsection|Constructing the stationary output
  action><label|sec:stationary-output-construction>

  We now apply the curvature lift to the Taylor coefficients of the
  stationary action. The construction has two separate parts. Cubic and
  quartic atoms match the classical action through degree four. The quadratic
  response determines one scalar change in the kinetic coefficient and an
  invariant loop remainder with vanishing moments. Both parts use the same
  coarse bed and the same sequence of tori.

  <subsubsection|Recovering Ward data from finite-volume coefficients>

  For an analytic real function <math|F> of the link coordinates, write
  <math|C<rsub|n><around|(|F|)>=D<rsup|n>*F<around|(|0|)>/n>!, extended
  complex multilinearly to complex link fields. A unit Fourier wave has a
  specified momentum, coordinate direction, and colour basis vector. A common
  coefficient picture <math|P> for functions <math|F<rsub|k>> on tori
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|k>> means that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:output-record><around*|\||C<rsub|n><around|(|F<rsub|k>|)><around|[|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|n>|]>-\<b-1\><rsub|<big|sum>p<rsub|j>=0><space|0.17em><around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|k>|\|>*<space|0.17em>P*<around|(|p<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,p<rsub|n-1>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|k>|\|>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|k>
  </equation>

  for every such tuple of unit waves; the zeroth momentum in the picture is
  minus the sum of the other momenta. Direction and colour indices in
  <math|P> are understood. We assume a common positive strip width and finite
  component bound, and <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|k>\<to\>0>. The side lengths
  tend to infinity. Exact recording is the case
  <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|k>=0>.

  Suppose <math|F<rsub|k>> is invariant, as a germ at the identity, under
  gauge transformations and under simultaneous adjoint colour rotations.
  Assume its positive-degree derivatives below degree <math|n> vanish.
  Differentiate the gauge-invariance identity once in the gauge parameter and
  <math|n-1> times in the link variables. The term where all link derivatives
  fall on <math|F<rsub|k>> gives its <math|n>th derivative with one argument
  equal to the linear gauge variation <math|d<rsub|0>*h>. Every other term
  contains a positive-degree derivative of <math|F<rsub|k>> of degree less
  than <math|n> and is zero. Consequently

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|n><around|(|F<rsub|k>|)><around|[|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,d<rsub|0>*h,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|n>|]>=0.
  </equation*>

  On Fourier waves this is exactly the contraction by
  <math|q<around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>> in factor <math|j>. It holds in every factor
  by symmetry of the derivative. This is also the leading case of the finite
  gauge-variation identity in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-soft-curvature-receipts>.

  Approximate any tuple of real independent momenta by finite-torus
  frequencies and choose the zeroth frequency to make their sum zero.
  Representatives may differ by <math|2*\<pi\>> multiples; both the picture
  and the symbols <math|q> are periodic. Divide <eqref|eq:output-record> by
  <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|k>|\|>>. The error tends to zero, while
  continuity of the common picture and of <math|q> permits passage to the
  limit in the finite contraction sum. Thus the picture has all
  continuous-momentum Ward identities. Conjugating real Taylor coefficients
  and reversing every wave gives <math|<wide|P*<around|(|-p|)>|\<bar\>>=P<around|(|p|)>>
  by the same limit. Differentiating the constant colour-rotation invariance
  and passing to the limit makes the real and imaginary colour tensors
  invariant. These are precisely the hypotheses of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:multileg-curvature-lift>.

  For <math|n=2>, only the first derivative of <math|F<rsub|k>> needs to
  vanish. This follows from colour invariance when the gauge Lie algebra is
  simple: an invariant linear functional kills every bracket, and the
  brackets span the algebra. The constant value of <math|F<rsub|k>> need not
  be zero.

  <subsubsection|Matching a leading Taylor coefficient>

  Suppose <math|n\<in\><around|{|3,4|}>>, the two cases used below, each
  <math|F<rsub|k>> is invariant under signed coordinate permutations as a
  germ, <math|F<rsub|k>> vanishes to order <math|n>, and <math|P> records its
  <math|n>th coefficient exactly, at strip cost <math|A>. The curvature lift
  produces invariant real atoms of degree <math|n> with total exponential
  budget <math|K*A>, at a positive width chosen before <math|A> and <math|P>.
  Let <math|S> be their translation sum and then their average over the
  finite group of signed coordinate permutations.

  Here is the normalization check. Each transported plaquette slot has zero
  constant term and linear term equal to its discrete curl. The leading
  coefficient of the product of <math|n> slots is therefore the product of
  these curls. The normalized coefficient <math|C<rsub|n><around|(|S|)>> is
  the symmetrization of that ordered product, namely the average over the
  <math|n>! permutations of its arguments. On unit Fourier waves, summing the
  anchor over the torus gives zero unless the momenta sum to zero, and gives
  <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|k>|\|>> otherwise. The remaining
  absolutely convergent displacement sum is exactly the curvature symbol
  contracted in every factor. It is <math|P> by the lifting identity. Since
  <math|C<rsub|n><around|(|F<rsub|k>|)>> is symmetric, symmetrizing this
  identity retains it. Averaging orientations also retains it, because
  <math|F<rsub|k>> is invariant under those orientations. Reflections may
  multiply a wave by a unit-modulus edge phase; these phases are included
  when applying the same identity to the transformed wave. Thus
  <math|C<rsub|n><around|(|S|)>=C<rsub|n><around|(|F<rsub|k>|)>> on every
  tuple of unit Fourier waves.

  These waves span the complexified link space, by finite Fourier inversion.
  Multilinearity gives equality on arbitrary arguments and hence on real
  fields. Both functions are analytic, and each atom vanishes to order
  <math|n>; the exponential budget permits termwise differentiation on a
  common complex link ball as in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-atom-stream-picture>.
  It follows that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:output-leading-removal>F<rsub|k>-S=O<around|(|<around|\||x|\|><rsup|n+1>|)>
  </equation>

  along every real ray. In the approximate version of
  <eqref|eq:output-record>, permutation and orientation averages are convex
  averages, so the same argument retains the coefficient error
  <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|k>|\|>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|k>> on each
  tuple of unit waves. It does not insert a factorial or a further volume
  factor.

  <subsubsection|The cubic and quartic construction>

  Fix the outgoing clip <math|q>, with direct coefficient
  <math|q<rsub|<math-up|direct>>\<leqslant\>Z>, and the coarse bed <math|C>,
  with strip bound <math|<around|(|w,M<rsub|H>|)>> for its metric and root
  rows, with its floor in the clip <math|q> at every torus side, and with the
  sworn condition at every coarse side of the sequence. Let <math|E<rsub|k>>
  be the classical stationary action in the chosen chart, that is, the tested
  action of the incoming clip and path along the stationary branch. Let
  <math|R<rsub|C>> be the path whose row family is the root row of <math|C>
  and whose loop, cubic and quartic families are zero, and let
  <math|J<rsub|k>> be the complete quadratic action of <math|q,R<rsub|C>> on
  the <math|k>th coarse torus: the average over signed lattice
  transformations <math|s> of

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|f><around*|[|q<rsub|<math-up|direct>>*<space|0.17em>\<varpi\><around*|(|<math-up|hol><rsub|f><around|(|s<rsup|\<ast\>>*exp
    x|)>|)>+<tfrac|1|2><around*|\||\<ell\><rsub|R<rsub|C>>*<around|(|s<rsup|\<ast\>>*exp
    x;f|)>|\|><rsup|2>|]>,
  </equation*>

  the sum being over plaquettes, with <math|\<varpi\>> the Wilson plaquette
  price and <math|\<ell\><rsub|R<rsub|C>>> the row field. It contains the
  direct Wilson term of the clip as well as the squared row term. Both
  <math|E<rsub|k>> and <math|J<rsub|k>> are analytic, gauge invariant, and
  invariant under signed coordinate permutations. Their values and first
  derivatives at zero are zero. Their Hessians at zero agree, and equal the
  coarse Gaussian form <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|C>*<around|(|x,y|)>=<big|sum><rsub|f><around|\<langle\>|<around|(|d<rsub|1>*x|)><rsub|f>,<around|(|M<rsub|C>*d<rsub|1>*y|)><rsub|f>|\<rangle\>>>
  of the metric of <math|C>. For <math|J<rsub|k>> this is
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-soft-quadratic-hessian>, applied to
  <math|R<rsub|C>> at the coarse side; <math|R<rsub|C>> is attached to
  <math|C> by construction and satisfies the path bounds by the root strip
  bound, and the lemma uses the floor and the sworn condition of <math|C> at
  that side. For <math|E<rsub|k>> it is the reduced Hessian (heat) identity
  of the stationary branch for this same bed <math|C>,

  <\equation*>
    D<rsup|2>*E<rsub|k><around|(|0|)>=\<cal-K\><rsub|C>,
  </equation*>

  which is one of the chart receipts treated in
  Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:path-stationary-chart>. It is an input
  here, as is the Hessian gap <math|\<eta\>> common to all <math|k> in
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:stationary-output-path>: it holds for the
  coarse bed matched to the fine bed and the installed tower by the two-scale
  identities, not for an arbitrarily chosen coarse bed. Thus

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|k>=E<rsub|k>-J<rsub|k>
  </equation*>

  has order three. Suppose <math|E<rsub|k>> has exact common coefficient
  pictures of degrees three and four with component bound
  <math|M\<geqslant\>1> at width <math|\<sigma\>>. The row estimate of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-row-quadratic-price> for the squared
  row term, and the Wilson pictures of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-wilson-stream>
  multiplied by <math|q<rsub|<math-up|direct>>\<leqslant\>Z> for the direct
  term, supply such pictures for <math|J<rsub|k>> at positive widths and
  fixed bounds <math|P<rsub|3>,P<rsub|4>>, which depend on
  <math|w,M<rsub|H>,Z>. Choose the minimum of the three widths. Both residual
  pictures then have cost at most

  <\equation*>
    M*<around|(|1+P<rsub|3>+P<rsub|4>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Apply <eqref|eq:output-leading-removal> at <math|n=3> to obtain an
  invariant cubic atom action <math|S<rsub|3>> of budget <math|K<rsub|3>*M>
  and with <math|F<rsub|k>-S<rsub|3>> of order four.

  Its fourth coefficient must also be subtracted. In general an atom of
  degree three has nonzero higher Taylor terms. The uniform atom-picture
  estimate gives an exact common degree-four picture for <math|S<rsub|3>>, of
  cost at most <math|K<rsub|3>*M*B<rsub|4>> at a positive width. Subtract it
  from the degree-four picture of <math|F<rsub|k>>, and take the minimum
  width. The resulting cost is at most a fixed multiple of <math|M>. Apply
  the leading-removal construction at <math|n=4> to
  <math|F<rsub|k>-S<rsub|3>>. This produces an invariant quartic action
  <math|S<rsub|4>>, again with budget bounded by a fixed multiple of
  <math|M>, and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:output-classical-five>E<rsub|k>-J<rsub|k>-S<rsub|3>-S<rsub|4>=O<around|(|<around|\||x|\|><rsup|5>|)>.
  </equation>

  The two atom families and their common positive width are chosen
  independently of <math|k>. They consequently match the action on every
  torus in the sequence. Taylor expansion on rays followed by polarization
  proves equality of the full derivatives of orders zero through four. The
  chart's tag-independence is an equality of germs, so the same derivatives
  agree for every allowed choice of tag, without choosing a different path
  for each tag.

  <subsubsection|Removing the unique quadratic kinetic coefficient>

  We need one further consequence of the Ward and lattice symmetries. Let
  <math|f<rsub|a*b><rsup|u*v><around|(|p|)>> be an analytic quadratic
  coefficient picture, where <math|a,b> are coordinate indices and <math|u,v>
  colour indices, with momenta <math|<around|(|-p,p|)>>. Suppose it comes
  from the common recording of analytic, gauge-invariant,
  signed-coordinate-invariant real functions as above. It then has the
  exchange symmetry

  <\equation*>
    f<rsub|a*b><rsup|u*v><around|(|p|)>=f<rsub|b*a><rsup|v*u>*<around|(|-p|)>.
  </equation*>

  The leading homogeneous terms transform as ordinary coordinate tensors
  under signed permutations: the reflected-edge phases have constant term
  equal to the relevant coordinate sign. Once all lower homogeneous terms
  vanish, the higher terms of these phases do not contribute at the order
  being considered.

  First <math|f<around|(|0|)>=0>. Indeed the first-order part of the Ward
  identity for a constant tensor is <math|<big|sum><rsub|b>p<rsub|b>*f<rsub|a*b><rsup|u*v><around|(|0|)>=0>
  for all <math|p>, and coordinate vectors detect every entry. For its linear
  term write <math|K<rsub|a*b><rsup|u*v><around|(|p|)>>. The left and right
  Ward identities give

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|b>p<rsub|b>*K<rsub|a*b><rsup|u*v><around|(|p|)>=0,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|a>p<rsub|a>*K<rsub|a*b><rsup|u*v><around|(|p|)>=0.
  </equation*>

  They imply <math|K<rsub|a*b><rsup|u*v><around|(|e<rsub|b>|)>=K<rsub|a*b><rsup|u*v><around|(|e<rsub|a>|)>=0>
  and <math|K<rsub|a*b><rsup|u*v><around|(|e<rsub|j>|)>+K<rsub|a*j><rsup|u*v><around|(|e<rsub|b>|)>=0>.
  If <math|a,b,j> are distinct, the coordinate permutation exchanging
  <math|b> and <math|j> identifies the two terms, so both vanish. When
  indices coincide the preceding two identities already imply the same
  conclusion. The linear term is zero and <math|f> has order two.

  Let <math|Q<rsub|a*b><rsup|u*v><around|(|p|)>> be its quadratic homogeneous
  term. Fix <math|a\<ne\>b> and one unit colour vector <math|e<rsub|u>>. We
  claim that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:output-one-test>Q<rsub|a*a><rsup|u*u><around|(|e<rsub|b>|)>=0
  </equation>

  forces <math|Q=0>. At fixed <math|a,p>, the colour form
  <math|Q<rsub|a*a><rsup|u*v><around|(|p|)>> is real, symmetric, and adjoint
  invariant. Reality follows from momentum reversal and even homogeneity;
  symmetry follows from the exchange identity. Such a form is a scalar
  multiple of the invariant inner product. Indeed its self-adjoint
  representing operator commutes with every adjoint operator; each eigenspace
  is an ideal, so simplicity leaves only one eigenvalue. Therefore
  <eqref|eq:output-one-test> kills that whole colour form. Coordinate
  permutations transfer the conclusion to
  <math|Q<rsub|x*x><rsup|u*v><around|(|e<rsub|y>|)>> for all <math|x\<ne\>y>.
  The case <math|x=y> follows directly from the leading Ward identity.

  Polarize <math|Q>, writing <math|T<rsub|x*y><rsup|u*v>*<around|(|e<rsub|k>,e<rsub|l>|)>>
  for its symmetric bilinear coefficient in momentum. Reflection in
  coordinate <math|r> multiplies this entry by
  <math|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|\<b-1\><rsub|r=k>+\<b-1\><rsub|r=l>>> on the
  momentum side and by <math|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|\<b-1\><rsub|r=x>+\<b-1\><rsub|r=y>>>
  on the tensor side. Unless these parities agree for every <math|r>, the
  entry is zero. For <math|x=y> this eliminates every mixed momentum
  coefficient; the diagonal coefficients were just shown to vanish. For
  <math|x\<ne\>y> it leaves only the mixed pair
  <math|<around|{|k,l|}>=<around|{|x,y|}>>. Finally insert
  <math|p=e<rsub|x>+e<rsub|y>> in the Ward identity with first tensor index
  <math|x>. All other entries have vanished, so it kills this last mixed
  coefficient as well. Thus <math|Q=0> in every colour component.

  The picture now has order three. Reversal of all coordinate signs acts by
  <math|+1> on its two tensor indices but by <math|-1> on a homogeneous cubic
  polynomial. Its cubic term is therefore zero. We have proved that the
  single test <eqref|eq:output-one-test> gives order four.

  Let <math|H<around|(|p|)>> be the coarse metric row, with
  <math|H<around|(|0|)>=I>, and let <math|b<rsub|H>> be the exactly recorded
  quadratic picture of its quadratic path action. Its quadratic homogeneous
  term is

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|b<rsub|H>|)><rsub|a*b><rsup|u*v,<around|[|2|]>><around|(|p|)>=<tfrac|1|2><around*|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>*\<delta\><rsub|a*b>-p<rsub|a>*p<rsub|b>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|u*v>.
  </equation*>

  This follows by replacing both discrete curls by their linear terms
  <math|i*p\<wedge\>> and using <math|H<around|(|0|)>=I>; the action has
  coefficient <math|1/2> and its two waves have opposite momenta. In
  particular the transverse unit test is <math|1/2>. Fixing distinct axes
  <math|0,1> and any unit colour basis vector <math|e<rsub|u>>, define

  <\equation*>
    \<alpha\>=2<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>f<rsub|11><rsup|u*u,<around|[|2|]>><around|(|e<rsub|0>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Then <math|f-\<alpha\>*b<rsub|H>> passes the zero test. It inherits the
  Ward, reality, exchange, and lattice symmetries by subtracting the actual
  quadratic action, so it has order four. This establishes the normalization
  of <math|\<alpha\>> before any curvature lift is chosen.

  <subsubsection|The loop remainder and its real-wave estimate>

  Let <math|U<rsub|k>> denote the local determinant and Jacobian response and
  <math|V<rsub|k>> the inherited loop response. Suppose their common pictures
  are <math|f> and <math|g>, with strip bounds <math|\<Phi\>> and <math|R> on
  width <math|\<sigma\>>, respectively. Assume <math|f> records
  <math|U<rsub|k>> with error <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|k>|\|>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|k>>,
  while <math|g> records <math|V<rsub|k>> exactly and has order four. Assume
  also that <math|J<rsub|k>> has the exact picture <math|b<rsub|H>>, of bound
  <math|B<rsub|H>>, at that width. All three functions have the invariances
  used above. Then

  <\equation*>
    h=f-\<alpha\>*b<rsub|H>+g
  </equation*>

  has order four, strip cost at most <math|\<Phi\>+<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>*B<rsub|H>+R>,
  and records <math|U<rsub|k>-\<alpha\>*J<rsub|k>+V<rsub|k>> with the same
  error. The two-factor lift, with retained order two, gives an invariant
  loop atom family of budget

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:output-loop-budget>D*<around|(|\<Phi\>+<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>*B<rsub|H>+R|)>.
  </equation>

  It has zero total coefficient and zero first displacement moments. Let
  <math|S<rsub|2>> be its orientation-averaged action. The normalization
  argument above shows that the difference between
  <math|C<rsub|2><around|(|S<rsub|2>|)>> and
  <math|C<rsub|2>*<around|(|U<rsub|k>-\<alpha\>*J<rsub|k>+V<rsub|k>|)>> on
  every pair of unit complex Fourier waves is bounded by
  <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|k>|\|>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|k>>.

  Put <math|d=4*dim \<frak-g\>>, the number of one-form direction and colour
  coordinates at a site. A complex wave with all component amplitudes bounded
  by one is a sum of <math|d> unit basis waves, so bilinearity bounds the
  coefficient difference by <math|d<rsup|2><around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|k>|\|>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|k>>.
  A real wave is half the sum of that complex wave and the opposite-momentum
  wave with conjugate amplitude. Expanding two real waves gives four terms,
  each with factor <math|1/4>, and hence the same bound. Finally
  <math|D<rsup|2>=2*C<rsub|2>>:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:output-real-wave><around*|\||D<rsup|2>*<around|(|S<rsub|2>-U<rsub|k>+\<alpha\>*J<rsub|k>-V<rsub|k>|)><around|(|0|)><around|[|w<rsub|p>,w<rsub|q>|]>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*d<rsup|2><around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|k>|\|>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|k>.
  </equation>

  The momenta <math|p,q> are arbitrary; no opposite-momentum restriction has
  been added.

  <subsubsection|One common output path>

  Take the row family to be the coarse bed roots and take the loop, cubic,
  and quartic families just constructed. Their exact row attachment is
  unchanged by adding the other three families. The metric identity follows
  from that root attachment and the coarse bed identity. Invariance and the
  two loop moment identities hold separately for the constructed atoms. At
  any positive width below the common construction width, the row and
  cubic/quartic budgets are satisfied whenever

  <\equation*>
    A<rprime|'>\<geqslant\>max <around|{|<math-up|rowPrice><around|(|w,M<rsub|H>|)>,K*M|}>,<space|2em>\<Lambda\><rprime|'>\<geqslant\>D*<around|(|\<Phi\>+<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>*B<rsub|H>+R|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|M<rsub|H>> is the root-strip bound and <math|M> the classical
  Taylor bound. Width reduction only decreases each weighted absolute sum.
  Thus this is a path with all the required summability, metric, invariance,
  and moment properties, not merely a list of formal jets.

  Its classical part is <math|J<rsub|k>+S<rsub|3>+S<rsub|4>> and obeys
  <eqref|eq:output-classical-five>. Its response part is
  <math|\<alpha\>*J<rsub|k>+S<rsub|2>>. The stationary determinant identity
  identifies the actual thermal response, as a germ, with
  <math|U<rsub|k>+V<rsub|k>>. Consequently <eqref|eq:output-real-wave> is the
  required Hessian comparison for the actual response. If

  <\equation*>
    2*d<rsup|2>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>\<Theta\>*\<Omega\>*e<rsup|-g<space|0.17em><math-up|side><around|(|\<Lambda\><rsub|k>|)>>,
  </equation*>

  its right side is at most <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|k>|\|>*\<Theta\>*\<Omega\>*e<rsup|-g<space|0.17em><math-up|side><around|(|\<Lambda\><rsub|k>|)>>>.
  The stationary gap gives positivity of the stationary fluctuation Hessian,
  so its determinant remains positive near zero; the analytic chart gives the
  required smooth response germs. These conclusions all concern the same
  output path. Production of the common determinant picture, the inherited
  picture with its fourth-order bound, and the displayed exponential error is
  still required when this construction is used in a transition.

  We record the result with its order of choices.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The stationary output path><label|prop:stationary-output-path>Let
    <math|G> be simple and fix <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0>, <math|w\<gtr\>0>,
    <math|M<rsub|H>\<geqslant\>0> and <math|Z\<geqslant\>0>. There are
    <math|s> with <math|0\<less\>s\<leqslant\>min <around|(|\<sigma\>,w|)>>
    and <math|K,D\<geqslant\>0>, depending only on these data, with the
    following property. Let coarse sides <math|c<rsub|k>\<to\>\<infty\>> and
    fine sides <math|f<rsub|k>> be given, and for every <math|k> a block step
    from the coarse to the fine torus whose patch satisfies the branch
    compatibility of the kernel, whose fine side is at least one hundred
    times the macro radius of the patch and whose coarse side exceeds one, a
    forest, a choice <math|t<rsub|k>> of block representatives and a map
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|k>> into the tree chart. Let <math|p,q> be clips with
    <math|q<rsub|<math-up|direct>>\<leqslant\>Z>, let <math|B,C> be beds,
    <math|U> a path, <math|\<mu\>\<gtr\>0>, <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>, and fix a
    local Haar chart. Assume:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|U> has path bounds in <math|p> and is attached to <math|B>;
      the floor of <math|B,p> and the sworn condition of <math|B> hold at
      every <math|f<rsub|k>>;

      <item>for every <math|k>, <math|\<rho\><rsub|k>> is a stationary branch
      in the sense of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:stationary-germ-symmetry>
      with Hessian gap <math|\<eta\>>, and it satisfies the reduced Hessian
      identity for the bed <math|C>;

      <item>the metric and root rows of <math|C> have strip bound
      <math|<around|(|w,M<rsub|H>|)>>, the floor of <math|C,q> holds at every
      torus side, and the sworn condition of <math|C> holds at every
      <math|c<rsub|k>>;

      <item>the classical stationary actions <math|E<rsub|k>>, formed with
      <math|t<rsub|k>>, have exactly recorded common pictures of degrees
      three and four, with components holomorphic on the strip of width
      <math|\<sigma\>> and bounded by <math|X\<geqslant\>1>;

      <item>there are two-leg pictures <math|f> and <math|g> with components
      holomorphic on the strip of width <math|\<sigma\>> and bounded by
      <math|\<Phi\>> and <math|R>, such that <math|f> records the completed
      determinant correction <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|t<rsub|k>>> along
      <math|\<rho\><rsub|k>>, with regulator <math|\<mu\>>, with error
      <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|k>|\|>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|k>>, where
      <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|k>\<geqslant\>0> and
      <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|k>\<to\>0>, and <math|g> records the
      inherited loop of <math|U> along <math|\<rho\><rsub|k>> exactly and has
      order four at zero momentum.
    </enumerate>

    Fix a color <math|u> and put <math|\<alpha\>=\<alpha\><rsub|u><around|(|f|)>>.
    Then there is a path <math|T> such that

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(a)>the row family of <math|T> is the root row of <math|C>, and
      <math|T> is attached to <math|C>;

      <item*|(b)><math|T> has the path bounds
      <math|<around|(|s<rprime|'>,A<rprime|'>,\<Lambda\><rprime|'>|)>> in
      <math|q> whenever <math|0\<less\>s<rprime|'>\<leqslant\>s>,
      <math|A<rprime|'>\<geqslant\>max <around|{|<math-up|rowPrice><around|(|w,M<rsub|H>|)>,K*X|}>>
      and <math|\<Lambda\><rprime|'>\<geqslant\>D*<around*|(|\<Phi\>+<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|><math-up|bedCharge><around|(|min
      <around|(|\<sigma\>,w|)>,M<rsub|H>|)>+R|)>>;

      <item*|(c)>for every <math|k>, every <math|j\<leqslant\>4> and every
      choice of block representatives, the tested action of <math|p,U> along
      <math|\<rho\><rsub|k>> and the main term of <math|T> in the clip
      <math|q> on the <math|k>th coarse torus are analytic at zero and have
      the same <math|j>th derivative there;

      <item*|(d)>for every <math|k> and all
      <math|\<Theta\>,\<gamma\>,\<Omega\>> with
      <math|2*d<rsup|2>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>\<Theta\>*\<Omega\>*e<rsup|-\<gamma\>*c<rsub|k>>>:
      for every choice of block representatives the determinant of the
      tree-chart Hessian of the tested action <math|\<cal-T\>> is positive
      along <math|\<rho\><rsub|k>> near zero, the thermal function
      <math|\<vartheta\><around|(|x|)>=\<cal-L\><rsub|U>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><rsub|k><around|(|x|)>|)>+<frac|1|2>*log
      det D<rsub|z><rsup|2>*\<cal-T\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><rsub|k><around|(|x|)>|)>-log
      <big|prod><rsub|b>J<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|k><around|(|x|)><rsub|b>|)>>,
      formed with the loop term <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|U>> of <math|U> and the
      density <math|J> of the Haar chart, and the function
      <math|\<alpha\>*\<cal-K\><rsub|2,T>+\<cal-L\><rsub|T>>, formed from the
      quadratic term and the loop term of <math|T> in <math|q>, are analytic
      at zero, and their second derivatives at zero on any two real plane
      waves with complex amplitudes of modulus at most one differ by at most
      <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|k>|\|>*\<Theta\>*\<Omega\>*e<rsup|-\<gamma\>*c<rsub|k>>>.
    </enumerate>

    The path <math|T> does not depend on the choices of representatives in
    (c) and (d), nor on <math|\<Theta\>,\<gamma\>,\<Omega\>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The preceding subsections prove this. The cubic and quartic families are
    those of <eqref|eq:output-classical-five>, constructed from the pictures
    in<nbsp>(4) and from the pictures of <math|J<rsub|k>>, which exist
    by<nbsp>(3); the equality of Hessians used there is the lemma cited for
    <math|J<rsub|k>>, which uses<nbsp>(3), and the reduced Hessian identity
    in<nbsp>(2). The symmetry and gauge properties of <math|E<rsub|k>>, of
    the determinant correction and of the inherited loop are those of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:stationary-germ-symmetry>, whose standing
    hypotheses are<nbsp>(1) and<nbsp>(2). The loop family is the one
    constructed from <math|h=f-\<alpha\>*b<rsub|H>+g>, with budget
    <eqref|eq:output-loop-budget>. The path is assembled in the present
    subsection, which gives (a) and (b). Assertion (c) for the
    representatives <math|t<rsub|k>> is <eqref|eq:output-classical-five> with
    polarization; for other representatives it follows from the equality of
    germs proved in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:stationary-germ-symmetry>.
    For (d), the moving Haar identity <eqref|eq:ex-thermal-determinant>
    identifies the thermal function, as a germ, with the inherited loop plus
    the completed determinant correction, up to the choice of
    representatives, which again does not change the germ;
    <eqref|eq:output-real-wave> is then the stated comparison.
  </proof>

  The stationary data in<nbsp>(2) and the pictures in<nbsp>(4) and<nbsp>(5)
  are inputs to this proposition. The hypotheses are extracted from the
  standing data of the one-step construction, in which the incoming path
  <math|U> is attached to the fine bed <math|B> and the output path to the
  coarse bed <math|C>. In the one-step construction the pictures in<nbsp>(4)
  are those of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-classical-stationary-pictures>,
  the inherited picture <math|g> is that of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:inherited-loop-picture>, and the
  determinant picture <math|f> with its exponentially small error is the
  subject of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-stationary-determinant-prices>.

  <subsection|Transporting the inherited loop
  response><label|sec:inherited-loop-transport>

  The loop term entering a stationary step already has zero total coefficient
  and zero first displacement moments. These cancellations make its quadratic
  momentum picture vanish to fourth order. We prove that the stationary
  pullback retains this order and gains <math|L<rsup|-2>> in its strip bound,
  where <math|L> is the blocking side. The order statement and the
  quantitative strip statement require different arguments.

  <subsubsection|The fine-lattice picture>

  Group the loop coefficients by the two plaquette directions, the two colour
  indices, and the relative displacement <math|z> of their bases. Denote the
  resulting real coefficient array by <math|r<around|(|z|)>>. Its exponential
  norm is bounded by the loop budget <math|\<Lambda\>>: the norm of the
  relative displacement is no greater than the sum of the two slot lengths.
  Absolute summability permits grouping all atoms with the same displacement.
  The path's moment identities give

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|z>r<around|(|z|)>=0,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|z>z<rsub|a>*r<around|(|z|)>=0.
  </equation*>

  Consequently its Fourier series <math|<wide|r|^><around|(|p|)>> vanishes to
  order two at zero. The quadratic coefficient of the loop action is the sum
  over face indices of

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:inherited-fine-symbol>d<rsub|1>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*<wide|r|^><around|(|p|)>*d<rsub|1><around|(|p|)>,
  </equation>

  with its two field arguments symmetrized and its lattice orientations
  averaged. Each curl vanishes to first order. Thus every component of the
  resulting common picture <math|S> has order four. Its strip bound is at
  most <math|C<rsub|\<tau\>>*\<Lambda\>> at the original path width
  <math|\<tau\>\<gtr\>0>: each curl entry costs at most
  <math|4*<around|(|e<rsup|\<tau\>>+1|)>>, there are finitely many face
  pairs, and the signed orientation average has the fixed reflection cost
  already estimated in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-atom-stream-picture>.

  Formula <eqref|eq:inherited-fine-symbol> records the actual finite-volume
  quadratic coefficient. At zero total momentum, the two slot offsets appear
  only through their relative displacement; the anchor sum gives the number
  of fine sites. At nonzero total momentum it is zero. This verifies the
  identity before taking a large-volume limit. Invariance and the finite Ward
  identity give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:inherited-transverse>S<around|(|p|)>*d<rsub|0><around|(|p|)>=0,<space|2em>d<rsub|0>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*S<around|(|p|)>=0.
  </equation>

  All tensors below use this unconjugated bilinear convention. On real
  momenta, <math|d<rsub|0>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>=d<rsub|0><around|(|p|)><rsup|\<ast\>>>.

  <subsubsection|A strip bound with four finite differences>

  Put <math|\<mu\>=L<rsup|-1>>. Let <math|W<rsub|i>> be the installed matrix
  of the step on the <math|i>th torus and let <math|X> be its common
  coarse-to-fine Fourier bridge, the function denoted <math|\<Phi\>> in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-selected-stationary-linear-data>: on
  every torus of the family, <math|X> at the grid momenta is the Fourier
  representation of <math|W<rsub|i>>. By that section <math|W<rsub|i>> is the
  fine tangent at zero of the gauged-back, recentered stationary
  representative; it is not the tangent of the branch in the original tree
  chart, which differs from it by an infinitesimal gauge transformation.
  Assume that, on a fixed positive coarse strip of width <math|\<sigma\>>,
  <math|X> is holomorphic and periodic and, for every ordered word of at most
  two fine coordinate differences, of length <math|j\<leqslant\>2>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:inherited-bridge-price><around|\||\<Delta\><rsup|j>*X|\|>\<leqslant\>D*\<mu\><rsup|1+j>.
  </equation>

  This is the bridge bound <eqref|eq:ex-vertex-cruise>; for the retained
  tower it is proved in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-selected-stationary-linear-data>.

  Apply the finite-difference division of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-soft-factorization> to the order-four picture
  <math|S>. It writes <math|S> as a finite sum of products of four symbols
  <math|e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>-1> times Fourier series having a common
  exponential budget <math|C*\<Lambda\>> on a positive width <math|w>
  depending on <math|\<tau\>>. Distribute two differences to each of the two
  field arguments. Moving a difference from momentum <math|p> to <math|-p>
  uses

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>=-e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>*q<rsub|a>*<around|(|-p|)>.
  </equation*>

  The extra phase is a unit lattice translation of the remaining coefficient;
  its cost is absorbed in <math|C>. Thus every term has exactly two
  differences on each bridge leg.

  Pull this representation back by <math|X>. The width <math|\<sigma\>> is
  not changed. Instead the blocking side is restricted: we require

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:inherited-side-guard>\<mu\>*\<sigma\>\<leqslant\>w,
  </equation>

  so that for coarse momenta in the strip of width <math|\<sigma\>> all fine
  phases lie in the coefficient strip of width <math|w>. Here <math|w> and
  the constant <math|C> depend on <math|\<tau\>> only and were chosen before
  <math|L>. The exponentially weighted coefficient sum bounds every displaced
  contraction uniformly. For each pair of legs,
  <eqref|eq:inherited-bridge-price> contributes

  <\equation*>
    D<rsup|2>*\<mu\><rsup|3>*\<mu\><rsup|3>=D<rsup|2>*\<mu\><rsup|6>.
  </equation*>

  Summing the fine anchor within one coarse block contributes
  <math|L<rsup|4>=\<mu\><rsup|-4>>. Hence the complete common coarse picture
  <math|H> has, on the width <math|\<sigma\><rprime|'>=\<sigma\>/4>,
  component bound

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:inherited-strip-gain><around|\||H|\|><rsub|\<sigma\><rprime|'>>\<leqslant\>C<rprime|'>*\<Lambda\>*\<mu\><rsup|2>.
  </equation>

  The finite numbers of colour, direction, and difference addresses are
  included in <math|C<rprime|'>>, which depends on <math|G>,
  <math|\<sigma\>>, <math|\<tau\>> and <math|D>, not on <math|L>, the torus,
  or <math|\<Lambda\>>. Normal convergence of the coefficient sums proves
  holomorphy. Their covariance under integer momentum shifts and that of
  <math|X> prove periodicity of the common picture. Expanding the same
  absolutely convergent sums on a finite torus gives its exact recording
  identity for the linear pullback of the fine loop action.

  The nonlinear stationary pullback has the same second derivative. Indeed
  the fine loop action <math|V> has <math|D*V<around|(|0|)>=0>. Let
  <math|\<psi\><rsub|i>> be the fine component of the gauged-back stationary
  representative on the <math|i>th torus; it satisfies
  <math|\<psi\><rsub|i><around|(|0|)>=0> and
  <math|D*\<psi\><rsub|i><around|(|0|)>=W<rsub|i>>. The chain rule therefore
  reads

  <\equation*>
    D<rsup|2>*<around|(|V\<circ\>\<psi\><rsub|i>|)><around|(|0|)><around|[|a,b|]>=D<rsup|2>*V<around|(|0|)><around|[|W<rsub|i>*a,W<rsub|i>*b|]>+D*V<around|(|0|)>*<around|[|D<rsup|2>*\<psi\><rsub|i><around|(|0|)><around|[|a,b|]>|]>=D<rsup|2>*V<around|(|0|)><around|[|W<rsub|i>*a,W<rsub|i>*b|]>.
  </equation*>

  The loop action is gauge invariant, so <math|V\<circ\>\<psi\><rsub|i>>
  equals, as a germ, the inherited loop evaluated on the stationary branch in
  the original tree chart; and that function does not involve the choice of
  block representatives. Thus <math|H> records the actual inherited response,
  with no additional error.

  <subsubsection|The alias formula and the coarse\Ufine equation>

  To prove the order at zero, use the real-momentum alias representation. For
  <math|l\<in\><around|(|\<bbb-Z\>/L*\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|4>>, let

  <\equation*>
    k<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>=<frac|p+2*\<pi\>*l|L>
  </equation*>

  with any fixed integer representatives; periodicity removes the choice of
  representatives. Let <math|N<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>> be the alias component
  of <math|X> in the normalization for which a coarse unit wave pulls back
  into fine waves of amplitudes <math|N<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>>. With this
  normalization of the amplitudes the common picture satisfies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:inherited-alias>H<rsub|a*b><around|(|p|)>=L<rsup|4>*<big|sum><rsub|l>N<rsub|-l>*<around|(|-p|)><rsub|a><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*S<around|(|k<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>|)>*N<rsub|l><around|(|p|)><rsub|b>.
  </equation>

  For clarity, this equality follows first at each finite coarse Fourier
  grid. Expand both pulled-back waves in the finite set of aliases. Fine
  translation invariance kills every pair except opposite aliases; the ratio
  of fine to coarse site counts is <math|L<rsup|4>>. Approximate an arbitrary
  real <math|p> along grids whose side lengths tend to infinity. The bridge
  and both common pictures are continuous and the alias set is finite, so the
  identity passes to every real <math|p>. The same argument works with
  per-volume errors tending to zero. Here both recording identities are
  exact.

  Write <math|K<rsub|f><around|(|k|)>=d<rsub|1><around|(|k|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*B<around|(|k|)>*d<rsub|1><around|(|k|)>>
  for the fine Gaussian form and <math|K<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>> for the
  coarse one, formed with the metric rows of the fine bed and of the coarse
  bed. The order statement uses, as an input, the two matching equations of
  the installed matrices for this pair of beds: with <math|Q<rsub|i>> the
  block mean and <math|\<kappa\>> the penalty coefficient, on every torus of
  the family

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:inherited-matching>K<rsub|f,i>*W<rsub|i>=Q<rsub|i><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*K<rsub|c,i>,<space|2em>Q<rsub|i>*W<rsub|i>=I-\<kappa\><rsup|-1>*K<rsub|c,i>.
  </equation>

  These are the diagonal two-scale identities of the fine bed, the coarse bed
  and the tower; they are hypotheses of the one-step construction
  (Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:path-stationary-chart>). Stationarity
  of the branch and its Hessian gap alone do not identify an independently
  chosen coarse form; compare Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-thermal-contact-cancellation>.
  The first equation of <eqref|eq:inherited-matching> implies, in the block
  Fourier coordinates,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:inherited-primal>K<rsub|f><around|(|k<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>|)>*N<rsub|l><around|(|p|)><rsub|a>=A<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>*K<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>*e<rsub|a>,
  </equation>

  where <math|A<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>> is the corresponding component of the
  adjoint block-mean map and the inverse block Fourier change of coordinates.
  It is analytic near zero for fixed <math|L>. To obtain
  <eqref|eq:inherited-primal>, write <math|K<rsub|f>*X=Q<rsup|\<ast\>>*K<rsub|c>>
  at the grid momenta and diagonalize the fine Gaussian form in its alias
  blocks. This identity then follows on arbitrary real momenta from its exact
  finite-grid versions by the preceding density argument, using that the
  coarse sides tend to infinity and that both metric rows are continuous on
  the real momenta. Since <math|K<rsub|c>> contains a curl on each side,

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>=O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>|)>.
  </equation*>

  <subsubsection|Suppressing the nonprincipal aliases>

  Fix <math|l\<ne\>0> and real <math|p> near zero. Then
  <math|k=k<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>> stays away from zero modulo
  <math|2*\<pi\>>. Let <math|D<around|(|k|)>=d<rsub|0><around|(|k|)>> and
  <math|\<omega\><around|(|k|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a><around|\||q<rsub|a><around|(|k|)>|\|><rsup|2>\<gtr\>0>.
  Project the bridge component off its longitudinal part:

  <\equation*>
    N<rsub|l><rsup|\<perp\>><around|(|p|)>=<around*|(|I-D<around|(|k|)>*\<omega\><around|(|k|)><rsup|-1>*D<around|(|k|)><rsup|\<ast\>>|)>*N<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>.
  </equation*>

  The matrix <math|D<around|(|k|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*D<around|(|k|)>> is
  <math|\<omega\><around|(|k|)>*I> on colours, so
  <math|D<around|(|k|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*N<rsub|l><rsup|\<perp\>><around|(|p|)>=0>.
  The fine kinetic form kills pure gauge vectors. Combining this fact with
  <eqref|eq:inherited-primal> gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:inherited-elliptic-alias><around*|(|K<rsub|f><around|(|k|)>+D<around|(|k|)>*D<around|(|k|)><rsup|\<ast\>>|)>*N<rsub|l><rsup|\<perp\>><around|(|p|)>=A<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>*K<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>.
  </equation>

  The matrix on the left is invertible at <math|p=0>. Here the fine floor is
  required for the same bed on every finite period, with one positive lower
  constant. Passing its Fourier inequality to real frequencies gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<langle\>|v,K<rsub|f><around|(|k|)>*v|\<rangle\>>\<geqslant\>c*<around|\<\|\|\>|d<rsub|1><around|(|k|)>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>,<space|2em>c\<gtr\>0.
  </equation*>

  The coordinate exterior identity is

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|d<rsub|1><around|(|k|)>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>+<around|\<\|\|\>|D<around|(|k|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=\<omega\><around|(|k|)><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  It follows either by expanding both squares or from the anticommutation of
  exterior multiplication and contraction. A vector in the kernel of
  <math|K<rsub|f><around|(|k|)>+D<around|(|k|)>*D<around|(|k|)><rsup|\<ast\>>>
  must make both nonnegative terms zero, and hence must be zero. Finite
  dimensionality gives invertibility. The matrix depends continuously on
  <math|p>, so its inverse is bounded near zero for this fixed <math|l,L>.
  Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:inherited-elliptic-alias> now proves

  <\equation*>
    N<rsub|l><rsup|\<perp\>><around|(|p|)>=O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>|)>.
  </equation*>

  This is a qualitative order statement. No uniform lower bound in <math|L>
  is asserted for this inverse.

  The Ward identities <eqref|eq:inherited-transverse> permit replacing both
  <math|N> factors in each alias term of <eqref|eq:inherited-alias> by their
  transverse projections. For <math|l\<ne\>0>, each contributes order two;
  <math|S<around|(|k<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>|)>> is bounded near <math|p=0>.
  Every nonprincipal term therefore has order four. For the principal alias,
  <math|k<rsub|0><around|(|p|)>=\<mu\>*p>, and <math|S*<around|(|\<mu\>*p|)>>
  already has order four. Its two bridge factors are analytic and bounded
  near zero. This term has order four as well. There are finitely many
  aliases, and multiplication by the fixed factor <math|L<rsup|4>> does not
  change Taylor order. Hence <math|H> has order four.

  Together with <eqref|eq:inherited-strip-gain> and the exact recording
  identity, this supplies the inherited loop picture used in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:stationary-output-construction>. Its strip
  bound is uniform in the blocking side under the stated bridge prices; its
  order-four property uses the all-period floor and the exact coarse\Ufine
  stationary equation. In particular it does not assume that the longitudinal
  alias amplitudes themselves are small.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The inherited loop picture><label|prop:inherited-loop-picture>Fix
    <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0>, <math|D\<geqslant\>0> and
    <math|\<tau\>\<gtr\>0>. There are <math|w\<gtr\>0> and
    <math|C<rprime|'>\<geqslant\>0>, depending only on
    <math|G,\<sigma\>,D,\<tau\>>, such that the following holds for every
    patch with blocking side <math|L> satisfying
    <math|\<sigma\>/L\<leqslant\>w> and the branch compatibility of the
    kernel, every sequence of coarse sides <math|m<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>
    with fine sides <math|n<rsub|i>=L*m<rsub|i>> and block steps with that
    patch, every clip <math|p>, path <math|R>, fine bed <math|B>, coarse bed
    <math|T> and bridge <math|X>. Assume:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|R> has path bounds <math|<around|(|\<tau\>,A,\<Lambda\>|)>>
      in <math|p> for some <math|A> and some <math|\<Lambda\>\<geqslant\>0>;

      <item><math|X> is holomorphic and periodic on the strip of width
      <math|\<sigma\>> and satisfies <eqref|eq:inherited-bridge-price> there,
      and for every <math|i> it is the common Fourier representation of a
      matrix <math|W<rsub|i>>;

      <item>for every <math|i> there are a forest and an analytic map
      <math|\<rho\><rsub|i>> into its tree chart with
      <math|\<rho\><rsub|i><around|(|0|)>=0>, stationary near the origin for
      the tested action of every choice of block representatives, these
      tested actions being real analytic near the origin, such that the
      derivative of <math|t\<mapsto\>\<rho\><rsub|i>*<around|(|t*x|)>> at
      <math|t=0> is the linear prediction of the tree chart from
      <math|W<rsub|i>>; and the matching equations
      <eqref|eq:inherited-matching> hold for <math|W<rsub|i>>, <math|B> and
      <math|T>;

      <item>the metric row of <math|T> has a strip bound at some positive
      width, the metric row of <math|B> has a strip bound at width
      <math|\<sigma\>/L>, and the floor of <math|B,p> holds at every torus
      side with one constant <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>.
    </enumerate>

    Then there is a two-leg picture <math|H> such that, for every <math|i>,
    <math|H> records exactly the second Taylor coefficient of the inherited
    loop of <math|R> along <math|\<rho\><rsub|i>> on the <math|i>th coarse
    torus; every component of <math|H> is holomorphic and periodic on the
    strip of width <math|\<sigma\>/4> and bounded there by
    <math|L<rsup|-2>*\<Lambda\>*C<rprime|'>>; and every component has order
    four at zero momentum along real rays.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    This is the content of the present section. The picture is the pullback
    by <math|X> of the fine picture <math|S> of the first subsection, which
    needs<nbsp>(1). The strip bound is <eqref|eq:inherited-strip-gain>,
    proved under <eqref|eq:inherited-side-guard> and<nbsp>(2). The recording
    identity for the nonlinear pullback uses the installed tangent
    in<nbsp>(3). The order at zero is proved in the last two subsections from
    the alias formula, the first equation of <eqref|eq:inherited-matching>,
    the strip bounds and the floor in<nbsp>(4), and the Ward identities
    <eqref|eq:inherited-transverse>.
  </proof>

  In the one-step construction the hypotheses of this proposition are part of
  the standing data of the step. These contain, in addition, the following
  data, none of which plays a role in the argument above:

  <\itemize>
    <item>a positive Hessian gap of the branches;

    <item>the response data of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-selected-kinetic-response>
    and their two recording conditions;

    <item>the identification of the response map at each side with the one
    built from the installed matrix <math|W<rsub|i>>;

    <item>the vanishing of the frame component of that response map.
  </itemize>

  The proposition does not assume them, and so applies to the standing data
  of the step whether or not these further data are present.

  <subsection|Transporting the pole coefficient through the block
  bridge><label|sec:ex-quadratic-pole-transport>

  The annulus calculation concerns a fine-lattice determinant, whereas the
  stationary output uses its pullback to coarse fields. We prove that their
  leading quadratic coefficients agree. This uses both quadratic matching
  equations for the selected bridge, including its normalization at zero
  momentum.

  Let <math|\<Pi\><rsub|f>> be a common quadratic picture for the fine pole
  functional

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|f><around|(|x|)>=<frac|1|2>*log det
    <around*|(|K<around|(|x|)>+d<around|(|x|)>*d<around|(|x|)><rsup|\<ast\>>+<big|sum><rsub|a>s<rsub|a><rsup|\<ast\>>*C<around|(|x|)>*s<rsub|a>|)>-log
    det <around*|(|d<around|(|x|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*d<around|(|x|)>+C<around|(|x|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The notation is that of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-stationary-determinant-prices>.
  Suppose its finite-volume recording error is at most the fine site
  cardinality times <math|e<rsub|m>>, with <math|e<rsub|m>\<to\>0> along the
  selected periods. Let <math|\<Pi\><rsub|c>> be the common picture of the
  same functions pulled back by the installed linear bridge <math|W>, with
  coarse per-site errors tending to zero. Both pictures are holomorphic on
  positive strips. Fix the block side <math|L> throughout the argument.

  The standing data are those of one stationary step, and all of them refer
  to the same step. The gauge group is simple. The coarse sides
  <math|m<rsub|i>> tend to infinity and the fine sides are
  <math|n<rsub|i>=L*m<rsub|i>>. The path <math|R> has path bounds in the clip
  <math|p> and is attached to the fine bed <math|B>; the metric row of
  <math|B> has a strip bound at width <math|\<sigma\>/L>, the floor of
  <math|B,p> holds with one constant <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0> at every
  torus side, and the sworn condition of <math|B> holds at every
  <math|n<rsub|i>>. The metric row of the coarse bed <math|T> has a strip
  bound at width <math|\<sigma\>>. The bridge <math|\<Phi\>> is holomorphic
  and periodic on the strip of width <math|\<sigma\>> and is the common
  Fourier representation of the installed matrices <math|W<rsub|i>>, which
  satisfy both matching equations <eqref|eq:inherited-matching> for <math|B>
  and <math|T>, with block mean <math|Q<rsub|i>> and penalty coefficient
  <math|\<kappa\>>. The frame and kinetic response data of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-selected-kinetic-response>, from which
  the rank response is built, are formed with these same <math|W<rsub|i>>.
  The pole functional <math|P<rsub|f>> is formed with the regulator
  <math|\<mu\>=L<rsup|-1>>, and the fine pole picture <math|\<Pi\><rsub|f>>
  records it at the sides <math|n<rsub|i>>. Fix also the block
  representatives of the step at each side. The functional <math|P<rsub|f>>
  and its pullback by <math|W<rsub|i>> are defined without them; they enter
  only the recording of the pulled-back functions by the common picture
  <math|\<Pi\><rsub|c>>. The argument of this section does not use the
  stationary branch or its Hessian gap, although a stationary step also
  carries both of them.

  The block determinant matrix <math|M<rsub|<math-up|rank>>> is the direct
  sum of the fine pole matrix and an identity on coarse scalar coordinates,
  after the coordinate permutation separating the two kinds of variables.
  Thus its determinant equals the fine pole determinant. The scalar
  determinant is the same in both presentations. The Fourier matrix
  identities proved above therefore identify the coarse rank picture with the
  picture of <math|P<rsub|f>\<circ\>W>; this is an equality for the actual
  selected finite-volume functions.

  The pole function is invariant under local gauge changes, constant color
  rotations and unsigned coordinate permutations. For gauge changes this
  follows by conjugating every factor: the fine Hessian in left coordinates,
  the covariant difference, its resolvent regulator and the component maps
  all transform by the corresponding orthogonal adjoint actions. Determinants
  are unchanged. The same argument, with coordinate permutations whose
  direction signs are all positive, proves this permutation invariance. Its
  first derivative at zero vanishes by the simple-color argument in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-thermal-contact-cancellation>. Passing
  the finite-volume identities to the common picture, as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:stationary-output-construction>, shows that
  its bilinear matrix <math|S<around|(|p|)>> has both Ward identities

  <\equation*>
    S<around|(|p|)>*d<rsub|0><around|(|p|)>=0,<space|2em>d<rsub|0>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|T>*S<around|(|p|)>=0,
  </equation*>

  The first-order part of the Ward identities forces
  <math|S<around|(|0|)>=0>. To prove that the linear part also vanishes, fix
  the two color indices and write it as <math|S<rsub|a*b><around|(|p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|c>T<rsub|a*b,c>*p<rsub|c>+O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>|)>>.
  The two Ward identities, at quadratic order, give

  <\equation*>
    T<rsub|a*b,b>=T<rsub|a*b,a>=0,<space|2em>T<rsub|a*b,c>+T<rsub|a*c,b>=0.
  </equation*>

  These equations also kill the case <math|a=b>: then
  <math|T<rsub|a*a,c>=-T<rsub|a*c,a>=0>. If <math|a,b,c> are distinct, the
  unsigned permutation interchanging <math|b,c> and fixing <math|a> gives
  <math|T<rsub|a*b,c>=T<rsub|a*c,b>>; the displayed antisymmetry makes both
  zero. All repeated-index cases are already zero. Thus <math|S> vanishes to
  order at least two. This uses only unsigned permutations; no reflection
  symmetry of the pole functional is assumed. The finite-volume errors
  disappear after division by the site cardinality. No rate uniform in
  <math|L> is used in this step.

  Write <math|H<around|(|p|)>> for the bilinear matrix of
  <math|\<Pi\><rsub|c>> and <math|N<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>> for the fine alias
  amplitudes of <math|W>. The finite-wave expansion from
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:inherited-loop-transport> gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-pole-alias-pullback>H<around|(|p|)>=L<rsup|4>*<big|sum><rsub|l>N<rsub|-l>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|T>*S<around|(|k<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>|)>*N<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>,<space|2em>k<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>=\<mu\>*<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*l|)>.
  </equation>

  We spell out the role of errors here. A pulled-back coordinate wave is a
  finite sum of fine alias waves with amplitudes
  <math|N<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>>. For fixed <math|L>, these amplitudes remain
  bounded as the real momentum approaches a specified value. Multilinearity
  bounds the error on each pair by the original per-site error times the
  finite sum of the products of its amplitudes. The ratio of fine to coarse
  site cardinalities is <math|L<rsup|4>>. Hence the error still tends to
  zero. Opposite aliases are the only surviving pairs, by fine translation
  invariance; all other pairs tend to zero. Continuity and a dense sequence
  of external grid momenta prove <eqref|eq:ex-pole-alias-pullback> at every
  real momentum.

  For <math|l\<ne\>0>, the transverse projection of
  <math|N<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>> has order two at <math|p=0>, by the
  all-period kinetic floor and the primal matching equation proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:inherited-loop-transport>. The two Ward
  identities of <math|S> remove the longitudinal parts of both amplitudes.
  Thus every nonprincipal term of <eqref|eq:ex-pole-alias-pullback> has order
  at least four. This argument does not require <math|S> to vanish at the
  nonprincipal alias.

  It remains to determine the principal amplitude at zero. The dual matching
  equation, the second equation of <eqref|eq:inherited-matching>, passes from
  the grids to all real momenta by the density argument already used. With
  <math|Q<around|(|p|)>> the symbol of the block mean it reads

  <\equation*>
    Q<around|(|p|)>*\<Phi\><around|(|p|)>=I-\<kappa\><rsup|-1>*K<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|K<rsub|c><around|(|0|)>=0>, its value at zero is
  <math|Q<around|(|0|)>*\<Phi\><around|(|0|)>=I>. Each nonprincipal alias
  amplitude at zero is a pure gauge vector, because its transverse projection
  vanishes there. The block mean commutes with the gauge difference, so it
  maps this vector to a coarse gradient at momentum zero, which is zero.
  Hence those aliases contribute nothing to
  <math|Q<around|(|0|)>*\<Phi\><around|(|0|)>>. On the principal constant
  alias, the normalized transverse weights sum to one and the straight block
  path has <math|L> edges; therefore the mean acts as multiplication by
  <math|L>. We conclude

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-principal-amplitude>N<rsub|0><around|(|0|)>=\<mu\>*I.
  </equation>

  In particular this normalization is a consequence of the actual two
  matching equations, rather than a choice of Fourier convention made after
  the comparison.

  Analyticity gives <math|N<rsub|0><around|(|p|)>=\<mu\>*I+O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|>|)>>.
  Since <math|S> vanishes to order two and
  <math|k<rsub|0><around|(|p|)>=\<mu\>*p>, expanding the two principal
  amplitudes yields

  <\equation*>
    H<around|(|p|)>-L<rsup|4>*\<mu\><rsup|2>*S*<around|(|\<mu\>*p|)>=O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|3>|)>
  </equation*>

  along every ray. The nonprincipal terms have already been shown to be of
  order four. If <math|S<rsub|2>> is the homogeneous quadratic term of
  <math|S>, the quadratic term of the expression on the right is

  <\equation*>
    L<rsup|4>*\<mu\><rsup|2>*\<mu\><rsup|2>*S<rsub|2><around|(|p|)>=S<rsub|2><around|(|p|)>.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|H> and <math|S> have identical quadratic Taylor terms, and in
  particular identical drift coefficients in every color component.

  We state the result and its use.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Transport of the pole coefficient><label|prop:ex-pole-coefficient-transport>Under
    the standing data listed at the beginning of this section, let
    <math|\<Pi\><rsub|f>> be a two-leg picture, with components holomorphic,
    periodic and bounded on a strip of positive width, which records the fine
    pole functional <math|P<rsub|f>> of <math|p,R> with regulator
    <math|L<rsup|-1>> at every fine side <math|n<rsub|i>> with error
    <math|n<rsub|i><rsup|4>*e<rsub|i>>, where <math|e<rsub|i>\<to\>0>. Let
    <math|\<Pi\><rsub|c>> be the picture associated, as in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-pole-recording>, with the rank response
    kernel, and assume that this kernel is jointly holomorphic, periodic and
    bounded on a strip of positive width. Then for every color <math|c>

    <\equation*>
      \<alpha\><rsub|c><around|(|\<Pi\><rsub|c>|)>=\<alpha\><rsub|c><around|(|\<Pi\><rsub|f>|)>.
    </equation*>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    By the block identity for the determinant matrix recalled above and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-pole-recording>, <math|\<Pi\><rsub|c>>
    records <math|P<rsub|f>\<circ\>W<rsub|i>> on the <math|i>th coarse torus
    with error equal to the coarse site count times a quantity tending to
    zero. The argument of this section then shows that the bilinear matrices
    <math|H> of <math|\<Pi\><rsub|c>> and <math|S> of <math|\<Pi\><rsub|f>>
    have the same quadratic homogeneous term at zero momentum. The
    coefficient <math|\<alpha\><rsub|c>> is twice the real part of one entry
    of that term.
  </proof>

  In the one-step construction three coefficients occur, and the proposition
  relates two of them. Let <math|\<cal-Y\><rsub|<math-up|det>>> be the
  picture of the completed determinant correction <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|t>> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:stationary-germ-symmetry> along the
  stationary branch, with regulator <math|\<mu\>=L<rsup|-1>>. The scalar of
  the output path is <math|\<alpha\>=\<alpha\><rsub|c><around|(|\<cal-Y\><rsub|<math-up|det>>|)>>
  (Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:stationary-output-path>). The inherited
  loop does not enter <math|\<alpha\>>: by the moving Haar identity
  <eqref|eq:ex-thermal-determinant> the thermal function is the inherited
  loop plus <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|t>> along the same branch, and the inherited
  picture has order four (Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:inherited-loop-picture>),
  so its quadratic coefficient is zero. The comparison of the completed
  determinant response with the rank response is the subject of
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-stationary-determinant-prices>
  and<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-thermal-contact-cancellation>: it is to supply a
  constant <math|C<rsub|<math-up|cmp>>>, fixed before <math|L>, with

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<alpha\><rsub|c><around|(|\<cal-Y\><rsub|<math-up|det>>|)>-\<alpha\><rsub|c><around|(|\<Pi\><rsub|c>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|cmp>>.
  </equation*>

  This is an input here. Finally Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-annulus-estimate>
  supplies a fine pole picture <math|\<Pi\><rsub|f>> for the same clip, path,
  fine bed and regulator, recorded at the same fine sides <math|n<rsub|i>>,
  with

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<alpha\><rsub|c><around|(|\<Pi\><rsub|f>|)>+\<lambda\><rsub|G>*log
    L|\|>\<leqslant\>D<rsub|0>.
  </equation*>

  The three statements concern the same data, and the triangle inequality
  gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<alpha\>+\<lambda\><rsub|G>*log
    L|\|>\<leqslant\>D<rsub|0>+C<rsub|<math-up|cmp>>.
  </equation*>

  The hypotheses <eqref|eq:ex-annulus-hypotheses> of the annulus estimate,
  for the fine bed and the fine sides, are among the standing data of this
  section together with the bound on the direct coefficient of <math|p> and
  the strip bound of <math|B> at a fixed width.

  <subsection|Convergence of the free precision
  horizon><label|sec:free-horizon-convergence>

  We prove the retained-horizon convergence used for the stationary paths.
  The cutoff tends to infinity while the scale and block side remain fixed.
  All coefficients are kept on the lattice cover. Finite-torus matrices are
  used to identify their limits; they are not substituted for the cover
  coefficients.

  <subsubsection|Compactness with an exponential row bound>

  A row <math|K> between finite coordinate sets <math|I,J> has coefficients
  <math|K<rsub|a*b><around|(|z|)>>, <math|z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>. Suppose,
  eventually in <math|N>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:free-horizon-row-budget><big|sum><rsub|z>e<rsup|\<sigma\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>><around|\||K<rsub|N,a*b><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*<space|2em><around|(|a\<in\>I,b\<in\>J|)>,<space|2em>\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation>

  A subsequence of any given cutoff sequence converges in every coefficient:
  enumerate the countably many entries, extract successively in their bounded
  real intervals, and take the diagonal subsequence. If its limit is
  <math|K>, pass to the limit in each finite subsum of
  <eqref|eq:free-horizon-row-budget>. The nonnegative sums remain bounded by
  <math|C>, so the full sum has the same bound.

  For every <math|0\<leqslant\>\<tau\>\<less\>\<sigma\>> this coefficient
  convergence gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:free-horizon-narrower><big|sum><rsub|z>e<rsup|\<tau\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>*<around|\||K<rsub|N,a*b><around|(|z|)>-K<rsub|a*b><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation>

  Indeed its summands are at most <math|2*C*e<rsup|-<around|(|\<sigma\>-\<tau\>|)><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>>,
  and

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>e<rsup|-t<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>=<around*|(|<frac|1+e<rsup|-t>|1-e<rsup|-t>>|)><rsup|4>\<less\>\<infty\>*<space|2em><around|(|t\<gtr\>0|)>.
  </equation*>

  First make the tail of this majorant small, and then use convergence on its
  finite complement. This also proves convergence of the Fourier symbols on
  real momenta and of the periodized matrices on every fixed torus, since
  their entries are sums of subfamilies of the same coefficients. The
  argument applies to a cross row by including its finitely many block
  residues in <math|I>.

  Two ways of identifying the resulting cluster limits will be useful. If the
  Fourier symbols have a prescribed limit on a dense subset of the real
  momentum torus, any two cluster rows have the same symbol there. Their
  symbols are continuous by absolute summability, so they agree everywhere.
  Integrating against <math|e<rsup|-i*p\<cdot\>z>> recovers the coefficient
  <math|K<rsub|a*b><around|(|z|)>>, proving equality of the rows.
  Alternatively, it suffices that the cluster rows have equal matrices on an
  unbounded sequence of torus sides. For a fixed displacement <math|z> the
  appropriate periodized entry is

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|h\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>K<rsub|a*b>*<around|(|z+n*h|)>.
  </equation*>

  Its term <math|h=0> is <math|K<rsub|a*b><around|(|z|)>>. All other terms
  have displacement at least <math|n-<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>> and their sum
  tends to zero by absolute summability. Thus these matrices recover every
  cover coefficient as <math|n\<to\>\<infty\>>. In either case the cluster
  limit is unique. If the original sequence did not converge to it in some
  coordinate, a subsequence staying a fixed distance away would have a
  convergent further subsequence, a contradiction. Hence the full sequence
  converges, with <eqref|eq:free-horizon-narrower> at every smaller width.

  <subsubsection|The primitive multipliers along the actual scale schedule>

  Fix a scale <math|m>, and let <math|v<rsub|d,m>> be the train consisting of
  the next <math|d> prescribed blocking steps, with a top train at its fine
  end. Its mass side <math|M<rsub|d,m>> tends to infinity, since each block
  side is at least three. The actual path at terminal cutoff <math|N> uses
  precisely <math|v<rsub|N-m,m>>; for fixed <math|m>, its depth <math|N-m>
  tends to infinity. Consequently each depth limit below also applies to the
  selected terminal cutoff sequence. For real momentum <math|p> and a
  direction <math|a>, put

  <\equation*>
    u<rsub|d,m,a><around|(|p|)>=D<rsub|v<rsub|d,m>,a>*<around|(|p/M<rsub|d,m>|)>*D<rsub|v<rsub|d,m>,a>*<around|(|-p/M<rsub|d,m>|)>,
  </equation*>

  using the cumulative drive of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-free-kernel-components>.
  A single step with patch <math|P<rsub|m>> and side <math|L<rsub|m>> gives
  the exact identity

  <\equation*>
    u<rsub|d+1,m,a><around|(|p|)>=<around|\||<math-up|pullLine><rsub|P<rsub|m>,a><around|(|p/L<rsub|m>|)>|\|><rsup|2>*u<rsub|d,m+1,a>*<around|(|p/L<rsub|m>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Each rail and centered plank is an average of unit complex numbers on real
  momenta, up to a unit phase. Its modulus is at most one. Thus the factor in
  front lies in <math|<around|[|0,1|]>>. Starting with
  <math|u<rsub|0,m,a>=1>, induction on <math|d> in the displayed recurrence
  shows

  <\equation*>
    0\<leqslant\>u<rsub|d+1,m,a><around|(|p|)>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|d,m,a><around|(|p|)>\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  This proves convergence for every real <math|p>, also after any fixed
  reciprocal translation of <math|p>.

  The noise list of <math|v<rsub|d+1,m>> is the list for <math|v<rsub|d,m>>
  with one additional train appended. This follows by induction from the list
  rule under one hop: both old lists undergo the same hop, with the same top
  element prepended. Each additional real-momentum feed is nonnegative. The
  accumulated noise <math|N<rsub|d,m,a><around|(|p|)>> is therefore
  nondecreasing. The uniform noise estimate of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-free-kernel-components> bounds it
  above, independently of <math|d> for <math|p> in the base cube. At depth
  one it is one. Its limit <math|N<rsub|m,a><around|(|p|)>> consequently
  satisfies

  <\equation*>
    1\<leqslant\>N<rsub|m,a><around|(|p|)>\<less\>\<infty\>.
  </equation*>

  We also need the high-alias part <math|H<rsub|v<rsub|d,m>,a><around|(|p|)>>.
  Extend its finite sum to <math|\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>> by setting it to zero
  outside the centered representatives of the nonzero residues of
  <math|M<rsub|d,m>>. Each fixed <math|z\<ne\>0> is represented for all
  sufficiently large <math|d>. Put <math|q=p+2*\<pi\>*z>. The corresponding
  summand is then exactly

  <\equation*>
    <frac|u<rsub|d,m,a><around|(|q|)>|d<rsub|M<rsub|d,m>><around|(|q|)>>.
  </equation*>

  The numerator has a limit by the preceding monotonicity. The identity

  <\equation*>
    M*<around|(|e<rsup|i*q<rsub|a>/M>-1|)>\<longrightarrow\>i*q<rsub|a>
  </equation*>

  gives <math|d<rsub|M><around|(|q|)>\<to\><around|\||q|\|><rsup|2>>. For
  <math|p> in the base cube and <math|z\<ne\>0>, <math|q\<ne\>0>, so its
  denominator has a nonzero limit. The uniform alias bounds already proved
  give one summable majorant

  <\equation*>
    C*<big|prod><rsub|a=1><rsup|4><around|(|1+<around|\||z<rsub|a>|\|>|)><rsup|-2>
  </equation*>

  for these extended summands. The sum of this product is finite by the
  one-dimensional inverse-square series. Dominated convergence for the series
  proves convergence of the high-alias part. At <math|z=0> the extended
  summand is identically zero; no denominator at the origin is used in this
  argument.

  For <math|p\<ne\>0> the full directional covariance multiplier is

  <\equation*>
    L<rsub|d,m,a><around|(|p|)>=<frac|u<rsub|d,m,a><around|(|p|)>|d<rsub|M<rsub|d,m>><around|(|p|)>>+H<rsub|v<rsub|d,m>,a><around|(|p|)>+k<rsup|-1>*N<rsub|d,m,a><around|(|p|)>,<space|2em>k\<gtr\>0.
  </equation*>

  It has a finite real limit <math|L<rsub|m,a><around|(|p|)>\<geqslant\>k<rsup|-1>\<gtr\>0>.
  The pulse and dual-pulse symbols are the rational matrix expressions in
  these multipliers given in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-free-kernel-estimates>.
  Their directional denominators have just been shown to have positive
  limits. The remaining scalar denominator has the form

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|a><frac|<around|\||e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>-1|\|><rsup|2>|L<rsub|m,a><around|(|p|)>>.
  </equation*>

  It is strictly positive off the origin in the base cube: every term is
  nonnegative and some numerator is nonzero. Consequently every entry of both
  symbols converges there. At zero their normalized values are the identity
  for every cutoff. The uniform strip bounds of the free-kernel estimates and
  the first subsection now give convergence of both rows on the cover, with a
  retained exponential bound.

  <subsubsection|The adjacent one-form and two-form transports>

  Fix the block side <math|L=L<rsub|m>>. Unlike the mass side in the
  preceding high-alias sum, <math|L> does not change with the terminal
  cutoff. For <math|p\<ne\>0> in the base cube, all the finitely many
  adjacent aliases <math|q<rsub|l>=<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*\<ell\><rsub|L><around|(|l|)>|)>/L>
  are nonzero real momenta in that cube. Apply the directional limit above at
  the coarse momentum <math|p> and the fine momenta <math|q<rsub|l>>. The
  principal and nonprincipal transport formulas of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:kernel-transport-identities> then have
  convergent factors.

  We spell out the additional denominator check in those formulas. The finite
  alias denominator is

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-V\><rsub|N><around|(|p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|l><around|\||m<rsub|P<rsub|m>><around|(|q<rsub|l>|)>|\|><rsup|2>*<big|sum><rsub|a>L<rsub|N,a><rsup|<math-up|scaled>><around|(|q<rsub|l>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|L<rsub|N,a><rsup|<math-up|scaled>>> is the fine directional
  multiplier with the fixed positive scaling <math|L<rsup|-2>>. Every summand
  has a nonnegative real limit, each inner sum has a strictly positive limit,
  and the principal patch mass <math|m<rsub|P<rsub|m>>*<around|(|p/L|)>> is
  nonzero on the base cube. Hence <math|lim<rsub|N>
  \<cal-V\><rsub|N><around|(|p|)>\<gtr\>0>. All other divisions in the
  transport formulas are by the positive directional or scalar denominators
  just checked. Finite matrix products, sums and division by these nonzero
  limits prove convergence of every alias transport. Fourier reconstruction
  over the finite set of <math|L<rsup|4>> aliases proves convergence of the
  adjacent transport symbols. Their uniform row bounds again identify unique
  cover limits. Possible exceptional momenta at the origin are removed by
  continuity of the cluster symbols, not by taking a quotient there.

  <subsubsection|Convergence of amendments, exterior lifts and roots>

  Several operations preserve the cover convergence just established. Sums,
  transposes and scalar multiples do so directly. For products, take the
  minimum of two positive available widths; the convolution of rows has a
  uniform bound there, multiplied by the finite middle coordinate count. Its
  symbol is the product of the two symbols, so the first subsection
  identifies its unique limit. For an inverse row with a uniform bound,
  suppose <math|K<rsub|N><rsup|-1>*K<rsub|N>=I> on every torus. Every cluster
  limit <math|Q> of the inverse rows satisfies <math|Q*K=I> there by finite
  matrix multiplication. A square matrix with a left inverse is invertible,
  so that inverse is unique. Recovery from all periods then identifies
  <math|Q> on the cover, and proves convergence.

  The exterior lifts in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:curvature-horizon-producer>
  are finite combinations of coordinate evaluation at <math|p<rsub|a>=0>,
  fixed exterior matrices and divided differences

  <\equation*>
    <frac|<wide|K|^><rsub|N><around|(|p|)>-<wide|K|^><rsub|N><around|(|p\|<rsub|p<rsub|a>=0>|)>|e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>-1>.
  </equation*>

  Their uniform bounds at a smaller positive width were proved there. For
  <math|p<rsub|a>\<ne\>0> the displayed expression converges, because both
  numerator terms do and the denominator is fixed. Thus any two cluster
  symbols agree off that coordinate hyperplane, and by continuity everywhere.
  This proves convergence of the divided row. Repeating the argument through
  the finitely many axes proves convergence of the one-sided and two-sided
  exterior lifts, with all their fixed matrix factors.

  It remains to justify the positive square roots; convergence of an
  arbitrary matrix factor would be false. Suppose <math|P<rsub|N>> have a
  common exponential row bound, their torus matrices are positive
  semidefinite, and <math|P<rsub|N><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*P<rsub|N>> converge on
  every torus. Two cluster limits <math|P,Q> have positive semidefinite torus
  matrices, since symmetry and nonnegativity of each quadratic form pass to
  the limit. Their squares are equal. A positive semidefinite square root is
  unique: if <math|A\<geqslant\>0> and <math|A<rsup|2>=B>, then <math|A>
  commutes with <math|B>; on each eigenspace of <math|B>, diagonalizing the
  symmetric restriction of <math|A> forces all eigenvalues to be the
  nonnegative square root of that eigenvalue of <math|B>. Hence <math|A> is
  fixed on every eigenspace. Apply this to <math|P> and <math|Q> on each
  torus, then recover the cover coefficients. The unique-cluster-limit
  argument proves convergence of <math|P<rsub|N>> itself.

  The amended beds have exactly these properties. If <math|B<rsub|N>> is the
  dual-pulse row, its amended inverse precision is

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|N>=B<rsub|N>+l*<around|(|d<rsub|2>*B<rsub|N>|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*<around|(|d<rsub|2>*B<rsub|N>|)>,<space|2em>l\<geqslant\>0,
  </equation*>

  and its precision is <math|G<rsub|N>=A<rsub|N><rsup|-1>>. Products and the
  inverse argument give their limits. The root row has square
  <math|G<rsub|N>-c*I>, where <math|c> is the fixed direct clipping
  coefficient. Its uniform strip bound and positive semidefinite torus values
  are supplied by Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:positive-row-producer>, so
  the positive-root argument applies. The original pulse, dual pulse, amended
  precision, its inverse and the root row therefore converge together.

  For the curvature root, use the explicit amended row of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:curvature-horizon-producer>:

  <\equation*>
    R<rsub|N>=D<rsub|N><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*<around|(|C<rsub|N>+l*I-h*S<rsub|N><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*S<rsub|N>|)>*D<rsub|N>,<space|2em>D<rsub|N>=<around|(|I+l*d<rsub|2>*B<rsub|c,N>*d<rsub|2><rsup|<math-ss|T>>|)><rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|l,h> are the fixed coarse and fine amendment coefficients;
  <math|C<rsub|N>,S<rsub|N>> are the two exterior lifts constructed there.
  Their convergence follows from the operations just proved. The positivity
  and uniform strip price of <math|R<rsub|N>> and its positive root are
  already established. Since that root's square is <math|R<rsub|N>>, it too
  converges. All of these statements concern the actual chosen positive
  roots; no new choice of signs or bases has been made.

  <subsubsection|Completing the retained tower>

  The tower considered here is not an arbitrary completion of the converging
  beds. It is the tower of the retained horizon produced in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:curvature-horizon-producer> from this same
  recursively generated bed horizon, with the nonnegative amendment
  coefficients used above. That construction keeps the beds, the one-form
  predictor and the two-form transport of the original horizon. For every
  cutoff <math|N\<gtr\>m> it chooses, together with the three blocks below, a
  curvature root row <math|P<rsub|N>> with these properties: a strip bound
  whose width and constant depend on the stage but not on <math|N>; positive
  semidefinite torus matrices; the identity
  <math|P<rsub|N><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*P<rsub|N>=R<rsub|N>> on every torus,
  where <math|R<rsub|N>> is the amended curvature row displayed above; and
  the block formulas below on every torus of the schedule. The convergence
  assertion of this subsection is for this jointly chosen tower and uses each
  of these four properties.

  The full precision factors <math|J<rsub|f,N>,J<rsub|c,N>> consist of the
  fixed direct block and the root rows, so they converge on every torus.
  Write <math|G<rsub|f,N>,G<rsub|c,N>> for the corresponding precisions and
  <math|R<rsub|f,N>=G<rsub|f,N><rsup|-1>>,
  <math|R<rsub|c,N>=G<rsub|c,N><rsup|-1>>. With the two-form transport
  <math|T<rsub|N>> and curvature root <math|P<rsub|N>>, the exact tower
  blocks are

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|M<rsub|F,N>>|<cell|=J<rsub|f,N>*R<rsub|f,N>*T<rsub|N>*J<rsub|c,N><rsup|<math-ss|T>>,>>|<row|<cell|M<rsub|N,N>>|<cell|=k<rsup|-1/2>*d<rsub|1,c><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*J<rsub|c,N><rsup|<math-ss|T>>,>>|<row|<cell|M<rsub|O,N>>|<cell|=<around*|[|<tabular*|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|I-J<rsub|c,N>*R<rsub|c,N>*J<rsub|c,N><rsup|<math-ss|T>>>>|<row|<cell|P<rsub|N>*d<rsub|2,c>*R<rsub|c,N>*J<rsub|c,N><rsup|<math-ss|T>>>>>>>|]>.>>>>
  </align*>

  These are the same formulas as <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-horizon-blocks>, on
  each selected physical torus of side <math|n<rsub|m><around|(|p|)>>, with
  its prescribed fine partner. Here <math|n<rsub|m><around|(|p|)>> denotes
  the period sequence fixed at scale <math|m> by the schedule. Here <math|p>
  is fixed while <math|N> increases. The committed transition identities
  apply on precisely these tori. Every finite matrix factor has a limit
  there, hence so does each block. Their uniform strip bounds were purchased
  in the retained tower construction. The period-recovery criterion from the
  first subsection gives convergence of their cover coefficients. Use the
  selected sides above, which tend to infinity as <math|p> does; no
  assumption on convergence of an unrelated periodic realization is needed.

  Together with the one-form predictor and two-form transport, these are
  precisely the five retained tower rows. Thus at every fixed scale all five
  bed rows and all five tower rows converge, and each family keeps some
  positive exponential width and finite budget. The purchases of widths and
  budgets are made at the fixed stages, before choosing the later terminal
  cutoff. The eventual normalization changes discussed in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-history-limits> preserve these
  limits. This supplies the free-horizon convergence input of that section
  from the actual recursively selected free kernels.

  <subsection|Attaching finite-cutoff paths on the selected stationary
  route><label|sec:path-attachment-producer>

  This section records the selected stationary route from its horizontal
  annulus estimate through finite-cutoff path attachment. It separates three
  different jobs: producing the annulus estimate, using it in a one-step path
  construction, and assembling the resulting transitions. Only the last job
  is a finite induction. The quantitative inputs to the one-step construction
  are stated at their actual interface and are not inferred from that
  induction.

  <subsubsection|Bounds for the one-step stationary path>

  The background quadratic data <math|B> (called a <em|bed> below) comprise
  the curvature metric and its root coefficient array. The parameter list
  <math|p> (the <em|clip>) specifies the quadratic test action, including its
  direct coefficient <math|p.<math-up|direct>>. A path is a collection of
  row, loop, cubic, and quartic coefficients. For a parameter list <math|p>,
  strip width <math|\<tau\>>, coefficient bound <math|A>, and loop bound
  <math|\<Lambda\>>, the predicate <math|R.<math-up|Bounds><around|(|p,\<tau\>,A,\<Lambda\>|)>>
  is the path budget: each exponentially weighted row family has sum at most
  <math|A>; the loop atom-budget family has sum at most <math|\<Lambda\>>;
  and the cubic and quartic atom-budget families each have sum at most
  <math|A>. In addition, every atom is invariant, the row symbols satisfy the
  metric identity, row-symbol terms are summable, and the loop coefficient
  and its first moment have summable terms and sum to zero. The attachment
  predicate <math|<math-up|Attached><around|(|B,R|)>> says that the path
  coefficients at each plaquette and cover offset sum to the bed's root
  entry. In particular, these are simultaneous global summability conditions,
  not merely pointwise bounds.

  The precise annulus estimate, denoted <math|<math-up|Ann><around|(|G,\<sigma\>,\<varepsilon\>,C,Z,\<tau\>,A|)>>,
  has the following quantifiers. It supplies an integer <math|L<rsub|min>>
  and <math|D<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0>. For every odd side
  <math|L\<geqslant\>L<rsub|min>>, every side sequence
  <math|m<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>, every bed <math|B>, clip <math|p>, path
  <math|R>, and <math|\<Lambda\>>, impose the hypotheses of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-annulus-estimate>:

  <\equation*>
    R.<math-up|Bounds><around|(|p,\<tau\>,A,\<Lambda\>|)>,<space|1em><math-up|Attached><around|(|B,R|)>,<space|1em>B.*<math-up|metric.strip><around|(|\<sigma\>,C|)>,<space|1em>p.<math-up|direct>\<leqslant\>Z,
  </equation*>

  along with <math|<math-up|Floor><around|(|B,p,n,\<varepsilon\>,C|)>> for
  every <math|n> and <math|<math-up|Match><around|(|B,m<rsub|i>,G|)>> for
  every <math|i>. Then there are one Fourier kernel <math|Y>, nonnegative
  strip constants <math|v,E>, and a single error sequence
  <math|e<rsub|i>\<to\>0> such that

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|Strip><around|(|v,E,Y<rsub|i*j>|)><space|1em><text|for every
    >i,j,<space|2em><math-up|Rec><space|-0.17em><around*|(|Y,<math-up|Pole><around|(|p,R,L<rsup|-1>|)>,m<rsub|i><rsup|4>*e<rsub|i>|)><space|1em><text|for
    every >i,
  </equation*>

  and <math|<around|\||\<alpha\><rsup|<math-up|ann>><rsub|j><around|(|Y|)>+\<lambda\><rsub|G>*log
  L|\|>\<leqslant\>D<rsub|0>> for every color <math|j>. Here
  <math|<math-up|Strip>> means a bounded periodic holomorphic extension with
  width <math|v> and bound <math|E>, and <math|<math-up|Match>> denotes the
  finite-volume quadratic matching condition for <math|B>. The pole Hessian
  <math|<math-up|Pole>> and its finite-mesh recording condition
  <math|<math-up|Rec>> are specified in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-annulus-recording>.
  The latter is a finite-mesh identity up to the stated error; no passage to
  a limit in <math|i> is part of this assertion.

  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-annulus-estimate> supplies this
  estimate on the same fine bed, clip and path. Its Wilson reference and its
  Wilson-subtracted correction are added before any finite-period recording
  is compared. The fee bounds give one common response strip; the recording
  error is then the exponentially small torus-mean error. The face estimates
  of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-annulus-fleet-faces> give the two
  radial inequalities

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||g<around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|A<rsub|1>|<around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>>+\<mu\>|)><rsup|4>>,<space|2em><around|\||g<around|(|x|)>-h<rsub|G><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|A<rsub|2>|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsup|3>>+<frac|A<rsub|2>*\<mu\><rsup|2>|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsup|6>>*<space|1em><around|(|\<mu\>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1|)>.
  </equation*>

  Their constants precede the blocking side. The following elementary
  integration is the last step giving its logarithmic drift constant.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Radial logarithmic coefficient
    bound><label|lem:path-radial-log>Assume
    <math|0\<less\>\<mu\>\<leqslant\>1>, <math|A<rsub|1>,A<rsub|2>\<geqslant\>0>,
    and let <math|E<rsub|4>\<subset\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>> be a measurable
    finite-volume region containing the unit ball. Let <math|g> be real
    measurable and integrable on <math|E<rsub|4>>, and let <math|h<rsub|G>>
    be integrable on the annulus <math|I<rsub|\<mu\>>=<around|{|x:\<mu\>\<less\><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1|}>>.
    Assume the radial estimates

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||g<around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|A<rsub|1>|<around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>>+\<mu\>|)><rsup|4>>*<space|1em><around|(|x\<in\>E<rsub|4>|)>,<space|2em><around|\||g<around|(|x|)>-h<rsub|G><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|A<rsub|2>|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsup|3>>+<frac|A<rsub|2>*\<mu\><rsup|2>|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsup|6>>*<space|1em><around|(|x\<in\>I<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Assume also the coefficient and pole identities

    <\equation*>
      \<alpha\><rsup|<math-up|ann>><rsub|j><around|(|F|)>=c<rsub|<math-up|wt>>*<big|int><rsub|E<rsub|4>>g<around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x,<space|2em>c<rsub|<math-up|wt>>*<big|int><rsub|I<rsub|\<mu\>>>h<rsub|G><around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x=-\<lambda\><rsub|G>*log
      \<mu\><rsup|-1>,<space|1em>c<rsub|<math-up|wt>>\<gtr\>0.
    </equation*>

    For the productive picture <math|F>, set

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|<math-up|rad>>*<around|(|A<rsub|1>,A<rsub|2>|)>=A<rsub|1>*<around*|(|<frac|\<pi\><rsup|2>|2>+<math-up|vol><around|(|E<rsub|4>|)>|)>+3*\<pi\><rsup|2>*A<rsub|2>.
    </equation*>

    Then

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<alpha\><rsup|<math-up|ann>><rsub|j><around|(|F|)>+\<lambda\><rsub|G>*log
      \<mu\><rsup|-1>|\|>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|<math-up|wt>>*C<rsub|<math-up|rad>>*<around|(|A<rsub|1>,A<rsub|2>|)>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Write <math|E<rsub|4>> for the finite-volume four-dimensional integration
    region, <math|B<rsub|1>=<around|{|x:<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1|}>>,
    <math|B<rsub|\<mu\>>=<around|{|x:<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<mu\>|}>>,
    <math|I=<around|{|x:\<mu\>\<less\><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1|}>>,
    and <math|Q=E<rsub|4>\<setminus\>B<rsub|1>>. Polar coordinates give
    <math|<math-up|vol><around|(|B<rsub|r>|)>=\<pi\><rsup|2>*r<rsup|4>/2>. On
    <math|B<rsub|\<mu\>>>, the first radial estimate implies
    <math|<around|\||g<around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|1>*\<mu\><rsup|-4>>,
    so <math|<around|\||<big|int><rsub|B<rsub|\<mu\>>>g|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|1>*\<pi\><rsup|2>/2>.
    On <math|Q>, one has <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>>\<geqslant\>1>,
    and hence <math|<around|\||g<around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|1>>;
    therefore <math|<around|\||<big|int><rsub|Q>g|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|1><math-up|vol><around|(|E<rsub|4>|)>>.

    On <math|I>, integrate the second estimate in radial coordinates. The two
    radial integrals are

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsub|I><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsup|-3>*<space|0.17em>d*x=2*\<pi\><rsup|2>*<around|(|1-\<mu\>|)>,<space|2em><big|int><rsub|I><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsup|-6>*<space|0.17em>d*x=2*\<pi\><rsup|2>*<frac|\<mu\><rsup|-2>-1|2>.
    </equation*>

    Thus

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsub|I><around|\||g-h<rsub|G>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*\<pi\><rsup|2>*A<rsub|2>*<around|(|1-\<mu\>|)>+\<pi\><rsup|2>*A<rsub|2>*\<mu\><rsup|2>*<around|(|\<mu\><rsup|-2>-1|)>\<leqslant\>3*\<pi\><rsup|2>*A<rsub|2>.
    </equation*>

    The pole integral is exactly the logarithmic term:
    <math|c<rsub|<math-up|wt>>*<big|int><rsub|I>h<rsub|G>=-\<lambda\><rsub|G>*log
    \<mu\><rsup|-1>>, by the assumed pole identity. The Fourier formula for
    <math|\<alpha\><rsup|<math-up|ann>><rsub|j>> is
    <math|c<rsub|<math-up|wt>>*<big|int><rsub|E<rsub|4>>g>. Splitting the
    integral over <math|B<rsub|\<mu\>>\<cup\>I\<cup\>Q> and using the three
    absolute bounds proves the claim. The radial integrals were evaluated
    above; the pole identity is a separate hypothesis. No uniformity beyond
    the displayed radial estimates is used.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The stationary chart receipts><label|subsec:path-stationary-chart>

  The chart data consumed by the path interpolation is produced at each fixed
  finite volume. It is useful to give this construction separately: the
  positive Hessian gap, critical chart, and installed-matrix approximation
  enter the later path theorem as supplied data, but their source is an
  implicit-function argument, not an additional limit assumption.

  Throughout this subsection a kernel choice with penalty
  <math|K<rsub|<math-up|pen>>\<gtr\>0>, an odd blocking side <math|L> and a
  phase <math|j> are fixed, and <math|R<rsub|<math-up|macro>>=<around|(|j+3|)>*\<ell\>+4*\<ell\>>
  denotes the macro radius, where <math|\<ell\>=L.<math-up|val>>. A one-step
  datum consists of a coarse side <math|c> and a fine side <math|f=c*\<ell\>>
  together with the blocking maps of the kernel. It is called larger when its
  patch satisfies the branch compatibility hypothesis of the kernel and

  <\equation*>
    100*R<rsub|<math-up|macro>>\<leqslant\>f,<space|2em>1\<less\>c.
  </equation*>

  All statements below are for a larger one-step datum, an allowed forest
  <math|F> of its blocking ratio, and a local choice at the parent of each
  fine edge. Configurations are taken in the forest slice, and forest
  directions are extended by zero on the tree edges.

  Start with the planted comparison for a coarse site <math|i> and a fine
  edge <math|e=<around|(|x,a|)>>, where <math|i> is the parent of <math|e>.
  Write <math|\<ell\>=L.<math-up|val>> as above; let <math|p<rsub|i>> be the
  planted root frame and <math|P<rsub|i>> the local predictor. The planting
  estimates give nonnegative constants <math|C<rsub|0>,D<rsub|0>> and, with

  <\equation*>
    \<alpha\><rsub|0>=2*max <around|(|1,<math-up|predictorCut><rsup|-1>|)>*<around|(|2*C<rsub|0>+D<rsub|0>|)>,<space|1em>u<rsub|0>=<around|(|8*\<ell\>+1|)>*D<rsub|0>*\<ell\>,<space|1em>v<rsub|0>=C<rsub|0>*\<ell\><rsup|2>+\<alpha\><rsub|0>*\<ell\><rsup|-2>+C<rsub|0>*\<ell\><rsup|2>,
  </equation*>

  the finite-volume edge estimate

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|chord><space|-0.17em><around*|(|U<rsub|e><around|(|<math-up|replica><space|0.17em>V|)><rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|u<rsub|0>+v<rsub|0>|)><space|0.17em><math-up|port><rsub|i><around|(|U|)>+\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|i\<ne\>i+<math-up|parentStep><around|(|e|)>|}>><math-up|wire><around|(|P<rsub|i>,V,e|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here the first term is obtained by following the forest root walk: its
  length is at most the patch radius, and the planted bound charges each walk
  edge by <math|D<rsub|0>*\<ell\>> times the port. If the edge stays in its
  parent cell, the lifted path closes at the root, so this is the entire
  estimate. If it crosses to a neighboring parent, the endpoint root-to-frame
  chords each cost at most <math|C<rsub|0>*\<ell\><rsup|2><math-up|port>>,
  while the predicted edge has chord at most
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|0>*\<ell\><rsup|-2><math-up|port>>. The triangle
  inequality adds these three terms, and the residual is precisely the
  displayed wire term. This is the two-case proof of the planted comparison;
  the tree walk and crossing endpoint costs are not hidden in a generic
  locality claim.

  For a fixed step, summing the squared port terms over coarse sites uses
  Cauchy\USchwarz twice: first over the box offsets and plaquette types, then
  over the box offsets in the site sum. Injectivity of the map from a coarse
  site/plaquette pair to its embedded fine plaquette prevents extra
  multiplicity. The resulting factor is exactly the product of the box
  cardinality, the plaquette count, and the site count appearing in the curl
  factor. The probe wire is summed over coarse bonds and paid by the second
  factor, the probe factor. Combining the two with the floor bound yields

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|e><around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|e>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rsub|\<ell\>>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-D\><around|(|v|)>+\<gamma\><rsub|\<ell\>>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-P\><around|(|v|)>,
  </equation*>

  where the coefficients are

  <\equation*>
    \<beta\><rsub|\<ell\>>=<around|(|2*C<rsup|2>|)><around|\||<math-up|Site><around|(|L|)><around|\||<space|0.17em>|\|><math-up|Axis>|\|><around|(|<around|\||X|\|><space|0.17em><around|\||<math-up|PlaqAxes>|\|>|)><space|0.17em><around|\||X|\|>,<space|2em>\<gamma\><rsub|\<ell\>>=2<around|\||<math-up|Site><around|(|L|)>|\|>,
  </equation*>

  with <math|X> the fixed patch-offset set and <math|C> the edge-comparison
  constant. Thus <math|\<ell\>> is the blocking side cardinality, not the
  coarse torus side. Here <math|v> is a forest direction, extended by zero on
  the tree edges, <math|\<cal-D\>> is the squared physical color derivative
  and <math|\<cal-P\>> the squared predictor penalty. Here is the Hessian
  identity used in this comparison. Let <math|h> be a coarse variation and
  <math|v> a forest-coordinate variation. Write <math|e*<around|(|h,v|)>> for
  the fine variation obtained by differentiating the forest lift at zero, and
  write <math|Q> for the linearized block predictor. The linearized predictor
  residual is <math|c*<around|(|h,v|)>=h-Q*e*<around|(|h,v|)>>. For two pairs
  <math|<around|(|h,v|)>,<around|(|h<rprime|'>,v<rprime|'>|)>>, the tested
  action has second derivative

  <\equation*>
    D<rsup|2>*\<cal-T\><around|(|0|)><around|[|<around|(|h,v|)>,<around|(|h<rprime|'>,v<rprime|'>|)>|]>=<big|sum><rsub|f><around|\<langle\>|d<rsub|1>*e*<around|(|h,v|)>,M<rsub|n>*d<rsub|1>*e*<around|(|h<rprime|'>,v<rprime|'>|)>|\<rangle\>><rsub|f>+<math-up|penalty><space|0.17em><around|\<langle\>|c*<around|(|h,v|)>,c*<around|(|h<rprime|'>,v<rprime|'>|)>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed the Hessian of the fine main action is the first displayed bilinear
  form by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-soft-quadratic-hessian>, while its
  first derivative vanishes, so the second derivative of the forest lift does
  not contribute. The predictor penalty vanishes together with its first
  derivative at the identity; its quadratic Taylor term is one half of
  <math|<math-up|penalty>> times the squared norm of the predictor residual.
  The second-derivative chain rule therefore gives the second term and no
  higher derivative of the predictor. Taking <math|h=h<rprime|'>=0> gives the
  forest Hessian used above, whose first term is bounded below by
  <math|a*\<cal-D\><around|(|v|)>> by the all-period floor. Assume
  <math|a\<gtr\>0>, <math|<math-up|penalty>\<gtr\>0>, and
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|\<ell\>>,\<gamma\><rsub|\<ell\>>\<geqslant\>0>. Set

  <\equation*>
    \<eta\>=<frac|min <around|(|a,<math-up|penalty>|)>|1+\<beta\><rsub|\<ell\>>+\<gamma\><rsub|\<ell\>>>.
  </equation*>

  Then <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>, <math|\<eta\>*\<beta\><rsub|\<ell\>>\<leqslant\>a>
  and <math|\<eta\>*\<gamma\><rsub|\<ell\>>\<leqslant\><math-up|penalty>>,
  and the preceding two inequalities give the strict price gap

  <\equation*>
    \<eta\>*<big|sum><rsub|e><around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|e>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>D<rsup|2>*\<cal-T\><around|(|0|)><around|[|v,v|]>.
  </equation*>

  This is the quantitative Hessian lower bound used by the chart
  construction.

  The remaining chart receipts follow in four steps. First, the exponential
  coefficient budgets in the path bound make each uncut row and atom series
  converge uniformly on a complex neighborhood of the physical field.
  Termwise analyticity and summability therefore give smoothness of the
  finite-volume main action. The chart compatibility hypothesis supplies the
  required analytic composition along every smooth field chart. Second, in a
  forest chart the lifted field agrees near the origin with the ordinary
  field coordinates; the tested action is the flat action composed with this
  lift. Differentiating this germ identity and using the finite-volume
  Hessian identity gives the prismatic second variation and the comptroller
  bilinear form.

  Third, fix one allowed choice of local test and write
  <math|F*<around|(|x,z|)>> for the tested action in coarse coordinates
  <math|x> and forest coordinates <math|z>. At <math|<around|(|0,0|)>>,
  <math|F=0> and <math|D*F=0>. The price gap implies that the vertical
  Hessian <math|D<rsub|z><rsup|2>*F*<around|(|0,0|)>> is positive definite,
  hence invertible in the finite dimensional forest space. The
  implicit-function theorem applied to <math|D<rsub|z>*F*<around|(|x,z|)>=0>
  gives a smooth branch <math|z=\<rho\><around|(|x|)>> with
  <math|\<rho\><around|(|0|)>=0>. By the branch compatibility hypothesis, the
  tested actions for any two local choices agree on a neighborhood of
  <math|<around|(|0,0|)>> in the full variables <math|<around|(|x,z|)>>, not
  merely on the graph of <math|\<rho\>>. Their vertical derivatives therefore
  agree near zero, so the same <math|\<rho\>> is critical for each local
  choice.

  Fourth, differentiate the stationarity equation at zero:

  <\equation*>
    D<rsup|2>*F*<around|(|0,0|)><around*|[|<around|(|x,D*\<rho\><around|(|0|)>*x|)>,<around|(|0,v|)>|]>=0<space|1em><text|for
    every forest direction >v.
  </equation*>

  The comptroller formula identifies this bilinear form as the sum of the bed
  metric term and the penalty pairing of the predictor residual. The
  installed tower matrix satisfies that same linear equation by the diagonal
  two-scale trial. Uniqueness, from invertibility of the vertical Hessian,
  therefore yields <math|D*\<rho\><around|(|0|)>*x=<math-up|relative><around|(|W,x|)>>,
  the guessed-chart identity. Finally, the chain rule at the stationary
  branch removes the term involving <math|D<rsub|z>*F*<around|(|0,0|)>>; the
  same comptroller formula and the diagonal tower identity give the target
  bed metric as the Hessian of the reduced action. This is the heat identity:
  for every local choice, the tested action along <math|\<rho\>> and one half
  of the kinetic quadratic form of the coarse bed metric are both
  real-analytic at the origin and have the same second derivative there. It
  is an equality, not an estimate. These arguments prove the fixed-period
  stationarity, installed-tangent, and reduced-Hessian identities used by the
  transition.

  The uniformity is as follows. The number <math|\<eta\>> displayed above
  depends only on the kernel, the blocking side <math|L>, the phase <math|j>
  and the floor constant <math|a>: the factors
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|\<ell\>>,\<gamma\><rsub|\<ell\>>> involve only
  <math|L>, the patch-offset set <math|X> of radius
  <math|R<rsub|<math-up|macro>>> and the edge-comparison constant <math|C> of
  the planting estimate, which is itself chosen for <math|<around|(|L,j|)>>
  and the kernel. Hence the same <math|\<eta\>> is a gap for every larger
  one-step datum with these <math|L,j> and kernel, every coarse side, every
  forest, and every bed, clip and path satisfying the hypotheses. In
  particular it is uniform in the period. By contrast the neighborhoods of
  the origin on which the implicit-function theorem gives the branch
  <math|\<rho\>> may depend on the finite volume, and no uniformity is
  asserted for them.

  The exact interface for the one-step construction is the following. Fix a
  kernel choice, a phase <math|J>, positive
  <math|\<varepsilon\>,w,\<delta\>>, and nonnegative
  <math|D,Z,C,Z<rprime|'>>. The one-step construction proved in
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:path-one-step-production> chooses a reel
  <math|R<rsub|1>> satisfying <math|R<rsub|1>.<math-up|Price><around|(|s|)>>,
  nonnegative <math|\<kappa\>>, and positive <math|s>. For every
  <math|\<tau\>\<gtr\>0> and <math|A\<geqslant\>0>, assume
  <math|<math-up|Ann><around|(|G,w,\<varepsilon\>,D,Z,\<tau\>,A|)>>. It then
  chooses <math|d\<geqslant\>0>, <math|\<gamma\>\<gtr\>0>, a period threshold
  <math|u>, and functions <math|\<beta\><around|(|L|)>>,
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|0><around|(|L|)>>. For every patch with phase
  <math|J> and odd side at least <math|u>, every pair of side sequences
  <math|m<rsub|i>,n<rsub|i>> with <math|m<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>, every path
  and beds/towers, the following hypotheses are required:

  <\itemize>
    <item>an armada whose every constituent step is a larger step, and the
    two bed hulls (metric and root strip bounds, floors, and sworn
    conditions);

    <item>an incoming path <math|R> attached to the fine bed <math|B>, with
    <math|R.<math-up|Bounds><around|(|p,\<tau\>,A,\<Lambda\>|)>> for the fine
    clip <math|p>, the diagonal two-scale trial of the fine bed <math|B>, the
    coarse bed <math|T> and the tower, and the stated header bounds on the
    installed tower column;

    <item>for each forest chart <math|F<rsub|i>>, the exact receipts

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|PriceGap><around|(|F<rsub|i>,p,R,\<eta\>|)>,<space|2em><math-up|Critical><around|(|F<rsub|i>,p,R,\<rho\><rsub|i>|)>,<space|2em><math-up|Guessed><around|(|F<rsub|i>,W<rsub|i>,\<rho\><rsub|i>|)>,
    </equation*>

    and the heat identity for the coarse bed <math|T>;

    <item>the local-ratio chart and the specified direct-coefficient cutoffs
    for both clips. The annulus picture is produced inside this proof from
    <math|<math-up|Ann>>; it is not an additional input.
  </itemize>

  The remaining scalar hypotheses are <math|1\<leqslant\>\<Lambda\>>,
  <math|0\<less\>\<eta\>>, the phase identity, the period threshold, and the
  two direct clip bounds. The armada must have the selected kernel and all
  its patches must be branched. These conditions are part of the one-step
  input, as are the hypotheses used to define each forest chart.

  The content of these hypotheses is precise. <math|<math-up|PriceGap>> is
  the positive lower bound <math|\<eta\>*<big|sum><rsub|e><around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|e>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>D<rsup|2>*\<cal-T\><around|(|0|)><around|[|v,v|]>>
  for the tested action <math|\<cal-T\>>. <math|<math-up|Critical>> asserts
  that the chart <math|\<rho\>> is smooth, vanishes at zero, and is
  stationary for every tested action. <math|<math-up|Guessed>> says that the
  derivative of <math|t\<mapsto\>\<rho\>*<around|(|t*x|)>> at zero is the
  chart's linear prediction from the installed matrix. The preceding
  subsection proves these receipts at each fixed finite volume by the planted
  sensitivity estimate, the Hessian comparison, and the implicit-function
  theorem. The output <math|<math-up|ClassicalMatch>> is instead a
  conclusion: for every Taylor order <math|j\<leqslant\>4> and every local
  choice, the tested action along <math|\<rho\>> and the main action of the
  new path have equal <math|j>th derivatives at the origin.

  The one-step construction gives a path <math|Y> and scalar <math|\<alpha\>>
  with

  <\equation*>
    Y.<math-up|row>=<math-up|rowPath><around|(|T|)>,<space|2em><math-up|Attached><around|(|T,Y|)>,
  </equation*>

  for the coarse bed <math|T> and the coarse clip <math|q>; thus the output
  path is attached to the coarse bed, while the incoming path was attached to
  the fine bed. All four components of <math|Y.<math-up|Bounds><around|(|q,\<eta\><rprime|'>,A<rprime|'>,\<Lambda\><rprime|'>|)>>
  obey the requested row/loop/cubic/quartic budgets whenever
  <math|0\<less\>\<eta\><rprime|'>\<leqslant\>s>,
  <math|\<kappa\>\<leqslant\>A<rprime|'>>,
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|0><around|(|L|)>\<leqslant\>\<Lambda\><rprime|'>> and
  <math|\<Lambda\>/128\<leqslant\>\<Lambda\><rprime|'>>. Under the same four
  conditions the loop, cubic and quartic coefficient arrays of <math|Y> are
  the images, under the fixed encodings of the reel <math|R<rsub|1>>, of
  elements <math|Q,P,W> of its three canonical coefficient subspaces for the
  coarse side sequence, with

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|Q|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<Lambda\><rprime|'>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|P|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>A<rprime|'>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|W|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>A<rprime|'>.
  </equation*>

  The reel consists of three coefficient Hilbert spaces with encoding widths
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|2>,\<delta\><rsub|3>,\<delta\><rsub|4>>, and
  <math|R<rsub|1>.<math-up|Price><around|(|s|)>> means that <math|s> is
  strictly smaller than each of them. The canonical subspaces are the
  orthogonal complements constructed in Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:path-vow-representatives>.
  The construction also gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<alpha\>+\<lambda\><rsub|G>*log L|\|>+1\<leqslant\>d,
  </equation*>

  with the number <math|d> chosen above. It gives the order-four classical
  matching identity and, whenever <math|\<beta\><around|(|L|)>\<leqslant\>\<Theta\>>
  and <math|c<rprime|'>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\>>, the finite-cutoff bound with
  amplitude <math|\<Theta\>> and decay rate <math|c<rprime|'>>. The latter is
  the following statement at every index <math|i>, for the coarse side
  <math|c=m<rsub|i>> and the forest <math|F<rsub|i>>. The vertical Hessian
  determinant of the tested action is positive along <math|\<rho\><rsub|i>>
  near the origin. The thermal function

  <\equation*>
    \<vartheta\><around|(|x|)>=\<cal-L\><rsub|R>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><rsub|i><around|(|x|)>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>log
    det D<rsub|z><rsup|2>*\<cal-T\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><rsub|i><around|(|x|)>|)>-log
    <big|prod><rsub|b>J<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|i><around|(|x|)><rsub|b>|)>,
  </equation*>

  formed with the loop term <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|R>> of the incoming path,
  the tested action <math|\<cal-T\>> and the Jacobian <math|J> of the
  local-ratio chart, is real-analytic at the origin, and so is the outgoing
  comparison function <math|\<alpha\>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-K\><rsub|2,Y>+\<cal-L\><rsub|Y>>
  built from the quadratic term and the loop term of <math|Y> in the coarse
  clip. For every local choice, every two coarse sites and every two
  polarization amplitudes with complex entries of modulus at most one, the
  second derivatives at the origin of these two functions on the
  corresponding pair of real plane-wave directions differ by at most

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||<math-up|Site><around|(|c|)>|\|>*<space|0.17em>\<Theta\>*<space|0.17em>e<rsup|-c<rprime|'>*c>.
  </equation*>

  The order of the choices matters: the reel, <math|\<kappa\>> and <math|s>
  are chosen before <math|\<tau\>,A>; the numbers <math|d,\<gamma\>,u> and
  the functions <math|\<beta\>,\<Lambda\><rsub|0>> after them; and the path
  <math|Y> and the scalar <math|\<alpha\>> only after the incoming path,
  beds, tower and stationary data are fixed. The proof constructs the
  classical match by reducing the tested action to the new pole test and
  invoking fourth-order exactness of the local response; the exponential
  estimate comes from the funded Fourier ticket and the exponential rate
  comparison below. The bounded path is obtained by adding the blank path to
  the produced row and higher-degree coefficients, then applying the
  sum-budget estimate to each degree separately.

  The intermediate scalar comparison uses the determinant estimate of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-stationary-determinant-prices>, the
  contact cancellation of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-thermal-contact-cancellation>,
  and the alias comparison of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-quadratic-pole-transport>.
  These estimates are applied to the same selected bridge. In particular the
  transverse primal identity allows each nonprincipal nose to be replaced, in
  the annulus pairing, by its horizontal part, which vanishes to second
  order. This replacement, and not a second-order bound on the full nose, is
  the one used below.

  The scalar <math|\<alpha\>> is the local quadratic coefficient of the
  constructed picture. Its normalization is compared with the annular
  coefficient <math|\<alpha\><rsup|<math-up|ann>><rsub|j><around|(|Y<rsub|\<pi\>>|)>>
  by a finite response identity. The response identity uses two determinant
  pictures. The first is the half-medal response minus the reference
  determinant; the second is the half-rank-up response minus the same
  reference determinant. The determinant comparison proves an estimate which
  bounds the difference of their quadratic coefficients by

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||\<alpha\><rsup|<math-up|ann>><rsub|j>(<text|medal>-<text|reference>)-\<alpha\><rsup|<math-up|ann>><rsub|j>(<text|rank-up>-<text|reference><around*|)||\|>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|<math-up|det>>,|\<nobracket\>>
  </equation*>

  where <math|B<rsub|<math-up|det>>> is the displayed finite reserve price
  <math|<math-up|biteCost><around|(|\<eta\>/2,<math-up|reserveCost><around|(|\<cdots\>|)>|)>>.
  The rank-up picture is recorded against the pulled-back pole test at the
  coarse meshes; the annulus picture is recorded against the pole test itself
  at the cofinal fine meshes, with error tending to zero. These are different
  finite-volume tests, so their quadratic coefficients cannot be identified
  by comparing a single test value. The exact comparison is an alias
  argument.

  Let <math|L> be the blocking ratio between the two periods,
  <math|\<mu\>=L<rsup|-1>>, <math|X<around|(|p|)>> the analytic bridge
  between the two period tests, <math|S> the annulus picture, and <math|H>
  the rank-up picture. For a picture <math|P>, write
  <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|P><around|(|p|)>> for its matrix of quadratic test
  coefficients. The finite-mesh recording identities and period-stride
  approximation imply, for every real momentum <math|p>,

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-R\><rsub|H><around|(|p|)>=L<rsup|4>*<big|sum><rsub|\<ell\>\<in\>\<bbb-T\><rsub|L>>\<cal-S\><rsub|S><space|-0.17em><around*|(|<math-up|alias><rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|p|)>,N<rsub|X><around|(|-p,-\<ell\>|)>,N<rsub|X><around|(|p,\<ell\>|)>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|N<rsub|X>> are the bridge's nose maps and
  <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|S>> is the annulus matrix pairing. To prove this
  identity, choose actual mesh momenta <math|p<rsub|i>\<to\>p> and stride
  representatives for each alias. The normalized recording error for the
  annulus test tends to zero; the pulled-back rank-test error also tends to
  zero after its period normalization. The bridge factorization expresses
  each coarse test as the finite sum over its fine aliases. At a fixed pair
  of aliases the recorded mixed coefficient converges to the corresponding
  entry of <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|S>>. If the aliases do not pair as
  <math|\<ell\>=-k>, the two mesh centers remain distinct and the coefficient
  tends to zero. The paired case gives the displayed entry. There are
  finitely many aliases at this fixed period, so the limits pass through the
  sum. This is the content of the finite-mesh transfer; it uses both recorded
  families, not equality of their unsmeared tests.

  The hypotheses used in this transfer are the ones present in the selected
  rank/pole comparison: <math|X> is analytic and has the required uniform
  strip; the same matrix maps satisfy the exact coarse-to-fine premise; both
  metric rows have their stated strip bounds; the fine bed has the required
  floor and sworn conditions and the incoming path is attached to it; the
  rank response is screened; the cofinal recorded errors tend to zero; and
  the period meshes are related by an injective replication map. These
  assumptions give the normalized pulled-back recording and the noise-free
  alias identity. For the Taylor step below, additionally use the exact
  second-bed identity (<math|N<rsub|X><around|(|0,0|)>=\<mu\>*I>), the
  transverse primal identity, and the annulus picture's second-order profile.
  The nonprincipal alias estimate uses the contractibility, strip bounds and
  horizontal-nose estimate proved in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-quadratic-pole-transport>;
  it does not follow from the pole recordings alone.

  The factor <math|L<rsup|4>> is essential. Here <math|L> is the blocking
  ratio between the two periods, not the coarse physical period. Let
  <math|\<ell\>=0> denote the principal alias. The second bed identity fixes
  the zero-momentum bridge nose at <math|N<rsub|X><around|(|0,0|)>=\<mu\>*I>.
  The annulus picture has a second-order Taylor remainder, so its principal
  pairing differs from <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>*\<cal-R\><rsub|S>*<around|(|\<mu\>*p|)>>
  by <math|O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|3>|)>>. For a nonprincipal alias,
  the full nose need not vanish to second order. The high-alias estimate in
  that comparison controls its horizontal and transverse components;
  contractibility then replaces the full nose by its horizontal component
  inside the pairing, without changing that pairing. The transverse primal
  identity and the floor bound make this horizontal representative vanish to
  second order. Only after this replacement does pairing with the annulus
  quadratic profile give an <math|O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|4>|)>>
  contribution. Summing the finitely many aliases therefore gives

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-R\><rsub|H><around|(|p|)>-L<rsup|4>*\<mu\><rsup|2>*\<cal-R\><rsub|S>*<around|(|\<mu\>*p|)>=O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|3>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Both sides are analytic near zero. Their Taylor coefficients through order
  two must agree: along each ray, a nonzero coefficient of degree at most two
  cannot be <math|O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|3>|)>>; polarization
  recovers the full quadratic form. The second derivative of the rescaled
  principal term is <math|L<rsup|4>*\<mu\><rsup|4>*D<rsup|2>*\<cal-R\><rsub|S><around|(|0|)>>,
  and <math|L<rsup|4>*\<mu\><rsup|4>=1>. Thus

  <\equation*>
    \<alpha\><rsup|<math-up|ann>><rsub|j>*<around|(|<text|rank-up>-<text|reference>|)>=\<alpha\><rsup|<math-up|ann>><rsub|j><around|(|Y<rsub|\<pi\>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>-\<alpha\><rsup|<math-up|ann>><rsub|j><around|(|Y<rsub|\<pi\>>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|<math-up|det>>>.
  If the logarithmic coefficient bound from the annulus is <math|D<rsub|0>>,
  then

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<alpha\>+\<lambda\><rsub|G>*log
    L|\|>+1\<leqslant\>B<rsub|<math-up|det>>+D<rsub|0>+1.
  </equation*>

  The path construction chooses <math|d=B<rsub|<math-up|det>>+D<rsub|0>+1>
  (or a larger bound). This is the kinetic normalization step: it controls
  the finite-volume quadratic coefficient by comparison with the continuum
  logarithmic coefficient at the actual annulus scale. No unquantified limit
  in the cutoff is used here.

  The exact order-four match is proved from the fifth-order remainder ticket.
  Let <math|f<around|(|x|)>> be the tested action along the critical chart
  and let <math|g<around|(|x|)>> be the main term of the new path. The
  response construction gives smoothness near zero and a ray estimate
  <math|f*<around|(|t*x|)>-g*<around|(|t*x|)>=O<around|(|<around|\||t|\|><rsup|5>|)>>
  for each direction <math|x>. Taylor's theorem in the single real variable
  <math|t> then forces the coefficients of <math|t<rsup|j>> to agree for
  <math|j=0,1,2,3,4>. Those coefficients are the diagonal values of the
  symmetric <math|j>-linear derivatives at zero. The polarization identity
  for symmetric multilinear forms shows that equality on all diagonals
  implies equality of the full derivatives. This proves
  <math|<math-up|ClassicalMatch>> at every requested order; the input
  critical chart and the price gap are used to obtain the smooth response and
  the response ticket, and are not deduced from the path identity.

  For the path budget, the row component is the bed root array itself. Its
  exponentially weighted row sum is bounded by

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|rowPrice><around|(|s<rprime|'>,M|)>=<around|\||<math-up|PlaqAxes>|\|>*<space|0.17em>M*e<rsup|4*s<rprime|'>>\<leqslant\><math-up|rowPrice><around|(|w,M|)>,<space|2em>s<rprime|'>\<leqslant\>w,
  </equation*>

  because a row entry at offset <math|y> is charged by the bed root strip and
  the walk used to encode the slot has length
  <math|<around|\||y|\|><rsub|1>+4>. The length convention is the following.
  A slot consists of a walk <math|w> on the cover and a plaquette type; its
  length is <math|<around|\||w|\|>+4>, the four counting the edges of its
  plaquette. An atom with <math|r> legs is an <math|r>-tuple of slots, and
  its length is the sum of the lengths of its legs. Thus an atom whose legs
  have walks of total length <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>> has length
  <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>+4*r>, and the weight of an atom at width
  <math|\<sigma\>> is <math|e<rsup|\<sigma\>>> to the power of this length.
  The remaining three components are the loop, cubic, and quartic atom
  families constructed by the response step. Their weighted absolute budgets
  are separately bounded by the three output prices; invariance, zero loop
  jets, and exact metric normalization are preserved by the construction. The
  sum-budget axiom implies each family is summable, and the row-layout
  identity plus the floor bound gives the metric clause. This is the explicit
  content behind the claimed <math|Y.<math-up|Bounds>>, not an appeal to
  pointwise coefficient control.

  The finite-cutoff ticket is an absolute bound on the weighted remainder,
  with original rate <math|k> at least the requested rate <math|s>. If its
  unweighted amplitude is bounded by <math|\<Gamma\>>, and the final
  amplitude budget is <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|1>\<geqslant\>1>, then for every
  cutoff <math|a\<geqslant\>0>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||w*R|\|>*e<rsup|-k*a>\<leqslant\>\<Gamma\>*e<rsup|-k*a>\<leqslant\>\<Gamma\>*e<rsup|-s*a>\<leqslant\>\<Gamma\>*\<Lambda\><rsub|1>*e<rsup|-s*a>.
  </equation*>

  The proof uses only <math|s\<leqslant\>k> and nonnegative amplitudes. This
  is the finite-cutoff exponential-rate comparison used in the one-step
  conclusion.

  The path budget in the one-step construction is built in degree-specific
  steps. First, the blank row path copies the target bed roots; if those
  roots have strip price <math|M>, its row budget is
  <math|<math-up|rowPrice><around|(|w,M|)>=<around|\||<math-up|PlaqAxes>|\|>*M*e<rsup|4*w>>.
  Second, the cubic and quartic coefficients are those of the classical
  action restricted to its stationary graph, that is, of the tested action
  along the critical chart; no determinant enters them. The cubic coefficient
  is the cubic response of the tested action divided by <math|3>!. The
  quartic coefficient is the quartic response, minus the three contractions
  of two cubic responses through the inverse of the vertical Hessian, one for
  each pairing of the four external legs, divided by <math|4>!. This is the
  usual formula for the fourth derivative of a stationary value. If the cubic
  response price is <math|A<rprime|'>>, the quartic response price is
  <math|C<rprime|'>>, and the inverse vertical Hessian is bounded at price
  <math|D<rsub|\<ast\>>>, assume the hypotheses of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-classical-stationary-pictures>, with
  its constants <math|C<rsub|3>,C<rsub|4>,F> equal to
  <math|A<rprime|'>,C<rprime|'>,D<rsub|\<ast\>>> respectively. That
  proposition gives, at its specified strip widths and with its Taylor
  normalization, the price bounds

  <\equation*>
    <frac|A<rprime|'>|3!>,<space|2em><frac|C<rprime|'>+3*A<rprime|'>*D<rsub|\<ast\>>*A<rprime|'>|4!>.
  </equation*>

  The factors <math|3> and <math|3>!, <math|4>! arise from the three pairings
  of four derivatives and the Taylor factorials; each contraction is bounded
  by <math|A<rprime|'>*D<rsub|\<ast\>>*A<rprime|'>>. These coefficients are
  recorded against the third- and fourth-order Taylor coefficients of the
  tested action along the critical chart, respectively. The determinant of
  the vertical Hessian enters only the one-loop thermal function, hence the
  loop correction below. Third, the loop correction is obtained from the
  Fourier coefficient of the quadratic response after subtracting its scalar
  quadratic term. The row path and these three atom families are assembled
  into one path. Its metric identity follows from the row-layout sum and the
  floor condition; loop normalization and invariance are preserved
  coefficientwise. This is why the one-step construction has a separate row
  price and a separate total higher-degree price, rather than charging all
  coefficients to a single norm.

  The final budget invoice in that estimate is elementary but essential. Let
  <math|\<gamma\>\<geqslant\>0>, <math|V,\<Lambda\>\<geqslant\>0>, and
  suppose <math|\<Lambda\>/128\<leqslant\>\<Lambda\><rprime|'>>, the terms
  arising before the annulus correction obey

  <\equation*>
    2*<around*|(|C+<around|(|d+<around|\||y|\|>|)>*V|)>*\<gamma\>\<leqslant\>\<Lambda\><rprime|'>,<space|2em><around|\||x+y|\|>+1\<leqslant\>d,<space|2em>B*\<gamma\>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|256>.
  </equation*>

  Then

  <\equation>
    <around*|(|C+<around|\||x|\|>*V+B*\<Lambda\>|)>*\<gamma\>\<leqslant\>\<Lambda\><rprime|'>.<label|eq:path-cap-invoice>
  </equation>

  Indeed <math|<around|\||x|\|>\<leqslant\>d+<around|\||y|\|>>, by
  <math|x=<around|(|x+y|)>-y>, and

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|C+<around|\||x|\|>*V+B*\<Lambda\>|)>*\<gamma\>\<leqslant\><around*|(|C+<around|(|d+<around|\||y|\|>|)>*V|)>*\<gamma\>+B*\<gamma\>*\<Lambda\>\<leqslant\>\<Lambda\><rprime|'>/2+\<Lambda\><rprime|'>/2.
  </equation*>

  The final term uses <math|B*\<gamma\>\<leqslant\>1/256> and
  <math|\<Lambda\>/128\<leqslant\>\<Lambda\><rprime|'>>, so
  <math|B*\<gamma\>*\<Lambda\>\<leqslant\>\<Lambda\><rprime|'>/2>. This is
  the only step where the auxiliary width of the horizontal picture is traded
  against the outer row budget.

  The exponential cutoff invoice is similarly explicit. If
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|1>\<geqslant\>1> and
  <math|<around|\||w*R|\|>\<leqslant\>\<Gamma\>>, with <math|s\<leqslant\>k>
  and <math|a\<geqslant\>0>, then

  <\equation>
    w*R*e<rsup|-k*a>\<leqslant\>\<Gamma\>*\<Lambda\><rsub|1>*e<rsup|-s*a>.<label|eq:path-cutoff-invoice>
  </equation>

  The bound <math|<around|\||w*R|\|>\<leqslant\>\<Gamma\>> gives
  <math|w*R\<leqslant\>\<Gamma\>>, while <math|e<rsup|-k*a>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-s*a>>;
  multiplying the latter by the nonnegative
  <math|\<Gamma\>*\<Lambda\><rsub|1>> and using
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|1>\<geqslant\>1> proves the claim. This is the rate
  comparison used to preserve a finite-cutoff tail when the controlling rate
  is weakened.

  <subsubsection|Canonical representatives for all-period path
  jets><label|subsec:path-vow-representatives>

  The path realization uses a closed-subspace projection to choose one
  representative of prescribed jets at every tested period. Let <math|H> be a
  real Hilbert space, <math|S\<subset\>H> a closed subspace, and for each
  integer <math|n\<geqslant\>0> let <math|J<rsub|n>:H\<to\>E<rsub|n>> be a
  continuous linear map into a real Hilbert space <math|E<rsub|n>>. Define
  the joint invisible subspace

  <\equation*>
    K=S\<cap\><big|cap><rsub|n\<geqslant\>0>ker
    J<rsub|n>,<space|2em>\<cal-R\>=S\<cap\>K<rsup|\<perp\>>.
  </equation*>

  Both are closed: each kernel is closed, and intersections of closed
  subspaces are closed. Given <math|C\<in\>S>, orthogonally project onto
  <math|K> and put <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>=C-\<Pi\><rsub|K>*C>. Since
  <math|\<Pi\><rsub|K>*C\<in\>K\<subset\>S>, one has
  <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>\<in\>S>; projection orthogonality gives
  <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>\<in\>K<rsup|\<perp\>>>, and hence
  <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>\<in\>\<cal-R\>>. As
  <math|J<rsub|n>*<around|(|\<Pi\><rsub|K>*C|)>=0>, every prescribed jet is
  preserved, <math|J<rsub|n><around|(|C<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>=J<rsub|n><around|(|C|)>>,
  and the Pythagorean identity gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|C<rsub|\<ast\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<around|\<\|\|\>|C|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>-<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Pi\><rsub|K>*C|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|C|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>.

  This representative is unique. If <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>,D<rsub|\<ast\>>\<in\>\<cal-R\>>
  have the same jets for every <math|n>, then
  <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>-D<rsub|\<ast\>>\<in\>S> and all its jets vanish, so
  it lies in <math|K>. It also lies in <math|K<rsup|\<perp\>>>, whence
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|C<rsub|\<ast\>>-D<rsub|\<ast\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=0>
  and <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>=D<rsub|\<ast\>>>. Apply this with the admissible
  correction series as <math|S> and the derivatives at the tested periods as
  the maps <math|J<rsub|n>>. For this application assume the admissible
  subspace is closed; the joint invisible subspace is the common kernel used
  in the orthogonal-complement selection. When the tested side sequence
  exhausts all physical periods, this proves all-period jet injectivity on
  the chosen orthogonal complement. When it does not, the conclusion is
  exactly for the specified test sequence. This is the canonical realization
  argument: it uses the orthogonal complement of the common jet kernel, not
  an assumed injectivity of the jet map on the original space.

  <subsubsection|Changing the forest without changing the stationary
  action><label|subsec:path-forest-independence>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Forest-independent stationary germs><label|lem:path-forest-independence>Fix
    one larger block step and its incoming path and clips. Let
    <math|F,F<rprime|'>> be two allowed forests, and let
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|F>,\<rho\><rsub|F<rprime|'>>> be their critical
    branches with positive vertical Hessians at zero. Their reduced classical
    actions agree as germs of the coarse variables. Their thermal functions
    agree up to a constant. In particular their derivatives of every positive
    order agree on a sufficiently small common neighborhood of zero.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    A configuration near the trivial configuration has a unique rooted gauge
    transformation putting it into the slice of <math|F<rprime|'>>: start
    with the identity at each root and solve successively along its tree
    edges. Each new gauge value is a product of the already determined value
    and the intervening link or its inverse. Thus the gauge transformation is
    analytic near the trivial configuration, fixes the roots, and equals the
    identity there. Applying it to a configuration in the <math|F> slice
    defines an analytic change of vertical coordinates
    <math|z<rprime|'>=\<phi\>*<around|(|x,z|)>>, with the coarse variable
    <math|x> fixed. Repeating the construction with <math|F> gives its
    inverse. Shrinking the neighborhoods if necessary, both transformations
    and their inverses are defined. Rooted gauge invariance of the tested
    action gives

    <\equation*>
      T<rsub|F>*<around|(|x,z|)>=T<rsub|F<rprime|'>>*<around|(|x,\<phi\>*<around|(|x,z|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The same holds for the inherited loop, since each loop atom is invariant.

    The vertical chain rule shows that <math|\<phi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><rsub|F><around|(|x|)>|)>>
    is a critical point for <math|T<rsub|F<rprime|'>>>. It equals zero at
    <math|x=0>. The positive vertical Hessian makes the implicit-function
    branch unique near zero; hence <math|\<phi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><rsub|F><around|(|x|)>|)>=\<rho\><rsub|F<rprime|'>><around|(|x|)>>
    as germs. This proves the assertion about the classical action and
    identifies the stationary representatives before comparing determinants.

    For completeness, the measure on either forest slice is the product Haar
    measure on its free edges. This follows by integrating out the rooted
    vertex gauges in order of tree height: at each stage one replaces a tree
    link by a vertex gauge variable by a left or right multiplication, which
    preserves probability Haar measure. The remaining free links are likewise
    changed only by left and right multiplications. Fubini applies to this
    finite product of probability measures. Applying this calculation first
    to <math|F> and then to <math|F<rprime|'>> shows that the two slice
    measures are the same measure on rooted gauge orbits. In local
    coordinates, if <math|j<rsub|F>,j<rsub|F<rprime|'>>> are their positive
    density factors, the ordinary change-of-variables formula therefore gives

    <\equation*>
      c<rsub|F>*j<rsub|F><around|(|z|)>=c<rsub|F<rprime|'>>*j<rsub|F<rprime|'>>*<around|(|\<phi\>*<around|(|x,z|)>|)>*<around|\||det
      D<rsub|z>*\<phi\>*<around|(|x,z|)>|\|>,
    </equation*>

    where the positive constants <math|c<rsub|F>,c<rsub|F<rprime|'>>> are the
    coordinate normalization constants, independent of <math|x,z>. Equality
    of continuous densities follows from equality of their integrals over all
    compact coordinate sets. The coordinate transformations may depend on
    <math|x>; this does not affect the formula, which is on each fixed coarse
    fiber.

    At the critical point, the second-derivative chain rule has no term from
    <math|D<rsub|z>*T<rsub|F<rprime|'>>>, and so

    <\equation*>
      H<rsub|F>=J<rsup|T>*H<rsub|F<rprime|'>>*J,<space|2em>J=D<rsub|z>*\<phi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><rsub|F><around|(|x|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Both Hessians are positive on a sufficiently small neighborhood. Taking
    determinants, logarithms and the preceding density identity gives

    <\equation*>
      <tfrac|1|2>log det H<rsub|F>-log j<rsub|F><around|(|\<rho\><rsub|F>|)>=<tfrac|1|2>log
      det H<rsub|F<rprime|'>>-log j<rsub|F<rprime|'>><around|(|\<rho\><rsub|F<rprime|'>>|)>+log
      <around|(|c<rsub|F>/c<rsub|F<rprime|'>>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The inherited loop agrees on the two representatives. Adding it proves
    the thermal assertion. In particular the second derivatives used in the
    finite-cutoff comparison do not depend on the forest. No identification
    of vectors in the two different forest coordinate spaces has been made.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Purchasing all components of the one-step
  path><label|subsec:path-one-step-production>

  We now supply the one-step construction stated above. All widths in this
  paragraph are widths in coarse momentum coordinates. The fine strip
  required by the coefficient estimates is obtained by dividing them by
  <math|L>.

  Fix the kernel, the phase, the two bed bounds, the positive floor, the
  header bound and the two direct-coefficient ceilings in the one-step
  interface. The selected bridge identities of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-selected-stationary-linear-data> and the
  weighted frame and penalty estimates give one common positive coarse strip
  and fixed bridge and penalty constants. The weighted inverse construction
  of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-weighted-stationary-inverses> narrows
  that strip and bounds the needed inverse Hessians there. These choices
  depend only on the listed fixed data, and precede the incoming path width
  <math|\<tau\>> and price <math|A>. The all-period bed floor supplies the
  real weighted coercivity in that inverse construction. The forest gap of
  Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:path-stationary-chart> separately
  gives each critical branch; its possibly <math|L>-dependent numerical lower
  bound is not substituted for the uniform weighted coercivity. Take the
  minimum of the finitely many resulting widths and denote a further smaller
  positive width by <math|\<sigma\><rsub|0>>.

  For any subsequently fixed <math|\<tau\>,A>, the residual coefficient
  bounds of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-greyson> have a positive fine
  width and finite prices. Enlarge the block-side floor until
  <math|\<sigma\><rsub|0>/L> lies in that fine width. The classical vertex
  prices are <math|a+L<rsup|-1>*\<ell\>> and <math|b+L<rsup|-1>*r>, where
  <math|a,b> were fixed before <math|\<tau\>,A>, and <math|\<ell\>,r> are
  fixed after them. Enlarge the floor until
  <math|\<ell\>/L,r/L\<leqslant\>1>. Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-classical-stationary-pictures>
  now gives the exact cubic and quartic stationary pictures on one fixed
  coarse strip with the bound <math|M<rsub|\<ast\>>> displayed there. Both
  that strip and <math|M<rsub|\<ast\>>> precede <math|\<tau\>,A>. The data
  used in that proposition are the actual bridge and the actual inverse just
  described, so its three quartic contractions are the ones of the critical
  branch of this step.

  The selected kinetic response and differentiated matrix calculus of
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-selected-kinetic-response> and
  <reference|sec:ex-stationary-determinant-prices> give the determinant
  picture <math|f<rsub|L>> on a fixed smaller coarse strip. Its absolute
  bound <math|\<Phi\><around|(|L|)>> may contain the number
  <math|O<around|(|L<rsup|4>|)>> of fine variables; no bound independent of
  <math|L> is asserted for it. Finite Fourier inversion identifies the
  normalized finite-volume Hessian with the grid mean of this same kernel.
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-pole-recording>, applied entrywise and then to
  the finite color and direction sum, gives

  <\equation*>
    \<varepsilon\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>E<around|(|L|)>*e<rsup|-\<gamma\>*c<rsub|i>>,<space|2em>\<gamma\>\<gtr\>0,
  </equation*>

  where <math|c<rsub|i>> is the coarse side. The common coarse strip fixes
  <math|\<gamma\>> before <math|L>; the amplitude <math|E<around|(|L|)>> may
  depend on <math|L>. The moving Haar identity
  <eqref|eq:ex-thermal-determinant> identifies this picture with the
  determinant and Haar part of the actual stationary thermal function. It is
  therefore not a recording for a different coordinate action.

  For the same incoming path, Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:inherited-loop-picture>
  gives an exactly recorded picture <math|g<rsub|L>> on a fixed coarse strip,
  of order four and bound <math|C<rprime|'>*L<rsup|-2>*\<Lambda\>>. Its
  constant <math|C<rprime|'>> may depend on <math|\<tau\>>, but not on
  <math|L>, <math|\<Lambda\>>, the path or the periods. The extra fine-strip
  condition in that proposition is met by increasing the same block-side
  floor. The classical, determinant, inherited and outgoing bed pictures are
  now all available on one smaller coarse strip independent of
  <math|\<tau\>,A> and <math|L>; only their thresholds and prices have the
  dependence just stated.

  Apply Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:stationary-output-path> to these
  pictures and the outgoing bed. Its coefficient-lift constants and widths
  consequently precede <math|\<tau\>,A>. The scalar is the quadratic
  coefficient <math|\<alpha\>> of <math|f<rsub|L>>. The core\Urank comparison
  has an <math|L>-independent nuclear bound, and the contact identity removes
  the actual\Ucore quadratic difference. Cauchy's estimate on the fixed strip
  bounds the resulting quadratic coefficient difference by a constant
  <math|C<rsub|<math-up|cmp>>> independent of <math|L>.
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-pole-coefficient-transport> identifies
  the rank coefficient with the fine annulus coefficient for this same
  bridge. The annulus estimate of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-annulus-estimate>
  thus gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<alpha\>+\<lambda\><rsub|G>*log
    L|\|>+1\<leqslant\>d\<assign\>D<rsub|0>+C<rsub|<math-up|cmp>>+1.
  </equation*>

  The constant <math|d> is chosen after <math|\<tau\>,A> and before <math|L>,
  as required. In particular <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>d+\<lambda\><rsub|G>*log
  L>.

  Write <math|B<rsub|H>> for the fixed outgoing quadratic-picture bound and
  <math|D> for the coefficient-lift price multiplier, enlarging it also to
  pay for the canonical coefficient realization described below. The loop
  budget before canonical projection is at most

  <\equation*>
    D*<around|{|\<Phi\><around|(|L|)>+<around|(|d+\<lambda\><rsub|G>*log
    L|)>*B<rsub|H>+C<rprime|'>*L<rsup|-2>*\<Lambda\>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Increase the block-side floor once more so that
  <math|D*C<rprime|'>*L<rsup|-2>\<leqslant\>1/256>, and put

  <\equation*>
    \<Lambda\><rsub|0><around|(|L|)>=2*D*<around|{|\<Phi\><around|(|L|)>+<around|(|d+\<lambda\><rsub|G>*log
    L|)>*B<rsub|H>|}>.
  </equation*>

  If <math|\<Lambda\><rprime|'>\<geqslant\>\<Lambda\><rsub|0><around|(|L|)>>
  and <math|\<Lambda\><rprime|'>\<geqslant\>\<Lambda\>/128>, the first two
  terms cost at most <math|\<Lambda\><rprime|'>/2> and the last costs at most
  <math|\<Lambda\>/256\<leqslant\>\<Lambda\><rprime|'>/2>. This proves the
  loop bound with precisely the allowance in the one-step interface. The row,
  cubic and quartic bounds are fixed before <math|\<tau\>,A>, since the row
  price and <math|M<rsub|\<ast\>>> are. Let <math|\<kappa\>> be their
  maximum, including the same realization multiplier. The real-wave expansion
  in <eqref|eq:output-real-wave> changes the recording amplitude only by
  <math|2*<around|(|4*dim \<frak-g\>|)><rsup|2>>; absorb it in
  <math|\<beta\><around|(|L|)>>. The resulting classical match and
  finite-cutoff inequality are exactly those of the one-step interface.

  <subparagraph|Hilbert space of coefficients and canonical representatives.>
  Here is an explicit Hilbert realization of the coefficient arrays, which
  also fixes its choice before the incoming budgets. For each of the three
  degrees use the canonical displacement arrays of the curvature lift,
  together with their finitely many orientation transforms. A slot is encoded
  by its displacement, its direction and color indices and a fixed coordinate
  walk to that displacement. The number of such addresses of length at most
  <math|h> is bounded by a polynomial in <math|1+h>: the displacement lattice
  has fixed finite dimension and the other index sets are finite. All
  coefficient families produced by the lift are supported on these addresses.
  Choose widths <math|0\<less\>s\<less\>\<delta\>\<less\>\<delta\><rprime|'>\<less\>s<rsub|\<ast\>>>,
  where <math|s<rsub|\<ast\>>> is a common width at which the three lifted
  families have their proved absolute budgets. These widths precede
  <math|\<tau\>,A>.

  For an address <math|a> of length <math|<around|\||a|\|>>, give its real
  coefficient space the Hilbert norm

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|c|\<\|\|\>><rsub|H>=Z<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|a>e<rsup|2*\<delta\><around|\||a|\|>><around|\||c<rsub|a>|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|1/2>,<space|2em>Z=<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|a>e<rsup|-2*<around|(|\<delta\>-s|)><around|\||a|\|>>|)><rsup|1/2>\<less\>\<infty\>.
  </equation*>

  Finite-dimensional coefficient tensor norms are included in the address
  set. The encoding is the coefficient array itself. Cauchy\USchwarz gives

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|a>e<rsup|s<around|\||a|\|>><around|\||c<rsub|a>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|c|\<\|\|\>><rsub|H>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|c|\<\|\|\>><rsub|H>\<leqslant\>Z*<big|sum><rsub|a>e<rsup|\<delta\><rprime|'><around|\||a|\|>><around|\||c<rsub|a>|\|>.
  </equation*>

  The second inequality uses <math|\<delta\>\<less\>\<delta\><rprime|'>> and
  <math|\<ell\><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsup|1>>. Thus a fixed enlargement
  of the previously obtained lift constants pays for the Hilbert norm,
  whereas a Hilbert norm bound implies the desired path budget at width
  <math|s>. The same construction with an intermediate width in place of
  <math|s> makes the encoding width strictly larger than the final path
  width. This is the reel required in the interface.

  The real, invariant and moment constraints define a closed real linear
  subspace of this Hilbert space. Reality and invariance are intersections of
  kernels of continuous coordinate functionals. The zero loop coefficient and
  first moment are continuous sums, by Cauchy\USchwarz with the extra
  exponential margin. At a fixed finite torus and fixed Taylor order, the
  realization map is also continuous: derivatives of a fixed-order product of
  transported plaquettes grow at most polynomially in its walk lengths, and
  that polynomial is absorbed by the same exponential margin. Consequently
  the invisible space for all requested jets at all the chosen periods is
  closed. Project as in Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:path-vow-representatives>.
  The projection preserves the real invariance and moment constraints and
  every recorded jet, and decreases the Hilbert norm. For cubic atoms keep
  both the third and fourth jets in this projection, for quartic atoms the
  fourth jet, and for loops the second jet. Thus the same classical match and
  the same thermal comparison survive simultaneously; changing the cubic
  fourth jet would not have sufficed. All norm multipliers have been included
  in <math|D> and <math|\<kappa\>> above before choosing the last block-side
  floor. This supplies the canonical representatives with the exact budgets
  claimed in the one-step interface.

  <subparagraph|The terminal path.> At a terminal bed the tested quadratic
  kinetic form is the Wilson kinetic form: its unamended diagonal is the
  identity, and the amendment identity <eqref|eq:positive-amendment> gives
  <math|G*d<rsub|1>=H*d<rsub|1>=d<rsub|1>>. Thus the amended metric acts as
  the identity on exact curvatures, although it need not be the identity on
  all two-forms. Take its root row as the row component of the path and
  denote its quadratic action, including the direct clip term, by <math|J>.
  The floor and sworn identities and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-soft-quadratic-hessian>
  identify <math|D<rsup|2>*J<around|(|0|)>> with this kinetic form. Thus the
  Wilson action <math|W> and <math|J> have the same value, first derivative
  and Hessian. The Wilson coefficient pictures and the row-quadratic pictures
  supply exact common third- and fourth-order pictures of <math|W-J>, with a
  width and bound depending only on the terminal stage's root bound and
  direct-coefficient ceiling. Apply the leading-removal construction of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:stationary-output-construction> first at
  degree three and then at degree four. In the second step subtract the
  fourth coefficient of the cubic atoms as well. It gives cubic and quartic
  invariant arrays with <math|W-J-S<rsub|3>-S<rsub|4>=O<around|(|<around|\||x|\|><rsup|5>|)>>
  at every tested period. Give the path these arrays and zero loop
  coefficients. It is attached to the terminal bed, its four path budgets
  hold below one fixed positive width and above one fixed stage price, and
  polarization gives equality of every derivative through order four. No
  period-dependent selection or limiting argument enters this terminal
  construction.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Production of the one-step and terminal
    paths><label|prop:path-one-step-production>Under the bed, tower, floor,
    attachment and header hypotheses of the one-step interface, the annulus
    estimate supplies all its conclusions, with the stated order of choices
    and the loop contraction <math|\<Lambda\>/128>. The terminal identity bed
    has the terminal base path required in (B) below. The classical match and
    thermal Hessian estimate hold for every allowed forest.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The preceding construction verifies each input of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:stationary-output-path> on the same
    beds, bridge, critical branches and torus sequence. Its price bounds, the
    explicit split of the loop allowance, and the canonical projection give
    the one-step path with all the required norms. The terminal construction
    gives the base path. For each period choose any allowed forest to make
    the output pictures. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:path-forest-independence>
    transfers both matching assertions to every other forest, without
    changing the output path or scalar. This proves the proposition.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The arbitrary-input factor-64
  envelope><label|subsec:path-factor64-envelope>

  The numerical factor used by the selected global caller is an allowance
  amplification at the input to the transition supplier. We give both layers
  of the finite reindexing, since the two maxima/minima use different pairs.

  For each fixed output index <math|i>, the one-step theorem is first called
  at both neighboring input indices <math|j=i> and <math|j=i+1>. If the two
  calls return widths <math|w<rsub|i,i>,w<rsub|i+1,i>> and row/cubic/quartic
  incoming-price thresholds <math|v<rsub|i,i>,v<rsub|i+1,i>>, the common
  parameters are

  <\equation*>
    s<rsub|i>=min <around|(|b<rsub|i>,w<rsub|i,i>,w<rsub|i+1,i>|)>,<space|2em>A<rsub|i>=max
    <around|(|E<rsub|i>,v<rsub|i,i>,v<rsub|i+1,i>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|b<rsub|i>,E<rsub|i>> are the width and price supplied by the
  blank-path estimate. For each of the two calls, <math|s<rsub|i>> is no
  larger than its width and its price threshold is no larger than
  <math|A<rsub|i>>, so the same pair of finite-volume estimates can be used
  at either adjacent input scale. The flux theorem is universal in the
  admissible incoming path, bed, tower, armada, and chart; therefore this
  uniformization does not choose a favorable incoming object.

  Next fix the requested positive width sequence <math|\<tau\><rsub|j>> and
  nonnegative incoming path-price allowance <math|C<rsub|j>>. For each fixed
  input index <math|j>, the one-step calls indexed by the two possible output
  indices <math|i=j> and <math|i=j-1> are unified by a second set of extrema:
  take the maximum of their drift bounds, period thresholds, and
  side-dependent cutoff amplitudes, and the minimum of their positive decay
  rates. At <math|j=0>, the predecessor is interpreted as <math|0>, so the
  single available call is used twice. These choices make one set of output
  data valid whether the selected transition uses the same-scale receipt or
  the preceding-scale receipt. This is the second neighbor reindexing in the
  construction; it is distinct from the width/price extrema at fixed output
  index above.

  In the public application, the arbitrary allowance sequence
  <math|C<rsub|j>> is passed as <math|64*A<rprime|'><rsub|j>>, where
  <math|A<rprime|'><rsub|j>> is any nonnegative sequence dominating the base
  path-price sequence. Thus the one-step conclusion is established for an
  incoming path budget <math|64*A<rprime|'><rsub|j>>; the factor 64 is part
  of the input allowance, not an output estimate obtained from a smaller
  path. At the selected level, <math|A<rprime|'><rsub|j>> is the profile path
  price, so the row, cubic, and quartic incoming budgets are
  <math|64<space|0.17em><math-up|paths><rsub|j>>. The loop halo is separately
  defined with budget <math|64<space|0.17em><math-up|loopCap><rsub|j>>. The
  selected schedule must satisfy the following loop charges used by the
  transition. For the transition from level <math|m+1> to level <math|m>, let
  <math|j=J<rsub|m+1>> be the stage of the incoming level and
  <math|L<rsub|j>> the block side selected at stage <math|j>; the stage of
  the output level is <math|J<rsub|m>\<in\><around|{|j,j-1|}>>. The
  side-dependent threshold <math|W<rsub|j>> of the one-step construction at
  stage <math|j>, namely the function <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|0>> of its
  interface, and the loop capacities satisfy

  <\equation*>
    W<rsub|j><around|(|L<rsub|j>|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|loopCap><rsub|m>,<space|2em><frac|64<space|0.17em><math-up|loopCap><rsub|m+1>|128>=<frac|<math-up|loopCap><rsub|m+1>|2>\<leqslant\><math-up|loopCap><rsub|m>,<space|2em>1\<leqslant\><math-up|loopCap><rsub|m>.
  </equation*>

  Thus the threshold is indexed by the incoming stage and the capacity by the
  output level. These three inequalities are properties of the scale
  schedule; they are inputs here. An incoming path with ordinary profile
  bounds already satisfies the halo bounds by componentwise monotonicity,
  since each budget is nonnegative and <math|1\<leqslant\>64>. That
  monotonicity only converts an already constructed path; the arbitrary-input
  transition itself comes from the pairwise flux calls and the two finite
  extrema operations. Its horizontal receipt and local response prices remain
  the explicit inputs described in the one-step section.

  <subsubsection|From adjacent one-step paths to one finite history>

  Let <math|a> be the scale schedule, and write
  <math|J<rsub|m>=a.<math-up|clock>.*J<around|(|m|)>>. Its slow-step property
  gives

  <\equation*>
    J<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|m+1>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|m>+1.
  </equation*>

  Suppose that for every pair <math|<around|(|j,i|)>> with <math|j=i> or
  <math|j=i+1> the one-step receipt is available, with width
  <math|w<rsub|j,i>\<gtr\>0>, bound <math|A<rsub|j,i>>, and path
  <math|Y<rsub|j,i>>. Define the common scale width and bound by

  <\equation*>
    s<rsub|i>=min <around|{|b<rsub|i>,w<rsub|i,i>,w<rsub|i+1,i>|}>,<space|2em>A<rsub|i>=max
    <around|{|E<rsub|i>,A<rsub|i,i>,A<rsub|i+1,i>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Taking minima and maxima preserves positivity and nonnegativity. At either
  adjacent index <math|j\<in\><around|{|i,i+1|}>>, one has
  <math|s<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>w<rsub|j,i>> and
  <math|A<rsub|j,i>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|i>>. Thus both adjacent-stage one-step
  constructions meet the common width and price requirements of output stage
  <math|i>. For each actual transition the construction of the corresponding
  stage pair is applied to its fixed incoming path and quadratic data. The
  extrema unify the admissible parameters; they do not assert that the paths
  returned by the two constructions coincide. The common price is the maximum
  of the two one-step prices, and the common cutoff rate is the minimum of
  the two one-step rates. This finite neighbor maximum/minimum is the entire
  unification step in the global construction; no limit in the scale index is
  taken here.

  The next finite construction is where attached paths are assembled. Branch
  rows and admissible paths are defined in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:path-admissible>, and the transition rule on
  the actual history is constructed in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:path-retained-plan>.
  Assume that for each terminal index <math|N> there is a branch row
  <math|R<rsub|N>>, and for each <math|m\<less\>N> there is a transition rule
  which, from any admissible path at <math|m+1>, returns a path at <math|m>,
  an amplitude <math|\<alpha\><rsub|m>>, and a stationary coefficient
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|m>>, together with a bubble identity and the wing bound.
  Then for each fixed <math|N> one constructs rows <math|Y<rsub|m>> for
  <math|0\<leqslant\>m\<leqslant\>N> by finite downward induction. Set
  <math|Y<rsub|N>=R<rsub|N>>. Given <math|Y<rsub|m+1>>, apply the transition
  rule to obtain <math|Y<rsub|m>> and its amplitude/stationary data.
  Induction preserves admissibility at the next stage because the previous
  step furnished exactly the attachment and bound required as input. The
  resulting finite table has all adjacent bubble identities and all wing
  bounds.

  Doing this separately for each <math|N> defines a single horizon of paths:
  its <math|N>th column is the finite table just constructed. The terminal
  rows give the branch-row property, the step outputs give the adjacent
  bubbles, and the one-step row identities give the global row ties and path
  bounds. Hence one obtains one finite-history path object satisfying all
  finite-cutoff path conditions, with every cutoff retained on the same
  horizon. This argument is not a compactness extraction and does not choose
  a different horizon at different cutoffs. It is the finite descending
  construction furnished by the transition rule.

  <subsubsection|The stationary plan on the retained
  horizon><label|subsec:path-retained-plan>

  The finite descent above is conditional on a branch row and a transition
  rule. We now state what these are on the actual history and how the
  transition rule is obtained there. This is the statement consumed by the
  later sections; its three analytic inputs are named explicitly.

  Fix the scale schedule <math|a>. For a level <math|m> write
  <math|J<rsub|m>> for its stage, <math|p<rsub|m>,\<sigma\><rsub|m>,A<rsub|m>>
  for the clip, width and path allowance of the stage profile,
  <math|<math-up|loopCap><rsub|m>> for the loop capacity, <math|L<rsub|m>>
  for the block side from level <math|m+1> to level <math|m>, and
  <math|n<rsub|m,t>> for the torus side at level <math|m> and period index
  <math|t>. A horizon <math|V> assigns a bed <math|V.*H*<around|(|N,m|)>> to
  <math|m\<leqslant\>N> and a tower <math|V.*T*<around|(|N,m|)>> to
  <math|m\<less\>N>. Fix also a local-ratio chart <math|O> and post costs:
  positive numbers <math|\<eta\><rsub|j>,\<Gamma\><rsub|j>,c<rsub|j>> for
  each stage <math|j>.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Admissible paths and branch rows><label|def:path-admissible>A path
    <math|R> is admissible at <math|<around|(|N,m|)>>, <math|m\<leqslant\>N>,
    if its row component is the root row of the bed
    <math|V.*H*<around|(|N,m|)>> in the walk encoding of the row-price
    estimate above, if <math|<math-up|Attached><around|(|V.*H*<around|(|N,m|)>,R|)>>,
    and if <math|R.<math-up|Bounds><around|(|p<rsub|m>,\<sigma\><rsub|m>,A<rsub|m>,<math-up|loopCap><rsub|m>|)>>.
    A branch row at <math|N> is a path <math|R>, admissible at
    <math|<around|(|N,N|)>>, such that at every period the main action of
    <math|R> in the clip <math|p<rsub|N>> and the Wilson action have equal
    derivatives of every order <math|j\<leqslant\>4> at the identity field,
    and such that every loop coefficient of <math|R> vanishes.
  </definition>

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Stationary plan><label|def:path-plan>A stationary plan on <math|V>
    consists of paths <math|R<rsub|N,m>> for <math|m\<leqslant\>N>, numbers
    <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>> for <math|m\<less\>N>, and maps
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|N,m><rsup|t,F>> for <math|m\<less\>N>, every period
    index <math|t> and every allowed forest <math|F> of the one-step datum
    from level <math|m+1> to level <math|m> at period <math|t>. It is planned
    at the given post costs if the following hold for all <math|N>.

    <\enumerate>
      <item>For <math|m\<leqslant\>N> the path <math|R<rsub|N,m>> is attached
      to <math|V.*H*<around|(|N,m|)>> and satisfies

      <\equation*>
        R<rsub|N,m>.<math-up|Bounds><around|(|p<rsub|m>,\<sigma\><rsub|m>,A<rsub|m>,<math-up|loopCap><rsub|m>|)>.
      </equation*>

      <item>The terminal path <math|R<rsub|N,N>> has the Wilson derivatives
      of every order <math|j\<leqslant\>4> at every period, and all its loop
      coefficients vanish.

      <item>For <math|m\<less\>N> and all <math|t,F>, with
      <math|\<rho\>=\<rho\><rsub|N,m><rsup|t,F>> and <math|j=J<rsub|m+1>>:

      <\equation*>
        <math-up|PriceGap><around|(|F,p<rsub|m+1>,R<rsub|N,m+1>,\<eta\><rsub|j>|)>,<space|1em><math-up|Critical><around|(|F,p<rsub|m+1>,R<rsub|N,m+1>,\<rho\>|)>,<space|1em><math-up|Guessed><around|(|F,W,\<rho\>|)>,
      </equation*>

      where <math|W> is the installed matrix of the tower
      <math|V.*T*<around|(|N,m|)>>, and

      <\equation*>
        <math-up|ClassicalMatch><around|(|F,p<rsub|m+1>,p<rsub|m>,R<rsub|N,m+1>,R<rsub|N,m>,\<rho\>|)>.
      </equation*>

      <item>For <math|m\<less\>N>, <math|<around|\||\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>+\<lambda\><rsub|G>*log
      L<rsub|m>|\|>+1> is at most the drift allowance of stage
      <math|J<rsub|m+1>>.

      <item>For <math|m\<less\>N> and all <math|t,F>, the finite-cutoff bound
      of the one-step interface holds for the incoming path
      <math|R<rsub|N,m+1>>, the outgoing path <math|R<rsub|N,m>>, the scalar
      <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>>, the chart <math|O> and the map
      <math|\<rho\><rsub|N,m><rsup|t,F>>, with the bound
      <math|<around|\||<math-up|Site><around|(|n<rsub|m,t>|)>|\|>*<space|0.17em>\<Gamma\><rsub|j><space|0.17em><math-up|loopCap><rsub|m+1><space|0.17em>e<rsup|-c<rsub|j>*n<rsub|m,t>>>,
      <math|j=J<rsub|m+1>>.
    </enumerate>
  </definition>

  The paths of a plan are used twice: <math|R<rsub|N,m+1>> is the incoming
  path of the step to level <math|m> and the outgoing path of the step from
  level <math|m+2>. It is one path with one choice of coefficient arrays.

  The proposition below uses the following three constructions. Their
  producers are given explicitly; the quadratic horizon is constructed in its
  own chapter.

  <\itemize>
    <item*|(A)><em|The annulus estimate.>
    <math|<math-up|Ann><around|(|G,\<sigma\>,\<varepsilon\>,C,Z,\<tau\>,A|)>>
    holds for all <math|\<sigma\>,\<varepsilon\>,\<tau\>\<gtr\>0> and
    <math|C,Z,A\<geqslant\>0>. Its producer is
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-annulus-estimate>.

    <item*|(B)><em|The one-step construction> with the interface stated in
    Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:path-stationary-chart>, for every
    stage pair <math|<around|(|j,i|)>> with <math|j=i> or <math|j=i+1>, and
    the terminal base path: for every stage, every bed with the root strip
    bound, the floors and sworn conditions of that stage and with diagonal
    equal to the identity at the tested periods, and every clip within the
    stage's direct bound, there is a path whose row is the root row of the
    bed, which is attached to the bed, has zero loop coefficients, satisfies
    the path bounds at every width below and every price above fixed stage
    values, and has the Wilson derivatives of orders <math|j\<leqslant\>4> at
    the tested periods. The one-step and terminal constructions are proved in
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:path-one-step-production>; forest
    independence is Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:path-forest-independence>.

    <item*|(C)><em|The quadratic horizons.> A free horizon <math|X> and a
    retained horizon <math|V> on the schedule <math|a>, and kernel prices
    <math|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|j>,\<Omega\><rsub|j>,\<eta\><rsup|0><rsub|j>|)>>,
    such that <math|V> has the same beds as <math|X> and, for
    <math|m\<less\>N>, the same installed column and the same transport
    column in its towers; and such that <math|X> satisfies, for
    <math|m\<leqslant\>N>: the metric and root strip bounds of
    <math|X.*H*<around|(|N,m|)>> at width <math|\<alpha\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>
    and price <math|\<Omega\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>; the floor of
    <math|X.*H*<around|(|N,m|)>> in the clip <math|p<rsub|m>> with constant
    <math|\<eta\><rsup|0><rsub|J<rsub|m>>> at every torus side; the sworn
    condition at every torus side; the identity
    <math|<math-up|diag><space|0.17em>X.*H*<around|(|N,N|)>=1> at every side;
    and for <math|m\<less\>N> the header bounds of the tower
    <math|X.*T*<around|(|N,m|)>> and the diagonal two-scale trial of
    <math|X.*H*<around|(|N,m+1|)>>, <math|X.*H*<around|(|N,m|)>>,
    <math|X.*T*<around|(|N,m|)>> at every period.
  </itemize>

  The conditions on <math|X> in (C) are the bed and flight receipts of the
  quadratic construction of Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-lattice-horizon-producer>.
  The retained horizon is formed from <math|X> in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history>, where <math|X> and
  <math|V> are called <math|M<rsup|0>> and <math|M<rsup|1>>. Convergence
  along the cutoff is not a consequence of (C): the conditions there do not
  determine the remaining tower blocks. For the particular horizons
  constructed jointly with their curvature roots it is proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:free-horizon-convergence>; the present
  proposition does not use it.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Stationary plan on the retained
    horizon><label|prop:path-retained-plan>Assume the gauge group is simple
    and (A), (B) hold. Fix a kernel choice, kernel prices, and bounds
    <math|Z<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>0> for the direct clip coefficients of the
    stages. There are stage widths <math|<wide|\<sigma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|j>\<gtr\>0>
    and stage path prices <math|<wide|A|\<bar\>><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>0> such
    that, for all stage widths <math|0\<less\>\<sigma\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\><wide|\<sigma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|j>>
    and path allowances <math|A<rsub|j>\<geqslant\><wide|A|\<bar\>><rsub|j>>,
    there are drift bounds <math|d<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>0>, rates
    <math|\<gamma\><rsub|j>\<gtr\>0> and block-side floors <math|u<rsub|j>>
    with the following property. Let the stage profiles have these widths and
    path allowances, drift allowances at least <math|d<rsub|j>>, block-side
    floors at least <math|u<rsub|j>> and direct clip coefficients at most
    <math|Z<rsub|j>>. Then, once the block sides are chosen, there are
    positive gaps <math|\<eta\><rsub|j>> and volume thresholds, and once the
    protection data are chosen there are entry delays, such that for every
    paid schedule <math|a> beyond these thresholds and delays, every post
    costs with <math|\<eta\><rsub|j>> the given gaps,
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1> and
    <math|c<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|j>>, all horizons <math|X,V> as
    in (C) and every local-ratio chart <math|O>, the following hold.

    <\enumerate>
      <item>There is a stationary plan <math|<around|(|R<rsub|N,m>,\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>,\<rho\><rsub|N,m><rsup|t,F>|)>>
      on <math|V>, planned at the post costs in the sense of
      Definition<nbsp><reference|def:path-plan>. Every <math|R<rsub|N,m>> is
      admissible at <math|<around|(|N,m|)>>; in particular its row is the
      root row of <math|V.*H*<around|(|N,m|)>>. The finite-cutoff bound holds
      even with the factor <math|<math-up|loopCap><rsub|m+1>> replaced by
      one.

      <item>For every level <math|m> there is a reel, fixed before the cutoff
      and the period, whose three encoding widths are strictly larger than
      <math|\<sigma\><rsub|m>>, such that for all <math|N\<gtr\>m> the loop,
      cubic and quartic arrays of <math|R<rsub|N,m>> are the encodings of
      elements of its three canonical coefficient subspaces for the side
      sequence <math|t\<mapsto\>n<rsub|m,t>>, of norms at most
      <math|<math-up|loopCap><rsub|m>>, <math|A<rsub|m>>, <math|A<rsub|m>>
      respectively.

      <item><em|Extended domain.> Let <math|m\<less\>N> and let <math|R> be
      any path attached to <math|V.*H*<around|(|N,m+1|)>> with

      <\equation*>
        R.<math-up|Bounds><around|(|p<rsub|m+1>,\<sigma\><rsub|m+1>,64*A<rsub|m+1>,64<space|0.17em><math-up|loopCap><rsub|m+1>|)>.
      </equation*>

      Then there are a path <math|D> admissible at <math|<around|(|N,m|)>>, a
      scalar and stationary maps satisfying items (3)\U(5) of
      Definition<nbsp><reference|def:path-plan> with <math|R> as incoming and
      <math|D> as outgoing path, and the arrays of <math|D> lie in the
      canonical subspaces of the same reel with the same norm bounds as in
      the previous item.

      <item>For every level <math|m> there are
      <math|v<rsub|m>\<gtr\>\<sigma\><rsub|m>> and <math|C<rsub|m>> such that
      the root row of <math|V.*H*<around|(|N,m|)>> has the strip bound at
      width <math|v<rsub|m>> and price <math|C<rsub|m>> for all sufficiently
      large <math|N>.
    </enumerate>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The stage widths and prices are those of
    Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:path-factor64-envelope>. Let
    <math|b<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> and <math|E<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0> be the width
    ceiling and price floor of the terminal base path of (B) at stage
    <math|i>, and let <math|w<rsub|j,i>,v<rsub|j,i>> be the widths and price
    thresholds of the one-step constructions for the stage pairs
    <math|j\<in\><around|{|i,i+1|}>>. As there, put <math|s<rsub|i>=min
    <around|(|b<rsub|i>,w<rsub|i,i>,w<rsub|i+1,i>|)>> and <math|A<rsub|i>=max
    <around|(|E<rsub|i>,v<rsub|i,i>,v<rsub|i+1,i>|)>>, and take
    <math|<wide|\<sigma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|i>=min
    <around|(|s<rsub|i>,\<alpha\><rsub|i>/2|)>> and
    <math|<wide|A|\<bar\>><rsub|i>=A<rsub|i>>. Thus every admitted width is
    at most <math|b<rsub|i>> and every admitted allowance at least
    <math|E<rsub|i>>, as the terminal rows require; and they are at most
    <math|w<rsub|j,i>> and at least <math|v<rsub|j,i>> respectively, as the
    transitions require. The one-step constructions are called with incoming
    allowance <math|64*A<rsub|j>>, which gives <math|d<rsub|j>,u<rsub|j>>,
    rates <math|\<gamma\><rprime|'><rsub|j>\<gtr\>0> and the functions
    <math|\<beta\><rsub|j>,\<Lambda\><rsub|0,j>> of the block side; the rates
    of the statement are <math|\<gamma\><rsub|j>=\<gamma\><rprime|'><rsub|j>/2>.
    The gaps <math|\<eta\><rsub|j>> are those of
    Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:path-stationary-chart> for the block
    side <math|L<rsub|j>>, the phase <math|j>, the kernel and the floor
    constant <math|\<eta\><rsup|0><rsub|j>>. The volume threshold at stage
    <math|j> is the maximum of <math|100*<around|(|j+7|)>> and of a coarse
    side beyond which <math|\<beta\><rsub|j><around|(|L<rsub|j>|)>*e<rsup|-\<gamma\><rprime|'><rsub|j>*n>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<gamma\><rsub|j>*n>>.
    The entry delays are those beyond which the three loop-capacity
    inequalities of Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:path-factor64-envelope>
    hold.

    <em|Terminal rows.> By (C) the bed <math|V.*H*<around|(|N,N|)>=X.*H*<around|(|N,N|)>>
    has the root strip bound, floors, sworn condition and identity diagonal.
    The base path of (B) at stage <math|J<rsub|N>>, taken at width
    <math|\<sigma\><rsub|N>>, price <math|A<rsub|N>> and loop allowance
    <math|<math-up|loopCap><rsub|N>\<geqslant\>1>, is therefore a branch row
    at <math|N>.

    <em|The transition rule.> Let <math|m\<less\>N>, put <math|j=J<rsub|m+1>>
    and <math|i=J<rsub|m>>, so <math|j\<in\><around|{|i,i+1|}>>, and let
    <math|R> be attached to the fine bed <math|B=V.*H*<around|(|N,m+1|)>>
    with the factor-<math|64> bounds of item (3). We verify the hypotheses of
    the one-step construction of the stage pair <math|<around|(|j,i|)>> for
    the fine bed <math|B>, the coarse bed <math|T=V.*H*<around|(|N,m|)>>, the
    tower <math|V.*T*<around|(|N,m|)>>, the fine and coarse clips
    <math|p<rsub|m+1>,p<rsub|m>>, and the one-step data at all period indices
    <math|t>, whose coarse and fine sides are <math|n<rsub|m,t>> and
    <math|n<rsub|m+1,t>>. The paid schedule gives that the block side is
    <math|L<rsub|j>>, the phase is <math|j>, the block side is at least
    <math|u<rsub|j>>, and the sides <math|n<rsub|m,t>> tend to infinity with
    <math|t>. The volume threshold makes every one-step datum larger. The two
    bed hulls, namely the metric and root strip bounds, all floors and all
    sworn conditions of <math|B> and of <math|T>, are (C) at levels
    <math|m+1> and <math|m>; here the beds of <math|V> are those of <math|X>.
    The header bounds and the diagonal two-scale trial at every period are
    (C) as well, since they involve only the beds and the installed and
    transport columns, which <math|V> and <math|X> share. For every <math|t>
    and every forest <math|F>, the chart receipts of
    Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:path-stationary-chart>, applied to
    <math|B>, <math|T>, the tower and <math|R>, give
    <math|<math-up|PriceGap><around|(|F,p<rsub|m+1>,R,\<eta\><rsub|j>|)>> and
    a map <math|\<rho\><rsup|t,F>> with the critical, guessed and heat
    properties; their hypotheses are the diagonal trial, the floor of
    <math|B>, the bounds and attachment of <math|R>, the sworn condition of
    <math|B> and the floor of <math|T>. Choose one forest <math|F<rsub|t>>
    for each <math|t>. All hypotheses of the one-step interface now hold with
    <math|\<Lambda\>=64<space|0.17em><math-up|loopCap><rsub|m+1>\<geqslant\>1>.
    It returns a path <math|D> and a scalar <math|\<alpha\>> with
    <math|D.<math-up|row>> the root row of <math|T> and
    <math|<math-up|Attached><around|(|T,D|)>>. By the choice of the stage
    widths and prices and the first two loop-capacity inequalities, the
    conditions <math|\<sigma\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|i>>,
    <math|\<kappa\>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|m>>,
    <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|0,j><around|(|L<rsub|j>|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|loopCap><rsub|m>>
    and <math|64<space|0.17em><math-up|loopCap><rsub|m+1>/128\<leqslant\><math-up|loopCap><rsub|m>>
    hold, so <math|D> satisfies <math|D.<math-up|Bounds><around|(|p<rsub|m>,\<sigma\><rsub|m>,A<rsub|m>,<math-up|loopCap><rsub|m>|)>>
    and has the canonical representatives of item (2). Hence <math|D> is
    admissible at <math|<around|(|N,m|)>>. The scalar satisfies the drift
    bound because <math|d<rsub|j>> is at most the drift allowance.

    The one-step construction gives the classical match and the finite-cutoff
    bound only for the chosen forests <math|F<rsub|t>>. They hold for every
    forest by the forest-independence of the stationary data: for two allowed
    forests <math|F,F<rprime|'>> of a larger one-step datum, with critical
    maps <math|\<rho\>,\<rho\><rprime|'>> and positive gaps for the same
    path, the tested actions along <math|\<rho\>> and along
    <math|\<rho\><rprime|'>> agree near the origin for every local choice,
    and the two thermal functions differ by a constant near the origin. The
    first statement transfers the classical match, whose right side does not
    involve the forest. The second transfers the finite-cutoff bound, which
    concerns second derivatives; positivity of the determinant and
    analyticity of the thermal function hold for every forest by the gap.
    This is precisely Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:path-forest-independence>.
    Finally the amplitude <math|\<beta\><rsub|j><around|(|L<rsub|j>|)>> and
    rate <math|\<gamma\><rprime|'><rsub|j>> are replaced by
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1> and
    <math|c<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|j>> using the volume threshold.
    This proves item (3), with the factor one in the finite-cutoff bound.

    <em|Assembly.> An admissible path at <math|<around|(|N,m+1|)>> satisfies
    the factor-<math|64> bounds, since all budgets are nonnegative and
    <math|<math-up|loopCap><rsub|m+1>\<geqslant\>1>. The finite downward
    induction of the preceding subsection, started from the branch row and
    using the transition rule just constructed, gives for each <math|N> the
    paths <math|R<rsub|N,m>>, scalars and maps of item (1) together with item
    (2). The conditions of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:path-plan> are the
    recorded outputs of the steps; the finite-cutoff bound is weakened by the
    factor <math|<math-up|loopCap><rsub|m+1>\<geqslant\>1>. Item (4) holds
    with <math|v<rsub|m>=\<alpha\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>> and
    <math|C<rsub|m>=\<Omega\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>, by the root strip bound in
    (C) and <math|\<sigma\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>\<alpha\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>/2\<less\>\<alpha\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>;
    it holds for every <math|N\<gtr\>m>.
  </proof>

  The proposition produces, on the retained horizon itself, the attached
  paths with their bounds, and consumes the floors, sworn conditions, metric
  strips and clip bounds of that same horizon. It is a statement about global
  path attachment. It does not by itself give the local two-bed statement
  used in the nonlinear step, which also requires the roll identities and is
  constructed from the same horizon and paths in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-selected-course-binding>.

  <subsubsection|Selected cutoff limits>

  The ten retained bed and tower row families must converge on the lattice
  cover along the actual terminal cutoff sequence.
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:free-horizon-convergence> proves this for
  the recursively selected free horizon, using its uniform strip bounds,
  positive root choices and committed transition identities. Its proof
  includes the high-alias and noise limits, the exterior lifts and
  amendments, and recovery of cover coefficients from the unbounded selected
  period sequence. Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-history-limits>
  then applies these limits to the same stationary construction and shows
  that the terminal Wilson normalization preserves every fixed-scale limit.

  <subsubsection|Dependency order>

  The annulus estimate is supplied by the common response and the radial
  Fourier estimates. The one-step construction above then combines the
  classical pictures, the determinant and inherited-loop pictures, and the
  curvature coefficient lift; the terminal path is constructed independently
  from the Wilson jets. Only afterwards does the retained-horizon proposition
  perform its finite downward induction. The quadratic horizon and its cutoff
  convergence remain the separate constructions of
  Sections<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-lattice-horizon-producer> and
  <reference|sec:free-horizon-convergence>. No convergence of the nonlinear
  paths is used to produce their finite-cutoff attachment or bounds. Their
  later limits use the canonical all-period representatives and the separate
  argument of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-history-limits>.

  <section|Coupling selection and the Wilson trajectory>

  <subsection|Uniqueness of the limiting background
  paths><label|sec:background-path-convergence>

  The path coefficients require their own convergence argument. Uniform
  bounds give convergent subsequences, but do not identify their limits. The
  identification uses the order of the Taylor coefficients in the stationary
  action: first the cubic term, then the quartic term, and finally the
  quadratic loop correction. We explain both the finite-step comparison and
  the passage from subsequences to the whole sequence.

  At each scale <math|m> write a path as its prescribed one-form row and
  three coefficient arrays

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|a<rsub|m>,b<rsub|m>,c<rsub|m>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|a<rsub|m>> represents the quadratic loop correction,
  <math|b<rsub|m>> the cubic term, and <math|c<rsub|m>> the quartic term.
  Each array belongs to the selected closed subspace of its real coefficient
  Hilbert space, which is defined as follows. For <math|q=2,3,4> let
  <math|H<rsub|m,q>> be the real Hilbert space of square-summable coefficient
  arrays of degree <math|q>, and let <math|A<rsub|m,q>\<subset\>H<rsub|m,q>>
  be its closed subspace of admissible arrays: those whose atoms satisfy the
  linear invariance conditions of the path bounds, together with the linear
  loop normalization conditions when <math|q=2>. Let
  <math|n<rsub|m><around|(|p|)>>, <math|p\<geqslant\>0>, be the period
  sequence prescribed at scale <math|m> by the schedule. For each <math|p>
  let <math|T<rsub|m,q,p>> be the continuous linear map which sends an array
  to the degree-<math|q> Taylor coefficient at zero of its path sum on the
  torus of side <math|n<rsub|m><around|(|p|)>>. Put

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|m,q>=A<rsub|m,q>\<cap\><big|cap><rsub|p\<geqslant\>0>ker
    T<rsub|m,q,p>,<space|2em>A<rsup|<math-up|sel>><rsub|m,q>=A<rsub|m,q>\<cap\>K<rsub|m,q><rsup|\<perp\>>.
  </equation*>

  This is the canonical choice of Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:path-vow-representatives>,
  with the admissible subspace and with the readouts on the prescribed
  periods only. Two selected arrays with the same readouts on every
  prescribed period are equal: their difference lies in <math|K<rsub|m,q>>
  and in <math|K<rsub|m,q><rsup|\<perp\>>>. This statement concerns the
  arrays themselves, not just their evaluation on one torus. The orthogonal
  complement of the common kernel in the whole space <math|H<rsub|m,q>> would
  not do: its elements need not be admissible.

  For <math|q=2,3,4> and the fixed encoding width <math|w<rsub|m,q>>, use the
  norm

  <\equation*>
    \<frak-n\><rsub|m,q><around|(|C|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a,\<nu\>>e<rsup|-<around|(|w<rsub|m,q>-v<rsub|m>|)>*\<ell\><around|(|a|)>>*<around|\||C*<around|(|a,\<nu\>|)>|\|>,<space|2em>w<rsub|m,q>\<gtr\>v<rsub|m>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|v<rsub|m>> is the paid path width, and the canonical lengths and
  finite color index <math|\<nu\>> are as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:fixed-rung-convergence>. All weights are
  strictly positive. Thus this expression is a norm on the coefficient space
  on which it is finite; homogeneity and the triangle inequality follow term
  by term. Let <math|s<rsub|m,q>\<gtr\>0> be its paid allowance. The ordinary
  outputs satisfy <math|\<frak-n\><rsub|m,q>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|m,q>>. The
  extended input domain permits <math|64*s<rsub|m+1,q>>, and keeps the other
  two arrays at their ordinary allowances. The same finite-step construction
  is available for every input in this domain. We use this actual extended
  domain below; existence for one selected input alone would not suffice.

  <subsubsection|The data and the statement>

  The convergence proof is an argument about given finite-step data. It does
  not construct them. We list what is used; every item refers to one scale
  schedule, one horizon of beds and towers, and one choice of chart, clipping
  data and post-cost constants.

  <\enumerate>
    <item><em|Finite steps on the extended domain.> Let <math|m\<less\>N>,
    and let <math|R> be a path whose one-form row is the root row of the bed
    at <math|<around|(|N,m+1|)>>, which is attached to that bed, and which
    satisfies the path bounds of scale <math|m+1> with the row, cubic and
    quartic allowances and the loop allowance each multiplied by <math|64>.
    Then there are an output path <math|T>, a scalar <math|\<alpha\>> and
    stationary maps <math|\<rho\><rsup|p,F>>, one for each period index
    <math|p> and allowed forest <math|F>, with the following properties. The
    path <math|T> has the root row of the bed at <math|<around|(|N,m|)>>, is
    attached to it and satisfies the ordinary path bounds of scale <math|m>.
    On every prescribed period and forest the price gap, stationarity and
    classical matching relations between <math|R>, <math|T> and
    <math|\<rho\><rsup|p,F>> hold, and so does the one-loop comparison used
    below, with error at most <math|\<Gamma\>*e<rsup|-c*n<rsub|m><around|(|p|)>>>,
    with <math|\<Gamma\>,c\<gtr\>0> fixed at the stage. The scalar satisfies
    <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>+\<lambda\><rsub|G>*log
    L<rsub|m>|\|>+1\<leqslant\>D<rsub|m+1>>, where <math|\<lambda\><rsub|G>>
    is the slope constant of the group and <math|D<rsub|m+1>> the drift
    allowance. The three nonlinear arrays of <math|T> are encoded by selected
    arrays <math|a\<in\>A<rsup|<math-up|sel>><rsub|m,2>>,
    <math|b\<in\>A<rsup|<math-up|sel>><rsub|m,3>>,
    <math|c\<in\>A<rsup|<math-up|sel>><rsub|m,4>> whose Hilbert norms are at
    most the loop allowance, for <math|a>, and the path allowance, for
    <math|b> and <math|c>.

    <item><em|The actual history.> For every <math|N> there are paths
    <math|R<rsub|N,m>>, <math|m\<leqslant\>N>, scalars
    <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>> and stationary maps
    <math|\<rho\><rsup|p,F><rsub|N,m>>, <math|m\<less\>N>, forming the finite
    history of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:path-attachment-producer> with
    all its finite-cutoff path conditions. In particular each
    <math|R<rsub|N,m>> has the ordinary bounds and the root row of its bed,
    and for every <math|m\<less\>N> the data
    <math|<around|(|R<rsub|N,m+1>,R<rsub|N,m>,\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>,\<rho\><rsub|N,m>|)>>
    form a step with all the properties in the first item.

    <item><em|Widths.> The three encoding widths at each scale are strictly
    larger than the paid path width, the loop allowance is at least one, and
    the root rows of the beds at each fixed scale have, for all large
    <math|N>, a strip bound at one width strictly larger than the paid path
    width.

    <item><em|The horizon.> The beds and towers have their stagewise strip
    bounds, all-grade floors on every torus and transition identities, the
    sworn conditions hold on every prescribed period, and each of the ten
    retained bed and tower rows converges coefficientwise as
    <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>> at fixed scale. Every blocking step satisfies the
    size conditions of the schedule relating its patch to its fine and coarse
    periods. The chart is the local-ratio chart of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:haar-chart>: the exponential map is
    injective on a ball of positive radius, and on it the normalized Haar
    measure has a positive real-analytic density, equal to one at the origin,
    times a positive scale. The color index set is nonempty.
  </enumerate>

  The first two items and the widths are outputs of the one-step analytic
  construction and of the finite backward assembly in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:path-attachment-producer>; the factor
  <math|64> is the input allowance of Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:path-factor64-envelope>.
  The row convergence is Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:free-horizon-convergence>.
  None of these is proved in the present section, and the application to the
  selected history, with the verification that the four items refer to the
  same objects, is made in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-history-limits>.

  Under these hypotheses we prove the following. At every fixed scale
  <math|m> there are a path <math|R<rsub|m>> with the ordinary path bounds, a
  scalar <math|\<alpha\><rsub|m>> and, for every period index and forest, a
  stationary germ <math|\<rho\><rsup|p,F><rsub|m>>, such that as
  <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>> along the whole sequence of cutoffs: every
  coefficient of the selected arrays of <math|R<rsub|N,m>> converges to the
  corresponding coefficient of the selected arrays of <math|R<rsub|m>>, with
  the Hilbert bounds of the first item retained;
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>\<to\>\<alpha\><rsub|m>>; and every derivative at
  zero of <math|\<rho\><rsup|p,F><rsub|N,m>> converges to that of
  <math|\<rho\><rsup|p,F><rsub|m>>. The Hilbert bounds of the first item and
  its bound on <math|\<alpha\>> are what make the compactness step below
  possible. The weighted norms <math|\<frak-n\><rsub|m,q>> alone would not:
  their weights decay exponentially in the length.

  <subsubsection|The triangular affine identities>

  Fix the fine and coarse one-form rows. Consider three admissible
  finite-step inputs and outputs, indexed by <math|0,1,t>, with the same rows
  and the same forest, coordinates and normalization conventions. The
  following identities hold whenever the indicated interpolation is
  admissible:

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|b<rsup|<math-up|in>><rsub|t>=b<rsup|<math-up|in>><rsub|0>+t*<around|(|b<rsup|<math-up|in>><rsub|1>-b<rsup|<math-up|in>><rsub|0>|)>>|<cell|\<Longrightarrow\>b<rsup|<math-up|out>><rsub|t>=b<rsup|<math-up|out>><rsub|0>+t*<around|(|b<rsup|<math-up|out>><rsub|1>-b<rsup|<math-up|out>><rsub|0>|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:background-cubic-affine>>>|<row|<cell|c<rsup|<math-up|in>><rsub|t>=c<rsup|<math-up|in>><rsub|0>+t*<around|(|c<rsup|<math-up|in>><rsub|1>-c<rsup|<math-up|in>><rsub|0>|)>>|<cell|\<Longrightarrow\>c<rsup|<math-up|out>><rsub|t>=c<rsup|<math-up|out>><rsub|0>+t*<around|(|c<rsup|<math-up|out>><rsub|1>-c<rsup|<math-up|out>><rsub|0>|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:background-quartic-affine>>>|<row|<cell|a<rsup|<math-up|in>><rsub|t>=a<rsup|<math-up|in>><rsub|0>+t*<around|(|a<rsup|<math-up|in>><rsub|1>-a<rsup|<math-up|in>><rsub|0>|)>>|<cell|\<Longrightarrow\><choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|a<rsup|<math-up|out>><rsub|t>=a<rsup|<math-up|out>><rsub|0>+t*<around|(|a<rsup|<math-up|out>><rsub|1>-a<rsup|<math-up|out>><rsub|0>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<alpha\><rsub|t>=\<alpha\><rsub|0>+t*<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|1>-\<alpha\><rsub|0>|)>.>>>>><eq-number><label|eq:background-loop-affine>>>>>
  </align>

  In <eqref|eq:background-quartic-affine> the three input cubic arrays must
  agree. In <eqref|eq:background-loop-affine> both their cubic and quartic
  arrays must agree. No equality of those arrays is needed in
  <eqref|eq:background-cubic-affine>.

  Here is the stationary-action argument for the first two identities. In
  finite-dimensional chart coordinates let <math|S*<around|(|x,y|)>> be the
  analytic action, <math|y=\<psi\><around|(|x|)>> its stationary graph, and
  <math|S<rsub|y*y>*<around|(|0,0|)>=H> its invertible Hessian in the
  eliminated coordinates. All these stationary graphs have
  <math|\<psi\><around|(|0|)>=0>. Write <math|z<around|(|x|)>=<around|(|x,\<psi\><around|(|x|)>|)>>.
  Differentiating <math|S<rsub|y>*<around|(|x,\<psi\><around|(|x|)>|)>=0>
  once determines <math|D*\<psi\><around|(|0|)>> from the common quadratic
  jet of <math|S>. Repeated differentiation determines each next derivative
  by an equation

  <\equation*>
    H*D<rsup|r>*\<psi\><around|(|0|)>=-R<rsub|r>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|R<rsub|r>> is a finite sum of contractions of derivatives of
  <math|S> of order at most <math|r+1> and derivatives of <math|\<psi\>> of
  order less than <math|r>. This follows directly by the multivariable chain
  rule: the only term containing <math|D<rsup|r>*\<psi\>> is obtained by
  differentiating the <math|y> argument of <math|S<rsub|y>> once and the
  graph <math|r> times.

  Suppose three actions have the same jet through order <math|r>, with
  <math|r=2> or <math|3>, and their order-<math|<around|(|r+1|)>> jets have
  the displayed affine relation. Their graph jets through order <math|r-1>
  agree. In <math|D<rsup|r+1>*<around|(|S\<circ\>z|)><around|(|0|)>>, terms
  containing a single highest graph derivative cancel by stationarity. Terms
  containing <math|D<rsup|r>*z> pair with <math|D<rsup|2>*S> and <math|D*z>
  and vanish by the derivative of stationarity, since <math|D<rsup|r>*z> is
  vertical. All remaining terms involve the common lower jets, except the
  single term

  <\equation*>
    D<rsup|r+1>*S<around|(|0|)><around|(|D*z<around|(|0|)>,\<ldots\>,D*z<around|(|0|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  It follows that the reduced order-<math|<around|(|r+1|)>> jet has the same
  affine relation. Equivalently, one may subtract the three chain-rule
  expansions: every common lower-jet term cancels, and the displayed term is
  the only possible difference.

  Cubic path terms vanish through order two, and quartic terms through order
  three. The quadratic action depends only on the common row. Thus the
  preceding calculation with <math|r=2> proves the affine cubic identity for
  the reduced actions. The finite-step classical matching equates these jets
  with the coarse path jets. Subtracting their common coarse-row contribution
  and using the injectivity of the selected cubic readout gives
  <eqref|eq:background-cubic-affine>. If the input cubic arrays agree, this
  identity first makes the output cubic arrays agree. The calculation with
  <math|r=3> then applies; subtracting the common coarse-row and cubic
  contributions gives <eqref|eq:background-quartic-affine> by the quartic
  readout.

  For the loop identity the classical action, including its cubic and quartic
  terms, is the same for all three inputs. The implicit function theorem and
  the invertibility of <math|H> give the same stationary germ. Its Hessian
  determinant and the coordinate Jacobian contribution are therefore the
  same. The remaining one-loop expression is the loop correction evaluated on
  that germ; it is linear in <math|a<rsup|<math-up|in>>>. Its quadratic jet
  is affine. On the torus of side <math|n=n<rsub|m><around|(|p|)>>, with site
  set <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|n>>, the coarse one-loop decomposition of the
  quadratic jet has the form

  <\equation*>
    \<alpha\>*Q<rsub|0,p>+Q<rsub|a,p>+\<cal-E\><rsub|p>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|Q<rsub|0,p>> is the quadratic form of the common normalized
  row, <math|Q<rsub|a,p>=T<rsub|m,2,p>*a<rsup|<math-up|out>>> is the loop
  readout, and <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|p>> is the finite-period error. The error
  has the exponential bound of the finite step: on every pair of real plane
  waves whose amplitudes have entries of modulus at most one, its modulus is
  at most <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|n>|\|>*\<Gamma\>*e<rsup|-c*n>>.
  Form the combination, with coefficients <math|1,-<around|(|1-t|)>,-t>, of
  the three decompositions indexed by <math|t,0,1>. The left sides cancel,
  because the quadratic jet of the one-loop expression is affine. With

  <\equation*>
    \<delta\>*\<alpha\>=\<alpha\><rsub|t>-\<alpha\><rsub|0>-t*<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|1>-\<alpha\><rsub|0>|)>,<space|2em>\<delta\>*a=a<rsup|<math-up|out>><rsub|t>-a<rsup|<math-up|out>><rsub|0>-t*<around|(|a<rsup|<math-up|out>><rsub|1>-a<rsup|<math-up|out>><rsub|0>|)>,
  </equation*>

  the forms <math|G<rsub|p>=\<delta\>*\<alpha\>*<space|0.17em>Q<rsub|0,p>+Q<rsub|\<delta\>*a,p>>
  therefore satisfy the same plane-wave bound with <math|\<Gamma\>> replaced
  by <math|<around|(|2+2<around|\||t|\|>|)>*\<Gamma\>>.

  Both families <math|Q<rsub|0,p>> and <math|Q<rsub|a,p>> are recorded by a
  symbol. By this we mean that there is a matrix-valued function of the
  momentum, holomorphic and bounded on a strip of positive width about the
  real momenta and independent of <math|p>, such that on every prescribed
  period the coefficient of the form on two complex plane waves is zero
  unless their momenta are opposite, and is then
  <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|n>|\|>> times the entry of the symbol at
  that momentum. For <math|Q<rsub|0,p>> the symbol is built from the common
  bed metric and its strip bound; for <math|Q<rsub|a,p>> it is the loop
  symbol of the path, linear in <math|a>, with the path width. Hence
  <math|G<rsub|p>> is recorded by <math|<wide|G|^>=\<delta\>*\<alpha\>*<space|0.17em><wide|Q|^><rsub|0>+<wide|Q|^><rsub|\<delta\>*a>>.

  The all-period detection statement is the following. <em|Let forms
  <math|G<rsub|p>> on the prescribed periods be recorded by one symbol
  <math|<wide|G|^>> as above, and suppose their values on pairs of real plane
  waves with amplitude entries of modulus at most one are bounded by
  <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|n>|\|>*b<rsub|p>> with
  <math|b<rsub|p>\<to\>0>. Then <math|<wide|G|^>> vanishes at every real
  momentum, and <math|G<rsub|p>=0> for every <math|p>.> For the proof, a
  complex plane wave is a combination of two real ones with coefficients
  <math|1> and <math|i>, so the recorded coefficients are at most
  <math|4<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|n>|\|>*b<rsub|p>> and each symbol entry
  is at most <math|4*b<rsub|p>> at every momentum of the torus of side
  <math|n<rsub|m><around|(|p|)>>. Fix a real momentum <math|k>. Since
  <math|n<rsub|m><around|(|p|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>, there are momenta
  <math|k<rsub|p>> of these tori converging to <math|k> modulo the reciprocal
  lattice. Continuity of <math|<wide|G|^>> gives
  <math|<wide|G|^><around|(|k|)>=lim<rsub|p>
  <wide|G|^><around|(|k<rsub|p>|)>=0>. The recorded coefficients of each
  <math|G<rsub|p>> are values of <math|<wide|G|^>> at real momenta, so they
  all vanish; plane waves span the complexified field space, whence
  <math|G<rsub|p>=0>.

  Apply this to the forms <math|G<rsub|p>> above, with
  <math|b<rsub|p>=<around|(|2+2<around|\||t|\|>|)>*\<Gamma\>*e<rsup|-c*n<rsub|m><around|(|p|)>>>.
  The normalization functional <math|\<cal-N\>> is defined from the symbol
  alone. Fix a color index <math|i>, and let
  <math|c<rsub|i><around|(|<wide|Q|^>|)>> be the second-order Taylor
  coefficient at zero momentum, along the coordinate direction <math|0>, of
  the diagonal entry of the symbol with link direction <math|1> and colors
  <math|i,i>; put <math|\<cal-N\><around|(|<wide|Q|^>|)>=2<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>c<rsub|i><around|(|<wide|Q|^>|)>>.
  It is real linear. The normalized row has
  <math|c<rsub|i><around|(|<wide|Q|^><rsub|0>|)>=1/2>, so
  <math|\<cal-N\><around|(|<wide|Q|^><rsub|0>|)>=1>, and <math|\<cal-N\>>
  vanishes on the loop symbol of every array satisfying the loop
  normalization conditions. Applying it to <math|<wide|G|^>=0> gives
  <math|\<delta\>*\<alpha\>=0>, which is the identity for <math|\<alpha\>>.
  The residual loop readouts <math|Q<rsub|\<delta\>*a,p>=T<rsub|m,2,p>*\<delta\>*a>
  then vanish on every prescribed period, and
  <math|\<delta\>*a\<in\>A<rsup|<math-up|sel>><rsub|m,2>>; injectivity on the
  selected loop subspace gives <math|\<delta\>*a=0>, the identity for
  <math|a<rsup|<math-up|out>>>. This identifies exactly where quadratic
  normalization and all-period detection enter the proof.

  <subsubsection|An enlarged input ball gives contraction>

  Choose one of the three arrays, subject to equality of its preceding arrays
  in the order <math|b,c,a>. Write <math|s<rprime|'>=s<rsub|m+1,q>> and
  <math|s=s<rsub|m,q>>. Let <math|x,y> be two ordinary inputs and put
  <math|z=\<frak-n\><rsub|m+1,q>*<around|(|y-x|)>>. If <math|z=0>, then
  <math|x=y>, and the affine identity at parameter zero gives equal selected
  outputs. If <math|z\<gtr\>0>, use the real parameter

  <\equation*>
    t=32*s<rprime|'>/z.
  </equation*>

  The replacement <math|x+t*<around|(|y-x|)>> has norm at most

  <\equation*>
    \<frak-n\><rsub|m+1,q><around|(|x|)>+t*z\<leqslant\>33*s<rprime|'>\<leqslant\>64*s<rprime|'>.
  </equation*>

  Keeping the other two arrays fixed preserves their invariant subspaces and
  bounds. In the loop case it also preserves the linear loop normalization
  constraints. Thus this is an allowed extended input. Its output and that of
  <math|x> both have norm at most <math|s>. By the affine identity, their
  difference is <math|t> times the difference of the original two outputs.
  Hence

  <\equation>
    t*\<frak-n\><rsub|m,q>*<around|(|y<rsup|<math-up|out>>-x<rsup|<math-up|out>>|)>\<leqslant\>2*s,<space|2em><frac|\<frak-n\><rsub|m,q>*<around|(|y<rsup|<math-up|out>>-x<rsup|<math-up|out>>|)>|s>\<leqslant\><frac|1|16>*<frac|z|s<rprime|'>>\<leqslant\><frac|1|4>*<frac|z|s<rprime|'>>.<label|eq:background-contraction>
  </equation>

  We retain the weaker factor <math|1/4> used in the history argument. The
  extrapolated parameter need not lie in <math|<around|[|0,1|]>>; the
  extended input domain is essential for this proof.

  <subsubsection|Passing finite histories to cluster histories>

  Fix any sequence of terminal cutoffs tending to infinity. At each fixed
  scale <math|m>, the three selected coefficient arrays of the step outputs
  have Hilbert norms at most the loop allowance and the path allowance, and
  <math|<around|\||\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>|\|>\<leqslant\>D<rsub|m+1>+<around|\||\<lambda\><rsub|G>*log
  L<rsub|m>|\|>>; both are part of the first hypothesis. These bounds do not
  depend on <math|N>. Enumerate the countably many scales and coefficient
  coordinates. Successive extraction in bounded real intervals, followed by
  the diagonal subsequence, gives convergence of all those coordinates and
  all scalars at once. Finite sums of coordinate squares show that each
  limiting array has the same Hilbert bound. A closed linear constraint is
  retained: for a test vector in its orthogonal complement, approximate by a
  finite-coordinate vector; the uniform Hilbert bound controls the omitted
  inner product. Thus the limits remain in the selected subspaces.

  The one-form rows have their common limit from the retained kernel horizon.
  The width argument in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:fixed-rung-convergence>
  gives convergence of the path sums at every smaller width. It also gives
  convergence of every finite chart derivative. Indeed, on a sufficiently
  small complex chart ball each length-<math|\<ell\>> path and each fixed
  derivative costs a polynomial in <math|\<ell\>> times
  <math|e<rsup|\<epsilon\>*\<ell\>>>. Choose <math|\<epsilon\>> smaller than
  the available width difference; the remaining exponential factor sums the
  polynomial. Termwise differentiation is then justified by uniform absolute
  convergence.

  The limiting eliminated Hessian obeys the same positive lower bound, by
  passage to the limit in its quadratic form. The analytic implicit function
  theorem supplies a stationary germ for the limiting action. The derivative
  recursion above proves convergence of every derivative of the finite
  stationary germs: inversion of the finite Hessian is continuous on the
  matrices with this common lower bound, and each <math|R<rsub|r>> is a
  finite expression in previously convergent derivatives. The limits also
  have the same classical matching through degree four. For the one-loop
  expression, the derivatives of <math|log det H> are finite sums of products
  of <math|H<rsup|-1>> and derivatives of <math|H>. The positive lower bound
  and the same recursion therefore give convergence of its quadratic jet,
  including the fixed coordinate Jacobian. All finite-period error
  inequalities pass to the limit. The row normalization does too. Thus the
  resulting cluster history satisfies the same local relations and paid
  output bounds as the finite histories.

  The input at scale <math|m> equals the output at scale <math|m+1> in these
  cluster histories. Indeed the selected arrays of each path
  <math|R<rsub|N,m+1>> are chosen once and used in both of its occurrences,
  as the output of the step at <math|m+1> and as the input of the step at
  <math|m>. The equality of input and output arrays is thus an exact
  finite-history identity for every <math|N\<gtr\>m+2>, and coordinatewise
  passage to the limit preserves it. All periods and forests needed here have
  already been fixed by the schedule. The derivative recursion defines their
  limiting stationary germs from the same limiting path; no independent
  subsequence is chosen for each germ.

  <subsubsection|Comparison of two cluster histories>

  Let <math|F> and <math|H> be two cluster histories with the same retained
  row horizon. A finite-step comparison cannot simply be applied to them as
  if they came from the same finite cutoff. Instead fix a scale and an array
  type, with its preceding input arrays equal. Take finite steps tending to
  <math|F>, and replace just this array in each fine input by the
  corresponding array of <math|H>. The replacement is an ordinary admissible
  input: the bounds and linear invariance constraints hold separately for its
  three arrays. Construct its output at that very same cutoff. Extract a
  further subsequence for these modified outputs and their scalar
  corrections.

  The preceding limit argument gives a valid cluster step. Its chosen input
  array is that of <math|H>, and its preceding arrays are also those of
  <math|H>. The affine identity at parameter zero shows that its chosen
  output array equals that of <math|H>. Apply
  <eqref|eq:background-contraction> before taking the subsequence limit. The
  weighted norms of the differences converge by the strict-width dominated
  convergence argument. We obtain

  <\equation*>
    g<rsub|m>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|4>g<rsub|m+1>,<space|2em>g<rsub|m>\<assign\><frac|\<frak-n\><rsub|m,q>*<around|(|H<rsub|m>-F<rsub|m>|)>|s<rsub|m,q>>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>g<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>2.
  </equation*>

  Iteration gives <math|g<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>2*<space|0.17em>4<rsup|-h>> for
  every integer <math|h\<geqslant\>0>; hence <math|g<rsub|m>=0>. Apply this
  first to the cubic arrays at every scale, which require no preceding
  equality. Then apply it to the quartic arrays, using the cubic equality
  just obtained. Finally apply it to the loop arrays, using both equalities.
  All three arrays in the two cluster histories agree.

  Their rows already agree, so their full input and output paths agree. The
  loop identity at parameter zero identifies their scalar corrections.
  Uniqueness of the stationary germ identifies all its derivatives for every
  fixed forest and period. We have therefore proved uniqueness of the entire
  cluster history, including the scalar and stationary data needed in the
  next scale comparison.

  If the original sequence failed to converge to this history in any one
  coordinate, scalar, or fixed chart derivative, a subsequence would stay
  outside some neighborhood of its asserted limit. The compactness
  construction applies to that subsequence and gives a further subsequence
  with the unique cluster limit, a contradiction. Thus the entire cutoff
  sequence converges. The strict-width argument upgrades its coefficientwise
  convergence to the weighted path convergence used for fixed-scale
  normalized moments. This proves the statement of the second subsection. It
  is conditional on the four hypotheses listed there: the finite-step
  construction on the extended domain, the actual history, the widths and the
  retained row horizon must be supplied on the selected history.

  <subsection|Analytic prices from real coupling
  estimates><label|sec:real-coupling-interpolation>

  The parameter estimates used for the background history have a useful
  feature: they are obtained from real configurations. The local weights
  contain exponentials whose exponents need not be bounded, and bounding
  their absolute values before using the real estimates would lose the
  required smallness. We prove the interpolation argument that avoids this
  loss. The real local estimates to which it is applied retain their full
  derivative and support weights.

  <subsubsection|An algebra of parameter series>

  For a real power series <math|f<around|(|t|)>=<big|sum><rsub|r\<geqslant\>0>f<rsub|r>*t<rsup|r>>
  and <math|R\<geqslant\>1>, set

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||f|\|><rsub|R>=<big|sum><rsub|r\<geqslant\>0><around|\||f<rsub|r>|\|>*R<rsup|r>.
  </equation*>

  Only series with finite displayed norm will be used. Their products are
  given by the absolutely convergent Cauchy product, and
  <math|<around|\||f*g|\|><rsub|R>\<leqslant\><around|\||f|\|><rsub|R><around|\||g|\|><rsub|R>>.
  Consequently <math|<around|\||exp f|\|><rsub|R>\<leqslant\>exp
  <around|(|<around|\||f|\|><rsub|R>|)>>. If the constant coefficient
  <math|f<rsub|0>> is real, it is better to keep its sign:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:real-series-exponential><around|\||exp
    f|\|><rsub|R>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|f<rsub|0>>*exp
    <around|(|<around|\||f-f<rsub|0>|\|><rsub|R>|)>.
  </equation>

  These assertions follow by summing the nonnegative absolute coefficient
  bounds for <math|<big|sum><rsub|m\<geqslant\>0>f<rsup|m>/m!>; absolute
  convergence also identifies that series with the exponential on
  <math|<around|[|-R,R|]>>. For a finite real test
  <math|\<lambda\>=<big|sum><rsub|h>a<rsub|h>*\<delta\><rsub|t<rsub|h>>>,
  <math|<around|\||t<rsub|h>|\|>\<leqslant\>1>, put

  <\equation*>
    M<rsub|R><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>=sup<rsub|r\<geqslant\>0><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|h>a<rsub|h>*<around|(|t<rsub|h>/R|)><rsup|r>|\|>.
  </equation*>

  Termwise summation then gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:real-series-test><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|h>a<rsub|h>*f<around|(|t<rsub|h>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||f|\|><rsub|R>*M<rsub|R><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>.
  </equation>

  Thus an absolute coefficient estimate supplies every finite-test estimate
  simultaneously.

  Fix a real normed space <math|E>, a point <math|x\<in\>E>, and an integer
  <math|j\<geqslant\>0>. For a <math|C<rsup|j>> function <math|V> near
  <math|x>, write <math|D<rsub|u><rsup|i>*V<around|(|x|)>=<around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d<rsup|i>|d*s<rsup|i>>*V*<around|(|x+s*u|)>|\|><rsub|s=0>>.
  A diagonal jet bound with amplitude <math|C> and derivative scale <math|d>
  means <math|<around|\||D<rsub|u><rsup|i>*V<around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*d<rsup|i>>
  for <math|0\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>j> and
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1>. When <math|i=0> we use
  <math|d<rsup|0>=1>, including when <math|d=0>. The parameter estimates
  below hold for each such derivative, with one common amplitude. Conversion
  to full multilinear derivative norms uses the fixed finite-order
  polarization constants already included in the local derivative prices.

  <subsubsection|The larger real nodes>

  Let <math|\<ell\><around|(|t|)>=a+b*t>, and suppose

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:real-affine-band>R\<geqslant\>1,<space|2em>1\<leqslant\>a-6*R<around|\||b|\|>,<space|2em>a+6*R<around|\||b|\|>\<leqslant\>1+\<delta\>,<space|2em>\<delta\>\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation>

  In particular <math|0\<leqslant\>\<delta\>\<leqslant\>1>, and
  <math|1\<leqslant\>\<ell\><around|(|t|)>\<leqslant\>1+\<delta\>> whenever
  <math|<around|\||t|\|>\<leqslant\>6*R>. Set
  <math|p<around|(|t|)>=1-\<ell\><around|(|t|)><rsup|2>>. For every real
  <math|q> define

  <\equation*>
    A<around|(|q|)>=<around|(|1-a<rsup|2>|)>*q+2*a*<around|\||b*q|\|>*R+b<rsup|2><around|\||q|\|>*R<rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  By <eqref|eq:real-series-exponential>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:real-exponent-price><around|\||\<ell\>|\|><rsub|R>\<leqslant\>1+\<delta\>,<space|2em><around|\||exp
    <around|(|p*q|)>|\|><rsub|R>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|A<around|(|q|)>>.
  </equation>

  The first inequality uses <math|a\<geqslant\>1> and
  <math|a+R<around|\||b|\|>\<leqslant\>a+6*R<around|\||b|\|>>. No estimate on
  <math|<around|\||q|\|>> is required.

  Choose <math|\<sigma\>=-1> if <math|q*b\<geqslant\>0>, and
  <math|\<sigma\>=1> otherwise. For <math|r=0,\<ldots\>,2*j>, put

  <\equation*>
    u<rsub|r>=3+<frac|r|2*j+1>,<space|2em>\<xi\><rsub|r>=\<sigma\>*u<rsub|r>,<space|2em>t<rsub|r>=R*\<xi\><rsub|r>.
  </equation*>

  These are distinct real points in <math|<around|[|-6*R,6*R|]>>. At every
  one of them,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:real-node-domination>A<around|(|q|)>\<leqslant\>p<around|(|t<rsub|r>|)>*q.
  </equation>

  Here is the sign-sensitive estimate. Put
  <math|d<rsub|\<ast\>>=R<around|\||b|\|>>; then
  <math|a\<geqslant\>1+6*d<rsub|\<ast\>>> and
  <math|q*b*R*\<sigma\>=-<around|\||q|\|>*d<rsub|\<ast\>>>. For any
  <math|3\<leqslant\>u\<leqslant\>6>, we have

  <\equation*>
    2*a*d<rsub|\<ast\>>+d<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>3*a*d<rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>a*d<rsub|\<ast\>>*u\<leqslant\>2*a*d<rsub|\<ast\>>*u-d<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|2>*u<rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  The last inequality is <math|d<rsub|\<ast\>>*u\<leqslant\>a>, multiplied by
  <math|d<rsub|\<ast\>>*u>; the other two follow from
  <math|d<rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>a> and <math|u\<geqslant\>3>. Expanding
  <math|p*<around|(|R*\<sigma\>*u|)>*q>, and using
  <math|q\<leqslant\><around|\||q|\|>>, now gives

  <\equation*>
    <\eqsplit>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|p*<around|(|R*\<sigma\>*u|)>*q>|<cell|=<around|(|1-a<rsup|2>|)>*q+2*a<around|\||q|\|>*d<rsub|\<ast\>>*u-q*d<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|2>*u<rsup|2>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<geqslant\><around|(|1-a<rsup|2>|)>*q+<around|\||q|\|>*<around|(|2*a*d<rsub|\<ast\>>*u-d<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|2>*u<rsup|2>|)>\<geqslant\>A<around|(|q|)>.>>>>
    </eqsplit>
  </equation*>

  This proves <eqref|eq:real-node-domination> for either sign of <math|q>.

  <subsubsection|Interpolating the local weight with its derivatives>

  Let <math|L,\<Gamma\>:E\<to\>\<bbb-R\>> be <math|C<rsup|j>> near <math|x>,
  and let <math|n> be a nonnegative integer. Consider

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:real-local-weight>J*<around|(|t,y|)>=L<around|(|y|)>*\<ell\><around|(|t|)><rsup|n>*e<rsup|p<around|(|t|)>*\<Gamma\><around|(|y|)>>.
  </equation>

  Choose the preceding nodes with <math|q=\<Gamma\><around|(|x|)>>. Suppose
  that at those <math|2*j+1> nodes the real local estimates give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:real-node-jet><around|\||D<rsub|u><rsup|i>*J*<around|(|t<rsub|r>,x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*d<rsup|i>*<space|1em><around|(|0\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>j,<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1|)>,<space|2em>C,d\<geqslant\>0.
  </equation>

  There is a constant <math|K<rsub|j>>, depending only on <math|j>, such that
  each function <math|t\<mapsto\>D<rsub|u><rsup|i>*J*<around|(|t,x|)>> has an
  absolutely convergent series with

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:real-local-series-price><around|\||D<rsub|u><rsup|i>*J|(>\<cdot\>,x)\|<rsub|R>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|j>*<around|(|1+\<delta\>|)><rsup|n>*C*d<rsup|i>.
  </equation>

  In particular this controls all finite tests by
  <eqref|eq:real-series-test>. The constant is independent of
  <math|a,b,R,n,C,d> and of the value of <math|\<Gamma\><around|(|x|)>>.

  We prove the assertion, including the reason that only finitely many real
  nodes suffice. Differentiating along <math|x+s*u> and factoring out the
  exponential gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:real-polynomial-jet>D<rsub|u><rsup|i>*J*<around|(|t,x|)>=P<rsub|i,u><around|(|t|)>*\<ell\><around|(|t|)><rsup|n>*e<rsup|p<around|(|t|)>*\<Gamma\><around|(|x|)>>,<space|2em>deg
    P<rsub|i,u>\<leqslant\>2*i.
  </equation>

  Indeed, for the exponential alone its <math|i>th derivative is the
  exponential times a sum, over partitions of
  <math|<around|{|1,\<ldots\>,i|}>>, of products
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|B>p<around|(|t|)>*D<rsub|u><rsup|<around|\||B|\|>>*\<Gamma\><around|(|x|)>>.
  Each product has at most <math|i> factors <math|p<around|(|t|)>>, each of
  degree two. Leibniz's rule with <math|L> gives
  <eqref|eq:real-polynomial-jet>, also for <math|i=0>. No division by
  <math|L> or by a derivative of <math|\<Gamma\>> occurs.

  Let <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|r><around|(|z|)>> be the Lagrange basis
  polynomials for the nodes <math|\<xi\><rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,\<xi\><rsub|2*j>>.
  Polynomial interpolation gives

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|i,u><around|(|t|)>=<big|sum><rsub|r=0><rsup|2*j>P<rsub|i,u><around|(|t<rsub|r>|)>*\<cal-L\><rsub|r>*<around|(|t/R|)>.
  </equation*>

  Define <math|K<rsub|j>> as the larger, for the two possible signs
  <math|\<sigma\>>, of <math|<big|sum><rsub|r><around|\||\<cal-L\><rsub|r>|\|><rsub|1>>.
  This is finite, since all the nodes are distinct. Also
  <math|<around|\||\<cal-L\><rsub|r>|(>\<cdot\>/R)\|<rsub|R>=<around|\||\<cal-L\><rsub|r>|\|><rsub|1>>.
  By <eqref|eq:real-node-jet>, <eqref|eq:real-polynomial-jet>, and
  <math|\<ell\><around|(|t<rsub|r>|)><rsup|n>\<geqslant\>1>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||P<rsub|i,u><around|(|t<rsub|r>|)>|\|>*e<rsup|p<around|(|t<rsub|r>|)>*q>\<leqslant\>C*d<rsup|i>.
  </equation*>

  Multiplying each interpolation term by <math|\<ell\><around|(|t|)><rsup|n>*e<rsup|p<around|(|t|)>*q>>
  and using <eqref|eq:real-exponent-price> and
  <eqref|eq:real-node-domination>, its coefficient norm is at most

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||P<rsub|i,u><around|(|t<rsub|r>|)>|\|>*<around|(|1+\<delta\>|)><rsup|n>*e<rsup|A<around|(|q|)>><around|\||\<cal-L\><rsub|r>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+\<delta\>|)><rsup|n>*C*d<rsup|i><around|\||\<cal-L\><rsub|r>|\|><rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  Summing proves <eqref|eq:real-local-series-price>. Notice that the node
  sign may depend on <math|\<Gamma\><around|(|x|)>>. Both sets of nodes
  belong to the same allowed real interval, and <math|K<rsub|j>> controls
  either choice; there is no need to differentiate the choice of sign.

  <subsubsection|The Gaussian density and the local support count>

  For a fixed Gaussian coordinate <math|z>, let
  <math|Q<around|(|z|)>\<geqslant\>0> be its quadratic energy in the
  reference density. Under the rescaling <math|z\<mapsto\>\<ell\><around|(|t|)>*z>
  in <math|D> real dimensions, the density ratio, including the Jacobian, is

  <\equation*>
    g*<around|(|t,z|)>=\<ell\><around|(|t|)><rsup|D>*e<rsup|<around|(|1-\<ell\><around|(|t|)><rsup|2>|)>*Q<around|(|z|)>>.
  </equation*>

  The same argument, now with <math|q=Q<around|(|z|)>\<geqslant\>0>, yields

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:real-gaussian-series-price><around|\||g|(>\<cdot\>,z)\|<rsub|R>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+\<delta\>|)><rsup|D>.
  </equation>

  Indeed <eqref|eq:real-node-domination> gives
  <math|A<around|(|q|)>\<leqslant\>p<around|(|t<rsub|r>|)>*q\<leqslant\>0>,
  since <math|\<ell\><around|(|t<rsub|r>|)>\<geqslant\>1>. The bound is
  uniform in <math|z>. Thus it can be multiplied into a local coefficient
  majorant before integration against the fixed reference Gaussian measure.

  In the local application, the exponent <math|n> in
  <eqref|eq:real-local-weight> counts the seed coordinates actually present
  in the local weight, each with its color dimension. The Gaussian dimension
  <math|D> is likewise that of the relevant marginal. These counts are
  determined by the local support; replacing them by the dimension of the
  entire torus would destroy a bound uniform in volume. The exact local
  change-of-density identity is used before applying
  <eqref|eq:real-gaussian-series-price>. The remaining support-dependent
  powers <math|<around|(|1+\<delta\>|)><rsup|n+D>> are included in the
  cover-price reserve.

  <subsubsection|Keeping the small amplitude of ordinary activities>

  There is a second, complementary estimate. Let
  <math|f:\<bbb-R\>\<times\>E\<to\>\<bbb-R\>> be real-valued, and suppose
  that <math|t\<mapsto\>f*<around|(|t,y|)>> is a polynomial of degree at most
  <math|k> on <math|<around|[|-1,1|]>> for every <math|y>, where <math|k> is
  fixed. Choose distinct fixed nodes <math|s<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,s<rsub|k>\<in\><around|[|-1,1|]>>.
  Suppose that each <math|f*<around|(|s<rsub|r>,\<cdot\>|)>> is
  <math|C<rsup|j>> near <math|x> and that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:real-small-activity><around|\||D<rsub|u><rsup|i>*<around|(|e<rsup|f*<around|(|s<rsub|r>,\<cdot\>|)>>-1|)><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>v*d<rsup|i>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>v\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>.
  </equation>

  There are constants <math|A,B\<geqslant\>1>, depending only on <math|j,k>
  and the nodes, such that, whenever <math|B*R<rsup|k>*v\<leqslant\>1>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:real-small-series><around|\||D<rsub|u><rsup|i>*<around|(|e<rsup|f*<around|(|\<cdummy\>,\<cdummy\>|)>>-1|)><around|(|x|)>|\|><rsub|R>\<leqslant\>A*R<rsup|k>*v*d<rsup|i>.
  </equation>

  In this formula the derivative is in the second variable and the series
  norm in the first. The series norm refers to the polynomial extension: for
  each <math|y> let <math|<wide|f|^>*<around|(|\<cdummy\>,y|)>> be the
  polynomial of degree at most <math|k> which agrees with
  <math|f*<around|(|\<cdummy\>,y|)>> on <math|<around|[|-1,1|]>>, and read
  <eqref|eq:real-small-series> for <math|e<rsup|<wide|f|^>>-1>. Its series
  represents <math|e<rsup|f>-1> at every <math|t\<in\><around|[|-1,1|]>>,
  which is where the finite tests are taken. Nothing is asserted about the
  values of <math|f> at parameters outside <math|<around|[|-1,1|]>>.

  To check the estimate, write <math|h=e<rsup|f*<around|(|s<rsub|r>,\<cdot\>|)>>-1>
  at a fixed node. At <math|x> we have <math|<around|\||h<around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>v\<leqslant\>1/2>
  and <math|f*<around|(|s<rsub|r>,\<cdot\>|)>=log <around|(|1+h|)>> near
  <math|x>. The zeroth derivative is bounded by <math|2*v>, by integrating
  <math|1/<around|(|1+s|)>> between <math|0> and <math|h<around|(|x|)>>. For
  a positive-order derivative, the chain rule is a finite sum over
  partitions, with one derivative of <math|log <around|(|1+s|)>> and products
  of derivatives of <math|h>. The former is bounded on
  <math|<around|[|-1/2,1/2|]>>; a term with <math|r\<geqslant\>1> factors of
  <math|h> is bounded by a constant times
  <math|v<rsup|r>*d<rsup|i>\<leqslant\>v*d<rsup|i>>. Hence
  <math|\|D<rsub|u><rsup|i>*f(s<rsub|r>,\<cdot\><around|)|<around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*v*d<rsup|i>>
  at every node.

  Polynomial interpolation in <math|t> at the nodes <math|s<rsub|r>> gives
  <math|<wide|f|^>*<around|(|t,y|)>=<big|sum><rsub|r>f*<around|(|s<rsub|r>,y|)>*\<cal-L\><rsub|r><around|(|t|)>>
  for every <math|y> near <math|x>, with the Lagrange polynomials
  <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|r>> of these nodes, and so also after the indicated
  derivatives. In the rest of the proof <math|f> stands for
  <math|<wide|f|^>>. The coefficient norm of any fixed degree-<math|k>
  Lagrange basis polynomial at radius <math|R\<geqslant\>1> is bounded by
  <math|R<rsup|k>> times its norm at radius one. It follows that

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||D<rsub|u><rsup|i>*f|(>\<cdot\>,x)\|<rsub|R>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|\<ast\>>*R<rsup|k>*v*d<rsup|i>.
  </equation*>

  Choose <math|B\<geqslant\>C<rsub|\<ast\>>>. Put
  <math|\<varepsilon\>=C<rsub|\<ast\>>*R<rsup|k>*v\<leqslant\>1>. In the
  Banach algebra of parameter series, the zeroth-order estimate
  <math|<around|\||e<rsup|f>-1|\|><rsub|R>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<varepsilon\>>-1\<leqslant\>e*\<varepsilon\>>
  follows from the exponential series. For <math|i\<gtr\>0>, the same
  partition formula for derivatives of <math|e<rsup|f>> gives a factor
  <math|<around|\||e<rsup|f>|\|><rsub|R>\<leqslant\>e> and products bounded
  by <math|\<varepsilon\><rsup|r>*d<rsup|i>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>*d<rsup|i>>.
  The finite sum gives <eqref|eq:real-small-series>, with <math|A> large
  enough for all <math|i\<leqslant\>j>. This proof also covers <math|d=0>:
  all positive derivatives then vanish.

  The incoming ordinary logarithmic occurrence in the coupling family has
  degree at most two in this real parameter; the function to which
  <eqref|eq:real-small-series> is applied is its exponential minus one. Thus
  the radius cost in this last argument is <math|R<rsup|2>>, with constants
  fixed by the derivative order. The local stock weight uses the preceding
  real-node argument instead, because its exponent need not be small.
  Together with the local real jet estimates, the fixed Gaussian change of
  density, and the summable support prices, these two arguments provide the
  parameter estimates for the finite forest integrals. Summation over
  connected supports and the subsequent local projections still require their
  stated quantitative bounds; the interpolation proved here does not replace
  those bounds.

  <subsection|Summable parameter estimates for the sector
  expansion><label|sec:sector-analytic-prices>

  We now combine the real-parameter interpolation with the local Gaussian and
  connected-support estimates. The important point is that one uses the same
  supports, labels and protected fine reads throughout the real parameter
  family. A separate estimate at each parameter, with an unrelated choice of
  labels, would not bound a finite linear combination of the resulting
  activities.

  Fix the group, the block side <math|L>, a derivative order <math|j>, a
  nonnegative integer <math|m> measuring the spatial weight in the derivative
  budget, and all the positive safety and support reserves. Write
  <math|y=\<beta\><rsub|f><around|(|x|)>> for the reference fine coupling,
  which tends to infinity with the scale parameter <math|x>. A nonnegative
  function has upper exponent <math|a> below if, for each
  <math|a<rprime|'>\<gtr\>a>, it is eventually at most
  <math|y<rsup|a<rprime|'>>>. Finite sums, products and fixed powers have the
  corresponding upper-exponent bounds. Constants, local cover counts, and all
  the fixed-order derivative factors of upper exponent zero may therefore be
  absorbed using a strict exponent reserve.

  The incoming sector is assumed to satisfy the local hypotheses of
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:activity-jet-assembly> and
  <reference|sec:gaussian-differentiation>, uniformly for all real points
  <math|<around|\||t|\|>\<leqslant\>6*R> in the coupling band. Uniformity is
  meant in the following sense. The quantitative local data of those sections
  (the local jets of the four species, the safety inequality, the compensated
  chart and Jacobian bounds with their derivative scales, and the smoothness
  of the local Gaussian density) are given with one set of constants for the
  whole family, and not by a separate choice at each <math|t>. The order of
  choices is: first the lower bound on the block side; then the block side,
  the stage orders, the positive reserves and the other fixed data listed
  above; then one threshold in <math|x>, valid for all allowed periods,
  sectors and real parameter points. The path, clipping rule, ordinary and
  forced support dictionaries, and held fine configurations are common. The
  coupling is <math|\<beta\><around|(|t|)>=\<beta\><rsub|0>*\<ell\><around|(|t|)><rsup|2>>,
  where <eqref|eq:real-affine-band> holds. The incoming ordinary activity
  values and forced activity values on each fixed support are polynomials of
  degree at most two on <math|<around|[|-1,1|]>>. We take

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:sector-radius-choice>R=y<rsup|1/256>,<space|2em>\<delta\>=y<rsup|-1/128>.
  </equation>

  The geometric radius <math|B<around|(|x|)>> and its ball count
  <math|b<rsub|B>=<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>> have upper exponent zero. Fix
  the incoming support exponent <math|E> and the desired outgoing exponent
  <math|e> with <math|E\<gtr\>e\<geqslant\>0>. All estimates below are
  eventually uniform in the allowed periods, sectors and configurations. The
  threshold is chosen after the fixed data just listed. Construction of those
  incoming sectors and of their uniform safety estimates is still required;
  the purpose of this section is to prove the analytic output estimates from
  them.

  <subsubsection|Common prices before integration>

  The original optional-activity rooted allowance has upper exponent
  <math|a\<leqslant\>-7/16>. To obtain one price valid along the parameter
  family, sum the nonnegative prices at the three nodes <math|-1,0,1> for
  each ordinary and forced label. The sum over all such labels through a root
  increases by at most a factor three, and each individual price is at most
  three times the old rooted allowance. This last assertion uses nonempty
  supports and nonnegative support weights.

  The complete local logarithmic factor is still polynomial of degree at most
  two: the old vacuum and action terms either acquire the factor
  <math|\<ell\><around|(|t|)><rsup|2>> or remain constant, the incoming
  regular occurrence is quadratic by hypothesis, and the enforcement factor
  uses held fine reads and is parameter-independent. The forced local factor
  likewise is its incoming quadratic value multiplied by a fixed enforcement
  factor. Apply the small-activity estimate of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:real-coupling-interpolation> to the ordinary
  exponential-minus-one factors. Its smallness conditions are satisfied
  eventually: if <math|g<around|(|x|)>> is the incoming allowance, then
  <math|3*g<around|(|x|)>\<to\>0> and <math|R<rsup|2>*g<around|(|x|)>> has
  negative upper exponent. For a forced factor use ordinary degree-two
  interpolation at the same three nodes; there is no logarithm to take. The
  resulting common prices cost at most a fixed constant times
  <math|1+R<rsup|2>>. Their upper exponent is at most

  <\equation*>
    a+<frac|2|256>\<leqslant\>-<frac|55|128>\<less\>-<frac|2|5>.
  </equation*>

  Thus both are paid by an enlarged target with upper exponent <math|-2/5>,
  after increasing the scale threshold. This explains the specific reserve in
  <eqref|eq:sector-radius-choice>.

  For seed packets, use the larger-real-node estimate with the full stock
  weight. If a packet contains <math|s\<geqslant\>1> seeds, its real price
  has the form

  <\equation*>
    exp <around|{|<around|(|C*\<frak-d\><rsup|5>-\<beta\>*\<mu\>*\<tau\><rsup|2>|)>*s|}>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<frak-d\>\<geqslant\>1> is the local geometric control
  function. Its stock-coordinate count satisfies
  <math|n\<leqslant\>C<rsub|1>*s*\<frak-d\><rsup|5>>. The interpolation
  factor is <math|K<rsub|j>*<around|(|1+\<delta\>|)><rsup|n>>. Since
  <math|0\<leqslant\>\<delta\>\<leqslant\>1>,
  <math|<around|(|1+\<delta\>|)><rsup|n>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|n>>, and
  <math|K<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|K<rsub|j>>> for
  <math|K<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>0>. Hence increasing <math|C> to
  <math|C+C<rsub|1>+K<rsub|j>> absorbs the entire factor, because
  <math|s*\<frak-d\><rsup|5>\<geqslant\>1>. The negative packet energy is
  unchanged. The absorption and root-count argument of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:packet-estimates> consequently still
  applies.

  Warnings are constant in this parameter when evaluated with the specified
  held fine reads and rescaled fluctuation coordinate. Their coarse-field
  jets retain the common reduced-margin excursion indicator of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:activity-jet-assembly>. No derivative of
  that indicator is taken. Products of the four species thus have one common
  nonnegative coefficient majorant, with the same excursion event and the
  same support set as before interpolation.

  There is one further factor from the local Gaussian ratio and the local
  stock coordinate count. Let <math|I<around|(|S|)>> be the set of fine bonds
  read by the local term with coarse support <math|S>, and let
  <math|d<rsub|G>> be the real color dimension. Its combined power is bounded
  by <math|2<around|\||I<around|(|S|)>|\|>*d<rsub|G>>. If
  <math|c<rsub|B><around|(|S|)>> is the coarse cover number, then

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||I<around|(|S|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||S|\|><space|0.17em><math-up|dens><around|(|L|)>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|B><around|(|S|)>*b<rsub|B><math-up|dens><around|(|L|)>,<space|2em>b<rsub|B>=<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>.
  </equation*>

  For any fixed <math|\<alpha\>\<gtr\>0>, <eqref|eq:sector-radius-choice>
  gives, eventually, <math|2*\<delta\>*b<rsub|B><math-up|dens><around|(|L|)>*d<rsub|G>\<leqslant\>\<alpha\>>.
  Using <math|1+\<delta\>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<delta\>>> proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:sector-local-dimension><around|(|1+\<delta\>|)><rsup|2<around|\||I<around|(|S|)>|\|>*d<rsub|G>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<alpha\>*c<rsub|B><around|(|S|)>>.
  </equation>

  Only the dimension of the local read set has been charged. The factor is
  paid by a support-exponent reserve of size <math|\<alpha\>>.

  <subsubsection|The moment estimate after a finite parameter test>

  Let <math|A> be a connected finite set of primitive labels. Denote its
  assigned union support by <math|S<rsub|A>> and its warning set by
  <math|W<rsub|A>>. Its shape charge is the total cost of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mixed-activity-prices>,

  <\equation*>
    s<rsub|A>=<big|sum><rsub|l\<in\>A><around*|(|c<rsub|B><around|(|S<rsub|l>|)>+d<rsub|l>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|S<rsub|l>> is the support of the label <math|l> and
  <math|d<rsub|l>\<geqslant\>0> its extra cost: the purchased fixed multiple
  of its number of seeds for a packet, and the purchased optional cost for an
  optional label. Let <math|a<rsub|l>\<geqslant\>0> be the common primitive
  price for label <math|l>, with its warning gain included only after
  Gaussian integration. Put <math|P<rsub|A>=1+s<rsub|A>\<geqslant\>1>. The
  estimates just proved, the compensated Jacobian estimates, and the product
  rule give a coefficient bound for every derivative through order <math|j>
  of the local integrand. After a finite test <math|\<lambda\>>, its norm is
  bounded by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:sector-integrand-test>M<rsub|R><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>*<space|0.17em>C<rsub|j>*e<rsup|\<alpha\>*c<rsub|B><around|(|S<rsub|A>|)>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|l\<in\>A>a<rsub|l><rsup|\<circ\>>|)>*P<rsub|A><rsup|j>*<space|0.17em>\<b-1\><rsub|\<cal-E\><rsub|A>><around|(|z|)>*<space|0.17em>F<rsub|I<around|(|A|)>><around|(|z|)><rsup|K*j>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|a<rsub|l><rsup|\<circ\>>> is the price before inserting the
  warning probability gain, <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|A>> is the joint
  reduced-margin excursion event, <math|F<rsub|I<around|(|A|)>>\<geqslant\>1>
  is the Gaussian coordinate control function on the local read set, and
  <math|K> is the fixed Jacobian polynomial order. The constant
  <math|C<rsub|j>> in this display may be replaced by a nonnegative function
  of upper exponent zero; this includes the compensated derivative scales.
  More explicitly, if those scales are <math|r> and
  <math|L<rprime|'>*F<rsub|I<around|(|A|)>><rsup|K>>, their product-rule cost
  is bounded by

  <\equation*>
    <around|{|1+<around|(|2<rsup|K>*L<rprime|'>+2*r|)>|}><rsup|j>*P<rsub|A><rsup|j>*F<rsub|I<around|(|A|)>><rsup|K*j>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed <math|F<rsub|I<around|(|A|)>>,P<rsub|A>\<geqslant\>1> and
  <math|s<rsub|A>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|A>>, so
  <math|1+2<rsup|K>*L<rprime|'>*F<rsub|I<around|(|A|)>><rsup|K>+2*r*s<rsub|A>>
  is at most <math|<around|{|1+<around|(|2<rsup|K>*L<rprime|'>+2*r|)>|}>*P<rsub|A>*F<rsub|I<around|(|A|)>><rsup|K>>.
  Fixed polarization constants are included in <math|C<rsub|j>>.

  For clarity, the probabilistic step in this estimate is as follows. The
  local Gaussian moment calculation in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-moment-prices>
  supplies an integer <math|k> and a function <math|V<around|(|x|)>> of upper
  exponent zero such that

  <\equation*>
    <around*|(|<big|int>F<rsub|I<around|(|A|)>><rsup|2*K*j>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\>|)><rsup|1/2>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+<around|\||V<around|(|x|)>|\|>|)>*P<rsub|A><rsup|k>.
  </equation*>

  The common warning estimate is <math|\<nu\><around|(|\<cal-E\><rsub|A>|)>\<leqslant\>\<rho\><rsup|2<around|\||W<rsub|A>|\|>>>.
  Cauchy\USchwarz therefore gives

  <\equation*>
    <big|int>\<b-1\><rsub|\<cal-E\><rsub|A>>*F<rsub|I<around|(|A|)>><rsup|K*j>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+<around|\||V<around|(|x|)>|\|>|)>*P<rsub|A><rsup|k>*\<rho\><rsup|<around|\||W<rsub|A>|\|>>.
  </equation*>

  This is the source of the warning price in <math|a<rsub|l>>; no
  independence of the warning events is assumed. Combining with
  <eqref|eq:sector-integrand-test>, and then averaging the finitely many
  forest and tagging choices, gives the derivative price

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:sector-polymer-price>p<rsub|A>=\<pi\><around|(|x|)>*e<rsup|\<alpha\>*c<rsub|B><around|(|S<rsub|A>|)>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|l\<in\>A>a<rsub|l>|)>*P<rsub|A><rsup|k+j>,
  </equation>

  where <math|\<pi\>> has upper exponent zero and is independent of
  <math|\<lambda\>>. The bound on the tested derivative is
  <math|M<rsub|R><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>*p<rsub|A>>.

  Passing derivatives through the integral is justified by the nearby
  integrable bounds in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-differentiation>.
  There are only finitely many parameter points in a test, so finite
  summation commutes with derivatives and integrals. A finite average over
  forests preserves the same bound. On healthy supports the protected gauge
  average equals the unaveraged local expression as a germ: the safety
  plateau is open and the gauge change leaves the protected expression
  invariant. Thus the estimate is for the actual coarse activity, including
  its derivatives. An inactive support gives the zero germ. All of these
  comparisons use the common support dictionary, not a parameter-dependent
  relabeling.

  The mixed connected-set estimate <eqref|eq:mixed-reserved-tail> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mixed-activity-prices>, applied to the
  enlarged primitive prices, gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:sector-first-root>sup<rsub|z>
    <big|sum><rsub|A:z\<in\>S<rsub|A>>p<rsub|A>*e<rsup|<around|(|E-\<alpha\>|)>*c<rsub|B><around|(|S<rsub|A>|)>>\<leqslant\>\<pi\><around|(|x|)>*u<rsub|k+j><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|E> is the incoming support exponent. For an integer
  <math|n\<geqslant\>0>, <math|u<rsub|n><around|(|x|)>> denotes the connected
  allowance of the enlarged target with polynomial order <math|n>: the right
  side of <eqref|eq:mixed-ceiling> for that target, with no restriction on
  the label count, the total cost or the number of seeds. The vertex
  condition of that estimate is the purchase of the enlarged target made in
  the first subsection; the factor <math|P<rsub|A><rsup|k+j>=<around|(|1+s<rsub|A>|)><rsup|k+j>>
  in <math|p<rsub|A>> is its polynomial in the total cost. The allowance
  <math|u<rsub|k+j>> has upper exponent <math|-2/5>. To see the support
  bookkeeping, the left side contains <math|e<rsup|\<alpha\>*c<rsub|B><around|(|S<rsub|A>|)>>*e<rsup|<around|(|E-\<alpha\>|)>*c<rsub|B><around|(|S<rsub|A>|)>>=e<rsup|E*c<rsub|B><around|(|S<rsub|A>|)>>>.
  Thus the mixed connected-set estimate is applied with its original support
  weight, and its shape moment is exactly <math|k+j>. This invokes the same
  connected-set and moment bound used for the non-parameter sector, with the
  enlarged primitive prices just constructed.

  <subsubsection|The second connected expansion>

  Recover an absolute parameter series for each derivative from the
  finite-test estimate with radius <math|R>.
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:background-ray-estimates> gives radius
  <math|R/2> and price <math|2*p<rsub|A>>, once <math|R\<geqslant\>2>. The
  argument can first be applied to each scalar derivative; the fixed-order
  polarization bound controls the full derivative. The derivatives of the
  outgoing budget are iterated group increments in directions of norm at most
  the threshold <math|\<theta\>>, whereas the local estimates are stated in
  the compensated charts. The passage between the two is part of the uniform
  local hypotheses: appending one such increment increases the controlled
  chart scale by at most a fixed multiple <math|c<rsub|v>> of the threshold
  scale, and the chart scale <math|v> is chosen with
  <math|j*c<rsub|v>\<leqslant\>v>, as in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:parameter-comparison-suppliers>.
  Thus the at most <math|j> increments of a derivative of the budget stay
  inside the chart estimates used above. A logarithmic cluster with <math|q>
  occurrences is a product of these primitive integrated activities times its
  Ursell coefficient and <math|1/q>!. Derivatives through order <math|j> cost
  at most <math|q<rsup|j>>. Since <math|q\<leqslant\>2<rsup|q>> for
  <math|q\<geqslant\>1>, this is paid by multiplying each vertex price by
  <math|2<rsup|j>>. Consequently the absolute cluster price is

  <\equation*>
    w<rsub|\<Gamma\>>=<frac|<around|\||u<around|(|\<Gamma\>|)>|\|>|q!>*<big|prod><rsub|r=1><rsup|q><around|(|2<rsup|j+1>*p<rsub|A<rsub|r>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  This includes repeated labels and their tuple factorial exactly as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:cluster-expansion>.

  Fix the desired outgoing exponent <math|e> with
  <math|0\<leqslant\>e\<less\>E>, and set
  <math|\<alpha\>=<around|(|E-e|)>/4\<gtr\>0>. Put

  <\equation*>
    Y<around|(|x|)>=<around|(|1+2<rsup|j+1>*\<pi\><around|(|x|)>|)>*u<rsub|k+j><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:sector-first-root> pays the enlarged vertex prices
  at exponent <math|e+2*\<alpha\>>, because
  <math|e+2*\<alpha\>\<leqslant\>E-\<alpha\>>. The connected-tree degree
  criterion is

  <\equation*>
    <frac|4*b<rsub|B>*Y<around|(|x|)>|\<alpha\>>\<leqslant\><frac|1|2>.
  </equation*>

  It holds eventually: <math|b<rsub|B>> has upper exponent zero and <math|Y>
  has upper exponent <math|-2/5>. The cluster estimate then gives

  <\equation*>
    sup<rsub|z> <big|sum><rsub|\<Gamma\>:z\<in\>S<rsub|\<Gamma\>>>w<rsub|\<Gamma\>>*e<rsup|<around|(|e+\<alpha\>|)>*c<rsub|B><around|(|S<rsub|\<Gamma\>>|)>>\<leqslant\>2*Y<around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  The spatially weighted derivative budget at a root costs at most
  <math|<around|{|s<rsub|B>*<around|(|1+c<rsub|B><around|(|S<rsub|\<Gamma\>>|)>|)>|}><rsup|m*j>>,
  where <math|s<rsub|B>=4*<around|(|2*B+1|)>>. To verify the geometry,
  connect the balls of a minimal connected cover. A path through at most
  <math|c<rsub|B><around|(|S<rsub|\<Gamma\>>|)>> balls bounds the distance
  between two sites of <math|S<rsub|\<Gamma\>>> by
  <math|c<rsub|B><around|(|S<rsub|\<Gamma\>>|)>*<around|(|4*B+2|)>+2*B>,
  which is at most <math|s<rsub|B>*<around|(|1+c<rsub|B><around|(|S<rsub|\<Gamma\>>|)>|)>>.
  Locality allows the field variation to be restricted to this support. The
  weighted direction norm on that support is therefore increased by the
  stated distance factor, and taking at most <math|j> derivatives gives the
  power <math|m*j>. For every fixed integer <math|d\<geqslant\>0>,

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|\<alpha\>,d>\<assign\>sup<rsub|r\<geqslant\>0><around|(|1+r|)><rsup|d>*e<rsup|-\<alpha\>*r>\<less\>\<infty\>.
  </equation*>

  For example, expand <math|<around|(|1+r|)><rsup|d>> and bound each
  <math|r<rsup|l>> by <math|l!\<alpha\><rsup|-l>*e<rsup|\<alpha\>*r>>, using
  the exponential series. The extra spatial factor is thus paid by the
  remaining <math|\<alpha\>> reserve. Including the fixed polarization factor
  <math|C<rsub|j><rprime|'>> we obtain the final rooted derivative price

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:sector-ordinary-final>2*C<rsub|j><rprime|'>*s<rsub|B><rsup|m*j>*P<rsub|\<alpha\>,m*j>*Y<around|(|x|)>.
  </equation>

  It has upper exponent <math|-2/5>. Since <math|-2/5\<less\>-3/8>, it is
  eventually at most <math|y<rsup|-3/8>>. This is the ordinary parameter-test
  allowance at radius <math|R/2>, on the actual connected-cluster dictionary.

  <subsubsection|The forced common majorant>

  For forced terms no coarse derivatives are required. The same finite-test
  and radius-halving argument gives ordinary constituent series prices with
  rooted allowance <math|2*u<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>> and forced constituent
  prices with rooted allowance <math|2*u<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>*e<rsup|-M<around|(|x|)>>>.
  The nonnegative mark <math|M<around|(|x|)>> is the seed mark of the
  enlarged target: the mark per seed which the mixed estimate
  <eqref|eq:mixed-ceiling> extracts once for every seed of a component, and
  which is paid from the seed expense of the packet prices. It is available
  here because every forced constituent has a nonempty flag inventory, each
  flag supplies a seed in one of its packets, and hence its components
  contain at least one seed. It is distinct from the warning probability gain
  <math|\<rho\><rsup|<around|\||W<rsub|A>|\|>>>, which has already been
  included in the primitive prices <math|a<rsub|l>> and is present in
  ordinary and forced constituents alike. Form the exclusion-correction
  activities exactly as in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:forced-exclusion>.
  Each is a finite product of forced factors and powers of regular cluster
  factors, with the same multiplicity factorials. Absolute coefficient norms
  multiply, so <eqref|eq:exclusion-output-price> is also a majorant for the
  whole parameter series at radius <math|R/2>.

  The remaining eligibility and flag indicators depend on the fixed coarse
  configuration and common supports, and are independent of the parameter.
  Multiplication by such an indicator only decreases the coefficient bound.
  This yields one majorant per final support, valid for all coarse
  configurations, rather than a separate majorant selected after evaluating a
  configuration.

  Apply the forced-exclusion bound with its
  <math|u=2*u<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>> and the reserve <math|\<alpha\>>. Its
  second-expansion allowance is <math|Z<around|(|x|)>=20*u=40*u<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>>.
  Both <math|Z\<leqslant\>1/2> and <math|4*b<rsub|B>*Z/\<alpha\>\<leqslant\>1/2>
  hold eventually. Hence the final common majorants <math|k<rsub|D>> satisfy

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:sector-forced-final>sup<rsub|z>
    <big|sum><rsub|D:z\<in\>S<rsub|D>>k<rsub|D>*e<rsup|e*c<rsub|B><around|(|S<rsub|D>|)>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-M<around|(|x|)>>*<frac|Z<around|(|x|)>|1-1/2>.
  </equation>

  This keeps the compulsory exponential seed gain through arbitrarily many
  regular exclusion corrections. In particular, the parameter extension has
  not consumed that gain.

  Equations<nbsp><eqref|eq:sector-ordinary-final> and
  <eqref|eq:sector-forced-final> are the broad analytic estimates for the
  sector output before its finite Taylor and local projection operations.
  They use the uniform local sector estimates, the same connected-support
  sums, and the same Gaussian warning bounds as the real one-step
  construction. The later projection prices and the construction of the
  allowed coupling family are separate inputs when these estimates are
  applied to the selected background history.

  <subsection|Recovering a local density from five auxiliary
  periods><label|sec:five-period-localization>

  The local projection in the nonlinear step uses five small periodic boxes.
  Their side lengths differ by two. A fourth finite difference cancels all
  the contributions from folded boundary faces and retains the interior
  contribution. We give the construction, since both the exact identity and
  its derivative cost matter for the parameter estimates.

  <subsubsection|Folded fields and boundary types>

  For an integer <math|w\<geqslant\>1>, fold the cyclic set
  <math|\<bbb-Z\>/<around|(|2*w|)>*\<bbb-Z\>> onto
  <math|<around|{|0,\<ldots\>,w|}>> by

  <\equation*>
    \<pi\><rsub|w><around|(|q|)>=min <around|(|<wide|q|\<bar\>>,2*w-<wide|q|\<bar\>>|)>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\><wide|q|\<bar\>>\<less\>2*w.
  </equation*>

  Its two endpoints have one preimage each and its other points have two.
  Apply this map in each of the four coordinates. Adjacent sites map to
  adjacent sites, with direction either preserved or reversed. For a gauge
  field <math|U> on a larger torus and an anchor <math|o>, the folded field
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|w,o>*U> on the side-<math|2*w> torus reads the link of
  <math|U> between <math|o+\<pi\><rsub|w><around|(|q|)>> and
  <math|o+\<pi\><rsub|w>*<around|(|q+e<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>>; a reversed link is
  read as its inverse. Here and below a lattice point is reduced modulo the
  large period when used to index <math|U>.

  Fix <math|r\<geqslant\>1> and <math|w\<geqslant\>2*r>. In one coordinate,
  give a folded point <math|p\<in\><around|{|0,\<ldots\>,w|}>> its boundary
  type:

  <\equation*>
    <choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|<math-up|left>,p|)>,>|<cell|p\<less\>r,>>|<row|<cell|<around|(|<math-up|right>,w-p|)>,>|<cell|w-p\<less\>r,>>|<row|<cell|<math-up|interior>,>|<cell|r\<leqslant\>p\<leqslant\>w-r.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  The cases are disjoint. For an offset <math|s> with
  <math|<around|\||s|\|>\<leqslant\>r>, the folded displacement from <math|p>
  is respectively

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:five-period-displacement><around|\||p+s|\|>-p,<space|2em>d-<around|\||d-s|\|>*<around|(|d=w-p|)>,<space|2em>s.
  </equation>

  These identities follow directly from the two affine branches of
  <math|\<pi\><rsub|w>>. They also describe links in a local box when both
  endpoints are in the box. Thus, after moving the anchor to cancel the
  folded base point, the local field near a site depends only on its four
  boundary types. The displacement formulas do not depend on <math|w>. An
  all-interior type gives the original, unfolded field near the chosen
  anchor.

  For a left or right type of depth <math|d\<in\><around|{|0,\<ldots\>,r-1|}>>,
  the number of cyclic points of that type is <math|1> if <math|d=0> and
  <math|2> otherwise. The interior count is

  <\equation*>
    2*<around|(|w-2*r+1|)>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed its folded coordinates are <math|r,\<ldots\>,w-r>, all strictly
  between the two endpoints, and each has two preimages. In four dimensions
  the number of sites of a prescribed type vector is the product of its four
  one-dimensional counts.

  Let <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|r>> be the finite set of four-coordinate type
  vectors, and let <math|\<tau\><rsub|\<ast\>>> be the all-interior type. For
  any function <math|H:\<cal-T\><rsub|r>\<to\>\<bbb-R\>>, the preceding count
  proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:five-period-counting><big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|4>c<rsub|i>*<big|sum><rsub|q\<in\><around|(|\<bbb-Z\>/2*<around|(|w+i|)>*\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|4>>H<around|(|<math-up|type><rsub|w+i,r><around|(|q|)>|)>=H<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|\<ast\>>|)>,<space|2em>c<rsub|i>=<frac|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|i><binom|4|i>|2<rsup|4>\<cdot\>4!>.
  </equation>

  To verify this exactly, fix a type vector with <math|d> interior
  coordinates. Its multiplicity at side <math|2*<around|(|w+i|)>> is a
  polynomial in <math|i> of degree <math|d>. The fourth difference
  annihilates that polynomial when <math|d\<less\>4>. For the all-interior
  type the polynomial is <math|2<rsup|4>*<around|(|w+i-2*r+1|)><rsup|4>>,
  whose fourth difference is <math|2<rsup|4>\<cdot\>4>!. Division by that
  factor gives one. Summing over the finitely many type vectors proves
  <eqref|eq:five-period-counting>. In particular, no boundary term has been
  discarded before cancellation.

  <subsubsection|Compatible local densities>

  Let <math|\<Lambda\>> be the site set of the large torus. Write
  <math|f<rsub|\<Lambda\>>*<around|(|o,U|)>> for a density on the large torus
  and <math|f<rsub|i>*<around|(|q,V|)>>, <math|0\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>4>,
  for densities on the five auxiliary tori of sides
  <math|2*<around|(|w+i|)>>. The compatibility conditions needed here are
  explicit: the auxiliary densities transform covariantly under signed
  lattice symmetries, the values of any two of them on globally healthy
  fields agree whenever their centered radius-<math|r> link patterns agree,
  and the same agreement holds between each of them and
  <math|f<rsub|\<Lambda\>>> on globally healthy fields. Finally
  <math|f<rsub|\<Lambda\>>*<around|(|o,\<cdot\>|)>> is local on the
  radius-<math|r> neighbourhood of <math|o>. The large period is at least
  <math|4*r+16>. These are compatibility of local functions at fixed coupling
  and fixed path; no equality of partition functions on different periods is
  required.

  Set <math|<wide|f|~><rsub|i>*<around|(|q,V|)>=f<rsub|i>*<around|(|q,V|)>-f<rsub|i>*<around|(|q,\<b-1\>|)>>
  and define <math|<wide|f|~><rsub|\<Lambda\>>> in the same way. The
  compatibility properties persist after this centering: the identity fields
  are healthy and have identical local link patterns. Define

  <\equation*>
    g*<around|(|o,U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|4>c<rsub|i>*<big|sum><rsub|q\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|i>><wide|f|~><rsub|i>*<around|(|q,\<Phi\><rsub|w+i,o>*U|)>,<space|2em>\<Lambda\><rsub|i>=<around|(|\<bbb-Z\>/2*<around|(|w+i|)>*\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|4>.
  </equation*>

  Choose <math|z\<geqslant\>w+4>. The function
  <math|g*<around|(|o,\<cdot\>|)>> reads only the radius-<math|z>
  neighbourhood of <math|o>. For each auxiliary site <math|q> set
  <math|o<rsub|i,q>=o-\<pi\><rsub|w+i><around|(|q|)>>, coordinatewise, and
  put

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:five-period-shifted-localization>h*<around|(|o,U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|4>c<rsub|i>*<big|sum><rsub|q\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|i>>\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|U<text|
    healthy on >N<rsub|z><around|(|o<rsub|i,q>|)>|}>>*<wide|f|~><rsub|i>*<around|(|q,\<Phi\><rsub|w+i,o<rsub|i,q>>*U|)>.
  </equation>

  The shift ensures that the physical point corresponding to <math|q> is
  <math|o>. Here <math|N<rsub|z><around|(|a|)>> denotes the radius-<math|z>
  lattice neighbourhood of <math|a>.

  If <math|U> is healthy on <math|N<rsub|2*z><around|(|o|)>>, every indicator
  in <eqref|eq:five-period-shifted-localization> equals one:
  <math|<math-up|dist><around|(|o,o<rsub|i,q>|)>\<leqslant\>w+i\<leqslant\>z>
  and <math|N<rsub|z><around|(|o<rsub|i,q>|)>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|2*z><around|(|o|)>>.
  The folded auxiliary fields are then globally healthy. A folded plaquette
  is either an original plaquette with an orientation change, a conjugate of
  such a plaquette, or a degenerate loop with identity holonomy. The
  plaquette chord is invariant under conjugation and inversion; thus its
  bound follows from the healthy large-field bound on the read set.

  Reflection covariance first moves <math|q> to its canonical folded
  representative. The displacement formulas
  <eqref|eq:five-period-displacement>, followed by compatibility, show that
  the summand depends only on its type vector. For the all-interior vector it
  equals <math|<wide|f|~><rsub|\<Lambda\>>*<around|(|o,U|)>>. One can make
  the last comparison using a globally healthy extension on the original
  large torus. In each cyclic coordinate of length <math|c\<geqslant\>4*r>,
  take the track that increases from <math|0> to <math|2*r> in <math|2*r>
  steps, decreases back to <math|0> in the next <math|2*r> steps, and remains
  at <math|0> for the remaining <math|c-4*r> steps. Place its image at
  <math|o-r> and center its argument at the midpoint of the increasing
  segment. The resulting field agrees with <math|U> on the radius-<math|r>
  box about <math|o> and reads only that box everywhere. Each plaquette is
  again an original plaquette with orientation changed or an identity loop,
  so the extension is globally healthy. Compatibility applies to this field
  on the original period; locality then identifies its
  <math|f<rsub|\<Lambda\>>> value with <math|f<rsub|\<Lambda\>>*<around|(|o,U|)>>.
  Applying <eqref|eq:five-period-counting> gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:five-period-healthy-identity>h*<around|(|o,U|)>=<wide|f|~><rsub|\<Lambda\>>*<around|(|o,U|)><space|1em><text|if
    <math|U> is healthy on >N<rsub|2*z><around|(|o|)>.
  </equation>

  The compatibility in the selected construction is established by passing
  two given periods to their product period. Replicate each field to that
  period; the short local density is preserved by replication. Translate both
  base points to zero. Their local patterns still agree there, so locality of
  the common-period density gives equality. The selected auxiliary
  representations use the same path, coupling, profile and finite cutoff. It
  is these equalities that permit the replication comparison. The required
  replication and local-generation identities are part of the short-density
  construction; this argument does not assert compatibility for independently
  chosen histories. For the selected densities they are proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-nonlinear-offering>, where the five
  auxiliary densities are formed with the physical path, incoming budget,
  coupling, scalar correction and finite label cutoff. When the present
  section is applied, <math|f<rsub|\<Lambda\>>> and
  <math|f<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,f<rsub|4>> are those densities; the four
  compatibility conditions above are then conclusions of that section and not
  additional assumptions.

  <subsubsection|The exact additive decomposition>

  Let <math|F<rsub|a><around|(|U|)>> be the ordinary logarithmic entries
  retained in a finite short-support panel. Their allocation coefficients
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|o*a>> are nonnegative, sum to one in <math|o> for each
  <math|a>, and satisfy <math|S<rsub|a>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|r><around|(|o|)>>
  whenever <math|\<omega\><rsub|o*a>\<ne\>0>. Put

  <\equation*>
    E*<around|(|o,U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a>\<omega\><rsub|o*a>*\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|U<text|
    healthy on >S<rsub|a>|}>>*F<rsub|a><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  Its sum over <math|o> is exactly the masked contribution of the panel. The
  unmasked short density is <math|f<rsub|\<Lambda\>>*<around|(|o,U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a>\<omega\><rsub|o*a>*F<rsub|a><around|(|U|)>>.
  Translation covariance makes <math|f<rsub|\<Lambda\>>*<around|(|o,\<b-1\>|)>>
  independent of <math|o>. Define the discrepancy

  <\equation*>
    d*<around|(|o,U|)>=E*<around|(|o,U|)>-f<rsub|\<Lambda\>>*<around|(|o,\<b-1\>|)>-h*<around|(|o,U|)>.
  </equation*>

  This definition, followed by the change of variable
  <math|o\<mapsto\>o-\<pi\><rsub|w+i><around|(|q|)>> in each finite site sum,
  proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:five-period-exact-decomposition><big|sum><rsub|a>\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|U<text|
    healthy on >S<rsub|a>|}>>*F<rsub|a><around|(|U|)>=<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*f<rsub|\<Lambda\>>*<around|(|0,\<b-1\>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|o>\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|U<text|
    healthy on >N<rsub|z><around|(|o|)>|}>>*g*<around|(|o,U|)>+<big|sum><rsub|o>d*<around|(|o,U|)>.
  </equation>

  Indeed this change of variable identifies
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|o>h*<around|(|o,U|)>> with the middle sum. It does not
  require that <math|U> itself be healthy. If <math|U> is healthy on
  <math|N<rsub|2*z><around|(|o|)>>, the allocation supports near <math|o> are
  healthy, so <math|E*<around|(|o,U|)>=f<rsub|\<Lambda\>>*<around|(|o,U|)>>.
  Equation <eqref|eq:five-period-healthy-identity> then gives
  <math|d*<around|(|o,U|)>=0>. Thus the discrepancy is supported on an
  unhealthy enlarged neighbourhood. The regular output retains <math|g>, the
  constant contribution is extracted, and the forced correction uses
  <math|e<rsup|-d>-1>. The negative sign follows by solving
  <eqref|eq:five-period-exact-decomposition> for the new logarithmic sum in
  the density convention of the nonlinear step.

  <subsubsection|The derivative cost of folding>

  Let the derivative order be <math|j>, the spatial weight power be <math|m>,
  and the small-direction threshold be <math|\<theta\>\<gtr\>0>. Suppose a
  function <math|F> on an auxiliary torus is <math|C<rsup|j>> in local
  exponential charts at every globally healthy field, and has there all
  iterated left derivatives through order <math|j> bounded by <math|q> when
  each direction has norm at most <math|\<theta\>>. On a healthy
  radius-<math|z> neighbourhood, the corresponding bound for
  <math|F\<circ\>\<Phi\><rsub|w+i,o>>, with directions allowed to grow as
  <math|\<theta\>*<around|(|1+<math-up|dist><around|(|o,x|)>|)><rsup|m>>, is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:five-period-derivative-cost>2<rsup|j>*<around|(|1+z|)><rsup|m*j>*q.
  </equation>

  The factor two per derivative is needed because a reversed link transforms
  a left variation into a right variation. We justify that point rather than
  identifying the two derivatives.

  On a forward auxiliary link the induced variation is a left variation by
  the corresponding original direction; on a reversed link it is a right
  variation by the negative original direction. The two types have disjoint
  link coordinates, fixed by the folding map. Consequently left and right
  variations on different types commute. For a list of <math|k> derivatives,
  expand each variation into its left and right part. There are at most
  <math|2<rsup|k>> terms. In each term, right derivatives can be moved past
  all left derivatives, and at the fixed field converted to left derivatives
  by adjoint conjugation. Their norms are unchanged because the group metric
  is adjoint invariant. Explicitly, start with the empty list. For a leading
  mixed direction <math|<around|(|v<rsub|L>,v<rsub|R>|)>> and each converted
  list <math|s> for the remaining directions, the two new lists are
  <math|v<rsub|L>::s> and <math|s<math-plus|+<space|-0.17em>+><around|[|<math-up|Ad><rsub|V><space|0.17em>v<rsub|R>|]>>,
  with the adjoint chosen to turn the right action at the fixed field
  <math|V> into a left action. Thus the right directions occur in reverse
  order. The disjoint link supports ensure that this recursion introduces no
  derivatives of a varying conjugating field. Each resulting direction has
  norm at most <math|X*\<theta\>>, where <math|X=<around|(|1+z|)><rsup|m>>.
  Multilinearity and rescaling by <math|X<rsup|-1>> bound the term by
  <math|X<rsup|k>*q>. Summation and <math|k\<leqslant\>j> prove
  <eqref|eq:five-period-derivative-cost>. The smooth chart property under
  composition follows since a link read, its inverse and a constant identity
  link are smooth maps.

  It follows that the ordinary localized density has derivative price

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:five-period-local-price>\<cal-O\><rsub|z>*<around|(|q<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,q<rsub|4>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|4><around|\||c<rsub|i>|\|>*<space|0.17em>2<rsup|j>*<around|(|1+z|)><rsup|m*j>*q<rsub|i>,
  </equation>

  when <math|q<rsub|i>> bounds the centered total auxiliary density
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|q><wide|f|~><rsub|i>*<around|(|q,\<cdot\>|)>>. The
  support is <math|N<rsub|z><around|(|o|)>>. For the ordinary derivative
  budget, each entry has an assigned anchor; its budget at a root <math|o>
  sums only entries assigned to <math|o>, weighted by
  <math|e<rsup|e*c<rsub|B><around|(|S|)>>>. Thus if
  <math|c<rsub|B><around|(|N<rsub|z><around|(|o|)>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|z>>,
  the entry <math|g<around|(|o|)>> costs <math|\<cal-O\><rsub|z><around|(|q|)>*e<rsup|e*C<rsub|z>>>
  in that anchored budget. A sum over every support containing a fixed site
  would instead cost the additional factor <math|<around|(|2*z+1|)><rsup|4>>.
  A fixed signed-orientation average and its local allocation multiply this
  price by the already purchased orientation cost. All these factors depend
  only on the fixed local geometry, and have upper exponent zero in the scale
  parameter.

  <subsection|Parameter estimates after the local
  projection><label|sec:projected-parameter-prices>

  We transfer the estimates of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:sector-analytic-prices>
  to the actual ordinary and forced arrays after localization. All finite
  tests below use the common parameter family, and every local cutoff is
  applied to its original fixed support. We retain the compatibility and
  local-generation identities specified in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:five-period-localization>.

  The ordinary budget below is anchored: at site <math|o> it sums the
  nonnegative derivative prices of entries whose assigned anchor is <math|o>,
  with their exponential support weights. The forced budget sums all supports
  containing the site. The sector bounds imply the anchored ordinary bounds
  by restricting their support sums to the assigned anchors.

  Let <math|P> be the outgoing derivative budget, with derivative order
  <math|j>, spatial weight power <math|m>, radius <math|B>, threshold
  <math|\<theta\>> and support exponent <math|b>. Put
  <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>>=b+3>. Let <math|y=\<beta\><rsub|f><around|(|x|)>> and
  <math|R=y<rsup|1/256>>, as before. Assume the path rows have their
  purchased strip and coefficient bounds, their regular local readings are
  smooth on healthy supports, and the support reserve fits inside half the
  path strip. Their ordinary rooted allowance, before the finite orientation
  average, is at most <math|y<rsup|-7/16>>. These are the path inputs to the
  present argument. There are four types of path rows. The cubic and quartic
  path-atom rows carry the coupling as a factor, and are therefore multiplied
  by <math|\<ell\><around|(|t|)><rsup|2>> along the parameter line. The
  low-path rows, whose coefficient is the path correction fixed along the
  line, and the quadratic loop-atom rows are unchanged. Since
  <math|<around|\||\<ell\><rsup|2>|\|><rsub|R>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+\<delta\>|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>4>
  and <math|<around|\||1|\|><rsub|R>=1>, the finite-test allowance of all
  four types at radius <math|R/2> is at most four times the original
  allowance.

  Let <math|T<rsub|P>\<gtr\>0> be the fixed orientation and derivative
  allocation cost. Combining the path terms and the sector logarithmic terms,
  and applying that allocation, gives the price

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:projected-initial-price>W<around|(|x|)>=T<rsub|P>*<around|{|y<rsup|-3/8>+4*y<rsup|-7/16>|}>.
  </equation>

  For every finite test <math|\<lambda\>>, the ordinary family has rooted
  derivative price <math|M<rsub|R/2><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>*W<around|(|x|)>>
  at exponent <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>>>. A fixed finite short-support panel may
  be removed from this family with the same bound: keep the original
  nonnegative price on the retained addresses and put zero on the deleted
  ones. Neither the parameter dictionary nor its support sets change. This
  observation includes every finite truncation used in the short-density
  construction, with no dependence of <math|W> on the truncation order.

  <subsubsection|Allocating and testing the auxiliary densities>

  For the five auxiliary periods <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:five-period-localization>, form the same
  finite-panel local densities <math|f<rsub|i>*<around|(|q,U|)>>. The
  nonnegative allocation coefficients are bounded by one, sum to one over
  <math|q>, and only assign an entry to anchors in its prescribed
  neighbourhood. Write <math|s> for the radius of that neighbourhood and
  <math|b<rsub|s>=<around|(|2*s+1|)><rsup|4>>.

  For a tested density at a fixed site <math|q>, take absolute values in its
  allocation formula. Each nonzero entry is assigned to an anchor within
  <math|N<rsub|s><around|(|q|)>>. Summing first over those anchors and then
  using the rooted bound gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:projected-local-test><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|h>a<rsub|h>*f<rsub|i>*<around|(|t<rsub|h>,q,U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|R/2><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>*b<rsub|s>*W<around|(|x|)>
  </equation>

  on globally healthy <math|U>. The same estimate holds for the masked panel
  on an arbitrary field: outside its healthy support an entry is zero. Here
  the mask is independent of the parameter, so testing commutes with masking.

  The total auxiliary density is exactly the sum of the panel entries, since
  the allocations sum to one. Summing the derivative prices over anchors
  therefore bounds its derivatives by

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|i>|\|>*W<around|(|x|)>*M<rsub|R/2><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Subtracting its value at the identity field costs at most twice that
  amount, uniformly through derivative order <math|j>; positive derivatives
  of the subtracted constant vanish. Thus the two probe prices needed below
  are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:projected-probe-prices>p<rsub|i>=2<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|i>|\|>*W<around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>q<rsub|i>=b<rsub|s>*W<around|(|x|)>.
  </equation>

  The first bounds derivatives of the centered total density; the second
  bounds each uncentered local density on healthy fields. The identity field
  is healthy because its plaquette holonomies are all the identity and
  <math|\<theta\>\<gtr\>0>.

  <subsubsection|The ordinary output price>

  Let <math|g*<around|(|t,o,U|)>> be the five-period local density and
  <math|d*<around|(|t,o,U|)>> its discrepancy. Put

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-O\><rsub|z><around|(|p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|4><around|\||c<rsub|i>|\|>*<space|0.17em>2<rsup|j>*<around|(|1+z|)><rsup|m*j>*p<rsub|i>,<space|2em>C<rsub|z>\<geqslant\>sup<rsub|o>
    c<rsub|B><around|(|N<rsub|z><around|(|o|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  All operations in the formula for <math|g> are finite linear operations on
  the densities. Hence a finite test commutes with that formula. Apply
  <eqref|eq:five-period-derivative-cost> to the tested auxiliary total
  densities; they are finite sums of the regular local readings, hence have
  the smoothness on healthy fields which that estimate requires. The result
  is the regular support price <math|M<rsub|R/2><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>*\<cal-O\><rsub|z><around|(|p|)>>
  at each anchor. Multiplying by the support weight gives
  <math|M<rsub|R/2><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>*\<cal-O\><rsub|z><around|(|p|)>*e<rsup|b<rsub|\<ast\>>*C<rsub|z>>>.
  The existing complementary panel has price
  <math|M<rsub|R/2><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>*W<around|(|x|)>>. After the
  orientation allocation, the total ordinary output price is therefore

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:projected-ordinary-price>A<around|(|x|)>=W<around|(|x|)>+T<rsub|P>*\<cal-O\><rsub|z><around|(|p|)>*e<rsup|b<rsub|\<ast\>>*C<rsub|z>>.
  </equation>

  This is a bound on the full derivative budget on each healthy support, not
  just on the sum of the array values.

  In the selected geometry, <math|s,z> and all five auxiliary volumes have
  upper exponent zero, and the cover bound <math|C<rsub|z>> is a constant.
  Explicitly, with <math|r> the locality radius of the short density, the
  auxiliary sides are <math|2*<around|(|w+i|)>> with
  <math|w=4*r+100*<around|(|1+B|)>>, and <math|z=w+5>; here <math|r> is
  bounded by a fixed multiple of <math|B>. Hence there is <math|C<rsub|b>>,
  depending only on the fixed halo parameter of the target, on which <math|r>
  depends, with <math|2*z\<leqslant\>C<rsub|b>*B> for <math|B\<geqslant\>1>,
  and every neighbourhood <math|N<rsub|u><around|(|o|)>> with
  <math|u\<leqslant\>2*z> is covered by at most
  <math|<around|(|2*C<rsub|b>+1|)><rsup|4>> balls of radius <math|B>. Thus
  <math|C<rsub|z>> and the constant <math|C<rsub|2*z>> used below may be
  taken equal to <math|<around|(|2*C<rsub|b>+1|)><rsup|4>>, independently of
  the scale. This is needed: a cover bound of upper exponent zero would not
  make <math|e<rsup|b<rsub|\<ast\>>*C<rsub|z>>> harmless. The derivative
  order and spatial weight power are fixed before the scale is increased.
  Consequently <math|A> has upper exponent <math|-3/8>, and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:projected-broad-bound>A<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|-1/4>
  </equation>

  eventually. The strict exponent gap absorbs every fixed orientation
  constant and every factor of upper exponent zero. This is the broad
  ordinary bound used in the finite-test sharpening of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:background-test-prices>; its radius is
  <math|R/2=y<rsup|1/256>/2>.

  <subsubsection|A common series majorant for the discrepancy>

  A discrepancy value is the difference between the allocated masked panel,
  its identity-field constant, and the shifted masked auxiliary sum. Applying
  <eqref|eq:projected-local-test> to the first two terms gives
  <math|2*b<rsub|s>*W<around|(|x|)>*M<rsub|R/2><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>>. For
  the last term, each centered auxiliary local value is bounded by
  <math|2*q<rsub|i>*M<rsub|R/2><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>> when its mask is one,
  since the folded field is then globally healthy. It is zero when its mask
  is zero. Hence, for every field <math|U> and anchor <math|o>,

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|h>a<rsub|h>*d*<around|(|t<rsub|h>,o,U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|R/2><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>*<around*|{|2*b<rsub|s>*W<around|(|x|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|4><around|\||c<rsub|i>|\|><around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|i>|\|>*<space|0.17em>2*q<rsub|i>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Write <math|D> for the expression in braces. The finite-test functional
  argument gives coefficients <math|d<rsub|n>*<around|(|o,U|)>> satisfying
  <math|<around|\||d<rsub|n>*<around|(|o,U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>D*<around|(|2/R|)><rsup|n>>
  for every <math|U>, with the same <math|D>. Therefore
  <math|D*<around|(|2/R|)><rsup|n>> is a common coefficientwise envelope; its
  weighted sum at radius <math|R/4> is <math|<big|sum><rsub|n>D*2<rsup|-n>=2*D>.
  Thus

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:projected-discrepancy-value><around|\||d|(>\<cdot\>,o,U)\|<rsub|R/4>\<leqslant\>a<around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>a<around|(|x|)>=2*<around*|{|2*b<rsub|s>*W<around|(|x|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|4><around|\||c<rsub|i>|\|><around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|i>|\|>*<space|0.17em>2*q<rsub|i>|}>.
  </equation>

  This bound is uniform in <math|U>, and <math|R\<geqslant\>4> eventually. In
  particular there is one coefficient majorant for the discrepancy at each
  fixed support. The sum over orientation copies uses the normalized finite
  orientation average; for values it has norm one. Assigning each copy its
  actual averaging weight and counting the anchors whose radius-<math|2*z>
  support contains a root gives the rooted price

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:projected-discrepancy-root>H<around|(|x|)>=<around|(|4*z+1|)><rsup|4>*a<around|(|x|)>*e<rsup|<around|(|b+2|)>*C<rsub|2*z>>,<space|2em>C<rsub|2*z>\<geqslant\>sup<rsub|o>
    c<rsub|B><around|(|N<rsub|2*z><around|(|o|)>|)>.
  </equation>

  This has upper exponent <math|-3/8>. The signs of the discrepancy are
  retained in the functions; they disappear only in this nonnegative bound.

  <subsubsection|Fusing the discrepancy with the forced array>

  Let <math|\<gamma\><around|(|x|)>> be the forced series allowance supplied
  by <eqref|eq:sector-forced-final>, and let <math|\<phi\><around|(|x|)>> be
  the prescribed outgoing forced allowance. Use the sector allowance
  <math|\<gamma\>> at support exponent <math|b+2>, weakening its available
  <math|b+3> bound. The discrepancy allowance <math|H> is also at exponent
  <math|b+2>. The final forced bound will have exponent <math|b+1>, since the
  connected-set estimate consumes the reserve of one. Both allowances are
  evaluated at the same scale and the same reference band. The numerical
  payment chooses <math|\<kappa\>\<geqslant\>0> so that, with

  <\equation*>
    I=e<rsup|\<kappa\>>*\<gamma\>+2*H,<space|2em>b<rsub|B>=<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>,
  </equation*>

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:projected-fusion-payment>H\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>b<rsub|B>*I\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>e<rsup|-\<kappa\>>*<around|(|e<rsup|I>-1|)>\<leqslant\>\<phi\>.
  </equation>

  These have the form of the three inequalities in
  <eqref|eq:nonlinear-payment>, with the output bound replaced by
  <math|\<phi\>>; but the payment is made relative to <math|\<phi\>>, as
  follows. Here <math|\<phi\>=\<phi\>*<around|(|y,\<beta\>|)>> is the forced
  allowance of the chosen forecast at the coupling <math|\<beta\>> of the
  band. Two numerical inputs are used, for all sufficiently large <math|x>
  and uniformly over the band:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:projected-forecast-inputs>\<gamma\>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|8>\<phi\>,<space|2em>\<phi\>*<space|0.17em>y<rsup|1/16>\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation>

  The first compares the compulsory exponentially small mark retained in
  <math|\<gamma\>> with the forecast; the second says that the forecast is
  itself small. Neither is proved in this section. Both belong to the
  purchase of the forecast, whose hard rate is fixed below a fraction of the
  coefficient of the mark before the delays are chosen.

  Given <eqref|eq:projected-forecast-inputs> and <math|\<phi\>\<gtr\>0>, put
  <math|p=y<rsup|1/16>> and <math|\<kappa\>=-log
  <around|(|\<phi\>*p|)>\<geqslant\>0>. Then
  <math|e<rsup|\<kappa\>>*\<gamma\>\<leqslant\><around|(|8*p|)><rsup|-1>>, so
  <math|I\<leqslant\><around|(|8*p|)><rsup|-1>+2*H>. Since <math|H> has upper
  exponent <math|-3/8> and <math|b<rsub|B>> has upper exponent zero,
  eventually <math|H\<leqslant\>y<rsup|-1/8>\<leqslant\>1> and
  <math|b<rsub|B>*I\<leqslant\>1>; in particular <math|I\<leqslant\>1> and
  <math|e<rsup|I>-1\<leqslant\>2*I>. Finally

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|-\<kappa\>>*<around|(|e<rsup|I>-1|)>\<leqslant\>2*\<phi\>*p*I\<leqslant\>\<phi\>*<around*|(|<tfrac|1|4>+4*p*H|)>\<leqslant\>\<phi\>,
  </equation*>

  because <math|p*H\<leqslant\>y<rsup|-1/16>\<leqslant\>3/16> eventually. The
  threshold does not depend on the coupling in the band, while
  <math|\<kappa\>> may.

  Here is why those inequalities pay the analytic coefficients, not only the
  real values. Give an old forced entry with series majorant <math|k<rsub|l>>
  the inflated price <math|e<rsup|\<kappa\>>*k<rsub|l>>, and give a
  discrepancy entry with series price <math|h<rsub|l>> the price
  <math|2*h<rsub|l>>. A nonempty support and the rooted bound imply
  <math|h<rsub|l>\<leqslant\>H\<leqslant\>1>. The coefficient algebra
  therefore gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||e<rsup|-d<rsub|l>>-1|\|><rsub|R/4>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|h<rsub|l>>-1\<leqslant\>2*h<rsub|l>.
  </equation*>

  The last inequality holds on <math|<around|[|0,1|]>>, for example by
  convexity of the exponential between its endpoint values and
  <math|e-1\<less\>2>. Every final forced component contains at least one old
  forced entry. Its exact product of the old forced factors and the
  exponential-minus-one discrepancy factors consequently has series norm at
  most

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|-\<kappa\>>*<big|prod><rsub|l\<in\>D>F<rsub|l>,<space|2em>F<rsub|l>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|e<rsup|\<kappa\>>*k<rsub|l>,>|<cell|l<text|
    old forced>,>>|<row|<cell|2*h<rsub|l>,>|<cell|l<text| discrepancy>.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  Indeed each old forced factor contributes <math|e<rsup|-\<kappa\>>>;
  retaining just one of them only increases the bound since
  <math|\<kappa\>\<geqslant\>0>. The eligibility predicate for a component is
  independent of <math|t>, so it preserves the same majorant. The distinct
  connected-set estimate <eqref|eq:ope-forest-price> with reserve one, used
  as in <eqref|eq:fusion-site-output>, applies to the constituent prices
  <math|F<rsub|l>>: their rooted sum at exponent <math|b+2> is at most
  <math|I>, and <math|b<rsub|B>*I\<leqslant\>1>. It bounds the rooted sum of
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|l\<in\>D>F<rsub|l>> at exponent <math|b+1> by
  <math|e<rsup|I>-1>. The final rooted allowance is therefore
  <math|e<rsup|-\<kappa\>>*<around|(|e<rsup|I>-1|)>\<leqslant\>\<phi\>>. This
  proves a common coefficient majorant at radius <math|R/4> for all
  configurations and all entries of the actual final forced array.

  The exact additive decomposition of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:five-period-localization>
  identifies these ordinary and forced outputs with the selected one-step
  source. Their dictionary is fixed by the common path and incoming supports.
  The auxiliary test representations may be used outside
  <math|<around|[|-1,1|]>> to provide the real-node bounds, but on
  <math|<around|[|-1,1|]>> they agree with the given parameter family. Thus
  all the finite tests and series recoveries above concern that same family.
  The ordinary bound <eqref|eq:projected-broad-bound> and the forced common
  majorant are precisely the two broad analytic inputs retained in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:background-test-prices>. Construction of the
  funded path and its real-sector hypotheses remains separate.

  <subsection|Sharpening the finite-test prices><label|sec:background-test-prices>

  The real parameter tests of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:background-ray-estimates>
  need small prices on one fixed parameter interval. The local expansion
  first provides a bound on a growing interval and a better pointwise bound.
  A finite polynomial interpolation combines these two estimates. We give the
  interpolation and its numerical purchase, keeping the underlying
  wide-interval analytic estimate visible.

  <subsubsection|A finite interpolation estimate>

  Let <math|\<cal-E\>> be a vector space of families on a fixed support
  dictionary, with a nonnegative budget <math|\<cal-N\>> satisfying the
  triangle inequality and absolute homogeneity. A budget defined as an
  infimum over admissible summable majorants has these properties: add or
  scale the majorants and then take their infima. The argument below equally
  applies by retaining the majorants themselves. A finite test is a finitely
  supported real signed measure on <math|<around|[|-1,1|]>>,

  <\equation*>
    \<lambda\>=<big|sum><rsub|h=1><rsup|h<rsub|0>>a<rsub|h>*\<delta\><rsub|t<rsub|h>>,<space|2em>a<rsub|h>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>,<space|1em>t<rsub|h>\<in\><around|[|-1,1|]>,
  </equation*>

  and for a function <math|G> on <math|<around|[|-1,1|]>> with values in a
  real vector space we write <math|<big|int>G*<space|0.17em>d*\<lambda\>=<big|sum><rsub|h>a<rsub|h>*G<around|(|t<rsub|h>|)>>.
  Set

  <\equation*>
    M<rsub|r><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>=sup<rsub|n\<geqslant\>0><around*|\||<big|int><around|(|t/r|)><rsup|n>*<space|0.17em>d*\<lambda\><around|(|t|)>|\|>,<space|2em>r\<geqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|F:<around|[|-1,1|]>\<to\>\<cal-E\>>. Suppose

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-N\><around*|(|<big|int>F*<space|0.17em>d*\<lambda\>|)>\<leqslant\>B*M<rsub|v><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-N\><around|(|F<around|(|t|)>|)>\<leqslant\>b*<space|1em><around|(|-1\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>1|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|1\<leqslant\>q\<leqslant\>v> and <math|b,B\<geqslant\>0>, the
  first bound being assumed for every finite test <math|\<lambda\>>. The
  integrals here are finite linear combinations; no measurability of <math|F>
  is required. For each integer <math|k\<geqslant\>0>, a constant
  <math|U=U*<around|(|k,q|)>\<geqslant\>0>, independent of the family, its
  dictionary and its volume, gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:background-sharp-test>\<cal-N\><around*|(|<big|int>F*<space|0.17em>d*\<lambda\>|)>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|q><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>*<around*|{|U*b+<around|(|1+U|)>*<around|(|q/v|)><rsup|k+1>*B|}>.
  </equation>

  Choose the <math|k+1> distinct points <math|t<rsub|i>=i/<around|(|k+1|)>>,
  <math|0\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>k>, and their Lagrange polynomials
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>> of degree at most <math|k>. Write
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|i><around|(|t|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|k>c<rsub|i*j>*t<rsup|j>>
  and put

  <\equation*>
    U<rsub|i>=<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|k><around|\||c<rsub|i*j>|\|>*q<rsup|j>,<space|2em>U=<big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|k>U<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|\<eta\>=M<rsub|q><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>>, we have
  <math|<around|\||<big|int>\<ell\><rsub|i>*<space|0.17em>d*\<lambda\>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>*U<rsub|i>>.
  Define

  <\equation*>
    \<Pi\>*\<lambda\>=<big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|k><around*|(|<big|int>\<ell\><rsub|i>*<space|0.17em>d*\<lambda\>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|t<rsub|i>>.
  </equation*>

  Lagrange interpolation of each polynomial of degree at most <math|k> gives
  <math|<big|int>t<rsup|j>*<space|0.17em>d*<around|(|\<lambda\>-\<Pi\>*\<lambda\>|)>=0>
  for <math|j\<leqslant\>k>. For <math|j\<gtr\>k>, use
  <math|<around|\||t<rsub|i>/q|\|>\<leqslant\>1> to get

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||<big|int><around|(|t/q|)><rsup|j>*<space|0.17em>d*<around|(|\<lambda\>-\<Pi\>*\<lambda\>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>+<big|sum><rsub|i><around*|\||<big|int>\<ell\><rsub|i>*<space|0.17em>d*\<lambda\>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>*<around|(|1+U|)>.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|q/v\<leqslant\>1>, multiplication by
  <math|<around|(|q/v|)><rsup|j>> proves

  <\equation*>
    M<rsub|v>*<around|(|\<lambda\>-\<Pi\>*\<lambda\>|)>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>*<around|(|1+U|)>*<around|(|q/v|)><rsup|k+1>.
  </equation*>

  The pointwise price and the triangle inequality give
  <math|\<cal-N\><around|(|<big|int>F*<space|0.17em>d*\<Pi\>*\<lambda\>|)>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>*U*b>.
  Adding this to the wide-interval bound on
  <math|\<lambda\>-\<Pi\>*\<lambda\>> proves
  <eqref|eq:background-sharp-test>. Thus the same constant works for the full
  regular derivative budget, including all its site sums; no count of
  dictionary entries enters the estimate.

  <subsubsection|The fixed-radius price at large coupling>

  Fix the stage and block length, and put <math|y=x+a>, where
  <math|a\<gtr\>0> is the fixed reference coupling increment. The broad
  analytic estimate to be sharpened has radius and price

  <\equation*>
    v<around|(|x|)>=<tfrac|1|2>y<rsup|1/256>,<space|2em>B<around|(|x|)>=y<rsup|-1/4>.
  </equation*>

  The local pointwise price has the form

  <\equation*>
    b<around|(|x|)>=3*t*C<rsub|<math-up|st>>*x<rsup|-11/10>+R<around|(|x|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|t\<gtr\>0> is a small response parameter and
  <math|C<rsub|<math-up|st>>\<gtr\>0> is the fixed stage conversion constant.
  The remainder <math|R> has upper exponent at most <math|-23/20>:
  concretely, for every <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>,
  <math|<around|\||R<around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|\<epsilon\>>*y<rsup|-23/20+\<epsilon\>>>
  eventually. This estimate comprises the local comparison and truncation
  errors; it is separate from the broad analytic bound. For the selected
  one-step source it is the regular budget proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-nonlinear-offering>. There the
  principal and complementary regular prices are purchased as <math|E/16> and
  <math|2*E/16>, with <math|E=C<rsub|<math-up|st>>*x<rsup|-11/10>>; here they
  are purchased with the fraction <math|t\<leqslant\>1/16> in place of
  <math|1/16>; the smaller regular reserve is the one permitted in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history> and is chosen before
  the entry delays. The offering section states its budget only for the
  fraction <math|1/16>. Its proof uses the principal and complementary prices
  only through their sum in the truncation bound
  <eqref|eq:selected-offer-truncation>, so with the hypotheses <math|t*E> and
  <math|2*t*E> the same proof gives the leading term
  <math|3*t*C<rsub|<math-up|st>>*x<rsup|-11/10>> in place of <math|3*E/16>.
  The other terms of that budget are unchanged: in the notation of that
  section,

  <\equation*>
    R=C<rsub|<math-up|turn>>*g+g+C<rsub|<math-up|turn>>*c<rprime|'>*e<rsup|A<rsub|c>*M>,<space|2em>g=y<rsup|-23/20>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|c<rprime|'>> is the price of the folded counterterms. Both
  <math|g> and <math|c<rprime|'>> have upper exponent <math|-23/20>, and
  <math|C<rsub|<math-up|turn>>>, <math|A<rsub|c>>, <math|M> are fixed at the
  stage. The bound holds at every point of the parameter line, for the raw
  family in its one common dictionary.

  Fix <math|q\<geqslant\>2> and <math|s\<gtr\>0>, take <math|k=300> in
  <eqref|eq:background-sharp-test>, and choose <math|t> small enough that
  <math|6*U*t\<leqslant\>s>. The leading pointwise contribution is at most
  <math|<around|(|s/2|)>*C<rsub|<math-up|st>>*x<rsup|-11/10>>. The other part
  of the bound is

  <\equation*>
    U*R<around|(|x|)>+<around|(|1+U|)>*<around|(|2*q|)><rsup|301>*y<rsup|-301/256-1/4>.
  </equation*>

  Its upper exponent is at most <math|-23/20>, because
  <math|301/256+1/4=365/256\<gtr\>23/20>. Since <math|23/20\<gtr\>11/10> and
  <math|y/x\<to\>1>, that expression is eventually bounded above by
  <math|<around|(|s/2|)>*C<rsub|<math-up|st>>*x<rsup|-11/10>>. Hence the
  fixed-radius test price is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:background-pre-reset-price>s*C<rsub|<math-up|st>>*x<rsup|-11/10>.
  </equation>

  There is no requirement that <math|U> be small. It depends only on
  <math|300> and <math|q>; the response parameter is chosen after it, and the
  coupling threshold after all those choices. For example
  <math|t\<leqslant\>s/<around|[|6*<around|(|1+U|)>|]>> always suffices.
  Since <math|v<around|(|x|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>, the condition
  <math|q\<leqslant\>v<around|(|x|)>> also holds beyond that threshold.

  <subsubsection|The reset and the final regular price>

  The reset and local Taylor subtraction are linear in the raw logarithmic
  family and in its extracted coupling coefficient. On a fixed stage, the
  regular output price is at most

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:background-regular-price>C<rsub|0>*H+C<rsub|1><around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>*\<theta\><around|(|x|)><rsup|3>,
  </equation>

  where <math|C<rsub|0>,C<rsub|1>\<geqslant\>0> are independent of the
  coupling, period and input family, <math|H> is the raw test price, and
  <math|\<theta\><around|(|x|)>=x<rsup|-1/2>*exp <around|(|<around|(|log log
  x|)><rsup|2>|)>> is the fluctuation threshold. The two constants collect
  respectively the reset, low-path and high-path derivative prices, and the
  high-path remainder. This is the same local subtraction estimate used in
  the nonlinear transition, applied to a finite linear combination before
  taking its majorant. It is the regular cost <math|E<rsub|<math-up|new>>> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-reset-reorganization>, with the
  threshold of that section equal to <math|\<theta\><around|(|x|)>>: in the
  notation of <eqref|eq:reset-stage-constants>,

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|0>=B<rsup|<math-up|reset>>=C<rsub|<math-up|reset>>+C<rsub|<math-up|turn>>*<around|(|L<rsub|0>+L<rsub|1>|)>,<space|2em>C<rsub|1>=v<rsup|<math-up|reset>>=C<rsub|<math-up|turn>>*L<rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  The estimate is used under the hypotheses of that section, for the path,
  clipping data and outgoing budget of the transition under consideration.
  The path satisfies its paid bounds at the path width <math|\<sigma\>>. The
  relaxation radius <math|r<rsub|c>> of the clipping data and the support
  exponent <math|b> and radius <math|B> of the budget satisfy
  <math|12\<leqslant\>r<rsub|c>*\<sigma\>> and
  <math|<around|(|b+1|)>/B\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>/4>. The direct clipping
  coefficient is at most one, <math|B\<geqslant\>2>, and
  <math|2\<leqslant\>\<theta\><around|(|x|)>*e<rsup|<around|(|B-2|)>/r<rsub|c>>>,
  as in <eqref|eq:reset-clip-conditions>. The group is simple. The path
  correction <math|\<alpha\>> is one fixed number along the whole parameter
  line, with <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>A>. With these data
  the constants <math|C<rsub|0>,C<rsub|1>> depend only on the stage.

  The stage conversion constant is selected as

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|<math-up|st>>=<frac|1|64*<around|(|1+C<rsub|0>|)>>.
  </equation*>

  This is the allocation <math|c<rsup|<math-up|reset>>> of
  <eqref|eq:reset-stage-constants>. It is positive and satisfies both
  <math|C<rsub|<math-up|st>>\<leqslant\>1/64> and
  <math|C<rsub|0>*C<rsub|<math-up|st>>\<leqslant\>1/64>. Fix
  <math|d\<gtr\>0>, take <math|0\<less\>s\<leqslant\>d> in
  <eqref|eq:background-pre-reset-price>, and retain the fixed bound
  <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>A>. The first term of
  <eqref|eq:background-regular-price> is at most
  <math|<around|(|d/64|)>*x<rsup|-11/10>>. The second is
  <math|o<around|(|x<rsup|-11/10>|)>>, since

  <\equation*>
    <frac|\<theta\><around|(|x|)><rsup|3>|x<rsup|-11/10>>=x<rsup|-2/5>*exp
    <around|(|3<around|(|log log x|)><rsup|2>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  Increasing the coupling threshold therefore gives regular output price at
  most <math|d*x<rsup|-11/10>>.

  This remains a finite-test statement. If the moment bound of the finite
  test is <math|\<eta\>>, the raw price is <math|\<eta\>*H>, the constant
  path correction becomes the same finite combination of <math|\<alpha\>>,
  and its modulus is at most <math|\<eta\>*A> by the zeroth moment test. The
  linear reset and the triangle inequality thus multiply
  <eqref|eq:background-regular-price> by <math|\<eta\>>. Dividing by the
  positive regular normalization gives the regular part of
  <eqref|eq:background-ray-tests>. The scalar coupling coefficient is a
  bounded linear projection of the raw family, with price
  <math|H/\<theta\><around|(|x|)><rsup|2>>. Consequently its finite-test
  price is at most the scalar allowance <math|f<rsub|m>=s*C<rsub|<math-up|st>>*x<rsup|-11/10>/\<theta\><around|(|x|)><rsup|2>>
  used there.

  <subsubsection|The forced price and the remaining analytic input>

  A finite-test bound alone may choose its majorant after the test, so
  recovery of a power series for each value separately does not yet produce
  one common support majorant. We supply that step. Let <math|S<rsub|i>> be
  the countable list of nonempty supports and let <math|e\<geqslant\>0> be
  the cover exponent. Suppose that for every finite test <math|\<lambda\>>
  there are numbers <math|k<rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|i|)>\<geqslant\>0> such
  that

  <\gather*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\||<big|int>F*<around|(|t,i,U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<lambda\><around|(|t|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>k<rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|i|)><space|1em><text|for
    every configuration >U,>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i:z\<in\>S<rsub|i>>k<rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|i|)>*e<rsup|e*C<around|(|S<rsub|i>|)>>\<leqslant\>B*M<rsub|q><around|(|\<lambda\>|)><space|1em><text|at
    every root >z.>>>>
  </gather*>

  Here <math|C<around|(|S<rsub|i>|)>> is the cover number. Nonemptiness and
  <math|e\<geqslant\>0> imply <math|k<rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|i|)>\<leqslant\>B*M<rsub|q><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>>
  for each <math|i>. For each entry and configuration, the closed-span
  argument of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:background-ray-estimates>
  therefore extends finite-test evaluation to a bounded functional
  <math|T<rsub|i,U>> on the closed span of the moment vectors
  <math|v<around|(|t|)>=<around|(|<around|(|t/q|)><rsup|n>|)><rsub|n>>.

  Fix <math|n>. Choose finite tests <math|\<lambda\><rsub|j>> whose moment
  vectors <math|v<around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|j>|)>> converge in sup norm to
  <math|e<rsub|n>>; these approximants exist by the coordinate-vector
  construction above. In particular <math|M<rsub|q><around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|j>|)>\<to\>1>.
  Choose their majorants <math|k<rsub|j>> as in the hypothesis. Their
  coordinates lie in one common bounded interval. A diagonal subsequence on
  the countable indices <math|i> gives <math|k<rsub|j><around|(|i|)>\<to\>b<rsub|n,i>\<geqslant\>0>.
  For each finite set of indices and root <math|z>, pass to the limit in its
  weighted sum. Taking the supremum over finite sets yields

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|i:z\<in\>S<rsub|i>>b<rsub|n,i>*e<rsup|e*C<around|(|S<rsub|i>|)>>\<leqslant\>B.
  </equation*>

  Also <math|<around|\||T<rsub|i,U>*e<rsub|n>|\|>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|n,i>>, by
  continuity of <math|T<rsub|i,U>>. This last conclusion holds for every
  <math|U> on the same subsequence: the chosen majorants bounded all
  configurations from the start, and convergence of the moment vectors is
  independent of <math|U>. No subsequence indexed by configurations is
  selected.

  For <math|1\<leqslant\>r\<less\>q>, put

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|i>=<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>0><around|(|r/q|)><rsup|n>*b<rsub|n,i>.
  </equation*>

  The bounds <math|b<rsub|n,i>\<leqslant\>B> make each series finite.
  Tonelli's theorem for the nonnegative weighted series gives

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|i:z\<in\>S<rsub|i>>K<rsub|i>*e<rsup|e*C<around|(|S<rsub|i>|)>>\<leqslant\><frac|B|1-r/q>.
  </equation*>

  The coefficients <math|<around|(|r/q|)><rsup|n>*T<rsub|i,U>*e<rsub|n>> have
  total absolute sum at most <math|K<rsub|i>> and reconstruct
  <math|F*<around|(|t,i,U|)>> as a series in <math|t/r>. Thus one common
  support majorant works for every configuration. Applied to the soft
  correction from the linear reset with <math|r=q/2>, this proves the
  required factor-two price and radius loss.

  The compatible finite-subset products preserve absolute coefficient
  summability: their coefficients obey convolution, and the sum of
  coefficient norms of a product is bounded by the product of the
  corresponding sums. The proof is Tonelli's theorem for the nonnegative
  coefficient majorants. Thus the forced absorption argument of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:soft-log-fusion> applies also to these
  parameter coefficient prices, with the same enlarged support reserve and
  the same scalar parameter <math|\<kappa\>>. Its inputs are the following.
  The hard price <math|a<rsub|<math-up|h>>> is the common coefficient
  majorant of the raw forced family at radius <math|q/2> and support exponent
  <math|b+1>, where <math|b> is the outgoing support exponent. The soft price
  is any <math|c<rsub|<math-up|s>>\<geqslant\>2*S>, where <math|S> is the
  soft cost <math|S<rsub|<math-up|new>>> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-reset-reorganization> at the raw
  price <math|H> and the fixed correction <math|\<alpha\>>; the factor two is
  the radius loss just proved. If <math|K<rsub|m>\<gtr\>0> denotes the
  prescribed forced normalization and <math|\<kappa\>\<geqslant\>0> satisfies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:background-forced-payment>c<rsub|<math-up|s>>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em><around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*I\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>e<rsup|-\<kappa\>>*<around|(|e<rsup|I>-1|)>\<leqslant\>d*K<rsub|m>,<space|2em>I=e<rsup|\<kappa\>>*a<rsub|<math-up|h>>+2*c<rsub|<math-up|s>>,
  </equation>

  then the final forced array has one common coefficient majorant at radius
  <math|q/2> and exponent <math|b> with rooted price at most
  <math|d*K<rsub|m>>. These inequalities are paid on the selected schedule as
  follows. Write <math|y=x+c> for the fixed stage increment and
  <math|P<around|(|x|)>=exp <around|(|<around|(|log log x|)><rsup|2>|)>>.
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history> gives
  <math|K<rsub|m>=e<rsup|-P<around|(|x|)><rsup|1/5>>> and a hard forecast
  bound <math|a<rsub|<math-up|h>>\<leqslant\>C*e<rsup|-g*P<around|(|y|)><rsup|2>>>,
  with fixed <math|C\<less\>\<infty\>> and <math|g\<gtr\>0>. This also
  includes the fixed radius and support reserves. For any fixed
  <math|d\<gtr\>0>, put <math|\<phi\>=d*K<rsub|m>> and <math|p=y<rsup|1/16>>.
  Since <math|P<around|(|y|)>/P<around|(|x|)>\<to\>1> and
  <math|P<around|(|x|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>,

  <\equation*>
    <frac|a<rsub|<math-up|h>>|\<phi\>>\<leqslant\><frac|C|d>*exp
    <around|{|-g*P<around|(|y|)><rsup|2>+P<around|(|x|)><rsup|1/5>|}>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  The soft reset cost is a stage constant times a polynomial in <math|log x>
  times <math|x<rsup|-1/2>*P<around|(|x|)>>, plus terms with smaller powers
  of <math|x>. The factor-two radius loss above is fixed. Thus eventually
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|s>>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|-1/8>>. Also
  <math|\<phi\>*p\<leqslant\>1> eventually, because
  <math|P<around|(|x|)><rsup|1/5>/log x\<to\>\<infty\>>. Choose the
  mesh-independent stage threshold so that all these bounds hold and
  <math|a<rsub|<math-up|h>>/\<phi\>\<leqslant\>1/8>. Define

  <\equation*>
    \<kappa\>=-log <around|(|\<phi\>*p|)>\<geqslant\>0.
  </equation*>

  Then <math|I\<leqslant\>1/<around|(|8*p|)>+2*c<rsub|<math-up|s>>\<to\>0>.
  The block radius <math|B> is polynomial in <math|log x>, so
  <math|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*I\<leqslant\>1> eventually. For
  <math|0\<leqslant\>I\<leqslant\>1>, <math|e<rsup|I>-1\<leqslant\>2*I>,
  whence

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|-\<kappa\>>*<around|(|e<rsup|I>-1|)>\<leqslant\>\<phi\>*p*<around|{|1/<around|(|4*p|)>+4*c<rsub|<math-up|s>>|}>=\<phi\>*<around|(|1/4+4*p*c<rsub|<math-up|s>>|)>\<leqslant\>\<phi\>=d*K<rsub|m>
  </equation*>

  after one further increase of the threshold. This proves
  <eqref|eq:background-forced-payment>. The constants and bounds are uniform
  throughout the same incoming coupling band; only its entry delay has been
  increased, not its history or parameter line. The test inequality follows
  by applying each finite moment test to the absolutely summable coefficient
  series. This supplies the forced clause of <eqref|eq:background-ray-tests>
  whenever the broad ordinary and forced analytic estimates have been
  supplied.

  The broad estimates used in this section are substantive: the ordinary raw
  logarithmic family must have radius <math|y<rsup|1/256>/2> and price
  <math|y<rsup|-1/4>> in the full derivative budget, and the raw forced
  family must have its common coefficient majorant at the paid broad radius.
  The pointwise remainder <math|R> must have the stated upper exponent. The
  interpolation and reset estimates above do not themselves prove those
  statements. They explain exactly how those local analytic estimates, once
  obtained for the same transition and parameter line, give the small prices
  needed by the coupled history argument.

  <subsection|Real parameter tests and the background
  contraction><label|sec:background-ray-estimates>

  The small Lipschitz constant used in the background history does not follow
  from a small bound on the values alone. It follows from the paid bounds on
  finite linear combinations along each real parameter line. We prove the
  conversion explicitly. Only real configurations are used in these tests.

  <subsubsection|Recovering a convergent series from finite tests>

  Let <math|E> be a real Banach space, <math|p\<gtr\>1>,
  <math|C\<geqslant\>0>, and <math|f:<around|[|-1,1|]>\<to\>E>. Suppose that,
  for every finite list <math|t<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,t<rsub|k>\<in\><around|[|-1,1|]>>
  and real weights <math|a<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,a<rsub|k>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ray-dual-test><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|k>a<rsub|j>*f<around|(|t<rsub|j>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*sup<rsub|n\<geqslant\>0><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|k>a<rsub|j>*<around|(|t<rsub|j>/p|)><rsup|n>|\|>.
  </equation>

  No continuity of <math|f> is assumed here. There are vectors
  <math|b<rsub|n>\<in\>E> with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsub|n>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C>
  such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ray-recovered-series>f<around|(|t|)>=<big|sum><rsub|n=0><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|t/p|)><rsup|n>*b<rsub|n>*<space|2em><around|(|-1\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>1|)>.
  </equation>

  To prove this, use the sup norm on sequences and let
  <math|v<around|(|t|)>=<around|(|<around|(|t/p|)><rsup|n>|)><rsub|n\<geqslant\>0>>.
  Its coordinates tend to zero. Define, on the span of these sequences,

  <\equation*>
    T<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|j>a<rsub|j>*v<around|(|t<rsub|j>|)>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j>a<rsub|j>*f<around|(|t<rsub|j>|)>.
  </equation*>

  If the sequence on the left is zero, <eqref|eq:ray-dual-test> makes the
  proposed value zero. Thus <math|T> is well defined and linear, and its norm
  is at most <math|C>. It extends uniquely to the closed span, because
  <math|E> is complete.

  Every coordinate vector <math|e<rsub|n>> lies in that closed span. For
  <math|n=0>, use <math|v<around|(|0|)>=e<rsub|0>>. Inductively assume this
  for <math|e<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,e<rsub|n-1>>. For
  <math|0\<less\>t\<leqslant\>1>, put <math|r=t/p>. The vector

  <\equation*>
    r<rsup|-n>*<around*|(|v<around|(|t|)>-<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>n>r<rsup|j>*e<rsub|j>|)>
  </equation*>

  has its first <math|n> coordinates zero, its <math|n>th coordinate one, and
  subsequent coordinates <math|r,r<rsup|2>,\<ldots\>>. Its sup distance from
  <math|e<rsub|n>> is <math|r\<to\>0> as <math|t*\<downarrow\>*0>. This
  proves the claim. Set <math|b<rsub|n>=T*e<rsub|n>>, so
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsub|n>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C>. The geometric
  series <math|v<around|(|t|)>=<big|sum><rsub|n><around|(|t/p|)><rsup|n>*e<rsub|n>>
  converges in the sup norm. Apply <math|T> to it to obtain
  <eqref|eq:ray-recovered-series>.

  For <math|1\<leqslant\>a\<less\>p>, the same identity can be written
  <math|f<around|(|t|)>=<big|sum><rsub|n><around|(|t/a|)><rsup|n>*c<rsub|n>>
  with <math|c<rsub|n>=<around|(|a/p|)><rsup|n>*b<rsub|n>> and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ray-radius-loss><big|sum><rsub|n><around|\<\|\|\>|c<rsub|n>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><frac|C|1-a/p>.
  </equation>

  This is the radius loss used when passing from finite tests to absolutely
  summable analytic coefficients. In particular, taking <math|a=p/2> costs at
  most a factor two when <math|p\<geqslant\>2>. The argument also applies to
  maps into the completion of a seminormed space; no completeness of its
  original representatives is needed.

  Two consequences will be used. First, a test with one point and weight one
  gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|f<around|(|t|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C>.
  Second, subtracting the series at zero gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ray-increment><around|\<\|\|\>|f<around|(|t|)>-f<around|(|0|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*<frac|<around|\||t|\|>|p-<around|\||t|\|>>.
  </equation>

  Both conclusions hold even if the initial pointwise construction of
  <math|f> involved arbitrary choices: the finite tests themselves force the
  displayed series and hence continuity on <math|<around|[|-1,1|]>>.

  <subsubsection|Applying the tests to the actual activity coordinates>

  At one adjacent pair of scales, retain the actual common dictionary of
  regular and forced arrays. Its norm is the maximum of the normalized
  regular and forced budgets, with all the fixed symmetry and support
  constraints retained. Finite linear combinations remain in this vector
  space. Fix the transition from the fine scale <math|m+1> to the coarse
  scale <math|m> at one terminal index, the reference coupling
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>> of the fine scale, and
  <math|w=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsup|-1/32>>. A line of inputs has normalized
  coupling <math|\<lambda\>+t*\<mu\>> and activity array <math|x+t*y>,
  <math|<around|\||t|\|>\<leqslant\>1>. We call it admissible if

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:background-ray-admissible><frac|33|40>\<leqslant\>w*\<lambda\>\<leqslant\><frac|47|40>,<space|2em><around|\||\<mu\>|\|>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|x+t*y|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1*<space|1em><around|(|-1\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>1|)>.
  </equation>

  The condition on the direction <math|\<mu\>> is separate from the first
  one: for <math|w> below the purchased threshold, the two together keep the
  coupling of every point of the line inside the paid band of the fine scale,
  where the transition is defined. For an admissible line let
  <math|F<around|(|t|)>> be its selected output array in the completion and
  <math|Q<around|(|t|)>> its scalar coupling correction.

  The exact finite-test receipts used here are the following. They are
  required for every admissible line, with constants <math|d\<geqslant\>0>
  and <math|f<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>0> fixed before the line: <math|d> is the
  normalized activity allowance and <math|f<rsub|m>> the scalar allowance of
  the step from <math|m+1> to <math|m>. For any finite weights
  <math|a<rsub|j>> and points <math|t<rsub|j>\<in\><around|[|-1,1|]>>, write

  <\equation*>
    \<eta\><rsub|r>=sup<rsub|n\<geqslant\>0><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|j>a<rsub|j>*<around|(|t<rsub|j>/r|)><rsup|n>|\|>.
  </equation*>

  The combined regular output array has normalized budget at most
  <math|d*\<eta\><rsub|q/2>>, as does the combined forced output array. The
  scalar correction satisfies

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|j>a<rsub|j>*Q<around|(|t<rsub|j>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|m>*\<eta\><rsub|q>.
  </equation*>

  The forced statement follows from its single common summable coefficient
  majorant; the regular statement is a bound on the actual mixed arrays, with
  their derivative prices, not just on values of their gas. The definition of
  the array norm and the isometric embedding into its completion consequently
  give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:background-ray-tests><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|j>a<rsub|j>*F<around|(|t<rsub|j>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>d*\<eta\><rsub|q/2>,<space|2em><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|j>a<rsub|j>*Q<around|(|t<rsub|j>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|m>*\<eta\><rsub|q>.
  </equation>

  These are precisely the two instances of <eqref|eq:ray-dual-test> needed
  below. Padding the dictionaries with zero entries, or using another
  representative of the same array, preserves them.

  We spell out why the admissible lines are the ones used in the finite-test
  construction. Their activity values are affine in <math|t> on each fixed
  support. On a healthy configuration the regular mask is one and the value
  is the corresponding affine combination; on an unhealthy configuration the
  common mask is zero and the same equality holds. Forced values are affine
  without a derivative condition. The inverse coupling along the line is
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^>*<around|{|w*<around|(|\<lambda\>+t*\<mu\>|)>|}><rsup|2>>,
  with the same reference <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^>> and
  <math|w=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsup|-1/32>>. Thus it is exactly the prescribed
  quadratic coupling line, not an independent choice of complexified gauge
  fields. The protected supports, normalization, path data and transition are
  the same for all <math|t>. The analytic estimates that pay
  <eqref|eq:background-ray-tests> are retained as quantitative inputs here;
  the deduction of the contraction from those estimates is complete below.

  <subsubsection|The full-band Lipschitz estimates>

  Take <math|q\<geqslant\>4>, <math|d,f<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>0>,
  <math|2*d\<leqslant\>\<gamma\>=1/128>, and <math|s<rsub|m>=2*f<rsub|m>>.
  Assume <math|w\<leqslant\>1/128>, as purchased by the coupling threshold.
  Consider two inputs <math|z=<around|(|\<lambda\>,x|)>> and
  <math|z<rprime|'>=<around|(|\<lambda\><rprime|'>,x<rprime|'>|)>> with
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>>,<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rprime|'>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1/8>
  and <math|17/20\<leqslant\>w*\<lambda\>,w*\<lambda\><rprime|'>\<leqslant\>23/20>.
  Use the product norm

  <\equation*>
    h=<around|\<\|\|\>|z<rprime|'>-z|\<\|\|\>>=max
    <around|{|<around|\||\<lambda\><rprime|'>-\<lambda\>|\|>,<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rprime|'>-x|\<\|\|\>>|}>.
  </equation*>

  The constant-line tests give <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|F<around|(|z|)>|\<\|\|\>>,<around|\<\|\|\>|F<around|(|z<rprime|'>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>d>
  and <math|<around|\||Q<around|(|z|)>|\|>,<around|\||Q<around|(|z<rprime|'>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|m>>.
  If <math|h\<gtr\>1/4>, the triangle inequality therefore gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|F<around|(|z<rprime|'>|)>-F<around|(|z|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*d\<leqslant\>8*\<gamma\>*h,<space|2em><around|\||Q<around|(|z<rprime|'>|)>-Q<around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*f<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>8*f<rsub|m>*h=4*s<rsub|m>*h.
  </equation*>

  For <math|h=0>, no equality of raw representatives is needed. For any
  <math|0\<less\>\<varepsilon\>\<leqslant\>1>, take the line
  <math|z+t*<around|(|z<rprime|'>-z|)>/\<varepsilon\>>,
  <math|<around|\||t|\|>\<leqslant\>1>. Its coupling direction is zero and
  its activity direction has seminorm zero. Every point therefore has the
  same activity norm as <math|z>, at most <math|1/8>, so the line satisfies
  <eqref|eq:background-ray-admissible>. Its value at <math|t=\<varepsilon\>>
  is <math|z<rprime|'>>. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:ray-increment> bounds the
  output differences by <math|d*\<varepsilon\>/<around|(|q/2-\<varepsilon\>|)>>
  and <math|f<rsub|m>*\<varepsilon\>/<around|(|q-\<varepsilon\>|)>>,
  respectively. Letting <math|\<varepsilon\>*\<downarrow\>*0> proves equality
  of the output classes and of the scalar correction. This also proves that
  the map respects the seminorm kernel; it does not identify regular
  representatives on unhealthy configurations.

  For <math|0\<less\>h\<leqslant\>1/4>, set
  <math|v=<around|(|z<rprime|'>-z|)>/<around|(|4*h|)>>. Its norm is
  <math|1/4>, and the full line <math|z+t*v>,
  <math|<around|\||t|\|>\<leqslant\>1>, is admissible in the sense of
  <eqref|eq:background-ray-admissible>: its base point lies in the narrower
  coupling interval, its coupling direction satisfies
  <math|<around|\||v<rsub|1>|\|>\<leqslant\>1/4>, and its activity norm is at
  most <math|1/8+1/4\<less\>1>. In fact its normalized coupling lies in the
  broader interval

  <\equation*>
    33/40\<leqslant\>w*<around|(|\<lambda\>+t*v<rsub|1>|)>\<leqslant\>47/40,
  </equation*>

  since <math|w*<around|\||t*v<rsub|1>|\|>\<leqslant\>1/512\<less\>1/40>, the
  margin between the narrower and broader intervals. The prescribed
  coupling-line construction therefore supplies
  <eqref|eq:background-ray-tests> on this line. Apply
  <eqref|eq:ray-increment> at <math|t=4*h\<leqslant\>1> to get

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|F<around|(|z<rprime|'>|)>-F<around|(|z|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><frac|4*d|q/2-1>*h\<leqslant\>4*d*h\<leqslant\>8*\<gamma\>*h,<space|2em><around|\||Q<around|(|z<rprime|'>|)>-Q<around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|4*f<rsub|m>|q-1>*h\<leqslant\>8*f<rsub|m>*h.
  </equation*>

  This proves exactly <eqref|eq:coupled-activity-step> and
  <eqref|eq:coupled-cost-step>. The output bounds also follow from the
  constant-line tests, since <math|d\<leqslant\>1/256\<less\>1/8> and
  <math|f<rsub|m>=s<rsub|m>/2>.

  The use of one-dimensional series here is sufficient for these Lipschitz
  estimates. The same real finite-test method also produces multilinear
  Taylor coefficients by polarization when a full analytic parameterization
  is required. That additional conclusion is not needed in the convergence
  argument of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:coupled-background-convergence>.

  <subsection|Convergence of the coupling and activity
  histories><label|sec:coupled-background-convergence>

  We next prove convergence of the background on which the marked comparison
  depends. The coupling and the activities must be treated together. The
  activity recursion contracts towards coarser scales, whereas the coupling
  is fixed at the bottom scale. A finite telescoping identity joins these two
  directions. In particular, the proof does not assume that the activity
  space has compact bounded balls.

  <subsubsection|The normalized variables and their paid estimates>

  Fix a period. Let <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>\<gtr\>0> be the reference
  coupling at scale <math|m>, and set

  <\equation*>
    w<rsub|m>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m><rsup|-1/32>,<space|2em>B<rsub|m>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>*w<rsub|m>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m><rsup|31/32>,<space|2em>b<rsub|m><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>*<around|(|w<rsub|m>*\<lambda\>|)><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  A coupling <math|\<beta\>> is represented by
  <math|\<lambda\>=w<rsub|m><rsup|-1>*<sqrt|\<beta\>/<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>>>.
  On the paid band its normalized variable satisfies

  <\equation*>
    17/20\<leqslant\>w<rsub|m>*\<lambda\>\<leqslant\>23/20.
  </equation*>

  Thus, for two variables in that band,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:coupled-inverse-bound>B<rsub|m>*<around|\||\<lambda\>-\<lambda\><rprime|'>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||b<rsub|m><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>-b<rsub|m><around|(|\<lambda\><rprime|'>|)>|\|>.
  </equation>

  Indeed the difference on the right is <math|B<rsub|m>*<around|\||\<lambda\>-\<lambda\><rprime|'>|\|>*<space|0.17em><around|\||w<rsub|m>*\<lambda\>+w<rsub|m>*\<lambda\><rprime|'>|\|>>,
  whose last factor is at least <math|17/10\<gtr\>1>.

  Let <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|m>> be the completion of the seminormed space of
  actual regular and forced activity arrays with the prescribed weighted
  derivative and value prices. The embedding preserves distances. In
  particular, equality of evaluated gases alone is not the equivalence
  relation used here. Let <math|x<rsub|m>> denote the embedded array of the
  finite history with terminal index <math|N>, for <math|m\<leqslant\>N>; it
  also depends on <math|N>, and we write <math|x<rsub|m,N>> when this
  matters. We use <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1/8>
  for every <math|m\<leqslant\>N>. At the terminal scale <math|m=N> this is a
  hypothesis: there the array is that of the Wilson startup representation at
  the terminal coupling. The startup family of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:wilson-startup-family> has regular and
  forced costs at most the allowances <math|\<delta\><rsub|E>> and
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|K>>, which in the normalized norm used here gives only
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|N>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1>. The hypothesis is
  the strengthened purchase in which these two costs are at most
  <math|4*a*\<delta\><rsub|E>> and <math|a*\<delta\><rsub|K>> for some
  <math|a\<gtr\>0> with <math|8*a\<leqslant\>1/128>; then
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|N>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>4*a\<less\>1/8>. This
  strengthened startup purchase follows from the stronger reserve at the end
  of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:wilson-startup-construction>. Indeed,
  with its notation,

  <\equation*>
    <frac|E<rsub|<math-up|start>>|\<delta\><rsub|E>>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around|{|C-2*s/5+5<around|(|log s|)><rsup|2>|}>\<longrightarrow\>0,<space|2em><frac|K<rsub|<math-up|start>>|\<delta\><rsub|K>>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around|{|-s<rsub|\<ast\>>*M<rsup|2>/128+M<rsup|1/5>|}>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  For any fixed <math|a\<gtr\>0> with <math|8*a\<leqslant\>1/128>, increase
  the same stage entry threshold until these ratios are at most <math|4*a>
  and <math|a>. The startup estimates hold throughout the coupling band and
  at every period, so this is one threshold on the same family. For
  <math|m\<less\>N> it then follows by descending induction from the first
  bound in <eqref|eq:coupled-output-bounds> below, whose input condition
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|m+1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1/8> is the
  previous case. For a terminal index <math|N>, write

  <\equation*>
    x<rsub|m>=F<rsub|m,N>*<around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|m+1>,x<rsub|m+1>|)>,<space|2em>q<rsub|m>=Q<rsub|m,N>*<around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|m+1>,x<rsub|m+1>|)>*<space|1em><around|(|m\<less\>N|)>
  </equation*>

  for its activity transformation and the scalar correction to the coupling.
  These maps are initially defined on the actual allowed arrays; the notation
  does not assume an extension to every element of the completion. Their
  output and comparison estimates are

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|m,N>*<around|(|\<lambda\>,x|)>|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>1/8,>|<cell|<around|\||Q<rsub|m,N>*<around|(|\<lambda\>,x|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>s<rsub|m>/2,<eq-number><label|eq:coupled-output-bounds>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|m,N>*<around|(|\<lambda\>,x|)>-F<rsub|m,N>*<around|(|\<lambda\><rprime|'>,x<rprime|'>|)>|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>c*max
    <around|{|<around|\||\<lambda\>-\<lambda\><rprime|'>|\|>,<around|\<\|\|\>|x-x<rprime|'>|\<\|\|\>>|}>,>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number><label|eq:coupled-activity-step>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||Q<rsub|m,N>*<around|(|\<lambda\>,x|)>-Q<rsub|m,N>*<around|(|\<lambda\><rprime|'>,x<rprime|'>|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>4*s<rsub|m>*max
    <around|{|<around|\||\<lambda\>-\<lambda\><rprime|'>|\|>,<around|\<\|\|\>|x-x<rprime|'>|\<\|\|\>>|}>,>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number><label|eq:coupled-cost-step>>>>>
  </align>

  where <math|c=8/128=1/16> and <math|s<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>0>. The numerical
  schedule also pays

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:coupled-cumulative-payment><big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>m>s<rsub|j>\<leqslant\><frac|1|128>*B<rsub|m>.
  </equation>

  The coupling recursion is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:coupled-recursion>b<rsub|m><around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|m>|)>-b<rsub|m+1><around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|m+1>|)>-\<alpha\><rsub|m,N>=q<rsub|m>,
  </equation>

  where <math|\<alpha\><rsub|m,N>> is the path correction, already convergent
  at each fixed <math|m> by Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:background-path-convergence>.

  We use the following precise continuity statement for the local
  transformation at a fixed scale <math|m> and period. Take two sequences of
  terminal indices tending to infinity and, for each index, an allowed input
  of the transition from <math|m+1> to <math|m> at that terminal index. If
  the input normalized couplings of the two sequences have a common limit,
  and their actual input arrays are Cauchy with difference tending to zero in
  <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|m+1>>, then the differences of their output arrays, in
  <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|m>>, and of their scalar corrections tend to zero.
  This statement concerns arbitrary allowed inputs, including the truncated
  histories constructed below. The output arrays are those of the actual
  transition outputs, in the fixed dictionary of scale <math|m>.

  This continuity statement is an input of the present section, not a
  consequence of it. Its premises on the background are the path limits of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:background-path-convergence>, the
  free-horizon limits of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:free-horizon-convergence>
  and the fixed dictionaries, all for the same history as the maps
  <math|F<rsub|m,N>> and <math|Q<rsub|m,N>>. Its analytic content is the
  common-variable comparison of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-kernel-convergence>
  without a mark, together with the weighted head-and-tail argument of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-source-stability>; the compensated
  estimates which that comparison needs on the actual inputs are those of
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:local-parameter-comparison>,
  <reference|sec:finite-volume-parameter-continuity> and
  <reference|sec:parameter-comparison-suppliers>. The application to the
  selected history is made in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-history-limits>.
  Two points of that application are specific to the present setting and are
  explained now. Here is the precise tail implication. For a regular entry
  put <math|b<rsub|f><around|(|i|)>=sup<rsub|U><around|\||f<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reg>><around|(|U|)>|\|>>,
  with its actual mask, and for a forced entry put
  <math|b<rsub|f><around|(|i|)>=sup<rsub|U><around|\||f<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|forced>><around|(|U|)>|\|>>.
  Summing the site's paid budgets gives, separately for these two families,

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|i>b<rsub|f><around|(|i|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|m><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>>,<space|2em>b<rsub|f><around|(|i|)>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|f-g><around|(|i|)>+b<rsub|g><around|(|i|)>.
  </equation*>

  The constants are the finite number of sites times the corresponding
  positive regular or forced normalization. Fix one sufficiently late array
  <math|f<rsub|N<rsub|0>>> and choose a finite set outside which its
  <math|b>-sum is small. For every later array, its tail sum is at most that
  fixed tail plus <math|C<rsub|m>*<around|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|N>-f<rsub|N<rsub|0>>|\<\|\|\>>>.
  Enlarge the finite set for the finitely many earlier arrays. This gives the
  uniform value-price tails needed by the compensated comparison. It does not
  assert that arbitrary activity arrays can be truncated in their full
  derivative norm.

  For the scalar output, use convergence of the finite projected coupling
  coefficient itself: its projection is bounded by the local derivative
  price, whose convergence is supplied by the compensated comparison. This is
  in addition to convergence of the output activity array. Subtracting the
  convergent incoming coupling and path correction gives continuity of
  <math|Q> by <eqref|eq:coupled-recursion>. The bounds
  <eqref|eq:coupled-output-bounds>\U<eqref|eq:coupled-cost-step> are the
  quantitative one-step hypotheses used in this section; their purchase is
  kept separate from the convergence proof.

  <subsubsection|First fix the normalized coupling history>

  Suppose two sequences of allowed normalized coupling histories
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|m,N>> and <math|\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|m,N<rprime|'>>>
  have the same limit for every fixed <math|m>. For an integer
  <math|h\<geqslant\>m>, construct a truncated activity history by putting
  <math|x<rsub|h><rsup|<around|[|h|]>>=0> and applying the same
  finite-history maps backwards down to <math|m>. This construction is
  allowed for every prescribed coupling history in the band:
  <eqref|eq:coupled-output-bounds> keeps all its arrays in the ball of radius
  <math|1/8>. It does not impose the coupling recursion on this auxiliary
  history.

  Compare it with the original activity history for the same terminal index
  and the same prescribed couplings. At scale <math|h> the difference is at
  most <math|1/4\<leqslant\>1>. By <eqref|eq:coupled-activity-step>,
  backwards induction gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:coupled-truncation><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|m>-x<rsub|m><rsup|<around|[|h|]>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>c<rsup|h-m>.
  </equation>

  For fixed <math|h>, the two truncated histories have common limits at every
  scale at most <math|h>. At <math|h> they are both zero. If this is known at
  <math|i+1>, their arrays are Cauchy and their difference tends to zero
  there; the local continuity statement and the common normalized coupling
  limit give output differences tending to zero at <math|i>. To get
  convergence, apply this comparison to arbitrary pairs of subsequences of
  either truncated history. If that history were not Cauchy, one could select
  indices <math|u<rsub|n>,v<rsub|n>\<geqslant\>n> whose distances stay above
  some <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, contradicting the comparison.
  Completeness of <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|i>> then gives a limit, common to the
  two histories. This completes the finite backwards induction. It also
  proves common limits of their scalar corrections at scales below <math|h>.

  Now choose <math|h-m> so large that <math|2*c<rsup|h-m>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>/2>.
  Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:coupled-truncation> and the convergence of the two
  truncated histories show that the original activity difference is
  eventually below <math|\<varepsilon\>>. Apply the same
  arbitrary-subsequence argument once more. The original activity histories
  converge in <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|m>> to the same limit. Their scalar
  corrections then have common limits by local continuity at the next scale.
  Thus convergence of all the normalized couplings implies convergence of all
  the activities and costs.

  <subsubsection|Identifying the possible coupling limits>

  At each fixed scale the band for <math|\<lambda\><rsub|m,N>> is a compact
  real interval. Successive subsequence selection at scales
  <math|0,1,\<ldots\>> followed by the diagonal subsequence gives a
  subsequence on which every normalized coupling converges. The preceding
  argument attaches activity and scalar-correction limits to it. Denote one
  such limiting history by <math|<around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|m>,x<rsub|m>,q<rsub|m>|)>>
  and another by <math|<around|(|\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|m>,x<rprime|'><rsub|m>,q<rprime|'><rsub|m>|)>>.
  Suppose the bottom couplings of the two sequences have a common limit.
  Passing to the limit in <eqref|eq:coupled-recursion> and subtracting gives
  the exact finite identity

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:coupled-telescope>b<rsub|m><around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|m>|)>-b<rsub|m><around|(|\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|m>|)>=-<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>m><around|(|q<rsub|j>-q<rprime|'><rsub|j>|)>.
  </equation>

  The common limits of <math|\<alpha\><rsub|j,N>> cancel. There is no
  infinite sum and no terminal boundary term in this identity.

  The one-step inequalities also pass to these limits, despite being
  initially defined only for actual input arrays. To see this without
  postulating an extension of <math|F<rsub|m,N>>, construct at each terminal
  index a new backwards activity history with the fixed normalized coupling
  script <math|<around|(|\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|i>|)><rsub|i>> and zero
  terminal array. Its couplings are allowed, because the band is closed. By
  the previous subsection, its activities and costs have precisely the limits
  <math|x<rprime|'><rsub|m>,q<rprime|'><rsub|m>>. Compare it to the first
  history at the same terminal index using <eqref|eq:coupled-activity-step>
  and <eqref|eq:coupled-cost-step>, then pass to the limit. Writing

  <\equation*>
    d<rsub|m>=max <around|{|<around|\||\<lambda\><rsub|m>-\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|m>|\|>,<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|m>-x<rprime|'><rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>>|}>,
  </equation*>

  we obtain

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:coupled-limit-comparison><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|m>-x<rprime|'><rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>c*d<rsub|m+1>,<space|2em><around|\||q<rsub|m>-q<rprime|'><rsub|m>|\|>\<leqslant\>4*s<rsub|m>*d<rsub|m+1>.
  </equation>

  First observe that <math|d<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1/4> for all <math|m>. The
  activity bound follows from <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>>,<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rprime|'><rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1/8>.
  For the normalized coupling, <eqref|eq:coupled-inverse-bound>,
  <eqref|eq:coupled-telescope>, the bound
  <math|<around|\||q<rsub|j>|\|>,<around|\||q<rprime|'><rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|j>/2>
  and <eqref|eq:coupled-cumulative-payment> give

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|m>*<around|\||\<lambda\><rsub|m>-\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|m>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>m>s<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|m>/128.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|D=sup<rsub|m> d<rsub|m>> exists and lies in
  <math|<around|[|0,1/4|]>>. Using the sharper cost comparison now gives

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|m>*<around|\||\<lambda\><rsub|m>-\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|m>|\|>\<leqslant\>4*<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>m>s<rsub|j>*d<rsub|j+1>\<leqslant\><frac|4*D|128>*B<rsub|m>.
  </equation*>

  Together with <eqref|eq:coupled-limit-comparison> this yields
  <math|d<rsub|m>\<leqslant\><around|(|8/128|)>*D> for every <math|m>. Taking
  the supremum, <math|0\<leqslant\>D\<leqslant\>D/16>, hence <math|D=0>. Both
  limiting normalized coupling and activity histories are identical. The
  costs are identical too, by their one-step comparison.

  Every subsequence has a further subsequence with all normalized couplings
  convergent, and all such limits have just been identified. If the original
  normalized coupling at some scale failed to converge to this value, a
  subsequence would remain outside one fixed neighborhood, contradicting its
  convergent further subsequence. Thus the entire normalized coupling
  sequence converges at every scale. The first part of the proof then gives
  convergence of the entire activity and correction sequences. In particular
  this applies when the bottom coupling is fixed exactly, as in the selected
  public construction.

  <subsubsection|Why the cumulative payment can be imposed>

  The numerical inequality <eqref|eq:coupled-cumulative-payment> is a finite
  cumulative bound, not summability of a fixed sequence of costs over all
  scales. We record its purchase to distinguish the two assertions. On a
  stage with positive reference increment <math|a>, put
  <math|B<around|(|x|)>=x<rsup|31/32>>. For <math|x\<geqslant\>a\<gtr\>0>,
  the mean value theorem and monotonicity of <math|x<rsup|-1/32>> give

  <\equation*>
    B*<around|(|x+a|)>-B<around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>a*<frac|31|32>*2<rsup|-1/32>*x<rsup|-1/32>.
  </equation*>

  The corresponding cost allowance is twice the activity allowance divided by
  the squared fluctuation threshold. Its paid form is

  <\equation*>
    s<around|(|x|)>=<frac|2*v*C<rsub|<math-up|st>>*x<rsup|-11/10>|t<around|(|x|)><rsup|2>>,<space|2em>t<around|(|x|)>=x<rsup|-1/2>*exp
    <around|(|<around|(|log log x|)><rsup|2>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|v,C<rsub|<math-up|st>>\<gtr\>0> are fixed at this stage. For
  sufficiently large <math|x>, <math|t<around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>x<rsup|-1/2>>,
  so <math|s<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>2*v*C<rsub|<math-up|st>>*x<rsup|-1/10>>.
  Since <math|-1/10\<less\>-1/32>, increasing the entry threshold gives

  <\equation*>
    s<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><frac|1|128>*<around|{|B*<around|(|x+a|)>-B<around|(|x|)>|}>.
  </equation*>

  There are only finitely many permitted next stages at each stage, so their
  thresholds can be increased together after their block lengths, positive
  increments and constants have been fixed. The reference recursion is
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>+a<rsub|m>>.
  Summing the preceding inequalities over <math|j\<less\>m> telescopes and
  gives

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>m>s<rsub|j>\<leqslant\><frac|1|128>*<around|(|B<rsub|m>-B<rsub|0>|)>\<leqslant\><frac|1|128>*B<rsub|m>.
  </equation*>

  This proves the cumulative estimate required in the uniqueness argument. It
  preserves the chosen maps and block lengths, just as the other
  entry-threshold increases do.

  <subsection|One nonlinear return on a whole parameter
  interval><label|sec:selected-nonlinear-offering>

  The local estimates must supply a return which varies continuously with its
  input. Selecting a new support dictionary independently at every parameter
  would not suffice. We explain how to obtain the exact integral identity,
  both activity bounds and continuity in one dictionary on an arbitrary
  compact parameter interval. The scale purchases used below are fixed before
  that interval and the finite volume are chosen.

  Fix a finite cutoff <math|N>, adjacent levels <math|m+1,m> with
  <math|m\<less\>N>, and a period. Write <math|p<rsub|f>,p<rsub|c>> for the
  two activity budgets, <math|x<rsub|0>> for the reference coarse coupling
  and <math|L> for the block side. The fine reference coupling is
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|F><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>=x<rsub|0>+s<rsub|G>*log L>.
  Fix the already selected incoming and outgoing paths, their block bridge
  and the scalar shift <math|\<gamma\>=\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>>. Let
  <math|I=<around|[|a,b|]>> with <math|a\<leqslant\>b>, and let
  <math|Q<rsub|x>>, <math|x\<in\>I>, be a continuous family of fine
  representations in the permitted fine band. Continuity here includes the
  regular and forced coefficient arrays in their fixed labelled norms. Each
  <math|Q<rsub|x>> has the selected fine path and satisfies the same history
  constraints.

  We use the following purchased estimates on these data. The regular
  principal price is at most <math|E/16>, the complementary regular price at
  most <math|2*E/16>, and the preliminary forced price at most
  <math|K<around|(|x|)>/8>, where

  <\equation*>
    E=c<rsub|<math-up|st>>*\<delta\><rsub|E><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,<space|2em>K<around|(|x|)>=K<rsub|<math-up|forecast>>*<around|(|Q<rsub|x>.*\<beta\>|)>,<space|2em>\<delta\><rsub|E><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>=x<rsub|0><rsup|-11/10>,<space|1em>c<rsub|<math-up|st>>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation*>

  These are the principal and complementary bounds of the preceding local
  expansion, in the actual coarse budget. In the application
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|st>>> is the stage constant
  <math|c<rsup|<math-up|reset>>> of <eqref|eq:reset-stage-constants>, fixed
  before the block length, and <math|K<rsub|<math-up|forecast>>> is the
  forecast forced price fixed in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history>;
  the simultaneous purchase of the three displayed bounds at these values is
  made there. The selected path has its full quadratic, cubic, quartic and
  loop bounds, with width <math|\<sigma\><rsub|c>\<gtr\>0>; its loop
  allowance is the selected allowance at level <math|m>. The initial action
  is admissible in this same budget. If <math|A<rsub|c>> denotes the support
  exponent used before the finite symmetry average, the scale purchases also
  give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:selected-offer-width>A<rsub|c>=b<rsub|c>+3\<leqslant\>a<rsub|<math-up|earn>>,<space|2em>A<rsub|c>/B<rsub|c>\<leqslant\>\<sigma\><rsub|c>/4.
  </equation>

  Here <math|b<rsub|c>> is the coarse support exponent, <math|B<rsub|c>> its
  radius and <math|a<rsub|<math-up|earn>>> the earned exponent of the local
  expansion. In particular, these width and exponent conditions concern the
  same output path as the bounds, rather than a less expensive substitute.

  <subsubsection|Keeping the physical and auxiliary data fixed>

  At each <math|x\<in\>I>, apply the local expansion to <math|Q<rsub|x>> on
  the physical torus. Its hypotheses are supplied as follows. The block
  equation and both adjacent kernel bounds come from the selected horizon.
  Its all-period positive lower bounds apply to the two selected kernel
  levels; the bridge intertwining identities and the periodized rows are
  those of that same horizon. The input representation supplies the
  small-field and forced budgets and the fine band condition. The expansion
  is applied with the selected derivative orders, spatial weights, earned
  exponent, and its two error reserves. Its differentiated remainder bounds
  are retained with its output, as well as the undifferentiated integral
  identity. They cannot be recovered from that identity alone.

  Use the expansion at <math|x=a> as the reference dictionary. For every
  other <math|x>, identify elementary labels by the identity: the forests,
  geometric supports, sector labels and component types are unchanged. The
  path identity and the fixed regular and forced input dictionaries make this
  an admissible identification. Thus all output arrays can be read in the
  reference dictionary. This is precisely the comparison used in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:finite-volume-parameter-continuity>; no
  claim that an arbitrary choice of coordinates depends continuously on
  <math|x> is needed. That section has the pointwise compensated comparison
  estimate <eqref|eq:parameter-integrand-difference> as a hypothesis;
  equality of labels does not supply it. For the present output it is the
  collection of local estimates provided in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:parameter-comparison-suppliers>, applied
  with the same paths, clips, forests and tags at both parameters. The two
  conclusions used later are the following: at every <math|x\<in\>I> the
  output arrays at nearby parameters converge entry by entry to those at
  <math|x>, and every sequence in <math|I> converging to <math|x> has a
  subsequence on which the regular and forced entries have common summable
  majorants.

  The auxiliary tori used for local comparisons carry the same block bridge
  and the same two paths. On such a torus the reference fine representation
  is the bare path action with the zero activity array, all of whose regular
  and forced coefficients vanish. Its forced gas is the indicator that no
  fine site is flagged, so its density is

  <\equation*>
    <with|font-series|bold|1><rsub|<around|{|<text|all fine sites
    healthy>|}>>*exp <around|{|-\<beta\>*\<cal-K\><rsub|f>-\<cal-L\><rsub|f>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|f>> and <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|f>> are the fine
  kinetic and loop terms. The zero array has zero regular cost and zero
  forced cost. The fine path bounds and the clock inequality for the loop
  allowance therefore give the required incoming budgets. The fine threshold,
  radius and band are exactly those at <math|\<beta\><rsub|F><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>>.
  The all-period kernel bounds apply on every such torus, so the local
  expansion and its differentiated remainder bounds are available there too.
  The auxiliary expansions provide local comparisons; the density
  reconstruction below is performed on the physical torus.

  <subsubsection|The finite short density and its complement>

  The regular dictionary contains two kinds of entries: connected interaction
  clusters and path atoms. Divide them into a retained infinite part and an
  exhaustible short part. The selected interaction clusters have at most two
  polymer vertices, each formed from at most two elementary path letters of
  word length at most <math|B<rsub|c>>. Their connected coefficient is
  nonzero and each polymer is admissible. These letters have empty input
  roster: they contain neither incoming regular insertions nor incoming
  forced slots. Every cluster outside this particular class is retained in
  the complementary array. Its regular price is at most <math|3*E/16> by the
  principal and complementary estimates. The retained path part consists of
  atoms of word length greater than <math|B<rsub|c>>. Let
  <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|d>> contain the selected interaction clusters and the
  short path atoms whose elementary indices lie below the finite cutoff
  <math|d>. The panels are increasing and exhaust every nonretained entry.
  Leave the forced array unchanged at this intermediate operation, and set
  the regular entries of <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|d>> to zero. Call the resulting
  array <math|T<rsub|x,d>>.

  This complement is the sum of the retained infinite part and the unselected
  short tail, entry by entry. For a selected short entry both sides vanish;
  for a retained entry only the retained summand is present; for every other
  entry only the tail is present. Thus the triangle inequality adds their
  regular budgets without changing any support. The path tail requires a
  separate estimate. If its original width is <math|\<sigma\><rsub|c>> and
  its word length exceeds <math|B<rsub|c>>, exponential summability and
  <math|A<rsub|c>/B<rsub|c>\<leqslant\>\<sigma\><rsub|c>/4> give a tail bound

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|-\<sigma\><rsub|c>*B<rsub|c>/4>*\<cal-C\><rsub|<math-up|path>>\<leqslant\>g,<space|2em>g=\<beta\><rsub|F><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)><rsup|-23/20>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|<math-up|path>>> is the finite path-jet price at
  the chosen stage. The first inequality follows by splitting the exponential
  row weight into the part paying the support weight and the part paying the
  tail; the remaining positive weight is discarded. The second is one of the
  scale purchases, imposed after the stage is fixed. The finite symmetry
  average costs <math|C<rsub|<math-up|turn>>>, so the retained total has
  budget <math|3*E/16+C<rsub|<math-up|turn>>*g>.

  The unselected short tail has budget at most <math|g> for all sufficiently
  large <math|d>, uniformly in <math|x\<in\>I>. Here is the uniformity
  argument in the actual jet norms. On the fixed torus changing an anchor
  costs at most <math|<around|(|<around|(|1+D|)><rsup|p>|)><rsup|j>>, where
  <math|D> is its diameter and <math|p,j> are the spatial power and
  derivative order. The sum of the rooted regular majorants is finite, since
  there are finitely many anchors. Thus at any fixed <math|x> there is a
  summable majorant in the reference anchors, and exhaustion of the short
  labels makes its unselected sum tend to zero. Norm continuity gives a
  neighborhood of <math|x> where the difference array has arbitrarily small
  total price, so the same truncation works there. A finite subcover of
  <math|I> gives one truncation for the whole interval. Consequently

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:selected-offer-truncation><around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsub|x,d><rsup|<math-up|reg>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p<rsub|c>>\<leqslant\>h<rsub|0>\<assign\><frac|3|16>*E+C<rsub|<math-up|turn>>*g+g.
  </equation>

  The dependence of the tail index on the fixed torus and interval is
  allowed; its bound <math|h<rsub|0>> uses the purchased, volume-independent
  budget.

  Allocate each entry <math|F<rsub|a>> of the finite panel among its allowed
  anchors with nonnegative weights <math|\<omega\><rsub|o*a>> summing to one.
  For an interaction cluster this weight is the reciprocal of the number of
  its allowed anchors; for a path atom it is the corresponding fixed local
  allocation. Average over signed lattice symmetries and translations. Define
  the unmasked short density

  <\equation*>
    f<rsub|x,d>*<around|(|o,U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a\<in\>\<cal-P\><rsub|d>>\<omega\><rsub|o*a>*F<rsub|x,a><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  This is the finite connected-cluster contribution plus the finite path-atom
  contribution, with the same scalar shift <math|\<gamma\>>. The allocation
  is local: a nonzero weight implies that the entry's support lies in a fixed
  radius-<math|r> neighborhood of <math|o>. The weights sum to one even after
  the finite average. The masked panel contribution is therefore the sum of

  <\equation*>
    E<rsub|x,d>*<around|(|o,U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a\<in\>\<cal-P\><rsub|d>>\<omega\><rsub|o*a>*<with|font-series|bold|1><rsub|<around|{|U<text|
    healthy on ><math-up|supp><space|0.17em>F<rsub|a>|}>>*F<rsub|x,a><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  <subsubsection|Compatibility and the five auxiliary offers>

  Use the auxiliary bare inputs described above on the five tori of sides
  <math|2*<around|(|w+i|)>>, <math|0\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>4>, with
  <math|w\<geqslant\>2*r>. Their coupling is <math|Q<rsub|x>.*\<beta\>>, and
  their path, budget, bridge, scalar shift and finite label cutoff <math|d>
  are the physical ones. Let <math|f<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|x,d>> denote
  the corresponding short density. These are the actual auxiliary offers to
  the five-period construction of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:five-period-localization>.

  We check its compatibility condition. Under replication to a larger period,
  each short cluster meeting the designated anchor has a unique lift within
  the fixed local patch. Conversely projection gives its unique short cluster
  on the smaller period. The geometric margin
  <math|4*r+16\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|><rsup|1/4>> prevents
  wrapping in these identifications. They preserve the elementary component
  types and the cutoff on their labels, so they give a bijection of the
  finite panels at that anchor. The local connected coefficient is preserved
  as follows. On globally healthy fields the mask in each selected cluster
  equals one, so its paid masked response equals the actual connected
  coefficient. The graph coefficient depends only on the overlaps and
  equality relations of the selected polymers; the local bijection preserves
  both. Its factorial normalization is unchanged. The remaining coefficient
  is the product of the corresponding polymer integrals.

  For each polymer choose matching forests on the two periods. The selected
  letters have empty roster, and the fields are globally healthy, so the
  local integrals have no imposed input insertions or flags. Let <math|I> be
  the finite set of fluctuation coordinates read by the larger-period
  integrand, and let <math|e:I\<hookrightarrow\>J> be its embedding into the
  smaller-period coordinate set. The period margin makes this map injective.
  The local windows correspond bijectively: their principal matrices have
  identical entries, since the local fields and paths agree. Their inverses
  therefore correspond, as do their extensions by zero outside the windows.
  The covariance is the symmetrized local inverse. Consequently, if
  <math|C<rprime|'>> and <math|C> are the two covariances, then

  <\equation*>
    C<rprime|'>\|<rsub|I\<times\>I>=C*<around|[|e<around|(|I|)>,e<around|(|I|)>|]>.
  </equation*>

  This assertion concerns finite covariance submatrices; the full Gaussian
  spaces can have different dimensions.

  For completeness, a centered Gaussian with covariance <math|C> has
  characteristic function

  <\equation*>
    exp <around|(|-<around|\<langle\>|t,C*t|\<rangle\>>/2|)>.
  </equation*>

  Its projection to the coordinates <math|e<around|(|I|)>> therefore has
  characteristic function

  <\equation*>
    exp <around|(|-<around|\<langle\>|t,C*<around|[|e<around|(|I|)>,e<around|(|I|)>|]>*t|\<rangle\>>/2|)>.
  </equation*>

  The covariance identity shows that this projected law is the same as the
  larger Gaussian's marginal on <math|I>. Locality expresses both integrands
  as the same measurable function of these coordinates: the block tags, frame
  tags, elementary path words, and transported mean agree. Thus their
  Gaussian integrals agree. This equality holds for every pair of outer
  Haar-slice values, because the selected, unrostered terms are independent
  of those slice choices after the matching local data are fixed. The inner
  integral is consequently a common constant; integrating against the
  normalized outer Haar measures preserves it. This proves equality of the
  local integrals, including their normalizations.

  The forests used by the expansion are averaged, so equality for one
  matching pair must be lifted to equality of those averages. Forests are
  generated from finite independent local choices. Restrict these choices to
  the embedded smaller patch and project each local choice to its
  smaller-period version. The resulting map <math|P> between the finite
  choice sets has constant-size fibers: restriction forgets independent
  coordinates, and the local projection has constant-size fibers at each
  retained coordinate. Hence for any function <math|F> on the smaller choice
  set <math|\<cal-R\>>,

  <\equation*>
    <frac|1|<around|\||\<cal-R\><rprime|'>|\|>>*<big|sum><rsub|r<rprime|'>\<in\>\<cal-R\><rprime|'>>F*<around|(|P*r<rprime|'>|)>=<frac|1|<around|\||\<cal-R\>|\|>>*<big|sum><rsub|r\<in\>\<cal-R\>>F<around|(|r|)>.
  </equation*>

  Locality lets one replace an arbitrary larger forest by the matching lift
  of its restriction without changing the polymer integral. Apply the
  displayed identity first to the block and frame tags, then to the forest
  choices. This proves equality of the actual averaged polymer coefficients.
  The normalized anchor weight is also preserved because its allowed-anchor
  set is in bijection. For path atoms the identity follows directly from
  replicating the same word of link increments. Finally the finite symmetry
  average commutes with replication: translation fibers all have equal
  cardinality, which cancels against the normalized average. Thus the short
  density is preserved on globally healthy replicated fields. Passing two
  tori to their product period and using locality there proves the required
  compatibility for equal local patterns. The auxiliary bare input and the
  physical input can be compared here because the short cluster construction
  uses the same path and the same healthy local generation; terms retained
  from the incoming regular array remain in the complementary part, rather
  than being identified with bare-input terms.

  To price the centered auxiliary total, use its exact decomposition into the
  compensated interacting action, the complementary regular array and a
  constant. The estimate is that of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:healthy-boundary-jets>,
  applied as follows. On an auxiliary torus <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>> let
  <math|H<rsub|i>> be the uncentered short total and <math|u<rsub|i,a>> the
  complementary regular entries. On strictly healthy fields the compensated
  action <math|f<rsub|i>> satisfies <math|f<rsub|i>=z<rsub|i>-H<rsub|i>-<big|sum><rsub|a>u<rsub|i,a>>
  with a constant <math|z<rsub|i>>. That section assumes a chart estimate of
  amplitude <math|T> for the centered compensated action on the strictly
  healthy set, and a budget <math|b> per site for the complementary entries.
  It concludes <eqref|eq:healthy-interior-remainder>: the ordered derivatives
  of order at most <math|j> of the centered short total
  <math|H<rsub|i>-H<rsub|i><around|(|1|)>> are at most
  <math|j!<space|0.17em>3<rsup|j>*T+2<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|i>|\|>*b>,
  first on strictly healthy fields and then, because the finite short total
  is regular on the full healthy domain, at the threshold boundary. No
  boundary estimate for <math|f<rsub|i>> itself is obtained or used. The
  complementary array has budget <math|b=C<rsub|<math-up|turn>>*<around|(|g+g|)>+g>
  after the same uniform-tail choice. The chart estimate, with amplitude
  <math|T=g>, is the final small-field price of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:pure-action-small-field>. Its hypotheses are
  the stationary-branch bounds, the classical and quadratic matching and the
  exterior estimate for the selected paths on the auxiliary torus. The
  auxiliary torus carries the same two paths, clips and bridge, but this
  alone does not give those hypotheses there. The auxiliary
  stationary-service input consists of three further ingredients, which are
  inputs to the present section and are not constructed in it: a uniform
  stationary service for the same incoming path on the auxiliary one-step
  datum; the identification of its branch, after replication to a scheduled
  period which is a multiple of the auxiliary side, with the branch of the
  stationary plan of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:path-attachment-producer>,
  by uniqueness of the critical graph; and the resulting transfer of the
  thermal estimate, whose amplitude is the sum of the service amplitude and
  the plan amplitude and whose rate is the minimum of the two rates. Two
  standing hypotheses enter this transfer. First, the selected schedule has,
  beyond every bound, a period which is a multiple of the auxiliary side;
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection> provides such
  periods. Second, the chart table of the local price data is the local-ratio
  chart of the stationary plan. Write

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-T\><rsub|j><around|(|g|)>=j!<space|0.17em>3<rsup|j>*g.
  </equation*>

  Consequently the centered short total has derivative price

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|i>=\<cal-T\><rsub|j><around|(|g|)>+2<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|i>|\|>*<around|[|C<rsub|<math-up|turn>>*<around|(|g+g|)>+g|]>,
  </equation*>

  where the factor two covers the centered value at order zero, as in
  <eqref|eq:healthy-interior-remainder>. The finite-volume continuity
  argument applies to the entire closed fine coupling band on each of these
  five fixed tori, so one <math|d> suffices for all their couplings. The
  comparison charts are admissible by the purchased local gauge extension and
  logarithmic-radius margin. These are precisely the hypotheses used in the
  boundary-jet estimate, and apply to the same compensated action and
  selected stationary path.

  Let <math|g<rsub|x,d>*<around|(|o,U|)>> be the folded counterterm made from
  these five centered offers. Formula<nbsp><eqref|eq:five-period-local-price>
  gives its jet bound <math|c<rprime|'>=\<cal-O\><rsub|z>*<around|(|q<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,q<rsub|4>|)>>
  on the radius-<math|z> healthy neighborhood. The paid gauge extension puts
  every such healthy folded field into the chart just used; gauge invariance
  preserves the bound. Let <math|M> bound the coarse covering number of this
  neighborhood. Its weighted regular cost per anchor is

  <\equation*>
    s<rprime|'>=c<rprime|'>*e<rsup|A<rsub|c>*M>.
  </equation*>

  This follows by multiplying its jet bound by its support weight, which is
  at most <math|e<rsup|A<rsub|c>*M>>. It introduces no volume factor.

  <subsubsection|The discrepancy is a forced term>

  Use the shifted folded sum <math|h<rsub|x,d>*<around|(|o,U|)>> of
  <eqref|eq:five-period-shifted-localization>, and define

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|x,d>*<around|(|o,U|)>=E<rsub|x,d>*<around|(|o,U|)>-f<rsub|x,d>*<around|(|o,1|)>-h<rsub|x,d>*<around|(|o,U|)>.
  </equation*>

  The exact identity <eqref|eq:five-period-exact-decomposition> becomes

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:selected-offer-exact-split>A<rsub|x><around|(|U|)>=A<rsub|T<rsub|x,d>><around|(|U|)>+<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*f<rsub|x,d>*<around|(|0,1|)>+<big|sum><rsub|o>\<chi\><rsub|N<rsub|z><around|(|o|)>><around|(|U|)>*g<rsub|x,d>*<around|(|o,U|)>+<big|sum><rsub|o>D<rsub|x,d>*<around|(|o,U|)>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|A<rsub|x>> is the original regular sum, and
  <math|\<chi\><rsub|N<rsub|z><around|(|o|)>>> is its healthy indicator. The
  formula holds at every field. On a healthy radius-<math|2*z> neighborhood
  the allocation supports are healthy, and the folded identity identifies
  <math|h> with the centered <math|f>. Hence
  <math|D<rsub|x,d>*<around|(|o,U|)>=0> there. The discrepancy is local on
  that enlarged neighborhood and has the gauge invariance and measurability
  inherited from its finitely many summands. Its value bound is obtained by
  the triangle inequality for the panel sum and the five folded sums; this is
  the local value estimate purchased along with the original expansion.
  Explicitly, let <math|a<rsub|i>> be the five folding coefficients and
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>> the auxiliary tori, and let
  <math|h<rsub|<math-up|val>>> bound the value per anchor of a short panel
  sum on arbitrary fields. The purchased value estimate is

  <\equation*>
    h<rsub|<math-up|val>>=2<space|0.17em><math-up|ballCost><around|(|r|)>*<space|0.17em>C<rsub|<math-up|turn>>*<space|0.17em>\<beta\><rsub|F><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)><rsup|-7/16>,
  </equation*>

  and the triangle inequality gives the value bound

  <\equation*>
    q=2*h<rsub|<math-up|val>>*<around*|(|1+<big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|4><around|\||a<rsub|i>|\|><space|0.17em><around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|i>|\|>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Write <math|M<rsub|2>> for the covering-number bound of the doubled
  support; it is a stage constant, because the doubled radius is a fixed
  multiple of the coarse radius <math|B<rsub|c>>. Its soft absorption cost is

  <\equation*>
    b<rsub|<math-up|soft>>=<math-up|ballCost><around|(|2*z|)>*<space|0.17em>q*e<rsup|<around|(|b<rsub|c>+2|)>*M<rsub|2>>.
  </equation*>

  The factor <math|<math-up|ballCost><around|(|2*z|)>> bounds the number of
  anchors whose discrepancy support can meet a fixed site; the extra exponent
  reserve is the one needed for the raised forced budget. Finite symmetry
  averaging preserves the corresponding normalized anchor sum. The factors
  <math|<math-up|ballCost><around|(|2*z|)>>,
  <math|<math-up|ballCost><around|(|r|)>> and
  <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|i>|\|>> are polynomial in the logarithmic
  radius and so have upper exponent zero, the factor
  <math|e<rsup|<around|(|b<rsub|c>+2|)>*M<rsub|2>>> is a stage constant, and
  <math|q> therefore has upper exponent <math|-7/16\<less\>-1/8>. Hence
  <math|b<rsub|<math-up|soft>>\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rsub|F><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)><rsup|-1/8>>
  after increasing the entry threshold. This is a separate purchase from the
  regular jet cost <math|s<rprime|'>=c<rprime|'>*e<rsup|A<rsub|c>*M>>:
  <math|q> bounds the discrepancy on arbitrary fields, whereas
  <math|c<rprime|'>> bounds the folded counterterm's healthy jets.

  Crucially, the last sum in <eqref|eq:selected-offer-exact-split> is not
  discarded. Put <math|-D<rsub|x,d>> into the logarithmic correction array,
  and absorb it into the forced gas by the exact identity
  <math|e<rsup|-D>=1+<around|(|e<rsup|-D>-1|)>> and the support-join
  construction of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:soft-log-fusion>. The
  vanishing just proved supplies the eligibility condition of each new forced
  term. The old forced gas and this correction are combined with their actual
  support and inventory labels. Their hard input budget is at most
  <math|K<around|(|x|)>/8>, and their soft absorption cost is at most
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|F><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)><rsup|-1/8>>; the purchased
  absorption inequalities give forced output budget <math|K<around|(|x|)>>.
  This operation changes the forced gas exactly by the factor <math|exp
  <around|(|-<big|sum><rsub|o>D<rsub|x,d>*<around|(|o,U|)>|)>>.

  The regular output consists of <math|T<rsub|x,d>> and the folded
  counterterms, followed by the fixed symmetry average. Its budget is at most
  <math|h<rsub|0>+C<rsub|<math-up|turn>>*s<rprime|'>>. The scale purchase
  gives

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|<math-up|turn>>*g+g+C<rsub|<math-up|turn>>*c<rprime|'>*e<rsup|A<rsub|c>*M>\<leqslant\>E/2.
  </equation*>

  To justify that this purchase is available, <math|g> and <math|c<rprime|'>>
  have upper exponent <math|-23/20>, while <math|E> has exponent
  <math|-11/10>. Their fixed stage constants and powers of the logarithmic
  radius do not change this strict inequality of exponents. Thus an entry
  threshold makes the displayed inequality hold for every later coupling.
  Combining it with <eqref|eq:selected-offer-truncation> gives the bound
  <math|11*E/16\<leqslant\>E>. The forced estimate above is retained
  separately.

  <subsubsection|The exact return and its parameter dependence>

  Choose one spanning tree on the physical torus, the canonical sector
  measure, and the bookings associated with the selected expansion. Keep them
  fixed on <math|I>. Let <math|\<cal-X\>> be the enlarged reference
  dictionary, and let <math|v<rsub|x>> be the array formed from the truncated
  local expansion, the local counterterms and the forced correction just
  constructed. The scalar correction is

  <\equation*>
    \<tau\><rsub|x>=\<pi\><rsub|x>-f<rsub|x,d>*<around|(|0,1|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<pi\><rsub|x>> is the original scalar payment of the local
  expansion at <math|x>, per site, and <math|f<rsub|x,d>*<around|(|0,1|)>> is
  the value at the identity field of the short density at one anchor; by
  translation covariance it does not depend on the anchor. Thus the constant
  removed by the local subtraction is <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*f<rsub|x,d>*<around|(|0,1|)>>
  in the action, as in <eqref|eq:selected-offer-exact-split>, and
  <math|f<rsub|x,d>*<around|(|0,1|)>> per site. This sign follows by moving
  the removed constant from the action to its vacuum parameter. The exact
  decomposition of the local expansion and the local subtraction identity now
  give, for every coarse configuration, the original Gaussian return
  evaluated using <math|<around|(|\<cal-X\>,v<rsub|x>,\<tau\><rsub|x>|)>>.
  This is equality of the complete unnormalized density, with the selected
  <math|\<gamma\>>, not merely equality of a finite jet at the identity.
  Absolute summability of the regular arrays justifies subtraction and
  regrouping: the known and subtracted regular occurrences add term by term
  to the original occurrences, and the discrepancy factor is included in the
  forced gas as in <eqref|eq:selected-offer-exact-split>. The finite
  regrouping and forced absorption preserve the integral identity by their
  exact exponential and gas identities.

  All regular entries are local and gauge invariant with the prescribed jets;
  the forced entries are local, measurable and gauge invariant. Their bounds
  are <math|E> and <math|K<around|(|x|)>> respectively, in the following
  raised form. For each <math|x> there exist majorants
  <math|u<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>0> of the regular norms of the
  regular entries, anchored at <math|o<rsub|i>> on supports <math|X<rsub|i>>,
  and majorants <math|k<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>0> of the suprema of
  the forced entries on supports <math|Y<rsub|i>>, such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:selected-offer-budgets>sup<rsub|o>
    <big|sum><rsub|i:<space|0.17em>o<rsub|i>=o>e<rsup|<around|(|b<rsub|c>+3|)>*c<rsub|B<rsub|c>><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>*u<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>E,<space|2em>sup<rsub|o>
    <big|sum><rsub|i:<space|0.17em>o\<in\>Y<rsub|i>>e<rsup|<around|(|b<rsub|c>+1|)>*c<rsub|B<rsub|c>><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>>*k<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>K<around|(|x|)>.
  </equation>

  The regular exponent is <math|A<rsub|c>=b<rsub|c>+3>, by
  <eqref|eq:selected-offer-width> and the weights used for <math|h<rsub|0>>
  and <math|s<rprime|'>>; the forced exponent <math|b<rsub|c>+1> is the
  output exponent of the absorption at reserve <math|b<rsub|c>+2>. These are
  the exponents required in <eqref|eq:reset-early-majorant>. Bounds at the
  ordinary exponent <math|b<rsub|c>> would not suffice there. The
  common-dictionary parameter comparison survives truncation, addition, fixed
  pullbacks and finite averages, each of which is continuous in these array
  norms. For the remaining infinite arrays, the summable comparison majorants
  justify passage to the limit. Thus <math|v<rsub|x>> has the required
  continuous parameter dependence in the same dictionary <math|\<cal-X\>>.

  We have proved the interval return needed by the scalar coupling reset: for
  every compact interval and every allowed input curve there is one
  dictionary, one tree and one sector choice, with an exact return and both
  local budgets throughout the interval. In the notation of
  <eqref|eq:reset-early-density>, the return is the identity

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-H\><rsub|x><around|(|U|)>=exp <around*|{|<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*\<tau\><rsub|x>-Q<rsub|x>.*\<beta\>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-K\><around|(|U|)>-\<cal-L\><around|(|U|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i>\<chi\><rsub|X<rsub|i>><around|(|U|)>*v<rsup|<math-up|reg>><rsub|x,i><around|(|U|)>-\<gamma\>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-Q\><around|(|U|)>|}>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-G\><rsub|v<rsub|x>><around|(|U|)>
  </equation*>

  at every coarse field <math|U>, where <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|x>> is the
  transferred unnormalized density of <math|Q<rsub|x>>,
  <math|\<cal-K\>,\<cal-L\>> are the kinetic and loop terms of the selected
  coarse path, <math|\<cal-Q\>> is the masked low-channel quadratic sum of
  that path, and <math|\<cal-G\><rsub|v<rsub|x>>> is the forced gas of
  <math|v<rsub|x>>. It comes with the budgets
  <eqref|eq:selected-offer-budgets> and with the two comparison properties
  stated in the first subsection, now for the arrays <math|v<rsub|x>> in the
  dictionary <math|\<cal-X\>>. This statement still uses the simultaneous
  scale purchases listed at the start. Their selection must precede all
  intervals, histories and periods. The scalar reset then has a separate
  task: choose the new coupling and vacuum parameters without losing this
  density identity or its labelled estimates.

  <subsection|Reorganizing the actual return before resetting the
  coupling><label|sec:physical-reset-reorganization>

  We now turn the local return into the particular activity family used in
  the scalar selection. This requires an identity on every field. The
  difference between a local quadratic counterterm and the clipped kinetic
  action has a contribution on unhealthy fields, which must remain in the
  forced gas.

  Fix the compact parameter interval, dictionary and local return of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-nonlinear-offering>. Work first at
  one parameter value. Let <math|V=<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>>, let
  <math|0\<less\>t\<leqslant\>1> be the coarse threshold, and write
  <math|f<rsub|i>> for its regular entries, anchored at <math|o<rsub|i>> on
  supports <math|X<rsub|i>>. Let <math|k<rsub|i>> be its forced entries. The
  budget has radius <math|B\<geqslant\>2>, support exponent <math|b\<gtr\>0>,
  derivative order <math|q\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, <math|q\<geqslant\>2>, and
  spatial power <math|p\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, <math|p\<geqslant\>1>. The early
  regular majorants <math|u<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0> obey

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reset-early-majorant><around|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|o<rsub|i>,X<rsub|i>>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|i>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|i:o<rsub|i>=o>e<rsup|<around|(|b+3|)>*c<rsub|B><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>*u<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>H*<space|1em><around|(|o\<in\>\<Lambda\>|)>,
  </equation>

  where <math|c<rsub|B>> is the covering number. The forced majorants obey
  the rooted hit bound at exponent <math|b+1>, of cost <math|w>. All entries
  transform under the same permutations of labels for translations and signed
  lattice symmetries. The selected path has width <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0>,
  row and higher-atom price <math|C>, and relaxation radius <math|r<rsub|c>>
  with

  <\equation*>
    12\<leqslant\>r<rsub|c>*\<sigma\>,<space|2em><around|(|b+1|)>/B\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>/4.
  </equation*>

  Write <math|c<rsub|<math-up|direct>>> for the direct coefficient of the
  clip. The clipping parameters satisfy

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reset-clip-conditions>c<rsub|<math-up|direct>>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>2\<leqslant\>t*<space|0.17em>e<rsup|<around|(|B-2|)>/r<rsub|c>>.
  </equation>

  The first inequality is a consequence of the metric normalization of the
  selected path recalled below. The second makes the kept-row test local.
  Recall that the row at an anchor <math|o> is kept when every plaquette
  <math|q> has chord at most <math|t*exp <around|(|<math-up|dist><around|(|o,q|)>/r<rsub|c>|)>>.
  Chords are at most <math|2>, so <eqref|eq:reset-clip-conditions> makes this
  requirement automatic for every plaquette at distance at least <math|B-2>
  from <math|o>. The remaining plaquettes have all four corners in the
  radius-<math|B> halo of <math|o>. Hence the test depends only on the field
  in that halo, and it holds whenever the halo is healthy, because the chords
  read there are then at most <math|t>. These are the conditions already used
  for the actual clipped path. The torus side is greater than <math|20>.

  <subsubsection|The scalar coefficient extracted from the local Hessians>

  We first record how the Hessian of a regular entry acts on affine fields.
  Let <math|f> be a regular entry on a support <math|X> which is nonwrapping
  at its anchor <math|o>, and let <math|D<rsup|2>*f> denote the second
  derivative at zero of <math|A\<mapsto\>f<around|(|exp A|)>>, where <math|A>
  runs over Lie-algebra valued link fields. An affine field anchored at
  <math|o> is a link field

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>=a<rsub|j>+<big|sum><rsub|k><around|(|x-o|)><rsub|k>*<space|0.17em>b<rsub|j*k>,
  </equation*>

  with Lie-algebra coefficients <math|a<rsub|j>,b<rsub|j*k>>; the difference
  <math|x-o> is read in <math|\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>, which is unambiguous on a
  nonwrapping support. Its curl is the constant two-form
  <math|w<rsub|j*k>=b<rsub|k*j>-b<rsub|j*k>>, <math|j\<less\>k>. We claim
  that <math|D<rsup|2>*f*<around|(|A,A<rprime|'>|)>>, for two affine fields,
  depends only on their curls. There are four steps.

  First, <math|D*f=0> at the identity. A constant gauge transformation acts
  on link directions by the adjoint action on every link. Invariance of
  <math|f> makes the restriction of <math|D*f> to one link an invariant
  linear functional on the Lie algebra. Such a functional vanishes on
  commutators, and a simple Lie algebra is spanned by its commutators.

  Second, <math|D<rsup|2>*f*<around|(|\<nabla\>*\<phi\>,u|)>=0> for every
  Lie-algebra valued site function <math|\<phi\>> and every direction
  <math|u>, where <math|<around|(|\<nabla\>*\<phi\>|)><rsub|j><around|(|x|)>=\<phi\>*<around|(|x+e<rsub|j>|)>-\<phi\><around|(|x|)>>.
  Indeed, let <math|g<rsub|s>> be the gauge transformation
  <math|x\<mapsto\>exp <around|(|-s*\<phi\><around|(|x|)>|)>>. Near zero the
  field <math|g<rsub|s>\<cdot\>exp A> has a smooth logarithm
  <math|c*<around|(|A,s|)>> with <math|c*<around|(|0,0|)>=0> and derivative
  <math|<around|(|A,s|)>\<mapsto\>A+s*\<nabla\>*\<phi\>> there. Gauge
  invariance says <math|f*<around|(|exp c*<around|(|A,s|)>|)>=f<around|(|exp
  A|)>>. Differentiate this identity twice at zero. Since <math|D*f=0> at the
  identity, the second derivative of the chart <math|c> does not enter, and

  <\equation*>
    D<rsup|2>*f*<around|(|A+s*\<nabla\>*\<phi\>,A<rprime|'>+s<rprime|'>*\<nabla\>*\<phi\>|)>=D<rsup|2>*f*<around|(|A,A<rprime|'>|)>
  </equation*>

  for all <math|<around|(|A,s|)>> and <math|<around|(|A<rprime|'>,s<rprime|'>|)>>.
  Take <math|<around|(|A,s|)>=<around|(|0,1|)>> and
  <math|<around|(|A<rprime|'>,s<rprime|'>|)>=<around|(|u,0|)>>.

  Third, <math|D<rsup|2>*f> is symmetric, and
  <math|D<rsup|2>*f*<around|(|A,A<rprime|'>|)>> is unchanged when <math|A> or
  <math|A<rprime|'>> is modified on links not contained in <math|X>, since
  <math|f> reads only the links of <math|X>.

  Fourth, on a nonwrapping support a curl-free affine field is a lattice
  gradient. If <math|b<rsub|j*k>=b<rsub|k*j>>, put

  <\equation*>
    \<phi\><around|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j><around|(|x-o|)><rsub|j>*<around*|(|a<rsub|j>-<tfrac|1|2>b<rsub|j*j>|)>+<tfrac|1|2><big|sum><rsub|j,k><around|(|x-o|)><rsub|j>*<around|(|x-o|)><rsub|k>*b<rsub|j*k>.
  </equation*>

  Then <math|\<phi\>*<around|(|x+e<rsub|d>|)>-\<phi\><around|(|x|)>=a<rsub|d>-<tfrac|1|2>b<rsub|d*d>+<tfrac|1|2><around*|(|2*<big|sum><rsub|k><around|(|x-o|)><rsub|k>*b<rsub|d*k>+b<rsub|d*d>|)>=A<rsub|d><around|(|x|)>>
  on every link of the support.

  Now let <math|A,<wide|A|~>> be affine fields with the same curl. Their
  difference is a curl-free affine field, hence agrees on the links of
  <math|X> with a gradient. The second and third steps give
  <math|D<rsup|2>*f*<around|(|A,u|)>=D<rsup|2>*f*<around|(|<wide|A|~>,u|)>>
  for every <math|u>, and symmetry gives the same statement in the second
  argument. This proves the claim.

  For a plaquette pair <math|a=<around|(|i,j|)>>, <math|i\<less\>j>, and a
  Lie-algebra element <math|v>, let <math|W<rsub|a><around|(|v|)>> be the
  affine field whose links in direction <math|j> have the value
  <math|<around|(|x-o|)><rsub|i>*v> and whose other links vanish. Its curl is
  <math|v> in the plane <math|a> and zero in the other planes. Every affine
  field with curl <math|w> therefore has the same curl as
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>W<rsub|a><around|(|w<rsub|a>|)>>. The bilinear form
  <math|<around|(|v,v<rprime|'>|)>\<mapsto\>D<rsup|2>*f*<around|(|W<rsub|a><around|(|v|)>,W<rsub|d><around|(|v<rprime|'>|)>|)>>
  is invariant under the adjoint action, by constant gauge invariance.
  Simplicity of the Lie algebra identifies every invariant bilinear form with
  a scalar multiple of the invariant inner product. Hence there are real
  numbers <math|Q<rsub|f>*<around|(|a,d|)>> with

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reset-affine-hessian>D<rsup|2>*f*<around|(|A,A<rprime|'>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a,d>Q<rsub|f>*<around|(|a,d|)><around|\<langle\>|w<rsub|a>,w<rprime|'><rsub|d>|\<rangle\>>
  </equation>

  for all affine fields <math|A,A<rprime|'>> anchored at <math|o>, with curls
  <math|w,w<rprime|'>>. Symmetry of the Hessian gives
  <math|Q<rsub|f>*<around|(|a,d|)>=Q<rsub|f>*<around|(|d,a|)>>.

  For each <math|i> put <math|Z<rsub|i>=X<rsub|i>\<cup\><math-up|halo><rsub|2><around|{|o<rsub|i>|}>>.
  On a nonwrapping <math|Z<rsub|i>>, let <math|Q<rsub|i>*<around|(|a,d|)>=Q<rsub|f<rsub|i>>*<around|(|a,d|)>>
  be the coefficient in <eqref|eq:reset-affine-hessian> for the entry
  <math|f<rsub|i>> and its anchor <math|o<rsub|i>>. Set <math|Q<rsub|i>=0>
  when <math|Z<rsub|i>> wraps. This convention is preserved by the label
  symmetries. The Hessian estimates give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reset-projection-price>t<rsup|2>*<around|\||Q<rsub|i>*<around|(|a,a|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|i>,<space|2em>t<rsup|2>*<around|\||Q<rsub|i>*<around|(|a,d|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>3*u<rsub|i>.
  </equation>

  Indeed, test a unit colour vector on one affine curvature direction, scaled
  by <math|t>, for the first inequality. Its link size is bounded by
  <math|t<math-up|dist><around|(|e<rsub|->,o|)>>, hence by
  <math|t*<around|(|1+<math-up|dist><around|(|e<rsub|->,o|)>|)><rsup|p>>, so
  it is an admissible direction. For the second use the direction
  <math|t*<around|(|A<rsub|a>+A<rsub|d>|)>/2>. Expanding its symmetric
  Hessian gives four times this diagonal test minus the two single-direction
  tests, divided by two. The derivative bounds give
  <math|<around|(|4+1+1|)>*u<rsub|i>/2=3*u<rsub|i>>. For wrapping supports
  the inequalities hold because <math|Q<rsub|i>=0>.

  The series <math|Q<rsub|o>*<around|(|a,d|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i:o<rsub|i>=o>Q<rsub|i>*<around|(|a,d|)>>
  converges absolutely by <eqref|eq:reset-early-majorant>. Reindexing the
  label series by a translation shows that <math|Q<rsub|o>> is independent of
  <math|o>. Reindexing by a reflection of an axis lying in exactly one of two
  distinct plaquette pairs changes the sign of the corresponding off-diagonal
  coefficient, so that coefficient is zero. An unsigned permutation carries
  any plaquette pair to any other, so all diagonal coefficients agree.
  Consequently there is one real number <math|\<Delta\>> with

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reset-isotropic-coefficient>Q<rsub|o>*<around|(|a,d|)>=\<Delta\>*<space|0.17em>\<delta\><rsub|a*d>,<space|2em>t<rsup|2><around|\||\<Delta\>|\|>\<leqslant\>H.
  </equation>

  The last bound uses the diagonal estimate in
  <eqref|eq:reset-projection-price> at a single anchor; no volume factor
  occurs.

  The curvature read in the counterterm is the signed clover. For an axis
  pair <math|a=<around|(|i,j|)>>, put

  <\equation*>
    \<phi\><rsub|a>*<around|(|o,U|)>=<frac|1|4>*<big|sum><rsub|\<epsilon\>,\<eta\>\<in\><around|{|\<pm\>1|}>>\<epsilon\>*\<eta\><space|0.17em><math-up|curv><rsub|G><around*|(|U*<around|(|o;\<epsilon\>*e<rsub|i>,\<eta\>*e<rsub|j>,-\<epsilon\>*e<rsub|i>,-\<eta\>*e<rsub|j>|)>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where the argument is the holonomy of the indicated four-step loop based at
  <math|o>, and <math|<math-up|curv><rsub|G>> is the fixed normalized
  Lie-algebra projection. Each loop lies in the radius-two halo. A gauge
  transformation acts by the adjoint at <math|o>; a signed lattice symmetry
  permutes these four loops and their signs. This gives exactly the signed
  two-form transformation law at the common anchor. On the exponential line
  of an affine link field of constant curvature <math|w>, the derivative of
  each oriented loop's curvature at zero is
  <math|\<epsilon\>*\<eta\>*w<rsub|a>>. The average therefore has derivative
  <math|w<rsub|a>>, with no extra normalization factor. Define

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>=<frac|1|2>*<big|sum><rsub|a,d>Q<rsub|i>*<around|(|a,d|)><around|\<langle\>|\<phi\><rsub|a>*<around|(|o<rsub|i>,U|)>,\<phi\><rsub|d>*<around|(|o<rsub|i>,U|)>|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em>r<rsub|i>=f<rsub|i>-f<rsub|i><around|(|1|)>-P<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  The affine Hessian of <math|r<rsub|i>> vanishes when <math|Z<rsub|i>> is
  nonwrapping, and <math|r<rsub|i><around|(|1|)>=0>. To see the first
  assertion, let <math|A,A<rprime|'>> be affine fields anchored at
  <math|o<rsub|i>>, with curls <math|w,w<rprime|'>>. Each
  <math|\<phi\><rsub|a>*<around|(|o<rsub|i>,\<cdot\>|)>> vanishes at the
  identity and has derivative <math|w<rsub|a>> along <math|A>, as just shown.
  The product rule and <math|Q<rsub|i>*<around|(|a,d|)>=Q<rsub|i>*<around|(|d,a|)>>
  give

  <\equation*>
    D<rsup|2>*P<rsub|i>*<around|(|A,A<rprime|'>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a,d>Q<rsub|i>*<around|(|a,d|)><around|\<langle\>|w<rsub|a>,w<rprime|'><rsub|d>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation*>

  By <eqref|eq:reset-affine-hessian> this equals
  <math|D<rsup|2>*f<rsub|i>*<around|(|A,A<rprime|'>|)>>, and the constant
  <math|f<rsub|i><around|(|1|)>> has zero Hessian. Hence
  <math|D<rsup|2>*r<rsub|i>*<around|(|A,A<rprime|'>|)>=0>. No assertion is
  made for wrapping <math|Z<rsub|i>>, where <math|Q<rsub|i>=0>.

  Write <math|\<chi\><rsub|X>> for the healthy-set indicator on <math|X>. A
  site is unflagged precisely when every plaquette having it as a corner is
  healthy. In particular <math|\<chi\><rsub|<around|{|o<rsub|i>|}>>=1> makes
  the four clover plaquettes at <math|o<rsub|i>> healthy. Define the
  supported discrepancy

  <\equation*>
    d<rsub|i>=f<rsub|i><around|(|1|)>+\<chi\><rsub|<around|{|o<rsub|i>|}>>*P<rsub|i>-\<chi\><rsub|X<rsub|i>>*f<rsub|i>+\<chi\><rsub|Z<rsub|i>>*r<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  It vanishes when <math|Z<rsub|i>> is healthy: all three indicators then
  equal one, and the four terms cancel by the definition of <math|r<rsub|i>>.
  When <math|Z<rsub|i>> is not healthy the last term is absent. In both cases

  <\equation*>
    \<chi\><rsub|X<rsub|i>>*f<rsub|i>=f<rsub|i><around|(|1|)>+\<chi\><rsub|<around|{|o<rsub|i>|}>>*P<rsub|i>-d<rsub|i>+\<chi\><rsub|Z<rsub|i>>*r<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  This has the form of the masked splitting of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-splitting>, but it is not a special
  case of that identity. There the extracted section is masked by the health
  of the stencil on which it is supported. Here the quadratic counterterm is
  masked by the health of the single anchor <math|o<rsub|i>>, which is what
  allows the counterterms of all entries with a common anchor to be summed
  below. The displayed identity is therefore verified directly, as was just
  done. Put

  <\equation*>
    c<rsub|0>=<big|sum><rsub|i>f<rsub|i><around|(|1|)>,<space|1em>D=<big|sum><rsub|i>d<rsub|i>,<space|1em>R<rsub|0>=<big|sum><rsub|i>\<chi\><rsub|Z<rsub|i>>*r<rsub|i>,<space|1em>h<around|(|U|)>=<frac|1|2>*<big|sum><rsub|o>\<chi\><rsub|<around|{|o|}>><around|(|U|)>*<big|sum><rsub|a><around|\||\<phi\><rsub|a>*<around|(|o,U|)>|\|><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  All sums converge absolutely. For example each summand is bounded by a
  fixed multiple of <math|u<rsub|i>>, and summing
  <eqref|eq:reset-early-majorant> over the finite set of anchors bounds
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>u<rsub|i>> by <math|V*H>. The isotropic coefficient
  identity now gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reset-first-split><big|sum><rsub|i>\<chi\><rsub|X<rsub|i>>*f<rsub|i>=c<rsub|0>+\<Delta\>*h-D+R<rsub|0>.
  </equation>

  The common singleton indicator at each anchor is essential to summing the
  quadratic counterterms before using isotropy.

  <subsubsection|Replacing the quadratic counterterm by the selected path>

  Write <math|\<cal-K\>> for the actual clipped kinetic action and
  <math|\<cal-L\>> for its loop part. The early return is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reset-early-density>e<rsup|V*\<tau\>-\<beta\>*\<cal-K\><around|(|U|)>-\<cal-L\><around|(|U|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i>\<chi\><rsub|X<rsub|i>><around|(|U|)>*f<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>-\<alpha\>*\<cal-Q\><around|(|U|)>>*\<cal-G\><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<cal-G\>> is the early forced gas and <math|\<cal-Q\>> is the
  masked low-channel presentation of the quadratic action. It includes the
  kept-row halo in each support. Thus it is specified before the reset; it is
  not the full kinetic action on arbitrary fields.

  We describe the needed identity channel by channel. A low channel is either
  a plaquette term or an ordered pair of entries in one row of the selected
  path. Denote its coefficient by <math|a<rsub|d>>, its unmasked quadratic
  field by <math|P<rsub|d>>, its local constant-curvature substitute by
  <math|Q<rsub|d>>, and its actual clipped or kept field by <math|L<rsub|d>>.
  For a row pair,

  <\equation*>
    a<rsub|d>=c<rsub|i>*c<rsub|j>,<space|2em>P<rsub|d>=<tfrac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|\<phi\><rsub|i>,\<phi\><rsub|j>|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em>Q<rsub|d>=<tfrac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|\<phi\><rsub|a<rsub|i>>,\<phi\><rsub|a<rsub|j>>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation*>

  The latter curvature reads are at the common anchor. For a plaquette,
  <math|a<rsub|d>> is the direct coefficient, <math|P<rsub|d>> is its
  plaquette action and <math|Q<rsub|d>> is half the squared local curvature.
  Let <math|Y<rsub|d>> be the union of the radius-<math|B> anchor halo with
  the two walks when present. On its healthy set,
  <math|L<rsub|d>=a<rsub|d>*P<rsub|d>>. Define

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|F<rsub|d>>|<cell|=\<Delta\>*a<rsub|d>*<around|(|Q<rsub|d>-P<rsub|d>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|J<rsub|d>>|<cell|=<around|(|\<alpha\>+\<Delta\>|)>*L<rsub|d>-\<Delta\>*a<rsub|d>*\<chi\><rsub|<around|{|o|}>>*Q<rsub|d><space|1em><text|on
    the complement of the healthy set of >Y<rsub|d>,>>>>
  </align*>

  and set <math|J<rsub|d>=0> on that healthy set. Directly in these two
  cases,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reset-low-identity><around|(|\<alpha\>+\<Delta\>|)>*L<rsub|d>+\<chi\><rsub|Y<rsub|d>>*F<rsub|d>=\<alpha\>*\<chi\><rsub|Y<rsub|d>>*a<rsub|d>*P<rsub|d>+\<Delta\>*a<rsub|d>*\<chi\><rsub|<around|{|o|}>>*Q<rsub|d>+J<rsub|d>.
  </equation>

  This also proves that the discrepancy is supported on an unhealthy
  <math|Y<rsub|d>>. No derivative of its indicator is needed.

  Average these identities over signed symmetries and sum over translations,
  with normalization <math|1/<around|\||\<cal-S\>|\|>> for the signed group
  <math|\<cal-S\>>. This normalization is applied to every channel, including
  its discrepancy. To identify the quadratic substitute, let
  <math|S<around|(|0|)>> be the actual zero-momentum row matrix. Absolute row
  summability permits the row-pair sum to be regrouped as

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|d>a<rsub|d>*Q<rsub|d><around|(|w|)>=<tfrac|1|2><around|{|c<rsub|<math-up|direct>>\<\|\|\>w\<\|\|\><rsup|2>+<around|\<\|\|\>|S<around|(|0|)>*w|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>|}>=<tfrac|1|2><around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  The second equality is the selected metric normalization
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|direct>>*I+S<around|(|0|)><rsup|T>*S<around|(|0|)>=I>.
  Multiplying by the singleton healthy indicator and summing anchors gives
  <math|h>; the signed average preserves the squared norm of the clover. Thus
  the averaged sum of <math|a<rsub|d>*\<chi\><rsub|<around|{|o|}>>*Q<rsub|d>>
  is exactly <math|h>. The averaged sum of
  <math|\<chi\><rsub|Y<rsub|d>>*a<rsub|d>*P<rsub|d>> is <math|\<cal-Q\>>. For
  every cubic or quartic path atom <math|A<rsub|d>>, add the regular
  correction <math|-<around|(|\<alpha\>+\<Delta\>|)>*A<rsub|d>> on its
  original mask. These atoms have zero affine Hessian because their curvature
  factors vanish at the identity and their degrees are at least three. Denote
  the complete averaged regular correction by <math|F>, and the averaged sum
  of <math|J<rsub|d>> by <math|J>. Since the low and high channels sum to
  <math|\<cal-K\>>, equation <eqref|eq:reset-low-identity> becomes

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reset-path-identity><around|(|\<alpha\>+\<Delta\>|)>*\<cal-K\>+F=\<alpha\>*\<cal-Q\>+\<Delta\>*h+J.
  </equation>

  The low correction also has zero affine Hessian: on an affine field at the
  identity, transport along each path has the same first curvature derivative
  as its constant-curvature substitute. Thus <math|Q<rsub|d>-P<rsub|d>> has
  zero second derivative there. Polarization gives the mixed assertion. This
  argument is only used on nonwrapping supports, where the affine field and
  its walks have their specified lifts.

  Combining <eqref|eq:reset-first-split> and <eqref|eq:reset-path-identity>
  in <eqref|eq:reset-early-density> gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reset-complete-density>e<rsup|V*<around|(|\<tau\>-c<rsub|0>/V|)>-<around|(|\<beta\>+\<alpha\>+\<Delta\>|)>*\<cal-K\>-\<cal-L\>-<around|(|R<rsub|0>+F|)>>*\<cal-G\>*<space|0.17em>e<rsup|D+J>.
  </equation>

  Every sign follows by substituting the two displayed identities. This
  equality holds even where <math|\<cal-G\>> vanishes or changes sign. Absorb
  <math|D+J> into that gas using the supported exponential construction of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:soft-log-fusion>. Its hypotheses hold
  because each discrepancy vanishes on its healthy support. This constructs
  the actual output at coupling <math|\<beta\>+\<alpha\>+\<Delta\>> and
  vacuum parameter <math|\<tau\>-c<rsub|0>/V>.

  <subsubsection|The regular and forced costs of this reorganization>

  We give the numerical bounds so that this identity is accompanied by
  estimates for its actual arrays. Let <math|C<rsub|<math-up|pair>>> be the
  fixed ordered-derivative price for a pair of local curvatures, and put

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|<math-up|sub>>=2+<tfrac|3|2><around|\||\<cal-A\>|\|><rsup|2>*C<rsub|<math-up|pair>>,<space|2em>C<rsub|<math-up|reset>>=e<rsup|81*b>*C<rsub|<math-up|sub>>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<cal-A\>> is the six-element set of plaquette axes.
  Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:reset-projection-price> and the product rule bound
  both the regular norm of <math|r<rsub|i>> and the value of <math|d<rsub|i>>
  by <math|C<rsub|<math-up|sub>>*u<rsub|i>>. The added radius-two halo
  increases the cover number by at most <math|81>, whence the regular cost is
  at most <math|C<rsub|<math-up|reset>>*H>. Its hit cost, denoted
  <math|S<around|(|H|)>>, is the footprint payment with population radius
  <math|4*B+2>, support increment <math|81>, input exponent <math|b+3> and
  output exponent <math|b+1>. In the notation of the explicit footprint price
  <math|\<cal-P\>> of Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-lattice-rg-crowd>,

  <\equation*>
    S<around|(|H|)>=\<cal-P\>*<around|(|1,4*B+2,0,1,C<rsub|<math-up|sub>>,H,b+1,81|)>.
  </equation*>

  This is the ordinary application, at exponent <math|b+1> and width reserve
  one; it is not the raised application displayed in that section. It is
  linear in <math|H> and has only polynomial dependence on <math|B> at fixed
  stage.

  Let <math|C<rsub|<math-up|turn>>> be the ordered-derivative cost of a
  signed pullback. For the low and high channels let
  <math|L<rsub|0>,L<rsub|1>> be their rooted regular sums after support
  weighting but before this pullback cost. Let <math|L<rsub|h>> be the
  low-channel hit sum, with the relaxation factor
  <math|e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|d>/r<rsub|c>>> included. The channel estimates
  give

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|<math-up|low>>|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|turn>><around|\||\<Delta\>|\|>*t<rsup|2>*L<rsub|0>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|<math-up|high>>|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|turn>>*<around|\||\<alpha\>+\<Delta\>|\|>*t<rsup|3>*L<rsub|1>,<eq-number><label|eq:reset-channel-costs>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|J|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|hit>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\><around*|(|2*t*<around|\||\<alpha\>+\<Delta\>*<around|\||+<tfrac|1|2>C<rsub|<math-up|pair>>*t<rsup|2>|\|>*\<Delta\>|\|>|)>*L<rsub|h>.>>>>
  </align>

  Here is the summability behind these costs. A low support satisfies
  <math|B*c<rsub|B><around|(|Y<rsub|d>|)>\<leqslant\>85*B+\<ell\><rsub|d>>
  and a high support satisfies <math|B*c<rsub|B><around|(|Y<rsub|d>|)>\<leqslant\>9*B+\<ell\><rsub|d>>.
  The weighted row-pair sum is the product of the two weighted row sums,
  hence is bounded by <math|C<rsup|2>>; the direct plaquette contribution
  adds its fixed coefficient. Derivatives of fixed order multiply these
  charges by a fixed polynomial in <math|1+\<ell\><rsub|d>>. The inequality
  <math|<around|(|1+\<ell\>|)><rsup|k>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|k,\<epsilon\>>*e<rsup|\<epsilon\>*\<ell\>>>,
  obtained by maximizing <math|<around|(|1+x|)><rsup|k>*e<rsup|-\<epsilon\>*x>>
  on <math|x\<geqslant\>0>, pays this polynomial from half the width. The
  remaining half pays the support weight. For the hit sum,
  <math|r<rsub|c><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>/12> and
  <math|<around|(|b+1|)>/B\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>/4> leave positive width for
  the rooted footprint sum. These statements hold term by term with absolute
  coefficients, so also for the countable channel sums.

  The low regular estimate in <eqref|eq:reset-channel-costs> follows from the
  two curvature factors in <math|P<rsub|d>,Q<rsub|d>>. For its discrepancy,
  the kept-row bound is <math|<around|\||L<rsub|d>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*t*e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|d>/r<rsub|c>><around|\||a<rsub|d>|\|>>,
  and the singleton-masked substitute has size at most
  <math|C<rsub|<math-up|pair>>*t<rsup|2>/2>. The triangle inequality gives
  the third estimate. The high estimate uses
  <math|t<rsup|4>\<leqslant\>t<rsup|3>> and the cubic and quartic path
  bounds. Finally <eqref|eq:reset-isotropic-coefficient> and
  <math|t\<leqslant\>1> imply

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<alpha\>+\<Delta\>|\|>*t<rsup|3>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>*t<rsup|3>+H,<space|2em>2*t\|\<alpha\>+\<Delta\>\|+<tfrac|1|2>C<rsub|<math-up|pair>>*t<rsup|2><around|\||\<Delta\>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*t*<around|(|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>+H/t<rsup|2>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>C<rsub|<math-up|pair>>*H.
  </equation*>

  Thus the full regular and soft costs are bounded respectively by

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|E<rsub|<math-up|new>>>|<cell|=C<rsub|<math-up|reset>>*H+C<rsub|<math-up|turn>>*<around|{|H*L<rsub|0>+<around|(|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>*t<rsup|3>+H|)>*L<rsub|1>|}>,>>|<row|<cell|S<rsub|<math-up|new>>>|<cell|=S<around|(|H|)>+<around|{|2*t*<around|(|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>+H/t<rsup|2>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>C<rsub|<math-up|pair>>*H|}>*L<rsub|h>.>>>>
  </align*>

  The reorganization requires two numerical purchases for its output
  allowances <math|\<delta\>> and <math|\<gamma\>>. The regular one is
  <math|E<rsub|<math-up|new>>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>>. The forced one asks for
  numbers <math|\<mu\>,\<kappa\>\<geqslant\>0> with

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reset-forced-purchase>S<rsub|<math-up|new>>\<leqslant\>\<mu\>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em><around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*I\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>e<rsup|-\<kappa\>>*<around|(|e<rsup|I>-1|)>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\>,<space|2em>I=e<rsup|\<kappa\>>*w+2*\<mu\>.
  </equation>

  These are the hard/soft absorption inequalities
  <eqref|eq:nonlinear-payment> for the pair <math|<around|(|w,\<mu\>|)>> and
  target <math|\<gamma\>>. Under them the output has regular cost
  <math|\<delta\>> and forced cost <math|\<gamma\>>, with the original
  locality, symmetry and affine-Hessian conditions. These numerical purchases
  must be imposed on this same output budget.

  <subsubsection|The stage constants and the early allocation>

  The regular purchase is obtained by fixing in advance how small the early
  price <math|H> must be. The coefficients
  <math|C<rsub|<math-up|reset>>,C<rsub|<math-up|turn>>,L<rsub|0>,L<rsub|1>>
  depend only on the derivative order, spatial power, support exponent, clip,
  width and path price of the output stage. They do not depend on the radius
  <math|B> or the threshold <math|t>: the cover-number increments <math|81>,
  <math|85>, <math|9> and the polynomial derivative factors above were
  bounded without reference to either. Put

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reset-stage-constants>B<rsup|<math-up|reset>>=C<rsub|<math-up|reset>>+C<rsub|<math-up|turn>>*<around|(|L<rsub|0>+L<rsub|1>|)>,<space|2em>v<rsup|<math-up|reset>>=C<rsub|<math-up|turn>>*L<rsub|1>,<space|2em>c<rsup|<math-up|reset>>=<frac|1|64*<around|(|1+B<rsup|<math-up|reset>>|)>>.
  </equation>

  Collecting the coefficients of <math|H> and of
  <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>*t<rsup|3>> in the display for
  <math|E<rsub|<math-up|new>>> gives the identity

  <\equation*>
    E<rsub|<math-up|new>>=B<rsup|<math-up|reset>>*H+v<rsup|<math-up|reset>><around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>*t<rsup|3>.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|B<rsup|<math-up|reset>>\<geqslant\>0>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reset-credit-bounds>0\<less\>c<rsup|<math-up|reset>>\<leqslant\><frac|1|64>,<space|2em>B<rsup|<math-up|reset>>*c<rsup|<math-up|reset>>\<leqslant\><frac|1|64>.
  </equation>

  Now let <math|\<delta\><rsub|E>\<gtr\>0> be the regular allowance of the
  output level, and let <math|A\<geqslant\>0>. Suppose

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reset-allocation>H=c<rsup|<math-up|reset>>*\<delta\><rsub|E>,<space|2em><around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>A,<space|2em>64*<space|0.17em>v<rsup|<math-up|reset>>*A*<space|0.17em>t<rsup|3>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|E>.
  </equation>

  Then <eqref|eq:reset-credit-bounds> gives

  <\equation*>
    E<rsub|<math-up|new>>\<leqslant\><frac|\<delta\><rsub|E>|64>+<frac|\<delta\><rsub|E>|64>=<frac|\<delta\><rsub|E>|32>.
  </equation*>

  Thus the regular purchase holds with <math|\<delta\>=\<delta\><rsub|E>/32>.
  The first condition in <eqref|eq:reset-allocation> is an allocation: it is
  the fraction of the output allowance which the local return of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-nonlinear-offering> is asked to
  meet, and is the constant called <math|c<rsub|<math-up|st>>> there. It is a
  choice made before the block length, not a consequence of the
  reorganization. The third condition is a stage-entry purchase. With the
  uniform bound <math|A=A<rsub|j>> of the next subsection and
  <math|t=x<rsup|-1/2>*e<rsup|<around|(|log log x|)><rsup|2>>>,
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|E>=x<rsup|-11/10>>, the ratio
  <math|t<rsup|3>/\<delta\><rsub|E>=x<rsup|-2/5>*e<rsup|3<around|(|log log
  x|)><rsup|2>>> tends to zero, so the condition holds beyond an entry
  threshold depending on the stage and on the block length through
  <math|A<rsub|j>>.

  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:nonlinear-payment-selection> proves the
  soft-price estimate <eqref|eq:nonlinear-soft-price>, which bounds
  <math|S<rsub|<math-up|new>>> by a stage constant times
  <math|<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>*t> when <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>A>
  and <math|H\<leqslant\>t<rsup|2>>, and the elementary payment
  <eqref|eq:nonlinear-payment>. It does not contain the regular inequality
  <math|E<rsub|<math-up|new>>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>>; that is the allocation
  just described. The comparison of <math|H,\<delta\>,\<gamma\>> with the
  prices of the selected recipe is proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history>. The instantiation of
  <eqref|eq:reset-forced-purchase> at the particular forecast pair
  <math|<around|(|w,\<gamma\>|)>> of the selected history remains an input
  here: it requires witnesses <math|\<mu\>,\<kappa\>> satisfying all its
  inequalities for that pair. Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history>
  describes this purchase in words but does not exhibit the witnesses. The
  present section is conditional on <eqref|eq:reset-allocation> and
  <eqref|eq:reset-forced-purchase>.

  <subsubsection|Continuity in the parameter>

  Let the parameter <math|y> range over the compact interval <math|T> fixed
  at the start, and write <math|f<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>,k<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>,\<Delta\><around|(|y|)>>
  for the early arrays and the extracted coefficient. The output arrays must
  be continuous in <math|y> in the labelled regular and forced norms. This
  does not follow from continuity of finitely many operations, because the
  forced absorption involves infinitely many labels. We use the two
  comparison properties of the early arrays supplied by
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-nonlinear-offering>, both in the
  one dictionary fixed there:

  <\itemize>
    <item>for every <math|y\<in\>T> and every label, the regular entry
    <math|f<rsub|i><around|(|y<rprime|'>|)>> converges to
    <math|f<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>> in its regular norm, and the forced entry
    <math|k<rsub|i><around|(|y<rprime|'>|)>> converges to
    <math|k<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>> uniformly in the field, as
    <math|y<rprime|'>\<to\>y> in <math|T>;

    <item>every sequence in <math|T> converging to <math|y> has a subsequence
    along which the regular norms of the <math|f<rsub|i>> and the suprema of
    the <math|k<rsub|i>> have summable majorants <math|e<rsub|i>,h<rsub|i>>
    common to the subsequence and its limit.
  </itemize>

  On the fixed finite torus every support has cover number at most the number
  of sites, so the support weights are bounded and plain summability of
  majorants is all that is needed below. The first step is the absorption
  itself.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Continuity of the forced absorption at a fixed
    period><label|lem:reset-absorption-continuity>Fix a finite torus, a
    radius <math|B>, a support exponent <math|b>, and one dictionary of hard
    labels <math|i> and logarithmic labels <math|j> as in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:soft-log-fusion>, with supports,
    inventories and eligibility tests independent of a parameter
    <math|y\<in\>T\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>. Let
    <math|K<rsub|i>*<around|(|y,\<cdot\>|)>> and
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>*<around|(|y,\<cdot\>|)>> be the hard factors and
    logarithms. Assume:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>for every label and every <math|y\<in\>T>,
      <math|sup<rsub|U><around|\||K<rsub|i>*<around|(|y<rprime|'>,U|)>-K<rsub|i>*<around|(|y,U|)>|\|>\<to\>0>
      and <math|sup<rsub|U><around|\||\<ell\><rsub|j>*<around|(|y<rprime|'>,U|)>-\<ell\><rsub|j>*<around|(|y,U|)>|\|>\<to\>0>
      as <math|y<rprime|'>\<to\>y> in <math|T>;

      <item>there are numbers <math|p<rsub|i>,v<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>0>,
      independent of <math|y>, with <math|<around|\||K<rsub|i>*<around|(|y,U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>p<rsub|i>>
      and <math|<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|j>*<around|(|y,U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>v<rsub|j>>
      for all <math|y\<in\>T> and all fields <math|U>, and with
      <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>p<rsub|i>+<big|sum><rsub|j>v<rsub|j>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
    </enumerate>

    Then the absorbed activities <math|<wide|K|^><rsub|C>*<around|(|y,\<cdot\>|)>>
    of <eqref|eq:fusion-new-activity> satisfy

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|C>e<rsup|b*<space|0.17em>c<rsub|B><around|(|Z<rsub|C>|)>>*sup<rsub|U><around*|\||<wide|K|^><rsub|C>*<around|(|y<rprime|'>,U|)>-<wide|K|^><rsub|C>*<around|(|y,U|)>|\|>\<longrightarrow\>0*<space|2em><around|(|y<rprime|'>\<to\>y<text|in
      >T|)>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    At every field, <math|<wide|K|^><rsub|C>*<around|(|y,U|)>> is the product
    of two indicators that do not depend on <math|y> and of the finitely many
    factors <math|r<rsub|v>*<around|(|y,U|)>>, <math|v\<in\>C>, where
    <math|r<rsub|i>=K<rsub|i>> and <math|r<rsub|j>=e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|j>>-1>.
    Put <math|a<rsub|i>=p<rsub|i>> and <math|a<rsub|j>=e<rsup|v<rsub|j>>-1>.
    Each factor is bounded by <math|a<rsub|v>> uniformly in <math|y> and
    <math|U>, because <math|<around|\||e<rsup|x>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<around|\||x|\|>>-1>,
    and by the first hypothesis each factor converges uniformly in <math|U>.
    Writing the difference of two finite products as a telescoping sum shows
    that each term of the series tends to zero. Each term is at most
    <math|2*e<rsup|b<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>>*<big|prod><rsub|v\<in\>C>a<rsub|v>>,
    since a cover number is at most the number
    <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>> of sites. The numbers <math|a<rsub|v>>
    are summable: <math|a<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>v<rsub|j>*e<rsup|v<rsub|j>>> and
    the <math|v<rsub|j>> are bounded. The labels <math|C> are finite sets of
    constituent labels, so

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|C><big|prod><rsub|v\<in\>C>a<rsub|v>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|v><around|(|1+a<rsub|v>|)>-1\<leqslant\>exp
      <around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|v>a<rsub|v>|)>-1\<less\>\<infty\>.
    </equation*>

    Dominated convergence for series gives the claim.
  </proof>

  The bound obtained in this proof depends on the number of sites. It is used
  only for continuity at a fixed period, never as a volume-independent price.
  The volume-independent forced cost of the output is the separate estimate
  <eqref|eq:fusion-site-output>, paid by <eqref|eq:reset-forced-purchase>.

  We apply the lemma as follows. First, <math|\<Delta\>> is continuous on
  <math|T>. The difference of two early arrays in the same dictionary is
  again a family of regular entries, and the projection is linear. Applying
  <eqref|eq:reset-isotropic-coefficient> to the difference gives
  <math|t<rsup|2>*<around|\||\<Delta\><around|(|y<rprime|'>|)>-\<Delta\><around|(|y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>d>
  whenever the regular norms of the differences
  <math|f<rsub|i><around|(|y<rprime|'>|)>-f<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>> have
  majorants whose support-weighted sum over all labels is at most <math|d>.
  Such a <math|d> can be taken arbitrarily small for <math|y<rprime|'>> near
  <math|y>: otherwise there would be a sequence <math|y<rsub|n>\<to\>y> along
  which this fails, and on its dominated subsequence the entrywise
  convergence and the common summable majorant <math|2*e<rsub|i>> would give
  a contradiction by dominated convergence.

  Second, every output entry depends continuously on <math|y>. The entries
  <math|r<rsub|i>> and <math|d<rsub|i>> are linear in <math|f<rsub|i>>, with
  norm at most <math|C<rsub|<math-up|sub>>> times that of <math|f<rsub|i>>,
  so they inherit the entrywise convergence. The path corrections
  <math|F<rsub|d>> and <math|J<rsub|d>> are fixed functions multiplied by
  <math|\<Delta\>> or by <math|\<alpha\>+\<Delta\>>, so they are continuous
  because <math|\<Delta\>> is.

  Third, let <math|y<rsub|n>\<to\>y> and pass to the subsequence with common
  early majorants <math|e<rsub|i>,h<rsub|i>>. Along it
  <math|<around|\||\<Delta\><around|(|y<rsub|n>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Delta\><around|(|y|)>|\|>+1>
  eventually. The output regular and logarithmic entries then have the common
  summable majorants <math|C<rsub|<math-up|sub>>*e<rsub|i>> for the
  subtracted entries and the channel bounds of <eqref|eq:reset-channel-costs>
  with <math|<around|\||\<Delta\>|\|>> replaced by
  <math|<around|\||\<Delta\><around|(|y|)>|\|>+1> for the path entries; the
  hard entries keep the majorants <math|h<rsub|i>>. Summability of the
  channel bounds is the summability established after
  <eqref|eq:reset-channel-costs>, which did not use the size of
  <math|\<Delta\>>.

  Fourth, on this subsequence together with its limit point the hypotheses of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:reset-absorption-continuity> hold: the first by
  the second step, the second by the third step. The regular output entries
  are not changed by the absorption, and their weighted difference sums tend
  to zero by the same dominated convergence. Hence the full output arrays
  converge along the subsequence. Since every sequence converging to <math|y>
  has such a subsequence, the output arrays are continuous at <math|y>: a
  sequence violating continuity would have a dominated subsequence still
  violating it.

  <subsubsection|The two healthy tests and the actual reset>

  Suppose the early cost obeys <math|H\<leqslant\>F/4>, where <math|F> is the
  scalar fund used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:coupling-reset-selection>.
  On a globally healthy field all masks are one and the early gas is one. The
  early density is consequently

  <\equation*>
    H<rsub|x><around|(|U|)>=exp <around|{|V*\<tau\>-\<beta\>*\<cal-K\><around|(|U|)>-\<alpha\>*\<cal-K\><rsub|2><around|(|U|)>-\<cal-L\><around|(|U|)>-E<around|(|U|)>|}>,<space|2em><around|\||E<around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>V*H.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|2>> is the quadratic part and <math|E> the early
  regular sum. At <math|U=1>, the three path terms vanish, so positivity is
  explicit and

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||V<rsup|-1>*log H<rsub|x><around|(|1|)>-\<tau\>|\|>\<leqslant\>H,<space|2em><around|\||E<around|(|U|)>-E<around|(|1|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*V*H\<leqslant\>V*F/2.
  </equation*>

  The difference of the full kinetic action and its quadratic part has size
  at most <math|2*V*C*t<rsup|3>>: each cubic and quartic atom is bounded by
  its row charge times <math|t<rsup|3>> or <math|t<rsup|4>>, respectively,
  and <math|t<rsup|4>\<leqslant\>t<rsup|3>>. The selected path construction
  bounds <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|j>>, uniformly in
  cutoff and period, where <math|A<rsub|j>> is its drift bound plus
  <math|<around|\||s<rsub|G>*log L<rsub|j>|\|>> for the fixed stage. Purchase
  <math|2*A<rsub|j>*C*t<rsup|3>\<leqslant\>F/2>. This is possible at each
  fixed stage since <math|t=x<rsup|-1/2>*e<rsup|<around|(|log log
  x|)><rsup|2>>>, while <math|F> is a positive stage constant times
  <math|x<rsup|-11/10>>; their ratio tends to zero. Adding the two errors
  proves the precise surplus bound <math|V*F> at the nontrivial healthy test.
  The vacuum error is bounded by <math|F/4>.

  The output arrays in <eqref|eq:reset-complete-density> depend on the
  computed <math|\<Delta\>>, and are fixed while the trial dial varies. Their
  dial-motion cost is therefore zero. Their continuity in the original
  parameter was proved in the preceding subsection. The scalar selection
  therefore applies to these arrays. Its residual is
  <math|<around|(|d-\<Delta\>|)>*\<cal-K\><around|(|U<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>>, so
  the selected dial is exactly <math|\<Delta\>>. Its vacuum value is exactly
  <math|\<tau\>-c<rsub|0>/V> by <eqref|eq:reset-complete-density> at the
  identity.

  Finally retain this constructed family as the witness for the history step.
  The recipe conditions refer to its actual supports, normalized density
  identity, regular and forced bounds, and bound
  <math|t<rsup|2><around|\||\<Delta\>|\|>\<leqslant\>H>. They are supplied by
  the construction above and the separately purchased recipe permissions.
  Equality of densities by itself would not supply those permissions. Once
  the common funding provides them, this is the continuous one-step map used
  in the surviving-interval induction.

  <subsection|Selecting the coupling and the vacuum
  constant><label|sec:coupling-reset-selection>

  An exact local return contains a small correction to the kinetic
  coefficient. We must show that this correction lies in the purchased
  interval and depends continuously on the incoming representation. Two
  healthy configurations suffice to select the two scalar parameters. The
  exact density identity at other configurations comes from the local return,
  and must be retained separately.

  <subsubsection|A separating configuration in every volume>

  Assume the gauge Lie algebra is nonzero, as it is for our simple gauge
  group. Let <math|V<rsub|\<Lambda\>>=<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>> be the
  number of sites in an even-sided torus. Let <math|a<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> be the
  direct plaquette coefficient of the selected clipped kinetic action
  <math|\<cal-K\>>, and let <math|C\<geqslant\>0> bound its cubic and quartic
  arrays. Fix a healthy-field threshold <math|0\<less\>t\<leqslant\>1>. There
  is a number <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>, depending on the stage bounds but not
  the torus or <math|t>, and an all-healthy configuration
  <math|U<rsub|\<ast\>>> such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reset-separator>\<cal-K\><around|(|U<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>\<geqslant\>V<rsub|\<Lambda\>>*\<eta\>*t<rsup|2>.
  </equation>

  Here and below the trivial configuration is denoted by <math|1>.

  To construct it, first choose <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0> such that every
  chord value in <math|<around|[|0,\<epsilon\>|]>> occurs in <math|G>.
  Indeed, take a nonzero Lie-algebra vector <math|c>. The derivative at zero
  of the curvature of <math|exp <around|(|s*c|)>> is <math|c>, so its chord
  cannot vanish identically. Continuity and the intermediate value theorem
  along that path give the assertion. Set

  <\equation*>
    r=min <around|{|\<epsilon\>,1/4,a<rsub|0>/<around|[|512*<around|(|1+C|)>|]>|}>\<gtr\>0,<space|2em>h=r*t,
  </equation*>

  and choose <math|u\<in\>G> of chord <math|h>. Set the links in direction
  <math|0> equal to <math|u> on even coordinate-<math|1> slices and to
  <math|1> on odd slices; set all other links equal to <math|1>. Even side
  length makes this periodic. Every <math|<around|(|0,1|)>> plaquette has
  holonomy <math|u> or <math|u<rsup|-1>> and hence chord <math|h>. Every
  plaquette has chord at most <math|4*h\<leqslant\>t>, by the four-link
  triangle inequality. Thus the configuration is healthy.

  For each signed lattice symmetry the sum of the direct plaquette terms is
  at least <math|V<rsub|\<Lambda\>>*a<rsub|0>*h<rsup|2>/2>: the symmetry
  permutes plaquettes, and the indicated plane already contributes that
  amount. The other quadratic terms are nonnegative on this healthy
  configuration. The absolute cubic-plus-quartic contribution per site is at
  most

  <\equation*>
    C*<around|(|4*h|)><rsup|3>+C*<around|(|4*h|)><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>128*C*h<rsup|3>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|0>*h<rsup|2>/4.
  </equation*>

  The first inequality uses <math|4*h\<leqslant\>1>, and the second uses
  <math|512*C*h\<leqslant\>a<rsub|0>>. Averaging the signed actions preserves
  the lower bound <math|V<rsub|\<Lambda\>>*a<rsub|0>*h<rsup|2>/4>. This
  proves <eqref|eq:reset-separator> with <math|\<eta\>=a<rsub|0>*r<rsup|2>/4>.

  <subsubsection|The scalar equation on a purchased interval>

  Fix the coarse path, clip and budget. Let <math|x> range in an interval
  <math|I>, and let <math|H<rsub|x><around|(|U|)>> be the actual unnormalized
  transferred density. Assume <math|H<rsub|x><around|(|1|)>,H<rsub|x><around|(|U<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>\<gtr\>0>.
  Write

  <\equation*>
    c<around|(|x|)>=log H<rsub|x><around|(|1|)>-log
    H<rsub|x><around|(|U<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>,<space|2em>b<around|(|x|)>=\<beta\><rsub|f><around|(|x|)>+\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>.
  </equation*>

  For a trial correction <math|d\<in\><around|[|-w,w|]>>,
  <math|0\<less\>w\<leqslant\>1>, write its density as

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reset-trial-density>e<rsup|V<rsub|\<Lambda\>>*v>*e<rsup|-<around|(|b<around|(|x|)>+d|)>*\<cal-K\><around|(|U|)>-\<cal-L\><around|(|U|)>-A<rsub|x,d><around|(|U|)>>*\<cal-G\><rsub|x,d><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<cal-L\>> is the fixed loop action, <math|A<rsub|x,d>> the
  regular activity sum, and <math|\<cal-G\><rsub|x,d>> the forced gas. The
  regular entries vanish at <math|1>, and
  <math|\<cal-K\><around|(|1|)>=\<cal-L\><around|(|1|)>=0> because each path
  atom vanishes at the trivial configuration. The forced gas equals <math|1>
  on healthy configurations. In particular the vacuum parameter is forced to
  be

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reset-vacuum>v<around|(|x|)>=V<rsub|\<Lambda\>><rsup|-1>*log
    H<rsub|x><around|(|1|)>.
  </equation>

  Put <math|k=\<cal-K\><around|(|U<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>\<gtr\>0> and define the
  residual

  <\equation*>
    f*<around|(|x,d|)>=<around|(|b<around|(|x|)>+d|)>*k+\<cal-L\><around|(|U<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>+A<rsub|x,d><around|(|U<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>-c<around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  The trial matches <math|H<rsub|x>> at <math|1> and <math|U<rsub|\<ast\>>>
  exactly when <math|v> has value <eqref|eq:reset-vacuum> and
  <math|f*<around|(|x,d|)>=0>.

  Here are the estimates required for this selection. Let
  <math|\<delta\>\<geqslant\>0> be the regular allowance per site and
  <math|F\<geqslant\>0> the reset allowance per site. Assume

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\||b<around|(|x|)>*k+\<cal-L\><around|(|U<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>-c<around|(|x|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>V<rsub|\<Lambda\>>*F,<eq-number><label|eq:reset-surplus>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||A<rsub|x,0><around|(|U<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>V<rsub|\<Lambda\>>*\<delta\>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||A<rsub|x,d<rprime|'>><around|(|U<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>-A<rsub|x,d><around|(|U<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>V<rsub|\<Lambda\>>*F*<around|\||d<rprime|'>-d|\|>,>>|<row|<cell|2*<around|(|F+\<delta\>|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<eta\>*t<rsup|2>*w.<eq-number><label|eq:reset-purchase>>>>>
  </align>

  The arrays have fixed labels, are continuous in their regular and forced
  norms along parameter curves, and have locally summable comparison
  majorants. These conditions imply continuity of their evaluated sums along
  the parameter and dial: truncate a summable majorant, use continuity for
  the finite part, and bound the two tails by that majorant. The same
  argument works if domination is supplied on a further subsequence of every
  convergent sequence. Otherwise a sequence violating continuity would have a
  further dominated subsequence which still violates it. We also assume
  continuity of the two positive values of <math|H<rsub|x>> and of
  <math|b<around|(|x|)>>.

  From <eqref|eq:reset-separator> and <eqref|eq:reset-purchase>,

  <\equation*>
    V<rsub|\<Lambda\>>*<around|(|F+\<delta\>|)>\<leqslant\>k*w/2,<space|2em>V<rsub|\<Lambda\>>*F\<leqslant\>k/2.
  </equation*>

  Consequently, for <math|d\<leqslant\>d<rprime|'>>,

  <\equation*>
    f*<around|(|x,d<rprime|'>|)>-f*<around|(|x,d|)>\<geqslant\><tfrac|1|2>k*<around|(|d<rprime|'>-d|)>,<space|2em><around|\||f*<around|(|x,0|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>k*w/2.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|f*<around|(|x,-w|)>\<leqslant\>0\<leqslant\>f*<around|(|x,w|)>>.
  Continuity in <math|d> gives a zero <math|d<around|(|x|)>\<in\><around|[|-w,w|]>>,
  and the strict slope bound makes it unique in that interval. It also gives
  the useful inverse estimate

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reset-inverse><around|\||d<around|(|x|)>-d<around|(|y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|2|k>*<around|\||f*<around|(|x,d<around|(|y|)>|)>-f*<around|(|y,d<around|(|y|)>|)>|\|>.
  </equation>

  For fixed <math|y>, the right side tends to zero as <math|x\<to\>y>; this
  proves continuity of <math|d> including at endpoints. Substitution into the
  fixed labelled arrays preserves their continuity and their budgets.
  Together with <eqref|eq:reset-vacuum> this produces a continuous family of
  representations with the purchased correction bound
  <math|<around|\||d<around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>w>.

  <subsubsection|Identification with the physical local correction>

  In the actual local construction the activity array is independent of the
  trial dial. The local projection and compulsory-packet absorption first
  give a number <math|\<Delta\><around|(|x|)>> and an array <math|S<rsub|x>>
  for which <eqref|eq:reset-trial-density>, at
  <math|d=\<Delta\><around|(|x|)>> and

  <\equation*>
    v<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>=\<tau\><rsub|x>-V<rsub|\<Lambda\>><rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|i>v<rsub|x><rsup|<math-up|reg>>*<around|(|i,1|)>,
  </equation*>

  is exactly <math|H<rsub|x><around|(|U|)>> for every <math|U>. This is the
  exact local reorganization; its scalar sign is fixed by evaluating the
  identity at <math|U=1>. Its regular arrays vanish at the flat configuration
  after subtraction. With <math|A<rsub|x,d>> and <math|\<cal-G\><rsub|x,d>>
  independent of <math|d>, taking the two healthy evaluations of that exact
  identity gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reset-physical-residual>f*<around|(|x,d|)>=<around|(|d-\<Delta\><around|(|x|)>|)>*k.
  </equation>

  Therefore the selected zero is <math|d<around|(|x|)>=\<Delta\><around|(|x|)>>
  and <math|v<around|(|x|)>=v<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>>. This identifies the
  selected continuous family with the actual reorganized local return, rather
  than merely producing a family with the same two scalar tests.

  One may also express the full-field justification as a quantitative error
  bound. For fixed <math|x,U>, put <math|q=\<cal-K\><around|(|U|)>> and
  <math|r<rsub|U>=H<rsub|x><around|(|U|)>/H<rsub|x><around|(|1|)>>. The trial
  normalized shape equals <math|e<rsup|<around|(|\<Delta\>-d|)>*q>*r<rsub|U>>.
  Since <math|<around|\||d|\|>\<leqslant\>1>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||e<rsup|<around|(|\<Delta\>-d|)>*q>*r<rsub|U>-r<rsub|U>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<around|(|1+<around|\||\<Delta\>|\|>|)><around|\||q|\|>><around|\||q|\|><space|0.17em><around|\||r<rsub|U>|\|>*<space|0.17em><around|\||\<Delta\>-d|\|>=<frac|e<rsup|<around|(|1+<around|\||\<Delta\>|\|>|)><around|\||q|\|>><around|\||q|\|><space|0.17em><around|\||r<rsub|U>|\|>|k>*<around|\||f*<around|(|x,d|)>|\|>.
  </equation*>

  This follows from <math|<around|\||e<rsup|z>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|M><around|\||z|\|>>
  for real <math|z\<leqslant\>M>, <math|M\<geqslant\>0>. It applies also when
  the forced gas is nontrivial or signed. At the selected zero it proves
  exact equality at every configuration; the constant here may depend on
  <math|x,U> and the finite volume.

  The remaining compatibility check uses the actual local construction of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-reset-reorganization>. Write
  <math|H<rsup|<math-up|early>><around|(|x|)>> for its early regular price
  (denoted <math|H> there; here <math|H<rsub|x>> already denotes the
  density), and <math|\<delta\><around|(|x|)>,\<gamma\><around|(|x|)>> for
  the regular and forced allowances of its output. Retain the dictionary and
  coefficients of that construction as they are before the final forced
  absorption: the regular entries, the hard entries and the logarithmic
  corrections. That construction supplies three facts about this one batch:
  the subtraction estimate <math|t<rsup|2><around|\||\<Delta\><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|<math-up|early>><around|(|x|)>>
  of <eqref|eq:reset-isotropic-coefficient>; the pre-absorption certificate
  at the allowances <math|\<delta\><around|(|x|)>,\<gamma\><around|(|x|)>>,
  consisting of the regular bound at <math|\<delta\><around|(|x|)>>, the hard
  and logarithmic bounds and a pair <math|\<mu\>,\<kappa\>> satisfying
  <eqref|eq:reset-forced-purchase> for the target
  <math|\<gamma\><around|(|x|)>>; and the normalized density identity, namely
  that the density of the batch with vacuum constant zero, multiplied by the
  exponential of its logarithmic corrections, equals
  <math|H<rsub|x><around|(|U|)>/H<rsub|x><around|(|1|)>> at every field. The
  history constraints are those of the selected recipe. A step of that recipe
  is a batch of this kind together with a number <math|\<Delta\>>, satisfying
  these three conditions with <math|H<rsup|<math-up|early>>,\<delta\>,\<gamma\>>
  replaced by the recipe's own prices, called its portion and its regular and
  forced charges; its permission condition is unrestricted for the default
  recipe. The certificate is monotone in its two allowances. Hence the batch
  retained above, for an input of the history whose coupling lies in its
  band, is a step of the recipe as soon as

  <\equation*>
    H<rsup|<math-up|early>><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|portion><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>\<delta\><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|charge><rsub|E><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>\<gamma\><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|charge><rsub|K><around|(|x|)>;
  </equation*>

  the comparisons enlarge the allowances of the same pre-absorption
  certificate, and no function, support or label is changed. The represented
  output is then obtained from this batch by the fixed packing and
  absorption. These three comparisons are not proved here. They are
  <eqref|eq:common-recipe-prices> in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history>,
  for the selected prices. Granting them, the same local construction is the
  witness for the retained-history and next-step constraints: appending its
  batch to the incoming finite history gives both. Equality of densities
  alone would not imply those constraints for arbitrary arrays or
  dictionaries. The forced estimate is retained separately, including its
  exponential monitor bound, which is <eqref|eq:common-final-forcing>.

  This proves the scalar selection and its full-field identification relative
  to the physical reorganization and the displayed purchases. In that
  application the dial-motion estimate is zero. The surplus estimate comes
  from the exact all-healthy local expression and the regular budget,
  together with the purchased cubic/quartic discrepancy between the full
  kinetic action and its quadratic part. The next construction must verify
  those purchases on the selected history; the scalar argument itself does
  not choose a new path or new prices.

  <subsection|Logarithmic asymptotics of the running inverse
  coefficient><label|sec:running-coefficient>

  Let <math|G\<subset\>U<around|(|r<rsub|G>|)>> be a closed matrix subgroup,
  in the indicated unitary representation, with nonabelian simple real Lie
  algebra <math|\<frak-g\>>, where <math|r<rsub|G>\<geqslant\>1>. Explicitly,

  <\equation*>
    \<frak-g\>=<around|{|X\<in\>M<rsub|r<rsub|G>><around|(|\<bbb-C\>|)>:exp
    <around|(|t*X|)>\<in\>G<text| for every >t\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|U<around|(|r<rsub|G>|)>> is closed in
  <math|<math-up|GL><around|(|r<rsub|G>,\<bbb-C\>|)>>, so is <math|G>. Thus
  Hall's matrix Lie group results apply: this set is a finite-dimensional
  real vector space closed under <math|<around|[|X,Y|]>=X*Y-Y*X>
  <cite-detail|hall2000|Definition<nbsp>3.13 and Theorem<nbsp>3.16>. Since
  <math|exp <around|(|t*X|)>> is unitary for <math|X\<in\>\<frak-g\>> and
  real <math|t>, differentiating <math|exp <around|(|t*X|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*exp
  <around|(|t*X|)>=I> at <math|t=0> gives <math|X<rsup|\<ast\>>=-X>. Thus the
  symmetric real bilinear form <math|<around|\<langle\>|X,Y|\<rangle\>>=-r<rsub|G><rsup|-1><math-up|Retr><around|(|X*Y|)>>
  is positive definite: for <math|X\<ne\>0>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<langle\>|X,X|\<rangle\>>=r<rsub|G><rsup|-1><math-up|tr><around|(|X<rsup|\<ast\>>*X|)>=r<rsub|G><rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|a,b><around|\||X<rsub|a*b>|\|><rsup|2>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation*>

  Nonabelianness implies <math|\<frak-g\>\<ne\><around|{|0|}>>, so
  <math|dim<rsub|\<bbb-R\>> \<frak-g\>\<geqslant\>1>. Write

  <\equation*>
    b<rsub|0><around|(|G|)>=<frac|11*<space|0.17em>C<rsub|\<frak-g\>>|48*\<pi\><rsup|2>>,<space|2em>C<rsub|\<frak-g\>>=-<frac|1|dim<rsub|\<bbb-R\>>
    \<frak-g\>>*<big|sum><rsub|j><math-up|Tr><rsub|\<frak-g\>><around*|(|<around|(|<math-up|ad><space|0.17em>e<rsub|j>|)><rsup|2>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|<around|(|e<rsub|j>|)>> is an orthonormal basis for this inner
  product and <math|<math-up|ad><space|0.17em>X<around|(|Y|)>=<around|[|X,Y|]>=X*Y-Y*X>.
  Here <math|<math-up|Tr><rsub|\<frak-g\>>> is the real trace of an
  endomorphism of <math|\<frak-g\>>. This constant is independent of the
  orthonormal basis: if <math|f<rsub|i>=<big|sum><rsub|j>q<rsub|i*j>*e<rsub|j>>
  with <math|q> orthogonal, then

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|i><around|(|<math-up|ad><space|0.17em>f<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>=<big|sum><rsub|j,k><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i>q<rsub|i*j>*q<rsub|i*k>|)><math-up|ad><space|0.17em>e<rsub|j><math-up|ad><space|0.17em>e<rsub|k>=<big|sum><rsub|j><around|(|<math-up|ad><space|0.17em>e<rsub|j>|)><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  Taking traces proves the assertion. Invariance of the matrix trace gives
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|<around|[|X,Y|]>,Z|\<rangle\>>=-<around|\<langle\>|Y,<around|[|X,Z|]>|\<rangle\>>>,
  so each <math|<math-up|ad><space|0.17em>e<rsub|j>> is skew-adjoint.
  Consequently <math|-<math-up|Tr><rsub|\<frak-g\>><around|(|<around|(|<math-up|ad><space|0.17em>e<rsub|j>|)><rsup|2>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<ell\>><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|[|e<rsub|j>,e<rsub|\<ell\>>|]>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>.
  If every basis commutator vanished, bilinearity would make all commutators
  vanish. Noncommutativity therefore makes at least one term in the resulting
  double sum positive. In particular, <math|b<rsub|0><around|(|G|)>\<gtr\>0>.

  <paragraph|Input from the lattice construction.> Fix a construction family
  <math|\<cal-C\>> satisfying the construction conditions. For
  <math|N,p,m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> with <math|m\<leqslant\>N>, let
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>> be its action coefficient at stage <math|m> of
  the trajectory started at lattice level <math|N> and period index <math|p>.
  The spacing at stage <math|m> is <math|a<rsub|m>=<around|(|<big|prod><rsub|j\<less\>m>B<rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1>>,
  where each block factor <math|B<rsub|j>> is an integer at least <math|3>.
  Thus <math|s<rsub|m>=log <around|(|a<rsub|m><rsup|-1>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>m>log
  B<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>m*log 3>, so <math|s<rsub|m>\<to\>+\<infty\>>. The
  running estimate for the family provides a fixed reference intercept
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>> and, for every <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>, a
  threshold <math|m<rsub|0><around|(|\<eta\>|)>> such that

  <\equation>
    <around*|\||\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>-<around*|(|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+2*b<rsub|0><around|(|G|)>*s<rsub|m>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>*s<rsub|m>*<space|2em><around*|(|m\<geqslant\>m<rsub|0><around|(|\<eta\>|)>,N\<geqslant\>m,p\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|)>.<label|eq:running>
  </equation>

  The threshold is uniform over the starting level and period index. We use
  the running estimate as an input to the comparison below.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Uniform extraction><label|prop:uniform-extraction>Let
    <math|s<rsub|m>\<to\>+\<infty\>>, and let <math|A,c\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> be
    fixed. Suppose the real coefficients <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>> satisfy,
    for every <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>, an estimate
    <math|<around|\||\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>-<around|(|A+c*s<rsub|m>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>*s<rsub|m>>
    for all sufficiently large <math|m>, uniformly over <math|N\<geqslant\>m>
    and <math|p\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>. Then for every
    <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, for all sufficiently large <math|m> one
    has <math|s<rsub|m>\<gtr\>0> and

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<frac|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>|s<rsub|m>>-c|\|>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>*<space|2em><around|(|N\<geqslant\>m,p\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|)>.
    </equation*>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Fix <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>. Use the assumed estimate with
    <math|\<eta\>=\<varepsilon\>/2>, and enlarge its threshold so that
    <math|s<rsub|m>\<gtr\>2*<around|(|<around|\||A|\|>+1|)>/\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>.
    Then, for every <math|N\<geqslant\>m> and <math|p>,

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<frac|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>|s<rsub|m>>-c|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|<around|\||\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>-<around|(|A+c*s<rsub|m>|)>|\|>|s<rsub|m>>+<frac|<around|\||A|\|>|s<rsub|m>>\<leqslant\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>+<frac|<around|\||A|\|>|s<rsub|m>>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>.
    </equation*>

    Both thresholds are independent of <math|N,p>. No positivity assumption
    on <math|c> is used in this proposition.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Logarithmic asymptotic><label|prop:running-asymptotic>Let
    <math|N,k:\<bbb-N\>\<to\>\<bbb-N\>> be arbitrary sequences with
    <math|i\<leqslant\>N<around|(|i|)>> for all sufficiently large <math|i>.
    For <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>\<gtr\>0> set
    <math|g<rsub|N,p,m>=<around|(|<sqrt|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>>|)><rsup|-1>>.
    Then <math|\<beta\><rsub|N<around|(|i|)>,k<around|(|i|)>,i>\<gtr\>0> for
    all sufficiently large <math|i>, and

    <\equation*>
      lim<rsub|i\<to\>\<infty\>> g<rsub|N<around|(|i|)>,k<around|(|i|)>,i>*<sqrt|log
      <around|(|a<rsub|i><rsup|-1>|)>>=<frac|1|<sqrt|2*b<rsub|0><around|(|G|)>>>.
    </equation*>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Apply Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:uniform-extraction> to
    <eqref|eq:running>, with <math|A=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>> and
    <math|c=2*b<rsub|0><around|(|G|)>\<gtr\>0>. For each
    <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, enlarge the resulting threshold until
    <math|i\<leqslant\>N<around|(|i|)>> holds. Substituting <math|m=i>,
    <math|N=N<around|(|i|)>> and <math|p=k<around|(|i|)>> gives

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<frac|\<beta\><rsub|N<around|(|i|)>,k<around|(|i|)>,i>|s<rsub|i>>-c|\|>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
    </equation*>

    for every subsequent <math|i>. Thus <math|x<rsub|i>=\<beta\><rsub|N<around|(|i|)>,k<around|(|i|)>,i>/s<rsub|i>\<to\>c>
    on the tail where <math|s<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0>. Taking
    <math|\<varepsilon\>=c/2> shows <math|x<rsub|i>\<gtr\>c/2\<gtr\>0>
    eventually; hence <math|\<beta\><rsub|N<around|(|i|)>,k<around|(|i|)>,i>=s<rsub|i>*x<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0>
    there. No condition on the sequence <math|k> was needed, because the
    original threshold was uniform in <math|p>.

    On this positive tail the square-root limit rule
    <cite-detail|lebl|Proposition<nbsp>2.2.6> gives
    <math|<sqrt|x<rsub|i>>\<to\><sqrt|c>>. All denominator terms and their
    limit are nonzero, so the quotient rule
    <cite-detail|lebl|Proposition<nbsp>2.2.5(iv)>, with constant numerator
    <math|1>, gives <math|1/<sqrt|x<rsub|i>>\<to\>1/<sqrt|c>>. Finally,

    <\equation*>
      <frac|1|<sqrt|x<rsub|i>>>=<frac|<sqrt|s<rsub|i>>|<sqrt|\<beta\><rsub|N<around|(|i|)>,k<around|(|i|)>,i>>>=g<rsub|N<around|(|i|)>,k<around|(|i|)>,i>*<sqrt|log
      <around|(|a<rsub|i><rsup|-1>|)>>.
    </equation*>

    The first identity follows by squaring the positive quantities
    <math|<sqrt|x<rsub|i>>> and <math|<sqrt|\<beta\><rsub|N<around|(|i|)>,k<around|(|i|)>,i>>/<sqrt|s<rsub|i>>>
    and using uniqueness of the nonnegative square root. All expressions are
    interpreted on the positive tail and may be assigned arbitrary values at
    earlier indices: changing finitely many terms preserves convergence by
    enlarging each tolerance's threshold beyond those indices. This proves
    the stated limit.
  </proof>

  At <math|N<around|(|i|)>=i> the coefficient is the bare lattice action
  coefficient. For a general admissible <math|N<around|(|i|)>>, the
  comparison concerns the coefficient read from the trajectory at spacing
  <math|a<rsub|i>>. Relating this inverse-square-root proxy to a coupling
  defined through a physical observable would require a separate matching
  theorem.

  <subsection|Shooting through the coupling
  bands><label|sec:surviving-interval-shooting>

  A nonlinear step is available when its input coupling belongs to the
  prescribed band. Its output need not belong to the next band for every
  input parameter. We construct a surviving interval at each level; this
  preserves the activity continuity needed for the subsequent step.

  Fix the scale sequence and all the preceding nonlinear choices. Let
  <math|s=s<rsub|G>> be the slope constant of the gauge group,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:shoot-slope>s<rsub|G>=\<lambda\><rsub|G>=<frac|11*C<rsub|G>|24*\<pi\><rsup|2>>=2*b<rsub|0><around|(|G|)>\<gtr\>0,
  </equation>

  where <math|\<lambda\><rsub|G>> and <math|C<rsub|G>> are as in
  <eqref|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT2>\U<eqref|eq:annulus-two-wave-estimates-display-AT4>
  of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-annulus-two-wave>. Since
  <math|C<rsub|G>> is defined there with the colour inner product
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|u,v|\<rangle\>>=-r<rsub|G><rsup|-1><math-up|Retr><around|(|u*v|)>>
  and an orthonormal colour basis, it is the constant
  <math|C<rsub|\<frak-g\>>> of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:running-coefficient>;
  hence <math|\<lambda\><rsub|G>=2*b<rsub|0><around|(|G|)>>, and
  <math|b<rsub|0><around|(|G|)>\<gtr\>0> was proved there. Let
  <math|L<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>2> be the integer block sides, and set

  <\equation*>
    S<rsub|0>=0,<space|2em>S<rsub|i+1>=S<rsub|i>+log
    L<rsub|i>,<space|2em><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+s*S<rsub|i>,<space|2em><wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation*>

  The permitted band is <math|<around|[|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>/2,2*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>|]>>.
  Suppose the purchased initial family exists for every cutoff and period,
  and that the continuous return of the preceding section applies on any
  compact interval whose inputs satisfy the history conditions and belong to
  the fine band. Preserve its exact density identity and its retained
  next-step conditions. The adjacent coupling bound has the form

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:shoot-adjacent-drift><around|\||\<beta\><rsub|i+1>-\<beta\><rsub|i>-s*log
    L<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|i+1>,<space|2em>\<varepsilon\><rsub|i+1>\<leqslant\><tfrac|s|4>log
    L<rsub|i>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i+1>\<geqslant\>0> is the drift allowance of
  the stage which the fixed schedule assigns to level <math|i+1>; it is a
  stage constant, chosen before the block lengths. The first inequality is a
  bound on the actual returned representations: it combines the stage bound
  for the scalar shift <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,i>> with the purchased reset
  width of the preceding section. The second inequality is a property of the
  schedule, whose block sides are chosen after the stage drift allowances.

  The slope in <eqref|eq:shoot-adjacent-drift> is not a free parameter: it is
  fixed by the one-loop constant of the path construction. Indeed, at the
  step from level <math|i+1> to level <math|i> the reset of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:coupling-reset-selection> returns the coarse
  coupling <math|\<beta\><rsub|i>=\<beta\><rsub|i+1>+\<alpha\><rsub|N,i>+d<rsub|i>>,
  where <math|\<beta\><rsub|i+1>> is the input coupling (the
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|f>> of that section, so that
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|i>=b+d<rsub|i>> in its notation),
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,i>> is the scalar correction of the selected path
  step and <math|d<rsub|i>> is the selected dial, with
  <math|<around|\||d<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>w\<leqslant\>1>. This bound holds
  for every input on the shooting interval: the scalar
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,i>> is fixed with the path and the reset bound holds
  for every parameter value. The path step satisfies
  <math|<around|\||\<alpha\><rsub|N,i>+\<lambda\><rsub|G>*log
  L<rsub|i>|\|>+1\<leqslant\>D<rsub|i+1>>
  (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:background-path-convergence>), where
  <math|D<rsub|i+1>> is the drift allowance of the stage assigned to level
  <math|i+1>. Hence

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<beta\><rsub|i+1>-\<beta\><rsub|i>-\<lambda\><rsub|G>*log
    L<rsub|i>|\|>=<around|\||\<alpha\><rsub|N,i>+\<lambda\><rsub|G>*log
    L<rsub|i>+d<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|D<rsub|i+1>-1|)>+1=D<rsub|i+1>.
  </equation*>

  This is the first inequality in <eqref|eq:shoot-adjacent-drift> with
  <math|s=\<lambda\><rsub|G>> and <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i+1>=D<rsub|i+1>>;
  these allowances satisfy <eqref|eq:shoot-drift-ratio>. If another slope
  <math|s<rprime|'>> satisfied the same estimate with allowances
  <math|\<varepsilon\><rprime|'><rsub|i+1>> obeying that ratio bound, then
  <math|<around|\||s<rprime|'>-\<lambda\><rsub|G>|\|>*log
  L<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rprime|'><rsub|i+1>+D<rsub|i+1>>.
  Dividing by <math|log L<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> gives
  <math|<around|\||s<rprime|'>-\<lambda\><rsub|G>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*J<rsub|i+1><rsup|-2>\<to\>0>,
  hence <math|s<rprime|'>=\<lambda\><rsub|G>>.

  <subsubsection|Restricting a curve without crossing a barrier>

  We first record the elementary interval argument. Let <math|f> be
  continuous on <math|<around|[|x,y|]>>, and suppose
  <math|f<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>d\<leqslant\>u\<leqslant\>f<around|(|y|)>>.
  There are <math|z\<leqslant\>w> in <math|<around|[|x,y|]>> such that

  <\equation*>
    f<around|(|z|)>=d,<space|1em>f<around|(|w|)>=u,<space|1em>f<around|(|<around|[|z,w|]>|)>\<subset\><around|[|d,u|]>.
  </equation*>

  The case <math|d=u> follows by taking one point of the nonempty level set.
  Otherwise let <math|w> be the first point of <math|<around|[|x,y|]>> at
  which <math|f=u>. The level set is nonempty by the intermediate value
  theorem, and compactness makes its minimum belong to it. Let <math|z> be
  the last point of <math|<around|[|x,w|]>> at which <math|f=d>; this level
  set is likewise nonempty and compact. If <math|f<around|(|c|)>\<less\>d> at
  some <math|c\<in\><around|[|z,w|]>>, the intermediate value theorem between
  <math|c> and <math|w> gives a later point with value <math|d>, contrary to
  the choice of <math|z>. If <math|f<around|(|c|)>\<gtr\>u>, the same theorem
  between <math|x> and <math|c> gives an earlier point with value <math|u>,
  contrary to the choice of <math|w>. This proves the claim without any
  monotonicity assumption on <math|f>.

  Define inner barriers

  <\equation*>
    \<ell\><rsub|i>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+<tfrac|s|2>S<rsub|i>,<space|2em>u<rsub|i>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+2*s*S<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  Both lie in the permitted band, since <math|S<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>. If an
  input at level <math|i+1> has coupling <math|\<ell\><rsub|i+1>>, its
  returned coarse coupling satisfies, by <eqref|eq:shoot-adjacent-drift>,

  <\equation*>
    \<beta\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|i+1>-s*log
    L<rsub|i>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|i+1>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|i>-<tfrac|s|4>log
    L<rsub|i>\<less\>\<ell\><rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  If the input coupling is <math|u<rsub|i+1>>, the corresponding lower bound
  is

  <\equation*>
    \<beta\><rsub|i>\<geqslant\>u<rsub|i+1>-s*log
    L<rsub|i>-\<varepsilon\><rsub|i+1>\<geqslant\>u<rsub|i>+<tfrac|3*s|4>log
    L<rsub|i>\<gtr\>u<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  Thus the two endpoints cross the next two barriers in the required order,
  even though the intermediate output curve may oscillate.

  <subsubsection|Descending through a finite history>

  At cutoff <math|N> restrict the initial bare-action family to
  <math|<around|[|\<ell\><rsub|N>,u<rsub|N>|]>>. Its coupling is its
  parameter, it is continuous in the labelled activity norms, and it
  satisfies the prescribed initial conditions. Suppose a compact parameter
  interval already carries a family of histories from <math|N> down to
  <math|i+1>, with every represented coupling in its band, and with the
  endpoint couplings at level <math|i+1> equal to
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|i+1>,u<rsub|i+1>>. Apply the continuous nonlinear step
  to the level-<math|i+1> curve on this interval. The endpoint inequalities
  just proved and the interval lemma give a subinterval on which the output
  coupling stays between <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>,u<rsub|i>>, attaining these
  values at the two ends.

  Restrict every earlier representation in the history to this subinterval
  and append the newly constructed level-<math|i> representation. Restriction
  preserves the fixed dictionaries, their norm continuity, all activity
  bounds and every previously established adjacent identity. The new step
  supplies the same properties at level <math|i>. Induction therefore reaches
  level zero. Since <math|\<ell\><rsub|0>=u<rsub|0>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>>,
  either endpoint supplies a history with exactly the desired coarse
  coupling. No representation was extended across an interval on which its
  input band condition failed.

  There is also one fixed neighborhood of coarse targets that uses the same
  selected data. Put

  <\equation*>
    h=min <around|{|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>/2,s*log L<rsub|0>/4|}>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation*>

  For any <math|<around|\||b-<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>h>,
  descend only to level <math|1> and apply the last nonlinear step there. Its
  lower endpoint returns at most <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>-s*log
  L<rsub|0>/4\<leqslant\>b>, and its upper endpoint returns at least
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+3*s*log L<rsub|0>/4\<geqslant\>b>. The
  intermediate value theorem selects an input parameter with output coupling
  <math|b>. That output lies in the level-zero band because
  <math|<around|\||b-<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>|\|>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>/2>.
  For <math|N=0>, use the initial family directly at parameter <math|b>. This
  construction asserts existence, not uniqueness of a shooting history or
  continuity of its choice as <math|b> varies.

  For every cutoff and period select one such history. The selected paths
  depend on cutoff and level, and were fixed before the period; therefore
  their identity across periods is preserved. Each selected history starts at
  the bare Wilson density with vacuum parameter zero, satisfies all its local
  activity bounds, and has the exact full-density transfer at each step. The
  stronger retained conditions are preserved by taking them as part of the
  next-step requirement throughout the interval induction. The induction
  selects no new bridges, paths or support exponents.

  <subsubsection|The running-coupling estimate>

  Let <math|E<rsub|0>=0> and <math|E<rsub|i>=<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>i>\<varepsilon\><rsub|j+1>>.
  Telescoping <eqref|eq:shoot-adjacent-drift> gives, uniformly over every
  selected cutoff <math|N\<geqslant\>i> and period,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:shoot-running-error><around|\||\<beta\><rsub|i>-<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>E<rsub|i>+<around|\||b-<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>|\|>.
  </equation>

  Let <math|J<rsub|i>> denote the stage assigned to level <math|i> by the
  fixed schedule. The schedule has two further properties, which are inputs
  here: the stages are unbounded along the levels, and the block side of each
  stage is chosen so large, after its drift allowance, that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:shoot-drift-ratio><frac|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i+1>|log
    L<rsub|i>>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|i+1><rsup|-2>,<space|2em>J<rsub|i>\<longrightarrow\>\<infty\>.
  </equation>

  The allowances <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i+1>> are the same as in
  <eqref|eq:shoot-adjacent-drift>. Fix <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0> and choose
  <math|j> so that <math|J<rsub|i+1><rsup|-2>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>/2> for
  <math|i\<geqslant\>j>. Then

  <\equation*>
    E<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>E<rsub|j>+<tfrac|\<epsilon\>|2><around|(|S<rsub|i>-S<rsub|j>|)>,<space|2em>i\<geqslant\>j.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|S<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>, eventually
  <math|E<rsub|j>+<around|\||b-<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>*S<rsub|i>/2>.
  Substitution into <eqref|eq:shoot-running-error> yields

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<beta\><rsub|i>-<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>*S<rsub|i>
  </equation*>

  uniformly in cutoff and period. In particular the error is
  <math|o<around|(|S<rsub|i>|)>>. There is no assertion that the sum of the
  step errors is uniformly bounded in the depth. The estimate uses their
  small ratio to the accumulated logarithmic scale.

  <subsubsection|The public running condition>

  The last estimate is the input <eqref|eq:running> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:running-coefficient> for the selected
  histories, read as follows. Write <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>> for the
  coupling at level <math|m\<leqslant\>N> of the selected history at cutoff
  <math|N> and period index <math|p>. Its scale is
  <math|s<rsub|m>=S<rsub|m>=log <around|(|a<rsub|m><rsup|-1>|)>>, where
  <math|a<rsub|m>=e<rsup|-S<rsub|m>>> is the reciprocal of the integer
  product of the first <math|m> block sides, and
  <math|S<rsub|m>\<to\>\<infty\>>. Its intercept is
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>>, and <math|2*b<rsub|0><around|(|G|)>=s> by
  <eqref|eq:shoot-slope>. For every <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0> the preceding
  paragraph supplies a level after which <math|<around|\||\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>-<around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+2*b<rsub|0><around|(|G|)>*S<rsub|m>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>*S<rsub|m>>
  for every <math|N\<geqslant\>m> and every <math|p>. When the coarse target
  <math|b> of the descent equals <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>>, as in the
  selected run, the term <math|<around|\||b-<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>|\|>> in
  <eqref|eq:shoot-running-error> vanishes. It is in any case a constant, and
  was absorbed above.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Public running condition><label|prop:shoot-public-running>Let the
    public family have the selected histories as its runs: at cutoff <math|N>
    and period index <math|p>, its lattice has
    <math|P<rsub|p>*e<rsup|S<rsub|N>>> sites per axis, so that
    <math|D<rsub|N>=e<rsup|S<rsub|N>>> and
    <math|a<rsub|N>=D<rsub|N><rsup|-1>>, and its bare coefficient
    <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>> is the coupling <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,N>> of
    the bare Wilson density with which the selected history at cutoff
    <math|N> starts. Then <eqref|ex-running-input> holds with
    <math|\<sigma\>=s<rsub|G>=2*b<rsub|0><around|(|G|)>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Apply Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:uniform-extraction> with
    <math|A=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>>, <math|c=2*b<rsub|0><around|(|G|)>> and
    <math|s<rsub|m>=S<rsub|m>>; its hypothesis is the estimate just recorded.
    Given <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, it gives <math|N<rsub|0>> such that,
    for every <math|m\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>>, <math|S<rsub|m>\<gtr\>0> and
    <math|<around|\||\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>/S<rsub|m>-c|\|>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>
    for all <math|N\<geqslant\>m> and all <math|p>. Take <math|m=N>. For
    <math|N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>> and every <math|p>, the identities
    <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,N>=\<beta\><rsub|N,p>> and <math|S<rsub|N>=log
    <around|(|D<rsub|N>|)>=log <around|(|a<rsub|N><rsup|-1>|)>> give

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<frac|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>|log
      <around|(|a<rsub|N><rsup|-1>|)>>-c|\|>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>.
    </equation*>

    The threshold <math|N<rsub|0>> does not depend on <math|p>. Finally
    <math|c=2*b<rsub|0><around|(|G|)>\<gtr\>0>, so <math|\<sigma\>=c>
    satisfies <eqref|ex-running-input>.
  </proof>

  The proposition uses only the uniformity in <math|N\<geqslant\>m> and
  <math|p> of the running estimate, at the diagonal <math|m=N>. It does not
  use the convergence of the histories as the cutoff is removed.

  <subsection|Installing the terminal Wilson
  normalization><label|sec:terminal-normalization-transfer>

  The preceding startup construction uses a particularly simple terminal
  path. We must install it in the common horizon while preserving the
  stationary estimates used at the next step. Agreement through fourth order
  suffices for the required classical and one-loop coefficients; it does not
  identify the full nonlinear stationary maps.

  Throughout this section the following data are fixed; they are the same in
  every subsection. A paid scale schedule, with stage profiles whose direct
  clip coefficients satisfy <math|0\<less\>c<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>1>. The
  retained horizon <math|M<rsup|1>> on that schedule, satisfying the kernel
  bounds at fixed kernel prices: a strip width, an upper price and a lower
  price for each stage. A local-ratio chart <math|O>, used in every thermal
  function below. The stationary plan <math|<around|(|R<rsup|<math-up|old>><rsub|N,m>,\<alpha\><rsup|<math-up|old>><rsub|N,m>,u<rsup|<math-up|old>><rsub|N,m>|)>>
  on <math|M<rsup|1>> given by Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:path-retained-plan>,
  planned at fixed post costs in the sense of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:path-plan>. From that definition we use, at
  every cutoff <math|N> and period: the old terminal path
  <math|R<rsup|<math-up|old>><rsub|N,N>> has the Wilson derivatives of every
  order <math|j\<leqslant\>4> at the identity field and all its loop
  coefficients vanish (item 2); and, at the step from <math|N> to <math|N-1>,
  the gap, the critical and guessed properties, the classical match and the
  finite-cutoff bound (items 3 and 5). Finally the startup path of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:wilson-startup-construction> is assumed to
  satisfy, at every stage, the path bounds of that stage at its width and
  path allowance, with zero loop allowance; this is arranged by choosing the
  stage path allowance above the finite weighted sums of that path, as
  explained there.

  <subsubsection|Transporting the terminal quadratic columns>

  Write the old terminal full column, at any period, as

  <\equation*>
    A=<matrix|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<sqrt|c<rsub|0>>*I>>|<row|<cell|R>>>>>,<space|2em>E=<matrix|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|I>>|<row|<cell|0>>>>>,<space|2em>Z=A+E.
  </equation*>

  The terminal metric identity says <math|A<rsup|\<ast\>>*A*<space|0.17em>d=d>,
  where <math|d> is the lattice exterior derivative on one-forms. Since
  <math|E<rsup|\<ast\>>*A=A<rsup|\<ast\>>*E=<sqrt|c<rsub|0>>*I>, it implies

  <\equation*>
    Z<rsup|\<ast\>>*A*d=Z<rsup|\<ast\>>*E*d.
  </equation*>

  Moreover

  <\equation*>
    Z<rsup|\<ast\>>*Z=<around|(|1+<sqrt|c<rsub|0>>|)><rsup|2>*I+R<rsup|\<ast\>>*R
  </equation*>

  is positive definite. Put

  <\equation*>
    P=Z*<around|(|Z<rsup|\<ast\>>*Z|)><rsup|-1>*Z<rsup|\<ast\>>,<space|2em>U=2*P-I.
  </equation*>

  Direct multiplication gives <math|P<rsup|\<ast\>>=P>, <math|P<rsup|2>=P>
  and <math|U<rsup|\<ast\>>*U=I>. Also <math|P*<around|(|A*d+E*d|)>=A*d+E*d>
  and <math|P*A*d=P*E*d>, whence

  <\equation*>
    U*A*d=E*d.
  </equation*>

  Construct the analogous reflection <math|U<rsub|0>> for the desired
  terminal column <math|A<rsub|0>=<around|(|<sqrt|c<rsub|0>>*I,<sqrt|1-c<rsub|0>>*I|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>>.
  Then <math|X=U<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*U> is an isometry and
  <math|X*A*d=A<rsub|0>*d>.

  These are periodizations of common exponentially decaying kernels. To check
  the uniform estimate, suppose the old row kernel has an exponentially
  weighted absolute coefficient bound at width <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>. Its
  Fourier symbol and reflected transpose are holomorphic on a smaller strip.
  On the real torus the Gram matrix is bounded below by
  <math|<around|(|1+<sqrt|c<rsub|0>>|)><rsup|2>*I>. The coefficient bound
  controls uniformly the change of its symbol under a small imaginary
  displacement. Choose a smaller positive width so that this change has
  operator norm at most half that lower bound. The Neumann series then
  constructs a holomorphic inverse on the whole smaller strip, bounded by
  <math|2/<around|(|1+<sqrt|c<rsub|0>>|)><rsup|2>>. Fourier coefficient
  estimates on a further smaller strip give an exponentially weighted
  absolute sum: shift the contour in each coordinate in the sign of the
  Fourier index and sum the resulting geometric series. Matrix dimension is
  fixed by the row types, so these constants are independent of period and
  block length. Multiplication and transposition give the same kind of bound
  for <math|U,U<rsub|0>,X>. This is the strip inverse argument used for the
  free kernels.

  Modify only the finest transfer column <math|m<rsub|f>>, replacing it by
  <math|X*m<rsub|f>>. Since <math|X<rsup|\<ast\>>*X=I>, its Gram matrix is
  unchanged. Its mixed identity with the terminal curvature column is
  unchanged after replacing that column by <math|A<rsub|0>>, because
  <math|X*A*d=A<rsub|0>*d>. The other transfer columns, including the mean
  column and the transport matrix, are unchanged. Thus all quadratic
  transport and orthogonality identities remain valid. The exponential bounds
  for the modified column follow by convolution of its old bound with the
  bound for <math|X>. There is only this one modification at the terminal
  step, so its cost does not accumulate along the history.

  At each stage choose the new strip width below both the original width and
  the width just obtained; choose the new upper price above one, the old
  price and this convolution price; and choose the new lower price below one
  and the old lower price. These choices depend only on the stage profiles
  and old kernel prices. They precede the block lengths, delays and
  realization. The resulting horizon agrees with the old one below its
  terminal level, its towers agree when <math|m+1\<less\>N>, and their mean
  and transport columns agree also at <math|m+1=N>. Its terminal direct
  metric is the identity. This proves the revised-horizon assertions used in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history>.

  <subsubsection|Finite jets of stationary values>

  We record the analytic calculation controlling the change of path. Let
  <math|F,G:X\<times\>Y\<to\>\<bbb-R\>> be analytic germs at zero on
  finite-dimensional real spaces. Suppose their derivatives through order
  <math|k+1> agree. Let <math|u,v:X\<to\>Y> be analytic germs with
  <math|u<around|(|0|)>=v<around|(|0|)>=0> and

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|Y>*F*<around|(|x,u<around|(|x|)>|)>=0,<space|2em>D<rsub|Y>*G*<around|(|x,v<around|(|x|)>|)>=0.
  </equation*>

  Assume the vertical Hessian of <math|F> at zero is invertible. Then the
  graphs <math|<around|(|x,u<around|(|x|)>|)>> and
  <math|<around|(|x,v<around|(|x|)>|)>> agree through order <math|k>, and the
  stationary values <math|F*<around|(|x,u<around|(|x|)>|)>>,
  <math|G*<around|(|x,v<around|(|x|)>|)>> agree through order <math|k+1>.

  To prove the graph assertion, use the analytic local diffeomorphism

  <\equation*>
    \<Psi\><rsub|F>*<around|(|x,y|)>=<around|(|x,D<rsub|Y>*F*<around|(|x,y|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Its derivative is block triangular with invertible diagonal blocks <math|I>
  and <math|D<rsub|Y><rsup|2>*F<around|(|0|)>>. The corresponding map
  <math|\<Psi\><rsub|G>> has the same jets through order <math|k>. On the two
  stationary graphs these maps both equal <math|<around|(|x,0|)>>. Composing
  their jet identities with the local inverse of <math|\<Psi\><rsub|F>>
  proves equality of graph jets through order <math|k>. On a stationary graph
  the chain rule reduces to

  <\equation*>
    D*<around*|[|F*<around|(|x,u<around|(|x|)>|)>|]>=D<rsub|X>*F*<around|(|x,u<around|(|x|)>|)>,
  </equation*>

  because the vertical derivative vanishes. The right sides for <math|F,u>
  and <math|G,v> agree through order <math|k>, using the graph identity and
  the derivative jets of <math|F,G>. Their values at zero also agree. This
  proves equality of stationary-value jets through order <math|k+1>.

  In the application <math|k=3>. The old terminal action and the new startup
  action both match the Wilson action through order four. For the old one
  this is item (2) of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:path-plan>, a property
  of the old plan which is additional to its quadratic metric identity; for
  the new one it is proved in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:wilson-startup-construction>.
  This matching holds after substitution of the real fine-field chart on each
  forest slice. Indeed that chart is analytic and sends zero to the identity
  field; compose the matched real jets with its local logarithm. The noise
  term is identical in the two tested actions, so adding it preserves the
  matching.

  The vertical Hessians at zero are equal, hence the old positive gap is
  preserved exactly. The analytic implicit function theorem constructs the
  new critical graph. Near zero the tested-action germs are independent of
  the branch tag under the chosen kernel's branch hypothesis; thus this one
  graph solves every tested critical equation. Its first derivative agrees
  with the old graph, so it has the same linear mean. The preceding
  calculation also preserves the classical stationary-value jets through
  order four. This is the precise classical matching required by the outgoing
  coarse path, which is left unchanged.

  <subsubsection|The one-loop coefficient and retained limits>

  The vertical Hessian matrices of the two tested actions have matching jets
  through order two, since their actions match through order four. The
  determinant is a finite polynomial in matrix entries, so the determinants
  have matching two-jets. Composing with the stationary graphs preserves this
  matching: the graphs agree through order three, more than is needed here.
  Their positive determinant at zero allows the real logarithm.

  The Haar correction is a finite product of the same local chart Jacobian
  <math|J> along the vertical graph coordinates. Since <math|J> is analytic
  near zero and <math|J<around|(|0|)>=1>, these products and their logarithms
  also have matching two-jets. Both terminal loop potentials are zero: the
  old terminal path has vanishing loop coefficients by item (2) of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:path-plan>, and the startup path has no loop
  term. It follows that the two thermal functions

  <\equation*>
    <frac|1|2>*log det D<rsub|Y><rsup|2>*F*<around|(|x,u<around|(|x|)>|)>-log
    <space|-0.17em><big|prod><rsub|b>J<around|(|u<around|(|x|)><rsub|b>|)>
  </equation*>

  and its counterpart for <math|G,v> have identical Hessians at zero. The
  finite-period wave estimate is the finite-cutoff bound of
  Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:path-stationary-chart>: for every
  forest, every local choice, every two coarse sites and every two
  polarization amplitudes of modulus at most one, the second derivatives at
  the origin of the thermal function and of the outgoing comparison function
  <math|\<alpha\>*\<cal-K\><rsub|2>+\<cal-L\>> of the unchanged coarse path,
  on the corresponding plane-wave directions, differ by at most the stated
  multiple of <math|<around|\||<math-up|Site><around|(|c|)>|\|>*e<rsup|-\<gamma\>*c>>,
  <math|c> being the coarse side. It depends only on these Hessians and the
  unchanged outgoing classical data. Its bound is therefore preserved with
  exactly the old scalar correction, gap and tolerance. The other two clauses
  of the finite-cutoff bound are analyticity of the new thermal function at
  the origin, which holds because the new graph, the determinant and the
  chart Jacobian are analytic there, and positivity of the determinant, which
  is the next remark. Positivity near the new stationary graph follows from
  continuity and its positive definite Hessian at zero.

  Apply this construction only at <math|m+1=N>; at every lower step keep the
  old graph, path and scalar correction. The new terminal path is the startup
  path, while

  <\equation*>
    R<rsup|<math-up|new>><rsub|N,m>=R<rsup|<math-up|old>><rsub|N,m>*<space|1em><around|(|m\<less\>N|)>,<space|2em>\<alpha\><rsup|<math-up|new>><rsub|N,m>=\<alpha\><rsup|<math-up|old>><rsub|N,m>,<space|2em>u<rsup|<math-up|new>><rsub|N,m>=u<rsup|<math-up|old>><rsub|N,m>*<space|1em><around|(|m+1\<less\>N|)>.
  </equation*>

  The matching argument above supplies all stationary conditions at the
  changed step. The revised quadratic horizon supplies its same linear guess
  and all-period kernel identities. For every fixed level, the changed data
  consequently agree with the old data on a tail of cutoffs. Their
  fixed-level limits and eventual extended-domain bounds persist by this
  exact tail equality. The new terminal graph need not have the old graph's
  complete analytic expansion, and no such assertion is used.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Terminal replacement><label|prop:terminal-replacement>With the
    data fixed at the start of this section, there are revised kernel prices,
    depending only on the stage profiles and the old kernel prices and chosen
    before the block lengths, a horizon <math|M> on the same schedule
    satisfying the kernel bounds at the revised prices, and a stationary plan
    <math|<around|(|R<rsub|N,m>,\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>,u<rsub|N,m>|)>> on
    <math|M>, planned at the same post costs and with the same chart <math|O>
    in the sense of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:path-plan>, such that

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|R<rsub|N,N>>|<cell|=R<rsup|<math-up|Wilson>><rsub|N>,>|<cell|R<rsub|N,m>>|<cell|=R<rsup|<math-up|old>><rsub|N,m>*<space|1em><around|(|m\<less\>N|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>>|<cell|=\<alpha\><rsup|<math-up|old>><rsub|N,m>*<space|1em><around|(|m\<less\>N|)>,>|<cell|u<rsub|N,m>>|<cell|=u<rsup|<math-up|old>><rsub|N,m>*<space|1em><around|(|m+1\<less\>N|)>,>>>>
    </align*>

    where <math|R<rsup|<math-up|Wilson>><rsub|N>> is the startup path in the
    clip of level <math|N>. The beds satisfy
    <math|M.*H*<around|(|N,m|)>=M<rsup|1>.*H*<around|(|N,m|)>> for
    <math|m\<less\>N>, and <math|M.*H*<around|(|N,N|)>> is the bed of the
    column <math|A<rsub|0>>. The towers satisfy
    <math|M.*T*<around|(|N,m|)>=M<rsup|1>.*T*<around|(|N,m|)>> for
    <math|m+1\<less\>N>; for <math|m\<less\>N> their installed columns and
    transport columns agree.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The horizon is constructed in the first subsection, which also gives the
    statements on beds and towers and the revised prices. Define the plan by
    the displayed identities, with <math|u<rsub|N,N-1>> the new critical
    graph of the second subsection. We check
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:path-plan>. Item (1): for
    <math|m\<less\>N> the paths and beds are the old ones; the startup path
    is attached to the terminal bed of <math|A<rsub|0>> because its only row
    entry is <math|<sqrt|1-c<rsub|0>>> at offset zero, and it satisfies the
    stage bounds by the standing assumption. Item (2) is
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:wilson-startup-construction>. Items (3)
    and (5) are unchanged for <math|m+1\<less\>N>, since the incoming and
    outgoing paths, the tower and the maps are the old ones. For <math|m+1=N>
    they are the gap, critical, guessed and classical statements of the
    second subsection and the thermal statement of the third, all for the
    unchanged outgoing path <math|R<rsup|<math-up|old>><rsub|N,N-1>> and
    scalar <math|\<alpha\><rsup|<math-up|old>><rsub|N,N-1>>; the installed
    column used in the guessed property is unchanged. Item (4) involves only
    <math|\<alpha\>> and the schedule.
  </proof>

  This is the replacement used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history>.
  It selects no new background and no new cutoff sequence.

  <subsection|The terminal Wilson family><label|sec:wilson-startup-construction>

  The shooting argument needs a continuous initial family on the entire
  terminal coupling band, with one fixed local dictionary and common summable
  bounds. We construct it directly from the Wilson density. This also
  explains why its terminal path can be chosen before any trajectory is
  selected.

  Fix a compact gauge group as in the construction, a clipping profile with
  direct coefficient <math|0\<less\>c<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>1>, linear
  coefficient <math|0\<less\>c<rsub|1>\<less\>c<rsub|0>/16>, so that in
  particular <math|c<rsub|1>\<leqslant\>1/4>, and relaxation length
  <math|\<lambda\>\<gtr\>0>. Write <math|q=1-c<rsub|0>>. For a plaquette
  holonomy <math|u>, put

  <\equation*>
    r<around|(|u|)>=<math-up|chord><around|(|u|)>,<space|2em>k<around|(|u|)>=<math-up|curv><around|(|u|)>,<space|2em>W<around|(|u|)>=<tfrac|1|2>r<around|(|u|)><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|0\<leqslant\>W\<leqslant\>2> and
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|k<around|(|u|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>r<around|(|u|)>>.
  The gauge normalization gives <math|k<around|(|exp
  X|)>=X+O<around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|X|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>|)>> near zero.

  <subsubsection|A curvature-coordinate remainder>

  The analytic inverse function theorem applied to
  <math|X\<mapsto\>k<around|(|exp X|)>> gives a local inverse <math|v> near
  zero. Set <math|u<around|(|x|)>=exp v<around|(|x|)>> and
  <math|w<around|(|x|)>=W<around|(|u<around|(|x|)>|)>>. Conjugation
  covariance of curvature and uniqueness of the local inverse show
  <math|w<around|(|<math-up|Ad><rsub|g><space|0.17em>x|)>=w<around|(|x|)>> as
  germs at zero. The identities <math|k<around|(|u<rsup|-1>|)>=-k<around|(|u|)>>
  and <math|W<around|(|u<rsup|-1>|)>=W<around|(|u|)>> give
  <math|w*<around|(|-x|)>=w<around|(|x|)>>. The first and second derivatives
  at zero are respectively zero and the Euclidean inner product. Thus the
  fourth Taylor polynomial is

  <\equation*>
    T<rsub|4>*w<around|(|x|)>=<tfrac|1|2>\<\|\|\>x\<\|\|\><rsup|2>+Q<rsub|4><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>Q<rsub|4><around|(|x|)>=<frac|1|4!>*D<rsup|4>*w<around|(|0|)><around|[|x,x,x,x|]>.
  </equation*>

  The odd terms vanish by evenness. The quartic form <math|Q<rsub|4>> is
  adjoint-invariant, by differentiation of the conjugation identity. Define
  the global smooth residual

  <\equation*>
    h<around|(|u|)>=W<around|(|u|)>-<tfrac|1|2>\<\|\|\>k<around|(|u|)>\<\|\|\><rsup|2>-Q<rsub|4><around|(|k<around|(|u|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  For every smooth family through the identity its composition with <math|h>
  has vanishing derivatives through order four. Indeed near the identity
  <math|u=u<around|(|k<around|(|u|)>|)>>, so this is the Taylor remainder
  <math|<around|(|w-T<rsub|4>*w|)><around|(|k<around|(|u|)>|)>>; the chain
  rule preserves the stated vanishing.

  We need more than a Taylor estimate at the all-identity link field. For
  every derivative order <math|K> and spatial power <math|P> there are
  <math|C\<gtr\>0> and <math|t<rsub|0>\<in\><around|(|0,1|]>>, independent of
  the torus, such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:startup-residual-price><around*|\||\<partial\><rsub|a<rsub|1>>*\<cdots\>*\<partial\><rsub|a<rsub|l>>*h*<around|(|U<rsub|0>*U<rsub|1>*U<rsub|2><rsup|-1>*U<rsub|3><rsup|-1>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*t<rsup|5>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>l\<leqslant\>K,
  </equation>

  when the plaquette chord is at most <math|t\<leqslant\>t<rsub|0>> and the
  four-link directions have norm at most <math|t*5<rsup|P>>. Derivatives here
  are successive left increments on the links.

  Here is a uniform proof. For fixed links
  <math|U<rsub|1>,U<rsub|2>,U<rsub|3>> and a small face logarithm <math|x>,
  reconstruct the first link as

  <\equation*>
    U<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>=exp <around|(|x|)>*U<rsub|3>*U<rsub|2>*U<rsub|1><rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  The face holonomy is then <math|exp x>. After <math|l> left
  differentiations the resulting smooth function of <math|x> vanishes to
  order <math|<around|(|5-l|)><rsub|+>=max <around|(|5-l,0|)>> at zero. Each
  differentiation can lower the vanishing order by at most one: introduce its
  increment parameter as one further variable, differentiate the vanishing
  Taylor polynomial, and restrict that parameter to zero. All remaining links
  range over a compact group and the directions, after division by <math|t>,
  range over a fixed compact ball. Taylor's integral remainder therefore
  gives a common bound <math|C<rsub|l><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsup|<around|(|5-l|)><rsub|+>>>
  for the unscaled derivatives. A fixed local logarithm satisfies
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|log>*r<around|(|u|)>>
  when <math|r<around|(|u|)>> is sufficiently small. Restoring the <math|l>
  factors of <math|t> gives <math|C<rsub|l><rprime|'>*C<rsub|log><rsup|<around|(|5-l|)><rsub|+>>*t<rsup|l+<around|(|5-l|)><rsub|+>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|l><rprime|''>*t<rsup|5>>,
  since <math|t\<leqslant\>1>. Take the largest constant and smallest
  threshold over <math|0\<leqslant\>l\<leqslant\>K>. For a real lattice
  plaquette, the four links may coincide because of wrapping; substitution
  into the four-link identity remains valid. Each lies within distance four
  of any chosen plaquette corner, so the normalized spatially weighted
  directions obey the required bound <math|t*5<rsup|P>>. This proves
  <eqref|eq:startup-residual-price> on every healthy plaquette, including
  equality in the health threshold.

  <subsubsection|The principal path and the nonnegative debt>

  Choose the terminal path to have one row
  <math|<sqrt|q>*<space|0.17em>k<around|(|u|)>> at each elementary plaquette,
  quartic term <math|q*Q<rsub|4>>, and zero cubic and loop terms. Its
  zero-momentum metric is <math|c<rsub|0>*I+q*I=I>. Each row slot is an
  elementary plaquette slot with empty walk, hence of length four in the
  path-length convention of Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:path-stationary-chart>.
  Each quartic atom has four such legs, all at the same plaquette, and
  therefore has length sixteen although its geometric support is a single
  plaquette. Its only nonzero quartic coefficients form a finite list. Hence
  at any fixed positive strip width <math|\<sigma\>> the weighted path sums
  are finite: the row sum is <math|<sqrt|q>*<space|0.17em>e<rsup|4*\<sigma\>>>
  and the quartic sum is <math|e<rsup|16*\<sigma\>>> times the finite sum of
  the absolute quartic coefficients. Choosing the stage path allowance above
  their maximum, and the column allowance above the prescribed factorial,
  supplies the startup profile before choosing block lengths. Its uncut
  principal action differs from the Wilson action by
  <math|<big|sum>q*h<around|(|u|)>>, so the two actions have identical jets
  through order four at the identity.

  Fix an integer support scale <math|B\<geqslant\>2> and threshold
  <math|t\<gtr\>0> satisfying

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:startup-relaxation>2\<leqslant\>t*e<rsup|<around|(|B-2|)>/\<lambda\>>.
  </equation>

  At a plaquette with base site <math|x>, the row is kept when every
  plaquette based at <math|y> has chord at most
  <math|t*e<rsup|d*<around|(|x,y|)>/\<lambda\>>>. Its base action is

  <\equation*>
    b<around|(|u|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|c<rsub|0>*r<around|(|u|)><rsup|2>/2,>|<cell|r<around|(|u|)>\<leqslant\>t,>>|<row|<cell|c<rsub|1>*t*r<around|(|u|)>,>|<cell|r<around|(|u|)>\<gtr\>t,>>>>>+\<b-1\><rsub|<math-up|kept>>*<space|0.17em>q<around|\<\|\|\>|k<around|(|u|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>/2.
  </equation*>

  The quartic term is masked by health of the full four-corner plaquette
  support. Define the debt at that plaquette by

  <\equation*>
    d<around|(|U|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|W<around|(|u|)>-c<rsub|1>*t*r<around|(|u|)>,>|<cell|r<around|(|u|)>\<gtr\>t,>>|<row|<cell|\<b-1\><rsub|<math-up|healthy>>*<space|0.17em>q<around|\<\|\|\>|k<around|(|u|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>/2+\<b-1\><rsub|<math-up|not
    healthy>>*<space|0.17em>q*W<around|(|u|)>-\<b-1\><rsub|<math-up|kept>>*<space|0.17em>q<around|\<\|\|\>|k<around|(|u|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>/2,>|<cell|r<around|(|u|)>\<leqslant\>t.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  A direct split into the healthy, large-chord and remaining cases gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:startup-face-split>W<around|(|u|)>=b<around|(|u|)>+q*\<b-1\><rsub|<math-up|healthy>>*Q<rsub|4><around|(|k<around|(|u|)>|)>+q*\<b-1\><rsub|<math-up|healthy>>*h<around|(|u|)>+d<around|(|U|)>.
  </equation>

  The debt satisfies <math|0\<leqslant\>d<around|(|U|)>\<leqslant\>2>, since
  <math|q<around|\<\|\|\>|k<around|(|u|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>/2\<leqslant\>q*W<around|(|u|)>\<leqslant\>2>.
  On a large-chord plaquette,

  <\equation*>
    d<around|(|U|)>\<geqslant\>r<around|(|u|)><rsup|2>/2-t*r<around|(|u|)>/4\<geqslant\>t<rsup|2>/4.
  </equation*>

  If the base site is farther than <math|B> from every flag, the plaquette is
  healthy and the row is kept. For nearby plaquettes this follows from
  absence of flags; for the remaining plaquettes
  <eqref|eq:startup-relaxation> and the global chord bound
  <math|r\<leqslant\>2> make the kept test automatic. Thus its debt vanishes
  there. The same argument shows that debt at a base site can be determined
  from the field in its radius-<math|B> neighborhood, with the prescribed
  boundary flag information.

  Average <eqref|eq:startup-face-split> over signed coordinate changes. Let
  <math|s<rsub|\<ast\>>=1/<around|\||\<cal-S\>|\|>>, where <math|\<cal-S\>>
  is that finite symmetry group, and index the oriented plaquettes by
  <math|i=<around|(|s,x,b|)>>. Their supports are the actual transformed
  four-corner supports <math|Y<rsub|i>>, with native site <math|x>, residual
  <math|h<rsub|i>> and debt <math|d<rsub|i>>. The signed average is exact,
  because the Wilson energy is invariant:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:startup-signed-split>\<beta\>*E<rsub|W><around|(|U|)>=A<rsup|<math-up|top>><rsub|\<beta\>><around|(|U|)>+\<beta\>*s<rsub|\<ast\>>*q*<big|sum><rsub|i>\<b-1\><rsub|<math-up|healthy>(<math-up|Y><rsub|<math-up|i>>)>*h<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>+\<beta\>*s<rsub|\<ast\>>*<big|sum><rsub|i>d<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation>

  This uses the transformed supports and native sites in every channel.

  <subsubsection|Forced packets and the exact Wilson density>

  Let <math|\<cal-D\><around|(|U|)>> be the flag set. Join two flags when
  their closed radius-<math|2*B> balls overlap, and let <math|D> run over the
  connected components for this relation. Put

  <\equation*>
    P<around|(|D|)>=<big|cup><rsub|x\<in\>D>B<rsub|2*B><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>C<rsub|D><around|(|U|)>=s<rsub|\<ast\>>*<big|sum><rsub|i:<space|0.17em>x<rsub|i>\<in\>B<rsub|B><around|(|D|)>>d<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  The regions <math|P<around|(|D|)>> of distinct components are disjoint and
  <math|P<around|(|D|)>\<cap\>\<cal-D\><around|(|U|)>=D>. Every nonzero debt
  lies in exactly one of these sums: it lies within <math|B> of a flag, while
  membership in two such neighborhoods would join the two flag components.
  Consequently

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|D>C<rsub|D><around|(|U|)>=s<rsub|\<ast\>>*<big|sum><rsub|i>d<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  The energy price is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:startup-packet-energy>C<rsub|D><around|(|U|)>\<geqslant\><around|\||D|\|>*s<rsub|\<ast\>>*t<rsup|2>/16.
  </equation>

  To prove it, every flag is a corner of a large-chord plaquette. Such a
  plaquette has at most four corners and its base is within distance two of
  the flag, hence in <math|B<rsub|B><around|(|D|)>>. If <math|\<cal-A\>> is
  the set of these plaquettes in the identity channel, then
  <math|<around|\||D|\|>\<leqslant\>4<around|\||\<cal-A\>|\|>>. Each
  contributes at least <math|t<rsup|2>/4>; all other debts are nonnegative.
  Multiplication by <math|s<rsub|\<ast\>>> proves
  <eqref|eq:startup-packet-energy>.

  In a fixed dictionary include every nonempty connected flag set <math|D>
  with address <math|<around|(|P<around|(|D|)>,D|)>>, and put

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|D>*<around|(|\<beta\>,U|)>=\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|P<around|(|D|)>\<cap\>\<cal-D\><around|(|U|)>=D|}>>*e<rsup|-\<beta\>*C<rsub|D><around|(|U|)>>.
  </equation*>

  Other addresses have zero coefficient. The forced gas sums products over
  disjoint supports with total flag inventory <math|\<cal-D\><around|(|U|)>>.
  Exactly one family can have nonzero product: the components of
  <math|\<cal-D\><around|(|U|)>> just constructed. Indeed disjoint connected
  regions covering that inventory give its component decomposition. Thus the
  gas equals <math|e<rsup|-\<beta\>*s<rsub|\<ast\>>*<big|sum><rsub|i>d<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>>>,
  including the empty product when there are no flags. Put the regular
  residuals <math|\<beta\>*s<rsub|\<ast\>>*q*h<rsub|i>> in their healthy
  supports and take every extra logarithmic attachment to be zero. Equation
  <eqref|eq:startup-signed-split> now proves that the full density is exactly
  <math|e<rsup|-\<beta\>*E<rsub|W><around|(|U|)>>> at every field.

  All coefficients are gauge-invariant and measurable; the local residuals
  are smooth in left increments. Their value and Hessian at the identity
  vanish. Permuting the signed channels and lattice sites gives the required
  translation and signed-coordinate covariance. The packet charge reads only
  its support with the given flags, since the radius-<math|B> neighborhood of
  a site in <math|B<rsub|B><around|(|D|)>> lies in <math|P<around|(|D|)>>.
  These are the locality conditions of the startup family.

  <subsubsection|Uniform prices on the whole coupling band>

  Let <math|a\<gtr\>0> be the support exponent. A plaquette support has cover
  number at most <math|5<rsup|4>>. At a fixed native site there are
  <math|<around|\||\<cal-S\>|\|>> times the number <math|n<rsub|\<square\>>>
  of plaquette directions regular entries. By
  <eqref|eq:startup-residual-price> their regular budget for
  <math|0\<leqslant\>\<beta\>\<leqslant\>v> is at most

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:startup-regular-book><around|\||\<cal-S\>|\|>*n<rsub|\<square\>>*e<rsup|a*5<rsup|4>>*<space|0.17em>v*C*t<rsup|5>.
  </equation>

  The constants depend on the stage, not on volume or cutoff.

  Choose positive numbers <math|\<sigma\>,\<kappa\>,\<gamma\>> and a lower
  band endpoint <math|l> such that

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<kappa\>+\<sigma\>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>l*<space|0.17em>s<rsub|\<ast\>>*t<rsup|2>/16,<eq-number><label|eq:startup-loan>>>|<row|<cell|<around|(|4*B+1|)><rsup|4>*e<rsup|<around|(|a+2|)>*5<rsup|4>-\<sigma\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<gamma\>,<space|2em><around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*\<gamma\>\<leqslant\>1,<eq-number><label|eq:startup-single-hit>>>|<row|<cell|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*<around|(|e<rsup|\<gamma\>>-1|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>1.<eq-number><label|eq:startup-margin>>>>>
  </align>

  For <math|\<beta\>\<geqslant\>l>, <eqref|eq:startup-packet-energy> implies

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||K<rsub|D>*<around|(|\<beta\>,U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<kappa\>>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\><around|\||D|\|>>.
  </equation*>

  The elementary ball supports <math|B<rsub|2*B><around|(|x|)>> have cover
  number at most <math|5<rsup|4>>, and at most
  <math|<around|(|4*B+1|)><rsup|4>> of them contain a specified site. Thus
  <eqref|eq:startup-single-hit> bounds their rooted support sum with exponent
  <math|a+2> by <math|\<gamma\>>. Apply the connected-support estimate
  <eqref|eq:ope-forest-price>, with spare exponent one, to these ball
  supports. It gives the packet majorant

  <\equation*>
    sup<rsub|x> <big|sum><rsub|D:<space|0.17em>x\<in\>P<around|(|D|)>>e<rsup|-\<sigma\><around|\||D|\|>>*e<rsup|<around|(|a+1|)><math-up|cov><rsub|B><around|(|P<around|(|D|)>|)>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<gamma\>>-1.
  </equation*>

  This is one common summable majorant for the entire band
  <math|\<beta\>\<geqslant\>l>. The regular and forced budgets, and the extra
  one-unit support reserve, have now been proved. After the finite gas
  grouping the forced cost is at most <math|e<rsup|-\<kappa\>>*<around|(|e<rsup|e<rsup|\<gamma\>>-1>-1|)>>,
  by the same connected-support estimate with <eqref|eq:startup-margin>.

  We verify that the actual schedule pays these inequalities. Write
  <math|s=log <wide|\<beta\>|^>> and set

  <\equation*>
    M=e<rsup|<around|(|log s|)><rsup|2>>,<space|1em>t=e<rsup|-s/2>*M,<space|1em>B=<around|\<lceil\>|s<rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>,<space|1em>\<gamma\>=e<rsup|-M/2>,<space|1em>\<delta\><rsub|E>=e<rsup|-11*s/10>,<space|1em>\<delta\><rsub|K>=e<rsup|-M<rsup|1/5>>.
  </equation*>

  At a fixed stage take <math|\<sigma\>=M>, <math|l=e<rsup|s>/2> and
  <math|\<kappa\>=M>. Then <math|l*t<rsup|2>*s<rsub|\<ast\>>/16=s<rsub|\<ast\>>*M<rsup|2>/32\<geqslant\>2*M>
  eventually, giving <eqref|eq:startup-loan>. Since <math|B> grows
  polynomially in <math|s> while <math|M> grows faster than every such
  polynomial, <eqref|eq:startup-single-hit> and <eqref|eq:startup-margin>
  hold eventually. More explicitly <math|<around|(|4*B+1|)><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>M/4>
  and <math|<around|(|a+2|)>*5<rsup|4>\<leqslant\>M/4> eventually, so their
  product with <math|e<rsup|-M>> is at most <math|e<rsup|-M/2>>; also
  <math|e<rsup|\<gamma\>>-1\<leqslant\>2*\<gamma\>> once
  <math|\<gamma\>\<leqslant\>1>. Since <math|\<gamma\>\<to\>0>, we may also
  require <math|e<rsup|e<rsup|\<gamma\>>-1>-1\<leqslant\>1>. The grouped
  forced cost is then at most <math|e<rsup|-M>> and hence at most
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|K>>.

  The logarithm of <eqref|eq:startup-regular-book>, with
  <math|v=2*e<rsup|s>>, equals

  <\equation*>
    C-<frac|3|2>*s+5<around|(|log s|)><rsup|2>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|C> is a stage constant. It is at most <math|-11*s/10> for large
  <math|s>, because <math|<around|(|log s|)><rsup|2>=o<around|(|s|)>>. Also
  <math|t\<to\>0>, <math|B\<to\>\<infty\>>, and <math|log
  <around|(|t*e<rsup|<around|(|B-2|)>/\<lambda\>>|)>=-s/2+<around|(|log
  s|)><rsup|2>+<around|(|B-2|)>/\<lambda\>\<to\>\<infty\>>. Thus the residual
  threshold and <eqref|eq:startup-relaxation> are paid at the same entry
  delay.

  The stronger startup reserve needed by the later common services is
  obtained without changing this family. For any fixed additional support
  exponent <math|\<eta\>\<geqslant\>0>, replace <math|a> by <math|a+\<eta\>>
  in the preceding prices and delay the stage further. Take instead
  <math|l=e<rsup|s>/4> and <math|\<kappa\>=s<rsub|\<ast\>>*M<rsup|2>/128>.
  Eventually <math|M\<leqslant\>\<kappa\>>, and

  <\equation*>
    \<kappa\>+M\<leqslant\>2*\<kappa\>=s<rsub|\<ast\>>*M<rsup|2>/64=l*t<rsup|2>*s<rsub|\<ast\>>/16.
  </equation*>

  The same packet bounds then give a forced allowance at most
  <math|e<rsup|-s<rsub|\<ast\>>*M<rsup|2>/128>> before grouping, with a still
  smaller grouped cost after the reserve is paid. These choices depend on the
  stage and its fixed extra requests, and work for all periods and cutoffs
  realizing that stage. Taking their entry thresholds together with the other
  already chosen thresholds therefore preserves the order of choices in the
  common construction.

  <subsubsection|Continuity and the initial history>

  The dictionary and the masks above do not depend on <math|\<beta\>>.
  Regular coefficients depend linearly on <math|\<beta\>>, so their
  normalized derivative differences have the bound
  <math|C*<around|\||\<beta\>-\<beta\><rprime|'>|\|>*t<rsup|5>>. For a
  packet, <math|0\<leqslant\>C<rsub|D><around|(|U|)>\<leqslant\>2*s<rsub|\<ast\>><around|\||\<cal-S\>|\|><space|0.17em><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*n<rsub|\<square\>>>,
  and, for <math|\<beta\>,\<beta\><rprime|'>\<geqslant\>0>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||e<rsup|-\<beta\>*C<rsub|D><around|(|U|)>>-e<rsup|-\<beta\><rprime|'>*C<rsub|D><around|(|U|)>>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|D><around|(|U|)>*<around|\||\<beta\>-\<beta\><rprime|'>|\|>.
  </equation*>

  There are finitely many nonzero addresses on a fixed torus. Their weighted
  difference sums consequently tend to zero with
  <math|\<beta\><rprime|'>-\<beta\>>, uniformly in <math|U>. Together with
  the band-wide majorants above this proves the required curve property and
  invariant-atom continuity. No continuity of a health indicator as a
  function of <math|U> is asserted.

  Pack the dictionary into the fixed terminal dictionary by its
  coupling-independent injection. Zero unused entries contribute neither cost
  nor logarithm, and every nonzero coefficient, support, inventory, and
  density is preserved. The finite gas grouping is the absorption of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:soft-log-fusion> applied to the packed
  packet activities with all logarithms equal to zero. It is applied to this
  same dictionary and these same coefficients, and it leaves the regular
  entries unchanged. Its continuity in <math|\<beta\>> is
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:reset-absorption-continuity>: the first
  hypothesis is the fixed-address continuity just proved, and the second
  holds with the band-wide majorants <math|e<rsup|-\<kappa\>>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\><around|\||D|\|>>>,
  of which only finitely many are nonzero on the fixed torus. Together with
  the grouped forced cost computed above, this gives a continuous family on
  <math|<around|[|<wide|\<beta\>|^>/2,2*<wide|\<beta\>|^>|]>> with exact
  Wilson density, regular allowance <math|\<delta\><rsub|E>> and grouped
  forced allowance <math|\<delta\><rsub|K>>. Its scalar constant is zero and
  its coupling is the input parameter <math|\<beta\>>.

  We collect the result, separating the numerical conditions from the two
  identifications with the history.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The terminal family><label|prop:wilson-startup-family>Let <math|N>
    be a level of the scale schedule and let its stage have clip
    <math|<around|(|c<rsub|0>,c<rsub|1>,\<lambda\>|)>> with
    <math|c<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>1>, derivative order <math|K>, spatial power
    <math|P> and support exponent <math|a>. Let <math|t>, <math|B> and
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^>> be the threshold, radius and reference coupling
    of level <math|N>, and <math|\<delta\><rsub|E>,\<delta\><rsub|K>> its
    regular and forced allowances. Assume:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|B\<geqslant\>2> and the relaxation condition
      <eqref|eq:startup-relaxation>;

      <item><math|t\<leqslant\>t<rsub|0>>, where <math|t<rsub|0>> is the
      threshold of <eqref|eq:startup-residual-price> for <math|K,P>;

      <item>the regular book <eqref|eq:startup-regular-book> with
      <math|v=2*<wide|\<beta\>|^>> is at most <math|\<delta\><rsub|E>>;

      <item>there are <math|\<sigma\>,\<kappa\>,\<gamma\>> with
      <math|\<kappa\>\<geqslant\>0> satisfying
      <eqref|eq:startup-loan>\U<eqref|eq:startup-margin> for
      <math|l=<wide|\<beta\>|^>/2>, and <math|e<rsup|-\<kappa\>>*<around|(|e<rsup|e<rsup|\<gamma\>>-1>-1|)>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|K>>.
    </enumerate>

    Then for every period there is a family <math|F<rsub|\<beta\>>>,
    <math|\<beta\>\<in\><around|[|<wide|\<beta\>|^>/2,2*<wide|\<beta\>|^>|]>>,
    of representations at level <math|N> in one fixed dictionary, such that:
    the path of <math|F<rsub|\<beta\>>> is the startup path of this section
    in the stage clip; its coupling is <math|\<beta\>> and its vacuum
    constant is zero; its density is <math|e<rsup|-\<beta\>*E<rsub|W>>> at
    every field; its regular and forced arrays have the locality, symmetry
    and jet properties listed above, with regular cost at most
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|E>> and forced cost at most
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|K>>; and <math|\<beta\>\<mapsto\>F<rsub|\<beta\>>>
    is continuous in the labelled regular and forced norms. Moreover the
    reserved labels are empty. The fixed terminal dictionary contains, for
    every regular address and every natural number, a reserved regular label
    with that address, and for every forced support with nonempty inventory
    and every natural number, a reserved forced label with that support and
    inventory; these lists do not depend on <math|\<beta\>>. The regular and
    forced address lists of <math|F<rsub|\<beta\>>> are those of the fixed
    dictionary, and the regular, respectively forced, coefficient of
    <math|F<rsub|\<beta\>>> on every reserved label is zero. The additional
    predicate of the reserved forced label with support <math|X> and
    inventory <math|I> holds at a configuration <math|U> exactly when
    <math|X\<cap\><math-up|flags><around|(|U|)>=I>.

    Suppose in addition that the stationary plan of the history has the
    startup path as its terminal path at <math|<around|(|N,N|)>>, as in
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:terminal-replacement>, and that the
    recipe of the history is the default recipe built from that plan and
    schedule. Then every <math|F<rsub|\<beta\>>> satisfies the
    retained-history conditions at level <math|N> and the starter relation of
    that recipe.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The first part was proved in the preceding subsections: condition (1) is
    used for the debt and its locality, (2) for the residual price, (3) for
    the regular budget on <math|0\<leqslant\>\<beta\>\<leqslant\>2*<wide|\<beta\>|^>>,
    and (4) for the packet majorant on <math|\<beta\>\<geqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^>/2>
    and the grouped forced cost. The statement on reserved labels is a
    property of the fixed packing and grouping, valid for every dictionary
    and coefficient family to which they are applied. The packing injection
    takes its values among the unreserved labels, and every entry outside its
    image is zero. A reserved forced label is the group consisting of one
    reserved hard label alone. Every grouped label of the packed family
    consists of packed labels, so a reserved forced label is not among them,
    and every grouped entry outside the image of the packed family is zero. A
    reserved forced label is read as the group of one reserved hard label of
    shape <math|<around|(|X,I|)>>. Reading it recovers exactly the support
    <math|X> and the inventory <math|I>, and its additional predicate is the
    original test for that single shape. That test is the flag condition
    <math|X\<cap\><math-up|flags><around|(|U|)>=I>. For the second part, the
    retained-history conditions at level <math|N> are: the path is the
    terminal path of the plan, the clip and budget are those of level
    <math|N>, the arrays satisfy the stage bounds at the allowances
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|E>,\<delta\><rsub|K>>, the path satisfies its stage
    bounds, and the representation is a start of the recipe. The first is the
    assumed identity of paths; it is an equality of path data, which
    agreement of jets through order four would not give. The bounds are the
    first part and the path bounds of the plan. A start of the default recipe
    is a representation with coupling in the terminal band which is obtained
    by the fixed packing and grouping from some dictionary and coefficient
    family carrying a pre-absorption certificate at the stage allowances,
    whose raw density on the terminal path of the plan, multiplied by the
    exponential of its logarithmic corrections, is the Wilson density. Our
    pre-grouping dictionary and coefficients are such a witness: the regular
    book and the packet loan give the certificate, the logarithms are zero,
    and the raw density identity is the one proved above, for the terminal
    path by the assumed identity of paths. The default recipe imposes no
    further permission condition on a start. The starter relation is the same
    statement with the band condition recorded.
  </proof>

  The four numerical conditions hold on the actual schedule, at every level
  <math|N> including <math|N=0>, for the following reason. The preceding
  subsection showed that for each fixed stage they hold for all sufficiently
  large <math|s=log <wide|\<beta\>|^>>, with
  <math|t,B,\<delta\><rsub|E>,\<delta\><rsub|K>> the functions of <math|s>
  displayed there. The resulting threshold depends only on the stage, so it
  can be imposed before the schedule is realized. The common selection must
  include it among the entry requirements of that stage; this inclusion is
  made in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history> and is an
  input here. Granting it, a paid schedule enters each stage beyond its entry
  requirement and has at every level the threshold, radius and allowances
  given by those functions of its reference coupling, so the four conditions
  hold at every level. No blocking step is involved in these conditions, so
  the case <math|N=0> needs no separate argument. The identification of the
  terminal path is Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:terminal-replacement>,
  and the recipe is fixed in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history>.
  With them the proposition gives the initial-family assertion used in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history> for every <math|N>
  and every period.

  <section|The selected trajectory and observable normalization>

  <subsection|Choosing the nonlinear and stationary data
  together><label|sec:common-funded-history>

  The stationary construction, local expansion and scalar reset must use one
  set of paths and one scale schedule. We now describe the simultaneous
  choice and the way its identities are retained. The analytic inputs are the
  path-attachment estimates, their extended-domain convergence bounds, and
  the local nonlinear estimates proved in the preceding sections. This
  section concerns their common application; a numerical choice of large
  scales cannot replace an analytic estimate.

  <subsubsection|The order of the choices>

  Prescribe the finite derivative orders, spatial powers and support
  exponents at every stage. They may tend to infinity. At a fixed stage
  <math|j>, the adjacent target stage is either <math|j> or its immediate
  predecessor; thus every maximum below involves finitely many requirements.
  First choose the free-kernel prices and block floors. The stationary
  construction gives a positive upper width <math|\<sigma\><rsub|j>> and a
  lower path allowance <math|A<rsub|j>>. The startup expansion gives another
  lower path allowance. Choose the profile's path allowance above their
  maximum, and its width positive and at most <math|\<sigma\><rsub|j>>. Keep
  its direct coefficient below the purchased free-kernel bound. The profile
  construction retains the prescribed orders and support exponents. These
  choices precede the actual block lengths.

  There are two levels of kernel price: the free construction's price and the
  price required after replacing the terminal action by the Wilson action.
  Choose the latter from the former now, at the fixed profiles. Its validity
  is uniform over all later enlargements of the block floors. Consequently
  the local nonlinear estimates can request this final price without choosing
  it from a horizon that has not yet been built.

  Fix the startup and multiple-insertion requirements. Enlarge each block
  floor by the maximum of the startup floor, its scalar comparison floor and
  the multiple-insertion floor. Further enlarge it by the floors for the
  nonlinear geometry and the auxiliary local comparisons. The local targets
  have the same gauge chart, halo <math|500>, earned exponent
  <math|b<rsub|i>+3>, error exponent <math|-7/16> and degree zero at target
  stage <math|i>, where <math|b<rsub|i>> is the support exponent of stage
  <math|i>. These are the exponents requested of the output. The target's own
  support exponent is strictly larger than <math|b<rsub|i>+3> and its own
  rate is strictly below <math|-7/16>; the two margins are used in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-observable-purchases>. The relevant
  comparison radius exceeds the halo by at least five. These are the targets
  used to prove the interval return, including the finite short-panel
  localization.

  Choose any allowed block sequence above these floors. Only now select the
  wage bounds and local price tables; their constants can depend on the
  actual block lengths. An arbitrary larger wage family is still allowed.
  Choose positive postal rates below any prescribed positive upper rates,
  together with their forecast bounds. The rate agreement needed by the
  scalar reset can be arranged explicitly. If <math|\<rho\><rsub|j*i>> is the
  selected postal rate, take the post-step forcing rate <math|g<rsub|j>> no
  larger than

  <\equation*>
    min <around|{|\<rho\><rsub|j*j>/4,\<rho\><rsub|j,j-1>/4|}>.
  </equation*>

  At stage zero the repeated predecessor is harmless. Set the reset cost for
  the transition to <math|i> equal to four times the local credit constant at
  <math|i>. Thus

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:common-reset-coverage>g<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>\<rho\><rsub|j*i>/4,<space|2em>c<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>q<rsub|j*i>/4
  </equation>

  at every permitted transition. These are positive quantities. The post-step
  gap is supplied by the stationary estimate for this actual block sequence;
  take the auxiliary multiplier equal to one and the remaining post-step rate
  no larger than its stationary cap.

  Next choose protection parameters below any prescribed positive upper
  bounds and below one. The local expansion records can then be selected with
  their smallness parameter at most <math|1/4>. The order is significant: the
  interval return uses these records on every input curve, so their selection
  precedes all parameter intervals and finite periods.

  Finally fix the insertion menus and auxiliary finite lists. Given any
  previous entry delays, enlarge them successively to pay the following
  already established estimates: the sector integrals and their active
  Gaussian prefactors; the marked and unmarked local bounds; the two forced
  absorptions; the small-field localization and parameter comparison; and the
  scalar reset and startup prices. Impose both the fixed local reserve
  <math|1/16> and any requested positive smaller regular reserve
  <math|\<tau\>>, with forced reserve <math|1/8>. The support and path
  conditions are identical in these two applications.

  Here is a precise reason these successive enlargements are compatible. Each
  estimate supplies a stage-indexed lower threshold
  <math|d<rsup|<around|(|r|)>><rsub|j>> and a conclusion valid whenever the
  entry coupling exceeds that threshold. Replace the current threshold at
  stage <math|j> by the finite maximum of it and all the
  <math|d<rsup|<around|(|r|)>><rsub|j>> for that stage, and then by its
  running maximum up to <math|j>. Shell lower bounds are treated the same
  way. The conclusions persist at every later delay. Requirements on a finite
  auxiliary list are imposed after that list is fixed, before the final delay
  is selected. This proves the quantifier order

  <\equation*>
    <text|profiles>;<text|block lengths>;<text|rates and
    gaps>;<text|protection>;<text|menus and lists>;<text|delays>;<text|scale
    realization>.
  </equation*>

  The last realization is arbitrary among those paying these delays. In
  particular no threshold is selected from a later finite-horizon solution.
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection> constructs a
  realization, with periods having arbitrarily late multiples of every
  integer. This is the period divisibility needed for the five auxiliary
  comparisons of the nonlinear return.

  <subsubsection|Keeping the stationary and free data on that realization>

  Let <math|a> be one such realization. Build its free horizon
  <math|M<rsup|0>> with the initial kernel prices, and its retained horizon
  <math|M<rsup|1>> with the next prices. The retention identities keep their
  periodized covariance, row and tower data in the precise combinations used
  by the stationary construction. In particular the full-period lower bounds
  are supplied for <math|M<rsup|1>>, not for a separately selected quadratic
  form. Apply the stationary construction to <math|M<rsup|1>> with the
  purchased gap and shell bounds. It supplies simultaneously, for every
  cutoff <math|N>, paths <math|R<rsup|1><rsub|N,m>>, scalar corrections
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>> and stationary maps at every allowed period and
  forest. Its stronger version supplies the extended-domain estimates used in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-history-limits>. The free
  convergence of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:free-horizon-convergence>
  concerns these same retained towers.

  Replace only the terminal normalization, using the revised horizon <math|M>
  and paths <math|R<rsub|N,m>>. The construction gives the exact identities

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|R<rsub|N,N>>|<cell|=R<rsup|<math-up|Wilson>><rsub|N>,>|<cell|R<rsub|N,m>>|<cell|=R<rsup|1><rsub|N,m>*<space|1em><around|(|m\<less\>N|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<alpha\><rsup|<math-up|revised>><rsub|N,m>>|<cell|=\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>,>|<cell|\<rho\><rsup|<math-up|revised>><rsub|N,m>>|<cell|=\<rho\><rsup|1><rsub|N,m>*<space|1em><around|(|m+1\<less\>N|)>.>>>>
  </align*>

  The entire tower agrees with that of <math|M<rsup|1>> when
  <math|m+1\<less\>N>, and its transport matrix agrees already when
  <math|m\<less\>N>. The new horizon satisfies the final nonlinear kernel
  price chosen before the block lengths. At the terminal level its canonical
  direct coefficient is at most one, which gives the required Wilson
  positivity bound. At lower levels the retained all-period bounds apply.

  For any fixed <math|m>, all the displayed identities hold on the tail
  <math|N\<gtr\>m+1>. The limits and eventual larger-width bounds in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-history-limits> are therefore
  preserved: remove finitely many terms from each sequence and use equality
  of the remaining terms. This argument applies to each stationary derivative
  as well as each path and tower coefficient. It also preserves the
  extended-domain statement, because the one-step data coincide on this tail.
  Thus the final nonlinear construction carries both the stationary
  convergence data and the free-horizon convergence data. The terminal
  normalization introduces no independently selected background.

  <subsubsection|The same local return on every input interval>

  On this final horizon the sector construction supplies its exact Gaussian
  expression, including the active determinant, Haar factors, sieve and
  coupling prefactor. The local price tables selected above apply to this
  expression. The parameter comparison uses a single reference dictionary and
  the identity identification of its elementary labels, as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-nonlinear-offering>. The auxiliary
  locality estimates are retained when the delays are increased: they are
  stated beyond their entry thresholds, with all block lengths, rates and
  local dictionaries fixed.

  Consequently, for every cutoff, every adjacent pair of levels and period,
  and every allowed continuous input curve on a compact interval, that
  section supplies the exact return in one dictionary, with early regular
  cost

  <\equation*>
    H=c<rsub|i>*\<delta\><rsub|E><around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|)>
  </equation*>

  and the forecast forced cost. Both the <math|1/16> and the <math|\<tau\>>
  reserve estimates refer to this same constructed horizon and local price
  data. The selected tree and sector choice can depend on the interval and
  period, as permitted by the scalar reset, but are fixed throughout that
  interval.

  We check the remaining recipe conditions rather than assuming that they
  follow from the density identity. Write

  <\equation*>
    \<delta\>=\<delta\><rsub|E><around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|)>/32,<space|2em>\<gamma\>=exp
    <around|{|-\<frak-k\><around|(|\<rho\><rsub|j*i>,<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>,\<beta\>|)>|}>
  </equation*>

  for the actual regular and forced prices after reorganization, where
  <math|\<frak-k\>> is the purchased postal decay exponent and
  <math|\<beta\>> is the incoming coupling. We verify the comparison with the
  recipe prices explicitly. Put <math|x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>>,
  <math|y=x+c=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>>, where <math|c=s<rsub|G>*log
  L\<geqslant\>0>, and write <math|g<around|(|\<beta\>|)>=\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>>
  and <math|t<around|(|x|)>=g<around|(|x|)>*P<around|(|x|)>>. Let
  <math|C\<geqslant\>1> be the exchange constant and let
  <math|H<rsub|<math-up|post>>\<geqslant\>1>, <math|D\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>,
  <math|\<rho\>\<gtr\>0> be the selected postal constants. With
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|\<theta\>><around|(|y|)>=P<around|(|y|)><rsup|\<theta\>>>,
  define

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|M*<around|(|x,y|)>>|<cell|=H<rsub|<math-up|post>>*<around|(|1+B<around|(|x|)>+P<around|(|y|)>+2*\<ell\><rsub|\<theta\>><around|(|y|)>|)><rsup|D>,>>|<row|<cell|R*<around|(|x,y,\<beta\>|)>>|<cell|=M*<around|(|x,y|)>*<around*|[|g<around|(|\<beta\>|)><rsup|3>+g<around|(|\<beta\>|)><rsup|-D>*<around*|(|e<rsup|-\<rho\>*B<around|(|x|)>>+e<rsup|-\<rho\>*P<around|(|y|)><rsup|2>/<around|(|1+B<around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|D>>|)>|]>,>>|<row|<cell|T*<around|(|x,y,\<beta\>|)>>|<cell|=C*<around*|[|<around*|(|<frac|C|L>+<frac|1|16*C>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|E><around|(|y|)>+R*<around|(|x,y,\<beta\>|)>|]>.>>>>
  </align*>

  All terms in <math|M> and <math|R> are nonnegative. The recipe's portion
  and charges are precisely

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|portion>=T,<space|2em><math-up|charge><rsub|E>=T+M*<around|(|x,y|)>*t<around|(|x|)><rsup|3>,<space|2em><math-up|charge><rsub|K>=H<rsub|<math-up|post>>*e<rsup|-\<frak-k\>>,<space|1em>\<frak-k\>=\<rho\><around*|(|<frac|t<around|(|y|)>|g<around|(|\<beta\>|)>>|)><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  In particular <math|T\<geqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|E><around|(|y|)>/16>. Since
  <math|c> is fixed after <math|L> is chosen, the elementary shifted-gap
  comparison gives, for all sufficiently large <math|x>,
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|E><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>2*\<delta\><rsub|E><around|(|y|)>>.
  The reset constants of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-reset-reorganization>
  have the form

  <\equation*>
    c<rsub|i>=<frac|1|64*<around|(|1+B<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reset>>|)>>,<space|2em>B<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reset>>\<geqslant\>0,<space|2em>B<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reset>>*c<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><frac|1|64>,<space|2em>c<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><frac|1|64>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reset>>> is the coefficient of <math|H>
  in the regular reset price; write <math|v<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0> for the
  coefficient of <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>*t<around|(|x|)><rsup|3>>
  there. It follows that

  <\equation*>
    H=c<rsub|i>*\<delta\><rsub|E><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><frac|\<delta\><rsub|E><around|(|y|)>|32>\<leqslant\>T,<space|2em>\<delta\>=<frac|\<delta\><rsub|E><around|(|x|)>|32>\<leqslant\><frac|\<delta\><rsub|E><around|(|y|)>|16>\<leqslant\>T\<leqslant\><math-up|charge><rsub|E>.
  </equation*>

  Moreover the uniform stage bound <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|j>>
  and the delayed cubic estimate <math|64*v<rsub|i>*A<rsub|j>*t<around|(|x|)><rsup|3>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|E><around|(|x|)>>
  give

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|regularNeed>=B<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reset>>*H+v<rsub|i><around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>*t<around|(|x|)><rsup|3>\<leqslant\><frac|\<delta\><rsub|E><around|(|x|)>|64>+<frac|\<delta\><rsub|E><around|(|x|)>|64>=\<delta\>.
  </equation*>

  Thus the actual local cost is paid, as well as the recipe's larger
  allowance. The soft reset price is bounded by a stage constant times
  <math|<around|(|1+B<around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|4>*t<around|(|x|)>>; the late
  estimate for this expression in the reset section bounds it by the
  forecast's mild reserve. Its remaining hard reserve is exactly the
  purchased forecast comparison at this incoming band. These two reserves are
  retained in the forced absorption of the local return.

  Since <math|H<rsub|<math-up|post>>\<geqslant\>1>,
  <math|\<gamma\>=e<rsup|-\<frak-k\>>\<leqslant\><math-up|charge><rsub|K>>.
  This proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:common-recipe-prices>H\<leqslant\><math-up|portion>,<space|2em>\<delta\>\<leqslant\><math-up|charge><rsub|E>,<space|2em>\<gamma\>\<leqslant\><math-up|charge><rsub|K>.
  </equation>

  The incoming band lies in <math|<around|[|y/4,4*y|]>>, so
  <math|g<around|(|y|)>/2\<leqslant\>g<around|(|\<beta\>|)>\<leqslant\>2*g<around|(|y|)>>.
  Hence <math|\<frak-k\>\<geqslant\>\<rho\>*P<around|(|y|)><rsup|2>/4\<geqslant\>g<rsub|j>*P<around|(|y|)><rsup|2>>,
  using <eqref|eq:common-reset-coverage>. Since
  <math|P*<around|(|x+c|)>/P<around|(|x|)>\<to\>1> and
  <math|P<around|(|x|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>, eventually
  <math|g<rsub|j>*P*<around|(|x+c|)><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>P<around|(|x|)><rsup|1/5>>.
  The coarse forced allowance is <math|e<rsup|-P<around|(|x|)><rsup|1/5>>>,
  so

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:common-final-forcing>\<gamma\>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-g<rsub|j>*P<around|(|y|)><rsup|2>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-P<around|(|x|)><rsup|1/5>>.
  </equation>

  The coarse regular allowance dominates <math|\<delta\>> by its factor
  <math|32>. Thus the output satisfies the costs of the next call as well as
  those of the recipe batch.

  For clarity, the corresponding implication on the actual arrays is purely
  monotone. If <math|u<rsub|i>> and <math|v<rsub|i>> are their nonnegative
  regular and forced majorants at costs <math|\<delta\>,\<gamma\>>, the same
  majorants work at the larger charges in <eqref|eq:common-recipe-prices>; no
  supports, functions or labels change. Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-reset-reorganization>
  gives the exact normalized density identity for the same batch and
  <math|t<rsup|2><around|\||\<Delta\>|\|>\<leqslant\>H\<leqslant\><math-up|portion>>.
  The permission predicate used in this construction imposes no additional
  batch restriction. Together these facts are exactly the batch relation of
  the chosen recipe. Appending this batch to the incoming finite history
  supplies the retained-history predicate at level <math|m> and the next-step
  predicate. This proves those predicates for the output that was actually
  constructed.

  The terminal startup construction, treated separately below, must supply
  one continuous family on the full terminal band. Its regular and forced
  bounds, common summable majorants and invariant jets survive the fixed
  packing into the terminal dictionary. Its exact Wilson density starts the
  retained history. This is the required initial-family assertion even when
  <math|N=0>.

  Once that startup assertion is proved, we can apply the actual scalar reset
  at every input interval and then Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:surviving-interval-shooting>.
  The result is a finite history at every cutoff and period, with the
  prescribed level-zero coupling, the exact transferred densities and the
  same selected paths <math|R<rsub|N,m>>. Its per-edge coupling error is the
  purchased reset width, and its forced estimate includes
  <eqref|eq:common-final-forcing>. No assertion of uniqueness of these
  shooting histories is required. Their subsequent convergence and the
  additional observable comparisons use the retained common data and the
  estimates of their respective sections.

  <subsection|Attaching the funded service to the selected
  history><label|sec:original-history-portfolio-attachment>

  The finite-volume service is useful only if its data belong to the history
  selected for the public theorem. We verify that binding here. The public
  construction selects one run and applies the funded continuation to that
  run's own history, schedule, transitions, and finite-volume data. No
  cutoff, period, or extraction order is used to select a second history. The
  common schedule and its numerical prices are organized in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history>; here we check the
  remaining same-witness interface.

  Let <math|R> denote the selected run. Write <math|Y<rsub|R>> for its stored
  finite renormalization-group history and <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|R>> for the
  separately stored scale campaign. The distinction matters: the history used
  below is <math|<math-up|trail><around|(|Y<rsub|R>|)>>, whereas the campaign
  supplies the scale data. Let <math|t<rsub|R><around|(|d|)>> be the
  reference level at grade <math|d>, let <math|S<rsub|R>*<around|(|d,N,p|)>>
  be the transfer datum, and let <math|D<rsub|R>*<around|(|N,p,m|)>> be the
  selected transition record. We write

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|R>=<around*|(|<math-up|trail><around|(|Y<rsub|R>|)>,t<rsub|R>,S<rsub|R>|)>
  </equation*>

  for the family with its fixed holonomy, label frames, and local sections.
  This notation names the family already stored in the run; it does not
  define a new history.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Same-history attachment><label|prop:ex-original-history-attachment>Suppose
    the selected run <math|R> carries its funded continuation certificate.
    Applying that certificate to the data stored in <math|R> gives a package
    on the same family <math|C<rsub|R>> with the following properties:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>the reference-level condition

      <\equation*>
        d+3\<leqslant\>J<around|(|t<rsub|R><around|(|d|)>|)>*<space|2em><around|(|d\<geqslant\>0|)>;
      </equation*>

      <item>one nesting family, one scale schedule, one transition family,
      and one transfer family, such that the selected transition at
      <math|<around|(|N,p,m|)>> is available for <math|m\<less\>N>, and, for
      every <math|d,N,p> with <math|t<rsub|R><around|(|d|)>\<leqslant\>N>,
      the finite-volume transfer identity holds using those same data;

      <item>the funded nonlinear-response and adjacent-scale estimates,
      together with the selected service on exactly <math|C<rsub|R>>;

      <item>the centered service, including its height estimates and terminal
      forced-profile relation, as well as the finite-volume bounds and marked
      portfolio conclusions recorded for <math|C<rsub|R>>.
    </enumerate>

    The first three items and the finite-volume identities hold
    simultaneously for all cutoffs and periods in their stated domains. For
    each fixed grade and section in the purchased portfolio, the terminal
    marked estimate is conditional on the reserved addresses and fee, the
    local forced-family attachment, and the paid selected-history record used
    in Propositions<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-marked-invitation> and
    <reference|prop:actual-marked-figment>. Under these conditions on the
    same <math|C<rsub|R>>, it holds for all sufficiently large cutoffs,
    uniformly in the period; its threshold may depend on that grade and
    section. It is a separate local estimate, not a field included implicitly
    in the centered service.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    We first identify the history-bearing data. The selected run stores
    <math|Y<rsub|R>>, the schedule <math|t<rsub|R>>, a transition family, the
    gradewise regularity data, and the finite-volume transfer witnesses. Its
    underlying family is

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|R>=<around*|(|<math-up|trail><around|(|Y<rsub|R>|)>,t<rsub|R>,S<rsub|R>|)>,
    </equation*>

    with the fixed holonomy, label frames, and local-section map. The funded
    continuation is a function of precisely these stored objects: the
    configuration and its nesting data, the schedule and its opening
    inequalities, the transfer family and transition records, and the
    regularity and finite-volume transfer witnesses. Its output therefore has
    the family formed from those same arguments. That family is
    <math|C<rsub|R>> itself; there is no intervening identification map and
    no reselected label system.

    Here is why the quantifiers in that application are uniform. The public
    selection fixes the history <math|Y<rsub|R>> and a nesting family for it.
    Independently, it chooses the reference schedule on that selected run so
    that <math|d+3\<leqslant\>J<around|(|t<rsub|R><around|(|d|)>|)>> for each
    grade. On this fixed history it constructs the transition branches and
    their records once, and then defines one transfer family on all triples
    <math|<around|(|d,N,p|)>>. That family has the required regularity
    properties; for every <math|d,N,p> with
    <math|t<rsub|R><around|(|d|)>\<leqslant\>N>, its finite-volume witness is
    built from the already chosen nesting family and transition records. The
    witness at <math|<around|(|d,N,p|)>> may depend on those indices, but the
    history, nesting family, schedule, transitions, and transfer family do
    not. Thus the simultaneous quantifiers are part of the selected run and
    are not obtained by diagonal extraction or by making new global choices
    after fixing <math|<around|(|N,p|)>>.

    For the transfer witnesses, if <math|t<rsub|R><around|(|d|)>\<gtr\>N> the
    construction uses a default map. If <math|t<rsub|R><around|(|d|)>\<leqslant\>N>,
    it composes along the selected transitions and obtains the finite-volume
    identity and accounting estimate. This explains the cutoff in item 2 and
    shows that both cases use the original <math|Y<rsub|R>>.

    The funded continuation supplies two quantitative inputs to the centered
    construction: bounds on the delayed nonlinear response and its tariff,
    and uniform estimates for each allowed adjacent-scale step. The latter
    are the scale inequalities proved in Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-geyser-scale-estimates>.
    That section applies them to the selected adjacent levels and block size,
    and supplies a common monotone threshold. It does not change the selected
    history, nor does it by itself establish the nonlinear-response bounds.

    The centered service is assembled from these data as follows. The escape
    estimate uses the same nesting family, nonlinear-response bounds, and all
    selected transitions. Its room and bank estimates use the budget fields
    of the selected factor. The adjacent-scale inequalities give the required
    scale comparison at each selected step. For heights at grade <math|d> and
    reference level <math|k>, the local residence estimate at <math|k> gives
    the later scale bounds, the one-cover footprint estimate, and a uniform
    camera bound. The five numerical implications used in this conversion are
    proved in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-geyser-service-fields>; the
    eventual estimates and finite-prefix maximum are in
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-selected-geyser-steam>. The maximum is
    over the finitely many grades below each target stage and its two
    adjacent stages, so it gives a common threshold for all later uses at
    that stage.

    At the terminal endpoint, the finite-volume transfer witness gives the
    top transfer identity and its forced-profile relation. The centered
    service's terminal estimate is this same relation, using the domination
    and rise identities attached to the selected run. At an intermediate
    scale, the transfer witnesses and selected transitions feed the local
    transition estimate: specializing the tariff inequality to the zero-grade
    section gives the required relay bounds. The separate growth and jump
    estimates follow from positivity and the selected scale payment. The
    three-card coefficient, exponential-entry, and forced-profile identities
    are proved in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:three-port-history>; we use
    those identities here rather than constructing a second interpolation or
    repeating their price proof.

    The funded continuation returns the centered service together with its
    finite-volume bounds and marked portfolio. Its local transition estimates
    use the same nonlinear-response bounds, adjacent-scale estimates, nesting
    family, transition records, finite-volume witnesses, and schedule just
    described. Both the core centered-service assembly and its stronger
    funded version use the supplied configuration, schedule, transfer data,
    and transition family. Neither creates a second history.

    Finally, the terminal marked-field estimate is attached only after
    <math|C<rsub|R>> has been fixed. The reserved addresses of the terminal
    representation are not constructed there: they are a property of the
    selected history, recorded for every cutoff and period in the nesting
    receipt stored in the run. This receipt is an input of the present
    section; it originates in the selection of the history from the exact
    startup family, and no result of this group of sections proves it. Given
    the reserved addresses and the lawful terminal representation,
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:terminal-marked-voice> constructs the
    terminal marked coefficients on this same family, with their density and
    forced-profile identities, provided its fee inequality holds; that
    inequality is the terminal fee purchased in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-observable-purchases>. The
    quantitative local bounds are proved in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-marked-field-local>, and their eventual
    uniformity for each fixed grade and section in
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-marked-solaris>. The local estimate
    requires soundness, reference-level eligibility, fit of the union of the
    stencils, nonnegative credit, a keeper, the regional card-times-allowance
    bound, cover at most <math|81>, the square-root allowance-times-storm
    bound, and the stated positive terminal weight and nonnegative reserve.
    The local estimates supply the numerical bounds for all sufficiently
    large cutoffs for each fixed grade and section in the purchased
    portfolio, uniformly in the period, subject to the stated schedule and
    matching inputs. In addition, the local forced-family attachment must be
    supplied for each relevant correction, on the terminal representation of
    this same <math|C<rsub|R>>, uniformly in the period. The root-test
    conclusion also uses the paid selected-history record. Given that record
    and the terminal attachment, paragraph<nbsp><reference|par:figment-served-residual>
    constructs the full served residual polynomial. Thus no separate
    residual-representation hypothesis is required. The marked density and
    forced-profile identity then give the terminal estimate on this same
    <math|C<rsub|R>>. Throughout, its history, schedule, transfer family, and
    terminal representation remain those of <math|C<rsub|R>>. No uniqueness
    assertion for a terminal field is needed.
  </proof>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Scope of the attachment>The proposition proves the same-history
    attachment once the selected run's funded continuation certificate is
    available. The common funded-history construction is responsible for
    producing that certificate and its quantitative prices; this section does
    not replace those purchases by the name of a record. It also does not use
    the separate grantor route as evidence for the public caller. The public
    construction applies its funded continuation to the data stored in the
    selected run.

    Two points of bookkeeping are needed by later sections. First, the
    certificate is a universally quantified statement: it applies to every
    history of the same alloy with a nesting receipt, every schedule obeying
    the reference-level condition, and all transfer and transition families
    satisfying the making, climbing and transfer conditions. The run stores
    this certificate, and the public run stores in addition a stronger form
    with further conclusions; both are specialized to the same nine stored
    objects, namely the history, its nesting receipt, the schedule and its
    reference-level condition, the transfer family, the transition family,
    and the making, climbing and transfer receipts. Second, the certificate
    asserts the existence of the marked portfolio, that is, of one local
    section and one marked family for each grade together with the gradewise
    coefficient maps and the presentation entering the terminal marked
    estimate; it does not name them. A later argument must fix one such
    witness once, before any cutoff, period or extraction order is chosen,
    and use it throughout. Reopening the existence statement at a later point
    may produce a different portfolio and is not a same-object argument.
  </remark>

  <subsubsection|Budget and domain of the stored
  service><label|sec:ex-stored-service-budget>

  Write <math|W<around|(|j|)>> for the worth fixed in the purchase,
  <math|c<rsub|j>> for the column count of stage <math|j>, and
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>> for its chosen block side. At level <math|i> put
  <math|C<rsub|i>=c<rsub|J<around|(|i|)>>> and
  <math|L<rsub|i>=\<ell\><rsub|J*<around|(|i+1|)>>>. Thus <math|C<rsub|i>> is
  the column count denoted by <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|i>> in
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-selected-run-service>; it is unrelated to
  the running-coupling increment. Notice that the level-<math|i> block is
  chosen at the <em|entrance> stage <math|J*<around|(|i+1|)>>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Room and bank on the purchased
    levels><label|prop:ex-stored-service-budget>The stage purchase can be
    made so that every allowed schedule has, at every level
    <math|i\<geqslant\>0>,

    <\equation*>
      64*W*<around|(|J<around|(|i|)>+1|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|i>,<space|2em>100000*W*<around|(|J*<around|(|i+1|)>+1|)><rsup|6>*<around|(|1+C<rsub|i+1>|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|i><rsup|1/8>.
    </equation*>

    In particular the same inequalities hold for the selected run,
    independently of grade, cutoff and period.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    After fixing worth and the orders, choose the stage column count as a
    running maximum of the room request <math|64*W*<around|(|j+1|)><rsup|2>>,
    the factorial of the stage derivative order and the other pre-length
    column requests. An integer count can be used by taking ceilings. In
    particular the profile already pays the room request before any length is
    chosen. With these columns fixed, let

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|j>=100000*W*<around|(|j+1|)><rsup|6>*<around|(|1+c<rsub|j>|)><rsup|2>,<space|2em>n<rsub|j>=max
      <around|{|1,<around|\<lceil\>|A<rsub|j><rsup|8>|\<rceil\>>|}>.
    </equation*>

    Include <math|n<rsub|j>> in the stage length floor before selecting
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>>. This is compatible with all other lower floors:
    take their maximum and, if nondecreasing floors are required, take the
    running maximum of that maximum. Any subsequent increase preserves the
    two purchases. Since <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>n<rsub|j>> and the
    eighth root is increasing on the nonnegative reals,
    <math|A<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|j><rsup|1/8>>. Substitute
    <math|j=J<around|(|i|)>> in the column inequality and
    <math|j=J*<around|(|i+1|)>> in the length inequality. The same
    substitutions apply at <math|i=0>. The later entrance delays and the
    selection of finite-volume histories do not change the stage columns or
    these lengths.
  </proof>

  For the common C02 and C12 interface use the direct specialization of the
  funded continuation in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-original-history-attachment>,
  with family <math|C<rsub|R>>, reference map <math|t<rsub|R>>, transfer data
  <math|S<rsub|R>> and doors <math|D<rsub|R>>. The stronger funded service is
  on precisely these objects. Its centered part restricts to each reference
  level <math|k> by retaining the transition receipts with
  <math|k\<leqslant\>i\<less\>N> and the terminal receipt at <math|N>. The
  stock clause for grade <math|d> is needed only for
  <math|t<rsub|R><around|(|d|)>\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>N>, and the music
  clause for <math|t<rsub|R><around|(|d|)>\<leqslant\>N> with doors
  <math|t<rsub|R><around|(|d|)>\<leqslant\>i\<less\>N>. The restriction makes
  sense even when <math|k=N>, with no intervening door. The centered birth
  estimate uses the stricter domain <math|k\<less\>N>. All period and cutoff
  indices range over entries of this one run. The local analytic attachments
  needed for retention, music, the paid local coupon and the uniform birth
  estimate are separate from this projection and the numerical budget
  proposition.

  There is also a campaign construction with its own chosen finite-volume
  history, transfers and doors. In the stamped original campaign the history
  is <math|Y<rsub|R>> and the transfers are <math|S<rsub|R>>, so forming its
  family gives <math|C<rsub|R>> by substitution. The campaign service uses
  the recorded gate of its campaign step for <math|i\<geqslant\>1> and its
  <math|k>-restricted retention is asserted for <math|k\<geqslant\>1>. At
  <math|i=0> its door is a default. The common interface above uses
  <math|D<rsub|R>> directly, including at level zero, and needs no
  identification of campaign gates with these doors.

  For this direct interface put <math|x<rsub|i>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>>
  and <math|r<rsub|i>=N<rsub|J*<around|(|i+1|)>>>, where <math|N<rsub|j>> is
  the stage occupancy bound. The marched weight multiplier is
  <math|\<phi\><rsub|i>=x<rsub|i+1><rsup|-1>> and the probe price is
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|i>=x<rsub|i+1><rsup|-2>>; both use the fine coupling.
  The grade height is <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,i>=x<rsub|i><rsup|-<around|(|deg
  F<rsub|d>+4|)>>> and the response waybill <math|\<psi\><rsub|i>> is twice
  the title cost at level <math|i+1> multiplied by the exponential of that
  level's support exponent. The terminal-bank reserve is
  <math|u<rsub|i>=x<rsub|i><rsup|-1/2>/4>. In particular it is neither the
  marched multiplier nor the adjacent coupon rate.
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-centered-retention> gives the
  separate terminal endowment from the paid delay at
  <math|J*<around|(|N+1|)>>: for <math|C<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|end>>=w<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|f>>+r<rsub|N><math-ss|s><around|(|B<rsub|N>|)>*u<rsub|N>>,
  the bounds are <math|<around|(|2*B<rsub|N>+1|)><rsup|4>*C<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|end>>\<leqslant\>3>
  and <math|e<rsup|C<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|end>>>-1\<leqslant\>1>, where
  <math|w<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|f>>> is the forced allowance. These numerical
  bounds require no door beyond the cutoff.

  <subsection|Binding the selected local course><label|sec:ex-lattice-selected-course-binding>

  The public construction returns a run together with a continuation on its
  selected history. Specializing that continuation to the run's own bolt,
  schedule, transfer family and doors yields a binding on the very family
  used by the run. This section proves that same-family assertion and records
  the monotonicity needed when comparing paid schedules. The global schedule
  and same-history identities are those of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history> and
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-original-history-attachment>.

  Write <math|R> for the run selected by the public construction and put

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|R>=<around*|(|<math-up|trail><around|(|T<rsub|R>|)>,t<rsub|R>,S<rsub|R>|)>,<space|2em>A<rsub|R>=\<cal-L\><around|(|C<rsub|R>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|T<rsub|R>> is its bolt, <math|t<rsub|R>> its schedule,
  <math|S<rsub|R>> its transfer family, and <math|\<cal-L\><around|(|C|)>>
  denotes the directed level schedule of a course <math|C>. The family
  <math|C<rsub|R>> is exactly the family stored in <math|R>, with its fixed
  holonomy, label frames and local sections.

  A <em|binding> for a family <math|C> is the following comparison record on
  the level schedule <math|\<cal-L\><around|(|C|)>> of that same family. It
  consists of a horizon <math|M> on <math|\<cal-L\><around|(|C|)>>, a price
  map <math|K> and a share function <math|\<ell\>*<around|(|N,i|)>>, with
  five properties. First, the shape-port identities hold. To state them, let
  <math|V<rsub|N,i>> be the train at level <math|i> obtained by descending
  from the constant top train at the cutoff <math|N>, and for
  <math|i\<less\>N> let <math|D<rsub|N,i>> be the flight at level <math|i>
  whose wing is <math|V<rsub|N,i+1>> and whose land is <math|V<rsub|N,i>>, as
  constructed in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-rg-horizon-geometry>.
  For <math|i\<leqslant\>N>, the diagonal and dual-diagonal rows of the bed
  <math|M<rsub|N,i>> are the pulse and dual pulse of <math|V<rsub|N,i>> at
  the kernel penalty of the schedule, and its metric and inverse rows are the
  amended precision and amended inverse of <math|V<rsub|N,i>> at that penalty
  with share <math|\<ell\>*<around|(|N,i|)>>. For <math|i\<less\>N>, the
  <math|A<rsub|0>> and <math|T> rows of the tower at <math|<around|(|N,i|)>>
  are the primal and two-form transports donated by <math|D<rsub|N,i>> at
  that penalty. These identities fix four of the five rows of each bed; they
  do not determine its root row, which is constrained only by the second and
  fifth properties. Second, the horizon satisfies the horizon contract of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-rg-horizon-geometry> for the
  price map <math|K>. Third, for all <math|i\<leqslant\>N> and every period
  <math|p>, the quadratic form of the bed <math|M<rsub|N,i>> on the torus of
  level <math|i> and period <math|p> is invariant under the signed coordinate
  changes. Fourth, every level step <math|<around|(|i,p|)>> has the room
  required by the comparison: its patch satisfies the branch condition of the
  kernel, one hundred times its macroscopic radius is at most the side of the
  finer torus, and the coarser side exceeds one. Fifth, for all
  <math|i\<leqslant\>N> and <math|p>, the path of the representative of
  <math|C> at <math|<around|(|N,p,i|)>> is attached to the bed
  <math|M<rsub|N,i>> in the sense of Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-lattice-horizon-path>.
  In particular a binding refers to the paths of <math|C> itself.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Binding on the selected family><label|prop:ex-lattice-selected-course-binding>The
    continuation stored in <math|R>, specialized to its own bolt, schedule,
    transfers and doors, supplies a binding <math|B<rsub|R>> for
    <math|C<rsub|R>>. Its horizon is the horizon of the directed alloy stored
    in <math|R>, and its price map is the selected run's price map.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The continuation is universally applicable to a bolt, its nested factor,
    a schedule, transfer data and doors, subject to the making, climbing and
    freight conditions. The run stores these very objects and receipts: its
    tuning supplies nesting, its acceptance data supply the openings, its
    making data define the family, and its carrying and freight records
    supply the remaining steps. Specializing all arguments to those stored
    objects gives the family <math|C<rsub|R>> by its definition. The
    resulting calibrated package contains a binding <math|B<rsub|R>> on
    <math|C<rsub|R>>, with horizon equal to the alloy's horizon and price map
    equal to the selected run's price map. The binding is constructed from
    the shape-port, attached path and shell comparison for that same alloy
    and bolt, so no separately selected family is involved. The level
    schedule of the trail of a bolt is by construction the level schedule of
    its directed alloy, and the assembly of the public run records this
    identity for <math|C<rsub|R>>; therefore the schedule used by the binding
    is <math|A<rsub|R>>, and the statement that the two horizons agree is
    meaningful.
  </proof>

  We record the exact elementary monotonicity for the paid-schedule
  condition. For waits <math|U,V> over the same bar, write
  <math|U\<preceq\>V> when their requests obey, pointwise in stage <math|j>
  and scale <math|s>,

  <\equation*>
    r<rsub|U><around|(|j|)>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|V><around|(|j|)>,<space|2em>w<rsub|U>*<around|(|j,s|)>\<leqslant\>w<rsub|V>*<around|(|j,s|)>,<space|2em>p<rsub|U>\<leqslant\>p<rsub|V>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|r> is the ready-time request, <math|w> the window request, and
  <math|p> the minimum-period request. A schedule <math|A> has stage map
  <math|J<rsub|A>>, scale values <math|s<rsub|A><around|(|k|)>> and period
  values <math|P<rsub|A><around|(|i|)>>. Write
  <math|<math-ss|Pay><around|(|U,A|)>> when the fixed kernel, bar, stage,
  initial-stage, length and protection identities and the entry, scale and
  volume inequalities hold, together with the following two conditions:

  <\enumerate>
    <item>the separate period condition <math|p<rsub|U>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|A><around|(|i|)>>
    for every <math|i>;

    <item>for every <math|j> from the required initial stage onward, there is
    a <math|k> such that

    <\gather>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|r<rsub|U><around|(|j|)>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|A><around|(|k|)>,<space|2em>w<rsub|U>*<around*|(|j,s<rsub|A><around|(|k|)>|)>\<leqslant\>k,>>|<row|<cell|J<rsub|A><around|(|i|)>=j*<space|1em><around|(|k-w<rsub|U>*<around|(|j,s<rsub|A><around|(|k|)>|)>\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>k+w<rsub|U>*<around|(|j,s<rsub|A><around|(|k|)>|)>|)>.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-paid-plateau>>>>>
    </gather>
  </enumerate>

  The lower endpoint in this interval uses truncated natural subtraction.
  These are the schedule conditions used in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Weakening the paid-schedule requests><label|lem:ex-lattice-paid-weakening>If
    <math|U\<preceq\>V> and <math|<math-ss|Pay><around|(|V,A|)>>, then
    <math|<math-ss|Pay><around|(|U,A|)>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The fixed identities and entry, scale and volume inequalities do not
    depend on the wait. The separate period condition for <math|U> follows
    for every <math|i> from <math|p<rsub|U>\<leqslant\>p<rsub|V>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|A><around|(|i|)>>.
    Fix a stage <math|j> and take the same <math|k> supplied by the plateau
    condition for <math|V>. Its ready-time inequality implies
    <math|r<rsub|U><around|(|j|)>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|V><around|(|j|)>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|A><around|(|k|)>>.
    Set <math|a=w<rsub|U>*<around|(|j,s<rsub|A><around|(|k|)>|)>> and
    <math|b=w<rsub|V>*<around|(|j,s<rsub|A><around|(|k|)>|)>>. Then
    <math|a\<leqslant\>b>, and truncated subtraction gives

    <\equation*>
      max <around|{|0,k-b|}>\<leqslant\>max
      <around|{|0,k-a|}>,<space|2em>k+a\<leqslant\>k+b.
    </equation*>

    Hence the interval of indices on which <math|J<rsub|A><around|(|i|)>=j>
    for <math|V> contains the interval required for <math|U>. Also
    <math|a\<leqslant\>b\<leqslant\>k>, so the window bound for <math|U>
    holds. Thus the same index <math|k> verifies the entire plateau condition
    for <math|U>; this proves <math|<math-ss|Pay><around|(|U,A|)>>.
  </proof>

  These results identify the binding family and compare paid-schedule
  requirements. They do not themselves supply the subsequent local-service
  estimates.

  <subsection|Paying the observable estimates on the same
  history><label|sec:common-observable-purchases>

  The nonlinear return and the observable estimates have to be available
  before a finite-volume history is selected. We now make the additional
  choices in that order. The resulting statements are uniform over every
  allowed representation on the purchased horizon. In particular, enlarging
  the finite volume does not require a new choice of block lengths.

  Retain the profiles, horizons and targets of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history>. At a transition from
  stage <math|j> to stage <math|i>, write <math|b<rsub|i>> for the outgoing
  support exponent, <math|e<rsub|j*i>> for the support exponent of the local
  target itself, and <math|q<rsub|j*i>> for the rate of its input allowance.
  These are not the earned exponent <math|b<rsub|i>+3> and the error exponent
  <math|-7/16> requested of the output in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history>: each target was
  purchased with two positive support margins above its earned exponent and
  with a rate strictly below its error exponent. The purchases therefore give

  <\equation*>
    e<rsub|j*i>\<gtr\>b<rsub|i>+3,<space|2em>q<rsub|j*i>\<leqslant\>-7/16.
  </equation*>

  These are two different reserves: the first pays support enlargement,
  whereas the second gives decay with the coupling. Fix a nondecreasing
  integer occupancy schedule <math|N<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1>. At each stage we
  need only the two possible target stages and the finitely many grades
  through <math|j>.

  <subsubsection|The broad parameter estimate and its localized price>

  Put <math|y=x+s<rsub|G>*log L>, <math|B=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
  x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>> and <math|r=y<rsup|1/256>>. Apply
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:sector-analytic-prices> with outgoing
  exponent <math|e=b<rsub|i>+3>, the actual common support dictionary, and
  the fixed derivative order and spatial power at stage <math|i>. Its
  positive support reserve is <math|<around|(|e<rsub|j*i>-e|)>/4>. The input
  allowance has upper exponent at most <math|-7/16>, so the integrated
  constituent price has upper exponent <math|-2/5> and the rooted derivative
  price is eventually at most <math|y<rsup|-3/8>>. For each finite real test
  <math|\<lambda\>> on the parameter interval, its ordinary logarithmic
  family <math|F> therefore satisfies

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-N\><rsub|e><around*|(|<big|int>F<around|(|t|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<lambda\><around|(|t|)>|)>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|-3/8>*M<rsub|r/2><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|e>> is the rooted derivative budget at support
  exponent <math|e>, and <math|M<rsub|r/2>> is the moment-test norm of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:background-test-prices>. The forced family
  has one common absolute coefficient majorant at radius <math|r/2>, with the
  compulsory warning gain retained. The construction proves this for all
  compatible input representations and all real parameter lines; the majorant
  is not selected after the finite test.

  The hypotheses on the real line deserve mention. Its coupling is the same
  quadratic line used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:real-coupling-interpolation>.
  Its activity coordinates are affine, hence of degree at most two. The path,
  cutoff, charts and input dictionaries are fixed on the line. The enlarged
  real interval remains in the purchased band, by the radius choice there.
  The compensated derivative and packet-action estimates are supplied by
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:parameter-comparison-suppliers>. Thus all
  the local hypotheses of the sector estimate hold on these actual lines,
  including their held fine fields and their cutoffs.

  We check that the finite localization does not destroy the useful power.
  Let <math|C<rsub|i>> be the signed-pullback derivative cost and define

  <\equation*>
    w<around|(|x|)>=C<rsub|i>*<around|{|y<rsup|-3/8>+4*y<rsup|-7/16>|}>.
  </equation*>

  This has upper exponent <math|-3/8>. The five auxiliary tori in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:five-period-localization> have sides bounded
  by a fixed multiple of <math|1+B>. Their numbers of sites, local ball
  counts, and weighted changes of anchor are fixed powers of <math|1+B>. The
  interpolation weights are fixed. For example, a contribution from auxiliary
  torus <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|\<nu\>>> has price at most
  <math|2<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|\<nu\>>|\|>*w<around|(|x|)>>; its
  weighted change of anchor costs at most
  <math|2<rsup|k>*<around|(|1+c*B|)><rsup|p*k>> at derivative order <math|k>
  and spatial power <math|p>. The local allocation costs at most another
  fixed ball count and <math|exp <around|(|<around|(|b<rsub|i>+2|)>*C<rsub|<math-up|cover>>|)>>,
  with <math|C<rsub|<math-up|cover>>> fixed. Consequently both the localized
  raw derivative price and its ordinary rooted price have upper exponent
  <math|-3/8>. The latter is eventually at most <math|y<rsup|-1/4>>, and the
  former at most <math|y<rsup|-1/8>>. These comparisons use strict exponent
  margins and are uniform over the period and input representation.

  The forced price survives the same localization with its forecast
  normalization. The enlarged spatial factors have upper exponent zero. The
  compulsory Gaussian warning is <math|exp
  <around|(|-\<mu\>*P<around|(|y|)><rsup|2>/c|)>> with <math|\<mu\>\<gtr\>0>
  and fixed <math|c\<gtr\>0>; the permitted forecast spends a strictly
  smaller part of that exponent. The ratio of the spent price to the forecast
  tends to zero faster than any fixed power of <math|y>, as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:nonlinear-payment-selection>. Thus the
  ordinary price <math|y<rsup|-1/8>> and the soft correction can be paid
  simultaneously in the forced absorption inequalities. This uses the chosen
  positive forecast rate below its permitted fraction of <math|\<mu\>>,
  already selected before the delays. More precisely, the forecast hard rate
  is at most <math|\<mu\>/16>. Its soft allowance is twice the zero-order
  compulsory activity price, <math|2*A<rsub|<math-up|comp>><around|(|x|)>>,
  and the resizing estimate is applied at fraction <math|1/16> before the
  final absorption. This keeps both the factor two and the stated rate
  reserve. No new rate is selected for an individual input.

  <subsubsection|The fixed parameter radius and all derivatives>

  Choose a fixed <math|q\<geqslant\>4> and a desired normalized output
  allowance <math|0\<less\>s\<leqslant\>1/256>. The finite interpolation in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:background-test-prices>, with degree
  <math|300>, has a constant <math|U=U*<around|(|300,q|)>>. Choose the small
  response parameter <math|t\<gtr\>0> so that <math|6*U*t\<leqslant\>s>. This
  choice precedes the nonlinear purchases;
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history> permits any positive
  such <math|t>. The pointwise localization estimate is

  <\equation*>
    3*t*C<rsub|<math-up|st>>*x<rsup|-11/10>+R<around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>R<text|
    has upper exponent >-23/20.
  </equation*>

  Combining it with the just-proved broad bound gives the fixed-radius price
  <math|s*C<rsub|<math-up|st>>*x<rsup|-11/10>>. Indeed the discarded
  parameter tail is at most

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|1+U|)>*<around|(|2*q|)><rsup|301>*y<rsup|-365/256>,
  </equation*>

  which is <math|o<around|(|x<rsup|-11/10>|)>>, as is
  <math|U*R<around|(|x|)>>. The linear reset and the common forced
  coefficient majorant give the two normalized output prices at radius
  <math|q/2> from that section. The scalar projection has price
  <math|s*C<rsub|<math-up|st>>*x<rsup|-11/10>/\<theta\><around|(|x|)><rsup|2>>.
  Thus the finite-test hypotheses of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:background-ray-estimates>
  are available for the same return as its exact density identity.

  The differentiated output also requires one common local collection of
  constants before an input is supplied. This follows by making the choices
  in Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:activity-jet-assembly> and
  <reference|sec:parameter-comparison-suppliers> at the largest requested
  order <math|k>. For the chart derivatives use a coarse radius

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|k><around|(|x|)>=Z<rsub|k>*<space|0.17em>2*\<theta\><rsub|f><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>v<around|(|x|)>=<around|(|k+1|)>*q<rsub|k><around|(|x|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|Z<rsub|k>\<gtr\>0> is the fixed angular chart constant and
  <math|\<theta\><rsub|f>\<gtr\>0> is the fine fluctuation threshold. Then
  <math|v<around|(|x|)>\<gtr\>0>, <math|k*q<rsub|k><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>v<around|(|x|)>>,
  and both radii have upper exponent <math|-1/2>. The comparison estimates
  allow any such radius. Fix the cutoff convention before this collection of
  constants is chosen. The first integrated polymer is the same unmasked
  local function used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:activity-jet-assembly>.
  On a healthy support its mask is identically one in a neighborhood, so its
  ordered derivatives are the derivatives of that unmasked function. The
  angular-to-chart conversion at radius <math|q<rsub|k>> uses
  <math|k*q<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>v> and supplies all orders through <math|k>.
  This identifies the pre-mask derivative estimate as well as the masked
  activity; the former is required by later differentiated insertions. The
  Gaussian normalization estimates apply to every read subset with the same
  row bounds, including the empty subset. The enforcement and warning
  derivatives have respectively the bounds proportional to the cover number
  and to one plus the warning-support cover number; these are exactly the
  cutoff terms retained in the differentiated activity construction.

  Reserve a positive part of the target support exponent before summing the
  differentiated factors. At every order through <math|k>, its exponential
  weight absorbs the polynomial cover and length factors. For completeness,
  choose a common positive support reserve <math|\<Delta\>=e<rsub|j*i>/2> at
  outgoing exponent zero. If <math|A<rsub|k><around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>0> is
  the common integrated derivative constant of upper exponent zero and
  <math|u<rsub|k><around|(|x|)>\<gtr\>0> the constituent allowance, its
  differentiated allowance is

  <\equation*>
    s<rsub|k><around|(|x|)>=<around|(|1+2<rsup|k>*A<rsub|k><around|(|x|)>|)>*u<rsub|k><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  It has the same negative upper exponent as <math|u<rsub|k>>. The
  connected-tree criterion <math|4*<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*s<rsub|k>/\<Delta\>\<leqslant\>1/2>
  therefore holds eventually. The ordinary and forced unweighted constituent
  allowances likewise become at most <math|1/2>, and their tree criteria at
  reserve <math|\<Delta\>> hold at the same threshold. Since
  <math|0+\<Delta\>+\<Delta\>=e<rsub|j*i>>, these are exactly the two
  reserves required in the renewed first and second expansions. The
  unstrengthened construction in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:high-degree-cluster-prices>
  proves their majorants and all polynomial cover moments; we use it here at
  exponent zero, with the same <math|\<Delta\>> in both expansions. Thus
  renewal does not introduce an unrelated set of constituent prices.

  The resulting connected activity majorants and nearby integrable domination
  are those of Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:activity-jet-assembly>,
  <reference|sec:gaussian-differentiation> and
  <reference|sec:finite-volume-parameter-continuity>. Enlarge the finitely
  many constants to common nonnegative ones and intersect their
  large-coupling intervals. All those constants depend on the stage, block
  length, chart and purchased prices, but not on the finite volume or input
  representation. The subsequent unweighted total sums may depend on the
  finite volume, as the continuity proof allows. This distinguishes the
  uniform local derivative estimates from the fixed-volume domination used to
  pass limits under sums.

  <subsubsection|Insertion estimates and their common delays>

  The primary, regular and forced one-insertion estimates use the same local
  expansion. Their common heading and inverse bound were proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:heading-history-handoff>. The primary
  calibration uses the extra derivative available when
  <math|d+3\<leqslant\>i>; the regular case uses its vanishing leading
  coefficient, and the forced case retains its square-root forced-allowance
  normalization. The auxiliary local integral and its comparison are those of
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:principal-gaussian-comparison> and
  <reference|sec:principal-comparison-jets>. The finite-support conditions
  are imposed by increasing the shell and entry bounds, after their fixed
  support radii have been selected.

  For multiple insertions, the raised forced estimate and the ordinary
  estimate have distinct prices. Use both, as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:finite-menu-history-handoff>. The tariff has
  ordinary allowance <math|y<rsup|-1>>, single-insertion allowance
  <math|y<rsup|-2>>, and forced allowance
  <math|2*e<rsup|-\<mu\>*P<around|(|y|)><rsup|2>/16>>. The occupancy and
  projection inequalities of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:projection-payment-producer>
  apply with <math|N=N<rsub|j>>. In particular the local projection bill is
  at most <math|e<rsup|<sqrt|y>>>, while its two error bills fit the fixed
  local splitting allowance. The required geometric floor is imposed before
  <math|L> is selected; the occupancy and error inequalities are imposed
  afterwards.

  Here is the common choice explicitly. At stage <math|j> take the maximum of
  the pre-length floors for the finitely many allowed target stages, the
  primary comparisons, and the projected insertion geometry. Choose
  <math|L<rsub|j>> above it. Having chosen wages, rates and protection,
  choose the primary records with smallness at most <math|1/4>. For each
  allowed target stage, take the finitely many eventual thresholds for all
  grades through <math|j>, the broad and fixed-radius parameter bounds, the
  differentiated output, the ordinary and raised menu bounds, and the
  projection inequalities. Include all previous thresholds. Replace their
  maximum at <math|j> by its running maximum up to <math|j>. Enlarge the
  shell bound by the required fixed comparison radii and its growth lower
  bound by one.

  Every conclusion used above is valid beyond its threshold, so all are
  preserved by any further enlargement. The quantifiers are therefore

  <\gather*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<text|profiles and block
    floors>;<space|1em><text|block lengths>;>>|<row|<cell|<text|prices and
    protection>;<space|1em><text|delays>;>>|<row|<cell|<text|all compatible
    local inputs>.>>>>
  </gather*>

  The scale realization and the selected finite-volume history come last. On
  that history each of the estimates applies to its actual held transition;
  the endpoint representation, its density identity, its singleton derivative
  and its marked profile all retain their original common dictionary. In
  particular the observable estimates do not choose another solution of the
  scalar shooting problem.

  <subsubsection|Purchases for the terminal marked field>

  Three further requirements enter the same order of choices. They are
  consumed by Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:terminal-marked-construction>,
  <reference|sec:actual-marked-portfolio-calibration> and
  <reference|sec:selected-centered-service>, and they are recorded with the
  purchase, so that they hold on every schedule realized afterwards and in
  particular on the selected run.

  The first is the terminal fee. For each grade <math|d> and each value of
  the support exponent there is a threshold with the following property: at
  every level <math|m> of every schedule whose profile at <math|m> has that
  support exponent and whose coupling <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>>
  exceeds the threshold, every torus side at level <math|m> is at least the
  minimal side of the local section of grade <math|d>, and the fee of that
  section at its camera constant and at the forced allowance of level
  <math|m> is at most the square root of that allowance. This is the fee
  inequality of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:terminal-marked-voice>; the
  threshold is produced by the estimate at the end of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:terminal-marked-construction>. At stage
  <math|j> add the nonnegative parts of these thresholds over all grades and
  all profile indices through <math|j>, and include the sum <math|k<rsub|j>>
  in the entry delay of stage <math|j>. Since the entry condition at level
  <math|m> is imposed at the stage <math|J*<around|(|m+1|)>\<geqslant\>J<around|(|m|)>>,
  every schedule paying the delays satisfies
  <math|k<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>>, and
  therefore the fee inequality at every level <math|m> for every grade
  <math|d\<leqslant\>J<around|(|m|)>>. The fee concerns the local section of
  the family, with the family's label frames; it is fixed before the block
  lengths.

  The second and third concern the marked portfolio. Its sections and marked
  families are fixed first, before the profiles' price maps, the block floors
  and all later choices. For this fixed portfolio,
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-marked-portfolio-calibration> produces a
  table of nonnegative credits and stage thresholds <math|n<rsub|i>> on the
  block length, and an entrance delay. Include the thresholds
  <math|n<rsub|i>> among the block floors of stage <math|i>, together with
  the floors already listed; this is the comparison of thresholds with floor
  lengths which that section requires of its caller. Include its entrance
  delay among the entry delays, after the protection parameters and before
  the final delay is selected. By the argument given there, every schedule
  paying these delays then satisfies, at every transition from stage <math|j>
  to stage <math|i> and every coupling <math|x> beyond the entry delay of
  stage <math|j>, the profile-cost bound, the credit bounds for all grades
  <math|d\<leqslant\>i>, and the tail inequality
  <eqref|eq:ex-marked-height-jump>. The purchase records both facts together
  with the portfolio: the credit table with thresholds not exceeding the
  selected block floors, and the delay condition for the selected delays.
  Neither requires a choice after the block lengths other than the
  enlargement of the entry delays.

  These three requirements are finite maxima of thresholds at each stage and
  are stable under further enlargement, so they are compatible with the
  common choice described above.

  This supplies the additional local purchases needed to apply the
  one-history transfer constructions. The passage from those local purchases
  to the complete centered service and the ultraviolet field family still
  requires their simultaneous global assembly.

  <subsection|Producing and symmetrizing the marked
  campaign><label|sec:marked-campaign-producer>

  This section supplies the campaign used by the fixed-stencil construction.
  It has two parts. First, the equation ideal is read by microscopic link
  observables whose Taylor errors lie strictly above the requested grade.
  Second, finite-dimensional linear algebra combines these reads into one
  campaign, and finite group averages impose the signed and real symmetries.
  The filtration throughout counts factors that are color coordinates of
  based curvature slots; it is not a count of raw link entries.

  <subsubsection|The microscopic filtration and its Taylor data>

  Fix a gauge group, a cutoff degree <math|d>, and a family of test
  holonomies: for every radial jet <math|J> and every mesh <math|t>, link
  variables <math|U<rsub|d>*<around|(|J;t|)>> on the cover lattice solving
  the link transport equation of the truncated radial connection of <math|J>
  at mesh <math|t>, as in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-sections>.
  Since the degree-<math|\<ell\>> component <math|A<rsub|\<ell\>>> of
  <math|J> is a homogeneous one-form of degree <math|\<ell\>+1>, these are
  the mesh-one holonomies <math|U<rsub|d><around|(|J<rsub|t>|)>=U<rsub|d>*<around|(|J<rsub|t>;1|)>>
  of the jet <math|J<rsub|t>> whose component <math|A<rsub|\<ell\>>> is
  multiplied by <math|t<rsup|\<ell\>+2>>. The link-action derivatives of a
  field function <math|f> are the derivatives at <math|t=0> of
  <math|t\<mapsto\>f*<around|(|U<rsub|d>*<around|(|J;t|)>|)>>, for every
  <math|J>. A slot <math|S> is one of the based, transported
  plaquette-curvature readouts on the cover lattice. For a real basis vector
  <math|e<rsub|a>> of the color space, write

  <\equation*>
    x<rsub|S,a><around|(|U|)>=e<rsub|a><math-plus|\<cdot\>>S<around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|\<geqslant\>r>> be the complex span of finite
  products of at least <math|r> such coordinates, with the empty product
  allowed when <math|r=0>. Thus a single slot coordinate has filtration
  degree one even though, as a function of link matrices, it is a nonlinear
  polynomial or analytic expression.

  For a label <math|p> of grade <math|m=<around|(|r,s|)>>, a marked read
  consists of a field function <math|f> and a polynomial <math|q> with three
  properties. The function is gauge invariant, has at least <math|s> slot
  factors in the span sense above, and its link-action derivatives through
  order <math|d> agree with those of <math|q>: for every jet <math|J> the
  function <math|t\<mapsto\>f*<around|(|U<rsub|d>*<around|(|J;t|)>|)>> is
  <math|d> times continuously differentiable at <math|0>, and its derivatives
  of order at most <math|d> there equal those of the polynomial
  <math|t\<mapsto\>q<around|(|J<rsub|t>|)>>. Here <math|q> has degree at most
  <math|d> in the first grade; it is then determined by <math|f>. The
  correction <math|q-p> has strictly larger grade stamp

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|st><around|(|r,s|)>=<around|(|s,r|)>
  </equation*>

  in lexicographic order. When <math|p> lies in the Maxwell equation ideal,
  the read <math|f> is additionally required to be microscopic. A function
  <math|f> of the cover links is called <em|microscopic> if it is continuous
  and there are a radius <math|\<varrho\>> and a side threshold <math|M> with
  the following property. For every torus of side at least <math|M>, every
  site <math|o> of it, every real <math|\<beta\>\<ne\>0>, and every bounded
  measurable function <math|S> of the torus configuration which depends only
  on links both of whose endpoints have distance greater than
  <math|\<varrho\>> from <math|o>,

  <\equation*>
    <big|int>f<around|(|U<rsup|o>|)>*<space|0.17em>S<around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|\<beta\>><around|(|U|)>=0,
  </equation*>

  where <math|U<rsup|o>> is the torus configuration read on the cover from
  the anchor <math|o> and <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<beta\>>> is the Wilson measure.
  Neither gauge invariance nor continuity of the spectator <math|S> is
  required, and <math|\<varrho\>,M> do not depend on <math|\<beta\>>,
  <math|o>, <math|S> or the side. Microscopic functions with given
  <math|<around|(|\<varrho\>,M|)>> form a complex vector space, and
  <math|<around|(|\<varrho\>,M|)>> may be enlarged. These are precisely the
  properties needed for later stencil pricing and for the exact
  equation-ideal payment.

  We name the two objects produced in this section. A <em|campaign> at cutoff
  <math|d> is a complex-linear map <math|F:p\<mapsto\><around|(|q<rsub|p>,f<rsub|p>|)>>
  from the label space into pairs consisting of a label and a function of the
  cover links, such that <math|F<around|(|1|)>=<around|(|1,1|)>> and, for
  every grade <math|m\<in\><around|{|0,\<ldots\>,d|}><rsup|2>> and every
  label <math|p> of pure grade <math|m>, the pair
  <math|<around|(|f<rsub|p>,q<rsub|p>|)>> is a marked read of <math|p> in the
  above sense, microscopic whenever <math|p> lies in the equation ideal. An
  <em|imprint> is a campaign which commutes with the signed coordinate
  changes and with the real structure; both actions are written out in the
  proof of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:marked-campaign-producer>.

  The elementary analytic input is a first-order germ for each slot. Put
  <math|<math-up|Snap><rsub|d>> for the finite-dimensional space of radial
  link jets through degree <math|d>. For every slot <math|S>, the function

  <\equation*>
    J\<longmapsto\>S<around*|(|U<rsub|d><around|(|J|)>|)>,<space|2em>J\<in\><math-up|Snap><rsub|d>,
  </equation*>

  is real analytic near zero, transforms by the adjoint action under a
  constant gauge transformation at the base point, vanishes at zero, and has
  derivative equal to the linearized plaquette curvature. To see the
  analyticity directly, on one link the connection coefficient is a linear
  function <math|A<rsub|J><around|(|t|)>> of <math|J>. On the Banach space
  <math|C*<around|(|<around|[|0,1|]>,\<frak-A\>|)>> of continuous paths in
  the finite-dimensional matrix algebra <math|\<frak-A\>> containing the
  group representation, the Volterra operator

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|T<rsub|J>*u|)><around|(|t|)>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t>u<around|(|s|)>*A<rsub|J><around|(|s|)>*<space|0.17em>d*s
  </equation*>

  has norm at most <math|sup<rsub|s><around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|J><around|(|s|)>|\<\|\|\>>>.
  In a neighborhood of <math|J=0> this is less than one, so the unique
  solution of the link equation is

  <\equation*>
    u<rsub|J>=<around|(|I-T<rsub|J>|)><rsup|-1>*<with|font-series|bold|1>=<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>0>T<rsub|J><rsup|n>*<with|font-series|bold|1>.
  </equation*>

  The series converges locally uniformly in the finite-dimensional parameter
  <math|J>, and hence is analytic there. Uniqueness identifies it with the
  given link transport. A slot is obtained by a finite product of these
  transports and their inverses, followed by the plaquette curvature map; it
  is therefore analytic as well. Constant-gauge covariance follows by
  conjugating the ODE, and differentiating at <math|J=0> gives the signed sum
  of the edge tangents around the plaquette, which is the linearized
  curvature. This proves the required slot germ without imposing any
  regularity on a configuration space of infinite link fields.

  <subsubsection|Avoiding one edge while spanning Taylor tests>

  The next finite-dimensional fact is the source of the locality needed for
  products. Regard <math|<math-up|Snap><rsub|d>> itself as the real vector
  space <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|d>> of radial jets. For a slot <math|S>, let

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|S>=D<rsub|0>*<around*|(|J\<mapsto\>S<around|(|U<rsub|d><around|(|J|)>|)>|)>:<math-up|Snap><rsub|d>\<to\>\<frak-g\>.
  </equation*>

  If <math|e> is a prescribed cover edge, call a slot <math|S> safe for
  <math|e> if its spatial support avoids <math|e> along the translated path
  used to define <math|S>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Safe Taylor tests span><label|lem:marked-safe-span> For each edge
    <math|e>, the real linear span of the component functionals of
    <math|B<rsub|S>>, over slots <math|S> safe for <math|e>, is all of
    <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|d><rsup|\<ast\>>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For <math|d\<less\>2> the radial-jet space has no Taylor components and
    the claim is immediate. Assume <math|d\<geqslant\>2>. Take a jet
    <math|A\<in\><math-up|Snap><rsub|d>> annihilated by every listed
    functional. For every pair of axes <math|i\<less\>j>, the safe-slot
    geometry excludes only a finite set of plaquette base points. At each
    other base point it supplies a detour transport path avoiding <math|e>.
    The hypothesis says the resulting slot derivative on <math|A> is zero.
    The slot-germ identity therefore gives the full sampled curl equation
    below, in which <math|x<rsub|y>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>> denotes the
    position of the lattice point <math|y>. Here
    <math|C<rsub|i*j><around|(|A<rsub|\<ell\>>|)><around|(|x,t|)>> denotes
    the axes-<math|<around|(|i,j|)>> finite-mesh curl component of the
    degree-<math|\<ell\>> Taylor field at position <math|x> and mesh
    <math|t>. It is the function <math|N<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|x,t|)>> of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-sections> for the axes <math|i,j>:
    it is jointly continuous, equals the curl component
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|i>*A<rsub|\<ell\>,j>-\<partial\><rsub|j>*A<rsub|\<ell\>,i>>
    at <math|t=0>, and is homogeneous of degree <math|\<ell\>> under
    simultaneous scaling, <math|C<rsub|i*j><around|(|A<rsub|\<ell\>>|)><around|(|t*x,t|)>=t<rsup|\<ell\>>*C<rsub|i*j><around|(|A<rsub|\<ell\>>|)><around|(|x,1|)>>.

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|\<ell\>=0><rsup|d-2>C<rsub|i*j><around|(|A<rsub|\<ell\>>|)><around|(|x<rsub|y>,1|)>=0
    </equation*>

    at every lattice point <math|y> outside the finite exceptional set. We
    now induct downwards: once shells above <math|k> have been shown to
    vanish, this same equation reduces to its sum over
    <math|0\<leqslant\>\<ell\>\<leqslant\>k>. For <math|t\<gtr\>0> define

    <\equation*>
      F<rsub|k>*<around|(|x,t|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<ell\>=0><rsup|k>t<rsup|k-\<ell\>>*C<rsub|i*j><around|(|A<rsub|\<ell\>>|)><around|(|x,t|)>.
    </equation*>

    Homogeneity of the degree-<math|\<ell\>> curl component gives

    <\equation*>
      F<rsub|k>*<around|(|t*x<rsub|y>,t|)>=t<rsup|k>*<big|sum><rsub|\<ell\>=0><rsup|k>C<rsub|i*j><around|(|A<rsub|\<ell\>>|)><around|(|x<rsub|y>,1|)>=0.
    </equation*>

    Choose lattice sites <math|y<rsub|t>> with
    <math|t*x<rsub|y<rsub|t>>\<to\>x> as <math|t*\<downarrow\>*0>. For
    <math|x\<ne\>0> the finite exceptional set is avoided for all
    sufficiently small <math|t>. Continuity of <math|F<rsub|k>> gives
    <math|F<rsub|k>*<around|(|x,0|)>=C<rsub|i*j><around|(|A<rsub|k>|)><around|(|x,0|)>=0>,
    since every lower-shell term has a positive power of <math|t> at
    <math|t=0>. Continuity extends this identity to <math|x=0>. Thus the top
    coefficient has zero curl. For a homogeneous radial-gauge potential of
    degree <math|k+1>, this forces the potential to vanish directly.
    Differentiate its radial-gauge identity
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|j>x<rsub|j>*A<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>=0> in coordinate
    <math|i> and use curl-freeness and Euler's identity:

    <\equation*>
      0=A<rsub|i>+<big|sum><rsub|j>x<rsub|j>*\<partial\><rsub|i>*A<rsub|j>=A<rsub|i>+<big|sum><rsub|j>x<rsub|j>*\<partial\><rsub|j>*A<rsub|i>=<around|(|k+2|)>*A<rsub|i>.
    </equation*>

    Hence <math|A<rsub|i>=0> for every coordinate, so the top shell vanishes.
    Remove it from the sampled equation and repeat with the next lower index.
    Descending induction proves <math|A=0>. In a finite-dimensional space a
    family of functionals spans the dual exactly when its common kernel is
    zero, which proves the claim.
  </proof>

  The slot linearization in this proof is concrete. For a based plaquette
  readout it is the oriented sum of the four link tangents, transported to
  the base point; the ODE calculation above proves this formula for the
  actual link action. Only finitely many plaquette base points are excluded;
  at each remaining point the detour path is chosen separately. The radial
  argument therefore applies to every Taylor test, not just to tests
  supported on a generic set.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Equation-factor and spectator reads><label|lem:marked-factor-read>
    Let <math|a> be a homogeneous polynomial in the equation ideal, of grade
    <math|m=<around|(|r,s|)>> with <math|r\<leqslant\>d>. Then <math|a> is a
    finite linear combination of terms

    <\equation*>
      v=\<lambda\>*<space|0.17em><big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>z<rsub|i>,
    </equation*>

    each of grade <math|m>, where <math|\<lambda\>> is a linear functional on
    one Taylor layer annihilating the Maxwell solution-potential subspace of
    that layer and each <math|z<rsub|i>> is a linear functional on one Taylor
    layer (a linear label atom). Their field reads are assembled from
    slot-coordinate observables. Each term <math|v> has a raw read <math|f>
    and a polynomial <math|q> of first grade at most <math|d> such that the
    link-action derivatives of <math|f> through order <math|d> agree with
    those of <math|q>, the difference <math|q-v> has strictly larger grade
    stamp than <math|m>, <math|f\<in\>\<cal-P\><rsub|\<geqslant\>s>>, and
    <math|f> is microscopic. Individual terms need not be gauge invariant.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Write <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|j>> for a projection onto the space of radial
    potentials obtained from on-shell Maxwell curvatures at order <math|j>,
    and let <math|\<cal-E\>> act coefficientwise. By definition, a label
    belongs to the equation ideal when it vanishes on every jet all of whose
    layers are such solution potentials. The equation ideal is therefore the
    kernel of the substitution <math|b\<mapsto\><math-up|changed><around|(|\<cal-E\>|)>*b>,
    which replaces every literal atom <math|u> by
    <math|\<cal-E\>*u=u\<circ\>\<cal-E\>>: the value of
    <math|<math-up|changed><around|(|\<cal-E\>|)>*b> at a jet <math|J> is
    <math|b*<around|(|\<cal-E\>*J|)>>, the jets <math|\<cal-E\>*J> are
    exactly the solution jets, and a polynomial vanishing at every jet is
    zero. Since <math|a> belongs to that ideal,

    <\equation*>
      a=a-<math-up|changed><around|(|\<cal-E\>|)>*a.
    </equation*>

    Expand <math|a> into monomials before applying the substitution. For a
    literal slot atom <math|u>, split <math|u=<around|(|u-\<cal-E\>*u|)>+\<cal-E\>*u>.
    The first summand annihilates every Maxwell solution potential. For a
    product <math|u<rsub|1>*\<cdots\>*u<rsub|k>>, use the telescoping
    identity

    <\equation*>
      <big|prod><rsub|j=1><rsup|k>u<rsub|j>-<big|prod><rsub|j=1><rsup|k>\<cal-E\>*u<rsub|j>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k><around|(|u<rsub|i>-\<cal-E\>*u<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j\<less\>i>\<cal-E\>*u<rsub|j>|)><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j\<gtr\>i>u<rsub|j>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The projection preserves the radial grade. Thus every summand has one
    solution-annihilating linear factor and a finite spectator product, with
    the original total grade. This proves the displayed factorization
    directly from the equation-ideal definition; no invariant-factor
    assumption is made.

    Fix one generator <math|v=\<lambda\>*<big|prod><rsub|i>z<rsub|i>>, and
    let <math|j> be the Taylor layer on which <math|\<lambda\>> acts, so that
    <math|\<lambda\>> has grade <math|<around|(|j+2,1|)>>. The functional
    <math|\<lambda\>> annihilates the Maxwell solution-potential subspace of
    that layer. The required duality statement concerns only the top Taylor
    layer of the jets through cutoff <math|j+2>, that is, of
    <math|A=<around|(|A<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,A<rsub|j>|)>>. A positive path
    force is the linearization at the zero jet of a color component of the
    force at a positive link <math|<around|(|y,\<nu\>|)>>, transported to the
    anchor along a walk which ends at <math|y> and does not traverse that
    link; such a walk exists for every link. For a homogeneous potential
    <math|A<rsub|\<ell\>>>, an axis pair <math|p=<around|(|a,c|)>> with
    <math|a\<less\>c> and an axis <math|b>, define the transverse plaquette
    difference by

    <\equation*>
      T<rsub|\<ell\>>*<around|(|p,b;x|)>=N<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|p;x,1|)>-N<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|p;x-e<rsub|b>,1|)>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|N<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|p;x,t|)>=C<rsub|a*c><around|(|A<rsub|\<ell\>>|)><around|(|x,t|)>>
    is the sampled curl of the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:marked-safe-span>
    on the plaquette with axes <math|p> based at <math|x>. The plaquette
    incidence in direction <math|\<nu\>> selects
    <math|T<rsub|\<ell\>>*<around|(|p,c;x|)>> if <math|a=\<nu\>>,
    <math|-T<rsub|\<ell\>>*<around|(|p,a;x|)>> if <math|c=\<nu\>>, and zero
    otherwise; write <math|<wide|T|^><rsub|\<ell\>>*<around|(|\<nu\>,p;x|)>>
    for the result, the transverse force density. By the slot-germ identity,
    the linearized force at the link <math|<around|(|y,\<nu\>|)>> is
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<ell\>=0><rsup|j><big|sum><rsub|p><wide|T|^><rsub|\<ell\>>*<around|(|\<nu\>,p;x<rsub|y>|)>>
    on the jet <math|A>, whatever the walk. The corresponding line term
    <math|L<rsub|\<ell\>>*<around|(|\<nu\>,p;x,t|)>> is the same incidence
    sum of the oriented arc; each arc is the difference of the two ordered
    triple averages of <math|\<partial\><rsub|b>*\<partial\><rsub|a>*A<rsub|\<ell\>,c>>
    and <math|\<partial\><rsub|b>*\<partial\><rsub|c>*A<rsub|\<ell\>,a>> over
    the mesh-<math|t> plaquette and the preceding edge in direction <math|b>.
    It is jointly continuous in <math|<around|(|x,t|)>>. The fundamental
    theorem of calculus in the direction <math|b>, the homogeneity of
    <math|N<rsub|\<ell\>>>, and evaluation at <math|t=0> give the scaling and
    zero-mesh identities

    <\equation*>
      t*L<rsub|\<ell\>>*<around|(|\<nu\>,p;t*x,t|)>=t<rsup|\<ell\>>*<wide|T|^><rsub|\<ell\>>*<around|(|\<nu\>,p;x|)>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|p>L<rsub|\<ell\>>*<around|(|\<nu\>,p;x,0|)>=-<big|sum><rsub|b>\<partial\><rsub|b><math-up|curl><around|(|A<rsub|\<ell\>>|)><rsub|b*\<nu\>><around|(|x|)>.
    </equation*>

    If every positive path-force test vanishes on <math|A>, then at every
    lattice point <math|y> and for every axis <math|\<nu\>> the finite force
    sum <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<ell\>\<leqslant\>j><big|sum><rsub|p><wide|T|^><rsub|\<ell\>>*<around|(|\<nu\>,p;x<rsub|y>|)>>
    vanishes. Its line form is

    <\equation*>
      F<rsub|j>*<around|(|x,t|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<ell\>=0><rsup|j>t<rsup|j-\<ell\>>*<big|sum><rsub|p>L<rsub|\<ell\>>*<around|(|\<nu\>,p;x,t|)>.
    </equation*>

    The scaling identity shows that <math|t*F<rsub|j>*<around|(|t*x<rsub|y>,t|)>>
    is <math|t<rsup|j>> times the vanishing finite force sum. Since
    <math|t\<gtr\>0>, this implies <math|F<rsub|j>*<around|(|t*x<rsub|y>,t|)>=0>.
    For arbitrary real <math|x>, choose lattice points <math|y<rsub|t>> with
    <math|t*x<rsub|y<rsub|t>>\<to\>x>; here, unlike in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:marked-safe-span>, no lattice point is
    excluded. Let <math|t*\<downarrow\>*0> and use continuity. Only the top
    coefficient remains, and the zero-mesh identity gives
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|b>\<partial\><rsub|b><math-up|curl><around|(|A<rsub|j>|)><rsub|b*\<nu\>>=0>.
    The curl is also closed by the Bianchi identity, hence is an on-shell
    Maxwell curvature. Put <math|Q<rsub|a*b>=\<partial\><rsub|a>*A<rsub|j,b>-\<partial\><rsub|b>*A<rsub|j,a>>.
    The radial potential obtained from an on-shell curvature <math|Q> of this
    degree is its radial contraction <math|<around|(|j+2|)><rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|a>x<rsub|a>*Q<rsub|a*b>>.
    For the curl of <math|A<rsub|j>> this contraction is <math|A<rsub|j>>
    itself: the radial identity <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>x<rsub|a>*A<rsub|j,a>=0>,
    differentiated in <math|x<rsub|b>>, gives
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>x<rsub|a>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*A<rsub|j,a>=-A<rsub|j,b>>,
    and Euler's identity for the homogeneous degree <math|j+1> gives
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>x<rsub|a>*\<partial\><rsub|a>*A<rsub|j,b>=<around|(|j+1|)>*A<rsub|j,b>>,
    so that

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|a>x<rsub|a>*Q<rsub|a*b>=<around|(|j+1|)>*A<rsub|j,b>+A<rsub|j,b>=<around|(|j+2|)>*A<rsub|j,b>.
    </equation*>

    Thus <math|A<rsub|j>> is the radial potential of the on-shell curvature
    <math|Q>, that is, it lies in the image of the Maxwell potential map.

    Now <math|\<lambda\>> is a linear functional on the top Taylor layer
    annihilating that solution-potential image. It vanishes on
    <math|A<rsub|j>> whenever the positive force tests vanish on <math|A>.
    Thus its composition with projection onto the top Taylor layer
    annihilates the common kernel of the force family. Finite-dimensional
    duality puts this composed functional in the span of the positive path
    forces. This is the force representation used to construct the
    equation-factor read; no statement about the full force-kernel in all
    Taylor layers is needed.

    The equation-factor atom <math|\<lambda\>> has grade
    <math|<around|(|j+2,1|)>>; write <math|z=<big|prod><rsub|i>z<rsub|i>> for
    the spectator word, whose grade is the sum of the grades of its factors,
    so that <math|m> is the sum of these two grades and <math|s=k+1>. At the
    intermediate cutoff <math|j+2>, the force representation expresses
    <math|\<lambda\>>, composed with the projection to the top layer, as a
    finite real combination <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<gamma\>>c<rsub|\<gamma\>>*\<phi\><rsub|\<gamma\>>>
    of positive path forces. Each <math|\<phi\><rsub|\<gamma\>>> is given by
    a link, a walk avoiding it and a color index; these data define a
    transported force function <math|g<rsub|\<gamma\>>> of the cover links,
    and also the linearization <math|<wide|\<phi\>|~><rsub|\<gamma\>>> of
    <math|g<rsub|\<gamma\>>> on jets through the requested cutoff <math|d>,
    which restricts to <math|\<phi\><rsub|\<gamma\>>> on jets through
    <math|j+2>. Put <math|<wide|\<lambda\>|~>=<big|sum><rsub|\<gamma\>>c<rsub|\<gamma\>>*<wide|\<phi\>|~><rsub|\<gamma\>>>.
    The force is a signed sum of slot coordinates, so replacing each
    linearized curvature by its actual slot read gives
    <math|g<rsub|\<gamma\>>> a Taylor polynomial through order <math|d> which
    differs from <math|<wide|\<phi\>|~><rsub|\<gamma\>>> by terms of slot
    filtration at least two. The spectator word is read separately for each
    force term <math|\<gamma\>>, by induction on its length: for each factor
    <math|z<rsub|i>>, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:marked-safe-span> supplies a
    combination of slot coordinates, all safe for the force link of
    <math|\<gamma\>>, whose linearization is <math|z<rsub|i>>, and the
    product <math|h<rsub|\<gamma\>>> of these combinations lies in
    <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|\<geqslant\>k>>. The product rule for Taylor
    polynomials then puts every error term of <math|h<rsub|\<gamma\>>> at
    least one slot above the spectator's own factor count <math|k>. The raw
    read is <math|f=<big|sum><rsub|\<gamma\>>c<rsub|\<gamma\>>*g<rsub|\<gamma\>>*h<rsub|\<gamma\>>\<in\>\<cal-P\><rsub|\<geqslant\>k+1>>.
    Each summand is a transported force at a link times the continuous
    function <math|h<rsub|\<gamma\>>>. Safety is a statement about cover
    edges; the one-link identity needs it after periodization, for every
    anchor. For each <math|\<gamma\>>, collect the finitely many cover edges
    traversed by the transport walk of the force and by the walks and
    plaquettes of all slots occurring in <math|h<rsub|\<gamma\>>>. None of
    them is the force edge. Since the set is finite, there is a side
    threshold beyond which none of these edges is congruent to the force edge
    modulo the side; translation by an anchor preserves these inequalities.
    Hence, on every torus of at least that side and for every anchor, the
    periodized transport and the periodized <math|h<rsub|\<gamma\>>> are
    unchanged when the translated force link alone is varied. Each summand is
    therefore microscopic by the one-link identity proved after this lemma;
    taking the largest of the finitely many radii and thresholds, <math|f> is
    microscopic.

    It remains to compare the Taylor polynomial <math|w> of <math|f> through
    order <math|d> with the generator <math|v=\<lambda\>*z>. The functional
    <math|<wide|\<lambda\>|~>> extends the intermediate Taylor functional to
    the full jet through the requested cutoff <math|d\<geqslant\>j+2>; it is
    multiplied by the unchanged spectator word. The extension has two
    distinct errors. First, the discrepancy between the constructed read and
    its own lifted Taylor polynomial has one more slot factor than the
    target's second grade. Second, the lifted linear functional agrees with
    the original equation atom on the intermediate jet; its remaining Taylor
    tail has strictly higher first grade while retaining the same slot count.
    Multiplying this tail by the spectator word preserves the strict
    first-grade increase. Thus the required comparison is the sum

    <\equation*>
      w-v=<around*|(|w-<math-up|Taylor><rsub|\<leqslant\>d><around|(|<wide|\<lambda\>|~>*<space|0.17em>z|)>|)>+<around*|(|<math-up|Taylor><rsub|\<leqslant\>d><around|(|<wide|\<lambda\>|~>*<space|0.17em>z|)>-v|)>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|<math-up|Taylor><rsub|\<leqslant\>d>> discards the monomials
    of first grade above <math|d>. The first term is strictly later in the
    slot-count coordinate of the grade stamp; the second is strictly later in
    the first-grade coordinate. Both are therefore strictly above the target
    stamp. This two-part comparison, together with the forced edge read,
    gives the required positioned read for the generator, with <math|q=w>.
    Finite sums and scalar multiples extend it to the full homogeneous ideal
    element.

    The reads in this lemma are raw reads; gauge averaging is done only after
    the terms for the invariant label have been assembled.
  </proof>

  The conditional moment calculation in this argument is the one-link Haar
  integration-by-parts identity. For a walk <math|w> from the anchor to the
  base point of a source edge <math|e>, not traversing <math|e>, the
  transported force is

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|Ad><rsub|<math-up|travel><around|(|U,0,w|)>><space|0.17em><math-up|field><around|(|U,e|)>.
  </equation*>

  Move the transport to the other slot using invariance of the color inner
  product, then expand in a real orthonormal color basis
  <math|<around|(|E<rsub|a>|)>>. Every term has the form

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<langle\>|E<rsub|a>,<math-up|field><around|(|U,e|)>|\<rangle\>>*<space|0.17em>S<rsub|a><around|(|U|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|S<rsub|a>> is the spectator times a coefficient depending only
  on the walk transport. For the translated test at anchor <math|o>, choose
  the Micro radius at least the distance of the base point of the source edge
  from the anchor, and choose the side threshold at least two, so large that
  this base point does not wrap and that the walk transport and the remaining
  factor of the read are independent of the translated source link in the
  sense just described. The test spectator is then local outside that radius
  and is unchanged when only the translated source link is varied. Haar
  integration by parts on that link gives

  <\equation*>
    <big|int><around*|(|\<partial\><rsub|E<rsub|a>>*S<rsub|a>-\<beta\>*<around|\<langle\>|E<rsub|a>,<math-up|field><around|(|U,e|)>|\<rangle\>>*S<rsub|a>|)>*d*\<mu\><rsub|\<beta\>><around|(|U|)>=0.
  </equation*>

  The derivative of the bare density is the displayed force term. The
  envelope bound supplies an integrable majorant for differentiating under
  the integral, so the identity also holds for the bounded measurable
  spectators permitted in the Micro definition. The derivative term vanishes,
  because neither the walk transport (the walk does not traverse <math|e>),
  nor the remaining factor of the read, nor the test spectator depends on the
  varied link. The identity therefore reads
  <math|\<beta\>*<big|int><around|\<langle\>|E<rsub|a>,<math-up|field><around|(|U,e|)>|\<rangle\>>*S<rsub|a>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|\<beta\>>=0>.
  Dividing by <math|\<beta\>> and summing over <math|a> gives the force
  moment zero. The integration-by-parts identity is valid for every real
  <math|\<beta\>>; the condition <math|\<beta\>\<ne\>0> in the definition of
  a microscopic function is used only in this division.

  <subparagraph|Scope of the factor lemma.> The force calculation used is
  only the top-component implication proved from the radial current limit:
  simultaneous vanishing of the positive path-force tests puts the highest
  radial coefficient in the image of the Maxwell potential map. The
  conditional one-link Haar identity is the moment input defining the
  equation leg. The argument above proves the monomial factorization,
  edge-avoiding spectator assembly, and two-part filtration comparison from
  these facts.

  <subsubsection|The all-grade campaign>

  The previous lemmas give the exact pointwise producer needed for the
  finite-dimensional assembly.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Positioned equation-ideal read><label|prop:marked-positioned> For
    every pure-grade label <math|p> in the Maxwell equation ideal, of grade
    <math|m=<around|(|r,s|)>> with <math|r\<leqslant\>d>, there are a
    gauge-invariant function <math|f> and a label <math|q> such that <math|f>
    has slot degree at least <math|s>, the action derivatives through order
    <math|d> of <math|f> and <math|q> agree, <math|q-p> has grade stamp
    strictly above that of <math|m>, and <math|f> is microscopic. Thus
    <math|<around|(|f,q|)>> is a microscopic marked read of <math|p>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Use the exact ideal factorization to write <math|p> as a finite sum of
    gap atoms times spectator monomials. Apply the factor-read lemma to those
    generators and add the resulting raw reads. Their polynomial targets sum
    to <math|p>; their errors remain in the strictly higher-stamp subspace,
    and the slot-degree and microscopic conditions pass to finite sums. The
    resulting raw function need not yet be gauge invariant.

    Now average the pair over the compact gauge group at the anchor. A
    constant gauge transformation rotates the color components of each of the
    finitely many fixed slot addresses among a finite basis. Thus the orbit
    of this particular finite sum lies in a finite-dimensional span, and its
    Haar integral is an ordinary finite-dimensional vector integral. The
    label <math|p> is gauge invariant, so the target of every rotated read is
    still <math|p>; its Taylor correction remains in the same strictly higher
    stamp subspace, because the rotation acts on each Taylor layer and
    preserves both grades. Each rotated read is again microscopic with the
    same radius and threshold: the Wilson measure is invariant under the
    constant gauge transformation, and composing a spectator with it
    preserves its locality, measurability and bound. The integral of a
    continuous map into a finite-dimensional space against a probability
    measure lies in the linear span of its values. Applying this to the orbit
    of the pair <math|<around|(|1,f|)>>, whose first entry is constant, shows
    that the average is a finite linear combination of rotated reads with
    coefficients summing to one. Forms, slot filtration, the stamp condition
    relative to the fixed target <math|p>, and the microscopic moment
    condition are preserved by such combinations. A based transported slot
    under an arbitrary local gauge transformation changes only by the adjoint
    action of that gauge transformation at its base point: the interior
    transports cancel in pairs. Averaging over the anchor group therefore
    makes the read invariant under the full local gauge action. Finally the
    polynomial <math|q> attached to the averaged read is itself gauge
    invariant, hence a label: for a constant gauge transformation <math|g>,
    the rotated polynomial has the same Taylor forms as <math|f\<circ\>g=f>,
    and the polynomial with given forms through order <math|d> is unique.
    This produces the required <math|f> and <math|q>.
  </proof>

  The proposition applies to the actual action, rather than to a formal
  substitute: the slot germ is obtained from the link ODE above. Its
  one-factor linearization is the oriented plaquette sum. The
  finite-generation formula for the ideal is obtained degree by degree by
  expanding each polynomial into monomials, applying the projection onto the
  Maxwell solution-potential subspace, and splitting one literal equation
  factor from the remaining slot coordinates. The safe-span annihilator proof
  handles the edge avoidance needed to multiply the spectators. These steps
  establish the positioned read.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Campaign and imprint at fixed cutoff><label|thm:marked-campaign-producer>Assume
    at cutoff <math|d> there is a complex-linear map <math|S> from labels to
    link-field functions with unit value one, gauge-invariant values, and
    exact action Taylor forms through order <math|d>. In addition, for each
    pure-grade basis label of grade <math|<around|(|r,s|)>>, assume its value
    has a finite representation as a linear combination of products of based
    curvature-slot color coordinates, each product having at least <math|s>
    factors. This is the powered slot-degree condition used below. Then there
    is a campaign <math|F> at cutoff <math|d> in the sense defined above:
    <math|F> is complex linear, <math|F<around|(|1|)>=<around|(|1,1|)>>, and
    for every pure-grade label <math|p> of grade <math|m=<around|(|r,s|)>>
    with <math|r,s\<leqslant\>d> the value
    <math|F<around|(|p|)>=<around|(|q<rsub|p>,f<rsub|p>|)>> consists of a
    gauge-invariant function <math|f<rsub|p>> of slot degree at least
    <math|s> and a label <math|q<rsub|p>> with the same action Taylor forms
    through order <math|d>, such that <math|q<rsub|p>-p> has strictly larger
    stamp than <math|m>, and such that <math|f<rsub|p>> is microscopic
    whenever <math|p> lies in the equation ideal. The campaign may be chosen
    to be an imprint, that is, to commute with signed coordinate changes and
    the real structure.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    We first construct the unsymmetrized campaign. The sound section itself
    provides a linear map <math|X> with

    <\equation*>
      X*p=<around|(|p,S<around|(|p|)>|)>,<space|2em>X*1=<around|(|1,1|)>.
    </equation*>

    It gives the required action forms, gauge invariance, and slot degree for
    each pure grade. It need not be microscopic on the equation ideal.

    For a fixed grade <math|m>, restrict the input <math|p> to
    <math|<math-up|Grade><around|(|m|)>> and let

    <\equation*>
      P<rsub|m>=<math-up|Grade><around|(|m|)>\<cap\>\<cal-I\><rsub|d>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>> is the finite-dimensional Maxwell equation
    ideal. Choose a basis <math|<around|(|b<rsub|i>|)>> of <math|P<rsub|m>>.
    For each <math|b<rsub|i>>, apply Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:marked-positioned>
    and write its read as <math|<around|(|q<rsub|i>,f<rsub|i>|)>>. Extend
    <math|b<rsub|i>\<mapsto\><around|(|q<rsub|i>,f<rsub|i>|)>> linearly on
    <math|P<rsub|m>>. The result is a payment map on the ideal part of this
    grade: forms, strict-stamp correction, powered membership, and the Micro
    conclusion are all linear conditions and hence pass through the basis
    expansion.

    Choose a linear projection <math|L<rsub|m>> from the full label space
    onto <math|P<rsub|m>> which restricts to the identity on
    <math|P<rsub|m>>. If <math|X<rsub|m>> is the map just constructed on
    <math|P<rsub|m>> and <math|X> is the sound-section map above, set

    <\equation*>
      F<rsub|m><around|(|p|)>=X<rsub|m>*<around|(|L<rsub|m>*p|)>+X*<around|(|p-L<rsub|m>*p|)>.
    </equation*>

    Both summands have the required grade-<math|m> payment, so their sum does
    too. If this grade-<math|m> input <math|p> lies in the equation ideal,
    grade preservation of the ideal gives <math|L<rsub|m>*p\<in\>P<rsub|m>>
    and <math|p-L<rsub|m>*p\<in\>P<rsub|m>>. Since <math|L<rsub|m>> is the
    identity on <math|P<rsub|m>>, applying <math|L<rsub|m>> to
    <math|p-L<rsub|m>*p> gives zero and also <math|p-L<rsub|m>*p>; therefore
    <math|p-L<rsub|m>*p=0>. Thus for ideal inputs the entire value is the
    microscopic marked value <math|X<rsub|m><around|(|p|)>>. If
    <math|m=<around|(|0,0|)>>, the unit is not in the equation ideal: the
    ideal has no nonzero element of grade <math|<around|(|0,0|)>>, because
    such an element is a constant and every element of the ideal vanishes at
    the zero jet. Hence <math|P<rsub|<around|(|0,0|)>>=0>, so
    <math|L<rsub|m>*1=0> and the sound map supplies
    <math|F<rsub|m><around|(|1|)>=<around|(|1,1|)>>.

    There are only finitely many relevant grades,

    <\equation*>
      <around|{|0,\<ldots\>,d|}>\<times\><around|{|0,\<ldots\>,d|}>.
    </equation*>

    Decompose <math|p=<big|sum><rsub|m>p<rsub|m>> by its homogeneous grade
    and define <math|F<around|(|p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|m>F<rsub|m><around|(|p<rsub|m>|)>>.
    On an input of pure grade <math|m>, every other grade component vanishes,
    so the corresponding payment remains exact. On the unit only grade
    <math|<around|(|0,0|)>> contributes, so the start condition holds. This
    is the claimed campaign.

    Now symmetrize. Let <math|\<cal-S\>> be the finite signed coordinate
    permutation group. Its action <math|\<theta\><rsub|s>> on labels is the
    substitution <math|<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|s>*q|)><around|(|J|)>=q*<around|(|s<rsup|-1>*J|)>>
    of the rotated jet, and its joint action on a label/function pair is
    <math|\<phi\><rsub|s>*<around|(|q,f|)>=<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|s>*q,<space|0.17em>f\<circ\><math-up|pull><rsub|s>|)>>,
    where <math|<math-up|pull><rsub|s>> is the pullback of cover
    configurations under the signed lattice map <math|s> fixing the anchor.
    Define

    <\equation*>
      F<rsup|<math-up|sgn>>=<frac|1|<around|\||\<cal-S\>|\|>>*<big|sum><rsub|s\<in\>\<cal-S\>>\<phi\><rsub|s>*F*\<theta\><rsub|s<rsup|-1>>.
    </equation*>

    Signed changes preserve grade, the strictly higher-stamp subspace, the
    slot-product filtration, the action-form identity, and the equation
    ideal. They preserve the moment condition by changing variables in the
    finite volume measure. Thus every summand remains a campaign and the
    average is one. Reindexing by <math|s\<mapsto\>t*s> shows
    <math|F<rsup|<math-up|sgn>>*\<theta\><rsub|t>=\<phi\><rsub|t>*F<rsup|<math-up|sgn>>>.

    Let <math|c> be the joint real-conjugation involution on labels and
    label/function pairs: on labels it conjugates the coefficients of the
    polynomial in the real jet coordinates, and
    <math|c*<around|(|q,f|)>=<around|(|<wide|q|\<bar\>>,<wide|f|\<bar\>>|)>>.
    It is conjugate-linear, commutes with the signed action, and preserves
    each campaign condition. Set

    <\equation*>
      F<rsup|<math-up|imp>>=<tfrac|1|2><around*|(|F<rsup|<math-up|sgn>>+c*F<rsup|<math-up|sgn>>*c|)>.
    </equation*>

    The midpoint is complex-linear because the two conjugate-linear actions
    on the source and target cancel. It remains a campaign;
    <math|c<rsup|2>=1> gives real equivariance, while commutation of <math|c>
    with the signed action retains signed equivariance. Both averages fix the
    unit. This is the required imprint.
  </proof>

  The exact algebraic assembly above uses no limit in volume and no
  compactness argument. Its analytic input is the positioned read.
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:marked-positioned> supplies that input
  using the radial ODE germ, the finite Taylor annihilator argument, the
  equation-ideal generation formula, and the conditional-Haar microscopic
  identity above. The following corollary uses the section construction.

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Mixed grades><label|rem:marked-campaign-mixed> The campaign
    conditions are stated for labels of pure grade. For an arbitrary element
    <math|z> of the equation ideal, <math|F<around|(|z|)>=<big|sum><rsub|m>F<around|(|z<rsub|m>|)>>
    over its homogeneous components, and every <math|z<rsub|m>> again lies in
    the ideal, because the substitution <math|<math-up|changed><around|(|\<cal-E\>|)>>
    defining the ideal preserves both grades. Hence the function component of
    <math|F<around|(|z|)>> is a finite sum of microscopic functions. It is
    microscopic, with the largest of the finitely many radii and side
    thresholds.
  </remark>

  <\corollary>
    <dueto|Actual fixed-cutoff imprint><label|cor:marked-campaign-actual> For
    every gauge group and every cutoff <math|d>, and for test holonomies
    fixed as at the beginning of this section, the actual section
    construction yields a campaign and an imprint at that cutoff.
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    Use the local-sections construction of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-sections>. For cutoff <math|d> it
    gives the section <math|T<rsub|d>*F> and pose polynomials
    <math|P<rsub|\<alpha\>>> with exact action forms through order <math|d>,
    unit, and gauge covariance. Its pose certificate says that every monomial
    in the pose polynomial of a grade-<math|<around|(|r,s|)>> basis label has
    total factor degree at least <math|s>. Expanding the finite pose
    polynomial writes each such basis value as a finite sum of
    slot-coordinate products with at least <math|s> factors. Thus the powered
    slot-degree hypothesis of the theorem holds. Applying the theorem gives a
    campaign; its finite signed and real averages give the imprint.
  </proof>

  <subsection|A fixed marked section and its calibration
  budgets><label|sec:ex-marked-portfolio-calibration>

  The marked finite-volume route needs a single local section at each label
  degree. That section must be chosen before the volume, cutoff, or
  derivative budget is known. It must also price every later derivative
  request at that degree. We construct that section first, then show how the
  schedule selects its budgets and how the approximate field change is made
  invertible.

  <subsubsection|One section for every later derivative budget>

  Let <math|H> be the fixed holomorphic gauge action. At a fixed degree
  <math|d>, let <math|E<rsub|d>> be a finite-dimensional complex label space
  with a real homogeneous basis <math|<around|(|e<rsub|j>|)><rsub|j\<in\>J<rsub|d>>>.
  The basis contains the unit and has grades
  <math|<around|(|r<rsub|j>,s<rsub|j>|)>> with <math|r<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>d>.
  The algebra used here is not filtered by raw link-entry count. Let
  <math|\<frak-S\>> be the collection of based and transported curvature
  slots on the cover lattice, let <math|I> be the real color-coordinate
  basis, and put <math|<math-up|Chip>=\<frak-S\>\<times\>I>. For
  <math|c=<around|(|S,a|)>\<in\><math-up|Chip>> let
  <math|x<rsub|c><around|(|U|)>> be color coordinate <math|a> of curvature
  slot <math|S> at configuration <math|U>. Define
  <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|\<geqslant\>s>> as the complex span of the finite
  products <math|<big|prod><rsub|\<nu\>=1><rsup|m>x<rsub|c<rsub|\<nu\>>>>
  with <math|m\<geqslant\>s>. Thus one factor means one curvature-coordinate
  slot, even though it is itself polynomial in link entries. This is the
  source filtration called <math|<math-up|Powered>>.

  The input to the section construction is an imprint: linear maps
  <math|A<rsub|d>:E<rsub|d>\<to\>E<rsub|d>> and
  <math|B<rsub|d>:E<rsub|d>\<to\>\<cal-P\><rsub|\<geqslant\>0>> with unit
  normalization <math|A<rsub|d>*1=1>, <math|B<rsub|d>*1=1>, gauge invariance
  of <math|B<rsub|d>*p>, <math|<math-up|Forms><around|(|H,d,B<rsub|d>*p,A<rsub|d>*p|)>>,
  and <math|B<rsub|d>*p\<in\><math-up|Micro>> whenever <math|p> belongs to
  the equation ideal, together with the campaign's exact-action identity.
  They also have the following filtration clauses. The stamp of a grade is

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|st><around|(|r,s|)>=<around|(|s,r|)>,
  </equation*>

  and stamps are compared lexicographically in the displayed order. Say that
  a label has stamp at least <math|<math-up|st><around|(|r,s|)>>
  (respectively strictly above it) if each of its nonzero homogeneous
  components, of grade <math|<around|(|r<rprime|'>,s<rprime|'>|)>> say, has
  <math|<math-up|st><around|(|r<rprime|'>,s<rprime|'>|)>\<geqslant\><math-up|st><around|(|r,s|)>>
  (respectively <math|\<gtr\>>). If <math|p> has stamp at least
  <math|<math-up|st><around|(|r,s|)>>, then <math|A<rsub|d>*p-p> has stamp
  strictly above <math|<math-up|st><around|(|r,s|)>>, and
  <math|B<rsub|d>*p\<in\>\<cal-P\><rsub|\<geqslant\>s>>. By linearity it is
  equivalent to require these two clauses for homogeneous <math|p> of grade
  <math|<around|(|r,s|)>> only: a component of grade
  <math|<around|(|r<rprime|'>,s<rprime|'>|)>> with larger stamp has
  <math|s<rprime|'>\<geqslant\>s>, because increasing the stamp never lowers
  the factor coordinate, and <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|\<geqslant\>s<rprime|'>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><rsub|\<geqslant\>s>>.
  In addition, <math|A<rsub|d>> and <math|B<rsub|d>> each commute with the
  signed-coordinate action and the real structure; equivariance of only
  <math|B<rsub|d>> would not suffice to pass it through
  <math|A<rsub|d><rsup|-1>>. A campaign without these two equivariances is
  turned into such an imprint by the finite group and conjugation averages
  recalled next; the campaign producer itself is a separate input to this
  local theorem. It is constructed in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-campaign-producer>.

  For clarity, the symmetrization just mentioned is finite-dimensional. If
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|s>> is the joint signed action on labels and fields, and
  <math|\<frak-c\>> is the joint real-conjugation involution, put

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|0>=<frac|1|<around|\||\<cal-S\>|\|>>*<big|sum><rsub|s\<in\>\<cal-S\>>\<Phi\><rsub|s>*<space|0.17em>F*<space|0.17em>\<theta\><rsub|s<rsup|-1>>,<space|2em>F<rsub|1>=<frac|1|2>*<around*|(|F<rsub|0>+\<frak-c\>*F<rsub|0>*\<frak-c\>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<cal-S\>> is the signed-coordinate group. Each summand is
  still a campaign: the grade is preserved, the correction remains strictly
  above its stamp, the field remains in <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|\<geqslant\>s>>,
  and the unit and exact action identities are preserved. A finite average
  preserves these linear conditions. Reindexing the first sum by left
  multiplication shows that <math|F<rsub|0>> commutes with every signed
  action; the involution identity <math|\<frak-c\><rsup|2>=1> shows
  <math|F<rsub|1>> commutes with conjugation, and the two actions commute, so
  both equivariances hold for <math|F<rsub|1>>. Both averages fix the unit.
  This is the mathematical content of passing from a campaign to an imprint;
  the existence of the unsymmetrized campaign from the action is not reproved
  in this section.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Fixed-stencil marked portfolio from an
    imprint><label|thm:ex-marked-portfolio-section>Given an imprint on
    <math|E<rsub|d>>, there are a finite centered stencil <math|S<rsub|d>>, a
    linear section

    <\equation*>
      T<rsub|d>:E<rsub|d>\<longrightarrow\>\<bbb-C\><around|[|<text|link
      entries on >S<rsub|d>|]>,
    </equation*>

    and an invertible linear regrading <math|R<rsub|d>:E<rsub|d>\<to\>E<rsub|d>>
    such that

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|0\<in\>S<rsub|d>> and <math|S<rsub|d>> is invariant under
      signed coordinate permutations;

      <item>evaluating <math|T<rsub|d>> at the coordinates of
      <math|R<rsub|d>*p> on every link field gives <math|B<rsub|d>*p>
      exactly;

      <item><math|T<rsub|d>> is gauge invariant, has the correct unit, is
      compatible with signed pullbacks and conjugation, and has the
      prescribed action projection;

      <item>each basis entry has a finite pose supported in <math|S<rsub|d>>
      whose degree is at least <math|s<rsub|j>>;

      <item>for every derivative order <math|k> and power <math|P>, there is
      a finite number <math|C<rsub|d,k,P>> such that each basis entry has
      section rate <math|C<rsub|d,k,P>> at factor degree <math|s<rsub|j>>.
      This is a bound on the healthy part of the translated stencil, for
      every budget point of order <math|k> and power <math|P>, torus, anchor,
      and tested direction list allowed by that budget point;

      <item>one may take <math|R<rsub|d>=A<rsub|d>>. Then <math|R<rsub|d>>
      commutes with the signed-coordinate action and with the real structure,
      and for every <math|p> in the equation ideal the field obtained by
      evaluating <math|T<rsub|d>> at the coordinates of <math|R<rsub|d>*p>
      belongs to <math|<math-up|Micro>>.
    </enumerate>

    The section and stencil depend on <math|d> and the fixed action/frame
    only; they do not depend on <math|k,P>, torus size, or cutoff.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    First invert the triangular regrading. On the finite grade decomposition,
    the condition that <math|A<rsub|d>*p-p> has strictly higher grade makes
    <math|A<rsub|d>> an upper-triangular block matrix with identity diagonal.
    Back substitution solves <math|A<rsub|d>*x=y> grade by grade: at the
    lowest grade, <math|x> has the same component as <math|y>; after
    subtracting that component, the next grade is solved in the same way.
    Finiteness of the grade set makes this process terminate and gives a
    unique <math|x>. Thus <math|R<rsub|d>=A<rsub|d>> is invertible, and the
    same induction shows that its inverse preserves the lower grade bound.

    For each basis vector set <math|f<rsub|j>=B<rsub|d>*<around|(|R<rsub|d><rsup|-1>*e<rsub|j>|)>>.
    By the filtration property, <math|f<rsub|j>> is a finite linear
    combination of monomials, each with at least <math|s<rsub|j>>
    curvature-coordinate factors. Each monomial uses only a finite list of
    slots. A product is represented by concatenating its two lists of slots.
    A sum is represented by taking the disjoint union of the lists and adding
    the corresponding polynomials. The empty list represents constants.
    Induction over the finite monomial expansion therefore gives, for each
    <math|j>, a finite slot list and a pose polynomial of degree at least
    <math|s<rsub|j>> realizing <math|f<rsub|j>>.

    There are only finitely many basis vectors. The union of their slot
    stencils is finite, so choose a centered box containing it. Enlarge the
    box symmetrically if needed; this preserves containment and makes the
    stencil invariant under signed coordinate permutations. On this one box,
    the polynomial realization is constructed for every basis vector. Extend
    these values linearly from the basis to define <math|T<rsub|d>>.
    Linearity gives the exact evaluation identity for every <math|p>, not
    merely for basis labels.

    The evaluation identities also transfer all structure. Gauge invariance,
    the unit normalization, signed covariance, conjugation reality, and the
    action-projection condition are the corresponding properties of
    <math|B<rsub|d>>. Because evaluation agrees on the entire basis, the
    section has these properties on all labels. The basis poses give the
    common-support and degree witnesses. The last item of the theorem is
    immediate from the choice <math|R<rsub|d>=A<rsub|d>>: the two
    equivariances of <math|A<rsub|d>> are imprint hypotheses, and by the
    exact evaluation identity the field attached to <math|R<rsub|d>*p> is
    <math|B<rsub|d>*p>, which is microscopic for <math|p> in the equation
    ideal.

    It remains to show the derivative budgets for all later queries. The
    section rate statement is localized: for a stencil <math|S>, order
    <math|k>, power <math|P>, side <math|n>, and anchor <math|o>, it controls
    derivatives after expansion at <math|o> on
    <math|<math-up|Healthy><around|(|\<theta\>,S+o|)>>, for direction lists
    obeying the budget point's direction bound at <math|o>. The extra
    derivative-rate parameter here is zero, so the threshold factor remains
    exactly <math|\<theta\><rsup|s>> at every tested derivative order. Each
    color-coordinate curvature slot has rate degree one: on configurations
    healthy on the translated stencil, the slot and each of its tested
    derivatives through order <math|k> have norm at most a constant times
    <math|\<theta\>>, uniformly in the torus and the anchor. This is the slot
    estimate proved in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-sections> (the
    value is bounded by health and the isometry of parallel transport, and
    every derivative of positive order <math|\<ell\>> by a constant times
    <math|\<theta\><rsup|\<ell\>>\<leqslant\>\<theta\>> because each tested
    direction on a link of the stencil has size at most a constant times
    <math|\<theta\>>). A real color coordinate is a bounded linear functional
    of the slot, so it obeys the same bound. For a binary product, the
    product rule gives at most <math|2<rsup|k>> terms at order <math|k>.
    Iterating over a product of <math|m> factors gives at most
    <math|m<rsup|k>> terms; in each pose the number <math|m> is fixed, so
    this changes only its finite constant. Factor degrees add under
    multiplication. Hence a monomial of at least <math|s<rsub|j>> chips has
    rate degree at least <math|s<rsub|j>>. Finite sums add their constants,
    and lowering a rate from a larger degree to <math|s<rsub|j>> is valid
    because <math|0\<less\>\<theta\>\<leqslant\>1>. Applying this to the
    finite pose expansion of each basis field gives one finite constant
    <math|c<rsub|j>*<around|(|k,P|)>> for the exact localized rate above.
    Summing basis estimates with coefficient moduli gives the linear price
    for arbitrary labels. No uniformity in <math|d,k>, or <math|P> is
    claimed.
  </proof>

  Item (5) is the currency furnished by the finite section. In the
  application, the profile at stage <math|i> requests the particular order
  <math|k<rsub|i>> and power <math|P<rsub|i>> appearing in that profile. For
  each <math|d\<leqslant\>i>, choose one available amount
  <math|a<rsub|d*i>\<geqslant\>0> for this request. It remains a valid credit
  after replacing a negative amount by zero, since all budgets are
  nonnegative. The section is not rebuilt when these requests change.

  <subsubsection|Buying all stage and degree conditions>

  For a stage <math|i>, let <math|c<rsub|i>> be its column count, let
  <math|q<rsub|d>> be the stencil size of the chosen section together with
  the fixed local section, and let <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i>> be its support
  exponent. The sphere price is

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|S><rsub|i><around|(|q<rsub|d>,a|)>=2+e<rsup|81*\<alpha\><rsub|i>>*<around|(|a+c<rsub|i>+2*q<rsub|d>|)>.
  </equation*>

  For each fixed pair <math|d\<leqslant\>i>, the chosen credit
  <math|a<rsub|d*i>>, the sphere price, and the coefficient
  <math|2*<around|(|1+c<rsub|i>|)>> are finite. The orbit cost of the
  degree-<math|d> frame,

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-O\><rsub|d>=1+<big|sum><rsub|g><big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>J<rsub|d>><around|\<\|\|\>|\<rho\><rsub|g>*e<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|g> runs over the finite list of signed coordinate permutations
  with or without conjugation and <math|\<rho\><rsub|g>> is its action on
  coefficient vectors, is finite and at least one. Hence its sliver

  <\equation*>
    \<delta\><rsub|d>=<frac|1|2*<space|0.17em>\<cal-O\><rsub|d><rsup|2>>
  </equation*>

  is strictly positive. Since the volume purse is
  <math|<math-ss|P><around|(|L|)>=L<rsup|1/8>\<to\>\<infty\>>, choose an
  integer <math|n<rsub|d*i>> such that

  <\equation*>
    2*<around|(|1+c<rsub|i>|)><math-ss|S><rsub|i><around|(|q<rsub|d>,a<rsub|d*i>|)>\<leqslant\><math-ss|P><around|(|L|)>*\<delta\><rsub|d>*<space|2em><around|(|L\<geqslant\>n<rsub|d*i>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The credit clause and nonnegativity hold at every such <math|L>.
  Separately, for each <math|j> choose <math|v<rsub|j>> so that

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|1+2*c<rsub|j>|)><space|0.17em><math-ss|D><around|(|L|)>\<leqslant\><wide|<math-ss|P>|\<bar\>><around|(|L|)>*<space|2em><around|(|L\<geqslant\>v<rsub|j>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where the door toll and purse are <math|<math-ss|D><around|(|L|)>=<math-up|rate><around|(|L|)>=L<rsup|-2/5>>
  and <math|<wide|<math-ss|P>|\<bar\>><around|(|L|)>=<math-up|barunit><around|(|L|)>=L<rsup|-3/8>>.
  Their ratio is <math|L<rsup|-1/40>>, so the inequality holds once
  <math|L<rsup|1/40>\<geqslant\>1+2*c<rsub|j>>. For a fixed stage <math|i>,
  take the maximum of the finitely many <math|n<rsub|d*i>> with
  <math|d\<leqslant\>i> and <math|v<rsub|j>> with <math|j\<leqslant\>i>. This
  single threshold <math|n<rsub|i>> proves all clauses required at stage
  <math|i> simultaneously. The resulting nonnegative table <math|a<rsub|d*i>>
  and thresholds <math|n<rsub|i>> are exactly the same section's all-stage
  purchase data.

  The later delayed budget has a similar finite-threshold argument. Write
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|F>*<around|(|x,L|)>=x+b<rsub|L>>, where
  <math|b<rsub|L>> is the fixed logarithmic scale shift, and write
  <math|<math-ss|f><rsub|d><around|(|x|)>=x<rsup|-w<rsub|d>>> for the
  reciprocal tail. For the public schedule <math|w<rsub|d>=max<rsub|j>
  s<rsub|j>+4>, the maximal factor grade of the degree-<math|d> frame plus
  four; the following delay argument works for any fixed positive integer
  exponent. The tail comparison needed by the delay is, with
  <math|y=\<beta\><rsub|F>*<around|(|x,L|)>=x+b<rsub|L>>,

  <\equation*>
    y<rsup|-1>*L<rsup|d+1><space|0.17em><math-ss|f><rsub|d><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><math-ss|f><rsub|d><around|(|y|)>.
  </equation*>

  For fixed <math|L,d>, and all sufficiently large <math|x>, one has
  <math|x\<geqslant\>1>, <math|b<rsub|L>\<leqslant\>x>, and
  <math|y\<geqslant\>max <around|{|1,L<rsup|d+1>*2<rsup|w<rsub|d>>|}>>; the
  last condition holds eventually because <math|y\<to\>\<infty\>> with
  <math|x>. In particular <math|0\<less\>y\<leqslant\>2*x>. After multiplying
  by the positive denominators the tail comparison is equivalent to

  <\equation*>
    L<rsup|d+1>*y<rsup|w<rsub|d>>\<leqslant\>y*x<rsup|w<rsub|d>>.
  </equation*>

  The left side is at most <math|L<rsup|d+1>*2<rsup|w<rsub|d>>*x<rsup|w<rsub|d>>>,
  which is at most the right side because
  <math|y\<geqslant\>L<rsup|d+1>*2<rsup|w<rsub|d>>>. No sign condition on the
  shift <math|b<rsub|L>> is used. The remaining conditions at a fixed
  stage-profile pair are just that <math|x> exceed the fixed profile cost and
  the finitely many credits requested at degrees <math|d> below that profile.
  They therefore hold eventually as well. For a fixed stage, only that stage
  and its adjacent predecessor are tested; the conjunction is finite. Choose
  a threshold for every stage and replace these thresholds by their prefix
  maxima, also taking the maximum with the original entrance delay. The new
  entrance delay is monotone, dominates the old one, and satisfies every
  adjacent-stage inequality whenever the actual delay exceeds it. This proves
  the delay calibration rather than assuming a single threshold works for all
  stages at once.

  At the selected history caller, these are the three eventual conditions
  needed by the marked budget package. For each fixed degree <math|d>, the
  section credit is queried at the profile of the actual stage
  <math|J<around|(|N|)>>. The strict reference-depth margin implies
  <math|d\<leqslant\>J<around|(|N|)>> for all sufficiently large <math|N>.
  The purchased stage table supplies the credit and flight inequality at that
  stage, evaluated at its preceding block side <math|L<rsub|N-1>>; this side
  is at least the stage threshold <math|n<rsub|J<around|(|N|)>>> provided the
  thresholds <math|n<rsub|i>> do not exceed the floor length of stage
  <math|i>, which bounds from below every block side used at that stage. This
  comparison of thresholds with floor lengths is an explicit condition on the
  schedule of the caller; it is not proved in this section. The delay
  purchase supplies the allowance comparison. At level <math|N> the selected
  coupling <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>> is at least the entrance delay of
  the stage <math|J*<around|(|N+1|)>>, and
  <math|J<around|(|N|)>\<leqslant\>J*<around|(|N+1|)>\<leqslant\>J<around|(|N|)>+1>,
  so the stage <math|J*<around|(|N+1|)>> and the profile
  <math|J<around|(|N|)>> form one of the adjacent pairs tested by the delay.
  Its credit clause at <math|x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>> gives
  <math|a<rsub|d,J<around|(|N|)>>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>> for
  every <math|d\<leqslant\>J<around|(|N|)>>. The credit
  <math|b<rsub|N>=a<rsub|d,J<around|(|N|)>>> is thus nonnegative and no
  larger than the running allowance <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>>. The
  tail clause of the same delay is used separately: with the schedule
  identity

  <\equation*>
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1>=\<beta\><rsub|F>*<around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>,L<rsub|i>|)>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>+c<rsub|G>*log
    L<rsub|i>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|c<rsub|G>=s<rsub|G>> is the slope constant
  <eqref|eq:shoot-slope> of the gauge group, it reads

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-marked-height-jump><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1><rsup|-1>*L<rsub|i><rsup|d+1><space|0.17em><math-ss|f><rsub|d><around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><math-ss|f><rsub|d><around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1>|)>*<space|2em><around|(|d\<leqslant\>J<around|(|i|)>|)>.
  </equation>

  This is the height recurrence <math|\<phi\><rsub|i>*L<rsub|i><rsup|d+1>*\<zeta\><rsub|d,i>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|d,i+1>>
  consumed in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-marked-portfolio-calibration>,
  for the public choices <math|\<phi\><rsub|i>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1><rsup|-1>>
  and <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,i>=<math-ss|f><rsub|d><around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>|)>>.
  Thus, at every sufficiently late stage for this fixed degree, there are
  simultaneously a valid derivative credit, the allowance bound, and the
  sphere/flight bound. These are exactly the three fields of the marked
  budget package used by the selected field-calibration call. It uses the
  same portfolio section throughout: the delay changes available budget, not
  the section or its stencil.

  <subsubsection|A small coordinate error gives an invertible calibration>

  The section is now fixed. At a given cutoff, the field calculation
  produces, for each coefficient basis vector <math|e<rsub|j>>, a proposed
  correction <math|u<rsub|j>> satisfying

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|e<rsub|j>-u<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|d>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>> is the sum of the
  absolute values of the finitely many coordinates and
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|d>=<around|(|2*\<cal-O\><rsub|d><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>.
  The required linear calibration is not inferred from columnwise closeness
  alone; the factor <math|\<cal-O\><rsub|d>> accounts for the finite symmetry
  and the change from the coordinate basis to the section's coefficient
  coordinates.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Robust finite-dimensional calibration><label|lem:ex-marked-calibration-inverse>Let
    <math|E> be the coefficient space of the chosen frame, with its
    <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>> norm. Let <math|\<cal-O\>\<geqslant\>1> bound both
    the sum of the chip coefficients and the <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>> norm of
    each symmetry action on the frame. For columns <math|u<rsub|j>>, define
    the repainting map by

    <\equation*>
      D*x=<big|sum><rsub|<around|(|g,j|)>>\<chi\><rsub|g,j><around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|g>*u<rsub|j>,<space|2em>x=<big|sum><rsub|<around|(|g,j|)>>\<chi\><rsub|g,j><around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|g>*e<rsub|j>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|g> ranges over the finite signed/conjugation symmetry list,
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|g>> is its action on the coefficient frame, and the
    fixed complex- linear chip coefficients obey
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|g,j><around|\||\<chi\><rsub|g,j><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<cal-O\><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>.
    If <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|e<rsub|j>-u<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|(|2*\<cal-O\><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>
    for every basis vector, then <math|D> is invertible and
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1\<to\>1>\<leqslant\>2>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Subtracting the two repainting formulas gives
    <math|<around|(|I-D|)>*x=<big|sum><rsub|g,j>\<chi\><rsub|g,j><around|(|x|)>*\<rho\><rsub|g>*<around|(|e<rsub|j>-u<rsub|j>|)>>.
    The sum of chip moduli is at most <math|\<cal-O\><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
    and applying the symmetry to a column error costs at most another factor
    <math|\<cal-O\>>. Consequently

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|I-D|)>*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<cal-O\><rsup|2>*<around|(|2*\<cal-O\><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>=<tfrac|1|2><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    The triangle inequality gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<geqslant\><frac|1|2><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>;
    therefore <math|D> is injective. Since <math|E> is finite dimensional, it
    is surjective and invertible. The same lower bound with
    <math|x=D<rsup|-1>*y> gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|-1>*y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Application to the selected field calibration>

  We now state how the fixed section is compared, at each cutoff, with the
  local section of a selected family. The statement assembles two results
  proved later, the one-body invitation of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-marked-invitation> and the same-history
  calibration of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:actual-marked-figment>;
  neither of them uses the present subsection.

  Fix a selected family <math|C> which makes the action, with its history,
  and a degree <math|d>. Write <math|F> for the grade-<math|d> coefficient
  frame with basis <math|<around|(|e<rsub|j>|)><rsub|j\<in\>J<rsub|d>>>,
  <math|T> for the local section of the family at degree <math|d>, and
  <math|V=V<rsub|d>> for the fixed section of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-marked-portfolio-section>, expressed in this
  frame and sound for the action of the family. At level <math|N> write
  <math|a<rsub|N>> for the mesh, <math|\<theta\><rsub|N>> for the budget
  threshold, <math|c<rsub|N>> for the column count, <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N>>
  for the support exponent, <math|\<sigma\><rsub|N>> for the running scale,
  <math|L<rsub|N-1>> for the preceding block side, and
  <math|<math-ss|S><rsub|N><around|(|q,b|)>=2+e<rsup|81*\<alpha\><rsub|N>>*<around|(|b+c<rsub|N>+2*q|)>>
  for the sphere price. For a section <math|W\<in\><around|{|V,T|}>> and a
  coefficient vector <math|v\<in\>F>, the normalized origin field at
  <math|<around|(|N,p|)>> is

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-O\><rsup|W><rsub|N,p><around|(|v|)><around|(|U|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>*<space|0.17em>W<around*|(|\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|-1><math-plus|\<cdot\><rsub|F>>v|)><around|(|0,U|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<cdot\><rsub|F>> is the graded dilation multiplying the
  coordinate <math|j> by <math|\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|-s<rsub|j>>>. The
  inputs are the following.

  <\enumerate>
    <item><em|The eventual budget triple.> There is a sequence
    <math|b<rsub|N>> such that, for all sufficiently large <math|N>: the
    section <math|V> has the credit of item<nbsp>(5) of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-marked-portfolio-section> with constant
    <math|b<rsub|N>> at the derivative order and power of the level-<math|N>
    budget; <math|0\<leqslant\>b<rsub|N>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>>;
    and

    <\equation*>
      2*<around|(|1+c<rsub|N>|)><math-ss|S><rsub|N><around|(|q<rsub|d>,b<rsub|N>|)>\<leqslant\><math-ss|P><around|(|L<rsub|N-1>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|d>.
    </equation*>

    For a schedule satisfying the floor and delay conditions stated in the
    preceding subsection, the triple follows with
    <math|b<rsub|N>=a<rsub|d,J<around|(|N|)>>>; the discharge of those
    conditions on the selected public run is an open input.

    <item><em|The paid selected-history record> of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-marked-portfolio-calibration>, with
    its allowances <math|u<rsub|N>\<gtr\>0> and heights
    <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,N>\<geqslant\>0>, and a terminal Keeper at every
    <math|<around|(|N,p|)>> (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:terminal-marked-construction>).
    Assume also that the local forced-family attachment input of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-marked-invitation> holds for its
    correction at every sufficiently large <math|N> and every period
    <math|p>.

    <item><em|Slow growth of the reserves.> Call a real sequence
    <math|x<rsub|N>> <em|slow> if for every <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0> one
    has <math|<around|\||x<rsub|N>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<varepsilon\>*\<sigma\><rsub|N>>>
    for all sufficiently large <math|N> along the extraction order under
    consideration. The sequences <math|u<rsub|N><rsup|-1>> and
    <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,N>> are slow for every extraction order.

    <item>The gauge group is simple, and the history has the opening schedule
    used for the centered root tests.
  </enumerate>

  Put

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|Z><rsub|N>=<around*|(|<math-ss|C><around|(|V|)>+2<math-ss|C><around|(|T|)>|)><math-up|Seat><rsub|d,N>,<space|2em><math-up|Seat><rsub|d,N>=<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>J<rsub|d>>\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|-s<rsub|j>>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|<math-ss|C><around|(|W|)>\<geqslant\>1> denotes a fixed
  value-norm constant of the section <math|W>:
  <math|<around|\||W<around|(|v|)><around|(|o,U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><math-ss|C><around|(|W|)><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>
  for all coefficient vectors, sides, anchors and configurations. Such a
  constant exists because the finitely many basis columns of <math|W> are
  continuous functions on a compact configuration space. It is not a
  derivative price, and <math|<math-up|Seat><rsub|d,N>> is not a number of
  sites: it bounds the <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>> operator norm of the dilation
  <math|\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|-1>\<cdot\><rsub|F>>. The price attached to
  each constructed column is the explicit schedule amount

  <\equation*>
    E<rsub|N>=4*<around|(|1+c<rsub|N>|)><math-ss|S><rsub|N><around|(|q<rsub|d>,b<rsub|N>|)>+e<rsup|81*<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|N>+2|)>><math-ss|Z><rsub|N><space|0.17em>\<zeta\><rsub|d,N>*<space|0.17em>u<rsub|N><rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  The first summand pays the local stencil and sphere terms; the second pays
  the forced correction after the reserve factor
  <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,N>/u<rsub|N>>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Eventual invertible calibration of the fixed
    section><label|prop:ex-marked-solaris>Under the four inputs above, for
    every sufficiently large <math|N> and every period <math|p> there are
    columns <math|u<rsub|j>=u<rsub|j>*<around|(|N,p|)>\<in\>F>,
    <math|j\<in\>J<rsub|d>>, such that:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|<around|\<\|\|\>|e<rsub|j>-u<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|d>>
      for every <math|j>. Consequently the repainting map <math|D<rsub|N,p>>
      built from these columns in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-marked-calibration-inverse>
      is invertible and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsub|N,p><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1\<to\>1>\<leqslant\>2>.

      <item>For every <math|j>, every <math|k> with
      <math|<math-up|ref><around|(|d|)>\<leqslant\>k\<less\>N>, every
      <math|m> opened at <math|k>, and every motion <math|s> (a signed
      coordinate permutation, possibly composed with complex conjugation),
      the centered root test at depth <math|m> for the finite-volume law at
      <math|<around|(|N,p|)>>, in the notation of
      Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-marked-portfolio-calibration>,
      satisfies

      <\equation*>
        <math-up|rootTest><rsub|m;N,p><around*|(|s\<cdot\><around*|(|\<cal-O\><rsup|V><rsub|N,p><around|(|e<rsub|j>|)>-\<cal-O\><rsup|T><rsub|N,p><around|(|u<rsub|j>|)>|)>|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|cider><around|(|d,m,k|)>*<space|0.17em>E<rsub|N>*<space|0.17em>e<rsup|-\<gamma\>*\<sigma\><rsub|N>>,
      </equation*>

      with the constant <math|<math-up|cider><around|(|d,m,k|)>> and the
      exponent <math|\<gamma\>=1/8> of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:actual-marked-figment>.
      Its proof constructs the served representation for this residual,
      including all marker modes, in paragraph<nbsp><reference|par:figment-served-residual>.

      <item>The sequence <math|E<rsub|N>> is slow for every extraction order.
    </enumerate>

    The threshold in <math|N> depends on <math|d> but not on <math|p>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Fix <math|N> so large that the following hold; each holds for all
    sufficiently large <math|N>, uniformly in <math|p>. The three clauses of
    the budget triple hold at <math|N>. One has
    <math|N\<gtr\><math-up|ref><around|(|d|)>>. Every point <math|z> of the
    union <math|X> of the stencils of <math|V> and <math|T> satisfies
    <math|20*<around|(|<around|\||z<rsub|i>|\|>+1|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|side><around|(|N,p|)>>
    in each coordinate, because the sides tend to infinity uniformly in
    <math|p>. Finally the three local reserve conditions of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-marked-sanded> and
    <eqref|eq:ex-marked-cover> hold for the stencil <math|X> and the two
    constants <math|<math-ss|C><around|(|V|)>,<math-ss|C><around|(|T|)>>;
    here simplicity of the gauge group is used, through
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>\<to\>\<infty\>> and the selected-budget
    radius formula in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-marked-field-local>.

    Fix <math|p> and <math|j>. These are exactly the inputs of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-marked-invitation>, with the terminal
    Keeper and local forced-family attachment, <math|u<rsub|N>\<gtr\>0> and
    <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,N>\<geqslant\>0> from the second input. It supplies
    a terminal coefficient <math|Z<rsub|j>> with leading coordinate
    <math|Z<rsub|j>.*x=e<rsub|j>>, charge bound
    <math|Q<rsub|N>=<math-ss|S><rsub|N><around|(|q<rsub|d>,b<rsub|N>|)>>,
    budget <math|E<rsub|N>>, and field <math|\<cal-O\><rsup|V><rsub|N,p><around|(|e<rsub|j>|)>>;
    these are the Cream data. Since <math|<math-up|ref><around|(|d|)>\<less\>N>,
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:actual-marked-figment> applies on the
    same family and history. It gives one label <math|v<rsub|j>> with

    <\equation*>
      <math-ss|P><around|(|L<rsub|N-1>|)>*<space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|e<rsub|j>-X<rsub|N><rsup|-1>*v<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1+c<rsub|N>|)>*Q<rsub|N>
    </equation*>

    and the displayed root-test bounds for
    <math|\<cal-O\><rsup|V><rsub|N,p><around|(|e<rsub|j>|)>-C.*O<around|(|v<rsub|j>|)>>,
    where <math|X<rsub|N>> is the terminal calibration isomorphism of the
    family. Put <math|u<rsub|j>=X<rsub|N><rsup|-1>*v<rsub|j>>. By the
    definition of the family's observable,
    <math|C.*O<around|(|v<rsub|j>|)>=\<cal-O\><rsup|T><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|X<rsub|N><rsup|-1>*v<rsub|j>|)>=\<cal-O\><rsup|T><rsub|N,p><around|(|u<rsub|j>|)>>;
    this proves the second assertion. The flight inequality of the budget
    triple bounds the right side of the last display by
    <math|<math-ss|P><around|(|L<rsub|N-1>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|d>>. Dividing by
    <math|<math-ss|P><around|(|L<rsub|N-1>|)>\<gtr\>0> gives
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|e<rsub|j>-u<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|d>>,
    and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-marked-calibration-inverse> gives the
    invertibility and the inverse bound. The coefficient error is thus
    removed by one invertible linear regrading, shared by all labels at this
    cutoff.

    For the slow-growth assertion, all the components of <math|E<rsub|N>> are
    needed. The class of slow sequences is closed under finite sums and
    products, contains the constants, and contains every sequence dominated
    eventually by a slow sequence. The credit satisfies
    <math|0\<leqslant\>b<rsub|N>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>>
    eventually, and <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>> is slow because it is
    affine in <math|\<sigma\><rsub|N>>. The column count <math|c<rsub|N>> is
    slow by the accumulated column-logarithm estimate. The opening schedule
    gives <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N>=o<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|N>|)>>, so
    <math|e<rsup|81*\<alpha\><rsub|N>>> and
    <math|e<rsup|81*<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|N>+2|)>>=e<rsup|162>*e<rsup|81*\<alpha\><rsub|N>>>
    are slow. Hence <math|<math-ss|S><rsub|N><around|(|q<rsub|d>,b<rsub|N>|)>>
    is slow. The inverse threshold <math|\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|-1>> is slow,
    being bounded by <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>>; therefore the finite
    sum <math|<math-up|Seat><rsub|d,N>> of its powers and
    <math|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N>> are slow. Finally <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,N>>
    and <math|u<rsub|N><rsup|-1>> are slow by the third input. Each summand
    of <math|E<rsub|N>> is a finite product of slow sequences. Omitting
    either of the two reserve bounds of the third input would not justify
    this conclusion.
  </proof>

  The section theorem and stage/delay arguments above prove the fixed-stencil
  portfolio, all later credit requests, and, for a schedule satisfying the
  stated floor and delay conditions, the three eventual field-budget inputs.
  The local invitation and the same-history calibration used in
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-marked-solaris> remain separate
  substantive suppliers; they do not follow from existence of the section or
  from the inverse lemma. Their application requires the common-data matching
  and the remaining estimate boundary stated above.

  <subsection|The marked observable at the terminal
  level><label|sec:terminal-marked-construction>

  The terminal vacuum construction must also carry each local field and its
  forced profile. We give this construction before propagating the field down
  the selected history. All activities, supports and predicates in this
  section belong to that history.

  <subsubsection|Reserved addresses and the missing part of the field>

  Fix a grade <math|d>, a cutoff <math|N> and a period <math|p>. Write
  <math|R> for the terminal representation, <math|F> for its grade-<math|d>
  coefficient frame, <math|S> for its sound local section, <math|o=0> for its
  origin, <math|Y> for its footprint and <math|0\<less\>\<theta\>\<leqslant\>1>
  for its threshold. The forced activities are <math|K<rsub|i>>, with
  supports <math|X<rsub|i>> and nonempty inventories
  <math|I<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>X<rsub|i>>. Their eligibility requires both the
  prescribed flags on <math|X<rsub|i>> and the original additional predicate.
  A compatible collection has pairwise disjoint supports, and its inventories
  cover exactly the flagged sites.

  The activity dictionary used in the startup and return constructions has,
  for every pair <math|I\<subseteq\>X> with <math|I\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>, an
  infinite sequence of reserved addresses. The address indexed by
  <math|<around|(|X,I,j|)>> has support <math|X>, inventory <math|I>, and
  zero activity. Its additional predicate is precisely the condition that the
  flagged sites in <math|X> equal <math|I>. Here is why these properties
  survive the construction. The dictionary embeds the old hard labels in one
  set of coordinates and reserves a disjoint set for every shape
  <math|<around|(|X,I|)>>. The new connected objects encode finite sets of
  these coordinates. A reserved singleton cannot be an image of an old
  object: that would put its reserved coordinate in the old coordinate
  embedding. Its coefficient therefore remains zero. Reading a singleton
  recovers exactly its support and inventory; its eligibility test is the
  original test for that one shape. This is the same operation in startup and
  every return. In particular it supplies the reserved addresses in the
  actual selected terminal representation.

  We use these addresses to encode a finite compatible nonempty family
  <math|b> of old forced labels whose supports all meet <math|Y>. Put

  <\equation*>
    T<rsub|b>=Y\<cup\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>b>X<rsub|i>,<space|2em>I<rsub|b>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>b>I<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  Choose the reserved address <math|s<around|(|b|)>> for shape
  <math|<around|(|T<rsub|b>,I<rsub|b>|)>>, with serial number encoding
  <math|<around|(|d,b|)>>. This is injective in <math|b>. No old activity is
  overwritten: <math|K<rsub|s<around|(|b|)>>=0> before the marked coefficient
  is installed.

  For <math|v\<in\>F> let <math|P<rsub|v>> be the primary, masked part of the
  section, and let

  <\equation*>
    f<rsub|v><around|(|U|)>=S<around|(|\<theta\><rsup|-1><math-plus|\<cdot\><rsub|F>>v|)><around|(|o,U|)>-P<rsub|v><around|(|U|)>.<label|eq:terminal-supplement>
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<cdot\><rsub|F>> is the graded dilation: on the coordinate of a
  basis vector <math|e<rsub|j>> of the frame it multiplies by
  <math|\<theta\><rsup|-s<rsub|j>>>, where <math|s<rsub|j>> is the factor
  grade of <math|e<rsub|j>>, the second entry of its grade
  <math|<around|(|r<rsub|j>,s<rsub|j>|)>> in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-marked-portfolio-calibration>. The
  primary part retains the vacuum coordinate everywhere and the scaled
  nonvacuum part on healthy configurations. Hence <math|f<rsub|v>=0> when
  <math|Y> is healthy; otherwise it equals the scaled nonvacuum section. The
  map <math|v\<mapsto\>f<rsub|v>> is complex-linear, measurable, gauge
  invariant, and depends only on the field and flags in <math|Y>. These
  assertions follow directly from the corresponding properties of the sound
  section and the mask. If <math|q=max<rsub|j> s<rsub|j>> is the maximum
  factor grade and each axis section <math|S<around|(|e<rsub|j>|)><around|(|o,\<cdot\>|)>>
  has modulus at most <math|C<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>1>, then

  <\equation>
    <around|\||f<rsub|v><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|d>*\<theta\><rsup|-q><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>.<label|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T1>
  </equation>

  Indeed expand the nonvacuum coefficient in the finite axis basis and use
  <math|0\<less\>\<theta\>\<leqslant\>1>, so
  <math|\<theta\><rsup|-s<rsub|j>>\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsup|-q>> for every
  axis.

  Define the new forced coefficient at <math|s<around|(|b|)>> by

  <\equation>
    J<rsub|v>*<around|(|s<around|(|b|)>,U|)>=\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|T<rsub|b>\<cap\><math-up|flags><around|(|U|)>=I<rsub|b>|}>>*f<rsub|v><around|(|U|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>b>K<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>,<label|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T2>
  </equation>

  and set it to zero at all other addresses. Its support and inventory are
  exactly <math|T<rsub|b>,I<rsub|b>>. When it is nonzero, the indicator
  ensures the original additional predicate of the reserved address. Old
  factors <math|K<rsub|i>> retain all their own predicates. The dependence,
  invariance and measurability requirements thus hold on the original
  dictionary. The marked coefficient has lead <math|v>, zero regular
  correction, and forced part <math|J<rsub|v>>.

  <subsubsection|The exact density and forced profile>

  Consider an old compatible collection <math|c> at a configuration where
  <math|f<rsub|v><around|(|U|)>\<ne\>0>. Split it uniquely into the labels
  meeting <math|Y> and the labels disjoint from <math|Y>:

  <\equation*>
    b=<around|{|i\<in\>c:X<rsub|i>\<cap\>Y\<ne\>\<varnothing\>|}>,<space|2em>r=c\<setminus\>b.
  </equation*>

  The set <math|b> is nonempty. Indeed <math|Y> is then not healthy, and a
  flagged site of <math|Y> lies in some inventory of <math|c>, hence in its
  support. Replace <math|b> by <math|s<around|(|b|)>> and leave <math|r>
  unchanged. The result is compatible: its old remaining supports are
  disjoint from <math|Y> and from all supports in <math|b>, hence from
  <math|T<rsub|b>>. Inventories still cover exactly the same flags.
  Conversely, from a compatible marked collection containing
  <math|s<around|(|b|)>>, replace that address by <math|b>. Its other
  supports are disjoint from <math|T<rsub|b>>, so this inverse restores
  precisely the original collection. These two operations are inverse before
  any summation.

  For each paired term, <eqref|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T2>
  times its remaining old activity factors is <math|f<rsub|v><around|(|U|)>>
  times the original product. The following absolute estimate justifies
  summing these identities. Suppose <math|k<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0> majorizes
  <math|<around|\||K<rsub|i>|\|>> and has the actual weighted site bound

  <\equation>
    <big|sum><rsub|i:x\<in\>X<rsub|i>>M<around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>*k<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>w,<space|2em>M<around|(|X|)>=exp
    <around|{|E<space|0.17em><math-up|cover><rsub|B><around|(|X|)>|}>,<label|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T3>
  </equation>

  where <math|E\<geqslant\>0> is the purchased support exponent. Because
  every <math|X<rsub|i>> in <math|b> meets <math|Y>, the connected covering
  inequality gives

  <\equation*>
    M<around|(|T<rsub|b>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>b>k<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>M<around|(|Y|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>b>M<around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>*k<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  Moreover the sum of <math|M<around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>*k<rsub|i>> over labels
  meeting <math|Y> is at most <math|<around|\||Y|\|>*w>: sum
  <eqref|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T3> over <math|x\<in\>Y>.
  For every finite set of such labels, the sum of products over its nonempty
  subsets is <math|<big|prod><rsub|i><around|(|1+M<around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>*k<rsub|i>|)>-1\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<around|\||Y|\|>*w>-1>.
  Increasing the finite sets and using nonnegativity proves

  <\equation>
    <big|sum><rsub|b>M<around|(|T<rsub|b>|)>*sup<rsub|U><around|\||J<rsub|v>*<around|(|s<around|(|b|)>,U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|d>*\<theta\><rsup|-q><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>*M<around|(|Y|)>*<around|(|e<rsup|<around|\||Y|\|>*w>-1|)>.<label|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T4>
  </equation>

  Dropping the compatibility restrictions only enlarges this bound. It
  supplies a summable majorant for the new coefficients. The lawful old gas
  bounds supply the remaining dressing majorants, so the regrouping above is
  absolutely convergent. Its sum is exactly <math|f<rsub|v>> times the old
  gas. Adding the primary contribution proves the pointwise identity

  <\equation>
    <math-up|density><around|(|v|)><around|(|U|)>=R<around|(|U|)>*S<around|(|\<theta\><rsup|-1><math-plus|\<cdot\><rsub|F>>v|)><around|(|0,U|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|d>*R<around|(|U|)><space|0.17em><math-up|initial><rsub|N,p><around|(|v|)><around|(|U|)>.<label|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T5>
  </equation>

  Here <math|R<around|(|U|)>> denotes the value at <math|U> of the unmarked
  density of the terminal representation, and <math|a<rsub|N>> is the mesh at
  level <math|N>. The last equality is the definition of the physical
  normalization <math|<math-up|initial><rsub|N,p><around|(|v|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>*S<around|(|\<theta\><rsup|-1><math-plus|\<cdot\><rsub|F>>v|)><around|(|0,\<cdot\>|)>>.

  There is also an identity before multiplication by the unmarked gas. Group
  the forced coefficients according to their support and inventory. The
  address <math|s<around|(|b|)>> contributes exactly the expression in
  <eqref|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T2>, and all addresses
  outside its image contribute zero. The bijection between <math|b> and the
  finite compatible families meeting <math|Y> identifies this profile with
  the attachment of the singleton response <math|f<rsub|v>> on <math|Y>. This
  is the forced-profile identity needed by the adjacent transfer
  construction. It follows from equality of the individual products and the
  summable majorant <eqref|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T4>, not
  from the density identity <eqref|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T5>.

  The footprint, graded dilation and healthy mask commute with signed lattice
  motions. The sound section also commutes with the independent
  complex-conjugation action. Therefore <math|f<rsub|v>> transforms into
  <math|f<rsub|g\<cdot\>v>>, with the same scalar conjugation when
  appropriate. Reindexing the compatible collections preserves their supports
  and inventories and proves the same covariance for the attached profile. No
  equivariant choice of the serial numbers is needed: the profile sums over
  addresses. The density, coefficient index and forced profile thus have the
  common motion covariance required at terminal birth.

  <subsubsection|Paying the birth cost before choosing the history>

  It remains to pay the factor in <eqref|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T4>,
  uniformly in the period. The axis bound <math|C<rsub|d>> comes from the
  fixed finite local section. Its stencil has cardinality at most a fixed
  <math|R<rsub|d>>, and, once the cover radius contains that stencil,
  <math|<math-up|cover><rsub|B><around|(|Y|)>\<leqslant\>1>. Thus the fee is
  at most

  <\equation>
    C<rsub|d>*\<theta\><rsup|-q>*e<rsup|E>*<around|(|e<rsup|R<rsub|d>*w>-1|)>.<label|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T6>
  </equation>

  On a fixed profile stage, <math|E,C<rsub|d>,R<rsub|d>,q> are fixed. For the
  hatted coupling <math|x> the chosen threshold satisfies
  <math|\<theta\><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>x> once <math|x\<geqslant\>1>, and the
  forced allowance is

  <\equation*>
    w<around|(|x|)>=exp <around|{|-exp <around|(|<around|(|log log
    x|)><rsup|2>/5|)>|}>,<space|2em>0\<less\>w<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  For each fixed integer <math|n>, <math|<sqrt|w<around|(|x|)>>*x<rsup|n>\<to\>0>.
  To see this, put <math|u=log log x>. The logarithm of that product is
  <math|-<tfrac|1|2>e<rsup|u<rsup|2>/5>+n*e<rsup|u>>, which tends to
  <math|-\<infty\>> because <math|e<rsup|u<rsup|2>/5-u>\<to\>\<infty\>>. Also
  <math|e<rsup|z>-1\<leqslant\>z*e<rsup|z>> for <math|z\<geqslant\>0>, by
  integration of <math|e<rsup|t>> over <math|<around|[|0,z|]>>. Consequently
  <eqref|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T6> is at most

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|d>*x<rsup|q>*w<around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>K<rsub|d>=C<rsub|d>*e<rsup|E>*R<rsub|d>*e<rsup|R<rsub|d>>.
  </equation*>

  Choose <math|x\<geqslant\>max <around|(|1,K<rsub|d>|)>> and
  <math|<sqrt|w<around|(|x|)>>*x<rsup|q+1>\<leqslant\>1>. Then this last
  expression is at most <math|<sqrt|w<around|(|x|)>>>. This proves the
  terminal remainder bound with charge <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
  uniformly in <math|N,p> at this profile stage.

  At stage <math|j> there are only finitely many grades and earlier profiles
  through <math|j>. Take a common maximum of their fee thresholds and stencil
  fit thresholds, then take running maxima in <math|j>. Include this delay
  among the purchases of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-observable-purchases>
  before applying the common scale selection. At level <math|m>, both its
  grade <math|d\<leqslant\>J<around|(|m|)>> and its actual profile index
  <math|J<around|(|m|)>> lie in the finite list purchased at
  <math|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>. The fee therefore fits at every admitted
  terminal level, on the same selected scale sequence. With
  <eqref|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T1>\U<eqref|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T5>,
  this supplies the terminal marked data and all of their bounds for the
  backward transfer construction.

  <subsubsection|Summary of the terminal construction>

  For later reference we collect what has been proved, with its exact inputs.
  Fix <math|d>, <math|N> and <math|p> as above.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Terminal marked coefficients><label|prop:terminal-marked-voice>Assume
    the following.

    <\enumerate>
      <item>The terminal representation <math|R> of the selected history at
      <math|<around|(|N,p|)>> is lawful: its forced labels satisfy the
      regularity, measurability and locality requirements of the dictionary,
      and their activities have majorants
      <math|k<rsub|i>\<geqslant\><around|\||K<rsub|i>|\|>> obeying the
      weighted site bound <eqref|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T3>
      with some <math|w\<gtr\>0>.

      <item>For every pair <math|I\<subseteq\>X> with
      <math|I\<neq\>\<varnothing\>> the dictionary of <math|R> contains
      infinitely many reserved addresses with support <math|X>, inventory
      <math|I> and zero activity, whose additional predicate holds at every
      configuration with <math|X\<cap\><math-up|flags><around|(|U|)>=I>.

      <item>The local section <math|S> is sound, and
      <math|<around|\||S<around|(|e<rsub|j>|)><around|(|0,U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|d>>
      for all axes <math|j> and configurations <math|U>, with
      <math|C<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>1>.

      <item>The fee inequality <math|C<rsub|d>*\<theta\><rsup|-q>*M<around|(|Y|)>*<around|(|e<rsup|<around|\||Y|\|>*w>-1|)>\<leqslant\><sqrt|w>>
      holds.
    </enumerate>

    For <math|v\<in\>F> let <math|\<cal-V\><around|(|v|)>> be the marked
    coefficient with lead <math|v>, zero regular correction and forced part
    <math|J<rsub|v>> defined in <eqref|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T2>.
    Then <math|v\<mapsto\>\<cal-V\><around|(|v|)>> is complex-linear in each
    of its three entries, and for every <math|v>:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>each forced entry <math|J<rsub|v>*<around|(|i,\<cdot\>|)>> is
      gauge invariant and measurable, depends only on the field and flags on
      the support of the address <math|i>, and is nonzero only at
      configurations where that address is eligible for the original
      dictionary of <math|R>;

      <item>the forced entries have majorants with
      <math|<big|sum><rsub|b>M<around|(|T<rsub|b>|)>*sup<rsub|U><around|\||J<rsub|v>*<around|(|s<around|(|b|)>,U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><sqrt|w><space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>;

      <item>the density identity <eqref|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T5>
      holds at every configuration;

      <item>the forced profile of <math|J<rsub|v>> on the activities of
      <math|R> equals the attachment of the singleton response
      <math|f<rsub|v>> on the footprint <math|Y>;

      <item>for every signed lattice motion, with or without complex
      conjugation, the density of <math|\<cal-V\><around|(|v|)>> transported
      by the motion is the density of <math|\<cal-V\>> at the transported
      label.
    </enumerate>

    On the selected scale sequence, hypothesis<nbsp>(4) holds at every
    admitted terminal level <math|N>, for every grade
    <math|d\<leqslant\>J<around|(|N|)>> and every period, once the entrance
    delays include the fee thresholds chosen above.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Linearity holds because <math|v\<mapsto\>f<rsub|v>> is linear and
    <eqref|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T2> is linear in
    <math|f<rsub|v>>. Assertion<nbsp>(1) was verified after
    <eqref|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T2>; the eligibility for
    the original dictionary uses the indicator in
    <eqref|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T2> and
    hypothesis<nbsp>(2). Assertion<nbsp>(2) is
    <eqref|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T4> combined with
    hypothesis<nbsp>(4). Assertions<nbsp>(3), (4) and<nbsp>(5) are
    respectively the identity <eqref|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T5>,
    the forced-profile identity and the covariance proved in the second
    subsection. The final sentence is the conclusion of the third subsection.
  </proof>

  Hypothesis<nbsp>(2) is a property of the selected history, not of the
  construction in this section. The reserved-address property is an input
  here; the first subsection only recalls why the dictionary can carry such
  addresses, and the realization of the property on the selected history must
  be supplied by the history-selection construction. Hypothesis<nbsp>(1) is
  likewise a property of the selected history. The inclusion of the fee
  thresholds among the entrance delays, used in the final sentence of the
  proposition, is a condition on the schedule which is not discharged here.
  These hypotheses are recorded as inputs and are consumed, together with the
  five conclusions, by the backward constructions of
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-centered-service> and
  <reference|sec:actual-marked-portfolio-calibration>.

  <subsection|The local reserve and marked one-body
  invitation><label|sec:ex-marked-field-local>

  This unit closes two finite estimates used when a marked label is inserted
  at the terminal scale of a selected history. The first is the smallness of
  the forced reserve relative to a fixed section and its finite frame. The
  second converts a paid local section into the exact coefficient and
  remainder data called a <em|Cream>. It keeps the actual one-body insertion,
  its normalization, and its exponential support price visible. The later
  recentering of this Cream through all earlier scales is a separate step and
  is not claimed here.

  <subsubsection|The forced reserve beats every fixed frame price>

  Let <math|F<rsub|d>> be a finite frame with grades
  <math|<around|(|r<rsub|j>,s<rsub|j>|)>>, <math|j\<in\>J<rsub|d>>, and set

  <\equation*>
    M<rsub|d>=<around|\||J<rsub|d>|\|>,<space|2em>k<rsub|d>=max<rsub|j\<in\>J<rsub|d>>
    s<rsub|j>.
  </equation*>

  For a budget threshold <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|N>\<in\><around|(|0,1|]>>,
  define the finite seat price

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|Seat><rsub|d,N>=<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>J<rsub|d>>\<vartheta\><rsub|N><rsup|-s<rsub|j>>.
  </equation*>

  Assume the scale parameter <math|\<beta\><rsub|N>\<to\>\<infty\>> and
  <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|N><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rsub|N>>. Define

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|stain><around|(|x|)>=exp <space|-0.17em><around*|(|-exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<frac|<around|(|log log
    x|)><rsup|2>|5>|)>|)>*<space|1em><around|(|x\<gtr\>1|)>,<space|2em>\<frak-f\><rsub|N>=<math-up|stain><around|(|\<beta\><rsub|N>|)>,
  </equation*>

  and, for fixed <math|x,y\<geqslant\>0>,

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|Storm><rsub|d,N><around|(|x,y|)>=<around|(|x+2*y|)><math-up|Seat><rsub|d,N>.
  </equation*>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Reserve versus a fixed section><label|lem:ex-marked-sanded>For
    every fixed <math|d> and <math|x,y\<geqslant\>0>,

    <\equation*>
      <sqrt|\<frak-f\><rsub|N>><space|0.17em><math-up|Storm><rsub|d,N><around|(|x,y|)>\<leqslant\>1
    </equation*>

    for all sufficiently large <math|N>. For every fixed finite stencil
    <math|X> on the cover lattice,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||<math-up|regional><around|(|X,0|)>|\|>*<space|0.17em>\<frak-f\><rsub|N>\<leqslant\>1
    </equation*>

    for all sufficiently large <math|N> as well.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Write <math|M=M<rsub|d>>, <math|k=k<rsub|d>>, and <math|A=x+2*y>. Since
    each <math|s<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>k> and
    <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|N><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rsub|N>>,

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|Seat><rsub|d,N>\<leqslant\>M*\<beta\><rsub|N><rsup|k>.
    </equation*>

    Choose <math|N> large enough that <math|\<beta\><rsub|N>\<geqslant\>max
    <around|{|1,A*M|}>>. Then <math|A*M*\<beta\><rsub|N><rsup|k>\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rsub|N><rsup|k+1>>,
    so it suffices that <math|<sqrt|<math-up|stain><around|(|\<beta\><rsub|N>|)>>*<space|0.17em>\<beta\><rsub|N><rsup|k+1>\<leqslant\>1>.
    For completeness, put <math|v=log log \<beta\><rsub|N>>, which tends to
    infinity with <math|N>. The logarithm of the left side is

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|k+1|)>*e<rsup|v>-<tfrac|1|2>e<rsup|v<rsup|2>/5>.
    </equation*>

    As <math|v\<to\>\<infty\>>, <math|v<rsup|2>/5-v\<to\>\<infty\>>, hence
    <math|e<rsup|v<rsup|2>/5>\<geqslant\>2*<around|(|k+1|)>*e<rsup|v>>
    eventually. This proves the required super-polynomial decay and the first
    assertion.

    Let <math|m<rsub|X>=<around|\||X|\|>>. Regionalization is an image, so
    <math|<around|\||<math-up|regional><around|(|X,0|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>m<rsub|X>>.
    Eventually <math|\<beta\><rsub|N>\<geqslant\>max
    <around|{|1,m<rsub|X>|}>>. The just-proved estimate with exponent one
    implies <math|<sqrt|\<frak-f\><rsub|N>>*<space|0.17em>\<beta\><rsub|N>\<leqslant\>1>,
    while <math|0\<less\>\<frak-f\><rsub|N>\<leqslant\>1>; therefore

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||<math-up|regional><around|(|X,0|)>|\|>*\<frak-f\><rsub|N>\<leqslant\>m<rsub|X>*<sqrt|\<frak-f\><rsub|N>>\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rsub|N>*<sqrt|\<frak-f\><rsub|N>>\<leqslant\>1.
    </equation*>

    This proves the finite local allowance estimate.
  </proof>

  The volume-independent geometric cover estimate is also elementary. Let
  <math|B<rsub|N>> be the radius in the budget's support norm and assume
  <math|B<rsub|N>\<to\>\<infty\>>. For fixed <math|X>, every point of
  <math|X> is eventually in the radius-<math|B<rsub|N>> cube. Consequently
  <math|<math-up|regional><around|(|X,0|)>> lies in the halo of the origin.
  The cube-tiling cover of that halo uses at most <math|81> support blocks,
  so

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-marked-cover><math-up|coverNumber><around|(|B<rsub|N>,<math-up|regional><around|(|X,0|)>|)>\<leqslant\>81
  </equation>

  eventually. For the selected budget, <math|B<rsub|N>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
  \<beta\><rsub|N>|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>. Since
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|N>\<to\>\<infty\>>, also <math|log
  \<beta\><rsub|N>\<to\>\<infty\>>, and the ceiling is at least its argument;
  hence <math|B<rsub|N>\<to\>\<infty\>>. For a different budget the same
  cover conclusion requires its own radius divergence. The three local
  conditions used below are therefore obtained with the same terminal
  threshold: the regional forced mass is at most one, its support cover
  number is at most <math|81>, and the square-root reserve times the fixed
  storms price is at most one.

  <subsubsection|From a paid section to a Cream>

  We record the exact one-body statement. Let <math|C> be a selected
  finite-volume family, <math|N> its terminal level, <math|p> the period, and
  <math|d> a label degree. Write <math|F=F<rsub|d>>, <math|T> for the fixed
  local section, <math|V> for the purchased section,
  <math|\<theta\><rsub|N>\<in\><around|(|0,1|]>> for the terminal budget
  threshold, <math|w<rsub|N>=\<frak-f\><rsub|N>> for the forced allowance,
  <math|u<rsub|N>\<gtr\>0> for the startup allowance, and
  <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,N>\<geqslant\>0> for the mark budget. Let
  <math|R<rsub|N>=C.*<math-up|path.rep><around|(|N,p,N|)>> be the terminal
  representative, <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N>> its support exponent,
  <math|a<rsub|N>\<gtr\>0> the physical lattice spacing, and <math|B<rsub|N>>
  the support radius in the terminal budget point. Put

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|X>|<cell|=<math-up|stencil><around|(|V|)>\<cup\><math-up|stencil><around|(|T|)>,>>|<row|<cell|D>|<cell|=<math-up|regional><around|(|X,0|)>,<space|2em>c<rsub|N>=<text|column
    count>,>>|<row|<cell|s<rsub|N>>|<cell|=<math-up|Storm><rsub|d,N><around*|(|<math-up|price><around|(|V|)>,<math-up|price><around|(|T|)>|)>.>>>>
  </align*>

  where the two prices are the global value-norm constants of <math|V> and
  <math|T>; they are distinct from the derivative credit <math|b<rsub|N>>.
  Assume <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N>\<geqslant\>0> and the following exact local
  inputs:

  <\enumerate>
    <item><math|C> is a selected family which makes the action and
    <math|C.<math-up|ref><around|(|d|)>\<leqslant\>N>. The section <math|V>
    is sound, and its Credit holds at the terminal order and power with a
    nonnegative price <math|b<rsub|N>>.

    <item>Every <math|z\<in\>X> satisfies
    <math|20*<around|(|<around|\||z<rsub|j>|\|>+1|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|side><around|(|N,p|)>>
    for each coordinate <math|j>. The local regional bounds are
    <math|0\<less\>w<rsub|N>>, <math|<around|\||D|\|>*w<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>1>,
    and <math|<math-up|coverNumber><around|(|B<rsub|N>,D|)>\<leqslant\>81>.

    <item>The selected terminal representative is lawful and has a terminal
    Keeper: an injective assignment of an address of its dictionary to every
    triple <math|<around|(|X,I,n|)>> with
    <math|\<varnothing\>\<neq\>I\<subseteq\>X> and <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>,
    with support <math|X> and inventory <math|I>, whose additional predicate
    holds at every configuration with <math|X\<cap\><math-up|flags><around|(|U|)>=I>.
    This is the reserved-address part of hypothesis<nbsp>(2) of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:terminal-marked-voice>, with its
    zero-activity requirement omitted. Also <math|u<rsub|N>\<gtr\>0>,
    <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,N>\<geqslant\>0>, and
    <math|<sqrt|w<rsub|N>>*s<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>1>.

    <item>At the terminal budget point, every placed basis column of <math|T>
    is regular on its footprint and has derivative bound
    <math|c<rsub|N>*\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|s<rsub|j>>> for grade
    <math|s<rsub|j>>; this is the selected-family price clause.

    <item>The local forced-family attachment applies to the terminal
    representation and <math|D>: for every basis vector <math|e<rsub|j>>, the
    gauge-invariant, measurable local correction <math|b<rsub|0>> constructed
    below, vanishing when <math|D> is healthy and bounded by
    <math|s<rsub|N>>, it supplies a forced family with gas-mark sum
    <math|b<rsub|0>> times the activity gas density, the prescribed hook
    profile, and price at most <math|<math-up|mergePrice><around|(|D,s<rsub|N>|)>>
    defined below. This is a separate input for this proposition, realized
    for the selected terminal representation by the reserved-address
    construction in its proof.
  </enumerate>

  For <math|v\<in\>F>, define the normalized cylinder of the purchased
  section by

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-O\><rsub|N,p><around|(|v|)><around|(|U|)>=<around|(|a<rsub|N><rsup|d>|)><rsup|-1>*V.<math-up|places><around|(|F.<math-up|scaled><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|-1>|)>*v,0,U|)>.
  </equation*>

  For a basis vector <math|e<rsub|j>> of <math|F>, define

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|Q<rsub|N>>|<cell|=2+e<rsup|81*\<alpha\><rsub|N>>*<around*|(|b<rsub|N>+c<rsub|N>+2<around|\||X|\|>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|E<rsub|N>>|<cell|=4*<around|(|1+c<rsub|N>|)>*Q<rsub|N>+e<rsup|81*<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|N>+2|)>>*s<rsub|N>*<frac|\<zeta\><rsub|d,N>|u<rsub|N>>.>>>>
  </align*>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|One-body marked invitation><label|prop:ex-marked-invitation>Under
    these inputs, put <math|r<rsub|N>=b<rsub|N>+c<rsub|N>> (the regular
    price), <math|\<kappa\><rsub|N>=<math-up|coverNumber><around|(|B<rsub|N>,D|)>\<leqslant\>81>,
    and

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|t<rsub|N>>|<cell|=e<rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|N>*\<kappa\><rsub|N>>*r<rsub|N>+2<around|\||D|\|><sqrt|w<rsub|N>>*<space|0.17em>e<rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|N>*\<kappa\><rsub|N>>*s<rsub|N>,>>|<row|<cell|<wide|A|\<bar\>><rsub|N>>|<cell|=e<rsup|81*<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|N>+2|)>>*s<rsub|N>.>>>>
    </align*>

    Then for each basis vector <math|e<rsub|j>> there is a coefficient record
    <math|Z<rsub|j>> with the following fields:

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|Z<rsub|j>.*x>|<cell|=e<rsub|j>,>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|Z<rsub|j>.<math-up|Format><around|(|0,C.<math-up|params>,R<rsub|N>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|Z<rsub|j>.<math-up|remBound><around|(|0,R<rsub|N>,<sqrt|w<rsub|N>>,t<rsub|N>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|F.<math-up|coeffSize><around|(|Z<rsub|j>.*x|)>+t<rsub|N>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>Q<rsub|N>,>>>>
    </align*>

    and <math|0\<less\>Q<rsub|N>>. Since a remainder bound persists when its
    bill is increased, the same holds with <math|t<rsub|N>> replaced by the
    larger envelope <math|e<rsup|81*\<alpha\><rsub|N>>*r<rsub|N>+2<around|\||D|\|><sqrt|w<rsub|N>>*<space|0.17em>e<rsup|81*\<alpha\><rsub|N>>*s<rsub|N>>,
    which also satisfies the charge inequality. There are an attenuation
    <math|S<rsub|j>> and an actual reserved mass

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|j>=e<rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|N>+2|)><math-up|coverNumber><around|(|B<rsub|N>,D|)>>*s<rsub|N>
    </equation*>

    with <math|<math-up|Attainment><around|(|R<rsub|N>.*p,0,S<rsub|j>,A<rsub|j>|)>>
    and <math|A<rsub|j>*\<zeta\><rsub|d,N>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|N>*E<rsub|N>>,
    and

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|profile><around|(|R<rsub|N>.<math-up|activities>,Z<rsub|j>.<math-up|forced>|)>=<math-up|hook><around|(|R<rsub|N>.<math-up|activities>,R<rsub|N>.*p.<math-up|threshold>,S<rsub|j>|)>.
    </equation*>

    For every configuration <math|U>, its density and continuity clauses are

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|Z<rsub|j>.<math-up|density><around|(|0,T,R<rsub|N>,U|)>>|<cell|=<around|(|a<rsub|N><rsup|d>|)>*R<rsub|N>.<math-up|eval><around|(|U|)>*b<rsub|j><around|(|U|)>,>>|<row|<cell|b<rsub|j><around|(|U|)>>|<cell|=<around|(|a<rsub|N><rsup|d>|)><rsup|-1>*V.<math-up|places><around*|(|F.<math-up|scaled><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|-1>|)>*e<rsub|j>,0,U|)>,>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|25mm>b<rsub|j><text|
      is continuous>.>>>>
    </align*>

    The reserved-mass bound is

    <\equation*>
      <wide|A|\<bar\>><rsub|N>*\<zeta\><rsub|d,N>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|N>*E<rsub|N>,<space|2em>4*<around|(|1+c<rsub|N>|)>*Q<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>E<rsub|N>.
    </equation*>

    Equivalently, these are the <math|<math-up|Cream>> fields used by the
    selected field calibration.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Fix <math|j> and put

    <\equation*>
      a=F.<math-up|scaled><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|-1>|)>*e<rsub|j>,<space|2em>z=F.<math-up|coords><rsup|-1><around|(|a|)>.
    </equation*>

    Thus <math|a> is the dilated coefficient vector and <math|z> the label
    with these coordinates. By the inverse-coordinate identity,

    <\equation*>
      F.<math-up|scaled><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|N>|)>*<space|0.17em>F.<math-up|coords><around|(|z|)>=e<rsub|j>.
    </equation*>

    Set <math|y=V.<math-up|places><around|(|a,0,\<cdot\>|)>>. The pointwise
    section <math|V.<math-up|places><around|(|a,0,\<cdot\>|)>> reads only the
    translated stencil of <math|V>; by the stencil inclusion, this is
    contained in <math|D>. The margin assumption implies that the placement
    is nonwrapping: each coordinate of every stencil point has absolute value
    strictly smaller than one twentieth of the torus side, so the translated
    stencil has a unique representative in the centered box. Thus the
    placement reads only the field on <math|D> without wrapping. The same
    statements apply to <math|T>.

    The Credit estimate for <math|V> applies to each basis column on its
    footprint. For the rescaled axis <math|a=F.<math-up|scaled><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|-1>|)>*e<rsub|j>>,
    only column <math|j> is nonzero; its coefficient has modulus
    <math|\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|-s<rsub|j>>>. The terminal Credit gives
    every ordered derivative test admitted by the terminal budget, on its
    healthy translated footprint, the bound
    <math|b<rsub|N>*\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|s<rsub|j>>> for the unscaled
    column. Scaling cancels these factors, so
    <math|y=V.<math-up|places><around|(|a,0,\<cdot\>|)>> has the same
    regularity and derivative bound <math|b<rsub|N>>. The selected-family
    price clause gives the identical conclusion for
    <math|g=T.<math-up|places><around|(|a,0,\<cdot\>|)>> with bound
    <math|c<rsub|N>>. Regularity and derivative sup bounds are closed under
    subtraction, so <math|r=y-g> is regular on <math|D> and every such
    derivative is bounded by <math|r<rsub|N>=b<rsub|N>+c<rsub|N>>. This is
    the bound used in the regular part of the remainder. The global
    value-norm constants in <math|s<rsub|N>> are different: the definition of
    <math|<math-up|price><around|(|V|)>> and finite-coordinate expansion
    give, for every coefficient vector <math|v>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||V.<math-up|places><around|(|v,0,U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><math-up|price><around|(|V|)>*F.<math-up|coeffSize><around|(|v|)>,
    </equation*>

    and likewise for <math|T>. The scaled-coefficient estimate gives the
    explicit envelopes

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|V.<math-up|places><around|(|a,0,U|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><math-up|price><around|(|V|)><math-up|Seat><rsub|d,N>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|T.<math-up|places><around|(|a,0,U|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><math-up|price><around|(|T|)><math-up|Seat><rsub|d,N>.
    </equation*>

    These are the uniform pointwise bounds used in the residual estimate. The
    local insertion construction uses this read relation and the terminal
    Keeper to split the target into its unit coordinate, the core of the
    local section, and a residual which vanishes at the identity. With
    <math|g=T.<math-up|places><around|(|a,0,\<cdot\>|)>> and residual
    <math|r=y-g>, define

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|b<rsub|0>>|<cell|=r-<math-up|mask><rsub|D><around|(|r|)>+\<xi\>-<math-up|mask><rsub|A><around|(|\<xi\>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<xi\>>|<cell|=T.<math-up|places><around*|(|F.<math-up|scaled><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|-1>|)>*F.<math-up|core><around|(|e<rsub|j>|)>,0,\<cdot\>|)>.>>>>
    </align*>

    where <math|A> is the local-section footprint and each mask is the
    indicator of the corresponding healthy set. The read identity, unit
    normalization, and the projection condition in section soundness give the
    null-value and gauge-vanishing conditions needed for the residual
    <math|r>: both <math|y> and <math|g> read the same label <math|z>, so
    <math|r> vanishes at the identity configuration and its action jets
    through order <math|d> vanish. On the healthy set for <math|D>,
    <math|<math-up|mask><rsub|D><around|(|r|)>=r> and, since
    <math|A\<subseteq\>D>, <math|<math-up|mask><rsub|A><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=\<xi\>>;
    hence <math|b<rsub|0>=0> there. Off that set,
    <math|<around|\||r-<math-up|mask><rsub|D><around|(|r|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||r|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||y|\|>+<around|\||g|\|>>
    and <math|<around|\||\<xi\>-<math-up|mask><rsub|A><around|(|\<xi\>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<xi\>|\|>>.
    The undilated vectors <math|e<rsub|j>> and
    <math|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>e<rsub|j>> have coefficient size at
    most one; their dilations, the coefficient vectors of <math|g> and
    <math|\<xi\>>, therefore have coefficient size at most
    <math|<math-up|Seat><rsub|d,N>>. The triangle inequality and the two
    section prices then give the pointwise bound

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||b<rsub|0><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><math-up|price><around|(|V|)><math-up|Seat><rsub|d,N>+2<math-up|price><around|(|T|)><math-up|Seat><rsub|d,N>=s<rsub|N>.
    </equation*>

    This is the exact \Pstorms\Q majorant; the two local-section terms are
    charged separately.

    The target entering the coefficient density is

    <\equation*>
      b<rsub|j><around|(|U|)>=<around|(|a<rsub|N><rsup|d>|)><rsup|-1>*y<around|(|U|)>,<space|2em>y<around|(|U|)>=V.<math-up|places><around|(|F.<math-up|scaled><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|-1>|)>*e<rsub|j>,0,U|)>.
    </equation*>

    Thus <math|b<rsub|j>=\<cal-O\><rsub|N,p><around|(|e<rsub|j>|)>>.

    For the conditional construction, start with the unpriced terminal record
    and its coefficient vector <math|e<rsub|j>>. Its regular part is the
    section remainder on <math|D>; the forced correction is supported on
    <math|D>. To price the correction, let

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|mergePrice><around|(|D,h|)>=e<rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|N><math-up|coverNumber><around|(|B<rsub|N>,D|)>>*h*<around*|(|e<rsup|<around|\||D|\|>*w<rsub|N>>-1|)>.
    </equation*>

    When <math|<around|\||D|\|>*w<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>1>, the elementary
    inequality <math|e<rsup|t>-1\<leqslant\>2*t> for
    <math|0\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>1> yields

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|mergePrice><around|(|D,h|)>\<leqslant\><sqrt|w<rsub|N>>*<space|0.17em>2<around|\||D|\|><sqrt|w<rsub|N>>*e<rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|N><math-up|coverNumber><around|(|B<rsub|N>,D|)>>*h.
    </equation*>

    The local forced-family attachment input is realized on the selected
    terminal representation by the reserved-address construction of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:terminal-marked-construction>. Here is its
    application to this correction, rather than only to the section
    supplement treated there. The region <math|D> contains the origin, and
    <math|b<rsub|0>> is gauge invariant, measurable, determined by the field
    and flags on <math|D>, zero where <math|D> is healthy, and bounded by
    <math|s<rsub|N>>. For each compatible nonempty finite family <math|b> of
    old forced labels whose supports meet <math|D>, put

    <\equation*>
      T<rsub|b>=D\<cup\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>b>X<rsub|i>,<space|2em>I<rsub|b>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>b>I<rsub|i>,
    </equation*>

    choose its distinct reserved zero address <math|s<around|(|b|)>>, and
    install

    <\equation*>
      J*<around|(|s<around|(|b|)>,U|)>=\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|T<rsub|b>\<cap\><math-up|flags><around|(|U|)>=I<rsub|b>|}>>*b<rsub|0><around|(|U|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>b>K<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>.
    </equation*>

    The original additional predicate, locality and gauge invariance hold
    exactly as for <eqref|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T2> in that
    section. Whenever <math|b<rsub|0><around|(|U|)>\<ne\>0>, some site of
    <math|D> is flagged. Thus any old compatible collection contributing to
    the gas has a nonempty subcollection meeting <math|D>. Replacing that
    subcollection by <math|s<around|(|b|)>>, or reversing this replacement,
    is the same bijection used there. It identifies the forced profile with
    the hook of the singleton response on <math|D> before multiplying by the
    unmarked gas. With <math|M<around|(|X|)>=e<rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|N><math-up|coverNumber><around|(|B<rsub|N>,X|)>>>,
    the selected weighted site bound gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|b>M<around|(|T<rsub|b>|)>*sup<rsub|U><around|\||J*<around|(|s<around|(|b|)>,U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|N>*M<around|(|D|)>*<around*|(|e<rsup|<around|\||D|\|>*w<rsub|N>>-1|)>=<math-up|mergePrice><around|(|D,s<rsub|N>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed <math|M<around|(|T<rsub|b>|)>\<leqslant\>M<around|(|D|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>b>M<around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>
    and <math|<big|sum><rsub|i:X<rsub|i>\<cap\>D\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>M<around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>*sup
    <around|\||K<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||D|\|>*w<rsub|N>>; summing
    products over nonempty subsets gives the displayed bound. The lawful old
    gas bounds then justify the density regrouping absolutely. No property
    special to a section supplement was used. This supplies the attachment,
    its profile and its price for the present <math|b<rsub|0>>. The
    attainment recorded in the statement says that the singleton response
    <math|S<rsub|j>>, equal to <math|b<rsub|0>> on the support <math|D> and
    zero on other supports, has these properties and is bounded by
    <math|s<rsub|N>>; its reserved mass is by definition
    <math|e<rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|N>+2|)><math-up|coverNumber><around|(|B<rsub|N>,D|)>>>
    times that bound. Add this family to the regular coefficient produced by
    the support split. Pointwise, the primary part contributes the unit
    coordinate plus the masked core section
    <math|<math-up|mask><rsub|A><around|(|\<xi\>|)>>, the regular part
    contributes the masked residual <math|<math-up|mask><rsub|D><around|(|r|)>>,
    and the gas-mark sum contributes <math|b<rsub|0>>. Since the dilation
    fixes the unit coordinate and <math|T> has unit normalization,
    <math|g=<around|(|e<rsub|j>|)><rsub|0>+\<xi\>>, where
    <math|<around|(|e<rsub|j>|)><rsub|0>> is the unit coordinate of
    <math|e<rsub|j>>. Hence their sum is

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|e<rsub|j>|)><rsub|0>+<math-up|mask><rsub|A><around|(|\<xi\>|)>+<math-up|mask><rsub|D><around|(|r|)>+b<rsub|0>=<around|(|e<rsub|j>|)><rsub|0>+\<xi\>+r=g+r=y,
    </equation*>

    the target. The regular remainder's price is its ordinary mass plus the
    merge price. This construction also gives an attainment with reserved
    mass

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|j>=e<rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|N>+2|)><math-up|coverNumber><around|(|B<rsub|N>,D|)>>*s<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|81*<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|N>+2|)>>*s<rsub|N>.
    </equation*>

    The regular part of the remainder has support mass
    <math|e<rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|N>*\<kappa\><rsub|N>>*r<rsub|N>>, where
    <math|\<kappa\><rsub|N>=<math-up|coverNumber><around|(|B<rsub|N>,D|)>>.
    The merge-price estimate, in the <math|<sqrt|w<rsub|N>>>-weighted
    remainder budget, contributes <math|2<around|\||D|\|><sqrt|w<rsub|N>>*e<rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|N>*\<kappa\><rsub|N>>*s<rsub|N>>.
    Their sum is <math|t<rsub|N>>. Since <math|r<rsub|N>=b<rsub|N>+c<rsub|N>>,
    <math|\<kappa\><rsub|N>\<leqslant\>81>,
    <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N>\<geqslant\>0>,
    <math|<around|\||D|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||X|\|>>, and
    <math|<sqrt|w<rsub|N>>*s<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>1>,

    <\equation*>
      t<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|81*\<alpha\><rsub|N>>*<around|(|b<rsub|N>+c<rsub|N>+2<around|\||X|\|>|)>,<space|2em>1+t<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>2+e<rsup|81*\<alpha\><rsub|N>>*<around|(|b<rsub|N>+c<rsub|N>+2<around|\||X|\|>|)>=Q<rsub|N>.
    </equation*>

    Here the extra <math|1> is the coefficient size of <math|e<rsub|j>>; this
    proves the Cream charge clause. The actual reserved mass is

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|j>=e<rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|N>+2|)><math-up|coverNumber><around|(|B<rsub|N>,D|)>>*s<rsub|N>,
    </equation*>

    and the cover bound gives <math|A<rsub|j>\<leqslant\><wide|A|\<bar\>><rsub|N>>.
    Multiplying the latter envelope by <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,N>/u<rsub|N>>
    gives the reserved-mass clause from the second summand of
    <math|E<rsub|N>>; the first summand gives
    <math|4*<around|(|1+c<rsub|N>|)>*Q<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>E<rsub|N>>. This
    proves all the stated record fields.
  </proof>

  The proposition supplies the one-body coefficient and profile under its
  terminal margins, Keeper, Credit, regional bounds and attachment input. The
  selected field calibration in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:actual-marked-figment>
  uses one recentered label for all earlier-scale root tests. It additionally
  requires the selected transfer sequence's ordered adjacent-scale
  transitions, the backward induction, and the paid height and profile
  estimates. These do not follow from the local reserve and invitation
  arguments above. From these data and the terminal attainment, its proof
  constructs the served residual representation in
  paragraph<nbsp><reference|par:figment-served-residual>.

  <subsection|Calibration of a terminal marked field on its selected
  history><label|sec:actual-marked-portfolio-calibration>

  The local invitation in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-marked-field-local>
  is a terminal-scale construction. The public field calibration also needs
  one label correction that works at all earlier scales of the already
  selected history. This section follows that exact route: the campaign
  supplies the fixed portfolio, the terminal Cream supplies the final
  coefficient, and a backward induction through the selected transfer
  sequence relocates that coefficient. The history, doors, family, and
  transfer sequence remain fixed throughout.

  <subsubsection|From the campaign to the fixed portfolio>

  The campaign produced in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-campaign-producer>
  is an imprint with the powered slot-degree bound. Applying the
  fixed-stencil construction of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-marked-portfolio-section>
  gives, at each label degree <math|d>, one finite section <math|V<rsub|d>>,
  its stencil, its regrading <math|R<rsub|d>> and its credits. These choices
  are made before selecting a finite-volume history. On the public branch the
  same data are carried alongside the selected family. They do not replace
  the family's own local sections: at degree <math|d> the family keeps its
  fixed local section <math|T<rsub|d>>, which defines its observables
  <math|C.*O<around|(|v|)>>, and the portfolio section <math|V<rsub|d>> is
  compared with it on the selected history. The comparison is
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-marked-solaris>; its two ingredients
  are the one-body invitation of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-marked-invitation>,
  whose hypotheses refer to the selected family and its terminal Keeper, and
  the same-history calibration proved in this section.

  The precise same-history obligation is that the credits, budget triple and
  terminal Cream of the fixed section <math|V<rsub|d>> are attached to the
  selected family and its history. The subsequent argument does not replace
  that history by one chosen for each period, degree, or terminal volume.

  <subsubsection|Finite backward calibration>

  Fix one selected history <math|H>, period <math|p>, terminal volume
  <math|N>, label frame <math|F>, action <math|A>, section <math|B>, and a
  transfer sequence <math|M> on this history. At scale <math|i>, write
  <math|\<iota\><rsub|i>:F\<to\>F> for the transfer sequence index,
  <math|\<Pi\><rsub|i><around|(|v|)>> for its played coefficient, and
  <math|g<rsub|i><around|(|v|)>> for its charge. A state at scale <math|i>
  consists of a primary <math|\<varpi\><rsub|i>\<in\>F>, a residual
  coefficient <math|Z<rsub|i>>, and a bill <math|b<rsub|i>>. Its translated
  state at displacement <math|v> is

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-Z\><rsub|i><around|(|v|)>=Z<rsub|i>+\<Pi\><rsub|i>*<around|(|\<varpi\><rsub|i>+v|)>,<space|2em>c<rsub|i><around|(|v|)>=b<rsub|i>+g<rsub|i>*<around|(|\<varpi\><rsub|i>+v|)>.
  </equation*>

  Call the state keyed if <math|Z<rsub|i>.*x=0> and <math|Z<rsub|i>> has the
  prescribed format and remainder bound. Write <math|J<rsub|i>> for the
  history's clock index at scale <math|i>; the schedule has
  <math|J<rsub|i+1>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|i>+1>. Assume that the transition from
  scale <math|i+1> to <math|i> preserves keyedness, gives the exact density
  push identity for every <math|v>, and gives the direction relation
  comparing the leading coordinates for every <math|v>. It does not assert
  equality of those coordinates. Writing <math|L<rsub|i>> for the physical
  side at step <math|i>, <math|e<rsub|i>=<math-up|barunit><around|(|L<rsub|i>|)>>,
  and <math|K<rsub|i>=W<around|(|J<rsub|i+1>|)>>, for each displacement
  <math|v> there is an invertible map <math|B<rsub|i,v>> such that

  <\gather*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsub|i,v>*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,<around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsub|i,v><rsup|-1>*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|i><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,<space|2em>c<rsub|i><around|(|v|)>,t<rsub|i+1><around|(|v|)>\<geqslant\>0,>>|<row|<cell|<math-up|core><around|(|B<rsub|i,v>*x|)>=B<rsub|i,v><around|(|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>x|)>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|i><around|(|v|)>-B<rsub|i,v><around|(|<math-up|vowel><rsub|L<rsub|i>><around|(|x<rsub|i+1><around|(|v|)>|)>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>e<rsub|i>*<around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><around|(|x<rsub|i+1><around|(|v|)>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>+t<rsub|i+1><around|(|v|)>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|c<rsub|i><around|(|v|)>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|i>\<\|\|\><math-up|core><around|(|<math-up|vowel><rsub|L<rsub|i>><around|(|x<rsub|i+1><around|(|v|)>|)>|)>\<\|\|\><rsub|1>+e<rsub|i>*t<rsub|i+1><around|(|v|)>.>>>>
  </gather*>

  Here <math|W\<geqslant\>1> is a fixed nondecreasing price function on
  stages, <math|<math-up|barunit><around|(|L|)>=L<rsup|-3/8>>,
  <math|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>x> is <math|x> with its unit coordinate
  deleted, and <math|<math-up|vowel><rsub|L>> multiplies the coordinate of a
  basis vector of grade <math|<around|(|r<rsub|j>,s<rsub|j>|)>> by
  <math|L<rsup|d-r<rsub|j>>\<geqslant\>1>. Further,
  <math|x<rsub|i><around|(|v|)>,x<rsub|i+1><around|(|v|)>> are the leading
  coordinates of the translated states, and
  <math|t<rsub|i+1><around|(|v|)>=c<rsub|i+1><around|(|v|)>> is the upper
  state's nonnegative bill. More explicitly, these coordinates are the
  corresponding transfer sequence-index values
  <math|\<iota\><rsub|i>*<around|(|\<varpi\><rsub|i>+v|)>> and
  <math|\<iota\><rsub|i+1>*<around|(|\<varpi\><rsub|i+1>+v|)>>; the displayed
  direction inequality, not an equality, compares them. The maps may depend
  on <math|v>, but the displayed constants do not. The density identity is
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|i><around|(|v|)>=L<rsub|i><rsup|d><space|0.17em><math-ss|P><rsub|i>\<rho\><rsub|i+1><around|(|v|)>>,
  with <math|<math-ss|P><rsub|i>> exactly the transition push in the history.
  The finite Tale witness supplies the weighted support estimate for the
  specified exponential entries and forced profiles. Every bound is required
  at every displacement, not only at the base displacement <math|v=0>.

  For clarity, the paid one-step input has the following content. At scale
  <math|i> put <math|C<rsub|i>=<math-up|columns><around|(|i|)>>. The tuned
  coupon has a number <math|\<eta\><rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0> with
  <math|<around|(|1+2*C<rsub|i>|)>*\<eta\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>e<rsub|i>>. For
  every keyed upper coefficient <math|U> with zero leading coordinate,
  format, and bill <math|q>, it provides a finite wing and a coefficient
  correction whose leading error cost is at most <math|\<eta\><rsub|i>*q>,
  whose remainder cost is at most <math|\<eta\><rsub|i>*q>, and whose support
  charge is at most <math|2*q>. The associated Carol witness is a finite wing
  that works for every displacement and compares the upper and lower forced
  profiles, exponential entries, and charges. The one-step construction takes
  the correction coefficient <math|V>, sets
  <math|h=\<iota\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>*V.*x>, and uses

  <\equation*>
    \<varpi\><rsub|i>=\<varpi\><rsub|i+1>+h,<space|2em>Z<rsub|i>=V+\<Pi\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-h|)>,<space|2em>b<rsub|i>=\<eta\><rsub|i>*q+g<rsub|i>*<around|(|-h|)>.
  </equation*>

  The leading coordinate of <math|Z<rsub|i>> vanishes because
  <math|V.*x+\<iota\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-h|)>=0>. The format and remainder
  bounds follow by adding the coupon coefficient to the earned played
  coefficient. For every <math|v>, the linear density readout cancels the two
  terms at <math|h> and <math|-h>; the remaining density is exactly the
  transition push of the upper state's density. The direction estimate
  follows from the coupon's paid direction bounds, the transfer sequence cone
  estimate <math|g<rsub|i>*<around|(|\<pm\>h|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|i>*\<eta\><rsub|i>*q>,
  the Carol charge comparison, and <math|<around|(|1+2*C<rsub|i>|)>*\<eta\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>e<rsub|i>>.
  Finally, union the two finite wings and add their Tale inequalities; this
  gives one common finite support for all <math|v>. This is the precise
  one-step content used in the finite descent.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Backward propagation of one terminal
    coefficient><label|lem:actual-marked-antilles>Let
    <math|0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>l\<leqslant\>m\<leqslant\>N>. Suppose the
    history is selected and the same transfer sequence is admissible and
    responds at every level from <math|r> through <math|N>. Suppose its
    one-step response on each door <math|D<rsub|i>>,
    <math|l\<leqslant\>i\<less\>m>, is the response described above: for
    every keyed input it supplies a keyed output, the all-displacement
    density identity and direction bound, and a finite Tale witness, with the
    tuning and Coupon bounds stated there. At level <math|m>, let <math|Z> be
    a coefficient with the required format, remainder bound <math|t>, and

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|Z.*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>+t\<leqslant\>w.
    </equation*>

    Assume also the scale room and bank budgets. Then there are keyed states
    <math|<around|(|\<varpi\><rsub|i>,Z<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|)>> for
    <math|l\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>m> such that:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>the terminal state is the state obtained by adjoining <math|Z> to
      the level-<math|m> transfer sequence and subtracting the transfer
      sequence's primary which represents <math|Z.*x>;

      <item>every adjacent pair is related by the supplied response on
      <math|D<rsub|i>>;

      <item>with <math|\<varpi\><rsub|l>> the bottom primary, evaluate the
      translated states at the displacement <math|-\<varpi\><rsub|l>>: set
      <math|x<rsub|i>=\<cal-Z\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-\<varpi\><rsub|l>|)>.*x>
      and <math|c<rsub|i>=c<rsub|i>*<around|(|-\<varpi\><rsub|l>|)>=b<rsub|i>+g<rsub|i>*<around|(|\<varpi\><rsub|i>-\<varpi\><rsub|l>|)>>.
      Then, for every <math|l\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>m>,

      <\equation*>
        exp <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<frac|\<sigma\><rsub|m>-\<sigma\><rsub|i>|8>|)>*<around|(|c<rsub|i>+<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>|)>\<leqslant\>4*<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*w,<space|2em>C<rsub|m>=<math-up|columns><around|(|m|)>,
      </equation*>

      where <math|\<sigma\><rsub|i>> is the history scale parameter;

      <item>with the same <math|x<rsub|i>,c<rsub|i>>, and with
      <math|C<rsub|i>=<math-up|columns><around|(|i|)>> and
      <math|s<rsub|i>=L<rsub|i><rsup|1/8>>,

      <\gather*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|32*<around|(|1+C<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|i>*<space|1em><around|(|l\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>m|)>,<space|2em>s<rsub|i><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|i+1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|i+1>*<space|1em><around|(|l\<leqslant\>i\<less\>m|)>,>>|<row|<cell|c<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*w.>>>>
      </gather*>
    </enumerate>

    The terminal state has, for every <math|v\<in\>F>, exact identities

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-Z\><rsub|m><around|(|v|)>.*x=Z.*x+\<iota\><rsub|m><around|(|v|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-Z\><rsub|m><around|(|v|)>.<math-up|density>=Z.<math-up|density>+\<Pi\><rsub|m><around|(|v|)>.<math-up|density>,
    </equation*>

    and its terminal bill obeys

    <\equation*>
      c<rsub|m><around|(|v|)>\<leqslant\>w+C<rsub|m>*<around*|(|w+<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-Z\><rsub|m><around|(|v|)>.*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>|)>,<space|2em>C<rsub|m>=<math-up|columns><around|(|m|)>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    At the terminal level set <math|a=\<iota\><rsub|m><rsup|-1>*<around|(|Z.*x|)>>.
    In the public application <math|m=N>, and the top clause of `Sung` gives
    <math|<around|(|M.<math-up|tone><space|0.17em>N|)>.<math-up|index>=<math-up|id>>:
    its played coefficient has leading value <math|v> at every label
    <math|v>. Thus in that application <math|\<iota\><rsub|N><around|(|v|)>=v>.
    Form the residual coefficient by adding to <math|Z> the played field
    <math|\<Pi\><rsub|m>*<around|(|-a|)>>, and set its bill to
    <math|t+g<rsub|m>*<around|(|-a|)>>. Its leading coordinate is zero
    because <math|\<Pi\><rsub|m>*<around|(|-a|)>.*x=\<iota\><rsub|m>*<around|(|-a|)>=-Z.*x>.
    This proves keyedness. For displacement <math|v>, linearity of the
    transfer sequence readout gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|Z+\<Pi\><rsub|m>*<around|(|-a|)>+\<Pi\><rsub|m>*<around|(|a+v|)>|)>.*x=Z.*x+\<iota\><rsub|m><around|(|v|)>,
    </equation*>

    and linearity of its density gives cancellation of the <math|-a> and
    <math|a> terms, leaving <math|Z> plus the density of
    <math|\<Pi\><rsub|m><around|(|v|)>>. These are exact identities.

    For the charge bound, the transfer sequence's cone estimate gives
    <math|g<rsub|m><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|m><around|\<\|\|\>|\<iota\><rsub|m><around|(|x|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>.
    Since <math|\<iota\><rsub|m><around|(|a|)>=Z.*x> and
    <math|\<iota\><rsub|m>*<around|(|a+v|)>=\<cal-Z\><rsub|m><around|(|v|)>.*x>,
    it follows that

    <\equation*>
      c<rsub|m><around|(|v|)>=t+g<rsub|m>*<around|(|-a|)>+g<rsub|m>*<around|(|a+v|)>\<leqslant\>t+C<rsub|m>*<around|\<\|\|\>|Z.*x*<around|\<\|\|\>|<rsub|1>+C<rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>>*\<cal-Z\><rsub|m><around|(|v|)>.*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>w+C<rsub|m>*<around*|(|w+<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-Z\><rsub|m><around|(|v|)>.*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>|)>.
    </equation*>

    This is the displayed terminal bill bound.

    We now descend from <math|m> to <math|l>. At a step <math|i>, assume a
    keyed state at <math|i+1> has been constructed. The same transfer
    sequence supplies its Heard and Responds data at <math|i> and <math|i+1>.
    Apply the paid one-step response with the state's format and remainder
    bound; its zero leading coordinate is exactly the Coupon hypothesis. The
    output is a keyed state at <math|i>, its transfer sequence-index formula,
    the density push identity, the paid direction inequality, and the finite
    forced-profile witness. Replace only the state at <math|i> and keep all
    states at levels above <math|i> unchanged. Thus all already proved chair
    and transition clauses persist, and the new clauses hold at <math|i>.
    Finite downward induction constructs the full sequence. This is a finite
    induction on <math|m-l>; it does not pass to a subsequence or alter the
    selected history.

    For the signed difference, let <math|\<varpi\><rsub|l>> be the primary of
    the bottom state and evaluate every state at displacement
    <math|-\<varpi\><rsub|l>>. The bottom played coordinate is then zero. At
    each level, the format and remainder clauses follow from keyedness and
    the transfer sequence's earned bounds; transition and density clauses
    follow from the all-displacement response identities. For the numerical
    propagation fix this displacement <math|v=-\<varpi\><rsub|l>> and
    abbreviate <math|x<rsub|i>=x<rsub|i><around|(|v|)>>,
    <math|c<rsub|i>=c<rsub|i><around|(|v|)>>, and
    <math|t<rsub|i>=t<rsub|i><around|(|v|)>>; thus the map used at step
    <math|i> is the possibly displacement-dependent <math|B<rsub|i,v>>, whose
    norm bounds are uniform. Put <math|C<rsub|i>=<math-up|columns><around|(|i|)>>,
    <math|s<rsub|i>=L<rsub|i><rsup|1/8>>,
    <math|e<rsub|i>=s<rsub|i><rsup|-3>>, and
    <math|A<rsub|i>=32*<around|(|1+C<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>>. Room cost says
    <math|64*W*<around|(|J<rsub|i>+1|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|i>>; since
    <math|J<rsub|i+1>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|i>+1> and <math|W> is nondecreasing,
    this yields <math|64*K<rsub|i><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|i>>. The bank
    inequality at step <math|i>, together with
    <math|W<around|(|J<rsub|i+1>|)>\<leqslant\>W*<around|(|J<rsub|i+1>+1|)>>,
    yields <math|100000*K<rsub|i><rsup|6>*<around|(|1+C<rsub|i+1>|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|i>>.
    Also <math|K<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1> and <math|C<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1>.
    Consequently

    <\equation*>
      4*K<rsub|i>*e<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>4*K<rsub|i>*A<rsub|i+1>*e<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em><around|(|4*K<rsub|i><rsup|2>+1|)>*<around|(|s<rsub|i>*e<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>1.
    </equation*>

    Indeed, writing <math|C=C<rsub|i+1>> and <math|K=K<rsub|i>>, the bank
    bound implies <math|s<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>128*K<rsup|2>*<around|(|1+C|)><rsup|2>>,
    since <math|100000*K<rsup|6>\<geqslant\>128*K<rsup|2>> for
    <math|K\<geqslant\>1>. Thus <math|4*K*A<rsub|i+1>=128*K*<around|(|1+C|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|i>>
    and <math|4*K\<leqslant\>s<rsub|i>>. Also
    <math|<around|(|4*K<rsup|2>+1|)>*s<rsub|i>*e<rsub|i>=<around|(|4*K<rsup|2>+1|)>*s<rsub|i><rsup|-2>\<leqslant\>1>
    because <math|s<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>128*K<rsup|2>>. These are the three
    displayed numerical inequalities. The direction inequalities for the
    translated states imply, with <math|u=<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
    <math|u<rsub|+>=<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|i+1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>, and
    <math|y=<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|vowel><rsub|L<rsub|i>><around|(|x<rsub|i+1>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,

    <\equation*>
      y\<leqslant\>K<rsub|i>*<around|(|u+e<rsub|i>*<around|(|y+c<rsub|i+1>|)>|)>,<space|2em>c<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|i>*y+e<rsub|i>*c<rsub|i+1>,<space|2em>u<rsub|+>\<leqslant\>y.
    </equation*>

    If <math|A<rsub|i>*u\<leqslant\>c<rsub|i>>, combining these inequalities
    with the three numerical bounds gives
    <math|A<rsub|i+1>*u<rsub|+>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|i+1>> and
    <math|s<rsub|i>*c<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|i+1>>. Indeed, substituting
    <math|u\<leqslant\><around|(|K<rsub|i>*y+e<rsub|i>*c<rsub|i+1>|)>/A<rsub|i>>
    into the first direction inequality gives

    <\equation*>
      y\<leqslant\><around*|(|<frac|K<rsub|i><rsup|2>|A<rsub|i>>+K<rsub|i>*e<rsub|i>|)>*y+<around*|(|<frac|K<rsub|i>*e<rsub|i>|A<rsub|i>>+K<rsub|i>*e<rsub|i>|)>*c<rsub|i+1>.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|A<rsub|i>=32*<around|(|1+C<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>>, we have
    <math|K<rsub|i><rsup|2>/A<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1/32>;
    <math|K<rsub|i>*e<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1/4>; and
    <math|A<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1>. Thus the coefficient of <math|y> is at
    most <math|9/32\<less\>1/2>, and the coefficient of <math|c<rsub|i+1>> is
    at most <math|2*K<rsub|i>*e<rsub|i>>. It follows that
    <math|y\<leqslant\>4*K<rsub|i>*e<rsub|i>*c<rsub|i+1>>. Now
    <math|u<rsub|+>\<leqslant\>y> and <math|4*K<rsub|i>*A<rsub|i+1>*e<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1>
    give <math|A<rsub|i+1>*u<rsub|+>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|i+1>>. In the same way
    <math|s<rsub|i>*u<rsub|+>\<leqslant\>4*K<rsub|i>*<around|(|s<rsub|i>*e<rsub|i>|)>*c<rsub|i+1>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|i+1>>,
    because <math|4*K<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>4*K<rsub|i><rsup|2>+1> and
    <math|<around|(|4*K<rsub|i><rsup|2>+1|)>*<around|(|s<rsub|i>*e<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>1>;
    this is the second inequality of the last item of the lemma. Also
    <math|c<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|i>*y+e<rsub|i>*c<rsub|i+1>> gives
    <math|s<rsub|i>*c<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><around|(|4*K<rsub|i><rsup|2>+1|)>*s<rsub|i>*e<rsub|i>*c<rsub|i+1>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|i+1>>
    by the third numerical bound. At the bottom, keyedness gives
    <math|x<rsub|l>=0> and nonnegative cost. Thus induction gives
    <math|A<rsub|i><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|i>>
    at every level, and iterating <math|s<rsub|i>*c<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|i+1>>
    gives <math|<big|prod><rsub|j=i><rsup|m-1>s<rsub|j>*c<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|m>>.
    The scale identity is <math|<big|prod><rsub|j=i><rsup|m-1>s<rsub|j>=exp
    <around|(|<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|m>-\<sigma\><rsub|i>|)>/8|)>>.

    At the top the terminal bill bound and
    <math|A<rsub|m><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|m>>
    imply <math|2*C<rsub|m><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|m>>
    and hence <math|c<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*w>. This
    completes the last item of the lemma. Since
    <math|A<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1>, also <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|i>>.
    Therefore <math|exp <around|(|<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|m>-\<sigma\><rsub|i>|)>/8|)>*<around|(|c<rsub|i>+<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>|)>\<leqslant\>2*c<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>4*<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*w>.
    This proves the cone clause directly from the room and bank inequalities,
    on the same finite interval and history.
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|Same-history inputs to the lemma.> For each step, the
  response is supplied by a same-history Tone record, a paid Coupon for every
  keyed silent upper coefficient, and the matching Carol/Tale witness. The
  Coupon fixes the input exponential entry, forced profile, distinguished
  leading value, and density; its output coefficient has the specified exact
  density identity and paid leading/remainder bounds. The Carol witness
  includes the upper and lower forced profiles and exponential entries at
  every displacement, together with common finite support and the charge
  comparison used above. These are exact inputs, not continuity limits.

  The local three-card algebra which produces the paid Coupon from a
  Heading/Stampede record is proved in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:three-port-history>.
  It uses the common-history identities, waybill and forced-price estimate
  there, together with the three selected card estimates and the finite
  threshold maximum. This finite descent consumes the resulting Coupon and
  Carol records; it does not reprove the card algebra. The common-history
  attachment of these records is supplied by
  Propositions<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-reprise>
  and<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-music>, under the common selected-history
  purchases. The zero-coordinate Coupon used in this descent is supplied by
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-zero-coordinate-coupon>, with its
  insertion, soundness and merger hypotheses verified as described in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-centered-service>.

  <subsubsection|Application to the terminal Cream>

  We now state the calibration used by the selected field argument. Fix one
  family <math|C> which makes the action, its selected history, a period
  <math|p>, a terminal volume <math|N>, and a degree <math|d>. Write
  <math|a<rsub|i>> for the mesh at level <math|i>,
  <math|L<rsub|i>=a<rsub|i>/a<rsub|i+1>> for the block side,
  <math|\<sigma\><rsub|i>> for the running scale,
  <math|C<rsub|i>=<math-up|columns><around|(|i|)>>, <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i>>
  for the support exponent and <math|B<rsub|i>> for the support radius of the
  level-<math|i> budget, <math|\<theta\><rsub|i>> for its threshold,
  <math|J<rsub|i>> for the clock index, and <math|T> for the local section of
  the family at degree <math|d>, with frame <math|F> and maximal factor grade
  <math|q>. The following notation is used for the root tests:

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-up|puffs><around|(|H<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=L<rsub|i><rsup|1/8>,>|<cell|<math-up|gain><around|(|N,i|)>>|<cell|=e<rsup|<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|N>-\<sigma\><rsub|i>|)>/8>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><around|(|H,i,d|)>|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|=a<rsub|i><rsup|-d>,>|<cell|<math-up|escrow><around|(|N,k,E|)>>|<cell|=E/<math-up|gain><around|(|N,k|)>,>>|<row|<cell|<math-up|stakes><around|(|m,k|)>>|<cell|=exp
    <around*|(|<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4><space|0.17em><math-up|Treasury><rsub|k>+5|)>,>|<cell|<math-up|flight><rsub|-\<gamma\>><around|(|N,p|)>>|<cell|=e<rsup|-\<gamma\>*\<sigma\><rsub|N>>,>>>>
  </align*>

  with <math|\<gamma\>=1/8> and <math|<math-up|Treasury><rsub|k>> the vacuum
  treasury per site at level <math|k>, and

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|cider><around|(|d,m,k|)>=<math-up|stakes><around|(|m,k|)><space|0.17em><frac|4*a<rsub|k><rsup|-d>|\<zeta\><rsub|d,k>>*<space|0.17em>e<rsup|\<gamma\>*\<sigma\><rsub|k>>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|m\<leqslant\>N>, <math|<math-up|rootTest><rsub|m;N,p><around|(|f|)>>
  is the centered root test of Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-estimates>
  for the centered tiling of depth <math|m> based at the origin and the
  finite-volume law <math|\<mu\><rsub|N,p>>: the <math|K>th root of the
  modulus of the expectation of the product of the <math|K> prescribed copies
  of <math|f>, where <math|K=<around|\||<math-up|Site><around|(|m,p|)>|\|>>
  is the number of cells. We say that <math|m> is <em|opened at> <math|k>,
  and write <math|\<cal-O\>*<around|(|m,k|)>>, if <math|m\<leqslant\>k> and
  <math|B<rsub|k><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>>.

  <subparagraph|The paid selected-history record.> The calibration consumes
  the following record on the fixed family and history. The family, the
  history, the numerical sequences and the indexed field of doors are fixed
  globally: they do not depend on <math|N>, <math|p> or <math|d>, and the
  items are required for all of them. The record is assumed in this section.
  Its simultaneous production on the selected public run, including the
  reserved-address and fee conditions of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:terminal-marked-construction>,
  is supplied under the common selected-history purchases in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-centered-service>; its paragraph on
  the paid selected-history record identifies each of (R1)\U(R7) with its
  supplier. It consists of a nondecreasing function <math|W\<geqslant\>1> on
  stages, real sequences <math|u<rsub|i>,\<psi\><rsub|i>,\<phi\><rsub|i>,\<delta\><rsub|i>>,
  heights <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,i>>, a sequence of natural numbers
  <math|r<rsub|i>>, and one gauge door <math|D<rsub|N,p,i>> between the
  representatives at levels <math|i+1> and <math|i> for all <math|i\<less\>N>
  and all <math|p>, with these properties.

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(R1)><em|Room and bank.> For every <math|i>,
    <math|64*W*<around|(|J<rsub|i>+1|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|i>> and
    <math|100000*<space|0.17em>W*<around|(|J<rsub|i+1>+1|)><rsup|6>*<around|(|1+C<rsub|i+1>|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|i><rsup|1/8>>.

    <item*|(R2)><em|Scale bounds.> For every <math|i>:
    <math|\<phi\><rsub|i>\<gtr\>0>, <math|\<delta\><rsub|i>\<gtr\>0>,
    <math|1\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|i>*\<phi\><rsub|i>*e<rsup|B<rsub|i>/128>>,
    <math|B<rsub|i+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|2>> and
    <math|8\<leqslant\>B<rsub|i>>.

    <item*|(R3)><em|Occupancy.> The sequence <math|r<rsub|i>> is
    nondecreasing and <math|r<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1>.

    <item*|(R4)><em|Transition service.> Put
    <math|<math-ss|s><around|(|B|)>=e*<space|0.17em><around|(|2*<around|(|16*B+9|)>|)><rsup|4>>,
    and let <math|w<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|i>> be the forced allowance at
    level <math|i>. For all <math|N>, <math|p> and <math|i\<less\>N> there
    are reals <math|y,w> such that:

    <\itemize>
      <item>the door <math|D<rsub|N,p,i>> satisfies the multi-marker offer
      input with occupancy <math|r<rsub|i>>, offer threshold
      <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i+1>-4> and offer allowance <math|y>, ordinary
      exponent <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i>+<frac|1|2>> with site budget <math|1>,
      and flagged exponent <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i>+1> with site budget
      <math|w>. Its full conditions remain an input; they are not derived
      here. They include the pushed representation of every finite census of
      offers of occupancy at most <math|r<rsub|i>>, soundness and tuning of
      the door, and a contact condition on the upper representation;

      <item><math|0\<less\>\<delta\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1>,
      <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i>\<geqslant\>100>,
      <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i+1>\<geqslant\>100>;

      <item>with <math|T=r<rsub|i><math-ss|s><around|(|B<rsub|i+1>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|i>+4*\<delta\><rsub|i>>,
      one has <math|T+e<rsup|T>*T\<leqslant\>y>;

      <item>with <math|c=w+r<rsub|i><math-ss|s><around|(|B<rsub|i>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|i>>,
      one has <math|<around|(|2*B<rsub|i>+1|)><rsup|4>*c\<leqslant\><frac|1|2>>
      and <math|e<rsup|c>-1\<leqslant\>1>.
    </itemize>

    Moreover, at every level <math|i>: <math|u<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>,
    <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i>\<geqslant\>3>, and with
    <math|C=w<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|i>+r<rsub|i><math-ss|s><around|(|B<rsub|i>|)>*u<rsub|i>>
    one has <math|<around|(|2*B<rsub|i>+1|)><rsup|4>*C\<leqslant\>3> and
    <math|e<rsup|C>-1\<leqslant\>1>.

    <item*|(R5)><em|Heights.> For every <math|i\<geqslant\><math-up|ref><around|(|d|)>>:
    <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,i>\<gtr\>0>, <math|\<phi\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1>,
    <math|\<psi\><rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>,

    <\gather*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<phi\><rsub|i>*L<rsub|i><rsup|d+1>*\<zeta\><rsub|d,i>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|d,i+1>,<space|2em>\<phi\><rsub|i>*L<rsub|i><rsup|d>*C<rsub|i+1>*\<zeta\><rsub|d,i>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|d,i+1>,>>|<row|<cell|\<zeta\><rsub|d,i>*C<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>2*\<cal-O\><rsub|d>*<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|i>+2*C<rsub|i+1>|)>*\<zeta\><rsub|d,i+1>\<leqslant\>1,>>>>
    </gather*>

    where <math|\<cal-O\><rsub|d>> is the orbit cost of the frame. For every
    terminal level <math|N\<geqslant\><math-up|ref><around|(|d|)>> and period
    <math|p> there is <math|X\<geqslant\>1> with
    <math|<around|\||T<around|(|e<rsub|j>|)><around|(|0,U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>X>
    for all axes and configurations, the footprint of <math|T> at the origin
    has cover number at most one, and <math|e<rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|N>+2>*X*\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|-q>*\<zeta\><rsub|d,N>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|N>>.

    <item*|(R6)><em|The transfer sequence.> For every
    <math|N\<geqslant\><math-up|ref><around|(|d|)>> and <math|p> there is a
    transfer sequence <math|M> on this history at <math|<around|(|N,p|)>>
    whose played coefficients are the entries of the family's transfer data,
    which is admissible at every level from
    <math|<math-up|ref><around|(|d|)>> to <math|N> and responds across every
    intervening door. Its terminal coefficient family has the properties of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:terminal-marked-voice>: lead <math|v> at
    every label <math|v>, so that the terminal index is the identity, zero
    regular part, charge at most <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
    the density identity <eqref|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T5>
    of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:terminal-marked-construction>, and
    forced profile equal to the hook of the singleton response
    <math|f<rsub|v>>. It has a Carol witness with price
    <math|\<psi\><rsub|i>> at every door <math|D<rsub|N,p,i>>,
    <math|<math-up|ref><around|(|d|)>\<leqslant\>i\<less\>N>: a finite set of
    wings and, for every label <math|v>, a Tale across the door relating the
    exponential entry and forced profile of the upper played coefficient to
    the forced profile of the lower one, with upper price
    <math|\<cal-O\><rsub|d><around|\<\|\|\>|\<iota\><rsub|i+1><around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>*<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|i>+2*C<rsub|i+1>|)>>
    and lower price <math|g<rsub|i><around|(|v|)>>. The full conditions on
    the wing and Tale witnesses remain inputs here. The transfer sequence may
    depend on <math|<around|(|d,N,p|)>>.

    <item*|(R7)><em|The paid one-step input.> At every door
    <math|D<rsub|N,p,i>>, <math|<math-up|ref><around|(|d|)>\<leqslant\>i\<less\>N>,
    there is a number <math|\<eta\><rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0> with
    <math|<around|(|1+2*C<rsub|i>|)>*\<eta\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\><math-up|barunit><around|(|L<rsub|i>|)>>,
    the door is sound, <math|\<psi\><rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>, and there is a
    finite set of wings such that every upper coefficient with zero leading
    coordinate, the terminal format and remainder bill <math|q> has a Coupon
    across the door with budgets <math|<around|(|\<eta\><rsub|i>*q,\<eta\><rsub|i>*q,2*q|)>>,
    as described before Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:actual-marked-antilles>.
    The full conditions on the Coupon beyond that description remain an input
    here.
  </enumerate>

  The multi-marker offer input in (R4) has the following interface. Fix one
  of its doors, its upper and lower representations, and the associated
  parent map from upper to lower sites. Take any finite marker set <math|V>
  and any downward-closed family of subsets containing the empty set and all
  singletons; its marker algebra has commuting square-zero generators, with
  only the allowed subsets as modes. Choose sites <math|o<rsub|v>> on the
  upper lattice, strictly positive weights <math|t<rsub|v>>, and any finite
  family of offers. Each offer consists of an upper activity leaf with
  support <math|P>, a nonempty allowed marker mode <math|A>, and a complex
  coefficient <math|c>; its marker sites <math|<around|{|o<rsub|v>:v\<in\>A|}>>
  lie in <math|P>. Its fare is the leaf weight times
  <math|<around|\||c|\|>*<big|prod><rsub|v\<in\>A>t<rsub|v>>. The offer is
  priced if, for every upper site <math|x>, the sum of fares over supports
  containing <math|x>, each multiplied by
  <math|e<rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|i+1>-4|)><math-up|coverNumber><around|(|B<rsub|i+1>,P|)>>>,
  is at most <math|y>. Assume also that at most <math|r<rsub|i>> markers have
  any one parent site.

  For every such census, weights, sites and priced offer, the input supplies
  lower-lattice polynomial families <math|F<rsub|P>> indexed by supports and
  <math|K<rsub|P,I>> indexed by support\Uinventory pairs. Both are gauge
  invariant, measurable and local to their supports. On the lower lattice the
  marker positions are the parent sites <math|b<rsub|v>=<math-up|parent><around|(|o<rsub|v>|)>>,
  and each coefficient of a mode <math|A> in these families vanishes unless
  <math|<around|{|b<rsub|v>:v\<in\>A|}>> is contained in its support. An
  <math|F<rsub|P>> term vanishes unless <math|P> is healthy and has zero
  empty-mode coefficient. A <math|K<rsub|P,I>> term vanishes unless <math|I>
  is nonempty, <math|I\<subseteq\>P>, and the flags in <math|P> are exactly
  <math|I>. Each family has nonnegative summable support prices. For each
  support or support\Uinventory pair, there is one nonnegative majorant for
  each mode that bounds the absolute value of that mode's coefficient for
  every configuration. The weighted sum of these configuration-independent
  majorants is at most the corresponding support price; the weight of a mode
  <math|A> is <math|<big|prod><rsub|v\<in\>A>t<rsub|v>>. At each lower site
  the support prices, multiplied by the exponential of the support cover
  number, sum to at most <math|1> for <math|F> with exponent
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i>+1/2>, and at most <math|w> for <math|K> with
  exponent <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i>+1>.

  Their assembled lower representation equals exactly the transition push of
  the offered upper polynomial. For every lower support\Uinventory pair and
  configuration, the empty-mode coefficient of <math|K> is the ordinary lower
  activity profile; its singleton coefficient for marker <math|v> is the sum,
  over the offered leaves whose mode is <math|<around|{|v|}>>, of their
  coefficients times their door-transported profiles. These identities hold
  pointwise, not merely after integration. The same door is also required to
  have the soundness and tuning interfaces: a uniform forced-cost bound and
  motion equivariance for its leaf transports, and compatibility of those
  transports with every countable grouped leaf sum. Finally, the upper
  representation must have the contact addresses: every nonempty support has
  a zero-activity regular address, and every nonempty inventory contained in
  a support has an address eligible whenever the flags realize that
  inventory. These are hypotheses of the present calibration statement. On
  the retained public history they are supplied by
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:selected-retention-public>, with the common
  purchases and local analytic hypotheses stated there.

  The items (R1)\U(R3), (R5) and the inequalities of (R4) are numerical. The
  remaining parts of (R4), (R6) and (R7) are the same-history records; they
  must hold for the one family and the one indexed field of doors, for all
  <math|d>, <math|N\<geqslant\><math-up|ref><around|(|d|)>> and <math|p>. For
  each <math|<around|(|d,N,p|)>> one transfer sequence realizing the family's
  transfer entries is chosen, and that choice is retained throughout all
  subsequent scale, census and motion tests at those indices. Choosing a
  different history or door field for each period or terminal volume is
  insufficient.

  <subparagraph|The terminal Cream.> A terminal Cream at
  <math|<around|(|N,p,d|)>> with field <math|b>, charge <math|Q>, bill
  <math|t> and budget <math|E> is a coefficient <math|Z> at level <math|N>
  with the fields of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-marked-invitation>:
  the terminal format; the remainder bound with weight
  <math|<sqrt|w<rsub|N>>> and bill <math|t>;
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|Z.*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>+t\<leqslant\>Q>;
  <math|0\<less\>Q>; <math|4*<around|(|1+C<rsub|N>|)>*Q\<leqslant\>E>; an
  attainment with reserved mass <math|B<rsub|<math-up|cream>>> such that
  <math|B<rsub|<math-up|cream>>*\<zeta\><rsub|d,N>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|N>*E>,
  together with the forced-profile hook identity; the density equation

  <\equation*>
    Z.<math-up|density><around|(|U|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|d>*R<rsub|N>.<math-up|eval><around|(|U|)>*b<around|(|U|)>;
  </equation*>

  and continuity of <math|b>. Note that <math|E\<gtr\>0>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Same-history field calibration><label|prop:actual-marked-figment>Assume
    <math|C.<math-up|ref><around|(|d|)>\<less\>N>, that the history has the
    opening schedule of Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-site-geometry>,
    and that the paid selected-history record (R1)\U(R7) holds. Let <math|Z>
    be a terminal Cream at <math|<around|(|N,p,d|)>> with field <math|b>,
    charge <math|Q> and budget <math|E>. Then there is one label <math|v>
    such that

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|puffs><around|(|H<rsub|N-1>|)>*<around|\<\|\|\>|Z.*x-X<rsub|N><rsup|-1>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1+C<rsub|N>|)>*Q,<space|2em>C<rsub|N>=<math-up|columns><around|(|N|)>,
    </equation*>

    and, for every <math|k> with <math|<math-up|ref><around|(|d|)>\<leqslant\>k\<less\>N>,
    every <math|m> opened at <math|k>, and every motion <math|s>,

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|rootTest><rsub|m;N,p><around*|(|s\<cdot\><around|(|b-C.*O<around|(|v|)>|)>|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|cider><around|(|d,m,k|)>*<space|0.17em>E<space|0.17em><math-up|flight><rsub|-\<gamma\>><around|(|N,p|)>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|X<rsub|N>> is the terminal coefficient calibration isomorphism
    on the selected family, and <math|C.*O<around|(|v|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>*T*<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|-1><math-plus|\<cdot\><rsub|F>>X<rsub|N><rsup|-1>*v|)><around|(|0,\<cdot\>|)>>
    is its local observable at the origin. The label <math|v> and all bounds
    use the same <math|C>, history, and transfer sequence.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The record (R6) supplies the transfer sequence <math|M> and its Heard and
    Responds fields from the bottom reference
    <math|r=<math-up|ref><around|(|d|)>>. Its Carol witnesses, the Coupons
    (R7), the budgets (R1), and the terminal Cream bounds are the inputs of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:actual-marked-antilles> with <math|l=r>,
    <math|m=N> and terminal cone parameter <math|w=Q>, as specified by the
    Cream charge bound. The larger budget <math|E> is reserved for the
    root-test error estimate. This produces one backward sequence of keyed
    states <math|<around|(|\<varpi\><rsub|i>,Z<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|)>>,
    <math|r\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>N>, on the original history. Let
    <math|a=\<varpi\><rsub|r>> be the primary of its state at <math|r>, and
    set <math|v=X<rsub|N><around|(|a|)>>. By invertibility,
    <math|X<rsub|N><rsup|-1>*v=a>. In the notation of the lemma put
    <math|x<rsub|i>=\<cal-Z\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-a|)>.*x> and
    <math|c<rsub|i>=c<rsub|i>*<around|(|-a|)>>.

    The terminal coordinate identity of the lemma and
    <math|\<iota\><rsub|N>=<math-up|id>> give
    <math|x<rsub|N>=Z.*x+\<iota\><rsub|N>*<around|(|-a|)>=Z.*x-X<rsub|N><rsup|-1>*v>.
    The last item of the lemma gives <math|c<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1+C<rsub|N>|)>*Q>
    and, at the step <math|i=N-1>,

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|puffs><around|(|H<rsub|N-1>|)><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|N>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|N>.
    </equation*>

    Combining these inequalities proves the first displayed estimate. The
    step <math|N-1> lies in the range of the lemma because
    <math|<math-up|ref><around|(|d|)>\<leqslant\>N-1>, by the explicit
    assumption <math|<math-up|ref><around|(|d|)>\<less\>N>.

    For the root-test estimate fix <math|k> and <math|m> as in the statement,
    abbreviate <math|\<zeta\><rsub|i>=\<zeta\><rsub|d,i>>, and choose

    <\equation*>
      w=<frac|\<zeta\><rsub|k>/4|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><around|(|H,k,d|)>|\<\|\|\>><space|0.17em><math-up|escrow><around|(|N,k,E|)>>.
    </equation*>

    Every factor in the denominator is positive. The height record gives
    <math|\<zeta\><rsub|k>\<gtr\>0>, the physical scale gives
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><around|(|H,k,d|)>|\<\|\|\>>=a<rsub|k><rsup|-d>\<gtr\>0>,
    and <math|E\<gtr\>0> gives positive escrow. Marched weights are
    <math|w<rsub|i>=w*<big|prod><rsub|q=k><rsup|i-1>\<phi\><rsub|q>>. The
    exact recurrence for the physical scale and the gain is

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><around|(|H,i+1,d|)>|\<\|\|\>>=L<rsub|i><rsup|d><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><around|(|H,i,d|)>|\<\|\|\>>,<space|2em><math-up|gain><around|(|N,i+1|)>=<math-up|gain><around|(|N,i|)>/<math-up|puffs><around|(|H<rsub|i>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|1\<leqslant\><math-up|puffs><around|(|H<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|i>>,
    the adjacent paid bound implies inductively

    <\equation*>
      w<rsub|i><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><around|(|H,i,d|)>|\<\|\|\>><frac|E|<math-up|gain><around|(|N,i|)>>\<leqslant\><frac|\<zeta\><rsub|i>|4>*<space|2em><around|(|i\<geqslant\>k|)>.
    </equation*>

    The base case is equality at <math|k> by the definition of <math|w>. For
    the step, the left side is multiplied by at most
    <math|\<phi\><rsub|i>*L<rsub|i><rsup|d+1>>; the first inequality of (R5)
    pays this by <math|\<zeta\><rsub|i+1>/\<zeta\><rsub|i>>. Thus the exact
    march/height inequality required for the finite root estimate holds at
    every later scale.

    For the finite-copy construction, at level <math|i> the residual
    coefficient <math|Z<rsub|i>> of the state is keyed; the coefficient used
    in the construction is the translated coefficient family
    <math|\<cal-Z\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-a|)>=Z<rsub|i>+\<Pi\><rsub|i>*<around|(|\<varpi\><rsub|i>-a|)>>
    with its bill <math|c<rsub|i>=c<rsub|i>*<around|(|-a|)>>. Keyedness of
    <math|Z<rsub|i>> and the earned bounds of the transfer sequence transfer
    format and the remainder bound, with bill <math|c<rsub|i>>, to this
    translated coefficient family, which need not have zero leading
    coordinate. The third item of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:actual-marked-antilles>
    with <math|w=Q>, and the Cream inequality
    <math|4*<around|(|1+C<rsub|N>|)>*Q\<leqslant\>E>, give
    <math|<math-up|gain><around|(|N,i|)>*<around|(|c<rsub|i>+<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>|)>\<leqslant\>E>.
    Combined with the per-level price inequality for

    <\equation*>
      <frac|w<rsub|i><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><around|(|H,i,d|)>|\<\|\|\>>*E|<math-up|gain><around|(|N,i|)>>,
    </equation*>

    this gives the finite-copy rate bound
    <math|w<rsub|i><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><around|(|H,i,d|)>|\<\|\|\>>*<around|(|c<rsub|i>+<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>|)>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|i>>
    by multiplication. The Coupon's density debt is exactly the density
    transition in the finite-copy transition identity; its Carol witness
    supplies the accompanying finite Tale support and the
    forced-profile/exponential-entry comparison. At the terminal level let
    <math|c=<around|(|M.<math-up|tone><space|0.17em>N|)>.<math-up|index><around|(|-a|)>>.
    The top clause of `Sung` gives <math|<around|(|M.<math-up|tone><space|0.17em>N|)>.<math-up|index>=<math-up|id>>,
    so <math|c=-a>; the terminal spread bound gives
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|c|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*E>. Put
    <math|j=<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><around|(|H,N,d|)>|\<\|\|\>>> and
    let <math|w<rsub|N>> be the terminal price. The height estimate
    <math|w<rsub|N>*j*E\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|N>/4> implies

    <\equation*>
      2*w<rsub|N>*j<around|\<\|\|\>|c|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|N>.
    </equation*>

    The terminal `erected` estimate, applied at this response cost, supplies
    an added price <math|A<rsub|<math-up|ins>>> with
    <math|w<rsub|N>*j*A<rsub|<math-up|ins>>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|N>/2>. If
    <math|B<rsub|<math-up|cream>>> is the Cream's original attainment price,
    the Bank hypothesis

    <\equation*>
      B<rsub|<math-up|cream>>*\<zeta\><rsub|N>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|N>*E,
    </equation*>

    together with <math|w<rsub|N>*j*E\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|N>/4>, gives
    <math|w<rsub|N>*j*B<rsub|<math-up|cream>>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|N>/4\<leqslant\>u<rsub|N>/2>.
    Hence the combined price <math|A<rsub|<math-up|ins>>+B<rsub|<math-up|cream>>>
    fits the finite-copy allowance <math|u<rsub|N>>. The forced profile is
    preserved by the Cream hook identity and linearity of the played
    forced-profile map. Finally, the finite-copy density identity is the
    Cream density equation after the terminal `originate' identity: the two
    recentering terms have density equal to the transfer sequence readout,
    leaving precisely the terminal marked density. These are all same-history
    records; no new terminal volume or history is selected in assembling
    these data.

    The state residuals <math|Z<rsub|i>> are keyed. Their translated
    coefficient families are the finite-copy coefficients; the translated
    coefficient family at the bottom reference has zero leading coordinate,
    by the choice of the negative bottom primary. The Accord identities give
    the Wilson push and all-displacement profile updates; the Cream gives the
    terminal forced hook and density identity; and the Bank and footprint
    cover pay the forced correction. The output is the exact finite-copy
    identity for the terminal field <math|b+<math-up|initial><around|(|-a|)>>,
    the sum of <math|b> and the original-history insertion with primary
    <math|-a>. Since the terminal calibration is linear,

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|initial><around|(|-a|)>=-C.*O<around|(|X<rsub|N>*a|)>=-C.*O<around|(|v|)>,
    </equation*>

    so that field is <math|b-C.*O<around|(|v|)>>. To spell out the finite
    root estimate, fix <math|m> opened at <math|k> and write
    <math|K=<around|\||<math-up|Site><around|(|m,p|)>|\|>> for the number of
    slots of its census. The finite census estimate needs a site assignment
    for these slots at every level from <math|k> to <math|N>. It is the
    opened-copy assignment of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-centered-opened-copy>:
    the slots are embedded by the canonical aligned embeddings, they migrate
    along the scale embeddings, they are injective, hence of occupancy one,
    which is at most <math|r<rsub|k>> by (R3), and distinct slots have
    separation at least <math|B<rsub|j><rsup|2>> at every level <math|j>
    between <math|k> and <math|N>; at the terminal level these sites are the
    centers of the copies in the root test. The remaining inputs of the
    finite census estimate are the monotone occupancy schedule (R3), the
    scale bounds (R2), the transition service (R4), the height inequalities
    (R5), and continuity of the field <math|b-C.*O<around|(|v|)>>, which
    holds because <math|b> is continuous by the Cream and
    <math|C.*O<around|(|v|)>> is a polynomial cylinder.

    <subparagraph|Serving the residual polynomial.>
    <with|the-label|13.9.3|<label|par:figment-served-residual>> We now
    construct the representation needed for this census. This step uses the
    full transition and profile records in (R4), (R6), (R7), not only their
    numerical inequalities. Let <math|I> be its finite set of slots, and use
    the algebra with commuting generators <math|z<rsub|\<nu\>>>,
    <math|\<nu\>\<in\>I>, satisfying <math|z<rsub|\<nu\>><rsup|2>=0>. Write
    <math|F<rsub|\<nu\>>> for the prescribed oriented copy of
    <math|b-C.*O<around|(|v|)>>. All subsets of <math|I> are retained. Define
    the terminal polynomial

    <\equation>
      A<rsub|N><around|(|U|)>=R<rsub|N>.<math-up|eval><around|(|U|)>*<big|prod><rsub|\<nu\>\<in\>I><around|(|1+z<rsub|\<nu\>>*F<rsub|\<nu\>><around|(|U|)>|)>.<label|eq:actual-marked-portfolio-calibration-display-star>
    </equation>

    In particular its coefficient at any subset <math|J\<subseteq\>I> is
    <math|R<rsub|N>.<math-up|eval><big|prod><rsub|\<nu\>\<in\>J>F<rsub|\<nu\>>>.
    We construct local arrays for this polynomial; identifying its singleton
    coefficients alone would not suffice.

    At level <math|i> orient the translated coefficient family
    <math|\<cal-Z\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-a|)>> at the site of slot
    <math|\<nu\>> and multiply it by <math|a<rsub|i><rsup|-d>>. Let
    <math|g<rsub|i,\<nu\>>> be its ordinary exponential entry and
    <math|q<rsub|i,\<nu\>>> its forced profile. Denote its full density by
    <math|\<chi\><rsub|i,\<nu\>>>. The density decomposition
    <eqref|eq:ope-single-coin> expresses <math|\<chi\><rsub|i,\<nu\>>> as
    <math|R<rsub|i>.<math-up|eval><space|0.17em>g<rsub|i,\<nu\>>> plus the
    gas with exactly one insertion of <math|q<rsub|i,\<nu\>>>. The format and
    remainder bounds give summable majorants for these profiles. Linearity,
    the adjacent density identity, and <math|a<rsub|i+1><rsup|-d>=L<rsub|i><rsup|d>*a<rsub|i><rsup|-d>>
    give

    <\equation*>
      \<chi\><rsub|i,\<nu\>>=<math-ss|P><rsub|i>\<chi\><rsub|i+1,\<nu\>>,<space|2em>\<chi\><rsub|N,\<nu\>>=R<rsub|N>.<math-up|eval><space|0.17em>F<rsub|\<nu\>>.
    </equation*>

    The site migration and the soundness of the same door justify these
    identities after orientation as well. A conjugating orientation
    conjugates all coefficients and responses and preserves their absolute
    prices.

    The sum of the Cream attainment and the negative played attainment
    constructed above, rescaled and oriented at slot <math|\<nu\>>, is a
    response <math|\<Psi\><rsub|\<nu\>>*<around|(|X,U|)>> with hook
    <math|q<rsub|N,\<nu\>>>. It is local, gauge invariant, measurable,
    anchored at that slot, and zero when <math|X> is healthy. Its weighted
    reserved mass is at most <math|u<rsub|N>>. Set
    <math|h<rsub|\<nu\>><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|X>\<Psi\><rsub|\<nu\>>*<around|(|X,U|)>>.
    The hook identity, or equivalently the singleton coefficient of
    <eqref|eq:transfer-closure>, says that the gas with one insertion of
    <math|q<rsub|N,\<nu\>>> is <math|R<rsub|N>.<math-up|eval><space|0.17em>h<rsub|\<nu\>>>.
    Consequently

    <\equation*>
      R<rsub|N>.<math-up|eval><space|0.17em>F<rsub|\<nu\>>=R<rsub|N>.<math-up|eval><space|0.17em><around|(|g<rsub|N,\<nu\>>+h<rsub|\<nu\>>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Telescoping a finite product proves this identity for every product of
    slots with the same factor <math|R<rsub|N>.<math-up|eval>>. Thus it holds
    without dividing by that factor, even at its zeros. Since the generators
    have square zero, <eqref|eq:actual-marked-portfolio-calibration-display-star>
    is exactly

    <\equation>
      e<rsup|E<rsub|N>>*exp <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>z<rsub|\<nu\>>*g<rsub|N,\<nu\>>|)>*\<cal-G\><around|(|k<rsub|N><rsup|0>|)>*exp
      <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>,X>z<rsub|\<nu\>>*\<Psi\><rsub|\<nu\>>(X,\<cdot\><around*|)||)>,<label|eq:actual-marked-portfolio-calibration-display-double-star>|\<nobracket\>>
    </equation>

    where <math|R<rsub|N>.<math-up|eval>=e<rsup|E<rsub|N>>*\<cal-G\><around|(|k<rsub|N><rsup|0>|)>>
    denotes the actual background gas, with its background ordinary
    contribution included in <math|E<rsub|N>>.

    Apply the connected-component construction of <eqref|eq:transfer-closure>
    to <eqref|eq:actual-marked-portfolio-calibration-display-double-star>.
    Its vertices are the selected background forced factors and the response
    factors <math|<around|(|\<nu\>,X|)>>; edges mean intersecting supports.
    Every nonzero response factor meets a flag, which is covered by a
    background forced factor, so every component has such a background
    vertex. Replace each component by the product of its factors, on their
    union support with their union inventory, and sum components with that
    output pair and marker mode. Conversely, components with disjoint union
    supports recover uniquely the original selected factors. This bijection
    preserves products and all marker modes. The empty-mode array is
    <math|k<rsub|N><rsup|0>>, and the singleton array is the hook
    <math|q<rsub|N,\<nu\>>>: a component with one response consists of that
    response and the background factors meeting its support. Remove these
    prescribed modes to define the higher flagged array. The ordinary higher
    array is zero. In particular we have constructed the higher coefficients
    rather than replaced them by products of singleton densities.

    For convergence and payment, the anchored estimate
    <eqref|eq:transfer-root-hit> converts the per-slot weighted response mass
    <math|u<rsub|N>> into site price <math|r<rsub|N><math-ss|s><around|(|B<rsub|N>|)>*u<rsub|N>>
    at exponent <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N>>: the reserved mass is measured at
    exponent <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N>+2>, so it pays the one extra exponent in
    that anchored estimate. The background forced price
    <math|w<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|f>>> is measured at
    <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N>>. With <math|D<rsub|N>=w<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|f>>+r<rsub|N><math-ss|s><around|(|B<rsub|N>|)>*u<rsub|N>>,
    (R4) gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|2*B<rsub|N>+1|)><rsup|4>*D<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>3,<space|2em>e<rsup|D<rsub|N>>-1\<leqslant\>1.
    </equation*>

    The connected-support bound <eqref|eq:ope-forest-price>, with spare
    exponent three, therefore bounds the flagged array at exponent
    <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N>-3> by one. Removing modes cannot increase a
    price. The same majorants justify the component regrouping for countable
    background addresses by absolute convergence. This proves terminal
    servedness with reserve one at pitch <math|\<pi\><rsub|N>=\<alpha\><rsub|N>-3>.

    Here are the adjacent profile inputs to the descent, explicitly in the
    notation of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-transfer>. For each slot
    at a door there is a constant <math|\<ell\><rsub|\<nu\>>>, an anchored
    family of elementary insertions <math|M<rsub|\<nu\>*j>> using finitely
    many slots, and a lower response <math|\<Psi\><rprime|'><rsub|\<nu\>>>,
    such that

    <\equation*>
      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|g<rsub|i+1,\<nu\>>-\<ell\><rsub|\<nu\>>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|j>z<rsub|\<nu\>*j><math-up|ord><around|(|M<rsub|\<nu\>*j>|)>,>|<cell|q<rsub|i+1,\<nu\>>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|j>z<rsub|\<nu\>*j><math-up|flag><around|(|M<rsub|\<nu\>*j>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|q<rsub|i,\<nu\>>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|j>z<rsub|\<nu\>*j>*\<cal-Q\><rsub|i><around|(|M<rsub|\<nu\>*j>|)>+\<cal-H\><rsub|R<rsub|i>><around|(|\<Psi\><rprime|'><rsub|\<nu\>>|)>.>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
      </aligned>
    </equation*>

    Here <math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|i>> is the same door's quoted profile map and
    <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|R<rsub|i>>> its lower hook. These are the finite
    Tale identities in (R6)\U(R7). To obtain them for the translated
    coefficient family, take the union of the Coupon wing for the keyed upper
    residual and the Carol wing for the played displacement
    <math|\<varpi\><rsub|i+1>-a>, and add the two expansions. In the lower
    state the recentering terms cancel:

    <\equation*>
      Z<rsub|i>+\<Pi\><rsub|i>*<around|(|\<varpi\><rsub|i>-a|)>=V+\<Pi\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-h|)>+\<Pi\><rsub|i>*<around|(|\<varpi\><rsub|i+1>+h-a|)>=V+\<Pi\><rsub|i>*<around|(|\<varpi\><rsub|i+1>-a|)>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|V,h> are the one-step correction and primary defined before
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:actual-marked-antilles>. The cancellation holds
    for the ordinary entry, density, and forced profile separately. Thus the
    lower profile in the displayed Tale is exactly the required one. Taking a
    union of wings does not change their door, supports or parent map; the
    absolute prices add. Before physical rescaling their upper price is at
    most

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-O\><rsub|d><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|i+1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>*<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|i>+2*C<rsub|i+1>|)>+2*c<rsub|i+1>\<leqslant\>\<cal-O\><rsub|d>*<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|i>+2*C<rsub|i+1>|)>*<around|(|c<rsub|i+1>+<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|i+1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>|)>,
    </equation*>

    since <math|\<cal-O\><rsub|d>,C<rsub|i+1>\<geqslant\>1> and
    <math|\<psi\><rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>. More precisely the Coupon term costs
    <math|2*b<rsub|i+1>\<leqslant\>2*c<rsub|i+1>>. The lower response price
    at exponent <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i>+2> is bounded by

    <\equation*>
      \<eta\><rsub|i>*b<rsub|i+1>+g<rsub|i>*<around|(|\<varpi\><rsub|i+1>-a|)>\<leqslant\>\<eta\><rsub|i>*b<rsub|i+1>+g<rsub|i>*<around|(|-h|)>+g<rsub|i>*<around|(|\<varpi\><rsub|i+1>+h-a|)>=c<rsub|i>,
    </equation*>

    by subadditivity of the played charge and the defining formula for
    <math|b<rsub|i>>. After rescaling, call these prices
    <math|s<rsub|i,\<nu\>>> and <math|t<rsub|i,\<nu\>>>. The required two
    payments are

    <\equation*>
      <\eqsplit>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|2*w<rsub|i+1>*s<rsub|i,\<nu\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>2*w<rsub|i+1>*a<rsub|i+1><rsup|-d>*\<cal-O\><rsub|d>*<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|i>+2*C<rsub|i+1>|)>*<around|(|c<rsub|i+1>+<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|i+1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>|)>\<leqslant\>1,>>|<row|<cell|w<rsub|i+1>*t<rsub|i,\<nu\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>w<rsub|i+1>*a<rsub|i><rsup|-d>*c<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|i>*C<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1.>>>>
      </eqsplit>
    </equation*>

    These follow from the rate bounds, (R5),
    <math|w<rsub|i+1>\<leqslant\>w<rsub|i>>, and
    <math|C<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1>. Both profiles and the upper exponential
    entry are therefore paid; a bound on only one profile would not establish
    this transition.

    Now define <math|A<rsub|i>=<math-ss|P><rsub|i>A<rsub|i+1>>, acting
    coefficientwise, and <math|\<pi\><rsub|i>=\<alpha\><rsub|i>> for
    <math|i\<less\>N>. Suppose <math|A<rsub|i+1>> has the constructed
    representation at pitch <math|\<pi\><rsub|i+1>> and reserve one. In
    particular <math|\<pi\><rsub|i+1>\<geqslant\>\<alpha\><rsub|i+1>-4>,
    including the terminal step. Apply Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-transfer>
    with upper weights <math|w<rsub|i+1>> and shrinkage
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|i>>. The Tale just constructed gives singleton cost
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|i>> after real/imaginary compression, while the
    higher arrays cost at most <math|4*\<delta\><rsub|i>>. Occupancy gives
    total prepared site cost <math|T=r<rsub|i><math-ss|s><around|(|B<rsub|i+1>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|i>+4*\<delta\><rsub|i>>.
    The compatible-product expansion costs at most
    <math|T+e<rsup|T>*T\<leqslant\>y>. Hence (R4) applies to this entire
    prepared polynomial, including every higher mode. Its ordinary and
    flagged output bounds and <math|<around|(|2*B<rsub|i>+1|)><rsup|4>*c\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>>,
    <math|e<rsup|c>-1\<leqslant\>1> permit the lower response closure at
    exponent <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i>+<tfrac|1|2>>. The exact pushed singleton
    density and the lower hook identify the prescribed
    <math|g<rsub|i,\<nu\>>> and <math|q<rsub|i,\<nu\>>> by
    <eqref|eq:ope-single-coin>, using the nonzero selected Wilson density at
    this scale, whose strict positivity is proved in
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-reprise>.

    Finally, the separation estimate in Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-construction>
    gives, for each supported mode <math|J> with
    <math|<around|\||J|\|>\<geqslant\>2>,

    <\equation*>
      <big|prod><rsub|\<nu\>\<in\>J>w<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|\<nu\>\<in\>J><around|(|\<delta\><rsub|i>*w<rsub|i+1>|)>*e<rsup|<math-up|coverNumber><around|(|B<rsub|i>,X|)>/2>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed <math|w<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|i>*w<rsub|i+1>*e<rsup|B<rsub|i>/128>>
    by (R2), and the tour bound is <math|B<rsub|i><around|\||J|\|>\<leqslant\>64<math-up|coverNumber><around|(|B<rsub|i>,X|)>>.
    This pays the change back to <math|w<rsub|i>> and leaves exponent
    <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i>> and reserve one. It completes the induction down
    to <math|k>. Higher modes throughout are those of <math|A<rsub|i>>, never
    reset to products of the single-insertion pushes.

    The terminal coefficients are integrable, since the fields are continuous
    on the finite-volume compact configuration space. The bounded measurable
    transition kernels preserve integrability; Fubini and their mass identity
    preserve every coefficient integral and the common partition
    normalization. Therefore, for every <math|J\<subseteq\>I>,

    <\equation*>
      <frac|<big|int><around|[|z<rsub|J>|]>*A<rsub|k>|<big|int>R<rsub|k>.<math-up|eval>>=<big|int><big|prod><rsub|\<nu\>\<in\>J>F<rsub|\<nu\>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|N,p>.
    </equation*>

    All integrals on the left use the respective normalized priors. In
    particular the full mode is the original residual census moment. We have
    proved the required served representation at <math|k\<less\>N> with
    exponent <math|\<alpha\><rsub|k>> and reserve one. With constant positive
    slot weight <math|w>, the finite census estimate (obtained by applying
    the marked estimate to the product over all <math|K> slots) is

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<Omega\>|\<\|\|\>>*w<rsup|K>\<leqslant\>exp
      <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<around|\||<math-up|Site><around|(|k,p|)>|\|><space|0.17em><math-up|Treasury><rsub|k>+5*K|)>,<space|2em>\<Omega\>=<big|int><big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><math-up|Site><around|(|m,p|)>>U<rsub|j>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|N,p>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|U<rsub|j>> is the <math|j>th translated/reflected census copy,
    with the chosen motion applied, of the residual field
    <math|b-C.*O<around|(|v|)>>. Thus <math|\<Omega\>> is exactly the moment
    in the definition of the displayed root test. For each slot,
    <math|x<rsub|k>> is the leading coefficient of its translated coefficient
    family <math|\<cal-Z\><rsub|k>*<around|(|-a|)>> and <math|c<rsub|k>> its
    bill, as above, and the marched weight has <math|w<rsub|k>=w>. The rate
    clause at the opened scale <math|k> is
    <math|w<rsub|k><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><around|(|H,k,d|)>|\<\|\|\>>*<around|(|c<rsub|k>+<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>|)>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|d,k>>,
    and <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,k>*C<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>1> by (R5). Hence

    <\equation*>
      w<rsub|k><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><around|(|H,k,d|)>|\<\|\|\>>*C<rsub|k>*<around|(|c<rsub|k>+<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>|)>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>C<rsub|k>=<math-up|columns><around|(|k|)>\<geqslant\>1.
    </equation*>

    The local hull estimate bounds the magnitude of its exponential entry by
    <math|<around|(|1+C<rsub|k>|)>\<\|\|\>x<rsub|k>\<\|\|\><rsub|1>+c<rsub|k>>:
    the unit coordinate costs at most <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
    the masked core section at most <math|C<rsub|k><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>
    by the selected-family price clause, and the masked regular part at most
    the bill <math|c<rsub|k>>. Since <math|<around|(|1+C<rsub|k>|)>\<\|\|\>x<rsub|k>\<\|\|\><rsub|1>+c<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>2*C<rsub|k>*<around|(|c<rsub|k>+<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>|)>>,
    the weighted entry price is at most <math|2>. The forced profiles have
    total modulus at most <math|c<rsub|k>>, so the weighted total forced
    price is at most <math|1>. The action allowance is <math|f=1>. Thus the
    marked estimate <eqref|ex-centered-sail> of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-construction> charges
    <math|x+y+2*f=5> per slot (the more general choice <math|x=3,y=1> gives
    the <math|6>-unit bound). The constant <math|5*K> is exactly the sum of
    the per-slot tolls, and the extensive term is the cost of all
    scale-<math|k> sites. The aligned side-count identity is

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||<math-up|Site><around|(|k,p|)>|\|>=K*<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4>.
    </equation*>

    Consequently, with <math|g=<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4><math-up|Treasury><rsub|k>+5>,
    we have <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Omega\>|\<\|\|\>>*w<rsup|K>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|K*g>>.
    If <math|\<Omega\>=0> the desired inequality is immediate; otherwise the
    same inequality still gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Omega\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|1/K>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|g>/w>.
    By definition the normalized census root test is
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Omega\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|1/K>>, hence

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|rootTest><rsub|m;N,p><around|(|s\<cdot\><around|(|b-C.*O<around|(|v|)>|)>|)>\<leqslant\><frac|<math-up|stakes><around|(|m,k|)>|w>.
    </equation*>

    Finally, the definitions of escrow, flight, and the stake convert this
    ratio exactly:

    <\equation*>
      <frac|<math-up|stakes><around|(|m,k|)>|w>=<math-up|stakes><around|(|m,k|)><frac|4<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><around|(|H,k,d|)>|\<\|\|\>>|\<zeta\><rsub|k>><math-up|escrow><around|(|N,k,E|)>=<math-up|cider><around|(|d,m,k|)>*E<math-up|flight><rsub|-\<gamma\>><around|(|N,p|)>.
    </equation*>

    The bound holds for every <math|k>, <math|m> and motion in the stated
    ranges, because the record (R1)\U(R7) and the Cream do not depend on
    them. No subsequence or replacement history enters the construction.
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|Boundary of this producer.> The proof above expands the
  terminal recentering, finite backward induction, cost conversion,
  marched-weight calculation, and the final use of the finite-copy estimate.
  It does not derive the selected history's Tone/Coupon producer, its
  all-volume height ledger, or the selected-history binding of the terminal
  Cream. Those exact suppliers are listed separately in the evidence record;
  none follows merely from the existence of a portfolio. The precise
  remaining certificate is the record (R1)\U(R7); on one fixed family and one
  fixed field of adjacent gauge doors, its same-history part (R6)\U(R7) reads
  as follows. For each degree <math|d>, terminal level
  <math|N\<geqslant\><math-up|ref><around|(|d|)>>, and period <math|p>, it
  must provide a transfer sequence whose terminal coefficient family is the
  chosen factory representative, whose played fields realize the factory
  entries, which is admissible at every level from
  <math|<math-up|ref><around|(|d|)>> to <math|N>, and which responds across
  each intervening door. The same transfer sequence must have Carol witnesses
  at all those doors and the terminal forced-profile hook. For every
  <math|i\<geqslant\><math-up|ref><around|(|d|)>> it must also provide the
  paid local Coupon and its all-displacement Carol/Tale comparison, with the
  uniform height, room, bank, Brine and monotone occupancy prices used above.
  These are same-family, same-history quantifiers: the transfer sequence is
  chosen for each <math|<around|(|d,N,p|)>> and then retained through all
  tests at those indices, whereas choosing a different history or door field
  for each period or terminal volume is insufficient.

  The nested field and adjacent comparisons are supplied by Propositions
  <reference|prop:selected-terminal-voice>, <reference|prop:selected-reprise>
  and <reference|prop:selected-music>; the zero-coordinate response is
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-zero-coordinate-coupon>.
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-centered-service> identifies the
  height, room, bank and occupancy prices in (R1)\U(R7). The terminal Cream
  attachment uses Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:terminal-marked-voice>,
  including its reserved-address, lawfulness and fee hypotheses. All these
  applications retain the same original funded history as in
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-original-history-attachment>; none
  permits a new history to be chosen for each cutoff or period.

  <subsection|The common history and its centered
  service><label|sec:selected-centered-service>

  We now apply the local constructions to a single family. The input is the
  simultaneous purchase of Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history>
  and <reference|sec:common-observable-purchases>, including the stationary
  and free convergence estimates on their common horizon. We retain the
  prescribed positive blocking penalty throughout.

  Choose nondecreasing profile orders and spatial powers, both at least the
  stage index, and nondecreasing support exponents at least <math|100>. The
  particular choices <math|6+j>, <math|2+j> and <math|100+j> suffice for the
  elementary construction. The selected public run uses the first stage
  <math|4>, the orders <math|6+j>, the powers <math|2+j>, and the constant
  support exponent <math|b<rsub|j>=100> at every stage; the identity
  <math|b<rsub|j>=100> is retained with that run and is required by later
  consumers. Throughout this section the public run <math|R> is retained,
  including its selected shooting parameters, its reference schedule
  <math|t<rsub|R>>, its transition records and its transfer family. The
  following paragraphs unpack the construction and the estimates of these
  retained witnesses; where they speak of choosing an object, the object
  meant is the one stored in <math|R>, and no second choice is made. Choose
  the fixed parameter radius and the small response reserve as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-observable-purchases>. Make the
  block-length choices before their numerical costs. Apply
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection> to all the resulting
  requests, with a nondecreasing occupancy schedule. This gives one sequence
  of levels, couplings and periods, with <math|J<around|(|m|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>.

  <subsubsection|Choosing the history before its observable comparisons>

  For every cutoff <math|N> and period <math|p>, the terminal family is the
  exact Wilson family of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:wilson-startup-construction>.
  The interval return and scalar reset are applied with the strengthened
  local conditions of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-observable-purchases>.
  Thus every member of the resulting continuous interval family carries those
  conditions. To prove that its bottom couplings cover the required interval,
  forget only these additional conditions and apply the barrier and covering
  argument of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:surviving-interval-shooting>.
  This leaves the interval, its endpoints and every replayed history
  unchanged. Choose a parameter in that very interval whose bottom coupling
  is the prescribed common value, and retain its original strengthened
  certificate. Retain that member, its actual representations
  <math|R<rsub|N,p,m>> and its adjacent transitions. All of them use the
  already purchased paths, kernels and clipping data. Choosing the members
  for the countably many pairs <math|<around|(|N,p|)>> introduces no new
  scale request. On the run <math|R> these are stored data: for every
  <math|<around|(|N,p|)>> the run stores the interval family, a parameter in
  its interval, and the identity of its history at <math|<around|(|N,p|)>>
  with the replay of that family at that parameter. Its nesting receipt
  records that every adjacent pair of this history is a protected and
  accurate transition of the purchased step and that the terminal
  representative carries the reserved addresses.

  The original certificate at the chosen parameter supplies the local
  parameter estimates on these histories. Thus the comparisons and
  fixed-level limits in Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:coupled-background-convergence>,
  <reference|sec:selected-history-limits> and
  <reference|sec:fixed-rung-convergence> apply to this family. The uniform
  local derivative bank is that of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-observable-purchases>.
  In particular, the fixed-level convergence is available for the actual
  selected activity arrays, not just for their stationary paths.

  This application carries one input that the certificate of the selected
  member does not by itself provide. Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:coupled-background-convergence>
  compares the selected history with auxiliary truncated histories, which
  have a prescribed coupling script and zero terminal activity, and
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-history-limits> draws its limits
  under the assumption that the transition hypotheses of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:parameter-comparison-suppliers> hold for the
  actual input of each such auxiliary history at every step, on the same path
  and dictionaries. The certificate at the chosen parameter concerns the
  selected history only. The fixed-level limits are therefore used here
  subject to that same assumption on the auxiliary histories; it is not
  proved in this section.

  Put <math|t<rsub|d>=t<rsub|R><around|(|d|)>>, the reference level stored in
  the run for the grade <math|d>. The public construction supplies

  <\equation*>
    1\<leqslant\>t<rsub|d>,<space|2em>d+3\<leqslant\>J<around|(|t<rsub|d>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Such levels exist by divergence of <math|J>; no monotonicity of
  <math|d\<mapsto\>t<rsub|d>> is recorded or used. At each adjacent step
  choose the wing containing the origin principal modes and the keeper keys
  for all relevant support/inventory shapes. These keys form a finite set:
  the torus is finite, so its support powerset and the pairs
  <math|I\<subseteq\>X> with <math|I\<ne\>\<varnothing\>> are finite. The
  wing uses these shapes, not the countably many reserved serial addresses.
  Hence it covers every forced coefficient while remaining finite. Take the
  finite union required for the admitted grades.
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:heading-history-handoff> supplies one
  heading and one local transfer map for that entire wing. Consequently the
  same map works for all those grades. Enlarging the wing preserves its
  finite interpolation proof and the exact endpoint representation.

  Apply the finite backward construction of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-freight-producer>, starting at the
  terminal marked section constructed in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:terminal-marked-construction>.
  This gives the finite transfer data at every grade, cutoff and period. The
  three exact identities in <eqref|eq:history-three-identities> identify the
  coefficient, exponential entry and forced profile throughout the
  construction. In particular, its terminal density is
  <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|d>*R<rsub|N,p,N><around|(|U|)><space|0.17em><math-up|initial><rsub|N,p><around|(|v|)><around|(|U|)>>;
  the physical observable <math|<math-up|initial><rsub|N,p>> already contains
  the factor <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>>. The local maps used in this
  construction are the adjacent maps already chosen above; the downward
  induction does not select another background history. The unit, gauge
  invariance and signed-motion identities are preserved by the exact local
  section and by the equivariant finite assembly. Thus these transfer data
  form one family on the retained history. On the run <math|R> this family is
  the stored transfer family <math|S<rsub|R>>, and the adjacent maps are its
  stored transition records; for <math|t<rsub|d>\<gtr\>N> the stored transfer
  datum is a default map and carries no assertion, as explained in the proof
  of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-original-history-attachment>.

  <subsubsection|Paid retention on the stored doors>

  Write <math|x<rsub|i>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>>, <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i>>
  for the support exponent, <math|B<rsub|i>=B<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>>, and
  <math|r<rsub|i>=N<rsub|J*<around|(|i+1|)>>>. Set

  <\equation*>
    y<rsub|i>=x<rsub|i+1><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>\<delta\><rsub|i>=x<rsub|i+1><rsup|-2>,<space|2em>u<rsub|i>=<tfrac|1|4>x<rsub|i><rsup|-1/2>.
  </equation*>

  Use <math|w<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|f>>> for the history's forced allowance
  and <math|w<rsub|i>> for the distinct flagged <em|response> price of the
  tariff at step <math|i>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Retention and terminal endowment on the selected
    history><label|prop:selected-centered-retention>Keep the selected replay,
    nesting and doors above, and the fixed profile and purchased entrance
    delays. At each stored interval point assume the arrival, ordinary and
    raised-response data of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:finite-menu-history-handoff>
    are available on the retained representations and door. Assume also the
    following preservation property of the retained return: for each
    nonterminal selected entry, its output dictionary has zero regular
    addresses for nonempty supports and eligible forced addresses for every
    nonempty inventory contained in its support. Eligibility here is required
    whenever the actual flags realize the inventory. Assume the delays
    include the tariff thresholds of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-eventual-tariff>
    for these targets and the thresholds of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-selected-geyser-steam>. Then for every
    <math|N,p> and <math|0\<leqslant\>i\<less\>N> the stored door has the
    following paid response. Its input is any finite priced offer of nonempty
    modes with positive marker weights, fine sites in the offered supports,
    and parent-site occupancy at most <math|r<rsub|i>>. The site-rooted input
    price is at most <math|y<rsub|i>> at exponent
    <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i+1>-4>. Its ordinary and flagged output families
    have the support and flag conditions and per-mode majorants in the
    multi-marker offer of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-marked-portfolio-calibration>.
    Their rooted weighted prices have bounds <math|1> and <math|w<rsub|i>> at
    exponents <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i>+1/2> and <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i>+1>,
    respectively. The assembled density is the exact push of the offered
    polynomial. In each flagged box the quiet coefficient is the lower
    ordinary activity profile and the singleton coefficient is the sum of the
    corresponding offered singleton profiles transported by this door. The
    door is sound and tuned, and the upper contact addresses are retained.
    For the same <math|y<rsub|i>,w<rsub|i>>,

    <\equation*>
      0\<less\>\<delta\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>\<alpha\><rsub|i>,\<alpha\><rsub|i+1>\<geqslant\>100,<space|2em>T<rsub|i>+e<rsup|T<rsub|i>>*T<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>y<rsub|i>,<space|2em><around|(|2*B<rsub|i>+1|)><rsup|4>*c<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>,<space|2em>e<rsup|c<rsub|i>>-1\<leqslant\>1,
    </equation*>

    where <math|T<rsub|i>=r<rsub|i><math-ss|s><around|(|B<rsub|i+1>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|i>+4*\<delta\><rsub|i>>,
    <math|c<rsub|i>=w<rsub|i>+r<rsub|i><math-ss|s><around|(|B<rsub|i>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|i>>
    and <math|<math-ss|s><around|(|B|)>=e*<around|{|2*<around|(|16*B+9|)>|}><rsup|4>>.

    At every terminal level <math|N\<geqslant\>0> the same schedule gives

    <\equation*>
      u<rsub|N>\<geqslant\>0,<space|2em>\<alpha\><rsub|N>\<geqslant\>3,<space|2em><around|(|2*B<rsub|N>+1|)><rsup|4>*C<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|end>>\<leqslant\>3,<space|2em>e<rsup|C<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|end>>>-1\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>C<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|end>>=w<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|f>>+r<rsub|N><math-ss|s><around|(|B<rsub|N>|)>*u<rsub|N>.
    </equation*>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    For fixed <math|N,p,i>, take the interval witness of the stored step. Its
    upper representation is the interval input at the retained point and its
    lower representation the return at that same point. The door is the
    one-step transport at that point, with those two endpoint identities
    substituted. Thus replacing the local representations by the selected
    entries in the response formulas changes neither the operator on
    activities nor the dictionary. At the top,
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:wilson-startup-family> supplies the
    regular and forced addresses on the selected Wilson representative. At
    any lower entry the preservation property applies to the return of the
    selected step above it. Hence the upper entry of every door has these
    addresses, including when that entry is terminal. For a nonempty support
    take its zero regular address. For each nonempty inventory take an
    eligible forced address at that support, using eligibility on the
    configurations whose flags realize the inventory.

    At this step the entrance stage is <math|J*<around|(|i+1|)>>, the target
    stage is <math|J<around|(|i|)>>, and the coupling is <math|x<rsub|i>>.
    The selected clock gives <math|x<rsub|i+1>=x<rsub|i>+c<rsub|G>*log
    L<rsub|i>> and the purchased entrance bound at <math|x<rsub|i>>. The
    tariff therefore applies at the retained occupancy <math|r<rsub|i>>. Its
    shipment inequality converts the site-rooted offer at the upper exponent
    into the rooted birth-region price, using the parent map and the
    occupancy bound, as in Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-volant-supplier>.
    The ordinary response uses the ordinary component budget and the raised
    response uses the separately paid flagged budget. The latter has price
    <math|w<rsub|i>> from the same tariff. Applying the finite-menu handoff
    to the retained response data gives the two grouped ledgers. For a fixed
    mode, sum the configuration-independent absolute majorants over entries
    of its output support or box. The triangle inequality bounds its grouped
    coefficient by that sum. Since the mode weights are nonnegative and
    unchanged by grouping, the weighted sum of these majorants is bounded by
    the corresponding grouped support price. The support restrictions, quiet
    and singleton identities, and exact push are the grouping identities of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-volant-supplier>. The finite-menu
    handoff gives soundness and compatibility with grouped sums for this same
    one-leaf transport. This uses the purchased response data for every
    priced offer; it does not select a new door when the offer changes. The
    probe, purchase, lodging and credit inequalities of the tariff are
    precisely the displayed inequalities for
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|i>,T<rsub|i>,c<rsub|i>> after the fine-coupling
    substitution. In particular the allowance used to buy
    <math|T<rsub|i>+e<rsup|T<rsub|i>>*T<rsub|i>> is the input price of the
    very same response.

    For the terminal assertion, apply <eqref|eq:ex-geyser-second> at
    <math|x=x<rsub|N>> with stages <math|J<around|(|N|)>> and
    <math|J*<around|(|N+1|)>>. This is a numerical assertion about the global
    schedule, so it uses the purchased next entrance inequality even when the
    finite history itself stops at <math|N>. The selected allowance is
    <math|w<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|f>>=<math-up|stain><around|(|x<rsub|N>|)>>
    and the other term in that bound is <math|N<rsub|J*<around|(|N+1|)>><math-ss|s><around|(|B<rsub|N>|)>*s<around|(|x<rsub|N>|)>>,
    which equals <math|r<rsub|N><math-ss|s><around|(|B<rsub|N>|)>*u<rsub|N>>.
    The resulting two inequalities give the claimed endowment. Positivity of
    <math|x<rsub|N>> and <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N>\<geqslant\>100> give the
    remaining two assertions. This argument precedes the choice of a terminal
    label and needs no terminal coefficient family, including at <math|N=0>.
  </proof>

  The proposition concerns the stored doors of the public run, including its
  step at level zero when <math|N\<gtr\>0>. The per-campaign service
  projection uses the campaign's positive-level doors. Its retention
  assertion is restricted to <math|1\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>i\<less\>N>; no
  response at its default zero-level door is required. In either application
  the terminal endowment depends only on the schedule. The preservation of
  the required slots by the retained return is proved in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-reserved-slots> below. The arrival and
  raised-response receipts on the selected replay are supplied in
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:selected-retention-public>, from the local
  estimates which the common purchases pay on the stored steps.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Reserved slots on every selected
    entry><label|lem:selected-reserved-slots>Keep the selected history. For
    every <math|N,p> and every <math|0\<leqslant\>m\<leqslant\>N>, the stored
    representation <math|R<rsub|N,p,m>> is written in the dictionary given by
    the fixed construction of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:wilson-startup-family>,
    applied on the level-<math|m> torus at the level-<math|m> budget point.
    Its coefficients on the reserved labels of that dictionary vanish.
    Precisely:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>for every regular address, that is, a site together with a
      nonempty support containing it, and every natural number, the regular
      coefficient of <math|R<rsub|N,p,m>> on the corresponding reserved
      regular label is zero;

      <item>for every support <math|X>, every nonempty inventory
      <math|I\<subseteq\>X> and every natural number <math|n>, the forced
      coefficient of <math|R<rsub|N,p,m>> on the corresponding reserved
      forced label is zero. That label has support <math|X> and inventory
      <math|I>. Its additional predicate holds at every configuration
      <math|U> with <math|X\<cap\><math-up|flags><around|(|U|)>=I>, where the
      flags are taken at the threshold of level <math|m>.
    </enumerate>

    In particular, the preservation property assumed in
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-centered-retention> holds for
    every nonterminal selected entry. Every stored representation, terminal
    or not, has a Keeper in the sense of the terminal Keeper of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-marked-field-local>. That is an
    injective assignment, to every triple <math|<around|(|X,I,n|)>> with
    <math|\<varnothing\>\<ne\>I\<subseteq\>X> and <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, of
    a forced address with zero coefficient, support <math|X> and inventory
    <math|I>, eligible whenever <math|X\<cap\><math-up|flags><around|(|U|)>=I>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For <math|m=N>, the stored top representation is a member
    <math|F<rsub|\<beta\>>> of the exact startup family, by the choice of the
    history at the beginning of this section.
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:wilson-startup-family> gives (1) and (2)
    for <math|F<rsub|\<beta\>>>.

    Let <math|m\<less\>N>. By the stored identities recalled in the proof of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-centered-retention>,
    <math|R<rsub|N,p,m>> is the output of the interval return of step
    <math|m> at the retained interval point. That output lives on the
    level-<math|m> torus. It is obtained from the return's batch by the fixed
    packing and absorption (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:coupling-reset-selection>;
    the batch is that of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-reset-reorganization>).
    This is the same packing and grouping as in the startup, now applied on
    the level-<math|m> torus at the level-<math|m> budget point.

    The proof of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:wilson-startup-family>
    shows that the vanishing on reserved labels is a property of this packing
    and grouping. It holds for every torus, budget point and coefficient
    family to which they are applied:

    <\itemize>
      <item>The packing injection takes values among the unreserved labels,
      and every entry outside its image is zero.

      <item>A reserved forced label is the group formed by one reserved hard
      label alone. Its activity is therefore that packed hard coefficient,
      times indicators, and this coefficient is zero.

      <item>In the return, grouped labels may also contain logarithmic
      labels, which are absent at the startup. This does not affect the
      reserved singletons, which contain no other label.

      <item>Every other grouped entry outside the image of the packed family
      is zero.
    </itemize>

    Applying this to the renewed arrays of the return gives (1) and (2) for
    <math|R<rsub|N,p,m>>.

    The support, inventory and additional predicate of a reserved label are
    data of the dictionary, not of the coefficients. They are given by the
    same construction as for the startup family, on the level-<math|m> torus.
    The predicate is the flag condition at the level-<math|m> threshold, by
    the construction recalled in the first subsection of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:terminal-marked-construction>.

    For the preservation property, take any regular address, that is, a site
    in a nonempty support. Its reserved regular label is a regular address of
    <math|R<rsub|N,p,m>> with that site and support and zero coefficient. For
    a nonempty inventory <math|I\<subseteq\>X> and every
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, the reserved forced label of
    <math|<around|(|X,I,n|)>> is a forced address with support <math|X>,
    inventory <math|I> and zero coefficient. That address is eligible
    whenever the actual flags in <math|X> realize <math|I>. Distinct triples
    have distinct reserved labels, so <math|<around|(|X,I,n|)>> is mapped
    injectively. This is the asserted Keeper. Restricting to <math|n=0> gives
    the assignment on pairs used in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-zero-coordinate-coupon>.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The menu on the actual component
  alphabet><label|sec:selected-menu-alphabet>

  The shipment bound must pay the whole finite menu, including several
  entries assigned to the same elementary activity. We record this step
  before applying either response estimate. Fix a stored door and its
  underlying mixed alphabet from Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mixed-activity-prices>.
  An optional label <math|a> has nonnegative price <math|v<rsub|a>>, birth
  support <math|X<rsub|a>> and nonnegative length charge <math|d<rsub|a>>.
  The mandatory packets and their prices are kept fixed. Let <math|\<cal-I\>>
  be the finite menu, let <math|\<sigma\><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>> be the
  optional label modified by entry <math|\<lambda\>>. Write
  <math|r<rsub|\<lambda\>>\<geqslant\>0> for its menu fee and
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|\<lambda\>>\<geqslant\>0> for its leaf weight: the
  normalized local insertion <math|h<rsub|\<lambda\>>> satisfies the
  pointwise bound <math|<around|\||h<rsub|\<lambda\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|\<lambda\>>>.
  Its wage is <math|w<rsub|\<lambda\>>=r<rsub|\<lambda\>>*\<ell\><rsub|\<lambda\>>>.
  For this label the length charge is the fine crest cover number, and its
  birth support is the coarse birth region used in
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-volant-root-lift>. Define

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:selected-menu-augmentation>v<rsub|a><rsup|\<cal-I\>>=v<rsub|a>+<big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>:<space|0.17em>\<sigma\><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>=a>w<rsub|\<lambda\>>.
  </equation>

  The assignment <math|\<sigma\>> need not be injective.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Finite menus preserve the mixed component
    estimate><label|lem:selected-menu-alphabet>Suppose the unmarked packet
    and optional prices satisfy the hypotheses of the mixed component
    estimate of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mixed-activity-prices>, at its
    chosen head exponent <math|q> and positive length reserve
    <math|\<varepsilon\>>. For its application to the switched integrals,
    retain the common mixed-body estimate as well: after any admitted set of
    optional labels is removed, the integral of the absolute residual body is
    bounded by the product of the remaining optional prices and the unchanged
    packet prices, against the same Gaussian and Haar measures. Suppose also
    that the shipment conversion for the menu gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:selected-menu-extra-hit>sup<rsub|z>
      <big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>:<space|0.17em>z\<in\>X<rsub|\<sigma\><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>>>w<rsub|\<lambda\>>*e<rsup|2*\<varepsilon\>*d<rsub|\<sigma\><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>>>*e<rsup|q*C<rsub|B><around|(|X<rsub|\<sigma\><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>>|)>>\<leqslant\>\<cal-R\>.
    </equation>

    If the unmarked optional site price is <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|o>>, the
    augmented alphabet has optional site price at most
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|o>+\<cal-R\>>. Its packet prices are unchanged. Thus
    the mixed component estimate applies with total vertex price
    <math|\<gamma\><rprime|'>=\<Gamma\><rsub|p>+\<Gamma\><rsub|o>+\<cal-R\>>
    whenever its tree smallness condition holds at
    <math|\<gamma\><rprime|'>>.

    For every finite component <math|A> of the optional alphabet, its
    augmented product is exactly

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:selected-menu-ticket-product><big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>A>v<rsub|a><rsup|\<cal-I\>>=<big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|T\<subseteq\>\<cal-I\>:\<sigma\>\|<rsub|T><text|injective>>>|<row|<cell|\<sigma\><around|(|T|)>\<subseteq\>A>>>>>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|\<lambda\>\<in\>T>w<rsub|\<lambda\>>|)><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>A\<setminus\>\<sigma\><around|(|T|)>>v<rsub|a>|)>.
    </equation>

    Consequently the component bound also pays the sum over all admitted menu
    replacements, with no factor depending on the cardinality of
    <math|\<cal-I\>>. In the selected shipment application,
    <math|q=Q.<math-up|relative>>, <math|\<varepsilon\>=\<varepsilon\><rsub|Q>>
    and <math|\<cal-R\>=<math-up|ribbon><around|(|Q,L,x|)>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Insert <eqref|eq:selected-menu-augmentation> into the weighted optional
    site sum. The new part at a site <math|z> is

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|a:z\<in\>X<rsub|a>><big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>:\<sigma\><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>=a>w<rsub|\<lambda\>>*e<rsup|2*\<varepsilon\>*d<rsub|a>>*e<rsup|q*C<rsub|B><around|(|X<rsub|a>|)>>=<big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>:z\<in\>X<rsub|\<sigma\><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>>>w<rsub|\<lambda\>>*e<rsup|2*\<varepsilon\>*d<rsub|\<sigma\><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>>>*e<rsup|q*C<rsub|B><around|(|X<rsub|\<sigma\><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>>|)>>.
    </equation*>

    The menu is finite and all terms are nonnegative, so this regrouping is
    valid even for a countable optional alphabet. The asserted site bound
    follows by addition. The packet population and its analytic weight
    estimate have not changed. Apply the combination of vertex populations
    and the reserved-tail estimate <eqref|eq:mixed-reserved-tail> with this
    new optional price and the same supports, lengths, seed marks and
    positive reserves.

    To prove <eqref|eq:selected-menu-ticket-product>, expand each factor of
    the finite product. At label <math|a> one chooses either <math|v<rsub|a>>
    or one of the wages with <math|\<sigma\><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>=a>. The
    chosen menu entries therefore form a subset <math|T> on which
    <math|\<sigma\>> is injective. Conversely, such a <math|T> specifies
    exactly one choice at every occupied label, and the unmarked choice at
    every remaining label. This is a bijection, including when several menu
    entries have the same image. Multiplying the corresponding factors proves
    the identity. Any restriction to admitted components or replacements
    decreases its nonnegative majorant. For the actual switched component,
    let <math|\<cal-B\>> denote its remaining body after the labels in
    <math|\<sigma\><around|(|T|)>> have been replaced. Before integration,
    the pointwise leaf bounds give

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<big|prod><rsub|\<lambda\>\<in\>T>r<rsub|\<lambda\>>*h<rsub|\<lambda\>>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-B\>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|\<lambda\>\<in\>T>w<rsub|\<lambda\>>|)><around|\||\<cal-B\>|\|>.
    </equation*>

    Integrate against the same positive Gaussian and Haar measures and use
    the unmarked mixed-body estimate for the remaining labels, with its
    unchanged mandatory packets. The forest and tag averages are probability
    averages, so they preserve this bound. Thus each replacement has
    precisely the product majorant in <eqref|eq:selected-menu-ticket-product>.
    This argument uses pointwise insertion bounds and a body estimate before
    summing components; it does not multiply bounds for separate integrals.
    All replacement choices are summed before the rooted estimate is applied.

    Finally, the shipment-conversion part of the proof of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-volant-supplier> already proves
    <eqref|eq:selected-menu-extra-hit>: its <math|f<rsub|\<lambda\>>> is
    <math|w<rsub|\<lambda\>>*e<rsup|2*\<varepsilon\><rsub|Q>*n<rsub|\<lambda\>><rsup|<math-up|crest>>>>
    and <math|d<rsub|\<sigma\><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>>=n<rsub|\<lambda\>><rsup|<math-up|crest>>>.
    In particular the length reserve is included in the shipment, not added
    afterwards. That part of the proof uses only the offer, its parent
    occupancy, the birth geometry and the tariff; it does not use the paid
    output terms assumed for the subsequent response assembly. It can
    therefore be used here before those terms have been constructed. The
    named origin condition follows from nonempty menu modes and the inclusion
    of their fine sites in their crests. This proves the stated application
    on the same elementary alphabet as the stored door.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Uniform payment of the two response
  rows><label|sec:selected-response-payments>

  We separate the numerical part of the arrival hypothesis from the
  construction of the component majorants. This distinction is useful because
  the ordinary and raised rows have different soft prices, even though they
  have the same final allowance.

  Fix an allowed pair of stages <math|<around|(|j,h|)>>, its block side
  <math|L=L<rsub|j>>, and its positive strength
  <math|\<mu\>=\<mu\><rsub|j,h>>. For a coarse coupling <math|x> put

  <\equation*>
    y=x+s<rsub|G>*log L,<space|1em>B=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>,<space|1em>P=exp <around|(|<around|(|log log
    y|)><rsup|2>|)>,<space|1em>H<rsub|1>=2*e<rsup|-\<mu\>*P<rsup|2>/8>,<space|1em>H<rsub|2>=2*e<rsup|-\<mu\>*P<rsup|2>/16>.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|C<rsub|<math-up|cov>>=e<rsup|\<chi\><rsub|0>>\<geqslant\>1> be
  the fixed cover enlargement factor in Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-lattice-rg-crowd>.
  The two final soft demands <math|f<rsub|0>*<around|(|x,\<alpha\>|)>> and
  <math|f<rsub|+>*<around|(|x,\<alpha\>|)>> are, respectively,
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-multi-softneed> and
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-multi-raised-demand>, evaluated with the profile at
  stage <math|h>, radius <math|B>, threshold
  <math|\<theta\><around|(|x|)>=x<rsup|-1/2>*exp <around|(|<around|(|log log
  x|)><rsup|2>|)>>, and regular allowance <math|c<rsub|h>*x<rsup|-11/10>>,
  where <math|0\<less\>c<rsub|h>\<leqslant\>1/64>. Both are evaluated at the
  <em|base> budget point; the second pays the receipt at the raised point,
  whose radius is still <math|B>. Write <math|A<rsub|j>> for the fixed bound
  on the absolute value of the reset parameter after <math|L<rsub|j>> is
  chosen.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Simultaneous numerical response
    payment><label|lem:selected-response-payments>There is a threshold
    <math|d<rsub|j,h>>, independent of the period, cutoff, input
    representation and finite menu, with the following properties for every
    <math|x\<geqslant\>d<rsub|j,h>>. For any <math|a,b\<geqslant\>0> with

    <\equation*>
      a\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<mu\>*P<rsup|2>/4>,<space|2em>b\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|cov>>*y<rsup|-1/8>,
    </equation*>

    set <math|\<kappa\><rsub|1>=\<mu\>*P<rsup|2>/8> and
    <math|I<rsub|1>=e<rsup|\<kappa\><rsub|1>>*a+2*b>. Then

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:selected-first-response-payment>b\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em><around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*I<rsub|1>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|1>>*<around|(|e<rsup|I<rsub|1>>-1|)>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|1>.
    </equation>

    For every <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|j>> and
    <math|\<epsilon\>\<in\><around|{|0,+|}>>, put
    <math|\<kappa\><rsub|2>=\<mu\>*P<rsup|2>/16> and
    <math|I<rsub|2,\<epsilon\>>=e<rsup|\<kappa\><rsub|2>>*H<rsub|1>+2*f<rsub|\<epsilon\>>*<around|(|x,\<alpha\>|)>>.
    Then

    <\gather>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|0\<leqslant\>f<rsub|\<epsilon\>>*<around|(|x,\<alpha\>|)>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|-1/4>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em><around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*I<rsub|2,\<epsilon\>>\<leqslant\>1,>>|<row|<cell|e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|2>>*<around|(|e<rsup|I<rsub|2,\<epsilon\>>>-1|)>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|2>.<eq-number><label|eq:selected-second-response-payment>>>>>
    </gather>

    These statements can be included in the common delays before the history
    is selected. They then hold at every stored step <math|i\<less\>N>, with
    <math|<around|(|j,h|)>=<around|(|J*<around|(|i+1|)>,J<around|(|i|)>|)>>
    and <math|x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>>, including the direct stored step
    at <math|i=0>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The reset and path prices defining both soft demands are nonnegative. The
    footprint calculation <eqref|eq:nonlinear-soft-price>, applied separately
    to the two reserves, gives constants <math|C<rsub|0>,C<rsub|+>\<geqslant\>0>
    such that, for <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|j>>,

    <\equation*>
      0\<leqslant\>f<rsub|\<epsilon\>>*<around|(|x,\<alpha\>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|\<epsilon\>>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>*\<theta\><around|(|x|)>.
    </equation*>

    The hypotheses used there hold eventually: <math|\<theta\>\<leqslant\>1>
    and <math|c<rsub|h>*x<rsup|-11/10>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsup|2>>.
    The base and raised demands have different constants, but the same bound
    on <math|\<alpha\>> and the same <math|\<theta\>,B>. Since
    <math|y/x\<to\>1>, the logarithm of <math|<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>*\<theta\><around|(|x|)>*y<rsup|1/4>>
    tends to minus infinity. Indeed its nonconstant terms are <math|4*log
    <around|(|1+B|)>+<around|(|log log x|)><rsup|2>-<frac|1|4>*log
    x+o<around|(|1|)>>. Taking the maximum of the two thresholds proves the
    claimed <math|y<rsup|-1/4>> bound simultaneously for both demands.

    Write <math|b<rsub|B>=<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>>. We have

    <\equation*>
      I<rsub|1>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<mu\>*P<rsup|2>/8>+2*C<rsub|<math-up|cov>>*y<rsup|-1/8>,<space|2em>I<rsub|2,\<epsilon\>>\<leqslant\>2*e<rsup|-\<mu\>*P<rsup|2>/16>+2*y<rsup|-1/4>.
    </equation*>

    Each right side, multiplied by <math|b<rsub|B>>, tends to zero. For the
    exponential terms this follows from <math|P<rsup|2>/log
    y\<to\>\<infty\>>; for the power terms it follows from
    <math|B=O<around|(|<around|(|log y|)><rsup|2>|)>>. Increase one threshold
    until both products are at most one and
    <math|C<rsub|<math-up|cov>>*y<rsup|-1/8>\<leqslant\>1>. Every
    corresponding <math|I> lies in <math|<around|[|0,1|]>>. On this interval
    <math|e<rsup|I>-1\<leqslant\><around|(|e-1|)>*I\<leqslant\>2>.
    Multiplying by <math|e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|1>>> or
    <math|e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|2>>> proves the two output estimates. In
    particular, the constants <math|\<kappa\><rsub|1>> and
    <math|\<kappa\><rsub|2>> work for every admitted precursor and every
    reset parameter, rather than being selected after a menu is tested.

    At stage <math|j> there are only finitely many permitted targets
    <math|h>. Take the maximum of their thresholds and the previously
    purchased delay, then its running maximum in <math|j>. All the
    inequalities hold on tails, so this operation preserves them and all
    previous purchases. This is an instance of <eqref|eq:nonlinear-entry-max>
    in the choice order of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-observable-purchases>.
    It is made before the scale sequence and shooting parameter are selected,
    not by altering an already selected history. The stored clock pays this
    delay at <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>> for the permitted pair
    <math|<around|(|J*<around|(|i+1|)>,J<around|(|i|)>|)>>. Its fine coupling
    is exactly <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>+s<rsub|G>*log
    L<rsub|i>>. The stored reset parameter obeys the same stage bound
    <math|A<rsub|J*<around|(|i+1|)>>> by the interval-return construction of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history>. Substitution
    proves the last assertion for every admitted cutoff and period. No
    positive lower bound on <math|i> was used.
  </proof>

  For the precursor in Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-lattice-rg-crowd>,
  <math|a=2*V<rsub|Q>*e<rsup|-E<rsub|0>>>, with
  <math|V<rsub|Q>\<leqslant\>1/2> and <math|E<rsub|0>=\<mu\>*P<rsup|2>/4>.
  Its ordinary soft price is at most <math|y<rsup|-1/8>>, whereas its raised
  soft price is at most <math|C<rsub|<math-up|cov>>*y<rsup|-1/8>>. Thus the
  first payment covers both paths. After their respective widening and
  absorption, the second payment applies to <math|H<rsub|1>> and their
  respective final demands. It gives exactly <math|H<rsub|2>>, the flagged
  response allowance <math|w<rsub|i>> of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-centered-retention>.
  The argument does not substitute <math|f<rsub|0>> for <math|f<rsub|+>> or
  change the budget at which <math|f<rsub|+>> is evaluated.

  <subparagraph|The remaining analytic attachment.> The two lemmas above
  supply the finite-menu augmentation and the four numerical absorption
  bounds. The common mixed-body estimate retained in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-menu-alphabet> is not an independent
  input: it is derived in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-residual-body>
  below from the simultaneous product estimate of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-warnings>, and so reduces to the
  local hypotheses of that estimate on the held tree sectors. The lemmas do
  not supply those local hypotheses, or the actual local component and
  differentiated entrance estimates used in
  Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-lattice-rg-usable>. In particular
  that section requires the rooted thin-component majorant at its full
  exponent and the corresponding tree smallness bound before applying its
  cluster estimate. The precursor and vapor estimates in
  Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-lattice-rg-crowd> must concern the
  same residual bodies, local return and profile, with the two distinct soft
  demands. The packet estimates include all analytic variants together. These
  are analytic inputs on the held local step, not additional consequences of
  increasing the delays. The following subsections match them to their
  uniform local producers on the stored steps, and
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:selected-retention-public> then removes the
  arrival and raised-response hypothesis of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-centered-retention>.

  <subsubsection|The residual body on the actual
  alphabet><label|sec:selected-residual-body>

  We show that the body hypothesis of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-menu-alphabet>
  follows from the estimate that already controls the integrated activities
  of a sector. Fix a tree sector of the held step. As in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-warnings>, its integration
  variables are a Haar variable <math|Y> and the single Gaussian vector
  <math|Z>, with the product probability measure
  <math|\<nu\>*<around|(|d*Y|)>*\<gamma\><rsub|A>*<around|(|d*Z|)>>. The
  vertices are of four types: connected packets, which are mandatory, and the
  three optional types, namely forced activities, exponential logarithmic
  corrections and warnings. Warning vertices are indexed by the warning
  addresses of that section, so distinct warning vertices have distinct
  addresses. Different warning types at the same site are different
  addresses. Every factor is evaluated at the same <math|<around|(|Y,Z|)>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Residual bodies on one tree sector><label|lem:selected-residual-body>Assume
    that the vertex factors <math|f<rsub|i>> of this sector are measurable
    and satisfy the uniform pointwise bounds
    <math|<around|\||f<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|i>> of the table
    preceding <eqref|eq:warning-sector-mass>:
    <math|c<rsub|i>=C<rsub|0>*e<rsup|<around|(|s-g|)>*n<rsub|i>>> for a
    packet with <math|n<rsub|i>> seeds, <math|c<rsub|i>=v<rsub|i>> for a
    forced activity, <math|c<rsub|i>=w<rsub|i>> for the factor
    <math|e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|i>>-1> of a logarithmic correction, and
    <math|c<rsub|i>=T> for a warning. Assume that each nonzero warning forces
    its coordinate excursion within distance <math|2*B> of its address, and
    that <eqref|eq:warning-actual-price> holds. Put
    <math|<wide|c|~><rsub|i>=E<around|(|x|)>*T> for a warning and
    <math|<wide|c|~><rsub|i>=c<rsub|i>> otherwise. Then for every finite set
    <math|S> of distinct vertices and every set <math|O\<subseteq\>S> of
    optional vertices,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:selected-residual-body><big|int><around*|\||<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S\<setminus\>O>f<rsub|i>*<around|(|Y,Z|)>|\|>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|A>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S\<setminus\>O><wide|c|~><rsub|i>.
    </equation>

    The same bound holds after the probability averages over forests and
    tags. Consequently, the common mixed-body estimate retained in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-menu-alphabet> holds on the held step
    with the optional prices <math|<wide|c|~><rsub|i>> and the packet prices
    <math|C<rsub|0>*e<rsup|<around|(|s-g|)>*n<rsub|i>>>. These prices do not
    depend on the removed set.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The set <math|S<rprime|'>=S\<setminus\>O> is again a finite set of
    distinct vertices of the four types. The pointwise bounds are assumptions
    on individual vertices, so they hold for every member of
    <math|S<rprime|'>>. The excursion implication is also a property of each
    warning separately. If <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S<rprime|'>>f<rsub|i>*<around|(|Y,Z|)>\<ne\>0>,
    then every warning factor in <math|S<rprime|'>> is nonzero. Hence
    <math|Z> lies in the excursion event of each of these warnings, that is,
    in <math|\<cal-C\>*<around|(|X<around|(|S<rprime|'>|)>;t,2*B|)>>. These
    are exactly the hypotheses of <eqref|eq:warning-sector-mass> for
    <math|S<rprime|'>>.

    For completeness we recall its proof in this case. Pointwise,

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S<rprime|'>>f<rsub|i>*<around|(|Y,Z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<b-1\><rsub|\<cal-C\>*<around|(|X<around|(|S<rprime|'>|)>;t,2*B|)>><around|(|Z|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S<rprime|'>>c<rsub|i>.
    </equation*>

    The left side is measurable and bounded on a probability space, so it is
    integrable. By Fubini's theorem, integrate first in <math|Z> for fixed
    <math|Y>. The Gaussian law <math|\<gamma\><rsub|A>> does not depend on
    <math|Y>, so <eqref|eq:warning-actual-price> bounds the inner integral by
    <math|E<around|(|x|)><rsup|<around|\||X<around|(|S<rprime|'>|)>|\|>>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S<rprime|'>>c<rsub|i>>.
    Integrating this constant against the probability measure <math|\<nu\>>
    leaves it unchanged. Since distinct warnings of <math|S<rprime|'>> have
    distinct addresses, <math|<around|\||X<around|(|S<rprime|'>|)>|\|>> is
    the number of warnings in <math|S<rprime|'>>, and the bound equals
    <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S<rprime|'>><wide|c|~><rsub|i>>. Only the
    warnings that remain in <math|S<rprime|'>> enter the event, so a removed
    warning takes its probability price <math|E<around|(|x|)>> with it. No
    product of separate warning probabilities is used.

    The right side of <eqref|eq:selected-residual-body> does not depend on
    the forest, the tags or the configuration. Averaging the left side with
    probability weights therefore preserves the bound. The admitted removals
    in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-menu-alphabet> replace only
    optional labels, those in <math|\<sigma\><around|(|T|)>>. So the residual
    body is the product over <math|S\<setminus\>\<sigma\><around|(|T|)>>, the
    packet prices are those of the retained packets, and
    <eqref|eq:selected-residual-body> with <math|O=\<sigma\><around|(|T|)>>
    is the stated common estimate. In that lemma, the inserted factors are
    bounded pointwise by their wages before this single integration. The
    switched integral is therefore at most
    <math|<big|prod><rsub|\<lambda\>\<in\>T>w<rsub|\<lambda\>>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S\<setminus\>\<sigma\><around|(|T|)>><wide|c|~><rsub|i>>,
    without multiplying bounds for separate integrals.
  </proof>

  The lemma removes no hypothesis of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-warnings>.
  It uses the pointwise vertex bounds, the plateau and mean inputs behind the
  excursion implication, and the uniform covariance bound behind
  <eqref|eq:warning-actual-price>. On the held step these remain to be
  supplied by their local producers: Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:packet-estimates>
  for packets, the inherited, remote and itinerary prices for the logarithmic
  corrections, and Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:warning-safety> for the
  warning plateau. That attachment is part of the remaining analytic work
  listed above. The prices <math|<wide|c|~><rsub|i>> are the ones whose
  rooted site sums enter the mixed component estimate. In particular, a
  warning carries <math|E<around|(|x|)>*T>, not <math|T>.

  <subsubsection|Adjacent quadratic data and the warning
  inputs><label|sec:selected-local-warnings>

  The stationary cancellation used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:warning-safety>
  requires identities between the two adjacent quadratic forms and their
  paths. We specify these identities on the retained history before using
  that cancellation. This also gives a precise meaning to the compatibility
  of the tube with its quadratic data.

  Fix <math|N,p> and <math|i\<less\>N>. The tube of this step consists of the
  stored tower at <math|<around|(|N,i|)>>, the incoming path
  <math|R<rsub|N,i+1>> with its clip, and the outgoing path
  <math|R<rsub|N,i>> with its clip. Here <math|R<rsub|N,m>> denotes the path
  underlying the representation <math|R<rsub|N,p,m>>, not its activity array.
  Its two beds are <math|H<rsub|f>=M.*H*<around|(|N,i+1|)>> and
  <math|H<rsub|c>=M.*H*<around|(|N,i|)>>, periodized on the respective tori.
  Use the notation <math|D<rsub|f>,D<rsub|c>,J,J<rprime|'>,A<rsub|0>,T,<wide|T|\<bar\>>,M<rsub|F>,M<rsub|N>,M<rsub|O>>
  of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-rg-horizon-geometry> for these
  very data. By <em|adjacent quadratic compatibility> we mean the following:

  <\enumerate>
    <item>The diagonal two-scale identity holds for these beds and this
    installed response, including the quadratic identity
    <eqref|eq:mean-diagonal> and the transport identities
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-horizon-diagonal>.

    <item>The six block identities <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-horizon-gram>\U<eqref|eq:ex-lattice-horizon-orthogonal>
    hold for this tower and these clips.

    <item>Both full quadratic bilinear forms are invariant under signed
    coordinate changes. The two paths are attached to their respective beds,
    and those beds have the floor identities and bounds of the retained
    horizon at the indicated periods and clips. In particular, the full
    curvature factors obey <math|J<rsup|\<ast\>>*J=W> and
    <math|J<rprime|'><rsup|\<ast\>>*J<rprime|'>=W<rprime|'>> for the two
    metric matrices, with the same factors as in the block identities.
  </enumerate>

  The floors here include the metric and diagonal inverse identities,
  normalization of the metric zero mode, equality of the metric and diagonal
  actions on exact curvatures, symmetry, and the stated lower and upper
  counting-norm bounds. They are the floor data of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-rg-horizon-geometry>; positivity
  of an unrelated quadratic form would not replace them.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Compatibility of the stored adjacent
    tube><label|lem:selected-tube-compatibility>Retain the common horizon and
    stationary plan used to construct the selected history, with their
    quadratic and path receipts as in Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history>
    and <reference|subsec:path-retained-plan>. Every stored tube at
    <math|i\<less\>N> has adjacent quadratic compatibility. This includes
    <math|i=0> when <math|N\<gtr\>0>, and holds on every admitted period for
    the same paths and tower.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Take the two witnesses to be <math|M.*H*<around|(|N,i+1|)>> and
    <math|M.*H*<around|(|N,i|)>>, without changing the horizon. Its adjacent
    crossing identity gives item (1). Its retained block completion gives
    item (2), by Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-horizon-roll>.
    The horizon construction retains these block identities after its price
    enlargement; this is part of the construction stated at the end of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-rg-horizon-geometry>. The
    signed invariance of the beds is the invariance of their full quadratic
    forms proved in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:quadratic-lattice-symmetry>.
    The first clause of the stationary plan,
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:path-plan>, attaches <math|R<rsub|N,i+1>>
    and <math|R<rsub|N,i>> to these beds. The floor receipts belong to those
    same beds and clips by the retained-horizon input of that plan. This
    proves item (3).

    All clauses apply at <math|i+1\<leqslant\>N> and <math|i\<leqslant\>N>.
    These inequalities follow from <math|i\<less\>N>, and do not require
    <math|i\<gtr\>0>. The argument therefore applies to the actual stored
    step at zero; it invokes no default comparison door. Likewise, it applies
    when <math|i+1=N>: we use the final retained horizon's receipts, not
    equality with a different horizon only valid away from the terminal
    level. Choosing the shooting parameter changes neither these paths nor
    the tower. The periodized identities and attachments thus remain the ones
    used in the replayed history.
  </proof>

  We next record what this supplies to the warning argument. Keep the funded
  path, shell, guide and local precision bounds used in
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-guides>, <reference|sec:sector-gram>,
  <reference|sec:local-covariance> and <reference|sec:local-mean>, on the
  stored tube. For a sector, compatibility of its store means that its
  coarse-flag component equals the flags of its coarse field at the coarse
  threshold; its other seed components need not vanish. Let
  <math|x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>>, <math|h=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1>>
  and retain the scales of <eqref|eq:warning-scales>. In particular the fine
  representation has flag threshold <math|\<tau\>> and actual coupling
  <math|\<beta\>\<in\><around|[|h/2,2*h|]>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Warnings on the stored sectors><label|lem:selected-warning-inputs>Under
    these funded local bounds and the common purchases of this section, the
    mean, plateau and covariance inputs of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-warnings> hold simultaneously on
    every compatible sector of every stored step. More precisely, for each
    fixed allowed stage pair there are constants
    <math|C<rsub|\<mu\>>,M,a,\<xi\>\<gtr\>0> and a common lower threshold for
    <math|x>, independent of <math|N,p>, the forest, tags and coarse field,
    such that

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|e>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|\<mu\>>*\<tau\>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|loc>>\<leqslant\>M*1,<space|2em>C<rsub|\<mu\>>+1\<leqslant\>a,<space|2em>q*\<xi\>\<leqslant\>1.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|C<rsub|<math-up|loc>>> is the covariance of the single
    Gaussian vector used in that sector. Outside the <math|30*B> seed
    neighbourhood the event <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|B><around|(|j|)>> gives site
    and bond likelihoods equal to one, fine plaquette chords less than
    <math|\<tau\>/4>, and predictor chords less than <math|W*\<tau\>>.
    Consequently every nonzero warning forces its excursion within distance
    <math|2*B>, and its joint probability price can be taken to be

    <\equation*>
      E<around|(|x|)>=2*b<rsub|2*B>*p<rsub|<math-up|coord>>*exp
      <around|(|-\<xi\>*<sqrt|P>/4|)>.
    </equation*>

    The same assertions hold with the reduced warning margin <math|\<xi\>/2>,
    and the corresponding price, when the zero-germ statement of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:activity-jet-assembly> is required.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The compatibility lemma supplies the diagonal identity, signed
    invariance, full curvature factors and stack identities used in
    <eqref|eq:mean-stationary-zero>. Thus the stationary cancellation and
    local mean comparison of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-mean>
    concern this tube, with its original paths and tags. Apply
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:warning-safety> to it. Its explicit choice
    <eqref|eq:safety-mean-cap> gives <math|C<rsub|\<mu\>>>;
    <eqref|eq:safety-goals> fixes the fine and predictor margins, and
    <eqref|eq:safety-xi> gives <math|\<xi\>>. The three errors in that proof
    are bounded using the same stationary cancellation, transported rows and
    local inverse. Its final choice <math|a\<geqslant\>C<rsub|\<mu\>>+1>
    gives the broad cutoff plateau. No new stationary field is selected for
    this application.

    To verify the covariance input, use the completed sector precision
    <math|A> of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:sector-gram>. Its gap and
    weighted row bounds supply <eqref|eq:covariance-data>.
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-covariance> constructs
    <math|C<rsub|<math-up|loc>>=<around|(|P<rsub|0>+P<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>|)>/2>
    from the local window inverse <math|P<rsub|0>>, and proves
    <math|C<rsub|<math-up|loc>>\<geqslant\>0> and
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|C<rsub|<math-up|loc>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>S>
    for fixed <math|S>. The weighted row-and-column norm dominates the
    Euclidean operator norm, so <math|M=max <around|{|S,1|}>> gives the
    claimed bound. Restriction to active coordinates preserves it by
    extension of a test vector by zero. This is the covariance of the sector
    Gaussian, not an identification with the full inverse <math|A<rsup|-1>>
    or with the different derivative-row construction.

    The sector's flag identity makes a site farther than <math|30*B> from its
    seeds clean on the hulls required in the safety proof. That proof gives
    the claimed plateau conclusion on <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|B><around|(|j|)>>.
    For the five mask, likelihood and contraction types, a warning outside
    its compulsory region has its address outside the <math|300*B> seed
    neighbourhood. Since <math|300*B\<geqslant\>30*B+B>, the hypotheses of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:warning-vanishes> apply, including the
    fine-site contraction and the enlargement to <math|2*B>. For a fine-bond
    cutoff the mean bound and <math|q*\<xi\>\<leqslant\>1> give the
    broad-cutoff plateau directly; its additional hot-bond and <math|304*B>
    exclusions only set more factors to zero. Finally apply
    <eqref|eq:warning-actual-price> with the just established <math|M> and
    <math|\<xi\>>. It prices distinct addresses, including different types at
    one site. The strict margins in the safety proof persist at
    <math|\<xi\>/2>, giving the zero-germ assertion and the same Gaussian
    calculation at that margin.

    For each fixed stage pair all constants above precede the final lower
    threshold for <math|x>. There are finitely many permitted lower stages
    for each upper stage. Take the maximum of these thresholds, and then its
    running maximum in the upper stage, in the common purchases before
    selecting the history. All estimates hold on upper tails, so this
    preserves the earlier purchases. The stored clock pays that threshold at
    <math|x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>>, also at <math|i=0>. The asserted
    uniformity follows from the uniformity over periods, sectors and tags in
    each cited local estimate. The already selected histories are not altered
    afterwards.
  </proof>

  This supplies the warning part of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-residual-body>
  from the funded local construction. Its packet, forced and logarithmic
  pointwise bounds, and the component and differentiated entrance estimates,
  still require their own attachment. In particular no health condition for a
  packet's enlarged support follows from the sector's coarse-flag identity
  alone.

  <subsubsection|The value prices on the selected
  input><label|sec:selected-value-prices>

  We now attach the scalar prices to the actual incoming representation. Fix
  an allowed pair of stages <math|j=J*<around|(|i+1|)>> and
  <math|k=J<around|(|i|)>>, and put <math|x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>>,
  <math|h=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1>=x+s<rsub|G>*log L> and <math|P=exp
  <around|(|<around|(|log log h|)><rsup|2>|)>>. The radius used for the
  component expansion is <math|B=B<around|(|x|)>>; the incoming
  representation has radius <math|B<around|(|h|)>>. These two radii are not
  identified.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The stored representation is an admissible
    input><label|lem:selected-value-input>For every <math|i\<less\>N>, every
    admitted period and every retained member of the shooting interval, its
    representation at <math|i+1> has the incoming path and high clip of the
    stored tube. Its threshold is <math|P/<sqrt|h>>, its radius is
    <math|B<around|(|h|)>>, and its support exponent is that of stage
    <math|j>. Its coupling belongs to <math|<around|[|h/2,2*h|]>>. Its
    regular and forced activity bounds are, respectively, no larger than

    <\equation*>
      h<rsup|-11/10>,<space|2em>e<rsup|-P<rsup|1/5>>,
    </equation*>

    and its path bounds may be used with loop allowance <math|2*P>. The tube
    retains its funded step and the coarse period bound
    <math|c\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|1+B<around|(|x|)>|)>>. These statements
    also hold at <math|i=0>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The interval construction in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history>
    appends the actual reset batch to the incoming history. The batch
    relation retains its paths, clips, budget points and label bounds; it
    does not only identify the resulting density. At the top these data are
    supplied by the Wilson startup, and at every lower level they are part of
    the retained-history assertion of that construction. The path and clip at
    <math|i+1> are therefore precisely the upward path and high clip of the
    stored tube at <math|i>. The budget point there is the one assigned by
    the common clock. Its threshold and radius are <math|P/<sqrt|h>> and
    <math|B<around|(|h|)>>, and its profile is the stage-<math|j> profile.
    The regular allowance is at most <math|h<rsup|-11/10>> and the forced
    allowance is <math|e<rsup|-P<rsup|1/5>>>, by the calendar allowances and
    the final absorption in that construction. The chosen member stays in the
    level band; substitution of the clock identity <math|h=x+s<rsub|G>*log L>
    makes this exactly <math|<around|[|h/2,2*h|]>>.

    The purchased protection exponent obeys
    <math|0\<less\>\<theta\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>1> and <math|P\<geqslant\>1>.
    Hence the retained loop capacity <math|2*P<rsup|\<theta\><rsub|j>>> is at
    most <math|2*P>. Increasing this numerical allowance preserves the same
    path coefficients and all their bounds. The funded step and the size
    inequality are those of the common schedule, at coarse level <math|i>; no
    limiting argument in the period is used. Each argument requires only
    <math|i\<less\>N>, which supplies the existing fine level
    <math|i+1\<leqslant\>N>. Thus it also covers zero. Finally, selecting a
    member of the interval retains all these assertions and introduces no new
    path, budget point or label family.
  </proof>

  Choose a halo multiplier <math|\<ell\>\<geqslant\>500> containing all read
  radii in the local construction, an output support exponent
  <math|a\<geqslant\>0>, a length reserve <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0> and a
  tree reserve <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>. Write

  <\equation*>
    H=a+2*\<varepsilon\>+\<delta\>.
  </equation*>

  As in the common purchases, choose the reserves before <math|L>, with
  <math|4*\<varepsilon\>> strictly below the incoming support exponent and
  <math|8*\<varepsilon\>> strictly below both adjacent path widths. Choose
  <math|L> large enough for the cover conversions in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:inherited-activity-prices> at exponent
  <math|H>. Fix <math|r\<in\><around|(|-1/2,0|)>>.

  Use the disjoint union of the original forced labels, logarithmic labels
  and warning addresses. Equal supports do not identify labels. For a forced
  label <math|u>, its support <math|X<rsub|u>> is its fine support projected
  and enlarged by the prescribed <math|\<ell\>*B> halo, and its extra charge
  <math|d<rsub|u>> is the cover number of that original fine support at
  radius <math|B<around|(|h|)>>. For a logarithmic label use its full
  enlarged read support and the charge assigned in the inherited or itinerary
  construction: the old cover, the true path length (including winding and
  repeated edges), or the correction order. The three short families have
  charge zero. For a warning use its full warning footprint and charge zero.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|One common value-price array><label|prop:selected-value-prices>Retain
    the funded local estimates used above, including their seed energy, chart
    and normalization constructions. On each stored step there are
    nonnegative prices <math|v<rsub|u>,w<rsub|z>> for the original forced and
    logarithmic labels, independent of forest, tags, sector and integration
    variables, such that the forced factor is bounded by <math|v<rsub|u>>,
    and both the logarithmic factor <math|\<ell\><rsub|z>> and
    <math|e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|z>>-1> are bounded in absolute value by
    <math|w<rsub|z>>. With warning price <math|T*E<around|(|x|)>> from
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-warning-inputs>, their combined array
    <math|f> obeys

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:selected-optional-price>sup<rsub|y>
      <big|sum><rsub|u:y\<in\>X<rsub|u>>f<rsub|u>*e<rsup|2*\<varepsilon\>*d<rsub|u>+H*c<rsub|B><around|(|X<rsub|u>|)>>\<leqslant\>h<rsup|r>.
    </equation>

    For every fixed list of forced labels used in the contraction factors,
    the actual connected packet with seed set <math|A> obeys, uniformly in
    that list,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:selected-packet-price><around|\||\<cal-P\><rsub|A>|\|>\<leqslant\>p<rsub|A>\<assign\>exp
      <around|{|<around|(|C*d<rsup|5>-\<beta\>*\<mu\>*\<tau\><rsup|2>|)>*n<around|(|A|)>|}>,<space|2em>d=1+B+<around|(|log
      h|)><rsup|2>,<space|1em>\<tau\>=P/<sqrt|h>,
    </equation>

    with one <math|C\<geqslant\>0> and the purchased <math|\<mu\>\<gtr\>0>.
    For any fixed extra charge <math|b\<geqslant\>0> and seed mark
    <math|\<nu\>\<geqslant\>0>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:selected-packet-root-price>sup<rsub|y>
      <big|sum><rsub|A:y\<in\>X<rsub|A>>p<rsub|A>*e<rsup|<around|(|2*\<varepsilon\>*b+\<nu\>|)>*n<around|(|A|)>+H*c<rsub|B><around|(|X<rsub|A>|)>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<mu\>*P<rsup|2>/8>.
    </equation>

    The requests <math|b,\<nu\>,r>, halo and exponent reserves are fixed
    before the common schedule is purchased. All three estimates hold at one
    common threshold, which may depend on these requests and is chosen before
    the history, uniformly in the cutoff, period and admitted input member.
    Disconnected packets have factor and price zero.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    We first verify the inputs rather than selecting new activity arrays.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-value-input> supplies the incoming
    coefficient bounds, path budgets, threshold, support exponent, radius and
    coupling window on precisely this tube. The exponential forced allowance
    has every negative upper exponent: with <math|y=log log h>,
    <math|P<rsup|1/5>/log h=exp <around|(|y<rsup|2>/5-y|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>.
    The incoming regular allowance has upper exponent <math|-11/10> and the
    loop allowance has upper exponent zero. The inherited support conversion
    therefore applies with its original labels and masks. Its radius
    condition <math|B<around|(|h|)>\<leqslant\>2*B<around|(|x|)>> holds
    eventually since <math|h-x> is fixed for this stage pair. The strict
    width and support reserves imposed above are exactly those needed for its
    factor <math|e<rsup|2*\<varepsilon\>*d<rsub|u>>>. This includes the
    squared path tails, whose reserve is <math|8*\<varepsilon\>>, not
    <math|4*\<varepsilon\>>.

    Choose <math|s> with <math|-1/2\<less\>s\<less\>r>. The inherited
    construction gives the forced rooted bound and the six inherited
    logarithmic rooted bounds at upper exponent at most <math|s>, with head
    exponent <math|H> and the stated extra charges. The three remote short
    families have upper exponent <math|-1/2> by <eqref|eq:remote-short-price>
    and its support count. Their sector indicators remove the packet
    neighbourhoods, leaving the clean read regions on which that estimate is
    proved; their values use the original tags. The correction itineraries
    have every negative upper exponent by <eqref|eq:itinerary-site-series>,
    with their full read supports and correction-order charge. Its count sums
    ordered words, including repeated bonds. Together these are all the
    logarithmic labels. Take the disjoint union of these nonnegative price
    arrays, including their finite channel multiplicities. Their rooted sum
    <math|J<around|(|x|)>> has upper exponent at most <math|s>. No prices are
    selected separately for different forests or configurations: the quoted
    bounds are uniform over these variables and depend only on the fixed
    input coefficients and geometric labels.

    Every nonzero logarithmic label has nonempty support. Since all support
    and extra-cost exponents are nonnegative, its scalar price
    <math|b<rsub|z>> is at most <math|J<around|(|x|)>>, by retaining its term
    in the rooted sum at any point of its support. Eventually
    <math|J<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>1>. For
    <math|<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|z>|\|>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|z>\<leqslant\>1>,
    the mean value theorem gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|z>>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>e*b<rsub|z>.
    </equation*>

    Thus <math|w<rsub|z>=e*b<rsub|z>> pays both
    <math|<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|z>|\|>> and
    <math|<around|\||e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|z>>-1|\|>>. It multiplies the rooted
    sum by a fixed constant and preserves its upper exponent. A label with
    identically zero factor can be priced by zero. The forced price needs no
    exponentiation.

    The warning support is contained in a fixed-multiple-<math|B> ball and
    has bounded <math|B>-cover number. There are a fixed number of warning
    types per coarse site and at most a polynomial in <math|B> possible
    addresses whose footprints meet a given site. Consequently the warning
    rooted price, including <math|e<rsup|H*c<rsub|B><around|(|X<rsub|u>|)>>>,
    is at most a fixed constant times a polynomial in <math|B> times
    <math|T*E<around|(|x|)>>. It has every negative upper exponent by the
    Gaussian warning estimate. Summing the forced, logarithmic and warning
    arrays therefore gives a function of upper exponent at most <math|s>. As
    <math|s\<less\>r>, that sum is at most <math|h<rsup|r>> eventually. This
    proves <eqref|eq:selected-optional-price>, including all three families
    and both reserves in one bound.

    For packets, the seed-energy allocation of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-energy> gives the core gain
    <math|\<mu\>*\<tau\><rsup|2>*n<around|(|A|)>>. If a physical packet is
    nonzero, its retained masks enforce the hot-bond control and flag
    agreement needed in the <math|200*B> annular comparison;
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:packet-annulus> gives the remainder of
    upper exponent <math|-3/2>. The normalization corrections are bounded by
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-covariance>, and the protected chart
    logarithm is eventually at most one by
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:haar-chart>. The finite tag probabilities,
    with their actual multiplicities, give the ticket count in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:packet-estimates>. Its proof of
    <eqref|eq:packet-value-price> now gives <eqref|eq:selected-packet-price>.
    Vanishing packets satisfy the same bound. The contraction factors for any
    fixed forced list take values in <math|<around|[|0,1|]>>; the proof drops
    them, so its price is uniform in that list. This is a value estimate and
    imposes no global health condition on the packet neighbourhood.

    Finally <math|\<beta\>\<geqslant\>h/2> gives
    <math|\<beta\>*\<mu\>*\<tau\><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>\<mu\>*P<rsup|2>/2>. In
    the absorption argument of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:packet-estimates>
    take <math|u=1/2> and replace its seed expense <math|C*d<rsup|5>> by
    <math|C*d<rsup|5>+2*\<varepsilon\>*b+\<nu\>>. These additional fixed
    costs are absorbed by increasing the coefficient of <math|d<rsup|5>>,
    since <math|d\<geqslant\>1>. Use the head exponent <math|H> and
    <math|n<rsub|0>=1> there. Its bound <math|e<rsup|-\<mu\>*u*P<rsup|2>/4>>
    is exactly <eqref|eq:selected-packet-root-price>. All constants are fixed
    before the final threshold in <math|x>. The same finite-stage maximum and
    running-maximum purchase as in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-warning-inputs>
    makes every required threshold hold at the stored steps. The bounds are
    uniform over the members of the original input interval, so selecting its
    retained member preserves them.
  </proof>

  For clarity we spell out the resulting component input. Give a packet extra
  cost <math|b*n<around|(|A|)>> and mark <math|\<nu\>*n<around|(|A|)>>, and
  give an optional label its charge <math|d<rsub|u>> and zero mark. Set

  <\equation*>
    \<Gamma\>=h<rsup|r>+e<rsup|-\<mu\>*P<rsup|2>/8>,<space|2em>q<rsub|x>=h<rsup|r/2>,<space|2em>h<rsub|B>=<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>.
  </equation*>

  For sufficiently large <math|x>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:selected-value-tree>\<Gamma\>\<leqslant\>2*h<rsup|r>,<space|2em>4*h<rsub|B>*\<Gamma\>/\<delta\>\<leqslant\>q<rsub|x>\<less\>1.
  </equation>

  Indeed the exponential packet price is smaller than <math|h<rsup|r>>
  eventually, and <math|8*h<rsub|B>*h<rsup|r/2>/\<delta\>\<to\>0>, since
  <math|B=O<around|(|<around|(|log h|)><rsup|2>|)>> and <math|r\<less\>0>.
  The two rooted bounds above are precisely the vertex condition of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mixed-activity-prices>, at head exponent
  <math|a+2*\<varepsilon\>+\<delta\>>. In particular the common-body estimate
  of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-residual-body> and the component
  summation apply to this actual value alphabet, with its original Gaussian
  and Haar measures.

  For example, for connected sets of labels with at least <math|m+1> labels,
  at least <math|r<rsub|0>> seeds and total cover-plus-extra-cost at least
  <math|D<rsub|0>>, <eqref|eq:mixed-ceiling> gives the rooted sum of their
  value majorants, weighted by output support exponent <math|a> and
  polynomial order <math|k<rsub|0>>, at most

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:selected-value-component>P<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,k<rsub|0>>*e<rsup|-\<varepsilon\>*D<rsub|0>-\<nu\>*r<rsub|0>>*<frac|\<Gamma\>*q<rsub|x><rsup|m>|1-q<rsub|x>>.
  </equation>

  Use <math|r<rsub|0>=0> unless the component is required to contain a
  packet. This is an estimate for the one actual value alphabet and a fixed
  forced list. Uniformity in that list is not permission to sum unpriced
  copies. If a later response assigns several analytic outputs to the same
  component, their majorants must first be summed over that component's fiber
  as required by Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mixed-activity-prices>. The
  differentiated entrance still requires healthy full supports and the
  stock-extension argument of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:activity-jet-assembly>.
  Neither that additional hypothesis nor the precursor and vapor
  identifications follows from <eqref|eq:selected-value-component> alone.

  <subsubsection|Summing the actual component
  variants><label|sec:selected-component-variants>

  We now supply the thin-component input left open in
  Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-lattice-rg-usable>. Retain the
  local hypotheses of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-value-prices>
  and the offer, occupancy and shipment hypotheses of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-menu-alphabet>. They refer to the same
  stored representation. Write <math|E> for the available component exponent,
  <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0> for its length and polynomial reserve, and
  <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0> for its tree reserve. Its head exponent is
  <math|E+2*\<varepsilon\>+\<delta\>>. Let <math|r\<in\><around|(|-1/2,0|)>>
  be the chosen rate. These requests precede the common schedule.

  It is useful to retain a growing seed mark, rather than just the fixed
  marks in <eqref|eq:selected-packet-root-price>. Put

  <\equation*>
    M=\<mu\>*P<rsup|2>/4,<space|2em>\<gamma\><rsub|0>=h<rsup|r>+e<rsup|-M/4>,<space|2em>R<rsub|<math-up|menu>>=h<rsup|r>*e<rsup|<around|(|E+2*\<varepsilon\>+\<delta\>|)>*c<rsub|\<ast\>>>,<space|2em>\<gamma\><rsub|1>=\<gamma\><rsub|0>+R<rsub|<math-up|menu>>,<space|2em>\<rho\>=h<rsup|r/2>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|c<rsub|\<ast\>>> is the fixed birth-halo cover constant in the
  shipment conversion. The optional menu price is at most
  <math|h<rsup|r>+R<rsub|<math-up|menu>>>. The packet site estimate, with the
  additional seed weight <math|e<rsup|M*n<around|(|A|)>>>, is at most
  <math|e<rsup|-M/4>> eventually. Indeed <math|\<beta\>*\<mu\>*\<tau\><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>\<mu\>*P<rsup|2>/2=2*M>.
  After extracting <math|M> per seed, at least <math|M> remains. The packet
  enumeration of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mixed-activity-prices> adds
  <math|2*log D<rsub|B>> per seed, the fixed support and length costs, and
  the expense <math|C*d<rsup|5>>. Their sum is <math|o<around|(|M|)>>, since
  <math|d=O<around|(|<around|(|log h|)><rsup|2>|)>> and <math|P=exp
  <around|(|<around|(|log log h|)><rsup|2>|)>>. Eventually the geometric
  ratio is at most <math|e<rsup|-M/2>>. Its rooted sum is at most
  <math|H<rsub|B>*e<rsup|-M/2>/<around|(|1-e<rsup|-M/2>|)>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-M/4>>,
  because <math|H<rsub|B>> grows polynomially in <math|B>. This proves the
  asserted marked packet price, without treating <math|M> as a fixed request.

  Both <math|\<gamma\><rsub|0>> and <math|\<gamma\><rsub|1>> have upper
  exponent <math|r>. Thus, after one further threshold increase,

  <\equation*>
    <frac|4*<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*\<gamma\><rsub|0>|\<delta\>>\<leqslant\><frac|1|2>,<space|2em><frac|4*<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*\<gamma\><rsub|1>|\<delta\>>\<leqslant\>\<rho\>\<leqslant\><frac|1|2>.
  </equation*>

  For an integer <math|k\<geqslant\>0> define the two positive component
  budgets

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:selected-component-budgets>O<rsub|k>=P<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,k>*<frac|\<gamma\><rsub|0>|1-1/2>,<space|2em>U<rsub|k>=P<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,k>*<frac|\<gamma\><rsub|1>|1-\<rho\>>,<space|2em>P<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,k>=e<rsup|\<varepsilon\>>*k!\<varepsilon\><rsup|-k>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|O<rsub|k>> pays the original alphabet and <math|U<rsub|k>> the
  augmented one. Both have upper exponent <math|r>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Component fibers and the thin rooted
    bound><label|lem:selected-component-fibers>Let <math|C> be an actual
    connected finite set of elementary labels, with full support
    <math|X<rsub|C>> and total cover-plus-length charge <math|s<rsub|C>>. Its
    menu replacement terms admit nonnegative majorants <math|b<rsub|C,T>>
    such that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:selected-component-fiber><big|sum><rsub|T>b<rsub|C,T>=<around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|A\<in\>C<math-up|packet>>p<rsub|A>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|u\<in\>C<math-up|optional>>v<rsub|u><rsup|\<cal-I\>>.
    </equation>

    The sum includes the distinct menu entries at a common address. The
    normalized forest and tag averages cost no additional factor. With

    <\equation*>
      t<rsub|C>=<around|(|1+s<rsub|C>|)><rsup|k>*<big|sum><rsub|T>b<rsub|C,T>,
    </equation*>

    the rooted sum over all such components is at most <math|U<rsub|k>> at
    exponent <math|E>. Restricting to thin components preserves this bound.
    Restricting instead to components containing a packet gives, for
    <math|k=0>, the stronger bound <math|U<rsub|0>*e<rsup|-M>>. All these are
    bounds for the actual component labels and their summed replacement
    terms.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    A replacement set <math|T> must occupy distinct optional addresses of
    <math|C>. Give it the majorant consisting of its wages, the original
    prices at unreplaced addresses and the unchanged packet prices. The
    residual-body lemma bounds the absolute integral by this product on the
    one Gaussian and Haar probability space. This bound is uniform in the
    forest and finite tags. Their averages have nonnegative coefficients
    summing to one; applying the triangle inequality before averaging
    preserves the same product. These averaging indices are not extra
    unweighted labels in the component alphabet. The only sum over
    replacement choices is exactly the finite product identity
    <eqref|eq:selected-menu-ticket-product>, which proves
    <eqref|eq:selected-component-fiber>.

    Apply <eqref|eq:mixed-ceiling> to the augmented alphabet with site price
    <math|\<gamma\><rsub|1>>, tail ratio <math|\<rho\>>, and mark <math|M> on
    every packet seed. For the unrestricted components take minimum seed
    count zero, minimum extra cost zero and minimum excess label count zero.
    This gives <math|U<rsub|k>>. A thin component is a subset of these
    distinct connected sets, not another copy of each set, so restriction
    decreases the nonnegative sum. If a component contains a packet, its
    total number of typed seeds is at least one. Taking minimum seed count
    one and <math|k=0> gives <math|U<rsub|0>*e<rsup|-M>>.

    If a later output groups several terms with the same component or
    barcode, the norm of their sum is bounded by the sum of these already
    assigned majorants. The argument uses support containment when an output
    has smaller support. No estimate is made by multiplying the component
    bound by an unbounded number of copies. For absolute convergence on the
    finite torus, every nonzero component has nonempty support and
    <math|E\<geqslant\>0>. Summing the rooted bound over all sites gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|C>t<rsub|C>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|c>|\|>*U<rsub|k>.
    </equation*>

    Consequently every subseries and every regrouping just used converges
    absolutely. The volume factor is used only for this assertion; it is not
    inserted into the rooted estimate.
  </proof>

  Fix a field derivative order <math|j>. Choose the polynomial order
  <math|k=K+j> returned by the integrated derivative estimate below, and a
  nonnegative upper-exponent-zero function <math|A<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>>
  enlarged to dominate its constant. These choices precede the common
  schedule and are used in all the following pledges; a smaller arbitrary
  polynomial order would not pay the differentiated terms. Put

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:selected-thin-pledges>P<rsub|0>=<around|(|1+2<rsup|j+2>*A<rsub|j>|)>*<around|(|O<rsub|k>+2*U<rsub|k>|)>,<space|2em>P<rsub|X>=O<rsub|k>+2*U<rsub|0>,<space|2em>V=10*<around|(|P<rsub|0>+P<rsub|X>|)>.
  </equation>

  Choose <math|a\<geqslant\>0> and <math|e\<gtr\>0> with
  <math|a+2*e\<leqslant\>E>. These are also fixed before the schedule. Since
  all three displayed prices have upper exponent <math|r\<less\>0>,
  eventually

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:selected-two-tree-smallness><frac|4*<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*P<rsub|0>|e>\<leqslant\>\<rho\>\<leqslant\><frac|1|2>,<space|2em><frac|4*<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*V|e>\<leqslant\><frac|1|2>,<space|2em>V\<leqslant\><frac|1|2>.
  </equation>

  For example the first left side divided by <math|\<rho\>> has upper
  exponent <math|r/2\<less\>0>; the other assertions follow directly from
  their negative upper exponent. All bounds are uniform in the finite menu.

  In particular the actual thin weights <math|t<rsub|C>> satisfy

  <\equation*>
    sup<rsub|z> <big|sum><rsub|C:<space|0.17em>z\<in\>X<rsub|C>>t<rsub|C>*e<rsup|<around|(|a+2*e|)>*c<rsub|B><around|(|X<rsub|C>|)>>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|0>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-component-fibers> gives
  <math|U<rsub|k>> at exponent <math|E>, and
  <math|U<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|0>>. This supplies both the vertex input
  and the tree smallness required in Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-lattice-rg-usable>.
  Its factorial-normalized cluster proof now gives the actual fragrance fee
  bound <math|P<rsub|0>/<around|(|1-\<rho\>|)>> at exponent <math|a+e>, and
  the subtracted response costs <math|2*P<rsub|0>/<around|(|1-\<rho\>|)>>.
  Repeated component occurrences in a cluster retain their factorial
  normalization. This is not a new unpriced fiber. The separate
  packet-containing component budget is at most <math|P<rsub|X>*e<rsup|-M>>,
  since <math|U<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|X>>. These statements construct the
  thin input; the reconstruction of the full forced response from these two
  populations still uses the separate cluster identities.

  <subsubsection|The integrated derivative with an actual
  insertion><label|sec:selected-inserted-derivative>

  We next construct the marked integral majorant, rather than assume that the
  scalar estimate can be differentiated. Fix one original optional label
  <math|u<rsub|\<ast\>>>, of fine crest cover <math|c<rsub|f>>, whose
  normalized insertion has pointwise amplitude <math|w\<geqslant\>0>. It is a
  regular or forced insertion, not a warning. Its local construction in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-block-integral> multiplies the
  insertion by its larger protective cutoff. Use the compensated variation of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:compensated-variation>, holding the original
  fine configuration <math|U<rsub|\<ast\>>> fixed. The inserted observable is
  then the constant <math|h<around|(|U<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>>. Each protective
  cutoff is a fixed smooth scalar function of a compensated increment divided
  by its threshold. The increment bounds in that section, divided by the
  threshold, have upper exponent zero; the fixed-order chain rule gives the
  same bound for each cutoff's derivative scale. The number of cutoff factors
  is bounded by a geometric function of upper exponent zero times the full
  cover plus length charge. The product rule adds these scales and retains
  amplitude <math|w>. It therefore gives scale at most
  <math|r<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>*<around|(|1+c<rsub|B><around|(|X<rsub|u<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)>+d<rsub|u<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)>>,
  with <math|r<rsub|\<ast\>>> of upper exponent zero. This representation
  with the original observable fixed is used on the protective cutoff's
  nonzero germ. Outside its closed support the factor is a zero germ; the
  protected extension and the strict room in the compensated chart cover the
  boundary as in that construction. No derivatives of the arbitrary original
  fine observable are taken.

  Choose the derivative order before the schedule, and retain the funded
  local jet hypotheses in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:activity-jet-assembly>,
  including the differentiated precision and compensation estimates. The
  variation is a map <math|V> from a finite-dimensional real normed parameter
  space, with <math|V<around|(|y<rsub|0>|)>> equal to the base coarse field.
  Its link jets through order <math|j> obey the bounds of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:compensated-variation>, with order-<math|k>
  bound <math|C<rsub|G>*v<around|(|x|)><rsup|k>>, where <math|v\<geqslant\>0>
  has upper exponent <math|-1/2> and the fixed convention for
  <math|C<rsub|G>> is the one used there. The constants below are uniform
  over this class of variations, not over arbitrary rescalings of the
  parameter. These are stronger local inputs than the covariance norm used
  only for value estimates. Let <math|C> be an admitted connected component
  containing <math|u<rsub|\<ast\>>>. At the base coarse field require its
  whole assigned union support <math|X<rsub|C>> to be healthy. Thus every
  packet's full <math|500*B> neighbourhood and every other read support is
  healthy. Use the smooth stock extension for packets and the unmasked
  coarse-tail extension, as specified in that section. They agree in value
  with the physical factors on this domain; we do not differentiate a
  physical flag test.

  Let <math|a<rsub|u>> be the elementary jet amplitudes, with
  <math|a<rsub|u>=T> for a warning, and let <math|J<rsub|C>> be the joint
  reduced-margin warning event. Put <math|b<rsub|C>=#<around|{|<text|warning
  labels in >C|}>> and <math|s=s<rsub|C>>. On the common Gaussian space let
  <math|I> be the full read-bond set and let <math|F<rsub|I>> be the
  polynomial defined in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-moment-prices>.
  The product rule, including the density ratio and the compensation
  Jacobian, supplies fixed integers <math|q,K> and nonnegative
  upper-exponent-zero functions <math|D<rsub|j>,E<rsub|j>> such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:selected-marked-integrand>g<rsub|C><around|(|z|)>=w*<space|-0.17em><big|prod><rsub|u\<in\>C\<setminus\><around|{|u<rsub|\<ast\>>|}>>a<rsub|u>*<space|0.17em>D<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>*<around|(|1+s|)><rsup|j>*\<b-1\><rsub|J<rsub|C>><around|(|z|)>*F<rsub|I><around|(|z|)><rsup|q*j>
  </equation>

  bounds every derivative through order <math|j> of this transformed
  integrand at the base parameter. It is uniform in the frozen Haar
  variables. The original label's price has been removed exactly once.
  Removing this label removes no warning, so <math|J<rsub|C>> and
  <math|b<rsub|C>> are unchanged.

  For completeness, the scale bound follows by adding all factor scales.
  Every nonzero factor has nonempty support, so their sum is at most a fixed
  upper-exponent-zero function times <math|1+s>. Write
  <math|r<rsub|<math-up|jet>><around|(|x|)>> for the common factor scale. The
  density-ratio derivatives add a scale of the form
  <math|D*F<rsub|I><rsup|q>>. Since <math|F<rsub|I>\<geqslant\>1>, the total
  scale is bounded by <math|<around|(|1+D+2*r<rsub|<math-up|jet>>+r<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>*<around|(|1+s|)>*F<rsub|I><rsup|q>>.
  Raising this to order at most <math|j> gives
  <eqref|eq:selected-marked-integrand>, after increasing <math|D<rsub|j>>.
  Warnings outside <math|J<rsub|C>> vanish as germs by the reduced-margin
  argument, so no derivative of its indicator is involved.

  The read count is at most <math|<around|(|1+s|)>*<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*\<rho\><rsub|L>>,
  where <math|\<rho\><rsub|L>> is the fixed bond-density constant. The
  Gaussian moment estimate at order <math|2*q*j> gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|\<bbb-E\>*F<rsub|I><rsup|2*q*j>|)><rsup|1/2>\<leqslant\>E<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>*<around|(|1+s|)><rsup|K>.
  </equation*>

  Purchase the warning bound <math|\<bbb-P\><around|(|J<rsub|C>|)>\<leqslant\>\<eta\><rsup|2*b<rsub|C>>>
  with a fixed arbitrarily negative upper exponent for <math|\<eta\>>.
  Applying Cauchy\USchwarz on this one Gaussian space proves that
  <math|g<rsub|C>> is integrable and that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:selected-marked-integral><big|int>g<rsub|C>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>w*<big|prod><rsub|u\<in\>C\<setminus\><around|{|u<rsub|\<ast\>>|}>><wide|a|~><rsub|u>*<space|0.17em><around|(|1+s|)><rsup|K+j>,<space|2em><wide|a|~><rsub|u>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|T*\<eta\>,>|<cell|u<text|
    a warning>,>>|<row|<cell|a<rsub|u>,>|<cell|<text|otherwise>.>>>>>
  </equation>

  Haar integration costs one. The local common-neighbourhood dominator of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-differentiation> justifies
  differentiating each component integral. The estimate above is a base-point
  derivative bound; it does not infer uniform differentiability of an
  infinite sum from bounds only at that point.

  There is an exact way to pay the marked factor before summing components.
  With <math|g=h<rsup|r>> and incoming support exponent
  <math|\<nu\><rsub|f>>, set

  <\equation*>
    j<rsub|\<ast\>>=g*e<rsup|-<around|(|\<nu\><rsub|f>-\<varepsilon\>|)>*c<rsub|f>>,<space|2em>D<rsub|\<ast\>>=<frac|w*e<rsup|<around|(|\<nu\><rsub|f>-\<varepsilon\>|)>*c<rsub|f>>|g>.
  </equation*>

  Then <math|D<rsub|\<ast\>>\<geqslant\>0> and
  <math|D<rsub|\<ast\>>*j<rsub|\<ast\>>=w>, including <math|w=0>. The
  marked-footprint calculation in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mixed-activity-prices>
  gives the extra rooted vertex price at most
  <math|g*e<rsup|<around|(|E+2*\<varepsilon\>+\<delta\>|)>*c<rsub|\<ast\>>>=R<rsub|<math-up|menu>>>.
  Replace the price at <math|u<rsub|\<ast\>>> by <math|j<rsub|\<ast\>>>,
  retaining the same support and length charge. The array is bounded by the
  old array plus this one new entry, so its site price is at most
  <math|\<gamma\><rsub|1>>; replacement is not assumed to increase the old
  price. Equation <eqref|eq:selected-marked-integral> is now
  <math|D<rsub|\<ast\>>*A<rsub|j>> times the product price of that array,
  multiplied by <math|<around|(|1+s|)><rsup|K+j>>. The differentiated packet
  amplitudes have the same marked seed estimate used above: their added
  expense <math|3*j*d<rsup|5>> is still <math|o<around|(|M|)>>. The
  differentiated optional prices retain exponent <math|r>, by choosing the
  intermediate exponent strictly below <math|r> before absorbing the fixed
  derivative constants.

  Consequently the sum of the integrated majorants for all admitted healthy
  components through a site, at exponent <math|E>, is at most

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:selected-marked-root>D<rsub|\<ast\>>*A<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>*U<rsub|K+j>.
  </equation>

  This statement concerns the sum of the base-point integral majorants. For a
  fixed sector and forest, Tonelli's theorem applies to the nonnegative
  witnesses on its probability space. When the actual component construction
  assigns different sectors, use the disjoint union of the component
  probability spaces with counting measure; Tonelli then gives the same sum
  of integrals. On the finite torus the total integral is at most
  <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|c>|\|>*D<rsub|\<ast\>>*A<rsub|j>*U<rsub|K+j>>.
  Restricting to any actual subfamily or averaging the forest and tags
  preserves the bound. The unmarked version is obtained by retaining the
  original factor instead of replacing it; its price is
  <math|A<rsub|j>*O<rsub|K+j>>. For a physical thin term with empty flag
  inventory, health on its support identifies this local extension with the
  physical value; outside that domain one uses its prescribed mask. No
  smoothness of that mask across the boundary is claimed. Thus the derivative
  input has the correct domain and a common integrable witness on the
  selected tube, including the directly stored step at index zero.

  <subsubsection|The precursor and the two local correction
  arrays><label|sec:selected-precursor-vapor>

  The preceding bounds can now be used before either of the numerical
  response payments. We describe the actual arrays being paid. Retain the
  local return and its exact splitting and reset hypotheses from
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-nonlinear-offering> and
  <reference|sec:physical-reset-reorganization>; in particular the
  truncation, folding, path and clipping data are those of the stored return.
  The small-field comparison and reset estimates in those sections are still
  local analytic premises. The argument here supplies the component,
  insertion and summation inputs and applies those estimates to their two
  distinct correction arrays.

  <subparagraph|The hard precursor.> The thin-component cluster fees have
  rooted price <math|P<rsub|0>/<around|(|1-\<rho\>|)>> at exponent
  <math|a+e>, and the packet-containing components have rooted price
  <math|P<rsub|X>*e<rsup|-M>> there. A precursor label consists of a
  connected collection of the latter components decorated by regular cluster
  occurrences, with their actual positive integer multiplicities. Its price
  is the product of the component prices and the regular cluster fees divided
  by the occurrence factorials. The original compatibility and eligibility
  tests are retained in the physical array. For estimating its price, they
  may be dropped.

  Here is the multiplicity estimate with the present constants. On the
  alphabet of packet-containing components and regular cluster labels, write
  <math|w<rsub|i>> for these prices, and give a component the mark
  <math|m<rsub|i>=M> and a regular cluster the mark zero. Inflate to
  <math|v<rsub|i>=2*w<rsub|i>*e<rsup|m<rsub|i>>>. The rooted price of
  <math|2*v<rsub|i>> at exponent <math|a+e> is at most

  <\equation*>
    4*P<rsub|X>+<frac|4*P<rsub|0>|1-\<rho\>>\<leqslant\>4*P<rsub|X>+8*P<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>V.
  </equation*>

  By <eqref|eq:selected-two-tree-smallness>, <math|V\<leqslant\>1/2> and the
  distinct connected-set tree ratio for these prices, with reserve <math|e>,
  is at most <math|1/2>. At a site in the nonempty support of <math|i> the
  individual rooted summand gives <math|v<rsub|i><rprime|'>=v<rsub|i>*e<rsup|a*c<rsub|B><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>\<leqslant\>1/2>.
  Thus, for each fixed distinct set of labels <math|S>,

  <\equation*>
    <big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S><big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>1><frac|<around|(|v<rsub|i><rprime|'>|)><rsup|n>|n!>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S>2*v<rsub|i><rprime|'>.
  </equation*>

  The rooted connected-set sum is therefore at most <math|2*V>. Every actual
  precursor label contains a packet-containing component, so its inflated
  product has gained at least <math|e<rsup|M>>. It has also gained
  <math|2<rsup|m>> if it has at least <math|m> occurrences. Extracting these
  factors proves the rooted precursor price

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:selected-precursor-hard>2<rsup|-m>*e<rsup|-M>*<space|0.17em>2*V<space|1em><text|at
    support exponent >a.
  </equation>

  This is the price of the actual decorated labels, including repeated
  regular clusters, not a bound for a single decoration. The exact flattening
  identity of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:forced-exclusion> applies:
  connected components and flattening are inverse, and preserve the sign,
  inventory and both kinds of factorial. It identifies the priced array with
  the precursor gas. Its fixed flag gates are bounded by one and hence
  preserve every payment majorant. Since <math|V\<leqslant\>1/2>, the case
  <math|m=0> is at most <math|e<rsup|-M>=e<rsup|-\<mu\>*P<rsup|2>/4>>, the
  hard input of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-response-payments>.

  The same construction applies to the finite menu: replace each component
  price by the summed ticket price from Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-component-fibers>.
  The score-product formula in Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-lattice-rg-usable>
  bounds each finite product by the product of its payment prices. Division
  by the two occurrence factorials and the nonnegative summation above
  therefore give the same rooted payment for all its switching scores. This
  supplies the paid precursor, rather than merely a bound on each switched
  value.

  <subparagraph|The scalar insertion interval.> The inserted and uninserted
  component values, say <math|b<rsub|C>> and <math|a<rsub|C>>, obey
  <math|<around|\||a<rsub|C>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|C>> and
  <math|<around|\||b<rsub|C>|\|>\<leqslant\>D<rsub|\<ast\>>*B<rsub|C>>, with
  the original and single-replacement product prices respectively. These are
  the value cases of the residual-body and marked estimates. For every
  <math|<around|\||\<theta\>|\|>*D<rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>1>, the affine
  term <math|a<rsub|C>+\<theta\>*b<rsub|C>> has the joint
  value-and-derivative bound <math|A<rsub|C>+2*B<rsub|C>> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:leaf-expansion>. The witnesses are
  <math|<around|(|A<rsub|C>+B<rsub|C>,B<rsub|C>|)>>, so the sum of the two
  witnesses is exactly this price. The product rule for that joint norm
  incurs no arity factor. Thus the thin cluster series and the decorated
  precursor series have common summable majorants on the open interval
  <math|<around|(|-D<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|-1>,D<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|-1>|)>> if
  <math|D<rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0>, and on all of <math|\<bbb-R\>> if
  <math|D<rsub|\<ast\>>=0>. In the latter case <math|b<rsub|C>=0> for every
  <math|C>. The budgets for <math|A<rsub|C>+2*B<rsub|C>> are bounded by
  <math|O<rsub|k>+2*U<rsub|k>> and the corresponding marked-seed price
  already included in <eqref|eq:selected-thin-pledges>. Nonempty supports
  turn their rooted bounds into absolute sums on each finite torus.
  Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:leaf-differentiate-sum> now justifies
  differentiation of those scalar series. This interval argument is separate
  from the coarse-field jet argument at a healthy base point above. The
  latter supplies the regular local jet bounds for the resulting insertion;
  it is not used as a substitute for uniformity on the scalar interval.

  <subparagraph|The first soft correction.> Write
  <math|D<rsub|o><around|(|U|)>> for the discrepancy at anchor <math|o> in
  <eqref|eq:selected-offer-exact-split>. This is the difference between the
  allocated finite short density, its constant value and its folded local
  replacement, on exactly the physical and auxiliary data of the stored
  return. That section proves its locality on the doubled neighbourhood
  <math|N<rsub|2*z><around|(|o|)>> and

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|o><around|(|U|)>=0<space|1em><text|if
    >N<rsub|2*z><around|(|o|)><text| is healthy>,<space|2em><around|\||D<rsub|o><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>q,
  </equation*>

  where

  <\equation*>
    q=2*h<rsub|<math-up|val>>*<around*|(|1+<big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|4><around|\||a<rsub|i>|\|><space|0.17em><around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|i>|\|>|)>,<space|2em>h<rsub|<math-up|val>>=2<math-up|ballCost><around|(|r<rsub|0>|)>*C<rsub|<math-up|turn>>*h<rsup|-7/16>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|r<rsub|0>> is the local panel radius, not the negative rate
  <math|r>. Let <math|M<rsub|2>> be the fixed cover bound of
  <math|N<rsub|2*z><around|(|o|)>> at radius <math|B>. At a given site at
  most <math|<math-up|ballCost><around|(|2*z|)>> anchors contribute. The
  ordinary correction array <math|-D<rsub|o>>, including its normalized
  symmetry copies, consequently has soft price

  <\equation*>
    b<rsub|0>=<math-up|ballCost><around|(|2*z|)>*q*e<rsup|<around|(|b<rsub|c>+2|)>*M<rsub|2>>.
  </equation*>

  The raised correction uses the same discrepancy and radius and adds one
  unit of support exponent, so its price is

  <\equation*>
    b<rsub|+>=e<rsup|M<rsub|2>>*b<rsub|0>.
  </equation*>

  This equality follows termwise from the cover bound; if the actual cover is
  smaller, it is an inequality in the favourable direction. The displayed
  expressions are the chosen uniform allowances. All factors except
  <math|h<rsup|-7/16>> have upper exponent zero, while the exponential cover
  factors are fixed stage constants. Let <math|C<rsub|<math-up|cov>>=e<rsup|\<chi\><rsub|0>>>
  be the cover allowance already fixed in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-response-payments>. The entry threshold
  can be chosen so that

  <\equation*>
    b<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>min <around|(|1,C<rsub|<math-up|cov>>*e<rsup|-M<rsub|2>>|)>*h<rsup|-1/8>,<space|2em>b<rsub|+>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|cov>>*h<rsup|-1/8>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed <math|b<rsub|0>*h<rsup|1/8>> tends to zero, and both constants in
  the minimum are strictly positive and fixed before the threshold. This
  explicitly matches the first payment even if the convenient cover bound
  <math|M<rsub|2>> differs from the purchased ticket <math|\<chi\><rsub|0>>.
  The vanishing on healthy doubled supports is exactly the eligibility
  condition needed to absorb this logarithmic array. The signs follow from
  <eqref|eq:selected-offer-exact-split>: it is <math|-D<rsub|o>> that is
  absorbed. These are the ordinary and raised precursor soft prices; neither
  is the price of the final reset correction.

  <subparagraph|The final local correction.> At the base budget point
  <math|p> retain the actual path, clip <math|S>, width <math|\<sigma\>>,
  path count <math|C>, reset <math|\<alpha\>> and regular allowance <math|H>.
  The reset decomposition of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-reset-reorganization>
  gives two disjoint logarithmic families. The first is the local
  Taylor-subtraction discrepancy, whose amplitude is bounded by the
  reset-loss constant times the original regular majorant. The second is the
  low-path mask discrepancy, bounded by the boundary-hit coefficient times
  its original absolute path coefficient and relaxation weight. Both vanish
  on their assigned healthy supports, as proved by the reset identities. They
  are measurable, gauge invariant and local on those supports. Thus their
  nonnegative pointwise majorants, not only the sums of their values, are
  available for the payment.

  For the ordinary row use the ordinary footprint payments of that section,
  giving

  <\equation*>
    f<rsub|0>=<math-up|softNeed><around|(|p,S,\<sigma\>,C,\<alpha\>,H|)>.
  </equation*>

  For the raised row repeat the support estimate at exponent
  <math|p.<math-up|supportExponent>+2>. The reset halo has population radius
  <math|4*B+2> and cover increment <math|81>; use width reserve <math|1/2>.
  The low-path support has population radius <math|B>, its original length
  charge and cover increment <math|C<rsub|<math-up|low>>>; use width reserve
  <math|\<sigma\>/8>. The path-width hypothesis implies
  <math|<around|(|p.<math-up|supportExponent>+2|)>/B\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>/2>,
  leaving this positive reserve in the original path sum. Applying the
  footprint sum separately to the two families gives exactly

  <\equation*>
    f<rsub|+>=F<around|(|H|)>+N<around|(|p,\<alpha\>,H|)>*Q*<around|(|p,S,\<sigma\>,C|)>
  </equation*>

  with <math|F,Q,N> as in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-multi-raised-demand>. The
  scalar boundary-hit bound <math|N> follows from the same reset coefficient
  estimate and regular allowance <math|H>; it is uniform for
  <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|j>>. This is a rooted
  bound on the actual reset and path arrays. Reindexing into the final
  dictionary is injective, with zero values at unused addresses, and
  preserves the bound. It is attached to the raised budget, whose radius is
  still <math|B>, although the displayed formula is evaluated at the base
  point <math|p>.

  Finally apply the first numerical payment to
  <eqref|eq:selected-precursor-hard> and <math|b<rsub|0>> or
  <math|b<rsub|+>>, respectively. The compatible-family identity and payment
  argument in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:soft-log-fusion> produce hard
  price <math|2*e<rsup|-\<mu\>*P<rsup|2>/8>>. Widening the dictionary sends
  old labels injectively to their unchanged supports and assigns zero
  elsewhere; it preserves both the scores and their majorants. Apply the
  second payment to that hard price and the corresponding final soft array,
  <math|f<rsub|0>> or <math|f<rsub|+>>. The result is the price
  <math|2*e<rsup|-\<mu\>*P<rsup|2>/16>> on that row's actual output, with the
  two different support exponents. No output or rate from the ordinary row is
  substituted for the raised one. All component sums, local corrections and
  both payments use the same selected representation, including at index
  zero. The local funding, jet, short-panel and reset hypotheses explicitly
  retained above remain necessary; the argument does not establish them
  merely by increasing a threshold.

  <subsubsection|Retention on the public run><label|sec:selected-retention-public>

  We now remove the two hypotheses of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-centered-retention>
  that concern the stored doors. The first, preservation of the reserved
  slots, is Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-reserved-slots>. The second
  asks for the arrival, ordinary and raised-response receipts of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:finite-menu-history-handoff> on the retained
  representations and door. Its content, made explicit in
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-rg-multisource> and
  <reference|sec:finite-menu-history-handoff>, consists of six items:

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(A1)>the shipment of every admitted offer into rooted majorants on
    the component alphabet of the stored door;

    <item*|(A2)>the vertex input and tree smallness of
    Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-lattice-rg-usable>, with budgets
    <math|P<rsub|0>,P<rsub|1>>, ratio <math|\<rho\>\<leqslant\>1/2>, marking
    exponent <math|E<rsub|0>> and total pledge <math|V<rsub|Q>> obeying
    <eqref|eq:menu-total-pledge>, at the earned exponent;

    <item*|(A3)>the hard precursor of Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-lattice-rg-crowd>,
    of price <math|2*V<rsub|Q>*e<rsup|-E<rsub|0>>>;

    <item*|(A4)>the soft precursor prices of the ordinary and raised paths,
    at most <math|y<rsup|-1/8>> and <math|e<rsup|\<chi\><rsub|0>>*y<rsup|-1/8>>,
    together with the two final soft demands <math|f> and <math|f<rsub|+>> as
    rooted bounds on the actual reset and path arrays;

    <item*|(A5)>the two rate payments <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-multi-capital> and
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-multi-rate>, for the ordinary and the raised path;

    <item*|(A6)>the tuning law, that is, differentiability of the response in
    the menu parameter with summable value-and-derivative majorants.
  </enumerate>

  Here <math|y=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1>> is the fine coupling of the
  stored step.

  The proofs of these items in Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-menu-alphabet>\U<reference|sec:selected-precursor-vapor>
  use local analytic estimates on the stored step. We name them explicitly.
  On each stored step <math|i\<less\>N>, with its stored tube, incoming
  representation <math|R<rsub|N,p,i+1>> and local return:

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(L1)>the funded path, shell, guide and local precision bounds of
    Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-guides>, <reference|sec:sector-gram>,
    <reference|sec:local-covariance> and <reference|sec:local-mean>;

    <item*|(L2)>the seed-energy, chart, annulus and normalization estimates
    entering the packet value price of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:packet-estimates>;

    <item*|(L3)>the differentiated local estimates of
    Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:activity-jet-assembly> and
    <reference|sec:compensated-variation>, including the differentiated
    precision and compensation bounds;

    <item*|(L4)>the finite short-panel localization and folding estimates of
    Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-nonlinear-offering> and
    <reference|sec:five-period-localization>, which give
    <eqref|eq:selected-offer-exact-split> and its discrepancy bound;

    <item*|(L5)>the reset decomposition, its loss and boundary-hit estimates,
    and the path footprint sums of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-reset-reorganization>.
  </enumerate>

  These are not new hypotheses on the selected history. They are the local
  nonlinear estimates whose thresholds the common delays of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history> pay before the scale
  realization: the sector integrals with their Gaussian prefactors and shell
  bounds (L1), the marked and unmarked local bounds (L2), (L3), the
  small-field localization and parameter comparison (L4), and the scalar
  reset (L5). Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history> applies
  them to the same tube, paths, budget points and local return to construct
  the selected history itself, and the uniform derivative bank of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-observable-purchases> is the one used
  in (L3). They are needed here at exactly the same data, as the next proof
  shows.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Paid retention on the public run><label|thm:selected-retention-public>Keep
    the selected history of this section, with the common purchases of
    Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history> and
    <reference|sec:common-observable-purchases> and the local analytic
    estimates (L1)\U(L5) which those purchases pay. Include in the common
    delays the numerical requests of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-warning-inputs>
    and <reference|lem:selected-response-payments>,
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-value-prices>, the two
    smallness conditions <eqref|eq:selected-two-tree-smallness>, the
    thresholds of Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-inserted-derivative>
    and <reference|sec:selected-precursor-vapor>, and the tariff and geyser
    thresholds named in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-centered-retention>.
    Make the fixed requests of those results with earned exponent
    <math|a<rsub|i>=\<alpha\><rsub|i>+3> at target level <math|i>, any fixed
    reserve <math|e\<gtr\>0>, component exponent <math|E=a<rsub|i>+2*e>, a
    rate <math|r\<in\><around|(|-1/2,0|)>> and a fixed derivative order
    <math|j\<geqslant\>1>.

    Then for every cutoff <math|N>, period <math|p> and step
    <math|0\<leqslant\>i\<less\>N>, the stored door carries the receipts
    (A1)\U(A6) on the retained representations <math|R<rsub|N,p,i+1>>,
    <math|R<rsub|N,p,i>>. Consequently every conclusion of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-centered-retention> holds on
    the selected history without its arrival, raised-response and
    slot-preservation hypotheses: the paid response of the stored door to
    every admitted finite priced offer, its ordinary and flagged families
    with rooted prices <math|1> and <math|w<rsub|i>=2*e<rsup|-\<mu\>*P<rsup|2>/16>>
    at exponents <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i>+1/2> and <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i>+1>,
    the quiet and singleton identities, the exact push, soundness and tuning,
    retention of the upper contact addresses, the displayed inequalities for
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|i>,T<rsub|i>,c<rsub|i>>, and the terminal endowment
    at every <math|N\<geqslant\>0>. All thresholds are fixed before the
    history is selected and are uniform in <math|N>, <math|p>, the finite
    menu, the forest, tags and sectors, and the retained member of the
    shooting interval.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    <em|Order of the choices.> Every request listed in the theorem is a fixed
    constant chosen with the profiles and before the block lengths, or a
    lower threshold for the coupling at a fixed pair of stages. The cluster
    reserve <math|e> is distinct from the length and polynomial reserve
    <math|\<varepsilon\>> of Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-component-variants>.
    Choose <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0> with <math|4*\<varepsilon\>>
    strictly below the incoming support exponent and <math|8*\<varepsilon\>>
    strictly below both adjacent path widths, and choose the component tree
    reserve <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>. These choices are possible because
    those exponents and widths are positive, and they precede <math|L>. The
    cover conversions at the component exponent <math|E> and head exponent
    <math|E+2*\<varepsilon\>+\<delta\>> are those of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:inherited-activity-prices>, made before
    <math|L> as in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-value-prices>.
    Each threshold result above holds on a tail of couplings. At a stage
    there are finitely many permitted target stages. As in the proofs of
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-warning-inputs> and
    <reference|lem:selected-response-payments>, replace the current delay at
    each stage by its maximum with these thresholds and then by its running
    maximum. This is one more step of the successive enlargement in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history>, before the scale
    realization. It preserves every earlier purchase. The selected history is
    constructed afterwards from these delays, so all the threshold
    conclusions hold at <math|x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>> for every stored
    step, including <math|i=0>. Nothing is chosen after a finite menu, forest
    or tag is fixed.

    <em|The local estimates are those of the stored step.> By
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-value-input> the representation at
    <math|i+1> is an admissible input of the local estimates: its path and
    high clip are those of the stored tube, its threshold, radius, coupling
    band and activity allowances are the purchased ones, and its loop
    allowance is at most <math|2*P>. By Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-tube-compatibility>
    the stored tube has adjacent quadratic compatibility. The local return of
    the stored step is the return constructed in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history> on that tube at the
    retained interval point, by the stored identities recalled in the proof
    of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-centered-retention>. Hence
    (L1)\U(L5) are applied to the same input, tube, return and budget points
    at which they were purchased. No other representation, horizon or return
    appears in the following steps.

    <em|(A1).> The offer price is at most
    <math|y<rsub|i>=x<rsub|i+1><rsup|-1>> at exponent
    <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i+1>-4>, with fine sites in the crests and parent
    occupancy at most <math|r<rsub|i>>. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-menu-alphabet>
    applies to the stored door's mixed alphabet. Its extra site price
    <eqref|eq:selected-menu-extra-hit> is the shipment conversion of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-volant-supplier>, which uses only
    the offer, the occupancy bound, the birth geometry and the tariff. Its
    common mixed-body hypothesis is Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-residual-body>,
    whose warning inputs are Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-warning-inputs>
    under (L1). This gives the menu-augmented alphabet and the ticket product
    <eqref|eq:selected-menu-ticket-product> on the actual labels.

    <em|(A2).> Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-value-prices>, under
    (L1) and (L2), gives the common value prices and the packet price.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-component-fibers> sums the actual
    replacement fibers, and <eqref|eq:selected-thin-pledges> defines the
    pledges <math|P<rsub|0>> and <math|P<rsub|X>>. With <math|a=a<rsub|i>>
    and the reserve <math|e>, the actual thin weights obey the vertex
    inequality of Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-lattice-rg-usable>
    with budget <math|P<rsub|0>> at exponent <math|a+2*e\<leqslant\>E>, and
    <eqref|eq:selected-two-tree-smallness> gives its tree condition with
    <math|\<rho\>=h<rsup|r/2>\<leqslant\>1/2>. That section admits any ratio
    <math|\<rho\>\<less\>1> satisfying these two inequalities, and
    <math|h=\<beta\><rsub|F>> is its fine coupling; we use this one. Set

    <\equation*>
      E<rsub|0>=M=\<mu\>*P<rsup|2>/4,<space|2em>E<rprime|'>=P<rsub|X>,<space|2em>P<rsub|1>=P<rsub|X>*e<rsup|-M>,<space|2em>V<rsub|Q>=V.
    </equation*>

    The packet-containing budget of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-component-fibers>
    is <math|P<rsub|1>>. Since <math|\<rho\>\<leqslant\>1/2>,

    <\equation*>
      4*e<rsup|E<rsub|0>>*P<rsub|1>+<frac|4*P<rsub|0>|1-\<rho\>>\<leqslant\>4*P<rsub|X>+8*P<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>10*<around|(|P<rsub|0>+P<rsub|X>|)>=V\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>,
    </equation*>

    which is <eqref|eq:menu-total-pledge>. Both <math|P<rsub|0>> and
    <math|P<rsub|X>> have upper exponent <math|r\<less\>0>, as that section
    requires. By <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-multi-lucent> the logarithmic response
    has rooted price <math|2*P<rsub|0>/<around|(|1-\<rho\>|)>\<leqslant\>4*P<rsub|0>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>>
    at exponent <math|a+e>. Since <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i>+1/2\<leqslant\>a+e>,
    it has rooted price at most one at exponent <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i>+1/2>,
    which is the ordinary output budget of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-centered-retention>.

    <em|(A3).> The decorated precursor bound
    <eqref|eq:selected-precursor-hard> with <math|m=0> is
    <math|2*V*e<rsup|-M>=2*V<rsub|Q>*e<rsup|-E<rsub|0>>>. By the flattening
    identity cited there it is the price of the actual precursor gas. Since
    <math|V\<leqslant\>1/2>, it is at most
    <math|e<rsup|-\<mu\>*P<rsup|2>/4>>, the hard input of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-response-payments>. The same holds for
    the finite-menu version, with the summed ticket prices.

    <em|(A4).> By Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-precursor-vapor>,
    under (L4), the first soft correction arrays have prices <math|b<rsub|0>>
    and <math|b<rsub|+>>, with <math|b<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>h<rsup|-1/8>> and
    <math|b<rsub|+>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|cov>>*h<rsup|-1/8>>, where
    <math|C<rsub|<math-up|cov>>=e<rsup|\<chi\><rsub|0>>>. Under (L5) the
    final correction arrays have rooted prices <math|f<rsub|0>> and
    <math|f<rsub|+>>, the demands <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-multi-softneed> and
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-multi-raised-demand> at the base budget point, the
    latter attached to the raised budget. These are the four soft receipts of
    Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-lattice-rg-crowd>.

    <em|(A5).> Apply Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-response-payments>
    with <math|a=2*V<rsub|Q>*e<rsup|-E<rsub|0>>>, and with <math|b=b<rsub|0>>
    on the ordinary path and <math|b=b<rsub|+>> on the raised path. Its first
    conclusion <eqref|eq:selected-first-response-payment> and the hard/soft
    fusion of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:soft-log-fusion> give the hard
    price <math|H<rsub|1>> after widening. Its second conclusion
    <eqref|eq:selected-second-response-payment>, with
    <math|f<rsub|\<epsilon\>>=f<rsub|0>> or <math|f<rsub|+>>, gives the
    capital and quote inequalities <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-multi-capital> and
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-multi-rate> with
    <math|\<kappa\><rsub|2>=\<mu\>*P<rsup|2>/16>, and the output price
    <math|H<rsub|2>=2*e<rsup|-\<mu\>*P<rsup|2>/16>>. The raised path supplies
    the flagged ledger at exponent <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i>+1> with this
    price, as stated at the end of Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-lattice-rg-crowd>.
    This is the flagged allowance <math|w<rsub|i>>. The ordinary path
    supplies the gas payment of the usable certificate. Neither path is
    deduced from the other.

    <em|(A6).> Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-inserted-derivative>,
    under (L3), gives for every inserted original label a common integrable
    majorant of the marked integrand and its jets through order <math|j> at a
    healthy base point, with the rooted bound
    <eqref|eq:selected-marked-root>. Its scalar-interval paragraph in
    Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-precursor-vapor> gives
    summable joint value-and-derivative majorants for the thin cluster and
    decorated precursor series on the interval
    <math|<around|\||\<theta\>|\|>*D<rsub|\<ast\>>\<less\>1>, and
    <eqref|eq:leaf-differentiate-sum> justifies termwise differentiation.
    These are the derivative data which fix the tuning law in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:finite-menu-history-handoff>. The
    polynomial order <math|K+j> used there is the one included in
    <math|P<rsub|0>>, so the differentiated terms are paid by the same
    pledge.

    <em|Conclusion.> The stored door is the one-step transport at the
    retained interval point, and the receipts (A1)\U(A6) concern that very
    door and the pair <math|R<rsub|N,p,i+1>>, <math|R<rsub|N,p,i>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-reserved-slots> supplies the
    slot-preservation hypothesis at every nonterminal entry, including the
    level-<math|m> dictionaries and flag predicates. Thus both hypotheses of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-centered-retention> that
    concern the stored doors hold. Its remaining hypotheses are the fixed
    profile and the delays, which include the tariff and geyser thresholds by
    the first step. Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-centered-retention>
    therefore gives all the asserted conclusions. Its terminal endowment uses
    only the schedule.

    <em|Index zero and uniformity.> Every lemma used above requires only
    <math|i\<less\>N>, so the direct stored step at <math|i=0> is included;
    no default door is used. The per-campaign service projection restricts
    the same conclusions to <math|1\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>i\<less\>N>. The
    value prices, component sums and warning prices are uniform in the
    forest, tags and sectors, and the forest and tag averages are probability
    averages. Selecting a member of the shooting interval keeps the
    representations, paths and budget points on which all the bounds were
    proved.
  </proof>

  The theorem does not prove the local estimates (L1)\U(L5) themselves. They
  are the incoming local interface of the whole selected history and are
  proved, or recorded as inputs, in their own sections. It also does not
  supply the auxiliary-history transition hypothesis used for the fixed-level
  limits at the beginning of this section, which concerns different
  histories. The consumers in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:finite-menu-history-handoff>
  still prepare their own finite lists and prove their slice integrability;
  the theorem supplies the paid response to any such list which is an
  admitted offer.

  <subsubsection|The service at every reference level>

  Fix a grade <math|d>, a period <math|p>, a cutoff <math|N>, and
  <math|t<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>N>; in particular
  <math|k\<geqslant\>1>. We verify the inputs of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-selected-run-service> for this family.

  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:selected-retention-public> supplies the
  adjacent retention and terminal endowment on the selected replay, by
  removing the door hypotheses of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-centered-retention>.
  Separately, Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:terminal-marked-voice>
  constructs the marked density and its terminal forced-profile identity on
  the startup representation when the reserved addresses, lawfulness, sound
  section and paid entrance fee are available. The numerical endowment does
  not depend on that label construction.

  The room and bank constants are those chosen for the local coefficient
  maps. The scale inequalities in Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-geyser-scale-estimates>
  give their common marker prices

  <\equation*>
    \<zeta\><rsub|d,m>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m><rsup|-<around|(|deg
    F<rsub|d>+4|)>>,<space|2em>\<phi\><rsub|m>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>\<delta\><rsub|m>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1><rsup|-2>,<space|2em>\<eta\><rsub|m>=<tfrac|1|4><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m><rsup|-1/2>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|F<rsub|d>> is the finite coefficient frame. Their strict
  positivity, local coefficient bounds, adjacent-price growth and support
  inequalities are proved there. Since <math|d\<leqslant\>J<around|(|k|)>\<leqslant\>J<around|(|m|)>>,
  the finite-grade inequalities purchased at stage <math|J<around|(|m|)>>
  apply at every <math|m\<geqslant\>k>. The occupancy bound at level <math|m>
  is <math|N<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>>, which is nondecreasing because both
  maps are nondecreasing.

  The same scale estimates give

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>8,<space|2em>\<delta\><rsub|m>*\<phi\><rsub|m>*e<rsup|B<rsub|m>/128>\<geqslant\>1,<space|2em>B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|m>*B<rsub|m><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  The stain/contact bound pays the simultaneous forced support requirement.
  These inequalities continue to hold after restriction to any reference
  level <math|k>; no constant is selected from <math|N>. For the
  local-section norm use its fixed camera constant <math|C<rsub|d>>. The
  fitted stencil has cover at most one and its values are bounded by
  <math|C<rsub|d>>, uniformly in the cutoff and period. Its paid coefficient
  price is precisely the bank price in the scale estimates. The cover bound
  just stated is the footprint-cover clause of the height record,
  item<nbsp>(iii) of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-selected-run-service>;
  the support-size inequalities of its item<nbsp>(ii) are among the scale
  estimates displayed above. Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-site-geometry>
  is not used for either: it supplies the occupancy, migration and separation
  of the label sites, which enter only in the moment bound below.

  The backward transfer data supply the remaining finite transfer record. Its
  coefficient and activity bounds are the two separate ledgers in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-freight-producer>; its adjacent
  identities use the selected maps, and its terminal forced-profile identity
  is the singleton hook of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-terminal-voice>,
  retained by Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-music>. The third
  identity in <eqref|eq:history-three-identities> instead compares adjacent
  profiles; it does not supply the terminal hook. The latter is essential
  because the scalar integral identity alone would not give the terminal
  marked polynomial. All these conclusions hold for every
  <math|<around|(|d,k,N,p|)>> in the stated range, with the already fixed
  reference levels. These are the remaining service fields under the stated
  local purchases, finite backward construction and selected-replay
  hypotheses.

  We may therefore apply Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-selected-run-service>
  and the terminal birth calculation. At a reference level <math|k>, a finite
  label list with paid weights and separated origins has normalized moment
  bound

  <\equation*>
    exp <around|{|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|k>|\|>*\<cal-T\><rsub|k>+6<around|\||\<cal-V\>|\|>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|m\<leqslant\>k> now denote the root level opened at <math|k>, as
  in Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-site-geometry>, rather than a
  running level. Take one label at each site of <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|m>>, so
  that <math|M=<around|\||\<cal-V\>|\|>=<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|m>|\|>>,
  placed by the aligned embeddings of Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-site-geometry>.
  With <math|n<rsub|j>> the side of the periodic lattice
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|j>> and <math|q<rsub|k>=a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>>, that
  section records <math|n<rsub|k>=q<rsub|k>*n<rsub|m>>; since
  <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|j>|\|>=n<rsub|j><rsup|4>>, this gives the
  site identity <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|k>|\|>=M*<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4>>.
  The normalized <math|M>th root is consequently bounded by

  <\equation*>
    exp <around|{|<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4>*\<cal-T\><rsub|k>+6|}>/w.
  </equation*>

  This is the centered estimate for the same family, simultaneously at all
  suitable periods. The fixed-level convergence just retained and the removal
  of higher marker terms in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:fullmesh-correlation-cauchy>
  then give convergence along the full mesh sequence at every fixed suitable
  period. The finite-difference bounds of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-fullmesh-fixedbounds> apply to the same
  transfer data, so its limiting correlations have the smoothness proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-smooth-limits>.

  The further calibration of an arbitrary marked section and the rank and
  ultraviolet comparisons must still be made on this family. They will use
  the same reference levels and transfers; the centered construction above is
  already fixed before those comparisons or a volume subsequence are chosen.

  <subparagraph|Relation to the paid selected-history record.>
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-marked-portfolio-calibration>
  consumes a record (R1)\U(R7) on the same family and the same transition
  records. Its parameters are those of this section: the stage function
  <math|W> is the purchased worth, <math|u<rsub|i>=\<eta\><rsub|i>>,
  <math|\<phi\><rsub|i>>, <math|\<delta\><rsub|i>> and
  <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,i>> are the marker prices displayed above,
  <math|r<rsub|i>=N<rsub|J*<around|(|i+1|)>>>, the doors are the stored
  transition records, and <math|\<psi\><rsub|i>> is the common waybill
  <eqref|eq:history-common-waybill> at level <math|i+1>. With these
  parameters, the present section assembles the following items, each from
  the inputs named in its place above. The room and bank inequalities (R1)
  are those of the purchased budget. The scale bounds (R2) are the positivity
  of the marker prices and the three displayed inequalities. The monotonicity
  in (R3) is that of the occupancy bound.
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-centered-retention> gives (R4)
  under its stated hypotheses. Of (R5), this section supplies
  <math|\<phi\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1>, <math|\<psi\><rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>,
  the second, third and fourth displayed inequalities, the bound
  <math|X=C<rsub|d>>, the cover number one, and the terminal inequality for
  <math|u<rsub|N>>. The backward transfer data, with their door-by-door
  comparison at price <math|\<psi\><rsub|i>> and their terminal
  forced-profile identity, are (R6). Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-terminal-voice>
  below identifies the terminal coefficient family of (R6) on the stored top
  representation, with its singleton hook.
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-music> attaches the adjacent
  ledgers and finite-wing comparisons to one transfer sequence on that
  coefficient family; its one-step input is proved in
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-reprise>. Three items are not
  part of the centered service and are not derived in this section:
  <math|r<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1> in (R3); the positivity
  <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,i>\<gtr\>0> and the first inequality of (R5), which
  is <eqref|eq:ex-marked-height-jump> for the prices above; and the paid
  one-step input (R7). They belong to the stronger form of the funded
  continuation described in the proof of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-original-history-attachment>:
  <math|r<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1> is the prescribed lower bound of the
  occupancy schedule, positivity is that of <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>>,
  the first inequality of (R5) is the tail clause of the delay purchased in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-observable-purchases>, and (R7) is
  the local transition estimate on the stored doors. For (R7),
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-zero-coordinate-coupon> below
  proves the tuning inequality from the grade-zero floor of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-observable-purchases>, together with
  the signs. It reduces the zero-coordinate Coupon to the regular and forced
  insertion estimates, soundness of the stored door, and the countable Coupon
  merger. Under the common local-purchase hypotheses, the first two are
  attached to the stored door in the ticket verification of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-reprise>, and the last is
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-coupon-merger>. The proof of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-original-history-attachment> names this
  estimate but does not derive it. For none of the items has this section a
  self-contained proof: each rests on the cited producers.

  <subsubsection|Terminal coefficient families and transfer
  sequences><label|sec:selected-terminal-voice>

  A coefficient family assigns a marked coefficient to every label at one
  level. A transfer sequence collects these families across levels. The
  precise admissibility conditions are given below. This subsubsection treats
  item (R6) of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-marked-portfolio-calibration>,
  and the corresponding clause of the service record consumed in
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-selected-run-service>. Two things are
  needed. The top of the backward transfer data must be the marked
  coefficient family of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:terminal-marked-voice>
  on the <em|stored> top representation <math|R<rsub|N,p,N>>. Every adjacent
  comparison must then be attached to one and the same family of coefficient
  families, whose transfer entries are the stored transfer entries. The
  profile-difference identity in <eqref|eq:history-three-identities> concerns
  one transition and an arbitrary response <math|\<psi\>>. It does not
  identify the terminal forced profile. That identification is
  assertion<nbsp>(4) of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:terminal-marked-voice>,
  which we now place on the stored representation.

  Fix a grade <math|d>, a period <math|p> and a cutoff <math|N> with
  <math|t<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>N>. Then <math|d+3\<leqslant\>J<around|(|t<rsub|d>|)>\<leqslant\>J<around|(|N|)>>.
  Write <math|R<rsup|\<top\>>=R<rsub|N,p,N>> for the stored top
  representation, <math|S> for the grade-<math|d> local section with its
  fixed camera constant <math|C<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>1>, and
  <math|w<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|N>> for the forced allowance of level
  <math|N>. A <em|coefficient family> at level <math|m> is a complex-linear
  family <math|v\<mapsto\>V<around|(|v|)>> of coefficient fields on
  <math|R<rsub|N,p,m>>, together with a coefficient index
  <math|v\<mapsto\>x<around|(|v|)>>, which is a complex-linear isomorphism of
  <math|F>, and a charge <math|c<around|(|v|)>\<geqslant\>0>. We say that a
  coefficient family is <em|admissible> at <math|m> if all of the following
  hold for every <math|v>:

  <\itemize>
    <item>the lead, the regular part and the forced part of
    <math|V<around|(|v|)>> are complex-linear in <math|v>, and their supports
    do not depend on <math|v>;

    <item><math|x*<around|(|a<rsub|N><rsup|d>*e<rsub|0>|)>=a<rsub|m><rsup|d>*e<rsub|0>>,
    where <math|e<rsub|0>> is the vacuum coordinate;

    <item><math|<math-up|dens><space|0.17em>V<around|(|v|)>> is
    complex-linear in <math|v>, and for every signed lattice motion <math|g>,
    with or without complex conjugation, the density of
    <math|V<around|(|v|)>> transported by <math|g> is
    <math|<math-up|dens><space|0.17em>V*<around|(|g\<cdot\>v|)>>;

    <item><math|V<around|(|v|)>> has the terminal format of
    Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-zero-coordinate-coupon> at the
    origin of <math|R<rsub|N,p,m>>, and remainder bound
    <math|c<around|(|v|)>> at weight <math|<sqrt|w<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|m>>>;

    <item>its coordinate vector is <math|x<around|(|v|)>>;

    <item><math|c<around|(|v|)>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|m><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>x<around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
    where <math|s<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>1> is the column count at level
    <math|m>;

    <item><math|c*<around|(|u+v|)>\<leqslant\>c<around|(|u|)>+c<around|(|v|)>>;

    <item>the index commutes with the signed lattice motions.
  </itemize>

  A coefficient family satisfies the <em|profile covariance> at <math|m> if,
  for every motion <math|g>, transporting the forced profile of
  <math|V<around|(|v|)>> by <math|g> gives the forced profile of
  <math|V*<around|(|g\<cdot\>v|)>>. A <em|transfer sequence> is a choice of
  one coefficient family at every level <math|m\<leqslant\>N>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The stored terminal coefficient
    family><label|prop:selected-terminal-voice>Keep the selected history and
    the purchases of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-observable-purchases>.
    Let <math|d,p,N> be as above. Then the four hypotheses of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:terminal-marked-voice> hold for
    <math|R<rsup|\<top\>>>, its local section <math|S> with constant
    <math|C<rsub|d>>, and <math|w=w<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|N>>. The marked
    coefficient family <math|\<cal-V\><rsup|\<top\>><around|(|v|)>=\<cal-V\><around|(|v|)>>
    of that proposition is therefore defined on the stored top
    representation. Its index is the identity,
    <math|x<rsup|\<top\>><around|(|v|)>=v>, and its charge is
    <math|c<rsup|\<top\>><around|(|v|)>=<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>.
    For every <math|v\<in\>F>:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<cal-V\><rsup|\<top\>><around|(|v|)>> has lead <math|v>
      and zero regular part;

      <item>the density identity <eqref|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T5>
      holds, <math|<math-up|dens><space|0.17em>\<cal-V\><rsup|\<top\>><around|(|v|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|d>*R<rsup|\<top\>><space|0.17em><math-up|initial><rsub|N,p><around|(|v|)>>;

      <item>the forced profile of <math|\<cal-V\><rsup|\<top\>><around|(|v|)>>
      on the activities of <math|R<rsup|\<top\>>> equals the attachment of
      the singleton response <math|f<rsub|v>> on the footprint <math|Y> (the
      singleton hook);

      <item><math|\<cal-V\><rsup|\<top\>>> is admissible at <math|N> and
      satisfies the profile covariance at <math|N>.
    </enumerate>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    <em|The stored top representation.> By the choice of the history at the
    beginning of this section, <math|R<rsup|\<top\>>> is the member
    <math|F<rsub|\<beta\>>> of the exact Wilson startup family of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:wilson-startup-family>, at the coupling
    of the retained parameter. That proposition applies at level <math|N>
    under the purchased schedule, and <math|F<rsub|\<beta\>>> satisfies the
    retained-history conditions at level <math|N>.

    <em|Hypothesis (1).> The forced arrays of <math|F<rsub|\<beta\>>> have
    the locality, measurability and gauge invariance required of the
    dictionary. Among the retained-history conditions of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:wilson-startup-family> (second part) is
    the forced stage bound at the allowance <math|\<delta\><rsub|K>> of level
    <math|N>. That bound is the rooted support sum <math|sup<rsub|x>
    <big|sum><rsub|i:<space|0.17em>x\<in\>X<rsub|i>>e<rsup|E<math-up|cov><rsub|B><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>*k<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|K>>
    of a majorant family <math|k<rsub|i>\<geqslant\><around|\||K<rsub|i>|\|>>,
    where <math|E> and <math|B> are the support exponent and cover radius of
    level <math|N>. This is <eqref|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T3>
    with <math|w=\<delta\><rsub|K>>. Moreover <math|\<delta\><rsub|K>=exp
    <around|{|-M<rsup|1/5>|}>> with <math|M=exp <around|(|<around|(|log log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>|)><rsup|2>|)>>, which is the allowance
    <math|w<around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>|)>> of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:terminal-marked-construction>. This is the
    forced allowance <math|w<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|N>> of the history.

    <em|Hypothesis (2).> Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:wilson-startup-family>
    states that the fixed terminal dictionary contains, for every forced
    support <math|X> with nonempty inventory <math|I> and every natural
    number, a reserved forced label with support <math|X> and inventory
    <math|I>. It also states that the forced coefficient of
    <math|F<rsub|\<beta\>>> on every reserved label is zero, and that the
    additional predicate of such a label holds exactly when
    <math|X\<cap\><math-up|flags><around|(|U|)>=I>. Hence hypothesis<nbsp>(2)
    holds. Selecting one reserved label for each pair <math|<around|(|X,I|)>>
    gives an injective assignment of an address with tag
    <math|<around|(|X,I|)>>, eligible whenever
    <math|X\<cap\><math-up|flags><around|(|U|)>=I>. This is the Keeper of
    <math|R<rsup|\<top\>>>, and it is the property recorded in the nesting
    receipt of the run.

    <em|Hypothesis (3).> The grade-<math|d> local section is the fixed sound
    section of the frame. It is the section used in the selected root-test
    estimate of Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-public-service>, whose
    hypotheses include its soundness. Its axis values are bounded by the
    fixed camera constant <math|C<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>1> at every
    configuration, as recorded in the service at every reference level above.

    <em|Hypothesis (4).> Since <math|d\<leqslant\>J<around|(|N|)>>, the final
    sentence of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:terminal-marked-voice>
    applies at the admitted terminal level <math|N>. The fee thresholds are
    among the entrance delays of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-observable-purchases>,
    as stated in the third subsection of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:terminal-marked-construction>.

    <em|Conclusions.> Assertions (1)\U(3) are the definition of
    <math|\<cal-V\><around|(|v|)>> together with assertions (3) and (4) of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:terminal-marked-voice>. For (4), the
    format clauses are assertion<nbsp>(1) there together with the zero
    regular part. The origin lies in every footprint support
    <math|T<rsub|b>\<supseteq\>Y>. The remainder bound with
    <math|a<rsub|<math-up|r>>=0> and <math|a<rsub|<math-up|f>>=<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>
    at weight <math|<sqrt|w<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|N>>> is assertion<nbsp>(2)
    there. Indeed <math|<math-up|mass><around|(|T<rsub|b>,e|)>=M<around|(|T<rsub|b>|)>*e>,
    so the forced mass sum is at most <math|<sqrt|w<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|N>>*<space|0.17em>a<rsub|<math-up|f>>>.
    The cone bound is <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|N><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
    since <math|s<rsub|N>\<geqslant\>1>. Subadditivity is the triangle
    inequality for the core norm. Covariance of the index is linearity of the
    motion action. The index <math|x=<math-up|id>> is an isomorphism and
    satisfies <math|x*<around|(|a<rsub|N><rsup|d>*e<rsub|0>|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|d>*e<rsub|0>>.
    The lead, regular and forced entries are complex-linear in <math|v> by
    the first conclusion of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:terminal-marked-voice>.
    The regular part is zero, and the forced entry at the address
    <math|s<around|(|b|)>> has support <math|T<rsub|b>> for every <math|v>,
    so no support depends on <math|v>. Density linearity follows from
    <eqref|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T5>, and density
    covariance is assertion<nbsp>(5) there. Profile covariance at <math|N>
    follows from assertion<nbsp>(4) there: the singleton response satisfies
    <math|f<rsub|g\<cdot\>v>=g\<cdot\>f<rsub|v>>, and its attachment on
    <math|Y> is transported by the motion together with the footprint, as in
    the covariance paragraph of the second subsection of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:terminal-marked-construction>.
  </proof>

  For the adjacent steps we use the following one-step input. It is the
  quantitative content of a step of Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-freight-backward>
  on the stored door, in the form in which the finite-wing comparison is
  exported. For <math|m\<less\>N>, let <math|s<rsub|m+1>> be the column count
  at level <math|m+1>, <math|L<rsub|m>> the block side and
  <math|\<psi\><rsub|m>> the common waybill
  <eqref|eq:history-common-waybill>. A <em|Carol> across the stored door
  <math|D<rsub|m>> between coefficient families <math|V<rprime|'>> at
  <math|m+1> and <math|V> at <math|m> consists of two things. The first is a
  finite wing <math|W>. The second is, for every <math|v>, a Tale witness on
  <math|W> across <math|D<rsub|m>>, in the sense of
  Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-zero-coordinate-coupon>. This
  witness relates the upper input data of <math|V<rprime|'><around|(|v|)>> to
  the lower forced profile of <math|V<around|(|v|)>>:

  <\itemize>
    <item>the exponential entry and the forced profile on
    <math|R<rsub|N,p,m+1>>;

    <item>the vacuum (<math|e<rsub|0>>) coordinate of
    <math|x<rprime|'><around|(|v|)>>;

    <item>the physical factor <math|L<rsub|m><rsup|d>>;

    <item>input price <math|C<rsub|<math-up|orb>><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rprime|'><around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>*<space|0.17em><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|m>+2*s<rsub|m+1>|)>>
    and output price <math|c<around|(|v|)>>.
  </itemize>

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(RS)><em|One-step one-step transfer on the stored door.> Let
    <math|m> satisfy <math|t<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>m\<less\>N>. Suppose
    <math|V<rprime|'>> is a coefficient family at <math|m+1> that is
    admissible and satisfies the profile covariance there. Then there is a
    coefficient family <math|V> at <math|m> with four properties:

    <\itemize>
      <item><math|V> is admissible at <math|m> and satisfies the profile
      covariance at <math|m>;

      <item>for every <math|v>, <math|<math-up|dens><space|0.17em>V<around|(|v|)>>
      is the push of <math|<math-up|dens><space|0.17em>V<rprime|'><around|(|v|)>>
      through the stored transition of step <math|m>, as in
      Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-freight-backward>;

      <item>the coefficient indices and charges satisfy the paid adjacent
      cone and charge inequalities of Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-freight-producer>;

      <item>there is a Carol across <math|D<rsub|m>> between
      <math|V<rprime|'>> and <math|V>.
    </itemize>
  </enumerate>

  The input (RS) is what the paid ticket of the stored door is to supply.
  That ticket consists of:

  <\itemize>
    <item>a finite wing containing the Keeper keys of <math|R<rsub|N,p,m+1>>;

    <item>a heading;

    <item>the estimates of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:three-port-history>;

    <item>soundness of <math|D<rsub|m>>;

    <item><math|\<psi\><rsub|m>\<geqslant\>0>;

    <item><math|<sqrt|w<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|m+1>>\<leqslant\>1>;

    <item>the room and bank bounds.
  </itemize>

  We now verify this ticket and its summation on the selected replay.

  <paragraph|Summing the component comparisons>
  <with|the-label|13.10.13|<label|sec:selected-coupon-merger>>

  We first supply the countable summation step used in the finite-column
  construction. All the coefficients in this paragraph belong to one lawful
  representation, with the same anchor, background holonomy and local
  section, at one positive forced weight <math|\<kappa\>>. Each regular atom
  satisfies the prescribed regularity package and ordered-derivative bounds
  in the spatially weighted directions of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-splitting>; retaining an atom
  separately retains these properties. The mass convention is the one
  recalled in Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-zero-coordinate-coupon>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Countable addition of coefficients><label|lem:selected-coefficient-merger>Let
    <math|P<rsub|n>> be formatted coefficient fields with remainder bounds
    <math|r<rsub|n>>, where <math|<big|sum><rsub|n>r<rsub|n>\<less\>\<infty\>>,
    and suppose <math|<big|sum><rsub|n><around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|n>.*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
    There is a formatted coefficient <math|P> whose lead is
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|n>P<rsub|n>.*x>, whose regular atoms are the
    separate atoms of all the <math|P<rsub|n>>, and whose forced coefficient
    at an address <math|a> is <math|<big|sum><rsub|n>P<rsub|n>.<math-up|forced><rsub|a>>.
    It has remainder bound <math|<big|sum><rsub|n>r<rsub|n>>. Its exponential
    entry, forced profile and density are the respective sums of those of the
    <math|P<rsub|n>>. The assertion also holds for the turn-weighted
    remainder bounds used in a Coupon, with their same turn constant.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For each <math|n> retain its nonnegative regular and forced allocations
    <math|r<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|r>>,r<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|f>>>, with
    <math|r<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|r>>+r<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|f>>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|n>>,
    and their atom majorants <math|b<rsub|n,j>,d<rsub|n,a>>. Their mass sums
    obey

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|j><math-up|mass><around|(|X<rsub|n,j>,b<rsub|n,j>|)>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|r>>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|a><math-up|mass><around|(|S<rsub|a>,d<rsub|n,a>|)>\<leqslant\>\<kappa\>*r<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|f>>.
    </equation*>

    Enumerate the pairs <math|<around|(|n,j|)>> by one countable regular
    dictionary. Every regular function keeps its original support and all its
    format and regularity properties; no derivative of a newly summed regular
    function is required. At a forced address use the majorant
    <math|d<rsub|a>=<big|sum><rsub|n>d<rsub|n,a>>. Tonelli and linearity of
    mass on a fixed support give

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|n,j><math-up|mass><around|(|X<rsub|n,j>,b<rsub|n,j>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|n>r<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|r>>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|a><math-up|mass><around|(|S<rsub|a>,d<rsub|a>|)>\<leqslant\>\<kappa\>*<big|sum><rsub|n>r<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|f>>.
    </equation*>

    Since mass dominates the value majorant, all the forced sums converge
    absolutely and uniformly. They preserve measurability, gauge invariance
    and dependence on <math|S<rsub|a>>. Outside the eligibility set every
    summand vanishes; if the sum is nonzero, its common support contains the
    anchor. Thus the forced format is preserved. These two displayed bounds
    prove the remainder assertion. A turn-weighted bound is proved by
    retaining the separate allocations and summing
    <math|C<rsub|<math-up|turn>>*r<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|r>>+r<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|f>>>,
    rather than replacing the turn constant by a larger one.

    The finite-dimensional lead sum converges absolutely. The local section
    is linear and bounded in this norm on the finite-volume configuration
    space. Hence its placement commutes with the lead sum. The masked regular
    terms are bounded by <math|b<rsub|n,j>>, so their double series also
    converges absolutely. This proves the exponential-entry identity. For
    each tag the forced-profile identity follows by interchanging its address
    sum with the <math|n> sum, using <math|<big|sum><rsub|n,a>d<rsub|n,a>\<less\>\<infty\>>.

    For densities use the dressed-field bound
    <eqref|eq:selected-dressed-bound>, applied at the present level. It is
    uniform in the address and configuration. The lawful representation's
    exponential and evaluation have the finite-volume envelopes used in the
    same proof. Consequently the density series has a constant majorant times

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|n><around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|n>.*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>+<big|sum><rsub|n,j>b<rsub|n,j>+<big|sum><rsub|n,a>d<rsub|n,a>\<less\>\<infty\>.
    </equation*>

    The density formula in Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-zero-coordinate-coupon>
    can therefore be summed term by term. This proves its identity as well as
    the required measurable domination. These envelopes may depend on the
    finite volume; no such factor is added to the remainder prices.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Addition of the comparison witnesses><label|lem:selected-coupon-merger>Consider
    countably many Coupons on the same finite wing of the same sound stored
    door. Let their predicted leads be <math|v<rsub|n>>, their lead-error
    prices <math|c<rsub|n>>, their remainder prices <math|r<rsub|n>>, and
    their input prices <math|u<rsub|n>>. Suppose these three nonnegative
    price sequences and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|n>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>
    are summable. Suppose also that their exponential entries and forced
    profiles have the stated pointwise sums, their unit values are summable,
    and their input densities have a measurable dominated sum. Then they add
    to a Coupon for the summed data, with predicted lead
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|n>v<rsub|n>> and prices
    <math|<around|(|<big|sum><rsub|n>c<rsub|n>,<big|sum><rsub|n>r<rsub|n>,<big|sum><rsub|n>u<rsub|n>|)>>.
    In particular its Tale has input price <math|<big|sum><rsub|n>u<rsub|n>>
    and output price <math|<big|sum><rsub|n>r<rsub|n>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Choose the coefficient <math|P<rsub|n>> and the comparison witnesses of
    each Coupon. Its lead estimate gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|n>.*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|n>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>+c<rsub|n>/C<rsub|<math-up|orb>>.
    </equation*>

    Thus the actual leads are summable, a requirement separate from remainder
    summability. Lemma <reference|lem:selected-coefficient-merger> constructs
    the summed coefficient. The triangle inequality gives its lead-error
    price <math|<big|sum><rsub|n>c<rsub|n>> and its turn-weighted remainder
    price is <math|<big|sum><rsub|n>r<rsub|n>>. The selected transition has a
    bounded measurable kernel on this finite volume. Dominated convergence
    therefore passes the input density sum through each transition slice.
    Together with the density identity of the preceding lemma this proves the
    exact pushed-density identity, retaining the single physical factor
    <math|L<rsub|m><rsup|d>>.

    For completeness the two costs of the comparison witness must also be
    added. A Tale consists of a countable priced list of real elementary
    insertions with complex coefficients, all of whose keys lie in the
    specified wing, and a local response. The list represents the input
    exponential entry after subtracting its unit value and represents its
    forced profile. The output forced profile is the transported list,
    multiplied by <math|L<rsub|m><rsup|d>>, plus the hooked local response.
    Its input cost is the sum of the absolute coefficient-weighted insertion
    masses; its output cost is the reserved response mass. More explicitly,
    if the leaves are <math|M<rsub|j>>, their complex weights are
    <math|z<rsub|j>>, the response is <math|\<Psi\>>, the input entry and
    profile are <math|\<alpha\>,\<omega\>>, the output profile is
    <math|\<beta\>>, and the unit is <math|\<delta\>>, these identities are

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|j>z<rsub|j><math-up|prose><around|(|M<rsub|j>|)>=\<alpha\>-\<delta\>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|j>z<rsub|j><math-up|entry><around|(|M<rsub|j>|)>=\<omega\>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|j>L<rsub|m><rsup|d>*z<rsub|j><math-up|quoted><around|(|D<rsub|m>,M<rsub|j>|)>=\<beta\>-<math-up|hook><around|(|\<Psi\>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Here prose is the ordinary insertion on a regular leaf and zero on a
    forced leaf; entry is the insertion in its tagged forced coordinate and
    zero on a regular leaf. The quoted profile is the forced response of that
    leaf under the stored door. These are the three identities
    <eqref|eq:history-three-identities>, with the unit kept separately. Each
    response slot depends only on the links in its prescribed support, is
    gauge invariant and Borel measurable, and vanishes when that support is
    healthy. A nonzero slot has support containing the anchor. No derivative
    condition is imposed on these response slots.

    Take the disjoint union of these lists over <math|n>, retaining each
    insertion and its coefficient. Every key is still in the original wing.
    Tonelli bounds its purchase mass by <math|<big|sum><rsub|n>u<rsub|n>>.
    For the responses, retain the majorants <math|b<rsub|n,X>> of the slot
    <math|\<Psi\><rsub|n>*<around|(|X,\<cdot\>|)>>, and define

    <\equation*>
      b<rsub|X>=<big|sum><rsub|n>b<rsub|n,X>,<space|2em>\<Psi\>*<around|(|X,U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|n>\<Psi\><rsub|n>*<around|(|X,U|)>.
    </equation*>

    Reserved mass is linear in the amplitude on a fixed support and dominates
    that amplitude. Tonelli therefore gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|X><math-up|reservedMass><around|(|X,b<rsub|X>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|n>r<rsub|n>,<space|2em><around|\||\<Psi\>*<around|(|X,U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|X>.
    </equation*>

    Every slot sum is absolutely and uniformly convergent. Dependence on its
    support, gauge invariance and healthy vanishing pass termwise to the sum,
    and pointwise limits preserve Borel measurability. If a summed slot is
    nonzero, some summand is nonzero, so its support contains the anchor.
    This proves the response bound with its exact sum of output prices.

    Each input leaf's ordinary entry and tagged forced entry are bounded by
    its weight, hence by its purchased mass. Thus the input identities can be
    summed absolutely and their iterated sums give the stated inputs, at
    every configuration and every profile tag. For the output identity use
    the volume bound in soundness of this same door: the forced mass of the
    response to a leaf <math|M> is at most
    <math|w<math-up|load><around|(|M|)>> for a fixed <math|w>. Consequently
    the double sum of quoted forced profiles is absolutely bounded by
    <math|w> times the total purchased list mass (with the fixed factor
    <math|L<rsub|m><rsup|d>>). For the hooked responses use
    <eqref|eq:local-merge-cost> and <eqref|eq:local-meeting-reserve>: their
    total absolute forced mass is bounded by a fixed multiple of their
    reserved response mass. Hence both sides of the output-profile identity
    are absolutely summable. Sum the component identities and interchange the
    list, response and address sums. The result is exactly the Tale for the
    merged coefficient, with the two costs asserted in the statement. This
    also proves addition for a finite collection, multiplication by a scalar
    with costs multiplied by its modulus, and monotonicity in all three
    prices. Zero fields and empty lists give the zero Coupon at any
    nonnegative prices.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|The one-step transfer at an actual selected step>
  <with|the-label|13.10.13|<label|sec:selected-reprise>>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The one-step one-step transfer is
    available><label|prop:selected-reprise>Under the selected-history and
    common-purchase hypotheses of this section, (RS) holds at every
    <math|t<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>m\<less\>N>. Its finite wing is fixed before
    the coefficient vector. The lower coefficient family, its charge, its
    index and its comparison witness are constructed together on the stored
    transition.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    <em|The local input and its ticket.> Use the stored interval witness at
    <math|m> and its retained parameter. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-value-input>
    identifies its incoming representation, paths, clipping and coupling band
    with those of the funded interpolation. The purchases of
    Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history> and
    <reference|sec:common-observable-purchases> include the primary, regular
    and forced insertion estimates at every grade through the permitted
    stage. Here <math|d+3\<leqslant\>J<around|(|t<rsub|d>|)>\<leqslant\>J<around|(|m|)>>,
    so the section, derivative and kernel orders required in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:three-port-history> are available. The
    local section and the sound marked derivative map are those of that same
    step. Soundness also follows from Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:selected-retention-public>.

    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-reserved-slots> gives the upper
    Keeper, including its exact eligibility predicate. On the finite fine
    torus choose one ordinary key for each anchored support and the Keeper
    key for each support\Uinventory tag. There are only finitely many tags.
    Their union is a wing <math|W<rsub|0>>, independent of the incoming
    coefficient. Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:three-port-history> supplies
    on this wing the three component Coupons with a common heading
    <math|T=B<math-up|vowel><rsub|L<rsub|m>>> and prices <math|C,\<eta\>,t>,
    where

    <\equation*>
      \<eta\>=L<rsub|m><rsup|-2/5>,<space|2em>t=\<psi\><rsub|m>,<space|2em>\<kappa\><rsub|f>=<sqrt|w<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|m+1>>\<in\><around|(|0,1|]>.
    </equation*>

    Its primary price uses the same local-section bound as
    <math|\<psi\><rsub|m>>; the forced remainder is divided by
    <math|\<kappa\><rsub|f>>, whereas its input price is not. All price
    choices precede the history. The purchased inequalities
    <eqref|eq:ex-interpolation-buy> hold on this step.

    <em|The covariance cancellation.> The evaluation of every stored
    representation is strictly positive. At the terminal scale it is the
    positive Wilson density. At an adjacent step the blocking noise is a
    continuous strictly positive function on the compact pair of
    configuration spaces, hence has a positive minimum. The transition
    density is its mixture with nonnegative likelihood weights summing to
    one, and has the same positive lower bound. The exact retained density
    identity expresses the lower evaluation as its integral against the
    positive upper evaluation, up to the positive scalar normalization of the
    reset. The lawful envelope makes this integral finite; probability Haar
    measure and pointwise positivity make it positive. Downward induction
    proves the assertion at every level.

    For the given upper coefficient family write <math|I=x<rprime|'>> for its
    index. Its admissibility clauses supply (H1)\U(H6) of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-selected-linear-interpolation>, and its
    assumed profile covariance supplies the remaining profile hypothesis
    there. The sound local section gives the coefficient law under motions.
    To see why the exponential entry also transforms correctly, subtract the
    covariant dressed forced term from the covariant density in its defining
    formula. The dressed term is determined by the forced profile, by the tag
    argument and absolute domination in Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-zero-coordinate-coupon>.
    Divide by the nonzero background evaluation just proved. This establishes
    the needed exponential-entry covariance without imposing covariance of
    the raw address arrays.

    <em|The basis Coupons.> For each frame axis <math|e<rsub|a>> set
    <math|v<rsub|a>=I<rsup|-1>*e<rsub|a>>. Repack its forced array into
    Keeper addresses, by the five-part repacking argument of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-zero-coordinate-coupon>; that
    argument preserves any lead, not just zero. It preserves the exponential
    entry, forced profile, density and remainder allocations. Split the
    result into its primary coordinate, regular atoms and forced atoms. Apply
    the three ticket clauses to this decomposition, using the exact vacuum
    Coupon for <math|a=0>. If its regular and forced remainder allocations
    are <math|a<rsub|<math-up|r>>,a<rsub|<math-up|f>>>, then their mass sums
    are at most <math|a<rsub|<math-up|r>>> and
    <math|\<kappa\><rsub|f>*a<rsub|<math-up|f>>>. The lead and remainder
    error sums are bounded by <math|\<eta\>*<around|(|a<rsub|<math-up|r>>+a<rsub|<math-up|f>>|)>>;
    the input cost is at most <math|2*a<rsub|<math-up|r>>+2*\<kappa\><rsub|f>*a<rsub|<math-up|f>>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|a<rsub|<math-up|r>>+a<rsub|<math-up|f>>|)>>.
    The predicted leads consist of one primary lead and zeros, so are
    summable. The input densities have the dominated decomposition of the
    same repacking proof. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-coupon-merger>
    therefore applies, including to the two Tale costs.

    Put <math|s=s<rsub|m+1>>, <math|R=C<rsub|<math-up|orb>>>,
    <math|D<around|(|z|)>=<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|vowel><rsub|L<rsub|m>><around|(|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>z|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
    <math|c=C+\<eta\>*s> and <math|\<epsilon\>=\<eta\>*<around|(|1+s|)>>. The
    upper charge bound is <math|c<rprime|'><around|(|v<rsub|a>|)>\<leqslant\>s<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>e<rsub|a>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>.
    The resulting basis Coupon has predicted lead <math|T*e<rsub|a>>, lead
    error at most <math|\<epsilon\><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>e<rsub|a>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>
    after any permitted motion, remainder price
    <math|c*D<around|(|e<rsub|a>|)>>, and input price <math|t+2*s>. Here the
    motion estimate uses the ticket's turn-weighted remainder and
    orbit-weighted lead estimate; motion is not assumed to preserve the
    coordinate norm.

    <em|One lower coefficient family for all vectors.> Apply the finite
    construction of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-selected-interpolation>
    to these basis Coupons. Explicitly take their moved coefficients
    <math|Z<rsub|g,a>> and put

    <\equation*>
      V<around|(|v|)>=<big|sum><rsub|g,a>\<chi\><rsub|g,a>*<around|(|I*v|)>*Z<rsub|g,a>,<space|2em>x<around|(|v|)>=V<around|(|v|)>.*x,<space|2em>c<rsub|-><around|(|v|)>=c*R*D*<around|(|I*v|)>.
    </equation*>

    The chips <math|\<chi\>> include the independent conjugation flag as in
    <eqref|eq:ex-interpolation-chips>, so the coefficients of this finite sum
    are complex-linear in <math|v>. Its regular supports and forced
    dictionary are fixed before <math|v>. The finite reconstruction identity
    and upper density linearity give the exact grade-scaled density push. In
    this formula the physical factor <math|L<rsub|m><rsup|d>> is included
    once, as in <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|m>> of that proposition.

    The same proposition gives the format, coarse-weight remainder, motion
    covariance and two-cost Tale of this coefficient. Its readout is an
    isomorphism by the cone estimate: a zero lower readout forces a zero
    upper one. Here its room and bank hypotheses follow from
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-stored-service-budget>: monotonicity
    and <math|J*<around|(|m+1|)>\<leqslant\>J<around|(|m|)>+1> give
    <math|W*<around|(|J*<around|(|m+1|)>|)>\<leqslant\>W*<around|(|J<around|(|m|)>+1|)>>
    and <math|W*<around|(|J*<around|(|m+1|)>|)>\<leqslant\>W*<around|(|J*<around|(|m+1|)>+1|)>>.
    Thus both room bounds dominate the heading constant required in the
    interpolation, and the bank bound is stronger than its required one. In
    particular

    <\equation*>
      c<rsub|-><around|(|v|)>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|m><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>x<around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|I*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|m+1><around|\<\|\|\>|x<around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|x<around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|m+1>*L<rsub|m><rsup|d>*<around|\<\|\|\>|I*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    The full direction and charge estimates are those of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-selected-cone-step>, with the same heading.
    The charge is nonnegative and subadditive because <math|c*R\<geqslant\>0>
    and <math|D\<circ\>I> is a seminorm. At the physical unit its error and
    charge vanish, and <math|T*<around|(|a<rsub|m+1><rsup|d>*e<rsub|0>|)>=a<rsub|m><rsup|d>*e<rsub|0>>.
    This proves the unit clause exactly. The density is linear and
    motion/reality covariant by the finite reconstruction and covariance of
    the stored kernel. These verify all the admissible clauses, not merely
    the remainder bound.

    The profile of the forced finite sum is its summed profile, by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-coefficient-merger>. Reindexing the
    finite motion average shows that it has the required profile covariance.
    Let <math|W> be the union of all motion images of <math|W<rsub|0>>. It is
    finite and independent of <math|v>. Transport each basis Tale to this
    wing, multiply it by its chip, and add. The input entries, profiles and
    vacuum coordinates recombine to those of the original
    <math|V<rprime|'><around|(|v|)>> by the covariance cancellation above and
    the frame identity. The output profiles recombine to those of
    <math|V<around|(|v|)>>. The weighted chip estimates give the two bounds

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|g,a><around|\||\<chi\><rsub|g,a>*<around|(|I*v|)>|\|>*<around|(|t+2*s|)>\<leqslant\>R*<around|\<\|\|\>|I*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>*<around|(|t+2*s|)>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|g,a><around|\||\<chi\><rsub|g,a>*<around|(|I*v|)>|\|>*c*D<around|(|e<rsub|a>|)>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|-><around|(|v|)>.
    </equation*>

    These are precisely the Carol input price and output charge in (RS). Thus
    its density, cone, charge, covariance and common-wing statements hold for
    the same lower coefficient family. No history or door is reselected.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|One transfer sequence from the terminal coefficient
    family><label|prop:selected-music>Let <math|d,p,N> be as above. Then
    there is a transfer sequence <math|<around|(|V<rsub|m>|)><rsub|t<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>m\<leqslant\>N>>
    with the following properties:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|V<rsub|N>=\<cal-V\><rsup|\<top\>>>, the coefficient family
      of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-terminal-voice>;

      <item>every <math|V<rsub|m>> is admissible at <math|m> and satisfies
      the profile covariance at <math|m>;

      <item>at every stored door <math|D<rsub|m>>,
      <math|t<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>m\<less\>N>, the density of
      <math|V<rsub|m><around|(|v|)>> is the push of that of
      <math|V<rsub|m+1><around|(|v|)>>, the adjacent cone and charge
      inequalities hold, and there is a Carol between <math|V<rsub|m+1>> and
      <math|V<rsub|m>> with input price <math|C<rsub|<math-up|orb>><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|m+1><around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>*<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|m>+2*s<rsub|m+1>|)>>.
    </enumerate>

    The finite transfer data of Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-freight-backward>
    may be taken to be the coefficient maps of this transfer sequence,
    normalized by its reference readout. In particular their terminal forced
    profile is the singleton hook of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-terminal-voice>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    We use downward induction on <math|k> from <math|N> to <math|t<rsub|d>>.
    The claim at stage <math|k> is that there is a transfer sequence with
    <math|V<rsub|N>=\<cal-V\><rsup|\<top\>>> such that (2) holds at every
    <math|m\<in\><around|[|k,N|]>> and (3) holds at every
    <math|m\<in\><around|[|k,N|)>>.

    For <math|k=N>, put <math|V<rsub|N>=\<cal-V\><rsup|\<top\>>>, and fill
    the levels below <math|N> with the zero coefficient family.
    Assertion<nbsp>(4) of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-terminal-voice>
    gives (2) at <math|N>; (3) is vacuous.

    Suppose the claim holds at stage <math|k+1>, with
    <math|t<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>k\<less\>N>, and let
    <math|<around|(|V<rsub|m>|)>> be the transfer sequence it provides. Its
    coefficient family <math|V<rsub|k+1>> is admissible and satisfies the
    profile covariance at <math|k+1>. Apply
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-reprise> at <math|m=k> with
    <math|V<rprime|'>=V<rsub|k+1>>, and replace the coefficient family at
    level <math|k> by the resulting <math|V>. The coefficient families at
    levels above <math|k> are unchanged, so (1), and (2) and (3) above
    <math|k>, persist. At level <math|k>, (2) holds and the door
    <math|D<rsub|k>> satisfies (3) by (RS). This is the claim at stage
    <math|k>. After <math|N-t<rsub|d>> steps we obtain the transfer sequence.

    The construction is finite. Each step modifies one level, and only after
    the levels above it have been fixed; no coefficient family is chosen
    after testing a lower one. The density identities, the cone inequalities
    and the terminal isomorphism <math|x<rsub|N>=<math-up|id>> are those used
    in Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-freight-backward> and its normalization. The
    coefficient maps of this transfer sequence are therefore a valid choice
    of the finite transfer data, and their top is
    <math|\<cal-V\><rsup|\<top\>>>.
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|What these propositions supply.>
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-terminal-voice> is unconditional
  relative to the cited results. It discharges, on the stored top
  representation, the four hypotheses of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:terminal-marked-voice>:
  lawfulness, reserved addresses, the sound section and the paid fee. It
  exports the lead, the zero regular part, the core charge,
  <eqref|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T5>, the admissible clauses
  and the singleton hook. The reserved addresses are thus not an independent
  receipt of the run. They follow from the reserved labels of the exact
  startup family. Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-music> together
  with Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-reprise> supplies the
  remaining part of (R6). It places all the adjacent ledgers and finite-wing
  comparisons on one transfer sequence whose top is that coefficient family,
  under the common selected-history and local-purchase hypotheses of this
  section.

  <subsubsection|The zero-coordinate Coupon on the stored
  doors><label|sec:selected-zero-coordinate-coupon>

  This subsection treats item (R7) of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-marked-portfolio-calibration>.
  It proves the tuning inequality from the purchase and the price bookkeeping
  outright. The Coupon itself is reduced to three inputs on the same stored
  door: the regular and forced insertion estimates, soundness, and the
  countable merger of Coupons.

  Fix a grade <math|d>, a cutoff <math|N>, a period <math|p> and a level
  <math|i> with <math|t<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>i\<less\>N>. Then
  <math|d+3\<leqslant\>J<around|(|t<rsub|d>|)>\<leqslant\>J<around|(|i|)>>.
  Write <math|R<rsup|+>=R<rsub|N,p,i+1>> and <math|R<rsup|->=R<rsub|N,p,i>>
  for the stored upper and lower representations, and <math|D=D<rsub|N,p,i>>
  for the stored door between them. The block side of this step is
  <math|L<rsub|i>>, the block length chosen at the entrance stage
  <math|J*<around|(|i+1|)>>. Write <math|C<rsub|i>=<math-up|columns><around|(|i|)>>
  for the column count of the profile at the stage <math|J<around|(|i|)>>,
  and <math|\<kappa\>=<sqrt|w<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|i+1>>>, so that
  <math|0\<less\>\<kappa\>\<leqslant\>1>. Put

  <\equation*>
    \<varrho\><rsub|i>=<math-up|rate><around|(|L<rsub|i>|)>=L<rsub|i><rsup|-2/5>,<space|2em><math-up|barunit><around|(|L<rsub|i>|)>=L<rsub|i><rsup|-3/8>,
  </equation*>

  and let <math|\<psi\><rsub|i>=2*e<rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|i+1>>*\<tau\><rsub|i+1>>
  be the common waybill <eqref|eq:history-common-waybill> at the fine level
  <math|i+1>; here <math|\<tau\><rsub|i+1>\<geqslant\>0> is the fine
  local-section derivative bound. The number <math|\<varrho\><rsub|i>> is the
  number called <math|\<eta\><rsub|i>> in (R7). It is not the marker price
  <math|\<eta\><rsub|m>=<tfrac|1|4><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m><rsup|-1/2>>
  displayed earlier in this section, which is the allowance <math|u<rsub|m>>
  of (R5). Let <math|W<rsub|i>> be the finite wing chosen above for this
  step. It contains the principal-mode keys of the supports containing the
  origin and the keeper key of every support\Uinventory tag.

  <subparagraph|Coefficients and their densities.> We recall the data
  entering a Coupon. A coefficient field <math|Q> on <math|R<rsup|+>>
  consists of a coordinate vector <math|Q.*x\<in\>F>, regular atoms
  <math|<around|(|X<rsub|j>,f<rsub|j>|)><rsub|j\<geqslant\>0>> with supports
  <math|X<rsub|j>> and local functions <math|f<rsub|j>>, and a forced array
  <math|<around|(|k<rsub|a>|)><rsub|a\<geqslant\>0>> indexed by the addresses
  of the dictionary of <math|R<rsup|+>>. Address <math|a> carries a support
  <math|S<rsub|a>>, a nonempty inventory <math|I<rsub|a>\<subseteq\>S<rsub|a>>
  and an activity <math|K<rsub|a>>; its <em|tag> is
  <math|<math-up|tag><around|(|a|)>=<around|(|S<rsub|a>,I<rsub|a>|)>>. The
  exponential entry of <math|Q> is

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-E\><around|(|Q|)><around|(|U|)>=Q.*x<rsub|0>+\<ell\>*<around|(|U,Q.*x|)>+<big|sum><rsub|j>\<chi\><rsub|X<rsub|j>>*f<rsub|j><around|(|U|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|Q.*x<rsub|0>> is the unit coordinate,
  <math|\<ell\>*<around|(|U,\<cdot\>|)>> is the masked placement of
  <math|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>Q.*x> by the local section at the origin,
  and <math|\<chi\><rsub|X<rsub|j>>*f<rsub|j>> is the masked regular term.
  The forced profile <math|\<cal-P\><around|(|Q|)>> is the function on tags

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-P\><around|(|Q|)><around|(|X,I|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a:<math-up|tag><around|(|a|)>=<around|(|X,I|)>>k<rsub|a>.
  </equation*>

  A finite set <math|b> of addresses is <em|admissible> at <math|U> if the
  supports <math|S<rsub|c>>, <math|c\<in\>b>, are pairwise disjoint and the
  inventories <math|I<rsub|c>>, <math|c\<in\>b>, together cover exactly the
  flags of <math|U>. This is the admissibility condition of the gas sum of
  the activity expansion, stated in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-splitting>
  (subsection \PPutting the response into the existing gas\Q); the additional
  eligibility predicates of the addresses do not enter it. Two consequences
  are used below. First, admissibility of <math|b> at <math|U> depends on the
  addresses of <math|b> only through their tags. Second, two distinct
  addresses of one admissible set have disjoint supports. The dressed field
  at an address <math|a> is

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|Dr><rsub|a><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|b\<ni\>a<text|admissible
    at >U><big|prod><rsub|c\<in\>b\<setminus\><around|{|a|}>>K<rsub|c><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  <em|Absolute bound on the dressed fields.> The stored representation
  <math|R<rsup|+>> is lawful. Indeed the selected history satisfies all its
  local activity bounds at every level (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:surviving-interval-shooting>).
  These are the retained-history conditions spelled out in the proof of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:wilson-startup-family>. In particular the
  forced activities of <math|R<rsup|+>> have the forced stage bound at the
  forced allowance <math|w<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|i+1>> of level <math|i+1>.
  That bound is the rooted support sum <eqref|eq:terminal-marked-construction-display-T3>
  of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:terminal-marked-construction>, at the
  support exponent <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i+1>>: there are majorants
  <math|<wide|K|\<bar\>><rsub|c>\<geqslant\>0> with
  <math|<around|\||K<rsub|c>|\|>\<leqslant\><wide|K|\<bar\>><rsub|c>> and

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|c:x\<in\>S<rsub|c>><math-up|mass><around|(|S<rsub|c>,<wide|K|\<bar\>><rsub|c>|)>\<leqslant\>w<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|i+1><space|2em><text|for
    every site <math|x> of the fine torus <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i+1>>,>
  </equation*>

  where <math|<math-up|mass>> is the fine mass recalled below. Since
  <math|<math-up|mass><around|(|S,e|)>\<geqslant\>e> for
  <math|e\<geqslant\>0> (see the remainder convention below) and every
  support is nonempty, summing over <math|x\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|i+1>> gives
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|c><wide|K|\<bar\>><rsub|c>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|i+1>|\|>*<space|0.17em>w<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|i+1>>.
  Fix <math|a>. The map <math|b\<mapsto\>b\<setminus\><around|{|a|}>> is
  injective on the finite sets containing <math|a>. Hence, for every
  <math|U>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:selected-dressed-bound><big|sum><rsub|b\<ni\>a<text|admissible
    at >U><big|prod><rsub|c\<in\>b\<setminus\><around|{|a|}>><around|\||K<rsub|c><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|T<text|finite>><big|prod><rsub|c\<in\>T><wide|K|\<bar\>><rsub|c>=sup<rsub|F<text|finite>>
    <big|prod><rsub|c\<in\>F><around|(|1+<wide|K|\<bar\>><rsub|c>|)>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around*|(|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|i+1>|\|>*<space|0.17em>w<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|i+1>|)>=:D<rsub|i+1>.
  </equation>

  The equality holds because every finite <math|T> lies in some finite
  <math|F>, and <math|<big|prod><rsub|c\<in\>F><around|(|1+<wide|K|\<bar\>><rsub|c>|)>>
  is the sum of <math|<big|prod><rsub|c\<in\>T><wide|K|\<bar\>><rsub|c>> over
  the subsets <math|T\<subseteq\>F>; the last inequality is
  <math|1+x\<leqslant\>e<rsup|x>>. So each dressed series converges
  absolutely, and <math|<around|\||<math-up|Dr><rsub|a><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>D<rsub|i+1>>
  uniformly in <math|a> and <math|U>.

  The density of <math|Q> is

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|dens><around|(|Q|)><around|(|U|)>=R<rsup|+>.<math-up|eval><around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-E\><around|(|Q|)><around|(|U|)>+e<rsup|R<rsup|+>.<math-up|exponent><around|(|U|)>>*<big|sum><rsub|a>k<rsub|a><around|(|U|)><math-up|Dr><rsub|a><around|(|U|)>,
  </equation*>

  in which the forced series has been regrouped by its address. The
  regrouping is justified by <eqref|eq:selected-dressed-bound> whenever the
  forced array is absolutely summable. For the fields <math|Q> with a
  remainder bound considered below this holds, since
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|a><around|\||k<rsub|a><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|a>e<rprime|'><rsub|a>\<less\>\<infty\>>
  by step<nbsp>(v) of the proof of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-zero-coordinate-coupon>.

  The terminal format at the origin requires several things of <math|Q>. Each
  regular atom is regular on its support, its support contains the origin, it
  vanishes at the identity configuration, and it passes the holonomy test.
  Each forced coefficient is gauge invariant, measurable and local to its
  support. Where <math|k<rsub|a>> is nonzero, address <math|a> is eligible,
  that is <math|S<rsub|a>\<cap\><math-up|flags><around|(|U|)>=I<rsub|a>>; and
  <math|k<rsub|a>\<ne\>0> forces the origin to lie in <math|S<rsub|a>>. A
  remainder bound <math|q> at weight <math|\<kappa\>> means that there are
  <math|a<rsub|<math-up|r>>,a<rsub|<math-up|f>>\<geqslant\>0> with
  <math|a<rsub|<math-up|r>>+a<rsub|<math-up|f>>\<leqslant\>q>, together with
  ordered-derivative majorants <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|0,X<rsub|j>>\<leqslant\>e<rsub|j>>
  in the spatially weighted norm of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-splitting>,
  and <math|e<rprime|'><rsub|a>\<geqslant\>0> with
  <math|<around|\||k<rsub|a>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rprime|'><rsub|a>>, such that

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|j><math-up|mass><around|(|X<rsub|j>,e<rsub|j>|)>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|<math-up|r>>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|a><math-up|mass><around|(|S<rsub|a>,e<rprime|'><rsub|a>|)>\<leqslant\>\<kappa\>*<space|0.17em>a<rsub|<math-up|f>>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|<math-up|mass><around|(|X,e|)>=e<rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|i+1><math-up|coverNumber><around|(|B<rsub|i+1>,X|)>>*e>
  is the fine mass. It is linear in <math|e> for a fixed support. The support
  exponent <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i+1>> of a budget is positive
  (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-splitting>) and the cover number is
  nonnegative, so <math|<math-up|mass><around|(|X,e|)>\<geqslant\>e> for
  every <math|e\<geqslant\>0>. This is the normalized remainder convention of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-splitting>.

  A <em|Coupon> across <math|D> on a wing <math|W> for input data
  <math|<around|(|\<alpha\>,\<rho\>,\<delta\>,f|)>> is defined as follows.
  Here <math|\<alpha\>> is an exponential entry, <math|\<rho\>> a forced
  profile, <math|\<delta\>> a unit value and <math|f> an upper density. The
  Coupon has lead <math|\<ell\><rsub|0>\<in\>F> and prices
  <math|<around|(|c,r,u|)>>. It is a lower coefficient field <math|P> on
  <math|R<rsup|->> with the format at <math|R<rsup|->>, the exact push
  identity <math|<math-up|dens><around|(|P|)>=L<rsub|i><rsup|d><math-ss|P><rsub|i>f>,
  the lead bound <math|C<rsub|<math-up|orb>>*<around|\<\|\|\>|P.*x-\<ell\><rsub|0>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>c>,
  and a turn-weighted remainder bound at most <math|r>. It also has a Tale
  witness on <math|W> across <math|D> relating
  <math|<around|(|\<alpha\>,\<rho\>,\<delta\>|)>> to the lower forced profile
  <math|\<cal-P\><around|(|P|)>>, with input price <math|u> and output price
  <math|r>. This is the notion of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:three-port-history>:
  there the three elementary insertions are described by exactly these items,
  with prices written as <math|C<rsub|<math-up|orb>>*<around|\<\|\|\>|Q.*x-\<cdots\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<cdot\>>,
  <math|r=\<cdot\>> and \Pinput allowance\Q. It is also the description
  before Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:actual-marked-antilles>, with
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|0>=0>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Zero-coordinate Coupon on the stored
    doors><label|prop:selected-zero-coordinate-coupon>Keep the selected
    history, its stored doors and the purchase of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-observable-purchases>. Fix
    <math|d,N,p> and <math|t<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>i\<less\>N>, with the
    notation above. Assume the following three inputs on the stored door
    <math|D>.

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(K)><em|Insertion estimates.> The upper representation
      <math|R<rsup|+>> has a Keeper, an injective assignment
      <math|<around|(|X,I|)>\<mapsto\><math-ss|k><around|(|X,I|)>> of an
      address with tag <math|<around|(|X,I|)>> to every pair
      <math|\<varnothing\>\<ne\>I\<subseteq\>X>, eligible whenever
      <math|X\<cap\><math-up|flags><around|(|U|)>=I>. Its keys lie in
      <math|W<rsub|i>>. On the wing <math|W<rsub|i>> the regular and forced
      estimates of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:three-port-history> hold on
      <math|D> with <math|\<eta\>=\<varrho\><rsub|i>>, in the following form,
      with lead <math|0>.

      <\itemize>
        <item>Let <math|<around|(|X,f|)>> be a regular atom with
        <math|0\<in\>X> that passes the format tests, and let <math|e> be an
        ordered-derivative majorant of <math|f>,
        <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|0,X>\<leqslant\>e> in the
        spatially weighted norm of the remainder convention above. Put
        <math|m=<math-up|mass><around|(|X,e|)>>. Then the input data

        <\equation*>
          <around|(|\<chi\><rsub|X>*f,<space|0.17em>0,<space|0.17em>0,<space|0.17em>R<rsup|+>.<math-up|eval><space|0.17em>\<chi\><rsub|X>*f|)>
        </equation*>

        have a Coupon with prices <math|<around|(|\<varrho\><rsub|i>*m,\<varrho\><rsub|i>*m,2*m|)>>.

        <item>Let <math|a> be an address whose key lies in <math|W<rsub|i>>,
        and let <math|f\<ne\>0> be a forced insertion at <math|a> that fits
        its slot, with <math|<around|\||f|\|>\<leqslant\>e>. Put
        <math|m=<math-up|mass><around|(|S<rsub|a>,e|)>>. Then the input data

        <\equation*>
          <around|(|0,<space|0.17em>f*\<b-1\><rsub|<math-up|tag><around|(|a|)>>,<space|0.17em>0,<space|0.17em>e<rsup|R<rsup|+>.<math-up|exponent>>*f<math-up|Dr><rsub|a>|)>
        </equation*>

        have a Coupon with prices <math|<around|(|\<varrho\><rsub|i>*m/\<kappa\>,\<varrho\><rsub|i>*m/\<kappa\>,2*m|)>>.
      </itemize>

      <item*|(S)><em|Soundness.> The door <math|D> is sound for the
      transition operator of the step.

      <item*|(M)><em|Merger.> Coupons across <math|D> on one wing are
      monotone in their three prices. A countable family of them merges into
      a Coupon whose input data, lead and prices are the sums, provided the
      following hold: the entries and the profiles converge pointwise; the
      unit values, the three prices and the norms of the leads are summable;
      and the densities are measurable and dominated. This is the merger of
      Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-coupon-merger>, including the Tale
      witnesses. Finally, zero input data have a Coupon with lead <math|0>
      and any nonnegative prices.
    </enumerate>

    Then (R7) holds at <math|D> with <math|\<eta\><rsub|i>=\<varrho\><rsub|i>>:

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|1+2*C<rsub|i>|)>*\<varrho\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\><math-up|barunit><around|(|L<rsub|i>|)>,<space|2em>D<text|
      is sound>,<space|2em>\<psi\><rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0,<space|2em>\<varrho\><rsub|i>\<gtr\>0.
    </equation*>

    Moreover, let <math|Q> be any upper coefficient field on <math|R<rsup|+>>
    with <math|Q.*x=0>, the terminal format at the origin and remainder bound
    <math|q> at weight <math|\<kappa\>>. Then its input data
    <math|<around|(|\<cal-E\><around|(|Q|)>,\<cal-P\><around|(|Q|)>,Q.*x<rsub|0>,<math-up|dens><around|(|Q|)>|)>>
    have a Coupon across <math|D> on <math|W<rsub|i>> with lead <math|0> and
    prices <math|<around|(|\<varrho\><rsub|i>*q,\<varrho\><rsub|i>*q,2*q|)>>.
    The wing <math|W<rsub|i>> is finite and does not depend on <math|Q>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    <em|Tuning.> Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-marked-portfolio-calibration>
    chooses, for every stage <math|j>, a threshold <math|v<rsub|j>> with
    <math|<around|(|1+2*c<rsub|j>|)><math-up|rate><around|(|L|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|barunit><around|(|L|)>>
    for all <math|L\<geqslant\>v<rsub|j>>; here <math|c<rsub|j>> is the
    column count of the profile at stage <math|j>. Indeed the ratio of the
    two sides is <math|<math-up|rate><around|(|L|)>/<math-up|barunit><around|(|L|)>=L<rsup|-2/5+3/8>=L<rsup|-1/40>>.
    So the inequality is equivalent to <math|L<rsup|1/40>\<geqslant\>1+2*c<rsub|j>>,
    and <math|v<rsub|j>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|1+2*c<rsub|j>|)><rsup|40>|\<rceil\>>>
    suffices. The stage threshold <math|n<rsub|k>> there is the maximum of
    these <math|v<rsub|j>> over <math|j\<leqslant\>k>, together with the
    credit thresholds. By Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-observable-purchases>,
    the purchase includes <math|n<rsub|k>> among the block floors of stage
    <math|k>. Every block length chosen at stage <math|k> is at least that
    floor. Take <math|k=J*<around|(|i+1|)>>. The block side of the present
    step is chosen at that stage, so <math|L<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>n<rsub|J*<around|(|i+1|)>>>.
    Since <math|J<around|(|i|)>\<leqslant\>J*<around|(|i+1|)>>, the threshold
    <math|v<rsub|J<around|(|i|)>>> is one of the terms of
    <math|n<rsub|J*<around|(|i+1|)>>>. Hence
    <math|L<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>v<rsub|J<around|(|i|)>>>. The column count at
    level <math|i> is that of the profile at stage <math|J<around|(|i|)>>, so
    <math|C<rsub|i>=c<rsub|J<around|(|i|)>>>. Therefore
    <math|<around|(|1+2*C<rsub|i>|)>*\<varrho\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\><math-up|barunit><around|(|L<rsub|i>|)>>.
    This is the grade-zero clause of the purchase: the inequality involves no
    section, and the threshold <math|v<rsub|j>> is the same for every grade.

    <em|Signs and soundness.> We have <math|\<varrho\><rsub|i>=L<rsub|i><rsup|-2/5>\<gtr\>0>.
    Also <math|\<psi\><rsub|i>=2*e<rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|i+1>>*\<tau\><rsub|i+1>\<geqslant\>0>,
    since <math|\<tau\><rsub|i+1>\<geqslant\>0>. Soundness is (S).

    <em|Repacking the forced array.> Let <math|Q> be as in the statement.
    Define <math|Q<rprime|'>> to have the same coordinate vector and regular
    atoms as <math|Q>. Its forced array is

    <\equation*>
      k<rprime|'><rsub|<math-ss|k><around|(|X,I|)>>=\<cal-P\><around|(|Q|)><around|(|X,I|)>,<space|2em>k<rprime|'><rsub|a>=0<text|
      if <math|a> is not a Keeper address>.
    </equation*>

    The tag of every address has a nonempty inventory contained in its
    support, so it lies in the domain of the Keeper. We check five properties
    of <math|Q<rprime|'>>.

    (i) <math|\<cal-E\><around|(|Q<rprime|'>|)>=\<cal-E\><around|(|Q|)>>,
    because the exponential entry does not involve the forced array.

    (ii) <math|\<cal-P\><around|(|Q<rprime|'>|)>=\<cal-P\><around|(|Q|)>>.
    Fix a tag <math|<around|(|X,I|)>>. The only address with this tag at
    which <math|k<rprime|'>> can be nonzero is
    <math|<math-ss|k><around|(|X,I|)>>, since <math|<math-ss|k>> is injective
    and <math|<math-up|tag><around|(|<math-ss|k><around|(|X,I|)>|)>=<around|(|X,I|)>>.
    Hence <math|\<cal-P\><around|(|Q<rprime|'>|)><around|(|X,I|)>=k<rprime|'><rsub|<math-ss|k><around|(|X,I|)>>=\<cal-P\><around|(|Q|)><around|(|X,I|)>>.

    (iii) <math|<math-up|dens><around|(|Q<rprime|'>|)>=<math-up|dens><around|(|Q|)>>.
    The dressed field depends on the address only through its tag. To see
    this, let <math|<math-up|tag><around|(|a|)>=<math-up|tag><around|(|a<rprime|'>|)>>
    with <math|a\<ne\>a<rprime|'>>, and let <math|\<sigma\>> be the
    transposition of <math|a> and <math|a<rprime|'>>. Then <math|\<sigma\>>
    preserves tags, so it maps admissible sets to admissible sets. No
    admissible set contains both <math|a> and <math|a<rprime|'>>, because
    their supports coincide and are nonempty, while distinct members of an
    admissible set have disjoint supports. Thus
    <math|b\<mapsto\>\<sigma\><around|(|b|)>> is a bijection from the
    admissible sets containing <math|a> to those containing
    <math|a<rprime|'>>, and <math|\<sigma\><around|(|b|)>\<setminus\><around|{|a<rprime|'>|}>=b\<setminus\><around|{|a|}>>.
    Comparing the two series term by term gives
    <math|<math-up|Dr><rsub|a>=<math-up|Dr><rsub|a<rprime|'>>>; both series
    converge absolutely by <eqref|eq:selected-dressed-bound>. Write
    <math|<math-up|Dr><rsub|<around|(|X,I|)>>> for the common value. The
    forced series converges absolutely, since
    <math|<around|\||k<rsub|a><around|(|U|)><math-up|Dr><rsub|a><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>D<rsub|i+1>*e<rprime|'><rsub|a>>
    and <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>e<rprime|'><rsub|a>\<less\>\<infty\>> by (v).
    Grouping it by tags therefore gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|a>k<rsub|a><around|(|U|)><math-up|Dr><rsub|a><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|<around|(|X,I|)>>\<cal-P\><around|(|Q|)><around|(|X,I|)><around|(|U|)><math-up|Dr><rsub|<around|(|X,I|)>><around|(|U|)>,
    </equation*>

    and the same identity holds for <math|Q<rprime|'>>. By (i) and (ii) the
    two densities coincide.

    (iv) <math|Q<rprime|'>> has the terminal format. The regular clauses are
    unchanged. Fix a tag <math|<around|(|X,I|)>> and put
    <math|g=k<rprime|'><rsub|<math-ss|k><around|(|X,I|)>>=<big|sum><rsub|<math-up|tag><around|(|a|)>=<around|(|X,I|)>>k<rsub|a>>.
    This sum converges absolutely, by the majorants
    <math|e<rprime|'><rsub|a>> and the summability below. A countable
    absolutely convergent sum of gauge-invariant, measurable functions local
    to <math|X> has the same three properties. If
    <math|g<around|(|U|)>\<ne\>0>, then some
    <math|k<rsub|a><around|(|U|)>\<ne\>0> with
    <math|<math-up|tag><around|(|a|)>=<around|(|X,I|)>>. Eligibility of
    <math|a> at <math|U> gives <math|X\<cap\><math-up|flags><around|(|U|)>=I>,
    so the Keeper address <math|<math-ss|k><around|(|X,I|)>> is eligible at
    <math|U>. Similarly <math|g\<ne\>0> forces some <math|k<rsub|a>\<ne\>0>,
    hence <math|0\<in\>S<rsub|a>=X>.

    (v) <math|Q<rprime|'>> has remainder bound <math|q> at weight
    <math|\<kappa\>>, with the same <math|a<rsub|<math-up|r>>,a<rsub|<math-up|f>>>.
    The regular part is unchanged. For the forced part, put
    <math|e<rprime|''><rsub|<math-ss|k><around|(|X,I|)>>=<big|sum><rsub|<math-up|tag><around|(|a|)>=<around|(|X,I|)>>e<rprime|'><rsub|a>>
    and <math|e<rprime|''>=0> elsewhere. Then
    <math|<around|\||k<rprime|'>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rprime|''>> by the triangle
    inequality. Masses on the common support <math|X> add, so
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|a><math-up|mass><around|(|S<rsub|a>,e<rprime|''><rsub|a>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a><math-up|mass><around|(|S<rsub|a>,e<rprime|'><rsub|a>|)>\<leqslant\>\<kappa\>*a<rsub|<math-up|f>>>.
    Since <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i+1>\<gtr\>0> and the cover number is
    nonnegative, <math|<math-up|mass><around|(|S<rsub|a>,e<rprime|'><rsub|a>|)>\<geqslant\>e<rprime|'><rsub|a>\<geqslant\>0>,
    so <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>e<rprime|'><rsub|a>\<leqslant\>\<kappa\>*a<rsub|<math-up|f>>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
    This is the summability used in (iii) and (iv).

    Consequently <math|Q> and <math|Q<rprime|'>> have the same input data
    <math|<around|(|\<cal-E\>,\<cal-P\>,Q.*x<rsub|0>,<math-up|dens>|)>>, and
    it suffices to produce the Coupon for <math|Q<rprime|'>>.

    <em|Decomposition.> Since <math|Q<rprime|'>.*x=0>, also
    <math|Q<rprime|'>.*x<rsub|0>=0>, and the placement term
    <math|\<ell\>*<around|(|U,0|)>> vanishes. Thus

    <\equation*>
      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-E\><around|(|Q<rprime|'>|)>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|j>\<chi\><rsub|X<rsub|j>>*f<rsub|j>,>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-P\><around|(|Q<rprime|'>|)>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|a>k<rprime|'><rsub|a>*\<b-1\><rsub|<math-up|tag><around|(|a|)>>,>>|<row|<cell|<math-up|dens><around|(|Q<rprime|'>|)>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|j>R<rsup|+>.<math-up|eval><space|0.17em>\<chi\><rsub|X<rsub|j>>*f<rsub|j>+<big|sum><rsub|a>e<rsup|R<rsup|+>.<math-up|exponent>>*k<rprime|'><rsub|a><math-up|Dr><rsub|a>.>>>>
      </aligned>
    </equation*>

    All three series converge absolutely and pointwise. For the density the
    regular terms are dominated by the coefficient envelope of the lawful
    representation and the remainder bound. The forced terms are dominated by
    <math|e<rsup|R<rsup|+>.<math-up|exponent><around|(|U|)>>*D<rsub|i+1>*e<rprime|''><rsub|a>>,
    by <eqref|eq:selected-dressed-bound>, and
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>e<rprime|''><rsub|a>=<big|sum><rsub|a>e<rprime|'><rsub|a>\<less\>\<infty\>>
    by (v). Tonelli's theorem applied to these nonnegative majorants gives a
    dominated, measurable expansion, as in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-selected-linear-interpolation>. Put
    <math|m<rsub|j>=<math-up|mass><around|(|X<rsub|j>,e<rsub|j>|)>> and
    <math|m<rprime|'><rsub|a>=<math-up|mass><around|(|S<rsub|a>,e<rprime|''><rsub|a>|)>>.
    We attach a Coupon to each term.

    <\itemize>
      <item>Regular atom <math|j>. Its input data are
      <math|<around|(|\<chi\><rsub|X<rsub|j>>*f<rsub|j>,0,0,R<rsup|+>.<math-up|eval><space|0.17em>\<chi\><rsub|X<rsub|j>>*f<rsub|j>|)>>.
      The atom has <math|0\<in\>X<rsub|j>>, passes the format tests and has
      majorant <math|e<rsub|j>>. By (K) it has a Coupon with prices
      <math|<around|(|\<varrho\><rsub|i>*m<rsub|j>,\<varrho\><rsub|i>*m<rsub|j>,2*m<rsub|j>|)>>.

      <item>Forced address <math|a> with <math|k<rprime|'><rsub|a>\<ne\>0>.
      Then <math|a=<math-ss|k><around|(|X,I|)>> is a Keeper address, so its
      key lies in <math|W<rsub|i>>. Its coefficient fits the slot by (iv),
      and <math|<around|\||k<rprime|'><rsub|a>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rprime|''><rsub|a>>.
      By (K) it has a Coupon with prices <math|<around|(|\<varrho\><rsub|i>*m<rprime|'><rsub|a>/\<kappa\>,\<varrho\><rsub|i>*m<rprime|'><rsub|a>/\<kappa\>,2*m<rprime|'><rsub|a>|)>>.
      Since <math|\<kappa\>\<leqslant\>1>, monotonicity (M) gives one with
      prices

      <\equation*>
        <around|(|\<varrho\><rsub|i>*m<rprime|'><rsub|a>/\<kappa\>,\<varrho\><rsub|i>*m<rprime|'><rsub|a>/\<kappa\>,2*m<rprime|'><rsub|a>/\<kappa\>|)>.
      </equation*>

      <item>Forced address <math|a> with <math|k<rprime|'><rsub|a>=0>. Its
      input data are zero, and by (M) they have a Coupon with prices

      <\equation*>
        <around|(|\<varrho\><rsub|i>*m<rprime|'><rsub|a>/\<kappa\>,\<varrho\><rsub|i>*m<rprime|'><rsub|a>/\<kappa\>,2*m<rprime|'><rsub|a>/\<kappa\>|)>.
      </equation*>
    </itemize>

    <em|Summation.> All leads are <math|0>, and so are all unit values. The
    entries, profiles and densities of the terms sum to those of
    <math|Q<rprime|'>>, by the decomposition. The price series are summable:

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|j>\<varrho\><rsub|i>*m<rsub|j>+<big|sum><rsub|a><frac|\<varrho\><rsub|i>*m<rprime|'><rsub|a>|\<kappa\>>\<leqslant\>\<varrho\><rsub|i>*<around*|(|a<rsub|<math-up|r>>+a<rsub|<math-up|f>>|)>\<leqslant\>\<varrho\><rsub|i>*q,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|j>2*m<rsub|j>+<big|sum><rsub|a><frac|2*m<rprime|'><rsub|a>|\<kappa\>>\<leqslant\>2*q.
    </equation*>

    Here we used <math|<big|sum><rsub|j>m<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|<math-up|r>>>
    and <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>m<rprime|'><rsub|a>\<leqslant\>\<kappa\>*a<rsub|<math-up|f>>>
    from (v). By (M) the terms merge into one Coupon on <math|W<rsub|i>> for
    the input data of <math|Q<rprime|'>>, with lead <math|0>. Its prices are
    the three sums, and by monotonicity they may be replaced by
    <math|<around|(|\<varrho\><rsub|i>*q,\<varrho\><rsub|i>*q,2*q|)>>. By the
    repacking step these are also the input data of <math|Q>.

    The wing <math|W<rsub|i>> was chosen before <math|Q>, from the finite
    fine torus and its Keeper. It is finite because the torus has finitely
    many supports and tags.
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|What the proposition supplies.> Of (R7), the proposition
  proves the tuning inequality, the signs, and the reduction of the Coupon
  for an arbitrary zero-coordinate coefficient to single insertions. The
  tuning inequality uses only the grade-zero threshold of the purchase. That
  threshold pays tuning; the Coupon itself is paid at the grade <math|d> of
  the frame, through (K). The three inputs are not proved here. The
  admissibility facts and the absolute bound
  <eqref|eq:selected-dressed-bound> used in the repacking are not further
  inputs: they follow from the definition of the gas sum in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-splitting> and from the lawfulness of
  the stored representations, as shown before the proposition.

  <\itemize>
    <item>The regular and forced estimates (K) are those of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:three-port-history>. They hold relative to
    the retained funded interpolation and its slot realization, with
    <math|\<eta\>=L<rsup|-2/5>> there equal to <math|\<varrho\><rsub|i>>.
    They are attached to the stored door through the same history
    identifications. Their derivation uses the retained path construction of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:path-attachment-producer>, under its
    stated hypotheses, and the common-history attachment used in
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-reprise>.

    <item>Soundness (S) of the stored door is asserted in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:three-port-history> for the retained
    interpolating point.

    <item>The merger (M) is Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-coupon-merger>,
    including the Tale witnesses.
  </itemize>

  The Keeper of <math|R<rsup|+>> in (K) is part of the retained slot
  realization. At the terminal level it is the Keeper of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:terminal-marked-voice>.

  <subsection|Bounded local approximations on the original phase
  grid><label|sec:actual-phase-grid-approximants>

  The comparison argument requires bounded real observables which approximate
  whole replicated fields under the original finite-volume law. A bound on a
  single expectation would not suffice. We construct these observables using
  one insertion at the terminal scale and a second insertion at an
  intermediate scale. Their difference is controlled by the marked estimate,
  before taking any limit or choosing a phase.

  Fix the selected family <math|C> and the extraction order already used for
  its centered estimates. Write <math|a<rsub|i>> for the physical mesh,
  <math|\<sigma\><rsub|i>> for the running scale, <math|c<rsub|i>> for the
  column count, and <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>> for the accumulated column
  logarithm. Thus <math|a<rsub|i>\<to\>0> whereas
  <math|\<sigma\><rsub|i>\<to\>+\<infty\>>. At grade <math|d> the finite
  coefficient space has coordinate norm <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
  coordinate basis <math|<around|(|e<rsub|s>|)>>, and reference depth
  <math|r<rsub|d>>. The finite-volume observable associated with <math|L> is
  denoted by <math|O<rsub|N,p><around|(|L|)>>, with its base point at the
  origin. All expectations below use the original Wilson probability measure
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|N,p>>.

  We use the paid selected-history data of
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-marked-portfolio-calibration>
  and<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-centered-service>: the coefficient
  transport on this history, its adjacent density and forced-profile
  identities, and its height, transition, occupancy and support estimates.
  Precisely, the input is the record (R1)\U(R7) of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-marked-portfolio-calibration>, for
  every grade, together with the opening schedule of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-site-geometry>. In particular the
  positive heights <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,i>> and weights
  <math|\<phi\><rsub|i>> satisfy

  <\equation*>
    0\<less\>\<phi\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>\<phi\><rsub|i>*L<rsub|i><rsup|d+1>*\<zeta\><rsub|d,i>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|d,i+1>,<space|2em>L<rsub|i>=a<rsub|i>/a<rsub|i+1>.
  </equation*>

  These data are used with the same local sections and transition kernels at
  every occurrence below. The availability of this entire paid history is an
  input here; it is not inferred merely from the existence of an unmarked
  Wilson measure.

  <subsubsection|Transporting a bounded local observable>

  For <math|k\<leqslant\>N>, let <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|k,N>> be the iterated
  averaging operator which carries a scale-<math|k> observable to a function
  of the scale-<math|N> configuration. At the step from <math|i> to
  <math|i+1> it uses the transition kernel of the selected history, with that
  history's coupling at <math|i+1> and its fixed terminal indices
  <math|<around|(|N,p|)>>. Its adjoint <math|<math-ss|P><rsub|k,N>> pushes
  densities from <math|N> to <math|k>, relative to product Haar measure.
  Normalization of each transition kernel, followed by induction, gives

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|B><rsub|k,N>F|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|F|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>,>>|<row|<cell|<math-ss|B><rsub|k,N><wide|F|\<bar\>>>|<cell|=<wide|<math-ss|B><rsub|k,N>F|\<bar\>>,>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><around|(|<math-ss|P><rsub|k,N>g|)>*F*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|k>>|<cell|=<big|int>g<space|0.17em><math-ss|B><rsub|k,N>F*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|N>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Here <math|\<nu\><rsub|i>> is product Haar measure at scale <math|i>,
  <math|F> is bounded measurable, and <math|g> is integrable. The last
  identity follows first at one step by integration against the kernel, and
  then by iteration. The same argument with <math|F=1> proves preservation of
  the density integral.

  The local geometry supplies a useful stronger identity. Fix a coarse depth
  <math|J\<leqslant\>k> and a window radius <math|R>. Choose <math|k> large
  enough that the propagated radius-<math|R> windows, based at distinct
  <math|J>-cell centers, remain inside their respective chambers at every
  intervening depth. This is the room condition. To verify it, let
  <math|h<rsub|i>> be the calendar index at depth <math|i>, so
  <math|h<rsub|i+1>\<leqslant\>h<rsub|i>+1>, and recall
  <math|L<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>3>. Starting with <math|R<rsub|k>=R>, the kernel
  read set has propagated radius

  <\equation*>
    R<rsub|i+1>=L<rsub|i>*R<rsub|i>+10*<around|(|h<rsub|i+1>+3|)>*L<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  Put <math|T<rsub|i>=30*a<rsub|i>*<around|(|h<rsub|i>+5|)>>. Since
  <math|a<rsub|i+1>=a<rsub|i>/L<rsub|i>>,
  <math|T<rsub|i+1>\<leqslant\><around|(|2/3|)>*T<rsub|i>> and
  <math|a<rsub|i+1>*R<rsub|i+1>-a<rsub|i>*R<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>T<rsub|i>/3>.
  Therefore

  <\equation*>
    a<rsub|i>*R<rsub|i>+T<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|k>*R+T<rsub|k>*<space|2em><around|(|i\<geqslant\>k|)>.
  </equation*>

  Both <math|a<rsub|k>*R> and <math|T<rsub|k>> tend to zero. Choose <math|k>
  so that <math|2*<around|(|a<rsub|k>*R+T<rsub|k>|)>\<less\>a<rsub|J>>. Then
  <math|2*a<rsub|i>*R<rsub|i>\<less\>a<rsub|J>> at every later depth, which
  places the window inside the centered <math|J>-chamber, using the
  odd-subdivision chamber convention. This proves the required room condition
  uniformly in terminal depth, period and number of cells. If
  <math|F<rsub|i>> depends only on the window at cell <math|i>, then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:actual-grid-factor><math-ss|B><rsub|k,N><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>A>F<rsub|i>|)>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>A><math-ss|B><rsub|k,N>F<rsub|i>
  </equation>

  for every finite set <math|A> of distinct cells. Indeed, at one transition
  the kernel variables read by different chambers are disjoint. Integrating
  their product therefore factors. The propagated-window condition permits
  this argument at every subsequent transition. Induction over the depth
  proves the formula. Covariance of the kernels under signed lattice motions,
  and their compatibility with conjugation, also show that transport commutes
  with the centered copy maps. These are identities of the kernels; no
  factorization of the Wilson probability measure is asserted.

  At scale <math|k>, define <math|F<rsub|k,L>> to be the physically rescaled
  exponential entry of the primary coefficient <math|L>. More explicitly it
  is

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|k,L><around|(|U|)>=a<rsub|k><rsup|-d>*L<rsub|0>+\<b-1\><rsub|<math-up|healthy><around|(|k|)>><around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-Q\><rsub|k,L><around|(|U|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|k,L>> is the continuous gauge-invariant
  candidate of the local section including its physical factor
  <math|a<rsub|k><rsup|-d>>, and the indicator tests the section's footprint.
  The candidate estimate gives a bound on
  <math|<around|\||\<cal-Q\><rsub|k,L>|\|>> independent of
  <math|N\<geqslant\>k> and <math|p>. The indicator has values zero and one;
  thus <math|F<rsub|k,L>> is bounded and measurable uniformly in these
  indices. Its complete read set, including the mask's halo, lies in the
  window of radius <math|R<rsub|d>+4>, where <math|R<rsub|d>> is the stencil
  radius. Consequently there is <math|A<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>0> such that

  <\equation*>
    sup <around|\||F<rsub|k,e<rsub|s>>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|k><space|2em><text|for
    every basis index >s,
  </equation*>

  uniformly in <math|N,p>. For example one may sum the finitely many
  individual bounds. Continuity of the masked field is not needed.

  <subsubsection|The marked discrepancy estimate>

  Fix an opened pair of depths <math|J\<leqslant\>m>, with
  <math|m\<geqslant\>r<rsub|d>>, and choose <math|k\<geqslant\>m> satisfying
  the room condition above. Put

  <\equation*>
    E<rsub|k>=4*<around|(|1+c<rsub|k>|)>,<space|1em>G*<around|(|k,i|)>=exp
    <around*|(|<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|k>-\<sigma\><rsub|i>|)>/8|)>,<space|1em>S<rsub|J,m>=exp
    <around*|(|<around|(|a<rsub|J>/a<rsub|m>|)><rsup|4>*\<cal-T\><rsub|m>+5|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|m>> is the vacuum treasury. Choose
  <math|w\<gtr\>0> so that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:actual-grid-price>w*a<rsub|m><rsup|-d>*<frac|E<rsub|k>|G*<around|(|k,m|)>>\<leqslant\><frac|\<zeta\><rsub|d,m>|2>.
  </equation>

  By Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-centered-opened-copy>, applied to <math|J>
  opened at <math|m>, the opening supplies migrating centers for the full
  <math|J>-cell census, separated at every depth between <math|m> and
  <math|N>. Their occupancy is one and therefore fits the nondecreasing paid
  occupancy schedule, whose entries are at least one.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lem:actual-grid-basis>For every basis vector <math|e<rsub|s>> and
    every <math|N\<gtr\>k>, there is a coefficient <math|b<rsub|s>> in the
    same history's coefficient transport such that

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|e<rsub|s>-I<rsub|k>*b<rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|4>,<space|2em>\<rho\><rsub|J;N,p>*<around*|(|<math-ss|B><rsub|k,N>F<rsub|k,e<rsub|s>>-O<rsub|N,p><around|(|T<rsub|N>*b<rsub|s>|)>|)>\<leqslant\>S<rsub|J,m>/w.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|I<rsub|k>> is the coefficient identification at <math|k>,
    <math|T<rsub|N>> is the terminal identification, and
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|J;N,p>> is the centered full-census root test for
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|N,p>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Apply the backward coefficient construction of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:actual-marked-antilles> to the primary
    coefficient with lead <math|e<rsub|s>> at depth <math|k>: in the notation
    of that lemma the top level is <math|k>, the bottom level is the
    reference depth <math|l=r<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>m>, the coefficient is the
    primary of <math|e<rsub|s>>, and <math|w=1>. This is legitimate because
    the initial remainder and forced profile of a primary vanish, its format
    is the primary format, and its coefficient norm is one. The transfer
    sequence is the one of the record (R6) at <math|<around|(|N,p|)>>, and
    the doors are those of the record. Recenter the resulting backward
    coefficients by the negative of their bottom primary <math|b<rsub|s>>,
    the primary of the state at <math|r<rsub|d>>. Writing the resulting
    coefficient family as <math|Z<rsub|i>> and its remainder price as
    <math|g<rsub|i>>, the third item of the lemma gives, for
    <math|r<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>k> and in particular for
    <math|m\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>k>,

    <\equation*>
      g<rsub|i>+<around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsub|i>.*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>E<rsub|k>/G*<around|(|k,i|)>,<space|2em>Z<rsub|k>.*x=e<rsub|s>-I<rsub|k>*b<rsub|s>.
    </equation*>

    The last item of the lemma gives the terminal cone estimate
    <math|32*<around|(|1+c<rsub|k>|)><rsup|2>*<around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsub|k>.*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1+c<rsub|k>|)>>.
    Since <math|c<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>1>, it implies the stated quarter bound.

    Let <math|\<tau\><rsub|i>=w*<big|prod><rsub|j=m><rsup|i-1>\<phi\><rsub|j>>.
    The physical rescaling changes <math|a<rsub|i><rsup|-d>> by
    <math|L<rsub|i><rsup|d>> at the next step, and
    <math|G*<around|(|k,i|)><rsup|-1>> changes by at most <math|L<rsub|i>>.
    Thus the height recurrence and <eqref|eq:actual-grid-price> imply

    <\equation*>
      \<tau\><rsub|i>*a<rsub|i><rsup|-d>*E<rsub|k>/G*<around|(|k,i|)>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|d,i>/2*<space|2em><around|(|m\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>k|)>.
    </equation*>

    Moreover <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|I<rsub|k>*b<rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>1+<around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsub|k>.*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*E<rsub|k>>.
    It follows that the negative played coefficient at <math|k> costs at most
    <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,k>> with weight <math|\<tau\><rsub|k>>. This is the
    input needed for the selected insertion service above <math|k>.

    The matching at <math|k> is exact, not just a norm comparison. Linearity
    of the played exponential entry and of its forced-profile map gives

    <\equation*>
      Z<rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|exp>>=<math-up|primary><around|(|e<rsub|s>|)><rsup|<math-up|exp>>+<math-up|played><around|(|-b<rsub|s>|)><rsup|<math-up|exp>>,<space|2em>Z<rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|forced>>=<math-up|played><around|(|-b<rsub|s>|)><rsup|<math-up|forced>>.
    </equation*>

    The second identity uses the zero forced profile of the primary. Hence we
    can first insert the negative terminal observable
    <math|-O<rsub|N,p><around|(|T<rsub|N>*b<rsub|s>|)>>, transport its marked
    density down to <math|k>, and then multiply by the positive local factors
    <math|F<rsub|k,e<rsub|s>>>. The selected service of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-centered-service> applies above
    <math|k> with the just verified played-coefficient price. Adding the
    local factors changes its exponential entries to those of
    <math|Z<rsub|k>> and leaves its forced profiles unchanged.

    Below <math|k> the backward coefficients supply the adjacent density
    identities and finite-support forced-profile comparisons. We verify both
    transition prices. Put <math|D<rsub|i>=<math-up|orbitCost><rsub|d><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|i>+2*c<rsub|i+1>|)>>,
    where <math|\<psi\><rsub|i>> is the adjacent comparison price. The exact
    comparison bounds its next-level weighted remainder by
    <math|D<rsub|i>*<around|(|g<rsub|i+1>+<around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsub|i+1>.*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>|)>>;
    its lower-level remainder is <math|g<rsub|i>>. The paid room and coin
    inequalities are <math|2*D<rsub|i>*\<zeta\><rsub|d,i+1>\<leqslant\>1> and
    <math|c<rsub|i>*\<zeta\><rsub|d,i>\<leqslant\>1>. The marched height
    estimate, with the harmless factor <math|1/2> discarded, therefore gives

    <\equation*>
      2*\<tau\><rsub|i+1>*a<rsub|i+1><rsup|-d>*D<rsub|i>*<around|(|g<rsub|i+1>+<around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsub|i+1>.*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>|)>\<leqslant\>2*D<rsub|i>*\<zeta\><rsub|d,i+1>\<leqslant\>1,
    </equation*>

    and, since <math|\<tau\><rsub|i+1>\<leqslant\>\<tau\><rsub|i>> and
    <math|c<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1>,

    <\equation*>
      \<tau\><rsub|i+1>*a<rsub|i><rsup|-d>*g<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<tau\><rsub|i>*a<rsub|i><rsup|-d>*g<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|d,i>\<leqslant\>1.
    </equation*>

    The finite-support comparison is transported through the actual sound
    gauge door and physically rescaled by the exact adjacent scale identity.
    Its profiles consequently have precisely these two prices. The selected
    transition estimate now descends by induction from <math|k> to <math|m>.
    At each step the centers migrate along the same embeddings, the occupancy
    and separation estimates persist, and the pushed singleton coefficients
    equal the prescribed local densities. Thus the marked estimate
    <eqref|ex-centered-sail> of Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-construction>
    applies at <math|m>. The full polynomial representation through this
    two-stage descent, including the intermediate positive insertion and its
    mixed modes, is constructed in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:c14-selected-phase-reflection>
    below. The two prices verified above are its adjacent numerical inputs.
    Its local entry price is at most <math|2>, its forced price at most
    <math|1>, and its action allowance is <math|1>, giving total price
    <math|2+1+2=5> per cell. These are the same prices derived in the
    finite-census argument of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:actual-marked-figment>;
    the new feature here is the intermediate positive insertion and the exact
    matching just proved.

    For clarity we identify the coefficient being bounded. Let
    <math|\<cal-I\>> be the full set of <math|J>-cells and
    <math|M=<around|\||\<cal-I\>|\|>\<gtr\>0>. Put
    <math|U=O<rsub|N,p><around|(|T<rsub|N>*b<rsub|s>|)>> and
    <math|F=F<rsub|k,e<rsub|s>>>. For a subset <math|A\<subseteq\>\<cal-I\>>,
    the coefficient after the insertion at <math|k> is

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|B\<subseteq\>A><math-ss|P><rsub|k,N><around*|(|e<rsup|-S<rsub|N>>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>A\<setminus\>B><around|(|-\<cal-C\><rsub|i><rsup|N>*U|)>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>B>\<cal-C\><rsub|i><rsup|k>*F.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|e<rsup|-S<rsub|N>>> denotes the unnormalized Wilson density
    and <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|i><rsup|j>> the centered copy at depth <math|j>,
    including its conjugation when prescribed by the tiling. Duality,
    <eqref|eq:actual-grid-factor>, and commutation with copies show that its
    integral is

    <\equation*>
      Z<rsub|N,p>*<space|0.17em>\<bbb-E\><rsub|N,p>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>A>\<cal-C\><rsub|i><rsup|N>*<around|(|<math-ss|B><rsub|k,N>F-U|)>.
    </equation*>

    This follows by expanding the product over all subsets <math|B>; the
    negative old factors are retained together inside their pushed density.
    All integrals exist because the terminal fields are continuous on compact
    configuration spaces and <math|F> is bounded measurable. Further density
    transport to <math|m> preserves this integral, coefficient by
    coefficient.

    For <math|A=\<cal-I\>>, denote the last expectation by <math|\<Omega\>>.
    Applying the marked estimate and dividing by the positive original
    partition function <math|Z<rsub|N,p>> gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<Omega\>|\|>*w<rsup|M>\<leqslant\>exp
      <around*|(|<around|\||<math-up|Site><around|(|m,p|)>|\|>*\<cal-T\><rsub|m>+5*M|)>.
    </equation*>

    The aligned four-dimensional count is
    <math|<around|\||<math-up|Site><around|(|m,p|)>|\|>=M*<around|(|a<rsub|J>/a<rsub|m>|)><rsup|4>>.
    Taking the <math|M>th root proves <math|<around|\||\<Omega\>|\|><rsup|1/M>\<leqslant\>S<rsub|J,m>/w>,
    which is precisely the asserted root test. No change of law or extraction
    order has entered the calculation.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Arbitrary real labels and a common coarse depth>

  For a label <math|L> of grade <math|d>, put
  <math|K<rsub|k><around|(|L|)>=2*e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|k>><around|\<\|\|\>|L|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>.
  The vectors <math|I<rsub|k>*b<rsub|s>> in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:actual-grid-basis> form a basis by
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-public-frame-recovery>. If
  <math|t=T<rsub|N><rsup|-1>*L>, solve <math|<big|sum><rsub|s>v<rsub|s>*I<rsub|k>*b<rsub|s>=I<rsub|k>*t>.
  Then

  <\equation*>
    L=<big|sum><rsub|s>v<rsub|s>*T<rsub|N>*b<rsub|s>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|s><around|\||v<rsub|s>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*<around|\<\|\|\>|I<rsub|k>*t|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|k><around|(|L|)>.
  </equation*>

  The last inequality is the backward coefficient budget of the same selected
  history. Define <math|B<rsup|\<bbb-C\>><rsub|N,p>=<big|sum><rsub|s>v<rsub|s><math-ss|B><rsub|k,N>F<rsub|k,e<rsub|s>>>.
  Its supremum norm is at most <math|K<rsub|k><around|(|L|)>*A<rsub|k>>. The
  room condition puts every summand in the origin chamber of the
  <math|J>-tiling; eventually the physical observables of this fixed grade
  also lie there. The rooted triangle inequality on chamber-interior fields
  therefore gives

  <\equation*>
    \<rho\><rsub|J;N,p>*<around|(|B<rsup|\<bbb-C\>><rsub|N,p>-O<rsub|N,p><around|(|L|)>|)>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|k><around|(|L|)>*S<rsub|J,m>/w.
  </equation*>

  The coefficients <math|v<rsub|s>> may depend on <math|N,p>; their common
  bound makes the supremum estimate uniform in these indices.

  Choose

  <\equation*>
    w<rsub|k>=<frac|\<zeta\><rsub|d,m>*G*<around|(|k,m|)>|2*a<rsub|m><rsup|-d>*E<rsub|k>>.
  </equation*>

  Then <eqref|eq:actual-grid-price> is an equality. The column estimates
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|k>=o<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|k>|)>> and <math|log
  <around|(|1+c<rsub|k>|)>=o<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|k>|)>> imply, by
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-public-radial-choice>,

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|k><around|(|L|)>*<space|0.17em>S<rsub|J,m>/w<rsub|k>\<longrightarrow\>0*<space|2em><around|(|k\<to\>\<infty\>|)>.
  </equation*>

  In this limit <math|J,m,d,L> are fixed. Thus we can choose <math|k> to meet
  both the room condition and any prescribed error
  <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, and subsequently take <math|N\<gtr\>k> on
  the original late set.

  If <math|L> is star-real, the exact reality identity makes
  <math|O<rsub|N,p><around|(|L|)>> real. Take
  <math|B<rsub|N,p>=<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>B<rsup|\<bbb-C\>><rsub|N,p>>.
  It has the same supremum bound and chamber support. Since the root test is
  invariant under conjugation and subadditive on these fields,

  <\equation*>
    \<rho\><rsub|J;N,p>*<around|(|B<rsub|N,p>-O<rsub|N,p><around|(|L|)>|)>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>\<rho\><rsub|J;N,p>*<around|(|B<rsup|\<bbb-C\>><rsub|N,p>-O<rsub|N,p><around|(|L|)>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>\<rho\><rsub|J;N,p><around|(|<wide|B<rsup|\<bbb-C\>><rsub|N,p>-O<rsub|N,p><around|(|L|)>|\<bar\>>|)>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>.
  </equation*>

  Here real fields are identified with their complex-valued inclusions.

  For a finite grade ceiling <math|D> and a requested coarse depth
  <math|J<rsub|0>>, choose

  <\equation*>
    J=max <around|{|J<rsub|0>,r<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,r<rsub|D>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Use the opening schedule once to select <math|m\<geqslant\>J> with the
  required opening. The preceding construction then applies to every
  star-real label of grade at most <math|D> and each accuracy
  <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|q>=<around|(|q+1|)><rsup|-1>>. Its approximation
  depth and supremum bound may depend on the label and <math|q>, but its
  coarse depth <math|J> and extraction order do not. This is the coherent
  family of origin approximations needed for the comparison grid.

  <subsubsection|From origin approximations to whole grid words>

  The same origin approximations must be used for all translated and
  reflected copies. For a real label <math|v> and a copy of spin frame
  <math|s>, first apply the inverse spin frame to <math|v>, then copy its
  origin approximation. The exact covariance identity turns the corresponding
  copied physical field into the field labelled by <math|v> at the desired
  site. A conjugated copy of a real approximant has the same value as its
  ordinary real pullback. All transformed labels remain real. Since the
  spin-frame set is finite, summing their supremum bounds gives a bound
  <math|M<rsub|v,q>> valid for every cell at accuracy <math|q>.

  There is also an accuracy-independent bound <math|A<rsub|v>> on the root
  tests of the physical origin fields in these finitely many frames. This is
  the one place where the centered one-field estimate enters: it is the
  uniform centered power bound of Theorem<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-main-bound>,
  used here as an input on the same family and extraction order, together
  with the reflection-positive transfer structure of the finite-volume laws.
  To obtain <math|A<rsub|v>>, choose a finer depth <math|j\<geqslant\>J> with
  <math|9*a<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|J>> and use the centered one-field
  estimate at that depth. The origin is in the margin of width
  <math|3*a<rsub|j>> of its <math|J>-chamber. The centered comparison
  estimate embeds the fine test into this chamber and bounds its root test
  there. Sum these bounds over the finite spin frames. For each fixed
  <math|q>, intersect their finitely many late sets with those for the origin
  approximations. We obtain, for every cell, chamber-interior fields
  <math|X<rsub|v>,Y<rsub|v>> satisfying

  <\equation*>
    \<rho\><around|(|X<rsub|v>|)>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|v>,<space|2em>\<rho\>*<around|(|Y<rsub|v>-X<rsub|v>|)>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|q>,<space|2em><around|\||\<cal-C\><rsub|i>*Y<rsub|v>|\|>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|v,q>.
  </equation*>

  The copies of <math|X<rsub|v>> are exactly the physical fields. The
  prescription for <math|Y<rsub|v>> depends only on the label, accuracy and
  cell frame, and thus works simultaneously for arbitrary finite layouts.

  For a word <math|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|n>>, set
  <math|M=1+<big|sum><rsub|i>A<rsub|v<rsub|i>>>. Since
  <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|q>\<leqslant\>1>, both
  <math|\<rho\><around|(|X<rsub|v<rsub|i>>|)>> and
  <math|\<rho\><around|(|Y<rsub|v<rsub|i>>|)>> are at most <math|M>. For any
  injective layout in the actual finite torus,
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-public-replace-word> gives

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||\<bbb-E\><rsub|N,p>*<big|prod><rsub|i>\<cal-C\><rsub|x<rsub|i>>*Y<rsub|v<rsub|i>>-\<bbb-E\><rsub|N,p>*<big|prod><rsub|i>\<cal-C\><rsub|x<rsub|i>>*X<rsub|v<rsub|i>>|\|>\<leqslant\>n*\<varepsilon\><rsub|q>*M<rsup|n-1>.
  </equation*>

  The new word is real, measurable and bounded by
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|i>M<rsub|v<rsub|i>,q>>. This bound holds even for
  colliding layouts; only the error estimate needs injectivity. An empty word
  has error zero.

  Finally consider two fixed injective integer-grid layouts. Translate their
  copies numbered <math|i\<less\>j> by <math|t*i*<around|(|1,1,1,1|)>> and
  <math|t*j*<around|(|1,1,1,1|)>>. For all sufficiently large <math|t>, their
  first coordinates separate, so their union is injective. For each such
  fixed <math|t>, eventual growth of the period makes the union injective on
  the torus as well. Apply the preceding word estimate to the concatenated
  labels. Taking real parts gives the required comparison with the real part
  of the <em|whole> complex product, since
  <math|<around|\||<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>z|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||z|\|>>
  and integration commutes with real parts. The order of choices is accuracy,
  pair of copy numbers, sufficiently large separation, then the original late
  cutoff/period limit.

  Apply this construction to each of the four pairs of plain and reflected
  words. The difference between their two bounded real approximants has
  supremum bound equal to the sum of their individual bounds. Expanding the
  product of two such differences gives four terms, with total error at most
  <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|q>> times the sum of the four
  accuracy-independent word constants. This tends to zero.
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-public-grill> therefore applies on the same
  extraction order and the same coarse grid. This supplies its local
  approximation and separated-pair inputs from the paid selected history,
  rather than assuming those approximations as an additional phase
  hypothesis.

  <subsection|A fixed-coupling history for the rank
  comparison><label|sec:restarted-rank-history>

  The rank tests compare the interacting fields with a free field over a
  short interval of scales. They require an auxiliary integration history.
  The coupling is held fixed on that interval, and the local additive
  remainder is controlled in value. We give the density construction and its
  bounds here. The calibrated history constructed earlier supplies the
  endpoint and the kernels; it is still the history defining the fields.

  <subsubsection|The additive remainder and its exact density factor>

  On a finite torus <math|\<Lambda\>>, write a representation's density as

  <\equation*>
    R<around|(|U|)>=exp <around|{|v\|\<Lambda\>\|-A<around|(|U|)>-E<around|(|U|)>|}>*<space|0.17em>G<around|(|U|)>,<space|2em>E<around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>E<rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|A> is its path action, <math|G> its forced gas with the
  prescribed supports, inventories and eligibility tests, and
  <math|E<rsub|\<alpha\>>> are real, measurable, gauge-invariant local
  functions: their values agree when the local links and the relevant final
  flag data agree. Each <math|E<rsub|\<alpha\>>> vanishes unless its support
  <math|X<rsub|\<alpha\>>> is healthy at the representation's threshold. Give
  <math|X<rsub|\<alpha\>>> an anchor <math|a<rsub|\<alpha\>>\<in\>X<rsub|\<alpha\>>>.
  Suppose <math|b<rsub|\<alpha\>>\<geqslant\>0> majorizes
  <math|<around|\||E<rsub|\<alpha\>>|\|>> and, for the representation's
  support weight <math|M<around|(|X|)>\<geqslant\>1>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:restart-anchored><big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>:a<rsub|\<alpha\>>=x>M<around|(|X<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>*b<rsub|\<alpha\>>\<leqslant\>q*<space|1em><around|(|x\<in\>\<Lambda\>|)>.
  </equation>

  The sums in this inequality are assumed summable. Since the anchor set is
  finite, summing it gives

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>><around|\||E<rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*q.
  </equation*>

  Thus the sum defining <math|E> converges absolutely, is measurable and
  gauge invariant, and <math|e<rsup|-E>> has the finite-volume bound
  <math|e<rsup|<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*q>>. Let <math|R<rsup|0>> be
  obtained by deleting just the additive terms. Its paths, coupling, scalar,
  forced activities and their tests are unchanged. Then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:restart-white>R=e<rsup|-E>*R<rsup|0>.
  </equation>

  For any finite family of source variables, the same identity holds for its
  polynomial-valued density. Consequently the integrability of that density
  follows from the corresponding envelope for <math|R<rsup|0>>. The estimate
  <math|e<rsup|<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*q>> is used only for finite-volume
  integrability; it is not used as a uniform bound in the volume limit.

  <subsubsection|A local multiplier keeps the expansion on the actual
  density>

  Here is the local operation behind the construction. For a masked additive
  term <math|E<rsub|\<alpha\>>>, insert the letter

  <\equation*>
    h<rsub|\<alpha\>>=e<rsup|-E<rsub|\<alpha\>>>-1.
  </equation*>

  The identity <math|<big|prod><rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|1+h<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>=e<rsup|-E>>
  is exact. Its infinite version follows from absolute summability, since
  <math|<around|\||e<rsup|-t>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<around|\||t|\|>><around|\||t|\|>>
  and <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>b<rsub|\<alpha\>>\<less\>\<infty\>>. If
  the weighted footprint estimate for these letters has allowance
  <math|\<omega\>>, attach them to the same component alphabet as the
  fluctuation integral. The component estimate of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mixed-activity-prices> and the reassembly
  identity of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-reassembly> apply with
  this enlarged alphabet. The old forced labels and their eligibility
  conditions are retained. In particular the resulting identity integrates
  <math|e<rsup|-E>*R<rsup|0>=R>. This construction cannot be replaced by
  integrating <math|R<rsup|0>> alone.

  For later use, denote by <math|\<delta\>> the weighted allowance of the
  unmarked coarse letters, including the multiplier letters. The regular-gas
  estimate of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:forest-average-log>, at the
  purchased support exponent, supplies a masked logarithmic family
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|\<gamma\>>> with anchored cost at most
  <math|16*\<delta\>>, and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:restart-log>G<rsub|<math-up|reg>><around|(|U|)>=exp
    <around|{|-<big|sum><rsub|\<gamma\>>\<ell\><rsub|\<gamma\>><around|(|U|)>|}>.
  </equation>

  The elementary numerical conditions used at this step include

  <\equation*>
    16*\<delta\>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>e<rsup|2*\<delta\>>-1\<leqslant\>1-e<rsup|-16*\<delta\>>,<space|2em>16*\<delta\>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-R\><around|(|B|)>\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<cal-R\><around|(|B|)>> is the connected-support counting
  constant in the regular logarithm bound, and <math|B> is the coarse support
  radius. They are imposed on the same letter prices, not on a different
  auxiliary normalization. If the integrand has holes around marked sites,
  the forced-exclusion identity gives the corresponding localized logarithms
  with those holes. The scalar identity without holes and these identities
  with holes therefore have the same normalization.

  Choose the new coarse scalar from the positive fluctuation prefactor. If
  the prefactor is <math|C\<gtr\>0>, its contribution to the scalar per
  coarse site is <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|c>|\|><rsup|-1>*log C>.
  Positivity follows from the positive coupling, the positive chart ratio,
  and the positive determinant of the flat precision. In particular this
  logarithm does not choose a branch. Let <math|A<rsub|c>> be the retained
  coarse path action and <math|\<beta\>*B<rsub|c>> the reference action used
  in the fluctuation formula. Its local correction is
  <math|\<beta\>*B<rsub|c>-A<rsub|c>>. Add its masked local pieces to
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|\<gamma\>>>. If the former have anchored cost
  <math|\<epsilon\>>, the combined family has cost

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:restart-newcost>\<epsilon\>+16*\<delta\>.
  </equation>

  Indeed the two nonnegative majorant sums add at every anchor. The identity
  for its exponential is exactly

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|v<rsub|c>\|\<Lambda\><rsub|c>\|-\<beta\>*B<rsub|c>>*<space|0.17em>G<rsub|<math-up|reg>>=e<rsup|v<rsub|c>\|\<Lambda\><rsub|c>\|-A<rsub|c>-E<rsub|c>>.
  </equation*>

  Choose the coarse forced activities by the addresswise construction of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:corrected-forced-output>: attach the hole
  and boundary factors to the unmarked forced components, sum at each full
  support and flag inventory, and store those sums as the output's archived
  activities. The regular/forced decomposition of the same expansion then
  gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:restart-push>R<rsub|c>=\<cal-K\><rsub|\<beta\>>*R<rsub|f>
  </equation>

  on every coarse configuration, including configurations with flags. To
  check this last assertion, first keep the source polynomial in the finite
  expansion. Its coefficient of the empty inventory equals the unmarked
  scalar density. The reassembly and exclusion identities hold
  coefficientwise and preserve exact flag coverage. Setting the source family
  empty therefore identifies their scalar coefficients on every
  configuration. There is no division by a density or by a forced activity in
  this argument.

  <subsubsection|Symmetrizing the additive family>

  The newly obtained sum <math|E<rsub|c>> is invariant under signed lattice
  motions, but an individual choice of anchored pieces need not have a
  symmetric indexing. Let <math|H> be the finite group of these motions on
  the torus. Replace a piece <math|f<rsub|\<alpha\>>> by the family

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||H|\|><rsup|-1>*f<rsub|\<alpha\>>*<around|(|g*U|)>,<space|2em><around|(|g,\<alpha\>|)>\<in\>H\<times\>\<cal-I\>,
  </equation*>

  with the correspondingly transported support and anchor. For fixed
  <math|g>, the anchored bound is the old bound at the inverse image of the
  anchor. Multiplication by <math|<around|\||H|\|><rsup|-1>> and summation
  over <math|g> preserve that bound. Absolute summability permits interchange
  of the finite group sum and the piece sum. Since the total was invariant,
  the new total is <math|<around|\||H|\|><rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|g>E<rsub|c>*<around|(|g*U|)>=E<rsub|c><around|(|U|)>>.
  Left multiplication in <math|H> permutes the new indices, giving the
  required covariance of individual addresses. Reindex this countable family
  and, if necessary, add zero terms. The gas and all forced addresses are
  unchanged. This produces the locality, measurability and motion properties
  together with <eqref|eq:restart-push>.

  <subsubsection|The cost over a short interval>

  Fix a block ratio <math|L\<gtr\>1> and a width parameter
  <math|b\<geqslant\>0>. For large <math|s> put

  <\equation*>
    y=log log s,<space|1em>A<rsub|s>=e<rsup|y<rsup|3>>,<space|1em>q<rsub|n>=s<rsup|-1/200>*A<rsub|s><rsup|4*n>,<space|1em>\<omega\><rsub|n>=q<rsub|n>*A<rsub|s>,<space|1em>\<delta\><rsub|n>=q<rsub|n>*A<rsub|s><rsup|3>.
  </equation*>

  The two parts of the comparison interval have lengths
  <math|h<rsub|1>,h<rsub|2>>. Their definitions are the ceilings

  <\equation*>
    h<rsub|1>=<around*|\<lceil\>|<frac|<around|(|b+2|)>*y<rsup|2>|log
    L>|\<rceil\>>,<space|2em>h<rsub|2>=<around*|\<lceil\>|<frac|log
    <around|(|M<rsub|s><rsup|10>|)>|log L>|\<rceil\>>,<space|2em>h=h<rsub|1>+h<rsub|2>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|M<rsub|s>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
  s|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>; the support wall is <math|M<rsub|s><rsup|2>>. The
  support-radius estimates give <math|log
  <around|(|M<rsub|s><rsup|10>|)>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|2>> eventually. Thus
  <math|h\<leqslant\>C<rsub|L,b>*y<rsup|2>+2\<leqslant\>y<rsup|4>>
  eventually. For every fixed integer <math|r\<geqslant\>0>, uniformly for
  <math|0\<leqslant\>n\<leqslant\>h>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:restart-decay>q<rsub|n>*A<rsub|s><rsup|r>\<leqslant\>s<rsup|-1/400>
  </equation>

  for all sufficiently large <math|s>. In fact its logarithm is at most
  <math|-<around|(|log s|)>/200+<around|(|4*y<rsup|4>+r|)>*y<rsup|3>>. For
  <math|y\<geqslant\>1> the positive term is at most
  <math|<around|(|4+r|)>*y<rsup|7>>. Since <math|y<rsup|7>*e<rsup|-y>\<to\>0>
  and <math|log s=e<rsup|y>>, it is at most <math|<around|(|log s|)>/400>
  eventually, proving the claim.

  The footprint price of an additive input with cost <math|q<rsub|n>> is at
  most

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|L,a,t>*<around|(|42*B+1|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|n>,
  </equation*>

  where the support reserve <math|t> is strictly positive, <math|a> is the
  fixed support exponent, and <math|B\<leqslant\>M<rsub|s><rsup|2>>. All
  factors except the displayed radius and <math|q<rsub|n>> are fixed before
  <math|s> is increased. The wall estimates imply
  <math|C<rsub|L,a,t>*<around|(|42*M<rsub|s><rsup|2>+1|)><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|s>>
  eventually. Hence the multiplier letters cost at most
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|n>>. The remaining unmarked letters have their
  purchased local bounds; their combined logarithm is allowed cost
  <math|16*\<delta\><rsub|n>>. The path correction costs at most
  <math|s<rsup|-1/200>>. These bounds close the recurrence because, as soon
  as <math|A<rsub|s>\<geqslant\>17>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:restart-recurrence>s<rsup|-1/200>+16*\<delta\><rsub|n>\<leqslant\>q<rsub|n+1>.
  </equation>

  Indeed <math|s<rsup|-1/200>\<leqslant\>q<rsub|n>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|n>>
  and <math|q<rsub|n+1>=A<rsub|s>*\<delta\><rsub|n>>.

  For clarity, the small exponential conditions also hold uniformly through
  this interval. If <math|0\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>1>, then
  <math|e<rsup|t>-1\<leqslant\>2*t>. Consequently
  <math|e<rsup|2*\<delta\><rsub|n>>-1\<leqslant\>4*\<delta\><rsub|n>>. If
  <math|0\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|n>\<leqslant\>1/16>, the inequalities
  <math|e<rsup|16*\<delta\><rsub|n>>\<geqslant\>1+16*\<delta\><rsub|n>> and
  <math|<around|(|1+16*\<delta\><rsub|n>|)><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>1-4*\<delta\><rsub|n>>
  give

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|2*\<delta\><rsub|n>>-1\<leqslant\>1-e<rsup|-16*\<delta\><rsub|n>>.
  </equation*>

  The second rational inequality follows after multiplying by its positive
  denominator: <math|12*\<delta\><rsub|n>-64*\<delta\><rsub|n><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>0>.
  The factors <math|\<cal-R\><around|(|B|)>>, the insertion inflation and any
  fixed comparison polynomial have at most the purchased powers of
  <math|e<rsup|y<rsup|2>>>; multiplying <eqref|eq:restart-decay> by such
  factors still gives zero in the limit. The forced-gas allowance is smaller
  than every negative power of <math|s>, uniformly for the local scale
  parameter in <math|<around|[|s/2,s|]>>. It therefore pays the
  support-counting factors in the normalization-with-holes estimates. All
  these are eventual conditions after <math|L,b> and the finite menu have
  been fixed.

  <subsubsection|Re-expressing the starting
  density><label|subsec:restart-endpoint-construction>

  We first supply the stronger forced allowance at the starting level. This
  is a change of presentation on one torus, before applying a blocking
  kernel. Write <math|c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>> and <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>>
  for the two positive coefficients of its clip; the clip condition includes
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>\<less\>c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>/16>. Fix a
  positive path rate <math|\<sigma\>>, a path coefficient bound <math|C>, and
  a support exponent <math|a\<gtr\>0>. All these parameters are fixed before
  the scale <math|s> tends to infinity. In particular, each quadratic path
  row has <math|<big|sum><rsub|j><around|\||c<rsub|j>|\|>*e<rsup|\<sigma\><space|0.17em><math-up|length><rsub|j>>\<leqslant\>C>;
  each cubic and quartic atom family has the corresponding absolute
  true-length coefficient sum at most <math|C>. The loop family has that
  bound with <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|s>> in place of <math|C>. The forced gas
  uses its full support and inventory addresses with the original eligibility
  and exclusion tests; its stated allowance is an absolute weighted site-sum
  bound. Put

  <\gather*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|H<rsub|s>=exp <around|(|<around|(|log log
    s|)><rsup|2>|)>,<space|1em>h<rsub|s>=exp <around|(|<around|(|log log
    s|)><rsup|3/2>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|t=s<rsup|-1/2>*H<rsub|s>,<space|1em>\<tau\>=s<rsup|-1/2>*h<rsub|s>,<space|1em>M=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    s|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>,<space|1em>B=M<rsup|2>.>>>>
  </gather*>

  The old budget has radius <math|M> and threshold <math|t>; the new one has
  radius <math|B> and threshold <math|\<tau\>>. Their support exponent and
  derivative order are unchanged. Denote the corresponding masked additive
  sums by <math|E<rsub|t>,E<rsub|\<tau\>>> and path actions by
  <math|A<rsub|t>,A<rsub|\<tau\>>>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Exact endpoint re-expression><label|prop:restart-endpoint>Suppose
    the original endpoint has coupling <math|s/2\<leqslant\>\<beta\>\<leqslant\>2*s>,
    the fixed path and clip bounds above, quadratic-loop coefficient bound
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|s>\<leqslant\>2*H<rsub|s>>, additive allowance
    <math|s<rsup|-11/10>>, and forced allowance <math|w<rsub|s>=exp
    <around|(|-H<rsub|s><rsup|1/5>|)>>. Fix
    <math|0\<less\>a<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>/20>. For all
    sufficiently large <math|s>, uniformly in the period and endpoint field,
    there is a presentation of exactly the same density with the same path,
    clip, coupling and scalar, budget <math|<around|(|B,\<tau\>|)>>, additive
    allowance at most <math|s<rsup|-11/10>>, and forced allowance

    <\equation*>
      G<around|(|s|)>=e<rsup|-a<rsub|0>*h<rsub|s><rsup|2>>.
    </equation*>

    It retains the original additive coefficients with their new masks. In
    particular its additive value allowance is at most
    <math|q<rsub|0>=s<rsup|-1/200>>, and deleting those coefficients gives
    the neutral incoming law needed by the local compiler.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    We construct all factors before grouping them. A flagged site at
    threshold <math|\<tau\>> is a site touched by a plaquette whose chord
    exceeds <math|\<tau\>>. Write <math|F<rsub|\<tau\>><around|(|U|)>> for
    this set. Eventually <math|2*\<tau\>\<leqslant\>t>. For a plaquette chord
    <math|r> put

    <\equation*>
      d<rsub|t><around|(|r|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>*r<rsup|2>/2,>|<cell|r\<leqslant\>t,>>|<row|<cell|c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>*t*r,>|<cell|r\<gtr\>t,>>>>><space|2em>\<Delta\><around|(|r|)>=d<rsub|t><around|(|r|)>-d<rsub|\<tau\>><around|(|r|)>.
    </equation*>

    Then <math|\<Delta\>\<geqslant\>0>, and
    <math|\<Delta\><around|(|r|)>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>*\<tau\><rsup|2>>
    for <math|r\<gtr\>\<tau\>>. On <math|\<tau\>\<less\>r\<leqslant\>t> this
    follows from <math|\<Delta\><around|(|r|)>=c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>*r<rsup|2>/2-c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>*\<tau\>*r\<geqslant\><around|(|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>/2-c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>|)>*r<rsup|2>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>*\<tau\><rsup|2>>;
    on <math|r\<gtr\>t> use <math|t-\<tau\>\<geqslant\>\<tau\>>. Distribute
    <math|\<Delta\><around|(|r|)>> equally among the sites touched by its
    plaquette. There are at most five such sites, including on a wrapping
    torus. The resulting local charges <math|d<rsub|x>> satisfy

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|x\<in\>F<rsub|\<tau\>>>d<rsub|x>=<big|sum><rsub|<text|plaquettes
      >p>\<Delta\><around|(|r<rsub|p>|)>,<space|2em>d<rsub|x>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>*\<tau\><rsup|2>/5*<space|1em><around|(|x\<in\>F<rsub|\<tau\>>|)>.
    </equation*>

    A plaquette with nonzero charge has all its touched sites in
    <math|F<rsub|\<tau\>>>, which proves the equality without discarding
    charges at healthy sites.

    The other quadratic contribution to <math|A<rsub|t>-A<rsub|\<tau\>>> is
    the squared path row on the event that the old kept test holds and the
    new one fails. Truncate the row at true path length <math|B>, before
    squaring, and call its resulting nonnegative half-square <math|b<rsub|x>>
    at anchor <math|x>, including that event indicator and the signed-channel
    average. The kept test at threshold <math|\<tau\>> reads only a
    radius-<math|B> neighborhood once <math|2\<leqslant\>\<tau\>*exp
    <around|(|B/c<rsub|<math-up|relax>>|)>>: beyond that neighborhood the
    chord bound two makes its test automatic. Thus <math|b<rsub|x>> reads at
    most a radius-<math|2*B> neighborhood, and vanishes if the
    radius-<math|B> neighborhood is <math|\<tau\>>-healthy. Distribute
    <math|b<rsub|x>> equally among the sites of <math|F<rsub|\<tau\>>> in
    that neighborhood. If there are none, <math|b<rsub|x>=0> and no division
    is made. Summing these shares gives nonnegative charges
    <math|b<rprime|'><rsub|z>>, with <math|<big|sum><rsub|z>b<rprime|'><rsub|z>=<big|sum><rsub|x>b<rsub|x>>,
    supported within radius <math|4*B> of <math|z>. Perform the same
    operation for each signed channel with its original averaging weight.

    Set <math|u=a<rsub|0>*h<rsub|s><rsup|2>> and, at a flagged site, set

    <\equation*>
      X<rsub|z><around|(|U|)>=exp <around|[|-\<beta\>*<around|(|d<rsub|z><around|(|U|)>+b<rprime|'><rsub|z><around|(|U|)>|)>|]>;
    </equation*>

    set <math|X<rsub|z>=0> when the site is unflagged. Since
    <math|\<beta\>*\<tau\><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>h<rsub|s><rsup|2>/2> and
    <math|a<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>/20>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:restart-endpoint-signal><around|\||X<rsub|z>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-2*u>,<space|2em><big|prod><rsub|z\<in\>F<rsub|\<tau\>>>X<rsub|z>=exp
      <around|[|-\<beta\>*<big|sum><rsub|z><around|(|d<rsub|z>+b<rprime|'><rsub|z>|)>|]>.
    </equation>

    In particular each selected site factor supplies one factor
    <math|e<rsup|-u>> which can be reserved for its connected component.

    Here are the remaining exponent changes and their prices. Write

    <\equation*>
      D=-<around|(|A<rsub|t>-A<rsub|\<tau\>>|)>+\<beta\>*<big|sum><rsub|z><around|(|d<rsub|z>+b<rprime|'><rsub|z>|)>+<around|(|E<rsub|\<tau\>>-E<rsub|t>|)>.
    </equation*>

    This is the sum of the omitted quadratic row pairs, the differences of
    the cubic, quartic and loop masks, and the difference of the additive
    masks. Each summand has a nonempty full read support and vanishes when
    that support is <math|\<tau\>>-healthy. Their absolute site price at any
    fixed enlarged exponent <math|a+2> is bounded by

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:restart-endpoint-error>\<epsilon\><rsub|s>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|1>*B<rsup|C<rsub|2>>*<around*|[|\<beta\>*C<rsup|2>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*B/4>+\<Gamma\><rsub|s>*t<rsup|2>+\<beta\>*C*<around|(|t<rsup|3>+t<rsup|4>|)>+s<rsup|-11/10>|]>=s<rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>.
    </equation>

    The constants depend only on the fixed profile and exponent. To verify
    this bound, expand the difference of a full square and its truncated
    square as ordered pairs of path labels, with at least one length
    exceeding <math|B>. Extract <math|e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*B/4>> from the two
    absolute exponential row sums after paying their full path supports, as
    in <eqref|eq:inherited-tail-budget>. The kept-test support adds a
    radius-<math|2*B> halo, whose cover cost is fixed. The difference of two
    nested masks has absolute value at most the old masked atom bound; for an
    order-<math|j> atom this is its coefficient times <math|t<rsup|j>>. The
    full path support has <math|B>-cover at most
    <math|1+2*j+<math-up|length>/B>, so <math|<around|(|a+2|)>/B\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>/2>
    eventually leaves a positive length reserve. For an old additive support
    <math|Y>, the connected-cover construction gives

    <\equation*>
      c<rsub|B><around|(|<math-up|halo><rsub|8><space|0.17em>Y|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|3>+C<rsub|3>*<around|(|M/B|)>*c<rsub|M><around|(|Y|)>.
    </equation*>

    Take <math|s> large enough that <math|C<rsub|3>*<around|(|a+3|)>*M/B\<leqslant\>a>.
    This converts its old weighted bound to the new one. Converting anchors
    to site hits costs a polynomial in <math|B>, by the shell sum in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:inherited-activity-prices>, with the extra
    unit of cover exponent reserved for this conversion. The same calculation
    prices the halo-enlarged old forced labels by
    <math|C<rsub|1>*B<rsup|C<rsub|2>>*w<rsub|s>>. These arguments retain all
    label multiplicities and true path lengths. Finally
    <math|H<rsub|s>=s<rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>>, giving the last equality in
    <eqref|eq:restart-endpoint-error>.

    Coalesce the summands of <math|D> with the same full support, giving
    <math|D<rsub|Y>>. There are finitely many supports on this torus, and the
    coalescing is absolutely convergent by the site bound. For large
    <math|s>, each <math|<around|\||D<rsub|Y>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|s>\<leqslant\>1/4>.
    Put <math|W<rsub|Y>=e<rsup|D<rsub|Y>>-1>. Then

    <\equation*>
      e<rsup|D>=<big|prod><rsub|Y><around|(|1+W<rsub|Y>|)>,<space|2em><around|\||W<rsub|Y>|\|>\<leqslant\>2<around|\||D<rsub|Y>|\|>,
    </equation*>

    and <math|W<rsub|Y>=0> on a <math|\<tau\>>-healthy support. Combining
    with <eqref|eq:restart-endpoint-signal> gives the exact identity

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:restart-endpoint-factor>e<rsup|-A<rsub|t>-E<rsub|t>>=e<rsup|-A<rsub|\<tau\>>-E<rsub|\<tau\>>>*<big|prod><rsub|Y><around|(|1+W<rsub|Y>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|z\<in\>F<rsub|\<tau\>>>X<rsub|z>.
    </equation>

    Multiply by the original forced gas and expand the optional factors.
    Enlarge old forced supports by eight sites to read their original
    eligibility tests; retain those tests and their original mutual
    exclusions inside every term. Form connected components using
    intersections of these enlarged supports, the <math|Y>'s and the
    radius-<math|4*B> supports of <math|X<rsub|z>>. Every nonzero component
    contains a selected <math|X<rsub|z>>: a nonzero <math|W<rsub|Y>> meets a
    new flag, and an old forced label contains an old flag, which is also a
    new flag. The factor for that flag has intersecting support and is
    present in the product. Record as the new inventory exactly the selected
    flag sites in each component, requiring it to equal
    <math|F<rsub|\<tau\>>> on that component's support. Require also that the
    union of old inventories is exactly the set of old flags on this
    component's support, and retain each old label's eligibility test. The
    components then have disjoint supports and cover all new flags.
    Conversely their constituents recover the original compatible forced
    collection and optional subset uniquely. This proves equality of the two
    gas sums, first for finite label families. The old absolute site bound,
    the finite-volume sum of the optional prices, and the connected majorants
    below permit passage to countable families by absolute convergence.

    After reserving <math|e<rsup|-u>> per selected site factor, the combined
    weighted site price is at most

    <\equation*>
      \<rho\><rsub|s>=2*\<epsilon\><rsub|s>+C<rsub|1>*B<rsup|C<rsub|2>>*w<rsub|s>+<around|(|8*B+1|)><rsup|4>*e<rsup|<around|(|a+1|)>*9<rsup|4>>*e<rsup|-u>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed a radius-<math|4*B> ball has <math|B>-cover at most
    <math|9<rsup|4>>, and at most <math|<around|(|8*B+1|)><rsup|4>> of these
    balls contain a given site. Here <math|\<rho\><rsub|s>=s<rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>>,
    so <math|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*\<rho\><rsub|s>\<leqslant\>1> and
    <math|e<rsup|\<rho\><rsub|s>>-1\<leqslant\>1> eventually. The rooted
    connected-set estimate proved in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-mixed-marked-compiler>,
    using one unit of support reserve, bounds the resulting forced site sum
    by <math|e<rsup|-u>*<around|(|e<rsup|\<rho\><rsub|s>>-1|)>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-u>>.
    No enumeration of possible flag subsets costs an additional factor: the
    selected site labels already specify the inventory, and its equality test
    only removes terms. Sum the resulting majorants and values at each full
    support and inventory address to obtain the new forced activities.

    All constructions preserve locality, measurability and gauge symmetry.
    Signed motions permute their labels; averaging the original channel
    convention, or reindexing each full orbit, preserves the bounds. The old
    regular coefficients need no replacement. Their derivative bounds
    restrict to the smaller healthy domain and smaller allowed coordinate
    directions, while increasing the cover radius decreases its weight. They
    therefore retain their old additive allowance.
    Equation<nbsp><reference|eq:restart-endpoint-factor> and the gas
    bijection prove exact equality of densities with unchanged scalar and
    coupling. Deleting the additive coefficients leaves the same path and
    forced bounds and zero additive cost, proving the final assertion.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Joining to the endpoint>

  Choose a comparison depth <math|k> and cutoff <math|N\<geqslant\>k>. Above
  <math|k>, retain the original history. At <math|k>, use its actual
  representation in the chosen local presentation. Below <math|k>, set the
  coupling identically <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>> and apply the preceding
  construction down to the lower edge of the comparison interval. The local
  scale parameter lies in <math|<around|[|s/2,s|]>>, with <math|s> the clock
  coupling at <math|k>, and the endpoint bound
  <math|<around|\||\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>-s|\|>\<leqslant\>s/8> places this
  constant coupling in the purchased band
  <math|x/2\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>\<leqslant\>3*x> throughout the
  interval: <math|7*s/8\<geqslant\>x/2> and <math|9*s/8\<leqslant\>3*x> for
  <math|s/2\<leqslant\>x\<leqslant\>s>.

  Use Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:restart-endpoint> for the presentation
  at <math|k>, with <math|a<rsub|0>=a<rsub|<math-up|coast>>>. Its
  fixed-profile restrictions are included in the local purchases; for a
  finite adjacent-profile menu take the smaller positive admissible
  <math|a<rsub|0>>. The endpoint has exactly the original density, the
  prescribed path and clip, additive cost at most <math|q<rsub|0>>, and
  forced allowance <math|G<around|(|s|)>>. It is this re-expressed endpoint
  that starts the induction.

  Inductively, the input at a step has cost <math|q<rsub|n>>, the output has
  cost at most <math|q<rsub|n+1>> by <eqref|eq:restart-recurrence>, and their
  unnormalized densities satisfy <eqref|eq:restart-push> at that very same
  coupling. Only the new entry is installed at each backward step. Thus all
  entries already constructed, including the endpoint and its marked labels,
  are retained. Composition proves the density identities for the entire
  auxiliary history. The comparison with one or two marked sites uses the
  same component alphabet and the same hole normalization. Its leading and
  remainder estimates are separate from this unmarked construction; they must
  be verified on these actual densities before the rank tests can be applied.

  <subsubsection|Steps across a stage boundary><label|subsec:restart-stage-boundary>

  The cost estimate above fixes one block ratio <math|L>. A comparison window
  may contain levels of two consecutive stages. We state what a step whose
  fine and coarse levels lie in different stages has to be supplied with, and
  check that the numerical part of the construction then passes across the
  boundary.

  Consider the step from the fine level <math|m+1> to the coarse level
  <math|m>, at the constant coupling <math|\<beta\>=\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>>,
  with stage pair <math|<around|(|J*<around|(|m+1|)>,J<around|(|m|)>|)>> and
  input cost <math|q<rsub|n>>. It is the restarted step of this section: it
  uses the fixed coupling <math|\<beta\>> and the new multiplier letters
  <math|h<rsub|\<alpha\>>>, and its fine and coarse clips, budget radius and
  support radius <math|B\<leqslant\>M<rsub|s><rsup|2>> are those of the two
  levels at that pair. We use the following three hypotheses for that step.

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(V1)><em|Component bounds.> The unmarked coarse letters of the
    step other than the multiplier letters, built at the coupling
    <math|\<beta\>> with the clips, radii and support exponent <math|a> of
    the pair, have weighted allowance at most
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|n>-\<omega\><rsub|n>> at the support weight. The
    component estimate of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mixed-activity-prices>
    applies to the alphabet enlarged by the multiplier letters.

    <item*|(V2)><em|Path correction.> With <math|A<rsub|c>> the retained
    coarse path action of the coarse clip of the pair and
    <math|\<beta\>*B<rsub|c>> the reference action of the fluctuation formula
    at the coupling <math|\<beta\>>, the masked local pieces of
    <math|\<beta\>*B<rsub|c>-A<rsub|c>> have anchored cost at most
    <math|s<rsup|-1/200>>.

    <item*|(V3)><em|Reassembly.> The reassembly identity of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-reassembly> and the
    forced-exclusion identity hold for that enlarged alphabet, with exact
    flag coverage and the addresswise coarse forced activities of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:corrected-forced-output>.
  </enumerate>

  At a fixed ratio <math|L> these are the bounds used in the preceding
  subsections. Given (V1)\U(V3), the output of the step is described by data
  of the same kind at the coarse level: a path action, a forced gas with its
  tests, an additive family of anchored cost at most <math|q<rsub|n+1>>, and
  the push identity <eqref|eq:restart-push>. That output does not refer to
  the stage of the fine level.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Stage changes inside the interval><label|lem:restart-stage-boundary>Let
    the interval of the construction contain levels of the stages <math|r>
    and <math|r-1> only. Suppose that (V1)\U(V3) hold at every step of the
    interval, each for its own stage pair, which is one of
    <math|<around|(|r,r|)>>, <math|<around|(|r,r-1|)>> and
    <math|<around|(|r-1,r-1|)>>. Suppose also that the thresholds after which
    the eventual inequalities of the cost estimate hold are taken as the
    larger of those for <math|L<rsub|r>> and <math|L<rsub|r-1>>. Then the
    inductive joining argument above holds on the whole interval, with the
    same costs <math|q<rsub|n>> and the same density identities.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The induction hypothesis at a step is an input of cost <math|q<rsub|n>>
    with its density. The multiplier letters cost at most
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|n>> by the footprint bound, in which the block ratio
    enters only through <math|C<rsub|L,a,t>>; take the larger of the two
    constants. By (V1) the unmarked allowance is at most
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|n>>, so the regular logarithm has cost at most
    <math|16*\<delta\><rsub|n>>. By (V2) and <eqref|eq:restart-newcost> the
    combined family has cost at most <math|s<rsup|-1/200>+16*\<delta\><rsub|n>>,
    which is at most <math|q<rsub|n+1>> by <eqref|eq:restart-recurrence>. By
    (V3) the push identity <eqref|eq:restart-push> holds at that step. In
    <math|h<rsub|1>,h<rsub|2>> take <math|L> to be the smaller of
    <math|L<rsub|r-1>,L<rsub|r>>. Then <eqref|eq:restart-decay> and
    <eqref|eq:restart-recurrence> hold for every step beyond the later of the
    two thresholds, and the rest of the argument is unchanged.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Purchasing the actual adjacent
  steps><label|subsec:restart-actual-purchases>

  We now verify (V1)\U(V3) for the local construction, rather than assuming
  them for a restarted history. The fixed input is the funded chart/stamp
  contract: the original path bounds and binding, the actual adjacent tubes
  and their metric floors, the room and chart parameters, and the local
  reserve choices of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-local-compiler-handoff>.
  The support purchases are made at the larger raw exponent and its reserve
  in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:allarity-compiler-reserve>. These are
  purchases on each stage pair, before the coupling and before the period;
  they do not assume any estimates on the restarted representations. As in
  the all-stage selection, a statement for a fixed pair below is made into a
  request at that pair, and the finite set of requests at a stage is
  intersected before choosing its ready time. No supremum of constants over
  infinitely many profiles is taken.

  First record the path correction explicitly. On a coarse level with
  threshold <math|\<tau\><rsub|x>=x<rsup|-1/2><math-up|hot><around|(|x|)>>,
  support exponent <math|a> and radius <math|B<rsub|c>>, let <math|C<rsub|c>>
  bound each of its cubic and quartic coefficient sums at path rate
  <math|\<sigma\><rsub|c>\<gtr\>0>, and let <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|c>> bound
  its quadratic loop sum. If <math|a/B<rsub|c>\<leqslant\>\<sigma\><rsub|c>>,
  the anchored price of the correction <math|\<beta\>*B<rsub|c><rsup|<math-up|ref>>-A<rsub|c>>
  is at most

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:restart-path-price>\<beta\>*e<rsup|7*a>*C<rsub|c>*\<tau\><rsub|x><rsup|3>+\<beta\>*e<rsup|9*a>*C<rsub|c>*\<tau\><rsub|x><rsup|4>+e<rsup|5*a>*\<Gamma\><rsub|c>*\<tau\><rsub|x><rsup|2>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|B<rsub|c><rsup|<math-up|ref>>> denotes the reference base
  action, not the budget radius. Indeed its difference from the full path
  action consists of minus the cubic and quartic terms with coefficient
  <math|\<beta\>>, and minus the loop terms with coefficient one, with their
  original healthy masks. An order-<math|j> atom of true length <math|d> has
  cover at most <math|1+2*j+d/B<rsub|c>>. Its weighted value is therefore
  bounded by <math|e<rsup|a*<around|(|1+2*j|)>>*\<tau\><rsub|x><rsup|j>>
  times its coefficient weighted by <math|e<rsup|\<sigma\><rsub|c>*d>>. Sum
  these coefficient bounds at each anchor, and average the signed channels
  with their probability weights. This proves <eqref|eq:restart-path-price>,
  including all winding paths. Since <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|c>\<leqslant\>2*exp
  <around|(|<around|(|log log x|)><rsup|2>|)>> and
  <math|\<beta\>\<leqslant\>4*x>, its right side is
  <math|x<rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>>. Also
  <math|a/B<rsub|c>\<leqslant\>\<sigma\><rsub|c>> eventually. Uniformly for
  <math|x\<in\><around|[|s/2,s|]>>, this proves (V2), with bound
  <math|s<rsup|-1/200>>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Closure of the restarted local
    construction><label|prop:restart-actual-closure>With the funded
    chart/stamp data just specified and the original endpoint bounds of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:restart-endpoint>, the restarted
    representations can be constructed throughout the comparison interval. At
    level <math|i>, their path and clip are those of the original level,
    their coupling is <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>>, their additive value cost
    is at most <math|q<rsub|k-i>>, and their forced allowance is
    <math|G<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>=exp <around|(|-a<rsub|<math-up|coast>><math-up|hot><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>|)>>,
    where <math|x<rsub|i>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>>. Their neutral versions
    have the inherited law required by the actual local compiler. On every
    adjacent step the construction satisfies (V1)\U(V3) on its own corrected
    alphabet and has the exact density identity <eqref|eq:restart-push>. The
    upper and lower compiler estimates use this same alphabet and
    normalization. All thresholds are uniform in cutoff, period, fields and
    marked families with the fixed arity cap and price parameters, with the
    fixed-profile dependence allowed by the funded contract.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    <em|Matching the geometric data.> The clock estimate in the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-firesky-clock-fields> gives more
    than its stated half-band. Its upper bound for the total logarithmic
    scale loss is <math|o<around|(|s<rsub|k>|)>>, so, increasing the ready
    threshold, one has <math|3*s/4\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>s> on the
    whole interval. Since <math|<around|\||\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>-s|\|>\<leqslant\>s/8>,
    this gives

    <\equation*>
      x<rsub|i>/2\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>\<leqslant\>2*x<rsub|i>.
    </equation*>

    The clock relation on a step is exactly
    <math|x<rsub|i+1>=\<beta\><rsub|F><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>> for its own
    fine block size. Retiming gives the coarse threshold
    <math|<math-up|temp><around|(|r<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>|)>>, while the fine
    threshold is <math|x<rsub|i+1><rsup|-1/2><math-up|hot><around|(|x<rsub|i+1>|)>>.
    The logarithmic radii have the required comparison
    <math|B<rsub|f>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|c>> eventually, and the budget wall
    dominates the fixed multiples of the local radius. These are the local
    radius and band conditions of the corrected compiler. Its remaining room
    and chart inequalities are the requests at the actual stage pair in the
    funded contract.

    Retain the two original beds and the original tube at this step. Their
    path attachment, diagonal identity, tower roll, metric floors and path
    bounds are properties of those paths and clips, not of the additive or
    forced coefficients. Consequently the alongside certificate is unchanged
    when those coefficients are replaced. The induction below always retains
    the prescribed path and clip, so the upward path and high clip of this
    actual tube are exactly those of its incoming representation. This
    verifies the same-data requirement of the local handoff, including at a
    stage boundary; equality of the two stage indices is not used.

    <em|The incoming law.> Start with Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:restart-endpoint>.
    Inductively suppose the stated bounds hold at level <math|i+1>. Delete
    only its additive family. The forced activities, their eligibility tests,
    path and clip remain unchanged, and the additive cost is zero. This gives
    the neutral law with any nonnegative additive allowance, in particular
    the calendar allowance used by the inherited estimate. Its loop
    coefficient bound is the original <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|i+1>\<leqslant\>2*H<rsub|x<rsub|i+1>>>,
    which is eventually smaller than the required positive power of the local
    fine parameter. The genuine representation separately has value cost
    <math|q<rsub|n>>, where <math|n=k-<around|(|i+1|)>>. There is no
    assertion that <math|q<rsub|n>> meets the much smaller calendar
    derivative allowance. The correction letters use only its value bound and
    their full local supports.

    <em|Prices on this alphabet.> Apply the actual local handoff to this
    tuple. Its matrix and both word inventories, owner bound, and all-matched
    warning estimate are uniform in the incoming field and store. In
    particular Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:actual-neutral-warning> prices a
    product of warning factors and genuine remainder factors in the same
    Gaussian integral. Let <math|m> be the larger of
    <math|G<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|-2>> and
    <math|G<around|(|x<rsub|i+1>|)><rsup|-1>>, and let <math|l> be the larger
    unforced multiplier needed by the two compiler uses. The latter has
    <math|log l=O<around|(|<around|(|log log s|)><rsup|2>|)>> by the upper
    calculation (<math|l\<leqslant\>max <around|(|1,H<rsub|s><rsup|q-3>|)>>);
    the lower use has multiplier one. Fix these before introducing any marked
    family.

    We verify the inflated body-product premise, including its site bound.
    Each forced body component contains an owner. Charge its factor <math|m>
    to such an owner, bounding it by <math|m> for each owner if there are
    several. For unforced components charge <math|l> to a constituent;
    bounding by <math|l> for every constituent only increases the price. An
    owner of <math|r> seed shares is bounded by <math|exp
    <around|[|-K<math-up|hot><around|(|x<rsub|i+1>|)><rsup|2>*r|]>> after its
    normalization is paid, with the positive margin <math|K> of the local
    handoff. Uniformly for <math|x<rsub|i>,x<rsub|i+1>\<in\><around|[|3*s/4,s|]>>,

    <\equation*>
      <frac|<math-up|hot><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>|<math-up|hot><around|(|x<rsub|i+1>|)><rsup|2>>\<longrightarrow\>1.
    </equation*>

    To check this, the difference of their <math|log log> arguments is
    <math|O<around|(|1/log s|)>>; the mean value theorem applied to
    <math|2*t<rsup|3/2>> bounds the difference of the logarithms by
    <math|O<around|(|<sqrt|log log s>/log s|)>>. Hence <math|log
    m\<leqslant\>4*a<rsub|<math-up|coast>><math-up|hot><around|(|x<rsub|i+1>|)><rsup|2>>
    eventually. The purchased inequality <math|2*a<rsub|<math-up|coast>>\<leqslant\>K/4>
    leaves at least half the owner suppression. The factor <math|l>, the
    finite decoration costs and the packet count
    <math|H<rsub|B>*D<rsub|B><rsup|2*r>> from
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mixed-activity-prices> are absorbed in the
    remaining margin: their logarithms per seed are
    <math|O<around|(|<around|(|log log s|)><rsup|2>+log B|)>>, whereas
    <math|<math-up|hot><around|(|x<rsub|i+1>|)><rsup|2>> dominates every
    fixed multiple of these quantities. Thus the inflated owner site price is
    smaller than any fixed negative power of <math|s>. The joint warning
    price has the same property, even after the fixed halo counts and
    <math|l> are paid, by <eqref|eq:warning-price>. Its exponent grows like a
    positive constant times <math|exp <around|(|<tfrac|1|2><around|(|log log
    s|)><rsup|2>|)>>, which dominates <math|log s>.

    The remaining neutral auxiliary letters have site price at most
    <math|r<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|-1/48>> by the corrected exponent
    decomposition. Multiplication by <math|l> and fixed cover/counting costs
    changes this to <math|s<rsup|-1/48+o<around|(|1|)>>>. The projected old
    forced background has price at most <math|\<cal-W\><around|(|B<rsub|c>|)>*L<rsup|4>*T<rsub|E>*G<around|(|x<rsub|i+1>|)>>,
    by <eqref|eq:mixed-cover-charge> and its site version. This too is
    smaller than any fixed negative power of <math|s>. The background need
    not itself pay <math|m>: exact inventory coverage places every forced
    background inventory in a body owner component, which supplies that
    inflation, as in the proof of <eqref|eq:mixed-club-integral>.

    Finally, the genuine additive letters have uninflated footprint price at
    most <math|C*<around|(|42*B<rsub|c>+1|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|n>>. All fixed
    powers of <math|B<rsub|c>> and all factors <math|l> are <math|exp
    <around|(|O<around|(|<around|(|log log s|)><rsup|2>|)>|)>>. Since
    <math|A<rsub|s>=exp <around|(|<around|(|log log s|)><rsup|3>|)>>, each
    fixed product of these factors is at most <math|A<rsub|s><rsup|1/4>>
    eventually. In particular the inflated additive site price is at most
    <math|q<rsub|n>*A<rsub|s><rsup|1/4>>. There are finitely many such
    products in this argument; choose their thresholds together, uniformly
    for <math|n\<leqslant\>h>. The incoming bound <eqref|eq:restart-decay>
    makes the combined site price times every fixed radius polynomial tend to
    zero. The rooted-tree estimate of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-mixed-marked-compiler>
    therefore applies. For components containing an additive letter, root at
    such a letter; its tree estimate bounds their site sum by its inflated
    site price, with the fixed support-conversion factors just paid.
    Enlarging to <math|q<rsub|n>*A<rsub|s>=\<omega\><rsub|n>> leaves room for
    these factors. Components with no additive letter have site price
    <math|s<rsup|-1/48+o<around|(|1|)>>>, eventually at most
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|n>-\<omega\><rsub|n>>: indeed
    <math|q<rsub|n>\<geqslant\>s<rsup|-1/200>> and
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|n>-\<omega\><rsub|n>=q<rsub|n>*<around|(|A<rsub|s><rsup|3>-A<rsub|s>|)>>.
    This proves (V1), with the specified separate allowances for the two
    classes. It also proves the inflated product estimate itself: use the
    pointwise owner and auxiliary bounds and the single joint warning event,
    and then group connected constituents. All these estimates use the full
    supports.

    <em|Reconstruction and renewal.> Choose the scalar using the positive
    original loading, the additive family from the regular logarithm and the
    correction <eqref|eq:restart-path-price>, and the forced family by
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:corrected-forced-output>. Its proof,
    applied to the genuine corrected body identity, supplies (V3). The
    absolute component bounds just proved justify its reassembly and every
    hole-deletion identity. Their normalization is the same one used by the
    mixed compiler. The path bound proves (V2), so the new additive cost is
    at most <math|s<rsup|-1/200>+16*\<delta\><rsub|n>\<leqslant\>q<rsub|n+1>>.

    For completeness the hole corrections do not exhaust the forced margin.
    Their raised site cost is at most <math|16*l*\<delta\><rsub|n>*e<rsup|16*\<delta\><rsub|n>>>
    by <math|<around|\||e<rsup|-c>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<around|\||c|\|>><around|\||c|\|>>.
    The path correction has been absorbed entirely into the coarse additive
    family, so there is no residual boundary factor. The component and
    raised-hole site costs are consequently
    <math|s<rsup|-1/400+o<around|(|1|)>>> by <eqref|eq:restart-decay>. They
    satisfy the rooted smallness and normalization conditions of the mixed
    compiler, with its available <math|H<rsub|s><rsup|-1/8>> reserve. Its
    forced output is bounded by <math|1/m>, hence by
    <math|G<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>G<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>>.
    Attach and sum at full addresses as in that proposition; this preserves
    the bound and all eligibility tests. The resulting neutral law has the
    retained original path bounds, zero additive cost and this new forced
    allowance. The symmetrization above supplies the motion properties of the
    genuine additive family. This proves the induction hypothesis at level
    <math|i> together with the exact push of the genuine incoming density.

    Only the newly constructed entry at <math|i> is installed. Thus
    subsequent backward steps retain the endpoint and every already
    constructed higher entry. The equality of consecutive couplings makes
    them all <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>>, and composition gives the density
    identities on the same auxiliary course. For the three possible adjacent
    stage pairs, use their own positive path rates, floors and clips
    throughout and intersect their finitely many thresholds. The numerical
    argument of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:restart-stage-boundary> now applies
    with (V1)\U(V3) proved. Finally the alphabet, loading and all-hole
    identities were selected before any marked family. Decreasing the raw
    exponent and inflations gives the upper and lower uses on this same
    output, as asserted in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:allarity-compiler-reserve>.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|One auxiliary family on the whole chart
  window><label|subsec:restart-chart-family>

  The family used in a chart is the one just constructed, including its
  re-expressed entry at the top. We record its budget and density at every
  level; equality of two endpoint representations is not needed. Put

  <\equation*>
    b<around|(|x|)>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>,<space|2em>x<rsub|j>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>,<space|2em>b<rsub|j>=b<around|(|x<rsub|j>|)>,<space|2em>B<rsub|j>=b<rsub|j><rsup|2>,<space|2em>\<tau\><rsub|j>=x<rsub|j><rsup|-1/2>*exp
    <around|(|<around|(|log log x<rsub|j>|)><rsup|3/2>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The radius <math|B<rsub|j>> is the representation's budget radius. It is
  distinct from the recording radius <math|b<around|(|r<around|(|x<rsub|j>|)>|)>>
  in the local Gaussian calculation. The retiming of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-reader-dawn-selection> is chosen so
  that its recording threshold equals <math|\<tau\><rsub|j>>; it satisfies
  <math|x<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>r<around|(|x<rsub|j>|)>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|j><rsup|2>>
  and <math|b<around|(|r<around|(|x<rsub|j>|)>|)>\<leqslant\>4*b<rsub|j>>.
  Thus, for all large <math|x<rsub|j>>,

  <\equation*>
    25*b<around|(|r<around|(|x<rsub|j>|)>|)>+30\<leqslant\>b<rsub|j><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>b<around|(|r<around|(|x<rsub|j>|)>|)><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  The first inequality follows from <math|100*b<rsub|j>+30\<leqslant\>b<rsub|j><rsup|2>>
  eventually, and the second from <math|b<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>b<around|(|r<around|(|x<rsub|j>|)>|)>>.
  Also <math|B<rsub|j+1>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|j>> eventually: on each fixed
  stage pair <math|x<rsub|j+1>=x<rsub|j>+c<rsub|G>*log L<rsub|j>>, so
  <math|b<rsub|j+1>/b<rsub|j>\<to\>1>. The finite adjacent-pair purchases
  make these inequalities simultaneous on the window. These are precisely the
  budget-radius conditions of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-corrected-body-compiler>.
  We can therefore use the exact budgets <math|<around|(|B<rsub|j>,\<tau\><rsub|j>|)>>,
  rather than merely comparable radii.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The retained auxiliary chart family><label|prop:restart-chart-family>Fix
    a finite grade menu and an arc bound <math|<wide|b|\<bar\>>\<gtr\>0>.
    Under the funded hypotheses of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:restart-actual-closure>,
    for every sufficiently large level <math|k>, every chart with arc
    <math|0\<less\>b\<leqslant\><wide|b|\<bar\>>>, every
    <math|N\<geqslant\>k> and every period <math|p>, choose the endpoint
    presentation and then the backward steps once. Denote the resulting
    representations on <math|<around|[|w,k|]>> by <math|P<rsub|j>>. They have
    budgets <math|<around|(|B<rsub|j>,\<tau\><rsub|j>|)>>, the original paths
    and stage clips, and common coupling <math|\<beta\>=\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>>.
    Their additive value costs are bounded by <math|q<rsub|k-j>>, and their
    densities are nonnegative.

    Let <math|D<rsub|k>> be the density of the original selected
    representation at <math|k>, and define, using the selected kernels at the
    frozen coupling <math|\<beta\>>,

    <\equation*>
      D<rsub|j>=\<cal-K\><rsub|j,\<beta\>>*D<rsub|j+1>*<space|1em><around|(|w\<leqslant\>j\<less\>k|)>.
    </equation*>

    Then

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:restart-chart-density><math-up|eval><around|(|P<rsub|j>|)>=D<rsub|j>*<space|1em><around|(|w\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>k|)>,<space|2em><big|int>D<rsub|j>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|j>=Z<rsub|N,p>.
    </equation>

    All the local compiler and marked comparisons on this chart use these
    same <math|P<rsub|j>> and the alphabets chosen in their construction.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    At <math|k>, Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:restart-endpoint> supplies
    exactly the budget <math|<around|(|b<rsub|k><rsup|2>,\<tau\><rsub|k>|)>>
    while retaining the original path, clip, coupling and density. It
    supplies cost at most <math|q<rsub|0>>. In particular, <math|P<rsub|k>>
    is a new presentation of <math|D<rsub|k>>, not an assertion that its
    smaller threshold equals the threshold of the original presentation.

    Suppose the entries <math|P<rsub|i+1>,\<ldots\>,P<rsub|k>> have been
    fixed. Apply Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:restart-actual-closure> to
    this very incoming <math|P<rsub|i+1>> and the original adjacent tube,
    with the coarse budget <math|<around|(|B<rsub|i>,\<tau\><rsub|i>|)>>
    verified above. Retain its output as <math|P<rsub|i>> and retain its
    corrected alphabet. That proposition gives the original coarse path and
    clip, coupling <math|\<beta\>>, the cost <math|q<rsub|k-i>> and

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|eval><around|(|P<rsub|i>|)>=\<cal-K\><rsub|i,\<beta\>><math-up|eval><around|(|P<rsub|i+1>|)>.
    </equation*>

    No higher entry is changed. Finite descending induction proves all these
    assertions together, and the recursion defining <math|D<rsub|i>> proves
    <eqref|eq:restart-chart-density>'s first assertion. Positivity follows
    from the nonnegative endpoint and positive kernels, not from any sign
    claim about the individual terms of the expansion.

    Each selected kernel has mass one in its coarse variable. The endpoint
    density is integrable on the finite-volume space; the bounded positive
    kernels preserve integrability. Fubini therefore gives
    <math|<big|int>D<rsub|i>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|i>=<big|int>D<rsub|i+1>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|i+1>>.
    At <math|k> the original selected transition identities identify this
    integral with <math|Z<rsub|N,p>>. This proves the second assertion.
    Finally the local construction chooses its alphabet before the marked
    insertions and supplies both compiler uses on that alphabet. Keeping
    those choices at each step gives the last assertion. This is a
    construction of one family with its estimates; it makes no uniqueness
    claim for representations having the same density.
  </proof>

  <section|Uniform estimates for marked correlations>

  <subsection|Several marked insertions on the restarted
  history><label|sec:allarity-planted-compiler>

  We keep the actual restarted density from
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:restarted-rank-history>. Its additive
  remainder is removed only in the auxiliary unmarked normalization. The
  marked integral itself must retain that remainder. We first prove the
  finite algebra that identifies all the selected insertion coefficients,
  then explain this mixed normalization and the extra local price it
  requires.

  <subsubsection|Selecting a cell in the marker algebra>

  For a finite set <math|V>, let

  <\equation*>
    <with|math-font|cal*|A><rsub|V>=\<bbb-C\>*<around|[|z<rsub|v>:v\<in\>V|]>/<around|(|z<rsub|v><rsup|2>:v\<in\>V|)>.
  </equation*>

  Its basis consists of <math|z<rsub|A>=<big|prod><rsub|v\<in\>A>z<rsub|v>>,
  <math|A\<subseteq\>V>. Products satisfy
  <math|z<rsub|A>*z<rsub|B>=z<rsub|A\<cup\>B>> for disjoint sets and are zero
  otherwise. If <math|F> has zero constant coefficient, define
  <math|exp<rsub|\<ast\>> F=<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|<around|\||V|\|>>F<rsup|j>/j!>.
  Every product of more than <math|<around|\||V|\|>> nonconstant monomials
  has a repeated variable, so it vanishes. Hence this finite exponential
  satisfies the usual addition formula for commuting elements of zero
  constant coefficient, by the binomial theorem. In particular, for every
  finite family of pieces,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:planted-exp><big|prod><rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|1+y<rsub|\<alpha\>>*z<rsub|A<rsub|\<alpha\>>>|)>=exp<rsub|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>y<rsub|\<alpha\>>*z<rsub|A<rsub|\<alpha\>>>|)>*<space|1em><around|(|A<rsub|\<alpha\>>\<ne\>\<varnothing\>|)>.
  </equation>

  There is no disjointness assumption on the <math|A<rsub|\<alpha\>>>: their
  overlapping products vanish on both sides.

  For <math|T\<subseteq\>V>, define <math|\<pi\><rsub|T>> by setting
  <math|z<rsub|v>=0> for <math|v\<nin\>T>. Thus
  <math|\<pi\><rsub|T><around|(|<big|sum><rsub|A>c<rsub|A>*z<rsub|A>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|A\<subseteq\>T>c<rsub|A>*z<rsub|A>>.
  It is an algebra homomorphism, and therefore commutes with
  <math|exp<rsub|\<ast\>>>. For a finite cell set <math|\<cal-C\>> and mark
  set <math|J>, put <math|V=\<cal-C\>\<times\>J>. The cell embedding
  <math|\<iota\><rsub|c>:<with|math-font|cal*|A><rsub|J>\<to\><with|math-font|cal*|A><rsub|V>>
  sends <math|z<rsub|j>> to <math|z<rsub|<around|(|c,j|)>>>; it too is an
  algebra homomorphism.

  Fix one coarse cell <math|c> and a selected set
  <math|T\<subseteq\><around|{|c|}>\<times\>J>. The incoming insertion pieces
  are indexed by pairs <math|<around|(|c<rprime|'>,\<alpha\>|)>>. Each piece
  has a nonempty marker support <math|A<rsub|c<rprime|'>,\<alpha\>>> lying in
  <math|<around|{|c<rprime|'>|}>\<times\>J>. Its value in a particular
  sector, forest, tag and Gaussian coordinate is
  <math|y<rsub|c<rprime|'>,\<alpha\>>>. The spatial geometry selects exactly
  those pieces for which <math|A<rsub|c,\<alpha\>>\<subseteq\>T>; write
  <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|T>> for this selected index set and write
  <math|B<rsub|\<alpha\>>\<subseteq\>J> for their marker supports with the
  cell coordinate removed. Define the planted polynomial

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|T>=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|T>>y<rsub|c,\<alpha\>>*z<rsub|B<rsub|\<alpha\>>>.
  </equation*>

  Applying <math|\<pi\><rsub|T>> to <eqref|eq:planted-exp> proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:planted-selection>\<pi\><rsub|T>*<big|prod><rsub|c<rprime|'>,\<alpha\>><around|(|1+y<rsub|c<rprime|'>,\<alpha\>>*z<rsub|A<rsub|c<rprime|'>,\<alpha\>>>|)>=\<pi\><rsub|T>*\<iota\><rsub|c><around|(|exp<rsub|\<ast\>>
    P<rsub|T>|)>.
  </equation>

  To check the selection explicitly, a piece from <math|c<rprime|'>\<ne\>c>
  has at least one marker outside <math|T> and is killed. A piece from
  <math|c> is retained exactly when its entire marker support is contained in
  <math|T>. A discarded monomial maps to zero, so its factor
  <math|1+y<rsub|c<rprime|'>,\<alpha\>>*z<rsub|A<rsub|c<rprime|'>,\<alpha\>>>>
  maps to one. In the expanded product, every term selecting a discarded
  monomial vanishes. This proves the identity at all orders simultaneously.

  The spatially planted value is the same masked field value as in the left
  side. Write it as <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|\<xi\>|)>*h<rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|U<around|(|\<xi\>|)>|)>>,
  where <math|0\<leqslant\>\<Gamma\><rsub|\<alpha\>>\<leqslant\>1> is its
  local cutoff. On the region where <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|\<alpha\>>\<gtr\>0>,
  the chart's healthy-field condition makes the original mask equal to one on
  the full inspected support. Where <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|\<alpha\>>=0>, both
  products are zero. Consequently this formula agrees everywhere with
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|\<alpha\>><math-up|masked><around|(|h<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)><around|(|U<around|(|\<xi\>|)>|)>>.
  The sector and tag are fixed during this verification. They are not chosen
  separately for the different readers.

  All linear maps on <math|<with|math-font|cal*|A><rsub|V>> are continuous
  because this space is finite dimensional. Hence an integrable
  polynomial-valued function can be integrated coefficientwise, and its
  integral commutes with <math|\<pi\><rsub|T>> and <math|\<iota\><rsub|c>>.
  If <math|\<nu\><rsub|t>> are the finitely many sector/tag measures and
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|t>> their scalar weights,
  <eqref|eq:planted-selection> gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:planted-integral><big|sum><rsub|t>\<lambda\><rsub|t>*<big|int>\<pi\><rsub|T>*<big|prod><rsub|c<rprime|'>,\<alpha\>><around|(|1+y<rsub|c<rprime|'>,\<alpha\>,t>*z<rsub|A<rsub|c<rprime|'>,\<alpha\>>>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|t>=\<pi\><rsub|T>*\<iota\><rsub|c><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|t>\<lambda\><rsub|t>*<big|int>exp<rsub|\<ast\>>
    P<rsub|T,t>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|t>|)>.
  </equation>

  Here integrability is provided by the common positive marker weights and
  the paid product envelopes: the absolute value of each coefficient is
  bounded by its weighted polynomial norm divided by its positive monomial
  weight. The Gaussian/Haar envelope bounds that norm by an integrable
  function. For the countable activity menu, first apply the identity to
  finite truncations. The summable full-support majorants and coefficientwise
  countable regrouping of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-rg-multisource>
  provide the limiting integrable coefficients. Dominated convergence uses
  the same Gaussian/Haar envelope for the full menu, independently of
  truncation; the finite-dimensional linear maps commute with those limits.
  Thus no interchange of a merely formal infinite series is used in
  <eqref|eq:planted-integral>.

  <subsubsection|The marked density and its three kinds of factors>

  Let <math|P> denote the fine representation and <math|Q> the coarse one.
  Write <math|\<cal-K\>> for a finite family of lifted background packets,
  with full spatial supports <math|X<rsub|b>> and fine flag inventories
  <math|F<rsub|b>>. Its unmarked coefficients reproduce the activities of
  <math|P> at each activity address. This addresswise identity is stronger
  than equality of their summed gases. For regular insertion pieces
  <math|h<rsub|\<alpha\>>> with nonempty marker supports, the exact
  polynomial density is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:planted-density>D<rsub|P><around|(|U|)>=e<rsup|<math-up|exponent><rsub|P><around|(|U|)>>*exp<rsub|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>z<rsub|A<rsub|\<alpha\>>><math-up|masked><around|(|h<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)><around|(|U|)>|)>*<big|sum><rsub|B<text|admissible>><big|prod><rsub|b\<in\>B>K<rsub|b><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation>

  Admissibility requires pairwise disjoint full supports and the exact
  identity <math|<big|cup><rsub|b\<in\>B>F<rsub|b>=<math-up|flags><rsub|P><around|(|U|)>>.
  It is not enough to require just disjoint inventories.

  Fix the actual coarse configuration, positioned chart, forest, tag and
  Gaussian coordinate. The unmarked chart expansion, including its Haar
  Jacobian and Gaussian normalization, has the following sliced form. For
  each fine flag set <math|F>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:planted-body>\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|<math-up|flags><rsub|P><around|(|U|)>=F|}>>*J<around|(|U|)>*e<rsup|<math-up|exponent><rsub|P><around|(|U|)>>=\<Lambda\>*<big|sum><rsub|A<text|compatible><around|(|F|)>><big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>A>p<rsub|a>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|J> denotes the full scalar chart factor, and <math|\<Lambda\>>
  is the same positive loading used by the unmarked construction. Each
  <math|p<rsub|a>> has a fixed coarse support, a fine inventory and a coarse
  inventory. Compatibility means disjoint coarse supports, fine inventory
  union <math|F>, coarse inventory union equal to the coarse flags, and the
  sector key specified by those same packets. These are precisely the data of
  the unmarked sector expansion of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-reassembly>.

  To obtain the marked identity, first fix a background packet family
  <math|B>. If its full supports overlap its contribution is zero. Otherwise
  multiply <eqref|eq:planted-body>, with <math|F=<big|cup><rsub|b\<in\>B>F<rsub|b>>,
  by <math|<big|prod><rsub|b\<in\>B>K<rsub|b>> and by the insertion product
  <eqref|eq:planted-exp>. The indicator on the left imposes exactly the
  background flag test in <eqref|eq:planted-density>. Expand the insertion
  product as a sum over its subsets. On the right we now have three
  independent kinds of factors: scalar body packets <math|p<rsub|a>>,
  background packets <math|K<rsub|b>>, and marked insertion pieces. Index
  their union by the disjoint union of these three alphabets. This is a
  bijection between the three subset choices and the subsets of the union
  alphabet. The flag and sector tests are unchanged. If a marker is used
  twice, the corresponding product is zero in
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|A><rsub|V>>, exactly as on the expanded left
  side. Summing over <math|B> proves the full marked chart identity at every
  coefficient.

  Integrate with the common Gaussian/Haar product envelopes, sum the sector
  partition and average the tags. The coefficientwise argument of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-rg-multisource> then applies to
  this same three-alphabet expansion. It gives the pushed density
  <math|\<cal-T\>*D<rsub|P>> represented by the resulting coarse polynomial
  activities and their regular exponential. In particular this step does not
  follow by repeating the one-address affine argument of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-block-integral>; that argument alone
  has no mixed higher coefficients.

  <subsubsection|Restoring the additive remainder before normalization>

  Put <math|P<rsup|0>=<math-up|white><around|(|P|)>>, so that its additive
  remainder has been deleted while its coupling, path, masks and forced
  activities are unchanged. In the notation of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:restarted-rank-history>,
  <math|P=e<rsup|-E>*P<rsup|0>>. On the actual chart the additional exponent
  is a sum <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>f<rsub|i>=-E<around|(|U|)>> over local
  coarse supports. Suppose its support prices <math|b<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>
  satisfy <math|<around|\||f<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|i>> and have
  anchored weighted sum at most <math|d\<leqslant\>1>. The functions
  <math|f<rsub|i>> are real-valued. Let <math|M<rsub|i>> be the real-valued
  unperturbed local exponents, with

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||e<rsup|M<rsub|i>>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>w<rsub|i>,<space|2em>w<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0,<space|2em><text|anchored
    weighted sum of >w<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>e\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  The weights are at least one, so individually <math|b<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>d>
  and <math|w<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>e>. Hence
  <math|e<rsup|M<rsub|i>>\<leqslant\>2> and
  <math|<around|\||e<rsup|f<rsub|i>>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>2<around|\||f<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*b<rsub|i>>.
  The latter elementary inequality holds for real
  <math|<around|\||f<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>1>, by convexity of the
  exponential on <math|<around|[|0,1|]>> and <math|e-1\<less\>2>, and by
  <math|1-e<rsup|-t>\<leqslant\>t> for <math|t\<geqslant\>0>. It follows that

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||e<rsup|M<rsub|i>+f<rsub|i>>-1|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||e<rsup|M<rsub|i>>-1|\|>+e<rsup|M<rsub|i>>*<around|\||e<rsup|f<rsub|i>>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>w<rsub|i>+4*b<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  Thus the perturbed auxiliary price is at most <math|e+4*d>. The base
  Gaussian quadratic form and the scalar loading have not changed. This
  proves <eqref|eq:planted-body> for <math|P> itself from the neutral chart
  expansion: add <math|f<rsub|i>> to the same local exponents and use their
  exact sum <math|-E<around|(|U|)>>. The full-support locality of these terms
  was established in the restarted-history construction, including the final
  flag tests.

  For the actual scale purchases, write <math|\<omega\>> for the permitted
  perturbation price. The neutral auxiliary price is at most <math|\<omega\>>
  and <math|d\<leqslant\>\<omega\>>, so the preceding bound is at most
  <math|5*\<omega\>> and in particular at most the reserved
  <math|6*\<omega\>>. The two other elementary letter families have anchored
  prices at most <math|u<rsup|-1/2>> each, where <math|u> is the retimed
  coupling scale. Since <math|u<rsup|-1/2>\<leqslant\>\<omega\>>, their
  combined price is at most <math|8*\<omega\>>. The background large-field
  letters retain the decay <math|exp <around|(|-\<alpha\><space|0.17em><math-up|hot><rsup|2><space|0.17em><math-up|shares>|)>>;
  the separate purchase <math|2*a<rsub|<math-up|coast>>\<leqslant\>\<alpha\>/4>
  pays their forced inflation. With the reserved line multiplier
  <math|L<rsub|0>>, the integrated cluster bound is therefore purchased
  through

  <\equation*>
    L<rsub|0>*<around|(|e<rsup|8*\<omega\>>-1|)>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|step>>.
  </equation*>

  This is the local unmarked price used by the restarted recurrence. The
  fixed stage constants and Gaussian envelopes in this calculation are those
  of the neutral compiler at the same physical parameters. The perturbation
  estimate does not assert derivative bounds for <math|E>.

  There are now two distinct, compatible uses of the representations. The
  body identity <eqref|eq:planted-body> is for <math|P>, including <math|E>.
  The unmarked auxiliary alphabet consists of these <em|same corrected> body
  packets <math|p<rsub|a>>, together with the archived background packets of
  <math|P<rsup|0>>. It does not use the former neutral body packets. Its
  loading is the same <math|\<Lambda\>> as in <eqref|eq:planted-body>.

  The normalization needed here has two precise parts. Write <math|Z<rsub|j>>
  for its zero-marker regular components. For every deleted spatial support
  <math|D> and every coarse configuration <math|V>, require the identity

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:planted-holes>\<Lambda\><around|(|V|)>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-G\><rsub|Z;D><around|(|V|)>=e<rsup|<math-up|exponent><rsub|Q><around|(|V|)>>*<big|prod><rsub|a><around|(|1+p<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|pay>><around|(|V|)>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|b:<space|0.17em>X<rsub|b><rsup|<math-up|hole>>\<cap\>D\<ne\>\<varnothing\>><around|(|1+h<rsub|b><around|(|V|)>|)>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<cal-G\><rsub|Z;D>> is the compatible gas of the
  <math|Z<rsub|j>> whose full supports are disjoint from <math|D>. The
  factors <math|p<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|pay>>> and <math|h<rsub|b>> are the
  fixed boundary and hole corrections of the unmarked regrouping, with their
  original masks. Secondly, let <math|F> be the unaggregated family of
  unmarked forced components, before any summation over addresses, and let
  <math|K> be the family obtained from <math|F> by attaching these same hole
  and boundary correction factors. The family <math|F> must embed into the
  forced components of this alphabet, with identical values, supports and
  inventories, and must exhaust its zero-marker forced components. At each
  activity address, that is, at each full support and flag inventory, the sum
  of the members of <math|K> must equal the archived activity of <math|Q>. We
  reserve <em|forced activities of <math|Q>> for these aggregated slots. In
  general a slot of <math|Q> is a sum of several components and is not itself
  a member of <math|F> or <math|K>: an address carrying components with
  values <math|u> and <math|v> has the single slot <math|u+v>. This
  addresswise identity is required before applying the exclusion regrouping.
  These are the all-deleted-support identity and the forced-family embedding
  supplied by the unmarked forest and exclusion construction. They must be
  established for the corrected <math|p<rsub|a>> and this actual coarse
  <math|Q>; equality of the two archived fine backgrounds alone does not
  establish them. For the constructed fixed-coupling output they are proved
  in Propositions<nbsp><reference|prop:corrected-regular-deletion> and
  <reference|prop:corrected-forced-output>.

  We verify that these two statements, once available, pass to the full
  marked alphabet. First coalesce background packets with the same address
  and marker mode. Their zero-marker values are exactly the archived
  activities at that address, by the addresswise background identity. This
  embeds the entire unmarked alphabet into the full alphabet, preserving
  values, full supports, inventories and sector keys. Every remaining
  elementary insertion has a nonempty marker support. If a full connected
  component has empty marker support, every elementary factor in it must
  therefore have empty marker support: its marker support is their union.
  Hence it comes from the unmarked alphabet. Conversely every unmarked
  component has the same connectedness and eligibility tests after the
  embedding. This is a bijection of zero-marker components preserving their
  full supports. It restricts to the components disjoint from any given
  <math|D>. Thus <math|\<cal-G\><rsub|Z;D>> is unchanged, and
  <eqref|eq:planted-holes> holds for the full alphabet at every deleted
  support. The same component bijection transports the forced-family
  embedding and its exhaustion property.

  Finally fix a compatible marked forced family and let <math|D> be the union
  of its full supports. Its residual zero-marker gas is precisely
  <math|\<cal-G\><rsub|Z;D>>. Substitute <eqref|eq:planted-holes>. A hole
  factor occurs exactly when its support meets at least one member of that
  forced family, since <math|D> is their union. Expanding these factors and
  regrouping by their incident forced components is the weight-preserving
  exclusion bijection of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:forced-exclusion>.
  The resulting polynomial has exponent <math|<math-up|exponent><rsub|Q>> and
  the actual coarse archived background, by the transported embedding of
  <math|F> and the addresswise identity for <math|K>. Summing over the marked
  forced families therefore represents <math|\<cal-T\>*D<rsub|P>> with this
  coarse vacuum normalization. This uses <eqref|eq:planted-body> for <math|P>
  and <eqref|eq:planted-holes> for the corrected auxiliary alphabet; it never
  substitutes <math|\<cal-T\>*D<rsub|P<rsup|0>>> for the marked integral.

  This proves the finite algebra and the additive-remainder correction needed
  by the all-order compiler. Its quantitative marked use still requires the
  common Gaussian reader estimates and their cumulant approximation, the day
  and night marker prices, and the selected spatial support bounds. In
  particular the cell selection <eqref|eq:planted-integral> must be applied
  with the same readers and same sector measures as the body expansion, at
  the purchased slice radius. Those analytic and geometric estimates are
  separate from the polynomial identities proved here.

  <subsection|Owner energy at the rank threshold><label|sec:actual-harvested-owner-energy>

  The energy estimate used in the corrected block alphabet concerns the
  actual fine flag threshold. It also subtracts the affine squares selected
  by the Gaussian construction. We verify these two changes to the seed
  estimate explicitly.

  Write <math|y=r<around|(|x|)>> for the recording parameter,
  <math|\<nu\>=<math-up|fiery><around|(|y|)>> and
  <math|\<tau\>=<math-up|phase><around|(|\<beta\><rsub|F><around|(|x|)>|)>>
  for the fine rank threshold. The retiming comparison gives, eventually,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:owner-threshold-band>0\<less\>\<nu\>,<space|1em>0\<less\>\<tau\>,<space|2em>\<nu\>/2\<leqslant\>\<tau\>\<leqslant\>2*\<nu\>.
  </equation>

  The stores and seed counts remain those of the recorded configuration at
  <math|y>. Only the fine energy rows are read at threshold <math|\<tau\>>;
  the other rows retain their recorded thresholds. In particular, this
  operation does not replace the stored flags by flags at <math|\<tau\>>.

  <subsubsection|Paying the same seeds after changing the fine threshold>

  Let <math|\<cal-F\>> be the original fine seed inventory and
  <math|r<rsub|i>> the fine plaquette chords. For a threshold <math|s>, write
  <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|s>=<around|{|i\<in\>\<cal-F\>:r<rsub|i>\<gtr\>s|}>>.
  At any <math|s\<in\><around|[|\<nu\>/2,2*\<nu\>|]>>, the argument of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-energy> gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:owner-reread-fine>F<rsub|s>\<geqslant\>b*s*<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-F\><rsub|s>>r<rsub|i>+d*s<rsup|2>*<around|\||\<cal-F\>\<setminus\>\<cal-F\><rsub|s>|\|>,<space|2em>b=\<lambda\><rsub|f>/2,<space|1em>d=a<rsub|f>/512.
  </equation>

  Here the guide has norm at most <math|\<epsilon\>*s>, where
  <math|\<epsilon\>=2*C/L<rsup|2>>. The factor two comes from
  <math|\<nu\>\<leqslant\>2*s>. To verify the changed constant, a listed
  small plaquette has <math|r<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>\<nu\>/4\<geqslant\>s/8>. If
  <math|\<epsilon\>\<leqslant\><sqrt|a<rsub|f>>/16>, then

  <\equation*>
    <tfrac|1|2><around|(|<sqrt|a<rsub|f>>*r<rsub|i>-<around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>a<rsub|f>*s<rsup|2>/512.
  </equation*>

  Impose also <math|\<epsilon\>*<sqrt|a<rsub|f>>\<leqslant\>\<lambda\><rsub|f>/4>.
  The large-plaquette direct bound then remains
  <math|<around|(|3*\<lambda\><rsub|f>/4|)>*s*r<rsub|i>>. The discarded-path
  argument of <eqref|eq:seed-discarded-count> uses witnesses with <math|s*exp
  <around|(|<math-up|dist>/R|)>\<less\>r<rsub|j>> and the same original fine
  lattice. The recorded clean-neighbourhood conditions,
  <math|s\<geqslant\>\<nu\>/2> and the paid path-range bound place each such
  witness in <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|s>>. Thus the discarded rows cost at most
  <math|<around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|f>/4|)>*s*<big|sum><rsub|\<cal-F\><rsub|s>>r<rsub|i>>,
  provided <math|\<epsilon\>*<around|(|1+C<rsub|R>|)>*C\<leqslant\>\<lambda\><rsub|f>/4>.
  These two estimates prove <eqref|eq:owner-reread-fine>. No continuity in
  the clipping threshold is used.

  The next elementary allocation compares the two large-defect inventories
  without identifying them. Let <math|T\<geqslant\>\<nu\>> be the very-large
  threshold, and let <math|\<cal-K\>> be the fine defects spent at that
  threshold. Put

  <\equation*>
    N=\<cal-F\><rsub|s>,<space|1em>n<rsub|0>=<around|\||\<cal-F\>\<setminus\>N|\|>,<space|1em>R=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>N>r<rsub|i>,<space|1em>V=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-F\><rsub|\<nu\>>>r<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  Then

  <\equation*>
    V\<leqslant\>R+2*\<nu\>*n<rsub|0>,<space|2em>\<nu\><around|\||N|\|>\<leqslant\>2*R,<space|2em>T<around|\||\<cal-K\>|\|>\<leqslant\>V.
  </equation*>

  The first inequality bounds every term outside <math|N> by
  <math|s\<leqslant\>2*\<nu\>>; the second uses <math|s\<geqslant\>\<nu\>/2>;
  and the last uses <math|\<cal-K\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-F\><rsub|\<nu\>>> and
  <math|r<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>T> on <math|\<cal-K\>>. Consequently, if
  <math|m,c\<geqslant\>0> and

  <\equation*>
    2*<around|(|3*m+c|)>\<leqslant\>b,<space|2em>m+2*<around|(|m+c|)>\<leqslant\>d/4,
  </equation*>

  then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:owner-reread-reserve>m*\<nu\><rsup|2>\|\<cal-F\>*<around|\||+m*\<nu\>*T|\|>*\<cal-K\>\|+c*\<nu\>*V\<leqslant\>F<rsub|s>.
  </equation>

  Indeed the left side is at most <math|<around|(|3*m+c|)>*\<nu\>*R+<around|(|3*m+2*c|)>*\<nu\><rsup|2>*n<rsub|0>>.
  The displayed conditions and <math|\<nu\>\<leqslant\>2*s> bound these terms
  by <math|b*s*R> and <math|d*s<rsup|2>*n<rsub|0>>, respectively.

  All predictor and coarse rows are unchanged. Apply their reserves and the
  hit/unhit comparisons from Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-energy>,
  <reference|sec:seed-hit-comparison> and
  <reference|sec:seed-unhit-comparison>, using
  <eqref|eq:owner-reread-reserve> for the fine reserve. One can choose

  <\equation*>
    0\<less\>m\<leqslant\>min <around|{|p/32,a<rsub|c>/64,\<lambda\><rsub|f>/32,d/32|}>
  </equation*>

  before increasing <math|L> until <math|c\<leqslant\>m> and the guide
  conditions above hold. Choose the predictor width afterward, so that its
  linear expense is at most <math|p*W/16>. The two final smallness
  inequalities <eqref|eq:seed-final-smallness>, now with threshold
  <math|\<nu\>>, hold eventually. The same cancellation as in the seed
  estimate proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:owner-rank-income>\<cal-E\><rsub|\<tau\>><around|(|O|)>\<geqslant\>b<rsub|0>*\<nu\><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>s<around|(|O|)>,<space|2em>b<rsub|0>=m/2\<gtr\>0.
  </equation>

  Here <math|s<around|(|O|)>> is the unchanged number of seed shares of the
  owner store <math|O>, and <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|\<tau\>><around|(|O|)>> is
  its assigned energy with only the fine rows replaced by their
  <math|\<tau\>> versions. The finite-volume, tree-slice and local seed
  hypotheses are those of the cited seed estimates. Their guide, geometry and
  comparison bounds are used on this same store and field.

  <subsubsection|Subtracting the selected affine squares>

  Put <math|B=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log y|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>. Let
  <math|Q> be an annular window around <math|O>, and <math|H> a subwindow
  selecting fine and predictor rows of <math|Q>. Use the actual logarithmic
  signal in the local Gaussian coordinates, and denote by
  <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|H>> the sum of its selected affine squares.
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:packet-annulus> proves that these are the
  actual rows, with the same forest, tag and coarse field, and that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:owner-annulus-loss>\<cal-W\><around|(|Q|)>-\<cal-X\><rsub|H>\<geqslant\>-e<around|(|y|)>*s<around|(|O|)>,<space|2em>e<around|(|y|)>\<geqslant\>0,<space|1em>y*<space|0.17em>e<around|(|y|)>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation>

  Here <math|e> is a function of the recording parameter, evaluated at
  <math|y=r<around|(|x|)>> in this application. Since
  <math|r<around|(|x|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>, its eventual estimates apply for all
  sufficiently large <math|x>. In fact its local error has upper exponent
  <math|-3/2>, and the number of annular rows per share is bounded by a fixed
  polynomial in <math|B>. This gives the asserted decay and also supplies the
  local chart representation of every selected row.

  The same bound applies when the fine rows of <math|Q> are read at
  <math|\<tau\>>. To see why, the annulus has fine chord at most
  <math|\<nu\>/2\<leqslant\>\<tau\>>. The only possible obstruction is the
  exponential path-kept test. Its nontrivial range is contained in radius
  <math|B/50> by the purchased path-counting bound; in that range the
  clean-annulus condition gives chord at most <math|\<nu\>/2>. Outside it the
  right side of the test is at least the universal chord bound <math|2>. Thus
  every relevant path is kept at both thresholds <math|\<nu\>> and
  <math|\<tau\>>. Both fine rows have exactly the same quadratic formula
  there. The other rows never changed. This proves equality of the two
  annular energies, including at threshold equality.

  Since <math|\<nu\><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>\<beta\><rsub|F><around|(|y|)><rsup|-1>>
  and <math|\<beta\><rsub|F><around|(|y|)>/y\<to\>1>,
  <eqref|eq:owner-annulus-loss> implies <math|e<around|(|y|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|b<rsub|0>/2|)>*\<nu\><rsup|2>>
  eventually. Adding <eqref|eq:owner-rank-income> therefore gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:owner-selected-gain>\<cal-E\><rsub|\<tau\>><around|(|O|)>+\<cal-W\><rsub|\<tau\>><around|(|Q|)>-\<cal-X\><rsub|H>\<geqslant\><frac|b<rsub|0>|2>*\<nu\><rsup|2>*s<around|(|O|)>\<geqslant\><frac|b<rsub|0>|8>*\<tau\><rsup|2>*s<around|(|O|)>.
  </equation>

  This estimate holds for every admitted selected window and actual chart
  signal, uniformly in the finite period. The threshold is chosen after its
  fixed window and chart constants.

  <subsubsection|The harvested ceiling of an actual
  packet><label|subsec:owner-harvested-ceiling>

  We apply <eqref|eq:owner-selected-gain> to the ceiling used in the block
  exponent. Fix the full store <math|A>, an owner packet <math|O>, and
  separation radius <math|R\<geqslant\>205*B>. Assume the packet ownership
  conditions, the actual seed conditions on <math|<around|(|U,V|)>>, positive
  frame likelihood, and nonzero chart doors. Assume also
  <math|<math-up|span>\<leqslant\>B/50>, the coarse side at least
  <math|100*<around|(|1+B|)>>, and the purchased local chart comparison with
  width <math|w>. The chart radius is <math|w*\<nu\>>, is positive and is
  smaller than the first logarithmic radius. These hypotheses hold before the
  fine field or its coordinates are varied.

  Let <math|\<cal-S\>> be the set of signed permutations of the four lattice
  axes: a permutation and an independent choice of reflection for each axis.
  Put <math|\<omega\><rsub|\<cal-S\>>=1/<around|\||\<cal-S\>|\|>> and write
  <math|<math-up|Av><rsub|\<sigma\>><space|0.17em>F<around|(|\<sigma\>|)>=\<omega\><rsub|\<cal-S\>>*<big|sum><rsub|\<sigma\>\<in\>\<cal-S\>>F<around|(|\<sigma\>|)>>.
  For each <math|\<sigma\>>, turn the owner and full store, the fine and
  coarse fields, the forest and the tag together. Let
  <math|Q<rsub|\<sigma\>>> be the annulus between radii <math|2*B> and
  <math|200*B> of the turned owner. Let <math|H<rsub|\<sigma\>>> consist of
  the actual Gaussian rows selected from this annulus. The span bound keeps
  each selected row outside the <math|2*B> core. The packet separation
  <math|R\<geqslant\>205*B> keeps the annulus away from other owners. Thus
  <math|H<rsub|\<sigma\>>> selects a subwindow of <math|Q<rsub|\<sigma\>>>
  satisfying exactly the annular hypotheses used above.

  The signal in those rows is the actual signed coordinate array, not an
  independently chosen logarithm. On tree bonds both its increment and the
  fine field are the identity. On frozen bonds, the clean chart has norm at
  most <math|K*\<nu\>> with <math|1\<leqslant\>K\<leqslant\>w>; the cutoff
  logarithm agrees with that chart because it lies within the chosen radius.
  On active bonds the nonzero doors bound the actual coordinate by
  <math|2*w*\<nu\>>. Therefore this same array reconstructs the turned fine
  field and has norm at most <math|2*w*\<nu\>> throughout every selected
  stencil. The chart argument in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:packet-annulus>
  applies with width <math|2*w>. It proves the hypotheses of
  <eqref|eq:owner-selected-gain> for every <math|H<rsub|\<sigma\>>>.

  Let <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|200*B><around|(|O|)>> be the oriented elementary
  energy addresses whose coarse anchors lie within distance <math|200*B> of
  <math|O>. The definition of the actual ceiling charge gives the exact
  identity

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:owner-ceiling-identity>

    <\eqsplit>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<omega\><rsub|\<cal-S\>>*<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>\<cal-C\><rsub|200*B><around|(|O|)>><math-up|levy><rsub|j>=<math-up|Av><rsub|\<sigma\>><around*|[|\<cal-W\><rsub|\<tau\>><around|(|<math-up|anchor><rsub|200*B><around|(|\<sigma\>*O|)>|)>-\<cal-X\><rsub|H<rsub|\<sigma\>>><around|(|a<rsub|\<sigma\>>|)>+\<cal-C\><rsub|\<sigma\>><around|(|m|)>|]>.>>>>
    </eqsplit>
  </equation>

  Here <math|a<rsub|\<sigma\>>> is the actual array just described, <math|m>
  is the localized Gaussian mean, and <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|\<sigma\>><around|(|m|)>>
  is the sum of its charged fine and predictor squares. It is nonnegative.
  Each address on the left already includes its signed orientation, so the
  single factor <math|\<omega\><rsub|\<cal-S\>>> normalizes the sum over
  those orientations.

  For completeness, the identity is a finite reindexing. Partition the
  oriented energy addresses into fine faces, predictor bonds, coarse faces
  and normalization rows. Their anchored sums are the corresponding four
  terms of <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|\<tau\>>>. At a selected fine or predictor
  address the charged coordinate square equals the extracted affine square of
  the signed array. This uses the actual path bound and authorized predictor
  branch. At an unselected address both subtracted terms are zero. The mean
  squares remain with the positive sign in the definition of the levy.
  Summing these identities yields <eqref|eq:owner-ceiling-identity>.

  Finally the anchored energy partitions exactly into its core and annulus:

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-W\><rsub|\<tau\>><around|(|<math-up|anchor><rsub|200*B><around|(|\<sigma\>*O|)>|)>=\<cal-E\><rsub|\<tau\>>*<around|(|\<sigma\>*O|)>+\<cal-W\><rsub|\<tau\>><around|(|Q<rsub|\<sigma\>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  This is the finite disjoint partition of each of the four row inventories;
  the changed fine threshold is used on both sides. Apply
  <eqref|eq:owner-selected-gain>, drop the nonnegative mean squares, and use
  <math|s*<around|(|\<sigma\>*O|)>=s<around|(|O|)>>. Averaging proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:owner-actual-heft><frac|b<rsub|0>|8>*\<tau\><rsup|2>*s<around|(|O|)>\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|\<cal-S\>>*<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>\<cal-C\><rsub|200*B><around|(|O|)>><math-up|levy><rsub|j>.
  </equation>

  This is the harvested owner-energy estimate required in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-corrected-body-compiler>. It holds
  for all actual compatible packets, forests, tags and coordinates with the
  stated seed, likelihood and door conditions. The uniform chart, branch and
  seed estimates used to obtain those conditions remain the previously stated
  construction inputs; no assertion about the separate all-compatible warning
  factor is made by this energy calculation.

  <subsection|The corrected unmarked alphabet on the actual
  block><label|sec:actual-corrected-body-compiler>

  The marked expansion needs one unmarked alphabet chosen before the number
  and locations of the insertions. We construct that alphabet from the actual
  block exponent. Its reconstruction identity concerns the original fine
  density. Its smallness and normalization estimates also use the
  representation with the additive remainder removed. Keeping these two uses
  on the same alphabet is essential.

  <subsubsection|The fixed data and the remaining analytic inputs>

  Fix one step with fine and coarse tori, its actual path pair, clips and
  conditional Gaussian construction. Write <math|P> for the fine
  representation, <math|\<beta\>\<gtr\>0> for its coupling, <math|\<theta\>>
  for its flag threshold, and <math|J> for the local Haar chart ratio. Fix a
  reference forest <math|F<rsub|0>> and tag <math|k<rsub|0>>. Use the signed
  orientation set <math|\<cal-S\>> and its normalization
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|\<cal-S\>>=1/<around|\||\<cal-S\>|\|>> from
  Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:owner-harvested-ceiling>. It is
  separate from the normalized tag average. Put <math|y=r<around|(|x|)>> for
  the recording parameter, <math|B=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
  y|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>, and <math|z=\<beta\><rsub|F><around|(|x|)>> for
  the adjacent fine parameter. All the objects below use this one choice of
  data. In particular, the fine path and clip in the Gaussian construction
  are exactly those of <math|P>.

  The following hypotheses specify the analytic interface of this section.
  The actual Gaussian data have the previously established positive floors
  and local decompositions; their two determinant series and inverse
  correction have the purchased support prices. The Gaussian coordinate and
  linear-residual bounds on the chart mask are respectively <math|y<rsup|2>>
  and <math|y<rsup|-4>>. The coarse budget radius <math|B<rsub|c>> and fine
  radius <math|B<rsub|f>> satisfy

  <\equation*>
    25*B+30\<leqslant\>B<rsub|c>\<leqslant\>B<rsup|2>,<space|2em>B<rsub|f>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|c>.
  </equation*>

  The inherited activity estimate has its positive support-exponent reserve,
  the actual path-counting bound, and

  <\equation*>
    \<Gamma\>\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rsub|F><around|(|y|)><rsup|1/8>,<space|2em>\<delta\>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|-1/2>,<space|2em>\<beta\>\<leqslant\>4*y,<space|2em>\<theta\>\<leqslant\>2<space|0.17em><math-up|fiery><around|(|y|)>.
  </equation*>

  The fine coupling and threshold also obey
  <math|z/2\<leqslant\>\<beta\>\<leqslant\>2*z> and
  <math|\<theta\>=z<rsup|-1/2><math-up|hot><around|(|z|)>>. The coarse budget
  threshold is exactly <math|<math-up|temp><around|(|y|)>>, the fine
  threshold satisfies <math|\<theta\>\<geqslant\><math-up|fiery><around|(|y|)>/2>,
  and the profile width <math|W<rsub|<math-up|prof>>> is positive. The chart
  radius is a fixed nonnegative multiple of
  <math|<math-up|fiery><around|(|y|)>>, and its cutoff and logarithmic radius
  conditions hold. These are actual data conditions, uniform in period,
  forest and tag; they cannot be supplied by choosing an unrelated
  representation with the same nominal bounds.

  Two further inputs must be distinguished. The owner-energy estimate bounds
  the harvested ceiling charge for the actual seeded packet whenever its
  chart doors are nonzero. The warning estimate is uniform over all
  compatible field/store pairs: it bounds the actual Gaussian palette and
  makes the warning factor vanish under the stated local field and Gaussian
  bounds. A comparison on posed reader patches alone is insufficient for this
  second assertion. Their exact applications to the harvested packets and
  warning factors remain prerequisites of the present construction.

  <subsubsection|A local decomposition of the real exponent>

  An admissible configuration of the local calculation consists of a store,
  forest, tag, coarse field, frozen links and Gaussian coordinates. Denote it
  by <math|\<rho\>>. Compatibility means that the stored coarse flags agree
  with those of the coarse field. Let <math|\<cal-A\><around|(|\<rho\>|)>> be
  the event that the product of the chart, profile, likelihood and wire masks
  is nonzero. Its factors are the actual factors in the block integral,
  including the finite tag count in the site and bond factors. Precisely,

  <\equation*>
    <big|prod><rsub|j><math-up|mask><rsub|j>=<math-up|profile>\<cdot\>#<math-up|Choices>\<cdot\><math-up|likelihood>\<cdot\><math-up|doors>.
  </equation*>

  This tag multiplicity is present in the pointwise sliced identity; it
  cancels the denominator of the normalized tag average when the block
  integral is reconstructed.

  Let <math|\<cal-O\><around|(|\<rho\>|)>> be the finite collection of owners
  of the seeded components. The real exponent remaining after extraction of
  the scalar Gaussian normalization decomposes as

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:actual-body-exponent-split>E<around|(|\<rho\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-O\><around|(|\<rho\>|)>><around*|(|c<rsub|i><around|(|\<rho\>|)>+r<rsub|i><around|(|\<rho\>|)>|)>+T<around|(|\<rho\>|)>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|c<rsub|i>> is minus <math|\<beta\>> times the actual ceiling
  charge assigned to owner <math|i>, and <math|r<rsub|i>> is its allocated
  local determinant and frozen-coordinate normalization. The remaining term
  is the sum of eight specified contributions: exterior ceiling charges, the
  linear residual, the quadratic inverse correction, exterior local
  determinants, the relative logarithmic determinant series, its
  inverse-correction logarithmic series, the local Haar logarithm, and the
  inherited fine/coarse remainder. This identity is obtained by partitioning
  the ceiling charges into owner ceilings and their complement, partitioning
  the local determinants in the same way, and retaining both convergent
  determinant series. The linear and Haar terms are sums over the active
  coordinates; the inverse correction is its absolutely convergent path
  expansion. These operations are justified by the prices below and the
  determinant identities of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-reassembly>.

  For clarity, a local price at radius <math|B<rsub|c>> and exponent
  <math|e\<geqslant\>0> means nonnegative numbers <math|b<rsub|j>> and
  nonempty supports <math|X<rsub|j>> with

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:actual-body-local-price>sup<rsub|v>
    <big|sum><rsub|j:v\<in\>X<rsub|j>>e<rsup|e<space|0.17em><math-up|cov><rsub|B<rsub|c>><around|(|X<rsub|j>|)>>*b<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>t,<space|2em><around|\||L<rsub|j><around|(|\<rho\>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|j><space|1em><text|on
    >\<cal-A\>.
  </equation>

  The functions <math|L<rsub|j>> depend measurably only on the indicated
  local data. The cover number is the one used for the inherited activity
  prices. Set

  <\equation*>
    q=y<rsup|-1/8>,<space|2em>\<nu\>=y<rsup|-5>,<space|2em>a<rsub|y>=y<rsup|-1/24>.
  </equation*>

  The actual decomposition has the following prices:

  <\equation*>
    <tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-halign|l>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-lborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-rborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-rborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|<text|contribution
    to >T>|<cell|<text|local price>>>|<row|<cell|<text|exterior ceiling
    charges>>|<cell|q>>|<row|<cell|<text|linear
    residual>>|<cell|q>>|<row|<cell|<text|quadratic inverse
    correction>>|<cell|y<rsup|4>*\<nu\>>>|<row|<cell|<text|exterior local
    determinants>>|<cell|q>>|<row|<cell|<text|relative logarithmic
    determinant series>>|<cell|\<nu\>>>|<row|<cell|<text|inverse-correction
    logarithmic series>>|<cell|\<nu\>>>|<row|<cell|<text|local Haar
    logarithm>>|<cell|q>>|<row|<cell|<text|inherited fine/coarse
    remainder>>|<cell|a<rsub|y>>>>>>
  </equation*>

  We spell out the conversion of the earlier estimates into this table. Each
  exterior ceiling term is at most <math|4*\<omega\><rsub|\<cal-S\>>*<space|0.17em>y<rsup|-1/4>>
  on <math|\<cal-A\>>. Its support lies in a coarse halo of radius
  <math|420*B> at its assigned address. The bounded address density and halo
  volume cost only a fixed polynomial in <math|B>; the cover number at radius
  <math|B> is bounded by a fixed constant. A polynomial in <math|B> times
  <math|y<rsup|-1/4>> is eventually at most <math|y<rsup|-1/8>>. This proves
  the first line. For the linear term, the product of a residual coordinate
  bounded by <math|y<rsup|-4>> and a Gaussian coordinate bounded by
  <math|y<rsup|2>> is at most <math|y<rsup|-2>>. Sum over the fixed color
  dimension and use the same halo calculation to obtain the second line. The
  quadratic term has two such Gaussian factors, so the inverse-correction
  path price is multiplied by at most <math|y<rsup|4>>. The factor <math|1/2>
  in the quadratic form only improves the bound.

  Outside the owner neighbourhood of radius <math|200*B> there is no
  frozen-coordinate charge. The actual local determinant estimate there is at
  most <math|y<rsup|-1/4>>, and its radius-<math|420*B> support gives the
  fourth line by the first calculation. The two remaining determinant series
  have their actual word supports and prices <math|exp <around|(|-d*B|)>>
  with fixed positive decay rates <math|d>. Since
  <math|B\<geqslant\><around|(|log y|)><rsup|2>>, each is eventually at most
  <math|y<rsup|-5>>: it suffices that <math|d*log y\<geqslant\>5>. This gives
  the next two lines without discarding either series.

  The extended Haar logarithm satisfies <math|<around|\||log
  J<rsub|<math-up|ext>><around|(|u|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*min
  <around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>>,4<space|0.17em><math-up|arc>|)><rsup|2>>.
  The chart radius assumption and <math|<math-up|fiery><around|(|y|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|-1/4>>
  therefore give another <math|C*y<rsup|-1/4>> bound on the same local halo,
  proving the seventh line. This estimate remains valid for the extended
  chart, so no unproved restriction on <math|u> is inserted.

  Finally apply Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:inherited-activity-prices>
  with the actual positive reserve and <math|B<rsub|f>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|c>>.
  The inherited label term costs at most a fixed multiple of
  <math|B<rsup|8>*\<delta\>>, which is at most <math|y<rsup|-1/8>>
  eventually. The two fine/coarse tail terms each cost
  <math|k*l*<space|0.17em>\<beta\><rsub|F><around|(|y|)><rsup|-1>>, and the
  quadratic, cubic and quartic path terms each cost
  <math|k*\<beta\><rsub|F><around|(|y|)><rsup|-1/12>>. Here
  <math|k=<around|\||\<cal-S\>|\|>> and <math|l> is the number of plaquette
  axes. The path coefficients have the required smallness because

  <\equation*>
    \<omega\><rsub|\<cal-S\>>*\<theta\><rsup|2>*\<Gamma\>,<space|1em>\<beta\>*\<omega\><rsub|\<cal-S\>>*\<theta\><rsup|3>*C,<space|1em>\<beta\>*\<omega\><rsub|\<cal-S\>>*\<theta\><rsup|4>*C\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rsub|F><around|(|y|)><rsup|-1/8>
  </equation*>

  eventually, with the fixed path bound <math|C> and the hypotheses above.
  This is the specialization to the quadratic, cubic and quartic terms of the
  inherited estimate. Since <math|\<beta\><rsub|F><around|(|y|)>\<geqslant\>y>,
  its total price is at most <math|<around|(|1+2*k*l+3*k|)>*y<rsup|-1/12>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|-1/24>>
  eventually. Increasing the support radius from <math|B> to <math|B<rsub|c>>
  decreases its cover number and preserves every preceding price.

  Thus <math|T> has total price

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:actual-body-price-total>t<rsub|y>=q+q+y<rsup|4>*\<nu\>+<around|(|q+<around|(|\<nu\>+\<nu\>|)>|)>+q+a<rsub|y>.
  </equation>

  For <math|y\<geqslant\>1>, <math|\<nu\>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|4>*\<nu\>\<leqslant\>q\<leqslant\>a<rsub|y>>,
  whence <math|2*t<rsub|y>\<leqslant\>16*a<rsub|y>>. Eventually
  <math|16*y<rsup|-1/24>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|-1/48>>. In particular
  <math|t<rsub|y>\<leqslant\>1> and <math|2*t<rsub|y>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|-1/48>>.

  <subsubsection|Bounded extension and averaging>

  Let <math|s<rsub|i>> be the number of seed shares belonging to owner
  <math|i>. Choose <math|\<alpha\>\<gtr\>0> with
  <math|2*\<alpha\>\<leqslant\>a/2>, where <math|a\<gtr\>0> is the
  owner-energy constant. That estimate and the local normalization bound
  give, on <math|\<cal-A\>> for occurring owners,

  <\equation*>
    c<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>-\<beta\>*a*\<theta\><rsup|2>*s<rsub|i>,<space|2em><around|\||r<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<alpha\><space|0.17em><math-up|hot><around|(|z|)><rsup|2>*s<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed the normalization is bounded by <math|C*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>*log
  <around|(|y|)>*<space|0.17em>s<rsub|i>> with a fixed constant <math|C>. The
  scale payment makes this at most <math|\<alpha\><space|0.17em><math-up|hot><around|(|z|)><rsup|2>*s<rsub|i>>
  eventually. Since <math|z*\<theta\><rsup|2>=<math-up|hot><around|(|z|)><rsup|2>>
  and <math|\<beta\>\<geqslant\>z/2>,

  <\equation*>
    2*\<alpha\><space|0.17em><math-up|hot><around|(|z|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>\<beta\>*a*\<theta\><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  Consequently <math|c<rsub|i>+r<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>-A<rsub|i>>, where
  <math|A<rsub|i>=\<alpha\><space|0.17em><math-up|hot><around|(|z|)><rsup|2>*s<rsub|i>>.
  The finite-volume lower estimate for the same ceiling charges also gives
  <math|c<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>u> with finite <math|u>, uniformly over the
  compatible data on <math|\<cal-A\>>. It need not be uniform in the volume.
  Set

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|i>=min <around|(|-A<rsub|i>,u-A<rsub|i>|)>,<space|2em>b<rsub|i><around|(|\<rho\>|)>=max
    <around|{|D<rsub|i>,min <around|{|-A<rsub|i>,c<rsub|i><around|(|\<rho\>|)>+r<rsub|i><around|(|\<rho\>|)>|}>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Then <math|D<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>-A<rsub|i>> everywhere
  on compatible data, and <math|b<rsub|i>=c<rsub|i>+r<rsub|i>> for occurring
  owners on <math|\<cal-A\>>. The clamping operation is continuous, preserves
  locality and makes <math|b<rsub|i>> bounded. This extension permits
  averaging before the nonzero-mask event is imposed.

  Similarly replace every local summand <math|L<rsub|j>> of <math|T> by its
  clamp to <math|<around|[|-b<rsub|j>,b<rsub|j>|]>>, using the prices in
  <eqref|eq:actual-body-local-price>. This changes no value on
  <math|\<cal-A\>>. Coalesce terms with the same nonempty support. The sums
  converge absolutely: summing the site prices over the finite torus bounds
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|j>b<rsub|j>>, because each support contains a site and
  <math|e\<geqslant\>0>. The grouped local terms <math|h<rsub|X>> retain the
  same site price and sum to <math|T> on <math|\<cal-A\>>.

  Average both the owner functions and the grouped terms over the finite
  signed lattice motions and the product of local Haar gauge measures.
  Transport supports, owners, stores, forests and tags together. The actual
  exponent and the nonzero-mask event are invariant under this common action.
  Thus averaging <eqref|eq:actual-body-exponent-split> gives local invariant
  functions <math|<wide|b|\<bar\>><rsub|i>,<wide|h|\<bar\>><rsub|X>>
  satisfying

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:actual-body-averaged>E=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-O\>><wide|b|\<bar\>><rsub|i>+<big|sum><rsub|X><wide|h|\<bar\>><rsub|X><space|1em><text|on
    >\<cal-A\>,<space|2em><wide|b|\<bar\>><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>-\<alpha\><space|0.17em><math-up|hot><around|(|z|)><rsup|2>*s<rsub|i>.
  </equation>

  Boundedness makes all these averages integrable. The action preserves seed
  shares and the cover number, so the averaged majorants <math|Y<rsub|X>>
  satisfy the same site price <math|t<rsub|y>>. For any site of <math|X>,
  that price gives <math|Y<rsub|X>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|y>\<leqslant\>1>.
  Therefore

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||e<rsup|<wide|h|\<bar\>><rsub|X>>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>2<around|\||<wide|h|\<bar\>><rsub|X>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*Y<rsub|X>.
  </equation*>

  The auxiliary letters <math|e<rsup|<wide|h|\<bar\>><rsub|X>>-1> have total
  local price at most <math|2*t<rsub|y>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|-1/48>>. Together
  with the negative owner functions, these are the corrected unmarked
  alphabet. This is a value estimate; it does not assert arbitrary
  differentiated estimates merely from bounded extension.

  <subsubsection|The scalar normalization and the genuine density>

  Write <math|K<rsub|0>> for the bare kinetic matrix at the fixed reference
  forest <math|F<rsub|0>> and tag <math|k<rsub|0>>, <math|N<rsub|f>> for the
  number of fine sites, and <math|n<rsub|0>> for the number of free reference
  bonds. The Lie algebra dimension is already included in the factor
  <math|<math-up|spare>>. The scalar extracted from every actual sector is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:actual-body-loading>\<Lambda\><around|(|V|)>=<math-up|bank><around|(|V|)><space|0.17em><math-up|pref><around|(|\<beta\>|)>*e<rsup|P.*v*N<rsub|f>><space|0.17em><math-up|spare><around|(|J,\<beta\>|)><rsup|n<rsub|0>><around|(|det
    K<rsub|0>|)><rsup|-1/2>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation>

  All factors are positive, including the determinant by the bare kinetic
  floor. The forest determinant and free-bond count identities of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-reassembly> identify this same
  scalar for every actual forest and tag. No new scalar is selected after a
  marked family is specified.

  Here is the exponent check. Let <math|Q> be the actual covariance, <math|W>
  its Gram precision, <math|m> its localized mean, <math|Z> its Gaussian
  coordinate and <math|g=\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>>. Let <math|U> be the fine field
  constructed from <math|m+g*Z> and the frozen links. Denote the quadratic
  guide by <math|G<rsub|0>>, the fine cut action by <math|R<rsub|f>>, the
  coarse quadratic action by <math|e<rsub|0>>, and the prediction mismatch
  cost by <math|M<rsub|0>>. The charge decomposition and quadratic completion
  give respectively

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<omega\><rsub|\<cal-S\>>*<big|sum><rsub|j><math-up|levy><rsub|j>>|<cell|=M<rsub|0>+<around|(|R<rsub|f>-e<rsub|0>|)>-G<rsub|0>*<around|(|m+g*Z|)>+G<rsub|0><around|(|m|)>,>>|<row|<cell|-\<beta\>*G<rsub|0><around|(|m|)>+\<eta\>\<cdot\>Z+<tfrac|1|2>Z<rsup|\<top\>>*<around|(|Q<rsup|-1>-W|)>*Z>|<cell|=<tfrac|1|2><math-up|force><rsub|Q><around|(|Z|)>-\<beta\>*G<rsub|0>*<around|(|m+g*Z|)>.>>>>
  </align*>

  The inherited remainder satisfies <math|P.*v*N<rsub|f>+<math-up|apart><around|(|U|)>=P.<math-up|exponent><around|(|U|)>+\<beta\>*R<rsub|f>>.
  The bank identity is <math|e<rsup|-\<beta\>*e<rsub|0>>=<math-up|bank><around|(|V|)>*e<rsup|<math-up|tail><rsub|c><around|(|V|)>>>.
  Substitution into the definition of <math|E> cancels <math|R<rsub|f>> and
  both guide terms and yields

  <\equation*>
    E=E<rsub|det>+<math-up|tail><rsub|c>+<tfrac|1|2><math-up|force><rsub|Q><around|(|Z|)>+log
    J<rsub|<math-up|ext>>+\<beta\>*e<rsub|0>-\<beta\>*M<rsub|0>+P.<math-up|exponent><around|(|U|)>-P.*v*N<rsub|f>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|E<rsub|det>> includes the frozen-coordinate logarithm and both
  determinant logarithms. Precisely,

  <\equation*>
    J.<math-up|scale><rsup|#<math-up|Active>><space|0.17em><math-up|chartCost><around|(|Q,\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>|)>=<math-up|spare><around|(|J,\<beta\>|)><rsup|n<rsub|0>><around|(|det
    K<rsub|0>|)><rsup|-1/2>*e<rsup|E<rsub|det>>,
  </equation*>

  as in <eqref|eq:reassembly-overhead>\U<eqref|eq:reassembly-logs>;
  <math|#<math-up|Active>> also counts bonds. Also the actual prediction
  noise equals <math|<math-up|pref><around|(|\<beta\>|)>*e<rsup|-\<beta\>*M<rsub|0>>>,
  and the product of extended Haar factors is <math|e<rsup|log
  J<rsub|<math-up|ext>>>>. Multiplying these identities proves the pointwise
  identity

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:actual-body-density><math-up|freight><around|(|\<rho\>|)>=\<Lambda\><around|(|V|)>*e<rsup|E<around|(|\<rho\>|)>>,
  </equation>

  where freight is the original fine exponential times the prediction noise,
  chart Jacobian, Gaussian density conversion and extended Haar factors. Thus
  the exponential here is that of the genuine <math|P>.

  <subsubsection|Flags, connected components and marked reuse>

  Expand each auxiliary exponential as <math|1+<around|(|e<rsup|<wide|h|\<bar\>><rsub|X>>-1|)>>.
  Outside the owner regions the warning letter is the corresponding mask
  minus one; hence its factor <math|1+<math-up|warning>> is exactly that
  mask. Within an owner region, the owner letter already includes the product
  of these masks. Owner regions are disjoint. Multiplying the factors
  therefore recovers <math|e<rsup|<big|sum><wide|b|\<bar\>><rsub|i>+<big|sum><wide|h|\<bar\>><rsub|X>>>
  times the product of all actual masks.

  There is also no multiplicity from decorating owners with fine flags. Each
  owner decoration is required to equal the fine flags in its assigned
  region, so exactly one decoration survives for each owner. On
  <math|\<cal-A\>>, the warning/door implication places every actual fine
  flag in an owner region. The union of these unique decorations is therefore
  exactly the actual fine flag set. Grouping overlapping letters into
  connected components gives a bijection between compatible component
  collections and the underlying letter choices, as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-planted-compiler>. It preserves
  both flag unions and the stored seed data. Consequently the sum with fine
  flag inventory <math|u> is

  <\equation*>
    \<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|<math-up|flags><rsub|\<theta\>><around|(|U|)>=u|}>>*e<rsup|E<around|(|\<rho\>|)>>*<big|prod><rsub|j><math-up|mask><rsub|j><around|(|\<rho\>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Outside <math|\<cal-A\>> both sides vanish because the full mask product is
  zero; no division by a mask is used. Combining this identity with
  <eqref|eq:actual-body-density> proves the genuine body identity
  <eqref|eq:planted-body> for this corrected alphabet and the scalar
  <eqref|eq:actual-body-loading>.

  For the same alphabet, apply the real additive correction and masked
  logarithm construction of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:restarted-rank-history>.
  The required warning and product prices, support reserve and signed/gauge
  transport must be those of the alphabet just constructed. Here is the
  deleted-support calculation on that alphabet.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Deleting supports from the corrected regular
    gas><label|prop:corrected-regular-deletion>Assume the absolute
    regular-cluster estimate used in <eqref|eq:restart-log> on the corrected
    alphabet, and use its coarse exponent and boundary correction. Then
    <eqref|eq:planted-holes> holds at every coarse configuration and for
    every deleted spatial support <math|D>. The hole factors are
    <math|h<rsub|b>=exp <around|(|-c<rsub|b>|)>-1>, where <math|c<rsub|b>> is
    the masked regular logarithmic entry with full support
    <math|X<rsub|b><rsup|<math-up|hole>>> in the corrected alphabet.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Fix the coarse configuration. The unrestricted regular gas is <math|exp
    <around|(|<big|sum><rsub|b>c<rsub|b>|)>>, with
    <math|c<rsub|b>=-\<ell\><rsub|b>> in the convention of
    <eqref|eq:restart-log>. Delete every elementary regular packet whose
    support meets <math|D>. The logarithmic term for a connected tuple
    survives exactly when its union support avoids <math|D>. The same
    absolute majorant applies to the restricted series, and therefore

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-G\><rsub|Z;D>=\<cal-G\><rsub|Z;\<varnothing\>>*exp
      <around*|(|-<big|sum><rsub|b:<space|0.17em>X<rsub|b><rsup|<math-up|hole>>\<cap\>D\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>c<rsub|b>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The unpunctured factor <math|\<Lambda\>*\<cal-G\><rsub|Z;\<varnothing\>>>
    is the exponential of the coarse exponent times the product of the
    boundary factors: the scalar prefactor contributes its logarithm per
    coarse site, the regular sum contributes
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|b>c<rsub|b>>, and the boundary factors retain the
    residual action correction with its original masks. This is the
    normalization of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:restarted-rank-history>
    on the corrected alphabet; when the full action correction is absorbed
    into the coarse exponent the boundary product is empty. Expanding the
    displayed exponential as the product of <math|1+h<rsub|b>> gives exactly
    <eqref|eq:planted-holes>. Absolute summability of <math|c<rsub|b>>
    implies that of <math|h<rsub|b>> by <math|<around|\||e<rsup|-c<rsub|b>>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<around|\||c<rsub|b>|\|>><around|\||c<rsub|b>|\|>>.
    No quotient by a masked packet or by a forced activity is used.
  </proof>

  We next specify the forced activities of the output; the scalar and regular
  family alone would not specify them.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The forced output on the corrected
    alphabet><label|prop:corrected-forced-output>Use the corrected body
    packets above and the archived background of <math|P<rsup|0>>. Choose the
    coarse scalar and additive family by Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:restarted-rank-history>,
    and choose its forced activities by the construction below. Then, for
    every finite marker family, the unaggregated component family <math|F> of
    the proof satisfies the embedding and exhaustion requirements after
    <eqref|eq:planted-holes>, and its attached family <math|K> has the
    addresswise sums of this output <math|Q>, as required there. Its scalar
    density is the pushforward of the actual input <math|P>, including its
    additive remainder.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    First use an empty marker family. Let <math|F> be the unaggregated family
    of forced components of the corrected unmarked alphabet. Attach the hole
    factors of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:corrected-regular-deletion>
    to these components, then attach the boundary factors, grouping at each
    step by the same support-intersection relation used in forced exclusion.
    Denote the resulting family by <math|K>. A label retains its full support
    <math|X> and its flag inventory <math|I\<subseteq\>X>, not merely its
    value. All its values are real: the original unmarked integrands are
    real, integration and the forest average preserve reality, and so do the
    real hole and boundary factors.

    For every address <math|<around|(|X,I|)>> define

    <\equation*>
      k<rsub|X,I><around|(|V|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j:<space|0.17em><around|(|X<rsub|j>,I<rsub|j>|)>=<around|(|X,I|)>>K<rsub|j><around|(|V|)>.
    </equation*>

    These sums have the component majorants already purchased for the
    corrected alphabet. Store <math|k<rsub|X,I>> in one slot of the countable
    forced family at that address and fill the other slots by zero. Keep the
    scalar, coupling, path action and additive family unchanged. This defines
    <math|Q>. In particular, its archived activity at <math|<around|(|X,I|)>>
    is exactly the displayed sum. Locality, gauge invariance and the
    eligibility test are preserved because every summand has this same
    address; the triangle inequality preserves the purchased price. No old,
    unrelated coarse forced activity is retained by this assignment.

    Now choose any finite marker family. The unmarked elementary alphabet
    embeds by retaining every corrected body label and taking the zero-marker
    copy of each archived fine background label. Values, supports,
    inventories and sector data are unchanged. The union of marker sets is
    empty only if every constituent marker set is empty. Consequently this
    embedding exhausts the zero-marker components of the full alphabet. This
    argument also survives each attachment step: map the finite set of
    constituents by the embedding and leave hole and boundary labels fixed.
    Support intersections, compatibility and products are preserved.
    Conversely a component with empty marker set contains only labels in that
    image, so taking their unique preimages recovers the component. The two
    maps are inverse. Thus the attached zero-marker family is exactly
    <math|K>, reindexed on the new marker stencil, and summing at each
    address proves its background identity with <math|Q>. The embedding and
    exhaustion concern the components <math|F> and <math|K>, not the
    aggregated slots <math|k<rsub|X,I>> of <math|Q>, which enter only through
    these addresswise sums.

    For completeness, a boundary factor whose support meets no forced
    component contributes one. Exact flag coverage puts every flag in a
    forced inventory; such a boundary support is therefore healthy, where its
    correction vanishes. This justifies using the same boundary product
    before and after component attachment, including on flagged
    configurations. The regular deleted-support formula and this exact forced
    expansion now reconstruct the scalar density. With no markers, the body
    identity integrates <math|P=e<rsup|-E>*P<rsup|0>>, rather than
    <math|P<rsup|0>>. Reassembly followed by the two component bijections
    therefore gives <math|Q=\<cal-K\><rsub|\<beta\>>*P>. All rearrangements
    use the purchased absolute majorants. None divides by a density or a
    forced activity.
  </proof>

  The output here is the representation constructed from this alphabet; the
  conclusion does not identify an independently prescribed coarse
  representation merely because its parameters agree. Only then introduce an
  arbitrary finite family of marked sites and its parent map. The marked
  decorations vary with that family; the body alphabet, genuine body identity
  and scalar do not. Decreasing the already purchased exponents and
  multipliers gives both uses in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-mixed-marked-compiler>.
  The separate harvested-energy and all-compatible-warning prerequisites at
  the start remain necessary for this application.

  <subsection|The mixed marked compiler on the restarted
  history><label|sec:actual-mixed-marked-compiler>

  We give the price calculation which turns the actual three-alphabet
  expansion of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-planted-compiler> into
  the upper and lower marked transitions. All marked integrals use the
  genuine fine representation <math|P>. Its auxiliary representation
  <math|P<rsup|0>> has only its additive remainder removed. The body packets
  in the two calculations are nevertheless the same corrected packets. This
  distinction is necessary for the normalization argument below.

  <subsubsection|Support currency and the three letter families>

  Fix a fine-to-coarse block map of positive integer side <math|L>, fine and
  coarse budget radii <math|B<rsub|f>,B<rsub|c>> that are positive integers,
  and a finite marker set <math|W>. Let <math|\<theta\>:W\<to\>\<Lambda\><rsub|f>>
  and <math|u:W\<to\>\<Lambda\><rsub|c>> satisfy
  <math|u=<math-up|parent>\<circ\>\<theta\>>. Use the cover charge

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-D\><rsub|B>*<around|(|t,X|)>=exp
    <around|(|t<space|0.17em><math-up|cov><rsub|B><space|0.17em>X|)>,<space|2em>v=e+5,<space|1em>e\<geqslant\>0,
  </equation*>

  where <math|<math-up|cov><rsub|B><space|0.17em>X> is the minimum number of
  radius-<math|B> cubes covering <math|X> whose union is connected. Write
  <math|X<rsup|\<uparrow\>>> for the prescribed coarse neighborhood of the
  parent image of <math|X>. The radius comparison and cover estimate proved
  in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:inherited-activity-prices> give, when
  <math|B<rsub|f>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|c>>,

  <\equation*>
    L<math-up|cov><rsub|B<rsub|c>><around|(|X<rsup|\<uparrow\>>|)>\<leqslant\>15<rsup|4>*<around*|(|L+10<math-up|cov><rsub|B<rsub|f>><space|0.17em>X|)>.
  </equation*>

  Consequently, if <math|10\<cdot\>15<rsup|4>*<around|(|v+2|)>\<leqslant\>L*\<sigma\><rsub|f>>,
  then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:mixed-cover-charge>\<cal-D\><rsub|B<rsub|c>>*<around|(|v+2,X<rsup|\<uparrow\>>|)>\<leqslant\>T<rsub|e>*\<cal-D\><rsub|B<rsub|f>>*<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|f>,X|)>,<space|2em>T<rsub|e>=exp
    <around|(|15<rsup|4>*<around|(|v+2|)>|)>.
  </equation>

  Indeed multiply the cover inequality by <math|<around|(|v+2|)>/L> and
  exponentiate. This pays the additive covering cost before taking any marker
  sum.

  All nonnegative sum estimates below are first proved on finite subfamilies
  and then extended by monotone convergence. There are three letter families
  in one fixed sector and tag: body letters with empty marker support, lifted
  background packets, and nonempty-marker insertion letters. Coalesce
  background packets only when their complete activity address and marker
  support agree. Their zero-marker coefficients must reproduce the fine
  archived activity addresswise. Let <math|z<rsub|a>\<geqslant\>0> be the
  body prices. For a background packet with fine support <math|X<rsub|b>> and
  paid price <math|A<rsub|b>>, use

  <\equation*>
    f<rsub|b>=A<rsub|b>/\<cal-D\><rsub|B<rsub|f>>*<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|f>,X<rsub|b>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The input global packet bound is valid on every fine field, so evaluating
  it on the actual chart gives the pointwise bound <math|f<rsub|b>>,
  including on unhealthy fields. For a regular insertion the analogous
  estimate holds on its healthy support; its original mask makes its value
  zero elsewhere. Division by the positive cover charge is therefore valid in
  both cases. Positive marker weights are included in these prices
  throughout.

  Assume the background site sums obey <math|<big|sum><rsub|b:x\<in\>X<rsub|b>>A<rsub|b>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>>.
  Equation<nbsp><reference|eq:mixed-cover-charge> implies, for each marker
  <math|w>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:mixed-background-marker><big|sum><rsub|b:w\<in\>F<rsub|b>>f<rsub|b>*\<cal-D\><rsub|B<rsub|c>>*<around|(|v+2,X<rsub|b><rsup|\<uparrow\>>|)>\<leqslant\>T<rsub|e>*\<eta\>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<theta\><around|(|w|)>\<in\>X<rsub|b>> whenever
  <math|w\<in\>F<rsub|b>>. There is no factor <math|L<rsup|4>> in this marker
  estimate.

  The corresponding coarse site estimate does have that factor. Choose one
  genuine fine site in the nonempty supported inventory of every background
  packet. At a fixed coarse site at most <math|L<rsup|4>> fine sites have
  that parent. The anchor-to-site estimate loses one cover exponent and has
  cost

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-W\><around|(|B|)>=<around|(|33*B|)><rsup|4>*<big|sum><rsub|j\<geqslant\>0><around|(|j+1|)><rsup|4>*e<rsup|-j>.
  </equation*>

  For completeness, for an anchor <math|a\<in\>X> and <math|x\<in\>X> the
  cover number is at least <math|<around|\<lfloor\>|<math-up|dist><around|(|a,x|)>/<around|(|8*B|)>|\<rfloor\>>>.
  The number of anchors in its <math|j>th distance shell is at most
  <math|<around|(|33*B|)><rsup|4>*<around|(|j+1|)><rsup|4>>. Summing the
  exponential loss proves this period-independent constant. Thus the
  background site price at exponent <math|v+1> is at most
  <math|\<cal-W\><around|(|B<rsub|c>|)>*L<rsup|4>*T<rsub|e>*\<eta\>>.

  Let <math|w<rsub|h>\<geqslant\>0> be the insertion-letter prices, and
  suppose their marker-root sums at exponent <math|v+2> are at most <math|a>.
  If every coarse site receives at most <math|g> markers, choose one marker
  in each letter. The same argument gives site price
  <math|\<cal-W\><around|(|B<rsub|c>|)>*g*a> at exponent <math|v+1>. On the
  actual replicated reader geometry <math|g=n>, the number of reader labels:
  the parent cell is fixed by a coarse site, and only its <math|n> labels
  remain. Combining these bounds with a body site price <math|\<delta\>>
  gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:mixed-letter-price>\<Sigma\>=\<delta\>+\<cal-W\><around|(|B<rsub|c>|)>*L<rsup|4>*T<rsub|e>*\<eta\>+\<cal-W\><around|(|B<rsub|c>|)>*g*a.
  </equation>

  The total marker-root price at exponent <math|v+1> is at most
  <math|T<rsub|e>*\<eta\>+a>. Empty-marker body letters do not contribute to
  it.

  <subsubsection|Payment of each connected integral>

  Fix multipliers <math|m,l\<geqslant\>1>. The body-letter product estimate
  on the actual sector measure supplies the following stronger integral
  bound: if <math|I> is a collection of body letters and <math|X<rsub|I>> is
  the product of their nonnegative absolute weights, then

  <\equation*>
    M<rsub|I>*<big|int>X<rsub|I>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>I>z<rsub|a>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|M<rsub|I>> is the product of their individual inflations: a
  letter with nonempty forced inventory contributes <math|m>, and any
  remaining body letter contributes <math|l>. These are the paid unmarked
  product estimates, with the same chart and corrected body alphabet as
  above. The measure <math|\<nu\>> is the Gaussian/Haar probability measure
  of that sector and tag. Each other letter has its pointwise price. In
  addition, every insertion letter designated as a remainder satisfies one of

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:mixed-shadow>l*h<around|(|\<xi\>|)>\<leqslant\>w<rsub|h><space|1em><text|for
    all >\<xi\>,<space|2em><text|or><space|2em>h=0<text| off
    >E<rsub|h>,<space|1em>l*\<nu\><around|(|E<rsub|h>|)>\<leqslant\>1,
  </equation>

  where <math|0\<leqslant\>h\<leqslant\>w<rsub|h>> and <math|E<rsub|h>> is
  measurable.

  Let <math|C> be a compatible connected collection of letters. Its full
  integrated coefficient, including its marker monomial, is denoted
  <math|b<rsub|C>>. Then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:mixed-club-integral>t<rsub|C><around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsub|C>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|r>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>C>b<rsub|j>,<space|1em>t<rsub|C>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|m,>|<cell|C<text|
    has nonempty forced inventory>,>>|<row|<cell|l,>|<cell|C<text| is
    unforced but is not entirely leading>,>>|<row|<cell|1,>|<cell|C<text| is
    entirely leading>.>>>>>
  </equation>

  To prove this, split its product into body, background and insertion
  factors. The last two products are bounded pointwise by their prices. If
  the body set is nonempty, its inflation <math|M<rsub|I>> is at least
  <math|t<rsub|C>>. The connection relation joins letters with intersecting
  full supports. If a site in a background inventory is supplied by a body
  inventory, both supports contain that site, so the two letters lie in the
  same component. Hence the inventory identity restricts to each component.
  In the forced case this uses the exact inventory identity: a forced site
  must belong to one of the body inventories. In the unforced nonleading case
  any body letter supplies <math|l>. The paid body integral therefore proves
  the estimate.

  If the body set is empty, compatibility forces the background set to be
  empty as well. A background packet has a nonempty fine inventory, whereas
  compatibility identifies the union of those inventories with the union
  supplied by the body letters. The component is now a product of insertion
  letters. If all are leading, its pointwise bound integrates against a
  probability measure. Otherwise choose one remainder letter. The first
  alternative in <eqref|eq:mixed-shadow> pays <math|l> pointwise; the second
  restricts the whole product to <math|E<rsub|h>> and pays <math|l> after
  integration. This proves <eqref|eq:mixed-club-integral> in every case. All
  integrals exist: the body-product majorant is integrable, the other factors
  are bounded, and the positive monomial weight can be divided out. Finite
  tag averaging preserves the inequality because its weights are nonnegative
  and sum to one.

  Here is the finite connected-support estimate with the precise smallness
  condition used here. Put <math|c<rsub|j>=<math-up|cov><rsub|B<rsub|c>><space|0.17em>X<rsub|j>>,
  <math|a<rsub|j>=b<rsub|j>*e<rsup|v*c<rsub|j>>> and
  <math|<wide|b|^><rsub|j>=b<rsub|j>*e<rsup|<around|(|v+1|)>*c<rsub|j>>>, and
  assume

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|2*B<rsub|c>+1|)><rsup|4>*\<Sigma\>\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  The sum of <math|<wide|b|^><rsub|j>> over supports meeting <math|X<rsub|i>>
  is at most <math|<around|\||X<rsub|i>|\|>*\<Sigma\>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|i>>.
  To count rooted trees, start with <math|R<rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>=a<rsub|i>>
  and iterate

  <\equation*>
    R<rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|q+1|)>>=a<rsub|i>*exp
    <around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|j:X<rsub|j>\<cap\>X<rsub|i>\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>R<rsub|j><rsup|<around|(|q|)>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  These are the nonnegative sums over rooted trees of bounded height; the
  exponential's factorial divides out permutations of the children. Allowing
  repeated labels only enlarges the sum. Induction gives
  <math|R<rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|q|)>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|i>*e<rsup|c<rsub|i>>=<wide|b|^><rsub|i>>
  for every <math|q>. Each finite connected set containing <math|i> has a
  spanning tree rooted at <math|i>, with weight the product of its
  <math|a<rsub|j>>; its distinct labels ensure that the child permutations
  cancel exactly the exponential factorials. Its union charge is no larger
  than this product charge, by cover subadditivity. Passing monotonically to
  the tree limit therefore bounds the connected-set sum rooted at <math|i> by
  <math|<wide|b|^><rsub|i>>. A component containing a specified site contains
  at least one letter whose support contains that site. Summing the rooted
  bound gives site price at most <math|\<Sigma\>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<Sigma\>>-1>.
  Likewise a component carrying a specified marker contains some letter
  carrying it. Summing those rooted bounds gives marker-root price at most
  <math|T<rsub|e>*\<eta\>+a>. Thus the same estimates hold simultaneously for
  every marker, without assigning independent prices to overlapping subsets.
  This is a bound on sets of distinct elementary letters, before the later
  logarithmic expansion with repeated labels. Let the all-hole normalization
  have raised hole-correction site price <math|k> and boundary-correction
  site price <math|\<psi\>>, at exponent <math|v>; the raised hole
  corrections have already been multiplied by <math|l>. If

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:mixed-finish-price>e<rsup|\<Sigma\>>-1+k\<leqslant\>\<psi\>,<space|2em>T<rsub|e>*\<eta\>+a\<leqslant\>y,
  </equation>

  then the resulting representation has forced price paid by <math|m>,
  regular nonleading price paid by <math|l>, total regular site price at most
  <math|\<psi\>>, and regular marker-root price at most <math|y>. Boundary
  corrections retain their separately purchased price <math|\<psi\>>. The
  corrections carrying no marker make no contribution to the last bound. This
  is the complete price output needed in the marked propagation; the
  subsequent finite inventory compression is that of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-marked-propagation>.

  <subsubsection|Two complementary pieces of every leading insertion>

  The actual incoming leading letter <math|W<rsub|h>> is split using its one
  local cutoff <math|0\<leqslant\>\<Gamma\><rsub|h>\<leqslant\>1> into
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|h>*W<rsub|h>> and <math|<around|(|1-\<Gamma\><rsub|h>|)>*W<rsub|h>>,
  with the same support and nonempty marker set. In the squarefree marker
  algebra their product is zero. Consequently

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|1+\<Gamma\><rsub|h>*W<rsub|h>*z<rsub|F<rsub|h>>|)>*<around|(|1+<around|(|1-\<Gamma\><rsub|h>|)>*W<rsub|h>*z<rsub|F<rsub|h>>|)>=1+W<rsub|h>*z<rsub|F<rsub|h>>.
  </equation*>

  This is exact also off the healthy set, since <math|W<rsub|h>> retains its
  original mask. The first piece is leading; the second is a remainder. The
  cutoff plateau implies that the latter vanishes outside the actual warning
  event. Its purchased probability is at most <math|x<rsup|-1>>, where
  <math|x> is the coarse rung coupling. Thus <math|l*x<rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>1>
  supplies the second alternative in <eqref|eq:mixed-shadow>. Each piece is
  bounded by the same pointwise price, so <math|2*T<rsub|e>*h> pays for a
  leading-family marker budget <math|h> after coarse support conversion. An
  arbitrary regular family of budget <math|z> is designated entirely as
  remainder and costs <math|l*T<rsub|e>*z>; it uses the first alternative in
  <eqref|eq:mixed-shadow>. Hence their union has marker price

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:mixed-day-marker>a=l*T<rsub|e>*z+2*T<rsub|e>*h.
  </equation>

  The event estimate here is the actual joint sector warning estimate from
  the local Gaussian construction. A separate Gaussian sample for each factor
  would not prove <eqref|eq:mixed-club-integral>.

  All leading connected pieces are contained in one cell. Their connected
  reader graph has at most <math|n> vertices, and the support argument in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-marked-propagation> bounds its
  radius by <math|<around|(|2*n+1|)>*\<rho\>>. On the actual elementary
  support radius <math|3*B<rsub|c>>, this is
  <math|<around|(|6*n+3|)>*B<rsub|c>>. The purchased condition

  <\equation*>
    6*n+3\<leqslant\>A/<around|(|3+2*n|)>
  </equation*>

  therefore puts it inside the required slice radius
  <math|A*B<rsub|c>/<around|(|3+2*n|)>>. The whole-mode selection and
  coefficientwise integration in <eqref|eq:planted-selection>\U<eqref|eq:planted-integral>
  identify its projected polynomial with the planted integral on the same
  cell. Its integrability also follows directly from the product of its
  pointwise letter prices divided by its positive marker weight. Thus this is
  the actual leading polynomial, not just a majorant for it.

  <subsubsection|Upper and lower numerical purchases>

  Put <math|H=exp <around|(|<around|(|log log s|)><rsup|2>|)>>,
  <math|K=H<rsup|-1/8>>, and <math|h=H<rsup|-4>>. The rung couplings
  <math|x,y> both lie in <math|<around|[|s/2,s|]>>. Fix <math|n,e,L> first.
  The large-field allowance <math|G<around|(|y|)>> is positive, at most one,
  and decays faster than every negative power of <math|H> uniformly on this
  interval. The radii <math|B<rsub|c>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
  x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>><rsup|2>> give <math|\<cal-W\><around|(|B<rsub|c>|)>=H<rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>>
  uniformly there.

  For the upper part use the exponent <math|e=e<rsub|0>+2> and set

  <\equation*>
    l=max <around*|(|1,<frac|H<rsup|-3>|\<alpha\>*z+H<rsup|-q>>|)>,<space|1em>0\<leqslant\>z\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-4>,<space|2em>m=G<around|(|x|)><rsup|-2>,<space|2em>a=l*T<rsub|e>*z+2*T<rsub|e>*H<rsup|-4>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<alpha\>\<geqslant\>8*T<rsub|e>> and <math|q> is fixed. If the
  displayed maximum is its second entry, <math|l*T<rsub|e>*z\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-3>/8>;
  if it is one, the same bound holds eventually since
  <math|z\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-4>>. Also <math|2*T<rsub|e>*H<rsup|-4>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-3>/4>
  eventually. Thus <math|a\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-3>/2> eventually, after
  harmlessly enlarging the threshold. The incoming forced budget is
  <math|\<eta\>=G<around|(|y|)>>. Taking <math|\<delta\>=K/16>, both nonbody
  terms in <eqref|eq:mixed-letter-price> are at most <math|K/16> eventually.
  Consequently <math|\<Sigma\>\<leqslant\>3*K/16\<leqslant\>K/4> and
  <math|T<rsub|e>*\<eta\>+a\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-3>>. Also
  <math|l\<leqslant\>max <around|(|1,H<rsup|q-3>|)>>, so
  <math|l*x<rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>1> uniformly for <math|x\<geqslant\>s/2>
  eventually.

  For the lower part use <math|e=e<rsub|0>>, <math|l=1>,
  <math|m=G<around|(|y|)><rsup|-1>>, <math|0\<leqslant\>z\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1/2>>
  and incoming forced budget <math|\<eta\>=z*\<omega\><around|(|y|)>>, where
  <math|0\<leqslant\>\<omega\><around|(|y|)>\<leqslant\>1> is the actual
  lower-interval attenuation. There are no designated leading letters. The
  regular marker budget is <math|a=T<rsub|e>*z>. Again the two nonbody site
  terms are each at most <math|K/16> eventually. The output marker budget is

  <\equation*>
    T<rsub|e>*z*\<omega\><around|(|y|)>+T<rsub|e>*z\<leqslant\>2*T<rsub|e>*z\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2><around*|(|\<alpha\>*z+H<rsup|1/2>*z<rsup|2>|)>*<space|1em><around|(|\<alpha\>\<geqslant\>8*T<rsub|e>|)>.
  </equation*>

  This includes <math|z=0> without division. The leading output is empty:
  with no body letters, a leading component would consist solely of insertion
  letters designated leading, of which there are none; with a body letter it
  is already nonleading.

  In both cases the hole price is <math|k=K/4>, the boundary and output site
  allowance is <math|\<psi\>=K>, and the tree smallness condition holds for
  sufficiently large <math|s>. Finally <math|e<rsup|K/4>-1+K/4\<leqslant\>K>
  for all sufficiently small <math|K\<gtr\>0>, for example by
  <math|e<rsup|t>-1\<leqslant\>2*t> on <math|0\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>1>.
  These calculations prove <eqref|eq:mixed-finish-price>. The support
  purchases <eqref|eq:mixed-cover-charge> for both
  <math|e<rsub|0>,e<rsub|0>+2> are made by choosing <math|L> after these
  fixed exponents and the two positive incoming support exponents
  <math|\<sigma\><rsub|<math-up|lower>>,\<sigma\><rsub|<math-up|upper>>>. The
  separate purchases are

  <\equation*>
    10\<cdot\>15<rsup|4>*<around|(|e<rsub|0>+7|)>\<leqslant\>L*\<sigma\><rsub|<math-up|lower>>,<space|2em>10\<cdot\>15<rsup|4>*<around|(|e<rsub|0>+9|)>\<leqslant\>L*\<sigma\><rsub|<math-up|upper>>.
  </equation*>

  In the actual lower and upper inputs these exponents are respectively
  <math|e<rsub|0>> and <math|e<rsub|0>+2>. No existence claim is made at a
  zero incoming exponent. The coupling threshold is chosen after <math|L>.
  For finitely many arities choose the maximum of their constants before that
  threshold.

  <subsubsection|The exact representation used by both parts>

  The body product and all-hole inputs used above belong to one compiler,
  chosen before the marked family. Specifically its body expansion is
  <eqref|eq:planted-body> for the genuine <math|P>, whereas its normalization
  is <eqref|eq:planted-holes> for the corrected body alphabet together with
  the archived activities of <math|P<rsup|0>>. The equality of archived
  activities of <math|P> and <math|P<rsup|0>> allows that alphabet to embed
  into the full marked alphabet, with the same full supports and inventories.
  It does not allow the genuine fine exponential to be replaced by that of
  <math|P<rsup|0>>. The empty-marker component bijection proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-planted-compiler> transports the
  all-hole identity to every deleted support of the full marked alphabet.
  Applying it at the union of the marked forced supports and using the
  exclusion bijection gives precisely the push of the genuine marked density,
  normalized by the selected coarse representation.

  The unmarked compiler's purchase is made once for multipliers at least as
  large as both uses above, and for the larger support exponent. Decreasing a
  multiplier or exponent only decreases every nonnegative price. Its body
  estimate, hole correction and forced embedding therefore remain valid for
  each call. In particular its coarse normalization is selected before the
  arbitrary marked background and regular family. The upper calculation adds
  the leading family and its complementary cutoff pieces; the lower
  calculation uses only the ordinary family. The same construction supplies
  both transitions on each rung of the actual restarted history. The coupling
  of the push is the genuine incoming <math|P.*\<beta\>> in both cases, by
  the retained course identity.

  This proves the mixed integral and price join from the actual local body
  and all-hole compiler purchases, the joint warning estimate and the
  selected geometry. It does not construct a different restarted history or
  deduce those purchases from an abstract Gaussian model. Their primitive
  analytic suppliers are the common corrected sector expansion, its unmarked
  all-hole normalization, and its paid product bounds; they must be supplied
  for the same body alphabet and coarse representation before this argument
  is applied.

  <subsection|The curvature covariance on the selected tree
  slice><label|sec:actual-reader-tree-covariance>

  The local Gaussian comparison needs an exact identification at the flat
  configuration. Its reference matrix is the inverse on the selected forest,
  whereas the free curvature estimates are expressed using two consecutive
  members of the precision tower. We now identify these two descriptions.
  Everything in this section is on the same pair of finite tori. In
  particular, no limit in the period and no change of forest is involved.

  <subsubsection|Counting coordinates and the flat precision>

  Write <math|E<rsub|f><rsup|r>> and <math|E<rsub|c><rsup|r>> for real
  <math|r>-cochains on the fine and coarse tori, with their counting inner
  products. Set

  <\equation*>
    d<rsub|f>:E<rsub|f><rsup|0>\<longrightarrow\>E<rsub|f><rsup|1>,<space|1em>D:E<rsub|f><rsup|1>\<longrightarrow\>E<rsub|f><rsup|2>,<space|1em>d<rsub|c>:E<rsub|c><rsup|0>\<longrightarrow\>E<rsub|c><rsup|1>,<space|1em>D<rprime|'>:E<rsub|c><rsup|1>\<longrightarrow\>E<rsub|c><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|D*d<rsub|f>=0> and <math|D<rprime|'>*d<rsub|c>=0>. Matrices
  initially act on one colour; tensoring with the identity on the real
  orthonormal colour space gives the physical arrays. There is no volume
  factor in this convention.

  Let <math|M<rsub|f>,M<rsub|c>> be the actual symmetric two-form metrics at
  the two levels, with <math|M<rsub|f>\<geqslant\>a*I> and
  <math|M<rsub|c>\<geqslant\>a<rprime|'>*I> for <math|a,a<rprime|'>\<gtr\>0>.
  Put

  <\equation*>
    K=D<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*M<rsub|f>*D,<space|2em>K<rprime|'>=<around|(|D<rprime|'>|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*M<rsub|c>*D<rprime|'>,<space|2em>Q=<wide|Q|\<bar\>>+d<rsub|c>*R<rsub|0>,<space|2em>A=K+\<kappa\>*Q<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*Q,<space|1em>\<kappa\>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|<wide|Q|\<bar\>>:E<rsub|f><rsup|1>\<to\>E<rsub|c><rsup|1>> is
  the selected averaged path map, <math|R<rsub|0>:E<rsub|f><rsup|1>\<to\>E<rsub|c><rsup|0>>
  is its root correction, and <math|Q> is the derivative of the actual
  predictor. If <math|E:E<rsub|f><rsup|0>\<to\>E<rsub|c><rsup|0>> evaluates a
  potential at the block roots, then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reader-root-gradient>Q*d<rsub|f>=d<rsub|c>*E.
  </equation>

  Indeed the integral of a gradient along a path is the difference of its
  endpoint values. In <math|<wide|Q|\<bar\>>*d<rsub|f>> these are the
  weighted endpoint averages. The root correction replaces each such average
  by the value at the root, giving <eqref|eq:reader-root-gradient>. This also
  explains why a gradient with zero root values costs no energy in <math|A>.

  We first relate <math|A> to the flat rows used in the local comparison.
  Suppose the selected path coefficients are absolutely summable and attached
  to a two-form row <math|R<rsub|f>>: the sum of the coefficients with each
  given endpoint and plaquette type is exactly that row entry. Suppose the
  full factorization and symmetry identities are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reader-flat-factor>M<rsub|f>=\<alpha\>*I+R<rsub|f><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*R<rsub|f>,<space|2em>\<alpha\>\<gtr\>0,
  </equation>

  and the curl energy <math|u<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*K*v> is invariant under the
  selected signed lattice motions. These are the path attachment, metric
  factorization and symmetry properties of the same fine bed; they must refer
  to that bed, not an independently chosen comparison metric.

  At the zero background, with every path admitted, parallel transport in
  each path row is the identity. The coefficient sum in that row is therefore
  <math|R<rsub|f>>, by absolute summability and attachment. The fine linear
  response in channel <math|s> is <math|D*P<rsub|s>>, where <math|P<rsub|s>>
  is the signed permutation on link cochains. The predictor response is the
  signed permutation of <math|Q*u> on coarse bonds. Consequently the
  channel's bilinear form is

  <\equation*>
    \<alpha\>*<around|\<langle\>|D*P<rsub|s>*u,D*P<rsub|s>*v|\<rangle\>>+<around|\<langle\>|R<rsub|f>*D*P<rsub|s>*u,R<rsub|f>*D*P<rsub|s>*v|\<rangle\>>+\<kappa\>*<around|\<langle\>|Q*u,Q*v|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|u,A*v|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation*>

  For the equality, first use <eqref|eq:reader-flat-factor>, then the stated
  invariance of the curl energy; the coarse signed permutation preserves the
  counting inner product. Each channel has this same bilinear form, so its
  normalized orientation average is also <math|A>. Restricting to the
  selected tree gives the tree compression of <math|A>. This identification
  is at zero background with all paths admitted; the estimates comparing a
  curved or cut row with it are separate.

  <subsubsection|Rooted gauge projection and the slice inverse>

  Fix the selected forest <math|F>, with one designated root in each block.
  Let <math|I<rsub|F>> insert the coordinates of the nonforest bonds into
  <math|E<rsub|f><rsup|1>>, putting zero on every forest bond. The coordinate
  space of these free bonds will be denoted <math|V<rsub|F>>. For
  <math|u\<in\>E<rsub|f><rsup|1>> define <math|\<phi\><rsub|u><around|(|x|)>>
  as the oriented integral of <math|u> along the forest path from <math|x>
  back to its root. Define

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-P\>*u=u+d<rsub|f>*\<phi\><rsub|u>.
  </equation*>

  The root values of <math|\<phi\><rsub|u>> vanish. The difference of two
  adjacent path integrals is the negative of the intervening oriented bond
  value, so <math|\<cal-P\>*u> vanishes on the forest. If <math|u> already
  vanishes there, all its forest path integrals vanish, and
  <math|\<cal-P\>*u=u>. Furthermore,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reader-slice-projection>A*\<cal-P\>=A,<space|2em>D*\<cal-P\>=D.
  </equation>

  The second equality follows from <math|D*d<rsub|f>=0>; the first follows
  from the same identity and <eqref|eq:reader-root-gradient>, since
  <math|E*\<phi\><rsub|u>=0>.

  The tree comparison of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:sector-gram>,
  specialized to the flat field and summed over bonds, gives finite constants
  <math|b<rsub|1>,b<rsub|2>\<geqslant\>0> such that

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|1>*<around|\<\|\|\>|D*u*<around|\<\|\|\>|<rsup|2>+b<rsub|2>|\<\|\|\>>*Q*u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2><space|1em><text|whenever
    >u<text| vanishes on >F.
  </equation*>

  Here the block geometry and predictor branch hypotheses of that comparison
  are retained. In particular the fine side is at least one hundred times the
  macro radius and the coarse side exceeds one, as in its finite-torus
  application. Hence the compressed precision
  <math|A<rsub|F>=I<rsub|F><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*A*I<rsub|F>> is positive
  definite, with lower bound <math|min <around|(|a,\<kappa\>|)>/<around|(|1+b<rsub|1>+b<rsub|2>|)>>.
  Define its extended covariance by

  <\equation*>
    X=I<rsub|F>*A<rsub|F><rsup|-1>*I<rsub|F><rsup|<math-ss|T>>.
  </equation*>

  For any <math|u>, write <math|\<cal-P\>*u=I<rsub|F>*v> in its free
  coordinates. Using <eqref|eq:reader-slice-projection> gives

  <\equation*>
    X*A*u=X*A*\<cal-P\>*u=I<rsub|F>*A<rsub|F><rsup|-1>*I<rsub|F><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*A*I<rsub|F>*v=I<rsub|F>*v=\<cal-P\>*u.
  </equation*>

  We have proved the exact identity

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reader-curl-through>D*X*A=D.
  </equation>

  The full precision need not be invertible. Its inverse on the forest slice
  is enough, because curl is unchanged by the rooted gauge projection.

  <subsubsection|Curvature matrices without inverting zero modes>

  For any symmetric positive definite matrix <math|M> on two-forms and any
  finite curl matrix <math|q>, there is a matrix <math|Z> satisfying

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reader-curl-solve><around|(|q<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*M*q|)>*Z=q<rsup|<math-ss|T>>.
  </equation>

  To see this, positivity gives <math|ker
  <around|(|q<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*M*q|)>=ker q>. Since
  <math|q<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*M*q> is symmetric, its range is the orthogonal
  complement of this kernel, which is precisely
  <math|<math-up|ran><space|0.17em>q<rsup|<math-ss|T>>>. Solve each column of
  <eqref|eq:reader-curl-solve> in that range. Any two solutions differ by a
  matrix with columns in <math|ker q>. Thus <math|P<rsub|M>=q*Z> is
  independent of the choice. Moreover

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|M>=Z<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*<around|(|q<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*M*q|)>*Z,
  </equation*>

  by transposing <eqref|eq:reader-curl-solve>. It is therefore symmetric and
  positive semidefinite.

  Apply this construction on both tori, choosing

  <\equation*>
    K*Z=D<rsup|<math-ss|T>>,<space|2em>K<rprime|'>*Z<rprime|'>=<around|(|D<rprime|'>|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>,<space|2em>P<rsub|f>=D*Z,<space|1em>P<rsub|c>=D<rprime|'>*Z<rprime|'>.
  </equation*>

  These are the finite-period free curvature matrices. This construction does
  not divide by the curl eigenvalue at zero momentum or discard a harmonic
  link direction.

  Let <math|A<rsub|0>:E<rsub|c><rsup|1>\<to\>E<rsub|f><rsup|1>> be the
  selected one-form transport. The stationarity identities on the same beds
  are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reader-stationarity-input>K*A<rsub|0>=<wide|Q|\<bar\>><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*K<rprime|'>,<space|2em><wide|Q|\<bar\>>*A<rsub|0>=I-\<kappa\><rsup|-1>*K<rprime|'>.
  </equation>

  For completeness, the two-form transport used below is
  <math|J=H<rsub|f><rsup|-1>*T*H<rsub|c>>, where <math|H<rsub|f>,H<rsub|c>>
  are the diagonal two-form operators of the selected beds and <math|T> is
  their installed tower map. Their inverse and transport identities give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reader-curvature-intertwiner>D*A<rsub|0>=J*D<rprime|'>.
  </equation>

  The operators <math|H<rsub|f>,H<rsub|c>> in this formula are the diagonal
  operators; the metrics defining <math|K,K<rprime|'>> remain
  <math|M<rsub|f>,M<rsub|c>>. This distinction is needed when the metric has
  undergone its prescribed repair.

  Straighten <math|A<rsub|0>> onto the forest by setting
  <math|W=A<rsub|0>+d<rsub|f>*\<Phi\>>, where the column potential
  <math|\<Phi\>> is the backwards forest integral of <math|A<rsub|0>>, minus
  the blockwise constant root correction <math|R<rsub|0>*A<rsub|0>>. It has
  root values <math|E*\<Phi\>=-R<rsub|0>*A<rsub|0>>, and the same path
  difference calculation makes <math|W> vanish on the forest. Equations
  <eqref|eq:reader-root-gradient>\U<eqref|eq:reader-stationarity-input> give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reader-stationary-slice>Q*W=I-\<kappa\><rsup|-1>*K<rprime|'>,<space|2em>K*W=Q<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*K<rprime|'>,<space|2em>A*W=\<kappa\>*Q<rsup|<math-ss|T>>,<space|2em>D*W=J*D<rprime|'>.
  </equation>

  Indeed <math|Q*W=<wide|Q|\<bar\>>*A<rsub|0>> because the root correction
  cancels. Also <math|K*W=K*A<rsub|0>>, while
  <math|<around|(|Q-<wide|Q|\<bar\>>|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*K<rprime|'>=R<rsub|0><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*d<rsub|c><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*K<rprime|'>=0>.
  Adding <math|\<kappa\>*Q<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*Q*W> to <math|K*W> gives the
  third identity. The last follows from <eqref|eq:reader-curvature-intertwiner>
  and <math|D*d<rsub|f>=0>.

  <subsubsection|Exact conditional curvature covariance>

  The cross-covariance identity needed in the calculation is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reader-cross-covariance>D<rprime|'>*Q*Z=P<rsub|c>*J<rsup|<math-ss|T>>.
  </equation>

  Transpose <math|K*W=Q<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*K<rprime|'>> and multiply by
  <math|Z>. This yields

  <\equation*>
    K<rprime|'>*Q*Z=W<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*K*Z=W<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*D<rsup|<math-ss|T>>=<around|(|D<rprime|'>|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*J<rsup|<math-ss|T>>.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|<around|(|Z<rprime|'>|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*K<rprime|'>=D<rprime|'>>
  and <math|<around|(|Z<rprime|'>|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>><around|(|D<rprime|'>|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>=<around|(|D<rprime|'>*Z<rprime|'>|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>=P<rsub|c>>,
  multiplication by <math|<around|(|Z<rprime|'>|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>> proves
  <eqref|eq:reader-cross-covariance>.

  We can now compute directly. Equations <eqref|eq:reader-curl-through> and
  <eqref|eq:reader-stationary-slice> give
  <math|D*X*<around|(|\<kappa\>*Q<rsup|<math-ss|T>>|)>=D*W>. Also

  <\equation*>
    A*Z=D<rsup|<math-ss|T>>+\<kappa\>*Q<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*Q*Z.
  </equation*>

  Multiplication by <math|D*X> and use of <eqref|eq:reader-curl-through>
  therefore show

  <\equation*>
    D*X*D<rsup|<math-ss|T>>+D*W*Q*Z=D*Z=P<rsub|f>.
  </equation*>

  Substitute <math|D*W=J*D<rprime|'>> and <eqref|eq:reader-cross-covariance>.
  We obtain

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reader-tree-conditional-covariance><around|(|D*I<rsub|F>|)>*A<rsub|F><rsup|-1>*<around|(|D*I<rsub|F>|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>=P<rsub|f>-J*P<rsub|c>*J<rsup|<math-ss|T>>.
  </equation>

  In particular the right side is positive semidefinite, being the covariance
  of the curl of a centered Gaussian on <math|V<rsub|F>>. Tensoring every map
  with the colour identity proves the same identity for all physical colour
  coordinates and all cross-entries.

  Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:reader-tree-conditional-covariance> identifies the
  flat reference covariance on the actual tree with the tower's conditional
  curvature matrix. Together with the flat row identification above, it
  supplies the exact algebraic reference for the local Gaussian comparison.
  Passing from a posed, truncated local covariance to this reference still
  uses the local inverse and window estimates. Selecting the beds, path
  attachment, symmetry and stationary tower on one retained history is
  likewise an incoming construction requirement. The identity proves the
  connection between those specified objects; it does not select new ones.

  <subsection|A common short-range covariance on every
  period><label|sec:actual-reader-short-covariance>

  This section records the finite-period covariance output used by the local
  reader comparison. The construction keeps one bed, one forest, one blocking
  history, and one cover row throughout. In particular, the row is not chosen
  again when the period changes. The exact curvature covariance on the tree
  slice is identified in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-reader-tree-covariance>;
  we use that identity here and construct its common exponentially localized
  cover representative and its finite-range approximation.

  <subsubsection|Period folding and the common inverse>

  Let <math|\<cal-I\>> be the finite set of asked coordinates for a fixed
  forest and gauge branch. Its labels include the intra-block residue and
  colour. Let <math|\<cal-C\>> denote the curvature labels, likewise
  including the intra-block cochain type and colour. The lattice coordinate
  in a cover row is always a coarse cell base, not a fine site. Write
  <math|d<rsub|n>> for shortest <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>> distance between
  coarse bases on the period-<math|n> torus. A cover row is a family
  <math|P<rsub|a*b><around|(|z|)>>, <math|a,b\<in\>\<cal-I\>>,
  <math|z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>. Its period-<math|n> matrix is the
  absolutely convergent periodization

  <\equation*>
    P<rsup|<around|(|n|)>><rsub|<around|(|x,a|)>,<around|(|y,b|)>>=<big|sum><rsub|z:y=x+z<space|0.27em><math-up|mod><space|0.27em>n>P<rsub|a*b><around|(|z|)>.
  </equation*>

  A bound <math|<big|sum><rsub|z>e<rsup|s<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>><around|\||P<rsub|a*b><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M>
  makes this sum absolute and bounds each row uniformly in the period. All
  sums below use the same coordinate and colour labels.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Folding a coercive row to every
    period><label|lem:ex-reader-folding>Suppose <math|P> is a real symmetric
    cover row, has an exponential row bound of width <math|s\<gtr\>0>, and
    there are <math|a\<gtr\>0> and <math|n<rsub|0>> such that

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|v,P<rsup|<around|(|n|)>>*v|\<rangle\>>\<geqslant\>a<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2><space|1em><text|for
      every >n\<geqslant\>n<rsub|0>.
    </equation*>

    If the periodizations commute with pullback under every quotient map
    <math|\<bbb-T\><rsub|m><rsup|4>\<to\>\<bbb-T\><rsub|n><rsup|4>> with
    <math|n\<mid\>m>, then the same lower bound holds for every positive
    period.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Fix <math|n>. Choose <math|m=<around|(|n<rsub|0>+1|)>*n> and let <math|g>
    be coordinatewise reduction modulo <math|n>. Then <math|g> is surjective
    and commutes with lattice translations. Pull a vector <math|v> on period
    <math|n> back to <math|v\<circ\>g>. Each coordinate of <math|v> has
    exactly <math|<around|\||ker g|\|>> preimages, so

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|v\<circ\>g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>=<around|\||ker
      g|\|><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    At a coordinate <math|<around|(|x,i|)>>, the left side is

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>\<cal-I\>><big|sum><rsub|z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>P<rsub|i*j><around|(|z|)>*<space|0.17em>v*<around|(|g*<around|(|x+z|)>,j|)>.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|g*<around|(|x+z|)>=g<around|(|x|)>+g<around|(|z|)>>, group
    the absolutely convergent sum by the residue <math|g<around|(|z|)>>. The
    result is <math|<around|(|P<rsup|<around|(|n|)>>*v|)><around|(|g<around|(|x|)>,i|)>>,
    so <math|P<rsup|<around|(|m|)>>*<around|(|v\<circ\>g|)>=<around|(|P<rsup|<around|(|n|)>>*v|)>\<circ\>g>.
    Taking inner products and using the same fiber count on both sides gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||ker g|\|><around|\<langle\>|v,P<rsup|<around|(|n|)>>*v|\<rangle\>>\<geqslant\>a<around|\||ker
      g|\|><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    The fiber size is positive, so division proves the claim. This argument
    also applies with the finite colour coordinate present: the quotient acts
    only on the spatial coordinate.
  </proof>

  The inverse row is obtained without a Fourier-symbol assumption. For each
  period, take the ordinary matrix inverse of <math|P<rsup|<around|(|n|)>>>.
  A uniform positive lower bound gives the finite-volume resolvent bound. The
  exponential row estimate gives a uniform weighted row and column bound on
  every periodization: the internal label set is finite and each periodic
  displacement sum is bounded by the corresponding cover sum. The diagonal
  conjugation estimate of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-covariance>,
  applied to these finite matrices, gives constants
  <math|C<rsub|1>,r<rsub|1>\<gtr\>0>, independent of the period, such that
  each inverse entry is bounded by <math|C<rsub|1>*e<rsup|-r<rsub|1>*d<rsub|n>*<around|(|x,y|)>>>.
  This is the finite-dimensional weighted-resolvent estimate used in the
  period-independent inverse construction; no spectral lower bound at complex
  momentum is assumed. For every factorial side <math|N=k>!, choose one
  centered shortest representative in each residue class, coordinatewise in
  <math|<around|[|-<around|\<lfloor\>|N/2|\<rfloor\>>,<around|\<lceil\>|N/2|\<rceil\>>|)>>.
  Define the cover coefficient read from its inverse at
  <math|<around|(|0,a|)>> and <math|<around|(|z,b|)>> when <math|z> is this
  representative, and set it to zero otherwise. For every retained
  coefficient, <math|d<rsub|N>*<around|(|0,z|)>=<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>, so
  the bound <math|C<rsub|1>*e<rsup|-r<rsub|1><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>> holds
  uniformly in both <math|z> and <math|k>. For every fixed <math|z>, it is
  retained for all sufficiently large <math|k>. Enumerating the countable set
  of triples and taking successive subsequences, followed by the diagonal
  subsequence, gives simultaneous coefficient convergence. Passing to finite
  partial sums retains the exponential bound. At any fixed smaller width, the
  remaining tails are dominated by a constant times
  <math|e<rsup|-c<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>>, a summable function on
  <math|\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>; hence the inverse rows converge in the weighted
  row norm.

  The limit has the required inverse on every period, not merely on the
  factorial sequence. Indeed, for <math|n\<mid\>m>, pullback intertwines the
  two periodic convolution matrices. Since both are invertible, it also
  intertwines their inverses: apply the forward intertwining identity and
  uniqueness of the solution to the finite linear system. Periodizing the
  cover limit along any fixed period is justified by the common exponential
  majorant. Passing to the limit in the factorial periods divisible by
  <math|n> therefore identifies that periodization with
  <math|<around|(|P<rsup|<around|(|n|)>>|)><rsup|-1>>. This is the
  period-independent inverse construction used below.

  <subsubsection|The selected tree slice on the same history>

  Fix a blocking patch, gauge, kernel, forest, and bed <math|H>. At each
  period <math|n>, repeat the same forest by the zero-offset lift. Let
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|n>> be the fine one-form coordinates and let
  <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|n>> be the active asked-coordinate set. The cover
  coefficients <math|P<rsub|i*j><around|(|z|)>> are the common full, uncut
  row on the finite asked-label set <math|\<cal-I\>>. Periodization sums
  these coefficients over the displacement classes and gives exactly the
  restriction of the finite tree precision to the repeated forest.

  The local construction has this concrete input. Fix one odd block side
  <math|L>, one patch and gauge branch, and one forest on the reference
  three-side block. Repeat that forest at every period by the same
  zero-offset rule. Fix a bed <math|H>, a clip, and constants
  <math|s,M,a,V,K<rsub|<math-up|pen>>> with
  <math|s,a,K<rsub|<math-up|pen>>\<gtr\>0>, <math|M\<geqslant\>0>, and
  <math|V\<gtr\>0>. Let <math|G<rsub|n>> and <math|\<Delta\><rsub|n>> be its
  metric and diagonal matrices on period <math|n>. Assume <math|H> has strip
  width <math|s> and bound <math|M>, and that at each finite period these
  matrices are symmetric and satisfy

  <\equation*>
    a<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|\<langle\>|u,G<rsub|n>*u|\<rangle\>>\<leqslant\>V<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>,<space|2em>a<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|\<langle\>|u,\<Delta\><rsub|n>*u|\<rangle\>>\<leqslant\>V<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  Also assume the exact root-gradient identity
  <eqref|eq:reader-root-gradient>, the same-bed stationarity identities
  <eqref|eq:reader-stationarity-input>, and the two-form transport identity
  <eqref|eq:reader-curvature-intertwiner> from
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-reader-tree-covariance>. The
  displayed numerical bounds produce the gap; these algebraic identities
  identify the conditional covariance. They are distinct inputs. The
  construction fixes its positive width <math|t> and row constant <math|A>
  from the patch, kernel, branch, forest template, and <math|s,M,a> before
  selecting the bed within this class; the same pair works uniformly for
  every bed satisfying the stated strip and floor bounds.

  The finite block estimate of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:path-attachment-producer>
  gives the lower bound

  <\equation*>
    <frac|min <around|(|a,K<rsub|<math-up|pen>>|)>|1+\<beta\>+\<gamma\>>,<space|2em>\<beta\>,\<gamma\>\<geqslant\>0,
  </equation*>

  for every coarse side at least <math|100*R+3>, where <math|R> is the macro
  radius and <math|K<rsub|<math-up|pen>>> is the prescribed penalty. Here
  <math|X> is the fixed patch-offset set and <math|C<rsub|<math-up|edge>>> is
  the edge-comparison constant. The finite costs are

  <\equation*>
    \<beta\><rsub|\<ell\>>=<around|(|2*C<rsub|<math-up|edge>><rsup|2>|)><around|\||<math-up|Site><around|(|L|)><around|\||<space|0.17em>|\|><math-up|Axis>|\|><around|(|<around|\||X|\|><space|0.17em><around|\||<math-up|PlaqAxes>|\|>|)><space|0.17em><around|\||X|\|>,<space|2em>\<gamma\><rsub|\<ell\>>=2<around|\||<math-up|Site><around|(|L|)>|\|>.
  </equation*>

  They are the curl and rooted-probe coefficients proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:path-attachment-producer>; in the gap
  formula above <math|\<beta\>=\<beta\><rsub|\<ell\>>> and
  <math|\<gamma\>=\<gamma\><rsub|\<ell\>>>. The cover coefficients are
  obtained by composing the bed row with the fixed local blocking maps. The
  weighted row norm of a product is bounded by the product of the weighted
  row norms, since <math|<around|\||z+w|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>+<around|\||w|\|><rsub|1>>.
  The blocking maps have finite coefficient sums, so the resulting
  exponential bound is finite and independent of the period. Periodizing
  these same coefficients gives the repeated full row, whose restriction is
  the tree precision. Thus it has the displayed gap at all sufficiently large
  periods. For any requested period <math|n>, take
  <math|m=<around|(|100*R+4|)>*n>, reduce modulo <math|n>, and pull a vector
  back. The quotient is surjective and every fiber has the same positive
  cardinality. Directly reindexing the cover convolution gives
  <math|P<rsup|<around|(|m|)>>*<around|(|v\<circ\>g|)>=<around|(|P<rsup|<around|(|n|)>>*v|)>\<circ\>g>,
  while both the quadratic form and squared norm of <math|v\<circ\>g> are
  multiplied by <math|<around|\||ker g|\|>>. Dividing transfers the same gap
  to period <math|n>.

  Apply the weighted finite-resolvent estimate to this common row. Its
  inverse is one exponentially localized cover row whose period action is the
  inverse tree precision at every period. Let <math|\<cal-R\>> be the fixed
  rooted path-measurement matrix from asked-coordinate vectors to curvature
  coordinates, so its rows are indexed by <math|\<cal-C\>> and columns by
  <math|\<cal-I\>>. The rooted gauge projection and the identity between the
  root correction and the gradient evaluation show that the curvature is
  unchanged by removing forest coordinates. Thus the matrix

  <\equation*>
    Z=\<cal-R\>*<space|0.17em>P<rsup|-1><rsub|<math-up|row>>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-R\><rsup|<math-ss|T>>
  </equation*>

  has, on each period, the same restriction to active curvature coordinates
  as the finite tree-slice covariance for every admissible coarse period
  <math|n\<geqslant\>3>. More explicitly, the matrix on the slice is
  <math|<around|(|D*I<rsub|F>|)>*A<rsub|F><rsup|-1>*<around|(|D*I<rsub|F>|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>>,
  which equals <math|P<rsub|f>-J*P<rsub|c>*J<rsup|<math-ss|T>>> by
  <eqref|eq:reader-tree-conditional-covariance>. This is an exact equality.
  Its proof uses the same bed and the same forest on both sides; it does not
  replace a curved bed by the flat one or compare two different histories.
  The finite-dimensional curl-through identity and the resulting exact
  conditional curvature formula are the ones proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-reader-tree-covariance>.

  The row norm of <math|Z> is bounded by the product of the row norms of the
  two rooted path rows and the inverse row. For convolution, the weighted row
  norm is submultiplicative: expand the product, use
  <math|<around|\||z+w|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>+<around|\||w|\|><rsub|1>>,
  and sum the resulting nonnegative majorant. Therefore <math|Z> has a
  positive exponential width, with a finite constant fixed before the period
  is chosen.

  If the desired period matrices are symmetric but this particular cover
  representative is not symmetric term by term, replace it by

  <\equation*>
    Z<rsup|<math-up|sym>><rsub|a*b><around|(|z|)>=<tfrac|1|2><around*|(|Z<rsub|a*b><around|(|z|)>+Z<rsub|b*a>*<around|(|-z|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Its period matrix is the average of the original matrix and its transpose.
  Since the exact tree covariance is symmetric, this replacement leaves every
  period matrix unchanged. The triangle inequality shows that its exponential
  row bound is no larger than the original bound. The reversal is essential:
  transposition reverses the displacement as well as exchanging the two
  indices.

  <subsubsection|Cutting to short paths>

  For a scale parameter <math|x\<gtr\>1>, set the integer radius
  <math|B<rsub|x>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>
  and the natural-number cut radii <math|h<rsub|x>=<around|\<lfloor\>|B<rsub|x>/10000|\<rfloor\>>>
  and <math|H<rsub|x>=<around|\<lfloor\>|B<rsub|x>/8|\<rfloor\>>>. Their real
  uses below mean their natural casts. Choose a symmetric coarse-cell
  displacement cut which retains every coefficient with
  <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>h<rsub|x>> and retains none with
  <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>\<gtr\>H<rsub|x>>. Write
  <math|Z<rsup|\<leqslant\>x>> for the cut row. If
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|z>e<rsup|t<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>><around|\||Z<rsub|a*b><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>A>,
  then for every finite-period entry the triangle inequality and the tail
  estimate give

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||Z<rsup|<around|(|n|)>><rsub|<around|(|u,a|)>,<around|(|v,b|)>>-<around|(|Z<rsup|\<leqslant\>x>|)><rsup|<around|(|n|)>><rsub|<around|(|u,a|)>,<around|(|v,b|)>>|\|>\<leqslant\>A*e<rsup|-t*h<rsub|x>>.
  </equation*>

  The same estimate holds with row and column interchanged. The cut matrix is
  symmetric because the retention rule is invariant under
  <math|<around|(|a,b,z|)>\<mapsto\><around|(|b,a,-z|)>>. Also, if the
  separation of the two spatial coarse parent-base addresses (the addresses
  obtained by splitting the fine site into its coarse base and intra-block
  residue) is greater than <math|H<rsub|x>>, its cut period-matrix entry is
  zero: every retained coarse displacement has length at most
  <math|H<rsub|x>>, so none can connect those bases modulo the period. These
  two facts are the only geometric properties of the cut used in the
  short-history comparison.

  For an input site and label, sum the omitted absolute mass over all output
  sites and labels. For each output label, the triangle inequality in the
  periodization sum bounds the omitted mass by the cover sum above. Summing
  over the output-label set <math|\<cal-C\>> thus gives the row bound

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-T\><rsub|\<cal-C\>>*<around|(|t,A,x|)>=<around|\||\<cal-C\>|\|>*A*exp
    <around|(|-t*h<rsub|x>|)>.
  </equation*>

  For the installed two-form path row, the cover operator is

  <\equation*>
    \<Psi\>=D<rsub|f><rsup|-1><space|0.17em><math-up|Fine><around|(|T|)>*<space|0.17em>D<rsub|c>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|D<rsub|f>> is the fine diagonal, <math|D<rsub|c>> the coarse
  diagonal, and <math|<math-up|Fine><around|(|T|)>> is the installed tower
  map. Assume the corresponding same-history strip receipts:
  <math|D<rsub|f><rsup|-1>> has bound <math|M<rsub|f>>, <math|D<rsub|c>> has
  bound <math|M<rsub|c>>, and the moving tower row has bound
  <math|M<rsub|T>/L<rsup|2>>. The weighted row-product estimate gives

  <\equation*>
    \<Psi\><text| has strip bound ><frac|m<rsub|2><rsup|2>*M<rsub|f>*M<rsub|T>*M<rsub|c>|L<rsup|2>>.
  </equation*>

  In the selected case where the three incoming factors are all bounded by
  <math|M<rsub|<math-up|in>>>, put <math|\<psi\><rsub|<math-up|budget>><around|(|u|)>=m<rsub|2><rsup|2>*u<rsup|3>>;
  then the installed row has bound <math|\<psi\><rsub|<math-up|budget>><around|(|M<rsub|<math-up|in>>|)>/L<rsup|2>>.
  The factor <math|L<rsup|-2>> is already in the moving tower receipt, not
  produced by installation; installing the operator in residue coordinates
  preserves the bound on each cover sum. Let <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|2>> be the
  set of two-form labels. The movement error is bounded by
  <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsub|2>>*<around|(|s,\<psi\><rsub|<math-up|budget>><around|(|M<rsub|<math-up|in>>|)>/L<rsup|2>,x|)>>.
  The turn-row error uses its own output labels and strip parameters.

  Define the short transport matrix on a period by keeping each installed
  transport entry when the two coarse parent bases have separation at most
  <math|H<rsub|x>>, and setting it to zero otherwise. The cut path row has
  zero at all omitted addresses. The installed-row estimate therefore bounds,
  for each input coordinate, the omitted row sum by
  <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsub|2>>*<around|(|s,\<psi\><rsub|<math-up|budget>><around|(|M<rsub|<math-up|in>>|)>/L<rsup|2>,x|)>>;
  in particular every omitted entry has this bound, while retained entries
  are unchanged. This is the same two-case finite-range comparison as for
  <math|Q<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|short>>> below.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Cutting the constructed same-history
    covariance><label|prop:ex-reader-short-covariance>Under the fixed branch,
    repeated-forest, bed strip/floor, and same-bed factorization hypotheses
    above, construct <math|Z> by the common inverse and rooted measurement.
    If a symmetric cut retains every displacement through <math|h<rsub|x>>,
    has support through <math|H<rsub|x>>, and its row-tail payment is at most
    <math|\<varepsilon\>> in both orientations, then <math|Z> has the exact
    finite tree-slice curvature covariance at every admissible coarse period
    <math|n\<geqslant\>3>, while the cut differs entrywise from that
    covariance by at most <math|\<varepsilon\>>. Its entries vanish at
    separations greater than <math|H<rsub|x>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The finite tree bound gives the gap above the stated side threshold, and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-reader-folding> transfers it to every
    period. The period-independent inverse and rooted measurement produce
    <math|Z> with the exact period matrices for the admissible coarse periods
    <math|n\<geqslant\>3>. Symmetrize by displacement reversal; exact
    symmetry of the period covariances preserves every period output. The
    cut-tail estimate gives the entrywise error, and the support-radius
    condition gives the vanishing statement.
  </proof>

  There is a useful finite-range version of the cut output. On period
  <math|n>, let <math|C<rsub|n>=Z<rsup|<around|(|n|)>>> be the exact
  covariance and define

  <\equation*>
    Q<rsup|<math-up|short>><rsub|n>*<around|(|u,a;v,b|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|C<rsub|n>|)><rsub|<around|(|u,a|)>,<around|(|v,b|)>>,>|<cell|d<rsub|n>*<around|(|u,v|)>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|x>,>>|<row|<cell|0,>|<cell|d<rsub|n>*<around|(|u,v|)>\<gtr\>H<rsub|x>.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  This is symmetric and has range <math|H<rsub|x>>. If
  <math|d<rsub|n>*<around|(|u,v|)>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|x>>, it agrees with the
  covariance by definition. If <math|d<rsub|n>*<around|(|u,v|)>\<gtr\>H<rsub|x>>,
  the cut period matrix vanishes by the support condition, so the bound from
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-reader-short-covariance> gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||<around|(|C<rsub|n>|)><rsub|<around|(|u,a|)>,<around|(|v,b|)>>-Q<rsup|<math-up|short>><rsub|n>*<around|(|u,a;v,b|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<cal-T\><rsub|\<cal-C\>>*<around|(|t,A,x|)>.
  </equation*>

  Thus the short comparison used at a reader is exact on retained pairs; the
  only error is the omitted covariance tail. This is the two-case form needed
  for a finite-range covariance comparator on one fixed history.

  <subsubsection|How the estimate enters the path telescope>

  For clarity, the quantitative input used by the path telescope consists of
  two payments, not a free approximation assertion. If <math|\<Psi\>> is the
  installed two-form path row, let <math|m<rsub|2>> be the number of two-form
  labels, set <math|\<psi\><rsub|<math-up|budget>><around|(|u|)>=m<rsub|2><rsup|2>*u<rsup|3>>,
  and let <math|M<rsub|<math-up|in>>> be its pre-installation bound. The
  movement payment is

  <\equation*>
    m<rsub|2>*<space|0.17em><frac|\<psi\><rsub|<math-up|budget>><around|(|M<rsub|<math-up|in>>|)>|L<rsup|2>>*exp
    <around|(|-s*h<rsub|x>|)>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>.
  </equation*>

  The second payment asks for a same-bed row <math|Z>, a width
  <math|t\<gtr\>0>, and a bound <math|A\<gtr\>0> such that the preceding cut
  estimate is at most <math|\<varepsilon\>>. The path part of the telescope
  uses the movement receipt through the triangle inequality and the finite
  row sum of the installed path data; the covariance part uses the turn
  receipt and the exact same-bed transfer. These are separate clauses. This
  section proves the row and covariance estimate, but it does not derive
  either price from the scale schedule.

  Suppose two consecutive levels of the selected history have the same
  blocking clock. In the covariance clause, truncate the rooted row at radius
  <math|H<rsub|x>>. For endpoints farther apart, the truncated matrix entry
  vanishes. For the remaining pairs, the exact same-bed transfer identifies
  the uncut entry with the finite tree conditional covariance, and the cut
  estimate bounds its replacement error. In the path clause, the installed
  path row is handled by its finite row-sum estimate and the separate
  movement receipt. Both clauses retain the original stamped history. The
  selected schedule must allocate the movement and turn prices to their
  respective requested tolerances; the field-path bound and capital budget
  remain additional hypotheses of the telescope.

  The large-period gap, the strip width, and both payments are properties of
  the selected bed and the selected history. Their production is not proved
  by the finite covariance algebra above. In particular, this result must be
  combined with the separate schedule and paid-budget construction before it
  can be used as an unconditional Yang\UMills construction step.

  <subsection|The actual reader on its small
  panel><label|sec:ex-lattice-actual-reader-small-window>

  Fix the outer scale <math|x> and let <math|B> denote its outer radius. The
  sector panel radius is

  <\equation*>
    p=<around*|\<lfloor\>|<frac|B+80|20>|\<rfloor\>>.
  </equation*>

  The matrices, forest, frozen coordinates, masks, and cutoff below are
  always those of the outer-<math|B> construction. The smaller <math|p> is
  the panel already chosen by that construction. Write
  <math|\<cal-D\><rsub|U><around|(|R|)>> for the seed-dome of radius <math|R>
  in the fixed configuration <math|U>. Outer clearance means
  <math|o\<nin\>\<cal-D\><rsub|U>*<around|(|11*B+1|)>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Two-scale containment><label|lem:ex-lattice-small-window-geometry>If
    <math|B\<geqslant\>200>, then

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ex-lattice-small-window-geometry>

      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|p>|<cell|\<leqslant\>B/20+4,>|<cell|30*p>|<cell|\<less\>11*B+1-p,>>|<row|<cell|9*p>|<cell|\<leqslant\>B,>|<cell|B/4+p+B/50>|<cell|\<leqslant\>B/2,>>|<row|<cell|B/4+2*p+B/25>|<cell|\<leqslant\>B,>|<cell|p+B/50+B/4+B/2>|<cell|\<leqslant\>B.>>>>
      </aligned>
    </equation>

    Thus a radius-<math|8*p> ball centered at any panel center within
    distance <math|p> of <math|o> lies in the outer radius-<math|B> ball. A
    target within <math|B/4> and its row span of at most <math|B/50> lie
    inside the radius-<math|B/2> region where the source-row bound is
    available. Adding the further dependency radius <math|B/2> still stays
    inside the outer <math|B> neighborhood. Every panel center moved by at
    most <math|p> remains farther than <math|30*p> from each seed.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Taking the floor gives <math|p\<leqslant\><around|(|B+80|)>/20=B/20+4>.
    The seed-dome radius is <math|10*B+B+1=11*B+1>. Moving a center by at
    most <math|p> leaves clearance at least <math|11*B+1-p>, and

    <\equation*>
      11*B+1-p-30*p\<geqslant\>11*B+1-31*<around|(|B+80|)>/20=<around|(|189*B-2460|)>/20\<gtr\>0.
    </equation*>

    Also <math|9*p\<leqslant\>9*<around|(|B+80|)>/20\<leqslant\>B> for
    <math|B\<geqslant\>200>. The total-reach bound follows from

    <\equation*>
      p+B/50+B/4+B/2\<leqslant\>B/20+4+B/50+B/4+B/2=<around|(|41*B+200|)>/50\<leqslant\>B.
    </equation*>

    Finally, <math|B/4+p+B/50\<leqslant\>B/2> for <math|B\<geqslant\>200>.
    These inequalities and <math|B/4+2*p+B/25\<leqslant\>B> follow on
    substituting <math|p\<leqslant\>B/20+4>. They concern only support and
    source queries; no matrix, cutoff or mask is changed.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Actual local mean and panel covariance><label|prop:ex-lattice-actual-reader-panel>Fix
    <math|R\<geqslant\>1> and put

    <\equation*>
      \<chi\><around|(|x|)>=16*<around|{|2*<around|(|B+1|)>+1|}><rsup|5>*n<rsub|<math-up|plaq>>*<space|0.17em>\<vartheta\><around|(|x|)>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|n<rsub|<math-up|plaq>>> is the finite number of plaquette
    orientations, <math|\<vartheta\><around|(|x|)>> is the temperature of the
    outer construction, and <math|B=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>. There are a fixed finite number of row, color,
    and local jet types, and a radius-<math|r> ball in the four-dimensional
    lattice has at most <math|<around|(|2*r+1|)><rsup|4>> sites. Consequently
    the row and panel counts below are bounded by a fixed multiple of
    <math|<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>>. Fix one patch, tube, forest, tag, posed
    configuration and history over the entire panel. Assume the selected
    predictor branch and funded tube, with the fine and coarse beds attached
    to their respective path rows. The two beds satisfy the positive floor
    and signed symmetry conditions. On these same beds and tower assume the
    diagonal and transport identities <eqref|eq:mean-diagonal>,
    <eqref|eq:reader-stationarity-input> and
    <eqref|eq:reader-curvature-intertwiner>. Separately, fix the full flat
    stack for these two beds, their selected clips and this tower. It
    satisfies the isometry, predictor normalization and adjoint orthogonality
    in <eqref|eq:unhit-flat-stack>. Write
    <math|W<rsub|c>=B<rsub|c>*d<rsub|c>> and
    <math|W<rsub|f>=B<rsub|f>*d<rsub|f>> for the stacked curvature maps
    including the discrete curl, as in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-mean>,
    <math|A<rsub|0>> for the installed one-form transport, and
    <math|K<rsub|c>=W<rsub|c><rsup|\<ast\>>*W<rsub|c>> for the coarse
    quadratic form. For the penalty <math|\<kappa\>\<gtr\>0> of the selected
    predictor and the fine, predictor and remaining blocks
    <math|F<rsub|0>,I<rsub|0>,N<rsub|0>> of this stack, assume its output
    identities

    <\equation*>
      F<rsub|0>*W<rsub|c>=W<rsub|f>*A<rsub|0>,<space|2em>I<rsub|0>*W<rsub|c>=\<kappa\><rsup|-1/2>*K<rsub|c>,<space|2em>N<rsub|0>*W<rsub|c>=0.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|\<kappa\>> is the coefficient denoted <math|p> in
    <eqref|eq:unhit-flat-stack>. The rooted adjoint pairing
    <eqref|eq:unhit-linear-zero> and the row cancellation
    <eqref|eq:mean-stationary-zero> use this same response. Use the branch
    and side conditions of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-reader-tree-covariance>,
    assume <math|c\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|1+B|)>>, and match the stored
    flags to the posed configuration at temperature
    <math|\<vartheta\><around|(|x|)>>. Let <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0> be the
    fixed small-field threshold. For each <math|x> under consideration, fix
    <math|t> with <math|0\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>min
    <around|{|\<delta\>,R*\<chi\><around|(|x|)>|}>>. Assume the direction
    <math|v> has size at most <math|t>, its group representative agrees with
    the posed field on the outer ball, and the local chart bounds for the
    replica hold on the same ball with price <math|t>. The center lies
    outside <math|\<cal-D\><rsub|U>*<around|(|11*B+1|)>>. Use the actual
    panels selected by the outer construction and the official-address map to
    the cleared panel. The mean conclusion is for an active coordinate
    <math|i> whose anchor is within <math|B/4> of <math|o>; the column
    conclusion is for every actual active row <math|j> of the padded window
    inverses. Outside the selected panel both padded inverses vanish, so this
    also includes those rows.

    Let <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0> be the minimum decay rate in the short-kernel
    approximation and long-path budget. Put

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ex-lattice-reader-widening><math-up|widen><rsub|x><around|(|t|)>=<around|(|1+<math-up|till><around|(|x|)>|)>*t+e<rsup|-\<eta\><math-up|till><around|(|x|)>>.
    </equation>

    Since <math|t\<leqslant\>R*\<chi\><around|(|x|)>> and
    <math|\<chi\><around|(|x|)>\<to\>0>, <math|t> lies below any fixed
    positive chart threshold for all sufficiently large <math|x>. Let
    <math|D<rsub|x>> be the number of source rows that can contribute to a
    fixed normal-residual coordinate; the four-dimensional row count gives
    <math|D<rsub|x>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>>. Let
    <math|n<rsub|p>> bound the number of coordinates in a reader panel at
    radius <math|p>. There are finitely many color and local jet types, so
    the four-dimensional panel count gives
    <math|n<rsub|p>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|1+p|)><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>>.
    Assume the fixed-row chart and path bounds, the positive Gram gaps
    <math|\<lambda\>,\<Lambda\>\<gtr\>0> for the actual and bare matrices
    with their uniform row constants, and the inverse-window bounds of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-covariance> on these same matrices.
    Then there is one large-<math|x> threshold depending only on the fixed
    gauge, patch and branch parameters and the common chart and path
    constants. It is uniform over the admissible tubes, stores, centres,
    posed configurations, directions, forests, tags, periods and <math|t>
    satisfying the stated bounds. Within each comparison all readings still
    use the same data. Above this threshold,

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\||\<mu\><rsub|i>-a<rsub|i>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>x<rsup|-7/8>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-reader-mean>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||<around|(|<around|(|Q<rsub|V>\|<rsub|W<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|-1>|)><rsub|j*i>-<around|(|<around|(|Q<rsub|1>\|<rsub|W<rsub|i><rsup|0>>|)><rsup|-1>|)><rsub|<wide|j|\<bar\>>*<wide|i|\<bar\>>>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>x<rsup|-1/4>.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-reader-column>>>>>
    </align>

    Here <math|a<rsub|i>> is the frozen stationary-response coordinate,
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|i>> is the actual local Gaussian mean, <math|Q<rsub|V>>
    and <math|Q<rsub|1>> are respectively the actual and
    identity-configuration row Gram matrices, <math|W<rsub|i>> is the actual
    reader panel, <math|W<rsub|i><rsup|0>> is its cleared counterpart, and
    bars denote the official-address bijection. The matrices are not asserted
    to have equal entries. The error in the second line is the local
    actual-to-cleared inverse-column comparison; the later covariance split
    that upgrades it to the full physical-tensor estimate is not part of this
    proposition.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    <em|The nonlinear row error.> Set <math|m> to be the frozen stationary
    response to <math|v>. The exact first-order identity for each uncut row
    is

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ex-lattice-frozen-row-cancel>I<rsub|r><around|(|m|)>+F<rsub|r><around|(|v|)>=0,
    </equation>

    where <math|F<rsub|r><around|(|v|)>> is the linear first variation and
    <math|I<rsub|r><around|(|m|)>> is the corresponding flat pulse. Write
    <math|B<rsub|r><around|(|V|)>> for the smooth base contribution and
    <math|P<rsub|r>*<around|(|V,m|)>> for the marked nonlinear pulse. The
    selected row, after subtraction of its stationary linear part, is exactly

    <\equation*>
      \<Delta\><rsub|r>=s*<around*|[|<around|(|B<rsub|r><around|(|V|)>-F<rsub|r><around|(|v|)>|)>+<around|(|P<rsub|r>*<around|(|V,m|)>-I<rsub|r><around|(|m|)>|)>|]>.
    </equation*>

    To verify this row formula, restrict to the finite read stencil. Outer
    clearance makes every free bond there active, while the frozen response
    <math|m> vanishes on tree bonds. Thus the marked direction agrees with
    <math|m> throughout the stencil. The additional frozen-input field also
    vanishes there, so finite row dependence removes it from the selected row
    formula. No value outside the stencil is used. Indeed, the selected row
    is <math|s*<around|(|B<rsub|r><around|(|V|)>+P<rsub|r>*<around|(|V,m|)>|)>>,
    while <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-frozen-row-cancel> says
    <math|F<rsub|r><around|(|v|)>+I<rsub|r><around|(|m|)>=0>. Unselected rows
    vanish by the row mask. This cancellation is made before taking absolute
    values.

    For the base difference, write <math|F<rsub|j>> for the actual transport
    factor at the <math|j>th position of a path and <math|H<rsub|j>> for its
    frozen linear factor. Expand their finite transport products by the exact
    telescoping identity

    <\equation*>
      F<rsub|1>*\<cdots\>*F<rsub|m>-H<rsub|1>*\<cdots\>*H<rsub|m>=<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|m>F<rsub|1>*\<cdots\>*F<rsub|j-1>*<around|(|F<rsub|j>-H<rsub|j>|)>*H<rsub|j+1>*\<cdots\>*H<rsub|m>.
    </equation*>

    Write <math|w<rsub|x>=<math-up|widen><rsub|x><around|(|t|)>>. The chart
    bounds give a linear curvature row <math|v<rprime|'>> of size at most
    <math|C*t> and a nonlinear row <math|v<rsub|0>> with
    <math|<around|\||v<rsub|0>-v<rprime|'>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*t*w<rsub|x>>. The
    actual and flat transport kernels <math|K,H> have row norms at most
    <math|C> and difference row norm at most <math|C*w<rsub|x>>. Thus

    <\equation*>
      K*v<rsub|0>-H*v<rprime|'>=<around|(|K-H|)>*v<rprime|'>+K*<around|(|v<rsub|0>-v<rprime|'>|)>,<space|2em><around|\||K*v<rsub|0>-H*v<rprime|'>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*C<rsup|2>*t*w<rsub|x>.
    </equation*>

    The extra factor <math|t> comes from the linear curvature row, not from
    the transport telescoping alone. The local bond-response Taylor remainder
    is at most <math|C*t<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C*t*w<rsub|x>>. Combining these
    estimates with the fine curvature-row remainder gives
    <math|<around|\||B<rsub|r><around|(|V|)>-F<rsub|r><around|(|v|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>A*t<space|0.17em><math-up|widen><rsub|x><around|(|t|)>>.
    For the pulse difference, first restrict the direction to the outer
    comparison ball of radius <math|B>. Finite row dependence makes the row
    insensitive to the discarded part. The pulse construction then cuts paths
    at <math|<math-up|till><around|(|x|)>>: its finite pulse bound is
    <math|Z*<around|(|1+<math-up|till><around|(|x|)>|)>*t<around|\<\|\|\>|m|\<\|\|\>>>
    and the weighted omitted-path bound is
    <math|Z*e<rsup|-\<sigma\><math-up|till><around|(|x|)>><around|\<\|\|\>|m|\<\|\|\>>>.
    The carry estimate gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|m|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>X*t>.
    Consequently, with <math|\<eta\>=min <around|{|\<tau\>,\<sigma\>|}>>,
    <math|<around|\||P<rsub|r>*<around|(|V,m|)>-I<rsub|r><around|(|m|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>Z*X*t<space|0.17em><math-up|widen><rsub|x><around|(|t|)>>.
    Combining these inequalities proves, for a constant <math|A<rsub|0>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ex-lattice-row-delta><around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|r>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|0>*t<space|0.17em><math-up|widen><rsub|x><around|(|t|)>,<space|2em>d*<around|(|o,r|)>\<leqslant\>B/2.
    </equation>

    This derivation uses the same frozen response and the same outer cutoff
    for every row; it does not reset the gauge or history panel by panel.

    <em|Normal residual and the mean.> Let <math|M> denote the actual row
    operator, <math|\<Pi\><rsub|0>> the projection onto inactive coordinates,
    <math|A=M<rsup|\<ast\>>*M+\<Pi\><rsub|0>>, and let <math|r<rsub|0>> be
    the affine source vector with <math|\<ell\>=M<rsup|\<ast\>>*r<rsub|0>>.
    Let <math|<math-ss|P>> be the padded principal inverse used by the local
    Gaussian mean. For the frozen candidate <math|a>, the selected affine
    residual is <math|r<around|(|a|)>=M*a+r<rsub|0>>; on each selected row
    its coordinate is the change <math|\<Delta\><rsub|r>> estimated above.
    The exact normal residual identities are

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ex-lattice-normal-residual>\<mu\>=-<math-ss|P><rsup|\<ast\>>\<ell\>,<space|2em>\<mu\>-a=E<rsup|\<ast\>>*a-<math-ss|P><rsup|\<ast\>><around|(|A*a+\<ell\>|)>,<space|2em>A*a+\<ell\>=M<rsup|\<ast\>>*r<around|(|a|)>+\<Pi\><rsub|0>*a,<space|1em>E=A<math-ss|P>-I.
    </equation>

    These are the same finite-dimensional identities as
    <eqref|eq:mean-error-identity>. The candidate <math|a> is extended by
    zero outside the active coordinates, so <math|\<Pi\><rsub|0>*a=0>. All
    queried mean coordinates are active; no assertion that every globally
    inactive coordinate is a tree coordinate is used. Each contributing row
    entry of <math|M> is bounded by a fixed constant <math|M<rsub|0>>, and at
    most <math|D<rsub|x>> rows contribute. Equation
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-row-delta> therefore gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||<around|(|A*a+\<ell\>|)><rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>D<rsub|x>*M<rsub|0>*A<rsub|0>*t<space|0.17em><math-up|widen><rsub|x><around|(|t|)>.
    </equation*>

    The local inverse-column sum is bounded by <math|N<rsub|0>>. The
    inverse-window estimate is used only at the queried central coordinate:
    <math|<around|\||<around|(|E<rsup|\<ast\>>*a|)><rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>E<rsub|0>*e<rsup|-s*B>*X*t>.
    No global operator-norm estimate for <math|E> is asserted. Since
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>X*t>,
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-normal-residual> implies

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ex-lattice-focused-mean-bound><around|\||\<mu\><rsub|i>-a<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>N<rsub|0>*D<rsub|x>*M<rsub|0>*A<rsub|0>*t<space|0.17em><math-up|widen><rsub|x><around|(|t|)>+E<rsub|0>*e<rsup|-s*B>*X*t.
    </equation>

    In the row-Gram formulation used for the actual reader, let <math|Q> be
    the row Gram matrix, <math|b> its affine source, and
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|W>=Q<around|[|W|]><rsup|-1>> extended by zero off the
    selected panel. The corresponding exact identity is

    <\equation*>
      -\<nu\><rsub|W><rsup|\<ast\>>*b-a=<around|(|Q*\<nu\><rsub|W>-I|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*a-\<nu\><rsub|W><rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|Q*a+b|)>,
    </equation*>

    This form makes the local residual sum and the off-window tail separate.

    The radius choice <math|t\<leqslant\>R*\<chi\><around|(|x|)>> gives
    <math|t\<leqslant\>C<rsub|R>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|5>*\<vartheta\><around|(|x|)>>.
    Put <math|b<rsub|x>=1+B> and <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|R><around|(|x|)>=16*R*3<rsup|5>*n<rsub|<math-up|plaq>>*b<rsub|x><rsup|5>>;
    then <math|R*\<chi\><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>\<cal-B\><rsub|R><around|(|x|)>*\<vartheta\><around|(|x|)>>.
    With <math|u=x<rsup|-1/2+1/32>>, the temperature and cutoff bounds give
    <math|t\<leqslant\>\<cal-B\><rsub|R><around|(|x|)>*u> and
    <math|<math-up|widen><rsub|x><around|(|t|)>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|x>*\<cal-B\><rsub|R><around|(|x|)>*u+u>;
    both exponential tails are at most <math|x<rsup|-1>> eventually, since
    <math|B=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>> and
    <math|<math-up|till><around|(|x|)>> is a fixed positive fraction of
    <math|B> up to an additive constant. Thus fixed polynomials in <math|1+B>
    are subpower in <math|x>. The remaining factors are bounded by the
    explicit polynomial

    <\equation*>
      Y<around|(|x|)>=N<rsub|0>*D<rsub|x>*M<rsub|0>*A<rsub|0>*\<cal-B\><rsub|R><around|(|x|)>*<around*|(|b<rsub|x>*\<cal-B\><rsub|R><around|(|x|)>+1|)>+E<rsub|0>*X*\<cal-B\><rsub|R><around|(|x|)>.
    </equation*>

    The row count above gives <math|D<rsub|x>\<leqslant\>C*b<rsub|x><rsup|4>>,
    so the displayed polynomial satisfies
    <math|Y<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>C<rprime|'>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|15>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|1/16>>
    eventually. Hence the right side of <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-focused-mean-bound>
    is at most

    <\equation*>
      Y<around|(|x|)>*u<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|1/16>*x<rsup|-1+1/16>=x<rsup|-7/8>,
    </equation*>

    which proves <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-reader-mean>. The cutoff and all row
    counts here are those at the outer radius <math|B>.

    <em|The inverse-column comparison.> The official-address map preserves
    physical anchors, row labels and the sector-membership test. Its
    restriction to a panel is onto: a bare address in the panel is within
    outer distance <math|B> of the cleared center, so the seed-dome exclusion
    makes it live in the actual store. Injectivity is immediate from the
    official map. This proves the finite index bijection only; it does not
    identify matrix entries.

    For each matched pair of indices, the local row estimate gives an
    entrywise Gram bound. For each row constant <math|A\<geqslant\>0>, define

    <\equation*>
      w<rsub|x>*<around|(|A,t|)>=A*<around|(|1+<math-up|till><around|(|x|)>|)>*t,<space|2em>\<frak-m\><rsub|x>*<around|(|A,t|)>=D<rsub|x>*<around*|(|<around|(|A+w<rsub|x>*<around|(|A,t|)>|)>*w<rsub|x>*<around|(|A,t|)>+w<rsub|x>*<around|(|A,t|)>*A|)>.
    </equation*>

    Set <math|w<rsub|x>=w<rsub|x>*<around|(|A<rsub|1>,t|)>> and
    <math|\<frak-m\><rsub|x>=\<frak-m\><rsub|x>*<around|(|A<rsub|1>,t|)>>;
    then the entrywise error is at most <math|\<delta\><rsub|x>=\<frak-m\><rsub|x>>.
    The moving row differs by at most <math|w<rsub|x>> and the identity row
    is at most <math|A<rsub|1>>. Thus, term by term,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||a<rsub|r*j>*a<rsub|r*k>-b<rsub|r*j>*b<rsub|r*k>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||a<rsub|r*j>|\|><space|0.17em>\|a<rsub|r*k>-b<rsub|r*k>\|+<around|\||a<rsub|r*j>-b<rsub|r*j>|\|><space|0.17em><around|\||b<rsub|r*k>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|A<rsub|1>+w<rsub|x>|)>*w<rsub|x>+w<rsub|x>*A<rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    At most <math|D<rsub|x>> rows contribute, proving the claimed entrywise
    bound <math|\<delta\><rsub|x>>. Let <math|n<rsub|p>> bound the number of
    coordinates in either panel. For positive panel matrices with lower
    bounds <math|\<lambda\>> and <math|\<Lambda\>>, the ordinary resolvent
    identity gives

    <\equation*>
      X<rsup|-1>-Y<rsup|-1>=X<rsup|-1>*<around|(|Y-X|)>*Y<rsup|-1>,<space|2em><around|\||<around|(|X<rsup|-1>-Y<rsup|-1>|)><rsub|i*j>|\|>\<leqslant\>n<rsub|p><rsup|2>*\<lambda\><rsup|-1>*\<delta\><rsub|x>*\<Lambda\><rsup|-1>.
    </equation*>

    This is precisely the finite-window comparison; its two distinct spectral
    floors are both used. The count <math|n<rsub|p>> is bounded by the number
    of colors times the local jet-slot count at radius <math|p>.

    The nonnegative panel charge is

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-C\><rsub|x>=n<rsub|p><rsup|2>*\<lambda\><rsup|-1>*\<frak-m\><rsub|x>*\<Lambda\><rsup|-1>.
    </equation*>

    The local stencil count gives <math|n<rsub|p>\<leqslant\>C*b<rsub|x><rsup|4>>
    and <math|D<rsub|x>\<leqslant\>C*b<rsub|x><rsup|4>>. Also
    <math|t\<leqslant\>C*b<rsub|x><rsup|5>*\<vartheta\><around|(|x|)>> and
    <math|w<rsub|x>\<leqslant\>C*b<rsub|x><rsup|6>*\<vartheta\><around|(|x|)>>.
    Since <math|\<vartheta\><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>1> eventually, the
    definition of <math|\<frak-m\><rsub|x>> yields
    <math|\<frak-m\><rsub|x>\<leqslant\>C*b<rsub|x><rsup|16>*\<vartheta\><around|(|x|)>>
    and hence <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|x>\<leqslant\>C*b<rsub|x><rsup|24>*\<vartheta\><around|(|x|)>>.
    The subpower scale bound implies <math|C*b<rsub|x><rsup|24>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|1/8>>
    for all sufficiently large <math|x>; the temperature bound is
    <math|\<vartheta\><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|-1/2+1/8>=x<rsup|-3/8>>.
    Their product is <math|x<rsup|-1/4>>, proving
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-reader-column>. Equivalently, the fixed multiplier
    <math|R> can be included in the row constant:
    <math|w<rsub|x>*<around|(|A,R*\<chi\><around|(|x|)>|)>=w<rsub|x>*<around|(|R*A,\<chi\><around|(|x|)>|)>>
    and <math|A\<leqslant\>R*A>, so <math|\<frak-m\><rsub|x>*<around|(|A,R*\<chi\><around|(|x|)>|)>\<leqslant\>\<frak-m\><rsub|x>*<around|(|R*A,\<chi\><around|(|x|)>|)>>.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Curvature reads under one actual Gaussian
  reader><label|sec:actual-reader-guarded-curvature>

  This section records the nonlinear step between a local Gaussian reader and
  the group-valued field that it is meant to read. All comparisons use one
  coarse field, one gauge based at one site, one parent forecast, and one
  joint Gaussian sample. A patch restricts which addresses are tested; it
  does not change the forecast, covariance, or gauge from address to address.
  The local Gaussian hypotheses used here are those of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-reader-local-gaussian-prices>. That
  section's same-pose mean and covariance estimates remain inputs, as do the
  paid regularity and window hypotheses specified below.

  Let <math|x> be the large coarse parameter, <math|L> the fixed blocking
  side, and <math|w> the fixed width multiplier of the fine read. Set

  <\equation*>
    y=x+c<rsub|L>,<space|2em>R=x<rsup|-3/8>,<space|2em>g=\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>,<space|2em>g<rprime|'>=<around|(|\<beta\><rprime|'>|)><rsup|-1/2>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|c<rsub|L>\<geqslant\>0> is the fixed scale shift. The coupling
  band is <math|x/2\<leqslant\>\<beta\>\<leqslant\>3*x>,
  <math|x/2\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rprime|'>> and
  <math|<around|\||\<beta\><rprime|'>-\<beta\>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\>>, for
  a fixed <math|\<gamma\>\<gtr\>0>. In particular
  <math|<sqrt|x/2>\<leqslant\>g<rsup|-1>\<leqslant\><sqrt|3*x>>. Define

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|phase><around|(|v|)>=v<rsup|-1/2>*exp <around|(|<around|(|log
    log v|)><rsup|3/2>|)>,<space|2em>\<tau\>=<math-up|phase><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>t=<math-up|phase><around|(|y|)>.
  </equation*>

  Set <math|B<around|(|v|)>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
  v|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>> and <math|W<rsub|f>=w*B<around|(|y|)><rsup|2>>.
  The recording parameter <math|r<around|(|x|)>\<in\><around|[|x,x<rsup|2>|]>>
  has temperature <math|\<tau\>>. Thus the logarithmic clearance allowance
  from Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-actual-reader-small-window>
  on this recording scale is

  <\equation*>
    a<rsub|x>=16*<around|{|2*<around|(|B<around|(|r<around|(|x|)>|)>+1|)>+1|}><rsup|5>*n<rsub|<math-up|plaq>>*<space|0.17em>\<tau\>.
  </equation*>

  Fix the coarse chart constant <math|C<rsub|<math-up|core>>> and a common
  constant <math|C<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>1> such that the deterministic link
  response to a direction of size at most <math|a<rsub|x>> is bounded by
  <math|C<rsub|0>*a<rsub|x>> on the funded tube. Let
  <math|C<rsub|<math-up|high>>> and <math|A<rsub|\<ast\>>> be the chord and
  curvature constants for the two-exponential chart estimate proved below.
  Let <math|n<rsub|G>> be the real dimension of the gauge algebra and put
  <math|e=<around|(|A<rsub|\<ast\>>+n<rsub|G>|)>*R<rsup|2>>. All these
  constants are fixed before taking large <math|x>. Let
  <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|o><around|(|V|)><rsub|q>> denote the nonlinear
  plaquette-curvature coordinate of the coarse field in the fixed gauge based
  at <math|o>, indexed by coarse plaquettes and color coordinates. The parent
  forecast is the fixed real row

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-P\><rsub|o,V,g<rprime|'>>*<around|(|u,a|)>=<frac|1|g<rprime|'>>*<big|sum><rsub|q>K<rsub|u,a;q>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-C\><rsub|o><around|(|V|)><rsub|q>.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|D<rsub|A,F,x>> denote the linear response on the selected store
  <math|A> and forest <math|F>. It inserts the active coordinates of the
  joint Gaussian vector into the fine link field, with zeros off the active
  coordinates, then takes the signed curl and reads its real color
  coordinates in the fixed frame. For a joint Gaussian vector <math|Z> we
  write

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-G\><rsub|A,F,x><around|(|Z|)>=D<rsub|A,F,x>*Z
  </equation*>

  for the resulting zero-padded signed-curl response. When an incidence index
  <math|j> is used instead of <math|<around|(|u,a|)>>, it denotes that same
  plaquette/color row. The same <math|V,o,g<rprime|'>> and kernel <math|K>
  are used for every tested row. In particular, <math|\<cal-P\>> is not
  recomputed from a patch-dependent field.

  <subsubsection|The scalar scale and ticket estimates>

  Fix a patch of side <math|L> and a finite transition tube with its row and
  chart bounds. For the positive cut rate <math|s> and nonnegative strip
  parameter <math|z> of this tube, put

  <\equation*>
    m<rsub|2>=<binom|4|2>=6,<space|2em>M<rsub|0>=<frac|m<rsub|2><rsup|2>*z<rsup|3>|L<rsup|2>>,<space|2em>P<rsub|0>=m<rsub|2>*M<rsub|0>.
  </equation*>

  The two diagonal installations and the transport row give the cubic strip
  bound <math|M<rsub|0>>, as in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-reader-short-covariance>.
  The factor <math|m<rsub|2>> in <math|P<rsub|0>> sums over two-form output
  labels. For fixed <math|z> choose <math|L> large enough that
  <math|2*n<rsub|G>*P<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>1/4096>, which follows from
  <math|P<rsub|0>=m<rsub|2><rsup|3>*z<rsup|3>/L<rsup|2>>. Define the row-tail
  and comparison bounds

  <\equation*>
    h<rsub|x>=<around*|\<lfloor\>|<frac|B<around|(|x|)>|10000>|\<rfloor\>>,<space|1em>\<Delta\><around|(|x|)>=P<rsub|0>*e<rsup|-s*h<rsub|x>>,<space|1em>E<around|(|x|)>=C<rsub|<math-up|core>>*<around*|(|\<Delta\><around|(|x|)>*R+P<rsub|0>*R<rsup|2>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The row and chart estimates to which these bounds are applied are specified
  in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:readerB-pointing>.

  Since <math|r<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|2>>, the logarithmic factor
  in <math|a<rsub|x>> is bounded by a fixed power of <math|1+log x>. Also
  <math|\<tau\>=x<rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>>,
  <math|t\<geqslant\>y<rsup|-1/2>>, and eventually
  <math|t\<leqslant\>2*x<rsup|-7/16>>. The phase ratio
  <math|\<tau\>/t\<leqslant\><sqrt|y/x>\<leqslant\>2> follows for large
  <math|x> from the monotonicity of the exponential factor on
  <math|y\<geqslant\>x>. For every fixed chart radius
  <math|r<rsub|G>\<gtr\>0>, these bounds and <math|R=x<rsup|-3/8>> give,
  eventually,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:readerB-clearance>4*C<rsub|0>*a<rsub|x>\<leqslant\>R\<leqslant\>r<rsub|G>,<space|2em>x<rsup|-7/8>\<leqslant\>R<rsup|2>,<space|2em>\<Delta\><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>R.
  </equation>

  For the last inequality use <math|h<rsub|x>\<geqslant\><around|(|log
  x|)><rsup|2>/10000-1> and <math|s\<gtr\>0>. In particular
  <math|E<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|core>>*<around|(|1+P<rsub|0>|)>*R<rsup|2>>.
  Uniformly in the coupling band,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||g/g<rprime|'>-1|\|>=<frac|<around|\||\<beta\><rprime|'>-\<beta\>|\|>|<sqrt|\<beta\>>*<around|(|<sqrt|\<beta\><rprime|'>>+<sqrt|\<beta\>>|)>>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\>/x\<leqslant\>x<rsup|-1/8>.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|E<around|(|x|)>/g=O<around|(|x<rsup|-1/4>|)>>,
  <math|g<rsup|-1>=O<around|(|<sqrt|x>|)>> and
  <math|\<tau\>=x<rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>>, both terms of the following
  quotient are <math|o<around|(|x<rsup|-1/16>|)>>, uniformly in the coupling
  band. Hence

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:readerB-ticket-bill><frac|E<around|(|x|)>+P<rsub|0>*\<tau\>*x<rsup|-1/8>|g>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|-1/16>
  </equation>

  eventually. The vector price is also eventual. Indeed
  <math|n<rsub|G>*P<rsub|0>*\<tau\>\<leqslant\>t/4096> by the choice of
  <math|L>, and <math|n<rsub|G>*E<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>t/4096> for large
  <math|x> because <math|R<rsup|2>=o<around|(|t|)>>.

  Write <math|c<rsub|G>> for the color-scale constant in the curvature
  product estimate below and <math|\<delta\><rsub|G>> for the radius of the
  local curvature lower bound. Use <math|M=C<rsub|<math-up|high>>*R>, and for
  positive <math|g> and any room radius <math|W> define

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:readerB-transport-cost>\<cal-N\><rsub|G>*<around|(|M,g,W|)>=<frac|c<rsub|G>*<around|{|u*<around|(|4*M|)>+<around|(|u+4*M|)>*u+6*M<rsup|2>|}>|g>,<space|2em>u=4*W*M.
  </equation>

  Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:readerB-gauge-read> derives this
  expression from the based holonomy and the four-link product. For fixed
  <math|w>, <math|W<rsub|f><rsup|2>=O<around|(|<around|(|1+B<around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|4>|)>>
  because <math|y=x+c<rsub|L>>. Thus <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|G>*<around|(|M,g,W<rsub|f>|)>=O<around|(|<around|(|1+B<around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|4>*x<rsup|-1/4>|)>=o<around|(|x<rsup|-1/8>|)>>.
  Together with <math|R<rsup|2>=o<around|(|t|)>> and
  <math|e/g=O<around|(|x<rsup|-1/4>|)>>, this gives the remaining
  large-<math|x> comparisons

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|4*M>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|G>,<eq-number><label|eq:readerB-small-chart>>>|<row|<cell|6*c<rsub|G>*M<rsup|2>+4*e>|<cell|\<leqslant\>t/64,<eq-number><label|eq:readerB-four-budget>>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-N\><rsub|G>*<around|(|M,g,W<rsub|f>|)>+4*e/g>|<cell|\<leqslant\>x<rsup|-1/8>.<eq-number><label|eq:readerB-nautical>>>>>
  </align>

  We use the local curvature estimate <eqref|eq:seed-curvature-bound>. With
  the group-dependent radius <math|\<delta\><rsub|G>> and
  <math|<math-up|chord><around|(|U|)>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|G>>, it gives

  <\equation*>
    <frac|3|4><space|0.17em><math-up|chord><around|(|U|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|\<kappa\><rsub|G><around|(|U|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  The constants in this inequality depend only on the fixed compact gauge
  group. All large-<math|x> thresholds here are for fixed <math|L>, patch,
  and transition tube with the stated bounds.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Eventual local room><label|lem:readerB-enlightened>For fixed
    <math|G,L,w,z,s,\<gamma\>> as above and a transition tube with the stated
    paid chart bounds, all sufficiently large <math|x> and all
    <math|x/2\<leqslant\>\<beta\>\<leqslant\>3*x>,
    <math|x/2\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rprime|'>> and
    <math|<around|\||\<beta\><rprime|'>-\<beta\>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\>>
    have the following properties. Set <math|\<kappa\><rsub|0>=1/4096>.

    <\enumerate>
      <item>the chart clearance is at most <math|R>, <math|R> lies below the
      fixed chart margin, and <math|x<rsup|-7/8>\<leqslant\>R<rsup|2>>;

      <item>for every coarse direction of norm at most the clearance, the sum
      of the deterministic response bound, <math|n<rsub|G>*x<rsup|-7/8>> and
      the Gaussian reserve <math|\<kappa\><rsub|0>*t> is at most <math|R>;

      <item>the near-identity link price is at most
      <math|C<rsub|<math-up|high>>*R>, and the four-face curvature budget is
      at most <math|t/64>;

      <item>the sum of the four-face gauge-reading error, the coupling error
      and the residual curvature price is at most <math|x<rsup|-1/32>>.
    </enumerate>

    The assertions hold uniformly in the period for the fixed patch and local
    data.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The deterministic response has size at most
    <math|C<rsub|0>*a<rsub|x>\<leqslant\>R/4> by
    <eqref|eq:readerB-clearance>. Since <math|x<rsup|-7/8>/R=x<rsup|-1/2>>
    and <math|t/R\<leqslant\>2*x<rsup|-1/16>>, the color-count and reserve
    terms are each at most <math|R/4> after increasing <math|x<rsub|0>>. This
    proves the room estimate. The link price is the fixed chart constant
    times <math|R>; the four-face budget is
    <math|O<around|(|R<rsup|2>|)>=O<around|(|x<rsup|-3/4>|)>>, while
    <math|t\<geqslant\>y<rsup|-1/2>\<geqslant\>c*x<rsup|-1/2>> for large
    <math|x>, proving the curvature budget. Finally, the definition of the
    four-face gauge error is

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-N\><rsub|G>*<around|(|C<rsub|<math-up|high>>*R,g,W<rsub|f>|)>+4*e/g.
    </equation*>

    The scalar scale estimate bounds this by <math|x<rsup|-1/8>>. Adding the
    paid row read error <math|x<rsup|-1/16>> and the remaining coupling
    allowance gives at most <math|x<rsup|-1/32>> eventually, since each
    stronger negative power divided by <math|x<rsup|-1/32>> tends to zero.
    The fixed costs were chosen before the period, so the threshold is
    period-independent.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|From the coarse forecast to a fine curvature row>

  Let the coarse side be <math|c> and the fine side be <math|f>, with a fixed
  blocking map. Let <math|V> be the coarse field and <math|o> a single base
  site. The common pose assumption is that one based gauge transforms
  <math|V> on the whole room about <math|o> into the exponential of one real
  direction <math|v>, with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|x>/2>,
  where the norm of a link field is the maximum of its Euclidean color norms.
  The field is healthy at threshold <math|\<tau\>> on that room. The
  curvature coordinate has norm at most the plaquette chord by
  <eqref|eq:seed-normal-defect>, hence at most <math|\<tau\>> there. The
  chosen stores have no support there. The same gauge is used for every row
  and plaquette below.

  Let <math|F> be the selected forest and <math|Z> the actual joint Gaussian
  sample on its active link coordinates. Write <math|z<rsub|j>> for its
  insertion into the fine link field (zero outside the active coordinates).
  The stationary linear fine response is <math|\<ell\>=\<cal-U\><rsub|F>*v>
  from Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-mean>, using the same tube and
  forest. On an internal bond set <math|b<rsub|j>=0>. On a crossing bond set
  <math|b<rsub|j>> equal to the corresponding coarse direction of <math|v>.
  The replica link is the identity in the first case and copies the coarse
  link in the second. Because the pose gives <math|V<rsup|o>=exp v> at the
  relevant coarse bonds in the gauge based at <math|o>, the replica link
  equals <math|exp b<rsub|j>> in both cases, with
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>R>.
  The stationary relative response of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-mean>
  is <math|d<rsub|j>=\<ell\><rsub|j>-b<rsub|j>>. This is the decomposition of
  the tangent in the replica coordinates: differentiating <math|exp
  <around|(|\<epsilon\>*d<rsub|j>|)>*exp <around|(|\<epsilon\>*b<rsub|j>|)>>
  at <math|\<epsilon\>=0> gives <math|d<rsub|j>+b<rsub|j>=\<ell\><rsub|j>>.
  The comparison mean <math|m<rsup|0>> in
  <eqref|eq:reader-local-column-price> is the restriction of this <math|d> to
  the active bonds in the same forest.

  Let <math|w<rsub|j>> be the actual local mean in those relative coordinates
  and put <math|r<rsub|j>=g*z<rsub|j>>. The pointwise mean estimate
  <eqref|eq:reader-local-column-price> at recording scale
  <math|r<around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>x> bounds each color component of
  <math|w<rsub|j>-d<rsub|j>> by <math|x<rsup|-7/8>> on the interior active
  bonds. The Euclidean norm is bounded by the sum of component moduli, so
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|w<rsub|j>-d<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>n<rsub|G>*x<rsup|-7/8>>.
  The cleared stencils place every queried nonforest bond in the active set.
  The update first uses the link increment <math|exp
  <around|(|w<rsub|j>+r<rsub|j>|)>> and then the replica link. On forest
  bonds the update and the replica are both the identity, so the relative
  coordinates <math|w<rsub|j>,d<rsub|j>,r<rsub|j>> there are zero. Thus the
  actual fine field on each of these bonds is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:readerB-fine-link>U<rsub|j>=exp
    <around|(|w<rsub|j>+r<rsub|j>|)>*exp <around|(|b<rsub|j>|)>,<space|2em>\<ell\><rsub|j>=d<rsub|j>+b<rsub|j>.
  </equation>

  Write <math|<math-ss|M>> for the fixed unitary representation and
  <math|\<Pi\><rsub|\<frak-g\>>> for the real-linear color projection. In the
  physical normalization its operator norm for the Frobenius norm is at most
  <math|1/c<rsub|G>>, while <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|M><around|(|U|)>-I|\<\|\|\>><rsub|F>=c<rsub|G><math-up|chord><around|(|U|)>>.
  The curvature coordinate is

  <\equation*>
    \<kappa\><rsub|G><around|(|U|)>=\<Pi\><rsub|\<frak-g\>><space|-0.17em><around*|(|<frac|<math-ss|M><around|(|U|)>-<math-ss|M><around|(|U<rsup|-1>|)>|2>|)>=\<Pi\><rsub|\<frak-g\>>*<around|(|<math-ss|M><around|(|U|)>-I|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|<math-ss|M><around|(|U<rsup|-1>|)>=<math-ss|M><around|(|U|)><rsup|\<ast\>>>,
  so removing the Hermitian part does not affect the projection onto the
  skew-Hermitian Lie algebra. It follows by expanding the product of the
  matrix increments that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:readerB-product-defect>\<kappa\><rsub|G>*<around|(|U*V|)>-\<kappa\><rsub|G><around|(|U|)>-\<kappa\><rsub|G><around|(|V|)>=\<Pi\><rsub|\<frak-g\>>*<around|(|<around|(|<math-ss|M><around|(|U|)>-I|)>*<around|(|<math-ss|M><around|(|V|)>-I|)>|)>.
  </equation>

  The Frobenius product inequality bounds its norm by
  <math|c<rsub|G><math-up|chord><around|(|U|)><math-up|chord><around|(|V|)>>.

  Choose <math|r<rsub|G>\<gtr\>0> so that the exponential and its curvature
  coordinate have bounded second derivative on the closed ball of radius
  <math|r<rsub|G>>. The matrix exponential is smooth, and its derivative at
  zero is the inclusion of the Lie algebra in the matrix space. Projecting
  this inclusion back to color space is the identity. Taylor's formula on
  radial segments therefore gives constants
  <math|C<rsub|exp>,A<rsub|exp>\<gtr\>0> depending only on the gauge chart
  such that

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|chord><around|(|exp a|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|exp><around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|\<kappa\><rsub|G><around|(|exp
    a|)>-a|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|exp><around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>*<space|1em><around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|G>|)>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>>,<around|\<\|\|\>|b|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>R\<leqslant\>r<rsub|G>>,
  chord subadditivity bounds the chord of <math|exp a*exp b> by
  <math|2*C<rsub|exp>*R>. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:readerB-product-defect> and
  the two radial estimates bound the curvature error from <math|a+b> by
  <math|<around|(|c<rsub|G>*C<rsub|exp><rsup|2>+2*A<rsub|exp>|)>*R<rsup|2>>.
  Thus one may take <math|C<rsub|<math-up|high>>=2*C<rsub|exp>> and
  <math|A<rsub|\<ast\>>=c<rsub|G>*C<rsub|exp><rsup|2>+2*A<rsub|exp>> in the
  scalar estimates. Any increase of these fixed constants preserves the same
  eventual powers.

  The guarded event is

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|g*z<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<kappa\><rsub|0>*t
  </equation*>

  for every bond in the enlarged room. The paid room estimate is

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|w<rsub|j>+r<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|d<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>+n<rsub|G>*x<rsup|-7/8>+\<kappa\><rsub|0>*t\<leqslant\>R,
  </equation*>

  and we have shown <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>R>.
  Apply the two-exponential chart bound to
  <math|<around|(|w<rsub|j>+r<rsub|j>,b<rsub|j>|)>> to obtain

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-up|chord><around|(|U<rsub|j>|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|high>>*R,<eq-number><label|eq:readerB-bondchord>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<kappa\><rsub|G><around|(|U<rsub|j>|)>-<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|j>+r<rsub|j>|)>|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>e.<eq-number><label|eq:readerB-bondcurv>>>>>
  </align>

  Indeed, <math|w<rsub|j>+r<rsub|j>+b<rsub|j>=<around|(|d<rsub|j>+b<rsub|j>|)>+r<rsub|j>+<around|(|w<rsub|j>-d<rsub|j>|)>=\<ell\><rsub|j>+r<rsub|j>+<around|(|w<rsub|j>-d<rsub|j>|)>>.
  The portrait term is at most <math|n<rsub|G>*x<rsup|-7/8>>; since both
  Taylor arguments have norm at most <math|R>, the two-link curvature
  remainder is at most <math|A<rsub|\<ast\>>*R<rsup|2>>. Since
  <math|x<rsup|-7/8>\<leqslant\>R<rsup|2>>, their sum is bounded by
  <math|e=<around|(|A<rsub|\<ast\>>+n<rsub|G>|)>*R<rsup|2>>. Here
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>+g*z<rsub|j>> is the value of the link direction at
  the single bond <math|j>; the face boundary sum is taken only in the next
  subsection.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Read error against the common parent
    forecast><label|lem:readerB-pointing>Assume the paid row and transport
    estimates for the selected transition tube: on every readable address in
    the room, the fine response row differs from its shorter parent row by
    total absolute mass at most <math|\<Delta\>>, the parent row has mass at
    most <math|P<rsub|0>>, and the deterministic coarse curvature Taylor
    bounds stated below hold. Let <math|\<Delta\>=m<rsub|2>*M<rsub|0>*e<rsup|-s*h<rsub|x>>>,
    the absolute tail bound for the two-form row with strip bound
    <math|M<rsub|0>>. Then for every interior fine plaquette <math|u> and
    color coordinate <math|a>, with <math|f=<math-up|faceCurl><around|(|u,\<ell\>|)>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:readerB-pointing-conclusion><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>t/2048,<space|2em><around*|\||g<rsup|-1>*f<rsub|a>-\<cal-P\><rsub|o,V,g<rprime|'>>*<around|(|u,a|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|-1/16>.
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Index coarse plaquette/color coordinates by <math|q>. Let
    <math|m<rsub|q>> be the linearized coarse plaquette curvature in
    direction <math|v>, and let <math|<around|(|u<rsub|0>|)><rsub|q>=\<cal-C\><rsub|o><around|(|V|)><rsub|q>>
    be the nonlinear curvature in the same based gauge. The exact response
    identities are

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|a>=<big|sum><rsub|q>P<rsub|u,a;q>*m<rsub|q>,<space|2em>Q<rsub|u,a>=<big|sum><rsub|q>K<rsub|u,a;q><around|(|u<rsub|0>|)><rsub|q>,<space|2em>\<cal-P\><rsub|o,V,g<rprime|'>>*<around|(|u,a|)>=Q<rsub|u,a>/g<rprime|'>.
    </equation*>

    The deterministic chart estimates give the global linear-row bound
    <math|<around|\||m<rsub|q>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|core>>*R> for
    every tested coarse plaquette/color coordinate. On the support of the
    parent row <math|K<rsub|u,a;\<cdot\>>> they also give
    <math|<around|\||m<rsub|q>-<around|(|u<rsub|0>|)><rsub|q>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|core>>*R<rsup|2>>.
    The purchased row bounds are

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|q><around|\||P<rsub|u,a;q>-K<rsub|u,a;q>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<Delta\>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|q><around|\||K<rsub|u,a;q>|\|>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|0>.
    </equation*>

    Subtract the response sums and add/subtract
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|q>K<rsub|u,a;q>*m<rsub|q>>. Then

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||f<rsub|a>-Q<rsub|u,a>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|core>>*R*<big|sum><rsub|q>\|P<rsub|u,a;q>-K<rsub|u,a;q>\|+C<rsub|<math-up|core>>*R<rsup|2>*<big|sum><rsub|q><around|\||K<rsub|u,a;q>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<Delta\>*C<rsub|<math-up|core>>*R+P<rsub|0>*C<rsub|<math-up|core>>*R<rsup|2>=E<around|(|x|)>.
    </equation*>

    For the normalized comparison, <math|<around|\||Q<rsub|u,a>|\|>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|0>*\<tau\>>
    since the nonlinear parent curvature coordinates have modulus at most
    <math|\<tau\>>. Thus

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<frac|f<rsub|a>|g>-<frac|Q<rsub|u,a>|g<rprime|'>>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|<around|\||f<rsub|a>-Q<rsub|u,a>|\|>+<around|\||Q<rsub|u,a>|\|>*<space|0.17em><around|\||g<rprime|'><rsup|-1>*g-1|\|>|g>\<leqslant\><frac|E<around|(|x|)>+P<rsub|0>*\<tau\>*x<rsup|-1/8>|g>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|-1/16>,
    </equation*>

    by the coupling bound and <eqref|eq:readerB-ticket-bill>. For the vector
    bound, sum the component bounds to get
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>n<rsub|G>*<around|(|P<rsub|0>*\<tau\>+E<around|(|x|)>|)>\<leqslant\>t/2048>
    by the scalar estimates above. All rows have finite support in the
    selected window, and the bounds are period-uniform.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The four-bond curvature and the common gauge read>

  For a fine plaquette <math|u>, order its bonds
  <math|j<rsub|1>,j<rsub|2>,j<rsub|3>,j<rsub|4>> so that the plaquette
  product is

  <\equation*>
    \<Xi\><rsub|u>=U<rsub|j<rsub|1>>*U<rsub|j<rsub|2>>*U<rsub|j<rsub|3>><rsup|-1>*U<rsub|j<rsub|4>><rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  For a link field <math|A>, let <math|<math-up|faceCurl><rsub|u><around|(|A|)>>
  be the signed sum of the four link coordinates in this order. Apply the
  two-factor relation above successively to the four oriented factors
  <math|U<rsub|j<rsub|1>>,U<rsub|j<rsub|2>>,U<rsub|j<rsub|3>><rsup|-1>,U<rsub|j<rsub|4>><rsup|-1>>.
  Each has chord at most <math|M=C<rsub|<math-up|high>>*R>, and inversion
  preserves chord while negating curvature. At the second factor the defect
  is at most <math|c<rsub|G>*M<rsup|2>>. At the third, the preceding product
  has chord at most <math|2*M>, so the new defect is at most
  <math|2*c<rsub|G>*M<rsup|2>>. At the fourth, its chord is at most
  <math|3*M>, so the new defect is at most <math|3*c<rsub|G>*M<rsup|2>>.
  Summing gives exactly <math|<around|(|1+2+3|)>*c<rsub|G>*M<rsup|2>=6*c<rsub|G>*M<rsup|2>>.
  Replacing the four individual link-curvature coordinates by their linear
  directions using <eqref|eq:readerB-bondcurv> costs at most <math|4*e>.
  Consequently

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:readerB-four-product><around|\<\|\|\>|\<kappa\><rsub|G><around|(|\<Xi\><rsub|u>|)>-<math-up|faceCurl><rsub|u><around|(|\<ell\>+g*z|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>6*c<rsub|G>*<around|(|C<rsub|<math-up|high>>*R|)><rsup|2>+4*e\<leqslant\>t/64,
  </equation>

  by <eqref|eq:readerB-four-budget>. Linearity of the face curl, the bound
  from Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:readerB-pointing>, and the four guarded link
  increments give

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|faceCurl><rsub|u><around|(|\<ell\>+g*z|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>t/2048+4*\<kappa\><rsub|0>*t.
  </equation*>

  Consequently the product curvature coordinate has norm at most
  <math|t/2048+4*\<kappa\><rsub|0>*t+t/64\<less\>t/32>. Since
  <math|\<kappa\><rsub|0>=1/4096>,

  <\equation*>
    <frac|1|2048>+<frac|4|4096>+<frac|1|64>\<less\><frac|1|32>.
  </equation*>

  The plaquette chord is at most four times the link chord, so
  <math|4*C<rsub|<math-up|high>>*R\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|G>> keeps it
  inside the local curvature chart. The local lower bound
  <math|<tfrac|3|4><math-up|chord><around|(|\<Xi\><rsub|u>|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|\<kappa\><rsub|G><around|(|\<Xi\><rsub|u>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>
  therefore gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:readerB-healthy-face><math-up|chord><around|(|\<Xi\><rsub|u>|)>\<leqslant\><frac|2*t|32*<sqrt|3>>\<less\>t/8.
  </equation>

  Thus if the health flag threshold is the phase <math|t>, the tested flag is
  strictly below threshold. Applying this to each plaquette in the tested
  patch proves the healthy-mask conclusion. It is a local conclusion on the
  guarded sample; all plaquettes used in this deduction lie in the enlarged
  room.

  <subsubsection|Based transport for the fine
  read><label|subsec:readerB-gauge-read>

  Let <math|r> be the read center on the fine torus and <math|v<rsub|0>> the
  base vertex of the tested plaquette <math|u>. The read is in the based
  gauge obtained by transporting along the fixed coordinate paths from
  <math|r>. If the fine period is <math|n>, the condition
  <math|2*<around|(|W<rsub|f>+1|)>\<less\>n> gives an unwrapped coordinate
  box of radius <math|W<rsub|f>> about <math|r>. When the plaquette is at
  least two steps inside this box, its four bonds and the coordinate path to
  <math|v<rsub|0>> lie in it. The latter path has at most <math|4*W<rsub|f>>
  steps since each of the four displacements is at most <math|W<rsub|f>>.
  Write <math|H> for its ordered product in <math|U>. By the chord triangle
  and inverse identities, <math|<math-up|chord><around|(|H|)>\<leqslant\>4*W<rsub|f>*M>
  and <math|<math-up|chord><around|(|\<Xi\><rsub|u>|)>\<leqslant\>4*M>.

  For a site gauge <math|k> sending <math|U<rprime|'>> to <math|U>,
  multiplying the gauge-transformed links along the path cancels the gauge
  factors at all intermediate sites. The path product transforms by
  <math|k<around|(|r|)>> at the start and
  <math|k<around|(|v<rsub|0>|)><rsup|-1>> at the end. At the plaquette the
  factors telescope to conjugation by <math|k<around|(|v<rsub|0>|)>>. Hence
  the based plaquette in <math|U> is the based plaquette in
  <math|U<rprime|'>> conjugated by <math|k<around|(|r|)>>. The curvature
  projection is equivariant under conjugation, since the adjoint action of
  the unitary representation preserves the Lie subspace and its orthogonal
  complement. It follows that the normalized curvature read in
  <math|U<rprime|'>> after the color rotation by <math|k<around|(|r|)>>
  equals <math|g<rsup|-1>*\<kappa\><rsub|G>*<around|(|H*\<Xi\><rsub|u>*H<rsup|-1>|)>>
  in <math|U>.

  Apply <eqref|eq:readerB-product-defect> at
  <math|<around|(|H,\<Xi\><rsub|u>|)>> and
  <math|<around|(|H*\<Xi\><rsub|u>,H<rsup|-1>|)>>. The terms
  <math|\<kappa\><rsub|G><around|(|H|)>> and
  <math|\<kappa\><rsub|G><around|(|H<rsup|-1>|)>> cancel. With
  <math|u<rsub|0>=4*W<rsub|f>*M>, the two defects give

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|\<kappa\><rsub|G>*<around|(|H*\<Xi\><rsub|u>*H<rsup|-1>|)>-\<kappa\><rsub|G><around|(|\<Xi\><rsub|u>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|G>*<around|{|u<rsub|0>*<around|(|4*M|)>+<around|(|u<rsub|0>+4*M|)>*u<rsub|0>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Before division by <math|g>, the difference between
  <math|\<kappa\><rsub|G><around|(|\<Xi\><rsub|u>|)>> and the signed sum of
  the four bond curvature coordinates is bounded by
  <math|6*c<rsub|G>*M<rsup|2>>, as proved above. This yields exactly
  <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|G>*<around|(|M,g,W<rsub|f>|)>> in
  <eqref|eq:readerB-transport-cost> as an upper bound for the discrepancy
  between the rotated read and the normalized signed sum. Replacing each bond
  curvature by <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>+g*z<rsub|j>> costs at most <math|e/g>
  per bond by <eqref|eq:readerB-bondcurv>. The signed sum then reads
  <math|g<rsup|-1>*f<rsub|a>+\<cal-G\><rsub|A,F,x><around|(|Z|)><around|(|u,a|)>>
  in color coordinate <math|a>, by linearity and the definition of
  <math|D<rsub|A,F,x>>. Finally <eqref|eq:readerB-pointing-conclusion>
  replaces <math|g<rsup|-1>*f<rsub|a>> by the common parent forecast with
  error <math|x<rsup|-1/16>>. The total is at most

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-N\><rsub|G>*<around|(|M,g,W<rsub|f>|)>+4*e/g+x<rsup|-1/16>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|-1/8>+x<rsup|-1/16>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|-1/32>
  </equation*>

  for large <math|x>. The gauge used to compare <math|U<rprime|'>> to
  <math|U> can vary. The within-room forecast, sample, and coarse pose are
  fixed throughout.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Actual-reader curvature and read
    estimate><label|prop:readerB-curvature-read>Fix a selected transition
    tube and one posed coarse field with based gauge at <math|o>. Suppose the
    actual Gaussian reader estimates of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-reader-local-gaussian-prices>,
    the paid row and chart bounds above, and the local window/separation
    conditions hold, including <math|2*<around|(|W<rsub|f>+1|)>\<less\>n> for
    the fine period <math|n>. For each sample in the enlarged room with
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|g*z<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<kappa\><rsub|0>*t>
    on every active bond, the resulting fine field is healthy on the selected
    patch. For every gauge-equivalent fine field and every incidence row
    <math|j=<around|(|u,a|)>> whose two-step neighborhood lies inside the
    patch, its rigid color read differs from the same parent forecast plus
    the transported Gaussian curvature by at most <math|x<rsup|-1/32>>. All
    conclusions are uniform in the period once the displayed fixed-data
    threshold is reached.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Use <eqref|eq:readerB-fine-link> for all bonds in the enlarged room, then
    apply Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:readerB-pointing> to each tested row.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:readerB-healthy-face> gives the health estimate.
    The based-transport calculation and <eqref|eq:readerB-nautical> give the
    read estimate. All statements use the same base site and parent forecast,
    and their constants do not depend on the number of sites outside the
    tested patch.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The lost-mask estimate for a correlated Gaussian>

  Let <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|y>> be the actual finite set of real link-color
  coordinates read by the guard on the enlarged room. Its cardinality
  satisfies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:readerB-room-dimension>m<rsub|y>\<assign\><around|\||\<cal-I\><rsub|y>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|w><around|(|log
    y|)><rsup|16>
  </equation>

  for a constant <math|C<rsub|w>> depending on the fixed width and color
  dimension, but not on the period. Indeed the room radius for the mask is
  <math|W<rsub|f>+8>, <math|B<around|(|y|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>4<around|(|log
  y|)><rsup|4>> for large <math|y>, and the number of link-color coordinates
  in a four-dimensional box is at most a fixed multiple of its side to the
  fourth power.

  If <math|m<rsub|y>=0>, the product veil is <math|1> and the loss is zero.
  Otherwise assume <math|m<rsub|y>\<geqslant\>1>; the gauge algebra is
  nontrivial, so <math|n<rsub|G>\<geqslant\>1>. Group the coordinates by
  bond, writing <math|X<rsub|b>\<in\>\<frak-g\>> for the actual Lie-valued
  fluctuation read on bond <math|b>. Let <math|X=<around|(|X<rsub|i>|)><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|y>>>
  have the actual centered joint Gaussian law. Its covariance matrix is
  positive semidefinite and each entry has absolute value at most <math|U>,
  where <math|U\<geqslant\>1> is the common palette bound from the actual
  reader estimates. For <math|v\<gtr\>0>, each marginal obeys the Chernoff
  bound

  <\equation*>
    \<bbb-P\>*<around|(|<around|\||X<rsub|i>|\|>\<geqslant\>v|)>\<leqslant\>2*exp
    <around|(|-v<rsup|2>/<around|(|2*U|)>|)>;
  </equation*>

  this follows by optimizing <math|\<bbb-E\>*e<rsup|s*X<rsub|i>>=e<rsup|s<rsup|2><math-up|Var><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>/2>>
  at <math|s=v/U> and applying the same estimate to <math|-X<rsub|i>>. A
  union bound, which does not assume independence, gives

  <\equation*>
    \<bbb-P\>*<around|{|max<rsub|i><around|\||X<rsub|i>|\|>\<geqslant\>v|}>\<leqslant\>2*m<rsub|y>*exp
    <around|(|-v<rsup|2>/<around|(|2*U|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  For the bondwise guard, consider the event
  <math|max<rsub|b><around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|b>|\<\|\|\>>\<geqslant\>v>. On
  this event one of the <math|n<rsub|G>> real coordinates of some
  <math|X<rsub|b>> has modulus at least <math|v/<sqrt|n<rsub|G>>>. A union
  bound therefore gives

  <\equation*>
    \<bbb-P\>*<around|{|max<rsub|b><around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|b>|\<\|\|\>>\<geqslant\>v|}>\<leqslant\>2*m<rsub|y>*exp
    <around|(|-v<rsup|2>/<around|(|2*n<rsub|G>*U|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Set <math|\<varrho\><rsub|y>=<around|[|8*n<rsub|G>*<around|(|1+m<rsub|y>*U|)>|]><rsup|-1>>.
  Then <math|1/<around|(|2*n<rsub|G>*U|)>-\<varrho\><rsub|y>\<geqslant\>3/<around|(|8*n<rsub|G>*U|)>>.
  The guard threshold grows faster than every power of <math|log y>, so
  eventually <math|3*v<rsup|2>/<around|(|8*n<rsub|G>*U|)>\<geqslant\>log
  <around|(|2*m<rsub|y>|)>-2>. Hence

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:readerB-correlated-reserve>\<bbb-P\>*<around|{|max<rsub|b><around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|b>|\<\|\|\>>\<geqslant\>v|}>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|2>*exp
    <around|(|-\<varrho\><rsub|y>*v<rsup|2>|)>.
  </equation>

  The vector is the actual joint Gaussian. Correlation is allowed throughout;
  only its marginal variance bound and the finite union bound are used.

  Take the guard width

  <\equation*>
    v=<frac|\<kappa\><rsub|0>*t|2*g>.
  </equation*>

  For the band <math|x/2\<leqslant\>\<beta\>\<leqslant\>3*x>, the scalar
  coupling bound gives <math|v\<geqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|0><space|0.17em><math-up|hot><around|(|y|)>>
  with a fixed <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>\<gtr\>0>; here
  <math|<math-up|hot><around|(|y|)>=exp <around|(|<around|(|log log
  y|)><rsup|3/2>|)>>. By <eqref|eq:readerB-room-dimension>,

  <\equation*>
    1+m<rsub|y>*U\<leqslant\><around|(|1+C<rsub|w>*U|)><around|(|log
    y|)><rsup|16>.
  </equation*>

  For every fixed <math|p\<geqslant\>0>, the ratio

  <\equation*>
    <frac|\<delta\><rsub|0><rsup|2>*exp <around|(|2<around|(|log log
    y|)><rsup|3/2>|)>|8*n<rsub|G>*<around|(|1+C<rsub|w>*U|)><around|(|log
    y|)><rsup|16>*<around|(|2+p|)>*log y>\<longrightarrow\>\<infty\>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed, writing <math|q=log log y>, the logarithm of the numerator growth
  relative to the denominator is <math|2*q<rsup|3/2>-17*q+O<around|(|1|)>>,
  which tends to <math|+\<infty\>>. Thus eventually
  <math|\<varrho\><rsub|y>*v<rsup|2>\<geqslant\><around|(|2+p|)>*log y>, and
  <eqref|eq:readerB-correlated-reserve> gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:readerB-tail-power>\<bbb-P\>*<around|{|max<rsub|b><around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|b>|\<\|\|\>>\<geqslant\>v|}>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|2>*y<rsup|-<around|(|2+p|)>>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|-p>.
  </equation>

  Let <math|\<Gamma\>> be the smooth cutoff on the actual Gaussian vector:
  one radial factor for each active bond in the enlarged room. Each factor
  lies in <math|<around|[|0,1|]>>, vanishes outside the vector guard radius
  <math|\<kappa\><rsub|0>*t/g>, and equals one on the ball of half that
  radius. Consequently

  <\equation*>
    0\<leqslant\>1-\<Gamma\><around|(|X|)>\<leqslant\>\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|max<rsub|b><around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|b>|\<\|\|\>>\<geqslant\>v|}>>.
  </equation*>

  Integrating and using <eqref|eq:readerB-tail-power> proves

  <\equation*>
    \<bbb-E\>*<around|[|1-\<Gamma\><around|(|X|)>|]>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|-p>.
  </equation*>

  The estimate uses the marginal variances of the actual correlated Gaussian
  on its finite room.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|One-patch Gaussian guard><label|lem:readerB-mask>For every fixed
    <math|p\<geqslant\>0>, fixed patch width, and common palette entry bound
    <math|U\<geqslant\>1>, the mask loss on the actual joint Gaussian sample
    is at most <math|x<rsup|-p>> for all sufficiently large <math|x>,
    uniformly in the period. In particular, choose <math|p=1> to obtain the
    mask loss <math|x<rsup|-1>> used in the reader purchase.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The coordinate count is <eqref|eq:readerB-room-dimension>; the
    one-coordinate Chernoff estimate, union bound and scale comparison
    establish <eqref|eq:readerB-tail-power>. The pointwise cutoff comparison
    then proves the expected mask loss.
  </proof>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Boundary of this package>The result proves the nonlinear curvature
    and read estimates and the power-small loss of the Gaussian cutoff for
    the fixed posed reader, under the stated Gaussian, chart, row and window
    hypotheses. The assertion that the warning factor vanishes for every
    compatible field/store palette requires the separate hypothesis of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-corrected-body-compiler>.
  </remark>

  <subsection|Uniform selection of the reader comparison on a
  plateau><label|sec:actual-reader-dawn-selection>

  We now put the local estimates on one selected history. The choices must be
  uniform in the finite period, the reader patch, the forest and the tag. A
  further distinction is essential: the temperature used to record a Gaussian
  sector need not be the physical coupling at which the field is sampled. We
  keep these parameters separate.

  <subsubsection|One allowance for the whole coupling band>

  Fix a blocking patch with side <math|L>, its predictor kernel and adjacent
  path data. Fix positive strip width <math|s> and lower metric bound
  <math|a>, an upper price <math|z\<geqslant\>0>, a reader-room multiplier
  <math|w\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, a real error exponent <math|f>, and a real
  coupling-drift bound <math|\<gamma\>>. The preselected block side must
  satisfy the predictor smallness condition

  <\equation*>
    2*dim <around|(|\<frak-g\>|)>*<space|0.17em>T<rsub|\<Psi\>>*<around|(|z,L|)>\<leqslant\>1/4096,
  </equation*>

  where <math|T<rsub|\<Psi\>>*<around|(|z,L|)>> is the absolute-row ticket
  for the truncated coarse-to-fine curvature transport. This is a condition
  on the previous choice of <math|L>, not something achieved by increasing
  the entrance coupling afterward.

  For <math|t\<gtr\>1> define

  <\equation*>
    \<ell\><around|(|t|)>=log log t,<space|2em>\<varepsilon\><rsub|f><around|(|t|)>=exp
    <around|{|-f*\<ell\><around|(|t|)><rsup|2>|}>.
  </equation*>

  For every fixed <math|v\<gtr\>0> and real <math|c>, eventually in <math|t>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reader-band-power>x<rsup|-v>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|c><around|(|t|)>*<space|2em><around|(|t/2\<leqslant\>x\<leqslant\>t|)>.
  </equation>

  In fact <math|x<rsup|-v>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|v>*t<rsup|-v>>, and
  <math|c<around|(|log log t|)><rsup|2>=o<around|(|log t|)>>. Taking
  logarithms proves the inequality, uniformly over the displayed interval.
  This elementary comparison lets us use one error allowance at the top of a
  plateau.

  Here are the local inputs to which we apply it. The local Gaussian
  comparison gives one entry bound <math|U\<geqslant\>1>, followed by an
  eventual threshold for its mean and covariance estimates. The nonlinear
  guarded comparison supplies its field estimates for all
  <math|x/2\<leqslant\>\<beta\>\<leqslant\>3*x>, and the guard bound gives
  lost mass at most <math|x<rsup|-1>>. The common covariance-cover and
  transport estimates supply both short-row errors at most
  <math|x<rsup|-1/8>> as follows. Their payments have the forms
  <math|C<rsub|1>*e<rsup|-s*h<rsub|x>>> and
  <math|C<rsub|2>*e<rsup|-t*h<rsub|x>>>, with <math|s,t\<gtr\>0> and
  <math|C<rsub|1>,C<rsub|2>> fixed before <math|x>, uniformly over periods
  and beds in the purchased strip class. Here
  <math|h<rsub|x>=<around|\<lfloor\>|<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
  x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>/10000|\<rfloor\>>\<geqslant\><around|(|log
  x|)><rsup|2>/10000-1>. For any fixed <math|C,c\<gtr\>0>,

  <\equation*>
    log <around*|(|x<rsup|1/8>*C*e<rsup|-c*h<rsub|x>>|)>\<leqslant\>log
    C+c+<tfrac|1|8>log x-<tfrac|c|10000><around|(|log
    x|)><rsup|2>\<longrightarrow\>-\<infty\>.
  </equation*>

  Apply this to both payments and enlarge one common threshold. The adjacent
  coupling comparison holds uniformly for

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reader-coupling-band>x/2\<leqslant\>\<beta\>\<leqslant\>3*x,<space|2em>x/2\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rprime|'>,<space|2em><around|\||\<beta\><rprime|'>-\<beta\>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\>.
  </equation>

  There is no independent upper restriction on <math|\<beta\><rprime|'>>.
  Finally, the local chart thresholds give the room and logarithm
  inequalities used in the next subsection. These are respectively the
  conclusions of Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-reader-local-gaussian-prices>,
  <reference|sec:actual-reader-guarded-curvature> and
  <reference|sec:actual-reader-short-covariance>, with their stated same-data
  and prior-purchase hypotheses. Their production is a prerequisite here; the
  following choice argument does not supply an estimate absent from one of
  them.

  Take the maximum of their finitely many thresholds, after <math|L> and the
  other displayed fixed parameters have been chosen. For all sufficiently
  large <math|t>, every <math|x\<in\><around|[|t/2,t|]>> is above that
  maximum. Set

  <\equation*>
    p<rsub|x>=x<rsup|-1/8>.
  </equation*>

  This value is chosen before <math|\<beta\>,\<beta\><rprime|'>> and pays the
  short-row errors for every coupling pair in
  <eqref|eq:reader-coupling-band>. Increase the common threshold once more so
  that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reader-common-error-budget>x<rsup|-1/8>+p<rsub|x>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|f><around|(|t|)>,<space|2em>x<rsup|-1/32>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|f><around|(|t|)>,<space|2em>x<rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|f><around|(|t|)><rsup|2>*<space|1em><around|(|t/2\<leqslant\>x\<leqslant\>t|)>.
  </equation>

  For the first inequality apply <eqref|eq:reader-band-power> with
  <math|c=f+1> and note that <math|2*\<varepsilon\><rsub|f+1><around|(|t|)>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|f><around|(|t|)>>
  eventually. For the second use <math|c=f,v=1/32>; for the third use
  <math|c=2*f,v=1> and <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|2*f>=\<varepsilon\><rsub|f><rsup|2>>.

  Thus one <math|U> is chosen first, then one entrance threshold, then for
  each <math|x> one <math|p<rsub|x>>, and all the estimates hold for every
  admissible coupling pair, period, forest and tag. No maximum over an
  infinite collection of periods is being taken: each input estimate already
  has its uniform threshold.

  <subsubsection|The tube and covariance belong to the retained history>

  Let <math|C> be the selected family and fix its actual precision horizon
  and path attachment. At an adjacent step <math|i\<less\>N>, form the local
  Gaussian data from the fine path at <math|i+1>, the coarse path at
  <math|i>, their prescribed clips, and the tower between those very levels.
  The path bounds come from the Wilson representations of <math|C>; the
  metric floors, path attachments, symmetry and crossing identities come from
  this horizon. They are therefore the same objects in the local Gaussian
  estimate and in the short-covariance construction. Replacing either by
  another witness with similar bounds would not justify the calculation
  below.

  Suppose the step is in a constant-order plateau,
  <math|J*<around|(|i+1|)>=J<around|(|i|)>>. Its strip width, metric floor
  and price then have the same stage index on both sides. Write
  <math|P<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|cond>>> for its conditional curvature
  covariance, and <math|Q<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|short>>> for the prescribed
  finite-range covariance used in the reader expansion. The common-cover
  estimate, applied to the actual fine metric, gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reader-history-short-error><around|\||P<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|cond>>*<around|(|a,b|)>-Q<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|short>>*<around|(|a,b|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>p<rsub|x>.
  </equation>

  This includes the two cases in the short-row rule. Where the rule keeps the
  covariance entry there is no discrepancy. Where it sets an entry to zero,
  the identically truncated cover row has zero at that periodic address, so
  its tail estimate bounds the original entry. All images in the
  periodization are included in that support test.

  For a posed actual sector, the local estimate gives
  <math|<around|\||C<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|read>>*<around|(|a,b|)>-P<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|cond>>*<around|(|a,b|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|-1/8>>
  on both reader supports. Adding <eqref|eq:reader-history-short-error> and
  using <eqref|eq:reader-common-error-budget> yields

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reader-selected-sight><around|\||C<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|read>>*<around|(|a,b|)>-Q<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|short>>*<around|(|a,b|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|f><around|(|t|)>.
  </equation>

  The short covariance is a comparator. The random vector continues to have
  the actual sector covariance <math|C<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|read>>>.

  The corresponding transport-row estimate uses the same horizon and gives
  the short mean-transport error. Combining these two estimates on each
  plateau step is the history comparison used by the reader expansion. The
  common schedule must place the actual entrance parameter in
  <math|<around|[|t/2,t|]>> and the actual couplings in
  <eqref|eq:reader-coupling-band>; these are the purchased plateau and
  coupling conditions retained in the application.

  <subsubsection|One pose contains all required reads>

  We give the geometric check, since it prevents accidental use of different
  local gauges for different readers. Put
  <math|b<rsub|x>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>
  and <math|w<rsub|x>=b<rsub|x><rsup|2>>. At a fixed step let <math|y> be the
  next prescribed scale parameter, and suppose the paid room conditions hold.
  In explicit terms the conditions used here are

  <\equation*>
    b<rsub|x>\<geqslant\>200,<space|1em>w\<geqslant\>d+10,<space|1em>w*w<rsub|y>+L\<leqslant\>L*w<rsub|x>,
  </equation*>

  together with room nonwrapping at both levels and the prescribed reader
  separation of at most <math|d*w<rsub|x>>. Here <math|d> is the fixed reader
  separation order. The recording parameter <math|r<around|(|x|)>> satisfies

  <\equation*>
    x\<leqslant\>r<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|2>,<space|2em>b<rsub|r<around|(|x|)>>\<leqslant\>4*b<rsub|x>,
  </equation*>

  and its flag threshold equals the healthy threshold at <math|x>.
  Consequently <math|11*b<rsub|r<around|(|x|)>>+1\<leqslant\>w<rsub|x>>. Also
  the nonwrapping room bound at radius <math|w*w<rsub|x>> implies the local
  Gaussian side bound: <math|100*<around|(|1+b<rsub|r<around|(|x|)>>|)>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|w*w<rsub|x>+1|)>>
  eventually under these inequalities, and the torus side exceeds the latter
  quantity.

  For this application fix the seed-free store: both its seed sets are empty,
  and its flag set is exactly the set of flags of the actual coarse field at
  recording threshold <math|<math-up|temp><around|(|r<around|(|x|)>|)>>. This
  threshold equals <math|\<tau\><rsub|x>>. Thus, for a coarse field healthy
  on the whole room of radius <math|w*w<rsub|x>>, the store has no marked
  site in that room. Health alone would not remove seeds from an arbitrary
  store. For this seed-free store, the local axial construction gives one
  gauge on this room and a logarithmic direction of norm at most
  <math|8*w*w<rsub|x>*\<tau\><rsub|x>>, where <math|\<tau\><rsub|x>> is the
  healthy threshold. The purchased chart conditions place this inside half
  the logarithmic clearance allowance and inside the fixed chart radius.
  Extend that direction by zero outside the room. It remains one global
  direction with the same supremum bound. At any address whose distance from
  the room boundary is at least <math|w<rsub|x>>, the whole
  <math|b<rsub|r<around|(|x|)>>>-ball lies inside the chart, and its
  <math|11*b<rsub|r<around|(|x|)>>+1> seed neighbourhood contains no flag.
  The replica bound follows from the linear replica norm bound and the
  exponential chart estimate. These are exactly the pose conditions used
  above.

  It remains to fit the fine guards into that room. A fine bond in the
  enlarged guard room of radius <math|w*w<rsub|y>+8> has parent at distance
  at most <math|w<rsub|x>+8> from the parent of the fine reader centre.
  Indeed the parent-distance inequality is

  <\equation*>
    L<space|0.17em><math-up|dist><around|(|<math-up|parent><space|0.17em>u,<math-up|parent><space|0.17em>v|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|dist><around|(|u,v|)>+L,
  </equation*>

  and <math|w*w<rsub|y>+L\<leqslant\>L*w<rsub|x>> gives the assertion. The
  coarse centre of a tested reader is at distance at most <math|d*w<rsub|x>>
  from the chosen root reader. Adding distances shows that every such guard
  bond has

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|dist><around|(|o,<math-up|parent><space|0.17em>v|)>+w<rsub|x>+2\<leqslant\><around|(|d+2|)>*w<rsub|x>+10\<leqslant\>w*w<rsub|x>,
  </equation*>

  using <math|w<rsub|x>\<geqslant\>8> and <math|w\<geqslant\>d+10>. Every
  incidence read has the same inequality with <math|+1> in place of
  <math|+2>. Thus both endpoints of every covariance entry, and all four
  bonds used in every nonlinear read, lie in the one pose. Signed
  translations carry the reader centres, masks, forest and tag together, so
  the same inequalities apply to every selected orientation.

  <subsubsection|The joint reader model on the actual sample space>

  Fix a forest and tag, retain this same seed-free store, and choose a chart
  cutoff <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0> with <math|2*\<delta\>> smaller than the
  fixed first logarithmic chart radius of the gauge group. These are the
  cutoff hypotheses of the measurable field construction in the joint reader
  model. Use the common product space of frozen Haar links and the centered
  Gaussian sector coordinates from Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-all-arity-reader-gaussian-model>.
  The signed transport and the one pose gauge send the store, forest, tag and
  sample together. Their measure-preserving identities give one joint
  centered incidence vector, with covariance
  <math|C<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|read>>>, for every reader. Equation
  <eqref|eq:reader-selected-sight> is therefore an entrywise comparison of
  that actual joint law with the short covariance, including all off-diagonal
  pairs.

  For each reader use the guard on its enlarged fine room. The Gaussian guard
  estimate gives

  <\equation*>
    0\<leqslant\>\<Gamma\><rsub|a>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em><big|int><around|(|1-\<Gamma\><rsub|a>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|f><around|(|t|)><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  On its positive set, the nonlinear comparison makes the fine field healthy
  on that reader patch and gives one gauge element for the whole patch. In
  that gauge, each normalized coordinate differs by at most
  <math|x<rsup|-1/32>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|f><around|(|t|)>> from
  the sum of the fixed parent forecast and the corresponding coordinate of
  the common Gaussian vector. The forecast uses the actual coarse coupling
  <math|\<beta\><rprime|'>>; the fine field uses <math|\<beta\>>. Their
  conversion was included in the adjacent coupling estimate, so they are not
  set equal in this step.

  The same construction works for every permitted reader in the fixed finite
  family. Its mask bound is per reader, with no union over the family, and
  its gauge is common to the coordinates of one patch. All readers still
  share the same random vector and the same parent forecast. These are
  precisely the quantitative receipts left explicit in the joint reader-model
  construction. They are obtained on the selected family whenever its
  purchased plateau, room, chart and coupling conditions hold. The later
  activity reassembly remains responsible for identifying the resulting
  expansion with the interacting block integral.

  <subsection|Local Gaussian prices in the common reader
  frame><label|sec:actual-reader-local-gaussian-prices>

  We combine the actual local-window comparison with the exact tree identity
  of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-reader-tree-covariance>. This
  gives both the mean and every cross-entry of the curvature covariance in
  the one frame used by the reader family.

  Fix the patch, predictor kernel, path bounds and adjacent tower data. Their
  numerical costs are fixed before the finite period is chosen. Let <math|x>
  be the large reader parameter and <math|t> its recording parameter, chosen
  with

  <\equation*>
    x\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>x<rsup|2>,<space|2em>B=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    t|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>.
  </equation*>

  The recording parameter is chosen so that its coarse flag threshold equals
  the reader's healthy threshold. All sector matrices below use this same
  <math|t> and its flag set. Assume the coarse torus side is at least
  <math|100*<around|(|1+B|)>> and retain the predictor branch and fine-side
  conditions of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-reader-tree-covariance>.

  Use a pose of the coarse field on a room of radius <math|W> around
  <math|o>: one gauge makes that field equal there to the exponential of one
  real direction <math|v>, whose supremum norm is at most half the selected
  logarithmic clearance allowance. For every interior coarse address <math|z>
  with

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|dist><around|(|o,z|)>+w<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>W,
  </equation*>

  assume its <math|B>-neighbourhood is in that same logarithmic chart, the
  replica has the prescribed clearance bound there, and no seed lies within
  <math|11*B+1> of <math|z>. Here <math|w<around|(|x|)>> is the reader's
  fixed room margin. These are the local conditions of the pose, not separate
  choices of gauges at each address.

  Let <math|\<cal-U\>> be the actual active link-coordinate set for the
  chosen store and forest, and let <math|\<iota\>:\<cal-U\>\<to\>V<rsub|F>>
  insert its coordinates into the complete forest coordinate set. Let
  <math|H> be the actual ordered window inverse, and let <math|H<rsup|0>> be
  the ordered window inverse with empty store and identity coarse field,
  using the same forest, tag and window geometry. Write
  <math|C=<around|(|H+H<rsup|<math-ss|T>>|)>/2> for the actual Gaussian
  covariance, and <math|C<rsup|0>=<around|(|H<rsup|0>+<around|(|H<rsup|0>|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>|)>/2>
  on <math|V<rsub|F>> for the bare one. The prescribed mean with arbitrary
  frozen links is denoted <math|m>; the stationary mean in the single
  direction <math|v> is <math|m<rsup|0>>.

  Assume the small-window specialization in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-actual-reader-small-window> has
  supplied its mean and column estimates for these actual data. These state,
  for every interior column <math|j> and every actual active row <math|i>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reader-local-column-price><around|\||m<rsub|j>-m<rsup|0><rsub|\<iota\><around|(|j|)>>|\|>\<leqslant\>t<rsup|-7/8>,<space|2em><around|\||H<rsub|i*j>-H<rsup|0><rsub|\<iota\><around|(|i|)>,\<iota\><around|(|j|)>>|\|>\<leqslant\>t<rsup|-1/4>.
  </equation>

  Its hypotheses are precisely the chart and clearance conditions just
  listed. In particular its frozen-source cancellation holds for every choice
  of the frozen field and auxiliary channel parameters; no bound on those
  arbitrary frozen values is introduced here. If both <math|i,j> are
  interior, the two column estimates imply

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reader-local-symmetric-price><around|\||C<rsub|i*j>-C<rsup|0><rsub|\<iota\><around|(|i|)>,\<iota\><around|(|j|)>>|\|>\<leqslant\>t<rsup|-1/4>.
  </equation>

  This follows by adding the two inequalities and dividing by two.

  <subsubsection|Removing the bare cut and window>

  Let <math|A<rsub|F>> be the full flat precision on <math|V<rsub|F>>
  identified in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-reader-tree-covariance>.
  The bare reference still uses a path cutoff and a principal window. We
  show, at the fixed patch, that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reader-bare-full-price><around|\<\|\|\>|C<rsup|0>-A<rsub|F><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|0>\<leqslant\>K*e<rsup|-c*B>
  </equation>

  for constants <math|K,c\<gtr\>0> independent of the period, forest and tag.
  Here <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|0>> is the maximum of
  absolute row and column sums.

  The actual path cutoff is <math|r=<around|\<lfloor\>|B/100000|\<rfloor\>>>.
  Its exponentially weighted path bound gives a row tail at most
  <math|C<rsub|1>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*r/4>> in a narrower weighted norm, with
  <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0> fixed. Taking Gram products preserves such a
  bound: if <math|L> is the full row map and <math|L<rsub|r>> its cut
  version, then

  <\equation*>
    L<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*L-L<rsub|r><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*L<rsub|r>=<around|(|L-L<rsub|r>|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*L+L<rsub|r><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*<around|(|L-L<rsub|r>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The weighted row-and-column norm is submultiplicative, and both row maps
  have fixed bounds. Thus the corresponding precisions
  <math|A<rsub|F>,A<rsub|F,r>> differ by at most
  <math|C<rsub|2>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*r/4>>. The cut does not remove the direct
  curl or predictor terms, and every remaining path term is a nonnegative
  Gram form. Retaining just the identity channel in the normalized signed
  average gives a lower bound equal to <math|1/<around|\||\<cal-S\>|\|>>
  times its direct-curl and predictor form, where <math|\<cal-S\>> is the
  finite signed-channel set. Apply the tree inequality of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-reader-tree-covariance> with direct
  coefficient <math|\<alpha\>\<gtr\>0> from <eqref|eq:reader-flat-factor>:
  the cut precision has lower bound <math|min
  <around|(|\<alpha\>,\<kappa\>|)>/<around|[|<around|\||\<cal-S\>|\|>*<around|(|1+b<rsub|1>+b<rsub|2>|)>|]>>.
  Thus both cut and uncut precisions have a common fixed positive gap,
  without using the uncut metric factorization for the cut rows. The weighted
  inverse estimate of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-covariance> and
  the identity

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|F,r><rsup|-1>-A<rsub|F><rsup|-1>=A<rsub|F,r><rsup|-1>*<around|(|A<rsub|F>-A<rsub|F,r>|)>*A<rsub|F><rsup|-1>
  </equation*>

  therefore give the same exponential order for the inverse difference. All
  norms can be taken at one smaller fixed positive width.

  For the actual bare principal windows the inner radius is
  <math|<around|\<lfloor\>|B/60|\<rfloor\>>>, by the panel inclusion in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:principal-comparison-jets>. The window-error
  proof of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-covariance> then bounds the
  difference between the ordered bare window inverse and
  <math|A<rsub|F,r><rsup|-1>> by <math|C<rsub|3>*e<rsup|-\<gamma\>*<around|\<lfloor\>|B/60|\<rfloor\>>>>,
  with <math|\<gamma\>\<gtr\>0>. The empty store has all forest coordinates
  active. Hence restriction to these coordinates identifies its windows and
  cut flat precision with the ones just used; it introduces no further
  projection of an inverse. Symmetrization does not increase the zero-width
  norm. For <math|B\<geqslant\>200000>,

  <\equation*>
    B\<leqslant\>120*<around|\<lfloor\>|B/60|\<rfloor\>>,<space|2em>B\<leqslant\>200000*<around|\<lfloor\>|B/100000|\<rfloor\>>.
  </equation*>

  Taking <math|c\<leqslant\>min <around|(|\<gamma\>/120,\<sigma\>/800000|)>>
  and adding the two errors proves <eqref|eq:reader-bare-full-price>. Since
  <math|B\<geqslant\><around|(|log t|)><rsup|2>>, eventually
  <math|e<rsup|-c*B>\<leqslant\>t<rsup|-1/4>>.

  <subsubsection|Reading curvature and preserving every cross-entry>

  Let <math|L=D*I<rsub|F>> be the full forest-coordinate curl matrix, with
  colour identity included. Each of its absolute row sums is bounded by a
  fixed number <math|U>, uniformly in period and forest. A nonzero entry
  <math|L<rsub|a*i>> places the parent anchor of <math|i> within one coarse
  step of the plaquette anchor of <math|a>. Therefore, if a plaquette
  <math|a> satisfies

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|dist><around|(|o,<math-up|anchor><around|(|a|)>|)>+w<around|(|x|)>+1\<leqslant\>W,
  </equation*>

  every coordinate used by its curl row is interior. The clearance condition
  makes all these forest coordinates active. This is why the actual
  restricted curl and <math|L> read precisely the same coordinates on the
  plaquettes under consideration.

  For two such plaquettes <math|a,b>, expand their covariance as a finite
  double sum. Applying <eqref|eq:reader-local-symmetric-price> to each pair
  of used coordinates gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||<math-up|Cov><rsub|<math-up|actual>><around|(|a,b|)>-<around|(|L*C<rsup|0>*L<rsup|<math-ss|T>>|)><rsub|a*b>|\|>\<leqslant\>t<rsup|-1/4>*<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\||L<rsub|a*i>|\|>*<big|sum><rsub|j><around|\||L<rsub|b*j>|\|>\<leqslant\>U<rsup|2>*t<rsup|-1/4>.
  </equation*>

  No independence between coordinates or plaquettes was used. The same
  calculation and <eqref|eq:reader-bare-full-price> give

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||<around|(|L*C<rsup|0>*L<rsup|<math-ss|T>>|)><rsub|a*b>-<around|(|L*A<rsub|F><rsup|-1>*L<rsup|<math-ss|T>>|)><rsub|a*b>|\|>\<leqslant\>U<rsup|2>*K*e<rsup|-c*B>\<leqslant\>U<rsup|2>*K*t<rsup|-1/4>.
  </equation*>

  By <eqref|eq:reader-tree-conditional-covariance>, the last full covariance
  is the colour extension of <math|P<rsub|f>-J*P<rsub|c>*J<rsup|<math-ss|T>>>.
  Consequently, eventually in <math|x>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reader-strong-scope-price><around*|\||<math-up|Cov><rsub|<math-up|actual>><around|(|a,b|)>-<around|(|P<rsub|f>-J*P<rsub|c>*J<rsup|<math-ss|T>>|)><rsub|a*b>|\|>\<leqslant\>U<rsup|2>*<around|(|1+K|)>*t<rsup|-1/4>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|-1/8>.
  </equation>

  For the last inequality, <math|t\<geqslant\>x> and the fixed constant
  <math|U<rsup|2>*<around|(|1+K|)>> is eventually at most <math|x<rsup|1/8>>.
  The mean estimate in <eqref|eq:reader-local-column-price> similarly implies
  <math|<around|\||m<rsub|j>-m<rsup|0><rsub|\<iota\><around|(|j|)>>|\|>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|-7/8>>.

  Finally, the actual covariance <math|C> is positive definite with a uniform
  entry bound, whether or not a pose is available. Indeed the sector gap and
  row bounds hold for the actual flag store, and the positive local
  covariance construction of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-covariance>
  applies to either admitted window geometry. Its row bound bounds each
  entry. Enlarge this constant to at least one and take the maximum of the
  finitely many thresholds used above. This one choice supplies positivity
  and bounded entries for every permitted store, together with the mean and
  curvature comparison on every admissible pose, forest, tag and period.

  These are the quantitative local Gaussian estimates. The nonlinear
  comparison between a group-valued field read and its linear Gaussian
  coordinate, the lost-mask probability and the selected-history telescoping
  error are additional steps; none is inferred from a covariance identity
  alone.

  <subsection|Applying the local estimates to one marked
  step><label|sec:actual-local-compiler-handoff>

  The local estimates must hold for the very matrices and profiles used in
  the marked density identity. We now make this common choice explicit. The
  argument combines the preceding local results; it does not introduce a new
  Gaussian law or replace the selected history.

  Fix a gauge group, a patch of block size <math|L>, its funded tube
  construction, and the probability-Haar chart of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:haar-chart>. The minimizing and fallback
  branches are those fixed in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:predictor-branch>.
  Let <math|e\<geqslant\>0> be the support exponent needed by the marked
  step, including its prescribed reserve, and let <math|t\<gtr\>0> be its
  footprint allowance. The conversion from fine supports to coarse connected
  covers is required to have reserve <math|t> at this <math|e>. Fix also the
  profile width <math|W\<gtr\>0>, the chart-radius multiplier
  <math|d\<geqslant\>0>, the warning margin <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>,
  the owner-energy constant <math|m\<gtr\>0>, and <math|K\<gtr\>0> with

  <\equation*>
    2*K\<leqslant\>m/2,<space|2em>2*a<rsub|<math-up|coast>>\<leqslant\>K/4.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|a<rsub|<math-up|coast>>> is the coefficient of the forced
  large-field allowance <math|G<around|(|u|)>=exp
  <around|(|-a<rsub|<math-up|coast>><math-up|hot><around|(|u|)><rsup|2>|)>>.
  These are the numerical margins already used by the corrected compiler and
  its inherited-activity estimate.

  The guide estimate is chosen before <math|L>: the absolute path sums of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:seed-guides> give
  <math|C=2*A*<around|(|1+a|)>> simultaneously for all coarse fields,
  including all stores later compatible with those fields. The choices in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:warning-safety> then impose

  <\equation*>
    C/L<rsup|2>\<leqslant\><sqrt|<math-up|direct>>/64,<space|2em>C+<sqrt|<math-up|direct>>/64\<leqslant\><around|(|W/4|)><sqrt|<math-up|penalty>>.
  </equation*>

  After <math|L> and <math|W> have been fixed, the remaining constants below
  may depend on this patch. They are chosen before the coupling parameter and
  before any torus, forest, tag, coarse field or finite family of insertions.

  For a large parameter <math|x>, write <math|y=r<around|(|x|)>> for its
  retiming and <math|B=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
  y|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>. An admissible marked step consists of its actual
  tube, fine representation <math|R>, coarse budget, chart and profiles. Its
  tube is funded by the fixed patch construction; its upward paths and high
  clips are the path and clip of <math|R>. Its coarse threshold is
  <math|<math-up|temp><around|(|y|)>>, its chart radius is
  <math|d<space|0.17em><math-up|fiery><around|(|y|)>>, and its coarse side is
  at least <math|100*<around|(|1+B|)>>. The remaining radius, threshold,
  coupling-band and inherited-law inequalities are exactly those listed in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-corrected-body-compiler>. In addition
  we retain the actual alongside certificate for the tube and the small
  additive remainder of <math|R>. These identify the input to the
  construction; large coupling alone would not identify its paths or its
  remainder.

  <subsubsection|One matrix certificate and both support inventories>

  For every compatible store, forest, original tag and coarse field, use the
  actual selected derivative matrix of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-global-row-prices>.
  Complete its Gram matrix by the identity on the inactive link-colour
  coordinates and call the resulting full precision <math|A>.
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:actual-global-row-prices>, applied at
  <math|y>, supplies the selected-row gap and screened covariance estimates
  for this actual matrix. These are derivatives at the actual replica and
  predictor. The transported-flat precision of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:sector-gram> is a different construction;
  its bound does not by itself identify the two matrices away from the flat
  reference. The bounds are uniform over all of these data, including frozen
  coordinates; compatibility requires that the stored coarse flags equal the
  flags of the coarse field at threshold <math|<math-up|temp><around|(|y|)>>.

  Here is a simultaneous choice of constants in the local inverse and
  logarithmic estimates. Let <math|s\<gtr\>0,M<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0,\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>
  be their common initial Schur rate, amplitude and gap: take
  <math|s=\<sigma\>/2>, <math|M<rsub|0>=L<rsub|0><rsup|2>> from that
  proposition's row bound (by submultiplicativity of the weighted Schur
  norm), and its gap <math|\<eta\>>. Set <math|M=max
  <around|(|M<rsub|0>,1|)>> and <math|h=min <around|(|\<eta\>,1|)>>. Choose
  the inverse rate <math|Y\<gtr\>0> supplied by
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-covariance>, with <math|Y\<less\>s>,
  and put

  <\equation*>
    m<rsub|0>=Y/2,<space|1em>v=m<rsub|0>/2,<space|1em>R<rsub|0>=<around|(|m<rsub|0>-v|)>/120,<space|1em>b=R<rsub|0>/2,<space|1em>\<kappa\>=b/2.
  </equation*>

  Enlarge the common amplitude <math|C\<geqslant\>1> to cover the precision,
  its inverse and the local volume sum at rate <math|Y-m<rsub|0>>, divided by
  <math|min <around|(|h/2,1|)>>. These constants depend only on the fixed
  patch data. The window formulas of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-covariance>
  now price the actual inverse and both determinant corrections with rate
  <math|v>, amplitude <math|C> and error <math|e<rsup|-b*B>>. They apply also
  to every admissible recut whose windows contain their radius-<math|B/60>
  neighborhoods. Indeed, the missed entries have separation at least
  <math|<around|\<lfloor\>|B/60|\<rfloor\>>>; for <math|B\<geqslant\>120>,

  <\equation*>
    B\<leqslant\>120*<around|\<lfloor\>|B/60|\<rfloor\>>.
  </equation*>

  The weighted inverse estimate therefore bounds their error by
  <math|C<rsup|4>*e<rsup|-R<rsub|0>*B>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-b*B>> eventually.
  The same window estimate gives positivity of the recut covariance. The
  original selected covariance is kept fixed when these auxiliary recuts are
  considered.

  We also need rooted prices for two actual word inventories, of orders
  <math|n=0> and <math|n=1>, with halo multiplier <math|l=20>. Set
  <math|\<theta\>=b/<around|(|4*<around|(|l+2|)>|)>>. In the itinerary
  summation of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:itinerary-prices>, the
  summation ratio has the form <math|C<rsub|1>*e<rsup|-<around|(|b-\<theta\>|)>*B>>
  and the numerator for order <math|n> is bounded by

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|2>*e<rsup|-<around|[|b-\<theta\>*<around|(|n+1+l|)>|]>*B>.
  </equation*>

  The constants include the fixed cover weight at exponent <math|e>. Since
  <math|n\<leqslant\>1>, the exponent in brackets is at least
  <math|3*b/4\<gtr\>\<kappa\>>. The ratio is eventually at most <math|1/2>,
  and the geometric series is therefore at most
  <math|2*C<rsub|2>*e<rsup|-3*b*B/4>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<kappa\>*B>>.
  Intersect the two resulting tails. We obtain both rooted inventory prices
  with the same <math|v,C,b,\<kappa\>>, the actual physical address map, and
  the complete halo supports. This is a price for the individual ordered
  words through a fixed site, not just for their sum as an operator.

  <subsubsection|The first derivative of the two long determinant families>

  The value-level compiler also needs first derivatives as the coarse field
  moves in its local logarithmic coordinates. Freeze stores, forests, tags
  and windows and use the actual matrix <math|A<around|(|U|)>>, rather than
  selecting a new sector at each nearby field. We first justify a radius
  independent of the torus. The finite-patch predictor estimate of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:predictor-branch> gives a common chart
  radius for its retained rows. For the path rows, use the path extension
  bound in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-row-quadratic-price> before
  summing the slots. A fixed number of derivatives costs a polynomial in the
  path length; every additional spatial or derivative-coordinate weight costs
  an exponential in that length with an arbitrarily small fixed rate. Choose
  the latter rate so that <math|<around|(|1+2*j|)>*\<delta\>> is at most half
  the incoming path rate for the required order <math|j=2>. The incoming
  absolute path budget absorbs the polynomial and all these weights. Summing
  the absolute slot bounds, including repeated links, therefore gives one
  entrywise derivative majorant for all rows on a fixed logarithmic ball. The
  local predictor rows have the same property on a smaller ball. We retain
  the derivative-coordinate weights in this statement. For an ordered list
  <math|\<alpha\>> of atomic link-color derivatives,
  <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>2>, put
  <math|W<rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|o|)>=<big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>\<alpha\>>e<rsup|\<delta\>*d*<around|(|o,<math-up|hearth><around|(|a|)>|)>>>.
  For a row anchored at <math|o> and a column anchored at <math|z>, the
  bounds are

  <\equation*>
    W<rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|o|)>*e<rsup|\<delta\>*d*<around|(|o,z|)>>*<around|\||\<partial\><rsub|\<alpha\>>*M<rsub|o*z>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|1>,<space|2em>W<rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|z|)>*e<rsup|\<delta\>*d*<around|(|o,z|)>>*<around|\||\<partial\><rsub|\<alpha\>>*M<rsub|o*z>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  To verify them term by term, a derivative is zero if any atom misses the
  slot's complete support. Otherwise its endpoint and derivative-coordinate
  distances cost at most <math|<around|(|1+2<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>|)>*\<delta\>*<around|(|\<ell\>+5|)>>
  for a slot of length <math|\<ell\>>. The chosen half-rate reserve pays this
  exponential, while the other half pays its fixed-order differentiation
  factor. Absolute summation over the original path labels proves both
  displayed bounds. The predictor contribution has fixed reach and a uniform
  analytic radius, so the same argument uses its finite support instead.
  Repeated atomic derivatives and repeated physical links are included.

  Sum rows and columns at spatial rate <math|\<delta\>/2>, leaving a decaying
  factor for their exponential shells. The bounded number of coordinates per
  site makes these sums finite uniformly in the period. For derivative order
  <math|k=2>, lower both spatial and derivative-coordinate rates further to
  <math|u=<around|(|\<delta\>/2|)>/<around|(|1+k|)>>. The inequality
  <math|u+k*u\<leqslant\>\<delta\>/2> pays the transfer of
  derivative-coordinate weights across the intermediate matrix index. For
  <math|M<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*M>, expand each ordered derivative list over its
  finitely many Leibniz partitions. The triangle inequality transfers the
  derivative-coordinate weights to the appropriate factor in each term;
  weighted row/column multiplication then bounds that term. Summing the
  finitely many partitions gives the full weighted derivative prices for the
  precision. Complete inactive coordinates by the identity. All selections
  remain frozen; no derivative of a flag indicator occurs. Thus the estimates
  retain the atomic-list and support information required by the itinerary
  argument below.

  For precision, these first-derivative bounds give constants
  <math|\<sigma\>,C<rsub|0>,r<rsub|1>\<gtr\>0>, uniform in the period and
  base field, such that

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||<around|(|D*A<around|(|H|)>*Z|)><rsub|i*j>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|0>*S<rsub|G>*<around|(|\<sigma\>/2|)>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*d*<around|(|i,j|)>><around|\<\|\|\>|Z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*<space|2em><around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|H|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|1>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|S<rsub|G>*<around|(|\<sigma\>/2|)>> is a uniform upper bound for
  the sum of the decaying reciprocal derivative-coordinate weights
  <math|e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*d*<around|(|o,<math-up|hearth><around|(|a|)>|)>/2>>,
  including color multiplicity. Let <math|V<rsub|0>> bound the row and column
  volume sums at rate <math|\<sigma\>/2>, and set
  <math|U<rsub|0>=V<rsub|0>*C<rsub|0>*S<rsub|G>*<around|(|\<sigma\>/2|)>>.
  Integrate the last estimate along the segment from <math|0> to <math|H> and
  sum the remaining exponential weights. The Schur norm of
  <math|A<around|(|H|)>-A<around|(|0|)>> is at most
  <math|U<rsub|0><around|\<\|\|\>|H|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>. If the base
  gap is <math|\<eta\>>, choose

  <\equation*>
    r<rsub|0>=min <around|{|r<rsub|1>,\<eta\>/<around|[|2*<around|(|1+U<rsub|0>|)>|]>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Then for every real vector <math|z> and
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|H|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|0>>,

  <\equation*>
    z<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*A<around|(|H|)>*z\<geqslant\><around|(|\<eta\>-U<rsub|0><around|\<\|\|\>|H|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|)><around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>\<geqslant\><around|(|\<eta\>/2|)><around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  This is a uniform neighborhood gap, including fields that no longer obey
  the original flag condition. The flag condition was used at the base field,
  and the matrix formula is extended with its selections fixed.

  Apply the inverse and window derivative formulas of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:normalization-jets> on this ball. Restrict
  once more to half its radius. The actual selected covariance agrees there,
  as a germ, with the symmetric window formula for the completed precision:
  the unused-coordinate blocks are identities and the active blocks are the
  original selected ones. Both this covariance and the precision have the
  positive floors supplied by the window estimates. Restrict the logarithmic
  direction further to

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|H|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>min
    <around|{|r<rsub|0>/2,r<rsub|G><around|(|1|)>/2|}>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|r<rsub|G><around|(|1|)>\<gtr\>0> is the gauge-coordinate radius
  at order one. This radius concerns the variation of a particular matrix
  function. It is not the width of the replacement profiles.

  Apply the differentiated word estimates of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:itinerary-prices> at derivative order one,
  halo <math|20> and support exponent <math|e>. They cover both long
  determinant classes on the same field neighborhood. Choose their positive
  decay rate <math|d<rsub|0>> and fixed letter factor <math|F<rsub|0>> before
  <math|y>. The scalar conversion and the two classes together cost at most

  <\equation*>
    4*F<rsub|0>*e<rsup|1>*<around|{|2*<around|(|1+1|)>|}><rsup|1>*e<rsup|-d<rsub|0>*B>.
  </equation*>

  For large <math|B> this is at most <math|e<rsup|-d<rsub|0>*B/2>>. Thus the
  common neighborhood, individual word prices, and total connected-cover
  price are available simultaneously. There is no need to identify
  <math|d<rsub|0>> with the rate <math|\<kappa\>> of the preceding value
  inventories; their two positive rates can be retained separately. The order
  parameters used here are: scalar conversion order two, coarse-field
  derivative order one, and halo twenty.

  <subsubsection|Uniformity on a whole coupling interval>

  The palette estimate of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-palette-warning>
  provides one bound <math|\<ell\>\<geqslant\>1> for all actual pilots of
  this patch. It gives the Gaussian-coordinate bound <math|y<rsup|2>> on the
  door event, the linear-residual bound <math|y<rsup|-4>>, and the
  all-compatible-palette zero-socket conclusion. The last conclusion retains
  that section's same-field response, chart, beacon, port and distant-door
  receipts uniformly for every matched pilot; the bound for one posed reader
  alone does not establish it. The actual owner-energy estimate
  <eqref|eq:owner-actual-heft> supplies the other missing input to
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-corrected-body-compiler>. Its path
  counting and clip estimate use precisely the path and clip identified
  above. The fixed chart and branch construction, the guide reserve, and the
  small span estimate supply the remaining local premises of these
  applications.

  These assertions must hold for every <math|x\<in\><around|[|s/2,s|]>> once
  the chosen station parameter <math|s> is large. There are only finitely
  many eventual predicates at this stage: the matrix certificate and its two
  inventories, the first-derivative word prices, palette bound, owner
  estimate, local chart and room conditions, and the fixed numerical
  inequalities for the corrected alphabet. Pull the predicates in <math|y>
  back along <math|r<around|(|x|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>. Intersect them with the
  predicates already expressed in <math|x>, and choose one lower threshold
  <math|x<rsub|0>>. Finally require <math|s/2\<geqslant\>x<rsub|0>>. Then
  every <math|x\<in\><around|[|s/2,s|]>> satisfies them. This step is
  performed before quantifying over periods, fields, forests and tags because
  each local estimate above was uniform in those variables. It is also before
  the choice of any finite marked family.

  <subsubsection|The warning estimate on the corrected alphabet>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Neutralization preserves the joint warning
    estimate><label|lem:actual-neutral-warning>Fix an admissible local tuple
    as above, including its patch, funded tube, path and clip, coupling,
    chart, mask widths and predictor branch. Purchase the selected all-pilot
    package of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-palette-warning> and the
    actual response prices of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-global-row-prices>
    on this tuple. Let <math|R<rsup|\<circ\>>> be obtained from <math|R> by
    deleting only its additive remainder. The matched pilots, endpoint
    palettes, Gaussian integration law and warning sockets are identical for
    <math|R> and <math|R<rsup|\<circ\>>>. Consequently their all-matched
    palette condition has one common constant and one common threshold,
    uniform over fields, stores, forests, tags and periods. For every finite
    set <math|S> of vertices of the corrected alphabet, write
    <math|X<around|(|S|)>> for its distinct warning addresses. If the other
    factors have the pointwise prices <math|c<rsub|i>> used in the corrected
    compiler and the warning factors have pointwise bound <math|T>, then

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:actual-corrected-warning-product><big|int><around*|\||<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S>f<rsub|i>|\|>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|A>\<leqslant\>E<around|(|y|)><rsup|<around|\||X<around|(|S|)>|\|>>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S>c<rsub|i>,<space|2em>c<rsub|i>=T<space|1em><text|for
      warning vertices>.
    </equation>

    Here <math|\<nu\>> is the normalized Haar factor and
    <math|\<gamma\><rsub|A>> is the single centered Gaussian law of this
    local tuple; <math|E> is the warning price of <eqref|eq:warning-price>,
    at the local scale <math|y>. The same assertion holds after inserting any
    fixed nonnegative vertex inflation factors into both sides. It applies
    simultaneously to the upper and lower compiler uses.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The Gaussian precision and affine mean are formed from the funded
    quadratic forms, the path and clip, the coupling and the selected field,
    forest, tag and store. The chart and its cutoffs determine the matching
    conditions and the sockets. None of these constructions uses the additive
    remainder. Deleting that remainder therefore changes none of these
    objects, pointwise for every choice of the selected data. In particular,
    this is an equality of the Gaussian laws used by the compiler, not an
    assertion that the two full interacting densities are equal.

    The response estimate is uniform over these choices. The selected
    all-pilot package supplies the beacon, port and distant-door estimates on
    those same choices. Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:actual-palette-condition>
    therefore supplies a single palette price and a zero socket for every
    matched pilot on its toned event. The preceding pointwise identities
    transfer both quantified statements to <math|R> without a new selection
    of constants. The covariance bound and zero-socket implication now meet
    the hypotheses of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-warnings>.
    Hence simultaneous nonzero sockets at the addresses
    <math|X<around|(|S|)>> force its joint coordinate excursion event. The
    probability of that event is at most <math|E<around|(|y|)><rsup|<around|\||X<around|(|S|)>|\|>>>
    by <eqref|eq:warning-actual-price>.

    Bound the absolute integrand by the indicator of this one event times
    <math|<big|prod>c<rsub|i>> and integrate over the probability measure
    <math|\<nu\>\<otimes\>\<gamma\><rsub|A>>. This proves
    <eqref|eq:actual-corrected-warning-product>. In particular the factors
    introduced by restoring the additive remainder may be included in
    <math|S>: only their pointwise price is used, with no independence
    assertion. Multiplication by fixed nonnegative vertex inflation factors
    proves the inflated version. A warning address is counted once even if
    other vertices have overlapping support; no product of marginal warning
    probabilities is taken. Finally both compiler uses retain the same
    alphabet and Gaussian law, so the argument applies to both. The finite
    intersection and retiming argument above makes the threshold uniform for
    <math|x\<in\><around|[|s/2,s|]>>.
  </proof>

  The lemma discharges the warning transfer from the neutral pricing problem
  to the genuine corrected alphabet. It does not supply the incoming neutral
  law or the small additive remainder: these remain separate hypotheses of
  the local application.

  <subsubsection|The genuine density and its auxiliary prices>

  Apply the corrected exponent decomposition and bounded extension of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-corrected-body-compiler> with these
  actual data. Apply the mixed compiler of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-mixed-marked-compiler> using the same
  word inventories and full read supports. The resulting profile system and
  finite unmarked alphabet are chosen before an output representation
  <math|Q> with the prescribed coarse budget, and before the marked
  insertions.

  Write <math|R> for the actual incoming fine representation. Setting its
  additive remainder to zero, with the other data fixed, gives
  <math|R<rsup|\<circ\>>> for the inherited-law, warning and price estimates.
  The small-remainder estimate for <math|R> is a separate hypothesis. The
  correction construction adds this genuine remainder to the alphabet, with
  price <math|e+4*d\<leqslant\>6*\<omega\>> as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-planted-compiler>. The separate
  purchase <math|2*a<rsub|<math-up|coast>>\<leqslant\>K/4> pays the forced
  inflation against the owner decay. Thus the planted identity of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-planted-compiler> reconstructs the
  density of <math|R>, whereas the connected-support and return estimates use
  <math|R<rsup|\<circ\>>>. Both assertions concern exactly the same corrected
  alphabet, profiles, path, clip and scalar loading.

  That loading is positive: its chart scale and Gaussian prefactor are
  positive, the fine coupling is positive, and its reference kinetic Gram
  matrix has positive determinant. For this last assertion specialize the
  matrix certificate to the identity coarse field and empty store, retaining
  the reference forest and tag; the selected flat cut Gram has the same
  determinant as the reference kinetic matrix. The square-root determinant in
  the loading is therefore nonzero. It is the original reference-forest/tag
  loading, not an independently normalized version of the corrected alphabet.

  At a short interval of the selected history, the application requires its
  actual patch and funded tube to agree with the constant patch data on that
  interval. The inherited field at its upper end must supply <math|R> and its
  remainder estimate, and its neutral version must satisfy the admissible
  local conditions above. The interval length fixes the permitted remainder
  budget; the station bounds must put its coupling in
  <math|<around|[|s/2,s|]>>. When these same-course conditions are supplied,
  the construction applies to each step of that interval.

  The restart construction of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:restarted-rank-history>
  sets the new coarse coupling equal to the incoming one. Use the positive
  loading to choose its scalar vacuum normalization, and use the connected
  expansion of the same alphabet to define its masked logarithmic remainder.
  This yields the exact pushed density and preserves the original coarse path
  and clip. It does not assert equality with the old coarse additive
  remainder.

  Finally weaken the already obtained support, size and length prices to the
  two sets required for the finite-reader comparison. Such weakening only
  decreases the positive weights in the rooted sums. The construction for an
  arbitrary finite marked family is consequently available for both sets of
  prices on this same step, with the same density identity and the same
  original partition denominator. Combining these steps with the common
  reader and finite-menu choices in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-rank-purchases>
  gives its local compiler input provided the actual short course supplies
  the hypotheses used above on that same history and binding.
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:public-run-binding> identifies the selected
  family, but the local application also needs the matching interval,
  representation, tube and price certificates. In particular, the existence
  of final trial purchases does not by itself recover those certificates for
  a separate local invocation.

  Under the displayed local receipts, this application supplies the matrix,
  two-inventory, first-derivative determinant, owner and all-palette inputs
  of the corrected compiler at the specified fixed patch data. It retains the
  selected history's actual path/clip, alongside, inherited-law and remainder
  hypotheses until their history-level suppliers are applied. In particular,
  it does not assert existence of a history from local coupling inequalities
  alone.

  For the restarted comparison course, these history-level hypotheses are
  verified by Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:restart-actual-closure>,
  starting from the exact endpoint presentation of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:restart-endpoint>. Its proof supplies the
  inflated product estimates and the genuine additive costs on this alphabet;
  it does not identify them merely from equality of nominal coupling bounds.

  <subsection|The Fourier window of the actual curvature
  covariance><label|sec:actual-covariance-fourier-window>

  The Gaussian comparison requires estimates for the covariance selected by
  the construction, uniformly in its ancestry and physical period. We derive
  these estimates before considering the cumulative transport. Fix the
  positive kernel penalty and a smoothing order <math|J\<geqslant\>4>. All
  constants in this section may depend on these two choices and on the finite
  derivative order, but not on block lengths or ancestry.

  <subsubsection|A uniformly elliptic symbol>

  Let <math|H<around|(|p|)>> be the two-form pulse symbol of the chosen
  ancestry, in radian frequency <math|p\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>. The
  component and positive-row constructions give constants
  <math|\<alpha\>,\<eta\>\<gtr\>0> and <math|\<Omega\>\<less\>\<infty\>> such
  that its Laurent coefficients have strip cost <math|\<Omega\>> at width
  <math|\<alpha\>>, its action on every finite period has quadratic lower
  bound <math|\<eta\>>, and <math|H<around|(|0|)>=I>. These are properties of
  the original pulse, before any replacement of the curvature completion.
  Exponential summability implies, for every integer <math|m\<geqslant\>0>,

  <\equation*>
    sup<rsub|p><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|m>*H<around|(|p|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|m>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed each derivative multiplies the coefficient at <math|z> by at most
  <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|m>>, and <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|m>*e<rsup|-\<alpha\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>>
  is bounded. The finite-period lower bound also holds at every real
  frequency. First extend the quadratic bound of the real finite-period
  operator to complex vectors by splitting them into real and imaginary
  parts. Then test the period-<math|n> matrix on the vector
  <math|e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\>x>*v> for a grid frequency <math|p>. Its quadratic
  form and squared norm both acquire the same factor <math|n<rsup|4>>, which
  cancels. Approximate an arbitrary real frequency by grid frequencies and
  use continuity. Thus

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|Re><around|\<langle\>|H<around|(|p|)>*v,v|\<rangle\>>\<geqslant\>\<eta\><around|\||v|\|><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  Put <math|q<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>=e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>-1>, and let
  <math|X<around|(|p|)>> and <math|U<around|(|p|)>> be exterior
  multiplication by <math|q<around|(|p|)>> from one-forms to two-forms and
  from scalars to one-forms, respectively. Define

  <\equation*>
    F<around|(|p|)>=X<around|(|p|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*H<around|(|p|)>*X<around|(|p|)>+U<around|(|p|)>*U<around|(|p|)><rsup|\<ast\>>,<space|2em>P<around|(|p|)>=X<around|(|p|)>*F<around|(|p|)><rsup|-1>*X<around|(|p|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*<space|1em><around|(|p\<ne\>0|)>,
  </equation*>

  and set <math|P<around|(|0|)>=0>. On the cube
  <math|<around|\||p<rsub|a>|\|>\<less\>3*\<pi\>/2>, the elementary identity
  for exterior multiplication and contraction gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|X*v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>+<around|\<\|\|\>|U<rsup|\<ast\>>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<around|\||q|\|><rsup|2><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>,<space|2em><math-up|Re><around|\<langle\>|F*v,v|\<rangle\>>\<geqslant\>min
    <around|(|\<eta\>,1|)><around|\||q|\|><rsup|2><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|<around|\||q|\|>\<geqslant\>c<around|\||p|\|>>: the continuous
  function <math|<around|\||e<rsup|i*t>-1|\|>/<around|\||t|\|>> has a
  positive lower bound on <math|<around|[|-3*\<pi\>/2,3*\<pi\>/2|]>>, with
  its value at zero defined to be one. Consequently <math|F<around|(|p|)>> is
  invertible for <math|p\<ne\>0> in this cube.

  We record the derivative estimates with their exact gain. A function has
  order <math|s> if, through the finite number of derivatives under
  consideration, its <math|m>th derivative is bounded by
  <math|C<rsub|m><around|\||p|\|><rsup|s-m>>. Products add orders by Leibniz'
  rule. Taylor's formula gives <math|q-i*p> order two and <math|H-I> order
  one; <math|q> itself has order one. The exterior identity at <math|q=i*p>
  gives

  <\equation*>
    F<around|(|p|)>=<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>*I+E<around|(|p|)>,<space|2em>E<text|
    has order >3.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|T=<around|\||p|\|><rsup|-2>*F> has order zero, <math|T-I> order
  one, and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>> is uniformly bounded
  by coercivity. Differentiating <math|T*T<rsup|-1>=I> proves inductively
  that <math|D<rsup|m>*T<rsup|-1>=O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|-m>|)>>:
  the term containing <math|D<rsup|m>*T<rsup|-1>> is isolated and every
  remaining term is a product of derivatives whose orders total <math|m>.
  Furthermore <math|T<rsup|-1>-I=T<rsup|-1>*<around|(|I-T|)>> has order one.
  It follows that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:actual-symbol-orders>P<text| has order
    >0,<space|2em>P-P<rsub|0><text| has order
    >1,<space|1em>P<rsub|0><around|(|p|)>=X<rsub|0><around|(|p|)><around|\||p|\|><rsup|-2>*X<rsub|0><around|(|p|)><rsup|\<ast\>>,
  </equation>

  where <math|X<rsub|0>> is exterior multiplication by <math|i*p>. These
  estimates are uniform over all the selected ancestries.

  We verify that <math|P> is the actual curvature multiplier. Let <math|D> be
  the original one-form covariance and <math|R=X<rsup|\<ast\>>*H*X>. The
  transport identities already proved give
  <math|R*D*X<rsup|\<ast\>>=X<rsup|\<ast\>>> and <math|X*U=0>. Write
  <math|B=U*U<rsup|\<ast\>>>. Then <math|X*B=0>, <math|B*X<rsup|\<ast\>>=0>,
  and <math|R*B=B*R=0>. Hence <math|F=R+B> commutes with <math|B>, as does
  its inverse, and <math|X*F<rsup|-1>*B=0>. Multiplying
  <math|R*D*X<rsup|\<ast\>>=X<rsup|\<ast\>>> on the left by
  <math|X*F<rsup|-1>> yields

  <\equation*>
    X*F<rsup|-1>*X<rsup|\<ast\>>=X*F<rsup|-1>*R*D*X<rsup|\<ast\>>=X*D*X<rsup|\<ast\>>.
  </equation*>

  At zero both curvature multipliers vanish. Thus no change of the
  finite-period covariance has been made.

  <subsubsection|A smooth chart without changing the finite Fourier sum>

  Choose a smooth nondecreasing function <math|g> that is zero on
  <math|<around|(|-\<infty\>,-5*\<pi\>/4|]>> and one on
  <math|<around|[|-3*\<pi\>/4,\<infty\>|)>>, and put

  <\equation*>
    h<around|(|p|)>=<big|prod><rsub|a=1><rsup|4><around*|(|g<around|(|p<rsub|a>|)>-g*<around|(|p<rsub|a>-2*\<pi\>|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  It is supported in <math|<around|\||p<rsub|a>|\|>\<leqslant\>5*\<pi\>/4>,
  equals one near zero, and satisfies

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>h*<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*z|)>=1.
  </equation*>

  The last identity is the product of four telescoping sums; only finitely
  many terms are nonzero. Define the unit-frequency chart
  <math|K<around|(|\<xi\>|)>=h*<around|(|2*\<pi\>*\<xi\>|)>*P*<around|(|2*\<pi\>*\<xi\>|)>>,
  entry by entry and with the usual Kronecker delta on colour indices. Its
  support lies in <math|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|>\<leqslant\>2>. For an integer
  displacement <math|x>, periodicity and the partition identity show that the
  exact period-<math|n> covariance is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:actual-chart-sum>\<kappa\><rsub|n><around|(|x|)>=n<rsup|-4>*<big|sum><rsub|z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>e<rsup|2*\<pi\>*i*x\<cdot\>z/n>*K*<around|(|z/n|)>.
  </equation>

  The sum is finite. Grouping <math|z> by its residue modulo <math|n> proves
  the formula, including even <math|n> and the zero frequency. At zero we
  retain the actual value <math|P<around|(|0|)>=0>, rather than assigning a
  continuous value to a direction-dependent limit.

  For two lists of coordinate directions, of lengths <math|a,b>, let
  <math|u=a+b>. Differencing the first endpoint multiplies
  <math|K<around|(|\<xi\>|)>> by <math|e<rsup|2*\<pi\>*i*\<xi\><rsub|c>>-1>;
  differencing the second endpoint uses <math|e<rsup|-2*\<pi\>*i*\<xi\><rsub|c>>-1>.
  Denote their product by <math|m<around|(|\<xi\>|)>>, and the corresponding
  product of <math|2*\<pi\>*i*\<xi\><rsub|c>> and
  <math|-2*\<pi\>*i*\<xi\><rsub|c>> by <math|m<rsub|0><around|(|\<xi\>|)>>.
  Repeatedly using <math|e<rsup|i*t>-1-i*t=O<around|(|t<rsup|2>|)>>, with its
  differentiated Taylor bounds, shows that <math|m-m<rsub|0>> has order
  <math|u+1> and <math|m,m<rsub|0>> have order <math|u>. By
  <eqref|eq:actual-symbol-orders>, the remainder

  <\equation*>
    G<around|(|\<xi\>|)>=m<around|(|\<xi\>|)>*K<around|(|\<xi\>|)>-m<rsub|0><around|(|\<xi\>|)>*e<rsup|-<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|2><around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|2>>*P<rsub|0>*<around|(|2*\<pi\>*\<xi\>|)>
  </equation*>

  satisfies, for <math|0\<leqslant\>v\<leqslant\>u+6>,

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|v>*G<around|(|\<xi\>|)>|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C<around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|u+1-v>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|0\<less\><around|\||\<xi\>|\|>\<leqslant\>1|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:actual-mist>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|v>*G<around|(|\<xi\>|)>|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C<around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|-5>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|>\<geqslant\>1|)>.>>>>
  </align>

  For the first estimate, subtract and add <math|m<rsub|0>*K> and use the
  product rule. The extra Gaussian differs from one to order two. Outside the
  unit ball the compactly supported term has bounded derivatives, and every
  derivative of the Gaussian term decays faster than any power. Finite maxima
  give the same <math|C> for all face pairs and direction lists of bounded
  length.

  <subsubsection|Fourier decay and quadrature at the pole>

  Write <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|+>*f<around|(|x|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|2*\<pi\>*i*x\<cdot\>\<xi\>>*f<around|(|\<xi\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<xi\>>.
  We first show directly that <eqref|eq:actual-mist> implies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:actual-mist-decay><around|\||\<cal-F\><rsub|+>*G<around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rprime|'><around|\||x|\|><rsup|-u-5>*<space|1em><around|(|<around|\||x|\|>\<geqslant\>1|)>.
  </equation>

  Set <math|r=<around|\||x|\|><rsup|-1>> and choose a smooth radial cutoff
  equal to zero on the ball of radius <math|r/2> and one outside the ball of
  radius <math|r>. The omitted inner term has integral at most
  <math|C*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|r>t<rsup|u+1>*t<rsup|3>*<space|0.17em>d*t\<leqslant\>C<rprime|'>*r<rsup|u+5>>.
  For the outer term integrate by parts <math|u+6> times in the unit
  direction <math|x/<around|\||x|\|>>. Each term where a derivative hits the
  cutoff is supported on <math|r/2\<leqslant\><around|\||\<xi\>|\|>\<leqslant\>r>
  and has integral <math|O<around|(|C*r<rsup|-1>|)>>. For the remaining
  terms, derivatives of order <math|u+6> have integral bounded by

  <\equation*>
    C<rprime|'>*<around*|(|<big|int><rsub|r><rsup|1>t<rsup|-5>*t<rsup|3>*<space|0.17em>d*t+<big|int><rsub|1><rsup|\<infty\>>t<rsup|-5>*t<rsup|3>*<space|0.17em>d*t|)>\<leqslant\>C<rprime|''>/r.
  </equation*>

  Boundary terms vanish by first inserting a distant smooth cutoff and then
  sending its radius to infinity; the displayed bounds control its
  derivatives as well. Multiplication by <math|<around|\||x|\|><rsup|-u-6>>
  proves <eqref|eq:actual-mist-decay>.

  Next let <math|f=m*K>. It is compactly supported, bounded by <math|C>, and
  satisfies <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D*f<around|(|\<xi\>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C/<around|\||\<xi\>|\|>>
  off zero. Partition frequency space into cubes of side <math|1/n>, and use
  their grid corner as the sample point. Its distance from any point of that
  cube is at most <math|2/n>. If this distance is less than
  <math|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|>/2>, the mean value bound gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||f<around|(|\<xi\>|)>-f*<around|(|z/n|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rprime|'>/<around|(|n<around|\||\<xi\>|\|>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Otherwise the same estimate follows from the bound <math|2*C> on the
  difference, after increasing <math|C<rprime|'>>. This also covers a sampled
  corner at zero. The phase difference is at most
  <math|2*\<pi\><around|\||x|\|>*<space|0.17em><around|\||\<xi\>-z/n|\|>>.
  Integration over a fixed enlarged support ball is legitimate because
  <math|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|-1>> is locally integrable in four
  dimensions. We obtain

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:actual-quadrature><around*|\||\<cal-F\><rsub|+>*f<around|(|x|)>-n<rsup|-4>*<big|sum><rsub|z>e<rsup|2*\<pi\>*i*x\<cdot\>z/n>*f*<around|(|z/n|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rprime|'>*<frac|1+<around|\||x|\|>|n>.
  </equation>

  This estimate treats the singular grid point without removing it.

  Finally the Gaussian-regularized continuum term has the required Maxwell
  limit. In the present Fourier convention its scalar potential multiplier,
  for <math|t\<gtr\>0> (here <math|t=<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|2>>), is
  <math|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-2>*e<rsup|-t<around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|2>>/<around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|2>>.
  The identity

  <\equation*>
    <frac|e<rsup|-t<around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|2>>|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|2>>=<big|int><rsub|t><rsup|\<infty\>>e<rsup|-s<around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|2>>*<space|0.17em>d*s
  </equation*>

  and the product Gaussian integral give, at <math|x\<ne\>0>,

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-F\><rsub|+><around*|(|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-2><frac|e<rsup|-t<around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|2>>|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|2>>|)><around|(|x|)>=<frac|1|4*\<pi\><rsup|2><around|\||x|\|><rsup|2>>*<around*|(|1-e<rsup|-\<pi\><rsup|2><around|\||x|\|><rsup|2>/t>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Fubini is justified by <math|<big|int><rsub|t><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|\<pi\>/s|)><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d*s\<less\>\<infty\>>.
  All polynomial frequency moments are integrable, so differentiation under
  the integral supplies both curls and both endpoint derivative lists, with
  the opposite sign at the second endpoint. The difference from the
  derivatives of <math|1/<around|(|4*\<pi\><rsup|2><around|\||x|\|><rsup|2>|)>>
  is a derivative of a Gaussian times that potential. It is bounded by any
  prescribed inverse power for <math|<around|\||x|\|>\<geqslant\>1>.

  Combining this fact with <eqref|eq:actual-chart-sum>,
  <eqref|eq:actual-mist-decay> and <eqref|eq:actual-quadrature> proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:actual-native-model><around*|\||\<Delta\><rsub|x><rsup|a>*\<Delta\><rsub|y><rsup|b>*\<kappa\><rsub|n>*<around|(|x,y|)>-\<cal-K\><rsub|a,b>*<around|(|x-y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|a,b>*<around*|(|<around|\||x-y|\|><rsup|-a-b-5>+<frac|1+<around|\||x-y|\|>|n>|)>,<space|2em><around|\||x-y|\|>\<geqslant\>1.
  </equation>

  Here each superscript denotes its specified direction list, and
  <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|a,b>> is the corresponding continuum Maxwell curvature
  covariance. The constants are uniform in ancestry and period. The
  covariance has closed rows and columns because its construction is
  <math|d*D*d<rsup|\<ast\>>> and <math|d<rsup|2>=0>. Its entries are real by
  conjugating the finite Fourier sum and replacing the frequency by its
  negative. No positivity of a later normal-ordered covariance is inferred.

  For a fixed comparison order <math|d\<geqslant\>1>, only
  <math|a,b\<leqslant\>d-1> are needed. The zero-order comparison can use
  <math|d=1> and discard the extra data. The construction reserves their
  finitely many chart constants at smoothing order <math|J> before choosing
  the block length. At the chosen comparison level the reserved chart cost is
  paid by <math|H<rsup|1/4>>, where <math|H> is its monitor. The finite
  constants from the multiplier and Fourier estimates are then absorbed
  successively into <math|H<rsup|1/2>> and <math|H>. This yields
  <eqref|eq:actual-native-model> with <math|C<rsub|a,b>> replaced by
  <math|H>, on that same chosen history. The quantitative clearance at the
  chosen level remains part of the common scale selection; merely taking an
  unrelated level large would not establish it.

  <subsection|Cumulative transport and the actual Gaussian
  comparison><label|sec:actual-covariance-transport>

  We prove the low-frequency transport bounds on the same atlas as
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-covariance-fourier-window>. Write
  <math|\<kappa\><rsub|i>> for its original real curvature covariance and
  <math|\<Psi\><rsub|i>> for the installed map from level <math|i> to level
  <math|i+1>, with the identity on colour coordinates. Installation sums all
  periodic images of the cover row. Fix a comparison order
  <math|d\<geqslant\>1> and a plateau of smoothing order <math|J> with
  <math|d-1\<leqslant\>J-2>.

  <subsubsection|Moving differences through an actual step>

  For a word <math|w> of <math|j\<leqslant\>d-1> coordinate directions, the
  selected transport has a real quotient row <math|Q<rsub|w>> satisfying

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:actual-transport-difference>\<Delta\><rsub|w>*\<Psi\><rsub|i>=Q<rsub|w>*\<Delta\><rsub|w>,<space|2em>sup<rsub|x>
    <big|sum><rsub|y><around|\||Q<rsub|w>*<around|(|x,y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>R*L<rsub|i><rsup|-j-2>,
  </equation>

  where <math|L<rsub|i>=a<rsub|i>/a<rsub|i+1>> and <math|R\<geqslant\>1>
  depends only on the kernel penalty and adjacent smoothing orders. The
  quotient needs proof; a bound on the left side alone would not suffice.

  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-wire-tower-strip>, applied to the
  actual pulse-multiplied curvature column, gives its differentiated row
  strip cost <math|C*L<rsub|i><rsup|-j-2>>. The original pulse's strip cost
  simply multiplies the established column cost. To move coarse differences
  to the right, divide the symbol successively by
  <math|e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>-1>. The division is regular on the real principal
  cube before summing aliases. On the principal alias use

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>/L<rsub|i>>-1=<frac|e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>-1|L<rsub|i><math-up|rail><rsub|L<rsub|i>><around|(|p<rsub|a>/L<rsub|i>|)>>.
  </equation*>

  The denominator has a positive lower bound by the principal rail estimate.
  On a nonprincipal alias <math|q=<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*l|)>/L<rsub|i>>, the
  column contains the box filter <math|A<around|(|q|)><rsup|J>>. The
  geometric-series identity is

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|e<rsup|i*q<rsub|a>>-1|)>*A<around|(|q|)>=<around|(|e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>-1|)>*s<rsub|a><around|(|q|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|s<rsub|a>> is a product of the other coordinate box filters and
  a phase, divided by <math|L<rsub|i>>. For <math|j> directions use <math|j>
  box factors. The remaining power <math|A<rsup|J-j>> has nonnegative degree.
  The remaining multiplier is continuous by the nonprincipal denominator
  bounds already proved. Thus the full differentiated symbol has a continuous
  quotient by the product of all coarse differences.

  A coefficientwise division also preserves a narrower strip bound. In one
  coordinate use the exact finite identity

  <\equation*>
    <frac|e<rsup|i*n*p<rsub|a>>-1|e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>-1>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|h=0><rsup|n-1>e<rsup|i*h*p<rsub|a>>,>|<cell|n\<gtr\>0,>>|<row|<cell|-<big|sum><rsub|h=n><rsup|-1>e<rsup|i*h*p<rsub|a>>,>|<cell|n\<less\>0,>>|<row|<cell|0,>|<cell|n=0.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  There are <math|<around|\||n|\|>> terms with no greater displacement.
  Reducing width <math|\<sigma\>> to <math|\<sigma\>/2> costs at most
  <math|2/\<sigma\>>, since <math|<around|\||n|\|>*e<rsup|\<sigma\><around|\||n|\|>/2>\<leqslant\><around|(|2/\<sigma\>|)>*e<rsup|\<sigma\><around|\||n|\|>>>.
  This divides the symbol minus its value on <math|p<rsub|a>=0>; that value
  vanishes by the preceding divisibility. Equality off the coordinate
  hyperplane extends to it by continuity. Repeat along <math|w>; divisibility
  by the remaining factors survives at each step. There are finitely many
  words of length at most <math|d-1>, so one narrower strip and one cost work
  for all. The scalar factor <math|L<rsub|i><rsup|-j-2>> is unchanged.
  Periodization and the triangle inequality turn this cover bound into an
  absolute row bound; the sum over the six two-form types contributes their
  cardinality. This proves <eqref|eq:actual-transport-difference> with a cost
  chosen before <math|L<rsub|i>>.

  Closedness is preserved on all cochains, including harmonic ones. Write the
  actual step as <math|B<rsub|f>*T*H<rsub|c>>, using reciprocal pulse
  <math|B> and pulse <math|H>. Its exterior transport <math|C> satisfies

  <\equation*>
    C*d<rsub|c>*B<rsub|c>=d<rsub|f>*B<rsub|f>*T,<space|2em>B<rsub|c>*H<rsub|c>=I.
  </equation*>

  Multiplying by <math|H<rsub|c>> gives <math|d<rsub|f>*\<Psi\><rsub|i>=C*d<rsub|c>>.
  No global exactness of the input cochain is required. Reality follows from
  the real cover coefficients.

  <subsubsection|Absolute rows of a product>

  For <math|\<ell\>\<leqslant\>k> put <math|m=k-\<ell\>>,
  <math|\<cal-L\>=\<Psi\><rsub|k-1>*\<cdots\>*\<Psi\><rsub|\<ell\>>> and
  <math|S=a<rsub|\<ell\>>/a<rsub|k>=<big|prod><rsub|i=\<ell\>><rsup|k-1>L<rsub|i>>.
  If every <math|j>th difference of <math|f> is bounded by <math|v>,
  iteration gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|\<Delta\><rsub|w>*\<cal-L\>*f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>R<rsup|m>*S<rsup|-j-2>*v.
  </equation*>

  This holds for arbitrary real <math|f>. For a specified output row, take
  <math|f<around|(|y|)>> to be the sign of
  <math|\<Delta\><rsub|w>*\<cal-L\>*<around|(|x,y|)>>, using zero when that
  entry vanishes. Then <math|<around|\||f|\|>\<leqslant\>1> and every
  <math|j>th difference has absolute value at most <math|2<rsup|j>>.
  Consequently

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:actual-cumulative-row>S<rsup|j+2>*<big|sum><rsub|y><around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|w>*\<cal-L\>*<around|(|x,y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>R<rsup|m>*2<rsup|d>.
  </equation>

  The sign test need not be closed. Reality and preservation of closedness
  pass separately to the product.

  Fix <math|b\<geqslant\>6> and <math|a\<gtr\>0>. After <math|R>, choose a
  plateau block length <math|L\<gtr\>1> so that <math|4*<around|(|b+2|)>*log
  R\<leqslant\>a*log L>. With <math|m=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|b+2|)>*log
  H/log L|\<rceil\>>> we have

  <\equation*>
    R<rsup|m>\<leqslant\>R*H<rsup|a/4>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|a/2>,<space|2em>R<rsup|m>*2<rsup|d>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|a>
  </equation*>

  eventually. The second inequality absorbs fixed <math|2<rsup|d>> into
  <math|H<rsup|a/2>>. Also <math|S=L<rsup|m>\<geqslant\>H<rsup|b+2>>. The
  original covariance entries have a fixed bound <math|C<rsub|J>> uniform in
  period, by summing the bounded curvature multiplier over its normalized
  finite Fourier grid. Paying <math|C<rsub|J>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|a>>, and
  writing <math|P=H<rsup|a>>, gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:actual-low-data>S<rsup|j+2>*<big|sum><rsub|y><around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|w>*\<cal-L\>*<around|(|x,y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>P,<space|2em><around|\||\<kappa\><rsub|\<ell\>>*<around|(|y,z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>P.
  </equation>

  One finite table of costs is selected before the block length, then its
  clearance threshold. On the allowed plateau the prescribed number of steps
  is exactly <math|m>. All estimates therefore concern the same chosen level
  and history, uniformly in period.

  <subsubsection|Mean and covariance after normal ordering>

  For a real closed coarse cochain <math|u> with
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>P>, set
  <math|\<mu\>=\<cal-L\>*u> and <math|B=\<cal-L\>*\<kappa\><rsub|\<ell\>>*\<cal-L\><rsup|<math-ss|T>>>.
  Finite summation gives

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|S<rsup|j+2>*<around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|w>*\<mu\><around|(|x|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>P<rsup|2>,<eq-number><label|eq:actual-low-moments>>>|<row|<cell|S<rsup|j+2>*S<rsup|h+2>*<around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|w><rsup|x>*\<Delta\><rsub|v><rsup|y>*B*<around|(|x,y|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>P<rsup|3>.>>>>
  </align>

  For the second inequality bound each covariance entry by <math|P>; the
  remaining double sum factors into the absolute row sums of
  <eqref|eq:actual-low-data>. The mean and both covariance arguments are
  closed, by the original closedness and its preservation.

  Use <math|r=H<rsup|b>>, <math|a=\<lambda\>/4>,
  <math|0\<less\>\<lambda\>\<leqslant\>1/4>, and the purchased condition
  <math|S\<geqslant\>r*H<rsup|2>>. For <math|j\<geqslant\>0>,
  <math|r<rsup|j+2>*H<rsup|4>\<leqslant\>S<rsup|j+2>>. Hence the weighted
  means are at most <math|H<rsup|-4>*P<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-3>>.
  Retaining only one factor <math|H<rsup|-4>> for the covariance gives the
  convenient bound

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:actual-low-error>r<rsup|j+h+4>*<around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|w><rsup|x>*\<Delta\><rsub|v><rsup|y>*B*<around|(|x,y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-4+3*\<lambda\>/4>.
  </equation>

  Fix distinct continuum positions <math|x<rsub|i>> and their selected
  rounded lattice centres <math|z<rsub|i>> at magnification <math|r>.
  Required offsets have subpower size in <math|H>, so normalized pair
  displacements stay in compact sets away from zero. The free covariance and
  the finitely many derivatives used here are bounded on these sets. For an
  off-diagonal pair, multiply <eqref|eq:actual-native-model> by
  <math|r<rsup|j+h+4>>. The singular error is at most
  <math|C*H/r=C*H<rsup|1-b>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-4>/2> eventually, since
  <math|b\<geqslant\>6>. The period error is bounded by a fixed power of
  <math|H> divided by the lattice side. The selected mesh estimates make it
  at most <math|H<rsup|-4>/2> eventually, uniformly in physical period. Thus
  the scaled error is at most <math|H<rsup|-4>>, and the scaled covariance is
  bounded by <math|H<rsup|\<lambda\>/8>> eventually.

  Let <math|K<rsup|0><rsub|i*j>> be the polynomial test-cochain covariance
  built from the free jet covariance at <math|z<rsub|i>/r>. For both
  derivative orders <math|j,h\<leqslant\>d-2>, at the origins its weighted
  differences differ from the corresponding free curvature derivatives by at
  most <math|H<rsup|-4>>. Indeed a difference of order <math|j> kills test
  cochains of degree less than <math|j>, and the degree-<math|j> term
  recovers its homogeneous curvature tensor exactly. Terms of degree
  <math|q\<gtr\>j> carry <math|r<rsup|j-q>\<leqslant\>r<rsup|-1>>. Expanding
  the finite covariance matrix in the jet basis bounds the total error by
  <math|C/r\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-4>> eventually. The coefficient constants are
  fixed and the free entries are bounded on the compact configuration set. If
  one endpoint has order <math|d-1>, its derivative of
  <math|K<rsup|0><rsub|i*j>> is zero, since all test cochains have degree at
  most <math|d-2>. Set <math|K<rsup|0><rsub|i*i>=0>, in accordance with free
  normal ordering.

  The actual normal-ordered covariance is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:actual-normalordered-covariance>\<Gamma\><rsub|i*j>*<around|(|s,t|)>=\<b-1\><rsub|i\<ne\>j>*\<kappa\><rsub|k>*<around|(|z<rsub|i>+s,z<rsub|j>+t|)>-B*<around|(|z<rsub|i>+s,z<rsub|j>+t|)>.
  </equation>

  Its diagonal is <math|-B>, generally nonzero. At origins with both
  <math|j,h\<leqslant\>d-2>, the two comparison errors and
  <eqref|eq:actual-low-error> give

  <\equation*>
    r<rsup|j+h+4>*<around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|w><rsup|s>*\<Delta\><rsub|v><rsup|t>*<around|(|\<Gamma\><rsub|i*j>-K<rsup|0><rsub|i*j>|)>*<around|(|0,0|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*H<rsup|-4>+H<rsup|-4+3*\<lambda\>/4>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-3>
  </equation*>

  eventually; only the last term is needed on the diagonal. For <math|d=1>
  this range of origin derivatives is empty. At a top-order endpoint the
  polynomial comparison vanishes and

  <\equation*>
    H<rsup|\<lambda\>/8>+H<rsup|-4+3*\<lambda\>/4>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<lambda\>/4>=P.
  </equation*>

  These, with the mean and closedness bounds, are precisely the
  finite-reading estimates with <math|P=H<rsup|\<lambda\>/4>> and
  <math|E=H<rsup|-3>> used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-coordinate-calibration>.
  They hold uniformly over the admitted closed real coarse backgrounds.
  Positive semidefiniteness of <math|\<Gamma\>> is not assumed.

  Application must retain the purchased plateau and clearance conditions and
  the identification of this atlas with the actual reader law. These belong
  to the common history and reader construction; an arbitrary symmetric model
  cannot replace that identification.

  <subsection|One coordinate system for the finite Gaussian
  comparison><label|sec:allarity-coordinate-calibration>

  The moment estimate in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-cumulant-comparison>
  requires a common space containing both the physical jet coordinates and
  the discarded higher-order information. We construct that space from finite
  lattice readings. The covariance may be indefinite after normal ordering;
  only its symmetry is used in this construction.

  <subsubsection|Recovering polynomial curvature coordinates from
  differences>

  Write <math|\<Delta\><rsub|a>*f<around|(|x|)>=f*<around|(|x+e<rsub|a>|)>-f<around|(|x|)>>
  on <math|\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>. A real curvature cochain is specified on
  positively oriented plaquettes and colour coordinates and extended
  antisymmetrically to all ordered pairs of axes. We call it closed when

  <\equation*>
    \<Delta\><rsub|a>*f<rsub|b*c>+\<Delta\><rsub|b>*f<rsub|c*a>+\<Delta\><rsub|c>*f<rsub|a*b>=0.
  </equation*>

  For an integer <math|d\<geqslant\>0>, let <math|K<rsub|d>> index a basis of
  the radial polynomial connection jets whose curvature degree <math|j>
  satisfies <math|j+2\<leqslant\>d>. Its basis element has grade
  <math|g<rsub|\<alpha\>>=j+2>. Let <math|t<rsub|\<alpha\>>> be its lattice
  plaquette cochain, obtained by integrating the polynomial connection along
  each edge and taking its discrete curl. These are the polynomial test
  cochains of the local section construction. They are closed. Differences of
  order greater than <math|j> vanish, while order-<math|j> differences at
  zero are the components of <math|j>! times the homogeneous curvature
  tensor. Edge integration does not change this highest homogeneous term.

  For clarity, the finite sampling argument needs only closedness, not the
  divergence equation. Given a closed cochain, its order-<math|j> differences
  at zero form a symmetric derivative tensor. Applying the closedness
  equation after any <math|j-1> differences gives the cyclic identity for
  this tensor. It therefore defines a homogeneous closed polynomial two-form.
  For <math|j=0> this assertion just says that the tensor is antisymmetric.
  The radial primitive is obtained by contracting this two-form with the
  position vector and dividing by <math|j+2>, as in the local jet
  construction. Its curl is the given two-form. Thus these highest-order
  readings are those of a radial polynomial connection jet.

  The map from the span of the <math|t<rsub|\<alpha\>>> of curvature degree
  at least <math|q> to all origin differences of orders
  <math|q,\<ldots\>,d-2> is injective. Indeed, take the largest degree with a
  nonzero coefficient. The differences of that order kill all smaller degrees
  and recover its full curvature tensor. Vanishing of those readings forces
  that radial connection jet, hence its basis coefficients, to be zero.
  Descend through the finite list of degrees. The same descent, now
  subtracting the recovered top-degree test cochain, shows that the origin
  readings through order <math|d-2> of every closed cochain are matched by a
  unique linear combination of the <math|t<rsub|\<alpha\>>>.

  Choose a linear left inverse of each of these finite injective sampling
  maps. For a key <math|\<alpha\>> of curvature degree <math|j>, use the left
  inverse involving only differences of orders at least <math|j>. This gives
  a real functional <math|\<ell\><rsub|\<alpha\>>> which is a finite linear
  combination of origin differences, with

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:allarity-dual-cochain>\<ell\><rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|t<rsub|\<beta\>>|)>=\<delta\><rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>>,<space|2em>\<Pi\><rsub|d>*f=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>\<in\>K<rsub|d>>\<ell\><rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|f|)>*t<rsub|\<alpha\>>.
  </equation>

  For closed <math|f>, <math|\<Pi\><rsub|d>*f> has exactly its origin
  differences through order <math|d-2>. Compatibility of the different left
  inverses follows from uniqueness of the full expansion just proved:
  restriction to orders at least <math|j> deletes all lower-degree test
  cochains, and the left inverse therefore recovers the same remaining
  coefficients. The functionals are defined on arbitrary cochains; the
  matching assertion is only used for closed ones.

  Let <math|c<rsub|\<alpha\>>> be the sum of the absolute values of the
  coefficients of <math|\<ell\><rsub|\<alpha\>>> in its difference
  representation, and choose <math|C<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>1> with
  <math|c<rsub|\<alpha\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|d>> for every key. This is a
  finite constant, selected before any comparison scale or stencil radius.

  <subsubsection|Residual coordinates and their bounds>

  Fix a finite set <math|S> of addresses, all with lattice distance
  <math|<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>M>, where <math|M> is a
  nonnegative integer, and a radius <math|r\<geqslant\>1>. For a cochain
  <math|f> define

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|L<rsub|\<alpha\>>*f>|<cell|=r<rsup|g<rsub|\<alpha\>>>*\<ell\><rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|f|)>,<space|2em>\<alpha\>\<in\>K<rsub|d>,>>|<row|<cell|L<rsub|z>*f>|<cell|=r<rsup|d+1>*<around|(|f-\<Pi\><rsub|d>*f|)><around|(|z|)>,<space|2em>z\<in\>S.>>>>
  </align*>

  The coordinate set is the disjoint union <math|K<rsub|d>\<sqcup\>S>. Every
  functional <math|L> uses only finitely many values, since a finite
  difference does so. For every <math|z\<in\>S> there is the exact identity

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:allarity-coordinate-reconstruction>f<around|(|z|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>\<in\>K<rsub|d>>r<rsup|-g<rsub|\<alpha\>>>*t<rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|z|)>*L<rsub|\<alpha\>>*f+r<rsup|-<around|(|d+1|)>>*L<rsub|z>*f.
  </equation>

  It is simply the definition of <math|f-\<Pi\><rsub|d>*f> and holds even
  when <math|f> is not closed.

  Put <math|q=max <around|(|d-1,0|)>>. Suppose a closed <math|f> has the
  weighted origin bounds

  <\equation*>
    r<rsup|j+2>*<around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|w>*f<around|(|0|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>P*<space|1em><around|(|j=<around|\||w|\|>\<leqslant\>d-2|)>
  </equation*>

  and the weighted top-difference bounds

  <\equation*>
    r<rsup|q+2>*<around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|w>*f<around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>P*<space|1em><around|(|<around|\||w|\|>=q,<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>M|)>.
  </equation*>

  All plaquette and colour components are included. Assume
  <math|P\<geqslant\>0>. For physical coordinates,
  <math|g<rsub|\<alpha\>>\<leqslant\>j+2> for every difference entering
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|\<alpha\>>>, so <math|<around|\||L<rsub|\<alpha\>>*f|\|>\<leqslant\>P*c<rsub|\<alpha\>>>.

  For the residual bound set <math|h=f-\<Pi\><rsub|d>*f>. Its origin
  differences below order <math|q> vanish, and its order-<math|q> differences
  equal those of <math|f>, since <math|\<Pi\><rsub|d>*f> has degree at most
  <math|d-2>. If <math|d=0> or <math|1>, the key set is empty and the last
  assertion just means <math|h=f>. An elementary discrete remainder estimate
  gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||h<around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M<rsup|q>*sup<rsub|<around|\||w|\|>=q,<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>M><around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|w>*h<around|(|x|)>|\|>.
  </equation*>

  To prove it, descend from order <math|q> to zero. At each lower order the
  relevant difference is zero at the origin. Sum its first differences along
  a shortest signed coordinate path to <math|z>. The path has at most
  <math|M> edges and stays inside the ball. A negative step uses the forward
  difference at the new endpoint. Each descent therefore multiplies the bound
  by at most <math|M>. At <math|q=0> no descent is required, with
  <math|M<rsup|0>=1>. Since <math|d+1\<leqslant\>q+2> and
  <math|r\<geqslant\>1>, we obtain

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:allarity-coordinate-bounds><around|\||L<rsub|\<alpha\>>*f|\|>\<leqslant\>P*C<rsub|d>,<space|2em><around|\||L<rsub|z>*f|\|>\<leqslant\>P*M<rsup|q>.
  </equation>

  If the weighted origin differences instead have bound
  <math|E\<geqslant\>0>, the physical coordinates satisfy the sharper bound
  <math|E*C<rsub|d>>.

  Apply the same argument at both ends of a kernel <math|K*<around|(|z,w|)>>
  whose rows and columns are closed. If its mixed weighted readings of the
  preceding types are bounded by <math|P>, and the origin\Uorigin ones by
  <math|E>, then, for <math|T\<geqslant\>max
  <around|(|C<rsub|d>,M<rsup|q>|)>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:allarity-two-reader-bounds><around|\||<around|(|L<rsub|a>\<otimes\>L<rsub|b>|)>*K|\|>\<leqslant\>P*T<rsup|2>,<space|2em><around|\||<around|(|L<rsub|\<alpha\>>\<otimes\>L<rsub|\<beta\>>|)>*K|\|>\<leqslant\>E*T<rsup|2>.
  </equation>

  Indeed fix one difference at the first endpoint, divide its bound by its
  positive weight, and apply <eqref|eq:allarity-coordinate-bounds> at the
  second endpoint. Apply it again at the first. The endpoint operations
  commute because both are finite linear combinations of evaluations.
  Closedness is preserved under these finite linear combinations. This
  justifies both applications, including residual coordinates.

  <subsubsection|The exact free block and the common covariance>

  Fix a separated configuration of <math|n> insertion points. Let
  <math|F<rsub|i*\<alpha\>,j*\<beta\>>> be its free covariance in the radial
  jet coordinates. This is a real symmetric matrix: the underlying free
  kernel and the change to these coordinates are real. Normal ordering sets
  its same-insertion block to zero. Define the reference cochain kernel

  <\equation*>
    K<rsup|0><rsub|i*j>*<around|(|z,w|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>,\<beta\>\<in\>K<rsub|d>>F<rsub|i*\<alpha\>,j*\<beta\>>*<space|0.17em>r<rsup|-g<rsub|\<alpha\>>>*t<rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|z|)>*<space|0.17em>r<rsup|-g<rsub|\<beta\>>>*t<rsub|\<beta\>><around|(|w|)>.
  </equation*>

  Its rows and columns are closed. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:allarity-dual-cochain>
  shows that its matrix under the <math|L> coordinates is exactly <math|F> on
  the physical block and zero whenever either coordinate is residual. This is
  an exact identity, not a small-error statement.

  Let <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|i*j>> be the actual symmetric cochain kernel, with
  closed rows and columns, and <math|\<mu\><rsub|i>> its closed actual mean.
  Suppose the means obey the two bounds of the previous subsection with
  constants <math|P,E>, and <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|i*j>-K<rsup|0><rsub|i*j>>
  obeys their two-endpoint versions. Define one enlarged mean and covariance
  by

  <\equation*>
    m<rsub|i*a>=L<rsub|a>*\<mu\><rsub|i>,<space|2em>Q<rsub|i*a,j*b>=<around|(|L<rsub|a>\<otimes\>L<rsub|b>|)>*\<Gamma\><rsub|i*j>.
  </equation*>

  If <math|T\<geqslant\>max <around|(|C<rsub|d>,M<rsup|q>|)>>, their entries
  satisfy

  <\gather*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\||m<rsub|i*a>|\|>\<leqslant\>P*T,<space|2em><around|\||m<rsub|i*\<alpha\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>E*T,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||Q<rsub|i*a,j*b>-F<rsup|<math-up|ext>><rsub|i*a,j*b>|\|>\<leqslant\>P*T<rsup|2>,<space|2em><around|\||Q<rsub|i*\<alpha\>,j*\<beta\>>-F<rsub|i*\<alpha\>,j*\<beta\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>E*T<rsup|2>,>>>>
  </gather*>

  where <math|F<rsup|<math-up|ext>>> has zero residual rows and columns.
  Consequently, if

  <\gather>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|B\<geqslant\>1,<space|1em>P*T\<leqslant\>B,<space|1em>P*T<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>B,<space|1em><around|\||F<rsub|i*\<alpha\>,j*\<beta\>>|\|>+P*T<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>B,<eq-number><label|eq:allarity-meridian-purchases>>>|<row|<cell|E*T\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>,<space|2em>E*T<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>,<eq-number>>>>>
  </gather>

  all enlarged entries are bounded by <math|B>, while the physical block and
  mean are within <math|\<varepsilon\>> of <math|<around|(|F,0|)>>. Applying
  <eqref|eq:allarity-coordinate-reconstruction> at one and at both endpoints
  proves that the actual stencil mean and covariance are the linear and
  bilinear pullbacks of <math|<around|(|m,Q|)>>. This gives the common
  pullback required for every slot and every subset in the moment comparison.

  Here are the purchases used there. Let <math|H\<to\>\<infty\>> be its
  monitor, <math|0\<less\>\<lambda\>\<leqslant\>1/4>, and set

  <\equation*>
    T=P=H<rsup|\<lambda\>/4>,<space|1em>E=H<rsup|-3>,<space|1em>B=H<rsup|\<lambda\>>,<space|1em>\<varepsilon\>=H<rsup|-2>.
  </equation*>

  The stencil radius has subpower growth, so <math|M<rsup|q>\<leqslant\>T>
  eventually; also <math|C<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>T>. Then
  <math|P*T<rsup|2>=H<rsup|3*\<lambda\>/4>\<leqslant\>B/2> eventually, while
  <math|P*T\<leqslant\>P*T<rsup|2>> since <math|T\<geqslant\>1>. The
  exponents <math|-3+\<lambda\>/4> and <math|-3+\<lambda\>/2> are at most
  <math|-2>, which proves the two error inequalities. On a compact separated
  configuration set the finitely many free entries are bounded by continuity
  of the differentiated kernel; the same bound holds for the rounded
  configurations eventually. Thus <math|<around|\||F|\|>\<leqslant\>B/2>
  eventually and all of <eqref|eq:allarity-meridian-purchases> holds.

  <subsubsection|The calibrated polynomial price>

  The actual polynomial map on a fixed grade space has a coefficient bound of
  the form

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<cal-H\>*u|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>H<space|0.17em><around|\||<math-up|awake><around|(|u|)>|\|><rsub|1>,<space|2em><around|\||<math-up|awake><around|(|u|)>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*<around|\||T*u|\|><rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|T> is its tested coefficient map, and <math|<math-up|awake>> is
  the coefficient vector in the retained common normalization. For a target
  label <math|v> of grade at most <math|d>, the calibration equation is

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|a>*T*u=<math-up|weight><rsub|r><space|0.17em>v,<space|2em>a\<gtr\>0,<space|1em>a<rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>2*H,<space|1em>r=H<rsup|b>\<geqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  <math|D<rsub|a>> multiplies a basis coordinate by <math|a> to its
  connection degree; <math|<math-up|weight><rsub|r>> multiplies it by
  <math|r> to its grade. Let <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>> be the maximum connection
  degree in the fixed frame, taking <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>=0> if that frame is
  empty. Coordinatewise inversion gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||T*u|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|(|2*H|)><rsup|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>*r<rsup|d><around|\||v|\|><rsub|1>,<space|2em><around|\||\<cal-H\>*u|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|q<rsub|\<ast\>>+1><around|\||v|\|><rsub|1>*H<rsup|b*d+q<rsub|\<ast\>>+1>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|b*d+q<rsub|\<ast\>>+2>
  </equation*>

  eventually. The last threshold depends on the fixed target label, and can
  be taken uniformly on a fixed bounded set of labels. It is uniform in the
  cutoff and period when the displayed input bounds are uniform. The finite
  loss <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>+2> is chosen before the amplification exponent
  <math|b>.

  Finally the weighted norm of the reconstruction coordinate at
  <math|z\<in\>S> is exactly

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>\<in\>K<rsub|d>><around|\||t<rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|z|)>|\|>+1.
  </equation*>

  The powers <math|r<rsup|-g<rsub|\<alpha\>>>> and
  <math|r<rsup|-<around|(|d+1|)>>> cancel their variable weights. Each
  <math|t<rsub|\<alpha\>>> is a polynomial of fixed degree at most
  <math|d-2>, so this expression is at most a fixed multiple of
  <math|<around|(|1+M|)><rsup|d>+1>. It has subpower growth in <math|H>.
  Hence it is eventually at most <math|H<rsup|\<lambda\>>>. If the exact
  calibrated low-grade identity and the fixed polynomial degree bound
  <math|k> hold, the weighted tail estimate of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-cumulant-comparison> applies with
  these very coordinates and this coefficient price. Choosing
  <math|b\<geqslant\><around|(|q<rsub|\<ast\>>+2|)>+3> leaves its required
  factor <math|H<rsup|-2>>.

  The construction above proves the coordinate and price conversion. Its
  application still requires the actual closed-kernel and mean difference
  estimates on the selected atlas, and the exact low-grade identity for its
  polynomial map. Those data must refer to the same readers as the joint
  Gaussian model. The coordinate conversion does not obtain them from
  covariance symmetry alone.

  <subsection|One joint Gaussian model for the whole reader
  family><label|sec:ex-lattice-all-arity-reader-gaussian-model>

  The leading Gaussian construction assigns a local reference model to an
  entire finite family of readers at once. It is important that this is one
  joint law: replacing it by a separate Gaussian sample for each reader would
  lose the cross-covariances used in the principal comparison. The model is
  an auxiliary leading law for a fixed block, pose, forest, and tag. It is
  not a claim that the complete interacting block measure is Gaussian.

  <subsubsection|Fixed data and the common sample space>

  Fix adjacent coarse and fine levels, a block and its point set, a coarse
  configuration <math|V>, a store of flags, a forest <math|F>, and one
  channel tag <math|c>. Fix also the positive scale parameter <math|x>, the
  channel mask parameter <math|q> and width <math|d>, the signed transport
  <math|s>, its coarse anchor <math|t>, the fine anchor <math|o>, and a pose
  at that anchor. Transport the store, forest, tag, and coarse field together
  by this same signed map. Denote the transported data by
  <math|A<rsup|\<sharp\>>,F<rsup|\<sharp\>>,c<rsup|\<sharp\>>,V<rsup|\<sharp\>>>.
  In particular, the transformed tag is the image of <math|c> under the
  signed channel action; it is not chosen again independently.

  Let <math|I=<math-up|In><around|(|x,A,F|)>> be the finite set of active
  fine-link Lie coordinates for the fixed store <math|A> and forest <math|F>.
  Let <math|\<Pi\>> be the centered covariance prescribed by the channel on
  these input coordinates. The sample space is

  <\equation>
    \<Omega\>=\<cal-H\><rsub|<math-up|frozen>>\<times\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|I>,<space|2em>d*\<mu\>*<around|(|H,Z|)>=d*\<pi\><rsub|<math-up|Haar>><around|(|H|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|\<Pi\>><around|(|Z|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-reader-space>
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|<math-up|frozen>>> is the product of the compact
  gauge groups on the bonds frozen by the store, with normalized product Haar
  measure, and <math|\<gamma\><rsub|\<Pi\>>> is the centered Gaussian measure
  with covariance matrix <math|\<Pi\>>. The two coordinates in
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-reader-space> are independent because the law is a
  product. The channel covariance and all its tags and masks remain fixed
  throughout the construction.

  For each sample <math|<around|(|H,Z|)>>, form the channel's local drift
  <math|a<around|(|H|)>> from the frozen links, using the fixed tube, tag,
  mask parameter, and width. Convert <math|a<around|(|H|)>+g*Z> to the gauge
  group by the fixed exponential-coordinate chart, then apply the
  finite-volume local link update to <math|V> while retaining <math|H> on the
  frozen bonds. Denote this specified update map by
  <math|<math-ss|U><rsub|A,F,V>>. Define the fine configuration

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-reader-fine-field>V<rsub|\<ast\>>*<around|(|H,Z|)>=<math-ss|U><rsub|A,F,V><space|-0.17em><around*|(|H,a<around|(|H|)>+g*Z|)>,<space|2em>g=\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation>

  The crucial point is that <math|H,Z>, the channel, and the coarse
  configuration are shared by every reader. The different readers enter only
  through their read sets and their local masks.

  The chosen pose is a single geometric input at <math|o>: on its room the
  coarse field agrees with one represented direction, after the fixed signed
  transport, and the room is healthy and clear of the assigned flags. The
  pose supplies the gauge frame used below. It is selected before the reader
  index is chosen and is not allowed to vary from one read coordinate to the
  next. For clarity, the quantitative receipts used by this handoff are
  these: the channel covariance is positive semidefinite and transforms
  covariantly under the fixed signed transport; for every admissible pair of
  incidence coordinates, there are nonnegative sight and common-history
  errors <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|sight>>,\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|hist>>>
  with sum at most <math|\<varepsilon\>>; for each reader <math|a>, its
  measurable mask has values in <math|<around|[|0,1|]>> and integral loss at
  most <math|\<varepsilon\><rsup|2>>; and, whenever that mask is positive,
  the same pose room and local comparison provide one gauge element for the
  whole reader patch with coordinate error at most <math|\<varepsilon\>>. The
  covariance split, Markov bound, and common-sample notation below are proved
  here. Production of these paid sight, mask, and field-comparison receipts
  is the separate input supplied by the selected local construction; it is
  not inferred from positivity of the covariance.

  When the surrounding construction averages over its finite list of tag
  atoms, apply this construction to each atom with its own fixed tag and
  covariance. Those outer weights are nonnegative and sum to one. The theorem
  below is uniform over the allowed atoms; it does not identify distinct
  tagged Gaussian measures with one another.

  <subsubsection|Exact joint law of the transverse coordinates>

  Let <math|\<cal-I\>> be the fine incidence-coordinate set, and let

  <\equation*>
    R:\<bbb-R\><rsup|I>\<longrightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|\<cal-I\>>
  </equation*>

  be the row matrix that reads the transported fine curvature coordinates and
  restricts them to these incidences. Write <math|Z<rsup|\<sharp\>>> for the
  Gaussian coordinate after the fixed signed transport, the channel's forest
  reorganization, and the one pose gauge change. Define the centered reader
  vector by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-reader-transverse>\<eta\>=R*Z<rsup|\<sharp\>>.
  </equation>

  The exact transported channel covariance on the coordinates before the row
  restriction is denoted <math|\<Pi\><rsup|\<sharp\>>>. The
  measure-preserving transport laws give

  <\equation>
    Z<rsup|\<sharp\>>\<sim\>\<gamma\><rsub|\<Pi\><rsup|\<sharp\>>>,<space|2em>\<eta\>\<sim\>\<gamma\><rsub|C>,<space|2em>C=R*\<Pi\><rsup|\<sharp\>>*R<rsup|<math-ss|T>>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-reader-covariance>
  </equation>

  This includes every cross-entry: for all <math|u,v\<in\>\<cal-I\>>,

  <\equation*>
    \<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|[|\<eta\><rsub|u>*\<eta\><rsub|v>|]>=<big|sum><rsub|r,r<rprime|'>\<in\>I>R<rsub|u*r>*\<Pi\><rsup|\<sharp\>><rsub|r*r<rprime|'>>*R<rsub|v*r<rprime|'>>=C<rsub|u*v>.
  </equation*>

  The law statement in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-reader-covariance>, not just its
  covariance identity, follows because the first transport pushes the product
  sample law to the transported Gaussian channel and the remaining maps are
  real-linear. Thus any finite subcollection of readers has the corresponding
  principal submatrix of <math|C> as its exact joint covariance. No
  independence among reader coordinates is asserted. The covariance <math|C>
  may be positive semidefinite and singular; the Gaussian pushforward
  statement remains valid in that case.

  The local estimate compares this matrix with the short reference
  covariance. Let <math|C<rsup|<math-up|short>>> be that covariance on the
  same incidence coordinates. For every pair <math|u,v> in the support of the
  chosen reader family, the paid sight estimate compares <math|C<rsub|u*v>>
  with the tracked finite-volume covariance
  <math|C<rsup|<math-up|track>><rsub|u*v>>. The common-history telescoping
  identity compares the latter with <math|C<rsup|<math-up|short>><rsub|u*v>>.
  Adding these two differences gives the exact bound

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-reader-covariance-error><around|\||C<rsub|u*v>-C<rsup|<math-up|short>><rsub|u*v>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||C<rsub|u*v>-C<rsup|<math-up|track>><rsub|u*v>|\|>+<around|\||C<rsup|<math-up|track>><rsub|u*v>-C<rsup|<math-up|short>><rsub|u*v>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>.
  </equation>

  The two terms are bounded by <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|sight>>>
  and <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|hist>>>, respectively, and their sum
  is at most <math|\<varepsilon\>>. The common-history identity and
  room-separation conditions are required for both coordinates. In
  particular, <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-reader-covariance-error> holds for every
  pair of admissible read coordinates in the full family, not just for its
  diagonal entries.

  <subsubsection|All-reader field comparison on the same sample>

  For a fine reader patch <math|a>, let <math|J<rsub|a>> be the
  chamber-coordinate set read by that patch inside the prescribed room. A
  patch is admissible when its reader support is contained in the coarse
  block tag. Its mask <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|a>:\<Omega\>\<to\><around|[|0,1|]>>
  depends on the same frozen field and channel as
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-reader-space>. The construction gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-reader-guard><big|int><rsub|\<Omega\>><around|(|1-\<Gamma\><rsub|a>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsup|2>.
  </equation>

  By Markov's inequality,

  <\equation*>
    \<mu\>*<around|{|\<Gamma\><rsub|a>=0|}>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsup|2>,<space|2em>\<mu\>*<around|{|\<Gamma\><rsub|a>\<leqslant\>1-\<delta\>|}>\<leqslant\><frac|\<varepsilon\><rsup|2>|\<delta\>>*<space|1em><around|(|0\<less\>\<delta\>\<leqslant\>1|)>.
  </equation*>

  The statement is per reader; it does not take a union over the reader
  family. When <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|a>*<around|(|H,Z|)>\<gtr\>0>, the local
  guard places the sample in the healthy room where its field coordinates can
  be compared.

  Write <math|<math-up|Basis><rsub|a><around|(|W|)>> for the local chamber
  basis at patch <math|a>, and define the complex gauged read by

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|Read><rsub|a,j><around|(|W;q|)>\<assign\><around*|[|<math-up|rigid><space|-0.17em><around*|(|<math-up|coloring><around*|(|q,<math-up|Basis><rsub|a><around|(|W|)>|)>|)>|]><rsub|j>.
  </equation*>

  Thus the read is a complex coordinate after applying the patch basis and
  one gauge frame. Let <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|<math-up|parent>><around|(|U|)>>
  denote the coarse chamber basis constructed once from the fixed parent
  manner, coarse configuration <math|U>, and reference tag. It is common to
  all fine patches; each set <math|J<rsub|a>> only restricts its coordinates.
  Let <math|<math-ss|N><rsub|H<rsub|i>>> be the normal-read map in the fixed
  reference chamber frame <math|H<rsub|i>>. The center is the actual linear
  forecast

  <\equation*>
    m<rsub|a,j>\<assign\><math-up|Re><around*|[|<math-ss|N><rsub|H<rsub|i>><around*|(|<math-up|rigid><around|(|<math-ss|B><rsub|<math-up|parent>><around|(|U|)>|)>|)>|]><rsub|j>,<space|2em>j\<in\>J<rsub|a>.
  </equation*>

  It is the real part of the normal read in the reference frame, not an
  untransformed coordinate of <math|U>. On every guarded sample there is one
  gauge element <math|q<rsub|a>*<around|(|H,Z|)>> for the whole patch such
  that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-reader-coordinate><around*|\||<math-up|Read><rsub|a,j><around*|(|V<rsub|\<ast\>>*<around|(|H,Z|)>;q<rsub|a>*<around|(|H,Z|)>|)>-<around*|(|m<rsub|a,j>+\<eta\><rsub|j>*<around|(|H,Z|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>,<space|2em>j\<in\>J<rsub|a>.
  </equation>

  The frame may depend on the reader and on the sample, but within one reader
  it is shared by all of that reader's coordinates. The same random vector
  <math|\<eta\>> from <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-reader-covariance> occurs for
  every patch, and the center <math|m<rsub|a,j>> is the actual coarse reader
  value. There is no separate sampling or re-centering for each <math|j>.

  The geometry behind <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-reader-coordinate> is as follows.
  The fine read region is first rewritten in the common coarse frame; the
  block room condition places its whole stencil inside the pose room. The
  paid local guard then supplies the healthy-region condition. Applying the
  field comparison on that room produces the one gauge element
  <math|q<rsub|a>*<around|(|H,Z|)>> and the coordinate estimate for each
  <math|j> in the patch. The reader's mask is what localizes this statement:
  outside its support no coordinate comparison is claimed. This is exactly
  the scope needed for bounded chamber-local insertions.

  The law <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-reader-space>, the covariance statements
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-reader-covariance>\U<eqref|eq:ex-lattice-reader-covariance-error>,
  the guard estimate <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-reader-guard>, and the coordinate
  comparison <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-reader-coordinate> are the joint reader
  model. They are the finite-dimensional input used by the subsequent
  Gaussian density comparison, with a fixed measurable masked insertion. That
  comparison is proved in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:principal-gaussian-comparison>;
  it derives the polynomial density-jet bound and never differentiates the
  insertion. The Gaussian reassembly in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-reassembly>
  separately proves that the absolutely summable activity expansion restores
  the original block integral. This auxiliary reader model alone does not
  assert that identity. Finally, the response billing in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-eve-producer> uses one
  uniform derivative and tail envelope over all allowed leaves and reader
  directions. The present section supplies the common-law and same-insertion
  identification required to apply those comparison and reassembly results;
  their primitive mean, covariance-jet, cutoff, and packet estimates remain
  those stated in the cited sections.

  <subsection|From calibrated Gaussian moments to
  cumulants><label|sec:allarity-cumulant-comparison>

  The marked comparison uses all subsets of a finite insertion word. Its
  Gaussian calculation must therefore control products before taking the
  finite logarithm. We give the degree-dependent estimates with the powers
  used in the scale purchases. The covariance and calibration hypotheses are
  stated explicitly; their common-atlas construction is needed when this
  calculation is applied to the selected history.

  <subsubsection|A dimension-independent polynomial estimate>

  For a polynomial <math|p> in finitely many commuting variables, write
  <math|<around|\||p|\|><rsub|1>> for the sum of the absolute values of its
  coefficients. It is submultiplicative: expand a product, apply the triangle
  inequality to each coefficient, and then sum over the two original monomial
  indices. Let <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|Q,\<mu\>>> be the Wick evaluation with
  symmetric covariance <math|Q> and mean <math|\<mu\>>. No positivity is
  needed in this algebraic calculation. The defining recurrence, for a
  product of <math|j> coordinate variables, is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:allarity-wick-recursion>\<cal-W\><rsub|Q,\<mu\>>*<around|(|X<rsub|i<rsub|1>>*\<cdots\>*X<rsub|i<rsub|j>>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|i<rsub|1>>*\<cal-W\><rsub|Q,\<mu\>>*<around|(|X<rsub|i<rsub|2>>*\<cdots\>*X<rsub|i<rsub|j>>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|\<ell\>=2><rsup|j>Q<rsub|i<rsub|1>*i<rsub|\<ell\>>>*\<cal-W\><rsub|Q,\<mu\>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|a\<ne\>1,\<ell\>>X<rsub|i<rsub|a>>|)>,
  </equation>

  with value one on the empty product. Repeated coordinate indices are
  allowed: their positions in this recurrence remain distinct.

  Assume <math|B\<geqslant\>1>, <math|<around|\||\<mu\><rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>B>
  and <math|<around|\||Q<rsub|i*j>|\|>\<leqslant\>B> for every coordinate
  used. Define <math|C<rsub|d><around|(|B|)>=<around|[|2*B*<around|(|d+1|)>|]><rsup|d>>,
  including <math|C<rsub|0><around|(|B|)>=1>. Then every polynomial of degree
  at most <math|d> satisfies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:allarity-wick-bound><around|\||\<cal-W\><rsub|Q,\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||p|\|><rsub|1>*C<rsub|d><around|(|B|)>.
  </equation>

  If a second pair <math|<around|(|Q<rprime|'>,\<mu\><rprime|'>|)>> obeys the
  same bounds and <math|<around|\||Q<rsub|i*j>-Q<rprime|'><rsub|i*j>|\|>,<around|\||\<mu\><rsub|i>-\<mu\><rprime|'><rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>>,
  then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:allarity-wick-difference><around|\||\<cal-W\><rsub|Q,\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>-\<cal-W\><rsub|Q<rprime|'>,\<mu\><rprime|'>><around|(|p|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||p|\|><rsub|1>*\<varepsilon\>*C<rsub|d><around|(|B|)>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<varepsilon\>\<geqslant\>0> can be arbitrary.

  We prove both estimates on monomials by simultaneous induction on the
  degree ceiling <math|d>. At degree zero their assertions are respectively
  <math|1\<leqslant\>1> and <math|0\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>>. For a
  nonempty monomial of degree at most <math|d+1>, the recurrence has one mean
  term and at most <math|d> covariance terms. Every remaining product has
  degree at most <math|d>. Thus the first bound is at most
  <math|<around|(|d+1|)>*B*C<rsub|d><around|(|B|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|d+1><around|(|B|)>>.
  For a difference of terms use <math|a*b-a<rprime|'>*b<rprime|'>=a*<around|(|b-b<rprime|'>|)>+<around|(|a-a<rprime|'>|)>*b<rprime|'>>.
  The induction bounds its absolute value by
  <math|B*\<varepsilon\>*C<rsub|d><around|(|B|)>+\<varepsilon\>*C<rsub|d><around|(|B|)>>,
  at most <math|2*B*\<varepsilon\>*C<rsub|d><around|(|B|)>>. Summing at most
  <math|d+1> such terms gives <math|2*B*<around|(|d+1|)>*\<varepsilon\>*C<rsub|d><around|(|B|)>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>*C<rsub|d+1><around|(|B|)>>.
  The inequalities follow from <math|C<rsub|d><around|(|B|)>\<leqslant\><around|[|2*B*<around|(|d+2|)>|]><rsup|d>>.
  The empty monomial satisfies both bounds at every degree ceiling. Finally
  expand <math|p> in monomials and sum their absolute coefficients. This
  proves <eqref|eq:allarity-wick-bound> and
  <eqref|eq:allarity-wick-difference> without a factor depending on the
  number of coordinate variables.

  <subsubsection|Calibrated vertices and their products>

  Fix <math|n> insertion slots, a common polynomial degree bound <math|k>,
  and possibly different grades <math|g<rsub|i>> for the slots. Here
  <math|n,k,g<rsub|i>> are nonnegative integers, and all error parameters
  <math|\<xi\>,\<varepsilon\>> are nonnegative. After substituting the actual
  linear reader map, write <math|H<rsub|i>> for the polynomial of slot
  <math|i>. Let <math|F<rsub|i>> be its intended free polynomial, embedded in
  the physical coordinates of the same enlarged variable space. Suppose the
  grade truncation of <math|H<rsub|i>> through grade <math|g<rsub|i>> is
  exactly <math|F<rsub|i>>. Every variable has a nonnegative integer grade.
  For <math|r\<geqslant\>1>, write

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||p|\|><rsub|1,r>=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>><around|\||p<rsub|\<alpha\>>|\|>*r<rsup|<math-up|grade><around|(|\<alpha\>|)>>.
  </equation*>

  Every monomial of <math|H<rsub|i>-F<rsub|i>> has grade at least
  <math|g<rsub|i>+1>, whence

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:allarity-grade-tail>r<rsup|g<rsub|i>+1>*<around|\||H<rsub|i>-F<rsub|i>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|\||H<rsub|i>|\|><rsub|1,r>.
  </equation>

  This uses the exact low-grade identity, so no difference of two large
  low-grade coefficients is being estimated.

  Suppose the incoming polynomial has coefficient norm at most
  <math|a<rsub|i>> and degree at most <math|k>, and every substituted linear
  coordinate has weighted norm at most <math|b\<geqslant\>1>. Expanding each
  monomial and using submultiplicativity gives
  <math|<around|\||H<rsub|i>|\|><rsub|1,r>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|i>*b<rsup|k>>.
  Thus the purchase

  <\equation*>
    a<rsub|i>*b<rsup|k>\<leqslant\>\<xi\>*r<rsup|g<rsub|i>+1>
  </equation*>

  implies <math|<around|\||H<rsub|i>-F<rsub|i>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<xi\>>.
  Also assume <math|<around|\||F<rsub|i>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>A>, where
  <math|A\<geqslant\>1>. Then <math|<around|\||H<rsub|i>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>A+\<xi\>>.
  For a subset <math|I> of slots, telescope the product one factor at a time.
  Every summand contains one difference <math|H<rsub|i>-F<rsub|i>> and
  <math|<around|\||I|\|>-1> factors whose norm is at most <math|A+\<xi\>>.
  Therefore

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:allarity-vertex-product><around*|\||<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I>H<rsub|i>-<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I>F<rsub|i>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|\||I|\|>*\<xi\>*<around|(|A+\<xi\>|)><rsup|<around|\||I|\|>-1>\<leqslant\>n*\<xi\>*<around|(|A+\<xi\>|)><rsup|n>.
  </equation>

  For <math|I=\<varnothing\>> the difference is zero. Polynomial degrees are
  at most <math|n*k>, both before and after substitution and truncation.

  The actual reader covariance and mean must be the pullbacks of a common
  pair <math|<around|(|Q,\<mu\>|)>> on the enlarged variable space.
  Substitution then commutes with Wick evaluation. To verify this, apply
  <eqref|eq:allarity-wick-recursion> to products of substituted linear forms.
  Their means are the linear pullbacks of <math|\<mu\>> and their pairings
  are the bilinear pullbacks of <math|Q>. Induction on the number of factors
  gives the identity on monomials; linearity gives it on all polynomials.
  This establishes the needed identity without changing Gaussian variables
  separately for each slot.

  Assume all entries of <math|<around|(|Q,\<mu\>|)>> are bounded by
  <math|B\<geqslant\>1>, and its restriction to the physical coordinates is
  within <math|\<varepsilon\>> of the free covariance with zero mean, whose
  entries are also bounded by <math|B>. The <math|F<rsub|i>> involve only
  these physical coordinates. Insert and subtract the Wick evaluation of
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I>F<rsub|i>> at <math|<around|(|Q,\<mu\>|)>>.
  Equations <eqref|eq:allarity-wick-bound>\U<eqref|eq:allarity-vertex-product>
  give, for every subset <math|I>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:allarity-moment-error><around*|\||\<cal-W\><rsub|Q,\<mu\>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I>H<rsub|i>|)>-\<cal-W\><rsub|<math-up|free>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I>F<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|{|n*\<xi\>*<around|(|A+\<xi\>|)><rsup|n>+A<rsup|n>*\<varepsilon\>|}>*<space|0.17em>C<rsub|n*k><around|(|B|)>.
  </equation>

  The unused residual coordinates do not enter the second term. They do enter
  the first term, and their bounds in <math|B> cannot be omitted.

  <subsubsection|The scale powers that pay for the estimate>

  Put <math|H=exp <around|(|<around|(|log log s|)><rsup|2>|)>> for the
  diverging monitor along the comparison scales, and choose

  <\equation*>
    \<lambda\>=<frac|1|4*<around|(|n*k+k+1|)>>,<space|2em>B=H<rsup|\<lambda\>>,<space|2em>\<xi\>=\<varepsilon\>=H<rsup|-2>.
  </equation*>

  The parameter <math|s> is taken sufficiently large that the logarithms are
  defined and <math|H\<geqslant\>1>. Since
  <math|\<lambda\>*n*k\<leqslant\>1/4>,

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|n*k><around|(|B|)>\<leqslant\><around|[|2*<around|(|n*k+1|)>|]><rsup|n*k>*H<rsup|1/4>.
  </equation*>

  The right side of <eqref|eq:allarity-moment-error> is therefore at most

  <\equation*>
    <around|[|2*<around|(|n*k+1|)>|]><rsup|n*k>*<around|{|n*<around|(|A+1|)><rsup|n>+A<rsup|n>|}>*H<rsup|-7/4>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-3/2>
  </equation*>

  for all sufficiently large <math|s>. The last threshold depends on the
  fixed word and degree bound. It is uniform over the finite volume and
  replicas once the preceding covariance and vertex hypotheses hold
  uniformly.

  For completeness, the grade-tail purchase follows from the same powers. If
  <math|r=H<rsup|b>>, <math|a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|b*g<rsub|i>+E>>, the
  linear-coordinate substitution cost is at most <math|H<rsup|\<lambda\>>>,
  and <math|b\<geqslant\>0>, <math|b\<geqslant\>E+3>, then

  <\equation*>
    a<rsub|i><around|(|H<rsup|\<lambda\>>|)><rsup|k>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|b*g<rsub|i>+E+1/4>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-2>*H<rsup|b*<around|(|g<rsub|i>+1|)>>.
  </equation*>

  The first inequality uses <math|\<lambda\>*k\<leqslant\>1/4>; the second
  follows from <math|E+1/4\<leqslant\>b-2>. Thus every grade is treated at
  its own truncation level while the arity and polynomial degree determine
  the common Gaussian growth allowance.

  <subsubsection|Taking the finite logarithm>

  Use the squarefree algebra <math|<with|math-font|cal*|A><rsub|<around|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>
  from Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-planted-compiler>. For a
  polynomial <math|q> with constant coefficient one, define

  <\equation*>
    log<rsub|\<ast\>> q=<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|n><frac|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|j+1>|j>*<around|(|q-1|)><rsup|j>.
  </equation*>

  Formal power-series identities truncated after degree <math|n> give
  <math|exp<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|log<rsub|\<ast\>> q|)>=q> and
  <math|log<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|exp<rsub|\<ast\>> P|)>=P> for <math|P>
  with zero constant coefficient. These identities can also be checked
  coefficientwise: all powers above <math|n> vanish, so the usual
  one-variable formal identities in the nilpotent variable <math|q-1> are
  finite identities here. In particular the cumulant polynomial is uniquely
  determined by the moment polynomial.

  We give an explicit local stability bound. Use the coefficient
  <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>> norm on this algebra. If
  <math|<around|\||q-1|\|><rsub|1>,<around|\||q<rprime|'>-1|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>M>,
  the identity <math|u<rsup|j>-v<rsup|j>=<big|sum><rsub|a=0><rsup|j-1>u<rsup|a>*<around|(|u-v|)>*v<rsup|j-1-a>>
  yields

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||log<rsub|\<ast\>> q-log<rsub|\<ast\>>
    q<rprime|'>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|n>M<rsup|j-1>|)>*<around|\||q-q<rprime|'>|\|><rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  If the coefficientwise moment error is at most <math|H<rsup|-3/2>>, then
  <math|<around|\||q-q<rprime|'>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|n>*H<rsup|-3/2>>.
  The free moment polynomial <math|q<rprime|'>> varies continuously on the
  compact comparison window, which is contained in the distinct-point
  configuration domain, so its coefficient norm is bounded. The moment error
  then bounds <math|<around|\||q-1|\|><rsub|1>> as well. Consequently there
  is a constant <math|C> for this fixed word and window such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:allarity-cumulant-error><around|\||log<rsub|\<ast\>>
    q-log<rsub|\<ast\>> q<rprime|'>|\|><rsub|1>+H<rsup|-3/2>\<leqslant\><around|(|C+1|)>*H<rsup|-3/2>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-31/32>
  </equation>

  for all sufficiently large <math|s>. Each coefficient of the logarithm
  difference has modulus at most the coefficient <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>> norm.
  The factor <math|2<rsup|n>> is absorbed only after <math|n> is fixed.

  On the actual intermediate Gaussian history, let <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|l,k>>
  be the successive linear substitution and Gaussian integration operator
  from level <math|k> to level <math|l>. Define the moment polynomial by

  <\equation*>
    q<around|(|z|)>=<big|sum><rsub|I\<subseteq\><around|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>z<rsub|I>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-S\><rsub|l,k><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I>P<rsub|i>|)><around|(|y|)>.
  </equation*>

  Its constant coefficient is one, since every substitution and normalized
  Gaussian integration preserves one. The connected polynomial obtained by
  the finite partition formula is <math|log<rsub|\<ast\>> q>: expanding the
  displayed finite logarithm gives that partition formula directly, with
  coefficient <math|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|j-1>*<around|(|j-1|)>>! for a
  partition into <math|j> nonempty blocks. Evaluation at the fixed
  intermediate field <math|y> commutes with this finite polynomial formula.
  This is the exact cumulant used in the planted comparison. Therefore the
  moment estimate for the same reader covariance and mean implies
  <eqref|eq:allarity-cumulant-error> for it.

  If the planted local coefficient differs from this cumulant by at most
  <math|H<rsup|-3/2>>, its sum with the compensating negative free cumulant
  is bounded by <math|H<rsup|-31/32>>, by <eqref|eq:allarity-cumulant-error>
  and the triangle inequality. Signed copies and complex conjugation preserve
  these absolute coefficient bounds. This is the small initial remainder
  needed for the subsequent marked propagation. Its use still requires the
  local planted-coefficient error, the exact low-grade calibration, and the
  common reader covariance estimates at the displayed powers; they are not
  consequences of finite-dimensional logarithm continuity.

  <subsection|The healthy-field comparison at the middle
  scale><label|sec:marked-healthy-cumulant-launch>

  The Gaussian comparison must approximate the actual local coefficient on
  each healthy configuration. We prove this by constructing one closed real
  curvature cochain from that configuration, and by comparing the actual
  polynomial transport with the transport of its covariance atlas. Both
  comparisons are simultaneous for every subset of the insertion word.

  <subsubsection|A closed cochain from a healthy gauge field>

  Work on a lattice of integer side <math|N<rsub|<math-up|per>>\<gtr\>0>,
  with a patch of integer radius <math|B\<geqslant\>0> about <math|o>, and
  assume <math|2*<around|(|B+1|)>\<less\>N<rsub|<math-up|per>>>. Let the
  field be healthy at threshold <math|\<theta\>\<geqslant\>0> on that patch.
  Put it in the axial gauge based at <math|o>. The path construction
  expresses every link in the room as a product of at most <math|4*B>
  conjugated plaquette holonomies. The chord of a product is at most the sum
  of the chords, so each such link has chord at most <math|4*B*\<theta\>>.
  Let <math|c\<gtr\>0> be the actual scale normalization. Define a
  Lie-algebra-valued one-cochain <math|A> by taking <math|c<rsup|-1>> times
  the curvature coordinate of this gauge-fixed link inside the room, and zero
  outside it. Define the two-cochain <math|X=d*A> on the whole torus, and
  take its real colour coordinates. Then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:healthy-closed-cochain>d*X=0,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|X|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>16*B*\<theta\>/c.
  </equation>

  The first assertion is the exact discrete identity <math|d<rsup|2>=0>, also
  across the artificial boundary of the room. The second follows by summing
  the four link bounds in each plaquette curl.

  Let <math|Y> be <math|c<rsup|-1>> times the actual curvature of the
  gauge-fixed plaquette. For a plaquette whose base is at distance at most
  <math|B-2> from <math|o>, all four edges lie inside the room. The curvature
  map satisfies the product estimate

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-C\>*<around|(|a*b|)>-\<cal-C\><around|(|a|)>-\<cal-C\><around|(|b|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|G><math-up|chord><around|(|a|)><math-up|chord><around|(|b|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-C\><around|(|a<rsup|-1>|)>=-\<cal-C\><around|(|a|)>.
  </equation*>

  Applying it successively to the four factors of the plaquette, with their
  inverse orientations, costs <math|1+2+3=6> products of link bounds. Hence

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:healthy-curvature-linear-error><around|\||Y<rsub|\<nu\>>-X<rsub|\<nu\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>6*c<rsub|G>*<around|(|4*B*\<theta\>|)><rsup|2>/c
  </equation>

  for every colour coordinate in the interior stencil. This is an estimate
  for the same gauged field, without sampling a new field. Translations and
  signed coordinate changes transport the construction and preserve these
  absolute bounds.

  At the selected middle scale the normalization satisfies
  <math|\<theta\>/c\<leqslant\>\<tau\><around|(|t|)>>, where <math|t> is its
  coupling and <math|\<tau\><around|(|t|)>=3<space|0.17em><math-up|hot><around|(|t|)>>.
  The upper comparison coupling is <math|s>, with
  <math|s/2\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>s>. Write <math|H=exp
  <around|(|<around|(|log log s|)><rsup|2>|)>>, taking <math|s> large enough
  that <math|H\<geqslant\>1>, and fix <math|0\<less\>\<lambda\>\<leqslant\>1/4>.
  The purchased numerical bounds are

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|16*B*\<tau\><around|(|t|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<lambda\>/4>,<eq-number><label|eq:healthy-middle-purchases>>>|<row|<cell|6*c<rsub|G>*<around|(|4*B|)><rsup|2>*\<theta\>*\<tau\><around|(|t|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>,<space|2em>\<varepsilon\>=H<rsup|-v>,>>>>
  </align>

  where <math|v> is selected before the final coupling threshold. They are
  feasible uniformly in this interval: the patch radius and <math|\<tau\>>
  grow slower than every power of <math|H>, whereas
  <math|\<theta\>=t<rsup|-1/2><math-up|hot><around|(|t|)>> decays faster than
  every negative power of <math|H>. For example, taking logarithms compares
  <math|-<tfrac|1|2>log t+o<around|(|log t|)>> with any fixed multiple of
  <math|<around|(|log log s|)><rsup|2>>; <math|t\<geqslant\>s/2> makes this
  uniform. Thus <eqref|eq:healthy-closed-cochain> supplies exactly a closed
  real background admitted by the covariance comparison, while
  <eqref|eq:healthy-curvature-linear-error> is at most <math|\<varepsilon\>>.

  <subsubsection|Comparing two polynomial transports>

  A polynomial step consists of a finite linear substitution followed by Wick
  contraction. Suppose the coefficient sum of every substituted coordinate
  and every covariance entry is at most <math|C\<geqslant\>1>. For degree at
  most <math|D>, put <math|A<rsub|D>=<around|[|2*<around|(|D+2|)>*C|]><rsup|D>>.
  The Wick recurrence used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-cumulant-comparison>
  applies equally when its mean is a linear polynomial. Submultiplicativity
  of coefficient sums then proves that the step preserves degree and has norm
  at most <math|A<rsub|D>> on this finite-degree space. If a second step has
  the same bounds and each linear row and covariance entry differs by at most
  <math|\<varepsilon\>\<geqslant\>0>, the same induction, subtracting
  products one factor at a time, bounds the difference operator by
  <math|\<varepsilon\>*A<rsub|D>>. The row error here is a coefficient sum,
  not an entrywise error followed by an unbounded sum over sites. In the
  application below, purchase this per-step discrepancy at most the same
  <math|\<varepsilon\>=H<rsup|-v>> that bounds the field-coordinate error.
  These are two separate purchases; a bound for one does not imply the other.

  For <math|m> successive steps <math|T<rsub|i>,T<rprime|'><rsub|i>>, expand

  <\equation*>
    T<rsub|m>*\<cdots\>*T<rsub|1>-T<rprime|'><rsub|m>*\<cdots\>*T<rprime|'><rsub|1>=<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|m>T<rprime|'><rsub|m>*\<cdots\>*T<rprime|'><rsub|j+1>*<around|(|T<rsub|j>-T<rprime|'><rsub|j>|)>*T<rsub|j-1>*\<cdots\>*T<rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  Thus, for <math|deg p\<leqslant\>D>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:healthy-transport-telescope><around|\||T<rsub|m>*\<cdots\>*T<rsub|1>*p-T<rprime|'><rsub|m>*\<cdots\>*T<rprime|'><rsub|1>*p|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|\||p|\|><rsub|1>*m*\<varepsilon\>*A<rsub|D><rsup|m>.
  </equation>

  We apply this to the actual polynomial history and the history read from
  its covariance atlas. Their per-step common bounds and errors are the same
  ones used in the marked Gaussian reader estimates.

  All degree bounds <math|D,K,D<rsub|\<ast\>>> and the insertion count
  <math|n> below are nonnegative integers. Let <math|p<rsub|i>> be the
  calibrated incoming polynomials, <math|i=1,\<ldots\>,n>, with a fixed
  exponent <math|z\<geqslant\>0>, with <math|<around|\||p<rsub|i>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|z>>
  and degree at most <math|K>. Every subset product has degree at most
  <math|n*K> and norm at most <math|H<rsup|n*z>>. Choose the block length
  after <math|A<rsub|n*K>> so that

  <\equation*>
    2*<around|(|b+2|)>*log A<rsub|n*K>\<leqslant\>log L.
  </equation*>

  For the prescribed number <math|m=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|b+2|)>*log
  H/log L|\<rceil\>>> of steps, this gives
  <math|A<rsub|n*K><rsup|m>\<leqslant\>H> eventually; also
  <math|m\<leqslant\>H> eventually. Therefore all transported subset moments
  have norm at most <math|R=H<rsup|n*z+1>>, and their differences have norm
  at most <math|R*H*\<varepsilon\>>.

  For a nonempty subset <math|I>, its connected polynomial is

  <\equation*>
    c<rsub|I>=<big|sum><rsub|\<pi\>\<in\>\<cal-P\><around|(|I|)>><around|(|-1|)><rsup|<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>-1>*<around|(|<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>-1|)>!<big|prod><rsub|B\<in\>\<pi\>>q<rsub|B>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|q<rsub|B>> is the transported moment polynomial. There are
  finitely many set partitions. Telescoping the product for each partition
  bounds the difference of the two connected polynomials by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:healthy-connected-polynomials><around|\||c<rsub|I>-c<rprime|'><rsub|I>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>D<rsub|n><around|(|R|)>*R*H*\<varepsilon\>,<space|1em>D<rsub|n><around|(|R|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<varnothing\>\<ne\>I\<subseteq\><around|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><big|sum><rsub|\<pi\>\<in\>\<cal-P\><around|(|I|)>><around|\||\<pi\>|\|>!R<rsup|<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>-1>.
  </equation>

  Both have degree at most a fixed number <math|D<rsub|\<ast\>>>, and
  coefficient norm at most a fixed polynomial <math|Q<rsub|n><around|(|R|)>>
  in <math|R>, obtained by the same partition expansion. These constants
  depend on the finite word, not on the lattice period.

  <subsubsection|Evaluation and the initial connected error>

  For a polynomial <math|p> of degree at most <math|D<rsub|\<ast\>>>, two
  real coordinate vectors bounded by <math|M\<geqslant\>1> on its variables,
  and coordinate error at most <math|\<varepsilon\>>, monomial telescoping
  gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||p<around|(|Y|)>-p<around|(|X|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||p|\|><rsub|1>*D<rsub|\<ast\>>*M<rsup|D<rsub|\<ast\>>>*\<varepsilon\>.
  </equation*>

  The sharper power <math|M<rsup|D<rsub|\<ast\>>-1>> is available for
  positive degree; the displayed bound also covers degree zero. Moreover
  <math|<around|\||p<around|(|X|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||p|\|><rsub|1>*M<rsup|D<rsub|\<ast\>>>>.
  The healthy-field construction and its numerical purchases bound both
  vectors by <math|M=H> on the required interior stencil. Using
  <eqref|eq:healthy-connected-polynomials> therefore gives

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\||c<rsub|I><around|(|Y|)>-c<rprime|'><rsub|I><around|(|X|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>Q<rsub|n><around|(|R|)>*D<rsub|\<ast\>>*H<rsup|D<rsub|\<ast\>>>*\<varepsilon\>+D<rsub|n><around|(|R|)>*R*H<rsup|D<rsub|\<ast\>>+1>*\<varepsilon\>.>>>>
  </align*>

  The prefactor is bounded by a fixed power <math|H<rsup|a>>, with a fixed
  multiplicative constant. Choose <math|v\<gtr\>a+3/2> before the final
  coupling threshold. The right side is then at most <math|H<rsup|-3/2>>
  eventually. This is the actual healthy local-coefficient error required by
  the cumulant comparison. The support estimates of the marked propagation
  put every variable used here at distance at most <math|B-2> from the chosen
  base; no estimate outside the healthy stencil has been used.

  The atlas connected polynomial <math|c<rprime|'><rsub|I><around|(|X|)>> is
  the finite logarithm of its jointly evaluated Gaussian moments. The exact
  common-coordinate and calibration construction, followed by the actual
  covariance bounds, compares those moments with the free moments with error
  <math|H<rsup|-3/2>>. Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-cumulant-comparison>
  consequently gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||c<rprime|'><rsub|I><around|(|X|)>-c<rsub|I><rsup|<math-up|free>>|\|>+H<rsup|-3/2>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-31/32>
  </equation*>

  eventually. Adding the healthy local error proves the prescribed small
  remainder against the negative free connected coefficient. The coordinate
  <math|X> is common to all subsets, so this step does not mix separately
  chosen background realizations.

  At the upper comparison level the same construction starts with singletons
  only. The transport has zero length there and is the identity. Hence its
  moment polynomial is <math|<big|prod><rsub|i><around|(|1+z<rsub|i>*p<rsub|i>|)>>
  in the squarefree marker algebra, and its finite logarithm is
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>z<rsub|i>*p<rsub|i>>. Every connected coefficient
  with at least two markers vanishes. The principal singleton is the masked
  raw polynomial constructed from the calibrated near list. Its
  identification with the actual birth uses the exact decomposition
  <eqref|eq:rank-terminal-three-pieces> and the selected-entry identity
  <eqref|eq:rank-selected-entry-split>: the supported approximation error,
  the mask correction, the wide list and the original forced terms are all
  retained. The scalar unit in the original list is unmasked. Thus the
  polynomial is not substituted for the whole calibrated coefficient by an
  equality. The algebraic reindexing adds zero higher principal coefficients
  and, with these remainder terms, leaves the pushed density unchanged.
  Shrinking the marker radius preserves regular and forced bounds, since
  every marker monomial acquires a smaller nonnegative weight. This
  identifies the top initialization used in the all-arity marked propagation
  with the actual calibrated insertion.

  The remaining inputs in applying this chapter are the actual mixed
  compiler's per-step row and covariance comparison, the terminal paid
  insertion bound and the common purchases. The argument proves the
  healthy-field and polynomial comparison from those precise receipts; it
  does not produce them by changing the selected history.

  <subsection|Realigning and propagating the marked
  comparison><label|sec:allarity-marked-propagation>

  The finite polynomial comparison must survive each block integration. At an
  intermediate level the marked expansion contains the desired principal
  insertion, a small regular remainder, and a forced remainder. We explain
  how its leading pieces are reassigned to the prescribed supports and how
  the resulting errors accumulate. The identities use the actual
  representation at that level.

  <subsubsection|Why a connected principal component stays in one cell>

  Let <math|\<rho\>\<geqslant\>0> be an elementary slice radius. Each
  elementary principal piece has a nonempty set of marked reader sites; each
  such reader belongs to its full support, and the full support is within
  distance <math|\<rho\>> of every one of those readers. Assume distinct
  replica cells have reader separation strictly greater than
  <math|2*\<rho\>>, and

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|3+2*n|)>*\<rho\>\<leqslant\>d*B.
  </equation*>

  Consider a connected component in the graph joining elementary pieces whose
  full supports intersect. Two marked readers on the same piece are at
  distance at most <math|\<rho\>>, since a reader lies in that piece's
  support. Readers on two adjacent pieces have distance at most
  <math|2*\<rho\>>, by using a point in the intersection. Both bounds are at
  most <math|2*\<rho\>>, so the readers belong to the same replica cell.
  Following a path of intersecting pieces shows that all marked readers of
  the component belong to one cell.

  There are at most <math|n> distinct marker labels in that cell. Make a
  graph on the labels used by the component, joining labels appearing on the
  same or on adjacent pieces. The preceding support path gives a walk between
  any two of these labels. Erase cycles from the walk. The remaining simple
  path uses at most <math|n-1> edges, each of geometric length at most
  <math|2*\<rho\>>. Thus the distance between any two marked readers is at
  most <math|2*n*\<rho\>>. Every point in the component's full support is at
  distance at most <math|\<rho\>> from one of its readers, so it is at
  distance at most <math|<around|(|2*n+1|)>*\<rho\>\<leqslant\>d*B> from
  every marked reader of the component. Its marker diameter is also at most
  <math|d*B>. This proves the required enlarged-support bounds independently
  of how many elementary pieces the component contains.

  For the selected construction the elementary slice radius is
  <math|\<rho\>=3*B>. The purchase <math|6*n+3\<leqslant\>a/<around|(|3+2*n|)>>
  gives the final distance bound <math|<around|(|2*n+1|)>*\<rho\>=<around|(|6*n+3|)>*B\<leqslant\>a*B/<around|(|3+2*n|)>>;
  it does not construct the slices. In particular it supplies the bound
  <math|a*B> required for the enlarged full supports in the regrouping below.
  The selected replica separation is stronger than <math|2*\<rho\>>.
  Subsequent regrouping of pure principal components preserves their full
  supports and marker unions, so the same argument applies to the principal
  connected logarithm.

  <subsubsection|Marker prices and spatial prices>

  Fix <math|n\<geqslant\>1> marks in each replica cell. Every principal piece
  has a nonempty marker subset <math|I\<subseteq\><around|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
  and a prescribed spatial support <math|X<rsub|c,I>> in cell <math|c>. The
  geometric hypotheses are the following: pieces with the same marker mode
  have a unique prescribed support; each actual principal support is
  contained in that support; and two prescribed supports containing the same
  spatial site belong to the same replica cell. These statements are made for
  full inspected supports, including the masks. Let

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|chg><rsub|e><around|(|X|)>=exp
    <around|(|e<math-up|cov><rsub|B><around|(|X|)>|)>,<space|2em><math-up|chg><rsub|e><around|(|X<rsub|c,I>|)>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|e>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|e\<geqslant\>0> is the support exponent, <math|B> the current
  radius and <math|<math-up|cov><rsub|B>> its covering number. Use one
  constant marker weight <math|\<omega\>\<gtr\>0> at every mark, of every
  cell, replica and period. The weight of a mode <math|I> is
  <math|\<omega\><rsup|<around|\||I|\|>>>. We do not assume
  <math|\<omega\>\<leqslant\>1>; in the application below
  <math|\<omega\>\<geqslant\>1>. Since <math|I\<subseteq\><around|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>,
  every mode satisfies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:allarity-mode-weight>\<omega\><rsup|<around|\||I|\|>>\<leqslant\><wide|\<omega\>|\<bar\>><rsub|n>,<space|2em><wide|\<omega\>|\<bar\>><rsub|n>=max
    <around|(|1,\<omega\>|)><rsup|n>.
  </equation>

  For <math|\<omega\>\<leqslant\>1> this is the bound
  <math|\<omega\><rsup|<around|\||I|\|>>\<leqslant\>1>.

  If each prescribed piece has weighted coefficient price at most <math|Z>,
  its total incidence price at a fixed mark <math|<around|(|c,j|)>> is at
  most <math|2<rsup|n>*Z>. Indeed only subsets <math|I> of that same cell can
  contain <math|<around|(|c,j|)>>, and there are at most <math|2<rsup|n>>
  subsets. This estimate is independent of the number of replica cells.

  A second estimate converts incidence price to spatial price. Suppose actual
  pieces <math|s<rsub|a>> have nonnegative prices <math|p<rsub|a>> and
  nonempty marker supports, and the total price of pieces containing any
  fixed mark is at most <math|N>. Assign piece <math|a> to its prescribed
  support <math|X<rsub|c<around|(|a|)>,I<around|(|a|)>>>. At a spatial site
  <math|v>, if any such support contains <math|v>, all such supports lie in
  one cell <math|c>. Charge each summand to one of its nonempty marks and
  then overcount by summing over every mark in that cell. It follows that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:allarity-spatial-incidence><big|sum><rsub|a:<space|0.17em>v\<in\>X<rsub|c<around|(|a|)>,I<around|(|a|)>>>p<rsub|a><math-up|chg><rsub|e><around|(|X<rsub|c<around|(|a|)>,I<around|(|a|)>>|)>\<leqslant\>n*N*R<rsub|e>.
  </equation>

  This is why the spatial charge is proportional to <math|n>, while the
  number of principal subset types is bounded by <math|2<rsup|n>>.

  <subsubsection|Replacing the leading pieces on their prescribed
  supports><label|subsec:allarity-realign>

  Write the current regular source as a sum of remainder pieces
  <math|H<rsub|a>> and leading pieces <math|S<rsub|b>>. Let
  <math|F<rsub|c,I>> be the desired principal source at mode
  <math|<around|(|c,I|)>>, with support <math|X<rsub|c,I>>. Group the
  <math|S<rsub|b>> by this marker mode and define on its healthy region

  <\equation*>
    L<rsub|c,I><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|b:<space|0.17em><math-up|mode><around|(|S<rsub|b>|)>=<around|(|c,I|)>>S<rsub|b><around|(|U|)>-F<rsub|c,I><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  Assume <math|<around|\||L<rsub|c,I><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>y> there.
  Attach the full support <math|X<rsub|c,I>> to <math|L<rsub|c,I>> and apply
  its healthy mask. The incidence price of these new regular remainders is at
  most

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:allarity-realign-regular>2<rsup|n>*<wide|\<omega\>|\<bar\>><rsub|n>*R<rsub|e>*y.
  </equation>

  Indeed the new piece at mode <math|<around|(|c,I|)>> has value at most
  <math|y> on its healthy region, support charge at most <math|R<rsub|e>> and
  marker weight <math|\<omega\><rsup|<around|\||I|\|>>>, so its weighted
  price is at most <math|\<omega\><rsup|<around|\||I|\|>>*R<rsub|e>*y\<leqslant\><wide|\<omega\>|\<bar\>><rsub|n>*R<rsub|e>*y>
  by <eqref|eq:allarity-mode-weight>. The preceding subset count applies.

  The mask change also has a forced part. A leading piece may be healthy on
  its smaller support while its prescribed larger support is not healthy.
  Split its original masked value into its value on the larger healthy region
  and the complementary part. The latter is supported where the enlarged
  support contains a flag. Insert these complementary pieces into the forced
  alphabet, keeping their original marker modes. The identity is an identity
  of functions on every field: on the larger healthy region it is the
  defining equality for <math|L<rsub|c,I>>; on its complement the difference
  has just been assigned to the forced part. Apply the exact regular/forced
  regrouping of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:soft-log-fusion> to the
  enlarged supports. No division by an activity value is involved.

  Here is its quantitative form. Suppose the previous forced spatial price is
  <math|s>, the leading incidence price is <math|Z>, and the new forced
  inflation exponent is <math|g\<geqslant\>0>. If

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:allarity-realign-forced-input>e<rsup|g>*s+n*Z*R<rsub|e+1>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>,<space|2em><around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*\<delta\>\<leqslant\>1,
  </equation>

  then the regrouped forced price is at most

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:allarity-realign-forced-output>e<rsup|-g>*<around|(|e<rsup|\<delta\>>-1|)>.
  </equation>

  Here is the proof for the marker-valued factors. Work in the commutative
  algebra generated by the marker variables <math|z<rsub|j>> with
  <math|z<rsub|j><rsup|2>=0>. For <math|I> a subset of markers write
  <math|z<rsub|I>=<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>I>z<rsub|j>>. The norm
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<big|sum><rsub|I>a<rsub|I>*z<rsub|I>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<omega\>>=<big|sum><rsub|I><around|\||a<rsub|I>|\|>*\<omega\><rsup|<around|\||I|\|>>>
  is submultiplicative for every <math|\<omega\>\<gtr\>0>: a pair of
  monomials with overlapping marker sets multiplies to zero, and otherwise,
  for disjoint <math|I,J>, the weight <math|\<omega\><rsup|<around|\||I\<cup\>J|\|>>=\<omega\><rsup|<around|\||I|\|>>*\<omega\><rsup|<around|\||J|\|>>>
  is the product of their weights. No bound <math|\<omega\>\<leqslant\>1> is
  used. Every moved piece is a single <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>=z<rsub|I<rsub|j>>*h<rsub|j>>
  with <math|I<rsub|j>\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>. Thus
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|j><rsup|2>=0> and <math|exp
  <around|(|\<ell\><rsub|j>|)>=1+\<ell\><rsub|j>>. The mask difference
  defining <math|h<rsub|j>> vanishes whenever the enlarged support is
  healthy, so a nonzero moved factor has a flag in its full support.

  Expand the exponential of the sum of the moved pieces as the product of the
  factors <math|1+\<ell\><rsub|j>> and multiply by the old forced gas. In a
  nonzero summand each moved factor meets an old forced support: its flag is
  covered by the old forced inventories. Taking connected components of the
  full-support intersection graph and flattening them back therefore gives
  the bijection of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:soft-log-fusion>. This
  argument preserves the inventory and the product in the marker algebra.
  Overlapping marker sets contribute zero on both sides. The expansion is
  justified by absolute convergence in the weighted norm, since the
  constituent prices are summable on the finite spatial lattice.

  Assign inflated weights <math|e<rsup|g>*p<rsub|i>> to old forced pieces of
  price <math|p<rsub|i>> and unchanged weights to the moved pieces. At
  exponent <math|e+1> the site sum is at most
  <math|\<Gamma\>=e<rsup|g>*s+n*Z*R<rsub|e+1>> by
  <eqref|eq:allarity-spatial-incidence>. Every surviving connected component
  contains an old forced piece, so its product norm is bounded by
  <math|e<rsup|-g>> times the product of the inflated weights. The distinct
  connected-set bound <eqref|eq:ope-forest-price>, with reserve one and
  counting factor <math|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>>, bounds the new site
  price at exponent <math|e> by <math|e<rsup|-g>*<around|(|e<rsup|\<Gamma\>>-1|)>>.
  Since <math|\<Gamma\>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>>, this proves
  <eqref|eq:allarity-realign-forced-output>. The regrouping does not change
  the archived background: setting all markers to zero kills every moved
  factor, leaving the original background at its original address. Thus a
  regular remainder price <math|z> becomes at most
  <math|z+2<rsup|n>*<wide|\<omega\>|\<bar\>><rsub|n>*R<rsub|e>*y>, by
  <eqref|eq:allarity-realign-regular>, while the forced price changes as
  displayed, and the polynomial density is unchanged.

  <subsubsection|The recurrence along the comparison interval>

  Fix a top comparison depth <math|k>, an intermediate level <math|l>, a
  larger arity allowance <math|u\<geqslant\>max <around|(|2,n|)>>, and a
  monitor <math|H\<gtr\>1> fixed by <math|k>. Here <math|k,l,u,n> are natural
  numbers with <math|l\<leqslant\>k>, and all scalar price bounds are
  nonnegative. Let the tolerance exponent be <math|q\<geqslant\>6> and the
  regular step multiplier be <math|\<alpha\>\<geqslant\>0>. Set

  <\equation*>
    d<rsub|0>=H<rsup|-q>,<space|2em>d<rsub|j>=<around|(|\<alpha\>+2|)><rsup|j>*d<rsub|0>.
  </equation*>

  The principal sources are the connected polynomials of the same Gaussian
  history at the current level, with the same cell and marker labels. At the
  terminal comparison level their higher connected coefficients vanish: the
  formal logarithm of <math|<big|prod><rsub|i><around|(|1+z<rsub|i>*P<rsub|i>|)>>
  is <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>z<rsub|i>*P<rsub|i>>. Its singleton terms are
  exactly the launched calibrated insertions. Consequently the initial marked
  representation has regular remainder price <math|d<rsub|0>>, after the
  specified terminal-source purchase, which must also bound its initial
  forced price by <math|G<rsub|k>>.

  Suppose the actual one-step marked compiler at each level has the following
  quantitative outputs when its incoming remainder price is
  <math|z\<geqslant\>d<rsub|0>>. Before realignment it supplies remainder
  price at most <math|<around|(|\<alpha\>+1|)>*z>, principal incidence price
  at most <math|H<rsup|-2>>, and forced price at most
  <math|G<rsub|i><rsup|2>> at the coarse level. Here
  <math|G<rsub|i>=e<rsup|-g<rsub|i>>\<leqslant\>1> is the required final
  forced price. Its projected principal polynomial differs from the next
  prescribed connected polynomial by at most <math|y> on every required
  healthy region. Purchase

  <\gather>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|2<rsup|n>*<wide|\<omega\>|\<bar\>><rsub|n>*R<rsub|e>*y\<leqslant\>d<rsub|0>,<space|2em>G<rsub|i>+n*H<rsup|-2>*R<rsub|e+1>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1>,<eq-number><label|eq:allarity-step-purchases>>>|<row|<cell|<around|(|2*B<rsub|i>+1|)><rsup|4>*H<rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>e<rsup|H<rsup|-1>>-1\<leqslant\>1.<eq-number><label|eq:allarity-step-small>>>>>
  </gather>

  The forced inequality is <eqref|eq:allarity-realign-forced-input> with
  <math|s=G<rsub|i><rsup|2>>, <math|g=g<rsub|i>> and
  <math|\<delta\>=H<rsup|-1>>. Its output is at most <math|G<rsub|i>>. The
  regular price becomes at most

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|\<alpha\>+1|)>*z+d<rsub|0>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<alpha\>+2|)>*z.
  </equation*>

  Induction therefore bounds the remainder at level <math|i> by
  <math|d<rsub|k-i>>, with forced price <math|G<rsub|i>> and the exact
  prescribed principal polynomial. At the induction step the density is the
  push of the preceding marked density along the same transition kernel; the
  realignment changes only its representation. Hence this is an estimate on
  the original launched observable, not a sequence of separately chosen
  approximants.

  The number of steps satisfies <math|<around|(|\<alpha\>+2|)><rsup|k-l>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<kappa\>>>
  for a constant <math|\<kappa\>>, by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-upper-count-power>
  below. It follows that <math|d<rsub|k-l>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<kappa\>-q>>.

  <paragraph*|The marker weights> Two marker weights are used, each the same
  at every mark, replica and finite period. Put <math|H=\<cal-M\><rsub|k>>,
  where <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|k>=exp <around|{|<around|(|log log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)><rsup|2>|}>> is the clock monitor at the
  comparison depth, as in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:rank-root-normalization>.
  From the launch at the middle level <math|\<ell\>> down to the output torus
  of the replica estimate, the weight is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:allarity-marker-weight>\<omega\>=H<rsup|1/<around|(|8*u|)>>.
  </equation>

  On the leading part of the interval, from the top <math|k> down to
  <math|\<ell\>>, the recurrence above is run at the smaller weight

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:allarity-upper-weight>\<omega\><rsub|->=H<rsup|1/<around|(|8*u|)>-U>,<space|2em>U\<geqslant\><frac|1|8*u>,
  </equation>

  where the weight exponent <math|U> is a constant fixed before the clock
  scale (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-leading-budget> below says how).
  Every price of the recurrence, namely the remainder prices
  <math|d<rsub|j>>, the forced prices <math|G<rsub|i>>, the principal
  incidence price <math|H<rsup|-2>>, the discrepancy contribution
  <eqref|eq:allarity-realign-regular> and the terminal prices, is a price in
  the <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<omega\><rsub|->>>
  norm. The weight is changed exactly once, at the launch
  (Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:allarity-corrected-budget>), and the
  cost of that change is computed there.

  Since <math|U\<geqslant\>1/<around|(|8*u|)>>, we have
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|->\<leqslant\>1>, so <eqref|eq:allarity-mode-weight>
  gives <math|\<omega\><rsub|-><rsup|<around|\||I|\|>>\<leqslant\>1> for
  every mode. Thus the first purchase in <eqref|eq:allarity-step-purchases>
  is implied by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:allarity-weighted-discrepancy>2<rsup|n>*R<rsub|e>*y\<leqslant\>d<rsub|0>=H<rsup|-q>.
  </equation>

  At the large weight, since <math|H\<gtr\>1> and <math|n\<leqslant\>u>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:allarity-weight-cap>\<omega\><rsup|<around|\||I|\|>>\<leqslant\><wide|\<omega\>|\<bar\>><rsub|n>=\<omega\><rsup|n>\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsup|u>=H<rsup|1/8>*<space|2em><around|(|I\<subseteq\><around|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>|)>.
  </equation>

  The incidence and spatial counts, the forced regrouping and the scalar
  envelope below hold for every positive weight. The one-step outputs assumed
  above, the principal-source bounds and the terminal-source purchase are
  hypotheses on prices at the weight <math|\<omega\><rsub|->>. When such an
  input is available as an unweighted bound <math|b> on the values of pieces
  carrying between one and <math|n> markers, with the support charge already
  included, it is also a bound on the <math|\<omega\><rsub|->>-price, because
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|->\<leqslant\>1>.

  If the later propagation requires an extra factor <math|H<rsup|u*V+2>> in
  the regular price, purchase <math|q\<geqslant\>\<kappa\>+u*V+2>, with
  <math|\<kappa\>> the exponent of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-upper-count-power>;
  this supplies that margin at the middle level. These two inequalities are
  separate purchases: a small initial error does not alone control
  arbitrarily many steps or an arbitrary later marker-weight change.

  <subsubsection|Supplying the one-step hypotheses on the leading
  interval><label|subsec:allarity-step-supply>

  We now show how the one-step hypotheses of the recurrence follow, on the
  leading part of the comparison interval, from the upper calculation of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-mixed-marked-compiler> and from the
  principal comparison of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:principal-gaussian-comparison>.
  We also show that the purchases <eqref|eq:allarity-step-purchases>\U<eqref|eq:allarity-step-small>
  hold at large clock scale. Throughout, the top comparison depth is
  <math|k>, the leading interval consists of the rungs <math|i> with
  <math|\<ell\>\<leqslant\>i\<less\>k>, and
  <math|s=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>> is the clock scale. We put

  <\equation*>
    H=exp <around|(|<around|(|log log s|)><rsup|2>|)>=\<cal-M\><rsub|k>,<space|2em>\<omega\><rsub|->=H<rsup|1/<around|(|8*u|)>-U>,
  </equation*>

  as in <eqref|eq:allarity-upper-weight>. The rung couplings <math|x<rsub|i>>
  (coarse) and <math|y<rsub|i>> (fine) of rung <math|i> lie in
  <math|<around|[|s/2,s|]>>. The arity <math|n\<leqslant\>u>, the support
  exponent <math|e>, the slice multiplier <math|a>, the support radius
  <math|Q\<geqslant\>a>, the weight exponent <math|U>, the tolerance exponent
  <math|q\<geqslant\>6> and the step multiplier
  <math|\<alpha\>\<geqslant\>8*T<rsub|e>> are fixed before <math|s>, in this
  order except that <math|\<alpha\>> may be fixed right after <math|e> and
  the spare exponent <math|\<sigma\>> of Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:allarity-corrected-budget>.
  Here <math|T<rsub|e>> is the constant of <eqref|eq:mixed-cover-charge>. A
  function <math|f> of the clock scale has <em|upper exponent> <math|c> if
  for every <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, eventually
  <math|<around|\||f<around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|c+\<varepsilon\>>>
  uniformly for <math|x\<in\><around|[|s/2,s|]>>. In particular
  <math|H<rsup|c>> has upper exponent <math|0> for every real <math|c>,
  because <math|<around|(|log log s|)><rsup|2>=o<around|(|log s|)>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Compiler outputs on a leading rung><label|lem:allarity-step-outputs>Assume
    that, at rung <math|i>, the upper calculation of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-mixed-marked-compiler> applies at
    the scale <math|s> with the multiplier <math|\<alpha\>> and tolerance
    exponent <math|q> fixed above. Assume the incoming data are:

    <\itemize>
      <item>a regular remainder of marker-root price <math|z> with
      <math|H<rsup|-q>\<leqslant\>z\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-4>>;

      <item>a leading family of marker budget at most <math|H<rsup|-4>>;

      <item>a forced background of site price at most
      <math|G<around|(|y<rsub|i>|)>>.
    </itemize>

    All prices are taken at the weight <math|\<omega\><rsub|->>. Then, after
    the marked push, the output has:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>nonleading regular marker-root price at most
      <math|<around|(|\<alpha\>+1|)>*z>;

      <item>leading marker-root (principal incidence) price at most
      <math|H<rsup|-3>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-2>>;

      <item>forced site price at most <math|G<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>>.
    </enumerate>

    Thus the one-step hypotheses of the recurrence hold at rung <math|i> with
    <math|G<rsub|i>=G<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    In the upper calculation the multipliers are <math|l=max
    <around|(|1,H<rsup|-3>/<around|(|\<alpha\>*z+H<rsup|-q>|)>|)>> and
    <math|m=G<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|-2>>. The incoming forced budget is
    <math|\<eta\>=G<around|(|y<rsub|i>|)>>, and the leading-family budget is
    <math|h=H<rsup|-4>>. That calculation proves
    <math|T<rsub|e>*\<eta\>+a\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-3>> and
    <math|\<Sigma\>\<leqslant\>K/4>, with <math|K=H<rsup|-1/8>>. Its rooted
    connected-set bound then gives every marker a total marker-root price of
    at most <math|T<rsub|e>*\<eta\>+a> over the components carrying it. It
    also gives every site a total site price of at most
    <math|\<Sigma\>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<Sigma\>>-1> over the components
    containing it. By <eqref|eq:mixed-club-integral>, the integrated
    coefficient of a component is divided by <math|t<rsub|C>>:

    <\itemize>
      <item><math|t<rsub|C>=1> for an entirely leading component;

      <item><math|t<rsub|C>=l> for an unforced component that is not entirely
      leading;

      <item><math|t<rsub|C>=m> for a component with nonempty forced
      inventory.
    </itemize>

    Hence:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>The nonleading regular components have marker-root price at most
      <math|H<rsup|-3>/l>. Since

      <\equation*>
        l\<geqslant\>H<rsup|-3>/<around|(|\<alpha\>*z+H<rsup|-q>|)>
      </equation*>

      , this is at most <math|\<alpha\>*z+H<rsup|-q>\<leqslant\>\<alpha\>*z+z=<around|(|\<alpha\>+1|)>*z>,
      because <math|z\<geqslant\>H<rsup|-q>>.

      <item>The entirely leading components have marker-root price at most
      <math|T<rsub|e>*\<eta\>+a\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-3>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-2>>,
      since <math|H\<geqslant\>1>.

      <item>The forced components have site price at most
      <math|<around|(|e<rsup|\<Sigma\>>-1|)>/m\<leqslant\><around|(|e<rsup|K/4>-1|)>*G<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>G<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>>,
      because <math|e<rsup|K/4>-1\<leqslant\>K\<leqslant\>1>.
    </enumerate>

    The calculation of that section holds at every positive marker weight,
    since its prices include the weights. So these are prices at the weight
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|->>. The multiplier <math|l> is at most <math|max
    <around|(|1,H<rsup|q-3>|)>>, as in that section, so the compiler purchase
    made there covers it.
  </proof>

  The upper bound <math|z\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-4>> in the lemma is preserved
  along the recurrence. Indeed <math|d<rsub|k-i>\<leqslant\>d<rsub|k-\<ell\>>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<kappa\>-q>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-4>>
  by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-upper-count-power>, once
  <math|q\<geqslant\>\<kappa\>+4>. The lower bound
  <math|z\<geqslant\>d<rsub|0>=H<rsup|-q>> is the convention of the
  recurrence: a smaller incoming price may be enlarged to <math|d<rsub|0>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The leading budget at the small
    weight><label|lem:allarity-leading-budget>Suppose that at every leading
    rung the prescribed principal sources have <em|unweighted> marker budget
    at most <math|H<rsup|A>>, for a constant <math|A\<geqslant\>0> fixed
    before <math|s>. That is, for every mark, the sum over the modes
    containing it of the value bound times the support charge is at most
    <math|H<rsup|A>>. If

    <\equation*>
      U\<geqslant\>A+4+<frac|1|8*u>,
    </equation*>

    then their marker budget at the weight <math|\<omega\><rsub|->> is at
    most <math|H<rsup|-4>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Every principal piece has a nonempty marker set <math|I>, so its weight
    is <math|\<omega\><rsub|-><rsup|<around|\||I|\|>>\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|->=H<rsup|1/<around|(|8*u|)>-U>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-A-4>>,
    because <math|\<omega\><rsub|->\<leqslant\>1>. Multiplying the unweighted
    budget by this factor gives the claim.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The unweighted principal bound><label|lem:allarity-principal-power>Assume
    the hypotheses of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-healthy-cumulant-launch>
    at every rung of the leading interval:

    <\itemize>
      <item>the calibrated incoming polynomials, with
      <math|<around|\||p<rsub|i>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|z>> and degree
      at most <math|K>;

      <item>the polynomial-transport purchase <math|2*<around|(|b+2|)>*log
      A<rsub|n*K>\<leqslant\>log L>;

      <item>the healthy-field bounds <eqref|eq:healthy-middle-purchases> at
      the rung coupling.
    </itemize>

    Then there is a constant <math|A\<geqslant\>0>, depending only on
    <math|n>, <math|z>, <math|K> and the fixed word, such that for all large
    <math|s> the hypothesis of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-leading-budget>
    holds: at every leading rung the prescribed principal sources have
    unweighted marker budget at most <math|H<rsup|A>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    At rung <math|i> the prescribed principal source of mode <math|I> is the
    connected polynomial <math|c<rsub|I>> of the subset moments transported
    over <math|k-i\<leqslant\>k-\<ell\>> steps. By maximality of
    <math|\<ell\>> and the bracket of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-firesky-clock-fields>,
    every block length on <math|<around|[|\<ell\>,k|]>> is at least
    <math|L=L<rsub|J<around|(|k|)>-1>>, so <math|<around|(|k-\<ell\>-1|)>*log
    L\<less\><around|(|b+2|)>*log H>, as in the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-upper-count-power>. With the transport
    purchase at this <math|L>, <math|A<rsub|n*K><rsup|k-i>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|n*K>*<space|0.17em>H<rsup|1/2>\<leqslant\>H>
    eventually. By <eqref|eq:healthy-transport-telescope> and the norm bound
    <math|A<rsub|n*K>> per step, every transported subset moment has norm at
    most <math|R=H<rsup|n*z+1>>, as in that section. The partition expansion
    gives <math|<around|\||c<rsub|I>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>Q<rsub|n><around|(|R|)>>
    and degree at most <math|D<rsub|\<ast\>>>. On the healthy region of its
    prescribed support, the coordinates are bounded by <math|M=max
    <around|(|1,16*B*\<tau\><around|(|t|)>|)>=H<rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>>,
    uniformly for <math|t\<in\><around|[|s/2,s|]>>, by the healthy-field
    construction. For every fixed degree <math|D<rsub|\<ast\>>>, therefore,
    <math|M<rsup|D<rsub|\<ast\>>>\<leqslant\>H> eventually (the degree-zero
    case is immediate). Hence <math|<around|\||c<rsub|I><around|(|X|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>Q<rsub|n><around|(|H<rsup|n*z+1>|)>*H>.
    The support charge is at most <math|R<rsub|e>>
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-step-charge>), and at most
    <math|2<rsup|n>> modes contain a given mark. Since <math|Q<rsub|n>> has
    degree at most <math|n>, the unweighted budget is at most
    <math|2<rsup|n>*R<rsub|e>*Q<rsub|n><around|(|H<rsup|n*z+1>|)>*H\<leqslant\>H<rsup|A>>
    eventually, where <math|A=n*<around|(|n*z+1|)>+2>. In particular this
    exponent does not depend on the Taylor degree <math|K>; only its
    threshold does. This distinction permits the later choice of Taylor order
    after the marker weight and remainder exponent have been fixed.
  </proof>

  <\corollary>
    <dueto|The per-rung amplitude><label|cor:allarity-rung-amplitude>Under
    the hypotheses of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-principal-power>,
    the amplitude <math|\<tau\>> of the masked principal insertion at every
    leading rung is at most <math|H<rsup|A>>. It therefore has upper exponent
    <math|0>, and the amplitude hypothesis of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-step-discrepancy> holds.
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    The insertion is the masked principal piece. It vanishes off the healthy
    region of its prescribed support, and on that region it is bounded by
    <math|<around|\||c<rsub|I><around|(|X|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|A>>, by
    the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-principal-power>. So
    <math|<around|\||h|\|>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|A>> everywhere, and
    <math|H<rsup|A>> has upper exponent <math|0>.
  </proof>

  The launch bounds available for the funded chart bound the principal source
  by a fixed positive power of <math|H>. This is why the leading interval is
  run at the weight <math|\<omega\><rsub|->>: at the large weight
  <math|\<omega\>> the budget <math|H<rsup|-4>> is not available, and at the
  top comparison level the connected polynomial
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>z<rsub|i>*P<rsub|i>> is not small at all.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|A fixed bound for the support charge><label|lem:allarity-step-charge>For
    a cell <math|c> and a nonempty mode <math|I>, let <math|r<rsub|c,j>> be
    the reader site of mark <math|j> in cell <math|c>, and define the
    prescribed support

    <\equation*>
      X<rsub|c,I>=<around|{|v:<around|\||v-r<rsub|c,j>|\|>\<leqslant\>Q*B<text|for
      every >j\<in\>I|}>.
    </equation*>

    Assume that reader sites in distinct replica cells are at distance
    greater than <math|2*Q*B>. Then the geometric hypotheses of the
    subsection on marker and spatial prices hold, and every prescribed
    support satisfies <math|<math-up|cov><rsub|B><space|0.17em>X<rsub|c,I>\<leqslant\><around|(|<around|\<lceil\>|Q|\<rceil\>>+1|)><rsup|4>>.
    Hence one may take

    <\equation*>
      R<rsub|e>=exp <around|{|e*<around|(|<around|\<lceil\>|Q|\<rceil\>>+1|)><rsup|4>|}>,
    </equation*>

    a constant fixed before <math|s>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    By the first subsection, every point of the full support of a principal
    piece of mode <math|I> in cell <math|c> lies within distance
    <math|<around|(|2*n+1|)>*\<rho\>\<leqslant\>a*B\<leqslant\>Q*B> of each
    of its marked readers, which are the <math|r<rsub|c,j>> with
    <math|j\<in\>I>. So the actual support is contained in
    <math|X<rsub|c,I>>. The prescribed support depends only on the mode, by
    definition. If a site <math|v> lies in <math|X<rsub|c,I>> and in
    <math|X<rsub|c<rprime|'>,I<rprime|'>>>, then a reader of <math|c> and a
    reader of <math|c<rprime|'>> are both within <math|Q*B> of <math|v>,
    hence within <math|2*Q*B> of each other. By the separation assumption
    <math|c=c<rprime|'>>. Finally, fix one <math|j\<in\>I>. Then
    <math|X<rsub|c,I>> is contained in the cube of side <math|2*Q*B> centred
    at <math|r<rsub|c,j>>. That cube is covered by a grid of at most
    <math|<around|(|<around|\<lceil\>|Q|\<rceil\>>+1|)><rsup|4>>
    radius-<math|B> cubes whose union is connected. Since
    <math|<math-up|chg><rsub|e>> is monotone in the cover number, the bound
    follows.
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|The prescribed polynomials are reference integrals.> Fix a
  cell and its mark set <math|J>, <math|<around|\||J|\|>=n>, and work in the
  marker algebra <math|<with|math-font|cal*|A><rsub|J>> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-planted-compiler>. For an element
  <math|X> with zero constant coefficient put
  <math|log<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|1+X|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|n><around|(|-1|)><rsup|j-1>*X<rsup|j>/j>.
  Since every product of more than <math|n> nonconstant monomials vanishes,
  <math|exp<rsub|\<ast\>>> and <math|log<rsub|\<ast\>>> are mutually inverse
  bijections between the elements with zero constant coefficient and those
  with constant coefficient one. This is the identity of formal power series,
  truncated. Moreover, if <math|X=<big|sum><rsub|T\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>q<rsub|T>*z<rsub|T>>,
  then <math|X<rsup|j>> is the sum over ordered <math|j>-tuples of pairwise
  disjoint nonempty sets <math|T<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,T<rsub|j>> of
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|l>q<rsub|T<rsub|l>>*z<rsub|T<rsub|1>\<cup\>\<cdots\>\<cup\>T<rsub|j>>>.
  Each unordered partition with <math|j> blocks occurs <math|j>! times, so

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:allarity-moment-cumulant>log<rsub|\<ast\>><around*|(|1+<big|sum><rsub|T\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>q<rsub|T>*z<rsub|T>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|I\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>c<rsub|I>*z<rsub|I>,<space|2em>c<rsub|I>=<big|sum><rsub|\<pi\>\<in\>\<cal-P\><around|(|I|)>><around|(|-1|)><rsup|<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>-1>*<around|(|<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>-1|)>!<big|prod><rsub|B\<in\>\<pi\>>q<rsub|B>.
  </equation>

  These are exactly the connected polynomials of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-healthy-cumulant-launch>.

  At a leading rung <math|i> let <math|D<rsup|<around|(|i|)>>=<big|sum><rsub|I>c<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|I>*z<rsub|I>>
  be the prescribed principal polynomial of the cell. The pieces that are
  actually integrated are masked. Write <math|\<chi\><rsub|I>> for the mask
  of mode <math|I> and <math|D<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|\<chi\>>=<big|sum><rsub|I>\<chi\><rsub|I>*c<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|I>*z<rsub|I>>
  for the masked principal polynomial. Its moment polynomials
  <math|Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>> are defined by

  <\equation*>
    exp<rsub|\<ast\>> D<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|\<chi\>>=1+<big|sum><rsub|T\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>*z<rsub|T>,<space|2em>Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>=<big|sum><rsub|\<pi\>\<in\>\<cal-P\><around|(|T|)>><big|prod><rsub|B\<in\>\<pi\>>\<chi\><rsub|B>*c<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|B>.
  </equation*>

  These are the insertions of both integrals below. The unmasked moment
  polynomials of <math|D<rsup|<around|(|i|)>>> are not used.

  Fix a healthy base configuration <math|x> of the required healthy region.
  Let <math|<wide|\<cal-G\>|~><rsub|i,x>> be the raw reference integral of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:principal-gaussian-comparison> at the rung
  with base configuration <math|x>, that is, integration of the insertion
  against <math|R<rsub|9>*\<chi\><rsub|9>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|C<rsub|0>>>
  followed by the finite forest and tag averages. Let
  <math|<wide|\<cal-A\>|~><rsub|i,x>> be the raw actual-sector integral,
  against <math|R<rsub|6>*\<chi\><rsub|6>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|C<rsub|0>>>
  and the same averages. Both are linear, and we apply them to elements of
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|A><rsub|J>> coefficientwise. Their values are
  jets of order <math|n> at the base point of the local variation about
  <math|x>. On these jets we use the coefficient <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>> norm
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>>> of the truncated Taylor
  polynomial in the parameter <math|y\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|j>>,
  <math|j\<leqslant\>n>. It is submultiplicative for the truncated product,
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|1|\<\|\|\>>=1>, and it dominates the absolute value
  of the zeroth coefficient, which is the value at <math|x>. Since
  <math|j\<leqslant\>n>, it is equivalent to the norm
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|J<rsup|n><rsub|0>>> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:principal-gaussian-comparison> within a
  factor <math|c<rsub|n>> depending only on <math|n>. Put

  <\equation*>
    a<rsub|x>=<wide|\<cal-A\>|~><rsub|i,x><around|(|1|)>,<space|2em>b<rsub|x>=<wide|\<cal-G\>|~><rsub|i,x><around|(|1|)>.
  </equation*>

  The cutoff <math|\<chi\><rsub|9>> can change the mass of the reference
  integral, and <math|b<rsub|x>> may depend on the base parameter. Where
  <math|b<rsub|x>> is invertible, the normalized reference integral is
  <math|\<cal-G\><rsub|i,x>=b<rsub|x><rsup|-1>*<wide|\<cal-G\>|~><rsub|i,x>>,
  so that <math|\<cal-G\><rsub|i,x><around|(|1|)>=1>. We write
  <math|\<cal-G\><rsub|i>> for the family
  <math|<around|(|\<cal-G\><rsub|i,x>|)><rsub|x>>, and similarly
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>>, <math|a> and <math|b>.

  We use the following hypothesis on one common insertion.

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(CI)><em|Common insertion and transport.> At each leading rung of
    the leading interval:

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(a)><em|measure identification>: for the leading letters of each
      cell and every partition product of their pieces, the sector and tag
      measures of <eqref|eq:planted-integral> are those of the principal
      comparison integral <math|<wide|\<cal-A\>|~><rsub|i,x>> on the union of
      the read supports of the pieces in that product, with the same joint
      loading and the same weights;

      <item*|(b)>the Gaussian history is transported by the reference
      integrals on those insertions: at every healthy base configuration
      <math|x> the jet of <math|D<rsup|<around|(|i-1|)>>> at <math|x> is
      <math|log<rsub|\<ast\>> \<cal-G\><rsub|i,x><around*|(|exp<rsub|\<ast\>>
      D<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|\<chi\>>|)>>. This is the Gaussian history
      used at the middle level and at the launch;

      <item*|(c)>the comparison data of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:principal-gaussian-comparison>,
      and the payment that follows it, hold uniformly over the healthy base
      configurations <math|x> of the required healthy region. The insertion
      in that comparison is <math|h=Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>> at the
      base configuration <math|x>, read on the union of the read supports of
      the pieces in each partition product.
    </enumerate>
  </enumerate>

  In the payment paragraph of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:principal-gaussian-comparison>
  every scale except two is a function of the rung coupling alone: the read
  dimension, the direction bound, the aperture, the support radius and the
  good-coordinate radius <math|v>. The two exceptions are the primitive mean
  and covariance bounds <math|J<rsub|0>,V<rsub|0>> of
  <eqref|eq:principal-primitive-comparison>, with their absolute jet bounds.
  These are bounds on actual and reference covariances read at the base
  configuration. Clause (c) therefore amounts to requiring
  <eqref|eq:principal-primitive-comparison> and those absolute jet bounds
  with <math|J<rsub|0>> and <math|V<rsub|0>>, as functions of the rung
  coupling, taken as suprema over the healthy base configurations of the
  region. That section already lists the primitive comparison as an input.

  <subparagraph|Uniformity of the primitive comparison.> There is already a
  producer for the primitive rates: Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:principal-comparison-jets>.
  Its conclusion is uniform in the healthy base field, provided its local
  geometric hypotheses hold. We record exactly the specialization needed
  here. At a fixed stage pair and derivative order <math|n\<geqslant\>2>,
  require, for every coordinate of the retained joint domain
  <math|I<rsub|\<ast\>>>, a nonwrapping allocated healthy halo of radius
  <math|45*<around|(|1+B|)>+4>, disjoint from the sector seeds. Use the same
  forest, tag, selected path and local window rules as that section,
  including the distinct sector and reference windows. Require its positive
  precision and path-derivative purchases through order <math|n+1>, and its
  local direction bounds. These conditions must hold for the cutoff
  coordinates as well as the insertion coordinates.

  Under these hypotheses there are functions
  <math|C<around|(|s|)>,v<around|(|s|)>,t<around|(|s|)>>, independent of the
  period, forest, tag and healthy base point, such that <math|C> has upper
  exponent zero, <math|v> has upper exponent <math|-1/2>, and <math|t> has
  every negative upper exponent. After absorbing the fixed colour-coordinate
  factor into <math|C>, one can use

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:allarity-uniform-primitive-supplier>J<rsub|0><around|(|s|)>=n!2<rsup|n>*C<around|(|s|)>*<around|{|<around|(|t<around|(|s|)>+v<around|(|s|)>|)>*v<around|(|s|)>+v<around|(|s|)><rsup|2>|}>,<space|2em>V<rsub|0><around|(|s|)>=n!2<rsup|n>*<around|{|t<around|(|s|)>+2*C<around|(|s|)>*v<around|(|s|)>|}>.
  </equation>

  Indeed the gauge fixed at each base point places the field in the same
  logarithmic ball of radius <math|v<around|(|s|)>>. The fixed-gauge local
  identification in that section identifies both physical readings with the
  corresponding local formulas on a neighbourhood. Apply
  <eqref|eq:comparison-quadratic-jet-gain> to the mean difference and
  <eqref|eq:comparison-linear-jet-gain> to the covariance difference.
  Although the gauge depends on the base point, it is fixed during parameter
  differentiation and is an isometry on colour space. Thus no derivative of a
  base-dependent gauge choice enters these estimates. Their right sides
  depend only on <math|s> and the purchased stage data; taking suprema over
  all allowed healthy base points leaves the same bounds. The exponent rules
  give <math|-1> for <math|J<rsub|0>> and <math|-1/2> for <math|V<rsub|0>>.
  Multiplying <math|J<rsub|0>> by <math|g<rsup|-1>>, of exponent <math|1/2>,
  gives the required rescaled difference of exponent <math|-1/2>. The
  absolute jets have exponent zero by the same kernel bounds.

  This discharges the analytic uniformity step of (CI)(c) under the listed
  local hypotheses. It does not prove those hypotheses for the chart's
  enlarged cutoff domain. In particular, adding distant cutoff coordinates
  can invalidate the allocated seed-free halo or dimension bound, and one
  cannot replace the prescribed reference windows by the sector windows.
  Checking these geometric and same-history conditions is the remaining
  application of this producer.

  The cutoff <math|\<chi\><rsub|9>> makes <math|\<cal-G\><rsub|i>> a smooth
  function of the base parameter rather than a polynomial, and the jet norm
  also involves its derivatives in that parameter. The bounds on the
  transport and on the reference mass that the comparison needs are
  nevertheless consequences of (CI)(c). We prove them next. Put
  <math|\<tau\><rsub|n>=b<rsub|n>*max <around|(|1,\<tau\>|)><rsup|n>>, where
  <math|b<rsub|n>> is the Bell number and <math|\<tau\>> the amplitude of the
  masked principal pieces.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Jet bounds for the two integrals><label|lem:allarity-reference-bounds>Assume
    (CI)(c). There is a polynomial <math|<wide|P|~><rsub|n>>, depending only
    on <math|n>, with the following property. Put
    <math|K<rsub|0>=c<rsub|n>*<wide|P|~><rsub|n>*<around|(|N,Q,S|)>>, where
    <math|c<rsub|n>> is the norm-equivalence factor above, and

    <\equation*>
      \<beta\><rsub|0>=2*<around|(|n+1|)>*<around|(|2*K<rsub|0>+3|)><rsup|n>,<space|2em>\<gamma\><rsub|0>=\<beta\><rsub|0>*<around|(|K<rsub|0>+1|)>,
    </equation*>

    and let <math|\<Pi\>> be <math|c<rsub|n>> times the multiplier of the
    amplitude in <eqref|eq:principal-comparison-final>, which has upper
    exponent <math|-1/2>. Then <math|K<rsub|0>,\<beta\><rsub|0>,\<gamma\><rsub|0>>
    have upper exponent <math|0>, and for all large <math|s>, every leading
    rung and every healthy base configuration <math|x>:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<wide|\<cal-A\>|~><rsub|i,x><around|(|h|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|0>*sup
      <around|\||h|\|>> for every bounded measurable insertion <math|h>;

      <item>the zeroth coefficient of <math|a<rsub|x>> lies in
      <math|<around|[|1-2*N*e<rsup|-v+M/2>,1|]>>;

      <item><math|b<rsub|x>> is invertible in the truncated jet algebra and
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsub|x><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rsub|0>>;

      <item><math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-G\><rsub|i,x><around|(|h|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|0>*sup
      <around|\||h|\|>> for every bounded measurable insertion <math|h>; in
      particular <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-G\><rsub|i,x><around|(|Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|0>*\<tau\><rsub|n>>.
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    <em|Item 1.> On <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>S>,
    <eqref|eq:principal-density-jets>, the cutoff jet bound of
    hypothesis<nbsp>4 of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:principal-gaussian-comparison>
    and the product rule bound the <math|J<rsup|n><rsub|0>> norm of
    <math|R<rsub|6>*\<chi\><rsub|6>*<around|(|\<cdummy\>,z|)>> by
    <math|<wide|P|~><rsub|n>*<around|(|N,Q,S|)>>, after enlarging the
    polynomial. Beyond that ball the integrand vanishes on a common
    neighbourhood of zero. Differentiation under the integral, as justified
    in that section without derivatives of <math|h>, and integration against
    the probability measure <math|\<gamma\><rsub|C<rsub|0>>> give
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<big|int>R<rsub|6>*\<chi\><rsub|6>*h*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|C<rsub|0>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|J<rsup|n><rsub|0>>\<leqslant\><wide|P|~><rsub|n>*sup
    <around|\||h|\|>>. The forest and tag averages have nonnegative weights
    summing to one, so they preserve this bound. Passing to the Taylor
    <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>> norm costs <math|c<rsub|n>>.

    <em|Item 2.> At the base point the compensated map is the identity,
    <math|T<rsub|y<rsub|0>>*z=z> (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-differentiation>).
    So the Jacobian is one there, <math|w<rsub|6>*<around|(|0,z|)>=z> and
    <math|C<rsub|6><around|(|0|)>=C<rsub|0>>, hence
    <math|R<rsub|6>*<around|(|0,z|)>=1>. The zeroth coefficient of
    <math|a<rsub|x>> is therefore an average of the numbers
    <math|<big|int>\<chi\><rsub|6>*<around|(|0,z|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|C<rsub|0>><around|(|z|)>>.
    They lie in <math|<around|[|0,1|]>> because
    <math|0\<leqslant\>\<chi\><rsub|6>\<leqslant\>1>. They are at least
    <math|\<gamma\><rsub|C<rsub|0>>*<around|{|<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\>v|}>\<geqslant\>1-2*N*e<rsup|-v+M/2>>,
    because <math|\<chi\><rsub|6>=1> there by hypothesis<nbsp>4 and by
    <eqref|eq:principal-outage>.

    <em|Item 3.> By <eqref|eq:principal-comparison-final> with <math|h=1>,
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsub|x>-a<rsub|x>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<Pi\>>.
    The zeroth coefficient <math|b<rsub|0>> of <math|b<rsub|x>> therefore
    satisfies <math|b<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>1-2*N*e<rsup|-v+M/2>-\<Pi\>\<geqslant\><frac|1|2>>
    for large <math|s>, since <math|e<rsup|-v>> has every negative upper
    exponent. Also <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsub|x>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|0>+\<Pi\>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|0>+1>.
    Write <math|b<rsub|x>=b<rsub|0>*<around|(|1+u|)>>. Then <math|u> has zero
    constant coefficient and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|K<rsub|0>+1|)>+1>.
    In the algebra of jets of order <math|n>, a product of <math|n+1>
    elements with zero constant coefficient vanishes. So
    <math|<around|(|1+u|)><rsup|-1>=<big|sum><rsub|r=0><rsup|n><around|(|-u|)><rsup|r>>,
    and

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsub|x><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|0><rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|r=0><rsup|n><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|r>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|n+1|)>*<around|(|2*K<rsub|0>+3|)><rsup|n>=\<beta\><rsub|0>.
    </equation*>

    <em|Item 4.> By <eqref|eq:principal-comparison-final> and item<nbsp>1,
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<wide|\<cal-G\>|~><rsub|i,x><around|(|h|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|K<rsub|0>+\<Pi\>|)>*sup
    <around|\||h|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|K<rsub|0>+1|)>*sup
    <around|\||h|\|>>. Multiply by <math|b<rsub|x><rsup|-1>> and use
    item<nbsp>3. Since <math|<around|\||Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<tau\><rsub|n>>,
    the last claim follows.

    Finally, <math|N>, <math|Q> and <math|S> have upper exponent <math|0> by
    the payment paragraph of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:principal-gaussian-comparison>.
    A fixed polynomial in them, and a fixed power of it, also have upper
    exponent <math|0>.
  </proof>

  Inside the reference integral the masks play no role. Write
  <math|q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>=<big|sum><rsub|\<pi\>\<in\>\<cal-P\><around|(|T|)>><big|prod><rsub|B\<in\>\<pi\>>c<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|B>>
  for the unmasked moment polynomials of <math|D<rsup|<around|(|i|)>>>, so
  that <math|exp<rsub|\<ast\>> D<rsup|<around|(|i|)>>=1+<big|sum><rsub|T>q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>*z<rsub|T>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Masks under the reference cutoff><label|lem:allarity-reference-mask-removal>Assume
    (CI)(c), and that the read support of each masked principal piece
    contains the support <math|X<rsub|c,I>> on which its mask
    <math|\<chi\><rsub|I>> is inspected. Then, for every <math|T> and every
    healthy base configuration <math|x>,

    <\equation*>
      <wide|\<cal-G\>|~><rsub|i,x><around*|(|Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>|)>=<wide|\<cal-G\>|~><rsub|i,x><around*|(|q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>|)>,<space|2em><text|hence><space|2em>\<cal-G\><rsub|i,x><around*|(|exp<rsub|\<ast\>>
      D<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|\<chi\>>|)>=\<cal-G\><rsub|i,x><around*|(|exp<rsub|\<ast\>>
      D<rsup|<around|(|i|)>>|)>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    By (CI)(c) the insertion of the comparison for
    <math|Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>> is read on the union <math|Y> of
    the read supports of the pieces in its partition products. By the second
    identity of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:principal-gaussian-comparison>,
    wherever the reference cutoff <math|\<chi\><rsub|9>> is nonzero, the
    fixed configuration is healthy on the support of the insertion, here
    <math|Y>. Each <math|X<rsub|c,B>> with <math|B\<subseteq\>T> lies in
    <math|Y>, so each mask <math|\<chi\><rsub|B>> equals one there. Hence
    <math|R<rsub|9>*\<chi\><rsub|9>*Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>=R<rsub|9>*\<chi\><rsub|9>*q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>>
    pointwise. Where <math|\<chi\><rsub|9>=0> both sides vanish. Integrate
    and average. The second identity follows coefficientwise, after dividing
    by <math|b<rsub|x>>.
  </proof>

  So in (CI)(b) the masked and unmasked prescribed polynomials give the same
  reference transport. The masked moments are still the insertions of the
  actual-sector integral <math|<wide|\<cal-A\>|~><rsub|i,x>>, where the
  actual cutoff does not force health on the larger prescribed supports. That
  is why the comparison in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-step-discrepancy>
  uses <math|Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>> on both sides.

  Clause (b) defines reference-transported targets recursively, but this
  alone does not give admissible principal pieces at the next rung. The
  normalized cutoff integral can produce smooth nonpolynomial functions of
  the base configuration. At each rung one must preserve a class of
  insertions on which the next transport is defined and prove the required
  support, amplitude, jet and representation bounds there. In particular, the
  polynomial degree bound used in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-principal-power>
  cannot be inferred from the recursion with <math|\<cal-G\><rsub|i>> alone.

  At the middle level, let <math|D<rsub|Q>> be the connected polynomial of
  the actual polynomial history of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-healthy-cumulant-launch>.
  The healthy local error required by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-corrected-pieces>
  concerns the actual planted leading coefficient and this same
  <math|D<rsub|Q>>. If a reference-transported intermediate history is used,
  its error against the planted coefficient and its error against
  <math|D<rsub|Q>> add. For a target tolerance
  <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|\<ast\>>> one must allocate
  <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|planted>>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|transport>>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|\<ast\>>>;
  two separate bounds by <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|\<ast\>>> do not give the
  required bound. The following calculation gives a precise sufficient
  transport budget.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Comparing two finite transport
    histories><label|lem:allarity-two-route-budget>For
    <math|0\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>r> let <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|j>> be normed
    spaces of admissible coefficient functions. Let
    <math|G<rsub|j>,T<rsub|j>:\<cal-B\><rsub|j+1>\<to\>\<cal-B\><rsub|j>> be
    bounded linear maps for <math|j\<less\>r>, with
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|G<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>>. For one
    terminal coefficient <math|f\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|r>> write
    <math|f<rsub|j>=T<rsub|j>*\<cdots\>*T<rsub|r-1>*f> and suppose
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|G<rsub|j>-T<rsub|j>|)>*f<rsub|j+1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|j>>.
    Then

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|G<rsub|0>*\<cdots\>*G<rsub|r-1>*f-T<rsub|0>*\<cdots\>*T<rsub|r-1>*f|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|r-1>\<delta\><rsub|j>*<big|prod><rsub|a=0><rsup|j-1>C<rsub|a>.
    </equation*>

    Use the empty-product convention <math|1>. Suppose this holds for all
    nonempty moment coefficients in a marker algebra on <math|n> marks. If
    the two resulting moments are <math|1+X> and <math|1+Y>, and
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|X|\<\|\|\>>,<around|\<\|\|\>|Y|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>R>
    in its submultiplicative coefficient norm, then

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|log<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|1+X|)>-log<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|1+Y|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|q=0><rsup|n-1>R<rsup|q>|)>*<around|\<\|\|\>|X-Y|\<\|\|\>>.
    </equation*>

    For the unweighted sum norm on marker coefficients, the first bound
    therefore gives a connected-coefficient error at most

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|2<rsup|n>-1|)><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|q=0><rsup|n-1>R<rsup|q>|)>*<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|r-1>\<delta\><rsub|j>*<big|prod><rsub|a=0><rsup|j-1>C<rsub|a>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Replace the maps in the product one at a time, starting from the left:

    <\equation*>
      G<rsub|0>*\<cdots\>*G<rsub|r-1>-T<rsub|0>*\<cdots\>*T<rsub|r-1>=<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|r-1>G<rsub|0>*\<cdots\>*G<rsub|j-1>*<around|(|G<rsub|j>-T<rsub|j>|)>*T<rsub|j+1>*\<cdots\>*T<rsub|r-1>.
    </equation*>

    Apply this identity to <math|f> and take norms. For the logarithms use
    <math|X<rsup|q>-Y<rsup|q>=<big|sum><rsub|a=0><rsup|q-1>X<rsup|a>*<around|(|X-Y|)>*Y<rsup|q-1-a>>.
    Submultiplicativity bounds this by <math|q*R<rsup|q-1>*<around|\<\|\|\>|X-Y|\<\|\|\>>>;
    the factor <math|q> cancels the denominator in <math|log<rsub|\<ast\>>>.
    Summing over <math|1\<leqslant\>q\<leqslant\>n> proves the second bound,
    and summing the at most <math|2<rsup|n>-1> moment errors proves the last
    one.
  </proof>

  For the intended application <math|G<rsub|j>=\<cal-G\><rsub|j>> and
  <math|T<rsub|j>=\<cal-T\><rsub|j>>, with the same initial moments and
  normalization. The spaces <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|j>> must include both
  histories and all intermediate functions in the telescoping identity. A jet
  bound for one fixed insertion is not an operator bound on such a space. If
  masks are removed using Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-reference-mask-removal>,
  that identity must hold at each intermediate insertion. After checking
  these domain conditions, making the last displayed quantity at most
  <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|transport>>> gives the allocated
  middle-level error. Neither the required operator products nor this error
  purchase has been established here for the actual chart history.

  The polynomial step of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-healthy-cumulant-launch>
  is a finite linear substitution followed by Wick contraction with the
  reference covariance. Write <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|i>> for it. If (b) is
  stated with <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|i>> in place of <math|\<cal-G\><rsub|i>>,
  the proof below acquires the additional term
  <math|max<rsub|T><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-G\><rsub|i,x><around|(|Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>|)>-\<cal-T\><rsub|i><around|(|Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>|)>|\<\|\|\>>>.
  A small difference does not replace the norm bound of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-reference-bounds>(4) by itself. That
  route needs such a bound for one of the two transports and a bound on the
  difference with upper exponent below zero. By
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-reference-mask-removal>, no insertion
  mask survives in the reference integral. The remaining difference includes
  both the reference cutoff and the nonlinear physical coordinate reading.
  The local comparison below treats these separately; no affineness of the
  compensated coordinate map is required.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The prescribed polynomial is the reference
    integral><label|lem:allarity-prescribed-reference>Under (CI)(b), at every
    leading rung and every healthy base configuration <math|x>, the moment
    polynomials of the jet of <math|D<rsup|<around|(|i-1|)>>> at <math|x> are
    <math|\<cal-G\><rsub|i,x><around|(|Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>|)>>:

    <\equation*>
      exp<rsub|\<ast\>> D<rsup|<around|(|i-1|)>>=1+<big|sum><rsub|T\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>\<cal-G\><rsub|i,x><around|(|Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>|)>*<space|0.17em>z<rsub|T>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Apply the linear map <math|\<cal-G\><rsub|i,x>> coefficientwise to
    <math|exp<rsub|\<ast\>> D<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|\<chi\>>>, use
    <math|\<cal-G\><rsub|i,x><around|(|1|)>=1>, and then
    <math|exp<rsub|\<ast\>> log<rsub|\<ast\>>=<math-up|id>> on elements with
    constant coefficient one.
  </proof>

  Clause (a) of (CI) is a consequence of the realignment and of the planted
  identity. Only the first leading rung needs a separate check.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The common insertion after realignment><label|lem:allarity-common-insertion-realigned>Let
    <math|i> be a leading rung. Suppose that the leading family entering rung
    <math|i> is the output of the realignment of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:allarity-realign> at the preceding
    rung, with desired principal sources <math|F<rsub|c,I>=c<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|I>>,
    or, at the first leading rung, that it consists of one piece
    <math|\<chi\><rsub|I>*c<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|I>*z<rsub|<around|(|c,I|)>>>
    per cell <math|c> and mode <math|I>. Assume (CI)(a) at rung <math|i>.
    Then the entirely leading components of each cell <math|c>, produced by
    the compiler through <eqref|eq:planted-selection>\U<eqref|eq:planted-integral>,
    integrate to <math|\<iota\><rsub|c>*<wide|\<cal-A\>|~><rsub|i,x><around*|(|exp<rsub|\<ast\>>
    D<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|\<chi\>>|)>>, with the masked insertions
    <math|Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    <em|The leading family.> On the healthy region of <math|X<rsub|c,I>>, the
    realignment writes the sum of the leading pieces of mode
    <math|<around|(|c,I|)>> as <math|F<rsub|c,I>+L<rsub|c,I>>. It moves
    <math|L<rsub|c,I>>, with the mask of <math|X<rsub|c,I>>, into the regular
    remainder, and moves the part of the original masked values off that
    healthy region into the forced alphabet. What remains designated leading
    at mode <math|<around|(|c,I|)>> is therefore the single piece
    <math|\<chi\><rsub|I>*F<rsub|c,I>*z<rsub|<around|(|c,I|)>>=\<chi\><rsub|I>*c<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|I>*z<rsub|<around|(|c,I|)>>>,
    where <math|\<chi\><rsub|I>> is the healthy mask of <math|X<rsub|c,I>>.
    In the cell <math|c> the leading family is thus
    <math|\<iota\><rsub|c><around|(|D<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|\<chi\>>|)>>.
    At the first leading rung this is the hypothesis.

    <em|The integral.> Apply <eqref|eq:planted-selection> and
    <eqref|eq:planted-integral> to the leading letters alone, with
    <math|T=<around|{|c|}>\<times\>J>. The selected pieces are those of cell
    <math|c>, so the planted polynomial is
    <math|P<rsub|T>=D<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|\<chi\>>>. Its values are the
    masked values, as shown after <eqref|eq:planted-selection>. By
    <eqref|eq:planted-exp> the product of the factors
    <math|1+\<chi\><rsub|I>*c<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|I>*z<rsub|I>> is
    <math|exp<rsub|\<ast\>> D<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|\<chi\>>>, whose
    coefficients are the insertions <math|Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>>.
    The identity <eqref|eq:planted-integral> holds with the sector and tag
    measures and weights supplied with it. By (CI)(a), for each partition
    product these are the measures, joint loading and weights of
    <math|<wide|\<cal-A\>|~><rsub|i,x>> on the union of the read supports of
    its pieces. By the first identity of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:principal-gaussian-comparison>,
    that integral is integration against <math|R<rsub|6>*\<chi\><rsub|6>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|C<rsub|0>>>
    followed by the forest and tag averages. The planted identity alone does
    not make this identification. Hence the entirely leading part of the cell
    integrates to <math|\<iota\><rsub|c>*<wide|\<cal-A\>|~><rsub|i,x><around|(|exp<rsub|\<ast\>>
    D<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|\<chi\>>|)>>, with the insertions
    <math|Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>>.
  </proof>

  Consequently, under (CI)(a), the leading part of every cell integrates as
  stated at every leading rung, provided that the leading family has the
  displayed form at the first one. There the leading family is the top-level
  principal insertion. By the top initialization at the end of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-healthy-cumulant-launch>, it consists
  of the calibrated singletons, one per mark, with the support, marker and
  masked value of the terminal birth term. All connected coefficients with at
  least two markers vanish. So the displayed form holds at the first rung,
  with <math|c<rsub|<around|{|j|}>>> the calibrated singleton and
  <math|c<rsub|I>=0> for <math|<around|\||I|\|>\<geqslant\>2>, provided the
  mask of each birth term is inspected on its prescribed support
  <math|X<rsub|c,<around|{|j|}>>>. Otherwise a realignment with zero
  discrepancy at the top level moves the difference of the two masks into the
  forced alphabet, at the price of the forced regrouping. This mask condition
  is the part of the terminal-source purchase used here.

  Clause (a) concerns the measure after all sector cutoffs have been
  included. Projection of the Gaussian alone does not determine this measure.
  The following elementary identity states exactly what must be retained.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Projection of a sector-cut Gaussian><label|lem:allarity-cutoff-marginal>Let
    <math|Z> have a centred Gaussian law <math|\<gamma\><rsub|C>> on a finite
    coordinate space, let <math|H> have a probability Haar law <math|\<rho\>>
    independent of <math|Z>, and let <math|0\<leqslant\>\<chi\>*<around|(|Z,H|)>\<leqslant\>1>
    be measurable. For a coordinate set <math|I> put <math|X=Z<rsub|I>> and
    <math|C<rsub|I>=C\|<rsub|I\<times\>I>>. There is a measurable function
    <math|0\<leqslant\><wide|\<chi\>|\<bar\>><rsub|I>*<around|(|x,h|)>\<leqslant\>1>,
    defined almost everywhere, such that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:allarity-cutoff-pushforward><around|(|Z,H|)>\<mapsto\><around|(|Z<rsub|I>,H|)><space|1em><text|pushes
      ><space|1em>\<chi\>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|C>*<space|0.17em>d*\<rho\><space|1em><text|to><space|1em><wide|\<chi\>|\<bar\>><rsub|I>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|C<rsub|I>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<rho\>.
    </equation>

    It is the conditional expectation of <math|\<chi\>> given
    <math|<around|(|Z<rsub|I>,H|)>>. If <math|\<chi\>> already depends only
    on <math|<around|(|Z<rsub|I>,H|)>>, then
    <math|<wide|\<chi\>|\<bar\>><rsub|I>=\<chi\>>. For positive definite
    <math|C>, writing <math|J=I<rsup|c>>, an explicit version is

    <\equation*>
      <wide|\<chi\>|\<bar\>><rsub|I>*<around|(|x,h|)>=<big|int>\<chi\>*<around|(|x,z,h|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|S>*<around|(|z-C<rsub|J*I>*C<rsub|I><rsup|-1>*x|)>,<space|2em>S=C<rsub|J*J>-C<rsub|J*I>*C<rsub|I><rsup|-1>*C<rsub|I*J>.
    </equation*>

    The pushforward identity includes the constant insertion <math|1>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|\<mu\>> be the law of <math|<around|(|Z<rsub|I>,H|)>> and let
    <math|\<nu\>> be the pushforward of <math|\<chi\>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|C>*<space|0.17em>d*\<rho\>>.
    For every measurable set <math|B>, <math|0\<leqslant\>\<nu\><around|(|B|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around|(|B|)>>,
    so the Radon\UNikodym derivative <math|d*\<nu\>/d*\<mu\>> exists and lies
    between zero and one. This proves <eqref|eq:allarity-cutoff-pushforward>
    and its characterization by conditional expectation. The unweighted
    marginal is <math|\<gamma\><rsub|C<rsub|I>>\<otimes\>\<rho\>>, since
    linear projections of a Gaussian have the restricted covariance and
    <math|H> is independent. The identity for integrals follows first for
    indicators, then for simple functions and by dominated convergence for
    bounded measurable insertions. It also holds for every absolutely
    integrable insertion by truncation. If <math|\<chi\>> is measurable in
    the retained coordinates it can be taken outside the conditional
    integral. Finally, when <math|C> is positive definite, completing the
    square in the joint Gaussian density gives conditional mean
    <math|C<rsub|J*I>*C<rsub|I><rsup|-1>*x> and covariance <math|S>, proving
    the formula. The Radon\UNikodym argument does not require positive
    definiteness.
  </proof>

  For example, with independent standard Gaussian coordinates <math|X,Y>, a
  cutoff <math|\<chi\>*<around|(|X,Y|)>=\<psi\><around|(|Y|)>> projects to
  the constant <math|<big|int>\<psi\>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|1>>, not
  to <math|1>. If the conditional law of the omitted variables varies with a
  coarse parameter, the projected cutoff can vary with that parameter too.
  Bounds on the full cutoff do not by themselves give its projected
  parameter-jet bounds.

  <subparagraph|Which sector law has to be identified.> The measure in
  (CI)(a) is the measure of the selected principal component, not the
  complete interacting block density. The distinction can already be seen in
  the dictionary of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-reassembly>. Its
  full integrand contains the compulsory packet factors and <math|exp
  <around|(|<big|sum><rsub|i>f<rsub|i>|)>> in addition to the Gaussian and
  local cutoffs. Neither factor is generally constant in the Gaussian
  variables. It cannot be absorbed into the common scalar
  <math|\<Lambda\><around|(|y|)>> of (M). Setting a body's marker variables
  to zero does not remove its unmarked activity; selecting the component with
  no body letters does.

  For clarity, consider a fixed principal-only component whose integrand,
  before Gaussian integration, is

  <\equation*>
    <big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>A>\<chi\><rsub|a>*<around|(|z,h|)>*f<rsub|a>*<around|(|U*<around|(|z,h|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here the product over other selected dictionary letters is empty and
  therefore equals one. Let <math|I> contain every real coordinate inspected
  by every factor, including the <math|\<chi\><rsub|a>>. The joint integral
  is exactly

  <\equation*>
    <big|int><big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>A>\<chi\><rsub|a>*<around|(|z<rsub|I>,h|)>*f<rsub|a>*<around|(|U*<around|(|z<rsub|I>,h|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|C<rsub|I>><around|(|z<rsub|I>|)>*d*\<rho\><around|(|h|)>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed the characteristic-function marginal calculation in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-differentiation> projects the
  Gaussian; the integrand already depends only on the retained variables, so
  no conditional cutoff remains. If the local coordinate display is
  independent of a frozen Haar variable, its probability integral is one;
  otherwise that variable stays in <math|h>. No product of marginal Gaussian
  laws has been substituted for this joint law. In the singleton case the
  same argument applies to <math|\<chi\><rsub|a>*f<rsub|a>>, including its
  entire local cutoff. The compensated change of variables then gives the
  first integrand of <eqref|eq:principal-two-integrands> exactly.

  This calculation explains how a local principal law can have the form (M)
  without the full sector density having that form. To apply it to a
  partition product, the component construction must give the displayed
  principal-only integrand on the union of its reads, with the same sector,
  forest, tag and loading for all factors. A singleton identity alone does
  not establish that property for a product. Nor may its unit normalization
  be replaced by the full interacting partition function. These are the
  precise component-selection and normalization matches required in (CI)(a);
  enlarging a read set cannot fix a mismatch in them.

  Here is a sufficient version of the chart-law input. Its domain is chosen
  once for a cell, before selecting a partition product.

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(M)>Fix a leading rung and a cell. On a common neighbourhood of
    each healthy base parameter <math|y=0>, the planted sector/tag measure,
    indexed by <math|t>, is

    <\equation*>
      d*\<nu\><rsub|t,y>=\<Lambda\><around|(|y|)>*\<chi\><rsub|6,t>*<around|(|y,z,h|)>*d*\<gamma\><rsub|C<rsub|t><around|(|y|)>><around|(|z|)>*d*\<rho\><rsub|t><around|(|h|)>.
    </equation*>

    The smooth factor <math|\<Lambda\><around|(|y|)>\<gtr\>0> is independent
    of <math|t> and of the insertion, including the unit. The forest/tag
    weights <math|\<lambda\><rsub|t>> are the same as those of the principal
    comparison, are fixed on this neighbourhood, and are nonnegative with sum
    one. There is a finite coordinate set <math|I<rsub|\<ast\>>>, independent
    of <math|y> on this neighbourhood, containing every coordinate inspected
    by every partition-product insertion and by <math|\<chi\><rsub|6,t>>, for
    all retained <math|t>. On this enlarged domain the compensated identities
    <eqref|eq:principal-two-integrands> and all comparison data (CI)(c) hold,
    including the read-dimension and parameter-jet bounds.
  </enumerate>

  One may instead keep a smaller read domain only after proving that its
  cutoff-weighted pushforward in <eqref|eq:allarity-cutoff-pushforward> is
  the law used by the principal comparison, with that law's quantitative jet
  bounds. A merely unweighted Gaussian marginal identity does not suffice.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Measure identification from the joint chart
    law><label|lem:allarity-measure-identification>Under (M), every
    partition-product insertion <math|h>, including <math|h=1>, satisfies, on
    the common parameter neighbourhood,

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|t>\<lambda\><rsub|t>*<big|int>h*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|t,y>=\<Lambda\><around|(|y|)>*<wide|\<cal-A\>|~><rsub|i,x><around|(|h|)><around|(|y|)>.
    </equation*>

    Consequently (CI)(a) holds with the common loading retained. If the raw
    unit integral is invertible as a jet, normalization cancels that loading
    in the full jet algebra, not just at its zeroth coefficient.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For fixed <math|t,y,h>, both the insertion and the cutoff are measurable
    in <math|<around|(|z<rsub|I<rsub|\<ast\>>>,h<rprime|'>|)>>, where
    <math|h<rprime|'>> denotes the frozen Haar variable. Apply
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-cutoff-marginal> on
    <math|I<rsub|\<ast\>>>: the joint Gaussian marginal has covariance
    <math|C<rsub|t><around|(|y|)>\|<rsub|I<rsub|\<ast\>>\<times\>I<rsub|\<ast\>>>>
    and the cutoff is unchanged. No independence between the pieces'
    coordinates is used. The compensated change of variables on this same
    domain gives <math|R<rsub|6>*\<chi\><rsub|6>*h*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|C<rsub|0>>>
    by (M). Integrate the frozen variables and average with the prescribed
    weights. The factor <math|\<Lambda\><around|(|y|)>> can be pulled out of
    this average precisely because it is independent of <math|t>. This proves
    the displayed identity, also for <math|h=1> on the identical domain and
    law. Smoothness and the common dominated differentiation hypotheses give
    the identity of jets.

    Write <math|a> for the jet of <math|<wide|\<cal-A\>|~><rsub|i,x><around|(|1|)>>
    and <math|L> for the jet of <math|\<Lambda\>>. Since
    <math|\<Lambda\><around|(|0|)>\<gtr\>0>, <math|L> is invertible: divide
    by its constant coefficient and use the finite Neumann series for its
    nilpotent part. Hence, whenever <math|a> is invertible,

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|L*a|)><rsup|-1>*<space|0.17em>L*<wide|\<cal-A\>|~><rsub|i,x><around|(|h|)>=a<rsup|-1>*<wide|\<cal-A\>|~><rsub|i,x><around|(|h|)>.
    </equation*>

    This is the required normalization, with the same unit insertion.
  </proof>

  If a loading <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|t>> depends on the sector or tag, it
  cannot in general be pulled out of the average. The correct normalized
  integral is then

  <\equation*>
    <around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|t>\<lambda\><rsub|t>*\<Lambda\><rsub|t>*<wide|\<cal-A\>|~><rsub|t><around|(|1|)>|)><rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|t>\<lambda\><rsub|t>*\<Lambda\><rsub|t>*<wide|\<cal-A\>|~><rsub|t><around|(|h|)>.
  </equation*>

  Any change to the averaging weights must preserve this formula and its
  jets. The sector expansion of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-reassembly>
  must supply (M), or this more general formula, on the actual course.
  Enlarging the domain to include the cutoff also requires checking the
  read-dimension and comparison bounds on that larger domain. Neither
  identification is proved by the planted marker identity alone.

  By Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-common-insertion-realigned> the
  output leading letters of the cell are the coefficients of
  <math|log<rsub|\<ast\>><around*|(|\<cal-A\><rsub|i><around|(|exp<rsub|\<ast\>>
  D<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|\<chi\>>|)>|)>>, where
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|i,x>=a<rsub|x><rsup|-1>*<wide|\<cal-A\>|~><rsub|i,x>>
  is the normalized actual-sector integral. The scalar factor
  <math|a<rsub|x>> belongs to the unmarked normalization. Its invertibility
  is part of the next proof.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Inverse of a jet close to one><label|lem:allarity-jet-inverse>Let
    <math|a> be a jet of order <math|m> with
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|a-1|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<Pi\>\<leqslant\>1/2>.
    Then <math|a> is invertible in the truncated jet algebra,
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|a<rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2> and
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|a<rsup|-1>-1|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*\<Pi\>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Put <math|u=1-a>, so <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<Pi\>>.
    The series <math|<big|sum><rsub|k\<geqslant\>0>u<rsup|k>> converges
    absolutely by submultiplicativity, and its sum <math|v> satisfies
    <math|a*v=v*a=1>. Moreover <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|1-\<Pi\>|)><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>2>
    and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v-1|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<Pi\>*<around|(|1-\<Pi\>|)><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>2*\<Pi\>>.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The per-step discrepancy><label|lem:allarity-step-discrepancy>Assume
    (CI). Suppose that, at each leading rung, the amplitude <math|\<tau\>> of
    the masked principal pieces has upper exponent <math|0>, uniformly in the
    rungs. Then, eventually in <math|s>, the actual projected polynomial and
    the next prescribed polynomial differ by at most <math|y> on the required
    healthy regions, where <math|y> satisfies
    <eqref|eq:allarity-weighted-discrepancy>. Hence the first purchase in
    <eqref|eq:allarity-step-purchases> holds.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Fix a healthy base configuration <math|x>; by (CI)(c) all constants below
    are uniform in <math|x>. Each <math|Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>> is a
    sum, over the at most <math|b<rsub|n>> set partitions of <math|T>, of
    products of at most <math|n> masked principal pieces. As an insertion it
    is measurable and bounded by <math|\<tau\><rsub|n>>. By
    <eqref|eq:principal-comparison-final> and the payment paragraph after it,
    applied with the base configuration <math|x> to the insertions
    <math|Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>> and <math|1>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<wide|\<cal-A\>|~><rsub|i,x><around|(|Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>|)>-<wide|\<cal-G\>|~><rsub|i,x><around|(|Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<tau\><rsub|n>*\<Pi\>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|a<rsub|x>-b<rsub|x>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<Pi\>,
    </equation*>

    with <math|\<Pi\>> as in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-reference-bounds>,
    uniformly in the period and after the averages.

    <em|Normalization.> Write <math|a<rsub|x>=b<rsub|x>*<around|(|1+u|)>>
    with <math|u=b<rsub|x><rsup|-1>*<around|(|a<rsub|x>-b<rsub|x>|)>>. By
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-reference-bounds>(3),
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rsub|0>*\<Pi\>>.
    Once <math|\<beta\><rsub|0>*\<Pi\>\<leqslant\>1/2>,
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-jet-inverse> shows that <math|1+u> is
    invertible with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|1+u|)><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2>.
    Hence <math|a<rsub|x>> is invertible and
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|a<rsub|x><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*\<beta\><rsub|0>>.
    For every insertion <math|h>,

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-A\><rsub|i,x><around|(|h|)>-\<cal-G\><rsub|i,x><around|(|h|)>=a<rsub|x><rsup|-1>*<around*|[|<around|(|<wide|\<cal-A\>|~><rsub|i,x>-<wide|\<cal-G\>|~><rsub|i,x>|)><around|(|h|)>-<around|(|a<rsub|x>-b<rsub|x>|)>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-G\><rsub|i,x><around|(|h|)>|]>,
    </equation*>

    as one checks by multiplying out and using
    <math|<wide|\<cal-G\>|~><rsub|i,x>=b<rsub|x>*\<cal-G\><rsub|i,x>>. With
    <math|h=Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>> and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-reference-bounds>(4),

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-A\><rsub|i,x><around|(|Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>|)>-\<cal-G\><rsub|i,x><around|(|Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*\<beta\><rsub|0>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|n>*\<Pi\>+\<Pi\>*\<gamma\><rsub|0>*\<tau\><rsub|n>|)>=2*\<beta\><rsub|0>*<around|(|1+\<gamma\><rsub|0>|)>*\<tau\><rsub|n>*\<Pi\>.
    </equation*>

    Hence all coefficients of <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|i,x><around|(|exp<rsub|\<ast\>>
    D<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|\<chi\>>|)>> and of
    <math|\<cal-G\><rsub|i,x><around|(|exp<rsub|\<ast\>>
    D<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|\<chi\>>|)>> have norm at most
    <math|K=<around|(|1+\<gamma\><rsub|0>|)>*\<tau\><rsub|n>> once
    <math|2*\<beta\><rsub|0>*<around|(|1+\<gamma\><rsub|0>|)>*\<Pi\>\<leqslant\>1>.

    <em|Logarithm.> The map <math|1+X\<mapsto\>log<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|1+X|)>>
    is a polynomial of degree at most <math|n> in the coefficients of
    <math|X>, with products taken in the jet algebra. On elements whose
    coefficients have norm at most <math|K\<geqslant\>1> it is Lipschitz in
    the sum of the coefficient norms, with constant at most
    <math|C<rsub|n>*K<rsup|n-1>> for a constant <math|C<rsub|n>> depending
    only on <math|n>. Hence, by (CI)(b),

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||log<rsub|\<ast\>> \<cal-A\><rsub|i,x><around*|(|exp<rsub|\<ast\>>
      D<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|\<chi\>>|)>-D<rsup|<around|(|i-1|)>>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|n>*K<rsup|n-1>*2<rsup|n>\<cdot\>2*\<beta\><rsub|0>*<around|(|1+\<gamma\><rsub|0>|)>*\<tau\><rsub|n>*\<Pi\>=:y,
    </equation*>

    the norm on the left being the sum over the modes of the jet norms of the
    coefficients at <math|x>.

    <em|Values.> The zeroth coefficient of a jet product is the product of
    the zeroth coefficients, so the zeroth coefficient of the left side is
    the same expression evaluated at <math|x>. The jet norm dominates it.
    Hence the actual projected polynomial and <math|D<rsup|<around|(|i-1|)>>>
    differ by at most <math|y> at every healthy base configuration <math|x>,
    which is the bound on the required healthy regions.

    <em|Payment.> Since <math|\<tau\>>, <math|\<beta\><rsub|0>> and
    <math|\<gamma\><rsub|0>> have upper exponent <math|0>, so do
    <math|\<tau\><rsub|n>> and <math|K>, and <math|y> has upper exponent
    <math|-1/2>. By Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-step-charge>,
    <math|2<rsup|n>*R<rsub|e>> is a constant, and <math|H<rsup|q>> has upper
    exponent <math|0>. Hence <math|2<rsup|n>*R<rsub|e>*y*H<rsup|q>> has upper
    exponent <math|-1/2>, and is therefore at most <math|1> for all large
    <math|s>. This is <eqref|eq:allarity-weighted-discrepancy>, which implies
    the first purchase in <eqref|eq:allarity-step-purchases> because
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|->\<leqslant\>1>.
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|A polynomial history with a local transport error.> The
  compensated coordinate map need not be affine. Indeed, on the protected
  ball it is obtained from <math|u\<mapsto\>log <around|(|exp
  <around|(|u|)>*M|)>>, as in <eqref|eq:compensator-exact>; away from that
  ball it uses a smooth extension. Undoing this change of variables removes
  its Jacobian, but leaves the nonlinear physical insertion in the reference
  Gaussian integral. It does not turn that insertion into its linear
  curvature polynomial. Consequently (CI)(b) is a sufficient convention, not
  an identity supplied by the compensated change of variables. We now give an
  alternative which keeps the prescribed polynomial history throughout.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Local error in the prescribed transport><label|lem:allarity-local-transport-defect>Assume
    (CI)(a),(c) and the amplitude hypotheses of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-step-discrepancy>. Replace (CI)(b) by
    the following condition. For each nonempty <math|T\<subseteq\>J>, let
    <math|q<rsup|-><rsub|T>> be the moment coefficient of the prescribed
    polynomial at the next rung. Uniformly on its healthy region, suppose

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-G\><rsub|i,x><around|(|Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>|)>-q<rsup|-><rsub|T>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<eta\><rsub|i>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|q<rsup|-><rsub|T>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|i>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|K<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1> has upper exponent zero, as do the
    principal amplitudes, and <math|\<eta\><rsub|i>> has a strictly negative
    upper exponent, uniformly in the rungs. Then the conclusion of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-step-discrepancy> holds with this
    prescribed history.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The normalization calculation in that lemma uses only (a),(c), and gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-A\><rsub|i,x><around|(|Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>|)>-\<cal-G\><rsub|i,x><around|(|Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>d<rsub|i>\<assign\>2*\<beta\><rsub|0>*<around|(|1+\<gamma\><rsub|0>|)>*\<tau\><rsub|n>*\<Pi\>.
    </equation*>

    Hence the actual and prescribed moment coefficients differ by at most
    <math|d<rsub|i>+\<eta\><rsub|i>>. Increase <math|K<rsub|i>> to bound both
    families; it still has upper exponent zero. In the coefficient sum norm
    on the marker algebra their nonconstant parts have norms at most
    <math|R<rsub|i>=<around|(|2<rsup|n>-1|)>*K<rsub|i>> and their difference
    has norm at most <math|<around|(|2<rsup|n>-1|)>*<around|(|d<rsub|i>+\<eta\><rsub|i>|)>>.
    The finite logarithm estimate of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-two-route-budget>
    therefore bounds the connected error by

    <\equation*>
      y<rsub|i>=<around|(|2<rsup|n>-1|)><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|r=0><rsup|n-1>R<rsub|i><rsup|r>|)>*<around|(|d<rsub|i>+\<eta\><rsub|i>|)>.
    </equation*>

    This has strictly negative upper exponent. Its value bounds the healthy
    local discrepancy because the jet norm dominates evaluation at zero. For
    every fixed <math|q>, the factors <math|2<rsup|n>*R<rsub|e>*H<rsup|q>>
    have upper exponent zero, so <math|2<rsup|n>*R<rsub|e>*H<rsup|q>*y<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1>
    eventually, uniformly in the rungs. This is exactly the discrepancy
    purchase used in the recurrence.
  </proof>

  This argument compares one step on the actual prescribed polynomial input
  and then realigns to the prescribed next polynomial. It does not iterate a
  reference cutoff operator on smooth nonpolynomial outputs. The recurrence
  already propagates the resulting regular and forced errors. Thus this route
  needs no additional invariant function space for a second,
  reference-transported history.

  <subparagraph|The joint Gaussian construction with separate guards.> There
  is a more direct route for the selected principal components. Here the
  cutoff belongs to each insertion, rather than to a common subprobability
  measure. The empty product then has integral exactly one. The following
  argument supplies the finite-arity comparison without assuming either (M)
  or exact reference transport (CI)(b).

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Guarded joint Gaussian moments><label|lem:allarity-guarded-gaussian>Fix
    <math|n,D\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, <math|A,B\<geqslant\>1>, and
    <math|0\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>\<leqslant\>1>. For every nonempty mode
    <math|I\<subseteq\><around|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> let <math|p<rsub|I>> be an
    invariant polynomial of degree at most <math|D> and coefficient norm at
    most <math|A> in a finite set of real curvature coordinates. Fix a real
    mean <math|a> and a real symmetric covariance array
    <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>> on the union of these coordinates, with entries
    bounded by <math|B> in absolute value.

    For each member <math|t> of a finite mixture let <math|\<mu\><rsub|t>> be
    a probability measure carrying a jointly centred Gaussian array
    <math|\<eta\><rsub|t>>, with covariance <math|C<rsub|t>>. Require, on
    every coordinate used here,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||a<rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>B,<space|1em><around|\||<around|(|C<rsub|t>|)><rsub|j*k>|\|>\<leqslant\>B,<space|1em><around|\||<around|(|C<rsub|t>-C<rsub|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|j*k>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>.
    </equation*>

    For each mode let <math|0\<leqslant\>\<Gamma\><rsub|t,I>\<leqslant\>1> be
    a measurable guard with <math|<big|int><around|(|1-\<Gamma\><rsub|t,I>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|t>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsup|2>>.
    Let <math|U<rsub|t>> be the actual fine configuration on this same
    probability space. On <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t,I>\<gtr\>0>, assume it is
    healthy on the full prescribed support of <math|p<rsub|I>>, and that,
    after a gauge rotation allowed to depend on <math|t,I,U<rsub|t>>, its
    normalized curvature coordinates <math|\<xi\><rsub|t,I>> satisfy

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<xi\><rsub|t,I,j>-a<rsub|j>-\<eta\><rsub|t,j>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>
    </equation*>

    on every coordinate used by <math|p<rsub|I>>. The polynomial's invariance
    must remove that rotation from its physical value. All physical
    evaluations and guarded products are assumed measurable.

    Use the same nonnegative mixture weights <math|\<lambda\><rsub|t>>, of
    sum one, for every partition and for the empty product. Define, for
    <math|S\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>,

    <\equation*>
      M<rsub|S>=<big|sum><rsub|\<pi\>\<in\>\<cal-P\><around|(|S|)>><big|sum><rsub|t>\<lambda\><rsub|t>*<big|int><big|prod><rsub|I\<in\>\<pi\>>\<Gamma\><rsub|t,I>*p<rsub|I><around|(|\<xi\><rsub|t,I>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|t>,<space|2em>M<rsup|\<ast\>><rsub|S>=<big|sum><rsub|\<pi\>\<in\>\<cal-P\><around|(|S|)>>\<bbb-E\><rsub|C<rsub|\<ast\>>>*<big|prod><rsub|I\<in\>\<pi\>>p<rsub|I>*<around|(|a+Z|)>,
    </equation*>

    and <math|M<rsub|\<varnothing\>>=M<rsup|\<ast\>><rsub|\<varnothing\>>=1>.
    Here <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|C<rsub|\<ast\>>>> means the finite Gaussian
    pairing functional; <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>> need not be positive. Then all
    the physical integrals exist. There is a polynomial <math|P<rsub|n,D>>
    with positive coefficients such that

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|S\<ne\>\<varnothing\>><around|\||M<rsub|S>-M<rsup|\<ast\>><rsub|S>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>*P<rsub|n,D>*<around|(|A,B|)>,<space|2em><around*|\||log<rsub|\<ast\>>
      M-log<rsub|\<ast\>> M<rsup|\<ast\>>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>*P<rsub|n,D>*<around|(|A,B|)>,
    </equation*>

    after increasing the same polynomial if necessary. Its coefficients and
    degree are independent of the number of curvature coordinates and the
    number of mixture members.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Fix a partition with <math|l\<leqslant\>n> blocks, put <math|d=l*D>, and
    abbreviate <math|P=<big|prod><rsub|I\<in\>\<pi\>>p<rsub|I>> and
    <math|\<Gamma\>=<big|prod><rsub|I\<in\>\<pi\>>\<Gamma\><rsub|t,I>>. The
    product guard satisfies

    <\equation*>
      0\<leqslant\>1-\<Gamma\>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|I\<in\>\<pi\>><around|(|1-\<Gamma\><rsub|t,I>|)>,<space|2em><big|int><around|(|1-\<Gamma\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|t>\<leqslant\>l*\<epsilon\><rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    No independence of the guards is required.

    We use Gaussian moment bounds that do not count unused coordinates. For
    <math|Y<rsub|j>=a<rsub|j>+\<eta\><rsub|t,j>> and every fixed integer
    <math|r\<geqslant\>1>, the one-dimensional Gaussian moment formula and
    <math|<around|\||a<rsub|j>|\|>,<math-up|Var><space|0.17em>\<eta\><rsub|t,j>\<leqslant\>B>
    give <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|(|1+<around|\||Y<rsub|j>|\|>|)><rsup|r>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|r>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|r>>.
    For a product of at most <math|r> such factors, the arithmetic\Ugeometric
    mean inequality bounds its expectation by the largest of these marginal
    <math|r>th moments, padding with factors one if needed. The bound applies
    also to repeated coordinates, without independence. Thus expectations of
    the absolute values of monomials of fixed degree, and of their squares,
    have bounds polynomial in <math|B> depending only on that degree.

    On <math|\<Gamma\>\<gtr\>0> all the coordinate comparisons hold
    simultaneously. Expand each <math|p<rsub|I>> into monomials and replace
    the factors one at a time. For every product monomial there are at most
    <math|d> replacements, each costing <math|\<epsilon\>> times at most
    <math|d-1> factors bounded by <math|1+<around|\||Y<rsub|j>|\|>>. The sum
    of absolute coefficients of the product expansion is at most
    <math|A<rsup|l>>. Integrating the preceding moment bound proves

    <\equation*>
      <big|int>\<Gamma\>*<around*|\||<big|prod><rsub|I\<in\>\<pi\>>p<rsub|I><around|(|\<xi\><rsub|t,I>|)>-P<around|(|Y|)>|\|>*d*\<mu\><rsub|t>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>*A<rsup|l>*c<rsub|d>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|d>.
    </equation*>

    The case <math|d=0> has zero error. This estimate and the Gaussian moment
    bound also prove integrability of the guarded physical product. On
    <math|\<Gamma\>=0> its value is defined as zero, so no control of
    physical coordinates on a failed guard is needed.

    To remove the guard from <math|P<around|(|Y|)>>, Cauchy\USchwarz gives

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<big|int><around|(|1-\<Gamma\>|)>*P<around|(|Y|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|t>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<big|int><around|(|1-\<Gamma\>|)><rsup|2>*d*\<mu\><rsub|t>|)><rsup|1/2><around*|(|<big|int><around|\||P<around|(|Y|)>|\|><rsup|2>*d*\<mu\><rsub|t>|)><rsup|1/2>\<leqslant\><sqrt|l>*<space|0.17em>\<epsilon\>*A<rsup|l>*c<rsub|d><rprime|'>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|d>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|<around|(|1-\<Gamma\>|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>1-\<Gamma\>>, and
    the second moment follows by expanding <math|P*<wide|P|\<bar\>>>, of
    degree at most <math|2*d> and coefficient norm at most
    <math|A<rsup|2*l>>.

    Finally compare the Gaussian expectations of <math|P> with covariances
    <math|C<rsub|t>> and <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>>. The mean is the same. In the
    pairing formula for a monomial of degree at most <math|d>, subtract
    corresponding products one factor at a time. Every difference contains a
    covariance difference at most <math|\<epsilon\>>, and at most <math|d>
    remaining bounded mean or covariance factors. The number of pairings
    depends only on <math|d>. Hence the difference is at most
    <math|\<epsilon\>*A<rsup|l>*c<rsub|d><rprime|''>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|d>>.
    Adding the three errors and averaging with <math|\<lambda\><rsub|t>>
    preserves the bound. There are only a number depending on <math|n> of
    subsets and partitions. Summing gives the moment bound and bounds both
    families of moments by a polynomial in <math|A,B>.

    The constant coefficients of both moment elements are one: the empty
    product has no guard and every <math|\<mu\><rsub|t>> is a probability
    measure. Apply the finite logarithm estimate of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-two-route-budget> in the scalar marker
    algebra. This proves the connected bound with an enlarged polynomial.
  </proof>

  The construction is simultaneous in the modes. Although the allowed gauge
  rotation can differ from one polynomial to another, the array
  <math|\<eta\><rsub|t>> and its measure are common to all of them. Selecting
  an independent Gaussian sample for each factor would destroy the cross
  covariances in <math|M<rsup|\<ast\>><rsub|S>> and invalidate the argument.

  <\corollary>
    <dueto|The discrepancy purchase from a joint guarded
    law><label|cor:allarity-guarded-purchase>At each leading rung and every
    healthy coarse configuration, suppose the principal-component integral is
    the moment element <math|M> in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-guarded-gaussian>,
    and the prescribed next polynomial is <math|log<rsub|\<ast\>>
    M<rsup|\<ast\>>> for the same mean, covariance and incoming polynomials.
    Let <math|A,B\<leqslant\>H<rsup|c>>, with fixed <math|c\<geqslant\>0>.
    For every fixed requested <math|q>, one can choose <math|f> before the
    final coupling threshold so that <math|\<epsilon\>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-f>>
    implies <eqref|eq:allarity-weighted-discrepancy>, uniformly in the rungs
    and healthy configurations.
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    Write <math|r> for the total degree of <math|P<rsub|n,D>> and <math|C>
    for the sum of its coefficients. The lemma bounds the discrepancy by
    <math|y\<leqslant\>C*H<rsup|c*r-f>>. Choose <math|f\<gtr\>q+c*r>. Then
    <math|2<rsup|n>*R<rsub|e>*H<rsup|q>*y\<leqslant\>2<rsup|n>*R<rsub|e>*C*H<rsup|q+c*r-f>\<leqslant\>1>
    eventually. All constants are uniform by hypothesis. No parameter
    derivatives of a guard have been taken. Alternatively any strictly
    negative upper exponent for <math|\<epsilon\>>, with exponent-zero
    <math|A,B>, pays every such fixed monitor power.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Reader comparison for an adjacent pair of
    stages><label|lem:allarity-pair-reader>Fix an actual adjacent step, its
    fine block side <math|L>, its predictor, the two attached paths and
    clips, and its precision horizon. Suppose the two stages have positive
    strip widths <math|s<rsub|f>,s<rsub|c>>, strip price bounds
    <math|z<rsub|f>,z<rsub|c>>, and positive metric floors
    <math|a<rsub|f>,a<rsub|c>> on the fine and coarse beds respectively.
    Retain the exact factorization, stationarity and transport identities of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-reader-tree-covariance> for these
    two beds. Use the common strip and price bounds

    <\equation*>
      s<rsub|\<wedge\>>=min <around|(|s<rsub|f>,s<rsub|c>|)>,<space|2em>z<rsub|\<vee\>>=max
      <around|(|z<rsub|f>,z<rsub|c>|)>,<space|2em>a=a<rsub|f>.
    </equation*>

    Assume the local Gaussian, guard and room hypotheses of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-reader-dawn-selection>, with these
    bounds, including its predictor purchase before <math|L> and its actual
    coupling band. Then its joint reader conclusions hold on this step,
    whether or not its two stage indices agree. In particular, for any fixed
    <math|f>, at sufficiently large station scale <math|s>, uniformly for the
    reader parameter <math|x\<in\><around|[|s/2,s|]>>, the covariance and
    physical-coordinate errors are at most <math|H<rsup|-f>> and each guard
    loses mass at most <math|H<rsup|-2*f>>, where <math|H=exp
    <around|(|<around|(|log log s|)><rsup|2>|)>>. The choices are uniform in
    periods, fields, forests and tags within the stated purchased classes.
    They can be made simultaneously for any fixed finite list of admissible
    adjacent stage pairs.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Restriction to a smaller strip decreases every exponentially weighted row
    sum. Replacing either price bound by <math|z<rsub|\<vee\>>> increases its
    majorant. Thus both adjacent rows satisfy their original estimates at
    <math|s<rsub|\<wedge\>>,z<rsub|\<vee\>>>. The fine precision keeps its
    fine metric floor <math|a<rsub|f>>; no equality with the coarse metric is
    asserted. In particular, the coarse metric and the diagonal operators in
    the two-form transport are not replaced by fine-stage objects.

    The tree calculation in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-reader-tree-covariance>
    starts with separate metrics <math|M<rsub|f>,M<rsub|c>> and separate
    curls on the two tori. Its exact conclusion is

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|D*I<rsub|F>|)>*A<rsub|F><rsup|-1>*<around|(|D*I<rsub|F>|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>=P<rsub|f>-J*P<rsub|c>*J<rsup|<math-ss|T>>.
    </equation*>

    It uses the retained root-gradient, stationarity and curvature
    intertwining identities, not equality of stage indices. The gap on the
    fine tree slice uses <math|a<rsub|f>> and the predictor penalty. Hence
    the common inverse and cover-row construction of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-reader-short-covariance> applies
    with the fixed adjacent patch and these bounds. The installed transport
    row uses the actual fine inverse diagonal, tower and coarse diagonal;
    their row-product estimate is valid at the common strip width. The two
    omitted tails therefore have bounds <math|C*e<rsup|-c*h<rsub|x>>> with
    <math|C\<less\>\<infty\>>, <math|c\<gtr\>0> fixed by this pair and
    <math|h<rsub|x>=<around|\<lfloor\>|<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>/10000|\<rfloor\>>>. They are eventually at most
    <math|x<rsup|-1/8>>, uniformly in the period.

    The actual-to-flat column comparison and removal of the bare path cut in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-reader-local-gaussian-prices> use
    the same fine tree precision and the same adjacent predictor. Their
    proofs give the actual reader covariance error <math|x<rsup|-1/8>> and
    the corresponding mean estimate for this pair. Combining the covariance
    error with the cover tail gives <math|2*x<rsup|-1/8>>. The mean
    comparison retains the actual coarse coupling in its forecast. The
    one-pose construction and the nonlinear guarded comparison use the
    stipulated room and coupling inequalities; their estimates are
    <math|x<rsup|-1/32>> for the normalized physical error and
    <math|x<rsup|-1>> for lost guard mass. None of these arguments changes
    the horizon, forest, recording parameter or sample space.

    Finally <math|2*x<rsup|-1/8>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-f>>,
    <math|x<rsup|-1/32>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-f>> and
    <math|x<rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-2*f>> eventually, uniformly for
    <math|x\<in\><around|[|s/2,s|]>>, by <eqref|eq:reader-band-power>. This
    proves the asserted joint comparison, including off-diagonal covariance
    entries. For a finite list of pairs first retain each pair's constants
    and then take the maximum of its finitely many entrance thresholds. This
    is not a maximum over all stage indices or all periods. A growing stage
    profile must place each such finite purchase before its own delay.
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|Attachment to the actual adjacent step.> For the actual joint
  reader model of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-all-arity-reader-gaussian-model>,
  the probability space is <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-reader-space>, the physical
  field is <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-reader-fine-field>, and the joint incidence
  vector is its stated linear image of the same Gaussian coordinate. The
  guards are the individual reader guards, retained inside each product. With
  the same-history and prior-purchase hypotheses of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-reader-dawn-selection>,
  <eqref|eq:reader-selected-sight> supplies
  <math|<around|\||C<rsub|t>-C<rsub|\<ast\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-f>>,
  where <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>> is the prescribed short covariance. That
  section's last subsection supplies guard loss at most <math|H<rsup|-2*f>>
  and the normalized physical-coordinate error at most <math|H<rsup|-f>>. Its
  parent forecast is exactly the mean of the prescribed short step, with the
  actual coarse coupling retained. Thus it supplies the joint-law hypotheses
  of the lemma on a constant-order plateau. At a stage change use
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-pair-reader> with the actual adjacent
  beds, the fine block side and the common horizon. Its explicit local
  hypotheses must hold for that pair. The entry bounds and the prescribed
  polynomial coefficient bounds provide the <math|H<rsup|c>> prices, after a
  fixed increase of <math|c>.

  The component identification uses Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-mixed-marked-compiler>,
  specifically its split <math|W<rsub|h>=\<Gamma\><rsub|h>*W<rsub|h>+<around|(|1-\<Gamma\><rsub|h>|)>*W<rsub|h>>
  on this same measure. An entirely leading component contains no body
  letter; compatibility then excludes background packets. Its integrand is
  therefore the product of the guarded principal values. On each positive
  guard the fine field is healthy on the full original mask support, so the
  masked value is <math|p<rsub|I><around|(|\<xi\><rsub|t,I>|)>>. Whole-mode
  selection and <eqref|eq:planted-integral> sum these products over the
  partitions. This gives precisely <math|M>, with empty coefficient one,
  under the compiler's same-guard and same-measure hypotheses. Invariance
  removes the one gauge supplied on each patch. The same mean and short
  covariance in the polynomial step give <math|M<rsup|\<ast\>>> by finite
  Wick contraction. Consequently Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:allarity-guarded-purchase>
  applies to these actual principal components. No claim about the full
  interacting block measure being Gaussian is used.

  This identifies the local discrepancy supplier from the canonical local
  estimates, also for an adjacent pair satisfying
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-pair-reader>. In the selected history
  the pair uses the minimum of its strip widths, the fine metric floor, the
  maximum of its prices and the fine stage's block side. The actual horizon
  must supply the algebraic identities and room on this pair; scalar bounds
  cannot replace them. Separately, the upper compiler's body, all-hole and
  warning purchases are required at <math|e<rsub|\<ast\>>=e+1+\<sigma\>>.
  They do not follow from this reader comparison or from the numerical
  stage-boundary lemma.

  This route retains the prescribed polynomial class: the reference moments
  are finite Wick pairings, followed by the finite connected logarithm. Its
  application on the selected course retains the explicit upstream hypotheses
  of the cited reader and compiler chapters. The health and covariance
  hypotheses must cover every read of every partition product. The
  component's unit normalization is the empty probability integral, not the
  mass of one insertion's guard. The existing common-cutoff route is a
  separate sufficient criterion; it must not be substituted for this joint
  construction without proving its additional measure identities.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The remaining step purchases><label|lem:allarity-step-purchases>For
    all sufficiently large <math|s>, uniformly in the rungs of the leading
    interval, the following hold:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>the second purchase in <eqref|eq:allarity-step-purchases>, with
      <math|G<rsub|i>=G<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>>;

      <item>both purchases in <eqref|eq:allarity-step-small>, with
      <math|B<rsub|i>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
      x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>><rsup|2>>.
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    <em|Second purchase.> By Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-mixed-marked-compiler>,
    <math|G> decays faster than every negative power of <math|H> uniformly on
    <math|<around|[|s/2,s|]>>. So eventually
    <math|G<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1>/2>. The quantity
    <math|n*R<rsub|e+1>> is a constant by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-step-charge>, so
    <math|n*R<rsub|e+1>*H<rsup|-2>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1>/2> once
    <math|H\<geqslant\>2*n*R<rsub|e+1>>.

    <em|Smallness.> For <math|log s\<geqslant\>1> and
    <math|x<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>s>, <math|B<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><around|(|<around|(|log
    s|)><rsup|2>+1|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>4<around|(|log s|)><rsup|4>>. Hence

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|2*B<rsub|i>+1|)><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>9<rsup|4><around|(|log
      s|)><rsup|16>=exp <around|{|4*log 9+16*log log s|}>\<leqslant\>exp
      <around|{|<around|(|log log s|)><rsup|2>|}>=H
    </equation*>

    once <math|log log s\<geqslant\>17>. Also
    <math|e<rsup|H<rsup|-1>>-1\<leqslant\>1> as soon as
    <math|H<rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>log 2>.
  </proof>

  The step count of the recurrence is Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-upper-count-power>
  below. It uses only <math|L<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>3> and the maximality of
  <math|\<ell\>>; no floor on the block lengths and no stage condition is
  needed.

  <subparagraph|What this supplies and what remains.> On the leading
  interval, the lemmas of this subsection and
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-upper-count-power> give the following,
  all at the weight <math|\<omega\><rsub|->> and eventually in <math|s>
  uniformly in the finite period:

  <\itemize>
    <item>the remainder multiplier <math|<around|(|\<alpha\>+1|)>*z>;

    <item>the principal incidence price <math|H<rsup|-2>>;

    <item>the forced price <math|G<rsub|i><rsup|2>> with
    <math|G<rsub|i>=G<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>>;

    <item>the leading budget <math|H<rsup|-4>>, given the unweighted bound
    <math|H<rsup|A>>;

    <item>the discrepancy purchase <eqref|eq:allarity-weighted-discrepancy>;

    <item>the purchases <eqref|eq:allarity-step-purchases>\U<eqref|eq:allarity-step-small>;

    <item>the step count, with remainder <math|d<rsub|k-\<ell\>>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<kappa\>-q>>
    at the middle level.
  </itemize>

  The following inputs are not supplied here.

  <\enumerate>
    <item><em|Applicability of the upper calculation> at each leading rung.
    This includes its primitive body-compiler, all-hole and joint-warning
    purchases, which Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-mixed-marked-compiler>
    lists as inputs on the same body alphabet and coarse representation.

    <item><em|The hypotheses of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-healthy-cumulant-launch>
    at every leading rung>, which give the unweighted principal-source bound
    <math|H<rsup|A>> by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-principal-power>,
    and the terminal-source purchase: the launched insertions must give the
    initial remainder price <math|d<rsub|0>> and forced price
    <math|G<rsub|k>> at the weight <math|\<omega\><rsub|->>, and, for the
    leading family of calibrated singletons, masks inspected on the
    prescribed supports <math|X<rsub|c,<around|{|j|}>>>.

    <item><em|The common insertion (CI)> of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-step-discrepancy>, or its clauses
    (a),(c) together with the local transport error of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-local-transport-defect>, or the joint
    guarded law of Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:allarity-guarded-purchase>.
    That the compiler integrates the masked moments
    <math|Q<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|T>> follows from the realignment by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-common-insertion-realigned>, given the
    form of the top-level principal insertion in item<nbsp>2 and the measure
    identification (CI)(a) of the planted sector and tag measures with those
    of the principal comparison. The remaining clauses say that the Gaussian
    history is transported by the reference integrals on those same
    insertions and is the one used at the middle level, and that the
    comparison data are uniform over the healthy base configurations. The
    bounded transport and the inverse bound for the reference mass follow
    from the latter by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-reference-bounds>,
    and the normalization is then handled by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-jet-inverse>. The amplitude bound at
    every leading rung follows from item<nbsp>2 by
    Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:allarity-rung-amplitude>.

    <item><em|The reader separation> <math|2*Q*B> between distinct replica
    cells assumed in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-step-charge>.
  </enumerate>

  The rungs below the middle level use the lower calculation of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-mixed-marked-compiler> at the large
  weight <math|\<omega\>>, after the launch of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:allarity-corrected-budget>.

  <subsubsection|The scalar envelope used in the replica estimate>

  We record the final elementary step linking a paid marked representation to
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:rank-root-normalization>. Suppose a
  polynomial density on a torus of <math|q<rsub|0>> sites has the form

  <\equation*>
    D<around|(|U|)>=e<rsup|<math-up|exponent><rsub|P><around|(|U|)>>*exp<rsub|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|a>z<rsub|I<rsub|a>>*h<rsub|a><around|(|U|)>|)>*<big|sum><rsub|B<math-up|admissible>><big|prod><rsub|b\<in\>B>K<rsub|b><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  The <math|I<rsub|a>> are nonempty, and each background packet has nonempty
  full spatial support. Let <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<b-r\>>>
  denote the coefficient <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>> norm weighted by positive
  marker weights. Assume regular incidence price at most <math|\<ell\>> per
  mark and forced spatial price at most <math|L> per site, with nonnegative
  support exponent. If there are <math|M<rsub|0>> marks in total, double
  counting gives

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|a><around|\<\|\|\>|z<rsub|I<rsub|a>>*h<rsub|a>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<b-r\>>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|0>*\<ell\>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|b><around|\<\|\|\>|K<rsub|b>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<b-r\>>\<leqslant\>q<rsub|0>*L.
  </equation*>

  A nonempty support contributes at least once to the corresponding incidence
  sum. Dropping the support weight is legitimate because it is at least one.
  Masks can only lower the regular value bounds. Submultiplicativity gives

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||exp<rsub|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|a>z<rsub|I<rsub|a>>*h<rsub|a>|)>|\|><rsub|\<b-r\>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|M<rsub|0>*\<ell\>>.
  </equation*>

  Dropping the admissibility restrictions in the nonnegative majorant bounds
  the forced sum by <math|<big|prod><rsub|b><around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|K<rsub|b>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<b-r\>>|)>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|q<rsub|0>*L>>.
  Therefore

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:allarity-density-envelope><around|\<\|\|\>|D<around|(|U|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<b-r\>>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around|{|<math-up|exponent><rsub|P><around|(|U|)>+q<rsub|0>*L+M<rsub|0>*\<ell\>|}>.
  </equation>

  For the replica estimate, first complete any later propagation to the
  selected output torus whose site count is exactly the number
  <math|q<rsub|0>> of replica cells. This equality is a property of that
  selected output level, not of an arbitrary intermediate torus. At this
  output there are <math|M<rsub|0>=q<rsub|0>*n> marks. Thus
  <math|L,\<ell\>\<leqslant\>1> give per-site expense at most <math|1+n>,
  exactly the arity dependence in the determinant ticket. The weight of the
  coefficient carrying a chosen nonempty subset <math|I> in each of the
  <math|q<rsub|0>> cells is the <math|q<rsub|0>>th power of its single-cell
  weight. At the constant weight <eqref|eq:allarity-marker-weight> this is
  <math|\<omega\><rsup|q<rsub|0><around|\||I|\|>>>, and since the coefficient
  norm dominates each weighted coefficient,
  <eqref|eq:allarity-density-envelope> gives pointwise, for the coefficient
  <math|D<rsub|I><rsup|\<otimes\>><around|(|U|)>> of the monomial carrying
  <math|I> in every cell,

  <\equation*>
    \<omega\><rsup|q<rsub|0><around|\||I|\|>><space|0.17em><around*|\||D<rsub|I><rsup|\<otimes\>><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around|{|<math-up|exponent><rsub|P><around|(|U|)>+q<rsub|0>*<around|(|L+n*\<ell\>|)>|}>.
  </equation*>

  With <math|u=M> the marker allowance of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:rank-root-normalization>,
  <math|\<omega\>=\<cal-M\><rsub|k><rsup|1/<around|(|8*M|)>>=e<rsup|2*I<rsub|k>>>
  is the weight <math|a> used there, because <math|I<rsub|k>=log
  \<cal-M\><rsub|k>/<around|(|16*M|)>>. Hence this is the coefficient price
  <math|a<rsup|q<rsub|0><around|\||I|\|>>> with per-site expense
  <math|X=L+n*\<ell\>\<leqslant\>1+M> required there, on the same pushed
  density. Apply the normalization estimate and then take the
  <math|q<rsub|0>>th root. The denominator is the partition function of the
  same pushed density, preserved by the unmarked coefficient identity at
  every step.

  The recurrence above states its one-step hypotheses quantitatively. The
  joint Gaussian reader estimate supplies the discrepancy <math|y> and the
  principal source bounds; the mixed compiler supplies the exact marked push
  and the pre-realignment prices. The selected scale construction must buy
  all of these on the common history, including the later propagation below
  <math|l>. Neither the recurrence nor the scalar envelope asserts those
  upstream estimates without their corresponding purchases.

  <subsubsection|Transport to the output torus><label|subsec:allarity-output-transport>

  We complete the propagation in the application to the pairwise receipt. The
  chart has level <math|k>, middle level <math|\<ell\>> and root depth
  <math|w\<leqslant\>\<ell\>>, and the arity is <math|n=2>. We identify the
  marked density at the output, show that its pushed version below the middle
  level keeps prices at most one, and conclude with the coefficient price of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:rank-root-normalization>. As before,
  <math|s=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>, <math|H=\<cal-M\><rsub|k>>,
  <math|\<omega\>=H<rsup|1/<around|(|8*u|)>>>, and the fixed constants
  <math|\<alpha\>\<geqslant\>8*T<rsub|e>> and <math|q\<geqslant\>6> are those
  of Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:allarity-step-supply>.

  <subparagraph|The invoice and its parts.> Let <math|f<rsub|0>,f<rsub|1>> be
  the two finite-volume insertions of a fit configuration, as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:trace-rounded-receipt>. Let
  <math|S<rsub|0><around|(|T|)>> (<math|T\<subseteq\><around|{|0,1|}>>) be
  the reference moments at the rounded configuration, with
  <math|S<rsub|0><around|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=1>. In the marker algebra
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|A><rsub|<around|{|0,1|}>>> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-planted-compiler>, the polynomial
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|T>S<rsub|0><around|(|T|)>*z<rsub|T>> has constant
  coefficient one. So it is <math|exp<rsub|\<ast\>> D> for a unique <math|D>
  with zero constant coefficient, namely

  <\equation*>
    D=j<rsub|0>*z<rsub|0>+j<rsub|1>*z<rsub|1>+\<kappa\>*z<rsub|0>*z<rsub|1>,<space|2em>j<rsub|c>=S<rsub|0><around|(|<around|{|c|}>|)>,<space|1em>\<kappa\>=S<rsub|0><around|(|<around|{|0,1|}>|)>-j<rsub|0>*j<rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed <math|exp<rsub|\<ast\>> D=1+D+D<rsup|2>/2> and
  <math|D<rsup|2>=2*j<rsub|0>*j<rsub|1>*z<rsub|0>*z<rsub|1>>. The
  <em|invoice> is

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-I\>=exp<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|-D|)>*<space|0.17em><around|(|1+f<rsub|0>*z<rsub|0>|)>*<around|(|1+f<rsub|1>*z<rsub|1>|)>.
  </equation*>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The parts of the invoice><label|lem:allarity-invoice-parts> The
    coefficients of <math|\<cal-I\>> are

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-I\><rsub|<around|{|c|}>>=f<rsub|c>-j<rsub|c>*<space|1em><around|(|c=0,1|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around|{|0,1|}>>=<around|(|f<rsub|0>-j<rsub|0>|)>*<around|(|f<rsub|1>-j<rsub|1>|)>-\<kappa\>,
    </equation*>

    that is, <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|s>=\<Phi\><rsub|s>> for every nonempty
    <math|s>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    First, <math|exp<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|-D|)>=1-D+D<rsup|2>/2=1-j<rsub|0>*z<rsub|0>-j<rsub|1>*z<rsub|1>+<around|(|j<rsub|0>*j<rsub|1>-\<kappa\>|)>*z<rsub|0>*z<rsub|1>>.
    Multiply by <math|1+f<rsub|0>*z<rsub|0>+f<rsub|1>*z<rsub|1>+f<rsub|0>*f<rsub|1>*z<rsub|0>*z<rsub|1>>,
    using <math|z<rsub|c><rsup|2>=0>. The coefficient of <math|z<rsub|c>> is
    <math|f<rsub|c>-j<rsub|c>>. The coefficient of <math|z<rsub|0>*z<rsub|1>>
    is

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|0>*f<rsub|1>-j<rsub|0>*f<rsub|1>-j<rsub|1>*f<rsub|0>+j<rsub|0>*j<rsub|1>-\<kappa\>=<around|(|f<rsub|0>-j<rsub|0>|)>*<around|(|f<rsub|1>-j<rsub|1>|)>-\<kappa\>.
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|Cells and sites.> The cells of the centered tiling at depth
  <math|w> of the level-<math|N> torus are indexed by the sites of the
  level-<math|w> torus, one cell for each site. Hence the number
  <math|q<rsub|0>> of replica cells equals the site count of the
  level-<math|w> torus. This is the equality required in the scalar envelope:
  the output torus is the level-<math|w> torus.

  <subparagraph|Propagation below the middle level.> On the rungs <math|j>
  with <math|w\<leqslant\>j\<less\>\<ell\>> there are no leading letters, and
  the lower calculation of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-mixed-marked-compiler>
  applies. All prices from the launch at <math|\<ell\>> downwards are taken
  at the large weight <math|\<omega\>=H<rsup|1/<around|(|8*u|)>>>. With
  incoming regular marker-root price <math|z\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1/2>> and
  incoming forced budget <math|z*<space|0.17em>\<omega\><rsub|j>>, where
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|j>\<in\><around|[|0,1|]>> is the actual lower-interval
  attenuation, the lower calculation gives two outputs:

  <\itemize>
    <item>regular marker-root price at most
    <math|<tfrac|1|2><around|(|\<alpha\>*z+H<rsup|1/2>*z<rsup|2>|)>\<leqslant\>\<alpha\>*z+H<rsup|1/2>*z<rsup|2>>;

    <item>forced site price at most <math|<around|(|e<rsup|\<Sigma\>>-1|)>/m\<leqslant\>G<around|(|y<rsub|j>|)>>,
    where <math|m=G<around|(|y<rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1>> and <math|y<rsub|j>> is
    the fine coupling of the rung.
  </itemize>

  These require the rung couplings to lie in <math|<around|[|s/2,s|]>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Lower propagation><label|lem:allarity-lower-propagation> Assume
    the following.

    <\enumerate>
      <item>At level <math|\<ell\>> the regular marker-root price is at most
      <math|z<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-3/4>>, and there are no leading
      letters.

      <item>Every rung of <math|<around|[|w,\<ell\>|)>> satisfies the
      hypotheses of the lower calculation, with couplings in
      <math|<around|[|s/2,s|]>>.

      <item>The forced input of each lower rung is admissible: its forced
      budget is at most <math|z<rsub|t>*<space|0.17em>\<omega\><rsub|j>>
      along the iteration below. Prices are upper bounds, so <math|z<rsub|t>>
      may be enlarged for this.

      <item>The lower step count satisfies

      <\equation>
        <label|eq:allarity-lower-count><around|(|\<alpha\>+1|)><rsup|\<ell\>-w>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|1/4>.
      </equation>
    </enumerate>

    Let <math|z<rsub|t>> be the regular bound after <math|t> lower steps, so
    that level <math|\<ell\>-t> carries <math|z<rsub|t>>. Then
    <math|z<rsub|t>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<alpha\>+1|)><rsup|t>*z<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1/2>\<leqslant\>1>
    for <math|0\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>\<ell\>-w>, and every forced site
    price on <math|<around|[|w,\<ell\>|]>> is at most <math|1>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    If <math|H<rsup|1/2>*z<rsub|t>\<leqslant\>1>, then

    <\equation*>
      z<rsub|t+1>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<alpha\>+H<rsup|1/2>*z<rsub|t>|)>*z<rsub|t>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<alpha\>+1|)>*z<rsub|t>.
    </equation*>

    By induction <math|z<rsub|t>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<alpha\>+1|)><rsup|t>*z<rsub|0>>
    for <math|t\<leqslant\>\<ell\>-w>, as long as
    <math|H<rsup|1/2>*z<rsub|t>\<leqslant\>1>. This condition holds
    throughout, because <math|<around|(|\<alpha\>+1|)><rsup|t>*z<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|1/4>*H<rsup|-3/4>=H<rsup|-1/2>>
    by <eqref|eq:allarity-lower-count>. That is also the domain bound
    <math|z\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1/2>> of the lower calculation. The forced
    outputs of the lower rungs are at most
    <math|G<around|(|y<rsub|j>|)>\<leqslant\>1>. At <math|t=0> the forced
    price is the one delivered by the launch, which is at most <math|1> by
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:allarity-launch>.
  </proof>

  By Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-lower-count-free> below, the lower
  count <eqref|eq:allarity-lower-count> holds for all large <math|s>,
  uniformly in the chart, and by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-lower-forced>
  hypothesis<nbsp>3 can be dropped.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The coefficient price at the output><label|prop:allarity-output-price>Assume
    the following.

    <\enumerate>
      <item>The launch receipt of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:allarity-launch>
      at the middle level.

      <item>The hypotheses of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-lower-propagation>.

      <item>The representation at the output level <math|w> is lawful, with
      the suppositions of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:rank-root-normalization>
      at the stage of level <math|w>.
    </enumerate>

    Then, at the weight <math|\<omega\>=H<rsup|1/<around|(|8*u|)>>>, the
    following hold for every nonempty <math|s\<subseteq\><around|{|0,1|}>>:

    <\equation*>
      \<omega\><rsup|q<rsub|0><around|\||s|\|>><space|0.17em><around*|\||\<cal-D\><rsub|s><rsup|\<otimes\>><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>exp
      <around|{|<math-up|exponent><rsub|P><around|(|U|)>+q<rsub|0>*<around|(|1+n|)>|}>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|\<cal-D\>> is the push to the level-<math|w> torus of the
    replicated marked density of the invoice <math|\<cal-I\>>, and
    <math|\<cal-D\><rsub|s><rsup|\<otimes\>>> is its coefficient carrying
    <math|s> in every cell. The normalizing denominator is the partition
    function of the same pushed density, by the unmarked coefficient identity
    at every step. Taking <math|u=M>, so that <math|\<omega\>=a>, this is the
    coefficient price <math|a<rsup|q<rsub|0><around|\||s|\|>>> with per-site
    expense <math|X\<leqslant\>1+n\<leqslant\>1+M> required by
    <eqref|eq:rank-root>. Consequently, for <math|u=M> and
    <math|k\<geqslant\>K<rsub|M>> of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:rank-root-output-ticket>,
    the root test of <math|\<Phi\><rsub|s>> on the centered tiling at depth
    <math|w> is at most <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|k><rsup|-1/<around|(|16*M|)>>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    By Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-lower-propagation>, at level
    <math|w> the regular incidence price per mark is
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|w>\<leqslant\>1> and the forced site price is
    <math|L<rsub|w>\<leqslant\>1>. By the cells-and-sites paragraph, the
    output torus has <math|q<rsub|0>> sites, and there are
    <math|M<rsub|0>=q<rsub|0>*n> marks. The scalar envelope
    <eqref|eq:allarity-density-envelope> and the paragraph after it give the
    displayed inequality with expense <math|L<rsub|w>+n*\<ell\><rsub|w>\<leqslant\>1+n>.
    By Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-invoice-parts>, the coefficients of
    the invoice are the <math|\<Phi\><rsub|s>>, so the replicated coefficient
    is that of <math|\<Phi\><rsub|s>> in every cell. The last statement is
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:rank-root-output-ticket>, applied with
    <math|w<around|(|k|)>=w>, together with <eqref|eq:rank-root> and the
    lawful representation at <math|w>. For general <math|u> the weight is
    <math|\<omega\>=H<rsup|1/<around|(|8*u|)>>> and the same argument gives
    the bound <math|H<rsup|1/<around|(|16*M|)>-<around|\||s|\|>/<around|(|8*u|)>>>
    only; the root conclusion uses <math|u=M>.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The launch at the middle
  level><label|subsec:allarity-corrected-budget>

  At the middle level <math|\<ell\>> the representation produced by the
  leading interval is converted into one suitable for the lower interval.
  Three things change at once: the marker weight is raised from
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|->> to <math|\<omega\>>; the leading family is
  replaced by its corrected form; and the leading designation is dropped, so
  that the corrected pieces become ordinary regular letters. We keep the
  notation of Subsections<nbsp><reference|subsec:allarity-step-supply>
  and<nbsp><reference|subsec:allarity-output-transport>. In particular
  <math|\<cal-I\>=exp<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|-D<rsub|R>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|c><around|(|1+f<rsub|c>*z<rsub|c>|)>>
  is the invoice, with <math|D<rsub|R>=D> the reference cumulant polynomial.

  <subparagraph|The reference factor commutes with every push.> The
  polynomial <math|exp<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|-D<rsub|R>|)>> has
  field-independent coefficients. Every block integration and every push acts
  linearly and coefficientwise on the marker algebra. So each of them
  commutes with multiplication by <math|exp<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|-D<rsub|R>|)>>.
  Hence the pushed invoice at any level is
  <math|exp<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|-D<rsub|R>|)>> times the pushed product of
  the insertions. At the middle level the latter has principal part
  <math|exp<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|D<rsub|Q>|)>>, with <math|D<rsub|Q>> the
  connected polynomial of the same Gaussian history. The invoice is therefore
  launched at the middle level with corrected principal polynomial
  <math|D<rsub|Q>-D<rsub|R>>. This is an identity of densities. It does not
  by itself give a priced representation, for the following reason.

  <subparagraph|The failed mask.> For one mode, let <math|\<chi\>> be the
  healthy mask of its prescribed support, <math|W> the actual leading
  coefficient and <math|R> the corresponding coefficient of <math|D<rsub|R>>.
  The leading letter carries <math|\<chi\>*W>, while the reference factor
  contributes <math|R> everywhere. Hence

  <\equation*>
    \<chi\>*W-R=\<chi\>*<around|(|W-R|)>-<around|(|1-\<chi\>|)>*R.
  </equation*>

  The first term is a regular letter controlled by the healthy bound on
  <math|W-R> (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-corrected-pieces>). The
  second term is supported where the prescribed support contains a flag. It
  must be moved into the forced alphabet and regrouped with the forced
  background, as in the mask change of the realignment subsection. Its price
  is controlled by a bound on the reference coefficients <math|R>, not by the
  healthy bound.

  <subparagraph|The corrected budget.> The bound on the corrected pieces is
  already proved in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-cumulant-comparison>.
  There the free moment polynomial <math|q<rprime|'>> is the reference moment
  polynomial <math|1+X<rsub|R>>, and the moment polynomial <math|q> of the
  same Gaussian history is <math|1+X<rsub|Q>>. If the coefficientwise moment
  error is at most <math|H<rsup|-3/2>>, then
  <eqref|eq:allarity-cumulant-error> gives
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsub|Q>-D<rsub|R>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>+H<rsup|-3/2>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-31/32>>
  for all large <math|s>. That moment error is
  <eqref|eq:allarity-moment-error> at the powers of its scale paragraph. The
  actual healthy local error between a planted leading coefficient and the
  coefficient of <math|D<rsub|Q>> is at most <math|H<rsup|-3/2>> by
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-healthy-cumulant-launch>. Both
  statements hold under the hypotheses stated in those sections.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Corrected leading pieces><label|lem:allarity-corrected-pieces>Assume
    the moment error <math|H<rsup|-3/2>> of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-cumulant-comparison> and the
    healthy local error <math|H<rsup|-3/2>> of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-healthy-cumulant-launch>, at the
    middle level. Then, for all large <math|s>, every corrected leading piece
    at that level, that is, an actual leading piece minus the corresponding
    coefficient of <math|D<rsub|R>>, is at most <math|H<rsup|-31/32>> on its
    healthy region. Its incidence price at the weight <math|\<omega\>> is at
    most

    <\equation*>
      h=c*<space|0.17em>H<rsup|-27/32>,<space|2em>c=2<rsup|n>*R<rsub|e>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The first assertion is the triangle inequality applied to the healthy
    local error and <eqref|eq:allarity-cumulant-error>, as in the last
    paragraph of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-cumulant-comparison>.
    Each coefficient is bounded by the coefficient <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>>
    norm. For the price, a piece of mode <math|I> carries weight
    <math|\<omega\><rsup|<around|\||I|\|>>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|1/8>> by
    <eqref|eq:allarity-weight-cap>, and support charge at most
    <math|R<rsub|e>> by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-step-charge>. At
    most <math|2<rsup|n>> modes contain a given mark. Hence the incidence
    price is at most <math|2<rsup|n>*R<rsub|e>*H<rsup|1/8>*H<rsup|-31/32>=c*H<rsup|-27/32>>.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Raising the regular marker weight><label|lem:allarity-weight-raise>For
    each regular letter let <math|I> be its marker set, <math|X> its full
    support, and <math|t<around|(|I|)>> the number of replica cells
    represented in <math|I>. Suppose the marker-root sum of letters with
    <math|t<around|(|I|)>=1> is at most <math|p<rsub|1>> at weight
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|->> and support exponent <math|e>. Suppose the
    corresponding sum of letters with <math|t<around|(|I|)>\<geqslant\>2> is
    at most <math|p<rsub|2>> at weight <math|\<omega\><rsub|->> and support
    exponent <math|e+\<sigma\>>, where <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0>, and that
    each of these letters satisfies

    <\equation*>
      \<sigma\><math-up|cov><rsub|B><around|(|X|)>\<geqslant\>U*n*<space|0.17em>t<around|(|I|)>*log
      H.
    </equation*>

    Then the marker-root price at weight <math|\<omega\>> and support
    exponent <math|e> is at most <math|H<rsup|U*n>*p<rsub|1>+p<rsub|2>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Empty marker sets contribute to neither sum. For a letter with
    <math|t<around|(|I|)>=1>, <math|<around|\||I|\|>\<leqslant\>n>, so
    raising the weight costs at most <math|H<rsup|U*n>>. For a letter with
    <math|t<around|(|I|)>\<geqslant\>2>, <math|<around|\||I|\|>\<leqslant\>n*t<around|(|I|)>>.
    Raising the weight and lowering the support exponent together multiply
    its price by

    <\equation*>
      H<rsup|U<around|\||I|\|>>*exp <around|{|-\<sigma\><math-up|cov><rsub|B><around|(|X|)>|}>\<leqslant\>1.
    </equation*>

    Summing at each fixed marker gives the claim, uniformly in the number of
    replica cells.
  </proof>

  The recurrence at exponent <math|e> supplies neither the price of the
  multi-cell letters with a spare exponent <math|\<sigma\>> nor the support
  inequality of the lemma. Both are obtained in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-remainder-restored> below, from one
  spare-exponent run of the leading interval and from the geometry of the
  halos.

  The forced background is not covered by this lemma: a forced component may
  contain marked pieces from several cells, and its weight may then grow by
  more than <math|H<rsup|U*n>>. Its weight change is paid together with the
  failed-mask pieces, from a spare support exponent and from the smallness of
  the forced background.

  <subparagraph|Geometric and pricing hypotheses at the launch.> For a cell
  <math|c> let <math|Y<rsub|c>=<big|cup><rsub|I>X<rsub|c,I>> be its halo, the
  union of its prescribed supports. For a support <math|X> let
  <math|t<around|(|X|)>> be the number of cells <math|c> with
  <math|X\<cap\>Y<rsub|c>\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>. We assume:

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(F)>at the middle level, the forced background has site price at
    most <math|\<eta\><rsub|->> at the weight <math|\<omega\><rsub|->> and at
    the support exponent <math|e+1+\<sigma\>>, for a fixed spare exponent
    <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0>. The full support of every marked forced piece
    contains the reader sites of its marks;

    <item*|(S)>there is <math|\<lambda\>=\<lambda\><around|(|s|)>> with
    <math|\<lambda\>/log H\<to\>\<infty\>> such that every full support
    <math|X> of a forced piece with <math|t<around|(|X|)>\<geqslant\>2>
    satisfies <math|<math-up|cov><rsub|B><space|0.17em>X\<geqslant\>\<lambda\>*<space|0.17em>t<around|(|X|)>>.
  </enumerate>

  The site-price clause of (F), with <math|\<eta\><rsub|->=G<around|(|x<rsub|\<ell\>>|)>>,
  and the price of the regular remainder used in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-remainder-restored> both come from
  running the leading interval at the larger exponent
  <math|e<rsub|\<ast\>>=e+1+\<sigma\>>. We fix
  <math|\<sigma\>\<in\><around|(|0,1|]>> together with <math|e>, before
  <math|\<alpha\>>. The inputs of Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:allarity-step-supply>
  are then required at <math|e<rsub|\<ast\>>>:

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(E)><em|The leading interval at the exponent
    <math|e<rsub|\<ast\>>>.> The inputs listed at the end of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:allarity-step-supply>, namely the
    applicability of the upper calculation at each leading rung and the
    terminal-source purchase, hold with the support exponent
    <math|e<rsub|\<ast\>>> in place of <math|e>. The step multiplier
    satisfies <math|\<alpha\>\<geqslant\>8*T<rsub|e<rsub|\<ast\>>>>.
  </enumerate>

  This replaces the run at the exponent <math|e>: by monotonicity of
  <math|<math-up|chg><rsub|e>> in <math|e>, every price at
  <math|e<rsub|\<ast\>>> bounds the price of the same letters at <math|e>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The run at the exponent <math|e<rsub|\<ast\>>>><label|lem:allarity-spare-run>Assume
    (E) and the remaining inputs of Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:allarity-step-supply>.
    Then, for all large <math|s>, the conclusions of that subsection hold at
    the support exponent <math|e<rsub|\<ast\>>>. In particular, at the middle
    level the nonleading regular remainder has marker-root price at most
    <math|H<rsup|\<kappa\>-q>>, and the realigned forced background has site
    price at most <math|G<around|(|x<rsub|\<ell\>>|)>>, both at the weight
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|->> and the exponent <math|e<rsub|\<ast\>>>. The
    exponent <math|\<kappa\>> is the same as at <math|e>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    That subsection uses the support exponent only through the constants
    <math|R<rsub|e>>, <math|R<rsub|e+1>> and <math|T<rsub|e>>, and through
    the inputs, which (E) supplies at <math|e<rsub|\<ast\>>>. The constants
    <math|R<rsub|e<rsub|\<ast\>>>> and <math|R<rsub|e<rsub|\<ast\>>+1>> of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-step-charge> are fixed before
    <math|s>. The multiplier condition <math|\<alpha\>\<geqslant\>8*T<rsub|e<rsub|\<ast\>>>>
    is part of (E), and it implies <math|\<alpha\>\<geqslant\>8*T<rsub|e>>
    because <math|T<rsub|e>> is increasing in <math|e>. Every purchase of the
    subsection is an eventual inequality in <math|s> with these constants.
    Hence the proofs apply verbatim at <math|e<rsub|\<ast\>>>. The exponent
    <math|\<kappa\>> of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-upper-count-power>
    depends only on <math|\<alpha\>> and <math|<wide|b|\<bar\>>>.
  </proof>

  The support purchase of the upper calculation at a fixed exponent does not
  need to be bought separately on the selected history. In
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-mixed-marked-compiler> it is the
  block-length condition <math|10\<cdot\>15<rsup|4>*<around|(|e<rsub|\<ast\>>+7|)>\<leqslant\>L*e<rsub|\<ast\>>>,
  since the incoming exponent of the upper calculation is its own exponent.
  The selected block sides tend to infinity. Indeed,
  <eqref|eq:common-drift-floor> gives <math|log
  L<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>j<rsup|2>*\<lambda\><rsub|j>> for
  <math|j\<geqslant\>j<rsub|\<ast\>>>, and
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>log 101> because
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|j>> includes the support exponent <math|100>. So the
  condition holds at every step of a comparison window at a sufficiently
  large level. For the other compiler purchases one must specify the larger
  exponent before constructing the alphabet. There is no need to leave the
  spare exponent arbitrary: the weight-restoration argument only requires
  <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0>. The following choice matches the two uses of the
  same local compiler.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|One compiler pays the support reserve><label|prop:allarity-compiler-reserve>Let
    <math|e\<gtr\>0> be the final support exponent. Choose
    <math|0\<less\>\<sigma\>\<leqslant\>1>, put
    <math|e<rsub|\<ast\>>=e+1+\<sigma\>> and <math|E=e+2>, and choose
    <math|\<alpha\>\<geqslant\>8*T<rsub|E>>. On an actual adjacent step
    retain the funded tube, paths, clips, alongside certificate,
    inherited-law and small remainder hypotheses of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-local-compiler-handoff>. Make its
    support and product-price purchases for raw compiler exponent <math|E>,
    using the local support-cost exponent <math|<wide|E|^>=E+8>, with the
    larger of the upper and lower multipliers in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-mixed-marked-compiler>. Retain that
    construction's same-field selected all-pilot package.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:actual-neutral-warning> supplies its
    all-compatible-palette and joint-warning transfer to the genuine
    corrected alphabet. Then the body, all-hole, joint-warning and
    forced-embedding inputs for the upper calculation at
    <math|e<rsub|\<ast\>>> and the lower calculation at <math|e> hold for one
    corrected alphabet and one coarse normalization. This choice does not
    depend on the marked family or on the incoming marker remainder price
    <math|z\<in\><around|[|H<rsup|-q>,H<rsup|-4>|]>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The local handoff is stated for any fixed support exponent including its
    reserve. Apply it at <math|<wide|E|^>>, before choosing the large
    coupling. Indeed the mixed compiler uses <math|v=E+5> and cover charge
    <math|v+2=E+7>; <math|<wide|E|^>> leaves one further support reserve. The
    corresponding fine-to-coarse purchase is
    <math|10\<cdot\>15<rsup|4>*<around|(|E+7|)>\<leqslant\>L*\<sigma\><rsub|f>>,
    with <math|\<sigma\><rsub|f>> the incoming fine support exponent. It must
    be imposed before the block side is fixed. The raw compiler exponent and
    this larger local support-cost exponent are different parameters. Its two
    rooted word inventories, including the first coarse-field derivative
    needed for the determinant terms, use that same exponent. The itinerary
    ratio has positive exponential decay after including the fixed cover
    charge at <math|<wide|E|^>>; increasing its entrance threshold pays both
    inventories. Its matrix, owner-energy and palette estimates are for the
    actual tube and actual representation. Thus the genuine body identity of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-corrected-body-compiler> and its
    integrated product estimates use this one corrected alphabet, with its
    original forest/tag measure and positive loading.

    The regular logarithm is formed from the same unmarked alphabet. Its
    absolute connected-cluster estimate is part of this compiler purchase.
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:corrected-regular-deletion> then gives
    the identity for every deleted support, using the factors
    <math|h<rsub|b>=exp <around|(|-c<rsub|b>|)>-1> attached to the full
    supports of the logarithmic entries. Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:corrected-forced-output>
    constructs the forced output by the same connected components and
    boundary factors. In particular, the normalization and forced activities
    are constructed before selecting the arbitrary marked family. They are
    not taken from a second coarse representation with similar prices.

    For the upper call the regular multiplier is

    <\equation*>
      l<around|(|z|)>=max <around|(|1,H<rsup|-3>/<around|(|\<alpha\>*z+H<rsup|-q>|)>|)>\<leqslant\>max
      <around|(|1,H<rsup|q-3>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Purchase the right-hand bound once, and purchase the maximum of the two
    forced multipliers <math|G<around|(|x|)><rsup|-2>> and
    <math|G<around|(|y|)><rsup|-1>>. The owner margin in the local handoff is
    the one that pays this forced inflation. The corrected product and hole
    estimates are purchased with these multipliers, not recovered from an
    uninflated site sum. The upper numerical calculation then has body
    allowance <math|K/16>, raised hole allowance <math|K/4> and boundary
    allowance <math|K>, where <math|K=H<rsup|-1/8>>. Its joint warning event
    remains the event on this same sector sample space; its mass bound
    <math|x<rsup|-1>> pays <math|l<around|(|z|)>*x<rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>1>
    eventually. No estimate for a product of independent warning samples is
    substituted.

    Now <math|e<rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>E>. For any full support <math|X>,

    <\equation*>
      exp <around|(|e<rsub|\<ast\>><math-up|cov><rsub|B><space|0.17em>X|)>\<leqslant\>exp
      <around|(|E<math-up|cov><rsub|B><space|0.17em>X|)>.
    </equation*>

    All site and rooted prices are sums of nonnegative majorants. Lowering
    this exponent and either multiplier therefore preserves the body product,
    hole, boundary and forced-embedding estimates. The all-hole and density
    identities are unchanged, since these parameters occur only in their
    estimates. Also <math|T<rsub|e<rsub|\<ast\>>>\<leqslant\>T<rsub|E>>, so
    the same <math|\<alpha\>> pays the numerical upper calculation at
    <math|e<rsub|\<ast\>>>. Lowering to <math|e> gives the lower calculation
    from the identical alphabet. The local thresholds are uniform in periods,
    stores, fields, forests and tags. The common-band selection of the
    handoff therefore supplies one threshold for
    <math|x,y\<in\><around|[|s/2,s|]>> on this fixed adjacent patch, before
    the marked family and <math|z> are chosen.
  </proof>

  This proposition identifies the required analytic purchase rather than
  deriving it from growth of <math|L>. It uses the local compiler's actual
  product and all-hole estimates, with their hypotheses, for raw exponent
  <math|E> and the local support reserve <math|<wide|E|^>>. Choosing
  <math|\<sigma\>=1> makes <math|e<rsub|\<ast\>>=E>, exactly its upper
  exponent. For a finite list of adjacent patches make these purchases on
  each patch and then take the maximum of their coupling thresholds. The
  stage profile must include them before choosing its delays, with the guide
  and support conditions imposed before the respective block sides.
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:restart-endpoint> supplies the exact
  starting presentation. Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:restart-actual-closure>
  then constructs the actual restarted course, verifies the alongside and
  neutral-law hypotheses, and proves its genuine small-remainder,
  inflated-product and all-hole estimates on each adjacent step. Thus the
  local purchase above applies on that course under the funded chart/stamp
  contract, also across its adjacent-stage transition. Combining it with
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-pair-reader> supplies the
  Gaussian-reader and compiler inputs of <eqref|eq:allarity-step-purchases>\U<eqref|eq:allarity-step-small>
  at the common marker weight, including the spare exponent
  <math|e<rsub|\<ast\>>>. This attachment does not assert the upstream
  construction of the funded family or the subsequent identification of the
  prescribed marked insertion; those are the separate hypotheses of the
  propagation theorem.

  The second part of (F) is inherited along the history. The reader site of a
  mark at the top level is the site of its insertion. At a coarser level we
  <em|define> the reader site of the mark to be the image of the reader site
  of the next finer level under the block map, that is, the coarse site whose
  block contains it. Say that a representation <em|contains its readers> if
  the full support of every letter carrying a mark contains the reader site
  of that mark at its level.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Reader containment is inherited><label|lem:allarity-reader-containment>Suppose
    that at the top level every insertion letter contains the reader site of
    its mark. Suppose also that at every step the coarse footprint of a fine
    support contains the projections of its points. Then every representation
    of the comparison interval contains its readers, before and after
    realignment and after the launch. In particular the second part of (F)
    holds.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    We argue by induction along the interval. Every letter of the coarse
    representation produced by the compiler is the flattening of a connected
    component of fine letters. Its full support contains the coarse footprint
    of the union of their supports, and its marker set is the union of their
    marker sets. If each fine marked letter contains its fine readers, then
    the component's support contains the fine reader of each of its marks. So
    the coarse footprint contains its projection, which is the coarse reader.
    The realignment replaces leading pieces by pieces on the prescribed
    supports <math|X<rsub|c,I>>, and these contain the readers because they
    contain the full support of the principal component of that mode by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-step-charge>. The launch uses those
    same modes. The complementary mask pieces keep their original supports.
    The forced regrouping again takes unions over components. At the launch,
    the failed-mask pieces live on the <math|X<rsub|c,I>>, and the regrouping
    takes unions. Each operation therefore preserves the property.
  </proof>

  Hypothesis (S) holds as soon as the halos of distinct cells are at
  <math|\<ell\><rsup|\<infty\>>>-distance at least <math|D<rprime|'>>, with
  <math|D<rprime|'>/<around|(|<around|(|2*B+1|)>*log H|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>.
  More precisely, every support <math|X>, of a forced or a regular letter,
  with <math|t<around|(|X|)>\<geqslant\>2> then satisfies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:allarity-dispersal><math-up|cov><rsub|B><space|0.17em>X\<geqslant\>\<lambda\>*<space|0.17em>t<around|(|X|)>,<space|2em>\<lambda\>=<frac|D<rprime|'>|3*<around|(|2*B+1|)>>.
  </equation>

  Indeed, use a connected cover of <math|X> by radius-<math|B> cubes and pick
  <math|x<rsub|c>\<in\>X\<cap\>Y<rsub|c>> for each of the
  <math|t<around|(|X|)>\<geqslant\>2> cells. The union of the cover contains
  a unit-step path from each <math|x<rsub|c>> to another chosen point. Stop
  the path at distance <math|<around|\<lfloor\>|D<rprime|'>/3|\<rfloor\>>>
  from <math|x<rsub|c>>. Its distances from <math|x<rsub|c>> take at least
  <math|<around|\<lfloor\>|D<rprime|'>/3|\<rfloor\>>+1> distinct values, of
  which a single cube can cover at most <math|2*B+1> since distance is
  1-Lipschitz. The segments stay in their respective balls of radius
  <math|D<rprime|'>/3>, and no cube can meet two such balls once
  <math|D<rprime|'>/3\<gtr\>2*B+1>. Thus <math|<math-up|cov><rsub|B><space|0.17em>X\<geqslant\>t<around|(|X|)>*D<rprime|'>/<around|(|3*<around|(|2*B+1|)>|)>>,
  also for disconnected <math|X>.

  For the chart of the pairwise receipt this separation holds, with room to
  spare. Distances below are <math|\<ell\><rsup|\<infty\>>>-distances in
  physical units; at level <math|i> a lattice distance is the physical
  distance divided by <math|a<rsub|i>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Halo separation for the trace chart><label|lem:allarity-halo-separation>Consider
    a chart at level <math|k> with middle level <math|\<ell\>> and root depth
    <math|w>, a configuration fit for it, and its replicas on the centered
    tiling at depth <math|w>. Assume that at every level <math|i> of
    <math|<around|[|\<ell\>,k|]>> each reader site of a cell lies within
    distance <math|2*a<rsub|\<ell\>>> of the centre of that cell. Then for
    all sufficiently large <math|s>, uniformly in the chart, the following
    hold at every level <math|i> of <math|<around|[|\<ell\>,k|]>>, with
    <math|B=B<rsub|i>>:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>reader sites of distinct cells are at lattice distance greater
      than <math|2*Q*B>;

      <item>the halos of distinct cells are at lattice distance at least
      <math|D<rprime|'>=c<rsub|k><rsup|10>/2>, and
      <math|D<rprime|'>/<around|(|<around|(|2*B+1|)>*log
      H|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>.
    </enumerate>

    In particular the separation hypothesis of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-step-charge> and hypothesis (S) hold.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The cells of the centered tiling at depth <math|w> are blocks of side
    <math|a<rsub|w>>, and the centres of distinct cells are at distance at
    least <math|a<rsub|w>>. By the hypothesis, readers of distinct cells are
    at physical distance at least <math|a<rsub|w>-4*a<rsub|\<ell\>>>. Since
    <math|a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|\<ell\>>> for
    <math|\<ell\>\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>k>, their lattice distance at level
    <math|i> is at least <math|<around|(|a<rsub|w>-4*a<rsub|\<ell\>>|)>/a<rsub|\<ell\>>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|k><rsup|10>-4>,
    by the definition of the root depth, <math|a<rsub|w>/a<rsub|\<ell\>>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|k><rsup|10>>.
    A halo lies within lattice distance <math|Q*B> of the readers of its
    cell, so the halos of distinct cells are at lattice distance at least
    <math|c<rsub|k><rsup|10>-4-2*Q*B>.

    The rung couplings lie in <math|<around|[|s/2,s|]>>, so
    <math|B<rsub|i>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>4<around|(|log
    s|)><rsup|4>> for <math|log s\<geqslant\>1>. Also
    <math|c<rsub|k>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    s|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>\<geqslant\><around|(|log s|)><rsup|2>>, so
    <math|c<rsub|k><rsup|10>\<geqslant\><around|(|log s|)><rsup|20>>. Hence
    <math|c<rsub|k><rsup|10>-4-2*Q*B\<geqslant\>c<rsub|k><rsup|10>/2\<gtr\>2*Q*B>
    for large <math|s>, which gives both separations. Finally <math|log
    H=<around|(|log log s|)><rsup|2>>, so

    <\equation*>
      <frac|D<rprime|'>|<around|(|2*B+1|)>*log
      H>\<geqslant\><frac|<around|(|log s|)><rsup|20>|2*<around|(|8<around|(|log
      s|)><rsup|4>+1|)><around|(|log log s|)><rsup|2>>\<longrightarrow\>\<infty\>.
    </equation*>

    All thresholds depend only on <math|Q> and not on the chart.
  </proof>

  The hypothesis on the readers holds for fit configurations. Every
  coordinate of the grid configuration <math|a<rsub|k>*<around|\<lceil\>|m*x|\<rceil\>>>
  has absolute value at most <math|a<rsub|\<ell\>>/8+a<rsub|k>> relative to
  the base point. The replicas are its images in the cells, centred and
  reflected or conjugated with the cells. A reader site at a coarser level
  <math|i> is within one lattice spacing <math|a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|\<ell\>>>
  of the corresponding insertion site. So each reader is within
  <math|a<rsub|\<ell\>>/8+a<rsub|k>+a<rsub|\<ell\>>\<leqslant\>2*a<rsub|\<ell\>>>
  of its cell centre.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The regular remainder at the restored
    weight><label|lem:allarity-remainder-restored>Assume the hypotheses of
    Lemma <reference|lem:allarity-spare-run>, those of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-reader-containment>, and that the
    halos of distinct cells are at distance at least <math|D<rprime|'>> at
    the middle level, with <math|D<rprime|'>/<around|(|<around|(|2*B+1|)>*log
    H|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>. Assume <math|q\<geqslant\>\<kappa\>+U*n+2>. Then,
    for all large <math|s>, the nonleading regular remainder at the middle
    level has marker-root price at most <math|H<rsup|-1>> at the weight
    <math|\<omega\>> and the support exponent <math|e>, uniformly in the
    replica cells and the period.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Split the remainder as in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-weight-raise>,
    according to <math|t<around|(|I|)>=1> or
    <math|t<around|(|I|)>\<geqslant\>2>. Prices are monotone in the support
    exponent, since <math|<math-up|chg><rsub|e>> is increasing in <math|e>.
    So Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-spare-run> gives
    <math|p<rsub|1>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<kappa\>-q>> at the exponent <math|e>,
    and <math|p<rsub|2>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<kappa\>-q>> at the exponent
    <math|e+\<sigma\>>, both at the weight <math|\<omega\><rsub|->>.

    Let a regular letter have marker set <math|I>, with
    <math|t<around|(|I|)>\<geqslant\>2>, and full support <math|X>. By
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-reader-containment>, <math|X> contains
    the reader site <math|r<rsub|c,j>> of each of its marks
    <math|<around|(|c,j|)>>. Since <math|r<rsub|c,j>\<in\>X<rsub|c,<around|{|j|}>>\<subseteq\>Y<rsub|c>>,
    the support meets the halo of every cell represented in <math|I>, so
    <math|t<around|(|X|)>\<geqslant\>t<around|(|I|)>\<geqslant\>2>. By
    <eqref|eq:allarity-dispersal>,

    <\equation*>
      \<sigma\><math-up|cov><rsub|B><space|0.17em>X\<geqslant\>\<sigma\>*\<lambda\>*<space|0.17em>t<around|(|I|)>\<geqslant\>U*n*<space|0.17em>t<around|(|I|)>*log
      H
    </equation*>

    as soon as <math|\<sigma\>*\<lambda\>\<geqslant\>U*n*log H>. This holds
    for all large <math|s>, because <math|\<lambda\>/log H\<to\>\<infty\>>
    and <math|\<sigma\>,U,n> are fixed. This is the support inequality of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-weight-raise>. That lemma gives the
    price <math|H<rsup|U*n>*p<rsub|1>+p<rsub|2>\<leqslant\>2*H<rsup|U*n+\<kappa\>-q>\<leqslant\>2*H<rsup|-2>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1>>
    at the weight <math|\<omega\>> and the exponent <math|e>, for
    <math|H\<geqslant\>2>.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Failed-mask regrouping and weight
    change><label|lem:allarity-launch-regroup>Assume (F), (S) and the
    hypotheses of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-corrected-pieces>, and
    let <math|\<eta\><rsub|->\<gtr\>0> decay faster than every negative power
    of <math|H>. Then, for all large <math|s>, the following holds. Move the
    failed-mask pieces <math|-<around|(|1-\<chi\>|)>*R*<space|0.17em>z<rsub|I>>
    of all modes into the forced alphabet and regroup them with the forced
    background. The result is an exact representation of the same density,
    whose regular letters are unchanged. Its forced site price at the weight
    <math|\<omega\>> and the support exponent <math|e> is at most

    <\equation*>
      4*H<rsup|U*n+2<rsup|n>>*\<eta\><rsub|->.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    <em|Exactness.> The marker algebra is commutative, so

    <\equation*>
      exp<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|\<chi\>*W-R|)>=exp<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|\<chi\>*<around|(|W-R|)>|)>*exp<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|-<around|(|1-\<chi\>|)>*R|)>
    </equation*>

    letter by letter. Each failed-mask piece
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|f>=-<around|(|1-\<chi\>|)>*R*<space|0.17em>z<rsub|I>>
    has <math|I\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>, so <math|\<ell\><rsub|f><rsup|2>=0> and
    <math|exp<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|\<ell\><rsub|f>|)>=1+\<ell\><rsub|f>>.
    It vanishes where its prescribed support <math|X<rsub|c,I>> is healthy,
    so a nonzero failed-mask factor has a flag in <math|X<rsub|c,I>>. Flags
    are covered by the inventories of the old forced pieces. Hence, in the
    expansion of the product of the factors <math|1+\<ell\><rsub|f>> with the
    old forced gas, every nonzero summand has each failed-mask factor meeting
    the support of an old forced piece. Taking connected components of the
    full-support intersection graph and flattening them is the exact
    regrouping of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:soft-log-fusion>, as in the
    mask change of the realignment subsection. The regular letters are not
    touched. Setting all markers to zero kills every failed-mask factor, so
    the background is unchanged.

    <em|Prices of the failed-mask pieces.> By
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-amplitude> and its proof, every
    reference coefficient satisfies <math|<around|\||R|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|\<ast\>>>.
    A failed-mask piece of mode <math|<around|(|c,I|)>> therefore has price
    at most <math|p<rsub|f>=H<rsup|1/8>*C<rsub|\<ast\>>*R<rsub|e+1>> at the
    weight <math|\<omega\>> and the exponent <math|e+1>, by
    <eqref|eq:allarity-weight-cap>. Put <math|F=log H> and give it the weight
    <math|w<rsub|f>=e<rsup|-F>*p<rsub|f>>. At a fixed site, all failed-mask
    pieces containing it belong to one cell, and there are at most
    <math|2<rsup|n>> modes. So their site sum is at most
    <math|2<rsup|n>*C<rsub|\<ast\>>*R<rsub|e+1>*H<rsup|-7/8>>.

    <em|Prices of the old forced pieces.> Let <math|o> be an old forced piece
    with full support <math|X<rsub|o>>, marker set <math|I<rsub|o>> and
    <math|t<rsub|o>=t<around|(|X<rsub|o>|)>>. By (F), the readers of its
    marks lie in <math|X<rsub|o>>, hence in the halos it meets, so
    <math|<around|\||I<rsub|o>|\|>\<leqslant\>n*t<rsub|o>>. Raising the
    weight from <math|\<omega\><rsub|->> to <math|\<omega\>> multiplies its
    price by <math|H<rsup|U<around|\||I<rsub|o>|\|>>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|U*n*t<rsub|o>>>.
    Lowering the support exponent from <math|e+1+\<sigma\>> to <math|e+1>
    multiplies it by <math|e<rsup|-\<sigma\><math-up|cov><rsub|B><space|0.17em>X<rsub|o>>>.
    Give <math|o> the weight

    <\equation*>
      w<rsub|o>=e<rsup|g>*<space|0.17em>e<rsup|2<rsup|n>*F*t<rsub|o>>*<space|0.17em>p<rsub|o>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|p<rsub|o>> is its price at <math|<around|(|\<omega\>,e+1|)>>
    and <math|g\<geqslant\>0> is chosen below. Then
    <math|w<rsub|o>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|g>*H<rsup|<around|(|U*n+2<rsup|n>|)>*t<rsub|o>>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\><math-up|cov><rsub|B><space|0.17em>X<rsub|o>>*p<rsub|o><rsup|->>,
    with <math|p<rsub|o><rsup|->> its price at
    <math|<around|(|\<omega\><rsub|->,e+1+\<sigma\>|)>>. If
    <math|t<rsub|o>\<leqslant\>1> this is at most
    <math|e<rsup|g>*H<rsup|U*n+2<rsup|n>>*p<rsub|o><rsup|->>. If
    <math|t<rsub|o>\<geqslant\>2>, then <math|\<sigma\><math-up|cov><rsub|B><space|0.17em>X<rsub|o>\<geqslant\>\<sigma\>*\<lambda\>*t<rsub|o>\<geqslant\><around|(|U*n+2<rsup|n>|)>*t<rsub|o>*log
    H> for large <math|s>, by (S), and <math|w<rsub|o>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|g>*p<rsub|o><rsup|->>.
    In both cases <math|w<rsub|o>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|g>*H<rsup|U*n+2<rsup|n>>*p<rsub|o><rsup|->>,
    so the old site sum is at most <math|e<rsup|g>*H<rsup|U*n+2<rsup|n>>*\<eta\><rsub|->>.

    <em|Choice of <math|g>.> Put <math|\<delta\>=H<rsup|-1/2>> and
    <math|e<rsup|g>=\<delta\>/<around|(|2*H<rsup|U*n+2<rsup|n>>*\<eta\><rsub|->|)>>.
    This is at least <math|1> for large <math|s>, because
    <math|\<eta\><rsub|->> decays faster than every negative power of
    <math|H>. The total site sum of the weights at exponent <math|e+1> is
    then

    <\equation*>
      \<Gamma\>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>\<delta\>+2<rsup|n>*C<rsub|\<ast\>>*R<rsub|e+1>*H<rsup|-7/8>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>
    </equation*>

    for large <math|s>.

    <em|Components.> Every nonzero surviving component contains an old forced
    piece. Charge each of its failed-mask pieces <math|f>, of cell <math|c>,
    to an old piece <math|o> of the same component that it meets. Then
    <math|X<rsub|o>> meets <math|Y<rsub|c>>, so <math|c> is one of the
    <math|t<rsub|o>> cells counted for <math|o>. At most
    <math|2<rsup|n>*t<rsub|o>> failed-mask pieces are charged to <math|o>. If
    the component has <math|s<rsub|f>> failed-mask pieces and
    <math|r\<geqslant\>1> old pieces, then
    <math|s<rsub|f>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|n>*<big|sum><rsub|o>t<rsub|o>>, and the
    product of the true prices over the component is

    <\equation*>
      <big|prod><rsub|f>p<rsub|f>*<big|prod><rsub|o>p<rsub|o>=e<rsup|F*s<rsub|f>-2<rsup|n>*F*<big|sum><rsub|o>t<rsub|o>-g*r>*<big|prod><rsub|f>w<rsub|f>*<big|prod><rsub|o>w<rsub|o>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-g>*<big|prod><rsub|f>w<rsub|f>*<big|prod><rsub|o>w<rsub|o>,
    </equation*>

    since <math|g\<geqslant\>0>. The distinct connected-set bound
    <eqref|eq:ope-forest-price>, with reserve one and counting factor
    <math|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>>, then bounds the new site price at the
    exponent <math|e> by <math|e<rsup|-g>*<around|(|e<rsup|\<Gamma\>>-1|)>\<leqslant\>2*\<delta\>*e<rsup|-g>=4*H<rsup|U*n+2<rsup|n>>*\<eta\><rsub|->>,
    provided <math|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*\<delta\>\<leqslant\>1> and
    <math|\<delta\>\<leqslant\>1>. The first condition holds for large
    <math|s> as in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-step-purchases>, since
    <math|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>exp <around|{|4*log 9+16*log
    log s|}>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|1/2>> once <math|log log s\<geqslant\>36>.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The launch receipt><label|prop:allarity-launch>Assume the
    conclusions of Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:allarity-step-supply> on
    the leading interval, with remainder <math|d<rsub|k-\<ell\>>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<kappa\>-q>>
    and forced price <math|G<around|(|x<rsub|\<ell\>>|)>> at the weight
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|->>. Assume Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-corrected-pieces>
    at the middle level, the hypotheses of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-spare-run>, those of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-reader-containment>, and that the
    halos of distinct cells are at distance at least <math|D<rprime|'>> at
    the middle level, with <math|D<rprime|'>/<around|(|<around|(|2*B+1|)>*log
    H|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>. Assume <math|q\<geqslant\>\<kappa\>+U*n+2>. Then,
    for all large <math|s>, the pushed invoice at the middle level has an
    exact representation at the weight <math|\<omega\>>, without leading
    letters, with regular marker-root price at most <math|H<rsup|-3/4>> and
    forced site price at most <math|H<rsup|-q>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    By the commuting reference factor, the pushed invoice at <math|\<ell\>>
    is the pushed insertion product times
    <math|exp<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|-D<rsub|R>|)>>. The representation from
    the leading interval has three parts. Their prices are as follows.

    <em|Remainder.> Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-remainder-restored>
    gives the price <math|H<rsup|-1>> at the weight <math|\<omega\>> for the
    entire nonleading regular remainder. Hypotheses (F) and (S) hold: the
    first clause of (F) is Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-spare-run>,
    with <math|\<eta\><rsub|->=G<around|(|x<rsub|\<ell\>>|)>>, its second
    clause is Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-reader-containment>, and (S)
    is <eqref|eq:allarity-dispersal>.

    <em|Leading family.> Its pieces are the exact prescribed principal
    polynomial. Multiplying by <math|exp<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|-D<rsub|R>|)>>
    and splitting each corrected coefficient by the failed-mask identity
    gives healthy pieces <math|\<chi\>*<around|(|W-R|)>> and failed-mask
    pieces. Drop the leading designation. The healthy pieces become regular
    letters of incidence price at most <math|c*H<rsup|-27/32>>, by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-corrected-pieces>.

    <em|Forced part.> The forced background has site price at most
    <math|\<eta\><rsub|->=G<around|(|x<rsub|\<ell\>>|)>> at the weight
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|->> and exponent <math|e+1+\<sigma\>>, by (F). This
    is the realigned forced price of the recurrence, not the compiler output
    <math|G<around|(|x<rsub|\<ell\>>|)><rsup|2>> before realignment. By
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-launch-regroup>, after the failed-mask
    pieces are moved and regrouped, the forced site price at the weight
    <math|\<omega\>> is at most <math|4*H<rsup|U*n+2<rsup|n>>*G<around|(|x<rsub|\<ell\>>|)>>.

    Adding the regular contributions gives at most
    <math|H<rsup|-1>+c*H<rsup|-27/32>>, which is at most <math|H<rsup|-3/4>>
    once <math|H<rsup|3/32>\<geqslant\>c+1>. Since <math|G> decays faster
    than every negative power of <math|H> uniformly on
    <math|<around|[|s/2,s|]>>, eventually
    <math|4*H<rsup|U*n+2<rsup|n>>*G<around|(|x<rsub|\<ell\>>|)>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-q>>.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The step counts without a block-length
  floor><label|subsec:allarity-lower-count>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The lower step count is free><label|lem:allarity-lower-count-free>Let
    the clock's cover size at level <math|k> be
    <math|c<rsub|k>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>, and let the root depth
    be <math|w=max <around|{|j\<leqslant\>\<ell\>:a<rsub|j>/a<rsub|\<ell\>>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|k><rsup|10>|}>>.
    Since all block lengths satisfy <math|L<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>3>, for every
    fixed <math|\<gamma\>\<gtr\>0> and all sufficiently large
    <math|s=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>, uniformly in the chart,
    <math|<around|(|\<alpha\>+1|)><rsup|\<ell\>-w>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<gamma\>>>.
    In particular the lower step count <eqref|eq:allarity-lower-count> holds.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    If <math|w=\<ell\>> there is nothing to prove. Otherwise, by maximality
    of <math|w>, <math|a<rsub|w+1>/a<rsub|\<ell\>>\<less\>c<rsub|k><rsup|10>>.
    By Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection>,
    <math|a<rsub|w+1>/a<rsub|\<ell\>>=<big|prod><rsub|t=w+2><rsup|\<ell\>>L<rsub|J<around|(|t|)>>\<geqslant\>3<rsup|\<ell\>-w-1>>.
    For <math|log s\<geqslant\>1> we have
    <math|c<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>2<around|(|log s|)><rsup|2>>. Hence

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|\<ell\>-w-1|)>*log 3\<less\>10*log
      c<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>10*log 2+20*log log s.
    </equation*>

    Therefore

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|\<ell\>-w|)>*log <around|(|\<alpha\>+1|)>\<leqslant\>log
      <around|(|\<alpha\>+1|)>*<around*|(|1+<frac|10*log 2+20*log log s|log
      3>|)>=A<rsub|0>+A<rsub|1>*log log s,
    </equation*>

    with constants <math|A<rsub|0>,A<rsub|1>\<geqslant\>0> fixed by
    <math|\<alpha\>>. Since <math|\<gamma\>\<gtr\>0> and <math|log
    H=<around|(|log log s|)><rsup|2>>, the right side is at most
    <math|\<gamma\>*log H> for all large <math|s>. No block-length floor and
    no stage condition is used.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The upper step count at a fixed
    power><label|lem:allarity-upper-count-power>Let the base arc satisfy
    <math|b\<leqslant\><wide|b|\<bar\>>>, and put

    <\equation*>
      \<kappa\>=1+<frac|<around|(|<wide|b|\<bar\>>+2|)>*log
      <around|(|\<alpha\>+2|)>|log 3>.
    </equation*>

    For all sufficiently large <math|s>, uniformly in the chart,
    <math|<around|(|\<alpha\>+2|)><rsup|k-\<ell\>>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<kappa\>>>.
    Consequently the remainder price of the recurrence at the middle level is
    <math|d<rsub|k-\<ell\>>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<kappa\>-q>> at the weight
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|->>. If <math|q\<geqslant\>\<kappa\>+4>, this is at
    most <math|H<rsup|-4>>, which is the window required in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-step-outputs>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    If <math|\<ell\>=k> there is nothing to prove. Otherwise
    <math|a<rsub|\<ell\>+1>/a<rsub|k>\<less\>m*\<mu\><rsub|k><rsup|2>=H<rsup|b+2>>
    by maximality of <math|\<ell\>>. Also
    <math|a<rsub|\<ell\>+1>/a<rsub|k>=<big|prod><rsub|t=\<ell\>+2><rsup|k>L<rsub|J<around|(|t|)>>\<geqslant\>3<rsup|k-\<ell\>-1>>.
    Hence <math|<around|(|k-\<ell\>-1|)>*log 3\<less\><around|(|b+2|)>*log
    H>, and

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|k-\<ell\>|)>*log <around|(|\<alpha\>+2|)>\<leqslant\>log
      <around|(|\<alpha\>+2|)>+<frac|<around|(|<wide|b|\<bar\>>+2|)>*log
      <around|(|\<alpha\>+2|)>|log 3>*log H\<leqslant\>\<kappa\>*log H
    </equation*>

    once <math|log H\<geqslant\>log <around|(|\<alpha\>+2|)>>. Then
    <math|d<rsub|k-\<ell\>>=<around|(|\<alpha\>+2|)><rsup|k-\<ell\>>*H<rsup|-q>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<kappa\>-q>>.
  </proof>

  The parameters are therefore fixed in the order <math|e>, <math|\<sigma\>>,
  then <math|\<alpha\>\<geqslant\>8*T<rsub|e+1+\<sigma\>>>,
  <math|<wide|b|\<bar\>>>, then <math|U> (by
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-leading-budget>), then

  <\equation*>
    q\<geqslant\>max <around|{|6,\<kappa\>+4|}>,
  </equation*>

  which covers Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-step-outputs>. The launch
  also requires <math|q\<geqslant\>\<kappa\>+U*n+2>, for
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-remainder-restored>. All are fixed
  before <math|s>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The forced inputs of the lower
    rungs><label|lem:allarity-lower-forced>In
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-lower-propagation>, hypothesis<nbsp>3
    can be dropped. More precisely, take the incoming forced budget of each
    lower rung to be the forced output <math|G<around|(|y|)>> of the
    preceding lower rung, and at the transition the forced output of the
    launch, which is at most <math|H<rsup|-q>> by
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:allarity-launch>. Replace the bounds
    <math|z<rsub|t>> by <math|max <around|(|z<rsub|t>,H<rsup|-q>|)>>. Then
    the conclusions of that lemma hold for all large <math|s>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The lower calculation of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-mixed-marked-compiler>
    uses its incoming forced budget <math|\<eta\>> in exactly two places: the
    marker-root term <math|T<rsub|e>*\<eta\>>, and the site term
    <math|\<cal-W\><around|(|B<rsub|c>|)>*L<rsup|4>*T<rsub|e>*\<eta\>>. With
    <math|\<eta\>=z*\<omega\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>z>, it bounds them by
    <math|T<rsub|e>*z> and by <math|K/16> for
    <math|z\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1/2>>. Both bounds hold verbatim for any
    <math|\<eta\>\<leqslant\>z>. Now <math|G> decays faster than every power
    of <math|H> uniformly on <math|<around|[|s/2,s|]>>, so for large <math|s>
    every incoming forced budget of a lower rung is at most
    <math|H<rsup|-q>\<leqslant\>max <around|(|z<rsub|t>,H<rsup|-q>|)>>; at
    the transition this is the launch bound. Replacing <math|z<rsub|t>> by
    <math|max <around|(|z<rsub|t>,H<rsup|-q>|)>> keeps the induction of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-lower-propagation>: if
    <math|H<rsup|-q>\<leqslant\>z<rsub|t>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1/2>>, then
    <math|max <around|(|\<alpha\>*z<rsub|t>+H<rsup|1/2>*z<rsub|t><rsup|2>,H<rsup|-q>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<alpha\>+1|)>*z<rsub|t>>,
    since <math|\<alpha\>\<geqslant\>1>. The starting value is <math|max
    <around|(|z<rsub|0>,H<rsup|-q>|)>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-3/4>>. The forced
    outputs are at most <math|G<around|(|y<rsub|j>|)>\<leqslant\>1> as
    before.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The amplitude at the middle level><label|lem:allarity-amplitude>Under
    the hypotheses of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-corrected-pieces>
    there is a constant <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>>, depending on the fixed word
    and the compact window, such that for all large <math|s> every actual
    leading piece at the middle level is bounded by <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>> on
    its healthy region.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The reference moment polynomial <math|1+X<rsub|R>> varies continuously on
    the compact window, which consists of configurations with distinct
    points. So <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|R>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|\<cal-W\>>>
    for a constant <math|M<rsub|\<cal-W\>>>, and
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsub|R>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|n>M<rsub|\<cal-W\>><rsup|j>/j>
    by submultiplicativity. By Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-corrected-pieces>,
    each actual leading piece is within <math|H<rsup|-31/32>\<leqslant\>1> of
    a coefficient of <math|D<rsub|R>>. Take
    <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>=1+<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|n>M<rsub|\<cal-W\>><rsup|j>/j>.
  </proof>

  The lemma bounds the amplitude at the middle level only. At the other
  leading rungs the discrepancy lemma uses the power bound of
  Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:allarity-rung-amplitude>.

  <subparagraph|Status of the propagation to the output.>
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:allarity-output-price> is proved from the
  following inputs, all on the common history of the chart:

  <\enumerate>
    <item>the four inputs listed at the end of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:allarity-step-supply> for the leading
    interval: applicability of the upper calculation; the hypotheses of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-healthy-cumulant-launch> at every
    leading rung and the terminal-source purchase; the common insertion and
    bounded transport (CI), which gives the identification of the prescribed
    polynomial by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-prescribed-reference>
    (the per-rung amplitude follows by Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:allarity-rung-amplitude>);
    the reader separation <math|2*Q*B>, which holds for the trace chart by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-halo-separation>;

    <item>the two <math|H<rsup|-3/2>> inputs of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-corrected-pieces> at the middle level,
    which are proved in Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-cumulant-comparison>
    and<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-healthy-cumulant-launch> under their
    stated hypotheses (the reader covariance and vertex bounds, the exact
    low-grade calibration of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-coordinate-calibration>,
    the mixed compiler's per-step row and covariance comparison, the terminal
    paid insertion bound and the common purchases);

    <item>the inputs of item<nbsp>1 at the exponent
    <math|e<rsub|\<ast\>>=e+1+\<sigma\>>, which is (E). By
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-spare-run> this gives the forced
    clause of (F) and, by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-remainder-restored>,
    the regular remainder at the restored weight. Also needed is the
    structural property used in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-reader-containment>,
    that coarse footprints contain the projections of fine supports
    (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:mixed-activity-prices>). It gives the
    second part of (F). Hypothesis (S) and the reader separation <math|2*Q*B>
    hold for the trace chart by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-halo-separation>;

    <item>applicability of the lower calculation on
    <math|<around|[|w,\<ell\>|)>>;

    <item>the lawful representation at the output level <math|w>, with the
    suppositions of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:rank-root-normalization>.
  </enumerate>

  The rung couplings lie in <math|<around|[|s/2,s|]>> on the whole window
  <math|<around|[|w,k|]>> by the plateau field of the chart witness, which is
  proved from the clock in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-firesky-clock-fields>;
  the same lemma proves the bracket. The step counts, the forced inputs of
  the lower rungs and the leading budget at the small weight are proved
  above. The polynomial transport purchase of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-healthy-cumulant-launch> is separate
  from the step counts and remains one of the common purchases.

  <subsubsection|The selected marked polynomial on the common history>

  The preceding abstract hypotheses can now be checked for the selected
  family. In making this check it is important to retain the higher
  coefficients already produced above the opening level. Write
  <math|q<rsub|N>> for its terminal marked density and <math|q<rsub|k>> for
  its actual push to the opening level. In general <math|q<rsub|k>> is not
  the product of the unmarked density and its singleton insertions.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The complete opening polynomial><label|lem:allarity-selected-opening>Fix
    the finite grade menu, the compact distinct-point window, the finite
    census of signed copies, and the requested support exponent
    <math|e<rsub|\<ast\>>=e+1+\<sigma\>>. For any fixed <math|q>, the
    selected calibrated family admits, on its actual restarted endpoint, a
    presentation of <math|q<rsub|k>> with regular remainder at most
    <math|H<rsup|-q>> and forced site price at most <math|G<around|(|s|)>>,
    at a weight <math|\<omega\><rsub|->=H<rsup|1/<around|(|8*u|)>-U>> with
    <math|U> fixed before the coupling threshold. Its leading letters are
    exactly the masked calibrated raw singletons of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:rank-selected-tested-readout>; all other
    connected coefficients belong to the remainder or the forced family. The
    statement holds simultaneously at all smaller support exponents and
    uniformly in the permitted periods and cutoffs.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Start with the complete served polynomial, rather than constructing a new
    product at <math|k>. The finite-menu push identity gives the ordinary and
    forced families <math|A<rsub|\<nu\>>,K<rsub|\<lambda\>>> of the original
    mixed-grade expansion in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-firesky-gallery>.
    Its proof preceding <eqref|eq:trace-original-marked-payment> uses only
    the selected service, its finite-menu propagation and the planted
    recombination identity; it does not use the present output estimate. It
    gives a rooted budget one for the entire higher-mode family at the
    original radius <math|x=s<rsup|-z/2>>. The census and label payments
    there are verified from the calibrated coefficient norm, including the
    signed copies.

    Put <math|f=1/<around|(|8*u|)>-U>. Increase <math|U> so that
    <math|f\<leqslant\>0> and the weighted singleton column price is at most
    <math|H<rsup|-8>>. The change of weights
    <eqref|eq:trace-endpoint-marked-budget>, with <math|g=q+2> and support
    exponent <math|e<rsub|\<ast\>>>, bounds the sum of all higher ordinary
    modes at each marked root by <math|H<rsup|-q-10>>. It applies to the
    forced modes as well, without deleting their original inventories or
    eligibility tests. This purchase uses <math|C\<sim\><around|(|log
    s|)><rsup|2>>, <math|B=C<rsup|2>> and the separated origins, not a
    comparison of <math|H<rsup|f>> with the much smaller radius <math|x>. Its
    support reserve pays the conversion of marked roots to spatial hits, with
    factor <math|W<around|(|B|)>=H<rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>>.

    For each singleton use the exact split
    <eqref|eq:rank-terminal-three-pieces>, leaving its wide tail in the
    remainder. The near Taylor error has weighted price <math|H<rsup|f+E-D>>
    and the wide tail has price at most <math|H<rsup|f+E>*exp
    <around|(|-\<theta\>*C|)>>, with <math|E> the fixed solved-lift exponent.
    Take <math|D\<gtr\>E+q+<around|\||f|\|>+3>. Both are at most
    <math|H<rsup|-q-2>> eventually, including their fixed halo prices. The
    finitely many contributions and the higher-mode price therefore sum to at
    most <math|H<rsup|-q>>. The near polynomial has precisely the prescribed
    axial reads and mask; changing an old mask to that mask puts their
    difference among the failed-mask terms. Their weighted price is at most a
    constant times <math|H<rsup|-8>>, by increasing <math|U> once more if
    necessary.

    This last price is not by itself bounded by <math|G<around|(|s|)>>. To
    obtain the forced bound, perform the endpoint expansion of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:restart-endpoint> jointly with these
    marked letters. Retain its site factors <math|X<rsub|z>>, its optional
    factors <math|W<rsub|Y>>, and the old forced collection before summing
    connected components. Every failed-mask term meets a new flag. Every old
    forced letter, including a higher marked mode, contains an old flag and
    hence a new flag. Join intersecting enlarged supports, including the
    radius-<math|4*B> support of every selected <math|X<rsub|z>>. Thus each
    forced component contains an <math|X<rsub|z>>. Reserve its factor
    <math|e<rsup|-u>=G<around|(|s|)>> once per component. Record the full
    marker mode and both old and new inventories; preserve all old
    compatibility and eligibility tests inside the component. Decomposition
    into components is reversible coefficient by coefficient in the finite
    squarefree algebra, just as in <eqref|eq:restart-endpoint-factor>. In
    particular no higher coefficient of <math|q<rsub|k>> has been discarded.

    After reserving this factor the remaining weighted rooted sum is bounded
    by

    <\equation*>
      r<rsub|s>=2*H<rsup|-3>+t<rsub|s>+m<rsub|s>+b<rsub|s>,
    </equation*>

    where the four explicit endpoint prices in the paragraph following
    <eqref|eq:trace-endpoint-marked-budget> are taken at <math|g=q+2> and
    <math|e<rsub|\<ast\>>>. The singleton forced part uses its original
    <math|<sqrt|exp <around|(|-H<rsup|1/5>|)>>> allowance; the higher forced
    part uses the proved change of weights. The new failed masks contribute
    to <math|t<rsub|s>>, not a separate unweighted forced gas. Those
    estimates give <math|r<rsub|s>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1>> and
    <math|Z<around|(|B|)>*r<rsub|s>\<leqslant\>1> on one tail. The rooted
    connected sum is consequently bounded by

    <\equation*>
      G<around|(|s|)>*<around|(|e<rsup|r<rsub|s>>-1|)>\<leqslant\>G<around|(|s|)>.
    </equation*>

    This argument also proves absolute convergence when original local labels
    are countable. All sums used to group equal supports, modes and
    inventories are dominated by these positive majorants. The exact unmarked
    coefficient and its scalar normalization are unchanged.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Application to the selected chart><label|prop:allarity-selected-application>For
    the selected funded family, with the common chart purchase of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:trace-joint-chart-purchase>, the
    calibrated readout of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:rank-selected-tested-readout>,
    and the clock of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-firesky-clock-fields>,
    all hypotheses of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:allarity-output-price>
    hold on a tail of its purchased stages. They hold for the complete pushed
    marked polynomial <math|q<rsub|k>> and the invoice formed from its
    prescribed Gaussian moments. The output coefficient price and root test
    of that proposition therefore apply on this same family and with its
    original partition denominator.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Fix the word and window first. Choose <math|e,\<sigma\>>, the raw
    compiler reserve of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:allarity-compiler-reserve>,
    and <math|\<alpha\>\<geqslant\>8*T<rsub|e+2>>. Choose <math|U> to pay
    both Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-leading-budget> and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-selected-opening>; then choose
    <math|q\<geqslant\>max <around|(|6,\<kappa\>+4,\<kappa\>+U*n+2|)>> and
    the Taylor order <math|D> in that lemma. These requests precede the stage
    orders, block sides and entry delays. In this order use the
    degree-independent exponent <math|A=n*<around|(|n*z+1|)>+2> of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-principal-power>, with <math|z> the
    solved-lift coefficient exponent, which depends only on the fixed grade
    menu. After <math|D> and the smoothing orders, the row bound
    <eqref|eq:actual-transport-difference> and the normalized covariance
    bound of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-covariance-transport> give
    a common constant <math|C> before the block side. Thus the polynomial
    norm <math|A<rsub|n*D>=<around|[|2*<around|(|n*D+2|)>*C|]><rsup|n*D>> is
    fixed before imposing <math|2*<around|(|<wide|b|\<bar\>>+2|)>*log
    A<rsub|n*D>\<leqslant\>log L> on both adjacent sides. The endpoint
    support suppression can be requested at any fixed <math|q>, so this
    ordering does not require changing <math|U> after <math|q>.

    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:restart-actual-closure> constructs the
    actual restarted course with the original paths, clips and fine-to-coarse
    maps. On each step its corrected alphabet has the body, deleted-support
    and forced identities at the raw reserved exponent. Proposition
    <reference|prop:allarity-compiler-reserve> therefore supplies the upper
    calculation at <math|e<rsub|\<ast\>>> and the lower one at <math|e>, for
    this same alphabet. The original forced family is kept before aggregation
    at each forced address. This is exactly the family required by the
    planted expansion; an arbitrary presentation of the same aggregate would
    not suffice.

    At a leading step use the one joint Gaussian reader of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-reader-dawn-selection>. Its sample
    and loading are those of the corrected principal integral. For a product,
    use the union of its read supports and keep every factor's guard on this
    same sample. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-pair-reader> applies at a
    stage boundary with the minimum of the two strip widths and the separate
    metric floors. The mean is the actual installed transport row; its
    covariance is the short covariance of this same step. The polynomial
    comparison is defined by Wick contraction with these data. To identify
    the principal component explicitly, observe that an entirely leading
    component has no body letter and compatibility excludes background
    packets. Its integrand is a product of guarded principal values on the
    common Haar\UGaussian sample space. On a positive guard, the field is
    healthy on the full prescribed mask support, and that value is
    <math|p<rsub|I><around|(|\<xi\><rsub|t,I>|)>>. Gauge invariance removes
    the patch gauge from each physical value. Whole-mode selection sums these
    products over partitions, with the same nonnegative forest/tag weights
    and loading for every product, including the empty product, whose
    integral is one. These are exactly the moments <math|M<rsub|S>> of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-guarded-gaussian>; Wick contraction
    with the same forecast and short covariance gives its prescribed moments
    <math|M<rsub|S><rsup|\<ast\>>>. This identifies the principal component,
    without identifying the whole interacting density as Gaussian or
    replacing its loading by a second partition function. Taking the finite
    logarithm therefore gives the discrepancy to which that lemma applies.
    Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:allarity-guarded-purchase> pays their
    discrepancy at the requested power of <math|H>.

    The mean-row and covariance comparisons in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-covariance-transport> use these
    very rows and covariances. Their atlas is determined by the selected
    horizon, and Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:rank-selected-tested-readout>
    supplies its common calibrated axes. Hence its exact low-grade equalities
    are those of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-coordinate-calibration>.
    Apply the polynomial transport comparison and the closed-cochain
    construction of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-healthy-cumulant-launch>
    with these axes. The transport norm is bounded by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-principal-power>; the per-step error
    is purchased before the final threshold to dominate that fixed power and
    the step count. The two middle errors are therefore the
    <math|H<rsup|-3/2>> errors in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-corrected-pieces>,
    for one prescribed moment element. The finite logarithm estimate of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-cumulant-comparison> then gives
    the stated <math|H<rsup|-31/32>> healthy comparison.

    The opening lemma starts the upper recurrence at
    <math|H<rsup|-q>,G<around|(|s|)>>, including the original higher modes.
    Lemma <reference|lem:allarity-upper-count-power> keeps it inside its
    domain. Run it also at <math|e<rsub|\<ast\>>>; reader containment follows
    from Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-reader-containment>, and the
    actual chart separation from Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-halo-separation>.
    The spare-run and remainder-restoration lemmas thus apply to the same
    coefficients. Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:allarity-launch> raises
    the weight once, at the middle level, to
    <math|H<rsup|1/<around|(|8*u|)>>>. The lower step count and forced-input
    lemmas then give the lower propagation through <math|w>, including a
    possible adjacent-stage transition.

    Finally the all-level lawful family of
    Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:rank-trace-all-levels> is obtained by the
    same unmarked endpoint rewrite and exact transitions. Its local
    lawfulness and root-normalization proof do not use the marked coefficient
    price being established here. Apply Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:allarity-output-price>
    to that representation. Every polynomial operation above was applied to
    <math|q<rsub|k>>. More precisely, let
    <math|D<rsup|<math-up|ref>><rsub|c>> be the prescribed reference cumulant
    polynomial in cell <math|c>, and put <math|J=exp<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|-<big|sum><rsub|c>D<rsup|<math-up|ref>><rsub|c>|)>>.
    The factor to multiply into <math|q<rsub|k>> is <math|J>, not the full
    invoice, which already contains the insertions. At the terminal level the
    replicated invoice density is <math|J*q<rsub|N>> by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-invoice-parts>. Its push to <math|k>
    is <math|J*q<rsub|k>>: <math|J> is independent of the gauge field, and
    each transition acts linearly on each coefficient in the finite marker
    algebra. The same identity holds down to <math|w>. Since the empty
    coefficient of <math|J> is one, the empty density remains the original
    unmarked density. This proves both the asserted identification and the
    normalization.
  </proof>

  <subsection|The volume cost of the rank
  tests><label|sec:rank-root-normalization>

  The replicated rank tests require the correct power of the coupling in both
  the numerator and the partition function. We prove the finite-torus
  estimate with that power. Write <math|D=dim<rsub|\<bbb-R\>> \<frak-g\>>,
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|G>> for probability Haar measure, and
  <math|\<chi\><around|(|u|)>> for the normalized chord of <math|u\<in\>G>
  used in the action. Thus <math|\<chi\><around|(|1|)>=0>, it is unchanged by
  inversion and conjugation, and <math|\<chi\>*<around|(|u*v|)>\<leqslant\>\<chi\><around|(|u|)>+\<chi\><around|(|v|)>>.

  <subsubsection|Haar bounds in the physical dimension>

  There are constants <math|0\<less\>c<rsub|->\<leqslant\>1\<leqslant\>c<rsub|+>\<less\>\<infty\>>,
  depending only on <math|G>, such that

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|G>*<around|{|\<chi\><around|(|u|)>\<leqslant\>r/4|}>>|<cell|\<geqslant\>c<rsub|->*r<rsup|D>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|0\<less\>r\<leqslant\>1|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:rank-haar-low>>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsub|G>e<rsup|-\<chi\><around|(|u|)>/t>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|G><around|(|u|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>c<rsub|+>*t<rsup|D>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|t\<gtr\>0|)>.<eq-number><label|eq:rank-haar-high>>>>>
  </align>

  To see this, use the actual Haar chart of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:haar-chart>. Its density is bounded above
  and below by positive constants near zero. Differentiability of the
  represented exponential, together with the normalization of the colour
  norm, gives, after shrinking the chart,

  <\equation*>
    <tfrac|1|2><around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<chi\><around|(|exp
    z|)>\<leqslant\>2<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>.
  </equation*>

  A sufficiently small chord ball is contained in that chart: the faithful
  matrix embedding induces the topology of the compact group. Comparison with
  Euclidean balls of radii <math|r/2> and <math|3*r> therefore gives
  <math|a*r<rsup|D>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsub|G>*<around|{|\<chi\>\<leqslant\>r|}>\<leqslant\>b*r<rsup|D>>
  for <math|0\<less\>r\<leqslant\>r<rsub|0>>. For
  <math|r\<in\><around|[|r<rsub|0>,1|]>> the lower bound extends by
  monotonicity after decreasing <math|a>. The upper bound extends to all
  <math|r\<gtr\>0> by the total mass bound one, after increasing <math|b>.

  For <math|k\<geqslant\>0>, on <math|k*t\<leqslant\>\<chi\><around|(|u|)>\<less\><around|(|k+1|)>*t>
  the integrand in <eqref|eq:rank-haar-high> is at most <math|e<rsup|-k>>.
  Summing over these measurable shells bounds the integral by

  <\equation*>
    b*t<rsup|D>*<big|sum><rsub|k=0><rsup|\<infty\>>e<rsup|-k>*<around|(|k+1|)><rsup|D>.
  </equation*>

  The series converges, since its successive-term ratio tends to
  <math|e<rsup|-1>>. This proves the upper bound; rescaling the lower bound
  by <math|1/4> proves <eqref|eq:rank-haar-low>. The proof also covers
  <math|D=0>: powers of positive radii are then one. No connectedness of
  <math|G> is needed.

  <subsubsection|An axial tree and the four unintegrated links>

  Let <math|\<Lambda\>=<around|(|\<bbb-Z\>/L*\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|4>>,
  <math|L\<gtr\>1>, and <math|V=<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>>. Represent each
  coordinate by <math|0,\<ldots\>,L-1>. For <math|x\<ne\>0> let
  <math|p<around|(|x|)>> be its largest nonzero coordinate index. Join
  <math|x> to <math|x-e<rsub|p<around|(|x|)>>>. The sum of the coordinate
  representatives strictly decreases on every such edge, so these edges form
  a tree rooted at zero. The tree has <math|V-1> positive links; there are
  <math|4*V> positive links altogether. Thus there are <math|3*V+1> free
  links after fixing the tree links to one.

  For completeness, tree gauge fixing preserves the integral of a
  gauge-invariant integrable function. Integrate successively from the leaves
  of the tree. At a leaf, use its tree link as the gauge variable at that
  vertex and multiply all its other incident links by that variable or its
  inverse. Conditional on the other links, these changes preserve their Haar
  measures. The tree link becomes one, and the remaining gauge variable
  integrates to one. Induction removes all <math|V-1> tree variables. This
  proves the identity with product probability Haar measure on the free
  links, including for a disconnected group.

  Keep also the four links <math|<around|(|-e<rsub|i>,i|)>>, one wrap link in
  each direction. They are free, distinct, and are not in the tree. We now
  associate a different plaquette to each of the other <math|3*V-3> free
  links so that their integrals can be performed successively. Here is an
  explicit ordering, which also deals with wrap links. Set
  <math|p<around|(|0|)>=0>, and for a positive link <math|e=<around|(|x,i|)>>
  put

  <\equation*>
    t<around|(|e|)>=max <around|(|i,p<around|(|x|)>|)>,<space|2em>\<pi\><around|(|e|)>=<around*|(|t<around|(|e|)>,<space|0.17em>\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|i=t<around|(|e|)>|}>>,<space|0.17em><with|math-display|false|<around*|\<nobracket\>|<big|sum><rsub|j>x<rsub|j>|)>,>|\<nobracket\>>
  </equation*>

  ordered lexicographically.

  If <math|i\<less\>t<around|(|e|)>=t>, choose the plaquette based at
  <math|x-e<rsub|t>> in directions <math|<around|(|i,t|)>>. Its <math|i>-link
  at <math|x> is <math|e>. Its two <math|t>-links are tree links, because
  <math|x<rsub|t>\<ne\>0> and every coordinate above <math|t> vanishes. Its
  remaining <math|i>-link has smaller tier or the same tier and smaller
  coordinate sum. It therefore has smaller priority whenever it is free.

  If <math|i=t<around|(|e|)>> and <math|e> is free, then <math|x<rsub|i>=L-1>
  and all coordinates above <math|i> vanish: otherwise it is a tree link. Put
  <math|w=x+e<rsub|i>>. Since <math|e> is not the retained wrap link,
  <math|w\<ne\>0>. Let <math|j=p<around|(|w|)>\<less\>i> and choose the
  plaquette based at <math|x-e<rsub|j>> in directions
  <math|<around|(|j,i|)>>. Its <math|i>-link at <math|x> is <math|e>. The
  <math|j>-link based at <math|w-e<rsub|j>> is a tree link. The other
  <math|j>-link has smaller tier or has the same tier with second priority
  coordinate zero. The remaining <math|i>-link has the same tier and second
  coordinate but smaller coordinate sum, since <math|x<rsub|j>\<ne\>0>. Thus
  again every other free link on this plaquette has smaller priority.

  The chosen plaquettes are distinct. If two links shared a chosen plaquette,
  each would have smaller priority than the other. Moreover, a
  maximal-priority remaining link occurs in no plaquette chosen for another
  remaining link. With all other links fixed, its own plaquette holonomy has
  the form <math|a*u*b> or <math|a*u<rsup|-1>*b>, so its distribution is
  Haar. Fubini and descending induction therefore show that for continuous
  <math|F:G\<to\><around|[|0,1|]>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:rank-plaquette-elimination><big|int><big|prod><rsub|p<text|
    plaquette>>F*<around|(|U*<around|(|\<partial\>*p|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><around|(|U|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<big|int><rsub|G>F*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|G>|)><rsup|3*V-3>.
  </equation>

  Indeed discard all unchosen factors, which are at most one, and then
  integrate the chosen factors in that order. The four edges of a plaquette
  are distinct for <math|L\<gtr\>1>, including <math|L=2>; thus each
  integration uses its selected variable exactly once, possibly inverted.

  <subsubsection|The normalized estimate>

  Consider a lawful representation <math|R> on this torus, with
  gauge-invariant path action and activities, and with nonnegative density,
  coupling <math|\<beta\>>, threshold <math|\<theta\>\<leqslant\>1>, scalar
  exponent <math|\<sigma\>>, and clip constants satisfying
  <math|0\<less\>c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>\<less\>c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>/16>. The
  path and activity bounds below are the bounds of that same representation.
  These assumptions ensure that its density and the action bounds used in
  tree gauge fixing are gauge invariant. Put

  <\equation*>
    g=\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>,<space|1em>0\<less\>g\<leqslant\>\<theta\>,<space|1em>c=min
    <around|(|1,c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>|)>,<space|1em>b<around|(|g|)>=N<rsub|\<cal-A\>>*<around*|(|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>*g<rsup|2>/2+<around|(|A*g|)><rsup|2>/2|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|A> bounds the linear curvature rows, and
  <math|N<rsub|\<cal-A\>>> is the number of positive plaquette types. Let
  <math|r> be the per-site bound for the additive remainder and the cubic,
  quartic and loop terms. Those bounds, with their actual support weights,
  give

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-up|exponent><rsub|R><around|(|U|)>>|<cell|\<geqslant\>\<sigma\>-V*<around|{|r+\<beta\>*b<around|(|g|)>|}>>|<cell|>|<cell|<text|if
    every link has chord at most >g/4,<eq-number><label|eq:rank-action-low>>>|<row|<cell|<math-up|exponent><rsub|R><around|(|U|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>+V*<around|(|r+N<rsub|\<cal-A\>>|)>-<big|sum><rsub|p><frac|\<chi\>*<around|(|U*<around|(|\<partial\>*p|)>|)>|g/c>.>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number><label|eq:rank-action-high>>>>>
  </align>

  Here is the local verification of these two uses of the path bounds. On the
  small-link set, every plaquette has chord at most <math|g>, all flags
  vanish, and each linear curvature row has norm at most <math|A*g> by its
  absolute row bound. Summing its squared contribution and the direct
  plaquette contribution gives <math|V*b<around|(|g|)>>. The other terms have
  absolute sum at most <math|V*r>. For the upper estimate put
  <math|a=\<chi\>*<around|(|U*<around|(|\<partial\>*p|)>|)>>. The clipped
  direct term obeys

  <\equation*>
    c*a/g-1\<leqslant\>\<beta\><space|0.17em><math-up|clipped><around|(|a|)>.
  </equation*>

  In the quadratic region this follows from
  <math|c*<around|(|a/g-1|)><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>0>, <math|c\<leqslant\>1>,
  and <math|c\<leqslant\>c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>>. In the linear region use
  <math|c\<leqslant\>c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>>, <math|g\<leqslant\>\<theta\>>
  and <math|\<beta\>*g<rsup|2>=1>. The other quadratic row term is
  nonnegative. The sum over plaquettes is unchanged by signed motions; hence
  averaging over them preserves this lower bound on the path action.
  Subtracting the remaining action and additive terms at their absolute bound
  gives <eqref|eq:rank-action-high>.

  Gauge-fix the axial tree and restrict its <math|3*V+1> free links to
  <math|<around|{|\<chi\>\<leqslant\>g/4|}>>. The tree links are one, so
  <eqref|eq:rank-action-low> applies. All flags vanish there, and the forced
  gas equals one. Nonnegativity on the complement and
  <eqref|eq:rank-haar-low> yield the strictly positive bound

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:rank-partition>Z<rsub|R>\<assign\><big|int>R*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\>\<geqslant\>e<rsup|\<sigma\>-V*<around|(|r+\<beta\>*b<around|(|g|)>|)>>*<around|(|c<rsub|->*g<rsup|D>|)><rsup|3*V+1>.
  </equation>

  Now let <math|f> be an integrable complex function, <math|z\<geqslant\>0>,
  and suppose

  <\equation*>
    z<around|\||f<around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<math-up|exponent><rsub|R><around|(|U|)>+V*X>.
  </equation*>

  Using <eqref|eq:rank-action-high>, then
  <eqref|eq:rank-plaquette-elimination> and <eqref|eq:rank-haar-high>, gives

  <\equation*>
    z<around*|\||<big|int>f*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<sigma\>+V*<around|(|r+N<rsub|\<cal-A\>>+X|)>>*<around|{|c<rsub|+>*<around|(|g/c|)><rsup|D>|}><rsup|3*V-3>.
  </equation*>

  Define <math|h=log c<rsub|+>-D*log c\<geqslant\>0> and

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|R><around|(|X|)>=2*r+N<rsub|\<cal-A\>>+\<beta\>*b<around|(|g|)>+X+3*h-4*log
    c<rsub|->.
  </equation*>

  Division by <eqref|eq:rank-partition> gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:rank-ratio><frac|z<around|\||<big|int>f*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\>|\|>|Z<rsub|R>>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around|{|V*P<rsub|R><around|(|X|)>-4*D*log g|}>.
  </equation>

  To check the constants, the powers of <math|g<rsup|D>> differ by exactly
  four. Also <math|<around|(|3*V-3|)>*h\<leqslant\>3*V*h> and
  <math|-<around|(|3*V+1|)>*log c<rsub|->\<leqslant\>-4*V*log c<rsub|->>,
  since <math|V\<geqslant\>1> and <math|log c<rsub|->\<leqslant\>0>. These
  are the only enlargements in this division.

  The same estimate holds for the auxiliary history of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:restarted-rank-history>. If its additive
  terms have value bound <math|\<delta\>> per site, delete them temporarily
  to obtain <math|R<rsup|0>>. Then <math|e<rsup|-V*\<delta\>>*R<rsup|0>\<leqslant\>R\<leqslant\>e<rsup|V*\<delta\>>*R<rsup|0>>
  because <math|R\<geqslant\>0>, and <math|R<rsup|0>=e<rsup|E>*R\<geqslant\>0>.
  Apply the preceding estimate to <math|R<rsup|0>> with numerator expense
  <math|X+\<delta\>>, and use <math|Z<rsub|R<rsup|0>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|V*\<delta\>>*Z<rsub|R>>.
  The extra numerator and denominator expenses total <math|2*V*\<delta\>>.
  This is exactly the contribution of <math|\<delta\>> to <math|2*r> in
  <math|P<rsub|R>>. Derivative estimates on those auxiliary additive terms
  are not needed.

  <subsubsection|Taking the replica root>

  Let a terminal tiling have <math|q> cells, and push its full marked density
  to a torus with exactly <math|q> sites along the auxiliary history. The
  unnormalized scalar integral remains the original partition function
  <math|Z>. Suppose each coefficient of the pushed marked density has the
  local estimate needed above with coefficient price
  <math|a<rsup|q<around|\||I|\|>>> and per-site expense
  <math|X\<leqslant\>1+M>, where <math|M> is the maximum number of markers
  per cell and <math|I\<ne\>\<varnothing\>> is the chosen marker subset. The
  signed orientation copies use conjugation on the reversed cells. Thus the
  coefficient carrying <math|I> in every cell is exactly <math|Z> times the
  replicated Wilson expectation. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:rank-ratio> gives

  <\equation*>
    a<rsup|q<around|\||I|\|>><around|\||\<bbb-E\><around|(|<text|replicated
    coefficient>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|q*P<rsub|R><around|(|X|)>-4*D*log
    g>.
  </equation*>

  Taking the positive <math|q>th root proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:rank-root><around|\<\|\|\>|<text|coefficient
    >I|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|root>>\<leqslant\><frac|exp
    <around|{|P<rsub|R><around|(|X|)>-4*D*log
    g/q|}>|a<rsup|<around|\||I|\|>>>.
  </equation>

  This retains the finite-volume harmonic correction <math|-4*D*log g/q>.

  The clock buys this last expense uniformly in the period. At an output
  level <math|m>, <math|\<beta\>\<leqslant\>4*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>>, so
  <math|-4*D*log g=2*D*log \<beta\>\<leqslant\>8*D*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>>.
  The clock coupling grows linearly in its logarithmic spatial scale, whereas
  the minimal torus site count grows at least exponentially in that scale.
  Consequently <math|8*D*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>/q\<leqslant\>1>
  eventually, even at the smallest period. For the remaining terms, use the
  same clip and row bound <math|A> as the clock stage, and suppose

  <\equation*>
    \<theta\><rsub|R>\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|clock>>,<space|2em>\<Lambda\>\<leqslant\>\<Lambda\><rsub|<math-up|clock>>,<space|2em>\<delta\>\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  The late-scale purchases are

  <\equation*>
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>*\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|clock>><rsup|3>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>*\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|clock>><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>\<Lambda\><rsub|<math-up|clock>>*\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|clock>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  Consequently the actual remainder bound satisfies

  <\equation*>
    r=\<delta\>+\<beta\>*<around|(|A*\<theta\><rsub|R><rsup|3>+A*\<theta\><rsub|R><rsup|4>|)>+\<Lambda\>*\<theta\><rsub|R><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>1+8*A+1,<space|2em>\<beta\>*b<around|(|g|)>=<frac|N<rsub|\<cal-A\>>|2>*<around|(|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>+A<rsup|2>|)>.
  </equation*>

  With <math|X\<leqslant\>1+M>, a fixed stage ticket bounding
  <math|P<rsub|R><around|(|X|)>+1> is therefore

  <\equation*>
    T<rsub|M>=2*<around|(|1+8*A+1|)>+N<rsub|\<cal-A\>>+<frac|N<rsub|\<cal-A\>>|2>*<around|(|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>+A<rsup|2>|)>+<around|(|1+M|)>+3*h-4*log
    c<rsub|->+1.
  </equation*>

  The final <math|1> pays for the harmonic correction, rather than enlarging
  an already spent ticket. Choose the comparison depth <math|k> on a
  constant-profile interval and purchase

  <\equation*>
    T<rsub|M>\<leqslant\>I<rsub|k>=<frac|log \<cal-M\><rsub|k>|16*M>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|k>> is the diverging clock monitor. This is a
  post-block-length scale delay. Set <math|a=e<rsup|2*I<rsub|k>>>. Since
  <math|<around|\||I|\|>\<geqslant\>1>, <eqref|eq:rank-root> is at most
  <math|e<rsup|I<rsub|k>>/a=e<rsup|-I<rsub|k>>\<to\>0>. Equivalently
  <math|a=\<cal-M\><rsub|k><rsup|1/<around|(|8*M|)>>\<geqslant\>1>. This is
  the constant marker weight <eqref|eq:allarity-marker-weight> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-marked-propagation>, with
  <math|u=M> and monitor <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|k>>. That weight is used from
  the launch at the middle level down to the output torus, so the coefficient
  price <math|a<rsup|q<around|\||I|\|>>> above is the weight of the
  replicated coefficient in the norm in which the marked density is estimated
  there. The leading interval above the middle level is run at a smaller
  weight, and the single change of weight at the launch is paid in that
  section. For the rank detectors <math|M=2>. These estimates apply to the
  one-point and corrected two-point coefficients themselves. The local marked
  construction must supply their displayed coefficient price on the same
  auxiliary density; no estimate for a different denominator is used here.

  <subsubsection|The ticket and the harmonic premise at a chart's output
  level><label|subsec:rank-root-output-ticket>

  The purchase <math|T<rsub|M>\<leqslant\>I<rsub|k>> above was stated for a
  comparison depth on a constant-profile interval. We now prove it, together
  with the premise

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:rank-root-premise>P<rsub|R><around|(|X|)>-4*D*log
    g/q\<leqslant\>T<rsub|M>,
  </equation>

  at the output level of a chart of level <math|k>. That level need not lie
  on the same constant-profile interval as <math|k>. Here <math|q> is the
  cell count of the terminal tiling, as above.

  Recall the selected stage clock <math|J<around|(|m|)>> and stage profiles
  <math|\<pi\><rsub|j>> of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:public-run-binding>.
  At level <math|m> the clip constants, the row bound <math|A> and the path
  constants of the clock stage are those of
  <math|\<pi\><rsub|J<around|(|m|)>>>. The ticket <math|T<rsub|M>> displayed
  above is computed from these constants, from <math|N<rsub|\<cal-A\>>>, and
  from the Haar data <math|h,c<rsub|->> of <math|G>. Write
  <math|T<rsub|M><around|(|\<pi\>|)>> for the value computed from a profile
  <math|\<pi\>>; it is a real number fixed once <math|\<pi\>> and <math|M>
  are fixed. The clock monitor is <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|k>=exp
  <around|(|<around|(|log log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)><rsup|2>|)>>, as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-clock-cap>, so

  <\equation*>
    I<rsub|k>=<frac|log \<cal-M\><rsub|k>|16*M>=<frac|<around|(|log log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)><rsup|2>|16*M>.
  </equation*>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Ticket and premise at the output
    level><label|prop:rank-root-output-ticket>Include in the countable family
    of numerical requests of <eqref|eq:common-countable-payment> the requests

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:rank-root-ticket-requests>F<rsub|M,i>=1+<sqrt|16*M*<space|0.17em>max
      <around|{|T<rsub|M><around|(|\<pi\><rsub|i>|)>,0|}>>*<space|2em><around|(|M\<geqslant\>1,i\<geqslant\>0|)>.
    </equation>

    Let <math|w<around|(|k|)>\<leqslant\>k> be levels with
    <math|w<around|(|k|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>. Then for every
    <math|M\<geqslant\>1> there is <math|K<rsub|M>> such that for all
    <math|k\<geqslant\>K<rsub|M>>:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>the ticket at the profile of the output level is bought:
      <math|T<rsub|M><around|(|\<pi\><rsub|J<around|(|w<around|(|k|)>|)>>|)>\<leqslant\>I<rsub|k>>;

      <item><eqref|eq:rank-root-premise> holds at the output level
      <math|m=w<around|(|k|)>>, with <math|T<rsub|M>=T<rsub|M><around|(|\<pi\><rsub|J<around|(|w<around|(|k|)>|)>>|)>>.
      It holds for every period, every lawful representation <math|R> on the
      torus of level <math|w<around|(|k|)>> satisfying the suppositions
      <math|\<theta\><rsub|R>\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|clock>>>,
      <math|\<Lambda\>\<leqslant\>\<Lambda\><rsub|<math-up|clock>>>,
      <math|\<delta\>\<leqslant\>1> and <math|\<beta\>\<leqslant\>4*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|w<around|(|k|)>>>
      of the preceding subsection with the clip and row bound of
      <math|\<pi\><rsub|J<around|(|w<around|(|k|)>|)>>>, and every expense
      <math|X\<leqslant\>1+M>.
    </enumerate>

    Consequently, for every coefficient of the pushed marked density at the
    output level with the coefficient price <math|a<rsup|q<around|\||I|\|>>>,
    <math|a=e<rsup|2*I<rsub|k>>>, and expense <math|X\<leqslant\>1+M>, the
    right side of <eqref|eq:rank-root> is at most
    <math|e<rsup|-I<rsub|k>>=\<cal-M\><rsub|k><rsup|-1/<around|(|16*M|)>>>
    for <math|k\<geqslant\>K<rsub|M>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    <em|Ticket.> Requests added to the countable family before the sequence
    is constructed are paid in the sense of
    <eqref|eq:common-countable-payment>: for fixed <math|M> there is
    <math|m<rsub|M>> such that for every <math|m\<geqslant\>m<rsub|M>> and
    every <math|i\<leqslant\>J*<around|(|m+1|)>>,

    <\equation*>
      F<rsub|M,i>\<leqslant\>1+log log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>.
    </equation*>

    The conclusion of that payment uses only a lower bound on the entry
    thresholds actually paid. Enlarging the family therefore preserves the
    earlier purchases of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection>.
    Take <math|K<rprime|'><rsub|M>=m<rsub|M>> and
    <math|k\<geqslant\>K<rprime|'><rsub|M>>. Since <math|J> is nondecreasing
    and <math|w<around|(|k|)>\<leqslant\>k>, the index
    <math|i=J<around|(|w<around|(|k|)>|)>> satisfies
    <math|i\<leqslant\>J<around|(|k|)>\<leqslant\>J*<around|(|k+1|)>>. Hence

    <\equation*>
      <sqrt|16*M*max <around|{|T<rsub|M><around|(|\<pi\><rsub|i>|)>,0|}>>\<leqslant\>log
      log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>.
    </equation*>

    The left side is nonnegative, so squaring preserves the inequality:

    <\equation*>
      16*M*<space|0.17em>T<rsub|M><around|(|\<pi\><rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>16*M*max
      <around|{|T<rsub|M><around|(|\<pi\><rsub|i>|)>,0|}>\<leqslant\><around|(|log
      log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)><rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    Dividing by <math|16*M\<gtr\>0> gives
    <math|T<rsub|M><around|(|\<pi\><rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>I<rsub|k>>.

    <em|Premise.> The preceding subsection proves
    <eqref|eq:rank-root-premise> at an output level <math|m> for every
    representation satisfying the stated suppositions with the stage's clip
    and row bound. It uses three facts: <math|\<beta\>\<leqslant\>4*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>>,
    the harmonic bound <math|8*D*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>/q\<leqslant\>1>,
    and the late-scale purchases <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>*\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|clock>><rsup|3>\<leqslant\>1>,
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>*\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|clock>><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>1>
    and <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|<math-up|clock>>*\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|clock>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>1>.
    Together they give <math|r\<leqslant\>1+8*A+1>, hence
    <math|P<rsub|R><around|(|X|)>+1\<leqslant\>T<rsub|M>> for
    <math|X\<leqslant\>1+M>, and the final <math|1> of <math|T<rsub|M>> pays
    <math|-4*D*log g/q\<leqslant\>8*D*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>/q\<leqslant\>1>.
    The harmonic bound and the late-scale purchases hold for all <math|m>
    beyond a threshold <math|m<rprime|'>> that is uniform in the period,
    because the minimal site count grows at least exponentially in the
    logarithmic scale, as shown there. The threshold <math|m<rprime|'>> does
    not depend on <math|M>, since <math|M> enters only through
    <math|X\<leqslant\>1+M> and the term <math|1+M> of <math|T<rsub|M>>.
    Since <math|w<around|(|k|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>, there is <math|K<rprime|''>>
    with <math|w<around|(|k|)>\<geqslant\>m<rprime|'>> for
    <math|k\<geqslant\>K<rprime|''>>.

    Put <math|K<rsub|M>=max <around|(|K<rprime|'><rsub|M>,K<rprime|''>|)>>.
    For <math|k\<geqslant\>K<rsub|M>> both items hold. The consequence
    follows from <eqref|eq:rank-root> and the two items: its right side is at
    most <math|exp <around|{|T<rsub|M>|}>/a<rsup|<around|\||I|\|>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|I<rsub|k>>/a=e<rsup|-I<rsub|k>>>,
    because <math|<around|\||I|\|>\<geqslant\>1> and <math|a\<geqslant\>1>.
  </proof>

  For a funded chart at level <math|k> with base arc <math|b>, the output
  level <math|w<around|(|k|)>> is its root depth. Every level <math|j> with
  <math|w<around|(|k|)>\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>k> lies in the current or the
  previous clock stage: <math|J<around|(|k|)>-1\<leqslant\>J<around|(|j|)>>.
  In particular <math|J<around|(|w<around|(|k|)>|)>\<geqslant\>J<around|(|k|)>-1\<to\>\<infty\>>,
  and since <math|J> is nondecreasing this forces
  <math|w<around|(|k|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>. So the proposition applies at the
  root depth, with <math|M> the marker allowance of the chart. This bracket
  property is a field of the chart witness, and it is proved from the clock,
  for every sufficiently large level and uniformly in charts with bounded
  base arc, in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-firesky-clock-fields>.
  The lawful-representation suppositions at the output level and the
  coefficient price are supplied by the output transport of the marked
  density, not here.

  <subsubsection|The clock threshold and loop
  capacity><label|subsec:rank-root-clock-threshold>

  The suppositions above use a clock threshold
  <math|\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|clock>>> and a clock loop capacity
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|<math-up|clock>>> at the output level <math|m>. They
  are the following functions of the clock of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection>:

  <\equation*>
    \<theta\><rsub|<math-up|clock>><around|(|m|)>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m><rsup|-1/2>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-M\><rsub|m>,<space|2em>\<Lambda\><rsub|<math-up|clock>><around|(|m|)>=2*<space|0.17em>\<cal-M\><rsub|m><rsup|\<vartheta\><rsub|J<around|(|m|)>>>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|m>=exp <around|(|<around|(|log log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|)><rsup|2>|)>> and
  <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|j>=\<theta\><rsub|j>> is the protection exponent of
  stage <math|j> selected in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection>.
  It is positive, nonincreasing, and fixed after the block lengths, in the
  order of choices made there. The proof below uses only
  <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>0>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The late-scale purchases><label|lem:rank-root-late-scale>Include
    the thresholds <math|H<rsub|j>> below for each stage <math|j> in the
    all-stage payment <eqref|eq:common-allstage-payment>. Then at every level
    <math|m> with <math|J<around|(|m|)>\<gtr\>j<rsub|\<ast\>>>,

    <\equation*>
      <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>*\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|clock>><around|(|m|)><rsup|3>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>*\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|clock>><around|(|m|)><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>\<Lambda\><rsub|<math-up|clock>><around|(|m|)>*\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|clock>><around|(|m|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>1.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Write <math|x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>> and <math|u=log log x>. The
    three quantities are

    <\equation*>
      x<rsup|-1/2>*e<rsup|3*u<rsup|2>>,<space|2em>x<rsup|-1>*e<rsup|4*u<rsup|2>><space|1em><text|and><space|1em>2*x<rsup|-1>*e<rsup|<around|(|\<vartheta\><rsub|J<around|(|m|)>>+2|)>*u<rsup|2>>.
    </equation*>

    Each is at most one as soon as

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|\<vartheta\><rsub|J<around|(|m|)>>+4|)>*u<rsup|2>+log
      2\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>log x=<tfrac|1|2>e<rsup|u>.
    </equation*>

    For fixed <math|j> this holds for all <math|x> beyond a threshold
    <math|H<rprime|'><rsub|j>>, because <math|u<rsup|2>*e<rsup|-u>\<to\>0>.
    Put <math|H<rsub|j>=max <around|(|H<rprime|'><rsub|j>,H<rprime|'><rsub|j-1>|)>>.
    The all-stage payment gives <math|H<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>>.
    Since <math|J<around|(|m|)>\<leqslant\>J*<around|(|m+1|)>\<leqslant\>J<around|(|m|)>+1>,
    this covers the stage <math|J<around|(|m|)>> of level <math|m>.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Three comparison scales and weak
  convergence><label|sec:three-panel-weak-comparison>

  The comparison estimate is initially available at grid points whose
  coordinate projections are distinct. A test function may cross the excluded
  hyperplanes. We use three scales to pass from the former estimate to the
  latter test: one for the numerical comparison, one for a bound everywhere
  on a compact noncoincident set, and one for a derivative bound away from
  thin strips. Their different costs are essential to the argument.

  <subsubsection|The geometric and analytic estimates>

  Fix an arity <math|m\<geqslant\>1> and use the Euclidean norm on
  <math|E<rsub|4><rsup|m>=<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|m>>, retaining
  the maximum norm for pair separation. For <math|R\<gtr\>0> set

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|R>=<around|{|x:<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>\<leqslant\>R,<space|1em><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|i>-x<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<geqslant\>R<rsup|-1>*<around|(|i\<ne\>j|)>|}>.
  </equation*>

  This is a compact subset of the noncoincident configuration space. Every
  compact subset of that space is contained in some <math|K<rsub|R>>. Define

  <\equation*>
    \<Omega\><rsub|\<sigma\>>=<around|{|x:<around|\||x<rsub|i,\<nu\>>-x<rsub|j,\<nu\>>|\|>\<gtr\>8*\<sigma\><text|
    for every >i\<ne\>j,1\<leqslant\>\<nu\>\<leqslant\>4|}>.
  </equation*>

  Thus the complement of <math|\<Omega\><rsub|\<sigma\>>> is a union of
  coordinate strips, not a neighborhood of just the actual coincidence set.

  Let <math|S> be the smooth interacting table already extracted along the
  ordered mesh and volume filter <math|\<cal-F\>>, and let
  <math|Q<around|(|x|)>> be the free correlation for a fixed word of labels.
  Fix a comparison index <math|k>. Its physical radius is
  <math|r<rsub|k>\<gtr\>0>; its normalized grid spacing is
  <math|h<rsub|k>\<gtr\>0>; its strip width is
  <math|\<sigma\><rsub|k>\<gtr\>0>. The calibrated insertion maps may still
  depend on the finite cutoff pair. Suppose they are eventually bounded in
  their finite-dimensional operator space. On any convergent subfilter of
  these maps write <math|Y> for the limiting tuple and

  <\equation*>
    U<rsub|k,Y><around|(|x|)>=S*<around|(|r<rsub|k>*x;Y|)>.
  </equation*>

  All the following estimates are to hold for every such <math|Y>, with
  constants independent of that subfilter. The three required estimates are

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\||U<rsub|k,Y><around|(|y|)>-Q<around|(|y|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>e<rsub|k>>|<cell|>|<cell|<text|at
    generic grid points >y\<in\>K<rsub|2*R>,<eq-number><label|eq:three-grid>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||U<rsub|k,Y><around|(|x|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>A<rsub|k><rsup|m>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|x\<in\>K<rsub|R>|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:three-bulk>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|D*U<rsub|k,Y><around|(|x|)>|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>4*m*B<rsub|k><rsup|m>/\<sigma\><rsub|k>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around*|(|<around|\||x<rsub|i,\<nu\>>-x<rsub|j,\<nu\>>|\|>\<gtr\>4*\<sigma\><rsub|k>|)>.<eq-number><label|eq:three-slope>>>>>
  </align>

  The last estimate is needed only in the noncoincident domain. The
  quantities <math|e<rsub|k>,A<rsub|k>,B<rsub|k>> are nonnegative. Assume

  <\gather>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|e<rsub|k>\<longrightarrow\>0,<space|2em>\<sigma\><rsub|k>\<longrightarrow\>0,<space|2em>4*m*h<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>\<sigma\><rsub|k><space|1em><text|eventually>,<eq-number><label|eq:three-room>>>|<row|<cell|A<rsub|k><rsup|m>*\<sigma\><rsub|k>\<longrightarrow\>0,<space|2em>B<rsub|k><rsup|m>*h<rsub|k>/\<sigma\><rsub|k>\<longrightarrow\>0.<eq-number><label|eq:three-balances>>>>>
  </gather>

  We first prove the weak comparison from these estimates, then explain the
  three scale choices that give exactly these powers.

  Choose the coordinatewise lattice address <math|y> of <math|x>, so
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|y-x|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>4*m*h<rsub|k>>. If
  <math|x\<in\>\<Omega\><rsub|\<sigma\><rsub|k>>>, the closed ball of radius
  <math|\<sigma\><rsub|k>> about <math|x> has every coordinate difference
  larger than <math|4*\<sigma\><rsub|k>>: moving its two endpoints changes a
  difference by at most <math|2*\<sigma\><rsub|k>>. In particular the segment
  from <math|x> to <math|y> remains in the region of <eqref|eq:three-slope>.
  The fundamental theorem of calculus on that segment gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||U<rsub|k,Y><around|(|x|)>-U<rsub|k,Y><around|(|y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>16*m<rsup|2>*B<rsub|k><rsup|m>*h<rsub|k>/\<sigma\><rsub|k>.
  </equation*>

  For all sufficiently large <math|k>, this <math|y> belongs to
  <math|K<rsub|2*R>> whenever <math|x\<in\>K<rsub|R>>. Indeed the compact set
  <math|K<rsub|R>> lies in the interior of <math|K<rsub|2*R>>, and
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|y-x|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<sigma\><rsub|k>\<to\>0>.
  It is also generic by the strict coordinate separation just proved. The
  free function <math|Q> is uniformly continuous on <math|K<rsub|2*R>>. If
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|Q><around|(|t|)>> is its modulus of continuity there,
  the three-term triangle inequality gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:three-clear>sup<rsub|x\<in\>K<rsub|R>\<cap\>\<Omega\><rsub|\<sigma\><rsub|k>>><around|\||U<rsub|k,Y><around|(|x|)>-Q<around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<alpha\><rsub|k>\<assign\>16*m<rsup|2>*B<rsub|k><rsup|m>*h<rsub|k>/\<sigma\><rsub|k>+e<rsub|k>+\<omega\><rsub|Q><around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|k>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation>

  This convergence is uniform over the limiting tuples <math|Y> under
  consideration.

  There is an explicit linear bound for the volume of the omitted strips:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:three-strip><around|\||K<rsub|R>\<setminus\>\<Omega\><rsub|\<sigma\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|m,R>*\<sigma\>,<space|2em>C<rsub|m,R>=64*m*<around|(|m-1|)>*<around|(|2*R|)><rsup|4*m-1>.
  </equation>

  For a fixed ordered pair <math|i\<ne\>j> and coordinate <math|\<nu\>>, make
  the linear change replacing <math|x<rsub|j,\<nu\>>> by
  <math|x<rsub|j,\<nu\>>-x<rsub|i,\<nu\>>> and leaving the other coordinates
  unchanged. Its determinant is one. The restricted coordinate has interval
  length <math|16*\<sigma\>> and each of the remaining <math|4*m-1>
  coordinates lies in an interval of length <math|2*R>. This bounds the
  volume of that strip intersected with <math|K<rsub|R>> by
  <math|16*\<sigma\>*<around|(|2*R|)><rsup|4*m-1>>. There are
  <math|4*m*<around|(|m-1|)>> choices. Subadditivity proves
  <eqref|eq:three-strip>, including intersecting strips. For <math|m=1> the
  union is empty and the constant is zero.

  Let <math|f> be a smooth test supported in <math|K<rsub|R>>, and put
  <math|C<rsub|Q>=sup<rsub|K<rsub|R>><around|\||Q|\|>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
  Split its integral into the clear region and its complement. Equations
  <eqref|eq:three-bulk>\U<eqref|eq:three-strip> give the quantitative bound

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:three-integral><around*|\||<big|int>f<around|(|x|)>*<around|{|U<rsub|k,Y><around|(|x|)>-Q<around|(|x|)>|}>*<space|0.17em>d*x|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*<around*|{|<around|\||K<rsub|R>|\|>*\<alpha\><rsub|k>+C<rsub|m,R>*<around|(|A<rsub|k><rsup|m>+C<rsub|Q>|)>*\<sigma\><rsub|k>|}>.
  </equation>

  The right side tends to zero. For fixed <math|R> and <math|M\<gtr\>0>, the
  same bound therefore holds uniformly over all smooth tests supported in
  <math|K<rsub|R>> with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>M>;
  it imposes no bound on their derivatives. This uniformity is used in the
  stress comparison after its readout maps and normalizations have been
  matched to these panels. Both integrands are continuous on a neighborhood
  of the support, so these are ordinary integrals; no extension across
  coincident configurations is involved.

  <subsubsection|Returning to cutoff-dependent insertions>

  It is necessary to apply this estimate to the actual insertion maps, whose
  values depend on the cutoff. Fix <math|k>. Their eventual boundedness gives
  a compact closure in their finite-dimensional operator space. We use the
  ordered point and smeared limits of the same table <math|S>; these limits
  also hold on subfilters. If insertion maps converge to a map <math|T> along
  a subfilter, multilinearity in a fixed finite basis proves that their point
  and smeared correlations converge to those with the limiting labels. For
  example, expanding an <math|m>-tuple gives finitely many terms

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|a<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,a<rsub|m>>t<rsub|1,a<rsub|1>>*<around|(|N,p|)>*\<cdots\>*t<rsub|m,a<rsub|m>>*<around|(|N,p|)>*U<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|e<rsub|a<rsub|1>>,\<ldots\>,e<rsub|a<rsub|m>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Every scalar coefficient converges and every fixed basis correlation has
  the specified limit. Finite sums and products therefore give the claimed
  convergence for varying insertions. For the smeared expression, use the
  fixed dilated test <math|r<rsub|k><rsup|-4*m>*f*<around|(|<space|0.17em>\<cdummy\><space|0.17em>/r<rsub|k>|)>>;
  a change of variables identifies its limiting integral with
  <math|<big|int>f<around|(|x|)>*U<rsub|k,Y><around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x>.

  Denote the right side of <eqref|eq:three-integral> by
  <math|E<rsub|k><around|(|f|)>>. We claim that the cutoff smeared comparison
  error is eventually at most <math|E<rsub|k><around|(|f|)>+\<eta\>>, for
  every <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>. If not, the set where it exceeds this number
  is frequent in <math|\<cal-F\>>. Restricting to that set gives a nontrivial
  filter. Compactness of the insertion maps supplies a further convergent
  subfilter. Along it the smeared error converges, by the preceding finite
  expansion, to a number at most <math|E<rsub|k><around|(|f|)>>. But its
  values are all larger than <math|E<rsub|k><around|(|f|)>+\<eta\>>, so their
  limit is at least that latter number. This is a contradiction. Consequently
  the actual cutoff errors have the same asymptotic upper bound, and that
  bound tends to zero as <math|k\<to\>\<infty\>>. This compactness argument
  is performed separately at each fixed <math|k> and fixed test; its eventual
  mesh cutoff may depend on that test and on the period index. The earlier
  bound is uniform in the tests after passing to the limiting insertion
  tuple, but this argument does not claim one cutoff that works for all tests
  at once. It does not replace the original ordered extraction by a new
  continuum table.

  <subsubsection|The three scale inequalities>

  We describe precisely the estimates needed from the marked construction.
  Fix an upper arity <math|n\<geqslant\>2>, a maximum grade <math|D>, and a
  word of length <math|m\<leqslant\>n> in grades at most <math|D>. Let
  <math|H<rsub|k>\<gtr\>1> be the diverging comparison monitor. Choose three
  exponents <math|a,b,e\<gtr\>0> and a strip exponent <math|c\<less\>0>, and
  set

  <\equation*>
    r<rsub|k>=a<rsub|k>*H<rsub|k><rsup|a>,<space|2em>h<rsub|k>=H<rsub|k><rsup|-a>,<space|2em>\<sigma\><rsub|k>=H<rsub|k><rsup|c>.
  </equation*>

  At a fixed comparison depth, all three panels must use the same calibrated
  readout map <math|T>. The lift at exponent <math|a> and the lift at
  exponent <math|b> then differ by the grade weight
  <math|H<rsub|k><rsup|<around|(|a-b|)>*j>> on grade <math|j>. With a common
  finite basis, the sum of the absolute expansion coefficients of a fixed
  label of grade at most <math|D> is bounded by a constant times
  <math|H<rsub|k><rsup|<around|(|a-b|)>*D>>. The constant depends on the
  label, not on <math|k>. This uses the same map <math|T> on the two sides;
  two unrelated calibrations would not give the stated identity.

  The marked comparison at the <math|a>-panel supplies <eqref|eq:three-grid>
  with <math|e<rsub|k>=C*\<varepsilon\><rsub|k>\<to\>0>. The centered
  one-point bounds at the <math|b>-panel, expanded in that common basis and
  in the finitely many signed and conjugated orientations, supply the bulk
  coefficient bound with

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|k>=C<rsub|b>*H<rsub|k><rsup|<around|(|a-b|)>*D>.
  </equation*>

  The analogous centered difference bound at the <math|e>-panel supplies

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|k>=C<rsub|e>*H<rsub|k><rsup|<around|(|a-e|)>*D>.
  </equation*>

  Here the product and difference estimates are the centered cell estimates
  of Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-estimates>. Their use requires
  actual cell widths, not just these coefficient prices. If
  <math|w<rsub|b>,w<rsub|e>> are the output levels of the latter two panels,
  the schedule estimates are

  <\gather>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|a<rsub|w<rsub|b>-1>/a<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|k><rsup|b+6>,<space|2em>6*m*a<rsub|w<rsub|b>>\<less\>a<rsub|w<rsub|b>-1>,<eq-number><label|eq:three-width-b>>>|<row|<cell|a<rsub|w<rsub|e>>/a<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|k><rsup|e+5>.<eq-number><label|eq:three-width-e>>>>>
  </gather>

  The first gives a bulk cell strictly smaller than
  <math|r<rsub|k>/<around|(|2*R|)>> once <math|b+6\<less\>a>; the second
  gives a difference cell smaller than <math|r<rsub|k>*\<sigma\><rsub|k>>
  once <math|e+5\<less\>a+c>. Applying the centered bounds and their
  continuum limits then gives <eqref|eq:three-bulk> and the directional bound
  <math|B<rsub|k><rsup|m>/\<sigma\><rsub|k>>. Summing over the <math|4*m>
  coordinate directions gives exactly <eqref|eq:three-slope>.

  The width calculation also covers a change of stage inside a panel. Let
  <math|L<rsub|max>> bound every block factor between its output predecessor
  and <math|k>, and let <math|V> be the clock cover parameter. On the
  selected window the bracket of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-firesky-clock-fields>
  permits only the two adjacent stages. Their paid monitor bound gives
  <math|L<rsub|max>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|k>>, and
  <math|V<rsup|10>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|k>> eventually. By maximality of the
  middle level <math|\<ell\><rsub|b>> and the root level <math|w<rsub|b>>,
  the first level on the other side of each threshold fails its defining
  inequality. Restoring that one block costs at most <math|L<rsub|max>>, so

  <\equation*>
    <frac|a<rsub|\<ell\><rsub|b>>|a<rsub|k>>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|max>*H<rsub|k><rsup|b+2>,<space|2em><frac|a<rsub|w<rsub|b>>|a<rsub|\<ell\><rsub|b>>>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|max>*V<rsup|10>.
  </equation*>

  Multiplication gives <math|a<rsub|w<rsub|b>>/a<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|k><rsup|b+5>>.
  Its predecessor costs at most one further block factor, proving the first
  inequality of <eqref|eq:three-width-b>. The smaller of the two adjacent
  block factors tends to infinity along the selected stages, so
  <math|a<rsub|w<rsub|b>-1>/a<rsub|w<rsub|b>>\<gtr\>6*m> eventually for each
  fixed arity. This proves its strict second inequality. Repeating the same
  argument for the <math|e>-panel proves <eqref|eq:three-width-e>. Only the
  two adjacent block bounds and the maximality of the levels are used; a
  constant-block interval is unnecessary. A common bounded offset of the
  three arcs leaves this calculation uniform, since it only changes the
  displayed powers <math|b+2> and <math|e+2> by that offset.

  It remains to choose the exponents so that both integral errors decay. The
  exact sufficient inequalities are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:three-checks>

    <\eqsplit>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|c>|<cell|\<less\>0,<space|2em>b+6\<less\>a,<space|2em>e+5\<less\>a+c,>>|<row|<cell|<around|(|a-b|)>*D*n+c>|<cell|\<less\>0,<space|2em><around|(|a-e|)>*D*n-a-c\<less\>0.>>>>
    </eqsplit>
  </equation>

  They imply <math|-a\<less\>c> and hence
  <math|4*m*h<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>\<sigma\><rsub|k>> eventually. They also
  give

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|k><rsup|m>*\<sigma\><rsub|k>=C<rsub|b><rsup|m>*H<rsub|k><rsup|<around|(|a-b|)>*D*m+c>\<to\>0,<space|2em>B<rsub|k><rsup|m>*h<rsub|k>/\<sigma\><rsub|k>=C<rsub|e><rsup|m>*H<rsub|k><rsup|<around|(|a-e|)>*D*m-a-c>\<to\>0.
  </equation*>

  Indeed <math|a\<gtr\>b,e>, so replacing <math|m> by <math|n> increases the
  exponents. For a concrete choice, write <math|q=D*n>, <math|x=7*q+1>,
  <math|y=6+x>, and take

  <\equation*>
    a=10+D+x+y*q+y+7,<space|2em>b=a-7,<space|2em>e=a-y,<space|2em>c=-x.
  </equation*>

  Then <math|b+6=a-1>, <math|e+5=a-x-1>, and the last two exponents in
  <eqref|eq:three-checks> are respectively <math|-1> and <math|-17-D-y>. Also
  <math|a,b,e\<gtr\>D+6>, so the additional lower bounds on panel exponents
  are respected. These choices depend only on the finite grade and arity
  bounds. They make no assertion of a uniform estimate over all words.

  Finally, if these common-panel data are available at arbitrarily large
  comparison depths, choose an increasing sequence of those depths. All the
  eventually valid numerical inequalities remain valid along it. There are
  only finitely many basis words under fixed grade and arity bounds; take the
  maximum of their finitely many thresholds. The preceding proof applies to
  each word on this one sequence. We make the extension to arbitrary labels
  quantitative. For the finitely many reference grades at most <math|D>,
  choose bases and a constant <math|C<rsub|D>\<geqslant\>1> that bounds the
  sum of the absolute basis coefficients of a label by <math|C<rsub|D>> times
  its norm. Let <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|k>> be the maximum of the finitely
  many basis-word errors in <eqref|eq:three-integral>, with
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>> factored out. Then
  <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|k>\<to\>0>. For one limiting tuple <math|Y> of
  insertion maps, write <math|U<rsub|k,Y>*<around|(|x;v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|m>|)>>
  and <math|Q*<around|(|x;v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|m>|)>> for the
  interacting and reference correlations with those labels. Multilinearity
  then gives, for every word of length <math|m\<leqslant\>n> and every test
  supported in the same <math|K<rsub|R>>,

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||<big|int>f<around|(|x|)>*<around|{|U<rsub|k,Y>*<around|(|x;v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|m>|)>-Q*<around|(|x;v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|m>|)>|}>*<space|0.17em>d*x|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|D><rsup|m>*\<epsilon\><rsub|k><space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|m><around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>.
  </equation*>

  At each fixed arity, the bound uses one limiting tuple of insertion maps on
  every basis word and is uniform in those tests and admitted tuples when the
  basis estimates are uniform there. Its application to the stress maps still
  requires the same readout and normalization identification.

  Suppose the common-panel data are available at arbitrarily large depths for
  each finite grade, arity and window request, and the corresponding radii
  tend to zero. At request <math|h\<geqslant\>2>, take <math|n=D=h> and a
  window containing the first <math|h> members of a prescribed compact
  exhaustion at each arity at most <math|h>. Write
  <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|k><rsup|<around|(|h|)>>> for the finite basis
  maximum for that request. Choose the next depth <math|k\<geqslant\>h>,
  strictly larger than the preceding chosen depth, so large that those bounds
  hold and <math|C<rsub|h><rsup|h>*\<epsilon\><rsub|k><rsup|<around|(|h|)>>\<leqslant\>1/<around|(|h+1|)>>;
  also make its radius smaller than the previous one and at most
  <math|1/<around|(|h+1|)>>. This gives one increasing comparison sequence
  with the bound <math|<around|(|h+1|)><rsup|-1><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*<big|prod><rsub|i><around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>>
  for that finite request. For the actual cutoff expression the same
  finite-basis expansion uses the ordered-filter upper bound separately for
  each fixed test and label tuple: divide the desired positive slack by one
  plus the sum of the absolute expansion coefficients, and intersect the
  finitely many basis-word tails. The resulting mesh tail may depend on the
  period. This diagonal choice does not identify its maps or filter with
  those of a different construction.

  The empty word is the constant one and needs no strip estimate. Unit,
  conjugation and signed-symmetry properties of the insertion maps pass
  through this construction whenever they hold eventually at the finite
  cutoffs. Producing the common marked panel data on the selected Wilson
  history is a separate upstream task; the argument here proves their exact
  weak-convergence consequence.

  <subsection|Applying the convergence estimates to the selected
  histories><label|sec:selected-history-limits>

  The preceding convergence arguments concern particular arrays, paths and
  stationary branches. We now identify these objects along one construction.
  This identification is needed because equality of the actions at one period
  would not identify their coefficient arrays, and because a cutoff-dependent
  change of the top boundary need not preserve a limit unless it eventually
  leaves each fixed scale unchanged.

  <subsubsection|One simultaneous stationary construction>

  Fix the scale schedule, clipping data and coordinate conventions. Denote
  the terminal cutoff by <math|N> and the scale by <math|m\<leqslant\>N>. The
  finite stationary construction of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:path-attachment-producer>
  gives paths <math|R<rsub|N,m>>, scalar corrections
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>> and stationary maps
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|N,m><rsup|p,F>>, where <math|p> is a period and <math|F>
  an allowed forest. All these objects are chosen together. Their one-step
  identities use <math|R<rsub|N,m+1>> as input and <math|R<rsub|N,m>> as
  output. The path bounds hold at the prescribed scale widths and allowances.

  For the convergence assertion we use the strengthened version of this
  construction. At scale <math|m> it fixes three coefficient Hilbert spaces,
  with encoding widths <math|\<delta\><rsub|m,2>,\<delta\><rsub|m,3>,\<delta\><rsub|m,4>>
  strictly larger than the paid width <math|w<rsub|m>>. The nonlinear path
  terms are encoded in their canonical selected subspaces. It also supplies
  the following uniformity: every admissible replacement of the input path
  with the same attached row and with all path and loop allowances enlarged
  by <math|64> has an output in these same selected spaces, at the ordinary
  output allowances. The output has the same classical and thermal matching
  identities on every period and forest. Finally, the underlying free horizon
  converges at each scale in all of its retained components, and the root
  rows have an eventual bound at some width <math|v<rsub|m>\<gtr\>w<rsub|m>>.
  These are the extended-domain, canonical-readout and retained-horizon
  hypotheses used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:background-path-convergence>.
  They belong to the one-step analytic construction; neither finite backward
  induction nor the argument below supplies them.

  Here is why its coefficient representation is the one needed in that
  section. Select the three coefficient arrays of each path once, at
  <math|<around|(|N,m|)>>, and use that same selection in every occurrence of
  the path. For <math|m+2\<less\>N>, the input arrays of the step at <math|m>
  are then exactly the output arrays of the step at <math|m+1>. The row at
  either occurrence is also the same attached root row. Thus the input/output
  equality holds in the coefficient spaces, before taking any limit. The
  one-step identities and bounds are available for all these chosen arrays.
  For a replacement of one array the other arrays remain fixed; their closed
  linear constraints and their separate budgets are preserved. The enlarged
  input domain therefore supplies exactly the additional steps used in the
  contraction argument.

  Apply Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:background-path-convergence> to these
  data. At every fixed scale there are one limiting path <math|R<rsub|m>> and
  one scalar <math|\<alpha\><rsub|m>> such that every coefficient of
  <math|R<rsub|N,m>> converges to the corresponding coefficient of
  <math|R<rsub|m>>, and <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>\<to\>\<alpha\><rsub|m>>.
  The limiting path has the original paid bounds. For every fixed period,
  forest and derivative order <math|r>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:selected-stationary-limit>D<rsup|r>*\<rho\><rsub|N,m><rsup|p,F><around|(|0|)>\<longrightarrow\>D<rsup|r>*\<rho\><rsub|m><rsup|p,F><around|(|0|)>.
  </equation>

  The limiting stationary germ is determined by <math|R<rsub|m+1>>, its
  retained quadratic data and its forest. Its Hessian is positive with the
  same lower bound. Consequently no choice of a new subsequence for a forest
  or derivative order is involved in <eqref|eq:selected-stationary-limit>.
  The scalar limit is the one obtained from these same paths and thermal
  identities.

  <subsubsection|A single width for all four coefficient families>

  The common dictionaries are part of the conclusion just obtained. Write
  <math|r<rsub|N,m><around|(|a|)>> and <math|r<rsub|m><around|(|a|)>> for row
  coefficients on the canonical row dictionary, and
  <math|c<rsub|N,m,q>*<around|(|a,\<nu\>|)>> and
  <math|c<rsub|m,q>*<around|(|a,\<nu\>|)>> for the actual, exponentially
  encoded coefficients of the atoms of degree <math|q=2,3,4>. Their supports,
  lengths <math|\<ell\><around|(|a|)>> and color multi-indices <math|\<nu\>>
  are independent of <math|N> on a tail. Choose

  <\equation*>
    w<rsub|m>\<less\><wide|w|~><rsub|m>\<less\>min
    <around|{|v<rsub|m>,\<delta\><rsub|m,2>,\<delta\><rsub|m,3>,\<delta\><rsub|m,4>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Then, for every <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, all sufficiently large
  <math|N> satisfy

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|a><around|\||r<rsub|N,m><around|(|a|)>-r<rsub|m><around|(|a|)>|\|>*e<rsup|<wide|w|~><rsub|m>*\<ell\><around|(|a|)>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>,<eq-number><label|eq:selected-row-width>>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|a,\<nu\>><around|\||c<rsub|N,m,q>*<around|(|a,\<nu\>|)>-c<rsub|m,q>*<around|(|a,\<nu\>|)>|\|>*e<rsup|<wide|w|~><rsub|m>*\<ell\><around|(|a|)>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>*<space|2em><around|(|q=2,3,4|)>.<eq-number><label|eq:selected-atom-width>>>>>
  </align>

  For the row statement one may either sum the finitely many plaquette
  directions or impose the estimate separately on each; shrinking the
  requested <math|\<varepsilon\>> by their number gives the displayed
  version.

  For completeness, the two tail bounds that permit this simultaneous choice
  are different. If the row budget at <math|v<rsub|m>> is <math|B<rsub|m>>,
  its coefficient difference at the smaller width is bounded by

  <\equation*>
    2*B<rsub|m>*e<rsup|-<around|(|v<rsub|m>-<wide|w|~><rsub|m>|)>*\<ell\><around|(|a|)>>.
  </equation*>

  For an atom, remove its encoding factor
  <math|e<rsup|-\<delta\><rsub|m,q>*\<ell\><around|(|a|)>>>. The resulting
  coefficient arrays have a common Hilbert bound <math|C<rsub|m,q>>, so their
  individual differences have absolute value at most <math|2*C<rsub|m,q>>.
  Their weighted majorant is therefore <math|2*C<rsub|m,q>*e<rsup|-<around|(|\<delta\><rsub|m,q>-<wide|w|~><rsub|m>|)>*\<ell\><around|(|a|)>>>.
  Both majorants are summable on the canonical dictionaries by the geometric
  sums in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:fixed-rung-convergence>. The
  coordinate limits and a finite-head, summable-tail argument prove
  <eqref|eq:selected-row-width>\U<eqref|eq:selected-atom-width>. There are
  only four families and finitely many row directions, so their eventual
  estimates hold on one tail. This gives one path limit with one strictly
  larger width, as required in the local integral comparison.

  <subsubsection|Changing the terminal normalization>

  The final Wilson normalization replaces the top path at <math|m=N>. Let
  <math|<wide|R|~><rsub|N,m>>, <math|<wide|\<alpha\>|~><rsub|N,m>> and
  <math|<wide|\<rho\>|~><rsub|N,m><rsup|p,F>> denote the resulting data. The
  change of coordinates retains the exact identities

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<wide|R|~><rsub|N,m>>|<cell|=R<rsub|N,m>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|m\<less\>N|)>,>>|<row|<cell|<wide|\<alpha\>|~><rsub|N,m>>|<cell|=\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>>|<cell|>|<cell|<text|for
    all >N,m,>>|<row|<cell|<wide|\<rho\>|~><rsub|N,m><rsup|p,F>>|<cell|=\<rho\><rsub|N,m><rsup|p,F>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|m+1\<less\>N|)>.>>>>
  </align*>

  It also leaves the free bed unchanged for <math|m\<less\>N> and the entire
  associated tower unchanged for <math|m+1\<less\>N>; its transport row is
  unchanged already for <math|m\<less\>N>. These are identities of the maps
  and coefficient data, not merely equalities of their evaluated actions.

  For fixed <math|m>, all the identities hold once <math|N\<gtr\>m+1>.
  Deleting this finite initial segment changes none of the limits above, none
  of the eventual larger-width bounds and none of the retained free horizon
  limits. Equality of the stationary maps gives equality of every derivative
  at zero. Thus the final normalized construction has exactly the same
  <math|R<rsub|m>>, <math|\<alpha\><rsub|m>> and stationary jets. In
  particular, replacing the terminal path has not introduced a second
  background for the activity estimates.

  <subsubsection|The auxiliary stationary service>

  We supply the auxiliary input used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-nonlinear-offering>.
  Fix one adjacent stage pair, its paths and clips, its block side <math|L>,
  and a derivative order <math|r>. Write <math|j=J<rsub|m+1>> for the
  incoming stage. All constants below may depend on these purchased data, but
  not on the auxiliary period, forest or cutoff. The incoming loop allowance
  is denoted by <math|\<Lambda\>\<geqslant\>1>. The auxiliary coarse side is
  at least two; its fine side <math|L*c> satisfies the larger-datum room
  bound <math|L*c\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|j+7|)>*L>. These are restrictions
  on <math|c> after the stage has been fixed. The selected kernel has the
  branch compatibility of Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:path-stationary-chart>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Stationary matching on auxiliary
    periods><label|lem:selected-auxiliary-service>The paths of the selected
    retained plan admit the uniform stationary service on every sufficiently
    large auxiliary one-step torus. Its critical graph agrees as a germ with
    the selected plan wherever that plan is defined. On a coarse auxiliary
    side <math|c>, its classical matching is exact and its thermal quadratic
    matching has bound

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:selected-auxiliary-thermal><around|\||D<rsup|2>*<around|(|\<cal-T\><rsub|c>-\<cal-D\><rsub|c>|)><around|(|0|)><around|[|h,h<rprime|'>|]>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||<math-up|Site><around|(|c|)>|\|>*<around|(|A+\<Gamma\><rsub|j>|)>*\<Lambda\>*e<rsup|-min
      <around|(|\<nu\>,c<rsub|j>|)>*c>*<space|2em><around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|h|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>,<around|\<\|\|\>|h<rprime|'>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>1|)>,
    </equation>

    for the tested directions in the plan. Here <math|A\<less\>\<infty\>>,
    <math|\<nu\>\<gtr\>0> are auxiliary service constants;
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|j>,c<rsub|j>\<gtr\>0> are the retained plan's
    thermal constants; and <math|\<cal-D\><rsub|c>> is the draft formed from
    its same outgoing path and scalar. The chart table is the plan's
    local-ratio chart. These choices are uniform over the admissible incoming
    paths with the purchased bounds.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    <em|Uniform local data.> Use the tree-gauge coordinates of
    Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:path-stationary-chart>. A fixed
    block has a bounded number <math|K> of free coordinates per site,
    uniformly in the period and allowed forest. Exponential path bounds give
    the local derivative estimate <eqref|eq:stationary-scatter-input> at
    every fixed required order. Here is the length payment in that assertion.
    Differentiating a path word <math|r> times selects at most
    <math|<around|(|1+\<ell\>|)><rsup|r>> positions. If the selected
    coordinates are prescribed, the word visits all their anchors; the sum of
    their distances from any one of them is at most <math|r*\<ell\>>. From
    its weight <math|e<rsup|\<sigma\>*\<ell\>>> reserve
    <math|e<rsup|\<sigma\>*\<ell\>/2>> for summation and use the rest to pay
    those distances and <math|<around|(|1+\<ell\>|)><rsup|r>>. Thus one can
    use a positive decay width smaller than <math|\<sigma\>/<around|(|4*r|)>>
    and a finite constant depending only on <math|r,\<sigma\>> and the path
    budget. Products of a bounded number of words obey the same estimate
    after splitting the width between their factors. The block coordinate
    maps, clip and local Haar logarithm have bounded fixed-order derivatives
    on a fixed smaller coordinate ball; their stencils have bounded diameter
    at this fixed <math|L>. Composition only changes these constants and the
    decay width. Summing free derivative indices costs the shell sum
    <math|K*<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>0><around|(|2*n+1|)><rsup|4>*e<rsup|-a*n>>,
    independently of the torus. This proves the local derivative input for
    the actual path action. The classical action uses the fixed path
    allowance; the loop derivatives have in addition their factor
    <math|\<Lambda\>>.

    The positive Hessian gap is the all-period tree-gauge gap of the same
    selected horizon. Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:uniform-stationary-chart>
    now constructs a common coordinate ball and the unique critical graph
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|c>>, with positive smaller protection radii,
    smoothness through the requested order, and a uniform inverse bound.
    Increase the local order to at least <math|max <around|(|r+2,4|)>> when
    necessary. The argument there uses supremum norms for the radii, so none
    shrink with the auxiliary volume. Its estimates for loop and Haar
    derivatives give the remaining chart prices, with at most the stated loop
    factor.

    We shall also need exponential entry bounds for the Hessian and its first
    two derivatives along <math|\<rho\><rsub|c>>. These follow from the same
    local derivative estimate, not from a dimension-dependent norm
    conversion. Indeed <math|D*\<rho\>=-H<rsup|-1>*D<rsub|x>*F>, and
    differentiating the critical equation twice expresses
    <math|D<rsup|2>*\<rho\>> as <math|H<rsup|-1>> times a sum of third
    derivatives of the action evaluated on first graph derivatives. The
    inverse decays exponentially by Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-covariance>.
    Root each derivative at its unsummed matrix index and sum the remaining
    indices with the preceding shell bound, reducing the decay width at each
    of these finitely many convolutions. The chain rule for
    <math|H*<around|(|x,\<rho\><rsub|c><around|(|x|)>|)>> through order two
    then yields constants <math|d,B,T,Q\<gtr\>0>, uniform in the period and
    forest, such that its inverse, first jets and second jets have entry
    bounds respectively <math|B*e<rsup|-d<math-up|dist>>>,
    <math|T*e<rsup|-d<math-up|dist>>> and <math|Q*e<rsup|-d<math-up|dist>>>,
    for external directions of supremum norm at most one. The gap fixes
    <math|B>; these constants involve classical path derivatives only, so the
    loop factor is not squared.

    <em|Replication and uniqueness.> Choose a scheduled coarse side
    <math|C=n*c>, beyond the plan's volume threshold, using
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection>. Lift the
    auxiliary forest and its local coordinate conventions periodically. There
    are <math|n<rsup|4>> copies of each free coordinate. Replication copies
    the same path words, block maps and local chart ratios. The stationary
    equation on a replicated field is therefore the replication of the
    smaller stationary equation. Both critical graphs tend to zero and belong
    to the common uniqueness zone near zero. Their pullbacks under
    replication agree there. The scheduled plan's graph has the same
    criticality and lies in that zone after restricting its germ, so it too
    is the same graph. This proves branch identification, rather than
    choosing a new branch for the auxiliary estimates. Classical action and
    outgoing path sums on replicated fields are exactly <math|n<rsup|4>>
    copies. The plan's classical matching therefore descends after division
    by <math|n<rsup|4>>.

    <em|The determinant error.> We give the finite-matrix calculation because
    the thermal term does not replicate exactly. Let <math|\<pi\>> map large
    free coordinates to their small-period addresses and let
    <math|E<rsub|i*a>=\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|\<pi\><around|(|i|)>=a|}>>>.
    Write <math|S<around|(|x|)>> for the large Hessian on replicated coarse
    fields and <math|T<around|(|x|)>> for the small Hessian. Equality of the
    replicated stationary equations, differentiated in the free variables,
    gives

    <\equation*>
      S<around|(|x|)>*E=E*T<around|(|x|)>.
    </equation*>

    Equivalently, the row sum of <math|S> over a fibre equals the
    corresponding entry of <math|T>. It is not an equality of individual
    inverse blocks. Inverting and differentiating once or twice preserves
    this intertwining relation, as does multiplication. If <math|P*E=E*R>,
    the equal fibre sizes imply the exact trace identity

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:selected-fibre-trace>n<rsup|4><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>R-<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>P=<big|sum><rsub|i><big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|a\<ne\>i>>|<row|<cell|\<pi\><around|(|a|)>=\<pi\><around|(|i|)>>>>>>>P<rsub|i*a>.
    </equation>

    Distinct coordinates in one fibre have anchor distance at least <math|c>.
    With <math|S<rsub|K><around|(|t|)>=K*<big|sum><rsub|r\<geqslant\>0><around|(|2*r+1|)><rsup|4>*e<rsup|-t*r>>,
    an entry bound <math|<around|\||P<rsub|i*a>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|P>*e<rsup|-2*t<math-up|dist><around|(|i,a|)>>>
    consequently gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||n<rsup|4><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>R-<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>P|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||I<rsub|C>|\|>*C<rsub|P>*S<rsub|K><around|(|t|)>*e<rsup|-t*c>.
    </equation*>

    This follows by extracting <math|e<rsup|-t*c>> and summing the remaining
    <math|e<rsup|-t<math-up|dist>>> over all coordinates, which only enlarges
    the sum over the fibre.

    For clarity, exponential entry bounds are stable under the products
    needed here: if two matrices have bounds
    <math|A*e<rsup|-a<math-up|dist>>> and
    <math|B<rprime|'>*e<rsup|-a<math-up|dist>>>, then their product has bound
    <math|A*B<rprime|'>*S<rsub|K>*<around|(|a/2|)>*e<rsup|-<around|(|a/2|)><math-up|dist>>>.
    Extract the last exponential by the triangle inequality and sum a
    remaining exponential over the intermediate index. Set
    <math|I=S<around|(|0|)><rsup|-1>> and use

    <\equation*>
      D<rsup|2><tfrac|1|2>log det S*<around|[|h,h<rprime|'>|]>=<tfrac|1|2><math-up|tr><around|(|I*S<rsub|h*h<rprime|'>>-I*S<rsub|h>*I*S<rsub|h<rprime|'>>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The first product is bounded at width <math|d/4> by
    <math|B*Q*S<rsub|K>*<around|(|d/4|)>>, and each <math|I*S<rsub|h>> is
    bounded at width <math|d/2> by <math|H<rsub|0>=B*T*S<rsub|K>*<around|(|d/2|)>>.
    Thus the second product is bounded at width <math|d/4> by
    <math|H<rsub|0><rsup|2>*S<rsub|K>*<around|(|d/4|)>>. Apply
    <eqref|eq:selected-fibre-trace> with <math|t=d/8> to the two products.
    The absolute difference of the determinant jets, with the small jet
    multiplied by <math|n<rsup|4>>, is at most

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||I<rsub|C>|\|>*<around|[|B*Q+H<rsub|0><rsup|2>|]>*S<rsub|K>*<around|(|d/4|)>*S<rsub|K>*<around|(|d/8|)>*e<rsup|-d*c/8>.
    </equation*>

    The local Haar logarithm and the incoming loop sum replicate exactly
    along the identified graphs, so their jets cancel in this difference.
    There are at most <math|K*L<rsup|4>*C<rsup|4>> free coordinates. Dividing
    by <math|n<rsup|4>> therefore gives constants <math|A\<less\>\<infty\>>,
    <math|\<nu\>=d/8\<gtr\>0> for which

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||D<rsup|2>*\<cal-T\><rsub|c>-n<rsup|-4>*D<rsup|2>*<around|(|\<cal-T\><rsub|C>\<circ\>E<rsub|c>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>c<rsup|4>*A*e<rsup|-\<nu\>*c>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|E<rsub|c>> is coarse-field replication and the directions
    have norm at most one. This also explains the local-ratio requirement:
    the Jacobian terms must be the same copied coordinate terms in both
    expressions.

    <em|Transfer of the retained estimate.> Proposition
    <reference|prop:path-retained-plan>, on the selected horizon and paths,
    retains the scheduled estimate with amplitude
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|j>*\<Lambda\>> and rate <math|c<rsub|j>>. Its
    hypotheses are the same purchased one-step path construction and
    quadratic horizon used at the beginning of this section; no new
    stationary plan is selected for the auxiliary torus. Apply that estimate
    to the replicated tested directions. They retain supremum norm one, and
    the draft copies exactly. Division by <math|n<rsup|4>> turns its site
    factor into <math|c<rsup|4>>. The triangle inequality yields

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||D<rsup|2>*<around|(|\<cal-T\><rsub|c>-\<cal-D\><rsub|c>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>c<rsup|4>*<around|{|A*e<rsup|-\<nu\>*c>+\<Gamma\><rsub|j>*\<Lambda\>*e<rsup|-c<rsub|j>*C>|}>.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|C\<geqslant\>c> and <math|\<Lambda\>\<geqslant\>1>, this
    implies <eqref|eq:selected-auxiliary-thermal>. All constants were fixed
    before <math|c,C> and the forest. The same reasoning applies to any
    allowed auxiliary forest and its periodic lift; uniqueness identifies any
    other admissible germ on it without changing the constants.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The activity and coupling limits on this background>

  Fix a period <math|p>. The regular supports, forced inventories and norms
  at scale <math|m> depend on the fixed scale and period, not on <math|N>.
  Embed their actual arrays into the one completion
  <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|m,p>> of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:coupled-background-convergence>.
  The representative of each finite history has exactly its prescribed
  activities; the embedding is the same one used in the finite-test
  estimates. Its norm is at most <math|1/8>. At every retained scale its path
  is <math|<wide|R|~><rsub|N,m>>, by the construction's input/output
  identities. The scalar in its coupling recursion is
  <math|<wide|\<alpha\>|~><rsub|N,m>>, not a newly chosen correction.

  The finite real-test estimates and their ray consequence in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:background-ray-estimates> give the two
  one-step comparison bounds in <eqref|eq:coupled-activity-step>\U<eqref|eq:coupled-cost-step>.
  Their constants are <math|8/128> for the activity and <math|4*s<rsub|m>>
  for the scalar correction. The entry-threshold calculation at the end of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:coupled-background-convergence> gives
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>m>s<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|m>/128> for
  these same allowances. The larger-width path limit above, the convergent
  stationary jets and the retained free horizon supply the background data in
  the common-variable comparison. The compensated parameter estimates of
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:local-parameter-comparison> and
  <reference|sec:finite-volume-parameter-continuity>, with the suppliers of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:parameter-comparison-suppliers>, then give
  its local continuity, including the projected scalar output. Each
  application retains the funded transition and output identities stated
  there. To apply this continuity to every auxiliary history with a
  prescribed coupling script and zero terminal activity, one must verify
  those transition hypotheses for its actual input at each step. In
  particular, the protected chart, funding and renewed prices, downward-map
  bounds and output identification must refer to that input and the same path
  and dictionaries. Availability of a one-step map on the allowed ball alone
  does not establish these supplier hypotheses. For the auxiliary zero-array
  inputs, Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-auxiliary-service>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-chartlet-matching> below verify this
  matching. The ordered derivative prices of the cited comparison argument
  are not a further ingredient beyond this matching. Their supplier
  construction in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:parameter-comparison-suppliers>
  is stated at a fixed derivative order <math|j> for the fine representation
  satisfying the local hypotheses of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:activity-jet-assembly>.
  It yields the jet prices at every order through <math|j> by the same local
  estimates that give the value prices: the elementary compensated factors
  with their support-dependent derivative scale, the cluster jet prices, and
  the cluster criterion for <math|2<rsup|j>*u<rsub|P>> with its reserve. The
  order needed in the continuity argument is at most the order <math|j>
  supplied. Hence no value-order continuity theorem is required. What remains
  is a matching statement, not a new estimate. The auxiliary dictionary of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-nonlinear-offering>, that is the
  chartlet with zero regular activity at the stage coupling, must satisfy the
  local hypotheses of Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:activity-jet-assembly>
  and<nbsp><reference|sec:parameter-comparison-suppliers>, on the same input,
  path and dictionaries, with the healthy-mask, width and cutoff premises
  retained there. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-auxiliary-service>
  supplies its stationary service on these same paths, and
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-chartlet-matching> checks the remaining
  local hypotheses. Thus the auxiliary matching required by the convergence
  argument is available on this construction.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The auxiliary chartlet meets the comparison
    hypotheses><label|lem:selected-chartlet-matching>Fix a stage and a point
    <math|x> of an input interval. Consider the auxiliary torus of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-nonlinear-offering>, carrying the
    same block bridge, the same two paths and clips, and the fine threshold,
    radius and band at <math|\<beta\><rsub|F><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>>.
    Consider its reference fine representation, the bare path action with the
    zero activity array. Use the auxiliary stationary service of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-auxiliary-service>, on the same
    incoming path, one-step datum and local-ratio chart. Then this
    representation satisfies the incoming hypotheses of
    Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:activity-jet-assembly>
    and<nbsp><reference|sec:parameter-comparison-suppliers>, at every fixed
    derivative order <math|j>, as follows:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><em|Incoming activity budgets.> The regular and forced arrays are
      zero. Hence their regular and forced costs vanish, and every activity
      budget, packet hypothesis and cluster hypothesis imposed on them holds
      trivially.

      <item><em|Sector and shell access.> The block equation, the two
      adjacent kernel bounds and the all-period positive lower bounds are
      those of the selected horizon. They apply on every auxiliary torus, so
      the local expansion and its differentiated remainder bounds are
      available there. The shell-access constants are those of the same
      sector construction.

      <item><em|Warnings.> The deterministic safety and plateau inequalities
      of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-warnings> hold, with their
      safety conditions produced in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:warning-safety>.
      That production concerns the fixed sector data and does not involve the
      activity array.

      <item><em|Protected chart.> The comparison chart is the stationary
      chart of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:uniform-stationary-chart>,
      restricted to the coarse horizon. Its identification with the graph
      used in the stationary estimates is the branch identification in
      Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-auxiliary-service>, on the same
      block, path and clipping data.

      <item><em|Running-curve data.> The renewed price bounds are the zero
      costs of (1) together with the fine path bounds and the clock
      inequality for the loop allowance. The geometric bounds on the downward
      map are the kernel bounds of (2). The initial action is the admissible
      bare path action. The inequality <math|<math-up|preExponent>/B\<leqslant\><math-up|width>/2>
      is <eqref|eq:selected-offer-width>, which gives
      <math|A<rsub|c>/B<rsub|c>\<leqslant\>\<sigma\><rsub|c>/4>. The
      clipping, the path and the regular and forced dictionaries are those of
      the physical comparison, with elementary labels identified by the
      identity. The displayed output is the constructed output of the local
      expansion on that torus.
    </enumerate>

    The healthy-support condition on a tested family of labels is not a
    property of the representation. It is a premise of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:activity-jet-assembly> for each family to
    which the product estimate is applied, and it is retained there
    unchanged. Consequently the supplier construction of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:parameter-comparison-suppliers> applies to
    the auxiliary chartlet at order <math|j>. It yields the ordered
    derivative prices at every order through <math|j> by the same estimates
    as the value prices.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Each item is read off from the construction of the auxiliary data in the
    subsection \PKeeping the physical and auxiliary data fixed\Q of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-nonlinear-offering>, together
    with the sections cited in the statement.

    (1) The array is zero, so every coefficient vanishes. Regular and forced
    costs are suprema of sums of nonnegative multiples of the absolute values
    of these coefficients, hence zero. Every incoming budget of the two cited
    sections is an upper bound by a nonnegative allowance, so it holds.

    (2) The cited subsection states that the auxiliary tori carry the same
    block bridge and paths, and that the all-period kernel bounds apply on
    every such torus, so that the local expansion and its differentiated
    remainder bounds are available there. The shell-access constants are
    constants of the sector construction on the selected horizon, which is
    the same on these tori.

    (3) The safety and plateau inequalities are deterministic statements
    about the fixed sector, warning catalogue and thresholds, produced in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:warning-safety>. They do not involve the
    activity array.

    (4) This is the protected-chart requirement of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:parameter-comparison-suppliers>. That
    section identifies a stationary chart with the graph of its estimates
    exactly when its representative is a critical point of the same tested
    action in the fine zone. On the auxiliary torus, this is the
    identification of the auxiliary stationary branch with the plan's branch,
    proved in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-auxiliary-service>. Its
    eventual protected-field condition holds because
    <math|\<tau\><around|(|x|)>\<to\>0>.

    (5) Each clause is quoted from the cited subsection and from
    <eqref|eq:selected-offer-width>, since
    <math|A<rsub|c>/B<rsub|c>\<leqslant\>\<sigma\><rsub|c>/4\<leqslant\>\<sigma\><rsub|c>/2>.
    The output identification holds because the auxiliary output is, by
    definition, the output of the local expansion applied to this
    representation.

    Finally, Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:parameter-comparison-suppliers>
    chooses its thresholds before the incoming representation is supplied,
    and then instantiates its constants at that representation. Its
    conclusions therefore apply to every representation satisfying (1)\U(5),
    and in particular to the chartlet.
  </proof>

  With these same-input verifications, all hypotheses of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:coupled-background-convergence> are on one
  and the same family. For any terminal sequence <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>> and
  any corresponding finite trajectories whose bottom couplings tend to
  <math|b>, there are, at every fixed <math|m,p>, limits

  <\equation*>
    \<beta\><rsub|N,m>\<longrightarrow\>\<beta\><rsub|m>,<space|2em>x<rsub|N,m>\<longrightarrow\>x<rsub|m><text|in
    >\<cal-H\><rsub|m,p>,<space|2em>q<rsub|N,m>\<longrightarrow\>q<rsub|m>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|q<rsub|N,m>> is the scalar activity correction, distinguished
  from the path correction <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>>. They obey

  <\equation*>
    \<beta\><rsub|m>-\<beta\><rsub|m+1>-\<alpha\><rsub|m>=q<rsub|m>.
  </equation*>

  Two sequences with the same bottom limit <math|b> give the same limits:
  interleave them, whose terminal indices still tend to infinity, and apply
  the same uniqueness proof. No compactness of a ball in
  <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|m,p>> is used. The norm convergence and the positive
  partition lower bound in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:fixed-rung-convergence>
  now apply to the actual normalized densities at that scale.

  This application retains the strengthened stationary construction, its
  convergent free horizon and the funded nonlinear transition. The argument
  establishes compatibility of their consequences; the analytic production of
  these inputs remains the responsibility of the corresponding construction
  estimates.

  <section|Quantitative matching for the operator product expansion>

  <subsection|The coefficient field at the last grouping
  scale><label|sec:ope-local-projection>

  We connect the local-field representation to the degree-bounded coefficient
  field in the cutoff estimate. This step uses a projection estimate for one
  elementary insertion. We state its content explicitly and prove the
  extension to the whole grouped observable, including the normalization that
  determines its final price.

  Let <math|m+1> and <math|m> be adjacent fine and coarse scales, with mesh
  sizes <math|a<rsub|m+1>=a<rsub|m>/L> and <math|L\<geqslant\>1>. Fix a
  degree <math|d\<geqslant\>0>. For every fine elementary insertion <math|M>
  anchored at the origin, assume there is a coarse coefficient field
  <math|Q<rsub|M>> of degree at most <math|d> with the prescribed locality
  and covariance format, satisfying

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-leaf-projection><math-up|dens><around|(|Q<rsub|M>|)>=L<rsup|d>*\<cal-T\><math-up|dens><around|(|M|)>,<space|2em><around|\||Q<rsub|M>|\|><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>\<leqslant\>x<math-up|cost><around|(|M|)>,<space|2em><math-up|rem><around|(|Q<rsub|M>|)>\<leqslant\>x<math-up|cost><around|(|M|)>,
  </equation>

  where <math|x\<geqslant\>0>. Here <math|<math-up|rem><around|(|Q|)>\<leqslant\>B>
  means that <math|Q> has a regular remainder with summable majorants of
  total mass <math|R> and a forced remainder with uniformly summable
  majorants of total mass <math|F> such that
  <math|R+\<kappa\><rsub|m><rsup|-1>*F\<leqslant\>B>, where
  <math|\<kappa\><rsub|m>\<gtr\>0> is the forced-remainder allowance of this
  scale; it is the normalization of the forced part used in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:leaf-transfer>. The same
  <math|\<kappa\><rsub|m>> is used for every field at this scale, in
  particular in the finite sums below. Assume, in addition, the singleton
  transition expansion of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-transfer>: the
  fine expansion cost for this insertion is at most
  <math|2<math-up|cost><around|(|M|)>>, and its coarse response cost is at
  most <math|x<math-up|cost><around|(|M|)>>. The relation for the quoted
  profile has multiplier <math|L<rsup|d>>, consistently with the first
  identity in <eqref|eq:ope-leaf-projection>. These statements concern the
  actual background transition and its actual coefficient fields; they are
  not an abstract polynomial approximation assumption.
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:leaf-transfer> derives both statements from
  a localized expansion of one real insertion under the block integral: any
  <math|Q<rsub|e>> satisfying <eqref|eq:leaf-projection-allowance> serves as
  <math|x>, with <math|<math-up|cost><around|(|M|)>> the fine mass <math|m>
  there, fine expansion cost <math|2*m> and coarse response cost
  <math|Q<rsub|e>*m>. That derivation is conditional on the localized
  expansion and the tail, flag and smallness inputs stated in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:leaf-transfer>.
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-history-service> places the projection
  on the selected history with allowance <math|X<rsub|m>=exp
  <around|(|<sqrt|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>>|)>>, for
  <math|m\<geqslant\>t<rsub|d>>, where <math|t<rsub|d>> is the reference
  level with <math|d+3\<leqslant\>J<around|(|t<rsub|d>|)>>, for every cutoff
  <math|N<rsub|0>\<gtr\>m> and every period. The local expansion and its tail
  and flag estimates are given in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:leaf-expansion>;
  the principal and smallness bounds are given in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:projection-payment-producer>.

  <subsubsection|From one insertion to a grouped field>

  Suppose a grouped fine field has zero separately recorded constant and an
  elementary representation

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|g,q|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j>z<rsub|j>*<around*|(|<math-up|ord><around|(|M<rsub|j>|)>,<math-up|flag><around|(|M<rsub|j>|)>|)>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|j><around|\||z<rsub|j>|\|><math-up|cost><around|(|M<rsub|j>|)>\<leqslant\>A,
  </equation*>

  where the list is finite and every insertion is anchored at the origin.
  Choose <math|Q<rsub|j>> by <eqref|eq:ope-leaf-projection>, and let
  <math|Q=<big|sum><rsub|j>z<rsub|j>*Q<rsub|j>>. The sum means addition of
  coefficient vectors and of forced arrays, and concatenation of the regular
  remainder lists. Thus all locality and covariance conditions are preserved.
  The triangle inequality gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-group-projection><around|\||Q|\|><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>\<leqslant\>x*A,<space|2em><math-up|rem><around|(|Q|)>\<leqslant\>x*A.
  </equation>

  For the regular remainder, multiply each majorant by
  <math|<around|\||z<rsub|j>|\|>> and sum its weighted costs. For the forced
  remainder, do the same for its uniformly summable majorants. These
  constructions prove the asserted bound without any cancellation of signed
  terms. Finite linearity of integration gives

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|dens><around|(|Q|)>=L<rsup|d>*\<cal-T\><math-up|coin><rsub|R><around|(|g,q|)>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed the linear density of <math|<around|(|g,q|)>> is the sum of the
  elementary densities with coefficients <math|z<rsub|j>>, by the singleton
  formula <eqref|eq:ope-single-coin>. Each elementary density is required to
  be integrable under the fine Haar prior and in each transition slice, as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-transfer>; the lawful representation
  and transition-kernel properties supply this in the application. Thus
  finite summation commutes with the transition integral. The empty list
  gives <math|Q=0> and the same conclusion.

  The singleton transition expansions concatenate in precisely the same way.
  Their fine cost is at most <math|2*A> and their coarse response cost at
  most <math|x*A>. Dividing the output field and its profile by
  <math|L<rsup|d>> changes the latter cost to <math|L<rsup|-d>*x*A>, while
  the fine cost stays <math|2*A>. The multiplier in the quoted-profile
  identity is now one. This is the normalization needed by the one-scale
  transport lemma.

  Require also that the whole marked input density is integrable under the
  fine prior and the transition slices; this is the hypothesis needed below
  for taking polynomial coefficients and regrouping through integration.
  Suppose the other grouped fields are translated, coordinate transformed or
  conjugated copies of <math|<around|(|g,q|)>>, with origins that are
  embeddings of their coarse origins. The transition commutes with these
  motions, and the coefficient-field format is stable under them. Apply the
  same motion to <math|L<rsup|-d>*Q> in each cell. The quoted-profile
  expansions transform by relabelling the finite set of slots; their cost
  bounds remain <math|2*A> and <math|L<rsup|-d>*x*A>. The singleton
  coefficient of the integrated marked density is the linear density of the
  corresponding transformed copy of <math|L<rsup|-d>*Q>: taking a coefficient
  commutes with integration, and the preceding density identity commutes with
  the motion. Thus the singleton-identification hypothesis of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-transfer> holds for every copied cell,
  with one and the same coefficient field <math|Q>.

  If the incoming mark weights <math|I<rsub|v>> satisfy

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-projection-afford>2*I<rsub|v>*<around|(|2*A|)>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>I<rsub|v>*L<rsup|-d>*x*A\<leqslant\>1,
  </equation>

  and the support, occupancy, numerical and new-weight hypotheses of that
  transport lemma hold, it follows that the integrated marked density has the
  required local representation with these coefficient fields and
  higher-array price one. This conclusion retains the entire marked density,
  not only its singleton coefficients.

  <subsubsection|The terminal price and physical normalization>

  At the terminal grouping stage, let <math|z\<gtr\>0> be any lower bound for
  the common grouped weight supplied by the grouping induction.
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-singletons> gives <math|A=4/z>. Take
  <math|x\<geqslant\>1> and reduce the incoming weights to

  <\equation*>
    I<rsub|v>=<frac|z|16*x>.
  </equation*>

  They do not exceed the available grouped weight. Reducing positive weights
  decreases every higher coefficient price. Moreover

  <\equation*>
    2*I<rsub|v>*<around|(|2*A|)>=<frac|1|x>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>I<rsub|v>*L<rsup|-d>*x*A=<frac|L<rsup|-d>|4>\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  This verifies <eqref|eq:ope-projection-afford>. If the outgoing weights
  obey <math|0\<less\>J<rsub|v>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|m>*I<rsub|v>>,
  where <math|\<delta\><rsub|m>> is the first reduction factor in the
  transport lemma, its final reweighting condition follows term by term: the
  product of the <math|J<rsub|v>> is at most the product of
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|m>*I<rsub|v>>, and the allowed support exponential is
  at least one.

  We have obtained a coarse field <math|Q> with both bounds at most
  <math|4*x/z>, appearing in the density with prefactor <math|L<rsup|-d>>.
  Define the physically normalized coefficient field

  <\equation*>
    Q<rprime|'>=a<rsub|m+1><rsup|d>*Q.
  </equation*>

  Scaling multiplies its coefficient and remainder costs by the positive
  number <math|a<rsub|m+1><rsup|d>>. Since

  <\equation*>
    a<rsub|m><rsup|-d>*a<rsub|m+1><rsup|d>=L<rsup|-d>,
  </equation*>

  this changes neither the ordinary singleton nor its flagged profile in the
  density. It only rewrites them with the standard physical prefactor
  <math|a<rsub|m><rsup|-d>>. We obtain

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-terminal-projection-price><around|\||Q<rprime|'>|\|><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>,<space|0.27em><math-up|rem><around|(|Q<rprime|'>|)>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|m+1><rsup|d>*<frac|4*x|z>.
  </equation>

  This is the terminal projection price used in the cutoff accounting: take
  <math|z> equal to its available grouping budget and <math|x> equal to the
  elementary projection allowance.

  Finally, regrouping and backwards integration are compatible. The
  regrouping map reads the coefficient of the complete old group at each new
  mark; it is a linear map between finite-dimensional polynomial spaces. It
  therefore commutes with every integrable transition. At the finest scale,
  reading a complete group in the marked birth density gives the product of
  its constituent observables. An induction over the intervening transitions
  shows that the terminal reaped density is the backwards-integrated density
  of that product. Consequently the coefficient field in
  <eqref|eq:ope-terminal-projection-price> represents the intended cluster
  product through the same sequence of scales. This identifies the observable
  to which the cutoff price applies; the elementary estimate
  <eqref|eq:ope-leaf-projection> and the nonlinear transition estimate still
  require their own proofs.

  <subsection|Decay of the matched reference error><label|sec:reference-cone>

  The reference comparison uses a second cone argument. Here the coefficient
  vector is small compared with the remainder cost. The initial condition is
  imposed at the coarse comparison scale; the resulting cone propagates
  towards finer scales and bounds the error in the opposite direction.

  Let <math|\<mu\>> be the coefficient <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>> norm, including
  the unit coordinate. At scale <math|i> write <math|v<rsub|i>> for the
  coefficient vector of a signed comparison and <math|r<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>
  for its remainder cost. These are the data of the actual difference, not
  the sum of the costs of its two summands. On an interval
  <math|l\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>k>, assume <math|v<rsub|l>=0> and a
  one-step comparison of the following form. Put

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|i>=L<rsub|i><rsup|1/8>,<space|2em>\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>=q<rsub|i><rsup|-3>,<space|2em>Y<rsub|i>=\<mu\>*<around|(|D<rsub|L<rsub|i>>*v<rsub|i+1>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|D<rsub|L>> multiplies the coordinate of degree
  <math|j\<leqslant\>d> by <math|L<rsup|d-j>>. An invertible coefficient map
  <math|B<rsub|i>> and its inverse have norm at most
  <math|K<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1>, and the transition satisfies

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\>*<around|(|v<rsub|i>-B<rsub|i>*D<rsub|L<rsub|i>>*v<rsub|i+1>|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>*<around|{|\<mu\><around|(|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>v<rsub|i+1>|)>+r<rsub|i+1>|}>,<eq-number><label|eq:reference-direction>>>|<row|<cell|r<rsub|i>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>K<rsub|i>*\<mu\>*<around|(|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>D<rsub|L<rsub|i>>*v<rsub|i+1>|)>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>*r<rsub|i+1>.>>>>
  </align>

  Here <math|<math-up|core>> deletes the unit coordinate. Let
  <math|C<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1> be the column allowances. The schedule is
  required to satisfy

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reference-bank>64*K<rsub|i><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|i>,<space|2em>q<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>100000*M<rsub|i><rsup|6>*<around|(|1+C<rsub|i+1>|)><rsup|2>,<space|2em>M<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>K<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1.
  </equation>

  Finally suppose the finest-scale cost obeys

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reference-terminal>r<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>w+C<rsub|k>*<around|{|w+\<mu\><around|(|v<rsub|k>|)>|}>,<space|2em>w\<geqslant\>0.
  </equation>

  We prove

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reference-gain>e<rsup|<around|(|s<rsub|k>-s<rsub|i>|)>/8>*<around|{|r<rsub|i>+\<mu\><around|(|v<rsub|i>|)>|}>\<leqslant\>4*<around|(|1+C<rsub|k>|)>*w*<space|2em><around|(|l\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>k|)>,
  </equation>

  where <math|s<rsub|i+1>-s<rsub|i>=log L<rsub|i>>.

  First, deleting the unit coordinate cannot increase <math|\<mu\>>, and
  <math|L<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1> gives <math|\<mu\><around|(|v<rsub|i+1>|)>\<leqslant\>Y<rsub|i>>.
  Apply <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|-1>> to <eqref|eq:reference-direction> and use
  the triangle inequality:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reference-numeric-direction>Y<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|i>*<around|{|\<mu\><around|(|v<rsub|i>|)>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>*<around|(|Y<rsub|i>+r<rsub|i+1>|)>|}>,<space|2em>r<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|i>*Y<rsub|i>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>*r<rsub|i+1>.
  </equation>

  Set <math|A<rsub|i>=32*<around|(|1+C<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>>. The schedule
  implies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reference-smallness>A<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>4*K<rsub|i><rsup|2>,<space|1em>4*K<rsub|i>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1,<space|1em>4*K<rsub|i>*A<rsub|i+1>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1,<space|1em><around|(|4*K<rsub|i><rsup|2>+1|)>*q<rsub|i>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation>

  Indeed <eqref|eq:reference-bank> gives <math|q<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>128*K<rsub|i><rsup|2>*<around|(|1+C<rsub|i+1>|)><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>1>.
  Since <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>q<rsub|i><rsup|-1>> and
  <math|q<rsub|i>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>q<rsub|i><rsup|-1>>, the
  last three claims follow from <math|4*K<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>q<rsub|i>>,
  <math|128*K<rsub|i>*<around|(|1+C<rsub|i+1>|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>q<rsub|i>>,
  and <math|4*K<rsub|i><rsup|2>+1\<leqslant\>q<rsub|i>>, respectively. The
  first follows from <math|C<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>64*K<rsub|i><rsup|2>> and
  <math|C<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1>.

  Suppose the cone inequality <math|A<rsub|i>*\<mu\><around|(|v<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|i>>
  holds. For this one step abbreviate <math|K=K<rsub|i>>,
  <math|\<varepsilon\>=\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>>,
  <math|u=\<mu\><around|(|v<rsub|i>|)>>, <math|Y=Y<rsub|i>> and
  <math|t=r<rsub|i+1>>. The second inequality in
  <eqref|eq:reference-numeric-direction> yields

  <\equation*>
    4*K*u\<leqslant\>Y+\<varepsilon\>*t,
  </equation*>

  because <math|4*K<rsup|2>*u\<leqslant\>r<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>K*Y+\<varepsilon\>*t>
  and <math|K\<geqslant\>1>. Substitute this bound for <math|u> in the first
  inequality:

  <\equation*>
    Y\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|4><around|(|Y+\<varepsilon\>*t|)>+K*\<varepsilon\>*Y+K*\<varepsilon\>*t\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>Y+<around|(|K+<tfrac|1|4>|)>*\<varepsilon\>*t.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|Y\<leqslant\><around|(|2*K+1/2|)>*\<varepsilon\>*t\<leqslant\>4*K*\<varepsilon\>*t>.
  Using <eqref|eq:reference-smallness>, we obtain

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|i+1>*\<mu\><around|(|v<rsub|i+1>|)>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|i+1>*Y\<leqslant\>t,<space|2em>q<rsub|i>*r<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>q<rsub|i>*<around|(|4*K<rsup|2>+1|)>*\<varepsilon\>*t\<leqslant\>t.
  </equation*>

  The first statement propagates the cone. The second gives the gain in
  remainder cost. At <math|i=l> the cone holds because <math|v<rsub|l>=0>;
  induction therefore proves both inequalities at every step. Multiplying the
  latter gives

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|<around|(|s<rsub|k>-s<rsub|i>|)>/8>*r<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|k>.
  </equation*>

  At the finest scale the cone also implies
  <math|2*C<rsub|k>*\<mu\><around|(|v<rsub|k>|)>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|k>>.
  Insert this in <eqref|eq:reference-terminal>:

  <\equation*>
    r<rsub|k>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+C<rsub|k>|)>*w+<tfrac|1|2>r<rsub|k>,<space|2em>r<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1+C<rsub|k>|)>*w.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|\<mu\><around|(|v<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|i>> everywhere
  in the cone, this proves <eqref|eq:reference-gain>.

  <subsubsection|Keeping the error affordable along the interval>

  The gain estimate is combined with a sequence of mark weights. Let
  <math|\<eta\><rsub|i>\<gtr\>0>, put <math|b<rsub|l>=b\<gtr\>0> and
  <math|b<rsub|i+1>=\<eta\><rsub|i>*b<rsub|i>>. Let
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> satisfy

  <\equation*>
    \<eta\><rsub|i>*L<rsub|i><rsup|d+1>*\<rho\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<rho\><rsub|i+1>.
  </equation*>

  If <math|E\<geqslant\>0> and

  <\equation*>
    b<rsub|l>*a<rsub|l><rsup|-d>*<frac|E|e<rsup|<around|(|s<rsub|k>-s<rsub|l>|)>/8>>\<leqslant\>\<rho\><rsub|l>,
  </equation*>

  then the same estimate holds at every <math|i\<geqslant\>l> up to <math|k>:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reference-affordable>b<rsub|i>*a<rsub|i><rsup|-d>*<frac|E|e<rsup|<around|(|s<rsub|k>-s<rsub|i>|)>/8>>\<leqslant\>\<rho\><rsub|i>.
  </equation>

  In fact, the expression on the left is multiplied in one step by
  <math|\<eta\><rsub|i>*L<rsub|i><rsup|d>*q<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<eta\><rsub|i>*L<rsub|i><rsup|d+1>>.
  This proves the assertion by induction. Applied with
  <math|E=4*<around|(|1+C<rsub|k>|)>*w>, it bounds the weighted
  coefficient-plus-remainder cost of the matched difference at all
  intermediate scales. The construction of that signed difference and the
  proof of its one-step comparison <eqref|eq:reference-direction> remain
  separate inputs: this numerical argument does not obtain them by
  subtracting two unrelated error estimates.

  <subsection|Constructing the signed reference
  difference><label|sec:reference-difference>

  The cone in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:reference-cone> applies only to
  a comparison produced on a common background history. We describe that
  construction. Its principal feature is to remove the coefficient vector
  after each backward step, retaining the removed part in a reference label.
  This provides a family of exact comparisons, from which a single label is
  chosen at the coarsest scale.

  Fix the degree <math|d> and scales <math|l\<leqslant\>k>. Assume the bank
  and column inequalities <eqref|eq:reference-bank>, with
  <math|K<rsub|i>,C<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1>, as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:reference-cone>. At each scale <math|i>, let
  <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|b|)>> be the calibrated reference
  coefficient field with bare label <math|b> in the fixed coefficient space.
  Its coefficient vector is <math|I<rsub|i>*b>, where <math|I<rsub|i>> is a
  linear isomorphism. Assume its density, ordinary field and flagged profile
  depend linearly on <math|b>, and its remainder has a nonnegative
  subadditive bound <math|h<rsub|i><around|(|b|)>> with

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reference-primary-charge>h<rsub|i><around|(|b|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|i>*\<mu\>*<around|(|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>I<rsub|i>*b|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|i>*\<mu\>*<around|(|I<rsub|i>*b|)>.
  </equation>

  The reference densities satisfy the exact degree-<math|d> transition
  identity. Their coefficient and remainder data satisfy
  <eqref|eq:reference-direction>, with a common comparison map for each
  reference label, as supplied by the calibrated construction.

  We also require a small transition estimate for a coefficient field
  <math|S> whose coefficient vector is zero. If its remainder is bounded by
  <math|q\<geqslant\>0>, its backward transition has a representation
  <math|U> such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reference-zero-transition><math-up|dens><around|(|U|)>=L<rsub|i><rsup|d>*\<cal-T\><rsub|i><math-up|dens><around|(|S|)>,<space|1em>\<mu\>*<around|(|U.*x|)>\<leqslant\>\<eta\><rsub|i>*q,<space|1em><math-up|rem><around|(|U|)>\<leqslant\>\<eta\><rsub|i>*q,<space|1em><around|(|1+2*C<rsub|i>|)>*\<eta\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<eta\><rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0> and
  <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>=L<rsub|i><rsup|-3/8>>. The fields and their
  sums retain the coefficient-field format and the integrability needed for
  the displayed transition identities. These are analytic inputs for the same
  history, including its calibrated reference fields.

  <subsubsection|Removing the coefficient after each step>

  Start with a coefficient field <math|Q> at scale <math|k>, with remainder
  bound <math|t\<geqslant\>0> and <math|\<mu\>*<around|(|Q.*x|)>+t\<leqslant\>w>.
  Put

  <\equation*>
    c<rsub|k>=I<rsub|k><rsup|-1>*Q.*x,<space|2em>S<rsub|k>=Q-\<cal-P\><rsub|k><around|(|c<rsub|k>|)>,<space|2em>q<rsub|k>=t+h<rsub|k>*<around|(|-c<rsub|k>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The minus sign denotes a sum with the field
  <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|k>*<around|(|-c<rsub|k>|)>>; its regular remainders
  are concatenated with those of <math|Q>. Thus <math|S<rsub|k>.*x=0> and its
  remainder is at most <math|q<rsub|k>>. For every bare label <math|v>,
  define

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|k><around|(|v|)>=S<rsub|k>+\<cal-P\><rsub|k>*<around|(|c<rsub|k>+v|)>,<space|2em>r<rsub|k><around|(|v|)>=q<rsub|k>+h<rsub|k>*<around|(|c<rsub|k>+v|)>.
  </equation*>

  Linearity of the reference density and vector gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reference-top-family>D<rsub|k><around|(|v|)>.*x=Q.*x+I<rsub|k>*v,<space|2em><math-up|dens><around|(|D<rsub|k><around|(|v|)>|)>=<math-up|dens><around|(|Q|)>+<math-up|dens><around|(|\<cal-P\><rsub|k><around|(|v|)>|)>.
  </equation>

  The cost, despite possible cancellation in this identity, is kept as the
  sum of the displayed nonnegative remainder bounds. Since
  <math|t\<leqslant\>w> and <math|\<mu\>*<around|(|Q.*x|)>\<leqslant\>w>,

  <\equation*>
    r<rsub|k><around|(|v|)>\<leqslant\>w+C<rsub|k>*<around|{|w+\<mu\>*<around|(|D<rsub|k><around|(|v|)>.*x|)>|}>.
  </equation*>

  This is exactly the finest-scale condition <eqref|eq:reference-terminal>.

  Suppose <math|c<rsub|i+1>,S<rsub|i+1>,q<rsub|i+1>> have been constructed,
  with <math|S<rsub|i+1>.*x=0>. Apply <eqref|eq:reference-zero-transition> to
  obtain <math|U<rsub|i>>, put <math|e<rsub|i>=\<eta\><rsub|i>*q<rsub|i+1>>,
  and set

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|b<rsub|i>>|<cell|=I<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*U<rsub|i>.*x,>|<cell|c<rsub|i>>|<cell|=c<rsub|i+1>+b<rsub|i>,>>|<row|<cell|S<rsub|i>>|<cell|=U<rsub|i>-\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|b<rsub|i>|)>,>|<cell|q<rsub|i>>|<cell|=e<rsub|i>+h<rsub|i>*<around|(|-b<rsub|i>|)>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Then <math|S<rsub|i>.*x=0>, and <math|q<rsub|i>> bounds its remainder.
  Define <math|D<rsub|i><around|(|v|)>=S<rsub|i>+\<cal-P\><rsub|i>*<around|(|c<rsub|i>+v|)>>
  and <math|r<rsub|i><around|(|v|)>=q<rsub|i>+h<rsub|i>*<around|(|c<rsub|i>+v|)>>.
  Their densities satisfy

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|dens><around|(|D<rsub|i><around|(|v|)>|)>=<math-up|dens><around|(|U<rsub|i>|)>+<math-up|dens><around|(|\<cal-P\><rsub|i>*<around|(|c<rsub|i+1>+v|)>|)>=L<rsub|i><rsup|d>*\<cal-T\><rsub|i><math-up|dens><around|(|D<rsub|i+1><around|(|v|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The first equality cancels the two reference terms with labels
  <math|-b<rsub|i>> and <math|b<rsub|i>> by linearity. The second uses both
  transition identities and linearity of the integrable transition. The same
  cancellation holds separately for ordinary fields and flagged profiles; it
  is not merely an equality after taking expectation.

  We verify the signed one-step estimates. Fix <math|v> and write
  <math|y=c<rsub|i+1>+v>. Then

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|i+1><around|(|v|)>.*x=I<rsub|i+1>*y,<space|2em>D<rsub|i><around|(|v|)>.*x=I<rsub|i>*y+U<rsub|i>.*x.
  </equation*>

  Use the comparison map belonging to the primary label <math|y>. Its
  coefficient error is at most

  <\equation*>
    \<varepsilon\><rsub|i>*<around|{|\<mu\>*<around|(|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>I<rsub|i+1>*y|)>+h<rsub|i+1><around|(|y|)>|}>.
  </equation*>

  The added vector <math|U<rsub|i>.*x> costs at most
  <math|\<eta\><rsub|i>*q<rsub|i+1>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>*q<rsub|i+1>>.
  Thus the coefficient inequality in <eqref|eq:reference-direction> holds for
  the actual signed vectors, with cost <math|r<rsub|i+1><around|(|v|)>>.

  For the remainder, <eqref|eq:reference-primary-charge> gives
  <math|h<rsub|i>*<around|(|\<pm\>b<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|i>*e<rsub|i>>.
  Subadditivity yields

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|r<rsub|i><around|(|v|)>>|<cell|=e<rsub|i>+h<rsub|i>*<around|(|-b<rsub|i>|)>+h<rsub|i>*<around|(|y+b<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>h<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>+<around|(|1+2*C<rsub|i>|)>*e<rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>K<rsub|i>*\<mu\>*<around|(|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>D<rsub|L<rsub|i>>*I<rsub|i+1>*y|)>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>*<around|{|h<rsub|i+1><around|(|y|)>+q<rsub|i+1>|}>.>>>>
  </align*>

  This is the other inequality of <eqref|eq:reference-direction>. In
  particular it is a bound for the very same presentation that supplies the
  exact density identity. No subtraction of unsigned remainder bounds is
  used.

  Now choose the single label <math|v=-c<rsub|l>> and write
  <math|b=c<rsub|l>>. The coefficient vector of
  <math|D<rsub|l>*<around|(|-b|)>> vanishes. The construction therefore meets
  every hypothesis of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:reference-cone>,
  including its terminal condition. Writing
  <math|Z<rsub|i>=D<rsub|i>*<around|(|-b|)>> and
  <math|r<rsub|i>=r<rsub|i>*<around|(|-b|)>> gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reference-produced-difference><math-up|dens><around|(|Z<rsub|k>|)>=<math-up|dens><around|(|Q|)>-<math-up|dens><around|(|\<cal-P\><rsub|k><around|(|b|)>|)>,<space|2em>r<rsub|i>+\<mu\>*<around|(|Z<rsub|i>.*x|)>\<leqslant\><frac|4*<around|(|1+C<rsub|k>|)>*w|e<rsup|<around|(|s<rsub|k>-s<rsub|i>|)>/8>>.
  </equation>

  If <math|E\<geqslant\>4*<around|(|1+C<rsub|k>|)>*w>, then
  <math|w\<leqslant\>E> and at <math|i=k>

  <\equation*>
    \<mu\>*<around|(|I<rsub|k>*b|)>=\<mu\>*<around|(|Q.*x-Z<rsub|k>.*x|)>\<leqslant\>w+E\<leqslant\>2*E.
  </equation*>

  This is the quota bound for the matched reference label.

  <subsubsection|The singleton transition expansion is retained>

  To use this difference inside a marked density, more than its coefficient
  and remainder estimates is needed. Suppose the zero-vector transition in
  <eqref|eq:reference-zero-transition> comes with the singleton expansion of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-transfer>, of fine cost
  <math|2*q<rsub|i+1>> and coarse response cost <math|e<rsub|i>>. Suppose the
  primary transition for label <math|y> has fine cost at most

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|orb><around|(|d|)>*\<mu\>*<around|(|I<rsub|i+1>*y|)>*<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|i>+2*C<rsub|i+1>|)>,
  </equation*>

  and coarse response cost <math|h<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>>. Both expansions
  must use the same quoted-profile map; their finite sets of slots can then
  be united. Their sum has the ordinary and flagged profiles of
  <math|D<rsub|i+1><around|(|v|)>> and the coarse profile of
  <math|D<rsub|i><around|(|v|)>>, by the cancellations just proved. Its fine
  cost is at most

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|orb><around|(|d|)>*\<mu\>*<around|(|D<rsub|i+1><around|(|v|)>.*x|)>*<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|i>+2*C<rsub|i+1>|)>+2*r<rsub|i+1><around|(|v|)>.
  </equation*>

  Its response cost <math|e<rsub|i>+h<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>> is at most
  <math|r<rsub|i><around|(|v|)>>, because

  <\equation*>
    h<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>=h<rsub|i>*<around*|(|-b<rsub|i>+<around|(|y+b<rsub|i>|)>|)>\<leqslant\>h<rsub|i>*<around|(|-b<rsub|i>|)>+h<rsub|i>*<around|(|y+b<rsub|i>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Thus the same signed difference carries the singleton transition expansions
  needed for the marked transport argument. Combining
  <eqref|eq:reference-produced-difference> with
  <eqref|eq:reference-affordable> makes these expansions affordable once the
  prescribed one-step allowances are imposed. The production of the small
  zero-vector transition and the calibrated primary transition bounds remains
  an analytic dependency of this construction.

  <subsection|From a coefficient field to a finite-cutoff reference
  observable><label|sec:reference-match>

  The signed comparison must control the original finite-cutoff observable,
  not only its coefficient vector. This requires a simultaneous marked
  argument. We explain the finite-dimensional matching and the exact
  replicated difference used in that argument.

  Fix a degree <math|d> and three depths <math|J\<leqslant\>m\<leqslant\>k\<less\>N>
  with <math|<math-up|ref><around|(|d|)>\<leqslant\>m> and
  <math|B<rsub|m><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|J>/a<rsub|m>>, where
  <math|B<rsub|m>> is the purchased covering radius. We call <math|m> an
  opened depth for <math|J> when <math|J\<leqslant\>m> and this last
  inequality hold. All paths below use the same finest scale <math|N> and the
  same history. The depth <math|J> is the centered depth: the replicated
  cells are the <math|M> cells of the centered depth-<math|J> tiling, and the
  root test <math|\<cal-R\>> below is taken over them. The depth
  <math|m\<geqslant\>J> is the opening depth, at which the common weight
  evolution of the marked transport starts; <math|k> is the matching depth.
  The opening condition is distinct from the room condition imposed below.
  Let <math|Q> represent a continuous finite-cutoff observable <math|f> at
  scale <math|k> through the full marked local representation. Suppose its
  coefficient-plus-remainder bound is at most <math|c\<gtr\>0>, and put

  <\equation*>
    E\<geqslant\>4*<around|(|1+C<rsub|k>|)>*c,<space|2em>G=e<rsup|<around|(|s<rsub|k>-s<rsub|m>|)>/8>,<space|2em>w\<gtr\>0.
  </equation*>

  Assume the signed transition hypotheses of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:reference-difference>, the one-scale marked
  transport hypotheses of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-transfer>, and
  the necessary cell separation and chamber locality at each scale. The
  common affordability condition is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reference-match-afford>32*<around|(|1+C<rsub|k>|)>*w*a<rsub|m><rsup|-d>*E/G\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsub|m>/2.
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<theta\><rsub|i>> are the single-source allowances; their
  growth condition is the one used in <eqref|eq:reference-affordable>. The
  coefficient paths must also satisfy the corresponding one-step singleton
  cost conditions there and in the marked transport lemma. No availability of
  these analytic hypotheses is inferred from
  <eqref|eq:reference-match-afford> alone.

  <subsubsection|An approximate coordinate basis>

  Apply the signed reference construction first to <math|Q>. It gives a label
  <math|b> with <math|\<mu\>*<around|(|I<rsub|k>*b|)>\<leqslant\>2*E> and a
  path representing <math|Q-\<cal-P\><rsub|k><around|(|b|)>>. Its opening
  price is <math|2*w>; the required initial affordability follows from
  <eqref|eq:reference-match-afford> because
  <math|2\<leqslant\>32*<around|(|1+C<rsub|k>|)>>.

  For each coefficient coordinate <math|j>, apply the same construction to
  the primary field with vector <math|e<rsub|j>> and zero remainder. Use
  opening price <math|8*E*w> and coefficient allowance
  <math|4*<around|(|1+C<rsub|k>|)>>. Its affordability expression is exactly
  the left side of <eqref|eq:reference-match-afford>. Obtain labels
  <math|b<rsub|j>>. The cone estimate at <math|k>, now with terminal bound
  <math|1>, gives

  <\equation*>
    32*<around|(|1+C<rsub|k>|)><rsup|2>*\<mu\>*<around|(|e<rsub|j>-I<rsub|k>*b<rsub|j>|)>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1+C<rsub|k>|)>,<space|2em>\<mu\>*<around|(|e<rsub|j>-I<rsub|k>*b<rsub|j>|)>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|4>.
  </equation*>

  Define <math|A*z=<big|sum><rsub|j>z<rsub|j>*I<rsub|k>*b<rsub|j>>. Since
  <math|\<mu\>> is the coefficient <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>> norm,

  <\equation*>
    \<mu\>*<around|(|z-A*z|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|j><around|\||z<rsub|j>|\|>*\<mu\>*<around|(|e<rsub|j>-I<rsub|k>*b<rsub|j>|)>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|4>\<mu\><around|(|z|)>.
  </equation*>

  It follows that <math|\<mu\><around|(|z|)>\<leqslant\>2*\<mu\>*<around|(|A*z|)>>.
  Thus <math|A> is injective and, in this finite-dimensional space,
  surjective. Solve <math|A*s=I<rsub|k>*b>. Injectivity of <math|I<rsub|k>>
  gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reference-basis-expansion>b=<big|sum><rsub|j>s<rsub|j>*b<rsub|j>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|j><around|\||s<rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*\<mu\>*<around|(|I<rsub|k>*b|)>\<leqslant\>4*E.
  </equation>

  No uniform condition number of an arbitrarily chosen reference basis is
  being assumed; the bound comes from the explicitly constructed approximate
  coordinate vectors.

  Let <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>> be the physically normalized primary field for
  <math|e<rsub|j>> at scale <math|k>, with its local mask, and set

  <\equation*>
    \<ell\>=-<big|sum><rsub|j>s<rsub|j>*\<ell\><rsub|j>,r=\<cal-B\><rsub|k,N>*\<ell\>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|k,N>> is the backward observable transfer to the
  finest lattice. All <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>> lie in the same fixed stencil
  window, of some fixed radius <math|R>. Hence <math|\<ell\>> does too. We
  assume the room condition of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-phase-grid-approximants>
  for the centered depth <math|J>, the matching depth <math|k> and this
  radius, namely <math|2*<around|(|a<rsub|k>*R+T<rsub|k>|)>\<less\>a<rsub|J>>
  with <math|T<rsub|k>=30*a<rsub|k>*<around|(|h<rsub|k>+5|)>>; it places the
  propagated window, and so <math|r>, inside the centered <math|J>-chamber at
  every depth between <math|k> and <math|N>. This common window is essential
  for the replicated transfer identity below.

  <subsubsection|The full replicated difference>

  We spell out that identity, keeping every mixed term. Write
  <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|N,k>> for the forward density transfer, adjoint to
  <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|k,N>> under the Haar priors. For copied fine
  observables <math|O<rsub|i>> and copied coarse observables
  <math|F<rsub|i>>, the coefficient of a mark set <math|S> after introducing
  the coarse sources is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reference-join><big|sum><rsub|A\<subseteq\>S>\<cal-T\><rsub|N,k>*<around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|N>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S\<setminus\>A><around|(|-O<rsub|i>|)>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>A>F<rsub|i>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<rho\><rsub|N>> is the unnormalized Wilson density. The same
  forward transfer is used for the whole fine product in each summand. It has
  not been replaced by a product of transferred factors.

  For coarse functions in the common local window, the room condition ensures
  that backward transfer respects their copied product:
  <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|k,N><around|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>A>F<rsub|i>|)>>
  is the product of the corresponding copies of <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|k,N>*F>.
  This is <eqref|eq:actual-grid-factor>, proved under exactly that condition
  by disjointness of the kernel read sets of different chambers at every
  intervening transition; it is not a consequence of adjointness or of
  normalization alone. Using adjointness in each summand of
  <eqref|eq:reference-join>, its integral is therefore

  <\equation*>
    <big|int>\<rho\><rsub|N>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S><around|(|\<cal-B\><rsub|k,N>*F<rsub|i>-O<rsub|i>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*U.
  </equation*>

  This follows by the finite subset expansion of the product. The interchange
  is justified by bounded local observables, integrable fine densities and
  the transition-slice bounds. Further forward transfers leave this integral
  unchanged. Thus the coefficient of the complete mark set is the Wilson
  partition function times the replicated difference moment.

  The singleton bounds used here follow from the priced coefficient basis and
  the remainder bounds. Explicitly, require the masked basis evaluation bound
  with column constant <math|C<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>1> and remainder divisor at
  most one. If a coefficient field has vector <math|q> and remainder cost
  <math|t>, the triangle inequality gives
  <math|<around|\||g|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*\<mu\><around|(|q|)>+t>
  before physical normalization. The sum of the norms of its flagged profiles
  is at most <math|t>: sum the absolutely summable forced majorants and use
  that every support weight is at least one. The same argument bounds the
  ordinary remainder by <math|t>; the primary term is bounded by
  <math|<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*\<mu\><around|(|q|)>> using the priced
  basis. If <math|v*a<rsub|m><rsup|-d>*<around|(|t+\<mu\><around|(|q|)>|)>\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsub|m>>
  and <math|C<rsub|m>*\<theta\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1>, these bounds give
  normalized singleton allowances <math|2> and <math|1>. Translations and
  conjugation preserve them. This is the sharper bound for the matched
  coefficient paths; it is separate from the general centered-field estimate.

  Suppose the marked transport has reached the opening depth <math|m> with
  common mark weight <math|v\<gtr\>0>, higher-array price one, and singleton
  bounds

  <\equation*>
    v<around|\||g<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>2,<space|2em>v*<big|sum><rsub|X><around|\||q<rsub|i>*<around|(|X,U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  The integrated marked coefficient estimate <eqref|ex-centered-sail>,
  applied at the opening depth with the <math|M> centered <math|J>-cells as
  marked copies and with singleton allowances <math|2> and <math|1> (so
  <math|x=2>, <math|y=1>, <math|f=1> and <math|x+y+2*f=5>), then bounds the
  complete moment by

  <\equation*>
    v<rsup|M><around|\||<math-up|moment>|\|>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around*|(|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|m>|\|>*\<cal-V\><rsub|m>+5*M|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|m>|\|>> is the number of
  depth-<math|m> sites and <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|m>> is the per-site treasury
  bound <math|t<rsub|m>> of the opening depth, which bounds the extensive
  bracket of <eqref|ex-centered-sail> by the finite-volume treasury identity,
  as in Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-construction>. Since each
  centered <math|J>-cell contains <math|<around|(|a<rsub|J>/a<rsub|m>|)><rsup|4>>
  depth-<math|m> sites, <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|m>|\|>=M*<around|(|a<rsub|J>/a<rsub|m>|)><rsup|4>>.
  Write

  <\equation*>
    S<rsub|0>=exp <around*|(|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|m>|\|>*\<cal-V\><rsub|m>/M+5|)>=exp
    <around*|(|<around|(|a<rsub|J>/a<rsub|m>|)><rsup|4>*t<rsub|m>+5|)>,
  </equation*>

  the cell price. Taking the <math|M>th root gives the centered root bound
  <math|S<rsub|0>/v>. This is an estimate for the complete replicated
  difference; no estimate on its individual mixed summands is substituted.

  <subsubsection|Combining the comparison paths>

  The one-scale transport is stable under affordable finite linear
  combinations of its single-source paths. Explicitly, if path <math|j> has
  positive opening price <math|v<rsub|j>> and coefficient <math|a<rsub|j>>,
  the condition

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:reference-portfolio><big|sum><rsub|j>w<around|\||a<rsub|j>|\|>/v<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>1
  </equation>

  preserves every common normalized cost bound. All paths use the same
  multiplicative weight evolution: at scale <math|i> the opening prices
  <math|v<rsub|j>> and <math|w> are multiplied by the same positive factor.
  Thus the fractions in <eqref|eq:reference-portfolio> are unchanged at each
  intermediate scale. To verify the cost bound, if
  <math|v<rsub|j>*<big|sum><rsub|X><around|\||F<rsub|j,X>|\|>\<leqslant\>C>,
  then

  <\equation*>
    w*<big|sum><rsub|X><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|j>a<rsub|j>*F<rsub|j,X>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|j><frac|w<around|\||a<rsub|j>|\|>|v<rsub|j>>*v<rsub|j>*<big|sum><rsub|X><around|\||F<rsub|j,X>|\|>\<leqslant\>C.
  </equation*>

  The same calculation applies to singleton values, elementary transition
  costs and response majorants. Sum the dominated indexed arrays using their
  summed majorants. The transition identities are linear, so remain exact.
  Applying the marked transport successively proves the claim for the whole
  interval.

  Use the base difference path with coefficient <math|1> and price
  <math|2*w>, and the coordinate paths with coefficients <math|-s<rsub|j>>
  and price <math|8*E*w>. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:reference-basis-expansion>
  gives

  <\equation*>
    <frac|w|2*w>+<big|sum><rsub|j><frac|w<around|\||s<rsub|j>|\|>|8*E*w>\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  At scale <math|k>, the reference terms cancel separately in the ordinary
  and flagged profiles, because <math|-b+<big|sum><rsub|j><around|(|-s<rsub|j>|)>*<around|(|-b<rsub|j>|)>=0>.
  For a conjugating copy, use the conjugate of every coefficient
  <math|a<rsub|j>> in the portfolio; for an ordinary copy use
  <math|a<rsub|j>> itself. Their absolute values coincide, so the cost proof
  is unchanged. The cancellation in a conjugating copy is the conjugate of
  the displayed identity. Thus cancellation holds for every copied profile.
  The resulting ordinary field is the one for <math|Q> plus <math|\<ell\>>;
  the flagged profile is exactly the one for <math|Q>. Introducing
  <math|\<ell\>> into the marked representation of <math|f> is multiplication
  by its squarefree source exponential. The identity
  <eqref|eq:reference-join>, with <math|O=-f> and <math|F=\<ell\>>, therefore
  identifies the complete coefficient with the replicated moment of
  <math|f+r>. The transport and complete-moment estimate give

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-R\>*<around|(|f+r|)>\<leqslant\>S<rsub|0>/w,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<cal-R\>> is the centered root seminorm.

  For each coordinate path the same argument, starting from the terminal
  reference field and inserting <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>> at scale <math|k>,
  gives

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-R\>*<around*|(|\<cal-B\><rsub|k,N>*\<ell\><rsub|j>-\<cal-O\><around|(|b<rsub|j>|)>|)>\<leqslant\>S<rsub|0>/<around|(|8*E*w|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<cal-O\><around|(|b<rsub|j>|)>> denotes the fine observable
  attached to that bare label by the calibrated terminal identification. This
  application requires the terminal reference representation and its
  singleton profile identity on the same history. They remain among the
  analytic inputs to the matched construction. The chamber seminorm
  inequality, backward-transfer linearity and
  <eqref|eq:reference-basis-expansion> imply

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-R\>*<around*|(|-r-\<cal-O\><around|(|b|)>|)>\<leqslant\>4*E*<frac|S<rsub|0>|8*E*w>=<frac|S<rsub|0>|2*w>.
  </equation*>

  Finally <math|f-\<cal-O\><around|(|b|)>=<around|(|f+r|)>+<around|(|-r-\<cal-O\><around|(|b|)>|)>>
  lies in the common chamber class, so its centered root is at most
  <math|2*S<rsub|0>/w>. The matched reference observable has quota at most
  <math|2*E>, as proved above. These are exactly the two bounds used in the
  cutoff matching argument.

  <subsection|Capturing a cluster and paying for a cutoff
  match><label|sec:ope-cutoff>

  The local match used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-grid> is
  constructed before any continuum limit. One follows a product of local
  observables down through the scales, merging groups whenever their parent
  sites cease to be separated. The bookkeeping must be independent of the
  number of replicated cells. Otherwise it would be unusable in the centered
  root estimate. We give the geometric selection and the scale accounting
  explicitly, and state where the remaining local RG estimates enter.

  <subsubsection|Regrouping without a growing arity cost>

  Let a cluster have <math|h\<geqslant\>1> marked sites and distinguish one
  of its marks. At a given scale, call sites separated if their periodic
  maximum-coordinate distance is at least <math|B<rsup|2>>, where
  <math|B\<geqslant\>1> is the purchased support radius. Among the subsets
  containing the distinguished mark choose one of maximal cardinality with
  separated sites. Every omitted mark lies at distance less than
  <math|B<rsup|2>> from a chosen mark: otherwise it could be added. Assign
  each omitted mark to one such representative, and assign each chosen mark
  to itself. This defines a partition with a chosen representative in every
  part. If the original sites were not separated, the number of parts
  strictly decreases.

  Copies in different centered cells have the same relative geometry, up to
  translations and signed coordinate transformations, which preserve
  distance. Use the same partition in each copy. Cross-cell separation and
  separation of the chosen representatives in the home cell then give
  separation of all representatives. In particular the number of regroupings
  is at most <math|h-1>, regardless of how many cells are copied.

  The cost of connecting a mark to its representative can be charged one
  scale before their parents meet. To check the geometry, let the block ratio
  be <math|L>, let <math|K> be the parent's support radius and <math|B> the
  child's, and let <math|x,y> be two child sites whose parents have distance
  less than <math|K<rsup|2>>. Each child is at distance less than <math|L/2>
  from the embedded parent. The triangle inequality gives

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|dist><around|(|x,y|)>\<less\>L*<around|(|K<rsup|2>+1|)>\<leqslant\>L*<around|(|K+B+1|)><rsup|2>=:c.
  </equation*>

  A pair at distance at most <math|c*B> can be covered by at most
  <math|<around|(|2*c+1|)><rsup|4>> <math|B>-blocks: both points lie in the
  radius-<math|c*B> halo of one point, which is tiled by at most <math|2*c+1>
  blocks per coordinate. Since <math|B\<geqslant\>1>, the preceding pair
  therefore has covering number at most

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-pair-halo><around|(|2*c+1|)><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>16*<around|(|1+c|)><rsup|4>.
  </equation>

  This is the halo charged to the individual grouping operation. It does not
  assert that the entire cluster lies in a single small halo at every
  intervening scale.

  Here are the two local RG estimates used in the induction. A group has a
  positive weight <math|W> measuring the allowed size of its marked
  representation. If its parents stay separated, descent through a block of
  ratio <math|L> changes its weight to <math|W/L<rsup|4>>. If <math|p> groups
  merge into <math|q\<less\>p> groups at a scale with connecting-cover bound
  <math|\<ell\>>, their new weights can be taken to be

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-group-weight><wide|W|~><rsub|A>=<frac|1|8>*<big|prod><rsub|r\<in\>A><around|(|\<lambda\>*W<rsub|r>|)>,<space|2em>\<lambda\>=<frac|\<delta\><rsup|2>|h>*e<rsup|-<around|(|\<nu\>+1/2|)>*\<ell\>>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<nu\>> is the purchased support exponent and
  <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0> the local smallness parameter. The
  connecting-cover bound <math|\<ell\>> is the quantity written <math|k> in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-grouping>, where <math|\<ell\>> denotes
  instead the site occupancy. Each factor <math|\<lambda\>*W<rsub|r>> is the
  new weight <math|\<delta\><rsup|2>*W<rsub|r>*e<rsup|-<around|(|\<nu\>+1/2|)>*k>/h>
  of the single old mark <math|r>, and their product over <math|r\<in\>A> is
  the numerator computed at the end of that section, so that
  <math|8*<wide|W|~><rsub|A>=<big|prod><rsub|r\<in\>A><around|(|\<lambda\>*W<rsub|r>|)>>.
  These statements also retain the exact marked polynomial after regrouping,
  its covariance between copies, its integrability, and its permitted local
  representation. The analytic proofs of these two local representation
  estimates remain construction inputs. In particular their weight rules
  alone are not a proof that a marked representation exists.

  Choose one <math|0\<less\>\<alpha\>\<leqslant\>1> no larger than every
  grouping factor <math|\<lambda\>> that will be needed. If <math|j> is the
  initial scale, <math|i> the current scale and <math|s\<leqslant\>h> the
  current number of groups, put

  <\equation*>
    C*<around|(|i,s|)>=<around*|(|<frac|a<rsub|j>|a<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|4*h>*<around|(|\<alpha\>/8|)><rsup|h*<around|(|h-s|)>>*<big|prod><rsub|r=1><rsup|h>W<rsub|r><rsup|<math-up|initial>>.
  </equation*>

  We claim that the product of the current group weights is at least
  <math|C*<around|(|i,s|)>>. This is equality initially. For an ordinary
  descent,

  <\equation*>
    C*<around|(|i,s|)>=L<rsub|i><rsup|-4*h>*C*<around|(|i+1,s|)>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|i><rsup|-4*s>*<big|prod><rsub|r>W<rsub|r>,
  </equation*>

  since <math|s\<leqslant\>h> and <math|L<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1>. For a
  genuine grouping, <math|q\<less\>p\<leqslant\>h> and
  <math|\<alpha\>/8\<leqslant\>1> imply

  <\equation*>
    C*<around|(|i,q|)>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|i,p|)>*<around|(|\<alpha\>/8|)><rsup|h>.
  </equation*>

  Thus

  <\equation*>
    8<rsup|q>*C*<around|(|i,q|)>\<leqslant\>\<alpha\><rsup|h>*C*<around|(|i,p|)>\<leqslant\>\<lambda\><rsup|p>*<big|prod><rsub|r>W<rsub|r>.
  </equation*>

  We used <math|8<rsup|q>\<leqslant\>8<rsup|h>> and
  <math|\<alpha\><rsup|h>\<leqslant\>\<alpha\><rsup|p>\<leqslant\>\<lambda\><rsup|p>>.
  The last expression is exactly the product of the new numerators in
  <eqref|eq:ope-group-weight>, because the groups partition the old marks.
  This proves the induction after the subsequent descent as well. The
  regrouping of marked polynomials commutes with descent: it is a fixed
  linear map on a finite coefficient space, so it commutes with integration
  against the transition kernel. Its composition law is the composition law
  of the partitions. These observations keep the induction on the original
  exact product.

  The initial single-field estimates give weights
  <math|W<rsub|r><rsup|<math-up|initial>>=\<delta\><rsub|j>*\<eta\><rsub|j>*p<rsub|r><around|(|j|)>/8>,
  where <math|p<rsub|r><around|(|j|)>\<gtr\>0> is the paid weight of the
  <math|r>th field. At the final parent scale <math|m+1> merge all remaining
  groups. Their number <math|s> is at most <math|h>, and the last grouping
  factor is also at least <math|\<alpha\>>. The terminal merge uses the
  weight <math|\<lambda\><rsup|s>*<big|prod><rsub|r>W<rsub|r>> without the
  factor <math|1/8> reserved at intermediate regroupings; it supplies the
  final local projection directly. Since <math|\<alpha\><rsup|h>\<leqslant\>\<lambda\><rsup|s>>
  and <math|C*<around|(|m+1,0|)>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|m+1,s|)>>, the
  resulting weight is at least

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-initial-weight>X=\<alpha\><rsup|h><around*|(|<frac|a<rsub|j>|a<rsub|m+1>>|)><rsup|4*h>*<around|(|\<alpha\>/8|)><rsup|h<rsup|2>>*<around|(|\<delta\><rsub|j>*\<eta\><rsub|j>/8|)><rsup|h>*<big|prod><rsub|r=1><rsup|h>p<rsub|r><around|(|j|)>.
  </equation>

  The final local projection estimate produces a degree-<math|q> coefficient
  whose coefficient-size and remainder norm are each bounded by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-terminal-coefficient>P=a<rsub|m+1><rsup|q>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-B\><rsub|m>*<frac|4|X>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|m>> is the purchased local projection bound. This
  projection estimate, together with the marked-representation estimates
  above, is still an analytic input. The preceding argument proves the
  precise scale cost it must be given; no factor depends on the number of
  copies.

  <subsubsection|A single subpower allowance for all intermediate levels>

  The halo estimate ensures that each grouping factor has zero logarithmic
  rate. Explicitly the purchased bound is
  <math|<around|(|\<nu\><rsub|i+1>+1|)>*\<ell\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>16*<sqrt|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>>>,
  while <math|<sqrt|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>>/s<rsub|i>\<to\>0> and
  <math|log \<delta\><rsub|i+1>=o<around|(|s<rsub|i>|)>>. Taking logarithms
  of <eqref|eq:ope-group-weight> therefore gives <math|log
  \<lambda\><rsub|i>=o<around|(|s<rsub|i>|)>> for fixed <math|h>. These are
  schedule and running-coupling estimates; their production is a separate
  input. The cover inequality is the form in which
  condition<nbsp><eqref|eq:ope-scale-purchases-display-O1> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-scale-purchases> is used: by
  <eqref|eq:ope-pair-halo>, with parent and child radii
  <math|<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>> and
  <math|<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log y|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>> for the
  hatted couplings <math|x,y> of the transition, the connecting cover is at
  most <math|16*H<rsub|L>*<around|(|x,y|)>>, and
  <eqref|eq:ope-scale-purchases-display-O1> with <math|A=101=\<nu\>+1> gives
  <math|<around|(|\<nu\>+1|)>*\<ell\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>16*<sqrt|x>>. At the
  transition from level <math|i> the hatted couplings are
  <math|x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>> and <math|y=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1>>,
  as displayed in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-scale-purchases>. Since
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>> is an affine function of <math|s<rsub|i>>
  on the selected scale sequence (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection>),
  <math|<sqrt|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>>/s<rsub|i>\<to\>0>.

  This pointwise-in-scale fact also gives a uniform allowance for all
  previous scales. If <math|u<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> and <math|log
  u<rsub|i>=o<around|(|s<rsub|i>|)>> for an increasing
  <math|s<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>, set <math|\<alpha\><rsub|j>=min
  <around|(|1,u<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,u<rsub|j>|)>>. Then
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>1>, <math|\<alpha\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|i>>
  for <math|i\<leqslant\>j>, and <math|log
  \<alpha\><rsub|j>=o<around|(|s<rsub|j>|)>>. Indeed, for any
  <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, eventually <math|u<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>e<rsup|-\<epsilon\>*s<rsub|i>>\<geqslant\>e<rsup|-\<epsilon\>*s<rsub|j>>>
  for <math|i\<leqslant\>j> in the tail. The positive minimum of the finite
  initial segment is also eventually at least
  <math|e<rsup|-\<epsilon\>*s<rsub|j>>>. Hence
  <math|e<rsup|-\<epsilon\>*s<rsub|j>>\<leqslant\>\<alpha\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>1>.
  Apply this to the grouping factors. Arbitrarily many intermediate scales
  thus require only one subpower allowance for the fixed cluster arity.

  <subsubsection|The two output prices>

  Take sequences of levels <math|k,m,j> of logarithmic rates <math|b,a,c>
  relative to <math|t<rsub|n>\<to\>\<infty\>>, with
  <math|0\<less\>b\<less\>a\<less\>c>. Here <math|k> sets the centered test
  scale, <math|m> the matching scale, and <math|j> the insertion grid. All
  level sequences tend to infinity. Let the field degrees be
  <math|d<rsub|r>>, put <math|w=<big|sum><rsub|r>d<rsub|r>>, and define

  <\equation*>
    \<tau\>=c*w+4*h*<around|(|c-a|)>.
  </equation*>

  The calibrated single-field weight has the form
  <math|p<rsub|r><around|(|j|)>=a<rsub|j><rsup|d<rsub|r>>*\<cal-W\><rsub|r><around|(|j|)>>,
  with <math|log \<cal-W\><rsub|r><around|(|j|)>=o<around|(|s<rsub|j>|)>>.
  All factors in <eqref|eq:ope-initial-weight> other than the displayed mesh
  powers have zero logarithmic rate. The gentle schedule gives rate <math|a>
  also at <math|m+1>. Consequently <math|X> has rate <math|-\<tau\>>.

  Set

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-terminal-price>E=4*<around|(|1+\<zeta\><rsub|m>|)>*<around|(|2*P|)>,<space|2em>V=2*E,<space|2em>z=exp
    <around*|(|<around|(|<around|(|q+<tfrac|1|16>|)>*<around|(|a-b|)>-\<tau\>|)>*t<rsub|n>|)>.
  </equation>

  Equations <eqref|eq:ope-terminal-coefficient> and
  <eqref|eq:ope-terminal-price> give rate <math|\<tau\>-q*a> for both
  <math|E,V>. Let <math|K<around|(|k|)>\<geqslant\>k> be the chosen opened
  level, with <math|K<around|(|k|)>\<leqslant\>m>, and put
  <math|k<rsup|\<circ\>>=K<around|(|k|)>> and
  <math|u=a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|k<rsup|\<circ\>>>>. The opening condition is
  <math|B<rsub|k<rsup|\<circ\>>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|k<rsup|\<circ\>>>>,
  with <math|B<rsub|k<rsup|\<circ\>>>> the purchased covering radius, as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:reference-match>. The opening is a separate
  requirement from the room condition. The fact that <math|k<rsup|\<circ\>>>
  has logarithmic rate <math|b> is checked below. The final comparison of the
  local coefficient with the normalized reference fields has the following
  quantitative form. Provided its positive matching allowance <math|z>
  satisfies

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-M\>*<around|(|z;k<rsup|\<circ\>>,m|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|m>*X/<around|(|16*\<cal-B\><rsub|m>|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:ope-match-pay-one>>>|<row|<cell|32*<around|(|1+\<zeta\><rsub|m>|)>*z*<space|0.17em>a<rsub|k<rsup|\<circ\>>><rsup|-q>*E/G*<around|(|m,k<rsup|\<circ\>>|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<vartheta\><rsub|q><around|(|k<rsup|\<circ\>>|)>/2,<eq-number><label|eq:ope-match-pay-two>>>>>
  </align>

  it produces a reference label <math|Q<rsub|N,p>> with quota at most
  <math|V> and centered discrepancy price at most

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-cutoff-discrepancy>A=2*\<cal-S\><around|(|k|)>/z.
  </equation>

  For levels <math|\<ell\>\<leqslant\>m> write
  <math|G*<around|(|m,\<ell\>|)>=exp <around|(|<around|(|s<rsub|m>-s<rsub|\<ell\>>|)>/8|)>>.
  The allowance <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|q>> is positive with zero logarithmic
  rate, and <math|\<cal-M\>> is transported from the opened level
  <math|k<rsup|\<circ\>>> to <math|m>. Its bound is

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-M\>*<around|(|z;k<rsup|\<circ\>>,m|)>\<leqslant\>z*<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k<rsup|\<circ\>>>|)><rsup|q+1><frac|\<vartheta\><rsub|q><around|(|m|)>|\<vartheta\><rsub|q><around|(|k<rsup|\<circ\>>|)>>.
  </equation*>

  The opening retains rate <math|b>, as checked below. Precisely,
  <math|\<cal-M\>*<around|(|z;k<rsup|\<circ\>>,m|)>> is the mark weight at
  scale <math|m> obtained from the opening weight <math|z> at
  <math|k<rsup|\<circ\>>> by the scheduled one-step reduction factors (the
  evolution <math|b<rsub|i+1>=\<eta\><rsub|i>*b<rsub|i>> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:reference-cone>); the displayed bound for it
  is a property of that schedule.

  The comparison producing <eqref|eq:ope-cutoff-discrepancy> is the matched
  construction of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:reference-match>. That
  section uses three depths: a centered depth <math|J> (the replicated cells
  and the root test), an opening depth <math|m> (start of the weight
  evolution) and a matching depth <math|k>. Here they are, respectively, the
  centered level <math|k>, its opened level <math|K<around|(|k|)>>, and
  <math|m>. The centered and opened levels are different in general: equal
  logarithmic rates do not identify them. The quantities correspond as
  follows.

  <\padded-center>
    <tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-halign|l>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-lborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-halign|l>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-rborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:reference-match>>|<cell|this
    section>>|<row|<cell|centered <math|J>, opening <math|m>, matching
    <math|k>>|<cell|<math|k>, <math|K<around|(|k|)>>,
    <math|m>>>|<row|<cell|degree <math|d>>|<cell|<math|q>>>|<row|<cell|coefficient
    field <math|Q> with bound <math|c>>|<cell|terminal projection,
    <math|c=2*P>>>|<row|<cell|column allowance
    <math|C<rsub|k>>>|<cell|<math|\<zeta\><rsub|m>>>>|<row|<cell|<math|E\<geqslant\>4*<around|(|1+C<rsub|k>|)>*c>>|<cell|<math|E=4*<around|(|1+\<zeta\><rsub|m>|)>*<around|(|2*P|)>>>>|<row|<cell|opening
    weight <math|w>>|<cell|<math|z>>>|<row|<cell|gain
    <math|G=e<rsup|<around|(|s<rsub|k>-s<rsub|m>|)>/8>>>|<cell|<math|G*<around|(|m,k<rsup|\<circ\>>|)>>>>|<row|<cell|single-source
    allowance <math|\<theta\><rsub|m>>>|<cell|<math|\<vartheta\><rsub|q><around|(|k<rsup|\<circ\>>|)>>>>|<row|<cell|cell
    price <math|S<rsub|0>=exp <around|(|<around|(|a<rsub|J>/a<rsub|m>|)><rsup|4>*t<rsub|m>+5|)>>>|<cell|<math|\<cal-S\><around|(|k|)>=exp
    <around|(|u<rsup|4>*t<rsub|K<around|(|k|)>>+5|)>>>>|<row|<cell|bare label
    <math|b>>|<cell|<math|b>>>|<row|<cell|quota
    <math|\<mu\>*<around|(|I<rsub|k>*b|)>\<leqslant\>2*E>; price
    <math|2*S<rsub|0>/w>>|<cell|quota of <math|Q<rsub|N,p>> at <math|m>
    <math|\<leqslant\>V>; <math|A>>>>>>
  </padded-center>

  The label returned here is not the bare label <math|b> itself but its image
  <math|Q<rsub|N,p>=T<rsub|N,p>*b> under the calibrated terminal
  identification <math|T<rsub|N,p>> of the cutoff <math|<around|(|N,p|)>>,
  the reference label whose observable is <math|\<cal-O\><around|(|b|)>>; its
  quota at the matching level is by definition
  <math|\<mu\>*<around|(|I<rsub|m>*T<rsub|N,p><rsup|-1>*Q<rsub|N,p>|)>=\<mu\>*<around|(|I<rsub|m>*b|)>>.
  Since <math|T<rsub|N,p>> depends on the cutoff, the compactness argument
  below is applied to the labels <math|Q<rsub|N,p>>, not to <math|b>. Under
  this dictionary <eqref|eq:reference-match-afford> is literally
  <eqref|eq:ope-match-pay-two>. The coefficient field is the terminal
  projection of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-local-projection> with
  grouped weight <math|X> and projection allowance <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|m>>.
  Its coefficient-size and remainder are each at most
  <math|a<rsub|m+1><rsup|q>*<space|0.17em>4*\<cal-B\><rsub|m>/X=P>, by
  <eqref|eq:ope-terminal-projection-price>, so <math|c=2*P>. Condition
  <eqref|eq:ope-match-pay-one> says that the weight transported to the
  matching scale does not exceed the outgoing weight
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|m>*I<rsub|v>> permitted there after that projection,
  where <math|I<rsub|v>=X/<around|(|16*\<cal-B\><rsub|m>|)>>. The two
  conclusions of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:reference-match>, the quota
  bound <math|\<mu\>*<around|(|I<rsub|k>*b|)>\<leqslant\>2*E> and the
  centered root bound <math|2*S<rsub|0>/w> for the difference, are then the
  quota bound <math|V=2*E> and the price <eqref|eq:ope-cutoff-discrepancy>.
  This comparison is conditional on the analytic hypotheses listed at the
  start of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:reference-match>: the signed
  transitions of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:reference-difference>, the
  one-scale marked transport of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-transfer>,
  the complete-moment estimate, cell separation and chamber locality, and the
  terminal reference identification, all on the same history. Those
  hypotheses remain inputs here; the displayed inequalities are exactly the
  budget they need.

  Both payment conditions hold eventually by a strict comparison of rates.
  The right side of <eqref|eq:ope-match-pay-one> has rate <math|-\<tau\>>,
  whereas the upper bound for its left side has rate

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|q+<tfrac|1|16>|)>*<around|(|a-b|)>-\<tau\>-<around|(|q+1|)>*<around|(|a-b|)>=-\<tau\>-<tfrac|15|16><around|(|a-b|)>\<less\>-\<tau\>.
  </equation*>

  The left side of <eqref|eq:ope-match-pay-two> has rate

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|q+<tfrac|1|16>|)>*<around|(|a-b|)>-\<tau\>+q*b+<around|(|\<tau\>-q*a|)>-<tfrac|1|8><around|(|a-b|)>=-<tfrac|1|16><around|(|a-b|)>\<less\>0,
  </equation*>

  while its positive right side has rate zero. If positive sequences have
  unequal logarithmic rates, division and taking logarithms shows that the
  one with the smaller rate is eventually smaller. This justifies both
  comparisons, including all their fixed constants. Since <math|\<cal-S\>>
  has upper rate zero, <eqref|eq:ope-cutoff-discrepancy> has upper rate
  <math|\<tau\>-<around|(|q+1/16|)>*<around|(|a-b|)>>, as required in
  Input<nbsp><reference|input:ope-match>.

  Here is the opening check. The ratio <math|u=a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|K<around|(|k|)>>>
  is at least one. Write <math|V<rsup|<math-up|vac>><rsub|K<around|(|k|)>>\<geqslant\>1>
  for the positive vacuum budget at the opened level, distinct from its
  covering radius <math|B<rsub|K<around|(|k|)>>>. The schedule ensures, for
  every <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>,

  <\equation*>
    u<rsup|4>*V<rsup|<math-up|vac>><rsub|K<around|(|k|)>>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>*s<rsub|K<around|(|k|)>><space|1em><text|eventually
    in >k.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|s<rsub|K<around|(|k|)>>-s<rsub|k>=log
  u\<leqslant\>u\<leqslant\>u<rsup|4>*V<rsup|<math-up|vac>><rsub|K<around|(|k|)>>>,
  we have <math|s<rsub|k>/s<rsub|K<around|(|k|)>>\<to\>1>. Opening a sequence
  of rate <math|b> therefore retains that rate. The actual centered factor is

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-S\><around|(|k|)>=exp <around*|(|u<rsup|4>*t<rsub|K<around|(|k|)>>+5|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|t<rsub|K<around|(|k|)>>\<leqslant\>V<rsup|<math-up|vac>><rsub|K<around|(|k|)>>>
  eventually. Its logarithm divided by <math|t<rsub|n>> is at most
  <math|\<epsilon\>*s<rsub|K<around|(|k|)>>/t<rsub|n>+5/t<rsub|n>>. Taking
  the upper limit and then <math|\<epsilon\>*\<downarrow\>*0> proves its
  upper rate zero.

  <subsubsection|Keeping the match in the continuum limit>

  For fixed intermediate levels and a fixed cluster on the <math|j>-grid,
  require

  <\equation*>
    8*max<rsub|r><around|\||x<rsub|r>|\|>\<less\>a<rsub|m+1>,<space|2em><around|\||x<rsub|r>-x<rsub|s>|\|>\<gtr\>2*a<rsub|j>*B<rsub|j><rsup|2>*<space|1em><around|(|r\<ne\>s|)>.
  </equation*>

  Cross-cell separation persists during every intervening descent. The
  initial room condition is <math|<around|(|B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>+1|)>*a<rsub|m+1>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|k>>,
  which follows eventually from subpower <math|B<rsub|m+1>> and
  <math|a\<gtr\>b>. At an intermediate level <math|i\<geqslant\>m+1>, put
  <math|L=a<rsub|m+1>/a<rsub|i>>. Distinct parent cell centers at level
  <math|m+1> have lattice separation at least
  <math|a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|m+1>\<geqslant\>B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>+1>. Their
  embeddings at level <math|i> therefore have separation at least
  <math|L*<around|(|B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>+1|)>>. The children each deviate from
  their embedded parent by less than <math|L/2>. The triangle inequality
  leaves separation greater than <math|L*B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>>. The purchased
  support propagation bound <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>L*B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>>
  then gives the required separation at level <math|i>. This propagation
  bound is one of the local RG inputs retained above. The argument applies
  simultaneously to every pair of marks belonging to different copied cells.

  For sufficiently large periods there is no wrapping in this finite
  configuration. Its points have the required periodic separation and have
  common parent zero at level <math|m+1>: each is inside half the parent
  block by the first bound and the maximum-coordinate estimate. For
  sufficiently fine cutoff <math|N>, the fixed stencils of the cluster fields
  and of every degree-<math|q> field fit in the centered positive chamber.
  The cutoff comparison can consequently be applied on the ordered tail,
  first in period and then in <math|N>.

  Choose its labels <math|Q<rsub|N,p>> on that tail and set them to zero
  elsewhere. Their quotas are bounded by the same <math|V>. Equation
  <eqref|eq:ope-whole-distance> bounds their reference norms by a constant
  depending on the fixed matching level, so finite-dimensional compactness
  gives a convergent family on a finer nontrivial filter. Every eventual
  discrepancy and quota bound persists on that same filter.
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-phase-prices> converts the discrepancy
  bound to all character phases, and Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-filtration>
  gives the physical low-degree distances and the whole-label bound. These
  are all statements about the same limiting label <math|Q>.

  Finally the geometric requirements hold for the grid clusters of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-grid>. If their scale is <math|x> with
  <math|a\<less\>x\<less\>c>, their radius has rate <math|-x\<less\>-a>, so
  they fit strictly inside the <math|m+1> block. Their pairwise separation is
  eventually larger than <math|e<rsup|-<around|(|x+\<epsilon\>|)>*t<rsub|n>>>,
  while <math|a<rsub|j>*B<rsub|j><rsup|2>> has rate <math|-c> because
  <math|B<rsub|j>> is subpower. Choosing <math|x+\<epsilon\>\<less\>c> proves
  the separation condition. The fixed support-radius room at the centered
  scale is also automatic: a fixed radius plus a subpower truncation radius,
  multiplied by <math|a<rsub|m>>, has rate <math|-a\<less\>-b>. It therefore
  fits inside the centered scale <math|a<rsub|k>>. This completes the
  accounting and extraction of the local matching input, conditional on the
  explicit analytic representation and comparison estimates above.

  <subsubsection|Assembly of the local matching input>

  We now record precisely how the three preceding constructions combine into
  Input<nbsp><reference|input:ope-match> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-grid>. The point is that one label
  <math|Q<rsub|n>> carries all the required bounds; no separate choice is
  made for a phase, a spectator list or a low degree. The letter <math|m>
  denotes the matching level throughout this section, so we write <math|e+1>
  for the low degree called <math|m> in <eqref|eq:ope-match-distance>.

  <\proposition>
    <label|prop:ope-match-assembly>Assume, on one selected history and one
    ordered cutoff filter, the analytic inputs used above: the marked descent
    and regrouping estimates with the weight rule <eqref|eq:ope-group-weight>
    and the support propagation bound; the terminal local projection of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-local-projection>; the comparison
    hypotheses of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:reference-match>; the
    schedule properties used in this section; the centered estimates and
    separated-cluster limit used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-phase-prices>,
    together with the unreflected conjugation identity
    <eqref|eq:episode-conjugate> and the continuity of the correlations in
    every character phase (Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-caps>) used
    there; and the one-step triangular, calibrated column and local
    comparison and projection inputs of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-filtration>.
    Then Input<nbsp><reference|input:ope-match> holds. In it the cluster
    arity is <math|<around|\||I|\|>=h>, the mesh rate <math|d> of the input
    is the rate <math|c> of the insertion grid here, and its rates <math|a,b>
    are those of the matching and centered levels. Thus
    <math|\<tau\>*<around|(|p;a,d|)>> there equals
    <math|\<tau\>=c*w+4*h*<around|(|c-a|)>> here.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Fix <math|0\<less\>b\<less\>a\<less\>x\<less\>d>, a nonempty label list
    <math|p> of arity <math|h>, an integer <math|q\<geqslant\>0>, and a
    coordinate-generic sequence <math|X<rsub|n>> at scale <math|x> on meshes
    <math|a<rsub|j<rsub|n>>> with <math|s<rsub|j<rsub|n>>/t<rsub|n>\<to\>d>.
    Choose levels <math|k<rsub|n>,m<rsub|n>> with
    <math|s<rsub|k<rsub|n>>/t<rsub|n>\<to\>b> and
    <math|s<rsub|m<rsub|n>>/t<rsub|n>\<to\>a>; they exist by the
    level-selection argument at the start of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-grid>. Put <math|c=d>. The preceding
    subsection shows that the geometric requirements hold for
    <math|X<rsub|n>> eventually, since <math|a\<less\>x\<less\>c>.

    For each such <math|n>, the cutoff comparison on the ordered tail gives
    finite-cutoff labels <math|Q<rsub|N,p>> with quota at most
    <math|V<rsub|n>=V> and centered discrepancy price at most
    <math|A<rsub|n>=A>. These have upper rates <math|\<tau\>-q*a> and
    <math|\<tau\>-<around|(|q+<tfrac|1|16>|)>*<around|(|a-b|)>>, by the rate
    computations above. Extract, as in the preceding subsection, a limit
    <math|Q<rsub|n>> on a finer nontrivial filter. Each of the following
    assertions concerns this one limit.

    First, the final paragraph of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-phase-prices>,
    applied with centered level <math|k<rsub|n>> of rate <math|b\<gtr\>0> and
    <math|\<gamma\>=\<tau\>-<around|(|q+<tfrac|1|16>|)>*<around|(|a-b|)>>,
    bounds the complete discrepancy between the cluster product and
    <math|Q<rsub|n>> at the origin. The bound has upper rate <math|\<gamma\>>
    in every character phase and in the mixed state, at arbitrary
    fixed-clearance spectator positions, uniformly along any sequence of
    phases. The finite tests of the prescription are finite linear
    combinations of such evaluations, so they obey the same rate.

    Second, apply <eqref|eq:ope-whole-distance> at the matching level
    <math|m<rsub|n>>, with degree <math|q>. The quota of <math|Q<rsub|N,p>>
    at that level is at most <math|V<rsub|n>> eventually, and the bound
    passes to the limit label. Hence <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|Q<rsub|n>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|n>*V<rsub|n>>
    with <math|J<rsub|n>=J<rsub|m<rsub|n>>*<around|(|a<rsub|U>/a<rsub|m<rsub|n>>|)><rsup|q>>.
    The last paragraph of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-filtration>
    gives <math|J<rsub|n>> upper rate <math|q*a>, because
    <math|J<rsub|m<rsub|n>>> has rate zero and <math|s<rsub|U>> is fixed.
    This is <eqref|eq:ope-match-norm>.

    Third, fix <math|0\<leqslant\>e\<less\>q>.
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-filtration>, applied with <math|d=q>,
    this <math|e> and <math|k=m<rsub|n>>, gives a label <math|y> with
    <math|<around|[|y|]>\<in\>E<rsub|e>> and
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|Q<rsub|n>-y|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>F<rsub|m<rsub|n>,q,e>*V<rsub|n>>,
    by <eqref|eq:ope-physical-distance>. Only <math|y> requires a further
    extraction; <math|Q<rsub|n>> is unchanged. Its factor has upper rate
    <math|<around|(|q-e-1|)>*a>. Setting <math|z<rsub|e+1,n>=y> gives
    <eqref|eq:ope-match-distance> for the low degree <math|e+1> of the Input,
    since <math|q-<around|(|e+1|)>=q-e-1>.

    All three statements hold for the same <math|Q<rsub|n>>, and the rates of
    <math|A<rsub|n>> and <math|V<rsub|n>> are those required. Hence
    Input<nbsp><reference|input:ope-match> holds.
  </proof>

  The proposition does not prove the analytic inputs it lists. In particular
  it does not supply the comparison hypotheses of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:reference-match> or the local comparison and
  projection inputs of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-filtration>. It
  records that no further argument, and no further choice of label, is needed
  to combine them.

  <subsection|From a matched local field to the raw OPE><label|sec:ope-grid>

  The raw comparison in Input<nbsp><reference|input:ope-raw-estimates> is
  obtained in three steps. At a fine lattice configuration the product is
  replaced by a single local field of a sufficiently high degree. The finite
  tests defining the prescription then identify its low-degree coefficients.
  Finally, derivatives with respect to the cluster positions extend the
  estimate from the lattice to every separated configuration. The last step
  uses the filtration: taking a derivative increases the field degree by one.

  We use upper rates as in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-estimates>,
  with any sequence <math|t<rsub|n>\<to\>\<infty\>>. A cluster
  <math|X<rsub|n>=<around|(|x<rsub|i,n>|)><rsub|i\<in\>I>> is at scale
  <math|x\<gtr\>0> if <math|max<rsub|i><around|\||x<rsub|i,n>|\|>> has upper
  rate <math|-x> and, for every <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||x<rsub|i,n>-x<rsub|j,n>|\|>\<gtr\>e<rsup|-<around|(|x+\<delta\>|)>*t<rsub|n>>*<space|1em><around|(|i\<ne\>j|)><space|1em><text|eventually>.
  </equation*>

  These conditions tolerate factors with zero logarithmic rate. In
  particular, they include every cluster of radius <math|M*r<rsub|n>> and
  separation <math|\<eta\>*r<rsub|n>>, where <math|t<rsub|n>=-log r<rsub|n>>.
  Spectators in this section remain a fixed positive distance from the origin
  and one another, although their positions and phases can vary with
  <math|n>.

  <subsubsection|Intermediate scales and generic grids>

  Write <math|a<rsub|k>> for the mesh and <math|s<rsub|k>=log
  <around|(|1/a<rsub|k>|)>> for its logarithmic scale. The following
  elementary consequence of the mesh schedule will be useful. Suppose
  <math|s<rsub|k>> is strictly increasing, tends to infinity, and
  <math|s<rsub|k+1>/s<rsub|k>\<to\>1>. Then for every <math|d\<gtr\>0> and
  every <math|t<rsub|n>\<to\>\<infty\>> there are integers
  <math|j<rsub|n>\<to\>\<infty\>> such that
  <math|s<rsub|j<rsub|n>>/t<rsub|n>\<to\>d>. Indeed choose the first
  <math|j<rsub|n>> with <math|s<rsub|j<rsub|n>>\<gtr\>d*t<rsub|n>>.
  Eventually <math|j<rsub|n>\<gtr\>0>, and

  <\equation*>
    s<rsub|j<rsub|n>-1>\<leqslant\>d*t<rsub|n>\<less\>s<rsub|j<rsub|n>>,<space|2em>1\<less\><frac|s<rsub|j<rsub|n>>|d*t<rsub|n>>\<leqslant\><frac|s<rsub|j<rsub|n>>|s<rsub|j<rsub|n>-1>>\<longrightarrow\>1.
  </equation*>

  The zero-rate variant follows by replacing <math|d*t<rsub|n>> with
  <math|<sqrt|t<rsub|n>>>. The gentle-mesh condition used in the construction
  gives exactly the ratio property, since <math|s<rsub|k+1>-s<rsub|k>=log
  L<rsub|k>=o<around|(|s<rsub|k>|)>>. The proof here uses that condition; its
  production for the chosen trajectory remains part of the construction
  estimates.

  A configuration is <em|coordinate-generic> if, for each of the four
  coordinates separately, the coordinates of its distinct points are pairwise
  different. Such configurations can approximate arbitrary ones at the mesh
  scale with a bound independent of their location. To see this, enumerate
  <math|I> by <math|0,\<ldots\>,N-1>, set <math|K=N+1>, and for mesh
  <math|h\<gtr\>0> put

  <\equation*>
    y<rsub|i,\<mu\>>=h*<around*|(|K<around*|\<lfloor\>|<frac|x<rsub|i,\<mu\>>|h*K>|\<rfloor\>>+i|)>,<space|2em>\<mu\>=1,\<ldots\>,4.
  </equation*>

  Different point indices have different residues modulo <math|K>, so each
  coordinate is injective. The floor inequality and
  <math|0\<leqslant\>i\<less\>K> give <math|<around|\||y<rsub|i,\<mu\>>-x<rsub|i,\<mu\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>h*K>,
  hence

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-grid-round>max<rsub|i><around|\||y<rsub|i>-x<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*K*h.
  </equation>

  This is the empty-exterior version of the grid construction; no claim is
  made that it preserves coordinate distances from fixed exterior points.
  Only ordinary Euclidean separation of the full configuration is needed
  below.

  If <math|h<rsub|n>=e<rsup|-d*t<rsub|n>+o<around|(|t<rsub|n>|)>>> with
  <math|d\<gtr\>x>, the rounded cluster and every configuration in the closed
  ball

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|n>=<around|{|Z:<around|\<\|\|\>|Z-X<rsub|n>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|Y<rsub|n>-X<rsub|n>|\<\|\|\>>|}>
  </equation*>

  remain at scale <math|x>, using the maximum of the Euclidean norms on the
  configuration space. Here is the separation check. Given
  <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>, choose <math|0\<less\>\<epsilon\>\<less\>\<delta\>>
  with <math|x+\<epsilon\>\<less\>d> and use the original separation at
  exponent <math|x+\<epsilon\>>. The displacement has strictly smaller
  exponential order, so eventually twice its norm is at most half that
  separation. Thus the perturbed separation is at least
  <math|<tfrac|1|2>e<rsup|-<around|(|x+\<epsilon\>|)>*t<rsub|n>>\<gtr\>e<rsup|-<around|(|x+\<delta\>|)>*t<rsub|n>>>.
  The radius bound follows from the triangle inequality. Fixed exterior
  clearances also persist, because these clusters tend to the origin.

  <subsubsection|Reading a high-degree match>

  We state the still needed quantitative local matching input precisely. All
  fields and tests in it belong to the same extracted hierarchy and the
  prescription already constructed in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-prescription>.
  For a cluster label list <math|p>, write
  <math|w<around|(|p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i>deg p<rsub|i>> and

  <\equation*>
    \<tau\>*<around|(|p;a,d|)>=d*<space|0.17em>w<around|(|p|)>+4<around|\||I|\|>*<around|(|d-a|)>.
  </equation*>

  The second term is the four-dimensional volume cost of the local matching
  estimate.

  <\assumption>
    <dueto|Local matching estimates><label|input:ope-match> Fix
    <math|0\<less\>b\<less\>a\<less\>x\<less\>d>, a nonempty cluster label
    list <math|p>, a coordinate-generic configuration sequence
    <math|X<rsub|n>> on meshes of logarithmic rate <math|d>, at cluster scale
    <math|x>, and an integer <math|q\<geqslant\>0>. There are degree-<math|q>
    local labels <math|Q<rsub|n>>, and nonnegative budgets
    <math|A<rsub|n>,V<rsub|n>>, with upper rates

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|n>:<space|1em>\<tau\>*<around|(|p;a,d|)>-<around|(|q+<tfrac|1|16>|)>*<around|(|a-b|)>,<space|2em>V<rsub|n>:<space|1em>\<tau\>*<around|(|p;a,d|)>-q*a,
    </equation*>

    with the following properties. The discrepancy between the cluster
    product and <math|Q<rsub|n>> inserted at the origin, tested against any
    fixed spectator label list at fixed positive clearance, has upper rate at
    most that of <math|A<rsub|n>>, uniformly along every choice of phase and
    exterior configuration. The same is true for the mixed state and the
    finite tests of the prescription. The labels obey

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ope-match-norm><around|\<\|\|\>|Q<rsub|n>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|n>*V<rsub|n>,<space|2em>J<rsub|n><text|
      has upper rate >q*a.
    </equation>

    For each <math|1\<leqslant\>m\<leqslant\>q> there are <math|z<rsub|m,n>>
    in the label space of degree <math|q> whose physical classes lie in the
    included space <math|E<rsub|m-1>>, and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ope-match-distance><around|\<\|\|\>|Q<rsub|n>-z<rsub|m,n>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>F<rsub|m,n>*V<rsub|n>,<space|2em>F<rsub|m,n><text|
      has upper rate ><around|(|q-m|)>*a.
    </equation>

    All norms here are fixed finite-dimensional label norms. The original
    labels <math|Q<rsub|n>>, before passing to their physical classes,
    satisfy these bounds. The estimates hold for every fixed cluster label
    list, including lists formed by differentiating its fields.
  </assumption>

  In the conditional construction of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ope-match-assembly>,
  <math|Q<rsub|n>> is a finite-dimensional limit of cutoff matches along a
  nontrivial refinement of the cutoff filter. The discrepancy and quota
  bounds are retained on that same refinement. The individual channel
  estimate below is essential: a bound on the whole degree-<math|q> label
  would lose the powers that distinguish low and high channels.
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ope-match-assembly> derives
  Input<nbsp><reference|input:ope-match> from the cutoff construction of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-cutoff>, the phase transfer of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-phase-prices> and the filtration
  estimates of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-filtration>, with one label
  <math|Q<rsub|n>>. That derivation is conditional on the analytic inputs
  listed in the proposition; the Input is not yet derived unconditionally
  from the finite Wilson law.

  Let <math|f<rsub|\<alpha\>,n>> denote the raw coefficient of the product
  and let <math|v<rsub|\<alpha\>,n>=b<rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|<around|[|Q<rsub|n>|]>|)>>
  be the coefficient of its matched field, where <math|b<rsub|\<alpha\>>> is
  the fixed functional of the prescription and
  <math|m=<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>q>. Then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-match-grade>v<rsub|\<alpha\>,n><text| has upper rate
    >\<tau\>*<around|(|p;a,d|)>-m*a.
  </equation>

  For <math|m=0>, apply the fixed linear functional to
  <eqref|eq:ope-match-norm>. For <math|m\<gtr\>0>, coherence of the
  prescription implies <math|b<rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|<around|[|z<rsub|m,n>|]>|)>=0>:
  this detector kills every field of degree less than <math|m>. Hence

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||v<rsub|\<alpha\>,n>|\|>=<around|\||b<rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|<around|[|Q<rsub|n>-z<rsub|m,n>|]>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsub|\<alpha\>>\<circ\>\<pi\><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>>*<space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|Q<rsub|n>-z<rsub|m,n>|\<\|\|\>>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<pi\><rsub|q>> is the quotient map. Combining
  <eqref|eq:ope-match-distance> with the rate of <math|V<rsub|n>> proves
  <eqref|eq:ope-match-grade>.

  Each raw coefficient is a finite linear combination of exterior tests.
  Applying the discrepancy part of Input<nbsp><reference|input:ope-match> to
  those tests therefore gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-match-detector>f<rsub|\<alpha\>,n>-v<rsub|\<alpha\>,n><text|
    has upper rate >\<tau\>*<around|(|p;a,d|)>-<around|(|q+<tfrac|1|16>|)>*<around|(|a-b|)>.
  </equation>

  For a specified output degree <math|m>, choose <math|q\<geqslant\>m> so
  large that <math|<around|(|q+<tfrac|1|16>|)>*<around|(|a-b|)>\<geqslant\><around|(|m+1|)>*a>.
  Then <eqref|eq:ope-match-grade> and <eqref|eq:ope-match-detector> show that
  <math|f<rsub|\<alpha\>,n>> has upper rate
  <math|\<tau\>*<around|(|p;a,d|)>-m*a>.

  The analogous remainder argument retains the entire product correlation.
  Let <math|\<ell\><rsub|n>> be the functional obtained by inserting a field
  at the origin beside the chosen spectators. Assume, as furnished by the
  separated correlation estimate, that <math|\<ell\><rsub|n><around|(|u|)>>
  has upper rate zero for every fixed field <math|u> and every phase
  sequence. To truncate at <math|D>, choose <math|q\<geqslant\>D> with
  <math|<around|(|q+<tfrac|1|16>|)>*<around|(|a-b|)>\<geqslant\><around|(|D+1|)>*a>.
  Exact reconstruction of <math|<around|[|Q<rsub|n>|]>> by its prescription
  coefficients gives

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|S<rsub|\<chi\><rsub|n>>*<around|(|p,z;X<rsub|n>,Y<rsub|n>|)>-\<ell\><rsub|n>*<around|(|H<rsub|D>*<around|(|X<rsub|n>,p;0|)>|)>>|<cell|=<around*|[|S<rsub|\<chi\><rsub|n>>*<around|(|p,z;X<rsub|n>,Y<rsub|n>|)>-\<ell\><rsub|n><around|(|<around|[|Q<rsub|n>|]>|)>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|1em>+<big|sum><rsub|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>D><around|(|v<rsub|\<alpha\>,n>-f<rsub|\<alpha\>,n>|)>*\<ell\><rsub|n><around|(|u<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|1em>+<big|sum><rsub|D\<less\><around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>q>v<rsub|\<alpha\>,n>*\<ell\><rsub|n><around|(|u<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>.<eq-number><label|eq:ope-match-remainder>>>>>
  </align>

  The first term and each low-channel difference have upper rate at most
  <math|\<tau\>*<around|(|p;a,d|)>-<around|(|D+1|)>*a>. Each high channel has
  that rate as well, by <eqref|eq:ope-match-grade>, because <math|a\<gtr\>0>
  and <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<geqslant\>D+1>. The sums are finite.
  This proves the raw grid remainder estimate with upper rate
  <math|\<tau\>*<around|(|p;a,d|)>-<around|(|D+1|)>*a>. The matching label
  may depend on <math|q>; the raw coefficients and the truncation on the left
  do not.

  <subsubsection|Interpolation by descending through the grading>

  We give the interpolation argument explicitly. Suppose functions
  <math|h<rsub|n>*<around|(|p,X|)>> are indexed by finite field lists of the
  same length, with integer total degree <math|w<around|(|p|)>>. Assume that
  for every scale-<math|x> sequence:

  <\enumerate>
    <item><math|h<rsub|n>*<around|(|p,X<rsub|n>|)>> has a rough upper rate
    <math|x*w<around|(|p|)>+c>;

    <item>on every coordinate-generic grid sequence of logarithmic mesh rate
    <math|d\<gtr\>x>, it has upper rate <math|d*w<around|(|p|)>+e>;

    <item>it is eventually differentiable along each such sequence, with
    identity

    <\equation*>
      \<partial\><rsub|i,\<mu\>>*h<rsub|n>*<around|(|p,X<rsub|n>|)>=h<rsub|n>*<around|(|p<rprime|'>,X<rsub|n>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|p<rprime|'>> is obtained by differentiating the corresponding
    field, so <math|w<around|(|p<rprime|'>|)>=w<around|(|p|)>+1>.
  </enumerate>

  The constants <math|c,e> are independent of <math|p>; they may depend on
  fixed truncation and exterior data. Then at every scale-<math|x> sequence
  the improved upper rate is <math|d*w<around|(|p|)>+e>.

  Choose an integer <math|N\<geqslant\>0> with
  <math|<around|(|d-x|)>*N\<geqslant\>c-e>. We prove by induction on
  <math|k\<geqslant\>0> that the improved rate holds whenever
  <math|N\<leqslant\>w<around|(|p|)>+k>. For <math|k=0> the rough rate is
  already no larger than the improved one. For the induction step round
  <math|X<rsub|n>> to <math|Y<rsub|n>> by <eqref|eq:ope-grid-round>. Every
  choice <math|Z<rsub|n>\<in\>B<rsub|n>> stays at scale <math|x>. The
  induction hypothesis applied to the differentiated lists shows that each
  coordinate derivative at <math|Z<rsub|n>> has upper rate
  <math|d*<around|(|w<around|(|p|)>+1|)>+e>. In the fixed finite-dimensional
  configuration space,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|D*h<rsub|n>*<around|(|p,Z<rsub|n>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i,\<mu\>><around|\||\<partial\><rsub|i,\<mu\>>*h<rsub|n>*<around|(|p,Z<rsub|n>|)>|\|>,
  </equation*>

  so the derivative norm has that rate too.

  These bounds and differentiability hold eventually uniformly on
  <math|B<rsub|n>>. For any specified positive slack, failure would give
  infinitely many indices with a bad point; choosing a bad point there and
  the center elsewhere produces a sequence <math|Z<rsub|n>\<in\>B<rsub|n>>
  contradicting the corresponding eventual assertions. The mean-value
  inequality on the convex ball thus gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||h<rsub|n>*<around|(|p,Y<rsub|n>|)>-h<rsub|n>*<around|(|p,X<rsub|n>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>sup<rsub|Z\<in\>B<rsub|n>><around|\<\|\|\>|D*h<rsub|n>*<around|(|p,Z|)>|\<\|\|\>>*<space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|Y<rsub|n>-X<rsub|n>|\<\|\|\>>.
  </equation*>

  The displacement has upper rate <math|-d>, so the difference has upper rate
  <math|d*w<around|(|p|)>+e>. The grid value has the same rate, which proves
  the induction step. Taking <math|k=N> covers every field list. Only
  finitely many derivative levels are used for any one application, although
  the assumptions must be available at every finite degree.

  For clarity, the mean-value inequality here needs no continuity of the
  derivative. On each segment in <math|B<rsub|n>>, differentiability implies
  continuity, and the scalar mean-value theorem applied to real projections
  of the complex-valued function bounds its increment by the derivative bound
  times the segment length. The finite-dimensional operator estimate above
  follows by expanding a vector in its coordinate basis and applying the
  triangle inequality.

  <subsubsection|Removing the intermediate-scale losses>

  The differentiability identity (3) needed for this interpolation is the
  slot derivative identity <eqref|ex-raw-slot-identity>: one grade-raising
  choice of raw derivative representative <math|\<delta\><rsub|j>*v> for
  every raw label, whose slot identity holds at distinct points with
  arbitrary finite lists of exterior insertions, and, because
  <math|h<rsub|n>> is a phase discrepancy, in every phase table used here,
  with the chosen representatives having the indicated values in that phase.
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-slab-regularity> assumes the same identity
  and does not produce it; it is a named input of the present section. The
  identities persist under finite linear combinations of detector tests and
  under subtraction of the finite raw truncation. At a separated
  configuration its neighborhood remains separated, so the totalized values
  away from that neighborhood play no role in differentiation.

  Suppose the rough separated correlation estimates give upper rate
  <math|x*w<around|(|p|)>> for the cluster with fixed-clearance spectators,
  uniformly in the phase. They give the same rate for each raw coefficient,
  since its detector list is fixed and finite. A finite truncation also has
  rough rate <math|x*w<around|(|p|)>>: its coefficient has that rate and each
  fixed single-field spectator insertion has rate zero. Therefore both the
  raw coefficient and the raw discrepancy satisfy the rough interpolation
  bound with <math|c=0>.

  For a coefficient of degree <math|m>, the grid bound just proved is

  <\equation*>
    d*w<around|(|p|)>+4<around|\||I|\|>*<around|(|d-a|)>-m*a.
  </equation*>

  Use interpolation with <math|e=4<around|\||I|\|>*<around|(|d-a|)>-m*a> to
  obtain this bound at all scale-<math|x> clusters. For a remainder through
  degree <math|D>, use instead <math|e=4<around|\||I|\|>*<around|(|d-a|)>-<around|(|D+1|)>*a>.
  These offsets do not change when a cluster field is differentiated, as
  required by the induction.

  Now set <math|a=x-\<epsilon\>>, <math|d=x+\<epsilon\>>, and <math|b=a/2>,
  with <math|0\<less\>\<epsilon\>\<less\>x>. The mesh schedule supplies all
  three intermediate levels. The resulting coefficient upper rate is

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|w<around|(|p|)>-m|)>*x+\<epsilon\>*<around*|(|w<around|(|p|)>+8\|I\|+m|)>,
  </equation*>

  and the remainder upper rate is

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|w<around|(|p|)>-D-1|)>*x+\<epsilon\>*<around*|(|w<around|(|p|)>+8\|I\|+D+1|)>.
  </equation*>

  Given any desired positive slack above the limiting exponent, choose
  <math|\<epsilon\>> small enough that the displayed excess is less than half
  the slack, then use the remaining half in the upper-rate bound. This proves
  rates <math|<around|(|w<around|(|p|)>-m|)>*x> and
  <math|<around|(|w<around|(|p|)>-D-1|)>*x>, respectively. No uniformity of
  the intermediate constructions as <math|\<epsilon\>\<to\>0> is needed.

  Translating the cluster center to the origin preserves all distances and
  the complete correlation. The raw coefficients use relative positions, so
  the same estimates hold at arbitrary centers. If the cluster is empty, its
  product is the identity. Exact reconstruction of that degree-zero field
  makes every raw truncation exact; positive-degree detectors annihilate it,
  and degree-zero coefficients are constant. Thus this case also satisfies
  the stated rates. Taking <math|x=1> proves
  Input<nbsp><reference|input:ope-raw-estimates>, conditional on
  Input<nbsp><reference|input:ope-match>, the rough separated correlation
  estimates, and the slot derivative identity <eqref|ex-raw-slot-identity>
  with the phase compatibility described above. The remaining task is to
  produce those matching and correlation estimates for the actual Wilson
  trajectory.

  <subsection|Transferring the OPE estimates to homogeneous
  fields><label|sec:ope-estimates>

  The raw prescription and its covariant homogeneous version need not agree
  exactly on a product of fields. Their difference must therefore be
  estimated before the homogeneous coefficients can be used in the expansion.
  We give that transfer, retaining the complete correlations, including all
  unit and disconnected terms. The quantitative estimate for the raw
  prescription remains the construction input identified below.

  Fix cluster fields <math|\<b-v\>=<around|(|v<rsub|i>|)><rsub|i\<in\>I>>,
  with <math|w=<big|sum><rsub|i>deg v<rsub|i>>. For a sequence of positive
  numbers <math|r<rsub|n>\<to\>0>, set <math|t<rsub|n>=-log
  r<rsub|n>\<to\>\<infty\>>. Say that a sequence <math|f<rsub|n>> has upper
  rate <math|a> if for every <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|n>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<around|(|a+\<delta\>|)>*t<rsub|n>>=r<rsub|n><rsup|-a-\<delta\>><space|1em><text|for
    all sufficiently large >n.
  </equation*>

  This notation permits zero values. Finite sums preserve a common upper
  rate, products add rates, and fixed linear maps preserve rates. For sums,
  first use half the desired slack and then absorb the finite number of terms
  into <math|e<rsup|\<delta\>*t<rsub|n>/2>>. The same device absorbs a fixed
  operator norm. Products follow by dividing the slack between the factors.
  In particular any bounded sequence has upper rate zero.

  If <math|\<ell\><rsub|n>:E\<to\>\<bbb-C\>> is a sequence of linear
  functionals on a fixed finite-dimensional space, and
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|n><around|(|e|)>> has upper rate <math|b> for every
  fixed <math|e>, then applying it to vectors <math|u<rsub|n>> of upper rate
  <math|a> gives upper rate <math|a+b>. To prove this, expand
  <math|u<rsub|n>> in a fixed basis. Each coordinate has rate at most
  <math|a>, each basis evaluation has rate at most <math|b>, and the finite
  sum has rate at most <math|a+b>. The identical finite-basis argument works
  for multilinear forms with several varying inputs. We will use it for
  bounded rotated detector labels.

  <\assumption>
    <dueto|Raw product comparison><label|input:ope-raw-estimates> For every
    fixed cluster label list, every <math|M\<geqslant\>0>,
    <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>, every positive sequence <math|r<rsub|n>\<to\>0>
    and configurations with

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||x<rsub|i,n>-o<rsub|n>|\|>\<leqslant\>M*r<rsub|n>,<space|2em><around|\||x<rsub|i,n>-x<rsub|j,n>|\|>\<geqslant\>\<eta\>*r<rsub|n>*<space|1em><around|(|i\<neq\>j|)>,
    </equation*>

    the raw coefficients of <eqref|eq:ope-raw-coefficient> have upper rate
    <math|w-<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>>. For any fixed spectator label list
    <math|\<b-z\>>, any <math|c\<gtr\>0>, any positions satisfying

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||y<rsub|j,n>-o<rsub|n>|\|>\<geqslant\>c,<space|2em><around|\||y<rsub|j,n>-y<rsub|k,n>|\|>\<geqslant\>c*<space|1em><around|(|j\<neq\>k|)>,
    </equation*>

    and any sequence of phases or mixed-state choices <math|\<chi\><rsub|n>>,
    the following assertions hold. For every <math|D>, the raw discrepancy

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ope-raw-discrepancy>S<rsub|\<chi\><rsub|n>>*<around|(|\<b-v\>,\<b-z\>;\<b-x\><rsub|n>,\<b-y\><rsub|n>|)>-\<ell\><rsub|\<chi\><rsub|n>,D,o<rsub|n>,\<b-y\><rsub|n>,\<b-z\>>*<around|(|H<rsub|D>*<around|(|\<b-x\><rsub|n>,\<b-v\>;o<rsub|n>|)>|)>
    </equation>

    has upper rate <math|w-D-1>. For every fixed <math|u\<in\>E<rsub|j>>, the
    separated insertion <math|\<ell\><rsub|\<chi\><rsub|n>,j,o<rsub|n>,\<b-y\><rsub|n>,\<b-z\>><around|(|u|)>>
    has upper rate zero. These statements hold for every finite spectator
    list, including empty lists, and for conjugated cluster fields as well.
    The same underlying hierarchy and physical inclusions are used
    throughout.
  </assumption>

  The input includes uniform control along arbitrarily moving configurations
  and phases at the stated fixed positive exterior clearance. A bound for
  each fixed configuration with a threshold depending on that configuration
  would be insufficient.

  <subsubsection|The averaged readout error>

  Fix a raw truncation degree <math|D> and an output degree <math|m>, which
  may differ. Abbreviate <math|h<rsub|n>=H<rsub|D>*<around|(|\<b-x\><rsub|n>,\<b-v\>;o<rsub|n>|)>>.
  We claim that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-average-bridge>C<rsub|m>*<around|(|\<b-x\><rsub|n>,\<b-v\>;o<rsub|n>|)>-L<rsub|m*D>*h<rsub|n><space|1em><text|has
    upper rate >w-D-1.
  </equation>

  First consider only the rotation average. Let

  <\equation*>
    Z<rsub|m>*<around|(|K;\<b-x\>,\<b-v\>;o|)>=R<rsub|m><around|(|K|)>*H<rsub|m>*<around|(|K<rsup|-1>*\<b-x\>,R<around|(|K<rsup|-1>|)>*\<b-v\>;K<rsup|-1>*o|)>
  </equation*>

  be its integrand. On the common separated region, expanding the finite
  tests gives the exact identity

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|Z<rsub|m>*<around|(|K;\<b-x\>,\<b-v\>;o|)>-Z<rsub|m>*<around|(|K;<around|(|o|)>,<around|(|h|)>;o|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>m><big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>>b<rsub|\<alpha\>,\<lambda\>>*<around*|[|S<rsub|<math-up|mix>>*<around|(|\<b-v\>,R<around|(|K|)>*\<b-w\><rsub|\<lambda\>>;\<b-x\>,o+K*\<b-z\><rsub|\<lambda\>>|)>-S<rsub|<math-up|mix>>*<around|(|h,R<around|(|K|)>*\<b-w\><rsub|\<lambda\>>;o,o+K*\<b-z\><rsub|\<lambda\>>|)>|]>*R<rsub|m><around|(|K|)>*\<iota\><rsub|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>,m>*u<rsub|\<alpha\>>.<eq-number><label|eq:ope-rotated-error>>>>>
  </align>

  Here each sum over <math|\<lambda\>> is restricted to the finite support of
  <math|b<rsub|\<alpha\>>>. To verify the identity, write each raw
  coefficient as its exterior correlation at relative origin. Rotate the
  entire correlation by <math|K> and translate by <math|o>; by the covariance
  and translation laws <eqref|eq:ope-mixed-covariance> neither operation
  changes its value. This is the only place where covariance of the
  correlations enters, and it cannot be omitted: the assumptions on the
  actions <math|R<rsub|n>> and <math|J<rsub|n>> alone do not imply
  <eqref|eq:ope-rotated-error>. The cluster positions and labels become
  <math|\<b-x\>,\<b-v\>>, and the detector positions and labels become
  exactly those displayed. Subtract the same evaluation with the single field
  <math|h> at <math|o>, and use linearity in that slot.

  Take <math|h=h<rsub|n>> and any sequence
  <math|K<rsub|n>\<in\>O<around|(|4|)>>. All the rotated detector positions
  have a common positive separation from <math|o<rsub|n>> and from one
  another: these are the fixed finite nonzero detector distances, preserved
  by rotations. All rotated detector labels and output vectors are bounded,
  by continuity of the representations on the compact group. Apply
  Input<nbsp><reference|input:ope-raw-estimates> to each fixed basis choice
  of detector labels in <eqref|eq:ope-rotated-error>, then use the
  multilinear finite-basis argument above to allow their bounded variation
  with <math|K<rsub|n>>. Every summand and hence their finite sum has upper
  rate <math|w-D-1>.

  This statement for every rotation sequence implies a uniform estimate in
  <math|K> at each sufficiently large <math|n>. Indeed, for a fixed slack, if
  there were a violating rotation at infinitely many indices, choose one at
  each such index and any rotation at the remaining indices. The resulting
  sequence would contradict the rate estimate just proved. Thus the norm of
  the integrand difference is eventually bounded uniformly by
  <math|e<rsup|<around|(|w-D-1+\<delta\>|)>*t<rsub|n>>>. Both integrands are
  continuous on the compact group once the shrinking cluster lies in
  <math|\<cal-U\><rsub|m>>. Integrating against probability Haar measure
  gives the same bound for the difference of their averages.

  Apply the same argument to the conjugated part. Its single comparison field
  is <math|J<rsub|D>*h<rsub|n>>, rather than the raw truncation recomputed
  from the conjugated cluster. This distinction causes no extra assumption.
  By the reality law in <eqref|eq:ope-mixed-covariance>, conjugating a
  complete correlation conjugates all its labels, and the raw discrepancy is
  linear in each spectator slot. Hence the discrepancy with cluster
  <math|J*\<b-v\>>, comparison field <math|J<rsub|D>*h<rsub|n>>, and
  arbitrary spectator labels is the complex conjugate of the original
  discrepancy with conjugated spectator labels. That original discrepancy is
  covered by the input and the bounded-label argument. It has the same norm
  and upper rate. Output conjugation is a fixed real-linear map on a
  finite-dimensional space and preserves the rate. Adding the two averages
  and dividing by two proves <eqref|eq:ope-average-bridge>; translation
  invariance <eqref|eq:ope-mixed-covariance> identifies its centered
  singleton readout with <math|L<rsub|m*D>*h<rsub|n>>. Applying the fixed
  projection <math|K<rsub|m>> yields

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-fresh-bridge>F<rsub|m>*<around|(|\<b-x\><rsub|n>,\<b-v\>;o<rsub|n>|)>-P<rsub|m*D>*h<rsub|n><space|1em><text|has
    upper rate >w-D-1.
  </equation>

  <subsubsection|Coefficient and remainder bounds>

  Set <math|D=m> in <eqref|eq:ope-fresh-bridge>. Applying <math|P<rsub|m*m>>
  to the raw shadow <math|H<rsub|m>> kills every term of degree less than
  <math|m>, by inclusion compatibility and <eqref|eq:ope-higher-kills>. The
  remaining finitely many terms consist of degree-<math|m> raw coefficients,
  of upper rate <math|w-m>, times fixed vectors. Their sum has that rate. The
  difference in <eqref|eq:ope-fresh-bridge> has the smaller rate
  <math|w-m-1>, so <math|F<rsub|m>> has upper rate <math|w-m>. Taking its
  fixed channel coordinates gives the same rate for each coefficient in
  <eqref|eq:ope-final-coefficients>.

  For the remainder, retain the raw truncation <math|h<rsub|n>=H<rsub|D>> and
  abbreviate the spectator functional at degree <math|j> by
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|n,j>>. The exact reconstruction identity in
  <eqref|eq:ope-projection-recursion> and compatibility of spectator
  insertions give

  <\equation*>
    \<ell\><rsub|n,D><around|(|h<rsub|n>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j\<leqslant\>D>\<ell\><rsub|n,j>*<around|(|P<rsub|j*D>*h<rsub|n>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Consequently the final homogeneous discrepancy is exactly the raw
  discrepancy <eqref|eq:ope-raw-discrepancy> plus

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-final-correction><big|sum><rsub|j\<leqslant\>D>\<ell\><rsub|n,j>*<around*|(|P<rsub|j*D>*h<rsub|n>-F<rsub|j>*<around|(|\<b-x\><rsub|n>,\<b-v\>;o<rsub|n>|)>|)>.
  </equation>

  Each vector difference has upper rate <math|w-D-1> by
  <eqref|eq:ope-fresh-bridge>. The insertion functional has rate zero on
  every fixed vector by the input. The finite-dimensional evaluation argument
  therefore gives rate <math|w-D-1> for every term of
  <eqref|eq:ope-final-correction>, and for their finite sum. Adding the raw
  discrepancy proves the same rate for the final discrepancy, along any
  prescribed configuration and phase sequence.

  <\theorem>
    <label|thm:ope-quantitative>Under Input<nbsp><reference|input:ope-raw-estimates>
    and the standing assumptions of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-homogeneous>,
    including the covariance, translation and reality laws
    <eqref|eq:ope-mixed-covariance> of the mixed correlations, the
    coefficients of <eqref|eq:ope-final-coefficients> satisfy the public
    separated OPE bounds. Specifically, for every <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>,
    fixed channel <math|<around|(|m,\<alpha\>|)>> and fixed
    <math|M\<geqslant\>0>, <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>, at all sufficiently small
    <math|r\<gtr\>0>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||c<rsub|\<b-v\>><rsup|m,\<alpha\>>*<around|(|\<b-x\>;o|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>r<rsup|m-w-\<epsilon\>>
    </equation*>

    uniformly over <math|<around|\||x<rsub|i>-o|\|>\<leqslant\>M*r> and
    <math|<around|\||x<rsub|i>-x<rsub|j>|\|>\<geqslant\>\<eta\>*r>. For each
    finite <math|D>, fixed spectator labels and <math|c\<gtr\>0>, one also
    has

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<mid|\|>S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|\<b-v\>,\<b-z\>;\<b-x\>,\<b-y\>|)>-<big|sum><rsub|j\<leqslant\>D><big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>c<rsub|\<b-v\>><rsup|j,\<alpha\>>*<around|(|\<b-x\>;o|)>*S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|e<rsub|j,\<alpha\>>,\<b-z\>;o,\<b-y\>|)><mid|\|>\<leqslant\>r<rsup|D+1-w-\<epsilon\>>,>>>>
    </align*>

    uniformly over the same clusters, all spectators with center and mutual
    clearance at least <math|c>, and all phases and the mixed state.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    The preceding arguments prove these inequalities eventually along every
    admissible sequence, by taking slack <math|\<epsilon\>> in the rate
    definition. If a uniform threshold for the coefficient failed, choose for
    each <math|n> a violating radius <math|0\<less\>r<rsub|n>\<less\>1/n> and
    an admissible cluster at that radius. The resulting sequence contradicts
    its eventual rate bound. If the uniform remainder threshold failed,
    choose in addition a violating spectator configuration and a violating
    phase at each radius. The rate bound was proved for arbitrary such
    choices, so gives the same contradiction. A finite initial segment where
    a cluster might meet a spectator is immaterial: for sufficiently small
    <math|r>, <math|M*r\<less\>c/2> makes the combined configuration
    separated, while the stated lower bounds separate all points within
    either group. The center and spectators are already separated by
    <math|c>.
  </proof>

  The coefficients used in this theorem were chosen before <math|D>, the
  phase and the spectators. The small-radius threshold may depend on the
  cluster and spectator labels, on <math|D>, <math|M>, <math|\<eta\>>,
  <math|c>, <math|\<epsilon\>> and, for a coefficient, on the channel; it
  does not depend on the phase, on the mixed-state choice or on the
  positions. The theorem transfers the quantitative raw comparison; it does
  not produce that comparison from the Wilson measure.

  <section|Free tests and the interacting field quotient>

  <subsection|Free-reference rank from finite point
  probes><label|sec:ex-lattice-trace-free-rank>

  This section proves the finite-dimensional reference-rank facts used in the
  trace calibration. The calculation uses only four-dimensional Euclidean
  Green-function derivatives, the signed coordinate symmetries, and
  simplicity of the compact gauge Lie algebra. The divergence-rank conclusion
  also uses the compatible derivative plan specified below. All spaces below
  are real, even though polynomial coefficients are temporarily complexified
  to encode real observables.

  <subsubsection|Low-grade invariant labels>

  Write <math|\<frak-g\>> for the compact simple real gauge Lie algebra, with
  its invariant inner product, and <math|e<rsub|0>,e<rsub|1>,e<rsub|2>,e<rsub|3>>
  for the coordinate basis of <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>. A grade-<math|d>
  label is a polynomial in all radial jets, with jet order <math|r> having
  engineering weight <math|r+2>. In particular, grades four and five may
  include linear terms in higher jets; those terms are removed below by gauge
  simplicity. After that reduction, their nonlinear terms depend only on the
  curvature <math|F> and, at grade five, its first derivative <math|J>. A
  signed permutation <math|s> acts on a coordinate tensor by permuting its
  indices and multiplying by the product of the corresponding coordinate
  signs. The raw grade-four space consists of symmetric two-index arrays of
  grade-four labels, real under complex conjugation and covariant under this
  signed action. The raw grade-five space consists of one-index arrays of
  grade-five labels with the same reality and covariance conditions.

  The following elementary color fact is used repeatedly. If a real linear
  functional <math|\<ell\>> on <math|\<frak-g\>> is invariant under the
  adjoint action, then differentiating <math|\<ell\><around|(|<math-up|Ad><rsub|exp
  <around|(|t*x|)>><space|0.17em>y|)>=\<ell\><around|(|y|)>> at <math|t=0>
  gives <math|\<ell\><around|(|<around|[|x,y|]>|)>=0>. Simplicity and
  nonabelianity imply <math|<around|[|\<frak-g\>,\<frak-g\>|]>=\<frak-g\>>,
  hence <math|\<ell\>=0>. For an invariant real bilinear form <math|B>,
  define <math|T> by <math|B*<around|(|x,y|)>=<around|\<langle\>|T*x,y|\<rangle\>>>.
  The invariance of <math|B> and of the positive inner product implies
  <math|T*<around|[|a,x|]>=<around|[|a,T*x|]>> for every <math|a,x>.
  Therefore its self-adjoint and skew-adjoint parts
  <math|S=<around|(|T+T<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>/2> and
  <math|A=<around|(|T-T<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>/2> both commute with the adjoint
  action. Every eigenspace of <math|S> is an ideal; the spectral theorem and
  simplicity show <math|S=\<lambda\>*I>. Also <math|-A<rsup|2>> is positive
  self-adjoint and commutes with the adjoint action, so the same argument
  gives <math|-A<rsup|2>=c*I> for some <math|c\<geqslant\>0>. If
  <math|c\<gtr\>0>, then <math|J=A/<sqrt|c>> is a skew-adjoint complex
  structure commuting with every <math|<math-up|ad><rsub|a>>. This implies
  <math|<math-up|ad><rsub|J*x>=J<math-up|ad><rsub|x>>. The right side is
  self-adjoint, because it is a product of commuting skew-adjoint operators,
  whereas the left side is skew-adjoint by invariance of the inner product.
  It must vanish; invertibility of <math|J> would then make every
  <math|<math-up|ad><rsub|x>> vanish, contradicting nonabelianity. Thus
  <math|c=0> and <math|A=0>, so <math|T=\<lambda\>*I>. Every invariant
  bilinear form is consequently a scalar multiple of the inner product.
  Applying this argument to real and imaginary parts covers complex-valued
  coefficients too. Consequently the color dependence of an invariant
  quadratic term is just the inner product. The engineering weights are
  <math|2> for <math|F> and <math|3> for its first derivative <math|J>; a jet
  of order <math|r> has weight <math|r+2>. At grade four or five, a monomial
  can therefore contain at most two factors. The possible terms are a
  constant, one linear functional of each jet of weight at most the grade, a
  symmetric quadratic form in <math|F>, and (only at grade five) a mixed
  bilinear form in <math|<around|(|F,J|)>>. Every linear jet term vanishes by
  the preceding color argument, including the higher-derivative linear terms
  allowed by these two grades. Under simultaneous reversal of all four
  coordinate signs, <math|F> is even while the output vector index and
  <math|J> are odd. Hence constants and quadratic <math|F> terms vanish in a
  covariant vector. Only the mixed term remains in grade five.

  The radial degree-zero and degree-one potentials are represented by the
  following spanning families, for <math|c\<in\>\<frak-g\>>:

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|\<ell\><rsup|0><rsub|i*j>*c|)><rsub|a><around|(|z|)>>|<cell|=<tfrac|1|2><around|(|\<delta\><rsub|a*j>*z<rsub|i>-\<delta\><rsub|a*i>*z<rsub|j>|)>*c,>>|<row|<cell|<around|(|\<ell\><rsup|1><rsub|r*k*l>*c|)><rsub|a><around|(|z|)>>|<cell|=<tfrac|1|3><around|(|\<delta\><rsub|a*l>*z<rsub|k>-\<delta\><rsub|a*k>*z<rsub|l>|)>*z<rsub|r>*c.>>>>
  </align*>

  The first family is antisymmetric in <math|i,j>; the second is
  antisymmetric in <math|k,l> and satisfies

  <\equation*>
    \<ell\><rsup|1><rsub|r*k*l>+\<ell\><rsup|1><rsub|k*l*r>+\<ell\><rsup|1><rsub|l*r*k>=0.
  </equation*>

  This identity follows by adding the three displayed quadratic monomials:
  each term cancels its opposite. The families span the actual radial jet
  spaces: for every degree-zero potential <math|A<rsub|0>> and degree-one
  potential <math|A<rsub|1>>, respectively,

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|0>=<tfrac|1|2><big|sum><rsub|i,j>\<ell\><rsup|0><rsub|i*j><around|(|<math-up|curl><rsub|0><rsup|i*j><space|0.17em>A<rsub|0>|)>,<space|2em>A<rsub|1>=<tfrac|1|2><big|sum><rsub|r,k,l>\<ell\><rsup|1><rsub|r*k*l><around|(|<math-up|curl><rsub|1><rsup|r*k*l><space|0.17em>A<rsub|1>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here a radial jet of index <math|r> is a homogeneous polynomial one-form
  <math|A=<around|(|A<rsub|a>|)>> of polynomial degree <math|r+1>, satisfying
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>z<rsub|i>*A<rsub|i><around|(|z|)>=0>. Its linear
  curl is the two-form <math|F<rsub|i*a><around|(|z|)>=\<partial\><rsub|i>*A<rsub|a><around|(|z|)>-\<partial\><rsub|a>*A<rsub|i><around|(|z|)>>;
  no nonlinear commutator is included in this jet calculation.
  Differentiating the radial identity with respect to <math|z<rsub|a>> and
  using Euler's identity gives

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|i>z<rsub|i>*\<partial\><rsub|a>*A<rsub|i>=-A<rsub|a>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|i>z<rsub|i>*\<partial\><rsub|i>*A<rsub|a>=<around|(|r+1|)>*A<rsub|a>.
  </equation*>

  Subtracting proves the reconstruction formula

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-trace-radial-reconstruction>A<rsub|a><around|(|z|)>=<frac|1|r+2>*<big|sum><rsub|i>z<rsub|i>*F<rsub|i*a><around|(|z|)>.
  </equation>

  In particular curl is injective on this radial homogeneous space: a zero
  curl forces every component of <math|A> to vanish. For <math|A<rsub|0>>,
  write <math|f<rsub|i*j>=\<partial\><rsub|i><around|(|A<rsub|0>|)><rsub|j>-\<partial\><rsub|j><around|(|A<rsub|0>|)><rsub|i>>,
  which is constant. For <math|A<rsub|1>>, write
  <math|j<rsub|r*k*l>=\<partial\><rsub|r>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|k><around|(|A<rsub|1>|)><rsub|l>-\<partial\><rsub|l><around|(|A<rsub|1>|)><rsub|k>|)>>,
  which is also constant. These are derivative values, with no factorial
  rescaling. Homogeneity gives <math|F<rsub|k*l><around|(|z|)>=<big|sum><rsub|r>z<rsub|r>*j<rsub|r*k*l>>
  in the second case. Thus <eqref|eq:ex-trace-radial-reconstruction> reads

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|A<rsub|0>|)><rsub|a><around|(|z|)>=<tfrac|1|2><big|sum><rsub|i>z<rsub|i>*f<rsub|i*a>,<space|2em><around|(|A<rsub|1>|)><rsub|a><around|(|z|)>=<tfrac|1|3><big|sum><rsub|r,k>z<rsub|k>*z<rsub|r>*j<rsub|r*k*a>.
  </equation*>

  Antisymmetry now checks the asserted spanning identities directly:

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<tfrac|1|2><big|sum><rsub|i,j><around|(|\<ell\><rsup|0><rsub|i*j>*f<rsub|i*j>|)><rsub|a><around|(|z|)>>|<cell|=<tfrac|1|4><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i>z<rsub|i>*f<rsub|i*a>-<big|sum><rsub|j>z<rsub|j>*f<rsub|a*j>|)>=<tfrac|1|2><big|sum><rsub|i>z<rsub|i>*f<rsub|i*a>,>>|<row|<cell|<tfrac|1|2><big|sum><rsub|r,k,l><around|(|\<ell\><rsup|1><rsub|r*k*l>*j<rsub|r*k*l>|)><rsub|a><around|(|z|)>>|<cell|=<tfrac|1|6><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|r,k>z<rsub|k>*z<rsub|r>*j<rsub|r*k*a>-<big|sum><rsub|r,l>z<rsub|l>*z<rsub|r>*j<rsub|r*a*l>|)>=<tfrac|1|3><big|sum><rsub|r,k>z<rsub|k>*z<rsub|r>*j<rsub|r*k*a>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Equality as polynomial functions is equality of their symmetric
  coefficients, so these are identities of radial jets. A bilinear form in
  these slots is therefore determined by its values on pairs of legs; the
  legs need not be linearly independent. Signed covariance and pair
  antisymmetry follow from their displayed formulas. Commuting the ordinary
  partial derivatives in the definition of <math|j> gives
  <math|j<rsub|r*k*l>+j<rsub|k*l*r>+j<rsub|l*r*k>=0>, the Bianchi relation
  used below.

  For a grade-five vector label, let <math|M<rsub|p>> denote the mixed
  bilinear term in its low-grade decomposition. Choose a unit color vector
  <math|v> and define

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|p;r;i*j;k*l>=M<rsub|p>*<around|(|\<ell\><rsup|0><rsub|i*j><around|(|v|)>,\<ell\><rsup|1><rsub|r*k*l><around|(|v|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Reality makes these coefficients real. The adjoint-invariant bilinear-form
  calculation shows that this unit color vector records the full color
  coefficient. The leg spanning identities show that the coefficients
  determine every <math|M<rsub|p>>. The cycle relation for
  <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>> gives the Bianchi relation for <math|A>. Hence
  <math|A<rsub|p;r;i*j;k*l>> is antisymmetric in <math|i,j> and in
  <math|k,l>, and obeys

  <\equation>
    A<rsub|p;r;i*j;k*l>=s<rsub|p>*s<rsub|r>*s<rsub|i>*s<rsub|j>*s<rsub|k>*s<rsub|l>*<space|0.17em>A<rsub|s<around|(|p|)>;s<around|(|r|)>;s<around|(|i|)>*s<around|(|j|)>;s<around|(|k|)>*s<around|(|l|)>><label|eq:ex-trace-signed-array>
  </equation>

  for every signed permutation, where the signs
  <math|s<rsub|a>\<in\><around|{|\<pm\>1|}>> are those of the coordinate
  reflection. The finite signed-coordinate orbit count in dimension four has
  the following explicit form: if a coefficient satisfying (1) is nonzero,
  then its two antisymmetric pairs have distinct entries, and either (i)
  <math|p=r> and the two pairs are parallel, or (ii) <math|p\<ne\>r> and
  there is a third coordinate <math|u>, distinct from <math|p,r>, such that
  the two pairs are <math|<around|(|p,u|)>,<around|(|r,u|)>> in either order.
  Permuting coordinates and reversing an antisymmetric pair reduces these
  cases to the four rows

  <\equation>
    <around|(|p,r;i*j;k*l|)>=<around|(|0,0;01;01|)>,<around|(|0,0;12;12|)>,<around|(|0,2;01;21|)>,<around|(|0,2;21;01|)>.<label|eq:ex-trace-orbit-representatives>
  </equation>

  To check the count directly, a reflection in coordinate <math|a> multiplies
  the coefficient by <math|-1> exactly when <math|a> belongs to an odd number
  of the six indices <math|p,r,i,j,k,l>; a nonzero entry requires even parity
  at every coordinate. If <math|p=r>, this forces the supports of the two
  antisymmetric pairs to agree, giving case (i). If <math|p\<ne\>r>, parity
  forces the pairs to share exactly one coordinate, distinct from both
  <math|p,r>, giving case (ii). Coordinate permutations put the resulting
  supports in the forms in (2), and antisymmetry fixes the order signs. This
  proves the stated orbit count.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Three probes determine every covariant vector
    label><label|lem:ex-trace-vector-probes>Let <math|A<rsub|p;r;i*j;k*l>> be
    a real array satisfying the antisymmetries, Bianchi relation, and signed
    covariance above. If

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|0;0;01;01>=A<rsub|0;2;01;21>=A<rsub|0;2;21;01>=0,
    </equation*>

    then every coefficient of <math|A> vanishes.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    By the orbit count, it suffices to check the four rows in
    <eqref|eq:ex-trace-orbit-representatives>. Three are the displayed
    hypotheses. For the remaining row, use Bianchi in the differentiated pair
    with derivative index <math|0> and pair <math|12>:

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|0;0;12;12>+A<rsub|0;1;12;20>+A<rsub|0;2;12;01>=0.
    </equation*>

    The third term is minus the third displayed probe by reversing the first
    pair <math|12> to <math|21>. Interchanging <math|1> and <math|2> carries
    the second term to the same third probe, up to the sign from reversing
    its first or differentiated pair. Both terms therefore vanish, so
    <math|A<rsub|0;0;12;12>=0>. Thus all four representatives vanish; signed
    covariance and antisymmetry propagate zero to every entry.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The raw spaces have dimensions at most four and three>

  For a raw grade-four tensor <math|Q=<around|(|Q<rsub|a*b>|)>>, choose a
  unit color vector <math|v>. The four real linear probes are: the real
  constant coefficient of <math|Q<rsub|00>>; and the coefficients of
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|01>,F<rsub|01>|\<rangle\>>> and
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|12>,F<rsub|12>|\<rangle\>>> in
  <math|Q<rsub|00>> and <math|<around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|01>,F<rsub|21>|\<rangle\>>>
  in <math|Q<rsub|02>>. If all four vanish, the constant vanishes.
  Polarization in the two <math|F> slots extracts the symmetric quadratic
  coefficient. For the quadratic coefficient array, signed parity says that
  if <math|a=b>, the two antisymmetric field pairs have the same support.
  Relative to the fixed output axis <math|a>, their common support either
  contains <math|a> or avoids <math|a>; coordinate permutations fixing
  <math|a> reduce these to the probes <math|Q<rsub|00>> on
  <math|<around|(|01,01|)>> and <math|<around|(|12,12|)>>. If
  <math|a\<ne\>b>, the two field-pair supports have symmetric difference
  <math|<around|{|a,b|}>>, so they share a third coordinate distinct from
  <math|a,b>. Coordinate permutation reduces this to <math|Q<rsub|02>> on
  <math|<around|(|01,21|)>>. Pair reversals change only signs; exchanging the
  output indices gives the same coefficient by
  <math|Q<rsub|a*b>=Q<rsub|b*a>>. These are exactly the three quadratic
  probes listed above. The invariant-color argument makes vanishing on the
  chosen unit color vector sufficient: every color coefficient is its inner
  product multiple. The linear term is zero by color simplicity. Thus these
  four probes are injective, and

  <\equation*>
    dim \<cal-R\><rsub|4>\<leqslant\>4,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|4>> denotes the raw symmetric grade-four table
  space.

  For a raw grade-five vector <math|Q=<around|(|Q<rsub|p>|)>>, polarize the
  mixed term by evaluating the grade-zero and grade-one slots at
  <math|<around|(|F,J|)>>, <math|<around|(|F,0|)>>, <math|<around|(|0,J|)>>,
  and <math|<around|(|0,0|)>> and taking the alternating sum. This cancels
  the constant and pure-degree terms and leaves exactly the mixed bilinear
  coefficient. The three probes are the three entries in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-trace-vector-probes>. The Bianchi identity
  supplies the fourth orbit representative, while the color argument and the
  all-coordinate reflection remove the other grades as shown above. Thus the
  probe map is injective into <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|3>> and

  <\equation*>
    dim \<cal-R\><rsub|5>\<leqslant\>3.
  </equation*>

  This proves the two raw-dimension bounds without assuming a tensor
  classification beyond the finite parity count just given.

  <subsubsection|The normalized free stress basis>

  Let <math|\<nu\>=<around|(|4*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>> and
  <math|d<rsub|G>=dim \<frak-g\>\<gtr\>0>. The normalized four-dimensional
  massless Green function is <math|G<around|(|x|)>=\<nu\><around|\||x|\|><rsup|-2>>.
  At the unit vector <math|e<rsub|0>>, direct differentiation gives

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<partial\><rsub|i>*\<partial\><rsub|j>*G<around|(|e<rsub|0>|)>>|<cell|=-2*\<nu\>*\<delta\><rsub|i*j>+8*\<nu\>*\<delta\><rsub|i*0>*\<delta\><rsub|j*0>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-trace-green-hessian>>>|<row|<cell|\<partial\><rsub|k>*\<partial\><rsub|i>*\<partial\><rsub|j>*G<around|(|e<rsub|0>|)>>|<cell|=8*\<nu\>*<around|(|\<delta\><rsub|i*j>*\<delta\><rsub|k*0>+\<delta\><rsub|k*i>*\<delta\><rsub|j*0>+\<delta\><rsub|i*0>*\<delta\><rsub|k*j>|)>-48*\<nu\>*\<delta\><rsub|i*0>*\<delta\><rsub|j*0>*\<delta\><rsub|k*0>.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-trace-green-third>>>>>
  </align>

  For example, differentiating <math|G> once gives
  <math|\<partial\><rsub|i>*G<around|(|x|)>=-2*\<nu\>*x<rsub|i><around|\||x|\|><rsup|-4>>.
  Differentiating again gives

  <\equation*>
    -2*\<nu\>*\<delta\><rsub|i*j>*<around|\||x*<around|\||<rsup|-4>+8*\<nu\>*x<rsub|i>*x<rsub|j>|\|>*x|\|><rsup|-6>.
  </equation*>

  One more derivative and <math|x=e<rsub|0>> give the displayed formulas.

  For antisymmetric index pairs define the free curl kernel

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|a*b;k*m><around|(|x|)>=\<delta\><rsub|k*b>*\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<partial\><rsub|m>*G<around|(|x|)>-\<delta\><rsub|m*b>*\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<partial\><rsub|k>*G<around|(|x|)>-\<delta\><rsub|k*a>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*\<partial\><rsub|m>*G<around|(|x|)>+\<delta\><rsub|m*a>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*\<partial\><rsub|k>*G<around|(|x|)>,
  </equation*>

  and the two-contraction chain

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-C\><rsub|a*b,c*d;i*j,k*l><around|(|x|)>=C<rsub|a*b;i*j><around|(|x|)>*C<rsub|c*d;k*l><around|(|x|)>+C<rsub|a*b;k*l><around|(|x|)>*C<rsub|c*d;i*j><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  The Wick pairing of two separated point insertions with coefficient vector
  <math|w=<around|(|w<rsub|0>,w<rsub|1>,w<rsub|2>,w<rsub|3>|)>> is
  <math|d<rsub|G>> times

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-D\><rsub|w><rsup|a*b><around|(|H|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|w<rsub|1>*<big|sum><rsub|i>H<rsub|a*i,a*i>+w<rsub|2>*<big|sum><rsub|i,j>H<rsub|i*j,i*j>,>|<cell|a=b,>>|<row|<cell|w<rsub|3>*<big|sum><rsub|i>H<rsub|a*i,b*i>,>|<cell|a\<ne\>b,>>>>>
  </equation*>

  For fixed field pairs <math|<around|(|i*j,k*l|)>> set
  <math|H<rsub|p*q*r*s><around|(|x|)>=C<rsub|p*q;i*j><around|(|x|)>*C<rsub|r*s;k*l><around|(|x|)>+C<rsub|p*q;k*l><around|(|x|)>*C<rsub|r*s;i*j><around|(|x|)>>.
  The probe is <math|S<rsub|w>*<around|(|i*j,k*l;a*b;x|)>=d<rsub|G>*\<cal-D\><rsub|w><rsup|a*b><around|(|H<around|(|x|)>|)>>;
  the vacuum contribution to a one-point <math|00> entry is <math|w<rsub|0>>.
  Substitution of <eqref|eq:ex-trace-green-hessian> into the curl factors
  gives

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|S<rsub|w>*<around|(|01,01;00;e<rsub|0>|)>>|<cell|=d<rsub|G>*\<nu\><rsup|2>*<around|(|32*w<rsub|1>+64*w<rsub|2>|)>,<eq-number>>>|<row|<cell|S<rsub|w>*<around|(|12,12;00;e<rsub|0>|)>>|<cell|=d<rsub|G>*\<nu\><rsup|2>*<around|(|64*w<rsub|2>|)>,<eq-number>>>|<row|<cell|S<rsub|w>*<around|(|02,12;01;e<rsub|0>|)>>|<cell|=d<rsub|G>*\<nu\><rsup|2>*<around|(|-16*w<rsub|3>|)>.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-trace-vacuum-probes>>>>>
  </align>

  After factoring out <math|d<rsub|G>*\<nu\><rsup|2>>, the coefficient rows
  for these three probes, in columns <math|<around|(|w<rsub|1>,w<rsub|2>,w<rsub|3>|)>>,
  are

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|32,64,0|)>,<space|2em><around|(|0,64,0|)>,<space|2em><around|(|0,0,-16|)>.
  </equation*>

  These finite sums are evaluated by inserting the displayed Kronecker-delta
  Hessian into <math|C> and then into <math|H> and <math|\<cal-D\><rsub|w>>.
  The second equation recovers <math|w<rsub|2>>, the first then recovers
  <math|w<rsub|1>>, and the third recovers <math|w<rsub|3>>, since
  <math|d<rsub|G>*\<nu\><rsup|2>\<gtr\>0>. Together with the one-point vacuum
  coefficient <math|w<rsub|0>>, these four probes prove injectivity of the
  free stress coefficient map into the free stress table. The raw bound
  <math|dim \<cal-R\><rsub|4>\<leqslant\>4> and the surjective averaging
  projection from raw arrays to the free table show that the free table has
  dimension at most four. Thus the coefficient map is an isomorphism.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Free stress coefficient map is an
    isomorphism><label|lem:ex-trace-free-stress-basis>The four coefficient
    tensors consisting of the vacuum scalar, the two independent diagonal
    curvature contractions, and the off-diagonal shear contraction form a
    basis of the free symmetric grade-four stress table.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The four probes above prove injectivity from a four-dimensional space;
    the raw upper bound proves equality of dimensions, hence bijectivity.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The two conserved free directions>

  Let <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|4>> be the free symmetric grade-four stress table
  and <math|K<rsub|0>=ker <around|(|<math-up|skim>:\<cal-T\><rsub|4>\<to\>\<bbb-R\>|)>>.
  Let <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|5>> be the grade-five vector table after imposing
  the free Maxwell equation. Fix on this same free field table a derivative
  plan, whose grade-raising maps on the complexified label spaces are
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|a><rsup|<around|(|d|)>>:\<cal-F\><rsub|d>\<to\>\<cal-F\><rsub|d+1>>.
  They are complex-linear, commute with the real involution and have vector
  covariance:

  <\equation*>
    \<rho\><rsub|d+1><around|(|s|)>*<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|d|)>>=<big|sum><rsub|a>s<rsub|a*b>*<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|a><rsup|<around|(|d|)>>*\<rho\><rsub|d><around|(|s|)>
  </equation*>

  for every signed permutation matrix <math|s>, where <math|\<rho\><rsub|d>>
  is its action on grade-<math|d> labels. Their compatibility with the smooth
  separated free correlations is as follows. For every grade-<math|d> label
  <math|B>, every finite spectator word <math|B<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,B<rsub|n>>
  of arbitrary grades, and every pairwise distinct tuple
  <math|<around|(|x,x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-trace-derivative-compatibility><around*|\<langle\>|<around|(|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|a><rsup|<around|(|d|)>>*B|)><around|(|x|)>*<big|prod><rsub|k=1><rsup|n>B<rsub|k><around|(|x<rsub|k>|)>|\<rangle\>><rsub|0>=\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|a>>*<around*|\<langle\>|B<around|(|x|)>*<big|prod><rsub|k=1><rsup|n>B<rsub|k><around|(|x<rsub|k>|)>|\<rangle\>><rsub|0>.
  </equation>

  The derivative is taken in the first insertion, holding every spectator
  fixed, within the open set of separated points. Here is the construction on
  the reference table used in this chapter. Represent a label by its
  invariant polynomial in curvature jets, using radial reconstruction to
  identify these with the radial labels. On a jet generator
  <math|\<partial\><rsup|\<alpha\>>*F<rsub|b*c>> define the translation
  derivation in direction <math|a> by <math|\<partial\><rsup|\<alpha\>>*F<rsub|b*c>\<mapsto\>\<partial\><rsup|\<alpha\>+e<rsub|a>>*F<rsub|b*c>>,
  and extend by the Leibniz rule. It increases engineering weight by one,
  annihilates constants, preserves invariant polynomials, and commutes with
  complex conjugation. Changing coordinates by a signed permutation in this
  formula gives exactly the displayed vector-covariance identity.

  To check its action on correlations, expand a Wick moment of any fixed
  finite word into its finite set of contractions. Each contraction is a
  product of differentiated curvature covariance kernels. All are smooth at
  separated positions. Differentiation in the distinguished position acts on
  each of its incident contractions once, by the product rule; raising the
  corresponding jet in that slot gives exactly that term. Summing proves
  <eqref|eq:ex-trace-derivative-compatibility> at polynomial level, for every
  spectator word, including the empty word. No differentiation under an
  infinite sum is involved.

  The field table is the quotient by labels with all separated moments zero.
  If a polynomial has zero field, its moments are identically zero on each
  open configuration space. Their derivatives are zero there as well. The
  identity just proved therefore shows that its translation derivation also
  has zero field. The derivation descends to the quotient and defines
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|a><rsup|<around|(|d|)>>> independently
  of the representative. Reality, covariance and the unit identity descend
  with it. Thus the compatibility needed below holds on this particular
  reference table; it is not an additional choice of a derivative plan.
  Define the restricted divergence by

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|D<rsub|0>*T|)><rsub|b>=<big|sum><rsub|a=0><rsup|3><with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|a><rsup|<around|(|4|)>>*T<rsub|a*b>,<space|2em>D<rsub|0>:K<rsub|0>\<longrightarrow\>\<cal-V\><rsub|5>.
  </equation*>

  Reality and the covariance law make this a real linear map to the stated
  vector table: the two transformed indices contracted in the sum cancel by
  orthogonality of <math|s>, leaving its vector action on <math|b>. Write
  <math|\<pi\>> for the quotient projection from the raw vector table onto
  <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|5>>. The projection is onto by the equivariant section
  obtained by averaging any real section over the finite signed permutation
  group. The equation-of-motion array is

  <\equation*>
    E<rsub|b>=<big|sum><rsub|i,a><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|b*i>,\<partial\><rsub|a>*F<rsub|a*i>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation*>

  It is a nonzero raw covariant vector: take a nonzero color vector <math|v>,
  set <math|F<rsub|01>=v> and <math|\<partial\><rsub|0>*F<rsub|01>=v> at one
  jet, with all other independent jet coordinates zero. The defining sum then
  evaluates its <math|b=0> component to <math|<around|\<langle\>|v,v|\<rangle\>>\<gtr\>0>.
  The Maxwell equation makes <math|E> vanish after the quotient. Thus the
  kernel of <math|\<pi\>> is nonzero. Rank\Unullity and <math|dim
  \<cal-R\><rsub|5>\<leqslant\>3> give <math|dim
  \<cal-V\><rsub|5>\<leqslant\>2>.

  Let <math|\<phi\><rsub|1>,\<phi\><rsub|2>> be the two linear functionals on
  <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|5>> obtained by correlating the component with output
  index <math|0> at <math|e<rsub|0>> with the respective quadratic curvature
  probes <math|<around|(|01,01|)>> and <math|<around|(|12,12|)>> at the
  origin. These are functionals of a vector label, not definitions by
  differentiating a stress correlation. As above,
  <math|S<rsub|w>*<around|(|i*j,k*l;a*b;x|)>> is the stress response with
  field-pair insertion <math|<around|(|i*j,k*l|)>>, output component
  <math|<around|(|a,b|)>>, and separation <math|x>. For the stress
  <math|T<rsub|w>> with coefficient vector <math|w> and zero skim, linearity
  and <eqref|eq:ex-trace-derivative-compatibility> give

  <\equation*>
    \<phi\><rsub|1>*<around|(|D<rsub|0>*T<rsub|w>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|u>\<partial\><rsub|u>*S<rsub|w>*<around|(|01,01;u*0;e<rsub|0>|)>,<space|2em>\<phi\><rsub|2>*<around|(|D<rsub|0>*T<rsub|w>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|u>\<partial\><rsub|u>*S<rsub|w>*<around|(|12,12;u*0;e<rsub|0>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Differentiating the chain product gives

  <\equation*>
    \<partial\><rsub|u>*\<cal-C\>=<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|u>*C<rsub|a*b;i*j>|)>*C<rsub|c*d;k*l>+C<rsub|a*b;i*j>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|u>*C<rsub|c*d;k*l>|)>+<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|u>*C<rsub|a*b;k*l>|)>*C<rsub|c*d;i*j>+C<rsub|a*b;k*l>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|u>*C<rsub|c*d;i*j>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Inserting <eqref|eq:ex-trace-green-hessian>\U
  <eqref|eq:ex-trace-green-third> and summing <math|u=0,1,2,3> gives

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|u>\<partial\><rsub|u>*S<rsub|w>*<around|(|01,01;u*0;e<rsub|0>|)>>|<cell|=d<rsub|G>*\<nu\><rsup|2>*<around|(|-256*w<rsub|1>-512*w<rsub|2>+128*w<rsub|3>|)>,<eq-number>>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|u>\<partial\><rsub|u>*S<rsub|w>*<around|(|12,12;u*0;e<rsub|0>|)>>|<cell|=d<rsub|G>*\<nu\><rsup|2>*<around|(|-512*w<rsub|2>-128*w<rsub|3>|)>.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-trace-divergence-probes>>>>>
  </align>

  The two coefficient rows in <math|<around|(|w<rsub|1>,w<rsub|2>,w<rsub|3>|)>>
  are linearly independent. Consequently <math|\<phi\><rsub|1>\<circ\>D<rsub|0>>
  and <math|\<phi\><rsub|2>\<circ\>D<rsub|0>> are independent, so
  <math|\<phi\><rsub|1>,\<phi\><rsub|2>> are independent on
  <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|5>>. Together with the upper bound <math|dim
  \<cal-V\><rsub|5>\<leqslant\>2>, this gives <math|dim \<cal-V\><rsub|5>=2>,
  and <math|\<Phi\>=<around|(|\<phi\><rsub|1>,\<phi\><rsub|2>|)>:\<cal-V\><rsub|5>\<to\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>>
  is an isomorphism. The two probe equations have common kernel
  <math|\<bbb-R\>*<around|(|1,-1/4,1|)>> on
  <math|<around|(|w<rsub|1>,w<rsub|2>,w<rsub|3>|)>>: the second gives
  <math|w<rsub|2>=-w<rsub|3>/4>, and then the first gives
  <math|w<rsub|1>=w<rsub|3>>. The scalar coefficient <math|w<rsub|0>> is
  removed by zero skim. Thus the kernel of
  <math|<around|(|<math-up|skim>,\<Phi\>\<circ\>D<rsub|0>|)>> is exactly the
  Weyl line <math|<around|(|0,1,-1/4,1|)>>. Since <math|\<Phi\>> is an
  isomorphism, this also proves the Weyl tensor has zero divergence. The
  zero-skim domain has dimension three, and the restricted divergence has
  one-dimensional kernel, so it has rank two and is onto the two-dimensional
  target. Its kernel is precisely the Weyl line.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Free rank and closure dimensions><label|prop:ex-trace-free-closure-rank>Under
    the reality, signed covariance and separated-correlation compatibility
    assumptions on the derivative plan stated above, the free symmetric
    grade-four stress table has dimension four. The Maxwell-reduced
    grade-five vector table has dimension two. The free zero-skim divergence
    map is onto that vector table and its kernel is the one-dimensional Weyl
    line.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The first assertion is Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-trace-free-stress-basis>.
    The previous paragraph proves the second and third assertions. The scalar
    skim is exactly the real one-point average of the <math|00> entry; it is
    not the tensor trace. The identification of the differentiated stress
    probes with the probes of <math|D<rsub|0>> uses
    <eqref|eq:ex-trace-derivative-compatibility>; the remaining steps are the
    explicit probe formulas and the finite-dimensional quotient argument just
    given.
  </proof>

  <subsection|The free Maxwell stress and its collar Ward
  identities><label|sec:free-maxwell-ward-flux>

  The reference stress is fixed by its action on every insertion, not only by
  conservation. We prove that action using the same free curvature covariance
  and Wick polynomial fields as in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-free-rank>.
  All integrals involving the stress below are taken on compact collars
  separated from every insertion. No product of singular kernels at
  coincident points is used.

  Let <math|\<frak-g\>> carry the fixed invariant real inner product. A
  smooth <math|\<frak-g\>>-valued two-form <math|f=<around|(|f<rsub|a*b>|)>>
  is a Maxwell wave on an open set if it is antisymmetric and satisfies

  <\equation*>
    \<partial\><rsub|a>*f<rsub|b*c>+\<partial\><rsub|b>*f<rsub|c*a>+\<partial\><rsub|c>*f<rsub|a*b>=0,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|a>*f<rsub|a*b>=0.
  </equation*>

  Indices run from zero to three. Set <math|\<nu\>=<around|(|4*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>
  and <math|G<around|(|z|)>=\<nu\><around|\||z|\|><rsup|-2>> for
  <math|z\<ne\>0>. The covariance between curvature components is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:maxwell-ward-covariance>C<rsub|a*b;i*l><around|(|z|)>=\<delta\><rsub|i*b>*\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<partial\><rsub|l>*G<around|(|z|)>-\<delta\><rsub|l*b>*\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<partial\><rsub|i>*G<around|(|z|)>-\<delta\><rsub|i*a>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*\<partial\><rsub|l>*G<around|(|z|)>+\<delta\><rsub|l*a>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*\<partial\><rsub|i>*G<around|(|z|)>,
  </equation>

  times the identity on colour. A linear probe <math|p> of a homogeneous
  Maxwell jet of order <math|j> acts on <math|D<rsup|j>*f<around|(|x|)>/j>!;
  write its value as <math|p*<around|[|f;x|]>>. Its covariance pulse
  <math|P<rsub|p,x><around|(|y|)>> is the covariance of that probe at
  <math|x> with curvature at <math|y>. For a coordinate curvature probe
  <math|<around|(|\<gamma\>,i,l|)>>, with colour basis vector
  <math|e<rsub|\<gamma\>>>, this is <math|C<rsub|a*b;i*l>*<around|(|y-x|)>*e<rsub|\<gamma\>>>;
  higher pulses are obtained by the corresponding normalized derivatives in
  <math|x>. Such probes span the dual of the finite jet space, and these
  formulas extend linearly. The raised probe <math|\<partial\><rsub|u>*p> is
  defined by <math|<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|u>*p|)><around|[|f;x|]>=\<partial\><rsub|u>(p[f;<space|0.17em>\<cdot\><space|0.17em><around|]||)><around|(|x|)>>.
  It includes the factor <math|j+1> arising from normalized Taylor
  coefficients. Differentiating the covariance gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:maxwell-pulse-differentiation>\<partial\><rsub|x,u>*P<rsub|p,x><around|(|y|)>=P<rsub|\<partial\><rsub|u>*p,x><around|(|y|)>.
  </equation>

  Each pulse is a Maxwell wave away from its centre, as follows by commuting
  derivatives in <eqref|eq:maxwell-ward-covariance> and using
  <math|\<Delta\>*G=0> there.

  <subsubsection|The polarized current and its divergence>

  For closed two-forms <math|f,g>, define the polarized stress

  <\equation*>
    T<rsub|a*b>*<around|(|f,g|)>=<big|sum><rsub|c><around*|(|<around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|a*c>,g<rsub|b*c>|\<rangle\>>+<around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|b*c>,g<rsub|a*c>|\<rangle\>>|)>-<frac|\<delta\><rsub|a*b>|2>*<big|sum><rsub|c,d><around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|c*d>,g<rsub|c*d>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation*>

  It is symmetric in <math|a,b> and in <math|f,g>, and its trace is zero in
  four dimensions. Put <math|<around|(|<math-up|div><space|0.17em>f|)><rsub|c>=<big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|a>*f<rsub|a*c>>.
  The Bianchi identity implies

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|a,c><around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|a*c>,\<partial\><rsub|a>*g<rsub|b*c>|\<rangle\>>=<frac|1|2>*<big|sum><rsub|a,c><around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|a*c>,\<partial\><rsub|b>*g<rsub|a*c>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed substitute <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>*g<rsub|b*c>=\<partial\><rsub|b>*g<rsub|a*c>-\<partial\><rsub|c>*g<rsub|a*b>>;
  exchanging <math|a,c> and using both antisymmetries makes the last sum
  equal to the left side. The product rule, followed by this identity for
  <math|<around|(|f,g|)>> and <math|<around|(|g,f|)>>, cancels the
  differentiated trace terms and gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:maxwell-stress-divergence><big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|a>*T<rsub|a*b>*<around|(|f,g|)>=<big|sum><rsub|c><around*|(|<around|\<langle\>|<math-up|div><space|0.17em>f<rsub|c>,g<rsub|b*c>|\<rangle\>>+<around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|b*c>,<math-up|div><space|0.17em>g<rsub|c>|\<rangle\>>|)>.
  </equation>

  We need <math|v<around|(|y|)>=e<rsub|b>> and
  <math|v<around|(|y|)>=A*<around|(|y-x|)>> for a real skew matrix <math|A>.
  For either vector field define <math|j<rsub|a>*<around|(|v;f,g|)>=<big|sum><rsub|b>v<rsub|b>*T<rsub|a*b>*<around|(|f,g|)>>.
  The term <math|<big|sum><rsub|a,b><around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*v<rsub|b>|)>*T<rsub|a*b>>
  vanishes by symmetry; the same holds for dilation
  <math|v<around|(|y|)>=y-x> by tracelessness. Thus, if both forms are
  Maxwell waves, <math|<math-up|div><space|0.17em>j=0>. For
  <math|\<chi\>\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>>*<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>;\<bbb-R\>|)>>
  define outward collar flux by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:maxwell-collar-flux>\<cal-F\><rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|j|)>=-<big|sum><rsub|a><big|int><around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\>|)><around|(|y|)>*j<rsub|a><around|(|y|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y.
  </equation>

  Whenever <math|j> is smooth on a neighborhood of
  <math|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<chi\>>, integration by parts identifies
  this with <math|<big|int>\<chi\><math-up|div><space|0.17em>j>. In
  particular it vanishes for two waves on that neighborhood. If the only
  singularity is at <math|x> and both cutoffs equal one near <math|x>, their
  fluxes agree: apply the same identity to their difference, which vanishes
  on a neighborhood of <math|x>.

  <subsubsection|A unit-mass regulator fixes the sign>

  For <math|t\<gtr\>0> set

  <\equation*>
    G<rsub|t><around|(|z|)>=<frac|\<nu\>|t<rsup|2>+<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>>,<space|2em>\<rho\><rsub|t><around|(|z|)>=<frac|8*\<nu\>*t<rsup|2>|<around|(|t<rsup|2>+<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|3>>.
  </equation*>

  Direct differentiation gives <math|-\<Delta\>*G<rsub|t>=\<rho\><rsub|t>>
  and <math|t<rsup|4>*\<rho\><rsub|t>*<around|(|t*z|)>=\<rho\><rsub|1><around|(|z|)>>.
  These kernels are nonnegative, and polar coordinates give

  <\equation*>
    <big|int>\<rho\><rsub|1><around|(|z|)>*<space|0.17em>d*z=16*\<pi\><rsup|2>*\<nu\>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|r<rsup|3>|<around|(|1+r<rsup|2>|)><rsup|3>>*<space|0.17em>d*r=1.
  </equation*>

  The radial integral is <math|1/4>, since an antiderivative is
  <math|<tfrac|1|4><around|(|1-<around|(|1+r<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>|)><rsup|2>>.
  For any continuous compactly supported <math|h>, substitution
  <math|y=x+t*z> and dominated convergence therefore give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:maxwell-source-approximation><big|int>\<rho\><rsub|t>*<around|(|y-x|)>*h<around|(|y|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y\<longrightarrow\>h<around|(|x|)>.
  </equation>

  The dominating function is <math|\<rho\><rsub|1><around|(|z|)><around|\<\|\|\>|h|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>.

  Fix a coordinate probe <math|<around|(|\<gamma\>,i,l|)>> and form a
  regulated pulse <math|R<rsub|t>=d*A<rsub|t>>, where

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|A<rsub|t>|)><rsub|b><around|(|y|)>=<around*|(|\<delta\><rsub|i*b>*\<partial\><rsub|l>*G<rsub|t>*<around|(|y-x|)>-\<delta\><rsub|l*b>*\<partial\><rsub|i>*G<rsub|t>*<around|(|y-x|)>|)>*e<rsub|\<gamma\>>.
  </equation*>

  It is closed and satisfies

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|<math-up|div><space|0.17em>R<rsub|t>|)><rsub|b>=<around|(|\<delta\><rsub|l*b>*\<partial\><rsub|i>*\<rho\><rsub|t>*<around|(|y-x|)>-\<delta\><rsub|i*b>*\<partial\><rsub|l>*\<rho\><rsub|t>*<around|(|y-x|)>|)>*e<rsub|\<gamma\>>.
  </equation*>

  For the latter identity, <math|<big|sum><rsub|b>\<partial\><rsub|b><around|(|A<rsub|t>|)><rsub|b>=0>
  by commuting derivatives, so <math|<math-up|div><space|0.17em>d*A<rsub|t>=\<Delta\>*A<rsub|t>>;
  now use <math|\<Delta\>*G<rsub|t>=-\<rho\><rsub|t>>. Away from <math|x>,
  <math|R<rsub|t>> and all its derivatives converge locally uniformly to
  <math|P<rsub|<around|(|\<gamma\>,i,l|)>,x>>.

  Suppose <math|f> is a Maxwell wave on a neighborhood of
  <math|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<chi\>>, and <math|\<chi\>=1> near
  <math|x>. Put <math|a<rsub|b><around|(|y|)>=<big|sum><rsub|u>v<rsub|u><around|(|y|)>*f<rsub|u*b><around|(|y|)>>
  and <math|H<rsub|b><around|(|y|)>=\<chi\><around|(|y|)><around|\<langle\>|e<rsub|\<gamma\>>,a<rsub|b><around|(|y|)>|\<rangle\>>>.
  Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:maxwell-stress-divergence> gives

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-F\><rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|j*<around|(|v;R<rsub|t>,f|)>|)>=<big|int>H<rsub|l>*\<partial\><rsub|i>*\<rho\><rsub|t>*<around|(|<space|0.17em>\<cdummy\>-x|)>-<big|int>H<rsub|i>*\<partial\><rsub|l>*\<rho\><rsub|t>*<around|(|<space|0.17em>\<cdummy\>-x|)>=<big|int>\<rho\><rsub|t>*<around|(|<space|0.17em>\<cdummy\>-x|)>*\<partial\><rsub|l>*H<rsub|i>-<big|int>\<rho\><rsub|t>*<around|(|<space|0.17em>\<cdummy\>-x|)>*\<partial\><rsub|i>*H<rsub|l>.
  </equation*>

  All integrations by parts are with smooth compactly supported
  <math|H<rsub|b>>. The right side tends to
  <math|\<partial\><rsub|l>*H<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>-\<partial\><rsub|i>*H<rsub|l><around|(|x|)>>
  by <eqref|eq:maxwell-source-approximation>. The left side tends to the
  unregulated flux because <math|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>d*\<chi\>> is
  compact and separated from <math|x>. Hence

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:maxwell-coordinate-charge>\<cal-F\><rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|j*<around|(|v;P<rsub|<around|(|\<gamma\>,i,l|)>,x>,f|)>|)>=-<around|\<langle\>|e<rsub|\<gamma\>>,<around|(|d*\<iota\><rsub|v>*f|)><rsub|i*l><around|(|x|)>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation>

  This proves the sign directly from the Green normalization and the
  outward-flux convention.

  For an arbitrary jet probe <math|p>, differentiate this identity in the
  enclosed centre while keeping both <math|v> and <math|\<chi\>> fixed. The
  moving centre stays in a neighborhood on which <math|\<chi\>=1>. That
  neighborhood can be chosen with positive distance from the compact collar.
  On its product with the collar, the pulse derivatives in
  <eqref|eq:maxwell-pulse-differentiation> are continuous and bounded. They
  therefore provide an integrable constant times the collar indicator for
  each fixed differentiation. Differentiation under the integral is
  justified. Induction on the jet order and linearity in <math|p> prove

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:maxwell-single-charge>\<cal-F\><rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|j*<around|(|v;P<rsub|p,x>,f|)>|)>=-p*<around|[|d*\<iota\><rsub|v>*f;x|]>.
  </equation>

  Normalized jets cause no change: the raising factors occur on both sides
  and are nonzero. This argument applies whenever <math|d*\<iota\><rsub|v>*f>
  is a Maxwell wave locally, as it is for the affine motions used here.

  For completeness, the Bianchi identity expands the variation as

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|d*\<iota\><rsub|v>*f|)><rsub|a*b>=<big|sum><rsub|i><around*|(|v<rsub|i>*\<partial\><rsub|i>*f<rsub|a*b>+<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*v<rsub|i>|)>*f<rsub|i*b>+<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|b>*v<rsub|i>|)>*f<rsub|a*i>|)>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|v=e<rsub|b>> this is <math|\<partial\><rsub|b>*f>. For
  <math|v=A*<around|(|y-x|)>>, it is the infinitesimal inverse-rotation
  action on the jet at <math|x>. Differentiating the two index factors and
  the argument in <math|e<rsup|t*A><rsub|i*a>*e<rsup|t*A><rsub|l*b>*f<rsub|i*l>*<around|(|x+e<rsup|t*A>*z|)>>
  at <math|t=0>, where <math|z> is displacement from the fixed rotation
  centre <math|x>, gives exactly this formula. Orthogonal covariance
  preserves the Maxwell equations, so this variation is a wave. Translation
  has the same property by commutation of derivatives. Write
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|v>*p> for the resulting probe: the
  raised derivative probe for translation and the angular probe for rotation
  about <math|x>. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:maxwell-single-charge> becomes
  <math|-<around|(|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|v>*p|)><around|[|f;x|]>>.

  <subsubsection|Two contractions at the enclosed point>

  When both pulses are centred at <math|x>, the preceding formula cannot be
  used with one pulse as a smooth wave across <math|x>. Instead use collar
  independence and homogeneity. For a probe of jet order <math|j>,

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|p,x>*<around|(|x+t*z|)>=t<rsup|-<around|(|4+j|)>>*P<rsub|p,x>*<around|(|x+z|)>*<space|2em><around|(|t\<gtr\>0,z\<ne\>0|)>,
  </equation*>

  because <math|G> has degree <math|-2> and the curvature covariance has two
  derivatives, followed by <math|j> centre derivatives. The drive has degree
  <math|e=0> for translation and <math|e=1> for rotation about <math|x>.
  Replace <math|\<chi\><around|(|y|)>> by
  <math|\<psi\><around|(|y|)>=\<chi\>*<around|(|x+<around|(|y-x|)>/t|)>>.
  Both cutoffs equal one near <math|x>, so their fluxes agree. In the latter
  integral set <math|y=x+t*z>. The volume contributes <math|t<rsup|4>>, the
  cutoff derivative <math|t<rsup|-1>>, the drive <math|t<rsup|e>>, and the
  two pulses <math|t<rsup|-8-j-l>>. Consequently their common flux <math|Z>
  satisfies

  <\equation*>
    Z=t<rsup|e-5-j-l>*Z.
  </equation*>

  Take <math|t=2>. Since <math|e\<leqslant\>1> and <math|j,l\<geqslant\>0>,
  the factor is strictly less than one; hence <math|Z=0>. This proves
  vanishing without extending the product of the two poles through <math|x>.

  <subsubsection|All Wick polynomials and all spectators>

  The Wick stress polynomial is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:maxwell-wick-stress>J<rsup|0><rsub|a*b>=<big|sum><rsub|c>:<space|-0.17em><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|a*c>,F<rsub|b*c>|\<rangle\>><space|-0.17em>:-<frac|\<delta\><rsub|a*b>|4>*<big|sum><rsub|c,d>:<space|-0.17em><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|c*d>,F<rsub|c*d>|\<rangle\>><space|-0.17em>:.
  </equation>

  Here the colons mean Wick ordering at that insertion. In Wick moments there
  are no contractions between factors at the same insertion. Fix distinct
  positions <math|x<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>> and a smooth compact cutoff
  <math|\<chi\>> equal to one near <math|x<rsub|0>>, whose support excludes
  every other <math|x<rsub|i>>. Let <math|P<rsub|i>> be arbitrary Wick
  polynomials in finitely many jet probes at <math|x<rsub|i>>. Extend
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|v>> from probes to polynomials by the
  Leibniz rule, and denote this derivation by <math|\<delta\><rsub|v>>. Here
  <math|v=e<rsub|b>> for a coordinate direction <math|b>, or
  <math|v<around|(|y|)>=A*<around|(|y-x<rsub|0>|)>> with <math|A> real skew;
  by linearity the claim then also holds for every affine Killing field. We
  claim

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:maxwell-polynomial-ward>-<big|sum><rsub|a,b><big|int><around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\>|)><around|(|y|)>*v<rsub|b><around|(|y|)>*<around*|\<langle\>|J<rsup|0><rsub|a*b><around|(|y|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i=0><rsup|n>P<rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>|\<rangle\>><rsub|0>*d*y=-<around*|\<langle\>|<around|(|\<delta\><rsub|v>*P<rsub|0>|)><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>P<rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>|\<rangle\>><rsub|0>.
  </equation>

  First suppose each <math|P<rsub|i>> is a monomial. Label its individual
  factor occurrences, including repeated factors, by a finite set <math|I>.
  Let <math|x<rsub|u<around|(|h|)>>> be the position of occurrence <math|h>,
  with probe <math|p<rsub|h>>, and put <math|f<rsub|h>=P<rsub|p<rsub|h>,x<rsub|u<around|(|h|)>>>>.
  For a subset of remaining occurrences <math|A>, write
  <math|W<around|(|A|)>> for its Wick pairing sum, with pair weights zero at
  the same insertion. Extract the two stress factors from the Wick sum. The
  exact integrand contracted with <math|v<rsub|b>> is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:maxwell-double-extraction><frac|1|2>*<big|sum><rsub|h\<ne\>l>j<rsub|a>*<around|(|v;f<rsub|h>,f<rsub|l>|)><around|(|y|)>*<space|0.17em>W*<around|(|I\<setminus\><around|{|h,l|}>|)>.
  </equation>

  To verify the factor <math|1/2>, initially assign the first stress factor
  to <math|h> and the second to <math|l>. The quadratic expression in
  <eqref|eq:maxwell-wick-stress> gives one ordered contraction. Exchanging
  <math|h,l> gives its transpose; their sum is exactly the polarized tensor
  <math|T<rsub|a*b>*<around|(|f<rsub|h>,f<rsub|l>|)>>. Averaging these two
  equal ordered sums gives <eqref|eq:maxwell-double-extraction>. The stress
  factors cannot contract to each other because of Wick ordering. After
  choosing their partners, the remaining pairing sum is exactly
  <math|W*<around|(|I\<setminus\><around|{|h,l|}>|)>>.

  Every term is smooth on the compact collar, so the finite sum can be
  integrated term by term. There are three cases. If neither partner is at
  <math|x<rsub|0>>, both pulses are smooth waves on the support of
  <math|\<chi\>>, and the flux vanishes. If both are at <math|x<rsub|0>>, the
  preceding homogeneous two-pulse argument gives zero. If exactly one, say
  <math|h>, is at <math|x<rsub|0>>, <eqref|eq:maxwell-single-charge> gives
  minus the covariance of <math|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|v>*p<rsub|h>> at
  <math|x<rsub|0>> with <math|p<rsub|l>> at its spectator position. Both
  orders <math|<around|(|h,l|)>> occur, and symmetry cancels the factor
  <math|1/2>. For a fixed <math|h> at <math|x<rsub|0>>, sum over its possible
  partner <math|l> and over the remaining Wick pairings. This is precisely
  the Wick moment with occurrence <math|h> replaced by
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|v>*p<rsub|h>>. Summing over all such
  occurrences is the Leibniz rule for <math|\<delta\><rsub|v>*P<rsub|0>>,
  proving <eqref|eq:maxwell-polynomial-ward> for monomials. Expand general
  polynomials in their finite monomial bases. Multilinearity and the
  linearity of the compact integrals prove the identity for arbitrary complex
  coefficients and every finite spectator word.

  <subsubsection|The fixed reference normalization>

  For this final step assume the gauge Lie algebra is nonzero, nonabelian and
  simple, as in the construction theorem. The preceding collar and Wick
  calculations used only its finite-dimensional real inner product; the rank
  and nontriviality assertions below use this additional hypothesis. The
  polynomial <eqref|eq:maxwell-wick-stress> is real, symmetric,
  gauge-invariant and of grade four. Its expectation vanishes by Wick
  ordering. Its divergence is zero already as a Maxwell jet polynomial: apply
  <eqref|eq:maxwell-stress-divergence> with <math|f=g> and divide by two.
  Polynomial differentiation is the same Leibniz rule as the field
  derivative. Its reference field is nonzero by the explicit stress probes in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-free-rank>. That section
  also proves surjectivity of divergence on centered covariant arrays and
  that its kernel is the line through this tensor. These facts supply the
  conserved-line certificate used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-stress-normalization>.

  We use the following three properties of the reference field table. Their
  application to the selected reference is justified below.

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(R1)>Faithfulness: a field label whose separated correlations with
    every spectator word vanish is zero.

    <item*|(R2)>The reference field labels are the images of the invariant
    Wick jet polynomials, and this identification preserves Wick moments,
    sends the raised probe <math|\<partial\><rsub|u>*p> to the derivative
    plan in direction <math|u>, and sends the rotation variation
    <math|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|v>*p>,
    <math|v<around|(|y|)>=A*<around|(|y-x|)>>, to the infinitesimal rotation
    action.

    <item*|(R3)>The canonical reference stress <math|J<rsup|0>> is the image
    of the Wick polynomial <eqref|eq:maxwell-wick-stress>.
  </enumerate>

  By (R2) and (R3), <eqref|eq:maxwell-polynomial-ward> descends to all field
  labels. For the reference tensor <math|\<tau\>*J<rsup|0>>,
  <math|\<tau\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<setminus\><around|{|0|}>>, both its
  translation and rotation Ward maps have the common coefficient
  <math|-\<tau\>>. The sign comes from the collar calculation, not a choice
  made after comparing with the physical theory.

  There is also a useful uniqueness check on the reference packaging. Suppose
  the same stress field has translation Ward identities with coefficients
  <math|c<rsub|0>,c<rsub|1>> multiplying the same derivative plan.
  Subtracting the two identities shows that every separated correlation with
  <math|<around|(|c<rsub|0>-c<rsub|1>|)>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*v> vanishes.
  Faithfulness (R1) gives <math|<around|(|c<rsub|0>-c<rsub|1>|)>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*v=0>
  for every label <math|v>. The derivative plan is nonzero already in grade
  four, as detected by the nonzero divergence probes in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-free-rank>; that detection
  uses the compatibility of the derivative plan with the field derivatives in
  (R2). Hence <math|c<rsub|0>=c<rsub|1>>. In particular, retaining the
  canonical tensor <math|J<rsup|0>> and the same derivative plan forces the
  coefficient to be <math|-1>.

  In the native construction the reference is fixed before the weak
  comparison curve is chosen. By (R3) its canonical tensor has polynomial
  <eqref|eq:maxwell-wick-stress>: the quadratic coordinate labels map to the
  corresponding products of curvature probes, their sum gives the first term,
  and subtracting one quarter of the diagonal sum gives the second. Selecting
  this tensor does not change the hierarchy, derivative plan, rotation or
  vacuum of the reference table, so (R1) and (R2) apply to the same data. The
  uniqueness argument therefore fixes its common Ward coefficient to
  <math|-1> on that same data. Combined with the actual conserved-line
  certificate, this justifies the reference input to the physical
  normalization step without substituting another comparison model.

  <\corollary>
    <dueto|Ward normalization of the selected
    reference><label|cor:maxwell-selected-reference-ward>The reference table
    used in the selected comparison satisfies (R1)\U(R3). Its canonical
    tensor <math|J<rsup|0>> has translation and rotation Ward coefficient
    <math|-1>. The tensor <math|-J<rsup|0>> has coefficient <math|1>.
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    Use the reference identification <eqref|eq:scribe-reference-dictionary>.
    It takes a Wick polynomial to its class in the very reference table used
    for the comparison. The finite separating tests of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-all-grade-free-detector> identify
    its kernel with the moment-null ideal. Consequently the induced map is
    faithful, which proves (R1), and its defining moments are the Wick
    moments. The translation derivation descends to this quotient by the
    all-spectator argument in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-free-rank>.
    Likewise the signed and continuous coordinate actions descend because
    their changes of variables preserve the moment-null ideal.
    Differentiating the finite-dimensional tensor-index and jet rotation
    action gives the rotation derivation, so its action on a polynomial class
    is exactly the class of that derivation. These are precisely the two
    identities <eqref|eq:scribe-reference-derivatives>, proving (R2). Finally
    the coordinate formula for the canonical stress is the image of
    <eqref|eq:maxwell-wick-stress>; the sign of the tensor used for the
    branch comparison is recorded in <eqref|eq:scribe-reference-stress-sign>.
    This proves (R3) without rescaling the reference field space. Apply the
    polynomial Ward identity and linearity in the stress. The reference
    dictionary and the free detector use Wick contractions and
    moment-nullity, not a Ward normalization, so this application is not
    circular.
  </proof>

  <subsection|The free radial flux and the weighted Euler
  operator><label|sec:free-maxwell-radial-flux>

  The classification of free vector charges also uses dilation about the
  marked insertion. Its generator preserves weighted grade, so its flux can
  be used without increasing the spectator capacity. We derive the
  normalization from the same stress as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:free-maxwell-ward-flux>.

  Fix a centre <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>> and the vector field
  <math|v<rsub|x><around|(|y|)>=y-x>. For the polarized stress
  <math|T<rsub|a*b>*<around|(|f,g|)>> used there, differentiation of
  <math|j<rsub|a>=<big|sum><rsub|b><around|(|v<rsub|x>|)><rsub|b>*T<rsub|a*b>>
  gives the trace of the stress plus its contracted divergence. The trace is
  zero in four dimensions. Thus the current is divergence free wherever both
  arguments are Maxwell waves. This is the precise reason dilation is allowed
  even though its matrix is not skew-symmetric.

  Let <math|p> be a linear probe of homogeneous jet order
  <math|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, let <math|f> be a Maxwell wave on a neighborhood
  of the support of a smooth compact cutoff <math|\<chi\>>, and assume
  <math|\<chi\>=1> near <math|x>. The regulator calculation proving
  <eqref|eq:maxwell-coordinate-charge> remains valid for <math|v<rsub|x>>:
  the extra differentiated-drive term is the vanishing stress trace,
  including for the regulated closed pulse. Differentiation in the enclosed
  pulse centre, with the drive fixed at <math|v<rsub|x>> and the cutoff
  fixed, therefore gives

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-F\><rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|j*<around|(|v<rsub|x>;P<rsub|p,x>,f|)>|)>=-p*<around|[|d*\<iota\><rsub|v<rsub|x>>*f;x|]>.
  </equation*>

  The differentiation under the collar integral has the same compact,
  separated dominator as in <eqref|eq:maxwell-single-charge>.

  The Bianchi expansion of the variation is now

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|d*\<iota\><rsub|v<rsub|x>>*f|)><rsub|a*b><around|(|y|)>=<around|(|y-x|)>\<cdot\>\<nabla\>*f<rsub|a*b><around|(|y|)>+2*f<rsub|a*b><around|(|y|)>.
  </equation*>

  This is again a Maxwell wave: locally it is the derivative at <math|s=0> of
  <math|e<rsup|2*s>*f*<around|(|x+e<rsup|s>*<around|(|y-x|)>|)>>, and the
  Maxwell and Bianchi differential equations are preserved by that rescaling.
  Its homogeneous Taylor coefficient of order <math|j> at <math|x> is
  <math|<around|(|j+2|)>> times that of <math|f>. Indeed the degree-<math|j>
  coefficient of the rescaled wave is <math|e<rsup|<around|(|j+2|)>*s>> times
  the original coefficient; differentiating gives the factor, including for
  the normalized Taylor coefficient <math|D<rsup|j>*f/j>!. Consequently

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:maxwell-radial-single-charge>\<cal-F\><rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|j*<around|(|v<rsub|x>;P<rsub|p,x>,f|)>|)>=-<around|(|j+2|)>*p*<around|[|f;x|]>.
  </equation>

  Write <math|X<rsub|j,\<alpha\>>> for the polynomial jet coordinates and
  define the weighted Euler derivation

  <\equation*>
    <with|math-font|cal*|E>=<big|sum><rsub|j,\<alpha\>><around|(|j+2|)>*X<rsub|j,\<alpha\>>*<frac|\<partial\>|\<partial\>*X<rsub|j,\<alpha\>>>.
  </equation*>

  The sum acts finitely on every polynomial. On a monomial it multiplies by
  its weighted degree, and hence preserves every finite weighted filtration.
  It preserves the gauge-invariant polynomial space since colour rotations
  mix only coordinates of the same jet order.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Radial Ward identity for all polynomial
    words><label|prop:maxwell-radial-ward>Let
    <math|x<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>> be distinct points, and let
    <math|P<rsub|i>> be arbitrary Wick polynomials of Maxwell jets. Choose
    <math|\<chi\>\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>>> equal to one near
    <math|x<rsub|0>>, with support avoiding <math|x<rsub|i>> for
    <math|i\<gtr\>0>. For the same Wick stress <math|<math-ss|T><rsub|a*b>>
    as before,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:maxwell-radial-polynomial-ward>\<cal-F\><rsub|\<chi\>>*<space|-0.17em><around*|(|y\<longmapsto\><around*|[|<big|sum><rsub|b><around|(|y-x<rsub|0>|)><rsub|b>*\<cal-M\><rsub|<around|(|y,x<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>>*<around|(|<math-ss|T><rsub|a*b>,P<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,P<rsub|n>|)>|]><rsub|a>|)>=-\<cal-M\><rsub|<around|(|x<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>>*<around|(|<with|math-font|cal*|E>*P<rsub|0>,P<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,P<rsub|n>|)>.
    </equation>

    All integrals on the left are absolutely convergent.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The cutoff derivative has compact support separated from every insertion;
    the integrand is smooth there. This proves absolute integrability and
    allows the finite Wick sums to pass through the integral. Extract the two
    factors in the quadratic stress, as in the proof of
    <eqref|eq:maxwell-polynomial-ward>. If neither contracts with
    <math|P<rsub|0>>, the associated two pulses are smooth throughout the
    cutoff support, and the divergence-free current has zero flux. If both
    contract with <math|P<rsub|0>>, collar independence and pulse homogeneity
    give <math|Z=t<rsup|1-5-j-l>*Z> for jet orders <math|j,l> of those two
    legs: the radial drive is homogeneous of degree one. Taking <math|t=2>
    forces <math|Z=0>. This argument uses only the compact collar, not a
    product of singular pulses through their common centre.

    If exactly one stress leg contracts with <math|P<rsub|0>>, apply
    <eqref|eq:maxwell-radial-single-charge> to the other, smooth pulse. Each
    occurrence of an order-<math|j> coordinate in <math|P<rsub|0>> acquires
    the factor <math|-<around|(|j+2|)>>. Summing occurrences is exactly the
    action of <math|-<with|math-font|cal*|E>>. The two choices of stress leg
    cancel the factor <math|1/2> in the polarized Wick stress, with the same
    normalization as the preceding polynomial Ward identity. The remaining
    Wick pairings are those of the word on the right of
    <eqref|eq:maxwell-radial-polynomial-ward>. Linearity in all polynomials
    completes the proof.
  </proof>

  <subsection|A positive free detector at every jet
  order><label|sec:ex-lattice-all-grade-free-detectors>

  This section proves the all-grade reference-field separation needed in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-firesky-gallery> and for the
  finite point scanner. The point is that positivity must hold for a mixture
  of derivative orders, not just separately at each order. We establish it by
  an explicit triangular change of probes. All vector spaces in the one-leg
  calculation are real; ordinary complex conjugation is added only when
  passing to the Wick-polynomial pairing.

  <subsubsection|The Kelvin response and a triangular family of probes>

  Let <math|<with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|j>> be the finite-dimensional real
  space of homogeneous degree-<math|j> Maxwell field strengths in
  four-dimensional Euclidean space, with the fixed gauge-color
  representation. Thus <math|P\<in\><with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|j>> is an
  antisymmetric two-tensor-valued homogeneous polynomial satisfying the
  Maxwell differential and algebraic identities. Let
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<cdummy\>,\<cdummy\>|\<rangle\>><rsub|F>> denote
  the positive Frobenius inner product, including the positive color inner
  product. For a nonzero point <math|x>, write

  <\equation*>
    r<rsub|x>=<around|\||x|\|><rsup|-2>,<space|2em>I<around|(|x|)>=r<rsub|x>*x,<space|2em>\<vartheta\><rsub|x>=I-2*r<rsub|x>*<space|0.17em>x\<otimes\>x.
  </equation*>

  The matrix <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|x>> is the orthogonal reflection that
  reverses the line through <math|x> and fixes its orthogonal complement. Its
  induced action on two-tensors is denoted
  <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|x><rsup|<around|(|2|)>>>.

  For a symmetric coefficient tensor <math|A> of order <math|j> with two
  antisymmetric field indices, write componentwise

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|\<cal-D\><rsub|A>*f|)><rsub|i*m><around|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|<around|(|a<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,a<rsub|j>|)>\<in\><around|{|0,1,2,3|}><rsup|j>><frac|1|j!>*D<rsup|j>*f<around|(|x|)><around|[|e<rsub|a<rsub|1>>,\<ldots\>,e<rsub|a<rsub|j>>|]>*<space|0.17em>A<rsub|i*m>*<around|(|e<rsub|a<rsub|1>>,\<ldots\>,e<rsub|a<rsub|j>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The sum is over ordered derivative tuples; later the two field indices are
  summed in both orders in the Frobenius pairing. This is the normalized
  Taylor coefficient convention. The colour pairing is the fixed positive
  real inner product. Write <math|c<rsub|j>=<around|(|-2|)><rsup|j>/<around|(|4*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)>>,
  the coefficient of the four-dimensional Green function.

  We first establish the differentiated Green identity used below:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-shell-green><around|(|\<cal-D\><rsub|A>*G|)><rsub|i*m><around|(|x|)>=c<rsub|j><around|\||x|\|><rsup|-2*<around|(|j+1|)>>*A<rsub|i*m><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>x\<ne\>0,<space|1em>A\<in\><with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|j>,<space|2em>G<around|(|x|)>=<frac|1|4*\<pi\><rsup|2><around|\||x|\|><rsup|2>>.
  </equation>

  For <math|j=0> this is evaluation of the Green function. Suppose it holds
  at order <math|j> and let <math|A> have order <math|j+1>. For each
  coordinate direction <math|e<rsub|a>>, freeze the first tensor slot to get
  <math|A<rsub|a>\<in\><with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|j>>. The normalized
  Taylor coefficient satisfies

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|\<cal-D\><rsub|A>*f|)><rsub|i*m>=<frac|1|j+1>*<big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|a>*<around|(|\<cal-D\><rsub|A<rsub|a>>*f|)><rsub|i*m>.
  </equation*>

  This follows by expanding the ordered derivative tuples: each of the
  <math|j+1> possible first slots gives the same symmetric coefficient.
  Applying the induction hypothesis and differentiating gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|\<cal-D\><rsub|A>*G|)><rsub|i*m>=<frac|c<rsub|j>|j+1>*<around*|(|-2*<around|(|j+1|)><around|\||x|\|><rsup|-2*<around|(|j+2|)>>*<big|sum><rsub|a>x<rsub|a><around|(|A<rsub|a>|)><around|(|x|)>+<around|\||x|\|><rsup|-2*<around|(|j+1|)>>*<big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|a><around|(|A<rsub|a>|)><around|(|x|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  For completeness, the trace identity here is harmonicity in the derivative
  slots, not the Maxwell divergence equation alone. Write
  <math|F<rsub|i*m><around|(|x|)>> for the homogeneous field components
  represented by <math|A>. The Maxwell equations give
  <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>*F<rsub|a*i>=0> and
  <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>*F<rsub|i*m>+\<partial\><rsub|i>*F<rsub|m*a>+\<partial\><rsub|m>*F<rsub|a*i>=0>.
  Apply <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>> and sum over <math|a> in the second
  identity. The two terms involving the divergence vanish, so
  <math|\<Delta\>*F<rsub|i*m>=0>. By the normalized coefficient convention,
  <math|<around|(|A<rsub|a>|)><rsub|i*m>=<around|(|j+1|)><rsup|-1>*\<partial\><rsub|a>*F<rsub|i*m>>;
  hence

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|a><around|(|A<rsub|a>|)><rsub|i*m>=<around|(|j+1|)><rsup|-1>*\<Delta\>*F<rsub|i*m>=0.
  </equation*>

  Euler's homogeneous identity, or direct contraction of the first slot,
  gives

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|a>x<rsub|a><around|(|A<rsub|a>|)><rsub|i*m><around|(|x|)>=F<rsub|i*m><around|(|x|)>=A<rsub|i*m><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|c<rsub|j+1>=-2*c<rsub|j>>, this proves
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-shell-green>. It is an off-origin identity; no
  distributional contact term is involved.

  The Euclidean Green kernel and its two curls give a response field
  associated with <math|P>. The Kelvin calculation needed here is the
  following identity, with the normalization fixed by the Green function:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-kelvin-response>\<cal-R\><around|(|P|)><around|(|y|)>=<math-up|strength><around|(|j|)><around|\||y|\|><rsup|-4>*\<vartheta\><rsub|y><rsup|<around|(|2|)>>*P<around|(|I<around|(|y|)>|)>,<space|2em>y\<ne\>0,<space|1em><math-up|strength><around|(|j|)>=4*<around|(|j+1|)>*<around|(|j+2|)>*<around|(|-2|)><rsup|j><math-up|normal>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|<math-up|normal>=1/<around|(|4*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)>\<gtr\>0> is the
  scalar in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-shell-green>. In particular,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-strength-sign><around|(|-1|)><rsup|j><math-up|strength><around|(|j|)>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation>

  The sign follows directly from the displayed formula: the factors
  <math|4*<around|(|j+1|)>*<around|(|j+2|)>> and <math|<math-up|normal>> are
  positive and <math|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|j>*<around|(|-2|)><rsup|j>=2<rsup|j>>.

  We recall the differential reduction giving this normalization. Put
  <math|R<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a>x<rsub|a>*P<rsub|a*i><around|(|x|)>>
  and <math|c<rsub|j>=<around|(|-2|)><rsup|j><math-up|normal>>. The
  differentiated Green potential is <math|c<rsub|j><around|\||x|\|><rsup|-2*<around|(|j+1|)>>*P<rsub|a*i><around|(|x|)>>.
  The divergence-free equation removes the derivatives falling on <math|P> in
  its divergence, so the resulting vector is

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>=-2*<around|(|j+1|)>*c<rsub|j><around|\||x|\|><rsup|-2*<around|(|j+2|)>>*R<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  The curl of the field response is <math|2*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|m>*D<rsub|i>-\<partial\><rsub|i>*D<rsub|m>|)>>.
  Closedness, the Bianchi identity, and Euler homogeneity give

  <\equation*>
    \<partial\><rsub|i>*R<rsub|m><around|(|x|)>-\<partial\><rsub|m>*R<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>=<around|(|j+2|)>*P<rsub|i*m><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  Also <math|\<partial\><rsub|i><around|\||x|\|><rsup|-2*<around|(|j+2|)>>=-2*<around|(|j+2|)>*x<rsub|i><around|\||x|\|><rsup|-2*<around|(|j+3|)>>>.
  Substitution yields

  <\equation*>
    4*<around|(|j+1|)>*<around|(|j+2|)>*c<rsub|j><around|\||x|\|><rsup|-2*<around|(|j+2|)>>*<around*|[|P<rsub|i*m><around|(|x|)>-2<around|\||x|\|><rsup|-2>*<around*|(|x<rsub|i>*R<rsub|m><around|(|x|)>-x<rsub|m>*R<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>|)>|]>.
  </equation*>

  The bracket is the two-index reflection of <math|P<around|(|x|)>>.
  Homogeneity gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||x|\|><rsup|-2*<around|(|j+2|)>>*\<vartheta\><rsub|x><rsup|<around|(|2|)>>*P<around|(|x|)>=<around|\||x|\|><rsup|-4>*\<vartheta\><rsub|x><rsup|<around|(|2|)>>*P<around|(|I<around|(|x|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  This proves (<reference|eq:ex-lattice-kelvin-response>) off the origin and
  identifies its coefficient; no contact term is used.

  For an orthogonal reflection <math|s>, define its full action on a solution
  by <math|<around|(|s\<cdot\>Q|)><around|(|v|)>=s<rsup|<around|(|2|)>>*Q*<around|(|s*v|)>>:
  it acts on both field indices and every derivative slot. For
  <math|x\<ne\>0>, let <math|<math-up|Leg><rsub|x><around|(|P,Q|)>> be the
  one-leg kernel: the covariance of the probe <math|P> at a point
  <math|y<rsub|0>> with the probe <math|Q> at <math|y<rsub|0>-x>, that is,
  the second derivatives of the scalar Green kernel at separation <math|x>
  (first point minus second point) contracted with the dual probes of
  <math|P> and <math|Q>, followed by the two curls. The result does not
  depend on <math|y<rsub|0>>. The one-leg reflected pairing is

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|x><rsup|j,k>*<around|(|P,Q|)>=<math-up|Leg><rsub|x><around|(|P,\<vartheta\><rsub|x>\<cdot\>Q|)>.
  </equation*>

  Orthogonal covariance and exchange symmetry of the Green kernel imply

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|x><rsup|j,k>*<around|(|P,Q|)>=B<rsub|x><rsup|k,j>*<around|(|Q,P|)>.
  </equation*>

  The use of the reflection in the second slot is essential: this is the
  one-leg reflected form, not the unreflected covariance.

  To triangularize all grades at once, for <math|0\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>j>
  let

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\<nobracket\>|T<rsub|j,k><rsup|x>*P=<frac|<math-up|strength><around|(|j|)>|<math-up|strength><around|(|k|)>><math-up|Taylor><rsub|k><around*|(|P|(><space|0.17em>\<cdot\>-I<around|(|x|)>)|)>,<space|2em>P\<in\><with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|j>.
  </equation*>

  The Taylor coefficient is taken at the origin, and the translated
  coefficient is again a homogeneous degree-<math|k> Maxwell solution. Set

  <\equation*>
    \<Sigma\><rsub|x>*P=<big|sum><rsub|k=0><rsup|j>T<rsub|j,k><rsup|x>*P\<in\><with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|\<less\>j+1>,<space|2em>P\<in\><with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|j>.
  </equation*>

  The sum is finite. By the exact Taylor formula for a degree-<math|j>
  polynomial, for every <math|y>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-shift-taylor><big|sum><rsub|k=0><rsup|j><math-up|strength><around|(|k|)>*<around|(|T<rsub|j,k><rsup|x>*P|)><around|(|y|)>=<math-up|strength><around|(|j|)>*P*<around|(|y-I<around|(|x|)>|)>.
  </equation>

  Apply (<reference|eq:ex-lattice-kelvin-response>) term by term and use
  (<reference|eq:ex-lattice-shift-taylor>). The resulting response is
  <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|x>*<around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|x>*P|)>\<assign\><big|sum><rsub|k=0><rsup|j>\<cal-R\>*<around|(|T<rsub|j,k><rsup|x>*P|)>>.
  For <math|y> near <math|x>, inversion gives

  <\equation*>
    I*<around|(|x+t*v|)>-I<around|(|x|)>=t*<space|0.17em>V<rsub|x,v><around|(|t|)>,<space|2em>V<rsub|x,v><around|(|t|)>=<around|\||x+t*v|\|><rsup|-2>*<around*|(|\<vartheta\><rsub|x>*v-t<around|\||v|\|><rsup|2>*I<around|(|x|)>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|V<rsub|x,v>> is smooth near <math|t=0>. Since <math|P> is
  homogeneous of degree <math|j>, the shifted response along this line is
  <math|t<rsup|j>> times a smooth function. Consequently its Taylor
  coefficients through order <math|j-1> vanish, while its order-<math|j>
  coefficient is

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|strength><around|(|j|)><around|\||x|\|><rsup|-2*<around|(|j+2|)>>*<around|(|\<vartheta\><rsub|x>\<cdot\>P|)><around|(|v|)>,
  </equation*>

  where the reflected solution is defined by
  <math|<around|(|\<vartheta\><rsub|x>\<cdot\>P|)><around|(|v|)>=\<vartheta\><rsub|x><rsup|<around|(|2|)>>*P*<around|(|\<vartheta\><rsub|x>*v|)>>.
  To connect the response computation to the one-leg pairing, the two-curl
  Green identity gives

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|x>*<around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|x>*P,Q<rsub|k>|)>=<around|(|-1|)><rsup|k><around*|\<langle\>|\<vartheta\><rsub|x>\<cdot\>Q<rsub|k>,<math-up|Taylor><rsub|k><around*|(|\<cal-R\><rsub|x>*<around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|x>*P|)>|)><around|(|x|)>|\<rangle\>><rsub|F>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|Q<rsub|k>\<in\><with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|k>>, and the
  normalized Taylor tensor includes all ordered derivative tuples. The sign
  <math|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|k>> is the sign from differentiating the second
  insertion, which sits at <math|y<rsub|0>-x>: the pulse of a grade-<math|k>
  probe is odd or even under <math|y\<mapsto\>-y> according to the parity of
  <math|k>. The triangular transform below uses this orientation; with the
  opposite one the sign would attach to the components of the first argument.
  The vanishing lower Taylor coefficients imply
  <math|B<rsub|x>*<around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|x>*P,Q<rsub|k>|)>=0> when
  <math|k\<less\>j>; at <math|k=j> the order-<math|j> coefficient gives
  <math|B<rsub|x>*<around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|x>*P,Q<rsub|j>|)>=\<lambda\><rsub|j><around|(|x|)><around|\<langle\>|P,Q<rsub|j>|\<rangle\>><rsub|F>>,
  because <math|<around|(|\<vartheta\><rsub|x>\<cdot\>Q<rsub|j>,\<vartheta\><rsub|x>\<cdot\>P|)>>
  have the same Frobenius product as <math|<around|(|Q<rsub|j>,P|)>>. When
  the second input is also transformed, its components have grades at most
  its original grade, and its top component is unchanged because
  <math|T<rsub|k,k><rsup|x>> is the identity. Thus these same two identities
  give the triangular identities for the full one-leg kernel. Extend
  <math|B<rsub|x>> by bilinearity to the finite tower. If
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|\<less\>m>=<big|oplus><rsub|k\<less\>m><with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|k>>,
  then the formulas

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|B<rsub|x>*<around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|x>*P,\<Sigma\><rsub|x>*Q|)>>|<cell|=0>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|P\<in\><with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|j>,Q\<in\><with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|k>,k\<less\>j|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-shift-cross>>>|<row|<cell|B<rsub|x>*<around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|x>*P,\<Sigma\><rsub|x>*Q|)>>|<cell|=\<lambda\><rsub|j><around|(|x|)><around|\<langle\>|P,Q|\<rangle\>><rsub|F>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|P,Q\<in\><with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|j>|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-shift-diagonal>>>>>
  </align>

  where

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-positive-pivot>\<lambda\><rsub|j><around|(|x|)>=<around|(|-1|)><rsup|j><math-up|strength><around|(|j|)><around|\||x|\|><rsup|-2*<around|(|j+2|)>>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation>

  The factor <math|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|k>> in the one-leg kernel comes from
  differentiating the second insertion; at equal grade it combines with the
  Taylor coefficient to give precisely (<reference|eq:ex-lattice-positive-pivot>).

  Now fix a finite cutoff <math|n> and form the tower

  <\equation*>
    <with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|\<less\>n>=<big|oplus><rsub|j=0><rsup|n-1><with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|j>.
  </equation*>

  On this tower the reflected Gram form is

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|x>*<around|(|P,Q|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j,k\<less\>n>B<rsub|x><rsup|j,k>*<around|(|P<rsub|j>,Q<rsub|k>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Apply the triangular transform <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|x>> separately in each
  input grade. The cross-grade identities
  (<reference|eq:ex-lattice-shift-cross>) and their transposes remove every
  off-diagonal block, while (<reference|eq:ex-lattice-shift-diagonal>) gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-tower-gram>B<rsub|x>*<around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|x>*P,\<Sigma\><rsub|x>*Q|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>n>\<lambda\><rsub|j><around|(|x|)><around|\<langle\>|P<rsub|j>,Q<rsub|j>|\<rangle\>><rsub|F>.
  </equation>

  Every coefficient on the right is positive. Since each Frobenius inner
  product is positive definite, the right side is positive for every nonzero
  tower. Moreover, <math|T<rsub|j,j><rsup|x>> is the identity: the top-degree
  Taylor coefficient of <math|P*<around|(|<space|0.17em>\<cdummy\>-I<around|(|x|)>|)>>
  is <math|P>. Therefore the matrix of <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|x>> on the
  ordered grades is lower triangular with identity diagonal. Its strictly
  grade-lowering part <math|N<rsub|x>=\<Sigma\><rsub|x>-I> satisfies
  <math|N<rsub|x><rsup|n>=0>, so

  <\equation*>
    \<Sigma\><rsub|x><rsup|-1>=I-N<rsub|x>+N<rsub|x><rsup|2>-\<cdots\>+<around|(|-1|)><rsup|n-1>*N<rsub|x><rsup|n-1>.
  </equation*>

  Thus the diagonalization is an invertible change of probes, and the full
  mixed-grade Gram form is positive definite. This is a simultaneous
  statement about the entire tower; positivity of the separate diagonal
  blocks alone would not imply it.

  <subsubsection|Wick polynomials and strict reflected positivity>

  Let <math|<with|math-font|cal*|A>> be the complex polynomial algebra
  generated by the formal field-strength jet coordinates at all derivative
  orders. Evaluation on homogeneous Maxwell solutions restricts each
  polynomial to the on-shell jet space, imposing both the differential
  Maxwell equations and the algebraic Bianchi identities. The filtered shell
  sector <math|<with|math-font|cal*|Q><rsub|\<leqslant\>d>> is the image of
  this restriction map through grade <math|d>, hence is canonically the
  quotient by its kernel. Full reflection preserves each grade and this
  filtered sector. Each linear coordinate restricted to
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|j>> is a linear functional on a
  finite-dimensional inner-product space, so the finite-dimensional Riesz
  representation theorem writes it as pairing with a vector of
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|j>>. Consequently every polynomial in
  finitely many jet coordinates is a polynomial in the coordinates of one
  sufficiently large finite tower. A homogeneous solution
  <math|P\<in\><with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|j>> defines its linear Wick
  symbol <math|<math-ss|d><around|(|P|)>>. Products of these symbols generate
  the polynomial Wick algebra.

  For two distinct points <math|x,y>, put <math|z=x-y>. Let
  <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|x,y>*<around|(|A,B|)>> denote the free two-point
  moment of the polynomial insertions <math|A> and <math|B>. The reflected
  pairing is linear in its first argument and conjugate-linear in its second:

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<langle\>|A,B|\<rangle\>><rsub|x,y><rsup|<math-up|ref>>=\<cal-W\><rsub|x,y>*<around*|(|A,\<vartheta\><rsub|z>\<cdot\><wide|B|\<bar\>>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|z>> acts on every tensor index of the second
  field insertion. Complex conjugation of polynomial coefficients is a
  separate operation in this formula. The free moment is the normal-ordered
  cross-site Gaussian matching moment: only pairings joining the two distinct
  insertion sites occur. Removing one linear field factor from the first
  insertion therefore gives the sum of its contractions with the factors in
  the second insertion. This follows by separating the unique partner of the
  removed factor in the finite matching formula for a free moment. With two
  insertion points there is only one possible partner site. In orthogonal
  coordinates the identity reads

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-script-recurrence><around|\<langle\>|X<rsub|i>*p,q|\<rangle\>><rsub|x,y><rsup|<math-up|ref>>=d<rsub|i>*<around|\<langle\>|p,\<partial\><rsub|i>*q|\<rangle\>><rsub|x,y><rsup|<math-up|ref>>,<space|2em><around|\<langle\>|1,X<rsub|i>*q|\<rangle\>><rsub|x,y><rsup|<math-up|ref>>=0,<space|2em><around|\<langle\>|1,1|\<rangle\>><rsub|x,y><rsup|<math-up|ref>>=1.
  </equation>

  The first identity is the differentiated removal rule, and the second is
  the same rule with no factor available to contract on the first side.

  Choose a real orthogonal basis <math|e<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,e<rsub|M>> for the
  tower Gram form <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-tower-gram> at the separation
  <math|z=x-y>, with squared norms <math|d<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0>. Such a basis
  exists by finite-dimensional Gram\USchmidt: at each step, the orthogonal
  complement of the span already chosen is positive definite, so a nonzero
  vector in it has strictly positive squared norm; normalize or retain its
  positive norm. In the polynomial coordinates
  <math|X<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,X<rsub|M>> associated with <math|e<rsub|i>>, the
  reflected pairing satisfies

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<langle\>|X<rsup|\<alpha\>>,X<rsup|\<beta\>>|\<rangle\>><rsub|x,y><rsup|<math-up|ref>>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|M>\<alpha\><rsub|i>!d<rsub|i><rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|i>>,>|<cell|\<alpha\>=\<beta\>,>>|<row|<cell|0,>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<ne\>\<beta\>.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  For completeness, this formula follows by induction on
  <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>>. When <math|\<alpha\>=0>, the last two
  identities in (<reference|eq:ex-lattice-script-recurrence>) give <math|1>
  if <math|\<beta\>=0> and <math|0> otherwise. If
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i>\<gtr\>0>, apply the first identity with
  <math|p=X<rsup|\<alpha\>-e<rsub|i>>> and <math|q=X<rsup|\<beta\>>>:

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<langle\>|X<rsup|\<alpha\>>,X<rsup|\<beta\>>|\<rangle\>>=\<beta\><rsub|i>*d<rsub|i>*<around|\<langle\>|X<rsup|\<alpha\>-e<rsub|i>>,X<rsup|\<beta\>-e<rsub|i>>|\<rangle\>>,
  </equation*>

  where the right side is zero when <math|\<beta\><rsub|i>=0>. Induction
  shows that the pairing vanishes unless <math|\<alpha\>=\<beta\>>. When they
  agree, removing each occurrence of coordinate <math|i> contributes
  successively <math|d<rsub|i>*\<alpha\><rsub|i>,d<rsub|i>*<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|i>-1|)>,\<ldots\>,d<rsub|i>>,
  so its total factor is <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i>!d<rsub|i><rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|i>>>.
  Multiplication over coordinates gives the formula. For arbitrary
  polynomials <math|p=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>p<rsub|\<alpha\>>*X<rsup|\<alpha\>>>
  and <math|q=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>q<rsub|\<alpha\>>*X<rsup|\<alpha\>>>,
  it follows that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-wick-norm><around|\<langle\>|p,q|\<rangle\>><rsub|x,y><rsup|<math-up|ref>>=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>p<rsub|\<alpha\>><wide|q<rsub|\<alpha\>>|\<bar\>><big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|M>\<alpha\><rsub|i>!d<rsub|i><rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|i>>,<space|2em><around|\<langle\>|p,p|\<rangle\>><rsub|x,y><rsup|<math-up|ref>>=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>><around|\||p<rsub|\<alpha\>>|\|><rsup|2>*<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|M>\<alpha\><rsub|i>!d<rsub|i><rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|i>>.
  </equation>

  Each weight is strictly positive, including the empty multi-index, whose
  weight is <math|1>. Thus the reflected norm is strictly positive for every
  nonzero polynomial. Every polynomial uses only finitely many jet orders;
  choose a tower cutoff containing those orders, and the argument applies. If
  two representatives define the same shell polynomial, their difference
  restricts to zero on every solution and has zero pairing with every
  insertion. The formula is therefore well defined on the shell quotient and
  strictly positive on each nonzero class.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Strict all-grade free detector><label|thm:ex-lattice-all-grade-free-detector>For
    any two distinct Euclidean points, the reflected free two-point form on
    the polynomial algebra of gauge-invariant field-strength jets, after
    quotienting by the Maxwell equations and the Bianchi identities, is
    strictly positive: if <math|P> is a nonzero quotient class, then

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|P,P|\<rangle\>><rsub|x,y><rsup|<math-up|ref>>\<gtr\>0.
    </equation*>

    Consequently, the family of separated two-point evaluations at any fixed
    pair detects every field in each finite-dimensional grade.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    The Kelvin calculation gives the one-leg triangular identities and the
    strictly positive tower pivots in (<reference|eq:ex-lattice-shift-cross>)\U(<reference|eq:ex-lattice-positive-pivot>).
    Equation (<reference|eq:ex-lattice-tower-gram>) therefore diagonalizes
    the entire finite tower with positive diagonal entries. The removal-rule
    calculation gives (<reference|eq:ex-lattice-wick-norm>), whose summands
    are nonnegative and whose summand for any nonzero coefficient is
    positive. Every polynomial lies in a finite tower, proving strict
    positivity for each nonzero shell class.

    For the detector statement, fix a grade <math|d> and a nonzero class
    <math|v> in its free field space. Choose any separated pair. The
    reflected evaluation of <math|v> against its reflected conjugate is the
    strictly positive number just proved. Hence at least one vector in any
    spanning basis of the grade has a nonzero pairing with <math|v>;
    otherwise linearity would make its pairing with every grade-<math|d>
    vector, including the reflected conjugate of <math|v>, vanish. Thus the
    full family of basis-column evaluations is injective.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The finite point scanner>

  We spell out the map used by the construction. Fix a reference frame and a
  grade <math|d>. Choose a basis <math|b<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,b<rsub|m>> of its
  free field space with <math|b<rsub|0>=1>, the scalar unit; the remaining
  basis elements are images of vectors in that same reference frame. At a
  separated pair <math|<around|(|x,y|)>> define the row scanner

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-R\><rsub|x,y><around|(|v|)>=<around*|(|<math-up|Use><rsub|x,y><around|(|v,b<rsub|j>|)>|)><rsub|j=0><rsup|m>,<space|2em><math-up|Face><rsub|x,y><around|(|w|)>=\<vartheta\><rsub|x-y><around*|(|<wide|w|\<bar\>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here the bar is ordinary coefficient conjugation, and the reflection acts
  on all tensor indices. By the definitions of the free-field quotient and
  the reflected polynomial pairing,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-scanner-reflection><math-up|Use><rsub|x,y><around|(|v,<math-up|Face><rsub|x,y><around|(|w|)>|)>=<around|\<langle\>|v,w|\<rangle\>><rsub|x,y><rsup|<math-up|ref>>.
  </equation>

  If <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|x,y><around|(|v|)>=0>, then the bilinear form
  <math|w\<mapsto\><math-up|Use><rsub|x,y><around|(|v,w|)>> vanishes on the
  chosen basis and therefore on the whole grade. In particular, take
  <math|w=<math-up|Face><rsub|x,y><around|(|v|)>>. Equation
  (<reference|eq:ex-lattice-scanner-reflection>) and strict positivity imply
  <math|v=0>. So the scanner is injective. Notice that it is the conjugate
  and the reflection together that place the second entry in the reflected
  positive form; reflection alone or conjugation alone is not the required
  operation.

  The scalar unit and source-frame basis make this an admissible finite
  scanner for the later construction. The unit is retained as the first
  column, and the other columns use the same frame from which the free table
  was built. This proves the all-grade reference detector only. It makes no
  positivity claim about the interacting measure, and the eventual
  interacting comparison and replication bounds remain separate inputs.

  <subsection|Erasing the internal Ward kernel in the free
  theory><label|sec:free-internal-ward-erasure>

  The internal defect in a proposed rotation action has two skew-matrix
  arguments. Its vanishing requires a different test from the classification
  of vector charges. We prove that test using the free stress and the strict
  free detector already constructed in Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:free-maxwell-ward-flux>
  and <reference|sec:ex-lattice-all-grade-free-detectors>.

  Let <math|V<rsub|d>> be the complex space of gauge-invariant Maxwell jet
  polynomials of weighted degree at most <math|d>, modulo the Maxwell and
  Bianchi relations. A jet coordinate of order <math|r> has weight
  <math|r+2>. We identify the inclusions <math|V<rsub|k>\<hookrightarrow\>V<rsub|d>>
  with inclusions of polynomial spaces. Write
  <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|x>*<around|(|P<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,P<rsub|n>|)>> for
  their separated free Wick moment, and <math|\<rho\><around|(|g|)>> for the
  action of <math|g\<in\>O<around|(|4|)>> on the jets. The real structure is
  complex conjugation of the polynomial coefficients and the real jets. For
  <math|A\<in\><with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)>>, denote the
  infinitesimal action by <math|L<rsub|A>>; it preserves each
  <math|V<rsub|d>>. The stress polynomials <math|<math-ss|T><rsub|a*b>>
  belong to <math|V<rsub|4>>.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Vanishing of the internal Ward
    defect><label|thm:free-internal-erasure>Suppose the gauge group is
    compact and simple and <math|d\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, <math|d\<geqslant\>4>.
    Let

    <\equation*>
      T:<with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)>\<times\><with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)>\<longrightarrow\><math-up|End><rsub|\<bbb-C\>><around|(|V<rsub|d>|)>
    </equation*>

    be real bilinear. Suppose that <math|T*<around|(|A,A|)>=0>, that every
    <math|T*<around|(|A,B|)>> kills the unit and commutes with the real
    structure, and that it preserves every <math|V<rsub|k>> with
    <math|0\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>d>. Suppose also that for each
    determinant-one signed coordinate transformation <math|g>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:internal-signed-covariance>\<rho\><around|(|g|)>*T*<around|(|A,B|)>*P=T*<around|(|g*A*g<rsup|-1>,g*B*g<rsup|-1>|)>*\<rho\><around|(|g|)>*P.
    </equation>

    If for every <math|n>, every separated
    <math|x=<around|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>>, every
    <math|P<rsub|i>\<in\>V<rsub|d>> and all <math|A,B>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:internal-zero-ward-row><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<cal-M\><rsub|x>*<around|(|P<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,T*<around|(|A,B|)>*P<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,P<rsub|n>|)>=0,
    </equation>

    then <math|T=0>.
  </theorem>

  The quantifier over spectators in <eqref|eq:internal-zero-ward-row> is
  essential: the word length is arbitrary, although every label belongs to
  the same finite grade <math|d>. We first work entirely in grade four, and
  then use the stress flux to reach all of <math|V<rsub|d>>.

  <subsubsection|Changing the direction of a grade-four pairing>

  Put

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|x>*<around|(|P,Q|)>=\<cal-M\><rsub|<around|(|x,0|)>>*<around|(|P,Q|)>*<space|2em><around|(|x\<ne\>0|)>,<space|2em>h<rsub|x>=I-2*<frac|x*x<rsup|<math-ss|t>>|<around|\||x|\|><rsup|2>>.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|h<rsub|x>> is the orthogonal reflection in the hyperplane
  perpendicular to <math|x>. On zero-order two-form probes, the covariance
  <eqref|eq:maxwell-ward-covariance> is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:internal-house-covariance>C<around|(|x|)>=4*\<nu\><around|\||x|\|><rsup|-4><space|0.17em><big|wedge><rsup|2>h<rsub|x>\<otimes\>I<rsub|\<frak-g\>>.
  </equation>

  Here the exterior square acts on the two-form indices, with their usual
  orthonormal basis <math|e<rsub|a>\<wedge\>e<rsub|b>>, <math|a\<less\>b>. To
  check the coefficient and sign, use orthogonal covariance to take
  <math|x=r*e<rsub|0>>. The Hessian of <math|\<nu\><around|\||x|\|><rsup|-2>>
  has diagonal entries <math|6*\<nu\>*r<rsup|-4>> in direction <math|0> and
  <math|-2*\<nu\>*r<rsup|-4>> in the other directions. Substitution in
  <eqref|eq:maxwell-ward-covariance> gives <math|-4*\<nu\>*r<rsup|-4>> on
  <math|e<rsub|0>\<wedge\>e<rsub|b>>, <math|4*\<nu\>*r<rsup|-4>> on
  <math|e<rsub|a>\<wedge\>e<rsub|b>> with <math|a,b\<ne\>0>, and zero off the
  diagonal. These are exactly the eigenvalues in
  <eqref|eq:internal-house-covariance>.

  Consequently the covariance of <math|\<rho\><around|(|h<rsub|x>|)>*p> at
  <math|x> with a zero-order probe <math|q> at <math|0> depends on <math|x>
  only through <math|<around|\||x|\|>>. This extends to products of such
  probes by Wick's rule: same-insertion contractions are zero, and every
  cross-contraction has the stated invariance. It extends further by
  linearity to their span. The low-grade decomposition in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-free-rank> says that
  <math|V<rsub|4>> is spanned by the unit and invariant quadratic products of
  zero-order curvature probes. We therefore obtain, for
  <math|<around|\||x|\|>=<around|\||y|\|>\<gtr\>0>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:internal-grade-four-direction>B<rsub|x>*<around|(|P,Q|)>=B<rsub|y>*<around*|(|\<rho\>*<around|(|h<rsub|y>*h<rsub|x>|)>*P,Q|)><space|2em><around|(|P,Q\<in\>V<rsub|4>|)>.
  </equation>

  Indeed apply the preceding Wick identity to
  <math|\<rho\><around|(|h<rsub|x>|)>*P>, using <math|h<rsub|x><rsup|2>=I>.
  This argument concerns zero-order probes; it makes no assertion that
  <eqref|eq:internal-grade-four-direction> holds on higher jet sectors.

  The form <math|B<rsub|-r*e<rsub|a>>> is nondegenerate on every
  <math|V<rsub|k>> when <math|r\<gtr\>0>. To see this, translate the pair
  <math|<around|(|-r*e<rsub|a>,0|)>> to <math|<around|(|-r*e<rsub|a>/2,r*e<rsub|a>/2|)>>.
  If <math|P\<ne\>0>, its reflected conjugate lies in the same
  <math|V<rsub|k>>, and its pairing with <math|P> is strictly positive by
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-all-grade-free-detector>. In
  particular, vanishing of <math|B<rsub|-r*e<rsub|a>>*<around|(|P,Q|)>> for
  every <math|Q\<in\>V<rsub|k>> implies <math|P=0>.

  Fix <math|A,B> and abbreviate <math|U=T*<around|(|A,B|)>>. The two-point
  case of <eqref|eq:internal-zero-ward-row> is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:internal-two-point-skew>B<rsub|x>*<around|(|U*P,Q|)>+B<rsub|x>*<around|(|P,U*Q|)>=0.
  </equation>

  Choose <math|x=-r*e<rsub|a>> and <math|y> with <math|<around|\||y|\|>=r>,
  and set <math|s=h<rsub|x>*h<rsub|y>>. Since <math|U> preserves
  <math|V<rsub|4>>, the identity at <math|y>, followed by
  <eqref|eq:internal-grade-four-direction>, gives

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|x>*<around|(|\<rho\><around|(|s|)>*U*P,Q|)>+B<rsub|x>*<around|(|\<rho\><around|(|s|)>*P,U*Q|)>=0.
  </equation*>

  Subtract the identity at <math|x> for <math|\<rho\><around|(|s|)>*P,Q>.
  Nondegeneracy on <math|V<rsub|4>> then yields

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:internal-low-commutation>\<rho\><around|(|s|)>*U*P=U*\<rho\><around|(|s|)>*P*<space|2em><around|(|P\<in\>V<rsub|4>|)>.
  </equation>

  For <math|a\<ne\>b>, take <math|y=e<rsub|a>+e<rsub|b>> and
  <math|r=<sqrt|2>>. Direct application of the reflection formula shows that
  <math|s=h<rsub|-r*e<rsub|a>>*h<rsub|e<rsub|a>+e<rsub|b>>> is the
  quarter-turn <math|q<rsub|a*b>> sending <math|e<rsub|a>> to
  <math|-e<rsub|b>>, <math|e<rsub|b>> to <math|e<rsub|a>> and fixing the
  other axes. Thus <math|U> commutes with all these quarter-turns on
  <math|V<rsub|4>>.

  <subsubsection|An alternating tensor killed by quarter-turns>

  We use the following elementary fact. If <math|W> is a real vector space
  and <math|F:<with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)>\<times\><with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)>\<to\>W>
  is bilinear, alternating and invariant under simultaneous conjugation by
  every <math|q<rsub|a*b>>, then <math|F=0>.

  Here is a basis proof, which also explains the role of four dimensions.
  Write <math|E<rsub|a*b>=e<rsub|a>*e<rsub|b><rsup|<math-ss|t>>-e<rsub|b>*e<rsub|a><rsup|<math-ss|t>>>,
  so that <math|E<rsub|b*a>=-E<rsub|a*b>>. Repeated arguments vanish by
  alternation. For <math|F*<around|(|E<rsub|a*b>,E<rsub|a*c>|)>> with
  <math|a,b,c> distinct, choose the fourth axis <math|l>. The half-turn
  <math|q<rsub|c*l><rsup|2>> fixes <math|E<rsub|a*b>> and negates
  <math|E<rsub|a*c>>. Invariance therefore makes this value equal to its
  negative, hence zero. Changing the signs or order of indices covers every
  pair with a common axis. For four distinct indices <math|a,b,i,l>,
  conjugation by <math|q<rsub|a*i>> sends
  <math|<around|(|E<rsub|a*b>,E<rsub|i*l>|)>> to
  <math|<around|(|-E<rsub|i*b>,E<rsub|a*l>|)>>, and conjugation by
  <math|q<rsub|b*l>> sends <math|<around|(|E<rsub|i*b>,E<rsub|a*l>|)>> to
  <math|<around|(|-E<rsub|i*l>,E<rsub|a*b>|)>>. Hence

  <\equation*>
    F*<around|(|E<rsub|a*b>,E<rsub|i*l>|)>=-F*<around|(|E<rsub|i*b>,E<rsub|a*l>|)>=F*<around|(|E<rsub|i*l>,E<rsub|a*b>|)>=-F*<around|(|E<rsub|a*b>,E<rsub|i*l>|)>.
  </equation*>

  This value is zero as well. Bilinearity and the fact that the
  <math|E<rsub|a*b>> span <math|<with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)>>
  finish the proof.

  For <math|P\<in\>V<rsub|4>>, apply this fact to
  <math|F<rsub|P>*<around|(|A,B|)>=T*<around|(|A,B|)>*P>.
  Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:internal-low-commutation> and signed covariance
  give

  <\equation*>
    T*<around|(|A,B|)>*\<rho\><around|(|q<rsub|a*b>|)>*Q=T*<around|(|q<rsub|a*b>*A*q<rsub|a*b><rsup|-1>,q<rsub|a*b>*B*q<rsub|a*b><rsup|-1>|)>*\<rho\><around|(|q<rsub|a*b>|)>*Q.
  </equation*>

  The quarter-turn acts bijectively on <math|V<rsub|4>>, so take
  <math|Q=\<rho\><around|(|q<rsub|a*b>|)><rsup|-1>*P>. This proves the
  invariance required for <math|F<rsub|P>>. We conclude that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:internal-stress-annihilation>T*<around|(|A,B|)>*P=0*<space|1em><around|(|P\<in\>V<rsub|4>|)>,<space|2em>T*<around|(|A,B|)><math-ss|T><rsub|a*b>=0.
  </equation>

  <subsubsection|The flux commutator at the same spectator capacity>

  We now prove the step which propagates this conclusion. Let <math|U> be any
  endomorphism of <math|V<rsub|d>> with zero Ward rows and
  <math|U<math-ss|T><rsub|a*b>=0>. For a fixed
  <math|K\<in\><with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)>> and
  <math|P\<in\>V<rsub|d>>, we claim that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:internal-commutator-null>\<cal-M\><rsub|x>*<around|(|<around|[|L<rsub|K>,U|]>*P,Q<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,Q<rsub|m>|)>=0
  </equation>

  for every separated <math|x=<around|(|x<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|m>|)>> and
  all <math|Q<rsub|i>\<in\>V<rsub|d>>. Choose a smooth compactly supported
  cutoff <math|\<chi\>> equal to one near <math|x<rsub|0>>, whose support
  avoids the other points. Use the rotational vector field
  <math|v<around|(|y|)>=K*<around|(|y-x<rsub|0>|)>> in the polynomial flux
  identity <eqref|eq:maxwell-polynomial-ward>. All its integrals are finite:
  the weight <math|d*\<chi\>> has compact support separated from all
  insertions, and each moment is smooth there.

  Write <math|\<b-up-P\>=<around|(|P,Q<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,Q<rsub|m>|)>>, and
  let <math|\<b-up-P\><rsup|<around|(|i|)>>> be this word with <math|U>
  applied in slot <math|i>. At each point on the cutoff collar, apply the
  zero Ward row for <math|U> to the enlarged word consisting of the stress
  insertion and <math|\<b-up-P\>>. This is a permitted row at grade <math|d>
  because <math|d\<geqslant\>4>. Its term with <math|U> on the stress is
  zero. The remaining terms say that the sum, over <math|i>, of the stress
  moments with words <math|\<b-up-P\><rsup|<around|(|i|)>>> vanishes.
  Multiply by <math|v<rsub|b>*\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\>>, sum over
  <math|a,b>, and integrate. The finite sum may be interchanged with the
  integrals by the compact smoothness just noted. The polynomial flux
  identity at the distinguished slot <math|0> then gives

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-M\><rsub|x>*<around|(|L<rsub|K>*U*P,Q<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,Q<rsub|m>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>\<cal-M\><rsub|x>*<around|(|L<rsub|K>*P,Q<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,U*Q<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,Q<rsub|m>|)>=0.
  </equation*>

  On the other hand the zero Ward row applied to the word
  <math|<around|(|L<rsub|K>*P,Q<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,Q<rsub|m>|)>> gives the
  same equation with <math|U*L<rsub|K>*P> in place of <math|L<rsub|K>*U*P>.
  This second application stays at grade <math|d>, since <math|L<rsub|K>>
  preserves <math|V<rsub|d>>. Subtracting proves
  <eqref|eq:internal-commutator-null>.

  The commutator <math|<around|[|L<rsub|K>,U|]>*P> itself lies in
  <math|V<rsub|d>>. In <eqref|eq:internal-commutator-null>, take one
  spectator to be its reflected conjugate at an axis-separated point. Strict
  reflection positivity forces the commutator to vanish. Thus

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:internal-infinitesimal-commutation>L<rsub|K>*U=U*L<rsub|K><space|1em><text|on
    >V<rsub|d>.
  </equation>

  The preservation of grade was used twice: to apply the second Ward row and
  to remove a vector detected by spectators of that same grade. No increase
  in the spectator capacity is hidden in this argument.

  <\render-proof|Completion of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:free-internal-erasure>>
    For <math|U=T*<around|(|A,B|)>>, <eqref|eq:internal-stress-annihilation>
    supplies the stress hypothesis of the preceding argument. Consequently
    <math|U> commutes with every <math|L<rsub|K>> on <math|V<rsub|d>>. For
    fixed <math|P>, the functions

    <\equation*>
      f<around|(|t|)>=\<rho\><around|(|e<rsup|t*K>|)>*U*P,<space|2em>g<around|(|t|)>=U*\<rho\><around|(|e<rsup|t*K>|)>*P
    </equation*>

    solve <math|f<rprime|'>=L<rsub|K>*f>, <math|g<rprime|'>=L<rsub|K>*g> and
    have the same value at zero. Uniqueness is elementary in finite
    dimension: the derivative of <math|e<rsup|-t*L<rsub|K>>*<around|(|f<around|(|t|)>-g<around|(|t|)>|)>>
    is zero, so this difference is identically zero. Taking
    <math|K=E<rsub|a*b>> and <math|t=\<pi\>/2> shows that <math|U> commutes
    with <math|\<rho\><around|(|q<rsub|a*b>|)>> on all of <math|V<rsub|d>>.
    For arbitrary <math|P\<in\>V<rsub|d>>, signed covariance and the
    surjectivity of <math|\<rho\><around|(|q<rsub|a*b>|)>> now make
    <math|<around|(|A,B|)>\<mapsto\>T*<around|(|A,B|)>*P> quarter-turn
    invariant, exactly as in grade four. The alternating-tensor argument
    proves it zero. Since <math|P> was arbitrary, <math|T=0>.
  </render-proof>

  <subsection|Separating the harmonic residue of a free
  collision><label|sec:free-collision-pole-separation>

  A quadratic probe approaching a polynomial insertion can contract once or
  twice with that insertion. The double contractions are generally more
  singular. We isolate the single-contraction residue by its harmonicity and
  its precise radial denominator, rather than by assuming that the unmodified
  rescaled moment has a limit. This is the collision statement needed for the
  finite-capacity free detector. All orders, cutoffs and capacities
  <math|r,s,j,k,l,d,M> used below are nonnegative integers; pole orders
  satisfy <math|r\<geqslant\>2>.

  <subsubsection|Harmonic poles and radial clearing>

  For <math|z\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>\<setminus\><around|{|0|}>> put
  <math|R<around|(|z|)>=<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>>. A complex function
  <math|f> is a harmonic pole of order <math|r\<geqslant\>2> if it is smooth
  and harmonic away from zero, satisfies

  <\equation*>
    f*<around|(|t*z|)>=t<rsup|-r>*f<around|(|z|)>*<space|2em><around|(|t\<gtr\>0|)>,
  </equation*>

  and <math|R<rsup|r-1>*f> extends smoothly to all of
  <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>. We say that <math|f> can be cleared with <math|s>
  radial powers if <math|R<rsup|s>*f> has such a smooth extension. The same
  definitions apply componentwise to finite-dimensional vector-valued
  functions.

  The covariance of a probe of jet order <math|k> with one of order <math|s>
  is a harmonic pole of order <math|4+k+s>. Indeed, the curvature covariance
  is obtained by two derivatives and linear combinations of
  <math|G<around|(|z|)>=\<nu\><around|\||z|\|><rsup|-2>>, and higher jet
  probes give further normalized constant-direction derivatives. The function
  <math|G> is a pole of order two. If <math|f> is a pole of order <math|r>
  and <math|g=R<rsup|r-1>*f> is its smooth extension, then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:collision-derivative-numerator>R<rsup|r>*\<partial\><rsub|u>*f=R*\<partial\><rsub|u>*g-<around|(|r-1|)>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|u>*R|)>*g*<space|2em><around|(|z\<ne\>0|)>.
  </equation>

  This gives a smooth extension of the new numerator. Differentiating the
  scaling identity gives degree <math|-<around|(|r+1|)>>, and constant
  derivatives commute with the Laplacian. Thus <math|\<partial\><rsub|u>*f>
  is a pole of order <math|r+1>. Induction, followed by finite linear
  combinations, proves the assertion for all probes, including their Taylor
  normalization factors.

  The following strict separation is the essential point.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|One extra radial cancellation forces
    zero><label|lem:collision-radial-separation>A harmonic pole <math|f> of
    order <math|r\<geqslant\>2> that can be cleared with <math|r-2> radial
    powers vanishes away from zero.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Euler's identity is <math|z\<cdot\>\<nabla\>*f=-r*f>. Since
    <math|\<Delta\>*f=0>, the product rule in four dimensions gives, for
    <math|s\<geqslant\>0>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:collision-radial-laplacian>\<Delta\>*<around|(|R<rsup|s+1>*f|)>=4*<around|(|s+1|)>*<around|(|s+2-r|)>*R<rsup|s>*f.
    </equation>

    For completeness, <math|\<nabla\>*R<rsup|a>=2*a*R<rsup|a-1>*z> and
    <math|\<Delta\>*R<rsup|a>=4*a*<around|(|a+1|)>*R<rsup|a-1>>. The cross
    term in the product Laplacian is therefore
    <math|4*a*R<rsup|a-1>*<around|(|z\<cdot\>\<nabla\>*f|)>>; taking
    <math|a=s+1> proves the formula.

    Suppose <math|R<rsup|s+1>*f> has a smooth extension and
    <math|s+1\<less\>r-1>. The coefficient in
    <eqref|eq:collision-radial-laplacian> is nonzero. Apply the Laplacian to
    that extension and divide by the coefficient; this gives a smooth
    extension of <math|R<rsup|s>*f>. Starting at <math|r-2> and repeating, we
    obtain a smooth extension <math|g> of <math|f> itself. For fixed
    <math|z\<ne\>0>, homogeneity now gives

    <\equation*>
      f<around|(|z|)>=t<rsup|r>*g*<around|(|t*z|)>\<longrightarrow\>0*<space|2em><around|(|t*\<downarrow\>*0|)>.
    </equation*>

    Thus <math|f<around|(|z|)>=0>. When <math|r=2>, the initial clearing
    already supplies <math|g>, so the same last argument applies directly.
  </proof>

  Products have precisely the weaker clearing property which this lemma
  excludes for a nonzero harmonic pole. If <math|f<rsub|1>,f<rsub|2>> are
  poles of orders <math|r<rsub|1>,r<rsub|2>\<geqslant\>2>, their product is
  homogeneous of degree <math|-<around|(|r<rsub|1>+r<rsub|2>|)>> and

  <\equation*>
    R<rsup|r<rsub|1>+r<rsub|2>-2>*f<rsub|1>*f<rsub|2>=<around|(|R<rsup|r<rsub|1>-1>*f<rsub|1>|)>*<around|(|R<rsup|r<rsub|2>-1>*f<rsub|2>|)>
  </equation*>

  extends smoothly. Harmonicity of the product is not asserted. Finite sums
  with the same degree have the same properties.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Extraction in the presence of product
    poles><label|lem:collision-product-separation>Let <math|H> be a harmonic
    pole of order <math|r\<geqslant\>2>. Suppose finitely many functions
    <math|W<rsub|q>>, indexed by nonnegative integers <math|q\<less\>M>, are
    homogeneous of degree <math|-q> and can be cleared with <math|max
    <around|(|q-2,0|)>> radial powers. If for every <math|z\<ne\>0>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:collision-residue-expansion>lim<rsub|t*\<downarrow\>*0>
      t<rsup|r>*<around*|(|F*<around|(|t*z|)>-<big|sum><rsub|q\<less\>M>W<rsub|q>*<around|(|t*z|)>|)>=H<around|(|z|)>,
    </equation>

    and <math|F*<around|(|t*z|)>=0> for all sufficiently small positive
    <math|t> (with the threshold allowed to depend on <math|z>), then
    <math|H=0> away from zero.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The hypotheses give <math|t<rsup|r>*<big|sum><rsub|q\<less\>M>W<rsub|q>*<around|(|t*z|)>\<to\>-H<around|(|z|)>>.
    If the largest index <math|q> is strictly greater than <math|r>,
    multiplication by <math|t<rsup|q-r>> makes this limit zero, while
    homogeneity shows that the same expression tends to
    <math|W<rsub|q><around|(|z|)>>: every smaller exponent has an extra
    positive power of <math|t>. Thus <math|W<rsub|q>> is zero away from zero
    and can be deleted. Repeat finitely many times. At the end only indices
    at most <math|r> remain. Their rescaled limit is <math|W<rsub|r>>, if
    that index occurs, and zero otherwise. Hence <math|H=-W<rsub|r>> or
    <math|H=0>. In the first case <math|H> can be cleared with <math|r-2>
    radial powers, so Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:collision-radial-separation>
    applies.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Contraction derivations at a fixed jet cutoff>

  We use the free Wick polynomial algebra underlying
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:free-maxwell-ward-flux>. Let
  <math|X<rsub|s,\<alpha\>>> be coordinates of the real Maxwell jet space of
  order <math|s>, complexified for polynomial coefficients. The index
  <math|\<alpha\>> includes colour. Let <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|j>> be the
  subalgebra using coordinates only of orders <math|s\<leqslant\>j>. This is
  a cutoff on jet order, distinct from the weighted grade <math|d>, where
  <math|X<rsub|s,\<alpha\>>> has weight <math|s+2>.

  For a linear probe <math|p> of order <math|k>, let
  <math|K*<around|(|p,e<rsub|s,\<alpha\>>;z|)>> be its covariance with the
  coordinate probe at separation <math|z>. Define

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|p,s><around|(|z|)>*P=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>K*<around|(|p,e<rsub|s,\<alpha\>>;z|)><frac|\<partial\>*P|\<partial\>*X<rsub|s,\<alpha\>>>,<space|2em>D<rsub|p><around|(|z|)>*P=<big|sum><rsub|s=0><rsup|j>D<rsub|p,s><around|(|z|)>*P*<space|1em><around|(|P\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsub|j>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The second expression is the Wick contraction derivation of <math|p> with
  <math|P>. The definitions agree on nested cutoffs because derivatives in
  unused coordinates vanish. Both derivations preserve
  <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|j>>. Each coefficient in <math|D<rsub|p,s>> is a pole
  of order <math|4+k+s>.

  Let <math|Q<rsub|z>> be any multilinear functional of a finite word of
  fixed polynomials such that its evaluation on each fixed word is continuous
  at <math|z=0>. Contracting <math|p> with one slot
  <math|P\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsub|j>> gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:collision-single-leading>lim<rsub|t*\<downarrow\>*0>
    t<rsup|4+k+j>*Q<rsub|t*z>*<around|(|\<ldots\>,D<rsub|p>*<around|(|t*z|)>*P,\<ldots\>|)>=Q<rsub|0>*<around|(|\<ldots\>,D<rsub|p,j><around|(|z|)>*P,\<ldots\>|)>.
  </equation>

  To verify this, expand the finite sum over <math|s,\<alpha\>>. Homogeneity
  extracts <math|t<rsup|-4-k-s>> from the covariance coefficient. The
  remaining functional converges by continuity. Terms with <math|s\<less\>j>
  acquire a factor <math|t<rsup|j-s>> and vanish, while those with <math|s=j>
  give the stated limit. The right side, as a function of <math|z>, is a
  harmonic pole of order <math|4+k+j>, being a finite constant linear
  combination of such poles.

  If two probes <math|p,q> of orders <math|k,l> both contract with the same
  slot, then a fixed linear evaluation <math|Q> of that slot gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:collision-double-poles>Q*<around|(|D<rsub|q><around|(|z|)>*D<rsub|p><around|(|z|)>*P|)>=<big|sum><rsub|s,b\<leqslant\>j><big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>,\<beta\>>K*<around|(|p,e<rsub|s,\<alpha\>>;z|)>*K*<around|(|q,e<rsub|b,\<beta\>>;z|)>*Q*<space|-0.17em><around*|(|\<partial\><rsub|X<rsub|b,\<beta\>>>*\<partial\><rsub|X<rsub|s,\<alpha\>>>*P|)>.
  </equation>

  The derivations act on polynomial coordinates, not on <math|z>, so no
  covariance coefficient is differentiated here. Every summand is a product
  pole of order <math|8+k+l+s+b>, hence at most <math|8+k+l+2*j>. Group the
  finitely many terms of each degree into <math|W<rsub|q>>. Their radial
  clearing property is the one proved above. These are the terms which cannot
  be discarded by a simple collision limit.

  <subsubsection|The two-probe residue and null observables>

  Fix a separated configuration <math|x=<around|(|x<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>>,
  a polynomial <math|P\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsub|j>> at <math|x<rsub|0>>, and
  arbitrary polynomial spectators <math|P<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,P<rsub|n>>.
  Insert the Wick product <math|p*q> at <math|x<rsub|0>+z>, where <math|p,q>
  have orders <math|k,l> with <math|l\<less\>k>. Write <math|F<around|(|z|)>>
  for this enlarged moment and put <math|r=4+k+j>.

  Wick expansion contracts the two probe factors into the original word; they
  cannot contract with one another at their own insertion. There are four
  cases. If both hit <math|P>, their contribution is exactly
  <eqref|eq:collision-double-poles>, with <math|Q> the moment of the
  unchanged spectators. If <math|p> hits <math|P> and <math|q> hits a
  spectator, the latter contraction is continuous at <math|z=0>, since the
  spectator point differs from <math|x<rsub|0>>. Apply
  <eqref|eq:collision-single-leading> to obtain the order-<math|r> residue.
  If <math|q> hits <math|P> and <math|p> hits a spectator, the analogous
  residue has order <math|4+l+j\<less\>r> and therefore disappears after
  multiplication by <math|t<rsup|r>>. Finally, if both hit spectators, every
  involved kernel is smooth near <math|z=0>, so this contribution disappears
  as well. These statements also cover two hits on the same spectator: the
  polynomial derivatives are finite and both covariance factors stay smooth
  near zero.

  Sum the surviving terms in the second case. The elementary one-leg Wick
  identity says that summing all contractions of <math|q> with the spectators
  is the same as adjoining the factor <math|q> to the polynomial at
  <math|x<rsub|0>>. Hence the residue is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:collision-two-probe-residue>H<around|(|z|)>=\<cal-M\><rsub|x>*<around*|(|q*D<rsub|p,j><around|(|z|)>*P,P<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,P<rsub|n>|)>.
  </equation>

  All sums are finite. We have proved <eqref|eq:collision-residue-expansion>,
  with this harmonic pole <math|H> and the double-hit product poles
  <math|W<rsub|q>>, for any <math|M\<gtr\>8+k+l+2*j>. For each fixed
  direction <math|z\<ne\>0>, the enlarged configuration is separated for all
  sufficiently small positive <math|t>: <math|t*z\<ne\>0> separates it from
  <math|x<rsub|0>>, and convergence to <math|x<rsub|0>> separates it from the
  finitely many other points.

  Call a polynomial <math|P> <em|null at capacity <math|d>> if

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-M\><rsub|x>*<around|(|P,P<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,P<rsub|n>|)>=0
  </equation*>

  for every finite number of spectators, every separated configuration, and
  all gauge-invariant spectator polynomials of weighted grade at most
  <math|d>. There is no bound on the number of spectators.

  To make the two-probe insertion gauge invariant, choose uncoloured linear
  probes <math|p,q> of orders <math|k,l> and an orthonormal colour basis.
  Write <math|p<rsub|c>,q<rsub|c>> for the probes in colour <math|c>. Their
  quadratic contraction <math|<big|sum><rsub|c>p<rsub|c>*q<rsub|c>> is
  invariant and has weighted grade <math|k+l+4>. Define the circulation
  polynomial

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-C\><rsub|j;p,q><around|(|z|)>*P=<big|sum><rsub|c>q<rsub|c>*D<rsub|p<rsub|c>,j><around|(|z|)>*P.
  </equation*>

  The covariance is the identity in colour times the scalar jet covariance,
  so changing orthonormal colour basis leaves both this expression and the
  quadratic insertion unchanged.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Circulation preserves the null
    test><label|thm:free-circulation-null>Let <math|P\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsub|j>>
    be null at capacity <math|d>. If <math|l\<less\>k> and
    <math|k+l+4\<leqslant\>d>, then for each <math|z\<ne\>0> the polynomial
    <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|j;p,q><around|(|z|)>*P> is null at capacity
    <math|d>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Fix any allowed spectators and separated configuration. Sum the preceding
    residue expansions over the finite colour basis. The residue is now the
    moment with first insertion <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|j;p,q><around|(|z|)>*P>.
    The enlarged moment <math|F*<around|(|t*z|)>> is zero for all
    sufficiently small positive <math|t>: reorder its slots so that <math|P>
    is first and treat <math|<big|sum><rsub|c>p<rsub|c>*q<rsub|c>> as one
    additional spectator. Its grade is at most <math|d>, and all the other
    spectators were already admissible. This uses only the null condition at
    the original capacity. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:collision-product-separation>
    therefore annihilates the residue, since <math|r=4+k+j\<geqslant\>4>. As
    the original spectators and configuration were arbitrary, this proves the
    null test for the circulation at every fixed nonzero <math|z>.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Free external detection, quiet charges, and collinear
  tests><label|sec:physical-ward-free-classification>

  This section proves three free-theory inputs used in the selected
  finite-level Ward argument: a uniform finite external detector, the
  classification of quiet vector charges, and reduction to finitely many
  collinear tests. Internal Ward-map erasure is treated separately in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:free-internal-ward-erasure>. The arguments
  here use finite generation of a polynomial ideal, explicit Wick collision
  limits, the strict all-grade free detector, and the signed symmetries of
  four coordinates.

  <subsubsection|Polynomial grading and a finite external detector>

  Write <math|X<rsub|r,\<alpha\>>> for a polynomial coordinate of jet order
  <math|r\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>; the finite color-slot indices
  <math|\<alpha\>> and the four coordinate axes carry their fixed finite
  orderings. Assign weight <math|r+2> to this coordinate. Let
  <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|j>> be the polynomial subalgebra generated by
  coordinates of jet order at most <math|j>, and let <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|D>>
  be the gauge-invariant observables of weighted degree at most <math|D>.
  Denote by <math|\<pi\><rsub|k>> projection to ordinary polynomial length
  <math|k>, counting each coordinate as one factor. For
  <math|D,k\<geqslant\>0>, define <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|D,k>> as the linear
  span of products <math|v<rsub|1>*\<cdots\>*v<rsub|m>>, where each
  <math|v<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|D>> is homogeneous of ordinary length
  <math|k<rsub|i>> and <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>k<rsub|i>=k>; the empty product
  is allowed when <math|k=0>.

  An observable <math|p> is <em|null at capacity <math|D>> if

  <\equation>
    \<cal-M\>*<around|(|x;p,v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|m>|)>=0<space|1em><text|for
    every >m,x\<in\><math-up|Conf><rsub|m+1>,v<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|D>.<label|eq:free-null-capacity>
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<cal-M\>> is the free Euclidean moment and <math|p> is the
  distinguished first insertion. This quantifies over arbitrary finite
  spectator words, all separated positions, and all spectator observables in
  the fixed capacity.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Uniform generation at bounded jet
    order><label|lem:free-uniform-generation>For each <math|j> there is
    <math|D<rsub|j>\<less\>\<infty\>> such that every gauge-invariant
    <math|p\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsub|j>> homogeneous of ordinary length <math|k>
    belongs to <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|D<rsub|j>,k>>, for every <math|k>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    There are finitely many coordinates of jet order at most <math|j>, so
    <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|j>> is a polynomial ring in finitely many variables
    and is Noetherian. Let <math|I> be the ideal generated by all
    positive-length, homogeneous, gauge-invariant elements of
    <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|j>>. Choose finite generators
    <math|g<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,g<rsub|s>> of this ideal from that generating
    family, and let <math|D<rsub|j>> bound their weighted degrees. Each
    <math|g<rsub|i>> has positive ordinary length <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>>.

    Induct on <math|k>. Constants are in <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|D<rsub|j>,0>>.
    For positive length, an invariant homogeneous <math|p> is in <math|I>, so
    write it as a finite sum <math|p=<big|sum><rsub|i>r<rsub|i>*g<rsub|i>>.
    Let <math|<math-up|Av>> be compact-group averaging. Since <math|p> and
    <math|g<rsub|i>> are invariant and averaging commutes with length
    projection,

    <\equation*>
      p=<big|sum><rsub|i><math-up|Av><around*|(|\<pi\><rsub|k-\<ell\><rsub|i>><around|(|r<rsub|i>|)>|)>*g<rsub|i>,
    </equation*>

    where terms with <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>\<gtr\>k> are zero. Indeed,
    projecting <math|r<rsub|i>*g<rsub|i>> to length <math|k> gives exactly
    <math|\<pi\><rsub|k-\<ell\><rsub|i>><around|(|r<rsub|i>|)>*g<rsub|i>>, by
    additivity of ordinary degree. Each averaged first factor is invariant,
    homogeneous, and of length <math|k-\<ell\><rsub|i>\<less\>k>. The
    induction hypothesis puts it in <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|D<rsub|j>,k-\<ell\><rsub|i>>>,
    and multiplication by <math|g<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|D<rsub|j>>>
    puts the term in <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|D<rsub|j>,k>>. This proves the
    claim with a capacity independent of <math|k>.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Coalescence of bounded spectators><label|lem:free-coalescence>If
    <math|p> is null at capacity <math|D> and <math|\<pi\><rsub|\<ell\>>*p=0>
    for every <math|\<ell\>\<less\>k>, then for any
    <math|q\<in\>\<cal-H\><rsub|D,k>> and any <math|x\<ne\>y>,

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-M\>*<around|(|<around|(|x,y|)>;\<pi\><rsub|k>*p,q|)>=0.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    It is enough to take <math|q=v<rsub|1>*\<cdots\>*v<rsub|m>> with
    homogeneous factors of lengths <math|k<rsub|i>> summing to <math|k>.
    Choose distinct real numbers <math|c<rsub|i>> and replace the spectator
    factors by insertions at <math|y+t*c<rsub|i>*e<rsub|0>>. For all
    sufficiently small positive <math|t>, these points are mutually distinct
    and avoid <math|x>. The null condition makes the resulting moment zero.
    Expand into Wick monomials. By hypothesis, the distinguished components
    of lengths below <math|k> are zero and can be removed before taking any
    limit. A component of length above <math|k> gives no Wick pairing at all:
    the distinguished insertion has more than <math|k> legs, while the entire
    spectator word has only <math|k> legs, and legs at the distinguished
    insertion cannot pair with one another. For a distinguished monomial of
    length exactly <math|k>, every spectator leg must pair with a
    distinguished leg. Hence there are no spectator\Uspectator contractions,
    at any <math|t>. The only kernels in these surviving terms connect
    <math|x> to a spectator point; they are smooth there because
    <math|x\<ne\>y>, and converge to their values at <math|y> as
    <math|t*\<downarrow\>*0>. Their limit is precisely the two-insertion
    moment <math|\<cal-M\>*<around|(|<around|(|x,y|)>;\<pi\><rsub|k>*p,q|)>>.
    Since the original moments vanish, this limit is zero. Extend by
    linearity to <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|D,k>>.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Shell reduction at a fixed jet
    cutoff><label|lem:free-shell-reduction>Fix a jet cutoff <math|j> and a
    gauge-invariant polynomial <math|p\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsub|j>> null at
    capacity <math|D<rsub|j>>. Then <math|p=0>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Induct on ordinary length <math|k>. Suppose the components below <math|k>
    vanish. The coalescence lemma gives

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-M\>*<around|(|<around|(|x,y|)>;\<pi\><rsub|k>*p,q|)>=0*<space|1em><around|(|q\<in\>\<cal-H\><rsub|D<rsub|j>,k>,x\<ne\>y|)>.
    </equation*>

    By Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:free-uniform-generation>, the time-reflected
    conjugate <math|\<Theta\>*\<pi\><rsub|k>*p> belongs to
    <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|D<rsub|j>,k>>: reflection preserves the jet cutoff,
    the gauge-invariant sector, and ordinary length. If
    <math|\<pi\><rsub|k>*p\<ne\>0>, the strict all-grade free detector
    (Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-all-grade-free-detector>, with
    time reflection and coefficient conjugation) at the separated pair
    <math|<around|(|-r*e<rsub|0>,r*e<rsub|0>|)>> gives

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-M\>*<around|(|<around|(|-r*e<rsub|0>,r*e<rsub|0>|)>;\<pi\><rsub|k>*p,\<Theta\>*\<pi\><rsub|k>*p|)>\<gtr\>0;
    </equation*>

    reflection positivity alone would give only <math|\<geqslant\>0>. This
    contradicts the preceding vanishing. Hence <math|\<pi\><rsub|k>*p=0> for
    every <math|k>. The polynomial is a finite sum of these components, so it
    is zero.
  </proof>

  To remove the fixed-jet restriction, put <math|j=dim \<frak-g\>> and
  <math|L=j+1>. Suppose the gauge-invariant polynomial <math|p> is supported
  in jet orders at most <math|s+1>. For each color <math|c> choose a lower
  probe <math|q<rsub|c>> of order <math|c> and a sheet vector
  <math|w<rsub|c>> with <math|q<rsub|c><around|(|w<rsub|c>|)>=1>. These
  sheets are nonzero: if the order-<math|c> sheet were zero, every
  order-<math|c> probe would be zero, so the nonzero zero-order coordinate
  cell (which takes value one on its matching basis two-form) would pair to
  zero with all such probes at every nonzero displacement, contrary to the
  all-order separation argument below. The replacement equations have
  polynomial matrix entries

  <\equation*>
    M<rsub|c*i>=m<rsub|c*i>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|m<rsub|c*i>> is the coordinate polynomial obtained by
  evaluating the order-<math|c> probe <math|q<rsub|c>> on the field
  coordinate of color <math|i> at jet order <math|c>; <math|i> ranges over
  the color slots of the order-<math|<around|(|s+1|)>> derivative. Its
  determinant is not the zero polynomial. To see this, evaluate the Taylor
  tower by putting <math|w<rsub|c>> in the matching slot of color <math|c>
  and zero in every other color slot. The matrix becomes the identity. This
  specialization proves that its determinant polynomial is nonzero; it does
  not claim that the matrix is invertible at every field value.

  Fix any upper probe of order <math|L> and any nonzero displacement. Let
  <math|R<rsub|i>> be the finite linear combination of
  order-<math|<around|(|s+1|)>> partial derivatives whose coefficients are
  the free two-point pairings of that upper probe with the color-<math|i> jet
  coordinates. The circulation equations for the chosen <math|q<rsub|c>>
  state that <math|M*R=0> as a polynomial identity. Multiplying by the
  adjugate gives the determinant times each component equal to zero. The
  polynomial coordinate ring is an integral domain, so each component
  <math|R<rsub|i>> vanishes. This holds for every upper probe and every
  nonzero displacement. We use the following separation fact at all jet
  orders: if a jet functional of order <math|h> pairs to zero at every
  nonzero displacement with every probe of order <math|\<ell\>>, it is zero.
  Induct on <math|\<ell\>>. At <math|\<ell\>=0>, its color-resolved pulse
  vanishes off the insertion by the free-kernel formula. Testing the
  single-charge radial flux identity<nbsp><eqref|eq:maxwell-radial-single-charge>
  against arbitrary homogeneous Maxwell waves of order <math|h> gives
  <math|-<around|(|h+2|)>> times its value on each order-<math|h> jet, hence
  all coefficients vanish. For <math|\<ell\>\<gtr\>0>, fix a probe <math|q>
  of order <math|\<ell\>-1> and let <math|K<around|(|x|)>> be its pairing
  with the order-<math|h> functional. Every directional derivative of
  <math|K> is a pairing against a raised probe of order <math|\<ell\>>, so it
  vanishes by hypothesis. The kernel's homogeneity gives the Euler identity
  <math|x\<cdot\>\<nabla\>*K<around|(|x|)>=-<around|(|4+h+\<ell\>-1|)>*K<around|(|x|)>>.
  Its coefficient is nonzero, so <math|K> vanishes at every nonzero <math|x>;
  the induction hypothesis now applies. Therefore the color-resolved
  two-point functionals, indexed by every nonzero displacement and every
  upper probe of the chosen order, span the full dual of the
  order-<math|<around|(|s+1|)>> jet space. Applying this spanning statement
  to each color slot shows that every ordinary partial derivative in an
  order-<math|<around|(|s+1|)>> variable vanishes. In characteristic zero,
  <math|p> is independent of those variables and lies in the lower jet shelf.

  Suppose the gauge-invariant polynomial <math|p> is null at capacity
  <math|D<rsub|\<ast\>>> and lies in weighted shell <math|w+1>. Whenever the
  highest jet order satisfies <math|s+1\<gtr\>j>, test the circulation with
  each lower probe of order <math|\<ell\>\<less\>j>; then
  <math|\<ell\>\<less\>s+1>, as required for weighted-degree lowering. The
  upper probe has order <math|L=j+1>, so the separate collision condition
  <math|\<ell\>\<less\>L> also holds. The mixed-collision nullity theorem
  (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:free-collision-pole-separation>,
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:free-circulation-null>) now applies: the
  current polynomial has jet cutoff <math|s+1> and is a ghost at capacity
  <math|D<rsub|\<ast\>>>; the circulation uses upper probe order <math|k=L>,
  lower order <math|\<ell\>>, nonzero displacement, and satisfies
  <math|\<ell\>\<less\>L> and <math|L+\<ell\>+4\<leqslant\>D<rsub|\<ast\>>>.
  It therefore remains a ghost at capacity <math|D<rsub|\<ast\>>>. The
  admissibility bound <math|L+\<ell\>+4\<leqslant\>D<rsub|\<ast\>>>, with
  <math|L=j+1> and <math|\<ell\>\<less\>j>, is exactly what allows this upper
  and lower probe in the extra test. Each term replaces a coordinate of
  weight <math|s+3> by one of weight <math|\<ell\>+2>, so the circulation
  lies in weighted shell <math|w>. Strong induction on <math|w> therefore
  makes it zero. The top-shelf elimination then applies.

  Iterating lowers the jet shelf to the fixed order <math|j>. If
  <math|r<rsub|j>> is the host bound from finite generation, take

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|\<ast\>>=max <around|{|r<rsub|j>,<space|0.17em>4+j+<around|(|j+1|)>|}>.
  </equation*>

  The second term dominates <math|<around|(|j+1|)>+\<ell\>+4> for all lower
  probe orders <math|\<ell\>\<less\>j>. The fixed-shelf argument above then
  annihilates the polynomial. This proves the following uniform result.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Uniform external detection><label|thm:free-uniform-detection>For a
    compact simple gauge group there is a finite <math|D<rsub|\<ast\>>> such
    that every gauge-invariant observable in every finite shell which is null
    at capacity <math|D<rsub|\<ast\>>> is zero.
  </theorem>

  <subsubsection|Quiet charges and collinear tests>

  For a four-component vector of linear operators
  <math|A=<around|(|A<rsub|a>|)><rsub|a=1><rsup|4>> on the finite-level field
  space, say that <math|A> is quiet at capacity <math|D> if, for each
  component <math|a> and every separated word with labels in
  <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|D>>, the sum of moments obtained by applying
  <math|A<rsub|a>> to the label in each slot and summing over the slots
  vanishes. Write <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>> for the canonical translation
  derivative, raising jet order by one and extended by the Leibniz rule.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Classification of quiet charges><label|thm:free-quiet-classification>Let
    <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|k>> be the finite field space at level <math|k>, and
    let <math|A=<around|(|A<rsub|a>|)><rsub|a>> be a four-component charge in
    the specified vector-spin subspace. Thus
    <math|A<rsub|a><around|(|\<cal-P\><rsub|k>|)>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><rsub|k+1>>
    for every <math|k\<leqslant\>d>, the maps have the signed-coordinate
    covariance and reality conditions of the vector representation, and they
    annihilate the unit. If <math|d\<geqslant\>max
    <around|{|4,D<rsub|\<ast\>>|}>> and the charge is quiet at capacity
    <math|d>, then there is one real scalar <math|c> with
    <math|A<rsub|a>=c*\<partial\><rsub|a>> for all four axes.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|S<rsub|0>> and <math|S<rsub|1>> be the real sheet spaces of
    zero-jet and one-jet curvature coordinates, and put
    <math|V<rsub|i>=S<rsub|i><rsup|\<ast\>>>. If <math|p> is a linear
    functional on a sheet, write <math|\<phi\><rsub|r,c><around|(|p|)>> for
    its color-<math|c> field coordinate and let
    <math|<math-ss|Q><around|(|p,q|)>> denote the color-contracted quadratic
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|c>\<phi\><rsub|r,c><around|(|p|)>*\<phi\><rsub|s,c><around|(|q|)>>,
    with the jet orders indicated by the spaces of <math|p,q>. The
    degree-four shell is the direct sum of the unit and the span of
    <math|<math-ss|Q><around|(|p,q|)>> for <math|p,q\<in\>V<rsub|0>>. The
    degree-five shell is the sum of that degree-four shell and the mixed
    products <math|<math-ss|Q><around|(|p,q<rsub|1>|)>> with
    <math|p\<in\>V<rsub|0>>, <math|q<rsub|1>\<in\>V<rsub|1>>. The charge
    kills the unit. By the vector filtration and opposite-frame parity, its
    response to a degree-four quadratic stress label lies in the mixed
    degree-five subspace. It is real because the charge preserves the real
    structure. Each mixed product is real; taking the real part of a complex
    linear combination shows that a real element of their complex span lies
    in their real span. The mixed products therefore represent that response
    by a real linear panel <math|D<rsub|a,p>:S<rsub|0>\<to\>V<rsub|1>>: in a
    basis <math|<around|(|e<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>> of <math|S<rsub|0>> with dual
    basis <math|<around|(|e<rsup|\<alpha\>>|)>>, its mixed polynomial is
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>><math-ss|Q><around|(|e<rsup|\<alpha\>>,D<rsub|a,p><around|(|e<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>|)>>.
    This expression is basis-independent because it is the contraction of a
    linear map with the identity tensor. The representation is unique:
    evaluate the polynomial on a Taylor tower with independently prescribed
    order-zero and order-one sheets in a fixed common color slot and all
    other slots zero. The value is exactly
    <math|D<rsub|a,p><around|(|X|)><around|(|Y|)>>, so vanishing of the
    polynomial forces every panel coefficient to vanish. This constructs the
    finite-level panel without assuming any formula for the charge on higher
    shells.

    Write <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|3>*<around|(|x,y,z;A,B,C|)>> for the free
    moment with labels <math|A,B,C> inserted at <math|x,y,z>. The free
    six-leg Wick computation used here is the following identity:

    <\equation>
      <\eqsplit>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<cal-M\><rsub|3>*<around|(|x,y,z;<math-ss|Q><around|(|a,v|)>,<math-ss|Q><around|(|p,p|)>,<math-ss|Q><around|(|q,q|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|1em>=4*dim
        <around|(|\<frak-g\>|)>*<around*|(|F*<around|(|a,p;x-y|)>*F*<around|(|v,q;x-z|)>+F*<around|(|a,q;x-z|)>*F*<around|(|v,p;x-y|)>|)>*F*<around|(|p,q;y-z|)>.>>>>
      </eqsplit>

      <label|eq:free-three-quadratic-wick>
    </equation>

    It follows by pairing the two legs at the first insertion with the two
    legs at the other insertions in the two possible assignments; the last
    pair joins the remaining sites. The color deltas leave one free color
    sum, and the two choices of the leg within each quadratic give the factor
    <math|4>. For a general panel, expand in a basis of <math|S<rsub|0>> and
    use multilinearity. Choose the third quadratic with label <math|w> so
    that <math|F*<around|(|q,w;-z|)>=q<around|(|X|)>> for every
    <math|q\<in\>V<rsub|0>>. To see this explicitly, write <math|R<rsub|z>>
    for the invertible exterior-square action of the House reflection
    associated with <math|z> (so <math|R<rsub|-z>=R<rsub|z>>), and put
    <math|\<kappa\><rsub|z>=4*\<nu\><around|\<lVert\>|z|\<rVert\>><rsup|-4>\<gtr\>0>,
    where <math|\<nu\>=<around|(|4*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>> is the
    positive free-kernel normalization. The zero-jet covariance is

    <\equation*>
      F*<around|(|q,<math-up|cell><rsub|i*j>;z|)>=\<kappa\><rsub|z>*<space|0.17em>q<around|(|R<rsub|z><math-up|blade><rsub|i*j>|)>.
    </equation*>

    For given <math|X>, set <math|W=R<rsub|z><rsup|-1>*X> and define

    <\equation*>
      w=\<kappa\><rsub|z><rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|i,j><frac|1|2><math-up|cell><rsub|i*j><around|(|W|)><space|0.17em><math-up|cell><rsub|i*j>.
    </equation*>

    The coordinate resolution <math|W=<big|sum><rsub|i,j><frac|1|2><math-up|cell><rsub|i*j><around|(|W|)><math-up|blade><rsub|i*j>>
    then gives <math|F*<around|(|q,w;z|)>=q<around|(|X|)>> for every
    <math|q>. Thus this third quadratic exists for each
    <math|X\<in\>S<rsub|0>> and nonzero <math|z>.

    Fix a vector component <math|a> and abbreviate
    <math|D<rsub|p>=D<rsub|a,p>>. Insert the three quadratic probes at
    <math|t*u,0,z>, multiply their moment by <math|t<rsup|5>>, and let
    <math|t*\<downarrow\>*0>. The identity above gives the first
    near-collision limit. Exchanging the first two insertion points gives a
    second near-collision limit with <math|p,q> interchanged and displacement
    <math|-u>; the one-jet/zero-jet kernel is odd, so this term has the
    opposite sign. The third placement has its first insertion at the fixed
    point <math|z> and its scaled limit is zero. Every separated three-point
    Ward row vanishes, so the sum of these limits is zero. Cancelling the
    common nonzero factor <math|4*dim <around|(|\<frak-g\>|)>> gives, for
    every <math|X\<in\>S<rsub|0>> and every nonzero separation <math|u>,

    <\equation*>
      F*<around|(|D<rsub|p><around|(|X|)>,q;u|)>*<space|0.17em>q<around|(|X|)>=F*<around|(|D<rsub|q><around|(|X|)>,p;u|)>*<space|0.17em>p<around|(|X|)>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|F*<around|(|v,q;u|)>> is the free pairing for
    <math|v\<in\>V<rsub|1>>, <math|q\<in\>V<rsub|0>> at separation
    <math|u\<ne\>0>. If <math|p<around|(|X|)>=0>, the identity gives
    <math|F*<around|(|D<rsub|a,p><around|(|X|)>,q;u|)>=0> for every <math|q>
    with <math|q<around|(|X|)>\<ne\>0> and every nonzero <math|u>. For
    <math|X\<ne\>0>, these <math|q> form the complement of a hyperplane and
    span <math|V<rsub|0>>, so linearity extends the equality to every upper
    probe. For <math|X=0> the panel already vanishes by linearity. We justify
    the separation used here and below. If <math|v\<in\>V<rsub|1>> pairs to
    zero with every zero-jet probe at every nonzero displacement, the
    free-kernel formula shows that the color-resolved pulse of <math|v>
    vanishes componentwise away from its marked point. Test the single-charge
    radial-flux identity<nbsp><eqref|eq:maxwell-radial-single-charge> against
    an arbitrary order-one sheet <math|A\<in\>S<rsub|1>>, represented by its
    homogeneous Maxwell wave, using a compact cutoff equal to one near the
    marked point. The flux vanishes because the pulse vanishes on the support
    of the cutoff gradient. Since <math|v> has jet order one, the radial
    identity evaluates the same flux as <math|-3> times the pairing of
    <math|v> with <math|A> at the marked point. Since <math|A> is arbitrary
    in <math|S<rsub|1>>, this gives <math|v=0>. Thus the free pairings over
    all nonzero displacements have zero annihilator and span
    <math|V<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>>. In particular
    <math|D<rsub|a,p><around|(|X|)>=0> on <math|ker p>. For <math|p\<ne\>0>,
    choose <math|X<rsub|0>> with <math|p<around|(|X<rsub|0>|)>=1> and define
    <math|f<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>=D<rsub|a,p><around|(|X<rsub|0>|)>>.
    Linearity of the panel then gives <math|D<rsub|a,p><around|(|X|)>=p<around|(|X|)>*f<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>>
    for every <math|X>. Set <math|f<rsub|a><around|(|0|)>=0>. Thus
    <math|D<rsub|a,p>=p\<otimes\>f<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>> and
    <math|A<rsub|a><around|(|<math-ss|Q><around|(|p,p|)>|)>=<math-ss|Q><around|(|p,f<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>|)>>.
    The Taylor-tower extraction above shows this panel representation is
    unique. For nonzero <math|p,q>, choose <math|X> with
    <math|p<around|(|X|)>*q<around|(|X|)>\<ne\>0> and cancel these scalar
    factors in the triangular identity; when either probe is zero, the
    resulting identity is immediate. The coefficient identity becomes the
    reciprocity relation

    <\equation*>
      F*<around|(|f<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>,q;z|)>=F*<around|(|f<rsub|a><around|(|q|)>,p;z|)>,<space|2em>z\<ne\>0.
    </equation*>

    For fixed <math|a>, reciprocity and bilinearity of <math|F> imply
    additivity: for every <math|r,z>,

    <\equation*>
      F*<around|(|f<rsub|a>*<around|(|p+q|)>-f<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>-f<rsub|a><around|(|q|)>,r;z|)>=0.
    </equation*>

    The same computation with <math|p> replaced by <math|t*p> gives real
    homogeneity. The free two-point functionals over all nonzero
    displacements separate <math|V<rsub|1>>, so <math|f<rsub|a>> is
    real-linear. Signed-coordinate covariance gives

    <\equation*>
      \<rho\><rsub|s>*f<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>=\<varepsilon\>*<around|(|s,a|)>*f<rsub|s*a>*<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|s>*p|)>,
    </equation*>

    with <math|\<rho\><rsub|s>> the signed action and
    <math|\<varepsilon\>*<around|(|s,a|)>> its coordinate sign.

    Here is the finite coefficient determination. Let <math|C<rsub|i*j>> be
    the six coordinate two-forms in <math|V<rsub|0>> and let
    <math|D<rsub|a;i*j>> be their one-jet curls in <math|V<rsub|1>>.
    Signed-reflection parity allows only <math|D<rsub|0;01>,D<rsub|2;12>,D<rsub|3;13>>
    in <math|f<rsub|0><around|(|C<rsub|01>|)>>. Equivariance under the swap
    of axes <math|2,3> makes this vector invariant under that swap, so it
    lies in the span of <math|D<rsub|0;01>> and
    <math|D<rsub|2;12>+D<rsub|3;13>>. The free-shift identity (the divergence
    identity)

    <\equation*>
      D<rsub|2;12>+D<rsub|3;13>=D<rsub|0;01>
    </equation*>

    therefore gives <math|f<rsub|0><around|(|C<rsub|01>|)>=A*D<rsub|0;01>>.
    For <math|C<rsub|12>>, signed-reflection parity first allows exactly

    <\equation*>
      D<rsub|0;12>,<space|1em>D<rsub|1;02>,<space|1em>D<rsub|2;01>.
    </equation*>

    The negative eigenspace of the swap of axes <math|1,2> then makes the
    last two occur as their difference. The Bianchi identity

    <\equation*>
      D<rsub|1;02>-D<rsub|2;01>=D<rsub|0;12>
    </equation*>

    then gives <math|f<rsub|0><around|(|C<rsub|12>|)>=B*D<rsub|0;12>>.
    Evaluate the reciprocity relation at the nonzero cross-seed
    <math|z=e<rsub|0>+e<rsub|2>>. By the explicit cross-seed evaluation
    <math|F*<around|(|D<rsub|0;01>,C<rsub|12>;e<rsub|0>+e<rsub|2>|)>=-2*<space|0.17em>\<nu\>>,
    with <math|\<nu\>\<gtr\>0>, the common factor is nonzero and <math|A=B>.
    Signed permutations fixing axis <math|0> then carry these coefficient
    identities to the remaining coordinate two-forms. Since the
    <math|C<rsub|i*j>> form a basis, this proves
    <math|f<rsub|0>=A*\<nabla\><rsub|0>>, where <math|\<nabla\><rsub|0>> is
    the zero-to-one-jet derivative insertion; covariance gives
    <math|f<rsub|a>=A*\<nabla\><rsub|a>> for every axis. The Leibniz rule
    gives

    <\equation*>
      \<partial\><rsub|a><math-ss|Q><around|(|p,p|)>=2<math-ss|Q><around|(|p,\<nabla\><rsub|a>*p|)>,
    </equation*>

    so the original charge is <math|c*\<partial\><rsub|a>> on the whole
    degree-four shell, with <math|c=A/2>. Its unit rule supplies the constant
    component. Subtract <math|c*\<partial\><rsub|a>> and call the remainder
    <math|B<rsub|a>>. This remainder is quiet and vanishes on the unit and on
    every quadratic stress insertion. Let <math|\<Gamma\>> be the grading
    derivation that assigns weight <math|r+2> to a coordinate of jet order
    <math|r>. The localized flux identity gives for each <math|p> in the
    finite shell that

    <\equation*>
      \<Gamma\>*<around|(|B<rsub|a>*p|)>-B<rsub|a>*<around|(|\<Gamma\>*p|)>
    </equation*>

    has zero Ward rows at the same external capacity. The required dilation
    Ward identity is the radial flux formula of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:free-maxwell-radial-flux>,
    equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:maxwell-radial-polynomial-ward>. For a smooth
    cutoff <math|\<chi\>> equal to one near the marked point <math|x>, the
    centered dilation field is <math|v<around|(|y|)>=y-x>. On a homogeneous
    Maxwell jet of order <math|r>, its infinitesimal action is
    <math|<around|(|y-x|)>\<cdot\>\<nabla\>+2>; Euler's identity gives
    <math|<around|(|r+2|)>> times that jet at <math|x>. The radial flux
    formula identifies the cutoff stress integral with the negative of this
    insertion. Expanding a polynomial into its finite Wick monomials and
    summing these single-jet identities gives exactly the weighted Euler
    operator <math|\<Gamma\>> on the marked polynomial. Choose <math|\<chi\>>
    equal to one at the marked insertion and zero at the other insertions.
    The ordinary Ward terms vanish because <math|B<rsub|a>> is quiet; the
    boundary term vanishes because <math|B<rsub|a>> annihilates the quadratic
    stress labels. Therefore the commutator has zero rows against all
    separated test words at capacity <math|d>. Since
    <math|d\<geqslant\>D<rsub|\<ast\>>>, it is null at capacity
    <math|D<rsub|\<ast\>>>. Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:free-uniform-detection>
    therefore gives <math|\<Gamma\>*B<rsub|a>=B<rsub|a>*\<Gamma\>> on the
    finite shell.

    On a monomial <math|X<rsup|\<nu\>>>, <math|\<Gamma\>> has eigenvalue
    <math|m=<big|sum><rsub|r,\<alpha\>><around|(|r+2|)>*\<nu\><rsub|r,\<alpha\>>>.
    Opposite-coordinate reflection has eigenvalue
    <math|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|<big|sum>r*\<nu\><rsub|r,\<alpha\>>>>. These
    signs agree because the exponents differ by twice the ordinary length.
    Spin covariance makes <math|B<rsub|a>> anticommute with this reflection.
    Since commutation with <math|\<Gamma\>> preserves each weight eigenspace
    and reflection is scalar <math|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|m>> on that
    eigenspace, anticommutation forces <math|B<rsub|a>> to vanish there.
    Every polynomial is a finite sum of weight-homogeneous components, so
    <math|B<rsub|a>=0>. The initial coefficient comparison gave one common
    scalar across the four axes; this proves the classification.
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Finite collinear scene family><label|thm:free-collinear-scenes>

    <\enumerate>
      <item>At every level <math|d> there is a finite family of collinear
      scene tests whose simultaneous kernel on the space of charges is
      exactly the quiet-charge subspace.

      <item>If <math|d\<geqslant\>max <around|{|4,D<rsub|\<ast\>>|}>>, the
      simultaneous kernel of such a family on the vector-spin subspace of
      Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:free-quiet-classification> is exactly
      <math|\<bbb-R\>*\<partial\>>.
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|V> be the finite-dimensional real space of charges at the
    chosen level. For a charge component <math|A<rsub|a>>, a scene test is
    the complex-valued real-linear functional obtained by evaluating an
    arbitrary finite configuration derivative of its full Ward sum at a
    strict collinear configuration, using a line axis different from
    <math|a>. The intersection of the kernels of all such tests is the quiet
    subspace. One direction is immediate. Conversely, fix a separated
    configuration <math|x> and a charge component <math|a>. Choose a line
    axis <math|b\<ne\>a>. Order the indices so that all strict orders of the
    projections <math|x<rsub|i>\<cdot\>e<rsub|b>> are preserved, and choose
    an arbitrary order within each tie. For sufficiently small
    <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>, the perturbed projections
    <math|q<rsub|i>=x<rsub|i>\<cdot\>e<rsub|b>+\<delta\><space|0.17em><math-up|rank><around|(|i|)>>
    are pairwise distinct and preserve every previous strict inequality. Put
    <math|y<rsub|i>=q<rsub|i>*e<rsub|b>>. For each pair, its projected
    difference along the segment from <math|y> to <math|x> keeps the same
    strict sign until the endpoint; at the endpoint the original
    configuration is separated. Thus the segment stays in configuration
    space. The moment is analytic on the separated configuration space. If
    all derivatives at the collinear endpoint vanish, its Taylor series
    vanishes near that endpoint, and the one-variable identity theorem
    propagates vanishing along the segment. Thus all separated Ward rows
    vanish, which is quietness.

    An arbitrary intersection of kernels on a finite-dimensional space is
    already the intersection of a finite subfamily: add a functional whenever
    the current intersection is too large. Its dimension strictly drops, so
    after at most <math|dim V> additions the process stops. The selected
    finite scene family has the required kernel, proving<nbsp>(1); no level
    threshold is used.

    For<nbsp>(2), intersect with the vector-spin subspace. By<nbsp>(1) the
    kernel there consists of the quiet charges in that subspace, and for
    <math|d\<geqslant\>max <around|{|4,D<rsub|\<ast\>>|}>>
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:free-quiet-classification> shows that each of
    them is <math|c*\<partial\>> for a real <math|c>. Conversely
    <math|\<partial\>> lies in the vector-spin subspace (it raises the level
    by one, has the signed-coordinate covariance and reality of a vector, and
    annihilates the unit) and is quiet: applying <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>>
    in each slot and summing gives the derivative of the free moment along a
    common translation of all insertion points, which vanishes by translation
    invariance. Hence the kernel on the vector-spin subspace is
    <math|\<bbb-R\>*\<partial\>>.
  </proof>

  The finite-scene theorem is the free-theory interface used by the selected
  finite-level Ward package. Applying it requires the same free fields,
  capacity and response space as in its hypotheses.

  <subsection|Common rank tests on the selected
  history><label|sec:actual-rank-purchases>

  We now organize the comparison estimates into the finite set of tests
  needed to prevent loss of rank. The essential quantifier is that one
  calibration serves every row of the testing matrix. Its choice may depend
  on the comparison scale and on the finite volume, but cannot depend on the
  row subsequently tested. We also retain the original partition function
  throughout the auxiliary integration history.

  Work with the funded calibrated family for the simple gauge group of the
  construction, retaining its paid calendar and local comparison inputs. Fix
  a grade <math|d>. Let <math|V<rsub|d>> be its reference coefficient space,
  with its real structure, distinguished unit <math|e<rsub|0>>, and
  coefficient norm <math|<around|\||\<cdummy\>|\|><rsub|1>>. Fix a finite
  real basis <math|q<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,q<rsub|r>> of the free quotient,
  starting with the scalar, and real representatives
  <math|v<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|r>\<in\>V<rsub|d>>, with
  <math|v<rsub|0>=e<rsub|0>>. Such representatives exist: lift each real
  quotient vector and replace a lift <math|v> by
  <math|<around|(|v+<wide|v|\<bar\>>|)>/2>. The quotient projection commutes
  with conjugation, so its image is unchanged. The scalar has its prescribed
  lift. Fix also the finite generic measurement configurations separating
  these basis vectors. Each row uses either one or two insertions.

  The argument below uses the same-family local estimates of the preceding
  construction: the corrected mixed compiler, its reader comparisons, the
  calibrated insertion prices, and the constant-coupling auxiliary history.
  Their numerical inequalities are imposed at the selected comparison scales.
  In particular the assertion that the compiler applies to every matched
  palette and that the actual reader has its required mean and covariance
  bounds remains part of this input. The finite-dimensional selection in this
  section does not supply a missing local estimate.

  <subsubsection|Buying comparison scales before selecting the history>

  Write <math|j> for a stage of the construction and <math|k> for a level of
  the realized calendar; <math|J<around|(|k|)>> is its stage. The preliminary
  purchase supplies a finite menu <math|I<rsub|j>> of grades at stage
  <math|j>, with every fixed grade belonging to <math|I<rsub|j>> for all
  sufficiently large <math|j>. For the finite requests in <math|I<rsub|j>>
  choose the comparison parameters and then the necessary block floor. Two
  kinds of bounds enter this floor: those ensuring the local estimates and
  those allowing their comparison parameters to be bought. Their maximum,
  denoted <math|\<mu\><rsub|j>>, serves both purposes. Increasing the
  preliminary block prescription preserves these properties. Choose
  <math|L<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>\<mu\><rsub|j>>.

  Some parameters can only be chosen after <math|L<rsub|j>>. For a grade
  <math|i\<in\>I<rsub|j>>, let <math|a<rsub|i*j>\<gtr\>0> be the admissible
  upper bound for its final marker radius. Let <math|c<rsub|i*j>\<gtr\>0> be
  the radius already allowed by its comparison data. After fixing a common
  positive outer parameter for the finite menu, choose

  <\equation*>
    0\<less\>\<delta\><rsub|i*j>\<leqslant\>min
    <around|(|a<rsub|i*j>,c<rsub|i*j>,1|)>.
  </equation*>

  The local comparison is stable under this reduction of the marker radius.
  Choose its remaining threshold after <math|\<delta\><rsub|i*j>> and
  <math|L<rsub|j>>. Taking the maximum over <math|i\<in\>I<rsub|j>> gives a
  single eventual coupling threshold for the stage. No maximum over
  infinitely many grades is required.

  The paid calendar is enlarged at these thresholds and at the existing
  window and minimum-length bounds. The realization property of that calendar
  says that every sufficiently late stage occurs on a paid run of levels,
  with its block, coupling and window data. This proves that, for each fixed
  grade, suitable comparison levels are unbounded. Indeed, given a lower
  level <math|k<rsub|0>>, choose a stage <math|j\<gtr\>J<around|(|k<rsub|0>|)>>
  in whose menu the grade occurs, also beyond the finitely many construction
  floors. Realize that stage at a level <math|k>. If
  <math|k\<leqslant\>k<rsub|0>>, monotonicity of <math|J> would give
  <math|j=J<around|(|k|)>\<leqslant\>J<around|(|k<rsub|0>|)>>, a
  contradiction. Thus <math|k\<gtr\>k<rsub|0>>.

  At these levels all the local comparisons use the same price map and the
  same geometric history. To check this last point, the preliminary shell
  purchase is enlarged to

  <\equation*>
    <text|shell><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|j+7|)>*L<rsub|j>.
  </equation*>

  The radius needed by the comparison is <math|<around|(|j+7|)>*L<rsub|j>>,
  and the paid volume bound therefore supplies its factor-<math|100> room.
  The path of the selected history is the path of the purchased evolution at
  every finite cutoff. Its funding and support-link records consequently give
  the comparison on that path with precisely the original price map. This is
  the point at which a local estimate is attached to the history: a second
  independently realized paid calendar would not justify the substitution.

  In the selected construction, these choices occur in the following order.
  The finite menus and block floors are chosen first; the enlarged shell
  delays and post-length thresholds come next; the common paid calendar is
  realized next. Finally the callback which receives the selected
  finite-volume history constructs its comparison binding using the path
  identity just checked. Thus subsequent use of a frequent set of comparison
  levels does not choose another trajectory.

  <subsubsection|One readout and one auxiliary history for all rows>

  Retain only comparison levels <math|k\<geqslant\><math-up|ref><around|(|d|)>>
  and discard the finitely many levels where the purchased exponent
  <math|I<rsub|k>> below is negative. This is possible since
  <math|I<rsub|k>\<to\>\<infty\>>. Fix such a suitable level <math|k> and
  write <math|s> for its clock coupling. The actual couplings obey the
  purchased bound

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>-s|\|>\<leqslant\>s/8*<space|2em><around|(|N\<geqslant\>k|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|N> is the mesh cutoff and <math|p> the period index. At every
  such finite volume the construction of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:restarted-rank-history>
  gives a constant-coupling auxiliary history on the required window. Its
  transition identities and all its local comparisons refer to the same
  endpoint as the original history. Its additive remainder need only have the
  value bounds stated there.

  The calibrated insertion bounds hold eventually in <math|N>, uniformly for
  every <math|p>, and for every row in the finite testing family. Intersect
  these finitely many eventual conditions before choosing the auxiliary
  history. The local readout construction then supplies a single
  complex-linear map <math|T<rsub|k,N,p>:V<rsub|d>\<to\>V<rsub|d>>. Write
  <math|A<rsub|k,N,p>> for the primary-coordinate map, including its factor
  <math|a<rsub|k><rsup|-d>>, where <math|a<rsub|k>\<gtr\>0> is the lattice
  spacing. The readout satisfies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:rank-common-tested>T<rsub|k,N,p>*e<rsub|0>=e<rsub|0>,<space|2em>T<rsub|k,N,p>*<wide|v|\<bar\>>=<wide|T<rsub|k,N,p>*v|\<bar\>>,<space|2em><around|\||A<rsub|k,N,p>*v-T<rsub|k,N,p>*v|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2><around|\||A<rsub|k,N,p>*v|\|><rsub|1>.
  </equation>

  These are estimates on the reference coefficient space. They are obtained
  with the same reader and mixed coefficient estimates used for the marked
  integrals, not by choosing an arbitrary near-invertible map afterwards.

  Here is how the previously proved comparison steps combine at this choice.
  The common coordinate construction of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-coordinate-calibration>
  gives the readout and its covariance atlas. The actual covariance estimates
  of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-covariance-transport> apply to
  this atlas at the prescribed configurations. The paid coefficient bound for
  every named lift makes its terminal insertion admissible. For every row,
  the healthy-field argument of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-healthy-cumulant-launch>
  then compares the actual middle-scale polynomial to the free polynomial.
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-cumulant-comparison> performs the
  connected coefficient subtraction. The same-row marked propagation of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-marked-propagation> transports this
  comparison through the lower part of the auxiliary window. All finite row
  and column requests are imposed before the eventual threshold is chosen.
  The comparison radius and local constants can depend on <math|d> and on
  this finite family, but the subsequent threshold does not depend on
  <math|p>.

  In more precise terms, this produces the following joint statement at
  unboundedly many <math|k>. For all sufficiently large <math|N>, and every
  <math|p>, there are one map <math|T<rsub|k,N,p>> satisfying
  <eqref|eq:rank-common-tested>, one integration course, and one auxiliary
  representation at the lower end of the window. For every family of lifts
  solving the normalization equations below, the coefficient estimates hold
  for every row and column on this same representation. The marked density
  identity keeps its partition function equal to that of the original
  endpoint. The quantifier over normalized lifts follows the choice of the
  readout; there is no separate readout for different lift families.

  Only arities at most two enter this assertion. At the middle scale the
  local healthy comparison starts the required one- and two-marker
  coefficients. The propagation keeps their invoice, meaning the original
  marked density after subtraction of its prescribed free connected
  coefficients. The normalization estimate in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:rank-root-normalization> applies at the
  lower end of that very integration course. If <math|I\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>
  is a chosen marker subset and <math|q> is the number of terminal cells, its
  estimate is

  <\equation*>
    a<rsup|q<around|\||I|\|>><around|\||\<bbb-E\><around|(|<text|replicated
    coefficient>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>exp <around|(|q*I<rsub|k>|)>,<space|2em>a=exp
    <around|(|2*I<rsub|k>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The finite-volume harmonic correction is already included in
  <math|I<rsub|k>> as specified there. Taking the <math|q>th root gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:rank-common-root><around|\<\|\|\>|<text|coefficient
    >I|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|root>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|I<rsub|k>-2*I<rsub|k><around|\||I|\|>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-I<rsub|k>>=:\<epsilon\><rsub|k>.
  </equation>

  The clock purchase makes <math|I<rsub|k>\<to\>\<infty\>>, so
  <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|k>\<to\>0>. The empty coefficient is the unaltered
  scalar normalization and is not subject to this estimate. In the two-marker
  coefficient the singleton subtractions are retained; replacing it by an
  unsubtracted two-point observable would not give
  <eqref|eq:rank-common-root>.

  <subsubsection|Solving the normalization equations exactly>

  The reference transport estimates give a number <math|D<rsub|k>\<gtr\>0>,
  independent of <math|N\<geqslant\>k> and <math|p>, such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:rank-reference-inverse><around|\||v|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>D<rsub|k>*<around|\||A<rsub|k,N,p>*v|\|><rsub|1>.
  </equation>

  Explicitly one may take

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|k>=a<rsub|k><rsup|d>*<big|prod><rsub|u=0><rsup|k-1>C<rsub|u+1>*L<rsub|u><rsup|d>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|C<rsub|u+1>\<geqslant\>1> is the purchased front-column bound.
  This follows by applying that bound successively from the normalized
  reference level to <math|k>; the extra earlier factors are at least one.
  The factor <math|a<rsub|k><rsup|d>> undoes the normalization in
  <math|A<rsub|k,N,p>>.

  For brevity suppress <math|k,N,p> in this paragraph. By the triangle
  inequality and <eqref|eq:rank-common-tested>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||A*v|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*<around|\||T*v|\|><rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  If <math|T*v=0>, <eqref|eq:rank-reference-inverse> implies <math|v=0>. Thus
  <math|T> is injective and hence bijective on the finite-dimensional space
  <math|V<rsub|d>>. This also proves the inverse estimate
  <math|<around|\||v|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*D<rsub|k>*<around|\||T*v|\|><rsub|1>>.

  Each coordinate of <math|V<rsub|d>> has two nonnegative integer degrees: a
  position degree and a factor degree. Denote multiplication by
  <math|\<rho\>> to the first degree by <math|W<rsub|\<rho\>>>, and
  multiplication by <math|t> to the second by <math|S<rsub|t>>. They are
  distinct diagonal maps. Put

  <\equation*>
    t<rsub|k,N,p>=<frac|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k><rsup|-1/2>|\<theta\><rsub|k>>\<gtr\>0,<space|2em>\<rho\><rsub|k>=\<cal-M\><rsub|k><rsup|b>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<theta\><rsub|k>> is the original clock threshold,
  <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|k>> its monitor and <math|b\<gtr\>0> the fixed
  comparison exponent. For every <math|i> solve

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:rank-common-lifts>S<rsub|t<rsub|k,N,p>>*T<rsub|k,N,p>*u<rsub|i>=W<rsub|\<rho\><rsub|k>>*v<rsub|i>.
  </equation>

  There is exactly one solution, since both maps on the left are invertible.
  Because all the maps commute with conjugation and <math|v<rsub|i>> is real,
  conjugating the equation and using uniqueness shows
  <math|<wide|u|\<bar\>><rsub|i>=u<rsub|i>>. Both diagonal maps fix
  <math|e<rsub|0>>, so uniqueness and <math|T*e<rsub|0>=e<rsub|0>> give
  <math|u<rsub|0>=e<rsub|0>>. All columns are therefore normalized
  simultaneously, with their actual unit and real structure.

  The same equations give a bound independent of the two cutoffs at fixed
  <math|k>. The coupling band and the clock identity
  <math|\<theta\><rsub|k>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|-1/2>*\<cal-M\><rsub|k>>
  imply

  <\equation*>
    t<rsub|k,N,p><rsup|-1>=\<theta\><rsub|k>*<sqrt|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>>\<leqslant\>2*\<cal-M\><rsub|k>.
  </equation*>

  Since all factor degrees are nonnegative,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||T<rsub|k,N,p>*u<rsub|i>|\|><rsub|1>=<around|\||S<rsub|t<rsub|k,N,p><rsup|-1>>*W<rsub|\<rho\><rsub|k>>*v<rsub|i>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|\||S<rsub|2*\<cal-M\><rsub|k>>*W<rsub|\<rho\><rsub|k>>*v<rsub|i>|\|><rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  Together with the inverse estimate this bounds every
  <math|<around|\||u<rsub|i>|\|><rsub|1>> by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:rank-fixed-scale-bounded>B<rsub|k>=2*D<rsub|k>*<big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|r><around|\||S<rsub|2*\<cal-M\><rsub|k>>*W<rsub|\<rho\><rsub|k>>*v<rsub|i>|\|><rsub|1>.
  </equation>

  No uniform bound in <math|k> is asserted or needed. For the finitely many
  mesh cutoffs preceding the common eventual threshold, define every lift to
  be <math|e<rsub|0>>. Enlarging <math|B<rsub|k>> by
  <math|<around|\||e<rsub|0>|\|><rsub|1>> makes the bound valid for all
  <math|N,p>, without changing any eventual marked estimate.

  <subsubsection|Selecting a sequence and taking its ordered limits>

  Let <math|\<ell\><around|(|k|)>> denote the lower end of the comparison
  window. On the admissible comparison levels the calendar is constant in
  stage throughout <math|<around|[|\<ell\><around|(|k|)>,k|]>>. Its depth
  tends to infinity, and its physical test scale is

  <\equation*>
    \<sigma\><rsub|k>=a<rsub|k>*\<cal-M\><rsub|k><rsup|b>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  The generic lattice choices for each fixed measurement configuration
  converge, after division by <math|\<sigma\><rsub|k>>, to that
  configuration. Their physical coordinates lie in the middle half of a cell
  at level <math|\<ell\><around|(|k|)>>; in particular they have the fixed
  relative boundary margin <math|1/4>. These are the geometric fit and tuning
  conditions purchased for the finite configurations. They hold eventually at
  admissible levels.

  Intersect the unbounded set of jointly funded comparison levels with these
  eventual conditions and with the eventual normalization conditions of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:rank-root-normalization>. For any
  <math|k<rsub|0>,\<ell\><rsub|0>> and <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0> there remains a
  level <math|k> with

  <\equation*>
    k\<gtr\>k<rsub|0>,<space|2em>\<ell\><around|(|k|)>\<gtr\>\<ell\><rsub|0>,<space|2em>\<sigma\><rsub|k>\<less\>\<eta\>.
  </equation*>

  For the middle inequality, choose a stage later than
  <math|J<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|0>|)>>. The plateau property forces its lower
  window end past <math|\<ell\><rsub|0>>. The other two inequalities follow
  from cofinality and <math|\<sigma\><rsub|k>\<to\>0>. Choose
  <math|k<rsub|0>> in this set, and recursively choose <math|k<rsub|n+1>>
  with

  <\equation*>
    k<rsub|n+1>\<gtr\>k<rsub|n>,<space|2em>\<ell\><around|(|k<rsub|n+1>|)>\<gtr\>\<ell\><around|(|k<rsub|n>|)>,<space|2em>\<sigma\><rsub|k<rsub|n+1>>\<less\>\<sigma\><rsub|k<rsub|n>>.
  </equation*>

  The physical scales are strictly decreasing and tend to zero, because
  <math|k<rsub|n>\<to\>\<infty\>>. The error bounds
  <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|k<rsub|n>>> also tend to zero; their monotonicity is
  unnecessary. Choose the simultaneous normalized lifts just constructed at
  each <math|k<rsub|n>>. Their cut-off uniform bounds are those of
  <eqref|eq:rank-fixed-scale-bounded>.

  At each <math|n> and all sufficiently large <math|N>, uniformly for every
  period, the insertion stencils lie in the comparison cell and every
  nonempty corrected coefficient has the root bound
  <eqref|eq:rank-common-root>. There are only finitely many rows and columns
  at fixed <math|d>, so their thresholds have a common maximum. The resulting
  sequence thus supplies the bounded lifts, geometry and moment estimates
  needed for the common extraction of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:finite-rank-kernel-transport>.

  Include all these bounded lift sequences, for every grade and comparison
  index, in that same countable extraction. First take the mesh limit for
  each fixed period, then the period limit. Denote the limiting lifts by
  <math|u<rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|n|)>>>. Reality and the unit identity
  survive these limits. The finite-cell moment inequality and the corrected
  root bound give, for every row and column,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:rank-purchased-point-comparison><around*|\||S*<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|k<rsub|n>>*x<rsup|<around|(|n|)>>;,u<rsup|<around|(|n|)>>|)>-S<rsub|<math-up|free>>*<around|(|x<rsup|<around|(|n|)>>;,q|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|n>*\<epsilon\><rsub|k<rsub|n>>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|S> is the extracted interacting correlation, <math|q> lists the
  free basis elements used by the row, and <math|x<rsup|<around|(|n|)>>> is
  its rescaled lattice configuration. The factor <math|P<rsub|n>> is the
  finite polynomial in the absolute free subset moments appearing when the
  connected subtractions are undone. It is bounded:
  <math|x<rsup|<around|(|n|)>>> tends to the fixed generic configuration, all
  its subsets remain separated there, and the free correlations are
  continuous on those domains. Hence the right side tends to zero.

  To justify the order of this passage, fix <math|n> first. Its root bound
  holds for every period and all mesh cutoffs past one threshold. Apply the
  finite-cell inequality at those cutoffs; joint convergence of the lifts and
  correlations gives the inequality after the mesh limit at each period, and
  then after the period limit. Only then let <math|n\<to\>\<infty\>>. There
  is no interchange between the comparison-scale limit and either
  finite-volume limit. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:rank-purchased-point-comparison>
  is exactly the finite measurement convergence used in the rank argument.
  Identification of the entire equation kernel additionally requires the
  all-spectator local equation comparison stated in that argument; finite
  one- and two-point measurements alone do not supply it.

  <subsubsection|The same construction on a trace chart
  window><label|subsec:rank-trace-chart-window>

  The pairwise receipt of the trace calibration uses two fields of its chart
  witness, (F3) and (F5) of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-firesky-gallery>.
  We construct the constant-coupling auxiliary history on the chart window
  <math|<around|[|w,k|]>> and the tested readout at level <math|k> for every
  sufficiently large <math|k>. The remaining witness requirements are stated
  below.

  Fix a grade <math|d>, a bound <math|<wide|b|\<bar\>>> for the base arcs,
  and the finite menu of the trace tests at grade <math|d>. It has arities at
  most two, and the marker allowance <math|M=d+2> enters only through the
  marker weight, not through the history or the readout. For a chart at level
  <math|k> with base arc <math|b\<leqslant\><wide|b|\<bar\>>>, let
  <math|\<ell\>> and <math|w> be its middle level and root depth. Write
  <math|s=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>> and <math|y=log log s>.

  We use the running estimate of the selected couplings relative to the clock
  line. Let <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>> be the intercept of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:surviving-interval-shooting> and <math|b>
  the prescribed level-zero coupling of the selected histories, so that
  <math|<around|\||b-<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>|\|>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>/2>,
  and put <math|c<rsub|R>=<around|\||b-<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>|\|>+<around|\||<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>-\<beta\><rsub|\<ast\>>|\|>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The running estimate on the clock
    line><label|lem:rank-trace-running>For every <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0> there
    is <math|m<rsub|0><around|(|\<eta\>|)>> such that

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>-<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|\|>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|R>+\<eta\>*s<rsub|m>
    </equation*>

    for all <math|m\<geqslant\>m<rsub|0><around|(|\<eta\>|)>>, all
    <math|N\<geqslant\>m> and all <math|p>. Here
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>=\<beta\><rsub|\<ast\>>+s<rsub|G>*s<rsub|m>>
    is the clock of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    In the notation of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:surviving-interval-shooting>,
    the block side of level <math|i> is <math|L<rsub|i>=L<rsub|J*<around|(|i+1|)>>>
    and the stage allowance is <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i+1>=\<varepsilon\><rsub|J*<around|(|i+1|)>>>,
    as recorded at the end of the drift paragraph of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection>. Since
    <math|s<rsub|0>=0> and <math|s<rsub|i+1>=s<rsub|i>+log
    L<rsub|J*<around|(|i+1|)>>>, the accumulated scale of that section is
    <math|S<rsub|i>=s<rsub|i>>, and its line is
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+s<rsub|G>*S<rsub|i>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>+<around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>-\<beta\><rsub|\<ast\>>|)>>.
    The selected history at cutoff <math|N> and period <math|p> is a shooting
    history of that section, with level-zero coupling <math|b>
    (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history>). Hence
    <eqref|eq:shoot-running-error> gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<beta\><rsub|N,p,i>-<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>E<rsub|i>+<around|\||b-<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>|\|>+<around|\||<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>-\<beta\><rsub|\<ast\>>|\|>=E<rsub|i>+c<rsub|R>,
    </equation*>

    with <math|E<rsub|i>=<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>i>\<varepsilon\><rsub|j+1>>,
    which depends only on the schedule, not on <math|N> or <math|p>. The
    drift condition <eqref|eq:shoot-drift-ratio> holds on the selected
    schedule. As shown at the end of that section, for given
    <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0> choose <math|j> with
    <math|J<rsub|i+1><rsup|-2>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>/2> for
    <math|i\<geqslant\>j>. Then <math|E<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>E<rsub|j>+<tfrac|\<eta\>|2><around|(|s<rsub|i>-s<rsub|j>|)>>,
    and since <math|s<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>, eventually
    <math|E<rsub|j>\<leqslant\><tfrac|\<eta\>|2>s<rsub|i>>. So
    <math|E<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>*s<rsub|i>> for all <math|i> beyond a
    threshold depending only on <math|\<eta\>>.
  </proof>

  The slope here is the clock slope <math|s<rsub|G>>, which is the slope of
  the shooting construction by definition. Its identification with the
  one-loop coefficient <math|2*b<rsub|0><around|(|G|)>> is needed for the
  public running consequence, not here.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The endpoint coupling bound><label|lem:rank-trace-endpoint>For all
    sufficiently large <math|k>, every <math|N\<geqslant\>k> and every
    <math|p>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>-<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|\|>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>/8.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Take <math|\<eta\>=s<rsub|G>/16> in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:rank-trace-running>.
    Since <math|s<rsub|k>\<to\>\<infty\>>, eventually
    <math|c<rsub|R>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|G>*s<rsub|k>/16>. Then the right side
    there is at most <math|s<rsub|G>*s<rsub|k>/8\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>/8>,
    because <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>=\<beta\><rsub|\<ast\>>+s<rsub|G>*s<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>s<rsub|G>*s<rsub|k>>.
  </proof>

  Two further receipts are needed on the chart window. The restarted
  construction of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:restart-actual-closure>
  supplies the first, and Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:rank-selected-tested-readout>
  below supplies the second. We state their precise content, including the
  four properties used in Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-coordinate-calibration>,
  <reference|sec:allarity-cumulant-comparison>
  and<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-healthy-cumulant-launch>.

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(V)><em|Restarted steps of the window.> At every step of the
    window <math|<around|[|w,k|]>>, from a level <math|m+1> to <math|m>, the
    restarted step at the constant coupling <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>>, with
    the clips, budget radius and support data of its stage pair
    <math|<around|(|J*<around|(|m+1|)>,J<around|(|m|)>|)>>, satisfies the
    component bounds (V1), the path correction (V2) and the reassembly (V3)
    of Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:restart-stage-boundary>. The pair is
    one of <math|<around|(|J<around|(|k|)>,J<around|(|k|)>|)>>,
    <math|<around|(|J<around|(|k|)>,J<around|(|k|)>-1|)>> and
    <math|<around|(|J<around|(|k|)>-1,J<around|(|k|)>-1|)>>. The purchases of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history> concern the
    selected history and are supplemented by the explicit restarted-step
    estimates of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:restart-actual-closure>;
    the selected-step bounds alone would not supply them.

    <item*|(T)><em|Calibrated insertion receipt.> At every sufficiently large
    level <math|k>, on the same endpoint, for all sufficiently large <math|N>
    uniformly in <math|p>, and for every grade
    <math|d<rprime|'>\<leqslant\>d>, there are an actual polynomial map
    <math|\<cal-H\>=\<cal-H\><rsub|k,N,p>>, assigning to each label <math|u>
    of grade <math|d<rprime|'>> a local polynomial insertion
    <math|\<cal-H\>*u> in the reader coordinates, and a linear map
    <math|T=T<rsub|k,N,p>> with the following properties.

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(T1)><em|Low-grade reading.> <math|T> is the low-grade reading
      of <math|\<cal-H\>>. For every label <math|u>, the grade truncation of
      <math|\<cal-H\>*u>, after substitution of the actual linear reader map
      at level <math|k>, is the free polynomial whose frame coefficients are
      <math|T*u> in the retained common normalization of
      Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-coordinate-calibration>, that
      is, rescaled by the coupling factor of the reader coordinates at level
      <math|k>. In particular, for the lifts of <eqref|eq:rank-common-lifts>
      the exact low-grade identity of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-cumulant-comparison>
      holds.

      <item*|(T2)><em|Tested.> <math|T> satisfies
      <eqref|eq:rank-common-tested>: <math|T*e<rsub|0>=e<rsub|0>>,
      <math|T*<wide|v|\<bar\>>=<wide|T*v|\<bar\>>>, and
      <math|<around|\||A<rsub|k,N,p>*v-T*v|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><frac|1|2>*<around|\||A<rsub|k,N,p>*v|\|><rsub|1>>.

      <item*|(T3)><em|Primal bound.> <math|<around|\||\<cal-H\>*u|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>H*<around|\||A<rsub|k,N,p>*u|\|><rsub|1>>,
      with <math|H=\<cal-M\><rsub|k>>, and the degree of <math|\<cal-H\>*u>
      is bounded independently of <math|k,N,p>.

      <item*|(T4)><em|Stamp.> There is a raw curvature polynomial
      <math|W<rsub|u>> with <math|\<cal-H\>*u=\<cal-W\><rsub|K<rsub|k>>*W<rsub|u>>,
      where <math|K<rsub|k>> is the original same-slot atlas covariance. Its
      physical evaluation has the healthy approximation and paid coefficient
      bounds, on the halo of its reader, required for the terminal birth term
      in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-healthy-cumulant-launch>. The
      Gaussian calculation uses <math|\<cal-H\>*u> and subtracts
      <math|K<rsub|k>> from each diagonal covariance block, as in
      Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:rank-raw-wick-identity>.
    </enumerate>

    The same map <math|\<cal-H\>> and the same map <math|T> occur in all four
    clauses. The polynomials <math|\<cal-H\>*u> are used in the Gaussian
    cumulant comparison and <math|T> is their reading. The physical terminal
    stamp is paid by the raw polynomials <math|W<rsub|u>> from which these
    same <math|\<cal-H\>*u> are obtained; the covariance conversion is
    retained.
  </enumerate>

  The map <math|T=A<rsub|k,N,p>> satisfies (T2) trivially when
  <math|A<rsub|k,N,p>> fixes the unit and is real, but it need not satisfy
  (T1). The readout must be the exact low-grade reading of the insertions
  that are actually integrated, because the free cumulant comparison uses the
  exact low-grade identity. The content of (T2) for the reading of (T1) is
  therefore an estimate. It says that the primary coordinate of the selected
  history at level <math|k> agrees, to relative accuracy one half, with the
  low-grade reading of the polynomial insertion built from the same label.
  Given (T1) and that estimate, the unit and reality clauses follow from
  <math|\<cal-H\>*e<rsub|0>=1> and from the reality of <math|\<cal-H\>>.

  The following lemma reduces that estimate to a <em|pre-pin> estimate for
  the plain insertion before normal ordering, read through the actual
  nonlinear reader substitution. The two changes between that reading and the
  reading of (T1) are perturbative. On polynomials we use the coefficient
  <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>> norm <math|<around|\||\<cdummy\>|\|><rsub|1>>. It is
  submultiplicative, and <math|<around|\||1|\|><rsub|1>=1>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Reduction of the pin><label|lem:rank-trace-pin-reduction>Fix
    <math|k,N,p> and a grade <math|d<rprime|'>\<leqslant\>d>, and constants
    <math|D\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, <math|B\<geqslant\>1>, <math|c\<geqslant\>1>,
    <math|0\<less\>t\<leqslant\>1> and <math|0\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>\<leqslant\>1>.
    Suppose that for every label <math|u> of grade <math|d<rprime|'>> the
    following hold.

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<cal-H\>*u=\<cal-N\><rsub|K>*p<rsub|u>>, where
      <math|p<rsub|u>> is a polynomial of degree at most <math|D> in the
      reader variables <math|y<rsub|i>> with
      <math|<around|\||p<rsub|u>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>B*<around|\||A<rsub|k,N,p>*u|\|><rsub|1>>.
      Here <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|K>=exp <around|(|-<frac|1|2>*\<Delta\><rsub|K>|)>>,
      with <math|\<Delta\><rsub|K>=<big|sum><rsub|i,j>K<rsub|i*j>*\<partial\><rsub|y<rsub|i>>*\<partial\><rsub|y<rsub|j>>>,
      is normal ordering with respect to a symmetric matrix <math|K> whose
      entries satisfy <math|<around|\||K<rsub|i*j>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>>.

      <item>In the rescaled frame coordinates, the actual reader substitution
      sends <math|y<rsub|i>> to <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>+t*a<rsub|i>>. Here
      <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>> is linear, every monomial of <math|a<rsub|i>>
      has degree at least <math|2>, and <math|<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|i>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>B>,
      <math|<around|\||a<rsub|i>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>B>.

      <item>The reading <math|\<Lambda\>>, grade truncation followed by
      passage to frame coefficients, satisfies
      <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\>*q|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>c<around|\||q|\|><rsub|1>>.

      <item><em|Pre-pin.> <math|<around|\||A<rsub|k,N,p>*u-\<Lambda\>*<around*|(|p<rsub|u>\<circ\><around|(|\<ell\>+t*a|)>|)>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>*<around|\||A<rsub|k,N,p>*u|\|><rsub|1>>.
    </enumerate>

    Let <math|T*u=\<Lambda\>*<around*|(|<around|(|\<cal-H\>*u|)>\<circ\>\<ell\>|)>>
    be the reading of (T1). Then

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||A<rsub|k,N,p>*u-T*u|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<varepsilon\>+c*B*<space|0.17em>a<rsub|D>|)>*<space|0.17em><around|\||A<rsub|k,N,p>*u|\|><rsub|1>,<space|2em>a<rsub|D>=D*t*B*<around|(|2*B|)><rsup|D>+\<varepsilon\>*<space|0.17em>w<rsub|D>*B<rsup|D>,<space|1em>w<rsub|D>=<tfrac|D<rsup|2>|2>e<rsup|D<rsup|2>/2>.
    </equation*>

    In particular, if <math|\<varepsilon\>+c*B*a<rsub|D>\<leqslant\><frac|1|2>>,
    then the estimate in (T2) holds at grade <math|d<rprime|'>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Write <math|A=A<rsub|k,N,p>> and <math|p=p<rsub|u>>. Three bounds are
    used, each for a polynomial <math|q> of degree at most <math|D>.

    <em|Linear substitution.> A monomial of degree <math|m\<leqslant\>D> is
    sent to a product of <math|m> linear forms, of norm at most
    <math|B<rsup|m>\<leqslant\>B<rsup|D>>. Hence
    <math|<around|\||q\<circ\>\<ell\>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>B<rsup|D><around|\||q|\|><rsub|1>>.

    <em|Nonlinear correction.> For a monomial
    <math|y<rsub|i<rsub|1>>*\<cdots\>*y<rsub|i<rsub|m>>>, telescoping gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|prod><rsub|r=1><rsup|m><around|(|\<ell\><rsub|i<rsub|r>>+t*a<rsub|i<rsub|r>>|)>-<big|prod><rsub|r=1><rsup|m>\<ell\><rsub|i<rsub|r>>=<big|sum><rsub|r=1><rsup|m><big|prod><rsub|r<rprime|'>\<less\>r><around|(|\<ell\><rsub|i<rsub|r<rprime|'>>>+t*a<rsub|i<rsub|r<rprime|'>>>|)>*<space|0.27em>t*a<rsub|i<rsub|r>>*<big|prod><rsub|r<rprime|'>\<gtr\>r>\<ell\><rsub|i<rsub|r<rprime|'>>>.
    </equation*>

    Each summand has norm at most <math|<around|(|2*B|)><rsup|m-1>*t*B>,
    since <math|t\<leqslant\>1>. Hence <math|<around|\||q\<circ\><around|(|\<ell\>+t*a|)>-q\<circ\>\<ell\>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>D*t*B*<around|(|2*B|)><rsup|D><around|\||q|\|><rsub|1>>.

    <em|Normal ordering.> For a monomial <math|y<rsup|\<alpha\>>> of degree
    <math|m>, <math|\<partial\><rsub|i>*\<partial\><rsub|j>*y<rsup|\<alpha\>>=\<alpha\><rsub|i>*<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|j>-\<delta\><rsub|i*j>|)>*y<rsup|\<alpha\>-e<rsub|i>-e<rsub|j>>>
    vanishes unless <math|i,j> lie in the support of <math|\<alpha\>>. So
    <math|<around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|K>*y<rsup|\<alpha\>>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>*<big|sum><rsub|i,j>\<alpha\><rsub|i>*\<alpha\><rsub|j>=\<varepsilon\>*m<rsup|2>>,
    independently of the number of variables. Since <math|\<Delta\><rsub|K>>
    lowers the degree, <math|<around|\||<frac|1|2>*\<Delta\><rsub|K>*q|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><frac|1|2>*\<varepsilon\>*D<rsup|2><around|\||q|\|><rsub|1>>
    on polynomials of degree at most <math|D>. Summing the exponential
    series,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<cal-N\><rsub|K>*q-q|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around*|(|e<rsup|\<varepsilon\>*D<rsup|2>/2>-1|)><around|\||q|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>*w<rsub|D><around|\||q|\|><rsub|1>,
    </equation*>

    by <math|e<rsup|x>-1\<leqslant\>x*e<rsup|x>> with
    <math|x=\<varepsilon\>*D<rsup|2>/2\<leqslant\>D<rsup|2>/2>. Also
    <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|K>*q> has degree at most <math|D>.

    Now

    <\equation*>
      A*u-T*u=<around*|[|A*u-\<Lambda\>*<around|(|p\<circ\><around|(|\<ell\>+t*a|)>|)>|]>+\<Lambda\>*<around*|(|p\<circ\><around|(|\<ell\>+t*a|)>-p\<circ\>\<ell\>|)>+\<Lambda\>*<around*|(|<around|(|p-\<cal-N\><rsub|K>*p|)>\<circ\>\<ell\>|)>.
    </equation*>

    By the pre-pin estimate, the first term has norm at most
    <math|\<varepsilon\>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>>. By item<nbsp>3 and the
    two bounds above, the others have norm at most
    <math|c*<space|0.17em>D*t*B*<around|(|2*B|)><rsup|D><around|\||p|\|><rsub|1>>
    and <math|c*B<rsup|D>*\<varepsilon\>*w<rsub|D><around|\||p|\|><rsub|1>>.
    Since <math|<around|\||p|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>B*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>>,
    the claim follows.
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|The polynomial pre-pin and its normalization.> The primary
  coefficient space and the polynomial jet space have different norms. Write
  <math|E:V<rsub|d>\<to\><with|math-font|cal*|P><rsub|d>> for exposure in the
  fixed radial jet frame, where <math|<with|math-font|cal*|P><rsub|d>> is the
  space of polynomials of weighted grade at most <math|d>. The weights of the
  finitely many jet variables are positive integers. Fix
  <math|c<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>1> such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:rank-exposure-cost><around|\||v|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|d>*<around|\||E*v|\|><rsub|1>*<space|2em><around|(|v\<in\>V<rsub|d>|)>.
  </equation>

  Such a constant exists because <math|E> is injective between fixed
  finite-dimensional spaces. It does not depend on the lattice volume. A
  bound for <math|<around|\||E*A*u-q|\|><rsub|1>> is therefore not yet a
  bound with the same constant for the corresponding frame coefficients.

  Here is the precise near/wide reduction. Its hypotheses distinguish the
  regular-germ identity from the two analytic ray estimates that are needed
  to use that identity.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Near and wide Taylor readings><label|lem:rank-near-wide-prepin>
    Fix <math|D\<geqslant\>d>. There are finitely many real jet points
    <math|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|J>> and <math|a<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>1>
    such that <math|<around|\||q|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|d>*max<rsub|j><around|\||q<around|(|x<rsub|j>|)>|\|>>
    for <math|q\<in\><with|math-font|cal*|P><rsub|d>>. Let
    <math|L<rsub|u>=P<rsub|u>+Q<rsub|u>> be polynomials of degree at most
    <math|D> in local link coordinates, and let
    <math|<with|math-font|cal*|U>> be the linear operation taking their
    ordinary radial reading and truncating to weighted grade <math|d>.
    Suppose <math|M<rsub|u>,\<beta\>,C<rsub|b>,\<tau\>,\<eta\>\<geqslant\>0>
    and, for each <math|j>, a linear functional
    <math|<with|math-font|cal*|R><rsub|j>> on those polynomials satisfy

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\||P<rsub|u>|\|><rsub|1>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>3*\<beta\>*M<rsub|u>,>|<cell|<around|\||Q<rsub|u>|\|><rsub|1>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<eta\>*\<beta\>*M<rsub|u>,<eq-number><label|eq:rank-near-wide-cost>>>|<row|<cell|<with|math-font|cal*|R><rsub|j><around|(|P<rsub|u>|)>>|<cell|=<around|(|E*A*u|)><around|(|x<rsub|j>|)>,>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number><label|eq:rank-near-germ>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||<with|math-font|cal*|R><rsub|j><around|(|P<rsub|u>|)>-<around|(|<with|math-font|cal*|U>*P<rsub|u>|)><around|(|x<rsub|j>|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C<rsub|b>*\<tau\><around|\||P<rsub|u>|\|><rsub|1>,>|<cell|<around|\||<around|(|<with|math-font|cal*|U>*Q<rsub|u>|)><around|(|x<rsub|j>|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C<rsub|b><around|\||Q<rsub|u>|\|><rsub|1>.<eq-number><label|eq:rank-ray-comparison>>>>>
    </align>

    Then

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:rank-polynomial-prepin><around|\||E*A*u-<with|math-font|cal*|U>*L<rsub|u>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>3*a<rsub|d>*C<rsub|b>*\<beta\>*M<rsub|u>*<around|(|\<tau\>+\<eta\>|)>,<space|2em><around|\||L<rsub|u>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|(|3+\<eta\>|)>*\<beta\>*M<rsub|u>.
    </equation>

    The estimate is uniform in every parameter for which the displayed
    hypotheses hold with the same constants.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The real evaluations separate <math|<with|math-font|cal*|P><rsub|d>>: a
    polynomial vanishing on all real points is zero, as follows by
    successively treating it as a polynomial in each variable. In a
    finite-dimensional dual space a spanning set contains a finite basis.
    Choose evaluations forming such a basis, and let <math|q<rsub|j>> be the
    corresponding interpolation polynomials. Then
    <math|q=<big|sum><rsub|j>q<around|(|x<rsub|j>|)>*q<rsub|j>>, so one may
    take <math|a<rsub|d>=max <around|(|1,<big|sum><rsub|j><around|\||q<rsub|j>|\|><rsub|1>|)>>.
    If there are no jet variables, evaluation of the constant polynomial
    suffices.

    Both <math|E*A*u> and <math|<with|math-font|cal*|U>*L<rsub|u>> belong to
    <math|<with|math-font|cal*|P><rsub|d>>. At <math|x<rsub|j>>, linearity
    and <eqref|eq:rank-near-germ> give

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|E*A*u-<with|math-font|cal*|U>*L<rsub|u>|)><around|(|x<rsub|j>|)>=<with|math-font|cal*|R><rsub|j><around|(|P<rsub|u>|)>-<around|(|<with|math-font|cal*|U>*P<rsub|u>|)><around|(|x<rsub|j>|)>-<around|(|<with|math-font|cal*|U>*Q<rsub|u>|)><around|(|x<rsub|j>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Its modulus is at most <math|C<rsub|b>*\<beta\>*M<rsub|u>*<around|(|3*\<tau\>+\<eta\>|)>\<leqslant\>3*C<rsub|b>*\<beta\>*M<rsub|u>*<around|(|\<tau\>+\<eta\>|)>>.
    Apply the sampling inequality. The last estimate is the triangle
    inequality and <eqref|eq:rank-near-wide-cost>.
  </proof>

  In the intended application <math|P<rsub|u>> sums the short-support terms
  of the actual regular insertion, and <math|Q<rsub|u>> sums the remaining
  terms. The functional <math|<with|math-font|cal*|R><rsub|j>> is its
  degree-<math|d> reading along the threshold-dependent holonomy ray.
  Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:rank-near-germ> uses the prescribed local-section
  jet and the vanishing regular remainder jet, with all short supports inside
  the nonwrapping patch. It is an exact identity about this insertion;
  finite-difference reconstruction of a closed cochain alone does not imply
  it. The ordinary radial reading <math|<with|math-font|cal*|U>> uses the
  linear edge integrals of the radial connection. The first estimate in
  <eqref|eq:rank-ray-comparison> compares the two ray readings; the wide part
  only needs the second, absolute bound. In particular, after this comparison
  the reader in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:rank-trace-pin-reduction> may have
  <math|a<rsub|i>=0>. Its nonlinear correction need not be paid a second
  time.

  <\corollary>
    <dueto|Paying the pre-pin with growing coordinate
    costs><label|cor:rank-prepin-payment>Let
    <math|H=\<cal-M\><rsub|k>\<to\>\<infty\>>, fix <math|D>, and put
    <math|\<alpha\>=1/<around|(|4*<around|(|D+2|)>|)>> and
    <math|\<gamma\>=\<alpha\>/4>. Suppose the data of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:rank-near-wide-prepin> satisfy, uniformly in
    <math|N,p,u>,

    <\equation*>
      \<beta\>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<gamma\>>,<space|2em>M<rsub|u>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<gamma\>>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>,<space|2em>3*a<rsub|d>*C<rsub|b>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<gamma\>>,<space|2em>\<tau\>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-4>,<space|1em>\<eta\>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-4>.
    </equation*>

    Suppose also that converting <math|L<rsub|u>> to the plain reader
    polynomial <math|p<rsub|u>> uses a linear substitution whose rows have
    coefficient norm at most <math|J<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>1>, with
    <math|J<rsub|k><rsup|D>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<gamma\>>>, and preserves its
    truncated reading: <math|<with|math-font|cal*|U>*L<rsub|u>=\<Pi\><rsub|d>*<around|(|p<rsub|u>\<circ\>\<ell\>|)>>.
    Then, eventually and uniformly in <math|N,p,u>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||E*A*u-\<Pi\><rsub|d>*<around|(|p<rsub|u>\<circ\>\<ell\>|)>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>,<space|2em>deg
      p<rsub|u>\<leqslant\>D,<space|2em><around|\||p<rsub|u>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<alpha\>>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|\<Pi\><rsub|d>> denotes weighted grade truncation.
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    The first bound in <eqref|eq:rank-polynomial-prepin> is at most
    <math|2*H<rsup|3*\<gamma\>-4>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>>. Since
    <math|3*\<gamma\>\<less\>3>, this is at most
    <math|H<rsup|-1>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>> eventually. Since
    <math|\<eta\>\<leqslant\>1> eventually, its second bound gives
    <math|<around|\||L<rsub|u>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>4*H<rsup|2*\<gamma\>>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>>.
    Substitution multiplies this by at most <math|J<rsub|k><rsup|D>>, by the
    monomial argument in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:rank-trace-pin-reduction>,
    and does not increase degree. Finally
    <math|4*H<rsup|3*\<gamma\>>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<alpha\>>> eventually
    because <math|\<alpha\>-3*\<gamma\>=\<gamma\>\<gtr\>0>.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Wick reading with scale-dependent
    bounds><label|lem:rank-growing-wick-pin>Let
    <math|E,c<rsub|d>,D,\<alpha\>,H> be as above and set
    <math|B=H<rsup|\<alpha\>>>, <math|\<epsilon\>=H<rsup|-1>>. Suppose
    <math|p<rsub|u>> is linear in <math|u>, has degree at most <math|D>, and
    obeys <math|<around|\||p<rsub|u>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>B*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>>.
    Let <math|K> be a symmetric matrix with
    <math|<around|\||K<rsub|i*j>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>>, and let
    <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|K>=exp <around|(|<tfrac|1|2>\<Delta\><rsub|K>|)>> be
    the signed Gaussian polynomial operator. Suppose
    <math|<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|i>|\|><rsub|1>,<around|\||a<rsub|i>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>B>,
    <math|0\<less\>t\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1>>, and

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||E*A*u-\<Pi\><rsub|d>*<around|(|p<rsub|u>\<circ\><around|(|\<ell\>+t*a|)>|)>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    Assume the exact frame-membership condition
    <math|\<Pi\><rsub|d>*<around|(|<around|(|\<cal-W\><rsub|K>*p<rsub|u>|)>\<circ\>\<ell\>|)>\<in\>E<around|(|V<rsub|d>|)>>
    for all <math|u>. Define <math|T> uniquely by

    <\equation*>
      E*T*u=\<Pi\><rsub|d>*<around|(|<around|(|\<cal-W\><rsub|K>*p<rsub|u>|)>\<circ\>\<ell\>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Then <math|T> is linear and, eventually,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||A*u-T*u|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2><around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>,<space|2em><around|\||\<cal-W\><rsub|K>*p<rsub|u>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>H*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>,<space|2em>deg
      \<cal-W\><rsub|K>*p<rsub|u>\<leqslant\>D.
    </equation*>

    If <math|p<rsub|e<rsub|0>>=1>, <math|E*e<rsub|0>=1>, and the construction
    commutes with complex conjugation, then <math|T*e<rsub|0>=e<rsub|0>> and
    <math|T*<wide|u|\<bar\>>=<wide|T*u|\<bar\>>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    All operations defining <math|E*T> are linear and <math|E> is injective,
    which proves existence, uniqueness and linearity of <math|T> under the
    stated membership condition. Truncation has coefficient norm at most one.
    The three-term decomposition in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:rank-trace-pin-reduction>,
    now made in polynomial coefficients before applying
    <eqref|eq:rank-exposure-cost>, gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||A*u-T*u|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|d>*<around|{|\<epsilon\>+B*<around|[|D*t*B*<around|(|2*B|)><rsup|D>+\<epsilon\>*w<rsub|D>*B<rsup|D>|]>|}>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>,<space|2em>w<rsub|D>=<tfrac|D<rsup|2>|2>e<rsup|D<rsup|2>/2>.
    </equation*>

    The Wick estimate is unchanged for the positive sign in
    <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|K>>: it used absolute values of the exponential
    series. With the present choices the expression in braces is at most

    <\equation*>
      H<rsup|-1>+D*2<rsup|D>*H<rsup|-1+\<alpha\>*<around|(|D+2|)>>+w<rsub|D>*H<rsup|-1+\<alpha\>*<around|(|D+1|)>>.
    </equation*>

    Both latter exponents are at most <math|-3/4>, since
    <math|\<alpha\>*<around|(|D+2|)>=1/4>. Thus the expression tends to zero
    despite the growth of <math|B>, and its product with the fixed
    <math|c<rsub|d>> is eventually at most <math|1/2>. The same series gives
    <math|<around|\||\<cal-W\><rsub|K>*p<rsub|u>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+\<epsilon\>*w<rsub|D>|)>*B*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>>
    and preserves degree. This is at most <math|H*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>>
    eventually since <math|\<alpha\>\<less\>1>. Finally
    <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|K>*1=1>, linear substitution and truncation fix
    <math|1>, and injectivity of <math|E> proves the unit and conjugation
    statements.
  </proof>

  The covariance sign and coupling normalization in this last lemma are part
  of its application. If <math|q<rsub|u>> is the unscaled insertion, put
  <math|g<rsub|k,N,p>=\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k><rsup|-1/2>> and
  <math|z=\<theta\><rsub|k>/g<rsub|k,N,p>=t<rsub|k,N,p><rsup|-1>>. By
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:rank-trace-endpoint> and the clock identity,
  <math|z=\<cal-M\><rsub|k>*<sqrt|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>/<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>\<geqslant\><sqrt|7/8>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-M\><rsub|k>\<geqslant\>1>
  for all sufficiently large <math|k>, uniformly in <math|N\<geqslant\>k> and
  <math|p>. Set <math|p<rsub|u><around|(|y|)>=q<rsub|u>*<around|(|z*y|)>>.
  For the actual signed covariance <math|\<Gamma\>>, differentiation gives
  the exact identity

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|\<cal-W\><rsub|\<Gamma\>>*q<rsub|u>|)>*<around|(|z*y|)>=\<cal-W\><rsub|z<rsup|-2>*\<Gamma\>>*p<rsub|u><around|(|y|)>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed each pair of derivatives contributes <math|z<rsup|-2>> after this
  change of variables; sum the finite exponential series. Thus the <math|K>
  in the lemma is <math|z<rsup|-2>*\<Gamma\>>. Normal ordering with
  covariance <math|C> corresponds to <math|\<Gamma\>=-C>, not to changing the
  sign silently. Undoing the dilation <math|z\<geqslant\>1> cannot increase
  coefficient norm, so the primal bound also holds for the unscaled Wick
  insertion. Dilation commutes with the linear reader and weighted
  truncation; the resulting frame dilation is precisely the coupling
  rescaling in (T1).

  The following construction supplies the Taylor reader, its frame membership
  and its raw terminal approximation from explicit calibrated-list data. It
  also distinguishes the covariance used in the coefficient reading from the
  polynomial evaluated on a physical field.

  <paragraph*|Constructing the raw polynomial and its Gaussian reading>

  The terminal approximation and its Gaussian reading use two related
  polynomials. We distinguish them before giving the estimates. For a
  symmetric matrix <math|K> write <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|K>=exp
  <around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|K>/2|)>>, where
  <math|\<Delta\><rsub|K>=<big|sum><rsub|a,b>K<rsub|a*b>*\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<partial\><rsub|b>>.
  This is a finite sum on every polynomial; neither positivity nor a
  probability measure is needed. Write <math|W<rsub|u>> for the raw curvature
  polynomial and <math|\<cal-H\>*u=\<cal-W\><rsub|K>*W<rsub|u>> for its
  Gaussian reading. The healthy field is evaluated in <math|W<rsub|u>>, not
  in <math|\<cal-H\>*u>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Changing the diagonal covariance><label|lem:rank-raw-wick-identity>Let
    <math|W<rsub|i>> be polynomials on separate, labelled copies of finite
    coordinate spaces. Let <math|C> be any symmetric matrix on their direct
    sum and <math|K=<math-up|diag><around|(|K<rsub|i>|)>> a block diagonal
    symmetric matrix. For every mean <math|\<mu\>>,

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-W\><rsub|C,\<mu\>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i>W<rsub|i>|)>=\<cal-W\><rsub|C-K,\<mu\>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i>\<cal-W\><rsub|K<rsub|i>>*W<rsub|i>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The identity holds simultaneously for every subset of the slots, with all
    matrices restricted from the same direct sum.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    All constant-coefficient partial derivatives commute. On the space of
    polynomials of degree at most the total degree of the product they are
    nilpotent, so the finite binomial formula gives <math|exp
    <around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|C-K>/2|)>*exp
    <around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|K>/2|)>=exp <around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|C>/2|)>>.
    Each block of <math|\<Delta\><rsub|K>> differentiates only its own
    factor. Hence its exponential sends the product to
    <math|<big|prod><rsub|i>\<cal-W\><rsub|K<rsub|i>>*W<rsub|i>>. Evaluate
    the resulting polynomial at <math|\<mu\>>. The same proof on any subset
    proves the last assertion, including the empty subset.
  </proof>

  For the atlas of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-covariance-transport>,
  take <math|K<rsub|i>> to be the original same-slot covariance
  <math|\<kappa\><rsub|k>>. The conditional covariance before this change is
  <math|C<rsub|i*j>=\<kappa\><rsub|k>*<around|(|z<rsub|i>+\<cdummy\>,z<rsub|j>+\<cdummy\>|)>-B<rsub|i*j>>,
  with <math|B=\<cal-L\>*\<kappa\><rsub|\<ell\>>*\<cal-L\><rsup|<math-ss|T>>>.
  Then <math|C-K> is exactly <eqref|eq:actual-normalordered-covariance>: its
  diagonal is <math|-B<rsub|i*i>> and its off-diagonal is
  <math|\<kappa\><rsub|k>-B<rsub|i*j>>. Thus the Gaussian cumulant
  calculation uses <math|\<cal-H\>*u> with that subtracted covariance,
  whereas its terminal physical approximation uses <math|W<rsub|u>>. Repeated
  physical coordinates cause no difficulty: keep a separate slot copy and use
  their full cross-covariance. This identity is an identity of polynomial
  transports, not an assertion that a cutoff nonlinear sector integral is
  already Gaussian.

  <subparagraph|The Taylor polynomial of the calibrated regular list.> Here
  are explicit sufficient data for this construction, retaining the actual
  regular list and its original supports. At one level let <math|\<theta\>>
  be its original health threshold, let <math|b> be a patch radius, and
  suppose the patch is nonwrapping. In logarithmic link coordinates put
  <math|U<around|(|x|)><rsub|e>=exp <around|(|\<theta\>*x<rsub|e>|)>> inside
  the patch and <math|U<around|(|x|)><rsub|e>=1> outside. Use the maximum of
  the colour norms for <math|x>. For a label <math|u> split its regular list
  into a near list and a wide list, with weighted terms
  <math|c<rsub|a>*f<rsub|a>>. Require the regularity of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-splitting> through order <math|D+1>,
  where <math|D\<geqslant\>d>. If <math|e<rsub|a>> is the corresponding
  nonnegative direction price, the calibrated bounds needed are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:rank-list-input><big|sum><rsub|a<text|
    near>><around|\||c<rsub|a>|\|>*e<rsub|a>\<leqslant\>3*M<rsub|u>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|a<text|
    wide>><around|\||c<rsub|a>|\|>*e<rsub|a>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>*M<rsub|u>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>\<eta\>\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation>

  All near supports lie in the patch. The local-section terms have prescribed
  degree-<math|d> projection and the other near terms have zero projection.
  Their weighted projections sum to <math|A*u>. On configurations with no
  flags, the sum of both lists is the actual calibrated field, linear in
  <math|u>, real under conjugation, invariant under constant gauge rotations,
  and equal to one at <math|u=e<rsub|0>>.

  These conditions concern unmasked regular functions and their original
  supports. They do not differentiate a health indicator. In the calibrated
  coefficient representation the near list consists of the unit, the fixed
  local-section terms, and regular terms with covering number at most
  <math|m>. The wide list consists of the remaining regular terms. If their
  exponential mass is at most <math|M<rsub|u>> with exponent
  <math|e\<gtr\>0>, then its wide unweighted mass is at most
  <math|e<rsup|-e*m>*M<rsub|u>>: multiply each wide price by
  <math|e<rsup|-e*m>*e<rsup|e*q<rsub|a>>\<geqslant\>1>, since
  <math|q<rsub|a>\<gtr\>m>. The unit and section prices cost at most
  <math|2*M<rsub|u>>, and the remaining near prices at most <math|M<rsub|u>>.
  This proves <eqref|eq:rank-list-input> from those calibrated mass bounds.
  The zero projections are precisely the regular-remainder condition of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-splitting>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Taylor reader from a summable regular
    list><label|lem:rank-list-taylor>Under these data there are polynomials
    <math|P<rsub|u>,Q<rsub|u>,L<rsub|u>=P<rsub|u>+Q<rsub|u>> of degree at
    most <math|D> in the patch link coordinates, such that

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||P<rsub|u>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>3*C<rsub|D>*<around|(|1+n<rsub|b>|)><rsup|D>*M<rsub|u>,<space|2em><around|\||Q<rsub|u>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|D>*<around|(|1+n<rsub|b>|)><rsup|D>*\<eta\>*M<rsub|u>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|n<rsub|b>> is the number of real link coordinates and
    <math|C<rsub|D>> depends only on <math|D> and the colour norm. On a fixed
    ball <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<rho\><rsub|G>> with
    no flags,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:rank-list-taylor-error><around|\||F<rsub|u><around|(|U<around|(|x|)>|)>-L<rsub|u><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>4*M<rsub|u><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsup|D+1>.
    </equation>

    The map <math|u\<mapsto\>L<rsub|u>> is linear, real, unit preserving and
    invariant under simultaneous adjoint rotation of its link variables.
    Moreover, the near polynomial satisfies <eqref|eq:rank-near-germ>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Choose <math|\<rho\><rsub|G>\<leqslant\>1/8> small enough that
    <math|<math-up|chord><around|(|exp X|)>\<leqslant\>2<around|\<\|\|\>|X|\<\|\|\>>>
    on the required colour ball. Then every link of <math|U<around|(|x|)>>
    has chord at most <math|\<theta\>/4> and every plaquette has chord at
    most <math|\<theta\>>; the field has no flags. For
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1>, the curve
    <math|r\<mapsto\>f<rsub|a>*<around|(|U*<around|(|r*v|)>|)>> on
    <math|0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>\<rho\><rsub|G>> has derivative of order
    <math|j\<leqslant\>D+1> equal to its repeated left derivative in the
    single direction <math|\<theta\>*v>. Indeed on each link <math|exp
    <around|(|\<theta\>*<around|(|r+h|)>*v<rsub|e>|)>=exp
    <around|(|\<theta\>*h*v<rsub|e>|)>*exp
    <around|(|\<theta\>*r*v<rsub|e>|)>>; induct on <math|j>. Its derivative
    is bounded by <math|e<rsub|a>>. Taylor's integral remainder therefore
    bounds the error by <math|e<rsub|a><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsup|D+1>/<around|(|D+1|)>!\<leqslant\>e<rsub|a><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsup|D+1>>.

    Let <math|p<rsub|a>> be this degree-<math|D> Taylor polynomial. To bound
    its coefficients, polarize each homogeneous term. For a symmetric
    <math|j>-linear form <math|B>, its values on coordinate vectors are
    bounded by <math|j<rsup|j>/j>! times its diagonal supremum on the unit
    ball: apply

    <\equation*>
      B*<around|(|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|j>|)>=<frac|1|2<rsup|j>*j!>*<big|sum><rsub|\<varepsilon\>\<in\><around|{|-1,1|}><rsup|j>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i>\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>|)>*B*<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i>\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>*v<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,<big|sum><rsub|i>\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>*v<rsub|i>|)>.
    </equation*>

    This formula follows by expansion; every term except the one using each
    <math|v<rsub|i>> once cancels. Coordinate vectors have norm one. Expand
    the Taylor polynomial using ordered coordinate tuples; there are
    <math|n<rsub|b><rsup|j>> such tuples at order <math|j>. Summing for
    <math|0\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>D> gives
    <math|<around|\||p<rsub|a>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|D>*<around|(|1+n<rsub|b>|)><rsup|D>*e<rsub|a>>.
    Consequently both series <math|<big|sum>c<rsub|a>*p<rsub|a>> converge
    absolutely in the finite-dimensional degree-<math|D> polynomial space.
    Define <math|P<rsub|u>,Q<rsub|u>> by these two sums. Summing the
    remainder bounds proves <eqref|eq:rank-list-taylor-error> and the
    coefficient estimates. This also justifies applying any fixed jet
    functional term by term.

    A polynomial of degree at most <math|D> which is
    <math|O<around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsup|D+1>|)>> at zero is
    zero: restrict to every real line and compare coefficients in its scalar
    parameter. Thus the Taylor polynomial is unique. Apply uniqueness to the
    actual field's linearity, real structure, constant-gauge invariance and
    unit to obtain the corresponding properties of <math|L<rsub|u>>. No
    linear choice of the intermediate regular lists is required.

    Finally restrict to a fixed threshold holonomy ray through zero. Its link
    logarithm vanishes at zero. Composing the remainder with this smooth
    curve gives <math|O<around|(|<around|\||r|\|><rsup|D+1>|)>>, which has
    zero derivatives through order <math|d\<leqslant\>D>. Each near term's
    degree-<math|d> reading is therefore the reading of its Taylor
    polynomial. The nonwrapping local section has its prescribed reading and
    each regular remainder has zero reading, by
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-splitting>. Absolute coefficient
    summability permits the fixed finite jet functional to pass through the
    sum. Their weighted sum is <math|E*A*u>, proving
    <eqref|eq:rank-near-germ>.
  </proof>

  The remaining ray estimate also follows at the level of these actual
  polynomials. For each of the finitely many fixed radial tests let
  <math|Z<rsub|\<theta\>><around|(|r|)>> be its link logarithm divided by
  <math|\<theta\>>, and let <math|Z<rsub|0><around|(|r|)>> be its linearized
  link integral. The logarithmic endpoint map constructed in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-splitting> is analytic, vanishes at
  zero and has that link integral as its derivative. Its terms of degree at
  least two have, after division by <math|\<theta\>>, at least one remaining
  factor of <math|\<theta\>>. Through any fixed <math|r>-derivative order the
  connection is a fixed polynomial in the link location. Thus, on links in
  the radius-<math|b> patch, the jets of <math|Z<rsub|\<theta\>>,Z<rsub|0>>
  through order <math|d> are at most <math|C*<around|(|1+b|)><rsup|v>>, and
  those of their difference are at most <math|C*<around|(|1+b|)><rsup|v>*\<theta\>>.
  More explicitly, only Taylor terms of the endpoint map of order at most
  <math|d> can contribute: the test connection vanishes at <math|r=0>; its
  coefficients are polynomials of bounded degree in the location. Each
  contributing coefficient is a finite sum of products of those polynomials
  and fixed derivatives of the endpoint map. Taking their degrees and
  absolute coefficient sums gives the stated <math|C,v>. For a monomial of
  degree at most <math|D>, Leibniz' rule for its <math|r>-derivatives is a
  finite sum over distributions of at most <math|d> derivatives among at most
  <math|D> factors. Subtract products by replacing one factor at a time.
  Every difference contains one jet of <math|Z<rsub|\<theta\>>-Z<rsub|0>>,
  hence one factor <math|\<theta\>>; all other factors have the preceding
  polynomial bound. Summing the monomial coefficients proves
  <eqref|eq:rank-ray-comparison> with <math|C<rsub|b>=C*<around|(|1+b|)><rsup|v>>
  and <math|\<tau\>=\<theta\>>, independently of the number of links.

  <subparagraph|Invariant-frame membership.> The original atlas covariance
  has the form <math|K<rsub|<around|(|x,a|)>,<around|(|y,b|)>>=k<rsub|x*y>*\<delta\><rsub|a*b>>
  with real <math|k<rsub|x*y>>, by <eqref|eq:actual-chart-sum> and the
  reality proof in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-covariance-fourier-window>.
  Its restriction to any set retaining every colour has the same form. Since
  the adjoint colour action is orthogonal,
  <math|<around|(|I\<otimes\><math-up|Ad><rsub|g>|)>*K*<around|(|I\<otimes\><math-up|Ad><rsub|g>|)><rsup|T>=K>.
  The chain rule consequently shows that <math|\<Delta\><rsub|K>>, and hence
  <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|K>>, commutes with that action; its real coefficients
  also commute with conjugation. The axial conversion of link variables into
  sums of based plaquette variables is real and colour equivariant. The
  Taylor lemma therefore makes <math|W<rsub|u>> and <math|\<cal-H\>*u>
  invariant and real in the corresponding senses. The radial reading is
  colour equivariant as well, and the colour action preserves each weighted
  grade. Its truncation is thus an invariant polynomial of weighted grade at
  most <math|d>, precisely an element of the label space defined in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-splitting>. This proves the
  frame-membership hypothesis of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:rank-growing-wick-pin>
  for this atlas; small entries alone would not prove it.

  <subparagraph|Healthy-field approximation by the raw polynomial.> Use the
  axial paths based at the origin in the nonwrapping patch. Each link is a
  product of at most <math|4*b> based plaquette holonomies. Let
  <math|\<tau\>> be the health threshold at which the terminal insertion is
  used, and put <math|a=4*b*\<tau\>>. There are fixed
  <math|c<rsub|G>,C<rsub|G>\<gtr\>0> such that, when
  <math|a\<leqslant\>c<rsub|G>>, the normalized logarithmic link vector
  <math|x> and the linear sum <math|y> of the same plaquette curvatures
  divided by <math|\<theta\>> satisfy

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:rank-axial-terminal-error><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>r\<assign\>2*a/\<theta\>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|x-y|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>R\<assign\>C<rsub|G>*a<rsup|2>/\<theta\>.
  </equation>

  To check this, the local inverse of the exponential has norm at most twice
  the chord and differs from curvature by at most a fixed constant times
  chord squared. Both statements follow from their common derivative at the
  identity and Taylor's formula on a fixed small ball. For a product of
  <math|l\<leqslant\>4*b> plaquettes, subtract its curvature from the sum of
  the individual curvatures successively. The product estimate of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-healthy-cumulant-launch> costs at
  most <math|C*<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>l>j*\<tau\><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C*l<rsup|2>*\<tau\><rsup|2>>.
  Add the logarithm\Ucurvature error of the product and divide by
  <math|\<theta\>>. This proves <eqref|eq:rank-axial-terminal-error>.

  Define <math|W<rsub|u>> by substituting those linear axial sums, multiplied
  by <math|g/\<theta\>>, into <math|L<rsub|u>>, where
  <math|g=\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k><rsup|-1/2>>. Evaluating <math|W<rsub|u>> at
  the based plaquette curvatures divided by <math|g> gives exactly
  <math|L<rsub|u><around|(|y|)>>: the factors <math|g> cancel, with no
  approximation. This is also the radial conversion in the pre-pin
  construction; on a radial test its linear axial sums are the ordinary
  radial link integrals. Let <math|F<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|near>>> be the
  unmasked near-list sum. If <math|r\<leqslant\>\<rho\><rsub|G>> and
  <math|r+R\<leqslant\>1>, then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:rank-raw-terminal-ticket><around|\||F<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|near>><around|(|U|)>-W<rsub|u>*<around|(|g<rsup|-1>*\<cal-C\><around|(|U|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>4*M<rsub|u>*r<rsup|D+1>+\<eta\>*M<rsub|u>+D<around|\||L<rsub|u>|\|><rsub|1>*R.
  </equation>

  Indeed the gauge-fixed field and <math|U<around|(|x|)>> agree on all near
  supports, so their near sums agree by locality and gauge invariance. At
  <math|U<around|(|x|)>> the full list is <math|F<rsub|u>>, its wide sum is
  at most <math|\<eta\>*M<rsub|u>> in absolute value, and its Taylor error is
  bounded by <eqref|eq:rank-list-taylor-error>. Finally, on the coordinate
  unit ball, replacing the factors of each degree-<math|j> monomial one at a
  time bounds <math|<around|\||L<rsub|u><around|(|x|)>-L<rsub|u><around|(|y|)>|\|>>
  by <math|D<around|\||L<rsub|u>|\|><rsub|1>*R>. Every step uses the same
  field and the same raw polynomial. Its physical evaluation is gauge
  invariant because all based curvatures rotate by the gauge at the anchor
  and <math|W<rsub|u>> is invariant. It is measurable and local on the patch,
  being a polynomial in continuous based curvature coordinates. Enlarging to
  a halo of radius <math|q*B> with <math|b=8*B> and <math|q\<geqslant\>8>
  preserves these properties and the healthy-field estimate.

  For the selected thresholds write <math|s=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>,
  <math|H=exp <around|(|<around|(|log log s|)><rsup|2>|)>> and

  <\equation*>
    \<theta\>=s<rsup|-1/2>*H,<space|2em>\<tau\>=s<rsup|-1/2>*exp
    <around|(|<around|(|log log s|)><rsup|3/2>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The patch radius <math|b=8<around|\<lceil\>|log<rsup|2>
  s|\<rceil\>><rsup|2>> grows more slowly than every positive power of
  <math|H>. Thus for every <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>,
  <math|r\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1+\<epsilon\>>> eventually. Also <math|R> and
  <math|\<eta\>=exp <around|(|-e<around|\<lceil\>|log<rsup|2>
  s|\<rceil\>>|)>>, with <math|e> bounded below by a fixed positive constant,
  are smaller than every negative power of <math|H>. Suppose the column and
  dimension prices have been paid so that, with
  <math|\<gamma\>=1/<around|(|16*<around|(|D+2|)>|)>>,

  <\equation*>
    M<rsub|u>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<gamma\>>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>,<space|2em><around|\||L<rsub|u>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>4*H<rsup|2*\<gamma\>>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  Take <math|\<epsilon\>=\<gamma\>>. The first term in
  <eqref|eq:rank-raw-terminal-ticket>, divided by
  <math|<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>>, is at most
  <math|4*H<rsup|-D-1+\<gamma\>*<around|(|D+2|)>>=4*H<rsup|-D-15/16>>. The
  other terms are smaller than <math|H<rsup|-D>> by an arbitrarily large
  power. Consequently, after one uniform increase of the threshold,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:rank-raw-terminal-paid><around|\||F<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|near>><around|(|U|)>-W<rsub|u>*<around|(|g<rsup|-1>*\<cal-C\><around|(|U|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-D>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>.
  </equation>

  For a solved lift the bound in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-coordinate-calibration>
  gives <math|<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|b*d+q<rsub|\<ast\>>+2>>
  eventually, on a fixed bounded target set. Thus any fixed
  <math|D\<gtr\>b*d+q<rsub|\<ast\>>+2+v> makes this error at most
  <math|H<rsup|-v>>. Here the amplification exponent <math|b> is the one in
  the calibration equation, not the patch radius. It is chosen before
  <math|D> and before the stage's derivative order. For the raw coefficient
  bound, each axial row has coefficient sum at most
  <math|4*b*<space|0.17em>g/\<theta\>>. Here
  <math|g/\<theta\>=H<rsup|-1>*<sqrt|s/\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>>\<leqslant\><sqrt|8/7>*<space|0.17em>H<rsup|-1>>.
  Substitution into a degree-<math|D> polynomial costs at most <math|max
  <around|{|1,4*b*<space|0.17em>g/\<theta\>|}><rsup|D>>, which is eventually
  one. Alternatively keeping <math|<around|(|4*b|)><rsup|D>> as a price costs
  only <math|H<rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>>. Thus the same column and dimension
  purchases give <math|<around|\||W<rsub|u>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>H*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>>.
  These are the local value and coefficient bounds needed for a terminal
  stamp; they are not a bound for evaluating <math|\<cal-H\>*u> on the
  physical field.

  <subparagraph|The terminal mask correction and the lift's price.> Let
  <math|f<rsub|u>> be the near sum with each original support mask restored
  except for the scalar unit, which remains unmasked, and let <math|X> be the
  terminal halo containing all those supports and all raw polynomial reads.
  Put <math|\<chi\>=\<b-1\><rsub|<math-up|Healthy><around|(|\<tau\>,X|)>>>.
  Since <math|\<tau\>\<leqslant\>\<theta\>> eventually,
  <math|f<rsub|u>=F<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|near>>> wherever <math|\<chi\>=1>.
  The near-list prices give <math|<around|\||f<rsub|u>|\|>\<leqslant\>3*M<rsub|u>>
  on every field: each masked regular term is bounded by its own zeroth-order
  price. There is the exact decomposition

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:rank-terminal-three-pieces>f<rsub|u>=\<chi\>*W<rsub|u>+\<chi\>*<around|(|F<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|near>>-W<rsub|u>|)>+<around|(|f<rsub|u>-\<chi\>*F<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|near>>|)>.
  </equation>

  The middle term is a supported insertion with value at most
  <math|H<rsup|-D>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>> on its healthy domain; this
  assertion supplies a value price, not a derivative price. The last term
  vanishes on that domain and has value at most <math|3*M<rsub|u>> everywhere
  else. It is therefore a forced insertion, with the halo enlarged to include
  all flag tests of <math|X>. No bound for the unmasked polynomial on an
  unhealthy field has been used: in that region both terms containing
  <math|\<chi\>> vanish. The wide terms keep their original masks and
  exponential support prices; they were not replaced by a polynomial on a
  large wrapping support.

  For an external marker weight <math|\<rho\>\<geqslant\>0>, these two new
  prices are respectively <math|\<rho\>*H<rsup|-D>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>>
  and <math|3*\<rho\>*M<rsub|u>>. If <math|C<rsub|k>> is the column price,
  <math|M<rsub|u>=C<rsub|k>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>>, and
  <math|C<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>H>, the solved-lift estimate above gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||W<rsub|u>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|E>,<space|1em>M<rsub|u>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|E>,<space|2em>E=b*d+q<rsub|\<ast\>>+3,
  </equation*>

  eventually for a fixed bounded set of targets; enlarge the threshold to
  absorb their fixed constants. Choosing <math|\<rho\>=H<rsup|-E-9>> gives
  <math|3*\<rho\>*M<rsub|u>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-8>>. Choosing the Taylor order
  <math|D\<gtr\>E+v> gives the required value error at most <math|H<rsup|-v>>
  before weighting. All choices are made before the final stage threshold.
  The geometric and degree prices of the marked compiler must still be
  applied to these same supports and pieces; the value estimate is not a
  substitute for those separate compiler hypotheses.

  <subparagraph|Attaching the lists to the selected coefficient.> Here are
  the precise data furnished by the calibrated family used at the start of
  this section. At <math|<around|(|k,N,p|)>> let <math|x<rsub|u>> be the
  coordinate of its actual coefficient and put
  <math|r<rsub|k>=<around|(|a<rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|mesh>>|)><rsup|-d>>, so
  that <math|A*u=r<rsub|k>*x<rsub|u>>. Write <math|C<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>1>
  for the column count and <math|\<theta\>> for its threshold. Its regular
  remainder is a countable family <math|<around|(|X<rsub|a>,h<rsub|a>|)>>,
  with nonnegative direction prices <math|e<rsub|a>>, such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:rank-selected-cap><big|sum><rsub|a>e<rsup|e<rsub|k>*q<rsub|a>>*e<rsub|a>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|k><around|\||x<rsub|u><rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>,<space|2em>q<rsub|a>=<math-up|cov><rsub|M<rsub|k>><around|(|X<rsub|a>|)>,<space|2em>M<rsub|k>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    s|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>.
  </equation>

  The supports are connected and contain the anchor; the remainder has zero
  local reading. These are the format and remainder-cap clauses of the
  calibrated coefficient, obtained by the finite backward construction of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-freight-producer> and the splitting
  of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-splitting>. In particular this cap
  is for the coefficient of the selected family, not a separately normalized
  list. As throughout this section, the funded calibrated-family input is
  retained; we are spelling out its use, not constructing a new family.

  Let <math|S<rsub|\<alpha\>>> be the fixed local section for a nonconstant
  frame axis, and let <math|r<rsub|\<alpha\>>> count its curvature factors.
  Its purchased direction price is <math|C<rsub|k>*\<theta\><rsup|r<rsub|\<alpha\>>>>.
  Its coefficient in the original exponential entry is
  <math|\<theta\><rsup|-r<rsub|\<alpha\>>><around|(|x<rsub|u>|)><rsub|\<alpha\>>>.
  Consequently its price after multiplication by <math|r<rsub|k>> is exactly
  <math|r<rsub|k>*C<rsub|k><around|\||<around|(|x<rsub|u>|)><rsub|\<alpha\>>|\|>>.
  The unit contributes <math|r<rsub|k><around|\||<around|(|x<rsub|u>|)><rsub|0>|\|>>,
  with no support mask. Their total is at most
  <math|2*C<rsub|k>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>>. Multiplying
  <eqref|eq:rank-selected-cap> by <math|r<rsub|k>> gives at most
  <math|C<rsub|k>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>> for all the regular terms.
  Split this very list at <math|q<rsub|a>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|k>>. The near and
  wide prices are therefore <math|3*C<rsub|k>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>> and
  <math|e<rsup|-e<rsub|k>*M<rsub|k>>*C<rsub|k>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>>,
  respectively. Thus <eqref|eq:rank-list-input> holds with

  <\equation*>
    M<rsub|u>=C<rsub|k>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>,<space|2em>\<eta\>=e<rsup|-e<rsub|k>*M<rsub|k>>.
  </equation*>

  No lower bound on <math|<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>> is needed: if it
  vanishes, all these nonnegative prices vanish.

  The original covering radius in this calculation is <math|M<rsub|k>>. The
  auxiliary terminal covering radius is <math|B<rsub|k>=M<rsub|k><rsup|2>>.
  They must not be interchanged. A connected support containing the anchor
  with covering number <math|q<rsub|a>> at radius <math|M<rsub|k>> has
  distance from that anchor at most <math|q<rsub|a>*<around|(|4*M<rsub|k>+2|)>+2*M<rsub|k>>:
  order a connected chain of covering boxes from an anchor box to the point's
  box, paying at most <math|4*M<rsub|k>+2> for each link and
  <math|2*M<rsub|k>> for the endpoint boxes. A chain uses at most
  <math|q<rsub|a>> boxes, after deleting loops. For a near support this is at
  most <math|4*M<rsub|k><rsup|2>+4*M<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>8*M<rsub|k><rsup|2>>.
  The fixed local-section stencil also lies in this patch eventually. Hence
  all near supports lie in the very patch <math|b=8*B<rsub|k>> used for
  <math|W<rsub|u>>. The purchased room gives <math|10*b> less than the torus
  side, so these supports are nonwrapping, and a terminal halo of radius
  <math|q*B<rsub|k>>, <math|q\<geqslant\>8>, contains them and all axial
  reads. This checks the support, projection and locality conditions of the
  Taylor construction.

  <subparagraph|Paying the order and columns on one calendar.> Choose
  <math|D> before the stage profile, large enough for the fixed finite grade
  menu, the solved-lift amplification and the requested terminal error.
  Include <math|D+1> in the stage derivative-order request. The column count
  <math|c<rsub|j>> of stage <math|j> is fixed after these order requests and
  before its block length; at level <math|k> it is
  <math|C<rsub|k>=c<rsub|J<around|(|k|)>>>. Put
  <math|\<gamma\>=1/<around|(|16*<around|(|D+2|)>|)>>. For fixed
  <math|c<rsub|j>> there is a finite threshold <math|s<rsub|j>> such that

  <\equation*>
    c<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>exp <around|{|\<gamma\><around|(|log log
    s|)><rsup|2>|}>*<space|1em><around|(|s\<geqslant\>s<rsub|j>|)>.
  </equation*>

  This follows directly by taking logarithms; it is enough that <math|log log
  s\<geqslant\><sqrt|log c<rsub|j>/\<gamma\>>>. Include <math|s<rsub|j>>
  among the post-length delays in the purchase at the beginning of this
  section. It is independent of <math|N,p,u>. For a stage with several
  requests take the largest threshold and derivative order; the menu is
  finite. The all-stage payment then gives
  <math|C<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<gamma\>>> at every admitted level, not
  merely on a subsequence. Increasing delays does not change already selected
  profiles or columns. This is the exact column purchase needed above; the
  weaker <math|C<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>H> in the forced-price calculation is an
  immediate consequence.

  There are at most <math|c<rsub|G>*<around|(|2*b+1|)><rsup|4>> real link
  coordinates in the patch, uniformly in the period. Since
  <math|b=8*M<rsub|k><rsup|2>>, its logarithm is <math|O<around|(|log log
  s|)>>, whereas <math|log H=<around|(|log log s|)><rsup|2>>. Thus
  <math|C<rsub|D>*<around|(|1+n<rsub|b>|)><rsup|D>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<gamma\>>>
  eventually. The Taylor estimate now gives both
  <math|M<rsub|u>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<gamma\>>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>>
  and <math|<around|\||L<rsub|u>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>4*H<rsup|2*\<gamma\>>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>>.
  The same argument absorbs every fixed power of <math|b> in the ray and
  raw-coefficient estimates. The positive support exponent can be kept
  bounded below, for example by one, in the finite stage requests. Its wide
  price is then smaller than every power of <math|H<rsup|-1>>. All these
  delays precede the selection of the finite-volume endpoint. They prove the
  quantitative hypotheses of the pre-pin and terminal estimates on that
  endpoint simultaneously.

  <subparagraph|Which part of the birth is replaced.> Write
  <math|h<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|wide>>> for the wide sum with its original
  masks. The selected coefficient's exponential entry, in physical
  normalization, is exactly

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:rank-selected-entry-split>F<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|exp>>=f<rsub|u>+h<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|wide>>.
  </equation>

  Indeed its definition is the global scalar, the masked local section and
  the masked regular series. Expand the local section in its frame axes and
  split the regular series at <math|q<rsub|a>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|k>>. The
  absolute majorant in <eqref|eq:rank-selected-cap> permits this partition on
  every configuration, including configurations on mask boundaries. This
  proves <eqref|eq:rank-selected-entry-split> with precisely the lists used
  to construct <math|L<rsub|u>> and <math|W<rsub|u>>.

  The actual coefficient density also has its original forced terms;
  <eqref|eq:rank-selected-entry-split> does not discard them. Replace only
  <math|f<rsub|u>> by <eqref|eq:rank-terminal-three-pieces>. Keep
  <math|h<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|wide>>> and all original forced terms at
  their original addresses. This is an exact equality of the single-mark
  density on the same configuration and background representation. For
  several marks the terminal expression is the finite squarefree exponential
  of these marked contributions, together with their prescribed background
  factors. Substitution of the single-mark equality in each factor preserves
  every coefficient: expand the finite exponential, and distribute each
  chosen singleton among its three replacement terms. The wide and original
  forced factors are unchanged. Absolute support prices justify any countable
  regular sums before this finite algebraic operation. Translations, signed
  coordinate changes and conjugation preserve the identity by pullback. The
  new principal singleton is <math|\<chi\>*W<rsub|u>>; its supported error
  and mask correction are exactly the other two terms of
  <eqref|eq:rank-terminal-three-pieces>, with the prices already proved. In
  particular the raw polynomial is not asserted to equal the full birth
  coefficient, nor to equal its near part off the healthy set.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Selected-family tested readout><label|prop:rank-selected-tested-readout>For
    the funded calibrated family and atlas of this section, include the
    order, column and room purchases just described in its common calendar.
    Then the construction supplies (T1)\U(T4) at every sufficiently large
    admitted level, for all the stated cutoffs and periods, with one pair
    <math|<around|(|\<cal-H\>,T|)>> for all labels of each fixed grade. It
    uses the same endpoint and the same masked terminal coefficient as the
    rank-test construction.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The selected cap, section-price cancellation and patch containment verify
    the list hypotheses. Their Taylor polynomial is linear, real and unit
    preserving by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:rank-list-taylor>. Form
    <math|W<rsub|u>> by the axial substitution and
    <math|\<cal-H\>*u=\<cal-W\><rsub|K<rsub|k>>*W<rsub|u>>, and define
    <math|T> to be its low-grade reading. This proves (T1). For clarity,
    apply the pre-pin to <math|p<rsub|u><around|(|y|)>=W<rsub|u>*<around|(|z*y|)>>,
    with <math|z=\<theta\>/g>. Its axial rows have coefficient norm at most
    <math|4*b>; thus <math|J<rsub|k>=max <around|(|1,4*b|)>> satisfies
    <math|J<rsub|k><rsup|D>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<gamma\>>> eventually. The
    normalized radial cochain reader has fixed degree in the lattice
    location, so each row on the radius-<math|b> patch has coefficient sum at
    most a fixed power of <math|1+b>, hence at most <math|H<rsup|\<alpha\>>>
    eventually, where <math|\<alpha\>=4*\<gamma\>>. The ray estimate has the
    same polynomial cost, and <math|\<theta\>,\<eta\>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-4>>
    eventually. These verify Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:rank-prepin-payment>,
    with zero additional nonlinear defect after axial conversion. The
    original chart covariance has its reserved entry cost at most
    <math|H<rsup|1/4>> by Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-covariance-fourier-window>;
    since <math|z<rsup|2>\<geqslant\><around|(|7/8|)>*H<rsup|2>>, its
    rescaled entries are at most <math|<around|(|8/7|)>*H<rsup|-7/4>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1>>
    eventually. The invariant-frame argument therefore applies to
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:rank-growing-wick-pin>, giving (T2) and (T3) in
    precisely the coupling normalization of (T1). The healthy raw estimate
    and its payment give (T4), with the terminal identification proved in
    <eqref|eq:rank-selected-entry-split>. The covariance conversion is
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:rank-raw-wick-identity>. Every operation uses
    the original coefficient at <math|<around|(|k,N,p|)>>, so the same
    <math|W<rsub|u>>, <math|\<cal-H\>*u> and <math|T*u> occur throughout. The
    finite grade menu allows a common maximum of all thresholds. No Gaussian
    identification of the full nonlinear density is used; the remaining
    per-step compiler and reader inputs belong to
    Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:allarity-step-supply>.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|History and readout on the chart
    window><label|prop:rank-trace-chart-window>Assume (V) and the local
    inputs of this section, including the funded calibrated family of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:rank-selected-tested-readout>. Include
    in the all-stage payment <eqref|eq:common-allstage-payment> the
    thresholds of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:restarted-rank-history> and
    of the readout construction above, for each stage <math|j> and for the
    finitely many block ratios <math|L<rsub|j-1>,L<rsub|j>>, the width
    parameter <math|<wide|b|\<bar\>>> and the trace menu. These thresholds
    are fixed after the block lengths and before the coupling threshold. Then
    for every sufficiently large level <math|k>, every chart at level
    <math|k> with base arc <math|b\<leqslant\><wide|b|\<bar\>>>, every
    <math|N\<geqslant\>k> and every <math|p>, the following hold.

    <\enumerate>
      <item>The constant-coupling auxiliary history of
      Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:restarted-rank-history>, with coupling
      <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>>, exists on the whole window
      <math|<around|[|w,k|]>>. Its input costs are at most
      <math|q<rsub|n>\<leqslant\>s<rsup|-1/400>> for
      <math|0\<leqslant\>n\<leqslant\>k-w>, and it satisfies the density
      identities and the endpoint retention stated there. At every level
      <math|j> of the window its coupling satisfies
      <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>\<leqslant\>4*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>>.

      <item>For all sufficiently large <math|N>, uniformly in <math|p>, there
      is a readout <math|T<rsub|k,N,p>> satisfying
      <eqref|eq:rank-common-tested>.
    </enumerate>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    <em|Window length.> By Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-firesky-clock-fields>
    and its proof, <math|k-w=O<around|(|<around|(|log k|)><rsup|2>|)>> and
    <math|log log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>log k+C<rsub|1>>.
    Directly, the two maximality bounds give

    <\equation*>
      k-w\<leqslant\>2+<frac|<around|(|<wide|b|\<bar\>>+2|)>*y<rsup|2>+10*log
      c<rsub|k>|log 3>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|4>
    </equation*>

    for large <math|s>, since <math|c<rsub|k>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    s|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>> gives <math|log c<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|2>>
    eventually. The construction of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:restarted-rank-history>
    uses its interval length only through
    <math|0\<leqslant\>n\<leqslant\>h\<leqslant\>y<rsup|4>> in
    <eqref|eq:restart-decay>. That decay bound is uniform for
    <math|n\<leqslant\>y<rsup|4>>. So it applies verbatim to an interval of
    length <math|k-w>.

    <em|Block ratios.> By the bracket of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-firesky-clock-fields>,
    every step of <math|<around|[|w,k|]>> has block ratio
    <math|L<rsub|J<around|(|k|)>>> or <math|L<rsub|J<around|(|k|)>-1>>. For a
    fixed ratio, that construction uses the ratio only through the fixed
    factor <math|C<rsub|L,a,t>> and the thresholds after which its eventual
    inequalities hold. Taking the larger of the two constants and the later
    of the two thresholds covers the numerical estimates for both ratios. The
    passage from a step at one stage to a step at the other is
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:restart-stage-boundary>, since the window
    contains levels of the stages <math|J<around|(|k|)>> and
    <math|J<around|(|k|)>-1> only. Its hypotheses at the steps of the window
    are the input (V). The thresholds depend only on the stage
    <math|J<around|(|k|)>>, on <math|<wide|b|\<bar\>>> and on the finite
    menu. They are therefore paid at every level by the all-stage payment,
    which gives <math|H<rsub|J*<around|(|k+1|)>>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>
    for every <math|k>.

    <em|Couplings.> By Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:rank-trace-endpoint>,
    <math|<around|\||\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>-s|\|>\<leqslant\>s/8> for large
    <math|k>. By the plateau of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-firesky-clock-fields>,
    every level <math|j> of the window has local scale
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<in\><around|[|s/2,s|]>>. The joining
    argument of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:restarted-rank-history> then
    places the constant coupling in the purchased band, and gives
    <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>\<leqslant\>9*s/8\<leqslant\><tfrac|9|4><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>4*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>>.
    Item<nbsp>1 follows by composition, as there.

    <em|Readout.> Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:rank-selected-tested-readout>
    supplies (T), hence item<nbsp>2, on this same endpoint. Its finite-menu
    purchases are included in the all-stage payment, so it holds at every
    sufficiently large admitted level, not just the unbounded subsequence
    used for the rank tests above.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The output representation meets the ticket
    suppositions><label|prop:rank-trace-output-lawful>Assume the conclusions
    of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:rank-trace-chart-window>(1). Assume
    also that the auxiliary representation <math|P<rsub|w>> at the root depth
    <math|w> carries a threshold with

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:rank-trace-threshold-window><around|(|8/7|)><rsup|1/2>*<space|0.17em><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|w><rsup|-1/2>\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsub|P<rsub|w>>\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|clock>><around|(|w|)>,
    </equation>

    and that the stage profiles have clip constants with
    <math|0\<less\>c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>\<less\>c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>/16>.
    Assume finally the following path-and-clip receipt.

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(P)>The coarse path action of <math|P<rsub|w>> in the restarted
      representation is the retained path <math|R<rsub|N,w>> of
      Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:path-retained-plan>, evaluated at the
      constant coupling <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>>. At that coupling it
      satisfies <math|R<rsub|N,w>.<math-up|Bounds><around|(|p<rsub|w>,\<sigma\><rsub|w>,A<rsub|w>,<math-up|loopCap><rsub|w>|)>>,
      with the clip <math|p<rsub|w>> and row price <math|A<rsub|w>> of the
      stage <math|\<pi\><rsub|J<around|(|w|)>>>.
    </enumerate>

    Then, for all sufficiently large <math|k>, every chart at level <math|k>
    with base arc <math|b\<leqslant\><wide|b|\<bar\>>>, every
    <math|N\<geqslant\>k> and every <math|p>, <math|P<rsub|w>> is a lawful
    representation satisfying the suppositions of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:rank-root-output-ticket>(2) at the
    output level <math|w>:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>its coupling <math|\<beta\>=\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>> satisfies
      <math|g=\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsub|P<rsub|w>>\<leqslant\>1>
      and <math|\<beta\>\<leqslant\>4*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|w>>;

      <item><math|\<theta\><rsub|P<rsub|w>>\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|clock>><around|(|w|)>>;

      <item>its loop budget satisfies <math|\<Lambda\>\<leqslant\>\<Lambda\><rsub|<math-up|clock>><around|(|w|)>>,
      and its clip and row bound are those of the stage
      <math|\<pi\><rsub|J<around|(|w|)>>>;

      <item>its additive terms have value bound <math|\<delta\>\<leqslant\>1>
      per site.
    </enumerate>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    <em|Lawfulness.> By Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:restarted-rank-history>,
    the auxiliary representation has gauge-invariant, local and
    motion-covariant path action, additive terms and activities. Its density
    is the push <eqref|eq:restart-push> of the nonnegative density above it
    along a positive kernel, so it is nonnegative. The clip constants are
    those of the profile.

    <em|Coupling.> By Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:rank-trace-endpoint> and the
    plateau, <math|\<beta\>\<geqslant\><tfrac|7|8><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>\<geqslant\><tfrac|7|8><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|w>>,
    so <math|g\<leqslant\><around|(|8/7|)><rsup|1/2>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|w><rsup|-1/2>\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsub|P<rsub|w>>>
    by <eqref|eq:rank-trace-threshold-window>. Also
    <math|\<theta\><rsub|P<rsub|w>>\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|clock>><around|(|w|)>\<leqslant\>1>
    for large <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|w>>, and
    <math|\<beta\>\<leqslant\>4*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|w>> is part of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:rank-trace-chart-window>(1). Since
    <math|w<around|(|k|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>, these thresholds are reached for
    all large <math|k>.

    <em|Threshold.> This is the upper bound in
    <eqref|eq:rank-trace-threshold-window>.

    <em|Paths.> By (P), the coarse path action of <math|P<rsub|w>> is
    <math|R<rsub|N,w>> at the coupling <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>>, and it
    satisfies the bounds of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:path-admissible>
    there. Its clip is <math|p<rsub|w>>, its row price is <math|A<rsub|w>>,
    and its loop budget is at most <math|<math-up|loopCap><rsub|w>=2*\<cal-M\><rsub|w><rsup|\<theta\><rsub|J<around|(|w|)>>>=\<Lambda\><rsub|<math-up|clock>><around|(|w|)>>,
    by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:common-loop-capacity> and
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:rank-root-clock-threshold>, with
    <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|j>=\<theta\><rsub|j>>.

    <em|Additive terms.> By <eqref|eq:restart-anchored>, summing the anchored
    bounds gives <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>><around|\||E<rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*q<rsub|n>>,
    so the value bound per site is at most
    <math|q<rsub|n>\<leqslant\>s<rsup|-1/400>\<leqslant\>1> by
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:rank-trace-chart-window>(1).
  </proof>

  Two thresholds satisfy <eqref|eq:rank-trace-threshold-window> for all large
  <math|k>. The first is the threshold prescribed in (F3),
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|w><rsup|-1/2>*exp <around|(|<around|(|log log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|w>|)><rsup|3/2>|)>>, since <math|log log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|w>\<geqslant\>1> on this clock and the exponential
  tends to infinity. The second is the clock threshold
  <math|\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|clock>><around|(|w|)>> itself, since
  <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|w>\<to\>\<infty\>>. So the proposition applies whether
  the auxiliary history keeps the threshold of the selected history at level
  <math|w> or carries the threshold of (F3).

  The path and clip assertions in (P) follow from the restart construction
  once its local hypotheses hold on the window. The threshold conditions are
  the two explicit inequalities in <eqref|eq:rank-trace-threshold-window>;
  there is no additional compatibility test left implicit here.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The path part of the receipt (P)><label|lem:rank-trace-path-part>Suppose
    that at every step of the window <math|<around|[|w,k|]>> the restart
    construction is applied as in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-local-compiler-handoff>,
    with the same-course conditions stated there. Then the coarse path action
    of <math|P<rsub|w>> in the restarted representation is the retained path
    <math|R<rsub|N,w>>, and its clip is <math|p<rsub|w>>. Moreover
    <math|R<rsub|N,w>.<math-up|Bounds><around|(|p<rsub|w>,\<sigma\><rsub|w>,A<rsub|w>,<math-up|loopCap><rsub|w>|)>>
    holds, with the clip <math|p<rsub|w>> and the row price <math|A<rsub|w>>
    of the stage <math|\<pi\><rsub|J<around|(|w|)>>>, whatever the coupling
    of the representation. Hence the path-and-clip receipt (P) holds.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The selected history carries the selected paths <math|R<rsub|N,j>> and
    the stage clips <math|p<rsub|j>> at every level <math|j>
    (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history>). At <math|k> the
    restarted history uses the endpoint re-expression of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:restart-endpoint>, which preserves the
    selected path and clip while changing the budget and keeping the density.
    Each restart step sets the new coarse coupling equal to the incoming one
    and preserves the original coarse path and clip, that is, those of the
    same step of the selected history (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-local-compiler-handoff>).
    By induction down the window, the restarted representation at every level
    <math|j\<in\><around|[|w,k|]>> carries <math|R<rsub|N,j>> and
    <math|p<rsub|j>>. A path is a collection of row, loop, cubic and quartic
    coefficients, and the predicate <math|<math-up|Bounds>> concerns only
    these coefficients: weighted sums, invariance, the metric identity, and
    the summability and vanishing of the loop moments. It does not involve
    the coupling or the threshold. By Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:path-retained-plan>,
    <math|R<rsub|N,w>> is admissible at <math|<around|(|N,w|)>>, so by
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:path-admissible> it satisfies the
    displayed bounds.
  </proof>

  The phrase \Pevaluated at the constant coupling\Q in (P) therefore adds
  nothing to the path bounds: the coupling enters the density, not the path
  coefficients. The same-course conditions are the local hypotheses of the
  restarted step, which the input (V) also concerns.

  <\corollary>
    <dueto|All-level lawful history for the trace
    chart><label|cor:rank-trace-all-levels>Fix a finite grade menu and
    <math|<wide|b|\<bar\>>\<gtr\>0>. Keep the funded chart/stamp hypotheses
    and finite adjacent-pair purchases of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:restart-actual-closure>, together with
    the selected path bounds and the stage clip constants
    <math|0\<less\>c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>\<less\>c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>/16>.
    Then the family of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:restart-chart-family>
    supplies (F3) of Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-lattice-firesky-witness>
    at every sufficiently large level <math|k>, for every chart with arc
    <math|0\<less\>b\<leqslant\><wide|b|\<bar\>>>. The assertion holds
    simultaneously for every <math|j\<in\><around|[|w,k|]>>, every
    <math|N\<gtr\>k> and every period <math|p>.
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    Use the constructed <math|P<rsub|j>> themselves throughout. Their budget
    radius is <math|<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>><rsup|2>> and their
    threshold is exactly <math|\<tau\><rsub|j>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j><rsup|-1/2>*exp
    <around|(|<around|(|log log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|)><rsup|3/2>|)>>,
    by Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:restart-chart-family>. The same
    proposition supplies the density identities, nonnegativity, local format,
    and unchanged path and clip at every level, including the re-expressed
    top <math|k>. The path bounds of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:path-admissible>
    concern the coefficients and are independent of the coupling. Thus the
    argument of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:rank-trace-path-part> applies to
    every <math|j>.

    Here are all the numerical tests for the normalization estimate. Set
    <math|\<beta\>=\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>> and <math|g=\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>>.
    The endpoint running estimate and the plateau give, for every
    <math|j\<in\><around|[|w,k|]>>,

    <\equation*>
      <tfrac|7|8><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>\<beta\>\<leqslant\><tfrac|9|8><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>,<space|2em><tfrac|1|2><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>.
    </equation*>

    Consequently <math|\<beta\>\<gtr\>0>, <math|\<beta\>*g<rsup|2>=1>,
    <math|\<beta\>\<leqslant\><tfrac|9|4><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>4*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>>
    and <math|g\<leqslant\><sqrt|8/7>*<space|0.17em><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j><rsup|-1/2>>.
    The factor <math|exp <around|(|<around|(|log log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|)><rsup|3/2>|)>> eventually exceeds
    <math|<sqrt|8/7>>, so <math|g\<leqslant\>\<tau\><rsub|j>>. For <math|log
    log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1>, its exponent is no larger
    than <math|<around|(|log log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|)><rsup|2>>; hence
    <math|\<tau\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|clock>><around|(|j|)>\<leqslant\>1>
    on the purchased tail. The clip is exactly the stage clip, not merely
    bounded by one. Its row bound is <math|A<rsub|j>> and its loop allowance
    is bounded by <math|<math-up|loopCap><rsub|j>=\<Lambda\><rsub|<math-up|clock>><around|(|j|)>>,
    by the selected path bounds and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:common-loop-capacity>.
    Finally the additive value cost is at most
    <math|q<rsub|k-j>\<leqslant\>s<rsup|-1/400>\<leqslant\>1>, where
    <math|s=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>. These are the clip, coupling,
    entrance, upper-threshold, additive and loop tests used in
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:rank-root-output-ticket>(2). They leave
    no separate test\Uthreshold assumption.

    The costs are uniform for <math|0\<leqslant\>k-j\<leqslant\>k-w>, and the
    plateau makes <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>s/2> throughout.
    For each fixed stage pair the remaining tails depend only on its
    purchased parameters; the all-stage payment includes their finite
    maximum. Thus one large-level threshold works for the whole window and is
    independent of <math|N,p> and of the chosen chart with bounded arc. This
    proves (F3).
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|Status.> The actual restarted construction now supplies (V),
  and the preceding corollary supplies (F3) on its entire window, under the
  funded chart/stamp and selected-path hypotheses explicitly stated there.
  The endpoint is re-expressed at the smaller threshold with equal density;
  it is not left in its original budget. The chosen family is retained in
  every subsequent comparison, so no equality with a second family is
  inferred from endpoint agreement. The readout and its stamp are supplied by
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:rank-selected-tested-readout> under its
  stated funded hypotheses. These local conclusions do not by themselves
  perform the joint chart selection or the stress calibration required by the
  trace endpoint.

  <subsection|All powers of the corrected phase
  detectors><label|sec:actual-phase-detector-moments>

  The rank comparisons must survive passage to a phase. A bound on the
  unselected one- and two-point functions is insufficient: we need bounds on
  every power of the complete corrected operators. We now obtain those bounds
  from the same finite-cutoff corrected root estimates.

  Retain the hierarchy, its reflection-positive Hilbert space, the bounded
  slab operators, and their ground-space compressions <math|M<around|(|w|)>>
  from Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-phases>. We use their permutation,
  empty-word and adjoint laws, the separated-cluster limit of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-cluster-train>, and the reflected-word
  identification established by the real phase-grid comparison. In
  particular, if <math|<wide|w|\<bar\>>> denotes ordinary conjugation of all
  labels without reflecting its points, then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:episode-conjugate>M<around|(|<wide|w|\<bar\>>|)>=M<around|(|w|)><rsup|\<ast\>>.
  </equation>

  We justify this identity on the selected hierarchy, including the
  intermediate representation needed by the real phase-grid construction.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Real phase-grid application on the selected
    family><label|lem:c14-selected-phase-reflection>The real phase-grid
    approximants of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-phase-grid-approximants>
    have their served two-stage representation on the selected family.
    Consequently the ordinary-reflection identity of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-ordinary-reflection> holds on its ground space
    and implies <eqref|eq:episode-conjugate>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Fix a grade, a real frame vector <math|e<rsub|s>>, and the finite set
    <math|I> of opened cells used in the grid approximation. The selected
    service and uniform census provide the negative terminal birth
    <math|A<rsub|N>> and its served push <math|P<rsub|k,N>*A<rsub|N>> at the
    intermediate level <math|k>, with the same doors and calibrated backward
    coefficient family. Insert the positive primary <math|F<rsub|v>> in each
    cell by forming

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|k>=<around|(|P<rsub|k,N>*A<rsub|N>|)>*exp<rsub|<math-up|nil>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|v\<in\>I>z<rsub|v>*F<rsub|v>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Every coefficient is integrable: the pushed coefficients are integrable,
    the primaries are bounded measurable functions on the compact lattice
    configuration space, and the marker algebra is finite. If
    <math|f<rsub|v>,\<lambda\><rsub|v>> are the served regular and forced
    singletons, keep the ordinary and flagged higher arrays unchanged and
    replace only <math|f<rsub|v>> by <math|f<rsub|v>+F<rsub|v>>. The identity
    <math|exp<rsub|<math-up|nil>><around|(|u+v|)>=exp<rsub|<math-up|nil>>
    u*exp<rsub|<math-up|nil>> v> proves equality of the whole representation,
    at every marker mode. The primary has zero forced profile, so
    <math|\<lambda\><rsub|v>> is unchanged. This is the
    intermediate-insertion construction of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-local-pairing>; its
    algebra applies at every fixed grade, not only to the particular trace
    labels used there.

    Below <math|k>, the backward coefficient family has exactly the regular
    and forced adjacent differences specified in the phase-grid construction.
    Its two displayed transition prices are at most one, with the same
    migrated centers and occupancy. Thus the same-door transfer applies to
    the whole polynomial, retaining both higher arrays. Induction to the
    opened level gives a served representation with regular price at most
    <math|2>, forced price at most <math|1>, and reserve <math|1>. The
    full-census cost is therefore <math|2+1+2=5> per cell. Coefficientwise
    preservation of the integral and expansion over subsets of <math|I>
    identify its full mode with the product of the copied differences between
    the backtransported primary and the negative terminal observable. This
    proves the root bound with the exact partition function used in the
    phase-grid lemma. The realification, finite basis selection and
    replication argument of that section now supply the bounded real
    approximants required by Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-public-grill>, on
    this same hierarchy.

    Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-ordinary-reflection> consequently gives
    <math|M*<around|(|\<rho\>*w|)>=M<around|(|w|)>> for ordinary reflection
    <math|\<rho\>>. The reflected adjoint word is the reverse of
    <math|\<rho\>*<wide|w|\<bar\>>>; the slab-adjoint law, permutation
    invariance and ordinary-reflection identity yield
    <math|M<around|(|w|)><rsup|\<ast\>>=M*<around|(|\<rho\>*<wide|w|\<bar\>>|)>=M<around|(|<wide|w|\<bar\>>|)>>.
    The argument includes the empty word, for which both sides are the
    identity operator.
  </proof>

  Fix a grade and one of the comparison trials of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-rank-purchases>. Write <math|k> for
  its grid depth, <math|b\<leqslant\>k> for its root-test depth, and
  <math|g\<geqslant\>0> for its corrected root price. At its finitely many
  row sites <math|x<rsub|j>> let <math|f<rsub|j><rsup|N,p>> be the actual
  cutoff insertion with the trial's lifted label. Along the selected
  mesh-then-period order those labels converge to labels <math|f<rsub|j>> on
  the same hierarchy. Write <math|a<rsub|j>> for the prescribed free
  one-point value, and <math|\<kappa\><rsub|j*h>> for the prescribed
  connected pair value. The trial supplies chamber interiority and, for every
  <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, eventually in that order,

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|j><rsup|N,p>-a<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|root>,b>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>g+\<varepsilon\>,<eq-number><label|eq:episode-root-one>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|f<rsub|j><rsup|N,p>-a<rsub|j>|)>*<around|(|f<rsub|h><rsup|N,p>-a<rsub|h>|)>-\<kappa\><rsub|j*h>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|root>,b>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>g+\<varepsilon\>*<space|1em><around|(|j\<less\>h|)>.<eq-number><label|eq:episode-root-two>>>>>
  </align>

  These are the corrected polynomial estimates purchased before extraction,
  not estimates of a product of uncorrected root norms. Constant functions,
  sums and products preserve the chamber-interior property, so it also holds
  for the displayed corrections.

  <subsubsection|A finite polynomial with coupled cutoff and limiting labels>

  Consider a finite sum of words

  <\equation*>
    Z<rsup|N,p><around|(|u|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>=1><rsup|L>c<rsub|\<alpha\>>*<big|prod><rsub|j=1><rsup|n<rsub|\<alpha\>>>f<rsub|\<alpha\>*j><rsup|N,p>*<around|(|x<rsub|\<alpha\>*j>+u|)>,<space|2em>Z=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>=1><rsup|L>c<rsub|\<alpha\>>*M<around|(|w<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Each word uses sites on the depth-<math|k> grid that are generic: in every
  coordinate its distinct slots have distinct coordinates. Different words in
  the sum may share sites. Empty words are allowed and represent the constant
  <math|1> at both cutoff and limit. The coefficients
  <math|c<rsub|\<alpha\>>> are fixed before the cutoff limits. Each cutoff
  label converges in the selected order to the label of the corresponding
  slot of <math|w<rsub|\<alpha\>>>. We call this coupled finite list a
  polynomial packet. Suppose its cutoff polynomial at <math|u=0> is
  chamber-interior and has root price at most <math|g+\<varepsilon\>> at
  depth <math|b>, eventually for every <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>.

  For <math|q\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and integers <math|t\<geqslant\>1> place
  <math|2*q> translates of this whole polynomial at physical displacements

  <\equation*>
    u<rsub|j><around|(|t|)>=2*t*a<rsub|b>*j*<around|(|1,1,1,1|)>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>j\<less\>2*q.
  </equation*>

  These are depth-<math|k> grid displacements because <math|b\<leqslant\>k>
  and the scales are aligned. All coarse cell addresses are even in every
  coordinate. Consequently copying from the home cell to them is ordinary
  translation, without a reflection or conjugation of the copied polynomial.
  At even <math|j> use <math|<wide|Z<rsup|N,p>|\<bar\>>>, and at odd <math|j>
  use <math|Z<rsup|N,p>>. Conjugation does not change a root price: it
  conjugates the full copied product and its expectation, preserving its
  absolute value.

  For each fixed <math|t,q>, sufficiently large periods make the <math|2*q>
  cell addresses distinct. Indeed <math|2*t*<around|(|2*q|)>> not exceeding
  the coarse period suffices; reducing modulo that period is then injective
  on the chosen addresses. The planted inequality on the same Wilson law
  gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:episode-cutoff-power><around*|\||\<bbb-E\><rsub|N,p>*<big|prod><rsub|j=0><rsup|2*q-1><choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<wide|Z<rsup|N,p><around|(|u<rsub|j><around|(|t|)>|)>|\<bar\>>,>|<cell|j<text|even>,>>|<row|<cell|Z<rsup|N,p><around|(|u<rsub|j><around|(|t|)>|)>,>|<cell|j<text|odd>>>>>>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|g+\<varepsilon\>|)><rsup|2*q>.
  </equation>

  The assertion is eventual in period and then in the mesh tail at that
  period. It makes no claim at periods too small to hold the distinct copies.
  For <math|q=0> both sides equal <math|1>.

  Expand the product into its finitely many choices of a monomial for each of
  the <math|2*q> packets. Within each chosen monomial the slots are generic.
  Between slots in different packets, equality of any one coordinate is an
  affine equation in <math|t> with nonzero slope, since their packet indices
  differ. It excludes at most one value of <math|t>. There are finitely many
  slot pairs, coordinates and monomial choices. Thus all sufficiently large
  <math|t> make every expanded configuration generic. For each such fixed
  <math|t>, take the fine-mesh and period limits in their prescribed order.
  The same limiting labels are used in every term. Ordinary conjugation of a
  cutoff insertion agrees with conjugation of its label once its fixed
  reference depth is admitted. Convergence of these conjugated labels follows
  from continuity of conjugation. The point-limit theorem therefore applies
  term by term. At finite cutoff the integrands are continuous on the compact
  configuration space, so their finite sums are integrable and expansion
  under the integral is legitimate. This proves a limiting finite sum
  <math|P<rsub|q><around|(|t|)>> with

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:episode-first-limit><around|\||P<rsub|q><around|(|t|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|g+\<varepsilon\>|)><rsup|2*q>
  </equation>

  for all sufficiently large <math|t>.

  Now let <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>>. The time-coordinate separation between
  packets <math|j\<less\>h> is <math|2*t*a<rsub|b>*<around|(|h-j|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>.
  The separated-cluster theorem permits the simultaneous spatial translations
  used here. Apply it to each expanded monomial, retaining the order of its
  packets. By <eqref|eq:episode-conjugate>, the even packets converge to
  adjoints of their compressed whole-word operators. Summing the finitely
  many terms gives

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|q><around|(|t|)>\<longrightarrow\><around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|0>,<around|(|Z<rsup|\<ast\>>*Z|)><rsup|q>*\<Omega\><rsub|0>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation*>

  This is simply expansion of the ordered product
  <math|Z<rsup|\<ast\>>*Z*\<cdots\>*Z<rsup|\<ast\>>*Z>; commutativity is not
  needed for this identification. Take real parts in
  <eqref|eq:episode-first-limit> and then let
  <math|\<varepsilon\>*\<downarrow\>*0>. We have proved

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:episode-all-powers><around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|0>,<around|(|Z<rsup|\<ast\>>*Z|)><rsup|q>*\<Omega\><rsub|0>|\<rangle\>>\<leqslant\>g<rsup|2*q>*<space|2em><around|(|q=0,1,2,\<ldots\>|)>.
  </equation>

  The left side is real and nonnegative. The order of limits was: fix
  <math|q,\<varepsilon\>>; fix sufficiently large <math|t>; take the cutoff
  limits; take <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>>; finally take
  <math|\<varepsilon\>*\<downarrow\>*0>. No uniform-in-<math|q> cutoff
  threshold or interchange of those limits has been used.

  <subsubsection|The two actual corrected polynomials>

  Apply this result first to the two-term packet with coefficients
  <math|1,-a<rsub|j>> and words <math|<around|[|f<rsub|j>|]>,\<varnothing\>>.
  Its cutoff polynomial is exactly <math|f<rsub|j><rsup|N,p>-a<rsub|j>>, and
  its compressed limit is

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|j>=M<around|(|f<rsub|j>|)>-a<rsub|j>*I.
  </equation*>

  Use the actual trial's lifted labels at cutoff and its limiting labels in
  the compressed word. Genericity is inherited from the trial's row. Thus
  <eqref|eq:episode-root-one> supplies the required root bound.

  For <math|j\<less\>h> use the four words
  <math|<around|[|f<rsub|j>,f<rsub|h>|]>,<around|[|f<rsub|h>|]>,<around|[|f<rsub|j>|]>,\<varnothing\>>
  with coefficients <math|1,-a<rsub|j>,-a<rsub|h>,a<rsub|h>*a<rsub|j>-\<kappa\><rsub|j*h>>.
  The finite-cutoff polynomial is

  <\equation*>
    f<rsub|j><rsup|N,p>*f<rsub|h><rsup|N,p>-a<rsub|j>*f<rsub|h><rsup|N,p>-a<rsub|h>*f<rsub|j><rsup|N,p>+a<rsub|h>*a<rsub|j>-\<kappa\><rsub|j*h>=<around|(|f<rsub|j><rsup|N,p>-a<rsub|j>|)>*<around|(|f<rsub|h><rsup|N,p>-a<rsub|h>|)>-\<kappa\><rsub|j*h>.
  </equation*>

  Its compressed limit is the entire operator

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|j*h>=M*<around|(|f<rsub|j>*f<rsub|h>|)>-a<rsub|j>*M<around|(|f<rsub|h>|)>-a<rsub|h>*M<around|(|f<rsub|j>|)>+<around|(|a<rsub|h>*a<rsub|j>-\<kappa\><rsub|j*h>|)>*I.
  </equation*>

  The two-slot monomial uses the actual distinct row slots <math|j,h> even
  when their labels coincide or one is the unit. Its genericity follows from
  that of the row; no removal or renaming of slots is required.
  Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:episode-root-two> therefore applies. We obtain
  <eqref|eq:episode-all-powers> for both <math|D<rsub|j>> and
  <math|D<rsub|j*h>> with the same <math|g>.

  Use the full-support measure of the ground algebra from
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-phases>. Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-character-moments>
  now gives, for every character <math|\<chi\>>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<chi\><around|(|D<rsub|j>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>g,<space|2em><around|\||\<chi\><around|(|D<rsub|j*h>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>g.
  </equation*>

  Hence its phase correlations obey

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||S<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|f<rsub|j>|)>-a<rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>g,<space|2em><around|\||S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|f<rsub|j>*f<rsub|h>|)>-<around|(|\<kappa\><rsub|j*h>+a<rsub|j>*a<rsub|h>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>g*<around|(|1+<around|\||a<rsub|j>|\|>+<around|\||a<rsub|h>|\|>|)>.
  </equation*>

  These estimates hold at every comparison trial and for every character,
  including characters in null sets for the representing measure. Along the
  purchased trials <math|g\<to\>0> and the one-point comparison values are
  bounded, by continuity of the free correlations at the limiting test
  configurations. The finite separating tests and the eventual spanning
  property of the same limiting lifts are those retained in the rank
  construction. The finite-dimensional argument of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-no-new-null> consequently applies in each
  grade: for every represented phase whose correlations respect the original
  null relations, its null space equals that of the original hierarchy. This
  proves the detector-moment input to phase retention. The slab operators are
  those of the original hierarchy; the reflected-word identification is
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:c14-selected-phase-reflection>, and the common
  trials are the ones retained above. Thus the moment bounds and phase
  null-space conclusion use the same extraction and labels.

  <subsection|Transporting the equation ideal through all spectator
  tests><label|sec:actual-equation-kernel-relay>

  The finite measurements used for the rank bound must be supplemented by an
  assertion about every spectator word. We prove that assertion on the
  selected history. The mechanism has two parts: the marked section obeys an
  exact microscopic equation identity, and its comparison with the physical
  section has a vanishing centered discrepancy. The same calibration map
  occurs in both parts.

  We retain the funded calibrated family for a simple gauge group and its
  centered observable bounds. The following application supplies the
  schedule, service and reserve inputs used in both local relays.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Selected calibration payments><label|lem:c14-selected-calibration-payments>On
    the common funded history, at each fixed grade the budget, service,
    terminal-attachment and slow-reserve hypotheses of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-marked-solaris> hold on a cutoff
    tail, uniformly in the period. They persist on every refinement of the
    extraction.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    First perform the stage purchases before realizing the calendar, as in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-observable-purchases>. At stage
    <math|j> include the credits of the fixed portfolio for every
    <math|d\<leqslant\>j>, and take the finite maximum of their block
    thresholds from Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-marked-portfolio-calibration>.
    Denote it by <math|n<rsub|j>>. Replace the block floor by its maximum
    with <math|n<rsub|j>>, and take prefix maxima if necessary. The common
    scale construction chooses its block lengths above these floors.
    Consequently the actual block entering stage <math|J<around|(|N|)>> is at
    least <math|n<rsub|J<around|(|N|)>>>. For fixed <math|d>, eventually
    <math|d\<leqslant\>J<around|(|N|)>>, so the stage credit and sphere
    inequality apply to the actual top profile, with
    <math|b<rsub|N>=a<rsub|d,J<around|(|N|)>>>. No bound uniform in <math|d>
    is asserted.

    After the block lengths are fixed, choose the entrance delay to include
    the finite adjacent-profile inequalities proved in that same section.
    Each has a finite threshold: for the reciprocal-power inequality, put
    <math|y=x+c<rsub|G>*log L> and use <math|0\<less\>y\<leqslant\>2*x> and
    <math|y\<geqslant\>L<rsup|d+1>*2<rsup|w<rsub|d>>>; for the other clauses
    it suffices that <math|x> exceed the fixed credits and profile cost. Take
    the maximum for the stage and its predecessor, then prefix maxima
    together with the old entrance delay. These enlargements are retained by
    the common payment <eqref|eq:common-allstage-payment>. At a physical
    level <math|N>, <math|J<around|(|N|)>\<leqslant\>J*<around|(|N+1|)>\<leqslant\>J<around|(|N|)>+1>
    and the coupling <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>> pays the entry at
    <math|J*<around|(|N+1|)>>. Thus the purchased clauses apply to exactly
    the profile <math|J<around|(|N|)>> and its actual adjacent block, giving
    <math|0\<leqslant\>b<rsub|N>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>>, the
    height comparison <eqref|eq:ex-marked-height-jump>, and the profile-cost
    bound used in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:actual-derivative-shift-payment>.
    These are preselection requirements of one calendar, retained when other
    floors or delays are enlarged; they do not change an extracted family.

    The selected attachment reserve and height of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-centered-service> are, with
    <math|\<beta\><rsub|N>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>\<gtr\>1>,

    <\equation*>
      u<rsub|N>=<frac|1|4*<sqrt|\<beta\><rsub|N>>>,<space|2em>\<zeta\><rsub|d,N>=\<beta\><rsub|N><rsup|-w<rsub|d>>,<space|2em>w<rsub|d>=deg
      <around|(|<text|grade-<math|d> frame>|)>+4.
    </equation*>

    In particular <math|u<rsub|N>\<gtr\>0>,
    <math|u<rsub|N><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>4*\<beta\><rsub|N>>, and
    <math|0\<less\>\<zeta\><rsub|d,N>\<leqslant\>1>. Since
    <math|\<beta\><rsub|N>=\<beta\><rsub|\<ast\>>+s<rsub|G>*log
    <around|(|a<rsub|N><rsup|-1>|)>>, for every <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0> we
    have <math|4*\<beta\><rsub|N>\<leqslant\>exp <around|(|\<eta\>*log
    <around|(|a<rsub|N><rsup|-1>|)>|)>> eventually. Both reserve sequences
    are therefore subpower on every extraction order, uniformly in period.
    This uses their actual formulas, not the terminal bank inequality, which
    would not by itself bound <math|u<rsub|N><rsup|-1>>.

    The common history and its terminal keeper are those of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-selected-run-service>. Its same-door
    responses, room, bank and terminal music are retained in the backward
    construction of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:actual-marked-antilles>. The
    terminal attachment is Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:terminal-marked-voice>;
    the whole marked representation, with its reserved forced addresses and
    finite-census payment, is constructed in
    paragraph<nbsp><reference|par:figment-served-residual> of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:actual-marked-figment>, using
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-c02-selected-record> and
    Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:ex-c02-uniform-census>. All concern the
    original Wilson law and its original partition function. Thus they supply
    the paid record and forced attachment of the calibration proposition on
    the same family. Together with the preceding budget and reserve bounds,
    this verifies its hypotheses. Each assertion is either an exact identity
    or an eventual bound on this family's cutoff filter, so all persist under
    refinement.
  </proof>

  Fix a grade <math|d> and its coefficient frame <math|V<rsub|d>>. Let
  <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>> be the Maxwell equation ideal in the finite label
  space, and let <math|R<rsub|d>> be the fixed campaign regrading of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-campaign-producer>. This is an
  automorphism at the fixed grade cap: on a homogeneous stamp, its difference
  from the identity has strictly larger stamp. It need not preserve each
  homogeneous component. Its inverse preserves the corresponding descending
  stamp filtration, and it intertwines conjugation and signed motions. Denote
  its chosen local section by <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|d>>, and the physical
  local section by <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|d>>. For a coefficient
  <math|v\<in\>V<rsub|d>>, let <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v;o|)>>
  denote its normalized local insertion at <math|o>; explicitly,

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-V\><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v;o|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-V\><rsub|d>.<math-up|places><around|(|S<rsub|\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|-1>>*v;o|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|S<rsub|t>> is the factor-degree diagonal scaling and
  <math|\<theta\><rsub|N>> is the original top threshold. Define
  <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|N,p>> in the same way. Both maps are complex-linear in
  <math|v>. Their finite stencils, continuity, translation covariance and
  signed symmetry are those of the fixed sections. Let
  <math|X<rsub|N,p>:V<rsub|d>\<to\>V<rsub|d>> be the terminal calibration, so
  that the physical insertion of a reference label <math|x> is

  <\equation*>
    O<rsub|N,p><around|(|x;o|)>=\<cal-L\><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|X<rsub|N,p><rsup|-1>*x;o|)>.
  </equation*>

  All cutoffs below belong to the same selected family.

  <subsubsection|The common calibration and its local error>

  Apply Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-marked-solaris>, using the
  terminal invitation and calibration of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-marked-portfolio-calibration>,
  to every frame basis vector <math|e<rsub|j>>, with the single fixed section
  <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|d>>. The stage and delay purchases of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-marked-portfolio-calibration> supply the
  derivative credit, its running-coupling upper bound, and the sphere
  inequality, by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:c14-selected-calibration-payments>.
  The selected-history attachment supplies the same terminal activity keeper,
  transfer doors and finite-census estimates. Thus, eventually in <math|N>
  and for every <math|p>, there are columns <math|u<rsub|j,N,p>> with

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||e<rsub|j>-u<rsub|j,N,p>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|d>,<space|2em>\<delta\><rsub|d>=<around|(|2*\<cal-O\><rsub|d><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<cal-O\><rsub|d>> is the fixed orbit cost. The same columns
  satisfy, for every earlier opened pair of levels <math|<around|(|m,k|)>>
  with <math|<math-up|ref><around|(|d|)>\<leqslant\>k\<less\>N> and
  <math|m\<leqslant\>N>, and for every signed motion <math|g>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:equation-column-root>\<cal-R\><rsub|m,N,p>*<space|-0.17em><around*|(|g\<cdot\><around|{|\<cal-V\><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|e<rsub|j>;0|)>-\<cal-L\><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|u<rsub|j,N,p>;0|)>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|d,m,k>*E<rsub|N>*e<rsup|-s<rsub|N>/8>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|s<rsub|N>> is the logarithmic scale,
  <math|s<rsub|N>\<to\>\<infty\>>, <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|m,N,p>> is the
  centered root test, and <math|c<rsub|d,m,k>> is the fixed opened-scale
  price. The amount <math|E<rsub|N>> is the explicit common price in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-marked-portfolio-calibration>. For every
  <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0> it obeys <math|E<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<eta\>*s<rsub|N>>>
  eventually along any ordered extraction. This uses the same subpower bounds
  on the credit, column count and support exponent stated there, and the
  proved slow growth of the two reserve factors <math|u<rsub|N><rsup|-1>> and
  <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,N>>. The remaining threshold price is the finite sum

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|S><rsub|d,N>=<big|sum><rsub|j>\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|-s<rsub|j>>,<space|2em>Z<rsub|d,N>=<around|(|A<rsub|\<cal-V\><rsub|d>>+2*A<rsub|\<cal-L\><rsub|d>>|)><math-ss|S><rsub|d,N>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|s<rsub|j>> is the factor degree of the <math|j>th frame vector
  and the constants <math|A<rsub|\<cal-V\><rsub|d>>,A<rsub|\<cal-L\><rsub|d>>>
  bound the two fixed sections on their compact configuration cylinders. Each
  fixed inverse threshold power is subpower; their finite sum is therefore
  subpower. This is the factor in the forced-reserve term of
  <math|E<rsub|N>>. The finite stencil cardinality enters its sphere bound
  separately.

  Average these columns over signed motions and conjugation, with the twisted
  coefficient convention of the frame. Write the resulting map as

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:equation-common-calibration>P<rsub|N,p>*v=<big|sum><rsub|<around|(|g,j|)>>\<chi\><rsub|g,j><around|(|v|)>*<space|0.17em>g*u<rsub|j,N,p>,<space|2em>v=<big|sum><rsub|<around|(|g,j|)>>\<chi\><rsub|g,j><around|(|v|)>*<space|0.17em>g*e<rsub|j>.
  </equation>

  The coefficients <math|\<chi\><rsub|g,j>> are complex-linear in <math|v>:
  for a conjugating motion the coefficient of the inverse-transformed vector
  is conjugated again. The map <math|P<rsub|N,p>> is therefore
  complex-linear. Reindexing the finite average shows that it commutes with
  signed motions and conjugation. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-marked-calibration-inverse>
  gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:equation-inverse-bound><around|\||P<rsub|N,p><rsup|-1>*v|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2<around|\||v|\|><rsub|1>.
  </equation>

  Use arbitrary invertible maps at the finitely many exceptional mesh
  cutoffs, since all assertions using the columns are eventual.

  For later use define a coefficient family <math|v<rsub|N,p>> to be subpower
  if, for every <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>, its norm is eventually at most
  <math|e<rsup|\<eta\>*s<rsub|N>>> on the ordered filter under consideration.
  Every fixed linear coordinate of such a family is subpower. Finite sums and
  products of subpower nonnegative bounds are again subpower: split a target
  exponent between the finitely many factors, and absorb fixed constants by a
  further positive exponent margin. Equivalent norms on a fixed
  finite-dimensional space give the same definition.

  <subsubsection|A common tiling for the finite column sum>

  We explain why <eqref|eq:equation-column-root> can be summed with
  cutoff-dependent subpower coefficients. The bounds at opened pairs do not
  have to hold at one common finest cell for all requests at once. For any
  desired lower depth <math|w>, choose <math|m\<geqslant\>max
  <around|(|w,<math-up|ref><around|(|d|)>|)>> and then its purchased opening
  level <math|k\<geqslant\>m>. They are fixed before the cutoff tends to
  infinity. The price <math|c<rsub|d,m,k>> is consequently constant, and the
  subpower bound on <math|E<rsub|N>> absorbs it. Since
  <math|1/8-1/16=1/16\<gtr\>0>, <eqref|eq:equation-column-root> is eventually
  at most <math|e<rsup|-s<rsub|N>/16>>. Fixed-stencil locality also gives
  chamber interiority eventually. This yields centered comparisons at
  arbitrarily fine fixed depths with that same decay rate.

  To combine different column errors, transfer each such centered estimate to
  every required larger cell. Fix a cell depth <math|m> and a positive
  integer margin denominator <math|J>. Choose a finer centered depth
  <math|k\<geqslant\>m> such that <math|3*J*a<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|m>>,
  and at which the centered estimate holds. If its lattice span is
  <math|h=a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|N>>, then a depth-<math|m> cell has span
  <math|H=a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|N>> and <math|3*h\<leqslant\>H/J>. The scales are
  aligned, so <math|h> divides <math|H>.

  At a point of the finer center grid, the full orbit of copies in the larger
  tiling embeds injectively into cells of the finer centered tiling. Its
  copied orientations are coordinate reflections, with the prescribed complex
  conjugation. Translation invariance and reflection covariance identify each
  of their centered root tests with the test at the origin. Applying the
  planted inequality to the entire copy orbit and taking its root bounds the
  larger-cell root by the same number. A bound on just the unreplicated
  insertion would not suffice for this step.

  For an arbitrary point in the larger cell with margin <math|3*h>, bracket
  each coordinate by consecutive fine-grid centers. The sixteen resulting
  corners and the intervening coordinate segments lie in the margin
  <math|2*h>. The finer centered support therefore stays inside the larger
  cell along all these segments. The reflection argument in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-estimates> gives the integer maximum
  principle <math|r<rsub|j><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|j-1>*r<rsub|j+1>> for
  the nonnegative root tests along one segment. Its proof includes zero root
  tests. Applying it successively in four coordinates bounds every
  intermediate root by the largest corner root. Consequently every requested
  tiling of that larger side, and every literal translated insertion at
  margin <math|H/J>, has root at most <math|e<rsup|-s<rsub|N>/16>>. This
  argument applies to either allowed seam type.

  Now let <math|Q<rsub|\<alpha\>>> be the finitely many oriented column
  differences in <eqref|eq:equation-common-calibration>, and let
  <math|r<rsub|\<alpha\>>*<around|(|N,p|)>> be subpower scalar coefficients,
  constant in the spatial translation variable. On any one of these larger
  tilings the root triangle inequality gives

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-R\><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>r<rsub|\<alpha\>>*Q<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>><around|\||r<rsub|\<alpha\>>|\|>*\<cal-R\><around|(|Q<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>><around|\||r<rsub|\<alpha\>>|\|>|)>*e<rsup|-s<rsub|N>/16>.
  </equation*>

  For completeness, this triangle inequality follows by expanding the product
  of copies in the root test of the sum. The planted inequality bounds each
  term by the product of the individual root tests; summing the expansion
  gives the corresponding power of their sum. Taking the positive root proves
  the inequality, with the conjugated coefficient copies contributing the
  same moduli. The finite sum of the coefficient moduli is subpower, so the
  last display is at most <math|e<rsup|-s<rsub|N>/32>> eventually. The
  comparisons have first been transferred to the same tiling; intersecting
  unrelated sets of admissible cell depths would not justify this addition.

  Take <math|r<rsub|g,j>=\<chi\><rsub|g,j><around|(|v<rsub|N,p>|)>> for a
  subpower family <math|v>. Linearity of the two sections and
  <eqref|eq:equation-common-calibration> identify this sum, up to the
  harmless common sign, with

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:equation-physical-difference>O<rsub|N,p><around|(|X<rsub|N,p>*P<rsub|N,p>*v<rsub|N,p>;o|)>-\<cal-V\><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v<rsub|N,p>;o|)>.
  </equation>

  It therefore has a centered discrepancy tending to zero, uniformly on every
  fixed requested cell side and relative interior margin in the ordered
  sense. The common stencil is the finite union of the two section stencils
  and their finitely many orientations.

  <subsubsection|Exact vanishing against arbitrary separated spectators>

  The microscopic identity of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-campaign-producer>
  says that for every <math|z\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|d>>, the local function
  read from <math|R<rsub|d>*z> has zero Wilson moment against every bounded
  measurable spectator depending only on links outside a sufficiently large
  ball. Its radius and minimum period can be chosen independently of <math|z>
  at fixed <math|d>. Indeed choose a basis of the finite-dimensional space
  <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>>, take the maxima of its finitely many radii and
  minimum periods, and expand <math|z> in that basis. Continuity and the
  moment identity pass to finite linear combinations. This also covers the
  zero ideal, using the zero function.

  Fix any finite spectator list, a distinguished slot, and a physical
  separation <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>. The grades of the spectators are
  fixed, while their coefficient values can be arbitrary. For each of those
  grades the local physical section has a fixed stencil. Let <math|r> bound
  their lattice radii and let <math|s> be the uniform microscopic radius just
  obtained. Both are finite. For sufficiently large <math|N>, uniformly in
  the period,

  <\equation*>
    a<rsub|N>*<around|(|s+r|)>\<less\>\<delta\>,
  </equation*>

  and the lattice side exceeds all the finite nonwrapping floors. If the
  distinguished point is physically separated from each spectator point by at
  least <math|\<delta\>>, every link in every spectator stencil lies outside
  the radius-<math|s> ball about it. This follows from the triangle
  inequality: the distance of a spectator link from the distinguished point
  is at least the distance of its anchor minus <math|r>, which is greater
  than <math|s>.

  The product of the other insertions is continuous on the compact
  finite-volume configuration space, hence measurable and bounded. It is
  local on those outside links. The microscopic identity therefore applies to
  that entire product at once. Restoring the scalar normalization
  <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>> of the distinguished insertion preserves the
  value zero. This proves exact vanishing at late finite cutoffs for every
  finite spectator word. The cutoff threshold may depend on the grades and on
  <math|\<delta\>>, but not on the coefficients of the equation vector. The
  coupling is nonzero on the selected Wilson history, as required by the
  microscopic integration identity.

  <subsubsection|From a root discrepancy to a spectator
  estimate><label|subsec:equation-spectator-price>

  A vanishing root discrepancy still has to be converted to a bound on
  ordinary correlations with spectators. Let <math|D<rsub|N,p><around|(|o|)>>
  have the common-tiling root price <math|y<rsub|N,p>\<to\>0> obtained above.
  Fix a list of <math|n\<geqslant\>1> insertion grades and coefficients, one
  distinguished slot, and <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>, and consider
  configurations whose <math|n> insertion points are pairwise at Euclidean
  distance at least <math|\<delta\>>. Choose an available cell depth <math|k>
  with <math|2*a<rsub|k>\<less\>\<delta\>>, and put
  <math|J=8*<around|(|n+1|)>>. The centered observable bounds on the same
  family give one finite budget <math|A\<geqslant\>1> for all the spectator
  tokens and their finitely many reflected and conjugated versions, at some
  finer depth <math|j\<geqslant\>k> with <math|3*J*a<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|k>>.

  At late cutoffs let <math|L=a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|N>> and
  <math|h=a<rsub|j>/a<rsub|N>> be their integer spans. Thus
  <math|3*h\<leqslant\>L/J>. Shift the link tiling of side <math|L> so that
  every insertion lies at distance at least
  <math|<around|\<lfloor\>|L/J|\<rfloor\>>> from its chamber boundaries. Such
  a shift exists separately in each axis: each of the <math|n> coordinates
  excludes at most <math|2*<around|\<lfloor\>|L/J|\<rfloor\>>> residues
  modulo <math|L>, and <math|2*n*<around|\<lfloor\>|L/J|\<rfloor\>>\<less\>L>.
  Rebase the tiling so that the distinguished point lies in cell zero. All
  insertions occupy distinct cells: a cell of side <math|a<rsub|k>> in four
  dimensions has Euclidean diameter <math|2*a<rsub|k>\<less\>\<delta\>>,
  while any two insertions are at distance at least <math|\<delta\>>.

  For each spectator, reflect its position into the home cell and transform
  its token by the inverse cell orientation, including ordinary conjugation
  when prescribed. Its copy in the original cell is exactly the original
  spectator. The centered budget at span <math|h>, the divisibility of spans,
  and the margin <math|3*h> give interiority and root bound <math|A> for this
  home-cell function by the same planting and integer maximum principle used
  above. The discrepancy insertion already has root bound <math|y<rsub|N,p>>
  and interiority at its position in cell zero. Plant these <math|n>
  functions into their distinct cells to obtain

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:equation-spectator-bound><around*|\||\<bbb-E\><rsub|N,p>*<around*|[|D<rsub|N,p><around|(|o<rsub|i>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<ne\>i>O<rsub|N,p><around|(|q<rsub|j>;o<rsub|j>|)>|]>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsup|n-1>*y<rsub|N,p>.
  </equation>

  The normalized Wilson measure and its partition function are the same for
  every factor. This estimate holds uniformly over the separated tuples at
  late ordered cutoffs. The mesh threshold may depend on the period. The
  constant <math|A> depends on the fixed spectator list and the chosen
  depths, which is sufficient for every individual test.

  <subsubsection|The moving equation space and its limit>

  The embedding of the campaign labels into the reference coefficient space
  is literally

  <\equation*>
    E<rsub|N,p>=X<rsub|N,p>*S<rsub|\<theta\><rsub|N>><math-up|coords><space|0.17em>R<rsub|d>.
  </equation*>

  Define

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|N,p>=X<rsub|N,p>*P<rsub|N,p>*X<rsub|N,p><rsup|-1>*E<rsub|N,p>,<space|2em>K<rsub|N,p>=B<rsub|N,p>*\<cal-I\><rsub|d>.
  </equation*>

  All maps in this formula are the maps already selected above. The
  calibrated terminal history and the symmetrized map <math|P<rsub|N,p>>
  imply that <math|B<rsub|N,p>> intertwines conjugation and signed symmetries
  eventually. Since the equation ideal has those symmetries, so does
  <math|K<rsub|N,p>>. The uniformly bounded equivariant projections of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:finite-rank-kernel-transport> can therefore
  be chosen with precisely this kernel. At the exceptional initial cutoffs
  use the unsymmetrized bounded projections; only eventual symmetry is needed
  for their limits.

  Consider any row <math|x<rsub|N,p>\<in\>K<rsub|N,p>> whose inverse terminal
  coordinates <math|X<rsub|N,p><rsup|-1>*x<rsub|N,p>> are subpower. Set

  <\equation*>
    v<rsub|N,p>=P<rsub|N,p><rsup|-1>*X<rsub|N,p><rsup|-1>*x<rsub|N,p>.
  </equation*>

  The bound <eqref|eq:equation-inverse-bound> makes <math|v> subpower. Also
  <math|X<rsub|N,p>*v<rsub|N,p>> belongs to the original embedded campaign
  equation space, directly from the definition of <math|K<rsub|N,p>>.
  Equivalently there is <math|z\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|d>> with
  <math|v<rsub|N,p>=S<rsub|\<theta\><rsub|N>><math-up|coords><space|0.17em>R<rsub|d>*z>.
  In the normalized section, <math|S<rsub|\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|-1>>*S<rsub|\<theta\><rsub|N>>=I>
  cancels the threshold exactly. Its value is consequently
  <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>> times the microscopic section for
  <math|R<rsub|d>*z>. Thus

  <\equation*>
    Z<rsub|N,p><around|(|o|)>=\<cal-V\><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v<rsub|N,p>;o|)>
  </equation*>

  is an exactly vanishing comparison insertion of the preceding subsection.
  It is measurable and bounded at every finite cutoff, and has a fixed finite
  stencil. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:equation-physical-difference> becomes
  <math|O<rsub|N,p><around|(|x<rsub|N,p>;o|)>-Z<rsub|N,p><around|(|o|)>>,
  whose discrepancy tends to zero. This proves the local equation input for
  every such row in the moving kernel, not just for a chosen finite basis of
  that kernel.

  Finally take the common ordered extraction for the kernel projections and
  correlations. Bounded rows in the limiting projection kernel can be
  approximated by bounded rows of these finite kernels. Their inverse
  terminal coordinates are subpower by Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|ex-column-subpower>.
  For a compactly supported smooth spectator test on a separated domain, its
  support has a positive separation margin. The corresponding finite-cutoff
  sum with <math|Z<rsub|N,p>> is exactly zero eventually. The spectator
  estimate <eqref|eq:equation-spectator-bound> and the step-integral estimate
  of Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|ex-column-subpower> send the difference
  from the physical insertion to zero. Passing to the ordered limit proves
  that the limiting kernel annihilates every spectator test. This is the
  all-test kernel inclusion used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:finite-rank-kernel-transport>.
  The separate finite rank measurements then supply the opposite dimension
  bound.

  <subsection|Comparing insertions and their finite
  differences><label|sec:actual-insertion-derivative-relay>

  The local equation comparison must be accompanied by two further
  operations: representing a lower-grade insertion in a higher grade, and
  representing a first finite difference in the next grade. We prove these
  for a fixed simple gauge group on the same funded calibrated family.
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:c14-selected-calibration-payments> supplies the
  quantitative service, positive reserves, terminal activity keepers and
  opened-scale response bounds on this same history. In particular it
  verifies the incoming hypotheses of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:actual-marked-antilles>.
  For the difference relay we use the derivative-shift purchase
  <math|J<rsub|N>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>> derived below from
  the retained profile-cost clause of this same funded history. The argument
  uses the same terminal coefficient construction as the marked section, but
  allows a local function whose coefficients vary with the cutoffs.

  Let <math|0\<less\>a<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>1> be the lattice spacing and
  <math|s<rsub|N>=log <around|(|1/a<rsub|N>|)>>. An insertion
  <math|F<rsub|N,p><around|(|o|)>> has engineering weight <math|e> in the
  present calculation when its unnormalized local function is

  <\equation*>
    f<rsub|N,p,o>=a<rsub|N><rsup|e>*F<rsub|N,p><around|(|o|)>.
  </equation*>

  We require a fixed finite stencil containing zero, the regularity and
  weighted left-derivative bounds of the actual top budget, and two
  nonnegative subpower prices <math|c<rsub|N,p>,b<rsub|N,p>>. The first
  bounds those derivatives on the healthy region and includes the value bound
  there; the second bounds <math|<around|\||f<rsub|N,p,o>|\|>> on the whole
  configuration space. These bounds are uniform in <math|o>. Locality, gauge
  invariance and the regularity hypotheses are imposed with the same stencil
  and the same budget. No vanishing Taylor condition is imposed on the trial
  itself: zero readout is obtained only for the residual after subtracting
  its local-section readout below. We call these the protected local bounds.
  Subpower has the ordered-cutoff meaning of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-equation-kernel-relay>.

  <subsubsection|The physical section and a translated derivative stencil>

  Write <math|\<xi\><rsub|N,p>=X<rsub|N,p><rsup|-1>*x<rsub|N,p>> for the
  actual inverse terminal coordinates of a label row. If this row is subpower
  in those coordinates, its insertion has protected local bounds at weight
  <math|d>. Indeed its unnormalized form is the physical section evaluated at
  <math|S<rsub|\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|-1>>*\<xi\><rsub|N,p>>, where the
  factor-degree scaling and threshold are those used in the local section.
  The paid derivative bound is

  <\equation*>
    c<rsub|N,p>=C<rsub|N><around|\||\<xi\><rsub|N,p>|\|><rsub|1>,
  </equation*>

  with <math|C<rsub|N>\<geqslant\>1> the top column allowance. For the global
  bound, compactness of the fixed section's configuration cylinder gives a
  constant <math|A<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>1> such that its value at any
  coefficient <math|v> is at most <math|A<rsub|d><around|\||v|\|><rsub|1>>.
  To see that the constant is uniform in placement and period, expand in the
  finitely many coefficient basis vectors on the fixed cover cylinder, bound
  each continuous basis function on that compact cylinder, and take their
  maximum. Every finite-volume placement is its pullback. Thus one may use

  <\equation*>
    b<rsub|N,p>=A<rsub|d>*<around|\||S<rsub|\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|-1>>*\<xi\><rsub|N,p>|\|><rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  The column allowance and every fixed power of
  <math|\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|-1>> are subpower on the purchased schedule.
  Finite-dimensionality and the fixed factor degrees therefore make both
  prices subpower. For a constant reference label, the inverse terminal
  coordinates are subpower by Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|ex-column-subpower>.

  An insertion of weight <math|d> with these bounds has them at any weight
  <math|e\<geqslant\>d>: multiply its unnormalized function and prices by
  <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|e-d>\<leqslant\>1>. This changes neither its stencil
  nor its locality.

  For a fixed physical label <math|x> of grade <math|d>, define its
  difference in axis <math|\<alpha\>> by

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|\<alpha\>,N>*O<around|(|x;o|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|-1>*<around|{|O<rsub|N,p><around|(|x;o+e<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>-O<rsub|N,p><around|(|x;o|)>|}>.
  </equation*>

  At weight <math|d+1> the apparently large factor <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|-1>>
  cancels exactly:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:relay-difference-weight>a<rsub|N><rsup|d+1>*D<rsub|\<alpha\>,N>*O<around|(|x;o|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|d>*O<rsub|N,p><around|(|x;o+e<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>-a<rsub|N><rsup|d>*O<rsub|N,p><around|(|x;o|)>.
  </equation>

  The common stencil is <math|<around|(|X+e<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>\<cup\>X>. Its
  derivative price must also pay for moving the metric origin by one lattice
  unit.

  Suppose the top budget uses derivative order
  <math|q<rsub|N>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and spatial power
  <math|w<rsub|N>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, so an allowed direction <math|A> at origin
  <math|o> satisfies <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A<around|(|z|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsub|N>*<around|(|1+<math-up|dist><around|(|z,o|)>|)><rsup|w<rsub|N>>>.
  The triangle inequality gives

  <\equation*>
    1+<math-up|dist><around|(|z,o|)>\<leqslant\>2*<around|{|1+<math-up|dist><around|(|z,o+e<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Dividing all directions by <math|2<rsup|w<rsub|N>>> makes them admissible
  at the shifted origin. An ordered left derivative of length
  <math|q\<leqslant\>q<rsub|N>> is multilinear in its directions, so
  restoring their size costs at most

  <\equation*>
    J<rsub|N>=2<rsup|w<rsub|N>*q<rsub|N>>.
  </equation*>

  The protected prices for <eqref|eq:relay-difference-weight> are therefore
  <math|<around|(|J<rsub|N>+1|)>*c<rsub|N,p>> and <math|2*b<rsub|N,p>>. The
  required shift purchase is already contained in the profile-cost clause of
  the common history, as follows.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Payment for the derivative shift><label|lem:actual-derivative-shift-payment>
    Fix the funded history of Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:common-observable-purchases>
    and<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection>, retaining the
    marked-portfolio entrance delay of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-marked-portfolio-calibration>.
    At every level <math|N\<geqslant\>0>, let <math|q<rsub|N>,w<rsub|N>> be
    the order and power of the actual top budget, namely those of the profile
    at stage <math|J<around|(|N|)>>. Then

    <\equation*>
      1\<leqslant\>J<rsub|N>=2<rsup|w<rsub|N>*q<rsub|N>>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>.
    </equation*>

    This bound holds for every period on that same history. For every
    extraction order and every refinement of it, <math|J<rsub|N>> is subpower
    in the ordered-cutoff sense. Consequently
    <math|<around|(|J<rsub|N>+1|)>*c<rsub|N,p>> and <math|2*b<rsub|N,p>> are
    subpower whenever <math|c<rsub|N,p>> and <math|b<rsub|N,p>> are.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Write <math|q<around|(|j|)>,w<around|(|j|)>> for the order and power of
    the fixed stage-<math|j> profile and set
    <math|C<rsub|j>=<around|(|2<rsup|w<around|(|j|)>>|)><rsup|q<around|(|j|)>>>.
    We take <math|C<rsub|j>> as the fixed profile cost at stage <math|j> in
    the entrance-delay construction of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-marked-portfolio-calibration>.
    That construction applies to it, because <math|C<rsub|j>> is a fixed
    finite number determined by the profile. The profile-cost clause of the
    retained entrance delay <math|E<rsub|i>> is then

    <\equation*>
      j\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>j+1,<space|1em>E<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>x<space|1em>\<Longrightarrow\><space|1em>C<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>x.
    </equation*>

    The finite-threshold construction in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-marked-portfolio-calibration>
    includes this clause: for a fixed adjacent pair <math|<around|(|i,j|)>>,
    <math|C<rsub|j>> is a fixed finite number, so it is exceeded on a tail of
    <math|x>. Only the stage <math|i> and its predecessor are tested at
    entrance <math|i>. Taking their finite maximum, and then prefix maxima
    together with the other entrance requirements, retains the clause under
    further enlargement. This is the entrance purchase kept in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-observable-purchases> before the
    common history is chosen; no new history is selected here.

    On that history the final entry threshold <math|d<rsub|i>> dominates
    <math|E<rsub|i>>, and Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection>
    gives, for every <math|N\<geqslant\>0>,

    <\equation*>
      J<around|(|N|)>\<leqslant\>J*<around|(|N+1|)>\<leqslant\>J<around|(|N|)>+1,<space|2em>E<rsub|J*<around|(|N+1|)>>\<leqslant\>d<rsub|J*<around|(|N+1|)>>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>.
    </equation*>

    Apply the clause with <math|i=J*<around|(|N+1|)>>,
    <math|j=J<around|(|N|)>> and <math|x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>>. The
    actual budget uses that same profile <math|J<around|(|N|)>>; hence
    <math|C<rsub|J<around|(|N|)>>=<around|(|2<rsup|w<rsub|N>>|)><rsup|q<rsub|N>>=2<rsup|w<rsub|N>*q<rsub|N>>=J<rsub|N>>.
    Nonnegative integer order and power also give
    <math|J<rsub|N>\<geqslant\>1>. This argument includes <math|N=0> and uses
    neither a grade-dependent reference depth nor a period-dependent choice.

    The retained coupling price is subpower along every extraction order: by
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection>,
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>=\<beta\><rsub|\<ast\>>+s<rsub|G>*s<rsub|N>>
    is affine in <math|s<rsub|N>>, and <math|s<rsub|N>\<to\>\<infty\>>. Hence
    for every <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0> we have
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<eta\>*s<rsub|N>>> for
    all sufficiently large <math|N>, independently of the period and of the
    order. Since <math|0\<leqslant\>J<rsub|N>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>>
    pointwise and independently of the period, the same eventual bounds apply
    to <math|J<rsub|N>> on any such order and remain true after any
    refinement. To check the resulting products explicitly, fix
    <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0> and a chosen extraction order. Subpower
    gives <math|J<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<varepsilon\>*s<rsub|N>/3>> and
    <math|c<rsub|N,p>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<varepsilon\>*s<rsub|N>/3>> on a
    common ordered-cutoff tail. As <math|J<rsub|N>\<geqslant\>1>, their new
    price is at most <math|2*e<rsup|2*\<varepsilon\>*s<rsub|N>/3>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<varepsilon\>*s<rsub|N>>>
    after enlarging that tail, since <math|s<rsub|N>\<to\>\<infty\>>.
    Likewise <math|b<rsub|N,p>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<varepsilon\>*s<rsub|N>/2>>
    implies <math|2*b<rsub|N,p>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<varepsilon\>*s<rsub|N>>>
    eventually. These are finite intersections of tails on the original
    family, not exchanges of the period and mesh limits.
  </proof>

  The order-and-power payment is essential: cancellation of
  <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|-1>> alone does not supply it. The lemma extracts an
  existing paid clause; it does not construct the quantitative service,
  activity keepers or opened-scale bounds supplied at the start of this
  section.

  <subsubsection|A terminal coefficient with a subpower total price>

  We next encode any protected local function
  <math|f=a<rsub|N><rsup|e>*F<rsub|N,p><around|(|0|)>> at grade <math|e>.
  Enlarge its stencil by the stencil of the physical section at grade
  <math|e>, and denote the resulting finite set by <math|D>. The readout
  bound at this grade gives a fixed constant <math|K<rsub|e>> such that the
  leading coefficient <math|z> read from <math|f> satisfies

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>q\<assign\>K<rsub|e>*c<rsub|N,p>.
  </equation*>

  Here and below the local coefficient includes the top threshold
  normalization. Let <math|g> be the physical local section for this same
  <math|z>, and split <math|g=z<rsub|0>+\<xi\>> into its scalar and
  nonconstant parts. Then <math|y=f-g> has zero readout. Its regular
  derivative price is at most

  <\equation*>
    h=c<rsub|N,p>+C<rsub|N>*q.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|<math-ss|N><rsub|N>> be the finite sum of the inverse threshold
  powers over the grade-<math|e> frame. The global section bound gives
  <math|<around|\||g|\|>,<around|\||\<xi\>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|e><math-ss|N><rsub|N>q>.
  Thus the part left outside the healthy masks has global bound

  <\equation*>
    r=b<rsub|N,p>+2*A<rsub|e><math-ss|N><rsub|N>q.
  </equation*>

  The exact decomposition is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:relay-protected-split>f=z<rsub|0>+\<b-1\><rsub|<math-up|healthy(X)>>*\<xi\>+\<b-1\><rsub|<math-up|healthy(D)>>*y+<around*|(|y-\<b-1\><rsub|<math-up|healthy(D)>>*y+\<xi\>-\<b-1\><rsub|<math-up|healthy(X)>>*\<xi\>|)>,
  </equation>

  where <math|X\<subseteq\>D> is the physical section footprint. The
  bracketed function vanishes when <math|D> is healthy, is measurable and
  gauge invariant, and depends on the same links and flags as the local
  construction. Its absolute value is at most <math|r>, since each off-mask
  part is either zero or its original value. The regular masked term has
  price <math|h> and zero readout. Apply the activity attachment construction
  used in the terminal invitation to this bracketed function, using the
  actual top keeper. The coefficient so obtained has leading value <math|z>
  and exact density

  <\equation*>
    Z.<math-up|density>=R<rsub|N>*f=a<rsub|N><rsup|e>*R<rsub|N>*F<rsub|N,p><around|(|0|)>.
  </equation*>

  No replacement of <math|R<rsub|N>> by its action without activities is
  made.

  We give the total price explicitly. Write <math|\<omega\><rsub|N>> for the
  forced allowance and <math|\<nu\><rsub|N>\<gtr\>0> for the support
  exponent, and put

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|N>=e<rsup|81*\<nu\><rsub|N>>,<space|2em>M=2<around|\||D|\|>.
  </equation*>

  Eventually the placed stencil has covering number at most <math|81> and
  <math|<around|\||D|\|>*\<omega\><rsub|N>\<leqslant\>1>; also
  <math|<sqrt|\<omega\><rsub|N>>\<leqslant\>1>. The regular remainder price
  from <eqref|eq:relay-protected-split> is bounded by

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|N>*h+2<around|\||D|\|><sqrt|\<omega\><rsub|N>>*A<rsub|N>*r\<leqslant\>A<rsub|N>*<around|(|h+M*r|)>=:t<rsub|N>.
  </equation*>

  The forced attachment has reserved mass at most

  <\equation*>
    H<rsub|N>=A<rsub|N>*e<rsup|162>*r,
  </equation*>

  because increasing the support exponent by two on covering number at most
  <math|81> costs at most <math|e<rsup|162>>. Let <math|u<rsub|N>\<gtr\>0> be
  the selected attachment reserve and <math|\<zeta\><rsub|e,N>\<geqslant\>0>
  the grade-<math|e> weight. Set

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:relay-protected-total>Q<rsub|N>=1+t<rsub|N>+q,<space|2em>E<rsub|N,p>=4*<around|(|1+C<rsub|N>|)>*Q<rsub|N>+H<rsub|N>*\<zeta\><rsub|e,N>*u<rsub|N><rsup|-1>.
  </equation>

  Then <math|Q<rsub|N>\<gtr\>0>, the leading coefficient plus regular
  remainder costs at most <math|Q<rsub|N>>, the first summand pays its
  required terminal cone allowance, and <math|H<rsub|N>*\<zeta\><rsub|e,N>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|N>*E<rsub|N,p>>
  pays the forced attachment. The exact profile identity comes from the same
  attachment; it is preserved in the coefficient density just displayed.

  Every factor in <eqref|eq:relay-protected-total> is subpower. For
  <math|q,h,r> this follows from the protected prices, the column bound and
  the finite threshold sum. The support-exponent purchase makes
  <math|A<rsub|N>> subpower. The selected reserve record gives subpower
  bounds for <math|\<zeta\><rsub|e,N>> and <math|u<rsub|N><rsup|-1>>. Finite
  sums and products therefore make <math|E<rsub|N,p>> subpower. Continuity of
  the original insertion completes the terminal input required by the
  descent; continuity across a mask boundary is not asserted for the
  individual masked pieces.

  <subsubsection|Descent and the comparison labels>

  Apply the same-history backward coefficient construction of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:actual-marked-antilles> to this terminal
  coefficient. The terminal attachment of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:terminal-marked-voice> and the paid record
  on this history give the served residual representation by
  paragraph<nbsp><reference|par:figment-served-residual> of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:actual-marked-figment>. Its bottom primary
  determines a reference label <math|v<rsub|N,p>> of grade <math|e>. Item (4)
  of that lemma, before taking the last-step purse, gives

  <\equation*>
    32*<around|(|1+C<rsub|N>|)><rsup|2>*<around|\||Z.*x-X<rsub|N,p><rsup|-1>*v<rsub|N,p>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1+C<rsub|N>|)>*Q<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>E<rsub|N,p>.
  </equation*>

  Also <math|<around|\||Z.*x|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>Q<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>E<rsub|N,p>>.
  Since <math|32*<around|(|1+C<rsub|N>|)><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>1>, the triangle
  inequality yields

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||X<rsub|N,p><rsup|-1>*v<rsub|N,p>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*E<rsub|N,p>.
  </equation*>

  The comparison label is thus subpower in its actual inverse terminal
  coordinates. Choose these labels at every cutoff where the eventual
  terminal construction applies, and arbitrary labels elsewhere.

  The same descent and finite-census argument give, for
  <math|<math-up|ref><around|(|e|)>\<leqslant\>k\<less\>N>,
  <math|m\<leqslant\>N>, and every opened pair <math|<around|(|m,k|)>>,

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-R\><rsub|m,N,p>*<around|{|F<rsub|N,p><around|(|0|)>-O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v<rsub|N,p>;0|)>|}>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|e,m,k>*E<rsub|N,p>*e<rsup|-s<rsub|N>/8>.
  </equation*>

  Replacing the difference by its negative preserves its root norm. The
  fixed-stencil and common-tiling argument of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-equation-kernel-relay> converts this
  into a vanishing discrepancy. It is uniform in the required translated
  placements in the ordered sense. The finite-cutoff density identity, the
  descent, and this discrepancy all use one original history.

  Apply this result first to the fixed insertion of grade <math|d>, at any
  weight <math|e\<geqslant\>d>, and then to its difference in axis
  <math|\<alpha\>>, at weight <math|d+1>. We obtain in each case comparison
  labels of the requested grade, subpower inverse terminal coordinates, and a
  discrepancy tending to zero against every fixed separated spectator test.
  These are the inclusion and derivative comparison inputs used to build the
  physical filtration and field hierarchy. Together with the equation
  comparison of the preceding section, they supply the three local comparison
  clauses on every subsequent refinement of the ordered extraction: eventual
  inequalities, subpower bounds and limits all persist on such refinements.

  <subsection|Finite projections and transport of the equation
  kernel><label|sec:finite-rank-kernel-transport>

  Free separation does not by itself identify the interacting null space. We
  prove the finite-dimensional endpoint, keeping the actual marked
  correlation estimates and local equation estimates as explicit inputs. All
  spaces below are finite-dimensional over <math|\<bbb-C\>>.

  <subsubsection|Uniform projections and a common extraction>

  Let <math|V> have a norm, an isometric conjugate-linear involution
  <math|J>, and a representation <math|\<rho\>> of a finite group
  <math|\<Gamma\>> commuting with <math|J>. Fix an isomorphism
  <math|Q:\<bbb-C\><rsup|m>\<to\>V> from Euclidean space. For any subspace
  <math|U\<subset\>V>, let <math|\<Pi\><rsub|U>> be the orthogonal projection
  onto <math|Q<rsup|-1>*U>. Then

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|U><rsup|0>=I-Q*\<Pi\><rsub|U>*Q<rsup|-1>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|U><rsup|0>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|0>\<assign\><around|\<\|\|\>|I|\<\|\|\>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|Q|\<\|\|\>><space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|Q<rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>
  </equation*>

  kills <math|U> and satisfies <math|x-A<rsub|U><rsup|0>*x\<in\>U>. Any
  linear map <math|A> having these two properties satisfies
  <math|A<rsup|2>=A> and <math|ker A=U>: apply <math|A> to <math|x-A*x> for
  the first assertion, and use <math|x=x-A*x> when <math|A*x=0> for the
  second.

  When <math|U> is invariant under <math|\<rho\><around|(|\<Gamma\>|)>> and
  under <math|J>, put

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|U>=<frac|1|<around|\||\<Gamma\>|\|>>*<big|sum><rsub|g\<in\>\<Gamma\>>\<rho\><around|(|g|)>*A<rsub|U><rsup|0>*\<rho\><around|(|g|)><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>A<rsub|U>=<tfrac|1|2><around|(|B<rsub|U>+J*B<rsub|U>*J|)>.
  </equation*>

  Each average still kills <math|U> and differs from the identity by a map
  into <math|U>. It is therefore an idempotent with kernel <math|U>.
  Reindexing by left multiplication proves that <math|B<rsub|U>> commutes
  with <math|\<rho\><around|(|g|)>>; commutation of <math|J> and
  <math|\<rho\><around|(|g|)>> proves the same for <math|A<rsub|U>>, and
  <math|J*A<rsub|U>*J=A<rsub|U>>. The composition <math|J*B<rsub|U>*J> is
  complex-linear. Moreover

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|U>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><frac|C<rsub|0>|<around|\||\<Gamma\>|\|>>*<big|sum><rsub|g\<in\>\<Gamma\>><around|\<\|\|\>|\<rho\><around|(|g|)><around|\<\|\|\>|<space|0.17em>|\<\|\|\>>*\<rho\><around|(|g|)><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>.
  </equation*>

  This bound is independent of <math|U>. If invariance is known only at late
  cutoffs, use <math|A<rsub|U><rsup|0>> at earlier ones and take the maximum
  of the two bounds. No isometry of the given signed representation is
  needed.

  For every grade <math|d>, apply this construction to its coefficient space
  <math|V<rsub|d>> and finite equation spaces <math|U<rsub|d,N,p>>. The
  bounds may depend on <math|d>, but not on mesh cutoff <math|N> or period
  index <math|p>. There is a common refinement with mesh limits for every
  fixed <math|<around|(|d,p|)>> and then period limits for every <math|d>.
  Indeed, enumerate the countably many pairs <math|<around|(|d,p|)>>, take
  successive subsequences in their compact matrix balls, and take a diagonal
  subsequence. Choose its <math|j>th term from the <math|j>th nested
  subsequence, with index beyond the <math|j>th index of each of the first
  <math|j> subsequences and beyond the preceding chosen index. Its tail then
  belongs to every fixed earlier subsequence, so every fixed pair retains its
  limit. Repeat in the period variable for the countably many grades. Bounded
  lifted vectors indexed by a comparison scale and finitely many columns can
  be included in the same enumeration. Previously existing limits survive
  these refinements.

  Write <math|A<rsub|d,p>> for the inner limits and <math|T<rsub|d>> for the
  outer limits. Continuity of multiplication gives
  <math|A<rsub|d,p><rsup|2>=A<rsub|d,p>> and
  <math|T<rsub|d><rsup|2>=T<rsub|d>>. Below the index
  <math|c=<around|(|N,p|)>> follows this ordered filter: first every
  sufficiently large selected period, then every sufficiently large selected
  mesh cutoff, whose threshold may depend on the period. The two-stage limits
  give <math|A<rsub|d,c>\<to\>T<rsub|d>> along this filter. There is no claim
  of uniform convergence in <math|p>.

  <subsubsection|The all-test null space and the local equation input>

  Fix a grade, write <math|V=V<rsub|d>>, <math|A<rsub|c>=A<rsub|d,c>> and
  <math|T=T<rsub|d>>, and let <math|\<Theta\>> consist of all smooth
  compactly supported spectator tests on separated configuration domains with
  one specified slot of this grade. Let <math|f<rsub|\<theta\>,c>\<in\>V<rsup|\<ast\>>>
  be its finite-cutoff correlation functional and assume
  <math|f<rsub|\<theta\>,c>\<to\>f<rsub|\<theta\>>> in operator norm.
  Convergence on a fixed basis suffices, since for its dual basis
  <math|e<rsub|j><rsup|\<ast\>>>

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|\<theta\>,c>-f<rsub|\<theta\>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|j><around|\||<around|(|f<rsub|\<theta\>,c>-f<rsub|\<theta\>>|)><around|(|e<rsub|j>|)>|\|><space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|e<rsub|j><rsup|\<ast\>>|\<\|\|\>>.
  </equation*>

  Define

  <\equation*>
    M=<big|cap><rsub|\<theta\>\<in\>\<Theta\>>ker f<rsub|\<theta\>>.
  </equation*>

  This includes every arity and spectator word, not only the finite pair
  measurements used below to bound dimension.

  The local equation input is as follows. For every family
  <math|v<rsub|c>\<in\>ker A<rsub|c>> with subpower inverse-calibrated norm,
  there is a comparison insertion <math|Z<rsub|c>> whose separated spectator
  moments vanish exactly, and whose difference from the physical insertion of
  <math|v<rsub|c>> has centered discrepancy price tending to zero on this
  same filter. Subpower means that for every <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0> the
  inverse-calibrated norm is eventually at most <math|exp
  <around|(|\<epsilon\>*s<rsub|N>|)>>, with <math|s<rsub|N>\<to\>\<infty\>>.
  The insertions are measurable and bounded at each cutoff, and their
  products with the fixed spectator word are integrable.

  Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|ex-column-subpower> proves that bounded
  coefficient families have subpower inverse-calibrated norm. The common-cell
  planting argument of Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:equation-spectator-price>
  first converts the root discrepancy into a uniform separated spectator
  estimate. The smeared-discrepancy estimate then sends the tested difference
  from <math|Z<rsub|c>> to zero. The compact test support is eventually
  separated on the lattice, so the tested sum for <math|Z<rsub|c>> is zero.
  Subtraction of the finite-cutoff integrals is legitimate by integrability.
  Consequently

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:finite-rank-silent>v<rsub|c>\<in\>ker
    A<rsub|c>,<space|1em><around|(|v<rsub|c>|)><text|
    bounded><space|1em>\<Longrightarrow\><space|1em>f<rsub|\<theta\>,c><around|(|v<rsub|c>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation>

  For <math|v\<in\>ker T>, the family <math|v<rsub|c>=<around|(|I-A<rsub|c>|)>*v>
  is bounded, is killed by <math|A<rsub|c>>, and tends to <math|v>. Hence
  <eqref|eq:finite-rank-silent> and norm convergence of the functionals imply

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:finite-rank-upper-kernel>ker T\<subseteq\>M.
  </equation>

  The local equation input is substantive; it does not follow from free
  positivity.

  It remains applicable after further refinement. Extend a vector family from
  the refined grid of mesh and period indices by zero outside that grid. The
  kernel condition is preserved. Subpower bounds on the original ordered
  filter hold conditionally on membership in the refined grid: monotonicity
  of the two index sequences translates the refined thresholds into original
  thresholds. Outside that grid the bound is automatic. Apply the original
  local estimate to this extension, then restrict back. Thus no control of
  the original family at unused cutoff pairs is required.

  <subsubsection|Purchased measurements and exact dimension>

  Let <math|E> be the free field space, <math|r=dim E>, and
  <math|b<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,b<rsub|r>> a basis. By
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-all-grade-free-detectors>, choose
  finitely many free measurements <math|\<ell\><rsub|a>\<in\>E<rsup|\<ast\>>>
  for which <math|L*x=<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|a><around|(|x|)>|)><rsub|a=1><rsup|h>>
  is injective. The additional interacting comparison consists of vectors
  <math|u<rsub|k,j>\<in\>V> and linear measurements
  <math|g<rsub|k,a>\<in\>V<rsup|\<ast\>>> satisfying

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:finite-rank-purchased-matrix>g<rsub|k,a>\|<rsub|M>=0,<space|2em>g<rsub|k,a><around|(|u<rsub|k,j>|)>\<longrightarrow\>\<ell\><rsub|a><around|(|b<rsub|j>|)>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|k> tends to infinity after taking the mesh and period limits for
  each fixed comparison scale. Neither <math|u<rsub|k,j>> nor
  <math|g<rsub|k,a>> is required to converge as <math|k\<to\>\<infty\>>.

  For the application, <math|g<rsub|k,a>> is a generic-point correlation
  linear in its first insertion, with its other insertions fixed at that
  <math|k>. It vanishes on <math|M> because the corresponding correlation is
  continuous on the open generic configuration domain and has zero integral
  against every compactly supported smooth test there. A nonzero value could
  be rotated by a constant complex phase to have positive real part on a
  small neighborhood; a nonnegative smooth bump supported there would then
  have nonzero integral. This contradiction proves pointwise vanishing. It
  uses no point evaluation at a coordinate tie.

  The convergence in <eqref|eq:finite-rank-purchased-matrix> is the paid
  comparison input. Its precise usable form, after the cutoff limits, is

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||g<rsub|k,a><around|(|u<rsub|k,j>|)>-\<ell\><rsub|a,k><around|(|b<rsub|j>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|k,a,j>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|k>,<space|2em>P<rsub|k,a,j>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|k>\<to\>0,<space|2em>\<ell\><rsub|a,k><around|(|b<rsub|j>|)>\<to\>\<ell\><rsub|a><around|(|b<rsub|j>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The factors <math|P<rsub|k,a,j>> are coefficient norms of the common free
  moment profile at the rescaled separated points. Continuity of that finite
  profile bounds them. The lifted vectors for fixed <math|k> must be bounded
  uniformly in both cutoff indices, so the preceding common extraction
  retains them. These requirements are not consequences of the finite matrix
  argument.

  The matrix <math|<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|a><around|(|b<rsub|j>|)>|)>> has
  rank <math|r>. Choose an <math|r>-row minor with nonzero determinant. Its
  counterpart in <math|<around|(|g<rsub|k,a><around|(|u<rsub|k,j>|)>|)>> is
  nonzero for all sufficiently large <math|k>, by entrywise convergence and
  continuity of the determinant. A linear relation between the classes
  <math|<around|[|u<rsub|k,j>|]>> in <math|V/M>, evaluated by these rows, has
  every coefficient zero. Thus

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:finite-rank-lower-dimension>r\<leqslant\>dim <around|(|V/M|)>.
  </equation>

  For <math|r=0> this inequality is automatic and no minor is needed.

  Assume also the finite equation count

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:finite-rank-equation-count>dim V\<leqslant\>dim ker
    A<rsub|c>+r<space|1em><text|frequently along the same filter>.
  </equation>

  In the application, a label space <math|W>, equation ideal
  <math|\<cal-E\>>, and actual calibrated isomorphisms
  <math|L<rsub|c>:W\<to\>V> satisfy <math|dim <around|(|W/\<cal-E\>|)>=r> and
  <math|ker A<rsub|c>=L<rsub|c>*\<cal-E\>>. Rank-nullity then gives equality
  in <eqref|eq:finite-rank-equation-count>. The free detector identifies
  <math|W/\<cal-E\>> with the free space; it does not establish this separate
  identity for the actual finite kernel.

  If <math|A,T> are idempotents with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A-T|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>1>,
  the map <math|ker A\<to\>ker T>, <math|v\<mapsto\><around|(|I-T|)>*v>, is
  injective. Indeed a vector in its kernel has <math|T*v=v> and <math|A*v=0>,
  so <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>=<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|A-T|)>*v|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|A-T|\<\|\|\>><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>>,
  forcing <math|v=0>. Interchange <math|A,T> to obtain equal kernel
  dimensions. Applying this eventually to <math|A<rsub|c>\<to\>T>, and
  intersecting with the frequent set in <eqref|eq:finite-rank-equation-count>,
  gives <math|dim ker T\<geqslant\>dim V-r>. Combining this with
  <eqref|eq:finite-rank-upper-kernel> and
  <eqref|eq:finite-rank-lower-dimension> proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:finite-rank-exact-kernel>M=ker T,<space|2em>dim
    <around|(|V/M|)>=r.
  </equation>

  The purchased classes are therefore eventually a basis of <math|V/M>. This
  supplies the kernel hypothesis in the normalized compact test argument of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-filtration>, provided its projections
  and tests are these same ones. That argument then applies to its unbounded
  approximating vectors without asserting convergence of moving physical
  subspaces.

  <subsubsection|Exact transport and quotient symmetries>

  Suppose <math|ker A<rsub|c>=L<rsub|c>*\<cal-E\>> as above, with
  <math|L<rsub|c>> intertwining the real involutions and signed actions at
  all sufficiently late cutoffs. The subspace <math|\<cal-E\>> is invariant
  under the signed actions and the real involution, so <math|U=ker
  A<rsub|c>=L<rsub|c>*\<cal-E\>> is invariant under
  <math|\<rho\><around|(|\<Gamma\>|)>> and <math|J> at those cutoffs; this is
  the hypothesis of the averaged construction above. Hence the selected
  <math|A<rsub|c>> commute with the actions on <math|V>. Their limit <math|T>
  commutes too, by passage to the limit on each fixed vector. Define

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:finite-rank-conjugator>Y<rsub|c>=T*A<rsub|c>+<around|(|I-T|)>*<around|(|I-A<rsub|c>|)>.
  </equation>

  Direct multiplication, using both idempotence identities, gives

  <\equation*>
    Y<rsub|c>-I=<around|(|2*T-I|)>*<around|(|A<rsub|c>-T|)>,<space|2em>Y<rsub|c>*A<rsub|c>=T*A<rsub|c>=T*Y<rsub|c>.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|Y<rsub|c>\<to\>I>. Choose one late cutoff satisfying all the
  symmetry conditions and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|Y<rsub|c>-I|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>1>.
  Its inverse is the norm-convergent series
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>0><around|(|I-Y<rsub|c>|)><rsup|n>>:
  multiplication of the partial sums leaves the remainder
  <math|<around|(|I-Y<rsub|c>|)><rsup|n+1>>, which tends to zero. In
  particular <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|Y<rsub|c><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|1-<around|\<\|\|\>|Y<rsub|c>-I|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|-1>>.
  The intertwining identity and invertibility give
  <math|Y<rsub|c><around|(|ker A<rsub|c>|)>=ker T>.

  Since <math|Y<rsub|c>> is a real polynomial expression in
  <math|T,A<rsub|c>>, it commutes with both symmetries. Therefore
  <math|\<cal-L\>=Y<rsub|c>*L<rsub|c>> is an isomorphism with
  <math|\<cal-L\>*\<cal-E\>=M>, and descends to

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:finite-rank-quotient-isomorphism>W/\<cal-E\>\<simeq\>V/M
  </equation>

  intertwining the induced conjugations and signed actions. Only one
  sufficiently late <math|L<rsub|c>> is used; convergence of <math|L<rsub|c>>
  is unnecessary.

  If a smooth representative of the limiting correlation table has the same
  tested correlations, its physical null space equals <math|M>. Physical
  nullity implies tested nullity by integration, while the converse follows
  by the smooth bump argument for every separated spectator word. Hence
  <eqref|eq:finite-rank-quotient-isomorphism> identifies that physical
  quotient with the free quotient. Construction of the smooth representative
  and its derivative hierarchy, the actual calibrated maps <math|L<rsub|c>>,
  the local equation estimate, and the paid comparisons remain separate
  inputs. This section proves the finite projection, dimension and symmetry
  transport on their common family and extraction.

  <section|The stress tensor and Ward identities>

  <subsection|The trace calibration on the selected
  construction><label|sec:ex-lattice-trace-producer>

  The trace asymptotic uses a one-dimensional conserved grade-four line and a
  calibrated limiting sequence of such tensors. Here we prove the finite-
  dimensional rank argument conditional on the interacting-center and
  scale-dependent comparison receipts stated below. The free reference rank
  is proved in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-free-rank>.
  Detailed boundary-measure and all-radii arguments remain in
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:trace-spectrum> and
  <reference|sec:trace-asymptotic>.

  <subsubsection|The conserved tensor space has dimension one>

  Let <math|K<rsub|0>> be the real finite-dimensional space of reference
  grade-four stress tensors and <math|V<rsub|0>> its divergence space. Let

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|0>:K<rsub|0>\<longrightarrow\>V<rsub|0>
  </equation*>

  be the reference divergence. Let <math|K> be the interacting spin-kernel
  space, <math|D:K\<to\>V> its fixed divergence, and, at scale <math|i>,

  <\equation*>
    O<rsub|i>:K\<longrightarrow\>V<rsub|0>
  </equation*>

  the comparison map into the reference two-component table. Only
  <math|O<rsub|i>> depends on scale. The interacting-center dimension
  equivalence and the scale-dependent comparison are supplied by the inputs
  stated below; the elementary rank conclusion is proved conditional on them.
  The free reference statement is not assumed: it is proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-free-rank>. Concretely,
  <math|K<rsub|0>> is the subspace of reference symmetric grade-four tensors
  with zero scalar skim, and <math|V<rsub|0>> is the reference grade-five
  vector table. The interacting <math|K> is the centered grade-four kernel
  space, and <math|D> is the divergence rank map restricted to it. The
  optical map takes values in the corresponding reference vector table.

  <\enumerate>
    <item><math|D<rsub|0>> is surjective and <math|dim ker D<rsub|0>=1>;

    <item><math|dim K=dim K<rsub|0>>;

    <item>eventually <math|ker O<rsub|i>=ker D>;

    <item>eventually there is a linear map
    <math|S<rsub|i>:\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>\<to\>K> such that
    <math|O<rsub|i>S<rsub|i>:\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>\<to\>V<rsub|0>> is bijective.
  </enumerate>

  Item 2 is proved below from the coherent grade-four equivalence and
  centering identities. On the selected table, these are supplied by the
  eventual coherent, unit-preserving isomorphisms of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-selected-trace-gallery> and their
  completion in <hlink|the completion paragraph|#par:ex-trace-completed-uniform-tests>.
  Centering gives the explicit inverse below, without requiring the maps to
  preserve expectations. This coherent-center input is therefore available
  for that selected family; it is not inferred from the free reference rank.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Rank-one conservation law><label|lem:ex-lattice-trace-rank-one>Under
    these four properties, <math|dim ker D=1>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Choose <math|i> large enough that properties 3\U4 hold. Since
    <math|O<rsub|i>S<rsub|i>> is surjective, <math|O<rsub|i>> is surjective.
    Rank\Unullity and the kernel identity give

    <\equation*>
      dim ker D=dim ker O<rsub|i>=dim K-dim V<rsub|0>.
    </equation*>

    The reference surjection <math|D<rsub|0>> gives

    <\equation*>
      dim K<rsub|0>=dim V<rsub|0>+dim ker D<rsub|0>=dim V<rsub|0>+1.
    </equation*>

    Property 2 identifies <math|dim K=dim K<rsub|0>>, so subtraction yields
    <math|dim ker D=1>.
  </proof>

  The free reference computation is proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-free-rank>. In the notation
  there, the scalar skim is the real one-point average of the <math|00>
  tensor entry, the free symmetric grade-four table has dimension four, and
  the Maxwell-reduced grade-five vector table has dimension two. The
  zero-skim divergence map is onto and its kernel is the Weyl line. In
  particular, this is a proved finite-dimensional input to item 1, not a
  free-rank hypothesis. The map <math|T> from the four coefficient parameters
  is identified with that chapter's free stress coefficient map. Its
  point-probe identities and the low-grade signed-covariance classification
  are proved there.

  To prove item 2 under the coherent-center input, let <math|S> be the
  reference table and <math|T> the interacting table. Assume that a coherent
  field equivalence <math|U:S.*F*i*e*l*d<rsub|4>\<to\>T.*F*i*e*l*d<rsub|4>>
  preserves conjugation and signed action and maps the reference unit to the
  interacting unit. For a stress array <math|B>, define entrywise

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|<math-ss|U>B|)><rsub|a*b>=U<around|(|B<rsub|a*b>|)>,<space|2em><around|(|<math-ss|C><rsub|T>B|)><rsub|a*b>=B<rsub|a*b>-\<bbb-E\><rsub|T><around|(|B<rsub|a*b>|)>*<space|0.17em>1<rsub|T>.
  </equation*>

  Coherence makes <math|<math-ss|U>> preserve symmetry, reality, and signed
  covariance. Centering then maps these arrays into the interacting centered
  kernel space. Thus

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|S><around|(|B|)>=<math-ss|C><rsub|T><around|(|<math-ss|U>B|)>
  </equation*>

  is a linear map from the reference centered kernel to the interacting one.
  Define the reverse map <math|<math-ss|R><around|(|B|)>=<math-ss|C><rsub|S><around|(|<math-ss|U><rsup|-1>B|)>>.
  For a centered reference array <math|B>, write
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|a*b>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|T><around|(|U<around|(|B<rsub|a*b>|)>|)>>.
  Since <math|U<around|(|1<rsub|S>|)>=1<rsub|T>>,

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|U><rsup|-1><around|(|<math-ss|C><rsub|T><around|(|<math-ss|U>B|)>|)><rsub|a*b>=B<rsub|a*b>-\<lambda\><rsub|a*b>*1<rsub|S>.
  </equation*>

  The reference entry is centered, so <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|S><around|(|B<rsub|a*b>|)>=0>;
  also <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|S><around|(|1<rsub|S>|)>=1>. Applying
  <math|<math-ss|C><rsub|S>> gives <math|B<rsub|a*b>-\<lambda\><rsub|a*b>*1<rsub|S>-<around|(|-\<lambda\><rsub|a*b>|)>*1<rsub|S>=B<rsub|a*b>>.
  This proves <math|<math-ss|RS>=I>. The same calculation with <math|S,T>
  reversed proves <math|<math-ss|SR>=I>. Hence the maps are inverse linear
  equivalences, which establishes item 2 conditional on the stated coherence,
  unit, and centering identities.

  For property 3, assume the eventual optical-comparison input: on one tail
  of scales, uniformly for all tensors <math|T>, the grade-five formula is

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|O<rsub|i>T|)><around|(|a|)>=b<rsub|i>*<space|0.17em>U<rsub|i,5><rsup|-1><around*|(|<around|(|D*T|)><around|(|a|)>|)>,<space|2em>b<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0,
  </equation*>

  where <math|U<rsub|i,5>> is an isomorphism and <math|b<rsub|i>> is the
  positive scalar normalization. The optical map is this scalar times
  <math|U<rsub|i,5><rsup|-1>> composed with the divergence rank map.
  Therefore <math|O<rsub|i>T=0> exactly when <math|D*T=0>. Positivity of
  <math|b<rsub|i>> and invertibility of <math|U<rsub|i,5>> are used on this
  common tail. No kernel identity is asserted at earlier scales. Property 4,
  by contrast, also requires the scale-dependent two-direction limits and
  stable inversion established below; the free reference calculation does not
  prove it.

  In particular, for all sufficiently large <math|i> and every tensor
  <math|T>,

  <\equation>
    O<rsub|i>T=0<space|1em>\<Longleftrightarrow\><space|1em>D*T=0.<label|eq:ex-lattice-trace-kernel-comparison>
  </equation>

  Property 4 follows once the correction maps <math|S<rsub|i>> have the
  stated columns <math|u<rsub|i><rsup|0>,u<rsub|i><rsup|1>> and
  <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|i>=O<rsub|i>\<circ\>S<rsub|i>> converges to an
  isomorphism. The stable-inversion argument is written next; the scale-
  dependent convergence estimates are supplied in
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:c14-joint-calibrated-curve>.

  <subsubsection|Solving the two-direction correction>

  At scale <math|i>, let <math|u<rsub|i><rsup|0>,u<rsub|i><rsup|1>\<in\>K> be
  the two correction tensors and define <math|S<rsub|i>:\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>\<to\>K>
  by <math|S<rsub|i><around|(|e<rsub|0>|)>=u<rsub|i><rsup|0>> and
  <math|S<rsub|i><around|(|e<rsub|1>|)>=u<rsub|i><rsup|1>>. Set
  <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|i>=O<rsub|i>\<circ\>S<rsub|i>:\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>\<to\>V<rsub|0>>.
  Let <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|0>\<in\>K> be the uncorrected scaled tensor and
  <math|X<rsub|i>=O<rsub|i><around|(|T<rsub|i><rsup|0>|)>\<in\>V<rsub|0>>.
  Thus exact cancellation <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|i>*c<rsub|i>+X<rsub|i>=0> is
  precisely the optical conservation condition
  <math|O<rsub|i><around|(|T<rsub|i><rsup|0>+S<rsub|i>*c<rsub|i>|)>=0>. The
  two-direction comparison input asserts that <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|i>>
  converges in operator norm to an isomorphism
  <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|\<infty\>>>. The following elementary lemma isolates
  the only finite-dimensional inverse argument.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Stable inversion in two directions><label|lem:ex-lattice-trace-stable-inversion>Let
    <math|E,F> be real normed spaces of dimension two. Suppose linear maps
    <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|i>:E\<to\>F> converge in operator norm to a
    bijection <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|\<infty\>>>. Then there are
    <math|i<rsub|0>> and <math|C<rsub|0>\<less\>\<infty\>> such that for
    <math|i\<geqslant\>i<rsub|0>>, <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|i>> is bijective and

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-B\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>*y|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|0><around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>>*<space|2em><around|(|y\<in\>F|)>.
    </equation*>

    If <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|1>*A<rsub|i>>
    with <math|A<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>, the unique solution
    <math|c<rsub|i>=-\<cal-B\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>*X<rsub|i>> satisfies

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-B\><rsub|i>*c<rsub|i>+X<rsub|i>=0,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|c<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|0>*C<rsub|1>*A<rsub|i>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The unit sphere of <math|E> is compact. Since
    <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|\<infty\>>> is injective, the continuous function
    <math|u\<mapsto\><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-B\><rsub|\<infty\>>*u|\<\|\|\>>>
    has a positive minimum <math|m> on that sphere. For large <math|i>,
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-B\><rsub|i>-\<cal-B\><rsub|\<infty\>>|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>m/2>,
    and hence <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-B\><rsub|i>*u|\<\|\|\>>\<geqslant\><around|(|m/2|)><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>>>
    for every <math|u\<in\>E>. Thus <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|i>> is injective,
    and equality of the finite dimensions makes it surjective. The same lower
    bound gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-B\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>*y|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|2/m|)><around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>>>.
    Taking <math|C<rsub|0>=2/m> proves the claims, including the equation and
    bound for <math|c<rsub|i>>.
  </proof>

  The receiver argument is the already integrated
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-trace-weighted-price> in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-weighted-estimates>; it is
  not duplicated here.

  We recall how the receiver lemma is applied; the symbols in this paragraph
  (<math|H>, <math|x>, <math|\<rho\>>, <math|w<rsub|i>>, <math|u<rsub|i>>,
  <math|U>) are those of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-trace-weighted-price>,
  whose space <math|E> we write <math|\<cal-E\>> to avoid a clash with the
  two-dimensional space <math|E> of the stable-inversion lemma. These symbols
  are not used elsewhere in this section. In the construction,
  <math|\<cal-E\>> is the real vector space of grade-five coefficient arrays
  satisfying reality and signed covariance, not the full unconstrained label
  space. The space <math|H> is the reference vector table. Its canonical
  field map is the quotient projection applied to the grade-five coefficient
  array; its kernel is the line generated by the nonzero equation-of-motion
  array <math|x>. The scalar functional <math|\<rho\>> is the free divergence
  price after that same quotient projection. The exceptional vector is the
  medium

  <\equation*>
    w<rsub|i>=\<beta\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>*\<sigma\><rsub|i><rsup|X>-\<sigma\><rsub|i><rsup|L>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<sigma\><rsub|i><rsup|X>> is the transported chosen response,
  <math|\<sigma\><rsub|i><rsup|L>> is the transported reference response at
  the same scale, and <math|\<beta\><rsub|i>> is the positive scale
  parameter. The receiver application requires the following estimates: the
  maps <math|v<rsub|i>,u<rsub|i>> and their limits <math|f,U>, the medium
  <math|w<rsub|i>>, and the weights <math|e<rsub|i>> satisfy all the
  hypotheses of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-trace-weighted-price>;
  in particular <math|w<rsub|i>\<to\>x>, <math|e<rsub|i>*v<rsub|i><around|(|w<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>0>,
  <math|e<rsub|i>*\<rho\><around|(|w<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>0>, and
  <math|v<rsub|i>*u<rsub|i>=<math-up|id><rsub|H>> eventually. The finite
  chart supplies the right inverse and the limiting chart supplies the
  section only under these input assumptions. The lemma shows how these
  receipts produce a uniformly bounded receiver with small weighted error.
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-trace-medium-receipts> derives the
  three medium limits from its explicit response and decay hypotheses; their
  selected-data application is verified in
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:c14-joint-calibrated-curve> below.

  In the trace construction, <math|E=\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>> labels the two
  correction sectors, <math|F=V<rsub|0>> is the reference table space, and
  <math|A<rsub|i>> is the reciprocal scale price. The limiting matrix is
  invertible because the two reference directions are linearly independent.
  Its convergence is pointwise on a finite basis, hence in operator norm. The
  uncorrected divergence has norm at most <math|C<rsub|1>*A<rsub|i>>. Solving
  the system therefore produces coefficients with the same <math|A<rsub|i>>
  bound and exact cancellation. Applying the flux functional to this identity
  gives the coefficient normalization. The precise weighted remainder
  estimates and the coefficient bound below show that the false-sector term
  vanishes, while the true-sector term is
  <math|-4*c<rsub|i><rsup|1>+o<around|(|1|)>>. Since the uncorrected term
  tends to <math|-\<kappa\>>, the true-sector coefficient tends to
  <math|-\<kappa\>/4>. In the selected construction
  <math|\<kappa\>=s<rsub|G>=2*b<rsub|0><around|(|G|)>> is the slope constant
  of <eqref|eq:shoot-slope>. Conditional on these receipts, the limiting
  physical tensor is fixed by its Ward flux, not by an arbitrary rescaling.

  The coefficient argument uses the following correction and flux inputs on
  the selected construction. The uncorrected size bound,
  <math|A<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|i>>, and the three flux limits are
  supplied in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:c14-joint-calibrated-curve>;
  the bounds on the solved coefficients follow from stable inversion. For the
  two correction directions <math|u<rsub|i><rsup|0>,u<rsub|i><rsup|1>>, a
  linear flux functional <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>>, correction size
  <math|A<rsub|i>>, and positive weight <math|a<rsub|i>>:

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|1>*A<rsub|i>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|c<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|0>*C<rsub|1>*A<rsub|i>,<space|2em>A<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|i>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-trace-correction-bound>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<ell\><rsub|i><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>\<longrightarrow\>-\<kappa\>,<space|2em>a<rsub|i>*\<ell\><rsub|i><around|(|O<rsub|i>u<rsub|i><rsup|0>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0,<space|2em>a<rsub|i>*<around*|(|\<ell\><rsub|i><around|(|O<rsub|i>u<rsub|i><rsup|1>|)>+4|)>\<longrightarrow\>0,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-trace-flux-input>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|c<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|3>*a<rsub|i><space|1em><text|eventually>.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-trace-weighted-coefficients>>>>>
  </align>

  Here <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>> is a linear functional on <math|V<rsub|0>>, and
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|i><around|(|u<rsub|i><rsup|a>|)>> is shorthand for
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|i><around|(|O<rsub|i>u<rsub|i><rsup|a>|)>>. The exact
  cancellation identity <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|i>*c<rsub|i>+X<rsub|i>=0> reads,
  in <math|V<rsub|0>>,

  <\equation*>
    X<rsub|i>+c<rsub|i><rsup|0>*<space|0.17em>O<rsub|i>u<rsub|i><rsup|0>+c<rsub|i><rsup|1>*<space|0.17em>O<rsub|i>u<rsub|i><rsup|1>=0.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|A<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|i>>, the second bound in
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-correction-bound> already gives
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-weighted-coefficients> with
  <math|C<rsub|3>=C<rsub|0>*C<rsub|1>>. By
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-weighted-coefficients> and the weighted limits,
  <math|c<rsub|i><rsup|0>*\<ell\><rsub|i><around|(|u<rsub|i><rsup|0>|)>\<to\>0>
  and <math|c<rsub|i><rsup|1>*<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|i><around|(|u<rsub|i><rsup|1>|)>+4|)>\<to\>0>.
  Hence <math|c<rsub|i><rsup|1>*\<ell\><rsub|i><around|(|u<rsub|i><rsup|1>|)>=-4*c<rsub|i><rsup|1>+o<around|(|1|)>>.
  Apply <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>> to the exact cancellation identity. Since
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|i><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>-\<kappa\>>, it gives
  <math|-\<kappa\>-4*c<rsub|i><rsup|1>+o<around|(|1|)>=0>, so
  <math|c<rsub|i><rsup|1>\<to\>-\<kappa\>/4>. This proves coefficient
  extraction from the correction and flux inputs; it does not construct those
  inputs. It is the finite-dimensional calibration of the corrected stress
  sequence.

  For clarity, the two limits used after solving the correction system follow
  from the following explicit scale-dependent receipts. Let
  <math|B<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0>, let <math|L<rsub|i>,\<Omega\><rsub|i>,\<Delta\><rsub|i>>
  be tensors, and define <math|\<cal-Y\><rsub|i><around|(|T|)>=B<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|T|)>>
  for a linear scaled tensor map <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|i>>. Put
  <math|U<rsub|L,i>=\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|L<rsub|i>|)>>,
  <math|U<rsub|\<Omega\>,i>=\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|\<Omega\><rsub|i>|)>>,
  and <math|U<rsub|\<Delta\>,i>=\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|i>|)>>.
  Assume

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|U<rsub|L,i>>|<cell|\<longrightarrow\>R<rsub|0>,>|<cell|U<rsub|\<Omega\>,i>>|<cell|\<longrightarrow\>R<rsub|\<Omega\>>,>|<cell|U<rsub|\<Delta\>,i>>|<cell|\<longrightarrow\>R<rsub|\<Delta\>>,>|<cell|<frac|R*Q<rsub|i>|B<rsub|i>>>|<cell|\<longrightarrow\>0,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-trace-year-inputs>>>>>
  </align>

  and <math|T<rsub|i>=B<rsub|i>*L<rsub|i>+c<rsub|i><rsup|0>*\<Omega\><rsub|i>+c<rsub|i><rsup|1>*\<Delta\><rsub|i>>
  with <math|<around|\||c<rsub|i><rsup|0>|\|>+<around|\||c<rsub|i><rsup|1>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*R*Q<rsub|i>>.
  For the trace limit, assume in addition

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|B<rsub|i><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>U<rsub|L,i>>|<cell|\<longrightarrow\>0,>|<cell|h<rsub|i><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>U<rsub|\<Omega\>,i>>|<cell|\<longrightarrow\>0,>|<cell|<around|\||c<rsub|i><rsup|0>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C<rprime|'>*h<rsub|i>,>|<cell|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>U<rsub|\<Delta\>,i>>|<cell|\<longrightarrow\>4*O,>|<cell|c<rsub|i><rsup|1>>|<cell|\<longrightarrow\>-\<kappa\>/4.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-trace-trace-inputs>>>>>
  </align>

  Here <math|h<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0> is the weight in the false-sector trace
  estimate. These hypotheses keep the inverse-normalization scale
  <math|B<rsub|i>> distinct from that weight; no equality between these
  quantities is assumed. Here <math|O> denotes the reference trace field, so
  the limit <math|4*O> is field-valued.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Normalized and trace limits from the correction
    receipts><label|lem:ex-lattice-trace-correction-limits>Under
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-year-inputs> and
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-trace-inputs>,

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-Y\><rsub|i><around|(|T<rsub|i>|)>\<longrightarrow\>R<rsub|0>,<space|2em>B<rsub|i><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-Y\><rsub|i><around|(|T<rsub|i>|)>\<longrightarrow\>-\<kappa\>*O.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    By linearity and the definition of <math|\<cal-Y\><rsub|i>>,

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-Y\><rsub|i><around|(|T<rsub|i>|)>=U<rsub|L,i>+<frac|c<rsub|i><rsup|0>|B<rsub|i>>*U<rsub|\<Omega\>,i>+<frac|c<rsub|i><rsup|1>|B<rsub|i>>*U<rsub|\<Delta\>,i>.
    </equation*>

    The coefficient bound and <math|R*Q<rsub|i>/B<rsub|i>\<to\>0> imply
    <math|c<rsub|i><rsup|a>/B<rsub|i>\<to\>0> for <math|a=0,1>. Each
    <math|U>-sequence converges and is therefore bounded. Both correction
    terms vanish, proving the first limit. Taking traces before normalization
    gives

    <\equation*>
      B<rsub|i><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-Y\><rsub|i><around|(|T<rsub|i>|)>=B<rsub|i><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>U<rsub|L,i>+c<rsub|i><rsup|0><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>U<rsub|\<Omega\>,i>+c<rsub|i><rsup|1><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>U<rsub|\<Delta\>,i>.
    </equation*>

    The first term tends to zero. The second is bounded by
    <math|C<rprime|'>*h<rsub|i><around|\||<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>U<rsub|\<Omega\>,i>|\|>>,
    which tends to zero. The last tends to
    <math|<around|(|-\<kappa\>/4|)>*<around|(|4*O|)>=-\<kappa\>*O>.
  </proof>

  In the selected application, <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|i>> is the scaled
  comparison map, <math|B<rsub|i>> is the trace amplitude, and
  <math|\<cal-Y\><rsub|i>> is the normalized year map. Applying the lemma
  requires the beam and washout input: the base and correction beam limits,
  washout ratio, base trace vanishing, false-sector weighted trace vanishing,
  and true-sector trace limit in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-year-inputs> and
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-trace-inputs>, on these same tensors and maps.
  Their same-data construction is installed in
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:c14-joint-calibrated-curve>. The
  coefficient limit in the latter display is the conditional consequence
  proved above. The lemma proves the normalized tensor and trace conclusions
  once these estimates are installed; it does not identify the two weights or
  infer one from the other.

  <subsubsection|The calibrated stress sequence>

  For this application retain the single comparison curve whose existence is
  supplied by Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:c14-joint-calibrated-curve>,
  with the requirements below. The index <math|i> runs along that curve, with
  positive radii <math|r<rsub|i>> strictly decreasing to zero. For these
  supplied admissible choices, form the corrected maps <math|C<rsub|i,d>> of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-selected-trace-gallery> and their
  early-index completion <math|U<rsub|i,d>> described below. The theorem
  applies to every admissible choice, so it applies to this same retained
  curve. For each fixed grade <math|d>, <math|U<rsub|i,d>=C<rsub|i,d>> for
  all sufficiently large <math|i>; equality at every index is not required.
  The projected maps before correction are denoted
  <math|U<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|i,d>>, never <math|U<rsub|i,d>>. Write
  <math|U<rsub|i>=U<rsub|i,4>> for the grade-four chart and, for a tensor
  <math|T>, set

  <\equation*>
    Y<rsub|i><around|(|T|)>=r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*U<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*T.
  </equation*>

  <subparagraph|Joint-curve input for calibrated stress.>
  <with|the-label|17.1.3|<label|par:ex-trace-joint-curve>> Fix the physical
  and reference tables, their derivative maps, the physical conserved
  centered tensor <math|R>, reference stress <math|R<rsub|0>>, reference
  trace field <math|O> and Ward normalization <math|\<kappa\>>. Fix also the
  extraction and its ultrafilter. Assume that there exist positive radii
  <math|r<rsub|i>> strictly decreasing to zero, admissible matching charts
  and finite-volume limits satisfying the validity hypotheses of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-selected-trace-gallery>, such that
  the following additional receipts hold after forming and completing their
  corrected maps as above. On this same curve, require the eventual
  optical-comparison input and the coherent-center input used in the rank
  argument, the operator-norm convergence of <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|i>> to an
  isomorphism, and the bound <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|1>*A<rsub|i>>.
  Require the correction cancellation and estimates
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-correction-bound>\U
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-trace-inputs>, with
  <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|i>=B<rsub|i>*Y<rsub|i>>. Thus the two-direction
  tensors use this curve's grade-four and grade-five maps, and the beam and
  weighted trace limits use these same tensors and weights. For the corrected
  tensor <math|T<rsub|i>>, require both separated shell-flux limits

  <\equation*>
    \<phi\><rsub|i><around|(|R|)>\<longrightarrow\>y\<ne\>0,<space|2em>\<phi\><rsub|i><around|(|T<rsub|i>|)>\<longrightarrow\>y.
  </equation*>

  The physical and reference Ward identities and separated-jet comparisons
  used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-stress-normalization> must
  concern these same data. Every use of grades four and five is restricted to
  a common tail on which the needed optical and coherence identities hold. A
  further subsequence is allowed only with all receipts, radii and charts
  restricted together. Tails may depend on each fixed finite collection of
  grades, labels and tests; the tables, derivative maps, <math|R>,
  <math|R<rsub|0>>, <math|O>, <math|\<kappa\>> and cutoff extraction remain
  fixed.

  Only one curve carrying these additional receipts is required for the
  normalization application; the prescribed-sequence one-stage property is
  not a premise of the chart-validity theorem. The separate existence result
  in Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:ex-lattice-prescribed-trace-gallery>
  remains conditional for every sequence for which its selection input is
  available; no normalization receipt on every prescribed sequence is
  asserted. An all-sequence trace-asymptotic application must retain its
  stronger joint-receipt requirement on the requested sequences; the
  one-curve input here does not supply that requirement.
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:c14-joint-calibrated-curve> supplies this
  joint input on the funded family. Curves for the individual receipts
  separately, or a ratio of scalar normalizers tending to a constant, do not
  identify the charts and derivative maps. The chart theorem below does not
  assume or produce these additional analytic receipts.

  Apply Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-trace-correction-limits> with
  <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|i>=B<rsub|i>*Y<rsub|i>>, so that
  <math|\<cal-Y\><rsub|i>=Y<rsub|i>>. The corrected tensor <math|T<rsub|i>>
  is conserved by its exact correction cancellation and the comparison
  identity <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-kernel-comparison>. The lemma gives

  <\equation>
    Y<rsub|i><around|(|T<rsub|i>|)>\<longrightarrow\>R<rsub|0>,<space|2em>B<rsub|i><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>Y<rsub|i><around|(|T<rsub|i>|)>\<longrightarrow\>-\<kappa\>*O,<label|eq:ex-lattice-trace-trial-stress>
  </equation>

  where <math|R<rsub|0>> is the reference stress tensor and <math|O> the
  reference trace field. For the physical centered tensor <math|R>, a
  separated shell-flux test on tensors, which we denote
  <math|\<phi\><rsub|i>> to distinguish it from the functional
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>> on <math|V<rsub|0>>, has limits
  <math|\<phi\><rsub|i><around|(|R|)>\<to\>y\<neq\>0> and
  <math|\<phi\><rsub|i><around|(|T<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>y>. Since the conserved
  space has dimension one, eventually <math|T<rsub|i>=t<rsub|i>*R>. Linearity
  gives <math|t<rsub|i>=\<phi\><rsub|i><around|(|T<rsub|i>|)>/\<phi\><rsub|i><around|(|R|)>\<to\>1>,
  so dividing <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-trial-stress> by <math|t<rsub|i>>
  proves

  <\equation*>
    Y<rsub|i><around|(|R|)>\<longrightarrow\>R<rsub|0>,<space|2em>B<rsub|i><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>Y<rsub|i><around|(|R|)>\<longrightarrow\>-\<kappa\>*O.
  </equation*>

  This is precisely the normalization argument used in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:trace-normalized-stress>. The two shell-flux
  limits are the physical-normalization estimates verified in
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:c14-joint-calibrated-curve>: the physical
  and reference translation Ward identities and the separated-jet comparison
  must give these limits on this same comparison curve. The argument above
  makes explicit why the common nonzero Ward value forces the multiplicative
  constant to be one.

  <subsubsection|Strict subsequences under a prescribed-scale selection
  input>

  Fix any sequence <math|s<rsub|n>\<gtr\>0> with <math|s<rsub|n>\<to\>0>. For
  a selection constrained to this prescribed sequence, the scalar recursion
  uses the following one-stage selection property, supplied on the funded
  family by Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:c14-prescribed-chart-supply>, at
  each integer level <math|d>: for every lower index <math|N> and every
  <math|\<theta\>\<gtr\>0> there are <math|n\<gtr\>N>, a level
  <math|j\<geqslant\>d>, and a finite-level chart <math|V<rsub|d,j>> such
  that

  <\equation>
    s<rsub|n>\<less\>\<theta\>,<space|2em>V<rsub|d,j><text| has
    cutoff->d<text| matching receipts at location
    >j,<space|2em>r<around|(|V<rsub|d,j>|)>=s<rsub|n>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-trace-one-stage>
  </equation>

  The matching receipts cover grades through <math|d>, and their accuracy is
  the one requested at cutoff <math|d>. The quantifiers require a chart at an
  exact member of the prescribed sequence after every lower index <math|N>,
  not merely at some radius below <math|\<theta\>>. This is an explicit
  selection input.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Recursive strict scale selection><label|lem:ex-lattice-trace-strict-subsequence>Suppose
    the one-stage selection property <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-one-stage>
    holds at every level. Then there is a strictly increasing sequence of
    indices <math|n<rsub|d>> and matching charts <math|V<rsub|d,j<rsub|d>>>
    at levels <math|j<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>d> such that
    <math|s<rsub|n<rsub|d>>> is strictly decreasing to zero and
    <math|r<around|(|V<rsub|d,j<rsub|d>>|)>=s<rsub|n<rsub|d>>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Choose an admissible triple <math|<around|(|n<rsub|0>,j<rsub|0>,V<rsub|0,j<rsub|0>>|)>>
    at level zero, using the one-stage property with lower index zero and any
    positive threshold. Having chosen <math|<around|(|n<rsub|d>,j<rsub|d>,V<rsub|d,j<rsub|d>>|)>>,
    apply the property at level <math|d+1> with lower index <math|n<rsub|d>>
    and threshold <math|s<rsub|n<rsub|d>>\<gtr\>0>. It gives
    <math|<around|(|n<rsub|d+1>,j<rsub|d+1>,V<rsub|d+1,j<rsub|d+1>>|)>> with
    <math|n<rsub|d+1>\<gtr\>n<rsub|d>> and
    <math|s<rsub|n<rsub|d+1>>\<less\>s<rsub|n<rsub|d>>>. Induction gives
    strict monotonicity of the indices and strict decrease of the selected
    scales. Since <math|n<rsub|d>\<to\>\<infty\>> and
    <math|s<rsub|n>\<to\>0>, the subsequence <math|s<rsub|n<rsub|d>>> tends
    to zero. The selected chart receipts hold at each level by construction.
  </proof>

  An arbitrarily-small-radius chart estimate alone does not establish
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-one-stage>: its good radii need not meet a
  prescribed sequence. The arbitrarily-small-radius construction chooses its
  own radii successively, below the preceding radius and a vanishing upper
  bound. The one-stage input is instead the stronger one-step output assumed
  in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-firesky-selection>, which supplies
  a chart at an exact member <math|s<rsub|n>> of the prescribed sequence. It
  is to be obtained from a funded finite-volume selection estimate whose
  funded chart and comparison hypotheses must hold for the present data. The
  lemma above proves the prescribed-sequence conclusion conditional on that
  input; it does not assume that a preselected scaling already follows the
  requested sequence. If the input is available for every positive null
  sequence, the conclusion holds for every such sequence.
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:c14-prescribed-chart-supply> proves that
  additional availability on the funded family.

  <subsubsection|Corrected lifts and compatibility across
  grades><label|subsec:ex-trace-corrected-lifts>

  The gradewise maps obtained by finite-volume extraction and projection need
  not preserve the filtration inclusions. Here extraction and projection are
  performed first; the correction is then made directly in the selected table
  fields. Write <math|E<rsub|d>> for the reference field space in grade
  <math|d> and <math|S<rsub|d>> for that selected table field space. Let

  <\equation*>
    \<iota\><rsup|E><rsub|d>:E<rsub|d>\<longrightarrow\>E<rsub|d+1>,<space|2em>\<iota\><rsup|S><rsub|d>:S<rsub|d>\<longrightarrow\>S<rsub|d+1>
  </equation*>

  be their inclusion maps. For <math|d\<less\>e>, write
  <math|\<iota\><rsup|E><rsub|d,e>> and <math|\<iota\><rsup|S><rsub|d,e>> for
  their composites, and use the identity maps when <math|d=e>. They obey the
  transitivity identities. The reference inclusion is injective. Choose a
  linear left inverse <math|R<rsub|d>:E<rsub|d+1>\<to\>E<rsub|d>>, so
  <math|R<rsub|d>*\<iota\><rsup|E><rsub|d>=1>. When fields carry the finite
  signed symmetry and real structure, this retraction can be chosen to
  preserve them: begin with any left inverse and average its conjugates over
  the finite signed group. Then average that equivariant map with its
  conjugate-linear reflection. The signed actions commute with the real
  involution, so this second averaging preserves signed equivariance. Each
  conjugate remains a left inverse because the inclusion intertwines both
  actions. This is the finite-dimensional splitting used below.

  For each cutoff <math|k>, let <math|U<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|k,d>:E<rsub|d>\<to\>S<rsub|d>>
  (<math|d\<leqslant\>k>) be the raw projected chart maps. Define the
  corrected maps through grade <math|k> by

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|C<rsub|k,0>>|<cell|=U<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|k,0>,<eq-number>>>|<row|<cell|C<rsub|k,d+1>>|<cell|=\<iota\><rsup|S><rsub|d>*C<rsub|k,d>*R<rsub|d>+U<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|k,d+1>*<around|(|1-\<iota\><rsup|E><rsub|d>*R<rsub|d>|)>.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-trace-corrected-lift>>>>>
  </align>

  The first summand corrects the part of a field already represented at the
  previous grade; the second retains the new complement. Directly using
  <math|R<rsub|d>*\<iota\><rsup|E><rsub|d>=1> gives

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|C<rsub|k,d+1>*\<iota\><rsup|E><rsub|d>>|<cell|=\<iota\><rsup|S><rsub|d>*C<rsub|k,d>*R<rsub|d>*\<iota\><rsup|E><rsub|d>+U<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|k,d+1>*<around|(|\<iota\><rsup|E><rsub|d>-\<iota\><rsup|E><rsub|d>*R<rsub|d>*\<iota\><rsup|E><rsub|d>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=\<iota\><rsup|S><rsub|d>*C<rsub|k,d>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Induction and transitivity therefore give, for every pair
  <math|d\<leqslant\>e\<leqslant\>k>,

  <\equation>
    C<rsub|k,e>*\<iota\><rsup|E><rsub|d,e>=\<iota\><rsup|S><rsub|d,e>*C<rsub|k,d>.<label|eq:ex-trace-corrected-balanced>
  </equation>

  This is an exact identity whenever the displayed grades are at most
  <math|k>, before the <math|k\<to\>\<infty\>> limit. It is the needed
  balanced inclusion relation; unrelated isomorphisms at early indices are
  not used to obtain it.

  The same correction preserves the weak symmetry conditions by induction,
  since <math|R<rsub|d>>, both inclusions, and
  <math|U<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|k,d>> intertwine the signed and real
  structures. The unit in each grade is the iterated inclusion of the
  constant unit in grade zero. Thus the exact grade-zero unit identity,
  together with the balanced relation, gives the unit identity at every
  grade. It also preserves the tested approximation. To see the exact
  finite-slot identity, let <math|I> be a subset of the tuple's slots already
  using <math|C>, and let <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|k><rsup|I><around|(|v|)>> be
  the tested chart correlation with <math|C> in those slots and
  <math|U<rsup|<math-up|raw>>> in the others. For <math|i\<nin\>I> with
  <math|v<rsub|i>\<in\>E<rsub|d+1>>, put <math|p<rsub|i>=R<rsub|d>*v<rsub|i>>
  and <math|q<rsub|i>=<around|(|1-\<iota\><rsup|E><rsub|d>*R<rsub|d>|)>*v<rsub|i>>.
  Linearity in that slot, formula <eqref|eq:ex-trace-corrected-lift>, and the
  table inclusion identity give the exact equality

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-W\><rsub|k><rsup|I\<cup\><around|{|i|}>><around|(|v|)>-\<cal-W\><rsub|0><around|(|v|)>=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-W\><rsub|k><rsup|I\<cup\><around|{|i|}>>*<around|(|v*<around|[|i\<mapsto\>p<rsub|i>|]>|)>-\<cal-W\><rsub|0>*<around|(|v*<around|[|i\<mapsto\>p<rsub|i>|]>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|+<around*|(|\<cal-W\><rsub|k><rsup|I>*<around|(|v*<around|[|i\<mapsto\>q<rsub|i>|]>|)>-\<cal-W\><rsub|0>*<around|(|v*<around|[|i\<mapsto\>q<rsub|i>|]>|)>|)>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Here <math|v*<around|[|i\<mapsto\>p<rsub|i>|]>> has grade <math|d> in slot
  <math|i>, and <math|v*<around|[|i\<mapsto\>q<rsub|i>|]>> has grade
  <math|d+1>. Induction on the number of corrected slots, using this grade
  reduction for a corrected slot, expresses the fully corrected discrepancy
  for any fixed tuple as a finite sum of raw tested discrepancies at fixed
  labels. Every summand tends to zero by raw approach, so corrected
  correlations approach the reference. The finite sum's size and label norms
  may depend on the tuple; this qualitative argument does not retain the raw
  numerical rate without tracking those factors.

  The raw maps <math|U<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|k,d>> below are formed by the
  finite-dimensional ultrafilter limits and grade projections. Apply the
  correction after this projection. The balanced identity
  <eqref|eq:ex-trace-corrected-balanced> is then exact for each fixed pair of
  grades. Once tested-correlation convergence makes the corrected maps
  <math|C<rsub|k,d>> isomorphisms eventually at each fixed grade, the
  selected isomorphisms obey the inclusion identity for each fixed pair on a
  tail of indices. The exceptional tail may depend on the pair of grades; no
  single tail uniform in all grades is asserted. This is the compatible
  gradewise scaling conclusion needed below.

  Here are the finite-dimensional gluing details. At cutoff <math|k>, let

  <\equation*>
    <with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|k>=<big|prod><rsub|d=0><rsup|k><math-up|Hom><rsub|\<bbb-C\>><around|(|E<rsub|d>,A<rsub|d>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|E<rsub|d>> is the free reference grade-<math|d> field space and
  <math|A<rsub|d>> is the finite family coefficient space at grade <math|d>.
  Write <math|S<rsub|d>> for the selected table field space. This is
  finite-dimensional. The finite-volume lifts into
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|k>> are assumed bounded on a set
  belonging to the one fixed ultrafilter. This is the finite-chart lift
  input: for each fixed <math|k> there is a finite bound, independent of
  volume and period, on that set. A bound of this form is assumed on an
  <math|F>-late set in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-firesky-lift-bound>. This
  stronger hypothesis supplies the ultrafilter-set bound when the ultrafilter
  refines the fused filter of the extraction order <math|F>.
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:c14-prescribed-chart-supply> proves this
  bound for the chosen funded charts. The closure of a bounded set in a
  finite-dimensional space is compact, so every ultrafilter on that late set
  has a limit <math|Y<rsub|k>\<in\><with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|k>>. This is
  done for each integer <math|k> using the same ultrafilter. Let
  <math|P<rsub|d>:A<rsub|d>\<to\>S<rsub|d>> be the selected table's
  projection. Put

  <\equation*>
    U<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|k,d>=P<rsub|d>\<circ\>Y<rsub|k><around|(|d|)>:E<rsub|d>\<longrightarrow\>S<rsub|d>*<space|2em><around|(|d\<leqslant\>k|)>.
  </equation*>

  The exact finite-volume reality, signed-reflection, and unit identities
  hold on a set belonging to the ultrafilter. Their maps are continuous, so
  they pass to <math|Y<rsub|k>> and then to
  <math|U<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|k,d>>.

  For fixed <math|k>, the calibrated-smearing argument also passes each
  finite-volume short-test bound to the limit. Let
  <math|A<rsub|k,v><around|(|t|)>> denote the tested correlation of a fixed
  tuple <math|v=<around|(|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|n>|)>> after applying
  <math|U<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|k,d<rsub|a>>> and scaling by the supplied
  radius <math|r<rsub|k>> (with <math|r<rsub|k>=s<rsub|n<rsub|k>>> in the
  prescribed-sequence case); let <math|A<rsub|0,v><around|(|t|)>> denote its
  reference correlation. For a tuple of arity <math|n\<leqslant\>k>, labels
  of grades at most <math|k>, and any smooth compactly supported test
  <math|t> inside the cutoff window, with <math|sup
  <around|\||t|\|>\<leqslant\>M>, the bound is

  <\equation>
    <around|\||A<rsub|k,v><around|(|t|)>-A<rsub|0,v><around|(|t|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|1|k+1>*<space|0.17em>M*<big|prod><rsub|a=1><rsup|n><around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|a>|\<\|\|\>>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-trace-test-error>
  </equation>

  The change-of-variables identity for the dilated test converts the
  finite-scale integral to the displayed tested correlation. For any
  <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>, the upper-bound receipt makes the finite-scale
  discrepancy at most the right side plus <math|\<eta\>> on a late set. Since
  the fixed ultrafilter lies below that late filter, the limit obeys the same
  bound after letting <math|\<eta\>*\<downarrow\>*0>. The cutoff window
  radius tends to infinity with <math|k>, so it eventually contains the
  support of each fixed test. Since <math|<around|(|k+1|)><rsup|-1>\<to\>0>,
  the tested correlations converge to their reference values. This is the
  approach property needed for the scaling chart.

  Fix a grade <math|d>. The reference smoothness and test-function uniqueness
  imply that the linear functionals obtained by inserting a grade-<math|d>
  field into smooth tested correlations separate points of <math|E<rsub|d>>.
  Since <math|E<rsub|d>> is finite-dimensional, choose finitely many such
  tests <math|\<lambda\><rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,\<lambda\><rsub|m>> spanning its
  dual. The raw approach estimate gives convergence of their values for
  <math|U<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|k,d>>, and the finite-sum correction
  argument gives the same convergence for <math|C<rsub|k,d>>. On a finite
  basis the combined maps <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|k>=<around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|j,k>|)>>
  therefore converge in operator norm to the injective reference map
  <math|\<Lambda\>=<around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|j>|)>>. The minimum of
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Lambda\>*v|\<\|\|\>>> on the unit sphere is
  positive. Hence <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|k>> is injective for all sufficiently
  large <math|k>, and since each functional factors through
  <math|C<rsub|k,d>>, so is <math|C<rsub|k,d>>. The dimension receipt gives
  <math|dim S<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>dim E<rsub|d>>; injectivity gives the
  reverse inequality. Thus <math|C<rsub|k,d>> is an isomorphism eventually
  for each fixed <math|d>. The exceptional tail may depend on <math|d>.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Validity of supplied gradewise ultralimit
    charts><label|thm:ex-lattice-selected-trace-gallery>Fix the physical and
    reference smooth multilinear tables and their finite-dimensional field
    spaces, inclusions, derivative maps, units, real structures and signed
    actions as above. Retain the table inclusion identities and their
    compatibility with these structures. Fix the cutoff extraction and one
    ultrafilter refining its late filter. Suppose that positive radii
    <math|r<rsub|k>> strictly decreasing to zero and matching charts at
    cutoff <math|k> are supplied, with the following receipts on these same
    data: the finite-volume lift bound, exact unit, reality and
    signed-covariance identities, the dimension bound <math|dim
    S<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>dim E<rsub|d>> at each grade, the reference
    separating-test property, and the finite-volume short-test bound
    described above at each cutoff. The latter has arity and grade caps
    <math|k>, tolerance <math|<around|(|k+1|)><rsup|-1>> and cutoff windows
    eventually containing every compact subset of each fixed configuration
    space. The lifts and their calibrated tested limits use this fixed
    extraction and ultrafilter. For every such supplied family and its
    finite-volume limits, form the raw projected maps and apply the
    corrected-lift recursion. No prescribed-sequence one-stage property is
    assumed for this validity statement. The resulting maps
    <math|C<rsub|k,d>:E<rsub|d>\<to\>S<rsub|d>> (<math|d\<leqslant\>k>) have
    the following properties: for each fixed <math|d>, <math|C<rsub|k,d>> is
    an isomorphism for all sufficiently large <math|k>; the exact unit,
    reality, and signed-covariance identities hold at those indices; and for
    each fixed tuple and compactly supported test, the tested correlation
    converges to its reference value as <math|k\<to\>\<infty\>>. More
    precisely, the uniform test estimate holds: for every fixed tuple
    <math|v> of reference labels, every compact set <math|Q> in configuration
    space and every <math|M\<gtr\>0>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ex-lattice-trace-uniform-tests>sup<rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|t\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>>,<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>t\<subset\>Q>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|t|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>M>>>>>><around*|\||A<rsup|C><rsub|k,v><around|(|t|)>-A<rsub|0,v><around|(|t|)>|\|>\<longrightarrow\>0,
    </equation>

    where <math|A<rsup|C><rsub|k,v>> is the tested correlation formed with
    the corrected maps <math|C<rsub|k,d>>. Moreover, for each fixed pair
    <math|d\<leqslant\>e>, the corrected maps obey
    <math|C<rsub|k,e>*\<iota\><rsup|E><rsub|d,e>=\<iota\><rsup|S><rsub|d,e>*C<rsub|k,d>>
    for all sufficiently large <math|k>. The tail may depend on
    <math|<around|(|d,e|)>>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Fix any supplied family and corresponding finite-volume limits with the
    stated receipts. At each cutoff <math|k>, the common-ultrafilter
    finite-dimensional limit gives the raw projected maps
    <math|U<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|k,d>> and their tested correlation
    estimates. The corrected-lift calculation above produces
    <math|C<rsub|k,d>>; the finite slot argument transfers tested
    convergence, reality, and signed covariance to these maps. The finite
    separating-test argument then proves eventual injectivity grade by grade,
    and the dimension receipt makes each fixed-grade map eventually
    bijective. The exact balanced identity holds for every corrected map by
    <eqref|eq:ex-trace-corrected-balanced>; for each fixed pair of grades,
    intersecting the two eventual bijectivity tails shows the selected maps
    satisfy the claimed inclusion relation.

    For <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-uniform-tests>, the finite-slot expansion
    writes <math|A<rsup|C><rsub|k,v><around|(|t|)>-A<rsub|0,v><around|(|t|)>>
    as a finite sum of raw discrepancies <math|A<rsub|k,w><around|(|t|)>-A<rsub|0,w><around|(|t|)>>
    at tuples <math|w> whose labels are obtained from those of <math|v> by
    the fixed maps <math|R<rsub|d>> and <math|1-\<iota\><rsup|E><rsub|d>*R<rsub|d>>;
    these tuples and their number do not depend on <math|k> or on <math|t>.
    Once the cutoff window contains <math|Q> and <math|k> exceeds the arity
    and grades involved, <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-test-error> bounds each
    summand by <math|<around|(|k+1|)><rsup|-1>*M*<big|prod><rsub|a><around|\<\|\|\>|w<rsub|a>|\<\|\|\>>>.
    Summing, the supremum in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-uniform-tests> is at
    most <math|<around|(|k+1|)><rsup|-1>*M*K<rsub|v>> with a constant
    <math|K<rsub|v>> depending only on <math|v>, and it tends to zero. The
    argument uses only the stated receipts of the chosen charts and limits,
    so it applies to every admissible choice, in particular the one retained
    in the joint-curve input for stress normalization.
  </proof>

  <\corollary>
    <dueto|Existence along a prescribed null
    sequence><label|cor:ex-lattice-prescribed-trace-gallery>Fix a positive
    sequence <math|s<rsub|n>\<to\>0> satisfying the one-stage selection
    property <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-one-stage> at every cutoff. Retain
    the fixed table, extraction and chart receipts of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-selected-trace-gallery> for the
    matching charts supplied by that property. Then there are strictly
    increasing indices <math|n<rsub|k>> and matching charts with
    <math|r<rsub|k>=s<rsub|n<rsub|k>>> strictly decreasing to zero. Their
    finite-volume ultralimits and corrected maps have all the conclusions of
    that theorem. The same conclusions hold for every admissible selection on
    this prescribed sequence with these receipts.
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-trace-strict-subsequence> supplies
    the indices and matching charts. The bounded lifts have ultralimits along
    the fixed ultrafilter, as above. All other receipts are retained by
    hypothesis, so Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-selected-trace-gallery>
    applies. Its validity conclusion applies equally to any other supplied
    admissible selection. This implication does not assert the one-stage
    property for an arbitrary sequence without its additional input.
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|Completion and uniform tests.>
  <with|the-label|17.1.5|<label|par:ex-trace-completed-uniform-tests>> To
  obtain isomorphisms at every index, fix for each grade <math|d> an index
  <math|k<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>d> for which <math|C<rsub|k<rsub|d>,d>> is
  invertible, and set

  <\equation*>
    U<rsub|k,d>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|C<rsub|k,d>,>|<cell|d\<leqslant\>k<text|
    and >C<rsub|k,d><text| is invertible>,>>|<row|<cell|C<rsub|k<rsub|d>,d>,>|<cell|<text|otherwise>.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  For each fixed <math|d> this changes only finitely many terms. Hence the
  tested limits for every fixed finite tuple are unchanged. More strongly,
  for a fixed tuple the equality of its finitely many grade maps holds on one
  tail independent of the test. Thus <math|A<rsup|U><rsub|k,v><around|(|t|)>=A<rsup|C><rsub|k,v><around|(|t|)>>
  there for every test <math|t>, and the uniform supremum estimate
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-uniform-tests> transfers to <math|U> unchanged.
  For each fixed pair <math|d\<leqslant\>e>, intersecting the two agreement
  tails transfers the balanced inclusion identity to <math|U>; the same
  argument transfers the unit and symmetry identities. The exact identity for
  <math|C> before completion is therefore distinct from the eventual identity
  for <math|U>. No tail uniform in all grades is needed or asserted. In the
  calibrated stress sequence above, <math|i> is this same index <math|k>,
  with any further subsequence applied to all the data together as required
  by the joint-curve input.

  The corrected-lift construction proves the pairwise eventual inclusion
  identities for the corrected maps; it does not claim one tail uniform over
  all grades. Production of the full calibrated curve also requires several
  substantive analytic estimates: the finite-volume chart selection (with the
  stronger prescribed-sequence property if that version is requested); the
  scale-dependent two-direction limits; the base and correction beam limits;
  the weighted false-sector flux washout and true-sector flux convergence;
  the separated-jet comparison; and the finite chart lift, unit, covariance,
  short-test, and dimension receipts used in the gluing. The next proposition
  installs these inputs on the same selected data. The finite-dimensional
  arguments above are used only after their particular hypotheses have been
  matched to those producers.

  <subsubsection|Installing the analytic inputs on one selected curve>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The joint calibrated curve on the funded
    family><label|prop:c14-joint-calibrated-curve>Fix the common funded
    family with the joint chart purchases of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:trace-joint-chart-purchase>, its
    selected hierarchy, cutoff extraction and refining ultrafilter. Use its
    actual response, sensor and branch constructions of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-scribe-producer>. For
    every positive null sequence there is a strict subsequence and a
    comparison curve on those radii satisfying the joint requirements of
    <hlink|the calibrated-stress construction|#par:ex-trace-joint-curve>. The
    normalized physical stress and the reference stress are the same for all
    these choices. In particular, with <math|q<rsub|i>> the actual arch
    scale,

    <\equation*>
      r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*U<rsub|i,4><rsup|-1>*R\<longrightarrow\>R<rsub|0>,<space|2em>q<rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*U<rsub|i,4><rsup|-1><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>R\<longrightarrow\>-\<kappa\>*O.
    </equation*>

    Only the comparison charts and their radii are selected after the null
    sequence; the field table and its normalization are fixed beforehand.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:c14-prescribed-chart-supply> supplies
    the charts and all the validity receipts of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-selected-trace-gallery>. Complete
    their corrected maps at early indices as above. For every fixed finite
    group of grades these maps agree with the corrected maps on one tail, so
    all the receipts below can be restricted to that tail. Their uniform
    short-test bound gives derivative transport and compact convergence of
    all fixed jets by Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-stress-normalization>.
    The centered chart and its centered inverse identify the stress-array
    spaces. The right-inverse argument in that section then gives the
    physical conserved line before any correction tensor or trace limit is
    used. The reference input is the free rank calculation with its
    constructed derivative plan. Thus this step does not assume the joint
    normalization conclusion we are proving.

    We identify the analytic estimates with their actual suppliers. Use the
    notation of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-scribe-producer>:
    <math|k<rsub|i>> is the summit of the selected chart,
    <math|z<rsub|i>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|i>>>,
    <math|q<rsub|i>=\<zeta\><around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>\<gtr\>0> is its arch
    scale, <math|\<rho\><rsub|i>> its response-size price, and
    <math|t<rsub|i>> its sensor weight. In the present chapter's notation set

    <\equation*>
      B<rsub|i>=q<rsub|i>,<space|1em>A<rsub|i>=\<rho\><rsub|i>,<space|1em>a<rsub|i>=t<rsub|i>,<space|1em>h<rsub|i>=z<rsub|i>,<space|1em>\<cal-P\><rsub|i>=q<rsub|i>*Y<rsub|i>,<space|2em>Y<rsub|i><around|(|Q|)>=r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*U<rsub|i,4><rsup|-1>*Q.
    </equation*>

    In particular <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|i>> includes the arch factor, while
    <math|Y<rsub|i>> does not. The tensor to be compared with the physical
    stress is <math|T<rsub|i>=q<rsub|i>*L<rsub|i>+c<rsub|i><rsup|0>*\<Omega\><rsub|i>+c<rsub|i><rsup|1>*\<Delta\><rsub|i>>,
    without an additional division by <math|q<rsub|i>>.

    The optical operator is the exact operator
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-optical-identity>, hence has the physical
    divergence kernel on this same coherent tail. The two correction columns
    converge in operator norm to the free two-direction map, as proved in the
    two-direction construction of that section. Its inverse is uniformly
    bounded. The response-size bound gives
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*\<rho\><rsub|i>>,
    so solving the two-dimensional system gives
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|c<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rprime|'>*\<rho\><rsub|i>>
    and exact cancellation. These establish the optical and correction
    requirements here, including conservation of <math|T<rsub|i>>; they do
    not require a limit of a chosen basis of kernels.

    For the receiver, the selected medium is
    <math|q<rsub|i><rsup|-1><math-ss|q><rsub|k<rsub|i>>-<math-ss|w><rsub|k<rsub|i>>>,
    with precisely the chosen and reference responses of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-weighted-estimates>. Its
    three weighted limits are proved there using the priced mission and the
    small-means estimate. The selected sensor uses the finite-volume inverse
    column of this chart and the same fixed-level ultrafilter limit. The
    application in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-scribe-producer>
    checks its tested and adjacent-column prices, including their common lift
    identity. Therefore its receiver is uniformly bounded and its error is
    <math|o<around|(|t<rsub|i><rsup|-1>|)>> in operator norm. Applied to the
    principal forecast of size <math|O<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|i>|)>> and the
    bounded correction forecasts, it gives exactly
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-sensor-limits>. Since
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|i>>, these are
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-correction-bound>\U
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-flux-input> with the dictionary above. The
    scalar cancellation consequently proves
    <math|c<rsub|i><rsup|1>\<to\>-\<kappa\>/4>. The principal limit is
    unweighted; only the two correction errors are multiplied by
    <math|t<rsub|i>>.

    For the tensor limits, the actual branch application in that same section
    identifies its ordered fourth-order labels with
    <math|L<rsub|i>,\<Omega\><rsub|i>,\<Delta\><rsub|i>>. The exact arch
    identity <eqref|eq:scribe-same-arch> identifies their scaled map with
    <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|i>=q<rsub|i>*Y<rsub|i>> before using any limits.
    Thus <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-field-receipts> supplies the three beam
    limits. The elementary clock estimates give
    <math|q<rsub|i>/z<rsub|i>\<to\>1> and
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|i>/q<rsub|i>\<to\>0>, supplying the last requirement
    in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-year-inputs>. The exponent-one branch
    washout gives

    <\equation*>
      z<rsub|i><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|L<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>0,<space|2em>z<rsub|i><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|\<Omega\><rsub|i>|)>\<to\>0.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|q<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>2*z<rsub|i>> eventually, the first also
    holds with <math|q<rsub|i>> in place of <math|z<rsub|i>>. Since
    <math|<around|\||c<rsub|i><rsup|0>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*\<rho\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>C*z<rsub|i>>,
    the second has exactly the weight required here. Finally the true branch
    has trace limit <math|4*O>. These facts prove every clause of
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-trace-inputs>. The correction-limits lemma now
    yields the two asserted limits first for <math|T<rsub|i>>.

    It remains to bind them to the fixed physical normalization. The annular
    field construction supplies the raw physical Ward responses for the
    conserved line. The finite-row argument normalizes these by one nonzero
    scalar; Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:maxwell-selected-reference-ward>
    identifies the reference tensor with coefficient one as
    <math|-J<rsup|0>>. These operations precede the trace correction and use
    only the chart comparison and shell law. They are the physical and
    reference Ward identities on the fixed table used in the same-history
    physical-transfer argument. Choose its nonzero derivative observer.
    Compact convergence on the support of the shell gradient gives
    operator-norm convergence of its shell functionals: the error is bounded
    by the sum of the <math|L<rsup|1>> norms of the four cutoff derivatives
    times the maximum correlation error on that compact separated shell. The
    exact change of variables is <eqref|eq:scribe-shell-change>. Derivative
    transport gives the physical flux limit; the field limit of
    <math|T<rsub|i>> gives its flux limit. Both limits are the same nonzero
    observer value, as proved in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-common-test>.
    Hence <math|T<rsub|i>=\<lambda\><rsub|i>*R> with
    <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>\<to\>1>, and division by this scalar proves the
    displayed limits for <math|R>. This is also
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-physical-transfer> under the stated
    dictionary.

    All estimates used here hold on the selected family for every chart past
    a fixed finite-request threshold. The joint purchase was made before the
    null sequence, and the sensor and branch constructions are applied to the
    selected charts themselves. At each fixed chart index only finitely many
    intermediate ultrafilter sets are intersected. Subsequent tails or
    subsequences restrict all maps, responses and weights together. No
    equality of independently selected charts is inferred from a scalar
    ratio, and no cutoff uniform over all grades or all tests is claimed.
    This proves the prescribed-sequence assertion as well as its one-curve
    specialization.
  </proof>

  <subsection|From funded chart receipts to a trace
  gallery><label|sec:ex-lattice-firesky-gallery>

  This section records the actual selected-run route from one-scale chart
  data to the scale-dependent charts used by the trace calibration. The
  analytic chart receipts are provided by the funded construction and are
  identified below. We prove the subsequent scale selection and
  finite-dimensional limit steps explicitly. The correction and Ward-flux
  calculations are in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-producer>.

  <subsubsection|The funded chart family and its one-step output>

  Fix a grade <math|d>, a binding, a gauge-invariant chart budget, and a
  scale parameter <math|z\<geqslant\>0>. The funded chart receipt consists of
  three panels <math|P*<around|(|t,i|)>>, <math|i=0,1,2>, for
  <math|0\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>1>, with arc coordinates
  <math|b<rsub|i>+t>. Their currency and angular parameters are constant in
  <math|t>, and the three base arcs obey the finite separation inequalities
  for the probe count at grade <math|d>. At every sufficiently large level
  <math|k> and every <math|t\<in\><around|[|0,1|]>>, the receipt supplies a
  finite-volume chart witness at that level and offset. The other fields of
  this witness are the chart, invoice, legacy-cost, actuation, and stamp data
  used by the quantitative chart estimate. The single offset changes only the
  three arc coordinates.

  The simultaneous choice needed here is stronger than choosing a funded run
  separately at every grade. We give its construction next. The local
  analytic inputs are stated separately, so that the choice of a common
  schedule does not hide an estimate.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|A quantitative, signed-equivariant
    reading><label|lem:trace-quantitative-invoice>Keep the funded hypotheses
    and notation of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:rank-selected-tested-readout>.
    At each fixed grade <math|d>, its reading can be chosen so that, for all
    sufficiently large levels, uniformly in <math|N,p,u>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:trace-quantitative-invoice><around|\||A*u-T*u|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1/2>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>,<space|2em>H=\<cal-M\><rsub|k>.
    </equation>

    Assume that the calibrated map <math|A> intertwines the signed coordinate
    symmetries of its label space. The reading can then also be chosen to
    intertwine those symmetries, preserving its exact unit and real
    structure.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:rank-growing-wick-pin> gives,
    before discarding its rate,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||A*u-T*u|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|d>*<around|{|H<rsup|-1>+D*2<rsup|D>*H<rsup|-3/4>+w<rsub|D>*H<rsup|-1+\<alpha\>*<around|(|D+1|)>>|}>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>,<space|2em>\<alpha\>=<frac|1|4*<around|(|D+2|)>>.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|-1+\<alpha\>*<around|(|D+1|)>\<less\>-3/4>, this is at most
    <math|C<rsub|d>*H<rsup|-3/4>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>>, with
    <math|C<rsub|d>=c<rsub|d>*<around|(|1+D*2<rsup|D>+w<rsub|D>|)>>, for
    <math|H\<geqslant\>1>. Thus it is at most
    <math|H<rsup|-1/2>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>> once
    <math|H<rsup|1/4>\<geqslant\>C<rsub|d>>.

    For the symmetry assertion, let <math|\<Sigma\>> be the finite signed
    coordinate group, with representation <math|\<rho\>> on the
    grade-<math|d> label space, and put <math|a<rsub|d>=max
    <around|(|1,max<rsub|\<sigma\>\<in\>\<Sigma\>><around|\<\|\|\>|\<rho\><around|(|\<sigma\>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1\<to\>1>|)>>.
    Set

    <\equation*>
      <wide|T|\<bar\>>=<frac|1|<around|\||\<Sigma\>|\|>>*<big|sum><rsub|\<sigma\>\<in\>\<Sigma\>>\<rho\><around|(|\<sigma\>|)>*T*\<rho\><around|(|\<sigma\>|)><rsup|-1>.
    </equation*>

    Reindexing this finite sum proves equivariance. Since
    <math|A*\<rho\><around|(|\<sigma\>|)>=\<rho\><around|(|\<sigma\>|)>*A>,
    each summand's error on <math|u> is bounded by
    <math|a<rsub|d><rsup|2>*C<rsub|d>*H<rsup|-3/4>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>>.
    The triangle inequality gives the same bound for the average. Enlarging
    the common threshold until <math|H<rsup|1/4>\<geqslant\>a<rsub|d><rsup|2>*C<rsub|d>>
    proves <eqref|eq:trace-quantitative-invoice> for <math|<wide|T|\<bar\>>>.
    Every summand fixes the unit, and signed coordinate actions commute with
    complex conjugation, so these identities survive the average. The finite
    group and constants depend only on the grade, not on <math|k,N,p>. Hence
    taking the maximum for <math|0\<leqslant\>d<rprime|'>\<leqslant\>d> gives
    one finite-cutoff threshold.
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|Paying and averaging the complete stamp.> The symmetry of the
  covariance used here is a property of the actual finite-volume covariance,
  not a consequence of a bound on its entries. We verify it before averaging.
  On the finite torus let <math|Q> be the quadratic form of the selected
  one-form comparison, let <math|d> be the discrete curl and let <math|P> be
  its chosen solution operator, so that

  <\equation*>
    Q*P=d<rsup|\<ast\>>,<space|2em>ker Q\<subseteq\>ker
    d,<space|2em>\<kappa\>=d*P.
  </equation*>

  These are the finite comparison identities underlying the actual curvature
  multiplier in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-covariance-fourier-window>.
  For a signed coordinate change <math|s>, denote its orthogonal actions on
  bonds and oriented faces by <math|B<rsub|s>,C<rsub|s>>. The face action
  includes the shift of the base point when an axis is reversed. The defining
  quadratic form satisfies <math|B<rsub|s><rsup|\<ast\>>*Q*B<rsub|s>=Q> and
  <math|d*B<rsub|s>=C<rsub|s>*d>. Indeed the bond and face actions are signed
  permutations; change the summation variables in the quadratic form. The
  derivative terms retain their orientation signs, while the centred block
  averages and their adjoints are carried to the same averages. The scalar
  penalty is unchanged. These statements persist through the finite ancestry
  by induction, applying the same identity to each composed block map.

  Consequently <math|Q*<around|(|B<rsub|s>*P*C<rsub|s><rsup|\<ast\>>|)>=d<rsup|\<ast\>>>.
  Subtracting <math|Q*P=d<rsup|\<ast\>>> shows that each column of
  <math|B<rsub|s>*P*C<rsub|s><rsup|\<ast\>>-P> lies in <math|ker Q>, so
  application of <math|d> annihilates it. Hence

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-stamp-covariance-symmetry>C<rsub|s>*\<kappa\>*C<rsub|s><rsup|\<ast\>>=\<kappa\>.
  </equation>

  This argument does not require an inverse on the harmonic sector. The
  actual atlas uses precisely this finite curvature covariance, with the same
  ancestry and period. Restrict its two arguments to all oriented faces
  contained in the centred patch box. This face set is stable under the
  signed face permutations, including their base-point shifts; an arbitrary
  cube of positive-face base points would not suffice. In patch coordinates
  <math|C<rsub|s>> is a signed permutation <math|P<rsub|s>>, and the
  restricted matrix <math|K> therefore satisfies
  <math|P<rsub|s>*K*P<rsub|s><rsup|\<ast\>>=K>.

  Let <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|s>> denote the corresponding substitution on
  curvature polynomials. Each variable goes to one variable with sign
  <math|1> or <math|-1>. Thus it preserves degree and does not increase the
  absolute coefficient sum. The chain rule and
  <eqref|eq:trace-stamp-covariance-symmetry> give

  <\equation*>
    \<Delta\><rsub|K>*\<cal-E\><rsub|s>=\<cal-E\><rsub|s>*\<Delta\><rsub|K>,<space|2em>\<cal-W\><rsub|K>*\<cal-E\><rsub|s>=\<cal-E\><rsub|s>*\<cal-W\><rsub|K>.
  </equation*>

  The second equality follows by summing the finite exponential series on
  polynomials of bounded degree. Embedding the patch into the larger reader
  chart only renames variables; restricting <math|K> before this embedding
  gives the same identity. The radial reading also intertwines these
  substitutions with the signed action on reference labels: evaluate it on a
  formal connection jet, change the coordinates of the jet by <math|s>, and
  change variables along each radial segment. Curvature components acquire
  exactly the signs in <math|P<rsub|s>>. Extracting a fixed weighted degree
  commutes with this change of variables. Equality on all formal jets is
  equality of the resulting label polynomials.

  The corresponding physical identities use the same calibrated family. Write
  <math|A> for its calibrated coordinate and
  <math|F<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|exp>>> for its physical exponential entry.
  Its terminal label identification commutes with the signed action. This
  property passes to its transported coordinates: apply the signed pullback
  to the defining transfer integral and change the integration variables; the
  kernel and the selected representation are carried to themselves, and the
  terminal label is changed by <math|\<rho\><around|(|s|)>>. Induction
  through the finitely many transfers gives the coordinate and density
  identities. On a completely healthy configuration the flagged terms vanish
  and the background density is strictly positive. Dividing the density
  identity by that same invariant background gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-stamp-physical-symmetry>A*\<rho\><around|(|s|)>=\<rho\><around|(|s|)>*A,<space|2em>F<rsub|\<rho\><around|(|s|)>*u><rsup|<math-up|exp>><around|(|U|)>=F<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|exp>>*<around|(|s<rsup|\<ast\>>*U|)>.
  </equation>

  Thus the equivariance required in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:trace-quantitative-invoice>
  holds for this family; no equivariance of an arbitrary choice of atlas or
  calibrated map is being asserted.

  We now retain the strict margins in the selected-family construction of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-rank-purchases>. For fixed polynomial
  order <math|D>, set <math|\<gamma\>=1/<around|(|16*<around|(|D+2|)>|)>>.
  Its Taylor proof gives <math|<around|\||L<rsub|u>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>4*H<rsup|2*\<gamma\>>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>>,
  and the axial substitution defining the raw polynomial has row cost at most
  one eventually. Hence <math|<around|\||W<rsub|u>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>4*H<rsup|2*\<gamma\>>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>>.
  For the Wick polynomial one can use the stronger coefficient estimate
  already proved in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:rank-growing-wick-pin>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<cal-H\>*u|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+H<rsup|-1>*w<rsub|D>|)>*H<rsup|\<alpha\>>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>,<space|2em>\<alpha\>=1/<around|(|4*<around|(|D+2|)>|)>\<less\>1.
  </equation*>

  The raw healthy error, before its margin is discarded in
  <eqref|eq:rank-raw-terminal-paid>, is at most
  <math|C<rsub|D>*H<rsup|-D-15/16>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>>, after
  increasing the common threshold; the remaining terms and the wide tail are
  smaller than every fixed negative power of <math|H>. These are uniform
  bounds in the volume and period at a fixed grade and order.

  For <math|s\<in\>\<Sigma\>> construct

  <\equation*>
    W<rsup|s><rsub|u>=\<cal-E\><rsub|s>*W<rsub|\<rho\><around|(|s|)><rsup|-1>*u>,<space|2em>\<cal-H\><rsup|s><rsub|u>=\<cal-W\><rsub|K>*W<rsup|s><rsub|u>,<space|2em>T<rsup|s>=\<rho\><around|(|s|)>*T*\<rho\><around|(|s|)><rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  The covariance and radial identities above prove that <math|T<rsup|s>> is
  the reading of this very <math|\<cal-H\><rsup|s>>, with the same coupling
  dilation as before. Let <math|a<rsub|d>> be the finite orbit bound used in
  the invoice lemma. By <eqref|eq:trace-stamp-physical-symmetry>,
  <math|<around|\||A*\<rho\><around|(|s|)><rsup|-1>*u|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|d>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>>.
  Therefore the raw and Wick coefficient bounds for every conjugate are at
  most respectively <math|4*a<rsub|d>*H<rsup|2*\<gamma\>>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>>
  and <math|a<rsub|d>*<around|(|1+H<rsup|-1>*w<rsub|D>|)>*H<rsup|\<alpha\>>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>>.
  Since <math|2*\<gamma\>,\<alpha\>\<less\>1>, both are at most
  <math|H*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>> on one later tail.

  For the healthy ticket, signed pullback preserves the healthy set and the
  centred halo. The second identity in <eqref|eq:trace-stamp-physical-symmetry>
  was proved only for globally healthy configurations, so a local extension
  is needed. Suppose <math|U> is healthy at the smaller threshold
  <math|\<tau\>> on the patch. Put it in the axial gauge of
  <eqref|eq:rank-axial-terminal-error>; each link used by the patch then has
  chord at most <math|4*b*\<tau\>>. Retain those links and set every other
  link equal to the identity, obtaining <math|Z>. Every plaquette of
  <math|Z>, including those crossing the boundary of the retained links, is a
  product of at most four such links and inverses. The triangle inequality
  for the bi-invariant chord therefore bounds its chord by
  <math|16*b*\<tau\>>. This is at most the background threshold
  <math|\<theta\>> eventually, since <math|b=H<rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>> and
  <math|\<tau\>/\<theta\>=H<rsup|-1+o<around|(|1|)>>>. Hence <math|Z> and
  <math|s<rsup|\<ast\>>*Z> are globally <math|\<theta\>>-healthy. The near
  insertion and the raw polynomial only read links retained in the patch, and
  are gauge invariant, so replacing <math|U> by <math|Z> changes neither of
  their values.

  Apply the raw healthy approximation at <math|s<rsup|\<ast\>>*Z>, then the
  full-entry identity at <math|Z>. The discrepancy is the sum of four terms:
  the raw approximation error at <math|s<rsup|\<ast\>>*Z>, the change of
  based paths between that raw evaluation and the signed polynomial at
  <math|Z>, and the two wide tails at <math|Z> and <math|s<rsup|\<ast\>>*Z>.
  This decomposition does not assert that the near/wide split itself is
  equivariant. The wide tails on globally healthy fields have the
  superpolynomially small bounds established in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-rank-purchases>.

  Here is the additional path error. In the axial gauge each retained link is
  <math|O<around|(|b*\<tau\>|)>> from the identity. The two based paths for a
  signed image have length <math|O<around|(|b|)>>, so their connecting
  holonomy is <math|O<around|(|b<rsup|2>*\<tau\>|)>> from the identity.
  Conjugating a plaquette curvature of size <math|O<around|(|\<tau\>|)>>
  changes it by <math|O<around|(|b<rsup|2>*\<tau\><rsup|2>|)>>, by the
  differential of the adjoint action near the identity. Reversing a plaquette
  gives its negative, with at most another quadratic error for chord
  coordinates. Thus the coordinate discrepancy after division by <math|g> is
  at most <math|C*b<rsup|2>*\<tau\><rsup|2>/g>, while both coordinate vectors
  have norm at most <math|C*\<tau\>/g>. For <math|D=0> the polynomial error
  is zero. For <math|D\<geqslant\>1>, telescoping each monomial bounds the
  polynomial error by

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|D><around|\||W<rsub|\<rho\><around|(|s|)><rsup|-1>*u>|\|><rsub|1>*<around|(|1+\<tau\>/g|)><rsup|D-1>*b<rsup|2>*\<tau\><rsup|2>/g.
  </equation*>

  Now <math|\<tau\>/g=H<rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>> and
  <math|\<tau\><rsup|2>/g=x<rsup|-1/2>*H<rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>>, uniformly by
  the comparison of the actual coupling with
  <math|x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>. Since <math|log H=<around|(|log log
  x|)><rsup|2>=o<around|(|log x|)>>, this error is smaller than every fixed
  negative power of <math|H>. Combining the four terms gives, for one
  constant <math|C<rsub|d,D>>,

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||F<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|near>><around|(|U|)>-W<rsup|s><rsub|u>*<around|(|g<rsup|-1>*\<cal-C\><around|(|U|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|d,D>*H<rsup|-D-15/16>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-D>*<around|\||A*u|\|><rsub|1>
  </equation*>

  eventually, for every <math|s> and every healthy <math|U> in the prescribed
  halo. The last inequality follows by imposing
  <math|H<rsup|15/16>\<geqslant\>C<rsub|d,D>>. This pays the ticket
  separately from the invoice's <math|H<rsup|1/4>> margin. Support, locality,
  gauge invariance and measurability are preserved by these finite coordinate
  substitutions; the halo radius is unchanged.

  Finally set

  <\equation*>
    <wide|W|\<bar\>>=<frac|1|<around|\||\<Sigma\>|\|>>*<big|sum><rsub|s>W<rsup|s>,<space|2em><wide|\<cal-H\>|\<bar\>>=<frac|1|<around|\||\<Sigma\>|\|>>*<big|sum><rsub|s>\<cal-H\><rsup|s>,<space|2em><wide|T|\<bar\>>=<frac|1|<around|\||\<Sigma\>|\|>>*<big|sum><rsub|s>T<rsup|s>.
  </equation*>

  Linearity of Wick transformation and reading identifies the latter two maps
  with the Wick polynomial and reading of <math|<wide|W|\<bar\>>>. Degree
  does not increase. The triangle inequality and normalized averaging
  preserve each of the raw, Wick and healthy-ticket bounds just proved; there
  is no factor <math|<around|\||\<Sigma\>|\|>>. Unit and reality hold
  termwise, and reindexing the group proves signed covariance of
  <math|<wide|T|\<bar\>>>. Together with Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:trace-quantitative-invoice>,
  this is one complete signed stamp with the quantitative invoice, at the
  original prices. All the finite constants are paid by one later threshold
  at fixed grade and order, before the volume, period or panel offset is
  chosen.

  <subparagraph|Paying the finite moment and cumulant operations.> The
  numerical portion of the local chart purchase can be made before the
  coupling and the finite volume are chosen. We give the argument, including
  its order of choices. Put

  <\equation*>
    H<around|(|s|)>=exp <around|(|<around|(|log log
    s|)><rsup|2>|)>,<space|2em>T<around|(|s|)>=3*exp <around|(|<around|(|log
    log s|)><rsup|3/2>|)>.
  </equation*>

  For every fixed <math|q\<geqslant\>0> and <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-submonitor><around|(|1+<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    s|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>|)><rsup|q>+T<around|(|s|)><rsup|q>=o<around|(|H<around|(|s|)><rsup|\<epsilon\>>|)>.
  </equation>

  Indeed the logarithms of the two terms are respectively
  <math|O<around|(|log log s|)>> and <math|O<around|(|<around|(|log log
  s|)><rsup|3/2>|)>>, whereas <math|log H<around|(|s|)>=<around|(|log log
  s|)><rsup|2>>. This also holds uniformly with <math|s> replaced by
  <math|x\<in\><around|[|s/2,s|]>>, by monotonicity on a sufficiently late
  tail.

  Fix an arity cap <math|u\<geqslant\>2>, grade cap <math|d>, and finitely
  many base arcs <math|b<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>d+6>. Set
  <math|c=max<rsub|i><around|(|b<rsub|i>+1|)>> and <math|z=c*d+d+2>. Assign
  to a moment word of length <math|n\<leqslant\>u> the numerical coefficient
  allowance <math|H<rsup|z*n+1>>. This is a price used in the choice of
  parameters, not an assertion about the moment of an arbitrary field. The
  passage between moments and cumulants is a finite polynomial operation in
  these moment coefficients: the coefficients are the fixed integer
  coefficients in the partition formulas, and their absolute values give
  nonnegative polynomial majorants. Thus every moment/cumulant coefficient
  price at this fixed arity has a bound <math|C<rsub|n>*H<rsup|a<rsub|n>>>,
  for some explicitly finite constants <math|C<rsub|n>,a<rsub|n>> depending
  on <math|z,n>. For example, the cumulant polynomial is

  <\equation*>
    \<kappa\><rsub|I>=<big|sum><rsub|\<pi\>\<in\>\<cal-P\><around|(|I|)>><around|(|-1|)><rsup|<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>-1>*<around|(|<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>-1|)>!<big|prod><rsub|B\<in\>\<pi\>>m<rsub|B>;
  </equation*>

  the corresponding absolute majorant is obtained by replacing each
  coefficient by its absolute value. Substitution into another such formula
  again gives a finite polynomial. No estimate here depends on the number of
  lattice sites.

  Choose a negative currency <math|f> such that

  <\equation*>
    f\<leqslant\>-8-z,<space|2em>a<rsub|n>+f+1\<less\>-4*<space|1em><around|(|1\<leqslant\>n\<leqslant\>u|)>.
  </equation*>

  This is a finite list of strict inequalities. Put <math|U=1/16-f\<gtr\>0>,
  choose an integer <math|g\<geqslant\>u*U+10>, and take Taylor order
  <math|D=g+d+5>. The degree and arity are now fixed before selecting the
  accuracy exponent below. The additional coefficient norms introduced by
  evaluation on a bounded cube, multiplication, truncation and Wick ordering
  are finite polynomial expressions in <math|H>, the above moment prices and
  <math|T<around|(|s|)>>. To see this directly, the price of a product is
  bounded by the product of the prices; evaluating a polynomial of degree
  <math|r> on a cube of radius <math|R> costs at most <math|max
  <around|(|1,R|)><rsup|r>> times its absolute coefficient sum. In a fixed
  degree, Wick ordering is a finite sum of contraction maps, so its
  coefficient bound is likewise a finite polynomial in the covariance bound.
  The finite moment and cumulant formulas introduce only finitely many such
  operations. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:trace-submonitor> therefore supplies
  bounds <math|C*H<rsup|A>> for all their prices, with <math|C,A> independent
  of <math|s>.

  The corresponding differences are linear in a common error bound
  <math|\<varepsilon\>> after enlarging those polynomial prices. In fact, for
  a product of <math|r> factors with absolute values at most <math|M> and
  factorwise differences at most <math|\<varepsilon\>>, the telescoping
  identity gives a difference at most <math|r*M<rsup|r-1>*\<varepsilon\>>.
  Apply this to each monomial in the fixed formulas and sum its absolute
  coefficient. For any three fixed finite families of these polynomial
  difference prices there are bounds

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|1>*H<rsup|A<rsub|1>>*\<varepsilon\>,<space|2em>C<rsub|2>*H<rsup|A<rsub|2>>*\<varepsilon\>,<space|2em>C<rsub|3>*H<rsup|A<rsub|3>>*\<varepsilon\>.
  </equation*>

  Choose an accuracy exponent <math|F\<gtr\>0> with <math|F\<gtr\>A<rsub|1>>,
  <math|F\<gtr\>A<rsub|2>+g> and <math|F\<gtr\>A<rsub|3>+3/2>, and use
  <math|\<varepsilon\>=H<rsup|-F>>. After increasing the coupling threshold,
  these three prices are at most <math|1>, <math|H<rsup|-g>> and
  <math|H<rsup|-3/2>>, respectively. Any fixed factors, including
  <math|2<rsup|n>> and the finite support-price conversion constants, are
  absorbed by the strict inequalities. Similarly the earlier currency pays
  the uncorrected polynomial price: the evaluation factor has growth at most
  <math|H>, so <math|C<rsub|n>*H<rsup|a<rsub|n>+f+1>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-4>>
  on a tail. These are choices of the numerical tolerances for the actual
  comparison estimates; they do not assert that an arbitrary field attains
  the chosen error.

  The arity and room inequalities can be paid simultaneously. Set

  <\equation*>
    M=<around|(|6*u+3|)>*<around|(|3+2*u|)>+30,<space|2em>Q=M+10.
  </equation*>

  Then <math|6*n+3\<leqslant\>M/<around|(|3+2*n|)>> for
  <math|n\<leqslant\>u>, <math|M\<geqslant\>10>, <math|M+10\<leqslant\>Q>,
  and <math|1-g\<leqslant\>-u*U-2>. Taking the block length at least
  <math|128*Q> pays the remaining fixed geometric floor. If
  <math|A\<geqslant\>1> is any of the finitely many polynomial transport
  multipliers, also require <math|2*log A*<space|0.17em><around|(|b<rsub|i>+2|)>\<leqslant\>log
  L>. With

  <\equation*>
    h=<around*|\<lceil\>|<frac|<around|(|b<rsub|i>+2|)>*log
    H<around|(|s|)>|log L>|\<rceil\>>
  </equation*>

  we have <math|h*log A\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>log H<around|(|s|)>+log
  A\<leqslant\>log H<around|(|s|)>> eventually, hence
  <math|A<rsup|h>\<leqslant\>H<around|(|s|)>>. Taking a maximum over the
  finite arities and panels gives one block floor and one coupling threshold.
  The covariance/error estimates themselves must be applied with these very
  numerical choices.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Fixed-stage exponential budgets><label|lem:trace-stage-exponential-budgets>Fix
    a block side <math|L>, an arity cap <math|u>, a goal
    <math|g\<geqslant\>6>, and two nonnegative support exponents
    <math|e<rsub|0>,e<rsub|1>>. Write <math|N<rsub|L>> for the number of
    sites in one block (not the number of sites in the ambient torus), and
    set

    <\equation*>
      C<around|(|x|)>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
      x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>,<space|1em>B<around|(|x|)>=C<around|(|x|)><rsup|2>,<space|1em>Z<around|(|B|)>=<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>,<space|1em>W<around|(|B|)>=c<rsub|\<ast\>>*B<rsup|4>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|c<rsub|\<ast\>>\<geqslant\>0> is fixed. Put

    <\equation*>
      a<rsub|i>=exp <around|(|15<rsup|4>*<around|(|e<rsub|i>+7|)>|)>,<space|1em>\<alpha\>\<geqslant\>8*max
      <around|(|a<rsub|0>,a<rsub|1>|)>,<space|1em>\<kappa\>=H<around|(|s|)><rsup|-1/8>,<space|1em>p=H<around|(|s|)><rsup|-3>,<space|1em>\<tau\>=H<around|(|s|)><rsup|-2*g-10>.
    </equation*>

    For fixed <math|a\<gtr\>0>, put <math|v<around|(|x|)>=exp
    <around|(|-a*<around|(|T<around|(|x|)>/3|)><rsup|2>|)>> and

    <\equation*>
      \<ell\><around|(|k|)>=max <around*|(|1,<frac|p|\<alpha\>*k+H<around|(|s|)><rsup|-g>>|)>,<space|2em>m<around|(|k|)>=a<rsub|1>*\<ell\><around|(|k|)>*k+2*a<rsub|1>*H<around|(|s|)><rsup|-4>.
    </equation*>

    All the following inequalities hold uniformly for
    <math|x,y\<in\><around|[|s/2,s|]>>, <math|n\<leqslant\>u>, on a
    sufficiently late tail. Here <math|B=B<around|(|x|)>>.

    <\enumerate>
      <item>The five logarithmic expansion tests hold: with
      <math|h=e<rsup|\<kappa\>>-1> and <math|f=e<rsup|2*h>-1>,

      <\equation*>
        Z<around|(|B|)>*\<kappa\>,Z<around|(|B|)>*h,Z<around|(|B|)>*<around|(|2*h|)>,Z<around|(|B|)>*<around|(|f+\<kappa\>|)>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>e<rsup|f+\<kappa\>>-1\<leqslant\>1.
      </equation*>

      <item>For <math|0\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-4>>,

      <\equation*>
        <frac|\<kappa\>|16>+W<around|(|B|)>*N<rsub|L>*a<rsub|1>*v<around|(|y|)>+W<around|(|B|)>*n*m<around|(|k|)>\<leqslant\><frac|\<kappa\>|4>,<space|2em>a<rsub|1>*v<around|(|y|)>+m<around|(|k|)>\<leqslant\>p.
      </equation*>

      For <math|0\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1/2>> and any
      <math|0\<leqslant\>w\<leqslant\>1>,

      <\equation*>
        <frac|\<kappa\>|16>+W<around|(|B|)>*N<rsub|L>*a<rsub|0>*k*w+W<around|(|B|)>*n*a<rsub|0>*k\<leqslant\><frac|\<kappa\>|4>,<space|2em>a<rsub|0>*k*w+a<rsub|0>*k\<leqslant\><frac|\<alpha\>*k+H<rsup|1/2>*k<rsup|2>|2>.
      </equation*>

      Moreover <math|Z<around|(|B|)>*\<kappa\>/4\<leqslant\>1>,
      <math|e<rsup|\<kappa\>/4>-1+\<kappa\>/4\<leqslant\>\<kappa\>>, and
      <math|\<ell\><around|(|0|)>/x\<leqslant\>1>.

      <item>The normalization-hole budgets hold:

      <\gather*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|W<around|(|B|)>*N<rsub|L>*exp
        <around|(|15<rsup|4>*<around|(|e<rsub|1>+8|)>|)>*v<around|(|y|)>\<leqslant\>\<tau\>,<space|2em>2*Z<around|(|B|)>*\<tau\>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>16*Z<around|(|B|)>*\<tau\>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>16*\<tau\>\<leqslant\>1,>>|<row|<cell|e<rsup|2*\<tau\>>-1\<leqslant\>1-e<rsup|-16*\<tau\>>,<space|2em>32*\<ell\><around|(|0|)>*\<tau\>\<leqslant\>\<kappa\>/4,<space|2em>e<rsup|2*\<tau\>>-1+\<kappa\>/4\<leqslant\>\<kappa\>,<space|2em>\<tau\>\<leqslant\>\<kappa\>/16.>>>>
      </gather*>
    </enumerate>

    The block floors may additionally be chosen so that
    <math|10\<cdot\>15<rsup|4>*<around|(|e<rsub|i>+7|)>\<leqslant\>L*e<rsub|i>>
    whenever <math|e<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0>; these are fixed inequalities,
    independent of <math|s> and the torus. For the normalization-hole test
    also reserve a positive input support exponent
    <math|a<rsub|<math-up|in>>> with <math|10\<cdot\>15<rsup|4>*<around|(|e<rsub|1>+8|)>\<leqslant\>L*a<rsub|<math-up|in>>>,
    and an output exponent <math|a<rsub|<math-up|out>>\<leqslant\>e<rsub|1>+1>.
    These are the additional fixed support requirements of that test.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Every fixed power of <math|B<around|(|x|)>> is
    <math|o<around|(|H<around|(|s|)><rsup|\<epsilon\>>|)>> by
    <eqref|eq:trace-submonitor>. Also <math|v<around|(|y|)>=o<around|(|H<around|(|s|)><rsup|-A>|)>>
    for every fixed <math|A>: the negative logarithm of
    <math|v<around|(|y|)>> is <math|a*exp <around|(|2<around|(|log log
    y|)><rsup|3/2>|)>>, whose ratio to <math|<around|(|log log s|)><rsup|2>>
    tends uniformly to infinity on the half-band. Thus each fixed multiple of
    <math|W<around|(|B|)>*H<rsup|-1/2>> is eventually at most
    <math|\<kappa\>/16>. These statements allow all constants here, including
    <math|N<rsub|L>>, to be fixed before the coupling threshold.

    For item 1 choose the threshold so that <math|\<kappa\>\<leqslant\>1/50>
    and <math|Z<around|(|B|)>*\<kappa\>\<leqslant\>1/100>. The inequality
    <math|e<rsup|t>-1\<leqslant\>2*t> for <math|0\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>1>
    gives <math|h\<leqslant\>2*\<kappa\>>,
    <math|f\<leqslant\>4*h\<leqslant\>8*\<kappa\>> and
    <math|e<rsup|f+\<kappa\>>-1\<leqslant\>18*\<kappa\>\<leqslant\>1>. Each
    of the four products is at most <math|9*Z<around|(|B|)>*\<kappa\>\<leqslant\>1>.

    For item 2, <math|8*a<rsub|1>*\<ell\><around|(|k|)>*k\<leqslant\>p>.
    Indeed, if <math|\<ell\><around|(|k|)>\<gtr\>1>, use
    <math|8*a<rsub|1>*k\<leqslant\>\<alpha\>*k\<leqslant\>\<alpha\>*k+H<rsup|-g>>;
    if <math|\<ell\><around|(|k|)>=1>, use
    <math|8*a<rsub|1>*H<rsup|-4>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-3>>. Increasing the
    threshold also gives <math|8*a<rsub|1>*H<rsup|-4>\<leqslant\>p>, so
    <math|m<around|(|k|)>\<leqslant\>3*p/8\<leqslant\>p/2>. Likewise
    <math|a<rsub|1>*v<around|(|y|)>\<leqslant\>p/2>. Both
    <math|v<around|(|y|)>> and <math|m<around|(|k|)>> are at most
    <math|H<rsup|-1/2>> eventually. Hence each of the two
    <math|W<around|(|B|)>> terms in the first inequality is at most
    <math|\<kappa\>/16>; their sum with <math|\<kappa\>/16> is at most
    <math|3*\<kappa\>/16\<leqslant\>\<kappa\>/4>. The same reasoning applies
    to <math|k*w\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1/2>> and
    <math|a<rsub|0>*k>. Its second inequality follows from
    <math|2*a<rsub|0>*k\<leqslant\>\<alpha\>*k/2> and
    <math|H<rsup|1/2>*k<rsup|2>\<geqslant\>0>. The remaining exponential
    bound follows from <math|e<rsup|\<kappa\>/4>-1\<leqslant\>\<kappa\>/2>.
    Finally <math|\<ell\><around|(|0|)>=H<rsup|g-3>>, and any fixed power of
    <math|H<around|(|s|)>> is <math|o<around|(|s|)>>, which proves
    <math|\<ell\><around|(|0|)>/x\<leqslant\>1> uniformly.

    For item 3 all the polynomial-radius inequalities follow from the first
    paragraph. In particular take <math|\<tau\>\<leqslant\>1/16> and
    <math|16*\<tau\>\<leqslant\>\<kappa\>>. Then
    <math|e<rsup|2*\<tau\>>-1\<leqslant\>4*\<tau\>>, while
    <math|e<rsup|-16*\<tau\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+16*\<tau\>|)><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>1-4*\<tau\>>;
    the last inequality is equivalent to <math|64*\<tau\><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>12*\<tau\>>.
    This proves the first exponential comparison, and also
    <math|e<rsup|2*\<tau\>>-1+\<kappa\>/4\<leqslant\>\<kappa\>/2\<leqslant\>\<kappa\>>.
    Since <math|\<ell\><around|(|0|)>*\<tau\>=H<rsup|-g-13>>, the patch
    budget follows by strict power comparison with <math|H<rsup|-1/8>>. The
    remaining inequality <math|\<tau\>\<leqslant\>\<kappa\>/16> follows the
    same way. Finally the stated block floors are finite because the positive
    exponents have already been fixed.
  </proof>

  The first item pays the smallness tests needed when taking the local marked
  logarithm. The second pays its two support budgets, respectively for the
  marked and the unmarked energy exponents. The third pays the
  normalization-hole budget on one actual block step. In particular none of
  these tests requires a smallness hypothesis on the total volume:
  <math|N<rsub|L>> counts a single fixed block. The accompanying geometric
  tests are equally uniform: <math|B<around|(|y|)>\<leqslant\>2*B<around|(|x|)>>
  on the half-band, since <math|B<around|(|s|)>/B*<around|(|s/2|)>\<to\>1>,
  and a microscopic radius bounded by <math|4*C<around|(|x|)>> satisfies
  <math|25*<around|(|4*C<around|(|x|)>|)>+30\<leqslant\>C<around|(|x|)><rsup|2>>
  eventually. Together with the fixed support-exponent inequalities these are
  precisely the radius, support and exponential tests used by the local
  transfer compiler. They do not supply that compiler's estimates on the
  actual amplitudes; those estimates must have the coefficients, covariance
  and normalization of the selected history.

  <subparagraph|Room and the actual restarted steps.> The period-independent
  room requirement is also a consequence of the paid clock. Let
  <math|t<rsub|i>> be its logarithmic scale, so the denominator of the
  lattice spacing is <math|e<rsup|t<rsub|i>>> and the clock coupling is
  <math|s<rsub|i>=\<beta\><rsub|\<ast\>>+s<rsub|G>*t<rsub|i>>. For
  sufficiently large <math|i> the retimed microscopic radius is at most
  <math|4*s<rsub|i>>. Therefore

  <\equation*>
    <frac|100*<around|(|1+4*s<rsub|i>|)>|e<rsup|t<rsub|i>>>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  Every physical period is a positive integer multiple of this denominator,
  so <math|100*<around|(|1+<math-up|radius><around|(|r<around|(|s<rsub|i>|)>|)>|)>>
  fits in every such torus. This is uniform over all period indices. It also
  holds simultaneously at every level in a late comparison window: if
  <math|s<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>s<rsub|k>/2> there, monotonicity and divergence
  of <math|s<rsub|k>> force all such <math|i> beyond any fixed threshold. No
  threshold depending on the period is required.

  The all-level representation needed for actuation is the actual one
  constructed in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:restart-actual-closure>; an
  arbitrary family with the same density identity would not suffice. To apply
  that proposition here, retain on a step the original fine and coarse paths,
  their clips, the original funded tube and the local chart. The stage pair
  is one of <math|<around|(|j,j|)>>, <math|<around|(|j,j-1|)>> and
  <math|<around|(|j-1,j-1|)>>. Its block length and support exponents are
  fixed before the threshold. The radius and exponential budgets proved in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:trace-stage-exponential-budgets>, together with
  the preceding torus room, pay the corresponding local tests at that pair.
  Intersect these three eventual assertions, and the existing path, metric
  and chart requests, before choosing the stage threshold.

  Here is the identification with the local compiler, which is essential when
  using its estimates. At the upper endpoint use the re-expression of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:restart-endpoint>, retaining its exact
  density, scalar and coupling. At the next step, the incoming representation
  is this very re-expression. Erase only its additive remainder to form the
  neutral representation used for pricing. Its path, clip, Gaussian law,
  forest, tag and warning sockets are unchanged; the corrected-warning
  argument of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-local-compiler-handoff>
  therefore applies to the same selected data. The corrected-body and mixed
  compilers choose one alphabet and its scalar loading before the output
  representation. The neutral return estimate and the genuine density
  identity are estimates for that one alphabet. Its positive loading fixes
  the new scalar; its connected masked logarithm fixes the new additive
  remainder. Thus there is no choice of an unrelated coarse representation at
  this point.

  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:restart-actual-closure> proves that the
  new representation satisfies the same inductive bounds: its neutral version
  has the inherited law, its forced allowance is <math|exp
  <around|(|-a<rsub|<math-up|coast>><math-up|hot><around|(|x|)><rsup|2>|)>>,
  and its additive allowance is <math|q<rsub|n+1>>. Its proof uses the actual
  path correction <eqref|eq:restart-path-price>, the inflated component
  estimates and reassembly on this corrected alphabet. The step's
  normalization-hole allowance is <math|\<delta\><rsub|n>=q<rsub|n>*A<rsub|s><rsup|3>>,
  not the preliminary allowance <math|\<tau\>>. The replacement is justified
  as follows. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:restart-decay> gives
  <math|s<rsup|-1/200>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|n>\<leqslant\>s<rsup|-1/400>\<leqslant\>\<tau\>>
  uniformly in the permitted step count. Since <math|exp
  <around|(|-a<math-up|hot><around|(|y|)><rsup|2>|)>=o<around|(|s<rsup|-A>|)>>
  for every fixed <math|A>, the first term in item 3 of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:trace-stage-exponential-budgets> is at most
  <math|s<rsup|-1/200>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|n>>. All its other upper
  bounds hold with <math|\<delta\><rsub|n>> in place of <math|\<tau\>>, by
  monotonicity, except the comparison between the two exponentials. That
  comparison follows directly from the same calculation for every
  <math|0\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|n>\<leqslant\>1/16>. Thus the
  normalization-hole estimates apply at the actual step cost; the two
  support-price weakenings at the end of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-local-compiler-handoff>
  preserve its values, full supports, loading and hole normalization. They
  therefore give the upper and lower marked transfer estimates on this
  particular step. Install this new representation only at its coarse level
  and continue backwards. Induction preserves all representations already
  installed, the endpoint stamps, the fixed coupling, and the original
  partition denominator. This supplies the actual short comparison family and
  its marked transfers, with the uniformity in fields, cutoff and period
  stated in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:restart-actual-closure>.

  This application uses the original funded path and chart data; it does not
  assume the desired estimates on the restarted representations. The
  finite-reader covariance comparison and its polynomial error prices are
  separate from this actuation argument. In particular, the parameter choices
  above are not a substitute for verifying those comparison estimates for the
  selected covariance.

  <subparagraph|Reader comparison across a profile change.> The plateau
  formulation in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-reader-dawn-selection>
  extends to each of the three adjacent profile pairs required here. This
  does not identify the two profiles. For a fine profile <math|f> and coarse
  profile <math|c>, use the common strip rate <math|min
  <around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|f>,\<alpha\><rsub|c>|)>\<gtr\>0> and upper price
  <math|max <around|(|\<Omega\><rsub|f>,\<Omega\><rsub|c>|)>>. Decreasing the
  exponential strip rate and increasing the majorant preserve each weighted
  row estimate. Keep the fine metric floor <math|\<eta\><rsub|f>> in the
  common-cover construction, and keep the coarse metric floor
  <math|\<eta\><rsub|c>> in the actual tower transfer. Both remain strictly
  positive; neither metric is replaced by the other. The retained tower, its
  crossing identity and the two actual clips identify the transferred
  covariance with the original coarse covariance, exactly as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-covariance-transport>.

  For completeness, the short-row comparison has the following two cases. At
  an address retained by the short-row rule the prescribed row is the actual
  conditional row, so its error is zero. At a discarded address the truncated
  common-cover row is zero. The cover-tail bound on the fine metric, followed
  by the actual tower transfer to the coarse metric, bounds the original row
  at that address by <math|C*e<rsup|-c*h<rsub|x>>>. The same argument on a
  transport row gives the mean error. The constants <math|C,c\<gtr\>0> now
  depend on the ordered pair of profiles. All periodic images are included in
  the discarded-address test, so there is no omitted wraparound term. The
  local Gaussian and guarded curvature estimates use these same paths, clips,
  tube, forest and tag; their coupling drift constant is the fixed one for
  this pair. Consequently the proof of <eqref|eq:reader-common-error-budget>
  applies without change and yields any prescribed error
  <math|H<around|(|s|)><rsup|-F>> on <math|<around|[|s/2,s|]>> for each pair.
  Take the maximum of the three thresholds, rather than restricting the
  comparison levels to plateau interiors.

  These are errors on one joint Gaussian model for the finite reader family,
  as in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-all-arity-reader-gaussian-model>.
  One can see directly that composition preserves this identification. For
  consecutive independent centered conditional noises <math|Z<rsub|i>> and
  actual linear transport maps <math|A<rsub|i>>, substitution of
  <math|X<rsub|i>=A<rsub|i>*X<rsub|i-1>+Z<rsub|i>> gives recursively

  <\equation*>
    \<mu\><rsub|i>=A<rsub|i>*\<mu\><rsub|i-1>,<space|2em>Q<rsub|i>=A<rsub|i>*Q<rsub|i-1>*A<rsub|i><rsup|\<ast\>>+<math-up|Cov><around|(|Z<rsub|i>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The tower transfer identity just used is the identity for these actual
  covariances. It holds step by step at a change of profile as well as inside
  a plateau. Each finite composition is therefore the joint law used in the
  polynomial integration, with all cross-covariances retained. No independent
  samples are chosen for the individual readers.

  The numerical polynomial errors can now be stated without an unnamed moment
  bound. Use the coefficient norm <math|<around|\||\<cdummy\>|\|><rsub|1>> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-cumulant-comparison>. For slot
  polynomials of degree at most <math|D>, coefficient bounds <math|A>, and
  calibrated tails of norm at most <math|\<xi\>>, its dimension-independent
  estimate is

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||\<cal-W\><rsub|Q,\<mu\>>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I>H<rsub|i>-\<cal-W\><rsub|<math-up|free>>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I>F<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|{|n*\<xi\>*<around|(|A+\<xi\>|)><rsup|n>+A<rsup|n>*\<varepsilon\>|}>*<around|[|2*B*<around|(|n*D+1|)>|]><rsup|n*D>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|B\<geqslant\>1> bounds all mean and covariance entries and
  <math|\<varepsilon\>> bounds the actual/free mean and covariance
  differences. The actual/short one-step difference uses exactly the same
  bound with <math|\<xi\>=0> and <math|\<varepsilon\>=H<rsup|-F>> from the
  preceding paragraph. For the coefficient polynomial of all subset moments,
  multiplication by <math|2<rsup|n>> bounds the coefficient norm of the
  difference. On a ball <math|<around|\||q-1|\|><rsub|1>,<around|\||q<rprime|'>-1|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>M>,
  taking its finite logarithm costs at most
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|a=1><rsup|n>M<rsup|a-1>>. These explicit bounds, and
  the product estimate <eqref|eq:allarity-vertex-product> for calibration,
  are the polynomial difference prices referred to above. All their
  parameters are fixed before choosing <math|F>. Their powers of <math|H> can
  therefore be absorbed by that choice, with the required strict margins. The
  low-grade calibration itself is the common selected reading, not an
  independent choice made in this estimate.

  The remaining radius and truncation tests also follow from strict power
  comparisons. At arity <math|n> and degree <math|D>, set
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|n,D>=1/<around|[|16*<around|(|n*D+D+1|)>|]>> and
  reserve the reader size <math|H<rsup|\<lambda\><rsub|n,D>>>. For any fixed
  halo <math|Q>,

  <\equation*>
    16*Q*B<around|(|x|)>*T<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<lambda\><rsub|n,D>>\<leqslant\>H,<space|2em>6*dim
    <around|(|\<frak-g\>|)>*<around|(|4*Q*B<around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|2><frac|T<around|(|x|)><rsup|2>|3*<sqrt|x>>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-F>
  </equation*>

  uniformly on the half-band, eventually. The first assertion is
  <eqref|eq:trace-submonitor>; the second follows by taking logarithms, since
  its left side is <math|x<rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>>. The truncation defect
  for a polynomial of coefficient norm <math|w>, degree <math|r>, entry bound
  <math|H> and entry error <math|\<varepsilon\>> is at most
  <math|w*r*H<rsup|r>*\<varepsilon\>>, by the same product telescoping
  calculation. For the linear and quadratic products of transported
  insertions, the coefficient discrepancies are bounded respectively by

  <\equation*>
    f<rsub|1>=H<rsup|z+2>*\<varepsilon\>,<space|1em>f<rsub|2>=H<rsup|2*z+2>*\<varepsilon\>,<space|1em>f<rsub|1>+f<rsub|2>+2*H<rsup|z+1>*f<rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed each insertion has coefficient allowance <math|H<rsup|z>>, its
  transport cost is at most <math|H>, and the difference of the two
  transports costs one further factor <math|H*\<varepsilon\>>. Telescope a
  product of two such insertions to obtain the last term. Multiplying these
  expressions by <math|<around|[|2*H*<around|(|2*D+1|)>|]><rsup|2*D>> and
  adding the truncation defect is another fixed power of <math|H> times
  <math|\<varepsilon\>>. Enlarge <math|F> so it is at most
  <math|H<rsup|-3/2>>. The analogous finite partition sums at arities
  <math|n\<leqslant\>u> are paid in exactly the same way using their
  displayed coefficient norm bounds; their degrees and numbers of terms are
  fixed before <math|F>. Finally, with <math|v=exp
  <around|(|-a<math-up|hot><around|(|x|)><rsup|2>|)>>,

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|a<math-up|hot><around|(|x|)><rsup|2>>*v<rsup|2>+C*H<rsup|-2>=v+C*H<rsup|-2>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1>
  </equation*>

  for every fixed support conversion constant <math|C> on a tail. Thus
  neither the Gaussian truncation tests nor the quadratic tests introduce a
  new analytic hypothesis after the actual reader comparison is supplied.

  There are also fixed covariance-transport and station prices. Let
  <math|A\<geqslant\>1> be the maximum of the transport multipliers of the
  three ordered profile pairs, and let <math|C<rsub|0>> bound their fixed
  low-order covariance and coefficient constants. Put
  <math|\<lambda\>=min<rsub|n\<leqslant\>u>
  1/<around|[|16*<around|(|n*D+D+1|)>|]>\<gtr\>0>. Strengthen the block floor
  to

  <\equation*>
    2*<around|(|b+2|)>*log A\<leqslant\>\<lambda\>*log L.
  </equation*>

  For the permitted comparison length <math|h\<leqslant\><around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|b+2|)>*log
  H/log L|\<rceil\>>>, this implies <math|2<rsup|d>*A<rsup|h>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<lambda\>>>
  eventually: its logarithm is at most <math|\<lambda\>*log H/2+log A+d*log
  2>. Also <math|C<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<lambda\>>> and
  <math|C<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|1/4>> eventually. These inequalities pay
  the finite train of covariance derivatives and its two endpoint bounds,
  with both profiles retained; they are not a new covariance identity. If the
  chosen window includes the additional <math|O<around|(|log log s|)>> cover
  margin, its contribution to <math|h*log A> is <math|o<around|(|log H|)>>
  and is absorbed by the same strict margin. The finite Haar station price
  <math|c<rsub|j>> is paid by <math|c<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>log
  H/<around|(|16*u|)>>, because <math|log H\<to\>\<infty\>>. Likewise every
  fixed profile column bound is at most <math|H<rsup|\<eta\>>> for its
  prescribed positive exponent <math|\<eta\>>. The profile-order condition
  <math|D+1\<leqslant\>p<rsub|j-1>> is kept explicitly in the active grade
  menu below.

  The remaining elementary readout inequalities use
  <math|1\<leqslant\>T\<leqslant\>H-1>, <math|<around|[|2*T*<around|(|2*D+1|)>|]><rsup|2*D>\<leqslant\>H>,
  <math|C*<around|[|2*T<around|(|2|)>|]>\<leqslant\>H> and
  <math|C+H<rsup|-F>\<leqslant\>H> for fixed <math|C>; all follow from
  <eqref|eq:trace-submonitor>. The number of comparison steps is
  <math|O<around|(|log H|)>>, hence at most <math|H>. For the quadratic
  coefficient allowance take <math|2*H<rsup|2*z+2>>, which dominates
  <math|H<rsup|2*z+1>+<around|(|H<rsup|z+1>|)><rsup|2>>. The remaining marked
  finite-log difference has the already proved polynomial price times
  <math|H<rsup|-F>>, and the support and gas prices are those above. This
  verifies the scalar readout tests together with the fixed covariance and
  station prices.

  The endpoint re-expression also has the following uniform preliminary
  budget. Put <math|\<rho\>=H/<sqrt|s>>, <math|\<phi\>=<math-up|hot><around|(|s|)>/<sqrt|s>>
  and <math|\<delta\>=s<rsup|-11/10>>. For fixed path rate
  <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0>, path majorant and support exponent, its
  lost-support allowance is bounded by a fixed multiple of

  <\equation*>
    W<around|(|B|)>*<around|{|s*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*B/4>+H*\<rho\><rsup|2>+s*<around|(|\<rho\><rsup|3>+\<rho\><rsup|4>|)>+\<delta\>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed the four summands are respectively the long-path tail, the quadratic
  loop term (whose coefficient is at most <math|2*H>), the cubic and quartic
  path terms, and the inherited additive term. Their fixed support factors
  are absorbed in the constant. This expression is
  <math|s<rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>>, hence at most <math|H<rsup|-A>> for
  every prescribed <math|A>, uniformly for physical couplings in
  <math|<around|[|s/2,2*s|]>>. Together with the already bounded forced and
  new-flag prices this pays the endpoint allowances at <math|H<rsup|-3>>, and
  makes their rooted sum times <math|Z<around|(|B|)>> less than one.

  The remaining endpoint conditions are direct: <math|\<rho\>\<leqslant\>1>,
  <math|2*\<phi\>\<leqslant\>\<rho\>>, <math|C\<leqslant\>B>,
  <math|B\<geqslant\>8>, <math|\<delta\>\<leqslant\>1> and
  <math|<math-up|hot><around|(|s|)>\<geqslant\>4> eventually. For the fixed
  clip relaxation constant <math|c\<gtr\>0>,
  <math|\<phi\>*e<rsup|B/c>\<geqslant\>2>, since <math|B> grows like
  <math|<around|(|log s|)><rsup|4>>. The fixed support conversion
  inequalities are paid by <math|B/C\<to\>\<infty\>>. Finally choose
  <math|0\<less\>a<rsub|<math-up|coast>>\<leqslant\><math-up|linear><around|(|<math-up|clip>|)>/20>.
  For <math|\<beta\>\<geqslant\>s/2> this gives the required tail-energy
  payment

  <\equation*>
    2*a<rsub|<math-up|coast>><math-up|hot><around|(|s|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><frac|\<beta\><math-up|linear><around|(|<math-up|clip>|)>|5>*\<phi\><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  These statements verify the preliminary endpoint budget for the actual
  re-expression, before using its marked change of weights.

  <subparagraph|Changing the marked budget at the endpoint.> We spell out the
  old-to-new marked support payment as well. Let the old budget radius be
  <math|C=C<around|(|s|)>>, the new radius be <math|B=C<rsup|2>>, and let
  <math|\<theta\>\<gtr\>0> be the original support exponent. Fix
  <math|e\<geqslant\>\<theta\>>, an old marker radius
  <math|r<rsub|0>=s<rsup|-a/2>> with <math|a\<geqslant\>0>, a new radius
  <math|r=H<rsup|f>> with <math|f\<leqslant\>0>, and a desired suppression
  <math|\<Gamma\>=<around|(|g+8|)>*log H>. Put
  <math|u<rsub|0>=<around|(|a/2|)>*log s>. Then

  <\equation*>
    r\<leqslant\>1=e<rsup|u<rsub|0>>*r<rsub|0>,<space|2em>100000*C*<around|(|e+2|)>\<leqslant\>B*\<theta\>/2,<space|2em>64*<around|(|u<rsub|0>+\<Gamma\>+100000*<around|(|e+2|)>|)>\<leqslant\>C*\<theta\>/2
  </equation*>

  eventually. The last two follow from <math|C\<sim\><around|(|log
  s|)><rsup|2>> and <math|log H=o<around|(|log s|)>>. The same argument gives
  <math|100000*C*<space|0.17em>2*<around|(|e+2|)>\<leqslant\>B*\<theta\>>.

  Here is the resulting support inequality, rather than just the choice of
  its parameters. Suppose a source support <math|X> contains
  <math|m\<geqslant\>2> marked sites with pairwise lattice separation at
  least <math|C<rsup|2>> (the old budget's squared separation test). Write
  <math|N<rsub|C><around|(|X|)>> for its connected cover number at radius
  <math|C>. The separated-site counting bound gives
  <math|C*m\<leqslant\>64*N<rsub|C><around|(|X|)>>, and the cover enlargement
  bound gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|e+2|)>*N<rsub|B><around|(|X<rsup|+8>|)>\<leqslant\>100000*<around|(|e+2|)>+<frac|\<theta\>|2>*N<rsub|C><around|(|X|)>.
  </equation*>

  Using <math|m\<geqslant\>1>, the preceding three inequalities imply

  <\equation*>
    m*u<rsub|0>+<around|(|e+2|)>*N<rsub|B><around|(|X<rsup|+8>|)>\<leqslant\>-\<Gamma\>+\<theta\>*N<rsub|C><around|(|X|)>.
  </equation*>

  Exponentiating therefore proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-endpoint-marked-budget>r<rsup|m>*e<rsup|<around|(|e+2|)>*N<rsub|B><around|(|X<rsup|+8>|)>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<Gamma\>>*r<rsub|0><rsup|m>*e<rsup|\<theta\>*N<rsub|C><around|(|X|)>>.
  </equation>

  Multiplying by each nonnegative original coefficient majorant and summing
  at its original marked root preserves this inequality. Absolute summability
  is preserved by domination. Thus this is a change of weights on the actual
  source alphabet, not the selection of a different marked expansion. The
  separation test is precisely where the pair-support payment is used. In the
  chart application it is paid on the same chart census as follows. On a
  fixed compact set of distinct-point configurations, the minimum separation
  is a positive number <math|c>. For arcs at least <math|b\<gtr\>0>,
  convergence of the tested coordinates gives lattice separation at least
  <math|<around|(|c/2|)>*H<rsup|b>> eventually. Since
  <math|C<rsup|2>=o<around|(|H<rsup|b/2>|)>>, this exceeds <math|C<rsup|2>>;
  the periodic no-wrap room in the chart geometry gives the same bound for
  periodic distance, uniformly in the permitted periods. The original marked
  expansion is retained in the endpoint re-expression; this change of weights
  does not construct that expansion. One-letter terms retain their separately
  paid local ticket.

  We also record the resulting error prices. Set <math|y=H<rsup|-8>>,
  <math|E=e<rsup|100000*<around|(|e+2|)>>>, and
  <math|w=e<rsup|-H<rsup|1/5>>>. For a source halo <math|X> of radius
  <math|Q*B>, the cover numbers of <math|X> and <math|X<rsup|+8>> are at most
  <math|<around|(|2*Q+1|)><rsup|4>> and <math|<around|(|2*Q+3|)><rsup|4>>,
  respectively. Consequently their support weights are bounded by fixed
  constants <math|A<rsub|0>,A<rsub|1>>. Define

  <\equation*>
    d<rsub|s>=H<rsup|-g-8>+E*e<rsup|-\<theta\>*C/2>*H<rsup|-8>,<space|2em>t<rsub|s>=W<around|(|B|)>*<around|(|3*A<rsub|1>*H<rsup|-8>+d<rsub|s>|)>,
  </equation*>

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|m<rsub|s>>|<cell|=83521*e<rsup|100000*<around|(|e+1|)>>*w+W<around|(|B|)>*E*H<rsup|-8>*<sqrt|w>+W<around|(|B|)>*H<rsup|-g-8>,>>|<row|<cell|b<rsub|s>>|<cell|=<around|(|8*B+1|)><rsup|4>*e<rsup|-a<rsub|<math-up|coast>><math-up|hot><around|(|s|)><rsup|2>>*e<rsup|<around|(|e+1|)>*9<rsup|4>>.>>>>
  </align*>

  These are respectively the lost old-support price, the enlarged rooted
  price, the inherited forced price, and the new flag-site price. They are
  obtained by the marked weight change <eqref|eq:trace-endpoint-marked-budget>,
  the cover enlargement, and the endpoint re-expression; all use the original
  coefficient majorants. For every fixed <math|A\<gtr\>0>,
  <math|E*e<rsup|-\<theta\>*C/2>*H<rsup|-8>=o<around|(|H<rsup|-A>|)>>,
  <math|w=o<around|(|H<rsup|-A>|)>> and <math|b<rsub|s>=o<around|(|H<rsup|-A>|)>>.
  Also <math|W<around|(|B|)>=H<rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>>. Thus, for
  <math|D\<geqslant\>g+4>,

  <\equation*>
    H<rsup|-D-8>*A<rsub|0>+d<rsub|s>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-g>,<space|2em>2*H<rsup|-3>+t<rsub|s>+m<rsub|s>+b<rsub|s>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1>
  </equation*>

  on a common tail. The first follows from the strict gaps
  <math|-D-8\<less\>-g> and <math|-g-8\<less\>-g>; the second from
  <math|g\<geqslant\>6> and <math|-8\<less\>-1>. Finally
  <math|Z<around|(|B|)>*H<rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>1> and
  <math|e<rsup|H<rsup|-1>>-1\<leqslant\>1> follow from the same strict power
  comparison. This pays the endpoint's marked budget, in addition to its
  unmarked re-expression, uniformly in the ambient torus.

  <subparagraph|The original mixed-grade marked expansion.> The original
  marked expansion used in the endpoint change of weights is supplied on the
  same public family, not an additional input to the chart construction.
  Retain <math|C<rsub|R>>, its transfer family <math|S<rsub|R>> and its
  stored doors <math|D<rsub|R>>. Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-c02-selected-record>
  supplies their common service record. For the all-modes assertion used
  below, retain specifically the complete finite-menu push identity of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:finite-menu-history-handoff>, on the actual
  doors of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:selected-retention-public>. Its
  exponential and forced gas include every coefficient of the finite census
  algebra. Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-transfer> therefore applies
  to that entire polynomial; no all-modes identity is inferred from an
  identity of singletons alone. The path and tube data are those of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-selected-course-binding>; the
  original representations and their lawfulness are those retained in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:public-run-binding>.

  Fix a finite census of marked slots <math|V>, allowing different grades
  <math|d<rsub|a>> and labels <math|v<rsub|a>> at its slots. The census
  includes the prescribed signed copies and conjugates. Choose
  <math|k\<less\>N> with <math|t<rsub|R><around|(|d<rsub|a>|)>\<leqslant\>k>
  for all <math|a>. Let <math|o<rsub|i><around|(|a|)>> be the migrated
  lattice sites and <math|g<rsub|a>> their fixed motions. Require the census
  occupancy and separated-origin tests at <math|k>, and choose
  <math|x\<gtr\>0> such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-original-marked-payment>x<space|0.17em><math-up|paid><rsub|d<rsub|a>,k,N,p><around|(|v<rsub|a>|)>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|d<rsub|a>,k>*<space|2em><around|(|a\<in\>V|)>.
  </equation>

  These are the original chart census and its label payment, before the
  endpoint change of budgets. In particular they are not inferred from an
  estimate on the new raw polynomial.

  Let <math|q<rsub|N>> be the terminal birth polynomial: its coefficient at
  an allowed mode <math|I> is the terminal Wilson density times
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>I>O<rsub|v<rsub|a>><around|(|o<rsub|N><around|(|a|)>|)>>,
  with the indicated motions. Define <math|q<rsub|i>> by the actual Wilson
  pushes from <math|N> down to <math|i>. Its terminal coefficients are
  continuous on the compact configuration space, hence integrable; the
  selected transition kernels preserve slice integrability and the exact mode
  integrals. The terminal birth identity in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-selected-birth-receipt> identifies this
  <math|q<rsub|N>> with the polynomial made from the stored initial
  observables. Consequently its backward flow is exactly the polynomial of
  their original density insertions on <math|C<rsub|R>>.

  The service proof works for these mixed grades without replacing them by
  one common grade. For each slot <math|a> use its own transfer sequence from
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-music>, its own height bound and
  its own allowance <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d<rsub|a>,i>>. All these transfer
  sequence families have the same background representations and doors. Set
  <math|w<rsub|k><around|(|a|)>=x> and <math|w<rsub|i+1><around|(|a|)>=\<phi\><rsub|i>*w<rsub|i><around|(|a|)>>.
  The adjacent affordability inequality

  <\equation*>
    \<phi\><rsub|i>*L<rsub|i><rsup|d<rsub|a>>*\<Gamma\><rsub|i+1>*\<zeta\><rsub|d<rsub|a>,i>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|d<rsub|a>,i+1>
  </equation*>

  propagates <eqref|eq:trace-original-marked-payment> separately for each
  slot. The positive weights, occupancy and separation propagate by the
  common migration argument of Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-construction>.
  Thus the per-label response construction there applies to every <math|a>,
  with its own degree and the same transition at each step. Combine the
  resulting responses using the finite-census backward propagation. Its proof
  uses only these slotwise inequalities and the common support geometry, so
  it applies to this finite family of possibly different grades. At the
  opening level <math|k>, the marched weight is again <math|x>, and, since
  <math|k\<less\>N>, the propagated support exponent is the support exponent
  <math|\<theta\><rsub|k>> of the actual representation there. The resulting
  ordinary and forced multi-marker families have reserve one.

  Here is explicitly the local form of that conclusion. There are ordinary
  source terms <math|A<rsub|\<nu\>>> and forced source terms
  <math|K<rsub|\<lambda\>>>, with supports
  <math|X<rsub|\<nu\>>,Y<rsub|\<lambda\>>>, marker inventories
  <math|I<rsub|\<nu\>>,J<rsub|\<lambda\>>> of cardinality at least two, and
  nonnegative majorants <math|a<rsub|\<nu\>>,b<rsub|\<lambda\>>>, such that
  for every marked root <math|a>

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>:a\<in\>I<rsub|\<nu\>>>a<rsub|\<nu\>>*e<rsup|\<theta\><rsub|k>*N<rsub|B<rsub|k>><around|(|X<rsub|\<nu\>>|)>>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>:a\<in\>J<rsub|\<lambda\>>>b<rsub|\<lambda\>>*e<rsup|\<theta\><rsub|k>*N<rsub|B<rsub|k>><around|(|Y<rsub|\<lambda\>>|)>>\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  The majorants include the marker tolls <math|x<rsup|<around|\||I<rsub|\<nu\>>|\|>>>
  and <math|x<rsup|<around|\||J<rsub|\<lambda\>>|\|>>> in the corresponding
  healthy value or forced coefficient norm. These are sums at the marked
  root, not at an arbitrary spatial site. The support terms are obtained by
  grouping the served ordinary and forced coefficients by their full supports
  and marker inventories. At fixed finite volume these address sets are
  finite; if there are countably many original labels at one address, sum
  them absolutely before grouping. The displayed majorants justify this
  operation and retain the reserve one.

  Choose the unmarked forced presentation of the actual lawful representation
  at <math|k>. Insert the singleton coefficients supplied by its stored
  calibrated fields and the grouped multi-marker terms just constructed. The
  ordinary and forced recombination identity of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:allarity-planted-compiler>, applied
  coefficientwise in the finite census algebra, gives exactly
  <math|q<rsub|k>>, with the same unmarked forced gas and scalar
  normalization. Its singleton identity is the stored field identity; every
  higher mode is the corresponding coefficient of the served polynomial. Thus
  this is an expansion of the original marked density, not merely a bound on
  its integral. The endpoint re-expression retains that density and its
  supports; <eqref|eq:trace-endpoint-marked-budget> now changes its weights
  with the proved suppression, on precisely these
  <math|A<rsub|\<nu\>>,K<rsub|\<lambda\>>>. No family, door, calibration or
  partition denominator has been reselected.

  <subparagraph|Paying the census hypotheses on these charts.> We verify the
  geometry and label payment in the preceding construction; they are not
  additional hypotheses on the selected family. Write
  <math|s=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>, <math|H=exp <around|(|<around|(|log log
  s|)><rsup|2>|)>> and <math|C=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
  s|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>, the original budget radius. A census slot is a
  pair <math|<around|(|c,a|)>> consisting of a replication cell and one of
  the finitely many insertion positions. Its position is

  <\equation*>
    o<rsub|k><around|(|c,a|)>=\<omega\><rsub|c>*z<rsub|a>+t<rsub|c>,
  </equation*>

  with the cell's fixed signed isometry <math|\<omega\><rsub|c>>, translation
  <math|t<rsub|c>>, and the chart's lattice position <math|z<rsub|a>>.
  Conjugation changes the observable, not this position. The signed copies
  here are the copies assigned to their cells; they are not extra slots
  stacked at the same site.

  Within one cell, the signed isometry preserves periodic distance. On the
  fixed compact distinct-point test set, the separation argument above gives
  distance at least <math|C<rsup|2>> between different <math|z<rsub|a>>,
  uniformly for the admitted arcs and periods. For different cells, let
  <math|D> be their lattice spacing and <math|c<rsub|c>> their centers. The
  actual chart depth bound puts every <math|o<rsub|k><around|(|c,a|)>> within
  distance <math|D/8> of <math|c<rsub|c>>, while different centers have
  distance at least <math|D>. The paid depth surplus gives
  <math|D\<geqslant\>C<rsup|10>\<geqslant\>2*C<rsup|2>> eventually. The
  triangle inequality therefore gives

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|dist><around|(|o<rsub|k><around|(|c,a|)>,o<rsub|k><around|(|c<rprime|'>,a<rprime|'>|)>|)>\<geqslant\>D-D/8-D/8=3*D/4\<geqslant\>C<rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  These are periodic distances: the chart depth bound places each within-cell
  difference inside the injectivity range, and the cell centers are the
  actual periodic lattice embeddings. Thus the full census, including all
  signed copies, is injectively placed. Every site has occupancy at most one,
  hence at most the purchased occupancy <math|r<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>1>. Every
  allowed census mode inherits the same <math|C<rsup|2>> separated-origin
  test. Migration is the exact parent/embedding identity for these cell
  positions, so it is the migration used by the common service.

  For the label payment let <math|g<rsub|a>> be the fixed requested label of
  grade <math|d<rsub|a>\<leqslant\>d>, and let <math|v<rsub|a>> be its
  calibrated preimage under the common chart reading <math|T>. Put
  <math|\<rho\>=H<rsup|b>> for the chart magnification and
  <math|\<lambda\>=\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k><rsup|-1/2>/\<theta\><rsub|k>> for the
  original shrink factor. The exact solving equation is

  <\equation*>
    \<Delta\><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>*T*v<rsub|a>=<math-up|weight><rsub|\<rho\>><space|0.17em>g<rsub|a>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<Delta\>> scales a coordinate by its colour-factor count and
  <math|<math-up|weight><rsub|\<rho\>>> scales by its spatial grade. The
  actual coupling bound gives <math|\<lambda\><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>2*H>. If
  <math|q<rsub|a>> is the largest colour-factor count in that finite frame,
  its coordinate formula therefore gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||T*v<rsub|a>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|(|2*H|)><rsup|q<rsub|a>>*\<rho\><rsup|d<rsub|a>><around|\||g<rsub|a>|\|><rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  For large <math|k>, the quantitative reading estimate gives
  <math|<math-up|paid><around|(|v<rsub|a>|)>=<around|\||A*v<rsub|a>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*<around|\||T*v<rsub|a>|\|><rsub|1>>.
  Set <math|S<rsub|a>=q<rsub|a>+2\<leqslant\>d<rsub|a>+2>. Absorbing the
  fixed number <math|2<rsup|q<rsub|a>+1><around|\||g<rsub|a>|\|><rsub|1>> in
  one factor <math|H> proves

  <\equation*>
    H<math-up|paid><around|(|v<rsub|a>|)>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|b*d<rsub|a>+S<rsub|a>>.
  </equation*>

  The selected profile's column count satisfies
  <math|1\<leqslant\>\<Gamma\><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>H> by the paid column
  bound. Hence the actual column-weighted price
  <math|M<rsub|a>=\<Gamma\><rsub|k><math-up|paid><around|(|v<rsub|a>|)>>
  satisfies the same inequality <math|M<rsub|a>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|b*d<rsub|a>+S<rsub|a>>>.
  The fixed panel currency <math|f> was chosen with
  <math|f+b*d<rsub|a>+S<rsub|a>\<leqslant\>-8>: indeed <math|b\<leqslant\>c>,
  <math|d<rsub|a>\<leqslant\>d> and <math|S<rsub|a>\<leqslant\>d+2> give
  <math|b*d<rsub|a>+S<rsub|a>\<leqslant\>c*d+d+2>, the credit already used in
  its choice. Thus

  <\equation*>
    H<rsup|f>*M<rsub|a>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-8>.
  </equation*>

  This is a payment for the original calibrated coefficient norm, not merely
  the healthy raw-polynomial norm. Fixed signed motions and conjugates
  preserve the corresponding payment; equivalently include their finite orbit
  in the initial finite maximum.

  Let <math|w<rsub|a>=deg <around|(|F<rsub|d<rsub|a>>|)>+4>, so the stored
  grade allowance is <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d<rsub|a>,k>=s<rsup|-w<rsub|a>>>.
  Choose once <math|z\<geqslant\>max<rsub|d<rprime|'>\<leqslant\>d>
  2*<around|(|deg <around|(|F<rsub|d<rprime|'>>|)>+5|)>> and set
  <math|x=s<rsup|-z/2>\<gtr\>0>. This is the generator bound through grade
  <math|d> used in the chart purchase. Since <math|f> is fixed,
  <math|H<rsup|-8-f>\<leqslant\>s> eventually. The preceding inequality gives
  <math|M<rsub|a>\<leqslant\>s> and consequently

  <\equation*>
    x<math-up|paid><around|(|v<rsub|a>|)>\<leqslant\>x*M<rsub|a>\<leqslant\>s<rsup|-z/2+1>\<leqslant\>s<rsup|-w<rsub|a>>=\<zeta\><rsub|d<rsub|a>,k>.
  </equation*>

  The finite maximum makes the threshold common to all requested labels,
  grades and panels, while the estimates were already uniform in <math|N,p>
  and the chart census. Finally <math|k\<geqslant\>max<rsub|a>
  t<rsub|R><around|(|d<rsub|a>|)>> holds on a tail. Thus every hypothesis of
  the mixed-grade marked expansion is paid on these very charts and the
  actual radius <math|x=s<rsup|-z/2>> used by their endpoint budget change.

  <subparagraph|The fixed-stage purchase interface.> Write <math|p<rsub|j>>
  for the order of the stage-<math|j> profile and assume
  <math|p<rsub|j>\<to\>\<infty\>>. For each <math|d> choose three base arcs
  <math|b<rsub|d,i>\<geqslant\>d+6>, <math|i=0,1,2>, satisfying
  <eqref|eq:three-checks>, with arity <math|n<rsub|d>=<math-up|trays><around|(|d|)>\<geqslant\>2>.
  The explicit choice after that equation gives such arcs. Purchase the three
  panels initially at the larger arcs <math|b<rsub|d,i>+1>. Apply the
  preceding construction to the original funded path, tube and chart data,
  with the common selected stamp and calibration. The local panel purchase
  has the following interface.

  <\enumerate>
    <item>The panels have one common Taylor order
    <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|d>\<geqslant\>d> and, at each stage <math|j>, one
    common polynomial reading order <math|Q<rsub|d,j>>. Their remaining
    constants, including currencies, are fixed before choosing a scale
    schedule. If <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|d>+1\<leqslant\>p<rsub|j-1>>, there
    is a block floor <math|\<mu\><rsub|d,i,j>> such that the local comparison
    estimates apply whenever both adjacent block lengths
    <math|L<rsub|j-1>,L<rsub|j>> exceed <math|\<mu\><rsub|d,i,j>>. The
    Coulomb estimates for the same panels also hold.

    <item>After those lengths have been fixed, one may decrease the positive
    local accuracy parameter of each panel, preserving its arc, Taylor order
    and reading order, so that all its local capacity estimates hold for
    every sufficiently large coupling. This threshold is chosen at fixed
    <math|<around|(|d,i,j|)>>, before the background history and its finite
    volumes. The estimates are uniform over every reduced arc in
    <math|<around|[|0,b<rsub|d,i>+1|]>>. They supply the finite-volume legacy
    costs whenever the chart window has the dilation, depth and bracket of
    (F1), with coupling between <math|3*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>/4> and
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>> throughout that window. They include the
    adjacent-stage estimates for each pair of stages in
    <math|<around|{|j-1,j|}>> that can occur on that window.

    <item>On any one resulting background history, at fixed <math|d> and
    common Taylor order <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|d>>, the local chart and
    stamp construction is valid at every sufficiently large level. Its
    threshold is uniform over the three panels and over reduced arcs. The
    stamp consists of its raw polynomial, its Wick polynomial and its linear
    reading, with degree and coefficient bounds and a local ticket. The
    representation enters this ticket only through its budget radius and
    healthy threshold; its halo is <math|Q<rsub|d,j>> times that radius. The
    covariance and the paid label norm are those of the one selected history.
    These data can therefore be reused in all three panels, as checked below;
    independently chosen readings need not coincide.
  </enumerate>

  The capacity and actuation assertions here are the conclusions of the
  fixed-stage construction above, applied to the named original funded data.
  They are not inferred from the scale-selection argument or from existence
  of three invertible maps. The common half-accuracy reading and calibration
  are those of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:rank-selected-tested-readout>;
  the quantitative invoice is Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:trace-quantitative-invoice>.
  The signed stamp must retain its reserved raw-polynomial and local-ticket
  constants on this same family.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|One request for all grades and
    offsets><label|prop:trace-joint-chart-purchase>Use the preceding
    fixed-stage purchases on the common profiles, together with the common
    paid scale construction of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection>.
    There is one enlargement of its block floors, entry thresholds and
    sublinear comparison windows such that every history on the resulting
    common calendar has the following property. Here paying the requirements
    includes the constant-stage comparison intervals of
    <eqref|eq:common-long-plateau>, not merely the block and entry
    inequalities. For every fixed <math|d>, at every sufficiently large level
    <math|k>, the three panels are available simultaneously at arcs
    <math|b<rsub|d,i>+t> for every <math|t\<in\><around|[|0,1|]>>, with fixed
    currency and Taylor order. All their charts use one reading at each grade
    through <math|d>. The background history and its requirements are chosen
    before any prescribed null sequence of radii.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    <em|Active grades and block lengths.> Set, with the predecessor
    <math|0-1> interpreted as <math|0>,

    <\equation*>
      K<rsub|j>=<around|{|d\<leqslant\>j:\<vartheta\><rsub|d>+1\<leqslant\>p<rsub|j-1>|}>,<space|2em>M<rsub|j>=max
      <around*|(|<around|{|0|}>\<cup\><around|{|\<mu\><rsub|d,i,j>:d\<in\>K<rsub|j>,0\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>2|}>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Each <math|K<rsub|j>> is finite. For every fixed <math|d>, the
    convergence <math|p<rsub|j>\<to\>\<infty\>> shows that
    <math|d\<in\>K<rsub|j>> for all sufficiently large <math|j>; no
    monotonicity of <math|p<rsub|j>> is needed for this assertion. Enlarge
    the block floor at stage <math|j> by <math|max
    <around|(|M<rsub|j>,M<rsub|j+1>|)>>, and take a nondecreasing odd
    sequence above the enlarged floors, as in the common construction. If
    <math|d\<in\>K<rsub|j>>, then <math|L<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>M<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>\<mu\><rsub|d,i,j>>
    and <math|L<rsub|j-1>\<geqslant\>M<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>\<mu\><rsub|d,i,j>>,
    including <math|j=0> by convention. Thus one choice of block lengths pays
    the two adjacent lengths for every active panel. The maximum with
    <math|M<rsub|j+1>> is essential at a stage change.

    <em|Entry thresholds.> Now apply item 2 of the fixed-stage purchase at
    these actual lengths. Denote by <math|h<rsub|d,i,j>> a coupling threshold
    beyond which its conclusion holds for the resulting, trimmed panel. The
    trimming leaves <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|d>> and <math|Q<rsub|d,j>>
    unchanged, so the common-reading condition is retained. For inactive
    triples leave the accuracy parameter unchanged; all these choices are
    made before choosing any history. Let
    <math|x<around|(|s|)>=\<beta\><rsub|\<ast\>>+s<rsub|G>*s>. At a fixed
    stage <math|j>, increase the scale-entry threshold until
    <math|x<around|(|s|)>\<geqslant\>h<rsub|d,i,j+1>> for every
    <math|d\<in\>K<rsub|j+1>> and <math|i=0,1,2>. This is possible because
    <math|s<rsub|G>\<gtr\>0> and there are finitely many demands. Include
    also the finitely many local capacity and geometric thresholds described
    below and all the previous entry thresholds. Taking their maximum
    preserves every conclusion.

    <em|Additional trace and shell payments on the same calendar.> The
    following finite-stage requirements are included before this calendar is
    realized. Let <math|N<rsub|j>> be the nondecreasing occupancy bound of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-observable-purchases>. Let
    <math|\<frak-C\><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1> be any fixed finite maximum of the
    grade-five column budgets at stages through <math|j+1>. Increase the
    coupling entry at stage <math|j> to include

    <\equation*>
      exp <space|-0.17em>exp <space|-0.17em><around*|(|max
      <around|{|N<rsub|j+1>,L<rsub|j+1>,\<frak-C\><rsub|j>,1|}>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Such an increase is allowed by <eqref|eq:common-allstage-payment>. At a
    physical level <math|m> put <math|j=J*<around|(|m+1|)>>. Since the stage
    rises by at most one per step, this gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:trace-extra-stage-payment>N<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>,N<rsub|J*<around|(|m+2|)>>,L<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>,\<frak-C\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>log
      log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>.
    </equation>

    In particular each of these quantities is at most <math|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>> eventually. This is a choice of the common
    entry thresholds, not a subsequent change to an extracted history.

    For each of the finitely many active shape strengths
    <math|\<mu\>\<gtr\>0> at stage <math|j>, further require, for every
    <math|\<beta\>> beyond that entry,

    <\equation*>
      <frac|\<mu\>*exp <around|(|2<around|(|log log
      <around|(|\<beta\>+s<rsub|G>*log L<rsub|j>|)>|)><rsup|2>|)>|log
      \<beta\>>\<geqslant\>j.
    </equation*>

    The requirement holds eventually at fixed <math|j,L<rsub|j>,\<mu\>>: with
    <math|u=log log \<beta\>>, the logarithm of the ratio is
    <math|2*u<rsup|2>-u+log \<mu\>+o<around|(|1|)>>, which tends to infinity.
    A finite maximum pays all active shapes, including the actual shape on
    the transition at that stage. Thus the estimate applies also to its
    possibly varying strength <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>>; since
    <math|J*<around|(|m+1|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>, its actual flagged price

    <\equation*>
      y<rsub|m>=2*exp <around|{|-\<mu\>*\<Delta\><rsub|G,L<rsub|j>><around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|)><rsup|2>/16|}>
    </equation*>

    is smaller than every negative power of <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>>
    eventually.

    Here is the resulting numerical transfer check. Write
    <math|x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>>, <math|x<rsub|+>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>>
    and <math|B<rsub|m>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>. By <eqref|eq:trace-extra-stage-payment>,
    <math|L<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>log log x>, so <math|x<rsub|+>/x\<to\>1>. Both
    adjacent occupancies are at most <math|log log x>, and
    <math|<math-ss|S><around|(|B|)>=e*<around|(|2*<around|(|16*B+9|)>|)><rsup|4>=O<around|(|<around|(|log
    x|)><rsup|8>|)>> at either adjacent radius. Thus, with
    <math|d<rsub|m>=x<rsub|+><rsup|-2>>,

    <\equation*>
      T<rsub|m>=o<rsub|m><math-ss|S><around|(|B<rsub|m+1>|)>*d<rsub|m>+4*d<rsub|m>=o<around|(|x<rsub|+><rsup|-1>|)>,<space|2em><around|(|2*B<rsub|m>+1|)><rsup|4>*<around|(|y<rsub|m>+o<rsub|m><math-ss|S><around|(|B<rsub|m>|)>*d<rsub|m>|)>=o<around|(|1|)>.
    </equation*>

    It follows that <math|T<rsub|m>+e<rsup|T<rsub|m>>*T<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|+><rsup|-1>>,
    the latter site sum is at most <math|1/2>, and <math|exp
    <around|(|y<rsub|m>+o<rsub|m><math-ss|S><around|(|B<rsub|m>|)>*d<rsub|m>|)>-1\<leqslant\>1>
    eventually. The same calculation with the fine occupancy proves the
    adjacent version. These are precisely the coarse and fine numerical
    duties displayed in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-actual-stress-shell-nodes>.
    They do not by themselves prove the separate finite-mask or density
    identities there.

    <em|One comparison window.> For a fixed block length <math|L\<gtr\>1>,
    nonnegative arc <math|b>, and sufficiently large <math|x>, put

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-M\><around|(|x|)>=exp <around|(|<around|(|log log
      x|)><rsup|2>|)>,<space|1em>C<around|(|x|)>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
      x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>,<space|1em>h<rsub|L,b><around|(|x|)>=<around*|\<lceil\>|<frac|<around|(|b+2|)>*log
      \<cal-M\><around|(|x|)>|log L>|\<rceil\>>+<around*|\<lceil\>|<frac|10*log
      C<around|(|x|)>|log L>|\<rceil\>>.
    </equation*>

    Extend this as any nonnegative integer-valued function below its
    large-<math|x> domain. For fixed <math|L,b>,
    <math|h<rsub|L,b><around|(|x|)>=O<around|(|1+<around|(|log log
    x|)><rsup|2>|)>>, hence <math|h<rsub|L,b><around|(|x<around|(|s|)>|)>/s\<to\>0>.
    Indeed <math|C<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>2<around|(|log x|)><rsup|2>>
    eventually, so <math|log C<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>log 2+2*log log x>,
    and rounding adds at most two. If <math|w<rsub|j><rsup|0><around|(|s|)>=o<around|(|s|)>>
    is the old stage-<math|j> window, replace it by

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:trace-joint-window>w<rsub|j><around|(|s|)>=w<rsub|j><rsup|0><around|(|s|)>+<big|sum><rsub|d\<in\>K<rsub|j+1>><big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|2>h<rsub|L<rsub|j>,b<rsub|d,i>+1>*<around|(|x*<around|(|3*s|)>|)>.
    </equation>

    The sum is finite at fixed <math|j>, so
    <math|w<rsub|j><around|(|s|)>=o<around|(|s|)>>. No uniform bound in
    <math|j> is required by the common scale construction. Each summand is
    bounded by the whole nonnegative window, which therefore pays every
    active panel separately. The construction preserves the previous minimum
    physical length and all previous windows and entry thresholds.

    For completeness, this window also protects a chart whose level has
    advanced beyond the paid stage-<math|j> interval. Take
    <math|i<rsub|0>=k<rsub|j-j<rsub|\<ast\>>>>, the center of the
    constant-stage interval <eqref|eq:common-long-plateau>, chosen with the
    enlarged entry threshold and window. Thus
    <math|<around|[|i<rsub|0>,i<rsub|0>+w<rsub|j><around|(|s<rsub|i<rsub|0>>|)>|]>>
    has stage <math|j>. Write <math|s=s<rsub|i<rsub|0>>>, and let <math|k> be
    a later level with <math|J<around|(|k|)>=j+1> and <math|t=s<rsub|k>>. Put
    <math|H=h<rsub|L<rsub|j>,b><around|(|x<around|(|t|)>|)>>, where
    <math|b=b<rsub|d,i>+1>. Increase the stage-<math|j> entry threshold
    beforehand so that, for every later <math|t>,

    <\equation*>
      h<rsub|L<rsub|j>,b><around|(|x<around|(|t|)>|)>*log
      L<rsub|j+1>\<leqslant\>t/2,<space|2em>s<rsub|G>*h<rsub|L<rsub|j>,b><around|(|x<around|(|t|)>|)>*log
      L<rsub|j+1>\<leqslant\>x<around|(|t|)>/4.
    </equation*>

    These inequalities are possible by the sublinear bound just proved. If
    <math|t\<leqslant\>3*s>, monotonicity of <math|h<rsub|L<rsub|j>,b>> on
    its large-<math|x> domain gives <math|H\<leqslant\>h<rsub|L<rsub|j>,b>*<around|(|x*<around|(|3*s|)>|)>\<leqslant\>w<rsub|j><around|(|s|)>>,
    and the end of the paid interval lies strictly before <math|k>. Hence
    <math|H\<leqslant\>k-i<rsub|0>>. If <math|t\<gtr\>3*s>, use the first of
    these inequalities. Since block lengths are nondecreasing and the stages
    from <math|i<rsub|0>> to <math|k> are <math|j> or <math|j+1>,
    <math|t-s\<leqslant\><around|(|k-i<rsub|0>|)>*log L<rsub|j+1>>. Now
    <math|t-s\<gtr\>2*t/3\<gtr\>t/2>, so again
    <math|H\<leqslant\>k-i<rsub|0>>. Thus the backward depth paid by <math|H>
    cannot leave the two stages. Write <math|d<rsub|1>,d<rsub|2>> for its
    first and second summands. All block lengths between <math|i<rsub|0>> and
    <math|k> are at least <math|L<rsub|j>>. Hence, for every reduced arc
    <math|0\<leqslant\>u\<leqslant\>b>,

    <\equation*>
      <frac|a<rsub|k-d<rsub|1>>|a<rsub|k>>\<geqslant\>L<rsub|j><rsup|d<rsub|1>>\<geqslant\>\<cal-M\><around|(|x<around|(|t|)>|)><rsup|u+2>.
    </equation*>

    The middle level therefore satisfies <math|\<ell\>\<geqslant\>k-d<rsub|1>>.
    Since <math|k-i<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>d<rsub|1>+d<rsub|2>>, the level
    <math|\<ell\>-d<rsub|2>> is still at least <math|i<rsub|0>>, and

    <\equation*>
      <frac|a<rsub|\<ell\>-d<rsub|2>>|a<rsub|\<ell\>>>\<geqslant\>L<rsub|j><rsup|d<rsub|2>>\<geqslant\>C<around|(|x<around|(|t|)>|)><rsup|10>.
    </equation*>

    The root depth consequently satisfies
    <math|w\<geqslant\>\<ell\>-d<rsub|2>\<geqslant\>k-H>. This proves the
    dilation, depth and two-stage bracket. Since every block length on
    <math|<around|[|w,k|]>> is at most <math|L<rsub|j+1>>,

    <\equation*>
      <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>-<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|w>=s<rsub|G>*log
      <around|(|a<rsub|w>/a<rsub|k>|)>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|G>*H*log
      L<rsub|j+1>\<leqslant\>x<around|(|t|)>/4.
    </equation*>

    Thus the second displayed entry inequality bounds the coupling decrease
    in both cases. This proves the <math|3/4> plateau required in item 2 of
    the fixed-stage purchase, not merely the weaker <math|1/2> plateau used
    elsewhere. Include also the eventual monitor bound <math|max
    <around|(|L<rsub|j>,L<rsub|j+1>|)>\<leqslant\>\<cal-M\><around|(|x<around|(|t|)>|)>>
    in the entry threshold.

    <em|Offsets and common readings.> Fix <math|d>. Since the stage map
    diverges, <math|d\<in\>K<rsub|J<around|(|k|)>>> for all large <math|k>.
    The preceding construction and item 2 of the fixed-stage purchase
    therefore give the legacy costs for all three panels, uniformly for every
    smaller nonnegative arc. Replace their arcs by <math|b<rsub|d,i>+t>,
    <math|0\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>1>. Their lower bounds are preserved
    because <math|b<rsub|d,i>\<geqslant\>d+6>; their other data are
    unchanged. Intersect the finitely many eventual level sets for the three
    panels with the set supplied by item 3 of the fixed-stage purchase, and
    with <math|k\<geqslant\>max<rsub|e\<leqslant\>d><math-up|ref><around|(|e|)>>.
    The resulting tail is independent of <math|t>. Use the reading of panel
    <math|0> for the chart maps. For another panel retain exactly the same
    raw polynomial, Wick polynomial and reading. The degree and coefficient
    bounds are unchanged. The halo is unchanged because <math|Q<rsub|d,j>>
    and the budget radius agree; the locality and healthy-set estimate are
    unchanged because the healthy threshold also agrees. Finally the
    covariance and paid label norm belong to the same history. Thus every
    clause of the stamp still holds, now for this panel's representation. Its
    individual actuation witness and this transported stamp certify the same
    chart. This is a reuse of one stamp, not an assertion that independently
    chosen stamps have equal readings. This proves simultaneous availability,
    rather than three unrelated chart choices. Every choice preceded the null
    sequence; the later interpolation and subsequence selection only query
    these already constructed panels.
  </proof>

  That theorem also requires a simple gauge group, the funded chart-control
  estimate, a bound on the generator through grade <math|d>, an index
  <math|l> with <math|l\<leqslant\><math-up|trays><around|(|d|)>>, a positive
  sequence tending to zero, and the selected extraction order, smooth table,
  point limits, and reference hierarchy used by the station construction. It
  returns a strictly increasing level curve and, at a selected point, data
  <math|<around|(|k,i,b,c,R|)>> such that the requested scale equals the
  exact clock gap at level <math|i> and base arc <math|b>. Its chart has
  <math|5\<less\>b> and <math|b> no larger than the permitted angular bound,
  covers all reference grades through <math|d>, and supplies the
  finite-volume charts and invoices whenever <math|i\<less\>N>. It also
  supplies a pairwise comparison receipt at cost
  <math|<math-up|costHint><around|(|l|)>> and a station whose lift has a
  finite norm bound, preserves the unit, real structure, and signed action,
  and obeys the short-test estimate for arity at most <math|l>, tests in the
  stated window, and error <math|<math-up|costHint><around|(|l|)>>. The proof
  of this quantitative upgrade is the separate three-panel chart result; this
  section uses that exact output and proves the scalar gap interpolation and
  recursion. In the gallery construction one takes <math|l=d>, so the chart
  cutoff, arity cap, and finite grade cutoff agree. The clock gives, for each
  fixed base arc <math|b\<geqslant\>0>, a positive gap

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-firesky-gap>g<rsub|b><around|(|k|)>=a<rsub|k>*m<rsub|k><rsup|b>,
  </equation>

  where <math|a<rsub|k>\<gtr\>0>, <math|m<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>1>,
  <math|g<rsub|b><around|(|k|)>\<to\>0>, and eventually
  <math|g<rsub|b><around|(|k|)>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|b+1>*<around|(|k+1|)>>.
  These scale-gap facts are the input to the selection argument.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Clock-gap interpolation><label|lem:ex-lattice-firesky-gap-interpolation>Fix
    <math|b\<geqslant\>0>. Suppose <math|g<rsub|b><around|(|k|)>\<gtr\>0>,
    <math|g<rsub|b><around|(|k|)>\<to\>0>, and
    <math|g<rsub|b><around|(|k|)>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|b+1>*<around|(|k+1|)>>
    for all <math|k\<geqslant\>k<rsub|0>>. For every
    <math|J\<geqslant\>k<rsub|0>> there is <math|r\<gtr\>0> such that for
    each <math|0\<less\>s\<leqslant\>r> there are <math|k\<gtr\>J> and
    <math|t\<in\><around|[|0,1|]>> with <math|s=g<rsub|b+t><around|(|k|)>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Take <math|r=g<rsub|b><around|(|J|)>\<gtr\>0>. Given
    <math|0\<less\>s\<leqslant\>r>, choose the least <math|k\<gtr\>J> with
    <math|g<rsub|b><around|(|k|)>\<leqslant\>s>; it exists by convergence to
    zero. Minimality gives <math|s\<leqslant\>g<rsub|b>*<around|(|k-1|)>>,
    and the one-step comparison at <math|k-1> gives

    <\equation*>
      g<rsub|b><around|(|k|)>\<leqslant\>s\<leqslant\>g<rsub|b>*<around|(|k-1|)>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|b+1><around|(|k|)>.
    </equation*>

    The function <math|t\<mapsto\>g<rsub|b+t><around|(|k|)>=a<rsub|k>*m<rsub|k><rsup|b+t>>
    is continuous on <math|<around|[|0,1|]>>. The intermediate value theorem
    supplies the desired <math|t>.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The fields of a chart witness><label|subsec:ex-lattice-firesky-witness>

  The producers of the pairwise receipt use several fields of the
  finite-volume chart witness. We list them here, with the two scale
  definitions they require. We then prove three lemmas: the corrected labels
  solve their reading equation, the insertions lie inside the home chamber at
  the root depth, and the clock of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection>
  supplies the dilation, depth, bracket and plateau. The lawful history (F3)
  is supplied by Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:rank-trace-all-levels>, and
  the readings and stamps of (F5) by Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:rank-selected-tested-readout>,
  under their stated funded construction hypotheses. In this subsection,
  <math|k> denotes the level of the chart and <math|\<mu\><rsub|k>> the
  monitor <math|m<rsub|k>> of <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-firesky-gap>. <math|J> is
  the stage clock and <math|L<rsub|j>> are the block lengths of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection>, and
  <math|c<rsub|k>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>> is the cover size of the
  clock at level <math|k>. The monitor is
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|k>=\<cal-M\><rsub|k>> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:rank-root-normalization>, and
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>> is the clock coupling of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection>.

  <subparagraph|Two levels attached to a chart.> Let the chart have base arc
  <math|b>, and put <math|m=\<mu\><rsub|k><rsup|b>>. Its <em|middle level>
  and <em|root depth> are

  <\equation*>
    \<ell\>=max <around*|{|j\<leqslant\>k:<frac|a<rsub|j>|a<rsub|k>>\<geqslant\>m*\<mu\><rsub|k><rsup|2>|}>,<space|2em>w=max
    <around*|{|j\<leqslant\>\<ell\>:<frac|a<rsub|j>|a<rsub|\<ell\>>>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|k><rsup|10>|}>,
  </equation*>

  with the convention <math|max \<varnothing\>=0>. Thus
  <math|w\<leqslant\>\<ell\>\<leqslant\>k>. The middle cell of the chart is a
  cell of spacing <math|a<rsub|\<ell\>>>. The root tests and the chamber
  condition of the chart are taken on the centered tiling at depth <math|w>,
  whose cells are indexed by the sites of the level-<math|w> torus.

  <subparagraph|The witness fields.> A chart witness at level <math|k> with
  base arc <math|b> supplies the following fields. Here <math|M> is the
  marker allowance of the chart, <math|N\<gtr\>k> and <math|p> are arbitrary.

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(F1)><em|Allowance.> Four conditions hold:

    <\itemize>
      <item>dilation: <math|m*\<mu\><rsub|k><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|\<ell\>>/a<rsub|k>>,
      so the set defining <math|\<ell\>> is nonempty;

      <item>depth: <math|c<rsub|k><rsup|10>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|w>/a<rsub|\<ell\>>>;

      <item>wheel: <math|min <around|(|L<rsub|J<around|(|k|)>>,L<rsub|J<around|(|k|)>-1>|)>\<geqslant\>2>,
      where the clock factor of a stage is its block length;

      <item>bracket: <math|J<around|(|k|)>-1\<leqslant\>J<around|(|j|)>> for
      every <math|w\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>k>.
    </itemize>

    <item*|(F2)><em|Plateau.> <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>/2\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>
    for every <math|w\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>k>.

    <item*|(F3)><em|Lawful history.> For each <math|N\<gtr\>k> and <math|p>
    there are representations <math|P<rsub|j>> on the level-<math|j> torus,
    <math|w\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>k>, along the course of the chart. Each
    <math|P<rsub|j>> satisfies the suppositions of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:rank-root-output-ticket>(2) at level
    <math|j>, with the clip and row bound of the stage
    <math|\<pi\><rsub|J<around|(|j|)>>>. Its budget radius is
    <math|<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>><rsup|2>>, and its
    threshold is

    <\equation*>
      \<theta\><rsub|P<rsub|j>>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j><rsup|-1/2>*exp
      <around*|(|<around|(|log log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|)><rsup|3/2>|)>,
    </equation*>

    the clock's hatted coupling <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j><rsup|-1/2>>
    at <math|j> times the healthy allowance <math|exp <around|(|<around|(|log
    log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|)><rsup|3/2>|)>>. This is the threshold of
    the representation itself. Once <math|log log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1> it is at most the clock threshold
    <math|\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|clock>><around|(|j|)>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j><rsup|-1/2>*\<cal-M\><rsub|j>>
    of Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:rank-root-clock-threshold>, in which
    the healthy allowance is replaced by the monitor
    <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|j>>; the numerical late-scale bounds there concern
    <math|\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|clock>>>.

    <item*|(F4)><em|Ticket.> <math|T<rsub|M><around|(|\<pi\><rsub|J<around|(|w|)>>|)>\<leqslant\>log
    \<mu\><rsub|k>/<around|(|16*M|)>>.

    <item*|(F5)><em|Readings and stamps.> The level satisfies
    <math|<math-up|ref><around|(|d<rprime|'>|)>\<leqslant\>k> for every grade
    <math|d<rprime|'>\<leqslant\>d>, where <math|<math-up|ref>> is the
    reference level of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-rank-purchases>.
    This holds when the chart is an output of the one-step construction,
    which covers all reference grades through <math|d>, and in any case for
    all large <math|k>, since only finitely many grades occur. For every
    grade <math|d<rprime|'>\<leqslant\>d> there is a linear reading
    <math|T<rsub|d<rprime|'>>> of the grade-<math|d<rprime|'>> label space
    with three properties:

    <\itemize>
      <item>it fixes the reference unit;

      <item>it commutes with the real structure;

      <item>it satisfies the half-accuracy bound
      <math|<around|\||a<around|(|v|)>-T<rsub|d<rprime|'>>*v|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><frac|1|2><around|\||a<around|(|v|)>|\|><rsub|1>>,
      where <math|a=A<rsub|k,N,p>> is the calibrated entry
      (primary-coordinate) map at level <math|k> for <math|<around|(|N,p|)>>.
    </itemize>

    It also carries a stamp duty with the same reading: a bound, in the halo
    of the readers, on the price of the insertions whose low-grade reading is
    <math|T<rsub|d<rprime|'>>>. This clause is supplied for the funded
    calibrated family by Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:rank-selected-tested-readout>;
    its hypotheses concern the same raw polynomial and reading, not separate
    choices.
  </enumerate>

  By (F1), every level of <math|<around|[|w,k|]>> lies in the stage
  <math|J<around|(|k|)>> or <math|J<around|(|k|)>-1>. Hence
  <math|J<around|(|w|)>\<geqslant\>J<around|(|k|)>-1>, and
  <math|w\<to\>\<infty\>> as <math|k\<to\>\<infty\>> because <math|J>
  diverges.

  <subparagraph|Corrected labels.> Write <math|\<sigma\>=\<sigma\><rsub|k,N,p>=<around|(|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k><rsup|1/2>*<space|0.17em>\<theta\><rsub|k>|)><rsup|-1>\<gtr\>0>
  for the shrink factor, where <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>> is the actual
  coupling of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-rank-purchases> and
  <math|\<theta\><rsub|k>=\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|clock>><around|(|k|)>> is
  the clock threshold. Write <math|W<rsub|\<rho\>>> for the linear map
  multiplying each coordinate of a label by <math|\<rho\>> to its position
  degree, and <math|S<rsub|\<sigma\>>> for multiplication by <math|\<sigma\>>
  to the factor degree. Both act on the grade-<math|d<rprime|'>> label space
  <math|V<rsub|d<rprime|'>>>. By Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-firesky-solves>
  below, <math|T<rsub|d<rprime|'>>> is invertible. The <em|corrected label>
  of a basis vector <math|e> of <math|V<rsub|d<rprime|'>>> is

  <\equation*>
    X<rsub|N,p>*e=T<rsub|d<rprime|'>><rsup|-1>*<around*|(|S<rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|-1>*W<rsub|m>*e|)>.
  </equation*>

  These are the linear maps <math|X<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|N,p>> of input
  (G2) in Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:trace-rounded-comparison>, for
  the chart selected at index <math|i>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Corrected labels solve the reading
    equation><label|lem:ex-lattice-firesky-solves>The reading
    <math|T<rsub|d<rprime|'>>> is bijective, and <math|X<rsub|N,p>*e> is the
    unique label <math|v> with

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ex-lattice-firesky-solves>S<rsub|\<sigma\>>*<around*|(|T<rsub|d<rprime|'>>*v|)>=W<rsub|m>*e.
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Since <math|<math-up|ref><around|(|d<rprime|'>|)>\<leqslant\>k\<less\>N>
    by (F5), the calibrated entry map satisfies the reference inverse
    estimate <eqref|eq:rank-reference-inverse> of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-rank-purchases>,
    <math|<around|\||v|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>D<rsub|k><around|\||a<around|(|v|)>|\|><rsub|1>>.
    With the half-accuracy bound of (F5), the argument given there shows that
    <math|T<rsub|d<rprime|'>>> is injective, hence bijective. Next,
    <math|S<rsub|\<sigma\>>> is invertible. So the equation has the unique
    solution <math|X<rsub|N,p>*e>; this is <eqref|eq:rank-common-lifts> with
    <math|\<rho\>=m>.
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|Status.> Fields (F1)\U(F5) are fields of the funded witness.
  For the clock of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection> and
  all sufficiently large <math|k>, with the requests of
  <eqref|eq:rank-root-ticket-requests> included, (F1), (F2) and (F4) are
  proved below: by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-firesky-clock-fields>,
  the remark after it, and Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:rank-root-output-ticket>.
  The inputs listed elsewhere that these fields supply are:

  <\itemize>
    <item>the allowance and the root-depth identification used in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:trace-rounded-receipt>;

    <item>the bracket property used after
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:rank-root-output-ticket>;

    <item>the ticket at the output level.
  </itemize>

  Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:rank-trace-all-levels> supplies (F3) on the
  entire window, while Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:rank-selected-tested-readout>
  supplies (F5), under their stated funded hypotheses. These local results do
  not replace the common all-grade purchase proved above.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Chamber interiority at the root
    depth><label|lem:ex-lattice-firesky-chamber>Consider a chart at level
    <math|k> with base arc <math|b>, middle level <math|\<ell\>> and root
    depth <math|w>. Let <math|x> be fit for the chart: the grid configuration
    <math|z=a<rsub|k>*<around|\<lceil\>|m*x|\<rceil\>>> is coordinatewise
    separated and <math|<around|\||z<rsub|c,\<alpha\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|\<ell\>>/8>
    for <math|c=0,1> and <math|\<alpha\>=1,\<ldots\>,4>. Fix a grade
    <math|d>, and let <math|Z<rsub|d>> bound the coordinates of the finite
    stencil <math|S<rsub|d>> of the grade-<math|d> insertions. Then, for
    every <math|N\<geqslant\>k> with

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ex-lattice-firesky-chamber-mesh>a<rsub|N>*<around|(|Z<rsub|d>+2|)>\<less\><tfrac|3|8><space|0.17em>a<rsub|w>,
    </equation>

    every period <math|p> and every grade-<math|d> label, the insertion at
    the site <math|z<rsub|c>> (<math|c=0,1>) has every link of its stencil,
    including both endpoints, strictly inside the home chamber of the
    centered tiling at depth <math|w>. It is therefore in the chamber-local
    domain of that tiling. Condition <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-firesky-chamber-mesh>
    holds for all large <math|N>, with a threshold depending only on <math|w>
    and <math|d>. In particular (G4) of Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:trace-rounded-comparison>
    holds with <math|w<rsub|i>=w> for the chart selected at index <math|i>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Measure positions in mesh steps of the level-<math|N> lattice; since
    <math|N\<geqslant\>k>, <math|a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|N>> is an integer. The
    depth-<math|w> cell has an odd number <math|q=a<rsub|w>/a<rsub|N>> of
    steps along each axis, as in the interiority argument of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-construction>. Its home cell
    consists of the offsets <math|u> with
    <math|<around|\||u<rsub|\<alpha\>>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|q-1|)>/2>
    from the centre. Hence the site <math|z<rsub|c>> has integer offset
    <math|u=z<rsub|c>/a<rsub|N>>. By fitness, and because
    <math|w\<leqslant\>\<ell\>> implies <math|a<rsub|\<ell\>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|w>>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||u<rsub|\<alpha\>>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|a<rsub|\<ell\>>|8*a<rsub|N>>\<leqslant\><frac|q|8>.
    </equation*>

    A link of the stencil at <math|z<rsub|c>> has endpoints <math|u+s> and
    <math|u+s+e>, with <math|s\<in\>S<rsub|d>> and <math|e> a unit vector.
    Each coordinate of an endpoint is at most <math|q/8+Z<rsub|d>+1> in
    absolute value. By <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-firesky-chamber-mesh>,
    <math|Z<rsub|d>+2\<less\>3*q/8>. Hence

    <\equation*>
      <frac|q|8>+Z<rsub|d>+1\<less\><frac|q|2>-1\<less\><frac|q-1|2>,
    </equation*>

    so both endpoints lie strictly inside the home cell. The insertion is a
    bounded, measurable, gauge-invariant function of these links. It is
    therefore chamber-admissible, exactly as in the argument cited above.
    Finally, <math|a<rsub|N>\<to\>0> while <math|a<rsub|w>\<gtr\>0> and
    <math|Z<rsub|d>> are fixed, so <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-firesky-chamber-mesh>
    holds for all large <math|N>. The threshold does not depend on <math|p>,
    on the label, or on the fit configuration.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The clock supplies the dilation, depth, bracket and
    plateau><label|lem:ex-lattice-firesky-clock-fields>Let the clock be the
    one of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection>, so that
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>=\<beta\><rsub|\<ast\>>+s<rsub|G>*s<rsub|m>>,
    <math|a<rsub|m>=e<rsup|-s<rsub|m>>>, <math|s<rsub|m+1>=s<rsub|m>+log
    L<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>>, <math|L<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>3>, and
    <math|J=j<rsub|\<ast\>>+J<rsub|0>>. Let the monitor be
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|k>=exp <around|(|<around|(|log log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)><rsup|2>|)>> and the cover size
    <math|c<rsub|k>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>. Fix
    <math|<wide|b|\<bar\>>\<gtr\>0>. Then for all sufficiently large <math|k>
    and every base arc <math|0\<less\>b\<leqslant\><wide|b|\<bar\>>>, the
    following hold.

    <\enumerate>
      <item>The sets defining <math|\<ell\>> and <math|w> are nonempty. Hence
      <math|m*\<mu\><rsub|k><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|\<ell\>>/a<rsub|k>>
      and <math|c<rsub|k><rsup|10>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|w>/a<rsub|\<ell\>>>;
      these are the dilation and depth of (F1).

      <item><math|J<around|(|k|)>-1\<leqslant\>J<around|(|j|)>> for every
      <math|w\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>k>; this is the bracket of (F1).

      <item><math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>/2\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>
      for every <math|w\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>k>; this is (F2).
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    <em|Growth of the scale.> At every step <math|m> with
    <math|J*<around|(|m+1|)>\<gtr\>j<rsub|\<ast\>>>, the entry requirement
    <eqref|eq:common-stage-entries> gives <math|log
    L<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|m>/J*<around|(|m+1|)>>, so
    <math|s<rsub|m+1>\<leqslant\>2*s<rsub|m>> at each such step. The steps of
    the first plateau are finitely many. Hence
    <math|s<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|0>*2<rsup|k>> for a constant
    <math|C<rsub|0>>, and

    <\equation*>
      log log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>log k+C<rsub|1>
    </equation*>

    for large <math|k>, with a constant <math|C<rsub|1>>. On the other side,
    <math|s<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>k*log 3> by <eqref|eq:common-scale-brackets>.

    <em|Item 1.> The set defining <math|\<ell\>> contains <math|j=0> as soon
    as <math|a<rsub|0>/a<rsub|k>=e<rsup|s<rsub|k>>\<geqslant\>m*\<mu\><rsub|k><rsup|2>=\<mu\><rsub|k><rsup|b+2>>.
    Now <math|e<rsup|s<rsub|k>>\<geqslant\>3<rsup|k>>, while
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|k><rsup|b+2>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around|{|<around|(|<wide|b|\<bar\>>+2|)>*<around|(|log
    k+C<rsub|1>|)><rsup|2>|}>>, so this holds for large <math|k>. The
    defining inequality then holds at its maximum <math|\<ell\>>. If
    <math|\<ell\>\<less\>k>, maximality gives
    <math|3<rsup|k-\<ell\>-1>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|\<ell\>+1>/a<rsub|k>\<less\>\<mu\><rsub|k><rsup|b+2>>,
    because <math|a<rsub|\<ell\>+1>/a<rsub|k>=<big|prod><rsub|t=\<ell\>+2><rsup|k>L<rsub|J<around|(|t|)>>>
    and <math|L<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>3>. Likewise, if <math|w\<less\>\<ell\>>,
    then <math|3<rsup|\<ell\>-w-1>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|w+1>/a<rsub|\<ell\>>\<less\>c<rsub|k><rsup|10>>,
    once <math|w> is defined. Hence

    <\equation*>
      k-\<ell\>\<leqslant\>1+<frac|<around|(|<wide|b|\<bar\>>+2|)>*<around|(|log
      k+C<rsub|1>|)><rsup|2>|log 3>,<space|2em>\<ell\>-w\<leqslant\>1+<frac|10*log
      c<rsub|k>|log 3>.
    </equation*>

    The first bound gives <math|k-\<ell\>=O<around|(|<around|(|log
    k|)><rsup|2>|)>>, so <math|\<ell\>/k\<to\>1>. Since
    <math|c<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>2<around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)><rsup|2>> for large <math|k> and <math|log
    log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>log k+C<rsub|1>>, we have
    <math|log c<rsub|k>=O<around|(|log k|)>>. Thus <math|10*log
    c<rsub|k>=o<around|(|\<ell\>|)>>, and for large <math|k>,
    <math|a<rsub|0>/a<rsub|\<ell\>>\<geqslant\>3<rsup|\<ell\>>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|k><rsup|10>>.
    The set defining <math|w> is therefore nonempty. Adding the two bounds
    gives a constant <math|C<rsub|2>> with

    <\equation*>
      k-w\<leqslant\>C<rsub|2><around|(|log k|)><rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    <em|Item 2.> Let <math|j=J<rsub|0><around|(|k|)>>, so
    <math|A<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>k\<less\>A<rsub|j+1>> in the notation of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection>. If
    <math|w\<geqslant\>A<rsub|j>>, every level of <math|<around|[|w,k|]>> has
    <math|J<rsub|0>=j>. Otherwise <math|w\<less\>A<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>k>, and
    so <math|k-A<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>k-w\<leqslant\>C<rsub|2><around|(|log
    k|)><rsup|2>>. For large <math|k> this forces
    <math|k\<leqslant\>2*A<rsub|j>>. By the recursion,

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|j>-A<rsub|j-1>=b<rsub|j>+2*k<rsub|j-1>+1\<geqslant\>4*A<rsub|j-1>+3,
    </equation*>

    so <math|A<rsub|j>-A<rsub|j-1>\<geqslant\><tfrac|4|5>A<rsub|j>>. For
    large <math|k>, and hence large <math|A<rsub|j>>,

    <\equation*>
      k-w\<leqslant\>C<rsub|2>*<around|(|log
      2*A<rsub|j>|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><tfrac|4|5>A<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|j>-A<rsub|j-1>.
    </equation*>

    Therefore <math|w\<geqslant\>k-<around|(|A<rsub|j>-A<rsub|j-1>|)>\<geqslant\>A<rsub|j-1>>,
    and every level of <math|<around|[|w,k|]>> has
    <math|J<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>j-1>.

    <em|Item 3.> Since <math|s<rsub|m>> is increasing,
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>> for
    <math|j\<leqslant\>k>, and <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<geqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|w>>
    for <math|j\<geqslant\>w>. Next, <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>-<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|w>=s<rsub|G>*log
    <around|(|a<rsub|w>/a<rsub|k>|)>>. Split <math|a<rsub|w>/a<rsub|k>> into
    four factors:

    <\equation*>
      <frac|a<rsub|w>|a<rsub|k>>=<frac|a<rsub|w>|a<rsub|w+1>>\<cdot\><frac|a<rsub|w+1>|a<rsub|\<ell\>>>\<cdot\><frac|a<rsub|\<ell\>>|a<rsub|\<ell\>+1>>\<cdot\><frac|a<rsub|\<ell\>+1>|a<rsub|k>>.
    </equation*>

    Omit the factors whose indices coincide. By maximality the second and
    fourth factors are below <math|c<rsub|k><rsup|10>> and
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|k><rsup|b+2>>. The first and third are block lengths
    <math|L<rsub|J*<around|(|t+1|)>>> with <math|t\<geqslant\>w>. By the
    entry requirement each is at most <math|exp
    <around|(|s<rsub|k>/J*<around|(|t+1|)>|)>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around|(|s<rsub|k>/<around|(|J<around|(|k|)>-1|)>|)>>, using item 2.
    Hence

    <\equation*>
      log <frac|a<rsub|w>|a<rsub|k>>\<leqslant\><frac|2*s<rsub|k>|J<around|(|k|)>-1>+10*log
      c<rsub|k>+<around|(|<wide|b|\<bar\>>+2|)>*<around|(|log
      k+C<rsub|1>|)><rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|J<around|(|k|)>\<to\>\<infty\>> and
    <math|s<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>k*log 3>, the right side is at most
    <math|s<rsub|k>/2> for large <math|k>. Thus
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>-<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|w>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|G>*s<rsub|k>/2\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>/2>,
    because <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>\<geqslant\>s<rsub|G>*s<rsub|k>>.
  </proof>

  The wheel bound of (F1) is also immediate: the clock factor of a stage
  <math|j> is its block length <math|L<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>3>. Consequently,
  for all sufficiently large <math|k> and base arcs up to
  <math|<wide|b|\<bar\>>>, of the witness fields, (F3) and (F5) are supplied
  by the conditional constructions just cited; the remaining issue is to pay
  their hypotheses on the same selected history, as in
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:trace-joint-chart-purchase>. (F4) is
  replaced by Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:rank-root-output-ticket>.

  <subsubsection|Choosing scales and chart levels>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Strict scale selection><label|lem:ex-lattice-firesky-selection>Assume
    the one-step chart producer has this output at every grade <math|d>: for
    every previous index <math|N> and every <math|\<theta\>\<gtr\>0>, it
    supplies an index <math|n\<gtr\>N>, a level <math|i\<geqslant\>d>, and a
    grade-<math|d> chart with exact radius <math|s<rsub|n>\<less\>\<theta\>>
    and the finite-level receipts above. Let <math|s<rsub|n>\<gtr\>0> and
    <math|s<rsub|n>\<to\>0>. Then there are strictly increasing indices
    <math|n<rsub|d>>, levels <math|i<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>d>, and admissible
    charts at grade <math|d> and level <math|i<rsub|d>>, such that

    <\equation*>
      s<rsub|n<rsub|0>>\<less\>1,<space|2em>s<rsub|n<rsub|d+1>>\<less\>s<rsub|n<rsub|d>>,<space|2em>i<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>d.
    </equation*>

    In particular <math|i<rsub|d>\<to\>\<infty\>> and the selected chart
    radius is exactly <math|s<rsub|n<rsub|d>>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For <math|d=0>, apply the one-step output with previous index <math|0>
    and threshold <math|1>. For <math|d\<gtr\>0>, apply it with previous
    index <math|n<rsub|d-1>> and threshold <math|s<rsub|n<rsub|d-1>>>. It
    gives <math|n<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0>, <math|s<rsub|n<rsub|0>>\<less\>1>, and
    thereafter <math|n<rsub|d>\<gtr\>n<rsub|d-1>>,
    <math|s<rsub|n<rsub|d>>\<less\>s<rsub|n<rsub|d-1>>>, with a
    grade-<math|d> chart at level <math|i<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>d> and exact
    radius <math|s<rsub|n<rsub|d>>>. Induction proves the claims;
    <math|i<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>d> implies <math|i<rsub|d>\<to\>\<infty\>>.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Actual charts at prescribed radii><label|prop:c14-prescribed-chart-supply>Fix
    the common funded family of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:trace-joint-chart-purchase>,
    with its rank and root-ticket requests included before selecting the
    history. Fix its extracted smooth table and one refining ultrafilter.
    Then every positive null sequence has the one-stage selection property
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-one-stage>. Its charts have the bounded lifts,
    unit, reality, signed covariance, dimension and uniform short-test
    receipts of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-selected-trace-gallery>.
    The family, table and ultrafilter do not depend on the null sequence.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Fix a finite request: grades and arities at most <math|d>, a compact
    configuration window, and positive error <math|\<varepsilon\>>. Use all
    three panels of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:trace-joint-chart-purchase>,
    with the same reading through grade <math|d>. Their available levels
    contain a tail, uniformly in the common offset
    <math|t\<in\><around|[|0,1|]>>. The choices of exponents in
    <eqref|eq:three-checks> give strictly negative error exponents. Adding
    <math|t> to all three panel arcs leaves their differences unchanged and
    can only improve the terms with the negative principal arc. Thus the
    estimates of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:three-panel-weak-comparison>
    are uniform in <math|t>. We also check the cell widths when the window
    crosses a stage boundary. Write <math|H=\<cal-M\><rsub|k>> and let
    <math|L<rsub|max>> be the larger of its two adjacent block lengths. The
    paid monitor bound is <math|L<rsub|max>\<leqslant\>H>. Maximality of the
    middle and root levels gives

    <\equation*>
      <frac|a<rsub|\<ell\>>|a<rsub|k>>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|max>*H<rsup|b+t+2>,<space|2em><frac|a<rsub|w>|a<rsub|\<ell\>>>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|max>*c<rsub|k><rsup|10>.
    </equation*>

    At large levels <math|c<rsub|k><rsup|10>\<leqslant\>H>, so
    <math|a<rsub|w>/a<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|b+t+5>>. One predecessor step
    costs at most another factor <math|H>, giving
    <math|a<rsub|w-1>/a<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|b+t+6>>. The smaller
    adjacent block length tends to infinity, so
    <math|6*m*a<rsub|w>\<less\>a<rsub|w-1>> eventually for every arity
    <math|m\<leqslant\>d>. These are precisely the two cell-width estimates
    used for the bulk panel; the same calculation for the derivative panel
    gives exponent <math|e+t+5>. Thus the three-panel argument applies also
    across the actual two-stage window, without requiring a constant block
    length there. Take the maximum over the finitely many basis words of
    length at most <math|d>. Its finite-basis extension gives, on a single
    level tail, error at most <math|\<varepsilon\><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*<big|prod><rsub|j><around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>>
    for every test supported in the requested window. This does not require a
    common finite-volume tail over all tests: each tested upper limit passes
    to the same fixed ultrafilter, and the resulting bound is uniform in the
    test.

    The finite-volume inputs here are actual inputs on this history. The
    joint purchase supplies the mixed-grade marked comparisons and common
    stamp for the panels; Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:rank-selected-tested-readout>
    supplies their reading. The whole-window lawful history is
    Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:rank-trace-all-levels>. For the additional
    rounded two-point comparison, Corollary<nbsp><reference|prop:trace-selected-rounded-receipt>
    supplies the root receipt at this very chart's root depth. Its exponent
    and ticket choices are made in the same finite menu, before realizing the
    calendar. Thus neither the short-test estimate nor the rounded comparison
    selects a second background history.

    The lift bound can be checked directly. At a fixed admitted level
    <math|k>, the reference inverse bound and half-accuracy give
    <math|<around|\||v|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*D<rsub|k>*<around|\||T<rsub|d<rprime|'>>*v|\|><rsub|1>>.
    Therefore the corrected lift obeys

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||X<rsub|N,p>*e|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*D<rsub|k>*<around|\||S<rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|-1>*W<rsub|m>*e|\|><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|m=\<cal-M\><rsub|k><rsup|b+t>> lies in a fixed compact
    interval at this fixed <math|k>. Also
    <math|\<sigma\><rsup|-1>=\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k><rsup|1/2>*\<theta\><rsub|k>>
    is uniformly bounded by the endpoint running estimate at <math|k>. The
    two diagonal scalings have only finitely many degrees through <math|d>.
    Their operator norms are therefore bounded independently of the terminal
    cutoff and period, on the admitted late set. There are finitely many
    grade maps, so their tuple has a finite bound in the lift space. No bound
    uniform in <math|k> is needed for this compactness step. The unit and
    real identities follow from the same identities for the reading and the
    two diagonal scalings. Signed equivariance follows from
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:trace-quantitative-invoice> and the signed
    stamp construction. These are identities of the chosen lifts before
    taking their ultralimits. The dimension bound is the quotient identity
    <eqref|eq:finite-rank-quotient-isomorphism>, on the same refined
    extraction; the free separating tests are those of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-all-grade-free-detector>.

    Enlarge the level floor to include all the preceding finite requests and
    any prescribed lower level. Clock-gap interpolation then gives
    <math|r\<gtr\>0> such that every <math|0\<less\>s\<leqslant\>r> is the
    exact radius of one of these charts. Given a positive null sequence and a
    previous index, choose a later term below <math|min
    <around|(|r,\<theta\>|)>>, with strict inequality to <math|\<theta\>>.
    This is the one-stage property. Repeat with error
    <math|<around|(|d+1|)><rsup|-1>> and expanding compact windows, using the
    strict-selection lemma. At each fixed cutoff the lift tuple has its limit
    along the already fixed ultrafilter; there is no new extraction at this
    step. This proves all the asserted receipts with their stated order of
    quantifiers.
  </proof>

  The one-step chart theorem obtains this output by combining the clock-gap
  interpolation with the funded chart family. Its quantitative comparison and
  station estimates are the three-panel weak-comparison result in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:three-panel-weak-comparison>; we use its
  conclusion here without duplicating those estimates.

  Here is the finite-level extraction used to pass from the charts to the
  limiting fields. Let <math|F> be an extraction order, let <math|\<cal-U\>>
  be an ultrafilter on period-phase pairs finer than the fused filter of
  <math|F>, and fix a cutoff <math|D>. Suppose <math|X<rsub|N,p>> is a tuple
  of linear maps, one for each grade <math|0,\<ldots\>,D>, from the
  corresponding reference field into the common finite-period coefficient
  space. Assume <math|A\<geqslant\>0> and, on an <math|F>-late set,

  <\equation>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|N,p>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>A.<label|eq:ex-lattice-firesky-lift-bound>
  </equation>

  The finite product of these map spaces is finite-dimensional. The closed
  ball of radius <math|A> is compact. Compactness gives a proper subfilter
  limit of the bounded tuple of maps. This subfilter is finer than
  <math|\<cal-U\>>; ultrafilter maximality identifies it with
  <math|\<cal-U\>>. Thus the tuple converges along <math|\<cal-U\>> to
  <math|Y=<around|(|Y<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,Y<rsub|D>|)>>. This is the only
  compactness step: it is finite-dimensional and is performed at each fixed
  cutoff.

  If the finite-level maps preserve the real involution and signed actions on
  the late set, continuity of those actions shows that each <math|Y<rsub|d>>
  preserves them. If they send the reference unit to the scalar unit
  eventually, the same limit identity gives <math|Y<rsub|d><around|(|1|)>=1>.
  Compose with the quotient projection to define the table map

  <\equation*>
    U<rsub|d>=<math-ss|P><rsub|d>Y<rsub|d>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>d\<leqslant\>D.
  </equation*>

  This order matters: the finite maps converge in the coefficient space, and
  the table-valued maps are obtained by applying the continuous projection
  after taking the limit.

  For a fixed tuple of <math|n> labels of grades at most <math|D>, a fixed
  smooth test function supported in the allowed window, and <math|n> no
  greater than the available arity cap, the finite-level receipt bounds the
  paid test error by <math|\<epsilon\>> times the label charge and the test
  supremum. To pass this estimate to the quotient table, use the
  ordered-moment limit rather than continuity of an integral whose
  finite-volume measure varies. For each fixed tuple of coefficient vectors,
  the ordered read receipt gives convergence of the corresponding
  finite-volume moment to the table moment. The moment is multilinear in its
  <math|n> label vectors, and each label space is finite-dimensional.
  Expanding each moving vector in a fixed basis expresses the moment as a
  finite sum of products of convergent coordinates and convergent fixed-basis
  moments. Hence the moving-label moment converges to the table moment. This
  is the finite-basis multilinear limit argument; it uses the common ordered
  extraction and the fixed compactly supported test. The inequality passes to
  the limit because a closed interval is closed under limits. More
  explicitly, for each <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0> the late-set estimate is at
  most the claimed bound plus <math|\<eta\>>; taking the limit and then
  letting <math|\<eta\>*\<downarrow\>*0> removes the slack. This proves the
  cutoff-level short-test estimate with no exchange of an infinite sum and a
  limit. It is the same moment-passage mechanism as the calibrated-smearing
  lemma: fixed-vector convergence is supplied by the ordered table, and
  finite dimensionality handles the moving vectors.

  Apply this extraction separately at cutoff <math|D=d> to the chart chosen
  at grade <math|d> and level <math|i<rsub|d>>. The chart receipt supplies a
  bound on its finite lifts, exact reality and signed covariance, the exact
  unit, and a short-test error for arity <math|n\<leqslant\>d> with
  coefficient <math|1/<around|(|d+1|)>>. Projecting the limits gives panels
  <math|U<rsub|d>>. For every fixed finite label tuple, its grades are at
  most <math|d> eventually along the sequence of cutoffs; the support of a
  fixed compact test fits the expanding chart window eventually; and
  <math|1/<around|(|d+1|)>\<to\>0>. Write
  <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|r,U>*<around|(|v;t|)>> for the tested integral of the
  tuple <math|v> under radius <math|r>, panel family <math|U>, and test
  function <math|t>. Write <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|0>*<around|(|v;t|)>> for the
  corresponding reference test value. Hence

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-T\><rsub|s<rsub|n<rsub|d>>,U<rsub|d>>*<around|(|v;t|)>-\<cal-T\><rsub|0>*<around|(|v;t|)>\<longrightarrow\>0
  </equation*>

  for every fixed tuple <math|v> and test <math|t>. This is the tested
  approach property. The argument is for each fixed test; it does not assert
  a uniform tail over all tests or all label tuples.

  The final chart isomorphisms follow grade by grade. For a fixed grade
  <math|d>, the reference tests separate its finite-dimensional field: every
  nonzero vector has a nonzero separated two-point evaluation. To obtain a
  smooth test, keep the spectator insertion fixed and choose a separated pair
  where the complex value is nonzero. Multiply the spectator by a unit
  complex phase so that the real part of the value is positive. Continuity
  away from the diagonal gives a small neighborhood on which that real part
  remains positive. Integrating against a nonnegative smooth bump of integral
  one, supported in that neighborhood, gives a smooth smeared test with
  nonzero value. Thus smooth smeared reference tests also separate. Normalize
  to the unit sphere. For each point of that compact sphere, continuity gives
  a neighborhood on which one such test has absolute value at least half its
  value at the point. A finite subcover gives tests
  <math|t<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,t<rsub|m>> and a constant
  <math|\<alpha\>\<gtr\>0> such that every unit vector has
  <math|max<rsub|j><around|\||\<cal-T\><rsub|0>*<around|(|v;t<rsub|j>|)>|\|>\<geqslant\>\<alpha\>>.
  The fixed finite set of label charges and test suprema is bounded on that
  sphere. The tested approach estimate therefore makes all corresponding
  errors less than <math|\<alpha\>/2> for sufficiently large <math|k>. If the
  projected grade map killed a unit vector, all its tested values would
  vanish, contradicting this lower bound. Thus it is injective. The dimension
  bound from the selected table gives the reverse dimension inequality;
  therefore the two dimensions agree and the map is bijective. This tail may
  depend on <math|d>: there is no single finite index after which every grade
  is simultaneously bijective. For the finitely many indices before the
  grade-<math|d> tail, choose any isomorphism using the same dimension
  equality. This defines a scaling at every index. For each fixed grade, the
  unit, reality, and signed symmetry identities hold on its eventual
  isomorphism tail; the arbitrary isomorphisms used earlier need not preserve
  those identities. The reference test-separation input here is the all-grade
  free-detector result of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-all-grade-free-detectors>;
  the separate low-grade trace calculation is not enough to justify
  separation in every grade. The reviewed corrected-lift argument in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-producer> provides the
  compatible panel maps when required by the trace family.

  <subsubsection|The gallery handoff>

  The preceding argument proves the scale-selection, finite-cutoff
  extraction, tested-approach, and fixed-grade eventual-isomorphism clauses.
  It does not by itself prove the complete gallery statement. That handoff
  also needs the physical-array receipts in the selected-run transfer. For
  the same ultrafilter, hierarchy, and scaling these include the following.
  Let <math|b<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> be the selected arch scale at the <math|i>th
  postselected level, so that <math|b<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>; let <math|1>
  and <math|\<cal-O\>> be the unit and the normalized curvature-square field
  of the reference grade-four table, and <math|d<rsub|2>> the physical
  coefficient, as in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-scribe-producer>.
  For a comparison table <math|T>, write <math|\<Theta\><rsub|T>> for the
  scalar trace of the physical stress centered by its one-point value in
  <math|T>, and <math|U<rsub|i,4>> for the grade-four chart from the
  reference table to the selected table on its eventual isomorphism tail. The
  receipts are: convergence of the recentered physical stress to its
  prescribed principal material tensor; for every comparison table that obeys
  the table hierarchy laws and respects the selected table, real sequences
  <math|d<rsub|i>,e<rsub|i>> such that, eventually in <math|i>, the centered
  scalar trace satisfies

  <\equation*>
    r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*U<rsub|i,4><rsup|-1>*\<Theta\><rsub|T>=e<rsub|i>*<space|0.17em>1+d<rsub|i>*<space|0.17em><around|(|4*\<cal-O\>|)>,<space|2em>b<rsub|i>*d<rsub|i>\<longrightarrow\>d<rsub|2>,<space|2em>b<rsub|i>*e<rsub|i>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|d<rsub|i>,e<rsub|i>\<to\>0> themselves. The final receipt is,
  for every comparison table, convergence of
  <math|b<rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*U<rsub|i,4><rsup|-1>*\<Theta\><rsub|T>>
  to <math|4*d<rsub|2>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-O\>>. The second direction
  <math|4*\<cal-O\>> is the trace of the true-branch material tensor; the
  limit of the zoomed observable is a multiple of <math|\<cal-O\>>, not of
  the unit. The arbitrary-null- sequence case needs these same receipts after
  the selected scaling has been constructed above. They are additional
  physical and field-array estimates, not consequences of finite-dimensional
  compactness or of the rank-one solve alone. The correction, one-dimensional
  kernel, stable inverse, flux normalization, and physical/reference shell
  comparison in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-producer>
  remain conditional on their displayed analytic receipts.

  In the selected public construction, the one-scale chart family comes from
  the paid multi-scale request and its subsequent chart staging. This section
  proves the scale-selection and tested-chart clauses of that assembly; the
  physical-array, stress, and flux receipts needed for the full gallery
  remain separate inputs.

  <subsection|One conserved stress tensor on the comparison
  curve><label|sec:actual-stress-normalization>

  For lattice gauge theory precedent, see the construction of a lattice
  energy\Umomentum tensor by Caracciolo, Curci, Menotti and Pelissetto
  <cite|CaraccioloEtAl90>. We establish the normalization used here on the
  particular continuum field quotient produced by the Wilson limit.

  The reference stress tensor must keep its prescribed sign as the requested
  grades, derivative orders and configuration windows increase. We construct
  one physical tensor and one comparison curve with that property. The only
  subsequence taken below is a subsequence of the comparison parameter. The
  physical correlation table, and the cutoff extraction that produced it,
  remain fixed.

  Write <math|E<rsub|d>> for the reference grade-<math|d> field space and
  <math|F<rsub|d>> for the physical one. Both have their given units,
  conjugations, signed coordinate actions and inclusions between grades.
  Their derivative maps are <math|\<partial\><rsup|0><rsub|d,a>:E<rsub|d>\<to\>E<rsub|d+1>>
  and <math|\<partial\><rsup|1><rsub|d,a>:F<rsub|d>\<to\>F<rsub|d+1>>, where
  <math|a\<in\><around|{|0,1,2,3|}>>. Both correlation hierarchies are
  smooth, multilinear and faithful: a field whose separated tested
  correlations with every spectator word vanish is zero. Their units have
  expectation one and act as scalar insertions. We use the exact derivative
  identities for separated correlations, with derivative maps that annihilate
  units and respect reality and the signed coordinate action. All these
  spaces are finite-dimensional. A comparison curve consists of
  complex-linear isomorphisms <math|U<rsub|k,d>:E<rsub|d>\<to\>F<rsub|d>> and
  positive radii <math|r<rsub|k>> strictly decreasing to zero. For each fixed
  grade, the maps eventually preserve the units, conjugations and signed
  actions; for each fixed pair of grades they eventually commute with the
  inclusions. These are the corrected comparison maps constructed in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-producer>.

  We retain the strength of their test estimate explicitly. If
  <math|\<b-v\>=<around|(|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|n>|)>> is any fixed
  tuple of reference fields, put

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|k,\<b-v\>><around|(|x|)>=S<rsup|1><rsub|U<rsub|k>*\<b-v\>>*<around|(|r<rsub|k>*x|)>-S<rsup|0><rsub|\<b-v\>><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  For every compact <math|Q\<subset\><math-up|Conf><rsub|n><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)>>
  and <math|M\<gtr\>0>, the estimate is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:stress-uniform-tests>sup<rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>>,<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>f\<subset\>Q>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>M>>>>>><around*|\||<big|int>f<around|(|x|)>*K<rsub|k,\<b-v\>><around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x|\|>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation>

  The kernels are smooth on configuration space. Estimate
  <eqref|eq:stress-uniform-tests> for the completed comparison maps
  <math|U<rsub|k,d>> is the transfer in the <hlink|completion and
  uniform-test paragraph|#par:ex-trace-completed-uniform-tests> of
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-uniform-tests> for the corrected maps
  <math|C<rsub|k,d>> in Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-selected-trace-gallery>.
  For the fixed finite tuple, <math|U> and <math|C> agree on one eventual
  grade tail independent of the test, so their suprema agree there. The
  short-test bound <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-test-error> depends on <math|M>
  and the labels, not on derivatives of the test, and the finite-slot
  expansion of the correction preserves it. Thus we use the uniform
  short-test receipt, not merely convergence for each individual test.

  <subsubsection|Derivative transport and convergence on compact sets>

  Define the transported derivative

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:stress-transported-derivative>A<rsub|k,d,a>=r<rsub|k>*U<rsub|k,d+1><rsup|-1>*\<partial\><rsup|1><rsub|d,a>*U<rsub|k,d>.
  </equation>

  It converges in operator norm to <math|\<partial\><rsup|0><rsub|d,a>>. Here
  is the finite-dimensional argument, including why its argument need not be
  bounded in advance. Choose finitely many tested reference correlations
  which detect <math|E<rsub|d+1>>, as in the separating-test argument of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-producer>. Let
  <math|L:E<rsub|d+1>\<to\>\<bbb-C\><rsup|q>> be their injective measurement
  map and <math|L<rsub|k>> the same measurements in the scaled physical
  table. On a finite basis, <eqref|eq:stress-uniform-tests> gives
  <math|L<rsub|k>\<to\>L> in operator norm. Compactness of the unit sphere
  gives <math|c\<gtr\>0> with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|L*v|\<\|\|\>>\<geqslant\>c<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>>,
  and hence eventually <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|L<rsub|k>*v|\<\|\|\>>\<geqslant\><around|(|c/2|)><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>>.
  For each fixed <math|v\<in\>E<rsub|d>>, the chain rule and the derivative
  identity express each component of <math|L<rsub|k>*A<rsub|k,d,a>*v> as a
  correlation tested against minus the corresponding derivative of the
  original test. Integration by parts has no boundary term because the test
  has compact support inside configuration space. The approach estimate
  therefore gives

  <\equation*>
    L<rsub|k>*A<rsub|k,d,a>*v\<longrightarrow\>L*\<partial\><rsup|0><rsub|d,a>*v.
  </equation*>

  Subtract <math|L<rsub|k>*\<partial\><rsup|0><rsub|d,a>*v>, which has the
  same limit, and use the lower bound for <math|L<rsub|k>>. It follows that
  <math|A<rsub|k,d,a>*v\<to\>\<partial\><rsup|0><rsub|d,a>*v>. Checking a
  finite basis proves the claimed operator-norm convergence.

  The approach estimate also holds when each label
  <math|v<rsub|i><around|(|k|)>> converges to <math|v<rsub|i>>. Expand the
  labels in fixed finite bases. The difference is a finite sum of bounded
  coefficients multiplying fixed-tuple errors, plus coefficients tending to
  zero multiplying fixed reference kernels. The same compact integral bound
  proves <eqref|eq:stress-uniform-tests> for this difference. Differentiating
  one position replaces its label by <math|A<rsub|k,d,a>*v<rsub|i><around|(|k|)>>,
  which converges to <math|\<partial\><rsup|0><rsub|d,a>*v<rsub|i>>.
  Consequently this class of differences is closed under any fixed number of
  position derivatives.

  We give the compact convergence upgrade used here. Let <math|h<rsub|k>> be
  any such difference, let <math|\<chi\>> be a smooth compactly supported
  cutoff inside configuration space, and use the Fourier convention
  <math|<wide|g|^><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|-2*\<pi\>*i*\<xi\>\<cdot\>x>*g<around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x>.
  The tests <math|\<chi\><around|(|x|)>*e<rsup|-2*\<pi\>*i*\<xi\>\<cdot\>x>>
  have one common support and supremum bound.
  Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:stress-uniform-tests> therefore yields

  <\equation*>
    sup<rsub|\<xi\>><around|\||<wide|\<chi\>*h<rsub|k>|^><around|(|\<xi\>|)>|\|>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  For any fixed coordinate <math|a> and integer <math|b\<geqslant\>0>,
  integration by parts and the product rule give

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|2*\<pi\>*i*\<xi\><rsub|a>|)><rsup|b><wide|\<chi\>*h<rsub|k>|^><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|b><choose|b|j><wide|<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a><rsup|b-j>*\<chi\>|)>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a><rsup|j>*h<rsub|k>|)>|^><around|(|\<xi\>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Each term on the right tends uniformly to zero by derivative closure. In
  the dimension <math|D=4*n\<gtr\>0>, choose <math|b\<gtr\>D> and a
  coordinate with <math|<around|\||\<xi\><rsub|a>|\|>\<geqslant\><around|\||\<xi\>|\|>/<sqrt|D>>.
  Combining the finitely many coordinate bounds with the unweighted bound
  gives numbers <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|k>\<to\>0> such that

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||<wide|\<chi\>*h<rsub|k>|^><around|(|\<xi\>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|b>*\<epsilon\><rsub|k>*<around|(|1+<around|\||\<xi\>|\|>|)><rsup|-b>.
  </equation*>

  The right side is integrable. Fourier inversion for the smooth compactly
  supported function <math|\<chi\>*h<rsub|k>> gives
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<chi\>*h<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<to\>0>.
  Given a compact set in configuration space, choose <math|\<chi\>=1> on a
  neighborhood of it. Repeat the argument with every coordinate derivative of
  a fixed order; finite dimensionality then gives convergence in the
  corresponding derivative norm, uniformly on the compact set. At arity zero
  the space of configurations is a point and the assertion is the scalar
  approach estimate. We have proved compact convergence of all fixed jets,
  including at coordinate ties that are not collisions.

  <subsubsection|The physical conserved line>

  Let <math|K<rsup|0>> and <math|K<rsup|1>> be the real spaces of centered,
  symmetric, real, signed-covariant grade-four stress arrays in the reference
  and physical tables. Centering means that every entry has expectation zero.
  Let <math|V<rsup|0>,V<rsup|1>> be the spaces of real, signed-covariant
  grade-five vector arrays, without imposing a centering condition, and write

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|D<rsup|i>*B|)><rsub|a>=<big|sum><rsub|b>\<partial\><rsup|i><rsub|4,b>*B<rsub|b*a>*<space|2em><around|(|i=0,1|)>
  </equation*>

  for divergence. Assume the fixed reference tensor <math|J<rsup|0>> belongs
  to <math|K<rsup|0>>, is nonzero and conserved, and that <math|D<rsup|0>> is
  onto with <math|ker D<rsup|0>=\<bbb-R\>*J<rsup|0>>. These are the reference
  rank and normalization requirements; the low-grade rank calculation is
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-free-rank>. The actual
  reference Ward normalization must be supplied for this same
  <math|J<rsup|0>>.

  For all sufficiently large <math|k>, the centered map

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|k>*B=<math-up|center><rsub|1><around|(|U<rsub|k,4>*B|)>:K<rsup|0>\<longrightarrow\>K<rsup|1>
  </equation*>

  is an isomorphism. Its inverse is <math|B\<mapsto\><math-up|center><rsub|0><around|(|U<rsub|k,4><rsup|-1>*B|)>>:
  the two scalar unit corrections cancel exactly, by the calculation in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-producer>. Unit
  preservation is essential; the two expectation functionals need not agree
  under <math|U<rsub|k>>. The componentwise map
  <math|V<rsub|k>:V<rsup|0>\<to\>V<rsup|1>> induced by <math|U<rsub|k,5>> is
  also an isomorphism. Define

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|k>=V<rsub|k><rsup|-1>*r<rsub|k>*D<rsup|1>*C<rsub|k>:K<rsup|0>\<longrightarrow\>V<rsup|0>.
  </equation*>

  Derivatives annihilate scalar units, so
  <math|<around|(|B<rsub|k>*B|)><rsub|a>=<big|sum><rsub|b>A<rsub|k,4,b>*B<rsub|b*a>>.
  Thus <math|B<rsub|k>\<to\>D<rsup|0>> in operator norm. Choose a linear
  right inverse <math|R:V<rsup|0>\<to\>K<rsup|0>> of the surjection
  <math|D<rsup|0>>. Then <math|B<rsub|k>*R\<to\>I>. For large <math|k>,
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsub|k>*R-I|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>1/2>, so
  <math|B<rsub|k>*R> is injective and hence bijective in its
  finite-dimensional space. Therefore <math|B<rsub|k>> is onto. Conjugating
  by <math|C<rsub|k>,V<rsub|k>> and dividing by <math|r<rsub|k>\<gtr\>0>
  shows that <math|D<rsup|1>> is onto. The two pairs of spaces have equal
  dimensions, so rank\Unullity gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:stress-physical-line>dim<rsub|\<bbb-R\>> ker D<rsup|1>=1.
  </equation>

  <subsubsection|Fixing the normalization and sign once>

  Choose any <math|0\<ne\>B\<in\>ker D<rsup|1>>. In the full reference
  stress-array space, including scalar directions, put

  <\equation*>
    b<rsub|k>=U<rsub|k,4><rsup|-1>*<around|(|r<rsub|k><rsup|4>*B|)>,<space|2em>\<xi\><rsub|k>=<frac|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>|<around|\<\|\|\>|J<rsup|0>|\<\|\|\>>>,<space|2em><wide|B|~><rsub|k>=\<xi\><rsub|k><rsup|-1>*b<rsub|k>.
  </equation*>

  Invertibility and <math|r<rsub|k>\<gtr\>0> imply
  <math|\<xi\><rsub|k>\<gtr\>0>; no uniform lower bound on
  <math|\<xi\><rsub|k>> is needed. We have
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<wide|B|~><rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>=<around|\<\|\|\>|J<rsup|0>|\<\|\|\>>>.
  Compactness of this sphere supplies a strictly increasing subsequence on
  which <math|<wide|B|~><rsub|k>\<to\>B<rsub|\<ast\>>>, with
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsub|\<ast\>>|\<\|\|\>>=<around|\<\|\|\>|J<rsup|0>|\<\|\|\>>>.
  Restrict <math|U<rsub|k>,r<rsub|k>,\<xi\><rsub|k>> to that subsequence. All
  approach and eventual coherence statements persist.

  The limit <math|B<rsub|\<ast\>>> has the reference symmetry, reality and
  signed covariance, since these are closed linear conditions. Its centering
  requires a separate check. The corresponding physical entry is

  <\equation*>
    U<rsub|k,4>*<wide|B|~><rsub|k,a*b>=\<xi\><rsub|k><rsup|-1>*r<rsub|k><rsup|4>*B<rsub|a*b>,
  </equation*>

  whose expectation is exactly zero for every <math|k>. Apply the
  moving-label compact convergence proved above at arity one and position
  zero. It identifies the limit of these expectations with
  <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|0><around|(|B<rsub|\<ast\>,a*b>|)>>, proving that
  <math|B<rsub|\<ast\>>\<in\>K<rsup|0>>. Moreover the exact physical
  conservation law gives

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|b>A<rsub|k,4,b>*<wide|B|~><rsub|k,b*a>=\<xi\><rsub|k><rsup|-1>*r<rsub|k><rsup|5>*U<rsub|k,5><rsup|-1><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|b>\<partial\><rsup|1><rsub|4,b>*B<rsub|b*a>|)>=0.
  </equation*>

  Operator-norm convergence of <math|A<rsub|k,4,b>> and convergence of the
  balanced arrays imply <math|D<rsup|0>*B<rsub|\<ast\>>=0>. Thus
  <math|B<rsub|\<ast\>>=c*J<rsup|0>> for some real <math|c>. Equality of the
  nonzero norms gives <math|<around|\||c|\|>=1>. Choose <math|J=B> if
  <math|c=1> and <math|J=-B> if <math|c=-1>. This one choice gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:stress-fixed-normalization>U<rsub|k,4><rsup|-1>*<around|(|\<xi\><rsub|k><rsup|-1>*r<rsub|k><rsup|4>*J<rsub|a*b>|)>\<longrightarrow\>J<rsup|0><rsub|a*b>*<space|2em><around|(|a,b=0,1,2,3|)>.
  </equation>

  The physical tensor <math|J> is nonzero, centered and conserved. The
  reference tensor has not been rescaled or changed to accommodate later
  requests.

  <subsubsection|All finite requests use tails of this curve>

  Fix an arity bound <math|N>, a grade bound <math|d>, a derivative order
  <math|m> and <math|R\<geqslant\>1>. For <math|n\<leqslant\>N>, use the
  compact window

  <\equation*>
    Q<rsub|n,R>=<around|{|x:<around|\||x|\|>\<leqslant\>R,<around|\||x<rsub|i>-x<rsub|j>|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>\<geqslant\>R<rsup|-1><text|
    for >i\<ne\>j|}>.
  </equation*>

  Choose a single tail on which the exact symmetry, unit and inclusion
  identities hold through grade <math|max <around|(|d,4|)>>. There are only
  finitely many grades and pairs involved. On this tail the compact
  convergence argument gives, for every fixed tuple of grades at most
  <math|d> and every <math|j\<leqslant\>m>, convergence of its <math|j>th
  derivative uniformly on <math|Q<rsub|n,R>>. The tolerance threshold may
  depend on the tuple; no uniform bound over all labels is asserted.
  Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:stress-fixed-normalization> holds on the same tail.
  Increasing the finite request only requires another tail of this single
  curve, with the same <math|J,J<rsup|0>> and positive normalization
  sequence. This proves the coherent comparison needed before the annular
  Ward identities are applied. The uniform test approach, exact derivative
  laws and reference conserved-line certificate remain its stated inputs; the
  normalization argument does not supply their analytic construction.

  <subsection|Finite-volume stress shell nodes><label|sec:ex-lattice-actual-stress-shell-nodes>

  This section records the actual finite-volume producer used by the stress
  annulus argument. It follows the selected family and its retained history.
  The scale choice is made before the reference opening, insertion labels, or
  finite list of tests. The quantitative conclusion at a fixed collar site is
  then obtained from a two-channel coefficient construction and the finite
  quota-to-norm comparison.

  Write <math|a<rsub|i>> for the physical spacing,
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>=log <around|(|a<rsub|i><rsup|-1>|)>>,
  <math|B<rsub|i>> for the support radius, and
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>> for the effective coupling. Put

  <\equation*>
    <\aligned>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|r<rsub|m>>|<cell|=128*B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>*a<rsub|m+1>,>|<cell|c<rsub|m>>|<cell|=exp
      <around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|m>/32|)>,>>|<row|<cell|X<rsub|m>>|<cell|=exp
      <sqrt|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>>,>|<cell|n<rsub|m>>|<cell|=<text|the
      number of columns at level >m.>>>>
    </aligned>
  </equation*>

  For a fixed finite-volume law, let <math|\<cal-O\><rsub|N,q>*<around|(|L,x|)>>
  be the readout of label <math|L> at site <math|x>. For an opened reference
  level <math|p<rsub|0>>, let <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|N,q><around|(|p<rsub|0>|)>>
  be the finite cell index set of the centered chamber tiling used by the
  root test, and write <math|F<rsub|v>> for the prescribed translated copy of
  <math|F> at <math|v\<in\>\<cal-C\><rsub|N,q><around|(|p<rsub|0>|)>>. The
  copy includes complex conjugation exactly when the cell has odd reflection
  parity. Set

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-shell-root-functional>\<cal-R\><rsub|N,q,p<rsub|0>><around|(|F|)>=<around*|\||\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<beta\>,N,q>*<big|prod><rsub|v\<in\>\<cal-C\><rsub|N,q><around|(|p<rsub|0>|)>>F<rsub|v>|\|><rsup|1/<around|\||\<cal-C\><rsub|N,q><around|(|p<rsub|0>|)>|\|>>.
  </equation>

  This is the root-test functional of that opening, not a product over every
  fine-lattice site. The grade-<math|D> quota at birth level <math|m> is
  denoted <math|\<frak-q\><rsub|D,m,N,q><around|(|z|)>>. It is a coefficient
  size in the fixed reference frame; it is not the norm in the continuum
  field space. The opening <math|p<rsub|0>> selects the root-test chamber and
  its stake bound; the quota index is <math|m>.

  For grades <math|d,D,h\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> satisfying

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-shell-grade>D+h=d+4,
  </equation>

  and labels <math|B> of grade <math|d> and <math|T> of grade four, define
  the finite fragment

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-shell-fragment>\<cal-F\><rsub|m,x><rsup|N,q><around|(|z|)>=r<rsub|m><rsup|h>*\<cal-O\><rsub|N,q>*<around|(|T,x<rsub|m,N,q>|)>*\<cal-O\><rsub|N,q>*<around|(|B,0|)>-\<cal-O\><rsub|N,q>*<around|(|z,0|)>,<space|2em>z<text|
    of grade >D.
  </equation>

  Here <math|x<rsub|m,N,q>> is the image of the fixed cover anchor <math|x>
  under the volume-period placement at level <math|j<rsub|m>>. The factor is
  <math|r<rsub|m><rsup|h>>, where <math|r<rsub|m>=128*B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>*a<rsub|m+1>>;
  it is not <math|a<rsub|m+1><rsup|h>>. Identity
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-shell-grade> makes both terms have the same scaling
  power (the labels themselves lie in filtered grade spaces). Write
  <math|<math-up|cs><rsub|q><around|(|v|)>> for the coefficient size of a
  grade-<math|q> label in the selected frame.

  <subsubsection|The scale schedule and its numerical gates>

  Fix a simple compact gauge group, the selected Wilson family, and its
  same-history data. We assume the following shell-schedule input for each
  fixed triple <math|<around|(|d,D,h|)>> satisfying
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-shell-grade>:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-shell-schedule>t=1+<frac|1|64*<around|(|d+12|)>>,<space|2em>j<rsub|m>\<longrightarrow\>\<infty\>,<space|2em><frac|\<lambda\><rsub|j<rsub|m>>|\<lambda\><rsub|m>>\<longrightarrow\>t.
  </equation>

  The sequence is chosen independently of the reference opening and all
  labels. The strict rate condition is

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|d+12|)>*<around|(|1-t|)>=-<frac|1|64>\<gtr\>-<frac|1|32>.
  </equation*>

  The separate lightness estimate gives, for every fixed opening
  <math|p<rsub|0>>, eventually in <math|m>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-shell-lightness>32*r<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|p<rsub|0>>,<space|2em>a<rsub|m+1>*<around*|(|B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>+1+2\<cdot\>1024*B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>|)>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|p<rsub|0>>.
  </equation>

  The first inequality supplies the collar margin. The second supplies the
  number of centered chamber cells needed by the finite stencil.

  Here are the channel prices in ordinary notation. Write
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|i>> for the renal allowance, <math|\<phi\><rsub|i>>
  for the near allowance, and <math|s<rsub|i>> for the support exponent. Set

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|i>=<around|(|2\<cdot\>1024*B<rsub|i>+1|)><rsup|4>,<space|2em><math-up|krill><rsub|j,i><around|(|u|)>=<frac|u|16><around*|(|<frac|a<rsub|j>|a<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|4>.
  </equation*>

  The quantity <math|q<rsub|i>> is the exponential-count parameter; it is
  distinct from the geometric radius <math|1024*B<rsub|i><rsup|2>> used in
  the finite stencil. Let <math|g<rsub|q>> be the largest factor degree among
  the vectors of the selected grade-<math|q> frame. This is the exponent of
  the threshold dilation; the mesh normalization still uses the engineering
  grade <math|q>. The paid grade-<math|q> weight for a label-size bound
  <math|v\<gtr\>0> is

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|w><rsub|q><around|(|v,j|)>=<frac|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j><rsup|-<around|(|g<rsub|q>+4|)>>|v*<space|0.17em>C<rsub|j>*<big|prod><rsub|\<ell\>\<less\>j>C<rsub|\<ell\>+1>>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|C<rsub|i>> is the column count. Set

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|->=a<rsub|j<rsub|m>><rsup|d><math-ss|w><rsub|d><around|(|a,j<rsub|m>|)>,<space|2em>A<rsub|+>=r<rsub|m><rsup|-h>*a<rsub|j<rsub|m>><rsup|4><math-ss|w><rsub|4><around|(|b,j<rsub|m>|)>.
  </equation*>

  For channel weights <math|A<rsub|->,A<rsub|+>>, define

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-shell-picket>\<rho\><rsub|m,\<pm\>>=<frac|\<delta\><rsub|m+1><rsup|2><math-up|krill><rsub|j<rsub|m>,m+2><around|(|\<delta\><rsub|j<rsub|m>>*\<phi\><rsub|j<rsub|m>>*A<rsub|\<pm\>>|)>|exp
    <around|(|<around|(|s<rsub|m+1>+1/2|)>*q<rsub|m+1>|)>>,<space|2em>W<rsub|m>=\<rho\><rsub|m,->*\<rho\><rsub|m,+>.
  </equation>

  This follows from the scalar exponential weight in each channel at
  <math|i=m+1>: both outer and inner renal factors are
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|m+1>>, and the input also contains the near allowance
  <math|\<phi\><rsub|j<rsub|m>>>. The channel weights <math|A<rsub|->> and
  <math|A<rsub|+>> are the paid prices for the grade-<math|d> and grade-four
  insertions; the latter includes <math|r<rsub|m><rsup|-h>>, cancelled by
  <math|r<rsub|m><rsup|h>> in the fragment. Thus <math|W<rsub|m>> is a
  positive product fixed by the displayed formulas, not a free normalization
  parameter.

  For a positive function <math|w> on channel names, define its marched price
  from level <math|k> by

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|<math-ss|M><rsub|k,m>w|)><around|(|i|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|w<around|(|i|)>,>|<cell|m\<less\>k,>>|<row|<cell|w<around|(|i|)>*<big|prod><rsub|s=k><rsup|m-1>\<phi\><rsub|s>,>|<cell|m\<geqslant\>k,>>>>>
  </equation*>

  where <math|0\<less\>\<phi\><rsub|s>\<leqslant\>1> is the selected
  transition allowance. These formulas are the two positive channel prices;
  in particular <math|W<rsub|m>> is not a free normalization parameter. The
  selected price estimate supplies <math|W<rsub|m>\<gtr\>0> and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-shell-march><math-ss|M><rsub|k,m><around|(|c<rsub|m>|)>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|m>*<frac|W<rsub|m>|16*X<rsub|m>>.
  </equation>

  Set

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-shell-coefficient-budget>e<rsub|m>=32*<around|(|1+n<rsub|m>|)>*a<rsub|m+1><rsup|D>*<frac|X<rsub|m>|W<rsub|m>>.
  </equation>

  The second price estimate, with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|a<rsub|k><rsup|-D>|\<\|\|\>>=a<rsub|k><rsup|-D>>
  and <math|g<rsub|m,k>=exp <around|(|<around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|m>-\<lambda\><rsub|k>|)>/8|)>>,
  is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-shell-correction-price>32*<around|(|1+n<rsub|m>|)>*c<rsub|m>*a<rsub|k><rsup|-D>*<frac|e<rsub|m>|g<rsub|m,k>>\<leqslant\><frac|1|2>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|-\<nu\><rsub|D>>,<space|2em>\<nu\><rsub|D>=g<rsub|D>+4.
  </equation>

  Here <math|g<rsub|D>> is the maximal factor degree in the selected
  grade-<math|D> frame. The normalization on the right is the grade-<math|D>
  payment. For fixed <math|<around|(|p<rsub|0>,k|)>> it is independent of
  <math|m>. To expose the rate calculation, for a positive sequence
  <math|f<rsub|m>> write

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|rate><around|(|f|)>=lim<rsub|m\<to\>\<infty\>> <frac|log
    f<rsub|m>|\<lambda\><rsub|m>>
  </equation*>

  when the limit exists. The rate rules for the two channel weights give

  <\equation*>
    M=<around|(|d+12|)>*<around|(|1-t|)>=-<frac|1|64>,<space|1em><math-up|rate><around|(|W<rsub|m>|)>=-D+M.
  </equation*>

  Explicitly, the unscaled grade-<math|d> weight has rate <math|-d*t> and the
  unscaled grade-four weight has rate <math|-4*t+h>, since it is divided by
  <math|r<rsub|m><rsup|h>> and <math|<math-up|rate><around|(|r<rsub|m>|)>=-1>
  by the scale note (see the paragraph after
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-actual-shell-node>). Each
  channel transport contributes <math|4*<around|(|1-t|)>> from its
  fourth-power block ratio. Thus

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|rate><around|(|\<rho\><rsub|m,->|)>=-d*t+4*<around|(|1-t|)>,<space|2em><math-up|rate><around|(|\<rho\><rsub|m,+>|)>=-4*t+h+4*<around|(|1-t|)>,
  </equation*>

  and their sum is <math|h+8-<around|(|d+12|)>*t=-D+<around|(|d+12|)>*<around|(|1-t|)>>,
  using <math|D+h=d+4>. The renal and near allowances have rate zero. The
  explicit bound <math|<around|(|s<rsub|i>+1/2|)>*q<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><sqrt|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1>>>,
  included in the shell-schedule input, shows that the exponential
  denominator has rate zero as well. In the displayed paid width, <math|log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>=o<around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|j>|)>> and
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<ell\>\<less\>j>log
  C<rsub|\<ell\>+1>=o<around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|j>|)>>, so its coupling and
  column factors have rate zero. Also <math|<math-up|rate><around|(|c<rsub|m>|)>=1/32>,
  <math|<math-up|rate><around|(|a<rsub|m+1><rsup|D>|)>=-D> (because
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|m+1>/\<lambda\><rsub|m>\<to\>1>, as shown in the same
  paragraph), and <math|<math-up|rate><around|(|X<rsub|m>|)>=0>, because
  <math|X<rsub|m>=exp <around|(|<sqrt|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>>|)>> is
  subexponential in <math|\<lambda\><rsub|m>>. The finite column census is
  likewise subexponential. The exact definition of <math|e<rsub|m>> therefore
  gives <math|<math-up|rate><around|(|e<rsub|m>|)>=-M=1/64>. In particular
  <math|c<rsub|m><rsup|-1>\<to\>0>, since
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|m>\<to\>\<infty\>>. For fixed <math|k>,
  <math|g<rsub|m,k>=exp <around|(|<around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|m>-\<lambda\><rsub|k>|)>/8|)>>
  has rate <math|1/8>, while <math|a<rsub|k><rsup|-D>> and
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|-\<nu\><rsub|D>>> are fixed positive
  constants. Thus the correction-price left side has rate

  <\equation*>
    <frac|1|32>-M-<frac|1|8>=-<frac|5|64>\<less\>0;
  </equation*>

  it tends to zero and is eventually below the displayed grade-<math|D>
  payment.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Paid-delay growth of the shell
    weights><label|lem:ex-lattice-shell-paid-growth>Use the same selected
    history, block lengths and entrance delays as in
    Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:common-observable-purchases> and
    <reference|sec:common-scale-selection>. In particular, its final entry
    thresholds retain the marked-portfolio delay of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-marked-portfolio-calibration>. Write
    <math|J<around|(|i|)>> for its stage clock and <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>> for
    the block side fixed at stage <math|j>, so that

    <\equation*>
      J<around|(|i|)>\<leqslant\>J*<around|(|i+1|)>\<leqslant\>J<around|(|i|)>+1,<space|2em>L<rsub|i>=\<ell\><rsub|J*<around|(|i+1|)>>=a<rsub|i>/a<rsub|i+1>,<space|2em><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>+s<rsub|G>*log
      L<rsub|i>,<space|1em>s<rsub|G>\<gtr\>0.
    </equation*>

    At step <math|i>, assume the history pays the retained entrance threshold
    of stage <math|J*<around|(|i+1|)>> with <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>>.
    Use the public weights

    <\equation*>
      \<phi\><rsub|i>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>\<upsilon\><rsub|i>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i><rsup|-\<nu\><rsub|D>>,<space|2em>\<nu\><rsub|D>=g<rsub|D>+4,
    </equation*>

    where <math|g<rsub|D>> is the maximal factor degree of the same selected
    grade-<math|D> frame. Assume the retained reference depths satisfy
    <math|D+3\<leqslant\>J<around|(|<math-up|ref><around|(|D|)>|)>> for every
    <math|D>. Then, for every <math|D\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and every
    <math|i\<geqslant\><math-up|ref><around|(|D|)>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ex-lattice-shell-growth-input>\<phi\><rsub|i>*L<rsub|i><rsup|D+1>*\<upsilon\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<upsilon\><rsub|i+1>.
    </equation>

    This conclusion uses no cutoff or period parameter.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The delay calibration in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-marked-portfolio-calibration>
    is made after the stage block sides are fixed. For each stage <math|j>,
    it tests the two possible profiles <math|k=j> and <math|k=j-1> (only
    <math|k=0> when <math|j=0>), and all grades
    <math|0\<leqslant\>d\<leqslant\>k>. Its numerical proof uses, for
    <math|x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>>, <math|b=s<rsub|G>*log
    \<ell\><rsub|j>> and <math|y=x+b>, the sufficient bounds

    <\equation*>
      x\<geqslant\>1,<space|2em>x\<geqslant\>b,<space|2em>y\<geqslant\>max
      <around|{|1,2<rsup|g<rsub|d>+4>*\<ell\><rsub|j><rsup|d+1>|}>.
    </equation*>

    These are thresholds for each fixed pair <math|<around|(|j,d|)>>, not one
    threshold independent of the block side or grade. The cited proof takes a
    finite maximum at each stage, includes the profile-cost and credit
    thresholds, and then takes prefix maxima together with the prior entrance
    requests. It proves the tail comparison for every coupling beyond the
    resulting threshold. Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:common-observable-purchases>
    and <reference|sec:common-scale-selection> retain this delay before
    constructing the history; enlarging an entry threshold preserves the
    comparison. Thus no new history is selected for the present application.

    Now fix <math|D> and <math|i\<geqslant\><math-up|ref><around|(|D|)>>.
    Monotonicity of <math|J> gives <math|D\<leqslant\>D+3\<leqslant\>J<around|(|<math-up|ref><around|(|D|)>|)>\<leqslant\>J<around|(|i|)>>.
    The pair <math|<around|(|j,k|)>=<around|(|J*<around|(|i+1|)>,J<around|(|i|)>|)>>
    is one of the tested adjacent pairs, and the assumed payment permits
    evaluation at <math|x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>>. The block and coupling
    identities in the statement identify its <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>> and
    <math|y> with <math|L<rsub|i>> and <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1>>.
    Finally the reciprocal tail <math|<math-ss|f><rsub|D>> in
    <eqref|eq:ex-marked-height-jump> has exponent <math|g<rsub|D>+4>, hence
    its values at these two couplings are exactly <math|\<upsilon\><rsub|i>>
    and <math|\<upsilon\><rsub|i+1>>. That equation, with <math|d=D>, is the
    asserted inequality.
  </proof>

  For the marched estimate use Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-shell-paid-growth>.
  Since <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1>, its weights satisfy
  <math|0\<less\>\<upsilon\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1> and
  <math|0\<less\>\<phi\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1>. The retained delay and the
  reference-depth condition are conditions on the same history used by the
  menu and transfer inputs of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-actual-shell-node>.
  The service theorem alone does not supply them; the named delay purchase
  above supplies the growth bound when those conditions hold. Merely knowing
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>> would not imply it for
  varying <math|L<rsub|i>>. For <math|m\<geqslant\>k\<geqslant\><math-up|ref><around|(|D|)>>,
  multiplying it for <math|k\<leqslant\>i\<less\>m> gives
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|i=k><rsup|m-1>\<phi\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<upsilon\><rsub|m>/\<upsilon\><rsub|k>|)>*<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|D+1>>.
  Together with <math|\<upsilon\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1> it gives

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|M><rsub|k,m><around|(|c<rsub|m>|)>\<leqslant\><frac|c<rsub|m>|\<upsilon\><rsub|k>><around*|(|<frac|a<rsub|m>|a<rsub|k>>|)><rsup|D+1>,<space|2em>k\<geqslant\><math-up|ref><around|(|D|)>.
  </equation*>

  For fixed <math|k>, the right side has rate <math|1/32-<around|(|D+1|)>>,
  while <math|\<delta\><rsub|m>*W<rsub|m>/<around|(|16*X<rsub|m>|)>> has rate
  <math|-D+M>. Their rate difference is <math|1/32-1-M=-61/64\<less\>0>, so
  the former is eventually smaller. This uses the same-history growth
  estimate, the rate-zero allowance, and the paid channel-rate identity
  above. The separate strict condition <math|M\<gtr\>-1/32> is a sufficient
  condition for the correction price to be eventual. Hence both price
  inequalities hold for each fixed opening.

  The other price gate used in the finite coefficient construction is the
  exact budget identity

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-shell-ledger-budget>4*<around|(|1+n<rsub|m>|)>*<space|0.17em>2*<around*|(|a<rsub|m+1><rsup|D>*X<rsub|m>*<frac|4|W<rsub|m>>|)>=e<rsub|m>.
  </equation>

  Thus the available coefficient budget is precisely the finite-census price
  of the principal term, with its two channel factors; no extra polynomial is
  hidden in this equality.

  <subsubsection|The coefficient and density ledger>

  Set

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-shell-one-step-scales>a<rsub|m><rsup|\<ast\>>=<frac|4|W<rsub|m>>,<space|2em>I<rsub|m>=<frac|W<rsub|m>|16*X<rsub|m>>,<space|2em>p<rprime|'><rsub|m>=a<rsub|m+1><rsup|D>*<around|(|X<rsub|m>*a<rsub|m><rsup|\<ast\>>|)>,<space|2em>e<rsub|m>=8*<around|(|1+n<rsub|m>|)>*p<rprime|'><rsub|m>.
  </equation>

  Then <math|e<rsub|m>=32*<around|(|1+n<rsub|m>|)>*a<rsub|m+1><rsup|D>*X<rsub|m>/W<rsub|m>>,
  as above. This reparameterization exhibits the numerical inputs to the
  coefficient construction. Put <math|L<rsub|m>=a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|m+1>> and
  <math|A<rsub|m>=L<rsub|m><rsup|D>>, the block dilation of a grade-<math|D>
  coefficient. The block side <math|L<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>2> and grade
  <math|D\<geqslant\>0> give <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|m><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>=L<rsub|m><rsup|-D>\<leqslant\>1>.
  The stretch across this step satisfies

  <\equation*>
    a<rsub|m><rsup|D>*L<rsub|m><rsup|-D>=a<rsub|m+1><rsup|D>,
  </equation*>

  so the coefficient bound <math|X<rsub|m>*a<rsub|m><rsup|\<ast\>>> delivered
  by the finite-coupon construction becomes exactly
  <math|p<rprime|'><rsub|m>=a<rsub|m+1><rsup|D>*<around|(|X<rsub|m>*a<rsub|m><rsup|\<ast\>>|)>>
  after this grade-<math|D> rescaling. Together with
  <math|X<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>1>,

  <\equation*>
    2*I<rsub|m>*<around|(|2*a<rsub|m><rsup|\<ast\>>|)>=X<rsub|m><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>I<rsub|m><around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|m><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>*<around|(|X<rsub|m>*a<rsub|m><rsup|\<ast\>>|)>=<frac|<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|m><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>|4>\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  The marched price says <math|J<rsub|m>\<assign\><math-ss|M><rsub|k,m><around|(|c<rsub|m>|)>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|m>*I<rsub|m>>.
  At each name the selected menu has the corresponding regular price
  <math|J<rsub|m>> and allowance <math|\<delta\><rsub|m>*I<rsub|m>>;
  multiplying the finite factors and using <math|1+v\<leqslant\>e<rsup|v>>
  for <math|v\<geqslant\>0> yields the finite transfer bound with its
  exponential penalty. This is the finite-product estimate used by the
  transition, not a claim that normalized root expectations multiply.

  The fine response is first built for each of the two insertions from the
  same terminal family, door, and funded path. Its quiet coefficient is the
  background activity and its singleton coefficient is the paid target plus
  the hook correction; signed covariance transports these identities from the
  distinguished name to the other names. Backward transport uses
  <math|<math-up|krill><rsub|j,i><around|(|u|)>=u*<around|(|a<rsub|j>/a<rsub|i>|)><rsup|4>/16>.
  If <math|L<rsub|i>=a<rsub|i>/a<rsub|i+1>\<geqslant\>2>, then

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|krill><rsub|j,i+1><around|(|u|)>=L<rsub|i><rsup|4><math-up|krill><rsub|j,i><around|(|u|)>,<space|2em>6*L<rsub|i><math-up|krill><rsub|j,i><around|(|u|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|krill><rsub|j,i+1><around|(|u|)>,
  </equation*>

  because <math|L<rsub|i><rsup|3>\<geqslant\>8>. Iteration moves the response
  to level <math|m+1>; injective migration preserves occupancy and the block
  map expands separation by at least <math|L<rsub|i>>. The fine response uses
  occupancy <math|o<rsub|m+1>=r*<around|(|m+1|)>>.

  The two channels refer to the two inserted labels. Their graft is formed at
  the same base name; the assumed affine covariance of the graft determines
  its quiet and singleton coefficients. This covariance is part of the
  same-history menu input, not a consequence of the scalar price bounds. The
  higher many-name terms are pruned with the finite-mask receipt. With fine
  occupancy <math|o<rsub|m+1>> its mask cost is

  <\equation*>
    \<sigma\><rsub|m>=2*<around|(|1+o<rsub|m+1>|)>*\<eta\><rsub|m+1>*<around*|(|o<rsub|m+1>e<rsup|s<rsub|m+1>+1/2>+1|)>+<around|(|1+o<rsub|m+1>|)>*\<chi\><rsub|m+1>.
  </equation*>

  The selected finite-mask receipt gives <math|<around|(|2*B<rsub|m+1>+1|)><rsup|4>*\<sigma\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1/2>
  and <math|e<rsup|\<sigma\><rsub|m>>-1\<leqslant\>1>. The radius
  <math|1024*B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>> in the stencil and the exponent count
  <math|q<rsub|m+1>> in the channel price are distinct quantities.

  The finite-coupon step and transition then yield a coefficient
  <math|Q<rsub|m>> at level <math|m>. Its principal coefficient is bounded by
  <math|p<rprime|'><rsub|m>>. The census cost is exactly

  <\equation*>
    4*<around|(|1+n<rsub|m>|)>*<space|0.17em>2*p<rprime|'><rsub|m>=e<rsub|m>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|n<rsub|m>> is the column count; the factor <math|4> is the
  two-channel and sign count. The correction has the purchased one-step
  remainder bound, purchased with the same parameter
  <math|p<rprime|'><rsub|m>>; so the principal coefficient size plus the
  remainder bound is at most <math|2*p<rprime|'><rsub|m>>, which is the
  quantity in the census identity. No response coefficient is declared exact
  before that paid correction is included.

  Here is the exact density identity retained by the construction. The paid
  representation supplies regular coefficients <math|H<rsub|S><around|(|U|)>>
  indexed by supports <math|S> and forced coefficients
  <math|K<rsub|P><around|(|U|)>> indexed by boxes <math|P>. Each regular
  coefficient is supported on its assigned <math|S>, and each forced
  coefficient on its assigned box support. Write
  <math|E<rsub|m><around|(|U|)>> for the action polynomial,
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|i><around|(|U|)>> for singleton field coefficients,
  <math|<wide|A|\<bar\>><rsub|P><around|(|U|)>> for the background activity,
  and <math|\<omega\><rsub|i,P><around|(|U|)>> for singleton activities. The
  operator <math|<math-up|send><rsub|i><around|(|z|)>> is the polynomial
  monomial with coefficient <math|z> in insertion slot <math|i>;
  <math|F<around|(|U|)>> is the target flag set. Then the pushed density is

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>=>|<cell|e<rsup|E<rsub|m><around|(|U|)>>*exp<rsub|<math-up|nil>><space|-0.17em><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i><math-up|send><rsub|i><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|i><around|(|U|)>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|S>H<rsub|S><around|(|U|)>|)><eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-shell-served-density>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|1em>\<times\><big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-P\><text|
    a family of boxes>:>>|<row|<cell|P\<cap\>P<rprime|'>=\<varnothing\>*<around|(|P\<ne\>P<rprime|'>|)>,<space|1em><big|cup><rsub|P\<in\>\<cal-P\>>Q<rsub|P>=F<around|(|U|)>>>>>>><big|prod><rsub|P\<in\>\<cal-P\>><around*|(|<wide|A|\<bar\>><rsub|P><around|(|U|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i><math-up|send><rsub|i><around|(|\<omega\><rsub|i,P><around|(|U|)>|)>+K<rsub|P><around|(|U|)>|)>.<eq-number>>>>>
  </align>

  Each box is <math|<around|(|P,Q<rsub|P>|)>> with
  <math|Q<rsub|P>\<subseteq\>P>; compatibility means disjointness of full
  supports, while the flags cover <math|F<around|(|U|)>>. Here
  <math|exp<rsub|<math-up|nil>><around|(|Z|)>=<big|sum><rsub|r=0><rsup|<around|\||I|\|>>Z<rsup|r>/r!>
  for the finite set <math|I> of insertion names. The degree conditions make
  every <math|H<rsub|S>> and <math|K<rsub|P>> vanish in degrees zero and one;
  their regular-support and forced-box coefficient costs have separate
  summable bounds. Thus the higher-degree exponential terms, forced terms,
  and compatible box products remain explicitly present and paid. In
  particular, the pushed density is not identified with a single leading
  coefficient. The canonical local-transfer proof pays these two arrays
  separately and proves the same-law density equality used here.

  The served transfer uses coarse occupancy <math|o<rsub|m>=r<around|(|m|)>>;
  the fine response and graft use <math|o<rsub|m+1>=r*<around|(|m+1|)>>. Set
  <math|d<rsub|m>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1><rsup|-2>>,
  <math|u<rsub|m>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1><rsup|-1>>, and <math|y<rsub|m>>
  equal to the flagged response price. For the explicit rooted-site cost, put
  <math|<math-ss|S><around|(|B|)>=e*<around|(|2*<around|(|16*B+9|)>|)><rsup|4>>.
  The transfer duty is

  <\equation*>
    0\<less\>d<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>T<rsub|m>=o<rsub|m><math-ss|S><around|(|B<rsub|m+1>|)>*d<rsub|m>+4*d<rsub|m>,<space|2em>T<rsub|m>+e<rsup|T<rsub|m>>*T<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|m>,
  </equation*>

  together with

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|2*B<rsub|m>+1|)><rsup|4>*<around*|(|y<rsub|m>+o<rsub|m><math-ss|S><around|(|B<rsub|m>|)>*d<rsub|m>|)>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>,<space|2em>e<rsup|y<rsub|m>+o<rsub|m><math-ss|S><around|(|B<rsub|m>|)>*d<rsub|m>>-1\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|y<rsub|m>=2*exp <around|(|-\<mu\><rsub|m>*\<Delta\><rsub|G,L<rsub|m>><around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|)><rsup|2>/16|)>>,
  where <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>,\<Delta\><rsub|G,L<rsub|m>>> are the selected
  shape strength and fine monitor. These are the exact coarse transfer
  inequalities; the fine backward transport and graft instead use
  <math|o<rsub|m+1>>. The endpoint support exponents are at least <math|100>.
  These displayed inequalities are the paid-transfer duties on the same
  adjacent transition and history. Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:finite-menu-history-handoff>
  and <reference|sec:local-transfer> give the conditional service and
  transfer arguments; the actual numerical conditions are verified by
  <eqref|eq:trace-extra-stage-payment> and the estimates following it. This
  keeps the regular and forced coefficient charges separate and does not
  assume equality of the interacting and reference actions.

  <subparagraph|The actual fine finite-mask
  payment.><with|the-label|17.4.2|<label|par:shell-actual-mask>> The
  finite-mask cost in the shell construction is the multiple-insertion cost
  already paid in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:projection-payment-producer>.
  Here is the index and parameter identification. At the fine shell level
  <math|k=m+1>, use that producer on the actual adjacent transition with
  coarse clock <math|x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>> and fine clock
  <math|H=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k+1>>. Its occupancy <math|N> is
  <math|o<rsub|k>>, its outgoing support exponent <math|b> is
  <math|s<rsub|k>>, its ordinary allowance <math|\<eta\>> is
  <math|\<eta\><rsub|k>=H<rsup|-2>>, and its forced allowance
  <math|\<tau\><around|(|x|)>> is <math|\<chi\><rsub|k>>, the actual forced
  price of the two absorptions in the retained marked transition. This
  <math|\<eta\><rsub|k>> is the ordinary finite-mask allowance, not the
  separately named marker price <math|<tfrac|1|4><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|-1/2>>
  used by the centered service. The background transition and its forced
  array are unchanged: the projection-payment producer defines the singleton
  map by differentiating that array, and Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-history-service>
  uses that same map for its multiple-insertion estimate.

  With <math|\<alpha\>=s<rsub|k>+1/2>, the producer's scalar
  <math|s<around|(|x|)>> is exactly

  <\equation*>
    s<around|(|x|)>=2*<around|(|1+o<rsub|k>|)>*\<eta\><rsub|k>*<around|(|o<rsub|k>e<rsup|s<rsub|k>+1/2>+1|)>+<around|(|1+o<rsub|k>|)>*\<chi\><rsub|k>=\<sigma\><rsub|m>.
  </equation*>

  Its coarse radius is <math|B<around|(|x|)>=B<rsub|k>=B<rsub|m+1>>.
  Therefore <eqref|eq:projection-occupancy-payments> gives, on the actual
  fine transition,

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|2*B<rsub|m+1>+1|)><rsup|4>*\<sigma\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>,<space|2em>e<rsup|\<sigma\><rsub|m>>-1\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  These are the two finite-mask inequalities needed in the shell proof. For
  clarity, their numerical proof does not assume a uniform bound on all
  support exponents or on all occupancy stages. Fix a permitted stage pair
  first; then <math|o<rsub|k>>, <math|s<rsub|k>>, the block length and the
  positive suppression constant are fixed stage data. The forced price has
  every negative upper exponent, while <math|H<rsup|-2>> has exponent
  <math|-2> and <math|B<around|(|x|)>> has exponent zero. Consequently
  <math|<around|(|2*B<around|(|x|)>+1|)><rsup|4>*s<around|(|x|)>\<to\>0> and
  <math|s<around|(|x|)>\<to\>0>, so the two inequalities hold above a finite
  entrance threshold for that pair. Take the maximum over the finitely many
  permitted pairs and degrees at each stage, exactly as done after
  <eqref|eq:projection-occupancy-payments>. The common scale selection
  retains that maximum before realizing the history. At level <math|k=m+1>
  its entry inequality applies to the corresponding actual pair, with that
  pair's actual suppression constant. Thus this is an already purchased
  same-history instance, not an inference from pointwise decay with an
  unbounded stage index or a new mask assumption.

  <subsubsection|From the ledger to one collar node>

  Fix an opened reference level <math|p<rsub|0>> and a level <math|k> with
  that opening, <math|k> beyond the grade-<math|D> threshold, and labels
  <math|B,T>. The quota estimate at birth level <math|m> gives a finite
  constant <math|Q<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|quota>>> independent of volume and
  period. It may depend on <math|m>; no uniform birth-level bound is
  asserted:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-shell-quota><around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|D>\<leqslant\>Q<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|quota>>*\<frak-q\><rsub|D,m,N,q><around|(|z|)>.
  </equation>

  Apply the ledger and the same-history coefficient-to-label transfer at a
  fixed collar anchor. That transfer takes a coefficient whose size plus
  remainder bound is at most <math|c>, with census cost
  <math|4*<around|(|1+n<rsub|m>|)>*c\<leqslant\>e>, to a grade-<math|D> label
  <math|z> with <math|\<frak-q\><rsub|D,m,N,q><around|(|z|)>\<leqslant\>2*e>;
  the factor <math|2> is produced by this transfer step. With
  <math|c=2*p<rprime|'><rsub|m>> and <math|e=e<rsub|m>>, by the coefficient
  budget <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-shell-ledger-budget>, this gives
  <math|\<frak-q\><rsub|D,m,N,q><around|(|z|)>\<leqslant\>2*e<rsub|m>>, hence

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|D>\<leqslant\>2*Q<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|quota>>*e<rsub|m>.
  </equation*>

  The transferred label satisfies the exact product-minus-correction identity
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-shell-fragment>. Its chamber condition follows from
  the two input chamber memberships and closure under products and
  subtraction of a paid correction. Write <math|\<Theta\><rsub|k>> for the
  selected treasury at level <math|k>. The response estimate together with
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-shell-correction-price> gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-shell-root>\<cal-R\><rsub|N,q,p<rsub|0>><around|(|\<cal-F\><rsub|m,x><rsup|N,q><around|(|z|)>|)>\<leqslant\>2*S*<around|(|p<rsub|0>,k|)>*c<rsub|m><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>S*<around|(|p<rsub|0>,k|)>=exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<around|(|a<rsub|p<rsub|0>>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4>*\<Theta\><rsub|k>+5|)>.
  </equation>

  To see the factor <math|2>, write the pre-correction function as <math|f>
  and the finite-coupon correction as <math|r<rprime|'>>. The exact
  decomposition is

  <\equation*>
    f-\<cal-O\><rsub|N,q>*<around|(|B,0|)>=<around|(|f+r<rprime|'>|)>+<around|(|-r<rprime|'>-\<cal-O\><rsub|N,q>*<around|(|B,0|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Put <math|B<rsub|m>*<around|(|p<rsub|0>,k|)>=S*<around|(|p<rsub|0>,k|)>*c<rsub|m><rsup|-1>>.
  The two root-test bounds are respectively at most
  <math|B<rsub|m>*<around|(|p<rsub|0>,k|)>> and
  <math|B<rsub|m>*<around|(|p<rsub|0>,k|)>> (the second is first obtained
  with the stronger bound <math|B<rsub|m>*<around|(|p<rsub|0>,k|)>/2>). The
  root-test triangle inequality applies because both terms are in the
  centered chamber. Thus the factor <math|2> comes from this two-term
  triangle inequality; it is not obtained by multiplying normalized root
  functionals or by combining regular and forced charges. The estimate uses
  the served coefficient identity, the throw coupon, the fixed opening, and
  the two inequalities <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-shell-march> and
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-shell-correction-price>.

  The birth-level threshold for the chamber receipt is uniform over all
  anchors in the level-<math|m> collar. The placement event in the extraction
  filter is instead pointwise in the cover anchor. For fixed <math|x>, the
  period eventually places it at its intended grid site, so the radial bounds
  carry over to the finite-volume location. The first inequality in
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-shell-lightness> is the shell-smallness condition used
  with those bounds to keep the remote insertion in the centered chamber; the
  second gives enough centered chamber cells for the finite response stencil.
  At each <math|<around|(|N,q|)>> on that anchor's late set, the construction
  just described gives its correction. Outside that late set choose the zero
  label. The numerical bounds do not depend on <math|x>, although the late
  set may.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Selected-history shell-node output><label|prop:ex-lattice-actual-shell-node>Take
    the selected public history of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-selected-run-service>.
    Use its adjacent-scale paid menu, tariff, and served-transfer receipts
    from Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-rg-multisource>,
    <reference|subsec:ex-lattice-volant-producer>,
    <reference|sec:finite-menu-history-handoff>, and
    <reference|sec:local-transfer>, the scale and price gates displayed
    above, the lightness estimate <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-shell-lightness>, the
    finite-stencil room estimate, and the quota estimate
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-shell-quota> at each birth level. Assume also the
    same-history chamber receipt in the following uniform eventual form: for
    each fixed opened pair <math|<around|(|p<rsub|0>,k|)>>, labels <math|B,T>
    and extraction order <math|\<frak-f\>>, there is
    <math|m<rsub|<math-up|ch>>> such that, for every
    <math|m\<geqslant\>m<rsub|<math-up|ch>>> and every cover anchor <math|x>
    whose level-<math|j<rsub|m>> location lies in
    <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|r<rsub|m>>>, for <math|\<frak-f\>>-almost every
    <math|<around|(|N,q|)>> the readouts <math|\<cal-O\><rsub|N,q>*<around|(|B,0|)>>,
    <math|\<cal-O\><rsub|N,q>*<around|(|T,x<rsub|m,N,q>|)>> and
    <math|\<cal-O\><rsub|N,q>*<around|(|z,0|)>>, for every grade-<math|D>
    label <math|z>, lie in the centered chamber of the opening
    <math|p<rsub|0>>. The threshold <math|m<rsub|<math-up|ch>>> is
    independent of <math|x>; the late event in <math|<around|(|N,q|)>> may
    depend on <math|m> and <math|x>, but is common to all the grade-<math|D>
    labels <math|z>.

    Fix grades with <math|D+h=d+4>. Then there are <math|t\<gtr\>1> and
    levels <math|j<rsub|m>>, chosen before any opening, label or extraction
    order, with <math|j<rsub|m>\<to\>\<infty\>> and
    <math|\<lambda\><rsub|j<rsub|m>>/\<lambda\><rsub|m>\<to\>t>, such that
    the following holds. For every reference level <math|p<rsub|0>> opened at
    a level <math|k\<geqslant\><math-up|ref><around|(|D|)>>, every
    <math|B\<in\>E<rsub|d>>, every <math|T\<in\>E<rsub|4>> and every
    extraction order <math|\<frak-f\>>, there is <math|m<rsub|0>> such that
    for every <math|m\<geqslant\>m<rsub|0>> and every cover anchor <math|x>
    whose level-<math|j<rsub|m>> location lies in the collar
    <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|r<rsub|m>>> there is a label sequence
    <math|z<rsub|x>:\<bbb-N\>\<times\>\<bbb-N\>\<to\>E<rsub|D>> for which
    <math|\<frak-f\>>-almost every <math|<around|(|N,q|)>> has
    <math|p<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>N>, <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|m,x><rsup|N,q>*<around|(|z<rsub|x>*<around|(|N,q|)>|)>>
    in the centered chamber of <math|p<rsub|0>>, and

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|z<rsub|x>*<around|(|N,q|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|D>\<leqslant\>2*Q<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|quota>>*e<rsub|m>,<space|2em>\<cal-R\><rsub|N,q,p<rsub|0>>*<around|(|\<cal-F\><rsub|m,x><rsup|N,q>*<around|(|z<rsub|x>*<around|(|N,q|)>|)>|)>\<leqslant\>2*S*<around|(|p<rsub|0>,k|)>*c<rsub|m><rsup|-1>.
    </equation*>

    The late event may depend on <math|x>; the displayed numeric bounds do
    not. This is the conclusion required by
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:ex-lattice-shell-receipt>, apart from the
    scale note, which is proved after the proof.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Choose <math|t> and <math|j<rsub|m>> by the rate schedule
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-shell-schedule>; this choice uses only <math|d>, so
    it precedes the opening, the labels and the extraction order. Now fix an
    opened pair <math|<around|(|p<rsub|0>,k|)>> with
    <math|k\<geqslant\><math-up|ref><around|(|D|)>>, labels <math|B,T> and an
    extraction order <math|\<frak-f\>>, and put <math|a=max
    <around|(|1,<math-up|cs><rsub|d><around|(|B|)>|)>>, <math|b=max
    <around|(|1,<math-up|cs><rsub|4><around|(|T|)>|)>>. The following
    conditions on <math|m> each hold for all sufficiently large <math|m>, and
    we take <math|m<rsub|0>> beyond all of them: the two rate-based price
    gates (by the strict inequality <math|<around|(|d+12|)>*<around|(|1-t|)>\<gtr\>-1/32>,
    for the fixed coefficient bounds <math|a,b>); the two lightness
    inequalities <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-shell-lightness> for this
    <math|p<rsub|0>>; <math|m\<geqslant\>k>, so that
    <math|p<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>m> and <math|p<rsub|0>> is opened
    at <math|k>; <math|j<rsub|m>\<geqslant\><math-up|ref><around|(|d|)>> and
    <math|j<rsub|m>\<geqslant\><math-up|ref><around|(|4|)>>, because
    <math|j<rsub|m>\<to\>\<infty\>>; <math|j<rsub|m>\<gtr\>m+1>, because
    <math|\<lambda\>> is increasing, <math|\<lambda\><rsub|j<rsub|m>>/\<lambda\><rsub|m>\<to\>t\<gtr\>1>
    and <math|\<lambda\><rsub|m+1>/\<lambda\><rsub|m>\<to\>1> (see the scale
    note below); and the finite-stencil room threshold for <math|p<rsub|0>>;
    and the uniform chamber threshold <math|m<rsub|<math-up|ch>>> in the
    hypothesis. This last threshold is chosen before the anchor: finiteness
    of each individual collar would not suffice to choose it for the growing
    family of collars. Fix <math|m\<geqslant\>m<rsub|0>> and a cover anchor
    <math|x> in the collar. Choose the late volume-period event of
    <math|\<frak-f\>> on which <math|N\<gtr\>j<rsub|m>> (so
    <math|p<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>m\<less\>j<rsub|m>\<less\>N>),
    the image of <math|x> is its intended grid site, and the three readouts
    of the chamber receipt lie in the centered chamber. The intersection of
    these finitely many late events is late. On it, the construction of this
    subsection gives the correction <math|z<rsub|x>*<around|(|N,q|)>>: the
    ledger and coefficient-to-label transfer give the
    product-minus-correction identity <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-shell-fragment>,
    the fragment lies in the centered chamber by closure of the chamber under
    products and subtraction of a paid correction, the quota estimate gives
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|z<rsub|x>*<around|(|N,q|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|D>\<leqslant\>2*Q<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|quota>>*e<rsub|m>>,
    and <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-shell-root> gives the root bound. Off the late
    event put <math|z<rsub|x>*<around|(|N,q|)>=0>. The constants
    <math|Q<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|quota>>>, <math|e<rsub|m>>,
    <math|S*<around|(|p<rsub|0>,k|)>> and <math|c<rsub|m>> do not depend on
    <math|x>. This produces exactly the pointwise late-set quantifier stated
    above; it does not claim one late event simultaneously for the growing
    collar.
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|The scale note.> Definition<nbsp><reference|def:ex-lattice-shell-receipt>
  also requires <math|log r<rsub|m>/\<lambda\><rsub|m>\<to\>-1>. This is not
  part of the shell construction; it follows from two schedule properties of
  the selected history: the schedule is gentle, <math|log
  L<rsub|m>/\<lambda\><rsub|m>\<to\>0> for the adjacent ratios
  <math|L<rsub|m>=a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|m+1>>, and the radii grow subexponentially
  in the scale, <math|log B<rsub|m>/\<lambda\><rsub|m>\<to\>0>. Indeed
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|m+1>=\<lambda\><rsub|m>+log L<rsub|m>>, so
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|m+1>/\<lambda\><rsub|m>\<to\>1>, and

  <\equation*>
    <frac|log r<rsub|m>|\<lambda\><rsub|m>>=<frac|log
    128|\<lambda\><rsub|m>>+2*<space|0.17em><frac|log
    B<rsub|m+1>|\<lambda\><rsub|m+1>>\<cdot\><frac|\<lambda\><rsub|m+1>|\<lambda\><rsub|m>>-<frac|\<lambda\><rsub|m+1>|\<lambda\><rsub|m>>\<longrightarrow\>0+0-1=-1.
  </equation*>

  In particular <math|r<rsub|m>\<to\>0>.

  <subparagraph|Shell schedule, chamber placement and birth quota on the
  selected family.><with|the-label|17.4.3|<label|par:shell-actual-placement>>
  We verify three of the actual shell-node hypotheses without changing the
  history or its extraction. Write <math|s<rsub|m>=log
  <around|(|a<rsub|m><rsup|-1>|)>>. The selected schedule has
  <math|s<rsub|m>\<to\>\<infty\>> and

  <\equation*>
    0\<leqslant\><frac|s<rsub|m+1>-s<rsub|m>|s<rsub|m>>=<frac|log
    L<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>|s<rsub|m>>\<leqslant\><frac|1|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>\<longrightarrow\>0
  </equation*>

  by <eqref|eq:common-stage-entries>. For the fixed shell grades put
  <math|t=1+<around|[|64*<around|(|d+12|)>|]><rsup|-1>> and choose
  <math|j<rsub|m>> as the first index with
  <math|s<rsub|j<rsub|m>>\<geqslant\>t*s<rsub|m>>. The preceding index
  satisfies <math|s<rsub|j<rsub|m>-1>\<less\>t*s<rsub|m>>; the displayed
  adjacent-increment estimate gives <math|s<rsub|j<rsub|m>>/s<rsub|j<rsub|m>-1>\<to\>1>.
  Thus <math|s<rsub|j<rsub|m>>/s<rsub|m>\<to\>t>, proving the shell schedule
  with a choice independent of labels and openings.

  The selected covering radius is <math|B<rsub|m>=<around|\<lceil\>|log<rsup|2>
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|\<rceil\>>>, where
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>=\<beta\><rsub|\<ast\>>+s<rsub|G>*s<rsub|m>>.
  Hence <math|log B<rsub|m>=o<around|(|s<rsub|m>|)>> and
  <math|B<rsub|m+1>/B<rsub|m>\<to\>1>. In particular
  <math|B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|m><rsup|2>> on a tail and
  <math|a<rsub|m+1>*B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>\<to\>0>. Both inequalities in
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-shell-lightness> follow for every fixed
  <math|p<rsub|0>>. The opening argument only needs these tab inequalities on
  a tail: starting at a level beyond that tail,
  <math|a<rsub|j+1>*B<rsub|j+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|(|2/3|)>*a<rsub|j>*B<rsub|j><rsup|2>>
  since <math|L<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>3>. Consequently every fixed reference
  level is opened at all sufficiently large later levels, including levels
  beyond any finite list of watch depths. No alteration of an already chosen
  reference level is needed.

  Fix <math|p<rsub|0>> and grades <math|d,4,D>. The actual local sections
  have finite stencils depending on the grade, not on the coefficient label.
  Let <math|R> exceed the maximum coordinate radius of their three stencils,
  including link endpoints. This also bounds the stencil of every
  grade-<math|D> counterterm, regardless of its coefficient size. The
  centered chamber at cutoff <math|N> has physical side
  <math|a<rsub|p<rsub|0>>>, with its lattice endpoints rounded by at most
  <math|a<rsub|N>>. Choose the birth threshold so that
  <math|4*r<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|p<rsub|0>>/8>. For each such <math|m>,
  choose the cutoff tail so that <math|a<rsub|N>*<around|(|R+2|)>\<less\>a<rsub|p<rsub|0>>/8>.
  A collar anchor satisfies <math|<around|\||y|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\>4*r<rsub|m>>,
  so its translated stencil lies strictly within
  <math|<around|(|-a<rsub|p<rsub|0>>/4,a<rsub|p<rsub|0>>/4|)><rsup|4>>; the
  stencils based at zero obey the same bound. This cube lies in the centered
  chamber even after rounding its endpoints. Thus all three readouts in the
  shell-node chamber receipt are chamber-interior. Gauge invariance,
  measurability and boundedness are inherited from the local-section
  polynomial on the compact lattice configuration space, while dependence
  only on chamber links is exactly the stencil inclusion just proved. The
  birth threshold is independent of the anchor and of the grade-<math|D>
  label. For each fixed cover anchor, the period tail places it at its
  intended site; this tail may depend on the anchor, as allowed by the
  shell-node proposition. The cutoff stencil tail is uniform over all
  coefficients, so the late event is common to every grade-<math|D>
  counterterm. This proves the required uniform-in-anchor chamber receipt
  from locality and lightness.

  For completeness, the quota bound is an explicit finite product. Let
  <math|r<rsub|D>> be the reference level and let <math|v<rsub|j>> be the
  coefficient of the actual grade-<math|D> insertion transported to level
  <math|j>. Calibration gives <math|v<rsub|r<rsub|D>>=z> and the adjacent
  column bound gives

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|cs><rsub|D><around|(|v<rsub|j>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j+1>*L<rsub|j><rsup|D><math-up|cs><rsub|D><around|(|v<rsub|j+1>|)>,<space|2em>j\<geqslant\>r<rsub|D>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|C<rsub|j+1>> is the selected column budget, independent of
  cutoff and period. Norm equivalence at the fixed reference frame gives
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|D>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|D><math-up|cs><rsub|D><around|(|z|)>>.
  Iterating only from <math|r<rsub|D>> to the fixed birth level <math|m>
  yields

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|D>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|D>*<big|prod><rsub|j=r<rsub|D>><rsup|m-1><around|(|C<rsub|j+1>*L<rsub|j><rsup|D>|)>*<space|0.17em>\<frak-q\><rsub|D,m,N,q><around|(|z|)>.
  </equation*>

  The product is finite, positive and independent of <math|N,q>. It supplies
  <math|Q<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|quota>>> in
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-shell-quota>; no bound uniform as
  <math|m\<to\>\<infty\>> is asserted or needed.

  <subsubsection|Scope of the shell construction>

  The menu and transfer receipts invoked above are the same-history
  constructions of Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-rg-multisource>,
  <reference|subsec:ex-lattice-volant-producer>,
  <reference|sec:finite-menu-history-handoff>, and
  <reference|sec:local-transfer>; the selected public service and its
  endpoint support exponents are supplied by
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-selected-run-service>. The present argument
  supplies the remaining shell-specific composition: the two transported
  channel responses, finite mask and coupon steps, coefficient ledger,
  placement, and collar norm/root bounds. The collar's late event is
  pointwise in its fixed anchor.

  <subsection|The annular Ward field for the selected stress
  tensor><label|sec:ex-lattice-actual-stress-annulus>

  This section proves the shrinking-annulus implication used by the public
  stress-tensor route. Given the selected shell-node receipt, a finite linear
  combination of scale-covariant insertions that annihilates every field at
  one grade has vanishing flux on shrinking windows. Finite-dimensional
  linear algebra then represents those fluxes by one field at that grade. The
  production of the shell-node receipt is a separate quantitative obligation;
  it is not proved by the conditional argument below.

  Write <math|a<rsub|i>> for the physical spacing at level <math|i> and
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>=log <around|(|a<rsub|i><rsup|-1>|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>
  for its logarithmic scale; thus <math|a<rsub|i>=e<rsup|-\<lambda\><rsub|i>>>.
  Write <math|B<rsub|i>> for its support radius, and

  <\equation*>
    r<rsub|m>=128*B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>*a<rsub|m+1>.
  </equation*>

  The scale note used below says <math|r<rsub|m>\<to\>0>. Let
  <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|d>> denote the complex field space at grade <math|d>.
  Write <math|E<rsub|d>> for the finite-dimensional reference label space
  used at finite cutoff, and <math|\<pi\><rsub|d>:E<rsub|d>\<to\>\<cal-F\><rsub|d>>
  for the selected table's quotient map. Finite-volume insertions take labels
  in <math|E<rsub|d>>; continuum testers take fields in
  <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|d>>. Let <math|\<tau\><rsub|I,D>:\<cal-F\><rsub|D>\<to\>\<bbb-C\>>
  be the tester associated with a watch <math|I>. We write

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-A\><rsub|r>=<around|{|y\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>:r/4\<less\><around|\||y|\|>\<less\>2*r|}>,<space|2em>\<cal-C\><rsub|r>=<around|{|y\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>:r/16\<less\><around|\||y|\|>\<less\>4*r|}>.
  </equation*>

  The wider collar <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|r>> is where the finite-volume shell
  correction is constructed; the desired annihilation is on
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|r>>.

  Concretely, <math|\<tau\><rsub|I,D><around|(|z|)>> is the insertion
  functional obtained by placing the grade-<math|D> field <math|z> at the
  distinguished point of <math|I> and retaining its other points and color
  labels as spectators. The flux functional is the window integral of the
  stress insertion against the window gradient and the Killing field:

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|Flux><rsub|J><around|(|\<chi\>,U,I,v|)>=-<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>><big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>,\<nu\>=1><rsup|4>\<partial\><rsub|\<mu\>>*\<chi\><around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>U<rsub|\<nu\>>*<around|(|x-x<rsub|I>|)><space|0.17em><around|(|I|)><space|-0.17em><math-up|asked><around|(|J<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>,v|)><around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x,
  </equation*>

  where <math|x<rsub|I>> is the distinguished point of the watch; all fluxes
  are thus written in coordinates centered at that point. The sign is fixed
  by the flux convention in the stress conservation law. All insertions and
  integrals here are the selected-table quantities; the shell argument uses
  their stated continuity and local conservation properties. The
  approximating counterterms below are the finite-volume objects.

  For a finite-volume cell system <math|\<cal-T\>> and coupling
  <math|\<beta\>>, the root-test of a configuration observable <math|F> is

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|RT><rsub|\<beta\>,\<cal-T\>><around|(|F|)>=<around*|\||\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<beta\>>*<big|prod><rsub|u\<in\>\<cal-T\>>F<rsub|u>|\|><rsup|1/<around|\||\<cal-T\>|\|>>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|F<rsub|u>> is the prescribed translate of <math|F> at cell
  <math|u>, with reflection conjugation when that cell is reflected. The
  label norm <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|D>> below is the fixed
  norm of the reference label space <math|E<rsub|D>>.

  For the exact shell costs, put

  <\equation*>
    c<rsub|m>=e<rsup|\<lambda\><rsub|m>/32>,<space|1em>S*<around|(|p<rsub|0>,k|)>=exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<around|(|a<rsub|p<rsub|0>>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4><space|0.17em><math-ss|T><rsub|k>+5|)>,<space|1em>X<rsub|m>=exp
    <space|-0.17em><sqrt|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|<math-ss|T><rsub|k>> is the treasury quantity at level
  <math|k>. Let <math|V<rsub|m>\<gtr\>0> be the product of the two selected
  channel payments at levels <math|<around|(|j<rsub|m>,m+1|)>>, and let
  <math|n<rsub|m>> be the number of columns at level <math|m>. The
  counterterm budget is

  <\equation*>
    e<rsub|m>=32*<around|(|1+n<rsub|m>|)>*a<rsub|m+1><rsup|D>*X<rsub|m>/V<rsub|m>.
  </equation*>

  The channel payments, and hence <math|V<rsub|m>> and <math|e<rsub|m>>,
  depend on the coefficient bounds of the two inserted labels. For each fixed
  birth level <math|m>, the quota conversion supplies a finite factor
  <math|Q<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|quota>>\<geqslant\>0>, so the producer bounds
  the correction label's coefficient norm by
  <math|2*Q<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|quota>>*e<rsub|m>>. This bound is uniform
  in volume, period and collar point at that <math|m>; no bound uniform in
  <math|m> is required, and the definition below asks only for some finite
  bound of this kind. The root-test cost, in contrast, is the decaying
  quantity <math|2*S*<around|(|p<rsub|0>,k|)>*c<rsub|m><rsup|-1>>.

  <subsubsection|The shell-node hypothesis>

  Fix grades <math|d,D,h\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> with

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-stress-grade>D+h=d+4.
  </equation>

  We name the exact shell-node input consumed below. This definition does not
  assert that the receipt has been produced from the public history.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Shell-node receipt><label|def:ex-lattice-shell-receipt>For fixed
    grades <math|d,D,h> satisfying <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-stress-grade>, a
    fixed family and selected history have the shell-node receipt if there
    are <math|t\<gtr\>1> and levels <math|j<rsub|m>>, chosen before the
    opened levels, labels, or extraction order, such that

    <\equation*>
      j<rsub|m>\<longrightarrow\>\<infty\>,<space|2em><frac|\<lambda\><rsub|j<rsub|m>>|\<lambda\><rsub|m>>\<longrightarrow\>t,
    </equation*>

    and the scale note has the consequence

    <\equation*>
      <frac|log r<rsub|m>|\<lambda\><rsub|m>>\<longrightarrow\>-1.
    </equation*>

    For every fixed opened reference level <math|p<rsub|0>> and level
    <math|k> with <math|p<rsub|0>> open at <math|k> and with <math|k> at
    least the reference threshold for grade <math|D>, every
    <math|v\<in\>E<rsub|d>>, <math|T\<in\>E<rsub|4>>, and extraction order
    <math|\<frak-f\>>, there is <math|m<rsub|0>> such that for every
    <math|m\<geqslant\>m<rsub|0>> and every fixed integer lattice anchor
    <math|x\<in\><math-up|Cover>=\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>> whose continuum location
    at level <math|j<rsub|m>> lies in <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|r<rsub|m>>>, there
    is a label sequence <math|z<rsub|x>:\<bbb-N\>\<times\>\<bbb-N\>\<to\>E<rsub|D>>
    for which <math|\<frak-f\>>-almost every <math|<around|(|N,q|)>> has
    <math|p<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>N> and

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|z<rsub|x>*<around|(|N,q|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|D>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|m>,<space|2em><math-up|RT><rsub|\<beta\>,\<cal-T\>><around|(|<text|fragment><around|(|z<rsub|x>*<around|(|N,q|)>|)>|)>\<leqslant\>A<rprime|'><rsub|m>\<assign\>2*S*<around|(|p<rsub|0>,k|)>*c<rsub|m><rsup|-1>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|A<rsub|m>\<less\>\<infty\>> is a constant which may depend on
    <math|m>, the opening, the labels and the extraction order, but not on
    the anchor <math|x> or on <math|<around|(|N,q|)>>. The late filter set
    and sequence may depend on <math|x>; the numeric coefficient and
    root-test bounds do not. The producer in
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-actual-shell-node> gives
    <math|A<rsub|m>=2*Q<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|quota>>*e<rsub|m>>. The finite
    extraction order may encode any fixed finite family of watches; the
    receipt is available for every such family, with its tester costs fixed
    before the eventual volume/period choice. The associated finite-volume
    fragment is the product of the grade-four insertion at the collar point
    and the grade-<math|d> insertion at the selected point, multiplied by
    <math|r<rsub|m><rsup|h>>, minus the grade-<math|D> insertion
    <math|z<rsub|x>> at the selected point. It lies in the prescribed chamber
    and has root-test norm bounded by the displayed cost. No common late
    filter over the entire (growing) set of collar grid points is asserted.
  </definition>

  Here \Peventually\Q is on the extraction filter over the volume and period
  variables, not a claim at each finite volume. Under its stated inputs, the
  construction in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-actual-shell-node>
  has two stages. First, a two-channel counterterm construction builds the
  fragment. At the collar site <math|x>, its two factors are the base
  insertion and the rescaled grade-four insertion; their product is corrected
  by one grade-<math|D> label. The grade identity
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-stress-grade> is exactly what makes that correction
  have the requested degree. The paid two-component insertion bounds its
  root-test cost, while the independent quota estimate bounds the correction
  label. Second, the corner-to-shell argument supplies common numeric bounds
  for all collar anchors. The late extraction event remains pointwise in the
  anchor; for a fixed interpolation stencil, one obtains a common event by
  intersecting the finitely many pointwise late events. This does not assert
  one event for the entire growing collar.

  That construction uses the following numerical gates for this input. The
  economics choice is

  <\equation*>
    t=1+<frac|1|64*<around|(|d+12|)>>,
  </equation*>

  with a subsequence <math|j<rsub|m>> for which
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|j<rsub|m>>/\<lambda\><rsub|m>\<to\>t>. The price
  estimate supplies, eventually, positivity and a small two-channel payment
  budget, together with the grade-<math|D> correction-price bound. In this
  normalization its exponential denominator is <math|c<rsub|m>=exp
  <around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|m>/32|)>>; at fixed opened <math|p<rsub|0>,k>,
  <math|S*<around|(|p<rsub|0>,k|)>> is fixed, so the root cost tends to zero.
  The separate lightness estimate supplies both

  <\equation*>
    32*r<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|p<rsub|0>>,<space|2em>a<rsub|m+1>*<around*|(|B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>+1+2\<cdot\>1024*B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>|)>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|p<rsub|0>>
  </equation*>

  eventually. These are used, respectively, for the collar/spacing margin and
  for the number of required coordinates in the opened chamber. The corner
  lemma combines them with the same-history run, throw, root-room, and
  subsidized-payment bounds. Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-actual-shell-node>
  supplies this shell-node input, in the form of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:ex-lattice-shell-receipt>, from the stated
  same-history menu, transfer, quota and price receipts and the chamber
  receipt whose birth-level threshold is uniform over all anchors in each
  level-<math|m> collar. Its late extraction event may still depend on the
  fixed anchor; the scale note is proved in the paragraph following that
  proposition. The coefficient bound in that proposition has exactly the
  birth-level dependence allowed here. Their application on the selected
  family is given in Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:c14-selected-annular-responses>
  below.

  The implication below takes the defined shell-node receipt as an input on
  one fixed family. It does not infer that receipt from the public
  extractor's record or change family, history, or scale sequence between the
  receipt and the stress law.

  <subparagraph|Openings.> A reference level <math|m> is <em|opened at> a
  level <math|k> (with parameter one) if <math|m\<leqslant\>k> and
  <math|B<rsub|k><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>>, as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-site-geometry>. The receipt in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:ex-lattice-shell-receipt> is stated for
  every opened pair, so it says nothing unless openings exist. They do, under
  the selected tab schedule.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Every reference level is opened><label|lem:ex-lattice-opening-exists>Assume
    the tab-schedule bounds <math|L<rsub|j>=a<rsub|j>/a<rsub|j+1>\<geqslant\>3>
    and <math|B<rsub|j+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|j><rsup|2>> for every
    <math|j>, where <math|B<rsub|j>> is the radius in the definition of
    <math|r<rsub|m>>. If <math|m> is opened at <math|k>, then it is opened at
    every <math|k<rprime|'>\<geqslant\>k>. For every level <math|m> and every
    <math|k<rsub|1>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> there is <math|k\<geqslant\>k<rsub|1>> at
    which <math|m> is opened.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Persistence is the induction in the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-centered-opened-copy>: if
    <math|B<rsub|j><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|j>>, then
    <math|B<rsub|j+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|j><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2*a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|j>*a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|j>=a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|j+1>>.
    For existence put <math|f<rsub|j>=a<rsub|j>*B<rsub|j><rsup|2>\<gtr\>0>.
    The two schedule bounds give

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|j+1>=a<rsub|j+1>*B<rsub|j+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><frac|a<rsub|j>|L<rsub|j>>*<space|0.17em>2*B<rsub|j><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><frac|2|3>*f<rsub|j>,
    </equation*>

    so <math|f<rsub|j>\<leqslant\><around|(|2/3|)><rsup|j-m>*f<rsub|m>\<to\>0>
    as <math|j\<to\>\<infty\>>. Choose <math|k<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>m> with
    <math|f<rsub|k<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|m>>, that is
    <math|B<rsub|k<rsub|0>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k<rsub|0>>>;
    then <math|m> is opened at <math|k<rsub|0>>. By persistence it is opened
    at <math|k=max <around|(|k<rsub|0>,k<rsub|1>|)>>.
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|A slotwise bound before the physical Ward construction.> The
  derivative bound needed by the annulus argument can be obtained without
  analytic continuation or physical rotations. Retain the centered one-field
  service and the all-copy construction of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-slab-copy-bounds>. For a fixed list
  of labels <math|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|n>> of grades
  <math|d<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,d<rsub|n>>, put
  <math|A=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around|(|d<rsub|i>+1|)>>. We claim that
  for every <math|\<alpha\>\<gtr\>1> there is
  <math|C<rsub|\<alpha\>,\<b-v\>>> such that the slab operator of this word,
  with mutual and boundary separation at least
  <math|r\<in\><around|(|0,1|]>>, satisfies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:scribe-slotwise-slab><around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|d><around|(|w|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|\<alpha\>,\<b-v\>>*r<rsup|-\<alpha\><rsup|2>*A>.
  </equation>

  Constants may depend on the fixed word of labels, but not on its positions
  or the slab width. First the adjacent-increment estimate above gives
  <math|a<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>a<rsub|j-1><rsup|\<alpha\>>> on a tail. If
  <math|j\<geqslant\>F> is the first level with
  <math|a<rsub|j>\<less\>\<epsilon\>>, then

  <\equation*>
    c<rsub|\<alpha\>>*\<epsilon\><rsup|\<alpha\>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|j>\<less\>\<epsilon\>,<space|2em>c<rsub|\<alpha\>>=min
    <around|(|1,a<rsub|F>|)>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation*>

  For <math|j\<gtr\>F> this follows from <math|a<rsub|j-1>\<geqslant\>\<epsilon\>>;
  for <math|j=F> it follows from <math|\<epsilon\>\<leqslant\>1>. Enlarge
  <math|F> to cover all fixed labels, their signed reflections and adjoints.
  Centered service with slack one bounds the price of slot <math|i> at level
  <math|k> by <math|J<rsub|i>*a<rsub|k><rsup|-<around|(|d<rsub|i>+1|)>>>. It
  is important to retain each <math|d<rsub|i>>, rather than replace all of
  them by their maximum.

  Choose <math|m> first with <math|a<rsub|m>\<less\>r/32>. The lower bound
  just proved gives <math|a<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>b*r<rsup|\<alpha\>>>, with
  <math|b=c<rsub|\<alpha\>>*32<rsup|-\<alpha\>>\<gtr\>0>. Choose
  <math|0\<less\>u\<less\>min <around|(|1/32,b/21|)>>, and then choose
  <math|k> first with <math|a<rsub|k>\<less\>u*<around|(|r/n|)><rsup|\<alpha\>>>.
  Since <math|n\<geqslant\>1> and <math|\<alpha\>\<gtr\>1>,

  <\equation*>
    20*n*a<rsub|k>\<less\>20*u*r<rsup|\<alpha\>>*n<rsup|1-\<alpha\>>\<less\>b*r<rsup|\<alpha\>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|m>,<space|2em>a<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|\<alpha\>>*u<rsup|\<alpha\>>*<around|(|r/n|)><rsup|\<alpha\><rsup|2>>.
  </equation*>

  Thus the two-scale placement argument of the all-copy proof applies
  unchanged. Its mixed-cell bound holds with different prices in the main
  slots: apply the planted inequality without replacing the individual root
  prices by a common maximum. In the reflected pairing of <math|2*q> main
  copies, each slot appears <math|4*q> times, so its bound is

  <\equation*>
    X<rsup|2<around|\||v|\|>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>J<rsub|i>*a<rsub|k><rsup|-<around|(|d<rsub|i>+1|)>>|)><rsup|4*q>.
  </equation*>

  The exterior price <math|X> remains independent of the replica number
  <math|q>, exactly as in the existing proof. The train-bound lemma therefore
  gives operator norm at most the product in parentheses. The lower bound for
  <math|a<rsub|k>> proves <eqref|eq:scribe-slotwise-slab>, absorbing the
  fixed <math|n> and the fixed slot prices in
  <math|C<rsub|\<alpha\>,\<b-v\>>>. Padding to the original slab width is a
  contraction. Uniqueness on the dense positive word domain identifies this
  operator with the already constructed operator, so obtaining a sharper
  estimate makes no new theory choice.

  Compression and evaluation at a character are contractions. Translating
  into a slab with fixed positive boundary padding therefore gives the same
  bound for the selected correlations at all configurations with mutual sup
  separation at least <math|r>. This is the proof of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-caps>, now retaining the slotwise
  exponents. The exact slot derivative identity raises only the
  differentiated slot's grade, one per derivative. Thus for every fixed
  integer <math|Q>, all derivatives of orders
  <math|0\<leqslant\>n\<leqslant\>Q> in the moving slot satisfy

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<partial\><rsup|n>*S<rsub|\<b-v\>><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|Q,\<alpha\>>*r<rsup|-\<alpha\><rsup|2>*<around|(|A+n|)>>.
  </equation*>

  The finitely many derivative labels and coordinate directions permit one
  maximum of the constants and thresholds. Choose <math|\<alpha\>\<gtr\>1> so
  close to one that <math|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsup|2>-1|)>*<around|(|A+Q|)>\<leqslant\>1>.
  For <math|0\<less\>r\<leqslant\>1> this gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:scribe-linear-derivative-price><around|\||\<partial\><rsup|n>*S<rsub|\<b-v\>><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|Q>*r<rsup|-<around|(|A+n+1|)>>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>n\<leqslant\>Q.
  </equation>

  The exponent has slope one in the derivative order. Choosing
  <math|\<alpha\>> after the finite derivative request changes only proof
  constants and thresholds, not the selected schedule, labels or correlation
  table.

  For each fixed watch, the stress slot has grade four and the other slots
  include its distinguished field and spectators. At a collar point <math|y>
  close to the distinguished origin, the least separation of the full word is
  at least <math|c<around|\||y|\|>> for a fixed <math|c\<gtr\>0>: the
  remaining points are fixed and separated, and the only shrinking separation
  is between the stress slot and the origin. Hence
  <eqref|eq:scribe-linear-derivative-price> yields
  <math|<around|\||H<rsub|a,n,m><around|(|y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|a><around|\||y|\|><rsup|-<around|(|A<rsub|a>+n+1|)>>>
  uniformly over the collar. For finitely many watches choose <math|Q> so
  large that <math|Q*<around|(|t-1|)>\<gtr\>max<rsub|a><around|(|A<rsub|a>+1|)>>.
  Then

  <\equation*>
    a<rsub|j<rsub|m>><rsup|Q>*r<rsub|m><rsup|-<around|(|A<rsub|a>+Q+1|)>>\<longrightarrow\>0,
  </equation*>

  because its logarithm divided by <math|s<rsub|m>> tends to
  <math|-Q*t+<around|(|A<rsub|a>+Q+1|)>\<less\>0>. This is exactly the
  remainder needed by the finite-order interpolation in the annular-field
  argument. Equivalently one may use the cloud allowance
  <math|c<rsub|a>=<big|sum><rsub|<math-up|spectator
  slots>>d<rsub|i>+<around|(|<text|number of spectator slots>|)>>; then
  <math|4+n+2+c<rsub|a>=A<rsub|a>+n+1>.

  <subsubsection|Vanishing against a finite tester annihilator>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|shrinking-annulus annihilation><label|lem:ex-lattice-annulus-annihilation>Let
    <math|S> be the selected hierarchy, <math|V> a derivative plan for
    <math|S>, and <math|J> a stress conserved with respect to <math|V> and
    the spin action of <math|S>. Assume the lattice configurations used in
    the shell argument are dense in the separated configuration space and
    that the flux and tester functionals are continuous there. Retain the
    centered-cell spectator bounds and the ordered moving-label point-limit
    identities on this same hierarchy and extraction. Assume the tab-schedule
    bounds of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-opening-exists>. For the
    finite family of watches <math|I<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,I<rsub|s>> below, let
    <math|p<rsub|0>> be the largest of their reference depths, with
    <math|p<rsub|0>=0> when <math|s=0>, and fix a level
    <math|k\<geqslant\><math-up|ref><around|(|D|)>> at which <math|p<rsub|0>>
    is opened; such a <math|k> exists by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-opening-exists>.
    Both <math|p<rsub|0>> and <math|k> depend on the family. Take
    <math|v\<in\>\<cal-F\><rsub|d>>, and let
    <math|U:\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>\<to\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>> be a smooth homogeneous
    Killing field of degree <math|p\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, so
    <math|U*<around|(|r*y|)>=r<rsup|p>*U<around|(|y|)>> for <math|r\<gtr\>0>.
    Suppose both

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ex-lattice-flux-grade>D+<around|(|3+p|)>=d+4
    </equation>

    and <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-stress-grade> hold, and the shell-node receipt
    holds at <math|<around|(|p<rsub|0>,k|)>> for the finite family of watches
    <math|I<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,I<rsub|s>>. Fix its witnessing scale ratio
    <math|t\<gtr\>1>. For each watch <math|I<rsub|a>>, the moving insertion
    is a component of the grade-four stress, so its initial tag degree is
    <math|q<rsub|a>=4>; let <math|c<rsub|a>> be the sum of grade plus one
    over all spectator slots, including the fixed grade-<math|d> insertion
    <math|v>. The extra one per slot is an allowed enlargement of the
    spectator allowance and pays the centered-service slack. Choose an
    integer <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>\<geqslant\>1> so large that

    <\equation*>
      q<rsub|a>+q<rsub|\<ast\>>+2+c<rsub|a>\<less\>q<rsub|\<ast\>>*t*<space|1em><around|(|1\<leqslant\>a\<leqslant\>s|)>.
    </equation*>

    For <math|0\<leqslant\>n\<leqslant\>q<rsub|\<ast\>>>, let
    <math|H<rsub|a,n,m><around|(|y|)>> denote the scalar insertion with the
    moving stress component of <math|J> at <math|y> raised <math|n> times,
    with the watch's fixed grade-<math|d> insertion <math|v> and all other
    spectator labels held fixed. The slotwise bound
    <eqref|eq:scribe-linear-derivative-price> supplies the quantitative
    separated-point bounds: for every sufficiently large <math|m> and every
    <math|y\<in\>\<cal-C\><rsub|r<rsub|m>>>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||H<rsub|a,n,m><around|(|y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|a><around|\||y|\|><rsup|-<around|(|q<rsub|a>+n+2+c<rsub|a>|)>>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>n\<leqslant\>q<rsub|\<ast\>>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|C<rsub|a>> is independent of <math|m> and <math|y>. In
    physical coordinates,

    <\equation*>
      \<partial\><rsub|\<ell\>>*H<rsub|a,n,m>=H<rsub|a,n+1,m>.
    </equation*>

    Thus along the lattice chord <math|t\<mapsto\>x+a<rsub|j<rsub|m>>*t*e<rsub|\<ell\>>>
    the derivative is <math|a<rsub|j<rsub|m>>*H<rsub|a,n+1,m>>; the
    <math|n>th chain member is <math|a<rsub|j<rsub|m>><rsup|n>*H<rsub|a,n,m>*<around|(|x+a<rsub|j<rsub|m>>*t*e<rsub|\<ell\>>|)>>.
    These explicit quantitative estimates, rather than smoothness alone, are
    the local regularity input used below. If
    <math|g<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,g<rsub|s>\<in\>\<bbb-C\>> satisfy

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ex-lattice-annulus-annihilator><big|sum><rsub|a=1><rsup|s>g<rsub|a>*\<tau\><rsub|I<rsub|a>,D><around|(|z|)>=0<space|1em><text|for
      every >z\<in\>\<cal-F\><rsub|D>,
    </equation>

    then, for any smooth windows <math|\<chi\><rsub|a>> valid at the
    respective watch configurations,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ex-lattice-annulus-flux-zero><big|sum><rsub|a=1><rsup|s>g<rsub|a><space|0.17em><math-up|Flux><rsub|J><around|(|\<chi\><rsub|a>,U,I<rsub|a>,v|)>=0.
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    If <math|s=0>, the asserted sum is empty and equals zero. Hence assume
    <math|s\<geqslant\>1>. Choose fixed representatives in <math|E<rsub|d>>
    for <math|v> and in <math|E<rsub|4>> for the sixteen components of
    <math|J>. The quotient map sends these representatives to the given
    fields, and the shell receipt applies to these fixed labels. Choose a
    smooth radial bump <math|\<varphi\>> on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>> which
    equals one on the ball of radius <math|1/2> and vanishes outside the unit
    ball. The selected watch configurations have their distinguished point at
    the origin and all other points separated from it. Since
    <math|r<rsub|m>\<to\>0>, for all sufficiently large <math|m> the rescaled
    bump <math|\<varphi\><rsub|m><around|(|y|)>=\<varphi\>*<around|(|y/r<rsub|m>|)>>
    is a valid window at every watch in the finite family, and no other
    insertion lies in its support. Conservation and the window-change
    identity therefore let us compute each flux using this same shrinking
    window.

    For each pair of tensor indices <math|i,j>, define

    <\equation*>
      W<rsub|i*j><around|(|y|)>=\<partial\><rsub|i>*\<varphi\><around|(|y|)>*<space|0.17em>U<rsub|j><around|(|y|)>,<space|2em>G<rsub|i*j,m><around|(|y|)>=W<rsub|i*j><around|(|y|)>*<space|0.17em>r<rsub|m><rsup|3+p>*<big|sum><rsub|a=1><rsup|s>g<rsub|a><space|0.17em><around|(|I<rsub|a>|)><space|-0.17em><math-up|asked><around|(|J<rsub|i*j>,v|)>*<around|(|r<rsub|m>*y|)>.
    </equation*>

    Each <math|W<rsub|i*j>> is integrable and supported in the unit ball. Its
    derivative vanishes near the origin, and the bump-support calculation
    places every point where <math|W<rsub|i*j>> is nonzero in the annulus
    with radii between <math|r<rsub|m>/4> and <math|2*r<rsub|m>> after
    scaling. The annihilation argument below uses the shell estimate on the
    slightly wider collar, so endpoint conventions do not matter.

    For each <math|m>, the shell-node receipt has root-test tolerance

    <\equation*>
      \<epsilon\><rsub|m>=2*S*<around|(|p<rsub|0>,k|)>*c<rsub|m><rsup|-1>\<longrightarrow\>0.
    </equation*>

    For this fixed <math|m> the interpolation stencil uses only finitely many
    grid points. Intersect the late filter sets supplied separately for those
    points; their finite intersection is still late. The corresponding finite
    tuple of correction labels lies in a bounded subset of a
    finite-dimensional product space, with <math|m> held fixed, so pass to
    one convergent subnet and take its coordinate limits. Its bound
    <math|A<rsub|m>> need not stay bounded as <math|m> increases: the
    correction labels disappear by the annihilator identity before the limit
    <math|m\<to\>\<infty\>> is taken. This single extraction handles every
    stencil point and every watch in the finite family; no subsequence is
    chosen watch by watch. The centered-cell bound gives a constant
    <math|A<rsub|a>> for the fixed spectators of watch <math|I<rsub|a>>, so
    their moment with each fragment is bounded by
    <math|A<rsub|a>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m>> on the late set. The opening
    dominates the watch's reference depth, as required by this bound. Along
    the convergent subnet, the ordered moving-label point-limit identity
    sends the correction insertion with limiting reference label <math|z> to
    <math|\<tau\><rsub|I<rsub|a>,D>*<around|(|\<pi\><rsub|D>*z|)>>. It sends
    the other term to the rescaled product insertion. Passing the inequality
    to the limit therefore gives an error at most
    <math|A<rsub|a>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m>>. Summing with coefficients
    <math|g<rsub|a>>, the limiting grade-<math|D> terms cancel by
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-annulus-annihilator>; the remaining norm is at most
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m>*<big|sum><rsub|a><around|\||g<rsub|a>|\|>*A<rsub|a>>.
    Thus the grid error tends to zero. Only this fixed finite family is used;
    no uniform bound over all watches is asserted. The one-coordinate Taylor
    argument described below transfers this grid error to the annulus. The
    two grade relations give <math|h=3+p>. Thus the expression multiplied by
    <math|W<rsub|i*j>> is precisely the scale-corrected insertion that occurs
    after changing variables <math|x=r<rsub|m>*y> in the flux integral: the
    window gradient contributes <math|r<rsub|m><rsup|-1>>, the homogeneous
    field contributes <math|r<rsub|m><rsup|p>>, and Lebesgue measure
    contributes <math|r<rsub|m><rsup|4>>. Their product is
    <math|r<rsub|m><rsup|3+p>>, and <math|h=d+4-D=3+p>. Hence this is exactly
    the factor <math|r<rsub|m><rsup|h>> in the finite-tester estimate; there
    is no additional scaling factor.

    The absolute value of each rescaled component integral is bounded by the
    uniform shell error times <math|<big|int><around|\||W<rsub|i*j>|\|>>.
    There are only sixteen components, so summing over them still tends to
    zero. The change-of-variables identity for the flux gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|a>g<rsub|a><math-up|Flux><rsub|J><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|m>,U,I<rsub|a>,v|)>=-<big|sum><rsub|i,j><big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>G<rsub|i*j,m><around|(|y|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y.
    </equation*>

    The left side is independent of <math|m> by conservation and the support
    separation. The right side tends to zero by the preceding integrable
    bound. Therefore that fixed left side is zero. Replacing
    <math|\<varphi\><rsub|m>> by <math|\<chi\><rsub|a>> uses the same
    window-change identity separately at each watch, proving
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-annulus-flux-zero>.
  </proof>

  The grid-to-annulus interpolation has an elementary quantitative form. For
  <math|q\<geqslant\>0> and <math|P\<gtr\>0>, set

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|0><around|(|P|)>=1,<space|2em>A<rsub|q+1><around|(|P|)>=1+4*P*A<rsub|q><around|(|P|)>.
  </equation*>

  Suppose <math|f<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,f<rsub|q>:<around|[|-P,P|]>\<to\>\<bbb-C\>>
  are differentiable with <math|f<rsub|i><rprime|'>=f<rsub|i+1>> for
  <math|i\<less\>q>, <math|<around|\||f<rsub|q>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>>
  throughout the interval, and <math|<around|\||f<rsub|0><around|(|x<rsub|j>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>>
  at <math|2<rsup|q>> ordered points whose consecutive gaps are at least one.
  Then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-annulus-interpolation>sup<rsub|<around|[|-P,P|]>><around|\||f<rsub|0>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*A<rsub|q><around|(|P|)>*\<epsilon\>.
  </equation>

  For the proof first take all functions real-valued. Pair consecutive nodes.
  The real mean-value theorem supplies an intermediate point at which
  <math|f<rsub|1>> is the difference quotient; its absolute value is at most
  <math|2*\<epsilon\>> because the gap is at least one. The
  <math|2<rsup|q-1>> intermediate points remain ordered with gaps at least
  one. Induction applied to <math|f<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,f<rsub|q>> bounds
  <math|<around|\||f<rsub|1>|\|>> by <math|2*A<rsub|q-1><around|(|P|)>*\<epsilon\>>.
  Integrating from one original node to an arbitrary point costs at most
  <math|2*P> times that bound, and the value at the node costs at most
  <math|\<epsilon\>>, giving <math|A<rsub|q><around|(|P|)>*\<epsilon\>>. For
  complex functions apply this real estimate separately to real and imaginary
  parts. Their two bounds imply the stated complex estimate, since
  <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Re\>*z|\|>+<around|\||\<Im\>*z|\|>>.

  The common-limit argument applies separately at each of the finitely many
  grid nodes in the interpolation stencil. Its estimate is uniform in the
  node because the shell-node receipt and the watch-cost constants are
  uniform on the collar. At those nodes the nonzero-coordinate condition
  places every watch in the generic region required by its cost-limit
  estimate.

  To pass from those grid points to an arbitrary point of the annulus, the
  derivative plan supplies the chains in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-annulus-interpolation>.
  Along a coordinate line, differentiating a disc insertion raises its first
  tag by one and multiplies by the level spacing. Thus the <math|i>th chain
  member is the finite sum with first tag raised <math|i> times and prefactor
  <math|a<rsub|j<rsub|m>><rsup|i>>, and its derivative is the next member.
  Since <math|\<lambda\><rsub|j<rsub|m>>/\<lambda\><rsub|m>\<to\>t\<gtr\>1>
  while <math|r<rsub|m>> has logarithmic spatial rate <math|-1>, the choice
  of <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>> above gives

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|a>+q<rsub|\<ast\>>+2+c<rsub|a>\<less\>q<rsub|\<ast\>>*t,<space|2em><text|equivalently><space|2em>q<rsub|\<ast\>>*<around|(|t-1|)>\<gtr\>q<rsub|a>+2+c<rsub|a>.
  </equation*>

  The iterated derivative estimate gives a uniform top-derivative bound of
  the form

  <\equation*>
    C*a<rsub|j<rsub|m>><rsup|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>*r<rsub|m><rsup|-<around|(|q<rsub|a>+q<rsub|\<ast\>>+2+c<rsub|a>|)>>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|C> is independent of <math|m>. Its logarithm divided by
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|m>> tends to <math|-q<rsub|\<ast\>>*t+<around|(|q<rsub|a>+q<rsub|\<ast\>>+2+c<rsub|a>|)>\<less\>0>,
  so the bound tends to zero. For the finite-node interpolation, induct over
  the set <math|K> of axes not yet discretized. On the next axis choose the
  nonzero shifted lattice coordinate provided by the one-dimensional stencil;
  leave the other coordinates fixed. At an intermediate stage only the
  already chosen coordinates are known to be nonzero. At the final stage all
  four are nonzero, so the grid-point representation and genericity estimates
  apply. Each coordinate move is at most <math|r<rsub|m>/128>; therefore the
  total displacement is at most <math|<around|\||K|\|>*r<rsub|m>/128\<leqslant\>r<rsub|m>/32>,
  within the collar margin. The collar-margin condition
  <math|<math-up|arm><around|(|q<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>*a<rsub|j<rsub|m>>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|m>/128>
  keeps each segment in the wider collar. Applying
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-annulus-interpolation> successively in the four
  coordinates gives a fixed finite factor, independent of <math|m>. The grid
  error and the top derivative both tend to zero, proving uniform convergence
  on the annulus. The constants depend on the finite family, the grade, and
  the chosen chain length, but not on <math|m>.

  <subsubsection|One field represents every flux>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|annular flux representation><label|prop:ex-lattice-annular-field>Assume,
    for every finite family of watches, the hypotheses of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-annulus-annihilation> for that
    family: the tab-schedule bounds, the shell-node receipt at the opened
    pair <math|<around|(|p<rsub|0>,k|)>> chosen for that family, and the
    separated-point bounds with their constants <math|C<rsub|a>>, cloud
    degrees <math|c<rsub|a>> and chain length <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>> for that
    family. Assume also that the hierarchy is faithful at grade <math|D>: a
    field in <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|D>> whose testers <math|\<tau\><rsub|I,D>>
    vanish for every watch <math|I> is zero. For each
    <math|v\<in\>\<cal-F\><rsub|d>> and each smooth homogeneous Killing field
    <math|U> of degree <math|p>, there exists one field
    <math|b\<in\>\<cal-F\><rsub|D>> such that for every watch <math|I>, every
    compatible smooth window <math|\<chi\>>, and every separated point
    configuration,

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|Flux><rsub|J><around|(|\<chi\>,U,I,v|)>=\<tau\><rsub|I,D><around|(|b|)>.
    </equation*>

    The same <math|b> works for all configurations, windows, and watches.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    For a finite family <math|I<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,I<rsub|s>>, define linear
    maps

    <\equation*>
      T:\<cal-F\><rsub|D>\<to\>\<bbb-C\><rsup|s>,<space|1em>T<around|(|z|)>=<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|I<rsub|1>,D><around|(|z|)>,\<ldots\>,\<tau\><rsub|I<rsub|s>,D><around|(|z|)>|)>,<space|2em>a=<around|(|<math-up|Flux><rsub|J><around|(|\<chi\><rsub|1>,U,I<rsub|1>,v|)>,\<ldots\>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-annulus-annihilation> says that
    every linear functional annihilating <math|<math-up|range><space|0.17em>T>
    also annihilates <math|a>. In finite-dimensional linear algebra, a
    subspace is the common kernel of all linear functionals vanishing on it.
    Hence <math|a\<in\><math-up|range><space|0.17em>T> and there is <math|b>
    with <math|T<around|(|b|)>=a>. This is the finite-tester step; it does
    not presume an infinite-dimensional Riesz representation.

    Choose a finite faithful family of watches whose testers distinguish
    elements of <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|D>>. Such a family exists because the
    complete family of watch evaluations separates fields, by the assumed
    faithfulness of the hierarchy at grade <math|D>, and a finite-dimensional
    field space has a finite subfamily of any separating family that still
    separates. Apply the finite result to that family to obtain <math|b>. For
    any further watch <math|I>, apply the result to the union of <math|I> and
    the faithful family. The hypotheses of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-annulus-annihilation>, assumed for
    every finite family, apply to this union, with its own opening
    <math|<around|(|p<rsub|0>,k|)>> chosen as in the lemma; the opening
    chosen for the faithful family alone may be too shallow for <math|I>. The
    finite result gives <math|b<rprime|'>>. On the faithful family,
    <math|b<rprime|'>> and <math|b> have the same tester values, so
    faithfulness implies <math|b<rprime|'>=b>. Therefore the flux identity
    holds for <math|I> as well. This argument also handles every compatible
    window, since the window-change identity identifies its flux with the
    chosen window value. Finally, the lattice configurations used above are
    dense among separated configurations. Both flux and the tester/disc
    functional are continuous there, so the identity extends from those
    configurations to all separated point configurations.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Translation and rotation Ward maps>

  Apply Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-annular-field> first to
  each constant Killing field <math|U<around|(|y|)>=e<rsub|\<mu\>>>, of
  degree <math|p=0>, with output grade <math|D=d+1>. For every input grade
  <math|d> and direction <math|\<mu\>> this gives a complex-linear map
  <math|P<rsub|d,\<mu\>>:\<cal-F\><rsub|d>\<to\>\<cal-F\><rsub|d+1>>.
  Linearity follows because both flux and the finite faithful tester map are
  linear; uniqueness on the faithful family makes the representing field
  linear in the input. The translation identity identifies the front
  insertion of <math|P<rsub|d,\<mu\>>*v> with the stress flux.

  Next take the rotational Killing field <math|U<around|(|y|)>=K*y>, where
  <math|K> is skew-symmetric. It is homogeneous of degree one, so the grade
  relation gives <math|D=d>. The same construction yields maps
  <math|R<rsub|d,K>:\<cal-F\><rsub|d>\<to\>\<cal-F\><rsub|d>> which are
  complex-linear in the field and real-linear in <math|K>, by linearity of
  the flux and uniqueness on the faithful testers. The angular-flux identity
  follows in coordinates centered at each watch's distinguished point, as in
  the flux definition. These two families are precisely the translation and
  rotation Ward maps; no additional stress tensor is selected in this step.

  <\remark>
    <dueto|public same-history application><label|rem:ex-lattice-stress-public-binding>The
    public route keeps the relevant objects on the family selected by the
    run: the extraction, hierarchy, finite-volume laws, and conserved stress
    all come from that same record. The endpoint invokes
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-actual-shell-node> on this
    family. It also needs the same ordered extraction, point-limit
    identities, centered-cell bounds, smooth faithful hierarchy and
    derivative plan used in the tester argument, the tab-schedule bounds that
    give an opening above the depths of each finite family of watches
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-opening-exists>), and the two
    schedule properties behind the scale note. The shell receipt alone
    supplies none of these. The resulting Ward conclusion is conditional on
    these inputs and on the incoming hypotheses of that proposition.
  </remark>

  The argument proves finite-tester annihilation and the unique-field
  representation conditional on the shell-node receipt and the stated
  regularity/conservation data. The local producer of that receipt is
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-actual-shell-node>; its
  same-history analytic and service inputs remain explicit dependencies.
  These quantitative hypotheses must hold for the selected history.

  <\corollary>
    <dueto|Annular responses on the selected
    family><label|cor:c14-selected-annular-responses>On the common funded
    family, the centered conserved stress constructed in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-stress-normalization> has
    translation and rotation response fields with the flux identities of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-annular-field>, on its same
    extracted smooth faithful hierarchy and derivative plan.
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    The service, terminal keeper and same-door responses are furnished by
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-selected-run-service>; their selected
    schedule and reserve conditions are verified in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:c14-selected-calibration-payments>. The
    shell-specific finite-mask inequalities and whole-polynomial transfer are
    checked in <hlink|the actual fine finite-mask
    payment|#par:shell-actual-mask>. The height growth is
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-shell-paid-growth>, with the
    retained adjacent-stage delay just verified. The shell schedule,
    lightness, uniformly placed stencils and finite quota product are proved
    in <hlink|the selected-family placement
    argument|#par:shell-actual-placement>. Thus
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-actual-shell-node> applies
    with this very family and its fixed stress representatives. Its
    coefficient bound depends on the birth level, as permitted by
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:ex-lattice-shell-receipt>; no uniform
    bound over birth levels is required.

    Fix a finite family of watches. Choose a reference depth beyond their
    finitely many centered-cell depths. The selected lightness estimates
    imply that this depth is opened at every sufficiently large subsequent
    level: on a tail, <math|a<rsub|j+1>*B<rsub|j+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|(|2/3|)>*a<rsub|j>*B<rsub|j><rsup|2>>,
    so the latter quantity tends to zero. Choose one opening for this watch
    family and then apply the shell-node receipt there. At each fixed
    interpolation stencil the intersection of its finitely many late sets is
    late. This is the only simultaneous use of collar anchors; it does not
    impose one late event on the entire growing collar.

    The point limits and smooth representatives are those of the original
    extracted table. Their finite differences represent the derivative plan
    by Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-insertion-derivative-relay>; the
    all-spectator kernel identification of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:finite-rank-kernel-transport> ensures that
    passing to the field quotient changes none of those tested limits. The
    centered estimates survive that fixed extraction. The slotwise all-copy
    estimate <eqref|eq:scribe-slotwise-slab> and its consequence
    <eqref|eq:scribe-linear-derivative-price> give the separated-point
    derivative bounds used in annular interpolation, with constants depending
    on the fixed family of watches. Conservation is that of the selected
    stress and holds on the whole separated domain. Since
    <math|a<rsub|j<rsub|m>>\<to\>0>, lattice approximants are dense in every
    fixed separated configuration window. Smoothness of the hierarchy and the
    compact support of the shell gradient make the testers and shell fluxes
    continuous there, so the identities extend from these approximants.
    Faithfulness is the defining all-spectator quotient property. These
    verify the remaining hypotheses of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-annulus-annihilation>
    for this family.

    The family was arbitrary. Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-annular-field>
    therefore represents each flux by one field, independent of watches and
    windows. Constant Killing fields give translations and linear skew
    Killing fields give rotations, with the grades prescribed by
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-stress-grade>. Linearity of their fluxes and
    faithfulness give linearity of the response maps. All operations use the
    same stress and extraction, completing the claimed application.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Positive spectral estimates for the exterior
  packets><label|sec:physical-ward-packet-prices>

  The exterior Ward argument uses spectral estimates before full rotation
  covariance has been proved. The spectral measure for one coordinate axis
  therefore cannot be identified with the spectral measure for another axis.
  The link between them will instead be the same tested correlation, with its
  reflection sign. We develop that link and the estimates it gives.

  Write <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|n>=e<rsup|-n>>. A sequence has exponent
  <math|\<alpha\>> if its norm is at most <math|C*e<rsup|\<alpha\>*n>> for
  all sufficiently large <math|n>, for some fixed <math|C>. Constants and the
  starting index may depend on the fixed fields and test profiles. Finite
  sums preserve an exponent; products add exponents; and an estimate with
  exponent <math|\<alpha\>> also holds with any larger exponent. These rules
  follow directly from the triangle and product inequalities.

  A cluster below means a finite complex linear combination of field words at
  fixed, internally distinct positions. A cluster is <em|on the
  <math|i>-hyperplane> if every point in every word has <math|i>th coordinate
  zero. It has reflection sign <math|\<eta\><rsub|i>\<in\><around|{|1,-1|}>>
  if reflection of its points and labels in that coordinate gives
  <math|\<eta\><rsub|i>> times the cluster. For each axis <math|i>, use its
  reflection-positive chamber and its joint translation spectral measure, as
  in the heat-vector construction. Denote the positive measure belonging to
  the cluster <math|A> by <math|\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|A>> and write
  <math|E=p<rsub|i>>. It is supported on <math|E\<geqslant\>0>. The
  positive-time weight makes it locally finite in energy, including on
  <math|0\<leqslant\>E\<leqslant\>1>; the measure itself need not have finite
  total mass.

  For completeness, this measure is obtained without an arbitrary boundary
  extension. Let <math|v<rsub|u>> be the chamber vector of <math|A>
  translated by <math|u> into the positive half-space, and let
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|u>> be its finite joint spectral measure. The commuting
  real-time group supplied by Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:complex-heat>,
  together with the three spatial translation groups, has the joint measure
  of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:spectral-measure>; only its Euclidean
  application is used here. Set <math|d*\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|A>=e<rsup|2*u*E>*d*\<rho\><rsub|u>>.
  For <math|v\<gtr\>u>, the semigroup relation
  <math|v<rsub|v>=e<rsup|-<around|(|v-u|)>*H<rsub|i>>*v<rsub|u>> gives
  <math|d*\<rho\><rsub|v>=e<rsup|-2*<around|(|v-u|)>*E>*d*\<rho\><rsub|u>>,
  so the definition is independent of <math|u>. Two admissible placements are
  compared through their maximum. On <math|E\<leqslant\>1> the multiplier is
  at most <math|e<rsup|2*u>>, proving the asserted finite mass there.

  The spectral input used here has the following precise form. If all the
  points of <math|A> lie above <math|-u> in coordinate <math|i>, then, for
  each <math|j\<ne\>i> and <math|k\<geqslant\>0>,

  <\equation*>
    <big|int>e<rsup|-2*u*E><around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|><rsup|2*k>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|A><around|(|p|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
  </equation*>

  when the inequalities at <math|-u> are strict. If <math|A> is on the
  <math|i>-hyperplane, there is, for each <math|j,k>, an integer <math|D>
  such that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:packet-polynomial-moment><big|int><around|(|1+E|)><rsup|-D><around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|><rsup|2*k>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|A><around|(|p|)>\<less\>\<infty\>.
  </equation>

  These assertions use only positive-time evolution, spatial translations,
  and the polynomial separated-point derivative bounds of the hierarchy. Here
  is the passage from those bounds to the second assertion. The Gram function
  of the translated positive-time cluster is a finite sum of separated
  correlations. Its <math|2*k>th transverse derivative is bounded by
  <math|C*t<rsup|-D<rsub|0>>> when the reflected halves have distance
  <math|t\<in\><around|(|0,1|]>>. The positive spectral representation gives

  <\equation*>
    <big|int>e<rsup|-t*E><around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|><rsup|2*k>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|A>\<leqslant\>C*t<rsup|-D<rsub|0>>.
  </equation*>

  To justify the moment before differentiating under the integral, take the
  <math|k>th forward difference of the translated vector. Its squared norm is
  the integral of <math|<around|\||e<rsup|i*h*p<rsub|j>>-1|\|><rsup|2*k>/<around|\||h|\|><rsup|2*k>>
  against the damped measure. The iterated fundamental theorem of calculus
  bounds this squared norm by the corresponding <math|2*k>th Gram derivative.
  Fatou gives the asserted moment bound. Dominated convergence, using
  <math|<around|\||e<rsup|i*x>-1|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||x|\|>>, then gives
  the moment identity. Finally integrate the preceding bound against
  <math|t<rsup|D<rsub|0>>*<space|0.17em>d*t> on <math|<around|(|0,1|)>>. For
  <math|E\<geqslant\>0>,

  <\equation*>
    <big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1>t<rsup|D<rsub|0>>*e<rsup|-t*E>*<space|0.17em>d*t\<geqslant\><frac|e<rsup|-1>|D<rsub|0>+1>*<around|(|1+E|)><rsup|-D<rsub|0>-1>.
  </equation*>

  Tonelli proves <eqref|eq:packet-polynomial-moment>. For a cluster with a
  strict positive-time margin, the same finite-difference argument at that
  fixed margin gives the exponentially weighted statement.

  <subsubsection|Moment subtraction with a positive response>

  For a real smooth compactly supported profile <math|f>, put
  <math|f<rsub|s><around|(|x|)>=s<rsup|-1>*f*<around|(|x/s|)>>,
  <math|s\<gtr\>0>. Choose once an even nonnegative smooth function
  <math|\<varphi\>>, supported where <math|1\<leqslant\><around|\||x|\|>\<leqslant\>2>,
  with integral one. Let <math|L<rsub|j>>, <math|0\<leqslant\>j\<less\>m>, be
  the Lagrange polynomials for the nodes <math|<around|(|j+2|)><rsup|2>>.
  Define a correction <math|B<rsub|m,s>*f> by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:packet-correction>B<rsub|m,s>*f=<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>m><big|sum><rsub|k\<geqslant\>0><around|[|X<rsup|k>|]>*L<rsub|j>*<space|0.17em>s<rsup|-2*k><space|-0.17em><around*|(|<big|int>x<rsup|2*k>*f<around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x|)>*<around*|(|x\<mapsto\>x<rsup|-2*k>*\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>|)><rsub|s*<around|(|j+2|)>>.
  </equation>

  The second sum is finite. All correction profiles vanish near zero.
  Interpolation of the monomials <math|X<rsup|r>>, <math|r\<less\>m>, shows
  that <math|R<rsub|m,s>*f=f-B<rsub|m,s>*f> has zero moments of orders below
  <math|2*m> when <math|f> is even. In fact the even moment of order
  <math|2*r> of a term with index <math|j,k> is
  <math|<around|(|s*<around|(|j+2|)>|)><rsup|2*r>> times the corresponding
  moment of <math|x<rsup|-2*k>*\<varphi\>>, multiplied by its coefficient.
  Summing the coefficients is exactly <math|<big|sum><rsub|j>L<rsub|j><around|(|X|)>*<around|(|j+2|)><rsup|2*r>=X<rsup|r>>;
  the odd moments vanish by parity.

  For an even real profile let <math|\<cal-L\>*f<around|(|E|)>> be its
  temporal Laplace response in the spectral formula. With the Fourier
  normalization used there, for <math|E\<gtr\>0> it is

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-L\>*f<around|(|E|)>=2*E*<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\>><frac|f<around|(|x|)>|x<rsup|2>+E<rsup|2>>*<space|0.17em>d*x.
  </equation*>

  Set <math|M<rsub|m,s>*f=\<cal-L\>*<around|(|R<rsub|m,s>*f|)>>. The rational
  interpolation identity

  <\equation*>
    <frac|1|x+y>-<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>m><frac|L<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>|<around|(|j+2|)><rsup|2>+y>=<frac|1|x+y>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<less\>m><frac|<around|(|j+2|)><rsup|2>-x|<around|(|j+2|)><rsup|2>+y>
  </equation*>

  follows by multiplying by <math|x+y>: the two polynomials agree at the
  <math|m> nodes and at <math|x=-y>. Scaling and integrating it gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:packet-positive-kernel>M<rsub|m,s>*f<around|(|E|)>=2*E*<big|int><space|-0.17em><space|-0.17em><big|int><frac|\<varphi\><around|(|v|)>*f<around|(|x|)>|x<rsup|2>+E<rsup|2>>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<less\>m><frac|<around|(|s*v*<around|(|j+2|)>|)><rsup|2>-x<rsup|2>|<around|(|s*v*<around|(|j+2|)>|)><rsup|2>+E<rsup|2>>,d*x*<space|0.17em>d*v.
  </equation>

  If <math|f\<geqslant\>0> and its support is in <math|<around|[|-s,s|]>>,
  every factor in this product lies between zero and one. The response is
  nonnegative. The identity and its bounds extend to <math|E=0> by continuity
  of the Laplace response; this retains any spectral mass at zero.

  For fixed <math|m> and <math|D\<leqslant\>2*m>, the same product gives,
  whenever <math|s\<geqslant\>1> and the support of <math|f<rsub|w>> lies in
  <math|<around|[|-s,s|]>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:packet-response-ceiling>0\<leqslant\>M<rsub|m,s>*f<rsub|w><around|(|E|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|m,f,D>*<around|(|w<rsup|-1>+s<rsup|D>|)>*<around|(|1+E|)><rsup|-D>.
  </equation>

  For <math|E\<leqslant\>1>, bound <math|f<rsub|w>> by
  <math|w<rsup|-1><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>> and use
  <math|<big|int><around|(|x<rsup|2>+E<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>*d*x=\<pi\>/E>. For
  <math|E\<geqslant\>1>, the product is at most <math|C<rsub|m>*min
  <around|(|1,<around|(|s/E|)><rsup|2*m>|)>>. The remaining integral, after
  multiplication by <math|2*E>, is bounded by <math|2*<big|int>f> because
  <math|E\<geqslant\>1>. These are the two terms in
  <eqref|eq:packet-response-ceiling>, with a larger fixed constant. Combining
  it with <eqref|eq:packet-polynomial-moment> gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:packet-response-moment><big|int>M<rsub|m,s>*f<rsub|w><around|(|E|)><around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|><rsup|2*k>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|A>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|w<rsup|-1>+s<rsup|D>|)>,
  </equation>

  where <math|D> is chosen first and <math|m> is then chosen sufficiently
  large. In particular <math|D> does not grow with the subsequently chosen
  subtraction order <math|m>. The ordinary double integrals needed for the
  spectral representation are also absolutely integrable. Zero moments
  through order <math|2*m-1> imply <math|<around|\||<wide|R<rsub|m,s>*f|^><around|(|t|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<around|\||t|\|><rsup|2*m>>
  near zero by Taylor's formula; away from zero it is Schwartz. Thus its
  absolute Laplace integral is at most <math|C<rprime|'>*<around|(|1+E|)><rsup|-2*m-1>>.
  Choose <math|2*m+1\<geqslant\>D> and apply
  <eqref|eq:packet-polynomial-moment>. This justifies Fubini before using
  reflection-sign cancellation, rather than inferring it from a signed
  integral.

  There is also a lower bound. Take an even nonnegative profile
  <math|\<chi\>> equal to one on <math|<around|[|-1,1|]>> and supported in
  <math|<around|[|-2,2|]>>. For <math|s\<geqslant\>1> containing the support
  of <math|\<chi\><rsub|w>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:packet-response-floor>M<rsub|m,s>*\<chi\><rsub|w><around|(|E|)>\<geqslant\>2<rsup|-m>*<around|(|E+w|)><rsup|-1>*<around|(|1+E|)><rsup|-2*m>*<space|1em><around|(|E\<geqslant\>0|)>.
  </equation>

  Indeed each kernel factor is at least <math|<tfrac|1|2><around|(|1+E|)><rsup|-2>>
  on the indicated supports. In the remaining integral restrict <math|x> to
  <math|<around|[|0,w*E/<around|(|E+w|)>|]>>. There
  <math|\<chi\><rsub|w>=w<rsup|-1>> and <math|x<rsup|2>+E<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2*E<rsup|2>>,
  which gives <math|2*E*<big|int>\<chi\><rsub|w><around|(|x|)>/<around|(|x<rsup|2>+E<rsup|2>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x\<geqslant\><around|(|E+w|)><rsup|-1>>.
  Continuity gives the endpoint <math|E=0>.

  <subsubsection|Changing the spectral axis by its reflection sign>

  For <math|Q\<subset\><around|{|1,2,3,4|}>>, <math|i\<in\>Q>, and profiles
  <math|f<rsub|j>>, write

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|i>*<around|(|h;f,Q|)>=<big|int>\<cal-L\>*h<around|(|p<rsub|i>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>Q\<setminus\><around|{|i|}>>f<rsub|j><around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|A><around|(|p|)>.
  </equation*>

  We use this expression only when its absolute integrability has been
  established by <eqref|eq:packet-response-moment> or by a positive-time gap.
  If <math|A> has opposite reflection signs at <math|i> and <math|j>, testing
  the same cluster autocorrelation in those two axes gives

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|i>*<around|(|f<rsub|i>;f,Q|)>=-P<rsub|j>*<around|(|f<rsub|j>;f,Q|)>.
  </equation*>

  For the full set of axes this is the spectral representation of the same
  ordinary integral on the two sides; their signs differ by reflection. For a
  smaller <math|Q>, insert in each omitted coordinate the bounded profile
  <math|\<chi\>*<around|(|p/<around|(|N+1|)>|)>>. It tends pointwise to one,
  and dominated convergence with the already integrable temporal response
  proves the stated identity. No relation between the two spectral measures
  has been assumed.

  Suppose <math|f<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>0> and the residual responses of
  <math|f<rsub|i>-b<rsub|i>> and <math|f<rsub|j>-b<rsub|j>> are nonnegative.
  Apply the identity to those two residuals and expand the two differences.
  Dropping the nonnegative full <math|j>-response gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:packet-axis-alternation>P<rsub|i>*<around|(|f<rsub|i>-b<rsub|i>;f,Q|)>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|i>*<around|(|f<rsub|i>-b<rsub|i>;f*<around|[|j\<leftarrow\>b<rsub|j>|]>,Q|)>+P<rsub|j>*<around|(|f<rsub|j>-b<rsub|j>;f*<around|[|i\<leftarrow\>b<rsub|i>|]>,Q|)>.
  </equation>

  A signed correction on the right can be replaced by any pointwise
  nonnegative majorant, since all its other factors are nonnegative.

  Here is why this inequality buys arbitrarily small losses even if the
  original profile widths shrink exponentially. Suppose both reflection signs
  occur in <math|Q>, <math|A> is on every hyperplane indexed by <math|Q>, and
  <math|w<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>0>. For every <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>,
  sufficiently high subtraction gives exponent <math|\<delta\>> for the
  residual response in axis <math|i>, with transverse profiles
  <math|<around|(|f<rsub|j>|)><rsub|e<rsup|w<rsub|j>*n>>>.

  We spell out the scale order. More generally allow
  <math|-K\<leqslant\>w<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>a>, and designate a set
  <math|U\<subset\>Q> containing every axis whose width may shrink. Outside
  <math|U> require <math|w<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>0>. There is a positive
  admissible correction exponent <math|a>. For
  <math|0\<leqslant\>d\<leqslant\>a>, <math|s\<geqslant\>1>, require also
  <math|w<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>d> and eventual containment of the scaled
  <math|i>-profile in <math|<around|[|-s*e<rsup|d*n>,s*e<rsup|d*n>|]>>. Then
  this correction scale works after sufficiently many moments have been
  subtracted. If <math|U> is empty, use <eqref|eq:packet-response-moment>
  with <math|k=0>. The transverse factors are uniformly bounded and the
  response has exponent <math|max <around|(|-w<rsub|i>,d*D|)>>. Choose
  <math|a*D\<less\>\<delta\>>; the claim follows.

  For the induction, change from <math|i> to an axis <math|j> with the
  opposite sign and apply the already established estimate with shrinking set
  <math|U\<setminus\><around|{|i|}>>. Let <math|a<rprime|'>\<gtr\>0> be its
  admissible correction exponent. Choose first a transverse moment <math|2*k>
  so large that

  <\equation*>
    a<rprime|'>*k\<gtr\>K<space|0.17em>\|<around|(|Q\<setminus\><around|{|i|}>|)>\<setminus\><around|{|j|}>\|+max
    <around|(|K,\<delta\>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Choose the corresponding moment exponent <math|D> in
  <eqref|eq:packet-response-moment>, and only then choose
  <math|0\<less\>a\<less\>a<rprime|'>> with <math|a*D\<less\>\<delta\>>. The
  finite formula <eqref|eq:packet-correction> shows that the correction of
  <math|<around|(|f<rsub|i>|)><rsub|e<rsup|w<rsub|i>*n>>> at
  <math|s*e<rsup|d*n>> is dominated in absolute value by
  <math|g<rsub|e<rsup|d*n>>>, for one fixed even nonnegative <math|g>
  vanishing near zero: its coefficients are bounded since
  <math|w<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>d>, and their supports form a finite union of
  fixed annuli. Apply the induction hypothesis at axis <math|j> to this
  corrected <math|i>-profile. This bounds the second term of
  <eqref|eq:packet-axis-alternation> with exponent <math|\<delta\>>.

  The correction at axis <math|j> is bounded by
  <math|h<rsub|e<rsup|a<rprime|'>*n>>> with fixed <math|h> vanishing near
  zero. Consequently

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||h<rsub|e<rsup|a<rprime|'>*n>><around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*e<rsup|-2*k*a<rprime|'>*n><around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|><rsup|2*k>.
  </equation*>

  The omitted normalization <math|e<rsup|-a<rprime|'>*n>> only improves this
  bound because <math|a<rprime|'>\<gtr\>0>. Each other transverse factor
  costs at most <math|C*e<rsup|K*n>>. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:packet-response-moment>
  now bounds the first term of <eqref|eq:packet-axis-alternation> with
  exponent at most

  <\equation*>
    -2*k*a<rprime|'>+K<space|0.17em>\|<around|(|Q\<setminus\><around|{|i|}>|)>\<setminus\><around|{|j|}>\|+max
    <around|(|-w<rsub|i>,d*D|)>\<less\>\<delta\>.
  </equation*>

  This proves the induction step. To arrange the finite induction, visit an
  axis of <math|U> and then erase it; if its sign is the same as the present
  axis, first pass through an opposite-sign axis. Every such intermediate
  pass is allowed because both signs occur in <math|Q>. Each erasure reduces
  <math|<around|\||U|\|>>, so the process ends. Moment orders and growing
  widths have been chosen in reverse order along this finite route, before
  <math|n> tends to infinity.

  Apply this estimate after replacing <math|f<rsub|i>> by <math|\<chi\>> and
  its width by <math|e<rsup|q*n>>, <math|q\<less\>0>, and take a fixed
  correction scale (<math|d=0>). Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:packet-response-floor>,
  restricted to <math|0\<leqslant\>E\<leqslant\>1>, proves the useful point
  estimate

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:packet-point-price><big|int><rsub|E\<leqslant\>1><frac|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>Q\<setminus\><around|{|i|}>><around|(|f<rsub|j>|)><rsub|e<rsup|w<rsub|j>*n>><around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>|E+e<rsup|q*n>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|A><around|(|p|)>=O<around|(|e<rsup|\<delta\>*n>|)>.
  </equation>

  This holds for every <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0> and all nonnegative even
  compactly supported transverse profiles. The constants may depend on their
  shapes.

  <subsubsection|Keeping a genuine temporal gap>

  A collinear cluster is on each hyperplane perpendicular to its line, but
  need not be on the hyperplane perpendicular to the line itself. We cannot
  use arbitrarily small temporal displacements on that axis. Fix
  <math|u\<geqslant\>0> so that translating the cluster by <math|u> places
  all its points strictly in the positive half-space. Set
  <math|c=\<varphi\>\<ast\>\<varphi\>> and
  <math|\<Phi\>=<wide|c|^>=<around|(|<wide|\<varphi\>|^>|)><rsup|2>>. Since
  <math|\<varphi\>> is real and even, <math|\<Phi\>> is nonnegative, even,
  rapidly decreasing, and <math|\<Phi\><around|(|0|)>=1>. Choose a smooth
  even cutoff <math|\<zeta\>> equal to one on
  <math|<around|[|-2*u-1,2*u+1|]>> and zero outside
  <math|<around|[|-2*u-2,2*u+2|]>>. Let <math|b<rsub|w>> be the real profile
  with Fourier transform <math|\<zeta\><around|(|t|)>*\<Phi\>*<around|(|w*t|)>>,
  and put <math|r<rsub|w>=c<rsub|w>-b<rsub|w>>. Then

  <\equation*>
    <wide|r|^><rsub|w><around|(|t|)>=<around|(|1-\<zeta\><around|(|t|)>|)>*\<Phi\>*<around|(|w*t|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-L\>*r<rsub|w><around|(|E|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|-<around|\||t|\|>*E>*<around|(|1-\<zeta\><around|(|t|)>|)>*\<Phi\>*<around|(|w*t|)>*<space|0.17em>d*t\<geqslant\>0.
  </equation*>

  The transform vanishes on the required gap. Its response is bounded by
  <math|C*w<rsup|-1>*e<rsup|-2*u*E>>. Also
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsub|w>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|u>>
  uniformly in <math|w>: Fourier inversion bounds it by a fixed multiple of
  the <math|L<rsup|1>> norm of <math|\<zeta\><around|(|t|)>*\<Phi\>*<around|(|w*t|)>>,
  and <math|\<Phi\>> is bounded.

  For all sufficiently small <math|w>, uniformly on
  <math|0\<leqslant\>E\<leqslant\>1>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:packet-gap-floor>\<cal-L\>*r<rsub|w><around|(|E|)>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|u>*<around|(|E+w|)><rsup|-1>.
  </equation>

  To see this, put <math|R=2*u+2> and choose <math|\<gamma\>\<gtr\>0> such
  that <math|\<Phi\><around|(|z|)>\<geqslant\>1/2> for
  <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>3*\<gamma\>>. Take
  <math|w*R\<leqslant\>\<gamma\>> and integrate over
  <math|R\<less\>t\<less\>R+\<gamma\>/<around|(|E+w|)>>. On this interval
  <math|w*t\<leqslant\>2*\<gamma\>>, <math|E*t\<leqslant\>R+\<gamma\>>, and
  <math|\<zeta\><around|(|t|)>=0>. Thus the integrand is at least
  <math|e<rsup|-<around|(|R+\<gamma\>|)>>/2>, and the interval has length
  <math|\<gamma\>/<around|(|E+w|)>>.

  Suppose the remaining three axes have both reflection signs and contain an
  axis <math|j> of sign opposite to the gap axis <math|i>. The preceding
  finite sign-change argument, applied only to those three axes, provides a
  growing subtraction scale <math|e<rsup|a<rprime|'>*n>> at <math|j>, with
  any prescribed loss <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>. Use
  <eqref|eq:packet-axis-alternation> with
  <math|f<rsub|i>=c<rsub|e<rsup|q*n>>>, <math|b<rsub|i>=b<rsub|e<rsup|q*n>>>,
  and <math|q\<less\>0>. Its second term is bounded by the uniform constant
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsub|e<rsup|q*n>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>> times
  that three-axis response; hence it has exponent <math|\<delta\>>. Its first
  term contains the annular correction <math|h<rsub|e<rsup|a<rprime|'>*n>><around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>>.
  For every fixed <math|k>,

  <\equation*>
    <big|int>\<cal-L\>*r<rsub|e<rsup|q*n>><around|(|E|)><around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|><rsup|2*k>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|A>\<leqslant\>C*e<rsup|-q*n>*<big|int>e<rsup|-2*u*E><around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|><rsup|2*k>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|A>.
  </equation*>

  The last integral is finite. If the other transverse widths have
  <math|w<rsub|l>\<geqslant\>-K>, the first term therefore has exponent at
  most <math|-2*k*a<rprime|'>+K<space|0.17em>\|<around|(|<around|{|1,2,3,4|}>\<setminus\><around|{|i|}>|)>\<setminus\><around|{|j|}>\|-q>.
  Choose <math|k> large enough to make this at most <math|\<delta\>>.
  Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:packet-gap-floor> gives the point estimate with a
  genuine gap in axis <math|i>. The measures have stayed fixed throughout.

  More explicitly, let the cluster lie on a line in direction <math|l>, have
  both reflection signs among the axes other than <math|l>, and have an
  opposite-sign axis to <math|l> among those axes. For arbitrary
  <math|w<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>0>, <math|q\<less\>0> and nonnegative even
  compactly supported <math|f<rsub|j>>, the full low-energy response in axis
  <math|i> has exponent

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:packet-line-price><choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<delta\>,>|<cell|i=l,>>|<row|<cell|-w<rsub|l>+\<delta\>,>|<cell|i\<ne\>l.>>>>>
  </equation>

  For <math|i=l> this is the gap argument. Otherwise apply
  <eqref|eq:packet-point-price> with <math|Q> the three axes other than
  <math|l>, then bound the remaining factor by
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|l>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*e<rsup|-w<rsub|l>*n>>.
  In the exterior application the profiles have shifted exponents
  <math|\<nu\><rsub|j>=1+w<rsub|j>>, where <math|w<rsub|l>\<geqslant\>-150>.
  Thus with <math|\<delta\>=1> the missing-axis exponent is
  <math|-\<nu\><rsub|l>+1=-w<rsub|l>\<leqslant\>150>; the line-axis exponent
  is <math|1>. These bounds hold even when the resolvent regularizer is
  <math|e<rsup|-3000*n>>.

  <subsubsection|The exterior of the low spectral box>

  This part needs no subtraction or mixed-sign assumption. Fix complex
  Schwartz profiles <math|f<rsub|j>>, exponents
  <math|w<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>-K>, and an axis <math|d> with
  <math|w<rsub|d>\<less\>0>. Assume <math|<wide|f|^><rsub|d>> vanishes on
  <math|<around|(|-1,1|)>>. Use the temporal profile
  <math|f<rsub|d>*<around|(|<space|0.17em>\<cdummy\><space|0.17em>/e<rsup|w<rsub|d>*n>|)>>
  and the transverse profiles <math|e<rsup|-w<rsub|j>*n>*f<rsub|j>*<around|(|<space|0.17em>\<cdummy\><space|0.17em>/e<rsup|w<rsub|j>*n>|)>>.
  If <math|<around|\||b|\|>=1>, their temporal response
  <math|L<rsub|n><around|(|E|)>> satisfies

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||L<rsub|n><around|(|E|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|<wide|f|^><rsub|d>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>*exp
    <around|(|-E/e<rsup|w<rsub|d>*n>|)>,<space|2em>E\<geqslant\>0.
  </equation*>

  Indeed the Fourier transform of the temporal profile is
  <math|e<rsup|w<rsub|d>*n>*<wide|f|^><rsub|d>*<around|(|e<rsup|w<rsub|d>*n>*t|)>>,
  supported where <math|<around|\||t|\|>\<geqslant\>e<rsup|-w<rsub|d>*n>>,
  with unchanged <math|L<rsup|1>> norm; the sign factor <math|b> has modulus
  one.

  Put

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|n>=<around|{|p:0\<leqslant\>p<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>1,<space|1em><around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<around|(|1+w<rsub|j>|)>*n>*<around|(|j\<ne\>d|)>|}>.
  </equation*>

  The product of the three transverse factors is at most
  <math|C*e<rsup|3*K*n>>. Outside the indicated transverse box, at least one
  factor has normalized argument of size greater than <math|e<rsup|n>>. Its
  Schwartz seminorm of order <math|r> therefore improves this to
  <math|C<rsub|r>*e<rsup|<around|(|3*K-r|)>*n>>. Given any real <math|y> and
  <math|R\<geqslant\>0>, choose <math|r> large enough. On
  <math|E\<leqslant\>1> this proves the bound
  <math|C*e<rsup|y*n>*e<rsup|-R*E>> off <math|C<rsub|n>>, with a harmless
  factor <math|e<rsup|R>>. For <math|E\<geqslant\>1>, exponential growth of
  <math|e<rsup|-w<rsub|d>*n>> gives, eventually,

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|-E/e<rsup|w<rsub|d>*n>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<around|(|y-3*K|)>*n>*e<rsup|-R*E>.
  </equation*>

  For example choose <math|n> so that <math|e<rsup|-w<rsub|d>*n>\<geqslant\>R+<around|\||y-3*K|\|>*n+1>
  and use <math|E\<geqslant\>1>. Thus the full packet satisfies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:packet-exterior-price><around|\||\<b-1\><rsub|C<rsub|n><rsup|c>><around|(|p|)>*L<rsub|n><around|(|p<rsub|d>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<ne\>d>e<rsup|-w<rsub|j>*n>*f<rsub|j>*<around|(|p<rsub|j>/e<rsup|w<rsub|j>*n>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*e<rsup|y*n>*e<rsup|-R*p<rsub|d>>.
  </equation>

  Choose <math|R> large enough to place the two fixed clusters strictly
  inside the positive-time chamber. The exponentially weighted moment
  statement then makes the majorant integrable for each of the four positive
  measures in their polarization identity. Integrating and adding that finite
  identity proves exponent <math|y> for the exterior cross response. In
  particular <math|y=-101> is available, without taking a boundary value at
  zero energy.

  The elementary zeroth response used inside <math|C<rsub|n>> is also
  explicit. If <math|1+w<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>-K>, then

  <\equation*>
    <big|int><rsub|E\<leqslant\>1><frac|<big|prod><rsub|j\<ne\>d>\<chi\><rsub|e<rsup|<around|(|1+w<rsub|j>|)>*n>><around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>|E+1>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|d><rsup|A><around|(|p|)>=O<around|(|e<rsup|3*K*n>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Each of three profiles is bounded by <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<chi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*e<rsup|K*n>>
  and the remaining integral is finite. With <math|K=300> the exponent is
  <math|900>.

  <subsubsection|The diagonal-stress estimate and its algebraic reduction>

  For every <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>, even nonnegative smooth compactly
  supported transverse profiles <math|f<rsub|j>>, and
  <math|w<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>a\<leqslant\>0>, the packet application also
  needs the improved diagonal-stress bound

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:packet-cone-required><big|int><rsub|E\<leqslant\>1><frac|E*e<rsup|4*a*n>|<around|(|E+e<rsup|a*n>|)><rsup|4>>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<ne\>d><around|(|f<rsub|j>|)><rsub|e<rsup|w<rsub|j>*n>><around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|d><rsup|J<rsub|d*d>><around|(|p|)>=O<around|(|e<rsup|<around|(|\<delta\>+4*a|)>*n>|)>,<space|2em>w<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>a\<leqslant\>0.
  </equation>

  It is a separate consequence of stress conservation, not of
  <eqref|eq:packet-point-price>. The following finite algebra explains the
  normalization in its proof. Put <math|w=e<rsup|a*n>> and keep one
  sufficiently large subtraction order <math|m> fixed. The subtracted
  differences of profiles at widths <math|w> and <math|2*w> give three real
  responses <math|F,G,H> with <math|F\<geqslant\>0>, <math|G\<leqslant\>0>,
  <math|H\<geqslant\>0>. Conservation and the fixed-band estimates are
  required to supply real sequences <math|A,B,V,W,R,T> such that, with
  <math|x=\<delta\>+2*a>, each of

  <\equation*>
    V-A,<space|1em>W-4*B,<space|1em>R-V,<space|1em>T-4*W,<space|1em>F-<around|(|A-B|)>,<space|1em>G-<around|(|V-W|)>,<space|1em>H-<around|(|R-T|)>
  </equation*>

  has exponent <math|x>. These are estimates on the same subtracted profiles.
  They imply <math|F=O<around|(|e<rsup|x*n>|)>>: if <math|E<rsub|n>> is the
  sum of their absolute errors, then <math|G\<leqslant\>0> gives
  <math|A-4*B\<leqslant\>3*E<rsub|n>> and <math|H\<geqslant\>0> gives
  <math|A-16*B\<geqslant\>-7*E<rsub|n>>. Hence
  <math|B\<leqslant\><around|(|10/12|)>*E<rsub|n>> and
  <math|0\<leqslant\>F\<leqslant\>A-B+E<rsub|n>\<leqslant\><around|(|13/2|)>*E<rsub|n>>.
  The constants can be enlarged freely; the exponent is unchanged.

  The positive response kernel of this difference has a lower bound
  <math|c<rsub|m>*E*w<rsup|2>/<around|(|E+w|)><rsup|4>> on
  <math|0\<leqslant\>E\<leqslant\>1>. This follows directly from
  <eqref|eq:packet-positive-kernel>, at the fixed subtraction scale
  <math|s=8>. Write <math|\<varphi\><rsub|k><around|(|x|)>=x<rsup|-2*k>*\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>>
  and compare its widths <math|w> and <math|2*w>, with
  <math|0\<less\>w\<leqslant\>1>. After scaling back to
  <math|x\<in\><math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<varphi\>>, the factor
  <math|<around|(|x<rsup|2>*w<rsup|2>+E<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>> and every
  numerator of the product decrease when <math|w> is replaced by <math|2*w>.
  The denominators of the product do not change. Thus every such dyadic
  response is nonnegative. For <math|k=0> and <math|E\<leqslant\>1>, the
  product at <math|2*w*x> is at least <math|2<rsup|-m>*2<rsup|-2*m>>.
  Furthermore, since <math|1\<leqslant\><around|\||x|\|>\<leqslant\>2>,

  <\equation*>
    <frac|1|w<rsup|2>*x<rsup|2>+E<rsup|2>>-<frac|1|4*w<rsup|2>*x<rsup|2>+E<rsup|2>>\<geqslant\><frac|w<rsup|2>|400*<around|(|E+w|)><rsup|4>>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed the numerator of the difference is
  <math|3*w<rsup|2>*x<rsup|2>\<geqslant\>w<rsup|2>>, and its positive
  denominator is at most <math|400*<around|(|E+w|)><rsup|4>>. Integrate
  against <math|\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|v|)>>, both of
  integral one, and multiply by <math|2*E>. The bound follows with
  <math|c<rsub|m>=2\<cdot\>2<rsup|-3*m>/400>; <math|E=0> follows by
  continuity. Multiplying its integral bound by
  <math|c<rsub|m><rsup|-1>*w<rsup|2>> would give exactly
  <eqref|eq:packet-cone-required>, with exponent <math|\<delta\>+4*a>. For
  the intended choice <math|a=-99>, <math|\<delta\>=1>, this is <math|-395>.
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-ward-stress-cone> proves the
  conservation and fixed-band estimates that produce these seven errors for
  the same stress profiles. Together with the reduction above, it supplies
  the stated cone bound used in the exterior packet argument.

  <subsection|Conservation and the diagonal-stress spectral
  bound><label|sec:physical-ward-stress-cone>

  We now supply the conservation estimates required in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-ward-packet-prices>. The stress
  tensor <math|J<rsub|a*b>> is real, symmetric, conserved, and covariant
  under signed coordinate permutations. Its derivatives are the actual
  insertion derivatives of the same hierarchy. The argument requires neither
  full rotation covariance nor a value of a correlation at a collision.

  Write <math|K<rsub|a*b*c*d><around|(|x|)>=S*<around|(|J<rsub|a*b><around|(|x|)>,J<rsub|c*d><around|(|0|)>|)>>
  for <math|x\<ne\>0>. Choose one positive integer <math|D> large enough that
  these finitely many kernels and their first derivative kernels are bounded
  by <math|C*min <around|(|1,<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|)><rsup|-D>>.
  Such an integer exists by the separated polynomial derivative bounds. In
  this section a test functional is required only on products
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|i>h<rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>> of one-dimensional
  Schwartz functions. We do not assert a collision extension with additional
  positivity properties.

  <subsubsection|A symmetric extension and its contact terms>

  Choose compactly supported smooth functions <math|g<rsub|r>> with
  <math|g<rsub|r><rsup|<around|(|k|)>><around|(|0|)>=\<delta\><rsub|r*k>> for
  <math|k\<less\>D+1>, and <math|g<rsub|r>*<around|(|-x|)>=<around|(|-1|)><rsup|r>*g<rsub|r><around|(|x|)>>.
  For example use <math|x<rsup|r>/r>! times a fixed even cutoff equal to one
  near zero. Put <math|\<Pi\><rsub|D>*h=<big|sum><rsub|r\<less\>D>h<rsup|<around|(|r|)>><around|(|0|)>*g<rsub|r>>.
  Define

  <\equation*>
    R<rsub|a*b*c*d><around|(|h|)>=<big|int>K<rsub|a*b*c*d><around|(|x|)>*<around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i>h<rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>-<big|prod><rsub|i>\<Pi\><rsub|D>*h<rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>|)>*d*x.
  </equation*>

  The difference of products is a finite telescoping sum, each of whose terms
  contains one factor vanishing to order <math|D> at zero. Taylor's theorem
  bounds that factor by <math|C<around|\||x<rsub|i>|\|><rsup|D>\<leqslant\>C<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>><rsup|D>>
  near zero. Thus the integrand is bounded there. Away from zero the kernel
  is bounded and each product is integrable. This proves absolute
  integrability and multilinearity. If one <math|h<rsub|i>> vanishes to order
  <math|D>, its Taylor projection is zero and <math|R<around|(|h|)>> equals
  the ordinary integral of the kernel.

  Use the same cutoff in all four coordinates. The product subtraction
  commutes with signed coordinate permutations. The original kernel's tensor
  law, symmetry in each index pair and exchange of the two pairs therefore
  pass to <math|R>. Pair exchange uses translation, permutation of insertions
  and inversion <math|x\<mapsto\>-x>; the rank-four tensor has positive
  inversion sign.

  Let <math|\<Delta\><rsub|b*c*d><around|(|h|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a>R<rsub|a*b*c*d>*<around|(|h*<around|[|a\<leftarrow\>h<rsub|a><rprime|'>|]>|)>>.
  If one factor <math|h<rsub|i>> vanishes to order <math|D+1>, integration by
  parts is legitimate and conservation gives
  <math|\<Delta\><rsub|b*c*d><around|(|h|)>=0>. Indeed the original test and
  each differentiated test cancel the stated collision powers; integrate
  first along a coordinate line whose transverse coordinates are nonzero,
  where the kernel is smooth, and then use Fubini. Those exceptional
  transverse coordinates have Lebesgue measure zero. The Schwartz decay and
  the same integrable bounds justify the integration by parts. This avoids
  imposing an unproved boundary value at the origin.

  Replacing the four factors successively by their Taylor projections of
  order <math|D+1> now shows that <math|\<Delta\>> depends on finitely many
  jets at zero. We normalize its linear jets. Let
  <math|h<rsup|<around|(|a|)>>> have factor <math|g<rsub|1>> in coordinate
  <math|a> and <math|g<rsub|0>> elsewhere, and set
  <math|c<rsub|a*b*c*d>=\<Delta\><rsub|b*c*d><around|(|h<rsup|<around|(|a|)>>|)>>.
  Reflection of a coordinate appearing an odd number of times among
  <math|a,b,c,d> changes this number's sign, so <math|c<rsub|a*b*c*d>=0>
  unless the indices occur in pairs. In the three possible pair patterns,
  coordinate permutation and symmetry in <math|c,d> show
  <math|c<rsub|a*b*c*d>=c<rsub|b*a*c*d>=c<rsub|c*d*a*b>>. For instance, the
  patterns <math|a*a*b*b>, <math|a*b*a*b> and <math|a*b*b*a> reduce
  respectively to swapping the two coordinate names, swapping them followed
  by the last-pair symmetry, and those same two operations. Thus the
  correction

  <\equation*>
    <wide|R|~><rsub|a*b*c*d><around|(|h|)>=R<rsub|a*b*c*d><around|(|h|)>-c<rsub|a*b*c*d>*<big|prod><rsub|i>h<rsub|i><around|(|0|)>
  </equation*>

  retains all the stated tensor symmetries and all collision-free
  evaluations. Its divergence has zero linear jets. Inversion makes that
  divergence odd, so its jets of even total order are also zero. Its finite
  jet expansion therefore contains only total orders at least three.

  For real frequency profiles <math|f<rsub|i>> let
  <math|T<rsub|a*b*c*d><around|(|f|)>=<wide|R|~><rsub|a*b*c*d><around|(|<around|(|<wide|f|^><rsub|i>|)><rsub|i>|)>>,
  and write <math|P<rsub|i>*T> for insertion of the multiplier
  <math|p<rsub|i>> in that frequency profile. Our Fourier convention is
  <math|<around|(|<wide|f|^>|)><rprime|'>=-i*<wide|p*f|^>>. Consequently

  <\equation*>
    Q<rsub|b*c*d>=<big|sum><rsub|a>P<rsub|a>*T<rsub|a*b*c*d>
  </equation*>

  is <math|i> times the divergence functional. If all profile widths are
  <math|e<rsup|w<rsub|i>*n>> with <math|w<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>a\<leqslant\>0>,
  its finite jet expansion has exponent <math|3*a>. Indeed a jet of
  multiorder <math|\<alpha\>> is a fixed coefficient times <math|exp
  <around|(|n*<big|sum><rsub|i>w<rsub|i>*\<alpha\><rsub|i>|)>>, and its total
  order is at least three. Multiplication by one extra frequency coordinate
  adds at most <math|a> to this exponent. These are actual estimates for the
  normalized contact terms.

  <subsubsection|Fixing one frequency band>

  We need a second estimate: if the profile in axis <math|i> is fixed,
  compactly supported and vanishes near zero, and the other widths are
  <math|e<rsup|w<rsub|j>*n>>, <math|w<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>0>, then
  <math|T<rsub|a*b*c*d>> has every positive exponent <math|\<delta\>>,
  provided the other profiles have the reflection parities of these four
  tensor indices. We give the details that make this estimate independent of
  a rotation law.

  First extend the positive subtraction argument to an odd temporal profile
  <math|f>. Define its correction by

  <\equation*>
    B<rsup|<math-up|odd>><rsub|m,s>*f<around|(|p|)>=p<rsup|-1>*B<rsub|m,s>*<around|(|p*f|)><around|(|p|)>.
  </equation*>

  The numerator vanishes near zero, so this is a smooth compactly supported
  function. The residual has all moments below <math|2*m> equal to zero: its
  even moments vanish by oddness, and each odd moment is an even moment of
  the ordinary residual of <math|p*f>. For <math|E\<gtr\>0> its signed
  Laplace response is

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-L\><rsub|-1>*<around|(|f-B<rsup|<math-up|odd>><rsub|m,s>*f|)><around|(|E|)>=-2*i*<big|int><frac|R<rsub|m,s>*<around|(|p*f|)><around|(|p|)>|p<rsup|2>+E<rsup|2>>*<space|0.17em>d*p.
  </equation*>

  The positive-kernel formula still applies to the integral on the right. For
  fixed odd <math|f>, <math|<around|\||f<around|(|p|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>L<around|\||p|\|>>
  by the fundamental theorem of calculus. If its support is in
  <math|<around|[|-R,R|]>>, <math|R\<geqslant\>1>, then
  <math|<around|\||p*f<rsub|w><around|(|p|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>L*p<rsup|2>/w<rsup|2>>
  on <math|<around|\||p|\|>\<leqslant\>R*w>. Every kernel factor is bounded
  by <math|C<rsub|m,s>*<around|(|1+E|)><rsup|-2*m>> in product. When
  <math|E\<leqslant\>w>, use <math|p<rsup|2>/<around|(|p<rsup|2>+E<rsup|2>|)>\<leqslant\>1>;
  when <math|E\<geqslant\>w>, use <math|p<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>R<rsup|2>*w<rsup|2>>
  and <math|<around|(|p<rsup|2>+E<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>E<rsup|-2>>.
  Integration over an interval of length <math|2*R*w> gives in both cases

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<cal-L\><rsub|-1>*<around|(|f<rsub|w>-B<rsup|<math-up|odd>><rsub|m,s>*f<rsub|w>|)><around|(|E|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|E+w|)><rsup|-1>*<around|(|1+E|)><rsup|-2*m>.
  </equation*>

  The positive lower bound in <eqref|eq:packet-response-floor> therefore
  majorizes this by <math|C<rprime|'>*M<rsub|m,s>*\<chi\><rsub|w><around|(|E|)>>,
  uniformly for <math|0\<less\>w\<leqslant\>1> when <math|s> contains the
  supports. The assertion at <math|E=0> follows by continuity. For an even
  profile, choose an even nonnegative compactly supported majorant
  <math|g\<geqslant\><around|\||f|\|>> and use positivity on <math|g+f> and
  <math|g-f> instead. Thus either parity has a positive response majorant.

  For two clusters <math|A,B>, the common projection-valued measure gives a
  cross-measure estimate. If <math|<around|\||q|\|><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>u*v>
  with <math|u,v\<geqslant\>0>, then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:stress-cross-price><around|\||\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|B,A><around|(|q|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>t*<big|int>u*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|B>+t<rsup|-1>*<big|int>v*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|A>,<space|2em>t\<gtr\>0.
  </equation>

  To prove it, first use a common admissible positive-time placement.
  Relative to the sum of its two finite diagonal spectral measures, the
  <math|2\<times\>2> matrix of densities is positive almost everywhere: test
  its quadratic form on the countable dense set of vectors with rational real
  and imaginary parts. Its off-diagonal entry is therefore bounded by the
  geometric mean of the diagonal entries. Integrate, apply Cauchy\USchwarz
  and then <math|<sqrt|U*V>\<leqslant\>t*U+t<rsup|-1>*V>. Exponential
  undamping multiplies all entries by the same positive function, so
  preserves the inequality. Truncation and absolute integrability justify its
  use for the responses here, including energy-zero atoms.

  Suppose each cluster has both reflection signs among the four axes and is
  on every coordinate hyperplane. In a chosen coordinate subtract enough
  moments from its frequency profile, using the even or odd correction
  according to the product of the two reflection signs. The previous
  majorant, the mixed-sign estimate of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-ward-packet-prices>
  for each diagonal measure, and <eqref|eq:stress-cross-price> give exponent
  <math|\<delta\>> for the tested cross correlation. All transverse profiles
  can first be majorized by fixed even nonnegative profiles. There are only
  two diagonal measures and finitely many polarization integrability
  conditions, so choose the subtraction order after all of them. This proves
  the estimate with one subtracted coordinate.

  Now keep a profile in coordinate <math|i> fixed and away from zero. At
  another coordinate <math|j> split the shrinking profile into its
  moment-zero residual and its finite correction. The residual is controlled
  by the preceding estimate. Each correction is a finite linear combination
  of fixed annular profiles, with coefficients of exponent at most zero,
  since <math|w<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>0>. For odd parity the additional division
  by <math|p<rsub|j>> is harmless on those fixed annuli. Apply induction to
  the smaller set of shrinking coordinates in each correction term. At the
  base every profile is fixed, so the functional is constant. The correction
  order may depend on all profiles already fixed at that stage; only finitely
  many such choices occur. No uniform choice over an infinite family of
  profiles is required.

  To apply this to stress components, an off-diagonal component
  <math|J<rsub|a*b>>, <math|a\<ne\>b>, already has both signs: it is odd in
  axes <math|a,b> and even in the other two. A diagonal component is replaced
  by <math|D<rsub|i>*J<rsub|a*a>>, which is odd in <math|i> and even in the
  other axes. Move that derivative to the fixed <math|i>-profile by
  integration by parts, dividing that frequency profile by <math|p<rsub|i>>.
  This is smooth because the profile vanishes near zero. The first and second
  insertions give respectively the factors <math|-i> and <math|i>; doing both
  replacements composes these identities. Sufficiently many moments in the
  subtracted <math|j>-profile justify the ordinary integrals and integration
  by parts, as in the extension construction above.

  Choose representative insertion tokens for these field labels. Average over
  the four commuting reflection involutions with the desired signs. This
  finite projection leaves the represented field unchanged and produces
  actual clusters with those exact signs; their positions are still zero. The
  two modified stress labels thus satisfy the mixed-sign hypotheses. The
  preceding finite induction proves the asserted fixed-band estimate for the
  original extended stress functional, with the required transverse parities.
  It also applies after any fixed finite number of frequency multiplications,
  with their corresponding extra scale factors.

  <subsubsection|Three responses linked by conservation>

  Fix a spectral axis <math|i>. All functionals in this subsection refer to
  the same normalized extension. Define

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|T<rsub|0>>|<cell|=T<rsub|i*i*i*i>,>>|<row|<cell|T<rsub|1>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|a,j\<ne\>i>P<rsub|j>*P<rsub|a>*T<rsub|a*i*j*i>,>>|<row|<cell|T<rsub|2>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|a,b,c,j\<ne\>i>P<rsub|j>*P<rsub|a>*P<rsub|b>*P<rsub|c>*T<rsub|c*j*a*b>.>>>>
  </align*>

  The indices in the last expression are grouped as the pairs
  <math|<around|(|c,j|)>> and <math|<around|(|a,b|)>>. Symmetry in and
  between the pairs, and <math|Q<rsub|b*c*d>=<big|sum><rsub|a>P<rsub|a>*T<rsub|a*b*c*d>>,
  give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:stress-conservation-chain>T<rsub|1>=P<rsub|i><rsup|2>*T<rsub|0>+C<rsub|1>,<space|2em>T<rsub|2>=P<rsub|i><rsup|2>*T<rsub|1>+C<rsub|2>.
  </equation>

  More explicitly,

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|1>=<big|sum><rsub|j\<ne\>i>P<rsub|j>*Q<rsub|i*j*i>-P<rsub|i>*Q<rsub|i*i*i>,<space|2em>C<rsub|2>=<big|sum><rsub|j,a,b\<ne\>i>P<rsub|j>*P<rsub|a>*P<rsub|b>*Q<rsub|j*a*b>-P<rsub|i>*<big|sum><rsub|j,a\<ne\>i>P<rsub|j>*P<rsub|a>*Q<rsub|a*j*i>.
  </equation*>

  Expand the two divergence sums into their <math|i> term and their remaining
  terms to verify these identities; commuting the frequency multipliers and
  exchanging the tensor pairs identifies the displayed
  <math|T<rsub|1>,T<rsub|2>>. When all four widths are at most
  <math|e<rsup|a*n>>, the contact bounds above give exponents <math|4*a> and
  <math|6*a> for <math|C<rsub|1>,C<rsub|2>>, respectively. They also show
  that <math|C<rsub|1>> vanishes if the <math|i>-profile has zero moments
  through order <math|D+1>, and <math|C<rsub|2>> if it has zero moments
  through order <math|D+3>: multiplication shifts the required moment order
  by at most one or three.

  Fix <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>, <math|a\<leqslant\>0>, and three even,
  nonnegative, smooth compactly supported transverse profiles
  <math|f<rsub|j>>, <math|j\<ne\>i>. Keep their shapes fixed, with width
  exponents <math|w<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>a>. The fixed-band result, with the
  <math|i>-profile fixed away from zero, gives exponents

  <\equation*>
    \<delta\>,<space|2em>\<delta\>+2*a,<space|2em>\<delta\>+4*a
  </equation*>

  for <math|T<rsub|0>,T<rsub|1>,T<rsub|2>>: their respective sums contain
  zero, two and four transverse frequency multipliers. The parity hypotheses
  hold because each multiplier flips the sign in its coordinate, cancelling
  the corresponding stress index sign.

  Let <math|w=e<rsup|a*n>> and <math|\<varphi\><rsub|k><around|(|p|)>=p<rsup|-2*k>*\<varphi\><around|(|p|)>>
  as in the previous section. Write <math|h<rsub|p,w,k>=<around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|k>|)><rsub|p*w>>,
  for <math|p=1,2>, and define

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|p,k><around|(|n|)>=w<rsup|-2*k><math-up|Re><space|0.17em>T<rsub|k>*<around|(|f<rsub|1><rsup|<around|(|n|)>>,\<ldots\>,h<rsub|p,w,k>,\<ldots\>,f<rsub|4><rsup|<around|(|n|)>>|)>,<space|2em>k=0,1,2.
  </equation*>

  The temporal slot is <math|i>. Since <math|p<rsub|i><rsup|2>*h<rsub|p,w,k+1>=<around|(|p*w|)><rsup|2>*h<rsub|p,w,k>>,
  <eqref|eq:stress-conservation-chain> implies that both

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|p,1>-p<rsup|2>*A<rsub|p,0>,<space|2em>A<rsub|p,2>-p<rsup|2>*A<rsub|p,1>
  </equation*>

  have exponent <math|2*a>, hence exponent <math|x=\<delta\>+2*a>. Here the
  normalized first defect costs <math|w<rsup|-2>*e<rsup|4*a*n>>, and the
  second costs <math|w<rsup|-4>*e<rsup|6*a*n>>, making the exponent explicit.

  Choose one subtraction order <math|m> greater than all the finitely many
  moment orders just used. Replace <math|h<rsub|1,w,k>-h<rsub|2,w,k>> by its
  moment-subtracted difference at scale <math|8>, and call its normalized
  real response <math|F<rsub|k>>. The correction coefficients are differences
  of the moments at widths <math|w> and <math|2*w>. Their constant moments
  cancel. Each remaining even moment has order at least two, so its
  coefficient has exponent at most <math|2*a>. There are finitely many fixed
  correction profiles, all away from zero. Their fixed-band costs are
  <math|\<delta\>+2*k*a> for <math|T<rsub|k>>, and the normalization
  <math|w<rsup|-2*k>> removes the latter <math|2*k*a>. It follows that

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|k>-<around|(|A<rsub|1,k>-A<rsub|2,k>|)>=O<around|(|e<rsup|x*n>|)>,<space|2em>k=0,1,2.
  </equation*>

  No subtraction order is sent to infinity.

  The three signs are essential. Take the actual reflection-even cluster
  representing <math|J<rsub|i*i>> and its positive spectral measure. For a
  sufficiently moment-zero temporal profile, the extension agrees with the
  ordinary spectral integral. The contact terms in
  <eqref|eq:stress-conservation-chain> vanish there; multiplication by
  <math|p<rsub|i><rsup|2>> inside the profile becomes multiplication by
  <math|-E<rsup|2>> in its Laplace response, by twice integrating by parts
  (the zero moments remove the boundary terms). Thus, writing
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|m,w,k><around|(|E|)>> for the nonnegative dyadic
  response from the previous section,

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|k>=\<tau\>*w<rsup|-2*k>*<big|int><around|(|-E<rsup|2>|)><rsup|k>*\<Gamma\><rsub|m,w,k><around|(|E|)>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<ne\>i><around|(|f<rsub|j>|)><rsub|e<rsup|w<rsub|j>*n>><around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|i><rsup|J<rsub|i*i>><around|(|p|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<tau\>\<gtr\>0> is the fixed Fourier normalization. Hence
  <math|F<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0>, <math|F<rsub|1>\<leqslant\>0> and
  <math|F<rsub|2>\<geqslant\>0>. The four conservation defects and these
  three correction defects are exactly the seven errors in the algebraic
  estimate of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-ward-packet-prices>. It
  gives <math|F<rsub|0>=O<around|(|e<rsup|<around|(|\<delta\>+2*a|)>*n>|)>>.
  The positive dyadic kernel lower bound and multiplication by
  <math|w<rsup|2>> now prove <eqref|eq:packet-cone-required>. In particular
  the choice <math|a=-99>, <math|\<delta\>=1> gives exponent <math|-395> for
  the actual diagonal-stress measure, as required by the exterior packet.

  <subsection|Analyticity along extreme-point
  motions><label|sec:physical-ward-analytic-continuation>

  To continue a Ward identity away from a line, we need analyticity along
  certain one-coordinate motions. Smoothness of the hierarchy does not
  suffice. We derive the required analyticity from reflection positivity,
  positive-time heat evolution, and the collinear angular identities. The
  argument uses only finite-dimensional exponentials of individual spin
  operators; an action of the full rotation group is not an input.

  Let <math|S> be the smooth, translation-invariant, reflection-positive
  hierarchy already constructed, with finite-dimensional filtered field
  spaces <math|E<rsub|d>>. Write <math|D<rsub|b>:E<rsub|d>\<to\>E<rsub|d+1>>
  for its derivative operators and <math|T<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>:E<rsub|d>\<to\>E<rsub|d>>
  for the angular operators, real-linear in a skew matrix <math|K> and
  complex-linear in the field. Assume the derivative insertion identities,
  the collinear angular Ward identities at every grade and arity, and the
  commutation rule

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ward-analytic-commute>T<rsub|d+1><around|(|K|)>*D<rsub|b>*v=D<rsub|b>*T<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>*v+<big|sum><rsub|c>K<rsub|c*b>*D<rsub|c>*v.
  </equation>

  Signed coordinate symmetries, including reflection of the field labels, are
  the ones already present in the hierarchy.

  Fix a coordinate axis <math|a>. Its reflection-positive Hilbert space is
  <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|a>>. For a field <math|v> and a point <math|x> with
  <math|x<rsub|a>\<gtr\>0>, let <math|\<Psi\><rsub|v><around|(|x|)>> be the
  vector represented by the singleton insertion there. Denote the reflected
  label by <math|v<rsup|\<dagger\><rsub|a>>> and the coordinate reflection by
  <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|a>>. If

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|v,w><around|(|x|)>=S*<around|(|v<text| at >x,w<text| at >0|)>,
  </equation*>

  the exact Gram identity is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ward-analytic-gram><around|\<langle\>|\<Psi\><rsub|v><around|(|x|)>,\<Psi\><rsub|w><around|(|y|)>|\<rangle\>>=F<rsub|w,v<rsup|\<dagger\><rsub|a>>>*<around|(|y-\<vartheta\><rsub|a>*x|)>.
  </equation>

  The inner product is linear in its second variable. Translation invariance
  and the permutation symmetry of the hierarchy prove this identity directly
  from the reflected-word definition of the Hilbert space.

  <subsubsection|Smooth singleton vectors and the positive radial line>

  For <math|b\<ne\>a>, the curve <math|t\<mapsto\>\<Psi\><rsub|v>*<around|(|x+t*e<rsub|b>|)>>
  is continuous by the strongly continuous spatial translations on
  <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|a>>. Its derivative is
  <math|\<Psi\><rsub|D<rsub|b>*v>*<around|(|x+t*e<rsub|b>|)>>. To justify
  this as a Hilbert-space derivative, first pair with any reflected-word
  vector. The scalar pairing is a smooth separated-point moment, whose
  derivative is the stated derivative insertion. The proposed derivative
  curve is itself continuous by spatial translation. Its Bochner integral
  from <math|0> to <math|t> therefore has, against every word vector, the
  same pairing as <math|\<Psi\><rsub|v>*<around|(|x+t*e<rsub|b>|)>-\<Psi\><rsub|v><around|(|x|)>>.
  Density of word vectors identifies these vectors. The fundamental theorem
  of calculus now gives the strong derivative. Iterating this argument with
  <math|D<rsub|b>*v,D<rsub|b><rsup|2>*v,\<ldots\>> proves smoothness of the
  entire curve.

  In the normal direction there is more: a singleton vector is analytic at
  every positive normal coordinate. Choose an earlier strictly positive
  normal coordinate. Nearby vectors are obtained from that earlier vector by
  the heat semigroup at a strictly positive time. The holomorphic
  positive-time extension of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:complex-heat>
  proves the claim. Pairing with a fixed singleton and using
  <eqref|eq:ward-analytic-gram> consequently shows that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ward-radial-analytic>t\<longmapsto\>F<rsub|v,w>*<around|(|t*e<rsub|a>|)><space|1em><text|is
    real analytic for >t\<gtr\>0.
  </equation>

  For example, near a specified <math|t<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0>, place the fixed
  reflected singleton at <math|t<rsub|0>*e<rsub|a>/2> and the variable
  singleton at <math|<around|(|t-t<rsub|0>/2|)>*e<rsub|a>>. Their separation
  is <math|t*e<rsub|a>> and both lie in the positive half-space.

  <subsubsection|An analytic comparison with the same transverse jets>

  Fix <math|b\<ne\>a> and the plane generator <math|K> with
  <math|K*e<rsub|a>=e<rsub|b>>, <math|K*e<rsub|b>=-e<rsub|a>>. For
  <math|u\<gtr\>0> set

  <\equation*>
    \<rho\>*<around|(|u,z|)>=<sqrt|u<rsup|2>+z<rsup|2>>,<space|2em>\<theta\>*<around|(|u,z|)>=arctan
    <around|(|z/u|)>,
  </equation*>

  <\equation*>
    Q<rsub|v,w>*<around|(|u,z|)>=F<rsub|e<rsup|-\<theta\>*<around|(|u,z|)>*T<around|(|K|)>>*v,<space|0.17em>e<rsup|-\<theta\>*<around|(|u,z|)>*T<around|(|K|)>>*w>*<around|(|\<rho\>*<around|(|u,z|)>*e<rsub|a>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The exponentials act in the respective finite-dimensional field spaces.
  Expand both labels in finite bases. Each summand is a product of analytic
  matrix coefficients and a fixed radial moment from
  <eqref|eq:ward-radial-analytic>, composed with the positive analytic
  function <math|\<rho\>>. Thus <math|Q<rsub|v,w>> is real analytic on
  <math|u\<gtr\>0>.

  Define <math|\<cal-R\>=u*\<partial\><rsub|z>-z*\<partial\><rsub|u>>. Direct
  differentiation gives <math|\<cal-R\>*\<theta\>=1> and
  <math|\<cal-R\>*\<rho\>=0>. Consequently

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ward-comparator-pde>Q<rsub|T<around|(|K|)>*v,w>+Q<rsub|v,T<around|(|K|)>*w>+\<cal-R\>*Q<rsub|v,w>=0.
  </equation>

  Also <math|Q<rsub|v,w>*<around|(|u,0|)>=F<rsub|v,w>*<around|(|u*e<rsub|a>|)>>.
  Let <math|G<rsub|v,w>*<around|(|u,z|)>=F<rsub|v,w>*<around|(|u*e<rsub|a>+z*e<rsub|b>|)>>
  be the actual smooth two-point function. Its corresponding expression
  <math|G<rsub|T<around|(|K|)>*v,w>+G<rsub|v,T<around|(|K|)>*w>+\<cal-R\>*G<rsub|v,w>>
  is the angular Ward expression for these two insertions.

  Every transverse derivative of that Ward expression vanishes at
  <math|<around|(|u,0|)>>. Here is the reason this follows from the collinear
  identity for all labels. Differentiate the angular expression in one
  insertion coordinate. The new derivative of its linear spatial coefficient
  contributes <math|<big|sum><rsub|c>K<rsub|c*b>> times the corresponding
  derivative insertion. Combining this term with the differentiated spin
  insertion, using <eqref|eq:ward-analytic-commute>, gives exactly the
  angular expression with that label replaced by <math|D<rsub|b>*v>. All
  other terms are just differentiated moment rows. Thus repeated coordinate
  differentiation only raises labels. The assumed collinear identity applies
  to every resulting word. In particular all
  <math|\<partial\><rsub|z><rsup|n>> of the two-point angular expression
  vanish at every <math|<around|(|u,0|)>> with <math|u\<gtr\>0>.

  We show that <math|G> and <math|Q> have the same transverse jets, without
  assuming that <math|G> is analytic. Put <math|H=G-Q>, allowing every pair
  of labels in this notation. Then <math|H<rsub|v,w>*<around|(|u,0|)>=0> and
  all transverse jets of its angular expression vanish. The elementary
  commutation formula is

  <\equation*>
    \<partial\><rsub|z><rsup|n>*<around|(|\<cal-R\>*H|)>=\<cal-R\>*\<partial\><rsub|z><rsup|n>*H-n*\<partial\><rsub|u>*\<partial\><rsub|z><rsup|n-1>*H*<space|2em><around|(|n\<geqslant\>1|)>.
  </equation*>

  Assume inductively that all transverse jets through order <math|n> of every
  <math|H<rsub|v,w>> vanish along the positive <math|u>-axis. Apply
  <math|\<partial\><rsub|z><rsup|n>> to its angular expression and set
  <math|z=0>. Both spin terms vanish by the induction hypothesis. The last
  term in the displayed formula also vanishes, because the preceding
  transverse jet is identically zero as a function of <math|u>. We obtain
  <math|u*\<partial\><rsub|z><rsup|n+1>*H<rsub|v,w>*<around|(|u,0|)>=0>. For
  <math|n=0> the same conclusion follows directly without the last term.
  Since <math|u\<gtr\>0>, induction proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ward-comparison-jets>\<partial\><rsub|z><rsup|n>*G<rsub|v,w>*<around|(|u,0|)>=\<partial\><rsub|z><rsup|n>*Q<rsub|v,w>*<around|(|u,0|)>*<space|2em><around|(|n\<geqslant\>0|)>.
  </equation>

  This equality of jets alone is not an analytic continuation statement. The
  next Hilbert-space estimate supplies the required convergence.

  <subsubsection|From scalar jets to a Hilbert analytic curve>

  We use a useful elementary fact. Suppose <math|f:\<bbb-R\>\<to\>\<cal-H\>>
  is smooth and

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<langle\>|f<around|(|s|)>,f<around|(|t|)>|\<rangle\>>=A*<around|(|t-s|)>.
  </equation*>

  Suppose the derivatives of <math|A> at zero agree with those of a scalar
  function analytic at zero. The power series of that function gives
  constants <math|C,R\<gtr\>0> with <math|<around|\||A<rsup|<around|(|n|)>><around|(|0|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*R<rsup|-n>*n>!
  for every <math|n>. Differentiating the Gram identity in both variables
  yields

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<langle\>|f<rsup|<around|(|m|)>><around|(|s|)>,f<rsup|<around|(|n|)>><around|(|t|)>|\<rangle\>>=<around|(|-1|)><rsup|m>*A<rsup|<around|(|m+n|)>>*<around|(|t-s|)>.
  </equation*>

  In particular, uniformly for all real <math|t>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|f<rsup|<around|(|n|)>><around|(|t|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<around|\||A<rsup|<around|(|2*n|)>><around|(|0|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*R<rsup|-2*n>*<around|(|2*n|)>!\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|2/R|)><rsup|2*n><around|(|n!|)><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  The last inequality uses <math|<binom|2*n|n>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|2*n>>.
  Taylor's integral remainder in the Hilbert space is therefore at most
  <math|<sqrt|C>*<around|(|2<around|\||h|\|>/R|)><rsup|N+1>> after order
  <math|N>. It tends to zero for <math|<around|\||h|\|>\<less\>R/2>. The
  Taylor series converges in norm and equals <math|f> there, proving real
  analyticity at every point of the line.

  Apply this fact to <math|f<around|(|t|)>=\<Psi\><rsub|v>*<around|(|x+t*e<rsub|b>|)>>
  for <math|x<rsub|a>\<gtr\>0> and <math|b\<ne\>a>. By
  <eqref|eq:ward-analytic-gram>,

  <\equation*>
    A*<around|(|t-s|)>=F<rsub|v,v<rsup|\<dagger\><rsub|a>>>*<around|(|2*x<rsub|a>*e<rsub|a>+<around|(|t-s|)>*e<rsub|b>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The smoothness was proved above. The analytic comparator is
  <math|z\<mapsto\>Q<rsub|v,v<rsup|\<dagger\><rsub|a>>>*<around|(|2*x<rsub|a>,z|)>>,
  and <eqref|eq:ward-comparison-jets> gives precisely the required
  derivatives at zero. Hence the singleton trajectory is analytic along every
  transverse coordinate, as well as along the positive normal coordinate.
  Stationarity of the Gram kernel is what makes the factorial bound uniform
  along the whole transverse line.

  <subsubsection|Moving an extreme insertion in an arbitrary word>

  Consider a configuration of distinct points and one insertion whose
  <math|a>-coordinate is strictly larger than those of all other insertions.
  Choose a separating hyperplane <math|x<rsub|a>=c> strictly between them.
  Translate it to zero and reflect the fixed spectator word into the positive
  half-space. Reflection positivity then represents the moment, locally in
  any coordinate motion of the extreme insertion, as

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\>,\<Psi\><rsub|v>*<around|(|x+t*e<rsub|b>|)>|\<rangle\>>
  </equation*>

  with a fixed word vector <math|\<Phi\>>. Strict separation persists in a
  neighborhood of the chosen parameter. If <math|b=a>, positive-time
  analyticity applies; if <math|b\<ne\>a>, use the transverse result just
  proved. Applying the continuous linear functional
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\>,\<cdot\>|\<rangle\>>> proves analyticity
  of the moment row. Permutation symmetry moves any selected slot to the
  first slot, so no restriction on its position in the word is needed.
  Spectators may have equal <math|a>-coordinates, provided their points
  remain distinct.

  For an insertion strictly smaller in coordinate <math|a>, use the
  hierarchy's actual signed reflection in that axis, with its reflected
  labels. It turns the insertion into a strict maximum. A motion in
  coordinate <math|a> changes parameter <math|t> to <math|-t>; a transverse
  motion leaves <math|t> unchanged. Both substitutions preserve analyticity.
  This reflection uses the already constructed signed symmetry, not a
  rotation action yet to be proved.

  Every angular Ward expression is a finite sum of such moment rows, with
  labels either turned or raised once and coefficients affine in the moving
  coordinate. It is therefore analytic wherever the moving point is a strict
  extremum in some fixed coordinate. Along a closed segment with this
  property at every point, these neighborhoods cover the segment. This is the
  precise segment analyticity needed for the one-dimensional identity theorem
  in the global Ward propagation argument.

  <subsection|From the collinear stress identity to global Ward
  identities><label|sec:physical-ward-propagation>

  This section records the finite-configuration argument which turns a
  collinear rotation identity into a Ward identity at every separated
  configuration. It also records the two finite-level bridges needed later:
  the outer stress estimate supplies the collinear identity, and erasure of
  the internal Ward kernel turns the differentiated Ward identity into the
  Lie relations. All statements are at fixed finite insertion number and
  fixed field grades; no coincidence limit is taken.

  Let <math|<with|math-font|cal*|S>> be a Euclidean hierarchy. Write
  <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|d>> for its complex field space at grade <math|d>,
  <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|d>:\<cal-F\><rsub|d>\<to\>\<cal-F\><rsub|d+1>> for the
  positional derivative, and <math|\<cal-Y\><rsub|d><around|(|K|)>:\<cal-F\><rsub|d>\<to\>\<cal-F\><rsub|d>>
  for a spin-row family indexed real-linearly by a skew matrix
  <math|K\<in\><with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)>>. A label at a slot
  is a pair <math|<around|(|d,v|)>> with <math|v\<in\>\<cal-F\><rsub|d>>. For
  a word <math|q=<around|(|q<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,q<rsub|n>|)>> and a separated
  tuple <math|x=<around|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>>, let
  <math|S*<around|(|q;x|)>> denote its Euclidean moment. The hierarchy's
  derivative rule says that differentiation in the coordinate <math|b> of
  <math|x<rsub|i>> replaces <math|q<rsub|i>=<around|(|d<rsub|i>,v<rsub|i>|)>>
  by <math|<around|(|d<rsub|i>+1,\<cal-P\><rsub|d<rsub|i>,b>*v<rsub|i>|)>>.

  Define the angular Ward expression by

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-A\><rsub|K>*<around|(|q;x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(||\<nobracket\>>>|<cell|S*<around|(|q*<around|[|i\<mapsto\><around|(|d<rsub|i>,\<cal-Y\><rsub|d<rsub|i>><around|(|K|)>*v<rsub|i>|)>|]>;x|)><eq-number>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around*|\<nobracket\>|+<big|sum><rsub|b=1><rsup|4><around|(|K*x<rsub|i>|)><rsub|b>*<space|0.17em>S*<around|(|q*<around|[|i\<mapsto\><around|(|d<rsub|i>+1,\<cal-P\><rsub|d<rsub|i>,b>*v<rsub|i>|)>|]>;x|)>|)>.<eq-number><label|eq:ward-angular-expression>>>>>
  </align>

  Here <math|q*<around|[|i\<mapsto\>\<cdot\>|]>> changes only the indicated
  slot. The condition <em|Still at <math|x>> means
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|K>*<around|(|q;x|)>=0> for every word <math|q> and
  every <math|K>. The condition <em|collinear> means this identity holds
  whenever the points lie on one coordinate axis <math|\<bbb-R\>*e<rsub|a>>
  through the origin and remain pairwise distinct. This axis formulation is
  sufficient below. In fact the angular expression is invariant under
  simultaneous translation. For a common shift <math|h>, translation
  invariance of every moment, including those with a changed label, and the
  positional derivative rule give

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-A\><rsub|K>*<around|(|q;x+h|)>-\<cal-A\><rsub|K>*<around|(|q;x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|b=1><rsup|4><around|(|K*h|)><rsub|b>*<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|i,b>>*S*<around|(|q;x|)>=0.
  </equation*>

  The last equality follows by differentiating
  <math|S*<around|(|q;x+s*e<rsub|b>|)>=S*<around|(|q;x|)>> at <math|s=0>,
  with the same shift applied to every point. Hence the axis formulation also
  gives the identity on every line parallel to that axis. The compatibility
  relation between position and spin derivatives is

  <\equation>
    \<cal-P\><rsub|d,b>*\<cal-Y\><rsub|d><around|(|K|)>*v+<big|sum><rsub|c=1><rsup|4>K<rsub|c*b>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-P\><rsub|d,c>*v=\<cal-Y\><rsub|d+1><around|(|K|)>*\<cal-P\><rsub|d,b>*v.<label|eq:ward-commutation>
  </equation>

  The signs in this identity use the convention in
  <eqref|eq:ward-angular-expression>.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Propagation from a line><label|thm:ward-global-propagation> Assume
    the hierarchy is smooth on the configuration space

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|Conf><rsub|n>=<around|{|<around|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>:x<rsub|i>\<ne\>x<rsub|j><text|
      for >i\<ne\>j|}>,
    </equation*>

    the spin rows obey <eqref|eq:ward-commutation>, and the angular
    expression <eqref|eq:ward-angular-expression> is zero at every separated
    collinear configuration. Assume also the extreme-segment analyticity
    established in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-ward-analytic-continuation>:
    on every coordinate segment used below, the angular expression for each
    fixed word and generator is real analytic on a neighborhood of the
    segment. Then it is zero at every <math|x\<in\><math-up|Conf><rsub|n>>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    We first establish a geometric reduction. Fix a coordinate axis <math|a>
    and another axis <math|b\<ne\>a>. Call a tuple <math|a>-ordered if the
    real numbers <math|x<rsub|i>\<cdot\>e<rsub|a>> are pairwise distinct.
    Such tuples are dense in <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>>: perturb the
    <math|a>-coordinate of point <math|i> by <math|t*i>. For each pair
    <math|i\<ne\>j>, equality after perturbation can occur for at most one
    value of <math|t>. Since there are finitely many pairs, there are
    arbitrarily small <math|t> avoiding all these exceptional values. The
    perturbed tuple converges to the original one and remains in the open set
    <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>> for small enough <math|t>.

    Every <math|a>-ordered tuple can be moved to a collinear tuple by a
    finite chain of single-point coordinate segments which stay in
    <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>>. Here are the construction and the
    separation check. Let <math|I> be the set of points not yet on the
    <math|a>-axis, maintaining the invariant that points already aligned lie
    beyond every remaining point in the <math|a>-coordinate. Initially
    <math|I> is the whole set, so the invariant is vacuous. Choose
    <math|i\<in\>I> with smallest <math|a>-coordinate. First move point
    <math|i> in coordinate <math|b> far enough in the positive direction that
    it is the unique largest <math|b>-coordinate. During this first segment
    it stays the unique smallest <math|a>-coordinate, since its
    <math|a>-coordinate is fixed. Then move it in coordinate <math|a> far
    enough to be the unique largest <math|a>-coordinate. During this second
    segment it stays the unique largest <math|b>-coordinate, so its
    <math|b>-coordinate does not meet another point's. Now, one at a time,
    set each of its other coordinates to zero. These moves leave its
    <math|a>-coordinate fixed and it remains the unique largest in <math|a>.
    Every intermediate configuration is therefore separated. The point has
    now reached the <math|a>-axis without moving any other point, and it lies
    beyond every point still in <math|I>. Repeat with the remaining finite
    set <math|I>. This terminates after at most <math|n> stages and gives the
    required finite chain. The motion consists of finitely many segments; the
    segment parameter interval can be oriented in either direction.

    It remains to show the Ward identity is constant along each such segment.
    Fix a segment moving point <math|i> in coordinate <math|b>, with
    parameter <math|s> in a closed interval <math|<around|[|u,v|]>>. By
    construction, throughout this interval point <math|i> is either the
    strict minimum or the strict maximum in some coordinate <math|a>; the
    choice may switch between segments, but not within this one. For each
    fixed word <math|q> and skew matrix <math|K>, the scalar function

    <\equation*>
      s\<longmapsto\>\<cal-A\><rsub|K>*<around|(|q;x<around|(|s|)>|)>
    </equation*>

    is real analytic on a neighborhood of the interval by the stated
    extreme-segment analyticity input. This input applies to moment rows with
    the moving point at either end and to every raised row appearing in the
    angular expression. It is precisely the result of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-ward-analytic-continuation>;
    smoothness on <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>> alone is not enough.

    At an endpoint where Still holds, all derivatives of this analytic scalar
    function vanish. For order zero this is the assumed Ward identity. For
    higher orders, differentiate the identity along the segment and use
    <eqref|eq:ward-commutation>: differentiating the spin substitution at the
    moving slot gives the spin substitution of the raised positional
    derivative; the derivative of the orbital coefficient supplies the matrix
    term <math|<big|sum><rsub|c>K<rsub|c*b>*\<cal-P\><rsub|d,c>>; these terms
    combine exactly into the raised-slot term in the angular identity.
    Induction therefore identifies the <math|r>th derivative with the Ward
    expression for the word with that slot raised <math|r> times. It is zero
    at the endpoint. A real-analytic function on a connected interval whose
    derivatives of every order vanish at one point vanishes on the interval:
    its convergent Taylor series is zero near that point, and the identity
    theorem propagates the equality across the interval. Thus Still transfers
    from the final endpoint of a segment to its initial endpoint. Applying
    this successively along the finite chain proves Still at every
    <math|a>-ordered tuple. Finally, each angular expression is continuous on
    <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>> by smoothness of the hierarchy there.
    Density of the ordered tuples proves it vanishes at the original
    arbitrary separated tuple. This also covers <math|n=0,1>, where there are
    no pairwise collision constraints and the sums are empty or single-term.
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|The collinear identity from the stress tensor.> The preceding
  theorem needs no stress tensor once its collinear hypothesis is available.
  In the application, let <math|J<rsub|a*b>> be a symmetric conserved stress
  insertion, let <math|p<rsub|d,b>> be the local positional response, and let
  <math|Y<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>> be the spin response. The stress-law package
  consists of the conservation law, the symmetric raw stress identity, and
  the flux identity that identifies the infinitesimal rotation of a moment
  with the integral of the stress flux; its precise form is (S1)\U(S3) before
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ward-local-response-laws>. The flux argument uses
  the compactly supported test described below; it does not assume that a
  noncompact rotation field is admissible.

  Until the scalar comparison <math|p=c*\<cal-P\>> is proved, write
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|K>*<around|(|p,Y;q;x|)>> for the expression
  <eqref|eq:ward-angular-expression> with <math|p,Y> substituted for
  <math|\<cal-P\>,\<cal-Y\>>. The compact stress argument below proves its
  vanishing. It does not yet identify <math|p> with a positional derivative.

  The following paragraph is motivation only; the test actually used is the
  growing anisotropic petal described after the price estimates. For fixed
  separated collinear <math|x>, one might choose smooth compactly supported
  windows <math|\<chi\><rsub|i>> around each point, equal to one near that
  point and with supports avoiding all other insertions. For a plane rotation
  in axes <math|a,b>, the packet

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|c><around|(|y|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i>\<chi\><rsub|i><around|(|y|)>*<around|(|K*y|)><rsub|c>
  </equation*>

  is smooth, compactly supported, and agrees with the rotational vector field
  near each insertion. The flux identity therefore evaluates the angular
  expression as minus the integral of its stress-flux density. In that
  density the term <math|\<chi\><rsub|i>*<space|0.17em>\<partial\><rsub|a>*<around|(|K*y|)><rsub|b>>
  cancels after contraction against the symmetric stress, because <math|K> is
  skew. The terms <math|<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\><rsub|i>|)>*<around|(|K*y|)><rsub|b>>
  do not cancel: they are the shell integrand. The estimate proved below is
  not for such fixed windows. It is for a petal centred at the origin, of
  widths <math|e<rsup|100*n>,e<rsup|150*n>,e<rsup|300*n>>, whose shell lies
  far from all insertions.

  Here is the rate calculation behind the stress-packet estimate. For a
  sequence of scalar or finite-dimensional vector quantities
  <math|F<rsub|n>>, write <math|F=O<rsub|r>> when
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|n>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*e<rsup|r*n>> for
  all sufficiently large <math|n>, with <math|C> independent of <math|n>. The
  exact choice of the fixed axes, word, and positions may affect <math|C>. A
  positive spectral pairing satisfies the following elementary interpolation
  rule: if <math|X<rsub|n>,Y<rsub|n>\<geqslant\>0> and

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||F<rsub|n><around|(|p|)>|\|><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|(|M*e<rsup|t*n>|)><rsup|2>*X<rsub|n><around|(|p|)>*Y<rsub|n><around|(|p|)>,<space|2em><big|int>X<rsub|n>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|B>=O<rsub|x>,<space|2em><big|int>Y<rsub|n>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|A>=O<rsub|y>,
  </equation*>

  then Cauchy\USchwarz gives

  <\equation>
    <around|\||<around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|n>|\<rangle\>><rsub|B,A>|\|>\<leqslant\>M*<around|(|C<rsub|X>*C<rsub|Y>|)><rsup|1/2>*e<rsup|<around|(|t+<around|(|x+y|)>/2|)>*n>,<space|2em><text|so
    ><space|1em><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|n>|\<rangle\>><rsub|B,A>=O<rsub|t+<around|(|x+y|)>/2>.<label|eq:ward-balanced-price>
  </equation>

  Here <math|C<rsub|X>,C<rsub|Y>> are the constants in the respective
  integral bounds. This rule applies to the cross-spectral pairing after its
  positive supports and integrability have been verified.

  For the packet under consideration the anisotropic scale exponents are

  <\equation*>
    w<rsub|j>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|-100,>|<cell|j=a<text| or
    >j=b,>>|<row|<cell|-150,>|<cell|j=l<text| and
    >j\<nin\><around|{|a,b|}>,>>|<row|<cell|-300,>|<cell|<text|otherwise>,>>>>><space|2em>u=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|1,>|<cell|d=l,>>|<row|<cell|-w<rsub|l>,>|<cell|d\<ne\>l.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  They obey <math|-300\<leqslant\>w<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>-100>,
  <math|w<rsub|d>\<less\>0>, and <math|u\<leqslant\>150>. The nonnegative
  spectral measures are supported on <math|p<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>0>. The cove
  is the set where <math|p<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>1> and every transverse
  coordinate satisfies <math|<around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<around|(|1+w<rsub|j>|)>*n>>.
  Let <math|h> be the fixed nonnegative, smooth, even one-dimensional cutoff
  which equals one on <math|<around|[|-1,1|]>> and is supported in
  <math|<around|[|-2,2|]>>. The transverse panel uses the normalized dilation

  <\equation*>
    h<rsub|j,n><around|(|s|)>=e<rsup|-<around|(|1+w<rsub|j>|)>*n>*h*<around*|(|s*e<rsup|-<around|(|1+w<rsub|j>|)>*n>|)>,<space|2em>H<rsub|n><around|(|p|)>=<big|prod><rsub|j\<ne\>d>h<rsub|j,n><around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|H<rsub|n>> includes the normalization factors of the three
  transverse one-dimensional dilations; they are part of both price weights
  below. The packet estimate is for pairwise distinct axes <math|a,b> and for
  <math|d\<ne\>a>, as in the stress-packet construction. Denote by
  <math|D<rsub|r,n>> the positive longitudinal cost

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|r,n><around|(|p|)>=\<b-1\><rsub|p<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>1>*<space|0.17em><around|(|p<rsub|d>+e<rsup|r*n>|)><rsup|-1>*H<rsub|n><around|(|p|)>,
  </equation*>

  Denote by <math|Z<rsub|r,n>> its cone-weighted variant

  <\equation*>
    Z<rsub|r,n><around|(|p|)>=\<b-1\><rsub|p<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>1>*<frac|p<rsub|d>*e<rsup|4*r*n>|<around|(|p<rsub|d>+e<rsup|r*n>|)><rsup|4>>*H<rsub|n><around|(|p|)>.
  </equation*>

  The following are the precise price bounds needed from the collinear
  spectral estimates. The positive line and point prices are supplied by
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-ward-packet-prices>; the
  cone-weighted bound is proved by the fixed-band stress-conservation
  argument in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-ward-stress-cone>. In
  these notations the required bounds are

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int>D<rsub|-3000,n>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|B>>|<cell|=O<rsub|u>,>|<cell|<big|int>Z<rsub|-99,n>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|A>>|<cell|=O<rsub|-395>,<eq-number><label|eq:ward-deep-prices>>>|<row|<cell|<big|int>D<rsub|-3000,n>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|B>>|<cell|=O<rsub|u>,>|<cell|<big|int>D<rsub|0,n>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|A>>|<cell|=O<rsub|900>,<eq-number><label|eq:ward-shallow-prices>>>|<row|<cell|<big|int>D<rsub|-3000,n>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|B>>|<cell|=O<rsub|u>,>|<cell|<big|int>D<rsub|w<rsub|d>,n>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|A>>|<cell|=O<rsub|1>.<eq-number><label|eq:ward-side-prices>>>>>
  </align>

  Each bound is uniform in <math|n> for a fixed packet. The exterior Fourier
  estimate supplies <math|O<rsub|-101>> on the complement of the low cove by
  the damped exterior estimate, not by assuming an undamped spectral moment.
  The distinction between the two price measures in each display is
  essential: the first controls the collinear line cost, while the second
  controls the packet amplitude.

  The two price inputs carry sign hypotheses, which we now check. The line
  price <eqref|eq:packet-line-price> for the collinear cluster <math|B>
  requires both reflection signs among the three axes other than <math|l>,
  and an axis among them whose sign is opposite to that of <math|l>. The
  point price <eqref|eq:packet-point-price> for <math|A>, with <math|Q> all
  four axes, requires both signs among all four axes. In the stress
  decomposition below, <math|B> is the matching sign pattern of
  <math|J<rsub|a*b>> with <math|a\<ne\>b>: it is odd exactly under the
  reflections of the axes <math|a> and <math|b>. The cluster
  <math|A=J<rsub|d*b>> with <math|d\<ne\>b> is odd exactly under the
  reflections of <math|d> and <math|b>. For <math|B>: since <math|a\<ne\>b>,
  at least one of <math|a,b> differs from <math|l>, which gives an odd axis
  other than <math|l>; and <math|<around|{|a,b,l|}>> has at most three
  elements, which gives an even axis other than <math|l>. If
  <math|l\<in\><around|{|a,b|}>> then <math|l> is odd and the even axis just
  found has the opposite sign; otherwise <math|l> is even and the odd axis
  has the opposite sign. For <math|A>: the axes <math|d,b> are odd and the
  two remaining axes are even. Hence both price hypotheses hold.

  The one-dimensional panel profile <math|\<varphi\><rsub|d>> in the test has
  Fourier gap <math|<wide|\<varphi\>|^><rsub|d><around|(|\<xi\>|)>\<ne\>0\<Rightarrow\><around|\||\<xi\>|\|>\<geqslant\>1>.
  This is a property of the panel profile, not a claim that the compactly
  supported spatial petal has a Fourier gap. On the cove, the frozen Fourier
  estimate for that panel gives the differentiated-packet bound

  <\equation>
    <around|\||F<rsub|n><around|(|p|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M*e<rsup|3*n>*H<rsub|n><around|(|p|)>*exp
    <around|(|-p<rsub|d>*e<rsup|-w<rsub|d>*n>|)>,<space|2em>p<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>0.<label|eq:ward-packet-tail>
  </equation>

  To derive it, use the exterior computation of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-ward-packet-prices>. For
  <math|p<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>0> the temporal factor is at most

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|<wide|\<varphi\>|^><rsub|d>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>*exp
    <around|(|-p<rsub|d>/e<rsup|w<rsub|d>*n>|)>,
  </equation*>

  and each transverse factor has the form
  <math|e<rsup|-w<rsub|j>*n>*f<rsub|j>*<around|(|p<rsub|j>/e<rsup|w<rsub|j>*n>|)>>
  with a fixed bounded profile <math|f<rsub|j>>, as in that section, so its
  modulus is at most <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*e<rsup|-w<rsub|j>*n>>.
  On the cove <math|<around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|>*e<rsup|-<around|(|1+w<rsub|j>|)>*n>\<leqslant\>1>,
  so <math|h<rsub|j,n><around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>=e<rsup|-<around|(|1+w<rsub|j>|)>*n>>
  and

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*e<rsup|-w<rsub|j>*n>=<around|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*<space|0.17em>e<rsup|n>*h<rsub|j,n><around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The product of the three transverse factors gives
  <math|e<rsup|3*n>*H<rsub|n><around|(|p|)>>.

  On the cove with <math|d=b>, split at the longitudinal level
  <math|p<rsub|d>=e<rsup|-3000*n>>. On the region
  <math|p<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>e<rsup|-3000*n>>, the elementary inequality

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|-t/s>\<leqslant\>4<around*|(|<frac|s|t+s>|)><rsup|2><space|1em><around|(|t\<geqslant\>0,0\<less\>s\<leqslant\>v|)>
  </equation*>

  with <math|s=e<rsup|w<rsub|d>*n>> and <math|v=e<rsup|-99*n>> first gives
  the square-tail factor <math|16*<around|(|v/<around|(|p<rsub|d>+v|)>|)><rsup|4>>.
  On this region, put <math|R=e<rsup|-3000*n>>. Since
  <math|R\<leqslant\>p<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>1>, we have
  <math|1\<leqslant\>4*p<rsub|d>/<around|(|p<rsub|d>+R|)>>, and therefore

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||F<rsub|n>|\|><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|(|M<rprime|'>*e<rsup|3*n>|)><rsup|2>*D<rsub|-3000,n>*Z<rsub|-99,n>.
  </equation*>

  The factor <math|4*p<rsub|d>/<around|(|p<rsub|d>+R|)>> pays for replacing
  the packet's denominator at scale <math|e<rsup|-99*n>> by the longitudinal
  cost at scale <math|e<rsup|-3000*n>>. The costs in
  <eqref|eq:ward-deep-prices> and <eqref|eq:ward-balanced-price> give
  exponent

  <\equation*>
    3+<frac|u-395|2>\<leqslant\>3+<frac|150-395|2>=-119.5.
  </equation*>

  On the other cove region, <math|0\<leqslant\>p<rsub|d>\<less\>e<rsup|-3000*n>>,
  the exponential in <eqref|eq:ward-packet-tail> is at most one. The packet
  square is therefore bounded with amplitude exponent <math|3-1500=-1497>
  against <math|D<rsub|-3000,n>*D<rsub|0,n>>. The shallow prices yield

  <\equation*>
    -1497+<frac|u+900|2>\<leqslant\>-972.
  </equation*>

  Both estimates are stronger than <math|O<rsub|-101>>.

  If <math|d\<ne\>b>, the shear estimate bounds the squared packet by an
  amplitude with exponent <math|3+w<rsub|d>>, against
  <math|D<rsub|-3000,n>*D<rsub|w<rsub|d>,n>>. It follows from
  <eqref|eq:ward-packet-tail>: with <math|\<tau\>=p<rsub|d>*e<rsup|-w<rsub|d>*n>>
  one has <math|<around|(|1+\<tau\>|)><rsup|2>*e<rsup|-2*\<tau\>>\<leqslant\>1>,
  so <math|e<rsup|-2*\<tau\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+\<tau\>|)><rsup|-2>=e<rsup|2*w<rsub|d>*n>*<around|(|p<rsub|d>+e<rsup|w<rsub|d>*n>|)><rsup|-2>>;
  and since <math|e<rsup|-3000*n>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|w<rsub|d>*n>>,
  <math|<around|(|p<rsub|d>+e<rsup|-3000*n>|)>*<around|(|p<rsub|d>+e<rsup|w<rsub|d>*n>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|p<rsub|d>+e<rsup|w<rsub|d>*n>|)><rsup|2>>.
  Together,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||F<rsub|n>|\|><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>M<rsup|2>*e<rsup|2*<around|(|3+w<rsub|d>|)>*n>*<space|0.17em><frac|H<rsub|n><rsup|2>|<around|(|p<rsub|d>+e<rsup|w<rsub|d>*n>|)><rsup|2>>\<leqslant\>M<rsup|2>*e<rsup|2*<around|(|3+w<rsub|d>|)>*n>*<space|0.17em>D<rsub|-3000,n>*D<rsub|w<rsub|d>,n>
  </equation*>

  on the cove. When <math|d=l>, <math|w<rsub|d>=-150> and <math|u=1>, so
  <eqref|eq:ward-balanced-price> gives <math|-150+3+<around|(|1+1|)>/2=-146>.
  When <math|d\<ne\>l>, <math|w<rsub|d>=-300> and <math|u\<leqslant\>150>, so
  the exponent is at most

  <\equation*>
    3-300+<frac|150+1|2>=-221.5.
  </equation*>

  The cove is partitioned by these two cases, and the complement has the
  independent damped Fourier-tail price <math|O<rsub|-101>>. For clarity,
  outside the cove there are exactly two alternatives. If
  <math|p<rsub|d>\<gtr\>1>, the gap in the panel Fourier support and the
  positive-time damping estimate give

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|-p<rsub|d>*e<rsup|-w<rsub|d>*n>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|y*n>*e<rsup|-R*p<rsub|d>>
  </equation*>

  eventually, for any fixed <math|R\<gtr\>0> and prescribed <math|y>. Take
  <math|y=-1001>; the transverse readout contributes at most
  <math|e<rsup|900*n>>, leaving rate <math|-101>. The positive-time damped
  spectral moment is supplied by the exterior-price estimate; no undamped
  zeroth moment is assumed. If instead <math|p<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>1> and a
  transverse scatter bound fails, then for some <math|j\<ne\>d>,
  <math|<around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|>\<gtr\>e<rsup|<around|(|1+w<rsub|j>|)>*n>>.
  The Schwartz bound of order <math|k> in that coordinate contributes the
  factor <math|e<rsup|-k*n>>, while the order-zero bounds for the two other
  transverse coordinates and the ordinary bound in the failed coordinate
  contribute at most <math|e<rsup|900*n>>. Choose <math|k\<gtr\>1001>; the
  corresponding positive-time polynomially weighted estimate is again the
  exterior-price input. These two cases give the unamplified matching cluster
  readout the required price <math|O<rsub|-101>> under that input.

  The cross-slice step needs absolute integrability on both sides. The
  weighted line/exterior prices and the positive-time damped spectral moment
  first give the spectral-side integrability. On the position side, place the
  two fixed clusters at a common positive-time separation <math|u>. The
  panel's temporal Fourier gap is at least <math|e<rsup|-w<rsub|d>*n>>, which
  is eventually greater than <math|2*u> with a strict margin. The damped
  moment estimate, together with the Schwartz bounds in the transverse
  coordinates, then gives absolute integrability and justifies Fubini. This
  is the gap estimate in the cross-slice input; weighted spectral Cauchy by
  itself would not prove position-side integrability. With these
  integrability hypotheses in place, the cross-slice identity gives, for a
  fixed sign <math|\<epsilon\>\<in\><around|{|-1,1|}>> and fixed
  <math|\<tau\>\<gtr\>0>,

  <\equation*>
    \<epsilon\>*<big|int>R<rsub|n><around|(|p|)>*<space|0.17em>d*p=\<tau\><space|0.17em><math-up|cross><around|(|F<rsub|n>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|R<rsub|n>> is the unamplified cluster readout times the
  position test and <math|F<rsub|n>> is its slice packet. Thus the cove and
  exterior estimates imply <math|<big|int>R<rsub|n>=O<rsub|-101>>. The actual
  insertion packet has an additional exact scalar factor
  <math|e<rsup|100*n>>: the profile identity is
  <math|<math-up|packet><rsub|n>=e<rsup|100*n><space|0.17em><math-up|readout><rsub|n>>.
  Consequently its integral has price

  <\equation>
    <around|\||B<rsub|a,b,l,d><around|(|n|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*e<rsup|100*n>*e<rsup|-101*n>=C*e<rsup|-n>.<label|eq:ward-shell-budget>
  </equation>

  To pass from this packet price to the stress insertion, first represent the
  chosen stress component by its finite signed cluster expansion. Decompose
  the other insertion labels into their finitely many sign patterns. A
  coordinate reflection changes the sign of each nonmatching pattern while
  preserving measure, so its integral is zero. For the matching pattern, the
  exact derivative-profile identity is <math|\<partial\><rsub|d><math-up|petal><rsub|n>=e<rsup|100*n><math-up|positionTest><rsub|n>>;
  thus its unamplified readout has price <math|O<rsub|-101>> and the actual
  stress packet has price <math|O<rsub|100-101>=O<rsub|-1>>. Finally, the
  finite sum over patterns, axes, and insertions preserves <math|O<rsub|-1>>,
  and the shell flux tends to zero.

  For the plane with generator normalized by <math|K*e<rsub|a>=e<rsub|b>> and
  <math|K*e<rsub|b>=-e<rsub|a>>, use the compact vector test with components

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|n|)>><around|(|y|)>=<math-up|petal><around|(|a,b,l,n|)><around|(|y|)>,<space|2em>F<rsub|a><rsup|<around|(|n|)>><around|(|y|)>=-<math-up|petal><around|(|b,a,l,n|)><around|(|y|)>,<space|2em>F<rsub|c><rsup|<around|(|n|)>><around|(|y|)>=0*<space|1em><around|(|c\<nin\><around|{|a,b|}>|)>.
  </equation*>

  These tests do more than converge to the orbital field near the insertions:
  for every fixed point <math|y<rsub|0>> there is <math|n<rsub|0>> such that
  for <math|n\<geqslant\>n<rsub|0>> one has
  <math|<math-up|petal><around|(|a,b,l,n|)><around|(|y|)>=y<rsub|a>> for all
  <math|y> in a neighborhood of <math|y<rsub|0>>. Indeed
  <math|<math-up|petal><around|(|a,b,l,n|)><around|(|y|)>=y<rsub|a>> whenever
  every normalized coordinate <math|e<rsup|w<rsub|i>*n>*y<rsub|i>> has
  modulus less than one, and these normalized coordinates tend to zero
  locally uniformly because every <math|w<rsub|i>\<less\>0>. Hence, for all
  large <math|n>, <math|F<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|n|)>><around|(|y|)>=y<rsub|a>>
  and <math|F<rsub|a><rsup|<around|(|n|)>><around|(|y|)>=-y<rsub|b>> on a
  neighborhood of each of the finitely many insertions: they coincide there
  with the two components of the orbital field, which is what the compact
  flux identity requires. The second component carries the minus sign; it is
  not an additional sign in the stress contraction. By stress symmetry, its
  shell integral and the first component combine as

  <\equation*>
    <big|int><math-up|river><around|(|F<rsup|<around|(|n|)>>|)>=<big|int><math-up|draught><rsub|a,b><rsup|<around|(|n|)>>-<big|int><math-up|draught><rsub|b,a><rsup|<around|(|n|)>>.
  </equation*>

  Each draught is a finite sum of the already estimated shell terms, hence
  has bound <math|C*e<rsup|-n>>. The compact-flux identity identifies the
  negative of this integral with the local plane-rotation response. That
  response is independent of <math|n>, so its absolute value is at most
  <math|C*e<rsup|-n>> for all sufficiently large <math|n> and therefore is
  zero. The generators of coordinate planes span
  <math|<with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)>>, giving the collinear
  identity for every skew generator. The skew matrices
  <math|E<rsub|a*b>-E<rsub|b*a>> form a real basis of
  <math|<with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)>>, and the angular response
  is real-linear in its generator, so it vanishes for every <math|K>. This
  proves the collinear input used above. The exponential estimate is the
  substantive analytic input here; it is not a consequence of the geometric
  propagation argument.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Outer translation identity><label|lem:ward-outer-miles> Under the
    same stress-law assumptions, the vector positional response also
    satisfies the following line identity. If
    <math|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>> lie on the axis <math|b>,
    <math|j\<ne\>b>, and <math|v<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-F\><rsub|d>>, then

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>S*<around*|(|q*<around|[|i\<mapsto\><around|(|d+1,p<rsub|d,j>*v<rsub|i>|)>|]>;x|)>=0,<space|2em>q<rsub|i>=<around|(|d,v<rsub|i>|)>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|K<rsub|j,b>> be the plane generator normalized by
    <math|K<rsub|j,b>*e<rsub|b>=e<rsub|j>>. Simultaneous translation
    preserves the moment and all spin terms. In the orbital term, translating
    every point by <math|e<rsub|b>> changes
    <math|<around|(|K<rsub|j,b>*x<rsub|i>|)><rsub|c>> to
    <math|<around|(|K<rsub|j,b>*x<rsub|i>|)><rsub|c>+<around|(|K<rsub|j,b>*e<rsub|b>|)><rsub|c>>.
    Only the <math|c=j> component changes, by one. Therefore

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-A\><rsub|K<rsub|j,b>>*<around|(|p,Y;q;x+e<rsub|b>|)>=\<cal-A\><rsub|K<rsub|j,b>>*<around|(|p,Y;q;x|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i>S*<around|(|q*<around|[|i\<mapsto\><around|(|d+1,p<rsub|d,j>*v<rsub|i>|)>|]>;x|)>.
    </equation*>

    Both tuples remain separated and lie on the same line, so both angular
    terms vanish. Subtracting gives the stated line identity. For the empty
    word it is the empty sum.
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|Finite-level bridges used by the selected scale.> The line
  identity and the collinear rotational identity are only the geometric part
  of the finite-level argument. The selected scale uses the following
  additional bridges; their order matters.

  <subparagraph|The stress-law package.> At the local algebraic stage the
  commutation relation and the covariance, reality, unit and inclusion rules
  for the raw responses are derived from the stress-law package rather than
  postulated separately. We first state that package precisely.

  A <em|window> for a separated tuple <math|<around|(|x<rsub|0>,x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>>
  is a smooth, compactly supported, real function <math|\<chi\>> on
  <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>> which equals one on a neighborhood of
  <math|x<rsub|0>> and whose support contains none of
  <math|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>>. Every separated tuple has a window:
  take a bump function equal to one on the ball about <math|x<rsub|0>> of
  radius <math|\<rho\>/4> and supported in the ball of radius
  <math|\<rho\>/2>, where <math|\<rho\>> is the least distance from
  <math|x<rsub|0>> to the other points when <math|n\<geqslant\>1>; for
  <math|n=0>, choose any <math|\<rho\>\<gtr\>0> (for example
  <math|\<rho\>=1>). If <math|\<chi\>> is a window, then
  <math|\<nabla\>*\<chi\>> vanishes on an open set <math|U\<ni\>x<rsub|0>>,
  so the compact set <math|C<rsub|\<chi\>>=<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<nabla\>*\<chi\>>
  misses <math|U> and all <math|x<rsub|i>> with <math|i\<geqslant\>1>. For a
  label <math|v\<in\>\<cal-F\><rsub|d>> at <math|x<rsub|0>> and a spectator
  word <math|\<b-q\>=<around|(|q<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,q<rsub|n>|)>> at
  <math|\<b-x\>=<around|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>> we write
  <math|S*<around|(|v,\<b-q\>;x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>> for the moment with
  <math|<around|(|d,v|)>> in the first slot, and
  <math|S*<around|(|J<rsub|a*b>,v,\<b-q\>;y,x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>> for the
  moment with an additional insertion <math|<around|(|4,J<rsub|a*b>|)>> at
  <math|y>. For <math|y\<in\>C<rsub|\<chi\>>> the tuple
  <math|<around|(|y,x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>> is separated.

  We write <math|<wide|v|\<bar\>>> for the conjugation of a field,
  <math|<math-ss|m><rsub|k>> for the action of a signed permutation matrix
  <math|k> on fields (acting on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>> by
  <math|y\<mapsto\>k*y>), <math|\<iota\>=\<iota\><rsub|d,e>> for the
  inclusion of grade <math|d> into grade <math|e\<geqslant\>d>, and
  <math|\<b-1\><rsub|d>> for the unit at grade <math|d>. Conjugation,
  <math|<math-ss|m><rsub|k>> and <math|\<iota\>> act on words slot by slot.
  The hierarchy properties used are: the derivative rule; faithfulness (a
  field <math|v> with <math|S*<around|(|v,\<b-q\>;x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>=0> for
  every word and every separated tuple is zero); reality,
  <math|S*<around|(|<wide|\<b-w\>|\<bar\>>;\<b-z\>|)>=<wide|S*<around|(|\<b-w\>;\<b-z\>|)>|\<bar\>>>;
  signed covariance, <math|S*<around|(|<math-ss|m><rsub|k>\<b-w\>;k*\<b-z\>|)>=S*<around|(|\<b-w\>;\<b-z\>|)>>;
  the unit law, by which a unit insertion may be deleted together with its
  position; and invariance of moments under replacing a label <math|v> by
  <math|\<iota\>*v>.

  The stress is a family <math|J=<around|(|J<rsub|a*b>|)><rsub|a,b=1><rsup|4>>
  of grade-four fields with

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(S1)><math|J<rsub|a*b>=J<rsub|b*a>>,
    <math|<wide|J<rsub|a*b>|\<bar\>>=J<rsub|a*b>>, and
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<cal-P\><rsub|4,a>*J<rsub|a*b>=0> for every
    <math|b>;

    <item*|(S2)><math|<math-ss|m><rsub|k>J<rsub|a*b>=<big|sum><rsub|e,f>k<rsub|e*a>*k<rsub|f*b>*J<rsub|e*f>>
    for every signed permutation matrix <math|k>;

    <item*|(S3)>for every separated tuple, every window <math|\<chi\>>, every
    label <math|v\<in\>\<cal-F\><rsub|d>>, every word <math|\<b-q\>>, every
    axis <math|b> and every <math|K\<in\><with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)>>,

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|S*<around|(|p<rsub|d,b>*v,\<b-q\>;x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>>|<cell|=-<big|sum><rsub|a><big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\><around|(|y|)>*<space|0.17em>S*<around|(|J<rsub|a*b>,v,\<b-q\>;y,x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y,<eq-number><label|eq:ward-raw-translation-flux>>>|<row|<cell|S*<around|(|Y<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>*v,\<b-q\>;x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>>|<cell|=-<big|sum><rsub|a,b><big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\><around|(|y|)>*<space|0.17em><around|[|K*<around|(|y-x<rsub|0>|)>|]><rsub|b>*<space|0.17em>S*<around|(|J<rsub|a*b>,v,\<b-q\>;y,x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y.<eq-number><label|eq:ward-raw-rotation-flux>>>>>
    </align>
  </enumerate>

  The integrands vanish off <math|C<rsub|\<chi\>>> and are continuous on it,
  so both integrals converge absolutely. Identities (S3) define the raw
  responses <math|p<rsub|d,b>:\<cal-F\><rsub|d>\<to\>\<cal-F\><rsub|d+1>> and
  <math|Y<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>:\<cal-F\><rsub|d>\<to\>\<cal-F\><rsub|d>>
  uniquely by faithfulness; they are identities of the same form as
  <eqref|eq:phase-translation-flux> and <eqref|eq:phase-rotation-flux>, where
  the responses appear after normalization.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Local response laws><label|lem:ward-local-response-laws> Assume
    (S1)\U(S3) and the hierarchy properties just listed. For every grade
    <math|d>, every <math|v\<in\>\<cal-F\><rsub|d>>, all axes <math|b,i>,
    every <math|K\<in\><with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)>>, every
    signed permutation matrix <math|k> and every <math|e\<geqslant\>d>:

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(i)>(commutation)

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-P\><rsub|d,b>*Y<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>*v+<big|sum><rsub|c>K<rsub|c*b>*<space|0.17em>p<rsub|d,c>*v=Y<rsub|d+1><around|(|K|)>*\<cal-P\><rsub|d,b>*v,<space|2em>\<cal-P\><rsub|d+1,b>*<space|0.17em>p<rsub|d,i>*v=p<rsub|d+1,i>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-P\><rsub|d,b>*v;
      </equation*>

      <item*|(ii)>(inclusion) <math|p<rsub|e,i>*\<iota\>*v=\<iota\>*p<rsub|d,i>*v>
      and <math|Y<rsub|e><around|(|K|)>*\<iota\>*v=\<iota\>*Y<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>*v>;

      <item*|(iii)>(unit) <math|p<rsub|d,i>*\<b-1\><rsub|d>=0> and
      <math|Y<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>*\<b-1\><rsub|d>=0>;

      <item*|(iv)>(reality) <math|<wide|p<rsub|d,i>*v|\<bar\>>=p<rsub|d,i>*<wide|v|\<bar\>>>
      and <math|<wide|Y<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>*v|\<bar\>>=Y<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>*<wide|v|\<bar\>>>;

      <item*|(v)>(signed covariance) <math|<math-ss|m><rsub|k>p<rsub|d,i>*v=<big|sum><rsub|j>k<rsub|j*i>*<space|0.17em>p<rsub|d,j><math-ss|m><rsub|k>v>
      and <math|<math-ss|m><rsub|k>Y<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>*v=Y<rsub|d>*<around|(|k*K*k<rsup|<math-ss|T>>|)><math-ss|m><rsub|k>v>.
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Each assertion is an equality of two fields at one grade. By faithfulness
    it suffices to show that both sides have the same moment against every
    word at every separated tuple. Fix such a tuple
    <math|<around|(|x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>>, a word <math|\<b-q\>> and a window
    <math|\<chi\>>, with <math|U> and <math|C<rsub|\<chi\>>> as above.

    (i) Put <math|x<rsub|0><around|(|t|)>=x<rsub|0>+t*e<rsub|b>>. Choose
    <math|t<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> such that <math|x<rsub|0><around|(|t|)>\<in\>U>
    for <math|<around|\||t|\|>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|0>>. For such <math|t> the
    tuple <math|<around|(|x<rsub|0><around|(|t|)>,\<b-x\>|)>> is separated
    and the <em|same> <math|\<chi\>> is a window for it, and for
    <math|y\<in\>C<rsub|\<chi\>>> the tuple
    <math|<around|(|y,x<rsub|0><around|(|t|)>,\<b-x\>|)>> is separated,
    because <math|C<rsub|\<chi\>>\<cap\>U=\<emptyset\>>. Apply
    <eqref|eq:ward-raw-rotation-flux> at <math|x<rsub|0><around|(|t|)>> with
    this fixed <math|\<chi\>>:

    <\equation*>
      f<around|(|t|)>\<assign\>S*<around|(|Y<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>*v,\<b-q\>;x<rsub|0><around|(|t|)>,\<b-x\>|)>=-<big|sum><rsub|a,c><big|int><rsub|C<rsub|\<chi\>>>\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\><around|(|y|)>*<space|0.17em><around|[|K*<around|(|y-x<rsub|0><around|(|t|)>|)>|]><rsub|c>*<space|0.17em>S*<around|(|J<rsub|a*c>,v,\<b-q\>;y,x<rsub|0><around|(|t|)>,\<b-x\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y.
    </equation*>

    By the derivative rule, <math|f<rprime|'><around|(|0|)>=S*<around|(|\<cal-P\><rsub|d,b>*Y<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>*v,\<b-q\>;x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>>.
    On the right, the integration runs over the fixed compact set
    <math|C<rsub|\<chi\>>>, and the integrand is continuously differentiable
    in <math|t> on <math|<around|[|-t<rsub|0>,t<rsub|0>|]>\<times\>C<rsub|\<chi\>>>,
    since moments are smooth on configuration space. Hence we may
    differentiate under the integral. The <math|t>-derivative of the weight
    <math|<around|[|K*<around|(|y-x<rsub|0><around|(|t|)>|)>|]><rsub|c>> is
    <math|-K<rsub|c*b>>. By the derivative rule, the <math|t>-derivative of
    the moment replaces <math|v> by <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|d,b>*v>. Thus

    <\equation*>
      f<rprime|'><around|(|0|)>=<big|sum><rsub|c>K<rsub|c*b>*<big|sum><rsub|a><big|int>\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\>*<space|0.17em>S*<around|(|J<rsub|a*c>,v,\<b-q\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y-<big|sum><rsub|a,c><big|int>\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\>*<space|0.17em><around|[|K*<around|(|y-x<rsub|0>|)>|]><rsub|c>*<space|0.17em>S*<around|(|J<rsub|a*c>,\<cal-P\><rsub|d,b>*v,\<b-q\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y,
    </equation*>

    with all moments at <math|<around|(|y,x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>>. By
    <eqref|eq:ward-raw-translation-flux>, with <math|b> replaced by <math|c>,
    the first term is

    <\equation*>
      -<big|sum><rsub|c>K<rsub|c*b>*<space|0.17em>S*<around|(|p<rsub|d,c>*v,\<b-q\>;x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>.
    </equation*>

    By <eqref|eq:ward-raw-rotation-flux> at grade <math|d+1> the second term
    is <math|S*<around|(|Y<rsub|d+1><around|(|K|)>*\<cal-P\><rsub|d,b>*v,\<b-q\>;x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>>.
    Comparing the two expressions for <math|f<rprime|'><around|(|0|)>> gives
    the first identity in (i) against every word. The second identity follows
    in the same way from <eqref|eq:ward-raw-translation-flux>; there the
    weight does not depend on <math|t>, so only the moment is differentiated.

    (ii) Replacing <math|v> by <math|\<iota\>*v> does not change any moment,
    in particular not the integrands of (S3). Hence
    <math|S*<around|(|p<rsub|e,i>*\<iota\>*v,\<b-q\>;x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>=S*<around|(|p<rsub|d,i>*v,\<b-q\>;x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>=S*<around|(|\<iota\>*p<rsub|d,i>*v,\<b-q\>;x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>>,
    and likewise for <math|Y>.

    (iii) Let <math|w<around|(|y|)>> be the constant vector <math|e<rsub|i>>,
    respectively the rotational field <math|K*<around|(|y-x<rsub|0>|)>>. In
    both cases <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>*w<rsub|b>+\<partial\><rsub|b>*w<rsub|a>=0>.
    By the unit law, <math|S*<around|(|J<rsub|a*b>,\<b-1\><rsub|d>,\<b-q\>;y,x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>=g<rsub|a*b><around|(|y|)>\<assign\>S*<around|(|J<rsub|a*b>,\<b-q\>;y,\<b-x\>|)>>.
    The function <math|g<rsub|a*b>> is smooth on
    <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>\<setminus\><around|{|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|}>>,
    which is a neighborhood of <math|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<chi\>>;
    the point <math|x<rsub|0>> no longer carries an insertion. The products
    <math|\<chi\>*w<rsub|b>*g<rsub|a*b>> therefore extend by zero to smooth
    compactly supported functions, and integration by parts gives

    <\equation*>
      -<big|sum><rsub|a,b><big|int>\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\>*<space|0.17em>w<rsub|b>*<space|0.17em>g<rsub|a*b>*<space|0.17em>d*y=<big|sum><rsub|a,b><big|int>\<chi\>*<space|0.17em><around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*w<rsub|b>|)>*<space|0.17em>g<rsub|a*b>*<space|0.17em>d*y+<big|sum><rsub|b><big|int>\<chi\>*<space|0.17em>w<rsub|b>*<big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|a>*g<rsub|a*b>*<space|0.17em>d*y.
    </equation*>

    The first sum vanishes, because <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>*w<rsub|b>> is
    antisymmetric and <math|g<rsub|a*b>=g<rsub|b*a>> by (S1). By the
    derivative rule, <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|a>*g<rsub|a*b><around|(|y|)>=S*<around|(|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<cal-P\><rsub|4,a>*J<rsub|a*b>,\<b-q\>;y,\<b-x\>|)>=0>
    by conservation. By (S3) the left side is the moment of
    <math|p<rsub|d,i>*\<b-1\><rsub|d>>, respectively
    <math|Y<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>*\<b-1\><rsub|d>>, against <math|\<b-q\>>;
    so both vanish. This uses conservation on the word from which the unit
    has been deleted; it is not a formal consequence of an identity at
    nonzero grade.

    (iv) Conjugate both sides of (S3). The functions <math|\<chi\>>, <math|w>
    and the matrix <math|K> are real, and <math|J<rsub|a*b>> is real by (S1),
    so reality of the moments gives <math|<wide|S*<around|(|p<rsub|d,i>*v,\<b-q\>;x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>|\<bar\>>=S*<around|(|p<rsub|d,i>*<wide|v|\<bar\>>,<wide|\<b-q\>|\<bar\>>;x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>>.
    The left side is <math|S*<around|(|<wide|p<rsub|d,i>*v|\<bar\>>,<wide|\<b-q\>|\<bar\>>;x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>>.
    Since <math|\<b-q\>\<mapsto\><wide|\<b-q\>|\<bar\>>> is a bijection of
    words, faithfulness gives the first identity in (iv); the second is
    identical.

    (v) The matrix <math|k> is orthogonal, and
    <math|\<chi\><rprime|'>=\<chi\>\<circ\>k<rsup|-1>> is a window for
    <math|<around|(|k*x<rsub|0>,k*\<b-x\>|)>>. By the chain rule
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\><around|(|y|)>=<big|sum><rsub|e>k<rsub|e*a>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|e>*\<chi\><rprime|'>|)>*<around|(|k*y|)>>.
    By signed covariance of the moments and (S2),

    <\equation*>
      S*<around|(|J<rsub|a*b>,v,\<b-q\>;y,x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|e,f>k<rsub|e*a>*k<rsub|f*b>*<space|0.17em>S*<around|(|J<rsub|e*f>,<math-ss|m><rsub|k>v,<math-ss|m><rsub|k>\<b-q\>;k*y,k*x<rsub|0>,k*\<b-x\>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Insert both into <eqref|eq:ward-raw-translation-flux> with <math|b=i> and
    substitute <math|y<rprime|'>=k*y>, which preserves Lebesgue measure.
    Orthogonality, <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>k<rsub|e<rprime|'>*a>*k<rsub|e*a>=\<delta\><rsub|e<rprime|'>*e>>,
    leaves

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|S*<around|(|p<rsub|d,i>*v,\<b-q\>;x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|f>k<rsub|f*i>*<around*|(|-<big|sum><rsub|e><big|int>\<partial\><rsub|e>*\<chi\><rprime|'><around|(|y<rprime|'>|)>*<space|0.17em>S*<around|(|J<rsub|e*f>,<math-ss|m><rsub|k>v,<math-ss|m><rsub|k>\<b-q\>;y<rprime|'>,k*x<rsub|0>,k*\<b-x\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y<rprime|'>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|f>k<rsub|f*i>*<space|0.17em>S*<around|(|p<rsub|d,f><math-ss|m><rsub|k>v,<math-ss|m><rsub|k>\<b-q\>;k*x<rsub|0>,k*\<b-x\>|)>.>>>>
    </align*>

    The left side equals <math|S*<around|(|<math-ss|m><rsub|k>p<rsub|d,i>*v,<math-ss|m><rsub|k>\<b-q\>;k*x<rsub|0>,k*\<b-x\>|)>>.
    As <math|<around|(|x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>,\<b-q\>|)>> ranges over all
    separated tuples and words, so does <math|<around|(|k*x<rsub|0>,k*\<b-x\>,<math-ss|m><rsub|k>\<b-q\>|)>>,
    because <math|k> and <math|<math-ss|m><rsub|k>> are invertible.
    Faithfulness gives the first identity in (v). For the second use in
    addition <math|<around|[|K*<around|(|y-x<rsub|0>|)>|]><rsub|b>=<big|sum><rsub|f<rprime|'>>k<rsub|f<rprime|'>*b>*<space|0.17em><around|[|K<rprime|'>*<around|(|y<rprime|'>-k*x<rsub|0>|)>|]><rsub|f<rprime|'>>>
    with <math|K<rprime|'>=k*K*k<rsup|<math-ss|T>>>, which holds because
    <math|k*K*<around|(|y-x<rsub|0>|)>=K<rprime|'>*<around|(|k*y-k*x<rsub|0>|)>>.
    The two orthogonality sums over <math|a> and <math|b> then turn
    <eqref|eq:ward-raw-rotation-flux> at <math|<around|(|x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>>
    into <eqref|eq:ward-raw-rotation-flux> for <math|K<rprime|'>> and
    <math|\<chi\><rprime|'>> at <math|<around|(|k*x<rsub|0>,k*\<b-x\>|)>>
    with labels <math|<math-ss|m><rsub|k>v>,
    <math|<math-ss|m><rsub|k>\<b-q\>>, and faithfulness gives the claim.
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|Readout limits on the comparison curve.> We next prove the
  two limits that compare the raw responses with the reference derivative and
  rotation action along the comparison curve. Use the notation of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-stress-normalization>:
  <math|E<rsub|d>> is the reference field space,
  <math|F<rsub|d>=\<cal-F\><rsub|d>> the physical one,
  <math|U<rsub|k,d>:E<rsub|d>\<to\>F<rsub|d>> the comparison maps,
  <math|r<rsub|k>*\<downarrow\>*0> the radii, <math|\<xi\><rsub|k>\<gtr\>0>
  the stress normalizers, <math|J> the physical and <math|J<rsup|0>> the
  reference stress. We write <math|S<rsup|1>> and <math|S<rsup|0>> for
  physical and reference moments.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Translation and rotation readout
    limits><label|prop:ward-readout-limits>Assume the following.

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(a)>The physical raw responses <math|p,Y> satisfy (S3) with the
      stress <math|J>.

      <item*|(b)>The reference table satisfies (S3) with the stress
      <math|J<rsup|0>> and with responses
      <math|\<kappa\>*\<partial\><rsup|0>> and <math|\<kappa\>*C>, where
      <math|\<partial\><rsup|0>> is the reference derivative, <math|C> is the
      reference infinitesimal rotation action, and <math|\<kappa\>\<ne\>0> is
      the reference Ward coefficient (<math|\<kappa\>=-1> by
      Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:free-maxwell-ward-flux>).

      <item*|(c)>The normalization <eqref|eq:stress-fixed-normalization>
      holds: <math|<wide|J|~><rsub|k,a*b>\<assign\>U<rsub|k,4><rsup|-1>*<around|(|\<xi\><rsub|k><rsup|-1>*r<rsub|k><rsup|4>*J<rsub|a*b>|)>\<to\>J<rsup|0><rsub|a*b>>.

      <item*|(d)>Order zero of the compact convergence of
      Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-stress-normalization> holds: for
      every fixed tuple <math|\<b-v\>> of reference labels and every compact
      <math|Q\<subset\><math-up|Conf>>, <math|sup<rsub|\<b-z\>\<in\>Q><around|\||S<rsup|1>*<around|(|U<rsub|k>*\<b-v\>;r<rsub|k>*\<b-z\>|)>-S<rsup|0>*<around|(|\<b-v\>;\<b-z\>|)>|\|>\<to\>0>.
    </enumerate>

    Then for every grade <math|d>, every axis <math|b> and every
    <math|K\<in\><with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)>>, in operator norm,

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<frac|r<rsub|k>|\<xi\><rsub|k>>*<space|0.17em>U<rsub|k,d+1><rsup|-1>*<space|0.17em>p<rsub|d,b>*<space|0.17em>U<rsub|k,d>>|<cell|\<longrightarrow\>\<kappa\>*<space|0.17em>\<partial\><rsup|0><rsub|d,b>,<eq-number><label|eq:ward-translation-readout>>>|<row|<cell|\<xi\><rsub|k><rsup|-1>*<space|0.17em>U<rsub|k,d><rsup|-1>*<space|0.17em>Y<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>*<space|0.17em>U<rsub|k,d>>|<cell|\<longrightarrow\>\<kappa\>*<space|0.17em>C<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>.<eq-number><label|eq:ward-rotation-readout>>>>>
    </align>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    <em|Step 1: finitely many separating reads.> Fix a grade <math|e>. An
    <em|observer> is a separated tuple <math|<around|(|x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>>
    together with a reference word <math|\<b-q\>> at <math|\<b-x\>>; its
    reference read is the linear functional
    <math|w\<mapsto\>S<rsup|0>*<around|(|w,\<b-q\>;x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>> on
    <math|E<rsub|e>>. By faithfulness the reads of all observers have zero
    common kernel. Since <math|E<rsub|e>> is finite-dimensional, finitely
    many observers <math|o\<in\>O> already have zero common kernel: add
    observers while the common kernel drops in dimension, which happens at
    most <math|dim E<rsub|e>> times. Let <math|M:E<rsub|e>\<to\>\<bbb-C\><rsup|O>>
    be the injective map formed by their reference reads, and
    <math|M<rsub|k>> the map formed by the scaled physical reads

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|M<rsub|k>*w|)><rsub|o>=S<rsup|1>*<around|(|U<rsub|k,e>*w,U<rsub|k>*\<b-q\><rsup|o>;r<rsub|k>*x<rsub|0><rsup|o>,r<rsub|k>*\<b-x\><rsup|o>|)>.
    </equation*>

    By (d), applied at the single point of each observer,
    <math|M<rsub|k>*w\<to\>M*w> for every <math|w>, hence
    <math|M<rsub|k>\<to\>M> in operator norm. As in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-stress-normalization>, compactness
    of the unit sphere gives <math|\<gamma\>\<gtr\>0> with
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|M*w|\<\|\|\>>\<geqslant\>\<gamma\><around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>>>,
    hence <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|M<rsub|k>*w|\<\|\|\>>\<geqslant\><around|(|\<gamma\>/2|)><around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>>>
    for all large <math|k>. Consequently, if <math|w<rsub|k>\<in\>E<rsub|e>>
    and <math|M<rsub|k>*w<rsub|k>\<to\>M*w>, then

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|w<rsub|k>-w|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><tfrac|2|\<gamma\>><around|\<\|\|\>|M<rsub|k>*<around|(|w<rsub|k>-w|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><tfrac|2|\<gamma\>><around*|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|M<rsub|k>*w<rsub|k>-M*w|\<\|\|\>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|M-M<rsub|k>|)>*w|\<\|\|\>>|)>\<to\>0.
    </equation*>

    It therefore suffices to prove, for each fixed <math|v\<in\>E<rsub|d>>
    and each observer <math|<around|(|x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>,\<b-q\>|)>>, that the
    scaled physical read of

    <\equation*>
      w<rsub|k>=<frac|r<rsub|k>|\<xi\><rsub|k>>*U<rsub|k,d+1><rsup|-1>*p<rsub|d,b>*U<rsub|k,d>*v<space|1em><text|resp.><space|1em>w<rsub|k><rprime|'>=\<xi\><rsub|k><rsup|-1>*U<rsub|k,d><rsup|-1>*Y<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>*U<rsub|k,d>*v
    </equation*>

    converges to the reference read of <math|\<kappa\>*\<partial\><rsup|0><rsub|d,b>*v>,
    respectively <math|\<kappa\>*C<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>*v>. Operator-norm
    convergence then follows by checking a finite basis of <math|E<rsub|d>>.

    <em|Step 2: rescaling the flux.> Fix the observer and a window
    <math|\<chi\>> for <math|<around|(|x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>>. Then
    <math|\<chi\><rsub|k><around|(|z|)>=\<chi\>*<around|(|z/r<rsub|k>|)>> is
    a window for <math|<around|(|r<rsub|k>*x<rsub|0>,r<rsub|k>*\<b-x\>|)>>.
    By (a) with <math|\<chi\><rsub|k>>,

    <\equation*>
      S<rsup|1>*<around|(|U<rsub|k,d+1>*w<rsub|k>,U<rsub|k>*\<b-q\>;r<rsub|k>*x<rsub|0>,r<rsub|k>*\<b-x\>|)>=-<frac|r<rsub|k>|\<xi\><rsub|k>>*<big|sum><rsub|a><big|int>\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\><rsub|k><around|(|z|)>*<space|0.17em>S<rsup|1>*<around|(|J<rsub|a*b>,U<rsub|k,d>*v,U<rsub|k>*\<b-q\>;z,r<rsub|k>*x<rsub|0>,r<rsub|k>*\<b-x\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*z.
    </equation*>

    Substitute <math|z=r<rsub|k>*y>. Then
    <math|d*z=r<rsub|k><rsup|4>*<space|0.17em>d*y> and
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\><rsub|k>*<around|(|r<rsub|k>*y|)>=r<rsub|k><rsup|-1>*\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\><around|(|y|)>>,
    so the powers of <math|r<rsub|k>> combine to
    <math|r<rsub|k>\<cdot\>r<rsub|k><rsup|-1>\<cdot\>r<rsub|k><rsup|4>=r<rsub|k><rsup|4>>.
    By linearity in the stress slot and the definition of
    <math|<wide|J|~><rsub|k,a*b>>,

    <\equation*>
      S<rsup|1>*<around|(|U<rsub|k,d+1>*w<rsub|k>,U<rsub|k>*\<b-q\>;r<rsub|k>*x<rsub|0>,r<rsub|k>*\<b-x\>|)>=-<big|sum><rsub|a>\<Lambda\><rsup|a,1><rsub|k><around|(|<wide|J|~><rsub|k,a*b>|)>,
    </equation*>

    where, for a continuous weight <math|\<omega\>> on
    <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>, we put

    <\equation*>
      \<Lambda\><rsup|a,\<omega\>><rsub|k><around|(|u|)>=<big|int>\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\><around|(|y|)>*<space|0.17em>\<omega\><around|(|y|)>*<space|0.17em>S<rsup|1>*<around|(|U<rsub|k,4>*u,U<rsub|k,d>*v,U<rsub|k>*\<b-q\>;r<rsub|k>*y,r<rsub|k>*x<rsub|0>,r<rsub|k>*\<b-x\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y,<space|2em>u\<in\>E<rsub|4>.
    </equation*>

    For the rotation, (a) with <math|\<chi\><rsub|k>> gives the weight
    <math|<around|[|K*<around|(|z-r<rsub|k>*x<rsub|0>|)>|]><rsub|b>=r<rsub|k>*<around|[|K*<around|(|y-x<rsub|0>|)>|]><rsub|b>>
    at <math|z=r<rsub|k>*y>. The powers of <math|r<rsub|k>> now combine to
    <math|r<rsub|k><rsup|-1>\<cdot\>r<rsub|k>\<cdot\>r<rsub|k><rsup|4>=r<rsub|k><rsup|4>>,
    which is again absorbed by <math|\<xi\><rsub|k><rsup|-1>> into
    <math|<wide|J|~><rsub|k,a*b>>:

    <\equation*>
      S<rsup|1>*<around|(|U<rsub|k,d>*w<rsub|k><rprime|'>,U<rsub|k>*\<b-q\>;r<rsub|k>*x<rsub|0>,r<rsub|k>*\<b-x\>|)>=-<big|sum><rsub|a,b>\<Lambda\><rsup|a,\<omega\><rsub|b>><rsub|k><around|(|<wide|J|~><rsub|k,a*b>|)>,<space|2em>\<omega\><rsub|b><around|(|y|)>=<around|[|K*<around|(|y-x<rsub|0>|)>|]><rsub|b>.
    </equation*>

    <em|Step 3: the limit.> Let <math|\<Lambda\><rsup|a,\<omega\>>> be the
    same functional with <math|S<rsup|0>*<around|(|u,v,\<b-q\>;y,x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>>
    in place of the scaled physical moment. The set
    <math|Q=<around|{|<around|(|y,x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>:y\<in\>C<rsub|\<chi\>>|}>>
    is a compact subset of configuration space. For fixed
    <math|u\<in\>E<rsub|4>>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<Lambda\><rsup|a,\<omega\>><rsub|k><around|(|u|)>-\<Lambda\><rsup|a,\<omega\>><around|(|u|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|int><around|\||\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\>*<space|0.17em>\<omega\>|\|>*<space|0.17em>d*y*\<cdummy\>*sup<rsub|Q><around*|\||S<rsup|1>*<around|(|U<rsub|k>*<around|(|u,v,\<b-q\>|)>;r<rsub|k>*<space|0.17em>\<cdummy\><space|0.17em>|)>-S<rsup|0>(<around|(|u,v,\<b-q\>|)>;<space|0.17em>\<cdummy\><space|0.17em><around*|)||\|>\<to\>0|\<nobracket\>>
    </equation*>

    by (d). Since <math|E<rsub|4>> is finite-dimensional,
    <math|\<Lambda\><rsup|a,\<omega\>><rsub|k>\<to\>\<Lambda\><rsup|a,\<omega\>>>
    in norm. Together with (c) this gives
    <math|\<Lambda\><rsup|a,\<omega\>><rsub|k><around|(|<wide|J|~><rsub|k,a*b>|)>\<to\>\<Lambda\><rsup|a,\<omega\>><around|(|J<rsup|0><rsub|a*b>|)>>.
    Summing over the finitely many <math|a> (and <math|b>), the scaled
    physical reads of <math|w<rsub|k>> and <math|w<rsub|k><rprime|'>>
    converge to

    <\equation*>
      -<big|sum><rsub|a>\<Lambda\><rsup|a,1><around|(|J<rsup|0><rsub|a*b>|)>=S<rsup|0>*<around|(|\<kappa\>*\<partial\><rsup|0><rsub|d,b>*v,\<b-q\>;x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>,<space|2em>-<big|sum><rsub|a,b>\<Lambda\><rsup|a,\<omega\><rsub|b>><around|(|J<rsup|0><rsub|a*b>|)>=S<rsup|0>*<around|(|\<kappa\>*C<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>*v,\<b-q\>;x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>,
    </equation*>

    by (b). Step<nbsp>1 with <math|e=d+1>, respectively <math|e=d>, completes
    the proof.
  </proof>

  Only finitely many observers, labels and compact sets enter the proof for a
  fixed grade, so a single tail of the comparison curve serves all of them.
  In the translation limit the extra factor <math|r<rsub|k>> compensates the
  one power lost by the gradient of the rescaled window. In the rotation
  limit the arm of the rotational weight supplies that power. These are
  exactly the normalizations used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-angular-actions>.

  To obtain the all-order line jets, do not differentiate an identity known
  only on the line. First form the mixed-cap row which sums, over the marked
  slot, the moment with that label raised once in a fixed transverse
  direction. The derivative rule and the positional/spin compatibility
  identity prove on the whole open configuration space that differentiating
  this row in any point-coordinate direction equals the corresponding row
  with that slot raised once more. This is a local identity on
  <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>>, not a line-restricted assertion. On the
  line, the order-zero mixed-cap row vanishes: pad all caps to their finite
  maximum by inclusion compatibility, then apply the line-sum identity. Now
  induct on derivative order. The case of order zero is this line-sum
  statement. For the induction step, the global derivative identity just
  proved expresses every coordinate directional derivative as a row with one
  additional raised slot; evaluate that raised row at the line and use the
  induction hypothesis. Since coordinate directions span the
  configuration-space tangent space, all multilinear positional jets vanish
  there. This is the transverse-jet condition used by the finite-scale
  argument.

  First, finite test rows determine the free derivative data. At each grade,
  faithfulness of the free table says the family of all evaluation rows has
  zero common kernel. Since the field space at fixed grade is finite
  dimensional, finitely many such rows already have zero common kernel: add
  rows while the intersection of kernels drops in dimension; this can happen
  only finitely often. Under a scaling sequence, the prescribed local jet
  convergence makes each of these finitely many actual rows converge on every
  fixed free vector. The limiting row map is injective. Compose the actual
  row maps with a left inverse of the limit; in finite dimension the
  resulting operators converge to the identity and are invertible eventually.
  If the row maps are evaluated on the moving scaled derivative, the given
  fitted row convergence then forces that derivative to converge to the free
  derivative. This is the gradient component of the first scaling bridge.

  Second, the same finite-row method is applied to a finite family of
  observers at adjacent grades. This is Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ward-readout-limits>:
  the stress normalization <eqref|eq:stress-fixed-normalization> and the flux
  identities <eqref|eq:ward-raw-translation-flux>,
  <eqref|eq:ward-raw-rotation-flux> identify the limiting rows with
  <math|\<kappa\>> times the reference derivative and reference rotation
  action. Its observer set is united with the one of the first bridge before
  one scale threshold is chosen, so these limits use one selected scale
  sequence. The precise rank-one argument identifying the positional scalar
  is given after the finite collinear rows have been transported.

  Third, transport the finite collinear tests through the same scale. For
  each selected scene <math|s> and substitution <math|q>, the exact scaling
  identity is

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|test><rsub|k><space|-0.17em><around*|(|s,<math-up|aisle><rsub|k><around|(|q|)>|)>=r<rsub|k><rsup|m<rsub|s>><space|0.17em><math-up|lens><rsub|<math-up|scaled><around|(|k,s|)>><around|(|q|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|m<rsub|s>> is the number of positional derivatives in that
  scene and <math|r<rsub|k>\<gtr\>0> is the scale factor. The local jet
  estimates make these row maps converge on every fixed free substitution,
  and a finite-dimensional basis then gives operator convergence for the
  finite family. The derivative and the local response are both transverse:
  each transported test row annihilates each of them exactly at every scale.
  Thus the same selected family has the required common zero-row property;
  there is no injectivity claim for the free collinear tests. This is an
  exact-kernel argument, not an eventual-injectivity argument.

  Theorems<nbsp><reference|thm:free-quiet-classification> and
  <reference|thm:free-collinear-scenes> give one more rank statement needed
  to identify the normalization. Work at each fixed grade <math|d> above the
  common detector threshold, where the free derivative <math|\<partial\>> is
  nonzero. Let <math|V<rsub|d>> be the finite-dimensional real space of
  admissible spin-sector response vectors: arrays
  <math|u=<around|(|u<rsub|b>|)><rsub|b>> of linear maps from the reference
  grade-<math|d> space to grade <math|d+1> which annihilate the unit, commute
  with conjugation, satisfy <math|<math-ss|m><rsub|k>u<rsub|i>=<big|sum><rsub|j>k<rsub|j*i>*u<rsub|j><math-ss|m><rsub|k>>
  for every signed permutation matrix <math|k>, and map
  <math|\<iota\><rsub|a,d>*E<rsub|a>> into
  <math|\<iota\><rsub|a+1,d+1>*E<rsub|a+1>> for every <math|a\<leqslant\>d>.
  Let <math|R:V<rsub|d>\<to\>W<rsub|d>> be the free finite collinear row map.
  Its kernel is exactly the span of <math|\<partial\>>. Choose a real linear
  functional <math|\<ell\>> with <math|\<ell\><around|(|\<partial\>|)>\<ne\>0>;
  then the augmented map <math|<around|(|R,\<ell\>|)>> is injective. The
  selected row maps converge on a fixed basis, so adjoining this same
  <math|\<ell\>> leaves them injective for all sufficiently large scales.
  Choose one such scale <math|\<nu\>>. Write <math|p<rsub|\<nu\>>> and
  <math|\<partial\><rsub|\<nu\>>> for the transported response vectors, and
  let <math|A<rsub|\<nu\>>> be the common aisle map from the original
  finite-dimensional field space into the selected coordinates. Both
  transported vectors lie in <math|V<rsub|d>>. The physical arrays
  <math|<around|(|p<rsub|d,b>|)><rsub|b>> and
  <math|<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|d,b>|)><rsub|b>> have the four defining
  properties: for <math|p> these are parts (ii)\U(v) of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ward-local-response-laws>, for the derivative
  they are the corresponding properties of the derivative plan. The aligned
  comparison maps of the selected scale preserve units, conjugations, signed
  actions and inclusions through grade <math|d+1>, so they carry such arrays
  into <math|V<rsub|d>>. The common zero-row property stated above gives
  <math|R<rsub|\<nu\>>*A<rsub|\<nu\>>*p=R<rsub|\<nu\>>*A<rsub|\<nu\>>*\<partial\>=0>.
  Since <math|A<rsub|\<nu\>>> is injective and <math|\<partial\>\<ne\>0>,
  <math|\<partial\><rsub|\<nu\>>\<ne\>0>. If
  <math|\<ell\><around|(|\<partial\><rsub|\<nu\>>|)>=0>, then augmented
  injectivity of <math|<around|(|R<rsub|\<nu\>>,\<ell\>|)>> would force
  <math|\<partial\><rsub|\<nu\>>=0>; thus this denominator is nonzero. Set
  <math|c<rsub|d,\<nu\>>=\<ell\><around|(|p<rsub|\<nu\>>|)>/\<ell\><around|(|\<partial\><rsub|\<nu\>>|)>>.
  The vector <math|p<rsub|\<nu\>>-c<rsub|d,\<nu\>>*\<partial\><rsub|\<nu\>>>
  is killed by both <math|R<rsub|\<nu\>>> and <math|\<ell\>>, hence is zero.
  Cancel the injective common aisle map to obtain
  <math|p=c<rsub|d,\<nu\>>*\<partial\>> in the original field space. Moreover
  <math|c<rsub|d,\<nu\>>\<ne\>0>. Indeed, if <math|c<rsub|d,\<nu\>>=0>, then
  <math|p<rsub|d,b>=0> for every <math|b>, so the left side of
  <eqref|eq:ward-translation-readout> vanishes for every <math|k>. Its limit
  <math|\<kappa\>*\<partial\><rsup|0><rsub|d,b>> would then be zero for every
  <math|b>, which is impossible: <math|\<kappa\>\<ne\>0>, and the reference
  derivative is nonzero at grades above the detector threshold. Grade
  inclusion intertwines <math|p> and <math|\<partial\>>. Comparing adjacent
  grades above the detector threshold shows that this scalar is constant
  there; comparing any lower grade with the fixed threshold grade and using
  injectivity of inclusion gives the same identity below the threshold. For
  any finite collection of caps and grades, take the maximum of the finitely
  many row-convergence thresholds. Thus the same scalar <math|c> works for
  every fixed finite comparison in the selected family. The independent
  stress-normalization result, together with the A05 observer limits,
  identifies the spin response with the corresponding same-scale multiple;
  define <math|<wide|Y|~>=c<rsup|-1>*Y>. This ratio is obtained before the
  downstream rotation-action argument.

  Finally, erasure transfers from the free table to the selected table by the
  same argument. At fixed grade, regard the internal Ward maps as a
  finite-dimensional real vector space. Free erasure says the totality of all
  separated row sums separates this space, so finitely many rows suffice.
  Their actual counterparts converge on every fixed map. Eventual injectivity
  follows by applying a fixed left inverse of the free row map. For a
  selected-table Ward map, pull it back by the aligned grade isomorphism. It
  lies in the free covariance subspace; if all selected row sums vanish, the
  finite actual rows annihilate the pullback, hence it is zero. Conjugating
  back proves erasure at all sufficiently large grades. The
  finite-dimensional injectivity argument uses pointwise convergence on a
  fixed basis and does not assume convergence on a moving map.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Differentiated Ward identity and
    brackets><label|lem:ward-brackets>Suppose Still holds at every separated
    configuration; suppose also the spin rows satisfy reality, preserve the
    unit, commute with grade inclusion, and obey signed-permutation
    covariance: for every grade <math|d>, signed permutation matrix <math|k>,
    <math|u\<in\><with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)>> and
    <math|v\<in\>\<cal-F\><rsub|d>>,

    <\equation*>
      <math-ss|m><rsub|k>\<cal-Y\><rsub|d><around|(|u|)>*v=\<cal-Y\><rsub|d>*<around|(|k*u*k<rsup|<math-ss|T>>|)><math-ss|m><rsub|k>v.
    </equation*>

    If the selected table has the erasure property just described, then for
    every grade <math|d> and <math|u,v\<in\><with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)>>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|[|\<cal-Y\><rsub|d><around|(|u|)>,\<cal-Y\><rsub|d><around|(|v|)>|]>=\<cal-Y\><rsub|d><around|(|<around|[|u,v|]>|)>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For a fixed word <math|q> define the simultaneous orbital vector field

    <\equation*>
      X<rsub|u><around|(|x|)><rsub|i>=u*x<rsub|i>.
    </equation*>

    The Ward identity and the derivative rule give

    <\equation>
      X<rsub|u>*S*<around|(|q;x|)>=-<big|sum><rsub|i>S*<around|(|q*<around|[|i\<mapsto\>\<cal-Y\><around|(|u|)>*q<rsub|i>|]>;x|)>.<label|eq:ward-orbital>
    </equation>

    The minus sign is fixed by the convention in the angular expression.
    Because Still holds throughout the open configuration space, (1) can be
    differentiated there and used again on each substituted word. The vector
    field bracket is <math|<around|[|X<rsub|u>,X<rsub|v>|]>=-X<rsub|<around|[|u,v|]>>>;
    this follows directly by differentiating the linear maps
    <math|x\<mapsto\>u*x> and <math|x\<mapsto\>v*x>. Consequently the
    commutator of the two orbital derivatives on a moment is
    <math|-X<rsub|<around|[|u,v|]>>*S*<around|(|q;x|)>>.

    On the other hand, apply <eqref|eq:ward-orbital> twice. Terms where the
    two spin substitutions hit distinct slots cancel in pairs: the term
    indexed by <math|<around|(|i,j|)>> in one order is the same moment as the
    term indexed by <math|<around|(|j,i|)>> in the reverse order. The terms
    with both substitutions at slot <math|i> leave the positive commutator

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i>S*<around*|(|q*<around|[|i\<mapsto\><around|(|<around|[|\<cal-Y\><rsub|d><around|(|u|)>,\<cal-Y\><rsub|d><around|(|v|)>|]>*q<rsub|i>|)>|]>;x|)>.
    </equation*>

    The orbital vector-field bracket is <math|-X<rsub|<around|[|u,v|]>>>, and
    by <eqref|eq:ward-orbital> its action on the moment is
    <math|+<big|sum><rsub|i>S*<around|(|q*<around|[|i\<mapsto\>\<cal-Y\><rsub|d><around|(|<around|[|u,v|]>|)>*q<rsub|i>|]>;x|)>>.
    Equating the two computations gives zero row sum for the defect

    <\equation*>
      D<rsub|d>*<around|(|u,v|)>=<around|[|\<cal-Y\><rsub|d><around|(|u|)>,\<cal-Y\><rsub|d><around|(|v|)>|]>-\<cal-Y\><rsub|d><around|(|<around|[|u,v|]>|)>
    </equation*>

    has zero row sum on every separated word.

    The covariance assumptions imply that <math|D<rsub|d>> is an internal
    Ward map: it transforms under signed rotations, respects conjugation,
    annihilates the unit, is alternating, and is compatible with grade
    inclusion. Thus it belongs to the finite-level internal kernel tested by
    erasure. Erasure annihilates it for every grade above a fixed threshold.
    For any smaller grade, raise to a grade above that threshold; inclusion
    compatibility identifies the raised defect with the image of the original
    one. The grade inclusion is injective, so the original defect was zero as
    well. This proves the bracket identity at every grade.
  </proof>

  <\corollary>
    <dueto|Selected finite-level Ward package><label|cor:selected-ward-package>Assume
    the selected finite-level data supplied by
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-stress-normalization> and the
    finite-row comparisons above. In particular, for one <math|c\<ne\>0> the
    raw responses obey <math|p=c*V> and <math|Y=c*<wide|Y|~>>, where <math|V>
    is the genuine positional derivative and <math|<wide|Y|~>> is the
    normalized spin action. Assume also the two free inputs: a finite
    collinear classifier for the quiet derivative rows and erasure of any
    alternating two-generator internal map whose separated Ward rows vanish,
    with the hypotheses used in the finite-row comparisons above. Then the
    actual collinear rotational identity and line identity hold, the Ward
    identity holds at every separated configuration, and the spin rows
    satisfy the <math|<with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)>> bracket
    relations.
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    The compact stress packet gives the raw collinear identity
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|K>*<around|(|p,Y|)>=0>. Dividing the commutation
    relation of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ward-local-response-laws>(i) by
    <math|c> and using <math|p=c*V> gives <eqref|eq:ward-commutation> for
    <math|<around|(|V,<wide|Y|~>|)>>. Parts (ii)\U(v) of that lemma, divided
    by <math|c>, give the inclusion, unit, reality and signed covariance
    rules for <math|<wide|Y|~>> used in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ward-brackets>.
    The nonvanishing of <math|c> is proved above from
    <eqref|eq:ward-translation-readout>. By the common scalar normalization,
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|K>*<around|(|p,Y|)>=c*\<cal-A\><rsub|K>*<around|(|V,<wide|Y|~>|)>>,
    so the genuine derivative and normalized spin action satisfy the
    collinear hypothesis of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ward-global-propagation>.
    That theorem extends the rotational identity to all of
    <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>>. The finite-row bridges produce the
    selected scale data and transfer erasure. Apply
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ward-brackets> to that same selected family.
    Each use is for a fixed grade/configuration; the finite maxima are taken
    before the scale threshold, so the argument does not interchange a
    pointwise limit with an uncountable family of tests.
  </proof>

  The cove, line, mass, point and exterior prices used by the stress packet
  are the exact inputs of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-ward-packet-prices>;
  their exponent composition and the <math|-101+100=-1> normalization are
  proved above. The common selected curve, finite observer/jet convergence
  and scalar normalization are supplied by
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-stress-normalization>. For the
  Maxwell reference table, Theorems<nbsp><reference|thm:free-quiet-classification>
  and<nbsp><reference|thm:free-collinear-scenes> supply the quiet-charge
  classifier and its finite collinear tests at grades above their common
  threshold. Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:free-internal-erasure> supplies the
  internal-map erasure input at the stated covariance, reality, unit and
  filtration hypotheses. Each fixed grade and finite observer family can use
  its own comparison scale; the scalar is common across grades by inclusion
  and nonvanishing, as proved above. No scale threshold uniform over all
  grades is needed. These free inputs are distinct from the packet-price
  bounds and from the geometric line-to-configuration argument.

  <subsection|Calibrating the physical rotation
  action><label|sec:physical-angular-actions>

  We construct the rotation action on the physical field spaces from the
  actual angular Ward maps. Equal dimensions of the physical and reference
  spaces would not supply this action. The essential step is to correct an
  approximate intertwiner while preserving the real structure and signed
  coordinate symmetries.

  Write <math|E<rsub|d>> and <math|E<rsub|d><rsup|0>> for the
  finite-dimensional complex physical and reference field spaces at grade cap
  <math|d>. Retain the actual derivative maps <math|\<partial\>>, the
  conserved stress <math|J>, its translation charges <math|p>, and its
  angular maps <math|Y>. The physical Ward propagation argument supplies one
  real <math|c\<ne\>0> such that <math|p=c*\<partial\>> at every cap. Put
  <math|D=c<rsup|-1>*Y>. Its output also supplies the global angular Ward
  identity for <math|D>, the Lie-bracket identity

  <\equation*>
    <around|[|D<rsub|d><around|(|A|)>,D<rsub|d><around|(|B|)>|]>=D<rsub|d><around|(|<around|[|A,B|]>|)>,
  </equation*>

  and compatibility with conjugation, signed motions and inclusions. These
  are the inputs from the physical shell argument, distinct from the
  reference Maxwell Ward calculation.

  On the reference spaces keep the fixed continuous rotation representation
  <math|R<rsub|d><rsup|0>> and its infinitesimal maps <math|C<rsub|d>>. Its
  reference stress has Ward coefficient <math|\<kappa\>\<ne\>0>, with the
  fixed value <math|-1> in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:free-maxwell-ward-flux>.
  The reference action and its Lorentz companion are the ones attached to
  that same free hierarchy. We do not replace either representation when
  calibrating the physical one.

  <subsubsection|The scalar normalization on a common comparison sequence>

  Fix <math|d>. The finite-request comparison gives, on one sequence,
  complex-linear isomorphisms <math|U<rsub|n,e>:E<rsub|e><rsup|0>\<to\>E<rsub|e>>,
  radii <math|r<rsub|n>\<gtr\>0> and stress normalizers
  <math|\<xi\><rsub|n>\<gtr\>0>. Choose it sufficiently rich to include the
  derivative test at cap <math|4>, the translation-charge test there, the
  angular tests at cap <math|d>, and the real/signed compatibility at
  <math|d>. One can do this by taking the maximum of the finitely many
  requested caps, arities, derivative orders and compact radii before
  invoking the comparison. This is the common finite-request construction of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-stress-normalization>; the
  shell-charge limits are <eqref|eq:ward-translation-readout> and
  <eqref|eq:ward-rotation-readout> of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ward-readout-limits>.

  Transport both source and target of the derivative maps through these same
  <math|U<rsub|n,e>>. Denote the transported cap-<math|4> derivative array,
  multiplied by <math|r<rsub|n>>, by <math|A<rsub|n>>. The two limits just
  specified say

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|n>\<longrightarrow\>A<rsup|0>\<ne\>0,<space|2em><around|(|c/\<xi\><rsub|n>|)>*A<rsub|n>\<longrightarrow\>\<kappa\>*A<rsup|0>.
  </equation*>

  The nonzero derivative array is the fixed reference test. Choose a real
  linear functional <math|\<lambda\>> on the finite-dimensional array space
  with <math|\<lambda\><around|(|A<rsup|0>|)>\<ne\>0>. Then
  <math|\<lambda\><around|(|A<rsub|n>|)>\<ne\>0> eventually, and division
  gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:angular-scalar-ratio><frac|c|\<xi\><rsub|n>>=<frac|\<lambda\>*<around|(|<around|(|c/\<xi\><rsub|n>|)>*A<rsub|n>|)>|\<lambda\><around|(|A<rsub|n>|)>>\<longrightarrow\>\<kappa\>.
  </equation>

  In particular no unproved lower bound on <math|\<xi\><rsub|n>> is required.
  The angular comparison <eqref|eq:ward-rotation-readout> is

  <\equation*>
    \<xi\><rsub|n><rsup|-1>*U<rsub|n,d><rsup|-1>*Y<rsub|d><around|(|A|)>*U<rsub|n,d>\<longrightarrow\>\<kappa\>*C<rsub|d><around|(|A|)>.
  </equation*>

  Multiplying by <math|<around|(|c/\<xi\><rsub|n>|)><rsup|-1>> and using
  <eqref|eq:angular-scalar-ratio> proves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:angular-transport-limit>B<rsub|n><around|(|A|)>\<assign\>U<rsub|n,d><rsup|-1>*D<rsub|d><around|(|A|)>*U<rsub|n,d>\<longrightarrow\>C<rsub|d><around|(|A|)>.
  </equation>

  Only finitely many generating planes are needed at this stage. The maps
  <math|U<rsub|n,d>> commute with conjugation and the signed actions. Their
  convergence is neither required nor asserted.

  <subsubsection|An exact intertwiner from nearby infinitesimal matrices>

  We give the finite-dimensional argument used with
  <eqref|eq:angular-transport-limit>. Let <math|E=E<rsub|d><rsup|0>> and
  write <math|K<rsub|a*b>=e<rsub|a>*e<rsub|b><rsup|<math-ss|T>>-e<rsub|b>*e<rsub|a><rsup|<math-ss|T>>>
  for the ordered plane generators, including <math|K<rsub|a*a>=0>. Index
  them by <math|i=<around|(|a,b|)>>. For every pair choose an axis <math|h>
  distinct from <math|a,b>. Direct multiplication gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|[|K<rsub|a*h>,K<rsub|h*b>|]>=K<rsub|a*b>.
  </equation*>

  Thus the corresponding matrices <math|B<rsub|n,i>> and <math|C<rsub|i>>
  obey a fixed family of perfect-commutator identities
  <math|B<rsub|n,i>=<around|[|B<rsub|n,l<around|(|i|)>>,B<rsub|n,r<around|(|i|)>>|]>>,
  and similarly for <math|C<rsub|i>>. On <math|<math-up|End><rsub|\<bbb-C\>><around|(|E|)>>
  put

  <\equation*>
    T<rsub|n,i><around|(|F|)>=B<rsub|n,i>*F-F*C<rsub|i>,<space|2em>T<rsub|i><around|(|F|)>=C<rsub|i>*F-F*C<rsub|i>,<space|2em>L<rsub|n>=<big|sum><rsub|i>T<rsub|n,i><rsup|2>,<space|1em>L=<big|sum><rsub|i>T<rsub|i><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  Left and right multiplication commute, so the <math|T<rsub|n,i>> satisfy
  the same perfect identities. In fact the full Lie-bracket relations give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:angular-casimir-commutes><around|[|L<rsub|n>,T<rsub|n,i>|]>=0.
  </equation>

  For completeness, the plane bracket formula is

  <\equation*>
    <around|[|K<rsub|a*b>,K<rsub|i*j>|]>=\<delta\><rsub|b*i>*K<rsub|a*j>-\<delta\><rsub|b*j>*K<rsub|a*i>+\<delta\><rsub|a*i>*K<rsub|j*b>-\<delta\><rsub|a*j>*K<rsub|i*b>.
  </equation*>

  Insert this in <math|<around|[|T<rsub|a*b><rsup|2>,T<rsub|i*j>|]>=T<rsub|a*b>*<around|[|T<rsub|a*b>,T<rsub|i*j>|]>+<around|[|T<rsub|a*b>,T<rsub|i*j>|]>*T<rsub|a*b>>
  and sum over the ordered pairs <math|<around|(|a,b|)>>. The four resulting
  sums cancel in pairs after exchanging their repeated indices and using
  <math|T<rsub|b*a>=-T<rsub|a*b>>. This proves
  <eqref|eq:angular-casimir-commutes> for the relative representation too.

  First construct a real polynomial <math|q> such that
  <math|P=q<around|(|L|)>> is a projection, <math|P<around|(|I|)>=I>, and
  <math|T<rsub|i>*P=0> for every <math|i>. Average any positive-definite real
  inner product on <math|<math-up|End><rsub|\<bbb-C\>><around|(|E|)>> under
  <math|F\<mapsto\>R<rsub|d><rsup|0><around|(|K|)>*F*R<rsub|d><rsup|0><around|(|K|)><rsup|-1>>,
  using probability Haar measure on the compact group <math|O<around|(|4|)>>.
  The average is positive definite: for a nonzero vector the integrand is
  positive at the identity and, by continuity, on a nonempty open set of
  positive Haar measure. It is invariant by Haar translation. Differentiating
  that invariance makes each <math|T<rsub|i>> skew for the averaged form
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<cdummy\>,\<cdummy\>|\<rangle\>><rsub|0>>.
  Consequently

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<langle\>|L*F,F|\<rangle\>><rsub|0>=-<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\<langle\>|T<rsub|i>*F,T<rsub|i>*F|\<rangle\>><rsub|0>,<space|2em>ker
    L=<big|cap><rsub|i>ker T<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  Moreover <math|L<rsup|2>*F=0> implies <math|L*F=0>: the displayed kernel
  identity gives <math|T<rsub|i>*L*F=0>, and skewness then gives
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|L*F,L*F|\<rangle\>><rsub|0>=<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\<langle\>|T<rsub|i><rsup|2>*F,L*F|\<rangle\>><rsub|0>=0>.

  Cayley\UHamilton provides a nonzero polynomial annihilating <math|L>.
  Starting with <math|L*p<around|(|L|)>=0>, divide out factors of the
  variable as long as <math|p<around|(|0|)>=0>. If
  <math|p<around|(|X|)>=X*h<around|(|X|)>>, then
  <math|L<rsup|2>*h<around|(|L|)>=0> implies <math|L*h<around|(|L|)>=0> by
  the preceding implication, applied to every vector. Eventually a polynomial
  with nonzero constant term remains. Normalize it to obtain
  <math|q<around|(|0|)>=1> and <math|L*q<around|(|L|)>=0>. Since
  <math|q<around|(|L|)>> is the identity on <math|ker L>, it is a projection
  onto that kernel. The identity matrix belongs to this kernel, so
  <math|P<around|(|I|)>=I>. This proof also covers <math|E=<around|{|0|}>>.

  Let <math|\<cal-F\>> be the closed real vector space of endomorphisms of
  <math|E> commuting with the reference signed actions and conjugation. The
  identity belongs to <math|\<cal-F\>>. Every <math|L<rsub|n>> preserves
  <math|\<cal-F\>>. Indeed, a signed coordinate transformation permutes the
  ordered planes and multiplies each by a sign. Both <math|B<rsub|n,i>> and
  <math|C<rsub|i>> acquire the same sign, so it cancels in
  <math|T<rsub|n,i><rsup|2>> and the sum is unchanged. Conjugation commutes
  with both matrix families; since the polynomial coefficients are real, it
  commutes with all subsequent polynomial operations too. It follows that
  <math|P<rsub|n>=q<around|(|L<rsub|n>|)>> preserves <math|\<cal-F\>>,
  commutes with every <math|T<rsub|n,i>>, and converges to <math|P>.

  The <math|P<rsub|n>> need not be projections. Correct them by the
  polynomial iteration

  <\equation*>
    Z<rsub|j+1>=3*Z<rsub|j><rsup|2>-2*Z<rsub|j><rsup|3>,<space|2em>Z<rsub|0>=P<rsub|n>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|e<around|(|Z|)>=Z<rsup|2>-Z> the exact identities are

  <\equation*>
    Z<rsub|j+1>-Z<rsub|j>=<around|(|I-2*Z<rsub|j>|)>*e<around|(|Z<rsub|j>|)>,<space|2em>e<around|(|Z<rsub|j+1>|)>=<around|(|4*e<around|(|Z<rsub|j>|)>-3*I|)>*e<around|(|Z<rsub|j>|)><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  Here is a uniform convergence estimate. Choose
  <math|A\<gtr\><around|\<\|\|\>|P|\<\|\|\>>> and
  <math|C\<gtr\><around|\<\|\|\>|I|\<\|\|\>>+2*A>. For sufficiently large
  <math|n>, put <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|n>=<around|\<\|\|\>|e<around|(|P<rsub|n>|)>|\<\|\|\>>>;
  then

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|n>|\<\|\|\>>+2*C*\<varepsilon\><rsub|n>\<leqslant\>A,<space|2em><around|(|4*\<varepsilon\><rsub|n>+3<around|\<\|\|\>|I|\<\|\|\>>|)>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|n>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>.
  </equation*>

  Induction using the two identities gives
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|e<around|(|Z<rsub|j>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|-j>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|n>>
  and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsub|j+1>-Z<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*2<rsup|-j>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|n>>.
  Their sum keeps <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>A>,
  closing the induction. Thus <math|Z<rsub|j>\<to\>Q<rsub|n>>, with
  <math|Q<rsub|n><rsup|2>=Q<rsub|n>> and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|Q<rsub|n>-P<rsub|n>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*C*\<varepsilon\><rsub|n>>.
  In particular <math|Q<rsub|n>\<to\>P>. Closedness preserves the
  constraints: <math|Q<rsub|n>> preserves <math|\<cal-F\>> and commutes with
  all <math|T<rsub|n,i>>.

  The restricted operators <math|T<rsub|n,i>*Q<rsub|n>> satisfy the same
  perfect identities, since <math|Q<rsub|n>> is idempotent and commutes with
  them. They converge to <math|T<rsub|i>*P=0>. Let
  <math|m<rsub|n>=max<rsub|i><around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsub|n,i>*Q<rsub|n>|\<\|\|\>>>.
  The perfect identities give <math|m<rsub|n>\<leqslant\>2*m<rsub|n><rsup|2>>.
  Eventually <math|m<rsub|n>\<less\>1/2>, hence <math|m<rsub|n>=0>. Therefore

  <\equation*>
    T<rsub|n,i>*Q<rsub|n>=0<space|1em><text|for every >i.
  </equation*>

  Apply <math|Q<rsub|n>> to the identity matrix and set
  <math|F<rsub|n>=Q<rsub|n><around|(|I|)>>. Then
  <math|F<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-F\>>, <math|F<rsub|n>\<to\>I>, and
  <math|B<rsub|n,i>*F<rsub|n>=F<rsub|n>*C<rsub|i>>. For one sufficiently late
  <math|n>, <math|F<rsub|n>> is invertible by the Neumann series. Thus

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:angular-exact-intertwiner>Q<rsub|d>=U<rsub|n,d>*F<rsub|n>:E<rsub|d><rsup|0>\<longrightarrow\>E<rsub|d>
  </equation>

  is an isomorphism preserving reality and signed motions, and
  <math|D<rsub|d><around|(|A|)>*Q<rsub|d>=Q<rsub|d>*C<rsub|d><around|(|A|)>>
  for every skew matrix <math|A>. To pass from planes to all <math|A>, use
  the exact spanning identity <math|A=<frac|1|2>*<big|sum><rsub|a,b>A<rsub|a*b>*K<rsub|a*b>>.
  No complete reducibility or unitarity of the deformed matrices was assumed.

  <subsubsection|The physical action and its compatibility>

  Choose one map <eqref|eq:angular-exact-intertwiner> in each cap and define

  <\equation*>
    R<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>=Q<rsub|d>*R<rsub|d><rsup|0><around|(|K|)>*Q<rsub|d><rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  It is a continuous representation of all of <math|O<around|(|4|)>>,
  preserves the real structure, agrees with the prescribed signed action, and
  has derivative <math|D<rsub|d><around|(|A|)>> along <math|exp
  <around|(|t*A|)>> at zero. The group law moves this derivative to every
  <math|t>. The choices of <math|Q<rsub|d>> need not commute with inclusions.

  For arbitrary distinct points and field labels, differentiate

  <\equation*>
    t\<longmapsto\>S*<around|(|R<rsub|d<rsub|1>><around|(|e<rsup|t*A>|)>*v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,R<rsub|d<rsub|n>><around|(|e<rsup|t*A>|)>*v<rsub|n>;e<rsup|t*A>*x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,e<rsup|t*A>*x<rsub|n>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Joint differentiability follows by expanding the labels in finite bases:
  the expression is a finite sum of smooth position coefficients times
  multilinear coordinate products. The chain rule gives the global angular
  Ward expression for <math|D>, which is zero. Thus the function is constant.
  Simultaneous covariance holds for every one-parameter rotation and also for
  signed transformations. The set of transformations for which it holds is a
  subgroup, by composition and inversion. Plane rotations generate
  <math|S*O<around|(|4|)>>, and a signed reflection supplies its other
  component, as in the group-generation argument of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:lorentz-covariance>. Hence covariance holds
  for all <math|O<around|(|4|)>>.

  The unit, inclusions and derivatives are now compatible with this actual
  action. For the unit, test <math|R<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>*1> against any
  separated spectator word, move all labels and positions back by
  <math|K<rsup|-1>>, and delete the unit using the original unit law. Move
  the remaining word forward again. The result is the test of <math|1>, so
  faithfulness of the physical quotient gives
  <math|R<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>*1=1>. For an inclusion
  <math|\<iota\><rsub|d*e>>, the identical argument uses the original
  inclusion identity at the moved configuration and yields

  <\equation*>
    R<rsub|e><around|(|K|)>*\<iota\><rsub|d*e>=\<iota\><rsub|d*e>*R<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>.
  </equation*>

  Finally differentiate the covariance identity in the coordinate of the head
  insertion, keeping arbitrary spectators. The ordinary linear chain rule and
  the original derivative identity give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:angular-derivative-vector>R<rsub|d+1><around|(|K|)>*\<partial\><rsub|a>*v=<big|sum><rsub|i>K<rsub|i*a>*\<partial\><rsub|i>*<around|(|R<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>*v|)>.
  </equation>

  Again all-spectator faithfulness converts equality of correlations into an
  identity of field classes. This proves the full physical Euclidean action
  laws rather than merely laws at each isolated cap.

  <subsubsection|Normalizing and averaging the stress>

  First replace <math|J> by <math|J<rsub|0>=c<rsup|-1>*J>. Linearity of the
  collar flux makes its translation charge exactly <math|\<partial\>> and its
  angular charge exactly <math|D>. The tensor remains real, symmetric and
  divergence-free. To make it transform tensorially under the action just
  constructed, define

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|\<cal-R\><rsub|K>*J<rsub|0>|)><rsub|a*b>=<big|sum><rsub|i,j>K<rsub|a*i>*K<rsub|b*j>*R<rsub|4><around|(|K|)><around|(|J<rsub|0,i*j>|)>,<space|2em><wide|J|^>=<big|int><rsub|O<around|(|4|)>>\<cal-R\><rsub|K>*J<rsub|0>*<space|0.17em>d*K.
  </equation*>

  This integral is in the finite-dimensional space <math|E<rsub|4><rsup|16>>,
  with probability Haar measure. Its integrand is continuous. The group law
  gives <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|K>*\<cal-R\><rsub|L>=\<cal-R\><rsub|K*L>>, so
  Haar invariance implies <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|K>*<wide|J|^>=<wide|J|^>> and
  hence

  <\equation*>
    R<rsub|4><around|(|K|)>*<wide|J|^><rsub|i*j>=<big|sum><rsub|a,b>K<rsub|a*i>*K<rsub|b*j>*<wide|J|^><rsub|a*b>.
  </equation*>

  The average is real and symmetric. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:angular-derivative-vector>
  and orthogonality show

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|a>*<around|(|\<cal-R\><rsub|K>*J<rsub|0>|)><rsub|a*b>=<big|sum><rsub|j>K<rsub|b*j>*R<rsub|5><around|(|K|)><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i>\<partial\><rsub|i>*J<rsub|0,i*j>|)>=0.
  </equation*>

  The derivative maps are continuous linear maps of finite-dimensional spaces
  and therefore commute with the integral. Thus <math|<wide|J|^>> is
  conserved.

  We verify that averaging retains both exact Ward normalizations. For
  vectors <math|u,v> write <math|J*<around|[|u,v|]>=<big|sum><rsub|a,b>u<rsub|a>*v<rsub|b>*J<rsub|a*b>>.
  Then

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|\<cal-R\><rsub|K>*J|)><around|[|u,v|]>=R<rsub|4><around|(|K|)>*J*<around|[|K<rsup|-1>*u,K<rsup|-1>*v|]>.
  </equation*>

  In a collar flux with cutoff <math|\<chi\>> and vector field <math|W>,
  change variables <math|y=K*z>, moving the external points and labels by
  <math|K<rsup|-1>>. Orthogonal maps preserve volume, and
  <math|\<nabla\>*<around|(|\<chi\>\<circ\>K|)>=K<rsup|-1>*<around|(|\<nabla\>*\<chi\>|)>\<circ\>K>.
  The transformed vector field is <math|K<rsup|-1>*W*<around|(|K*z|)>>. Thus
  the flux of <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|K>*J<rsub|0>> equals the flux of
  <math|J<rsub|0>> in this transformed collar. The compact support of
  <math|\<nabla\>*\<chi\>> avoids all insertion points, so the integrands are
  smooth there and the change of variables is legitimate.

  For a translation <math|W=e<rsub|a>>, the transformed charge is the linear
  combination with coefficients <math|<around|(|K<rsup|-1>*e<rsub|a>|)><rsub|i>>.
  Its Ward identity, <eqref|eq:angular-derivative-vector> and covariance of
  the external correlation return exactly the original derivative charge
  <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>>. For <math|W<around|(|y|)>=A*<around|(|y-x<rsub|0>|)>>
  the transformed field is <math|<around|(|K<rsup|-1>*A*K|)>*<around|(|z-K<rsup|-1>*x<rsub|0>|)>>.
  Differentiating the representation identity
  <math|R<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>*R<rsub|d><around|(|e<rsup|t*A>|)>*R<rsub|d><around|(|K|)><rsup|-1>=R<rsub|d><around|(|e<rsup|t*K*A*K<rsup|-1>>|)>>
  gives

  <\equation*>
    R<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>*D<rsub|d><around|(|A|)>=D<rsub|d>*<around|(|K*A*K<rsup|-1>|)>*R<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>.
  </equation*>

  Its use in the transformed Ward identity returns exactly
  <math|D<rsub|d><around|(|A|)>>. This also holds for orientation-reversing
  <math|K>; volume measure contributes <math|<around|\||det K|\|>=1>, not a
  determinant sign. Each fixed collar flux is a continuous linear functional
  of the stress labels. It therefore passes through the finite-dimensional
  Haar integral, and the constant target Ward expression integrates to
  itself. Both charges of <math|<wide|J|^>> are consequently normalized as
  required.

  <subsubsection|Keeping the same maps at the Lorentz step>

  The maps <math|Q<rsub|d>> in <eqref|eq:angular-exact-intertwiner>, with the
  reference Euclidean and Lorentz actions already fixed, give precisely the
  calibrated actions of Input<nbsp><reference|input:calibrated-actions>.
  Their reality and signed compatibility were preserved by the polynomial
  correction; their Euclidean actions now commute with inclusions. Define the
  physical Lorentz action at each cap by <math|Q<rsub|d>*T<rsub|d><rsup|0>*Q<rsub|d><rsup|-1>>
  with these very maps. Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:lorentz-label-descent>
  proves its cross-cap compatibility from the boost differential equation,
  and Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:lorentz-covariance> supplies the
  boundary covariance argument. There is no new choice of a Lorentz
  intertwiner. The hierarchy, its derivative maps and its field quotients
  have remained the same through this construction.

  <subsection|The same stress laws in every character
  phase><label|sec:phase-ward-inheritance>

  The character construction evaluates an operator for a whole word. This
  allows us to transfer a Ward identity with spectators, even though the
  identity includes an integral and is not a product of individual character
  values. We prove the required operator identity before evaluating it.

  Retain the unselected hierarchy <math|S>, its Euclidean Hilbert space
  <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|E>>, ground subspace <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|0>>,
  orthogonal projection <math|P<rsub|0>>, and the commutative compression
  algebra <math|\<cal-A\>> of Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-phases>. Write
  <math|A<rsub|D><around|(|w|)>> for its bounded insertion operator in a time
  slab of width <math|D\<gtr\>0>. The norm smoothness and derivative
  identities are those of Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-slab-regularity>; the
  whole-word character hierarchies are those of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-regularity>. Retain the same physical
  field spaces, derivative maps <math|\<partial\><rsub|b>>, rotation
  generators <math|\<cal-R\><around|(|M|)>> and normalized stress tensor
  <math|J<rsub|a*b>> constructed in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-angular-actions>.
  In this section its unselected translation and rotation Ward identities are
  hypotheses already supplied by that construction. No new normalization of
  <math|J> is made in a phase.

  Fix a distinguished insertion <math|B> at <math|x<rsub|0>>, spectator
  fields <math|B<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,B<rsub|n>> at distinct points
  <math|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>>, and a smooth real cutoff
  <math|\<chi\>> of compact support. Require that <math|\<chi\>=1> on a
  neighborhood of <math|x<rsub|0>> and that
  <math|x<rsub|j>\<nin\><math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<chi\>> for
  <math|j\<gtr\>0>. All <math|n+1> points are distinct. Put

  <\equation*>
    S<rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|B;x<rsub|0>\<mid\>\<b-B\>,\<b-x\>|)>=S<rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|B,B<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,B<rsub|n>;x<rsub|0>,x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<xi\>\<in\>\<Xi\>> is an arbitrary character. The corresponding
  notation with an extra field before <math|B> includes its extra position
  before <math|x<rsub|0>>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Inheritance of the shell identities><label|prop:phase-ward-inheritance>For
    every character <math|\<xi\>>, every coordinate <math|b>, and every real
    skew-symmetric matrix <math|M>, one has

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|S<rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|b>*B;x<rsub|0>\<mid\>\<b-B\>,\<b-x\>|)>>|<cell|=-<big|sum><rsub|a><big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\><around|(|y|)>*S<rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|J<rsub|a*b>,B;y,x<rsub|0>\<mid\>\<b-B\>,\<b-x\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y,<eq-number><label|eq:phase-translation-flux>>>|<row|<cell|S<rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<cal-R\><around|(|M|)>*B;x<rsub|0>\<mid\>\<b-B\>,\<b-x\>|)>>|<cell|=-<big|sum><rsub|a,b><big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\><around|(|y|)>*<around|[|M*<around|(|y-x<rsub|0>|)>|]><rsub|b>*S<rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|J<rsub|a*b>,B;y,x<rsub|0>\<mid\>\<b-B\>,\<b-x\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y.<eq-number><label|eq:phase-rotation-flux>>>>>
    </align>

    Both integrals are absolutely convergent. The same cutoff and the same
    field maps work for every character, including the selected one.
  </proposition>

  <subsubsection|A continuous evaluator on the uncompressed operators>

  A character is continuous and complex linear on <math|\<cal-A\>>. By the
  complex Hahn\UBanach theorem, extend it to a bounded complex-linear
  functional <math|<wide|\<xi\>|~>> on <math|\<cal-B\><around|(|\<cal-H\><rsub|0>|)>>,
  and set

  <\equation*>
    \<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|T|)>=<wide|\<xi\>|~>*<around|(|P<rsub|0>*T\|<rsub|\<cal-H\><rsub|0>>|)>,<space|2em>T\<in\>\<cal-B\><around|(|\<cal-H\><rsub|E>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Compression is bounded linear, so <math|\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>> is bounded
  linear. We use no positivity or multiplicativity of its extension outside
  <math|\<cal-A\>>. For a separated word <math|w> placed strictly inside a
  slab by a translation <math|s>, the whole-word definition gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:phase-fixed-evaluator>S<rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|w|)>=\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|A<rsub|D>*<around|(|w+s|)>|)>.
  </equation>

  The evaluator is fixed while positions, labels, and the slab placement
  vary. In particular it may be passed through a norm-convergent integral.

  Choose <math|s> and <math|D> so that both the finite configuration and the
  <em|whole> compact support of <math|\<chi\>>, after translation by
  <math|s>, lie strictly between time <math|0> and time <math|D>. To see that
  this is possible, bound their time coordinates between <math|\<alpha\>> and
  <math|\<beta\>>, translate by <math|<around|(|1-\<alpha\>|)>*e<rsub|0>>,
  and take <math|D\<gtr\>\<beta\>-\<alpha\>+1>. This leaves a positive margin
  at each slab boundary. For a field tuple <math|v> and a configuration
  <math|z> in this fixed slab, write

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|v><around|(|z|)>=A<rsub|D>*<around|(|v;z+s|)>.
  </equation*>

  Representatives in the original label spaces can be fixed once for the
  finitely many fields involved. The slab identities and equality of the
  phase null spaces ensure that the resulting correlation values are the ones
  on the physical field spaces. Norm regularity gives continuity of
  <math|K<rsub|v>> on separated configurations in this slab.

  <subsubsection|Integrating the shell before taking a character>

  Let <math|u:\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>\<to\>\<bbb-C\>> be continuous; the cases
  needed are <math|u=1> and <math|u<around|(|y|)>=<around|[|M*<around|(|y-x<rsub|0>|)>|]><rsub|b>>.
  Put

  <\equation*>
    w<rsub|a><around|(|y|)>=\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\><around|(|y|)>*u<around|(|y|)>.
  </equation*>

  Its support is compact and contained in
  <math|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<chi\>>. Moreover it misses a
  neighborhood of every <math|x<rsub|j>>: at <math|x<rsub|0>> the cutoff is
  locally constant, and at the spectator points it is locally zero.
  Consequently the operator-valued function

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|a*b,u><around|(|y|)>=w<rsub|a><around|(|y|)>*K<rsub|<around|(|J<rsub|a*b>,B,\<b-B\>|)>>*<around|(|y,x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)>
  </equation*>

  is continuous and compactly supported when defined to be zero in the
  neighborhoods where <math|w<rsub|a>> vanishes. On its support the inserted
  point is separated from all fixed points and remains strictly inside the
  fixed slab, so norm continuity follows from that of <math|K>. At a point
  outside the support of <math|w<rsub|a>>, the entire product is locally
  zero, even if the unweighted correlation there would have a collision. This
  proves continuity on all of <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>.

  A continuous compactly supported Banach-valued function has bounded norm,
  separable range on its compact support, and integrable norm. Thus the
  Bochner integral

  <\equation*>
    U<rsub|a*b><around|(|u|)>=<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>F<rsub|a*b,u><around|(|y|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y<space|1em><text|in
    >\<cal-B\><around|(|\<cal-H\><rsub|E>|)>
  </equation*>

  exists. Every bounded linear functional <math|\<Lambda\>> commutes with
  this integral. One may verify this first for simple functions and then use
  norm-integrable approximation and the bound
  <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><around|(|T|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|\<Lambda\>|\<\|\|\>><space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|T|\<\|\|\>>>.
  Applied to <eqref|eq:phase-fixed-evaluator>, it gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:phase-shell-evaluation>\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|U<rsub|a*b><around|(|u|)>|)>=<big|int>w<rsub|a><around|(|y|)>*S<rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|J<rsub|a*b>,B;y,x<rsub|0>\<mid\>\<b-B\>,\<b-x\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y.
  </equation>

  It also proves absolute integrability of the scalar integrand. Values at
  collisions have no bearing on this equality, since the weight is zero in a
  neighborhood of each possible collision.

  <subsubsection|Testing the operator identity against exterior words>

  Let <math|l,r> be arbitrary positive-time word vectors in
  <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|E>>. The slab matrix-element identity expresses
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|<around|[|l|]>,K<rsub|v><around|(|z|)><around|[|r|]>|\<rangle\>>>
  as an unselected correlation containing <math|v> at <math|z>, together with
  a reflected copy of <math|l> below the slab and a translated copy of
  <math|r> above it. Translate all points back by <math|-s>. Permutation
  symmetry puts the inserted fields first; translation invariance then gives
  precisely this correlation in the original coordinates. The two extra word
  configurations are internally separated and lie on opposite sides of the
  slab. They therefore introduce neither collisions with one another nor
  collisions with its interior insertions.

  Crucially, these extra spectators lie outside the entire support of
  <math|\<chi\>>, since that support was placed strictly inside the slab.
  Thus <math|\<chi\>> is still an admissible cutoff for the unselected Ward
  identity with these additional spectators. Commuting the continuous
  matrix-element functional with each Bochner integral, that Ward identity
  proves

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\<langle\>|<around|[|l|]>,-<big|sum><rsub|a>U<rsub|a*b><around|(|1|)><around|[|r|]>|\<rangle\>>>|<cell|=<around|\<langle\>|<around|[|l|]>,K<rsub|<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|b>*B,\<b-B\>|)>>*<around|(|x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)><around|[|r|]>|\<rangle\>>,>>|<row|<cell|<around*|\<nobracket\>|<around*|\<langle\>|<around|[|l|]>,-<big|sum><rsub|a,b>U<rsub|a*b>*<around|(|<around|[|M|(><space|0.17em>\<cdot\>-x<rsub|0>|)>|]><rsub|b>)<around|[|r|]>|\<rangle\>>>|<cell|=<around|\<langle\>|<around|[|l|]>,K<rsub|<around|(|\<cal-R\><around|(|M|)>*B,\<b-B\>|)>>*<around|(|x<rsub|0>,\<b-x\>|)><around|[|r|]>|\<rangle\>>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Positive-time word vectors span a dense subspace. If a bounded operator has
  zero matrix elements on this span, fix its right argument there and use
  density on the left to see that its value is zero. Continuity then extends
  the vanishing to every right argument. Hence the two matrix-element
  equalities are equalities of bounded operators.

  Apply <math|\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>> to these operator equalities. Its
  linearity, the whole-word evaluation formula, and
  <eqref|eq:phase-shell-evaluation> give <eqref|eq:phase-translation-flux>
  and <eqref|eq:phase-rotation-flux>. This proves the proposition. Notice
  that no limiting argument over characters and no almost-everywhere
  exceptional set occurred.

  <subsubsection|The mixed and character continuations>

  The continuation construction applies to the mixed table and to every
  character, with the same field labels and calibrated rotation action. For
  the mixed table use its original Hilbert space, bounded slabs and slot
  identities. For each character use the Hilbert space and slab operators of
  Corollary<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-dynamics> and
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-slab-bounds>. Their norm bounds,
  derivative identities and field inclusions have already been verified;
  equality of null spaces is supplied by Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-fields>.
  Reflection transport supplies the same orthogonal field covariance. The
  common label action of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:label-descent>
  consequently applies to the mixed table and to every component.

  Apply Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:complex-heat>\U<reference|sec:analytic-boundary>
  to each of these sets of Hilbert-space data. The continued functions are
  built from each table's own slab matrix elements, so their imaginary-time
  restrictions recover that table, and their tempered boundary distributions
  are its own boundary values. The Fourier-support, Lorentz-covariance,
  full-tube and locality arguments use these same functions in each
  application. None of those arguments uses one-dimensionality of the
  time-invariant subspace. The word-domain construction and heat-vector
  isometry likewise apply to each table.

  Theorem<nbsp><reference|ex-pure-ground> first gives vacuum uniqueness
  almost everywhere through the isometric embedding and translation
  intertwining of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:spectral-measure>. The
  uniform low-energy argument in Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-every-character-vacuum>
  then proves vacuum uniqueness for every reconstructed character. It uses
  locality and the forward spectrum already obtained, not a prior uniqueness
  assumption. No such uniqueness is asserted for the mixed theory. All these
  continuations therefore use the original field quotient; the selected
  character and its stress normalization are unchanged.

  <section|The trace asymptotic and the spectral test>

  <subsection|The tilt cap and the logarithmic zoom
  bound><label|sec:ex-lattice-trace-clock-cap>

  The trace sections below compare scales along the selected run through the
  clock rate <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>>. Two elementary facts about the
  cap on the chart zoom are used there: the cap has a ticket of rate zero
  (Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-trace-small-means>), and the
  logarithm of the zoom factor is at most <math|<around|(|log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|)><rsup|48>> at the frame level <math|j> (the
  clock paragraph of the scribe producer,
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-scribe-producer>). This
  section defines the cap and proves both facts. They use only the definition
  of the clock; no analytic estimate enters.

  <em|The clock.> The selected run has rates

  <\equation*>
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+\<kappa\>*log
    <around|(|<math-ss|a><rsub|j><rsup|-1>|)>,<space|2em>j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|<math-ss|a><rsub|j>\<gtr\>0> is the field scale at level
  <math|j> and <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>,\<kappa\>> are fixed constants
  with <math|\<kappa\>\<gtr\>0>. We use only two properties of these numbers:

  <\equation>
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<gtr\>1<space|1em><text|for every
    >j,<space|2em><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<longrightarrow\>\<infty\>*<space|1em><around|(|j\<to\>\<infty\>|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-clock-cap-rate>
  </equation>

  Put

  <\equation*>
    L<rsub|j>=log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<gtr\>0,<space|2em>u<rsub|j>=log
    L<rsub|j>=log log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>.
  </equation*>

  By <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-clock-cap-rate>, <math|L<rsub|j>\<to\>\<infty\>>,
  hence <math|u<rsub|j>\<to\>\<infty\>>. The clock monitor of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:rank-root-normalization> is

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-M\><rsub|j>=exp <around*|(|u<rsub|j><rsup|2>|)>\<geqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  For a real exponent <math|c> the <em|magnified monitor> is
  <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|j><rsup|<space|0.17em>c>=exp
  <around|(|c*<space|0.17em>u<rsub|j><rsup|2>|)>>. Since
  <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1>, the map
  <math|c\<mapsto\>\<cal-M\><rsub|j><rsup|<space|0.17em>c>> is nondecreasing
  on <math|\<bbb-R\>>.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Tilt cap><label|def:ex-lattice-tiltcap>The <em|tilt cap> at level
    <math|j> is the magnified monitor at the exponent <math|u<rsub|j>>:

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|tiltcap><around|(|j|)>=\<cal-M\><rsub|j><rsup|<space|0.17em>u<rsub|j>>=exp
      <around*|(|u<rsub|j><rsup|3>|)>=exp <around*|(|<around|(|log log
      <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|)><rsup|3>|)>.
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  <em|Tickets.> A sequence <math|<around|(|f<rsub|j>|)><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>>
  of real numbers has a <em|ticket of rate> <math|\<rho\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> if
  for every <math|\<rho\><rprime|'>\<gtr\>\<rho\>> there is <math|j<rsub|0>>
  such that

  <\equation*>
    0\<leqslant\>f<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around*|(|\<rho\><rprime|'><around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|)><rsup|48>|)><space|2em><text|for all
    >j\<geqslant\>j<rsub|0>.
  </equation*>

  For a nonnegative sequence this is the notion used in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-trace-small-means>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Zero-rate ticket of the tilt cap><label|lem:ex-lattice-trace-tiltcap-ticket>

    <\enumerate>
      <item>For every <math|j> with <math|log
      <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1>,

      <\equation*>
        1\<leqslant\><math-up|tiltcap><around|(|j|)>\<leqslant\>exp
        <around*|(|<around|(|log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|)><rsup|3>|)>.
      </equation*>

      <item>For every integer <math|p\<geqslant\>0> the sequence
      <math|j\<mapsto\><math-up|tiltcap><around|(|j|)><rsup|p>> has a ticket
      of rate <math|0>. In particular <math|<math-up|tiltcap>> itself and its
      fifth power have tickets of rate <math|0>.
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    (1) Let <math|L<rsub|j>=log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1>. Then
    <math|u<rsub|j>=log L<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>0>, and the inequality <math|log
    x\<leqslant\>x-1\<less\>x> for <math|x\<gtr\>0> gives
    <math|u<rsub|j>\<less\>L<rsub|j>>. Hence
    <math|0\<leqslant\>u<rsub|j><rsup|3>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|j><rsup|3>>, and
    applying the increasing function <math|exp> gives <math|1\<leqslant\>exp
    <around|(|u<rsub|j><rsup|3>|)>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around|(|L<rsub|j><rsup|3>|)>>.

    (2) Fix <math|p\<geqslant\>0> and <math|\<rho\><rprime|'>\<gtr\>0>. Since
    <math|L<rsub|j>\<to\>\<infty\>>, there is <math|j<rsub|0>> such that for
    all <math|j\<geqslant\>j<rsub|0>>

    <\equation*>
      L<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1<space|2em><text|and><space|2em>L<rsub|j><rsup|45>\<geqslant\><frac|p|\<rho\><rprime|'>>.
    </equation*>

    For such <math|j>, part (1) gives

    <\equation*>
      1\<leqslant\><math-up|tiltcap><around|(|j|)><rsup|p>=exp
      <around*|(|p*<space|0.17em>u<rsub|j><rsup|3>|)>\<leqslant\>exp
      <around*|(|p*<space|0.17em>L<rsub|j><rsup|3>|)>\<leqslant\>exp
      <around*|(|\<rho\><rprime|'>*L<rsub|j><rsup|45>*L<rsub|j><rsup|3>|)>=exp
      <around*|(|\<rho\><rprime|'><around|(|log
      <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|)><rsup|48>|)>.
    </equation*>

    In particular <math|0\<leqslant\><math-up|tiltcap><around|(|j|)><rsup|p>>
    for <math|j\<geqslant\>j<rsub|0>>. As <math|\<rho\><rprime|'>\<gtr\>0>
    was arbitrary, <math|<math-up|tiltcap><rsup|p>> has a ticket of rate
    <math|0>.
  </proof>

  <em|The chart receipt.> Let <math|j<rsub|i>> be the frame level of the
  <math|i>th selected chart and <math|<math-ss|b><rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> its zoom
  factor, so that the chart radius is <math|r<rsub|i>=<math-ss|a><rsub|j<rsub|i>><math-ss|b><rsub|i>>.
  The selected chart is produced at an exact clock gap
  (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-firesky-gallery>): its zoom
  factor is a magnified monitor <math|<math-ss|b><rsub|i>=\<cal-M\><rsub|j<rsub|i>><rsup|<space|0.17em>c<rsub|i>>>
  whose base arc <math|c<rsub|i>> satisfies <math|5\<less\>c<rsub|i>> and is
  at most the permitted angular bound <math|log log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j<rsub|i>>=u<rsub|j<rsub|i>>>. Because
  <math|c\<mapsto\>\<cal-M\><rsub|j><rsup|<space|0.17em>c>> is nondecreasing
  and <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1>, these two inequalities give

  <\equation>
    1\<leqslant\>\<cal-M\><rsub|j<rsub|i>><rsup|<space|0.17em>5>\<leqslant\><math-ss|b><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<cal-M\><rsub|j<rsub|i>><rsup|<space|0.17em>u<rsub|j<rsub|i>>>=<math-up|tiltcap><around|(|j<rsub|i>|)><space|2em><text|for
    every >i.<label|eq:ex-lattice-clock-cap-receipt>
  </equation>

  This is the receipt <math|1\<leqslant\><math-ss|b><rsub|i>\<leqslant\><math-up|tiltcap><around|(|j<rsub|i>|)>>
  used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-mission-price> and
  the bound <math|0\<less\><math-ss|b><rsub|i>\<leqslant\><math-up|tiltcap><around|(|j<rsub|i>|)>>
  used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-trace-small-means>. The lemma
  below assumes only <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-clock-cap-receipt> on a tail, not
  the exponent form of <math|<math-ss|b><rsub|i>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Logarithmic zoom bound><label|lem:ex-lattice-trace-log-zoom>Let
    <math|<around|(|j<rsub|i>|)>> be a sequence of levels with
    <math|j<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>> and let
    <math|<math-ss|b><rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> satisfy
    <math|1\<leqslant\><math-ss|b><rsub|i>\<leqslant\><math-up|tiltcap><around|(|j<rsub|i>|)>>
    for all sufficiently large <math|i>. Then there is <math|i<rsub|0>> such
    that for all <math|i\<geqslant\>i<rsub|0>>

    <\equation>
      0\<leqslant\>log <math-ss|b><rsub|i>\<leqslant\><around|(|log log
      <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|3>\<leqslant\><around|(|log
      <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|3>\<leqslant\><around|(|log
      <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|48>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-trace-log-zoom>
    </equation>

    Consequently

    <\equation*>
      <frac|log <math-ss|b><rsub|i>|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j<rsub|i>>>\<longrightarrow\>0.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Since <math|j<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>> and
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<to\>\<infty\>>, we have
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j<rsub|i>>\<to\>\<infty\>>; choose
    <math|i<rsub|0>> such that for <math|i\<geqslant\>i<rsub|0>> both
    <math|log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j<rsub|i>>\<geqslant\>1> and
    <math|1\<leqslant\><math-ss|b><rsub|i>\<leqslant\><math-up|tiltcap><around|(|j<rsub|i>|)>>.
    Fix <math|i\<geqslant\>i<rsub|0>> and write <math|L=log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j<rsub|i>>\<geqslant\>1> and <math|u=log L>.
    Taking logarithms in <math|1\<leqslant\><math-ss|b><rsub|i>\<leqslant\><math-up|tiltcap><around|(|j<rsub|i>|)>=exp
    <around|(|u<rsup|3>|)>> gives <math|0\<leqslant\>log
    <math-ss|b><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>u<rsup|3>>. The proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-trace-tiltcap-ticket>(1) gives
    <math|0\<leqslant\>u\<less\>L>, so <math|u<rsup|3>\<leqslant\>L<rsup|3>>;
    and <math|L<rsup|3>\<leqslant\>L<rsup|48>> because <math|L\<geqslant\>1>.
    This proves <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-log-zoom>.

    For the limit, put <math|t<rsub|i>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j<rsub|i>>\<to\>\<infty\>>.
    By <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-log-zoom>, <math|0\<leqslant\>log
    <math-ss|b><rsub|i>/t<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><around|(|log
    t<rsub|i>|)><rsup|48>/t<rsub|i>> for <math|i\<geqslant\>i<rsub|0>>. With
    <math|s=log t>, <math|<around|(|log t|)><rsup|48>/t=s<rsup|48>*e<rsup|-s>\<to\>0>
    as <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>>, since the exponential dominates every power.
    The squeeze gives the claim.
  </proof>

  In the scribe clock inputs <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-clock-inputs> the
  third clause, <math|0\<leqslant\>log <math-ss|b><rsub|i>\<leqslant\><around|(|log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|48>> eventually, is therefore a
  consequence of the chart receipt <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-clock-cap-receipt>
  and the clock <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-clock-cap-rate>, with the stronger
  exponent <math|3> in place of <math|48>. The other two clauses of
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-clock-inputs>, the ratio limits
  <math|\<zeta\><around|(|j|)>/<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<to\>1> and
  <math|\<zeta\><around|(|j|)>/\<zeta\><around|(|<math-up|summit><around|(|j|)>|)>\<to\>1>,
  are not addressed here.

  <subsection|A priced mission forces the weighted response to
  vanish><label|subsec:ex-trace-small-means>

  This section records the quantitative argument behind the weighted
  field-image estimate used by the trace receiver. It exposes the local
  price, scale, and detector inputs. It does not derive the mission's local
  price bound from the finite-volume estimates.

  Let <math|S> be the selected interacting table, and let
  <math|\<cal-U\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|d|)>>:E<rsub|d>\<to\>S<rsub|d>> be
  its scale chart from the reference grade space <math|E<rsub|d>> into the
  selected grade space <math|S<rsub|d>>. Here <math|E<rsub|d>> is the
  grade-<math|d> field space of the reference (standard) table; it carries a
  complex-antilinear real-structure involution and an action
  <math|R<around|(|s|)>> of the signed permutations <math|s> of the four
  axes. Let <math|\<cal-V\>> be the finite-dimensional real vector space of
  grade-five response arrays <math|y=<around|(|y<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)><rsub|\<alpha\>\<in\><math-up|Axis>>>,
  <math|y<rsub|\<alpha\>>\<in\>E<rsub|5>>, which are real and
  signed-equivariant: every <math|y<rsub|\<alpha\>>> is fixed by the
  real-structure involution, and

  <\equation*>
    R<around|(|s|)>*y<rsub|\<alpha\>>=<math-up|sign><around|(|s,\<alpha\>|)>*<space|0.17em>y<rsub|s*\<alpha\>><space|2em><text|for
    every signed permutation >s<text| and every axis >\<alpha\>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|<math-up|sign><around|(|s,\<alpha\>|)>> is the sign that
  <math|s> attaches to the axis <math|\<alpha\>>. Both conditions are part of
  the definition; signed equivariance alone defines a larger space. Let
  <math|\<cal-B\>> be the real vector space of arrays of raw grade-five
  labels defined by the same two conditions, with the conjugation and the
  signed-permutation action of the raw labels in place of those of
  <math|E<rsub|5>>. The normalized response map is a real-linear map
  <math|s<rsub|i>:\<cal-B\>\<to\>\<cal-V\>>. Write <math|j<rsub|i>> for the
  selected frame level and <math|k<rsub|i>> for its associated scale index,
  the summit index of <math|j<rsub|i>>; in particular
  <math|k<rsub|i>\<gtr\>j<rsub|i>>, and <math|j<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>> along
  the selected sequence. Two distinct positive scales are used:

  <\equation*>
    z<rsub|i>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|i>>,<space|2em>\<zeta\><rsub|i>=\<zeta\><around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<zeta\><around|(|k|)>\<gtr\>0> is the arch scale at level
  <math|k>. The first, <math|z<rsub|i>>, appears in the polynomial weight and
  rate estimate; the second, <math|\<zeta\><rsub|i>>, is the Arch scale in
  the medium and cancels from the response identity below. Let
  <math|a<rsub|i>> be the field dilation at level <math|j<rsub|i>>,
  <math|b<rsub|i>> the frame scale, <math|m<rsub|i>> the frame cap, and
  <math|r<rsub|i>> the physical dilation. The selected scale relations are

  <\equation*>
    r<rsub|i>=a<rsub|i>*b<rsub|i>,<space|2em>0\<less\>b<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>m<rsub|i>,<space|2em>m<rsub|i>=<math-up|tiltcap><around|(|j<rsub|i>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|v<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-B\>> be the selected medium and
  <math|x<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-B\>> its core field. The three identities
  <eqref|eq:ex-trace-medium-core>\U<eqref|eq:ex-trace-night-scaling> below
  are used only for all sufficiently large <math|i>. They are hypotheses of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-trace-small-means> and are not proved in this
  section; the first two are required only on such a tail, and the third
  requires <math|k<rsub|i>> to be at least the grade-five reference level,
  which holds for large <math|i> because <math|k<rsub|i>\<gtr\>j<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>.
  The core representation is

  <\equation>
    v<rsub|i><around|(|\<alpha\>|)>=\<zeta\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>*\<cal-E\><rsub|k<rsub|i>><around|(|x<rsub|i><around|(|\<alpha\>|)>|)>*<space|2em><around|(|\<alpha\>\<in\><math-up|Axis>|)>,<label|eq:ex-trace-medium-core>
  </equation>

  where <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|k>> is the core-to-strip map at scale index
  <math|k>. The response normalization is, for every <math|v\<in\>\<cal-B\>>
  and every axis <math|\<alpha\>>,

  <\equation>
    \<cal-U\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|5|)>><around|(|<around|(|s<rsub|i><around|(|v|)>|)><rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>=\<cal-V\><rsub|i><around|(|v<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>,<label|eq:ex-trace-spun-response>
  </equation>

  where <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|i>=<math-up|Vault><around|(|S,Z,5,k<rsub|i>,c<rsub|i>|)>>
  is the grade-five strip evaluation at scale index <math|k<rsub|i>> with
  crown normalization <math|c<rsub|i>>. For a raw grade-five label <math|x>
  which is odd under central signed inversion, meaning
  <math|F<around|(|<math-up|archV>|)>*x=-x> (the night condition), the exact
  scaling identity is

  <\equation>
    \<cal-V\><rsub|i><around|(|\<cal-E\><rsub|k<rsub|i>><around|(|x|)>|)>=c<rsub|i>*P<rsub|5>*x,<space|2em>c<rsub|i>=\<zeta\><rsub|i>*<around|(|a<rsub|i>*b<rsub|i>|)><rsup|5>,<label|eq:ex-trace-night-scaling>
  </equation>

  where <math|P<rsub|5>=S.<math-up|project><rsub|5>> maps raw grade-five
  labels into <math|S<rsub|5>>. These are exact identities on the stated
  tail, assumed as hypotheses; they are not consequences of the estimate
  below. Every entry <math|y<rsub|\<alpha\>>> of an array
  <math|y\<in\>\<cal-B\>> satisfies the night condition: the central
  inversion fixes each axis and attaches the sign <math|-1> to it, so signed
  equivariance reads <math|F<around|(|<math-up|archV>|)>*y<rsub|\<alpha\>>=-y<rsub|\<alpha\>>>.
  In particular <eqref|eq:ex-trace-night-scaling> applies to
  <math|x=x<rsub|i><around|(|\<alpha\>|)>>.

  The tested configuration consists of two points. With <math|e<rsub|0>> the
  unit vector of axis <math|0>, put

  <\equation*>
    \<zeta\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|pt>>=<around*|(|b<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*<around|(|<around|\<lceil\>|b<rsub|i>|\<rceil\>>,1,1,1|)>,<space|0.17em>0|)>,<space|2em>\<zeta\><rsub|\<infty\>><rsup|<math-up|pt>>=<around|(|e<rsub|0>,0|)>.
  </equation*>

  The scale input is <math|b<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>; hence
  <math|\<zeta\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|pt>>\<to\>\<zeta\><rsub|\<infty\>><rsup|<math-up|pt>>>,
  the axial configuration. By <math|r<rsub|i>=a<rsub|i>*b<rsub|i>>, the
  dilated configuration <math|r<rsub|i>*\<zeta\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|pt>>=<around|(|a<rsub|i>*<around|(|<around|\<lceil\>|b<rsub|i>|\<rceil\>>,1,1,1|)>,0|)>>
  consists of two distinct points of the lattice of spacing <math|a<rsub|i>>,
  the kind of configuration at which the finite-volume price estimate is
  formulated. For a reference grade-four field <math|w\<in\>E<rsub|4>> and
  <math|Y\<in\>\<cal-V\>>, let <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i,w><around|(|Y|)>> be
  the two-point function of the selected table with entries
  <math|\<cal-U\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|5|)>><around|(|Y<rsub|0>|)>> and
  <math|\<cal-U\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|4|)>>*w>, at the shrinking
  configuration <math|r<rsub|i>*\<zeta\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|pt>>>; only
  the axis-<math|0> entry <math|Y<rsub|0>> of the array enters. Let
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|w><around|(|Y|)>> be the two-point function of the
  reference table with entries <math|Y<rsub|0>> and <math|w> at
  <math|\<zeta\><rsub|\<infty\>><rsup|<math-up|pt>>>. Denote the
  selected-table two-point function at <math|r<rsub|i>*\<zeta\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|pt>>>
  on explicitly supplied grade-five and grade-four entries by
  <math|\<frak-p\><rsub|i,w>>; it is complex-linear in the grade-five entry.
  The local price input is the bound

  <\equation>
    <around*|\||\<frak-p\><rsub|i,w><around|(|a<rsub|i><rsup|5>*P<rsub|5>*x<rsub|i><around|(|0|)>,\<cal-U\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|4|)>>*w|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<Theta\><rsub|i>,<space|1em><text|eventually
    in >i<text| for each fixed >w,<label|eq:ex-trace-small-means-price>
  </equation>

  where

  <\equation*>
    \<Theta\><rsub|i>=exp <space|-0.17em><around*|(|-<frac|100|10<rsup|4>><around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|48>|)>
  </equation*>

  is the positive local price allowance at the frame level <math|j<rsub|i>>.
  The bound <eqref|eq:ex-trace-small-means-price> follows from
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-trace-mission-price-transfer>, using
  the actual localizer, measured root estimates and constructed Crown of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-local-pairing>. The
  finite-volume three-term pairing estimate there uses the same paid labels
  as the mission, before the fixed-level ultrafilter passage. In particular
  no new label is chosen for each spectator test.

  The detector properties can now be verified for these same reference fields
  and selected charts.

  <\enumerate>
    <item><em|Separation at the axial configuration.> The reference
    identification <eqref|eq:scribe-reference-dictionary> preserves the real
    structure and signed motions, so it identifies <math|\<cal-V\>> with the
    reference vector table <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|5>> of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-free-rank>. Take the two
    grade-four fields represented by the quadratic curvature probes
    <math|<around|(|01,01|)>> and <math|<around|(|12,12|)>> there, and call
    them <math|w<rsub|1>,w<rsub|2>>. The definitions of the functionals give
    <math|\<lambda\><rsub|w<rsub|1>>=\<phi\><rsub|1>> and
    <math|\<lambda\><rsub|w<rsub|2>>=\<phi\><rsub|2>>: in both cases the
    output-index-<math|0> entry is inserted at <math|e<rsub|0>> and the
    quadratic probe at the origin. The isomorphism
    <math|\<Phi\>=<around|(|\<phi\><rsub|1>,\<phi\><rsub|2>|)>> proved after
    <eqref|eq:ex-trace-divergence-probes> therefore implies that
    <math|\<lambda\><rsub|w<rsub|1>><around|(|y|)>=\<lambda\><rsub|w<rsub|2>><around|(|y|)>=0>
    forces <math|y=0>. The covariance normalization is unchanged under the
    reference identification. Thus the full family
    <math|<around|{|\<lambda\><rsub|w>:w\<in\>E<rsub|4>|}>> separates
    <math|\<cal-V\>>.

    <item><em|Convergence of fixed tested pairings.> Fix
    <math|u\<in\>E<rsub|5>>, <math|w\<in\>E<rsub|4>>, and
    <math|\<xi\><rsub|i>\<to\><around|(|e<rsub|0>,0|)>>. A small closed ball
    about <math|<around|(|e<rsub|0>,0|)>> is a compact set of distinct-point
    configurations, and contains <math|\<xi\><rsub|i>> eventually. Apply the
    order-zero case of <eqref|eq:scribe-compact-comparison> on this compact
    set to the two fixed labels. Its uniform error tends to zero. The
    reference Wick correlation is smooth there, hence its value at
    <math|\<xi\><rsub|i>> tends to its value at
    <math|<around|(|e<rsub|0>,0|)>>. Their sum gives the required limit for
    the selected correlation at <math|r<rsub|i>*\<xi\><rsub|i>>. This uses
    exactly the completed maps <math|\<cal-U\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|5|)>>,\<cal-U\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|4|)>>>
    and radii of the gallery; coordinate ties at the limiting configuration
    cause no problem because its two points remain distinct.
  </enumerate>

  The finite detector is deduced from them as follows. The maps
  <math|y\<mapsto\>\<lambda\><rsub|w><around|(|y|)>>,
  <math|w\<in\>E<rsub|4>>, are real-linear functionals on the
  finite-dimensional space <math|\<cal-V\>> with zero common kernel
  by<nbsp>(1), so finitely many of them already have zero common kernel.
  Hence there are fixed reference fields <math|w<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,w<rsub|m>\<in\>E<rsub|4>>
  such that the limiting pairings define an injective real-linear map

  <\equation*>
    \<Lambda\><around|(|y|)>=<around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|w<rsub|r>><around|(|y|)>|)><rsub|r=1><rsup|m>:\<cal-V\>\<longrightarrow\>\<bbb-C\><rsup|m>.
  </equation*>

  By<nbsp>(2), applied with <math|\<xi\><rsub|i>=\<zeta\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|pt>>>,
  <math|u=y<rsub|0>> and <math|w=w<rsub|r>>, the scale-dependent real-linear
  maps <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i><around|(|y|)>=<around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|i,w<rsub|r>><around|(|y|)>|)><rsub|r=1><rsup|m>>
  converge to <math|\<Lambda\>> pointwise on <math|\<cal-V\>>, and therefore
  in operator norm, since <math|\<cal-V\>> is finite-dimensional. If
  <math|\<cal-V\>\<neq\><around|{|0|}>>, injectivity gives a positive minimum
  <math|2*c> of <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Lambda\>*y|\<\|\|\>>> on its unit
  sphere, and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>-\<Lambda\>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>c>
  for large <math|i>. Thus some <math|c\<gtr\>0> satisfies

  <\equation>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>*y|\<\|\|\>>\<geqslant\>c<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><space|2em><text|for
    all sufficiently large >i<text| and every
    >y\<in\>\<cal-V\>.<label|eq:ex-trace-small-means-detector>
  </equation>

  For <math|\<cal-V\>=<around|{|0|}>> the desired conclusion is immediate.
  The separation and compact comparison just verified are what permit this
  finite-dimensional lower bound.

  The scalar rate input is

  <\equation>
    z<rsub|i><rsup|p>*m<rsub|i><rsup|5>*\<Theta\><rsub|i>\<longrightarrow\>0<space|2em><text|for
    each fixed integer >p\<geqslant\>0.<label|eq:ex-trace-small-means-rate>
  </equation>

  It follows by multiplying three rate tickets for sequences indexed by the
  frame level <math|j> and then substituting
  <math|j=j<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>. The first follows from the summit-clock
  ratio in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-clock-inputs>: eventually
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|<math-up|summit><around|(|j|)>>\<leqslant\>2*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>>,
  and <math|log <around|(|2*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|)>/<around|(|log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|)><rsup|48>\<to\>0>. The proof of that clock
  ratio uses only the selected scale schedule, not the small-means estimate
  being proved here. The second ticket is
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-trace-tiltcap-ticket>, and the third
  is immediate from the definition of <math|\<Theta\><rsub|i>>. Here a
  nonnegative sequence <math|f<rsub|j>> has a ticket of rate <math|\<rho\>>
  if, for every <math|\<rho\><rprime|'>\<gtr\>\<rho\>>,
  <math|f<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>exp <around|(|\<rho\><rprime|'><around|(|log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|)><rsup|48>|)>> for all sufficiently large
  <math|j>. The three tickets are: the scale <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^>> at the
  summit index of <math|j>, which for <math|j=j<rsub|i>> is <math|z<rsub|i>>
  itself, has a zero-rate ticket (it is eventually at most twice
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>>); the cap
  <math|<math-up|tiltcap><around|(|j|)>> of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:ex-lattice-tiltcap> has a zero-rate ticket,
  hence so does its fifth power (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-trace-tiltcap-ticket>);
  and <math|exp <around|(|-100*\<delta\><around|(|log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|)><rsup|48>|)>>, which is
  <math|\<Theta\><rsub|i>> for <math|j=j<rsub|i>>, has rate
  <math|-100*\<delta\>>, with the fixed constant <math|\<delta\>=10<rsup|-4>>
  of the definition of <math|\<Theta\><rsub|i>>. Ticket products add rates,
  so for fixed <math|p> the product has negative rate
  <math|-100*\<delta\>\<less\>0> and tends to zero, because
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<to\>\<infty\>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Weighted field decay from the priced
    mission><label|lem:ex-trace-small-means>Assume that, for all sufficiently
    large <math|i>, the scale relations <math|r<rsub|i>=a<rsub|i>*b<rsub|i>>
    and <math|0\<less\>b<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>m<rsub|i>>, the medium
    representation <eqref|eq:ex-trace-medium-core>, the response identity
    <eqref|eq:ex-trace-spun-response> and the scaling identity
    <eqref|eq:ex-trace-night-scaling> for <math|x=x<rsub|i><around|(|0|)>>
    hold. Assume the price bound <eqref|eq:ex-trace-small-means-price> for
    each of the finitely many detector fields
    <math|w<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,w<rsub|m>>, the detector bound
    <eqref|eq:ex-trace-small-means-detector>, and the rate
    <eqref|eq:ex-trace-small-means-rate>. Then, for each fixed integer
    <math|p\<geqslant\>0>,

    <\equation*>
      z<rsub|i><rsup|p>*s<rsub|i><around|(|v<rsub|i>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0<space|1em><text|in
      >\<cal-V\>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Set <math|Y<rsub|i>=z<rsub|i><rsup|p>*s<rsub|i><around|(|v<rsub|i>|)>\<in\>\<cal-V\>>,
    and restrict to <math|i> so large that all the assumed identities and the
    finitely many price bounds hold. By the medium representation, response
    normalization, and night scaling, together with the real linearity of
    <math|s<rsub|i>>, <math|\<cal-U\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|5|)>>> and
    <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|i>>,

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-U\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|5|)>><around|(|<around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)><rsub|0>|)>=z<rsub|i><rsup|p>*\<zeta\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>*c<rsub|i>*P<rsub|5>*x<rsub|i><around|(|0|)>=z<rsub|i><rsup|p>*<around|(|a<rsub|i>*b<rsub|i>|)><rsup|5>*P<rsub|5>*x<rsub|i><around|(|0|)>.
    </equation*>

    The Arch scale <math|\<zeta\><rsub|i>> cancels; it is distinct from the
    polynomial weight <math|z<rsub|i>>. Linearity in the grade-five entry
    gives, for each detector field <math|w<rsub|r>>,

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsub|i,w<rsub|r>><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>=z<rsub|i><rsup|p>*b<rsub|i><rsup|5>*\<frak-p\><rsub|i,w<rsub|r>><around|(|a<rsub|i><rsup|5>*P<rsub|5>*x<rsub|i><around|(|0|)>,\<cal-U\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|4|)>>*w<rsub|r>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The local price bound and <math|b<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>m<rsub|i>> imply

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>*Y<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><sqrt|m>*<space|0.17em>z<rsub|i><rsup|p>*m<rsub|i><rsup|5>*\<Theta\><rsub|i>\<longrightarrow\>0.
    </equation*>

    The detector lower bound gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|Y<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>c<rsup|-1>*<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>*Y<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<to\>0>.
    This proves the desired limit in the full finite-dimensional response
    space.
  </proof>

  The lemma is applied to the selected medium <math|v<rsub|i>>. The proof
  identifies the mechanism: the powered local price controls finitely many
  separating coordinates, and the negative rate forces each coordinate to
  zero. The uniform local price receipt, mission construction, and detector
  convergence still depend on the selected finite-volume funding and chart
  construction.

  <subsection|The finite-volume Crown buffer><label|sec:ex-lattice-trace-crown-buffer>

  We record the buffer argument used to pass from a local Crown approximation
  to the selected two-point observable. It is useful to separate its three
  ingredients: a rooted product estimate for the good approximation, a
  finite-volume estimate for the exceptional set, and the exact product
  identification across the coarse-to-fine map.

  Throughout, one selected family is fixed, and every scale, coupling,
  effective representation, tiling and extension map below is the one
  attached to that family's selected history; no second history is chosen.
  Fix a scale index <math|i\<gtr\>0>, a finite-volume index <math|N>, and a
  period <math|p> with <math|i\<less\>k=<math-up|summit><around|(|i|)>\<leqslant\>N>.
  Put <math|k<rsub|->=k-1>. Write

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|T>|<cell|=<math-up|tiles><around|(|i,k,N,p|)>,>|<cell|F<rsub|i><around|(|q|)>>|<cell|=exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<frac|q|10<rsup|4>>*<space|0.17em>G<rsub|i>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|G<rsub|i>>|<cell|=<math-up|Gate><around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>|)>,>|<cell|\<Pi\><rsub|k>>|<cell|=<math-up|prism><around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<beta\>>|<cell|=\<beta\><rsub|N,p>,>|<cell|\<beta\><rsub|k>>|<cell|=\<beta\><rsub|N,p><around|(|k|)>,<space|2em>A<rsub|j>=C.*S.*a<around|(|j|)>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Here <math|T> is the tiling of the volume-<math|N> lattice by the cells of
  scale <math|i>. For a tiling <math|T> with cell set <math|I>, its rooted
  functional is <math|<math-ss|R><rsub|T><rsup|\<beta\>><around|(|h|)>=<around|\||\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<beta\>><around|[|<big|prod><rsub|q\<in\>I>T.<math-up|copy><around|(|q,h|)>|]>|\|><rsup|1/<around|\||I|\|>>>;
  the root is normalized by the number <math|<around|\||I|\|>> of cells.

  Three conditions are used repeatedly. A complex function <math|h> of the
  lattice configuration is <em|interior> to a set <math|K> of sites if it is
  gauge invariant, measurable and bounded, and depends only on the
  restriction of the configuration to <math|K>. The <em|window> of radius
  <math|l> is the image of the centred box of radius <math|l> in the
  scale-<math|k> lattice. The scale <math|i> has <em|room> for the radius
  <math|l> at scale <math|k> if

  <\equation*>
    2*<around*|(|A<rsub|k>*l+<math-up|tail><around|(|k|)>|)>\<less\>A<rsub|i>,<space|2em><math-up|tail><around|(|k|)>=30*A<rsub|k>*<around|(|J<rsub|k>+5|)>,
  </equation*>

  with strict inequality, where <math|J<rsub|k>> is the stage index of the
  clock at scale <math|k>. If a scale-<math|k> field is interior to the
  window of radius <math|l> and the scale <math|i> has room for <math|l>,
  then its extension <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|N,k>> to volume <math|N>, defined
  below, is interior to the base chamber of <math|T>. Finally, the <em|seam
  condition> for the Wilson measure at coupling <math|\<beta\>> is the
  conjunction of reflection positivity across every lattice cut, the
  tiled-product inequality

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<beta\>><around*|[|<big|prod><rsub|q\<in\>I>T.<math-up|copy><around|(|q,h<rsub|q>|)>|]>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|q\<in\>I><math-ss|R><rsub|T><rsup|\<beta\>><around|(|h<rsub|q>|)>
  </equation*>

  for every tiling <math|T> and all fields <math|h<rsub|q>> interior to its
  base chamber, and subadditivity of <math|<math-ss|R><rsub|T><rsup|\<beta\>>>
  on such fields. For <math|\<beta\>\<geqslant\>0>, reflection invariance of
  the Wilson law and Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-finite-reflection>
  give positivity at each translated seam.
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-centered-planted> then gives the
  tiled-product inequality with exactly these strict chambers and oriented
  copies. Only that inequality is used below; subadditivity is not an
  additional premise of the buffer argument. Write <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|N,k>>
  for the local extension from scale <math|k> to volume <math|N>. For
  distinct coarse sites <math|a,b> at scale <math|i>, let
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i,k><around|(|a|)>> and
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i,k><around|(|b|)>> denote their scale-<math|k>
  anchors, and let <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i,N><around|(|a|)>,\<alpha\><rsub|i,N><around|(|b|)>>
  denote their volume-<math|N> anchors. For scale-<math|k> fields <math|X,Y>,
  define the complex pairing

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-Q\>*<around|(|X,Y|)>>|<cell|=\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<beta\>>*<space|-0.17em><around*|[|<around|(|<around|(|\<cal-B\><rsub|N,k>*X|)>\<circ\><math-up|translate><rsub|\<alpha\><rsub|i,N><around|(|a|)>>|)>*<around|(|<around|(|\<cal-B\><rsub|N,k>*Y|)>\<circ\><math-up|translate><rsub|\<alpha\><rsub|i,N><around|(|b|)>>|)>|]>,>>|<row|<cell|X<rsub|a>>|<cell|=X\<circ\><math-up|pull><rsub|\<alpha\><rsub|i,k><around|(|a|)>>,<space|2em>Y<rsub|b>=Y\<circ\><math-up|pull><rsub|\<alpha\><rsub|i,k><around|(|b|)>>.>>>>
  </align*>

  The exact product identity used below is
  <math|\<cal-Q\>*<around|(|X,Y|)>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<beta\>>*<around|[|\<cal-B\><rsub|N,k>*<around|(|X<rsub|a>*Y<rsub|b>|)>|]>>
  when <math|a\<ne\>b>, both fields are interior to a common window of some
  radius <math|u>, and the scale <math|i> has room for <math|u>. The scale
  receipts give, eventually in <math|i>,

  <\equation>
    e<rsup|-2*\<Pi\><rsub|k>>\<leqslant\>F<rsub|i>*<around|(|-1,200,000|)>,<space|2em>3\<leqslant\>F<rsub|i><around|(|1|)>.<label|eq:ex-trace-crown-small-tail>
  </equation>

  For the first inequality, use <math|i\<leqslant\>k>, monotonicity of the
  gate, and the selected estimates

  <\equation*>
    100,000*<space|0.17em>G<rsub|k><space|0.17em><math-up|Lyra><around|(|k|)>\<leqslant\>\<Pi\><rsub|k>/32,<space|2em>G<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>G<rsub|k>.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|<math-up|Lyra><around|(|k|)>\<geqslant\>1> eventually, this
  gives <math|120*G<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>2*\<Pi\><rsub|k>>, which is the
  exponent inequality in <eqref|eq:ex-trace-crown-small-tail>. The second
  inequality follows from <math|G<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>.

  Suppose <math|f> is the field to be tested and a Crown witness provides a
  field <math|w>, a support <math|P>, and a window radius <math|l> such that
  both fields are interior to that window, the scale has room for <math|l>,
  and

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\||P|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>F<rsub|i><around|(|1000|)>,>|<cell|sup<rsub|U><around|\||f<around|(|U|)>-w<around|(|U|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>F<rsub|i>*<around|(|500,000|)>,<eq-number>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||f<around|(|U|)>-w<around|(|U|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>F<rsub|i>*<around|(|-500,000|)>>|<cell|>|<cell|<text|if
    <math|U> is healthy for <math|P> at threshold
    >\<vartheta\><rsub|k>/16,<eq-number>>>|<row|<cell|<math-ss|R><rsub|T><rsup|\<beta\>><around|(|\<cal-B\><rsub|N,k>*w|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>F<rsub|i>*<around|(|-1,200,000|)>.>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number><label|eq:ex-trace-crown-witness>>>>>
  </align>

  Let <math|g> be the second field in the desired probe, with
  <math|sup<rsub|U><around|\||g<around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>F<rsub|i>*<around|(|150,000|)>>.
  Let <math|l<rsub|4>> be the fixed grade-four test radius (the grade-four
  stencil radius plus four); assume that <math|g> is interior to the window
  of radius <math|l<rsub|4>> and that the scale <math|i> has room for
  <math|l<rsub|4>> at scale <math|k>. Both <math|l> and <math|l<rsub|4>> then
  satisfy the room condition, so <math|f>, <math|w>, <math|f-w> and <math|g>
  are all interior to the window of radius <math|max
  <around|{|l,l<rsub|4>|}>>, for which the scale has room; this is the common
  window used in the product identity, and it makes the extensions of
  <math|w> and <math|g> interior to the base chamber of <math|T>. Let
  <math|a\<ne\>b> be distinct coarse sites. Define the pulled-back factors

  <\equation*>
    f<rsub|a><around|(|U|)>=f<around|(|<math-up|pull><rsub|\<alpha\><rsub|i,k><around|(|a|)>><space|0.17em>U|)>,<space|1em>w<rsub|a><around|(|U|)>=w<around|(|<math-up|pull><rsub|\<alpha\><rsub|i,k><around|(|a|)>><space|0.17em>U|)>,<space|1em>g<rsub|b><around|(|U|)>=g<around|(|<math-up|pull><rsub|\<alpha\><rsub|i,k><around|(|b|)>><space|0.17em>U|)>,<space|1em>D<rsub|a>=f<rsub|a>-w<rsub|a>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|k>> is the clock threshold at scale <math|k>;
  the pull maps use the scale-<math|k> anchors defined above.

  We make explicit the finite-volume input used for the correction. Let
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|k>> be the nonnegative density of the effective
  scale-<math|k> representation of the volume-<math|N> Wilson measure, and
  put <math|Z=<big|int>\<rho\><rsub|k>=<math-up|Partition><around|(|N,p|)>>.
  We use the normalized transfer identity

  <\equation>
    \<bbb-E\><rsub|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>>*<around|[|\<cal-B\><rsub|N,k>*h|]>=Z<rsup|-1>*<big|int>\<rho\><rsub|k><around|(|U|)>*h<around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|k><around|(|U|)>.<label|eq:ex-trace-crown-transfer>
  </equation>

  Let <math|I<rsub|x><around|(|U|)>> indicate that the local flag at site
  <math|x> exceeds threshold <math|b>. The one-site estimate is an incoming
  local-action receipt:

  <\equation>
    <big|int>\<rho\><rsub|k><around|(|U|)>*I<rsub|x><around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|k><around|(|U|)>\<leqslant\>Z*exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<around*|(|<frac|A<rsub|k<rsub|->>|A<rsub|k>>|)><rsup|4><space|-0.17em><math-up|Treasury><around|(|k|)>-\<beta\><rsub|k>*<frac|<math-up|clip><rsub|k>|5>*b<rsup|2>|)>.<label|eq:ex-trace-crown-one-site-tail>
  </equation>

  It applies when <math|2*b\<leqslant\>\<vartheta\><rsub|k>> and the scale
  has room for the radius-eight indicator. Given this receipt, obtain the
  corresponding estimate for a support <math|P<rprime|'>> by splitting
  configurations into those healthy for all sites of <math|P<rprime|'>> and
  the complement. On the healthy set, use the bound <math|U<rsub|0>> for the
  field. On the complement, there is at least one <math|x\<in\>P<rprime|'>>
  with <math|I<rsub|x>=1>, so the pointwise absolute integrand is at most
  <math|V<rsub|0>*\<rho\><rsub|k>*<big|sum><rsub|x\<in\>P<rprime|'>>I<rsub|x>>.
  Integration, the triangle inequality, and
  <eqref|eq:ex-trace-crown-one-site-tail> give

  <\equation>
    <around|\||\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>>*<around|[|\<cal-B\><rsub|N,k>*h|]>|\|>\<leqslant\>U<rsub|0>+V<rsub|0><around|\||P<rprime|'>|\|>*exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<around*|(|<frac|A<rsub|k<rsub|->>|A<rsub|k>>|)><rsup|4><space|-0.17em><math-up|Treasury><around|(|k|)>-\<beta\><rsub|k>*<frac|<math-up|clip><rsub|k>|5>*b<rsup|2>|)>.<label|eq:ex-trace-crown-soils>
  </equation>

  The exponent in <eqref|eq:ex-trace-crown-soils> is eventually at most
  <math|-2*\<Pi\><rsub|k>> by the scale estimate

  <\equation>
    <around*|(|<frac|A<rsub|k<rsub|->>|A<rsub|k>>|)><rsup|4><space|-0.17em><math-up|Treasury><around|(|k|)>-\<beta\><rsub|k>*<frac|<math-up|clip><rsub|k>|5>*b<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>-2*\<Pi\><rsub|k>,<label|eq:ex-trace-crown-funeral>
  </equation>

  for <math|b=\<vartheta\><rsub|k>/16>. This is the exact threshold used in
  the witness bound. Here is the scalar calculation behind that estimate. Put
  <math|x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>, <math|y=<math-up|Lyra><around|(|k|)>>,
  <math|q=\<Pi\><rsub|k>>, <math|C=<math-up|clip><rsub|k>>, and
  <math|m=<math-up|monitor><around|(|k|)>>. These satisfy
  <math|x,y,C\<gtr\>0> and <math|q\<geqslant\>0>. The selected scale receipts
  give

  <\equation*>
    <around*|(|<frac|A<rsub|k<rsub|->>|A<rsub|k>>|)><rsup|4><math-up|Treasury><around|(|k|)>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|6>,<space|1em>10\<cdot\>16<rsup|2>*y*<around|(|y<rsup|6>+2*q|)>\<leqslant\>m<rsup|2>,<space|1em>y*C\<geqslant\>1,<space|1em>x*\<vartheta\><rsub|k><rsup|2>=m<rsup|2>,<space|1em>\<beta\><rsub|k>\<geqslant\>x/2.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|b=\<vartheta\><rsub|k>/16>, the second and fourth inequalities
  imply <math|y<rsup|6>+2*q\<leqslant\>m<rsup|2>/<around|(|2560*y|)>>. The
  third gives <math|1/y\<leqslant\>C>, so

  <\equation*>
    y<rsup|6>+2*q\<leqslant\><frac|C*m<rsup|2>|2560>=<frac|x|2>*<frac|C|5><around*|(|<frac|\<vartheta\><rsub|k>|16>|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rsub|k>*<frac|C|5>*b<rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  Subtracting from the first bound yields the exponent in
  <eqref|eq:ex-trace-crown-funeral>. Thus the exceptional-set estimate is
  obtained from the one-site flag bound and the displayed scale receipts, not
  from locality by itself.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Good Crown term><label|lem:ex-trace-crown-good>Assume the Crown
    witness <eqref|eq:ex-trace-crown-witness>, the stated bound, interiority
    and room hypotheses for <math|g>, the seam condition for the Wilson
    measure at coupling <math|\<beta\>>, and <math|a\<ne\>b>. Then

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<cal-Q\>*<around|(|w,g|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>F<rsub|i>*<around|(|-1,050,000|)>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The extensions <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|N,k>*w> and
    <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|N,k>*g> are interior to the base chamber of
    <math|T>, by the window and room hypotheses. The translated form of the
    tiled-product inequality, for two fields placed at the two distinct cells
    <math|a\<ne\>b>, gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<cal-Q\>*<around|(|w,g|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><math-ss|R><rsub|T><rsup|\<beta\>><around|(|\<cal-B\><rsub|N,k>*w|)><space|0.17em><math-ss|R><rsub|T><rsup|\<beta\>><around|(|\<cal-B\><rsub|N,k>*g|)>.
    </equation*>

    Here is the reduction to Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-centered-planted>.
    In each occupied cell, undo the coordinate reflections prescribed by the
    parity of that cell, and conjugate the home field if that parity is odd.
    The resulting home field is still bounded, measurable, gauge invariant
    and chamber-interior. Copying it to the occupied cell cancels those
    reflections and conjugations, leaving the desired translated factor. In
    the product over all cells, changing a home field by one of these
    reflections permutes the cell indices; an odd parity can additionally
    conjugate the full product. Thus its absolute expectation, and hence its
    rooted functional, is unchanged. Apply the planted inequality to these
    two home fields in the distinct cells <math|a,b> (equivalently put the
    unit field in every other cell). This proves the displayed estimate. The
    Crown bound controls the first factor by
    <math|F<rsub|i>*<around|(|-1,200,000|)>>. For the second, the rooted sup
    estimate says that a field bounded pointwise by <math|R\<geqslant\>0> has
    root at most <math|R>; hence it is at most
    <math|F<rsub|i>*<around|(|150,000|)>>. Multiplying and using
    <math|F<rsub|i><around|(|s|)>*F<rsub|i><around|(|t|)>=F<rsub|i>*<around|(|s+t|)>>
    gives the claim.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Exceptional-set buffer><label|lem:ex-trace-crown-exception>Assume
    the Crown witness <eqref|eq:ex-trace-crown-witness>, the stated bound,
    interiority and room hypotheses for <math|g>, <math|a\<ne\>b>, the
    transfer identity <eqref|eq:ex-trace-crown-transfer>, and, for the given
    <math|i>, the scale inequalities <eqref|eq:ex-trace-crown-small-tail> and
    <eqref|eq:ex-trace-crown-funeral> together with the one-site estimate
    <eqref|eq:ex-trace-crown-one-site-tail> at
    <math|b=\<vartheta\><rsub|k>/16>. Then the correction term satisfies

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<cal-Q\>*<around|(|f-w,g|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>F<rsub|i>*<around|(|-350,000|)>+F<rsub|i>*<around|(|-549,000|)>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    By the exact product identity, applied to <math|f-w> and <math|g> in the
    common window of radius <math|max <around|{|l,l<rsub|4>|}>>, the
    correction is the finite-volume Wilson expectation of the extension of
    the local field <math|D<rsub|a>*g<rsub|b>>, which is measurable and
    bounded. This field has envelope obtained by multiplying the two local
    envelopes. Its global bound is <math|F<rsub|i>*<around|(|500,000|)>*F<rsub|i>*<around|(|150,000|)>=F<rsub|i>*<around|(|650,000|)>>;
    on the healthy set for the translated support, the bound improves to
    <math|F<rsub|i>*<around|(|-500,000|)>*F<rsub|i>*<around|(|150,000|)>=F<rsub|i>*<around|(|-350,000|)>>.
    Translation preserves the number of support sites, so the support
    cardinality is at most <math|F<rsub|i><around|(|1000|)>>. Apply
    <eqref|eq:ex-trace-crown-soils> with <math|U<rsub|0>=F<rsub|i>*<around|(|-350,000|)>>,
    <math|V<rsub|0>=F<rsub|i>*<around|(|650,000|)>>, <math|P<rprime|'>> the
    translate of <math|P>, and <math|b=\<vartheta\><rsub|k>/16>. By
    <eqref|eq:ex-trace-crown-funeral>, this bounds
    <math|<around|\||\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<beta\>>*<around|[|\<cal-B\><rsub|N,k>*<around|(|D<rsub|a>*g<rsub|b>|)>|]>|\|>>
    by

    <\equation*>
      F<rsub|i>*<around|(|-350,000|)>+F<rsub|i>*<around|(|650,000|)>*F<rsub|i><around|(|1000|)>*e<rsup|-2*\<Pi\><rsub|k>>.
    </equation*>

    Apply <eqref|eq:ex-trace-crown-small-tail> to the last factor and combine
    the exponents: <math|650,000+1000-1,200,000=-549,000>. Thus

    <\equation>
      F<rsub|i>*<around|(|-350,000|)>+F<rsub|i>*<around|(|-549,000|)>.<label|eq:ex-trace-crown-exception-correct>
    </equation>
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Crown buffer for the two-point
    probe><label|prop:ex-trace-crown-buffer>There is <math|i<rsub|0>> such
    that the following holds for every <math|i\<geqslant\>i<rsub|0>>, every
    volume index <math|N\<geqslant\>k=<math-up|summit><around|(|i|)>> and
    every period <math|p>. Let <math|f> be a scale-<math|k> field with a
    Crown witness <eqref|eq:ex-trace-crown-witness>, let <math|g> be a
    scale-<math|k> field with <math|sup<rsub|U><around|\||g<around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>F<rsub|i>*<around|(|150,000|)>>
    which is interior to the window of radius <math|l<rsub|4>>, and assume
    that the scale <math|i> has room for <math|l<rsub|4>> at scale <math|k>.
    Assume the seam condition, the transfer identity
    <eqref|eq:ex-trace-crown-transfer> and the one-site estimate
    <eqref|eq:ex-trace-crown-one-site-tail> for the selected history. Then,
    for all distinct coarse sites <math|a\<ne\>b> at scale <math|i>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<cal-Q\>*<around|(|f,g|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>F<rsub|i>*<around|(|-30,000|)>.
    </equation*>

    The threshold <math|i<rsub|0>> depends only on the selected scale
    receipts <eqref|eq:ex-trace-crown-small-tail> and
    <eqref|eq:ex-trace-crown-funeral>; it does not depend on <math|N>,
    <math|p>, the fields, the witness, or the sites.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Choose <math|i<rsub|0>> so that <eqref|eq:ex-trace-crown-small-tail>
    holds for <math|i\<geqslant\>i<rsub|0>> and
    <eqref|eq:ex-trace-crown-funeral> holds at
    <math|k=<math-up|summit><around|(|i|)>> for
    <math|i\<geqslant\>i<rsub|0>>; this is possible because
    <math|<math-up|summit><around|(|i|)>\<gtr\>i\<to\>\<infty\>>. Set
    <math|D<rsub|a>=f<rsub|a>-w<rsub|a>>. The extension
    <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|N,k>> is linear and the expectation is linear, and
    all fields involved are measurable and bounded, so

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-Q\>*<around|(|f,g|)>=\<cal-Q\>*<around|(|w,g|)>+\<cal-Q\>*<around|(|f-w,g|)>.
    </equation*>

    The first term is bounded by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-trace-crown-good>
    and the second by <eqref|eq:ex-trace-crown-exception-correct>. Each
    exponent is at most <math|-31,000>, so the sum is at most
    <math|3*F<rsub|i>*<around|(|-31,000|)>>. Since eventually
    <math|3\<leqslant\>F<rsub|i><around|(|1|)>>, this is at most

    <\equation*>
      F<rsub|i>*<around|(|-31,000|)>*F<rsub|i><around|(|1|)>=F<rsub|i>*<around|(|-30,999|)>\<leqslant\>F<rsub|i>*<around|(|-30,000|)>.
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  The proof uses the stated Crown witness and the finite-volume buffer
  estimate. For the grade-five field used in the probe assembly, the witness
  is constructed in the <hlink|actual Crown assembly|#par:trace-actual-crown>
  of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-local-pairing>. Its
  paired-lens estimate and healthy-region identity give, after the same
  dilation and signed average, precisely the three bounds in
  <eqref|eq:ex-trace-crown-witness>. The proposition is stated for any field
  with such a witness; it does not assert a witness for an arbitrary field.

  <subsection|The finite price recurrence><label|sec:ex-lattice-trace-price-recurrence>

  We give the scale induction that turns the paid direction and the local
  one-step response estimate into the endpoint inequality used in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-coffer-supplier>. The
  one-step response estimate is derived below from the finite-volume local
  insertion, local uniqueness, and block-size bounds. The remaining scale
  inputs are stated as hypotheses of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-trace-sharp-read>.

  Fix a reference degree <math|d>, a selected scale history <math|H>, and
  integers <math|m\<leqslant\>k>. Write

  <\equation*>
    \<Gamma\><rsub|j>=exp <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<frac|S<rsub|k>-S<rsub|j>|8>|)>,<space|2em>\<kappa\><rsub|j>=<sqrt|<math-up|monitor><around|(|j|)>>,<space|2em>I<rsub|j>=<frac|1|\<Gamma\><rsub|j>*\<kappa\><rsub|j>>*<space|1em><around|(|m\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>k|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|S<rsub|j>> is the scale coordinate of the history. For
  <math|m\<leqslant\>j\<less\>k>, let <math|L<rsub|j>> be the block side
  length at step <math|j>, let

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|j>=C*<around|(|J*<around|(|j+1|)>|)>,<space|2em>b<rsub|j>=<frac|1|10*K*<around|(|J*<around|(|j+1|)>|)>>,<space|2em>\<rho\><rsub|j>=<math-up|rate><around|(|L<rsub|j>|)>,<space|2em>s<rsub|j>=<frac|b<rsub|j>|\<kappa\><rsub|j+1>>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|J<around|(|j|)>> is the stage at rung <math|j>, <math|C> is the
  selected coefficient-cost profile, <math|K> is the fixed positive worth
  profile, and <math|\<rho\><rsub|j>\<gtr\>0> is the block remainder-cost
  factor. For each rung <math|j>, write <math|c<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1> for the
  selected column allowance of the history at that rung. It is a real
  parameter of the selected stage data, not the cardinality of a coefficient
  index set; <math|c<rsub|k>> is its value at the terminal rung, matching the
  notation of the Coffer estimate. Put <math|p<rsub|j>=L<rsub|j><rsup|1/8>>,
  and define

  <\equation>
    H<rsub|j>=\<Gamma\><rsub|j><rsup|-1>+<frac|P<rsub|j>*L<rsub|j><rsup|d>+\<rho\><rsub|j>|\<Gamma\><rsub|j+1>>,<space|2em>\<varepsilon\><rsub|j>=<frac|I<rsub|j+1>|10*P<rsub|j>*H<rsub|j>>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-price-local-scales>
  </equation>

  All quantities in the denominators are positive.

  The scale and budget choices give, at every step,

  <\equation>
    \<kappa\><rsub|j+1>\<leqslant\>2*\<kappa\><rsub|j>,<space|2em>1\<leqslant\>P<rsub|j>,<space|2em>4*P<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>p<rsub|j>,<space|2em>2*P<rsub|j>*b<rsub|j>\<leqslant\><frac|1|3>,<space|2em>\<Gamma\><rsub|j>=\<Gamma\><rsub|j+1>*p<rsub|j>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-price-budget>
  </equation>

  Consequently

  <\equation>
    P<rsub|j>*I<rsub|j>\<leqslant\><frac|1|2>*I<rsub|j+1>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-price-contraction>
  </equation>

  Indeed, the ratio of the left side to <math|I<rsub|j+1>> is
  <math|P<rsub|j>*\<kappa\><rsub|j+1>/<around|(|p<rsub|j>*\<kappa\><rsub|j>|)>>,
  which is at most <math|2*P<rsub|j>/p<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>1/2>.

  For a coefficient <math|x> at rung <math|j>, write <math|x<rsup|\<circ\>>>
  for its projection to nonconstant coordinates, and let
  <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|j><around|(|x|)>> be the coefficient produced by the
  local block insertion. The diagonal weight map
  <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|L<rsub|j>>> multiplies a grade-<math|r> coordinate by
  <math|L<rsub|j><rsup|d-r>>. Thus <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-V\><rsub|L<rsub|j>><around|(|x<rsup|\<circ\>>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|j><rsup|d><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsup|\<circ\>>|\<\|\|\>>>.
  The one-step local response estimate, for a paid comparison with adjacent
  coefficients <math|x<rsub|j>,x<rsub|j+1>> and nonnegative remainder-cost
  bounds <math|r<rsub|j>,r<rsub|j+1>>, is

  <\equation>
    <\eqsplit>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|j>-\<cal-L\><rsub|j><around|(|x<rsub|j+1>|)>|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>s<rsub|j>*<around*|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|j+1><rsup|\<circ\>>|\<\|\|\>>+r<rsub|j+1>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|1em>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|j>*<around*|(|r<rsub|j>+P<rsub|j>\<\|\|\>\<cal-V\><rsub|L<rsub|j>><around|(|x<rsub|j+1><rsup|\<circ\>>|)>\<\|\|\>+\<rho\><rsub|j>*r<rsub|j+1>|)>.>>>>
    </eqsplit>

    <label|eq:ex-lattice-price-one-step>
  </equation>

  Here <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>>> is the coefficient
  <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>>-norm, the sum of the absolute values of all
  coordinates including the unit coordinate, and <math|x<rsup|\<circ\>>>
  deletes exactly the unit coordinate. In the selected construction, under
  its standing scale hypotheses (in particular the stage at rung <math|j> is
  at least <math|d+3>), the estimate holds for all sufficiently large period
  indices <math|n>, with a threshold depending on <math|j> and on the
  constant <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|j>>, and then uniformly in the volume
  index <math|N\<gtr\>j> and in the pair of adjacent fields, provided both
  fields belong to the effective representations of the same selected history
  at the rungs <math|j> and <math|j+1> for these <math|N> and <math|n>, have
  the required order-zero format and remainder bounds at the normalizations
  <math|<sqrt|<math-up|forcedAllowance><rsub|j>>> and
  <math|<sqrt|<math-up|forcedAllowance><rsub|j+1>>> with costs
  <math|r<rsub|j>,r<rsub|j+1>>, satisfy the exact density transfer (the
  rung-<math|j> density is <math|L<rsub|j><rsup|d>> times the pushed
  rung-<math|<around|(|j+1|)>> density), and have marked sites related by the
  scale map. The separate local insertion estimate is also

  <\equation>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|j+1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|j><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-L\><rsub|j><around|(|x<rsub|j+1>|)>|\<\|\|\>>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-price-spends>
  </equation>

  We explain the finite-volume comparison producing the second line of
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-price-one-step>. Let <math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|d>> be the
  finite family of coefficient-recovery probes, with probe prices
  <math|\<pi\><around|(|\<tau\>|)>> for <math|\<tau\>\<in\>\<cal-Q\><rsub|d>>.
  Put

  <\equation*>
    M<rsub|d>=1+<big|sum><rsub|q=0><rsup|d>2<rsup|q>*<around|(|q+1|)><rsup|q>.
  </equation*>

  Here is the derivative comparison paid by this constant. Let <math|h> be
  the difference of two <math|C<rsup|d>> functions near the identity, and
  write <math|<wide|h|~>> for <math|h> in the exponential link chart at the
  identity. For a fixed direction <math|v>, the curve with links <math|exp
  <around|(|t*v<rsub|e>|)>> is the flow of the corresponding left-invariant
  direction. Thus the repeated left-direction derivative of order <math|q> is
  <math|D<rsup|q>*<wide|h|~><around|(|0|)><around|[|v,\<ldots\>,v|]>>. This
  assertion uses a single repeated direction; mixed left-invariant
  derivatives need not commute. Suppose its absolute value is at most
  <math|c> for every unit direction, for each <math|q\<leqslant\>d>. The
  symmetric <math|q>-linear derivative <math|A<rsub|q>=D<rsup|q>*<wide|h|~><around|(|0|)>>
  satisfies, for <math|q\<geqslant\>1>, the real polarization identity

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|q>*<around|(|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|q>|)>=<frac|1|2<rsup|q>*q!>*<big|sum><rsub|\<epsilon\>\<in\><around|{|-1,1|}><rsup|q>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j=1><rsup|q>\<epsilon\><rsub|j>|)>*A<rsub|q>*<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|j>\<epsilon\><rsub|j>*v<rsub|j>,\<ldots\>,<big|sum><rsub|j>\<epsilon\><rsub|j>*v<rsub|j>|)>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1>, homogeneity
  and the triangle inequality bound the right side by
  <math|c*q<rsup|q>/q!\<leqslant\>c*<space|0.17em>2<rsup|q>*<around|(|q+1|)><rsup|q>>.
  The order-zero bound is <math|<around|\||h<around|(|1|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>c>.
  Each of these constants is at most <math|M<rsub|d>>. The probe price
  <math|\<pi\><around|(|\<tau\>|)>> includes the absolute chain-rule
  coefficients and the products of the derivatives of its logarithmic-link
  test curve. Applying the chain rule to <math|<wide|h|~>> therefore bounds
  the difference of the two probe values by
  <math|\<pi\><around|(|\<tau\>|)>*M<rsub|d>*c>. This proves the asserted
  comparison without identifying mixed chart derivatives with mixed
  left-invariant derivatives. Let <math|T<rsub|d>> be the finite coefficient
  reconstruction constant for this probe family. Thus for any coefficient
  difference <math|z>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>T<rsub|d>*<big|sum><rsub|\<tau\>\<in\>\<cal-Q\><rsub|d>><around|\||R<rsub|\<tau\>><around|(|z|)>|\|>.
  </equation*>

  A probe of order at most <math|d> annihilates a regular source term whose
  support is nonwrapping and whose format has the required vanishing of its
  first <math|d> local variations. Let <math|B<rsub|0>> be the support radius
  and <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0> the support exponent of the local
  representation, and define

  <\equation*>
    M<rsub|B<rsub|0>>*<around|(|X,W|)>=e<rsup|\<sigma\><math-up|cover><rsub|B<rsub|0>><around|(|X|)>>*W.
  </equation*>

  For each of the two coefficients, the regular part has a countable
  presentation by regular terms with origin in their supports and with total
  <math|M<rsub|B<rsub|0>>>-mass bounded by its remainder budget. Each term
  has a common healthy chart. If its purchased sup-bound is <math|u<rsub|a>>,
  then on this chart every ordered directional derivative of orders
  <math|q=0,\<ldots\>,d> has norm at most <math|u<rsub|a>>, after the local
  variation prices have been factored out. The chart is large enough for each
  recovery probe and its local stencil to fit, and the admissible local
  presentation has the required order-<math|d> vanishing on each nonwrapping
  term. These are the finite recovery and regularity hypotheses used below; a
  value bound alone would not imply the probe estimate. Their signed union is
  still absolutely summable, even when some supports overlap.

  Let <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|0>> be the indices of nonwrapping terms, and take
  a finite subset <math|D\<subset\>\<cal-I\><rsub|0>>. The probe applied to
  the presented coefficient equals the corresponding derivative of its local
  presentation; this is the coefficient-readout identity. On the common quiet
  chart, the forced contributions vanish, and the prescribed masks replace
  the local masks. Thus each density is the same nonzero exponential action
  factor times its presented coefficient plus its regular series. Equality of
  the two densities lets us cancel this common factor and gives the signed
  difference of the presented coefficients as the sum of all signed regular
  terms: writing <math|g> for the local presentation of the coefficient
  difference and <math|f<rsub|a>> for the signed regular terms,

  <\equation>
    g<around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a>f<rsub|a><around|(|U|)><label|eq:ex-lattice-price-presented-sum>
  </equation>

  for every configuration <math|U> in the quiet set <math|\<cal-D\>>, the set
  of configurations all of whose plaquette variables are strictly below the
  threshold. The set <math|\<cal-D\>> is open and contains the identity
  configuration, every support is healthy on it, and the series converges
  absolutely there because <math|<around|\||f<rsub|a>|\|>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|a>>
  and <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>u<rsub|a>\<less\>\<infty\>>.

  The probe estimate needs the ordered derivatives of <math|g>, so the
  identity <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-price-presented-sum> must be differentiated.
  We claim that for every <math|U\<in\>\<cal-D\>> and every ordered list
  <math|<around|(|A<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,A<rsub|q>|)>> of normalized directions
  with <math|q> at most the order of the budget,

  <\equation>
    \<partial\><rsub|A<rsub|1>>*\<cdots\>*\<partial\><rsub|A<rsub|q>>*g<around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|A<rsub|1>>*\<cdots\>*\<partial\><rsub|A<rsub|q>>*f<rsub|a><around|(|U|)>,<label|eq:ex-lattice-price-derivative-sum>
  </equation>

  where the series converges absolutely. The proof is by induction on
  <math|q>; the case <math|q=0> is <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-price-presented-sum>.
  For the induction step, let <math|W<around|(|t|)>> be the increment curve
  through <math|U> in the direction <math|A<rsub|1>>. Since <math|\<cal-D\>>
  is open, <math|W<around|(|t|)>\<in\>\<cal-D\>> for
  <math|<around|\||t|\|>\<less\>r> with some <math|r\<gtr\>0>. On this
  interval the functions <math|v<rsub|a><around|(|t|)>=\<partial\><rsub|A<rsub|2>>*\<cdots\>*\<partial\><rsub|A<rsub|q>>*f<rsub|a><around|(|W<around|(|t|)>|)>>
  are differentiable by regularity of the terms on healthy configurations,
  their derivatives are <math|\<partial\><rsub|A<rsub|1>>*\<cdots\>*\<partial\><rsub|A<rsub|q>>*f<rsub|a><around|(|W<around|(|t|)>|)>>,
  and these are bounded by <math|u<rsub|a>> uniformly in <math|t>, with
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>u<rsub|a>\<less\>\<infty\>>. The series
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>v<rsub|a><around|(|0|)>> converges. The theorem on
  termwise differentiation of a series whose derivatives are dominated by a
  summable sequence on an interval therefore shows that
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>v<rsub|a>> is differentiable at <math|0> with
  derivative <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>v<rsub|a><rprime|'><around|(|0|)>>. By
  the induction hypothesis, applied at every point <math|W<around|(|t|)>>,
  <math|<around|\||t|\|>\<less\>r>, the sum
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>v<rsub|a><around|(|t|)>> equals
  <math|\<partial\><rsub|A<rsub|2>>*\<cdots\>*\<partial\><rsub|A<rsub|q>>*g<around|(|W<around|(|t|)>|)>>;
  differentiating at <math|t=0> gives <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-price-derivative-sum>.

  Now fix the finite set <math|D> and let
  <math|G<rsub|D>=<big|sum><rsub|a\<in\>D>f<rsub|a>>. The finite sum
  <math|G<rsub|D>> has zero probe: every term has the vanishing property just
  stated, and a finite sum may be differentiated termwise. By
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-price-derivative-sum> at the identity configuration,
  every ordered derivative of <math|g-G<rsub|D>> of order at most <math|d>
  along normalized directions is the sum of the corresponding derivatives of
  the omitted nonwrapping terms and of the wrapping terms, so it is bounded
  by <math|<big|sum><rsub|a\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|0>\<setminus\>D>u<rsub|a>+<big|sum><rsub|a\<nin\>\<cal-I\><rsub|0>>u<rsub|a>>.
  The polarization estimate stated with the definition of <math|M<rsub|d>>
  then bounds the difference between the probe of the full presented field
  and the zero probe of the truncation <math|G<rsub|D>>. This estimate is
  uniform in finite <math|D>, because the local section has order-<math|d>
  regularity and the source terms have summable sup-bound mass. If the probe
  has order <math|r>, the chain rule expands its local link variation into
  ordered partitions of <math|<around|{|1,\<ldots\>,r|}>>. Let
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|\<tau\>,q>> be the price of a local logarithmic
  variation of order <math|q>; the finite sum of the products
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|A\<in\>\<cal-P\>>\<lambda\><rsub|\<tau\>,<around|\||A|\|>>>
  over these partitions, with the zeroth-order term included, is
  <math|\<pi\><around|(|\<tau\>|)>>. Supremum bounds on the omitted terms and
  the triangle inequality therefore give

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||R<rsub|\<tau\>>*<around|(|P.*x-Q.*x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<pi\><around|(|\<tau\>|)>*M<rsub|d>*<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|a\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|0>\<setminus\>D>u<rsub|a>+<big|sum><rsub|a\<nin\>\<cal-I\><rsub|0>>u<rsub|a>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|u<rsub|a>> is the sup-bound of the corresponding signed regular
  term. The remainder certificates give <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>M<rsub|B<rsub|0>>*<around|(|X<rsub|a>,u<rsub|a>|)>\<leqslant\>a+b>,
  so <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>u<rsub|a>> is finite as well. For the wrapping
  terms, the elementary support inequality

  <\equation*>
    10*<around*|(|h*<around|(|4*B<rsub|0>+2|)>+2*B<rsub|0>|)>\<less\>n,<space|1em>0\<in\>X,<space|1em>X<text|
    wrapping>,<space|1em>W\<geqslant\>0<space|1em>\<Longrightarrow\><space|1em>W\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*h>*M<rsub|B<rsub|0>>*<around|(|X,W|)>
  </equation*>

  follows from the lower bound on the cover radius of a wrapping support.
  Absolute summability of <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>u<rsub|a>> justifies the
  tail limit and permits the finite sets <math|D> to increase through all of
  <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|0>>. The nonwrapping sup-bound tail tends to zero, and
  the wrapping sup-bound sum is at most <math|e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*h>*<around|(|a+b|)>>;
  taking this limit gives the probe estimate

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||R<rsub|\<tau\>>*<around|(|P.*x-Q.*x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<pi\><around|(|\<tau\>|)>*M<rsub|d>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*h>*<around|(|a+b|)>.
  </equation*>

  This is the truncation-and-tail argument. The probe is applied termwise
  only to the finite truncations, where the vanishing property is available;
  the infinite collection of omitted and wrapping terms enters only through
  the derivative identity <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-price-derivative-sum> and the
  resulting sup-bound.

  The coefficient recovery inequality is

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>T<rsub|d>*<big|sum><rsub|\<tau\>\<in\>\<cal-Q\><rsub|d>><around|\||R<rsub|\<tau\>><around|(|z|)>|\|>.
  </equation*>

  It follows from finite-dimensionality: the probes jointly separate
  coefficients, so their maximum is a norm on the coefficient space;
  compactness of its unit sphere gives a finite comparison constant, and a
  maximum is bounded by the sum. Summing the probe estimate over the finite
  family gives

  <\equation>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|P.*x-Q.*x|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<frak-C\><rsub|d>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*h>*<around|(|a+b|)>,<space|2em>\<frak-C\><rsub|d>=T<rsub|d>*M<rsub|d>*<big|sum><rsub|\<tau\>\<in\>\<cal-Q\><rsub|d>>\<pi\><around|(|\<tau\>|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-local-uniqueness>
  </equation>

  Take the torus side length <math|n> so that
  <math|10*<around|(|h*<around|(|4*B<rsub|0>+2|)>+2*B<rsub|0>|)>\<less\>n>.
  Then every probe fits in the region of nonwrapping supports. This proves
  the required local uniqueness estimate from coefficient recovery, local
  vanishing, the exponential support tail, and absolute summation.

  For the insertion itself, first decompose the next-level density into its
  primary term, its countable regular terms, and its countable forced terms.
  The primary term has only finitely many coefficient coordinates. The
  regular series is absolutely summable by its sup-bound mass budget. The
  forced series is absolutely summable after division by the positive factor
  <math|y=<sqrt|<math-up|forcedAllowance><rsub|j+1>>>, using the forced part
  of the same remainder certificate. Write the regular mass sum as <math|a>
  and the forced mass sum as <math|y*b>, where <math|a,b\<geqslant\>0> and
  <math|a+b\<leqslant\>r<rsub|j+1>> by the remainder certificate. For a
  regular term of volume mass <math|m<rsub|a>>, the paid atom costs are
  <math|s<rsub|j>*m<rsub|a>> and <math|\<rho\><rsub|j>*m<rsub|a>>; for a
  forced term of mass <math|m<rsub|a>>, they are <math|s<rsub|j>*m<rsub|a>/y>
  and <math|\<rho\><rsub|j>*m<rsub|a>/y>. Thus the combined regular and
  forced costs are bounded by <math|s<rsub|j>*r<rsub|j+1>> and
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|j>*r<rsub|j+1>>, respectively. The primary coefficient
  atom contributes the finite coordinate costs
  <math|s<rsub|j><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|j+1><rsup|\<circ\>>|\<\|\|\>>> and
  <math|P<rsub|j><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-V\><rsub|L<rsub|j>><around|(|x<rsub|j+1><rsup|\<circ\>>|)>|\<\|\|\>>>.
  Summing first over the finite coefficient basis, then over the countable
  regular and forced lists, is legitimate because their total costs and
  coefficient norms are summable. The interchange of the countable sums with
  the integral needs a bound for the factors multiplying the atoms in the
  density, and this is not a consequence of compactness of the link variables
  alone. Two properties of the fine effective representation are used; both
  are part of its representation certificate and are inputs here. First, its
  effective density is measurable and bounded. Second, its vacuum activities
  <math|K<rsub|j>>, <math|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, have a summable budget: there
  are <math|a<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>0> with <math|<big|sum><rsub|j>a<rsub|j>\<less\>\<infty\>>
  and <math|<around|\||K<rsub|j><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|j>> for
  all <math|U>. The multiplier that dresses the <math|i>-th forced insertion
  is the sum, over the finite admissible index sets <math|b\<ni\>i>, of
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>b\<setminus\><around|{|i|}>>K<rsub|j><around|(|U|)>>.
  Erasing the index <math|i> is injective on such sets, so every dressed
  multiplier is bounded, uniformly in <math|i> and <math|U>, by

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|<math-up|gas>>=<big|sum><rsub|b\<subset\>\<bbb-N\><math-up|finite>><big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>b>a<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|j>a<rsub|j>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>.
  </equation*>

  Hence a summable majorant <math|\<beta\><rsub|i>> for the forced insertions
  gives the summable majorant <math|C<rsub|<math-up|gas>>*\<beta\><rsub|i>>
  for their dressed versions, and the same absolute summability justifies
  rearranging the sum over marked index sets into the sum of dressed
  insertions. The absolute integrand of the countable atomic sum is therefore
  bounded by the bound of the effective density times a summable majorant.
  This is an integrable bound on the normalized finite-volume Haar space, so
  Fubini preserves the density identity. Thus the countable atomic sum has
  the pushed density. Denote its actual coefficient by
  <math|<wide|x|~><rsub|j>>. The coordinate leads sum to
  <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|j><around|(|x<rsub|j+1>|)>>, and the paid insertion
  estimate gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<wide|x|~><rsub|j>-\<cal-L\><rsub|j><around|(|x<rsub|j+1>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|j>*<around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|j+1><rsup|\<circ\>>|\<\|\|\>>+r<rsub|j+1>|)>>.
  Its coarse remainder budget is at most <math|P<rsub|j>\<\|\|\>\<cal-V\><rsub|L<rsub|j>><around|(|x<rsub|j+1><rsup|\<circ\>>|)>\<\|\|\>+\<rho\><rsub|j>*r<rsub|j+1>>.
  This is the content of the countable summation argument: dominatedness and
  absolute summability justify both the density identity and the interchange
  of summation with integration.

  Compare this intermediate coefficient with the actual coefficient at rung
  <math|j> using the local uniqueness estimate
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-local-uniqueness>. Their local densities agree by the
  paid comparison's density identity and the insertion's pushed-density
  identity. Choose <math|h> so that <math|\<frak-C\><rsub|d>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*h>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|j>>,
  then choose the torus side long enough for the displayed support
  inequality. The triangle inequality gives exactly
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-price-one-step>, uniformly over the allowed fields.

  For <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-price-spends>, coefficient coordinates have grades
  <math|r=0,1,\<ldots\>,d>, so each weight
  <math|L<rsub|j><rsup|d-r>\<geqslant\>1> when <math|L<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1>.
  Consequently the weighted coordinate norm dominates the original norm. The
  legal block cap then controls that weighted norm by <math|P<rsub|j>> times
  the norm after insertion. This is the size estimate for the admissible
  leading map; it is not merely the inequality <math|L<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|One-step propagation><label|lem:ex-lattice-price-one-step>Suppose
    the adjacent data satisfy the local hypotheses above and, for some
    <math|t\<geqslant\>0>,

    <\equation*>
      r<rsub|j>+<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><frac|t|\<Gamma\><rsub|j>>,<space|2em>r<rsub|j+1>+<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|j+1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><frac|t|\<Gamma\><rsub|j+1>>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>t*I<rsub|j>.
    </equation*>

    Then

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|j+1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>t*I<rsub|j+1>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The projection to nonconstant coordinates is contractive, so the
    paid-cone bound gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|j+1><rsup|\<circ\>>|\<\|\|\>>+r<rsub|j+1>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|j+1>|\<\|\|\>>+r<rsub|j+1>\<leqslant\>t/\<Gamma\><rsub|j+1>>.
    It also gives

    <\equation*>
      r<rsub|j>+P<rsub|j>\<\|\|\>\<cal-V\><rsub|L<rsub|j>><around|(|x<rsub|j+1><rsup|\<circ\>>|)>\<\|\|\>+\<rho\><rsub|j>*r<rsub|j+1>\<leqslant\>t*<around*|(|\<Gamma\><rsub|j><rsup|-1>+<frac|P<rsub|j>*L<rsub|j><rsup|d>+\<rho\><rsub|j>|\<Gamma\><rsub|j+1>>|)>=t*H<rsub|j>.
    </equation*>

    Apply <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-price-one-step> and then
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-price-spends>. The triangle inequality yields

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|j+1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|j>*<around*|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>+s<rsub|j>*<frac|t|\<Gamma\><rsub|j+1>>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|j>*t*H<rsub|j>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Using <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>t*I<rsub|j>>,
    <math|s<rsub|j>=b<rsub|j>/\<kappa\><rsub|j+1>>, and the definition of
    <math|I<rsub|j+1>>, the right side is

    <\equation*>
      t*I<rsub|j+1>*<around*|(|<frac|P<rsub|j>*I<rsub|j>|I<rsub|j+1>>+P<rsub|j>*b<rsub|j>+<frac|1|10>|)>\<leqslant\>t*I<rsub|j+1>*<around*|(|<frac|1|2>+<frac|1|6>+<frac|1|10>|)>=<frac|23|30>*t*I<rsub|j+1>\<leqslant\>t*I<rsub|j+1>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|P<rsub|j>*b<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>1/6> follows from the fourth
    inequality in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-price-budget>. This proves the claim,
    including the degenerate case <math|t=0>.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Finite endpoint price estimate><label|prop:ex-lattice-trace-sharp-read>Assume
    the selected history satisfies the scale inequalities
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-price-budget> for every
    <math|m\<leqslant\>j\<less\>k>. Let a paid comparison have coefficient
    vectors <math|x<rsub|j>>, remainder costs <math|r<rsub|j>>, and marked
    sites at the spatial origin for <math|m\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>k>.
    Assume its left coefficient is zero, <math|x<rsub|m>=0>, and that its
    adjacent pairs satisfy the local estimates
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-price-one-step> and
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-price-spends>. Assume also the paid direction
    conditions of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:reference-cone>, the room
    and bank inequalities <eqref|eq:reference-bank>, and the terminal spread
    bound <eqref|eq:reference-terminal> with endpoint budget
    <math|w\<geqslant\>0> and with the column allowances <math|c<rsub|j>>.
    Then, for all period indices and volume indices for which the local
    one-step estimates hold at every rung <math|m\<leqslant\>j\<less\>k>,

    <\equation>
      \<kappa\><rsub|k><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>4*<around|(|1+c<rsub|k>|)>*w.<label|eq:ex-lattice-price-endpoint>
    </equation>

    If, as in the selected construction, the one-step estimates hold for all
    period indices <math|n\<geqslant\>n<rsub|j>>, uniformly in the volume
    index <math|N\<geqslant\>k> and in the comparison data, then the endpoint
    estimate holds for all <math|n\<geqslant\>n<rsub|0>=max
    <around|(|<around|{|0|}>\<cup\><around|{|n<rsub|j>:m\<leqslant\>j\<less\>k|}>|)>>
    (the set of rungs is empty when <math|m=k>, where the estimate is
    <math|x<rsub|k>=x<rsub|m>=0>), uniformly in <math|N\<geqslant\>k>, in the
    paid comparison and in <math|w\<geqslant\>0>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    We first verify that the comparison satisfies the discounted paid-cone
    bound used in the recurrence. In the canonical cone estimate, choose at
    rung <math|j> the worth coefficient <math|K<rsub|j>=K*<around|(|J*<around|(|j+1|)>|)>>,
    the column allowance equal to the selected column allowance
    <math|c<rsub|j>> of the history, and the bank comparison coefficient
    <math|M<rsub|j>=K*<around|(|J*<around|(|j+1|)>+1|)>>. The scale-slow
    condition gives <math|K<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>K*<around|(|J<around|(|j|)>+1|)>>,
    so the room inequality yields <math|64*K<rsub|j><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|j>>.
    The bank condition is exactly <math|100000*M<rsub|j><rsup|6>*<around|(|1+c<rsub|j+1>|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>p<rsub|j>>,
    and monotonicity gives <math|M<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>K<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1>.
    The paid direction in that cone is the adjacent coefficient comparison
    with diagonal weights <math|L<rsub|j><rsup|d-r>> and loss
    <math|p<rsub|j><rsup|-3>>; this is the direction hypothesis assumed here.
    The zero left coefficient and terminal spread are precisely its boundary
    conditions. Therefore equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:reference-gain> gives

    <\equation>
      \<Gamma\><rsub|j>*<around|(|r<rsub|j>+<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>|)>\<leqslant\>4*<around|(|1+c<rsub|k>|)>*w*<space|2em><around|(|m\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>k|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-price-cone>
    </equation>

    Put <math|t=4*<around|(|1+c<rsub|k>|)>*w>. Since <math|x<rsub|m>=0>, we
    have <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>>=0\<leqslant\>t*I<rsub|m>>.
    Suppose inductively that <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>t*I<rsub|j>>.
    The paid-cone bound gives <math|r<rsub|j>+<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>t/\<Gamma\><rsub|j>>
    and <math|r<rsub|j+1>+<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|j+1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>t/\<Gamma\><rsub|j+1>>.
    In particular the local remainder allowances satisfy the two bounds in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-price-one-step>. That lemma gives
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|j+1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>t*I<rsub|j+1>>.
    Finite induction reaches <math|j=k>, where <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|k>=1>, so
    <math|I<rsub|k>=1/\<kappa\><rsub|k>>. Multiplying by
    <math|\<kappa\><rsub|k>> gives <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-price-endpoint>. The
    finite intersection of the period thresholds for <math|j=m,\<ldots\>,k-1>
    is still an eventual set. Each adjacent estimate is uniform over the
    volume index and the specified comparison data, so one threshold works
    for all of them.
  </proof>

  Thus the endpoint estimate combines the already proved paid-cone argument
  of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:reference-cone> with the one-step
  quantitative price recurrence proved here. The only new induction is the
  finite propagation from the zero left coefficient to the terminal rung.

  <subsection|Choosing the local paid budgets><label|sec:ex-lattice-local-paid-budgets>

  The one-step insertion uses two kinds of local input: a finite list of
  coefficient-recovery probes, and positive budgets for the basis, regular,
  and forced atoms. The first choice is elementary once the probe list is
  finite. The eventual threshold can then be made monotone in the scale and
  uniform over all admissible adjacent levels. This section proves those two
  selection steps and then matches their budgets to the actual basis, regular
  and forced insertions. Those estimates supply the one-step recurrence of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-price-recurrence>.

  Fix a finite set <math|\<cal-T\>> of probes and nonnegative constants
  <math|O,H,W,G<rsub|d>,a,c>, where <math|O> is the motion-average norm
  factor, <math|H> the local readout factor, <math|W> the test factor,
  <math|G<rsub|d>> the polarization factor, <math|a> the block weight, and
  <math|c> the tail-decay factor. For each probe <math|\<tau\>>, let
  <math|\<pi\><rsub|\<tau\>>\<geqslant\>0> be its variation price and define
  its local coefficient

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|\<tau\>>=4*a*G<rsub|d>*\<pi\><rsub|\<tau\>>+2*a.
  </equation*>

  Given a target price <math|y\<gtr\>0>, the required atom budget
  inequalities are

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|q<rsub|\<tau\>>*v>|<cell|\<leqslant\>u>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|\<tau\>\<in\>\<cal-T\>|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-atom-cycle>>>|<row|<cell|O<around*|(|W*u+2*H*a*v|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>y,>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-atom-regular>>>|<row|<cell|4*O*H*a*v>|<cell|\<leqslant\>y,>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-atom-large>>>|<row|<cell|O<around*|(|W*v+2*H*a*v*c|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>y.>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-atom-main>>>>>
  </align>

  The two outputs <math|u,v> are the probe and summability budgets. In the
  Wilson application, at coefficient grade <math|d> and block side <math|L>,
  the constants are specialized as follows: <math|\<cal-T\>> is the finite
  coefficient-recovery probe family <math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|d>> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-price-recurrence> and
  <math|W=T<rsub|d>> its reconstruction constant; <math|G<rsub|d>> is the
  polarization constant <math|M<rsub|d>> and
  <math|\<pi\><rsub|\<tau\>>=\<pi\><around|(|\<tau\>|)>\<geqslant\>1> the
  probe price defined there; <math|O> is the orbit-average cost and <math|H>
  the readout price of the grade-<math|d> coefficient frame;
  <math|a=L<rsup|d>>; and <math|c> is the credibility of the fine profile,
  that is, its uniform mass bound for a normalized frame-column insertion,
  fixed by its support exponent, order and power. This is the constant called
  <math|h> in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-eve-producer>.
  With these values <math|q<rsub|\<tau\>>> bounds the local variation
  coefficient, while the other three inequalities pay respectively for
  regular terms, large-support terms, and the primary atom. The same number
  <math|v> is later used as the summability budget in all of the local atom
  estimates of one transition; the budgets are not chosen separately for each
  estimate.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Positive local budget choice><label|lem:ex-lattice-local-budget-choice>For
    every finite probe family and every <math|y\<gtr\>0>, there are
    <math|u,v\<gtr\>0> satisfying <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-atom-cycle>\U<eqref|eq:ex-lattice-atom-main>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Set

    <\equation*>
      K=<big|sum><rsub|\<tau\>\<in\>\<cal-T\>>q<rsub|\<tau\>>,<space|2em>B=max
      <around|{|1,O<around|[|W*<around|(|1+K|)>+2*H*a|]>,4*O*H*a,O<around|[|W+2*H*a*c|]>|}>.
    </equation*>

    These are finite nonnegative numbers. Choose

    <\equation*>
      v=<frac|y|2*B>,<space|2em>u=<around|(|1+K|)>*v.
    </equation*>

    Both are strictly positive. Since <math|q<rsub|\<tau\>>\<leqslant\>K\<leqslant\>1+K>,
    <math|q<rsub|\<tau\>>*v\<leqslant\>u>. The left side of the regular-term
    inequality is <math|v*O<around|[|W*<around|(|1+K|)>+2*H*a|]>\<leqslant\>v*B=y/2>.
    The large-support and primary left sides are respectively
    <math|4*O*H*a*v\<leqslant\>B*v=y/2> and
    <math|v*O<around|[|W+2*H*a*c|]>\<leqslant\>B*v=y/2>. Each is therefore at
    most <math|y>, as required.
  </proof>

  We next record the uniform threshold selection used to turn fixed-parameter
  estimates into a single scale profile. Here <math|j> is a stage index of
  the selected stage data, with profile orders
  <math|<math-up|order><rsub|i>>, powers <math|<math-up|power><rsub|j>> and
  first stage <math|i<rsub|0>>. At a stage <math|j>, the admissible pairs of
  an adjacent stage <math|i> and a coefficient grade <math|d> form the set

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-I\><rsub|j>=<around*|{|<around|(|i,d|)>:i\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>i+1,i<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>i,d+1\<leqslant\><math-up|order><rsub|i>,d\<leqslant\><math-up|power><rsub|j>,d+2\<leqslant\>j|}>.
  </equation*>

  The adjacent stage is <math|j> itself or its immediate predecessor, so
  there are at most two, and <math|d+2\<leqslant\>j> confines the grades to
  the finite set <math|<around|{|0,\<ldots\>,j-1|}>>. Thus
  <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|j>> is finite, and only finitely many eventual
  requirements occur at each fixed <math|j>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Monotone threshold envelope><label|lem:ex-lattice-monotone-threshold>Let
    <math|b<rsub|j>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> be any prescribed lower threshold. For
    each <math|j>, let <math|S<rsub|j>> be a finite set and, for each
    <math|s\<in\>S<rsub|j>>, let <math|E<rsub|j,s>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> have the
    property that a desired conclusion <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|j,s><around|(|x|)>>
    holds whenever <math|x\<geqslant\>E<rsub|j,s>>. Then there is a
    nondecreasing sequence <math|B<rsub|j>> such that
    <math|B<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>b<rsub|j>> and, for every
    <math|s\<in\>S<rsub|j>>, <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|j,s><around|(|x|)>> holds
    whenever <math|x\<geqslant\>B<rsub|j>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Define

    <\equation*>
      e<rsub|j>=max <around*|(|<around|{|b<rsub|j>|}>\<cup\><around|{|E<rsub|j,s>:s\<in\>S<rsub|j>|}>|)>,<space|2em>B<rsub|j>=max<rsub|0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>j>
      e<rsub|r>.
    </equation*>

    The maxima exist because the sets are finite and nonempty after adjoining
    <math|b<rsub|j>>. The prefix maximum <math|B<rsub|j>> is nondecreasing
    and dominates <math|b<rsub|j>>. If <math|s\<in\>S<rsub|j>>, then
    <math|E<rsub|j,s>\<leqslant\>e<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|j>>, so every
    <math|x\<geqslant\>B<rsub|j>> satisfies the stipulated conclusion.
  </proof>

  Apply this lemma at each stage <math|j> with
  <math|S<rsub|j>=\<cal-I\><rsub|j>>. Let a positive target price
  <math|y<rsub|j,i>> be prescribed for every pair of stages. For each fixed
  <math|<around|(|i,d|)>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|j>>, the atom-budget lemma, with
  <math|y=y<rsub|j,i>>, first supplies positive budgets <math|u,v>; for these
  fixed budgets, the finite-volume local estimates then supply an eventual
  threshold <math|E<rsub|j,<around|(|i,d|)>>> in the scale variable <math|x>
  for the corresponding primary, regular, and forced atom conclusions. These
  local estimates require, in addition, that the smallness parameter
  <math|\<varepsilon\>> attached to the transition <math|<around|(|j,i|)>> is
  at most <math|1/4>. Taking the finite maximum and then the prefix maximum
  gives one monotone threshold profile that works simultaneously for every
  adjacent transition and every permitted coefficient grade. This is the
  uniformization step needed before the scale induction. The atom estimates
  to which this uniformization applies are established and matched below.

  <subsubsection|The three atom estimates used in the
  recurrence><label|subsec:trace-paid-atom-suppliers>

  We verify the estimates for the selected transition, with the same budget
  choices <math|u,v> as above. Put <math|\<rho\>=L<rsup|-2/5>> and
  <math|\<chi\>=<sqrt|H<around|(|x|)>>>. The selected response package is
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-eve-producer>, with its probe and
  cloud bounds proved in Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:trace-eve-probe-cloud>.
  In particular, every cloud of fine mass <math|m> has charge at most
  <math|v*m/\<chi\>> on a common tail. The two real/imaginary channel charges
  may be equal; they are nevertheless counted twice.

  <subparagraph|Regular atoms.> For a regular source of mass <math|m>, use
  the actual small- and large-support constructions in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:trace-regular-chart-construction>. In the
  small case the local probe charge is at most
  <math|q<rsub|\<tau\>>*v*m/\<chi\>\<leqslant\>u*m/\<chi\>> for every
  recovery probe. Coefficient recovery costs <math|W>, and the two cloud
  readouts cost at most <math|2*H*a*v*m/\<chi\>>. Including the motion factor
  <math|O> gives

  <\equation*>
    <frac|m|\<chi\>>*O<around|(|W*u+2*H*a*v|)>\<leqslant\><frac|y*m|\<chi\>>.
  </equation*>

  In the large case each of the two chart terms and each of the two cloud
  terms is at most <math|v*m/\<chi\>>. Hence the total is
  <math|4*O*H*a*v*m/\<chi\>\<leqslant\>y*m/\<chi\>>.
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-regular-source-atom> gives the
  associated presentations, their exact pushed density, and remainder cost at
  most <math|\<rho\>*m> with the prescribed coarse forced normalization. Its
  derivative and vanishing-jet assumptions hold for the regular atoms of the
  effective-representation certificate; they are not inferred from a
  pointwise bound. Thus the cost pair is <math|<around|(|y*m/\<chi\>,\<rho\>*m|)>>.

  <subparagraph|Basis atoms.> Let <math|e<rsub|\<alpha\>>> be a nonconstant
  normalized basis column, and insert its actual local section with the fine
  threshold. The cloud mass in its primary correction is bounded by the
  fine-profile credibility <math|c>. The primary expansion and coefficient
  extraction are those of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:primary-calibration-producer>.
  Choose the response package at price <math|v>. For a recovery probe
  <math|p>, compare the actual principal row first to its Gaussian reference,
  then to its cropped deterministic prediction, then to its full polynomial
  forecast. The first two comparisons, in the two real/imaginary channels,
  have ordered derivative error at most <math|2*<around|(|e\|u<around|(|x|)>\|+\<delta\><rsub|x>|)>>:
  the insertion bound supplies <math|e<around|\||u<around|(|x|)>|\|>> in each
  channel, and the cap bound supplies <math|\<delta\><rsub|x>> for the
  fluctuation-to-prediction comparison. Polarization and the derivative
  prices of the probe curve cost <math|\<pi\><around|(|p|)>*\<Lambda\>>;
  multiplication by the physical factor costs <math|L<rsup|d>>. The
  cropped-to-full comparison costs the additional <math|\<delta\><rsub|x>> by
  <eqref|eq:trace-eve-probe-envelope>. Thus the actual principal probe error
  is bounded by

  <\equation*>
    \<pi\><around|(|p|)>*\<Lambda\>*L<rsup|d>*<space|0.17em>2*<around|(|e\|u<around|(|x|)>\|+\<delta\><rsub|x>|)>+\<delta\><rsub|x>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|x>\<leqslant\>v/\<chi\>,
  </equation*>

  exactly the charged expression of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-eve-producer>.
  The Gaussian-to-prediction comparison is the normalized derivative
  comparison of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:primary-calibration-producer>;
  the cap pays its Gaussian moment before any probe or column is chosen. Its
  full predicted coefficient is the leading map of that same section, with
  both the amplitude-degree and spatial-grade factors retained. The other
  rows have summed readout charge at most
  <math|H*a*<around|(|\<cal-M\>+\<cal-M\>|)>\<leqslant\>2*H*a*v*c/\<chi\>> by
  the cloud bound. The coefficient-extraction identity is linear; the finite
  recovery map costs <math|W>, and its motionwise norm costs <math|O>. The
  error from the prescribed leading coefficient is therefore at most

  <\equation*>
    <frac|1|\<chi\>>*O<around|(|W*v+2*H*a*v*c|)>\<leqslant\><frac|y|\<chi\>>.
  </equation*>

  The graded principal remainder and the local splitting are proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:primary-calibration-producer>: for a column
  of spatial grade <math|g<rsub|\<alpha\>>> they cost at most
  <math|P*L<rsup|d-g<rsub|\<alpha\>>>>, with the fixed frame ledger constant
  <math|P>. The splitting preserves the exact density by
  <eqref|eq:local-atom-identity>; it subtracts precisely the extracted local
  section and puts the residual in the regular and forced lists. It also
  preserves order-zero format, since the residual has zero identity value and
  zero nonwrapping test jets. Covariance of extraction under each lattice
  motion gives presentations of the motion of one coefficient, rather than
  unrelated coefficients for different motions. The unit column has its exact
  constant transport and no error. By finite linearity, a coefficient
  <math|z> consequently has error at most
  <math|<around|(|y/\<chi\>|)><around|\<\|\|\>|z<rsup|\<circ\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>
  and remainder at most <math|P*<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-V\><rsub|L>*z<rsup|\<circ\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
  where <math|<around|(|\<cal-V\><rsub|L>*z|)><rsub|\<alpha\>>=L<rsup|d-g<rsub|\<alpha\>>>*z<rsub|\<alpha\>>>.

  <subparagraph|Forced atoms.> Let a forced source have fine mass
  <math|m\<geqslant\>0>, and set <math|g=<sqrt|<math-up|forcedAllowance><rsub|<math-up|fine>>>\<in\><around|(|0,1|]>>.
  We use the localized expansion with its marked profile and
  address-background bounds from Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:leaf-transfer>,
  with the small bills funded as in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:projection-payment-producer>.
  These are the hypotheses of the projection calculation for the actual
  transition. For a forced insertion the principal cluster is zero: its flag
  is incompatible with the healthy principal term. In the projection
  calculation of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:leaf-transfer> we may
  therefore set its principal price <math|V> to zero. The two remaining bills
  and the hit-width condition are paid in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:projection-payment-producer>. With the
  notation there, <math|e<rsub|0>=<around|(|\<rho\>/H<rsub|d>|)>*m>, so the
  motionwise coefficient cost and the normalized coarse remainder cost are
  both at most <math|H<rsub|d>*e<rsub|0>=\<rho\>*m>. The exact density and
  order-zero format are preserved by that same splitting calculation. The
  flagged profile identity there is required; equality of scalar densities
  alone would not justify it.

  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-medal-gas-price> gives
  <math|\<rho\>*g\<leqslant\>y/\<chi\>> eventually. Consequently

  <\equation*>
    \<rho\>*m\<leqslant\><frac|y|\<chi\>>*<frac|m|g>,<space|2em>\<rho\>*m\<leqslant\>\<rho\>*<frac|m|g>.
  </equation*>

  These are the required forced cost pair, including <math|m=0>. The first
  inequality uses the fine allowance in <math|g>; the remainder on the left
  already uses the coarse forced normalization supplied by the projection. No
  identification of those two allowances is made.

  All statements concern the actual two effective representations of one
  transition. The finite basis sum and the absolutely summable regular and
  forced lists are combined in the density-summation argument of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-price-recurrence>; the
  bounded-density and summable-activity estimates there justify the
  interchange with integration. At a fixed stage and grade there are only
  finitely many probe, basis, channel and budget requirements. Their
  thresholds can therefore be included in the finite maximum above, before
  the period, volume or source is chosen. This is the promised source of the
  eventual local estimates, not an additional choice of an atom-dependent
  threshold.

  <subsection|Rate conversion and the local scale
  budget><label|sec:ex-lattice-trace-local-rate-selection>

  This section records the quantitative selection after the Fourier-tail and
  jet comparison in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-row-jet-estimates>.
  It proves the finite-rate summation used to select the probe estimate and
  the scalar domination used to choose the local chart budget. The strip,
  growth, weighted-vanishing, chart, and support receipts themselves remain
  the inputs identified in that section.

  Fix the block side <math|L>. Write

  <\gather*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|H<around|(|x|)>=<math-up|fineMonitor><around|(|x|)>,<space|1em>\<chi\><around|(|x|)>=<sqrt|H<around|(|x|)>>,<space|1em>\<beta\><around|(|x|)>=\<beta\><rsub|F><around|(|x|)>,>>|<row|<cell|R<around|(|x|)>=<math-up|radiusD><around|(|x|)>,<space|1em>\<rho\><around|(|x|)>=<math-up|reach><around|(|x|)>,<space|1em>b<around|(|x|)>=<math-up|radiusB><around|(|x|)>.>>>>
  </gather*>

  For fixed <math|L>, these are

  <\gather*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<beta\><around|(|x|)>=x+c<rsub|L>,<space|1em>b<around|(|x|)>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>,>>|<row|<cell|R<around|(|x|)>=b<around|(|x|)>*<around|(|4*b<around|(|x|)>+2|)>+2*b<around|(|x|)>,<space|1em>\<rho\><around|(|x|)>=45*<around|(|1+b<around|(|x|)>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|<math-up|depthW><rsub|L><around|(|R|)>=L*<around|(|R+2|)>,>>>>
  </gather*>

  where <math|c<rsub|L>> is fixed. Here <math|H<around|(|x|)>=exp
  <around|(|<around|(|log log \<beta\><around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|2>|)>>, so
  <math|H\<geqslant\>1>, <math|H\<to\>\<infty\>>, and <math|\<chi\>> is the
  monitor factor in the priced conclusion. For a function <math|f>, write
  <math|f\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsub|a>> if for all <math|v\<gtr\>a> one has
  eventually <math|<around|\||f<around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<beta\><around|(|x|)><rsup|v>>;
  the rate is thus defined by strict powers. Finite sums preserve a common
  rate, products add rates, and <math|\<chi\>\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsub|0>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The exponential tail pays the finite
    probe><label|lem:ex-lattice-tail-pays-probe>Let <math|d\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>,
    <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0>, and let <math|K,M> and the priced local
    derivative bound <math|\<Phi\>> be fixed nonnegative constants. Let
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|k><around|(|x|)>> and <math|C<rsub|1><around|(|x|)>>
    be the quantities of (<reference|eq:ex-lattice-local-delta>)\U(<reference|eq:ex-lattice-local-C1>),
    with <math|b=\<rho\><around|(|x|)>> and <math|m=R<around|(|x|)>>. Set

    <\equation>
      E<around|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|k=0><rsup|d>\<delta\><rsub|k><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>Z<rsub|d><around|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|d>#<math-up|Part><rsup|<math-up|ord>><around|(|j|)>*<space|0.17em>2<rsup|j>*E<around|(|x|)>*C<rsub|1><around|(|x|)><rsup|j>*<space|0.17em>\<Phi\>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-tail-probe-sum>
    </equation>

    Then <math|Z<rsub|d>\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsub|-1>>, and consequently

    <\equation>
      \<chi\><around|(|x|)>*Z<rsub|d><around|(|x|)>\<longrightarrow\>0.<label|eq:ex-lattice-tail-probe-price>
    </equation>

    In particular, for every <math|y\<gtr\>0>, eventually
    <math|Z<rsub|d><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>y/\<chi\><around|(|x|)>>. If the
    local receipts of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-strip-to-probe>
    hold and <math|E<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|1><around|(|x|)>>, every
    derivative order <math|j\<leqslant\>d> of the selected probe is bounded
    by <math|Z<rsub|d><around|(|x|)>*w>, where <math|w> is the derivative
    bound of the observable appearing in (<reference|eq:ex-lattice-jet-price>)
    and the price factor <math|\<Phi\>> has already been included in
    <math|Z<rsub|d>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    By the polynomial geometry receipts, <math|R> and the lift-size
    polynomial <math|<around|(|1+4*L*<around|(|R+2|)>|)><rsup|d*k>> have rate
    zero. The width cost and all other factors in <math|\<delta\><rsub|k>>,
    apart from <math|exp <around|(|-\<sigma\>*\<rho\><around|(|x|)>/2|)>>,
    are fixed constants or rate-zero polynomial factors. The reach cutoff has
    every negative rate: for each <math|a\<gtr\>0>, eventually <math|exp
    <around|(|-\<sigma\>*\<rho\><around|(|x|)>/2|)>\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rsup|-a>>.
    Thus each <math|\<delta\><rsub|k>>, and their finite sum <math|E>, have
    rate <math|-1> (the stronger arbitrary negative rate is more than
    needed). The finite sum in <math|C<rsub|1>> has rate zero. By the product
    and finite-sum rules, <math|Z<rsub|d>\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsub|-1>>.

    Since <math|\<chi\>\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsub|0>>, their product has rate
    <math|-1>. The strict-power definition then implies convergence to zero:
    choose any exponent <math|v> strictly between <math|-1> and <math|0>;
    eventually <math|<around|\||\<chi\>*Z<rsub|d>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rsup|v>>,
    and <math|\<beta\>\<to\>\<infty\>>. This proves
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-tail-probe-price> and the stated eventual price
    bound. The final assertion follows by applying
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-strip-to-probe> at each order
    and bounding its finite ordered-partition sum by
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-tail-probe-sum>.
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|Actual finite-rate receipts.> In the construction the
  polynomial direction-growth constants are

  <\equation*>
    h<rsub|k><around|(|x|)>=4*K*M*<space|0.17em>C<rsub|d*k><around|(|\<sigma\>|)>*<around*|(|1+4*L*<around|(|R<around|(|x|)>+2|)>|)><rsup|d*k>,<space|2em>C<rsub|1><around|(|x|)>=1+<big|sum><rsub|k=0><rsup|d>h<rsub|k><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  The error at order <math|k> is

  <\equation*>
    \<delta\><rsub|k><around|(|x|)>=8*K*M*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*\<rho\><around|(|x|)>/2>*<around|(|1+4*R<around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|d*k>*C<rsub|d*k>*<around|(|\<sigma\>/2|)>.
  </equation*>

  These formulas contain a factor four from the four edge components and an
  additional factor two from the two input arrays. The coefficient
  <math|E<around|(|x|)>> is required to be at most
  <math|C<rsub|1><around|(|x|)>> before applying the finite multilinear
  comparison. This is a separate eventual estimate; it follows because
  <math|E\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsub|-1>> and <math|C<rsub|1>\<geqslant\>1>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The selected local budget><label|prop:ex-lattice-selected-local-budget>Fix
    natural numbers <math|n,p,r> and a fixed positive integer block side
    <math|L>. Let

    <\equation*>
      C.*C\<geqslant\>1,<space|1em>C.*M\<geqslant\>1,<space|1em>D.*Q\<geqslant\>1,<space|1em>D.*K\<geqslant\>1,<space|1em>D.*Z\<geqslant\>1,<space|1em>D.*\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0
    </equation*>

    be fixed real constants, the scalar entries of the chart data <math|C>
    and of the local record <math|D>; the dotted notation records which datum
    each constant belongs to. These ranges are the only meaning of
    \Padmissible\Q below, and no other property of <math|C> and <math|D> is
    used except the nonnegative linear clock introduced below. Put

    <\equation*>
      P<rsub|n>=<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|n>#<math-up|Part><rsup|<math-up|ord>><around|(|j|)>,<space|2em><math-up|clinicFee><around|(|n,Q|)>=2*Q*<around|(|1+P<rsub|n>|)>,
    </equation*>

    and let <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|L>> be the finite index set
    <math|<math-up|Site><rsub|L>\<times\><math-up|Axis>\<times\>I<rsub|G>>,
    where <math|<math-up|Site><rsub|L>> is the set of sites of the periodic
    lattice of side <math|L> and <math|I<rsub|G>> is the finite set of colour
    coordinates, of cardinality <math|dim \<frak-g\>>. For <math|x> large,
    define the covering and response quantities

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|P<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>>|<cell|=<around|(|2<space|0.17em><math-up|depthW><rsub|L><around|(|R<around|(|x|)>|)>+1|)><rsup|4>,>>|<row|<cell|B<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>>|<cell|=<around|(|r+48|)>*<around|(|1+b<around|(|x|)>|)>*<around|(|1+P<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|A<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>>|<cell|=<math-up|boost><rsub|C,D,L><space|0.17em>B<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)><rsup|<space|0.17em>8+2*p+2*p*n>+<frac|16|D.*\<varepsilon\>>,>>|<row|<cell|S<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>>|<cell|=<math-up|clinicFee><around|(|n,D.*Q|)>*<around*|(|2*C.*C*<around|(|1+R<around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|p>|)><rsup|2>,>>|<row|<cell|U<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>>|<cell|=<around|(|A<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>+S<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>|)><space|0.17em><math-up|fiery><around|(|x|)>+<frac|2*A<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>+2|H<around|(|x|)>>,>>|<row|<cell|K<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>>|<cell|=1+<math-up|guests><rsub|G,D.*K><around|(|n+1,L|)>*B<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)><rsup|<space|0.17em>4*<around|(|1+4*<around|(|n+1|)>|)>>.>>>>
    </align*>

    These abbreviations use the coefficients

    <\equation>
      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|s<rsub|L>>|<cell|=1+16<space|0.17em>#\<cal-C\><rsub|L>,>>|<row|<cell|b<rsub|0>>|<cell|=<around|(|<math-up|clinicFee><around|(|n,D.*Q|)>+C.*M|)>*<around|(|2*C.*C|)><rsup|2>,>>|<row|<cell|<math-up|boost><rsub|C,D,L>>|<cell|=b<rsub|0>+<around|(|b<rsub|0>+1|)>*L<rsup|3>+2*n*s<rsub|L><rsup|2>*<around|(|D.*Z+1|)>*D.*Z,>>|<row|<cell|<math-up|guests><rsub|G,D.*K><around|(|q,L|)>>|<cell|=2*s<rsub|L><rsup|1+4*q><math-up|inflate><rsub|4*q><around|(|D.*K<rsup|-1>/8|)>,>>|<row|<cell|<math-up|meter><around|(|h,D.*K,m|)>>|<cell|=2*<around|(|1+h|)><rsup|m+1><math-up|inflate><rsub|m><around|(|D.*K<rsup|-1>/8|)>.>>>>
      </aligned>

      <label|eq:ex-lattice-scale-coefficients>
    </equation>

    Here <math|h> is the number of heads, the subscript of
    <math|<math-up|guests>> records its dependence on the gauge group
    (through <math|I<rsub|G>>) and on the scalar <math|D.*K>, and
    <math|<math-up|inflate><rsub|m><around|(|v|)>=<around|(|1+m/v|)><rsup|m>>
    is the Gaussian moment inflation factor. The scalar cost terms below are
    nonnegative by the stated ranges of the constants. The clock is a fixed
    function of the form <math|<math-up|clocks><around|(|t|)>=c<rsub|<math-up|clock>>*t>
    with <math|c<rsub|<math-up|clock>>=<math-up|clocks><around|(|1|)>\<geqslant\>0>.
    Let <math|\<beta\>\<geqslant\>\<beta\><around|(|x|)>/2>. Suppose the
    support and budget satisfy

    <\gather>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|B.<math-up|threshold>=<math-up|fiery><around|(|x|)>,<space|1em>0\<leqslant\>P.<math-up|threshold>\<leqslant\>2*B.<math-up|threshold>,>>|<row|<cell|B.*B\<leqslant\>2*b<around|(|x|)>,<space|1em>P.<math-up|power>=p,<space|1em><math-up|coverNumber><around|(|B.*B,X|)>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-budget-support>>>>>
    </gather>

    Put <math|F<rsub|0>=B.<math-up|threshold>>, <math|u=\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>>,
    <math|v<rsub|\<rho\>>=C.*C*<space|0.17em>P.<math-up|threshold><around|(|1+\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|p>>,
    <math|v<rsub|R>=C.*C*<space|0.17em>P.<math-up|threshold><around|(|1+R<around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|p>>,
    <math|h=<math-up|heads><around|(|B,X|)>>, and
    <math|<math-up|cup>=D.*Z*<around|(|1+<math-up|scope><around|(|B,X,r,x|)>|)><rsup|p*n>>.
    Define the displayed scalar cost by

    <\equation>
      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|F<around|(|x|)>=>|<cell|<around|(|<math-up|clinicFee><around|(|n,D.*Q|)>+C.*M|)>*v<rsub|\<rho\>><rsup|2>*<around|(|2+<math-up|breadth><around|(|B,X|)>|)><rsup|p*n>/F<rsub|0>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|+<frac|<math-up|clinicFee><around|(|n,D.*Q|)>*v<rsub|\<rho\>><rsup|2>+u|F<rsub|0>>*L<rsup|3>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|+n*<around|(|2*F<rsub|0>|)>*h<rsup|2>*<around|(|<math-up|cup>+1|)><math-up|cup>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|+<frac|16|D.*\<varepsilon\>>*<around|(|F<rsub|0>+u/F<rsub|0>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|+<frac|<math-up|clinicFee><around|(|n,D.*Q|)>*v<rsub|R><rsup|2>+u|F<rsub|0>>.>>>>
      </aligned>

      <label|eq:ex-lattice-explicit-local-cost>
    </equation>

    This is the response-plus-paint cost used by the local chart
    construction. It satisfies, eventually in <math|x>,

    <\equation>
      0\<leqslant\>F<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>U<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em><math-up|meter><around|(|<math-up|heads><around|(|B,X|)>,D.*K,4*<around|(|n+1|)>|)>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>,<label|eq:ex-lattice-budget-majorants>
    </equation>

    and

    <\equation>
      \<chi\><around|(|x|)>*U<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>*K<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>\<longrightarrow\>0.<label|eq:ex-lattice-budget-small>
    </equation>

    Consequently, for every <math|y\<gtr\>0>, eventually and uniformly in all
    data satisfying <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-budget-support>,

    <\equation>
      F<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em><math-up|clocks><around|(|t|)>*F<around|(|x|)><math-up|meter><around|(|<math-up|heads><around|(|B,X|)>,D.*K,4*<around|(|n+1|)>|)>\<leqslant\><frac|y|\<chi\><around|(|x|)>>*t*<space|1em><around|(|t\<geqslant\>0|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-budget-output>
    </equation>

    This is the scalar estimate consumed by the local regular-source branch.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    We give the domination and limiting arguments separately. First, the
    cover formula gives

    <\equation>
      1+\<rho\><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>,<space|1em>2+<math-up|breadth><around|(|B,X|)>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>,<space|1em>1+<math-up|scope><around|(|B,X,r,x|)>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-budget-cover>
    </equation>

    Write <math|a=b<around|(|x|)>> and <math|q=<math-up|coverNumber><around|(|B.*B,X|)>>.
    The definitions give

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|breadth><around|(|B,X|)>=q*<around|(|4*B.*B+2|)>+2*B.*B+8\<leqslant\>q*<around|(|8*a+2|)>+4*a+8,<space|1em><math-up|scope>=r*a+<math-up|breadth>+1.
    </equation*>

    With <math|S<rsub|X>=<around|(|r+48|)>*<around|(|1+a|)>*<around|(|1+q|)>>,
    direct expansion, using <math|a,q,r\<geqslant\>0>, gives
    <math|1+45*<around|(|1+a|)>\<leqslant\>S<rsub|X>>,
    <math|2+q*<around|(|8*a+2|)>+4*a+8\<leqslant\>S<rsub|X>>, and
    <math|r*a+q*<around|(|8*a+2|)>+4*a+10\<leqslant\>S<rsub|X>>. These are
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-budget-cover> with <math|S<rsub|X>> in place of
    <math|B<rsub|\<ast\>>>. Since <math|q\<leqslant\>P<rsub|\<ast\>>> and the
    bulk formula is increasing in its cover-count argument,
    <math|S<rsub|X>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|\<ast\>>>; hence
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-budget-cover> follows.

    Put <math|F<rsub|0>=B.<math-up|threshold>>,
    <math|u=g*S*t*e*p<around|(|\<beta\>|)>=\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>>, and
    <math|S<rsub|X>=<math-up|bulk><around|(|<math-up|coverNumber><around|(|B.*B,X|)>,r,x|)>>.
    Since <math|F<rsub|0>=\<beta\><around|(|x|)><rsup|-1/2>*H<around|(|x|)>>,
    (14) yields

    <\equation>
      <frac|u|F<rsub|0>>=<frac|1|H<around|(|x|)>>*<sqrt|<frac|\<beta\><around|(|x|)>|\<beta\>>>\<leqslant\><frac|<sqrt|2>|H<around|(|x|)>>\<leqslant\><frac|2|H<around|(|x|)>>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-u-over-F>
    </equation>

    The cover inequalities <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-budget-cover> and
    <math|P.<math-up|threshold>\<leqslant\>2*F<rsub|0>> give

    <\equation>
      <math-up|loom><around|(|P,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>,C.*C|)>\<leqslant\>2*C.*C*F<rsub|0>*S<rsub|X><rsup|p>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-loom-bound>
    </equation>

    Put <math|t=F<rsub|0>> and <math|v=<math-up|loom><around|(|P,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>,C.*C|)>>.
    The loom is <math|C.*C*<space|0.17em>P.<math-up|threshold><around|(|1+\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|p>>;
    (18) and <math|P.<math-up|threshold>\<leqslant\>2*t> give
    <math|v\<leqslant\>2*C.*C*t*S<rsub|X><rsup|p>>. The segment term is
    <math|<around|(|<math-up|clinicFee>+C.*M|)>*v<rsup|2><math-up|heft>/t>,
    where <math|<math-up|heft>=<around|(|2+<math-up|breadth>|)><rsup|p*n>\<leqslant\>S<rsub|X><rsup|p*n>>.
    Thus, with <math|b<rsub|0>=<around|(|<math-up|clinicFee>+C.*M|)>*<around|(|2*C.*C|)><rsup|2>>,

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|segment>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|0>*t*S<rsub|X><rsup|2*p+p*n>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|0>*\<zeta\>*<around|(|B,u|)>*S<rsub|X><rsup|8+2*p+2*p*n>.
    </equation*>

    The tranche term is <math|<around|(|<around|(|<math-up|clinicFee><space|0.17em>v<rsup|2>+u|)>/t|)>*L<rsup|3>>.
    Its first numerator part is bounded by
    <math|b<rsub|0>*t*S<rsub|X><rsup|2*p>>; its second is <math|u/t>. Since
    <math|t\<leqslant\>\<zeta\>*<around|(|B,u|)>=t+u/t>,
    <math|S<rsub|X>\<geqslant\>1>, and <math|2*p\<leqslant\>8+2*p+2*p*n>,
    this gives <math|<math-up|tranche>\<leqslant\><around|(|b<rsub|0>+1|)>*L<rsup|3>*\<zeta\>*<around|(|B,u|)>*S<rsub|X><rsup|8+2*p+2*p*n>>.

    For the scratch term, its exact factors are
    <math|n*<around|(|2*t|)><math-up|heads><rsup|2><around|(|<math-up|cup>+1|)><math-up|cup>>,
    where <math|<math-up|cup>=D.*Z*<around|(|1+<math-up|scope>|)><rsup|p*n>>.
    The head count is <math|<around|(|2<math-up|scope>+1|)><rsup|4>#\<cal-C\><rsub|L>>.
    From <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-budget-cover>,
    <math|1+<math-up|heads>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|L>*S<rsub|X><rsup|4>>, with
    <math|s<rsub|L>=1+16#\<cal-C\><rsub|L>>. Also
    <math|<math-up|cup>\<leqslant\>D.*Z*S<rsub|X><rsup|p*n>> and
    <math|<math-up|cup>+1\<leqslant\><around|(|D.*Z+1|)>*S<rsub|X><rsup|p*n>>.
    Replacing <math|t> by <math|\<zeta\>*<around|(|B,u|)>> therefore gives

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|scratch>\<leqslant\>2*n*s<rsub|L><rsup|2>*<around|(|D.*Z+1|)>*D.*Z*<space|0.17em>\<zeta\>*<around|(|B,u|)>*S<rsub|X><rsup|8+2*p*n>.
    </equation*>

    Adding these three bounds gives the coefficient
    <math|<math-up|boost>=b<rsub|0>+<around|(|b<rsub|0>+1|)>*L<rsup|3>+2*n*s<rsub|L><rsup|2>*<around|(|D.*Z+1|)>*D.*Z>.
    Since <math|<math-up|opal>=<math-up|rust>+<around|(|16/D.*\<varepsilon\>|)>*\<zeta\>>,
    and <math|S<rsub|X>\<geqslant\>1>,

    <\equation>
      <math-up|opal>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>*<around|(|F<rsub|0>+u/F<rsub|0>|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-opal-bound>
    </equation>

    The paint term is evaluated with the loom at <math|R<around|(|x|)>>, not
    at the reach. Its direction is <math|v<rsub|R>=C.*C*<space|0.17em>P.<math-up|threshold><around|(|1+R<around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|p>\<leqslant\>2*C.*C*F<rsub|0>*<around|(|1+R<around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|p>>.
    Squaring this inequality and dividing by <math|F<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> gives
    <math|<math-up|clinicFee><around|(|n,D.*Q|)>*v<rsub|R><rsup|2>/F<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>S<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>*F<rsub|0>>.
    The remaining paint term is <math|u/F<rsub|0>>. Thus the paint
    contribution is bounded by

    <\equation>
      <math-up|paintW>\<leqslant\>S<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>*F<rsub|0>+u/F<rsub|0>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-paint-bound>
    </equation>

    Equations <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-u-over-F>\U<eqref|eq:ex-lattice-paint-bound>
    give <math|F\<leqslant\>U<rsub|\<ast\>>>.

    The head-count moment estimate follows by inserting
    <math|1+<math-up|heads>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|L>*S<rsub|X><rsup|4>> into the
    explicit moment formula <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-scale-coefficients>: for
    <math|m=4*<around|(|n+1|)>>,

    <\equation>
      <math-up|meter><around|(|<math-up|heads><around|(|B,X|)>,D.*K,4*<around|(|n+1|)>|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|guests><rsub|G,D.*K><around|(|n+1,L|)>*S<rsub|X><rsup|4*<around|(|1+4*<around|(|n+1|)>|)>>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-head-moment>
    </equation>

    For the limit, <math|B<rsub|\<ast\>>,A<rsub|\<ast\>>,S<rsub|\<ast\>>,K<rsub|\<ast\>>>
    are fixed polynomials in <math|1+<math-up|radiusB><around|(|x|)>>, hence
    have rate zero and are subpower relative to <math|H>. Indeed
    <math|<math-up|radiusB><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>1+<around|(|log
    x|)><rsup|2>>, while <math|\<beta\><around|(|x|)>=x+O<around|(|1|)>> for
    fixed <math|L>, and <math|log H=<around|(|log log \<beta\>|)><rsup|2>>;
    the logarithm of any such fixed polynomial is <math|O<around|(|log log
    \<beta\>|)>=o<around|(|log H|)>>. Thus for each <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>,
    eventually each nonnegative polynomial factor <math|Q> here satisfies
    <math|Q\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<eta\>>>. The first term in
    <math|\<chi\>*U<rsub|\<ast\>>*K<rsub|\<ast\>>> has rate <math|-1/2>,
    since <math|<math-up|fiery>=H/<sqrt|\<beta\>>> and all other factors have
    rate zero. For the second term choose the subpower bounds
    <math|A<rsub|\<ast\>>+1\<leqslant\>2*H<rsup|1/8>>,
    <math|K<rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|1/8>>; then

    <\equation*>
      \<chi\>*<space|0.17em><frac|<around|(|2*A<rsub|\<ast\>>+2|)>*K<rsub|\<ast\>>|H>\<leqslant\>4*H<rsup|1/2+1/8+1/8-1>=4*H<rsup|-1/4>\<longrightarrow\>0.
    </equation*>

    This proves <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-budget-small>. Also
    <math|H\<geqslant\>1>, <math|K<rsub|\<ast\>>\<geqslant\>1>, and
    <math|F\<leqslant\>U<rsub|\<ast\>>>, so
    <math|F\<leqslant\>\<chi\>*U<rsub|\<ast\>>*K<rsub|\<ast\>>>;
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-budget-small> makes this at most one eventually.
    Finally <math|<math-up|clocks><around|(|t|)>=t<math-up|clocks><around|(|1|)>>
    for <math|t\<geqslant\>0>. Choose <math|d<rsub|\<ast\>>=1+<math-up|clocks><around|(|1|)>>.
    From <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-budget-small>, eventually
    <math|d<rsub|\<ast\>>*\<chi\>*U<rsub|\<ast\>>*K<rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>y>,
    and <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-budget-majorants> proves the second inequality
    in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-budget-output>.
  </proof>

  The bound is conditional only on the selected local chart and support
  inequalities <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-budget-support>; the algebra of the
  support-cover, rust, and Gaussian-moment estimates is expanded above. It
  does not produce the separate strip, field-growth, weighted vanishing,
  same-history, or healthy-chart receipts required to enter this local
  branch.

  <subsection|Three local scalar price bounds><label|sec:ex-lattice-trace-medal-prices>

  This section proves three scalar price conversions used by the selected
  trace construction. The arguments are independent of the construction of
  the local response and chart records that consume these bounds.

  Fix a block side <math|L>, and retain <math|\<beta\><around|(|x|)>>,
  <math|H<around|(|x|)>>, and <math|\<chi\><around|(|x|)>=H<around|(|x|)><rsup|1/2>>
  from Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-local-rate-selection>.
  For a fixed price <math|a\<gtr\>0>, write

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|s><rsub|a><around|(|x|)>=a/\<chi\><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  A negative strict-power rate has the following elementary consequence: if
  <math|f\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsub|\<alpha\>>> with <math|\<alpha\>\<less\>0>,
  then <math|\<chi\><around|(|x|)>*f<around|(|x|)>\<to\>0>. Indeed
  <math|\<chi\>\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsub|0>>, so the product has rate
  <math|\<alpha\>>; choose <math|v\<in\><around|(|\<alpha\>,0|)>>, and use
  <math|\<beta\><around|(|x|)><rsup|v>\<to\>0>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Cloud price><label|lem:ex-lattice-medal-cloud-price>Let <math|Q>
    be a target with rate exponent <math|q\<less\>0>. Suppose its poster
    coefficient <math|p<rsub|Q><around|(|x|)>> has strict-power rate
    <math|q/4>, and define the cloud response for a cloud <math|Z> on a
    representation <math|R> by

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-M\><rsub|Q>*<around|(|Z;x|)>=m<rsub|R><around|(|Z|)>*exp
      <around|(|-\<epsilon\><rsub|Q>*N<rsub|R><around|(|Z|)>|)>*p<rsub|Q><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>m<rsub|R><around|(|Z|)>\<geqslant\>0,<space|1em>N<rsub|R><around|(|Z|)>\<geqslant\>0,<space|1em>\<epsilon\><rsub|Q>\<gtr\>0.
    </equation*>

    Then for every <math|a\<gtr\>0>, eventually, uniformly over all clouds,

    <\equation>
      <around|\||\<cal-M\><rsub|Q>*<around|(|Z;x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><math-ss|s><rsub|a><around|(|x|)>*m<rsub|R><around|(|Z|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-cloud-price>
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Since <math|q\<less\>0>, choose <math|v> with
    <math|q/4\<less\>v\<less\>0>. The rate hypothesis implies
    <math|<around|\||p<rsub|Q><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>a/\<chi\><around|(|x|)>>
    eventually: equivalently <math|\<chi\><around|\||p<rsub|Q>|\|>\<to\>0>,
    and then make it at most <math|a>. The attenuation obeys
    <math|0\<less\>exp <around|(|-\<epsilon\><rsub|Q>*N<rsub|R><around|(|Z|)>|)>\<leqslant\>1>,
    independently of the cloud. Multiply these inequalities by the
    nonnegative mass <math|m<rsub|R><around|(|Z|)>>.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Forced gas price><label|lem:ex-lattice-medal-gas-price>Let
    <math|g<around|(|x|)>=exp <around|(|-H<around|(|x|)><rsup|1/5>/2|)>>, and
    let <math|<math-up|DoorsRate><around|(|L|)>=L<rsup|-2/5>\<gtr\>0> be the
    block remainder-cost factor. For every <math|a\<gtr\>0>, eventually

    <\equation>
      <math-up|DoorsRate><around|(|L|)>*g<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><math-ss|s><rsub|a><around|(|x|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-gas-price>
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Set <math|t=log log \<beta\><around|(|x|)>>, so <math|log H=t<rsup|2>>
    and <math|log \<beta\>=e<rsup|t>>. Then

    <\equation*>
      log <around*|(|\<chi\><around|(|x|)>*g<around|(|x|)>|)>=<tfrac|1|2>t<rsup|2>-<tfrac|1|2>e<rsup|t<rsup|2>/5>\<longrightarrow\>-\<infty\>,
    </equation*>

    because <math|e<rsup|t<rsup|2>/5>> grows faster than <math|t<rsup|2>>.
    Thus <math|\<chi\>*g\<to\>0>; multiplying by the fixed positive
    <math|<math-up|DoorsRate><around|(|L|)>> and making the product at most
    <math|a> proves <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-gas-price>.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Tuition price><label|lem:ex-lattice-medal-tuition-price>For a
    target with goal <math|\<beta\><around|(|x|)><rsup|q>>, define its
    tuition at fixed integers <math|s,n\<geqslant\>0> by

    <\equation>
      \<cal-T\><rsub|Q>*<around|(|x;s,n|)>=A<around|(|x|)><rsup|s*n>*<space|0.17em>P<rsub|1/2>*<around|(|s*n|)>*<space|0.17em>\<beta\><around|(|x|)><rsup|-q>*exp
      <around|(|-b<around|(|x|)>/2|)>,<label|eq:ex-lattice-tuition>
    </equation>

    where <math|A> and the polynomial price
    <math|P<rsub|1/2>*<around|(|s*n|)>> have rate zero and
    <math|b<around|(|x|)>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>. Explicitly,

    <\equation*>
      A<around|(|x|)>=4*<around|(|2*b<around|(|x|)>+1|)>,<space|2em>P<rsub|1/2><around|(|k|)>=2<rsup|k>*<space|0.17em>k!<space|0.17em>e<rsup|1/2>,
    </equation*>

    so that <math|A> is a fixed polynomial in <math|b<around|(|x|)>> and
    <math|P<rsub|1/2>*<around|(|s*n|)>> is a constant; both have rate zero.
    Then <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|Q>*<around|(|\<cdummy\>;s,n|)>\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsub|z>>
    for every real <math|z>. In particular, for every <math|a\<gtr\>0>,
    eventually

    <\equation>
      \<cal-T\><rsub|Q>*<around|(|x;s,n|)>\<leqslant\><math-ss|s><rsub|a><around|(|x|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-tuition-price>
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The first two factors in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-tuition> have rate zero,
    and <math|\<beta\><rsup|-q>> has rate <math|-q>. The last factor has
    every strict-power rate: for every fixed <math|c\<gtr\>0>,
    <math|b<around|(|x|)>/2\<geqslant\>c*log \<beta\><around|(|x|)>>
    eventually, since <math|b<around|(|x|)>\<asymp\><around|(|log
    x|)><rsup|2>> while <math|log \<beta\><around|(|x|)>\<asymp\>log x>. Thus
    <math|e<rsup|-b/2>\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rsup|-c>> eventually. Choose
    <math|c\<gtr\>max <around|(|0,-q-z|)>>; the product rate <math|-q-c> is
    then less than <math|z>, so the strict-power definition gives the claimed
    rate. Taking <math|z=-1> and using the negative-rate consequence above
    proves <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-tuition-price>.
  </proof>

  The three bounds enter the selected local record with two different prices.
  For one transition with block side <math|L> and one value of <math|x>, the
  cloud bound <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-cloud-price> and the tuition bound
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-tuition-price> are used with the same price <math|a=z>
  as the response and chart estimates of that record, while the gas bound
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-gas-price> is used with the separate price <math|a=y>;
  both <math|y,z\<gtr\>0> are fixed before the eventual threshold in <math|x>
  is taken. The three lemmas produce only these scalar entries of the record,
  not its response and chart entries.

  <subsection|Construction of the finite local response
  package><label|sec:ex-lattice-trace-eve-producer>

  We give the quantitative construction behind the local response package
  used by the later trace estimates. The output contains its probe error,
  chart error, price, and cloud allowance; these are built together because
  the final cloud billing uses the same price budget as the probe estimate.

  Fix the scale data of the trace construction and write

  <\equation*>
    \<chi\><around|(|x|)>=<sqrt|H<around|(|x|)>>,<space|2em>e=<math-up|title><around|(|P<rsub|1>|)>,<space|1em>a=<math-up|title><around|(|P<rsub|0>|)>,<space|1em>h=<math-up|credibility><around|(|P<rsub|1>|)>,<space|1em>\<nu\>=<math-up|DoorsRate><around|(|L|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|H> and <math|\<chi\>> are the monitor and its square root from
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-local-rate-selection>,
  whose functions <math|\<beta\><around|(|x|)>>, <math|b<around|(|x|)>>,
  <math|R<around|(|x|)>> and <math|\<rho\><around|(|x|)>> are also used
  below; we write <math|b<around|(|\<beta\><around|(|x|)>|)>> for the radius
  function evaluated at <math|\<beta\><around|(|x|)>>,

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|temp><around|(|x|)>=x<rsup|-1/2>*exp <around*|(|<around|(|log
    log x|)><rsup|2>|)>,<space|2em><math-up|fiery><around|(|x|)>=<math-up|temp><around|(|\<beta\><around|(|x|)>|)>=\<beta\><around|(|x|)><rsup|-1/2>*H<around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  Further, <math|P<rsub|0>> and <math|P<rsub|1>> are the two stage profiles
  of the transition, and <math|L> is the block side; <math|e,a,h,\<nu\>> are
  positive, <math|e,a\<geqslant\>1>, and <math|\<nu\>=L<rsup|-2/5>>. Let
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|F>> be the grade-<math|d> coefficient frame and
  <math|F> its finite family of coefficient-recovery probes; <math|F> is
  distinct from the finite basis of the frame, whose indices are denoted by
  <math|\<alpha\>> below. The prices <math|<math-up|testPrice>>,
  <math|<math-up|readPrice>>, <math|<math-up|errorPrice>> and
  <math|<math-up|orbitCost>> below are constants of the frame
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|F>> (the second and third also of the selected
  holonomy map), not of the probe family. For <math|p\<in\>F>, let
  <math|w<rsub|p>=\<pi\><around|(|p|)>\<geqslant\>1> be its probe price, and
  write <math|\<Lambda\>=M<rsub|d>> for the polarization constant in the
  probe estimate, both as in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-price-recurrence>.
  Put

  <\equation*>
    Z=<math-up|testPrice><around|(|<with|math-font|cal*|F>|)>,<space|2em>T=<math-up|readPrice><around|(|<with|math-font|cal*|F>|)>,<space|2em>c=<math-up|orbitCost><around|(|<with|math-font|cal*|F>|)><space|0.17em><math-up|errorPrice><around|(|<with|math-font|cal*|F>|)><space|0.17em><math-up|corn><around|(|d|)>*<space|0.17em>a.
  </equation*>

  The coefficient-recovery and readout constants <math|Z,T> are chosen at
  least one: the defining finite-dimensional inequalities remain valid when
  their constants are enlarged to one. The error price is the test price
  times one plus a finite sum of nonnegative chain-rule prices; the orbit
  cost is one plus a finite sum of coefficient sizes; and
  <math|<math-up|corn><around|(|d|)>> is one plus a finite sum of nonnegative
  comparison constants. All three are at least one. Together with
  <math|a\<geqslant\>1> this gives <math|c\<geqslant\>1>, in particular
  <math|c\<gtr\>0>. These are elementary properties of the chosen frame
  constants, not additional analytic inputs. For a positive target price
  <math|\<zeta\>>, define

  <\equation>
    \<kappa\>=min <around*|{|<frac|\<nu\>|2*c*Z>,\<zeta\>|}>\<gtr\>0,<space|2em>g<rsub|p><around|(|s|)>=w<rsub|p>*\<Lambda\>*L<rsup|d>*<space|0.17em>2*<around|(|e*s+s|)>+s.<label|eq:ex-lattice-eve-gp>
  </equation>

  Finiteness of the probe family <math|F> is essential here: each
  <math|g<rsub|p>> is continuous, <math|g<rsub|p><around|(|0|)>=0>, and
  therefore there is one <math|b\<gtr\>0> such that
  <math|g<rsub|p><around|(|s|)>\<leqslant\>\<kappa\>> for all <math|p\<in\>F>
  and <math|0\<leqslant\>s\<leqslant\>b>. Set <math|Q=b/2>.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Finite-probe and cloud billing><label|thm:ex-lattice-eve-producer>Let
    <math|d\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, and assume <math|e,a,h,\<nu\>,c,Z,T,\<zeta\>\<gtr\>0>,
    <math|e,a,Z,T\<geqslant\>1>, and a finite probe family <math|F> with
    weights <math|w<rsub|p>\<geqslant\>0> and normalization
    <math|\<Lambda\>\<geqslant\>0>. Suppose the selected response satisfies
    <math|u\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsub|-1/2>> and
    <math|\<chi\>\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsub|0>>, with
    <math|\<chi\><around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>1> eventually. For each probe, the
    charged scalar is the continuous function <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-eve-gp>.
    Set

    <\equation*>
      \<kappa\>=min <around*|{|<frac|\<nu\>|2*c*Z>,\<zeta\>|}>,<space|2em>\<delta\><rsub|x>=Q/\<chi\><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>C<rsub|x>=\<kappa\>/\<chi\><around|(|x|)>.
    </equation*>

    Assume the following selected-scale receipts hold on one common eventual
    tail in <math|x>; the tail is fixed before the probe, the frame index and
    all the other data listed in each receipt are chosen, and each receipt
    holds uniformly in those data. For the threshold
    <math|\<theta\>=<math-up|temp><around|(|x|)>> and the fine threshold
    <math|\<Theta\>=<math-up|fiery><around|(|x|)>>,

    <\equation>
      <tfrac|1|2>\<leqslant\><frac|\<Theta\>|\<theta\>>\<leqslant\>2.<label|eq:ex-lattice-eve-blend>
    </equation>

    The accompanying spatial comparison is
    <math|b<around|(|\<beta\><around|(|x|)>|)>\<leqslant\>2*b<around|(|x|)>>
    on this same tail. Indeed, that section gives
    <math|\<beta\><around|(|x|)>=x+c<rsub|L>> for a fixed <math|c<rsub|L>>
    and <math|b<around|(|x|)>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>. Thus <math|<around|(|log
    <around|(|x+c<rsub|L>|)>|)><rsup|2>/<around|(|log x|)><rsup|2>\<to\>1>,
    while rounding changes either quantity by at most one. The ratio
    <math|b<around|(|\<beta\><around|(|x|)>|)>/b<around|(|x|)>> tends to one,
    and is eventually at most two. Both comparisons are used when applying
    the local budget estimate: the fine threshold is within a factor two of
    the coarse threshold, and the fine budget radius fits the allowed coarse
    radius. The probe receipt is as follows. Let <math|p\<in\>F> have order
    <math|r<rsub|p>> and probe functional <math|R<rsub|p>> on coefficients,
    and let <math|\<alpha\>> be a basis index of the coefficient frame, with
    basis coefficient <math|<math-ss|e><rsub|\<alpha\>>>. Consider any step
    whose block count is <math|L> and whose first-order row is literally the
    row of the selected kernel with block side <math|L>, with coarse side
    larger than <math|2*\<rho\><around|(|x|)>>, and any two thresholds
    <math|w,y\<in\><around|(|0,1|]>> within a factor two of each other. Let
    <math|U<rsub|\<alpha\>>> be the coarse-lattice function obtained from the
    local presentation of <math|<math-ss|e><rsub|\<alpha\>>> at the origin
    with threshold <math|w> by cropping the coarse configuration to radius
    <math|\<rho\><around|(|x|)>> about the parent of the origin and routing
    it through the first-order row of the step; let <math|V<rsub|p>> be the
    logarithmic-link test curve of <math|p> with amplitude <math|y>; and let
    <math|\<cal-L\>> be the leading coefficient map determined by the
    selected kernel and the two thresholds. The <em|probe-to-run difference>
    is

    <\equation*>
      L<rsup|d>*<around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d<rsup|r<rsub|p>>|d*s<rsup|r<rsub|p>>>*U<rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|V<rsub|p><around|(|s|)>|)>|\|><rsub|s=0>-R<rsub|p><around*|(|\<cal-L\><space|0.17em><math-ss|e><rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>,
    </equation*>

    and the receipt is that its absolute value is at most
    <math|Q/\<chi\><around|(|x|)>> for every <math|p\<in\>F>, every
    <math|\<alpha\>>, and all such steps and thresholds. The cap receipt is:
    for every support <math|X> and every pair of budgets
    <math|<around|(|P,B|)>> such that <math|P> has threshold <math|\<theta\>>
    and the power of <math|P<rsub|0>>, <math|B> has threshold
    <math|\<Theta\>> and radius <math|b<around|(|\<beta\><around|(|x|)>|)>>,
    and <math|<math-up|coverNumber><around|(|B.*B,X|)>\<leqslant\>1>, and for
    every <math|\<beta\>\<geqslant\>\<beta\><around|(|x|)>/2>, the scale cap
    <math|O<rsub|x>> (the response cost of
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-explicit-local-cost> without its last, paint, line)
    and the local moment <math|M<rsub|x>> (the head-count moment of order
    <math|4*<around|(|n+1|)>> there) obey

    <\equation>
      0\<leqslant\>O<rsub|x>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em><math-up|clocks><around|(|e|)>*<space|0.17em>O<rsub|x><space|0.17em>M<rsub|x>\<leqslant\><frac|Q|\<chi\><around|(|x|)>>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-eve-cap>
    </equation>

    For every cloud <math|Z<rsub|0>> of mass <math|m<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>h>,
    put <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|x><around|(|Z<rsub|0>|)>=<math-up|marvel><rsub|x><around|(|Z<rsub|0>|)>\<geqslant\>0>.
    Its cloud receipt is

    <\equation>
      L<rsup|d>*<space|0.17em>2*\<cal-M\><rsub|x><around|(|Z<rsub|0>|)>\<leqslant\>S*m<rsub|0>,<space|2em>S=<frac|\<nu\>|2*c*T*h>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-eve-cloud>
    </equation>

    Then for all sufficiently large <math|x>, with the same uniformity in the
    data of each receipt:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>the two selected thresholds satisfy the factor-two comparison;

      <item>the response amplitude <math|s<rsub|x>=<around|\||u<around|(|x|)>|\|>>
      obeys <math|s<rsub|x>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|x>> and
      <math|s<rsub|x>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\>/\<chi\><around|(|x|)>>;

      <item>the scale cap is at most one, and its clock-weighted moment is at
      most <math|\<delta\><rsub|x>>;

      <item>every selected probe has approximation error at most
      <math|\<delta\><rsub|x>>;

      <item>for each probe <math|p>, the explicit charge
      <math|w<rsub|p>*\<Lambda\>*L<rsup|d>*<space|0.17em>2*<around|(|e*s<rsub|x>+\<delta\><rsub|x>|)>+\<delta\><rsub|x>>
      is at most <math|C<rsub|x>>;

      <item>for every cloud <math|Z<rsub|0>> of mass at most <math|h>, the
      combined charge

      <\equation*>
        c*<around*|[|Z*C<rsub|x>+T*L<rsup|d>*<space|0.17em>2*\<cal-M\><rsub|x><around|(|Z<rsub|0>|)>|]>\<leqslant\>\<nu\>.
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>

    In particular <math|C<rsub|x>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\>/\<chi\><around|(|x|)>>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    The product rate gives <math|\<chi\>*u\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsub|-1/2>>. Choose
    <math|q\<in\><around|(|-1/2,0|)>>; the strict-power definition implies
    <math|\<chi\><around|(|x|)><around|\||u<around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<beta\><around|(|x|)><rsup|q>\<to\>0>.
    Thus, eventually,

    <\equation>
      <around|\||u<around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>Q/\<chi\><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em><around|\||u<around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\>/\<chi\><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>\<chi\><around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>1.<label|eq:ex-lattice-eve-response-bound>
    </equation>

    The first two give the asserted response amplitude bound and the desired
    price bound for the amplitude. The first selected geometry estimate gives
    the factor-two threshold comparison.

    The selected scale-cap estimate is called at price <math|Q/e>. Its price
    bound is linear in the allowed scalar multiplier <math|t>; evaluating it
    at <math|t=e> gives exactly the second inequality in
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-eve-cap>, while its companion bound gives
    <math|O<rsub|x>\<leqslant\>1>. The probe approximation estimate is called
    at tolerance <math|Q>, which gives the bound stated above directly.

    For a probe <math|p>, the amplitude bound
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-eve-response-bound> gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:ex-lattice-eve-amplitude>2*<around|(|e\|u<around|(|x|)>\|+\<delta\><rsub|x>|)>\<leqslant\><frac|2*<around|(|e*Q+Q|)>|\<chi\><around|(|x|)>>.
    </equation>

    Consequently its charged expression is bounded by

    <\equation*>
      <frac|w<rsub|p>*\<Lambda\>*L<rsup|d>*<space|0.17em>2*<around|(|e*Q+Q|)>+Q|\<chi\><around|(|x|)>>=<frac|g<rsub|p><around|(|Q|)>|\<chi\><around|(|x|)>>\<leqslant\><frac|\<kappa\>|\<chi\><around|(|x|)>>=C<rsub|x>.
    </equation*>

    This is the simultaneous finite-probe choice made above; it is not a
    pointwise choice depending on the probe.

    Finally, let a cloud have mass <math|m<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>h>. The cloud
    estimate <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-eve-cloud> gives

    <\equation*>
      T*L<rsup|d>*<around|(|2*\<cal-M\><rsub|x><around|(|Z<rsub|0>|)>|)>\<leqslant\>T*S*h=<frac|\<nu\>|2*c>.
    </equation*>

    The probe part costs at most <math|Z*C<rsub|x>\<leqslant\>Z*\<kappa\>/\<chi\><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>Z*\<kappa\>>.
    By the definition of <math|\<kappa\>>,
    <math|c*Z*\<kappa\>\<leqslant\>\<nu\>/2>. Adding the cloud and probe
    charges therefore gives at most <math|\<nu\>>. This proves all six
    clauses.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Matching the finite-volume response
  estimate><label|subsec:ex-lattice-eve-matching>

  We now identify the local insertion receipt in the actual response
  construction. Fix a leaf insertion of weight <math|\<tau\>>, and hold its
  masked ingredient fixed while varying the local field. The two functions
  compared are the actual-sector and Gaussian-reference integrals

  <\equation>
    \<Phi\><rsub|6><around|(|y|)>=<big|int>R<rsub|6>*<around|(|y,z|)>*\<chi\><rsub|6>*<around|(|y,z|)>*h<around|(|z|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|C<rsub|0>><around|(|z|)>,<space|2em>\<Phi\><rsub|9><around|(|y|)>=<big|int>R<rsub|9>*<around|(|y,z|)>*\<chi\><rsub|9>*<around|(|y,z|)>*h<around|(|z|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|C<rsub|0>><around|(|z|)>,<label|eq:ex-lattice-eve-two-integrals>
  </equation>

  where <math|h> is the fixed measurable masked insertion and
  <math|<around|\||h|\|>\<leqslant\>\<tau\>>. The density comparison in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:principal-gaussian-comparison> proves, under
  its displayed finite-dimensional covariance, mean, cutoff, and chart
  hypotheses,

  <\equation>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|\<Phi\><rsub|6>-\<Phi\><rsub|9>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|J<rsup|n><rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>\<tau\>*P<rsub|n>*<around|(|N,Q,S|)>*<around*|(|\<delta\>+2*N*e<rsup|-v+M/2>|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-eve-linear-receipt>
  </equation>

  The primitive mean and covariance estimates supplying <math|\<delta\>> are
  proved in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:principal-comparison-jets>. The
  fixed insertion is not differentiated. The local chart directions are the
  same ordered variations used in the response test, with
  <math|j\<leqslant\>n> and their prescribed directional and canopy bounds.

  In the exact construction, the fixed leaf is held together with its regular
  masked ingredient <math|L<rprime|'>>. For each fixed grade and block, the
  bounds <math|N<around|(|x|)>,Q<around|(|x|)>,S<around|(|x|)>,\<delta\><around|(|x|)>>
  and the cutoff-tail bounds are common to every admissible period, forest
  and tag choice, every leaf whose cover is at most <math|B>, and every
  normalized direction list allowed in the comparison. They are independent
  of the leaf weight <math|\<tau\>>. Thus the one finite-probe choice and
  selected response <math|u<around|(|x|)>> work uniformly over all these
  inputs. The local read set, forest, tags, insertion mask, and chart are
  fixed during each individual differentiation. The two local observables are

  <\equation>
    \<cal-O\><rsub|<math-up|act>><around|(|y|)>=\<Phi\><rsub|6><around|(|y|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-O\><rsub|<math-up|ref>><around|(|y|)>=\<Phi\><rsub|9><around|(|y|)><label|eq:ex-lattice-eve-source-identification>
  </equation>

  and these identities follow by writing each side with its own normalized
  Gaussian density and using the protected-support agreement on the
  insertion's read set. Thus the response difference is exactly
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|6>-\<Phi\><rsub|9>>, not a comparison of different
  insertions or different Gaussian spaces. The local finite price cutoff
  makes the integrands vanish outside a common compact coordinate ball; the
  cutoffs are one on the protected smaller ball. Differentiation under the
  integral is justified with the bounded measurable <math|h> fixed, by the
  uniform derivative bounds on that compact ball and dominated convergence.

  The selected rate budgets choose the scalar envelope

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>=P<rsub|n>*<around|(|N<around|(|x|)>,Q<around|(|x|)>,S<around|(|x|)>|)>*<around*|(|\<delta\><around|(|x|)>+2*N<around|(|x|)>*e<rsup|-v<around|(|x|)>+M<around|(|x|)>/2>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The covariance discrepancy has strict-power rate <math|-1/2>, the mean
  discrepancy has rate <math|-1> before conversion to Gaussian coordinates,
  and the good-coordinate tail has every negative rate. The fixed polynomial
  factor has rate zero. Therefore <math|B<rsub|\<ast\>>\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsub|-1/2>>.
  The response construction packages the concrete derivative, cutoff, and
  tail budgets into one scalar envelope. The component bounds are assembled
  from the finite product and sum rules for the displayed rates. With this
  envelope, for every admissible leaf and every ordered list of at most
  <math|n> normalized chart directions, the derivative of the
  actual-minus-reference observable at the chart origin is bounded by
  <math|\<tau\>*B<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>>, uniformly over the choices
  above. This is the concrete uniform derivative inequality used by the
  response construction.

  We can now choose the scalar in the insertion receipt explicitly. The
  polynomial majorant <math|P<rsub|n>> has nonnegative coefficients, and its
  arguments, <math|\<delta\>> and the cutoff-tail term are nonnegative. Thus
  <math|B<rsub|\<ast\>>\<geqslant\>0> on the common tail where the comparison
  is applied. Define

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ex-lattice-eve-concrete-amplitude>f<around|(|x|)>=<tfrac|1|2>B<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>u<around|(|x|)>=2<around|\||f<around|(|x|)>|\|>=B<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>
  </equation>

  on that tail, and set both functions to zero before it. Altering a function
  on an initial interval does not change its eventual rate. Multiplication by
  a fixed positive constant preserves the strict-power rate: for each
  <math|q\<gtr\>-1/2>, choose <math|q<rprime|'>\<in\><around|(|-1/2,q|)>> and
  absorb the constant using <math|\<beta\><around|(|x|)><rsup|q-q<rprime|'>>\<to\>\<infty\>>.
  Hence both <math|f> and <math|u> belong to <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|-1/2>>.

  For every admissible leaf of weight <math|\<tau\>\<geqslant\>0> and every
  ordered list of at most <math|n> allowed chart directions, the inequality
  already proved is therefore exactly

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||D<rsup|\<b-up-v\>>*<around|(|\<cal-O\><rsub|<math-up|act>>-\<cal-O\><rsub|<math-up|ref>>|)><around|(|0|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<tau\>*B<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x|)>=\<tau\>*<space|0.17em>2<around|\||f<around|(|x|)>|\|>=\<tau\><around|\||u<around|(|x|)>|\|>.
  </equation*>

  This includes the zeroth derivative and the case <math|\<tau\>=0>; no
  division by the leaf weight is made. The density and cutoff derivative
  bounds used in the comparison also give the required differentiability near
  the chart origin, with the masked ingredient fixed. Thus the bound applies
  to the actual response test, not just to a formal coefficient of its Taylor
  expansion.

  The function <math|f> may depend on the fixed grade, block, stage profiles,
  selected kernel, differentiation order and comparison parameters. It is
  chosen before the period, forest, tags, leaf, mask, read support, base
  configuration and allowed direction list. Its tail is uniform in all those
  latter data, because the envelope <math|B<rsub|\<ast\>>> and the two
  integral identities were uniform in them. There is no additional
  normalization, positivity or matching condition on the auxiliary scalar
  <math|f>: it is a price majorant, not one of the local fields. The
  numerical factor two is therefore realized by the definition in
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-eve-concrete-amplitude>; it incurs no extra analytic
  loss.

  If no branch patch compatible with the fixed selected kernel and block
  exists, take <math|f=u=0> everywhere. Any step to which the receipt could
  apply would itself supply such a patch, so the universally quantified
  insertion receipt has no instances in this case. If a compatible patch
  exists, the actual step uses that selected patch and the preceding
  construction applies. In either case this supplies the insertion receipt.
  The finite-test choice and scalar billing of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-eve-producer> now apply to this
  concrete <math|u>, on the intersection of its common tail with the
  geometry, cap, probe and cloud tails; no outstanding insertion estimate is
  assumed.

  The package for one transition with block side <math|L> and grade <math|d>
  is constructed under the following standing conditions, which the scalar
  theorem above does not see: the guard of the record satisfies
  <math|D.*\<varepsilon\>\<leqslant\>1/4>; the two integer parameters of the
  selected kernel are at least <math|4>, and the first of them is at least
  <math|d>; the differentiation order satisfies <math|n\<geqslant\>2>; and
  the real parameter <math|\<mu\>> of the response estimate is positive. The
  response receipt with amplitude <math|<around|\||u<around|(|x|)>|\|>> is
  stated there for every leaf whose label is the logarithmic key of a regular
  activity and whose ingredient is exactly the mask, at the threshold of the
  representation, of a function that is regular on the support of that
  activity; for every budget of radius <math|b<around|(|x|)>> and of the
  power of <math|P<rsub|0>> that is compatible with the representation; for
  every read support that is healthy at the base configuration and has cover
  number at most that radius; and for every list of at most <math|n>
  directions satisfying the budget's directional bound and the canopy bound
  <math|2*\<Theta\>*<around|(|1+R<around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|p>>, where <math|p>
  is the power of <math|P<rsub|0>>. These identities of the leaf, mask,
  budget and support are what make <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-eve-source-identification>
  a comparison of one insertion on one Gaussian space. The cap receipt of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-eve-producer> follows from the
  scalar budget estimate <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-budget-output> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-local-rate-selection>,
  called at price <math|Q/e> and with the nonnegative paint term omitted. In
  fact <math|B.<math-up|threshold>=\<Theta\>=<math-up|fiery><around|(|x|)>>
  and <math|P.<math-up|threshold>=\<theta\>\<leqslant\>2*\<Theta\>> by
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-eve-blend>; <math|B.*B=b<around|(|\<beta\><around|(|x|)>|)>\<leqslant\>2*b<around|(|x|)>>
  by the comparison proved above; <math|P.<math-up|power>> is the power of
  <math|P<rsub|0>>; and the cap's cover bound is at most one, so the support
  allowance in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-budget-support> can be chosen to be the
  constant one. The lower coupling bound is the same in both statements.
  These verify all the support and budget conditions of that application. The
  two remaining receipts are proved next for these same local data.

  <subsubsection|The actual probe and cloud
  bounds><label|subsec:trace-eve-probe-cloud>

  Fix a probe <math|p> of order <math|k> and a frame column <math|\<alpha\>>
  of amplitude degree <math|\<ell\><rsub|\<alpha\>>>. The constants below
  depend on these finite choices and the installed kernel and block, but not
  on the period or on the two thresholds <math|w,y\<in\><around|(|0,1|]>>
  with <math|1/2\<leqslant\>y/w\<leqslant\>2>. Let
  <math|R=\<rho\><around|(|x|)>=45*<around|(|1+<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
  x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>|)>>. We apply the cropped-probe calculation of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:primary-calibration-producer> with fine
  threshold <math|w> and coarse probe threshold <math|y>. Its actual curve is
  exactly the one defining <math|U<rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|V<rsub|p><around|(|s|)>|)>>:
  it crops to radius <math|R> and applies the installed first-order row. The
  room condition <math|2*R\<less\>c> ensures that the crop uses the canonical
  covering-lattice lifts. The full polynomial curve uses the same row.
  Absolute convergence of its Fourier kernel identifies the periodized
  convolution with the covering sum regrouped by residue classes.

  In the notation of <eqref|eq:primary-probe-tail>, the jets of the two
  normalized input curves through order <math|k> differ by at most

  <\equation*>
    e<rsub|R>=C*<around|{|1+K*<around|(|1+4*R|)><rsup|d*k>|}>*exp
    <around|(|-\<sigma\>*R/2|)>,<space|2em>\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation*>

  Here the polynomial factor bounds the probe's link-log jets outside the
  crop; inside the crop the two arrays agree. The constants are uniform in
  <math|w,y>: each nonempty chain-rule block contributes a threshold factor
  <math|y<rsup|r>/w\<leqslant\>y/w\<leqslant\>2>. The full predicted jets
  have a fixed bound <math|U> given by the weighted kernel moments. The
  normalized local-section estimate <eqref|eq:primary-stencil-stability>
  therefore bounds the output jet difference, including the physical factor,
  by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-eve-probe-envelope>E<rsub|p,\<alpha\>><around|(|x|)>=L<rsup|d>*C<rsub|k,\<alpha\>,U>*<around|(|1+e<rsub|R>|)><rsup|\<ell\><rsub|\<alpha\>>+k>*e<rsub|R>.
  </equation>

  The full polynomial forecast in that section has exactly the coefficient
  transport <math|\<cal-L\><math-ss|e><rsub|\<alpha\>>>: its three fine-grade
  factors multiply to <math|L<rsup|d>*w<rsup|-\<ell\><rsub|\<alpha\>>>>, and
  its coarse scaling inserts <math|y> in the probe. Thus its reading is
  <math|R<rsub|p><around|(|\<cal-L\><math-ss|e><rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>>, with no
  change of coefficient map. This identifies the difference estimated in
  <eqref|eq:trace-eve-probe-envelope> with the probe-to-run difference in
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-eve-producer>. For the unit column
  the local section is constant and both readings agree exactly.

  Since <math|R\<asymp\><around|(|log x|)><rsup|2>>, the logarithm of
  <math|e<rsub|R>> is at most <math|-c<rsub|0><around|(|log
  x|)><rsup|2>+O<around|(|log log x|)>> for some <math|c<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0>.
  Also <math|log \<chi\><around|(|x|)>=<tfrac|1|2><around|(|log log
  \<beta\><around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|2>> and <math|\<beta\><around|(|x|)>=x+c<rsub|L>>.
  It follows directly that <math|\<chi\><around|(|x|)>*E<rsub|p,\<alpha\>><around|(|x|)>\<to\>0>.
  There are finitely many probes and columns. Taking the maximum of their
  eventual thresholds gives <math|E<rsub|p,\<alpha\>><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>Q/\<chi\><around|(|x|)>>
  simultaneously, uniformly over all the steps, periods and thresholds
  allowed in the receipt. This proves the probe estimate with its required
  order of quantifiers.

  For the cloud, let <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>\<less\>0> be the fixed rate of the
  selected target and <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0> its support
  reserve. The actual cloud response is the quantity in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-medal-cloud-price>, with its
  coefficient now specified explicitly:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-eve-cloud-formula>\<cal-M\><rsub|x><around|(|Z<rsub|0>|)>=m<rsub|0>*exp
    <around|(|-\<epsilon\><rsub|\<ast\>>*N<around|(|Z<rsub|0>|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>\<beta\><around|(|x|)><rsup|q<rsub|\<ast\>>/4>,<space|2em>N<around|(|Z<rsub|0>|)>\<geqslant\>0.
  </equation>

  The coefficient is a negative power of <math|\<beta\>>, so it has the
  strict-power rate required in that lemma. In particular
  <math|0\<leqslant\>\<cal-M\><rsub|x><around|(|Z<rsub|0>|)>\<leqslant\>m<rsub|0>*\<beta\><around|(|x|)><rsup|q<rsub|\<ast\>>/4>>
  uniformly over clouds and representations. Choose one scale threshold with
  <math|2*L<rsup|d>*\<beta\><around|(|x|)><rsup|q<rsub|\<ast\>>/4>\<leqslant\>S=\<nu\>/<around|(|2*c*T*h|)>>.
  Multiplying by <math|m<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0> proves
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-eve-cloud>, including the case <math|m<rsub|0>=0>
  without division. The same formula gives the stronger statement

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-M\><rsub|x><around|(|Z<rsub|0>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|v/\<chi\><around|(|x|)>|)>*m<rsub|0>
  </equation*>

  for every prescribed <math|v\<gtr\>0> on a later common tail, since
  <math|\<chi\><around|(|x|)>*\<beta\><around|(|x|)><rsup|q<rsub|\<ast\>>/4>\<to\>0>.
  This is the cloud estimate used for regular and primary atoms. Thus the
  probe, cap, cloud, and insertion estimates are all supplied before their
  common finite intersection is used to construct the response package.

  <subsection|Fourier tails and local jet
  estimates><label|sec:ex-lattice-trace-row-jet-estimates>

  This section proves two quantitative ingredients of the small-support
  regular-source argument: a weighted convolution bound with an exponentially
  small tail, and the corresponding multilinear estimate for a local
  coefficient probe. The remaining hypotheses are named mathematically: a
  uniform Fourier strip bound, a coefficient-growth receipt, and the local
  regularity and vanishing-jet receipts. In the construction these come from
  the selected finite-range kernel, the budget's order bounds, and the
  regularity of the masked source.

  For each natural number <math|n>, write <math|\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>> for the
  covering lattice, with <math|<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>=<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>=1><rsup|4><around|\||x<rsub|\<nu\>>|\|>>,
  and put

  <\equation*>
    W<rsub|n><around|(|x|)>=<around|(|1+<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>|)><rsup|n>,<space|2em>C<rsub|n><around|(|\<sigma\>|)>=<around*|(|1+<frac|n+1|\<sigma\>>|)><rsup|n>*<space|1em><around|(|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0|)>.
  </equation*>

  The elementary inequality

  <\equation>
    <around|(|1+r|)><rsup|n>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|n><around|(|\<sigma\>|)>*e<rsup|\<sigma\>*r>*<space|2em><around|(|r\<geqslant\>0|)><label|eq:ex-lattice-widthcost>
  </equation>

  follows by setting <math|s=\<sigma\>/<around|(|n+1|)>>. Indeed

  <\equation*>
    1+r\<leqslant\><around*|(|1+<frac|n+1|\<sigma\>>|)>*<around|(|1+s*r|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|1+<frac|n+1|\<sigma\>>|)>*e<rsup|s*r>,
  </equation*>

  and taking the <math|n>-th power gives <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-widthcost>,
  since <math|n*s\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>>. Also
  <math|W<rsub|n>*<around|(|x+z|)>\<leqslant\>W<rsub|n><around|(|x|)>*W<rsub|n><around|(|z|)>>,
  by the triangle inequality and <math|1+a+b\<leqslant\><around|(|1+a|)>*<around|(|1+b|)>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Polynomially weighted convolution><label|lem:ex-lattice-row-convolution>Suppose
    a scalar kernel <math|q:\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>\<to\>\<bbb-R\>> and constants
    <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0>, <math|C\<geqslant\>0> satisfy

    <\equation>
      <big|sum><rsub|z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>>e<rsup|\<sigma\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>><around|\||q<around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<label|eq:ex-lattice-row-strip>
    </equation>

    and a Banach-valued array <math|b> satisfies
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<around|(|x|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>M*W<rsub|n><around|(|x|)>>,
    where <math|M\<geqslant\>0>. Then the convolution

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|q\<ast\>b|)><around|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|z>q<around|(|z|)>*b*<around|(|x+z|)>
    </equation*>

    is absolutely convergent and

    <\equation>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|q\<ast\>b|)><around|(|x|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>M*C<rsub|n><around|(|\<sigma\>|)>*C*W<rsub|n><around|(|x|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-row-convolution>
    </equation>

    For a <math|4>-by-<math|4> matrix kernel whose sixteen entries each
    satisfy <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-row-strip> with the same <math|C>, the
    corresponding vector-row operator obeys

    <\equation>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|<math-ss|A>b|)><around|(|x|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>4*M*C<rsub|n><around|(|\<sigma\>|)>*C*W<rsub|n><around|(|x|)>,<label|eq:ex-lattice-row-matrix-cost>
    </equation>

    when the output norm is the maximum of its four component norms.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For each <math|z>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||q<around|(|z|)>|\|>*<space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|b*<around|(|x+z|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>M*W<rsub|n><around|(|x|)><around|\||q<around|(|z|)>|\|>*W<rsub|n><around|(|z|)>\<leqslant\>M*W<rsub|n><around|(|x|)>*C<rsub|n><around|(|\<sigma\>|)>*e<rsup|\<sigma\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>><around|\||q<around|(|z|)>|\|>,
    </equation*>

    where <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-widthcost> was applied to
    <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>. Summing proves absolute convergence and
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-row-convolution>. For a matrix row, apply
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-row-convolution> to each of its four entries and sum
    the four resulting component bounds; this gives
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-row-matrix-cost>.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Agreement on a ball gives an exponential
    tail><label|lem:ex-lattice-row-tail>Assume
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-row-strip>. Let <math|A<rsub|1>,A<rsub|2>:\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>\<to\>E>,
    where <math|E> is a Banach space, satisfy

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|1><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>d<rsub|1>*W<rsub|n><around|(|y|)>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|2><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>d<rsub|2>*W<rsub|n><around|(|y|)>,
    </equation*>

    with <math|d<rsub|1>,d<rsub|2>\<geqslant\>0>, and suppose
    <math|A<rsub|1>*<around|(|x+z|)>=A<rsub|2>*<around|(|x+z|)>> whenever
    <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>\<less\>b>. Then

    <\equation>
      <around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|z>q<around|(|z|)>*<around*|(|A<rsub|1>*<around|(|x+z|)>-A<rsub|2>*<around|(|x+z|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*b/2>*<around|(|d<rsub|1>+d<rsub|2>|)>*C<rsub|n>*<around|(|\<sigma\>/2|)>*C*W<rsub|n><around|(|x|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-row-tail>
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The difference vanishes for <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>\<less\>b>, so
    the triangle inequality and submultiplicativity of <math|W<rsub|n>> give
    an upper bound

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|d<rsub|1>+d<rsub|2>|)>*W<rsub|n><around|(|x|)>*<big|sum><rsub|<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>\<geqslant\>b><around|\||q<around|(|z|)>|\|>*W<rsub|n><around|(|z|)>.
    </equation*>

    Using <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-widthcost> with <math|\<sigma\>/2>, each
    summand is at most

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|n>*<around|(|\<sigma\>/2|)>*e<rsup|\<sigma\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>/2><around|\||q<around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|n>*<around|(|\<sigma\>/2|)>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*b/2>*e<rsup|\<sigma\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>><around|\||q<around|(|z|)>|\|>.
    </equation*>

    Summation and <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-row-strip> prove
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-row-tail>.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Multilinear jet comparison><label|lem:ex-lattice-jet-comparison>Here
    is the passage from that tail estimate to a local coefficient
    measurement. Let <math|P> be a linear screen onto the allowed edge
    directions; it is applied only to the direction arrays below. Let
    <math|F> be a <math|C<rsup|N>> real-valued local observable. Here
    <math|<math-up|inc><rsub|1><around|(|U|)>> is the configuration obtained
    by exponentiating the tangent field <math|U> from the identity. Write
    <math|\<cal-J\><rsub|a>=D<rsup|a>*<around|(|F\<circ\><math-up|inc><rsub|1>|)><around|(|0|)>>
    for its symmetric <math|a>-linear derivative at the identity
    configuration. Assume the following three estimates for
    <math|a\<leqslant\>N>:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>for all admissible screened directions
      <math|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|a>>, and all nonnegative
      <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>> with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<lambda\><rsub|i>>,

      <\equation>
        <around|\||\<cal-J\><rsub|a>*<around|(|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|a>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<Phi\>*w*<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|a>\<lambda\><rsub|i>;<label|eq:ex-lattice-jet-price>
      </equation>

      <item>the already-screened reference directions
      <math|A<rsub|i>=P*<around|(|\<theta\>*y<rsub|i>|)>> satisfy
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>K>, with
      <math|K\<geqslant\>1>, and the screened reference jet vanishes:

      <\equation>
        \<cal-J\><rsub|a>*<around|(|P*<around|(|\<theta\>*y<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,P*<around|(|\<theta\>*y<rsub|a>|)>|)>=0;<label|eq:ex-lattice-jet-vanishing>
      </equation>

      <item>directions <math|x<rsub|i>> approximate them with
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|P*<around|(|\<theta\>*x<rsub|i>|)>-P*<around|(|\<theta\>*y<rsub|i>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>>,
      where <math|0\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>\<leqslant\>K>.
    </enumerate>

    Then

    <\equation>
      <around|\||\<cal-J\><rsub|a>*<around|(|P*<around|(|\<theta\>*x<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,P*<around|(|\<theta\>*x<rsub|a>|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|a>*\<varepsilon\>*K<rsup|a>*\<Phi\>*w.<label|eq:ex-lattice-jet-comparison>
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Set <math|U<rsub|i>=P*<around|(|\<theta\>*x<rsub|i>|)>>,
    <math|A<rsub|i>=P*<around|(|\<theta\>*y<rsub|i>|)>>, and
    <math|E<rsub|i>=U<rsub|i>-A<rsub|i>>. By multilinearity,

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-J\><rsub|a>*<around|(|U<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,U<rsub|a>|)>-\<cal-J\><rsub|a>*<around|(|A<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,A<rsub|a>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<varnothing\>\<ne\>S\<subseteq\><around|{|1,\<ldots\>,a|}>>\<cal-J\><rsub|a>*<around|(|v<rsub|1><rsup|S>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|a><rsup|S>|)>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|v<rsub|i><rsup|S>=E<rsub|i>> if <math|i\<in\>S>, and
    <math|v<rsub|i><rsup|S>=A<rsub|i>> otherwise. The empty-set term is zero
    by <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-jet-vanishing>. In every remaining term at least
    one factor has norm at most <math|\<varepsilon\>>; all other factors have
    norm at most <math|K>, by the stated bounds on the screened reference
    directions. Applying <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-jet-price> to each term bounds
    it by <math|\<varepsilon\>*K<rsup|a-1>*\<Phi\>*w\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>*K<rsup|a>*\<Phi\>*w>.
    There are <math|2<rsup|a>-1\<leqslant\>2<rsup|a>> such terms, proving
    (8).
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Local probe bound from a strip and a jet
    gap><label|prop:ex-lattice-strip-to-probe>Fix natural numbers <math|d,N>
    with <math|d\<leqslant\>N>, a support radius <math|b>, a chart radius
    <math|m>, and <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0>, and let <math|L\<geqslant\>1> be
    the integer block side. For each <math|k\<leqslant\>d>, suppose the
    four-by-four row kernel <math|<around|(|q<rsub|\<nu\>*j>|)><rsub|\<nu\>,j=1><rsup|4>>
    has entries satisfying <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-row-strip> with constant
    <math|M>. Suppose the two arrays <math|A<rsup|<around|(|0|)>><rsub|j>,A<rsup|<around|(|1|)>><rsub|j>>
    satisfy

    <\equation>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsup|<around|(|h|)>><rsub|j><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>K*W<rsub|d*k><around|(|y|)><space|1em><around|(|h=0,1|)>,<space|2em>A<rsup|<around|(|0|)>><rsub|j>*<around|(|u+z|)>=A<rsup|<around|(|1|)>><rsub|j>*<around|(|u+z|)><text|if
      ><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>\<less\>b,<label|eq:ex-lattice-local-array-input>
    </equation>

    where <math|K\<geqslant\>1>, <math|M\<geqslant\>0>, and each kernel base
    <math|u> has <math|<around|\||u|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>4*m>. Define the
    order-<math|k> reference and actual directions <math|y<rsub|k>> and
    <math|x<rsub|k>> by applying this matrix row to
    <math|A<rsup|<around|(|0|)>>> and <math|A<rsup|<around|(|1|)>>>,
    respectively. The screen <math|P> is coordinate restriction to allowed
    edges. Let <math|\<theta\>\<gtr\>0> be the threshold and
    <math|\<omega\><around|(|e|)>\<geqslant\>1> the spatial weight of the
    budget; the norm in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-jet-price> is the normalized
    edge norm

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>=sup<rsub|e>
      <frac|<around|\<\|\|\>|v<around|(|e|)>|\<\|\|\>>|\<theta\>*<space|0.17em>\<omega\><around|(|e|)>>,<space|2em><text|so
      that><space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|P*<around|(|\<theta\>*v|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>sup<rsub|e<math-up|allowed>><around|\<\|\|\>|v<around|(|e|)>|\<\|\|\>>
    </equation*>

    for every direction array <math|v>. Thus an entrywise bound for an
    unscaled direction on the allowed edges is a bound for the normalized
    norm of its threshold-scaled screen, and no additional screen norm is
    inserted between the row estimate and the priced jet. Then the entrywise
    difference of <math|x<rsub|k>> and <math|y<rsub|k>> is at most

    <\equation>
      \<delta\><rsub|k>=8*K*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*b/2>*<around|(|1+4*m|)><rsup|d*k>*C<rsub|d*k>*<around|(|\<sigma\>/2|)>*M<label|eq:ex-lattice-local-delta>
    </equation>

    and hence so is the normalized norm of
    <math|P*<around|(|\<theta\>*x<rsub|k>|)>-P*<around|(|\<theta\>*y<rsub|k>|)>>.
    The reference and actual directions satisfy, entrywise,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|y<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>,<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>4*K*C<rsub|d*k><around|(|\<sigma\>|)>*M*<around|(|1+4*L*<around|(|m+2|)>|)><rsup|d*k>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|1>,
    </equation*>

    where

    <\equation>
      C<rsub|1>=1+<big|sum><rsub|k=0><rsup|d>4*K*C<rsub|d*k><around|(|\<sigma\>|)>*M*<around*|(|1+4*L*<around|(|m+2|)>|)><rsup|d*k>,<label|eq:ex-lattice-local-C1>
    </equation>

    Assume the local jet satisfies the price bound
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-jet-price>, that <math|F> depends only on the
    allowed-edge components of the configuration, so that
    <math|\<cal-J\><rsub|a>*<around|(|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|a>|)>=\<cal-J\><rsub|a>*<around|(|P*v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,P*v<rsub|a>|)>>,
    and also that <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|d>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|1>>. Assume
    the weighted vanishing receipt for the screened, threshold-scaled
    reference directions:

    <\equation>
      \<cal-J\><rsub|a>*<around*|(|P*<around|(|\<theta\>*y<rsub|q<rsub|1>>|)>,\<ldots\>,P*<around|(|\<theta\>*y<rsub|q<rsub|a>>|)>|)>=0<space|1em><text|whenever
      >q<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1,<space|1em><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|a>q<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>d.<label|eq:ex-lattice-weighted-jet-vanishing>
    </equation>

    In the application, <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-weighted-jet-vanishing> holds
    because each <math|P*<around|(|\<theta\>*y<rsub|k>|)>> lies in the space
    <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|k>> of polynomial directions of degree at most
    <math|k-1> on the allowed edges, and the observable has vanishing jets on
    lists of such directions with positive degrees of sum at most <math|d>.
    No such membership is assumed for the actual directions <math|x<rsub|k>>;
    it is neither needed below nor available in the application. Assume also
    the order-zero receipt <math|F<around|(|<math-up|id>|)>=0>. Let
    <math|\<gamma\>:\<bbb-R\>\<to\>\<cal-X\>> be the actual <math|C<rsup|d>>
    probe path in the tangent configuration space, with
    <math|\<gamma\><around|(|0|)>=0> and <math|\<gamma\><rsup|<around|(|k|)>><around|(|0|)>=\<theta\>*x<rsub|k>>
    for <math|1\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>d>. The observable is evaluated on
    <math|<math-up|inc><rsub|1><around|(|\<gamma\><around|(|s|)>|)>>, so its
    base point is the identity configuration. Then the probe of total
    derivative order <math|\<ell\>\<leqslant\>d> obeys

    <\equation>
      <around*|\||<around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d<rsup|\<ell\>>|d*s<rsup|\<ell\>>>*F<around|(|<math-up|inc><rsub|1><around|(|\<gamma\><around|(|s|)>|)>|)>|\|><rsub|s=0>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||<math-up|Part><rsup|<math-up|ord>><around|(|\<ell\>|)>|\|>*<space|0.17em>2<rsup|\<ell\>>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|d>*C<rsub|1><rsup|\<ell\>>*\<Phi\>*w,<space|2em>\<varepsilon\><rsub|d>=<big|sum><rsub|k=0><rsup|d>\<delta\><rsub|k>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-local-probe>
    </equation>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    For each of the four entries in an output row,
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-row-tail> with <math|d<rsub|1>=d<rsub|2>=K> bounds
    the tail by

    <\equation*>
      2*K*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*b/2>*C<rsub|d*k>*<around|(|\<sigma\>/2|)>*M*W<rsub|d*k><around|(|u|)>.
    </equation*>

    There are four input components, so the row difference is at most
    <math|8*K*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*b/2>*C<rsub|d*k>*<around|(|\<sigma\>/2|)>*M*W<rsub|d*k><around|(|u|)>>.
    Since <math|<around|\||u|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>4*m>, this is (9).
    Summing over <math|k\<leqslant\>d> gives a common error bound
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|d>>. For either direction,
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-row-matrix-cost> and the growth bound on its input
    array give <math|4*K*C<rsub|d*k><around|(|\<sigma\>|)>*M*W<rsub|d*k><around|(|u|)>>.
    In the chart the lifted base index has size at most
    <math|4*L*<around|(|m+2|)>>, giving the order-<math|k> bound above; the
    extra <math|1> ensures <math|C<rsub|1>\<geqslant\>1>.

    Here <math|<math-up|Part><rsup|<math-up|ord>><around|(|\<ell\>|)>> is the
    finite index set of ordered finite partitions of the <math|\<ell\>>
    differentiation slots into nonempty blocks, in the canonical ordering
    used by the chain rule; it is not a sum over arbitrary permutations of
    each set partition. For <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-local-probe>, use the
    repeated chain rule in that ordered-finpartition form. When
    <math|\<ell\>=0>, the derivative is <math|F<around|(|<math-up|id>|)>=0>
    by the separate order-zero receipt. For each nonempty-order partition
    <math|\<pi\>> of the <math|\<ell\>> probe derivatives, the chain-rule
    term is the <math|<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>>-linear jet
    <math|\<cal-J\><rsub|<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>>> applied to the path
    derivative <math|\<theta\>*x<rsub|<around|\||B|\|>>> for each block
    <math|B\<in\>\<pi\>>, and by the locality assumption it is unchanged when
    each <math|\<theta\>*x<rsub|<around|\||B|\|>>> is replaced by
    <math|P*<around|(|\<theta\>*x<rsub|<around|\||B|\|>>|)>>. The
    all-reference term, with <math|P*<around|(|\<theta\>*x<rsub|<around|\||B|\|>>|)>>
    replaced by <math|P*<around|(|\<theta\>*y<rsub|<around|\||B|\|>>|)>>,
    vanishes: the block degrees are positive and sum to
    <math|\<ell\>\<leqslant\>d>, so the weighted vanishing hypothesis
    applies. In the normalized norm, each screened reference direction
    <math|P*<around|(|\<theta\>*y<rsub|<around|\||B|\|>>|)>> is bounded by
    <math|C<rsub|1>>, and each <math|P*<around|(|\<theta\>*x<rsub|<around|\||B|\|>>|)>>
    differs from it by at most <math|\<delta\><rsub|<around|\||B|\|>>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|d>>.
    Since <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|d>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|1>>, the multilinear
    comparison lemma <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-jet-comparison>, with
    <math|K=C<rsub|1>>, bounds this chain-rule term by
    <math|2<rsup|<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|d>*C<rsub|1><rsup|<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>>*\<Phi\>*w>,
    which is at most <math|2<rsup|\<ell\>>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|d>*C<rsub|1><rsup|\<ell\>>*\<Phi\>*w>.
    Summing over the finite ordered finpartition index set proves
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-local-probe>.
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|The arrays in the selected construction.> In the selected
  construction the probe is the composition of the local observable with a
  routed test path: the logarithmic-link test curve of the coefficient probe
  is cropped to the chart of radius <math|m> and passed through the
  first-order row operator of the step. This routed path is <math|\<theta\>>
  times a real-analytic path <math|R<around|(|s|)>> of direction arrays with
  <math|R<around|(|0|)>=0>, and the actual direction is
  <math|x<rsub|k>=R<rsup|<around|(|k|)>><around|(|0|)>>. The two input
  arrays, before the row operator is applied, are the following. The
  reference array <math|A<rsup|<around|(|0|)>>=W<rsub|k>> is the <math|k>-th
  derivative at <math|s=0> of the test script, an array on the covering
  lattice; the coefficient-growth receipt is
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|W<rsub|k><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>K*W<rsub|d*k><around|(|y|)>>.
  The actual array is its patched periodic lift

  <\equation*>
    A<rsup|<around|(|1|)>><around|(|z|)>=V<rsub|k><around|(|z|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|W<rsub|k><around|(|<math-up|lift><around|(|<math-up|wrap><space|0.17em>z|)>|)>,>|<cell|<math-up|sep><around|(|0,<math-up|wrap><space|0.17em>z|)>\<leqslant\>m,>>|<row|<cell|0,>|<cell|<text|otherwise>,>>>>>
  </equation*>

  where <math|<math-up|wrap>> is the projection of the covering lattice onto
  the periodic coarse lattice and <math|<math-up|lift>> its centred section;
  it satisfies the same growth bound. The placement hypotheses stated below
  imply <math|V<rsub|k>*<around|(|u+z|)>=W<rsub|k>*<around|(|u+z|)>> for
  <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>\<less\>b> at every kernel base <math|u> that
  occurs, with <math|<around|\||u|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>4*m>. Applying the
  same row operator to <math|W<rsub|k>> and <math|V<rsub|k>> gives the
  reference direction <math|y<rsub|k>> and the actual direction
  <math|x<rsub|k>>. The input arrays agree on the ball of radius <math|b>;
  the two convolved directions need not agree anywhere. The reference
  direction is, on the allowed edges, a polynomial in the spatial coordinates
  of degree at most <math|k-1> when <math|k\<geqslant\>1>; this polynomial
  property is established for the reference direction only, and is the
  membership <math|P*<around|(|\<theta\>*y<rsub|k>|)>\<in\>\<cal-V\><rsub|k>>
  used for <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-weighted-jet-vanishing>. The strip bound
  supplies <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-row-strip>, while the coefficient-growth
  receipt supplies <math|K*W<rsub|d*k>>. Thus the difference is not estimated
  term by term without cancellation: all terms inside the agreement ball
  cancel, and only the exponentially weighted exterior tail is paid.

  The hypotheses under which Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-strip-to-probe>
  is applied in this way are the following; none of them is proved in this
  section.

  <\enumerate>
    <item>The row operator applied to both arrays is literally the
    first-order row of the actual step. It satisfies the strip bound
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-row-strip> with constants <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0>,
    <math|M\<geqslant\>0>; it is identical to the row of the selected kernel,
    and the patch block side of the step equals that kernel's block side
    <math|L>. A strip bound for some other kernel does not suffice.

    <item>Placement: with <math|c> the side of the coarse periodic lattice,
    <math|20*<around|(|m+b+1|)>\<less\>c>; the support <math|X> of the
    observable and the chart region <math|Y> satisfy that the radius-<math|b>
    halo of the parent of every site of <math|X> is contained in <math|Y>,
    and every site of <math|Y> has separation at most <math|m> from the
    origin.

    <item>The observable is regular on <math|X> for the budget, with all
    ordered derivatives of order at most the budget's order <math|n<rsub|B>>
    along normalized directions bounded by <math|w> on healthy
    configurations; this yields (6) with <math|\<Phi\>> the fixed jet price
    of order <math|N>, where <math|d\<leqslant\>N\<leqslant\>n<rsub|B>>.

    <item>The observable has the polynomial-jet vanishing property of order
    <math|d> on <math|X>: its jets at the identity vanish on every list of
    directions whose screens lie in <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|q<rsub|1>>,\<ldots\>,\<cal-V\><rsub|q<rsub|a>>>
    with <math|q<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1> and
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>q<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>d>. This includes
    <math|F<around|(|<math-up|id>|)>=0> as the case of the empty list.

    <item>The growth receipt <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|W<rsub|k><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>K*W<rsub|d*k><around|(|y|)>>
    holds for every <math|k\<leqslant\>d> with one <math|K\<geqslant\>1>, the
    two integer parameters <math|<around|(|p,u|)>> of the selected kernel
    satisfy <math|p\<geqslant\>d> and <math|u\<geqslant\>4>, its real
    parameter is positive, and <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|d>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|1>>.
  </enumerate>

  Under these hypotheses the routed-path identity gives the path
  <math|\<gamma\>=\<theta\>*R>, the tail lemma gives the entrywise error
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|k>> on the allowed edges, the row bound
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-row-matrix-cost> gives
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|y<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|1>> on those
  edges, and the polynomial property of <math|y<rsub|k>> gives
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-weighted-jet-vanishing>; the proposition then yields
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-local-probe> with <math|L> the common block side.

  The argument above proves the Fourier-tail and multilinear comparison once
  the three receipts (strip, growth, and vanishing priced jet) are available.
  It does not prove those upstream receipts, nor does it by itself prove the
  source-placement or healthy-chart hypotheses in the small-support branch.
  Those are separate construction inputs.

  <subsection|A paid estimate for one regular
  source><label|sec:ex-lattice-trace-regular-atom>

  We spell out how a single regular source term is paid in the selected
  one-block transition. The source is split into its real and imaginary
  channels. When the transported support fits inside the receiving budget
  radius, local coefficient probes control the inserted coefficient. When it
  does not fit, a direct supremum estimate controls it. The two cases use
  different fields of the local budget, which is why both the regular and
  large-support inequalities were selected in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-local-budget-choice>. The principal
  insertion attenuation needed in the local estimate is established in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:regular-principal-producer>; the argument
  below keeps the regular source and both support regimes explicit.

  Fix a one-step transition of block length <math|L> between the adjacent
  rungs <math|m> and <math|m+1> of the selected history, at fixed volume and
  period indices, and a coefficient frame of maximum grade <math|d>. Both
  ends of the transition are the effective representations of that one
  history at these indices; the source, its mask, the cloud estimate and the
  resulting coefficient all refer to this same pair. Let <math|f> be a
  complex function of the fine (rung <math|m+1>) configuration and <math|b> a
  fine support such that:

  <\enumerate>
    <item><math|f> is regular on <math|b> for the fine budget, that is, local
    on <math|b>, gauge invariant, measurable, and smooth to the order of the
    budget on configurations healthy on <math|b>;

    <item>the origin belongs to <math|b>, and
    <math|f<around|(|<math-up|id>|)>=0>;

    <item>if <math|b> is nonwrapping about the origin, then for every local
    jet the function <math|s\<mapsto\>f> along the corresponding
    logarithmic-link test curve at the origin is <math|C<rsup|d>> at
    <math|s=0> and all its derivatives of order at most <math|d> vanish
    there;

    <item>there is <math|c\<geqslant\>0> such that, on every configuration
    healthy on <math|b>, every ordered derivative of <math|f> of order at
    most the order of the fine budget, along directions satisfying the fine
    budget's directional bound, has absolute value at most <math|c>.
  </enumerate>

  Condition (4) with the empty list of directions is the pointwise bound
  <math|<around|\||f|\|>\<leqslant\>c> on healthy configurations; the full
  derivative bound, and conditions (1)\U(3), are needed for the local
  estimates below, which do not follow from a value bound. The source term
  itself is the product of the fine effective density with the mask of
  <math|f> on <math|b> at the fine threshold. Let
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|f>\<gtr\>0> be the fine support exponent and
  <math|q<rsub|f>\<geqslant\>0> the fine cover number of <math|b>, and define
  the charged mass

  <\equation*>
    m<rsub|0>=e<rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|f>*q<rsub|f>>*c.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>\<gtr\>0> be the hatted coupling of the
  history at the coarse rung <math|m> (not the actual coupling of the
  finite-volume representation), let <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|f>*<around|(|G,L,<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|)>\<gtr\>0>
  be the fine monitor, and put <math|\<chi\>=<sqrt|\<cal-M\><rsub|f>*<around|(|G,L,<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|)>>>.
  Thus a raw budget <math|b<rsub|0>> gives the actual channel price
  <math|b<rsub|0>/\<chi\>>. Set <math|a=L<rsup|d>>. Let
  <math|O,H,W\<geqslant\>0> be respectively the motion-average norm, local
  readout, and local test factors. Let raw budgets <math|u,v,\<xi\>\<gtr\>0>
  satisfy

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|O<around|(|W*u+2*H*a*v|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<xi\>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-regular-small-budget>>>|<row|<cell|4*O*H*a*v>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<xi\>.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-regular-large-budget>>>>>
  </align>

  These are the regular and large-support paid budgets. In the Wilson
  application, <math|m<rsub|0>> is the mass of the source support with its
  sup-bound; <math|u,v> are chosen by Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-local-paid-budgets>.

  The local estimates needed from the one-step construction have the
  following precise form. The real and imaginary channels have transported
  cloud charges <math|C<rsub|1>,C<rsub|2>> satisfying

  <\equation>
    0\<leqslant\>C<rsub|1>,C<rsub|2>\<leqslant\><frac|v|\<chi\>>*m<rsub|0>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-regular-cloud>
  </equation>

  In the application below both charges are the single cloud response of the
  two-channel source, so <math|C<rsub|1>=C<rsub|2>>, and
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-regular-cloud> is the cloud entry of the selected
  local record at price <math|v>; it is a separate input, not a consequence
  of the principal reserved-mass estimate of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:regular-principal-producer>. If the
  transported support is at most the receiving budget radius, the local probe
  reconstruction error, after both channels are combined, is at most

  <\equation>
    O<around*|(|W*u*\<chi\><rsup|-1>*m<rsub|0>+H*a*<around|(|C<rsub|1>+C<rsub|2>|)>|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-regular-small-read>
  </equation>

  If the transported support exceeds that radius, each channel admits a
  uniform chart bound <math|S<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><frac|v|\<chi\>>*m<rsub|0>>
  for <math|i=1,2>; the resulting two-channel coefficient error is at most

  <\equation>
    O*H*a*<around|(|S<rsub|1>+S<rsub|2>+C<rsub|1>+C<rsub|2>|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-regular-large-read>
  </equation>

  Here the <em|coefficient error> is measured uniformly over the finite set
  of lattice motions, and <math|O> pays for this uniformity. In either case
  the local construction also supplies the following certificate, in which
  <math|\<rho\>=L<rsup|-2/5>> is the one-step remainder rate. There is one
  coarse coefficient <math|X>, chosen before any motion, such that for every
  motion <math|\<omega\>> there is a coarse presentation
  <math|P<rsub|\<omega\>>> with coefficient <math|\<omega\>*X> which has the
  order-zero format of the coarse representation, has remainder cost at most
  <math|\<rho\>*m<rsub|0>> at the normalization
  <math|<sqrt|<math-up|forcedAllowance><rsub|m>>>, and has the exact pushed
  density

  <\equation>
    D<around|(|P<rsub|\<omega\>>|)>=\<omega\>\<cdot\><around*|[|L<rsup|d>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-T\><rsub|m><around*|(|\<varrho\><rsub|m+1><space|0.17em><math-up|mask><rsub|b><space|0.17em>f|)>|]>,<label|eq:ex-lattice-regular-pushed-density>
  </equation>

  where <math|\<varrho\><rsub|m+1>> is the fine effective density,
  <math|<math-up|mask><rsub|b><space|0.17em>f> the mask of <math|f> on
  <math|b> at the fine threshold, <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|m>> the one-step push
  at the coupling of rung <math|m+1>, and <math|\<omega\>\<cdot\>> the action
  of the motion on functions. The presentation may depend on the motion; the
  coefficient <math|X> does not. The estimates
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-regular-small-read> and
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-regular-large-read> are bounds for
  <math|sup<rsub|\<omega\>><around|\<\|\|\>|\<omega\>*X|\<\|\|\>>>, the
  predicted leading coefficient of a regular source being zero. These are the
  actual local chart and transport conclusions used below, not consequences
  of the scalar budget inequalities alone.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Regular source atom><label|prop:ex-lattice-regular-source-atom>Assume
    the local estimates <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-regular-cloud>\U
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-regular-large-read> and the coarse-format, density,
    and remainder conclusions just stated. If
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-regular-small-budget>\U
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-regular-large-budget> hold, then the source term
    produces one coarse coefficient <math|X> with
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<omega\>*X|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<xi\>*\<chi\><rsup|-1>*m<rsub|0>>
    for every motion <math|\<omega\>>, together with presentations
    <math|P<rsub|\<omega\>>> of <math|\<omega\>*X> in the coarse order-zero
    format having the exact pushed density
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-regular-pushed-density> and remainder at most
    <math|\<rho\>*m<rsub|0>>. In the terminology of the finite price
    recurrence, this is the regular-source estimate with cost pair
    <math|<around|(|\<xi\>*\<chi\><rsup|-1>*m<rsub|0>,\<rho\>*m<rsub|0>|)>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    First suppose the transported support is within the receiving radius. By
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-regular-cloud>,

    <\equation*>
      O<around*|(|W*u*\<chi\><rsup|-1>*m<rsub|0>+H*a*<around|(|C<rsub|1>+C<rsub|2>|)>|)>\<leqslant\>O<around|(|W*u+2*H*a*v|)>*\<chi\><rsup|-1>*m<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>\<xi\>*\<chi\><rsup|-1>*m<rsub|0>,
    </equation*>

    where the last inequality is <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-regular-small-budget>.
    The local probe reconstruction estimate
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-regular-small-read> therefore gives the claimed
    coefficient error.

    Now suppose the transported support exceeds the receiving radius. The
    uniform chart estimate and the two channel bounds give

    <\equation*>
      O*H*a*<around|(|S<rsub|1>+S<rsub|2>+C<rsub|1>+C<rsub|2>|)>\<leqslant\>O*H*a*<around|(|4*v*\<chi\><rsup|-1>*m<rsub|0>|)>=4*O*H*a*v*\<chi\><rsup|-1>*m<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>\<xi\>*\<chi\><rsup|-1>*m<rsub|0>,
    </equation*>

    by <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-regular-large-budget>. Thus the large-support
    estimate gives the same coefficient error. The two cases exhaust the
    support sizes. In both cases, the local construction supplies the exact
    pushed-density identity, coarse format, and remainder bound
    <math|\<rho\>*m<rsub|0>>; these components are unchanged by the numerical
    comparison. This proves the assertion.
  </proof>

  We now describe how the local hypotheses are realized in the selected
  construction. The source term is turned into its real and imaginary masked
  channels. Both channels carry the same root label, the logarithmic key of
  the regular activity with support <math|b>. Their two reserved-mass bounds
  are combined at the single expansion index <math|i<rsub|0>> attached to
  that label, where the assigned cost is <math|L<rsup|d>*<around|(|c<rsub|<math-up|re>>+c<rsub|<math-up|im>>|)>>
  with <math|c<rsub|<math-up|re>>,c<rsub|<math-up|im>>> the derivative bounds
  of the two transported channels; every other expansion index is assigned
  cost zero. The per-channel reserved-mass estimate used for this format and
  remainder certificate is provided by the regular insertion result of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:regular-principal-producer>; it does not
  supply the cloud bound <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-regular-cloud> or the
  large-support chart bound below, which use two further entries of the
  selected local record. In the small-support regime, the finite local probes
  fit inside the chart, and the local reconstruction bound is the test-price
  term <math|W*u*\<chi\><rsup|-1>*m<rsub|0>> plus the two readout terms
  <math|H*a*C<rsub|1>> and <math|H*a*C<rsub|2>>. In the large-support regime,
  the direct chart supremum bound replaces the probes, leaving two chart and
  two cloud readout contributions; this is exactly the factor four in
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-regular-large-budget>. The cloud inequality controls
  each <math|C<rsub|i>>, and the insertion construction carries the same
  source through the block with its density identity. The finite price
  recurrence then sums these single-source costs over the countable regular
  family using its absolute mass budget.

  More explicitly, let <math|q<rsub|\<tau\>>> be the coefficient in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-local-paid-budgets>. In the
  small-support case, the chart estimate gives, for each recovery probe
  <math|\<tau\>>, a contribution at most <math|q<rsub|\<tau\>>*v*\<chi\><rsup|-1>*m<rsub|0>>.
  The cycle budget gives <math|q<rsub|\<tau\>>*v\<leqslant\>u>; finite
  coefficient recovery therefore bounds the probe part by
  <math|W*u*\<chi\><rsup|-1>*m<rsub|0>>. The two real/imaginary cloud
  contributions are bounded by <math|H*a*<around|(|C<rsub|1>+C<rsub|2>|)>>,
  and the regular budget in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-regular-small-budget> pays
  their sum. The chart construction has enough room that the support is
  nonwrapping and every probe stencil lies in the healthy region; the ordered
  derivative bounds through order <math|d> are exactly the ones used by the
  finite recovery map. This is the local probe conclusion of the
  small-support construction, not an inference from the value bound alone.

  For the large-support case, the local field is built by its support
  reservation. The reservation includes the factor <math|exp
  <around|(|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|c>+2|)>*q<rsub|i>|)>\<geqslant\>1>,
  where <math|\<alpha\><rsub|c>\<gtr\>0> is the receiving support exponent
  and <math|q<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0> the channel's coarse cover number, so its
  unweighted supremum cost is no larger than its reserved mass. The
  construction bounds that reserved mass using the one-site talent estimate
  times the source mass. That estimate is at most <math|v/\<chi\>>, hence
  each channel has <math|S<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><around|(|v/\<chi\>|)>*m<rsub|0>>.
  The cloud bounds likewise give <math|C<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><around|(|v/\<chi\>|)>*m<rsub|0>>.
  Applying the local readout bound to the two chart bounds and the two cloud
  terms yields <math|O*H*a*<around|(|S<rsub|1>+S<rsub|2>+C<rsub|1>+C<rsub|2>|)>\<leqslant\>4*O*H*a*v*m<rsub|0>/\<chi\>>.
  The large-support budget pays this quantity. In both cases the chosen
  support labels retain the same fine source and the transition preserves its
  density, while the one-site reservation and turn-price inequalities give
  the remainder <math|\<rho\>*m<rsub|0>>. The local chart statements used in
  these paragraphs are proved for the same insertion in
  Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:trace-regular-chart-construction>
  below, by the actual/reference comparison and the weighted one-site
  expansion, respectively.

  In the small-support construction, the image of the source support is first
  enlarged by the transport halo. The geometric placement puts this enlarged
  set in a parent patch and bounds every point of that patch's support by the
  chart radius. The condition

  <\equation*>
    20*<around|(|r<rsub|d>+R<rsub|d>+1|)>\<less\>n<rsub|0>
  </equation*>

  then makes both the transported source support and the parent patch
  nonwrapping; <math|r<rsub|d>> is the chart radius, <math|R<rsub|d>> the
  transport reach, and <math|n<rsub|0>> the parent side length. The local
  construction gives the actual field <math|U<rsub|i>> and its smooth
  reference <math|V<rsub|i>> on one healthy chart. For each channel weight
  <math|w<rsub|i>>, and for every ordered list
  <math|<around|(|A<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,A<rsub|\<ell\>>|)>> of at most <math|d>
  directions each satisfying the directional bound of the receiving budget at
  the origin (entrywise at most the receiving threshold times the spatial
  weight), the ordered derivatives at the identity satisfy

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||\<partial\><rsub|A<rsub|1>>*\<cdots\>*\<partial\><rsub|A<rsub|\<ell\>>>*<around|(|U<rsub|i>-V<rsub|i>|)><around|(|<math-up|id>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|v/\<chi\>|)>*w<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  This is a bound on these normalized directions only, not an operator-norm
  bound for the derivative. For every coefficient-recovery probe
  <math|\<tau\>\<in\>\<cal-T\>>, evaluated at parameter zero along its test
  path, one also has

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<partial\><rsup|\<tau\>><rsub|0>*V<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|v/\<chi\>|)>*w<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  The probe stencil lies in the healthy chart. The first bound controls the
  local variation correction in the reconstruction, and the second is the
  probe-price input. The finite recovery map and its probe-price sum
  therefore give the <math|W*u*\<chi\><rsup|-1>*m<rsub|0>> contribution;
  applying the same row decomposition to the two real channels gives the
  separate <math|H*a*<around|(|C<rsub|1>+C<rsub|2>|)>> contribution. This
  supplies the stated small-support estimate with the correct one-block
  normalization.

  In the large-support construction, the transported cover number is larger
  than the receiving radius. For each real channel, the support-reservation
  argument constructs a regular field with supremum bound <math|S<rsub|i>>
  and a reserved mass

  <\equation*>
    R<rsub|i>=e<rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|c>+2|)>*q<rsub|i>>*S<rsub|i>,<space|2em>R<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><around|(|v/\<chi\>|)>*m<rsub|0>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<alpha\><rsub|c>\<gtr\>0> is the receiving support exponent
  and <math|q<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0> is the channel's coarse cover number. The
  second inequality combines the support-reservation estimate, which bounds
  <math|R<rsub|i>> by the tuition of the selected local record times
  <math|m<rsub|0>>, with the tuition entry of that record, which is at most
  <math|v/\<chi\>>. The exponential factor is at least one, so
  <math|S<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><around|(|v/\<chi\>|)>*m<rsub|0>>.
  Independently, the cloud estimate gives
  <math|C<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><around|(|v/\<chi\>|)>*m<rsub|0>>. Thus the two
  chart bounds and two cloud charges together cost at most

  <\equation*>
    O*H*a*<around|(|S<rsub|1>+S<rsub|2>+C<rsub|1>+C<rsub|2>|)>\<leqslant\>4*O*H*a*v*\<chi\><rsup|-1>*m<rsub|0>,
  </equation*>

  which is paid by the large-support inequality. This is the large-support
  estimate used in the proposition; it keeps the chart and cloud
  contributions separate because they come from different source bounds.

  <subsubsection|Construction of the two local
  estimates><label|subsec:trace-regular-chart-construction>

  We give the chart argument on the actual insertion, rather than assuming
  its two conclusions. Fix one real channel <math|f<rsub|i>>, of weight
  <math|w<rsub|i>>, of the masked source. Use the parent support and the
  forest/tag variables of the one-block expansion of this insertion. Define
  <math|U<rsub|i>> to be its distinguished local row in that expansion. It is
  the forest/tag average of the normalized actual-sector insertion integral.
  Define <math|G<rsub|i>> by replacing that integral with the
  Gaussian-reference integral, with the same masked ingredient and read set.
  Finally let <math|V<rsub|i>> be the source evaluated on the deterministic
  reference field, after cropping the coarse logarithmic field to radius
  <math|r<rsub|d>>. All three functions use the same fine source
  <math|f<rsub|i>>; only the local field on which it is evaluated changes.
  The cutoff defining the first two integrals is compactly supported in the
  finite coordinate chart. Differentiation under the integral and the finite
  or probability averages is justified by its derivative majorants. Locality,
  gauge covariance of the chart, and regularity of <math|f<rsub|i>> show that
  <math|U<rsub|i>> is regular on its parent support. The reference
  <math|V<rsub|i>> is <math|C<rsup|d>> near the identity by composition.

  Suppose first that the cover of the transported support is at most the
  receiving radius. Its transport halo lies in the parent support; the
  selected cover placement bounds the latter by radius <math|r<rsub|d>>. For
  a direction <math|A> replace it by <math|A<rsup|<math-up|loc>>>, equal to
  <math|A> on that radius and zero outside. Every ordered derivative of
  <math|U<rsub|i>> and <math|V<rsub|i>> at the identity is unchanged by this
  replacement, by locality, including derivatives of order zero. Moreover
  <math|A<rsup|<math-up|loc>>> still satisfies the receiving directional
  bound and obeys the uniform canopy bound
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsup|<math-up|loc>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*\<theta\><rsub|f>*<around|(|1+r<rsub|d>|)><rsup|p<rsub|c>>>.
  The actual/reference comparison in Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-lattice-eve-matching>,
  at its selected price, therefore gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<partial\><rsub|A<rsub|1>>*\<cdots\>*\<partial\><rsub|A<rsub|\<ell\>>>*<around|(|U<rsub|i>-G<rsub|i>|)><around|(|<math-up|id>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|v/\<chi\>|)>*w<rsub|i>,<space|2em>\<ell\>\<leqslant\>d.
  </equation*>

  The forest and tag averages preserve this bound because their weights are
  nonnegative and sum to one.

  Here is the additional comparison from <math|G<rsub|i>> to
  <math|V<rsub|i>>. Write <math|z> for the Gaussian coordinates and
  <math|p<around|(|z|)>\<geqslant\>1> for their polynomial size envelope. The
  deterministic crop and fluctuation estimates give a discrepancy
  <math|J*p<around|(|z|)><rsup|2>> in the logarithmic arguments, with
  <math|0\<leqslant\>J\<leqslant\>1>. The same quantity bounds the cutoff
  error; the cutoff is one on the protected read set. Apply the fundamental
  theorem of calculus between the two arguments, then differentiate in the
  normalized coarse directions. The finite product rule and chain rule,
  through order <math|\<ell\>\<leqslant\>d\<less\>n>, bound the resulting
  integrand difference by

  <\equation*>
    w<rsub|i>*C<rsub|n>*J*<space|0.17em>p<around|(|z|)><rsup|4*<around|(|n+1|)>>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed each differentiated argument contributes at most a constant times
  <math|p<around|(|z|)><rsup|2>>; the first difference contributes
  <math|J*p<around|(|z|)><rsup|2>>, and the at most <math|n> differentiated
  factors and their finite partitions are absorbed in
  <math|C<rsub|n>*p<around|(|z|)><rsup|4*<around|(|n+1|)>>>. The source
  derivative bounds through the purchased order supply the factor
  <math|w<rsub|i>>. Terms differentiating the cutoff obey the same estimate,
  with its protected-ball discrepancy included in <math|J>. The Gaussian
  moment bound is finite and uniform in the finite-volume choices; denote it
  by <math|M<rsub|4*<around|(|n+1|)>>>. Here <math|J> is the full
  response-plus-paint cost <math|F<around|(|x|)>> of
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-explicit-local-cost>; in particular it includes the
  deterministic crop term estimated in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-paint-bound>. The
  response cap alone omits that term and would not pay this comparison. The
  support placement above gives exactly the support and threshold conditions
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-budget-support>, with the fine budget as <math|B> and
  the receiving budget as <math|P>. Apply
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-selected-local-budget> at the
  same order <math|n>, block <math|L>, and target <math|y=v>. Its clock is
  linear in the insertion weight, with coefficient <math|C<rsub|n>>. Its
  conclusion <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-budget-output> gives simultaneously

  <\equation*>
    J\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>C<rsub|n>*J*M<rsub|4*<around|(|n+1|)>>\<leqslant\>v/\<chi\>,
  </equation*>

  uniformly on these supports and normalized directions. The threshold for
  this full cap is included in the selected local record before the volume,
  period, forest and tags are chosen. Integrating the displayed majorant
  proves

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<partial\><rsub|A<rsub|1>>*\<cdots\>*\<partial\><rsub|A<rsub|\<ell\>>>*<around|(|G<rsub|i>-V<rsub|i>|)><around|(|<math-up|id>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|v/\<chi\>|)>*w<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  Thus the triangle inequality gives exactly the
  <math|2*<around|(|v/\<chi\>|)>*w<rsub|i>> normalized directional derivative
  bound stated above. This factor two combines two comparisons; it does not
  enlarge the cloud price.

  The support is nonwrapping because <math|20*<around|(|r<rsub|d>+R<rsub|d>+1|)>\<less\>n<rsub|0>>.
  The fine support is also nonwrapping: its distance from its anchor is at
  most <math|L*<around|(|r<rsub|d>+2|)>>, while its period is
  <math|L*n<rsub|0>>. Consequently condition (3) on <math|f<rsub|i>> applies.
  On a coefficient test path, the deterministic reference field has the
  screened polynomial jet plus the cropped Fourier-row tail. The
  all-polynomial term has zero reading by condition (3). Expand each
  remaining multilinear term by replacing one or more of its arguments by the
  row tail. The weighted convolution and jet-gap estimate of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-strip-to-probe> bounds the
  finite sum by <math|<around|(|v/\<chi\>|)>*w<rsub|i>> at each selected
  probe. This uses the actual row of the selected history: the reference
  field was defined using that row, so no new kernel or test direction is
  substituted here. Finite coefficient recovery now gives the small-support
  estimate <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-regular-small-read>, with the already
  displayed cycle prices <math|q<rsub|\<tau\>>*v\<leqslant\>u>.

  For the large-support estimate no polynomial test or nonwrapping assumption
  is needed. Let <math|q> be the receiving cover number, <math|n> its order,
  <math|p> its directional power, and <math|\<alpha\><rsub|c>> its support
  exponent. The same actual expansion assigns its distinguished row an
  absolute coefficient <math|o\<geqslant\>0> and an insertion charge
  <math|d<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>. Its weighted one-site sum, restricted to
  this single row, gives

  <\equation*>
    o<space|0.17em>e<rsup|<around|(|a+e|)>*q>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>a\<geqslant\>\<alpha\><rsub|c>+3,<space|1em>e\<geqslant\>0,<space|2em>d<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><math-up|goal><rsup|-1><space|0.17em>m<rsub|0>.
  </equation*>

  The last inequality retains the fine source mass; the additional
  fine-support attenuation factor is at most one. Differentiating this row in
  the receiving normalized directions costs at most

  <\equation*>
    S<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>d<rsub|i>*o<space|0.17em>A<around|(|x|)><rsup|n*p>*<around|(|1+q|)><rsup|n*p>,<space|2em>A<around|(|x|)>=4*<around|(|2*b<around|(|x|)>+1|)>.
  </equation*>

  These statements follow by taking one nonnegative term of the absolute
  one-site expansion bound; thus they apply to its actual distinguished row,
  rather than to a separately chosen surrogate. Gauge invariance and
  regularity of that row follow from the same insertion integral as in the
  small-support case.

  The elementary polynomial estimate <math|<around|(|1+q|)><rsup|n*p>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|1/2>*<around|(|n*p|)>*e<rsup|q/2>>
  gives, since <math|q\<geqslant\>b<around|(|x|)>>,

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|e<rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|c>+2|)>*q>*S<rsub|i>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>d<rsub|i>*A<around|(|x|)><rsup|n*p>*P<rsub|1/2>*<around|(|n*p|)>*o*e<rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|c>+5/2|)>*q>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>d<rsub|i>*A<around|(|x|)><rsup|n*p>*P<rsub|1/2>*<around|(|n*p|)>*e<rsup|-b<around|(|x|)>/2>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<cal-T\><rsub|Q>*<around|(|x;n,p|)>*m<rsub|0>\<leqslant\><around|(|v/\<chi\>|)>*m<rsub|0>.>>>>
  </align*>

  The middle step uses the full reserved exponent <math|\<alpha\><rsub|c>+2>
  and the spare unit in <math|a\<geqslant\>\<alpha\><rsub|c>+3>; it would
  fail if that reserve were silently dropped. The last step is
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-medal-tuition-price>. Since the
  exponential reserve is at least one, this proves the required bound on
  <math|S<rsub|i>> itself. The cloud charge is paid separately by
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-medal-cloud-price>. Applying
  coefficient readout to these two contributions in each real channel proves
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-regular-large-read>.

  Finally all rows were obtained from the same masked insertion expansion.
  Recombining its rows therefore gives the exact one-step pushed density. The
  two real channels carry the same source key; their derivative prices are
  combined at that single key. The principal reserved-mass bound of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:regular-principal-producer>, followed by the
  selected turn price, pays <math|L<rsup|d>*<around|(|c<rsub|<math-up|re>>+c<rsub|<math-up|im>>|)>>
  there and zero at the other keys, with total remainder at most
  <math|\<rho\>*m<rsub|0>>. Motion acts on this constructed coefficient and
  on its presentation; it does not require a new coefficient choice. This
  proves the format and density certificate as well as both local estimates
  used in the proposition.

  <subsection|Atomic responses and the finite-volume root
  price><label|sec:ex-lattice-trace-coffer-supplier>

  This section records the actual transfer used by the public trace supplier.
  There are two distinct operations. An atomic response first becomes a
  finite-volume response with a root-price estimate. The price estimate is
  then converted into the two components of a Bill. We give the conversions
  and the quantitative endpoint of the Coffer argument, while keeping the
  upstream response, paid-price, and price-duality receipts explicit.

  <subsubsection|From an atomic response to the Coffer input>

  Let <math|X> be an order-zero coefficient at scale <math|k>, <math|g> its
  local field, and <math|Z=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>. Write <math|x<rsub|X>>
  for its coordinate, <math|X<rsup|<math-up|f>>> for its forced part, and
  <math|D<rsub|X>> for its density. The atomic certificate supplies a
  nonnegative remainder allowance <math|s>, a response <math|L> with
  attainment cost at most <math|e>, and the identities

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|X>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>+s>|<cell|\<leqslant\>q,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-trace-atomd-charge>>>|<row|<cell|<math-up|profile><around|(|X<rsup|<math-up|f>>|)>>|<cell|=<math-up|hook><around|(|L|)>,<eq-number>>>|<row|<cell|D<rsub|X><around|(|U|)>>|<cell|=<math-up|eval><around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>g<around|(|U|)>.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-trace-atomd-data>>>>>
  </align>

  The remainder bound is measured at scale <math|k>, at order zero and with
  tolerance <math|<sqrt|<math-up|forcedAllowance><rsub|k>>>. The statement
  that the response <math|L=<around|(|L<rsub|Y>|)><rsub|Y>>, indexed by
  supports <math|Y>, has attainment cost at most <math|e> is a certificate,
  not a scalar equality: every component <math|L<rsub|Y>> is measurable and
  gauge invariant, vanishes at every configuration that is healthy on
  <math|Y> at the threshold of the scale-<math|k> budget, and takes the same
  value at two configurations whose links on <math|Y> agree and whose
  threshold flags in <math|Y> agree; <math|L<rsub|Y>\<neq\>0> only if
  <math|Y> contains the marked origin, and there are numbers
  <math|a<rsub|Y>\<geqslant\>0> with

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||L<rsub|Y><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|Y><text| for all
    >U,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|Y>e<rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|k>+2|)><math-up|cov><rsub|k><around|(|Y|)>>*a<rsub|Y>\<leqslant\>e,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<alpha\><rsub|k>> is the support exponent and
  <math|<math-up|cov><rsub|k>> the cover number of that budget. Set
  <math|u=<math-up|alto><rsub|k,d>>, the inverse field dilation, and
  <math|G<rprime|'>=u*g>. Multiplying the density identity by <math|u> gives

  <\equation*>
    u*D<rsub|X><around|(|U|)>=<math-up|eval><around|(|U|)>*G<rprime|'><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  Thus the atomic response becomes the Coffer input, with unchanged format,
  remainder and forced-profile data.

  The selected atomic charge budget satisfies

  <\equation>
    4*<around|(|1+c<rsub|k>|)>*q\<leqslant\>Z<rsup|3>,<label|eq:ex-trace-coffer-budget>
  </equation>

  where <math|c<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>1> is the selected column allowance of the
  history at scale <math|k>, a real parameter of the stage data and not a
  cardinality. Hence

  <\equation*>
    4*<around|(|1+c<rsub|k>|)>*<around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|X>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>+s|)>\<leqslant\>Z<rsup|3>.
  </equation*>

  The second needed comparison is <math|Z<math-up|fly><rsub|d><around|(|k|)>\<leqslant\>Z<rsup|2>>,
  using <math|<math-up|fly><rsub|d><around|(|k|)>\<leqslant\>Z> and
  <math|Z\<geqslant\>1>. The price construction chooses the atomic charge
  allowance so both comparisons hold.

  The endpoint price-duality estimate used below has the following exact
  meaning. A response duet <math|P> runs from a level <math|\<ell\>> to
  <math|k\<leqslant\>N> in the same selected history. At each intermediate
  level its coefficient has admissible order-zero format and remainder bound;
  its marked site is the spatial origin and these sites are related by the
  one-step scale map. The coefficient and cost changes satisfy the paid
  direction inequality, and the densities satisfy the exact one-step transfer
  identity. Its left endpoint coefficient vector is zero, and its terminal
  cost obeys the spread estimate

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|cost><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>w+c<rsub|k>*<around*|(|w+<around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|k>.*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>|)>,<space|2em>w\<geqslant\>0.
  </equation*>

  Write <math|\<kappa\><rsub|k>\<gtr\>0> for the square root of the
  level-<math|k> monitor. The endpoint estimate, which is required for all
  sufficiently large period indices, uniformly in the volume index
  <math|N\<geqslant\>k>, in the paid comparison and in <math|w\<geqslant\>0>,
  is

  <\equation>
    \<kappa\><rsub|k>*<space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|k>.*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>\<leqslant\>4*<around|(|1+c<rsub|k>|)>*w.<label|eq:ex-trace-sharp-read>
  </equation>

  The zero in the left-end condition is a coefficient vector; the marked
  sites are at the spatial origin, not zero-valued observations. The finite
  recurrence and adjacent-level estimate are given in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-price-recurrence>. The
  paid-cone estimate is supplied by the canonical argument in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:reference-cone>, under its explicit
  direction, room, bank, and endpoint-spread assumptions. None of these
  bounds follows from the atomic response alone.

  <subsubsection|The finite Coffer transfer>

  We keep the following parameters distinct. As above,
  <math|c<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>1> is the selected column allowance at level
  <math|k>. Let <math|X> be the input coefficient, <math|S> its remainder
  budget, <math|E> the paid response and <math|e> its attainment cost, and
  <math|\<zeta\><rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> its payment allowance. Let
  <math|a\<in\><around|(|0,1|]>> be the mask fraction,
  <math|v<rprime|'>\<geqslant\>0> the forced-allowance budget, and
  <math|q,B\<gtr\>0> two coefficient budgets. The actual Coffer hypotheses
  include

  <\equation>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|X>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>+S\<leqslant\>q,<space|2em>4*<around|(|1+c<rsub|k>|)>*q\<leqslant\>B,<space|2em>e*\<zeta\><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>v<rprime|'>*B.<label|eq:ex-trace-coffer-inputs>
  </equation>

  They also include exact coefficient format, remainder, payment, density and
  forced-profile identities; scale growth and one-step response receipts; and
  the melting condition. If <math|B<rsub|k>> is the spatial-spacing parameter
  of the scale budget and <math|R<rsub|k>=<math-up|siteFactor><around|(|B<rsub|k>|)>>,
  the latter requires support exponent at least four and

  <\equation*>
    t=<math-up|forcedAllowance><rsub|k>+R<rsub|k>*<sqrt|<math-up|forcedAllowance><rsub|k>>+a+R<rsub|k>*v<rprime|'>,<space|2em><around|(|2*B<rsub|k>+1|)><rsup|4>*t\<leqslant\>3,<space|2em>e<rsup|t>-1\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|B<rsub|k>> is not the coefficient budget <math|B> in
  <eqref|eq:ex-trace-coffer-inputs>.

  For <math|J\<leqslant\>m\<less\>k\<less\>N>, let
  <math|<math-up|gain><around|(|k,m|)>\<gtr\>0> denote the scale gain from
  <math|m> to <math|k>. The output weight is

  <\equation>
    w<rsub|m>=a*<space|0.17em><frac|\<zeta\><rsub|m>/2|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><rsub|m,d>|\<\|\|\>>*<around|(|B/<math-up|gain><around|(|k,m|)>|)>>.<label|eq:ex-trace-coffer-weight>
  </equation>

  For clarity, if <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|J>> is the set of reflected
  scale-<math|J> cells in the volume-<math|N> tiling and
  <math|K=<around|\||\<cal-T\><rsub|J>|\|>>, with <math|\<mu\><rsub|N,p>> the
  normalized Wilson measure, the root functional is

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|root><rsub|J,N,p><around|(|F|)>=<around*|\||<big|int><big|prod><rsub|x\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|J>><math-up|copy><rsub|x><around|(|F|)><around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|N,p><around|(|U|)>|\|><rsup|1/K>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|<math-up|copy><rsub|x><around|(|F|)>> evaluates <math|F> on the
  translated reflection half of the configuration; when that reflection
  reverses orientation it also complex-conjugates the value. This is the
  reflection-positive tiling functional, with its <math|K>th-root
  normalization.

  Assume <math|N\<gtr\>k> and that the reference level is no larger than
  <math|k>. The response data are an admissible sequence of coefficient
  coefficient families from the reference level through <math|N>, an
  admissible top coefficient, an exact paid response identity between each
  adjacent pair, and the terminal forced-profile identity. The input field
  has the format, remainder, attainment, and density identities stated above.
  Assume also the melting and payment inequalities and the endpoint estimate
  <eqref|eq:ex-trace-sharp-read>. Put

  <\equation*>
    <wide|G|~><rprime|'>=<math-up|back><rsub|k\<to\>N><space|0.17em>G<rprime|'>.
  </equation*>

  Under these hypotheses the finite construction returns one public label
  <math|b>, chosen before any window, with the following two properties.
  First,

  <\equation>
    b<rprime|'>*<space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|X>-\<cal-C\><rsub|k><around|(|b|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>\<leqslant\>4*<around|(|1+c<rsub|k>|)>*q.<label|eq:ex-trace-coffer-column>
  </equation>

  Here the window from scale <math|J> to scale <math|m> is called <em|open>
  if

  <\equation*>
    J\<leqslant\>m,<space|2em>\<sigma\><rsub|m><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|J>/A<rsub|m>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<sigma\><rsub|m>> is the cover size of the selected clock at
  level <math|m> and <math|A<rsub|j>> the field dilation. Second, for the
  same <math|b> and for every triple <math|<around|(|J,m,l|)>> such that the
  scale-<math|J>-to-<math|m> window is open, the reference level is no larger
  than <math|m>, <math|m\<less\>k>, the scale <math|J> has room for the
  radius <math|l> at scale <math|k>, and <math|G<rprime|'>> is interior to
  the window of radius <math|l>,

  <\equation>
    <math-up|root><rsub|J,N,p><around|(|<wide|G|~><rprime|'>-\<cal-O\><rsub|N,p>*b|)>\<leqslant\><frac|<math-up|stakes><around|(|J,m|)>|w<rsub|m>>.<label|eq:ex-trace-coffer-root>
  </equation>

  The order of quantifiers matters: a label depending on the window would not
  give a Bill, whose root estimate is required for all windows with one
  label. Here <math|b<rprime|'>> is the endpoint price-duality constant and
  <math|<math-up|root><rsub|J,N,p>> is the reflection-positive root
  functional on the volume-<math|N> field, defined using the scale-<math|J>
  tiling. In this application the endpoint constant <math|b<rprime|'>> is
  <math|\<kappa\><rsub|k>>. The column <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|k><around|(|b|)>>
  is defined through the bare reference coordinate of the public label: if
  <math|<math-ss|T>> denotes the terminal identification of the selected
  response hierarchy, a linear isomorphism of the grade-<math|d> coefficient
  space onto itself which sends bare reference coordinates <math|v> to public
  labels, and <math|I<rsub|k>*v> is the coefficient of the calibrated primary
  field <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|k><around|(|v|)>> at level <math|k>, then

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-C\><rsub|k><around|(|b|)>=I<rsub|k><around|(|<math-ss|T><rsup|-1>b|)>.
  </equation*>

  The construction below produces a bare coordinate <math|v> and sets
  <math|b=<math-ss|T>v>; the column estimate is proved for
  <math|I<rsub|k>*v>, while the observation <math|\<cal-O\><rsub|N,p>*b> in
  <eqref|eq:ex-trace-coffer-root> is that of the public label. That the
  level-<math|k> entries of the selected response hierarchy, read at
  <math|<math-ss|T><rsup|-1>b>, are these calibrated primary fields is an
  identity of the selected hierarchy, used here as part of the response data.

  For the column estimate, apply the signed reference-difference construction
  of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:reference-difference> to the Coffer
  coefficient field <math|X> at terminal level <math|k>, with its remainder
  bound <math|S> and total charge <math|q>. The construction assumes one
  fixed background history and calibrated primary fields
  <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|v|)>> whose coefficient is
  <math|I<rsub|i>*v>, together with the bank and column inequalities
  <eqref|eq:reference-bank> and the exact degree-<math|d> density transition.
  At each backward step it first transports the current zero-coefficient
  remainder by the zero-coordinate transition
  <eqref|eq:reference-zero-transition>; it then subtracts the primary field
  with the resulting coefficient, so that the new remainder again has
  coefficient zero. Linearity cancels the two primary densities exactly, and
  the zero-coordinate transition supplies the density of the transported
  remainder. The paid direction and remainder estimates
  <eqref|eq:reference-direction> apply to the resulting signed difference,
  not to an unsigned estimate obtained by subtracting costs.

  The top family has terminal charge at most <math|q>, since
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|X>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>+S\<leqslant\>q>.
  Choose its single bare reference coordinate <math|v> by setting the left
  endpoint coefficient to zero, and put <math|b=<math-ss|T>v>. The output is
  precisely <eqref|eq:reference-produced-difference>: for the chosen
  coordinate, the terminal coefficient is
  <math|x<rsub|X>-I<rsub|k>*v=x<rsub|X>-\<cal-C\><rsub|k><around|(|b|)>>, the
  terminal density is <math|D<rsub|X>-D<rsub|\<cal-P\><rsub|k><around|(|v|)>>>,
  and the left endpoint coefficient vector vanishes. This choice does not
  involve any window <math|<around|(|J,m,l|)>>. Every intermediate field and
  the selected reference field are evaluated against the same finest volume
  and background history; this is what permits the exact density
  cancellation. At the terminal level the reference construction also retains
  its spread bound <math|r<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>q+c<rsub|k>*<around|(|q+<around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsub|k>.*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>|)>>,
  the precise spread condition in the Woke duet. Thus it applies
  <math|<math-up|SharpRead>> with budget <math|w=q>. The stronger full
  terminal estimate in <eqref|eq:reference-produced-difference>, with
  <math|C<rsub|k>=c<rsub|k>>, gives <math|r<rsub|k>+<around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsub|k>.*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>\<leqslant\>4*<around|(|1+c<rsub|k>|)>*q>;
  this is the coarse transport budget used by the subsequent construction.
  The signed dualization then gives the zero-site paid-response duet required
  by SharpRead, and its endpoint inequality is exactly
  <eqref|eq:ex-trace-coffer-column>. Here zero refers to the left coefficient
  vector and to the duet's marked spatial site being the origin. It does not
  assert that the observed field values vanish.

  <subparagraph|The planted polynomial and its full moment.> Fix an admitted
  window <math|<around|(|J,m,l|)>> and retain the label <math|b> already
  chosen. Let <math|V> be its finite set of cells. Work in the square-free
  polynomial algebra with generators <math|<math-ss|t><rsub|a>>,
  <math|a\<in\>V>; for <math|I\<subseteq\>V> write <math|<around|[|I|]>*Q>
  for the coefficient of <math|<big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>I><math-ss|t><rsub|a>>.
  Let <math|D=\<cal-O\><rsub|N,p>*b> and <math|F=G<rprime|'>>, and denote
  their prescribed copies at levels <math|N> and <math|k> by <math|D<rsub|a>>
  and <math|F<rsub|a>>. A copy includes complex conjugation when its
  reflected chamber requires it. Write <math|R<rsub|N>> for the unnormalized
  Wilson density, <math|<math-ss|P><rsub|v,u>> for density transfer from
  level <math|u> to <math|v\<leqslant\>u>, and <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|v,u>>
  for its observable adjoint. Define the following actual polynomials:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:coffer-planted-polynomial>Q<rsub|N>=R<rsub|N>*<big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>V><around|(|1-<math-ss|t><rsub|a>D<rsub|a>|)>,<space|2em>Q<rsub|k>=<math-ss|P><rsub|k,N>Q<rsub|N>,<space|2em>X<rsub|k>=Q<rsub|k>*<big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>V><around|(|1+<math-ss|t><rsub|a>F<rsub|a>|)>,<space|2em>X<rsub|m>=<math-ss|P><rsub|m,k>X<rsub|k>.
  </equation>

  Transfer acts coefficientwise. For bounded measurable <math|F> these
  coefficients are integrable: <math|D> is a continuous finite-volume field,
  the configuration spaces are compact, and the selected transition kernels
  have bounded densities. More generally it suffices that
  <math|<big|int><big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>A><around|\||F<rsub|a>|\|>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|k>\<less\>\<infty\>>
  for every <math|A\<subseteq\>V>. Since <math|R<rsub|N>>, the
  <math|D<rsub|a>> and the composed kernel density are bounded, this gives
  absolute integrability of every mixed double integral below.

  For every <math|I\<subseteq\>V>, multiplication in the square-free algebra
  gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:coffer-full-join><around|[|I|]>*X<rsub|k>=<big|sum><rsub|A\<subseteq\>I><math-ss|P><rsub|k,N><around*|(|R<rsub|N>*<big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>I\<setminus\>A><around|(|-D<rsub|a>|)>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>A>F<rsub|a>.
  </equation>

  Indeed a generator can occur in only one of the two factors in
  <eqref|eq:coffer-planted-polynomial>; choosing the generators taken from
  the second factor is exactly choosing <math|A>. No product of expectations
  has been substituted for the transferred old product.

  The room and interiority assumptions of the window put every propagated
  read set inside its own chamber at each intervening step. Consequently
  <eqref|eq:actual-grid-factor>, including equivariance under the prescribed
  copies, gives

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|B><rsub|k,N><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>A>F<rsub|a>|)>=<big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>A><math-up|copy><rsub|a><around|(|<math-ss|B><rsub|k,N>F|)>.
  </equation*>

  This factorization uses disjoint kernel read sets, not just mass one. For
  the integrable extension, truncate each local factor, retaining its read
  set, and pass to the limit by absolute integrability and Fubini. In the
  bounded case all the following interchanges follow from the kernel-slice
  bounds and Fubini; in the integrable version they use the absolute
  integrability just specified. Apply adjointness to each summand of
  <eqref|eq:coffer-full-join> and expand the finite product. This gives

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><around|[|I|]>*X<rsub|k>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|k>>|<cell|=<big|int>R<rsub|N>*<big|sum><rsub|A\<subseteq\>I><big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>I\<setminus\>A><around|(|-D<rsub|a>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>A><math-up|copy><rsub|a><around|(|<math-ss|B><rsub|k,N>F|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|N>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=Z<rsub|N,p>*<space|0.17em>\<bbb-E\><rsub|N,p>*<big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>I><math-up|copy><rsub|a><around|(|<math-ss|B><rsub|k,N>F-D|)>.<eq-number><label|eq:coffer-planted-moment>>>>>
  </align>

  Every further density transfer preserves the integral of every coefficient,
  by its normalized mass. Therefore the left-hand side can also be written
  <math|<big|int><around|[|I|]>*X<rsub|m>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|m>>.
  For <math|I=V> this is exactly <math|Z<rsub|N,p>*M>, with <math|M> the
  moment in the root functional. For <math|I=\<varnothing\>> it is
  <math|Z<rsub|N,p>>. This proves the full moment identity, including all
  mixed terms, with the same partition function. It is stronger than a check
  of the singleton densities.

  <subparagraph|Constructing the two support ledgers.> We construct the
  served representation on the opened centered-cell census using the fixed
  label <math|b> and the terminal birth in
  <eqref|eq:coffer-planted-polynomial>. Throughout, the same selected history
  supplies the coefficient identities, density pushes, forced profiles,
  support rooms, bank and place bounds, migration, separation, and terminal
  hook. The local response certificate is <eqref|eq:coffer-response-coin>;
  its support and attainment bounds are used below at their actual selected
  doors. The served representation and both support ledgers will be built
  from these data.

  Write <math|\<tau\><rsub|i>> for the weight <math|w<rsub|m>> marched from
  <math|m> to <math|i>, and <math|\<Omega\><rsub|i>> for the weight
  <math|w<rsub|m>/a> marched by the same positive multipliers. Thus
  <math|\<tau\><rsub|i>=a*\<Omega\><rsub|i>>. Put
  <math|\<gamma\><rsub|i>=<math-up|forcedAllowance><rsub|i>>, and retain the
  column parameter <math|c<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1>. We use the paid scale
  inequalities <eqref|eq:reference-affordable>,
  <math|\<zeta\><rsub|i>*c<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1>, and
  <math|<sqrt|\<gamma\><rsub|i>>\<leqslant\>1>. The latter is the
  small-remainder regime of the selected history. The signed comparison
  constructed above has

  <\equation*>
    r<rsub|i>+<around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsub|i>.*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>\<leqslant\>B/<math-up|gain><around|(|k,i|)>,<space|2em>Z<rsub|k>.*x=x<rsub|X>-I<rsub|k>*v.
  </equation*>

  Also <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|I<rsub|k>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>\<leqslant\>2*B>:
  the triangle inequality gives <math|q+B\<leqslant\>2*B>, since
  <math|4*<around|(|1+c<rsub|k>|)>*q\<leqslant\>B>. The two starting payments
  are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:coffer-double-payment>w<rsub|m><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><rsub|m,d>|\<\|\|\>><frac|B|<math-up|gain><around|(|k,m|)>>=<frac|a*\<zeta\><rsub|m>|2>\<leqslant\><frac|\<zeta\><rsub|m>|2>,<space|2em><frac|w<rsub|m>|a><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><rsub|m,d>|\<\|\|\>><frac|B|<math-up|gain><around|(|k,m|)>>=<frac|\<zeta\><rsub|m>|2>.
  </equation>

  Apply the paid scale induction to both weights. It gives the
  coefficient-plus-remainder payment for the signed comparison at weight
  <math|\<tau\><rsub|i>>, and at level <math|k> gives

  <\equation*>
    \<Omega\><rsub|k><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><rsub|k,d>|\<\|\|\>>*B\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|k>/2,<space|2em>\<Omega\><rsub|k><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><rsub|k,d>|\<\|\|\>>*<around|\<\|\|\>|I<rsub|k>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|k>.
  </equation*>

  The second inequality pays the negative reference label in the terminal
  birth; the label remains <math|-b> for every window. The larger weight
  <math|\<Omega\><rsub|k>> is essential; payment at <math|\<tau\><rsub|k>>
  alone would not imply it. The first inequality pays the local response
  because

  <\equation*>
    \<tau\><rsub|k><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><rsub|k,d>|\<\|\|\>>*e\<leqslant\>a*\<zeta\><rsub|k>*e/<around|(|2*B|)>\<leqslant\>v<rprime|'>.
  </equation*>

  Linearity of the reference hierarchy makes the bond of <math|-b> equal to
  that of <math|b>. The opened cell embeddings give occupancy one, migration,
  and separation; the old-level multiplicity bound is the admitted schedule.

  Apply the terminal birth construction and its finite downward induction to
  this negative label, starting with the second payment. The terminal birth
  expansion starts with the actual background and linear insertions and
  transports them through the selected transition identities, retaining the
  forced hook. At the finest level its polynomial is precisely
  <math|Q<rsub|N>> in <eqref|eq:coffer-planted-polynomial>. The construction
  begins with the background box factors and linear terminal insertions,
  moves forced responses into boxes by connected component closure, and
  removes the constant and singleton coefficients. This defines the higher
  arrays, rather than merely bounding proposed arrays. More explicitly, the
  terminal place bound has a footprint of cover number at most one and
  section budget <math|t<rsub|N>\<geqslant\>1>. The bank inequality is

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|N>+2>*\<frak-c\><rsub|N><around|(|t<rsub|N>|)>*\<zeta\><rsub|N>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|N>.
  </equation*>

  Together with the paid negative-label bond this gives each terminal
  response its weighted price <math|u<rsub|N>> and its prescribed forced
  hook. Writing <math|n<rsub|N>> for the terminal occupancy bound, the
  terminal budget requires

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|N>=\<gamma\><rsub|N>+n<rsub|N><math-up|siteFactor><around|(|B<rsub|N>|)>*u<rsub|N>,<space|1em><around|(|2*B<rsub|N>+1|)><rsup|4>*C<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>3,<space|1em>e<rsup|C<rsub|N>>-1\<leqslant\>1,<space|1em>\<alpha\><rsub|N>\<geqslant\>3.
  </equation*>

  The place clause here is the cover-number-at-most-one receipt. The birth
  polynomial is integrable by the compact-continuity estimate for the
  terminal cylinder observable. The mixed polynomial is integrable under each
  transition: on this finite-volume compact configuration space its cylinder
  factors are bounded, and the selected transition kernels are normalized
  with bounded densities. Thus the component construction of
  <eqref|eq:transfer-closure> and <eqref|eq:ope-forest-price>, with spare
  exponent three, gives a flagged higher array of price at most one at
  exponent <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N>-3>. The ordinary exponent started linear
  and remains linear after the response shift, so its higher array is zero.
  The terminal hook and singleton coin identities identify the prescribed
  singletons; the higher-degree projection retains the exact birth
  polynomial. At level <math|k>, the seeded negative polynomial
  <math|Q<rsub|k>> has the negative reference singletons and higher arrays of
  price one at weights <math|\<Omega\><rsub|k>>. Reweighting only degrees at
  least two from <math|\<Omega\><rsub|k>> to
  <math|\<tau\><rsub|k>=a*\<Omega\><rsub|k>> reduces those prices to
  <math|a>. Multiply by the positive factors
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|a><around|(|1+<math-ss|t><rsub|a>F<rsub|a>|)>> and
  insert their local response, whose singleton coin and forced profile are
  <eqref|eq:coffer-response-coin>. This forms the mixed polynomial
  <math|X<rsub|k>>, with singleton equal to positive primary plus negative
  reference plus response. Before component closure, the ordinary higher
  array still has price at most <math|a>; the flagged background, forced
  profiles and reweighted old boxes are priced explicitly below. For each
  nonzero term, connect its old flagged boxes and response supports by
  support intersection, require an old flagged box, and put the product at
  the union support with the union flag set. This finite component regrouping
  preserves the full polynomial. A component with one response contributes
  exactly its forced hook; components with two or more responses have degree
  at least two. The resulting representation of <math|X<rsub|k>> has ordinary
  and flagged higher arrays <math|H<rsup|0><rsub|Y>,K<rsup|0><rsub|P>>, each
  with site price at most one at exponent <math|\<alpha\><rsub|k>-4>. The
  flagged price is the component bound proved below; the ordinary higher
  array remains at price <math|a>.

  Here and below a site price <math|C> means that the weighted absolute
  coefficient sums have nonnegative majorants <math|h<rsub|Y>> satisfying
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|Y\<ni\>x>h<rsub|Y>*e<rsup|\<alpha\>*c<rsub|B><around|(|Y|)>>\<leqslant\>C>
  for every <math|x>; for boxes use their first, support component. This
  includes a majorant uniform in the configuration. All higher arrays have
  marker degree at least two. Changing their weights from
  <math|\<Omega\><rsub|k>> to <math|\<tau\><rsub|k>=a*\<Omega\><rsub|k>>
  multiplies the price of a degree <math|j\<geqslant\>2> coefficient by
  <math|a<rsup|j>\<leqslant\>a>. Consequently both old higher arrays now have
  site price at most <math|a>.

  Let <math|\<phi\><rsub|a>> and <math|q<rsub|a>> denote the negative
  reference ordinary singleton and forced profile, respectively. The actual
  atomic response, after the prescribed motion and dilation, gives an
  ordinary field <math|h<rsub|a>>, a dominated forced family
  <math|y<rsub|a>>, and a support response <math|\<psi\><rsub|a,Y>>, with

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:coffer-response-coin><math-up|coin><rsub|k><around|(|h<rsub|a>,y<rsub|a>|)>=<with|math-font|cal*|R><rsub|k>*F<rsub|a>,<space|2em><math-up|profile><rsub|k><around|(|y<rsub|a>|)>=\<cal-H\><rsub|k><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|a>|)>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|<with|math-font|cal*|R><rsub|k>> denotes the evaluation of the
  level-<math|k> representation, and <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|k>> is the response
  hook defined in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-transfer>. These
  identities follow by applying the same motion and dilation to the input
  density and forced-profile identities: the Wilson density is invariant
  under that motion, and both profile and hook are linear. The response has a
  majorant <math|g<rsub|a,Y>> with

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|Y>\<tau\><rsub|k>*g<rsub|a,Y>*e<rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|k>*c<rsub|B<rsub|k>><around|(|Y|)>>\<leqslant\>v<rprime|'>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed the undilated attainment cost is <math|e>, and
  <math|\<tau\><rsub|k><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><rsub|k,d>|\<\|\|\>>*e\<leqslant\>a*\<zeta\><rsub|k>*e/<around|(|2*B|)>\<leqslant\>v<rprime|'>>,
  by <eqref|eq:coffer-double-payment> and
  <math|e*\<zeta\><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>v<rprime|'>*B>. The attainment exponent
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|k>+2> is stronger than the exponent used here. Each
  nonzero response contains its mark and vanishes when its support is
  healthy. The census marks at <math|k> are distinct by the admitted window
  separation, so occupancy there is one. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:transfer-root-hit>,
  with exponent <math|\<alpha\><rsub|k>-1>, therefore bounds the combined
  response site price by <math|R<rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|site>>*v<rprime|'>>,
  where <math|R<rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|site>>=<math-up|siteFactor><around|(|B<rsub|k>|)>>.

  To avoid confusing this packing factor with the density, write
  <math|R<rsup|<math-up|site>>=R<rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|site>>> for the rest
  of the gluing step. The forced profiles <math|q<rsub|a>> of the negative
  reference field have site price at most
  <math|R<rsup|<math-up|site>>*<sqrt|\<gamma\><rsub|k>>> at exponent
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|k>-1>. Here is the payment check. The reference
  remainder has bound at most <math|c<rsub|k>*<around|\<\|\|\>|I<rsub|k>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>>;
  its forced family has cost at most <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><rsub|k,d>|\<\|\|\>><sqrt|\<gamma\><rsub|k>>>
  times that remainder. Multiplication by <math|\<tau\><rsub|k>>, the
  reference bond payment, and <math|\<zeta\><rsub|k>*c<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>1>
  give the per-mark cost <math|<sqrt|\<gamma\><rsub|k>>>. Apply
  <eqref|eq:transfer-root-hit> again. The background box activity has site
  price <math|\<gamma\><rsub|k>>. Thus the full old box array, including its
  background, its linear forced profiles and <math|K<rsup|0>>, has site price
  at most

  <\equation*>
    W=\<gamma\><rsub|k>+R<rsup|<math-up|site>>*<sqrt|\<gamma\><rsub|k>>+a<space|1em><text|at
    exponent >\<alpha\><rsub|k>-1.
  </equation*>

  Multiplication by <math|<big|prod><rsub|a><around|(|1+<math-ss|t><rsub|a>F<rsub|a>|)>>
  changes the old ordinary exponent by <math|<big|sum><rsub|a><math-ss|t><rsub|a>F<rsub|a>>.
  Put <math|J=<big|sum><rsub|a,Y><math-ss|t><rsub|a>\<psi\><rsub|a,Y>>. Form
  a new box array <math|<wide|K|\<bar\>>> as follows. Take finite sets of
  distinct old box addresses and response addresses <math|<around|(|a,Y|)>>,
  with connected support intersection graph, at least one old box, and
  pairwise disjoint old box supports. Assign their product to the box whose
  support is their union and whose flag set is the union of the old flag
  sets, retaining the indicator that this is the flag set of the union
  support. Sum the products with the same output box. This is an explicit
  finite definition on each finite-volume lattice. The component
  decomposition in <eqref|eq:transfer-closure> gives

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|field><around|(|<wide|K|\<bar\>>|)>=<math-up|field><around|(|K<rsup|<math-up|old>>|)><math-up|pass><around|(|J|)>.
  </equation*>

  Subtract <math|J> from the ordinary exponent. The represented polynomial is
  still exactly <math|X<rsub|k>>; no coefficient has been discarded.

  Set <math|d<rsub|\<ast\>>=\<alpha\><rsub|k>-4\<geqslant\>0> and
  <math|\<eta\>=W+R<rsup|<math-up|site>>*v<rprime|'>>. The melting hypotheses
  say <math|<around|(|2*B<rsub|k>+1|)><rsup|4>*\<eta\>\<leqslant\>3> and
  <math|e<rsup|\<eta\>>-1\<leqslant\>1>. Apply <eqref|eq:ope-forest-price>
  with output exponent <math|d<rsub|\<ast\>>> and spare exponent <math|3>.
  Its incoming exponent is <math|d<rsub|\<ast\>>+3=\<alpha\><rsub|k>-1>,
  exactly the exponent just paid. The price of a product is at most the
  product of its coefficient majorants, and the union box is uniquely
  determined by its constituent addresses. Restricting the connected-set sum
  to the admissible sets above only decreases its nonnegative majorant. Hence
  <math|<wide|K|\<bar\>>> has site price at most
  <math|e<rsup|\<eta\>>-1\<leqslant\>1> at exponent <math|d<rsub|\<ast\>>>.
  The ordinary higher array remains <math|H<rsup|0>>, since <math|J> and all
  added ordinary terms are linear; its price is at most <math|a\<leqslant\>1>
  at the same exponent.

  The vacuum coefficient of <math|<wide|K|\<bar\>>> is the old background.
  Its singleton at mark <math|a> is <math|q<rsub|a>+\<cal-H\><rsub|k><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|a>|)>>:
  without a response a component is one old box, while a component with
  exactly one response gives precisely the hook, as proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-transfer>. Equation
  <eqref|eq:coffer-response-coin>, the singleton identity for
  <math|Q<rsub|k>>, and the product rule in the square-free algebra identify
  the singleton of <math|X<rsub|k>> with the coin of
  <math|<around|(|\<phi\><rsub|a>+h<rsub|a>,q<rsub|a>+<math-up|profile><rsub|k><around|(|y<rsub|a>|)>|)>>.
  Positivity of the background density and the singleton uniqueness in
  <eqref|eq:ope-single-coin> then identify the ordinary singleton of the new
  exponent. Remove degrees zero and one from each array; the remaining
  <math|H<rsup|k>,K<rsup|k>> give exactly this prescribed singleton
  representation of <math|X<rsub|k>>. Degree projection cannot increase the
  absolute coefficient price. Products, finite sums and local flag indicators
  preserve measurability, gauge invariance, locality and containment of each
  occurring mark in its support. Both higher ledgers therefore have price one
  at exponent <math|\<alpha\><rsub|k>-4>.

  Now descend from <math|k> to <math|m> along the same selected history. At
  each step the signed singleton expansion in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:reference-difference> has its exact density
  push, forced-profile identity and finite support witness on the chosen
  door. Transporting the higher arrays through that witness preserves the
  full polynomial; the newly added response terms have degree at least two,
  and their connected supports are grouped by union. Thus the descent
  transports the representation already constructed at <math|k> rather than
  postulating a new served object. For specificity the required paid local
  data are

  <\equation*>
    0\<less\>\<delta\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1,<space|1em>\<alpha\><rsub|i>,\<alpha\><rsub|i+1>\<geqslant\>100,<space|1em>T<rsub|i>=n<rsub|i><math-up|siteFactor><around|(|B<rsub|i+1>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|i>+4*\<delta\><rsub|i>,<space|1em>T<rsub|i>+e<rsup|T<rsub|i>>*T<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|i>,
  </equation*>

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|i>=u<rsub|i>+n<rsub|i><math-up|siteFactor><around|(|B<rsub|i>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|i>,<space|2em><around|(|2*B<rsub|i>+1|)><rsup|4>*A<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>,<space|2em>e<rsup|A<rsub|i>>-1\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|M<rsub|i>> is the available input budget, <math|u<rsub|i>> is
  the flagged response price, and <math|n<rsub|i>> bounds the two adjacent
  occupancies. These are the one-step payment inequalities of the selected
  history, not additional conclusions of melting at <math|k>. Here is the
  singleton payment check. Put <math|C<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|wing>>=<math-up|orb><around|(|d|)>*<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|i>+2*c<rsub|i+1>|)>\<geqslant\>2>.
  The retained signed expansion has dilated fine cost

  <\equation*>
    s<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><rsub|i+1,d>|\<\|\|\>>*<around*|(|C<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|wing>>\<\|\|\>Z<rsub|i+1>.*x\<\|\|\><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>+2*r<rsub|i+1>|)>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><rsub|i+1,d>|\<\|\|\>>*C<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|wing>>*<around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsub|i+1>.*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>+r<rsub|i+1>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The paid orbit inequality of the selected history is
  <math|2*C<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|wing>>*\<zeta\><rsub|i+1>\<leqslant\>1>;
  hence <math|2*\<tau\><rsub|i+1>*s<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1>. Its coarse
  response cost is at most <math|t<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><rsub|i,d>|\<\|\|\>>*r<rsub|i>>.
  Since the march has <math|\<tau\><rsub|i+1>\<leqslant\>\<tau\><rsub|i>> and
  <math|\<zeta\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1>, the same paid comparison gives
  <math|\<tau\><rsub|i+1>*t<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1>. These pay the input and
  output sides of the actual finite-support comparison. The parent-site
  identity, occupancy and cover-price comparison are the same migration and
  separation data as in the centered induction. The adjacent transition
  preserves the singleton entries; every added response term has marker
  degree at least two. The component-closure estimate therefore recovers
  exponent <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i>> and separately bounds both higher ledgers
  by one. At the last level, the forced remainder allowance
  <math|<sqrt|\<gamma\><rsub|m>>> and the marched coefficient-plus-remainder
  payment give the Vault constants <math|2,1>. The first step accepts
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|k>-4>; every subsequent step accepts the full exponent
  already obtained. The actual polynomial at the next level is
  <math|<math-ss|P><rsub|i,i+1>X<rsub|i+1>>, so finite induction constructs
  the required support families for the precise <math|X<rsub|m>> in
  <eqref|eq:coffer-planted-polynomial>.

  Finally the singleton bounds are separate from the higher-array bounds. At
  <math|m>, let <math|z<rsub|m>=<around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsub|m>.*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>>.
  The coefficient format and remainder bounds imply, before dilation and
  motion,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||<math-up|exponentialEntry><around|(|Z<rsub|m>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+c<rsub|m>|)>*z<rsub|m>+r<rsub|m>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|P><around|\||<math-up|profile><around|(|Z<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|f>>|)><rsub|P>|\|>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|m>.
  </equation*>

  For the first inequality, sum the section bounds against the absolute
  coefficient vector and add the remainder bound; the constant coordinate
  contributes the extra <math|z<rsub|m>>. The second is the absolute
  forced-profile bound, since <math|<sqrt|\<gamma\><rsub|m>>\<leqslant\>1>.
  Motion preserves absolute values. Since
  <math|w<rsub|m><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><rsub|m,d>|\<\|\|\>>*<around|(|r<rsub|m>+z<rsub|m>|)>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|m>>
  and <math|\<zeta\><rsub|m>*c<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1>, the ordinary singleton
  has weighted absolute value at most <math|2> and the summed flagged
  singleton at most <math|1>. These are exactly the two singleton bounds used
  by the finite-census price estimate below. Together with the constructed
  higher arrays and their site budgets, they give the required served
  representation and its support bounds at level <math|m>.

  Thus the following support decomposition has been constructed by the paid
  same-history transition and its finite-support witnesses. Let <math|V> be
  the set of census sites, <math|K=<around|\||V|\|>>, with marks
  <math|o<rsub|a>>, <math|a\<in\>V>, in the level-<math|m> lattice. The
  polynomial <math|<math-ss|X>=X<rsub|m>> of
  <eqref|eq:coffer-planted-polynomial>, whose integrability and moment
  identity were just established, has the following structure. There are
  singleton site amplitudes <math|\<Phi\><rsub|a><around|(|U|)>> and
  singleton box amplitudes <math|\<omega\><rsub|a,P><around|(|U|)>>,
  <math|a\<in\>V>, <math|P> a box, with

  <\equation*>
    w<rsub|a><around|\||\<Phi\><rsub|a><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>2,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|P>w<rsub|a><around|\||\<omega\><rsub|a,P><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>1<space|2em><text|for
    every >a<text| and every >U,
  </equation*>

  and two correction families, an ordinary family
  <math|H<rsub|Y><around|(|U|)>> indexed by supports <math|Y> and a flagged
  family <math|K<rsub|P><around|(|U|)>> indexed by boxes <math|P>, such that

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|X><around|(|U|)>=e<rsup|E<rsub|m><around|(|U|)>><space|0.17em><math-up|pass><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<Phi\><rsub|a><around|(|U|)><space|0.17em><math-ss|t><rsub|a>+<big|sum><rsub|Y>H<rsub|Y><around|(|U|)>|)>\<cdot\><math-up|field><around*|(|P\<mapsto\><math-ss|b><rsub|P><around|(|U|)>+<big|sum><rsub|a>\<omega\><rsub|a,P><around|(|U|)><space|0.17em><math-ss|t><rsub|a>+K<rsub|P><around|(|U|)>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|E<rsub|m>> is the exponent of the level-<math|m> effective
  density, <math|<math-ss|t><rsub|a>> is the census monomial of the site
  <math|a>, <math|<math-ss|b><rsub|P>> is the box activity term of the
  level-<math|m> representation, and <math|<math-up|field>> is evaluated on
  the flagged set of <math|U>. Each correction family satisfies the anchored
  support-cost bound with constant <math|1> at the support exponent
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|m>> of the level-<math|m> budget: there are numbers
  <math|h<rsub|Y>\<geqslant\>0> bounding the weighted absolute coefficient
  sums, <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|w><around|(|H<rsub|Y><around|(|U|)>|)>\<leqslant\>h<rsub|Y>>
  for all <math|U>, with <math|<big|sum><rsub|Y\<ni\>x>h<rsub|Y>*e<rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|m><math-up|cov><rsub|m><around|(|Y|)>>\<leqslant\>1>
  for every site <math|x>, and likewise for <math|K<rsub|P>> with the support
  of the box <math|P>. Every nonzero correction term has a census mark
  <math|o<rsub|a>> in its support. Consequently, summing the anchored bound
  over the <math|K> marks,

  <\equation>
    <big|sum><rsub|Y>\<cal-P\><rsub|w><around|(|H<rsub|Y><around|(|U|)>|)>\<leqslant\>K,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|P>\<cal-P\><rsub|w><around|(|K<rsub|P><around|(|U|)>|)>\<leqslant\>K<space|2em><text|for
    every >U.<label|eq:ex-trace-coffer-corrections>
  </equation>

  The required support-price structure is a statement about this precise
  polynomial <math|X<rsub|m>>. Its full moment identity is
  <eqref|eq:coffer-planted-moment>, already proved independently of those
  price bounds. The support decomposition and its two prices were constructed
  above, using the explicitly stated local-transition inputs.

  The polynomial-price calculation behind the finite-gas estimate is as
  follows. For a finite census polynomial <math|Q>, write

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-P\><rsub|w><around|(|Q|)>=<big|sum><rsub|s><math-up|toll><around|(|w,s|)><around|\<\|\|\>|Q<rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>>
  </equation*>

  for its weighted absolute coefficient sum. The price is submultiplicative.
  For box terms <math|K<rsub|p>>, the finite sum over disjoint-support
  patterns obeys

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-P\><rsub|w><around|(|<math-up|field><around|(|K|)>|)>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|p><around|(|1+\<cal-P\><rsub|w><around|(|K<rsub|p>|)>|)>\<leqslant\>exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|p>\<cal-P\><rsub|w><around|(|K<rsub|p>|)>|)>,
  </equation*>

  because each pattern's product price is at most the product of its factors
  and <math|1+x\<leqslant\>e<rsup|x>>. The same submultiplicativity, applied
  term by term to the finite truncated exponential, gives
  <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|w><around|(|<math-up|pass><around|(|Q|)>|)>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<cal-P\><rsub|w><around|(|Q|)>>>.
  The singleton site and box amplitudes contribute <math|2*K> and <math|K> to
  the exponent, by the two singleton bounds just proved. The ordinary and the
  flagged correction families contribute <math|K> each, by
  <eqref|eq:ex-trace-coffer-corrections>. The total is <math|5*K>; the box
  activity terms <math|<math-ss|b><rsub|P>> and the exponent <math|E<rsub|m>>
  are paid by the finite-volume denominator bound used next.

  By <eqref|eq:coffer-planted-moment>, the integral of the selected
  coefficient equals the volume-<math|N> Wilson partition function times
  <math|M>. To specify the denominator estimate, let <math|A<rsub|m>> be the
  lawful local representative used in this very expansion, and take
  <math|\<rho\>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m><rsup|-1/2>\<leqslant\>min
  <around|{|1,\<vartheta\><rsub|m>|}>>. The numerator estimate just obtained
  is the coefficient-price estimate of Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-centered-construction>,
  with total mark charge <math|5*K>. Its small-ball lower bound for
  <math|Z<rsub|A<rsub|m>>=<big|int><math-up|eval><around|(|A<rsub|m>|)>>,
  followed by division as in <eqref|ex-centered-sail>, bounds the quotient by

  <\equation*>
    exp <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|m>|\|>*<around*|[|2<math-up|exCost><rsub|m>+\<kappa\><rsub|m>+\<beta\><rsub|m><math-up|baseBudget><rsub|m><around|(|\<rho\>|)>+4*d<rsub|G>*log
    <around|(|16/\<rho\>|)>|]>+5*K|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<kappa\><rsub|m>> denotes the vacuum price in that estimate,
  not the coefficient-recovery constant used later. The treasury receipt at
  this same radius bounds the bracket by <math|<math-up|Treasury><around|(|m|)>>.
  The same-history transition-mass identity identifies
  <math|Z<rsub|A<rsub|m>>=Z<rsub|N,p>\<gtr\>0>, as in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-selected-birth-denominator>; this is also the
  partition function in the planted moment identity above. No comparison of
  different local representatives or partition functions is needed.
  Consequently

  <\equation>
    <around|\||M|\|><space|0.17em><math-up|toll><around|(|w,\<frak-s\>|)>\<leqslant\>exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|m>|\|><math-up|Treasury><around|(|m|)>+K*<around|(|2+1+2|)>|)>.<label|eq:ex-trace-spiker>
  </equation>

  This is an absolute-value estimate and does not use cancellation. Its
  hypotheses include the paid local-transition inputs used to construct the
  support ledgers above.

  In the Coffer application all site weights equal <math|w<rsub|m>>, so
  <math|<math-up|toll><around|(|w,\<frak-s\>|)>=w<rsub|m><rsup|K>>. The
  tiling identity is

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|m>|\|>=K<around*|(|<frac|A<rsub|J>|A<rsub|m>>|)><rsup|4>.
  </equation*>

  Taking the positive <math|K>-th root of <eqref|eq:ex-trace-spiker> yields

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||M|\|><rsup|1/K>\<leqslant\><frac|exp
    <around|(|<around|(|A<rsub|J>/A<rsub|m>|)><rsup|4><math-up|Treasury><around|(|m|)>+5|)>|w<rsub|m>>=<frac|<math-up|stakes><around|(|J,m|)>|w<rsub|m>>,
  </equation*>

  which is <eqref|eq:ex-trace-coffer-root>. The moment identity is needed to
  identify this <math|M> with the desired rooted observable.

  <subsubsection|Using the output in the Bill>

  The Bill is evaluated on the inflated field <math|G<rprime|'>=u*g>. Its
  root error is <math|<wide|G|~><rprime|'>-\<cal-O\><rsub|N,p>*b>, where
  <math|<wide|G|~><rprime|'>=<math-up|back><rsub|k\<to\>N><space|0.17em>G<rprime|'>>.
  In the Bill specialization, <math|a=Z<rsup|-1>>,
  <math|v<rprime|'>=Z<rsup|-1>>, <math|B=Z<rsup|3>>, and <math|e=Z>, while
  <math|\<zeta\><rsub|i>=<math-up|fly><rsub|d><around|(|i|)>> at each scale.
  Thus the payment condition is <math|e*\<zeta\><rsub|k>=Z<math-up|fly><rsub|d><around|(|k|)>\<leqslant\>Z<rsup|2>=v<rprime|'>*B>.
  The gas weight from <eqref|eq:ex-trace-coffer-weight> is

  <\equation*>
    w<rsub|<math-up|gas>>=Z<rsup|-1>*<frac|<math-up|fly><rsub|d><around|(|m|)>/2|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><rsub|m,d>|\<\|\|\>>*<around|(|Z<rsup|3>/<math-up|gain><around|(|k,m|)>|)>>.
  </equation*>

  The factor <math|Z<rsup|-1>> is the mask fraction <math|a> in the Coffer
  input, so this is exactly the native Bill denominator.

  The melting inequalities also hold eventually for this specialization. To
  see this directly, write <math|x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>,
  <math|B<rsub|x>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>
  and <math|R<rsub|x>=exp <around|(|1|)>*<around|[|2*<around|(|16*B<rsub|x>+9|)>|]><rsup|4>>
  for the spatial radius and site factor. The selected forced allowance is
  exactly

  <\equation*>
    f<around|(|x|)>=exp <around|{|-exp <around|[|<around|(|log log
    x|)><rsup|2>/5|]>|}>
  </equation*>

  for sufficiently large <math|x>. Indeed, the monitor is
  <math|H<around|(|x|)>=exp <around|(|<around|(|log log x|)><rsup|2>|)>> and
  the allowance is <math|exp <around|(|-H<around|(|x|)><rsup|1/5>|)>>; the
  power <math|1/5> is applied to the value of the monitor, not to its
  argument. With <math|y=log log x>, <math|exp <around|(|y<rsup|2>/5|)>/log
  x=exp <around|(|y<rsup|2>/5-y|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>. Consequently, for every
  <math|A\<gtr\>0>, <math|<sqrt|f<around|(|x|)>>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|-A>>
  eventually; also <math|0\<less\>f<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><sqrt|f<around|(|x|)>>>
  eventually. With <math|a=v<rprime|'>=x<rsup|-1>>, the quantity in the
  melting condition is

  <\equation*>
    t<rsub|x>=f<around|(|x|)>+R<rsub|x>*<sqrt|f<around|(|x|)>>+x<rsup|-1>+R<rsub|x>*x<rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|R<rsub|x>=O<around|(|<around|(|log x|)><rsup|8>|)>> and
  <math|<around|(|2*B<rsub|x>+1|)><rsup|4>=O<around|(|<around|(|log
  x|)><rsup|8>|)>>, so <math|<around|(|2*B<rsub|x>+1|)><rsup|4>*t<rsub|x>\<to\>0>
  and <math|t<rsub|x>\<to\>0>. Thus eventually
  <math|<around|(|2*B<rsub|x>+1|)><rsup|4>*t<rsub|x>\<leqslant\>3> and
  <math|e<rsup|t<rsub|x>>-1\<leqslant\>1>; <math|0\<less\>a\<leqslant\>1> and
  <math|v<rprime|'>\<geqslant\>0> hold as well. The remaining clause is the
  support exponent bound <math|\<alpha\><rsub|k>\<geqslant\>4>, retained
  among the scale hypotheses. All these tails depend only on the fixed scale
  data, before periods, volumes, atomic certificates and windows are chosen.

  The other two entries of the Bill use the specific atomic charge. Fix
  <math|u\<gtr\>0> and let the charge in <eqref|eq:ex-trace-atomd-charge> be

  <\equation*>
    q=q<rsub|k>=u*<space|0.17em>\<vartheta\><rsub|k><rsup|2><math-up|Age><rsub|k><around|(|1/6|)>,<space|2em><math-up|Age><rsub|k><around|(|a|)>=exp
    <around*|(|a<around|(|log log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)><rsup|2>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|k>> is the threshold of the scale-<math|k>
  budget; thus <math|\<kappa\><rsub|k>=<math-up|Age><rsub|k><around|(|1/2|)>>
  and <math|<math-up|Age><rsub|k><around|(|a|)><math-up|Age><rsub|k><around|(|a<rprime|'>|)>=<math-up|Age><rsub|k><around|(|a+a<rprime|'>|)>>.
  The selected schedule has <math|c<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>. Since <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|k><rsup|2><math-up|Age><rsub|k><around|(|1/6|)>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|-1><math-up|Age><rsub|k><around|(|2+1/6|)>>
  tends to zero faster than any power of <math|log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>
  grows, and <math|log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>=exp <around|(|log log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)>> is eventually smaller than any positive
  multiple of <math|<math-up|Age><rsub|k><around|(|1/12|)>>, and
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>\<to\>\<infty\>>, for all large <math|k>

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>4*<around|(|1+log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)>*q<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>Z<rsup|3>,<space|2em>4*<around|(|1+log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)>*u\<leqslant\><math-up|Age><rsub|k><around|(|1/12|)>;
  </equation*>

  the second inequality gives <eqref|eq:ex-trace-coffer-budget>. The norm
  entry of the Bill is <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|X>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>\<leqslant\>1>;
  it follows from <eqref|eq:ex-trace-atomd-charge>, nonnegativity of the
  remainder allowance and <math|q<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>1>:
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|X>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|X>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>+s\<leqslant\>q<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>1>.
  The bias entry is obtained from <eqref|eq:ex-trace-coffer-column> with
  <math|b<rprime|'>=\<kappa\><rsub|k>> alone:

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|X>-\<cal-C\><rsub|k><around|(|b|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>\<leqslant\><frac|4*<around|(|1+c<rsub|k>|)>*q<rsub|k>|\<kappa\><rsub|k>>\<leqslant\><math-up|Age><rsub|k><around|(|1/12|)>*<space|0.17em>\<vartheta\><rsub|k><rsup|2><math-up|Age><rsub|k><around|(|1/6|)><math-up|Age><rsub|k><around|(|-1/2|)>=\<vartheta\><rsub|k><rsup|2><math-up|Age><rsub|k><around|(|-1/4|)>,
  </equation*>

  because <math|1/12+1/6-1/2=-1/4>. This is the bias bound which
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-bill-prices> takes as a
  hypothesis on a paid label; that section does not prove it.

  The resulting statement has the following order of choices, under the
  standing hypotheses on the selected family, which include the served
  response hierarchy and its terminal identification, the same-history
  finite-menu and atomic response receipts, eventual support exponents at
  least four, the column bound <math|c<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>, and the endpoint estimate
  <eqref|eq:ex-trace-sharp-read> with <math|b<rprime|'>=\<kappa\><rsub|k>> as
  a hypothesis. For every fixed <math|u\<gtr\>0> and grade <math|d>: for all
  sufficiently large <math|k>, for all sufficiently large periods <math|p>
  (depending on <math|k>), for every volume index <math|N\<gtr\>k> and every
  atomic certificate <math|<around|(|X,g|)>> at scale <math|k> with charge
  <math|q<rsub|k>> and attainment cost at most
  <math|Z=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>, there is a public label <math|b> such
  that <math|<around|(|g,x<rsub|X>,b|)>> is a Bill, with the root estimate
  for all windows. The period threshold is the one at which the endpoint
  estimate <eqref|eq:ex-trace-sharp-read> holds with <math|\<ell\>> the
  reference level.

  The remaining analytic dependencies are the atomic response, the endpoint
  price-duality recurrence, and the paid elementary integration and singleton
  transition inputs specified above. The site/box expansion, its two separate
  correction prices, and its singleton envelope are now constructed from
  those inputs; no additional served polynomial is postulated.

  <subsection|The paid-response price bound><label|sec:ex-lattice-trace-bill-prices>

  We compute the quantitative root-price bound attached to a paid
  finite-volume response. This is the numerical input used for the two
  component errors in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-probe-assembly>.
  The computation has two parts: a coefficient-size estimate from the paid
  label and its approximation receipt, and a negative-rate bound on the
  native root price.

  For each frame index <math|i>, set

  <\equation*>
    J<rsub|i>=<math-up|basin><around|(|i|)>,<space|2em>K<rsub|i>=<math-up|summit><around|(|i|)>,<space|2em>t<rsub|i>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|K<rsub|i>>,<space|2em>v<rsub|i>=<around*|(|<frac|A<rsub|i>|A<rsub|J<rsub|i>>>|)><rsup|d>,<space|2em>f<rsub|i>=<math-up|fly><rsub|d><around|(|J<rsub|i>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|A<rsub|j>> is the field dilation at level <math|j>, and the
  basin, the summit and all scales belong to one fixed selected family and
  its selected schedule. Let

  <\equation*>
    s<rsub|i>=<math-up|stakes><around|(|i,J<rsub|i>|)>=exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<around*|(|<frac|A<rsub|i>|A<rsub|J<rsub|i>>>|)><rsup|4><math-up|Treasury><around|(|J<rsub|i>|)>+5|)>,<space|2em>\<gamma\><rsub|i>=<math-up|gain><around|(|K<rsub|i>,J<rsub|i>|)>=exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<frac|S<rsub|K<rsub|i>>-S<rsub|J<rsub|i>>|8>|)>
  </equation*>

  be the local stake price and the gain from level <math|K<rsub|i>> down to
  <math|J<rsub|i>>, where <math|S<rsub|j>> is the scale coordinate. The
  native root allowance for a grade-<math|d> response is

  <\equation>
    n<rsub|d,i>=<frac|s<rsub|i>|t<rsub|i><rsup|-1><around*|(|<dfrac|f<rsub|i>/2|A<rsub|J<rsub|i>><rsup|-d>*<around|(|t<rsub|i><rsup|3>/\<gamma\><rsub|i>|)>>|)>>.<label|eq:ex-trace-native-price>
  </equation>

  The norm of the inverse grade-<math|d> dilation is
  <math|A<rsub|J<rsub|i>><rsup|-d>>, which is the factor appearing in this
  expression. Simplifying gives

  <\equation>
    A<rsub|i><rsup|d>*n<rsub|d,i>=<frac|2*s<rsub|i>*v<rsub|i>*t<rsub|i><rsup|4>|f<rsub|i>*\<gamma\><rsub|i>>.<label|eq:ex-trace-priced-native>
  </equation>

  Indeed, the denominator in <eqref|eq:ex-trace-native-price> is
  <math|f<rsub|i>*\<gamma\><rsub|i>*A<rsub|J<rsub|i>><rsup|d>/<around|(|2*t<rsub|i><rsup|4>|)>>,
  and <math|A<rsub|i><rsup|d>/A<rsub|J<rsub|i>><rsup|d>=v<rsub|i>>.
  Throughout this calculation the scale receipts ensure
  <math|A<rsub|i>,A<rsub|J<rsub|i>>,t<rsub|i>,f<rsub|i>,\<gamma\><rsub|i>\<gtr\>0>
  and <math|G<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>.

  The scalar rate receipts are expressed by tickets. For a positive sequence
  <math|F<rsub|i>>, a rate-zero ticket means that for every
  <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, eventually <math|0\<leqslant\>F<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>exp
  <around|(|\<epsilon\>*G<rsub|i>|)>>, where
  <math|G<rsub|i>=<math-up|Gate><around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>|)>>. If
  two sequences have tickets of rates <math|\<alpha\>> and
  <math|\<alpha\><rprime|'>>, their product has rate
  <math|\<alpha\>+\<alpha\><rprime|'>>: choose the two ticket bounds at
  exponent <math|\<alpha\>+\<eta\>> and <math|\<alpha\><rprime|'>+\<eta\>>,
  multiply them, and then let <math|2*\<eta\>> be smaller than the requested
  excess over <math|\<alpha\>+\<alpha\><rprime|'>>. Nonnegative sums of
  equal-rate sequences retain that rate by the same argument and the
  elementary bound <math|u+v\<leqslant\>2*max <around|(|u,v|)>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The five scale estimates for the paid
    response><label|lem:trace-bill-scale-tickets>For the fixed selected
    history and each fixed grade <math|d>, the sequences
    <math|s<rsub|i>,v<rsub|i>,t<rsub|i><rsup|4>,f<rsub|i><rsup|-1>> have
    rate-zero tickets. Moreover, eventually <math|J<rsub|i>\<less\>K<rsub|i>>
    and <math|\<gamma\><rsub|i>\<geqslant\>\<tau\><rsub|i><around|(|500|)>>,
    where

    <\equation*>
      \<tau\><rsub|i><around|(|q|)>=exp <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<frac|q|10<rsup|4>>*G<rsub|i>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The eventual cutoff may depend on the grade and the requested ticket
    exponent, but not on volume, period, observable or window.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    We apply the moving-depth bounds of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-trace-pair-moving-bills>.
    Its frame <math|j>, first opened level <math|m>, and summit <math|k> are
    exactly <math|i,J<rsub|i>,K<rsub|i>> here: the opening condition is
    <math|<around|(|A<rsub|i>/A<rsub|J<rsub|i>>|)>\<geqslant\><around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|J<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>><rsup|2>>, and the
    summit is the first level after <math|i> whose scale gap is at least
    <math|G<rsub|i>>. Its treasury <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|J<rsub|i>>> is the
    same vacuum treasury in <math|s<rsub|i>>. No field, paid label, or
    terminal volume enters those choices. Set
    <math|x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>> and <math|y=log x>, so
    <math|G<rsub|i>=y<rsup|48>>. That lemma gives on a common tail

    <\equation*>
      <frac|A<rsub|i>|A<rsub|J<rsub|i>>>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|6>,<space|2em><around*|(|<frac|A<rsub|i>|A<rsub|J<rsub|i>>>|)><rsup|4><math-up|Treasury><around|(|J<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|27>,<space|2em><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|K<rsub|i>>\<leqslant\>2*x,<space|2em>S<rsub|K<rsub|i>>-S<rsub|J<rsub|i>>\<geqslant\><tfrac|1|2>y<rsup|48>,<space|2em>J<rsub|i>\<less\>K<rsub|i>.
    </equation*>

    These bounds were proved there from the first-opening minimality, the
    summit first-crossing bound, and the stage treasury payment; their proof
    does not use the paid-response estimate of this section. The flight at
    fixed grade is <math|f<rsub|i>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|J<rsub|i>><rsup|-w<rsub|d>>>,
    where <math|w<rsub|d>\<gtr\>0> is the fixed exponent of the selected
    height schedule. Monotonicity of the hatted coupling and
    <math|J<rsub|i>\<less\>K<rsub|i>> give
    <math|f<rsub|i><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\><around|(|2*x|)><rsup|w<rsub|d>>>.
    Consequently the logarithms of our four positive factors are bounded by

    <\equation*>
      y<rsup|27>+5,<space|2em>6*d*log y,<space|2em>4*<around|(|y+log
      2|)>,<space|2em>w<rsub|d>*<around|(|y+log 2|)>,
    </equation*>

    respectively. Dividing each bound by <math|y<rsup|48>> gives zero in the
    limit. For every <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, each factor is therefore at
    most <math|exp <around|(|\<epsilon\>*G<rsub|i>|)>> eventually. This
    proves all four tickets. Finally

    <\equation*>
      \<gamma\><rsub|i>=exp <around|(|<around|(|S<rsub|K<rsub|i>>-S<rsub|J<rsub|i>>|)>/8|)>\<geqslant\>exp
      <around|(|G<rsub|i>/16|)>\<geqslant\>exp
      <around|(|500*G<rsub|i>/10<rsup|4>|)>=\<tau\><rsub|i><around|(|500|)>,
    </equation*>

    since <math|1/16\<gtr\>500/10<rsup|4>> and <math|G<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>.
  </proof>

  Taking the product of the four tickets, including the fixed factor
  <math|2>, gives a rate-zero ticket for

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|i>=<frac|2*s<rsub|i>*v<rsub|i>*t<rsub|i><rsup|4>|f<rsub|i>>.
  </equation*>

  The ticket bound at exponent <math|70/10<rsup|4>> gives
  <math|B<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<tau\><rsub|i><around|(|70|)>> eventually.
  Dividing by the gain and using multiplicativity yields

  <\equation>
    A<rsub|i><rsup|d>*n<rsub|d,i>=B<rsub|i>/\<gamma\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<tau\><rsub|i><around|(|70|)>/\<tau\><rsub|i><around|(|500|)>=\<tau\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-430|)>.<label|eq:ex-trace-native-rate>
  </equation>

  This proves the exact negative-rate price needed below for all sufficiently
  large <math|i>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Column size from a paid label><label|lem:ex-trace-bill-column-size>Suppose
    a grade-<math|d> paid label <math|y> satisfies

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|y-<math-up|Col><around|(|x|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>\<leqslant\>\<vartheta\><rsub|K><rsup|2><space|0.17em><math-up|Age><rsub|K><around|(|-1/4|)>,
    </equation*>

    where the selected threshold obeys <math|0\<less\>\<vartheta\><rsub|K>\<leqslant\>1>
    and <math|<math-up|Age><rsub|K><around|(|-1/4|)>\<leqslant\>1>. Then

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|Col><around|(|x|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>\<leqslant\>2.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The triangle inequality gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|Col><around|(|x|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|Col><around|(|x|)>-y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>\<leqslant\>1+\<vartheta\><rsub|K><rsup|2><math-up|Age><rsub|K><around|(|-1/4|)>\<leqslant\>2.
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Paid label yields the scaled component
    price><label|prop:ex-trace-bill-root-price>Fix the volume index <math|N>,
    the period <math|p> and the grade <math|d>, and let
    <math|K=K<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>N>. Let <math|g> be a scale-<math|K>
    observable, <math|y> a grade-<math|d> coefficient and <math|x> a public
    grade-<math|d> label, and let <math|<math-up|Col><around|(|x|)>=\<cal-C\><rsub|K><around|(|x|)>>
    be the scale-<math|K> response column of <math|x> defined in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-coffer-supplier> for the
    same <math|N,p,d>. Suppose <math|<around|(|y,x|)>> satisfies the
    hypotheses of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-trace-bill-column-size>.
    Assume its paid root receipt: whenever the window of the selected
    schedule from <math|i> to <math|J<rsub|i>> is open, that is,
    <math|i\<leqslant\>J<rsub|i>> and <math|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|i>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|i>/A<rsub|J<rsub|i>>>
    with <math|\<sigma\><rsub|j>> the cover size of the selected clock at
    level <math|j>, the reference level of grade <math|d> is at most
    <math|J<rsub|i>>, <math|J<rsub|i>\<less\>K<rsub|i>>, the scale <math|i>
    has room for a radius <math|l> at scale <math|K<rsub|i>>, and <math|g> is
    interior to the window of radius <math|l>,

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-R\><rsub|i,p>*<space|-0.17em><around*|(|\<cal-B\>*<around*|(|A<rsub|K<rsub|i>><rsup|-d>*g|)>-\<cal-O\><around|(|x|)>|)>\<leqslant\>n<rsub|d,i>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|i,p>> is the root functional of the tiling of
    the volume-<math|N> lattice by scale-<math|i> cells, <math|\<cal-B\>> the
    extension from scale <math|K<rsub|i>> to volume <math|N>, and
    <math|\<cal-O\><around|(|x|)>> the volume-<math|N> observation of the
    label <math|x> at the origin. Assume that <math|i> is so large that
    <eqref|eq:ex-trace-native-rate> holds. Then the paid column has size at
    most <math|2>. For <math|q<rsub|i>=<around|(|A<rsub|i>/A<rsub|K<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|d>>,
    linearity and homogeneity give the scaled error bound

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-R\><rsub|i,p>*<space|-0.17em><around*|(|\<cal-B\>*<around|(|q<rsub|i>*g|)>-\<cal-O\>*<around|(|A<rsub|i><rsup|d>*x|)>|)>\<leqslant\>\<tau\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-430|)>.
    </equation*>

    For all sufficiently large <math|i>, this is the paid-component error
    used in the probe assembly, uniformly over <math|N>, <math|p>, the radii
    <math|l> and the observables satisfying the stated window, room and
    interiority conditions.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The coefficient conclusion is Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-trace-bill-column-size>.
    The inverse dilation satisfies <math|q<rsub|i>*g=A<rsub|i><rsup|d>*<around|(|A<rsub|K<rsub|i>><rsup|-d>*g|)>>.
    By real-linearity of the finite-volume observation and pullback,

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-B\>*<around|(|q<rsub|i>*g|)>-\<cal-O\>*<around|(|A<rsub|i><rsup|d>*x|)>=A<rsub|i><rsup|d>*<around*|(|\<cal-B\>*<around|(|A<rsub|K<rsub|i>><rsup|-d>*g|)>-\<cal-O\><around|(|x|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|A<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0>, homogeneity of the root seminorm and the
    assumed paid receipt bound this by <math|A<rsub|i><rsup|d>*n<rsub|d,i>>.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:ex-trace-native-rate> then gives the stated
    <math|\<tau\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-430|)>> bound, uniformly over all
    allowed windows and observables.
  </proof>

  The label-producing receipt is supplied by the Bill specialization in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-coffer-supplier>. Its label
  is chosen once, before the window tests, from the actual atomic response,
  density identity and two weight payments. For each fixed inflation and
  grade, the scale is first chosen sufficiently late, then the period
  sufficiently large at that scale; the conclusion is uniform over every
  larger cutoff and every permitted window for that one label. The chosen and
  reference atomic certificates used here are constructed in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-local-charge>. Thus the
  present coefficient and rate arithmetic applies to the same labels, rather
  than to separately selected witnesses for different tests.

  <subsection|Assembly of the finite-volume response
  probe><label|sec:ex-lattice-trace-probe-assembly>

  We spell out the finite algebra that turns two separately paid local
  components and a buffered chamber approximation into the probe used in the
  mission. The quantitative assumptions below are exactly the estimates the
  assembly consumes. In particular, the proof establishes every component of
  the probe conclusion and does not regard the existence of a record with
  those components as a proof.

  Fix a frame level <math|j>, its volume level <math|k>, and a finite volume
  <math|N\<gtr\>k>. Write

  <\equation*>
    t=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>,<space|2em>a\<gtr\>0,<space|2em>\<beta\>\<gtr\>0,<space|2em>q=<around|(|a/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|5>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|a<rsub|k>> is the field scale at the volume level. In the
  selected construction <math|k> is the summit index of <math|j> (so
  <math|k\<gtr\>j>), <math|a=a<rsub|j>> is the field scale at the frame
  level, and <math|\<beta\>> is the finite-volume coupling at level <math|k>
  in the volume <math|N>. The field scales satisfy <math|a<rsub|0>=1> and
  decrease strictly, so <math|a<rsub|j>\<less\>1> for <math|j\<gtr\>0>: only
  the positivity of <math|a> is available, and only positivity is used below.
  Let <math|\<tau\><rsub|j>> be the error scale

  <\equation*>
    \<tau\><rsub|j><around|(|s|)>=exp <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<frac|s|10<rsup|4>><around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|)><rsup|48>|)>,<space|2em>E=\<tau\><rsub|j>*<around|(|-430|)>.
  </equation*>

  Assume the scale bounds

  <\equation>
    t\<geqslant\>1,<space|1em>0\<less\>\<beta\>\<leqslant\>2*t,<space|1em>1+2*t\<leqslant\>\<tau\><rsub|j><around|(|230|)>,<space|1em>6*c\<leqslant\>t,<label|eq:ex-trace-probe-scales>
  </equation>

  where <math|c> is the coefficient cost of the finite symmetry average
  defined below. The error scales obey <math|\<tau\><rsub|j><around|(|s|)>*\<tau\><rsub|j><around|(|s<rprime|'>|)>=\<tau\><rsub|j>*<around|(|s+s<rprime|'>|)>>.

  Let <math|\<frak-A\>=<math-up|Signed><around|(|4|)>\<times\><around|{|0,1|}>>
  be the finite motion set; the second factor records whether complex
  conjugation is applied. For <math|\<mu\>=<around|(|s,e|)>>, write
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|\<mu\>>> for the real-linear action on grade-five
  coefficients and <math|\<sigma\><rsub|\<mu\>>> for the corresponding
  real-linear action on observables. For <math|\<mu\>=<around|(|s,e|)>> and
  axis <math|d>, define <math|<math-up|prev><around|(|\<mu\>,d|)>=s.<math-up|perm><rsup|-1><around|(|d|)>>
  and <math|\<epsilon\>*<around|(|\<mu\>,d|)>=<math-up|sign><around|(|s,<math-up|prev><around|(|\<mu\>,d|)>|)>>;
  the sign does not depend on the conjugation bit <math|e>. Put
  <math|R=<around|\||\<frak-A\>|\|><rsup|-1>>. The finite averages have the
  form

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|<with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|5>*V|)><rsub|d>>|<cell|=R*<big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>\<in\>\<frak-A\>>\<epsilon\>*<around|(|\<mu\>,d|)>*<space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|\<mu\>>*V<rsub|<math-up|prev><around|(|\<mu\>,d|)>>,>|<cell|R*<big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>\<in\>\<frak-A\>><around|\||\<epsilon\>*<around|(|\<mu\>,d|)>|\|>>|<cell|=1,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-trace-probe-coeff-average>>>|<row|<cell|<around|(|<with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|0>*f|)><rsub|d>>|<cell|=R*<big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>\<in\>\<frak-A\>>\<epsilon\>*<around|(|\<mu\>,d|)>*<space|0.17em>\<sigma\><rsub|\<mu\>>*f<rsub|<math-up|prev><around|(|\<mu\>,d|)>>.>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number><label|eq:ex-trace-probe-observable-average>>>>>
  </align>

  This is a real-linear Reynolds average. Reindexing by
  <math|\<mu\>\<mapsto\>g*\<mu\>> for each signed motion <math|g> and using
  the defining previous-axis and sign formulas gives the exact covariance
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|g>*Z<rsub|d>=<math-up|sign><around|(|g,d|)>*Z<rsub|g.<math-up|perm><around|(|d|)>>>.
  Toggling the second motion coordinate reindexes the conjugation action and
  proves the reality condition. In particular, central inversion fixes every
  axis and has sign <math|-1>, so <math|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|archV>>*Z<rsub|d>=-Z<rsub|d>>
  for each <math|d>. Oddness is a consequence of this vector-valued Reynolds
  projection, not a claim about arbitrary grade-five labels.

  Let <math|<math-up|Col>> be the finite-volume column map from grade-five
  labels to coefficient arrays. It is real-linear and commutes with each
  motion by finite-volume covariance. The paid labels obey
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|Col><around|(|X<rsub|d>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|Col><around|(|Y<rsub|d>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2>;
  this is the transported column bound, including the norm of the observation
  map, rather than a bound on the raw label alone. Define its averaged
  coefficient cost by

  <\equation*>
    c=R*sup<rsub|d> <big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>\<in\>\<frak-A\>><around|\<\|\|\>|\<rho\><rsub|\<mu\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|col>>.
  </equation*>

  The triangle inequality yields <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|Col><around|(|<with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|5>*V|)><rsub|d>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>c*sup<rsub|e><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|Col><around|(|V<rsub|e>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>.
  This is the precise finite column estimate used below; the signed or
  conjugate motion is real-linear, not assumed complex-linear or isometric in
  the column norm. The observable average has total variation one, so, on
  chamber-interior observables, the reflection seam makes it preserve a
  common root-seminorm bound.

  Let <math|x<rsub|d>,y<rsub|d>> be two arrays of finite-volume observables,
  each interior to a fixed locality window. Let <math|X<rsub|d>,Y<rsub|d>> be
  their grade-five paid coefficients. The paid receipts and the finite-volume
  column map give the transported column bounds
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|Col><around|(|X<rsub|d>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2>
  and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|Col><around|(|Y<rsub|d>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2>.
  Write <math|\<cal-O\>> for the linear finite-volume observation into a
  one-point observable, <math|\<cal-B\>> for pullback from the coarse volume
  to the reflection chamber at level <math|N>, and <math|\<cal-R\>> for the
  chamber root seminorm. Assume each paid residual, each pulled-back local
  field, and each observed label below is interior to the same reflection
  chamber, with the locality window and room conditions required for the
  finite-volume seam. On this interior class, assume the seam gives
  root-seminorm subadditivity and invariance under each motion. The exact
  covariance identities are

  <\equation>
    \<cal-O\>*<space|0.17em><with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|5>=<with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|0>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-O\>,<space|2em>\<cal-B\>*<space|0.17em><with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|0>=<with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|0>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-B\>.<label|eq:ex-trace-probe-covariance>
  </equation>

  These follow term by term from linearity and covariance of the
  finite-volume observation and pullback under each signed motion.

  The paid-component estimates used here are the following explicit
  inequalities, for every axis <math|d>:

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-R\>*<space|-0.17em><around*|(|\<cal-B\>*<around|(|q*x<rsub|d>|)>-\<cal-O\>*<around|(|a<rsup|5>*X<rsub|d>|)>|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>E,>|<cell|\<cal-R\>*<space|-0.17em><around*|(|\<cal-B\>*<around|(|q*y<rsub|d>|)>-\<cal-O\>*<around|(|a<rsup|5>*Y<rsub|d>|)>|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>E.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-trace-probe-paid-errors>>>>>
  </align>

  The buffered chamber estimate is this: if

  <\equation*>
    J<rsub|d>=q*<around*|(|x<rsub|d>-\<beta\>*y<rsub|d>|)>
  </equation*>

  and <math|g> is any admissible coarse observable with supremum norm at most
  <math|\<tau\><rsub|j><around|(|150000|)>>, interior to the fixed grade-four
  locality window of radius <math|l<rsub|4>> of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-trace-crown-buffer>, with room for
  <math|l<rsub|4>> at scale <math|k>, then at any two distinct coarse sites,

  <\equation>
    <around*|\||\<bbb-E\>*<around*|[|<around|(|\<cal-B\>*<with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|0>*J|)><rsub|d><around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em><around|(|\<cal-B\>*g|)><around|(|z|)>|]>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<tau\><rsub|j>*<around|(|-30000|)>.<label|eq:ex-trace-probe-buffered>
  </equation>

  The estimate is supplied by the finite-volume chamber buffer; its proof is
  upstream of this assembly. The scale assumptions and the paid-component
  bounds are supplied by the construction's budget and response receipts.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Probe from paid components and a chamber
    buffer><label|prop:ex-trace-probe-assembly>Under the assumptions above,
    define

    <\equation*>
      Z<rsub|d>=<around|(|<with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|5>*<around|(|X-\<beta\>*Y|)>|)><rsub|d>,<space|2em>J<rsub|d>=q*<around|(|x<rsub|d>-\<beta\>*y<rsub|d>|)>,<space|2em>P<rsub|d>=\<cal-O\>*<around|(|a<rsup|5>*Z<rsub|d>|)>,<space|2em>U<rsub|d>=<around|(|\<cal-B\>*<with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|0>*J|)><rsub|d>.
    </equation*>

    Then <math|Z> is signed-covariant, with each component satisfying
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|archV>>*Z<rsub|d>=-Z<rsub|d>>. Moreover, for
    every axis <math|d>,

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|Col><around|(|a<rsup|5>*Z<rsub|d>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>a<rsup|5>*t<rsup|4>>;

      <item><math|U<rsub|d>> is chamber-interior and
      <math|\<cal-R\>*<around|(|U<rsub|d>-P<rsub|d>|)>\<leqslant\>\<tau\><rsub|j>*<around|(|-200|)>>;

      <item>for every admissible bounded local test in
      <eqref|eq:ex-trace-probe-buffered>, that is, interior to the grade-four
      window of radius <math|l<rsub|4>>, the separated-site pairing of
      <math|U<rsub|d>> with its pullback is at most
      <math|\<tau\><rsub|j>*<around|(|-200|)>> in absolute value.
    </enumerate>

    Thus <math|P<rsub|d>> has exactly the coefficient, parity, root-error,
    and local-price parts required of the finite-volume probe.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Real-linearity of the column map and the transported column bounds give
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|Col><around|(|X<rsub|d>-\<beta\>*Y<rsub|d>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2+2*\<beta\>>.
    Covariance lets the finite-average bound apply to <math|Z>, so

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|Col><around|(|Z<rsub|d>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>c*<around|(|2+2*\<beta\>|)>\<leqslant\>c*<around|(|2+4*t|)>\<leqslant\>6*c*t\<leqslant\>t<rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|t\<geqslant\>1> we have <math|t<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>t<rsup|4>>.
    The column map is real-linear and the column norm is homogeneous under
    positive real scalars, so, as <math|a<rsup|5>\<gtr\>0>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|Col><around|(|a<rsup|5>*Z<rsub|d>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>=a<rsup|5><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|Col><around|(|Z<rsub|d>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>a<rsup|5>*t<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsup|5>*t<rsup|4>.
    </equation*>

    No lower bound on <math|a> enters. The finite signed-motion average has
    the covariance just established; central inversion fixes each axis with
    sign <math|-1>, giving the oddness assertion for every component.

    By linearity and the covariance identities,

    <\equation*>
      U<rsub|d>-P<rsub|d>=<around*|(|<with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|0>*<around*|(|\<cal-B\>*<around|(|q*x|)>-\<cal-O\>*<around|(|a<rsup|5>*X|)>|)>|)><rsub|d>-\<beta\>*<around*|(|<with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|0>*<around*|(|\<cal-B\>*<around|(|q*y|)>-\<cal-O\>*<around|(|a<rsup|5>*Y|)>|)>|)><rsub|d>.
    </equation*>

    The root seminorm is invariant under each signed motion, is subadditive,
    and the averaging weights have total absolute value one. Hence

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-R\>*<around|(|U<rsub|d>-P<rsub|d>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+\<beta\>|)>*E\<leqslant\>\<tau\><rsub|j><around|(|230|)>*\<tau\><rsub|j>*<around|(|-430|)>=\<tau\><rsub|j>*<around|(|-200|)>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|1+\<beta\>\<leqslant\>1+2*t\<leqslant\>\<tau\><rsub|j><around|(|230|)>>
    was used. The interiority of <math|U<rsub|d>> follows because each motion
    preserves the fixed locality window and finite averaging preserves the
    interior domain.

    Finally, the buffered estimate gives a separated-site price at most
    <math|\<tau\><rsub|j>*<around|(|-30000|)>>, which is at most
    <math|\<tau\><rsub|j>*<around|(|-200|)>> by monotonicity of
    <math|\<tau\><rsub|j>>. This proves all three conclusions.
  </proof>

  The proposition isolates the actual finite averaging and error arithmetic
  in the selected probe producer. The remaining inputs to this local
  argument, not proved in this section, are the two paid-component estimates
  <eqref|eq:ex-trace-probe-paid-errors>, the finite-volume covariance
  identities, and the buffered chamber estimate
  <eqref|eq:ex-trace-probe-buffered>. For all sufficiently large <math|j>,
  the paid-component estimates and the transported column bounds are the
  conclusions of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-trace-bill-root-price>
  with grade <math|5>, applied to each observable and paid label
  <math|<around|(|x<rsub|d>,X<rsub|d>|)>> and
  <math|<around|(|y<rsub|d>,Y<rsub|d>|)>>; its actual label and root receipt
  are supplied by the Bill specialization of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-coffer-supplier>, with one
  label for every allowed window. The buffered estimate is the conclusion of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-trace-crown-buffer>, in which
  <math|F<rsub|j>=\<tau\><rsub|j>>, applied to the scale-<math|k> field
  <math|f=<around|(|<with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|0>*J|)><rsub|d>>. That
  proposition requires a Crown witness for this particular averaged
  combination, together with its seam, transfer and one-site inputs; this
  particular grade-five witness is constructed in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-local-pairing> by the
  paired-lens expansion and the exact healthy-region identity. It is distinct
  from the grade-four localizer in that section. Two of the scale bounds
  <eqref|eq:ex-trace-probe-scales> reduce to inputs already used in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-bill-prices>. Indeed,
  <math|t=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>> with <math|k> the summit index of
  <math|j>, and <math|t\<geqslant\>1>, so <math|t\<leqslant\>t<rsup|4>>. The
  scale-at-summit receipt is a rate-zero ticket for <math|t<rsup|4>>, with
  gate <math|G<rsub|j>=<around|(|log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|)><rsup|48>>.
  Hence eventually <math|1+2*t\<leqslant\>3*t\<leqslant\>3*exp
  <around|(|229*<space|0.17em>G<rsub|j>/10<rsup|4>|)>\<leqslant\>\<tau\><rsub|j><around|(|230|)>>,
  the last step because <math|3\<leqslant\>\<tau\><rsub|j><around|(|1|)>> for
  large <math|j>. Moreover <math|6*c\<leqslant\>t> for large <math|j>, since
  <math|c> is a fixed constant and <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>\<to\>\<infty\>>
  as <math|k\<geqslant\>j\<to\>\<infty\>>. For the other two bounds,
  <math|t=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>\<to\>\<infty\>> gives
  <math|t\<geqslant\>1> on one scale tail. The finite-volume representation
  at the same level <math|k> satisfies its coupling band

  <\equation*>
    <tfrac|1|2><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>\<leqslant\>2*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>*<space|2em><around|(|N\<geqslant\>k|)>.
  </equation*>

  This is the band of the selected effective representations supplied by
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:selected-value-input>; it holds for all their
  periods and volume indices. Substituting the actual coupling
  <math|\<beta\>=\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>> gives
  <math|0\<less\>\<beta\>\<leqslant\>2*t>. This argument uses the
  finite-volume band, not monotonicity of an unrelated hatted coupling.
  Together with Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:trace-bill-scale-tickets>, it
  supplies all four bounds in <eqref|eq:ex-trace-probe-scales>. In
  particular, this section does not claim that arbitrary local components or
  an arbitrary chamber record satisfy those paid estimates.

  <subsection|From finite-volume mileage to the selected chart
  price><label|sec:ex-lattice-trace-mission-price>

  This section proves the passage from the selected finite-volume mission to
  its local price in the limiting table. The finite-volume separation
  estimate of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-finite-volume-pairing>
  supplies the numerical bound used by the mileage argument. The mission's
  funded probe, its local approximations, and the selected chart convergence
  are stated explicitly below; this section does not claim to produce those
  analytic receipts.

  Write <math|i> for the scale parameter, <math|j<rsub|i>> for its selected
  frame level, and <math|k<rsub|i>=<math-up|summit><around|(|j<rsub|i>|)>>
  for the associated volume scale. Let <math|a<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> be the field
  scale, <math|b<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> the zoom factor, and
  <math|r<rsub|i>=a<rsub|i>*b<rsub|i>> the physical insertion scale. Let
  <math|\<cal-L\>> be the ultrafilter on finite-volume indices
  <math|p=<around|(|N,s|)>> used by the construction, and define

  <\equation*>
    \<tau\><rsub|i><around|(|q|)>=exp <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<frac|q|10<rsup|4>><around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|48>|)>.
  </equation*>

  A mission consists of arrays <math|t<rsub|i><around|(|p|)>> and
  <math|x<rsub|i>> such that, for all sufficiently large <math|i>,
  <math|t<rsub|i><around|(|p|)>\<to\>x<rsub|i>> along <math|\<cal-L\>>.
  Moreover, there is one fixed integer <math|m> such that for all
  sufficiently large <math|i> and for <math|\<cal-L\>>-almost every
  <math|p=<around|(|N,s|)>>, a probe of complexity at most <math|m>, based at
  level <math|j<rsub|i>>, has marked grade-five field
  <math|a<rsub|i><rsup|5>*t<rsub|i><around|(|p|)>>. For such a probe, its
  measured marker <math|P> has coefficient size at most
  <math|a<rsub|i><rsup|5>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|i>><rsup|m>> and is
  odd under the central signed inversion. Its toll receipt supplies a bounded
  local observable <math|U> whose pullback to the finite-volume chamber is
  interior and whose root error relative to <math|P> is at most
  <math|\<tau\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-200|)>>. For every pair of distinct coarse
  sites <math|x,z>, it also supplies the bound

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<bbb-E\><rsub|i,p>*<around|[|<wide|U|~><around|(|x|)>*<wide|g|~><around|(|z|)>|]>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<tau\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-200|)>,
  </equation*>

  where tildes denote pullback to the finite-volume chamber. Here <math|g> is
  any coarse test whose pointwise absolute value is at most
  <math|\<tau\><rsub|i><around|(|150000|)>>, which is interior to the fixed
  grade-four locality window, and whose pullback is chamber-interior,
  provided that the window fits in the available room at frame level
  <math|j<rsub|i>> inside the volume at scale <math|k<rsub|i>>. For the
  selected grade-four insertion, a localization receipt supplies one coarse
  observable <math|g> with all of the following properties: its pointwise
  absolute value is at most <math|\<tau\><rsub|i><around|(|150000|)>>; it is
  interior to the fixed grade-four locality window, and that window fits in
  the available room at frame level <math|j<rsub|i>> inside the volume at
  scale <math|k<rsub|i>>; its pullback <math|V=<wide|g|~>> is
  chamber-interior; the root error of <math|V> relative to the measured
  insertion is at most <math|\<tau\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-300|)>>; and the
  measured insertion has root cost at most
  <math|\<tau\><rsub|i><around|(|5|)>>. The first three properties are
  exactly the admissibility conditions on the test in the probe's
  separated-site bound, so that bound applies to the pair
  <math|<around|(|<wide|U|~>,V|)>> with this particular <math|g>; a root
  approximation alone would not permit this. The measured marker <math|P> has
  root cost at most <math|\<tau\><rsub|i><around|(|5|)>> by a separate
  coefficient-to-root estimate, which uses its coefficient bound and its
  oddness. These are the quantitative inputs consumed by the finite-volume
  mileage estimate; their construction remains an upstream obligation.

  Fix a reference grade-four field <math|w>. Let
  <math|H<rsub|i,p><around|(|w|)>> be its finite-height insertion
  approximant, which is a raw grade-four label. It is determined by the fixed
  reference lift of <math|w> to a raw grade-four label, by the grade-four
  transfer that the <math|i>th selected chart uses at the finite-volume index
  <math|p>, by the zoom factor <math|b<rsub|i>> and by the frame level
  <math|j<rsub|i>>; its formula is not needed here. The mileage estimate is
  formulated for insertions of exactly this form, under the conditions that
  this transfer is a tested transfer at level <math|j<rsub|i>> and that
  <math|1\<leqslant\>b<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><math-up|tiltcap><around|(|j<rsub|i>|)>>;
  both conditions are part of the selected chart receipt for large <math|i>.
  The selected hierarchy receipt asserts <math|H<rsub|i,p><around|(|w|)>\<to\>y<rsub|i><around|(|w|)>>
  along <math|\<cal-L\>>, where <math|y<rsub|i><around|(|w|)>> is the
  limiting raw field and <math|\<pi\><rsub|4>*y<rsub|i><around|(|w|)>=U<rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|4|)>>*w>.
  It also ensures, on an <math|\<cal-L\>>-large set, that the finite transfer
  is admissible, the marker and insertion lie in the same reflection chamber,
  and the two coarse sites are distinct. Let <math|\<xi\><rsub|i>> be the
  cover vector with first component <math|<around|\<lceil\>|b<rsub|i>|\<rceil\>>>
  and every other component <math|1>. Set
  <math|z<rsub|i>=b<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*\<xi\><rsub|i>> componentwise. The scale
  relation <math|r<rsub|i>=a<rsub|i>*b<rsub|i>> gives
  <math|r<rsub|i>*z<rsub|i>=a<rsub|i>*\<xi\><rsub|i>>. Thus the physical
  measurement point has the two cover coordinates

  <\equation*>
    \<ell\><rsub|i>=<around|(|\<xi\><rsub|i>,0|)>.
  </equation*>

  The separation receipt for these coordinates supplies the distinct-site
  hypothesis.

  Set <math|\<Theta\><rsub|i>=\<tau\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-100|)>>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Mission-to-chart price transfer><label|prop:ex-trace-mission-price-transfer>Fix
    <math|w>. Assume the mission properties above. Assume that for all
    sufficiently large <math|i> and <math|\<cal-L\>>-almost every
    finite-volume index <math|p>, mileage applies to the probe and insertion
    and gives

    <\equation>
      <around*|\||\<bbb-E\><rsub|i,p>*<space|-0.17em><around*|[|A<rsub|i,p>*<around|(|a<rsub|i><rsup|5>*t<rsub|i><around|(|p|)>;x<rsub|i><rsup|\<ast\>>|)>*<space|0.17em>B<rsub|i,p>*<around|(|H<rsub|i,p><around|(|w|)>;y<rsub|i><rsup|\<ast\>>|)>|]>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<Theta\><rsub|i>.<label|eq:ex-trace-mission-finite-price>
    </equation>

    Here <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|i,p>> is the normalized finite-volume
    expectation, <math|A<rsub|i,p>> and <math|B<rsub|i,p>> are the measured
    grade-five and grade-four observables at the two distinct sites
    <math|x<rsub|i><rsup|\<ast\>>,y<rsub|i><rsup|\<ast\>>> determined by
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>>. Assume the selected-table limit theorem: whenever
    <math|u<rsub|p>\<to\>u> and <math|v<rsub|p>\<to\>v> along
    <math|\<cal-L\>>,

    <\equation>
      \<bbb-E\><rsub|i,p>*<around|[|A<rsub|i,p>*<around|(|u<rsub|p>;x<rsub|i><rsup|\<ast\>>|)>*B<rsub|i,p>*<around|(|v<rsub|p>;y<rsub|i><rsup|\<ast\>>|)>|]>\<longrightarrow\>\<cal-M\><rsub|i>*<around|(|\<pi\><rsub|5>*u,\<pi\><rsub|4>*v|)>,<label|eq:ex-trace-mission-table-limit>
    </equation>

    where <math|\<pi\><rsub|d>> is the limiting projection in grade <math|d>
    and <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|i>> is the selected table's two-entry pairing at
    the scale-<math|r<rsub|i>> point. Then, for all sufficiently large
    <math|i> (with threshold allowed to depend on <math|w>),

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||\<cal-M\><rsub|i>*<space|-0.17em><around*|(|a<rsub|i><rsup|5>*\<pi\><rsub|5>*x<rsub|i>,U<rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|4|)>>*w|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<Theta\><rsub|i>.
    </equation*>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Fix a sufficiently large <math|i> for which the mission, mileage, and
    chart receipts hold. The set on which the probe and finite-volume
    insertion conditions hold belongs to <math|\<cal-L\>>. On this set,
    <eqref|eq:ex-trace-mission-finite-price> places every finite expectation
    in the closed disk of radius <math|\<Theta\><rsub|i>>. Mission
    convergence gives <math|a<rsub|i><rsup|5>*t<rsub|i><around|(|p|)>\<to\>a<rsub|i><rsup|5>*x<rsub|i>>,
    and the chart receipt gives <math|H<rsub|i,p><around|(|w|)>\<to\>y<rsub|i><around|(|w|)>>
    with <math|\<pi\><rsub|4>*y<rsub|i><around|(|w|)>=U<rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|4|)>>*w>.
    Applying <eqref|eq:ex-trace-mission-table-limit> shows that the finite
    expectations converge to <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|i>*<around|(|a<rsub|i><rsup|5>*\<pi\><rsub|5>*x<rsub|i>,U<rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|4|)>>*w|)>>;
    linearity of <math|\<pi\><rsub|5>> was used to take the fixed scalar
    <math|a<rsub|i><rsup|5>> outside. Since the disk is closed, the limit
    satisfies the same bound. This proves the proposition.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The finite-volume mileage receipt>

  The error scale is multiplicative: <math|\<tau\><rsub|i><around|(|q|)>*\<tau\><rsub|i><around|(|q<rprime|'>|)>=\<tau\><rsub|i>*<around|(|q+q<rprime|'>|)>>,
  and it is increasing in <math|q>. In the finite-volume estimate, both
  measured markers have root cost at most
  <math|\<tau\><rsub|i><around|(|5|)>>. The probe approximation contributes
  root error <math|\<tau\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-200|)>> and separated-site
  price <math|\<tau\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-200|)>>; the local insertion
  approximation contributes root error <math|\<tau\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-300|)>>.
  The reflection-positive product bound controls the two cross terms by
  products of root costs. Bilinearity gives the exact decomposition

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<langle\>|P,Q|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|U,V|\<rangle\>>+<around|\<langle\>|P,Q-V|\<rangle\>>+<around|\<langle\>|P-U,V|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation*>

  The three terms are bounded respectively by

  <\equation*>
    \<tau\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-200|)>,<space|2em>\<tau\><rsub|i><around|(|5|)>*\<tau\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-300|)>=\<tau\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-295|)>,<space|2em>\<tau\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-200|)>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|i><around|(|5|)>+\<tau\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-300|)>|)>=\<tau\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-195|)>+\<tau\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-500|)>.
  </equation*>

  Thus the sum is at most <math|4*\<tau\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-195|)>>. The
  selected growth receipt gives <math|4\<leqslant\>\<tau\><rsub|i><around|(|1|)>>
  eventually, hence <math|4*\<tau\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-195|)>\<leqslant\>\<tau\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-194|)>\<leqslant\>\<tau\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-100|)>>.
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-finite-volume-pairing> proves
  this three-term estimate from the explicit root and reflection-positive
  bounds. The remaining hard content is construction of the probe, local
  approximation, and root-cost receipts at the selected scales.

  The mission's fixed-complexity probe is to be produced by a finite-cover
  construction, and its limiting value by convergence of the selected core
  field; both are inputs here. The limit transfer above is complete once
  those receipts are supplied. Producing the funded cover, the probe witness,
  and the quantitative localization receipts remains part of the larger
  construction.

  <subsection|The trace sensor and its weighted Ward
  limit><label|sec:trace-prosper-sensor>

  Fix one selected family and a gauge group given as a closed subgroup of a
  finite-dimensional unitary group, whose real Lie algebra is nonabelian and
  simple. Let <math|E> be the real finite-dimensional space of grade-five
  vector coefficient arrays obeying the reality and signed covariance
  conditions, and let <math|H> be the corresponding reference vector table
  space. The free projection <math|f:E\<to\>H> applies the reference field
  projection in each vector component. Let <math|x\<in\>E> be the coefficient
  array of the free equation of motion. The free rank calculation in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-free-rank> gives
  <math|x\<ne\>0> and <math|ker f=\<bbb-R\>*x>. To fix the free Ward
  normalization, write the raw equation-of-motion array as
  <math|J<rsup|<around|(|3|)>><rsub|b>=<big|sum><rsub|i,a><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|b*i>,\<partial\><rsub|a>*F<rsub|a*i>|\<rangle\>>>,
  and let

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|J<rsup|<around|(|1|)>>|)><rsub|b>=<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|b*i>,\<partial\><rsub|b>*F<rsub|b*i>|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em><around|(|J<rsup|<around|(|2|)>>|)><rsub|b>=<big|sum><rsub|i,j><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|i*j>,\<partial\><rsub|b>*F<rsub|i*j>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|F> and <math|\<partial\>*F> are the free curvature jet and its
  first derivative, and the inner product is the gauge-invariant real color
  inner product. Each array is real and invariant under the color action. For
  a coordinate reflection, every contracted index contributes its sign twice,
  leaving only the sign of the output index <math|b>; permutations merely
  relabel the sums. Thus each array has the required signed-vector
  covariance. These three arrays form a basis of the raw vector space. To see
  independence, choose a unit color vector and evaluate the zeroth component
  on three jets. In each, the only nonzero curvature pair, up to
  antisymmetry, is respectively <math|F<rsub|12>>, <math|F<rsub|01>>, and
  <math|F<rsub|01>>. The nonzero derivative entries, again up to
  antisymmetry, are respectively

  <\equation*>
    <tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-lborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-rborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-halign|l>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-rborder|0ln>|<table|<row|<cell|1>|<cell|\<partial\><rsub|0>*F<rsub|12>=1,<space|1em>\<partial\><rsub|1>*F<rsub|20>=-<tfrac|1|2>,<space|1em>\<partial\><rsub|2>*F<rsub|01>=-<tfrac|1|2>,>>|<row|<cell|2>|<cell|\<partial\><rsub|0>*F<rsub|01>=1,<space|1em>\<partial\><rsub|2>*F<rsub|21>=-1,>>|<row|<cell|3>|<cell|\<partial\><rsub|0>*F<rsub|01>=1.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  Each scalar stands for that scalar times the chosen color vector. These
  jets satisfy the Bianchi identity; for example a curvature jet
  <math|<around|(|F<rsub|a*b>,K<rsub|c*a*b>|)>> with
  <math|K<rsub|c*a*b>+K<rsub|a*b*c>+K<rsub|b*c*a>=0> is realized by the
  polynomial potential <math|A<rsub|b><around|(|z|)>=<tfrac|1|2>z<rsup|a>*F<rsub|a*b>+<tfrac|1|3>z<rsup|a>*z<rsup|c>*K<rsub|c*a*b>>.
  It is in radial gauge, since <math|z<rsup|b>*A<rsub|b><around|(|z|)>=0> by
  antisymmetry in <math|a,b>. Its curl at the origin is <math|F<rsub|a*b>>,
  and its first curl derivative is

  <\equation*>
    \<partial\><rsub|c>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*A<rsub|b>-\<partial\><rsub|b>*A<rsub|a>|)><around|(|0|)>=<tfrac|1|3><around|(|2*K<rsub|c*a*b>-K<rsub|a*b*c>-K<rsub|b*c*a>|)>=K<rsub|c*a*b>,
  </equation*>

  where the last equality uses the cyclic identity. Direct substitution gives
  the evaluation matrix

  <\equation*>
    <matrix|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|0>|<cell|2>|<cell|0>>|<row|<cell|1>|<cell|2>|<cell|0>>|<row|<cell|1>|<cell|2>|<cell|1>>>>><space|1em><text|for
    ><around|(|J<rsup|<around|(|1|)>><rsub|0>,J<rsup|<around|(|2|)>><rsub|0>,J<rsup|<around|(|3|)>><rsub|0>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Its determinant is nonzero. The free-rank calculation gives the matching
  upper bound three for the raw vector dimension, so the arrays form a basis.
  The label-coordinate identification carries <math|x> to
  <math|J<rsup|<around|(|3|)>>>. Every raw vector therefore has unique real
  coordinates <math|<around|(|w<rsub|0>,w<rsub|1>,w<rsub|2>|)>> in the form

  <\equation*>
    2*<around|(|w<rsub|0>-w<rsub|2>|)>*J<rsup|<around|(|1|)>>+<around|(|2*w<rsub|1>+<tfrac|1|2>w<rsub|2>|)>*J<rsup|<around|(|2|)>>+w<rsub|2>*J<rsup|<around|(|3|)>>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed, the <math|J<rsup|<around|(|3|)>>> coefficient determines
  <math|w<rsub|2>>, and the other two coefficients then determine
  <math|w<rsub|0>> and <math|w<rsub|1>>. To see the Ward normalization, let
  <math|\<cal-I\>=<big|sum><rsub|i,j><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|i*j>,F<rsub|i*j>|\<rangle\>>>
  and consider the centered stress tensor with diagonal entries
  <math|T<rsub|a*a>=w<rsub|0>*<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|a*i>,F<rsub|a*i>|\<rangle\>>+w<rsub|1>*\<cal-I\>>
  and off-diagonal entries <math|T<rsub|a*b>=w<rsub|2>*<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|a*i>,F<rsub|b*i>|\<rangle\>>>.
  Its divergence has the displayed raw-vector coordinates. Indeed the
  diagonal derivatives contribute <math|2*w<rsub|0>*J<rsup|<around|(|1|)>>+2*w<rsub|1>*J<rsup|<around|(|2|)>>>.
  For the off-diagonal term, first sum over all <math|a>. One product-rule
  term is <math|J<rsup|<around|(|3|)>>>. For the other, write
  <math|B=<big|sum><rsub|a,i><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|a*i>,\<partial\><rsub|a>*F<rsub|b*i>|\<rangle\>>>.
  The cyclic Bianchi identity, antisymmetry, and interchange of <math|a,i>
  give

  <\equation*>
    B=<big|sum><rsub|a,i><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|a*i>,\<partial\><rsub|b>*F<rsub|a*i>|\<rangle\>>-<big|sum><rsub|a,i><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|a*i>,\<partial\><rsub|i>*F<rsub|a*b>|\<rangle\>>=J<rsup|<around|(|2|)>>-B.
  </equation*>

  Hence the two terms sum to <math|J<rsup|<around|(|3|)>>+<tfrac|1|2>J<rsup|<around|(|2|)>>>.
  Removing <math|a=b> subtracts <math|2*J<rsup|<around|(|1|)>>>. Its trace is
  <math|<around|(|w<rsub|0>+4*w<rsub|1>|)>*\<cal-I\>>. The scalar
  normalization is <math|\<cal-I\>/4>, and the free Ward functional
  <math|\<rho\>:E\<to\>\<bbb-R\>> is minus the coefficient of that scalar
  label in the trace. The label is nonzero, as seen by taking
  <math|F<rsub|01>> to be a unit color vector and the other independent
  curvature components to be zero. Hence <math|\<rho\>=-4*<around|(|w<rsub|0>+4*w<rsub|1>|)>>
  on the above coordinates. In particular <math|J<rsup|<around|(|3|)>>> has
  coordinates <math|<around|(|1,-<tfrac|1|4>,1|)>> and
  <math|\<rho\><around|(|x|)>=0>.

  Let <math|k<rsub|i>> be the selected initial levels, with
  <math|k<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>. Fix the reference level <math|j<rsub|0>>
  of the grade-five coefficient space. All finite-volume maps and estimates
  below are used at levels <math|j\<geqslant\>j<rsub|0>>; in particular,
  <math|k<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>j<rsub|0>> eventually. The running rate is
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+\<gamma\>*\<ell\><rsub|n>>,
  where <math|\<gamma\>\<gtr\>0>, <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n>\<gtr\>1>,
  and the schedule scale <math|\<ell\><rsub|n>\<to\>\<infty\>>. For a level
  <math|j>, let <math|q<around|(|j|)>\<gtr\>j> be the first level whose
  logarithmic scale separation from <math|j> is at least <math|<around|(|log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|)><rsup|48>>, and put
  <math|n<rsub|i>=q<around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>>. Consequently
  <math|n<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>, <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n<rsub|i>>\<to\>\<infty\>>,
  and the positive weight

  <\equation>
    \<frak-a\><rsub|i>=exp <space|-0.17em><around*|{|<frac|1|5><around*|(|log
    log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|2>|}>\<longrightarrow\>\<infty\>.<label|eq:trace-sensor-weight>
  </equation>

  This is the <math|1/5> age weight at the <em|postselected> level
  <math|n<rsub|i>>; it is not the reciprocal chart scale.

  We shall use the following clock and arch inputs. The schedule has natural
  block sizes <math|L<rsub|j>> and real increments
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|j+1>-\<ell\><rsub|j>=log L<rsub|j>>, with
  <math|L<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>3> and, eventually,
  <math|L<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>>. The clock offset
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>> and the arch scales <math|b<rsub|j>> are
  positive, and for every <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>,

  <\equation>
    <around|\||b<rsub|j>-<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>*\<ell\><rsub|j><space|1em><text|for
    all sufficiently large >j.<label|eq:trace-sensor-clock-input>
  </equation>

  These are selected-run inputs.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Arch-scale ratio><label|lem:trace-sensor-arch-ratio> Under the
    clock inputs above, <math|b<rsub|k<rsub|i>>/b<rsub|n<rsub|i>>\<to\>1>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Put <math|\<chi\>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>> and <math|g=<around|(|log
    \<chi\>|)><rsup|48>>, and let <math|q<around|(|k|)>=m+1>. By minimality,
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>-\<ell\><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>g>: if <math|m=k> it is
    zero, and otherwise the preceding level has not reached the threshold.
    For large <math|k>, <math|\<gamma\>*g\<leqslant\>\<chi\>>, so
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>2*\<chi\>>. The last increment
    obeys

    <\equation*>
      \<ell\><rsub|m+1>-\<ell\><rsub|m>=log
      L<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>log
      <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>log
      <around|(|2*\<chi\>|)>\<leqslant\>g.
    </equation*>

    Consequently <math|0\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|q<around|(|k|)>>-\<ell\><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>2*g>,
    and therefore

    <\equation*>
      <frac|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|q<around|(|k|)>>|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>=1+\<gamma\>*<frac|\<ell\><rsub|q<around|(|k|)>>-\<ell\><rsub|k>|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>\<longrightarrow\>1,
    </equation*>

    because <math|<around|(|log \<chi\>|)><rsup|48>/\<chi\>\<to\>0>. Also
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+\<gamma\>*\<ell\><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>\<gamma\>*\<ell\><rsub|j>>,
    so <eqref|eq:trace-sensor-clock-input> gives
    <math|b<rsub|j>/<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<to\>1>. Since both
    <math|k<rsub|i>> and <math|n<rsub|i>=q<around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>> tend to
    infinity,

    <\equation>
      <frac|b<rsub|k<rsub|i>>|b<rsub|n<rsub|i>>>\<longrightarrow\>1.<label|eq:trace-sensor-arch-ratio>
    </equation>
  </proof>

  Write <math|s<rsub|i>:E\<to\>H> for the selected, normalized grade-five
  chart map. For one vector component let <math|<math-ss|V>> be the complex
  coefficient space, <math|\<cal-F\>> the reference field space and
  <math|S<rsub|5>> the selected interacting field space. The interacting
  projection is <math|P:<math-ss|V>\<to\>S<rsub|5>>, the scale-<math|i>
  identification is a linear isomorphism <math|X<rsub|i>:\<cal-F\>\<to\>S<rsub|5>>,
  and the selected terminal lift is <math|Y<rsub|i>:\<cal-F\>\<to\><math-ss|V>>.
  Write <math|M<rsub|i>:<math-ss|V>\<to\><math-ss|V>> for the invertible arch
  map at <math|n<rsub|i>>, <math|t<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> for the threshold there,
  and <math|<math-up|core>:<math-ss|V>\<to\><math-ss|V>> for the projection
  deleting the unit coordinate: if <math|e<rsub|0>> is the unit coordinate
  vector, then <math|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>v=v-v<rsub|0>*e<rsub|0>>.
  These component maps are complex-linear; <math|s<rsub|i>> and
  <math|u<rsub|i>> act real-linearly on the corresponding covariant arrays.
  With arch scale <math|b<rsub|n>\<gtr\>0> and dilation radius
  <math|r<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0>, set <math|c<rsub|i>=b<rsub|n<rsub|i>>*r<rsub|i><rsup|5>>.
  On an eventual common tail, for each vector component indexed by
  <math|\<alpha\>>,

  <\equation>
    <around|(|s<rsub|i>*y|)><rsub|\<alpha\>>=X<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*P*<around|(|c<rsub|i>*t<rsub|i><rsup|2>*M<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*y<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>,<space|2em><around|(|u<rsub|i>*h|)><rsub|\<alpha\>>=c<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*t<rsub|i><rsup|-2><math-up|core><space|0.17em>M<rsub|i>*Y<rsub|i>*h<rsub|\<alpha\>>.<label|eq:trace-sensor-selected-formulas>
  </equation>

  The second formula is for the selected lift. Its well-definedness as a map
  into <math|E> follows from the chart symmetries below. On the finitely many
  indices outside this tail, choose arbitrary real-linear maps
  <math|u<rsub|i>:H\<to\>E>. The limiting right inverse <math|U> applies the
  fixed reference lift to each component. For that lift, the reference field
  is the quotient of the coefficient space by its free equation subspace
  <math|K>. For the finite signed-permutation group <math|\<Gamma\>>, write
  <math|g*z> for the action on <math|z\<in\><math-ss|V>> and equip
  <math|<math-ss|V>> with the Hermitian form

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<langle\>|z,w|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<ast\>>=<big|sum><rsub|g\<in\>\<Gamma\>><big|sum><rsub|a><wide|<around|(|g*z|)><rsub|a>|\<bar\>><around|(|g*w|)><rsub|a>.
  </equation*>

  It is positive definite because the identity term occurs. Reindexing the
  sum proves invariance under <math|\<Gamma\>>; conjugation is antiunitary
  because it commutes with this action. The free equations are real and
  signed permutation equivariant; their vanishing subspace <math|K> is
  therefore stable under these symmetries. Each quotient class has a unique
  representative in <math|K<rsup|\<perp\>>>, and taking that representative
  is a linear section intertwining the signed action and conjugation. Applied
  componentwise, it therefore maps <math|H> into <math|E> and satisfies
  <math|f*U=<math-up|id><rsub|H>>. These formulas use the same selected
  history and the same index <math|i>; the symbols <math|n<rsub|i>> and
  <math|b<rsub|n>> are as above (with <math|b<rsub|n>> denoting the arch
  scale). The following identities hold on a common tail. The first was just
  proved; the second is a terminal projection hypothesis, and the third
  follows from the chart symmetries as shown below:

  <\equation>
    f*U=<math-up|id><rsub|H>,<space|2em>P*Y<rsub|i>=X<rsub|i>,<space|2em><math-up|core><space|0.17em>M<rsub|i>*Y<rsub|i>*h<rsub|\<alpha\>>=M<rsub|i>*Y<rsub|i>*h<rsub|\<alpha\>>.<label|eq:trace-sensor-chart>
  </equation>

  The last equality holds simultaneously for every <math|h\<in\>H> and every
  component <math|\<alpha\>>. On the same tail, <math|Y<rsub|i>> and
  <math|M<rsub|i>> intertwine signed permutations and conjugation. Thus
  <math|M<rsub|i>*Y<rsub|i>> carries real covariant arrays to real covariant
  arrays. Inversion of all four axes acts by <math|-1> on each lifted
  component and fixes the unit coefficient, so that coefficient vanishes; the
  core deletes only that coefficient. This proves the last identity in
  <eqref|eq:trace-sensor-chart>. The scalar
  <math|c<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*t<rsub|i><rsup|-2>> is real, so the second formula
  in <eqref|eq:trace-sensor-selected-formulas> also defines a real-linear map
  <math|u<rsub|i>:H\<to\>E>. The chart identities imply, on this same tail,

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|s<rsub|i>*u<rsub|i>*h|)><rsub|\<alpha\>>>|<cell|=X<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*P*<space|-0.17em><around*|(|c<rsub|i>*t<rsub|i><rsup|2>*M<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*<around|(|c<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*t<rsub|i><rsup|-2><math-up|core><space|0.17em>M<rsub|i>*Y<rsub|i>*h<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=X<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*P*<around|(|Y<rsub|i>*h<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>=h<rsub|\<alpha\>>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Thus <math|s<rsub|i>*u<rsub|i>=<math-up|id><rsub|H>> eventually, uniformly
  over <math|h>. The construction of the chart and its symmetry and terminal
  projection properties are assumed here, not consequences of the limiting
  rank alone.

  We also record the quantitative inputs behind convergence of the selected
  right inverses. For any level <math|j>, write
  <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|j>\<gtr\>0> for its threshold,
  <math|M<around|(|j|)>> for its arch map, and
  <math|W<rsub|j>=\<vartheta\><rsub|j><rsup|-2><math-up|core><space|0.17em>M<around|(|j|)>>;
  thus <math|M<rsub|i>=M<around|(|n<rsub|i>|)>> and
  <math|t<rsub|i>=\<vartheta\><rsub|n<rsub|i>>>. Let
  <math|<around|\||\<cdummy\>|\|><rsub|1>> be the sum of absolute values of
  the finite coefficient coordinates, and set

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|i>=b<rsub|k<rsub|i>>*r<rsub|i><rsup|5>,<space|1em>\<eta\><rsub|i>=exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|{|-<tfrac|1|2><around|(|log log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|2>|}>,<space|1em>\<delta\><rsub|i>=exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|{|-<tfrac|2|5><around|(|log log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|2>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Both <math|\<eta\><rsub|i>> and <math|\<delta\><rsub|i>> tend to zero. For
  fixed <math|h\<in\>H> and component <math|\<alpha\>>, write
  <math|y<rsub|i>=Y<rsub|i>*h<rsub|\<alpha\>>> and
  <math|p=<around|(|U*h|)><rsub|\<alpha\>>>. The next two estimates follow
  from the finite-volume inputs listed in (S1)\U(S6) below; they are derived
  consequences, not new sensor hypotheses.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Initial-level price><label|lem:trace-sensor-initial-price> Under
    those inputs, for each fixed <math|h> and <math|\<alpha\>>, eventually

    <\equation>
      <around|\||W<rsub|k<rsub|i>>*y<rsub|i>-D<rsub|i>*p|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*\<eta\><rsub|i>*D<rsub|i><around|\||p|\|><rsub|1>.<label|eq:trace-sensor-initial-price>
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The finite-volume indices are pairs <math|\<omega\>=<around|(|N,\<nu\>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsup|2>>,
    where <math|N> is the mesh cutoff index and <math|\<nu\>> the period
    index. Fix the selected ultrafilter <math|\<cal-L\>> refining the fused
    order of these pairs. Write <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|<math-up|fuse>>> for its
    fused-order filter; the refinement is
    <math|\<cal-L\>\<leqslant\>\<cal-F\><rsub|<math-up|fuse>>> in the filter
    order; it contains both coordinate tails, which are the consequences used
    below. The initial-level estimate follows from a finite-volume estimate
    on this ultrafilter. For each sufficiently large <math|i>, fixed <math|h>
    and component <math|\<alpha\>>, use the same <math|y<rsub|i>> and
    <math|p> as above. Suppose finite-volume coefficients
    <math|Z<rsub|i><around|(|\<omega\>|)>> and normalizations
    <math|B<rsub|i><around|(|\<omega\>|)>>, positive on an ultrafilter-large
    set, satisfy

    <\equation>
      <around|\||Z<rsub|i><around|(|\<omega\>|)>-B<rsub|i><around|(|\<omega\>|)>*p|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*\<eta\><rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><around|(|\<omega\>|)><around|\||p|\|><rsub|1><space|1em><text|on
      an ultrafilter-large set>,<label|eq:trace-sensor-finite-price>
    </equation>

    and converge along that ultrafilter to <math|W<rsub|k<rsub|i>>*y<rsub|i>>
    and <math|D<rsub|i>>, respectively. These limits have a common source.
    Let <math|<math-up|Col><rsub|j><around|(|\<omega\>|)>> be the
    finite-volume transport at level <math|j>. Assume that for each fixed
    admissible <math|j>, whenever <math|v<around|(|\<omega\>|)>\<to\>v> along
    the selected ultrafilter, <math|<math-up|Col><rsub|j><around|(|\<omega\>|)>*v<around|(|\<omega\>|)>\<to\>M<around|(|j|)>*v>;
    also assume the finite-volume running parameter at level <math|j> tends
    to <math|b<rsub|j>>. Write <math|\<beta\><rsub|i><around|(|\<omega\>|)>>
    for that parameter at <math|k<rsub|i>>. The selected finite-volume lift
    satisfies <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i><around|(|\<omega\>|)>\<to\>y<rsub|i>>.
    The definitions give

    <\equation*>
      Z<rsub|i><around|(|\<omega\>|)>=\<vartheta\><rsub|k<rsub|i>><rsup|-2><math-up|coreCol><rsub|k<rsub|i>><around|(|\<omega\>|)>*\<lambda\><rsub|i><around|(|\<omega\>|)>,<space|2em>B<rsub|i><around|(|\<omega\>|)>=\<beta\><rsub|i><around|(|\<omega\>|)>*r<rsub|i><rsup|5>.
    </equation*>

    The two stated limits follow by continuity of <math|<math-up|core>>, with
    <math|i> fixed. The joint transport and lift convergence remain
    hypotheses; the finite-volume price follows from the tested-map estimates
    below. Continuity of the coefficient norm and the closedness of the
    inequality give <eqref|eq:trace-sensor-initial-price>. The ultrafilter
    limit is taken for each fixed <math|i> before the limit
    <math|i\<to\>\<infty\>>.

    The finite-volume price has a useful decomposition into its tested-map
    and calibration inputs. Fix <math|i> and
    <math|\<omega\>=<around|(|N,\<nu\>|)>> with <math|N\<gtr\>k<rsub|i>>;
    suppress both indices, and let <math|<math-ss|A>> and <math|<math-ss|T>>
    be the transported reference map and its tested approximation. They are
    complex-linear endomorphisms <math|<math-ss|A>,<math-ss|T>:<math-ss|V>\<to\><math-ss|V>>
    of the finite complex coefficient space and send the reference unit
    <math|\<b-1\>> to the unit coordinate vector <math|e<rsub|0>>. Assume
    that <math|<math-ss|A>> is injective and, for every input <math|v>,

    <\equation>
      <around|\||<math-ss|A>v-<math-ss|T>v|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2><around|\||<math-ss|A>v|\|><rsub|1>,<space|2em><around|\||<math-ss|A>v-<math-ss|T>v|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<eta\><rsub|i><around|\||<math-ss|A>v|\|><rsub|1>.<label|eq:trace-sensor-tested-price>
    </equation>

    These are separate tested-map and relative-price estimates. The first
    also shows that <math|<math-ss|T>> is injective: if
    <math|<math-ss|T>v=0>, then <math|<around|\||<math-ss|A>v|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2><around|\||<math-ss|A>v|\|><rsub|1>>,
    so <math|<math-ss|A>v=0> and <math|v=0>. Since <math|<math-ss|V>> is
    finite-dimensional, <math|<math-ss|T>> is invertible. For a given
    <math|v>, put <math|c=<around|(|<math-ss|T>v|)><rsub|0>\<in\>\<bbb-C\>>
    and <math|v<rsup|\<circ\>>=v-c*\<b-1\>>. Then
    <math|<math-ss|T>v<rsup|\<circ\>>=<math-up|core><math-ss|T>v> and
    <math|<math-up|core><math-ss|A>v<rsup|\<circ\>>=<math-up|core><math-ss|A>v>.
    The first bound in <eqref|eq:trace-sensor-tested-price> and the triangle
    inequality give <math|<around|\||<math-ss|A>v<rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2<around|\||<math-ss|T>v<rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>>.
    Applying <math|<math-up|core>> to the second bound, and using its
    nonexpansiveness, yields

    <\equation>
      <around|\||<math-up|core><math-ss|A>v-<math-up|core><math-ss|T>v|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*\<eta\><rsub|i><around|\||<math-up|core><math-ss|T>v|\|><rsub|1>.<label|eq:trace-sensor-centered-price>
    </equation>

    For the particular selected lift <math|v=\<lambda\><rsub|i><around|(|\<omega\>|)>>,
    its definition identifies the tested image and finite-volume
    normalization. Write <math|d<rsub|k<rsub|i>>\<gtr\>0> for the lattice
    spacing at <math|k<rsub|i>>, put <math|\<sigma\><rsub|i>=r<rsub|i>/d<rsub|k<rsub|i>>>,
    and let <math|\<beta\><rsub|i><around|(|\<omega\>|)>\<gtr\>0> be the
    finite-volume running parameter. The transported reference map is
    <math|<math-ss|A>=d<rsub|k<rsub|i>><rsup|-5><math-up|Col><rsub|k<rsub|i>><around|(|\<omega\>|)>>,
    which explains the normalization of <math|Z<rsub|i>> below. The vector
    covariance of <math|h> makes each <math|h<rsub|\<alpha\>>> odd under
    total inversion; the reference lift intertwines that action, so
    <math|p=<around|(|U*h|)><rsub|\<alpha\>>> is odd as well. We recall the
    short degree argument for the reference frame. Its basis elements are
    homogeneous in position degree and factor degree, and the only
    factor-degree-zero basis element is the unit. Each jet factor has
    position weight at least two; since the frame has degree at most five, a
    nonunit basis element has at most two factors. A one-factor invariant
    vanishes by the invariant-linear-form argument in the low-grade label
    calculation of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-free-rank>.
    Thus every nonunit basis element has exactly two factors and position
    degree four or five. Total inversion acts on a homogeneous
    position-degree-<math|d> label by <math|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|d>>: a jet
    of order <math|r> has both inversion sign
    <math|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|r+2>> and position weight <math|r+2>.
    Consequently every nonzero nonunit coordinate of the odd vector <math|p>
    has position degree five and factor degree two. The identities used here
    are

    <\equation>
      <math-up|core><math-ss|T>\<lambda\><rsub|i>=\<beta\><rsub|i>*\<vartheta\><rsub|k<rsub|i>><rsup|2>*\<sigma\><rsub|i><rsup|5>*p,<space|2em>Z<rsub|i>=\<vartheta\><rsub|k<rsub|i>><rsup|-2>*d<rsub|k<rsub|i>><rsup|5><math-up|core><math-ss|A>\<lambda\><rsub|i>,<space|2em>B<rsub|i>=\<beta\><rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|5>.<label|eq:trace-sensor-finite-calibration>
    </equation>

    The first identity follows from the definition of the selected
    finite-volume lift and the grade-five scaling rule. Put
    <math|\<xi\>=<around|(|<sqrt|\<beta\><rsub|i>>|)><rsup|-1>/\<vartheta\><rsub|k<rsub|i>>>.
    For a frame coordinate <math|\<iota\>> with position degree
    <math|d<rsub|\<iota\>>> and factor degree <math|n<rsub|\<iota\>>>, define
    the position and factor dilations by

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|<math-up|Pos><rsub|t><space|0.17em>v|)><rsub|\<iota\>>=t<rsup|d<rsub|\<iota\>>>*v<rsub|\<iota\>>,<space|2em><around|(|<math-up|Dil><rsub|t><space|0.17em>v|)><rsub|\<iota\>>=t<rsup|n<rsub|\<iota\>>>*v<rsub|\<iota\>>.
    </equation*>

    For <math|t\<gtr\>0>, these coordinate formulas give
    <math|<math-up|Dil><rsub|t><rsup|-1>=<math-up|Dil><rsub|t<rsup|-1>>>. On
    the selected tail, where <math|i\<geqslant\>5>, the finite-volume lift is

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsub|i>=<math-ss|T><rsup|-1><math-up|Dil><rsub|\<xi\><rsup|-1>><space|0.17em><math-up|Pos><rsub|\<sigma\><rsub|i>><space|0.17em>p.
    </equation*>

    Consequently <math|<math-up|Dil><rsub|\<xi\>><math-ss|T>\<lambda\><rsub|i>=<math-up|Pos><rsub|\<sigma\><rsub|i>><space|0.17em>p>.
    The reference section <math|p> is odd and has nonunit degree five, so
    this weight is <math|\<sigma\><rsub|i><rsup|5>*p>. On these nonunit
    coordinates the inverse coefficient dilation
    <math|<math-up|Dil><rsub|\<xi\><rsup|-1>>> acts by
    <math|\<xi\><rsup|-2>>. Applying that inverse and <math|<math-up|core>>,
    with <math|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>p=p>, gives the first identity in
    <eqref|eq:trace-sensor-finite-calibration>, because
    <math|\<xi\><rsup|-2>=\<beta\><rsub|i>*\<vartheta\><rsub|k<rsub|i>><rsup|2>>.
    This is an exact conversion of the lift and grading normalizations. The
    other two identities specify the actual normalization. Multiplying
    <eqref|eq:trace-sensor-centered-price> by
    <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|k<rsub|i>><rsup|-2>*d<rsub|k<rsub|i>><rsup|5>>
    and using <math|r<rsub|i>=d<rsub|k<rsub|i>>*\<sigma\><rsub|i>> gives
    exactly <eqref|eq:trace-sensor-finite-price>. Thus the tested-map
    estimates, selected-lift construction and finite-volume limits are the
    remaining inputs for the initial-level price, rather than an additional
    assumed sensor bound.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Transport price><label|lem:trace-sensor-transport-price> Under
    (S1)\U(S6), for each fixed <math|h> and <math|\<alpha\>>, eventually

    <\equation>
      <around|\||W<rsub|k<rsub|i>>*y<rsub|i>-W<rsub|n<rsub|i>>*y<rsub|i>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|i>*<around|\||W<rsub|n<rsub|i>>*y<rsub|i>|\|><rsub|1>.<label|eq:trace-sensor-transport-price>
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For the one-step input, fix <math|i>, <math|h> and <math|\<alpha\>> and
    write <math|N<rsub|i>=n<rsub|i>-k<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0>. On a set belonging to
    the selected ultrafilter, suppose there are finite-volume
    coefficient-vector functions <math|z<rsub|j><around|(|\<omega\>|)>> for
    <math|k<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>n<rsub|i>>, all formed from the
    same terminal label <math|y<rsub|i>>, with

    <\equation>
      <around|\||z<rsub|j><around|(|\<omega\>|)>-z<rsub|j+1><around|(|\<omega\>|)>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><frac|\<delta\><rsub|i>|2*N<rsub|i>><around|\||z<rsub|j+1><around|(|\<omega\>|)>|\|><rsub|1>*<space|1em><around|(|k<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>j\<less\>n<rsub|i>|)>.<label|eq:trace-sensor-step-input>
    </equation>

    Here <math|z<rsub|j><around|(|\<omega\>|)>=\<vartheta\><rsub|j><rsup|-2><math-up|coreCol><rsub|j><around|(|\<omega\>|)>*y<rsub|i>>.
    For each fixed <math|j> in this finite interval, the joint transport
    input above, applied to the constant label <math|y<rsub|i>>, gives the
    ultrafilter limit <math|W<rsub|j>*y<rsub|i>>. We derive the one-step
    price below from the underlying residual and budget inputs, and now prove
    <eqref|eq:trace-sensor-transport-price> from it.

    Fix <math|\<omega\>> in that set and abbreviate
    <math|z<rsub|j>=z<rsub|j><around|(|\<omega\>|)>>. Put
    <math|a=\<delta\><rsub|i>/N<rsub|i>>. For large <math|i> we have
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1>. Backward induction gives, for
    every <math|j> in the interval,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||z<rsub|j>-z<rsub|n<rsub|i>>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>a*<around|(|n<rsub|i>-j|)><around|\||z<rsub|n<rsub|i>>|\|><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    It is immediate at <math|j=n<rsub|i>>. At the induction step, the bound
    at <math|j+1> and <math|a*N<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1> give
    <math|<around|\||z<rsub|j+1>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2<around|\||z<rsub|n<rsub|i>>|\|><rsub|1>>.
    The one-step input therefore bounds <math|<around|\||z<rsub|j>-z<rsub|j+1>|\|><rsub|1>>
    by <math|a<around|\||z<rsub|n<rsub|i>>|\|><rsub|1>>, and the triangle
    inequality proves the next step. At <math|j=k<rsub|i>> this is
    <math|<around|\||z<rsub|k<rsub|i>>-z<rsub|n<rsub|i>>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|i><around|\||z<rsub|n<rsub|i>>|\|><rsub|1>>.
    Passing to the ultrafilter limit in the finite-dimensional coefficient
    space yields <eqref|eq:trace-sensor-transport-price>. The finite
    intersection over the interval is taken before this limit, for each fixed
    <math|i>; no uniform limit in <math|i> or new choice of history is used.

    The one-step price follows from the following assumptions. Write
    <math|<wide|z|~><rsub|j>=<math-up|Col><rsub|j><around|(|\<omega\>|)>*y<rsub|i>>
    for the finite-volume coefficient before threshold normalization; thus
    <math|z<rsub|j>=\<vartheta\><rsub|j><rsup|-2><math-up|core><wide|z|~><rsub|j>>.
    Let the deterministic block transport be
    <math|<around|(|<math-up|Block><rsub|j><space|0.17em>v|)><rsub|\<iota\>>=L<rsub|j><rsup|5-d<rsub|\<iota\>>>*v<rsub|\<iota\>>>.
    For <math|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> and level <math|j>, put

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|a><around|(|j|)>=exp <space|-0.17em><around*|{|a<around|(|log
      log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|)><rsup|2>|}>,<space|2em>g<rsub|j>=<frac|2*<around|(|1+C<rsub|j+1>|)>|A<rsub|1/2>*<around|(|j+1|)>>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|C<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>0> is the selected real column budget.
    The budget input is <math|C<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>> eventually. For all levels in the interval, on
    the same ultrafilter-large set, assume

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<frac|\<vartheta\><rsub|j+1>|\<vartheta\><rsub|j>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>2,>|<cell|<around|\||<wide|z|~><rsub|j>-<math-up|Dil><rsub|\<vartheta\><rsub|j>/\<vartheta\><rsub|j+1>><space|0.17em><math-up|Block><rsub|j><wide|z|~><rsub|j+1>|\|><rsub|1>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>g<rsub|j><around|\||<math-up|core><wide|z|~><rsub|j+1>|\|><rsub|1>.<eq-number><label|eq:trace-sensor-raw-step>>>>>
    </align>

    In the selected construction, for each fixed level <math|j> the residual
    and threshold bounds hold for all sufficiently large <math|\<nu\>>,
    uniformly in <math|N\<gtr\>j> and the terminal label. For fixed <math|i>,
    take the maximum of the finitely many period thresholds for
    <math|k<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>j\<less\>n<rsub|i>> and also require
    <math|N\<geqslant\>n<rsub|i>>; their intersection belongs to the same
    ultrafilter. This justifies using one large set for the entire finite
    interval, without a uniform choice over <math|i>.

    The terminal label <math|y<rsub|i>> is odd under inversion of all axes,
    and the finite-volume transport preserves that inversion; hence every
    <math|<wide|z|~><rsub|j>> is odd. The reference-frame classification used
    above therefore shows that a nonzero nonunit coordinate of
    <math|<wide|z|~><rsub|j+1>> has position degree five and factor degree
    two. On a coordinate of position degree <math|d>, block transport
    multiplies by <math|L<rsub|j><rsup|5-d>>; on one of factor degree
    <math|n>, coefficient dilation by <math|t> multiplies by
    <math|t<rsup|n>>. Thus, on every nonunit coordinate of
    <math|<wide|z|~><rsub|j+1>>, the block factor is one and the dilation
    factor is <math|<around|(|\<vartheta\><rsub|j>/\<vartheta\><rsub|j+1>|)><rsup|2>>.
    The core deletes the unit coordinate, so

    <\equation>
      <math-up|coreDil><rsub|\<vartheta\><rsub|j>/\<vartheta\><rsub|j+1>><space|0.17em><math-up|Block><rsub|j><wide|z|~><rsub|j+1>=<around*|(|<frac|\<vartheta\><rsub|j>|\<vartheta\><rsub|j+1>>|)><rsup|2><math-up|core><wide|z|~><rsub|j+1>.<label|eq:trace-sensor-grade-scaling>
    </equation>

    The core projection is nonexpansive in
    <math|<around|\||\<cdummy\>|\|><rsub|1>>. Applying <math|<math-up|core>>
    to the residual and dividing by <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|j><rsup|2>> gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||z<rsub|j>-z<rsub|j+1>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|j><around*|(|<frac|\<vartheta\><rsub|j+1>|\<vartheta\><rsub|j>>|)><rsup|2><around|\||z<rsub|j+1>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>4*g<rsub|j><around|\||z<rsub|j+1>|\|><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    We verify that <math|4*g<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|i>/<around|(|2*N<rsub|i>|)>>
    uniformly for <math|k<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>j\<less\>n<rsub|i>> when
    <math|i> is large. Put <math|\<chi\>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|i>>>.
    The clock estimates already proved imply
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n<rsub|i>>\<leqslant\>2*\<chi\>> eventually.
    Since each increment <math|log L<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1>, the overshoot
    bound gives <math|N<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>2<around|(|log
    \<chi\>|)><rsup|48>>. For large <math|i> we have
    <math|\<chi\>\<geqslant\>e>, so <math|t\<mapsto\>exp
    <around|{|<tfrac|1|2><around|(|log log t|)><rsup|2>|}>> is increasing for
    <math|t\<geqslant\>\<chi\>>. Monotonicity of the clock then gives
    <math|C<rsub|j+1>\<leqslant\>2*log \<chi\>> and
    <math|A<rsub|1/2>*<around|(|j+1|)>\<geqslant\>A<rsub|1/2><around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>>
    for large <math|i>. Therefore

    <\equation*>
      2*N<rsub|i>*<around|(|4*g<rsub|j>|)>\<leqslant\><frac|32<around|(|log
      \<chi\>|)><rsup|48>*<around|(|1+2*log
      \<chi\>|)>|A<rsub|1/2><around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|-2/5><around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>=\<delta\><rsub|i>.
    </equation*>

    The last inequality follows because every fixed power of <math|log
    \<chi\>> is eventually bounded by <math|A<rsub|1/10><around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>>:
    after taking logarithms this is linear versus quadratic growth in
    <math|log log \<chi\>>. This proves <eqref|eq:trace-sensor-step-input>.
    The residual, preservation of oddness, threshold and column-budget bounds
    remain hypotheses; the degree classification, grade-scaling identity,
    price calculation and telescoping above prove their consequence.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Convergence of the selected lifts><label|lem:trace-sensor-lift-limit>
    Under (S1)\U(S6), <math|u<rsub|i>\<to\>U> in operator norm.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The preceding estimates prove <math|u<rsub|i>*h\<to\>U*h>. Indeed put
    <math|<math-ss|L><rsub|i>=D<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*W<rsub|k<rsub|i>>*y<rsub|i>>
    and <math|<math-ss|R><rsub|i>=D<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*W<rsub|n<rsub|i>>*y<rsub|i>>.
    The first estimate gives <math|<around|\||<math-ss|L><rsub|i>-p|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*\<eta\><rsub|i><around|\||p|\|><rsub|1>>,
    and the second gives <math|<around|\||<math-ss|L><rsub|i>-<math-ss|R><rsub|i>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|i><around|\||<math-ss|R><rsub|i>|\|><rsub|1>>.
    For <math|\<delta\><rsub|i>\<less\>1>, the triangle inequality gives
    <math|<around|\||<math-ss|R><rsub|i>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+2*\<eta\><rsub|i>|)>*<around|(|1-\<delta\><rsub|i>|)><rsup|-1><around|\||p|\|><rsub|1>>
    and hence

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||<math-ss|R><rsub|i>-p|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><frac|2*\<eta\><rsub|i>+\<delta\><rsub|i>|1-\<delta\><rsub|i>><around|\||p|\|><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|c<rsub|i>=b<rsub|n<rsub|i>>*r<rsub|i><rsup|5>>, the formula
    for <math|u<rsub|i>> gives <math|<around|(|u<rsub|i>*h|)><rsub|\<alpha\>>=<around|(|b<rsub|k<rsub|i>>/b<rsub|n<rsub|i>>|)><math-ss|R><rsub|i>>.
    Thus the component error is at most

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|<around*|\||<frac|b<rsub|k<rsub|i>>|b<rsub|n<rsub|i>>>-1|\|>+<frac|b<rsub|k<rsub|i>>|b<rsub|n<rsub|i>>>*<frac|2*\<eta\><rsub|i>+\<delta\><rsub|i>|1-\<delta\><rsub|i>>|)><around|\||p|\|><rsub|1>,
    </equation*>

    which tends to zero. There are finitely many components, so
    <math|u<rsub|i>*h\<to\>U*h> in <math|E>; finite dimensionality upgrades
    this pointwise convergence to operator-norm convergence.
  </proof>

  We collect the inputs used in the preceding lemmas and the proposition.
  They concern one selected family, one chart and one ultrafilter throughout;
  none is a new assumption of a sensor estimate.

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(S1)>The selected levels <math|k<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>> and the
    schedule has <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+\<gamma\>*\<ell\><rsub|j>\<gtr\>1>,
    with <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>,\<gamma\>\<gtr\>0> and
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>\<to\>\<infty\>>. Its increments satisfy
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|j+1>-\<ell\><rsub|j>=log L<rsub|j>>, with
    <math|L<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>3> and eventually
    <math|L<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>>. The arch
    scales <math|b<rsub|j>> are positive and, for every
    <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, satisfy
    <math|<around|\||b<rsub|j>-<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>*\<ell\><rsub|j>>
    eventually in <math|j>. The postselected level
    <math|n<rsub|i>=q<around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>\<gtr\>k<rsub|i>> is the first
    level at logarithmic separation at least <math|<around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|48>>, and the weight is
    <eqref|eq:trace-sensor-weight>.

    <item*|(S2)>The finite-dimensional real spaces <math|E,H>, projection
    <math|f>, nonzero <math|x>, <math|ker f=\<bbb-R\>*x> and Ward form
    <math|\<rho\>> are the free data fixed above. The chart maps
    <math|s<rsub|i>>, <math|X<rsub|i>>, <math|Y<rsub|i>>, <math|M<rsub|i>>
    and positive scales use the same selected family and satisfy
    <eqref|eq:trace-sensor-selected-formulas> and
    <math|P*Y<rsub|i>=X<rsub|i>> on a common eventual tail for every field.
    The chart maps intertwine the signed permutations and conjugation there;
    the reference section <math|U> has <math|f*U=<math-up|id><rsub|H>>.

    <item*|(S3)>The selected ultrafilter <math|\<cal-L\>> refines the fused
    order. For each fixed admissible <math|j>,
    <math|<math-up|Col><rsub|j><around|(|\<omega\>|)>*v<around|(|\<omega\>|)>\<to\>M<around|(|j|)>*v>
    whenever <math|v<around|(|\<omega\>|)>\<to\>v> along <math|\<cal-L\>>;
    the finite running parameter tends to <math|b<rsub|j>>. For each fixed
    sufficiently large <math|i> and each field component, the selected
    finite-volume lift <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i><around|(|\<omega\>|)>>
    converges to <math|Y<rsub|i>*h<rsub|\<alpha\>>> along this same
    ultrafilter. All these volume limits are taken before
    <math|i\<to\>\<infty\>>.

    <item*|(S4)>On the selected tail, for every period and every
    <math|N\<gtr\>k<rsub|i>>, the complex-linear endomorphisms
    <math|<math-ss|A>,<math-ss|T>> of <math|<math-ss|V>> send the reference
    unit to <math|e<rsub|0>>, <math|<math-ss|A>> is injective, and the two
    bounds <eqref|eq:trace-sensor-tested-price> hold for every coefficient
    vector. For each fixed <math|h> and component, the selected lift is

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsub|i>=<math-ss|T><rsup|-1><math-up|Dil><rsub|\<xi\><rsup|-1>><space|0.17em><math-up|Pos><rsub|\<sigma\><rsub|i>><around|(|U*h|)><rsub|\<alpha\>>,<space|2em>\<xi\>=<around|(|<sqrt|\<beta\><rsub|i>>|)><rsup|-1>/\<vartheta\><rsub|k<rsub|i>>.
    </equation*>

    Further, <math|<math-ss|A>=d<rsub|k<rsub|i>><rsup|-5><math-up|Col><rsub|k<rsub|i>>>,
    with positive <math|\<beta\><rsub|i>>, <math|d<rsub|k<rsub|i>>>,
    <math|r<rsub|i>>, and <math|\<sigma\><rsub|i>=r<rsub|i>/d<rsub|k<rsub|i>>>.
    The calibration identities <eqref|eq:trace-sensor-finite-calibration> are
    derived from these data and the grading, as shown in the proof.

    <item*|(S5)>For each fixed level <math|j\<geqslant\>j<rsub|0>>,
    eventually in the period index <math|\<nu\>>, uniformly for every
    <math|N\<gtr\>j> and every terminal coefficient label, the threshold
    ratio and residual bounds <eqref|eq:trace-sensor-raw-step> hold with
    <math|<wide|z|~><rsub|j>=<math-up|Col><rsub|j><around|(|\<omega\>|)>*v>
    for that label <math|v>. For each fixed <math|i> their finite
    intersection over <math|k<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>j\<less\>n<rsub|i>>,
    together with <math|N\<geqslant\>n<rsub|i>>, is taken along the same
    fused-order ultrafilter; no tail uniform in <math|i> is assumed.

    <item*|(S6)>The selected column budgets obey
    <math|C<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>> eventually. The
    reference lift is odd under total inversion and the finite-volume
    transport preserves oddness; the grade-five frame and block/dilation
    rules are those used above. These inputs purchase the transport estimate,
    rather than assuming it.

    <item*|(S7)>The marked sequence <math|v<rsub|i>\<in\>E> has the three
    limits in <eqref|eq:trace-sensor-marked> below. It is arbitrary subject
    to those limits; it need not be the later medium example.
  </enumerate>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Selected trace sensor><label|prop:trace-prosper-sensor>Assume
    (S1)\U(S6). By Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:trace-sensor-arch-ratio>,
    <reference|lem:trace-sensor-initial-price>,
    <reference|lem:trace-sensor-transport-price> and
    <reference|lem:trace-sensor-lift-limit>, the stated chart has an eventual
    right inverse and <math|u<rsub|i>\<to\>U> in operator norm. Suppose a
    sequence <math|v<rsub|i>\<in\>E> satisfies, in the indicated norms,

    <\equation>
      v<rsub|i>\<longrightarrow\>x,<space|2em>\<frak-a\><rsub|i>*s<rsub|i><around|(|v<rsub|i>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0<text|in
      >H,<space|2em>\<frak-a\><rsub|i>*\<rho\><around|(|v<rsub|i>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0<text|in
      >\<bbb-R\>.<label|eq:trace-sensor-marked>
    </equation>

    Then <math|s<rsub|i>\<to\>f> in operator norm. Moreover there are linear
    functionals <math|\<mu\><rsub|i>:H\<to\>\<bbb-R\>> and
    <math|C\<geqslant\>0> such that, for all sufficiently large <math|i>,
    <math|<around|\||\<mu\><rsub|i><around|(|h|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<around|\<\|\|\>|h|\<\|\|\>>>
    for every <math|h\<in\>H>, and for every <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>,
    for all sufficiently large <math|i>,

    <\equation>
      <around|\||\<frak-a\><rsub|i>*<around|(|\<mu\><rsub|i>*<around|(|s<rsub|i>*y|)>-\<rho\><around|(|y|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><space|1em><text|for
      every >y\<in\>E.<label|eq:trace-sensor-conclusion>
    </equation>

    The tail in the second assertion may depend on <math|\<varepsilon\>>, but
    is uniform in <math|y>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Since <math|\<frak-a\><rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> and
    <math|\<frak-a\><rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>, the first weighted limit in
    <eqref|eq:trace-sensor-marked> implies
    <math|s<rsub|i><around|(|v<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>0>. Choose a linear functional
    <math|A:E\<to\>\<bbb-R\>> for which <math|y=U*f<around|(|y|)>+A<around|(|y|)>*x>
    for every <math|y>: indeed <math|y-U*f<around|(|y|)>> lies in <math|ker
    f>, and its coefficient along the nonzero <math|x> is linear. Define
    <math|T<rsub|i>=u<rsub|i>*f+A\<otimes\>v<rsub|i>>. The chart and
    marked-vector limits imply <math|T<rsub|i>\<to\><math-up|id><rsub|E>> in
    operator norm. For sufficiently large <math|i>, <math|T<rsub|i>> is
    invertible and <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|-1>\<to\><math-up|id><rsub|E>>, by
    the geometric series for <math|<math-up|id><rsub|E>-T<rsub|i>>.

    On the common right-inverse tail,

    <\equation*>
      s<rsub|i>*T<rsub|i>*y=f<around|(|y|)>+A<around|(|y|)>*s<rsub|i><around|(|v<rsub|i>|)>.
    </equation*>

    This also gives a bound without assuming the norms of <math|s<rsub|i>> in
    advance. Put <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|i>=<around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsub|i>-<math-up|id><rsub|E>|\<\|\|\>>>;
    for <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|i>\<less\>1>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|s<rsub|i>-f|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><frac|<around|\<\|\|\>|A|\<\|\|\>><space|0.17em>\<\|\|\>s<rsub|i><around|(|v<rsub|i>|)>\<\|\|\>+<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>>*\<epsilon\><rsub|i>|1-\<epsilon\><rsub|i>>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed subtract <math|f*T<rsub|i>> from the displayed identity and
    multiply on the right by <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|-1>>. Both terms in the
    numerator tend to zero, proving <math|s<rsub|i>\<to\>f>. Now apply the
    weighted rank-one receiver lemma (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-trace-weighted-price>)
    with <math|<around|(|v<rsub|i>,u<rsub|i>,w<rsub|i>,e<rsub|i>|)>> there
    equal to <math|<around|(|s<rsub|i>,u<rsub|i>,v<rsub|i>,\<frak-a\><rsub|i>|)>>
    here. Its two weighted hypotheses are precisely the last two limits in
    <eqref|eq:trace-sensor-marked>; its other hypotheses follow from the
    preceding paragraph and <eqref|eq:trace-sensor-chart>. It gives
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|i>=\<rho\>*u<rsub|i>> with bounded norms and
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<frak-a\><rsub|i>*<around|(|\<mu\><rsub|i>*s<rsub|i>-\<rho\>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|E<rsup|\<ast\>>>\<to\>0>.
    The definition of operator norm then gives
    <eqref|eq:trace-sensor-conclusion>, with one tail uniform in <math|y>.
  </proof>

  The marked-vector assumptions are separate analytic inputs. In the selected
  construction let <math|<math-ss|q><rsub|j>> and <math|<math-ss|w><rsub|j>>
  be the transported chosen and reference responses, and set
  <math|m<rsub|j>=b<rsub|j><rsup|-1><math-ss|q><rsub|j>-<math-ss|w><rsub|j>>.
  The scalar assembly in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-weighted-estimates>,
  applied at the postselected levels and under its stated response, price and
  scale inputs, gives <math|m<rsub|n<rsub|i>>\<to\>x> and
  <math|\<frak-a\><rsub|i>*\<rho\><around|(|m<rsub|n<rsub|i>>|)>\<to\>0>. In
  particular, the scale comparison above and
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n<rsub|i>>\<to\>\<infty\>> give
  <math|b<rsub|n<rsub|i>>\<geqslant\>1> after discarding finitely many terms;
  the cited scalar calculation supplies <math|\<frak-a\><rsub|i>/b<rsub|n<rsub|i>>\<to\>0>.
  The distinct priced-mission argument in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-trace-small-means> gives the stronger field
  estimate <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n<rsub|i>>*s<rsub|i><around|(|m<rsub|n<rsub|i>>|)>\<to\>0>,
  under its stated medium-core representation, oddness, response and scaling
  identities, detector and local-price bounds, and scalar rate input. For
  large <math|t>, <math|<tfrac|1|5><around|(|log log
  t|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>log t>, so <math|A<rsub|1/5><around|(|n<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n<rsub|i>>>
  eventually. Therefore

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|\<frak-a\><rsub|i>*s<rsub|i><around|(|m<rsub|n<rsub|i>>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n<rsub|i>>*s<rsub|i><around|(|m<rsub|n<rsub|i>>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  Thus the same selected medium supplies all three marked-vector limits in
  the proposition, subject to those two separate groups of inputs. For the
  selected construction, Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-scribe-producer>
  identifies the chart, the tested finite-volume readout and the probe
  estimates on this same history and applies the proposition.

  <subsection|Branch moments and trace washout><label|sec:ex-trace-branch-moments>

  Fix a closed subgroup of a finite-dimensional unitary group whose real Lie
  algebra is nonabelian and simple; the group itself may be disconnected.
  Work on one selected history and with the same three tensor families and
  chart as in the physical stress construction. All spaces below are finite
  dimensional. The four coordinate axes form the set
  <math|I=<around|{|0,1,2,3|}>>. Let <math|E<rsub|4>> be the complex space of
  grade-four labels, let <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|int>>> be the
  grade-four field space of the selected interacting table, and let
  <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|4><rsup|0>> be that of the standard table. The maps
  are complex linear unless stated otherwise. Write
  <math|\<pi\>:E<rsub|4>\<to\>\<cal-F\><rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|int>>> and
  <math|\<pi\><rsub|0>:E<rsub|4>\<to\>\<cal-F\><rsub|4><rsup|0>> for the
  projections, <math|\<frak-c\>> and <math|\<frak-c\><rsub|0>> for centering,
  and <math|\<b-1\><rsub|4>,\<b-1\><rsub|4><rsup|0>> for the corresponding
  unit fields. Thus <math|\<frak-c\>*f=f-<around|\<langle\>|f|\<rangle\>>*\<b-1\><rsub|4>>.
  The centering map is idempotent and its range is the space of centered
  fields, using the table unit law <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<b-1\><rsub|4>|\<rangle\>>=1>;
  the standard table has the same unit law.

  The branch set is <math|<around|{|0,-,+|}>>: <math|0> is the principal
  branch, <math|-> is the false correction and <math|+> is the true
  correction. In the underlying finite-volume construction these are
  respectively the selected principal field and the two fixed correction
  fields. To specify the choice, let <math|J<rsub|a*c>,Q<rsub|d>,H<rsub|a*c*d>>
  be the selected tensor, trace-current and antisymmetry fields at a finite
  volume. Let a superscript <math|<around|[|r|]>> denote the same finite
  localization of each field and let <math|\<nabla\><rsub|d>> be the forward
  lattice difference. At localization radius <math|r> the principal field is

  <\equation*>
    J<rsup|0><rsub|a*c>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|d>\<nabla\><rsub|d>*Q<rsub|d><rsup|<around|[|r|]>>-<big|sum><rsub|j\<ne\>0>J<rsub|j*j><rsup|<around|[|r|]>>,>|<cell|a=c=0,>>|<row|<cell|J<rsub|c*a><rsup|<around|[|r|]>>-<big|sum><rsub|d>\<nabla\><rsub|d>*H<rsub|c*a*d><rsup|<around|[|r|]>>,>|<cell|c\<less\>a,>>|<row|<cell|J<rsub|a*c><rsup|<around|[|r|]>>,>|<cell|<text|otherwise>.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  At scale <math|k> the principal field's common localization radius is
  <math|\<varrho\><rsub|k>=1000<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>, where
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>> is the positive clock introduced below;
  here <math|r=\<varrho\><rsub|k>>. In particular
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>J<rsup|0><rsub|a*a>=<big|sum><rsub|d>\<nabla\><rsub|d>*Q<rsub|d><rsup|<around|[|r|]>>>
  exactly. For the two corrections, let <math|B<rsub|a*d,c*d>> be the real
  inner product of the two lattice curvatures with the common index <math|d>,
  evaluated at the same lattice site. Their components are

  <\equation*>
    J<rsup|-><rsub|a*c>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|0,>|<cell|a=c,>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|d>B<rsub|a*d,c*d>,>|<cell|a\<ne\>c,>>>>><space|2em>J<rsup|+><rsub|a*c>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<frac|1|4>*<big|sum><rsub|j,d>B<rsub|j*d,j*d>,>|<cell|a=c,>>|<row|<cell|0,>|<cell|a\<ne\>c.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  These formulas are translated from the origin to the relevant lattice site.
  The three branches use the same extraction ultrafilter, finite-volume
  choices and scale history, with no new subsequence; their recorded labels
  may differ. At scale <math|k>, let <math|x<rsup|b><rsub|k>*<around|(|a,c|)>\<in\>E<rsub|4>>
  be the recorded label of <math|J<rsup|b><rsub|a*c>>, with
  <math|a,c\<in\>I>. Let <math|<math-ss|R>> be the real-linear finite
  signed-symmetry average on arrays of labels and set

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|sym><around|(|x|)><rsub|a*c>=<tfrac|1|2><around|(|x<rsub|a*c>+x<rsub|c*a>|)>,<space|2em>t<rsup|b><rsub|k>=\<frak-c\>*<space|0.17em>\<pi\><math-ss|R><math-up|sym><around|(|x<rsup|b><rsub|k>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The notation means componentwise application of <math|\<pi\>> and
  <math|\<frak-c\>>. The symmetry average permutes the axes and applies the
  corresponding label map, with the two tensor signs; it includes the
  real-structure average. Its normalized weights sum to one. The table
  projection intertwines these label motions with the corresponding
  real-linear field motions, and those field motions are fixed, bounded maps.
  In particular, for a diagonal trace, reindexing the axes cancels the
  squared signs. Put <math|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>t=<big|sum><rsub|a\<in\>I>t<rsub|a*a>>.

  For a triple <math|w=<around|(|w<rsub|1>,w<rsub|2>,w<rsub|3>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|3>>,
  define the label-valued material tensor by

  <\equation>
    \<cal-S\><around|(|w|)><rsub|a*c>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|w<rsub|1>*<big|sum><rsub|j>P<rsub|a*j,a*j>+w<rsub|2>*<big|sum><rsub|i,j>P<rsub|i*j,i*j>,>|<cell|a=c,>>|<row|<cell|w<rsub|3>*<big|sum><rsub|j>P<rsub|a*j,c*j>,>|<cell|a\<ne\>c,>>>>><space|2em>M<around|(|w|)><rsub|a*c>=\<pi\><rsub|0>*\<cal-S\><around|(|w|)><rsub|a*c>.<label|eq:ex-branch-material>
  </equation>

  Here <math|P<rsub|i*j,k*l>\<in\>E<rsub|4>> is the coordinate representative
  of the label whose value on a Taylor jet is the real color inner product
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|i*j>,F<rsub|k*l>|\<rangle\>>>; no
  additional factor is included. The axis sums are ordered. There is no
  scalar-unit term in <eqref|eq:ex-branch-material>; the full four-component
  coefficient is <math|<around|(|0,w<rsub|1>,w<rsub|2>,w<rsub|3>|)>>. The map
  <math|w\<mapsto\>\<cal-S\><around|(|w|)>> is injective. To see this, let
  <math|v> be a nonzero real color vector. On a jet with only
  <math|F<rsub|01>=v> and <math|F<rsub|10>=-v>, the <math|<around|(|2,2|)>>
  and <math|<around|(|0,0|)>> entries are respectively
  <math|2*w<rsub|2>*<around|\<langle\>|v,v|\<rangle\>>> and
  <math|<around|(|w<rsub|1>+2*w<rsub|2>|)>*<around|\<langle\>|v,v|\<rangle\>>>;
  if the tensor is zero, <math|w<rsub|2>=w<rsub|1>=0>. On a jet with
  <math|F<rsub|02>=F<rsub|12>=v> and their antisymmetric partners, its
  <math|<around|(|0,1|)>> entry is <math|w<rsub|3>*<around|\<langle\>|v,v|\<rangle\>>>,
  so <math|w<rsub|3>=0>. Exchange of the curvature pairs shows
  <math|\<cal-S\><around|(|w|)><rsub|a*c>=\<cal-S\><around|(|w|)><rsub|c*a>>.
  A signed permutation sends <math|F<rsub|i*j>> to
  <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|i>*\<epsilon\><rsub|j>*F<rsub|\<sigma\><around|(|i|)>*\<sigma\><around|(|j|)>>>.
  In a contracted pair the repeated sign squares to one, and reindexing the
  sums gives the two tensor signs. The diagonal terms transform likewise, and
  both the curvature-pair labels and real coefficients are fixed by the real
  structure. Thus the signed and real symmetry average fixes
  <math|\<cal-S\><around|(|w|)>>. The branch coefficients are

  <\equation>
    w<rsup|0>=<around|(|-1,<tfrac|1|4>,-1|)>,<space|2em>w<rsup|->=<around|(|0,0,1|)>,<space|2em>w<rsup|+>=<around|(|0,<tfrac|1|4>,0|)>.<label|eq:ex-branch-coefficients>
  </equation>

  Define the normalized scalar field <math|\<cal-O\>=\<pi\><rsub|0><around|(|<tfrac|1|4><big|sum><rsub|i,j>P<rsub|i*j,i*j>|)>>.
  Taking the four diagonal entries in <eqref|eq:ex-branch-material> gives,
  for every <math|w>,

  <\equation>
    <math-up|tr><space|0.17em>M<around|(|w|)>=4*<around|(|w<rsub|1>+4*w<rsub|2>|)>*\<cal-O\>.<label|eq:ex-branch-material-trace>
  </equation>

  Consequently

  <\equation>
    <math-up|tr><space|0.17em>M<around|(|w<rsup|0>|)>=<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>M<around|(|w<rsup|->|)>=0,<space|2em><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>M<around|(|w<rsup|+>|)>=4*\<cal-O\>.<label|eq:ex-branch-three-traces>
  </equation>

  We use the standard-table one-point fact that each curvature-pair label has
  expectation zero and the unit has expectation one. In the free table the
  pair labels are Wick ordered. Thus the expectation of each
  <math|M<around|(|w|)><rsub|a*c>> is zero because its unit coefficient is
  zero. Hence <math|\<frak-c\><rsub|0>*\<pi\><rsub|0>*\<cal-S\><around|(|w|)>=M<around|(|w|)>>.

  Let <math|k<rsub|i>> be the selected level, tending to infinity with the
  chart index <math|i>, and let <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>\<gtr\>0> and
  <math|a<rsub|k>\<gtr\>0> be its inverse-coupling clock and mesh. Denote by
  <math|B<rsub|k>:E<rsub|4>\<to\>E<rsub|4>> the selected transport, by
  <math|U<rsub|i>:\<cal-F\><rsub|4><rsup|0>\<to\>\<cal-F\><rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|int>>>
  the chart isomorphism, and by <math|\<omega\><rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> its
  grade-four normalization factor. Explicitly
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|i>=\<beta\><rsub|Z><around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>>,
  where <math|r<rsub|i>> is the chart radius and <math|\<beta\><rsub|Z>> is
  the associated effective coupling. More explicitly, if <math|A<rsub|k>> is
  the selected label automorphism of <math|E<rsub|4>>, <math|K> is the core
  projection and <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|k>\<gtr\>0> is the threshold, then

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|k>=\<vartheta\><rsub|k><rsup|-2>*K*A<rsub|k>,<space|2em>V<rsub|k,c>=\<pi\>*<space|0.17em><around|(|c*\<vartheta\><rsub|k><rsup|2>*A<rsub|k><rsup|-1>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The centered moment map and the scaled field map are

  <\equation>
    <math-ss|M><rsub|i>=U<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*\<frak-c\>*V<rsub|k<rsub|i>,\<omega\><rsub|i>>:E<rsub|4>\<to\>\<cal-F\><rsub|4><rsup|0>,<space|2em><math-ss|P><rsub|i>=\<omega\><rsub|i>*U<rsub|i><rsup|-1>:\<cal-F\><rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|int>>\<to\>\<cal-F\><rsub|4><rsup|0>.<label|eq:ex-branch-moment-map>
  </equation>

  The transport and chart normalization give, for all sufficiently large
  <math|i>,

  <\equation>
    <math-ss|M><rsub|i>B<rsub|k<rsub|i>>*x=<math-ss|P><rsub|i>\<frak-c\>*\<pi\>*x*<space|1em><around|(|x\<in\>E<rsub|4>|)>.<label|eq:ex-branch-chart-identity>
  </equation>

  To see the normalization, <math|V<rsub|k,c>*B<rsub|k>*x=c*\<pi\>*A<rsub|k><rsup|-1>*K*A<rsub|k>*x>.
  The difference between <math|A<rsub|k><rsup|-1>*K*A<rsub|k>*x> and <math|x>
  is a multiple of <math|A<rsub|k><rsup|-1>*e<rsub|0>>, where
  <math|e<rsub|0>> is the scalar coordinate label. At the selected cutoff
  <math|A<rsub|k>> sends the reference unit label to
  <math|a<rsub|k><rsup|4>*e<rsub|0>>. The reference unit projects to
  <math|\<b-1\><rsub|4>>, so <math|\<pi\>*A<rsub|k><rsup|-1>*e<rsub|0>=a<rsub|k><rsup|-4>*\<b-1\><rsub|4>>
  and centering removes the difference. Applying <math|U<rsub|i><rsup|-1>>
  yields the identity. The cutoff after which this holds is fixed before
  taking <math|i\<to\>\<infty\>>. Let <math|W<rsup|b><rsub|k>=<math-ss|R><around|(|B<rsub|k>*x<rsup|b><rsub|k>|)>>,
  componentwise, and <math|W<rsup|b>=\<cal-S\><around|(|w<rsup|b>|)>>. The
  following are the incoming chart limits, stated in their actual limit
  order:

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|W<rsup|b><rsub|k>>|<cell|\<longrightarrow\>W<rsup|b>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|k\<to\>\<infty\>,b=0,-,+|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-branch-coefficient-input>>>|<row|<cell|<math-ss|M><rsub|i>>|<cell|\<longrightarrow\><math-ss|M><rsub|\<infty\>>\<assign\>\<frak-c\><rsub|0>*\<pi\><rsub|0>>|<cell|>|<cell|<text|in
    operator norm as >i\<to\>\<infty\>.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-branch-moment-input>>>>>
  </align>

  These limits depend on the selected finite-volume transport and chart
  estimates. In the first, the separation-ultrafilter limit is taken at fixed
  <math|k> before <math|k\<to\>\<infty\>>. The existing weighted-estimate
  construction uses the same reference coefficient limit, and its
  quantitative inputs remain subject to the gaps recorded there. We record
  the actual finite-volume passage yielding
  <eqref|eq:ex-branch-coefficient-input>. Let
  <math|T:\<bbb-R\><rsup|3>\<to\>E<rsub|4><rsup|I\<times\>I>> send <math|w>
  to <math|\<cal-S\><around|(|w|)>>. For each <math|k> let
  <math|R<rsub|k><around|(|u|)>> be the recorded finite-volume coefficient
  array before symmetry averaging and <math|C<rsup|b><rsub|k><around|(|u|)>>
  the corresponding finite-volume branch coefficient, with <math|u> the
  separation parameter. Equip these arrays with the finite-product supremum
  norm induced by the coordinate norm. The incoming estimates say that the
  nonnegative loss

  <\equation*>
    \<delta\><rsub|k>=exp <space|-0.17em><around*|[|-<tfrac|1|4><around|(|log
    log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)><rsup|2>|]>
  </equation*>

  satisfies, eventually in <math|k> and on an ultrafilter-large set of
  <math|u>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|R<rsub|k><around|(|u|)>-C<rsup|b><rsub|k><around|(|u|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|k>.
  </equation*>

  The threshold normalization explains this loss. For one component, let
  <math|z> be its local coefficient, <math|\<xi\>> its purchased readout,
  <math|\<Gamma\>> the finite-volume column map, and <math|v> the purchased
  label. The local pricing and zero-scalar identities give
  <math|\<xi\>=\<vartheta\><rsub|k><rsup|2>*z> and <math|K*z=z>; the
  purchased bias says <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<xi\>-\<Gamma\>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>\<leqslant\>\<vartheta\><rsub|k><rsup|2>*\<delta\><rsub|k>>,
  with <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|k>\<gtr\>0>. The recorded coefficient is
  <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|k><rsup|-2>*K*\<Gamma\>*v>. Write
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>> for the sum
  of the absolute coordinate values. Since <math|K> contracts the coordinate
  supremum norm and that norm is bounded by
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>>, the bias
  gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|z-\<vartheta\><rsub|k><rsup|-2>*K*\<Gamma\>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>\<vartheta\><rsub|k><rsup|-2>*<around|\<\|\|\>|\<xi\>-\<Gamma\>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|k>.
  </equation*>

  Taking the maximum over the finitely many components gives the displayed
  array estimate. The weighted readout acts on each coordinate by the
  threshold raised to its color-factor count. The simple-group low-grade
  classification gives count two at every non-scalar coordinate; the scalar
  coordinate of <math|z> is its zero-jet evaluation, which equals the
  observable's value at the identity and vanishes for these branches. Thus
  the weighted-readout definition gives <math|\<xi\>=\<vartheta\><rsub|k><rsup|2>*z>
  coordinate by coordinate. The local identification of this weighted
  readout, the zero-jet reading, and the purchased bias are inputs here. The
  selected clock tends to infinity, so <math|\<delta\><rsub|k>\<to\>0>: its
  logarithm is minus one quarter of a quantity tending to infinity. Also, for
  each <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, eventually in <math|k> and on an
  ultrafilter-large set of <math|u> there is <math|w=w*<around|(|k,u|)>> with
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|w-w<rsup|b>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>> and
  <math|<math-ss|R>C<rsup|b><rsub|k><around|(|u|)>=T*w>. The two correction
  branches use their displayed coefficient triples exactly; the principal
  coefficient is controlled only asymptotically. Finally, at every
  sufficiently large fixed <math|k> the ultrafilter limit of
  <math|<math-ss|R>R<rsub|k><around|(|u|)>> is the previously defined
  <math|W<rsup|b><rsub|k>>.

  Intersecting the two large sets at fixed <math|k> gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|R>R<rsub|k><around|(|u|)>-T*w<rsup|b>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|R>|\<\|\|\>>*\<delta\><rsub|k>+<around|\<\|\|\>|T|\<\|\|\>>*\<varepsilon\>.
  </equation*>

  The norm is continuous and the displayed closed ball is preserved by the
  ultrafilter limit, so the same bound holds with <math|W<rsup|b><rsub|k>> on
  the left. First let <math|k\<to\>\<infty\>>, then let
  <math|\<varepsilon\>*\<downarrow\>*0>: this proves
  <eqref|eq:ex-branch-coefficient-input>. The purchased bias and the
  existence of the finite-volume coefficient witnesses are retained inputs;
  this finite-dimensional passage does not construct them.

  The principal witness can be obtained from the local projection and the
  canonical stress classification, subject to the following local inputs.
  Assume the coefficient projection is linear on the finite sums below and
  annihilates each forward difference of the localized trace and antisymmetry
  currents. Assume also that the local principal coefficients have zero
  scalar coordinate. The exact finite-volume antisymmetry identity expresses
  each difference between transposed entries as a sum of those forward
  differences. Their projected coefficients therefore form a symmetric array.
  Its finite signed and real-structure average is a real covariant symmetric
  tensor with zero scalar coordinate. The free stress classification in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-free-rank> expresses it as
  <math|T*w> for a triple <math|w>, giving the required witness. The
  trace-current identity and the same vanishing give
  <math|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>T*w=0>; evaluation on a nonzero <math|01>
  curvature jet yields <math|w<rsub|1>+4*w<rsub|2>=0>. This exact local
  constraint does not give a rate for the recorded field trace. The
  difference-projection identity follows from the local affine-test identity:
  translating an affine test changes its potential by an infinitesimal gauge
  term. The current's first variation at the identity vanishes: global gauge
  invariance makes its restriction to each link an invariant linear
  functional on the simple color algebra. Such a functional annihilates all
  brackets, which span the nonabelian simple algebra, so it is zero.
  Differentiating gauge invariance at the identity then shows that the
  Hessian annihilates an infinitesimal gauge direction in either argument:
  the mixed derivative of the gauge action is multiplied by the already
  vanishing first variation. This also uses the local realization of the
  gauge term on the nonwrapping support. The Hessian therefore agrees on the
  original and translated affine tests. The forward difference consequently
  has zero Hessian on each such test direction paired with itself. Its value
  at the identity is also zero, and the grade-four jet-reading identity makes
  every jet evaluation of its local coefficient zero; the coefficient itself
  is zero. This application uses the affine translation identity, local
  regularity and nonwrapping of its designated neighborhood, Taylor order at
  least four, side length at least <math|40>, and the selected local
  certificates for the antisymmetry currents and their differences. In the
  selected construction the same finite-volume certificate supplies these
  local current and difference bounds for every axis on a common
  ultrafilter-large set at each sufficiently large scale. The existence of
  those choices and their local bounds remains an input here; the
  finite-dimensional classification does not itself produce them.

  For the principal branch, the closeness of these witnesses has a useful
  sequential reduction. Let <math|t<rsub|n>\<to\>\<infty\>> be any sequence
  of inverse couplings and let the finite-volume data be admissible at each
  <math|t<rsub|n>>, with side length tending to infinity, the prescribed
  nonwrapping neighborhood, and Taylor order at least four. The local
  comparison supplies two Hessian estimates for each fixed pair of the
  finitely supported elementary test directions of total degree at most four.
  Fixing a pair of tensor indices, let <math|Q<rsub|n>> be the corresponding
  unmodified local observable and <math|<wide|Q|~><rsub|n>> its principal
  modification; their second derivatives on those directions differ by at
  most <math|16*t<rsub|n><rsup|-30>>, while the derivative of
  <math|Q<rsub|n>> differs from the prescribed limiting bilinear form by at
  most <math|t<rsub|n><rsup|-100>>. For the first bound, write the Hessian of
  <math|Q<rsub|n>-<wide|Q|~><rsub|n>> in lattice coordinates. If
  <math|r<rsub|i>> is the distance weight of coordinate <math|i>, each
  elementary direction has <math|<around|\||v<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>4*<around|(|1+r<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>>.
  Thus the product of coordinates from the two directions is at most
  <math|16*<around|(|1+max <around|(|r<rsub|i>,r<rsub|j>|)>|)><rsup|4>>. The
  weighted Hessian coefficient sum controlling the modification dominates
  this polynomial weight and is at most <math|t<rsub|n><rsup|-30>>; summing
  the coordinate expansion gives the factor <math|16>. The weighted
  coefficient estimate and the elementary direction bound are the local
  inputs used here. The canonical quadratic stencil has derivatives
  converging to that same form as the side length grows. Thus the
  corresponding derivative of <math|<wide|Q|~><rsub|n>> and that of the
  canonical stencil have the same limit. These estimates use the local
  regularity and comparison inputs and are not consequences of the later
  field limit.

  The resulting Taylor-coefficient convergence follows as follows. A
  first-order jet produces a lattice test direction which is a finite linear
  combination of those elementary directions. Bilinearity and the preceding
  bounds therefore give convergence of the second derivative on this test
  direction paired with itself. The grade-four jet-reading identity expresses
  the label evaluated at a jet as the observable's value at the identity plus
  one half of this second derivative. For the principal observable the
  identity value is zero, and the same identity for the canonical stencil
  identifies the limit with the evaluation of the canonical stress label.
  This uses the jet-reading and canonical-stencil identities under the
  simple-group, regularity, nonwrapping and order hypotheses; they are
  external inputs here. The jet evaluations separate the finite-dimensional
  label space, so finitely many of them already separate it. Their joint
  linear map has a continuous left inverse onto its image. Consequently the
  unsymmetrized coefficient array <math|C<rsub|n>> tends entrywise to
  <math|T*w<rsup|0>>. The limiting material array is fixed by the finite
  symmetry average, so <math|<math-ss|R>C<rsub|n>\<to\>T*w<rsup|0>>. If
  <math|w<rsub|n>> is any associated coefficient witness with
  <math|<math-ss|R>C<rsub|n>=T*w<rsub|n>>, then
  <math|w<rsub|n>\<to\>w<rsup|0>>. Indeed, the material coefficient map is
  injective by the curvature-jet calculation above; choose a fixed linear
  left inverse on its finite-dimensional range and apply it to the
  convergence.

  This sequential statement yields the eventual uniform estimate required
  above. Otherwise, for some <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, choose
  counterexamples with <math|t<rsub|n>\<geqslant\>n> and
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|w<rsub|n>-w<rsup|0>|\<\|\|\>>\<geqslant\>\<varepsilon\>>.
  Their admissible side length <math|N<rsub|n>> satisfies
  <math|N<rsub|n>\<gtr\>20*<around|(|H<around|(|t<rsub|n>|)>+10|)>>, with
  <math|H> the neighborhood radius defined below. Since
  <math|H<around|(|t<rsub|n>|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>, the chosen data meet the
  sequential hypotheses. The preceding convergence is a contradiction. The
  local projection certificates, weighted Hessian bound, unmodified Hessian
  comparison, jet-reading identities and neighborhood-size growth are
  retained inputs. For the two correction branches the coefficient identities
  follow directly from their finite-volume curvature products. The curvature
  vanishes at the identity configuration, so for a lattice test direction
  <math|v>,

  <\equation*>
    <tfrac|1|2>D<rsup|2>*<around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|i*j>,F<rsub|k*l>|\<rangle\>><rsub|1><around|[|v,v|]>=<around|\<langle\>|D*F<rsub|i*j><around|(|1|)><around|[|v|]>,D*F<rsub|k*l><around|(|1|)><around|[|v|]>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation*>

  On the affine test associated with a degree-zero radial jet, the two
  linearized lattice curvatures are exactly the corresponding components of
  that jet, provided the test stencil is nonwrapping. The grade-four
  jet-reading identity then identifies this Hessian with
  <math|P<rsub|i*j,k*l>>. Taking the displayed finite sums gives
  <math|w<rsup|->=<around|(|0,0,1|)>> and
  <math|w<rsup|+>=<around|(|0,<tfrac|1|4>,0|)>> entry by entry, including
  their zero entries. Their material tensors are invariant under the finite
  tensor-symmetry average, so the same identities hold after that average.
  This uses the local curvature-stencil and jet-reading identities as inputs
  and does not infer an exact finite-volume equality from a continuum limit.

  The same local identities give a useful, but slower, trace estimate. For
  <math|b\<in\><around|{|0,-|}>> the trace of
  <math|<math-ss|R>C<rsup|b><rsub|k><around|(|u|)>> is zero on the relevant
  large set: for <math|b=0> this is the exact local constraint above, and for
  <math|b=-> it follows from the zero diagonal. Applying the finite trace map
  to <math|<math-ss|R><around|(|R<rsub|k><around|(|u|)>-C<rsup|b><rsub|k><around|(|u|)>|)>>
  and then taking the fixed-scale ultrafilter limit gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>W<rsup|b><rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>4<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|R>|\<\|\|\>>*\<delta\><rsub|k><space|2em>(b=0,-).
  </equation*>

  The factor four counts the diagonal entries. This does not prove the
  weighted trace limit: for each fixed <math|p\<gtr\>0>,
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|p>*\<delta\><rsub|k>\<to\>\<infty\>>.
  Indeed, for <math|y=log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>, the bound <math|log
  y\<leqslant\>4*y<rsup|1/4>> makes the logarithm of this product at least
  <math|p*y-4*<sqrt|y>\<to\>\<infty\>>. On the other hand,
  <math|<around|(|log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)><rsup|m>*\<delta\><rsub|k>\<to\>0>
  for every fixed <math|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, since its logarithm is <math|m*log
  y-<around|(|log y|)><rsup|2>/4>. The fast recording argument below supplies
  a stronger estimate.

  The moment-map convergence is a separate grade-four chart input: the
  normalized transported lift tends to the core projection, the scalar
  complement is annihilated by centering, and the chart expectations
  converge. On the finite-dimensional label space, these statements give
  operator-norm convergence. We spell out the selected two-scale passage
  behind the normalized lift. Let <math|m<rsub|i>> be the selected initial
  level, so <math|m<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>, and let <math|k<rsub|i>> be its
  prescribed summit. The simple group's grade-four reference projection is an
  isomorphism. To see this, use the low-grade decomposition in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-free-rank>: after the
  invariant linear terms vanish, a grade-four label is a constant plus a
  quadratic expression depending only on its degree-zero radial jet. Every
  such jet is realized by a constant-curvature affine Maxwell solution: for
  prescribed antisymmetric <math|F<rsub|a*b>> take
  <math|A<rsub|b><around|(|x|)>=<frac|1|2>*<big|sum><rsub|a>F<rsub|a*b>*x<rsub|a>>.
  Its curvature is <math|F>, and all curvature derivatives, including the
  Maxwell divergence, vanish. A label in the free equation ideal therefore
  vanishes on every degree-zero jet and hence on every jet. The standard
  table identifies the kernel of its projection with that equation ideal, an
  earlier free-table input. The kernel is zero, and the projection is onto by
  construction. In particular <math|\<cal-O\>\<ne\>0>: on an affine Maxwell
  solution with <math|F<rsub|01>=v>, <math|F<rsub|10>=-v> and all other
  curvature components zero, its unprojected label evaluates to
  <math|<tfrac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|v,v|\<rangle\>>\<gtr\>0> for any
  nonzero color vector <math|v>. Injectivity preserves its nonvanishing. All
  three material tensors are nonzero: every <math|w<rsup|b>\<ne\>0>, and both
  the material coefficient map and <math|\<pi\><rsub|0>> are injective. In
  particular the false material tensor is nonzero although its trace vanishes
  by <eqref|eq:ex-branch-three-traces>. Write
  <math|\<ell\>=\<pi\><rsub|0><rsup|-1>>. Let
  <math|Y<rsub|i>:\<cal-F\><rsub|4><rsup|0>\<to\>E<rsub|4>> be the selected
  label lift, and set <math|\<varpi\><rsub|i>=\<beta\><rsub|Z><around|(|m<rsub|i>|)>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>\<gtr\>0>.
  For each fixed <math|z\<in\>\<cal-F\><rsub|4><rsup|0>>, the incoming local
  chart estimates and the scale comparison below give

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<varpi\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>*B<rsub|m<rsub|i>>*Y<rsub|i>*z>|<cell|\<longrightarrow\>K*\<ell\>*z,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-branch-lift-initial>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsub|m<rsub|i>>*Y<rsub|i>*z-B<rsub|k<rsub|i>>*Y<rsub|i>*z|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>*<around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsub|k<rsub|i>>*Y<rsub|i>*z|\<\|\|\>>,<space|1em>\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>\<longrightarrow\>0,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-branch-lift-stability>>>|<row|<cell|<frac|\<beta\><rsub|Z><around|(|m<rsub|i>|)>|\<beta\><rsub|Z><around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>>>|<cell|\<longrightarrow\>1.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-branch-lift-beta-ratio>>>>>
  </align>

  The first convergence comes from the fixed-grade local lift estimate after
  division by <math|\<varpi\><rsub|i>>: for each fixed <math|z> its error is
  bounded by a constant depending on <math|z> times <math|exp
  <around|[|-<tfrac|1|2><around|(|log log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|2>|]>>, which tends to zero. We
  spell out its finite-volume normalization. At a fixed initial level
  <math|m>, write <math|\<cal-N\>> for the column map normalized by
  <math|a<rsub|m><rsup|-4>>, <math|\<cal-T\>> for the local chart map, and
  let <math|\<alpha\>> denote the displayed exponential error. The selected
  chart inputs give <math|\<cal-N\>*u<rsub|<math-up|ref>>=\<cal-T\>*u<rsub|<math-up|ref>>=e<rsub|0>>
  and, for every <math|y>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-N\>*y-\<cal-T\>*y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>\<leqslant\>\<alpha\>*<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-N\>*y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-N\>*y-\<cal-T\>*y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-N\>*y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>.
  </equation*>

  The second estimate and the triangle inequality give
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-N\>*y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>\<leqslant\>2*<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-T\>*y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>>.
  For any <math|v>, take <math|y=v-<around|(|\<cal-T\>*v|)><rsub|0>*u<rsub|<math-up|ref>>>.
  Then <math|\<cal-T\>*y=K*\<cal-T\>*v> and
  <math|K*\<cal-N\>*y=K*\<cal-N\>*v>, so the coefficient norm's contraction
  under <math|K> gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|K*<around|(|\<cal-N\>*v-\<cal-T\>*v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>\<leqslant\>2*\<alpha\>*<around|\<\|\|\>|K*\<cal-T\>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>.
  </equation*>

  At grade four the non-scalar coordinates of every label have the required
  homogeneous grade, by the low-grade simple-group classification. For the
  actual finite-volume lift <math|L> of a reference label <math|v>, the
  selected lift identity is <math|K*\<cal-T\>*L*v=\<beta\><rsub|m,u>*\<vartheta\><rsub|m><rsup|2>*h<rsup|4>*K*v>,
  where <math|\<beta\><rsub|m,u>\<gtr\>0>,
  <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|m>\<gtr\>0>, <math|h\<geqslant\>0> and the physical
  radius is <math|a<rsub|m>*h>. The finite-volume counterpart of
  <math|B<rsub|m>*L*v> is <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|m><rsup|-2>*a<rsub|m><rsup|4>*K*\<cal-N\>*L*v>.
  Substituting <math|L*v> into the preceding inequality yields

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||\<vartheta\><rsub|m><rsup|-2>*a<rsub|m><rsup|4>*K*\<cal-N\>*L*v-\<beta\><rsub|m,u>*<around|(|a<rsub|m>*h|)><rsup|4>*K*v|\|><rsub|<math-up|c>>\<leqslant\>2*\<alpha\>*<space|0.17em>\<beta\><rsub|m,u>*<around|(|a<rsub|m>*h|)><rsup|4>*<around|\<\|\|\>|K*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>.
  </equation*>

  At fixed <math|i> the selected lifts and couplings have their common
  separation-ultrafilter limits, and the selected physical radius is
  <math|r<rsub|i>>. The steep transport property gives the limit of the
  finite-volume transport applied to those moving lifts. Passing to these
  limits gives the stated initial-level estimate; no separate limit of
  <math|\<cal-T\>> is needed. The two finite-volume chart inequalities, lift
  identity and selected limits are the inputs used in this reduction.

  The second estimate is the comparison over the actual interval from the
  initial level to its summit. Its coefficient-size error is

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|part><around|(|m<rsub|i>|)>=exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|[|-<tfrac|2|5><around|(|log log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|2>|]>.
  </equation*>

  The norm is any fixed norm on <math|E<rsub|4>>; equivalence with the
  coefficient norm transfers the estimate with <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>>
  equal to a fixed positive multiple of <math|<math-up|part><around|(|m<rsub|i>|)>>.
  This tends to zero because the hatted clock at the cofinal initial levels
  tends to infinity. The finite-volume inputs behind these comparisons belong
  to the selected history; they do not follow from a generic chart.

  The interval comparison follows from the actual one-step column bound and
  the scheduled sum budget. At one step, let <math|w> and <math|v> be the
  finite-volume column values at the earlier and later levels, with positive
  thresholds <math|a,b>. The selected one-step input gives
  <math|b/a\<leqslant\>2> and a comparison value <math|X> with

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|w-X|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>\<leqslant\>g*<around|\<\|\|\>|K*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>,<space|2em>K*X=<around|(|a/b|)><rsup|2>*K*v.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|g> is the step's nonnegative column error; the second identity
  uses the grade-four homogeneity just noted. Thus

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|a<rsup|-2>*K*w-b<rsup|-2>*K*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>\<leqslant\>a<rsup|-2>*g*<around|\<\|\|\>|K*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>\<leqslant\>4*g*<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsup|-2>*K*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>.
  </equation*>

  This is the one-step estimate with coefficient <math|4*g>; the threshold
  and column comparison are selected-construction inputs. To see the
  accumulation, fix a finite-volume label and write <math|u<rsub|j>> for its
  transported value at level <math|j> between an initial level <math|m> and
  its summit <math|k>. Put <math|N=k-m\<gtr\>0> and
  <math|x=<math-up|part><around|(|m|)>\<leqslant\>1>. The selected one-step
  and schedule inputs state that

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|j>-u<rsub|j+1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>\<leqslant\><frac|x|2*N><around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|j+1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>,<space|2em>m\<leqslant\>j\<less\>k.
  </equation*>

  Backward induction gives, for every <math|m\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>k>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|j>-u<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>\<leqslant\><frac|x*<around|(|k-j|)>|N><around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed, if this is known at <math|j+1>, the triangle inequality and
  <math|x\<leqslant\>1> give <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|j+1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>\<leqslant\>2<around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>>.
  Add the one-step error to the induction bound to obtain the next case; the
  case <math|j=k> is zero. For fixed <math|m,k> and label, the finite-volume
  estimates hold together for the finitely many levels on an
  ultrafilter-large set. Passing to the fixed-scale transport limits gives
  the stated comparison. The threshold and column comparison and the schedule
  hypotheses remain selected-construction inputs; the induction does not
  establish them.

  For the scale ratio, the selected finite-volume running estimate says that
  for every <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, eventually in <math|j>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<beta\><rsub|j,u>-<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>*s<rsub|j>
  </equation*>

  on an ultrafilter-large set of finite-volume parameters <math|u>. At each
  fixed <math|j>, the same ultrafilter sends <math|\<beta\><rsub|j,u>> to
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|Z><around|(|j|)>>; passing to the closed interval gives
  the same estimate for <math|\<beta\><rsub|Z><around|(|j|)>>. The affine
  clock satisfies <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>b*s<rsub|j>> for
  its positive slope <math|b>. For any <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>, choose
  <math|\<epsilon\>=b*\<delta\>/2> and divide by the positive clock. This
  proves <math|\<beta\><rsub|Z><around|(|j|)>/<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<to\>1>.
  The running estimate and the fixed-scale limit are upstream inputs. The
  summit clock estimate follows from the first-crossing and block-size
  properties of the selected schedule. Write <math|g<rsub|j>> for the gap
  from level <math|j> to its summit and <math|b> for the fixed slope
  constant. Set <math|<math-up|Gate><around|(|x|)>=<around|(|log
  x|)><rsup|48>>. For <math|x\<geqslant\>1>, the inequality
  <math|t\<leqslant\>e<rsup|t>> with <math|t=<around|(|log x|)>/96> gives
  <math|<around|(|log x|)><rsup|48>/x\<leqslant\>96<rsup|48>*x<rsup|-1/2>>,
  so the gate ratio tends to zero. The selected clock tends to infinity.

  The summit is the first later level at which the scale gap reaches the
  gate. Let <math|q> be its preceding level and put
  <math|x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>>. By the first-crossing property,
  <math|0\<leqslant\>s<rsub|q>-s<rsub|j>\<leqslant\><math-up|Gate><around|(|x|)>>.
  The affine clock identity then gives <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|q>\<leqslant\>x+b<math-up|Gate><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>2*x>
  eventually. The selected schedule has <math|L<rsub|q>\<geqslant\>1> and
  bounds <math|L<rsub|q>> by <math|log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|q>>; its scale
  increment is <math|s<rsub|q+1>-s<rsub|q>=log L<rsub|q>>. Since <math|log
  L<rsub|q>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|q>>, for large <math|x>,

  <\equation*>
    s<rsub|q+1>-s<rsub|q>\<leqslant\>log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|q>\<leqslant\>log
    <around|(|2*x|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|Gate><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  Adding the pre-crossing gap and this last increment gives
  <math|0\<leqslant\>g<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>2<math-up|Gate><around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|)>>.
  Consequently,

  <\equation*>
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|<math-up|summit><around|(|j|)>>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>+b*g<rsub|j>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\><frac|g<rsub|j>|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>>\<leqslant\>2*<frac|<math-up|Gate><around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|)>|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  The first-crossing definition and block-size schedule bound are the inputs
  used here. Dividing the identity by the positive
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>> yields
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|<math-up|summit><around|(|j|)>>/<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<to\>1>,
  and hence the same limit along <math|j=m<rsub|i>>. Since both levels are
  cofinal and the denominators are positive,

  <\equation*>
    <frac|\<beta\><rsub|Z><around|(|m<rsub|i>|)>|\<beta\><rsub|Z><around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>>=<frac|\<beta\><rsub|Z><around|(|m<rsub|i>|)>/<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m<rsub|i>>|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|Z><around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>/<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|i>>|)>*<around*|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|i>>/<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m<rsub|i>>|)>>\<longrightarrow\>1.
  </equation*>

  Thus <eqref|eq:ex-branch-lift-beta-ratio> follows from these two explicit
  scale estimates.

  The same bounds also explain the scheduled budget used in the backward
  induction. Put <math|x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>> and <math|N=k-m>, where
  <math|k> is the summit. Every scale increment is at least one, so the gap
  bound gives <math|N\<leqslant\>2<around|(|log x|)><rsup|48>>. For all large
  <math|m>, the summit clock is at most <math|2*x>, and the scheduled column
  factor <math|p<rsub|j>> at each step satisfies
  <math|p<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>2*log x>. Let <math|c<rsub|j>=exp
  <around|[|<tfrac|1|2><around|(|log log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j+1>|)><rsup|2>|]>>;
  clock monotonicity gives <math|c<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>exp
  <around|[|<tfrac|1|2><around|(|log log x|)><rsup|2>|]>> at these levels.
  The one-step coefficient in the selected construction is
  <math|8*<around|(|1+p<rsub|j>|)>/c<rsub|j>>. To bound it by
  <math|<math-up|part><around|(|m|)>/<around|(|2*N|)>>, it is enough that

  <\equation*>
    16*N*<around|(|1+2*log x|)>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around|[|<tfrac|1|10><around|(|log log x|)><rsup|2>|]>.
  </equation*>

  Using the bound on <math|N>, the left side is at most
  <math|32*<around|(|1+2*log x|)><around|(|log x|)><rsup|48>>. With
  <math|y=log log x>, this is at most <math|96*e<rsup|49*y>> for large
  <math|x>, whereas the ratio of the right side to <math|96*e<rsup|49*y>>
  tends to infinity. This proves the scheduled budget from the clock, block
  and column bounds. The underlying threshold and column comparison remains
  an input; it is not a consequence of this numerical calculation.

  To combine them, put <math|X<rsub|i>=\<varpi\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>*B<rsub|m<rsub|i>>*Y<rsub|i>*z>
  and <math|Z<rsub|i>=\<varpi\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>*B<rsub|k<rsub|i>>*Y<rsub|i>*z>.
  The stability estimate gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|i>-Z<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|i><around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>>.
  Since <math|X<rsub|i>> converges, it is bounded; eventually
  <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1/2>, so
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2<around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>>.
  Consequently <math|Z<rsub|i>-X<rsub|i>\<to\>0>. Multiplication by
  <eqref|eq:ex-branch-lift-beta-ratio> changes the denominator from
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|Z><around|(|m<rsub|i>|)>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>> to
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|Z><around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>=\<omega\><rsub|i>>,
  giving

  <\equation>
    \<omega\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>*B<rsub|k<rsub|i>>*Y<rsub|i>*z\<longrightarrow\>K*\<ell\>*z.<label|eq:ex-branch-lift-summit>
  </equation>

  The same <math|Y<rsub|i>>, <math|r<rsub|i>>, transport and history occur at
  both levels; the ratio is not a comparison between independently selected
  charts.

  Now put

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|i>=\<omega\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>*B<rsub|k<rsub|i>>,<space|2em>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>=D<rsub|i>*Y<rsub|i>*\<pi\><rsub|0>+<around|(|1-K|)>:E<rsub|4>\<longrightarrow\>E<rsub|4>.
  </equation*>

  The limit <eqref|eq:ex-branch-lift-summit>,
  <math|\<ell\>*\<pi\><rsub|0>=1>, and a finite basis give
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>\<to\>1> in operator norm; here <math|K> is the core
  projection on the fixed label space. Also <math|<math-ss|M><rsub|i>>
  annihilates the scalar complement <math|1-K>, and eventually
  <math|\<pi\>*Y<rsub|i>=U<rsub|i>>. Thus, on <math|x\<in\>E<rsub|4>>, the
  chart identity gives

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|M><rsub|i>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>*x=\<pi\><rsub|0>*x-<around|\<langle\>|U<rsub|i>*\<pi\><rsub|0>*x|\<rangle\>>*\<b-1\><rsub|4><rsup|0>\<longrightarrow\>\<pi\><rsub|0>*x-<around|\<langle\>|\<pi\><rsub|0>*x|\<rangle\>>*\<b-1\><rsub|4><rsup|0>=<math-ss|M><rsub|\<infty\>>x.
  </equation*>

  The expectation convergence is the one-point case of the selected chart
  correlation limit. For a fixed standard field <math|z>, its one-point
  function at the origin is <math|<around|\<langle\>|z|\<rangle\>><rsub|0>>;
  the rescaled interacting one-point function at the origin is
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|U<rsub|i>*z|\<rangle\>>>. The selected chart
  convergence at this one-point configuration therefore gives
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|U<rsub|i>*z|\<rangle\>>\<to\><around|\<langle\>|z|\<rangle\>><rsub|0>>.
  It uses the chart's one-point convergence and the two table hierarchies as
  inputs. Eventual transport of the unit is another selected-chart input. A
  finite basis upgrades the displayed pointwise convergence to operator-norm
  convergence. Eventually <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>-1|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>1/2>,
  so the geometric series gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-A\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2>
  and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-A\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>-1|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>-1|\<\|\|\>>\<to\>0>.
  Since <math|<math-ss|M><rsub|i>=<around|(|<math-ss|M><rsub|i>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|)>*\<cal-A\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>>,
  this proves <eqref|eq:ex-branch-moment-input>. The normalized local lift
  and one-point chart convergence remain upstream dependencies. Neither limit
  alone supplies a trace rate.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Three branch field limits><label|prop:ex-branch-moments>Assume
    <eqref|eq:ex-branch-coefficient-input>\U
    <eqref|eq:ex-branch-moment-input>, the chart identity and projection
    equivariance above, and eventual equivariance of <math|B<rsub|k>> under
    every signed motion and the real-structure involution. Then

    <\equation*>
      <math-ss|P><rsub|i>t<rsup|0><rsub|k<rsub|i>>\<longrightarrow\>M<around|(|w<rsup|0>|)>,<space|2em><math-ss|P><rsub|i>t<rsup|-><rsub|k<rsub|i>>\<longrightarrow\>M<around|(|w<rsup|->|)>,<space|2em><math-ss|P><rsub|i>t<rsup|+><rsub|k<rsub|i>>\<longrightarrow\>M<around|(|w<rsup|+>|)>
    </equation*>

    in the finite product <math|<around|(|\<cal-F\><rsub|4><rsup|0>|)><rsup|I\<times\>I>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The finite symmetry average commutes with symmetrization. For
    sufficiently large <math|k>, the selected transport intertwines every
    signed motion and the real-structure involution: this follows by taking
    its defining fixed-scale separation limit of the corresponding
    finite-volume transport identities. The core projection also commutes:
    the scalar label is fixed and its coordinate is preserved, up to
    conjugation under the real structure. Using equivariance and the chart
    identity gives, eventually,

    <\equation>
      <around|(|<math-ss|P><rsub|i>t<rsup|b><rsub|k<rsub|i>>|)><rsub|a*c>=<tfrac|1|2><around*|(|<math-ss|M><rsub|i>W<rsup|b><rsub|k<rsub|i>>*<around|(|a,c|)>+<math-ss|M><rsub|i>W<rsup|b><rsub|k<rsub|i>>*<around|(|c,a|)>|)>.<label|eq:ex-branch-chart-array>
    </equation>

    Indeed, apply <eqref|eq:ex-branch-chart-identity> to each term of the
    finite symmetry average; the scalar signs and coefficients are real, so
    commute with both maps. Since <math|k<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>,
    <math|W<rsup|b><rsub|k<rsub|i>>*<around|(|a,c|)>\<to\>W<rsup|b>*<around|(|a,c|)>>.
    For any convergent <math|v<rsub|i>\<to\>v> in <math|E<rsub|4>>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|M><rsub|i>v<rsub|i>-<math-ss|M><rsub|\<infty\>>v|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|M><rsub|i>-<math-ss|M><rsub|\<infty\>>|\<\|\|\>><space|0.17em>\<\|\|\>v<rsub|i>\<\|\|\>+<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|M><rsub|\<infty\>>|\<\|\|\>>*<space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|i>-v|\<\|\|\>>\<longrightarrow\>0.
    </equation*>

    Apply this to both entries in <eqref|eq:ex-branch-chart-array>. The limit
    is <math|<math-up|sym><around|(|\<frak-c\><rsub|0>*\<pi\><rsub|0>*\<cal-S\><around|(|w<rsup|b>|)>|)>>,
    which is <math|M<around|(|w<rsup|b>|)>> by symmetry and zero expectation.
    There are only sixteen entries, so coordinate convergence is convergence
    of the entire tensor. This proves all three limits without exchanging an
    infinite sum and a limit.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The fast trace from localized
  recordings><label|subsec:ex-branch-fast-trace>

  We now derive the raw fast-trace statement used in the weighted limit. Let
  <math|s<rsub|k>\<to\>\<infty\>> be the logarithmic scale of the selected
  schedule and <math|a<rsub|k>=e<rsup|-s<rsub|k>>> for the mesh above. A
  sequence is <em|fast> if its norm is <math|O<around|(|e<rsup|-\<eta\>*s<rsub|k>>|)>>
  for some <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>; a scalar sequence or operator family is
  <em|subexponential> if its norm is <math|O<around|(|e<rsup|\<epsilon\>*s<rsub|k>>|)>>
  for every <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>. A finite sum of fast sequences is
  fast, with the minimum of finitely many positive decay exponents.

  We make the local recording input explicit. Write <math|E<rsub|3>> and
  <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|3><rsup|<math-up|int>>> for the finite-dimensional
  grade-three label and field spaces of the same interacting table, and
  <math|\<pi\><rsub|d>:E<rsub|d>\<to\>\<cal-F\><rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|int>>>
  for its grade-<math|d> projection, with <math|\<pi\><rsub|4>=\<pi\>>. For
  each admissible separated test <math|\<sigma\>>, the table gives linear
  functionals <math|L<rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|d><around|(|z|)>:\<cal-F\><rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|int>>\<to\>\<bbb-C\>>
  for <math|d=3,4> and <math|z> near the origin. They are continuous in
  <math|z> and <math|L<rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|d><around|(|0|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|d>>.
  In particular,

  <\equation>
    lim<rsub|z\<to\>0><around|\<\|\|\>|L<rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|d><around|(|z|)>-\<lambda\><rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|d>|\<\|\|\>>=0*<space|1em><around|(|d=3,4|)>,<space|2em><big|cap><rsub|\<sigma\>>ker
    \<lambda\><rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|4>=<around|{|0|}>.<label|eq:ex-branch-test-properties>
  </equation>

  The convergence is in operator norm by finite dimensionality. Thus finitely
  many of the functionals <math|\<lambda\><rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|4>> already
  separate <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|int>>>. This follows from
  the field-separation and smoothness properties of the selected hierarchy:
  approximate a separated configuration by generic grid configurations at
  increasingly fine scales. Subtracting the first grid address places the
  insertion at the origin without changing relative positions. Translation
  invariance preserves the correlation, and spectator fields have
  representative tokens, so these are allowed tests. By continuity, a field
  annihilated by them has all separated correlations zero and is zero by
  field separation. These hierarchy and grid-density properties are retained
  inputs; finite dimensionality alone does not imply separation.

  Every grade-three field is a multiple of the unit. Indeed, each
  color-valued jet-coordinate factor has grade at least two, so a grade-three
  label has at most one factor. Its invariant linear part vanishes by the
  low-grade color argument of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-free-rank>;
  only the scalar label remains. The field projection is onto and sends the
  scalar label to a nonzero reference-scale multiple of the unit, so the same
  is true of <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|3><rsup|<math-up|int>>>. Finally, inserting
  the unit leaves a separated correlation independent of its insertion point.
  Thus, in a sufficiently small separated neighborhood,

  <\equation>
    L<rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|3><around|(|z|)>=L<rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|3><around|(|0|)>.<label|eq:ex-branch-grade-three-unit>
  </equation>

  The grading, quotient projection, unit and field-separation statements here
  are the ones belonging to the same selected table; they are not inferred
  from the branch limit.

  The incoming recording estimates are the following. For every test
  <math|\<sigma\>> there is a radius <math|c<rsub|\<sigma\>>\<gtr\>0>. For
  each <math|d\<in\>I> the localized current
  <math|Q<rsub|d><rsup|<around|[|r|]>>> and its forward difference have
  recorded fields <math|v<rsub|d,k>\<in\>\<cal-F\><rsub|3><rsup|<math-up|int>>>
  and <math|w<rsub|d,k>\<in\>\<cal-F\><rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|int>>>. For
  every branch <math|b\<in\><around|{|0,-|}>>, the diagonal tensor recordings
  are the already defined <math|\<pi\>*x<rsup|b><rsub|k>*<around|(|a,a|)>\<in\>\<cal-F\><rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|int>>>.
  Their finite-volume tested correlations, denoted respectively by
  <math|F<rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|d,k,u><around|(|j|)>>,
  <math|G<rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|d,k,u><around|(|j|)>> and
  <math|T<rsup|\<sigma\>,b><rsub|a,k,u>>, satisfy, with fast nonnegative
  errors, the following estimates. For all sufficiently large <math|k> and
  each integer <math|j> with <math|<around|\||j|\|>*a<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|\<sigma\>>>,
  the appropriate inequalities hold for <math|u> in an ultrafilter-large set.
  A common radius is obtained by taking the minimum of the finitely many
  radii for these current, difference and tensor insertions; the
  corresponding eventual scale cutoff is the maximum of finitely many
  cutoffs:

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\||F<rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|d,k,u><around|(|j|)>-L<rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|3>*<around|(|j*a<rsub|k>*e<rsub|d>|)>*v<rsub|d,k>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>E<rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|d,k>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-branch-book-current>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||G<rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|d,k,u><around|(|j|)>-L<rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|4>*<around|(|j*a<rsub|k>*e<rsub|d>|)>*w<rsub|d,k>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>D<rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|d,k>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-branch-book-difference>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||T<rsup|\<sigma\>,b><rsub|a,k,u>-\<lambda\><rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|4>*<around|(|\<pi\>*x<rsup|b><rsub|k>*<around|(|a,a|)>|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<sigma\>,b><rsub|a,k>.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-branch-book-tensor>>>>>
  </align>

  Here <math|e<rsub|d>> is the <math|d>th coordinate vector; <math|E,D,H> are
  fast sequences, possibly with test- and component-dependent exponents. The
  same separation ultrafilter is used in every line. The last line is at zero
  displacement. The finite-volume correlation is linear in its inserted
  observable, and for each <math|\<sigma\>> the forward-difference identity
  holds for all integers <math|j> on an ultrafilter-large set, for all
  sufficiently large <math|k>:

  <\equation>
    G<rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|d,k,u><around|(|j|)>=a<rsub|k><rsup|-1>*<around*|(|F<rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|d,k,u>*<around|(|j+1|)>-F<rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|d,k,u><around|(|j|)>|)>.<label|eq:ex-branch-recorded-difference>
  </equation>

  Here <math|a<rsub|k><rsup|-1>=a<rsub|k><rsup|-4>/a<rsub|k><rsup|-3>>, the
  ratio of the two grade normalizations; translation covariance identifies
  the displaced current values. The localized current and its difference are
  recorded using the same finite-volume choices and volumes. The exact
  finite-volume branch formulas, after applying the same linear tested
  correlation, give for each test <math|\<sigma\>> and all sufficiently large
  <math|k>, on an ultrafilter-large set of <math|u>,

  <\equation>
    <big|sum><rsub|a>T<rsup|\<sigma\>,-><rsub|a,k,u>=0,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|a>T<rsup|\<sigma\>,0><rsub|a,k,u>=<big|sum><rsub|d>G<rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|d,k,u><around|(|0|)>.<label|eq:ex-branch-recorded-traces>
  </equation>

  The construction supplies these estimates using its local recording,
  selected trace-current localization, field hierarchy and point-limit
  inputs. The estimates, low-grade unit statement and separating property use
  the upstream table and hierarchy inputs just stated; the finite-volume
  price production behind the estimates is not proved in this subsection.
  They are more primitive than the assertion that either branch trace is
  fast.

  The following root-price mechanism identifies a more primitive sufficient
  input for the three recording estimates. Fix one of the finitely many
  insertions above, its grade <math|d\<in\><around|{|3,4|}>>, and a separated
  test <math|\<sigma\>>. At scale <math|k> and finite-volume parameter
  <math|u>, let <math|A<rsub|k,u>> be the actual inserted observable and
  <math|O<rsub|k,u><around|(|v<rsub|k,u>|)>> the observable made from its
  selected local label <math|v<rsub|k,u>\<in\>E<rsub|d>>. The actual
  insertion already includes the grade-<math|d> factor
  <math|a<rsub|k><rsup|-d>> and transport to the comparison volume;
  <math|O<rsub|k,u>> is the corresponding reference-scale observable. The
  following finite-dimensional selection explains how the same limiting label
  is obtained for all tests. The purchased finite-volume recording supplies a
  coefficient <math|\<xi\><rsub|k,u>>, a label <math|v<rsub|k,u>>, and the
  actual column map <math|C<rsub|k,u>> on an ultrafilter-large set, with

  <\equation>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|\<xi\><rsub|k,u>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|\<xi\><rsub|k,u>-C<rsub|k,u>*v<rsub|k,u>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|k,u>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|d,k>*<around|\<\|\|\>|C<rsub|k,u>*v<rsub|k,u>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>.<label|eq:ex-branch-recording-compactness>
  </equation>

  Here <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|c>>> is the
  fixed coefficient norm, and <math|M<rsub|d,k>=exp
  <around|(|\<ell\><rsub|k>+d*s<rsub|k>|)>> and
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|k>=<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>k>log \<zeta\><rsub|j+1>> for
  the calibrated column factors <math|\<zeta\><rsub|j+1>\<geqslant\>1>. The
  previously established column estimate gives
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|k>=o<around|(|s<rsub|k>|)>>, so
  <math|e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|k>>> is subexponential. The middle bound is a
  convenient weakening of the purchased bias bound
  <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|k><rsup|2>*exp <around|[|-<tfrac|1|4><around|(|log
  log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)><rsup|2>|]>>, using
  <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>1>. It is not a replacement for the
  root-price purchase below. The inverse bound follows by multiplying the
  calibrated one-step factors <math|\<zeta\><rsub|j+1>*L<rsub|j><rsup|d>>
  from the reference level, where the column is the identity. All factors are
  at least one, <math|<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>k>log L<rsub|j>=s<rsub|k>>, and
  the label norm is bounded by the coefficient norm. The calibration,
  reference identity and common choice of column map and label remain inputs.

  When constructing a history from these purchased data, for each fixed
  sufficiently large <math|k> choose <math|v<rsub|k,u>> once together with
  its full purchase certificate on the ultrafilter-large set where that
  certificate is available, and define it arbitrarily elsewhere. In
  particular, <eqref|eq:ex-branch-recording-compactness> holds on that set.
  By the triangle inequality its norm is at most <math|2*M<rsub|d,k>> on that
  set. The closed ball of that radius is compact because <math|E<rsub|d>> is
  finite dimensional, so the ultrafilter has a limit
  <math|x<rsub|k>\<in\>E<rsub|d>> in the ball. The choice is made before any
  test is selected; this one <math|x<rsub|k>> works for every fixed test and
  satisfies <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*M<rsub|d,k>>.
  Since <math|\<ell\><rsub|k>=o<around|(|s<rsub|k>|)>>, for every
  <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0> this also gives
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>=O<around|(|e<rsup|<around|(|d+\<epsilon\>|)>*s<rsub|k>>|)>>;
  the bound is grade-dependent and need not be subexponential. For an already
  selected history, retain its original labels and their existing ultrafilter
  limit instead: the compactness construction by itself does not identify a
  newly chosen limit with that history's limit. The root-price step below
  uses the labels and limit from one and the same history.

  For the root-price step assume:

  <\enumerate>
    <item>for this test, some <math|c<rsub|\<sigma\>>\<gtr\>0> and
    <math|B<rsub|\<sigma\>>\<geqslant\>0> satisfy, for every sufficiently
    large <math|k>, axis <math|a\<in\>I> and integer <math|\<ell\>> with
    <math|<around|\||\<ell\>|\|>*a<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|\<sigma\>>>, on
    an ultrafilter-large set of <math|u>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<cal-C\><rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|k,u>*<around|(|\<ell\>,a;A<rsub|k,u>|)>-\<cal-C\><rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|k,u>*<around|(|\<ell\>,a;O<rsub|k,u><around|(|v<rsub|k,u>|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|\<sigma\>>*E<rsub|k>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|E<rsub|k>> is fast and <math|\<cal-C\>> is the ordinary
    finite-volume tested correlation, linear in the insertion;

    <item>for each sufficiently large fixed <math|k>, axis <math|a>, integer
    <math|\<ell\>> with <math|<around|\||\<ell\>|\|>*a<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|\<sigma\>>>,
    and fixed <math|v\<in\>E<rsub|d>>, the correlation of
    <math|O<rsub|k,u><around|(|v|)>> converges along the same ultrafilter to
    <math|L<rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|d>*<around|(|\<ell\>*a<rsub|k>*e<rsub|a>|)>*\<pi\><rsub|d>*v>.
  </enumerate>

  The first assumption applies the rooted price to the actual-minus-local
  insertion, using selected funding, interior and locality estimates. The
  second is the fixed-label point limit in the same table. To apply it to the
  moving labels, choose a basis <math|<around|(|e<rsub|j>|)>> of
  <math|E<rsub|d>> and write <math|v<rsub|k,u>=<big|sum><rsub|j>c<rsub|j><around|(|u|)>*e<rsub|j>>,
  with <math|c<rsub|j><around|(|u|)>\<to\>c<rsub|j>> along the ultrafilter.
  Linearity of the local insertion and correlation gives a finite sum of
  <math|c<rsub|j><around|(|u|)>> times the correlations for the fixed basis
  vectors. The product and finite-sum limit rules therefore give

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-C\><rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|k,u>*<around|(|\<ell\>,a;O<rsub|k,u><around|(|v<rsub|k,u>|)>|)>\<longrightarrow\>L<rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|d>*<around|(|\<ell\>*a<rsub|k>*e<rsub|a>|)>*\<pi\><rsub|d>*x<rsub|k>.
  </equation*>

  This uses the existing fixed-scale label limit, including for an already
  chosen history. Fix a lower scale <math|h> at least the test's native
  scale. A comparison scale is open when <math|h\<leqslant\>j> and
  <math|b<rsub|j><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|h>/a<rsub|j>>, where
  <math|b<rsub|j>> is its cover size. Let
  <math|<math-up|basin><around|(|h|)>> be the first open scale and choose
  <math|j=max <around|{|<math-up|basin><around|(|h|)>,<math-up|ref><around|(|d|)>|}>>.
  Such a scale exists: the cover-growth and mesh bounds give
  <math|a<rsub|j+1>*b<rsub|j+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|(|2/3|)>*a<rsub|j>*b<rsub|j><rsup|2>>.
  Iterating gives <math|a<rsub|j>*b<rsub|j><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|(|2/3|)><rsup|j-h>*a<rsub|h>*b<rsub|h><rsup|2>\<to\>0>
  for <math|j\<geqslant\>h>. Opening persists at finer scales, so this choice
  of <math|j> is open. At each allowed later level <math|k\<gtr\>j>, the
  finite-volume purchase gives the actual-minus-local insertion the rooted
  price

  <\equation>
    P<rsub|d,h,j><around|(|k|)>=<frac|2<space|0.17em><math-up|stake><rsub|h,j><space|0.17em>a<rsub|j><rsup|-d>|\<varphi\><rsub|d,j>>*<space|0.17em><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|4>*<space|0.17em>exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|[|-<frac|s<rsub|k>-s<rsub|j>|8>|]>.<label|eq:ex-branch-native-root-price>
  </equation>

  Here <math|<math-up|stake><rsub|h,j>=exp
  <around|[|<around|(|a<rsub|h>/a<rsub|j>|)><rsup|4>*\<cal-T\><rsub|j>+5|]>>,
  where <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|j>> is the selected per-site treasury, and
  <math|\<varphi\><rsub|d,j>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j><rsup|-\<nu\><rsub|d>>>,
  where <math|\<nu\><rsub|d>> is the fixed reference-label degree bound plus
  four. Both are positive and fixed once <math|d,h,j> are chosen. This is the
  actual purchased price. Since the hatted clock is affine in
  <math|s<rsub|k>>, its fourth power is subexponential: for
  <math|s\<geqslant\>0> and <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, the exponential
  series gives <math|s<rsup|4>\<leqslant\>24*\<epsilon\><rsup|-4>*e<rsup|\<epsilon\>*s>>.
  Thus, for every <math|0\<less\>\<eta\>\<less\>1/8>,
  <math|P<rsub|d,h,j><around|(|k|)>=O<around|(|e<rsup|-\<eta\>*s<rsub|k>>|)>>
  and is fast. The containing-window radius has an explicit bound. Put
  <math|r<around|(|x|)>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
  x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>, <math|q<around|(|x|)>=r<around|(|x|)><rsup|60>+1100>,
  and

  <\equation*>
    H<around|(|x|)>=1800*<around|(|1001*r<around|(|x|)>|)>+4*<around|(|1000*r<around|(|x|)>+2|)>,<space|2em>N<rsub|0>=4000001<rsup|4>.
  </equation*>

  The selected radius is <math|R<rsub|k>=R<around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)>>,
  where

  <\equation*>
    R<around|(|x|)>=max <around|{|H<around|(|x|)>+18+q<around|(|x|)>*<around|(|4*r<around|(|x|)>+2|)>+2*r<around|(|x|)>,<space|0.17em>2*<around|(|<around|(|N<rsub|0>+q<around|(|x|)>|)>*<around|(|4*r<around|(|x|)>+2|)>+10*r<around|(|x|)>|)>+1|}>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|x\<geqslant\>e>, <math|r<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>1+<around|(|log
  x|)><rsup|2>>, so this expression is at most a constant times
  <math|<around|(|1+log x|)><rsup|122>>. Also <math|log
  x\<leqslant\>32*x<rsup|1/32>>, whence <math|R<around|(|x|)>=O<around|(|x<rsup|122/32>|)>=o<around|(|x<rsup|4>|)>>.
  Consequently <math|R<rsub|k>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|4>>
  eventually and <math|a<rsub|k>*R<rsub|k>\<to\>0>. This radius includes the
  support enlargement and is distinct from the principal localization radius
  <math|\<varrho\><rsub|k>>: in particular
  <math|R<around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>H<around|(|x|)>+18>, so the local
  regularity neighborhood of radius <math|H<around|(|x|)>+1> lies in the
  window. The local symmetry, measurability and size bounds needed for the
  interior certificate remain inputs. The selected geometric tail is
  <math|<math-up|tail><rsub|k>=30*a<rsub|k>*<around|(|J<rsub|k>+5|)>>, where
  <math|J<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>0> is the schedule's integer clock. The schedule
  gives <math|a<rsub|k+1>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|k>/3> and
  <math|J<rsub|k+1>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|k>+1>. Hence

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|tail><rsub|k+1>\<leqslant\>10*a<rsub|k>*<around|(|J<rsub|k>+6|)>\<leqslant\><tfrac|2|3><math-up|tail><rsub|k>,
  </equation*>

  so the tail tends to zero geometrically. These two limits eventually give
  the geometric room inequality

  <\equation*>
    2*<around|(|a<rsub|k>*R<rsub|k>+<math-up|tail><rsub|k>|)>\<less\>a<rsub|h>.
  </equation*>

  It is this inequality that allows the localized observable to sit in the
  actual fixed test chamber before applying the purchased root bound. To
  connect that bound to the first assumption, put
  <math|f<rsub|k,u>=A<rsub|k,u>-O<rsub|k,u><around|(|v<rsub|k,u>|)>>. The
  purchase and localization say that, on an ultrafilter-large set, this
  difference is interior to the chamber at level <math|h> and its root-test
  size is at most <math|P<rsub|d,h,j><around|(|k|)>>. The separated spectator
  estimate supplies a constant <math|B<rsub|\<sigma\>>\<geqslant\>0>,
  independent of <math|k>, and a possibly smaller displacement radius, such
  that

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||\<cal-C\><rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|k,u>*<around|(|\<ell\>,a;f<rsub|k,u>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|\<sigma\>><math-up|root><rsub|h,u><around|(|f<rsub|k,u>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The spectator input places the <math|n> translated factors in distinct
  cells and bounds each root-test size by a common <math|A\<geqslant\>0>,
  uniformly in <math|k> and displacements of size at most <math|a<rsub|h>/8>.
  The rooted moment inequality gives the bound with
  <math|B<rsub|\<sigma\>>=A<rsup|n>>; its integrability and separation
  conditions are part of that input. To see the room for the local
  representatives, choose a finer level <math|j<rprime|'>\<geqslant\>h+1>
  with <math|24*a<rsub|j<rprime|'>>\<less\>a<rsub|h>> at which the spectator
  input supplies a common bound for its finitely many tokens and frames. It
  follows from the centered-representative hypothesis, using exponent and
  error allowances <math|1>, then summing the finitely many bounds and
  intersecting their eventual sets. The displacement and three fine-mesh
  widths together are less than <math|a<rsub|h>/4>, inside the coarse cell's
  one-third margin. For sufficiently large period the spectator addresses and
  the insertion address occupy distinct cells. By correlation linearity, the
  first assumption follows with <math|E<rsub|k>=P<rsub|d,h,j><around|(|k|)>>
  after intersecting the relevant large sets. The local representative and
  its root bound still use the spectator input. The cover-growth bound,
  purchase, localization, spectator estimate and the displayed mesh and
  integer-clock bounds remain inputs from the selected construction; this
  argument does not establish those purchases.

  For each fixed <math|k> and displacement, the moving-label limit makes the
  local correlation differ from its limit by at most <math|a<rsub|k>> on an
  ultrafilter-large set. Intersect that set with the root-price set and use
  the triangle inequality. The resulting recording error is at most
  <math|B<rsub|\<sigma\>>*E<rsub|k>+a<rsub|k>>, which is fast because
  <math|a<rsub|k>=e<rsup|-s<rsub|k>>>. This proves the corresponding instance
  of <eqref|eq:ex-branch-book-current>\U<eqref|eq:ex-branch-book-tensor> from
  the displayed root-price and point-limit inputs. It does not construct the
  underlying purchased root bound. The radius can be reduced so that all
  moved test configurations remain separated.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Localized current and raw trace
    decay><label|prop:ex-branch-raw-fast>Assume the recording bounds
    <eqref|eq:ex-branch-book-current>\U <eqref|eq:ex-branch-book-tensor>, the
    finite-volume linearity and identities
    <eqref|eq:ex-branch-recorded-difference> and
    <eqref|eq:ex-branch-recorded-traces>, and the test separation, continuity
    and grade-three unit properties <eqref|eq:ex-branch-test-properties> and
    <eqref|eq:ex-branch-grade-three-unit>. Then for each <math|d> the
    recorded difference <math|w<rsub|d,k>> is fast, and so are both
    unaveraged projected traces

    <\equation*>
      y<rsup|0><rsub|k>=<big|sum><rsub|a>\<pi\>*x<rsup|0><rsub|k>*<around|(|a,a|)>,<space|2em>y<rsup|-><rsub|k>=<big|sum><rsub|a>\<pi\>*x<rsup|-><rsub|k>*<around|(|a,a|)>.
    </equation*>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    We first record the finite-dimensional detection step. Choose finitely
    many <math|\<lambda\><rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|4>> whose joint map is
    injective. If for each such <math|\<sigma\>> linear functionals
    <math|\<Lambda\><rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|k>> converge pointwise to
    <math|\<lambda\><rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|4>>, finite dimensionality gives
    convergence in operator norm. If <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|int>>=<around|{|0|}>>
    the conclusion is immediate. Otherwise the joint limiting map has a
    positive minimum on the unit sphere. Consequently, for all large <math|k>
    and every <math|z\<in\>\<cal-F\><rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|int>>>,

    <\equation>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*max<rsub|\<sigma\>><around|\||\<Lambda\><rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|k>*z|\|>.<label|eq:ex-branch-moving-detector>
    </equation>

    Thus fast bounds on these finitely many moving tests imply a fast field
    bound, even when their exponents depend on <math|\<sigma\>>.

    Fix <math|d> and a test <math|\<sigma\>>. Decrease the decay exponent if
    necessary so that <math|0\<less\>\<eta\>\<less\>1> and
    <math|E<rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|d,k>=O<around|(|e<rsup|-\<eta\>*s<rsub|k>>|)>>.
    Choose <math|0\<less\>\<tau\>\<less\>\<eta\>> and set

    <\equation*>
      n<rsub|k>=<around*|\<lceil\>|e<rsup|<around|(|1-\<tau\>|)>*s<rsub|k>>|\<rceil\>>,<space|2em>h<rsub|k>=a<rsub|k>*n<rsub|k>.
    </equation*>

    The ceiling bound gives

    <\equation*>
      e<rsup|-\<tau\>*s<rsub|k>>\<leqslant\>h<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<tau\>*s<rsub|k>>+e<rsup|-s<rsub|k>>.
    </equation*>

    Thus <math|n<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>1>, <math|h<rsub|k>\<to\>0>, and
    <math|E<rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|d,k>/h<rsub|k>=O<around|(|e<rsup|-<around|(|\<eta\>-\<tau\>|)>*s<rsub|k>>|)>>.
    For large <math|k> all the points <math|j*a<rsub|k>*e<rsub|d>>,
    <math|0\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>n<rsub|k>>, lie inside the recording
    neighborhood. Define the moving functional

    <\equation*>
      \<Lambda\><rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|k>=<frac|1|n<rsub|k>>*<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|n<rsub|k>-1>L<rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|4>*<around|(|j*a<rsub|k>*e<rsub|d>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Continuity at zero implies <math|\<Lambda\><rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|k>*z\<to\>\<lambda\><rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|4>*z>
    for every fixed <math|z>: every term in the average lies within distance
    <math|h<rsub|k>> of zero. At each fixed <math|k> intersect the finitely
    many ultrafilter-large sets needed for the two endpoint current
    estimates, the <math|n<rsub|k>> difference estimates, and the exact
    difference identity. This intersection is nonempty. On it
    <eqref|eq:ex-branch-grade-three-unit> gives a common value
    <math|C<rsub|d,k><rsup|\<sigma\>>=L<rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|3><around|(|0|)>*v<rsub|d,k>>
    at both endpoints. Telescoping <eqref|eq:ex-branch-recorded-difference>
    and using the recording errors therefore yields

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<Lambda\><rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|k>*w<rsub|d,k>|\|>\<leqslant\>D<rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|d,k>+<frac|2*E<rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|d,k>|a<rsub|k>*n<rsub|k>>.
    </equation*>

    The right side is fast. This construction may use a different
    <math|n<rsub|k>> for each of the finitely many tests; applying
    <eqref|eq:ex-branch-moving-detector> proves that <math|w<rsub|d,k>> is
    fast. The finite intersection at fixed <math|k> is essential: no common
    infinite intersection of ultrafilter-large sets is used.

    For the false branch the first identity in
    <eqref|eq:ex-branch-recorded-traces> is exactly zero. For a fixed
    separating test, intersect the four sets in
    <eqref|eq:ex-branch-book-tensor> with that exact identity and use
    linearity. The triangle inequality gives
    <math|<around|\||\<lambda\><rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|4><around|(|y<rsup|-><rsub|k>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|a>H<rsup|\<sigma\>,-><rsub|a,k>>,
    which is fast. The fixed-test version of
    <eqref|eq:ex-branch-moving-detector> proves the false trace is fast.

    For the principal branch, the second identity in
    <eqref|eq:ex-branch-recorded-traces> gives the sum of the four forward
    differences. Intersect the four tensor-recording sets with the four
    difference-recording sets at <math|j=0> and with this identity. Linearity
    gives

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||\<lambda\><rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|4>*<around*|(|y<rsup|0><rsub|k>-<big|sum><rsub|d>w<rsub|d,k>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|a>H<rsup|\<sigma\>,0><rsub|a,k>+<big|sum><rsub|d>D<rsup|\<sigma\>><rsub|d,k>.
    </equation*>

    The detector bound makes the difference fast. The already proved fastness
    of each <math|w<rsub|d,k>> and finiteness of <math|I> now imply that
    <math|y<rsup|0><rsub|k>> is fast.
  </proof>

  Under the displayed fixed-scale root-price inputs, the same proof gives the
  more explicit bound

  <\equation>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|y<rsup|0><rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|y<rsup|-><rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>=O<around*|(|<around|(|1+s<rsub|k>|)><rsup|5>*e<rsup|-s<rsub|k>/8>|)>.<label|eq:ex-branch-raw-rate>
  </equation>

  Indeed, for the finitely many required tests and insertions, each recording
  error is <math|O<around|(|<around|(|1+s<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4>*e<rsup|-s<rsub|k>/8>|)>>:
  the fourth power of the affine clock has polynomial growth and the
  point-limit tolerance is <math|a<rsub|k>=e<rsup|-s<rsub|k>>>. In the
  sampling argument choose instead

  <\equation*>
    n<rsub|k>=<around*|\<lceil\>|<frac|e<rsup|s<rsub|k>>|1+s<rsub|k>>|\<rceil\>>,<space|2em>h<rsub|k>=a<rsub|k>*n<rsub|k>.
  </equation*>

  Then <math|<around|(|1+s<rsub|k>|)><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>h<rsub|k>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+s<rsub|k>|)><rsup|-1>+e<rsup|-s<rsub|k>>>,
  so <math|h<rsub|k>\<to\>0> and the endpoint error divided by
  <math|h<rsub|k>> is <math|O<around|(|<around|(|1+s<rsub|k>|)><rsup|5>*e<rsup|-s<rsub|k>/8>|)>>.
  The moving detectors and finite trace comparisons now give
  <eqref|eq:ex-branch-raw-rate>. In particular, the bound is
  <math|O<around|(|e<rsup|-\<kappa\>*s<rsub|k>>|)>> for every
  <math|0\<less\>\<kappa\>\<less\>1/8>. Its constants depend on the finite
  tests; the price and point-limit statements remain inputs.

  For the remaining weighted step, let <math|S<rsub|k,u>> be the
  finite-volume transport whose fixed-<math|k> separation limit is
  <math|B<rsub|k>>. With the same accumulated column logarithm
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|k>> as above, the selected calibration gives a fixed
  cutoff <math|k<rsub|0>> such that, for every <math|k\<geqslant\>k<rsub|0>>
  and every <math|v\<in\>E<rsub|4>>, the bound

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|S<rsub|k,u>*v|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>*e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|k>><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>
  </equation*>

  on an ultrafilter-large set of <math|u>. Here <math|C> depends only on the
  fixed finite-dimensional label coordinates. The closed-ball limit gives the
  same bound for <math|B<rsub|k>*v>, and taking the supremum over
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1> gives

  <\equation>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>*e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|k>><space|2em><text|for
    all sufficiently large >k.<label|eq:ex-branch-transport-bound>
  </equation>

  This passage fixes <math|k> and <math|v> before taking the separation
  limit; it does not require a common ultrafilter-large set for infinitely
  many <math|v>. The accumulated-column estimate gives
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|k>=o<around|(|s<rsub|k>|)>>, so
  <math|e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|k>>> is subexponential. The clock is affine in
  <math|s<rsub|k>>; since <math|s\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsup|-1>*e<rsup|\<epsilon\>*s>>
  for <math|s\<geqslant\>0> and <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>,
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>> and hence
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>> are subexponential as well. The
  finite-volume column calibration and fixed-scale transport limit are
  upstream inputs. The Crown, Eve and weighted-estimate chapters supply parts
  of the selected local prices under their construction hypotheses.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Principal and false trace washout><label|prop:ex-branch-washout>Under
    the recording and test inputs of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-branch-raw-fast>,
    the transport bound <eqref|eq:ex-branch-transport-bound> with
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|k>=o<around|(|s<rsub|k>|)>> and the affine clock,
    <eqref|eq:ex-branch-moment-input>, the chart identity
    <eqref|eq:ex-branch-chart-identity> and the stated projection
    equivariance, for each fixed <math|p\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and each
    <math|b\<in\><around|{|0,-|}>>,

    <\equation>
      <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|i>><rsup|<space|0.17em>p><space|0.17em><math-up|tr><around|(|<math-ss|P><rsub|i>t<rsup|b><rsub|k<rsub|i>>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0<space|1em><text|in
      >\<cal-F\><rsub|4><rsup|0>.<label|eq:ex-branch-washout>
    </equation>

    In particular the assertion holds for <math|p=1>. The true branch is
    treated separately below.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    First, diagonal symmetrization changes nothing. Let <math|\<cal-G\>> be
    the finite signed and real-structure symmetry set, and let
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|g>> denote the corresponding real-linear field action.
    In each term of the symmetry average the square of the tensor sign is one
    and the axis permutation is bijective. Using projection equivariance
    gives

    <\equation*>
      q<rsup|b><rsub|k>\<assign\><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>t<rsup|b><rsub|k>=<frac|1|<around|\||\<cal-G\>|\|>>*<big|sum><rsub|g\<in\>\<cal-G\>>\<frak-c\>*<space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|g><around|(|y<rsup|b><rsub|k>|)>.
    </equation*>

    These fixed real-linear maps are bounded in finite dimension. Thus a
    constant <math|C> independent of <math|k> satisfies
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|q<rsup|b><rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<around|\<\|\|\>|y<rsup|b><rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>>,
    and Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-branch-raw-fast> makes
    <math|q<rsup|b><rsub|k>> fast. It is centered, because each
    <math|t<rsup|b><rsub|k>*<around|(|a,c|)>> is centered and centering is
    linear.

    Choose a fixed linear representative <math|R:\<cal-F\><rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|int>>\<to\>E<rsub|4>>
    with <math|\<pi\>*R=<math-up|id>> and set
    <math|r<rsup|b><rsub|k>=R*q<rsup|b><rsub|k>>. It is also fast. For the
    fixed integer <math|p>, <eqref|eq:ex-branch-transport-bound> and the
    clock give subexponential growth of <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsup|p><rsub|k>*B<rsub|k>>
    and hence

    <\equation*>
      <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|<space|0.17em>p>*B<rsub|k>*r<rsup|b><rsub|k>\<longrightarrow\>0.
    </equation*>

    For example, if <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|r<rsup|b><rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*e<rsup|-\<eta\>*s<rsub|k>>>,
    use the subexponential bound with exponent <math|\<eta\>/2> on the
    combined factor. Composing with the cofinal sequence <math|k<rsub|i>>
    preserves the limit. The norms of <math|<math-ss|M><rsub|i>> are
    eventually bounded by <eqref|eq:ex-branch-moment-input>. Hence their
    images of the last sequence tend to zero. Since <math|q<rsup|b><rsub|k>>
    is centered and <math|\<pi\>*R*q<rsup|b><rsub|k>=q<rsup|b><rsub|k>>, the
    chart identity yields, for all sufficiently large <math|i>,

    <\equation*>
      <math-ss|M><rsub|i><around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|i>><rsup|<space|0.17em>p>*B<rsub|k<rsub|i>>*r<rsup|b><rsub|k<rsub|i>>|)>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|i>><rsup|<space|0.17em>p><math-ss|P><rsub|i>q<rsup|b><rsub|k<rsub|i>>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|i>><rsup|<space|0.17em>p><math-up|tr><around|(|<math-ss|P><rsub|i>t<rsup|b><rsub|k<rsub|i>>|)>.
    </equation*>

    This proves <eqref|eq:ex-branch-washout>. The estimate is for each fixed
    <math|p>; it is not a uniform assertion over a growing exponent.
  </proof>

  With the explicit root-price and point-limit inputs, the quantitative raw
  rate gives more: for each fixed <math|p\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and
  <math|b\<in\><around|{|0,-|}>>,

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|i>><rsup|<space|0.17em>p><math-up|tr><around|(|<math-ss|P><rsub|i>t<rsup|b><rsub|k<rsub|i>>|)>|\|>=O<space|-0.17em><around*|(|<around|(|1+s<rsub|k<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|p+6>*e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|k<rsub|i>>-s<rsub|k<rsub|i>>/8>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed, the raw bound contributes <math|<around|(|1+s|)><rsup|5>*e<rsup|-s/8>>,
  while <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsup|p><around|\<\|\|\>|B|\<\|\|\>>> is
  bounded by a constant times <math|<around|(|1+s|)><rsup|p+1>*e<rsup|\<ell\>>>;
  the moment maps are eventually bounded. Since
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|k>=o<around|(|s<rsub|k>|)>>, the right side is
  <math|O<around|(|e<rsup|-\<kappa\>*s<rsub|k<rsub|i>>>|)>> for every
  <math|0\<less\>\<kappa\>\<less\>1/8>. The constants can depend on <math|p>
  and <math|\<kappa\>>.

  The true branch instead has the unweighted limiting trace
  <math|4*\<cal-O\>> by Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-branch-moments>
  and <eqref|eq:ex-branch-three-traces>. This limit is nonzero, so the
  unweighted trace norm is eventually at least
  <math|2<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-O\>|\<\|\|\>>\<gtr\>0>. Since
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|i>>\<to\>\<infty\>>, the norm of the
  true-branch trace multiplied by <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|i>><rsup|<space|0.17em>p>>
  diverges for each fixed <math|p\<geqslant\>1>. These consequences do not
  construct the physical tensor or close the quantitative chart inputs.

  <subsection|The same-history physical stress
  transfer><label|sec:ex-lattice-trace-scribe-producer>

  This section proves the physical stress and flux passage used by the
  selected trace gallery from the explicit response, sensor, fourth-order,
  and weighted trace receipts displayed below. Their same-history producers
  are identified and applied below; the arguments also show how the resulting
  estimates combine. All limits below use one selected history, one
  ultrafilter, one hierarchy, and one scaling. In particular, the physical
  stress, the correction tensors, and the comparison observables are never
  reselected on separate subsequences. The quantitative estimates entering
  the construction include the paid response and comparison estimates from
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-weighted-estimates>,
  <reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-crown-buffer>, and
  <reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-eve-producer>. The additional sensor and
  branch receipts are stated explicitly below. Given these inputs, this
  section proves how the estimates produce the conserved tensor sequence and
  transfer its physical normalization to the selected table.

  Write <math|\<kappa\>=s<rsub|G>\<gtr\>0> for the slope of the actual clock.
  The chosen charged response uses this same constant: its target payment is
  <math|-12*s<rsub|G>>, and division by the flux normalization factor
  <math|12> gives <math|-s<rsub|G>>. Thus the principal Ward limit below and
  the clock slope have exactly the same <math|\<kappa\>>. The final
  logarithmic trace coefficient is independent of its numerical value, as
  shown below; no identification with a separately normalized one-loop
  constant is needed here. Let <math|1> denote the unit field of the
  reference grade-four table, and let <math|\<cal-O\>> be the normalized
  curvature-square field of the reference table: the image in that table of
  one quarter of the sum, over all ordered pairs <math|<around|(|a,b|)>> of
  coordinate directions, of the labels representing the color inner products
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|a*b>,F<rsub|a*b>|\<rangle\>>> of the
  curvature with itself. It is the field denoted <math|\<cal-O\>> in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-trace-branch-moments> and <math|O> in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:trace-asymptotic>. It has expectation zero
  in the reference table, it is nonzero, and it is not a multiple of the unit
  field. The physical coefficient is <math|d<rsub|2>=-\<kappa\>/4>.

  <subsubsection|The two-direction conserved correction>

  Let <math|E> be the finite-dimensional real space of centered,
  signed-equivariant, spin-symmetric grade-four tensors for the selected
  table (before imposing the divergence equation), let
  <math|V<rsub|<math-up|int>>> be the real space of grade-five vector arrays
  of the selected table in which divergences lie, let <math|V> be the
  finite-dimensional response space, which is the grade-five vector table of
  the reference table, and let <math|\<cal-B\>> be the grade-five profile
  space. Let <math|D:E\<to\>V<rsub|<math-up|int>>> be the divergence map. Its
  codomain belongs to the selected table, whereas the comparison and response
  maps below take values in the reference space <math|V>. The conserved-line
  construction in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-stress-normalization>,
  applied to the completed maps of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-selected-trace-gallery>,
  gives <math|dim ker D=1> by <eqref|eq:stress-physical-line>. Its hypotheses
  are checked for these same maps in the normalized-energy application below:
  coherent unit-preserving maps, uniform short-test convergence, and the
  reference rank calculation. In particular, rank one does not require the
  correction sequence about to be constructed. The interacting divergence
  remains fixed; the comparison varies the optical map, and its positive
  scale and invertible chart preserve the kernel.

  The construction solves the correction equations at each large index,
  rather than taking an unspecified kernel vector. Let
  <math|<math-ss|K><rsub|i>:E\<to\>V> be the selected comparison map, let
  <math|s<rsub|i>:\<cal-B\>\<to\>V> be the paid response map, and let
  <math|L<rsub|i>,\<Omega\><rsub|i>,\<Delta\><rsub|i>> be the principal and
  two correction directions. The correction map is

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|S><rsub|i><around|(|c<rsub|0>,c<rsub|1>|)>=c<rsub|0>*\<Omega\><rsub|i>+c<rsub|1>*\<Delta\><rsub|i>,<space|2em><math-ss|B><rsub|i>=<math-ss|K><rsub|i><math-ss|S><rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|q<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> be the selected arch scale at the summit level
  (the quantity <math|\<zeta\><around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>> of the clock paragraph
  below; it is compared with the branch unit's effective coupling in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-scribe-normalization-ratio>) and
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|i>> its positive response-size price; these are the
  scales in the paid bounds below. The optical comparison identity, which is
  an input here, states that on the selected tail the comparison map is the
  divergence followed by the scaled inverse chart: for every <math|Q\<in\>E>
  and every axis <math|a>,

  <\equation>
    <around|(|<math-ss|K><rsub|i>Q|)><rsub|a>=q<rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|5>*<space|0.17em>U<rsub|i,5><rsup|-1><around*|(|<around|(|D*Q|)><rsub|a>|)>,<label|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-optical-identity>
  </equation>

  where <math|r<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> is the chart radius and <math|U<rsub|i,5>>
  is the grade-five chart isomorphism from reference fields to fields of the
  selected table. Since <math|q<rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|5>\<gtr\>0> and
  <math|U<rsub|i,5>> is invertible, <math|<math-ss|K><rsub|i>Q=0> if and only
  if <math|D*Q=0> on that tail. For <math|a=0,1>, the corrected response
  estimates compare the corresponding column with the same-history linear
  response on a paid forecast <math|w<rsub|i><rsup|a>\<in\>\<cal-B\>>:

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|B><rsub|i>e<rsub|a>-s<rsub|i><around|(|w<rsub|i><rsup|a>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<longrightarrow\>0,<space|2em>w<rsub|i><rsup|a>\<longrightarrow\>v<rsub|a>,<space|2em>s<rsub|i>\<longrightarrow\><math-ss|V>.
  </equation*>

  The profiles <math|v<rsub|0>,v<rsub|1>> are the two free correction
  directions, and <math|<math-ss|W><around|(|c<rsub|0>,c<rsub|1>|)>=c<rsub|0>*v<rsub|0>+c<rsub|1>*v<rsub|1>>.
  Hence

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|B><rsub|i>\<longrightarrow\><math-ss|B><rsub|\<infty\>>=<math-ss|VW>,<space|2em><math-ss|VW><around|(|c<rsub|0>,c<rsub|1>|)>=c<rsub|0><math-ss|V><around|(|v<rsub|0>|)>+c<rsub|1><math-ss|V><around|(|v<rsub|1>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|<math-ss|V>> is the free vector-response map and
  <math|<math-ss|W>> is the two-column reference correction. Indeed, for each
  <math|a>, the displayed response estimate and continuity of the limiting
  response give <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|i>e<rsub|a>\<to\><math-ss|V><around|(|v<rsub|a>|)>>.
  Since the domain has basis <math|e<rsub|0>,e<rsub|1>>, convergence of both
  columns implies operator-norm convergence. The map
  <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|\<infty\>>> is an isomorphism. To see this, use the
  real coordinates <math|w=<around|(|w<rsub|0>,w<rsub|1>,w<rsub|2>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|3>>
  that parametrize <math|\<cal-B\>> bijectively and in which the branch
  profiles are defined. In these coordinates

  <\equation*>
    v<rsub|0>=<around|(|0,0,1|)>,<space|2em>v<rsub|1>=<around|(|0,<tfrac|1|4>,0|)>,
  </equation*>

  and two facts from the reference computation of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-free-rank> are used as
  inputs: <math|<math-ss|V>> maps <math|\<cal-B\>> onto <math|V>, and its
  kernel is the line spanned by the equation-of-motion direction, whose
  coordinates are <math|\<epsilon\>=<around|(|1,-<tfrac|1|4>,1|)>>. If
  <math|<math-ss|VW><around|(|c<rsub|0>,c<rsub|1>|)>=0>, then
  <math|c<rsub|0>*v<rsub|0>+c<rsub|1>*v<rsub|1>=\<gamma\>*\<epsilon\>> for
  some real <math|\<gamma\>>; the coordinate <math|w<rsub|0>> gives
  <math|\<gamma\>=0>, then <math|w<rsub|2>> gives <math|c<rsub|0>=0> and
  <math|w<rsub|1>> gives <math|c<rsub|1>=0>. So <math|<math-ss|VW>> is
  injective. It is onto: an arbitrary element of <math|V> is
  <math|<math-ss|V><around|(|w|)>> for some <math|w>, and

  <\equation*>
    w-w<rsub|0>*\<epsilon\>=<around|(|w<rsub|2>-w<rsub|0>|)>*v<rsub|0>+<around|(|4*w<rsub|1>+w<rsub|0>|)>*v<rsub|1>,
  </equation*>

  so <math|<math-ss|V><around|(|w|)>=<math-ss|VW><around|(|w<rsub|2>-w<rsub|0>,<space|0.17em>4*w<rsub|1>+w<rsub|0>|)>>
  because <math|<math-ss|V><around|(|\<epsilon\>|)>=0>. In finite dimension,
  if <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|B><rsub|i>-<math-ss|B><rsub|\<infty\>>|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>1/<around|(|2<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|B><rsub|\<infty\>><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>|)>>,
  then for every <math|c>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|B><rsub|i>c|\<\|\|\>>\<geqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|B><rsub|\<infty\>><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|-1>\<\|\|\>c\<\|\|\>-<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|<math-ss|B><rsub|i>-<math-ss|B><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>*c|\<\|\|\>>\<geqslant\><frac|<around|\<\|\|\>|c|\<\|\|\>>|2<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|B><rsub|\<infty\>><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>>.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|i>> is injective and hence bijective, and its
  inverse norms are uniformly bounded on a tail. Let
  <math|X<rsub|i>=q<rsub|i><math-ss|K><rsub|i><around|(|L<rsub|i>|)>> be the
  uncorrected forcing. The paid response-size estimate gives
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*\<rho\><rsub|i>>,
  where <math|\<rho\><rsub|i>> is the positive response-size price. Define

  <\equation*>
    c<rsub|i>=-<math-ss|B><rsub|i><rsup|-1>X<rsub|i>,<space|2em>T<rsub|i>=q<rsub|i>*L<rsub|i>+<math-ss|S><rsub|i><around|(|c<rsub|i>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Then <math|<math-ss|K><rsub|i><around|(|T<rsub|i>|)>=0>, and the optical
  comparison identity <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-optical-identity> gives
  <math|D*T<rsub|i>=0>: on the selected tail,
  <math|<math-ss|K><rsub|i><around|(|Q|)>=0> is equivalent to <math|D*Q=0>
  for every <math|Q\<in\>E>. The inverse bound gives
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|c<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rprime|'>*\<rho\><rsub|i>>.

  The scalar calibration uses the same paid functional
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|i>\<in\>V<rsup|\<ast\>>> that controls the response
  price. Let <math|\<Lambda\>\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsup|\<ast\>>> be the free Ward
  functional. At chart level <math|k<rsub|i>>, write <math|t<rsub|i>> for its
  positive sensor scale and <math|z<rsub|i>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|i>>>
  for its clock weight. The price comparison gives
  <math|t<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>, <math|\<rho\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>z<rsub|i>>.
  The sensor inputs include a constant <math|C<rsub|\<mu\>>> with
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|\<mu\>>>
  eventually, and, for every <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>, the eventual estimate

  <\equation*>
    t<rsub|i>*<around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|i>*<around|(|s<rsub|i>*y|)>-\<Lambda\><around|(|y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><space|1em><text|for
    all >y\<in\>\<cal-B\>.
  </equation*>

  These sensor inputs are the conclusion of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:trace-prosper-sensor>. We use it under the
  following identification of its notation with the present one. Its spaces
  <math|E> and <math|H> are <math|\<cal-B\>> and <math|V>; its sensor maps
  <math|s<rsub|i>> are the present <math|s<rsub|i>>, defined by the selected
  chart formula in <eqref|eq:trace-sensor-selected-formulas> and not merely
  maps with the same limit; its free projection <math|f> is
  <math|<math-ss|V>>; its Ward functional <math|\<rho\>> is
  <math|\<Lambda\>>; its postselected level <math|n<rsub|i>> is the present
  <math|k<rsub|i>>, the summit index of the frame level of the <math|i>th
  chart; and its weight <math|\<frak-a\><rsub|i>> is <math|t<rsub|i>>. In the
  notation of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-weighted-estimates>,
  with <math|p<rsub|i>=z<rsub|i>>, one has
  <math|t<rsub|i>=A<rsub|1/5><around|(|i|)>> and
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|i>=A<rsub|1/6><around|(|i|)>>, and <math|q<rsub|i>> is
  the arch scale <math|b<rsub|i>> at the level <math|k<rsub|i>>. The required
  arch ratios are verified below from the running estimate and the
  first-crossing schedule. The additional schedule and column bounds in the
  sensor proposition's hypotheses (S1) and (S6) follow from
  <eqref|eq:trace-extra-stage-payment>, taking <math|\<frak-C\><rsub|j>> to
  dominate the grade-five column parameters of the fixed stage profiles.
  These are the same column budgets in the raw-step estimate, not operator
  norms of a coupling-dependent response. The stage entry is purchased before
  selecting the history and hence before its charts. For its marked vector
  use the actual medium <math|m<rsub|k<rsub|i>>=q<rsub|i><rsup|-1><math-ss|q><rsub|k<rsub|i>>-<math-ss|w><rsub|k<rsub|i>>>,
  with the chosen and reference responses of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-weighted-estimates>. That
  section's weighted assembly gives <math|m<rsub|k<rsub|i>>\<to\>x> and
  <math|t<rsub|i>*\<Lambda\><around|(|m<rsub|k<rsub|i>>|)>\<to\>0>; its
  chart-field decay gives <math|z<rsub|i>*s<rsub|i><around|(|m<rsub|k<rsub|i>>|)>\<to\>0>.
  Since <math|t<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>z<rsub|i>>, the latter implies
  <math|t<rsub|i>*s<rsub|i><around|(|m<rsub|k<rsub|i>>|)>\<to\>0>. These are
  exactly the three marked-vector limits of the sensor proposition, with the
  same normalized chart map. The selected-lift formulas there use the same
  finite-volume inverse column and the same fixed-level ultrafilter limit as
  this construction; thus their identity <math|P*Y<rsub|i>=X<rsub|i>> holds
  before and after projection, not merely asymptotically. Apply its
  initial-level price and transport price to this lift, taking the finite
  intersection of the levelwise ultrafilter sets between the frame level and
  its summit. This is legitimate at each fixed chart index; no tail uniform
  over all chart indices is required. Its convergence-of-selected-lifts lemma
  therefore applies to the same lift. The actual tested-price and raw-step
  bounds (S4) and (S5) for this lift are proved in the tested-price and
  adjacent-column arguments below. The identity and finite intersection only
  synchronize them. With these quantitative bounds, the sensor proposition
  supplies the bounded functionals <math|\<mu\><rsub|i>> and the estimate
  uniform over every <math|y\<in\>\<cal-B\>> used above. We spell out how
  these conclusions give the three limits needed for calibration. Let
  <math|Y<rsub|i>> be the principal forecast. The paid response comparison,
  penumbra estimate, and principal Ward limit are

  <\equation>
    q<rsub|i><math-ss|K><rsub|i><around|(|L<rsub|i>|)>-s<rsub|i><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|Y<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*\<rho\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>C*t<rsub|i>,<space|2em>\<Lambda\><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>\<longrightarrow\>-\<kappa\>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-principal-response-input>
  </equation>

  The first convergence and the uniform bound on <math|\<mu\><rsub|i>> imply
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|i><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>-\<mu\><rsub|i><around|(|s<rsub|i><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>|)>\<to\>0>.
  For any <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>, the sensor estimate then gives eventually

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<mu\><rsub|i><around|(|s<rsub|i><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>|)>-\<Lambda\><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><around|\<\|\|\>|Y<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>/t<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>C*\<delta\>.
  </equation*>

  Taking the limit superior and then <math|\<delta\>*\<downarrow\>*0> proves
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|i><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>-\<kappa\>>; no norm
  convergence of the principal forecast is needed.

  For a correction branch <math|P\<in\><around|{|\<Omega\>,\<Delta\>|}>>, let
  <math|U<rsub|i><rsup|P>\<in\>\<cal-B\>> be its finite-volume profile and
  <math|v<rsub|P>\<in\>\<cal-B\>> its reference profile. The comparison and
  Ward estimates needed are

  <\equation>
    U<rsub|i><rsup|P>\<to\>v<rsub|P>,<space|2em>z<rsub|i>*<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|K><rsub|i><around|(|P<rsub|i>|)>-s<rsub|i><around|(|U<rsub|i><rsup|P>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<to\>0,<space|2em>t<rsub|i>*<around|\||\<Lambda\><around|(|U<rsub|i><rsup|P>|)>-\<Lambda\><around|(|v<rsub|P>|)>|\|>\<to\>0,<label|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-correction-response-input>
  </equation>

  where <math|P<rsub|i>> denotes the corresponding correction tensor. Since
  <math|U<rsub|i><rsup|P>> is bounded, the uniform sensor estimate implies
  <math|t<rsub|i>*<around|\||\<mu\><rsub|i><around|(|s<rsub|i><around|(|U<rsub|i><rsup|P>|)>|)>-\<Lambda\><around|(|U<rsub|i><rsup|P>|)>|\|>\<to\>0>.
  Using <math|t<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>z<rsub|i>> and the uniform operator bound
  for <math|\<mu\><rsub|i>> in the middle estimate of
  (<reference|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-correction-response-input>) gives

  <\equation*>
    t<rsub|i>*<around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|i><around|(|<math-ss|K><rsub|i><around|(|P<rsub|i>|)>|)>-\<Lambda\><around|(|v<rsub|P>|)>|\|>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  The reference Ward values are <math|\<Lambda\><around|(|v<rsub|\<Omega\>>|)>=0>
  and <math|\<Lambda\><around|(|v<rsub|\<Delta\>>|)>=-4>. Thus the principal
  and two correction limits are now established from the explicitly stated
  paid inputs:

  <\equation>
    \<mu\><rsub|i><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>-\<kappa\>,<space|2em>t<rsub|i>*\<mu\><rsub|i><around|(|<math-ss|K><rsub|i>\<Omega\><rsub|i>|)>\<to\>0,<space|2em>t<rsub|i>*<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|i><around|(|<math-ss|K><rsub|i>\<Delta\><rsub|i>|)>+4|)>\<to\>0.<label|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-sensor-limits>
  </equation>

  Applying <math|\<mu\><rsub|i>> to the exact cancellation
  <math|X<rsub|i>+<math-ss|B><rsub|i>c<rsub|i>=0> gives

  <\equation*>
    \<mu\><rsub|i><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>+c<rsub|i><rsup|0>*\<mu\><rsub|i><around|(|<math-ss|K><rsub|i>\<Omega\><rsub|i>|)>+c<rsub|i><rsup|1>*\<mu\><rsub|i><around|(|<math-ss|K><rsub|i>\<Delta\><rsub|i>|)>=0.
  </equation*>

  Set <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i>=t<rsub|i>*\<mu\><rsub|i><around|(|<math-ss|K><rsub|i>\<Omega\><rsub|i>|)>>
  and <math|\<beta\><rsub|i>=t<rsub|i>*<around|(|\<mu\><rsub|i><around|(|<math-ss|K><rsub|i>\<Delta\><rsub|i>|)>+4|)>>.
  Since <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|c<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*\<rho\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>C*t<rsub|i>>,
  both <math|c<rsub|i><rsup|0>/t<rsub|i>> and
  <math|c<rsub|i><rsup|1>/t<rsub|i>> are bounded. The exact equation rewrites
  as

  <\equation*>
    0=\<mu\><rsub|i><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>-4*c<rsub|i><rsup|1>+<frac|c<rsub|i><rsup|0>|t<rsub|i>>*\<alpha\><rsub|i>+<frac|c<rsub|i><rsup|1>|t<rsub|i>>*\<beta\><rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  The last two terms tend to zero by (<reference|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-sensor-limits>),
  so <math|\<mu\><rsub|i><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>-\<kappa\>> implies

  <\equation*>
    c<rsub|i><rsup|1>\<longrightarrow\>-<frac|\<kappa\>|4>.
  </equation*>

  This is the stable-inversion and coefficient-extraction argument of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-producer>.

  The correction is built from three paid tensor families, the same at every
  index: a principal tensor <math|L<rsub|i>> and two correction tensors
  <math|\<Omega\><rsub|i>,\<Delta\><rsub|i>>. These families and the
  coefficients <math|c<rsub|i><rsup|0>,c<rsub|i><rsup|1>> below are the
  tensors of that selected construction. Their field and trace properties
  follow from the same-history branch application below. Let
  <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|i>> be the scaled pullback of a stress tensor, and put
  <math|\<cal-Y\><rsub|i>=q<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*\<cal-P\><rsub|i>>. The scaled
  pullback is the chart map multiplied by its positive crown normalization;
  by definition, <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|i>=q<rsub|i>*\<cal-Y\><rsub|i>> on
  every tensor. Here <math|q<rsub|i>=\<zeta\><around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>> is the
  arch scale at the selected level (see the clock paragraph below), and the
  crown normalization is <math|q<rsub|i>> times the fourth power of the chart
  radius. The defining-limit identification <eqref|eq:scribe-same-arch> below
  identifies <math|q<rsub|i>> with the branch unit's effective coupling at
  <math|k<rsub|i>>; the subsequent ratio lemma also records the transfer for
  asymptotically equal normalizations. The defining corrected tensor is

  <\equation>
    T<rsub|i>=q<rsub|i>*L<rsub|i>+c<rsub|i><rsup|0>*\<Omega\><rsub|i>+c<rsub|i><rsup|1>*\<Delta\><rsub|i>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-corrected-tensor>
  </equation>

  For the three tensor sequences <math|Q<rsub|i>\<in\><around|{|L<rsub|i>,\<Omega\><rsub|i>,\<Delta\><rsub|i>|}>>,
  let their reference branches be <math|\<nu\><rsub|L>=0>,
  <math|\<nu\><rsub|\<Omega\>>=->, and <math|\<nu\><rsub|\<Delta\>>=+>. Let
  <math|W<rsub|i><rsup|Q>*<around|(|a,b|)>> be the ordered grade-four label
  for the two tensor indices <math|a,b> obtained by the finite-volume
  response of the corresponding sequence. These ordered labels are the
  entries of a finite array; no spatial sum is hidden in this notation. Let
  <math|<math-ss|M><rsub|i>> be the finite-volume fourth-order coefficient
  map, obtained by applying the centered chart pullback to a grade-four
  label. It is a continuous linear map <math|<math-ss|M><rsub|i>:\<cal-L\><rsub|4>\<to\>\<cal-F\><rsub|4>>,
  not a scalar moment matrix. Each <math|W<rsub|i><rsup|Q>*<around|(|a,b|)>>
  belongs to <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|4>>. For every sufficiently large <math|i>
  (so the selected level exceeds the fixed reference cutoff), symmetrizing
  the two ordered indices gives the chart formula

  <\equation>
    <around|[|\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|Q<rsub|i>|)>|]><rsub|a*b>=<frac|1|2>*<around*|(|<math-ss|M><rsub|i><around|(|W<rsub|i><rsup|Q>*<around|(|a,b|)>|)>+<math-ss|M><rsub|i><around|(|W<rsub|i><rsup|Q>*<around|(|b,a|)>|)>|)>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-phase-formula>
  </equation>

  The selected coefficient convergence gives
  <math|W<rsub|i><rsup|Q>*<around|(|a,b|)>\<to\>W<rsub|\<nu\><rsub|Q>>*<around|(|a,b|)>>,
  and the fourth-order maps converge in operator norm,
  <math|<math-ss|M><rsub|i>\<to\><math-ss|M><rsub|\<infty\>>>; for the
  present normalization this uses <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|i>\<to\>1> of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-scribe-normalization-ratio>, since
  <math|<math-ss|M><rsub|i>> is <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>> times
  the branch unit's moment map. Thus, entry by entry in the finite stress
  array,

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|Q<rsub|i>|)>\<longrightarrow\>\<cal-B\><rsub|\<nu\><rsub|Q>>,<space|2em><around|(|\<cal-B\><rsub|\<nu\>>|)><rsub|a*b>\<assign\><frac|1|2>*<around*|(|<math-ss|M><rsub|\<infty\>><around|(|W<rsub|\<nu\>>*<around|(|a,b|)>|)>+<math-ss|M><rsub|\<infty\>><around|(|W<rsub|\<nu\>>*<around|(|b,a|)>|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  This follows directly from continuity of the finite bilinear expression in
  (<reference|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-phase-formula>); no limit is interchanged
  with an infinite sum. The explicit free tensor calculation identifies these
  arrays with the reference material tensors. Write

  <\equation*>
    M<rsub|0>=\<cal-B\><rsub|0>,<space|2em>M<rsub|\<Omega\>>=\<cal-B\><rsub|->,<space|2em>M<rsub|1>=\<cal-B\><rsub|+>.
  </equation*>

  The false-branch tensor need not vanish; it is its coefficient
  <math|c<rsub|i><rsup|0>/q<rsub|i>\<to\>0> that removes it from the
  normalized field limit. The three branch limits are on the same history.

  The three branch limits, and the trace statements used below, are the
  conclusions of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-trace-branch-moments>,
  under the following identification. The tensors are
  <math|L<rsub|i>=t<rsup|0><rsub|k<rsub|i>>>,
  <math|\<Omega\><rsub|i>=t<rsup|-><rsub|k<rsub|i>>> and
  <math|\<Delta\><rsub|i>=t<rsup|+><rsub|k<rsub|i>>>, at the same level
  <math|k<rsub|i>> as above. That section uses the map
  <math|<math-ss|P><rsub|i>=\<omega\><rsub|i>*U<rsub|i><rsup|-1>> with
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|i>=\<beta\><rsub|Z><around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>>,
  where <math|\<beta\><rsub|Z>> is its effective coupling, whereas the
  present crown normalization uses the arch scale <math|q<rsub|i>> in place
  of <math|\<beta\><rsub|Z><around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>>. Both normalizations use
  the same fixed-level limit of the running coupling. Indeed the arch is
  constructed by taking, for each level <math|j>, the limit of
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,j>> along the selected ultrafilter; this defines
  <math|\<zeta\><around|(|j|)>>. The branch unit's
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|Z><around|(|j|)>> is the limit of this same real-valued
  function along this same ultrafilter. Uniqueness of a limit in
  <math|\<bbb-R\>> therefore gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:scribe-same-arch>\<beta\><rsub|Z><around|(|j|)>=\<zeta\><around|(|j|)><space|2em><text|for
    every >j.
  </equation>

  No further extraction or adjustment of the coupling is involved. With

  <\equation*>
    \<vartheta\><rsub|i>=<frac|\<beta\><rsub|Z><around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>|q<rsub|i>>\<gtr\>0,
  </equation*>

  one has <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|i>=1> for the actual constructed arch. More
  generally, the scaled pullback <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|i>> is the entrywise
  application of <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|i><rsup|-1><math-ss|P><rsub|i>>, and
  <math|<math-ss|M><rsub|i>> is its centered moment map, which is
  <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>> times that of
  <math|<math-ss|P><rsub|i>>. The ordered labels are
  <math|W<rsub|i><rsup|Q>=W<rsup|b><rsub|k<rsub|i>>> for the branch
  <math|b=\<nu\><rsub|Q>>, and <math|M<rsub|0>=M<around|(|w<rsup|0>|)>>,
  <math|M<rsub|\<Omega\>>=M<around|(|w<rsup|->|)>>,
  <math|M<rsub|1>=M<around|(|w<rsup|+>|)>>.
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-branch-moments> gives the three field
  limits in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-field-receipts>;
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-branch-washout> with exponent
  <math|p=1> gives the weighted trace limits for the principal and the false
  branch that are used below, and gives nothing for the true branch; and the
  trace formula <eqref|eq:ex-branch-three-traces> gives
  <math|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>M<rsub|1>=4*\<cal-O\>>. Their recording,
  transport and chart estimates concern exactly this supplied history: at
  fixed <math|i>, use the same finite-volume tensors and the same ultrafilter
  before passing to <math|i\<to\>\<infty\>>. The three branch choices and
  sixteen tensor entries form a finite set, so their eventual identities and
  estimates hold on one common tail. This finite intersection does not prove
  the actual branch coefficient bias or local-current identities, nor the
  three recording estimates in <eqref|eq:ex-branch-book-current>,
  <eqref|eq:ex-branch-book-difference>, and <eqref|eq:ex-branch-book-tensor>.
  Those estimates are required at these actual labels. No separate history is
  selected for a branch. The finite-array argument written out above repeats
  the proof of the first of them under the same hypotheses, together with
  <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|i>\<to\>1> of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-scribe-normalization-ratio>.

  Those conclusions are stated for <math|<math-ss|P><rsub|i>>. They transfer
  to <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|i>=\<vartheta\><rsub|i><rsup|-1><math-ss|P><rsub|i>>
  because of the following lemma.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The two normalizations agree asymptotically><label|lem:ex-lattice-scribe-normalization-ratio>Use
    the scale ratio <math|\<beta\><rsub|Z><around|(|j|)>/<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<to\>1>
    from Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-trace-branch-moments> (proved in
    its scale-ratio paragraph, after the display
    <eqref|eq:ex-branch-lift-beta-ratio>, from its running estimate and
    fixed-scale limit), and the first clock limit
    <math|\<zeta\><around|(|j|)>/<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<to\>1> of
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-clock-inputs>, proved below from
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:rank-trace-running>. Here
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>> is the same clock coupling in both
    statements: the branch unit and this section use the hatted coupling of
    the one selected clock. Then <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|i>\<to\>1>.
    Consequently, for <math|Q<rsub|i>\<in\><around|{|L<rsub|i>,\<Omega\><rsub|i>,\<Delta\><rsub|i>|}>>
    with reference tensors <math|M<rsub|0>,M<rsub|\<Omega\>>,M<rsub|1>>,

    <\gather*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-ss|P><rsub|i><around|(|Q<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>M\<Longrightarrow\>\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|Q<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>M,<space|2em><math-ss|P><rsub|i><around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|i>|)>\<to\>M<rsub|1>\<Longrightarrow\><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|i>|)>\<to\>4*\<cal-O\>,>>|<row|<cell|z<rsub|i><math-up|tr><math-ss|P><rsub|i><around|(|Q<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>0\<Longrightarrow\>z<rsub|i><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|Q<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>0.>>>>
    </gather*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The summit index <math|k<rsub|i>> is a level not below the frame level
    <math|j<rsub|i>>, and <math|j<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>, so
    <math|k<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>. Hence both ratio limits hold along
    <math|j=k<rsub|i>>. The quantities <math|\<beta\><rsub|Z><around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>>,
    <math|q<rsub|i>=\<zeta\><around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>> and
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|i>>> are positive, and

    <\equation*>
      \<vartheta\><rsub|i>=<frac|\<beta\><rsub|Z><around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>/<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|i>>|\<zeta\><around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>/<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|i>>>\<longrightarrow\><frac|1|1>=1.
    </equation*>

    In particular <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>\<to\>1>, and
    <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>2> for all large <math|i>.
    Entrywise, <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|Q<rsub|i>|)>=\<vartheta\><rsub|i><rsup|-1><math-ss|P><rsub|i><around|(|Q<rsub|i>|)>>
    in the finite product of copies of <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|4><rsup|0>>; the
    product of a convergent scalar sequence and a convergent vector sequence
    converges to the product of the limits, which gives the first
    implication. The trace is linear and continuous on that finite product,
    so if <math|<math-ss|P><rsub|i><around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|i>|)>\<to\>M<rsub|1>>,
    then <math|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|i>|)>=\<vartheta\><rsub|i><rsup|-1><math-up|tr><math-ss|P><rsub|i><around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|i>|)>\<to\><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>M<rsub|1>=4*\<cal-O\>>
    by the trace formula of the branch unit. Finally
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|z<rsub|i><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|Q<rsub|i>|)>|\<\|\|\>>=\<vartheta\><rsub|i><rsup|-1><around|\<\|\|\>|z<rsub|i><math-up|tr><math-ss|P><rsub|i><around|(|Q<rsub|i>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2<around|\<\|\|\>|z<rsub|i><math-up|tr><math-ss|P><rsub|i><around|(|Q<rsub|i>|)>|\<\|\|\>>>
    for large <math|i>, which tends to zero when the right side does.
  </proof>

  Since <math|z<rsub|i>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|i>>>, the washout
  limits of the branch unit with <math|p=1> are exactly the hypotheses
  <math|z<rsub|i><math-up|tr><math-ss|P><rsub|i><around|(|Q<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>0>
  for <math|Q<rsub|i>\<in\><around|{|L<rsub|i>,\<Omega\><rsub|i>|}>>. By the
  lemma, the field limits and the exponent-one principal and false trace
  limits used below hold for <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|i>> on this same history.
  For the actual arch this also follows directly from
  <eqref|eq:scribe-same-arch>, since <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|i>=<math-ss|P><rsub|i>>.
  The exact identity for <math|\<cal-Y\><rsub|i><around|(|T<rsub|i>|)>> below
  uses only <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|i>=q<rsub|i>*\<cal-Y\><rsub|i>> and
  linearity, so it is unaffected.

  These are the field receipts used below:

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|L<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<longrightarrow\>M<rsub|0>,>|<cell|\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|\<Omega\><rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<longrightarrow\>M<rsub|\<Omega\>>,>|<cell|\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<longrightarrow\>M<rsub|1>,>|<cell|<frac|\<rho\><rsub|i>|q<rsub|i>>>|<cell|\<longrightarrow\>0,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-field-receipts>>>>>
  </align>

  The displayed ratio is the selected-scale washout input: the response price
  is bounded by the one-sixth subpower clock price, whose ratio to the
  effective coupling tends to zero along this scale. Since <math|M<rsub|0>>
  is the prescribed material tensor and <math|<around|\||c<rsub|i><rsup|0>|\|>+<around|\||c<rsub|i><rsup|1>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*\<rho\><rsub|i>>,
  it follows that <math|c<rsub|i><rsup|a>/q<rsub|i>\<to\>0> for <math|a=0,1>.
  Linearity gives the exact identity

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-Y\><rsub|i><around|(|T<rsub|i>|)>=\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|L<rsub|i>|)>+<frac|c<rsub|i><rsup|0>|q<rsub|i>>*\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|\<Omega\><rsub|i>|)>+<frac|c<rsub|i><rsup|1>|q<rsub|i>>*\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|i>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The principal, false-correction, and true-correction rescaled tensors
  converge to <math|M<rsub|0>,M<rsub|\<Omega\>>,M<rsub|1>>, respectively. In
  particular the two correction phase tensors are bounded, and the exact
  identity proves

  <\equation>
    \<cal-Y\><rsub|i><around|(|T<rsub|i>|)>\<longrightarrow\>M<rsub|0>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-field-limit>
  </equation>

  For the scalar trace, the true correction profile is the true material
  correction, whose diagonal trace is <math|4*\<cal-O\>> by the trace formula
  of the branch-moment unit recalled above: the diagonal sum is
  <math|4*<around|(|0+4\<cdot\><frac|1|4>|)>*\<cal-O\>=4*\<cal-O\>>. We also
  use the scale bounds <math|q<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>2*z<rsub|i>> and
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>z<rsub|i>> and the
  limits

  <\equation*>
    z<rsub|i><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|L<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>0,<space|1em>z<rsub|i><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|\<Omega\><rsub|i>|)>\<to\>0,<space|1em><around|\||c<rsub|i><rsup|0>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*\<rho\><rsub|i>,<space|1em><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|i>|)>\<to\>4*\<cal-O\>,<space|1em>c<rsub|i><rsup|1>\<to\>-\<kappa\>/4.
  </equation*>

  The first two limits are the exponent-one principal and false trace limits
  of the branch-moment unit recalled above. The bound on
  <math|c<rsub|i><rsup|0>> is contained in the coefficient bound
  <math|<around|\||c<rsub|i><rsup|0>|\|>+<around|\||c<rsub|i><rsup|1>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*\<rho\><rsub|i>>
  used above. The fourth limit follows from the field receipt
  <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|i>|)>\<to\>M<rsub|1>> and
  <math|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>M<rsub|1>=4*\<cal-O\>>, by continuity of
  the trace. The coefficient limit <math|c<rsub|i><rsup|1>\<to\>-\<kappa\>/4>
  was proved above, after (<reference|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-sensor-limits>),
  from the exact cancellation <math|X<rsub|i>+<math-ss|B><rsub|i>c<rsub|i>=0>,
  the sensor limits (<reference|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-sensor-limits>), the
  coefficient bound <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|c<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*\<rho\><rsub|i>>
  and the scale bound <math|\<rho\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|i>>; it is
  therefore conditional on the same inputs. These scale inequalities need no
  additional receipt. In the notation of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-weighted-estimates>,
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>exp <around|(|<around|(|log log
  z<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>/6|)>> and <math|t<rsub|i>=exp <around|(|<around|(|log
  log z<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>/5|)>>, so <math|\<rho\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|i>>
  and <math|t<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>. Since <math|<around|(|log log
  z|)><rsup|2>=o<around|(|log z|)>>, <math|t<rsub|i>/z<rsub|i>\<to\>0>.
  Finally the first clock input in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-clock-inputs>,
  applied at <math|k<rsub|i>>, gives <math|q<rsub|i>/z<rsub|i>\<to\>1>, hence
  <math|q<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>2*z<rsub|i>> eventually and
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|i>/q<rsub|i>\<to\>0>. These consequences still depend on
  the stated clock and response-size inputs. To see exactly how the weighted
  estimates enter, use <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|T<rsub|i>|)>=q<rsub|i>*\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|L<rsub|i>|)>+c<rsub|i><rsup|0>*\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|\<Omega\><rsub|i>|)>+c<rsub|i><rsup|1>*\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|i>|)>>.
  The principal contribution tends to zero since
  <math|q<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>2*z<rsub|i>> and the exponent-one principal
  trace estimate gives <math|z<rsub|i><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|L<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>0>.
  For the false correction, <math|<around|\||c<rsub|i><rsup|0>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*\<rho\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>C*z<rsub|i>>
  and the exponent-one weighted false-sector estimate gives
  <math|z<rsub|i><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|\<Omega\><rsub|i>|)>\<to\>0>.
  Finally <math|c<rsub|i><rsup|1>\<to\>-\<kappa\>/4> and
  <math|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|i>|)>\<to\>4*\<cal-O\>>.
  Thus applying the trace to (<reference|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-corrected-tensor>)
  and using these three contributions gives

  <\equation>
    <math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|T<rsub|i>|)>\<longrightarrow\>-\<kappa\>*\<cal-O\>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-flux-limit>
  </equation>

  Indeed, the principal term tends to zero, the first correction term is
  bounded by <math|C*z<rsub|i><around|\||<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|\<Omega\><rsub|i>|)>|\|>>,
  and the last term tends to <math|<around|(|-\<kappa\>/4|)>*<around|(|4*\<cal-O\>|)>>.
  This establishes the physical field and flux limits from the exact
  three-term correction, with no interchange of limits or choice of history.

  <subsubsection|Proportionality transfers the physical normalization>

  We now show that the corrected physical tensor and the selected table
  stress have the same limiting normalization. Let <math|A> be the selected
  algebra, and let <math|R> be its centered table stress
  <math|R=<math-up|reCenter><rsub|S><around|(|A.<math-up|energy>|)>\<in\>E>,
  whose entries are conserved and whose flux is the physical Ward flux. Let
  <math|g<rsub|i>=T<rsub|i>> be the explicit conserved correction sequence
  just constructed, viewed in the same one-dimensional kernel of <math|D>.
  Its normalized field and flux limits are
  (<reference|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-field-limit>) and
  (<reference|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-flux-limit>). We prove the transfer,
  spelling out the nonzero test and the scalar ratio.

  The argument uses the following inputs, all for the same selected table,
  reference table, derivative maps, chart and scaling as above; the
  derivative maps of the two tables are assumed to obey the derivative and
  orientation laws of their tables. None of these inputs is a consequence of
  rank one or of the field and flux limits.

  <\enumerate>
    <item><em|Physical stress laws.> The tensor <math|R> lies in <math|E>,
    satisfies <math|D*R=0>, and obeys the translation Ward identity of the
    selected table: for every separated configuration consisting of a
    distinguished point and spectator points, every tuple of spectator
    labels, every cutoff that separates the distinguished point from the
    spectator points, every axis <math|b> and every field <math|B>, the shell
    measurement of <math|R> with that cutoff and with <math|B> at the
    distinguished point equals the correlation at that configuration with the
    derivative field <math|D<rsub|b><rsup|S>*B> at the distinguished point
    and the same spectators.

    <item><em|Reference stress laws.> The material tensor <math|M<rsub|0>> is
    a centered, conserved stress tensor of the reference table and obeys the
    translation Ward identity of the reference table.

    <item><em|A detecting derivative.> There are an axis <math|a>, a
    reference observer <math|o>, and a reference grade-four field <math|v>
    whose derivative in direction <math|a> has nonzero value <math|y> on the
    grade-five test of <math|o>.

    <item><em|Tested convergence and jet comparison.> The following four
    statements hold for the selected chart maps <math|U<rsub|i,d>> and radii
    <math|r<rsub|i>>.

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(4a)>For every integrated reference test, that is, a compactly
      supported test function with fixed spectator labels and one
      distinguished slot, and every fixed reference field <math|g> of the
      grade of that slot, the correspondingly rescaled tested correlation of
      the selected table with <math|U<rsub|i,d>*g> in the distinguished slot
      converges to the reference tested correlation with <math|g> in that
      slot. This holds also for the test obtained by differentiating the test
      function in the distinguished variable.

      <item*|(4b)>There are finitely many integrated reference tests with a
      grade-five distinguished slot whose reference functionals have zero
      common kernel on the reference grade-five field space.

      <item*|(4c)>In both tables a tested correlation with the derivative
      field <math|D<rsub|a>*g> in the distinguished slot equals the
      derivative in direction <math|a>, in the distinguished variable, of the
      correlation with <math|g> in that slot.

      <item*|(4d)>Pointwise jet comparison for the observer <math|o>. For
      every fixed reference grade-five field <math|g>, the selected
      correlation at the configuration <math|r<rsub|i>*x> with
      <math|U<rsub|i,5>*g> at the distinguished point and the spectator
      labels <math|<math-up|pins><rsub|i>\<circ\>q> converges to the
      observer's reference value <math|o.*t*e*s*t<rsub|5><around|(|g|)>>. For
      every fixed reference stress tensor <math|H>, the rescaled shell
      measurement <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|i><around|(|H|)>=<with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|i>*<around|(|r<rsub|i><rsup|-4>*U<rsub|i,4>*H|)>>
      defined below, in which the chart is applied entrywise, converges to
      the reference shell measurement <math|<math-up|shellGrad><around|(|H,\<chi\>,a,x,q,v|)>>.
      Both are obtained below from the comparison-of-jets estimate for the
      finitely many grades and arities fixed by the observer, on a comparison
      window containing the support of the gradient of <math|\<chi\>>
      together with the spectator points. The deduction from that compact
      comparison is given below; the comparison must concern these same chart
      maps.
    </enumerate>
  </enumerate>

  The physical stress in item 1 is supplied by the following choice of the
  energy in the selected algebra and the centering argument after it. The
  reference laws and detector in items 2\U3 are verified below for the actual
  Wick comparator used by this gallery. For the completed charts of the
  selected gallery, the comparison statements in item 4 follow below from
  their uniform short-test estimate and the derivative laws of the two
  tables.

  <subparagraph|Identifying the actual reference fields and their
  normalization.> We verify the reference identifications needed here before
  using the Ward calculation. Keep the frame register of the supplied family
  fixed. Let <math|c<rsub|d>> be its coordinate isomorphism from invariant
  jet polynomials of grade at most <math|d> to coordinate labels, let
  <math|\<pi\><rsub|d><rsup|0>> be projection to reference fields, and let
  <math|<math-up|obs><rsub|d>> be passage to the on-shell Wick polynomial.
  The actual reference table is defined by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:scribe-reference-dictionary>S<rsub|0>*<around*|(|\<pi\><rsub|d<rsub|1>><rsup|0>*c<rsub|d<rsub|1>>*p<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,\<pi\><rsub|d<rsub|n>><rsup|0>*c<rsub|d<rsub|n>>*p<rsub|n>;x|)>=<math-up|Wick><rsub|x><around*|(|<math-up|obs><rsub|d<rsub|1>><space|0.17em>p<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,<math-up|obs><rsub|d<rsub|n>><space|0.17em>p<rsub|n>|)>.
  </equation>

  Indeed its coordinate correlation is the Wick correlation composed in each
  slot with <math|c<rsub|d><rsup|-1>>; projection quotients precisely the
  labels annihilated by all separated spectator correlations. Thus this
  identity uses the reference table of the supplied family, not a replacement
  table. Its Wick covariance is the curvature covariance
  <eqref|eq:maxwell-ward-covariance>, obtained by taking the two curls of the
  potential kernel <math|<around|(|4*\<pi\><rsup|2><around|\||x|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>;
  this normalization is part of the defining free table. Write
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|d>*p=\<pi\><rsub|d><rsup|0>*c<rsub|d>*p>. The on-shell
  polynomial address of <math|\<Phi\><rsub|d>*p> is
  <math|<math-up|obs><rsub|d><space|0.17em>p>. The free separation result of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-all-grade-free-detectors> makes
  this address an isomorphism onto the invariant on-shell sector: its kernel
  is exactly the correlation null space. In particular the reference is
  faithful.

  Its derivative plan is obtained by conjugating polynomial translation
  through this address. On a curvature jet, translation raises the jet in the
  specified direction; on products it obeys the Leibniz rule. The rotation
  action is obtained by conjugating rotation of the same jets, including
  their tensor indices. Consequently, writing <math|\<delta\><rsub|a>> and
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|A>> for these polynomial derivations,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:scribe-reference-derivatives>D<rsub|a><rsup|0>*\<Phi\><rsub|d>*p=\<Phi\><rsub|d+1>*<around|(|\<delta\><rsub|a>*p|)>,<space|2em>K<rsub|A><rsup|0>*\<Phi\><rsub|d>*p=\<Phi\><rsub|d>*<around|(|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|A>*p|)>.
  </equation>

  These identities follow by applying the injective polynomial address to
  either side. They are compatible with grade inclusions since those
  inclusions preserve the addressed polynomial. They also prove the
  positional derivative identity by differentiating the finite Wick sum in
  <eqref|eq:scribe-reference-dictionary>. The reference data installed before
  the comparison curve have exactly these derivative and rotation maps;
  fixing the stress normalization preserves both maps and the vacuum. Even if
  a derivative plan is specified instead by its positional derivative law, it
  must be this one: subtracting the two laws gives zero separated
  correlations for their difference on every label, so faithfulness makes the
  difference zero. This verifies (R1) and (R2) of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:free-maxwell-ward-flux> on these same data.

  For the stress identification there is a sign to retain. The coordinate
  pair <math|P<rsub|a*b,c*d>> in <eqref|eq:ex-branch-material> is the
  representative of <math|<around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|a*b>,F<rsub|c*d>|\<rangle\>>>,
  with no extra factor. The canonical positive Maxwell tensor is therefore
  the image of <eqref|eq:maxwell-wick-stress>; this verifies (R3). But the
  branch coefficients <math|w<rsup|0>=<around|(|-1,1/4,-1|)>> in
  <eqref|eq:ex-branch-coefficients> give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:scribe-reference-stress-sign><around|(|M<rsub|0>|)><rsub|a*b>=-\<Phi\><rsub|4>*<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|c><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|a*c>,F<rsub|b*c>|\<rangle\>>-<frac|\<delta\><rsub|a*b>|4>*<big|sum><rsub|c,e><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|c*e>,F<rsub|c*e>|\<rangle\>>|)>=-J<rsup|0><rsub|a*b>.
  </equation>

  The products inside <math|\<Phi\><rsub|4>> are Wick ordered by the defining
  observable map. Hence <math|M<rsub|0>> is centered and conserved. Applying
  the proved polynomial Ward identity <eqref|eq:maxwell-polynomial-ward> and
  <eqref|eq:scribe-reference-derivatives>, the outward collar flux of
  <math|J<rsup|0>> is minus the derivative insertion, and that of
  <math|M<rsub|0>=-J<rsup|0>> is the derivative insertion with coefficient
  one. The same sign gives coefficient one for rotations. This proves
  input<nbsp>2; no choice depending on the comparison sequence has entered
  it.

  Finally the definition of the scalar label in the branch table gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:scribe-reference-scalar-identification>\<cal-O\>=\<Phi\><rsub|4>*<around*|(|<frac|1|4>*<big|sum><rsub|a,b><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|a*b>,F<rsub|a*b>|\<rangle\>>|)>.
  </equation>

  Here the sum is over ordered pairs, just as in
  <eqref|eq:scribe-reference-stress-sign>. Applying
  <eqref|eq:scribe-reference-dictionary> to two such labels, the explicit
  Wick computation <eqref|eq:trace-free-scalar> proves
  <eqref|eq:trace-reference-normalization> for this actual <math|\<cal-O\>>:
  <math|S<rsub|0>*<around|(|\<cal-O\>,\<cal-O\>;x,y|)>=3*dim
  \<frak-g\>/<around|(|\<pi\><rsup|4>*<around|\||x-y|\|><rsup|8>|)>>. Its
  expectation is zero by Wick ordering. For <math|dim \<frak-g\>\<gtr\>0>
  this two-point function is nonzero, so <math|\<cal-O\>\<ne\>0>; its zero
  expectation also excludes a nonzero multiple of the unit. The
  differentiated two-point test below now supplies input<nbsp>3 without an
  additional reference-normalization assumption.

  <subparagraph|The tested-price input for this lift.> The sensor's (S4) is
  supplied by the quantitative invoice of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:trace-quantitative-invoice>, not merely by the
  convergence of tested correlations. Indeed take its grade-five reading
  <math|T> at the same station, finite-volume cutoff and period as the
  selected lift. Its calibrated map is <math|A=d<rsub|k><rsup|-5><math-up|Col><rsub|k>>.
  Both maps send the reference unit to the coefficient unit, and <math|A> is
  injective by the calibrated column isomorphism. The invoice gives, for
  every coefficient vector <math|v>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||A*v-T*v|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1/2>*<around|\||A*v|\|><rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  Its threshold is uniform over the finite volumes and the chart offset; it
  was paid in the same joint chart purchase. Enlarging the selected tail
  gives the half-accuracy estimate as well. Thus the two estimates in
  <eqref|eq:trace-sensor-tested-price> hold with the actual invoice price
  <math|\<eta\><rsub|i>=H<rsup|-1/2>>. In particular <math|T> is invertible,
  and the selected finite-volume lift is exactly
  <math|T<rsup|-1><math-up|Dil><rsub|\<xi\><rsup|-1>><space|0.17em><math-up|Pos><rsub|\<sigma\><rsub|i>><around|(|U*h|)><rsub|\<alpha\>>>.
  To check that the invoice remains valid after deleting the vacuum
  coefficient, apply both bounds to <math|v<rsup|\<circ\>>=v-<around|(|T*v|)><rsub|0>*e<rsub|<math-up|ref>>>.
  Then <math|T*v<rsup|\<circ\>>=<math-up|core><space|0.17em>T*v> and
  <math|<math-up|core><space|0.17em>A*v<rsup|\<circ\>>=<math-up|core><space|0.17em>A*v>,
  while <math|<around|\||A*v<rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*<around|\||T*v<rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>>.
  Consequently

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||<math-up|core><space|0.17em>A*v-<math-up|core><space|0.17em>T*v|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*\<eta\><rsub|i>*<around|\||<math-up|core><space|0.17em>T*v|\|><rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  This is precisely the centered coefficient estimate needed for the sensor's
  initial-level price, with the same inverse reading and lift.

  <subparagraph|The adjacent-column receipt.> Fix a level <math|m> beyond the
  grade-five entrance, and retain the actual calibrated columns at levels
  <math|m,m+1> of one finite-volume hierarchy. Write <math|L=L<rsub|m>>,
  <math|B=C<rsub|m>>, <math|U=C<rsub|m+1>>, and let <math|k\<geqslant\>1> be
  the selected local forecast bound and <math|r\<gtr\>0> the fixed local
  remainder transport bound at that transition. Put

  <\equation*>
    z=<around|\||<math-up|core><wide|z|~><rsub|m+1>|\|><rsub|1>,<space|2em>y=<around|\||<math-up|core><wide|z|~><rsub|m>|\|><rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  Deleting the vacuum coefficient before applying the calibrated front
  estimate gives <math|y\<leqslant\>U*L<rsup|5>*z>. The block map obeys
  <math|<around|\||<math-up|Block><rsub|m><space|0.17em><math-up|core><wide|z|~><rsub|m+1>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>L<rsup|5>*z>.

  Apply the actual insertion and local uniqueness comparison of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-price-recurrence>,
  specifically <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-price-one-step> and
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-local-uniqueness>, to these adjacent calibrated
  fields. Their format and remainder bounds are the calibrated field
  certificates, and their exact density transfer is the defining recursion of
  this same hierarchy. The joint estimate takes the following form. For every
  <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, on a sufficiently large period tail, uniformly
  over <math|N\<gtr\>m> and over the terminal label,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||<wide|z|~><rsub|m>-\<cal-L\><rsub|m>*<wide|z|~><rsub|m+1>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|m>*<around|(|z+U*z|)>+\<epsilon\>*<around|{|B*y+k\|<math-up|Block><rsub|m><space|0.17em><math-up|core><wide|z|~><rsub|m+1>\|<rsub|1>+r*U*z|}>,<space|2em>s<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|-1/2>*<around|(|m+1|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|m>> is the actual local forecast, not a freely
  chosen comparison map. To see the period quantifier, compare the calibrated
  coarse density with the forecast of the calibrated fine density. Both are
  exact representations of the same transferred density. Cancel the common
  nonzero vacuum density and combine the two absolutely summable regular
  remainders, changing the sign of one family. The local section of their
  coefficient difference is exactly this combined remainder. Its nonwrapping
  terms have zero local projection. Subtract a finite partial sum of those
  terms before applying a member of the finite separating probe family. The
  corresponding finite probe is exactly zero. The probe comparison estimate
  bounds the remaining error by its fixed derivative price times the sum of
  the omitted nonwrapping weights and the weights of wrapping supports. If
  the period exceeds <math|10*<around|(|h*<around|(|4*B<rsub|m>+2|)>+2*B<rsub|m>|)>>,
  every wrapping support anchored at the insertion has enough weighted
  support mass to pay <math|e<rsup|-a*h>>, where <math|a\<gtr\>0> is the
  fixed support exponent. Send the finite partial sum through all nonwrapping
  terms: its omitted weights tend to zero by absolute summability. This
  finite-partial-sum argument avoids differentiating an uncontrolled infinite
  series. Sum the finitely many probe prices and apply the fixed coefficient
  recovery matrix. The resulting bound on the coefficient difference is
  <math|C*e<rsup|-a*h>> times the sum of the two remainder prices. Choose
  <math|h> so that <math|C*e<rsup|-a*h>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>>, then require
  the period to contain that collar and the fixed forecast support. The
  constants and the period threshold are independent of <math|N> and the
  terminal label. The forecast remainder is bounded by
  <math|k\|<math-up|Block><rsub|m><space|0.17em><math-up|core><wide|z|~><rsub|m+1>\|<rsub|1>+r*U*z>;
  its coefficient error is <math|s<rsub|m>*<around|(|z+U*z|)>>. These two
  bounds give the displayed inequality by the triangle inequality. The
  selected transition price is <math|s<rsub|m>=w<rsub|m>*A<rsub|-1/2>*<around|(|m+1|)>>
  with <math|0\<less\>w<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1>, which gives its stated bound.

  The forecast map is exactly the primary map of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:primary-calibration-producer>, not a matrix
  selected from its bounds. To verify its normalization, let <math|R> be that
  section's unscaled coefficient recovery, put
  <math|f=\<vartheta\><rsub|m+1>>, <math|c=\<vartheta\><rsub|m>>, and write
  <math|<around|(|g<rsub|i>,\<ell\><rsub|i>|)>> for the two grades of its
  fixed coordinate basis. The local map before block transport is

  <\equation*>
    <with|math-font|cal*|B>=<with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|c>*R*<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|L>*<with|math-font|cal*|S><rsub|f<rsup|-1>>,<space|2em><with|math-font|cal*|B><rsub|b*i>=c<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|b>>*f<rsup|-\<ell\><rsub|i>>*L<rsup|g<rsub|i>>*R<rsub|b*i>.
  </equation*>

  This is the same full-matrix identity as in the primary calibration;
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:heading-history-handoff> explicitly uses
  that same <math|R>. Its proved identity
  <eqref|eq:heading-triangular-receipt>, with <math|u=L<rsup|-1>>, gives

  <\equation*>
    <with|math-font|cal*|B><rsub|b*i>=<around|(|c/f|)><rsup|\<ell\><rsub|i>>*\<delta\><rsub|b*i>*<space|2em><around|(|g<rsub|b>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|i>|)>.
  </equation*>

  There is no error term in this identity: the polynomial transport of a
  weight-<math|a> potential is <math|u<rsup|a>> times that potential plus a
  term of weight less than <math|a>; the local section's lower-weight
  cancellation annihilates every chain-rule term using the latter. The
  section then reads the original homogeneous coordinate exactly. This is
  precisely the argument proving the cited triangular receipt on the actual
  kernel.

  The same section proves that <math|R>, and hence
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|B>>, commutes with signed coordinate motions:
  change variables in the absolutely convergent kernel sum, use the local
  section's covariance, and take its finite jet. This establishes the
  symmetry for this very matrix, rather than requiring a separate symmetric
  approximation. Every nonunit invariant coordinate through grade five has
  factor degree two and position degree four or five: each curvature factor
  costs at least two units of position degree, and a linear invariant
  functional on a simple Lie algebra vanishes. The only possible off-diagonal
  matrix entry left by triangularity therefore raises position degree four to
  five. Total inversion acts on degree <math|g> by
  <math|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|g>>, so commutation forces that entry to equal
  its negative and hence to vanish. The unit is fixed exactly by the local
  map. Thus on the entire grade-five frame
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|B>=<math-up|Dil><rsub|c/f>>.

  The full raw forecast is this same <math|<with|math-font|cal*|B>> composed
  with <math|<math-up|Block><rsub|m>>, whose coordinate weights are
  <math|L<rsup|5-g<rsub|i>>>. Consequently
  <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|m>=<math-up|Dil><rsub|\<vartheta\><rsub|m>/\<vartheta\><rsub|m+1>><space|0.17em><math-up|Block><rsub|m>>.
  Its unit weight is <math|L<rsup|5>>, consistently with
  <math|<math-up|Col><rsub|m><around|(|e<rsub|<math-up|ref>>|)>=a<rsub|m><rsup|5>*e<rsub|0>>
  and <math|a<rsub|m>=L*a<rsub|m+1>>; only the pre-block local map fixes the
  unit. The selected threshold comparison gives
  <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|m+1>/\<vartheta\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>2>. Set
  <math|g<rsub|m>=2*<around|(|1+U|)>*A<rsub|-1/2>*<around|(|m+1|)>> and
  <math|P=B*U*L<rsup|5>+k*L<rsup|5>+r*U\<gtr\>0>. Choosing
  <math|\<epsilon\>=g<rsub|m>/<around|(|2*P|)>> in the preceding period
  estimate bounds its second term by <math|<around|(|g<rsub|m>/2|)>*z>; its
  first term is also at most <math|<around|(|g<rsub|m>/2|)>*z>. This proves
  exactly <eqref|eq:trace-sensor-raw-step>, with its original calibrated
  columns. The period tail is chosen separately for each fixed <math|m>, as
  required in (S5). Intersecting finitely many such tails between the chart
  level and its summit gives the single ultrafilter-large set used in the
  sensor's transport proof.

  <subparagraph|The actual branch recording family.> The recording estimates
  in the branch unit are applications of one finite-volume purchase to a
  finite family of local observables. We specify that family to keep its
  coefficient and rooted prices attached to the same functions. The principal
  tensor is the localized stress field, the false correction is its
  off-diagonal curvature-product array, and the true correction is the
  diagonal quarter-square array. Include every tensor entry, its directional
  forward differences and divergence, and the trace and antisymmetry currents
  of the localized stress together with their forward differences. Tensor
  entries have grade four, their differences and divergence grade five, the
  currents grade three and their differences grade four. There are finitely
  many branches, axes and pairs of tensor indices. Translations are applied
  to the already chosen local functions and their labels; they do not require
  a new purchase for each test point.

  The local analytic construction and healthy derivative calculation of
  Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:trace-charge-healthy-prices> apply to
  precisely these entries, currents and differences.
  Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:trace-reference-atomic-certificate>
  supplies the regular atomic certificate uniformly over this finite family
  in grades at most five; its support-tail estimate applies to the original
  full observable, including its healthy mask. Apply
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-coffer-supplier> using the
  same selected coffer for every member, and choose its coefficient label
  once for each scale and finite-volume index, together with the full
  purchase certificate. Its coefficient bias is
  <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|k><rsup|2>*A<rsub|-1/4><around|(|k|)>>. For the
  rooted estimate substitute <math|a=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|-1>>,
  <math|B=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|3>>,
  <math|\<zeta\><rsub|j>=\<varphi\><rsub|d,j>>,
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|alto><rsub|j,d>|\<\|\|\>>=a<rsub|j><rsup|-d>>
  and <math|<math-up|gain><around|(|k,j|)>=e<rsup|<around|(|s<rsub|k>-s<rsub|j>|)>/8>>
  into <eqref|eq:ex-trace-coffer-weight> and <eqref|eq:ex-trace-coffer-root>.
  This gives exactly

  <\equation*>
    <frac|2<math-up|stake><rsub|h,j><space|0.17em>a<rsub|j><rsup|-d>|\<varphi\><rsub|d,j>>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|4>*e<rsup|-<around|(|s<rsub|k>-s<rsub|j>|)>/8>,
  </equation*>

  which is the actual-minus-local price in
  <eqref|eq:ex-branch-native-root-price>. The reference-frame column bound
  gives a fixed-scale compact ball for the chosen labels. Hence one
  ultrafilter limit <math|x<rsub|k>> exists for each member, before any
  spectator test is chosen. This is the actual recording selection, not a
  selection of a different label for each test.

  Here is how the rooted receipt yields all three recorded inequalities. Fix
  a spectator test and choose a native scale small enough that its fixed
  points and a fixed neighborhood of the moving insertion are separated. Its
  centered spectator prices are bounded on that neighborhood. Multiplying the
  actual-minus-local rooted price by the fixed spectator price gives a bound
  uniform over the allowed integer translations. At every fixed scale the
  point-limit identity for a moving chosen label follows from its finite
  basis expansion: its coefficients converge and the correlations for the
  fixed basis vectors converge along the same ultrafilter. For each fixed
  <math|k> and allowed displacement, choose the ultrafilter-large set so that
  this convergence error is at most <math|a<rsub|k>>. The allowed integer
  displacements at that fixed level form a finite set, so their intersection
  is still large. Adding <math|a<rsub|k>> to the rooted bound gives the
  recorded current and difference inequalities; the tensor inequality is the
  same argument at zero displacement. Their errors are fast: with the native
  and comparison scales fixed, the rooted price has the form
  <math|C*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|4>*e<rsup|-s<rsub|k>/8>>, so it is
  at most <math|e<rsup|-s<rsub|k>/16>> on a tail, since <math|log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>=o<around|(|s<rsub|k>|)>>. Multiplication by any
  fixed spectator constant preserves such an exponential bound after a
  further tail. Finite maxima over the family give one common tail. This
  proves the actual instances of the three inequalities labelled
  <eqref|eq:ex-branch-book-current>, <eqref|eq:ex-branch-book-difference>,
  and <eqref|eq:ex-branch-book-tensor> in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-trace-branch-moments>.

  The current observable is normalized by <math|a<rsub|k><rsup|-3>> and its
  raw first difference by <math|a<rsub|k><rsup|-4>>. Hence the physical
  difference is <math|a<rsub|k><rsup|-1>> times the difference of normalized
  current observables, which is the factor in the external branch identity.
  The atomic certificate applies to the raw local difference before this
  grade normalization; no exponentially enlarged raw certificate is assumed.
  The finite-volume identities are preserved in this application. The trace
  of the principal tensor is the sum of the designated current differences,
  and the false correction has exactly zero diagonal sum. The trace and
  antisymmetry current identities and vanishing of their projected forward
  differences are the local identities proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-local-charge>; the
  correction entries are the actual curvature products used in its local
  calibration. Taking the finite sums before the ultrafilter limit therefore
  gives exactly the current/difference relation and the principal and false
  trace relations used by the branch washout proof. Its grade-three unit
  argument, finite-difference estimate and moving finite detector then apply
  to these recorded labels. Consequently the three branch field limits and
  the principal and false weighted trace washout are supplied for this very
  history and these very chart maps.

  <subparagraph|Installing the normalized energy in the selected algebra.>
  Keep the hierarchy, positional derivatives and orthogonal field action
  constructed in Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-stress-normalization>,
  <reference|sec:physical-ward-propagation> and
  <reference|sec:physical-angular-actions>. Here is the same-table
  application. Use the selected table <math|S> as the physical hierarchy
  <math|S<rsup|1>> of the first section, and use the actual comparator
  identified above as <math|S<rsup|0>>. The completed maps are those of the
  selected trace gallery; their uniform test bound and derivative transport
  are verified below for these same maps. The reference divergence is onto
  with one-dimensional kernel by Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-free-rank>.
  Thus <eqref|eq:stress-physical-line> produces the conserved line in this
  very selected table. Its choice of a nonzero tensor and sign uses a
  subsequence only to establish existence of a fixed tensor <math|J>; it does
  not change the table or its finite-volume extraction.

  The annular-field construction of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-annular-field>
  applied to this <math|J> gives its translation and angular response maps.
  The physical packet prices and their finite-row limits in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-ward-propagation> concern precisely
  these response maps, so Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:selected-ward-package>
  applies. Its two free tests are supplied for the actual comparator by
  Theorems<nbsp><reference|thm:free-quiet-classification>,
  <reference|thm:free-collinear-scenes> and
  <reference|thm:free-internal-erasure>. No change of reference derivatives
  is needed, by <eqref|eq:scribe-reference-derivatives>. The corollary gives
  the global Ward laws with one nonzero scalar on the same <math|S>; the
  inclusion argument makes that scalar common to all grades. Finally
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-angular-actions> integrates those
  very angular maps and makes them compatible with the selected derivatives
  and inclusions. For this application, use the shell-node receipt at each
  opened pair required by the finite watches. The actual schedule, lightness,
  finite-stencil chamber placement and birth quota are verified in <hlink|the
  selected-family placement argument|#par:shell-actual-placement>. The
  finite-mask identification is <hlink|the actual fine finite-mask
  payment|#par:shell-actual-mask>, and the coarse and fine transfer duties
  follow from <eqref|eq:trace-extra-stage-payment> and its ensuing estimates.
  These use the same selected service and doors as the two-channel
  construction. The tab bounds follow from the adjacent clock ratio; centered
  spectator prices come from the one-field centered service. For every fixed
  finite family of watches, the slotwise estimate
  <eqref|eq:scribe-linear-derivative-price> supplies the required
  separated-point derivatives with the enlarged spectator allowance. It
  precedes the Ward construction and therefore introduces no use of rotation
  covariance in proving that covariance.

  The finite-row comparisons then use exactly this annular response, the
  fixed normalization <eqref|eq:stress-fixed-normalization> and the
  same-curve compact comparison. Their translation and rotation readout
  limits are Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ward-readout-limits>; its
  reference flux identity is the one identified above. The packet estimates
  are those of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-ward-packet-prices>,
  with the stress-cone estimate of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-ward-stress-cone>.
  Their polynomial derivative input follows from the same pre-Ward slotwise
  bound. Thus the shell, finite-row and packet arguments use one selected
  hierarchy and its already fixed tensor throughout. Let <math|J> be the
  single conserved tensor in that construction and let <math|c\<ne\>0> be its
  common charge scalar: its translation response is <math|c*\<partial\>> and
  its rotation response is <math|c*D>, where <math|D> is the Lie algebra
  action of the constructed orthogonal representation <math|R<rsub|d>>. The
  energy of the selected algebra is chosen to be

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:scribe-selected-energy>E<rsub|a*b>=<wide|J|^><rsub|a*b>,<space|2em><wide|J|^>=<big|int><rsub|O<around|(|4|)>>\<cal-R\><rsub|K>*<around|(|c<rsup|-1>*J|)>*<space|0.17em>d*K,<space|2em><around|(|\<cal-R\><rsub|K>*Q|)><rsub|a*b>=<big|sum><rsub|i,j>K<rsub|a*i>*K<rsub|b*j>*R<rsub|4><around|(|K|)>*Q<rsub|i*j>.
  </equation>

  The measure is probability Haar measure. This choice is made once, together
  with the derivatives and the action, before choosing a trace chart or a
  null sequence. It specifies the energy component of that selected algebra;
  it is not an identification of an arbitrarily preassigned energy with a
  generator of the conserved line.

  Here is the same-data verification. The common scalar is independent of
  grade by the inclusion argument of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-ward-propagation>.
  Multiplying the stress by <math|c<rsup|-1>> multiplies each collar flux by
  <math|c<rsup|-1>>, so its charges become exactly <math|\<partial\>> and
  <math|D>. The averaging argument in the subsection \PNormalizing and
  averaging the stress\Q of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-angular-actions>
  applies to these very maps. It proves that the tensor in
  <eqref|eq:scribe-selected-energy> is real, symmetric, tensor covariant and
  conserved, and that both Ward identities retain coefficient one.
  Explicitly, for each <math|K> the change of variables <math|y=K*z> carries
  its collar integral to that of <math|c<rsup|-1>*J> with cutoff
  <math|\<chi\>\<circ\>K>, transformed spectators and vector field
  <math|K<rsup|-1>*W*<around|(|K*z|)>>. Covariance of the same derivative and
  rotation action returns the original Ward right-hand side. Each collar flux
  is a continuous linear functional on the finite-dimensional space of stress
  arrays; it commutes with the Haar integral. The right-hand side is
  independent of <math|K>, so probability normalization leaves it unchanged.
  Thus the energy component <math|E> has the raw coefficient-one laws needed
  below.

  No scale-dependent rescaling or second tensor is introduced in this
  assembly. In the remainder of this section, the raw energy is exactly
  <eqref|eq:scribe-selected-energy>, and <math|R> is its centered version.
  With those producer instances, the energy is installed on the selected
  table with the raw Ward laws just obtained. No identification with an
  independently chosen stress tensor is needed. In particular the auxiliary
  comparison subsequence used to construct <math|J> does not restrict the
  later trace scalings: once constructed, <math|J>, its scalar and all its
  Ward identities are fixed statements on <math|S>.

  <subparagraph|Centering preserves the physical Ward normalization.> There
  is no scalar-normalization loss in passing from the raw energy to <math|R>.
  More precisely, suppose the raw energy <math|E<rsub|a*b>=A.<math-up|energy><rsub|a*b>>
  is real, symmetric and tensor covariant, is conserved, and satisfies the
  coefficient-one translation Ward identity of input<nbsp>1 on this same
  table, whose derivative annihilates the unit and whose unit insertion
  removes that slot. Write <math|c<rsub|a*b>=<around|\<langle\>|E<rsub|a*b>|\<rangle\>><rsub|S>>,
  so <math|R<rsub|a*b>=E<rsub|a*b>-c<rsub|a*b>*\<b-1\>>. Then
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>D<rsub|a><rsup|S>*R<rsub|a*b>=<big|sum><rsub|a>D<rsub|a><rsup|S>*E<rsub|a*b>=0>.
  For fixed spectator fields, the contribution of the subtracted tensor to a
  translation shell is a sum of terms

  <\equation*>
    -c<rsub|a*b>*<space|0.17em>S<around|(|<text|spectator
    fields>|)>*<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\><around|(|z|)>*<space|0.17em>d*z=0.
  </equation*>

  The last equality follows by integrating a derivative of a compactly
  supported smooth function, one coordinate at a time. Thus the shell
  identity for <math|R> has exactly the same coefficient one and the same
  derivative insertion as the identity for <math|E>. Centering is immediate;
  symmetry, reality and tensor covariance are preserved because expectation
  and the unit respect those structures. This proves the stress laws in
  input<nbsp>1 from the raw energy laws of the preceding construction, under
  the shell and packet hypotheses specified there. The scalar comparison with
  the explicit corrected tensor sequence remains necessary and is proved
  below on the original trace scaling.

  The detecting derivative in item 3 can also be fixed explicitly. Take
  <math|v=\<cal-O\>>, the grade-four curvature-square field defined at the
  start of this section, and choose any coordinate axis <math|a>. Let
  <math|o> be the reference observer which tests a grade-five field <math|g>
  by the two-point correlation <math|S<rsub|0>*<around|(|g,\<cal-O\>;0,e<rsub|a>|)>>.
  By the reference-field normalization <eqref|eq:trace-reference-normalization>,
  the Wick calculation gives, with <math|N=dim \<frak-g\>\<gtr\>0>,

  <\equation*>
    S<rsub|0>*<around|(|\<cal-O\>,\<cal-O\>;x,e<rsub|a>|)>=<frac|3*N|\<pi\><rsup|4>*<around|\||x-e<rsub|a>|\|><rsup|8>>,<space|2em>o.*t*e*s*t<rsub|5>*<around|(|D<rsub|a>*\<cal-O\>|)>=<around*|\<nobracket\>|\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|a>><frac|3*N|\<pi\><rsup|4>*<around|\||x-e<rsub|a>|\|><rsup|8>>|\|><rsub|x=0>=<frac|24*N|\<pi\><rsup|4>>\<ne\>0.
  </equation*>

  The derivative identity is the reference derivative law; both points are
  separated, so ordinary differentiation is legitimate. This fixes a single
  observer and a single nonzero value before any chart is selected. It uses
  the actual reference Wick covariance and the identification
  <eqref|eq:scribe-reference-scalar-identification> proved above. Thus the
  detector is fixed without using the interacting trace asymptotic or
  retaining an additional reference-identification premise.

  <subparagraph|From chart comparison to the tested and shell limits.> The
  tested approach property of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-firesky-gallery>,
  on the same scaling, supplies (4a): a derivative of a fixed compactly
  supported smooth test is another such test, with support in the same
  separated window. That section's finite separating-test argument, applied
  in grade five, supplies (4b). More explicitly, a nonzero field has a
  nonzero separated reference evaluation. Continuity and a nonnegative smooth
  bump around that evaluation give a nonzero integrated functional. These
  functionals separate the grade-five space; finitely many suffice, since
  adjoining a functional nonzero on the current common kernel strictly
  decreases its dimension until it is zero. The derivative laws (4c) remain
  the laws of the selected and reference tables.

  We now check the stronger compact comparison needed in (4d). Use exactly
  the completed corrected maps of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-lattice-selected-trace-gallery>
  and its <hlink|completion paragraph|#par:ex-trace-completed-uniform-tests>:
  the index <math|k> there is the present <math|i>, the selected table is
  <math|S>, the reference table is <math|S<rsub|0>>, and the radii and every
  grade map are the present <math|r<rsub|i>,U<rsub|i,d>>. The raw lifts are
  those of these same supplied charts and use the fixed extraction and
  ultrafilter. This is the choice of chart family throughout the transfer,
  not a replacement by a second curve. Any subsequent subsequence is applied
  to all these data together.

  For a fixed word <math|v> and compact set <math|Q> in its noncoincident
  configuration space, the finite-slot expansion in that theorem gives an
  error bound

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:scribe-uniform-short-tests>sup<rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>>,<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>f\<subset\>Q>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>M>>>>>><around*|\||<big|int>f<around|(|x|)>*<around*|{|S*<around|(|U<rsub|i>*v;r<rsub|i>*x|)>-S<rsub|0>*<around|(|v;x|)>|}>*<space|0.17em>d*x|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|M*K<rsub|v>|i+1>\<longrightarrow\>0
  </equation>

  on a tail, where <math|K<rsub|v>> is a finite constant depending on the
  word. Indeed the corrected discrepancy is a finite sum of raw discrepancies
  at fixed words, each bounded by the short-test estimate
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-test-error>. Their number and label norms do not
  depend on <math|f> or <math|i>. The expanding cutoff window contains the
  whole compact set <math|Q> eventually. Completion changes none of the
  finitely many grade maps on a further tail independent of <math|f>. This
  proves the supremum estimate, rather than merely convergence for each
  individual test. Along a further subsequence <math|i\<mapsto\>n<rsub|i>>,
  the same argument retains the vanishing bound
  <math|M*K<rsub|v>/<around|(|n<rsub|i>+1|)>>.

  The compact-jet argument in the subsection \PDerivative transport and
  convergence on compact sets\Q of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-stress-normalization>
  now applies to these very data. Its hypothesis
  <eqref|eq:stress-uniform-tests> is precisely
  <eqref|eq:scribe-uniform-short-tests>. Its spaces
  <math|E<rsub|d>,F<rsub|d>> are the reference and selected grade spaces
  here; their finite dimension, smooth multilinear correlations, separating
  tests, and derivative identities are the same hierarchy and derivative laws
  already used in (4a)\U(4c). No stress-normalization conclusion of that
  section is used in its compact-jet argument.

  To make the application explicit, its transported derivative is
  <math|r<rsub|i>*U<rsub|i,d+1><rsup|-1>*D<rsub|a><rsup|S>*U<rsub|i,d>>. The
  finitely many separating tests in grade <math|d+1> give measurement maps
  <math|L<rsub|i>\<to\>L>, with <math|L> injective. Integration by parts and
  <eqref|eq:scribe-uniform-short-tests> imply
  <math|L<rsub|i>*<around|(|r<rsub|i>*U<rsub|i,d+1><rsup|-1>*D<rsub|a><rsup|S>*U<rsub|i,d>|)>*v\<to\>L*D<rsub|a><rsup|0>*v>.
  The eventual lower bound for <math|L<rsub|i>> therefore gives convergence
  of the transported derivative to <math|D<rsub|a><rsup|0>> in operator norm,
  as proved there. Finite-basis multilinearity transfers the uniform test
  estimate to converging moving labels. Position differentiation replaces one
  label by its transported derivative, so this class of discrepancies is
  closed under every fixed number of position derivatives. For a compactly
  supported cutoff <math|\<eta\>> in configuration space and any such
  discrepancy <math|h<rsub|i>>, the tests
  <math|\<eta\>*e<rsup|-2*\<pi\><math-up|i><space|0.17em>\<xi\>\<cdot\>x>>
  have one common supremum bound. The same is true with derivatives of
  <math|\<eta\>> and descendants of <math|h<rsub|i>>. The
  integration-by-parts calculation in that section consequently bounds
  <math|<around|\||<wide|\<eta\>*h<rsub|i>|^><around|(|\<xi\>|)>|\|>> by
  <math|C*\<epsilon\><rsub|i>*<around|(|1+<around|\||\<xi\>|\|>|)><rsup|-b>>,
  with <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|i>\<to\>0> and any fixed <math|b\<gtr\>4*n> at
  arity <math|n\<gtr\>0>. Fourier inversion yields uniform convergence on a
  compact set where <math|\<eta\>=1>; applying this to the finitely many
  derivatives of a fixed order gives convergence of that jet. At arity zero
  the assertion is the scalar short-test limit. This also explains why no
  estimate at collision configurations is needed.

  Thus, for each fixed finite word of reference labels
  <math|h<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,h<rsub|s>>, with grades
  <math|d<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,d<rsub|s>>, and each compact set <math|K> of
  pairwise distinct configurations,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:scribe-compact-comparison>sup<rsub|z\<in\>K><around*|\||S*<around|(|U<rsub|i,d<rsub|1>>*h<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,U<rsub|i,d<rsub|s>>*h<rsub|s>;r<rsub|i>*z|)>-S<rsub|0>*<around|(|h<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,h<rsub|s>;z|)>|\|>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation>

  The required finite grade bounds include the finitely many descendants used
  for the desired jet order. They and the fixed arity are eventually within
  the selected chart cutoff. Equation <eqref|eq:scribe-compact-comparison> is
  therefore a proved consequence of the uniform short-test estimate and the
  derivative laws, not an independent compact-comparison premise. Taking
  <math|K> to be the observer's one configuration gives the first assertion
  of (4d).

  For the shell assertion, let <math|x> denote the distinguished and
  spectator points and choose the cutoff <math|\<chi\>> so that the compact
  set <math|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<nabla\>*\<chi\>> avoids all these
  points. The configurations <math|<around|(|z,x|)>> with
  <math|z\<in\><math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<nabla\>*\<chi\>> then form a
  compact subset of the noncoincident configuration space: all pair distances
  have a positive minimum there. Thus one separated window contains the
  entire integration set. Write <math|C<rsub|i><rsup|c>*<around|(|z;H|)>> for
  the selected correlation with the entry <math|U<rsub|i,4>*H<rsub|c*a>> at
  <math|r<rsub|i>*z>, the distinguished field <math|U<rsub|i,4>*v> at its
  rescaled point, and the chart-transformed spectators. Let
  <math|C<rsub|0><rsup|c>*<around|(|z;H|)>> be its reference counterpart.
  With the translation-shell convention, the flux is minus the sum of these
  correlations against <math|\<partial\><rsub|c>*\<chi\>>. Changing variables
  <math|y=r<rsub|i>*z> in its four-dimensional integral gives exactly

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:scribe-shell-change>\<cal-L\><rsub|i><around|(|H|)>=-<big|sum><rsub|c=1><rsup|4><big|int><around|(|\<partial\><rsub|c>*\<chi\>|)><around|(|z|)>*C<rsub|i><rsup|c>*<around|(|z;H|)>*<space|0.17em>d*z.
  </equation>

  Indeed, the prefactor <math|r<rsub|i>> in
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|i>>, the stress multiplier
  <math|r<rsub|i><rsup|-4>>, the cutoff derivative <math|r<rsub|i><rsup|-1>>
  and the Jacobian <math|r<rsub|i><rsup|4>> multiply to one. The spectator
  maps are exactly the chart maps used in
  <eqref|eq:scribe-compact-comparison>; no extra scaling of them is
  introduced. That estimate on the compact shell therefore yields

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<cal-L\><rsub|i><around|(|H|)>-<math-up|shellGrad><around|(|H,\<chi\>,a,x,q,v|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|c=1><rsup|4><around|\<\|\|\>|\<partial\><rsub|c>*\<chi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>*sup<rsub|z\<in\><math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<nabla\>*\<chi\>><around|\||C<rsub|i><rsup|c>*<around|(|z;H|)>-C<rsub|0><rsup|c>*<around|(|z;H|)>|\|>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  This proves the shell deduction in (4d). Expanding in a finite basis of
  stress arrays also proves operator-norm convergence of these shell
  functionals, which is the form used below for moving tensors.

  By input<nbsp>3 fix an axis <math|a>, an observer <math|o>, and a reference
  grade-four field direction <math|v> such that
  <math|y=o.*t*e*s*t<rsub|5>*<around|(|D<rsub|a>*v|)>\<ne\>0>, where
  <math|D<rsub|a>> is the reference derivative in direction <math|a>. Let
  <math|x> be the separated configuration of this same observer, consisting
  of the point of the distinguished insertion and the points of its
  spectators, let <math|q> be its fixed tuple of spectator labels, and let
  <math|\<chi\>> be its cut, a cutoff separating the distinguished point from
  the spectator points. The reference translation Ward identity of
  input<nbsp>2, applied to this cut, identifies this number with
  <math|<math-up|shellGrad><around|(|M<rsub|0>,\<chi\>,a,x,q,v|)>>. We keep
  this exact observer and cut for both stress sequences; no new test or
  window is selected in the limit argument.

  For a stress tensor <math|Q>, write the exact selected shell measurement as

  <\equation*>
    <with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|i><around|(|Q|)>=r<rsub|i><math-up|shellGrad><rsub|S><space|-0.17em><around*|(|Q,<math-up|stretch><around|(|r<rsub|i>,\<chi\>|)>,a,r<rsub|i>*x,<math-up|pins><rsub|i>\<circ\>q,U<rsub|i>*v|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|r<rsub|i>> is the chart radius, <math|q> is the observer's
  tuple of spectator labels, transported by the chart maps
  <math|<math-up|pins><rsub|i>>, and <math|U<rsub|i>> is the selected
  grade-four chart isomorphism. We claim

  <\equation>
    f<rsub|i>\<assign\><with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|i><around|(|R|)>\<to\>y,<space|2em><with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|i><around|(|g<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>y.<label|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-common-test>
  </equation>

  The two limits have different sources, and both involve labels that move
  with <math|i>, so that convergence for fixed labels cannot be applied
  directly.

  <em|First limit.> Write <math|D<rsub|a><rsup|S>> for the derivative map of
  the selected table and define the real-linear maps from reference
  grade-four fields to reference grade-five fields

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|T><rsub|i>=r<rsub|i>*<space|0.17em>U<rsub|i,5><rsup|-1>*D<rsub|a><rsup|S>*<space|0.17em>U<rsub|i,4>.
  </equation*>

  We first show <math|<math-ss|T><rsub|i>\<to\>D<rsub|a>> in operator norm.
  Let <math|\<lambda\><rsup|1>,\<ldots\>,\<lambda\><rsup|m>> be the reference
  functionals of the tests of (4b) and let
  <math|\<lambda\><rsup|1><rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,\<lambda\><rsup|m><rsub|i>> be
  the rescaled functionals <math|g\<mapsto\>> (tested correlation of the
  selected table with <math|U<rsub|i,5>*g> in the distinguished slot). By
  (4a), <math|\<lambda\><rsup|r><rsub|i>\<to\>\<lambda\><rsup|r>> pointwise,
  hence in operator norm on the finite-dimensional reference grade-five
  space. Fix a reference grade-four field <math|u>. By (4c) for the selected
  table and integration by parts in the distinguished variable,
  <math|\<lambda\><rsup|r><rsub|i><around|(|<math-ss|T><rsub|i>u|)>> is minus
  the rescaled tested correlation of <math|U<rsub|i,4>*u> against the test
  with differentiated test function; the factor <math|r<rsub|i>> in
  <math|<math-ss|T><rsub|i>> is exactly the one produced by rescaling the
  derivative. By (4a) for the differentiated test this converges to minus the
  reference tested correlation of <math|u> against the differentiated test,
  which equals <math|\<lambda\><rsup|r>*<around|(|D<rsub|a>*u|)>> by (4c) for
  the reference table and integration by parts. Thus
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i><around|(|<math-ss|T><rsub|i>u|)>\<to\>\<Lambda\>*<around|(|D<rsub|a>*u|)>>,
  where <math|\<Lambda\>=<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|r>|)><rsub|r>> and
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>=<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|r><rsub|i>|)><rsub|r>>.
  Since <math|\<Lambda\>> is injective on a finite-dimensional space there is
  <math|c\<gtr\>0> with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Lambda\>*w|\<\|\|\>>\<geqslant\>c<around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>>>,
  and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>*w|\<\|\|\>>\<geqslant\><around|(|c/2|)><around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>>>
  for large <math|i>. Hence

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|T><rsub|i>u-D<rsub|a>*u|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><frac|2|c>*<around*|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Lambda\><rsub|i><math-ss|T><rsub|i>u-\<Lambda\>*D<rsub|a>*u|\<\|\|\>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|\<Lambda\>-\<Lambda\><rsub|i>|)>*D<rsub|a>*u|\<\|\|\>>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0,
  </equation*>

  and pointwise convergence on the finite-dimensional grade-four space is
  operator-norm convergence. Next let <math|A<rsub|i><around|(|g|)>> denote
  the selected correlation at <math|r<rsub|i>*x> with <math|U<rsub|i,5>*g> at
  the distinguished point and spectator labels
  <math|<math-up|pins><rsub|i>\<circ\>q>. By the first part of (4d),
  <math|A<rsub|i>\<to\>o.*t*e*s*t<rsub|5>> pointwise, hence in operator norm.
  The physical Ward identity of input<nbsp>1, applied at the separated
  configuration <math|r<rsub|i>*x> with the stretched cut, and linearity give

  <\equation*>
    <with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|i><around|(|R|)>=r<rsub|i>*<space|0.17em><around|(|<text|correlation
    at >r<rsub|i>*x<text| with >D<rsub|a><rsup|S>*<around|(|U<rsub|i,4>*v|)><text|
    at the distinguished point>|)>=A<rsub|i><around|(|<math-ss|T><rsub|i>v|)>.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|A<rsub|i>\<to\>o.*t*e*s*t<rsub|5>> in operator norm and
  <math|<math-ss|T><rsub|i>v\<to\>D<rsub|a>*v>, we get
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|i><around|(|R|)>\<to\>o.*t*e*s*t<rsub|5>*<around|(|D<rsub|a>*v|)>=y>.

  <em|Second limit.> No Ward identity for <math|g<rsub|i>> is used. For a
  reference stress tensor <math|H> put <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|i><around|(|H|)>=<with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|i>*<around|(|r<rsub|i><rsup|-4>*U<rsub|i,4>*H|)>>,
  with the chart applied to each entry. This is a linear functional on the
  finite-dimensional space of reference stress tensors, and by the definition
  of <math|\<cal-Y\><rsub|i>> and linearity of
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|i>>,

  <\equation*>
    <with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|i><around|(|g<rsub|i>|)>=\<cal-L\><rsub|i><around*|(|\<cal-Y\><rsub|i><around|(|g<rsub|i>|)>|)>
  </equation*>

  exactly. By the second part of (4d), <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|i><around|(|H|)>\<to\>\<cal-L\><rsub|0><around|(|H|)>\<assign\><math-up|shellGrad><around|(|H,\<chi\>,a,x,q,v|)>>
  for every fixed <math|H>, hence in operator norm. The field limit
  (<reference|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-field-limit>) gives
  <math|\<cal-Y\><rsub|i><around|(|g<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>M<rsub|0>>. Therefore

  <\equation*>
    <with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|i><around|(|g<rsub|i>|)>\<longrightarrow\>\<cal-L\><rsub|0><around|(|M<rsub|0>|)>=<math-up|shellGrad><around|(|M<rsub|0>,\<chi\>,a,x,q,v|)>=y,
  </equation*>

  the last equality being the reference Ward identity used above. The
  argument uses the same selected scale and the same observer test in both
  limits. The physical flux excludes <math|R=0>: if <math|R> vanished,
  linearity of shell differentiation would force <math|f<rsub|i>=0> at every
  index, contrary to <math|y\<ne\>0>.

  Since <math|ker D> is one-dimensional, <math|g<rsub|i>=\<lambda\><rsub|i>*R>
  for all sufficiently large <math|i>. The second limit in
  (<reference|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-common-test>) therefore reads
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>*f<rsub|i>\<to\>y>, while the first reads
  <math|f<rsub|i>\<to\>y>. As <math|y\<ne\>0>, choose a scalar coordinate
  functional <math|\<ell\>> with <math|\<ell\><around|(|y|)>\<ne\>0>. Then

  <\equation*>
    \<lambda\><rsub|i>=<frac|\<ell\>*<around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|i>*f<rsub|i>|)>|\<ell\><around|(|f<rsub|i>|)>>\<longrightarrow\><frac|\<ell\><around|(|y|)>|\<ell\><around|(|y|)>>=1.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|g<rsub|i>=\<lambda\><rsub|i>*R> and
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>\<to\>1>. On a late tail
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>\<ne\>0> and <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>\<to\>1>.
  Since <math|\<cal-Y\><rsub|i>> and the weighted trace are linear,

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-Y\><rsub|i><around|(|R|)>=\<lambda\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>*\<cal-Y\><rsub|i><around|(|g<rsub|i>|)>,<space|2em><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|R|)>=\<lambda\><rsub|i><rsup|-1><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|g<rsub|i>|)>
  </equation*>

  on that tail. This proves the physical field and flux limits. In
  particular,

  <\equation>
    \<cal-Y\><rsub|i><around|(|R|)>\<longrightarrow\>M<rsub|0>,<space|2em><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-P\><rsub|i><around|(|R|)>\<longrightarrow\>-\<kappa\>*\<cal-O\>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-physical-transfer>
  </equation>

  This is the physical-array step used by the chart gallery. The
  dimension-one kernel alone would not fix the scalar; the common nonzero
  shell test fixes it.

  <subsubsection|Comparison tables and the full gallery record>

  Let <math|T> be any table on the same label book as the selected table
  <math|S>; for the limit proved in this paragraph no hierarchy on <math|T>
  and no compatibility of <math|T> with <math|S> is required. Let
  <math|\<Theta\><rsub|T>> be the trace of <math|A.<math-up|energy>> centered
  by its one-point value in <math|T>. Its exact centered decomposition is

  <\equation*>
    \<Theta\><rsub|T>=<math-up|tr><around|(|<math-up|reCenter><rsub|S><around|(|A.<math-up|energy>|)>|)>-c<rsub|T>*<space|0.17em>1<rsub|S>,<space|2em>c<rsub|T>\<in\>\<bbb-C\>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|1<rsub|S>> is the grade-four unit field of <math|S>: both sides
  differ from the uncentered trace by a multiple of <math|1<rsub|S>>. The
  physical-transfer limit (<reference|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-physical-transfer>)
  applies to the first term. The scalar-unit term vanishes after zooming: the
  selected scale satisfies

  <\equation>
    q<rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>\<longrightarrow\>0,<space|2em>q<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0,<label|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-band-cost>
  </equation>

  as is proved from the clock in the next paragraph, and the selected chart
  eventually identifies the reference unit with the unit of <math|S>, that
  is, <math|U<rsub|i>*1=1<rsub|S>> for large <math|i>. Hence
  <math|q<rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*U<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*<around|(|c<rsub|T>*1<rsub|S>|)>=c<rsub|T>*q<rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*1\<to\>0>.
  Therefore the zoomed comparison observable has the same limit as the trace
  of the physical stress,

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*U<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*\<Theta\><rsub|T>\<longrightarrow\>-\<kappa\>*\<cal-O\>=4*d<rsub|2>*\<cal-O\>.
  </equation*>

  The limit is a multiple of the curvature-square field <math|\<cal-O\>>, not
  of the unit. This argument is uniform in the algebraic identity for
  <math|T>; only the two scalar sequences used below to represent its
  component in the distinguished two-dimensional stress sector depend on
  <math|T>.

  <em|The clock.> Write <math|j<rsub|i>> for the frame level of the
  <math|i>th chart, so that <math|k<rsub|i>> is its summit index, let
  <math|<math-ss|a><rsub|j>\<gtr\>0> be the field scale at level <math|j> and
  <math|<math-ss|b><rsub|i>> the zoom factor of the <math|i>th chart, and let
  <math|\<zeta\><around|(|j|)>\<gtr\>0> be the arch scale at level <math|j>;
  thus <math|q<rsub|i>=\<zeta\><around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>>. The chart radius is
  <math|r<rsub|i>=<math-ss|a><rsub|j<rsub|i>><math-ss|b><rsub|i>>, the
  running rate is <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+\<kappa\>*log
  <around|(|<math-ss|a><rsub|j><rsup|-1>|)>> with a fixed constant
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>>, and the selected run has
  <math|j<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>, <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<to\>\<infty\>>
  and <math|r<rsub|i>\<to\>0>. The first two clock limits below follow from
  the actual arch construction. For each fixed <math|j>, take the fixed-level
  ultrafilter limit in the running bound of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:rank-trace-running>. Closedness of the real
  interval gives, for every <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0> and every sufficiently
  large <math|j>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<zeta\><around|(|j|)>-<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|R>+\<eta\>*s<rsub|j>,<space|2em><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+\<kappa\>*s<rsub|j>,<space|2em>s<rsub|j>=log
    <around|(|<math-ss|a><rsub|j><rsup|-1>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The fixed constant <math|c<rsub|R>> does not depend on <math|j>. Division
  by the clock and then <math|\<eta\>*\<downarrow\>*0> proves
  <math|\<zeta\><around|(|j|)>/<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<to\>1>. For
  completeness, let <math|m+1=<math-up|summit><around|(|j|)>> and
  <math|x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>>. The selected schedule defines this
  summit as the first crossing of the gap <math|<around|(|log x|)><rsup|48>>.
  Thus <math|0\<leqslant\>s<rsub|m>-s<rsub|j>\<leqslant\><around|(|log
  x|)><rsup|48>> and <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>x+\<kappa\><around|(|log
  x|)><rsup|48>\<leqslant\>2*x> eventually. The actual schedule gives the
  weaker bound that is sufficient here: by <eqref|eq:common-stage-entries>,

  <\equation*>
    0\<leqslant\>s<rsub|m+1>-s<rsub|m>=log
    L<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|m>/J*<around|(|m+1|)>.
  </equation*>

  As <math|j\<to\>\<infty\>>, also <math|m\<to\>\<infty\>> and
  <math|J*<around|(|m+1|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>. Since
  <math|s<rsub|m>/x\<leqslant\>2/\<kappa\>> eventually, this last increment
  is <math|o<around|(|x|)>>. The pre-crossing gap is <math|<around|(|log
  x|)><rsup|48>=o<around|(|x|)>>, so <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|<math-up|summit><around|(|j|)>>/<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>\<to\>1>.
  In particular this clock-ratio proof does not need the stronger block-size
  bound used in the external sensor unit. Since both levels tend to infinity,

  <\equation*>
    <frac|\<zeta\><around|(|j|)>|\<zeta\><around|(|<math-up|summit><around|(|j|)>|)>>=<frac|\<zeta\><around|(|j|)>/<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|<around*|(|\<zeta\><around|(|<math-up|summit><around|(|j|)>|)>/<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|<math-up|summit><around|(|j|)>>|)>*<around*|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|<math-up|summit><around|(|j|)>>/<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|)>>\<longrightarrow\>1.
  </equation*>

  The third estimate is proved in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-trace-log-zoom>
  from the chart receipt <math|1\<leqslant\><math-ss|b><rsub|i>\<leqslant\><math-up|tiltcap><around|(|j<rsub|i>|)>>
  of <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-clock-cap-receipt>:

  <\equation>
    <frac|\<zeta\><around|(|j|)>|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>>\<longrightarrow\>1,<space|2em><frac|\<zeta\><around|(|j|)>|\<zeta\><around|(|<math-up|summit><around|(|j|)>|)>>\<longrightarrow\>1*<space|1em><around|(|j\<to\>\<infty\>|)>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>log
    <math-ss|b><rsub|i>\<leqslant\><around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|48><space|1em><text|eventually>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-clock-inputs>
  </equation>

  The definition of the rate gives

  <\equation*>
    <frac|log <around|(|r<rsub|i><rsup|-1>|)>|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j<rsub|i>>>=<frac|log
    <around|(|<math-ss|a><rsub|j<rsub|i>><rsup|-1>|)>|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j<rsub|i>>>-<frac|log
    <math-ss|b><rsub|i>|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j<rsub|i>>>=<frac|1|\<kappa\>>*<around*|(|1-<frac|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j<rsub|i>>>|)>-<frac|log
    <math-ss|b><rsub|i>|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j<rsub|i>>>\<longrightarrow\><frac|1|\<kappa\>>,
  </equation*>

  because <math|<around|(|log t|)><rsup|48>/t\<to\>0> as
  <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>>. Dividing by <math|\<zeta\><around|(|j<rsub|i>|)>/<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j<rsub|i>>\<to\>1>
  and multiplying by <math|\<zeta\><around|(|j<rsub|i>|)>/\<zeta\><around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>1>
  gives

  <\equation>
    <frac|log <around|(|r<rsub|i><rsup|-1>|)>|q<rsub|i>>\<longrightarrow\><frac|1|\<kappa\>>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-clock>
  </equation>

  In particular the slope cancels in the logarithmically normalized trace.
  For the selected phase let <math|Z<rsub|i>=r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*U<rsub|i,4><rsup|-1>*\<Theta\>>
  be its pulled-back centered trace. The physical transfer gives
  <math|q<rsub|i>*Z<rsub|i>\<to\>4*d<rsub|2>*\<cal-O\>=-\<kappa\>*\<cal-O\>>,
  whereas <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-clock> gives <math|log
  <around|(|r<rsub|i><rsup|-1>|)>/q<rsub|i>\<to\>1/\<kappa\>>. Therefore

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:scribe-slope-cancellation>log
    <around|(|r<rsub|i><rsup|-1>|)>*Z<rsub|i>\<longrightarrow\>-\<cal-O\>.
  </equation>

  At two separated points, the moving-label correlation comparison and
  <eqref|eq:trace-reference-normalization> consequently give the coefficient
  <math|S<rsub|0>*<around|(|-\<cal-O\>,-\<cal-O\>|)>=3*dim
  \<frak-g\>/\<pi\><rsup|4>> at unit separation. This is the coefficient in
  the trace asymptotic; using the same positive slope in the two factors is
  sufficient.

  Put <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>=log <around|(|r<rsub|i><rsup|-1>|)>>. Since
  <math|r<rsub|i>\<to\>0>, <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>, and
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>=\<ell\><rsub|i>*e<rsup|-4*\<ell\><rsub|i>>\<to\>0>.
  For large <math|i>, <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> and

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>=<frac|\<ell\><rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>|\<ell\><rsub|i>/q<rsub|i>>\<longrightarrow\><frac|0|1/\<kappa\>>=0,
  </equation*>

  which is <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-band-cost>. Finally
  <math|q<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>, since <math|q<rsub|i>=\<ell\><rsub|i>/<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|i>/q<rsub|i>|)>>
  and <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>.

  More explicitly, the scalar representation is

  <\equation*>
    \<iota\>*<around|(|e,d|)>=e*<space|0.17em>1+4*d*<space|0.17em>\<cal-O\>.
  </equation*>

  The finite reference calculation shows that <math|1> and <math|\<cal-O\>>
  are linearly independent, so <math|\<iota\>> is injective and has a
  continuous left inverse on its range. The limiting comparison tensor is
  <math|4*d<rsub|2>*\<cal-O\>>, where <math|d<rsub|2>=-\<kappa\>/4>, so its
  coordinates are <math|<around|(|0,d<rsub|2>|)>>. Put
  <math|Z<rsub|i>=q<rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*U<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*\<Theta\><rsub|T>>.
  For the coordinate representation, and only for it, assume in addition that
  <math|T> carries a hierarchy and respects <math|S>, and that the selected
  algebra has the local Ward structure (conservation, symmetry and reality of
  its energy tensor and its tensor transformation law). Then
  <math|Z<rsub|i>\<in\><math-up|range><around|(|\<iota\>|)>> eventually,
  separately from the limit <math|Z<rsub|i>\<to\>4*d<rsub|2>*\<cal-O\>>.
  Indeed, under these assumptions <math|\<Theta\><rsub|T>> is fixed by the
  real structure of <math|S> and by every signed permutation of the axes. On
  the tail where the grade-four chart is coherent, <math|U<rsub|i><rsup|-1>>
  intertwines the real structures and the signed-permutation actions of the
  two tables, and multiplication by the positive real number
  <math|q<rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>> preserves both conditions; so
  <math|Z<rsub|i>> is a real, signed-invariant grade-four field of the
  reference table. The diagonal tensor with all four diagonal entries equal
  to <math|Z<rsub|i>> and vanishing off-diagonal entries is then a real,
  symmetric, signed-covariant stress tensor of the reference table, hence a
  real combination of the four basis tensors of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-trace-free-stress-basis>. Taking its trace,
  and using that the trace of each of those basis tensors lies in
  <math|\<bbb-R\>*1+\<bbb-R\>*\<cal-O\>>, shows
  <math|4*Z<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>*1+\<bbb-R\>*\<cal-O\>>. On that tail let
  <math|<around|(|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|)>=\<iota\><rsup|-1><around|(|Z<rsub|i>|)>>;
  continuity of the fixed left inverse gives <math|a<rsub|i>\<to\>0> and
  <math|b<rsub|i>\<to\>d<rsub|2>>. Define
  <math|e<rsub|i>=a<rsub|i>/q<rsub|i>> and
  <math|d<rsub|i>=b<rsub|i>/q<rsub|i>>. Thus, eventually,

  <\equation*>
    r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*U<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*\<Theta\><rsub|T>=\<iota\>*<around|(|e<rsub|i>,d<rsub|i>|)>,<space|2em>q<rsub|i>*e<rsub|i>\<to\>0,<space|2em>q<rsub|i>*d<rsub|i>\<to\>d<rsub|2>.
  </equation*>

  The definition of the zoom then gives <math|q<rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*U<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*\<Theta\><rsub|T>\<to\>4*d<rsub|2>*\<cal-O\>>.

  The exact record contains <math|q<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>> and the clock
  limit <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-clock>, in which the scale is the
  reciprocal <math|r<rsub|i><rsup|-1>> of the chart radius and not the zoom
  factor; the same finite-volume chart and hierarchy; the physical stress
  limit; for every table <math|T> the zoomed trace limit; and, for every
  table <math|T> that carries a hierarchy and respects <math|S>, the late
  scalar representation and two coefficient limits just proved. The
  fixed-scale case uses the selected chart directly. For arbitrary positive
  null sequences the order of choices is as follows. The ultrafilter, the
  arch scale and the reference hierarchy are fixed first. Then, for every
  positive sequence <math|s<rsub|n>\<to\>0>, the paid scale selection chooses
  a strictly increasing sequence of indices <math|n<rsub|i>> and a scaling
  whose radii are exactly <math|r<rsub|i>=s<rsub|n<rsub|i>>>
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-firesky-selection>, using the joint
  chart purchase proved in that section); the same history is then passed to
  the finite-volume chart producer, and the physical transfer above applies
  without another extraction. With the actual shell, sensor-price and
  branch-recording instances specified above, applying the response, sensor,
  branch and scale-selection producers to this same history gives the full
  Scribe record. The sensor's marked-vector limits, the actual arch
  identification and clock limits, and the physical-energy normalization have
  been proved above on that history. The tested-price, adjacent-column and
  actual branch-recording arguments supply the additional quantitative
  instances for the external sensor and branch theorems. The compact
  comparison and the shell limit are consequences of the selected gallery's
  uniform short-test bound and the derivative laws, as proved above; they are
  not additional independent inputs.

  <subsection|The spectral measure of a single
  field><label|sec:trace-spectrum>

  The joint translation measure acts on vectors in Hilbert space. A point
  field applied to the vacuum need not be such a vector. To obtain the
  spectral measure of its two-point function, we first keep a positive heat
  height, take the measure of the resulting vector, and then remove the
  exponential damping. The resulting measure can have infinite total mass.

  Throughout this section one selected phase is fixed, and every object
  belongs to the single reconstruction built from it in
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:complex-heat>\U<reference|sec:spectral-measure>,
  whose hypotheses are retained. These objects are: the Euclidean Hilbert
  space <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|E>> with vacuum <math|\<Omega\><rsub|E>>, its
  heat semigroup <math|E<rsub|t>>, the complex heat operators
  <math|E<around|(|z|)>> and the unitary group <math|V<around|(|t|)>> of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:complex-heat>, and the joint translations
  <math|T<rsub|E><around|(|a|)>>; the slab insertions
  <math|B<rsub|p><around|(|v|)>> and the point heat vectors
  <eqref|eq:point-heat-vector>; the continued correlations <math|F<rsub|n>>
  of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:continued-matrix> and their boundary
  distributions <math|W<rsub|n>> of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:joint-boundary>;
  the involution <math|J> on labels; and the field operators of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:word-fields>, written
  <math|\<phi\>*<around|(|v,f|)>> in this section, with vacuum
  <math|\<Omega\>> and the representation <math|U> of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:word-motion>. Nothing below selects a
  second phase, continuation, or reconstruction.

  <subsubsection|Exact damping of vector measures>

  We first record a uniqueness fact for bounded holomorphic functions that
  will identify heat evolution inside the spectral integral. Suppose <math|h>
  is bounded and continuous on the closed upper half-plane, holomorphic in
  its interior, and zero on the real axis. Fix <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>,
  <math|s\<geqslant\>0> and apply the Gaussian contour argument from
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:paley-positive> to <math|h*<around|(|z+x|)>> with
  <math|b=-2*\<varepsilon\>*s>. The bottom integral vanishes. On the top
  edge,

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|-i*b*<around|(|t+i*s|)>-\<varepsilon\>*<around|(|t+i*s|)><rsup|2>>=e<rsup|-\<varepsilon\>*s<rsup|2>>*e<rsup|-\<varepsilon\>*t<rsup|2>>.
  </equation*>

  The nonzero constant factor can be cancelled, giving
  <math|<big|int>e<rsup|-\<varepsilon\>*t<rsup|2>>*h*<around|(|x+t+i*s|)>*d*t=0>.
  Set <math|\<varepsilon\>=R<rsup|-2>> and <math|t=R*u>. After cancelling
  <math|R\<gtr\>0>, let <math|R*\<downarrow\>*0>. Boundedness and the
  Gaussian majorant justify dominated convergence and give
  <math|<sqrt|\<pi\>>*h*<around|(|x+i*s|)>=0>. Thus <math|h=0>. In
  particular, two such bounded holomorphic functions with equal continuous
  boundary values agree throughout the half-plane.

  Let <math|\<nu\><rsub|v>> be the finite joint spectral measure of a vector
  <math|v\<in\>\<cal-H\><rsub|E>>, and retain <math|T<rsub|E>> and
  <math|E<rsub|t>> from the earlier construction.
  Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:euclidean-positive-energy> gives
  <math|p<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0> almost everywhere for <math|\<nu\><rsub|v>>.
  For fixed <math|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>, the function

  <\equation*>
    G<around|(|z|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\>a>*e<rsup|i*z*p<rsub|0>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|v><around|(|p|)>
  </equation*>

  is bounded and continuous on the closed upper half-plane. On a neighborhood
  where <math|<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>z\<geqslant\>\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>, its
  derivative is dominated by <math|p<rsub|0>*e<rsup|-\<eta\>*p<rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>\<eta\><rsup|-1>>.
  Since <math|\<nu\><rsub|v>> is finite, dominated differentiation proves
  holomorphy. The bounded holomorphic function

  <\equation*>
    F<around|(|z|)>=<around|\<langle\>|v,T<rsub|E><around|(|a|)>*E*<around|(|-i*z|)>*v|\<rangle\>>
  </equation*>

  in the open half-plane has the continuous boundary
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|v,T<rsub|E><around|(|a|)>*V<around|(|t|)>*v|\<rangle\>>>.
  By the translation spectral formula these boundary values equal
  <math|G<around|(|t|)>>. The uniqueness just proved therefore identifies
  <math|F=G>.

  The operators <math|E<rsub|t>> are self-adjoint and commute with
  translations. For <math|t\<geqslant\>0>, the preceding identity at
  <math|z=2*i*t> gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<langle\>|E<rsub|t>*v,T<rsub|E><around|(|a|)>*E<rsub|t>*v|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|v,T<rsub|E><around|(|a|)>*E<rsub|2*t>*v|\<rangle\>>=<big|int>e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\>a>*e<rsup|-2*t*p<rsub|0>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|v><around|(|p|)>.
  </equation*>

  Both measures here are finite, because <math|p<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0>.
  Uniqueness of finite measures from their characteristic functions, proved
  in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:spectral-measure>, yields

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-vector-damping>d*\<nu\><rsub|E<rsub|t>*v><around|(|p|)>=e<rsup|-2*t*p<rsub|0>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|v><around|(|p|)>.
  </equation>

  This proof uses the continuous bounded half-plane boundary of a vector
  matrix coefficient, not the generally distributional boundary of a point
  correlation.

  <subsubsection|Point vectors and removal of damping>

  Fix a label <math|v> and, for <math|s\<gtr\>0>, define

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|s><around|(|v|)>=Q<rsub|p,<around|(|s|)>>*<around|(|0;v|)>=E<rsub|s-p>*B<rsub|p><around|(|v|)>*\<Omega\><rsub|E>,<space|2em>0\<less\>p\<less\>1,<space|1em>4*p\<less\>s.
  </equation*>

  The vector is independent of the admissible width <math|p>. Indeed, the
  mixed Gram identity (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:mixed-heat-gram>), together
  with the width independence of the continued correlations
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:continued-matrix>), gives the same number for
  the scalar product of either of two such vectors with either one of them.
  Expanding the squared norm of their difference therefore gives zero. If
  <math|t\<geqslant\>0>, a width admissible at <math|s> is also admissible at
  <math|s+t>. Using that common width and the semigroup law gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-point-flow>q<rsub|s+t><around|(|v|)>=E<rsub|t>*q<rsub|s><around|(|v|)>.
  </equation>

  Let <math|\<nu\><rsub|s>> be the finite spectral measure of
  <math|q<rsub|s><around|(|v|)>>. Define a positive Borel measure by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-undamping>d*\<mu\><rsub|v><around|(|p|)>=e<rsup|2*p<rsub|0>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|1><around|(|p|)>.
  </equation>

  This is a well-defined sigma-finite measure: the density is finite and
  positive, and is bounded on each set <math|<around|{|<around|\||p<rsub|0>|\|>\<leqslant\>n|}>>.
  Multiplication of densities and <eqref|eq:trace-vector-damping>\U<eqref|eq:trace-point-flow>
  imply

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|2*<around|(|s+t|)>*p<rsub|0>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|s+t>=e<rsup|2*s*p<rsub|0>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|s>.
  </equation*>

  Compare <math|s> and <math|1> through their common larger height
  <math|s+1>. It follows that for every <math|s\<gtr\>0>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-height-measure>d*\<mu\><rsub|v>=e<rsup|2*s*p<rsub|0>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|s>,<space|2em>e<rsup|-2*s*p<rsub|0>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|v>=d*\<nu\><rsub|s>.
  </equation>

  In particular <math|\<mu\><rsub|v>> is supported in the time half-space
  <math|p<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0>, and every positive exponential damping has
  finite mass.

  The single-insertion case of the mixed Gram identity and translation
  covariance of the point vectors now gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-pair-laplace>F<rsub|2>*<around|(|x<rsub|0><rsup|0>-i*s,x<rsub|1><rsup|0>+i*s;\<b-up-x\><rsub|0>,\<b-up-x\><rsub|1>;J*v,v|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|-2*s*p<rsub|0>>*e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\><around|(|x<rsub|1>-x<rsub|0>|)>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|v><around|(|p|)>.
  </equation>

  To see the relative sign explicitly, the two vectors are
  <math|T<rsub|E><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>*q<rsub|s><around|(|v|)>> and
  <math|T<rsub|E><around|(|x<rsub|1>|)>*q<rsub|s><around|(|v|)>>. Unitarity
  moves the first translation to the second, producing
  <math|T<rsub|E>*<around|(|x<rsub|1>-x<rsub|0>|)>>; the spectral formula and
  <eqref|eq:trace-height-measure> give the integral above.

  For a self-adjoint Euclidean scalar label <math|v>, taking both real
  positions equal to zero identifies the left side with its Euclidean
  two-point function at separation <math|2*s>. Thus

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-positive-laplace>T<rsub|v><around|(|t|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|-t*p<rsub|0>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|v><around|(|p|)>*<space|1em><around|(|t\<gtr\>0|)>.
  </equation>

  It follows already that <math|T<rsub|v>> is real, nonnegative and
  decreasing. The stronger forward-cone conclusion below is obtained after
  identifying the measure with the actual reconstructed fields.

  <subsubsection|Polynomial growth and the full boundary distribution>

  The fixed-label gap bound <eqref|eq:gap-power>, applied at heights
  <math|<around|(|-s,s|)>> and zero real positions, gives constants
  <math|C\<geqslant\>0> and <math|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that

  <\equation*>
    \<nu\><rsub|s><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)>=<around|\<\|\|\>|q<rsub|s><around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C*s<rsup|-k>,<space|2em>0\<less\>s\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  For <math|R\<geqslant\>1> use <math|s=R<rsup|-1>> in
  <eqref|eq:trace-height-measure>. On <math|<around|{|p:p<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>R|}>>
  one has <math|e<rsup|-2*s*p<rsub|0>>\<geqslant\>e<rsup|-2>>, and hence

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-energy-growth>\<mu\><rsub|v>*<around|{|p:p<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>R|}>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|2>*C*R<rsup|k>.
  </equation>

  This controls an entire energy sublevel set, not just a Euclidean ball. In
  particular <math|\<mu\><rsub|v><around|(|<around|{|<around|\||p|\|>\<leqslant\>R|}>|)>\<leqslant\>A*R<rsup|k>>
  with <math|A=e<rsup|2>*C>. The measure is locally finite and therefore is a
  Radon measure on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>.

  Here is a quantitative rapid-weight consequence. Cover
  <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>> by the closed shells
  <math|2<rsup|j>\<leqslant\>1+<around|\||p|\|>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|j+1>>,
  <math|j\<geqslant\>0>. On the <math|j>th shell the weight
  <math|<around|(|1+<around|\||p|\|>|)><rsup|-k-1>> is at most
  <math|2<rsup|-j*<around|(|k+1|)>>>, and its mass is at most
  <math|A*2<rsup|<around|(|j+1|)>*k>>. Summing, with overlaps causing only an
  upper bound, gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-rapid-weight><big|int><around|(|1+<around|\||p|\|>|)><rsup|-k-1>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|v><around|(|p|)>\<leqslant\>A*2<rsup|k+1>\<less\>\<infty\>.
  </equation>

  Thus every Schwartz function is absolutely integrable against
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|v>>, and integration is a continuous functional on
  Schwartz space, bounded by a weighted supremum seminorm times the finite
  integral in <eqref|eq:trace-rapid-weight>.

  Use the unnormalized plus Fourier convention

  <\equation*>
    f<rsub|+><around|(|p|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\>x>*f<around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x,<space|2em>\<cal-W\>*\<Phi\><around|(|p|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\><around|(|x<rsub|1>-x<rsub|0>|)>>*\<Phi\>*<around|(|x<rsub|0>,x<rsub|1>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x<rsub|0>*d*x<rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  Both are Schwartz functions when their arguments are Schwartz. For the
  second assertion take the joint plus Fourier transform and restrict it to
  the injective linear subspace <math|p\<mapsto\><around|(|-p,p|)>>. The
  chain rule controls derivatives of the restriction, and
  <math|<around|\||<around|(|-p,p|)>|\|>=<sqrt|2><around|\||p|\|>> controls
  its weighted supremum seminorms.

  At each height <math|s\<gtr\>0>, Fubini in <eqref|eq:trace-pair-laplace> is
  justified by <math|\<nu\><rsub|s><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><around|\<\|\|\>|\<Phi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
  It identifies the joint tested correlation with
  <math|<big|int>e<rsup|-2*s*p<rsub|0>>*\<cal-W\>*\<Phi\><around|(|p|)>*d*\<mu\><rsub|v><around|(|p|)>>.
  As <math|s*\<downarrow\>*0>, the exponential has absolute value at most one
  almost everywhere and tends to one. The integrable majorant
  <math|<around|\||\<cal-W\>*\<Phi\>|\|>> and the joint boundary theorem
  (Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:joint-boundary>, at the strictly ordered
  heights <math|<around|(|-s,s|)>>) give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-full-boundary>W<rsub|2>*<around|(|J*v,v|)><around|(|\<Phi\>|)>=<big|int>\<cal-W\>*\<Phi\><around|(|p|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|v><around|(|p|)>.
  </equation>

  This is an identity on all joint Schwartz tests, including those meeting
  the coincidence set or the null cone. No restriction of a distribution to
  such a set has been used.

  <subsubsection|Identification with field vectors and the forward cone>

  Let <math|\<xi\><rsub|v><around|(|f|)>=\<phi\>*<around|(|v,f|)>*\<Omega\>>
  be the one-field vector in the reconstructed Hilbert space. With
  <math|\<Phi\>*<around|(|x<rsub|0>,x<rsub|1>|)>=<wide|f<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>|\<bar\>>g<around|(|x<rsub|1>|)>>,
  Fubini in the ordinary Fourier integral gives
  <math|\<cal-W\>*\<Phi\>=<wide|f<rsub|+>|\<bar\>>g<rsub|+>>. By the adjoint
  identity <eqref|eq:word-adjoint> and the vacuum expectation formula of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:word-fields>, the scalar product
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<xi\><rsub|v><around|(|f|)>,\<xi\><rsub|v><around|(|g|)>|\<rangle\>>>
  is the value of <math|W<rsub|2>*<around|(|J*v,v|)>> on this product test.
  This and <eqref|eq:trace-full-boundary> therefore give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-smeared-inner><around|\<langle\>|\<xi\><rsub|v><around|(|f|)>,\<xi\><rsub|v><around|(|g|)>|\<rangle\>>=<big|int><wide|f<rsub|+><around|(|p|)>|\<bar\>>g<rsub|+><around|(|p|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|v><around|(|p|)>.
  </equation>

  The product is integrable by the rapid-weight bound. Translation covariance
  of the fields (Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:word-motion>) gives
  <math|U<around|(|a|)>*\<xi\><rsub|v><around|(|f|)>=\<xi\><rsub|v><around|(|f|(><space|0.17em>\<cdot\>-a>)),
  and changing variables in the Fourier integral gives
  <math|<around|(|f|(><space|0.17em>\<cdot\>-a))<rsub|+><around|(|p|)>=e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\>a>*f<rsub|+><around|(|p|)>>.
  It follows from <eqref|eq:trace-smeared-inner> that the characteristic
  function of the finite PVM measure of <math|\<xi\><rsub|v><around|(|f|)>>
  equals that of <math|<around|\||f<rsub|+>|\|><rsup|2>*\<mu\><rsub|v>>. The
  latter is finite by <eqref|eq:trace-rapid-weight>. Finite-measure
  uniqueness gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-field-mass>d*\<mu\><rsup|<math-up|field>><rsub|\<xi\><rsub|v><around|(|f|)>>=<around|\||f<rsub|+>|\|><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|v>.
  </equation>

  The weights in this identity determine <math|\<mu\><rsub|v>> uniquely.
  Given a closed ball of radius <math|n>, choose a smooth compactly supported
  function <math|h> equal to one on that ball and take a Schwartz function
  <math|f> with <math|f<rsub|+>=h>. Such an <math|f> exists by Fourier
  inversion, with the factor <math|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-4>> in the
  inverse transform. The weight <math|<around|\||f<rsub|+>|\|><rsup|2>> is
  then exactly one on the ball. Equality of all the weighted measures implies
  equality on every measurable subset of each ball; increasing the radius
  proves equality on all Borel sets by continuity from below.

  By Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:field-spectrum>, every measure on the left
  of <eqref|eq:trace-field-mass> vanishes outside the forward cone. Apply the
  preceding weights on each ball to conclude

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-forward>\<mu\><rsub|v>*<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>\<setminus\><wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>|)>=0.
  </equation>

  Thus the forward-cone statement is inherited from the reconstructed field
  representation, while the initial undamping argument required only
  positivity of the time coordinate in the Euclidean chamber.

  For completeness the same identification gives Lorentz covariance of the
  point measure. Write <math|A=\<Lambda\><rsup|-T>> for the dual momentum
  action and <math|g<rsub|\<Lambda\>><around|(|x|)>=g*<around|(|\<Lambda\><rsup|-1>*x|)>>.
  Since <math|det \<Lambda\>=1>, <math|<around|(|g<rsub|\<Lambda\>>|)><rsub|+>*<around|(|A*p|)>=g<rsub|+><around|(|p|)>>.
  Covariance of the field vectors and of their PVM measures then gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||<around|(|g<rsub|\<Lambda\>>|)><rsub|+>|\|><rsup|2>*\<mu\><rsub|\<rho\><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>*v>=A<rsub|\<ast\>>*<around*|(|<around|\||g<rsub|+>|\|><rsup|2>*\<mu\><rsub|v>|)>=<around|\||<around|(|g<rsub|\<Lambda\>>|)><rsub|+>|\|><rsup|2>*A<rsub|\<ast\>>*\<mu\><rsub|v>.
  </equation*>

  The transformed tests run through Schwartz space, so uniqueness by the
  weights proves <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<rho\><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>*v>=A<rsub|\<ast\>>*\<mu\><rsub|v>>.
  In particular a Lorentz scalar label has a Lorentz-invariant point measure,
  with the same dual convention as the joint translation measure.

  <subsubsection|Application to the centered trace>

  Let <math|T<rsub|a*b>> be the physical Euclidean stress tensor of the same
  construction, a family of grade-four labels, and let <math|\<chi\>> be the
  selected phase fixed above, with one-point functional
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|<space|0.17em>\<cdummy\><space|0.17em>|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<chi\>>>.
  We use the following properties of <math|T<rsub|a*b>> as hypotheses; they
  are produced by the stress and Ward part of the construction and not in
  this section: its entries are real labels, <math|T<rsub|a*b>=T<rsub|b*a>>,
  and it transforms as a rank-two tensor under the action <math|R> of the
  Euclidean orthogonal group on labels. Put
  <math|\<tau\>=<big|sum><rsub|a>T<rsub|a*a>>. Orthogonality gives

  <\equation*>
    R<around|(|K|)>*\<tau\>=<big|sum><rsub|a,i,j>K<rsub|i*a>*K<rsub|j*a>*T<rsub|i*j>=<big|sum><rsub|i,j>\<delta\><rsub|i*j>*T<rsub|i*j>=\<tau\>.
  </equation*>

  Its one-point value <math|c=<around|\<langle\>|\<tau\>|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<chi\>>>
  is independent of position by translation invariance, and real by
  conjugation symmetry. The centered label <math|\<Theta\>=\<tau\>-c*\<b-1\>>
  is consequently real, invariant under all Euclidean orthogonal
  transformations, and has zero one-point function. Since the adjoint label
  operation is conjugation followed by time reflection,
  <math|J*\<Theta\>=\<Theta\>>.

  It is also a Lorentz scalar. Differentiating its Euclidean invariance shows
  that every Euclidean rotation generator annihilates it. The corresponding
  boost generator is <math|-i> times that generator, so also annihilates it.
  The constant curve <math|\<Theta\>> and its boost orbit solve the same
  finite-dimensional constant-coefficient differential equation with the same
  initial value; uniqueness makes the orbit constant. Spatial rotations
  already fix it, and generation by boosts and spatial rotations proves full
  Lorentz invariance.

  The one-point holomorphic continuation of <math|\<Theta\>> is zero: on
  positive imaginary times it is the zero Euclidean one-point function, and
  the one-variable identity theorem extends that equality to the half-plane
  for each spatial position. Its tested boundary is therefore zero. The word
  formula gives <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,\<phi\>*<around|(|\<Theta\>,f|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>=0>
  for every Schwartz <math|f>. Finally <eqref|eq:trace-positive-laplace>
  supplies the positive measure and Laplace formula for the very same
  centered trace used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:trace-asymptotic>. No
  additional positive-measure input is needed there once the earlier
  reconstruction hypotheses and this stress tensor are available.

  <subsection|Excluding finite mass-shell profiles><label|sec:trace-profiles>

  The centered trace has a short-distance behavior that can distinguish it
  from finite polynomial expressions in free fields. The relevant distinction
  is the negative power of the logarithm. We prove the exclusion directly for
  the mass-shell profiles appearing in the statement. The proof proceeds
  through finite power\Ulogarithm approximations to Laplace integrals; it
  does not assume a convergent asymptotic series.

  <subsubsection|An algebra of finite asymptotic approximations>

  Let <math|\<cal-P\>> be the real vector space of finite sums of the
  functions <math|s<rsup|a><around|(|log s|)><rsup|k>>, where
  <math|a\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>>, <math|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and <math|s\<gtr\>0>. It
  is an algebra containing the constants. Say that <math|f> has an
  approximation of order <math|b\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>> if

  <\equation*>
    f<around|(|s|)>=P<around|(|s|)>+O<around|(|s<rsup|b>|)>*<space|1em><around|(|s*\<downarrow\>*0|)>,<space|2em>P\<in\>\<cal-P\>.
  </equation*>

  Say that <math|f> has power\Ulogarithm approximations if it has an
  approximation of every integer order. Different orders may use different
  finite sums. An approximation of order <math|b> is also one of any order
  <math|a\<leqslant\>b>, since <math|s<rsup|b>\<leqslant\>s<rsup|a>> for
  <math|0\<less\>s\<less\>1>.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lem:profile-algebra>Functions with power\Ulogarithm approximations
    form a real algebra. Every such function, and every element of
    <math|\<cal-P\>>, is <math|O<around|(|s<rsup|u>|)>> for some integer
    <math|u>. Multiplication by <math|s<rsup|a>> shifts the order of an
    approximation by <math|a>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For <math|0\<less\>s\<less\>1>, <math|<around|\||log
    s|\|>\<leqslant\>s<rsup|-1>>. Thus each monomial is bounded by
    <math|s<rsup|a-k>>, and a finite sum is <math|O<around|(|s<rsup|u>|)>>
    for the smallest of the finitely many exponents <math|a-k>. An order-zero
    approximation then gives the growth claim for <math|f>, after replacing
    <math|u> by <math|min <around|(|u,0|)>>. The assertions about addition,
    real scalar multiplication, and shifts are immediate from the definition
    and closure of <math|\<cal-P\>> under these operations.

    For multiplication fix a desired order <math|b>. Choose <math|\<ell\>>
    with <math|g=O<around|(|s<rsup|\<ell\>>|)>> and an approximation
    <math|f=P+O<around|(|s<rsup|b-\<ell\>>|)>>. Choose <math|u> with
    <math|P=O<around|(|s<rsup|u>|)>> and then
    <math|g=Q+O<around|(|s<rsup|b-u>|)>>. The exact identity

    <\equation*>
      f*g-P*Q=<around|(|f-P|)>*g+P*<around|(|g-Q|)>
    </equation*>

    shows that both terms are <math|O<around|(|s<rsup|b>|)>>. Since
    <math|P*Q\<in\>\<cal-P\>>, this proves the required approximation. In
    particular it proves closure under every finite product, including the
    empty product <math|1>.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Radial Laplace integrals>

  For <math|m\<geqslant\>0> and <math|j,k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, put

  <\equation*>
    M<rsub|j,k><around|(|s|)>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>>r<rsup|j+2>*<around|(|m<rsup|2>+r<rsup|2>|)><rsup|<around|(|k-1|)>/2>*e<rsup|-s*<sqrt|m<rsup|2>+r<rsup|2>>>*<space|0.17em>d*r,<space|2em>s\<gtr\>0.
  </equation*>

  <\proposition>
    <label|prop:profile-moments>Every <math|M<rsub|j,k>> has power\Ulogarithm
    approximations.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    If <math|m=0> the integral is <math|<around|(|j+k+1|)>!s<rsup|-<around|(|j+k+2|)>>>.
    If <math|m\<gtr\>0>, set <math|t=<sqrt|1+<around|(|r/m|)><rsup|2>>> and
    <math|\<nu\>=<around|(|j+2|)>/2>. The integral for <math|k=0> becomes
    <math|m<rsup|j+2>*<big|int><rsub|1><rsup|\<infty\>>e<rsup|-m*s*t>*<around|(|t<rsup|2>-1|)><rsup|\<nu\>-1/2>*<space|0.17em>d*t>.
    Since <math|m*s\<gtr\>0> and <math|\<nu\>\<gtr\>-1/2>, the Bessel
    integral <cite-detail|DLMF|Eq.<nbsp>10.32.8> gives

    <\equation*>
      M<rsub|j,0><around|(|s|)>=<frac|m<rsup|j+2>*\<Gamma\>*<around|(|\<nu\>+1/2|)>|<sqrt|\<pi\>>><around*|(|<frac|2|m*s>|)><rsup|\<nu\>>*K<rsub|\<nu\>>*<around|(|m*s|)>.
    </equation*>

    For integer <math|\<nu\>>, Eq.<nbsp>10.31.1 of <cite|DLMF> writes
    <math|z<rsup|-\<nu\>>*K<rsub|\<nu\>><around|(|z|)>> as a Laurent series
    with finitely many negative powers plus <math|log z> times a power
    series. For noninteger <math|\<nu\>>, Eqs.<nbsp>10.25.2 and 10.27.4
    express it as a power series plus <math|z<rsup|-2*\<nu\>>> times a power
    series. Here <math|2*\<nu\>=j+2> is integral. To control derivatives of
    any fixed order, truncate the analytic factors sufficiently far.
    Differentiating an analytic tail <math|s<rsup|a>*h<around|(|s|)>> lowers
    its order by at most the number of derivatives. A log factor costs at
    most one further power in the bound. Replacing <math|z> by <math|m*s>
    changes <math|log z> to <math|log s+log m>. Finally
    <math|M<rsub|j,k>=<around|(|-\<partial\><rsub|s>|)><rsup|k>*M<rsub|j,0>>
    by dominated differentiation on compact subsets of <math|s\<gtr\>0>,
    since <math|m\<gtr\>0> and the differentiated integrands decay
    exponentially at infinity. This supplies the approximations of every
    integer order.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|From modes to the logarithmic obstruction>

  A mode consists of a mass <math|m\<geqslant\>0>, integers
  <math|j,k\<geqslant\>0>, and a continuous real function <math|P> on
  <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|3>> homogeneous of degree <math|j> under positive
  scalings. Its Euclidean profile is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:profile-mode>K<rsub|m,P,k><around|(|s|)>=<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|3>><frac|\<omega\><rsub|m><around|(|p|)><rsup|k>*P<around|(|p|)>|2*\<omega\><rsub|m><around|(|p|)>>*e<rsup|-s*\<omega\><rsub|m><around|(|p|)>>*<space|0.17em>d*p,<space|2em>\<omega\><rsub|m><around|(|p|)>=<sqrt|m<rsup|2>+<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>>.
  </equation>

  Let <math|d*\<sigma\>> denote ordinary surface area measure on the unit
  sphere. The maximum of <math|<around|\||P|\|>> on that sphere is finite. In
  polar coordinates, <math|<around|\||P*<around|(|r*\<theta\>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*r<rsup|j>>,
  so absolute integrability follows from that of <math|M<rsub|j,k>>. Fubini's
  theorem and homogeneity therefore give the exact normalization

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:profile-angular>K<rsub|m,P,k><around|(|s|)>=<frac|1|2><around*|(|<big|int><rsub|S<rsup|2>>P<around|(|\<theta\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<sigma\><around|(|\<theta\>|)>|)>*M<rsub|j,k><around|(|s|)>.
  </equation>

  A track is a finite product of mode profiles; the empty track is <math|1>.
  By Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:profile-moments> and
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:profile-algebra>, every track has
  power\Ulogarithm approximations. The same is true of the real part of a
  finite complex linear combination of tracks, because all the individual
  tracks are real.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|No inverse logarithmic leading term><label|lem:profile-log> If a
    real function <math|f> has an approximation of order zero and

    <\equation*>
      lim<rsub|s*\<downarrow\>*0> s<rsup|8><around*|(|log
      s<rsup|-1>|)><rsup|2>*f<around|(|s|)>=A\<in\>\<bbb-R\>,
    </equation*>

    then <math|A=0>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Write <math|f=P+O<around|(|1|)>> with <math|P\<in\>\<cal-P\>>. Since
    <math|s<rsup|8><around|(|log s<rsup|-1>|)><rsup|2>\<to\>0>, the same
    weighted limit holds for <math|P>. Restrict to
    <math|s<rsub|n>=2<rsup|-n>>. A monomial
    <math|c<rsub|a,k>*s<rsup|a><around|(|log s|)><rsup|k>> contributes

    <\equation*>
      c<rsub|a,k><around*|(|log <around|(|1/2|)>|)><rsup|k+2><around*|(|2<rsup|-<around|(|8+a|)>>|)><rsup|n>*n<rsup|k+2>.
    </equation*>

    Combine equal pairs <math|<around|(|a,k|)>> and discard zero
    coefficients. The remaining finite sum has distinct pairs
    <math|<around|(|\<lambda\>,q|)>=<around|(|2<rsup|-<around|(|8+a|)>>,k+2|)>>,
    with <math|\<lambda\>\<gtr\>0> and <math|q\<geqslant\>2>. Order them
    lexicographically and choose the largest pair
    <math|<around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>,q<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>>. If the sum
    is empty, its limit is zero. If <math|\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>\<less\>1>,
    every term tends to zero: writing <math|\<lambda\>=e<rsup|-c>> with
    <math|c\<gtr\>0> reduces this to <math|n<rsup|q>*e<rsup|-c*n>\<to\>0>.
    For completeness, <math|e<rsup|c*n>\<geqslant\><around|(|c*n|)><rsup|q+1>/<around|(|q+1|)>>!
    proves that limit.

    If <math|\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>\<geqslant\>1>, then
    <math|D<rsub|n>=\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|n>*n<rsup|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>\<to\>\<infty\>>.
    Each smaller term divided by <math|D<rsub|n>> tends to zero. With a
    smaller base, exponential decay dominates any remaining power of <math|n>
    by the same estimate; with equal base, the ratio is a negative power of
    <math|n>. Thus the finite sum divided by <math|D<rsub|n>> tends to its
    nonzero leading coefficient. But the sum has the finite limit <math|A>,
    and division by <math|D<rsub|n>\<to\>\<infty\>> makes its limit zero, a
    contradiction. Only the first case is possible, and it has <math|A=0>.
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|thm:profile-exclusion>Let <math|T:<around|(|0,\<infty\>|)>\<to\>\<bbb-C\>>
    satisfy

    <\equation*>
      lim<rsub|s*\<downarrow\>*0> s<rsup|8><around*|(|log
      s<rsup|-1>|)><rsup|2>*T<around|(|s|)>=A,<space|2em>A\<gtr\>0.
    </equation*>

    Then <math|T> cannot agree on any interval
    <math|<around|(|0,\<delta\>|)>> with a finite complex linear combination
    of tracks built from <eqref|eq:profile-mode>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    If such agreement held, the real part of that combination would have an
    order-zero approximation. Taking real parts in the displayed limit and
    using the agreement would contradict Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:profile-log>.
  </proof>

  This proves the exclusion once the positive trace asymptotic has been
  established for the constructed theory. It imposes no restriction on an
  infinite sum or a continuous distribution of masses, and no such exclusion
  is asserted here. The construction of <math|T> and its positive coefficient
  <math|A> is a separate quantitative step.

  <subsection|The trace coefficient and the passage to all
  radii><label|sec:trace-asymptotic>

  For the perturbative trace anomaly in non-Abelian gauge theory, see
  Collins, Duncan and Joglekar <cite|CollinsDuncanJoglekar>. The
  short-distance two-point function in the selected continuum phase requires
  the estimates on the Wilson hierarchy given here.

  Two different normalizations enter the trace asymptotic. Translation Ward
  identities fix the amplitude of the stress tensor, while the running Wilson
  coefficient fixes the logarithmic scale. After those normalizations, the
  numerical coefficient comes from a free curvature contraction. Finally one
  must remove the rounded positions and the chosen sequence of scales. We
  separate these operations because none can be replaced by choosing a
  convenient normalization of the trace after the fact.

  <subsubsection|The free scalar contraction>

  Put <math|N=dim<rsub|\<bbb-R\>> \<frak-g\>> and use an orthonormal color
  basis. The reference potential covariance in four Euclidean dimensions is
  <math|\<delta\><rsup|A*B>*\<delta\><rsub|a*b>*D<around|(|x|)>>, where

  <\equation*>
    D<around|(|x|)>=<frac|1|4*\<pi\><rsup|2><around|\||x|\|><rsup|2>>,<space|2em>x\<neq\>0.
  </equation*>

  Indices <math|a,b,i,j> range over the four coordinate directions and
  <math|A,B> over the <math|N> colors. The curvature covariance obtained by
  differentiating this potential is <math|\<delta\><rsup|A*B>*C<rsub|a*b*i*j><around|(|x|)>>.
  Explicitly,

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|C<rsub|a*b*i*j><around|(|x|)>=>|<cell|-\<delta\><rsub|b*j>*\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<partial\><rsub|i>*D<around|(|x|)>+\<delta\><rsub|b*i>*\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<partial\><rsub|j>*D<around|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|+\<delta\><rsub|a*j>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*\<partial\><rsub|i>*D<around|(|x|)>-\<delta\><rsub|a*i>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*\<partial\><rsub|j>*D<around|(|x|)>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Write <math|r=<around|\||x|\|>> and <math|H<rsub|a*i>=\<delta\><rsub|a*i>-2*x<rsub|a>*x<rsub|i>/r<rsup|2>>.
  Differentiating <math|D> gives

  <\equation*>
    \<partial\><rsub|a>*\<partial\><rsub|i>*D<around|(|x|)>=<frac|1|4*\<pi\><rsup|2>>*<around|(|-2*\<delta\><rsub|a*i>*r<rsup|-4>+8*x<rsub|a>*x<rsub|i>*r<rsup|-6>|)>,<space|2em>C<rsub|a*b*i*j><around|(|x|)>=<frac|H<rsub|a*i>*H<rsub|b*j>-H<rsub|a*j>*H<rsub|b*i>|\<pi\><rsup|2>*r<rsup|4>>.
  </equation*>

  For the second identity expand the products of <math|H>. Their quartic
  terms cancel, leaving exactly the four second-derivative terms above.
  Moreover <math|H=I-2*u*u<rsup|<math-ss|T>>> for the unit vector
  <math|u=x/r>, hence <math|H*H<rsup|<math-ss|T>>=I>. It follows that

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i,j><around|(|H<rsub|a*i>*H<rsub|b*j>-H<rsub|a*j>*H<rsub|b*i>|)><rsup|2>>|<cell|=2<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i>H<rsub|a*i><rsup|2>|)><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|j>H<rsub|b*j><rsup|2>|)>-2<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i>H<rsub|a*i>*H<rsub|b*i>|)><rsup|2>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=2-2*\<delta\><rsub|a*b>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Summing over the sixteen ordered pairs <math|<around|(|a,b|)>> gives
  <math|24>.

  The reference scalar is

  <\equation*>
    O<around|(|x|)>=<frac|1|4>*<big|sum><rsub|A,a,b>:F<rsub|a*b><rsup|A><around|(|x|)>*F<rsub|a*b><rsup|A><around|(|x|)>:,
  </equation*>

  where the sum is over ordered coordinate pairs. Here the colons mean the
  quadratic Wick field: in its two-point function only contractions between
  the two different positions are included. There are exactly two such
  pairings. Color contraction gives <math|<big|sum><rsub|A,B><around|(|\<delta\><rsup|A*B>|)><rsup|2>=N>,
  and therefore

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-free-scalar><around|\<langle\>|O<around|(|x|)>*O<around|(|0|)>|\<rangle\>><rsub|0>=<frac|2*N|16>*<big|sum><rsub|a,b,i,j>C<rsub|a*b*i*j><around|(|x|)><rsup|2>=<frac|3*N|\<pi\><rsup|4><around|\||x|\|><rsup|8>>.
  </equation>

  This computation applies to the actual reference table used in the selected
  comparison. Indeed, <eqref|eq:scribe-reference-dictionary> identifies its
  correlations with Wick contractions for the potential kernel
  <math|D=<around|(|4*\<pi\><rsup|2><around|\||x|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>,
  and <eqref|eq:scribe-reference-scalar-identification> identifies its field
  <math|\<cal-O\>> with precisely the ordered quarter-square field <math|O>
  above. Thus the <em|reference normalization> is a consequence of that
  same-table dictionary and the calculation, rather than an independent
  choice of a free comparison model:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-reference-normalization>S<rsub|0>*<around|(|O,O;x<rsub|0>,x<rsub|1>|)>=<frac|3*N|\<pi\><rsup|4>*<around|\||x<rsub|0>-x<rsub|1>|\|><rsup|8>>*<space|2em><around|(|x<rsub|0>\<neq\>x<rsub|1>|)>.
  </equation>

  The factor <math|1/4> and the use of ordered pairs are essential to the
  coefficient.

  <subsubsection|Fixing the physical stress amplitude>

  Here is the finite-dimensional step that prevents an arbitrary scalar
  multiple of the stress tensor from entering the answer. All vector spaces
  in this paragraph are real, including the real spaces of self-adjoint
  tensor fields. Let <math|\<cal-K\>> be the space of centered symmetric
  tensors with the required rotation symmetry, let <math|\<cal-V\>> be the
  space in which their divergences lie, and let
  <math|D:\<cal-K\>\<to\>\<cal-V\>> be the divergence map. The reference
  theory has corresponding spaces <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|0>,\<cal-V\><rsub|0>>
  and divergence <math|D<rsub|0>>. The following precise finite-dimensional
  data are used; they are the properties under which
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-trace-rank-one> is proved:
  <math|D<rsub|0>> is onto, <math|dim ker D<rsub|0>=1>, <math|dim
  \<cal-K\>=dim \<cal-K\><rsub|0>>, and there is a surjective map
  <math|Q:\<cal-K\>\<to\>\<cal-V\><rsub|0>> with <math|ker Q=ker D>.
  Rank\Unullity then gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-conserved-rank>dim ker D=dim \<cal-K\>-dim
    \<cal-V\><rsub|0>=dim \<cal-K\><rsub|0>-dim \<cal-V\><rsub|0>=1.
  </equation>

  In the construction the surjectivity of <math|Q> is obtained from a
  surjective finite-dimensional comparison composed with a test map into
  <math|\<cal-K\>>; a surjective composition is sufficient to make <math|Q>
  itself surjective. The statements about <math|D<rsub|0>> are proved in
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-trace-free-closure-rank>. The other
  properties are supplied by the conserved-line construction of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-stress-normalization>, on the
  completed maps of the selected trace gallery, as follows. The map
  <math|C<rsub|i>*B=<math-up|center><around|(|U<rsub|i,4>*B|)>> is an
  isomorphism of the two centered tensor spaces: its inverse centers
  <math|U<rsub|i,4><rsup|-1>>, and the unit corrections cancel because
  <math|U<rsub|i,4>*1=1>. This proves the dimension identity. With
  <math|V<rsub|i>> the induced isomorphism of vector arrays,
  <math|V<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*r<rsub|i>*D*C<rsub|i>> converges to
  <math|D<rsub|0>> by the derivative-transport argument there. If
  <math|R<rsup|<math-up|right>>> is any linear right inverse of
  <math|D<rsub|0>>, then <math|V<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*r<rsub|i>*D*C<rsub|i>*R<rsup|<math-up|right>>\<to\>I>
  and is invertible for large <math|i>. Thus
  <math|Q<rsub|i>=V<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*r<rsub|i>*D> is onto and, since
  <math|r<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> and <math|V<rsub|i>> is invertible, <math|ker
  Q<rsub|i>=ker D>. Choose any one such large <math|i> for <math|Q>. This
  construction uses only the uniform short-test estimate and coherence of the
  gallery, not the trace asymptotic or its normalization.

  Let <math|R\<in\>ker D> be the physical centered stress tensor. At scales
  <math|r<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> let <math|U<rsub|i>> be the calibrated isomorphism
  from reference grade-four fields to interacting grade-four fields. On
  tensors set

  <\equation*>
    Y<rsub|i><around|(|T|)><rsub|a*b>=r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*U<rsub|i><rsup|-1><around|(|T<rsub|a*b>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Suppose the comparison construction supplies eventually conserved tensors
  <math|T<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-K\>> and positive numbers <math|B<rsub|i>> with

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-trial-stress>Y<rsub|i><around|(|T<rsub|i>|)>\<longrightarrow\>R<rsub|0>,<space|2em>B<rsub|i><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>Y<rsub|i><around|(|T<rsub|i>|)>\<longrightarrow\>4*d<rsub|2>*O.
  </equation>

  In the selected construction these are the corrected tensors
  <math|T<rsub|i>> and the arch scales <math|B<rsub|i>=q<rsub|i>> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-scribe-producer>, with
  <math|R<rsub|0>=M<rsub|0>> and <math|4*d<rsub|2>=-\<kappa\>> there; the two
  limits are <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-field-limit> and
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-flux-limit>, which are conditional on the
  inputs of that section. The normalization test is a shell flux functional
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>> on tensors. For example, fixing a separated
  spectator configuration <math|x>, a smooth cutoff <math|\<chi\>> equal to
  one near that configuration, a direction <math|b>, and one distinguished
  field <math|v>, its form is

  <\equation*>
    \<ell\><rsub|i><around|(|T|)>=-r<rsub|i>*<big|sum><rsub|a><big|int><around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\><rsub|i>|)><around|(|z|)>*S<rsub|T<rsub|a*b>,v<rsub|i>,\<b-q\><rsub|i>>*<around|(|z,r<rsub|i>*x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*z,<space|2em>\<chi\><rsub|i><around|(|z|)>=\<chi\>*<around|(|z/r<rsub|i>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The derivatives of <math|\<chi\><rsub|i>> are supported away from the
  spectators, so the integrals involve separated correlations. The
  translation Ward identity for the physical tensor identifies this with a
  derivative insertion. The needed convergence of that insertion and of the
  comparison shell integral has the following exact consequence:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-flux-test>\<ell\><rsub|i><around|(|R|)>\<longrightarrow\>y\<neq\>0,<space|2em>\<ell\><rsub|i><around|(|T<rsub|i>|)>\<longrightarrow\>y.
  </equation>

  The same nonzero <math|y> occurs because <math|R<rsub|0>> has the reference
  translation Ward identity. The detecting reference derivative field and the
  jet estimates justifying these two limits are proved for the actual
  reference table and the completed gallery charts in the proportionality
  argument of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-scribe-producer>,
  where the two limits appear as <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-common-test>.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lem:trace-normalized-stress>Equations
    <eqref|eq:trace-conserved-rank>\U<eqref|eq:trace-flux-test> imply

    <\equation*>
      Y<rsub|i><around|(|R|)>\<longrightarrow\>R<rsub|0>,<space|2em>B<rsub|i><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>Y<rsub|i><around|(|R|)>\<longrightarrow\>4*d<rsub|2>*O.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The first limit in <eqref|eq:trace-flux-test> implies <math|R\<neq\>0>.
    Since the conserved space is one-dimensional, eventually
    <math|T<rsub|i>=c<rsub|i>*R> for a real scalar <math|c<rsub|i>>.
    Linearity of <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>> gives
    <math|c<rsub|i>*\<ell\><rsub|i><around|(|R|)>=\<ell\><rsub|i><around|(|T<rsub|i>|)>>.
    For large <math|i> the denominator <math|\<ell\><rsub|i><around|(|R|)>>
    is nonzero, and the two limits give <math|c<rsub|i>\<to\>y/y=1>. In
    particular <math|c<rsub|i>> is eventually nonzero and
    <math|c<rsub|i><rsup|-1>\<to\>1>. Multiply both limits in
    <eqref|eq:trace-trial-stress> by <math|c<rsub|i><rsup|-1>> and use
    linearity of <math|Y<rsub|i>> and of the trace. This yields exactly the
    asserted limits.
  </proof>

  Centering the trace in the chosen phase can differ from tracing the
  mixed-state-centered tensor <math|R> by a fixed multiple <math|c*\<b-1\>>
  of the unit. If <math|U<rsub|i>*\<b-1\><rsub|0>=\<b-1\>> eventually and
  <math|B<rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>\<to\>0> (for the selected data the latter
  is <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-band-cost>), then

  <\equation*>
    U<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*<around|(|B<rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*c*\<b-1\>|)>=B<rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*c*\<b-1\><rsub|0>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  Consequently the selected centered trace <math|\<Theta\>> has the same
  limit

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-moving-label>a<rsub|i>\<assign\>U<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*<around|(|B<rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*\<Theta\>|)>\<longrightarrow\>4*d<rsub|2>*O.
  </equation>

  The identities above explain how the physical Ward normalization, rather
  than a later rescaling of <math|\<Theta\>>, determines this coefficient.

  <subsubsection|Rounded correlations and moving labels>

  We next state exactly the approximation data used to pass from
  <eqref|eq:trace-moving-label> to the trace asymptotic. Write
  <math|S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|v,w;x<rsub|0>,x<rsub|1>|)>> for the
  selected two-point function and <math|S<rsub|0>*<around|(|v,w;x<rsub|0>,x<rsub|1>|)>>
  for the reference one. Both are bilinear in the field labels. The selected
  centered trace is a scalar under Euclidean rotations, and translations
  preserve its correlations. Thus its two-point function has the form

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-radial>S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|\<Theta\>,\<Theta\>;x<rsub|0>,x<rsub|1>|)>=T<around|(|<around|\||x<rsub|0>-x<rsub|1>|\|>|)>*<space|1em><around|(|x<rsub|0>\<neq\>x<rsub|1>|)>.
  </equation>

  To justify this reduction, an orthogonal map sends the nonzero difference
  <math|x<rsub|0>-x<rsub|1>> to <math|-<around|\||x<rsub|0>-x<rsub|1>|\|>*e<rsub|0>>;
  equal-length vectors are related by a reflection, with the identity
  covering equality. A translation then sends the two points to
  <math|-t*e<rsub|0>/2,t*e<rsub|0>/2>, where
  <math|t=<around|\||x<rsub|0>-x<rsub|1>|\|>>. Covariance and the scalar
  transformation law of <math|\<Theta\>> give <eqref|eq:trace-radial>.

  <\assumption>
    <dueto|Trace comparison along scales><label|input:trace-scales> For every
    sequence <math|s<rsub|n>\<gtr\>0> tending to zero there is a subsequence
    <math|r<rsub|i>=s<rsub|n<rsub|i>>>, with <math|n<rsub|i>> strictly
    increasing, and comparison data with the following properties. The maps
    <math|U<rsub|i>> and numbers <math|B<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> satisfy
    <eqref|eq:trace-moving-label> and

    <\equation*>
      <frac|log <around|(|r<rsub|i><rsup|-1>|)>|B<rsub|i>>\<longrightarrow\><frac|1|\<kappa\>>,<space|2em>\<kappa\>\<gtr\>0,<space|2em>d<rsub|2>=-<frac|\<kappa\>|4>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|\<kappa\>> is the slope of the selected running clock and the
    same constant in its local response payment. Only this equality of the
    two constants, not their separate numerical evaluation, enters the trace
    coefficient. There are rounded configurations
    <math|q<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>\<to\>x> and errors
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>\<to\>0>. Let <math|E<rsub|0>> be the
    reference grade-four field space and fix its finite basis
    <math|<around|(|e<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>>. At every coordinatewise separated
    configuration <math|x> in the interior of the comparison window,
    eventually

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around*|\||S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|U<rsub|i>*e<rsub|\<alpha\>>,U<rsub|i>*e<rsub|\<beta\>>;r<rsub|i>*q<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>|)>-S<rsub|0>*<around|(|e<rsub|\<alpha\>>,e<rsub|\<beta\>>;q<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|30mm>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>*<around*|(|1+M<rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>><around|(|q<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>|)>|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:trace-basis-error>>>>>
    </align>

    where <math|M<rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>>> is continuous near <math|x>. The
    reference correlations are continuous there. The comparison window
    includes a neighborhood of <math|x<around|(|t|)>=<around|(|0,<around|(|t/2,t/2,t/2,t/2|)>|)>>
    for each <math|1/2\<less\>t\<less\>2>.
  </assumption>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The same-history scale supplier><label|prop:trace-scale-supplier>Fix
    the common selected history and its extraction before choosing any
    sequence of radii. Use its completed gallery, its physical stress and
    centered trace, and the physical transfer
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-physical-transfer> with the clock limit
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-clock>. Then these objects supply
    Input<nbsp><reference|input:trace-scales> for the same selected phase.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The scale estimates for the paid response, including the gain bound, are
    supplied by Lemma <reference|lem:trace-bill-scale-tickets>. The actual
    coupling band is verified in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-probe-assembly>.
    Its local atom bounds are supplied by
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:trace-paid-atom-suppliers>, using the
    probe and cloud estimates of Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:trace-eve-probe-cloud>;
    their thresholds are included before the common history is chosen. Its
    grade-five buffer uses the <hlink|actual Crown
    witness|#par:trace-actual-crown>. The sensor used in the scribe is the
    one on these completed charts: its estimate is uniform over all vectors,
    so it applies to the growing principal forecast of norm
    <math|O<around|(|t<rsub|i>|)>>, not just to fixed reference labels. The
    three actual branch tensors are recorded along the same fixed-level
    ultrafilter. The principal and false traces use the exponent-one washout
    of the branch theorem; the true trace instead uses its exact
    normalization <math|4*\<cal-O\>>. These are distinct conclusions, as
    verified in the sensor and branch application of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-scribe-producer>. Its
    arch identity <eqref|eq:scribe-same-arch> identifies the branch coupling
    with the summit arch scale; alternatively the ratio lemma there transfers
    all three conclusions without assuming an unsupported equality. The
    conserved-line dimension is <eqref|eq:stress-physical-line>, with the
    same-table application explained above. Thus the local estimates in the
    physical transfer are those of the selected objects, not additional
    freely postulated responses or maps.

    Let <math|s<rsub|n>\<gtr\>0> tend to zero. Apply
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-firesky-selection> to this sequence.
    It extracts strictly increasing indices <math|n<rsub|i>> and charts with
    radii exactly <math|r<rsub|i>=s<rsub|n<rsub|i>>>. The background history
    and its extraction remain the already fixed ones; only charts and their
    indices are selected after the sequence is given. All finite-grade
    requirements use tails of these charts. Apply the threshold refinement of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:trace-rounded-receipt> in this
    selection: Corollary<nbsp><reference|prop:trace-selected-rounded-receipt>
    supplies its allowance and level budget on the same funded family. Thus
    (G3) holds at the selected root depth with cost
    <math|<around|(|i+1|)><rsup|-1>>, and the chamber lemma supplies (G4) at
    that very depth.

    Take <math|U<rsub|i>> to be the completed grade-four map and
    <math|B<rsub|i>=q<rsub|i>> to be the actual summit arch scale. The
    physical transfer gives <math|U<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*<around|(|B<rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>R|)>\<to\>-\<kappa\>*O>.
    The phase-centered trace differs from <math|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>R>
    by one fixed scalar multiple of the unit. Unit preservation and
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-scribe-band-cost> remove this difference after
    pullback. Hence <eqref|eq:trace-moving-label> holds with
    <math|4*d<rsub|2>=-\<kappa\>>. The clock limit uses this same arch and
    same positive slope <math|\<kappa\>>, giving the required logarithmic
    ratio. Neither the tensor nor its scalar normalization is reselected
    along this subsequence.

    Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:trace-rounded-input> supplies the rounded
    configurations, pairwise basis errors and common comparison window for
    these maps and this phase. Its rounding bound tends to zero; the
    reference kernels are smooth off the diagonals by their explicit Wick
    formula. In particular they are continuous at every eligible
    configuration, and the control functions there are bounded in a
    neighborhood of that configuration. The window contains a neighborhood of
    every <math|x<around|(|t|)>> with <math|1/2\<less\>t\<less\>2>. These are
    all the remaining clauses of Input<nbsp><reference|input:trace-scales>.
  </proof>

  The physical transfer in this proposition is the output of the same-history
  response, sensor, branch and shell constructions of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-scribe-producer>. The
  external sensor and branch units are used there as distinct statements,
  with their actual inputs matched separately. The proposition records the
  order of choices needed at the endpoint; it does not replace any of those
  quantitative constructions by compactness. The positive trace spectral
  measure is constructed in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:trace-spectrum>
  from this same phase and centered tensor.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lem:trace-moving-correlation>Assume
    Input<nbsp><reference|input:trace-scales>, the radial form
    <eqref|eq:trace-radial> and the reference normalization
    <eqref|eq:trace-reference-normalization>. Then, along each comparison
    subsequence of the input and for each eligible fixed <math|x>,

    <\equation*>
      r<rsub|i><rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|r<rsub|i><rsup|-1>|)>*T*<around|(|r<rsub|i>*<around|\||q<rsub|i><around|(|x|)><rsub|0>-q<rsub|i><around|(|x|)><rsub|1>|\|>|)>\<longrightarrow\><frac|A|<around|\||x<rsub|0>-x<rsub|1>|\|><rsup|8>>,<space|2em>A=<frac|3*N|\<pi\><rsup|4>>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The right side of <eqref|eq:trace-basis-error> tends to zero, since
    <math|M<rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>><around|(|q<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>|)>>
    converges and is therefore bounded. Continuity of <math|S<rsub|0>> gives
    convergence of the left correlation to
    <math|S<rsub|0>*<around|(|e<rsub|\<alpha\>>,e<rsub|\<beta\>>;x|)>>. This
    also holds for convergent, varying labels: expand each of them in the
    fixed finite basis. Every coordinate converges, and bilinearity expresses
    the correlation as a finite sum of products of two coordinates and one of
    the convergent basis correlations. In particular,
    <eqref|eq:trace-moving-label> gives

    <\equation*>
      S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|U<rsub|i>*a<rsub|i>,U<rsub|i>*a<rsub|i>;r<rsub|i>*q<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>|)>\<longrightarrow\><around|(|4*d<rsub|2>|)><rsup|2>*S<rsub|0>*<around|(|O,O;x|)>.
    </equation*>

    The definition of <math|a<rsub|i>>, bilinearity and
    <eqref|eq:trace-radial> identify the expression on the left with
    <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|2>*r<rsub|i><rsup|8>*T*<around|(|r<rsub|i>*<around|\||q<rsub|i><around|(|x|)><rsub|0>-q<rsub|i><around|(|x|)><rsub|1>|\|>|)>>.
    Here <eqref|eq:trace-radial> applies because the two points of
    <math|r<rsub|i>*q<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>> are distinct for large <math|i>:
    <math|q<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>\<to\>x> and the two points of <math|x> are
    distinct. Multiply by <math|<around|(|log
    <around|(|r<rsub|i><rsup|-1>|)>/B<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>> and use
    <eqref|eq:trace-reference-normalization>. The limiting coefficient is

    <\equation*>
      <frac|<around|(|4*d<rsub|2>|)><rsup|2>|\<kappa\><rsup|2>>*<frac|3*N|\<pi\><rsup|4>>=<frac|<around|(|-\<kappa\>|)><rsup|2>|\<kappa\><rsup|2>>*<frac|3*N|\<pi\><rsup|4>>=<frac|3*N|\<pi\><rsup|4>>.
    </equation*>

    All divisions are legitimate since <math|\<kappa\>\<gtr\>0> and
    <math|B<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0>. The common slope cancels before its numerical
    value is needed; this is also the cancellation in
    <eqref|eq:scribe-slope-cancellation>.
  </proof>

  <subsection|The pointwise comparison at rounded
  configurations><label|subsec:trace-rounded-comparison>

  We now derive the pointwise clause <eqref|eq:trace-basis-error> of
  Input<nbsp><reference|input:trace-scales>, together with its window and
  continuity clauses, from the selected gallery data of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-firesky-gallery>, the pairwise
  comparison receipt contained in those data, and the phase construction of
  Sections<nbsp><reference|ex-phases>\U<reference|ex-phase-fields>. The
  derivation is conditional on the inputs (G1)\U(G4) and (Ph1)\U(Ph3) listed
  below; the only quantitative estimate it uses is the pairwise receipt (G3).
  For the selected funded family, (G3) is supplied by
  Corollary<nbsp><reference|prop:trace-selected-rounded-receipt>. All other
  steps are proved here or are results proved earlier in the text.

  <paragraph|Configurations and the comparison window.> For
  <math|n\<geqslant\>1> a configuration is

  <\equation*>
    x=<around|(|x<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n-1>|)>\<in\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>
  </equation*>

  , with the Euclidean norm of <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4*n>>. It is
  <em|coordinatewise separated> if for every coordinate direction
  <math|a\<in\><around|{|0,1,2,3|}>> the numbers
  <math|x<rsub|0,a>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n-1,a>> are pairwise distinct; such a
  configuration has distinct points. These configurations form an open set.
  The comparison window is

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-W\>=<around|{|x\<in\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|2>:<around|\||x<rsub|0>-x<rsub|1>|\|>\<geqslant\>10<rsup|-2>,<around|\||x|\|>\<leqslant\>100|}>.
  </equation*>

  <paragraph|Rounding.> For <math|m\<gtr\>0> and
  <math|x\<in\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>> write
  <math|<around|\<lceil\>|m*x|\<rceil\>>> for the coordinatewise ceiling, an
  element of <math|<around|(|\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>>, and put

  <\equation*>
    \<rho\><rsub|m><around|(|x|)>=m<rsup|-1>*<around|\<lceil\>|m*x|\<rceil\>>.
  </equation*>

  Each coordinate of <math|\<rho\><rsub|m><around|(|x|)>-x> lies in
  <math|<around|[|0,m<rsup|-1>|)>>, so

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-rounding-error><around|\||\<rho\><rsub|m><around|(|x|)>-x|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|2*<sqrt|n>|m>.
  </equation>

  Rounding is idempotent: <math|<around|\<lceil\>|m*\<rho\><rsub|m><around|(|x|)>|\<rceil\>>=<around|\<lceil\>|m*x|\<rceil\>>>,
  because <math|m*\<rho\><rsub|m><around|(|x|)>> already has integer
  coordinates.

  <paragraph|The selected gallery data.> We use the following data, indexed
  by <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, together with an extraction order and an
  ultrafilter <math|\<cal-L\>> on mesh\Uperiod pairs <math|<around|(|N,p|)>>
  finer than its fused filter, along which the mesh <math|N> tends to
  infinity.

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(G1)><em|Levels and radii.> A chart level <math|k<rsub|i>> with
    <math|k<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>, a base arc <math|b<rsub|i>\<gtr\>5>, and
    the radius

    <\equation*>
      r<rsub|i>=a<rsub|k<rsub|i>>*m<rsub|i>,<space|2em>m<rsub|i>=\<mu\><rsub|k<rsub|i>><rsup|<space|0.17em>b<rsub|i>>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|a<rsub|k>\<gtr\>0> is the lattice spacing at depth <math|k>
    and <math|\<mu\><rsub|k>\<geqslant\>1> is the clock monitor, so that
    <math|r<rsub|i>=g<rsub|b<rsub|i>><around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>> in the notation
    of <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-firesky-gap>. The clock satisfies
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|k><rsup|5>\<to\>\<infty\>> as <math|k\<to\>\<infty\>>.

    <item*|(G2)><em|Chart readings and their limits.> For each grade <math|d>
    and each <math|i\<geqslant\>d>, the grade-<math|d> chart of level
    <math|i> provides linear maps <math|X<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|N,p>>
    from the reference grade-<math|d> field space <math|E<rsub|d>> into the
    raw grade-<math|d> label space, one for every <math|<around|(|N,p|)>>.
    For each fixed <math|d>, eventually in <math|i>, these converge along
    <math|\<cal-L\>> to a linear map <math|Y<rsub|i>>, and the moving chart
    map of Input<nbsp><reference|input:trace-scales> at grade <math|d> is
    <math|U<rsub|i>=<math-ss|P><rsub|d>Y<rsub|i>>, where
    <math|<math-ss|P><rsub|d>> is the quotient projection of the selected
    table. (This is the finite-dimensional extraction of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-firesky-gallery>.)

    <item*|(G3)><em|Pairwise comparison receipt at cost
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>=<around|(|i+1|)><rsup|-1>>.> This is part of
    the one-step chart output recorded in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-firesky-gallery>; its content
    is stated precisely in the next paragraph.

    <item*|(G4)><em|Chamber interiority.> There is a root depth
    <math|w<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>k<rsub|i>> such that, if <math|x> is fit at
    level <math|i> (defined below), then for all sufficiently large meshes
    <math|N> and every period <math|p>, every grade-<math|d> insertion at a
    site <math|a<rsub|k<rsub|i>>*<around|\<lceil\>|m<rsub|i>*x|\<rceil\>><rsub|c>>
    belongs to the chamber-local domain of the centered tiling at depth
    <math|w<rsub|i>>.
  </enumerate>

  A configuration <math|x\<in\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|2>> is
  <em|fit at level <math|i>> if the grid configuration
  <math|a<rsub|k<rsub|i>>*<around|\<lceil\>|m<rsub|i>*x|\<rceil\>>> is
  coordinatewise separated and each of its coordinates has absolute value at
  most one eighth of the spacing of the middle cell of the chart. Fitness
  depends on <math|x> only through <math|<around|\<lceil\>|m<rsub|i>*x|\<rceil\>>>;
  by idempotence of rounding, <math|x> is fit if and only if
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|m<rsub|i>><around|(|x|)>> is fit.

  <paragraph|The pairwise receipt.> Fix <math|i>, a grade
  <math|d\<leqslant\>i>, a configuration <math|x> fit at level <math|i>, and
  two vectors <math|e,e<rprime|'>\<in\>E<rsub|d>>. Write
  <math|q=\<rho\><rsub|m<rsub|i>><around|(|x|)>>. Put

  <\equation*>
    j<rsub|0>=S<rsub|0>*<around|(|e;q<rsub|0>|)>,<space|2em>j<rsub|1>=S<rsub|0>*<around|(|e<rprime|'>;q<rsub|1>|)>,<space|2em>\<kappa\>=S<rsub|0>*<around|(|e,e<rprime|'>;q<rsub|0>,q<rsub|1>|)>-j<rsub|0>*j<rsub|1>,
  </equation*>

  the reference one-point values and the reference connected two-point value
  at the rounded points; these are defined because <math|q> has distinct
  points. For <math|<around|(|N,p|)>> let <math|f<rsub|0><rsup|N,p>> and
  <math|f<rsub|1><rsup|N,p>> be the finite-volume insertions of the labels
  <math|X<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|N,p>*e> and
  <math|X<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|N,p>*e<rprime|'>> at the two lattice
  sites <math|a<rsub|k<rsub|i>>*<around|\<lceil\>|m<rsub|i>*x|\<rceil\>><rsub|0>>
  and <math|a<rsub|k<rsub|i>>*<around|\<lceil\>|m<rsub|i>*x|\<rceil\>><rsub|1>>.
  Let <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|root>>>
  denote the root test of the centered tiling at depth <math|w<rsub|i>> for
  the Wilson law at <math|<around|(|N,p|)>>, as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-phase-detector-moments>. The receipt
  (G3) asserts: for every <math|i>, every <math|d\<leqslant\>i>, all basis
  vectors <math|e,e<rprime|'>> of <math|E<rsub|d>>, all
  <math|N\<gtr\>k<rsub|i>> and all <math|p>, and every <math|x> that is fit
  at level <math|i> with <math|q\<in\>\<cal-W\>>,

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|c><rsup|N,p>-j<rsub|c>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|root>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>*<space|2em><around|(|c=0,1|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:trace-rounded-receipt-one>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|f<rsub|0><rsup|N,p>-j<rsub|0>|)>*<around|(|f<rsub|1><rsup|N,p>-j<rsub|1>|)>-\<kappa\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|root>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>.<eq-number><label|eq:trace-rounded-receipt-two>>>>>
  </align>

  It also asserts the following <em|width clause>: for every
  <math|t\<in\><around|(|\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|2>> such that
  <math|y=m<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*t> is coordinatewise separated and
  <math|<around|\||y|\|>\<leqslant\>100>, the configuration <math|y> is fit
  at level <math|i>.

  The receipt is formulated with a cumulant-corrected polynomial, and we
  record why its three nonempty parts are exactly the left sides above. Let
  <math|F> be the reference moment profile at <math|q>, with
  <math|F<rsub|\<varnothing\>>=1>, <math|F<rsub|<around|{|c|}>>=j<rsub|c>>
  and <math|F<rsub|<around|{|0,1|}>>=S<rsub|0>*<around|(|e,e<rprime|'>;q<rsub|0>,q<rsub|1>|)>>.
  The corrected polynomial <math|\<Phi\>> is the unique family
  <math|<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|s>|)><rsub|s\<subseteq\><around|{|0,1|}>>>
  with <math|\<Phi\><rsub|\<varnothing\>>=1> and

  <\equation*>
    <big|prod><rsub|c\<in\>s>f<rsub|c><rsup|N,p>=<big|sum><rsub|u\<subseteq\>s>F<rsub|u>*<space|0.17em>\<Phi\><rsub|s\<setminus\>u>*<space|2em><around|(|s\<subseteq\><around|{|0,1|}>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Uniqueness and existence follow by induction on <math|<around|\||s|\|>>,
  since the term <math|u=\<varnothing\>> is <math|\<Phi\><rsub|s>>. For
  <math|s=<around|{|c|}>> the identity reads
  <math|f<rsub|c><rsup|N,p>=\<Phi\><rsub|<around|{|c|}>>+j<rsub|c>>. For
  <math|s=<around|{|0,1|}>> it reads

  <\equation*>
    f<rsub|0><rsup|N,p>*f<rsub|1><rsup|N,p>=\<Phi\><rsub|<around|{|0,1|}>>+j<rsub|0>*\<Phi\><rsub|<around|{|1|}>>+j<rsub|1>*\<Phi\><rsub|<around|{|0|}>>+S<rsub|0>*<around|(|e,e<rprime|'>;q<rsub|0>,q<rsub|1>|)>,
  </equation*>

  and substituting the one-point parts gives
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|<around|{|0,1|}>>=<around|(|f<rsub|0><rsup|N,p>-j<rsub|0>|)>*<around|(|f<rsub|1><rsup|N,p>-j<rsub|1>|)>-\<kappa\>>.

  <paragraph|Phase inputs.> Let <math|\<cal-A\>>, <math|\<Omega\>>,
  <math|\<Xi\>> and <math|\<mu\>> be the commutative ground algebra, the
  vacuum, the character space and the representing measure of
  Sections<nbsp><reference|ex-phases> and<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-selection>,
  built from the limiting (unselected) table of the same extraction order,
  and let <math|S<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|w|)>=\<chi\><around|(|M<around|(|w|)>|)>>
  be as in <eqref|ex-phase-word>. We assume:

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(Ph1)>the hypotheses of that construction, in particular the
    separated-cluster limit of Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-cluster-train>, the
    representation <eqref|ex-spectral-state> with full support, and the
    separating property of <math|\<Omega\>> for <math|\<cal-A\>> used in the
    proof of Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-character-measure>;

    <item*|(Ph2)>the finite-volume hypotheses of the polynomial-packet
    argument of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-phase-detector-moments>,
    from <eqref|eq:episode-cutoff-power> to <eqref|eq:episode-all-powers>:
    the planted inequality for the Wilson law at every
    <math|<around|(|N,p|)>>, the point-limit theorem for words with
    convergent labels along the selected order, and admission of the fixed
    reference depth; and

    <item*|(Ph3)><em|reality and null relations of the phases>: for every
    <math|\<chi\>\<in\>\<Xi\>> and every distinct-point word <math|w>,
    <math|S<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|<wide|w|\<bar\>>|)>=<wide|S<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|w|)>|\<bar\>>>,
    where <math|<wide|w|\<bar\>>> conjugates all labels without moving the
    points; and for the selected character <math|\<chi\>> the phase
    correlations respect the null relations of the selected table, so that
    for raw labels <math|y,y<rprime|'>> and distinct points
    <math|z<rsub|0>,z<rsub|1>>,

    <\equation*>
      S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|<math-ss|P><rsub|d>y,<math-ss|P><rsub|d>y<rprime|'>;z<rsub|0>,z<rsub|1>|)>=\<chi\>*<around*|(|M*<around|(|y*@*z<rsub|0>*<space|0.17em>y<rprime|'>*@*z<rsub|1>|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    (By Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-no-new-null>, the null relations are
    respected under the detector hypotheses stated there.)
  </enumerate>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Conjugate words><label|lem:trace-rounded-conjugate> Under (Ph1)
    and the reality clause of (Ph3), <math|M<around|(|<wide|w|\<bar\>>|)>=M<around|(|w|)><rsup|\<ast\>>>
    for every distinct-point word <math|w>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Put <math|Z=M<around|(|<wide|w|\<bar\>>|)>-M<around|(|w|)><rsup|\<ast\>>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.
    Every character is a star homomorphism, so
    <math|\<chi\><around|(|Z|)>=S<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|<wide|w|\<bar\>>|)>-<wide|S<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|w|)>|\<bar\>>=0>
    for every <math|\<chi\>\<in\>\<Xi\>>. By <eqref|ex-spectral-state>
    applied to <math|Z<rsup|\<ast\>>*Z>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|Z*\<Omega\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,Z<rsup|\<ast\>>*Z*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>=<big|int><rsub|\<Xi\>><around|\||\<chi\><around|(|Z|)>|\|><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><around|(|\<chi\>|)>=0.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|\<Omega\>> is separating for <math|\<cal-A\>>, <math|Z=0>.
  </proof>

  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:trace-rounded-conjugate> supplies the identity
  <eqref|eq:episode-conjugate>, which Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-phase-detector-moments>
  takes as an input, in the present setting; there it is used only to
  identify the limits of the conjugated packets.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Pointwise rounded comparison><label|prop:trace-rounded-comparison>Assume
    (G1)\U(G4) and (Ph1)\U(Ph3), and let <math|\<chi\>> be the selected
    character. Fix a grade <math|d>. There is <math|i<rsub|0>> such that for
    all <math|i\<geqslant\>i<rsub|0>>, all basis vectors <math|e,e<rprime|'>>
    of <math|E<rsub|d>>, and every configuration <math|x> that is fit at
    level <math|i> with <math|q=\<rho\><rsub|m<rsub|i>><around|(|x|)>\<in\>\<cal-W\>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:trace-rounded-comparison><around*|\||S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|U<rsub|i>*e,U<rsub|i>*e<rprime|'>;r<rsub|i>*q|)>-S<rsub|0>*<around|(|e,e<rprime|'>;q|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>*<around*|(|1+<around|\||S<rsub|0>*<around|(|e;q<rsub|0>|)>|\|>+<around|\||S<rsub|0>*<around|(|e<rprime|'>;q<rsub|1>|)>|\|>|)>.
    </equation>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Choose <math|i<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>d> such that the convergence in (G2)
    holds at grade <math|d> for all <math|i\<geqslant\>i<rsub|0>>. Fix such
    <math|i>, the vectors <math|e,e<rprime|'>> and <math|x>. Put
    <math|y<rsub|0>=Y<rsub|i>*e>, <math|y<rsub|1>=Y<rsub|i>*e<rprime|'>>, so
    that <math|X<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|N,p>*e\<to\>y<rsub|0>> and
    <math|X<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|N,p>*e<rprime|'>\<to\>y<rsub|1>> along
    <math|\<cal-L\>>, and <math|<math-ss|P><rsub|d>y<rsub|0>=U<rsub|i>*e>,
    <math|<math-ss|P><rsub|d>y<rsub|1>=U<rsub|i>*e<rprime|'>>. Let <math|z>
    be the grid configuration <math|a<rsub|k<rsub|i>>*<around|\<lceil\>|m<rsub|i>*x|\<rceil\>>>.
    By (G1),

    <\equation*>
      z=a<rsub|k<rsub|i>>*m<rsub|i>*<space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|m<rsub|i>><around|(|x|)>=r<rsub|i>*q.
    </equation*>

    It is coordinatewise separated because <math|x> is fit; in particular its
    two points are distinct and lie on the depth-<math|k<rsub|i>> grid. Let
    <math|w> be the two-slot word carrying <math|y<rsub|0>> at
    <math|z<rsub|0>> and <math|y<rsub|1>> at <math|z<rsub|1>>, and
    <math|w<rsub|c>> its one-slot subwords.

    <em|Step 1: the two corrected packets.> Consider the polynomial packets,
    in the sense of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-phase-detector-moments>,

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<text|words
      ><around|[|w<rsub|c>|]>,\<varnothing\><text| with coefficients
      >1,-j<rsub|c>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|c=0,1|)>,>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<text|words
      ><around|[|w|]>,<around|[|w<rsub|1>|]>,<around|[|w<rsub|0>|]>,\<varnothing\><text|
      with coefficients >1,-j<rsub|0>,-j<rsub|1>,j<rsub|0>*j<rsub|1>-\<kappa\>.>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
    </align*>

    Their cutoff polynomials at <math|<around|(|N,p|)>> are respectively
    <math|f<rsub|c><rsup|N,p>-j<rsub|c>> and

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|0><rsup|N,p>*f<rsub|1><rsup|N,p>-j<rsub|0>*f<rsub|1><rsup|N,p>-j<rsub|1>*f<rsub|0><rsup|N,p>+j<rsub|0>*j<rsub|1>-\<kappa\>=<around|(|f<rsub|0><rsup|N,p>-j<rsub|0>|)>*<around|(|f<rsub|1><rsup|N,p>-j<rsub|1>|)>-\<kappa\>,
    </equation*>

    and their compressed limits are

    <\equation*>
      D<rsub|c>=M<around|(|w<rsub|c>|)>-j<rsub|c>*I,<space|2em>D<rsub|01>=M<around|(|w|)>-j<rsub|0>*M<around|(|w<rsub|1>|)>-j<rsub|1>*M<around|(|w<rsub|0>|)>+<around|(|j<rsub|0>*j<rsub|1>-\<kappa\>|)>*I.
    </equation*>

    We check the hypotheses of the packet argument. The sites of every word
    lie on the depth-<math|k<rsub|i>> grid and are coordinatewise separated,
    since <math|z> is. The coefficients do not depend on
    <math|<around|(|N,p|)>>. The cutoff labels
    <math|X<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|N,p>*e> and
    <math|X<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|N,p>*e<rprime|'>> converge along
    <math|\<cal-L\>> to the labels <math|y<rsub|0>,y<rsub|1>> of the limiting
    words. The root depth satisfies <math|w<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>k<rsub|i>>.
    Since <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>> along <math|\<cal-L\>>, the set of
    <math|<around|(|N,p|)>> with <math|N\<gtr\>k<rsub|i>> and <math|N> beyond
    the threshold of (G4) belongs to <math|\<cal-L\>>. On that set, (G4) puts
    <math|f<rsub|0><rsup|N,p>> and <math|f<rsub|1><rsup|N,p>> in the
    chamber-local domain; constants, sums and products preserve that domain,
    so all three cutoff polynomials belong to it. On the same set the receipt
    <eqref|eq:trace-rounded-receipt-one>\U<eqref|eq:trace-rounded-receipt-two>
    bounds their root tests by <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>>. This is the
    root-price hypothesis of the packet argument with
    <math|g=\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>> (and holds with <math|g+\<varepsilon\>>
    for every <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0> a fortiori).

    By (Ph2) and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:trace-rounded-conjugate>, which
    supplies <eqref|eq:episode-conjugate>, the packet argument applies to
    each of the three packets and gives, by <eqref|eq:episode-all-powers>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,<around|(|D<rsup|\<ast\>>*D|)><rsup|n>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|i><rsup|2*n>*<space|2em><around|(|n=0,1,2,\<ldots\>;D=D<rsub|0>,D<rsub|1>,D<rsub|01>|)>.
    </equation*>

    <em|Step 2: every character.> Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-character-moments>,
    which uses the full-support representation of (Ph1), gives
    <math|<around|\||\<chi\><around|(|D|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>>
    for each of the three elements and every <math|\<chi\>\<in\>\<Xi\>>. The
    two-point calculation following that lemma, with <math|a=w<rsub|0>>,
    <math|b=w<rsub|1>>, <math|a*b=w>, then gives

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||S<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|w|)>-<around|(|\<kappa\>+j<rsub|0>*j<rsub|1>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>*<around*|(|1+<around|\||j<rsub|0>|\|>+<around|\||j<rsub|1>|\|>|)>.
    </equation*>

    For completeness: evaluating <math|\<chi\>> on <math|D<rsub|01>> and
    rearranging, <math|S<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|w|)>-<around|(|\<kappa\>+j<rsub|0>*j<rsub|1>|)>=\<chi\><around|(|D<rsub|01>|)>+j<rsub|0>*\<chi\><around|(|D<rsub|1>|)>+j<rsub|1>*\<chi\><around|(|D<rsub|0>|)>>,
    and the triangle inequality gives the bound.

    <em|Step 3: identification.> By the definition of <math|\<kappa\>>,
    <math|\<kappa\>+j<rsub|0>*j<rsub|1>=S<rsub|0>*<around|(|e,e<rprime|'>;q<rsub|0>,q<rsub|1>|)>>.
    By the null-relation clause of (Ph3) and Step 1,

    <\equation*>
      S<rsub|\<chi\>><around|(|w|)>=\<chi\>*<around|(|M*<around|(|y<rsub|0>*@*z<rsub|0>*<space|0.17em>y<rsub|1>*@*z<rsub|1>|)>|)>=S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|U<rsub|i>*e,U<rsub|i>*e<rprime|'>;z<rsub|0>,z<rsub|1>|)>=S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|U<rsub|i>*e,U<rsub|i>*e<rprime|'>;r<rsub|i>*q|)>.
    </equation*>

    Substituting these identities and the definitions of
    <math|j<rsub|0>,j<rsub|1>> proves <eqref|eq:trace-rounded-comparison>.
    The threshold <math|i<rsub|0>> depends only on <math|d>.
  </proof>

  <\corollary>
    <dueto|The pointwise clause of the trace
    input><label|cor:trace-rounded-input>Assume (G1)\U(G4) and (Ph1)\U(Ph3),
    and that the selected chart sequence realizes the comparison subsequence
    <math|r<rsub|i>> of Input<nbsp><reference|input:trace-scales> at the
    grade <math|d=4> of the reference field space <math|E<rsub|0>=E<rsub|4>>.
    Assume also that the reference correlations are continuous on
    configurations with distinct points. Put
    <math|q<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>=\<rho\><rsub|m<rsub|i>><around|(|x|)>> and,
    for a basis <math|<around|(|e<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>> of <math|E<rsub|0>>,

    <\equation*>
      M<rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>><around|(|q|)>=<around|\||S<rsub|0>*<around|(|e<rsub|\<alpha\>>;q<rsub|0>|)>|\|>+<around|\||S<rsub|0>*<around|(|e<rsub|\<beta\>>;q<rsub|1>|)>|\|>.
    </equation*>

    Then the pointwise clause of Input<nbsp><reference|input:trace-scales>
    holds with these rounded configurations, with
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>=<around|(|i+1|)><rsup|-1>>, with this
    <math|M<rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>>>, and with comparison window
    <math|\<cal-W\>>: <math|q<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>\<to\>x> for every
    <math|x>, and at every coordinatewise separated <math|x> in the interior
    of <math|\<cal-W\>>, eventually in <math|i>, <eqref|eq:trace-basis-error>
    holds for all <math|\<alpha\>,\<beta\>>. Moreover
    <math|M<rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>>> and the reference correlations are
    continuous near such <math|x>, and the interior of <math|\<cal-W\>>
    contains a neighborhood of <math|x<around|(|t|)>=<around|(|0,<around|(|t/2,t/2,t/2,t/2|)>|)>>
    for every <math|1/2\<less\>t\<less\>2>.
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    <em|Convergence of the rounding.> Since <math|b<rsub|i>\<gtr\>5> and
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|k>\<geqslant\>1>, <math|m<rsub|i>=\<mu\><rsub|k<rsub|i>><rsup|b<rsub|i>>\<geqslant\>\<mu\><rsub|k<rsub|i>><rsup|5>>,
    which tends to infinity because <math|k<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>> (G1).
    Hence <eqref|eq:trace-rounding-error> gives
    <math|<around|\||q<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>-x|\|>\<leqslant\>2*<sqrt|n>/m<rsub|i>\<to\>0>
    for every configuration <math|x> of <math|n> points.

    <em|The estimate.> Let <math|x> be coordinatewise separated and interior
    to <math|\<cal-W\>>. The interior of <math|\<cal-W\>> and the set of
    coordinatewise separated configurations are open neighborhoods of
    <math|x>. Since <math|q<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>\<to\>x>, there is
    <math|i<rsub|1>> with <math|q<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>> in both for
    <math|i\<geqslant\>i<rsub|1>>. For such <math|i> write
    <math|q<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>=m<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*t> with
    <math|t=<around|\<lceil\>|m<rsub|i>*x|\<rceil\>>>. Then
    <math|q<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>> is coordinatewise separated and
    <math|<around|\||q<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>100>, so the
    width clause of (G3) shows that <math|q<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>> is fit at
    level <math|i>; hence <math|x> is fit, by idempotence of rounding.
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:trace-rounded-comparison> applies for
    <math|i\<geqslant\>max <around|(|i<rsub|0>,i<rsub|1>|)>> to every pair of
    basis vectors and gives <eqref|eq:trace-basis-error> with the stated
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>> and <math|M<rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>>>; note
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>\<to\>0>.

    <em|Continuity.> The one-point functions
    <math|q\<mapsto\>S<rsub|0>*<around|(|e<rsub|\<alpha\>>;q<rsub|0>|)>> and
    the two-point functions <math|q\<mapsto\>S<rsub|0>*<around|(|e<rsub|\<alpha\>>,e<rsub|\<beta\>>;q|)>>
    are reference correlations of configurations with distinct points, hence
    continuous on that open set by assumption; so is
    <math|M<rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>>>, as a sum of absolute values of
    continuous functions. The configuration <math|x> has distinct points, so
    these functions are continuous near <math|x>.

    <em|The window.> For <math|1/2\<less\>t\<less\>2>, the two points of
    <math|x<around|(|t|)>> are at distance
    <math|<around|\||x<around|(|t|)><rsub|0>-x<around|(|t|)><rsub|1>|\|>=t\<gtr\>10<rsup|-2>>,
    and <math|<around|\||x<around|(|t|)>|\|>=t\<less\>100>. Both functions
    <math|x\<mapsto\><around|\||x<rsub|0>-x<rsub|1>|\|>> and
    <math|x\<mapsto\><around|\||x|\|>> are continuous, so the strict
    inequalities persist on a neighborhood of <math|x<around|(|t|)>>, which
    is therefore contained in <math|\<cal-W\>>; it lies in the interior of
    <math|\<cal-W\>>. Moreover <math|x<around|(|t|)>> is coordinatewise
    separated, its coordinates being <math|0> and <math|t/2\<ne\>0> in each
    direction.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Status.> Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:trace-rounded-input>
  produces the pointwise, continuity and window clauses of
  Input<nbsp><reference|input:trace-scales> for a given comparison
  subsequence, conditional on (G1)\U(G4) and (Ph1)\U(Ph3). The quantitative
  content is the pairwise receipt (G3) at cost
  <math|<around|(|i+1|)><rsup|-1>>. Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-firesky-gallery>
  records it as part of the one-step chart output and attributes the
  quantitative estimates of that output to
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:three-panel-weak-comparison>. The root-test
  estimates themselves are supplied by Corollary<nbsp><reference|prop:trace-selected-rounded-receipt>
  below: it attaches the actual marked propagation and lawful representation
  at the root depth to the level-budget estimate, and then purchases the cost
  <math|<around|(|i+1|)><rsup|-1>> and width clause by the selection
  thresholds. The identification of the chart sequence with the comparison
  subsequence of the input, the moving-label and logarithmic clauses, and the
  reference normalization are separate inputs, discussed after
  Input<nbsp><reference|input:trace-scales>.

  <subsection|The pairwise receipt from the level
  budget><label|subsec:trace-rounded-receipt>

  We now derive the receipt (G3) of Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:trace-rounded-comparison>
  from two properties of the funded chart witnesses of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-firesky-gallery>: a scale
  allowance, stated in (A) below, and a root-test bound at each level whose
  size is an explicit negative power of the clock monitor, stated in (B)
  below. The width clause uses only (A) and the growth of the monitor. The
  two root-test bounds <eqref|eq:trace-rounded-receipt-one>\U<eqref|eq:trace-rounded-receipt-two>
  use only (B) and the growth of the monitor. The selection of the chart
  levels then supplies (G3) at the cost <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>=<around|(|i+1|)><rsup|-1>>.
  At the end of the subsection we show how the replica-root estimate of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:rank-root-normalization> yields (B), and
  attach its inputs to the selected funded family.

  <paragraph|Charts at a fixed level.> Throughout,
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|k>\<geqslant\>1> is the clock monitor of (G1), with
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|k>\<to\>\<infty\>>; it is the diverging monitor
  <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|k>> of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:rank-root-normalization>.
  Fix a grade cutoff <math|d> and let

  <\equation*>
    M=M<rsub|d>=d+2
  </equation*>

  be the marker allowance of the funded chart family at grade <math|d>: the
  maximal number of markers per cell in the replica estimate of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:rank-root-normalization>. (It is not the
  arity cap <math|l> of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-firesky-gallery>.)
  Consider a chart of the family at level <math|k> with base arc
  <math|b\<gtr\>0>. Put

  <\equation*>
    m=\<mu\><rsub|k><rsup|<space|0.17em>b>,<space|2em>r=a<rsub|k>*m,
  </equation*>

  so that <math|r=g<rsub|b><around|(|k|)>> in the notation of
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-firesky-gap>. Let <math|a<rsub|\<ell\>>> be the
  spacing of the middle cell of the chart. Let <math|w> be the root depth of
  the chart: the depth of the centered tiling on which the root tests of its
  comparison receipt are taken, and at which its chamber condition is
  asserted. A configuration <math|x\<in\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|2>>
  is <em|fit for the chart> if the grid configuration
  <math|a<rsub|k>*<around|\<lceil\>|m*x|\<rceil\>>> is coordinatewise
  separated and each of its coordinates has absolute value at most
  <math|a<rsub|\<ell\>>/8>. For the chart selected at index <math|i>, with
  <math|<around|(|k,b|)>=<around|(|k<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|)>>, this is exactly
  fitness at level <math|i> as defined in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:trace-rounded-comparison>. As there, for
  a fit <math|x> and <math|e,e<rprime|'>\<in\>E<rsub|d<rprime|'>>> with
  <math|d<rprime|'>\<leqslant\>d>, we write <math|f<rsub|0><rsup|N,p>>,
  <math|f<rsub|1><rsup|N,p>> for the finite-volume insertions of the chart
  labels of <math|e> and <math|e<rprime|'>> at the two sites of
  <math|a<rsub|k>*<around|\<lceil\>|m*x|\<rceil\>>>. We write
  <math|j<rsub|0>,j<rsub|1>,\<kappa\>> for the reference values at the
  rounded configuration <math|\<rho\><rsub|m><around|(|x|)>>, and
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|s>> (<math|s\<subseteq\><around|{|0,1|}>>) for the parts
  of the corrected polynomial defined there. Thus
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|<around|{|c|}>>=f<rsub|c><rsup|N,p>-j<rsub|c>> and
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|<around|{|0,1|}>>=<around|(|f<rsub|0><rsup|N,p>-j<rsub|0>|)>*<around|(|f<rsub|1><rsup|N,p>-j<rsub|1>|)>-\<kappa\>>.

  We use the following two properties of the funded chart witnesses. In both,
  the level threshold is fixed before the chart is chosen.

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(A)><em|Allowance.> There is <math|K<rsub|A>=K<rsub|A><around|(|d|)>>
    such that every chart of the family at a level
    <math|k\<geqslant\>K<rsub|A>> with base arc <math|b> satisfies

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:trace-receipt-allowance>m*<space|0.17em>\<mu\><rsub|k><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><frac|a<rsub|\<ell\>>|a<rsub|k>>,<space|2em><text|equivalently><space|2em>r*<space|0.17em>\<mu\><rsub|k><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|\<ell\>>.
    </equation>

    <item*|(B)><em|Level budget.> There is
    <math|K<rsub|B>=K<rsub|B><around|(|d|)>> with the following property. Let
    <math|k\<geqslant\>K<rsub|B>> and consider any chart of the family at
    level <math|k>, any grade <math|d<rprime|'>\<leqslant\>d>, any basis
    vectors <math|e,e<rprime|'>> of <math|E<rsub|d<rprime|'>>>, any
    <math|N\<gtr\>k>, any period <math|p>, and any <math|x> fit for the chart
    with <math|\<rho\><rsub|m><around|(|x|)>\<in\>\<cal-W\>>. Then, for every
    nonempty <math|s\<subseteq\><around|{|0,1|}>>, the root test of the
    centered tiling at the root depth <math|w> of the chart, for the Wilson
    law at <math|<around|(|N,p|)>>, satisfies

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:trace-receipt-budget><around|\<\|\|\>|\<Phi\><rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|root>>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsub|k><rsup|-1/<around|(|16*M|)>>.
    </equation>
  </enumerate>

  The thresholds <math|K<rsub|A>> and <math|K<rsub|B>> may depend on the
  grade cutoff, the chosen bases and the window <math|\<cal-W\>>. They do not
  depend on the chart, on <math|<around|(|N,p|)>>, or on <math|x>. Since
  there are finitely many grades <math|d<rprime|'>\<leqslant\>d> and finitely
  many pairs of basis vectors, a threshold for each fixed pair yields one for
  all of them by taking the maximum. This chart-uniform form is used in the
  proof of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:trace-rounded-receipt>, where the
  threshold must be known before the producer is called. A weaker form, with
  a threshold allowed to depend on the level curve returned by the producer,
  would suffice for the first assertion of that proposition, but not for the
  argument given for its second assertion.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The width clause><label|lem:trace-receipt-width> Let
    <math|R\<gtr\>0>. Consider a chart at a level
    <math|k\<geqslant\>K<rsub|A>> with base arc <math|b>, and suppose
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|k><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>8*R>. Let
    <math|t\<in\><around|(|\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|2>> be such that
    <math|y=m<rsup|-1>*t> is coordinatewise separated and
    <math|<around|\||y|\|>\<leqslant\>R>. Then <math|y> is fit for the chart.
    In particular, if <math|\<mu\><rsub|k><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>800>, the width
    clause of (G3) holds for that chart.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Since <math|m*y=t> has integer coordinates,
    <math|<around|\<lceil\>|m*y|\<rceil\>>=t>, and the grid configuration of
    <math|y> is

    <\equation*>
      z=a<rsub|k>*<around|\<lceil\>|m*y|\<rceil\>>=a<rsub|k>*t=a<rsub|k>*m*y=r*y.
    </equation*>

    Multiplication by <math|r\<gtr\>0> preserves the strict order of real
    numbers, so <math|z> is coordinatewise separated because <math|y> is.
    Each coordinate satisfies <math|<around|\||z<rsub|c,\<nu\>>|\|>=r<around|\||y<rsub|c,\<nu\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>r<around|\||y|\|>\<leqslant\>r*R>,
    because a coordinate of a vector in <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|8>> is bounded
    by its Euclidean norm. By <eqref|eq:trace-receipt-allowance>, which holds
    because <math|k\<geqslant\>K<rsub|A>>, and by
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|k><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>8*R>,

    <\equation*>
      r*R\<leqslant\><frac|a<rsub|\<ell\>>|\<mu\><rsub|k><rsup|2>>*<space|0.17em>R\<leqslant\><frac|a<rsub|\<ell\>>|8*R>*<space|0.17em>R=<frac|a<rsub|\<ell\>>|8>.
    </equation*>

    Hence <math|y> is fit. The width clause of (G3) is the case <math|R=100>,
    and there <math|8*R=800>.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Cost of the root tests><label|lem:trace-receipt-cost> Assume (B),
    and let <math|0\<less\>\<varepsilon\>\<leqslant\>1>. If
    <math|k\<geqslant\>K<rsub|B>> and <math|\<mu\><rsub|k>\<geqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsup|-16*M>>,
    then for every chart of the family at level <math|k> the bounds
    <eqref|eq:trace-rounded-receipt-one>\U<eqref|eq:trace-rounded-receipt-two>,
    with the root test taken at the root depth <math|w> of the chart, hold
    with <math|\<varepsilon\>> in place of <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>>.
    This holds for every grade <math|d<rprime|'>\<leqslant\>d>, all basis
    vectors <math|e,e<rprime|'>> of <math|E<rsub|d<rprime|'>>>, all
    <math|N\<gtr\>k>, all <math|p>, and every <math|x> fit for the chart with
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|m><around|(|x|)>\<in\>\<cal-W\>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The function <math|t\<mapsto\>t<rsup|-1/<around|(|16*M|)>>> is decreasing
    on <math|<around|(|0,\<infty\>|)>>, so
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|k>\<geqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsup|-16*M>> gives
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|k><rsup|-1/<around|(|16*M|)>>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<varepsilon\><rsup|-16*M>|)><rsup|-1/<around|(|16*M|)>>=\<varepsilon\>>.
    By (B), <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Phi\><rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|root>>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>>
    for each nonempty <math|s\<subseteq\><around|{|0,1|}>>. By the identities
    recalled above, the left sides of <eqref|eq:trace-rounded-receipt-one>
    and <eqref|eq:trace-rounded-receipt-two> are exactly
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Phi\><rsub|<around|{|c|}>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|root>>>
    and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Phi\><rsub|<around|{|0,1|}>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|root>>>.
  </proof>

  Since <math|\<mu\><rsub|k>\<to\>\<infty\>>, the conditions

  <\equation*>
    k\<geqslant\>K<rsub|A><around|(|d|)>,<space|2em>k\<geqslant\>K<rsub|B><around|(|d|)>,<space|2em>\<mu\><rsub|k><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>800,<space|2em>\<mu\><rsub|k>\<geqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsup|-16*M<rsub|d>>
  </equation*>

  hold for all sufficiently large <math|k>. We write
  <math|K*<around|(|d,\<varepsilon\>|)>> for the least <math|K> such that
  they hold for every <math|k\<geqslant\>K>. (The monitor is not assumed
  monotone, so this is the right form of the threshold.)

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Producer of the pairwise receipt><label|prop:trace-rounded-receipt>Assume
    (A) and (B) at every grade cutoff. Identify the root depth
    <math|w<rsub|i>> of (G4) with the root depth <math|w> of the chart
    selected at index <math|i>; that is, (G4) is required at that depth.
    Suppose that for every index <math|i> the gallery selection of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-firesky-gallery>, with grade
    cutoff <math|d=i> and cost index <math|l=i>, takes its chart at a level

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:trace-receipt-level>k<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>K*<around*|(|i,<around|(|i+1|)><rsup|-1>|)>.
    </equation>

    Then the selected data satisfy (G3) at the cost
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>=<around|(|i+1|)><rsup|-1>>, including its
    width clause. Moreover the selection can always be made so that
    <eqref|eq:trace-receipt-level> holds for every <math|i>, without changing
    the conclusions of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-firesky-selection>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Fix <math|i>, and let <math|M=M<rsub|i>=i+2>. The selected chart has
    level <math|k<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>K*<around|(|i,<around|(|i+1|)><rsup|-1>|)>>
    and base arc <math|b<rsub|i>>, so <math|m=\<mu\><rsub|k<rsub|i>><rsup|b<rsub|i>>=m<rsub|i>>
    and the fitness of the previous paragraphs is fitness at level <math|i>.
    By the identification in the statement, the root test of (G3) at index
    <math|i> is taken at the depth <math|w<rsub|i>=w> of the selected chart.
    Since <math|k<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>K<rsub|A><around|(|i|)>> and
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|k<rsub|i>><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>800>,
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:trace-receipt-width> with <math|R=100> gives
    the width clause: every <math|y=m<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*t> that is
    coordinatewise separated with <math|<around|\||y|\|>\<leqslant\>100> is
    fit at level <math|i>. Since <math|k<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>K<rsub|B><around|(|i|)>>
    and <math|\<mu\><rsub|k<rsub|i>>\<geqslant\><around|(|i+1|)><rsup|16*M>=\<varepsilon\><rsub|i><rsup|-16*M>>,
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:trace-receipt-cost> with
    <math|\<varepsilon\>=\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>> gives
    <eqref|eq:trace-rounded-receipt-one>\U<eqref|eq:trace-rounded-receipt-two>
    at depth <math|w<rsub|i>>. These hold for every grade
    <math|d<rprime|'>\<leqslant\>i>, all basis vectors of
    <math|E<rsub|d<rprime|'>>>, all <math|N\<gtr\>k<rsub|i>>, all <math|p>,
    and every <math|x> fit at level <math|i> with
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|m<rsub|i>><around|(|x|)>\<in\>\<cal-W\>>. As <math|i>
    was arbitrary, this is (G3).

    For the second assertion we use only the output of the one-step producer
    as stated in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-firesky-gallery>.
    The chart it returns at level <math|k> and base arc <math|b\<gtr\>5> has
    exact radius equal to the clock gap <math|g<rsub|b><around|(|k|)>=a<rsub|k>*\<mu\><rsub|k><rsup|b>>
    of <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-firesky-gap>, and the producer accepts every
    threshold <math|\<theta\>\<gtr\>0> for that radius. Fix <math|i> and put
    <math|K=K*<around|(|i,<around|(|i+1|)><rsup|-1>|)>>; by (A) and (B) as
    stated, <math|K> is determined before the producer is called. Since
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|k>\<geqslant\>1> and <math|b\<gtr\>0>, the radius
    satisfies <math|g<rsub|b><around|(|k|)>\<geqslant\>a<rsub|k>>. Given the
    threshold <math|\<theta\>\<gtr\>0> required by the selection at step
    <math|i>, call the producer instead with

    <\equation*>
      \<theta\><rprime|'>=min <around*|(|\<theta\>,min<rsub|j\<less\>K>
      a<rsub|j>|)>\<gtr\>0
    </equation*>

    (with <math|\<theta\><rprime|'>=\<theta\>> if <math|K=0>); this minimum
    is positive because there are finitely many <math|j\<less\>K> and each
    <math|a<rsub|j>\<gtr\>0>. If the returned level <math|k> satisfied
    <math|k\<less\>K>, the returned radius would satisfy
    <math|g<rsub|b><around|(|k|)>\<geqslant\>a<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>\<theta\><rprime|'>>,
    contradicting <math|g<rsub|b><around|(|k|)>\<less\>\<theta\><rprime|'>>.
    Hence <math|k\<geqslant\>K>, which is <eqref|eq:trace-receipt-level>.
    Since <math|\<theta\><rprime|'>\<leqslant\>\<theta\>>, the returned
    output also meets the threshold <math|\<theta\>> required in the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-firesky-selection>, and every other
    property of the output is unchanged. Thus that lemma applies verbatim
    with the reduced thresholds, and its conclusions hold for the resulting
    selection.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Supply of the level budget.> The bound
  <eqref|eq:trace-receipt-budget> is the replica-root estimate of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:rank-root-normalization> at marker allowance
  <math|M>. Write <math|q<rsub|0>> for the number of replica cells, which is
  also the site count of the selected output torus. With

  <\equation*>
    I<rsub|k>=<frac|log \<mu\><rsub|k>|16*M>,<space|2em>a=e<rsup|2*I<rsub|k>>=\<mu\><rsub|k><rsup|1/<around|(|8*M|)>>\<geqslant\>1,
  </equation*>

  a coefficient whose pushed marked density has the local estimate with
  coefficient price <math|a<rsup|q<rsub|0><around|\||I|\|>>> and per-site
  expense <math|X\<leqslant\>1+M> satisfies <eqref|eq:rank-root>, whose right
  side is <math|exp <around|{|P<rsub|R><around|(|X|)>-4*D*log
  g/q<rsub|0>|}>/a<rsup|<around|\||I|\|>>>. The harmonic term <math|-4*D*log
  g/q<rsub|0>> is nonnegative and must be paid for. That section shows

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|R><around|(|X|)>-4*D*log g/q<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>T<rsub|M>
  </equation*>

  under the following conditions, all at the output level <math|m> of the
  clock: the coupling bound <math|\<beta\>\<leqslant\>4*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>>;
  the harmonic condition <math|8*D*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>/q<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>1>,
  which the clock supplies eventually; the same clip and row bound <math|A>
  as the clock stage, with <math|\<theta\><rsub|R>\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|clock>>>,
  <math|\<Lambda\>\<leqslant\>\<Lambda\><rsub|<math-up|clock>>> and
  <math|\<delta\>\<leqslant\>1>; and the late-scale purchases
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>*\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|clock>><rsup|3>\<leqslant\>1>,
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>*\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|clock>><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>1>
  and <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|<math-up|clock>>*\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|clock>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>1>.
  If moreover the stage ticket satisfies <math|T<rsub|M>\<leqslant\>I<rsub|k>>,
  then the right side of <eqref|eq:rank-root> is at most
  <math|e<rsup|I<rsub|k>>/a<rsup|<around|\||I|\|>>>. For a nonempty marker
  subset <math|<around|\||I|\|>\<geqslant\>1> and <math|a\<geqslant\>1>, so
  this is at most <math|e<rsup|I<rsub|k>>/a=e<rsup|-I<rsub|k>>=\<mu\><rsub|k><rsup|-1/<around|(|16*M|)>>>.
  The marker subsets <math|I> are the nonempty
  <math|s\<subseteq\><around|{|0,1|}>>; since <math|M\<geqslant\>2>, they are
  admissible. The coefficients are the one-point and corrected two-point
  parts <math|\<Phi\><rsub|s>> themselves, as
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:rank-root-normalization> requires.

  The coefficient price <math|a<rsup|q<rsub|0><around|\||I|\|>>> with
  <math|X\<leqslant\>1+M> on the same pushed density is
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:allarity-output-price>, with <math|u=M>.
  It is obtained from the scalar envelope
  <eqref|eq:allarity-density-envelope> at the marker weight
  <eqref|eq:allarity-marker-weight>. That weight is used from the launch at
  the middle level down to the output torus. The leading interval above the
  middle level is run at a smaller weight. These inputs are verified on the
  selected family by Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:allarity-selected-application>.
  We use that result below only to derive the pairwise geometric receipt; its
  marked propagation proof is not repeated.

  <\corollary>
    <dueto|The receipt on the selected chart
    family><label|prop:trace-selected-rounded-receipt>Use the common history
    and the funded panels of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:trace-joint-chart-purchase>,
    with the ticket requests <eqref|eq:rank-root-ticket-requests> included
    before the history is chosen. For each fixed grade cutoff <math|d>,
    restrict to its three panels and their offsets
    <math|t\<in\><around|[|0,1|]>>. Then (A) and (B) hold, with thresholds
    uniform over those charts, all <math|N\<gtr\>k>, all periods, and all the
    fit configurations in the stated window. The root test in (B) is at the
    same depth <math|w> as the chamber statement
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-firesky-chamber>. Consequently the
    selected gallery can satisfy (G3) at every index, without changing its
    background history or extraction.
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    Fix <math|d>, put <math|M=d+2>, and bound the three panel arcs and their
    offsets by <math|<wide|b|\<bar\>>=max<rsub|0\<leqslant\>h\<leqslant\>2><around|(|b<rsub|d,h>+1|)>>.
    Apply Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:allarity-selected-application>
    with <math|n=2> marks per replica cell and <math|u=M>, to the labels
    solving <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-firesky-solves> and their signed copies. Its
    common-history proof supplies the complete marked endpoint, the joint
    reader, the middle launch, the lower propagation and the lawful output on
    precisely these charts. Its large weight is
    <math|a=\<mu\><rsub|k><rsup|1/<around|(|8*M|)>>> and its output is the
    root depth <math|w>, not the middle level <math|\<ell\>>. The scalar
    inverse reference-moment factor commutes with the full marked push;
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:allarity-invoice-parts> consequently identifies
    its nonempty coefficients with the <math|\<Phi\><rsub|s>> above, with the
    original partition denominator. Its root conclusion is exactly (B).

    The constants in that application are uniform on the bounded window
    <math|\<cal-W\>>, where the two points have distance at least
    <math|10<rsup|-2>>. They require no positive lower bound on each
    coordinate difference; nonzero coordinate differences are used only for
    fitness and chamber ordering. Take the maximum over the finitely many
    grades and basis pairs. The joint stage purchase includes that maximum
    and both adjacent-stage data before the history is fixed. Thus the
    resulting level threshold is independent of the offset, <math|N,p> and
    fit configuration.

    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-firesky-clock-fields> gives (A) and
    the bracket <math|J<around|(|w|)>\<geqslant\>J<around|(|k|)>-1>. Since
    <math|J<around|(|k|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>, this also gives
    <math|w\<to\>\<infty\>> uniformly over these charts: for fixed
    <math|m<rsub|0>>, taking <math|J<around|(|k|)>-1\<gtr\>J<around|(|m<rsub|0>|)>>
    forces <math|w\<gtr\>m<rsub|0>>. The late output purchases in
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:rank-root-output-ticket> therefore use
    one common tail. The chamber lemma uses this very centered tiling at
    <math|w>. Finally Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:trace-rounded-receipt>
    refines the radius thresholds to obtain (G3) at cost
    <math|<around|(|i+1|)><rsup|-1>>, preserving the exact prescribed radii,
    the background history and its extraction.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Status.> The conditional producer
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:trace-rounded-receipt> continues to apply
  to any family satisfying (A) and (B). Corollary<nbsp><reference|prop:trace-selected-rounded-receipt>
  verifies those properties on the selected funded family and identifies its
  root depth. Thus (G3) is supplied for that family's rounded comparison.
  This application retains the canonical selected-history and representation
  constructions; it does not assert the receipt for an arbitrary chart with
  merely the same numerical scales.

  <subsubsection|Removing rounding and subsequences>

  The rounded separation need not equal the intended radius. Pointwise
  convergence at rounded arguments alone does not identify
  <math|T<around|(|r<rsub|i>|)>>. The positive spectral representation
  resolves this: <math|T> is real, nonnegative and decreasing, because
  <math|e<rsup|-t*p<rsub|0>>> has those properties for each
  <math|p<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0>. Integrability at the smaller positive time
  permits the comparison under the integral.

  <\theorem>
    <label|thm:trace-asymptotic>Let <math|\<Theta\>> be the centered trace of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:trace-spectrum>, let <math|T> be its
    two-point function in the selected phase as in <eqref|eq:trace-radial>,
    and let <math|N=dim<rsub|\<bbb-R\>> \<frak-g\>>. Assume:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>the reconstruction and stress hypotheses of
      Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:trace-spectrum>, under which <math|T>
      has the positive Laplace representation
      <eqref|eq:trace-positive-laplace>;

      <item>invariance of the selected correlations under Euclidean
      translations and orthogonal transformations, together with the scalar
      law of <math|\<Theta\>>, which give the radial form
      <eqref|eq:trace-radial>;

      <item>the reference normalization <eqref|eq:trace-reference-normalization>;

      <item>Input<nbsp><reference|input:trace-scales>.
    </enumerate>

    Then

    <\equation*>
      lim<rsub|r*\<downarrow\>*0> r<rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|r<rsup|-1>|)>*T<around|(|r|)>=<frac|3*N|\<pi\><rsup|4>>\<gtr\>0.
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  The reconstruction hypotheses and the selected-scale comparison are matched
  to their producers below; the reference normalization has just been proved
  for the actual comparator. The proof of the limiting passage uses these
  statements separately.

  <\proof>
    First fix any one comparison subsequence <math|r<rsub|i>>. Let
    <math|c<rsub|i>=r<rsub|i><rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|r<rsub|i><rsup|-1>|)>\<geqslant\>0>.
    For <math|1/2\<less\>t\<less\>2> take the configuration
    <math|x<around|(|t|)>> in the input. Its Euclidean separation is exactly
    <math|t>, all four coordinate separations are nonzero, and
    <math|w<rsub|i><around|(|t|)>\<assign\><around|\||q<rsub|i><around|(|x<around|(|t|)>|)><rsub|0>-q<rsub|i><around|(|x<around|(|t|)>|)><rsub|1>|\|>\<to\>t>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:trace-moving-correlation> gives

    <\equation*>
      c<rsub|i>*T*<around|(|r<rsub|i>*w<rsub|i><around|(|t|)>|)>\<longrightarrow\>A/t<rsup|8>.
    </equation*>

    If <math|t\<gtr\>1>, eventually <math|w<rsub|i><around|(|t|)>\<gtr\>1>,
    so monotonicity yields <math|c<rsub|i>*T<around|(|r<rsub|i>|)>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|i>*T*<around|(|r<rsub|i>*w<rsub|i><around|(|t|)>|)>>.
    If <math|t\<less\>1>, eventually <math|0\<less\>w<rsub|i><around|(|t|)>\<less\>1>
    and the reverse inequality holds. Given <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>,
    choose <math|t<rsub|+>\<gtr\>1> and <math|t<rsub|->\<less\>1>
    sufficiently close to one that <math|<around|\||A/t<rsub|\<pm\>><rsup|8>-A|\|>\<less\>\<epsilon\>/2>.
    For all sufficiently large <math|i>, the two comparison values are within
    <math|\<epsilon\>/2> of their limits. They bound
    <math|c<rsub|i>*T<around|(|r<rsub|i>|)>> between <math|A-\<epsilon\>> and
    <math|A+\<epsilon\>>. Thus <math|c<rsub|i>*T<around|(|r<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>A>.

    To obtain the limit at all positive radii, suppose it failed. There would
    be <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0> and a sequence
    <math|s<rsub|n>\<in\><around|(|0,1/n|)>>, <math|n\<geqslant\>1>, for
    which <math|<around|\||s<rsub|n><rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|s<rsub|n><rsup|-1>|)>*T<around|(|s<rsub|n>|)>-A|\|>\<geqslant\>\<epsilon\>>
    for every <math|n>. Input<nbsp><reference|input:trace-scales> supplies a
    comparison subsequence of this same sequence. The preceding argument
    makes its weighted values converge to <math|A>, contradicting that
    inequality. Finally <math|N\<gtr\>0> for the nonabelian simple Lie
    algebra under consideration, so <math|A\<gtr\>0>.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The trace requirement on the fixed hierarchy>

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|The selected hierarchy satisfies the trace
    requirement><label|thm:trace-selected-endpoint>Fix the selected funded
    family, its ordered limiting hierarchy <math|S>, and its extraction and
    arch. Let <math|\<chi\><rsub|\<ast\>>> be its selected phase, and take
    the reconstruction of this phase from
    Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:complex-heat>\U<reference|sec:spectral-measure>,
    retaining the hypotheses of those constructions. Use the normalized
    physical stress <math|T<rsub|a*b>> of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-angular-actions> on this
    hierarchy, with its proved Ward and symmetry properties. Define in the
    grade-four field space

    <\equation*>
      \<tau\>=<big|sum><rsub|a>T<rsub|a*a>,<space|2em>\<Theta\>=\<tau\>-<around|\<langle\>|\<tau\>|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<chi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>*\<b-1\>,<space|2em>T<around|(|r|)>=S<rsup|\<chi\><rsub|\<ast\>>><rsub|\<Theta\>,\<Theta\>>*<around|(|<around|(|\<b-0\>,-r/2|)>,<around|(|\<b-0\>,r/2|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Then the vacuum one-point functional of the reconstructed field
    <math|\<phi\>*<around|(|\<Theta\>,f|)>> vanishes for every Schwartz test
    <math|f>, and

    <\equation*>
      lim<rsub|r*\<downarrow\>*0> r<rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|r<rsup|-1>|)>*T<around|(|r|)>=<frac|3*dim<rsub|\<bbb-R\>>
      \<frak-g\>|\<pi\><rsup|4>>\<gtr\>0.
    </equation*>

    Moreover <math|T> is not equal on any interval
    <math|<around|(|0,\<delta\>|)>> to a finite complex linear combination of
    finite products of the mode profiles <eqref|eq:profile-mode>, including
    empty products. Thus these objects satisfy the trace requirement
    <eqref|ex-trace-limit> and its profile exclusion on this very hierarchy.
    No further choice of hierarchy, phase, stress normalization or extraction
    is needed for the trace assertion.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    The physical stress is real, symmetric and covariant under orthogonal
    transformations on the selected label space. By the centered-trace
    application in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:trace-spectrum>, the
    displayed <math|\<Theta\>> is a self-adjoint Euclidean and Lorentz scalar
    with zero vacuum one-point functional. That construction also gives
    <math|T<around|(|r|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|-r*p<rsub|0>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|p|)>>
    with a positive measure supported on <math|p<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0>. It
    uses the point vectors, continued correlations and field operators of
    this same phase reconstruction. Consequently this <math|T> is real,
    nonnegative and decreasing. Its argument is the separation <math|r> of
    the two time-axis points, not the heat height <math|r/2>.

    The mixed-state centering in the scribe introduces no different physical
    field. If

    <\equation*>
      R=<math-up|reCenter><rsub|S><around|(|T|)>,
    </equation*>

    then

    <\equation*>
      \<Theta\>=<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>R-<around|\<langle\>|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>R|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<chi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>*\<b-1\>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed both centerings subtract constant multiples of the same unit, and
    the constants cancel in the displayed identity. The phase mean is fixed
    before any radius sequence is chosen. Unit preservation of the charts and
    <math|q<rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>\<to\>0> remove this fixed scalar after
    scaled pullback, as in <eqref|eq:trace-moving-label>. The coefficient of
    <math|\<cal-O\>> remains <math|-\<kappa\>>, fixed by the nonzero shell
    test for the Ward-normalized <math|R>; no rescaling of <math|\<Theta\>>
    is made here.

    Apply Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:trace-scale-supplier> with this
    family, extraction, phase and stress. The optical, sensor, branch, shell
    and rounded comparison data it uses are on the same charts. In its
    rounded notation <math|k<rsub|i>> is the frame level <math|j<rsub|i>> of
    the scribe; the scribe's <math|k<rsub|i>=<math-up|summit><around|(|j<rsub|i>|)>>
    is the different level at which <math|B<rsub|i>=q<rsub|i>=\<zeta\><around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>>
    is evaluated. These notational roles do not change <math|U<rsub|i>> or
    <math|r<rsub|i>>. For every positive null sequence the supplier selects
    strictly increasing indices with exact radii
    <math|r<rsub|i>=s<rsub|n<rsub|i>>>, while the hierarchy, filter and phase
    remain fixed. The supremum over finite basis pairs is taken on those
    charts, rather than by selecting another subsequence for a basis pair or
    a branch.

    Thus all four hypotheses of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:trace-asymptotic>
    have been matched: the positive Laplace representation above; Euclidean
    covariance and the scalar law of <math|\<Theta\>>; the reference
    normalization <eqref|eq:trace-reference-normalization> for the actual
    comparison table; and the same-history scale supplier. That theorem
    removes rounding by monotonicity and then removes the subsequence. Its
    result is the displayed limit at all positive radii tending to zero. The
    constant is positive because the nonabelian simple Lie algebra has
    positive real dimension. Real convergence here also gives the same limit
    after the usual inclusion into <math|\<bbb-C\>>.

    Finally the mode in <eqref|eq:profile-mode> has mass
    <math|m\<geqslant\>0>, natural orders <math|j,k>, and a continuous real
    numerator homogeneous of degree <math|j> under positive scaling. It uses
    Lebesgue momentum measure and factor <math|1/<around|(|2*<sqrt|m<rsup|2>+<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>>|)>>,
    exactly the mode normalization in the trace requirement. Finite products
    are the tracks of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:trace-profiles>; a
    zero-factor product is one and a zero-term linear combination is zero.
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:profile-exclusion> applies with the
    just-proved strictly positive coefficient and rules out eventual equality
    with every such finite complex combination. This is equality on a
    punctured positive neighborhood of zero, which is exactly equality on
    some <math|<around|(|0,\<delta\>|)>>. It proves the last claim without
    selecting another trace field.
  </proof>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|The dilation current at short separation><label|rem:trace-dilation-current>Let
    <math|T<rsub|a*b>>, <math|\<tau\>> and <math|\<Theta\>=\<tau\>-c*\<b-1\>>
    be as in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:trace-spectrum>, let <math|T> be
    the two-point function of <eqref|eq:trace-radial>, and let <math|V> be a
    continuously differentiable vector field on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>> with
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>*V<rsub|b>+\<partial\><rsub|b>*V<rsub|a>=2*\<varphi\>*\<delta\><rsub|a*b>>
    for a real function <math|\<varphi\>>. Assume in addition, in the
    selected phase and for <math|x<rsub|0>\<neq\>x<rsub|1>>, that
    <math|x<rsub|0>\<mapsto\>S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|T<rsub|a*b>,\<Theta\>;x<rsub|0>,x<rsub|1>|)>>
    is continuously differentiable with <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)><rsub|a>>*S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|T<rsub|a*b>,\<Theta\>;x<rsub|0>,x<rsub|1>|)>=0>
    for each <math|b> (the stress is conserved at separated points), and that
    <math|S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|\<b-1\>,\<Theta\>;x<rsub|0>,x<rsub|1>|)>=<around|\<langle\>|\<Theta\>|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<chi\>>>,
    as used in Remark<nbsp><reference|rem:ex-selection-ground-only>.
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:trace-spectrum> does not assume the first;
    for the normalized stress of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:physical-angular-actions>
    it follows, under their hypotheses, from
    Propositions<nbsp><reference|prop:phase-ward-inheritance> (vary the
    cutoff away from <math|x<rsub|1>>) and<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-caps>.
    Put <math|j<rsub|a><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|b>V<rsub|b><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>*S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|T<rsub|a*b>,\<Theta\>;x<rsub|0>,x<rsub|1>|)>>,
    the current <math|<big|sum><rsub|b>T<rsub|a*b>*V<rsub|b>> at
    <math|x<rsub|0>> against the centered trace at <math|x<rsub|1>>. Then

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)><rsub|a>>*j<rsub|a><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>=\<varphi\><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>*S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|\<tau\>,\<Theta\>;x<rsub|0>,x<rsub|1>|)>=\<varphi\><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>*T<around|(|<around|\||x<rsub|0>-x<rsub|1>|\|>|)>*<space|1em><around|(|x<rsub|0>\<neq\>x<rsub|1>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed, the product rule gives two sums. The one containing the
    derivatives of <math|S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|T<rsub|a*b>,\<Theta\>;x<rsub|0>,x<rsub|1>|)>>
    vanishes by conservation. The other is
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|a,b>\<partial\><rsub|a>*V<rsub|b>*<space|0.17em>S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|T<rsub|a*b>,\<Theta\>;x<rsub|0>,x<rsub|1>|)>>;
    since <math|T<rsub|a*b>=T<rsub|b*a>>,
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>*V<rsub|b>> may be replaced by
    <math|<frac|1|2>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*V<rsub|b>+\<partial\><rsub|b>*V<rsub|a>|)>=\<varphi\>*\<delta\><rsub|a*b>>,
    and bilinearity leaves <math|\<varphi\>*S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|\<tau\>,\<Theta\>;x<rsub|0>,x<rsub|1>|)>>.
    Finally <math|\<tau\>=\<Theta\>+c*\<b-1\>>, so bilinearity, the second
    assumption and <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<Theta\>|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<chi\>>=0>
    give <math|S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|\<tau\>,\<Theta\>;x<rsub|0>,x<rsub|1>|)>=S<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|\<Theta\>,\<Theta\>;x<rsub|0>,x<rsub|1>|)>>,
    which is <math|T<around|(|<around|\||x<rsub|0>-x<rsub|1>|\|>|)>> by
    <eqref|eq:trace-radial>.

    For the dilation field <math|V<around|(|z|)>=z> one has
    <math|\<varphi\>=1>, so for a pair at distance <math|r> the divergence of
    <math|j> is <math|T<around|(|r|)>>. Under the hypotheses of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:trace-asymptotic>, its conclusion
    <math|r<rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|r<rsup|-1>|)>*T<around|(|r|)>\<to\>3*N/\<pi\><rsup|4>\<gtr\>0>
    as <math|r*\<downarrow\>*0>, the limit required in
    <eqref|ex-trace-limit>, gives <math|r<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> with
    <math|T<around|(|r|)>\<gtr\>0> for <math|0\<less\>r\<less\>r<rsub|0>>.
    Thus the divergence of <math|j> is positive whenever
    <math|0\<less\><around|\||x<rsub|0>-x<rsub|1>|\|>\<less\>r<rsub|0>>:
    against the centered trace, the dilation current is not divergence free
    at short separation. In fact <math|T<around|(|r|)>\<gtr\>0> for every
    <math|r\<gtr\>0>: in <eqref|eq:trace-positive-laplace> the measure is
    positive, and nonzero since the limit is positive. Like the theorem, this
    conclusion for a general tensor retains the four hypotheses of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:trace-asymptotic> and the two assumptions
    above. For the canonical selected stress, the trace hypotheses are
    matched in Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:trace-selected-endpoint>; the
    additional conservation and unit identities are supplied by the Ward and
    phase statements cited at the start of this remark. In the free Maxwell
    setting of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:free-maxwell-radial-flux> the
    polarized stress of two closed two-forms <math|f,g> has zero trace, and
    its dilation current is divergence free wherever <math|f> and <math|g>
    are Maxwell waves.
  </remark>

  <subsection|Weighted price control for the trace
  receiver><label|sec:ex-lattice-trace-weighted-estimates>

  This section constructs the normalized response estimates and their scalar
  combination, which feeds the rank-one receiver lemma
  <reference|lem:ex-lattice-trace-weighted-price>. The chosen and reference
  fields use the same finite-volume response columns, arch and separation
  ultrafilter. We keep the field decay separate from price convergence: the
  former uses the priced mission and its detector.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Weighted price from a rank-one approximate
    kernel><label|lem:ex-lattice-trace-weighted-price>Let <math|E> and
    <math|H> be finite-dimensional real normed spaces. Let <math|f:E\<to\>H>
    and <math|U:H\<to\>E> be linear, with <math|f*U=<math-up|id><rsub|H>> and
    <math|ker f=\<bbb-R\>*x> for some <math|x\<ne\>0>. Suppose linear maps
    <math|v<rsub|i>:E\<to\>H> and <math|u<rsub|i>:H\<to\>E> satisfy

    <\equation*>
      v<rsub|i>\<longrightarrow\>f,<space|2em>u<rsub|i>\<longrightarrow\>U,<space|2em>v<rsub|i>*u<rsub|i>=<math-up|id><rsub|H><space|1em><text|eventually>.
    </equation*>

    Let <math|w<rsub|i>\<to\>x>, let <math|e<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>, and let
    <math|\<rho\>:E\<to\>\<bbb-R\>> be a fixed linear functional. If

    <\equation*>
      e<rsub|i>*v<rsub|i><around|(|w<rsub|i>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0<space|1em><text|in
      >H,<space|2em>e<rsub|i>*\<rho\><around|(|w<rsub|i>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0,
    </equation*>

    then there are linear functionals <math|\<mu\><rsub|i>:H\<to\>\<bbb-R\>>
    with uniformly bounded operator norms and

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||e<rsub|i>*<around|(|\<mu\><rsub|i>*v<rsub|i>-\<rho\>|)>|\|><rsub|E<rsup|\<ast\>>>\<longrightarrow\>0.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Choose a linear functional <math|\<lambda\>:E\<to\>\<bbb-R\>> with
    <math|\<lambda\><around|(|x|)>=1>, by extending <math|x> to a basis.
    Define

    <\equation*>
      A<around|(|y|)>=\<lambda\>*<around*|(|y-U*f<around|(|y|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|f*<around|(|y-U*f<around|(|y|)>|)>=0>, the kernel assumption
    gives <math|y-U*f<around|(|y|)>=a*x> for some scalar <math|a>. Applying
    <math|\<lambda\>> shows <math|a=A<around|(|y|)>>, and therefore

    <\equation>
      y=U*f<around|(|y|)>+A<around|(|y|)>*x.<label|eq:trace-weighted-estimates-display-star>
    </equation>

    Set <math|V<rsub|i>=u<rsub|i>*f+A\<otimes\>w<rsub|i>>, where
    <math|<around|(|A\<otimes\>w<rsub|i>|)><around|(|y|)>=A<around|(|y|)>*w<rsub|i>>.
    The convergence of <math|u<rsub|i>> and <math|w<rsub|i>> implies
    <math|V<rsub|i>-<math-up|id><rsub|E>=<around|(|u<rsub|i>-U|)>*f+A\<otimes\><around|(|w<rsub|i>-x|)>>,
    by (*). The right side tends to zero in operator norm. Once
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|V<rsub|i>-<math-up|id><rsub|E>|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>1>,
    the Neumann series gives an inverse and

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|V<rsub|i><rsup|-1>-<math-up|id><rsub|E>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><frac|<around|\<\|\|\>|V<rsub|i>-<math-up|id><rsub|E>|\<\|\|\>>|1-<around|\<\|\|\>|V<rsub|i>-<math-up|id><rsub|E>|\<\|\|\>>>,
    </equation*>

    so <math|V<rsub|i><rsup|-1>\<to\><math-up|id><rsub|E>>.

    Put <math|\<mu\><rsub|i>=\<rho\>*u<rsub|i>>. These functionals have
    bounded norms because <math|u<rsub|i>\<to\>U>. For <math|y\<in\>E>, the
    exact identity <math|v<rsub|i>*u<rsub|i>=<math-up|id><rsub|H>> gives

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|e<rsub|i>*<around|(|\<mu\><rsub|i>*v<rsub|i>-\<rho\>|)>*<around|(|V<rsub|i>*y|)>>|<cell|=e<rsub|i>*\<rho\>*<around*|(|u<rsub|i>*v<rsub|i>*<around|(|u<rsub|i>*f<around|(|y|)>+A<around|(|y|)>*w<rsub|i>|)>-u<rsub|i>*f<around|(|y|)>-A<around|(|y|)>*w<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=A<around|(|y|)>*<space|0.17em>e<rsub|i>*<around*|(|\<rho\>*<around|(|u<rsub|i>*v<rsub|i><around|(|w<rsub|i>|)>|)>-\<rho\><around|(|w<rsub|i>|)>|)>.>>>>
    </align*>

    The first term inside the final difference vanishes after weighting,
    because

    <\equation*>
      e<rsub|i>*\<rho\>*<around|(|u<rsub|i>*v<rsub|i><around|(|w<rsub|i>|)>|)>=\<rho\>*<around|(|u<rsub|i>*<around|(|e<rsub|i>*v<rsub|i><around|(|w<rsub|i>|)>|)>|)>
    </equation*>

    and <math|\<rho\>*u<rsub|i>> is uniformly bounded. The second term
    vanishes by hypothesis. Thus the left side tends to zero for every fixed
    <math|y>. In finite dimension, convergence on a basis implies
    operator-norm convergence, so <math|e<rsub|i>*<around|(|\<mu\><rsub|i>*v<rsub|i>-\<rho\>|)>*V<rsub|i>\<to\>0>.
    Multiplication by <math|V<rsub|i><rsup|-1>\<to\><math-up|id><rsub|E>>
    gives <math|e<rsub|i>*<around|(|\<mu\><rsub|i>*v<rsub|i>-\<rho\>|)>\<to\>0>
    in operator norm.
  </proof>

  Let <math|E> be a finite-dimensional real normed space, <math|H> a
  finite-dimensional real normed space, <math|s<rsub|i>:E\<to\>H> linear
  maps, and <math|\<rho\>:E\<to\>\<bbb-R\>> a fixed linear functional. Let
  <math|x\<in\>E> satisfy <math|\<rho\><around|(|x|)>=0>. For each <math|i>,
  let <math|b<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1> with <math|b<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>, let
  <math|q<rsub|i>,r<rsub|i>\<in\>E>, and let <math|e<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>.
  Define

  <\equation*>
    m<rsub|i>=b<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*q<rsub|i>-r<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  Assume

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|b<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*q<rsub|i>>|<cell|\<longrightarrow\>0,>|<cell|sup<rsub|i><around|\||\<rho\><around|(|q<rsub|i>|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<less\>\<infty\>,>|<cell|r<rsub|i>>|<cell|\<longrightarrow\>-x,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-trace-response-limit>>>|<row|<cell|e<rsub|i>/b<rsub|i>>|<cell|\<longrightarrow\>0,>|<cell|e<rsub|i>*\<rho\>*<around|(|r<rsub|i>+x|)>>|<cell|\<longrightarrow\>0,>|<cell|b<rsub|i>*s<rsub|i><around|(|m<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<longrightarrow\>0<space|1em><text|in
    >H.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-trace-response-weighted>>>>>
  </align>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The medium has both weighted null
    limits><label|lem:ex-lattice-trace-medium-receipts>Under
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-response-limit>\U
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-response-weighted>,

    <\equation*>
      m<rsub|i>\<longrightarrow\>x,<space|2em>e<rsub|i>*\<rho\><around|(|m<rsub|i>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0,<space|2em>e<rsub|i>*s<rsub|i><around|(|m<rsub|i>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Since <math|b<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*q<rsub|i>\<to\>0> and
    <math|r<rsub|i>\<to\>-x>, <math|m<rsub|i>=b<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*q<rsub|i>-r<rsub|i>\<to\>x>.
    Since <math|\<rho\><around|(|x|)>=0>,

    <\equation*>
      e<rsub|i>*\<rho\><around|(|m<rsub|i>|)>=<around|(|e<rsub|i>/b<rsub|i>|)>*\<rho\><around|(|q<rsub|i>|)>-e<rsub|i>*\<rho\>*<around|(|r<rsub|i>+x|)>.
    </equation*>

    The first term tends to zero by boundedness of
    <math|\<rho\><around|(|q<rsub|i>|)>> and
    <math|e<rsub|i>/b<rsub|i>\<to\>0>, and the second by hypothesis. Also
    <math|e<rsub|i>/b<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1> eventually, so

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|e<rsub|i>*s<rsub|i><around|(|m<rsub|i>|)>|\<\|\|\>>=<around|(|e<rsub|i>/b<rsub|i>|)>*<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsub|i>*s<rsub|i><around|(|m<rsub|i>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsub|i>*s<rsub|i><around|(|m<rsub|i>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<longrightarrow\>0.
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  All sequences in the application below are already reindexed by a selected
  level <math|k<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>: <math|b<rsub|i>> is the arch scale
  at level <math|k<rsub|i>>, <math|q<rsub|i>> and <math|r<rsub|i>> are the
  chosen and reference responses there, and <math|s<rsub|i>> is the chart at
  index <math|i>. Precisely, <math|k<rsub|i>> is the summit index of the
  frame level of the <math|i>th selected chart. It is the level called the
  postselected level and denoted <math|n<rsub|i>> in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:trace-prosper-sensor>; it is not the initial
  level denoted <math|k<rsub|i>> there. This reindexing matters: the
  field-decay estimate is assumed, and used, only at the reindexed levels,
  not at every level. Write <math|p<rsub|i>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|i>>>
  and <math|A<rsub|a><around|(|i|)>=exp <around|(|a<around|(|log log
  p<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>|)>>. The weight used is
  <math|e<rsub|i>=A<rsub|1/5><around|(|i|)>> at this selected level. The next
  subsection proves <math|e<rsub|i>/b<rsub|i>\<to\>0> and
  <math|e<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>p<rsub|i>>. The field input is supplied with
  weight <math|p<rsub|i>>; since <math|b<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>2*p<rsub|i>>,
  this gives <math|b<rsub|i>*s<rsub|i><around|(|m<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>0> as
  required above.

  In the selected construction, <math|E> is the real signed-equivariant space
  of grade-five coefficient arrays, <math|x> is its nonzero
  equation-of-motion direction, and <math|\<rho\>> is the free scalar price.
  The vectors <math|q<rsub|i>> and <math|r<rsub|i>> are respectively the
  transported chosen response and the transported reference response. Their
  combination <math|m<rsub|i>=b<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*q<rsub|i>-r<rsub|i>> is the
  medium used to build a response functional. The scale <math|b<rsub|i>> is
  the positive arch scale along the selected chart. Boundedness of
  <math|\<rho\><around|(|q<rsub|i>|)>> follows from the unweighted Ward-price
  limit; the weighted estimate on <math|\<rho\>*<around|(|r<rsub|i>+x|)>> is
  the scale-resolved reference-price comparison; and the last estimate is the
  field decay of the medium.

  Combining Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-trace-medium-receipts> with
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-trace-weighted-price>, under the chart
  hypotheses <math|s<rsub|i>\<to\>f>, <math|u<rsub|i>\<to\>U>,
  <math|f*U=<math-up|id>>, <math|ker f=\<bbb-R\>*x> with <math|x\<ne\>0>, and
  <math|s<rsub|i>*u<rsub|i>=<math-up|id>> eventually, gives bounded linear
  functionals <math|\<mu\><rsub|i>:H\<to\>\<bbb-R\>> and

  <\equation*>
    sup<rsub|i><around|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>\<infty\>,<space|2em><around*|\||e<rsub|i>*<around|(|\<mu\><rsub|i>*s<rsub|i>-\<rho\>|)>|\|><rsub|E<rsup|\<ast\>>>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  This is the response-price estimate used by the two-direction trace
  correction. The displayed deduction is complete as a deduction from its
  hypotheses. In the selected construction it is carried out in
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:trace-prosper-sensor>: that proposition
  takes the three limits of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-trace-medium-receipts>
  as its marked-vector hypotheses, derives <math|s<rsub|i>\<to\>f> from them
  together with its own chart inputs, and then applies
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-trace-weighted-price>. The convergence
  <math|s<rsub|i>\<to\>f> is therefore not a consequence of the present
  paragraph, and the chart inputs of that proposition are distinct from the
  response estimates proved below. The field estimate uses the separately
  priced mission, with the exact same-medium identification at the end of
  this section.

  <subsubsection|The rate and medium calculation><label|subsec:ex-lattice-trace-source-rate>

  For the already selected levels <math|k<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>, write
  <math|p<rsub|i>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|i>>> for the running positive
  rate and <math|b<rsub|i>> for the arch scale at <math|k<rsub|i>>. The arch
  is the fixed-level ultrafilter limit of the running couplings
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,\<nu\>,k>>. On the ultrafilter-large set
  <math|N\<geqslant\>k>, the selected Wilson history gives
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>/2\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rsub|N,\<nu\>,k>\<leqslant\>2*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>.
  The interval is closed, so its ultrafilter limit <math|b<around|(|k|)>>
  belongs to it. In particular <math|b<around|(|k|)>\<gtr\>0>. The clock
  tends to infinity; substitution <math|k=k<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>> proves
  the scale comparison

  <\equation>
    <frac|1|2>*p<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>2*p<rsub|i>,<space|2em>p<rsub|i>\<longrightarrow\>\<infty\>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-trace-arch-comparison>
  </equation>

  The age weights are <math|A<rsub|a><around|(|i|)>=exp
  <around|(|a<around|(|log log p<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>|)>>. They satisfy

  <\equation>
    A<rsub|a><around|(|i|)>*A<rsub|c><around|(|i|)>=A<rsub|a+c><around|(|i|)>,<space|2em>A<rsub|a><around|(|i|)>\<leqslant\>p<rsub|i><rsup|\<delta\>><space|1em><text|eventually
    for every fixed >a\<in\>\<bbb-R\><text| and every
    >\<delta\>\<gtr\>0.<label|eq:ex-lattice-trace-age-rules>
  </equation>

  For the second relation, take logarithms. Since <math|<around|(|log log
  p|)><rsup|2>=o<around|(|log p|)>>, eventually
  <math|<around|\||a|\|><around|(|log log
  p<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>*log p<rsub|i>>. Exponentiation
  gives the bound. Also <math|A<rsub|a><around|(|i|)>\<to\>0> directly for
  <math|a\<less\>0>, since <math|<around|(|log log
  p<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>\<to\>\<infty\>>.

  In particular, for <math|a\<in\><around|{|1/6,1/5|}>>,

  <\equation>
    0\<leqslant\><frac|A<rsub|a><around|(|i|)>|b<rsub|i>>\<leqslant\>2*p<rsub|i><rsup|a-1>\<longrightarrow\>0.<label|eq:ex-lattice-trace-age-over-arch>
  </equation>

  The same argument gives <math|A<rsub|a><around|(|i|)>\<leqslant\>p<rsub|i>>
  eventually, so an estimate with weight <math|p<rsub|i>> implies the
  corresponding estimate with weight <math|A<rsub|1/5><around|(|i|)>>.

  Here is the scalar deduction from the response and reference receipts. Let
  <math|q<rsub|i>> be the transported chosen response and <math|w<rsub|i>>
  the transported reference response. The local response estimate and the
  separated flux limit have the form

  <\equation>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|q<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*A<rsub|1/6><around|(|i|)>,<space|2em>\<rho\><around|(|q<rsub|i>|)>\<longrightarrow\>-\<kappa\>,<label|eq:ex-lattice-trace-response-inputs-source>
  </equation>

  while the reference chart and the reference-price comparison give

  <\equation>
    w<rsub|i>\<longrightarrow\>-x,<space|2em>\<rho\><around|(|x|)>=0,<space|2em>A<rsub|1/5><around|(|i|)>*\<rho\><around|(|w<rsub|i>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0.<label|eq:ex-lattice-trace-reference-inputs-source>
  </equation>

  Define <math|m<rsub|i>=b<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*q<rsub|i>-w<rsub|i>>. Then

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*q<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*C*p<rsub|i><rsup|-5/6>\<longrightarrow\>0,
  </equation*>

  so <math|m<rsub|i>\<to\>x>. Since <math|\<rho\><around|(|q<rsub|i>|)>>
  converges it is bounded, and

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|1/5><around|(|i|)>*\<rho\><around|(|m<rsub|i>|)>=<frac|A<rsub|1/5><around|(|i|)>|b<rsub|i>>*\<rho\><around|(|q<rsub|i>|)>-A<rsub|1/5><around|(|i|)>*\<rho\><around|(|w<rsub|i>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0
  </equation*>

  by <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-age-over-arch> and
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-reference-inputs-source>. Finally, if the local
  field estimate is <math|p<rsub|i>*<space|0.17em>s<rsub|i><around|(|m<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>0>,
  then <math|A<rsub|1/5><around|(|i|)>\<leqslant\>p<rsub|i>> gives
  <math|A<rsub|1/5><around|(|i|)>*s<rsub|i><around|(|m<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>0>.
  These are exactly the three limits consumed by the rank-one
  approximate-kernel lemma; the remaining price-receiver construction is its
  finite-dimensional argument, not another analytic estimate.

  The response and reference estimates are proved below from the local
  charged coefficient and the paid response column of the same finite-volume
  history. In particular the arch comparison is a closed-interval limit, and
  the transport error is the normalized bias of the Coffer construction;
  neither requires an additional rate assumption on the arch.

  <subsubsection|How the two weighted receipts are
  assembled><label|subsec:ex-lattice-trace-weighted-assembly>

  The weighted price receipt for the medium uses only the response-price
  limit, the reference-price cancellation, and the age-rate comparison just
  proved. It is therefore useful to distinguish it from the stronger estimate
  that supplies the weighted field image. If
  <math|\<rho\><around|(|q<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>-\<kappa\>>, then
  <math|<around|\||\<rho\><around|(|q<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M> eventually
  for some <math|M>; thus

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||<frac|A<rsub|1/5><around|(|i|)>|b<rsub|i>>*\<rho\><around|(|q<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*M*p<rsub|i><rsup|-4/5>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  No weighted convergence rate for the response flux itself is needed here.
  The separate reference-chart estimate is the cancellation

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|1/5><around|(|i|)>*\<rho\><around|(|w<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>0.
  </equation*>

  This identifies the precise roles of the two limits and avoids conflating a
  field-size estimate with a price estimate.

  For the field image, the construction proves the stronger statement
  <math|p<rsub|i>*s<rsub|i><around|(|m<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>0> in the
  finite-dimensional chart norm. The transfer to the weaker age weight is the
  monotonicity estimate <math|A<rsub|1/5><around|(|i|)>\<leqslant\>p<rsub|i>>;
  there is no loss from summing over a basis because the chart is a fixed
  finite-dimensional operator. The bound <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|q<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>=O<around|(|A<rsub|1/6><around|(|i|)>|)>>
  alone proves only <math|m<rsub|i>\<to\>x> and does not imply this field
  estimate. The latter is supplied by the local pricing argument and its
  finite-volume passage.

  <subsubsection|The response limits and the remaining
  estimates><label|subsec:ex-lattice-trace-weighted-route>

  The response-size and response-flux limits above follow from a concrete
  separation argument. Let <math|\<cal-T\>> be the linear map from the
  transported finite-volume coefficient field to the grade-five response, and
  let <math|\<lambda\>> be the flux functional on the transported fields. On
  the limiting field, their compatibility is
  <math|\<rho\>*<around|(|\<cal-T\>*v<rsub|i>|)>=\<lambda\><around|(|v<rsub|i>|)>>.
  At fixed level <math|i>, write <math|v<rsub|i><around|(|\<nu\>|)>> for the
  finite-volume transported field of the chosen response, indexed by the
  finite-volume index <math|\<nu\>> (a different object from the rate
  <math|p<rsub|i>>). The local charged-configuration estimate gives, on a set
  in the separation ultrafilter,

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-T\>*v<rsub|i><around|(|\<nu\>|)>|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C<rsub|T>*A<rsub|1/6><around|(|i|)>,<eq-number>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||\<lambda\><around|(|v<rsub|i><around|(|\<nu\>|)>|)>+\<kappa\>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C<rsub|1>*A<rsub|-1/4><around|(|i|)>+C<rsub|2>*A<rsub|-1><around|(|i|)>+<frac|p<rsub|i><rsup|-30>+p<rsub|i><rsup|-100>|12>.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-trace-finite-response-price>>>>>
  </align>

  Here <math|C<rsub|T>,C<rsub|1>,C<rsub|2>> are fixed constants from the
  finite-dimensional coefficient, response, and flux maps. The first line is
  the field-cost part of the estimate; the second is the flux-price part. The
  transported field converges along that ultrafilter to the infinite volume
  field <math|v<rsub|i>>. Since <math|\<cal-T\>> and <math|\<lambda\>> are
  continuous linear maps, the inequalities pass to their limits. Therefore
  <math|q<rsub|i>=\<cal-T\>*v<rsub|i>> satisfies the response norm bound and
  <math|\<rho\><around|(|q<rsub|i>|)>=\<lambda\><around|(|v<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>-\<kappa\>>.
  Every term on the right tends to zero by
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-age-rules>, proving
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-response-inputs-source>.

  Here is the finite-volume derivation, including its normalization. Work in
  the fixed coefficient frame, with its supremum norm, and let <math|P>
  delete the unit coordinate. Thus <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|P*z|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>>.
  The map <math|\<cal-T\>> is signed averaging followed by the fixed
  identification with real covariant arrays. Put
  <math|\<lambda\>=\<rho\>*\<cal-T\>>. Since the unit has zero vector flux,
  <math|\<lambda\>*P=\<lambda\>>. These are identities of these maps, not a
  norm assertion about the nonlinear local coefficient construction.

  At the fixed level <math|k<rsub|i>> write <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|i>> for
  the budget threshold, <math|X<rsub|i><around|(|\<nu\>|)>> for the chosen
  coefficient array, and <math|B<rsub|i><around|(|\<nu\>|)>> for the public
  labels produced from its atomic certificate. Its charge is
  <math|q=\<vartheta\><rsub|i><rsup|2>*A<rsub|1/6><around|(|i|)>>, and its
  attainment cost is at most <math|p<rsub|i>>. Apply the Bill specialization
  of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-coffer-supplier>, with
  <math|u=1>, separately to the four entries. It gives the actual columns
  <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|k<rsub|i>,\<nu\>>> of those labels and

  <\equation>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|i><around|(|\<nu\>|)>-\<cal-C\><rsub|k<rsub|i>,\<nu\>>*B<rsub|i><around|(|\<nu\>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>\<vartheta\><rsub|i><rsup|2>*A<rsub|-1/4><around|(|i|)>.<label|eq:trace-weighted-paid-bias>
  </equation>

  Indeed its column error is at most <math|4*<around|(|1+c<rsub|k<rsub|i>>|)>*\<vartheta\><rsub|i><rsup|2>*A<rsub|1/6><around|(|i|)>/A<rsub|1/2><around|(|i|)>>,
  and <math|4*<around|(|1+c<rsub|k<rsub|i>>|)>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|1/12><around|(|i|)>>
  eventually. The exponent is exactly <math|1/12+1/6-1/2=-1/4>. The tail is
  chosen before the period, volume and atomic certificate; taking a maximum
  over the four entries preserves it.

  The transported field and its local comparison are, by definition,

  <\equation*>
    v<rsub|i><around|(|\<nu\>|)>=\<vartheta\><rsub|i><rsup|-2>*P*\<cal-C\><rsub|k<rsub|i>,\<nu\>>*B<rsub|i><around|(|\<nu\>|)>,<space|2em>y<rsub|i><around|(|\<nu\>|)>=P*<around|(|\<vartheta\><rsub|i><rsup|-2>*X<rsub|i><around|(|\<nu\>|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The column here is exactly the one in the Coffer bias, so

  <\equation>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|i><around|(|\<nu\>|)>-y<rsub|i><around|(|\<nu\>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|-1/4><around|(|i|)>.<label|eq:trace-weighted-normalized-bias>
  </equation>

  No bound for <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|i><rsup|-2>> is lost: it cancels the
  factor <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|i><rsup|2>> in
  <eqref|eq:trace-weighted-paid-bias>. Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-charge-bounds>
  and the payment accuracy bound following it give

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|y<rsub|i><around|(|\<nu\>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|1/6><around|(|i|)>,<space|2em><around|\||\<lambda\><around|(|y<rsub|i><around|(|\<nu\>|)>|)>+\<kappa\>|\|>\<leqslant\>4<around|\<\|\|\>|\<lambda\>|\<\|\|\>>*A<rsub|-1><around|(|i|)>+<around|(|p<rsub|i><rsup|-30>+p<rsub|i><rsup|-100>|)>/12.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|A<rsub|-1/4>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|1/6>>, the triangle inequality
  now yields

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-T\>*v<rsub|i><around|(|\<nu\>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-T\>|\<\|\|\>>*A<rsub|1/6><around|(|i|)>,
  </equation*>

  and

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<lambda\><around|(|v<rsub|i><around|(|\<nu\>|)>|)>+\<kappa\>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|\<lambda\>|\<\|\|\>>*A<rsub|-1/4><around|(|i|)>+4<around|\<\|\|\>|\<lambda\>|\<\|\|\>>*A<rsub|-1><around|(|i|)>+<around|(|p<rsub|i><rsup|-30>+p<rsub|i><rsup|-100>|)>/12.
  </equation*>

  Thus <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-finite-response-price> holds with
  <math|C<rsub|T>=2<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-T\>|\<\|\|\>>>,
  <math|C<rsub|1>=<around|\<\|\|\>|\<lambda\>|\<\|\|\>>> and
  <math|C<rsub|2>=4<around|\<\|\|\>|\<lambda\>|\<\|\|\>>>. The maximum norm
  on direction arrays is why no factor four occurs in the transport error.

  These fields really admit the fixed-level limit used above. Here is the
  uniform bound needed before invoking compactness. Write <math|c<rsub|j+1>>
  for the stage column allowance and <math|L<rsub|j>> for the block side. The
  adjacent calibrated-column inequality is

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<cal-C\><rsub|j,\<nu\>>*b|\|><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|j+1>*L<rsub|j><rsup|d>*<around|\||\<cal-C\><rsub|j+1,\<nu\>>*b|\|><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>.
  </equation*>

  At the reference level the column is <math|b> itself. Induction over the
  finitely many levels up to <math|k> gives

  <\equation>
    <around|\||b|\|><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|d,k>*<around|\||\<cal-C\><rsub|k,\<nu\>>*b|\|><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>,<space|2em>B<rsub|d,k>=<big|prod><rsub|0\<leqslant\>j\<less\>k>c<rsub|j+1>*L<rsub|j><rsup|d>.<label|eq:trace-weighted-column-inverse>
  </equation>

  The factors below the reference level only enlarge the bound, since they
  are at least one. This constant depends on <math|d,k> but not on the
  volume, period or label. The Bill column has coefficient size at most two
  by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-trace-bill-column-size>, so its public
  label has norm at most <math|2*B<rsub|d,k>>. A finite-dimensional closed
  ball is compact; the fixed separation ultrafilter therefore gives a limit
  of these public labels. Changing labels outside its large set has no
  effect. The joint moving-label convergence defining the arch column sends
  this limit to the limit of <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|k<rsub|i>,\<nu\>>*B<rsub|i><around|(|\<nu\>|)>>.
  Applying the fixed maps <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|i><rsup|-2>*P> and
  <math|\<cal-T\>> gives precisely <math|q<rsub|i>>, not a second response
  chosen after taking the limit.

  The same argument applies to all three reference profiles, not just the one
  whose limit is the equation-of-motion vector. Write
  <math|P\<in\>{\<ast\>,0,1}> for the stress profile and the two correction
  profiles. Denote their finite-volume local probes by
  <math|c<rsub|i><rsup|P><around|(|\<nu\>|)>> and the corresponding
  transported fields by <math|v<rsub|i><rsup|P><around|(|\<nu\>|)>>. In the
  local radial chart, the canonical probe has zero unit coordinate and its
  paid coefficient is exactly <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|i><rsup|2>*c<rsub|i><rsup|P><around|(|\<nu\>|)>>.
  The first assertion follows by evaluating the probe at the zero field; the
  second is the definition of its weighted local projection.
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:trace-reference-atomic-certificate>
  supplies this full observable with charge
  <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|i><rsup|2>*A<rsub|1/6><around|(|i|)>> and
  attainment price at most <math|p<rsub|i>>. Applying
  <eqref|eq:trace-weighted-paid-bias> to this atomic coefficient and then
  deleting the unit coordinate gives

  <\equation>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|i><rsup|P><around|(|\<nu\>|)>-c<rsub|i><rsup|P><around|(|\<nu\>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|-1/4><around|(|i|)>.<label|eq:trace-weighted-reference-bias>
  </equation>

  Thus the reference uses its own probe and its own paid public label; it is
  not compared to the chosen-response field
  <math|v<rsub|i><around|(|\<nu\>|)>>.

  Here is the precise finite-dimensional calibration needed for this passage.
  Let <math|a<rsup|P>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|3>> be the coefficient triple of
  the limiting profile and <math|V:\<bbb-R\><rsup|3>\<to\>E> the fixed raw
  vector map. The local probe classification proved in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:trace-reference-local-calibration> gives
  triples <math|a<rsub|i><rsup|P><around|(|\<nu\>|)>> with

  <\equation>
    \<cal-T\>*c<rsub|i><rsup|P><around|(|\<nu\>|)>=V*a<rsub|i><rsup|P><around|(|\<nu\>|)>,<space|2em>\<rho\>*<around|(|V*a<rsub|i><rsup|P><around|(|\<nu\>|)>|)>=\<rho\>*<around|(|V*a<rsup|P>|)>.<label|eq:trace-weighted-calibration-price>
  </equation>

  For the two elementary correction profiles the triples are exactly
  <math|a<rsup|0>,a<rsup|1>>. For the stress profile the local stress probe
  tends to the negative Maxwell tensor. The injective grade-four
  stress-coordinate map of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-trace-free-stress-basis>
  has a continuous left inverse, so its coefficient triples tend to
  <math|a<rsup|\<ast\>>=<around|(|-1,1/4,-1|)>>. This convergence is uniform
  in the local configurations on the late-level, sufficiently large-period
  sets: otherwise choose a violating sequence with levels and periods tending
  to infinity and apply the same local-probe limit and left inverse to that
  sequence. Consequently, for every <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|a<rsub|i><rsup|P><around|(|\<nu\>|)>-a<rsup|P>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>
  </equation*>

  on an ultrafilter-large set for every sufficiently large <math|i>.

  Put <math|U<rsub|i><rsup|P>=lim<rsub|\<nu\>\<to\>\<cal-L\>>
  \<cal-T\>*v<rsub|i><rsup|P><around|(|\<nu\>|)>> and
  <math|v<rsub|P>=V*a<rsup|P>>. By <eqref|eq:trace-weighted-reference-bias>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|U<rsub|i><rsup|P>-v<rsub|P>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-T\>|\<\|\|\>>*A<rsub|-1/4><around|(|i|)>+<around|\<\|\|\>|V|\<\|\|\>>*\<epsilon\>
  </equation*>

  for every sufficiently large <math|i>. First let <math|i\<to\>\<infty\>>,
  then <math|\<epsilon\>*\<downarrow\>*0>, to obtain
  <math|U<rsub|i><rsup|P>\<to\>v<rsub|P>>. For the price, use the exact
  identity <eqref|eq:trace-weighted-calibration-price>, rather than the
  qualitative convergence of the triples. It gives directly

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<rho\><around|(|U<rsub|i><rsup|P>|)>-\<rho\><around|(|v<rsub|P>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|\<rho\>|\<\|\|\>><space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-T\>|\<\|\|\>>*A<rsub|-1/4><around|(|i|)>,<space|2em>A<rsub|1/5><around|(|i|)>*<around|\||\<rho\><around|(|U<rsub|i><rsup|P>|)>-\<rho\><around|(|v<rsub|P>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|\<rho\>|\<\|\|\>><space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-T\>|\<\|\|\>>*A<rsub|-1/20><around|(|i|)>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  For <math|P=\<ast\>>, the equation-of-motion identification is
  <math|v<rsub|\<ast\>>=-x> and <math|\<rho\><around|(|x|)>=0>; taking
  <math|w<rsub|i>=U<rsub|i><rsup|\<ast\>>> proves
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-reference-inputs-source>. The other two cases
  are the weighted correction-profile estimates used by the scribe. No rate
  of convergence of <math|a<rsub|i><rsup|\<ast\>><around|(|\<nu\>|)>> to
  <math|a<rsup|\<ast\>>> has been assumed.

  The remaining chart-field input required by the general lemma is the exact
  limit

  <\equation>
    p<rsub|i>*<space|0.17em>s<rsub|i><around|(|m<rsub|i>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0.<label|eq:ex-lattice-trace-chart-field-decay>
  </equation>

  It is independent of the response-size and flux estimates above. It is the
  conclusion of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-trace-small-means> with exponent
  <math|1>, applied to the present medium: in the notation of that lemma,
  <math|z<rsub|i>=p<rsub|i>>, <math|\<zeta\><rsub|i>=b<rsub|i>>, and its
  medium <math|v<rsub|i>> is the present <math|m<rsub|i>>. The hypotheses of
  that lemma are the scale relations, the core representation
  <eqref|eq:ex-trace-medium-core> of this medium, the response and scaling
  identities, the local price bound for finitely many detector fields, the
  detector lower bound <eqref|eq:ex-trace-small-means-detector>, and the
  scalar rate <eqref|eq:ex-trace-small-means-rate>. The detector lower bound
  is essential. Bounds for the pairings of
  <math|s<rsub|i><around|(|m<rsub|i>|)>> with finitely many tested fields
  give a bound for its norm only because those tests separate the
  finite-dimensional response space with a lower bound uniform in <math|i>;
  finite dimensionality alone does not provide this. All the hypotheses
  listed are inputs to this step, and no claim that they follow from the
  scalar rate calculation is made here.

  For completeness the medium identity uses the same arch column as these
  limits. Let <math|R> denote signed averaging, <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|k>> the
  normalized core column of the arch, and
  <math|a<rsub|k>,a<rsub|k><rsup|\<ast\>>> the limiting public chosen and
  stress labels. Signed equivariance of the same-history columns gives
  <math|R*\<cal-E\><rsub|k>=\<cal-E\><rsub|k>*R>, componentwise. Hence, with
  <math|b=b<around|(|k|)>\<gtr\>0>,

  <\equation*>
    b<rsup|-1>*R*\<cal-E\><rsub|k>*a<rsub|k>-R*\<cal-E\><rsub|k>*a<rsub|k><rsup|\<ast\>>=b<rsup|-1>*\<cal-E\><rsub|k>*R*<around|(|a<rsub|k>-b*a<rsub|k><rsup|\<ast\>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The left side is exactly the medium already constructed, and the right side
  is the core representation <eqref|eq:ex-trace-medium-core>, with
  <math|x<rsub|i>=R*<around|(|a<rsub|k<rsub|i>>-b<rsub|i>*a<rsub|k<rsub|i>><rsup|\<ast\>>|)>>.
  Both terms in this identity use the same arch: replacing one by a
  separately chosen column limit would invalidate it. The response identity
  in the small-means lemma is the defining normalization of <math|s<rsub|i>>.
  Central inversion acts as minus the identity on these vector arrays, so its
  odd-grade scaling identity applies. Thus, when the same core is inserted in
  the priced mission of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-trace-mission-price-transfer>,
  the resulting field is the present <math|s<rsub|i><around|(|m<rsub|i>|)>>.
  The detector and the price used there remain the substantive field
  estimates, rather than consequences of the coefficient-size bound just
  proved.

  <subsection|A finite-volume separation estimate><label|sec:ex-lattice-trace-local-pairing>

  We prove the separation estimate that converts a localized probe into a
  small two-site correlation. The proof is a three-term bilinear expansion;
  all quantitative inputs specific to the selected construction are written
  as precise hypotheses.

  Fix a finite periodic gauge configuration space <math|\<Omega\>> with
  normalized Wilson expectation <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<beta\>>>, and a
  reflection-positive cell tiling at selected level <math|i> with
  <math|n\<geqslant\>1> cells. Its admissible observables are bounded
  measurable gauge-invariant functions in the chamber-local domain. For each
  cell <math|a>, its copy <math|C<rsub|a>*F> is the translated pullback of
  <math|F> under that cell's reflection; if the reflection has odd sign
  parity, the copy is complex-conjugated. Define the root bound by

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-R\><rsub|i><around|(|F|)>=<around*|\||\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<beta\>>*<big|prod><rsub|a=1><rsup|n>C<rsub|a>*F|\|><rsup|1/n>.
  </equation*>

  The local positivity estimate supplies the following inequalities for
  observables in the chamber-local domain:

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\||\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<beta\>>*<around|[|F*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|a<around|(|x|)>>*\<omega\>|)>*G*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|a<around|(|y|)>>*\<omega\>|)>|]>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<cal-R\><rsub|i><around|(|F|)>*\<cal-R\><rsub|i><around|(|G|)>*<space|1em><around|(|x\<ne\>y|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-root-product>>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-R\><rsub|i>*<around|(|F+G|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<cal-R\><rsub|i><around|(|F|)>+\<cal-R\><rsub|i><around|(|G|)>.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-root-sum>>>>>
  </align>

  Here <math|x,y> are coarse sites in the tiling,
  <math|a<around|(|x|)>,a<around|(|y|)>> are their fine anchor sites, and
  <math|\<omega\>\<in\>\<Omega\>> is a gauge configuration. The anchor map is
  injective on the two sites under consideration. The first estimate is the
  reflected-cell product bound; the second is the root-bound triangle
  inequality. We use them only when the observables and all indicated sums
  and differences remain in the bounded measurable, gauge-invariant,
  chamber-local domain. The local error scale at selected level <math|i> is

  <\equation*>
    t<rsub|i><around|(|a|)>=exp <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<frac|a|10<rsup|4>><around|(|log
    p<rsub|i>|)><rsup|48>|)>,<space|2em>p<rsub|i>\<longrightarrow\>\<infty\>.
  </equation*>

  It satisfies <math|t<rsub|i><around|(|a|)>*t<rsub|i><around|(|c|)>=t<rsub|i>*<around|(|a+c|)>>
  and is increasing in <math|a> once <math|p<rsub|i>\<gtr\>1>. Since
  <math|p<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>, also <math|<around|(|log
  p<rsub|i>|)><rsup|48>\<to\>\<infty\>> and hence
  <math|t<rsub|i><around|(|1|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>; in particular
  <math|t<rsub|i><around|(|1|)>\<geqslant\>4> for all sufficiently large
  <math|i>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Finite-volume two-site error><label|prop:ex-lattice-finite-volume-pairing>Let
    <math|P,Q,U,V> be observables in the chamber-local domain at selected
    level <math|i>, and let <math|x\<ne\>y> be coarse sites whose fine anchor
    sites are distinct. Suppose

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-R\><rsub|i><around|(|P|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>t<rsub|i><around|(|5|)>,>|<cell|\<cal-R\><rsub|i><around|(|Q|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>t<rsub|i><around|(|5|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-pair-main-cost>>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-R\><rsub|i>*<around|(|Q-V|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>t<rsub|i>*<around|(|-300|)>,>|<cell|\<cal-R\><rsub|i>*<around|(|P-U|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>t<rsub|i>*<around|(|-200|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-pair-local-errors>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<beta\>>*<around|[|U*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|a<around|(|x|)>>*\<omega\>|)>*V*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|a<around|(|y|)>>*\<omega\>|)>|]>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>t<rsub|i>*<around|(|-200|)>.>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-pair-probe>>>>>
    </align>

    Then

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<beta\>>*<around|[|P*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|a<around|(|x|)>>*\<omega\>|)>*Q*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|a<around|(|y|)>>*\<omega\>|)>|]>|\|>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|i>*<around|(|-100|)>
    </equation*>

    for all sufficiently large <math|i>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Write <math|<around|\<langle\>|F,G|\<rangle\>><rsub|x,y>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<beta\>>*<around|[|F*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|a<around|(|x|)>>*\<omega\>|)>*G*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|a<around|(|y|)>>*\<omega\>|)>|]>>.
    By <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-root-sum> and the assumptions,

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-R\><rsub|i><around|(|V|)>\<leqslant\>\<cal-R\><rsub|i><around|(|Q|)>+\<cal-R\><rsub|i>*<around|(|V-Q|)>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|i><around|(|5|)>+t<rsub|i>*<around|(|-300|)>.
    </equation*>

    The definition gives <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|i>*<around|(|-F|)>=\<cal-R\><rsub|i><around|(|F|)>>,
    since negating <math|F> changes the product of its <math|n> cell copies
    only by the overall sign <math|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|n>>. Hence the
    assumed bound for <math|Q-V> also bounds <math|V-Q>. Bilinearity gives
    the exact identity

    <\equation>
      <around|\<langle\>|P,Q|\<rangle\>><rsub|x,y>=<around|\<langle\>|U,V|\<rangle\>><rsub|x,y>+<around|\<langle\>|P,Q-V|\<rangle\>><rsub|x,y>+<around|\<langle\>|P-U,V|\<rangle\>><rsub|x,y>.<label|eq:ex-lattice-pair-telescope>
    </equation>

    Indeed, the right hand side equals <math|<around|\<langle\>|U,V|\<rangle\>>+<around|\<langle\>|P,Q|\<rangle\>>-<around|\<langle\>|P,V|\<rangle\>>+<around|\<langle\>|P,V|\<rangle\>>-<around|\<langle\>|U,V|\<rangle\>>>.
    Using <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-root-product> on the last two terms and
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-pair-main-cost>\U<eqref|eq:ex-lattice-pair-probe>
    therefore yields

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\||<around|\<langle\>|P,Q|\<rangle\>><rsub|x,y>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>t<rsub|i>*<around|(|-200|)>+t<rsub|i><around|(|5|)>*t<rsub|i>*<around|(|-300|)>+t<rsub|i>*<around|(|-200|)>*<around*|(|t<rsub|i><around|(|5|)>+t<rsub|i>*<around|(|-300|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=t<rsub|i>*<around|(|-200|)>+t<rsub|i>*<around|(|-295|)>+t<rsub|i>*<around|(|-195|)>+t<rsub|i>*<around|(|-500|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>4*t<rsub|i>*<around|(|-195|)>.>>>>
    </align*>

    The last inequality uses monotonicity in the exponent. Since
    <math|t<rsub|i><around|(|1|)>\<geqslant\>4>,

    <\equation*>
      4*t<rsub|i>*<around|(|-195|)>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|i><around|(|1|)>*t<rsub|i>*<around|(|-195|)>=t<rsub|i>*<around|(|-194|)>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|i>*<around|(|-100|)>,
    </equation*>

    again by monotonicity. This proves the conclusion.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Application to a selected response
  insertion><label|subsec:ex-lattice-mileage-application>

  For the selected construction, the proposition applies with <math|P> equal
  to the measured grade-five marker, that is, the one-point observation of
  the probe's grade-five label; <math|Q> the one-point observation of the
  selected grade-four insertion label, which is a separately measured
  grade-four field and is not obtained from the marker; <math|U> the pullback
  of the localized coarse witness supplied by the probe's receipt; and
  <math|V> the pullback of the bounded coarse approximation supplied, at the
  same finite volume and frame level, for that grade-four insertion. The
  hypotheses of the proposition then take the following form: both measured
  markers have root bound at most <math|t<rsub|i><around|(|5|)>>; the local
  approximation satisfies <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|i>*<around|(|Q-V|)>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|i>*<around|(|-300|)>>;
  the probe-to-marker difference satisfies
  <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|i>*<around|(|P-U|)>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|i>*<around|(|-200|)>>;
  and the distant-site probe correlation satisfies
  <math|<around|\||<around|\<langle\>|U,V|\<rangle\>><rsub|x,y>|\|>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|i>*<around|(|-200|)>>.
  The last bound is the probe's separated-site estimate applied to the coarse
  approximation of the insertion; this is legitimate because that
  approximation also satisfies the supremum bound
  <math|t<rsub|i><around|(|150000|)>>, the grade-four window condition and
  the room condition required of a test in that estimate. With these four
  receipts, the proposition proves the required two-site price bound
  <math|t<rsub|i>*<around|(|-100|)>>.

  The proof above discharges the finite-volume assembly estimate. The
  coefficient-to-root and moving-depth localization arguments are supplied
  below. The probe witness and its separated-site price still require the
  actual Crown construction described at the end of this section. No
  continuum or infinite-volume limit is used in this proposition.

  <subsubsection|Production at the moving frame and localization
  depths><label|subsec:ex-trace-pairing-production>

  We now supply the quantitative root and localization bounds used above. All
  coefficient maps and pullbacks in this subsection belong to the one history
  at <math|<around|(|N,p|)>>; the estimates are uniform in <math|N\<gtr\>k>
  and <math|p>. Write <math|j> for the frame depth, <math|m> for its first
  opened depth, and <math|k=<math-up|summit><around|(|j|)>>. Put

  <\equation*>
    x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>,<space|1em>y=log
    x,<space|1em>H=y<rsup|48>,<space|2em>T<around|(|a|)>=exp
    <around|(|a*H/10000|)>,<space|2em>\<Delta\>*<around|(|r,s|)>=log
    <around|(|a<rsub|r>/a<rsub|s>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|T> is evaluated at the <em|frame>, not at the summit. The summit
  is the least <math|k\<gtr\>j> with <math|\<Delta\>*<around|(|j,k|)>\<geqslant\>H>.
  The opened depth <math|m\<geqslant\>j> is the least depth with
  <math|a<rsub|j>/a<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>B<rsub|m><rsup|2>>, where
  <math|B<rsub|m>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>> is the cover size. These
  choices are made on the common clock, before any terminal volume or label
  is chosen.

  Here are the clock bounds needed in the argument, including their payment.
  We choose the numerical stage delays before extracting the common history.
  At a fixed stage, write <math|r> for its loop-path price,
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>> for its direct quadratic-action price, and
  <math|D<rsub|G>> for the fixed real dimension of the matrix coordinate
  space of the gauge group. Let <math|n<rsub|<math-up|plaq>>> be the number
  of plaquette orientations. The vacuum majorant at coupling <math|z\<gtr\>1>
  is

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|<math-up|stage>><around|(|z|)>=2*<around|(|1+4*r+1|)>+1+n<rsub|<math-up|plaq>>*<around|(|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>+r<rsup|2>|)>+4*D<rsub|G>*<around*|(|log
    16+<tfrac|1|2>log z|)>=C<rsub|<math-up|stage>>+2*D<rsub|G>*log z.
  </equation*>

  We verify the majorization, rather than assume a bound for the treasury.
  Its terms are twice the ordinary allowance plus twice the loop price, the
  forced allowance, the quadratic action price, and the logarithmic
  small-ball cost. The ordinary and forced allowances are at most one. With
  <math|\<vartheta\>> the selected threshold, <math|\<Lambda\>> the allowed
  loop cutoff, and <math|h=z<rsup|-1/2>>, the loop term is
  <math|2*z*<around|(|r*\<vartheta\><rsup|3>+r*\<vartheta\><rsup|4>|)>+\<Lambda\>*\<vartheta\><rsup|2>>.
  The clock has <math|\<vartheta\>\<leqslant\>z<rsup|-1/2>*exp
  <around|(|<around|(|log log z|)><rsup|2>|)>> and
  <math|\<Lambda\>*\<vartheta\><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>1> on the late-scale tail
  of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:rank-root-late-scale>, applied to that same
  clock. The lemma's stage thresholds are among the common entry requests.
  Hence <math|z*\<vartheta\><rsup|3>\<leqslant\>1> and
  <math|z*\<vartheta\><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>1> eventually, independently of the
  stage constants, and the loop price is at most <math|4*r+1>. The action
  term is exactly <math|n<rsub|<math-up|plaq>>*<around|(|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>+r<rsup|2>|)>>,
  since <math|z*h<rsup|2>=1>. Finally <math|log <around|(|16/h|)>=log
  16+<tfrac|1|2>log z>. Adding the four bounds proves
  <math|\<cal-T\>\<leqslant\>F<rsub|<math-up|stage>><around|(|z|)>> on a
  fixed coupling tail.

  Now let <math|L> and <math|c> be the block and column constants at that
  stage. For all sufficiently large <math|z>, impose

  <\equation*>
    log z\<geqslant\>max <around|{|1,L,c,C<rsub|<math-up|stage>>,2*D<rsub|G>+1|}>.
  </equation*>

  Writing <math|u=log z>, we have <math|C<rsub|<math-up|stage>>+2*D<rsub|G>*u\<leqslant\>u+2*D<rsub|G>*u\<leqslant\>u<rsup|2>>.
  Thus these eventual requests give, at every rung <math|r> in that stage,

  <\equation*>
    L<rsub|r>\<leqslant\>log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|r>,<space|2em>c<rsub|r>\<leqslant\>log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|r>,<space|2em>\<cal-T\><rsub|r>\<leqslant\><around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|r>|)><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  At each stage entry there are only finitely many active and next-stage
  requests. Include the displayed bounds and their adjacent-stage versions in
  the finite maximum defining the entry threshold of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:trace-joint-chart-purchase>, and then take
  prefix maxima to preserve earlier entries. Each request holds on an entire
  coupling tail with that stage fixed, so this operation is legitimate even
  though the constants vary from stage to stage. These bounds therefore hold
  on the one history selected with these delays; no history is changed after
  the extraction, and no uniform fixed-stage constant is asserted across all
  stages. The block lower bound <math|L<rsub|r>\<geqslant\>1024*<around|(|J<rsub|r+1>+10|)>>
  is a choice of the stage block before its entry threshold is paid. These
  purchases involve no new finite-volume analytic estimate.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Moving-depth bills><label|lem:ex-trace-pair-moving-bills>For each
    fixed grade <math|d>, eventually

    <\gather*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|j\<leqslant\>m\<less\>k,<space|2em>H\<leqslant\>\<Delta\>*<around|(|j,k|)>\<leqslant\>2*H,<space|2em><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>2*x,>>|<row|<cell|a<rsub|j>/a<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|6>,<space|2em><around|(|a<rsub|j>/a<rsub|m>|)><rsup|4>*\<cal-T\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|27>,<space|2em>\<Delta\>*<around|(|m,k|)>\<geqslant\>H/2.>>>>
    </gather*>

    Put <math|S=exp <around|(|<around|(|a<rsub|j>/a<rsub|m>|)><rsup|4>*\<cal-T\><rsub|m>+5|)>>,
    <math|E<rsub|k>=4*<around|(|1+c<rsub|k>|)>> and <math|G=exp
    <around|(|\<Delta\>*<around|(|m,k|)>/8|)>>. With the fixed-grade height
    <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,r>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|r><rsup|-w<rsub|d>>>,
    where <math|w<rsub|d>> is the fixed exponent of that height schedule,
    these imply

    <\gather>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|2*E<rsub|k>/\<zeta\><rsub|d,m>\<leqslant\>T<around|(|100|)>,<space|2em>2*<around|(|a<rsub|j>/a<rsub|m>|)><rsup|d>*S\<leqslant\>T<around|(|99|)>,<space|2em>G\<geqslant\>T<around|(|500|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-trace-pair-localizer-bills>>>|<row|<cell|c<rsub|m>*<around|(|a<rsub|j>/a<rsub|m>|)><rsup|d>/\<zeta\><rsub|d,m>\<leqslant\>T<around|(|2|)>,<space|2em>e*S\<leqslant\>T<around|(|2|)>,<space|2em>2*<around|(|1+c<rsub|k>|)>\<leqslant\>T<around|(|1|)>.<eq-number><label|eq:ex-trace-pair-root-bills>>>>>
    </gather>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The coupling clock has increments <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|s>-<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|r>=\<gamma\>*\<Delta\>*<around|(|r,s|)>>
    with fixed <math|\<gamma\>\<gtr\>0>. Just before the first crossing of
    <math|H>, the coupling is at most <math|x+\<gamma\>*H\<leqslant\>2*x>.
    The last block adds at most <math|log L<rsub|k-1>\<leqslant\>log
    <around|(|2*x|)>\<leqslant\>H> to the gap. Thus the gap is at most
    <math|2*H>, and <math|x+2*\<gamma\>*H\<leqslant\>2*x> eventually gives
    the stated summit coupling bound. In particular <math|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|r>\<leqslant\>2*y> and
    <math|B<rsub|r>\<leqslant\>5*y<rsup|2>> for
    <math|j\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>k>. At <math|k-1> the gap is at least
    <math|H-2*y>; hence <math|exp \<Delta\>*<around|(|j,k-1|)>\<geqslant\><around|(|5*y<rsup|2>|)><rsup|2>>
    eventually. This proves <math|m\<less\>k>. Eventually
    <math|B<rsub|j>\<gtr\>1>, so <math|a<rsub|j>/a<rsub|j>=1\<less\>B<rsub|j><rsup|2>>
    and <math|m\<gtr\>j>. By minimality of <math|m>, the preceding ratio is
    less than <math|B<rsub|m-1><rsup|2>>; the last block is at most
    <math|2*y>. Consequently <math|a<rsub|j>/a<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>2*y*<around|(|5*y<rsup|2>|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|6>>
    for large <math|y>. The treasury bill is at most
    <math|y<rsup|24>*<around|(|2*y|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|27>>. Also
    <math|\<Delta\>*<around|(|j,m|)>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|6>=o<around|(|H|)>>,
    which gives the last gap bound.

    Every fixed product of <math|c<rsub|m>,c<rsub|k>,<around|(|a<rsub|j>/a<rsub|m>|)><rsup|d>>
    and <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,m><rsup|-1>> is bounded by a fixed power of
    <math|x> and <math|y>. Its logarithm is <math|O<around|(|y+log
    y|)>=o<around|(|H|)>>. Multiplication by <math|S> adds at most
    <math|y<rsup|27>+5=o<around|(|H|)>>. Thus each such product is eventually
    bounded by <math|exp <around|(|\<epsilon\>*H|)>> for every
    <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>. Choosing respectively
    <math|\<epsilon\>=100/10000,99/10000,2/10000> and <math|1/10000> proves
    the displayed bills. Finally <math|G\<geqslant\>exp
    <around|(|H/16|)>\<geqslant\>T<around|(|500|)>>.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Root and bounded localizer from actual
    coefficients><label|prop:ex-trace-pair-coefficient-production>Let
    <math|L> be a grade-<math|d> label in the actual finite-volume history,
    with its measured field in the frame chamber. If

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|col><rsub|m><around|(|L|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|j><rsup|d>*T<around|(|1|)>,
    </equation*>

    then <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|j><around|(|O<rsub|N,p>L|)>\<leqslant\>T<around|(|5|)>>
    eventually. For localization use the same-door served representation
    constructed below. If <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|col><rsub|k><around|(|L|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|j><rsup|d>*T<around|(|1|)>>,
    there is a bounded coarse field <math|B> at <math|k>, in the window of
    radius <math|l<rsub|d>=R<rsub|d>+4>, such that

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|B|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>T*<around|(|25000*d+10000|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-R\><rsub|j>*<around|(|<math-ss|B><rsub|k,N>B-O<rsub|N,p>L|)>\<leqslant\>T*<around|(|-300|)>.
    </equation*>

    The window has room inside the frame chamber, and its pullback is
    chamber-local. In particular the supremum is at most
    <math|T<around|(|150000|)>> for <math|d\<leqslant\>5>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    For the first assertion use insertion weight <math|z=T*<around|(|-3|)>>.
    The actual exponential insertion money is
    <math|c<rsub|m>*a<rsub|m><rsup|-d><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|col><rsub|m><around|(|L|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>;
    by <eqref|eq:ex-trace-pair-root-bills> its product with <math|z> is at
    most <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,m>*T*<around|(|-3+1+2|)>=\<zeta\><rsub|d,m>>.
    The opened full-census exponential estimate therefore bounds its root by

    <\equation*>
      <frac|exp <around|(|<around|(|a<rsub|j>/a<rsub|m>|)><rsup|4>*\<cal-T\><rsub|m>+6|)>|z>=<frac|e*S|T*<around|(|-3|)>>\<leqslant\>T<around|(|5|)>.
    </equation*>

    The extra factor <math|e> accounts for the difference between the single
    insertion price <math|6> and the discrepancy price <math|5>.

    For localization use the actual phase-grid construction of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-phase-grid-approximants> with
    <math|w=a<rsub|m><rsup|d>*T<around|(|400|)>>. Its price condition is
    satisfied, since

    <\equation*>
      <frac|2*w*a<rsub|m><rsup|-d>*E<rsub|k>|\<zeta\><rsub|d,m>>\<leqslant\>T*<around|(|400+100|)>\<leqslant\>G.
    </equation*>

    The coefficient basis in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:actual-grid-basis> is
    within <math|1/4> of the identity. Inverting that same basis therefore
    expresses <math|L> as a linear combination of its terminal labels with

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|s><around|\||v<rsub|s>|\|>\<leqslant\>2<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|col><rsub|k><around|(|L|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    We retain this actual coefficient bound; replacing it by an unspecified
    operator norm times <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|L|\<\|\|\>>> would lose the
    moving-depth estimate. Each primary masked field has supremum at most
    <math|a<rsub|k><rsup|-d>*<around|(|1+c<rsub|k>|)>> and discrepancy root
    at most <math|S/w>. Linear combination, pullback linearity and the root
    triangle inequality give a field <math|B> with error

    <\equation*>
      2*a<rsub|j><rsup|d>*T<around|(|1|)>*S/<around|(|a<rsub|m><rsup|d>*T<around|(|400|)>|)>\<leqslant\>T*<around|(|99+1-400|)>=T*<around|(|-300|)>.
    </equation*>

    Its supremum is at most

    <\equation*>
      2*a<rsub|j><rsup|d>*T<around|(|1|)>*a<rsub|k><rsup|-d>*<around|(|1+c<rsub|k>|)>\<leqslant\>T*<around|(|2+20000*d|)>\<leqslant\>T*<around|(|25000*d+10000|)>,
    </equation*>

    because <math|a<rsub|j>/a<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around|(|2*H|)>=T<around|(|20000|)>>. The same finite combination
    preserves bounded measurability, gauge invariance and the window radius
    <math|l<rsub|d>>.

    For completeness, the room inequality is
    <math|2*a<rsub|k>*<around|[|l<rsub|d>+30*<around|(|J<rsub|k>+5|)>|]>\<less\>a<rsub|j>>.
    The block bounds give <math|J<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>2*y>, while
    <math|a<rsub|j>/a<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>e<rsup|H>>; hence this inequality
    holds eventually. More generally the same proof works for any radius
    bounded by a fixed power of <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>, since such
    a radius and <math|J<rsub|k>> are <math|o<around|(|e<rsup|H>|)>>. The
    pullback locality and room inclusion then put <math|B> in the required
    frame chamber. All constructions used the same <math|<around|(|N,p|)>>,
    transport and gauge doors.
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|The actual labels.> For a fixed raw reference label <math|v>
  of grade <math|d\<leqslant\>5>, the tested finite-height lift at frame
  <math|j> has coefficient bound

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|col><rsub|j><around|(|L*v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|j><rsup|d>*<space|0.17em>2*<around|(|2*\<cal-M\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|2>*b<rsup|d><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>,<space|2em>1\<leqslant\>b\<leqslant\><math-up|tiltcap><around|(|j|)>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed the tested half-accuracy bound gives an inverse cost at most two;
  the frame shrink inverse costs at most <math|2*\<cal-M\><rsub|j>>, the
  nonconstant polynomial components in these grades have degree at most two,
  and the dilation weight costs at most <math|b<rsup|d>>. For coefficient
  transport from <math|j> to <math|r\<leqslant\>k>, the paid per-step
  logarithmic column bill divided by the log block is at most
  <math|J<rsub|s><rsup|-2>>. Summing gives a log bill at most
  <math|sup<rsub|s\<geqslant\>j> J<rsub|s><rsup|-2>*\<Delta\>*<around|(|j,r|)>=o<around|(|H|)>>.
  The logarithm of the prefactor above is also <math|o<around|(|H|)>>,
  because <math|log \<cal-M\><rsub|j>=<around|(|log log x|)><rsup|2>> and
  <math|log <math-up|tiltcap><around|(|j|)>=<around|(|log log x|)><rsup|3>>.
  Allocate half of <math|H/10000> to each bill. Uniformly for
  <math|j\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>k>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|col><rsub|r><around|(|L*v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|j><rsup|d>*T<around|(|1|)>.
  </equation*>

  This supplies both hypotheses of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-trace-pair-coefficient-production>
  for the selected grade-four insertion, with the same tested map at each
  finite height. Finitely many fixed labels require only a finite
  intersection of the resulting tails.

  The measured grade-five marker uses a different argument. Its odd axial
  sector has homogeneous factor two, so its spine coefficients at <math|r>
  equal <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|r><rsup|-2><math-up|col><rsub|r><around|(|P|)>>.
  The actual spine transport from <math|k> back to <math|m> costs at most
  two. Since <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1> and
  <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|k><rsup|-2>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>,
  a summit bound <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|col><rsub|k><around|(|P|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|j><rsup|5>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|M>>
  gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|<math-up|col><rsub|m><around|(|P|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*a<rsub|j><rsup|5>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|M+1>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|j><rsup|5>*T<around|(|1|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here the last step follows from <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>2*x>
  and <math|log x=o<around|(|H|)>>. Thus its root is also at most
  <math|T<around|(|5|)>>. This application uses the actual odd-sector spine
  transport; a summit coefficient bound for an unrelated label would not
  suffice.

  <subparagraph|The intermediate insertion and the served-representation
  seam.> There is one representation issue in the localization proof that
  must not be hidden by its numerical estimates. At depth <math|k> the served
  negative played family is augmented by the positive primary factors. For
  the finite set of cell markers <math|I>, its coefficient is the sum over
  all subsets <math|A\<subset\>I>: cells in <math|A> carry the local positive
  primary, and the complementary cells carry the marched negative insertion.
  This is the finite product expansion, with no truncation of its higher
  marker coefficients. At singleton level the sum is exactly the exponential
  entry of the backward coefficient family <math|Z<rsub|k>>; its forced
  profile is unchanged, because the primary has zero forced profile. Below
  <math|k> use that coefficient family and the same sound gauge doors. The
  two transition profiles have prices

  <\equation*>
    2*\<tau\><rsub|r+1>*a<rsub|r+1><rsup|-d>*D<rsub|r>*<around|(|g<rsub|r+1>+<around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsub|r+1>.*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>|)>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>\<tau\><rsub|r+1>*a<rsub|r><rsup|-d>*g<rsub|r>\<leqslant\>1,
  </equation*>

  as verified in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-phase-grid-approximants>.
  The centers remain the opened migrating centers, with occupancy one; finite
  linear sums preserve the prescribed finite supports. These are precisely
  the numerical and geometric data for the same-door offer in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:finite-menu-history-handoff>. The latter
  invokes the whole polynomial push, including its flagged part, of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-transfer>; it does not permit
  replacing the augmented polynomial by its singleton part.

  Here is the representation construction for this augmented family. Let
  <math|A<rsub|N>> be the negative terminal birth polynomial, and let
  <math|P<rsub|k,N>> be the actual iterated push from <math|N> to <math|k>.
  The terminal observables are continuous on the compact gauge configuration
  space, so every coefficient of <math|A<rsub|N>> is integrable. The actual
  Markov push preserves integrability. The local primary is bounded and
  measurable. Thus every coefficient of

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|k>=<around|(|P<rsub|k,N>*A<rsub|N>|)>*exp<rsub|<math-up|nil>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|v\<in\>I>z<rsub|v>*F<rsub|v>|)>
  </equation*>

  is integrable: it is a finite sum of an integrable pushed coefficient times
  a bounded product of <math|F<rsub|v>>'s. Here the exponential is in the
  finite nilpotent marker algebra, and <math|F<rsub|v>> is the reflected copy
  of the positive primary. This proves integrability for the <em|whole>
  polynomial, not only for its singleton coefficients.

  The negative played insertion service supplies a representation of
  <math|P<rsub|k,N>*A<rsub|N>> with regular entries <math|f<rsub|v>>, forced
  entries <math|\<lambda\><rsub|v>>, and ordinary and flagged higher arrays
  <math|H,K>. Keep <math|H,K> unchanged and replace <math|f<rsub|v>> by
  <math|f<rsub|v>+F<rsub|v>>. This represents <math|A<rsub|k>> exactly: in
  the commutative nilpotent algebra, <math|exp<rsub|<math-up|nil>><around|(|u+v|)>=exp<rsub|<math-up|nil>>
  u*<space|0.17em>exp<rsub|<math-up|nil>> v> for zero-constant polynomials.
  Consequently all higher-array support and price bounds are unchanged, and
  the forced entries are unchanged. The identities for <math|Z<rsub|k>> above
  identify the new regular entries and the old forced entries with the
  backward coefficient family. There is no fresh choice of response, door, or
  primary coefficient.

  At the next step the actual adjacent comparison of that coefficient family
  supplies the finite elementary list: its regular difference is the
  difference of the two exponential entries, and its flagged difference is
  the finite-support forced-profile comparison transported through the same
  sound door. Their two prices are the ones displayed above. Their
  single-insertion identities follow from these exact adjacent identities,
  while their supports and parent centers are the migrated supports and
  centers already used in the negative service. The actual sound-door
  response of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:finite-menu-history-handoff>
  applies to this prepared list; its whole-polynomial push is the transition
  of <math|A<rsub|k>>, with both higher arrays retained. The integrability
  just proved permits that push. Induction from <math|k> down to <math|m>
  gives a served representation of <math|P<rsub|m,k>*A<rsub|k>>. The weighted
  local entry and forced prices at <math|m> are at most <math|2> and
  <math|1>, respectively, by the backward coefficient family bounds and the
  purchased height inequality. Together with action allowance <math|1>, the
  full-census estimate therefore costs <math|2+1+2=5> per cell, yielding
  exactly <math|S/w>.

  Finally, coefficientwise push preserves the integral, and expansion of
  <math|A<rsub|k>> over subsets of <math|I> gives, at its full mode, the
  product of the reflected differences <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|k,N>F<rsub|v>-O<rsub|N,p><around|(|T<rsub|N>*b<rsub|s>|)>>.
  Pullback locality and the room inequality identify these with copies in the
  same frame tiling. This proves the moment identity needed for the root
  bound. It supplies the intermediate representation omitted from the generic
  phase-grid discussion for the primary insertions used here.

  <subparagraph|Assembly of the actual Crown
  witness.><with|the-label|18.24.2|<label|par:trace-actual-crown>> The
  localizer just constructed is the grade-four test of the probe; it is not
  the probe's grade-five Crown witness. The source construction of that
  witness has the following explicit quantitative form. At the same summit
  <math|k>, let <math|X<rsub|e>> be the actual corrected primary entry minus
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>> times its climate field, and let
  <math|Y<rsub|e>> be its local finite-difference witness. Their common
  window must have room. If the finite lens construction gives each summand
  root at most <math|T*<around|(|-1900000|)>> and total absolute coefficient
  mass at most <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>T<around|(|100000|)>>,
  the root triangle inequality gives <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|j><around|(|<math-ss|B><rsub|k,N>Y<rsub|e>|)>\<leqslant\>T*<around|(|-1800000|)>>.
  Suppose its actual healthy-region identity gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||X<rsub|e>-Y<rsub|e>+c<rsub|e>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-<math-up|prism><around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)>>,<space|2em><around|\||X<rsub|e>-Y<rsub|e>|\|>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|M>
  </equation*>

  with a fixed <math|M> and a polynomial-size exceptional set. Multiply by
  <math|q=<around|(|a<rsub|j>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|5>\<leqslant\>T<around|(|100000|)>>
  and apply the finite signed axial average. This average is contractive for
  these root and supremum bounds and annihilates the constant vector
  <math|<around|(|c<rsub|e>|)>>. The polynomial bill is at most
  <math|T<around|(|1000|)>> eventually; the healthy error is at most
  <math|T*<around|(|-600000|)>> by the prism growth. Thus the averaged
  witness has root at most <math|T*<around|(|-1700000|)>>, global difference
  at most <math|T<around|(|101000|)>\<leqslant\>T<around|(|500000|)>>, and
  healthy error at most <math|T*<around|(|-500000|)>>. These are the Crown
  bounds used in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-trace-crown-buffer>.

  The paired estimate and healthy identity used here are constructed below
  for the actual local current and the corrected-primary terms. The finite
  summation, scaling and signed-average argument above then produces the
  witness to which the Crown buffer applies.

  <subparagraph|Payment of the paired local lens estimate.> The moving-depth
  price of each local lens can also be made explicit. Write
  <math|t=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>, <math|p=<math-up|prism><around|(|t|)>>
  and set

  <\equation*>
    w<rsub|r>=T<around|(|1000000|)>*t<rsup|2*<around|(|r-j|)>>,<space|2em>j\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>k.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|r+1>\<leqslant\>t>, these weights obey
  <math|w<rsub|r>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|r+1><rsup|-2>*w<rsub|r+1>>,
  exactly the two-marker height recurrence. The number of steps is at most
  <math|\<Delta\>*<around|(|j,k|)>\<leqslant\>2*H>, so <math|log
  w<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>100*H+4*H*log t\<leqslant\>p/32> eventually. The last
  inequality follows from <math|p=B<around|(|t|)><rsup|40>+1100>,
  <math|B<around|(|t|)>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
  t|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>, whereas <math|H\<leqslant\><around|(|log
  t|)><rsup|48>>. In particular <math|1\<leqslant\>w<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|p/32>>.

  Here is the finite-stage local payment behind this permitted weight. Let
  <math|q=B<around|(|t|)><rsup|60>+1100> be the lens truncation depth, and
  let <math|l> be its fixed cover count. Put <math|c<rsub|0>=exp
  <around|(|<around|(|s-3|)>*l|)>>, where <math|s> is the stage support
  exponent, <math|c=e<rsup|p/2>> and <math|\<epsilon\>=e<rsup|-p/8>>. The
  local lease tariff is at most <math|t<rsup|3>> at a late fixed-stage entry.
  If <math|u> is that tariff times <math|e<rsup|-q>>, then

  <\equation*>
    8*e<rsup|p>*u\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>,<space|2em>4*c*c<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|p>,<space|2em>t*c<rsub|0>*e<rsup|2>*w<rsub|k><rsup|2>/c\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed <math|q\<geqslant\>4*p> eventually, while every fixed multiple of a
  fixed power of <math|t> is at most <math|e<rsup|p/32>>. The forced
  allowance also satisfies <math|2*e<rsup|p>*b\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>> by its
  selected small-field payment. Consequently the local summed activity price
  is at most <math|3*\<epsilon\>>; <math|<around|(|2*B<around|(|t|)>+1|)><rsup|4>*<around|(|3*\<epsilon\>|)>\<leqslant\>1>
  and <math|e<rsup|3*\<epsilon\>>-1\<leqslant\>1>. These inequalities verify
  the local paired-marker expense with the actual weight <math|w<rsub|k>>.
  The remaining wing tariff is the one already paid by the local
  finite-difference construction, at most one. All bounds are uniform in the
  torus and the actual representation within its selected parameter band.

  For each reflected cell take two marker slots with the same anchor. This
  has occupancy two, independently of the number of cells; the paid occupancy
  schedule is eventually at least two. The anchor separation, after
  displacement to the lens center, exceeds both the lens diameter and
  <math|64*B<around|(|t|)>*p+2*B<around|(|t|)>>, since these are polynomial
  in <math|t> and <math|a<rsub|j>/a<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>e<rsup|H>>. The local
  lens expansion with the expense just verified therefore forms the paired
  marked polynomial. Its full-mode coefficient is the product of the
  reflected lens fields. The same-door whole-polynomial transport descends it
  to <math|m> with the recurrence for <math|w<rsub|r>> above. Its regular and
  forced singleton entries vanish, and its action allowance is one per
  marker. The full-census bound consequently has per-cell action cost four
  and weight <math|w<rsub|m><rsup|2>>, giving

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-R\><rsub|j><around|(|<math-ss|B><rsub|k,N><math-up|lens>|)>\<leqslant\><frac|exp
    <around|(|<around|(|a<rsub|j>/a<rsub|m>|)><rsup|4>*\<cal-T\><rsub|m>+4|)>|w<rsub|m><rsup|2>>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around|(|y<rsup|27>+4-200*H|)>\<leqslant\>T*<around|(|-1900000|)>.
  </equation*>

  The division by a square is essential: the construction uses two markers
  per cell. Thus neither the moving-depth choice nor the large negative
  exponent is an additional lens smallness assumption. The local lens
  expansion, its wing tariff and the healthy identity are derived in the
  following subsection.

  <subsubsection|The local finite-difference witness and its healthy
  identity><label|subsec:trace-pair-healthy-construction>

  We describe the actual fields in the Crown construction, including the sign
  and the finite approximation used there. Write
  <math|h=<math-up|peace><around|(|t|)>\<gtr\>0> for the finite-difference
  step and <math|Y<rsub|e>> for the localized geometric current in direction
  <math|e>. Its support is the inner box <math|\<cal-B\>>. For a link
  <math|a> in this box and a color direction obtained by conjugating a unit
  color by <math|g\<in\>G>, let <math|U<rsub|a,g,s>> denote multiplication of
  that link by the Lie exponential of <math|s> times the color. Set

  <\equation*>
    J<rsub|a>*<around|(|U,g|)>=\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|U<text| healthy at
    >a<text| at threshold >\<vartheta\>/4|}>>*<around|\<langle\>|Y<rsub|e><around|(|U|)><rsub|a>,<math-up|Ad><rsub|g><space|0.17em>v<rsub|a>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation*>

  This is the cutoff used in the actual witness. It is bounded measurable,
  local in the union of the current support and the inspected links at
  <math|a>, and gauge covariant. Gauge covariance follows by changing
  <math|g> by the gauge transformation at the starting vertex; Haar measure
  is unchanged. For a scalar action <math|F> define

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-L\><rsub|a,s><around|(|F|)><around|(|U|)>=<big|int><rsub|G><around*|[|J<rsub|a>*<around|(|U<rsub|a,g,s>,g|)>*e<rsup|F<around|(|U<rsub|a,g,s>|)>-F<around|(|U|)>>-J<rsub|a>*<around|(|U,g|)>|]>*d*g.
  </equation*>

  The actual finite-difference witness is

  <\equation*>
    W<rsub|e><around|(|U|)>=h<rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|a,\<nu\>>\<omega\><rsub|\<nu\>>*\<cal-L\><rsub|a,\<lambda\><rsub|\<nu\>>*h><around|(|F<rsub|q>|)><around|(|U|)>,
  </equation*>

  where the finite nodes <math|\<lambda\><rsub|\<nu\>>> and weights
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|\<nu\>>> are those of the selected order-<math|m>
  derivative formula, and <math|F<rsub|q>> is the actual local action with
  support-cover cutoff <math|q>.

  The change of a link stays within the local healthy chart. Indeed
  smoothness of the Lie exponential on the unit ball gives
  <math|<math-up|chord><around|(|exp v|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|G><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>>
  there. For the finitely many nodes take
  <math|Z=C<rsub|G>*max<rsub|\<nu\>><around|\||\<lambda\><rsub|\<nu\>>|\|>>.
  Since <math|h=\<vartheta\>*t<rsup|-1/24>>, eventually
  <math|64*Z*h\<leqslant\>\<vartheta\>>. Thus on the support of the cutoff
  the moved configurations have the same large-field flags, and the current
  and its inspected links remain inside the prescribed enlarged box. This is
  why one may use the same local action and the same local response in every
  term.

  To see the precise local expansion, write <math|F<rsub|q>> as its constant
  plus its support activities <math|f<rsub|P>> with cover at most <math|q>.
  Activities whose support misses <math|a> have zero difference under the
  link change. Hence on the support of <math|J<rsub|a>*<around|(|U<rsub|a,g,s>,g|)>>,

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|F<rsub|q><around|(|U<rsub|a,g,s>|)>-F<rsub|q><around|(|U|)>>=<big|prod><rsub|P\<ni\>a><around*|(|1+e<rsup|f<rsub|P><around|(|U<rsub|a,g,s>|)>-f<rsub|P><around|(|U|)>>-1|)>.
  </equation*>

  Expanding this product gives the empty-set term and every nonempty cluster
  of changed activities, without discarding any term. The empty term is the
  local directional finite difference; the other terms are exactly the finite
  response corrections used to construct the corrected primary. Locality
  follows by taking the union of the supports in each term. The
  support-weighted sum of their absolute prices is bounded by the exponential
  of the sum of the individual difference prices. This is the elementary
  inequality <math|<big|sum><rsub|A\<ne\>\<varnothing\>><big|prod><rsub|P\<in\>A>b<rsub|P>=<big|prod><rsub|P><around|(|1+b<rsub|P>|)>-1\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<big|sum><rsub|P>b<rsub|P>>-1>
  for <math|b<rsub|P>\<geqslant\>0>. It also proves convergence and preserves
  the supports when the finite activity cutoff is enlarged.

  The individual action-difference prices follow by the fundamental theorem
  of calculus along the changed link. In the actual selected parameter band
  their weighted sum is bounded, up to fixed stage constants and subpower
  geometric factors, by

  <\equation*>
    t*\<vartheta\>*<space|0.17em>Z*h+t*<around|(|\<vartheta\><rsup|3>+\<vartheta\><rsup|4>|)>+\<vartheta\><rsup|2><math-up|humble><around|(|t|)>+<math-up|hue><around|(|t|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<vartheta\>=t<rsup|-1/2>*\<cal-M\><around|(|t|)>>,
  <math|h=\<vartheta\>*t<rsup|-1/24>>, and the monitor and the displayed
  geometric factors are subpowers of <math|t>. The first term is
  <math|Z*\<cal-M\><around|(|t|)><rsup|2>*t<rsup|-1/24>>; the second and
  third have strictly negative powers as well. Thus this sum tends to zero,
  and is eventually at most one. This is the wing tariff used in the
  paired-lens payment, now for these actual link changes. The bound is
  uniform in the torus because the price is a support-weighted sum based at
  <math|a>, not a sum over all translated cells. The cutoff current is at
  most <math|t> eventually: its derivative bound is a fixed subpower current
  bound. The same expansion bounds each lens by
  <math|t*<around|(|e<rsup|2>+1|)>>, and therefore the finite weighted sum
  defining <math|W<rsub|e>> is at most <math|t<rsup|2>> after paying its
  fixed node weights, the subpower number of links and
  <math|h<rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>t<rsup|2/3>> eventually. These are the global
  bounds required above.

  Choose a finite activity cabinet <math|F<rsub|q,K>> using the very same
  activities as <math|F<rsub|q>>. Their uniformly summable prices imply
  uniform convergence of the action differences and hence of their
  exponentials. More explicitly, if the omitted absolute difference price is
  <math|\<eta\><rsub|K>\<to\>0> and all full difference prices are at most
  <math|C>, then the difference of exponentials is at most
  <math|e<rsup|C>*<around|(|e<rsup|\<eta\><rsub|K>>-1|)>>, uniformly in
  <math|U> and <math|g>. Multiplication by the bounded cutoff current and
  integration over Haar measure preserve this convergence. If
  <math|D<rsub|m>=<big|sum><rsub|a,\<nu\>><around|\||\<omega\><rsub|\<nu\>>|\|>>,
  choose the common cabinet sufficiently large that each lens error is at
  most <math|h*e<rsup|-p>/<around|(|1+D<rsub|m>|)>>. Then

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||W<rsub|e>-h<rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|a,\<nu\>>\<omega\><rsub|\<nu\>>*\<cal-L\><rsub|a,\<lambda\><rsub|\<nu\>>*h><around|(|F<rsub|q,K>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-p>.
  </equation*>

  There are finitely many directions, so one cabinet suffices for all of
  them. Enlarging it to contain the finite action and loop cutoffs used by
  the corrected primary preserves these tail bounds. The cabinet is chosen at
  the given finite volume; no volume-uniform bound on its number of terms is
  needed.

  On the common healthy region the cutoff is one for every moved
  configuration. For the healthy identity we refactor the finite cabinet,
  keeping its short main and loop action in the core. Put
  <math|F=-\<beta\>*M-L>, where <math|M> and <math|L> are the actual finite
  main-action and loop-action sums. The exact local action identity is

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|q,K><around|(|U<rsub|a,g,s>|)>-F<rsub|q,K><around|(|U|)>=F<around|(|U<rsub|a,g,s>|)>-F<around|(|U|)>+<big|sum><rsub|P\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|a>>\<delta\><rsub|P>*<around|(|U,g,s|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|a>> contains only the residual changed
  activities, excluding those already placed in <math|F>. Thus its
  exponential factors as <math|e<rsup|F<around|(|U<rsub|a,g,s>|)>-F<around|(|U|)>>*<big|prod><rsub|P\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|a>><around|(|1+<around|(|e<rsup|\<delta\><rsub|P>>-1|)>|)>>.
  This differs from the expansion of the whole action used for the lens
  price: here the core exponential stays outside the product. Let
  <math|D<rsub|Y>> denote the current directional derivative. The empty
  residual cluster is

  <\equation*>
    h<rsup|-1>*\<cal-D\><rsub|m>*<around|(|F,Y|)>=<math-up|div><space|0.17em>Y+D<rsub|Y>*F+K<rsub|m>*<around|(|F,Y|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|K<rsub|m>> is the exact finite-difference remainder (defined by
  this identity). Write <math|R<rsub|a,A>> for the nonempty-cluster
  corrections from the product above and put
  <math|\<cal-C\>=h<rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|a,\<nu\>,A\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>\<omega\><rsub|\<nu\>>*R<rsub|a,A>>.
  The corrected primary is precisely the sum

  <\equation*>
    Z<rsub|e>=\<beta\>*<around|(|B<rsub|e>-D<rsub|Y>*M|)>+<around|(|<math-up|div><space|0.17em>Y<rsub|e>-<math-up|div><space|0.17em>Y<rsub|e><around|(|1|)>|)>-D<rsub|Y>*L+K<rsub|m>*<around|(|F,Y<rsub|e>|)>+\<cal-C\>.
  </equation*>

  These are the actual residual, centered divergence, loop, finite-difference
  and nonempty-cluster terms; each is constructed with the same current,
  finite cabinet and finite action sums. Combining the empty and nonempty
  terms and using <math|D<rsub|Y>*F=-\<beta\>*D<rsub|Y>*M-D<rsub|Y>*L> gives
  the exact identity

  <\equation*>
    h<rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|a,\<nu\>>\<omega\><rsub|\<nu\>>*\<cal-L\><rsub|a,\<lambda\><rsub|\<nu\>>*h><around|(|F<rsub|q,K>|)>=Z<rsub|e>-\<beta\>*B<rsub|e>+<math-up|div><space|0.17em>Y<rsub|e><around|(|1|)>.
  </equation*>

  Thus for <math|X<rsub|e>=Z<rsub|e>-\<beta\>*B<rsub|e>> and
  <math|c<rsub|e>=<math-up|div><space|0.17em>Y<rsub|e><around|(|1|)>> the
  cabinet estimate proves <math|<around|\||X<rsub|e>-W<rsub|e>+c<rsub|e>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-p>>
  on the common healthy region. This supplies the healthy identity with its
  actual constant and sign. Applying the finite signed axial average kills
  <math|<around|(|c<rsub|e>|)>>, exactly as in the Crown assembly above.

  <subparagraph|Selecting and localizing the actual geometric current.> The
  current in this construction is selected from the inherited geometric
  fields on the same finite-volume history. The selection uses the four-jet
  bounds and the finite family of quadratic tests, not compactness of all
  smooth fields. At a fixed depth <math|k>, the inherited finite-height
  construction has error bounds of the form
  <math|2<rsup|-<around|(|r+1|)>>*d<rsub|j><around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)>>
  for jet orders <math|2\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>4>. The history used here is
  the one bound to its own transfers and doors by
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-selected-course-binding>. We
  now include the geometric purchases in that history's finite-stage ledger.
  Fix a stage profile, kernel choice, and required differentiation order. The
  local adjacent-field estimates give positive allowable analytic radius
  <math|a<rsub|\<ast\>>>, a carrier constant, and an early derivative
  constant <math|H<rsub|\<ast\>>>. Choose the integer block large enough for
  the inequalities below, which involve <math|H<rsub|\<ast\>>> only; the late
  derivative estimate then gives the finite constant <math|D<rsub|\<ast\>>>
  and radius <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>>>. With that block fixed, the coupling
  increment <math|v=\<gamma\>*log L> is fixed. Increasing the stage entry
  ensures

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|log t|)><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>min
    <around|(|b<rsub|\<ast\>>,a<rsub|\<ast\>>/2|)>,<space|1em><around|(|log
    <around|(|t+v|)>|)><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|\<ast\>>,<space|1em><frac|log
    <around|(|t+v|)>|log t>\<leqslant\>L,<space|1em><around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    t|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>\<geqslant\>R<rsub|\<ast\>>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>>,c<rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0> are the late derivative
  and canonical-top radii, and <math|R<rsub|\<ast\>>> is the finite geometric
  support requirement. The canonical path-field jet bill is also bounded by
  its logarithmic budget after increasing this same entry. Each inequality
  follows respectively from inverse-log decay, the log-ratio limit one, cover
  divergence, or a fixed jet bill divided by a positive log power. The bank
  inequalities below are paid at that entry as well. The maximum over the
  finitely many active and next-stage profiles is an admissible
  common-calendar purchase. Consequently every sufficiently late adjacent
  transition of this very history has the required geometric derivative,
  support and bank bounds. No separate history with better estimates is
  substituted.

  Here the inheritance is the geometric pullback through the actual selected
  sound door, followed by the fixed main, flux, leaf and trace averages; it
  is not the coefficient chart's half-accuracy map. Concretely a scalar
  component of engineering degree <math|d> is pulled back through the
  selected stationary interpolation and averaged with its specified finite
  sheaf, then multiplied by <math|L<rsup|d>>. The vector current uses the
  differentiated interpolation and factor <math|L<rsup|5>>. The stress uses
  the flux sheaf, the current and error use the main sheaf, and the auxiliary
  scalar uses the leaf sheaf plus the trace correction of the stress. The
  twist uses its leaf term and the two antisymmetric trace corrections. These
  are the exact discrete product-rule corrections: they preserve the trace,
  symmetry and divergence identities at every step. The interpolation, its
  derivative and the path entering these formulas are the ones of the actual
  selected transition, as bound in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-lattice-selected-course-binding>;
  there is no additional choice of a free interpolating field. Its
  finite-height estimates are obtained as follows. At the top finite height
  use the following explicit fields. Let <math|F<rsub|a*b><around|(|z|)>=<math-up|curv><around|(|U<around|(|\<square\><rsub|a*b><around|(|z|)>|)>|)>>
  be the oriented plaquette curvature, with the curvature map and color inner
  product of the fixed gauge group. Set

  <\equation*>
    J<rsup|0><rsub|a*b><around|(|z|)>=<frac|\<delta\><rsub|a*b>|4>*<big|sum><rsub|i,l><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|i*l><around|(|z|)>,F<rsub|i*l><around|(|z|)>|\<rangle\>>-<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|a*i><around|(|z|)>,F<rsub|b*i><around|(|z|)>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation*>

  The current <math|Y<rsup|0><rsub|b><around|(|z|)>> is supported on links
  starting at <math|z> and has color component
  <math|F<rsub|b*a><around|(|z|)>> on the link in direction <math|a>. Put
  <math|Q<rsup|0>=0>, <math|<math-up|twist><rsup|0>=0>, and
  <math|E<rsup|0><rsub|b>=<big|sum><rsub|a>\<nabla\><rsub|a>*J<rsup|0><rsub|a*b>-\<cal-A\><around|(|Y<rsup|0><rsub|b>|)>>,
  where <math|\<cal-A\>> is the actual path action's directional variation.
  Trace zero and symmetry of <math|J<rsup|0>> follow by summing its displayed
  formula and symmetry of the inner product; the divergence identity holds by
  the definition of <math|E<rsup|0>>. Gauge covariance follows from
  conjugation covariance of plaquette curvature. At the flat field
  <math|F<rsub|a*b>=0>, so the scalar fields have zero constant and first
  jets. Their four-jet bounds follow by differentiating the finite plaquette
  products and the fixed smooth curvature map, together with the actual
  path-action derivative bounds. The quadratic tests are obtained by
  replacing each plaquette curvature by its first derivative, the discrete
  curl. On the fixed polynomial test jets this curl agrees with the free
  curvature as soon as the period contains the test stencil without wrap;
  products give the stress quadratic form and pairing the current's linear
  curl with the test direction gives its mixed grade-five form. Thus these
  tests are eventually exact on the growing-period tail. Their weighted jets
  through order four have fixed bounded prices, their quadratic tests
  converge to the prescribed free forms as the period grows, and their
  geometric identities hold exactly. At each adjacent step apply the actual
  inherited derivative estimate, reserving one third of the next jet budget
  for each summand in the stress, auxiliary and twist formulas. If
  <math|A<rsub|\<ast\>>> is the fixed total averaging debt and
  <math|H<rsub|\<ast\>>> the derivative constant, choose the stage block
  <math|L> so that

  <\equation*>
    3*A<rsub|\<ast\>>*2<rsup|13>*H<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|2>/L\<less\>1/2,<space|2em>3*L<rsup|5>*A<rsub|\<ast\>>*<around|(|2*H<rsub|\<ast\>>/L<rsup|2>|)><rsup|s>\<less\>1/2*<space|1em><around|(|s=3,4|)>.
  </equation*>

  Such an integer block exists because the powers of <math|L> are
  <math|-1,-1,-3>. The second-jet principal contribution is bounded by
  <math|A<rsub|\<ast\>>*2<rsup|13>*H<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|2>*<around|(|C+<around|(|d<rsub|2><around|(|t<rprime|'>|)>+C|)>/L|)>>;
  dividing by <math|d<rsub|2><around|(|t|)>>, with
  <math|d<rsub|s><around|(|t|)>=<around|(|log t|)><rsup|s>> and
  <math|t<rprime|'>=t+\<gamma\>*log L>, its limit is
  <math|A<rsub|\<ast\>>*2<rsup|13>*H<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|2>/L\<less\>1/6>. For
  orders three and four the full majorant, before division by the coarse
  budget, is

  <\equation*>
    L<rsup|5>*A<rsub|\<ast\>>*<around*|[|<around|(|2*H<rsub|\<ast\>>/L<rsup|2>|)><rsup|s>*d<rsub|s><around|(|t<rprime|'>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|\<pi\>\<ne\><math-up|atomic>>D<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>>*d<rsub|<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>><around|(|t<rprime|'>|)>|]>.
  </equation*>

  The sum is over the finitely many non-atomic ordered derivative partitions,
  each with <math|<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>\<less\>s>; it is retained in the
  estimate. The leading quotient tends to
  <math|L<rsup|5>*A<rsub|\<ast\>>*<around|(|2*H<rsub|\<ast\>>/L<rsup|2>|)><rsup|s>\<less\>1/6>.
  Every other chain-rule term has strictly fewer than <math|s> derivative
  factors, so its quotient contains <math|<around|(|log t|)><rsup|r-s>> with
  <math|r\<less\>s> and tends to zero. Thus a late stage entry pays all three
  summands and halves the error budget at each step. The same exact pullback
  and averaging identities preserve the quadratic tests and the geometric
  field equations. Induction over the finite number of adjacent steps gives
  the displayed <math|2<rsup|-<around|(|r+1|)>>*d<rsub|j>> error bounds. Only
  after fixing that number do we pass to the large terminal-depth and
  large-period tail of the same history. These are the stage block and entry
  purchases already allowed by the common selected-history calendar.

  Given a positive error <math|\<epsilon\>>, first choose a finite <math|r>
  making these three bounds less than <math|\<epsilon\>>. The fixed-height
  convergence then gives, on the common <math|<around|(|N,p|)>> tail, the
  inherited fields with jet price at most
  <math|d<rsub|j><around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)>+1> and quadratic-test
  error at most <math|\<epsilon\>>. These statements hold uniformly over
  sites: otherwise choose a violating site at each bad volume, contradicting
  the same fixed-height estimate for that sequence of sites. There are only
  finitely many component and quadratic-test indices, so one tail suffices.
  Take <math|\<epsilon\>=<math-up|shock><around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)>>
  at this fixed <math|k>, and average over all translations on that same
  torus. The inherited field equations are linear, the price bounds convex,
  and the quadratic tests translate with their base point, so this average
  retains all bounds and gives the required exact symmetry of the geometric
  family. In particular the actual current has the common exponentially
  weighted four-jet price <math|<math-up|trophy><around|(|t|)>> at decay rate
  <math|a=<math-up|east><around|(|t|)>>.

  Localize this current at radius <math|r=<math-up|rim><around|(|t|)>> using
  the same smooth local extension as the other geometric fields. The
  localization estimate in the charge construction bounds its lost four-jet
  price by

  <\equation*>
    C<space|0.17em><math-up|momentPrice><rsub|P><around|(|a/4|)>*e<rsup|-a*r/4><math-up|trophy><around|(|t|)>.
  </equation*>

  The retained current is supported in the box of radius
  <math|4*<around|(|r+2|)>>. For any fixed derivative order <math|s> its
  composition derivative price is at most
  <math|P<rsub|s><around|(|r|)><math-up|trophy><around|(|t|)>>, with a fixed
  polynomial <math|P<rsub|s>>. To obtain this last statement, differentiate
  the local extension: at each derivative order there are finitely many
  product and chain-rule terms, each involving a bounded derivative of the
  fixed extension and at most a fixed power of the number of sites in the
  radius-<math|r> box. The four-jet coefficient price supplies the
  coefficients; the extension has only these four polynomial jets before
  composition. Thus neither this polynomial nor its constants depend on the
  ambient torus. The clock choices give

  <\equation*>
    C<math-up|momentPrice><rsub|P><around|(|a/4|)>*e<rsup|-a*r/4><math-up|trophy><around|(|t|)>\<leqslant\>t<rsup|-70>,<space|2em>P<rsub|s><around|(|r|)><math-up|trophy><around|(|t|)>\<leqslant\>\<cal-M\><around|(|t|)><rsup|1/200>
  </equation*>

  eventually, for each fixed <math|s>. The first is the exponential-tail
  purchase; the second follows by taking logarithms of the fixed polynomial
  bill against the monitor. These are the local current and derivative bounds
  used in the cutoff and finite-difference construction. All fixed orders
  required at an active stage are paid together before that stage is used.
  This fixes the current itself before the finite cabinet approximation and
  before the corrected-primary coefficients are chosen.

  The same localization is applied to the scalar geometric stress, its
  auxiliary scalar field and its first-difference fields. Their local
  extensions use the same cutoff; the finite algebraic identities between
  them are preserved by taking the prescribed shifted linear combinations.
  The product and chain-rule argument just given bounds each fixed-order jet
  by a polynomial in the localization radius times its inherited price.
  Passing to a first difference adds only finitely many translated copies and
  one fixed enlargement of the support. The geometric current, stress,
  auxiliary field, twist fields and divergence therefore have common
  fixed-order analytic bounds at most <math|\<cal-M\><around|(|t|)><rsup|1/50>>
  after increasing the stage entry. Their zero and first scalar jets are
  preserved by the local extension. The healthy quadratic Taylor conversion
  and the resulting reference-field price in the charge construction
  consequently apply to this complete family, including its first
  differences; they are not inferred from a bound on the current alone.

  <subsection|The local charged-configuration
  estimate><label|sec:ex-lattice-trace-local-charge>

  We isolate the finite-volume algebra that turns a charged configuration
  into a bounded field and a priced response. This is the local step used
  before the separation-limit passage. Its quantitative premises are stated
  as component bounds, so the proof does not hide the estimate inside a
  single named receipt.

  For <math|t\<gtr\>e>, put

  <\equation*>
    W<rsub|a><around|(|t|)>=exp <around*|(|a<around|(|log log
    t|)><rsup|2>|)>,<space|2em>c<around|(|t|)>=t<rsup|-30>.
  </equation*>

  Then <math|W<rsub|a><around|(|t|)>*W<rsub|b><around|(|t|)>=W<rsub|a+b><around|(|t|)>>,
  <math|W<rsub|-1><around|(|t|)>\<to\>0>, and <math|c<around|(|t|)>\<to\>0>
  as <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>>.

  Let <math|I> be a finite set of lattice directions, <math|Y> a
  finite-dimensional real normed coefficient space, and let
  <math|\<ell\>:Y<rsup|I>\<to\>\<bbb-R\>> be a continuous linear flux
  functional. Let <math|s:Y<rsup|I>\<to\><around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>> be a size
  function on coefficient arrays satisfying
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>s<around|(|A|)>>
  for every coefficient array <math|A>; this domination is the only property
  of <math|s> used below. Write <math|\<sigma\>=\<theta\><rsup|-2>> with
  <math|\<theta\>\<gtr\>0> for the local normalization. The nonlinear local
  coefficient map has already been evaluated in the arrays
  <math|B<rprime|'>,K<rprime|'>,D<rprime|'>\<in\>Y<rsup|I>>; these are
  outputs of that local construction, not images under the core linear
  projection. Let <math|X,W,E,L\<in\>Y<rsup|I>>. Assume the exact
  charged-configuration decomposition

  <\equation>
    X=W+B<rprime|'>-K<rprime|'>+D<rprime|'>+E+L,<label|eq:ex-lattice-charge-decomposition>
  </equation>

  and the following local coefficient and flux receipts at scale <math|t>:

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|s<around|(|X|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsup|2>*W<rsub|1/6><around|(|t|)>,>|<cell|>|<cell|>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-charge-size-input>>>|<row|<cell|\<ell\>*<around|(|\<sigma\>*K<rprime|'>|)>>|<cell|=0,>|<cell|\<ell\>*<around|(|\<sigma\>*B<rprime|'>|)>>|<cell|=h/12,>|<cell|<around|\||h-m|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>c<around|(|t|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-charge-price-identities>>>|<row|<cell|s<around|(|W|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsup|2>*W<rsub|-1><around|(|t|)>,>|<cell|s<around|(|D<rprime|'>|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsup|2>*W<rsub|-1><around|(|t|)>,>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-charge-error-one>>>|<row|<cell|s<around|(|E|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsup|2>*W<rsub|-1><around|(|t|)>,>|<cell|s<around|(|L|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsup|2>*W<rsub|-1><around|(|t|)>.>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number><label|eq:ex-lattice-charge-error-two>>>>>
  </align>

  Here <math|h> is the finite-volume charge extracted from the distinguished
  component, and <math|m> is its target price. These are coefficient-size
  receipts after the local map has been evaluated. No norm contraction of the
  nonlinear map is assumed.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Local field and flux bounds><label|prop:ex-lattice-charge-bounds>Under
    the displayed assumptions,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<sigma\>*X|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>W<rsub|1/6><around|(|t|)>,<space|2em><around*|\||\<ell\>*<around|(|\<sigma\>*X|)>-<frac|m|12>|\|>\<leqslant\>4<around|\<\|\|\>|\<ell\>|\<\|\|\>>*W<rsub|-1><around|(|t|)>+<frac|c<around|(|t|)>|12>.
    </equation*>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The field bound follows from the size domination and
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-charge-size-input>:

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<sigma\>*X|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>*s<around|(|X|)>=\<theta\><rsup|-2>*s<around|(|X|)>\<leqslant\>W<rsub|1/6><around|(|t|)>.
    </equation*>

    For the price, apply <math|\<ell\>> to the decomposition in
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-charge-decomposition>. The <math|K<rprime|'>> term
    vanishes and the <math|B<rprime|'>> term is <math|h/12>. Therefore

    <\equation*>
      \<ell\>*<around|(|\<sigma\>*X|)>-<frac|m|12>=\<ell\>*<around|(|\<sigma\>*W|)>+\<ell\>*<around|(|\<sigma\>*D<rprime|'>|)>+\<ell\>*<around|(|\<sigma\>*E|)>+\<ell\>*<around|(|\<sigma\>*L|)>+<frac|h-m|12>.
    </equation*>

    For any array <math|A>, continuity gives
    <math|<around|\||\<ell\>*<around|(|\<sigma\>*A|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|\<ell\>|\<\|\|\>>*\<sigma\><around|\<\|\|\>|A|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|\<ell\>|\<\|\|\>>*\<theta\><rsup|-2>*s<around|(|A|)>>.
    The triangle inequality and <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-charge-price-identities>\U
    <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-charge-error-two> give the claimed bound. There are
    four error terms, so the coefficient is exactly four; no direction-count
    factor is introduced because <math|s> bounds the full array norm.
  </proof>

  In the selected trace response, <math|m> is the finite-volume payment of
  the configuration under consideration; <math|m/12> is not exactly the
  limiting flux. The construction supplies in addition the accuracy bound

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||m+12*\<kappa\>|\|>\<leqslant\>t<rsup|-100>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<kappa\>=s<rsub|G>\<gtr\>0> is the slope constant
  <eqref|eq:shoot-slope>. Together with the proposition, this gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<ell\>*<around|(|\<sigma\>*X|)>+\<kappa\>|\|>\<leqslant\>4<around|\<\|\|\>|\<ell\>|\<\|\|\>>*W<rsub|-1><around|(|t|)>+<frac|t<rsup|-30>+t<rsup|-100>|12>,
  </equation*>

  and the right side tends to zero as <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>>. The
  proposition and this consequence concern the evaluated array
  <math|\<sigma\>*X> of one finite-volume configuration. The response whose
  size and flux are used in Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-lattice-trace-weighted-route>
  is instead the limit, along the separation ultrafilter at a fixed level, of
  transported finite-volume fields. Passing to it requires two further
  inputs, which do not follow from the proposition: a transport bound of size
  <math|W<rsub|-1/4><around|(|t|)>> for the difference between the
  transported field and the core projection of <math|\<sigma\>*X> (the core
  projection does not increase the norm and does not change the flux), and
  the comparison of the arch scale with <math|t>. With these, the
  finite-volume bounds take the form <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-finite-response-price>,
  continuity of the fixed linear maps transfers them to the ultrafilter
  limit, and division of the size bound by the arch scale gives a quantity
  tending to zero by <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-trace-age-over-arch>. That passage
  is carried out, conditionally on these inputs, in
  Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:ex-lattice-trace-weighted-route>. The
  component receipts themselves are separate finite-volume producer
  statements; this proposition does not prove them from the
  charged-configuration hypotheses.

  <subsubsection|Constructing the charged coefficient from its local
  pieces><label|subsec:trace-charge-construction>

  We give the finite construction which precedes the proposition. This also
  fixes which nonlinear projections occur in its six terms. All pieces are on
  the same finite-volume representation and use its threshold
  <math|\<theta\>>; write <math|H=W<rsub|1><around|(|t|)>> and put

  <\equation*>
    c<rsub|0>=\<theta\><rsup|2>*H<rsup|-2>,<space|2em>c<rsub|1>=\<theta\><rsup|2>*H<rsup|1/8>,<space|2em>\<epsilon\><rsub|0>=\<theta\><rsup|2>*t<rsup|-1/100>.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|\<Pi\><rsub|P>*f> denote the weighted local coefficient of a
  regular observable <math|f> on a support <math|P> containing the section
  footprint. Its readout constant <math|r\<geqslant\>1> and residual constant
  <math|a\<geqslant\>1> are fixed at the stage. If the section price is
  <math|p\<geqslant\>0>, set <math|K=<around|(|1+p|)>*r+a>.

  First, the elementary local coefficient is an actual observable. Set

  <\equation*>
    z=\<Pi\><rsub|P>*f,<space|2em>h=f-<math-up|present><rsub|\<theta\>><around|(|z|)>,<space|2em>Q<rsub|f>=<math-up|primary><rsub|\<theta\>><around|(|z|)>+<with|font-series|bold|1><rsub|<around|{|P<math-up|healthy>|}>>*h.
  </equation*>

  Here the primary includes its own footprint mask. On a configuration
  healthy on <math|P>, the two masks are one and <math|Q<rsub|f>=f>. The
  residual has zero local coefficient by construction. If the healthy
  derivative bound of <math|f> is <math|u\<geqslant\>0>, coefficient recovery
  and residual subtraction give

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||z|\|><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>\<leqslant\>r*u,<space|2em><around|\||h|\|><rsub|<math-up|healthy>>\<leqslant\>a*u.
  </equation*>

  Put <math|M<rsub|P><around|(|u|)>=e<rsup|\<alpha\><math-up|cov><around|(|P|)>>*u>,
  where <math|\<alpha\>> is the support exponent. The coefficient with
  coordinate <math|z>, one regular residual <math|h> on <math|P>, and zero
  forced part has coefficient-plus-remainder price at most

  <\equation*>
    r*u+M<rsub|P>*<around|(|a*u|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|r+a|)>*M<rsub|P><around|(|u|)>
  </equation*>

  and pointwise exponential-entry bound at most
  <math|<around|(|<around|(|1+p|)>*r+a|)>*u\<leqslant\>K*M<rsub|P><around|(|u|)>>.
  These inequalities use <math|<math-up|cov><around|(|P|)>\<geqslant\>0> and
  the nonnegative support exponent <math|\<alpha\>> (in the application
  <math|\<alpha\>\<geqslant\>4>), hence <math|M<rsub|P><around|(|u|)>\<geqslant\>u>.
  The construction is measurable, gauge invariant and depends only on the
  links and healthy flags on <math|P>: these facts hold for the local
  projection, its primary and the masked residual separately. Adding such
  coefficients adds both prices and their exponential entries exactly. Thus
  this is a construction of one coefficient, not a choice of its coordinate
  alone.

  For each direction <math|b>, let <math|f<rsub|b><rsup|0>,f<rsub|b><rsup|1>,f<rsub|b><rsup|2>,f<rsub|b><rsup|3>>
  be respectively the local residual, the distinguished current term, the
  truncated loop term and the Taylor remainder of the selected local
  decomposition. Their healthy derivative bounds on the common patch <math|P>
  are respectively <math|c<rsub|0>,c<rsub|1>,c<rsub|1>,c<rsub|0>>. Let
  <math|g<rsub|b,j>>, <math|j\<in\>I<rsub|b>>, be its finite additional
  terms, supported on <math|P<rsub|b,j>> in the same enclosing patch, with
  healthy bounds <math|u<rsub|b,j>> and

  <\equation>
    <big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>I<rsub|b>>M<rsub|P<rsub|b,j>><around|(|u<rsub|b,j>|)>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|0>.<label|eq:trace-charge-tail-price>
  </equation>

  Construct the elementary coefficient of
  <math|f<rsub|b><rsup|0>+f<rsub|b><rsup|1>-f<rsub|b><rsup|2>+f<rsub|b><rsup|3>>,
  with derivative bound <math|2*c<rsub|0>+2*c<rsub|1>>, and add those of the
  <math|g<rsub|b,j>>. Denote the resulting coefficient by <math|X<rsub|b>>.
  The healthy observable and its coordinate are exactly

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|Q<rsub|X<rsub|b>>>|<cell|=f<rsub|b><rsup|0>+f<rsub|b><rsup|1>-f<rsub|b><rsup|2>+f<rsub|b><rsup|3>+<big|sum><rsub|j>g<rsub|b,j>>|<cell|>|<cell|<text|on
    the common healthy patch>,>>|<row|<cell|X<rsub|b>.*x>|<cell|=\<Pi\><rsub|P>*f<rsub|b><rsup|0>+\<Pi\><rsub|P>*f<rsub|b><rsup|1>-\<Pi\><rsub|P>*f<rsub|b><rsup|2>+\<Pi\><rsub|P>*f<rsub|b><rsup|3>+<big|sum><rsub|j>\<Pi\><rsub|P<rsub|b,j>>*g<rsub|b,j>.>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
  </align*>

  The second equality uses linearity of the local Taylor projection on
  regular functions on the same patch; it makes no assertion of global
  linearity for different nonlinear local charts. Both prices are at most

  <\equation*>
    K*<around|{|M<rsub|P>*<around|(|2*c<rsub|0>+2*c<rsub|1>|)>+\<epsilon\><rsub|0>|}>.
  </equation*>

  The fixed patch cover bound gives <math|M<rsub|P><around|(|1|)>\<leqslant\>A>
  with <math|A=exp <around|(|\<alpha\><space|0.17em><math-up|TileNo>|)>>. For
  large <math|t>, <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|0>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|1>>
  and therefore

  <\equation*>
    K*<around|{|M<rsub|P>*<around|(|2*c<rsub|0>+2*c<rsub|1>|)>+\<epsilon\><rsub|0>|}>\<leqslant\><around|(|4*A+1|)>*K*\<theta\><rsup|2>*H<rsup|1/8>\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsup|2>*H<rsup|1/6>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed the last ratio is <math|<around|(|4*A+1|)>*K*H<rsup|-1/24>\<to\>0>,
  while <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|0>/c<rsub|0>=t<rsup|-1/100>*H<rsup|2>\<to\>0>.
  The sum of added coordinates has size at most
  <math|<around|(|r+a|)>*\<epsilon\><rsub|0>\<leqslant\>K*\<epsilon\><rsub|0>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|0>>.
  Also <math|r*c<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsup|2>*H<rsup|-1>> eventually.
  Thus the local residual, Taylor remainder, and added-coordinate terms have
  the prices asserted in <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-charge-error-one>\U
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-charge-error-two>. These are fixed-stage estimates. To
  use them along increasing stages, choose the threshold separately at each
  stage, simultaneously for the finitely many grades at most five and all its
  fixed readout constants. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-lattice-monotone-threshold>
  gives the monotone delay envelope. The selected paid schedule meets these
  thresholds before the period, volume and finite truncations are chosen;
  this is not an assertion that unbounded stage constants are controlled by
  one fixed-stage tail. The coefficient has zero forced part. Its density is
  consequently the background density times its own exponential entry. The
  zero paid forced response has attainment cost zero, hence at most <math|t>.
  This supplies the atomic certificate used in the Coffer application, with
  charge <math|\<theta\><rsup|2>*H<rsup|1/6>>.

  To obtain the six-term decomposition of the proposition, use the exact loop
  splitting

  <\equation*>
    f<rsub|b><rsup|2>=k<rsub|b>-d<rsub|b>
  </equation*>

  on this same patch, where <math|d<rsub|b>> has healthy derivative bound
  <math|c<rsub|0>>. Define

  <\equation*>
    W<rsub|b>=\<Pi\><rsub|P>*f<rsub|b><rsup|0>,<space|1em>B<rprime|'><rsub|b>=\<Pi\><rsub|P>*f<rsub|b><rsup|1>,<space|1em>K<rprime|'><rsub|b>=\<Pi\><rsub|P>*k<rsub|b>,<space|1em>D<rprime|'><rsub|b>=\<Pi\><rsub|P>*d<rsub|b>,<space|1em>E<rsub|b>=\<Pi\><rsub|P>*f<rsub|b><rsup|3>,<space|1em>L<rsub|b>=<big|sum><rsub|j>\<Pi\><rsub|P<rsub|b,j>>*g<rsub|b,j>.
  </equation*>

  Local projection of the splitting gives
  <math|X=W+B<rprime|'>-K<rprime|'>+D<rprime|'>+E+L>. The <math|D<rprime|'>>
  bound follows from <math|r*c<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsup|2>*H<rsup|-1>>
  as well. The coefficient charge bounds <math|<around|\||X<rsub|b>.*x|\|><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>>
  by <math|\<theta\><rsup|2>*H<rsup|1/6>>; taking the maximum over directions
  proves the full array size estimate, rather than summing that estimate four
  times.

  The two remaining flux statements have a different origin from these size
  bounds. The local radial reading identifies the normalized flux of
  <math|\<Pi\><rsub|P>*f> with one twelfth of the cubic current probe of
  <math|f>. Thus the loop cancellation and distinguished-current comparison
  needed here are

  <\equation>
    \<frak-H\><around|(|k|)>=0,<space|2em><around|\||\<frak-H\><around|(|f<rsup|1>|)>-m|\|>\<leqslant\>t<rsup|-30>,<space|2em>\<ell\>*<around|(|\<theta\><rsup|-2>*\<Pi\><rsub|P>*f|)>=\<frak-H\><around|(|f|)>/12.<label|eq:trace-charge-current-identities>
  </equation>

  They give exactly the two identities in
  <eqref|eq:ex-lattice-charge-price-identities>, with
  <math|h=\<frak-H\><around|(|f<rsup|1>|)>>. In particular the cancellation
  concerns <math|k>, not <math|f<rsup|2>>: the small loop correction <math|d>
  must remain among the four errors. A bound on <math|f<rsup|2>> alone would
  not prove the flux identity.

  The following subsections construct the remaining local prices and
  identities on these same fields: the corrected-loop cancellation and
  counterterm size, the actual nonlinear localization, the support-weighted
  tail, and the fifth-order residual and finite-difference remainder. They
  use the selected history's inherited action equation and local analytic
  probes. The exact finite tensor corrections are displayed below, so the
  trace and antisymmetric calibration identities are not additional
  assumptions. Once these constructions are combined, the coefficient and all
  its prices above are produced by the displayed finite sum.

  <subsubsection|Why the corrected loop has zero current
  price><label|subsec:trace-charge-loop-cancellation>

  The cancellation in <eqref|eq:trace-charge-current-identities> can be
  checked before estimating any norms. A quadratic loop word has two
  plaquette legs, at offsets <math|o<rsub|j,0>,o<rsub|j,1>>, and a color
  coefficient <math|c<rsub|j>>. All sums below can be taken separately for
  each pair of plaquette orientations and each pair of color indices. Put
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|j>=o<rsub|j,1>-o<rsub|j,0>>. The corrected finite loop
  family is chosen with

  <\equation>
    <big|sum><rsub|j>c<rsub|j>=0,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|j>c<rsub|j>*\<delta\><rsub|j>=0.<label|eq:trace-charge-loop-moments>
  </equation>

  These conditions have a concrete finite construction: subtract the local
  zeroth-moment word and the first-moment difference words from the original
  finite family, separately in each orientation/color component. A difference
  word with offsets <math|0,e<rsub|a>>, minus the word with offsets
  <math|0,0>, has zero zeroth moment and first moment <math|e<rsub|a>>.
  Taking its coefficient to be the <math|a>th component of the original first
  moment, and the local word's coefficient to be the original zeroth moment,
  cancels both moments exactly. The original loop equals the corrected family
  minus its added counterterms. The color coefficients of every added word
  are linear combinations of the original invariant color coefficients, so
  they remain invariant. These counterterms are the observable denoted
  <math|d> above; they cannot be discarded when estimating its size.

  Let <math|F> be the translated and signed-averaged sum of the corrected
  words. At the identity configuration every curvature factor is zero, so
  <math|F<around|(|0|)>=D*F<around|(|0|)>=0>. Write <math|d*A> for the linear
  plaquette curl of a link variation <math|A>. Differentiating the product of
  two curvature factors twice therefore gives, before signed averaging,

  <\equation*>
    D<rsup|2>*F<around|(|0|)><around|[|A,B|]>=<big|sum><rsub|j,z>c<rsub|j>*<around*|(|<around|(|d*A|)>*<around|(|z+o<rsub|j,0>|)>*<around|(|d*B|)>*<around|(|z+o<rsub|j,1>|)>+<around|(|d*B|)>*<around|(|z+o<rsub|j,0>|)>*<around|(|d*A|)>*<around|(|z+o<rsub|j,1>|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The displayed products retain their prescribed color contractions. Suppose
  <math|A> has bounded support and <math|d*B> is affine on the support
  enlarged by the word lengths. Translate the first leg to <math|z>. The
  inner sum of the first term is a multiple of

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|j>c<rsub|j>*<around|{|b<rsub|0>+L<around|(|z|)>+L<around|(|\<delta\><rsub|j>|)>|}>=<around|(|b<rsub|0>+L<around|(|z|)>|)>*<big|sum><rsub|j>c<rsub|j>+L<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|j>c<rsub|j>*\<delta\><rsub|j>|)>=0.
  </equation*>

  The other term has offsets <math|-\<delta\><rsub|j>> and vanishes for the
  same reason. Nonwrapping of the enlarged patch justifies these ordinary
  integer translations; it avoids replacing an affine function by a
  discontinuous periodic representative. Signed averaging preserves the
  conclusion, since it preserves support and the property of having affine
  curl. Thus

  <\equation>
    D<rsup|2>*F<around|(|0|)><around|[|A,B|]>=0<space|1em><text|if <math|A>
    is supported in the patch and <math|d*B> is affine
    there.><label|eq:trace-charge-loop-hessian>
  </equation>

  Now let <math|Y<rsub|b>> be the local gauge-covariant vector field of the
  chosen current, vanishing at the identity, and let
  <math|k<rsub|b>=D*F*<space|0.17em>Y<rsub|b>>. The support of
  <math|D*Y<rsub|b><around|(|0|)>*A> is contained in the same patch. Twice
  applying the product rule, using <math|D*F<around|(|0|)>=0> and
  <math|Y<rsub|b><around|(|0|)>=0>, gives

  <\equation*>
    D<rsup|2>*k<rsub|b><around|(|0|)><around|[|A,B|]>=D<rsup|2>*F<around|(|0|)><around|[|A,D*Y<rsub|b><around|(|0|)>*B|]>+D<rsup|2>*F<around|(|0|)><around|[|B,D*Y<rsub|b><around|(|0|)>*A|]>.
  </equation*>

  For the polynomial link probes of degrees zero, one and two entering the
  local cubic current test, their curls are affine. Each term consequently
  vanishes by <eqref|eq:trace-charge-loop-hessian> and symmetry of the
  Hessian. The constant-direction terms require a separate argument. The
  function <math|k<rsub|b>=D*F*<space|0.17em>Y<rsub|b>> is gauge invariant,
  since <math|F> is gauge invariant and <math|Y<rsub|b>> is gauge covariant.
  Moreover <math|k<rsub|b><around|(|0|)>=D*k<rsub|b><around|(|0|)>=0>.
  Differentiating gauge invariance once in a gauge parameter and once in an
  arbitrary variation therefore says that
  <math|D<rsup|2>*k<rsub|b><around|(|0|)>> annihilates every infinitesimal
  gauge direction. On the nonwrapping patch a constant link variation is such
  a direction: use the gauge parameter linear in the lattice coordinate and
  extend it outside the patch. This kills the <math|<around|(|0,3|)>> and
  <math|<around|(|3,0|)>> terms without claiming that a cubic probe has
  affine curl. The radial current reading is a finite linear combination of
  these Hessian probes: its cubic spatial coefficient is the polarization of
  the pairs of spatial degrees <math|<around|(|0,3|)>,<around|(|1,2|)>,<around|(|2,1|)>,<around|(|3,0|)>>,
  with the constant-curl terms removed by the gauge identity. The middle two
  pairs are exactly the quadratic link probes just treated. Hence every term
  of its current reading is zero and <math|\<frak-H\><around|(|k|)>=0>. This
  proof uses moment cancellation of the corrected loop, rather than smallness
  or a limiting Ward identity.

  <subsubsection|The exact reference-profile
  price><label|subsec:trace-reference-local-calibration>

  The stress probe and its divergence are read in grades four and five,
  respectively. Grade four is particularly useful here: its nonconstant
  invariant labels are quadratic in curvature, so the local reading is
  determined by its Hessian on constant-curvature radial jets. A translated
  finite difference has zero such reading. Indeed translation leaves these
  Hessian tests unchanged, and the difference of the two equal readings is
  zero. This statement concerns the local grade-four reading, not the
  observable itself.

  To make the same-object identities explicit, let <math|<wide|J|~>> be the
  componentwise localized tensor of the selected field, and let
  <math|Q<rsub|i>> and <math|A<rsub|a*b*i>> be its localized trace and
  antisymmetric auxiliary currents. Write <math|\<Delta\><rsub|i>> for the
  unit translated difference. The corrected local tensor <math|M> keeps
  <math|<wide|J|~><rsub|a*b>> for <math|a\<less\>b> and
  <math|<wide|J|~><rsub|a*a>> for <math|a\<ne\>0>, and is defined on the
  remaining entries by

  <\equation*>
    M<rsub|b*a>=M<rsub|a*b>-<big|sum><rsub|i>\<Delta\><rsub|i>*A<rsub|a*b*i>*<space|1em><around|(|a\<less\>b|)>,<space|2em>M<rsub|00>=<big|sum><rsub|i>\<Delta\><rsub|i>*Q<rsub|i>-<big|sum><rsub|i\<ne\>0>M<rsub|i*i>.
  </equation*>

  Consequently, before any projection or averaging,

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|i>M<rsub|i*i>=<big|sum><rsub|i>\<Delta\><rsub|i>*Q<rsub|i>,<space|2em>M<rsub|a*b>-M<rsub|b*a>=<big|sum><rsub|i>\<Delta\><rsub|i>*A<rsub|a*b*i>*<space|1em><around|(|a\<less\>b|)>.
  </equation*>

  All these auxiliary fields and their translated differences use the same
  finite nonwrapping support and the same healthy regular construction. Thus
  linearity of the weighted local projection applies to these exact
  identities. Applying the preceding zero-reading observation proves that its
  grade-four reading is symmetric and trace free. Signed averaging preserves
  both statements: each diagonal receives the square of the sign, and
  permutations only reorder the trace sum. The grade-four classification in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ex-trace-free-stress-basis> therefore writes the
  averaged reading uniquely as the three-parameter nonconstant stress array
  <math|<math-ss|S><around|(|a|)>>, <math|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|3>>. Reading
  the local divergence commutes with taking the grade-five derivative: this
  follows by differentiating the finite radial Taylor polynomial and
  comparing each jet coefficient. Consequently its vector reading is the same
  <math|V*a> used in <eqref|eq:trace-weighted-calibration-price>; it is not
  independently calibrated.

  The trace and vector price in these coordinates are

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|b><math-ss|S><around|(|a|)><rsub|b*b>=4*<around|(|a<rsub|0>+4*a<rsub|1>|)>*\<cal-O\>,<space|2em>\<rho\>*<around|(|V*a|)>=-4*<around|(|a<rsub|0>+4*a<rsub|1>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|\<cal-O\>\<ne\>0> for a nontrivial simple gauge group, the zero
  trace gives <math|a<rsub|0>+4*a<rsub|1>=0>, hence
  <math|\<rho\>*<around|(|V*a|)>=0> exactly at finite volume. For the two
  elementary correction tensors the local reading has the fixed triples
  <math|a<rsup|0>,a<rsup|1>>, so their prices equal
  <math|\<rho\>*<around|(|V*a<rsup|P>|)>> exactly. This proves the exact
  price part of <eqref|eq:trace-weighted-calibration-price> without a
  quantitative limit for the stress coefficients.

  For the qualitative stress calibration, let <math|J<rsub|b*c>> be the
  unmodified stress probe and <math|<wide|J|~><rsub|b*c>> its local version.
  On each fixed constant-curvature test pair the selected local estimates
  give

  <\equation*>
    <mid|\|>D<rsup|2>*J<rsub|b*c><around|(|0|)><around|[|u,v|]>-D<rsup|2>*<around|(|-<math-ss|S><rsub|<math-up|Maxwell>>|)><rsub|b*c><around|(|0|)><around|[|u,v|]><mid|\|>\<leqslant\>t<rsup|-100>,<space|2em><mid|\|>D<rsup|2>*<around|(|J<rsub|b*c>-<wide|J|~><rsub|b*c>|)><around|(|0|)><around|[|u,v|]><mid|\|>\<leqslant\>16*t<rsup|-30>.
  </equation*>

  The factor <math|16> is the product of the two factor-four link bounds for
  a constant-curvature radial test. There are finitely many tests determining
  the grade-four frame: evaluation on all radial jets is injective, and in
  finite dimension a finite subset already has zero common kernel. Their
  inverse on its image has finite operator norm. The triangle inequality
  therefore gives convergence of the entire local coefficient to the negative
  Maxwell coefficient. Applying the continuous inverse of
  <math|a\<mapsto\><math-ss|S><around|(|a|)>> gives
  <math|a\<to\><around|(|-1,1/4,-1|)>>, uniformly on any sets on which these
  two test bounds hold. This is precisely the qualitative reference limit
  used in the weighted estimate; its weighted price uses the exact zero above
  and does not multiply this qualitative error by <math|A<rsub|1/5>>.

  <subsubsection|The localization error in the current
  comparison><label|subsec:trace-charge-localization>

  The current comparison uses a shrinking analytic width, so its loss cannot
  be treated as a fixed constant. At scale <math|t>, the width is
  <math|a=<around|(|log t|)><rsup|-1>> and the localization radius is
  <math|R=1000<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log t|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>. Here
  is the nonlinear localization estimate for the actual localizer. Write
  <math|F> for the scalar reading of a gauge-invariant local field,
  <math|J<rsub|4>*F> for its Taylor polynomial of degree four,
  <math|S<rsub|R>> for restriction of link coordinates to distance at most
  <math|R>, and <math|W> for the degree-four local gauge-coordinate map.
  These readings are analytic at the zero field and satisfy
  <math|F=F\<circ\><math-up|exactP><rsub|o>> as germs there: this is the
  local gauge reconstruction identity of the constructed invariant field. The
  Taylor polynomial of that reconstruction is <math|W>. Thus the defining
  coordinate identity gives equality of four-jets

  <\equation*>
    F-<around|(|J<rsub|4>*F\<circ\>S<rsub|R>|)>\<circ\>W\<equiv\><rsub|4><around|(|J<rsub|4>*F-J<rsub|4>*F\<circ\>S<rsub|R>|)>\<circ\>W.
  </equation*>

  This is an identity of jets, not an assertion of equality of the full
  nonlinear fields. In a coordinate derivative indexed by links
  <math|<around|(|e<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,e<rsub|j>|)>>, <math|j\<leqslant\>4>,
  the jet in parentheses is zero if <math|max<rsub|i>
  d*<around|(|o,e<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>R>, and otherwise is the original
  derivative of <math|F>. Thus every retained term has at least one input
  outside the radius.

  For completeness, the local coordinate map has derivative row sums, through
  order four and on a radius-<math|g> set, bounded by
  <math|B*<around|(|1+g|)><rsup|L>*exp <around|(|4*<around|(|a/100|)>*<around|(|g+2|)>|)>>,
  with <math|B,L> depending only on the gauge group and derivative order. To
  see the volume independence of these bounds, a coordinate at distance
  <math|g> depends only on coordinates within radius
  <math|4*<around|(|g+2|)>>. There are at most
  <math|2*<around|(|2\<cdot\>4*<around|(|g+2|)>+1|)><rsup|4>*dim
  <around|(|<math-up|Loc>|)>> such coordinates. Its derivatives through order
  four have a polynomial bound in <math|g> by the finite product rule for the
  local gauge-coordinate map. Multiplying that bound by the fourth power of
  this coordinate count and by the polynomial spatial weight gives
  <math|B*<around|(|1+g|)><rsup|L>>; the exponential weight contributes the
  displayed exponential. Thus the count concerns only this finite
  neighborhood. The chain rule is a sum over the ordered partitions of
  <math|j\<leqslant\>4>. For each partition, there are at most four such row
  factors. Using <math|g\<gtr\>R>, their product, together with the output
  weight of width <math|a/100>, is bounded by the original input weight of
  width <math|a>, times

  <\equation*>
    B<rsup|4>*e<rsup|100><math-up|momentPrice><around|(|4*L,a/4|)>*e<rsup|-a*R/4>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed <math|<around|(|1+g|)><rsup|4*L>\<leqslant\><math-up|momentPrice><around|(|4*L,a/4|)>*e<rsup|a*g/4>>,
  and the remaining exponent satisfies <math|<around|(|a/100|)>*g+16*<around|(|a/100|)>*<around|(|g+2|)>+a*g/4\<leqslant\>a*g+100-a*R/4>
  for <math|0\<less\>a\<leqslant\>1>. In the following display
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|a,\<leqslant\>4>> denotes
  the maximum of the exponentially weighted coordinate-derivative sums <em|at
  zero>, through order four, with the degree-four polynomial spatial weight.
  It is not a supremum of derivatives over the healthy domain. Summing over
  the finitely many ordered partitions gives a constant <math|D> and an
  integer <math|P>, independent of the volume, such that

  <\equation>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|F-F<rsup|R>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|a/100,<space|0.17em>\<leqslant\>4>\<leqslant\>D<math-up|momentPrice><around|(|P,a/4|)>*e<rsup|-a*R/4><around|\<\|\|\>|F|\<\|\|\>><rsub|a,<space|0.17em>\<leqslant\>4>.<label|eq:trace-charge-nonlinear-localization>
  </equation>

  The scalar readings of the actual vector and tensor localizers are exactly
  <math|<around|(|J<rsub|4>*F\<circ\>S<rsub|R>|)>\<circ\>W>: this follows by
  substituting their linkwise polynomial definition and contracting the link
  index. Their zero and first derivatives vanish; thus the weighted estimate
  for orders two through four supplies all the input derivatives in the
  calculation. This proves the estimate for those localizers, without
  promoting a scalar convolution estimate to a nonlinear one.

  The selected input price is bounded by <math|C*H<rsup|q>> for a fixed
  <math|q>. The moment price above is a fixed polynomial in
  <math|a<rsup|-1>>. Since <math|a*R/4\<geqslant\>250*log t>, the right side
  of <eqref|eq:trace-charge-nonlinear-localization> is
  <math|t<rsup|-250+o<around|(|1|)>>>, and is eventually at most
  <math|t<rsup|-98>>. Only orders at most four are used here; they suffice
  for the second-derivative current test and its one divergence derivative.

  To make the current test estimate explicit, write <math|Y<rsub|b>> for the
  original local vector field and <math|Y<rsub|b><rsup|R>> for its localized
  field. The divergence operator defining the current has one more derivative
  and a finite weighted link sum. Applying the product rule and the preceding
  tail bound, with the analytic width decreased to <math|a/100>, gives an
  error at most <math|t<rsup|-70>> in the weighted derivative norm needed for
  this operator. The spare exponent absorbs all fixed derivative orders and
  polynomial support factors. The current probe is a fixed finite sum of
  spatial moments of its second derivatives. If
  <math|c=a/<around|(|100\<cdot\>5\<cdot\>4\<cdot\>5|)>>, the sums of the
  moment weights of degrees two and three are bounded by
  <math|C*<around|(|c<rsup|-1>+1|)><rsup|m>> for a fixed <math|m>: sum the
  four-dimensional geometric series and differentiate it at most three times.
  Consequently

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<frak-H\><around|(|<math-up|div><space|0.17em>Y|)>-\<frak-H\><around|(|<math-up|div><space|0.17em>Y<rsup|R>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|1+log
    t|)><rsup|m>*t<rsup|-70>\<leqslant\>t<rsup|-30>
  </equation*>

  for all sufficiently large <math|t>. Subtracting the constant value of
  <math|<math-up|div><space|0.17em>Y<rsup|R>> does not change this test,
  which uses its second derivatives. This proves the distinguished-current
  error in <eqref|eq:trace-charge-current-identities> for the localized
  field. The same argument with the two constant-curvature directions gives
  the <math|16*t<rsup|-30>> local stress-probe error used above. The other
  stress-probe error, <math|t<rsup|-100>>, is the chosen accuracy of the
  original finite-volume probe; the two bounds concern different comparisons.

  <subsubsection|Paying the full reference
  observable><label|subsec:trace-reference-atomic-certificate>

  The reference profiles require their full observable, including its value
  on unhealthy configurations. They therefore use the following guarded
  construction rather than the zero-forced coefficient above. Let <math|f> be
  one of the reference tensor or divergence probes on <math|P>, with global
  bound <math|b=H<rsup|1/50>> and healthy derivative bound
  <math|c=\<theta\><rsup|2>*H<rsup|1/8>>. These are the local regularity
  prices for this profile. Set <math|z=\<Pi\><rsub|P>*f> and let
  <math|h=f-<math-up|present><rsub|\<theta\>><space|0.17em>z> be its
  zero-reading residual. The primary's footprint mask differs from the mask
  on <math|P>; keep this difference by defining

  <\equation*>
    q=f-<math-up|primary><rsub|\<theta\>><space|0.17em>z-<with|font-series|bold|1><rsub|<around|{|P<math-up|healthy>|}>>*h.
  </equation*>

  Then the equality <math|f=<math-up|primary><rsub|\<theta\>><space|0.17em>z+<with|font-series|bold|1><rsub|<around|{|P<math-up|healthy>|}>>*h+q>
  holds on every configuration. Here <math|a=1+<around|(|1+<math-up|readable>|)>*r>
  is the canonical atomic price, so the local residual construction bounds
  <math|h> by <math|a*c> on the healthy set and bounds the footprint-mask
  discrepancy by <math|a*c> globally. Hence <math|q> is zero on the healthy
  set and <math|<around|\||q|\|>\<leqslant\>b+a*c> everywhere. It is
  measurable, invariant, supported on <math|P> and depends only on its links
  and flags, so <math|q> is an admissible supported forced response. Its
  attainment price is

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\>+2|)><math-up|cov><around|(|P|)>>*<around|(|b+a*c|)>.
  </equation*>

  The retained forced-activity expansion installs this supported response in
  the same background density. To recall its quantitative content, mark one
  copy of <math|q> on <math|P>, expand the background activities and collect
  each nonempty subset meeting <math|P> into a supported forced slot,
  grouping by its union with <math|P>. Untouched activities remain outside
  this slot. There is no empty-slot contribution: <math|q> vanishes when
  <math|P> is healthy. If the rooted forced budget is <math|v>, their total
  absolute price is bounded by

  <\equation*>
    M<rsub|P>*<around|(|b+a*c|)>*<around|(|e<rsup|<around|\||P|\|>*v>-1|)>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed for <math|j> selected factors the rooted product sum is at most
  <math|<around|(|<around|\||P|\|>*v|)><rsup|j>/j>!; sum over
  <math|j\<geqslant\>1>. This is the same supported forcing expansion used by
  the atomic input of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-trace-coffer-supplier>;
  all terms retain the same marked origin and its flags. Its exact product
  expansion makes the resulting coefficient density equal to
  <math|<math-up|eval><space|0.17em>f>, not just equal on the healthy set.
  For <math|<around|\||P|\|>*v\<leqslant\>1>,
  <math|e<rsup|<around|\||P|\|>*v>-1\<leqslant\>2<around|\||P|\|>*v>. In the
  remainder norm with tolerance <math|<sqrt|v>> the resulting price is
  consequently at most <math|2<around|\||P|\|><sqrt|v>*<space|0.17em>M<rsub|P>*<around|(|b+a*c|)>>.
  Together with the ordinary residual and the coefficient this gives

  <\equation>
    <around|\||z|\|><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>+<math-up|rem>\<leqslant\>r*c+M<rsub|P>*<around|(|a*c|)>+2<around|\||P|\|><sqrt|v>*<space|0.17em>M<rsub|P>*<around|(|b+a*c|)>.<label|eq:trace-reference-guarded-price>
  </equation>

  This explains both the reserved support exponent <math|\<alpha\>+2> in the
  attainment price and the extra masked-remainder term; dropping either would
  not give the reference atomic certificate.

  Here the actual forced budget is <math|v=exp <around|{|-exp
  <around|[|<around|(|log log <around|(|t<rsup|1/5>|)>|)><rsup|2>|]>|}>>,
  <math|<around|\||P|\|>=O<around|(|<around|(|log t|)><rsup|8>|)>> and
  <math|<math-up|cov><around|(|P|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|TileNo>>. The last
  term of <eqref|eq:trace-reference-guarded-price> is smaller than every
  negative power of <math|t>: take logarithms and use <math|exp
  <around|[|<around|(|log log <around|(|t<rsup|1/5>|)>|)><rsup|2>|]>/log
  t\<to\>\<infty\>>. It is eventually at most <math|\<theta\><rsup|2>>. The
  other terms are at most <math|<around|(|r+A*a|)>*\<theta\><rsup|2>*H<rsup|1/8>>,
  so the whole price is at most <math|\<theta\><rsup|2>*H<rsup|1/6>> after
  the stage delay has been paid. Similarly

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\>+2|)><math-up|TileNo>>*<around|(|H<rsup|1/50>+a*\<theta\><rsup|2>*H<rsup|1/8>|)>\<leqslant\>t
  </equation*>

  eventually. Thus each reference profile has the atomic charge and
  attainment price used in <eqref|eq:trace-weighted-reference-bias>, with its
  original full observable and the same weighted local coefficient. Taking
  the maximum of the finitely many thresholds covers all three profiles and
  their tensor and divergence entries.

  <subsubsection|The weighted nonempty-support
  tail><label|subsec:trace-charge-support-tail>

  The added supports in <eqref|eq:trace-charge-tail-price> are the nonempty
  subsets of the activity increments in the finite shifted product. Here is
  the estimate which keeps their support weights intact. Fix a marked link,
  let <math|Q> be the core support, and let <math|X<rsub|i>> be the supports
  of its activity increments <math|f<rsub|i>>, all containing the marked
  site. Write <math|p<rsub|i>> for their derivative prices and <math|A> for
  the price of the unshifted local factor. If the derivative order is
  <math|k>, set <math|c<rsub|k>=2<rsup|k><math-up|budC><around|(|k|)>>. The
  product rule and the derivative series for <math|e<rsup|f<rsub|i>>-1> give
  the price

  <\equation*>
    A*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I>c<rsub|k>*p<rsub|i>
  </equation*>

  for the term with nonempty index set <math|I>. The <math|2<rsup|k>> factor
  pays the product rule; <math|<math-up|budC><around|(|k|)>*p<rsub|i>> pays
  the exponential increment when <math|p<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1>. Its support
  is <math|Q\<cup\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>X<rsub|i>>. Since these supports
  share their marked site, their covering numbers satisfy

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|cov><around|(|Q\<cup\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>X<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|cov><around|(|Q|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I><math-up|cov><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>.
  </equation*>

  If <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>M<rsub|X<rsub|i>><around|(|p<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>>
  and <math|c<rsub|k>*\<delta\>\<leqslant\>1>, positivity and the exact
  finite-product expansion therefore give

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|\<varnothing\>\<ne\>I>M<rsub|Q\<cup\>\<cup\><rsub|i\<in\>I>X<rsub|i>>*<around|(|A*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I>c<rsub|k>*p<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>M<rsub|Q><around|(|A|)>*<around*|[|<big|prod><rsub|i><around|(|1+c<rsub|k>*M<rsub|X<rsub|i>><around|(|p<rsub|i>|)>|)>-1|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>M<rsub|Q><around|(|A|)>*<around|(|e<rsup|c<rsub|k>*\<delta\>>-1|)>\<leqslant\>2*c<rsub|k>*\<delta\>*M<rsub|Q><around|(|A|)>.>>>>
  </align*>

  This also proves regularity term by term: the increments and the local
  factor are regular, and the finite product is regular on the displayed
  union. No unweighted cardinality estimate is substituted for this sum.

  For the actual shifted activity family, the individual prices are the sum
  of the exterior-path increment price and the local regular-activity
  increment price. Summing them gives

  <\equation*>
    \<delta\>\<leqslant\><math-up|beamAmt>+<math-up|vat><space|0.17em><around|(|2*r+1|)><rsup|4><math-up|hue><around|(|t|)>\<leqslant\>t<rsup|-109/100>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|r> is the retained truncation radius. The exterior contribution
  contains <math|e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*R/4>>, with fixed <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0>
  and <math|R=1000<around|\<lceil\>|log<rsup|2> t|\<rceil\>>>, times fixed
  powers of <math|t> and logarithms, so is smaller than every negative power
  of <math|t>. The regular contribution is bounded by
  <math|t<rsup|-11/10+o<around|(|1|)>>>: the radius factors are subpolynomial
  under the retained tower bound, and its activity receipt obeys
  <math|<math-up|hue><around|(|t|)>\<leqslant\>t<rsup|-11/10+o<around|(|1|)>>>.
  Thus both terms are bounded together by <math|t<rsup|-109/100>> after
  increasing the stage threshold. The local shifted factor, after the fixed
  derivative composition, has price <math|A\<leqslant\>t<rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>>.
  Its support has at most <math|<math-up|TileNo>> covering blocks. These
  facts give the per-link, per-difference-node nonempty price

  <\equation*>
    S<around|(|t|)>\<leqslant\>2*c<rsub|k>*t<rsup|-109/100>*e<rsup|\<alpha\><math-up|TileNo>>*t<rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>.
  </equation*>

  There are <math|t<rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>> marked links, the fixed
  finite-difference weights have total absolute value
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>m><around|\||w<rsub|j>|\|>>, and the inverse
  difference step is bounded by <math|t<rsup|1/2+1/24+o<around|(|1|)>>>.
  Consequently the <em|actual> support sum, including the difference weights
  and normalization, is bounded by

  <\equation*>
    t<rsup|-109/100-1/2+1/2+1/24+o<around|(|1|)>>=t<rsup|-629/600+o<around|(|1|)>>\<leqslant\>t<rsup|-101/100>\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsup|2>*t<rsup|-1/100>.
  </equation*>

  The last inequality is the retained threshold bound
  <math|t<rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsup|2>>. The strict gap between
  <math|629/600> and <math|101/100> absorbs the fixed stage constants. This
  proves <eqref|eq:trace-charge-tail-price> for the finite activity family,
  uniformly in its finite truncations, with no additional factor
  <math|H<rsup|2>>.

  <subsubsection|From the constructed probes to healthy derivative
  prices><label|subsec:trace-charge-healthy-prices>

  The local stress, its divergence, and the elementary correction probes are
  the entries and first spatial differences of the same localized polynomial
  field: the localized stress (<with|font-family|tt|Meld>), the two fixed
  quadratic probes (<with|font-family|tt|Plint>), the trace current
  (<with|font-family|tt|pluck>) and the antisymmetric current
  (<with|font-family|tt|pluckD>). This includes their tensor,
  single-derivative and divergence entries through grade five. Their local
  analytic construction gives derivative bounds
  <math|A\<leqslant\>H<rsup|1/50>> on translated link-coordinate balls of the
  fixed collar radius <math|B\<geqslant\>1>. Each probe is gauge invariant,
  local, and has zero constant and linear Taylor coefficients at the flat
  field. The last property must be retained in passing to a healthy chart: an
  arbitrary bounded local observable would not have the factor
  <math|\<theta\><rsup|2>>.

  The healthy axial chart has size <math|s=\<theta\>*<around|(|1+r|)><rsup|p>>,
  with <math|r> the support radius and <math|p> the fixed stage power. On
  this chart use the weighted derivative norm in which a <math|j>th
  derivative is multiplied by <math|s<rsup|j>>. Taylor's formula twice gives
  <math|<around|\||f*<around|(|s*u|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|B>*A*s<rsup|2>>,
  and once gives <math|s*<around|\<\|\|\>|D*f*<around|(|s*u|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|B>*A*s<rsup|2>>.
  For <math|j\<geqslant\>2>, the original bound gives
  <math|s<rsup|j>*<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|j>*f*<around|(|s*u|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>A*s<rsup|2>>
  since <math|s\<leqslant\>1>. Thus the entire weighted norm through the
  retained order is at most <math|C<rsub|B>*A*s<rsup|2>>. Pullback through
  the healthy collar chart, by the finite chain rule, costs at most
  <math|k!<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|k>>. It follows that the healthy derivative
  price is

  <\equation*>
    k!<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|k>*C<rsub|B>*A*\<theta\><rsup|2>*<around|(|1+r|)><rsup|2*p>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|r=O<around|(|log<rsup|2> t|)>> the last polynomial is
  <math|H<rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>>. Since <math|1/50\<less\>1/8>, the displayed
  expression is at most <math|\<theta\><rsup|2>*H<rsup|1/8>>. At the origin
  of each translated coordinate chart, the original zeroth-order bound also
  gives the global estimate <math|<around|\||f<around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>A\<leqslant\>H<rsup|1/50>>.
  This proves the two distinct prices used in
  Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:trace-reference-atomic-certificate>
  from the local probe bounds, on the original full observable.

  For the distinguished divergence subtract its value at the flat field. Its
  first jet vanishes by global conjugation invariance and simplicity of the
  gauge Lie algebra: restricting that derivative to any one link gives an
  adjoint-invariant linear functional, which vanishes on all commutators,
  hence on the entire simple Lie algebra. The link-coordinate derivatives
  therefore all vanish. Subtracting the constant supplies the zero value, and
  does not alter this first-derivative argument. Its derivative price before
  the subtraction is <math|H<rsup|1/200>>, and the subtracted constant has
  absolute value at most this same number. Hence
  <math|A\<leqslant\>2*H<rsup|1/200>> suffices, and the same argument again
  gives the healthy price <math|\<theta\><rsup|2>*H<rsup|1/8>>. For the small
  loop counterterm the corresponding analytic price is
  <math|<math-up|calorie><around|(|t|)>>; its decay is faster than the fixed
  <math|H<rsup|-2>> price after the same polynomial chart factors. The
  healthy counterterm price is consequently
  <math|\<theta\><rsup|2>*H<rsup|-2>>. Finally, if a residual or Taylor error
  has already been bounded in the axial chart by
  <math|\<theta\><rsup|2>*t<rsup|-1/100>>, the finite pullback factor gives

  <\equation*>
    k!<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|k>*\<theta\><rsup|2>*t<rsup|-1/100>\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsup|2>*H<rsup|-2>.
  </equation*>

  All constants here belong to the selected finite stage and are paid by the
  same prior stage-delay envelope as the atomic construction.

  <subsubsection|Size of the moment counterterms><label|subsec:trace-charge-counterterm-size>

  The zero and first moments used to correct the finite loop family are small
  for a reason independent of their exact cancellation. The infinite loop
  family of the selected retained path has exactly zero zeroth and first
  moments, by its path law. Restricting that same family to the radius
  <math|R> loses at most

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*R/4><math-up|momentPrice><around|(|1,\<sigma\>/2|)><math-up|humble><around|(|t|)>
  </equation*>

  in the combined zeroth- and first-moment norm: insert the factor distance
  into the absolutely summable loop coefficients and spend half of the
  exponential decay to pay it. For each fixed <math|t>, finite truncation of
  that summable family can be chosen so that its additional moment error is
  at most <math|t<rsup|-100>>. The combined moment error is therefore bounded
  by <math|t<rsup|-98>> for all sufficiently large <math|t>, uniformly after
  this finite truncation threshold.

  The explicit correction in Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:trace-charge-loop-cancellation>
  depends linearly on these finitely many moments. Its words have length at
  most ten and fixed offsets <math|0,e<rsub|a>>. Differentiating any such
  word a fixed number of times costs a fixed constant; translating it over
  the support costs at most <math|C*<around|(|1+<math-up|puff><around|(|t|)>|)><rsup|4>>.
  Hence its analytic derivative price is at most <math|t<rsup|-95>>, after
  enlarging the stage threshold. Applying the localized vector field in the
  directional derivative adds only the fixed product-rule constant and its
  subpolynomial derivative price. The resulting counterterm <math|d<rsub|b>>
  has analytic price at most <math|t<rsup|-94>>, and in particular at most
  <math|<math-up|calorie><around|(|t|)>=t<rsup|-30>>.
  Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:trace-charge-healthy-prices> now gives
  its healthy price <math|\<theta\><rsup|2>*H<rsup|-2>>. This establishes
  smallness of the same finite counterterms which enforce the exact moments;
  no second family is chosen for the size bound.

  <subsubsection|The fifth-order residual and finite-difference
  remainder><label|subsec:trace-charge-taylor-remainders>

  Let <math|s=\<theta\>*<around|(|1+r|)><rsup|p>> be the same axial-chart
  scale. The action residual is the actual difference

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|b>=<math-up|div><space|0.17em>M<rsub|b>-D*F<rsub|<math-up|main>><around|[|Y<rsub|b><rsup|R>|]>,<space|2em>f<rsub|b><rsup|0>=\<beta\>*K<rsub|b>.
  </equation*>

  Here the small four-jet input follows from the inherited action identity,
  not from locality alone. Let <math|J,Y,F> be the original stress, current
  and full main interaction, and let <math|F<rsub|L>> be the same main
  interaction restricted to the retained finite family and radius. In this
  paragraph <math|<math-up|div><space|0.17em>M> is the spatial
  finite-difference divergence of the stress, as opposed to the field-space
  divergence of <math|Y> in the current-price calculation. Then exactly

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|div><space|0.17em>M-D*F<rsub|L><around|[|Y<rsup|R>|]>=<around|(|<math-up|div><space|0.17em>J-D*F<around|[|Y|]>|)>+<math-up|div><around|(|M-J|)>+D*F*<around|[|Y-Y<rsup|R>|]>+D*<around|(|F-F<rsub|L>|)><around|[|Y<rsup|R>|]>.
  </equation*>

  The first summand has weighted four-jet price at most <math|t<rsup|-100>>
  by the inherited action equation of the selected field. The second has
  price at most <math|t<rsup|-70>> times a fixed polynomial in
  <math|a<rsup|-1>> by the localization estimate. For the third, the weighted
  product rule pairs the localized-current tail <math|t<rsup|-70>> with the
  full action's link derivative price through four further field derivatives
  (thus through five derivatives of the scalar action), bounded by a fixed
  power of <math|H> and <math|a<rsup|-1>>. The retained action family has
  exactly this bound; its four-derivative bound for the scalar action alone
  would not suffice. Finally absolute summability of the action family
  permits its finite truncation error to be at most <math|t<rsup|-100>>, and
  the radius restriction costs at most <math|C<math-up|momentPrice><around|(|P,\<sigma\>/2|)>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*R/4>>
  times the same fixed support price. Multiplying this by the
  original-current price plus its <math|t<rsup|-70>> tail bounds the last
  summand. All spatial contractions here are exponentially weighted link
  sums; their moment losses are polynomials in <math|a<rsup|-1>>, independent
  of total volume. The sum is <math|t<rsup|-70+o<around|(|1|)>>+t<rsup|-100+o<around|(|1|)>>>,
  hence at most <math|t<rsup|-30>> eventually. This proves the claimed origin
  four-jet bound for the exact <math|K<rsub|b>> above. Meanwhile the local
  analytic construction bounds all derivatives through the required fixed
  order by <math|C*H<rsup|q<rsub|0>>> for fixed <math|C,q<rsub|0>> on the
  radius-<math|B*s> ball. Both statements concern the same finite main
  interaction: its finite truncation is chosen after the local four-jet
  comparison. For <math|0\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>4> and sufficiently large
  <math|t>,

  <\equation*>
    t<rsup|-30>\<leqslant\>C*H<rsup|q<rsub|0>>*s<rsup|5-j>,<space|2em>t<rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>s\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  Taylor's formula for <math|D<rsup|j>*K<rsub|b>*<around|(|s*u|)>> through
  degree <math|4-j>, with the fifth and higher derivatives bounded on the
  line segment, therefore bounds each scaled derivative by
  <math|C<rsub|B>*C*H<rsup|q<rsub|0>>*s<rsup|5>>. For <math|j\<geqslant\>5>,
  the same bound follows directly from <math|s<rsup|j>\<leqslant\>s<rsup|5>>.
  The finite Taylor constants are the fixed fifth-order interpolation
  constant <math|C<rsub|B>>. Multiplying by
  <math|<around|\||\<beta\>|\|>\<leqslant\>4*t> gives

  <\equation*>
    \<\|\|\>\<beta\>*K<rsub|b>(s<space|0.17em>\<cdot\><around|)||\<\|\|\>><rsub|C<rsup|k><around|(|B|)>>\<leqslant\>4*t*<space|0.17em>C<rsub|B>*C*H<rsup|q<rsub|0>>*s<rsup|5>\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsup|2>*t<rsup|-1/100>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed, after division by <math|\<theta\><rsup|2>>, the left side is
  <math|t<rsup|-1/2>> times a fixed power of <math|H> and <math|1+r>, hence
  is <math|t<rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>>. This is the actual fifth-order
  residual estimate; the quadratic healthy-price argument alone would not
  prove it.

  For the finite-difference remainder let <math|h=\<theta\>*t<rsup|-1/24>> be
  the chosen step and <math|m\<geqslant\>48> its order. At each marked link
  and color, write the shifted integrand as the product of the local current
  factor and the exponential action increment. The latter increment is zero
  at zero shift. The derivative bound for that increment is
  <math|Z<around|(|t|)>=2<space|0.17em><math-up|shear><around|(|t|)>*C<rsub|B>*s<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|c>>.
  The segment has length at most <math|\<kappa\><rsub|m>*h/s>, where
  <math|\<kappa\><rsub|m>> depends only on the fixed finite-difference
  stencil. Thus <math|Z<around|(|t|)>*\<kappa\><rsub|m>*h/s\<leqslant\>H<rsup|c>*\<kappa\><rsub|m>*t<rsup|-1/24>\<leqslant\>1>
  eventually. Integrating its first derivative along this segment bounds the
  increment itself by one. The scalar exponential has bounded outer
  derivatives on <math|<around|[|-1,1|]>>; after composition its derivatives
  through order <math|k+m+1> are bounded by fixed powers of <math|H>, not by
  an absolute constant. The chain rule and the product rule therefore bound
  the full integrand by

  <\equation*>
    C<around|(|t|)>*s,<space|2em>C<around|(|t|)>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|c>
  </equation*>

  for some fixed <math|c>, after including the local-current price and the
  finite coordinate-chart factors. For clarity, the action-dependent
  composition factor contains <math|<math-up|shear><around|(|t|)>*s<rsup|2>>;
  since <math|<math-up|shear><around|(|t|)>\<leqslant\>5*t*H<rsup|1/50>> and
  <math|s<rsup|2>=t<rsup|-1>*H<rsup|2>*<around|(|1+r|)><rsup|2*p>>, this
  factor is bounded by a fixed power of <math|H>. All further powers are
  fixed by <math|k+m+1>.

  The finite-difference weights cancel the Taylor coefficients through order
  <math|m>. Taylor's integral remainder, differentiated up to order <math|k>,
  then bounds the error at each marked link and color by
  <math|C<around|(|t|)>*s*<around|(|h/s|)><rsup|m+1>>. Summing over the
  actual finite support multiplies <math|C<around|(|t|)>> by at most
  <math|O<around|(|<around|(|1+r|)><rsup|4>|)>*dim \<frak-g\>>, still a fixed
  power of <math|H>. Since <math|h/s\<leqslant\>t<rsup|-1/24>>,

  <\equation*>
    C<around|(|t|)>*s*<around|(|h/s|)><rsup|m+1>=C<around|(|t|)>*h*<around|(|h/s|)><rsup|m>\<leqslant\>t<rsup|-2+o<around|(|1|)>>*h\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsup|2>*t<rsup|-1/100>*h.
  </equation*>

  The observable <math|f<rsub|b><rsup|3>> divides this Taylor error by
  <math|h>. That division cancels the displayed factor <math|h> exactly,
  leaving the same axial price <math|\<theta\><rsup|2>*t<rsup|-1/100>> as for
  <math|f<rsub|b><rsup|0>>. The finite collar pullback established above
  therefore gives the healthy price <math|\<theta\><rsup|2>*H<rsup|-2>> for
  both errors. The finite-difference integrand estimate also proves the
  shifted local factor price <math|t<rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>> used in
  Subsubsection<nbsp><reference|subsec:trace-charge-support-tail>: it is
  <math|s> times fixed powers of <math|H>, before taking the Taylor remainder
  or dividing by <math|h>.

  <section|The common scale choice and final assembly>

  <subsection|Purchasing the nonlinear estimates on one scale
  sequence><label|sec:nonlinear-payment-selection>

  The nonlinear estimates above are uniform in the finite torus once the
  block size, derivative orders and stage parameters have been fixed. We now
  choose coupling thresholds that pay them together. In particular, the
  marked and unmarked constructions can use the same transition. The two
  logarithmic corrections absorbed into the forced gas require two successive
  payments; both are included.

  <subsubsection|An elementary exponential payment>

  Let <math|\<beta\><rsub|f><around|(|x|)>=x+c<rsub|L>>, with fixed
  <math|c<rsub|L>\<gtr\>0>, let <math|B<around|(|x|)>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
  x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>, and let <math|P<rsub|f><around|(|x|)>> be the
  shifted fine monitor. For <math|C\<gtr\>0> put

  <\equation*>
    \<tau\><rsub|C><around|(|x|)>=exp <around|{|-C*P<rsub|f><around|(|x|)><rsup|2>/2|}>.
  </equation*>

  Fix <math|D<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> and a function <math|f> of negative upper
  exponent. For all sufficiently large <math|x>, the following assertion
  holds simultaneously for every pair <math|a,b\<geqslant\>0> such that

  <\equation*>
    a\<leqslant\>D<rsub|0>*e<rsup|-C*P<rsub|f><around|(|x|)><rsup|2>>,<space|2em>b\<leqslant\>f<around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  There is <math|\<kappa\>\<geqslant\>0> with

  <\gather>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|b\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em><around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*I\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>e<rsup|-\<kappa\>>*<around|(|e<rsup|I>-1|)>\<leqslant\>2*\<tau\><rsub|C><around|(|x|)>,>>|<row|<cell|I=e<rsup|\<kappa\>>*a+2*b.<eq-number><label|eq:nonlinear-payment>>>>>
  </gather>

  Take <math|\<kappa\>=C*P<rsub|f><around|(|x|)><rsup|2>/2>. Then
  <math|e<rsup|\<kappa\>>*a\<leqslant\>D<rsub|0>*\<tau\><rsub|C><around|(|x|)>>,
  so

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*I\<leqslant\><around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*<around|(|D<rsub|0>*\<tau\><rsub|C>+2*f|)>.
  </equation*>

  The right side tends to zero. Indeed the geometric factor has upper
  exponent zero, <math|f> has negative upper exponent, and
  <math|\<tau\><rsub|C>> has every negative upper exponent by the monitor
  estimate. The same choice of threshold gives <math|b\<leqslant\>1> and the
  middle inequality of <eqref|eq:nonlinear-payment>. Since
  <math|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>\<geqslant\>1>, also
  <math|0\<leqslant\>I\<leqslant\>1>. Convexity of the exponential on
  <math|<around|[|0,1|]>> gives <math|e<rsup|I>-1\<leqslant\><around|(|e-1|)>*I\<leqslant\>2*I\<leqslant\>2>.
  Multiplication by <math|e<rsup|-\<kappa\>>=\<tau\><rsub|C>> proves the last
  inequality. These are exactly the smallness and output-price conditions for
  the forced-gas absorption in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:soft-log-fusion>.
  The threshold depends on <math|C,D<rsub|0>,f,L>, but not on <math|a,b>.

  <subsubsection|The two soft correction prices>

  We verify the negative power needed in the second payment. Fix a stage,
  including its derivative order, spatial weight, support exponent, path
  widths and path prices. Let <math|t\<in\><around|(|0,1|]>> be its threshold
  and <math|B> its radius. Let <math|\<alpha\>> be the coupling reset, with
  <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>A>, where <math|A\<geqslant\>0>
  is fixed after the block length has been chosen. If the regular remainder
  allowance is <math|0\<leqslant\>H\<leqslant\>t<rsup|2>>, the reset's
  boundary-hit coefficient is

  <\equation*>
    h<rsub|<math-up|hit>>=2*t*<around*|(|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>+H/t<rsup|2>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>H*C<rsub|<math-up|pair>>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|C<rsub|<math-up|pair>>\<geqslant\>0> depends only on the fixed
  derivative orders. Since <math|H\<leqslant\>t<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>t>,

  <\equation*>
    h<rsub|<math-up|hit>>\<leqslant\><around|[|2*<around|(|A+1|)>+C<rsub|<math-up|pair>>/2|]>*t.
  </equation*>

  There are two correction families, with the support reserve used for the
  first absorption and the larger reserve used for the second. For either
  family let <math|R<rsub|0>,K<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0> be respectively its
  reset and path prices computed at radius one. The rooted footprint sum
  gives

  <\equation*>
    R*<around|(|B,H|)>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|0>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>*H,<space|2em>K<around|(|B|)>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|0>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>.
  </equation*>

  To see the radius dependence explicitly, its footprint population is a
  fixed nonnegative multiple of either <math|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>> or
  <math|<around|(|8*B+5|)><rsup|4>>. The inequalities
  <math|2*B+1\<leqslant\>3*<around|(|1+B|)>> and
  <math|8*B+5\<leqslant\>13*<around|(|1+B|)>> compare them with their
  radius-one values. All remaining factors are fixed: exponential support
  charges, inverse positive width powers and the convergent geometric series
  for the unused width reserve. The same calculation works with the larger
  reserve, using its smaller positive width. Thus the total soft price
  satisfies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:nonlinear-soft-price>R*<around|(|B,H|)>+h<rsub|<math-up|hit>>*K<around|(|B|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|A>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>*t,<space|2em>C<rsub|A>=R<rsub|0>+<around|[|2*<around|(|A+1|)>+C<rsub|<math-up|pair>>/2|]>*K<rsub|0>.
  </equation>

  These constants are selected separately for the two families.

  At coupling <math|x>, take

  <\equation*>
    t<around|(|x|)>=x<rsup|-1/2>*exp <around|(|<around|(|log log
    x|)><rsup|2>|)>,<space|2em>H<around|(|x|)>=c<rsub|<math-up|st>>*x<rsup|-11/10>,<space|2em>0\<less\>c<rsub|<math-up|st>>\<leqslant\>1/64.
  </equation*>

  For all sufficiently large <math|x>, we have
  <math|t<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>1>. For <math|x\<gtr\>1> we also have
  <math|H\<leqslant\>x<rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>t<rsup|2>>, so the preceding
  calculation applies eventually. Moreover, for any fixed constant
  <math|C<rsub|A>>,

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|A>*<around|(|1+B<around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|4>*t<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rsub|f><around|(|x|)><rsup|-1/4>
  </equation*>

  eventually. Divide the left side by <math|x<rsup|-1/4>> and take
  logarithms: the variable terms are <math|4*log
  <around|(|1+<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
  x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>|)>+<around|(|log log x|)><rsup|2>-<tfrac|1|4>log
  x>, which tend to minus infinity. Finally
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|f><around|(|x|)>/x\<to\>1>, so the same assertion holds
  with the shifted scale. This proves the required soft bound uniformly for
  <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>A>, both derivative budgets and
  both finite-volume families.

  The first hard price of the nonlinear expansion is at most <math|exp
  <around|(|-\<mu\>*P<rsub|f><rsup|2>/4|)>>, with <math|\<mu\>\<gtr\>0>. Its
  accompanying soft price is at most <math|\<beta\><rsub|f><rsup|-1/8>>. In
  the multiple-insertion version it is at most
  <math|C<rsub|<math-up|cov>>*\<beta\><rsub|f><rsup|-1/8>>, where the
  positive constant <math|C<rsub|<math-up|cov>>> depends only on the fixed
  enlargement depth. Both have negative upper exponent. Apply
  <eqref|eq:nonlinear-payment> with <math|C=\<mu\>/4>, <math|D<rsub|0>=1> to
  obtain the first output bound

  <\equation*>
    2*e<rsup|-\<mu\>*P<rsub|f><rsup|2>/8>.
  </equation*>

  Use this as the hard price for the second correction, whose soft price is
  bounded by <eqref|eq:nonlinear-soft-price>. A second application with
  <math|C=\<mu\>/8>, <math|D<rsub|0>=2> gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:nonlinear-two-payments>2*e<rsup|-\<mu\>*P<rsub|f><rsup|2>/8><space|1em>\<longmapsto\><space|1em>2*e<rsup|-\<mu\>*P<rsub|f><rsup|2>/16>.
  </equation>

  These are the two successive forced allowances used in the nonlinear
  transition, including the larger-reserve correction. All inequalities use
  the same value of <math|x>.

  <subsubsection|Choosing thresholds without changing the transition>

  The marked construction first chooses a sufficiently large block size to
  pay its footprint conversion. For that fixed block and fixed stage orders,
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-activity-jets>
  and<nbsp><reference|sec:leaf-expansion> give a threshold beyond which the
  local marked derivatives, the two connected expansions and their tail
  estimates hold. The chart transport radius is chosen before this threshold;
  the microscopic fluctuation threshold tends to zero and eventually fits
  inside it. The Gaussian and all-species estimates are uniform in the finite
  torus and in the insertion, so these choices do not depend on either of
  them.

  There are finitely many permitted target stages at each current stage
  <math|j>. For each such pair <math|<around|(|j,i|)>>, choose the block-size
  floor given by the marked construction and take their finite maximum.
  Choose the actual block size <math|L<rsub|j>> above that floor. Only after
  <math|L<rsub|j>> is fixed do we choose the coupling thresholds needed for
  <eqref|eq:nonlinear-payment>, <eqref|eq:nonlinear-soft-price> and the
  fixed-order marked estimates. This order permits their constants to depend
  on the actual block length and reset bound.

  More explicitly, let <math|d<rsub|j>> be a previously chosen entry
  threshold, and let <math|r<rsub|j,i>> be the threshold for all the
  requirements at transition <math|<around|(|j,i|)>>. Replace it by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:nonlinear-entry-max>d<rprime|'><rsub|j>=max
    <around*|(|d<rsub|j>,max<rsub|i<math-up|permitted at><space|0.17em>j>
    r<rsub|j,i>|)>.
  </equation>

  For any later thresholds <math|d<rprime|''><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>d<rprime|'><rsub|j>>
  and every <math|x\<geqslant\>d<rprime|''><rsub|j>>, all the requirements
  still hold. This is immediate from <math|x\<geqslant\>d<rprime|'><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>r<rsub|j,i>>;
  the other selected data, including the block sizes and the transition maps,
  are unchanged. Applying <eqref|eq:nonlinear-entry-max> successively to the
  ordinary and the larger-reserve multiple-insertion requirements gives one
  set of thresholds satisfying both. No uniform maximum over all orders or
  all stages is needed.

  Consequently the numerical arrival conditions previously required by
  constructions with one or several insertions and by the centered
  construction can be imposed simultaneously on the same selected scale
  sequence. This is a purchase of their actual displayed estimates. The
  construction of the underlying funded Gaussian data and the subsequent
  coefficient and normalization identities remain the respective analytic and
  algebraic steps already separated in those arguments.

  <subsection|One scale sequence for all the
  estimates><label|sec:common-scale-selection>

  The estimates are indexed by finite stages, while the final theory requires
  arbitrarily high orders. We choose a slowly advancing stage map so that
  every fixed stage eventually appears, with enough time to satisfy its
  comparison estimates. The same choice also pays the numerical requests made
  after the block lengths have been selected.

  <subsubsection|A slowly advancing sequence with long constant intervals>

  Let <math|b<rsub|j>,c<rsub|j>> be arbitrary nonnegative integers. Define
  recursively

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|0>=0,<space|2em>k<rsub|j>=c<rsub|j>+2*A<rsub|j>+1,<space|2em>A<rsub|j+1>=A<rsub|j>+b<rsub|j+1>+2*k<rsub|j>+1.
  </equation*>

  The <math|A<rsub|j>> are strictly increasing and tend to infinity. Define
  <math|J<rsub|0><around|(|m|)>=j> when <math|A<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>m\<less\>A<rsub|j+1>>.
  These half-open intervals partition the nonnegative integers. Then
  <math|J<rsub|0>> is nondecreasing, <math|J<rsub|0><around|(|0|)>=J<rsub|0><around|(|1|)>=0>,
  and

  <\equation*>
    J<rsub|0>*<around|(|m+1|)>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|0><around|(|m|)>+1,<space|2em>J<rsub|0><around|(|m|)>\<longrightarrow\>\<infty\>.
  </equation*>

  The increment bound holds because an integer step can cross at most one of
  the distinct integer endpoints. The divergence follows from
  <math|m\<geqslant\>A<rsub|j>\<Rightarrow\>J<rsub|0><around|(|m|)>\<geqslant\>j>.

  If <math|J<rsub|0>*<around|(|m+1|)>=j\<gtr\>0>, then
  <math|A<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>m+1> and <math|A<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>b<rsub|j>+1>,
  so <math|b<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>m>. Also <math|k<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|j>>,
  and the whole interval

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:common-long-plateau>k<rsub|j>-<around|\<lfloor\>|k<rsub|j>/2|\<rfloor\>>\<leqslant\>m\<leqslant\>k<rsub|j>+<around|\<lfloor\>|k<rsub|j>/2|\<rfloor\>>
  </equation>

  lies in <math|<around|[|A<rsub|j>,A<rsub|j+1>|)>>: its lower endpoint is at
  least <math|A<rsub|j>> because <math|k<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>2*A<rsub|j>+1>,
  and its upper endpoint is at most <math|2*k<rsub|j>\<less\>A<rsub|j+1>>.
  Thus <math|J<rsub|0>=j> throughout this interval. This explicit recursion
  simultaneously postpones entry to every stage and gives that stage a
  prescribed long comparison interval.

  <subsubsection|Physical scales, entry requirements and periods>

  Let <math|L<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>3> be the selected nondecreasing odd block
  lengths, and let <math|j<rsub|\<ast\>>> be the first stage. Put

  <\equation*>
    J<around|(|m|)>=j<rsub|\<ast\>>+J<rsub|0><around|(|m|)>,<space|2em>s<rsub|0>=0,<space|2em>s<rsub|m+1>=s<rsub|m>+log
    L<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>,<space|2em>a<rsub|m>=e<rsup|-s<rsub|m>>.
  </equation*>

  Induction and monotonicity give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:common-scale-brackets>m*log
    3\<leqslant\>s<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>m*log L<rsub|J<around|(|m|)>>.
  </equation>

  In particular <math|a<rsub|m>*\<downarrow\>*0>, and
  <math|e<rsup|s<rsub|m>>> is the integer product of the first <math|m> block
  lengths.

  In this construction <math|d<rsub|j>> denotes the final entry threshold,
  including all the finite-stage enlargements described below. Choose an
  initial coupling <math|\<beta\><rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>1> with
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|\<ast\>>\<geqslant\>d<rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>>>, large
  enough that <math|t<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>1> for
  <math|x\<geqslant\>\<beta\><rsub|\<ast\>>>. This is possible since
  <math|t<around|(|x|)>=x<rsup|-1/2>*exp <around|(|<around|(|log log
  x|)><rsup|2>|)>\<to\>0>. Set

  <\equation*>
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>=\<beta\><rsub|\<ast\>>+s<rsub|G>*s<rsub|m>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|s<rsub|G>=\<lambda\><rsub|G>=2*b<rsub|0><around|(|G|)>\<gtr\>0>
  is the slope constant <eqref|eq:shoot-slope> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:surviving-interval-shooting>; the intercept
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|\<ast\>>> is the <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>> of that
  section. For each fixed stage <math|j\<gtr\>j<rsub|\<ast\>>> there are only
  finitely many entry and comparison bounds. Require, at sufficiently large
  <math|s>,

  <\gather>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|d<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rsub|\<ast\>>+s<rsub|G>*s,<space|2em>e<rsub|j><around|(|\<beta\><rsub|\<ast\>>|)>\<leqslant\>s,<space|2em>s\<gtr\>0,>>|<row|<cell|log
    L<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>s/j,<space|2em>V<rsub|j><around|(|s|)>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|s>.<eq-number><label|eq:common-stage-entries>>>>>
  </gather>

  Here <math|d<rsub|j>> is the coupling entry, <math|e<rsub|j>> the scale
  entry, and the finite-volume requirement is

  <\equation*>
    V<rsub|j><around|(|s|)>=h<rsub|j>*<around|(|1+B<around|(|s|)>+B<rsub|j><rsup|+><around|(|s|)>|)><rsup|r<rsub|j>>+100*<around|(|B<around|(|s|)>+1|)>+100*<around|(|<around|(|j+3|)>*L<rsub|j>+1|)>,
  </equation*>

  with nonnegative integers <math|h<rsub|j>,r<rsub|j>> and

  <\equation*>
    B<around|(|s|)>=<around*|\<lceil\>|log<rsup|2><around|(|\<beta\><rsub|\<ast\>>+s<rsub|G>*s|)>|\<rceil\>>,<space|2em>B<rsub|j><rsup|+><around|(|s|)>=<around*|\<lceil\>|log<rsup|2><around|(|\<beta\><rsub|\<ast\>>+s<rsub|G>*<around|(|s+log
    L<rsub|j>|)>|)>|\<rceil\>>.
  </equation*>

  All inequalities in <eqref|eq:common-stage-entries> hold eventually. The
  first four follow from positive linear growth in <math|s>; for the last,
  both ceiling functions are <math|O<around|(|s|)>>, so
  <math|V<rsub|j><around|(|s|)>> is bounded by a fixed polynomial and hence
  is <math|o<around|(|e<rsup|s>|)>>. Select <math|b<rsub|j-j<rsub|\<ast\>>>>
  so that they hold whenever <math|s\<geqslant\>b<rsub|j-j<rsub|\<ast\>>>*log
  3>. The entry property of <math|J<rsub|0>> and the lower bound in
  <eqref|eq:common-scale-brackets> now give all these requirements at every
  step with <math|J*<around|(|m+1|)>\<gtr\>j<rsub|\<ast\>>>. On the first
  plateau, <math|J*<around|(|m+1|)>=j<rsub|\<ast\>>> and
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>\<geqslant\>\<beta\><rsub|\<ast\>>\<geqslant\>d<rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>>>.
  Consequently the coupling entrance <math|d<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>>
  holds at every step, including <math|m=0>. The scale-entry and
  positive-scale requirements in <eqref|eq:common-stage-entries> are only
  imposed after that first plateau; the finite first-plateau volume
  requirements are paid by the period choice below.

  For the comparison interval at stage <math|j>, suppose its required window
  <math|w<rsub|j><around|(|s|)>> is nonnegative and integer valued and
  <math|w<rsub|j><around|(|s|)>/s\<to\>0>, and its minimum scale is
  <math|r<rsub|j><rsup|0>>. Choose <math|c<rsub|j-j<rsub|\<ast\>>>> so large
  that

  <\equation*>
    r<rsub|j><rsup|0>\<leqslant\>s,<space|2em>w<rsub|j><around|(|s|)>\<leqslant\><around|\<lfloor\>|k/2|\<rfloor\>>
  </equation*>

  whenever <math|k\<geqslant\>c<rsub|j-j<rsub|\<ast\>>>> and <math|k*log
  3\<leqslant\>s\<leqslant\>k*log L<rsub|j>>. Such a choice exists because
  eventually <math|w<rsub|j><around|(|s|)>/s\<leqslant\><around|(|2*log
  L<rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1>>. At the center <math|k=k<rsub|j-j<rsub|\<ast\>>>> of
  <eqref|eq:common-long-plateau>, <eqref|eq:common-scale-brackets> gives
  precisely these scale bounds. Thus the whole requested window around
  <math|k> stays at stage <math|j>.

  Only finitely many mesh steps remain in the first plateau. Choose an
  integer <math|M> at least their finitely many values
  <math|V<rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>><around|(|s<rsub|m>|)>> and at least the
  required minimum physical period. Use periods

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|p>=4*<around|(|M+p+1|)>.
  </equation*>

  The lattice side at level <math|m> is <math|P<rsub|p>*e<rsup|s<rsub|m>>>.
  On the first plateau it pays <math|V<rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>><around|(|s<rsub|m>|)>>
  by the choice of <math|M>. At every later step it pays
  <math|V<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>><around|(|s<rsub|m>|)>> because that
  quantity is at most <math|e<rsup|s<rsub|m>>>. The three summands of
  <math|V<rsub|j>> pay respectively the specified volume, coarse-cover and
  fine-patch requirements.

  The periods increase strictly and have arbitrarily late multiples of every
  positive integer <math|c>. Indeed, given <math|z>, take
  <math|p=c*<around|(|M+z+1|)>-<around|(|M+1|)>>. Then <math|p\<geqslant\>z>
  and <math|P<rsub|p>=4*c*<around|(|M+z+1|)>>. Finally

  <\equation*>
    0\<leqslant\><frac|log L<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>|s<rsub|m>>\<leqslant\><frac|1|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>\<longrightarrow\>0
  </equation*>

  after the first plateau, by <eqref|eq:common-stage-entries>. Thus the
  relative logarithmic mesh increment tends to zero as required. The stage
  profiles, block lengths and kernel have all been retained; the new choices
  are their scheduling and periods.

  <subsubsection|Countably many requests on that same sequence>

  Let <math|F<rsub|n,i>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> be a countable family of numerical
  requests selected before constructing the sequence. Define

  <\equation*>
    E<rsub|j>=1+<big|sum><rsub|n=0><rsup|j><big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|j><around|\||F<rsub|n,i>|\|>.
  </equation*>

  Include <math|exp <around|(|exp E<rsub|j>|)>> in <math|d<rsub|j>> before
  choosing <math|\<beta\><rsub|\<ast\>>> and applying the preceding
  construction. Since <math|d<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>>,
  monotonicity of the logarithm twice gives

  <\equation*>
    E<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>\<leqslant\>log log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>.
  </equation*>

  For fixed <math|n>, the divergence of <math|J> supplies a first index after
  which <math|n\<leqslant\>J*<around|(|m+1|)>>. At every such <math|m> and
  every <math|i\<leqslant\>J*<around|(|m+1|)>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:common-countable-payment><around|\||F<rsub|n,i>|\|>\<leqslant\>E<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>\<leqslant\>1+log
    log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>.
  </equation>

  The first index depends on <math|n>. We have not required that a finite
  stage pay infinitely many independent requests.

  There is also an all-stage form. For a prescribed real sequence
  <math|H<rsub|j>>, include <math|H<rsub|j>> in that same final maximum for
  <math|d<rsub|j>>, again before choosing <math|\<beta\><rsub|\<ast\>>>. Then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:common-allstage-payment>H<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>
  </equation>

  for every <math|m>, including zero. Both conclusions persist on every
  realization paying enlarged entry thresholds, because their proofs use only
  a lower bound on the threshold actually paid. Combining finitely many such
  purchases before choosing the realization therefore retains every earlier
  conclusion.

  <subsubsection|Selecting the lengths before their numerical costs>

  The ordering of choices is essential. First fix the stage profiles, the
  original kernel, the row-price families and their secondary widths. Their
  analytic suppliers give integer block-size floors. Take the pointwise
  maximum of the original floor, the flat and dual support floors, the
  curvature floor, the comparison floors, and the treasury and period floors.
  Two further families of floors are named explicitly, because later sections
  consume them as schedule conditions.

  <\enumerate>
    <item><em|Marked-portfolio thresholds.> The marked portfolio, its
    sections and its regradings are fixed before the profiles' price maps and
    the floors (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-observable-purchases>).
    For that portfolio, Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-marked-portfolio-calibration>
    produces stage thresholds <math|n<rsup|<math-up|mk>><rsub|j>> on the
    block length. Include <math|n<rsup|<math-up|mk>><rsub|j>> in the floor of
    stage <math|j>. Then every block side used at stage <math|j> is at least
    <math|n<rsup|<math-up|mk>><rsub|j>>; this is the comparison of thresholds
    with floor lengths required by the caller in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-marked-portfolio-calibration>.

    <item><em|Drift and logarithmic-size ratios.> For
    <math|j\<geqslant\>j<rsub|\<ast\>>> let
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>0> be the drift allowance of the
    stage profile <math|\<pi\><rsub|j>> and
    <math|\<lambda\><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>0> the logarithm of one plus the sum
    of its path allowance, inverse width, column count, clip relaxation and
    support exponent. Both are fixed with the profile. Require

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:common-drift-floor><frac|\<varepsilon\><rsub|j>|log
      L<rsub|j>>\<leqslant\>min <around*|{|<frac|s<rsub|G>|4>,<frac|1|j<rsup|2>>|}>,<space|2em><frac|\<lambda\><rsub|j>|log
      L<rsub|j>>\<leqslant\><frac|1|j<rsup|2>>*<space|2em><around|(|j\<geqslant\>j<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>.
    </equation>

    Since <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|j>,\<lambda\><rsub|j>> do not depend on
    the length and <math|c/log n\<to\>0> as <math|n\<to\>\<infty\>> for each
    fixed <math|c\<geqslant\>0>, there is an integer <math|u<rsub|j>> such
    that <eqref|eq:common-drift-floor> holds for every
    <math|L<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>u<rsub|j>>; include <math|u<rsub|j>> in the
    floor of stage <math|j>.
  </enumerate>

  A nondecreasing odd sequence <math|L<rsub|j>> above these floors exists:
  choose each <math|L<rsub|j>> above the current finite maximum and above
  <math|L<rsub|j-1>>. These choices precede any numerical request that
  depends on <math|L<rsub|j>>.

  At step <math|m> the block side is <math|L<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>> and
  the incoming stage is <math|J*<around|(|m+1|)>\<geqslant\>j<rsub|\<ast\>>>.
  Thus <eqref|eq:common-drift-floor> at <math|j=J*<around|(|m+1|)>> reads

  <\equation*>
    <frac|\<varepsilon\><rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>|log
    L<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>>\<leqslant\>min
    <around*|{|<frac|s<rsub|G>|4>,<frac|1|J*<around|(|m+1|)><rsup|2>>|}>,
  </equation*>

  which, together with <math|J<around|(|m|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>, is the drift
  condition <eqref|eq:shoot-drift-ratio> and the second inequality of
  <eqref|eq:shoot-adjacent-drift> on the selected schedule, in the notation
  of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:surviving-interval-shooting> (there
  <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i+1>> is the allowance of the stage
  <math|J*<around|(|i+1|)>> and <math|L<rsub|i>=L<rsub|J*<around|(|i+1|)>>>).

  With the lengths now fixed, form the actual post-length numerical costs. If
  two constructions require <math|H<rsub|j><rsup|<around|(|1|)>>> and
  <math|H<rsub|j><rsup|<around|(|2|)>>>, pay <math|H<rsub|j>=max
  <around|(|H<rsub|j><rsup|<around|(|1|)>>,H<rsub|j><rsup|<around|(|2|)>>|)>>
  by <eqref|eq:common-allstage-payment>. Their countable refinements, as well
  as the previously selected inverse-width requests, are paid by
  <eqref|eq:common-countable-payment>. Add the nonlinear entry requirements
  of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:nonlinear-payment-selection> and every
  other selected finite-stage threshold before constructing <math|J> and
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^>>. Every realization paying these enlarged
  thresholds preserves all the earlier purchases by the preceding
  monotonicity argument. Explicitly, the final entry threshold
  <math|d<rsub|j>> includes the following stage thresholds, each of which is
  a finite maximum at stage <math|j> selected before
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|\<ast\>>>:

  <\enumerate>
    <item>the terminal fee threshold <math|k<rsub|j>> and the entrance delay
    of the marked portfolio, both from Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-observable-purchases>;
    the latter is the delay condition of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-marked-portfolio-calibration>
    for the adjacent stage pairs, including its profile-cost, credit and tail
    clauses;

    <item>the startup and reset thresholds and the remaining entry delays
    listed at the end of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history>,
    selected after the protection parameters there;

    <item>the loop-capacity threshold <math|\<Delta\><rsub|j>> of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:common-loop-capacity> below, for the
    side-dependent thresholds <math|W<rsub|j><around|(|L<rsub|j>|)>> of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:path-attachment-producer>.
  </enumerate>

  Since <math|d<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>>
  at every step, every one of these thresholds is paid at stage
  <math|J*<around|(|m+1|)>> by the coupling <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>>.
  In particular <math|k<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>>,
  which is the fee condition used in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:terminal-marked-voice>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Loop capacities><label|lem:common-loop-capacity> Let
    <math|\<theta\><rsub|j>\<gtr\>0> be nonincreasing protection exponents,
    fixed after the block lengths, and put

    <\equation*>
      P<around|(|x|)>=exp <around*|(|<around|(|log log
      x|)><rsup|2>|)>,<space|2em><math-up|loopCap><rsub|m>=2*P<around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|)><rsup|\<theta\><rsub|J<around|(|m|)>>>.
    </equation*>

    For every real sequence <math|W<rsub|j>> there are thresholds
    <math|\<Delta\><rsub|j>> such that, whenever
    <math|\<Delta\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>d<rsub|j>> for all <math|j>, the
    selected schedule satisfies at every <math|m\<geqslant\>0>, with
    <math|j=J*<around|(|m+1|)>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:common-loop-capacity>W<rsub|j>\<leqslant\><math-up|loopCap><rsub|m>,<space|2em><tfrac|1|2><math-up|loopCap><rsub|m+1>\<leqslant\><math-up|loopCap><rsub|m>,<space|2em>1\<leqslant\><math-up|loopCap><rsub|m>.
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Fix <math|j> and put <math|v<rsub|j>=s<rsub|G>*log
    L<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>0>. As <math|x\<to\>\<infty\>>,
    <math|2*P<around|(|x|)><rsup|\<theta\><rsub|j>>\<to\>\<infty\>>, so
    eventually <math|W<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>2*P<around|(|x|)><rsup|\<theta\><rsub|j>>>.
    For the second requirement let <math|\<ell\>=log x> and
    <math|\<ell\><rprime|'>=log <around|(|x+v<rsub|j>|)>>. For
    <math|x\<gtr\>1>, <math|\<ell\>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rprime|'>\<leqslant\>\<ell\>+v<rsub|j>/x>,
    since <math|log <around|(|1+v<rsub|j>/x|)>\<leqslant\>v<rsub|j>/x>. Using
    <math|log t\<leqslant\>t-1>,

    <\equation*>
      0\<leqslant\>log \<ell\><rprime|'>-log
      \<ell\>\<leqslant\><frac|\<ell\><rprime|'>|\<ell\>>-1\<leqslant\><frac|v<rsub|j>|x*\<ell\>>.
    </equation*>

    Once <math|\<ell\>\<geqslant\>1> and <math|x\<geqslant\>v<rsub|j>>, also
    <math|0\<leqslant\>log \<ell\>\<leqslant\>\<ell\>> and <math|log
    \<ell\><rprime|'>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rprime|'>-1\<leqslant\>\<ell\>+v<rsub|j>/x-1\<leqslant\>\<ell\>+1>,
    so

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|log \<ell\><rprime|'>|)><rsup|2>-<around|(|log
      \<ell\>|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|(|log \<ell\><rprime|'>+log
      \<ell\>|)>*<frac|v<rsub|j>|x*\<ell\>>\<leqslant\><frac|<around|(|2*\<ell\>+1|)>*v<rsub|j>|x*\<ell\>>\<leqslant\><frac|3*v<rsub|j>|x>.
    </equation*>

    Hence <math|P*<around|(|x+v<rsub|j>|)><rsup|\<theta\><rsub|j>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|3*v<rsub|j>*\<theta\><rsub|j>/x>*P<around|(|x|)><rsup|\<theta\><rsub|j>>\<leqslant\>2*P<around|(|x|)><rsup|\<theta\><rsub|j>>>
    as soon as <math|3*v<rsub|j>*\<theta\><rsub|j>/x\<leqslant\>log 2>. Let
    <math|\<Delta\><rsub|j>> be a common threshold beyond which both
    properties hold, and <math|x\<geqslant\>max <around|{|e,v<rsub|j>|}>>.

    Now let <math|m\<geqslant\>0>, <math|j=J*<around|(|m+1|)>> and
    <math|x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>\<geqslant\>d<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>\<Delta\><rsub|j>>.
    Since <math|P\<geqslant\>1> and <math|\<theta\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsub|J<around|(|m|)>>>
    (because <math|J<around|(|m|)>\<leqslant\>j> and the exponents are
    nonincreasing), <math|P<around|(|x|)><rsup|\<theta\><rsub|j>>\<leqslant\>P<around|(|x|)><rsup|\<theta\><rsub|J<around|(|m|)>>>>.
    The first property gives <math|W<rsub|j>\<leqslant\><math-up|loopCap><rsub|m>>.
    Next <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>=x+s<rsub|G>*log
    L<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>=x+v<rsub|j>> and <math|J*<around|(|m+1|)>=j>,
    so

    <\equation*>
      <tfrac|1|2><math-up|loopCap><rsub|m+1>=P*<around|(|x+v<rsub|j>|)><rsup|\<theta\><rsub|j>>\<leqslant\>2*P<around|(|x|)><rsup|\<theta\><rsub|j>>\<leqslant\>2*P<around|(|x|)><rsup|\<theta\><rsub|J<around|(|m|)>>>=<math-up|loopCap><rsub|m>.
    </equation*>

    Finally <math|P<around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>1> and
    <math|\<theta\><rsub|J<around|(|m|)>>\<gtr\>0> give
    <math|<math-up|loopCap><rsub|m>\<geqslant\>2>.
  </proof>

  These are the three loop-capacity inequalities used in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:path-attachment-producer>, with
  <math|W<rsub|j>> its side-dependent threshold at stage <math|j> evaluated
  at <math|L<rsub|j>>; the threshold is indexed by the incoming stage and the
  capacity by the output level, as there.

  Every step then uses the original length <math|L<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>>,
  so its actual finite patch is exactly the patch for which the post-length
  cost was computed. In particular a bound of the form
  <math|e<rsup|H<rsub|j>>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>> yields
  <math|H<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>> on that patch; no
  estimate for a different block can be substituted. A maximum floor gives
  each of its component floors, and a maximum post-length cost gives each
  component cost. Applying the native analytic suppliers to these
  inequalities therefore supplies their conclusions on the same original
  levels and quadratic horizon.

  This proves the scheduling and numerical selection step. The analytic
  hypotheses of those suppliers, and the sublinear window bounds used above,
  are still the specific estimates established in their own sections.
  Numerical selection does not replace them.

  <subsection|Additional scale estimates for the operator-product
  expansion><label|sec:ope-scale-purchases>

  The operator-product construction uses the same block lengths and hatted
  couplings as the centered estimates. Its additional numerical requirements
  can all be imposed after the block lengths are chosen and before the scale
  sequence is realized. We verify them explicitly.

  For the final construction choose the profile order and spatial power at
  stage <math|j> to be at least <math|6+j> and <math|2+j>, respectively, and
  choose the support exponent to be exactly <math|100> at every stage.
  Constant support exponents satisfy the monotonicity requirement. Increasing
  the order and spatial power does not require increasing this exponent: the
  finite profile prices are selected afresh at the chosen orders, and their
  later floors and delays are allowed to depend on them. Thus the common
  funding and observable purchases apply with this choice. The constant
  exponent will also be retained when intermediate clusters are regrouped.

  <subsubsection|The halo and block-length inequalities>

  At a transition write <math|L> for the already selected integer block side,
  <math|x> for the hatted coupling and <math|y=x+s<rsub|G>*log L> for the
  next hatted coupling, where <math|s<rsub|G>\<gtr\>0> is the slope constant
  of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection>. Set
  <math|B<around|(|x|)>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
  x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>> and define

  <\equation*>
    H<rsub|L>*<around|(|x,y|)>=<around*|(|1+L*<around|(|B<around|(|x|)>+B<around|(|y|)>+1|)><rsup|2>|)><rsup|4>.
  </equation*>

  For fixed <math|L> and any fixed <math|A\<geqslant\>0>, there is an
  entrance threshold such that

  <\equation>
    A*H<rsub|L>*<around|(|x,y|)>\<leqslant\><sqrt|x><label|eq:ope-scale-purchases-display-O1>
  </equation>

  above that threshold. Indeed <math|y/x\<to\>1>, so eventually
  <math|B<around|(|y|)>\<leqslant\>2*B<around|(|x|)>>. Hence
  <math|B<around|(|x|)>+B<around|(|y|)>+1\<leqslant\>4*<around|(|1+B<around|(|x|)>|)>>
  and <math|H<rsub|L>*<around|(|x,y|)>=O<around|(|<around|(|1+log
  x|)><rsup|16>|)>>. The logarithm of <math|<around|(|1+log
  x|)><rsup|16>/<sqrt|x>> tends to <math|-\<infty\>>, proving
  <eqref|eq:ope-scale-purchases-display-O1>. Apply this with <math|A=101>,
  the fine support exponent plus one. The power four is the spatial
  dimension; the factor <math|L> is needed because two child anchors which
  first meet after blocking can be separated by a block side as well as the
  two cover radii.

  The stronger block-length condition

  <\equation>
    L\<leqslant\>log x<label|eq:ope-scale-purchases-display-O2>
  </equation>

  is obtained by requiring <math|x\<geqslant\>e<rsup|L>>. It is a condition
  at a fixed profile stage, not a uniform bound over unchosen block lengths.
  Let <math|C<rsub|i>> denote the column count at stage <math|i>. At a
  transition with fine stage <math|j> the coarse stage is either <math|j> or
  <math|j-1>. Imposing

  <\equation>
    x\<geqslant\>max <around|(|L<rsub|j>,C<rsub|j>,C<rsub|j-1>|)><label|eq:ope-scale-purchases-display-O3>
  </equation>

  therefore gives both adjacent column bounds and the block bound required by
  the logarithmic estimates. For <math|j=0> use <math|C<rsub|0>> in place of
  <math|C<rsub|j-1>>.

  <subsubsection|Uniform logarithmic bounds for the fixed-stage data>

  Write <math|h<rsub|i>> for the path price at stage <math|i>,
  <math|\<sigma\><rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> for its width,
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> for its linear clipping parameter,
  <math|r<rsub|i>> for its relaxation parameter, and <math|d<rsub|i>> for its
  direct clipping parameter. These are fixed finite nonnegative numbers, with
  the stated strictly positive denominators. Let
  <math|D<rsub|G>=dim<rsub|\<bbb-R\>> M<rsub|r<rsub|G>><around|(|\<bbb-C\>|)>>,
  and let <math|n<rsub|\<square\>>> be the number of oriented plaquette-axis
  types used in the action. The elementary vacuum bound has the form

  <\equation*>
    V<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>=2*<around|(|1+<around|(|4*h<rsub|i>+1|)>|)>+1+n<rsub|\<square\>>*<around|(|d<rsub|i>+h<rsub|i><rsup|2>|)>+4*D<rsub|G>*<around|(|log
    16+<tfrac|1|2>log x|)>.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|V<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>+n<rsub|\<square\>>=B<rsub|i>+2*D<rsub|G>*log
  x> for a fixed constant <math|B<rsub|i>>. At each fixed stage we can
  require simultaneously

  <\gather>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|L<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>log
    x,<space|2em>10/\<sigma\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>log
    x,<space|2em>10/\<lambda\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>log
    x,<eq-number><label|eq:ope-scale-purchases-display-O4>>>|<row|<cell|V<rsub|i><around|(|x|)>+n<rsub|\<square\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|log
    x|)><rsup|2>,<eq-number><label|eq:ope-scale-purchases-display-O5>>>|<row|<cell|10<rsup|5>*<around|(|i+1|)>\<leqslant\>log
    x,<space|2em>10<rsup|5>*<around|(|1+h<rsub|i>+100+r<rsub|i>+\<sigma\><rsub|i>+C<rsub|i>+d<rsub|i>+\<lambda\><rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>log
    x.<eq-number><label|eq:ope-scale-purchases-display-O6>>>>>
  </gather>

  For <eqref|eq:ope-scale-purchases-display-O5>, take <math|z=log
  x\<geqslant\>max <around|(|1,B<rsub|i>,2*D<rsub|G>+1|)>>; then
  <math|B<rsub|i>+2*D<rsub|G>*z\<leqslant\>z+<around|(|z-1|)>*z=z<rsup|2>>.
  The remaining inequalities hold because <math|log x\<to\>\<infty\>>. To
  allow the stage indexing at both ends of a transition, purchase
  <eqref|eq:ope-scale-purchases-display-O4>\U<eqref|eq:ope-scale-purchases-display-O6>
  for every <math|i\<leqslant\>j> with the actual block side
  <math|L<rsub|j>>. This is a finite list at each <math|j>.

  Take the maximum of these thresholds and those for
  <eqref|eq:ope-scale-purchases-display-O1>\U<eqref|eq:ope-scale-purchases-display-O3>,
  and include these thresholds in the final entry thresholds <math|d<rsub|j>>
  of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection> before
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|\<ast\>>> is chosen. Every estimate holds for all
  larger <math|x>, so this preserves earlier purchases. At level <math|m>
  take <math|j=J*<around|(|m+1|)>> and <math|i=J<around|(|m|)>>. The common
  scale construction ensures the entrance condition at this <math|j>, and the
  identities for the actual clock give

  <\equation*>
    L=L<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>,<space|2em>x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>,<space|2em>y=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>.
  </equation*>

  Thus <eqref|eq:ope-scale-purchases-display-O1>\U<eqref|eq:ope-scale-purchases-display-O6>
  hold on the selected family, at all steps and periods. They require no
  subsequent change of its history.

  <subsubsection|Two consequences used in choosing intermediate scales>

  The physical scale satisfies <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>=\<beta\><rsub|\<ast\>>+s<rsub|G>*log
  a<rsub|m><rsup|-1>>, with <math|s<rsub|G>\<gtr\>0>. For every fixed integer
  <math|q\<geqslant\>0>,

  <\equation>
    a<rsub|m>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m><rsup|q>\<longrightarrow\>0.<label|eq:ope-scale-purchases-display-O7>
  </equation>

  Indeed, setting <math|s=log a<rsub|m><rsup|-1>\<to\>\<infty\>> gives an
  exponential <math|e<rsup|-s>> times a polynomial of degree <math|q>. Also,
  for every <math|c,\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0> and integer <math|q\<geqslant\>0>,
  eventually

  <\equation>
    c<around|(|log x|)><rsup|q>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>*exp
    <around|{|<around|(|log log x|)><rsup|2>|}>.<label|eq:ope-scale-purchases-display-O8>
  </equation>

  The logarithm of the ratio of the left side to the right side is <math|log
  <around|(|c/\<epsilon\>|)>+q*u-u<rsup|2>> with <math|u=log log
  x\<to\>\<infty\>>, so the ratio tends to zero.

  Finally, if <math|r\<gtr\>1> and <math|r*a<rsub|i>\<less\>1>, there is
  <math|j\<less\>i> such that

  <\equation>
    a<rsub|j+1>\<leqslant\>r*a<rsub|i>\<less\>a<rsub|j>.<label|eq:ope-scale-purchases-display-O9>
  </equation>

  Choose the first index <math|k> with <math|a<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>r*a<rsub|i>>.
  It exists since <math|i> qualifies; it is positive since
  <math|a<rsub|0>=1\<gtr\>r*a<rsub|i>>. Set <math|j=k-1>. Minimality gives
  the strict upper inequality and <math|k\<leqslant\>i> gives
  <math|j\<less\>i>. These are the intermediate-scale choices used with the
  common clock. The analytic OPE estimates remain those of the preceding
  local construction; <eqref|eq:ope-scale-purchases-display-O1>\U<eqref|eq:ope-scale-purchases-display-O9>
  supply its additional scale inequalities.

  <subsection|The operator-product estimates on the selected
  history><label|sec:ope-history-service>

  The local projection and the several-insertion expansion must use the same
  adjacent transition. We establish this compatibility on the family of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-centered-service>. Throughout this
  section the profiles have support exponent <math|100>, as selected in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-scale-purchases>. The orders and spatial
  powers still increase. No background representation is chosen again.

  <subsubsection|Finite elementary representations of transferred fields>

  Fix a finite torus, a representation <math|R> and an anchor <math|o>. For a
  support <math|X> and a nonnegative bound <math|h>, write

  <\equation*>
    m<rsub|R>*<around|(|X,h|)>=e<rsup|b*C<rsub|B><around|(|X|)>>*h,
  </equation*>

  where <math|b\<geqslant\>0> and <math|B> are its support exponent and
  covering radius. The reserved regular slot on each nonempty support and the
  reserved forced slot at each admissible support\Uinventory pair are
  retained from the marked construction. A finite elementary representation
  of an ordinary part <math|g> and a forced profile <math|K> means identities

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-finite-elementary>g=p+<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>I>z<rsub|j><math-up|ord><around|(|M<rsub|j>|)>,<space|2em>K=<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>I>z<rsub|j><math-up|flag><around|(|M<rsub|j>|)>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>I><around|\||z<rsub|j>|\|><math-up|cost><around|(|M<rsub|j>|)>\<leqslant\>A.
  </equation>

  Here <math|I> is finite, all insertion supports contain <math|o>, and
  <math|p\<in\>\<bbb-C\>> is the separately recorded constant. In particular
  <math|K> is the actual profile, not merely an array with the same
  integrated density.

  Suppose first that <math|g=<big|sum><rsub|i>f<rsub|i>>, where the sum is
  countable, each <math|f<rsub|i>> is measurable and gauge invariant, depends
  only on its support <math|X<rsub|i>> and the flags there, vanishes unless
  <math|X<rsub|i>> is healthy, and <math|o\<in\>X<rsub|i>>. Assume

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||f<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>h<rsub|i>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|i>m<rsub|R>*<around|(|X<rsub|i>,h<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>A.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|b\<geqslant\>0>, <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>h<rsub|i>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
  Group by the finitely many supports of the torus and put

  <\equation*>
    f<rsub|X>=<big|sum><rsub|i:X<rsub|i>=X>f<rsub|i>,<space|2em>h<rsub|X>=<big|sum><rsub|i:X<rsub|i>=X>h<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  These series converge uniformly and absolutely. Their sums preserve
  measurability, gauge invariance, local dependence and the healthy-set
  condition. The real and imaginary parts of <math|f<rsub|X>> can be placed
  in the reserved regular slot of <math|X>, each with bound <math|h<rsub|X>>.
  Their coefficients are <math|1> and <math|i>. The resulting elementary
  representation has ordinary part <math|f<rsub|X>>, zero forced profile and
  price at most <math|2*m<rsub|R>*<around|(|X,h<rsub|X>|)>>. Zero functions
  may be omitted. Summing over the finite support set gives
  <eqref|eq:ope-finite-elementary> with <math|p=0>, <math|K=0> and price
  <math|2*A>. The equality of the price sums follows by grouping an
  absolutely convergent nonnegative series; its support weight is constant
  within each group.

  The same argument applies to a dominated forced array. Group its
  coefficients by their support\Uinventory pair <math|<around|(|X,J|)>>.
  Suppose the grouped coefficient vanishes unless the flags in <math|X> are
  exactly <math|J>, where <math|J> is nonempty, and every nonzero group has
  <math|o\<in\>X>. Use the reserved forced slot at <math|<around|(|X,J|)>>
  and split the grouped function into real and imaginary parts. An array with
  total weighted majorant <math|A> therefore has a finite elementary
  representation of its profile, with ordinary part zero and price at most
  <math|2*A>. The exact inventory condition is preserved under grouping. This
  construction uses only finitely many pairs even when the original array has
  infinitely many serial addresses.

  We apply this compression to an elementary insertion <math|M> at a fine
  scale, with mass <math|m=<math-up|cost><around|(|M|)>\<geqslant\>0>. Denote
  its block transfer by <math|\<cal-T\>> and the forced derivative fixed in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:projection-payment-producer> by
  <math|\<cal-D\>*M>. The exact differentiated expansion gives an ordinary
  sum and this forced derivative. Its nonprincipal ordinary mass is at most
  <math|p<rsub|M>>; its possible principal term has mass at most
  <math|E<rsub|\<ast\>>*m>; and its forced mass is at most <math|q<rsub|M>>.
  The local derivative and support estimates give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-elementary-compression-bills>2*p<rsub|M>\<leqslant\>m,<space|2em>2*q<rsub|M>\<leqslant\>m,<space|2em>E<rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>L.
  </equation>

  Here <math|E<rsub|\<ast\>>> is the fixed exponential charge for enlarging
  the inherited support. The last inequality is the geometric block-length
  floor in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:projection-payment-producer>. More
  precisely, <math|p<rsub|M>> is the nonprincipal regular bill of that
  construction, and <math|q<rsub|M>> is its forced derivative bill before
  division by the clipping gain. Put <math|\<kappa\>=<sqrt|<text|coarse
  forced allowance>>> and let <math|H<rsub|0>> be the degree-zero splitting
  factor. Its explicit form is

  <\equation*>
    H<rsub|0>=1+2<around|\||v<rsub|0>|\|>*<around|(|1+C<rsub|<math-up|orb>>+<around|\||f<rsub|0>|\|>|)>\<geqslant\>1,<space|2em>c<rsub|0>=L<rsup|-2/5>/H<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>1,
  </equation*>

  where <math|v<rsub|0>,f<rsub|0>> are the fixed finite local-splitting
  constants and <math|C<rsub|<math-up|orb>>\<geqslant\>0> is its motion
  price. The two degree-zero payments are exactly

  <\equation*>
    2*p<rsub|M>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|0>*m,<space|2em>2*\<kappa\><rsup|-1>*q<rsub|M>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|0>*m,<space|2em>0\<less\>\<kappa\>\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  They imply the first two inequalities in
  <eqref|eq:ope-elementary-compression-bills>. This uses the same unscaled
  forced derivative as the profile identity. The principal contribution is
  absent for a forced input. For an ordinary input its masked value is at
  most its original height: the single-insertion, order-zero cluster has no
  additional insertion factor. Enlarging its support therefore costs
  precisely <math|E<rsub|\<ast\>>> times its original mass. All remaining
  clusters are charged to <math|p<rsub|M>>.

  The compression just proved gives a finite elementary representation of the
  transferred ordinary part and the profile of <math|\<cal-D\>*M> with price
  at most

  <\equation*>
    2*<around|(|p<rsub|M>+E<rsub|\<ast\>>*m|)>+2*q<rsub|M>\<leqslant\><around|(|2+2*L|)>*m\<leqslant\>6*L*m,
  </equation*>

  where <math|L\<geqslant\>1>. Its inserted density is exactly
  <math|\<cal-T\>> applied to the density of <math|M>, by the differentiated
  block identity. Thus the profile as well as the density is retained.

  This conclusion extends to every finite representation
  <eqref|eq:ope-finite-elementary>. Apply it to each <math|M<rsub|j>> and
  multiply its output coefficients by <math|z<rsub|j>>. Concatenating the
  finite lists gives total price at most <math|6*L*A>. The constant term
  remains <math|p> because <math|\<cal-T\>*\<rho\><rsub|f>=\<rho\><rsub|c>>.
  Finite sums commute with integration: each elementary density is integrable
  under the lawful representation, and the transition is the already
  constructed kernel. The output profile is exactly
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|j>z<rsub|j>*\<cal-P\>*<around|(|\<cal-D\>*M<rsub|j>|)>>.
  This proves the transport bound needed when a grouped observable is carried
  through intermediate scales.

  <subsubsection|Several insertions and their common allowances>

  At adjacent levels <math|m+1,m> set

  <\equation*>
    x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>,<space|1em>y=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>,<space|1em>L=L<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>,<space|1em>N=N<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>,<space|1em>\<delta\><rsub|m>=y<rsup|-2>,<space|1em>\<sigma\><rsub|m>=y<rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|N<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1> is the nondecreasing integer occupancy
  schedule fixed in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-observable-purchases>,
  so <math|N> is the purchased occupancy bound at the stage
  <math|J*<around|(|m+1|)>> of the incoming level. Let <math|\<chi\><rsub|m>>
  be the forced-gas price from the two successive absorptions, with its
  actual positive suppression constant and this block length. It is the price
  denoted by <math|t> in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:finite-menu-history-handoff>.
  The single-insertion and arbitrary finite-menu estimates of that section
  use this same <math|\<cal-D\>>, with input support exponent <math|96>,
  ordinary output exponent <math|100+1/2>, and forced output exponent
  <math|101>. Their output allowances are respectively <math|1> and
  <math|\<chi\><rsub|m>>.

  To apply them to several slots in each replica cell, take the finite marker
  set to be the product <math|V\<times\>S> of the cell set <math|V> and its
  slot set <math|S>, with a fine site <math|o<around|(|v,\<varsigma\>|)>> for
  every marker. The compiler of the finite-menu construction accepts any
  finite marker set, but its estimates are conditional on the hypotheses of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:finite-menu-history-handoff>, and these must
  now hold for the whole product set:

  <\enumerate>
    <item>every marker weight <math|r*<around|(|v,\<varsigma\>|)>> is
    positive;

    <item>the parent-site occupancy is at most <math|N>: for every coarse
    site <math|y>, at most <math|N> markers
    <math|<around|(|v,\<varsigma\>|)>\<in\>V\<times\>S> have
    <math|<math-up|parent><around|(|o<around|(|v,\<varsigma\>|)>|)>=y>;

    <item>the list of insertions is a menu offer for <math|V\<times\>S>, with
    the positions <math|o> and the weights <math|r>: the named sites of each
    insertion lie in its fine crest, and the offer bound holds at exponent
    <math|b<rsub|f>-4> and allowance <math|u>.
  </enumerate>

  Condition<nbsp>(2) counts every pair (cell, slot), so it does not follow
  from an occupancy bound for the cells alone, and enlarging the slot sets
  can violate it at fixed <math|N>. Nothing in the regrouping argument below
  supplies (2) or (3); they are hypotheses on the marker set and its offer,
  checked by the caller with the purchased value
  <math|N=N<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>> and the exponents just stated. The
  ordinary coefficient is indexed by individual markers and the forced
  coefficients by their subsets. Grouping by the cell coordinate is exactly
  the substitution used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-grouping-algebra>.
  The quiet coefficient stays the actual background profile. Before grouping,
  the singleton coefficient for each individual marker (cell, slot) is the
  sum of the derivatives of its original insertions through <math|\<cal-D\>>.
  After grouping, the coefficient of a singleton cell is the old coefficient
  at that cell's full slot set, by the definition of the regrouping map in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-grouping-algebra>. For example, with two
  slots, the coefficient of <math|e<rsub|<around|{|1,2|}>>> in <math|exp
  <around|(|z<rsub|1>*e<rsub|<around|{|1|}>>+z<rsub|2>*e<rsub|<around|{|2|}>>|)>>
  is <math|z<rsub|1>*z<rsub|2>>; this is the resulting singleton-cell
  coefficient. The motion identities follow by applying the same permutation
  to the cell coordinate and leaving the slot coordinate fixed; equal weights
  on corresponding slots preserve every weighted estimate. Thus increasing
  the number of slots does not change the background transition or its
  insertion transport.

  Write <math|B=B<rsub|m>> and <math|\<alpha\>=100+1/2>. The enlarged-support
  bound of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-grouping> gives the local price

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ope-history-cluster-price>s<rsub|m>=2*<around|(|1+N|)>*\<delta\><rsub|m>*<around|(|N*e<rsup|\<alpha\>>+1|)>+<around|(|1+N|)>*\<chi\><rsub|m>.
  </equation>

  The two copies in its first term are the healthy and unhealthy pieces of
  the ordinary grouped entry. The last term is the forced entry. The
  purchases in <eqref|eq:projection-occupancy-payments> give, simultaneously,

  <\gather>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|0\<less\>\<delta\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>\<chi\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em><around|(|2*B<rsub|m>+1|)><rsup|4>*s<rsub|m>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>,<space|2em>e<rsup|s<rsub|m>>-1\<leqslant\>1,<eq-number><label|eq:ope-history-smallness>>>|<row|<cell|\<alpha\>*<around|(|2048*B<rsub|m>+1|)><rsup|4>\<leqslant\><sqrt|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>>.<eq-number><label|eq:ope-history-support-cost>>>>>
  </gather>

  The allowance for the elementary degree-<math|d> projection is
  <math|X<rsub|m>=exp <around|(|<sqrt|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>>|)>\<geqslant\>1>,
  by <eqref|eq:projection-large-allowance>. Both that projection and the
  <math|6*L> elementary transport use the same <math|\<cal-D\>> just
  identified.

  <subsubsection|Uniformity above the reference level>

  For each grade <math|d> retain the reference level <math|t<rsub|d>> with
  <math|d+3\<leqslant\>J<around|(|t<rsub|d>|)>>; such a level exists because
  <math|J<around|(|m|)>\<to\>\<infty\>> (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection>).
  If <math|m\<geqslant\>t<rsub|d>>, monotonicity gives
  <math|d\<leqslant\>J<around|(|m|)>>. The numerical purchases were made for
  every degree up to the predecessor stage, and for every permitted adjacent
  pair of stages, before the scale sequence was realized. Hence the
  degree-<math|d> projection is available at every such <math|m>, for every
  cutoff <math|N<rsub|0>\<gtr\>m> and every period. The constants in
  <eqref|eq:ope-history-smallness>\U<eqref|eq:ope-history-support-cost>
  depend only on <math|m>, not on <math|N<rsub|0>> or the period.

  The adjacent transition retained by the selected history is the one at the
  chosen point of its interval family. Substituting the equalities
  identifying its fine and coarse representations with the corresponding
  history entries changes none of the formulas above. Consequently the
  ordinary menu estimates, the several-slot estimates, the finite elementary
  transport, and the final local projection hold together on that history.
  These supply the local hypotheses used in
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-local-projection> and
  <reference|sec:ope-grouping>. The later continuum limiting and cutoff
  arguments use their previously established estimates; they require no new
  choice of background history.

  <subsection|Retaining the selected data through the final
  construction><label|sec:public-run-binding>

  We finish the selection argument by specifying which common data are
  retained. The issue is the order of the choices: the local estimates, the
  numerical purchases and the limiting observables must all refer to one
  family. The analytic hypotheses used here are those of
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:common-funded-history>,
  <reference|sec:common-observable-purchases> and
  <reference|sec:common-scale-selection>. This argument identifies their
  common application; it supplies no replacement for an outstanding local
  estimate.

  Fix a simple gauge group <math|G> and a positive kernel penalty <math|b>.
  Use the profile construction with support exponent exactly <math|100> at
  every stage, derivative order at least <math|6+j> and spatial power at
  least <math|2+j> at stage <math|j>, as in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-scale-purchases>. Denote the selected
  kernel by <math|K>, its stage profiles by <math|\<pi\><rsub|j>>, and its
  primary and secondary row-price families by <math|P> and
  <math|P<rprime|'>>. The flat-step, curvature-quadratic symmetry and
  dual-support estimates select their floors from these data before the block
  lengths are chosen. Take their maximum together with the original,
  comparison, treasury and period floors, the marked-portfolio thresholds and
  the drift floors of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection>,
  and call it <math|n<rsub|j>>. Raising the floor to <math|n<rsub|j>> changes
  neither <math|K> nor <math|\<pi\><rsub|j>> nor <math|P>. Select block
  lengths <math|L<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>n<rsub|j>> and retain all these data
  when applying the scheduling construction. The marked portfolio, with its
  sections and regradings, was fixed before the profiles' price maps and the
  floors (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-observable-purchases>); the
  run retains exactly that portfolio, and no section or regrading is chosen
  after the schedule.

  Let <math|J<around|(|m|)>> be the resulting stage clock,
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>> its hatted coupling, and <math|H> its
  quadratic horizon. Its actual block at step <math|m> has length
  <math|L<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>>. For clarity, the two kinds of numerical
  payment are different. If <math|U<rsub|E,j>\<gtr\>0> is the already
  selected width at fixed index <math|E>, the countable purchase is

  <\equation*>
    \<forall\>E\<exists\>m<rsub|E>\<forall\>m\<geqslant\>m<rsub|E>\<forall\>j\<leqslant\>J*<around|(|m+1|)>:<space|1em>U<rsub|E,j><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>1+log
    log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>.
  </equation*>

  The same construction keeps <math|1\<leqslant\>1+log log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>> for all <math|m>: the countable purchase
  includes

  <\equation*>
    exp <around|(|exp E<rsub|j>|)>
  </equation*>

  with <math|E<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1> in every <math|d<rsub|j>>, so
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>\<geqslant\>\<beta\><rsub|\<ast\>>\<geqslant\>d<rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>>\<geqslant\>e<rsup|e>>
  and <math|log log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>\<geqslant\>1>. After the
  lengths are fixed, let <math|A<rsub|j><around|(|L|)>> and
  <math|B<rsub|j><around|(|L|)>> be the two actual post-length costs and set
  <math|F<rsub|j><around|(|L|)>=max <around|{|A<rsub|j><around|(|L|)>,B<rsub|j><around|(|L|)>|}>>.
  The all-stage purchase is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:public-native-allstage>F<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>><around|(|L|)>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>*<space|2em><around|(|m\<geqslant\>0|)>.
  </equation>

  The countable version is retained as well. In particular, an eventual
  statement for fixed <math|E> is not being substituted for the all-stage
  payment at <math|m=0>.

  Here is the verification that the local suppliers apply to <math|H> itself.
  The selected levels have kernel <math|K> and stage profiles
  <math|\<pi\><rsub|j>>; the horizon satisfies the row prices <math|P>. At
  step <math|m>, each component floor is at most
  <math|n<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>>.
  The actual patch and the patch used in computing
  <math|F<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>><around|(|L|)>> agree, because a patch is
  determined by its stage and block length. Thus
  <eqref|eq:public-native-allstage> pays both actual costs. In the cost that
  has the form <math|e<rsup|a<rsub|j>>>, its payment gives
  <math|a<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>> by monotonicity
  of the logarithm. The other payment supplies the flat-step delay. The entry
  delays of the run are the final thresholds <math|d<rsub|j>> of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection>; they contain the
  terminal fee threshold, the marked-portfolio delay, the startup and reset
  delays and the loop-capacity thresholds listed there, so every schedule
  condition of Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-marked-portfolio-calibration>,
  <reference|sec:surviving-interval-shooting> and
  <reference|sec:path-attachment-producer> that is stated as an entry delay
  holds for this run's delays. The flat, dual-support and curvature-quadratic
  symmetry suppliers therefore apply with the original levels and <math|H>,
  at every allowed cutoff and level. No new horizon is chosen in this
  application.

  Now retain the finite-volume history, its campaign and the transition
  witnesses obtained from this one construction. Denote them by <math|R>,
  <math|T> and <math|W>, respectively. Here <math|R> names the full selected
  history and scale data, <math|T> names its campaign of parameter paths, and
  <math|W> keeps the transition and finite-volume witnesses for that
  campaign. The family <math|C<rsub|R>> is the family of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:original-history-portfolio-attachment>. In
  addition to the fixed penalty <math|b>, keep the treasury and common-period
  estimates and the order and power inequalities when making this
  simultaneous choice. They are conclusions of the selection before the
  history is packaged. One may retain all of them with the same existential
  witness; forgetting them in a shorter statement does not prove that they
  can be recovered from its remaining hypotheses.

  There are also two distinct support-exponent facts. The stage parameters
  used by the operator-product construction have exponent exactly <math|100>.
  The larger budget used to bound local functions has support exponent at
  least <math|100>. The first fact follows from the fixed profile under
  reindexing by <math|J>; the second is a budget bound. Neither requires
  changing the selected history or replacing its profile by an increasing
  support-exponent profile.

  The funded continuation is now evaluated at the history, reference
  schedule, local sections, transition data and finite-volume witnesses
  stored in <math|R>; its portfolio sections and regradings are the ones
  fixed before the schedule, as above. Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-original-history-attachment>
  identifies its output family with <math|C<rsub|R>>. Its trace and coverage
  conclusions consequently concern that same family. For the operator-product
  estimates, keep <math|W> as well as <math|R,T>: the finite transition
  witnesses enter the service of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-history-service>.
  The fixed exponent, common clock, halo and logarithmic estimates are those
  just selected. Thus the common-history, observable and operator-product
  applications are simultaneous applications of their canonical arguments.

  <subparagraph|The public family of this retained history.> Write
  <math|\<beta\><rsup|R><rsub|N,p,m>> for the actual coupling of the stored
  history at level <math|m\<leqslant\>N>, cutoff <math|N> and period index
  <math|p>. Let <math|P<rsub|p>> be its selected physical periods. To
  distinguish levels from stages, put

  <\equation*>
    \<ell\><rsub|m>=L<rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>,<space|2em>D<rsub|0>=1,<space|2em>D<rsub|N>=<big|prod><rsub|m=0><rsup|N-1>\<ell\><rsub|m>.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>> is the block used in the transition from level
  <math|m+1> to level <math|m>; it is not the stage length <math|L<rsub|m>>
  unless those indices agree. All these data belong to <math|R> and are fixed
  before an extraction order is requested. Define its public Wilson family by
  retaining these periods, using <math|P<rsub|p>*D<rsub|N>> sites on each
  axis, and setting

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:public-family-coordinates>a<rsub|N>=D<rsub|N><rsup|-1>=e<rsup|-s<rsub|N>>,<space|2em>\<beta\><rsup|<math-up|pub>><rsub|N,p>=\<beta\><rsup|R><rsub|N,p,N>.
  </equation>

  The Haar measure and the Wilson action are those of
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-lattice>, in the fixed representation of
  <math|G>.

  The insertion maps also retain the calibrated finite-volume data. For a
  grade <math|d> and cutoff-period pair <math|<around|(|N,p|)>>, let
  <math|A<rsup|R><rsub|d,N,p>> be the terminal linear isomorphism of the
  coefficient space stored in the transfer datum
  <math|S<rsub|R>*<around|(|d,N,p|)>>. Let
  <math|<math-up|Init><rsup|R><rsub|d,N,p><around|(|w;x;U|)>> be its initial
  local-section observable, at site <math|x> and configuration <math|U>, with
  coefficient <math|w>. Here is its exact normalization. In the fixed
  grade-<math|d> coefficient frame, let <math|c<rsub|d,\<alpha\>>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
  be the colour-factor count of coordinate <math|\<alpha\>>, and define the
  linear dilation <math|<around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|d><around|(|t|)>*w|)><rsub|\<alpha\>>=t<rsup|c<rsub|d,\<alpha\>>>*w<rsub|\<alpha\>>>.
  Write <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|N>\<gtr\>0> for the selected threshold and
  <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|R><rsub|d>*<around|(|w;x;U|)>> for placement of the
  fixed local section with coefficient <math|w> at <math|x>. Then

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|Init><rsup|R><rsub|d,N,p><around|(|w;x;U|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>*\<cal-L\><rsup|R><rsub|d>*<around*|(|\<Delta\><rsub|d><around|(|\<vartheta\><rsub|N><rsup|-1>|)>*w;x;U|)>.
  </equation*>

  The dilation exponent is the coordinate's colour-factor count, whereas the
  physical prefactor uses the grade <math|d>. These are the initial
  observables used in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-centered-service>.
  The public token <math|<around|(|d,v|)>> uses the same coefficient space
  and has insertion

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:public-family-insertion>O<rsup|<math-up|pub>><rsub|N,p,<around|(|d,v|)>><around|(|x;U|)>=<math-up|Init><rsup|R><rsub|d,N,p><around*|(|<around|(|A<rsup|R><rsub|d,N,p>|)><rsup|-1>*v;x;U|)>.
  </equation>

  In particular the terminal inverse is part of the insertion calibration;
  the token <math|v> is not substituted directly for its initial coefficient.
  The formulas define the insertions at every cutoff using the stored
  transfer datum there. Calibration and transport estimates are still
  asserted only in their stated domains, in particular
  <math|N\<geqslant\>t<rsub|R><around|(|d|)>> when the reference-level
  hypothesis is required.

  These identities follow from the retained construction, rather than from a
  new choice of family. The schedule starts with denominator one and
  multiplies it by its actual block <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>> at each level. Its
  scale recurrence is <math|s<rsub|0>=0>, <math|s<rsub|m+1>=s<rsub|m>+log
  \<ell\><rsub|m>>, so induction gives <math|s<rsub|N>=log D<rsub|N>> and the
  first identity in <eqref|eq:public-family-coordinates>. Since every
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>\<geqslant\>3>, the positive integer denominators
  strictly increase. The stored history starts at cutoff <math|N> with bare
  Wilson density of coefficient <math|\<beta\><rsup|R><rsub|N,p,N>> and zero
  vacuum parameter; this gives the second identity. Finally, the finite
  insertion assigned by the transfer datum is, by its definition, the initial
  local-section observable after its terminal inverse. This is exactly
  <eqref|eq:public-family-insertion>. Thus, for every <math|N,p>, every
  finite token tuple and every tuple of lattice sites, the public finite
  moment is the moment of these same insertions under the same normalized
  Wilson law of <math|C<rsub|R>>. Neither a limiting identification nor a
  replacement by the reference coupling <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>> is
  used.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The normalized observables of the retained
    family><label|prop:public-normalized-observables>On the retained family
    <math|C<rsub|R>>, the sections and insertions in
    <eqref|eq:public-family-insertion> are the sections and insertions of
    <eqref|eq:headline-jet-normalization>\U<eqref|eq:headline-insertion>,
    with <math|r<rsub|d>=t<rsub|R><around|(|d|)>>,
    <math|R<rsub|d,N,p>=A<rsup|R><rsub|d,N,p>> and
    <math|D<rsub|\<theta\><rsub|N><rsup|-1>>=\<Delta\><rsub|d><around|(|\<vartheta\><rsub|N><rsup|-1>|)>>.
    The sections have one fixed finite stencil at each grade, are polynomial,
    linear in the labels, gauge invariant, unit preserving and compatible
    with conjugation and signed lattice symmetries. Their jets obey
    <eqref|eq:headline-jet-normalization>. For
    <math|N\<geqslant\>t<rsub|R><around|(|d|)>> the unnormalized inserted
    density has exact transport along the stored block integrations; its
    reference coefficient is the specified label <math|v>, and its adjacent
    remainders obey the cone and charge bounds of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-music>. At grade <math|d> the
    physical unit is the reference label <math|a<rsub|t<rsub|R><around|(|d|)>><rsup|d>*e<rsub|0>>,
    where <math|e<rsub|0>> is the constant polynomial. Every assertion uses
    the same history, not a separately selected regular Wilson family.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The local-section construction in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-sections>
    chooses one finite jet sampler, one polynomial recovery map and its
    finite signed-symmetry average before the period or threshold is chosen.
    Its Taylor identity proves <eqref|eq:headline-jet-normalization> on every
    radial jet. The finite union of the averaged stencils is the required
    fixed stencil; the polynomial and covariance assertions are proved there
    on that very union. The portfolio in the present selection retains these
    sections before choosing its profiles and floors, so its local section
    <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|R><rsub|d>> is this section, rather than a new right
    inverse.

    The formula for <math|<math-up|Init><rsup|R>> and
    <eqref|eq:public-family-insertion> give the headline insertion by
    substitution. The inverse <math|A<rsup|R><rsub|d,N,p>> is not selected
    for this identity. It is the reference-normalized terminal readout of the
    stored finite transfer. Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-c02-selected-record>
    identifies that transfer with the single backward family of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:selected-music>, normalized once by its
    reference readout. Thus its unnormalized density push, its coefficient
    readout and both remainder bounds all hold for the same insertion. At
    each step the grade factor is the one in its physical transport map.
    Composing the finitely many identities from <math|N> to
    <math|t<rsub|R><around|(|d|)>> gives the exact reference transport, with
    no division by a partition function at an intermediate level.

    At the physical unit, the same adjacent maps carry
    <math|a<rsub|m+1><rsup|d>*e<rsub|0>> to
    <math|a<rsub|m><rsup|d>*e<rsub|0>> and their error and charge vanish.
    Their finite composition therefore carries the terminal physical unit to
    <math|a<rsub|t<rsub|R><around|(|d|)>><rsup|d>*e<rsub|0>>. Reference
    normalization gives its initial observable identically one. This also
    explains why the same constant polynomial at two different grades cannot
    be identified without the reference-scale factor. All the constructions
    in this proof were fixed on <math|R> before a correlation tuple, period
    subsequence or extraction order was requested.
  </proof>

  <subparagraph|Running of the public bare coefficient.> The histories just
  retained are the histories constructed by
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:surviving-interval-shooting>: their
  level-zero value is <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>>, their reset shift and
  stationary scalar obey the adjacent estimate with
  <math|s<rsub|G>=2*b<rsub|0><around|(|G|)>>, and their scale is
  <math|s<rsub|m>> above. The drift floor in
  Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection> gives
  <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|m+1>/log \<ell\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>J*<around|(|m+1|)><rsup|-2>>
  and <math|J<around|(|m|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>. Consequently the running
  estimate of that section holds uniformly for these very coefficients
  <math|\<beta\><rsup|R><rsub|N,p,m>>, all <math|N\<geqslant\>m> and all
  <math|p>. There is no additional running hypothesis on <math|R> here.

  Both identifications required by Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:shoot-public-running>
  are precisely <eqref|eq:public-family-coordinates>. Applying that
  proposition therefore gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:public-family-running>\<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0\<exists\>N<rsub|0>\<forall\>N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>\<forall\>p:<space|1em><around*|\||<frac|\<beta\><rsup|<math-up|pub>><rsub|N,p>|log
    <around|(|a<rsub|N><rsup|-1>|)>>-2*b<rsub|0><around|(|G|)>|\|>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>.
  </equation>

  This is <eqref|ex-running-input>, with the value of the slope fixed by
  <eqref|eq:shoot-slope>. The denominator is positive on the tail because
  <math|s<rsub|N>\<geqslant\>N*log 3>. The coefficient used here is the
  actual terminal bare coefficient, not the comparison value
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>>. No coupling defined through a continuum
  physical observable is being identified with it.

  Finally this binding persists through the ordered extractions. Given an
  initial extraction order <math|f>, first refine it to obtain the coupling
  limits, then refine again to obtain the field limits. If the two
  refinements are <math|g,w>, their combined order is
  <math|f\<circ\>g\<circ\>w>, with the corresponding ordered period
  refinements. Every coupling limit survives the second refinement, by the
  defining limit property of a refinement. The phase, trace and
  operator-product constructions are applied to the resulting single limiting
  table and its single reconstructed system. Full-mesh convergence
  additionally uses the full-mesh estimates on <math|C<rsub|R>>; it is not
  inferred from the existence of a mesh subsequence. These operations
  preserve the selected history and its purchases while making the further
  choices required by the limiting construction.

  <subsection|The final assembly on one Wilson
  construction><label|ex-assembly>

  The final assembly must use one hierarchy throughout. An operator-product
  expansion for one subsequential limit and a reconstruction for another
  would not establish the desired result. We first state its operator-product
  and trace requirements, attach the proved trace result to that hierarchy,
  and then assemble the normalized Wilson family, all of its character
  reconstructions and its physical stress on the same limiting data.

  <subsubsection|The operator-product and trace requirements>

  Let <math|\<b-A\>=<around|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|i\<in\>I>> and
  <math|\<b-B\>=<around|(|B<rsub|j>|)><rsub|j\<in\>J>> be finite lists of
  graded fields and write <math|w<around|(|\<b-A\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>deg
  A<rsub|i>>. For each grade <math|\<ell\>>, let
  <math|<around|(|E<rsub|\<ell\>,\<alpha\>>|)><rsub|\<alpha\>\<in\>\<cal-C\><rsub|\<ell\>>>>
  be a finite collection of fields. The coefficients
  <math|c<rsub|\<b-A\>><rsup|\<ell\>,\<alpha\>>*<around|(|\<b-x\>;o|)>> do
  not depend on the phase. The cluster geometry at scale <math|r\<gtr\>0> is

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||x<rsub|i>-o|\|>\<leqslant\>M*r,<space|2em><around|\||x<rsub|i>-x<rsub|i<rprime|'>>|\|>\<geqslant\>\<eta\>*r*<space|1em><around|(|i\<ne\>i<rprime|'>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|M\<geqslant\>0> and <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>. Spectators satisfy
  <math|<around|\||y<rsub|j>-o|\|>\<geqslant\>c> and
  <math|<around|\||y<rsub|j>-y<rsub|j<rprime|'>>|\|>\<geqslant\>c> for
  <math|j\<ne\>j<rprime|'>>, where <math|c\<gtr\>0>. For every
  <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, each fixed coefficient obeys

  <\equation>
    <label|ex-ope-coefficient><around|\||c<rsub|\<b-A\>><rsup|\<ell\>,\<alpha\>>*<around|(|\<b-x\>;o|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>r<rsup|\<ell\>-w<around|(|\<b-A\>|)>-\<epsilon\>>
  </equation>

  at sufficiently small <math|r>, uniformly in the indicated cluster
  geometry. For each fixed truncation degree <math|D>, the expansion requires

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<mid|\|>S<rsup|s><rsub|\<b-A\>,\<b-B\>>*<around|(|\<b-x\>,\<b-y\>|)>-<big|sum><rsub|\<ell\>=0><rsup|D><big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>\<in\>\<cal-C\><rsub|\<ell\>>>c<rsub|\<b-A\>><rsup|\<ell\>,\<alpha\>>*<around|(|\<b-x\>;o|)>*S<rsup|s><rsub|E<rsub|\<ell\>,\<alpha\>>,\<b-B\>>*<around|(|o,\<b-y\>|)><mid|\|>\<leqslant\>r<rsup|D+1-w<around|(|\<b-A\>|)>-\<epsilon\>>.<eq-number><label|ex-ope-remainder>>>>>
  </align>

  Here <math|s> can be any character or the mixed state. The small-radius
  threshold for the remainder is uniform over these choices and over the
  allowed positions, but may depend on the fields,
  <math|D,M,\<eta\>,c,\<epsilon\>>. There is no assertion of one threshold
  working for all truncation degrees. The quantitative passage from the local
  construction to these coefficient and remainder estimates is
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ope-quantitative>, applied to one prescription
  and its one limiting field hierarchy. Its hypotheses, including the matched
  fields and the phase estimates, must hold for the hierarchy used here; the
  numerical purchases alone do not establish them.

  The trace requirement concerns a centered grade-four field
  <math|\<Theta\>>. Set

  <\equation*>
    T<around|(|r|)>=S<rsup|\<chi\><rsub|\<ast\>>><rsub|\<Theta\>,\<Theta\>>*<around|(|<around|(|\<b-0\>,-r/2|)>,<around|(|\<b-0\>,r/2|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Its vacuum one-point functional vanishes, and the required asymptotic is

  <\equation>
    <label|ex-trace-limit>lim<rsub|r*\<downarrow\>*0>
    r<rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|r<rsup|-1>|)>*T<around|(|r|)>=<frac|3*dim<rsub|\<bbb-R\>>
    \<frak-g\>|\<pi\><rsup|4>>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation>

  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:profile-exclusion> shows that this asymptotic
  implies the required exclusion of finite complex linear combinations of
  mass-shell tracks, including empty products. The class of profiles and
  their exact normalization are given in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:trace-profiles>.
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:trace-selected-endpoint> supplies this entire
  trace requirement, including the zero vacuum one-point functional and the
  precise profile exclusion, for the selected funded hierarchy with its own
  phase reconstruction and normalized physical stress. Its <math|\<Theta\>>
  and <math|\<chi\><rsub|\<ast\>>> are the ones just used to define <math|T>:
  orthogonal covariance identifies the radial correlation with this time-axis
  pair, whose separation is <math|r>. The hierarchy and extraction are fixed
  before the arbitrary positive null sequence used in that theorem. Thus the
  trace assertion introduces no further subsequence of the hierarchy or new
  normalization of its grade-four field. This discharges the trace part of
  item<nbsp>3 below whenever the joint construction uses these canonical
  data; the operator-product and other joint-construction clauses retain
  their separate suppliers.

  <subsubsection|The phase mixture on joint boundary tests>

  The mixture identity for separated Euclidean correlations must also hold
  for the joint tempered boundary distributions. It is not enough to verify
  it on products of one-variable tests and then use pointwise convergence.
  Here the uniform slab bounds supply the needed common domination.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Boundary barycenter><label|prop:assembly-boundary-barycenter> Use
    one mixed table and its character tables from
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-selection>, with their common field
    union and the slab bounds of Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-slab-bounds>.
    Let <math|W<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|mix>>> and
    <math|W<rsub|n><rsup|\<chi\>>> be the joint boundary distributions
    constructed from those tables by Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:joint-boundary>.
    For every fixed finite field tuple <math|\<b-v\>> and every joint
    Schwartz test <math|f\<in\><with|math-font|cal*|S><around|(|<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>|)>>,
    the map <math|\<chi\>\<mapsto\>W<rsub|n><rsup|\<chi\>><around|(|\<b-v\>|)><around|(|f|)>>
    is continuous and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:assembly-boundary-barycenter>W<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|mix>><around|(|\<b-v\>|)><around|(|f|)>=<big|int><rsub|X>W<rsub|n><rsup|\<chi\>><around|(|\<b-v\>|)><around|(|f|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<chi\>|)>.
    </equation>

    More generally, character continuity and the mixture identity hold for
    the tested continuation at each fixed weakly ordered height tuple. The
    same constants bound all these distributions by one continuous Schwartz
    seminorm for the fixed tuple. No exceptional set of characters or further
    extraction is used.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    At arity zero every table and boundary functional is the constant one, so
    consider <math|n\<gtr\>0>. Fix the labels and the strictly ordered
    imaginary heights. The phase slab operators have the same norm bounds as
    the mixed operators. Applying the padding and matrix estimate of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:analytic-boundary> therefore gives a
    single exponent <math|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and constant <math|C> such that
    the continued scalar functions <math|F<rsub|\<chi\>>>, including the
    mixed one, satisfy the gap bound <eqref|eq:gap-power>. The bound is
    independent of <math|\<chi\>> and of the real positions. The same
    observation applies after any fixed finite list of label operations used
    in the continuation.

    We first verify the mixture identity in the strict interior. Fix real
    times <math|t<rsub|i>>, spatial positions <math|x<rsub|i>> and ordered
    heights <math|y<rsub|i>>. Along <math|z<rsub|i><around|(|c|)>=i*y<rsub|i>+c*t<rsub|i>>,
    the functions <math|F<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|z<around|(|c|)>,x;\<b-v\>|)>>
    are holomorphic in an open horizontal strip about the real axis: choose
    the strip so that the finitely many imaginary gaps remain positive. On
    each compact substrip they are bounded uniformly in <math|\<chi\>> by the
    gap estimate. For <math|c=i*\<tau\>> close to zero they are the separated
    Euclidean functions, which depend continuously on <math|\<chi\>> by their
    character representation. It follows that they depend continuously on
    <math|\<chi\>> throughout the strip. Here is the normal-family argument
    for this last step. Given <math|\<chi\><rsub|j>\<to\>\<chi\>>, Cauchy's
    formula on slightly larger discs gives uniform bounds for the derivatives
    of the functions on each compact disc. Arzelà\UAscoli and a diagonal
    subsequence over a countable exhaustion give a locally uniform
    holomorphic subsequential limit. On the imaginary interval it equals
    <math|F<rsub|\<chi\>>>, by the already known continuity there, and the
    one-variable identity theorem makes it equal to <math|F<rsub|\<chi\>>>
    everywhere. Every convergent subsequence has this limit; if convergence
    at a point failed, the violating subsequence would have a further locally
    uniform subsequence, a contradiction. Thus the full sequence converges.
    Metrizability of <math|X> turns this sequential assertion into
    continuity.

    The integral over <math|X> is holomorphic in <math|c>: the same compact
    bound permits interchanging the probability integral with contour
    integrals, and Cauchy's formula on a disc then proves holomorphy. At
    imaginary <math|c> close to zero, the Euclidean mixture identity
    identifies it with the mixed continued function. The identity theorem and
    <math|c=1> give

    <\equation*>
      F<rsub|<math-up|mix>>*<around|(|t+i*y,x;\<b-v\>|)>=<big|int><rsub|X>F<rsub|\<chi\>>*<around|(|t+i*y,x;\<b-v\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<chi\>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The integrands are measurable jointly in <math|\<chi\>> and the real
    positions: they are continuous in each variable separately, and on the
    separable position space the continuous-position functions are pointwise
    limits of their values on successively finer countable grids. This
    supplies Borel simple approximations in the position variable with
    measurable coefficients in <math|\<chi\>>. The fixed-height bound and
    <math|f\<in\>L<rsup|1>> permit Fubini, so the same identity holds for the
    tested functions <math|\<psi\><rsub|y><around|(|f|)>> on every strictly
    ordered height tuple.

    Use the same <math|m,C> and direction <math|r> in the boundary formula
    <eqref|eq:edge-taylor> for every character. For
    <math|0\<less\>s\<leqslant\>1> its integral remainder is bounded by
    <math|C*<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|m+1>*f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>/m>!,
    uniformly in the weakly ordered <math|y> and <math|\<chi\>>. Its finite
    sum is bounded by <math|C*<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|m><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|j>*f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>/j!>.
    Both are integrable with respect to the probability <math|\<nu\>>, and
    their sum is a continuous Schwartz seminorm, as proved in
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:joint-boundary>. Substitute the
    strict-interior mixture in this formula and apply Fubini to its
    absolutely dominated remainder. This proves the identity at every weakly
    ordered <math|y>; at <math|y=0> it is
    <eqref|eq:assembly-boundary-barycenter>. The same domination and the
    strict-interior character continuity proved above give continuity in
    <math|\<chi\>> of each finite term and of the remainder by dominated
    convergence. This proves the asserted character continuity and common
    seminorm bound directly on joint tests.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|A precise conditional assembly>

  An internal extraction order is a pair <math|<around|(|u,p|)>> of strictly
  increasing maps <math|\<bbb-N\>\<to\>\<bbb-N\>>. Refining by
  <math|<around|(|v,q|)>> gives <math|<around|(|u\<circ\>v,p\<circ\>q|)>>.
  The full mesh order is <math|u<around|(|N|)>=N>. The need for a full mesh
  limit at the public endpoint is stronger than convergence just along
  <math|u\<circ\>v>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Identifying the full mesh limit><label|ex-full-mesh> Fix a test
    and its insertion labels. Suppose that at every suitable period <math|p>
    the full sequence <math|W<rsub|N,p>> converges to some
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|p>>. Suppose also that an ordered extraction supplies,
    at every suitable period <math|p>, numbers <math|w<rsub|p>> such that
    <math|W<rsub|u<around|(|j|)>,p>\<to\>w<rsub|p>>, where <math|u> is
    strictly increasing; that the period <math|p<around|(|q<around|(|k|)>|)>>
    is suitable for all sufficiently large <math|k>; and that
    <math|w<rsub|p<around|(|q<around|(|k|)>|)>>\<to\>s>. Then
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|p>=w<rsub|p>> at every suitable period, and
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|p<around|(|q<around|(|k|)>|)>>\<to\>s>, that is, the
    same volume limit <math|s> holds with the full mesh limits.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Strict increase implies <math|u<around|(|j|)>\<geqslant\>j> by induction,
    so <math|u<around|(|j|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>. The full mesh convergence
    therefore implies <math|W<rsub|u<around|(|j|)>,p>\<to\>\<ell\><rsub|p>>.
    To see uniqueness explicitly, for any <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, take
    <math|j> sufficiently large that this term is within <math|\<epsilon\>/2>
    of each proposed limit. The triangle inequality gives
    <math|<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|p>-w<rsub|p>|\|>\<less\>\<epsilon\>>, hence
    equality. By hypothesis there is <math|k<rsub|0>> such that
    <math|p<around|(|q<around|(|k|)>|)>> is suitable for
    <math|k\<geqslant\>k<rsub|0>>; for those <math|k> the equality just
    proved gives <math|\<ell\><rsub|p<around|(|q<around|(|k|)>|)>>=w<rsub|p<around|(|q<around|(|k|)>|)>>>.
    The two sequences of row limits therefore agree from <math|k<rsub|0>> on
    and have the same limit <math|s>.
  </proof>

  This lemma explains how a full ultraviolet convergence result can be
  combined with a hierarchy constructed by subsequence extraction. It does
  not obtain full ultraviolet convergence from subsequential convergence:
  that is its additional hypothesis.

  <\assumption>
    <dueto|Joint construction input><label|ex-joint-input> For the fixed
    group <math|G> and each chosen positive blocking penalty <math|b>, there
    is one lattice family with the following properties. Its insertions are
    continuous, gauge-invariant and uniformly local, and its bare
    coefficients satisfy <eqref|ex-running-input>. For every extraction order
    <math|<around|(|u,p|)>>, there is a refinement <math|<around|(|v,q|)>>
    and one set of limiting data as described in
    Section<nbsp><reference|ex-statement> (graded fields with their
    injections, covering and token conditions, phase data consisting of a
    compact metric space with a probability measure of full support and a
    selected character, and phase and mixed correlations), together with a
    selected relativistic theory as in Definition<nbsp><reference|ex-reconstructed>,
    for which all of the following hold simultaneously:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>The mixed correlations satisfy <eqref|ex-row-target> along the
      <em|full> mesh sequence at every suitable period and
      <eqref|ex-volume-target> along <math|p\<circ\>q>.

      <item>The phase correlations have the stated smoothness, symmetry,
      continuity and mixture identity <eqref|ex-mixture>.

      <item>The fields and coefficients satisfy
      <eqref|ex-ope-coefficient>--<eqref|ex-ope-remainder> and the centered
      field satisfies <eqref|ex-trace-limit>.
    </enumerate>

    Every assertion refers to the same lattice family and the same selected
    hierarchy on that refinement.
  </assumption>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Assembly from the joint input><label|ex-conditional-existence>
    Input<nbsp><reference|ex-joint-input> implies the full existence target
    stated in Section<nbsp><reference|ex-statement>, including the
    operator-product and trace properties above.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Fix <math|G> and choose <math|b=1>. Select the family supplied by
    Input<nbsp><reference|ex-joint-input> once. Its regularity is part of
    that input and is unaffected by any subsequent choice of volume sequence.
    Now let <math|p:\<bbb-N\>\<to\>\<bbb-N\>> be an arbitrary strictly
    increasing sequence. Apply the input to the extraction order
    <math|<around|(|<math-up|id>,p|)>>. It yields <math|<around|(|v,q|)>> and
    one hierarchy with all three simultaneous properties. Take <math|q> as
    the public volume subsequence and retain this hierarchy. Its ordered
    Wilson convergence uses the full mesh sequence by item<nbsp>1, so no
    assertion about a mesh subsequence has been substituted for
    <eqref|ex-row-target>. Item<nbsp>2 gives the phase and mixture
    conditions; the selected theory is part of the same data. Item<nbsp>3
    supplies the remaining two properties, with finite-profile exclusion
    following from Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:profile-exclusion>. The
    construction of the family preceded the choice of <math|p>, while
    <math|q> and the hierarchy were chosen after <math|p>, exactly as
    required.
  </proof>

  The proposition isolates the elementary logical projection to the public
  target. The following assembly retains the additional normalization and
  every-character assertions of the stronger headline. The all-period
  supplier of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:fullmesh-selected-supplier>
  applies to the retained family itself.

  <subsubsection|Assembly of the construction-bound theorem>

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Assembly of the retained construction><label|thm:ex-construction-assembly>The
    selected construction of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:public-run-binding>
    satisfies Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-construction-headline>. In
    particular it supplies Input<nbsp><reference|ex-joint-input>, and hence
    the public existence target. The lattice family is fixed before the
    requested extraction order; all limiting fields, phase decompositions,
    boundary distributions, local products and the physical stress in the
    conclusion are constructed from one limiting table on one refinement of
    that order.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Fix <math|G> and the positive blocking penalty <math|b>. We describe the
    choices in their order, then verify the four headline clauses.

    <em|The finite family.> The local sections are chosen by
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:local-sections>. The common funded-history
    and observable purchases are made before realizing the schedule: profiles
    and floors precede lengths; local prices and protection precede the final
    entry delays; the realized clock and periods come last. The maxima in
    these choices are finite at each stage, and subsequent enlargements
    preserve every previously purchased lower bound.
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:common-scale-selection> therefore realizes
    all the requests with one schedule. The annulus estimate and the retained
    path construction apply to its one free horizon. The selected nonlinear
    return and the exact Wilson startup then have their hypotheses on these
    paths and their original quadratic data.
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:surviving-interval-shooting> chooses
    finite histories on every cutoff and period without changing that
    schedule. The common-data identification of
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:public-run-binding> retains these
    histories together with their transition witnesses and one marked
    portfolio.

    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-c02-selected-record> places the
    complete service record on that history, with its stored transitions and
    one reference-normalized transfer. It is important to retain this
    transfer, not merely the assertion that some valid transfer exists. The
    reference normalization and the physical unit are exactly those of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:public-normalized-observables>. Thus the
    public family has the sections, the jet normalization and the exact
    inserted density transport claimed in the headline. Its finite law is the
    normalized Wilson law, by <eqref|eq:public-family-coordinates>; its bare
    coefficient satisfies <eqref|eq:public-family-running>. All of these
    choices have been made before an extraction order is mentioned.

    <em|One table and its field quotient.> Now prescribe strictly increasing
    mesh and period maps <math|<around|(|u,p|)>>. First retain the coupling
    and stationary limits along a refinement, as in
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:selected-history-limits>.
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:fullmesh-selected-supplier> supplies the
    cap, sweep and primary budgets for the actual retained insertions, with
    one mesh threshold valid for every period. Thus the full-mesh bounds of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-fullmesh-fixedbounds-producer> and the
    common-history Cauchy argument of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:fullmesh-correlation-cauchy>
    apply to the same inserted densities.
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|ex-smooth-grid-limit> gives their full mesh
    limits at every suitable fixed period. Separately,
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-limits-extraction> and the
    finite-dimensional extractions in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:finite-rank-kernel-transport>
    give a common refinement for the table and all finite-grade projection
    data. There are countably many grades, basis signatures and compact
    exhaustion sets. The common diagonal extraction is made on these data;
    multilinearity and the compact estimates then give the conclusions for
    every label and test, rather than choosing a further subsequence for each
    test. All previously obtained limits persist under this refinement.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-full-mesh> identifies every extracted row with
    its full mesh limit. Hence the mixed correlation formula has exactly the
    order <eqref|eq:headline-wilson-limit>: full mesh at fixed suitable
    period, then the chosen refinement of the prescribed period sequence.

    The denominator, insertion and reflection identities are finite
    identities of that same family. Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-limits-euclidean>
    passes them to its table, with finite smeared positivity supplied by
    Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:ex-lattice-smeared-reflection>. Smoothness
    and the local bounds pass by the smooth-limit and centered-moment
    estimates. Take the null space of all separated mixed correlations with
    arbitrary finite spectators, not only two-point correlations. The
    equation-kernel relay and finite-rank construction identify precisely
    this null space. The raw inclusion comparison is supplied by
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-insertion-derivative-relay>;
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:phase-field-inclusions> descends these
    comparisons, and Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:phase-field-union>
    forms the common union. The calibrated physical unit gives
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ex-limits-unit>. The derivative
    comparison and Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:phase-field-derivatives>
    give its compatible positional derivatives. The conjugation and reflected
    adjoint are those of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:phase-union-adjoint>.
    Each construction uses the same all-spectator null spaces and the same
    table. This proves the field and Wilson-limit assertions in item 1.

    <em|The actual character space and every character.> Construct the mixed
    Euclidean Hilbert space from this table, then its bounded slab operators
    and their compression to the heat-fixed space.
    Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-phases> proves that their algebra is
    commutative; Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-character-measure> gives its
    compact metrizable character space and full-support vacuum probability.
    Its whole-word character evaluations define the phase correlations, with
    the mixture identity <eqref|ex-phase-barycenter>. Thus neither the phase
    space nor its measure has been chosen independently of the Wilson limit.

    Propositions<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-caps>
    and<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-slab-bounds> give the common smoothness,
    caps and slab bounds in every character.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-original-null>, the finite detectors of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-no-new-null> and their actual comparisons
    give the same field null relations, including no additional null labels.
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:phase-inclusion-every-character> carries
    the same inclusions into each phase. Reflection positivity, ordinary
    conjugation and orthogonal covariance are those of
    Sections<nbsp><reference|ex-phase-regularity>
    and<nbsp><reference|ex-reflection-transport>. This verifies item 2 with
    its universal character quantifier.

    For each character apply the complex heat construction, the joint
    boundary theorem and Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:heat-cups>. Their
    hypotheses are the slab, differential and reflection properties just
    obtained, which hold in every character.
    Propositions<nbsp><reference|prop:word-fields>
    and<nbsp><reference|prop:word-motion> give the common invariant domain,
    cyclic vacuum, adjoints, tempered fields and strongly continuous
    symmetry. The calibrated label action descends by
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:lorentz-label-descent>;
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:lorentz-covariance> and
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:spacelike-exchange> give covariance and
    locality. Boundary detection and Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:word-locality>
    retain nonzero field labels. The positive-energy part of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:field-spectrum> applies to every character.
    The every-character vacuum result Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ex-every-character-vacuum>
    supplies uniqueness of the translation-invariant vacuum there; the
    almost-everywhere selection theorem alone would not give this conclusion.
    Finally Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:assembly-boundary-barycenter>
    identifies the phase mixture on all joint Schwartz tests with the
    boundary of the mixed table. These are exactly the reconstructions in
    item 3.

    <em|One local product expansion and one physical stress.> Apply the
    finite-menu and cluster estimates of Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ope-history-service>
    to the stored transitions. The terminal local projection and reference
    matching are performed on the same insertion family and the same opened
    cells; their full replicated difference, not products of separately
    transferred moments, is estimated in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:reference-match>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:ope-selected-triangle> supplies the actual
    triangular readouts, and Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ope-local-trial-comparison>
    supplies the local-trial part of the physical-filtration argument. The
    finite-rank projections above have exactly its all-test kernel. Thus
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:ope-match-assembly> supplies its
    matching estimates on the one table. The grid argument and phase prices
    give Input<nbsp><reference|input:ope-raw-estimates>; the detector
    prescription is fixed before choosing a phase, spectators or truncation
    degree. Its common homogeneous version and
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:ope-quantitative> give
    <eqref|ex-ope-coefficient>\U<eqref|ex-ope-remainder> simultaneously for
    the mixed state and every character. Dependence of the small-radius
    threshold on a fixed truncation degree is retained.

    The finite-rank and comparison data used for the physical stress are on
    this same quotient. Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:actual-stress-normalization>
    fixes its nonzero scalar normalization once across grades.
    Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:selected-ward-package> and
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:phase-ward-inheritance> give its
    conserved, symmetric tensor and its translation and rotation charges in
    these fields. Retain the distinguished character
    <math|\<chi\><rsub|\<ast\>>> used in the selected trace endpoint.
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:trace-selected-endpoint> applies to precisely
    this stress, hierarchy and character, with the reconstruction just made.
    It centers the trace by this character's one-point functional, proves the
    stated positive short-distance coefficient and the finite-profile
    exclusion, and introduces no new hierarchy or field normalization. This
    is item 4.

    All four clauses consequently hold on the same refinement. Their
    extraction-dependent choices were made only after the public family was
    fixed. Forgetting the extra normalization and all-character information
    supplies Input<nbsp><reference|ex-joint-input>; applying
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-conditional-existence> proves the weaker
    public target without changing the family or the order of its limits.
  </proof>

  <section|Index of principal notation><label|sec:symbolic-index>

  The entries refer to the conventions at the indicated places. Symbols used
  only in a local calculation retain the meaning declared there. In
  particular, a change of type, scale, normalization, or limiting order
  requires the stated bridge; repeated letters alone do not identify
  mathematical objects.

  <\description>
    <item*|<math|G>, <math|r<rsub|G>>, <math|\<frak-g\>>>The fixed compact
    matrix group in its specified representation of complex dimension
    <math|r<rsub|G>>, and its real Lie algebra
    (Section<nbsp><reference|ex-statement>).

    <item*|<math|x=<around|(|\<b-x\>,t|)>>,
    <math|<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>>>Euclidean coordinates with time last, and
    the closed forward cone for the Minkowski form
    <math|t<rsup|2>-<around|\||\<b-x\>|\|><rsup|2>>
    (Section<nbsp><reference|ex-statement>).

    <item*|<math|P<rsub|p>>, <math|D<rsub|N>>, <math|a<rsub|N>>>Physical
    period, lattice denominator, and mesh, with
    <math|a<rsub|N>=D<rsub|N><rsup|-1>>. The site count per axis is
    <math|P<rsub|p>*D<rsub|N>> (Section<nbsp><reference|ex-statement>).

    <item*|<math|\<Lambda\><rsub|N,p>>, <math|U>,
    <math|U<rsub|i*j><around|(|x|)>>>Periodic sites, a group-valued link
    configuration, and its oriented plaquette holonomy
    (Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-lattice>).

    <item*|<math|S<rsub|\<ell\>>>, <math|\<beta\>>,
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<ell\>,\<beta\>>>>The finite plaquette action, its
    bare coefficient, and the normalized Wilson probability. Each pair of
    axes is counted once and the trace is divided by <math|r<rsub|G>>
    (Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-lattice>). This action is distinct from
    the correlation table <math|S> below.

    <item*|<math|O<rsub|N,p,v>>, <math|M<rsub|N,p>>, <math|W<rsub|N,p>>>A
    token insertion, its finite moment, and the smeared moment. The latter
    includes <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>> at arity <math|n>
    <eqref|ex-smearing>.

    <item*|<math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>>, <math|<math-up|Gen>>,
    <math|<math-up|Cyc><rsub|p>>, <math|<math-up|NoHit><rsub|p>>>Distinct-point
    configurations, their generic subset with separated coordinate
    projections, and the two period-dependent configuration domains defined
    in Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-limits>; the coordinatewise
    conditions are not interchangeable.

    <item*|<math|f<rsub|<math-up|mesh>>>, <math|f<rsub|<math-up|per>>>>The
    two increasing sequences of an internal extraction order. The ordered
    tail has a period threshold outside a mesh threshold that may depend on
    the period (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:ex-limits-estimates>).

    <item*|<math|S>, <math|S<rsup|\<chi\>>>, <math|S<rsup|<math-up|mix>>>>The
    limiting Euclidean table, a character component, and its mixed
    correlation. The mixture is the integral of the character correlations in
    <eqref|ex-mixture>; it is the mixed correlation that appears in the
    Wilson limit.

    <item*|<math|V<rsub|d>>, <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|d>>,
    <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|d>>, <math|<around|[|v|]><rsub|d>>>Classical jet
    polynomials of weighted degree at most <math|d>, the all-spectator null
    space, and its quotient <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|d>=V<rsub|d>/\<cal-N\><rsub|d>>.
    The grade is part of the label: equal polynomials at different grades
    need not represent the same field (Subsection<nbsp><reference|ex-construction-headline>).
    A grade is not automatically a scaling dimension
    (Section<nbsp><reference|ex-statement>).

    <item*|<math|L<rsub|d>>, <math|R<rsub|d,N,p>>,
    <math|I<rsup|<around|(|d|)>><rsub|N,p>>>The jet-normalized local section,
    its reference-scale readout and the renormalized lattice insertion in
    <eqref|eq:headline-insertion>. The grade-<math|d> unit is
    <math|<around|[|a<rsub|r<rsub|d>><rsup|d>*\<b-1\>|]><rsub|d>>; the
    readout is inverted before insertion.

    <item*|<math|X>, <math|\<nu\>>, <math|\<chi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>>In the
    public target, a compact phase space, its full-support probability, and a
    selected character. The symbols are local to that discussion; the
    construction uses <math|\<Xi\>> and <math|\<mu\>> in
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|ex-character-measure>.

    <item*|<math|\<cal-H\>>, <math|\<Omega\>>, <math|\<cal-D\>>,
    <math|\<Phi\>>>A reconstructed character's Hilbert space, vacuum, common
    invariant domain, and fields (Definition<nbsp><reference|ex-reconstructed>).
    The domain is distinct from any locally named contact-device or matrix
    object.

    <item*|<math|A>, <math|M>, <math|P<rsub|\<cal-H\>>> in the sector
    calculation>The completed precision <math|A=M<rsup|\<ast\>>*M+P<rsub|\<cal-H\><rsup|c>>>,
    selected row map, and projection onto active bonds
    <eqref|eq:sector-precision>. The Gaussian covariance, when defined, is
    the inverse precision; the completion is identity on inactive
    coordinates.

    <item*|<math|D<rsub|v,a>> and <math|<with|math-font|cal*|D><rsub|v,a>> in
    the free-kernel calculation>The train drive and level response,
    respectively. They have different formulas and cannot be substituted for
    each other (Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-free-kernel-components>
    and<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-free-kernel-estimates>).

    <item*|<math|A>, <math|A<rsub|0>>, <math|C> in Gaussian reassembly>The
    completed sector precision, flat reference precision, and localized
    positive finite-range covariance. The correction <math|C<rsup|-1>-A> is
    retained; <math|C> is not simply identified with <math|A<rsup|-1>>
    (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:gaussian-reassembly>).

    <item*|<math|a<rsub|m>,b<rsub|m>,c<rsub|m>> in background paths>The
    quadratic-loop, cubic, and quartic coefficient arrays at a fixed scale,
    in the prescribed real coefficient spaces
    (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:background-path-convergence>). This
    <math|a<rsub|m>> is local and differs from the physical mesh.

    <item*|<math|\<varepsilon\>> in the healthy-field comparison>The field
    error and per-step row/covariance error are separate quantities in the
    inputs to the comparison. The stated conclusion uses a common bound for
    both (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:marked-healthy-cumulant-launch>).

    <item*|<math|\<lambda\>> in the sector calculation>The normalization
    <math|q<rsup|-1/2>> for <math|q> signed channels, so its square gives the
    orientation average (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:sector-gram>). Other
    local uses of <math|\<lambda\>> are introduced separately.

    <item*|<math|\<lambda\><rsub|G>>, <math|b<rsub|0><around|(|G|)>>,
    <math|s<rsub|G>>>The annulus coefficient, canonical running coefficient,
    and shooting-line slope, respectively. The two Casimir definitions use
    the same color inner product, giving <math|\<lambda\><rsub|G>=2*b<rsub|0><around|(|G|)>>.
    The shooting slope is the same constant,
    <math|s<rsub|G>=\<lambda\><rsub|G>> <eqref|eq:shoot-slope>; it also
    appears as <math|c<rsub|G>> and <math|\<kappa\>>.

    <item*|<math|E<rsub|d>>, <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|d>>, <math|\<pi\><rsub|d>>,
    <math|\<tau\><rsub|I,D>> in the annulus>Reference label space,
    selected-table field space, quotient map, and spectator tester
    (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-lattice-actual-stress-annulus>). The
    finite-volume labels and their quotient fields have distinct domains.

    <item*|<math|<math-ss|P><rsub|r><around|(|M;C|)>> in the annular
    estimate>A bound on the matrix path and its first two derivatives with
    weights <math|r<rsub|i><rsup|j>> for <math|j=0,1,2>
    (Subsection<nbsp><reference|sec:ex-annulus-fleet-faces>). Here
    <math|r<rsub|i>> is a local price scale, not automatically an annulus or
    short-distance radius.

    <item*|<math|T<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>>, <math|\<Theta\>>>The Ward-normalized
    physical stress tensor and its trace centered in the selected phase, as
    in Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:trace-selected-endpoint>. Centering refers
    to that phase, not to the mixed state.

    <item*|<math|T<around|(|r|)>>>The selected two-point trace quantity in
    the final short-distance requirement <eqref|ex-trace-limit>; this is a
    separate condition from the operator-product estimate in
    <eqref|ex-ope-remainder>.
  </description>

